__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'analects',kind:'book',promise:'Become a good person, one step at a time',about:'What if being good is about what you do, more than what you believe? Confucius was a teacher in China long ago. His students wrote down his answers to their questions. Here are nine of his best ideas. Which one will you try first?',ideas:{analects_1:{title:'Advice for the Asker',card:{insight:'Good advice pushes back on what a person does too much.',tryThis:'Before you give advice, ask what this person does too much. Then push the other way.',skills:['communication','relationships']},review:{prompt:'A coworker who always rushes wants to send her report today. What do you say?',right:'Tell her to slow down, since she already rushes',wrong:'Tell her to send it, since she seems ready',why:'She already rushes, so she needs to hear slow down.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two friends ask you **the same thing**: should I try out for the team? One always says yes to everything. The other never tries anything new. Should they get **the same answer**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Confucius, a famous teacher in China, had this happen. Two students asked if they should act at once on what they learned. He told **the bold one** to wait. He told **the shy one** to go ahead.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Good advice isn\u2019t **the same for everyone**. First look at what the person **does too much**. Then you can help them with the other side.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two people on your team ask the same question: should I just **ship it**? One rushes everything out half-checked. The other polishes for weeks. Would you give them **one answer**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Two of Confucius\u2019s students asked if they should act at once on what they heard. Zilu, the bold one, was told to ask his father and elder brother first. Ran Qiu, the hesitant one, was told to go ahead.'},{kind:'explain',text:'A third student asked why. Confucius said Ran Qiu holds back, so he pushed him on. Zilu rushes, so he held him back. The contradiction **wasn\u2019t a slip**. It was **the method**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'A general rule gets **filtered by habit**. Advice aimed at the person **changes the habit**.',left:{title:'One rule',items:['The rusher hears speed','The ditherer hears care','Nobody changes']},right:{title:'Aimed advice',items:['The rusher hears slow down','The ditherer hears act now','Each gets what\u2019s missing']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Advice is **a diagnosis**, not a rule. Before you give it, ask what the person **already overdoes**. Then aim at that, even if it\u2019s the opposite of what you told someone else.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Several students ask Confucius about the same virtue. Each one gets **a different reply**, and the book rarely says why. Was the teacher **careless**, or was something else going on?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The Analects were collected by his students after he died. They record answers to **particular people**. Read that way, a contradiction isn\u2019t a flaw. It\u2019s **a diagnosis**, fitted to the person asking.'},{kind:'story',text:'One passage explains itself. Zilu and Ran Qiu each ask whether to act at once on what they hear. Zilu must defer to his family first. Ran Qiu should go ahead. One overreaches, Confucius says, and one hangs back.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a manager closing a meeting: move faster, but don\u2019t cut corners. The one who rushes hears *move faster*. The one who dithers hears the warning. Both nod. Both leave doing exactly what they did before.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The method needs a teacher who **knows the student**. Confucius watched his for years. Aimed at a stranger, the opposite of the rule is only a guess. Without real acquaintance, the general rule is **safer**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The right advice depends on **who\u2019s asking**. If you know them well, aim at **what they overdo**. If you don\u2019t, say less, and ask more.'}]},analects_2:{title:'Skip What You\u2019d Hate',card:{insight:'It is easier to know what you would hate than what others like.',tryThis:'Before your next joke or favor, ask if you would hate it done to you.',skills:['relationships','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'You are planning a surprise for a shy friend. Which question helps most?',right:'Would I hate this if it were done to me?',wrong:'Would I love this if it were done for me?',why:'You know what you would hate better than what she would like.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You love **loud games**. So for your friend\u2019s birthday, you plan a huge, noisy surprise party. But your friend is shy and hates being stared at. How will **her day** go?'},{kind:'explain',text:'One rule says: treat others how you want to be treated. But people want **different things**. Confucius, a teacher in China long ago, said it another way. Don\u2019t do to others what you\u2019d **hate done** to you.'},{kind:'moral',text:'It\u2019s hard to guess **what others like**. It\u2019s much easier to know **what you\u2019d hate**. So start there, and skip anything you wouldn\u2019t want done to you.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A student once asked Confucius for **a single word** to practise for his whole life. You might expect courage, or loyalty, or love. What word would you give **in his place**?'},{kind:'story',text:'The student was Zigong. The answer was reciprocity, and Confucius put it in the negative. What you don\u2019t want for yourself, don\u2019t impose on others. It asks for **restraint**, not a guess at anyone\u2019s tastes.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The positive rule assumes your tastes are **everyone\u2019s tastes**. The negative one only asks **what you\u2019d resent**.',left:{title:'The positive rule',items:['Do what you\u2019d want','Guesses their tastes']},right:{title:'The negative rule',items:['Skip what you\u2019d resent','Needs no guessing']}},{kind:'explain',text:'So before the feedback, the joke or the favour, picture yourself **in their place**. Would you resent it? If so, stop there. It\u2019s simple to say and **slow to practise**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You can rarely know **what others want**. You nearly always know **what you\u2019d resent**. Start from that, and you\u2019ll avoid the harm in front of you.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Zigong tells his teacher he already lives by a rule. What he doesn\u2019t want done to him, he won\u2019t do to others. Confucius doesn\u2019t praise him. He says it\u2019s **not yet within reach**. Why would he say **that**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The rule is reciprocity, said in the negative: don\u2019t impose on others what you wouldn\u2019t want. It needs no knowledge of **their tastes**. It assumes only that what people resent **mostly overlaps**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Elsewhere, Zigong asks for one word to practise for life. Confucius gives him this same rule. So it\u2019s both a starting point and **a lifetime\u2019s work**. Simple to state. Slow to practise.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a keen helper who reorganises a colleague\u2019s desk while she\u2019s away. He\u2019d love that done for him. She hates it. The positive rule licensed his favour. The negative rule would have stopped it.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. It\u2019s **a floor**, not a programme. It stops cruelty but won\u2019t make you useful. And your resentments are the measure, so someone who wouldn\u2019t mind harshness can **excuse giving it**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Start from **what you\u2019d resent**, not what you\u2019d like. It prevents **the specific harm** in front of you. Then add the harder part: helping others stand as you\u2019d wish to.'}]},analects_3:{title:'Read, Then Use It',card:{insight:'What you read does you no good until you use it.',tryThis:'After you read something, pick one idea from it. Use it this week, before you read more.',skills:['thinking','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'You read three books about work this month. What should you do next?',right:'Use one idea from them before you read more',wrong:'Start a fourth book while the topic feels fresh',why:'Reading only helps once you use it, so use one idea first.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You watch a video on **a skateboard trick**. It all makes sense. You feel like you\u2019ve got it. Then you step on the board and **fall straight off**. What went missing?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Confucius, a teacher in China long ago, said learning needs **two halves**. Reading without thinking leaves you with facts **you can\u2019t use**. Thinking without reading leaves you sure of things that are wrong.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Learning and thinking **need each other**. After you learn something, **try it out**. After you work something out, check what others already know.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You read forty books last year, plus **the summaries** and the podcasts. Now name one decision any of them changed. If you **can\u2019t think** of one, what were you doing?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Confucius put it in a short pair. Learning without thinking is **wasted effort**. Thinking without learning is **dangerous**. Each half describes a different person, and most of us lean one way.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'One has **read everything** and can do nothing. The other has read nothing and is **sure of everything**.',left:{title:'Only reading',items:['Well-read and useless','Feels informed, changes nothing']},right:{title:'Only thinking',items:['Confident nonsense','Repeats old, corrected mistakes']}},{kind:'explain',text:'The first trap is pleasant. **Feeling informed** comes from the consuming, so it arrives whether you understood or not. The test is simple. Has anything you took in **changed a decision**?'},{kind:'moral',text:'**Alternate** the two. After reading, **apply it** to a live decision. After your own reasoning, go and find out what people who studied the question concluded.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Confucius once said he spent **a whole day** without eating and a night without sleeping, just thinking. He gained nothing. What do you think he **did instead**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'His pair is short. Learning without thinking wastes your effort. Thinking without learning puts you in danger. **Study gives thought** its material, and **thought tests** what study supplies. You need both, in turns.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take a reader who gets through a summary a day. She can talk about dozens of ideas. Ask which one changed how she works, and there\u2019s a long pause. The feeling of being informed was real. The learning wasn\u2019t.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take someone who reasons from first principles about a field he\u2019s never studied. He\u2019s clever and very sure. He arrives, with great confidence, at mistakes the field corrected long ago.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The pair isn\u2019t symmetrical, even for him: after his sleepless night, he **leaned towards study**. And there\u2019s **no fixed ratio**. Some fields need years of reading before your judgement is worth much.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t let reading **pass for understanding**. Use what you take in, and test **your own reasoning** against people who studied the question. Each half corrects the other.'}]},analects_4:{title:'Manners That Shape You',card:{insight:'Kind acts you repeat with care slowly become who you are.',tryThis:'Pick one small kind habit, like saying good morning. Do it on purpose today, with care.',skills:['habits','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'Thanking your mom for dinner feels empty lately. What should you do?',right:'Keep saying it, and say it with real care',wrong:'Stop saying it until you feel thankful again',why:'Doing it with care is how the thankful feeling grows.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your family says thank you to whoever cooked, **every night**. You usually mumble it. One week you say it properly and **look at the cook**. What happens by Friday?'},{kind:'explain',text:'By Friday you **really feel** thankful. Confucius, a teacher in China long ago, cared a lot about manners. Not for show. He thought doing something kind on purpose could **train the heart**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The things you **do again and again** shape who you become. Do a kind thing on purpose, even before you feel it. That\u2019s how **the feeling grows**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Someone walks into the room and you **stand up**. You don\u2019t believe they outrank you. You just stand. So what is that small movement **actually doing** to you?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Everyone accepts that feeling produces conduct: you care, so you call. Confucius adds **the reverse**. Conduct also produces feeling. Standing up is **a rehearsal of attention**, and repeated, it becomes a habit of mind.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'He rejected **hollow form**, but he didn\u2019t wait for sincerity either. He wanted **attentive form**.',left:{title:'Hollow form',items:['Done on autopilot','Trains nothing']},right:{title:'Attentive form',items:['Done on purpose','Trains the feeling']}},{kind:'explain',text:'The modern version is unglamorous. You attend the stand-up properly. You answer each message on the very same day. Neither proves you care. They\u2019re how **caring becomes reliable**, not **just occasional**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t drop a form because it **feels empty**. **Practise it** with attention instead. Done that way, the ritual is how the feeling gets built.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'One book of the Analects records **almost nothing** he said. Instead it shows how Confucius stood, ate, dressed and greeted people. Why would his students write down **his table manners**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Because for him, ritual was **formative**, not decorative. The forms you repeat build the disposition you have. It isn\u2019t a costume over character. Practised with attention, it\u2019s **how character is built**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a junior doctor who stands each time a patient\u2019s family walks in. At first it\u2019s a rule she follows. Months later she notices she\u2019s already turned towards them before she thinks about it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a family that says thank you at every meal but mumbles it, eyes on their phones. The words haven\u2019t changed in years. Nothing in anyone has changed either. The form went hollow.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The idea can become **a demand for conformity**: if form makes character, enforce form. Confucian ritual also fixed rank for centuries. A form done **under compulsion** may train resentment, not care.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Hollow ritual is worthless, and so is waiting for sincerity. Keep **the form you choose**, and do it **with attention**. That\u2019s how your caring becomes reliable.'}]},analects_5:{title:'The Mistake You Don\u2019t Fix',card:{insight:'A mistake happens once, but leaving it unfixed is a choice you make each day.',tryThis:'Name one thing you have known was wrong for a month. Take the first step to fix it today.',skills:['habits','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'A spreadsheet at work has had a wrong number since spring. What is the worse mistake?',right:'Leaving it unfixed all these months',wrong:'Typing the wrong number in the first place',why:'You made the mistake once, but you leave it unfixed each day.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Every week your teacher marks **the same word** wrong. You know it\u2019s wrong. You keep meaning to learn it. Six weeks later, you **still spell it** the old way. What\u2019s the real problem?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Confucius, a teacher in China long ago, didn\u2019t ask students to **feel bad** about mistakes. Everybody makes them, he knew. He asked one plain question instead: **have you fixed it** yet?'},{kind:'moral',text:'Making a mistake is **normal**. The real mistake is **knowing and not fixing** it. So pick one thing you keep getting wrong, and fix part of it today.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'There\u2019s a formula in your team\u2019s budget sheet that\u2019s been wrong **since spring**. You meant to fix it. Every month, you **work around it**. How long has it been wrong on purpose?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Confucius put it plainly: having a fault and **not correcting it** is the real fault. A mistake is a fact about **one moment**. Leaving it uncorrected is a choice you make again every day.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The weight moves from **the slip** to **what you do next**.',left:{title:'The mistake',items:['Happened once, in spring','Forgivable, and common','Can\u2019t be undone']},right:{title:'Leaving it',items:['Chosen again every month','The fault that counts','Can be fixed today']}},{kind:'explain',text:'That\u2019s why the Analects are **light on guilt**. Confucius barely asks anyone to feel worse. He asks whether **the thing is fixed**. Energy spent on how a fault looks is energy not spent on the fault.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t judge yourself by **a clean record**. Judge yourself by **what gets fixed**. That spreadsheet formula is waiting, and the knowing isn\u2019t the work.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Asked which student **loved learning**, Confucius named Yan Hui. One of his two reasons sounds oddly modest. It wasn\u2019t that Yan Hui never erred. What could be **better than that**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The saying is brief: to have a fault and not amend it is **the true fault**. It moves accountability from the past, which **can\u2019t be altered**, to the present, which can.'},{kind:'story',text:'Yan Hui did make mistakes. What he never did was make the same one twice. He also didn\u2019t take his anger out on others. For Confucius, that was what loving learning looked like.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a manager who emailed her team the wrong deadline. She noticed a week later and said nothing, hoping it wouldn\u2019t matter. Months on, the typo is long forgotten. By now, **the silence** is a bigger fault than the typo ever was.'},{kind:'explain',text:'There\u2019s a second benefit. If error is expected, **admitting one** costs less. Less hiding means faster repair. The book even tells students **not to fear** changing once they see a fault.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some faults **can\u2019t be amended**, only owned: the harm is done and the person is gone. And where someone was hurt, amending means **making it right** with them, not just patching the process.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You\u2019ll make mistakes. That part is **forgivable**. What counts is whether you **amend them**, and never make the same one the same way again.'}]},analects_6:{title:'The Right Names',card:{insight:'When a word stops matching the thing it names, plans built on it fail.',tryThis:'When a plan keeps going wrong, check if one word means two things.',skills:['communication','leadership']},review:{prompt:'Your report says on track, but the project is late. What should you do first?',right:'Make the word match the facts, then decide what to do',wrong:'Hold a meeting so the team talks more',why:'Plans built on a wrong word go wrong too.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your mum says your room is tidy when **the floor** is clear. You think it\u2019s tidy when **the bed** is made. Every Saturday, you argue. What\u2019s the fight **really about**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'It\u2019s about **one word**: tidy. Confucius, a teacher in China long ago, was asked what he\u2019d do first if he ran a country. He said he\u2019d make sure things had **their right names**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When words don\u2019t match **the things** they name, people get mixed up. Plans go wrong. So next time you argue, ask: are we using **one word** for two things?'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A student asks his teacher what he\u2019d do first if a ruler put him **in charge** of government. You might guess armies, or taxes. The answer sounds like **a joke**. What would you pick?'},{kind:'story',text:'The student was Zilu, and the teacher was Confucius. His answer: **rectify the names**. Zilu told him this was wide of the mark. Confucius called him uncultivated, and then he explained.'},{kind:'explain',text:'If names aren\u2019t correct, **language doesn\u2019t fit** the facts. Statements become false without anyone lying. Plans built on them fail, and in the end people don\u2019t know **how to act**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Your project status says green. Everyone close to it knows it\u2019s amber. Decisions above it are made on green. When it fails, the review blames communication. The real fault was **a word** that stopped **meaning anything**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The repair is unglamorous. **Make the word match** the thing, or change **the thing to match** the word. Either works, but leaving the gap open never does.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Zilu asks Confucius what he\u2019d do first if the ruler of Wei gave him **charge**. Commentators link it to a dispute in Wei, where a son held the throne his exiled father claimed. Why might **the answer** be about words?'},{kind:'explain',text:'His answer was to **rectify the names**. When a word doesn\u2019t match its reality, statements turn false without a lie. Affairs fail. People **stop knowing** what to do. Which names fitted whom was a live question.'},{kind:'story',text:'Read that way, the question was concrete. Was the man on the throne a ruler, or a son keeping out his father? Each name demanded different conduct. Get the name wrong and every decision after it inherited the error.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a company where the title says *team lead* but the job is pure admin. Nobody lies. Yet every plan that assumes a leader goes wrong, and the confusion gets blamed on communication.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The doctrine **cuts two ways**. It can demand that a ruler actually rule well. It has often been used to keep everyone **in their station**. And some words are vague by necessity.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Before you fix anything else, check **your words**. If a name and its thing have drifted apart, close the gap. Don\u2019t blame **communication** for a vocabulary problem.'}]},analects_7:{title:'More Than a Tool',card:{insight:'If you only know one skill, you are a tool someone else uses.',tryThis:'Ask what you would be worth if your main skill was not needed. Name one thing to learn.',skills:['career','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'You are the best spreadsheet person at work. Which question helps your career most?',right:'What would I be worth if my main skill was not needed?',wrong:'How can I get even better at my one main skill?',why:'If one skill is all you have, you need someone to want it.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A spoon is great at **one job**. But can it decide what soup to make? Can it tell if the soup **tastes good**? Who has to do that part?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Confucius, a teacher in China long ago, had a saying just four characters long. It says a good person **isn\u2019t a tool**. A tool does one job, and **someone else** decides how to use it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You can be great at something and still **think for yourself**. Keep learning things **outside your best skill**. Then you can handle jobs you weren\u2019t trained for.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'She\u2019s **the best analyst** in the building, and every review says so. Then the company changes direction. The reports she built **stop being wanted**. What does she have left now?'},{kind:'story',text:'She finds she has no view on what should replace them. She was excellent, and wholly dependent on someone wanting that one thing. Confucius had a four-character saying for her situation: the gentleman is **not a utensil**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'A utensil has **one function**. Someone else decides where it goes, and it can\u2019t ask whether the job is the right job. Confucius wanted people who **do the deciding**, not instruments that execute.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'This isn\u2019t a case against **expertise**. He was exacting about competence. It\u2019s a case against being **only expertise**.',left:{title:'A utensil',items:['One function','Waits to be used','Can\u2019t judge the job']},right:{title:'A person',items:['Deep skill, plus more','Decides where skill goes','Meets the untrained-for']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Keep your depth, because it\u2019s valuable. But build **something outside it**. That\u2019s what lets you judge **where your skill** goes, and still matter when it isn\u2019t wanted.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Zigong once asked Confucius **what he thought** of him. A vessel, came the reply. Which vessel? A precious one, used for grain offerings in the temple. Was that **praise**, or something else?'},{kind:'explain',text:'It was praise and **a limit** in one answer. Elsewhere he says the gentleman is not a utensil. A vessel is defined by what it holds. A cultivated person is defined by **judgement**, which no single function contains.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take a superb translator who has only ever handled contracts. Her firm asks which work it should stop taking on. She has no view at all. She was always handed the job, and never once asked to choose it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a nurse who becomes a ward manager. Her clinical skill still matters. But the new job asks what the skills of twenty people **should be used on**. That\u2019s a judgement no single skill contains.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The ideal was shaped for **a small class** whose work was governing, and it could look down on craft. Societies need people superb at one thing. The saying can be used **to sneer** at them.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your depth is worth keeping. Just don\u2019t let it be **all you are**. Build the judgement to decide **where any skill** goes, your own included.'}]},analects_8:{title:'The Edge of Knowing',card:{insight:'To know well, you also have to know what you do not know.',tryThis:'Before a big choice, write what you know. Then write what you guess.',skills:['thinking','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'You are about to agree to a plan you half understand. What do you do?',right:'Sort what you know from what you only guess',wrong:'Trust your gut and say yes right away',why:'A guess you act on like a fact can cost you, so mark it first.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'In a team quiz, you write down an answer **you\u2019re not sure** of. You write it as if you were sure. Your team trusts you. You **lose the point**. What went wrong?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Confucius, a teacher in China long ago, said real knowing has **two parts**. Know what you know. And know **what you don\u2019t know**. A good map shows where the land ends and the sea begins.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Mixing up what you know with **what you\u2019re guessing** causes big mistakes. If you\u2019re not sure, **say so**. Someone else might know.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two people each face a choice they half understand. One marks her guesses and checks them. The other feels certain and **doesn\u2019t check** at all. Which one is in **real trouble**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Confucius gave this definition to Zilu, the brave, blunt student quick to act. If you know something, count it as known. If you don\u2019t, admit it\u2019s unknown. That, he said, is knowledge.'},{kind:'explain',text:'It looks like modesty. It\u2019s closer to **bookkeeping**. If you can\u2019t keep the two piles apart, you\u2019ll draw on the second **without noticing**. Then you spend confidence you don\u2019t have.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The danger isn\u2019t ignorance. It\u2019s **unmarked ignorance**, the kind you **act on** at full confidence.',left:{title:'Marked',items:['You ask','You check','You hedge']},right:{title:'Unmarked',items:['You act as if sure','Costly mistakes live here']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Before a decision, sort **what you\u2019re sure** of from **what you\u2019re assuming**. Then check the assumptions first. That\u2019s where the expensive mistakes live.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'You know the plan well. You\u2019ve read every page. But somewhere in it is a step you\u2019ve **never checked**, and you can\u2019t see which. How would you **ever find it**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Confucius defined knowing in **two layers**. You must know things, and also know **which things** you know. The second layer tells you where to ask, check or hedge. It\u2019s what makes the first one safe to use.'},{kind:'story',text:'He gave the definition to Zilu, whom the Analects portray as brave and quick to act. For a student like that, the risk isn\u2019t knowing too little. It\u2019s acting at full speed on a guess.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a doctor fresh from training who meets a rash she\u2019s never seen. If she marks it unknown, she asks a colleague. If she guesses and calls it eczema, the patient goes home with the wrong cream.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The boundary is **invisible from inside**, so the job is never finished. It can also be overplayed. Someone who labels everything unknown never commits. False modesty can **dodge responsibility** too.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Aim for **an accurate edge**, not a retreating one. Keep going back to **mark the line** between what you know and what you\u2019re guessing. You\u2019ll never finish, and that\u2019s the point.'}]},analects_9:{title:'A Life in One List',card:{insight:'You are growing when doing right takes less and less effort.',tryThis:'List three good habits. Mark which still take a push and which now come easily.',skills:['habits','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'You still drag yourself to the gym after two years. What does that show?',right:'This habit still takes effort, so it is not yours yet',wrong:'Your willpower is great, so you are doing fine',why:'You are growing when a good habit stops needing a push.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Remember learning to **ride a bike**? At first you held on tight and thought about every push. Then one day, you just rode. Where did all **that effort** go?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Confucius, a teacher in China long ago, told his whole life as **a short list** of stages. After thirty, each stage took ten years. At seventy, he said, he simply **wanted the right thing**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Being good takes **a long time**. At first you\u2019ve got to push yourself. Slowly you start to want the right thing, and **the pushing stops**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A sixty-day plan promises **a new you**. Confucius told his own story differently: one sentence, covering **fifty-five years**. So what\u2019s waiting at the end of it?'},{kind:'story',text:'At fifteen he set his heart on learning. At thirty he stood firm. Doubts left at forty. At fifty he knew what heaven asked. At sixty he heard truth readily. At seventy he followed his heart and **didn\u2019t overstep**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The last stage is **the point**. The goal isn\u2019t iron self-control. It\u2019s no longer needing it, because what you want and what\u2019s right **have become the same**. Discipline was only the scaffolding.'},{kind:'explain',text:'That gives you **a better measure**. Don\u2019t score how well you manage effort. Score how much of the right behaviour **still needs effort** at all. Less effort means further along.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Real change is slow, and its stages **can\u2019t be skipped**. Treat each one as somewhere you live. Your progress is how much good behaviour **no longer takes effort**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'An old man sums up his life **in one sentence**, decade by decade. Most of it describes effort: learning, standing firm, shedding doubt. Then the last stage says something **surprising**. What could it be?'},{kind:'story',text:'That man was Confucius. At fifteen, learning. At thirty, standing firm. At forty, free of doubt. Then knowing heaven\u2019s decrees, then an ear open to truth. At seventy, following his heart\u2019s desire without crossing the line.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Every earlier stage is **effort against something**: ignorance, doubt, resistance to hearing. The last describes **no friction at all**. What he desired and what was right had become the same. Discipline was the means.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now think of someone who\u2019s cooked for her family for decades. She doesn\u2019t force herself to make dinner, or check a recipe for the basics. The care she once had to push for is simply what she wants.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. It\u2019s one man\u2019s look back, and the stages may be **partly idealised**. It isn\u2019t a schedule. Nor does it mean you should trust every impulse. His desires were **trained over decades**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Self-control is **the scaffolding**. The building is **wanting the right thing**. So judge your progress by how much good behaviour still takes effort, and expect the answer to change slowly.'}]}}}},943,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'art-of-being-right',kind:'book',promise:'Spot the tricks people use to win arguments',about:'Have you ever lost an argument you knew you were right about? Around 1830, a German thinker named Arthur Schopenhauer wrote a short book about this. It lists 38 tricks people use to win, even when they are wrong. Want to learn to spot them?',ideas:{'art-of-being-right_1':{title:'Winning Versus Being Right',card:{insight:'You can win an argument and still be wrong.',tryThis:'After you lose an argument, look up the facts. Then decide if you were right or wrong.',skills:['thinking','communication']},review:{prompt:'A coworker wins a debate with jokes, but his numbers were wrong. What happened?',right:'He won the room, but he was still wrong',wrong:'His numbers must have been close enough to pass',why:'A whole room can agree with someone who is wrong.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Sam says loudly that a whale is a fish. Priya says quietly that it\u2019s a mammal. The class **laughs at Priya**. So who **looks like the winner**, and who is actually right?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Being right means what you say **is true**. Winning means people **think you\u2019re right**. A German thinker called Arthur Schopenhauer noticed these are two skills. He wrote down 38 tricks people use to win when they\u2019re wrong.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Sounding sure can **win the room** without the facts. Schopenhauer wrote the tricks down so you could **spot them**, not use them.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your colleague has the figures wrong, and you know it. But he answers fast, with a smile. The room **laughs at his joke** about your spreadsheet. The decision goes his way. Did **anyone check** the figures?'},{kind:'story',text:'Arthur Schopenhauer, the German philosopher, thought this happened constantly. He drafted a short book of thirty-eight stratagems for coming off best in a dispute. It came out only after his death.'},{kind:'explain',text:'His reason is about **pride**, not logic. You defend a view because you said it, and **vanity outlasts the evidence**. So technique, audience and stamina decide the dispute. Truth wins only when it lines up with them.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Schopenhauer kept these apart: **logic** asks what\u2019s true, **dialectic** asks who comes off best.',left:{title:'Being right',items:['The claim is true','The conclusion follows','Works even spoken quietly']},right:{title:'Winning',items:['The room agrees','Turns on nerve and timing','Works even when wrong']}},{kind:'moral',text:'The room\u2019s verdict measures **technique and mood**. Whether the figures were right is **a separate question**. Learn the moves so you can name them, not use them.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A philosopher drafts **thirty-eight tricks** for winning arguments you\u2019re losing. Then he leaves the book unpublished in his papers. Why would anyone write **a manual** he didn\u2019t seem to want used?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Schopenhauer splits two arts. **Logic** asks what\u2019s true and what follows. **Dialectic**, in his sense, asks who comes off best. The two part company constantly, because people defend a claim **because they made it**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a budget meeting. One manager has the numbers wrong but answers every point quickly. He gets a laugh at a rival\u2019s spreadsheet. The decision goes his way. Nobody in the room ever checks the figures.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a school debate. Leo talks fast and sounds certain. Mia speaks slowly and has the real facts. Most of the hall claps for Leo. The vote measured confidence, and confidence was all it measured.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The list can be read as a manual, which is the obvious misuse. Subtler still is calling every strong point a trick. And a fluent, confident opponent is **sometimes also right**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A win tells you **who handled the room**. It doesn\u2019t tell you who was right. Use the distinction to **check a result**, never to excuse every loss.'}]},'art-of-being-right_2':{title:'The Stretched Claim',card:{insight:'When your words get stretched, do not defend words you never said.',tryThis:'When someone says \u201cSo you mean,\u201d stop. Say your own words again before you answer.',skills:['communication','thinking']},review:{prompt:'You said some rules do not work. They say you want no rules. Now what?',right:'Say your own words again before you answer',wrong:'Explain why having no rules at all might work',why:'Only answer what you said, not what they made up.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'At dinner you say you don\u2019t much like broccoli. Your brother grins. So you hate **all vegetables**? Carrots are great, he says, and everyone nods. What did you **actually say**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'This trick is called **the stretched claim**. Someone makes your words bigger than they were. Then they knock down the bigger version. The small thing you really said **never gets an answer**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Listen for **big words** like all, never or always that you didn\u2019t use. Then say your **first words again**, exactly as you said them.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'In a meeting you say **some regulation** is counterproductive. The reply comes straight back: so you think all regulation should go? Heads turn your way. You\u2019re now **defending a claim** you never made.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Schopenhauer puts this first on his list: **extension**. Push a claim past its natural limits, often from one case to all cases. Then refute the exaggerated version. It\u2019s easy to beat because **nobody said it**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Why does it work? The audience **rarely rechecks** your original wording. And defending words you never said looks like retreat. After three exchanges, the stretch has become **what the room believes** you said.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Schopenhauer\u2019s defence is exact: restate your claim **in its original limits**, and **refuse the rest**.',left:{title:'What you said',items:['Some regulation backfires','I\u2019m wary of this hire']},right:{title:'What gets attacked',items:['All regulation should go','You never trust newcomers']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Catch the stretch **early**, while it\u2019s still one sentence old. Say your exact words again and **decline the wider claim**. Don\u2019t argue for words you never said.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'You say this film is **too long**. A friend answers: so you hate long films? Within a minute, you\u2019re defending a taste **you don\u2019t have**. How did that happen so fast?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Schopenhauer opens his catalogue with **extension**. Stretch the other side\u2019s claim beyond its natural limits, then refute the stretched version. The move rests on **one weakness**: listeners almost never go back to the original wording.'},{kind:'story',text:'A councillor says some regulation is counterproductive. An opponent asks why she wants every rule scrapped. She spends three exchanges explaining she doesn\u2019t. By then, the room remembers her as the one against all rules.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a hiring panel. One member says he\u2019s wary of this candidate. Another replies that he never trusts anyone new. He restates at once: this candidate, this reason. The wider charge never gets a hearing.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Not every broadening is a trick. If your reason for one case applies to all, saying so is **fair argument**. And someone who retreats to ever narrower wording is running the reverse move.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Check every restatement against **your own words**. If it\u2019s wider, say the original again and keep to it. The earlier you do it, **the cheaper it is**.'}]},'art-of-being-right_3':{title:'The Last Resort',card:{insight:'When someone gets rude right after your point, they may be losing.',tryThis:'Next time someone gets personal in a fight, notice when it happened. Then go back to your point.',skills:['communication','psychology']},review:{prompt:'You are winning on the numbers. Then they say you hate the project. Now what?',right:'Notice when it happened, then go back to the numbers',wrong:'Prove that you never hated the project at all',why:'People often get rude when they start to lose.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You and a friend argue about whose turn it is to pick the film. You\u2019re **clearly right**. Then your friend says, you always have to win. Wait. **What happened** to the film?'},{kind:'explain',text:'When someone starts losing, they sometimes **talk about you** instead. Your mood, your reasons, your manners. Schopenhauer put this **last on his list**. It\u2019s what people reach for when the real argument isn\u2019t going their way.'},{kind:'moral',text:'If the talk **turns to you**, you were probably winning. Stay calm and **go back** to the film, or whatever the point was.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re arguing the project is **over budget**, with the numbers on screen. The reply isn\u2019t about the numbers. It\u2019s that you\u2019ve always disliked this project. And your own report **was late**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Schopenhauer\u2019s final stratagem is **becoming personal**: attacking character, motives or conduct instead of the claim. He was frank about when it appears. It\u2019s the move of someone **losing on the substance**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Why does it land? The personal charge is **often true**, and the room accepts the swap. You may well dislike the project. But that\u2019s irrelevant to whether it\u2019s over budget. The **numbers haven\u2019t changed**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Watch the **timing**, not just the charge. A switch to you usually means **the point was landing**. Say so plainly, then go back to the numbers.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Schopenhauer saved one stratagem for the **very end** of his list. He was frank about the moment people use it. What do you reach for when the argument **is already lost**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The move is **argumentum ad personam**: abandon the question and attack the person. He kept it apart from **ad hominem**, which turns someone\u2019s own words against them. One leaves the subject. The other stays on it.'},{kind:'story',text:'A manager shows the project is over budget, figures on screen. A director replies that she never liked the project anyway. And wasn\u2019t her last report late? Both are true. Neither touches the figures.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a family argument about holiday plans. Your sister is making a good case on cost. Suddenly someone shifts to her tone, and why she always needs control. The cost question is never answered.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some remarks about a person are relevant. A witness\u2019s stake in the outcome bears on their testimony. And timing is **only a tell**: people also get personal because they\u2019re tired or hurt.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The fault is using **the person** in place of the argument. When the subject turns to someone\u2019s character, ask **when it turned**. You\u2019ll often find the point was being lost.'}]},'art-of-being-right_4':{title:'The Question Flood',card:{insight:'A pile of fast questions tests how fast you talk, not if you are right.',tryThis:'When you get many questions at once, answer one well. Say you will come back to the rest.',skills:['communication','thinking']},review:{prompt:'You ask for a new crosswalk. Five sharp questions come at you at once. What now?',right:'Answer one question well and say you will come back to the rest',wrong:'Give a fast answer to each one so you do not look stuck',why:'One good answer beats five rushed ones.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Someone throws **five balls** at you at once. You drop them all. Then they laugh and say you can\u2019t catch. But could **anyone catch** five at the same time?'},{kind:'story',text:'You tell a friend that dogs make better pets than cats. They fire back four questions at once, about walks, cats and proof. You start on the first one. They say you\u2019ve got no answer.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A pile of questions doesn\u2019t mean **you\u2019re wrong**. Asking is quick, but answering takes time. So **pick one**, answer it well, and say you\u2019ll get to the rest.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'At a town meeting you argue for **a cycle lane**. The reply is four quick questions: your definition of safe, your data, deliveries, cost. Each one is fair. Can you **answer them all** in fifteen seconds?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The trick rests on **an asymmetry**. Asking takes a sentence. Answering properly takes a paragraph. Audiences reward brevity, so your pause **reads as doubt**. Schopenhauer saw that most true claims can\u2019t be fully justified in thirty seconds.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'So the demand tests **recall and composure**, not whether **you\u2019re right**.',left:{title:'The questioner',items:['One sentence per question','Five in one breath','Looks sharp']},right:{title:'The answerer',items:['A paragraph per answer','One at a time','Looks evasive']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Name what\u2019s happening. Say the questions are fair, that **answering properly** takes longer, and pick one. Answer it fully, then **come back** for the rest.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Some demands sound like **pure rigour**: define your terms, name your source, explain the mechanism right now. Refusing looks weak. But what is a demand for **an instant answer** really testing?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Schopenhauer describes questions the other side **can\u2019t answer** in the time allowed. Inability to answer on demand is then presented as **inability to answer** at all. In front of an audience, any hesitation reads as doubt.'},{kind:'story',text:'A resident proposes a cycle lane. Within seconds she faces four fair questions about safety, data, deliveries and cost. She hesitates, and the chair moves on. Her case wasn\u2019t weak. It just needed more than fifteen seconds.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a job interview. A panellist asks three linked questions in one breath. The candidate says she\u2019ll take the second first, answers it fully, then returns to the others. Nobody reads that as evasion.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some questions deserve an instant answer. If you can\u2019t name one source for a confident claim, the pause **is evidence**. And asking for time is honest once, but evasive if you never return.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When questions pile up, name the pile. Answering properly takes longer than this moment allows, so **take one** and do it well. Then **keep your promise** and come back.'}]},'art-of-being-right_5':{title:'The Point Next Door',card:{insight:'If they answer a different point, your question is still open.',tryThis:'Before a big meeting, write down your question. When a reply comes, check that it answers it.',skills:['thinking','communication']},review:{prompt:'You ask for a bus route. They prove there is plenty of parking. Settled?',right:'No, ask your question again, since parking was not the point',wrong:'Yes, their numbers were good, so it is over',why:'Parking was not your question, so it still needs an answer.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Ella says the class should get **a longer break**, because everyone\u2019s tired. Tom says breaks don\u2019t make you smarter. The class cheers for Tom. But did Tom **answer Ella** at all?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Ella never said breaks make you smarter. Tom beat **a point next door** to hers, then acted as if he\u2019d won. It\u2019s like a fair: you aim at one coconut, hit **the one beside it**, and cheer.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Hitting **something** isn\u2019t the same as hitting the right thing. When a point gets knocked down, check **it was yours**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A resident says the new estate **needs a bus route**. The developer replies with figures showing **plenty of parking**, thanks everyone, and moves on. The figures are sound. So why do you never hear about the bus route again?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Nobody had questioned the parking. The developer **refuted a neighbour** of the real point, then behaved as if the matter was settled. It works because rooms **track momentum**, not logic. Correcting it means tedious restating.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Schopenhauer describes **several versions**. Each wins something, just **not the thing** in dispute.',left:{title:'What was disputed',items:['Does the rule apply here?','Is the rule true?','Does the policy work?']},right:{title:'What gets proved',items:['A general principle','One case where it held','Its aim is good']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Keep **the original question** written down, or restate it aloud. When a conclusion arrives, compare it with that question. If they don\u2019t match, you can say plainly: **that\u2019s not my claim**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'In a long meeting, someone wins **an exchange** nobody was having. The room relaxes, and the agenda moves on. Ten minutes later, can you still say **what was asked**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Schopenhauer describes **several versions** of this move. Prove a related claim instead of the disputed one. Establish a general principle when its application was the question. Or show **one instance** and claim the rule.'},{kind:'story',text:'A council debates whether a new estate needs a bus route. The developer shows the parking figures are excellent. They are. Everyone moves on, and nobody notices the bus route has left the agenda.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a team arguing about a strict deadline rule. The question is whether it fits this project. Someone argues that deadlines in general help teams. True, perhaps. It\u2019s still not the question anyone asked.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Sometimes the neighbouring point **really does settle** it, because the original claim depended on it. And some speakers answer every rebuttal by saying they meant something else. The test is whether the claim follows.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Substitution happens gradually, so you won\u2019t feel it happen. **Hold the question** still and compare every conclusion against it. A win only counts if it lands on **the claim in dispute**.'}]},'art-of-being-right_6':{title:'Admit When You Are Wrong',card:{insight:'Care more about the truth than the win, and say so when you are wrong.',tryThis:'Next time you find out you are wrong in a fight, say so right away.',skills:['communication','thinking']},review:{prompt:'In a fight, you check the calendar and see you had the date wrong. Now what?',right:'Say so right away and admit the date was wrong',wrong:'Find a new angle fast and keep pressing your point',why:'If you care about the truth, admit you are wrong as soon as you know.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Imagine a book that lists **38 tricks** for winning arguments. Who do you think wrote it? Someone who loved winning? Or someone who wanted you to **spot the tricks**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Arthur Schopenhauer wrote that list. Near the end he gave some plain advice. Don\u2019t argue with people who can never admit they\u2019re wrong. Argue with people who **listen to reasons**, and be ready to change your mind.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Knowing a trick lets you **name it**, and then it loses its power. The best defence is caring more about **the truth** than about winning.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Ten minutes into a disagreement about a deadline, you check the calendar. You **had the date wrong**. You could say so, change the subject, or find a new angle. **Which would you pick**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Two of those three choices are **on Schopenhauer\u2019s list**. Changing the subject and pressing on are both ways of winning without the truth. Only saying so treats the dispute as **a search**, not a contest.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Why learn the list at all? Each trick works by **going unnoticed**. A stretched claim only works if nobody sees the stretch. Name the move aloud, and **most of its force** is gone.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'You can read the book **as a manual** or **as a field guide**.',left:{title:'A manual',items:['Which move wins here?','Use it before they do']},right:{title:'A field guide',items:['Which move was just made?','Name it, then return']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Schopenhauer\u2019s real defence is caring more about **the truth** than winning. So when you see you\u2019re wrong, **admit it** then and there. That\u2019s the honest use of a dishonest book.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Schopenhauer catalogued **thirty-eight ways** to win an argument you\u2019re losing. Then, near the end, he seems **unpersuaded** by his own list. What\u2019s left when the man who wrote the tricks won\u2019t recommend them?'},{kind:'explain',text:'His answer is partly **recognition**. Each stratagem works only while it passes as ordinary argument. The other part is choosing opponents. Argue, he advises, only with people **capable of being wrong**, and decline the contest with the rest.'},{kind:'story',text:'A deadline dispute is ten minutes old when you check the calendar. You had the date wrong. Changing the subject is on his list. So is finding a new angle. Saying so isn\u2019t.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a meeting where a colleague stretches your proposal into something extreme. You don\u2019t answer the extreme version. You point out the stretch, calmly, and restate what you proposed. The room hears the difference.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Schopenhauer wasn\u2019t hopeful: he thought vanity made honest disputing rare. And failing to answer an argument now **doesn\u2019t prove** it\u2019s right. Asking for time to think is honest too.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Read the list as **a field guide**, never a manual. Name the moves when you see them. And when you find you\u2019re the one who\u2019s wrong, **say so plainly**.'}]},'art-of-being-right_7':{title:'The Loaded Name',card:{insight:'A name can win the fight before anyone gives a reason.',tryThis:'When your choice gets an unfair name, say what you did in plain words.',skills:['communication','thinking']},review:{prompt:'Your plan is called timid before anyone talks about it. What do you do first?',right:'Say what the plan does in plain words, then argue for it',wrong:'Argue for a long time that the plan is not timid',why:'A name can win before anyone gives a reason, so use plain words first.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your friend won\u2019t jump off the high wall. One kid calls her **careful**, and another calls her **scared**. She hasn\u2019t moved an inch. So which word will everyone remember tomorrow?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Nothing about her changed. Only **the word** did. Schopenhauer noticed that the name people pick often wins the argument **before it starts**. A kind word and an unkind word can describe exactly the same thing.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When someone gives a thing **a loaded name**, you don\u2019t have to accept it. Say what actually happened in **plain words** first, then argue.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Nobody in the meeting has argued anything yet. But your proposal has already been called **an experiment**, and the other one **a reform**. Can you guess which side is ahead?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Schopenhauer saw how much is settled by **which word** gets attached. The same policy is caution or cowardice. The same person is devout or a zealot. The description does **the judging** before any argument is made.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Why does nobody object? The name **looks like description**, so it passes unchallenged. And names arrive before reasons. Once a room has accepted one, pushing back later **looks like evasion**. That\u2019s why timing matters most.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Notice when **a loaded name** has been accepted. Restate the position in **plain description** before you argue for it. Do it early, while it still sounds natural.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two reports describe the same new policy. One calls it **a reform**. The other calls it **a gamble**. Neither report has offered a single reason yet. So why have you already picked a side?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Several stratagems concern **controlling the vocabulary**. Schopenhauer\u2019s examples belong to his time, but the mechanism doesn\u2019t. Whoever fixes the terms has often **settled the outcome**. The fixing happens early and looks like plain description.'},{kind:'story',text:'A board debates pausing a product launch. Before anyone speaks to the merits, the chair calls the idea timid. Supporters now spend their time insisting they aren\u2019t timid. The launch question barely gets discussed.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a friend describes a mutual acquaintance as devout. Another friend calls the same person a zealot. You haven\u2019t met her. Yet each word has already told you what to think of her.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. There\u2019s no fully neutral vocabulary, since every name selects. Demanding perfect neutrality can become **a stalling tactic**. And some judging names are accurate: calling a fraud a fraud is no trick.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You can\u2019t strip judgement out of words. You can make **the judgement argue** for itself. So swap the loaded name for **a plain description**, early, and then make your case.'}]},'art-of-being-right_8':{title:'The Wrong Box',card:{insight:'A bad name for your plan is not a reason against it.',tryThis:'Next time your idea gets a bad name, skip the name. Say your plan again.',skills:['communication','thinking']},review:{prompt:'You ask to work from home on Fridays. A coworker calls it slacking. What now?',right:'Skip the label and say your plan again',wrong:'Argue for a long time that it is not slacking',why:'If you fight the bad name, nobody talks about your plan.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You suggest the class trip goes to **the science museum**. Someone says that\u2019s just what the teachers want. A few kids groan. Has **anyone said** a single thing about the museum?'},{kind:'explain',text:'That\u2019s a trick. Someone puts your idea in **a box** that people already dislike. Now you feel you must shout *I\u2019m not!* But saying you\u2019re not something usually **sounds a bit defensive**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t argue about **the box**. Just say **your idea again**, plainly: the museum, and the reasons you think it\u2019s good.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You propose letting staff **choose their own hours**. The reply comes quickly: that\u2019s exactly what the old management tried. People wince. Has anyone **discussed your proposal** at all?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Schopenhauer describes this move: place the other side\u2019s view in **a detested category**. It needs only **a plausible resemblance**, not a real one. Then the burden falls on you to escape a position you never held.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Why not just deny it? Because **denial is weak**. Anyone protesting they\u2019re not the thing they\u2019ve just been called sounds defensive, **true or not**. And every minute spent denying is a minute not spent arguing.'},{kind:'moral',text:'**Refuse the frame**, not the label. Restate your specific proposal, without mentioning the category at all. Arguing about the box **concedes the box** is the question.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A shortcut many debaters love: don\u2019t refute a view, just **name its family**. That\u2019s just what the other lot say. Why does this so often work better than **a real counter-argument**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Schopenhauer\u2019s point is that the claim itself stays untouched. **The association** does the work. A **plausible resemblance** is enough, and then the opponent must distinguish themselves from a position they never held.'},{kind:'story',text:'A manager proposes flexible hours. A colleague says it\u2019s exactly what the old management tried. She spends five minutes explaining how it\u2019s different. The meeting ends, and nobody has asked whether flexible hours would work.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a neighbour suggests a shared tool shed for the street. Someone says that\u2019s just a commune. She doesn\u2019t argue about communes. She restates the plan: one shed, a sign-up sheet, a summer trial. The talk returns to the shed.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Sometimes the resemblance is real and relevant. If your proposal repeats the mechanism of a scheme that failed, saying so is **evidence, not smear**. Refusing every comparison can shield you from history.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Ask whether a **shared mechanism** has been shown, or just a family name. If it\u2019s only the name, **decline the file** and restate what you actually claimed.'}]},'art-of-being-right_9':{title:'The Sudden Fog',card:{insight:'Big words from a sure voice may hide that nothing was said.',tryThis:'Next time you cannot follow someone, say so. Ask them to say it again in short words.',skills:['communication','thinking']},review:{prompt:'You ask why the bill went up. You get three minutes of big words. Now what?',right:'Ask for the reason again in plain, short words',wrong:'Nod along and decide you must be too slow to follow',why:'A real reason still makes sense in short, plain words.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'An older kid explains why his team is **the best**. He uses lots of long words, and he sounds very sure. You don\u2019t follow any of it. So **whose fault** is that?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Most people blame themselves. They think, *I must be slow.* But some people switch to **foggy words** when they\u2019re losing an argument. Big words can hide the fact that **nothing was said**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'If you can\u2019t follow, **say so** and ask again in **shorter words**. A real idea can survive being said simply.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You ask why the forecast **missed by a third**. The answer is three minutes on structural headwinds, strategic realignment and second-order effects. Everyone nods. Afterwards, can **anyone say** what the reason was?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Schopenhauer lists this as a stratagem: when losing, become **unintelligible and confident**. An audience will often assume it failed to understand, rather than that **nothing was said**. A speaker\u2019s standing makes that assumption stronger.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Watch **the timing**. The profundity tends to arrive just after a point went unanswered, the same moment as the personal attack. A speaker who was clear while winning and **foggy when losing** has told you something.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Ask for **the claim** in plain, shorter words, and keep asking. A real reason survives being restated simply. **Fog doesn\u2019t**, which is why it\u2019s a last resort.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Schopenhauer spent decades attacking the most celebrated German philosopher of his day. His charge was that the man **passed off fog** as depth. Why would obscurity get under **his skin** so badly?'},{kind:'explain',text:'One stratagem is to become **unintelligible** when losing: abstract, elaborate, impossible to assess. Listeners tend to assume they\u2019ve failed to understand. The tell is timing. The fog arrives **just when** the argument was going badly.'},{kind:'story',text:'The philosopher he attacked was Hegel. Schopenhauer accused him of dressing up unintelligible writing as profound. Whatever you make of the feud, he\u2019d clearly seen how readily a crowd mistakes difficulty for depth.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a quarterly review. A manager asks why sales missed the forecast. The reply is three minutes of headwinds and realignment. One person asks for the reason in a single sentence. There isn\u2019t one.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some true things are hard, and can\u2019t be made simple without becoming false. The fair demand is for **the claim**, not the whole argument in a sentence. And a poor explainer isn\u2019t running a trick.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When you can\u2019t follow, the cost of asking is **smaller than it feels**. Ask for the claim in ordinary words. If none arrives, **you weren\u2019t slow**.'}]}}}},944,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'as-a-man-thinketh',kind:'book',promise:'Work on the part of your life you control',about:'Do your thoughts shape your whole life? In 1903, James Allen said they do. He was right about habits, but wrong about luck and sickness. These ideas keep the good parts. Which part will you try first?',ideas:{'as-a-man-thinketh_1':{title:'Two Lists',card:{insight:'Some of your life comes from your habits, but not all of it.',tryThis:'Write two lists, one for bad luck and one for your habits. Then change one habit.',skills:['thinking','psychology']},review:{prompt:'A friend says your money trouble is all in your head. What do you say?',right:'Some of it comes from habits, and some is bad luck',wrong:'Agree, since good thoughts will bring the money in',why:'Habits shape part of your life, but bad luck is real too.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You keep being **late for school**. A friend lends you an old book. It says your whole life comes from **the thoughts** you keep having. Could that be true?'},{kind:'explain',text:'James Allen wrote that book in England in 1903. Some of your lateness might be **a habit**, like staying up on your tablet. That part you **can change**. But if the bus breaks down, that isn\u2019t your thinking.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Some of your life **grows from habits**. Some of it just happens to you. Work on **your part**, and don\u2019t blame yourself for the rest.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A friend lends you a slim old book: change your thinking and **your life follows**. You like it. Then you remember your neighbour, laid off when **the factory shut**. Was she just thinking wrong?'},{kind:'story',text:'Your neighbour, Dana, was as hopeful and hard-working as anyone you know. The factory closed anyway. Her mood didn\u2019t shut it. But her habit of saving a little each month is why she could pay the rent while she looked.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'James Allen\u2019s 1903 book can be read **two ways**, and only one of them **holds up**.',left:{title:'The strong claim',items:['Thought causes every circumstance','Can\u2019t be tested','Makes suffering deserved']},right:{title:'The narrow claim',items:['Habit shapes part of life','Can be checked','Leaves room for luck']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Allen\u2019s strong claim is false, and it\u2019s cruel to the unlucky. The **narrow reading** is useful. Use it on **your own habits**, looking forward, never as a verdict on others.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'In 1903 an English writer named James Allen published **a short book**. Its title borrows a line from the Book of Proverbs. Its argument is bold: your circumstances grow out of **your thinking**. Is it right?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Read literally, Allen says thought **causes circumstance**. That\u2019s false. Read narrowly, he describes the share of a life that follows from **settled habits**. Think of how you spend evenings or handle money. That share is real.'},{kind:'story',text:'Sam saves a little every month. Years later he has savings, and his habit explains most of it. That\u2019s the narrow claim at work, and it fits ordinary experience. People who save do tend to have savings.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Sam\u2019s sister, who saves just as carefully. A flood takes her home. The strong claim says her thinking caused it. It borrows the first story\u2019s plausibility and stretches it over something no habit touches.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Even the narrow claim needs care. Your habits were shaped by upbringing, money and health, so they aren\u2019t **entirely your doing**. Turned on other people, the idea slides back towards blame.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Keep the **narrow claim** and drop the literal one. Use it on yourself, looking forward. Never use it as **a verdict** on someone else\u2019s bad luck.'}]},'as-a-man-thinketh_2':{title:'The Mind as a Garden',card:{insight:'Habits you do not choose will grow back, like weeds in a garden.',tryThis:'Pick one night each week to check your habits. Pull out one that crept back.',skills:['focus','habits']},review:{prompt:'In January, you kept your phone out of bed. By March, it is back. Why?',right:'No one kept watch, so the old habits grew back',wrong:'Your January plan was too weak, so make it stricter',why:'Habits grow back on their own, so check on them each week.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You plant **a few seeds** in a corner of the garden, then forget about it all summer. In September you go back to look. What do you think **you\u2019ll find** there?'},{kind:'explain',text:'You\u2019ll find **weeds**, lots of them. Nobody planted them. They just blew in. James Allen said your mind works **like a garden**. If you don\u2019t plant good habits, other habits grow there anyway.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your mind never stays empty. Something always grows in it. So **look after it** a little every week, not **just once**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'In January you **sort out your attention**: notifications off, phone out of the bedroom, reading every night. It\u2019s now March. The phone is back **on the pillow**. When did that happen?'},{kind:'story',text:'Nobody chose the slide back. One evening a message arrived and the phone came along to bed. A week later it was a habit. Each step was tiny. Together they undid January.'},{kind:'explain',text:'James Allen\u2019s image explains it. The mind is **a garden**. Leave a bed unplanted and weeds arrive on their own. Habits of thought form **whether or not** you choose them. Neglect hands the plot to whatever\u2019s nearest.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Most resolutions treat attention as **a switch**. Allen treats it as **a garden**.',left:{title:'A switch',items:['Decide once','Expect it to stay','Relapse feels like failure']},right:{title:'A garden',items:['Tend it weekly','Expect weeds back','Relapse is just weather']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Attention isn\u2019t set once. It\u2019s **tended**, or it **grows back** into whatever\u2019s nearest. So plan for the weeds and pull them every week.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A gardener takes a month off. When she comes back, the beds **aren\u2019t empty**. They\u2019re full of things she never planted. James Allen thought **your mind** works the same way. Does it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Allen\u2019s central image is the mind as a garden. You can **tend it** on purpose or **neglect it**. Either way, something comes up. Sow nothing worth having and weeds take the space. The ground is never simply empty.'},{kind:'story',text:'Priya cuts her evening scrolling in January. By March it\u2019s back, an hour a night. She calls it weakness. The garden image calls it weather: the old pattern returning, as anyone tending a plot would expect.'},{kind:'story',text:'Or take a manager who worries most about whatever came up last. Nobody chose that habit. It grew from what was nearest: the latest email, the loudest complaint. His attention went to the default.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The image assumes you control your mind as fully as a gardener controls a plot. You don\u2019t. Sleep, illness and mood **seed the ground** too. There the metaphor does work that evidence should do.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your attention is **always growing something**. Tending it is continuous work, not a single decision. When the old habits return, **pull them again** without calling it failure.'}]},'as-a-man-thinketh_3':{title:'What Hard Days Show',card:{insight:'Hard days show the habits you have practiced, and you can change them.',tryThis:'On a calm day, practice one slow breath before you answer. Then use it on a hard day.',skills:['psychology','habits']},review:{prompt:'In a hard week, you snapped at your roommate. How should you see it?',right:'As a sign of my habits, which I can change',wrong:'As a one-time thing that was not like me',why:'Hard days show your habits, and habits can change.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two kids **drop their lunch** trays on the same day. One laughs and helps clean up. The other shouts at everyone nearby. Same accident. So **what made** the difference?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The accident didn\u2019t make them different. It **showed** what each one usually does. James Allen said hard moments **reveal** you, a bit like a test. And the good news is you can practise a better reaction.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A bad day shows **your habits**. You can\u2019t always stop the bad day. But you **can practise** how you meet the next one.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A deadline moves forward and a parent goes into hospital **the same week**. You\u2019re curt with a colleague and short with your partner. Since then you\u2019ve called it *not really me*. **Is that right**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'James Allen argued that hard times mostly **reveal** a person, rather than shape them. Pressure shows what you **habitually are**. Your reaction that week is a reading of what you\u2019ve practised, not an exception to it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Two people are laid off on the same day. One spends a month in bed. The other rings everyone she knows. The setback was identical. The difference tells you about them, and it\u2019s the part that can change.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Hard weeks aren\u2019t exceptions to who you are. They\u2019re **a reading** of your habits. Use that reading to **practise** a better response next time.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture a press **squeezing a block**. What oozes out at the sides isn\u2019t new. It\u2019s what the block was made of all along. Could **a hard week** work on you like that?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Allen argues that hard circumstances **reveal** a person more than they form one. Read narrowly, that\u2019s defensible. How you respond under strain is **a readout** of what you\u2019ve practised. And the response is more open to change than the setback.'},{kind:'story',text:'Two colleagues lose the same big client in the same week. One goes quiet and blames the market. The other calls the client to ask what went wrong. Same blow. Their responses show two different habits.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take your own last hard stretch. Perhaps you snapped, went silent or stopped exercising. You\u2019ve filed it under *not really me*. Allen would say that\u2019s exactly where your real habits showed.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Allen slides into the strong claim, that circumstance is mostly a test of character. Poverty, illness and injustice **plainly impose**. And a heavy enough load tells you about the load, not just the person.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Pressure is **a reading** of your practice, and practice can change. Use it on **your own** hard weeks. Never use it to grade someone else\u2019s suffering.'}]},'as-a-man-thinketh_4':{title:'The Drifting Boat',card:{insight:'With no aim, small worries fill up your free time.',tryThis:'Write one small aim for the next three months, like baking bread. Stick it on your fridge.',skills:['focus','thinking']},review:{prompt:'A friend quits her job and keeps her days open. What happens?',right:'Small worries and errands fill up her empty days',wrong:'The right chance finds her while her days are open',why:'With no aim, the nearest small worries take over her days.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'It\u2019s the first day of the **summer holidays**. You\u2019ve got no plans at all, and that sounds brilliant. Six weeks later, school starts again. **What did you** actually do with them?'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a little boat with nobody rowing. It doesn\u2019t stay still. The water pushes it wherever the current goes. Your holiday was like that boat: whatever was nearest, a screen, a small worry, filled each day.'},{kind:'moral',text:'James Allen said that with **no aim**, you drift. Your aim can be **small**, like learning one song. Just pick something, so the current doesn\u2019t pick for you.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve **no plan** for the next six months, and you call it keeping your options open. Meanwhile your weeks are filling with email, **small worries** and whatever anyone asks. Who\u2019s steering?'},{kind:'explain',text:'James Allen is blunt here. Without **a central aim**, he argues, small fears and worries end up running you. His point is about capacity. Attention will attach to something. Small anxieties are **always nearest**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Allen adds that the aim **needn\u2019t be grand**. Even **a small one** changes things.',left:{title:'No aim',items:['Space fills with trivia','Worries set the agenda','Six months vanish']},right:{title:'One small aim',items:['The space has a use','You set the agenda','Six months add up']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Having no aim isn\u2019t a neutral wait. It\u2019s **a decision**, usually unmade, to let **small worries** steer. Name one aim, however modest, and take the oar.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two friends leave their jobs **in the same month**. Neither has a new job lined up. One calls it rest, with an end date. The other calls it staying open. A year on, **which one drifted**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'In his chapter on purpose, Allen is at his bluntest. Without **a central aim**, he says, you drift into whatever currents are nearby. Attention attaches to something. If nothing is chosen, it settles on **the nearest worry**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Leah, the one staying open, means to think about her next step. But each day fills with errands, news and small worries about money. After a year she still hasn\u2019t thought about it. The current chose for her.'},{kind:'story',text:'Tom, the one resting, set a small aim: three months off, then two applications a week. It wasn\u2019t grand. But it gave his attention somewhere to go, and the worries had less room.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some open stretches are chosen and useful: recovering, exploring, waiting for news. The test is whether **you decided it**, with an end. And an aim held too rigidly can blind you to better ones.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your attention will always **attach to something**. With no aim set, the nearest worries take it. A **small, defined aim** is enough to change that.'}]},'as-a-man-thinketh_5':{title:'The Short Old Book',card:{insight:'Many new books on mindset repeat what this short old book said.',tryThis:'Before you pay for a mindset course, read the short old book with the same claim.',skills:['thinking','history']},review:{prompt:'A video says your thoughts will bring you money. How does Allen\u2019s book help?',right:'His short old book shows the plain claim under it',wrong:'It proves the claim, since the idea is so old',why:'The first version shows the claim plainly, so you can judge it.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your favourite song gets **a new version**. It\u2019s louder, with extra bits added. Then you hear **the original**, and it\u2019s small and plain. Which one shows you what the song really is?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Lots of books today say **your thoughts** decide your life. Many of them grew from one short book, written by James Allen in 1903. You can read it **in an hour**. Then you\u2019ll see the idea before the extras.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When an idea has lots of versions, **find the first one**. It\u2019s usually shorter and plainer. That makes it **easier to judge** for yourself.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A podcast tells you **your thoughts** attract money. A bestseller says belief builds your future. A coach says mindset is everything. They sound new. Have you ever **traced them back**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Much modern self-help is James Allen\u2019s 1903 book with **the theology removed** and the promises enlarged. His claim was that thought shapes circumstance. The later versions keep that claim and add **bigger rewards**: money, health, success.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The original is short enough to read **in an hour**. So you see the whole argument, including the claim everything rests on. Met in a hundred later versions, each hedged differently, that claim is **hard to spot**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The source is **the cheap route**. An hour with Allen shows you the claim **without the gloss**. Then you\u2019re free to decide what you think of it.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A friend swears by a book about **thinking your way** to success. You notice its central claim sounds familiar. Where have you heard it before? And how far back does it **really go**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Allen\u2019s book has never been out of print since 1903. It\u2019s the clearest early statement that **thought determines circumstance**. That idea runs through a century of self-improvement writing and reaches today **almost intact**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Your friend\u2019s book adds disclaimers: results may vary, effort required. Each one makes the real claim harder to see. You can\u2019t tell what\u2019s promised and what\u2019s hedged. The claim itself sits underneath, unexamined.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now you read Allen\u2019s original in an evening. Nothing is hedged. The foundation is **in plain view**: thought causes circumstance. So is its lack of support. You can finally judge the idea directly.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. An origin isn\u2019t a refutation. Showing an idea is old, or was sold hard, doesn\u2019t show **it\u2019s wrong**. Later work tests some narrow claims about habit. Nor is the oldest version the purest. Allen\u2019s own title borrows from Proverbs.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Reading the source shows **the load-bearing claim** with nothing in the way. It doesn\u2019t settle the question for you. It just lets you **judge it directly**.'}]},'as-a-man-thinketh_6':{title:'Where Allen Was Wrong',card:{insight:'Bad thoughts do not make people sick, so do not blame the sick.',tryThis:'Next time a post says thoughts cause sickness, ask who it blames.',skills:['thinking','health']},review:{prompt:'A post says sickness comes from anger you hold inside. What is wrong?',right:'It blames sick people for being sick',wrong:'Nothing, as long as you keep your thoughts happy',why:'Germs and genes make people sick, so the post blames the wrong thing.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your friend\u2019s **grandad gets ill**. Someone gives him a book that says sad thoughts make people sick. Now he lies awake wondering **what he did wrong**. Did he do anything wrong?'},{kind:'explain',text:'No, he didn\u2019t. James Allen wrote that **bad thinking** causes illness. That isn\u2019t true. Germs, genes and accidents make people ill. Blaming someone\u2019s thoughts is **unfair**, and it stops you looking for the real cause.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Being ill **is never a punishment** for your thoughts. If a book says it is, the book is wrong. Be **kind**, and look for the real cause.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A friend with **a serious diagnosis** gets a parcel from a kind relative. It\u2019s a book: change your thoughts, heal your body. She reads it late at night. Then she starts wondering **what she did wrong**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Allen takes his claim from circumstance **to the body**. Disordered thinking, he says, produces disease. Here a book of useful metaphors turns harmful. It makes illness **a verdict** on the person who is ill.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Look at what the doctrine removes. If illness comes from thinking, there\u2019s no reason to look at **infection, genes** or poverty. Compassion and diagnosis vanish in one move. Your friend is left to blame **herself**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Allen\u2019s useful points about attention **don\u2019t need** this doctrine. When mindset talk reaches illness, ask what it implies about **the person who\u2019s ill**. That question stops the harm.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A cheerful woman **falls ill**. A believer in positive thinking says she had hidden negativity. Another patient recovers, and that **proves the method**. What evidence could ever count against this idea? Can you think of any?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Having argued that circumstance grows from thought, Allen applies **the same rule** to health. Disease, he says, is rooted in thought. On this account the sick, the poor and the harmed **thought their way** there.'},{kind:'story',text:'Back to the cheerful woman. When she falls ill, the doctrine says her thinking was secretly wrong. When the treatment fails, she hadn\u2019t got it right yet. Every outcome fits. Nothing can count against it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a man told his illness is stored resentment. He spends his energy policing his thoughts. Nobody asks about his job, his genes or his air. The real causes go unexamined, and his care can suffer.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **the strongest objection**. Mind and body **do interact**: stress and mood affect symptoms and recovery. But that\u2019s a partial, two-way influence among many causes. Allen claims one-way cause, with no exceptions.'},{kind:'moral',text:'An idea that **can\u2019t lose** should make you suspicious. Allen\u2019s points about attention and purpose don\u2019t need this doctrine. Keep those, and **refuse the blame**.'}]},'as-a-man-thinketh_7':{title:'One Goal for Years',card:{insight:'One goal kept for years shapes your choices, so stop starting over.',tryThis:'Write your one goal on a card. Tape it above your desk where you will see it.',skills:['focus','habits']},review:{prompt:'You switch side projects every few months. What would help most?',right:'Keep one goal in view for years, and stop starting over',wrong:'Find a more fun project that keeps you going',why:'One goal helps you choose, and each new start throws work away.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two kids start **learning guitar** on the same day. One switches to drums, then piano, then back again. The other sticks with guitar **for five years**. Who do you think plays best?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The one who **kept going**. Each new start throws away what you\u2019ve learned. James Allen said to hold a picture of **what you want** to build. Don\u2019t give it up at the first hard bit.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Pick **one picture** and keep it in view. Little by little, it shapes **what you practise**. Changing your mind every month means starting over every month.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two people start side projects with **the same hours** a week. One picks a new idea every few months. The other keeps one for five years. Five years on, **who\u2019s further along**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Ana kept restarting. A podcast in spring, a shop in summer, an app by winter. Each switch felt sensible at the time. After five years she had five beginnings and nothing that had grown.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Allen\u2019s chapter on dreamers says to **hold the picture** steadily. There\u2019s no magic needed. A stable aim gives every decision something to be measured against. And restarting throws away **the compounding** that makes long efforts pay.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Hold **one picture** of what you\u2019re building, long enough to work on it. Over years, not weeks, it shapes **what you notice** and practise.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A drawing is **pinned to the board** and left there for years. Its edges go soft from being looked at. Allen thought a picture held like that could **change a life**. How, exactly?'},{kind:'explain',text:'In his chapter on dreamers, Allen tells you to **hold a vision** and not drop it at the first difficulty. He states it mystically, as if the picture itself brings the thing about. **Underneath** sits a plainer claim.'},{kind:'story',text:'Ben wanted to write a novel. Each January he started a new one with a better idea. Each choice was sensible on its own. Ten years on, he had ten opening chapters and no book.'},{kind:'story',text:'His friend Mei pinned one sentence above her desk: *a book about my grandmother\u2019s village*. Some years she wrote little. But every choice, what to read, whom to ask, got measured against that one line.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Persistence isn\u2019t always a virtue. Some aims deserve dropping, because the evidence says they won\u2019t work or you\u2019ve changed. The idea guards against quitting **out of boredom**, not against revising for good reasons.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The mystical version says the picture brings the thing about. The **weaker version** is true and enough: a steady aim directs **your attention** and practice for years.'}]},'as-a-man-thinketh_8':{title:'Calm You Can See',card:{insight:'Calm is built by long practice, and people bring their problems to calm people.',tryThis:'Next time things go wrong, take one slow breath before you speak. See who comes to you.',skills:['leadership','psychology']},review:{prompt:'A new boss wants her team to bring problems early. What should she do?',right:'Stay calm when things go wrong, so people bring her problems',wrong:'Make a firm rule that the team must speak up early',why:'People bring problems to someone who stays calm when things go wrong.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Something breaks in class and **everyone panics**. One kid shouts, another cries. A third just quietly says, let\u2019s sort it out. Next time something goes wrong, **who would you** go to?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Most people go to **the calm one**. Calm shows on the outside, so others can see it. James Allen said calm isn\u2019t something you\u2019re born with. You build it by **practising self-control**, again and again.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Staying calm is **a skill** you can practise. People can see it, and they **trust** it. So next time things go wrong, take a breath before you speak.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'The system goes down **late on a Friday**. Watch where people take the problem. It\u2019s rarely to the most senior person in the room. So **who do they** go to?'},{kind:'story',text:'They go to Jo, who didn\u2019t raise her voice last time. When the payroll failed in spring, she listened, asked two questions and made a plan. People remembered. Now problems arrive at her desk first.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Allen\u2019s last chapter treats calm as **a competence**, built by long self-control. It\u2019s visible, and it\u2019s hard to fake for long. So your composure works as **evidence** of how you\u2019ll behave in the next difficulty.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Calm isn\u2019t just a private feeling. Other people **can see it**, and they bring their problems to whoever has it. You can **build it**, one difficulty at a time.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Think of the person **you take problems** to at work. They may not be the cleverest or the most senior. What have you watched them do that **earned it**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Allen ends the book on calm, and it\u2019s **his best chapter**. He treats serenity as the fruit of long, patient self-control. And he notes that people **turn to** the calm person, sensing they can rely on them.'},{kind:'story',text:'A hospital ward has a nurse who never hurries her voice. When an alarm goes, junior staff look to her first. She isn\u2019t the senior one. But years of steady nights have made her the signal they read.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a founder who stays calm in one crisis, then shouts through the next three. Investors remember the shouting. Calm shown once tells them little. Calm shown over years is hard to fake.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Calm can be mistaken for competence: a composed person can be **composed and wrong**. And suppressed distress isn\u2019t serenity. Someone who never shows strain may simply not be asking for help.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Calm is **evidence** others can read about how you\u2019ll handle the next difficulty. It\u2019s built, not born. Earn it over years, and **trust gathers** around you.'}]},'as-a-man-thinketh_9':{title:'Start With Today\u2019s Task',card:{insight:'With no big purpose yet, do the task in front of you well.',tryThis:'Pick one small task due today. Do it better than it needs, and finish it.',skills:['career','productivity']},review:{prompt:'A new grad in a dull first job cannot name a purpose. What helps?',right:'Do the small task in front of her as well as she can',wrong:'Keep looking until the one true purpose shows up',why:'The task in front of you is always there, so you can always start.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Grown-ups keep asking **what you want** to be. You honestly don\u2019t know. Your friends all seem to have answers. Does not knowing mean you **can\u2019t start** anything yet?'},{kind:'explain',text:'James Allen had advice for exactly this. If you don\u2019t have **a big aim**, do the job in front of you **really well**. Tidy your room properly. Finish your homework neatly. Bigger aims can grow from there.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You don\u2019t need **a big purpose** to begin. Do today\u2019s small job well. That\u2019s always **somewhere to start**, even when you feel lost.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve read **three books** on finding your purpose. You still can\u2019t name yours. Now, on top of not knowing, you feel bad about not knowing. **Where do you** go from here?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Allen anticipates you. In his chapter on purpose, he gives one plain instruction. With no great aim in view, do **the duty in front** of you perfectly, however small. Capacity shows **at whatever scale** is available.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Why does this work? Purpose advice often **freezes** people: nothing seems worth starting until the purpose is found. This asks for **nothing you lack**. The task in front of you is always there.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Kemi, a graduate in a dull first job. She stops signing up for courses to find herself. Instead she makes her weekly reports the clearest in the office. Within a year, she\u2019s asked to run the team\u2019s reporting.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You don\u2019t need to find **your purpose** first. Do the duty in front of you well, **however small**. Larger aims tend to form around good work.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Allen has told his reader that thought shapes circumstance, and that **a central purpose** is essential. Now picture a reader who can\u2019t find one. What can he possibly **offer them**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'His answer is **the least mystical** thing in the book. Where no great purpose can be found, do the immediate duty perfectly, however small. Capacity shows at **whatever scale** is available, and that scale tends to grow.'},{kind:'story',text:'A junior chef can\u2019t say what kind of cook she wants to be. So she makes the best stock in the kitchen, every morning. The head chef notices. Soon she\u2019s trusted with the sauces.'},{kind:'story',text:'Or take Raj, stuck on the purpose question for a year. He decides to stop asking for a month. Instead he does this week\u2019s ordinary work unusually well. For the first time in ages, he has somewhere to stand.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The scale **doesn\u2019t always grow**. Good work often goes unrewarded, especially where it\u2019s unseen. A careful worker can be kept small because they\u2019re useful where they are. This is a way out of paralysis, not a promise.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You don\u2019t need a grand purpose to begin. Do the duty in front of you, and **do it well**. Just **review now and then**, because diligence isn\u2019t a direction.'}]}}}},945,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'base-rates',kind:'book',promise:'Start each guess with how often it happens',about:'When you guess what will happen, do you trust the story or the count? Most of us go with the story. These ideas show why the plain count wins more often. Want to see how?',ideas:{'base-rates_1':{title:'Librarian or Farmer',card:{insight:'Before you guess a person\u2019s job, ask which job is more common.',tryThis:'Next time you guess a stranger\u2019s job, first ask which job more people have.',skills:['thinking','psychology']},review:{prompt:'A shy, neat man could be a librarian or a farmer. What do you check first?',right:'Which job more people have in general',wrong:'How well he fits your idea of a librarian',why:'Start with how common each job is, and let the clues nudge it.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A jar holds **90 red sweets** and 10 blue ones, all the same shape. You grab one in the dark. You\u2019ve got **a hunch** it\u2019s blue. Which colour is it most likely to be?'},{kind:'explain',text:'It\u2019s still most likely red, because **9 out of 10** are red. A hunch doesn\u2019t change the count. People forget this with stories too. A good description of one person can make you **forget the count**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Before you guess about one case, ask **how common** each answer is. Start with **the count**. Then let the clues nudge it, not replace it.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Someone is described to you as **quiet, tidy** and fond of puzzles. Is that person more likely to be a librarian or a farmer? Most people answer **without hesitating**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Most say librarian, because **the sketch fits**. But farmers outnumber librarians many times over, and plenty of farmers are quiet and tidy too. The count should win. Instead, **the description** beats it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a candidate who **interviews brilliantly**: fluent, warm, sharp. You leave sure you\u2019ve found the one. But brilliant interviews predict job performance **only weakly**. Most of what impressed you says little about the job.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Vivid detail **feels like information**, but often it\u2019s only atmosphere. So ask the boring question first: how common is this, **in general**? Then let the details adjust it.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A doctor sees a symptom that **matches a rare disease** perfectly. Every detail fits the textbook. It feels like the case is solved. Why might the honest answer still be **the common illness**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Judging by resemblance quietly swaps your question. You meant to ask how likely something is. You end up asking **how typical** it is. A vivid sketch answers that, says little about likelihood, and still **overrides the count**.'},{kind:'story',text:'The classic test describes someone quiet, tidy and fond of puzzles. Librarian or farmer? Most people say librarian. The sketch does fit librarians better. But farmers outnumber them many times over, and **the fit is weak** evidence.'},{kind:'story',text:'Back to the doctor. The symptom fits the rare disease, but the common illness causes it far more often. So the match is real, and the likeliest answer is **still the common one**. The count should lead.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The base rate is a starting point, not a verdict. Some details are **strongly telling** and should move you a long way. The mistake is ignoring the count, not using the case.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Details tell you **how typical** a case looks. The count tells you **how likely** it is. Ask how common it is first, then let the story adjust your answer.'}]},'base-rates_2':{title:'The Outside View',card:{insight:'Guess how long a job will take by asking how long it took others.',tryThis:'Before you set your next due date, ask friends how long jobs like it took.',skills:['thinking','productivity']},review:{prompt:'Your team is planning a new project. What should your time guess start from?',right:'How long jobs like it took others, then adjust',wrong:'A list of each step in your own plan',why:'Start from how other teams did, then adjust for your own plan.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your group is making **a model volcano** for the science fair. You all agree it\u2019ll take one afternoon. Every step is planned, and it sounds easy. How many afternoons do you think it **really takes**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'When you plan, you picture **each step** going right. You don\u2019t picture the glue that won\u2019t dry, or the friend who\u2019s off sick. Other groups had problems like that too. **Their times** already include them.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Before you plan your own thing, ask **how long** things like it **usually take**. Start from that number. Then think about what makes yours different.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your team plans a new project in detail. Every step has a date. The plan says **six months**, and it looks solid. Before you believe it, what question **hasn\u2019t anyone asked**?'},{kind:'story',text:'The psychologist Daniel Kahneman once led a team writing a textbook. They estimated **about two years**. It took eight. One member had seen similar projects and knew the typical figure. It played no part in the estimate.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Start from what **usually happens** to projects like yours. That number is the anchor. Your specifics should move you from it, not replace it. Detail feels like information, but it mostly works as **a story**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The same project, estimated from **the inside** or from **the outside**, gives different numbers.',left:{title:'Inside view',items:['Starts from your plan','Every step going right','Feels unique']},right:{title:'Outside view',items:['Starts from similar projects','Includes their usual delays','Then adjusts for yours']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Get the numbers for similar cases **before any planning**. Once a plan exists, the base rate sounds like **pessimism** about your plan, and it gets argued away.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Kahneman\u2019s team was writing a textbook. One member had seen many projects like it and, when asked, knew **the typical figure**. Yet the estimate was built **without it**. Why would good knowledge sit unused?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The **outside view** means starting from what usually happens to cases like this one. The specifics of your case are what produce **overconfidence**. They make your project feel unlike the class it belongs to.'},{kind:'story',text:'The team estimated about two years. The book took eight. The knowledge existed inside the room, but the estimate simply wasn\u2019t built from it. **The richer** the plan, the further it drifted from what usually happens.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture yourself planning a website launch. You list every step and allow six weeks. Before trusting that, you check how long your last three launches took. That figure goes down first. Your plan adjusts it.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The outside view needs a class that **genuinely fits**, and choosing one can go wrong. It also says what typically happens, not what must. You can beat the base rate, but argue for it rather than assume it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Establish **the anchor** before you plan, while it still reads as information. Then treat your specifics as **adjustments** to it. Most people never set the anchor at all.'}]},'base-rates_3':{title:'When Good Tests Are Wrong',card:{insight:'When a thing is rare, a good test is often wrong when it says yes.',tryThis:'Next time a test warns you about a rare thing, ask how rare it is. Then check again.',skills:['thinking','science']},review:{prompt:'A good test says you have a rare illness. What do you do?',right:'Treat it as a reason to test again, not an answer',wrong:'Trust it, since the test is right nearly every time',why:'Most people are healthy, so most of the alarms are false.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A game has **a cheat catcher**. Real cheats are rare: 1 player in 1,000. The catcher always spots a real cheat. But it wrongly flags **5 in every 100** honest players. You get flagged. Are you a cheat?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Picture 1,000 players. One is a real cheat, and gets flagged. Of the 999 honest ones, about **50 get flagged** too. So for every real cheat caught, around fifty honest players are wrongly called cheats. That\u2019s **the crowd problem**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When something is **very rare**, even a good test is usually wrong when it says yes. So don\u2019t treat one flag as the answer. **Check again** in a different way.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A screening test catches **every real case** of a condition. It wrongly flags just 5% of healthy people. The condition affects 1 in 1,000. Your result is positive. How worried should you **really be**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Set it out as counts. Take 1,000 people. One has the condition and **tests positive**. Of the 999 without it, about 50 test positive anyway. So a positive means it\u2019s real **roughly 2%** of the time.'},{kind:'explain',text:'People reliably get this wrong, **trained clinicians** included. Attention goes to the test\u2019s accuracy, not to how rare the condition is. But the small error rate is applied to **a vastly larger** pool of healthy people.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A positive result on something rare is a reason to **test again**, not a conclusion. Ask how rare it is, and how often the test raises **false alarms**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A bank\u2019s fraud check is **very accurate**. It flags a few hundred accounts every week. The team investigates each one carefully. Months later, almost all of them turned out fine. Was the check **broken**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'What a positive means depends on **how common** the thing is, as much as on accuracy. Screen for something rare and the false positives, drawn from **the huge unaffected pool**, swamp the true ones.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take 1,000 people and a condition affecting one of them. The test catches that one. Of the other 999, 5% test positive anyway: about 50. So a positive comes from a healthy person **fifty times** for every real case.'},{kind:'story',text:'The bank\u2019s check works the same way. Real fraud is rare, and honest accounts vastly outnumber it. Even a tiny error rate on that crowd makes **far more false alarms** than hits. The check wasn\u2019t broken.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. That arithmetic applies to someone drawn from the general population. If you\u2019re tested because of symptoms, you belong to **a different group**, and a positive means more. A retest helps only if it can fail differently.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Accuracy alone can\u2019t tell you what a positive means. **Prevalence** matters just as much. Screen for anything rare, from fraud to hiring, and expect **mostly false alarms**.'}]},'base-rates_4':{title:'The Good Contractor',card:{insight:'What happens to most plans can happen to yours, too.',tryThis:'Pick one plan of yours. Write how often plans like it go wrong, then one reason yours won\u2019t.',skills:['thinking','psychology']},review:{prompt:'Your friends\u2019 kitchen jobs all went over budget. You have a great contractor. What now?',right:'Plan for yours to go over too, unless you have a real reason',wrong:'Yours will not go over, since you hired a good contractor',why:'You know each step of your plan, but you only saw how theirs ended.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Every year, the school play starts **late** on the big night. This year you\u2019re helping to run it. You\u2019ve got a great plan. So you feel sure it\u2019ll **start on time**. Will it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'You know **every detail** of your own plan, and why it should work. You don\u2019t know the inside of other people\u2019s plans. You only hear **how they ended**. So yours always looks better than theirs did.'},{kind:'moral',text:'It\u2019s easy to think the usual rules are **for other people**. They\u2019re for you too. Start from **what usually happens**, even when the plan is yours.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Everyone you know whose building work ran over had **an excuse**. Yours won\u2019t run over, because you\u2019ve found a good builder. So, almost certainly, **did they**. What makes your case feel different?'},{kind:'explain',text:'You can see **the inside** of your own case: the reasons this project is different. The other cases are known only as **statistics**. So you\u2019re comparing a vivid particular with an abstraction, and the particular wins.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Willpower won\u2019t fix an unequal comparison, but **a procedure** can. Write the usual overrun down first. Then argue, in one sentence, why your build should **beat it**. Often the argument collapses once it\u2019s stated.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your own case **feels exempt**, but it **probably isn\u2019t**. The inside view has all the detail and none of the track record. That\u2019s why it\u2019s more confident and less accurate.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'You know plenty of people whose renovations ran over. Then you plan your own kitchen and budget for **no overrun at all**. Why do base rates always seem to be about **other people**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'This is the hardest version of the base-rate problem: applying it to **yourself**. You have the inside of your own case, every reason it\u2019s different. Other cases reach you only as **statistics**, and a vivid particular beats an abstraction.'},{kind:'story',text:'Your kitchen plan has a good builder, a fixed quote and a clear schedule. Everyone whose job overran had their own reasons too. They also found good builders. Their reasons felt just as **solid** at the start.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a manager sure her new hire will settle in faster than most. She knows the person and has watched them shine. The usual figure for new starters feels irrelevant. **That feeling** is the warning sign.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some cases really are different, and a **stated, checkable reason** can justify moving off the base rate. The opposite misuse is a ritual every case passes. A reason that fitted the overruns too is no reason.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Resolve can\u2019t fix an unequal comparison, so use **a procedure**. Write the general figure down first. Then demand **an explicit argument** for why your case should differ, and see if it survives.'}]},'base-rates_5':{title:'Pick the Right Group',card:{insight:'A number only helps if it comes from cases like yours.',tryThis:'Next time you look up a number, write down which group it came from. Then ask if it fits.',skills:['thinking']},review:{prompt:'You want to guess how fast your old car will sell. What do you check?',right:'Whether the number comes from cars like yours',wrong:'How fast most cars sell on the site',why:'A number from the wrong group gives you a wrong guess.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You want to guess how long **a new puppy** will take to learn to sit. Do you compare with all dogs? All puppies? Puppies of the **same kind**? Which group should you pick?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Each group gives **a different answer**. Picking the group is the hard part. And it\u2019s easy to pick the one you want. Hoping for a short guess, you\u2019d choose **the fastest group**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Starting from what usually happens only works with **the right group**. So say out loud which group you picked and **why it fits**. Then someone can tell you if it doesn\u2019t.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A firm forecasts how long customers take to pay, using **ten years** of its own records. The figure is precise and well documented. But the payment rules **changed last spring**. Can you still trust it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The outside view needs a **genuine reference class**. Here the numbers are real, but they describe a world that no longer exists. A base rate from a **superseded market**, technology or rulebook is confidently misleading.'},{kind:'explain',text:'It also fails when the class is **chosen badly**. That happens by accident, and more easily on purpose. Want a short estimate? You\u2019ll find the class of fast projects. Every class is a choice, and that\u2019s **where error enters**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Name the group you\u2019re comparing with, **argue for it** and let others challenge it. Ask whether its records **still describe** cases like yours. Most of the error hides in that choice.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two forecasters estimate **an unusual project**. Both say they\u2019re using base rates. One reaches for a statistic that looks similar. The other names her group and **invites challenge**. Which one should you trust?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The outside view is **only as good** as its reference class. It fails when the class is genuinely wrong, and when it\u2019s chosen badly. So the class must be named, argued for and **left open** to challenge.'},{kind:'story',text:'The first forecaster finds a figure for something superficially similar and calls it the outside view. The second explains why her class fits and asks colleagues to argue. Both use base rates. Only one uses them **honestly**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take the firm with ten years of payment records. Last spring the rules changed. Its old figure is precise and well documented, yet it describes **a world that\u2019s gone**. Real numbers, wrong population.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. This idea is easy to misuse. Claiming your case is **truly novel** is the quickest escape from every base rate. Real novelty is rarer than it feels. So state why the usual class doesn\u2019t fit.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Every reference class is **a choice**, and most error enters there. So say which population you claim to belong to, and why. Then let **someone argue** with you.'}]}}}},946,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'bd-1w0r4nf',kind:'podcast',credit:{show:'The Happiness Lab',guest:'Angela Duckworth'},promise:'Make hard things easier by changing your space',about:'What if the fix for a bad habit is around you? On the podcast The Happiness Lab, Laurie Santos talks with Angela Duckworth. Duckworth studies how people act. She shows how your space and your friends shape you. Want to make hard things easier?',ideas:{'bd-1w0r4nf_1':{title:'Fish in Water',card:{insight:'At least half of what people do comes from what is around them.',tryThis:'When someone lets you down, write two things in their week that could explain it.',skills:['psychology','thinking']},review:{prompt:'Your friend skips your Saturday lunch again. What do you check first?',right:'What is going on in their week, before you blame them',wrong:'Whether they are just the kind of person who flakes',why:'What is around a person explains at least half of what they do.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your friend says no to Saturday lunch. Then again the next week. You start to think they **don\u2019t like you**. Is that the only reason someone **says no**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Psychologist Angela Duckworth says we make this mistake a lot. We blame the person and **miss the situation**. Maybe your friend is coming down with flu. Maybe they\u2019re **buried in work**. Their week is the part you can\u2019t see.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Duckworth says **at least half** of what we do comes from our surroundings. So when someone lets you down, look at **their situation** first. It may be the bigger part.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your partner turns down a Saturday lunch with your friends. You\u2019ve got a **ready explanation**: *he\u2019s just not a people person.* It feels obvious. But how sure are you that it\u2019s **about him** at all?'},{kind:'story',text:'Angela Duckworth uses this exact example. A husband declines Saturday lunch. Maybe he\u2019s an introvert. Or maybe he\u2019s coming down with flu, or buried in work. Each reason fits. Only one of them is about who he is.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Duckworth describes this as the **fundamental attribution error**. We pin behavior on personality and neglect the circumstances. Why? Your attention treats surroundings as **blurry background**. Like a fish in water, you stop seeing what you\u2019re swimming in.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Research Duckworth cites finds the situation drives **at least half** of behavior, very likely more. So before you blame character, yours or anyone\u2019s, ask **what\u2019s around** the person.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Someone cancels on you twice, and you quietly file them under *unreliable*. **Case closed**. But what if the label was wrong from the start, and you never checked what was **going on around them**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Angela Duckworth gives a small case. A husband turns down Saturday lunch, and the easy label is introvert. Suppose he\u2019s really coming down with flu, or drowning in deadlines. The label stays, and the real cause goes unseen.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Why does the label win? Duckworth\u2019s answer: attention treats surroundings as **background blur**. You see the person, not the water they\u2019re swimming in. That mistake has a name: the **fundamental attribution error**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now turn it on yourself. You snap at a coworker and decide you\u2019re just short-tempered. Maybe. Or maybe you slept badly, skipped lunch and sat through four meetings. Same mistake, pointed inward.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The research Duckworth describes gives the situation **at least half** of behavior, likely more. But half isn\u2019t all. Sometimes he really is an introvert, and sometimes you\u2019re really short-tempered. Check first. Then judge.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Character matters, but it\u2019s **half the story** at most. When behavior puzzles you, yours included, look at **the water** first. Then decide what it says about the person.'}]},'bd-1w0r4nf_2':{title:'Scatter the Cookies',card:{insight:'When you keep giving in, put the thing out of reach instead.',tryThis:'Pick one snack or habit you keep fighting. Move it out of reach before tonight.',skills:['habits','psychology']},review:{prompt:'You keep snacking late at night, no matter how hard you try. What helps?',right:'Move the snacks out of reach, so there is nothing to fight',wrong:'Promise to be strong and try harder next time',why:'In her study, students who changed their space said they did better.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Frog and Toad have a pile of cookies they can\u2019t stop eating. They try to **be strong**. It doesn\u2019t work. What would **you do** if you were them?'},{kind:'story',text:'In the end, they scatter the cookies for the birds. No cookies, no fight. Angela Duckworth uses this story. She calls the trick **situational agency**: you change what\u2019s around you, so you don\u2019t have to win the battle.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Fighting a temptation head-on is hard, and you often lose. Duckworth\u2019s answer is simpler: **move the temptation**. Then there\u2019s **nothing to resist**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'It\u2019s late and the cookie tin is on the counter. You\u2019ve decided, firmly, that you\u2019re done for the night. Ten minutes later the lid is off again. Was the problem **your willpower**, or where you left **the tin**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Angela Duckworth once tried to train willpower in kids using a rowing machine. She doesn\u2019t sugarcoat the result. It **flopped completely**. Trying to make people tougher in the moment turned out to be the hard way.'},{kind:'explain',text:'In an experiment with James Gross, Duckworth taught some students they could **change their situation**. Others got a willpower message, or nothing. A week later, the situation group reported **better progress** on academic goals.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Duckworth\u2019s point is simple. **Willpower loses** more often than you\u2019d like. So stop relying on the fight, and **change the situation** so the temptation is out of reach.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture two students with the same goal. One is told to **try harder**. The other learns she can change her situation. A week later, one of them reports **better progress**. Which one would you bet on?'},{kind:'explain',text:'That\u2019s roughly the experiment Angela Duckworth ran with James Gross. Students taught they could **change their situation** reported more progress on academic goals. That beat a willpower message, and beat doing nothing. Duckworth calls the skill **situational agency**.'},{kind:'story',text:'You might think willpower just needs training. Duckworth once tried exactly that, building it in kids on a rowing machine. By her own account, it went nowhere. Training the resister wasn\u2019t the answer.'},{kind:'story',text:'What does the other road look like? Duckworth points to Frog and Toad. Their willpower keeps failing against the cookies. So they scatter the cookies for the birds, and the fight simply ends.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The Gross result is what students said about their own progress, a week on. It\u2019s a short window and **self-reported**. It shows changing the situation helped. It doesn\u2019t show willpower is useless.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You don\u2019t have to win every **fight with temptation**. Like Frog and Toad, you can **end the fight** early. Change the situation, and the hard thing gets easier.'}]},'bd-1w0r4nf_3':{title:'The Pit Crew',card:{insight:'Making a hard thing easier on yourself is smart.',tryThis:'Pick one goal you are doing all alone. Write down one person or trick that could help.',skills:['productivity','psychology']},review:{prompt:'A friend says your phone timer trick is cheating. What do you say?',right:'It makes a hard thing easier, which is smart',wrong:'Real effort means you do it all on your own',why:'Duckworth says to make hard things easier, not to skip them.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Think of the statues in a park, or the heroes in movies. They tend to celebrate one person who wins **all alone**. Why do you think we love the **lone hero** so much?'},{kind:'story',text:'Angela Duckworth had help writing her book Situated: about 45 people. Think of them as a pit crew, like the team that keeps a race car running. You wouldn\u2019t call the driver a cheat for using them.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Getting help isn\u2019t **cheating**. The hard thing still gets done. Duckworth\u2019s point is to **make it easier** on yourself, and you\u2019re allowed to.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You put your phone in a drawer so you can finally focus. It works. But a small voice says it **doesn\u2019t count**, that a stronger person wouldn\u2019t need the drawer. Ever felt **faintly guilty** for using a trick?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Angela Duckworth says this is common. We admire **white-knuckle willpower** as a moral win. Using an app or a trick feels a little like cheating. Even our statues and movies celebrate the person who **triumphs alone**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Duckworth has done it both ways. She wrote Grit **without help**. Situated had a huge pit crew of editors, consultants, friends and family. About 45 people shared **one Google Doc**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Duckworth isn\u2019t telling you to **skip hard things**, only to **make them easier**.',left:{title:'Going it alone',items:['Grit, written without help','White-knuckle willpower','The lone hero statue']},right:{title:'With a crew',items:['Situated, with about 45 people','A trick or an app','Asking for help']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Hard things still have to get done. Making them **easier on yourself** is a strength, not a shortcut. So before you grind, ask **who or what** could help.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Here\u2019s an honest worry. If you lean on a trick or a helper, maybe the win **doesn\u2019t count**. Shouldn\u2019t real effort be done **on your own**? Angela Duckworth has written books both ways. Which way was smarter?'},{kind:'story',text:'For Grit, Duckworth worked without help. For Situated, she opened one Google Doc to roughly 45 people, from editors and consultants to friends and family. Same author, same hard job. This time, a **pit crew**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'So why does the worry feel right? Duckworth says we treat **white-knuckle willpower** as the moral choice, and a trick as faintly suspect. Statues and movies feed it, celebrating **the lone winner**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Say you use an app to block distracting sites, and you finish the report early. Part of you shrugs: *the app did it.* That\u2019s the same bias, turned on your own win. You still wrote every word.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Duckworth isn\u2019t saying to avoid hard things. If a trick lets you **skip the goal** itself, you haven\u2019t made it easier. You\u2019ve swapped it for a smaller one. The work still has to happen.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Going it alone isn\u2019t more virtuous. It\u2019s just **harder than it needs** to be. Build your **pit crew**, use the trick, and still do the hard thing.'}]},'bd-1w0r4nf_4':{title:'What the Serenity Prayer Means',card:{insight:'Before you put up with a problem, check if you can change it.',tryThis:'Write down one thing you treat as stuck, like your desk. Then write one way to change it.',skills:['thinking','habits']},review:{prompt:'Your desk sits by a loud hallway, and it wears you out. What first?',right:'Check whether you can change it, like moving the desk',wrong:'Accept it and work on staying calm about the noise',why:'Staying calm is one answer, but often you can just move the desk.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your chair is wobbly. Every day it creaks, and every day you tell yourself to **stop minding it**. A month goes by. Is putting up with it really the **only choice** you have?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A famous prayer, the **Serenity Prayer**, is often read as advice to accept what you can\u2019t change. Angela Duckworth says that misses the point. Its writer meant it as a **call to act**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Accepting is sometimes right. But Duckworth\u2019s advice is to **check first** whether you can change the situation. If you can, **fix the chair**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your desk sits by a noisy hallway. Every day you remind yourself to **stay calm** and let it go. You\u2019ve made peace with it. Or have you just **stopped asking** whether the desk could move?'},{kind:'story',text:'Angela Duckworth points to the **Serenity Prayer**. Most of us read it as: accept what you can\u2019t change, and adjust your attitude. But she argues its author, Reinhold Niebuhr, wrote it as a **call to action**. She cites what his daughter wrote.'},{kind:'explain',text:'She adds Viktor Frankl, who wrote about the camps. Frankl held that suffering through a situation you could change would be **masochistic**. Endurance has its place. But enduring what you could fix is just **extra pain**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Duckworth says plenty of what **feels fixed** is really **up to you**.',left:{title:'Adjust your attitude',items:['Breathe through the noise','Tell yourself it\u2019s fine']},right:{title:'Change the situation',items:['Where your phone sits','Who you spend time with','Where your desk goes']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Acceptance is one answer, not the **only answer**. Before you make peace with a bad situation, check whether you can **change it**. Often, Duckworth says, you can.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve probably heard the Serenity Prayer. Most people take it as a gentle push to **accept things** and adjust their attitude. Angela Duckworth thinks that reading leaves out **the hard part**. Which part did we forget?'},{kind:'story',text:'Duckworth goes back to the prayer\u2019s author, Reinhold Niebuhr. She argues he meant it as **a call to action**, not a lesson in resignation. Her evidence is what his daughter wrote about it. On her reading, that includes acting on your situation.'},{kind:'story',text:'Duckworth also cites Viktor Frankl, who wrote about the camps. If anyone could speak for enduring, it\u2019s him. Yet Frankl held that suffering through a situation **you could change** would be **masochistic**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'So what\u2019s actually in your hands? Duckworth\u2019s answers are ordinary. You choose where your phone sits and **who you spend time** with. You choose where your desk goes. Often you even choose **where you live** or work.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some situations really can\u2019t move, however hard you look. Then acceptance is still the right tool. The mistake is reaching for acceptance first, **before you\u2019ve checked**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Serenity isn\u2019t the same as giving up. Accept **what truly can\u2019t change**, and act on the rest. Your phone, your desk and your people are often **the rest**.'}]},'bd-1w0r4nf_5':{title:'The Three-Foot Circle',card:{insight:'What you can see and reach shapes what you do.',tryThis:'For your next big task, put your phone in another room. Keep what you need close.',skills:['focus','habits']},review:{prompt:'You need to study for an hour, and your phone is next to you. What now?',right:'Put the phone in another room, out of reach',wrong:'Turn it face down next to you and ignore it',why:'Students did best on a test with the phone in another room.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture a bowl of chips right next to you on the couch. Your hand keeps finding it, almost **on its own**. Now picture the bowl across the room. Would you still eat **as many**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Angela Duckworth describes the space you can **see and reach** as about three feet around you. What\u2019s inside it has a big pull. Push the chips four feet away, and you have to **get up** for every single one.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Things close to you get used. Things far away get **used less**. So put what\u2019s good for you **within reach**, and the rest out of the room.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You sit down to do something hard that needs your **full brain**. Your phone is on the desk, face down and silent. You\u2019re not touching it. So it can\u2019t be **costing you** anything. Can it?'},{kind:'story',text:'Duckworth describes the brain drain study. Students took an IQ test with their phone in different places. Those with the phone on the desk scored lowest. Those with it in **another room** scored highest.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Duckworth\u2019s rule is simple. **Add friction** to what you want less of. Remove it from what you want more of. Your personal space, about **three feet** you can see and reach, gets an outsized say.'},{kind:'story',text:'The same pull works on drinks. A hospital cafeteria put water at eye level and a basket of it by the register. Water sales rose and soda sales fell. The shelf did the work.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You don\u2019t need more discipline around your phone. You need **more distance**. Keep the good stuff **within reach** and send the rest to another room.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Surely you can ignore a bowl of chips. Or a phone sitting silent on the desk. You\u2019re an adult, after all. Angela Duckworth isn\u2019t so sure **ignoring them** is free. What does **nearby** cost you?'},{kind:'story',text:'One hospital cafeteria moved its water up to eye level and added a basket at the register. Sales tell the story. More water went out. Less soda did. The change Duckworth describes is all placement.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Duckworth\u2019s frame is **personal space**, the three feet you can see and reach. What sits there pulls hard. In the brain drain study, students did worst on an IQ test with a phone on the desk. With it in **another room**, they did best.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take a bowl of chips. Within arm\u2019s reach, eating them takes no effort at all. Push the bowl four feet away, and every chip now costs you a trip off the couch. Same chips, more friction.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Friction only **adds a step**. Four feet won\u2019t stop you if you really want the chips. You can still get up. Distance tilts the easy choice. It doesn\u2019t make the choice for you.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your surroundings **tilt your choices**. They don\u2019t make them. So tilt them your way: keep the good things **within reach** and put your phone in another room.'}]},'bd-1w0r4nf_6':{title:'Goals in Plain Sight',card:{insight:'Your mind goes to what is in front of you, so put goals there.',tryThis:'Write one clear goal on a scrap of paper. Stick it where you will see it each morning.',skills:['habits','productivity']},review:{prompt:'Your goal slips your mind by lunch most days. What is the better fix?',right:'Write the goal clearly and put it where you will see it',wrong:'Say it to yourself each morning until it sticks',why:'Your mind goes to what is in front of you, so keep goals in view.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You decide on Monday to read more. By Wednesday, you haven\u2019t opened a book. You didn\u2019t stop caring. So where did **the goal go**, and how do you **bring it back**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Angela Duckworth tells a story about Michael Phelps. As a boy, he wrote his goals on paper. Then he posted them near his bed. The goals weren\u2019t just in his head. They were **in plain view**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your attention goes to **what\u2019s in front** of you. So don\u2019t just remember a goal. **Put it** where you\u2019ll see it.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You wrote a goal down once, in a notebook you never open. You still believe in it. You just **rarely think** about it. What would change if it were **staring at you** all day?'},{kind:'story',text:'Restaurateur Will Guidara can recite one of his father\u2019s sayings from memory. He still keeps it engraved on a paperweight. Why bother, when he already knows it? Knowing a line and having it in mind aren\u2019t the same.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Angela Duckworth says human attention is **very reactive**. It goes to whatever is in front of you. So she suggests putting what you want on your mind into **your space**, where your eyes will land.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Duckworth describes a study with the College Board. It looked at many strategies students used. The **best predictor** was plain: write your study goals **concretely**, then put them somewhere you\u2019ll see them.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A goal that lives only in your head **competes with** whatever\u2019s in front of you. So write it **concretely** and put it in view. Let your space do the reminding.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A sticky note with your goal on it can feel a bit silly. You know what you want. You\u2019re not going to **forget it**. So why would anyone put a goal they know by heart **on display**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Will Guidara is exactly that person. The restaurateur knows his father\u2019s saying by heart, yet keeps it engraved on a paperweight. For Duckworth, that\u2019s no contradiction. Knowing a line and thinking of it are different things.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Her reason: attention is **very reactive**. It follows whatever is in front of you, wanted or not. So Duckworth\u2019s fix is to install what you want on your mind **in your space**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Michael Phelps used the same move as a boy. He wrote his goals on paper and put them up by his bed. Posted there, the goals were hard to miss.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Duckworth describes a College Board study. Its best predictor was **two things together**: goals written concretely, and placed where students would see them. A vague note in view covers only half. And a predictor isn\u2019t proof of cause.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t trust your memory to **hold a goal**. Write it concretely and put it in your space, where your **reactive attention** will keep finding it.'}]},'bd-1w0r4nf_7':{title:'The Mentor Magnet',card:{insight:'The people near you rub off on you, so stay near the ones you admire.',tryThis:'Write down one person you admire. Add one small way to spend more time near them.',skills:['relationships','career']},review:{prompt:'You want to get more organized at work. What helps most?',right:'Spend time near people at work who plan well',wrong:'Work on your own and set strict rules for yourself',why:'You pick up the habits of the people near you, so choose them well.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Think about the friends you see most. You probably share some of their words, jokes and habits. Nobody sat you down and taught you. So how did **their habits** end up being **yours**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Angela Duckworth says spending time with organized economists made her about 50 percent more organized. The habit spread from them to her through time together. It can work that way for you too.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The people near you **shape you**, whether you notice or not. So pick them on purpose. Spend time with **people you admire**, and you\u2019ll grow toward them.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture graduates who all **end up** in the same line of work: management consulting. Did each of them choose it freely, after weighing everything? Or did **something else** pull them there?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Angela Duckworth says we **drift toward the norms** of the people around us. That\u2019s how so many graduates land in one field. It\u2019s peer pressure, but it isn\u2019t all bad. Point it the right way, and peers **lift you up**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Duckworth does this on purpose. She works with people she admires, including a group of organized economists. Time with them, she says, made her about 50 percent more organized. She chose the influence instead of leaving it to chance.'},{kind:'explain',text:'She also urges you to become a **mentor magnet**. That means being grateful and coming prepared. It means thanking the mentors who help you and offering help in return. You don\u2019t just find good mentors. You **attract them**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You\u2019ll **drift toward** the people around you either way. So choose them. Spend time with people **you admire**, and let the drift work for you.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Peer pressure has a bad name. Most of us would rather be the one **thinking for ourselves**. But what if being shaped by others is **less a flaw** than a tool you could use?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Angela Duckworth cites Joe Henrich here. In his view, humans are dominant because we **learn from each other**, as one **collective brain**. Copying isn\u2019t a glitch. Duckworth sees the power of peers as peer pressure\u2019s upside.'},{kind:'story',text:'She lives it. Duckworth works with people she admires. After time around organized economists, she reckons she\u2019s about 50 percent more organized. She didn\u2019t fight the pull. She aimed it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Leave the pull unaimed and it still works. Duckworth points to graduates who all end up in management consulting. Each may feel it was a free choice. Duckworth sees a drift toward the norms around them.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. You can\u2019t always pick who\u2019s around you. Classmates and coworkers often come assigned. And the drift follows whoever is near, **good or bad**. Choosing helps most where you actually have a say.'},{kind:'moral',text:'So aim the pull. Be a **mentor magnet**, as Duckworth puts it: grateful, prepared, quick to thank and to **offer help**. The people you admire will shape you, if you stay near them.'}]},'bd-1w0r4nf_8':{title:'The Four-Mile Move',card:{insight:'The culture right around you matters most, and you can change it.',tryThis:'Pick one way your team or street does things that bugs you. Write one small step to change it.',skills:['career','habits']},review:{prompt:'Your team always runs meetings late, and it wears you down. What now?',right:'Make one small move, like ending your meetings on time',wrong:'Accept it, since the boss sets how things are done',why:'You have more say over how your own team works than over big trends.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Every home, class and team has its own **way of doing things**. Who talks first. When you eat. How loud it gets. Have you ever wondered whether **your little world** could work differently?'},{kind:'story',text:'Angela Duckworth and her husband once moved just four miles, into Philadelphia. Four miles isn\u2019t far. Yet she says it changed the people she met. Your own small world can shift with a smaller step still.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Duckworth calls the culture right around you your **microculture**. It\u2019s small enough to change. You don\u2019t have to **move house**, but you can change something nearby.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You join a new team, and within a month you\u2019re working later, emailing faster, joking the same way. Nobody told you to. So **who decided** how you\u2019d work here? And could **you decide** differently?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Angela Duckworth defines culture simply: **how we do things** around here. And the culture that matters most is the one right next to you. She calls it your **microculture**. You have more say over it than over big trends.'},{kind:'story',text:'Duckworth made this kind of change herself. A design guru suggested she consider moving. So she and her husband moved four miles, into Philadelphia. Those four miles changed her commute, her kids\u2019 schools and the people she met.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Duckworth says you have **far more control** over the culture **right around you** than over big trends.',left:{title:'Macro trends',items:['Big trends','Little say over them']},right:{title:'Your microculture',items:['How your team works','Who lives on your block','The groups you join']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Big trends are mostly out of your hands. Your **microculture** isn\u2019t. Make **one small move** in your job, block or group, and you change the way things are done around you.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Culture sounds like **something enormous**: a country, an industry, an era. You can\u2019t change that by yourself. So when your workplace feels wrong, why even try? What could **one person** possibly move?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Angela Duckworth\u2019s move is to make the unit smaller. Forget the country. The culture that shapes your days is your **microculture**. It\u2019s the way things get done on your team, your block, your group. That one has **far more give**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a team whose meetings always run long. One person decides hers will end on time, with a clear last item. She can\u2019t fix the company. She can change how this room runs on Tuesdays.'},{kind:'story',text:'Duckworth pulled a bigger version of that lever. A design guru suggested she consider moving, so she and her husband went four miles, into Philadelphia. Her commute changed. So did her kids\u2019 schools and the people she met.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Not everyone can move four miles, and macro trends stay mostly out of reach. The claim here is narrower than it sounds. The **small stuff** right around you has more give than you\u2019d guess.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You don\u2019t have to accept the culture you\u2019re handed. Shape **your microculture**, one small move at a time. Duckworth says focusing on her situation has **added to her grit**, not taken from it.'}]}}}},947,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'being-ill-among-other-people',kind:'book',promise:'Help and be helped when someone is sick',about:'Why can being sick wear you out, even in bed? A lot of the work is about other people. You share the news and calm everyone down. You answer offers of help. These five ideas make that work lighter. Who could you help this week?',ideas:{'being-ill-among-other-people_1':{title:'The Unpaid Host',card:{insight:'When you are sick, you calm everyone down, so let one friend share the news.',tryThis:'Write down one person you would trust to share your news if you got sick.',skills:['relationships','health']},review:{prompt:'A coworker starts treatment and dreads telling people. What would help her most?',right:'One person who shares her news and answers the questions',wrong:'Telling each person herself, so no one feels left out',why:'Telling people is hard work, so it should move off the sick person.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You break your arm at football. Gran rings in tears, your aunt wants **the whole story**, and friends text all night. To each one you say *I\u2019m fine*. By bedtime, why are you **so worn out**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'When someone is ill, **lots of people** need telling. Each one is upset, and the ill person ends up calming them all down. That\u2019s **real work**, and it can be handed to someone else.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Being ill comes with **a hidden job**: looking after everyone else\u2019s worry. You don\u2019t have to do it alone. Let **one helper** pass the news on.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A diagnosis arrives, and with it **a queue**. Parents, friends, colleagues and neighbours all need telling. Each telling means taking in their shock and **handing back comfort**. Who looks after the person doing it?'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a woman just told she needs treatment. Each evening she phones another relative. She absorbs their distress, reassures them, keeps things light. By the end of the week, she\u2019s exhausted, and she still hasn\u2019t rested.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Why does it fall to her? There\u2019s **no settled script**. People arrive with real feeling and no idea what to do with it. She\u2019s the only one with an obvious role, so she becomes **the host**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'What helps is **one coordinator**. That person fields questions, updates the wider circle and handles the arrangements. Your aunt rings them, not you. Nobody cares less. **The job just moved**, and the relief is huge.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The person who\u2019s ill ends up **managing everyone else**, just when they have least to give. So plan for it. Appoint **one person** to tell everyone, and hand the job over.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Illness is usually described as **something in a body**. Yet much of what wears people down happens **between people**: the telling, the visits, the reassuring. Where does all that work land?'},{kind:'explain',text:'It lands on the ill person. They reassure visitors, absorb distress and perform **an easier version** of themselves. None of it looks like work. But each call asks them to take in shock and **hand back comfort**.'},{kind:'story',text:'A man starts treatment and tells his family himself. Each call is the same: the gasp, the questions, his steady voice saying it\u2019ll be fine. After a week of this, he\u2019s drained, and treatment has barely begun.'},{kind:'story',text:'His sister then offers to be the contact. Relatives ring her for news. She sends one update to everyone and handles the visits. He rests. Nobody cares about him less. The job has simply changed hands.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some people need to tell certain people themselves, and telling can help them **take it in**. A coordinator who decides what others may know becomes a gatekeeper. The ill person should choose the setup.'},{kind:'moral',text:'If you\u2019re ill, the telling is **a real job**, and you can hand it over. If someone near you is ill, **offer to carry it**. Few people think to.'}]},'being-ill-among-other-people_2':{title:'One Thing, One Day',card:{insight:'Good help names one thing and one day, so the helper picks the job.',tryThis:'Find the last time you said \u201clet me know.\u201d Send a new offer with one thing and one day.',skills:['relationships','communication']},review:{prompt:'Your friend\u2019s husband is in the hospital. How do you offer to help?',right:'Offer one thing and the day you will do it',wrong:'Tell her to let you know if she needs anything',why:'A clear offer means she does not have to think of a job and ask.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your friend is ill. One person says, *let me know if you need anything*. Another says **I\u2019ll walk your dog** on Saturday. Both are kind. Which one actually **helps more**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The first offer leaves your friend to **think of a job**, ask for it, and feel okay asking. When you\u2019re ill, all of that is hard. The second offer has already done **that work**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Saying *anything you need* sounds kind, but it **hands the work back**. Real help names **one thing** and one day. Then your friend only has to say yes.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your friend\u2019s partner is in hospital. She has **twelve messages** saying let me know if you need anything. She needs plenty. She asks nobody. A week later, **the messages stop**. What\u2019s gone wrong?'},{kind:'explain',text:'To ask, she must **know what would help**, believe the offer was real, and accept being the asker. Each step takes real effort. The last is **uncomfortable**, and it often stops people.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Both offers are **sincere**. The difference is **who holds the task**.',left:{title:'Open offer',items:['Let me know','She must decide and ask','Declining is the easy option']},right:{title:'Specific offer',items:['Dinner on Thursday','Help arrives by default','Declining takes the effort']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Offer **one specific thing** on one named day, with an easy way out. The care is the same. What changes is **who has to think**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Almost everyone has sent the message: **let me know** if you need anything. It\u2019s sincere. It\u2019s kind. And it\u2019s rarely taken up. Why does so much **real goodwill** go unused?'},{kind:'explain',text:'An open offer hands three jobs to the person least able to do them: **identifying**, requesting and coordinating. A specific offer flips that. Help becomes **the default**, and declining is the effortful option.'},{kind:'story',text:'A friend messages someone recovering at home: I\u2019m bringing dinner on Thursday, tell me if that doesn\u2019t suit. Accepting costs nothing. The dinner arrives, and nobody had to ask for anything.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a new parent drowning in a hard first month. Ten people say ask if you need anything. One says she\u2019ll take the baby for an hour on Sunday. Guess which offer gets used.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. A fixed offer can impose: dinner someone can\u2019t eat, a visit when they want quiet. The **way out** must be real and cheap. Pushed too far, carrying the decision becomes deciding for them.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The person with **more capacity** should carry the decision cost. Open offers do the opposite. So name the thing, name the day, and **make declining easy**.'}]},'being-ill-among-other-people_3':{title:'Who Shows Up',card:{insight:'Showing up in hard times is a skill, and caring is a different thing.',tryThis:'Write down two friends who went quiet in a hard time. Next to each, write a reason besides not caring.',skills:['relationships','psychology']},review:{prompt:'You had surgery. Your close friend never called, but a neighbor came each day. Why?',right:'Your friend cared but did not know how to help',wrong:'Your friend never cared much about you at all',why:'Who shows up depends on their skill with hard times, not how much they care.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re ill at home for a month. Your **best friend** sends one text, then goes quiet. A girl you hardly know brings **a card every week**. Why would that happen?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Lots of people love the beach but can\u2019t swim well. Liking the sea and swimming in it are **different things**. Caring about you and **coping with hard times** are different things too.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Being able to **show up** when things are hard is its own skill. It isn\u2019t the same as **how much** someone likes you. When it\u2019s over, say hello again.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'During a long illness, a close friend **goes silent**. Meanwhile a colleague you barely knew **turns up** every week with the right food. It\u2019s tempting to rewrite your friendships. Should you?'},{kind:'story',text:'The colleague, it turns out, nursed her own father through something similar. She knew what to bring, how long to stay and what not to say. The friend simply didn\u2019t know what to say, and each silent week made calling harder.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The usual model has **one dial**. A better one splits it into **two traits**.',left:{title:'One dial',items:['How much they care','Predicts who comes']},right:{title:'Two traits',items:['Affection','Ability to be near difficulty','Only loosely linked']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Showing up under difficulty is **a specific capability**. It tracks closeness only loosely. So read an absence as a fact about **coping**, not a verdict on love, and leave room to resume.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Illness often shows that **the categories** you sorted your friends into were wrong. Some close friends vanish. Acquaintances sometimes **turn out extraordinary**. What\u2019s really being revealed here?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The common conclusion is that **the vanished friends** were never real. A more accurate account is that showing up is **a specific capability**. It correlates only loosely with how close you are.'},{kind:'story',text:'One friend didn\u2019t know what to say in the first week. By the third, the silence itself had become awkward. Each week made a message harder to send, until it never came.'},{kind:'story',text:'Meanwhile, an acquaintance had been through something similar. She knew what to bring, how long to stay and what not to say. That knowledge made her good at it, not years of friendship.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some absences really are verdicts, and the reframe doesn\u2019t oblige you to **forgive everything**. The absent friend can borrow it as an excuse. And during the illness, the absence may simply hurt.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Who shows up tracks **tolerance for difficulty**, not affection. Knowing that makes an absence less of an injury. It also leaves the friendship **somewhere to resume**.'}]},'being-ill-among-other-people_4':{title:'After the Cards Stop',card:{insight:'Help fades once a sick person looks well, often when they still need it most.',tryThis:'Pick someone who was sick this year. Plan to check on them two months after it ends.',skills:['relationships','health']},review:{prompt:'A friend finished treatment six weeks ago and says she is fine. What now?',right:'Check in lightly anyway, since help fades once she looks well',wrong:'Leave her be, since the hard part is over now',why:'Help stops once you look well, but the hard part often comes later.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'At a fireworks show, everyone gathers while it\u2019s **loud and bright**. When the last one fades, they all go home. But being ill isn\u2019t like fireworks. What happens **after the bang**?'},{kind:'story',text:'You come home from hospital after an operation. The first week brought cards and visits. Now nobody calls. But you still can\u2019t play sport, and you still get tired very easily.'},{kind:'moral',text:'People gather when someone is very ill, then **drift away** when they look better. But that\u2019s often **the hardest part**. It\u2019s a good time to check in.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A colleague comes back after **three months off**. The team says great to have you back, and moves on. She\u2019s still tired by two. Nobody **asks again**. Why does the support stop there?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Support is **switched on** by a visible crisis and switched off by visible recovery. Neither signal tracks the real need. Nobody decides to abandon anyone. The signal they followed **simply went quiet**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Meanwhile, the load is often heaviest. She\u2019s returning to work, managing what limitation remains, and **absorbing what happened**. And because everyone\u2019s story says she got better, she\u2019s **the last one** allowed to say it isn\u2019t over.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Recovery isn\u2019t the end of it socially. If you know someone who\u2019s been ill, **mark the calendar** for two months on. That\u2019s when **almost nobody** checks in.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'When someone falls seriously ill, **attention floods in**: cards, visits, meals. Months later, when they look well, it\u2019s gone. Is that the moment they **need it least**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The pattern is **structural**, not callous. Support is mobilised by visible crisis and stood down by visible resolution. Neither tracks the need, which is often **heaviest afterwards**: work, residual limits, taking it all in.'},{kind:'story',text:'A teacher returns after treatment. Colleagues welcome her back on the Monday and then move on. She still has appointments and still tires by mid-afternoon. Inside, she\u2019s only beginning to absorb what happened.'},{kind:'story',text:'Her friend puts a note in his calendar for two months after the treatment ended. When it comes up, he asks how she\u2019s really doing. It\u2019s the first time anyone has asked in weeks.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Two months is a rule of thumb, not a clinical timeline. Some people want the episode closed and resent being **cast as ill**. So keep the check light, an offer you can wave off.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The crowd leaves when the story ends, not when the need does. So be the one who **checks in late**. A light question from you, months on, can **mean the most**.'}]},'being-ill-among-other-people_5':{title:'The Skill of Taking Help',card:{insight:'Taking help well is a skill, and it lets the people who love you help.',tryThis:'Next time someone helps you, say a plain thank you with no sorry.',skills:['relationships','psychology']},review:{prompt:'Your brother offers to do your shopping but will buy the wrong brands. What now?',right:'Take the help with the wrong brands, and skip the sorry',wrong:'Thank him but do the shopping yourself to get it right',why:'Letting him help is a gift to him, even if he gets it wrong.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re in bed with flu. Your little brother brings you **a hot drink**. He spilled a bit on the way. You could say sorry five times, or get up and **make it yourself**. What else could you do?'},{kind:'explain',text:'You could just take it, say thanks, and drink it. When you\u2019re ill, the people who love you **feel worried**. Helping gives that worry **somewhere to go**. So letting them help gives them something too.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Letting people look after you is **a skill**. Next time someone helps, say thank you and **don\u2019t add sorry**. That\u2019s a kind thing to do.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re the one who **organises things**. After an operation, a friend comes to cook. You apologise twice, hover in the kitchen, then do **the washing-up** yourself. She leaves feeling in the way. Why?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Being looked after is **a skill**, and most people reach the point of needing it **without practice**. It\u2019s hardest for anyone whose sense of themselves runs on doing things alone.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Each part of receiving help runs against **a capable habit**. Here\u2019s **the swap**.',left:{title:'The habit',items:['Paying the favour back','Apologising for needing it','Redoing it yourself']},right:{title:'The skill',items:['Accepting without repaying','Saying what you need','Letting it be imperfect']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Your friend has worry with **nowhere to go**. Cooking gives it an outlet. Refuse the help, and you\u2019re not sparing her. You\u2019re leaving her **helpless alongside you**, and the refusing tires you both.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Accepting help competently is **itself a contribution**. Take what\u2019s offered, say what you need, and **skip the apology**. Otherwise people stop offering, and you won\u2019t notice it happen.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Some people are **always the capable one**. They organise, fix and look after others. Then illness puts them on the receiving end, and they **refuse almost everything**. What is that costing them?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Receiving care is a skill most people never practise. The hard parts are specific: accepting without **paying it back**, asking without apologising, and letting a thing be done **imperfectly**. Each runs against a habit of competence.'},{kind:'story',text:'A woman recovering from surgery turns down every offer. She doesn\u2019t want to be a burden. Her friends keep offering, she keeps deflecting, and both sides tire. Within months, the offers have quietly stopped.'},{kind:'story',text:'Her neighbour, in the same position, just says yes. The soup is too salty and the shopping\u2019s slightly wrong. She says thank you anyway. Her helpers feel useful, and they keep coming back.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Not every offer should be accepted. Some help takes control you want to keep, and **independence** can matter deeply. Your acceptance shouldn\u2019t become a duty performed for the helper\u2019s sake.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Refusing help isn\u2019t consideration. It leaves the people who love you **helpless alongside you**. Receiving well gives their care **somewhere to go**.'}]}}}},948,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'beyond-good-and-evil',kind:'book',promise:'Ask why you believe what you believe',about:'What if your strongest beliefs say more about you than about the world? Friedrich Nietzsche was a German thinker. In 1886, he wrote this book to question what people call good. Here are nine of his best questions. Want to try them?',ideas:{'beyond-good-and-evil_1':{title:'Your Views Tell on You',card:{insight:'We often choose first and find our reasons later.',tryThis:'Write down a view you hold strongly. Then write what you would lose if it were wrong.',skills:['thinking','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'You often say fancy coffee is a waste of money. What do you ask first?',right:'What would I lose if I were wrong about it?',wrong:'What are the best reasons that it is a waste?',why:'What you would lose if you were wrong shows where to check.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Sam has lost at chess three times this week. Now Sam says chess is **a dull game** for dull people. Sam has lots of clever reasons. Why is Sam suddenly **so sure**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'In 1886 a German thinker called Friedrich Nietzsche noticed something. Big ideas often tell you about **the person** who holds them. So don\u2019t only ask if an idea is true. Ask **why they need** it to be true.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Sam\u2019s reasons came after **the losing**. Your reasons might work the same way. So turn the question **on yourself** too: what would you lose if you were wrong?'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A friend tells you, at length, that **ambition is overrated**. The reasons are good. Then you remember he stopped competing years ago, after one bruising loss. Does that **change the argument**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Early in his 1886 book, Nietzsche makes a claim about philosophers. Every great philosophy, he says, has been its author\u2019s **personal confession**. It\u2019s a memoir written without meaning to. The moral aims came first, like a seed. The reasoning grew around them.'},{kind:'explain',text:'He isn\u2019t calling thinkers liars. The author **sincerely believes** the reasoning came first. That\u2019s what makes the memoir involuntary. So his method swaps one question for another: what kind of person would **need this** to be true?'},{kind:'moral',text:'Where a view comes from doesn\u2019t make it false. It tells you **where to check** hardest. Turn the question **on your own** views first, or it\u2019s just a weapon.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Early in Beyond Good and Evil, Nietzsche **accuses philosophers** of something odd. Not lying, exactly. Writing their memoirs **by accident**. If he\u2019s right, what are your favourite arguments quietly saying about you?'},{kind:'explain',text:'His claim is that **temperament decides** first. The moral aim is the seed the whole plant grows from. The reasoning arrives later to justify it. So ask of any doctrine: what sort of person would **need it** to be true?'},{kind:'story',text:'Take a philosophy that praises detachment above everything. Nietzsche\u2019s question would be about its author. Was this someone temperamentally unable to attach? The doctrine might still be true. But you now know where its blind spots are likely to sit.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now turn it inward. Suppose you often argue that money doesn\u2019t matter much. Ask what it would cost you if money mattered after all. Perhaps a career you chose for love, not pay. That doesn\u2019t make you wrong. It shows you where to check.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Where a belief comes from doesn\u2019t settle whether it\u2019s true. A bitter person can be **right about bitterness**. Used only on opponents, the method dismisses any argument by diagnosing its author. It\u2019s a guide, not a refutation.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Nietzsche accepted that **his own philosophy** was a confession too. That\u2019s the honest way to hold the tool. Ask what a view does for **its holder**, and start with yours.'}]},'beyond-good-and-evil_2':{title:'Where Your Rules Come From',card:{insight:'Many rules you live by were handed down, not picked.',tryThis:'Write down one rule you live by. Next to it, write who gave it to you.',skills:['thinking','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'You feel bad after a lazy Saturday on the couch. What do you ask?',right:'Who gave me this rule, and did I choose it?',wrong:'How can I get more done on Saturdays?',why:'A rule you never chose is worth a second look.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'In some homes, everyone eats dinner at the table. In others, people eat **whenever they like**. Each family thinks its way is simply normal. So which one is **the right way**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Nietzsche asked an odd question about **wanting the truth**. Everyone wants it, but why? Nobody had checked. Your family\u2019s dinner rule is like that. Nobody chose it. It was **handed down**, like an old chair you never notice.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Pick one rule you follow **without thinking** and ask who gave it to you. You don\u2019t have to drop it. You just get to **choose it** now.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'It\u2019s Sunday evening. You\u2019ve watched a series on the sofa since dinner, and by ten you feel **faintly guilty**. Nobody told you to feel that. So **who taught you** that an empty evening is wasted?'},{kind:'story',text:'Nietzsche\u2019s book opens by turning on the thing philosophers prize most. If we want truth, he asks, why that and not falsehood? Why not doubt, or simply not knowing? Philosophers had rested everything on truth\u2019s worth without **once examining** it.'},{kind:'explain',text:'He **doesn\u2019t say** we should prefer lies. His complaint is that the preference is **invisible**. Your guilty Sunday works the same way. The value was **handed down**, not chosen, and you see with it so you never see it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your strongest values are often ones **nobody chose**. Don\u2019t throw them out. Hold them as **a choice**, not as furniture you inherited.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Most books about truth assume you want it. *Beyond Good and Evil* opens by asking **why you should**. Why truth, and not a comfortable untruth? It sounds like **a trick question**. Is it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Nietzsche calls it **the value of truth**, and suggests it has hardly been asked before. Philosophers took truth\u2019s worth as given. His objection isn\u2019t to truth. It\u2019s to a preference held **so deep** that nobody sees it as one.'},{kind:'story',text:'Think of a family in which money is **never discussed**. Ask any of them why, and they\u2019ll shrug. Nobody decided it. The rule was handed down with the furniture, and it shapes careers, marriages and arguments for decades.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a team that prizes speed above everything. Nobody asked what it trades for speed: care, testing, the quiet colleague who needs a day. Speed may still be right. But **nobody decided** it was.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The question is easily misheard as saying truth doesn\u2019t matter. Nietzsche never says that. And you **can\u2019t reopen** every value at once, or you\u2019d have no ground to stand on. Take them one at a time.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Truth is worth having. So is knowing **why you want** it. Pick one value you\u2019d defend without hesitation, and ask **where it came from**.'}]},'beyond-good-and-evil_3':{title:'The Danger of Being Sure',card:{insight:'Being sure can hurt the truth more than a lie can.',tryThis:'Next time you feel very sure and someone says you are wrong, check one fact first.',skills:['thinking','psychology']},review:{prompt:'Your friend is sure of the way and will not check the map. What now?',right:'Check the map, since being sure is not proof.',wrong:'Trust him, since he sounds so sure of it.',why:'Feeling sure is not proof, so look at the facts.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your friend Ben is sure the park is **left at the corner**. He doesn\u2019t look at the map. He walks fast and tells everyone to follow. **Twenty minutes later**, where are you all?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A liar still knows the truth. Ben **wasn\u2019t lying**. He was just sure. Being sure doesn\u2019t feel like a guess, so he **never checked**. When someone said he was wrong, it felt like an attack.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Being sure can block the truth **more than a lie**. So when you feel totally certain, ask how you\u2019d know **if you were wrong**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two colleagues get a project wrong. One **fudged a figure** in a report and knows it. The other is certain it\u2019ll succeed and has **stopped reading** the numbers. Which one should worry you more?'},{kind:'story',text:'Nietzsche first made this point in Human, All Too Human, in 1878. A firm conviction, he argued, does **more harm** to truth than a lie. The suspicion runs all through Beyond Good and Evil too.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The reason is **supervision**. The liar still holds the truth privately and can withdraw the lie. The certain colleague has no private copy to go back to. Their belief feels like knowledge, so **nobody\u2019s checking** it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Sincerity makes a false belief **last longer**, not shorter. So treat the certain person as **the harder problem**. And when you\u2019re certain, check yourself first.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'You catch a colleague in **a small lie**. It feels like the worst thing in the room. Nietzsche would point at someone else: the person across the table who\u2019s **absolutely sure**. Why would sureness be worse?'},{kind:'explain',text:'It\u2019s about **detection**. A lie is under supervision, because the liar knows it\u2019s a lie. A conviction has no supervisor. Its holder experiences it as knowledge, which **switches off** every way it might be checked.'},{kind:'story',text:'A manager is sure the new product will sell. Every warning sounds like disloyalty, so she stops hearing them. Her certainty also persuades the board, because it looks like evidence. In good faith, she defends it **until it fails**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Compare the colleague who fudged one figure. He knows exactly what he changed. Ask him the right question and he can **put it back**. The error is real, but it has an owner who still remembers the truth.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The comparison is a provocation, not a measurement. Lies do real damage, and liars often come to believe themselves. And **some certainty is earned**: you\u2019re right to be sure the stove is hot.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Feeling certain isn\u2019t **evidence**. The beliefs you hold most fiercely are often the ones **carrying the most** for you. Those are the ones to check.'}]},'beyond-good-and-evil_4':{title:'When You Fight Monsters',card:{insight:'Fight a thing long enough, and you may start to copy it.',tryThis:'Today, write one thing you stand for, not just one thing you fight.',skills:['focus','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'Your team fought a mean boss for a year. Now the team is mean. Why?',right:'You fought her so long that you started to copy her.',wrong:'The team let in the wrong kind of people.',why:'A long fight can make you use the other side\u2019s moves.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'In a tug of war, how you lean depends on **the other team**. Your feet, your arms, your whole body are set by their pull. Pull long enough and **what happens** to you?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Nietzsche wrote a short warning in 1886. If you fight monsters, take care you **don\u2019t become one**. And if you stare into a deep pit for long, **it stares back**. What you look at a lot changes you.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Fight something long enough and you can **start to copy** it. So don\u2019t just know what you\u2019re against. Know what you\u2019re **for**, and spend time there.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A campaign group forms to fight **a company\u2019s secrecy**. Ten years on, it vets its members, briefs against rivals and keeps its own decisions quiet. Nobody voted for this. So **how did it happen**?'},{kind:'story',text:'The warning sits in the book\u2019s fourth part, a run of short sayings with no argument around them. Fight monsters, it says, and take care you don\u2019t turn into one. Stare long into the abyss, and it\u2019s studying you too.'},{kind:'explain',text:'To fight an opponent, you adopt their **terms, tactics and tempo**. You answer their moves on their timetable. Those habits stick. The campaign group took **its shape** from fighting, so it came to resemble the thing it fought.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The abyss half is about **attention**. What you look at persistently reshapes you, whatever it is. So the real question is where your attention goes, and **whether you chose** it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Long opposition shapes you **into its target**. Long attention shapes you too. So spend your attention **on purpose**, and know what you\u2019re for.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'The line about **staring into the abyss** is quoted to the point of meaninglessness. Read in place, it\u2019s a specific warning. Who was it **aimed at**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'It\u2019s aimed at people whose work is **opposition**. Opposing someone means meeting them on their terms and at their tempo. A movement defined only by its enemy is **trained by that enemy** every day.'},{kind:'story',text:'Ava can\u2019t stand how Zoe bosses people around. So she starts a group to stop her. Within a term, Ava decides who can join and who can\u2019t. She\u2019s bossing people, exactly as Zoe did.'},{kind:'story',text:'The abyss half is stranger. Someone watches furious videos every evening, about people they despise. The videos aren\u2019t evil. But a year of watching has made them **angrier** than anyone they watch. Attention shaped them, whatever its object.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The saying is easily turned against anyone who opposes **a real wrong**. Some fights must be fought, and fighting isn\u2019t copying. A historian of tyranny doesn\u2019t become a tyrant. It\u2019s drift, not destiny.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You\u2019ll be shaped by **what you oppose** and by what you watch. So choose both deliberately, and write down what you\u2019re **actually for**.'}]},'beyond-good-and-evil_5':{title:'Love Needs No Rules',card:{insight:'When we act out of love, we do not stop to think about rules.',tryThis:'Tonight, do one kind thing for a person you love before they ask.',skills:['psychology','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'Your kid gets sick at night, and you are up at once. Why?',right:'You act from love, so you need no rule.',wrong:'You weigh your duty fast and do the right thing.',why:'Love moved you before any rule could.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your little brother falls off his bike. You **run to help**. Do you stop first and think, it\u2019s **my duty** to help him? Or do your legs just go?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Nietzsche\u2019s 1886 book attacks one big idea after another. But in the middle sits **one gentle line**. What we do out of love, it says, is **beyond good and evil**. Rules come in when love is missing.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When you act from love, you don\u2019t ask if it\u2019s **right**. You just do it. **Rules** are for when the love isn\u2019t there.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your child is ill in the night. You\u2019re up **before you\u2019ve decided** anything. Later someone praises you for being so dutiful. Why does that word sound **slightly wrong**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Among the short sayings in the book\u2019s fourth part is the one that gives it **its title**. What\u2019s done from love, it says, happens beyond good and evil. It\u2019s one of the few unguarded lines in the whole book.'},{kind:'explain',text:'He isn\u2019t saying love excuses harm. He\u2019s saying **nobody deliberates** about helping someone they love. The machinery of duty, weighing and restraint never starts. So morality is what we reach for when **the motive is missing**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Where love supplies the motive, **duty goes quiet**. Rules are what you need when it\u2019s absent. That says something about **what morality is for**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture a book that spends **section after section** knocking down moral certainties. Then, in among the short sayings, one line turns **almost generous**. Why would a demolisher leave that in?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The line says acts done from love happen **beyond good and evil**. Their doers don\u2019t experience them as moral choices at all. If moral language drops out exactly where behaviour is best, it\u2019s doing **something else**.'},{kind:'story',text:'You hear a friend has been in an accident. You\u2019re in the car before you\u2019ve thought. Nobody weighs obligations at a moment like that. If someone called you **dutiful** afterwards, it would sound like a misunderstanding.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now compare a reluctant volunteer on a rota. He checks the list, weighs his week and turns up because he should. Here the full vocabulary switches on: duty, fairness, guilt. It\u2019s needed because **the motive isn\u2019t there**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The line has been used as permission for anything done with enough feeling. Possessive parents and jealous partners act from something they call love, and the harm is **still harm**. It describes, it doesn\u2019t license.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Nietzsche builds no system, so his alternative shows **in the gaps**. When a relentless writer goes gentle, mark the spot. That\u2019s where you\u2019ll see **what they value**.'}]},'beyond-good-and-evil_6':{title:'Two Kinds of Good',card:{insight:'If you only know what you are against, you will always need an enemy.',tryThis:'Write what you love about your work. Name no one you are against.',skills:['thinking','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'A new store says it is just not like the big chains. What is missing?',right:'What it is good at, with no enemy needed.',wrong:'A sharper attack on the big chains.',why:'Good that starts from what you love can stand alone.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'One football team says, we pass well and we **never give up**. Another says, we\u2019re not cheats like the Rovers. Both sound proud. Which team **knows who it is**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Nietzsche asked where our ideas of good come from. Some start from **pride** in what you are. Others start from **a hurt**, so good just means not like them. The second team needs the Rovers to know itself.'},{kind:'moral',text:'If your idea of good is only **not like them**, you\u2019ll always need a them. Good that starts from **what you love** stands on its own.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two small firms write down their values. One lists careful work and **plain dealing**. The other says it\u2019s not like the big banks: it doesn\u2019t exploit or hide fees. Both sound principled. Which would survive **the banks vanishing**?'},{kind:'story',text:'In the book\u2019s last part, on what is noble, Nietzsche says two types of morality **keep recurring**. One starts from good and bad: the strong naming what they admire in themselves. The other starts from good and evil.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The difference is **where the value starts**: in yourself, or in **an enemy**.',left:{title:'Good and bad',items:['Starts from what you admire','Names itself first','Bad is an afterthought']},right:{title:'Good and evil',items:['Starts from what was done','Names the enemy first','Good is the negation']}},{kind:'explain',text:'He grants the second kind produced **subtlety and depth**. His objection is to it being presented as morality itself, origin forgotten. You don\u2019t need his verdict to see the structure. A value built from **a grievance** carries it along.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Check where **your strong positions** start. Can you describe them without mentioning anyone who wronged you? If not, they may need **an enemy** to survive.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Take a moral position you hold fiercely. Now describe it without mentioning **anyone who hurt** you or your side. Is that easy, or does the whole thing **fall apart**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Nietzsche names two recurring types: **master morality** and **slave morality**. The first affirms itself and calls what it isn\u2019t bad. The second calls the powerful evil, then defines good as **its negation**. He develops this the following year in On the Genealogy of Morality.'},{kind:'story',text:'A small workshop describes itself as careful, honest and finished on time. You could print that list in a world with no competitors. It would still make sense. It starts from **what they admire**.'},{kind:'story',text:'A rival shop defines itself as not like the chains: no tricks, no hidden fees. Remove the chains and the description **goes blank**. It took its whole identity from an opponent, and needs one to keep going.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Nietzsche writes with **open contempt** for equality and the weak, and that shouldn\u2019t be softened. Morality built from grievance has also driven just reform. The wronged are often right. Use the structure, not his ranking.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A value built from admiration **stands alone**. One built from grievance carries the grievance with it. Know which kind **you\u2019re holding** before you act on it.'}]},'beyond-good-and-evil_7':{title:'Use the Test on Yourself',card:{insight:'If you judge others by a rule, judge yourself by it too.',tryThis:'Next time you call someone biased, say out loud how you might be biased too.',skills:['thinking','communication']},review:{prompt:'You say a coworker likes her plan only because it helps her. What next?',right:'Say out loud how I might be biased too.',wrong:'Find more proof that her plan only helps her.',why:'If the rule fits her, it fits you too.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Max tells his team the ref was unfair, because she\u2019s **friends with** the other coach. Then Max\u2019s mum agrees to ref the next game. Max says that\u2019s **totally different**. Is it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Nietzsche said physics, the science of how things move, is **one way of seeing** the world. Then he spotted the catch. His own view was one way of seeing too. He agreed, and said that was **all the better**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'If you have a rule for judging others, **use it on yourself** too. Say so **out loud**. That\u2019s what makes the rule fair.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You tell a meeting the sales forecast is too rosy, because **the team\u2019s bonuses** depend on it. Heads nod. Then someone asks about your own estimate. Who gains from **your caution**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Early in the book, Nietzsche argues that physics is an interpretation of the world. It arranges things but doesn\u2019t explain them. Later he offers his own reading of nature. He expects the reply that this is only interpretation too.'},{kind:'explain',text:'His answer is that this would be **all the better**. He isn\u2019t being modest. He\u2019s being consistent. A tool that spares its user isn\u2019t a tool. It\u2019s **a weapon** with a handle on one side.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A sceptical tool has to work **on its user** too. So say it before anyone else does: your caution protects **your department**. Then the argument is about numbers.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Nietzsche spends a book arguing that philosophies are confessions and that truth-seeking hides **other motives**. Sooner or later someone points the finger back at him. What does he do when **the finger turns**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'He accepts it, every time. His philosophy is a confession too. His reading of nature is **only interpretation** too, and he says that\u2019s all the better. Any critique of bias either applies to **its author** or becomes an example of the thing it attacks.'},{kind:'story',text:'You argue the forecast is optimistic because bonuses depend on it. Before anyone else can, you add that your own figure is cautious because caution **protects you**. The meeting relaxes. Now it\u2019s about numbers, not motives.'},{kind:'story',text:'Compare a critic who calls every study funded by industry **hopelessly biased**. His own work is funded by a rival cause, and he never mentions it. When someone finally does, his whole case collapses at once.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Self-application can become **a dodge**. If every objection is simply welcomed, nothing could count against the view. And admitting you\u2019re biased doesn\u2019t show you\u2019re right. The standard is necessary, not sufficient.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Some readers call his concession **a collapse**, others the book\u2019s most honest move. Either way, the rule stands. Point your sceptical tools **at yourself** first, out loud.'}]},'beyond-good-and-evil_8':{title:'Writing Behind a Mask',card:{insight:'Some writers make their work hard to keep out readers in a rush.',tryThis:'When a book is hard, read one short part twice before you quit.',skills:['thinking','communication']},review:{prompt:'A book is hard and you cannot skim it. What do you ask?',right:'Is this hard on purpose, so I must go slow?',wrong:'Where can I find a quick summary of it?',why:'Some books are hard on purpose, so read slowly before you judge.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your friends start a club with **a secret password**. Anyone can join, but first they must crack a clue. The clue is hard on purpose. Why make it **so hard**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Nietzsche wrote his 1886 book in **short, strong bits**, not one smooth chain of reasons. You can\u2019t skim it. You have to slow down and fit the pieces together. That hard style was **his mask**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Some writing is hard because the writer **wasn\u2019t very good**. Some is hard **on purpose**. Before you give up, ask which one it is.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You open a book of philosophy expecting chapters. Instead you get **numbered fragments**, short and overstated, often at odds with each other. You try skimming the first lines. Nothing comes out. Is the book **badly written**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Nietzsche held that every deep mind **wants a disguise**. He even claimed a deep thinker would rather be misread than truly understood. His style follows from that. It\u2019s built to repel anyone unwilling to go slowly.'},{kind:'explain',text:'So the difficulty is **a deliberate filter**. No argument is laid out for you to extract. There are positions, and you assemble them. The reader who won\u2019t slow down gets **nothing usable**, as intended.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Obscurity can be **failure or filter**. Ask which before you decide. And when it\u2019s a filter, the only way through is **to slow down**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Nietzsche built a book to be **hard to quote**: fragments, overstatement, no laid-out argument. Today his lines circulate everywhere. How did such a guarded writer become one of the **most quoted**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Two passages frame it. In one, deep things and deep people **seek disguises**. In the other, a deep thinker would rather be misread than understood. Being misread only stings his pride. Being understood makes him **ache for** the reader.'},{kind:'story',text:'Try reading him the way you\u2019d skim a report: first line of each paragraph. You\u2019ll get nothing, because **no argument** is laid out. The positions only cohere once you assemble them yourself, slowly.'},{kind:'story',text:'His plea for slow reading comes from a preface written that same year. It was for an earlier book, Daybreak. It fits this one too. The reader in a hurry was never **the reader** he wanted.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The filter **failed comprehensively**. His lines now circulate stripped of exactly the context the form was built to enforce. And deliberate difficulty can shield muddled writing from criticism. Obscurity has to earn the benefit of the doubt.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Difficulty can be **a filter** rather than a failure. But a filter can fail too, and it can hide **bad writing**. So ask what it\u2019s for, then judge.'}]},'beyond-good-and-evil_9':{title:'The Sleep-Making Power',card:{insight:'An answer that just gives a thing a new name tells you nothing.',tryThis:'When someone says why a thing went wrong, ask what is new in that answer.',skills:['thinking','science']},review:{prompt:'A report says the project failed because the team lacked grit. What do you ask?',right:'What new thing does that answer tell us?',wrong:'How can we give the team more grit?',why:'A new name for a thing is not a reason for it.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Why is Jack so **good at maths**? Because he has a maths brain. But what\u2019s a maths brain? Just being good at maths. Did that answer **tell you anything**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Long ago, the French playwright Moli\xe8re wrote a comedy. A student doctor is asked why a drug makes people sleep. He says it has **a power** to make them sleep. Nietzsche borrowed that joke, and used it **in his book**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Some answers only give a thing **a new name**. A name isn\u2019t a reason. So ask what the answer told you that you **didn\u2019t already know**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A project fails. The review concludes the team **lacked resilience**, and everyone nods. But how do you know they lacked resilience? Because the project failed. Has anyone actually **explained anything**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Nietzsche borrows a joke from Moli\xe8re\u2019s comedy The Imaginary Invalid. A medical student is asked why opium puts people to sleep. He answers that it has a **sleep-making power**. The examiners applaud. Nietzsche aims the joke at Kant.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Kant had explained a kind of knowledge by **a faculty** for it. That\u2019s the student\u2019s answer again. The test is simple: does the explanation **predict** anything the failure or the sleep didn\u2019t already show? If not, it\u2019s just a name.'},{kind:'moral',text:'An answer with **the grammar** of an explanation can have none of the content. Naming the pattern again **closes the question**. Ask what it predicts before you nod.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Why does opium make people sleep? Because it has a **sleep-making power**. You laugh. Now: why did the project fail? Because the team lacked resilience. Why does that answer feel **so much better**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Nietzsche\u2019s target is the answer that **renames its object**. It has the grammar of an explanation and none of the content. His test: does it predict anything the thing itself didn\u2019t already show? If not, **nothing was learned**.'},{kind:'story',text:'His own case was Kant. Kant asked how certain judgements, known apart from experience yet adding knowledge, are possible. His answer, as Nietzsche tells it, was by means of **a faculty**. Moli\xe8re\u2019s student would have approved.'},{kind:'story',text:'The modern cases are comfortable. A behaviour put down to culture. An outcome put down to mindset. A failure put down to a lack of resilience. Each names the pattern again and **closes the question**. You stop asking.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Not every name is empty. A name for an unknown cause can be **an honest placeholder**. Biologists named the unit of heredity long before they knew what it was made of. The fault is treating the placeholder as the answer.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Nietzsche\u2019s sharper point is that **closing the question** is what such answers are for. They feel like knowledge. So when you\u2019re handed one, ask **what it predicts**.'}]}}}},949,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'carnegie-autobiography',kind:'book',promise:'Run your work like a careful owner',about:'How did a poor boy from Scotland get rich making steel? Andrew Carnegie told the story himself. He shows how he ran his business. He also skips some hard parts. Which of his rules will you use?',ideas:{'carnegie-autobiography_1':{title:'The Cost of One Ton',card:{insight:'Know what one costs you to make, and you know your lowest safe price.',tryThis:'Pick one thing you make or sell. Guess what one costs you, then add up what goes into it.',skills:['money','thinking']},review:{prompt:'Your coffee shop made a small profit last year. How do you find what makes money?',right:'Add up what each thing costs you to make',wrong:'Check this year\u2019s total profit against last year\u2019s',why:'One big total can hide the things that lose money.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You sell cakes at the school fair for **50p** each. Every cake goes, and it feels like **a great day**. But you never added up the flour and eggs. Did you make any money?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Andrew Carnegie made steel, the strong metal for railways and bridges. He kept asking what **one ton** cost him to make. His rivals couldn\u2019t say. So in bad years, he knew **how low** his price could safely go.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Add up what **one of anything** costs you. Then you\u2019ll know which price is safe. Guessing feels fine until **a bad year** arrives.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Demand for what you make **suddenly collapses**. Every firm in your trade has to cut prices. How low can you go before each sale **loses money**? Most owners, if they\u2019re honest, can only guess.'},{kind:'story',text:'Carnegie learned his habit on the Pennsylvania Railroad, which kept detailed cost systems because it had to. He carried it into steel. There, firms knew only their **year-end totals**. He kept asking what each process cost. Rivals couldn\u2019t answer.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Two firms can report the **same profit**. Only one knows **where it came from**.',left:{title:'Yearly total',items:['Profit seen once a year','Losses hidden inside','Price cuts are gambles']},right:{title:'Cost per ton',items:['A figure per process','Losses show up','A known floor price']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s why rivals **didn\u2019t copy** it. A new furnace can be seen and bought. A cost system sits **inside the books**, out of sight. Nobody feels pressure to match what they can\u2019t see.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Know what **each unit** costs you, process by process. Then you can price, cut and improve **on real figures**, while others guess.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Carnegie wasn\u2019t trained in steel. He started out on **a railway**. When he moved into steel, he found an industry run on **year-end totals**. What did he bring with him?'},{kind:'explain',text:'He brought a habit: knowing the **unit cost**, what it takes to make one ton, process by process. Unlike a yearly profit, a unit cost can\u2019t hide **a losing step** inside a winning year.'},{kind:'story',text:'The railway had built detailed cost systems out of necessity. Carnegie carried them into steel. He asked again and again what each process cost, and found his competitors couldn\u2019t say. In a downturn, he could price **below them** and survive.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Why did the edge **last so long**? A new furnace is visible, so rivals order one. A measurement habit lives **in the books**. Competitors who don\u2019t measure can\u2019t see what they\u2019re missing.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture your own caf\xe9. You know last year\u2019s profit, but not what each cake costs. You work it out one evening. Two items lose money every time they sell. The total had hidden them.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. A cost sheet counts what\u2019s easy to count. Carnegie\u2019s low costs rested partly on **twelve-hour days** and wage cuts, which the sheet records as savings. It shows where cost lives, not whether cutting it is right.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Measure **one unit** at a time and you\u2019ll see what totals hide. That edge is dull, quiet and **hard to copy**. Just remember what the sheet leaves out.'}]},'carnegie-autobiography_2':{title:'Watch One Basket',card:{insight:'If you run it yourself, keep your time on one thing and watch it.',tryThis:'List where your work time goes this week. Circle one side job that pulls you away.',skills:['focus','money']},review:{prompt:'A woman who runs a bakery wants to open a gym on the side. Your advice?',right:'Keep her time on the bakery and watch it',wrong:'Open the gym, since two jobs are safer than one',why:'When you run it yourself, a second job splits the time that keeps one going.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Everyone says never put all your eggs in **one basket**. Andrew Carnegie, a steel maker, said the opposite: put them all in one, then **watch that basket**. Could you both be right?'},{kind:'story',text:'Say you run a stall at the school fair. You could run three stalls. But you can only stand at **one**. The other two run out of change. One stall you watch does better than **three you leave**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When **you run it**, your attention makes it work, so keep it in one place. When you\u2019re **just saving**, spreading out is wiser.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A baker\u2019s shop is growing. To **spread her risk**, she opens a caf\xe9 and a catering arm too. A year later, all three are wobbling. What did she **actually spread**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Carnegie turned the usual advice round. Put all your eggs in **one basket**, he said, and then **watch that basket**. To him, spreading out was a strategy for people who can\u2019t watch. The watching is the whole condition.'},{kind:'story',text:'Back to the baker. Each business now gets **a third** of her week. The bakery\u2019s costs drift. A supplier problem goes unnoticed for a month. Her risk didn\u2019t spread at all. **Her attention** did.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The advice flips depending on **which job** you\u2019re doing with **the basket**.',left:{title:'The operator',items:['Runs it day to day','Can watch one thing closely','Concentrates to succeed']},right:{title:'The investor',items:['Puts money in, stays out','Can\u2019t watch every holding','Spreads to survive surprises']}},{kind:'moral',text:'If you\u2019re **running it**, attention is the scarce input, so concentrate and watch. If you\u2019re only **holding it**, spread out. Know which one you are.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Most advice tells you to **spread your bets**. One of the richest men of his age told readers the reverse. Was Carnegie simply lucky, or was he answering **a different question**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'His target was narrow. He spoke to the man **building a business**. For that person, attention is scarcer than capital. Every extra venture takes **a slice** of the noticing that makes one succeed.'},{kind:'story',text:'Carnegie\u2019s own edges came from watching one industry very closely. His cost figures, his timing, his readiness to scrap old plant all needed **constant attention**. Spread across many trades, that kind of noticing thins out.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take your savings. You don\u2019t run those companies and can\u2019t watch them. Putting everything in one share buys you **no extra attention**. It only buys extra exposure. Here the usual advice wins.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The advice comes from someone it worked for. People it ruined **didn\u2019t write memoirs**. And Carnegie never separates building a fortune from keeping one. Close watching can\u2019t save a single basket that falls.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Concentrate **your effort** where you can watch it daily. Spread **your savings** where you can\u2019t. The basket is only safe while someone\u2019s watching.'}]},'carnegie-autobiography_3':{title:'The Telegraph Office',card:{insight:'Being close to the boss can count as much as hard work.',tryThis:'Write down who makes the big choices where you work. Name one way to work closer to them.',skills:['career']},review:{prompt:'One job pays more. The other sits beside the people who decide. What do you weigh?',right:'How close each job is to the people who decide',wrong:'Which job pays more, since pay is the clearest sign',why:'Near the people who decide, you learn how things work and they see you.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture a poor boy, about **fourteen**, new to Pittsburgh from Scotland. His job is running telegrams round the city. It sounds like nothing much. Could a job like that **change your whole life**?'},{kind:'story',text:'The boy was Andrew Carnegie. He carried messages to **the most important** people in town, so he learned who they were. He learned to read the clicks **by ear**. A railway boss, Thomas Scott, noticed and hired him.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Working hard matters. But **where you stand** matters too, and Carnegie\u2019s job put him **next to the deciders**. Don\u2019t forget the luck of being there.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two job offers. One pays **15% more**. The other pays less, but sits right beside the people who make **the firm\u2019s decisions**. In ten years, which one will matter more to you?'},{kind:'story',text:'Carnegie started as a messenger in a Pittsburgh telegraph office. He learned to take messages by ear and became an operator. Thomas Scott of the Pennsylvania Railroad made him his own telegrapher. The railway, the investments and steel followed.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Why does nearness pay? Close to **the decisions**, you see how they\u2019re really made and who makes them. Those people **see you** too. Ability nobody important sees is easily overlooked and rarely trusted.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Carnegie is honest about this. The persistence was real. So was **the accident** of being in that office at that moment. By his own account, **the position** did more than any later decision.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Effort counts, but so does **where you sit**. When you choose a role, weigh its **nearness to decisions** against its pay. Ten years on, it often counts for more.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Samuel Smiles\u2019s Self-Help puts success down to **effort and persistence**. Its method tends to leave the luck out. Carnegie\u2019s story of his own start keeps both in. So what was **the lucky part**?'},{kind:'story',text:'He carried telegrams to the city\u2019s businesses, then learned to take messages by ear and became an operator. Thomas Scott of the Pennsylvania Railroad hired him as his personal telegrapher. Scott\u2019s backing brought railway management and his first investments.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Position compounds because **information moves** along a network. A boy handling every message learns who\u2019s doing what before almost anyone. Being seen by the deciders turns ability into chances, and each chance brings **more nearness**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a graduate choosing between two offers. One is a well-paid analyst job in a distant back office. The other is a smaller salary, assisting the chief executive. The second puts her where decisions get made.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Many boys carried telegrams, and few became Carnegie. Skill and drive mattered, and his later choices weren\u2019t trivial. Hindsight flatters a post too. Push the lesson far and you\u2019re **courting the powerful** instead of getting good.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Carnegie\u2019s rise had **two parts**: real persistence, and the luck of standing in that office. Keep both when you read it. And when you choose a role, weigh **where it sits**.'}]},'carnegie-autobiography_4':{title:'Buy in Hard Times',card:{insight:'To buy in hard times, you need cash first and courage second.',tryThis:'Write down what you could buy if prices dropped by half next month. Then ask if you would dare.',skills:['money']},review:{prompt:'In hard times, a rival sells his machines cheap. What do you check first?',right:'First, whether you have the cash to buy them',wrong:'Whether you feel brave enough to buy right now',why:'Cash comes first, because being bold with no cash can sink you.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'In December a coat costs **\xa360**. In the January sale, the same coat costs \xa330. Lots of people want it. So why can only **some of them** buy it?'},{kind:'story',text:'Andrew Carnegie made steel. In the 1870s, business got very bad and most firms stopped building. Carnegie built a **huge new** steel works, cheaply, because nobody else was. When good times came back, he was bigger than ever.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Hard times make things cheap, but only for **people who saved**. First you need the money. Then you need **the nerve** to spend it when others won\u2019t.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A recession hits. Building and equipment costs **fall sharply**. One firm spends the slump cutting back. Another buys **a rival\u2019s plant** for a fraction of its price. What did the second firm have that you might not?'},{kind:'story',text:'Carnegie expanded hardest in downturns, when construction and equipment were cheapest and rivals were retrenching. He built through the depressions of the 1870s and 1890s. Each time, he came out with **cheap capacity** and a bigger share of a recovering market.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Where did the cash come from? He kept **debt low** and ploughed profits back instead of paying them out. In good years that looked timid. In a slump, it\u2019s what made **the boldness** possible.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The order matters. **Cash** without **nerve** is a missed chance, and nerve without cash is bravado.',left:{title:'Rule one: cash',items:['Keep debt low','Reinvest profits','Built in good years']},right:{title:'Rule two: nerve',items:['Spend at the bottom','Act while others retreat']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Expanding in a downturn takes **cash and nerve**, in that order. The nerve gets the credit. But **the cash** you build quietly in good years is what makes it possible.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Everyone knows you should **buy when it\u2019s cheap**. Firms read the same textbooks and the same news. Yet in every slump, almost all of them cut back. Why is **the obvious move** so rare?'},{kind:'explain',text:'It needs **two things at once**. One is cash at the bottom of the cycle. The other is the nerve to spend it when every signal says hold on. Firms that borrowed in the boom **have no cash**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Carnegie had both. He kept debt low and reinvested profits, which looked conservative in good years. Then the depressions of the 1870s and 1890s came. He built hard, buying capacity at **a fraction** of its boom-time cost.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a small firm in a slump. A rival\u2019s equipment comes up for sale cheaply. The owner is keen. But the firm owes the bank heavily. Buying would leave it **no cushion** if the slump drags on.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Survivors tell this story. Some who expanded into a slump ran out of cash first, or built for a market that **never came back**. You only know a downturn was temporary afterwards.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Boldness in a slump is decided **years earlier**, by how much cash you keep. Build **the cushion** first. Then you\u2019ll have a real choice when prices fall.'}]},'carnegie-autobiography_5':{title:'What the Book Leaves Out',card:{insight:'People who tell their own story leave things out, so check the short parts.',tryThis:'Pick a life story or r\xe9sum\xe9 you know well. Find a big stretch of time that gets one line.',skills:['thinking','history']},review:{prompt:'A boss\u2019s life story gives forty pages to a deal, one line to layoffs. Now what?',right:'Trust the rest, but check the short part',wrong:'Trust all of it, since he was there himself',why:'People tell the easy parts in full and rush the parts that hurt them.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your little brother tells Mum about a fight. You took his game, he says, and you shouted. **All true**. But he skips one part: he **broke your model** first.'},{kind:'story',text:'Andrew Carnegie wrote his life story. In 1892, armed guards hired by his steel firm fought the workers, and people died. His book says **very little** about it. The workers **hardly get a word**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When people tell **their own story**, they choose what goes in. So notice **what\u2019s left out**, and how much. The gaps tell you a lot.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Who knows a life better than the person **who lived it**? That\u2019s why memoirs feel trustworthy. But the author also has **the most at stake**. So how far should you trust one?'},{kind:'story',text:'In 1892 a dispute at Carnegie\u2019s Homestead works became a lockout. Armed Pinkerton guards, hired by the company, fought the workers, and men were killed. Carnegie\u2019s book handles it **briefly**, blaming the officials on the spot. He was away in Scotland.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The same book earns **two kinds** of trust, depending on **what it costs** him to be candid.',left:{title:'On method',items:['Costs, timing, partnerships','Detailed and specific','Little reason to bend']},right:{title:'On labour',items:['Homestead kept brief','Wage cuts unexamined','A legacy being managed']}},{kind:'explain',text:'So read it as **a case being made**. Notice where the space goes and where it doesn\u2019t. A gap isn\u2019t proof of anything, but it\u2019s **evidence**, and it tells you which passages to check.'},{kind:'moral',text:'An autobiography is **candid** where candour is cheap and **brief** where it costs. Trust its method pages. Treat its silences as clues, not verdicts.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Carnegie wrote his autobiography late in life, and it appeared in 1920. It\u2019s **frank about method**. On one episode, the one that did most damage to his name, it goes **almost quiet**. Why?'},{kind:'story',text:'At Homestead, a dispute became a lockout. Henry Clay Frick, running the company, brought in armed Pinkerton guards. Men died on both sides in the fight that followed, and the state militia was sent in. Carnegie was in Scotland.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The book handles it briefly and **at a distance**, with responsibility on the people on the spot. The wage cuts before it go unexamined. So do the **twelve-hour days** behind the cost figures he reports with pride.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a former chief executive\u2019s memoir. Forty pages cover a merger. One paragraph covers the factory closures that followed. You don\u2019t have to call it lies. The space tells you where the reputation work is happening.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Reading for gaps can become **its own bias**. Brevity isn\u2019t always evasion: an author may say little from regret, or from not seeing events himself. An absence needs outside sources before it\u2019s a conclusion.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Gaps don\u2019t make the rest false. Give **the method passages** your trust and **the silences** your questions. One book can be candid and managed at once.'}]},'carnegie-autobiography_6':{title:'Let Someone Go First',card:{insight:'Let others pay to find the problems, then move fast once it works.',tryThis:'Name one new tool people at work are excited about. Write down if you will try it now or wait.',skills:['thinking','money']},review:{prompt:'A company waits for others to prove a new way works. When is that smart?',right:'When it can move fast and build bigger than the first one',wrong:'Any time it wants to save money and stay safe',why:'Waiting only pays if you can move fast once it works.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A brand new video game comes out. It\u2019s **expensive**, and players say it **keeps crashing**. Your friends rush to buy it on day one. Should you?'},{kind:'story',text:'Andrew Carnegie made steel in America. A new way of making it, the Bessemer process, was invented in England. Carnegie didn\u2019t rush in. He waited until others showed it worked. Then he built with it, **bigger and faster** than anyone.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Going first costs a lot, because **the first one** finds the problems. You can wait and learn. But waiting only works if you **move fast** afterwards.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Who gets rich from **a new technology**? The inventor seems the obvious answer. They had the idea and took the risk. Carnegie made a rule of betting **on someone else**. Why?'},{kind:'story',text:'The Bessemer process, a British invention, made steel far more cheaply. Carnegie didn\u2019t back it early. He let others take the risk of making it work. Once it was proved, he adopted it at scale **faster than anyone**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'His rule was that **pioneering doesn\u2019t pay**. The first mover pays to discover what fails. The second buys the proven version, faults removed. The profit goes to whoever **builds it at scale**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'There\u2019s a half he **doesn\u2019t state**. Following only pays if you can outbuild the pioneer once the proof exists. Carnegie could commit huge sums quickly. A firm that can\u2019t **isn\u2019t a follower**. It\u2019s just late.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When something new appears, decide whether **you\u2019re funding** the discovery or buying the debugged version. Waiting is a strategy only if you can **then move fast**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Among the book\u2019s most **contrarian rules** is one that cuts against instinct. Let somebody else be first. Watch **what it costs them**. Is that wisdom, or just a clever way of arriving late?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The logic is simple. The first mover bears **the cost of discovery**: every fault, every failed batch. The second entrant buys **proven technology** more cheaply, with the failures already gone. That describes most technologies since.'},{kind:'story',text:'Carnegie applied it to Bessemer steel. He waited while others proved the process. Then he adopted it at scale faster than anyone, putting enormous sums to work quickly. He held to the rule consistently and said so plainly.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a small firm that waits for a new method to be proven. When the proof arrives, it needs two years to raise money and build. By then, a bigger rival has **taken the market**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some markets reward being first: where customers **lock in**, or a network grows with size, or patents block followers. Waiting there can mean you never get in. And if everyone follows, nothing gets proved.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Fast following is **two moves**: wait, then outbuild. Skip the second and you\u2019re simply late. Before you wait, check you can **move fast** when the proof comes.'}]},'carnegie-autobiography_7':{title:'Compare With the Best',card:{insight:'Compare what you have with the best choice, not with having nothing.',tryThis:'Pick one thing you keep because it still works. Find the best choice and what the gap costs you.',skills:['thinking','money']},review:{prompt:'Your old vans run fine, but new ones use half the gas. What decides it?',right:'The gap between the old vans and the best choice',wrong:'Whether the old vans still work and make money',why:'A thing that still works looks good until you compare it with the best.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your way to school takes **thirty minutes**. It works fine. Then a friend shows you a way that takes fifteen. Your old way **isn\u2019t broken**. Should you keep it?'},{kind:'story',text:'Andrew Carnegie made steel. When a better machine came along, he ripped out his old ones. They **still worked** and still made money. Other steel makers thought he was mad. Soon he could **sell for less** than them.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t just ask if something **still works**. Ask if it beats **the best one** you could have. A thing that works can still be costing you.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your team\u2019s ordering system is **ten years old**, long paid for, and it still does the job. A newer one would **halve the time** each order takes. Someone asks: why throw away something that works?'},{kind:'story',text:'Carnegie kept doing what his rivals called madness. He tore out machinery that was profitable and not worn out, to install better equipment. They kept their working plant. Then the man who\u2019d written his off **undercut them**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Your old system wins or loses depending on **which comparison** you make. Pick **the honest one**.',left:{title:'Against nothing',items:['It works','It\u2019s paid for','Keeping it looks free']},right:{title:'Against the best',items:['Slower every order','Costs you daily','The gap is real']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Why do so few people switch? The **accounting is simple**. The psychology isn\u2019t. Scrapping something that works feels like destroying value. The loss is concrete and immediate, while the gain is **only projected**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A plant that earns money can still be **costing you money** against the alternative. So compare what you have with **the best option**, not with nothing.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Carnegie\u2019s competitors thought he\u2019d lost his mind. He kept tearing out **profitable machinery** that wasn\u2019t worn out. They kept theirs running. In the end, who was **undercutting whom**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The idea is **opportunity cost**: the value of the best alternative you give up. A machine\u2019s real comparison isn\u2019t its profit against zero. It\u2019s against what **the best machine** would earn in its place.'},{kind:'story',text:'Carnegie could make that comparison for real. His cost figures told him what the old plant cost per ton and what the new one would. Without those numbers, scrapping working machinery is **a gamble** that merely looks bold.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a manager defending an old machine. We paid a fortune for it, she says, so we must get our money\u2019s worth. But that money is gone either way. Only **the future** is still on the table.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The rule needs the new option to be **truly better**. The switch must be costed honestly, including disruption and retraining. Constant replacement can chase small gains. And the workers tied to the old plant carry losses this sum ignores.'},{kind:'moral',text:'What you\u2019ve already paid can\u2019t be recovered. The only live comparison is between **the old thing\u2019s future** and the best alternative\u2019s. Make it **with real figures**, not with feelings.'}]},'carnegie-autobiography_8':{title:'Pay With a Share',card:{insight:'People act like owners when part of the result is theirs.',tryThis:'Pick someone you want acting like an owner. Write down what part of the result is theirs.',skills:['leadership','money']},review:{prompt:'A manager keeps spending too much on work trips. Which fix fits Carnegie\u2019s idea?',right:'Give her a real share, so the waste is partly hers',wrong:'Send her a firm note about the trip rules',why:'A share makes the waste partly hers, so she starts to watch costs.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You run a lemonade stand, and your friend helps. You could pay her **\xa32** for the day. Or you could give her **half** of what you make. Which deal makes her care about wasted lemons?'},{kind:'story',text:'Andrew Carnegie owned big steel works. He didn\u2019t just pay his best managers a wage. He gave them **a part** of the business. Some started with no money and ended up rich. But if one left, the firm could buy his share back cheaply.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Give someone **a real share**, and every waste costs them too. That\u2019s why they start to care. Just check **who wrote the rules**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You need a manager to **cut costs** at a plant you own. You could offer a salary, a bonus for hitting a target, or **a share** of the business. Which one buys the most careful watching?'},{kind:'story',text:'Carnegie paid his best managers in partnership interests rather than large salaries. Charles Schwab started as a labourer at one of his works and rose to run the company. Through his share, he became **enormously wealthy**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'His reasoning was about **attention and retention**. A partner watches costs, because the waste is **partly his**. And a partner whose wealth is tied up in the firm doesn\u2019t walk off to a rival.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Now **read the terms**. The firm could buy back a partner\u2019s interest at book value, often far below its real worth. Control stayed with Carnegie. The alignment was real, but it ran **one way**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'If you want someone to **decide like an owner**, give them a real share of the outcome. And when you design the deal, be honest about **who keeps the growth**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture a young labourer at a steel works with **no fortune** at all. Later he\u2019s running the company and **enormously rich**. He wasn\u2019t paid a huge salary. So how did he get there?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The man was Charles Schwab, and the method was **partnership**. Carnegie gave his best managers a share instead of large salaries. It bought **attention and loyalty** at almost no cash cost, because owners watch costs and stay put.'},{kind:'story',text:'Inside the mills, this changed behaviour. The men running each works carried the consequences of their own decisions. Waste came partly out of **their own pockets**. Carnegie presents the system as central to his success.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a start-up that offers you equity. If you leave, the founders can buy it back at the original price. You carry the outcome while you stay. They keep the growth.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Carnegie\u2019s firm could buy a departing partner out at **book value**, far below what a growing firm would fetch. Partners carried the effort and the downside, but the buy-back capped what they took away.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Ownership makes people **decide like owners**. It worked for Carnegie, and he kept the terms in his hands. When you\u2019re offered a share, or offering one, read **who keeps the growth**.'}]},'carnegie-autobiography_9':{title:'The Talent Spotter',card:{insight:'Finding great people and keeping them is a real skill of its own.',tryThis:'Write what you are good at in one line. If it is finding great people, be proud of it.',skills:['leadership','career']},review:{prompt:'A hospital\u2019s boss is not a doctor. What is she most likely good at?',right:'Finding great doctors and making them want to stay',wrong:'Not much, since the doctors do all the hard work',why:'Spotting great work you can\u2019t do, and keeping those people, is a skill.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'In a group project, Mia **can\u2019t draw** and isn\u2019t great at maths. But she gets Sam, who draws brilliantly, and Leo, who loves numbers. Their project is **the best** in class. What was Mia\u2019s skill?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Andrew Carnegie got very rich making steel, but he **wasn\u2019t an expert** in steel. A line said to be his epitaph sums him up. He knew how to get people **better than him** to work for him.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You don\u2019t have to be **the expert** to be good at something. Finding great people and making them **glad to stay** is a skill. You can practise it.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Someone asks what **you\u2019re good at**. You\u2019re not the best engineer in the building. You\u2019re the one who gets the best ones working together, and keeps them. Why do you hesitate, as if it\u2019s **a confession**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Carnegie wasn\u2019t a metallurgist and didn\u2019t pretend to be. He found operators who could run the furnaces, Charles Schwab above all. He tied them in through partnership. His own part was costs, capital and timing.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The epitaph usually credited to him praises a man who knew how to **enlist people** better than himself. It\u2019s often read as modesty. It\u2019s closer to **a job description**: a claim about method.'},{kind:'explain',text:'It\u2019s a craft of its own. You need to **judge excellence** in work you don\u2019t do. Then you need terms that make staying **worth their while**. Both can be practised and improved.'},{kind:'moral',text:'If your strength is **finding and keeping** great people, stop apologising for it. It was Carnegie\u2019s real skill. Get better at it **on purpose**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Carnegie\u2019s name is tied to steel, Homestead and the **twelve-hour day**. Yet the line usually credited as his epitaph mentions **none of it**. It\u2019s about other people. Why would he be summed up that way?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Read straight, the line is **a claim about method**. He wasn\u2019t an engineer. His contributions were cost accounting, capital, timing, and finding exceptional operators. The epitaph names **the last of these**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Schwab is the clearest case. Carnegie picked him young, promoted him fast and bound him in through partnership. The technical excellence came from people like him. Carnegie\u2019s skill was seeing it and **making it stay**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now think of a hospital\u2019s chief executive who isn\u2019t a doctor. Her job is to know what good medicine looks like, hire it and keep it. If she does that well, you\u2019re lucky to have her.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The line also does a job. Where it first appeared is uncertain, but it fits his self-portrait. A man remembered for Homestead gets summed up by a sentence that\u2019s **true and incomplete**. And not being technical isn\u2019t a virtue by itself.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Take the line seriously and you\u2019ll see **a real skill**: judging excellence you lack, then keeping it. Take it at face value and you\u2019ll miss **what it hides**.'}]}}}},950,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'chemical-history-candle',kind:'book',promise:'Explain hard things so they stick',about:'How do you explain something hard and still tell the truth? Michael Faraday was a famous scientist in England. He gave six talks for kids about one candle. Here are nine ideas from those talks. Which one will you try?',ideas:{'chemical-history-candle_1':{title:'One Candle on the Bench',card:{insight:'Show people the real thing first, and give them the big words later.',tryThis:'Next time you explain something, show the real thing first. Say its name after they ask about it.',skills:['communication','science']},review:{prompt:'You are teaching a new coworker a hard report. Where do you start?',right:'Open the real report and let the questions come',wrong:'Start with the key words and what they mean',why:'The real thing brings up the questions, so each word comes when it is needed.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your teacher writes a **long new word** on the board. You copy it down. By lunch, it\u2019s gone from your head. What if she\u2019d brought in **something real** instead?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A scientist called Michael Faraday once gave talks to young people. He didn\u2019t start with hard words. He put **one lit candle** on the table. Every question the children asked came out of it, **one by one**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Start with **one real thing** people can see. Let the big ideas come later, when **you need them**. Then they\u2019ll stick.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A new colleague needs to learn your team\u2019s monthly report. You open a slide of **definitions** and start at the top. Twenty minutes later, their eyes have glazed. What would **a better start** look like?'},{kind:'story',text:'Faraday gave six Christmas lectures for young people, published in 1861. He didn\u2019t open with the periodic table. He set **one burning candle** before the room. He told them nothing offered a better way into the study of nature.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s why it works. A real object raises **questions in order**. Someone notices something odd and asks why, so the next idea arrives **because it\u2019s needed**. And every claim can be checked right there on the bench.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'A word given before **the thing** is just something to memorise. Given after, it **names** what you noticed.',left:{title:'Syllabus order',items:['Definitions first','Ideas come when next','Taken on trust']},right:{title:'Object order',items:['The thing first','Ideas come when needed','Checked on the spot']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Start with **the real thing**, whether that\u2019s the report, the email or the broken part. Hold **the summary** back until someone asks why. Then it lands.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A candle looks like the **dullest thing** in any room. Yet Faraday chose it to open six lectures on chemistry. He promised the young audience there was no better way in. What did he **see in it**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A burning candle holds **capillary action**, combustion, convection and the make-up of the air. All of it is happening on the table. So each question comes from the object, and each answer **raises the next**.'},{kind:'story',text:'In the lectures, the audience notices something about the candle and wants to know why. Each answer leads to a new question. Nothing is imported from a textbook. Every topic comes out of the object because the last answer raised it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture you\u2019re training someone on a new piece of software. Instead of a list of menus, you open a real customer file together. They ask what each button does as they need it. The menus stick because they mattered.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Not every subject has a candle. Some ideas in maths or law have no single object, and a forced example can mislead. The method is also **slow**, so it pays when understanding matters more than coverage.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Begin with **one real thing** and let it ask the questions for you. Each principle then arrives as an answer. That\u2019s why **it\u2019s remembered**.'}]},'chemical-history-candle_2':{title:'The Hollow Flame',card:{insight:'People remember what they see far longer than what they are told.',tryThis:'Find the one point people doubt in your next talk. Plan a quick way to show it.',skills:['communication','science']},review:{prompt:'You need your team to see that a step wastes time. What works best?',right:'Show the delay happening live, so they see it',wrong:'Tell them plainly that it wastes time',why:'Seeing it happen in front of you sticks far longer than being told.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Look closely at a candle flame. It seems like **one solid blob** of light, bright all the way through. But is it really? What might be hiding in **the dark middle**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Michael Faraday pushed a thin glass tube into the dark middle of a flame. Gas came out at the far end. He lit it, and a **second flame** popped up. The middle was full of fuel that hadn\u2019t burned yet.'},{kind:'moral',text:'If you want people to remember something, **show it happening**. Being told is easy to forget. Seeing it with **your own eyes** isn\u2019t.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Someone tells you a flame is **hollow**. You nod, then forget it by the weekend. Now someone lights **a second flame** using gas from the first. Which of those will you still remember next year?'},{kind:'story',text:'Faraday\u2019s audience had seen the dark space in the middle of a flame. He passed a glass tube into it. Vapour flowed along the tube, and he lit it at the far end. Fuel from one flame made **another**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s what it proves. The middle is wax vapour that hasn\u2019t met **enough air** yet. Burning only happens at **the boundary**, where fuel and oxygen meet. Nobody had to believe this. They watched the vapour come out.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The same fact lands very differently when it\u2019s **told** to you or **shown** to you.',left:{title:'Told',items:['A flat statement','You nod politely','Gone by next week']},right:{title:'Shown',items:['A second flame appears','You see the proof','Still there years later']}},{kind:'moral',text:'A demonstration can carry **the whole argument**. So find the claim people doubt, and look for a way to **show it happening**. Then you won\u2019t need to persuade.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Faraday\u2019s audience had already formed a picture: a flame is **solid light**. He didn\u2019t argue with them. He reached for **a glass tube**. Why would a teacher let the room be wrong first?'},{kind:'story',text:'He slid the tube into the dark centre of the flame. Unburnt vapour travelled along it and he lit it at the other end. One flame was feeding another, **across the bench**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Three things line up in **a model demonstration**. The first impression is wrong. The correction is **visible**, not argued. And the explanation comes after the correction, so it reads as a consequence. Remove one and it shrinks to an illustration.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now say you\u2019re asking your team to drop a slow approval step. You could list the delays. Or you could time one request live, in the meeting, while everyone waits. The waiting makes the argument for you.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some truths can\u2019t be shown on a bench: the very slow, the very large, **the statistical**. And a vivid demo can feel like proof of more than it shows. The tube proves only what\u2019s in the centre.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Let the first impression be wrong, then **show the correction**. The explanation that follows will feel like **your audience\u2019s own** discovery.'}]},'chemical-history-candle_3':{title:'The Self-Feeding Candle',card:{insight:'When something runs well on its own, find out what keeps it going.',tryThis:'Pick one thing that runs fine without you. Write one line on what keeps it steady.',skills:['thinking','science']},review:{prompt:'A weekly report has run fine for years. Before you count on it, what first?',right:'Name what keeps it steady before you depend on it',wrong:'Trust its long record and stop checking on it',why:'Running well for years does not tell you why, so find the reason.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A candle can burn **for hours** with nobody looking after it. It doesn\u2019t flare up, and it doesn\u2019t go out. So what keeps it **so steady**, without you doing a thing?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s the trick: the flame\u2019s heat **melts the wax**. The wick soaks the liquid up, like a straw. The flame burns that as gas. The wick isn\u2019t the fuel at all. The candle **makes its own** supply as it goes.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When something runs smoothly by itself, there\u2019s usually **a hidden reason**. Look for it. Then **you\u2019ll know** what to protect.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A candle doesn\u2019t flare up and it doesn\u2019t die. It burns at **one steady rate** for hours, with nobody adjusting it. You\u2019d need a thermostat to do that with a heater. Where\u2019s the candle\u2019s **control**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Faraday pointed out that the wick isn\u2019t what the candle burns for light. Only its tip slowly wears away. Heat melts the wax, the wick carries the liquid up, and the flame burns it as vapour.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Each step **sets the pace** of the next. The heat decides how fast wax melts. The wick decides how fast it rises. The flame can only burn what arrives. So the whole thing **settles** at one rate.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A simple object can hide **a stable machine**. When something runs well unattended, name **what keeps it steady** before you rely on it.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Candles look like the simplest thing in a room. No moving parts, no switch. Yet one burns at **a steady rate** for hours. Faraday asked his audience a better question: how does it **manage not to fail**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The answer is that it\u2019s **self-regulating**. Heat melts the wax, the wick lifts it, and the flame burns it as vapour. Each step **limits the next**, while the heat slowly trims the wick\u2019s tip.'},{kind:'story',text:'Faraday showed his audience that the wick isn\u2019t the fuel. Only its tip burns slowly away as the candle shortens. A candle that used up its wick as fast as its wax wouldn\u2019t last long.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a nightly data job your team hasn\u2019t touched for years. Then it fails. It only ever worked because one upstream table **stayed small**. It looked self-regulating. It was lucky, and nobody had named **the mechanism**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The wick does burn, slowly, and older wicks had to be **trimmed by hand** or they smoked. And steadiness alone isn\u2019t proof of control. A system can run unattended for years on luck.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t just admire **a steady record**. Ask what keeps the thing steady, and **name the mechanism**. Then you\u2019ll know what would break it.'}]},'chemical-history-candle_4':{title:'Breath and Flame',card:{insight:'You breathe the way a candle burns, only slower.',tryThis:'Pick one claim you believe about your body. Name a simple test you could run on yourself today.',skills:['science','thinking']},review:{prompt:'A friend says people and candles burn fuel the same way. How would Faraday check?',right:'Run the same test on breath and on the candle',wrong:'Look it up in a book and just trust it',why:'Two things are only alike if the same test works on both.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Put a glass jar over a lit candle. Soon the flame **goes out**. It has run short of something in the air. Here\u2019s the strange part: **you need it** too. What is it?'},{kind:'story',text:'It\u2019s oxygen. Faraday also tested what a flame gives out. He used **lime-water**, a liquid that turns cloudy when carbon dioxide gets in. Gas from a candle made it cloudy. Then breath was blown through it, and it clouded too.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When **you breathe**, your body does a slow, gentle version of what a candle does. Both take in oxygen. Both give out **carbon dioxide** and water.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Watch a candle burn and you\u2019re watching **something familiar**. Not the light or the wax, but the chemistry. It\u2019s happening inside you right now, more slowly. How could anyone **prove that** in a lecture hall?'},{kind:'story',text:'Lime-water turns milky when carbon dioxide passes through it. Faraday had already shown a candle\u2019s fumes do this. In his last lecture, breath went through **the same lime-water**. It clouded in exactly the same way.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Run **one test** on both, and the likeness stops being a metaphor and becomes **a finding**.',left:{title:'The candle',items:['Takes in oxygen','Gives off carbon dioxide','Fast, bright and hot']},right:{title:'Your body',items:['Takes in oxygen','Gives off carbon dioxide','Slow, steady and warm']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s why it mattered. The chemistry of breathing was **recently won**, worked out less than a century before. Faraday didn\u2019t hand it down as fact. If breath hadn\u2019t clouded the water, **the idea** would have failed in front of everyone.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Test the familiar with **the same tool** you used on the strange. If a comparison is real, it should **survive the test** when you run it on yourself.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'For most of the lectures, a candle sits on the bench as the thing being studied. Then, in the sixth, Faraday points his test at **the audience**. Why turn the instrument on **the people watching**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Lime-water had already shown carbon dioxide in the candle\u2019s fumes. Now breath goes through it, and it clouds **just the same**. The object on the bench has become a model of the people looking at it.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The point is **one chemistry** at two speeds. Both use oxygen and give off carbon dioxide and water. The method matters more: you test the familiar with **the same instrument** you used on the strange.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now say you believe coffee after lunch ruins your sleep. You could just keep believing it. Or you could skip it for a few days and note how you sleep. Then the belief has **faced a test**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The likeness is in the inputs and outputs, not **the mechanism**. A flame burns fuel in one hot rush. The body works in many small steps at body heat. Push the analogy and cells seem to hold fires.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A comparison stated is only **a likeness**. Run it through the same test on both sides, and it becomes **a claim** that could have failed. That\u2019s what earns it.'}]},'chemical-history-candle_5':{title:'Slow and True',card:{insight:'Give the true answer in small steps, not an easy wrong one.',tryThis:'Take one thing you often explain. Find the part that is not true, and fix it with one more step.',skills:['communication','thinking']},review:{prompt:'A new coworker asks how your prices are set. What do you say?',right:'The true version, given slowly in small steps',wrong:'It is just the cost plus a little extra',why:'A quick wrong answer must be fixed later, so go slow.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your little cousin asks **why it gets dark** at night. You could say the sun goes to sleep. It\u2019s quick, and she\u2019d be happy. But is that **really helping** her?'},{kind:'story',text:'Faraday\u2019s candle talks were for children. But he used **the real words** of science, like oxygen and carbon. He didn\u2019t leave out the hard parts. He showed each one on the table, so they were easy to follow.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Go **slower**, not smaller. Give the true answer in small steps, and show it if you can. An easy wrong answer **gets in the way** later.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A new colleague asks how your pricing works. You say it\u2019s **basically cost** plus a margin. It isn\u2019t, quite. A month later they quote a client on that basis, **confidently and wrongly**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Faraday\u2019s lectures were for children, yet he gave up **no true thing**. He used the real vocabulary: combustion, gases, the make-up of water. What he changed was the route. Every idea came **in small steps**, tied to something they could see.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Every explanation for **a beginner** has to change something. The question is **which thing** you change.',left:{title:'Smaller',items:['Leaves out the hard part','Easy now','Wrong later']},right:{title:'Slower',items:['Keeps the hard part','Takes it in steps','Still true later']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s why **a wrong model** is worse than none. Someone with no model knows they don\u2019t know. Someone with a tidy wrong one thinks **the matter\u2019s closed**. The true version has to dislodge it first.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Slow down, but **don\u2019t dilute**, and give the true version in small steps. A comfortable wrong answer doesn\u2019t save effort, it just moves **the effort** later.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Most explanations for beginners work by **removing the difficulty** and handing over a conclusion. Faraday\u2019s lectures for children did the opposite. Adults still read them today. What was he doing that **the usual method** isn\u2019t?'},{kind:'explain',text:'He adjusted **the route**, not the level. Every concept arrived attached to something visible on the bench. Nothing was asserted before it was shown. He kept the difficulty and gave the children **the means** to work through it.'},{kind:'story',text:'He also treated his audience as able to be wrong. He invited predictions. When one failed, he treated the failure as **the interesting part**. That assumes a listener can bear a difficulty, which is the opposite of talking down.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a nurse explaining a new medicine to a patient. She could say it just helps the heart. Instead she explains what it does, one step at a time. The patient can now spot **a warning sign** themselves.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Every explanation simplifies. Saying a flame burns wax vapour skips detail but **isn\u2019t false**. And if you\u2019re short of time, an honest partial answer beats a long one you never finish.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The rule forbids **false steps**, not missing ones. Go as slowly as **you need**, but make every step true at its own scale.'}]},'chemical-history-candle_6':{title:'The Towel and the Wick',card:{insight:'A strange thing is often a plain thing you have seen before.',tryThis:'Next time something looks strange, ask where you have seen the everyday kind. Explain it from there.',skills:['communication','science']},review:{prompt:'Sales shot up last week, and no one knows why. What first?',right:'Ask where you have seen this kind of thing before',wrong:'Treat it as a brand new case that needs new ideas',why:'Most strange cases are a normal thing in a new place, so look there first.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Leave the corner of a towel hanging in a full bath. Come back later. The towel is **wet far above** the water. Nothing pumped the water up. So **how did it climb**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Faraday told the children how a towel left in a basin can empty the water onto the floor. Then he showed **the same thing** in a candle wick. They\u2019d seen it at home. Now they had a name for it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Wax climbs a wick the way water climbs a towel. Something that looks special is often **a thing you\u2019ve seen** before. Look for **your own** everyday version.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A tea towel slips off the sink with one end left in the washing-up water. An hour later, **there\u2019s a puddle** on the floor. No pump, no tilt, no heat. What **moved the water**?'},{kind:'story',text:'In his first lecture, Faraday explains how wax reaches the flame. He reminds his audience of exactly that towel. Then he shows the same pull in a wick, and in **a column of salt** drawing coloured liquid upward.'},{kind:'explain',text:'This isn\u2019t **an analogy**. A towel and a wick are both bundles of fine fibres with narrow gaps. Liquid gets drawn into narrow gaps. It\u2019s **one effect** in three settings, and you already knew one of them.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'A term you\u2019re given first fades fast. A term **pinned to a memory** you already have **lasts**.',left:{title:'Defined first',items:['A term to memorise','Gone in a week']},right:{title:'Seen first',items:['The towel that flooded the floor','Hard to lose']}},{kind:'moral',text:'When something looks like **a special case**, find where you\u2019ve seen the ordinary version. Explain from there, and the name will **stick to it**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Capillary action sounds like something from a lab. Faraday explained it before he even named it, using a household mess **you\u2019ve probably** made yourself. Why start with a soggy towel instead of **a definition**?'},{kind:'story',text:'He described a towel with one end in a basin, carrying water over the rim and onto the floor. Then he showed the same climb in a wick and in a column of salt. One effect, three places.'},{kind:'explain',text:'An analogy says one thing is like another and hopes **the likeness holds**. Faraday\u2019s towel is **the same process**, so nothing needs translating back. You don\u2019t remember a comparison. You recognise a mechanism you\u2019ve watched.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now say your website crashes every Monday morning. It looks like a strange bug. But Monday is when everyone logs in at once, like **a busy shop** at opening. It\u2019s ordinary overload, seen in a new place.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Not every special case is an ordinary one in disguise. Teaching electricity as **water in pipes** misleads at the points that matter. So check it\u2019s the same mechanism, not just a resemblance.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Look for **the everyday version** first, then test that it really is the same thing. When it is, the explanation is done before **the name arrives**.'}]},'chemical-history-candle_7':{title:'The Cup the Flame Builds',card:{insight:'The step that bugs you may be doing a hidden job.',tryThis:'Before you cut a step that bugs you, write down what might break without it.',skills:['thinking','science']},review:{prompt:'Everyone wants to cut a long weekly meeting. What do you check first?',right:'What the meeting does that would break without it',wrong:'How much time the team would save by cutting it',why:'A step that bugs you may hold things together, so learn its job first.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A candle\u2019s flame is hot enough to **melt wax**. So why doesn\u2019t the melted wax just run down the sides? Look closely at the top of yours. **What\u2019s holding it** in?'},{kind:'explain',text:'There\u2019s **a little cup** of melted wax at the top. The flame pulls cool air up the sides, which keeps **the edge** solid. A draught spoils that. One side melts, and wax runs down.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The flame builds its own bowl, and you\u2019d never notice. Some parts do **quiet work** like that. So before **you fix** anything, ask what it\u2019s doing.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A candle burns on the table **all evening** without a drip. Someone opens the back door. Within minutes, a channel melts in one side and wax runs **onto the cloth**. What did the draught break?'},{kind:'story',text:'Faraday asked how the cup of liquid wax survives under a flame hot enough to burn it all. His answer was **the airflow**. The flame draws air up the sides, and that air keeps the rim solid.'},{kind:'explain',text:'So the flame **builds the vessel** that holds its own fuel. Disturb the draught, and one side overheats. The cup fails, the wax runs, and the candle gutters. A feature nobody notices was **holding it together**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a weekly meeting your team wants to cut because it runs long. It\u2019s also the only place two departments hear each other\u2019s problems. Cut it, and those problems arrive later, and bigger.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Awkward parts often do **quiet work**. Before you remove one, find out what it holds up, just as a draught shows **what the rim does**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A candle looks like it\u2019s simply burning. But Faraday showed it\u2019s also **making something**: a container for its own fuel. Why would a flame build **a vessel** it\u2019s about to empty?'},{kind:'explain',text:'It comes down to **convection**. The flame draws a current of air up past the candle\u2019s sides. That current cools the rim, so it stays solid and holds the melted wax. The feeding and the containing are **one process**.'},{kind:'story',text:'He pointed to what a draught does. It upsets the current on one side. The cup fails there, the wax runs down, and the candle gutters. Interrupt **one link**, and the arrangement collapses.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now an engineer replaces a slow overnight batch job with live updates. Nobody mentioned that finance checks its figures against **the snapshot** the batch left behind. At month end, the numbers stop agreeing.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Not every awkward feature is secretly essential. Some are **just leftovers**, and believing they all do hidden work becomes an excuse to never change anything. Find out first, then decide.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Before **you improve** a system, learn which awkward parts are doing **necessary work**. The rule isn\u2019t to keep everything. It\u2019s to know what you\u2019re removing.'}]},'chemical-history-candle_8':{title:'The Glowing Soot',card:{insight:'Sometimes the flaw is doing the job, so check before you fix it.',tryThis:'Name one thing in your life that works well. Write down which flaw in it might be helping.',skills:['thinking','science']},review:{prompt:'Users love the slow little animation in your app. A coworker wants it gone. Now what?',right:'Check whether that slowness is what makes it work',wrong:'Speed it up, since faster is always better',why:'A flaw can be what does the job, so test before you cut it.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A gas cooker flame is blue and gives **very little light**. A candle flame glows bright yellow. Both are burning fuel. So where do you think **the candle\u2019s light** comes from?'},{kind:'story',text:'Faraday held something cold in a candle flame. Black **soot** built up on it. That soot was the same stuff that had been glowing. The cold had caught it before it could burn away.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A candle shines because **tiny specks** of soot glow in its heat. Burn them cleanly and you\u2019d get a dimmer flame. So sometimes **the mess** is doing the job.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Ask most people what makes a flame bright, and they\u2019ll say **strong burning**. A bright flame must be a good one. Faraday showed the opposite. What if the glow is a sign of **something unfinished**?'},{kind:'story',text:'He asked why a candle is so bright when a hydrogen flame is **nearly invisible**. Then he held a cold surface in the candle flame and collected soot. That soot was the glowing carbon, caught before it burned.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The yellow light is **solid carbon**, heated white inside the flame and burnt before it leaves. Give the flame more air and it burns hotter but dimmer. So brightness means **unburnt fuel**. A perfect flame would be nearly dark.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now say your app\u2019s best-loved feature is a slightly slow animation. A developer wants to speed it up. But users like it because the pause tells them their tap worked. Remove it and **the value goes** too.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When something works unusually well, look for **the flaw** that\u2019s helping. Check before you **engineer it away**, or you may remove the very thing you valued.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A hydrogen flame can be **very hot** and still be almost invisible. A candle flame glows yellow enough to read by. So what is the candle\u2019s flame **carrying** that hydrogen\u2019s isn\u2019t?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The answer is **solid carbon**. Tiny particles are heated until they glow, then burnt before they leave the flame. So brightness is a sign of **incomplete burning**. The visible glow is unburnt fuel at a very high temperature.'},{kind:'story',text:'Faraday proved both halves. A cold surface held in the flame collected soot, the same carbon caught before it burned. Then he gave a flame **more air**. It burned hotter and gave less light, almost dark.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a handwritten note from a manager, a little messy and slow to write. Someone suggests a neat template instead. But people read the note because it\u2019s **clearly personal**. Tidy it up, and you lose the reason it works.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. In the candle, the flaw is the function. More often it\u2019s **just a flaw**, and calling waste secretly useful defends it. Even here the glow has a price: a sooty flame is bright but dirty.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A candle is bright because it\u2019s **slightly dirty**. When something works unusually well, find the imperfection doing the job before **you remove it**.'}]},'chemical-history-candle_9':{title:'The Wish at the End',card:{insight:'A big message works best at the end, after the facts that back it up.',tryThis:'Move the big line in your next talk to the end. Check that what comes first backs it up.',skills:['communication','thinking']},review:{prompt:'Your project went well, and you are closing the review. Where does the proud line go?',right:'At the end, after the facts that back it up',wrong:'At the start, to set a happy tone',why:'Said after the facts, a big line is one people can check.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two coaches say **never give up**. One says it before the game. The other says it after **you\u2019ve come back** from three goals down. Which one do you actually believe?'},{kind:'story',text:'Faraday spent six talks showing children how a candle works. At the very end, he said something new. He hoped they\u2019d be like a candle, giving light to people around them and doing their duty well.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A big message works best **at the end**, after the facts. Said first, it\u2019s just nice words. Said last, **people know** what it means.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A conference talk opens with a slide saying **you can change** the world. The room nods politely. Forty minutes later, nobody could say what change was meant, or **why to believe** it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Faraday ends his sixth lecture with a wish. He hopes his listeners might be like a candle. They\u2019d give light to those around them and do their duty to others. It comes after hours of chemistry, not before.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s why **the position** does the work. By the end, the audience knows what a candle does. It consumes itself to give light, steadily, keeping up its own burning. The metaphor now has **real content**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Same words, two places. Only **the order** changes, and the order decides **how they land**.',left:{title:'As an opening',items:['Nothing holds it up','Heard as moralising']},right:{title:'As a close',items:['Backed by the evidence','Heard as a conclusion']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Put the sentiment **after the evidence**. Where a moral sits decides whether it\u2019s a platitude or **a conclusion** you\u2019ve earned.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Read on its own, the closing wish sounds like **Victorian moralising**. Be like a candle. Give light. Do your duty. So why does it land, after six lectures, as **a real conclusion**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Because by then the candle is **fully understood**. The audience has watched it consume itself to make light and heat. It burns in order and keeps up its own conditions. Each part of the image now carries **a fact**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture the same words in Faraday\u2019s first minute. Without the chemistry, a candle just means something that gives light. The wish would be pleasant and empty. Six lectures later, **every word** has something behind it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture you\u2019re giving a talk about your grandad, who was a nurse. You tell the stories first: the night shifts, the patients. Then you say what he taught you about kindness. By then, it means something.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Position can\u2019t rescue a moral the evidence doesn\u2019t support. Chemistry shows how a candle works, not how anyone should live. That step is **a choice**, not a deduction. Good order earns attention, not proof.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Put the sentiment **at the end** of the evidence, not in front of it. Its position decides most of whether **your audience** believes it.'}]}}}},951,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'common-sense',kind:'book',promise:'Win people over with plain words and facts',about:'How did one short book help push America to break free from a king? In 1776, Thomas Paine wrote Common Sense in plain words for everyday people. Here are the ways he made his case. Want to try them?',ideas:{'common-sense_1':{title:'Write for the Decider',card:{insight:'A good plan can fail if it goes to someone who cannot say yes.',tryThis:'Before you ask for something, write down who gets to say yes. Then write about what that person cares about.',skills:['communication','history']},review:{prompt:'You want a new laptop at work. Who should you write your note for?',right:'Your boss, who gets to say yes, in her own words',wrong:'Your team, since they already love the idea',why:'Write to the person who can say yes, about what they care about.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You want a **later bedtime**. You make a brilliant case to your little brother. He nods at every word. But does he get to decide? Who are you **really talking** to?'},{kind:'explain',text:'In 1776 a man called Thomas Paine put out a short booklet. It said America should **rule itself**. Most booklets then used long, fancy words for rich, schooled men. Paine used **plain words** that farmers and shopkeepers already knew.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Paine\u2019s ideas weren\u2019t new. His readers were. Before you argue, ask **who decides**, then talk to them in **their own words**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You want funding for a new tool. You send a careful, glowing case to **your team**. They love it. The money never comes. What went wrong, when **the argument** was so strong?'},{kind:'story',text:'Thomas Paine reached Philadelphia from England late in 1774. Political pamphlets then were written for men with **classical educations**. Paine wrote Common Sense for everyone else, in plain words and short sentences. He used Bible stories his readers already knew.'},{kind:'explain',text:'His content **wasn\u2019t new**. The audience was. By writing for farmers and shopkeepers, he treated them as **the deciders**. Your tool\u2019s problem is the same. The person who signs off is the finance lead, and she wasn\u2019t your reader.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Most cases don\u2019t fail for being weak. They fail because they\u2019re aimed at **the wrong reader**. Name **who decides**, and write in the words they already use.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'In January 1776, colonial pamphlets mostly argued from **law and charters**. They quoted classical authors for an educated few. Then one arrived that a farmer could follow line by line. Why did **that choice** matter so much?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A register is the level of formality a text assumes. It also assumes **a reader**. A learned register quietly tells everyone else the matter isn\u2019t theirs. Paine\u2019s plain register told **ordinary colonists** the decision was theirs.'},{kind:'story',text:'Paine\u2019s references came from Scripture and daily life, which his readers shared. Copies were read aloud in taverns to people who couldn\u2019t read. The ideas in it were largely familiar. What was new was who they were addressed to.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a scientist who wants a town to ban a pesticide near its river. The town will decide by public vote. She writes a careful paper for other scientists. You can guess how the vote goes.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Plainness cuts away qualifications, and a stirring register carries a weak case as easily as a strong one. John Adams thought the pamphlet\u2019s plan for government far too simple. Writing to the decider is **a method**, not proof.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The choice of reader can matter more than the case itself. Find **who decides**, then write in **their references** and their words. That\u2019s where you start.'}]},'common-sense_2':{title:'The Necessary Evil',card:{insight:'You can love a group and still say one of its rules is bad.',tryThis:'Next time a rule bugs you, say what you love about the group first. Then name the rule.',skills:['communication','history']},review:{prompt:'You love your soccer team, but not its new rule on tickets. How do you start?',right:'Say what you love about the team, then name the rule',wrong:'Say the whole team just keeps getting worse',why:'When you say what you love first, people can hear the fix you want.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You and your friends start **a club**. At first there are no rules, and everyone helps. Then more people join, and a few stop playing fair. What do you **need now**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Thomas Paine said the same about countries, in 1776. People helping each other is **a good thing**. Rules and rulers are only needed because some people don\u2019t play fair. He called them **a necessary evil**. Think of a bike lock.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The group and its rules are **two things**. Keep them apart, and you can say a rule is bad without saying **the group** is bad.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve backed your football club for twenty years. Its new **ticket policy** is pricing out families. Each time you complain, someone calls you **disloyal**. How do you make the point without sounding like an enemy?'},{kind:'story',text:'Common Sense opens before Britain is even mentioned. Paine pictures a few settlers in an empty land. They cooperate because they need each other. Only as numbers grow and duty slackens do they set up **some government**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Paine pulls apart two things people usually run together: **society** and **government**.',left:{title:'Society',items:['Comes from our needs','Good in itself','The country you belong to']},right:{title:'Government',items:['Comes from our failings','Good only if it works','A tool you can replace']}},{kind:'moral',text:'If government is **a tool**, it earns its place by results. So you can judge the policy and still love **the club**. Say what you\u2019re not criticising first.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture a ward nurse who believes in her hospital. A new rota is wearing **her team** down. Each time she raises it, people hear **an attack** on the hospital. Why does the talk keep turning to loyalty?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Paine opens Common Sense with a split. Society comes from what we **need** from one another. Government comes from our failure to behave well. The first is a blessing. The second, at best, is **a necessary evil**.'},{kind:'story',text:'His picture is a handful of settlers in a clearing. They help each other because they must. Only later, as numbers grow and duties slacken, do they find government useful. It arrives as **a convenience**, not a sacred order.'},{kind:'story',text:'Back on the ward, the nurse tries Paine\u2019s move. She starts by saying she\u2019d choose this hospital again tomorrow. Then she says the rota is failing it. The meeting finally talks about **the rota**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The line is cleaner on paper than in life, since law helps create the trade society runs on. It\u2019s also borrowed to argue for **minimal government**, though Paine later proposed help for the old and poor.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Once government is a tool, you can ask **whether it works**. Separate the thing you love from **how it\u2019s run**, and your criticism lands where you meant it.'}]},'common-sense_3':{title:'The Royal Lottery',card:{insight:'Being born into a job does not mean you can do it well.',tryThis:'Next time someone gets a job near you, ask how they were picked. Then ask if that way finds skill.',skills:['thinking','leadership']},review:{prompt:'Your club always makes its oldest member the leader. What do you say?',right:'Being old does not show who can lead well',wrong:'It is unfair that new members never get a turn',why:'When you ask who can do the job well, it is hard to argue.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your team captain moves away. The coach names **her little brother** as the new captain. He\u2019s never played a match. Why him? Just because **he\u2019s her brother**. Does that seem like a good way to choose?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Kings used to be chosen like that. When a king died, **his son** took over, good at it or not. In 1776 Thomas Paine called this **a lucky dip**. Sometimes you pull out a prize, and sometimes you get junk.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A wise, brave parent doesn\u2019t always have **a wise child**. So picking leaders by birth leaves the whole job to **luck**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A founder builds a firm on her own judgement. She retires, and **her son** takes over. He might be brilliant. He might not. How would you know from **how he got** the job?'},{kind:'explain',text:'That\u2019s the question Paine put to kings. Inheriting an office **doesn\u2019t show** you can do it. Whatever made a ruler able, nothing passes it to **his heir**. So the system is sure to produce unfit rulers now and then.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Paine could have argued only about **justice**. He chose a plainer test: **competence**.',left:{title:'Justice objection',items:['Invites talk of duty','Can stall for ever','Needs a theory of rights']},right:{title:'Competence objection',items:['Asks if the method works','Hard to answer yes','Needs no theory at all']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Being born into a role says nothing about **doing it well**. When a moral objection stalls, look for **the practical one** underneath. It\u2019s much harder to dismiss.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Paine calls nature as **a witness** against inherited rule. Nature, he says, so often hands people **a donkey** where they expected a lion. Surely she\u2019s telling us something. What could a donkey prove about kings?'},{kind:'explain',text:'His point is **statistical**. Whatever makes a ruler capable, nothing carries it to a son. So a system that fills the office by birth will produce **unfit holders** at intervals. Worse, it has no way to remove them.'},{kind:'story',text:'Then he traces royal lines back to their start. Each one began with someone who **took power by force**. For England, he points to William the Conqueror. The holiness of the crown was added afterwards.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a family firm in its third generation. The grandson runs it because his name is on the door. The staff can see he\u2019s struggling. But who on the board will tell the owner\u2019s grandson to step down?'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The argument bites hardest on a monarch who actually governs, not a ceremonial one. And elections pick **incompetents** too. Paine\u2019s claim is comparative: birth has no link to ability, and no way to fix its failures.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You don\u2019t need a theory of rights to doubt a lottery. Ask whether **the method** picks able people, and whether you can **remove its failures**.'}]},'common-sense_4':{title:'The Closing Window',card:{insight:'Waiting feels safe, but some chances shrink while you wait.',tryThis:'Pick one change you keep putting off. Write what waiting costs and the date it starts to cost.',skills:['thinking','leadership']},review:{prompt:'Your team keeps putting off a switch that everyone agrees is due. What do you say?',right:'Say what waiting costs us, and by what date',wrong:'Let\u2019s wait for a slow month, so it goes smoothly',why:'Once the cost of waiting is plain, the case for change gets stronger.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You want to swap seats in class. Is it easier on **the first day** of term, or next summer, once you\u2019ve **thought it over**? Take a guess.'},{kind:'explain',text:'On day one, nobody has settled in. By summer, everyone has **friends nearby**. In 1776 Thomas Paine told Americans the same about breaking from Britain. They had enough people for an army and **no big debts**. Later would be harder.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Some changes get harder **the longer you wait**. So waiting isn\u2019t the safe choice. It\u2019s **a choice too**, and it has a cost.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Everyone agrees the old system should go. Nobody argues for keeping it. Yet every quarter the switch slips, because **this quarter is busy**. Two years later it\u2019s still running. Who actually **made that decision**?'},{kind:'story',text:'About a quarter of Common Sense is about timing. Paine listed what the colonies had right then. There were enough men for an army, timber for a navy, and **no national debt**. Growth, he warned, would wear those advantages away.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Your system switch is the same decision, made **now** or made **later**.',left:{title:'Now',items:['Less to move','Fewer people with a stake','The tools already here']},right:{title:'Later',items:['More records built up','More habits to unpick','Same tools, more resistance']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Delay isn\u2019t a pause. It\u2019s **a slow vote** for the old way. If you want change to win, make **what waiting costs** concrete, with a date.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'In early 1776, many colonists half agreed with breaking from Britain. Their answer was **not yet**. Paine spent a large part of his pamphlet on **one word**. Can you guess which?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The word was **now**. Paine treated timing as a real question, not a flourish. The ability to change course is highest when there\u2019s **least to protect**. What makes change worth doing and what makes it possible decay together.'},{kind:'story',text:'His proof was an inventory. The colonies had enough men to raise an army. They had timber and shipbuilding skill for a navy. They owed no national debt. A bigger, richer, more settled country would be **harder to unite**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a couple whose flat lease ends in June. After that, moving means fees and another year\u2019s contract. They keep saying they\u2019ll decide in spring. Spring is already part of the decision.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Urgency is the oldest trick in persuasion, and **a fake deadline** looks like a real one. Paine\u2019s forecasts were optimistic too. The colonies never built a navy to match Britain\u2019s. A window argument is only as good as its inventory.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You\u2019re always deciding, even when **you wait**. Check what the window is made of. If it\u2019s real, put **a date** on its closing.'}]},'common-sense_5':{title:'Easy to Pass On',card:{insight:'An idea that is hard to pass on will not get far.',tryThis:'Cut something you want shared down to a short note. Make it easy for friends to forward.',skills:['communication','history']},review:{prompt:'A nurse found a simple way to stop a common mistake. How should she share it?',right:'A short sheet that anyone can copy and pass on',wrong:'A long report sent only to her own boss',why:'If people cannot pass your idea on, it will not spread.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your school has an **ideas box**, and you can leave your name off. Why might an unsigned idea get **a fairer hearing** than one with a famous name on it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'In 1776 Thomas Paine\u2019s booklet came out with **no name** on it. It just said an Englishman wrote it. It was cheap and plain, so people **passed it round**. In inns, they read it aloud to friends who couldn\u2019t read.'},{kind:'moral',text:'How an idea gets around is **part of the idea**. Make it easy to judge on its own, and **easy to share**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve written the perfect proposal. It\u2019s forty pages, it sits in **one folder**, and it carries your name at the top. Who is going to read it, and what will they **judge first**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Common Sense appeared in Philadelphia in January 1776, signed only as the work of an Englishman. Paine was a recent arrival with **no name** worth using. Unsigned, it left readers nothing to judge but **the reasoning**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The price and the prose followed **the same logic**. It was cheap enough to pass hand to hand, and plain enough for anyone to follow. In taverns it was **read aloud**, so one copy could reach a room.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A case nobody can afford to read hasn\u2019t been made. Design **how your case travels** as carefully as **what it says**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A pamphlet appears with **no author\u2019s name**. It\u2019s cheap, it\u2019s plain, and strangers are reading it aloud in taverns. Was the design **an accident**, or part of the case?'},{kind:'story',text:'That was Common Sense in early 1776. Signed only by an Englishman, it gave readers no reputation to weigh. Its prose had no classical quotation or legal vocabulary. A farmer could follow it, and many heard it read aloud.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Each choice **removed a barrier**. No name meant no judging the case by its author. A low price meant no cost to passing it on. Plain words meant you didn\u2019t need **an education** to take part.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a nurse who spots a simple way to avoid a dosing error. She could write a long report for her manager. Instead she makes a one-page checklist that any ward can copy. Within months, other hospitals use it.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Anonymity removes **accountability** as well as prejudice. It shelters a smear as easily as an honest case. And the famous sales figures are hard to check, so the legend may outrun the facts.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Distribution is part of **your argument**. Strip out what lets people judge you instead of the case. Then strip out what stops them **passing it on**.'}]},'common-sense_6':{title:'The Island and the Continent',card:{insight:'If a setup is broken, the plain facts can show it for you.',tryThis:'Write one thing that keeps going wrong as plain facts. Use real times and distances, with no blame.',skills:['communication','thinking']},review:{prompt:'Renters want repairs handled in town, not by an owner far away. What line works best?',right:'Each leak waits six weeks for a reply from the owner',wrong:'The owner does not care about any of us',why:'Plain facts about the setup beat a long list of complaints.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Look at a map. There\u2019s **a huge land**, and far across the sea, a tiny island. Now imagine the island ruling **the huge land**, for ever. Does something look wrong to you?'},{kind:'explain',text:'In 1776 the tiny island was Britain, and the huge land was America. Thomas Paine said that **made no sense**. A letter took **months** to cross the sea and come back. By then, the problem had changed.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You don\u2019t always need a long argument. Just show the thing **at its real size**, and let people **see it** for themselves.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your branch runs every decision past **a head office** in another time zone. Approval takes weeks. By the time a reply arrives, the problem has moved on. Is anyone there careless, or is it **the arrangement**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Paine faced the same shape in 1776. A petition took months to cross the Atlantic and back. By the time an answer came, events had moved. He then put the two places side by side: **a continent**, ruled by **an island**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'There are two ways to argue: from **grievance** or from **arrangement**.',left:{title:'From grievance',items:['The king is wicked','Must be proved case by case','Met with concessions']},right:{title:'From arrangement',items:['The set-up cannot work','Visible at a glance','No concession fixes distance']}},{kind:'moral',text:'You don\u2019t have to prove anyone is wicked. Show **the real sizes** and distances plainly, and **the arrangement** argues against itself.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Which charge would a defender of British rule find **harder to answer**? That the king had treated the colonies unjustly? Or that an island **can\u2019t keep governing** a continent? Think about it before you read on.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Injustice can be denied, explained or met with **concessions**. A ratio of sizes can\u2019t. Paine backed the image with distance: an appeal took months to cross the ocean and return. No authority could **respond in time**.'},{kind:'story',text:'He added a picture from the sky. Nowhere in nature, he said, is a satellite larger than the planet it circles. Britain and America turned **that order** upside down. Readers could see the problem without a single legal argument.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a charity run by a board that meets **once a year**. The food bank it oversees needs decisions every day. Nobody on the board means harm. Yet volunteers wait months for answers about a broken van.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. A vivid ratio persuades whether or not the mismatch matters. And distant rule wasn\u2019t impossible: Britain later governed far bigger, farther lands. The real claim is narrower. Rule from there couldn\u2019t be **responsive**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A grievance can be met with goodwill. A shape can\u2019t. When **the arrangement** is the problem, show it at its size, and you\u2019ll find **no concession** answers you.'}]},'common-sense_7':{title:'Count the Safe Choice',card:{insight:'The choice that feels safe still has costs, so count them too.',tryThis:'Find one choice where staying put is called safe. Write down what it will cost you this year.',skills:['thinking','history']},review:{prompt:'Your boss calls keeping a late supplier the safe choice. What do you say?',right:'Keeping them keeps the delays, so count that cost too',wrong:'Switching now is far too risky to try',why:'Staying put is a choice too, so count its costs.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You and a friend share **a locker**. Every week you fight about the mess. Every week you make up and carry on. And next week? **The same fight**. So was carrying on the safe choice?'},{kind:'explain',text:'By 1776, America and Britain were already fighting. Many people wanted to **make up** and go back to how things were. Thomas Paine said going back would just bring back **the same problems**. That hope was the fantasy.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Keeping things the same isn\u2019t free. It\u2019s **a choice too**. When someone calls it safe, ask what it\u2019ll **really bring** you.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your firm keeps renewing **a supplier** whose delays cause its outages. Every year the board calls renewal **the safe option**. Nobody has ever asked what renewal costs. Have you?'},{kind:'story',text:'In early 1776, fighting with Britain had already begun. Cautious colonists saw **reconciliation** as the realistic choice and independence as the gamble. Paine turned that round. Patching things up, he argued, was the real fantasy.'},{kind:'explain',text:'He **counted what it delivered**. Trade would find America whatever flag flew, because people buy what they need. The tie dragged America into **Britain\u2019s wars**. And once blood was shed, the old footing couldn\u2019t be restored.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Renewal keeps **the cause** of your outages, so count it like any other choice. Staying put isn\u2019t neutral. It\u2019s **a risk** dressed as safety.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'In July 1775, Congress sent the King **a petition** seeking peace. In August, he declared the colonies in rebellion. Yet in January 1776, **patching things up** was still the respectable view. Why did Paine call it a fantasy?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Independence was supposed to be **the gamble**. Paine argued that reconciliation restored the very relationship that had caused the conflict. It would cause it again. So the choice wasn\u2019t safety against risk. It was **two risks**, one in disguise.'},{kind:'story',text:'Then he priced it. America\u2019s goods would sell whatever flag flew. The tie brought Britain\u2019s wars without real protection. And after killing, the old trust couldn\u2019t return, any more than lost innocence can be restored.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a town with an ageing bridge. Patching it again is called the cautious choice. Nobody adds up ten more years of repairs, closures and risk. Only the rebuild is asked to justify its cost.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Anyone can call the cautious option a fantasy without doing the counting. And a pamphlet written to persuade gives its own side **the gentler audit**. Paine never priced the years of war with equal care.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Staying put is a decision with consequences. Price what each option **delivers** for you, not only what it avoids. Then count both with **the same strictness**, even the one you favour.'}]},'common-sense_8':{title:'Crown the Law',card:{insight:'Write the rules down so they hold for all, even the leader.',tryThis:'Find one rule at home or work that lives in one person\u2019s head. Write it down for all to see.',skills:['leadership','history']},review:{prompt:'A club keeps fighting over what its leader wants that week. What helps?',right:'Written rules that hold the leader too',wrong:'A new leader who is more fair',why:'A new leader is still just one person, so write the rules down.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'In a playground game, **the biggest kid** makes the rules. When he\u2019s losing, the rules change. When he\u2019s winning, they stay put. How fair does that feel when **you\u2019re losing**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'In 1776 Thomas Paine wanted America free of its king. People asked who would be in charge. His answer was **the law**. Write the rules down, he said, and put **a crown** on them. Then break the crown up.'},{kind:'moral',text:'It\u2019s fairer when the rules are the boss, not a person. Write them down, and they hold for **everyone**, even **the strongest player**. That includes you.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A volunteer group\u2019s rules live in its **founder\u2019s head**. Whatever she says goes, and it\u2019s different every week. People keep quitting. Would **a better leader** fix it, or is something else wrong?'},{kind:'story',text:'Paine was asked who would rule an America without a king. He proposed a ceremony. Draw up a charter and crown it in public, so the world sees that **the law** rules here. Then break the crown up so nobody can claim it.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Under absolute rule **the king** is law. Paine put **written rules** there instead.',left:{title:'The king rules',items:['Authority sits in a person','Shifts with his mood','Passes to his heir']},right:{title:'The law rules',items:['Authority sits in a text','Binds the leader too','Can be changed openly']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Replacing the founder keeps power in **a person**. Written rules that bind the leader too move it to **the page**. You need something to obey, so choose a text you can change.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Sovereignty means **the final say** in a country. Under a monarchy, it sits in one person. So if you remove the king, where does **the final say** go? Critics put that to Paine.'},{kind:'story',text:'Paine\u2019s answer was theatre. Set a day and bring out a charter. Crown it in public, so the world knows the law is king here. Then break the crown and scatter its pieces among **the people**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The ceremony isn\u2019t decoration. A crown makes authority **visible** and obeyed, and people need something to be loyal to. Paine kept that job but handed it to a text. Unlike a person, a text **can be amended**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a flat share where the loudest flatmate sets the rules. One evening they write the rules down and stick them on the fridge. The loudest flatmate signs too. Arguments now start with the fridge, not with him.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. A document doesn\u2019t enforce itself; courts and citizens do. And the law can be king and still be **unjust**. The Constitution later adopted protected slavery. Written rules restrain rulers without guaranteeing good rules.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Authority can live in **a text** rather than a person. Keep the ceremony, but change **what it points at**. Then make sure the rules you crown deserve it.'}]},'common-sense_9':{title:'Save the Big Claim',card:{insight:'Save your biggest claim for last, after the plain facts have earned it.',tryThis:'Move the boldest line in your next pitch to the end. Check that the facts before it hold it up.',skills:['communication','leadership']},review:{prompt:'A principal wants support for a new school schedule. Where does the big promise go?',right:'Last, after the plain facts have earned it',wrong:'First, to grab people before the details start',why:'A big claim works once the plain facts before it have done the work.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two kids give speeches to be **class rep**. The first opens with a promise to change the school for ever. People roll their eyes. The second starts with **three plain plans**. Who do you believe?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Thomas Paine\u2019s 1776 booklet is mostly **plain reasons**: people, ships, wood and money. Only near the end does he say they could **start the world afresh**. That huge line works because of all the plain pages before it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Save your **biggest claim** for the end. Make it only once your plain reasons have **earned it**. Then people won\u2019t roll their eyes.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A charity asks the council for money. It opens by promising to **transform the town**. The room goes cold. What if it had opened with **the waiting list**, the costs and the pilot results instead?'},{kind:'story',text:'Common Sense spends some forty pages on navies, timber, debt and distance. Only in an appendix to a later edition does Paine reach for **scale**. The colonists, he says, had a chance to start the world afresh, unseen since Noah.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The same **big claim** lands very differently depending on **where you put** it.',left:{title:'Big claim first',items:['Sounds like a pitch','Every detail is doubted','Nothing has earned it']},right:{title:'Big claim last',items:['Sounds like a conclusion','Evidence has paid for it','The reader did the counting']}},{kind:'moral',text:'A huge claim placed first reads as **a sermon**. Placed last, it\u2019s the sum of checkable points. So give your plain evidence first, and let **the big line** close.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'After forty pages about navies, timber and debt, Common Sense suddenly **changes key**. The colonists, it says, can start the world afresh. Nothing like it since Noah. Why does **that leap** land instead of sounding absurd?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Paine\u2019s case for a fresh start is **specific**. A new government is normally built out of an old one, inheriting its structures and debts. The colonies had enough organisation to work and **no old order** to tear down.'},{kind:'story',text:'Look at the pages before it. Paine counts men for an army, timber for ships and a country without debt. So the vision arrives as **the sum** of checked points. Like a keystone set last, it holds. Set first, it would fall.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a founder who opens her pitch by promising to **reinvent an industry**. She shows no figures. A rival opens with costs, customers and a pilot. She closes on the same vision, and you believe her.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Most calls to begin again come from people who haven\u2019t counted what they\u2019d keep. And Paine\u2019s new world wasn\u2019t new for everyone. **Native nations** already lived there, and the republic that followed kept slavery.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A claim of **the largest scale** persuades only when the counting before it has paid. So count first. And ask who your fresh start is **fresh for**.'}]}}}},952,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'compound-interest-slowly',kind:'book',promise:'See what waiting costs your savings',about:'Why does saving early beat saving more later? This short piece from the Mindroll team does the money math slowly, step by step. Here are five ideas on how money grows and what shrinks it. How much is waiting costing you?',ideas:{'compound-interest-slowly_1':{title:'The Straight-Line Guess',card:{insight:'Money grows on its own growth, so your gut guesses far too low.',tryThis:'Use a free savings calculator to grow $100 at 7 percent for 30 years. Then compare it to your guess.',skills:['money','thinking']},review:{prompt:'At 7 percent, a friend says $100 makes $310 in 30 years. What is wrong?',right:'She adds the same $7 a year, but the growth grows too',wrong:'Nothing, 7 percent a year adds up to about that',why:'Each year\u2019s growth grows too, so the money doubles about every ten years.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You put **\xa3100** in a bank. Every year it adds 7p for each \xa31 inside. You leave it alone for thirty years. How much do you think is **in there now**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Most people guess about **\xa3310**. That guess adds \xa37 every year. But the bank pays on the whole pot, and the pot keeps growing. So it **doubles** roughly every ten years. That\u2019s about *\xa3760* by the end.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When money grows, **the growth grows** too. Your gut guesses a straight line, so it guesses **far too low**. Don\u2019t guess, just do the sum.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Someone asks you to guess. **\xa3100** grows at seven per cent a year for thirty years. What does it become? Most people say about three times as much. Are they **even close**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Two savers earn seven per cent. One pays \xa31,000 a year from 25 to 34, then stops. The other pays the same from 35 to 64. At 65 the early saver has about **\xa3112,500**. The late one has about \xa3101,000, from triple the money.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Your gut and the arithmetic take the **same seven per cent** and reach **very different** answers.',left:{title:'The gut',items:['Adds seven per cent thirty times','Ends near three times']},right:{title:'The sum',items:['Doubles about every ten years','Ends near 7.6 times']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s why waiting feels free. In the straight-line picture, ten years late costs **a third**. In the real sum, it drops **the last doubling**, which is the biggest. So a ten-year delay costs you about half.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Nobody grows **a better gut** for this. So don\u2019t trust the guess. **Run the number** once, and the cost of waiting becomes something you can see.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Everyone has heard that **compounding is powerful**. You\u2019ve probably nodded along yourself. Yet people repeat the slogan and still put off saving for years. Why does knowing it **change so little**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The piece\u2019s answer is that **intuition is linear**. It pictures seven per cent stacking up evenly, thirty times. The real sum **doubles every decade** or so. That gap is exactly how delay feels free while costing about half.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take \xa3100 at seven per cent. The straight-line guess for year thirty is about \xa3310. The real pot passes that in year seventeen. By year thirty it\u2019s nearer **\xa3760**, after three doublings.'},{kind:'story',text:'Two savers, same seven per cent. One pays \xa31,000 a year for ten years from 25, then stops. The other pays for thirty years from 35. At 65, the first has more: about \xa3112,500 against \xa3101,000.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Every figure assumes a **steady seven per cent**, and no real investment delivers that. At six per cent or below, the late saver\u2019s thirty years of payments win. The sum shows **the shape**, not a promise.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You won\u2019t ever **feel** compounding, so stop waiting to. Run the number once, with and without a ten-year delay. The gap is **the price of waiting**.'}]},'compound-interest-slowly_2':{title:'Slow Now, Fast Later',card:{insight:'The last years add the most, since they grow on the biggest pile.',tryThis:'Write down how much you have saved today. Look again in a year, and keep going.',skills:['money','habits']},review:{prompt:'Sam has saved for five years, and the pile has barely grown. Now what?',right:'Keep going, since the early years build the pile',wrong:'Stop, since five slow years prove it is not working',why:'The slow early years build the pile that the last big years grow on.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve saved for three years. The bank has added **a few pounds**, and that\u2019s all. It feels like nothing is happening. Should you take it out and **buy a bike**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Think of **a snowball**. At first each roll picks up a thin layer, so it grows slowly. Once it\u2019s big, each roll picks up loads. Money that grows on itself works **the same way**: slow first, fast at the end.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The quiet years **aren\u2019t wasted**. They build the pile that the big years grow from. So if you stop early, you **miss the fast part**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve paid **\xa31,000 a year** into savings for five years. The statement shows barely more than you put in. Friends say you\u2019d have more fun spending it. Is this **really working**?'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Pay \xa31,000 a year at **seven per cent** for thirty years, and compare **the two stretches**.',left:{title:'First twenty years',items:['\xa320,000 paid in','About \xa323,900 of growth']},right:{title:'Last ten years',items:['\xa310,000 paid in','About \xa347,200 of growth']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Each year\u2019s growth lands on **a bigger base**. So the last ten years add more than the first twenty. Half the deposits, **twice the growth**. Most of it is growth on money you laid down early.'},{kind:'explain',text:'That\u2019s why the usual failure is **quitting**, not bad maths. The early years show the least, just when sticking with it matters most. So the evidence in front of you **argues for stopping**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The years that look emptiest are **laying the base**. Don\u2019t judge the series by its start. **Keep going**, because the big years only come to those who stay.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Leave \xa3100 at seven per cent for thirty years. Which adds more: **the first twenty years**, or **the last ten**? Most people pick the long stretch. Do you?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The last ten win easily. The first twenty add about **\xa3290**. The last ten add about **\xa3370**. Each year\u2019s growth sits on a larger base, so a long series is **back-loaded**. Its big gains cluster at the end.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture someone four years into a pension. Each statement looks almost flat. The returns are least visible exactly when persistence matters most. So everything in front of her says **stop**. She stops, and never reaches the part that pays.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take someone learning the piano. The first year of practice sounds clumsy and feels like it\u2019s going nowhere. The piece suggests skills that build on themselves can share this shape. That slow look isn\u2019t evidence against it.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. For one lump sum, the last ten years beat the first twenty only above **about five per cent**. It also assumes the late years arrive. And many skills **run the other way**, fast at first, then slower.'},{kind:'moral',text:'In a long series, **the start builds** what the end collects. The early years feel empty because they\u2019re building the base. So when your progress looks flat, **don\u2019t read** that as failure.'}]},'compound-interest-slowly_3':{title:'The Fee That Grows',card:{insight:'A small fee that you pay each year can eat a quarter of your pile.',tryThis:'Find the yearly fee on one account you hold. Write it down next to what the account earns.',skills:['money','thinking']},review:{prompt:'A bank says its 1 percent fee is too small to matter. What is wrong?',right:'It comes out of the whole pile each year, so it adds up',wrong:'Nothing, since 1 percent is just a tiny bit',why:'The fee comes out of your whole pile each year, so it adds up fast.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two banks both grow your money by **7p for every \xa31** each year. One quietly takes 1p of it as a fee. That sounds like nothing. After thirty years, **what\u2019s the difference**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Put **\xa3100** in each. With no fee, it grows to about \xa3760. With the fee, it grows by 6p, not 7p, and reaches about \xa3570. That tiny fee took about **a quarter** of your money.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A fee is taken **every single year**. So it grows the way your money does, just **in the wrong direction**. Small fees aren\u2019t small over a long time.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your fund grows at seven per cent and charges **one per cent** a year. You barely notice it. Over thirty years, how much of your final balance do you think the fee takes? **Guess first**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Put \xa310,000 in at seven per cent and leave it thirty years. Without a fee, you\u2019d have about **\xa376,000**. Take one per cent off every year and it grows at six instead. You end with about \xa357,400.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Why so much? The fee is charged on **the whole balance**, every year, not just on the gain. So it grows as the pot grows. And every pound it takes is a pound that **never compounds**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The same fee looks **tiny or large**, depending on **what you measure** it against.',left:{title:'Share of assets',items:['One per cent a year','Sounds negligible']},right:{title:'Share of gain',items:['About 14% of a 7% return','Every single year']}},{kind:'moral',text:'One per cent sounds small, is quoted yearly and is **taken automatically**, so you never feel it. Don\u2019t judge a fee by its headline. Judge it by **what it removes**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve seen what compounding does **for you**. Now turn the formula around. Nothing in the maths changes when the percentage is **a charge**. It still runs on the whole balance, every year. So what does it do?'},{kind:'explain',text:'An annual fee is **a negative return** on the whole balance. The piece\u2019s trick is to measure it against the gain, not the assets. One per cent of a pot growing at seven is about **fourteen per cent** of the gain.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take \xa310,000 for thirty years. At seven per cent it reaches about \xa376,000. With a one per cent fee, it reaches about \xa357,400. You never wrote a cheque, yet roughly **a quarter** has gone.'},{kind:'story',text:'An adviser tells you their fee of one and a half per cent hardly matters. In a good year, you\u2019d barely notice it. But the piece\u2019s maths says that fee takes about **a third** of your final pot over thirty years.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. A fee is wasted only if it **buys nothing**. A dearer adviser who keeps you invested through a fall may be worth it. And the quarter depends on seven per cent, which is **never guaranteed**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A fee **compounds against you**, charged on everything you hold. So don\u2019t read the headline rate. Read it as a **share of your gain**, and decide whether it earns that.'}]},'compound-interest-slowly_4':{title:'The Silent Shrink',card:{insight:'When prices rise faster than your cash grows, your cash buys less each year.',tryThis:'Write down how much cash you need for a bad surprise. Then decide what the rest is for.',skills:['money','thinking']},review:{prompt:'Your savings earn 2 percent while prices rise 3 percent. What is going on?',right:'Your cash buys less each year, even though the number goes up',wrong:'It is growing slowly, since the number goes up each year',why:'When prices rise faster than your cash grows, your cash buys less.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A chocolate bar costs **\xa31** today. Say prices rise by 3p for every \xa31, each year. Next year it costs a bit more. Keep going for twenty-four years. What does **that same bar** cost now?'},{kind:'explain',text:'It costs about **\xa32**. Your \xa31 coin hasn\u2019t changed, but it now buys **half a bar**. Nobody took anything from you. The number stayed the same, while what it buys got smaller.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Money that just sits there **buys less** each year. Keep some cash for emergencies, like a broken phone. But money you won\u2019t need for years **shouldn\u2019t just sit**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You keep **\xa310,000** in a current account paying nothing. You check it every month. It\u2019s always \xa310,000. Nothing is deducted, and no statement shows a loss. Isn\u2019t this **the safe part** of your money?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Inflation runs **compounding in reverse** on what your money buys. At three per cent, prices roughly double in **twenty-four years**. By then your \xa310,000 buys only about what \xa34,900 buys today.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Why don\u2019t people react? The loss has **no transaction**. Nothing is taken, the balance never moves, and only its buying power changes. We respond to losses we see, and this one is **never seen**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Holding cash **isn\u2019t avoiding risk**. It\u2019s **choosing which risk** you take on.',left:{title:'Cash',items:['No ups and downs','A slow, near-certain real decline']},right:{title:'Invested',items:['Real ups and downs','A chance of beating prices']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Cash has a real job: **a buffer**, so a bad moment never forces you to sell. Keep that much. Money you won\u2019t need for twenty years is **shrinking** in it.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Nothing is deducted. No statement shows a loss. The balance in your account hasn\u2019t moved in years. So why does the piece call cash **compounding in reverse**, and **a poor place** for long-term money?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Inflation applies **the same formula** to prices. At three per cent, they roughly double in twenty-four years. Cash that earns less than that is shrinking, compounded. A return can look positive and still be **a real loss**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture someone who keeps their savings in cash that pays nothing, for a forty-year working life. Every statement shows the same number. Nothing ever looks wrong. By the end, that money buys under a third of what it once did.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now someone needs \xa320,000 for a house deposit in eight months. They hold it in cash. By the piece\u2019s own argument, that\u2019s right. Cash is **the buffer** that stops you selling at a bad moment.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Interest offsets part of the decline, and in some periods cash has paid close to inflation. Inflation isn\u2019t a steady three per cent either. And investments can **fall sharply** just when you need the money.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Cash is **a choice of risk**, not an escape from it. Keep the buffer **you really need**. For money that will sit for decades, watch the quiet loss.'}]},'compound-interest-slowly_5':{title:'Let It Run',card:{insight:'Long-term saving pays off most when you do not break the plan.',tryThis:'Set your bank app to save on each payday. Then write down the few things that would make you stop.',skills:['money','habits']},review:{prompt:'Your savings have dropped for 18 months, and the news is scary. What now?',right:'Keep the bank app paying in, so there is nothing to decide',wrong:'Stop paying in for a few months until things calm down',why:'Savings grow when you leave the plan alone, so do not stop for bad news.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You plant some seeds. A week later, **nothing shows**. So you dig one up to check. Then another. What happens to the seeds **you dig up**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'They can\u2019t grow. Saving money is **the same**. The sums are easy to learn. The hard part is **keeping going** when it looks like nothing\u2019s happening, or when the pot drops for a while.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Make saving happen **by itself**, so there\u2019s no choice to get wrong. Then check it **only now and then**. Leaving it alone is how it grows.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve paid into a fund every month for **eighteen months**. It\u2019s now worth slightly less than you put in. The news is gloomy. Pausing until things settle seems **obviously sensible**. Is it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The arithmetic takes **an afternoon** to learn. The behaviour is the hard part. A long strategy pays you mostly **for not breaking it**. And breaking it feels most urgent at the worst moments.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Holding through a fall, paying in when returns are negative, not shuffling after a good year. Each break from these feels **sensible on the day**. Added up, they **break the series**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'There are two ways to deal with a decision you\u2019ll **reliably get wrong**: improve it, or **remove it**.',left:{title:'Improve it',items:['Learn more about markets','Decide better in the moment']},right:{title:'Remove it',items:['Automatic contributions','A written plan in advance','Check the balance less']}},{kind:'moral',text:'You don\u2019t need **better nerve**. You need fewer chances to use it. Make carrying on automatic, so that **stopping takes effort** and continuing takes none.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'The maths of compounding can be taught **in an afternoon**. Yet almost nobody manages the behaviour it asks for. What\u2019s so hard about **doing nothing** when you know it works?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Compounding rewards **the absence of interruption**. Yet the urge to interrupt peaks at exactly the moments that matter. A fall. A run of losses. A good year that tempts you to shuffle. Each feels like **a reason to act**.'},{kind:'story',text:'The piece points to comparisons of what investors earn with what their funds earn. People earn less than the funds they own. It calls the gap **large and persistent**, and reads it as the cost of well-meant interventions.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a saver after a good year. She moves money into whatever did best. Next year she pauses after a fall. Each step is **locally reasonable**. Together, the loop quietly eats her return.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Automation locks in whatever plan you set, **including a bad one**. Lives change: a lost job, a new need, a buffer that\u2019s run out. So the rule is narrower. Market mood can\u2019t reopen the plan, but **your life** can.'},{kind:'moral',text:'For a decision you\u2019ll reliably get wrong, **remove it** rather than improve it. Automate the payment, write the plan down and **check less often**. Then carrying on costs nothing.'}]}}}},953,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'deciding-without-knowing',kind:'book',promise:'Learn from your choices, not your luck',about:'Did a choice go badly because you chose badly, or because of bad luck? This short essay from the Mindroll team says the result alone cannot tell you. These five ideas show how to learn from your choices anyway. Want to try them?',ideas:{'deciding-without-knowing_1':{title:'The Lucky Shot',card:{insight:'Every result is part choice and part luck, so judge what you knew.',tryThis:'Tonight, write down one choice that went well, and how much of it was luck.',skills:['thinking']},review:{prompt:'A coworker made a risky bet, and it paid off. What should you think?',right:'A good result can come from luck, not a smart choice',wrong:'She must make better choices than the rest of us',why:'Each result is part choice and part luck, so look at what she knew.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your friend shoots **from halfway** instead of passing. It goes in, and everyone cheers. Next match, the coach says to keep shooting like that. Is that **good advice**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Try that shot again and it\u2019ll **probably miss**. The goal was mostly luck. Passing was **the better choice**, even though it wouldn\u2019t have got the cheer. A good result doesn\u2019t prove you chose well.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Results are part choice and **part luck**. So don\u2019t judge a choice only by **how it turned out**. Ask if it was smart at the time.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two friends each **choose a job**. One researches carefully and picks a stable firm. The other picks a start-up **on a whim**. A year later, you\u2019re at dinner with both. Who gets called brilliant?'},{kind:'story',text:'The stable firm folded. The start-up thrived. At dinner, the careful friend is teased as naive, and the lucky one is toasted. Nobody asks what each of them knew when they chose. Only the result gets discussed.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Any decision worth agonising over involves **uncertainty**. So the result is part judgement and **part luck**, and you can\u2019t separate the two afterwards. Grade yourself on results alone and you\u2019ll learn **the wrong lesson**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Reviewing only outcomes **rewards** some choices and **punishes** others for the wrong reasons.',left:{title:'Judging the result',items:['Lucky risks look wise','Sound calls look foolish','Lessons come out backwards']},right:{title:'Judging the reasoning',items:['What was known then','What alternatives were weighed','A lesson that transfers']}},{kind:'moral',text:'You can\u2019t control the luck. You can control **the reasoning**: what you knew, what you assumed, what else you weighed. That\u2019s the part that **carries over** to the next decision.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A team takes **a reckless bet** on a new product, and it pays off. Another team plans carefully and misses. At the next review, guess **which process** gets copied. Should it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The essay\u2019s point is that outcomes **mix** decision quality with luck. After the fact, **the mixture** can\u2019t be separated. So outcome-only review rewards risks that happened to pay and punishes **sound judgement** that happened not to.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take the reckless team. Its launch worked, so its habits spread: skip the research, trust the hunch. The careful team\u2019s method is quietly dropped. Nobody checked whether the winners had simply been lucky.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now think of yourself choosing a route home. You take the usual road and hit a jam from a crash you couldn\u2019t have known about. Next day you avoid that road. You\u2019ve learned from an outcome, not from a mistake.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. From one decision you often **can\u2019t tell** skill from luck. A run of bad results may mean the reasoning was worse than it looked. Outcomes aren\u2019t the verdict, but they\u2019re **still evidence**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Judge the **reasoning**: what was known, what was assumed, what else was considered. That\u2019s **assessable**, and it\u2019s the only part you can take into your next decision.'}]},'deciding-without-knowing_2':{title:'Write It Down First',card:{insight:'You forget your first guess once you know the end, so write it down first.',tryThis:'Before a big choice, write down what you expect and why, then keep the note.',skills:['thinking','habits']},review:{prompt:'Your friend said your team would win. They lost. Now he says he knew. Why?',right:'He forgot his first guess once he knew the end',wrong:'He is lying to cover up his wrong guess',why:'Once you know how it ends, you forget the guess you made at the start.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your team **loses the final**. On the way home, your friend says he knew all along they\u2019d lose. But before the match, he said **you\u2019d win easily**. Is he lying?'},{kind:'explain',text:'He isn\u2019t lying. He **really remembers** it that way now. Our memory quietly changes to match **what happened**. That\u2019s why it helps to write your guess down before you find out.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your memory **can\u2019t be trusted** about what you expected. A note on paper **can\u2019t change** later. So write your guess down first, then check it.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Think back to a big decision from last year. What did you **expect to happen**? Now, honestly: is that your memory, or is it **what actually happened**, wearing your memory\u2019s clothes?'},{kind:'explain',text:'After the fact, people recall having expected **whatever happened**. They recall it confidently, with **no awareness** of the change. So unless you wrote it down first, you can\u2019t get honest feedback on your judgement.'},{kind:'story',text:'Maya is weighing a new role. Before answering, she writes three lines. She expects to be happier within six months. The reason is the smaller team. She\u2019ll know she was wrong if she dreads Mondays.'},{kind:'explain',text:'A written prediction **can\u2019t be revised** silently. Add a rough probability, and over time you build a record. It shows **calibration**: whether your seventy per cents come true seventy per cent of the time.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your memory edits **what you expected** to fit what happened. A few lines **written beforehand** are the only defence. Without them, you can\u2019t learn how good your judgement really is.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Ask people what they expected **before an election**, a match or a job change. Many will tell you, with total confidence, that they **saw it coming**. Were they really that good at predicting?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The essay\u2019s answer is **hindsight**. Memory of an earlier belief is rebuilt after the fact, and it\u2019s often **contaminated** by the outcome. People recall expecting what they saw, and they don\u2019t notice the revision.'},{kind:'story',text:'Before a hire, a manager writes a short note. She gives it seventy per cent that the person thrives, because of their record. A year later, the note shows what she really thought. Her memory had drifted.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now imagine you keep such notes for every big call. After fifty, you can check whether your seventy per cents come true seventy per cent of the time. That\u2019s calibration, and only a record reveals it.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. A record defeats silent revision, not **self-deception** while writing. People hedge, write vaguely or skip the call they\u2019re least sure of. And a handful of predictions tells you little; calibration needs **many**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You can\u2019t trust memory to report **what you expected**. So write it down before the result arrives. Without that note, honest feedback on **your judgement** isn\u2019t possible.'}]},'deciding-without-knowing_3':{title:'The Undo Question',card:{insight:'Go fast on choices you can undo, and slow on ones you cannot.',tryThis:'Before your next choice, ask what it would cost to undo in six months.',skills:['thinking','productivity']},review:{prompt:'You have spent a week picking a new desk lamp. What should you ask?',right:'What would it cost to undo if it is wrong?',wrong:'Which lamp is the very best one I could buy?',why:'You can send a lamp back, so save your slow thinking for big choices.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You spend **twenty minutes** choosing which sweets to buy. Then you pick a school club for the whole year **in ten seconds**, because the form is due. What went wrong there?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Some choices are **easy to undo**. If the sweets are bad, you buy different ones next time. Others are **hard to undo**. The club lasts all year. Those are the ones that deserve your slow thinking.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Before you decide, ask **how easy** it\u2019d be to change your mind. Easy means decide quickly. Hard means **take your time**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A team spends **three meetings** choosing a font for an internal slide deck. The same month, it signs **a two-year contract** with a supplier after one call, because the deadline was Friday. Sound familiar?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Decisions differ hugely in **the cost of error**. A reversible one can be made fast, on incomplete information, since being wrong costs only the reversal and some time. An irreversible one deserves **the slow process**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The common failure is giving **every choice** the same amount of thought, and **in the wrong places**.',left:{title:'Easy to undo',items:['The font','Decide fast','Fix it if wrong']},right:{title:'Hard to undo',items:['The two-year contract','Slow down','Get it right first']}},{kind:'explain',text:'So ask one question first: what would it cost to undo this in **six months**? Your answer sets the process. Asking takes **a minute**, which is less than the deliberation it often saves you.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Sort your decisions by **how reversible** they are before anything else. Move quickly on the ones you can undo. Save **your slow thinking** for the ones you can\u2019t.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Most people spend roughly the same worry on every decision. They agonise over **which phone case** to buy and rush **a job offer** because it arrived with a deadline. Why does the effort land in the wrong place?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The essay says **reversibility** should set how much analysis a choice gets. Usually it doesn\u2019t. Urgency does instead. So irreversible choices get rushed because they arrived urgently, while **reversible ones** get the agonising.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take someone choosing a gym class. She could research for a week. Instead she books one session, hates it, and tries another. Being wrong cost her an hour. Speed was the right call.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now the same person is offered a job abroad. Saying yes means giving up her flat and her current role. That can\u2019t be cheaply undone. Here a slow week of thinking is worth every hour.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Reversibility is **easy to misjudge**. A hire or a public promise is reversible on paper and sticky in practice. And a string of small reversible choices can add up to **an irreversible drift**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Before you deliberate, ask what **undoing it** would cost you. Cheap to undo means **move fast**. Expensive means slow down, whatever the deadline says.'}]},'deciding-without-knowing_4':{title:'It Already Failed',card:{insight:'If you say the plan already failed, people tend to name more problems.',tryThis:'Before a big plan starts, say it failed a year from now and write why.',skills:['thinking','leadership']},review:{prompt:'Your team asks what could go wrong. Only two small risks come up. What next?',right:'Say it failed a year from now, and ask why',wrong:'Take the two risks and move on with the plan',why:'Say the plan failed, and people share the fears they did not say out loud.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your family plans **a camping trip**. Someone asks what could go wrong. Everyone says *not much*. Then someone says, imagine it\u2019s Sunday and the trip was **a disaster**. What happens next?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Suddenly everyone has ideas: **the tent leaks**, the car\u2019s too full and nobody packed the torch. Pretending it already went wrong makes it **easier to explain** why. Now you can fix those things before you go.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Asking what *might* go wrong gets **polite answers**. Pretending it **already failed** gets real ones. Then you can fix them in time.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A team is reviewing **a launch plan**. The leader asks for risks. Two polite, minor ones come up, and everyone nods. Three people in the room have **a private doubt**. Will anyone say it?'},{kind:'story',text:'Then she changes the question. It\u2019s a year from now, she says, and the launch failed. Take five minutes and write down why. The list runs to a page. On it is the doubt three people had kept quiet.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The change is in **the tense**. Asking what *might* go wrong produces a short, polite list. Stating that it **has failed** licenses pessimism, so people give specific causes. They seem better at explaining a fact than predicting one.'},{kind:'explain',text:'It also makes **dissent safe**. In an ordinary review, a doubter has to challenge the group. In a pre-mortem, you\u2019re just **doing the exercise**. That\u2019s a much lower bar, so private worries come out.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Before you commit, **assume it failed** and explain why. You\u2019ll get **concrete causes** instead of polite guesses, and the doubts people were keeping to themselves.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Before a big project starts, most teams ask what **might go wrong**. The answers are usually short and polite. What if you asked a different question, one that assumes the worst **has already happened**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'That\u2019s **the pre-mortem**. Before committing, you assume the project has failed and write the explanation. Asserting failure licenses pessimism, so people name **specific, concrete causes** instead of a tidy list of maybes.'},{kind:'story',text:'A charity plans a fundraising walk. Asked for risks, the committee mentions rain. Told the walk flopped, they find more. The date clashes with a festival, the route has no toilets, and nobody owns the publicity.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a junior engineer who suspects the new system won\u2019t scale. In a normal review, saying so means challenging her seniors. In the pre-mortem, it\u2019s simply her answer to the exercise. So she writes it.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The evidence shows more problems get named, not that plans reviewed this way **go better**. A long list can sit unread. And if the leader clearly loves the plan, **the safety** for doubters is weaker.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Assume **it has failed**, then explain why. You\u2019ll surface causes and doubts that polite questions keep hidden. Then the hard part is yours: **acting on the list**.'}]},'deciding-without-knowing_5':{title:'Smart but Unlucky',card:{insight:'A smart choice can end badly, and a lucky win can be a warning.',tryThis:'Today, write down one choice that went badly, and if it made sense then.',skills:['thinking','leadership']},review:{prompt:'You did not study for a test but still got an A. What is the lesson?',right:'A good grade from a bad choice is a warning',wrong:'You do not need to study for tests like that',why:'A lucky win can make a bad choice look smart, so judge what you knew.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You didn\u2019t practise for a **spelling test**. You guessed, and you got **full marks**. The words just happened to be easy. What will you do before next week\u2019s test?'},{kind:'explain',text:'It\u2019s tempting to think **practice doesn\u2019t matter**. But the marks came from luck, not from a good plan. Next week the words could be hard. A lucky win is **a warning**, not a reason to skip practice again.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When something ends, ask if **your plan** was a good one. A good plan can still go wrong. A lucky win can still **teach you badly**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two campaigns finish. One beats its target, and its review lasts **ten minutes**, all congratulations. The other was planned with more care and misses. Its review lasts **two hours**. What\u2019s missing from both?'},{kind:'story',text:'The two-hour review ends with a new sign-off process. The ten-minute one changes nothing. Nobody asks whether either decision was sound given what was knowable then. The results wrote both lessons.'},{kind:'explain',text:'A bad result after **sound reasoning** is variance, and it should change nothing. A good result after **poor reasoning** is a warning, not a validation. That second case is where **the damage** is done.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Why? **Success reinforces** whatever process produced it, including reckless ones that got lucky. You won\u2019t see it happening. And a review that hands out blame can\u2019t also get **honest reasoning**, because everyone reconstructs favourably.'},{kind:'moral',text:'After any outcome, ask whether the call was **sensible** given what you knew. Sometimes the honest answer is *well made and unlucky*. Saying that **without flinching** is how you learn.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A shop owner skips **a safety check** to save time and has a record year. So the check gets dropped for good. Was that **a good decision**, or just a lucky one?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The essay calls reviewing **the reasoning** the discipline everything else rests on. Given what was knowable then, was it **a sensible call**? A good result after a reckless call is a warning, not a validation.'},{kind:'story',text:'The shop is a clean example. Its result wrote the lesson, and nobody checked it. The record year said nothing about the risk the owner was running. Success simply made the reckless habit permanent.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a hospital ward has one bad night and rewrites a careful rota in response. The rota may have been sound. The night may have been variance. Without reviewing the reasoning, nobody can tell which.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. *Well made and unlucky* is **easy to abuse**. File every failure as variance and the review becomes its own superstition. And some failures, like **harm to others**, need accountability straight away.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Separate your review from **the blame**, then ask if the call was sound. If you can say **well made, unlucky** without flinching, you\u2019re learning, not guessing.'}]}}}},954,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'decisions-that-look-obvious-afterwards',kind:'book',promise:'Judge old choices by what people knew then',about:'Why does an old choice look so obvious once you know how it ended? The Mindroll team, who make this app, wrote this short essay about it. Here are five ways to judge the past fairly, even your own choices. Want to try them?',ideas:{'decisions-that-look-obvious-afterwards_1':{title:'The Moving Memory',card:{insight:'Once you know the end, you think you were more sure than you were.',tryThis:'Next time a past choice looks obvious, look for a note from before the end.',skills:['thinking','history']},review:{prompt:'A project fails, and the team says the warning signs were clear. Why?',right:'Knowing the end made the signs look clearer than they were',wrong:'The team missed signs that anyone could have seen',why:'Once you know the end, your memory moves, so old signs look clearer.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Before the big match, you think your team **might win**. You\u2019re not sure at all. Then they win three nil. A week later, what do you **remember thinking**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Most people remember being **more sure** than they were. Picture your guess written in pencil. When the result comes, your mind rubs it out and writes it again, **closer to the end**. Then it forgets it used the rubber.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Knowing the ending **moves your memory** of what you expected. You can\u2019t feel it happen. So the past always looks **more obvious** than it really was.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A supplier your firm chose goes bust six months later. At the review, everyone agrees **the warning signs** were obvious. Nobody has opened the file from **the day of signing**. What are they really remembering?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s what experiments find. People who learn a result recall their earlier guess as **much closer to it**. They say so with confidence. Told that their memory has moved, they deny it. **Warning them** doesn\u2019t undo it.'},{kind:'explain',text:'That traps any review **done from memory**. To judge a call, you compare what was expected with what happened. But your only record of the expectation is a memory **the ending has edited**. So the review finds it foreseeable.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Once you know the ending, your memory of the odds **quietly moves**. Every past decision then looks obvious or foolish. Don\u2019t trust **either reading** until you\u2019ve found a record from before.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Last month you gave yourself **even odds** of getting a job. You got it. Now a friend asks how likely you thought it was. Notice the answer that rises first. Is it really **fifty-fifty**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Learning an outcome **rewrites your memory** of what you expected. The rewrite isn\u2019t a choice, so knowing about it doesn\u2019t let you refuse it. You **can\u2019t detect** the change from inside. And you can\u2019t correct what you can\u2019t see.'},{kind:'story',text:'In the experiments, people learned a result and then recalled their earlier prediction. Their recall sat much **closer to the result**. They gave it confidently. When it was suggested they\u2019d shifted, they rejected the idea.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a manager who doubted a plan that later failed. Today she remembers being **certain** it would fail. She isn\u2019t lying. But the memory has been edited in the direction that **flatters her**. Only an older note could say.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Hindsight doesn\u2019t mean nothing was foreseeable. Some decisions really were bad, and some warnings really were ignored. Memory alone just can\u2019t **tell those cases** from the rest. Evidence from the time still can.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A review from memory will find **the outcome foreseeable**, whether it was or not. That includes your reviews of yourself. So distrust how obvious the past looks, **most of all** when it flatters you.'}]},'decisions-that-look-obvious-afterwards_2':{title:'What Was on the Desk',card:{insight:'Judge a choice by what the person knew on the day they made it.',tryThis:'Before you judge a past choice, find notes that show what the person knew.',skills:['thinking','leadership']},review:{prompt:'A new hire quit after a month. What do you bring to the review?',right:'Notes from that day showing what you knew when you hired',wrong:'Your memory of the signs and how the month went',why:'Notes from that day show what you knew, but your memory has changed since.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'The sky is clear and the forecast says **sunny**. You leave your coat at home. At lunch, **it pours**. You\u2019re soaked. Was leaving the coat a bad choice?'},{kind:'story',text:'Your class picked Sam as team captain. Then Sam was ill on the day of the game, and you lost. People said it was a silly pick. But nobody knew Sam would be ill. On the day you chose, Sam was a good choice.'},{kind:'moral',text:'To judge a choice, don\u2019t ask **if it worked**. Ask what the person **knew at the time**, and how long they had to decide.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A product launch your team approved flops badly. At the review, a director calls it **a reckless call**. The room nods. Before you nod too, what should you **ask for** first?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The honest question isn\u2019t **whether it worked**. It\u2019s what was on the desk that day. How reliable was it? How long was there? What could reasonably have been found out, and what were **the alternatives**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'That takes **documents**, not recollection: the emails, the data as it stood, the forecast in its first version. Memory of what was known is exactly what **hindsight corrupts**. So you rebuild the desk from the record.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Judge by result and you learn about **luck**. Judge by **the desk** and you learn something.',left:{title:'By outcome',items:['Did it work?','Teaches you about luck','Doesn\u2019t carry over']},right:{title:'By information',items:['What was known then?','Teaches you about process','Carries to the next call']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Decisions that look absurd usually stop looking absurd once **the desk is restored**. A few look worse, and that\u2019s worth knowing too. Either way, you\u2019re judging **the process**, which you can use again.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A board meets to review **an acquisition** that failed. One director remembers his doubts vividly. Another brings the papers from the week it was approved. Whose account should you trust **more**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A useful post-mortem rebuilds **the information**, not the outcome. What was known, unknown and findable? What were the options, and the case for the ones **turned down**? Then you\u2019re assessing process, which transfers. Outcome doesn\u2019t.'},{kind:'story',text:'Back to the board. The director\u2019s memory of his doubts has been shaped by the failure. The papers from that week haven\u2019t. They show what was **on the desk**, and so they\u2019re the fairer witness.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a team whose careful plan **met bad luck**. Judged by result, they\u2019d drop it. Rebuild the desk and it was sound, so they should **repeat it**. Only this method can find a good process behind a bad result.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Documents aren\u2019t neutral either. People chose what to write down, and what mattered may never have been recorded. Be generous enough and **every decision** looks reasonable. Rebuilding the desk can become a defence.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Ask what was knowable, not whether it worked. Rebuild the desk **from the record**, not from memory. You\u2019ll judge **the process** fairly, and some verdicts will get kinder while a few get harsher.'}]},'decisions-that-look-obvious-afterwards_3':{title:'Good Plan, Bad Luck',card:{insight:'A good plan can fail and a risky one can win, so judge the plan.',tryThis:'Next time a risky move works, write down if it was a good plan or just luck.',skills:['thinking','leadership']},review:{prompt:'Your coworker ignores the safety rules and bets big. It works. Good call?',right:'A risky plan that got lucky this time',wrong:'A bold call that the win has proved right',why:'One win does not turn a risky plan into a good one.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two classmates build **paper towers**. Jo braces hers carefully, while Max stacks his fast and wobbly. A stray ball knocks Jo\u2019s down, but Max\u2019s stays up and **wins the prize**. Who built better?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Jo\u2019s way will **usually** win. Max got lucky this time. If you only praise the tower that\u2019s standing, you teach everyone to **build wobbly**. Next time, their luck may not hold.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A good choice can end badly. A silly one can end well. So ask two things: was **my plan good**, and was I **just lucky**?'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two project leads launch in the same quarter. One researches the market and **tests the plan**. The other skips the testing and **bets on a hunch**. Guess which one gets promoted?'},{kind:'story',text:'The careful lead launches into a sudden downturn, and the numbers sink. The hunch catches a lucky wave. Judged by results alone, the first is passed over. The second is promoted, and everyone watching takes notes.'},{kind:'explain',text:'That\u2019s how **rewarding outcomes** alone teaches people to gamble. It\u2019s hard to avoid, because the outcome is public and vivid. **The process** is private and has to be rebuilt. So even people who know better grade on results.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The fix is to review on **what was known** and what was considered. Do it **in writing**, before the result arrives. Once you know the outcome, it colours everything, and your notes are the one judgement it can\u2019t reach.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A sound process can fail, and a **reckless one** can succeed. Reward results alone and you reward **the luck**. Judge your decisions apart from their outcomes, ideally before you know them.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A careful decision fails. A careless one succeeds. If you had to criticise one of them, which would you pick? Most systems of judgement **pick the wrong one**, and they do it for **an honest reason**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The reason is visibility. The outcome is **public, vivid and final**. Process is private and has to be reconstructed. So results become the evidence in plain view. And once a result is in, memory of the process **bends towards it**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Two leads launch the same quarter. One tests her plan and walks into a downturn. The other bets on a hunch and catches a lucky wave. The first is passed over. The second is promoted.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a trader who ignores the risk team and **bets big**. It pays off handsomely. Reward him, and everyone else learns the lesson too. The careful colleague whose sound plan met bad luck learns that **care isn\u2019t rewarded**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Outcomes aren\u2019t irrelevant. Across **many decisions**, results are the best evidence about a method. One that keeps failing is probably flawed, however good it looked on paper. The split matters most for a single call.'},{kind:'moral',text:'One result is **evidence**, not a verdict, on one decision. Judge what was known and considered, and write it down **before the outcome**. Otherwise you\u2019ll end up rewarding gambles and punishing care.'}]},'decisions-that-look-obvious-afterwards_4':{title:'The Menu That Night',card:{insight:'Only judge a choice against the ones that were there at the time.',tryThis:'When you hear that someone should have done better, ask if that choice was there.',skills:['thinking','history']},review:{prompt:'A critic says a town should have built flood walls sooner. What do you check?',right:'If building them sooner was a real choice at the time',wrong:'If the flood would have done less harm with walls',why:'We often judge a choice against one that was not there yet.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your class trip was to **a museum**. It rained, and it was dull. Now someone says you should have gone to the **new water park**. Should you have?'},{kind:'story',text:'But the water park opened **last week**. When the trip was booked, it wasn\u2019t there to pick. It\u2019s like your sister wishing you\u2019d bought the seeded loaf, when the shop only had plain ones left.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When you judge a choice, only count **the options** that were really there. People often compare a choice with one that **never existed**. That isn\u2019t fair, and it isn\u2019t true.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re reading about a firm that kept **every record on paper** for years. Why didn\u2019t they just put it all on a computer? Then you notice **the date** at the top of the page.'},{kind:'story',text:'The cheap software that would have made it easy **hadn\u2019t been built** yet. The option you\u2019re judging them against wasn\u2019t on the menu. Most confident hindsight works this way: it compares the choice with something that didn\u2019t exist.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Why does this happen? **Constraints fade** from the story. The information, the deadline, the budget and the politics pressed hard at the time. Now only the choice is visible, so **the menu** looks far wider than it was.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Before you call it a mistake, **restore the menu** to **what was on it**.',left:{title:'The hindsight menu',items:['Tech not yet built','Allies not yet willing','Facts not yet known']},right:{title:'The real menu',items:['What existed that week','What they could do','What they knew']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Most confident hindsight compares a decision with **an option nobody had**. So rebuild the constraints first: the time, the people, the information. The real alternatives are usually **far fewer** than the story suggests.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A historian argues that a general should have used **aircraft** to scout the battlefield. It sounds damning. Before you agree, what single fact would **sink the argument** completely?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The usual hindsight error is judging a choice against an option that **wasn\u2019t there**. Maybe the technology wasn\u2019t built. Maybe an ally wasn\u2019t willing, or a fact wasn\u2019t known. It\u2019s a what-if **nobody could choose**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Back to the general. If planes for scouting didn\u2019t exist in his war, the criticism falls apart. It compares his decision with an alternative nobody in his army could have picked, however clever.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a town criticised for building its flood barrier ten years late. Rebuild the constraint set: the budget then, the forecasts then, what the council could legally do. **The honest list** is often short.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. You can restore the menu dishonestly. Narrow it enough and **any decision** looks forced. Some options really were available and dismissed. Some constraints were chosen, like a deadline set for convenience.'},{kind:'moral',text:'After the fact, the options always look wide. Ask what could **actually have been done** that week, by those people, with that information. Then judge the choice against **the real list**.'}]},'decisions-that-look-obvious-afterwards_5':{title:'The Note in the Drawer',card:{insight:'A note with a date, made before the end, keeps what you really thought.',tryThis:'Before your next big choice, write down your guess. Then add the date and how sure you are.',skills:['thinking','habits']},review:{prompt:'You may open a second store in a new town. What helps you judge it later?',right:'A note of your guess with a date that you never change',wrong:'Your clear memory of your reasons when you look back',why:'Your memory moves, but a note from before the end stays the same.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Before sports day, you write on a scrap of paper: *Red team will win, I\u2019m fairly sure.* You fold it into **a drawer**. Then **Blue team** wins. Why might that scrap matter later?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Remember how memory moves once you know the end? In a week, you might think you **always said Blue**. The paper doesn\u2019t move. It shows **what you really thought**, even when it\u2019s not fun to see.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Before you find out how something ends, **write down** your guess and how sure you are. **Date it**, and don\u2019t change it. That\u2019s how you learn when to feel sure.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two years ago you made a hiring call that you now remember as **easy**. Then you find an old note in **your own handwriting**. It says *55%*, with two worries you\u2019d completely forgotten.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Memory moves towards whatever happened, and effort won\u2019t stop it. So the only defence is **a contemporaneous note**: written before the outcome, dated, and never revised. The decision wasn\u2019t easy. You\u2019d just **lost the record** of it being hard.'},{kind:'story',text:'The smallest useful version takes a minute. *12 March: the new hire will still be here in a year, 70%.* Then one reason, such as strong references but doubts about the travel. A prediction, a probability, a reason.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Over time the notes become **a calibration record**. You see whether the things you call 70% likely happen about 70% of the time. Almost nobody keeps one, because **the results sting** and the payoff comes later.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your memory of what you expected **can\u2019t be trusted** once you know the ending. A dated note can. Write it before, **don\u2019t edit it**, and you\u2019ll finally see where your estimates go wrong.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Ask yourself if you judge well. You\u2019ll probably say yes. Ask how you know, and you\u2019ll **point to memory**. But it\u2019s the memory **already edited** by every outcome since. So what would honest evidence look like?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The shift in memory can\u2019t be seen from inside or undone by warning. So honesty and reflection **won\u2019t recover** a past belief. The belief has to be **stored outside** your mind, before the outcome arrives, where the outcome can\u2019t reach it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a leader about to expand into a new market. She writes one paragraph: what she expects, how sure she is, and why. She dates it and leaves it alone. In two years it will show what she really thought.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take someone who\u2019s kept such notes for years. Reading them back, he finds his confident calls come true **less often** than he\u2019d assumed. It stings. But now he knows **where he\u2019s overconfident**, which no amount of reflection could tell him.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The record judges you over **many calls**, not one. A 70% call should fail about three times in ten, so one miss proves little. It\u2019s also easy to game with safe or vague predictions.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Hindsight can\u2019t edit what you wrote **before you knew**. So record the prediction, the confidence and the reason, then date it. If new facts arrive, write **a new note** and leave the old one alone.'}]}}}},955,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'deliberate-difficulty',kind:'book',promise:'Practice in ways that feel hard but stick',about:'Why does the study time that feels best often teach you the least? This short essay by the Mindroll team looks at practice that feels worse but works better. Most people skip it. Ready to try?',ideas:{'deliberate-difficulty_1':{title:'The Easy Reading Trap',card:{insight:'Notes can look easy to read even when you do not know them.',tryThis:'Next time your notes feel easy, shut them. Write what you remember, then check what you missed.',skills:['habits','science']},review:{prompt:'You read your notes three times, and they feel easy. What now?',right:'Shut the notes and test yourself',wrong:'Read them once more to feel sure',why:'The easy feeling comes from the page, not from what you can recall.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'It\u2019s the night before a test. You\u2019ve read your notes **three times**, and every line looks familiar. You feel ready. But do you **really know** them?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Each time you read a page, it gets **easier to read**. Your brain notices and whispers, *I know this.* But the test won\u2019t show you the page. It asks you to **bring the answer** up on your own.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Easy to read **isn\u2019t the same** as known. The easy feeling comes from **the page**, not your memory. So shut the notes and see what\u2019s left.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'It\u2019s the evening before an exam. You\u2019re on your **third pass** through the highlighted notes, and every line looks familiar. Next morning the paper asks you to explain one line **from scratch**. Nothing comes. What went wrong?'},{kind:'story',text:'The essay describes a clear pattern. Students who re-read rated their mastery higher and did worse on a delayed test. Students who spent the same time testing themselves felt less sure. They scored better.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s why they part ways. Re-reading makes the page **smoother**, so it feels like progress. Testing yourself is **unpleasant**, so it feels like failing. Your confidence follows the comfort. Your score follows the work.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The same hour of revision can build **the feeling** or **the result**.',left:{title:'Reading again',items:['Feels smooth','You feel sure','Delayed test goes worse']},right:{title:'Testing yourself',items:['Feels hard','You feel less sure','Delayed test goes better']}},{kind:'moral',text:'**Distrust ease**. If studying feels smooth, you\u2019re probably rehearsing recognition, not building recall. Test yourself cold, and let **the gap** show you the truth.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Ask people how they revise, and most will say they **re-read and highlight**. It feels productive, line after familiar line. So why does this essay open by putting **that feeling** on trial?'},{kind:'explain',text:'**Fluency** is how easily something is processed. Re-read notes become fluent, so they feel known. But that ease belongs to **the material**, not your memory. Learners take it as proof anyway, and pick the method that teaches least.'},{kind:'story',text:'In the experiments the essay leans on, two groups spent the same time studying. One re-read. One tested itself. The re-readers rated their mastery higher and did worse on a delayed test. Confidence and competence moved **in opposite directions**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a new starter driven to the office every day for a week. By Friday, the route feels obvious. The first morning she drives it herself, she misses the turn. Seeing it often built familiarity, not the ability to find it.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Ease isn\u2019t always a lie. Once you can recall something cold, it will feel easy too, and **that ease is earned**. The question is where the ease came from. And hard doesn\u2019t automatically mean good.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Treat ease as **a question**, not an answer. Smooth revision usually means **recognition**, not recall. So test yourself cold, and trust the score over the feeling.'}]},'deliberate-difficulty_2':{title:'Close the Book',card:{insight:'Trying to remember helps more than reading again, even when you get it wrong.',tryThis:'Close the book before you feel ready and write what you remember. Then look up what you missed.',skills:['habits','science']},review:{prompt:'You read a chapter once and understood it. What should you do next?',right:'Close the book and write what you remember',wrong:'Read it one more time so it sticks',why:'Each try to recall it makes it easier to find next time.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You have ten spellings to learn by Friday. You could read the list **one more time**. Or you could cover it and try to write the words. You try, and get **four wrong**. Was that a waste?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Not at all. Trying to remember helps you learn **more than reading** again, even when you get it wrong. It\u2019s like walking a path across a field. Reading is **being carried**. Only the walking makes the path.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Cover it up and **have a go** before you look. Getting some wrong is fine, as long as you **check after**. The hard try is doing the work.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Next week you must present a report you\u2019ve just read. You have **twenty minutes** now. You could read it again, or shut it and write the main points **from memory**. Which helps more?'},{kind:'story',text:'Priya picks memory. It\u2019s slow and leaves gaps, and she gets two points wrong before checking. Her colleague reads the report twice and feels ready. In the meeting, Priya can bring the points up. Her colleague can\u2019t.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Why does pulling beat looking? Each attempt to recall appears to **strengthen the route** back to the memory. Re-reading only builds **familiarity**. And familiarity gives you confidence without access. You feel you know it, then can\u2019t reach it.'},{kind:'explain',text:'One honest catch. Recall works best once you\u2019ve **understood the material** at least once. Testing yourself on something you never followed brings frustration, not learning. So the order is **understand, then recall**, then repeat.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Close the book **earlier than feels right**. Write what you remember before you look, and expect gaps. The struggle is **doing the work**, which is why almost nobody chooses it.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'The essay\u2019s first idea was a diagnosis: re-reading fools you. So what\u2019s **the treatment**? It has to be something that works, and something people will **actually do**. Those two turn out to pull apart.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The treatment is **retrieval**: trying to rebuild material without looking. The essay calls the gap over re-reading **large and well replicated**. And it holds even when the attempt fails. The failed try isn\u2019t wasted. It\u2019s doing the work.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a new hire learning forty product terms. Each evening she covers the definitions and writes what she can. Half are blank on Monday. By Friday, most come back without the list. Re-reading would have felt easier all week.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a student who skips the reading and quizzes himself on a chapter he never followed. Every attempt draws a blank. He isn\u2019t building recall. He\u2019s just **getting frustrated**, because there was nothing there to retrieve.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Retrieval has **a prerequisite**: you must understand the material once first. It also fails if you never check the answer after a miss. And because it\u2019s unpleasant, knowing about it isn\u2019t the same as doing it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Understand it once, then **pull it back** without looking. The gaps you hit are **the practice**, not proof you\u2019re failing. Close the book earlier than feels comfortable.'}]},'deliberate-difficulty_3':{title:'Shuffle the Deck',card:{insight:'Mixed practice feels worse while you practice but works better on tests later.',tryThis:'Next time you practice, mix a few kinds of problems together. Expect more mistakes and keep going.',skills:['habits','science']},review:{prompt:'You are practicing three kinds of math problems for a test. Which plan?',right:'Mix the three kinds and expect more mistakes',wrong:'Finish ten of one kind before you move on',why:'Mixing makes you pick the right kind each time, and that helps later.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'In a football match, nobody shouts what\u2019s coming next. A pass, then a header, then a shot. You have to **see it and pick**. So how should you **practise** for that?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Say you\u2019re learning three kinds of sum. You could do ten adding, then ten taking away. That feels easy, because you **never choose**. Mix them up and each sum asks, *which kind is this?* Picking is **the part tests need**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Mixing makes you **worse while you practise** and **better later on**. It can feel like it isn\u2019t working, so most people stop. Keep going, because it is.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re learning three kinds of spreadsheet formula. After twelve lookups in a row, the last few feel **effortless**. Then a colleague mixes all three into one sheet, and you keep reaching for **the wrong one**. What happened?'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture two learners with the same hour. One works in blocks: twelve of each type in turn. The other gets them shuffled. During practice, the blocked learner scores higher. On a test days later, the order flips.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Blocks hide **a decision**. When twelve problems in a row are lookups, you never ask which tool fits. The worksheet chose for you. Real problems don\u2019t arrive labelled. **Choosing the method** is the part that transfers.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Judge the session by **a later test**, not by **how it felt**.',left:{title:'Blocked',items:['Smooth and fast','Feels effective','Worse on later tests']},right:{title:'Shuffled',items:['Slow, with more mistakes','Feels like slipping','Better on later tests']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Shuffle related practice together and **accept feeling worse** while you do it. Your sense of a session is **a poor instrument**. Trust the later test instead.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two groups practise the same three problem types for the same time. One works in blocks, the other **in a mix**. You watch both sessions. Which group looks **better** while it practises, and which one would you bet on?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The blocked group looks better, reliably. On a later test, **the order flips**. The reason is **discrimination**. Blocks let you repeat one procedure without deciding which one applies. A mix forces that decision on every item, and deciding is what transfers.'},{kind:'story',text:'A sales trainee practises answers to three kinds of objection: price, timing and trust. She stops drilling them in sets and shuffles her prompt cards instead. She stumbles more. Real calls never arrive sorted, and she\u2019s readier for them.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now staff on a new training scheme. They say it feels harder and less useful than the old one. Yet their error rates on the job have fallen. Their feeling about the training and its result have **parted ways**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The benefit comes from choosing between skills that **could be confused**. Shuffling unrelated topics adds switching without that choice. And if you can\u2019t yet do any of the skills, mixing them just adds load.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Interleaving is one case of a bigger point. **Felt effectiveness** and real effectiveness come apart, and the bias runs **towards fluency**. Distrust a smooth session until a later test agrees.'}]},'deliberate-difficulty_4':{title:'Good Hard, Bad Hard',card:{insight:'Hard practice helps only when the hard part is the skill itself.',tryThis:'Write what makes one practice hard. Keep the parts that are the skill, and fix one that is not.',skills:['habits','thinking']},review:{prompt:'You teach a coworker a new system from an unclear guide. What should you fix?',right:'The unclear guide, since it is not the skill',wrong:'Making them recall the steps, since it is the hardest part',why:'Hard work in the skill helps you learn, but a bad guide just slows you down.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your teacher says hard work helps you learn. So is a test with **smudged printing** better than a clear one? Is homework in a **noisy room** better than in a quiet one?'},{kind:'story',text:'Your brother is learning his times tables. Saying them **without looking** is hard in a good way. It\u2019s the skill he needs. Doing it with the TV on loud is hard in **a bad way**. It just slows him down.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Hard is good when **the hard part** is the thing you\u2019re learning. When it comes from **something around it**, fix that. Turn off the TV, and keep the no-looking part.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re learning Spanish after work. On Tuesday you quiz yourself on verbs without the book, and **it\u2019s hard**. On Wednesday you\u2019re in a noisy caf\xe9 with a lesson two units ahead. That\u2019s **hard too**. Which evening teaches you more?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The essay is clear that **harder isn\u2019t always better**. Difficulty that works the process you\u2019re building helps. Difficulty from bad instructions, poor conditions or **missing groundwork** just gets in the way. Telling them apart is the skill.'},{kind:'explain',text:'So ask **where the difficulty sits**. Recalling verbs unaided is hard inside the skill, so it\u2019s productive. A noisy room, an unclear explanation or a topic too far ahead is hard around it. That\u2019s **just friction**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The same evening can carry **both kinds**, so sort them before you **push harder**.',left:{title:'In the skill',items:['Recalling without the book','Choosing which rule applies','Rebuilding a sentence']},right:{title:'Around the skill',items:['A noisy caf\xe9','An unclear explanation','A lesson two units ahead']}},{kind:'moral',text:'*Struggle is good* is **easy to misapply**. Make things needlessly hard and you get the discomfort of learning **without the learning**. Keep the hard part that\u2019s the skill, and remove the rest.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A tutor has heard that **struggle helps** learning. So she hands out a smudged, hand-copied worksheet, pitched a year ahead, in a noisy hall. Her students struggle a great deal. Are they **learning more**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The essay separates two kinds. **Desirable difficulties** engage the processes that produce learning: retrieval, spacing and mixing. Other difficulty only **consumes effort**. It adds load without engaging anything. The test is whether it\u2019s in the material or around it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Back to the tutor. Every difficulty she added sits around the material: the smudges, the level, the noise. Her students get the discomfort of learning without the learning. They\u2019re also discouraged, just as the essay warns.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a second tutor. She hands out a clear sheet at her students\u2019 level, in a quiet room. Then she makes them **answer without notes**, mixing the problem types. It\u2019s just as hard. This time, the hard part is the skill.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The line isn\u2019t always clean. A noisy room is friction for most students, but **the very skill** for someone training to work in noise. So you have to apply the test to a learner, not a worksheet.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Before you make practice harder, ask **where the difficulty sits**. Keep what\u2019s in the skill. Strip out **what\u2019s around it**, or you\u2019ll teach discomfort and little else.'}]},'deliberate-difficulty_5':{title:'Count to Five',card:{insight:'When you are learning facts, a short pause before the answer helps a little.',tryThis:'Next time you quiz yourself, say your answer. Then count to five before you check.',skills:['habits','science']},review:{prompt:'Your friend is learning state capitals. She stops to think. What do you do?',right:'Let her guess, then give the answer after a short pause',wrong:'Tell her the answer at once so no wrong guess sticks',why:'For facts, a short wait lets her compare her own guess with the answer.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your little sister is stuck on a sum. She opens her mouth to answer. Before she can, you **tell her** the right answer. She nods. Did that **help her learn** it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'She heard the answer, but she **never made her own**. So she had nothing to check it against. **The try** is the part that teaches. Wait a moment, let her have a go, then show her.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When you\u2019re helping someone learn a fact, **wait a moment** before you put them right. Their own try is **doing the work**. Then show them the answer.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re teaching a new hire the product range. You ask what one feature does. As they **draw breath**, you supply the answer. They nod. A week later, can they **tell you**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The proposed reason is simple. To learn from a correction, you hold your own attempt in mind and **compare it** with the answer. Answer for someone instantly, and there\u2019s **nothing to compare**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'A **short delay** keeps the attempt alive and still fixes the error. In some experiments it appears to help long-term memory. But the essay is careful: the effect is **modest**, and smaller than retrieval\u2019s.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The pause suits **some learning** and harms **other learning**.',left:{title:'Wait a moment',items:['Facts and names','Ideas and concepts','What a feature does']},right:{title:'Correct at once',items:['A tennis swing','A procedure practised daily','Errors that become habits']}},{kind:'moral',text:'For facts and ideas, **count to five** before you correct someone. For skills where a wrong move would be repeated, **fix it at once**. The pause helps, but it isn\u2019t a rule for everything.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A driving instructor reads that **delayed feedback** can help learners remember. On Monday her pupil drifts towards the kerb. Should she **wait five seconds** before saying anything?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s the claim. Correct someone instantly and **you may stop** them generating an answer at all. A short delay keeps their attempt in mind long enough to compare. That comparison seems to help **the correction stick**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a tutor running vocabulary drills. Each time a student hesitates, he waits a few seconds before giving the word. The student commits to a guess, then sees the answer. Wrong or right, there\u2019s something to compare.'},{kind:'story',text:'Back to the driving instructor. Her pupil\u2019s drift is a **procedural error**. If she waits, he keeps steering the wrong way and practises it. So she corrects him at once, and that\u2019s exactly right.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The effect is **smaller and less robust** than retrieval or spacing. It depends on the material. Wherever a wrong version could become a habit, immediate feedback is clearly better.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When you teach facts or concepts, a short delay probably helps and **can\u2019t hurt**. For anything that could become a habit, **correct it at once**. The principle doesn\u2019t survive being applied everywhere.'}]}}}},956,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'democracy-in-america',kind:'book',promise:'See your own country through a visitor\u2019s eyes',about:'What did a young man from France see here that most people missed? In 1831, Alexis de Tocqueville came to visit for nine months. Then he wrote about the good and the bad he saw. Which of his ideas do you still see today?',ideas:{'democracy-in-america_1':{title:'The Sting of Small Gaps',card:{insight:'When someone just like you gets a little more, it can sting a lot.',tryThis:'Next time someone like you gets more, ask if the gap is really big.',skills:['psychology','relationships']},review:{prompt:'A coworker gets a raise a bit bigger than yours. Why does it sting?',right:'She is just like you, so even a small gap stings',wrong:'The extra cash would help you pay your bills',why:'A boss or a movie star does not sting, but someone just like you does.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A king far away lives in a **huge palace**. Your best friend gets **\xa31 more** pocket money than you. Which one bugs you more? Most people pick the friend. Why would **one pound** sting?'},{kind:'explain',text:'In 1831 a Frenchman, Alexis de Tocqueville, visited **the United States**. In France some people were born lords. In America nobody was. He saw this changed **how a gap feels**. The king is nothing like you. Your friend is **just like you**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When people are nearly equal, small gaps **hurt the most**. It\u2019s because a gap you could have closed feels like **a mark against you**. Ask if it\u2019s big, or just close.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Everyone on your team is paid within **a narrow band**. You hear a peer got a slightly bigger rise than you. The amount is tiny. Why are you still **thinking about it** a week later?'},{kind:'story',text:'Alexis de Tocqueville was a French aristocrat. In 1831 he sailed to America with his friend Gustave de Beaumont, officially to study prisons. He found a country with **no hereditary rank**. He came home with a book about what equality does to people.'},{kind:'explain',text:'His point is about **what a gap means**. Under fixed rank, a lord\u2019s wealth is a fact, like the weather. Among equals, the same gap becomes **a verdict on you**, because in principle you could have closed it.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Same gap, either way. What changes is **what it seems** to say about **you**.',left:{title:'Fixed rank',items:['A fact, like weather','Nobody could close it','Rarely taken personally']},right:{title:'Equal on paper',items:['A verdict on you','You might have closed it','Taken very personally']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Equality doesn\u2019t end comparison. It **sharpens it**. When a small gap stings, ask whether it\u2019s large or just **close enough** to feel like your fault.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture a social feed full of people **broadly like you**: same age, same kind of job. Now picture a glimpse of a billionaire\u2019s yacht. Which one leaves you feeling worse? And why would **the near one** win?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Tocqueville\u2019s claim is that the desire for equality **grows as equality grows**. Each remaining gap stands out against **a level background**. So comparison doesn\u2019t fade as things level out. It gets sharper, and it gets personal.'},{kind:'story',text:'Tocqueville came from a noble French family that had suffered in the Revolution. Touring America in 1831, he met a society with **no inherited rank**. He predicted that its people would feel the gaps left over more sharply, not less.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take two sisters with near-identical jobs and pay. One moves into a house with one extra bedroom. The other has never minded her cousin\u2019s mansion. That single bedroom, though, she thinks about for months.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The idea can be turned on people with **real grievances**. Call a complaint mere comparison, and you never check whether the gap is unjust. Some gaps are large, and some are rigged.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A small gap stings because it\u2019s **close**, not because it\u2019s big. So name which kind you\u2019re facing. A close gap **loosens its grip** once named, while an unfair one still needs checking.'}]},'democracy-in-america_2':{title:'The Joining Habit',card:{insight:'Working with others is a skill, and you lose it if you stop.',tryThis:'Write down one problem on your block, and two neighbors you could ask to help.',skills:['leadership','relationships']},review:{prompt:'The park near your home is full of trash. What do you do?',right:'Ask two neighbors to help you clean it up this weekend',wrong:'Call the city and wait for them to clean it',why:'You learn to team up by doing it, and you forget if you stop.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'The park by your school is covered in **litter**. You could wait for the council to clean it. Or you and **two friends** could bring bags on Saturday. Which one actually gets the park clean?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Almost 200 years ago, a Frenchman called Tocqueville visited America. He was amazed. People there formed **groups for everything**: building churches, starting hospitals, handing out books. In France, the government did those jobs. Americans just **got together** and did them.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Working in a group is **a skill**, like playing an instrument. You get better each time you do it. Stop, and you\u2019ll **forget how**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A pothole on your street has been there **for months**. Everyone complains about it. Each of you assumes someone else will report it, or the council will notice. What would it take to **get it fixed**?'},{kind:'story',text:'In 1831 Tocqueville, a French aristocrat, toured the United States. He kept noticing the same thing. Americans formed associations to build churches, found hospitals, distribute books. In France an official would do it. In England, a **great nobleman**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Why did he care? One person alone is **powerless** against a big central authority. A group of neighbours is the one body in between that can push back, without **becoming a state** itself.'},{kind:'explain',text:'And it\u2019s **a skill**. Neighbours who once fixed a road together know how to call a meeting and share the work. When something bigger comes, they\u2019re ready. People who never practise stay **passive**, whatever their rights.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t wait for someone else to fix your pothole. Find **two neighbours** and sort it **together**. The joining habit only lasts if you **use it**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A town needs a new hospital. In one country, a **government official** arrives with a plan. In another, a great lord pays for it. In a third, **the neighbours** meet and build it. Which town is better off afterwards?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Tocqueville\u2019s answer is **structural**. In a democracy everyone is independent, and each person is weak alone. Unless people learn to **combine freely**, they\u2019re helpless. Then the state steps in to do what they can\u2019t.'},{kind:'story',text:'Touring America in 1831, he saw associations everywhere. Citizens joined to build churches, send missionaries and hand out books. Where France sent an official, Americans simply formed a society. He came to see this as the country\u2019s **distinctive political skill**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture neighbours who once organised to repair a road. They learned to call a meeting, split the jobs and **chase each other** up. Years later a bad local law is proposed. They already know how to oppose it.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The skill that founds a hospital can also **organise a mob** or keep outsiders out. And groups tend to serve people who already have time and confidence. That\u2019s one reason states do things too.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Rights on paper don\u2019t make you **an active citizen**. Practice does. Each time you join with others to get something done, you keep **the habit** alive for the next fight.'}]},'democracy-in-america_3':{title:'When Everyone Agrees',card:{insight:'When most people agree, they can keep you quiet without any law.',tryThis:'After your next group vote, write down one worry no one said out loud.',skills:['communication','psychology']},review:{prompt:'A popular plan has a problem no one mentions. What is going on?',right:'The group makes it hard to say your worry out loud',wrong:'Everyone checked the plan, so the problem must be small',why:'A room that agrees can keep you quiet with no law at all.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your class votes on a trip. Hands shoot up for **the zoo**. You really want the museum. Nobody would tell you off for saying so. So why does your hand **stay down**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Tocqueville, a Frenchman who visited America long ago, worried about this. Most people agreeing isn\u2019t the danger. The danger is that they make it **feel too hard** to disagree. Nobody gets punished. Nobody **says the thing**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'If nobody says the other view, **nobody hears it**. Maybe it was the best idea in the room. When a group agrees very fast, ask what **wasn\u2019t said**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'The proposal is popular, and everyone senior has backed it. You can see **a flaw**. Nothing would happen to you if you raised it. So why do you leave the meeting **having said nothing**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Tocqueville\u2019s **tyranny of the majority** isn\u2019t about bad laws. It\u2019s about opinion. A democratic majority can make dissent **socially impossible** without punishing anyone. No fine, no prison, just a room that goes quiet.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'He drew a deliberately shocking comparison between **a king** and **the majority**.',left:{title:'An absolute king',items:['Strikes at the body','Bans the book','The mind escapes']},right:{title:'Majority opinion',items:['Leaves the body alone','Leaves the book unread','Goes straight for the mind']}},{kind:'explain',text:'In America, he argued, the majority puts **a fence around opinion**. Inside it you\u2019re free. Step outside and you aren\u2019t jailed, just unread, unelected and avoided. **No law** is needed.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Silence in a meeting isn\u2019t always agreement. Sometimes it\u2019s **majority opinion** at work. Ask what view **nobody voiced**, and why it felt unsayable.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Imagine you\u2019re a writer in 1830s America with an unpopular view. Nobody arrests you. Nobody burns **your book**. It simply goes unread, and you\u2019re never elected to anything. Are you **free to speak**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Tocqueville argued that a democratic majority\u2019s power is less over law than over **opinion**. It needn\u2019t punish dissent. It makes dissent **socially pointless**, which reaches further than censorship ever did.'},{kind:'story',text:'In his first volume, he argued that American majority opinion had **no rival**. The legislature, the executive and even the jury all answered to it. So a dissenter had nowhere to appeal. He claimed to know no country with less real independence of mind.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a modern team meeting. The founder loves a plan. Three people privately think it\u2019s doomed. Each assumes they\u2019re alone, so the meeting ends in **warm agreement**. Months later, all three say they knew.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Being disagreed with isn\u2019t being silenced. Anyone can borrow this idea to dress up a losing view as persecution. And he overstated it: Americans of the 1830s **argued fiercely**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The test isn\u2019t whether a view **wins**. It\u2019s whether it can be **voiced at all**. Tocqueville trusted courts, juries and local government to slow the majority, not its own restraint.'}]},'democracy-in-america_4':{title:'Restless in Plenty',card:{insight:'When anyone can climb higher, there is always one more step to reach.',tryThis:'Write down one thing you have now that you dreamed of five years ago.',skills:['psychology','career']},review:{prompt:'You have done better than your parents, yet you still feel behind. Why?',right:'There is always one more step above you in view',wrong:'You have not worked hard enough yet',why:'You can feel behind even when you do well, since there is a next step.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You saved for months and finally got **the bike** you wanted. You loved it. Two weeks later, a friend got **a faster one**. Why does yours suddenly feel a bit old?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Long ago, a Frenchman called Tocqueville visited America. People there were **better off** than most people anywhere. Yet they seemed **worried and rushed**. Anyone could climb to the top, so there was always a next step. Not reaching it felt like their fault.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A ladder with **no top rung** never lets you finish climbing. That restless feeling isn\u2019t a fault in you. It\u2019s **the ladder**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'By any outside measure, you\u2019ve done well: a better job than your parents had, a home, some savings. So why do you feel **behind**? And why does **the next rung** always seem so close?'},{kind:'story',text:'Tocqueville describes the free, well-off citizens of what he thought the luckiest country on earth. He finds them **anxious**. They\u2019re serious even at play, and they hurry as if afraid of missing **the shortest road** to happiness.'},{kind:'explain',text:'His explanation: a society that removes **the ceilings** also removes **the excuses**. When any place is open to you, failing to reach it is personal. And there\u2019s always another rung above the one you reached.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'You aren\u2019t comparing yourself with **where you started**. You\u2019re comparing yourself with **the next rung**.',left:{title:'Where you started',items:['Far below you now','Easy to forget','Proof of real progress']},right:{title:'The next rung',items:['Always just in view','Impossible to ignore','Moves when you arrive']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Tocqueville offers **no cure**, only a diagnosis. The restlessness belongs to the situation, not **a flaw in you**. So decide how much of the race you actually want to run.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A man builds a house for his old age, then sells it **before the roof** is on. He plants a garden and leases it before the trees fruit. What\u2019s **he running** towards?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Tocqueville, writing his second volume, saw **two forces**. First, comparison: equals measure themselves against each other, not against where they began. Second, time: someone chasing comfort with **one short life** is always in a hurry.'},{kind:'story',text:'The man with the house is his portrait of the American of the 1830s. These were free, comfortable people, more fortunate than any before. Yet he found them **serious even at play**, haunted by missing the shortest road to happiness.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a nurse who became a ward manager at forty. Ten years ago she\u2019d have been thrilled. Today she\u2019s eyeing the director\u2019s job. Her old goal is behind her. The new one is **always in view**, and she can\u2019t rest.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. A diagnosis can become **an excuse**: if the situation\u2019s to blame, nothing needs to change. And he\u2019s describing comfortable citizens. For the enslaved and the poor, anxiety was about survival, not the next rung.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your restlessness may be **the ladder\u2019s** doing, not yours. Seeing that changes the question. Stop asking why you\u2019re never satisfied, and ask **how far** you actually want to climb.'}]},'democracy-in-america_5':{title:'Help That Pays You Back',card:{insight:'Help that pays you back lasts longer than help from one hero.',tryThis:'Pick one shared chore, and make a chart so each person takes a turn.',skills:['philosophy','leadership']},review:{prompt:'Your office kitchen stays clean only when one kind person cleans it. What fixes it?',right:'A chart where each person takes a turn, so everyone gains',wrong:'Thank the kind person more so she keeps going',why:'Plans where each person gains last longer than plans that need one hero.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Sam helps a new kid find their classroom. Asked why, Sam shrugs: *one day I\u2019ll need help too*. Is that a **worse reason** than pure kindness? Or a **better one**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Long ago, a Frenchman called Tocqueville heard reasons like Sam\u2019s **all over America**. People said helping others helps you too. He thought it wasn\u2019t grand. But it got people helping **again and again**, not just once.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Being a hero is hard, so **few people** manage it. Helping because it helps you too is something **almost everyone** can keep up. That\u2019s why it lasts.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your neighbour asks you to take in **a parcel**. You do it without thinking. If someone asked why, you\u2019d probably say it\u2019s handy to stay on good terms. One day you\u2019ll need **the favour**. Is that selfish?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Tocqueville called this **self-interest properly understood**: serving others serves you too, so decency is useful rather than heroic. He found Americans holding it almost everywhere. He thought it **less noble** than virtue, and far more reliable.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'His case is a **comparison** between what each kind of morality actually gets **from people**.',left:{title:'Demand sacrifice',items:['Asks for great acts','A few rise to it','Most fall short']},right:{title:'Interest understood',items:['Asks for small habits','Almost everyone manages','It holds for years']}},{kind:'story',text:'Think of a shared kitchen at work. One saint tidies it daily until she leaves, and then **it collapses**. A rota, where a clean kitchen helps everyone including you, never needs a saint. It just keeps going.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t build anything that needs **a hero**. Heroes tire and move away. Build it so ordinary people, **you included**, gain from doing their part.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'In aristocratic ages, moralists praised **the beauty of virtue** to a class raised for it. Then a society of equals arrives, with no such class. What moral argument could possibly reach **everyone** in it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Tocqueville\u2019s answer was **enlightened self-interest**. Honesty, thrift and helpfulness pay you back over time, and so they **become habits**. It produces few heroes. It does produce a great many reliable small acts, every day.'},{kind:'story',text:'He\u2019s candid that he isn\u2019t admiring it. He even notes that Americans did themselves an injustice. They explained by calculation acts that came from real generosity. His case is simply that this doctrine is what a democracy can secure.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a shared allotment. It can rely on one saint who waters every plot. Or it can give each holder **a watering day** and a share of the harvest. The second never makes a hero. It never collapses either.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The doctrine falls silent when **duty and calculation** split: when doing right costs you and never repays. Think of reporting fraud at your own firm. Self-interest won\u2019t get you there.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Ordinary decency, built on **what pays**, outlasts heroism in everyday life. But a society that holds **nothing higher** won\u2019t know what to ask of you when doing right costs everything.'}]},'democracy-in-america_6':{title:'Schools for Liberty',card:{insight:'People learn to run big things by first deciding small, real ones.',tryThis:'Pick one small choice you usually make for others. Let them make it this week.',skills:['leadership','history']},review:{prompt:'You want your team to take more charge. Where do you start?',right:'Give them one small, real choice to make first',wrong:'Tell them they are now in charge of everything',why:'People learn to lead on small, real choices, where a slip costs little.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Nobody learns to swim in **the deep sea**. You start in the shallow end, where mistakes don\u2019t matter much. So where would you learn to **run a town**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Long ago, a Frenchman called Tocqueville visited **New England**, in the north-east of America. He saw towns run by the people who lived there. They met in a hall and decided things together: a road, a school, the town\u2019s money.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Small things are real, but **safe to get wrong**. Deciding them is how people **learn to govern**. If someone far away decides everything, you never learn how.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your residents\u2019 group gets to decide how to spend **\xa32,000** on the street. You argue, vote, and live with the result. Compared with a general election, it\u2019s tiny. Why might it **teach you more**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Before Congress or the presidency, Tocqueville\u2019s first volume describes the **New England township**. Neighbours met to decide a road, a school, a local rate. He thought the whole American system could only be understood **from there**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'His comparison: town meetings are to liberty what **primary schools** are to science. They put it within reach. Self-government is a skill, and skills need practice on something **small enough** to fail safely.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Liberty in a **distant capital** feels very different from liberty on **your own street**.',left:{title:'National liberty',items:['A distant idea','Seldom used','Easy to leave']},right:{title:'Township liberty',items:['A road, a school','Used every year','Neighbours expect you there']}},{kind:'moral',text:'A vote gives you **the right**. Small, real decisions give you **the habit**. If you want people to take charge at scale, let them decide something small first.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A capital city runs every road, school and rate across the country, and **runs them well**. Local people never have to decide anything. What could possibly be **wrong with that**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Tocqueville separates **centralised government**, which a nation needs, from **centralised administration**, which runs local details from the capital. The second may be efficient. But it leaves rights on paper that nobody has the skill or appetite to use.'},{kind:'story',text:'His evidence was the New England township. Neighbours decided their own roads, schools and constables in open meeting. Without such local bodies, he argued, a nation might have **a free government** and still lack **the spirit of liberty**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a manager who approves every expense herself. Her team is fast and tidy. Then she goes on leave for a month, and nobody knows **how to decide** anything. She\u2019s built subjects, not colleagues.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Local self-rule is also **local power**. New England towns could be narrow and closed to outsiders, and women had no voice in the meeting. Local majorities have often denied rights that only national action secured.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Rights you never practise **wither on paper**. If you want citizens rather than subjects, give people **small, real things** to decide. Just remember that practice in self-government isn\u2019t the same as justice.'}]},'democracy-in-america_7':{title:'The Quiet Retreat',card:{insight:'Staying in your own small circle feels fine, but it leaves shared things to others.',tryThis:'Name one shared problem near you, and one thing you could do about it.',skills:['relationships','history']},review:{prompt:'Your town library may close. You just buy more books for home. What is the risk?',right:'If all of us do this, shared things get left to others',wrong:'Buying books for yourself is selfish and wrong',why:'A calm step back is fine, but if all do it, shared places fall apart.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your street wants to plan **a summer party**. Nobody comes to the meeting. Everyone\u2019s at home, warm and happy with their families. Nobody\u2019s being mean. So why does the party **never happen**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Long ago, a Frenchman called Tocqueville named this. It isn\u2019t **selfishness**, which means caring only about yourself. It\u2019s quietly **pulling back** into your own small circle of family and friends. It looks sensible. But then nobody looks after the shared world.'},{kind:'moral',text:'If everyone stays home, **someone else** ends up running things for them. And people forget how to **work together**. Next time, step one small way outside your circle.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'The local playground is falling apart. You don\u2019t complain. You just buy **a swing** for your own garden. It\u2019s a kind, sensible thing to do. So why might Tocqueville have **worried about it**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'In his second volume, Tocqueville defined **individualism**, then a new word. It isn\u2019t selfishness. It\u2019s a calm feeling that draws each person into **family and friends**. The wider society is left to itself.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'He was careful to separate the two, because one is **easy to spot** and one is **easy to miss**.',left:{title:'Selfishness',items:['As old as humanity','Loves self above all','Plainly a vice']},right:{title:'Individualism',items:['Born of democratic ages','Loves a small circle','Looks perfectly reasonable']}},{kind:'explain',text:'His fear was **the sum** of many sensible retreats. Each family makes its own garden nicer. The shared playground rots. And a people who\u2019ve withdrawn will accept a power that **manages everything** for them.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Making your own circle comfortable isn\u2019t wrong. But when it\u2019s **your only answer** to a public problem, notice it. That\u2019s **the quiet retreat** Tocqueville described, and it adds up.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Imagine a country where **nobody owes anybody** anything. No lord, no servant, no inherited duty. You\u2019re free to live as you like, among the people you love. Why would a careful observer find that **frightening**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Tocqueville\u2019s answer: aristocracy bound everyone into **a chain**, from peasant to king, each owing something to the next. Democracy breaks the chain. That\u2019s a real gain. But acting together stops being automatic, and now **has to be chosen**.'},{kind:'story',text:'In his second volume, he stops to define individualism, a word he calls recent. Earlier ages had a word only for selfishness. This was something new: a mild, calm feeling, which democracy produces **as naturally** as aristocracy produced obligation.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a new estate where every family has a good fence and a good television. The residents\u2019 association folds for lack of members. When the council plans a road through the green, nobody knows **how to object**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. For many people, caring for a family takes **all the time** they have. A theory that reads that as civic decline can turn into scolding. And the old chains of obligation were often chains of domination.'},{kind:'moral',text:'His remedy isn\u2019t a sermon. It\u2019s **local government** and voluntary groups that give you things only **done together**. They pull you out of your circle without anyone telling you off.'}]},'democracy-in-america_8':{title:'The Lawyers\u2019 Brake',card:{insight:'Any group that can act fast needs a slow step, so anger can cool.',tryThis:'Name one slow step at work, and write what it protects you from.',skills:['leadership','history']},review:{prompt:'Your club wants to change its rules tonight in anger. One member asks to wait. Why?',right:'A slow step gives an angry mood time to cool',wrong:'She is just being difficult and blocking the vote',why:'A group that can act fast needs a slow step to let anger cool.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Brakes don\u2019t make **your bike** go any faster. They only slow it down. So why would you never ride down **a really steep hill** without them?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Long ago, a Frenchman called Tocqueville visited America. Most people there could change things **very fast**. He looked for something slow to check them, and found it in **lawyers**. They\u2019re trained to ask what the rules say and what happened last time.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Lawyers aren\u2019t always right, and sometimes they slow a **good change** too. But anything that moves fast needs **a brake**. Brakes are what make going fast safe.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your residents\u2019 association is furious about a decision. It wants to **vote tonight** to change its rules. One member asks to check the constitution and wait a week. Everyone groans. Is she **the most useful** person there?'},{kind:'story',text:'Tocqueville went looking for an aristocracy in a country that had **abolished one**. He found the nearest thing in the legal profession. Lawyers were everywhere in American life: in the courts, in the legislatures, and in **the jury room**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'His reasoning is about **training**, not virtue. Lawyers pick up a taste for order and form, and an instinct for **precedent**. That makes them prefer what\u2019s been established, whatever their politics. They\u2019re slow, and slow can check a fast mood.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'He set the **impatient majority** against **the legal class**, and thought a democracy needed both.',left:{title:'The majority',items:['Impatient','Changeable','Moved by today\u2019s mood']},right:{title:'The legal class',items:['Slow','Procedural','Moved by past decisions']}},{kind:'moral',text:'In any system that can move fast, find **the slow part**. Before you remove it, ask what it\u2019s **protecting you** from. It might be your own anger.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Tocqueville has just described a majority with **almost no rival** in America. Kings and lords are gone. So what, if anything, could slow it down when it\u2019s **in a hurry**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'His answer, in the first volume, is **the legal profession**. Lawyers are trained to love form and precedent. They\u2019re present throughout public life. And they\u2019re **instinctively slow**, which is exactly the counterweight a majority needs.'},{kind:'story',text:'In America, he noticed, almost every political question **sooner or later** becomes a judicial one. So lawyers\u2019 habits spread widely. They spread through judges who could set laws aside, through lawyers in the legislatures, and through **the jury**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a start-up that lets anyone ship code straight to customers. It\u2019s thrilling, until a bad change breaks everything at midnight. The team adds **one review step**. Nobody loves it. You\u2019d still never remove it.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The brake isn\u2019t neutral. Lawyers, we\u2019d add, profit from **the complexity** they maintain. And the instinct that blocks a dangerous change blocks a necessary one too. Precedent has preserved injustice as faithfully as liberty.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Tocqueville wasn\u2019t praising lawyers. He was pointing at **the only brake** left. So when you\u2019re tempted to sweep away a slow step, first ask what **it\u2019s stopping**.'}]},'democracy-in-america_9':{title:'Outlasting the Law',card:{insight:'Ending an unfair law does not end the harm it built.',tryThis:'Pick one unfair rule that has ended. Write down one harm from it that still lasts.',skills:['history','thinking']},review:{prompt:'A harmful rule ended years ago. Does all its harm end too?',right:'No, the harm it built can last for years',wrong:'Yes, once the law changes, the harm stops',why:'A law can end a wrong, but the hurt it built can last for years.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Imagine a school rule that was unfair to **one group** of pupils for years. Then it\u2019s scrapped. Would you expect everyone to treat that group fairly **the next morning**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'When Tocqueville visited America in the 1830s, millions of Black people were **enslaved** in the South. In most northern states, slavery had ended. But Black people there were **still shut out** and looked down on. A new law hadn\u2019t changed minds.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When you end a wrong by law, **unfair habits** can still outlast it. Tocqueville saw that clearly. But seeing a problem isn\u2019t the same as **seeing the fix**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'On his journey, Tocqueville crossed between **slave states** and **free states**. He also watched Choctaw families, forced off their land, crossing the Mississippi in winter. What would you expect a visitor to conclude from that?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Writing in 1835, he named slavery as **the gravest danger** to the Union. And he argued the trouble wouldn\u2019t end with emancipation. The prejudice slavery bred ran **deeper than law** could reach.'},{kind:'explain',text:'His evidence came from the North. Racial prejudice seemed **stronger** in states that had ended slavery than in those that kept it. Free Black people there had rights on paper and were **shut out** in practice.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'He was right about **the danger** and poor on **the remedy**.',left:{title:'His analysis',items:['Named the central fault','Saw prejudice outlasting law','Used the North as evidence']},right:{title:'His prescription',items:['Saw no path to equality','Speculated about separation','Shared his era\u2019s assumptions']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Removing an injustice from the law is **necessary**, but it isn\u2019t enough. The habits it built keep running until they\u2019re **tackled directly**. So when you see a law change, ask what\u2019s still running.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Democracy in America mostly describes equality among **white citizens**. Its last long chapter turns to everyone else. What happens to a book about equality when its author finally looks at **who was left out**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'In 1835 Tocqueville named slavery **the gravest threat** to the Union. He predicted its consequences would long outlast it, because law could end **the institution** but not the attitude. You can read it as a forecast.'},{kind:'story',text:'He saw Indigenous nations being driven west, some of it with his own eyes. He compared slave states with free ones. In the free North, prejudice seemed **stronger**, not weaker. Rights on paper hadn\u2019t reached daily life.'},{kind:'story',text:'Then history ran on. The Civil War came about a quarter of a century later. Slavery was abolished in 1865. Legal segregation and the loss of the vote followed, lasting in much of the South **into the 1960s**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. His framing often treats Black Americans\u2019 presence, not slavery and white prejudice, as **the problem**. Others in his time, Black abolitionists above all, did imagine an equal society. And he backed France\u2019s brutal conquest of Algeria.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You can name a society\u2019s central fault **with precision** and still fail to imagine the cure. Tocqueville did both. His lasting lesson: ending an injustice in law **doesn\u2019t end** what it built.'}]}}}},957,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'enchiridion',kind:'book',promise:'Worry only about what is up to you',about:'Do you lose sleep over things you cannot change? A teacher named Epictetus had a plan for that. He was born a slave and later taught in Greece. His student wrote his lessons in a small book. Which worries are yours to fix?',ideas:{enchiridion_1:{title:'The Two Piles',simple:{headline:'Work only on the worries you can fix',body:'Epictetus was born a slave and became a teacher. His handbook opens with a sorting job. Some things are up to you, like your effort and what you say. Others aren\u2019t, like the weather and what people think of you. You have a big presentation tomorrow and lie awake listing worries. **Put each worry in one of two piles, and work only on the pile that\u2019s yours.** You can\u2019t make them like it. You can prepare well tonight and get some sleep.',doToday:'Split today\u2019s biggest worry on paper into what you control and what you don\u2019t, then act on one part you control.'},card:{insight:'Most of your worries are about things you cannot change.',tryThis:'When you worry, split it into what is up to you and what is not. Then do your part.',skills:['philosophy','psychology']},review:{prompt:'It snows the morning of your job interview. What do you do first?',right:'Sort out your part, like leaving early',wrong:'Watch the news and hope the snow stops',why:'The snow is not yours to fix, but leaving early is.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'It\u2019s the big match and it starts to **pour with rain**. Your team is smaller than the other side. You feel sick with nerves. Which of those worries can you **actually do anything** about?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Try putting each worry in **one of two piles**. The rain, the ref and the other team go in *not up to me*. How hard you try and what you say go in **up to me**. Only one pile needs you.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Most worry comes from **the wrong pile**. Sort first, and you\u2019ll know where to **spend your effort**. The other pile can look after itself.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A big presentation is tomorrow. You lie awake listing worries. *What if they hate it? What if the boss is in a mood?* Which of those is **actually yours** to **work on**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Epictetus was born a slave and became a teacher. His handbook, written down by his student Arrian, opens with this sorting. Up to us: our judgements, aims and wants. Not up to us: body, reputation, other people.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Sort tomorrow\u2019s worries into **two columns**, and see which one **you can work on**.',left:{title:'Yours',items:['How well you prepare','What you choose to say','How you take questions']},right:{title:'Not yours',items:['Whether they like it','The boss\u2019s mood','Who turns up']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Almost every worry that kept you awake sits in **the second column**. Almost everything you can do sits in the first. So **sort first**, and don\u2019t wait for the feeling.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'The first thing Epictetus teaches isn\u2019t a comfort or a rule. It\u2019s **a sorting job**. Before anything else, he wants every worry you carry put in **one of two bins**. Why start there?'},{kind:'explain',text:'One bin holds what\u2019s **up to you**: your judgements, your aims, what you go after or avoid. The other holds **everything else**, including your body, your name and every other person. Most distress means you\u2019ve filed something in the wrong bin.'},{kind:'story',text:'Priya\u2019s train is cancelled on the way to an interview. She spends twenty minutes raging at the board. Then she sorts it. The train is not hers. The phone call to say she\u2019ll be late is. She makes it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a parent waits for exam results. The marks are already written and **not theirs**. What they say at the kitchen table when the envelope opens is entirely theirs. That\u2019s where to put the preparation.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The line isn\u2019t always clean. Your health is partly yours: sleep, food, effort. Treat it as wholly outside you and you\u2019ll neglect it. The sort works best on **clear cases**, not murky ones.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Sort **before you feel**, not after. Once distress arrives you\u2019re arguing with it. Done in advance, it\u2019s just **a filing decision**, and a cheap one.'}]},enchiridion_2:{title:'The Sentence You Attach',simple:{headline:'What you tell yourself hurts more than the news',body:'Two people lose their jobs from the same team on the same morning. One has interviews booked within a week. The other can\u2019t look at job ads for a month. One calls it a setback. The other calls it proof that they\u2019ve failed. Epictetus taught that the upset comes from your opinion of an event. **The difference is the sentence each one attaches to it.** The event is fixed. Your sentence about it isn\u2019t, and that sentence is what keeps hurting.',doToday:'Write down the sentence you\u2019ve attached to something that stung this week, then rewrite it as a calm friend would.'},card:{insight:'Bad news hurts most because of what you tell yourself about it.',tryThis:'When something bugs you, write down the thought you added. Then ask if a calm friend would think it.',skills:['psychology','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'You and a coworker both lose your jobs. What decides how bad it feels?',right:'The thought each of you adds to the news',wrong:'How unfair the boss was to let you go',why:'The news was the same, but the thoughts you added were not.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two friends both get **left out** of the same party. One shrugs and plans a film night instead. The other cries all weekend. It was the same party and the same list. So **what made the difference**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'It wasn\u2019t the party. It was **the sentence** each one told herself. One thought it was a mix-up. The other thought *nobody likes me.* You do this too, and the sentence **does the hurting**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Things happen, and then **you add a sentence**. The sentence is what stings. Change the sentence and you\u2019ll often find **the sting** goes too.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two people are made **redundant** on the same morning, from the same team. One has interviews lined up within a week. The other can\u2019t look at **job listings** for a month. What\u2019s different?'},{kind:'story',text:'Same job, same date, same terms. One tells the story as an inconvenience. The other tells it as a humiliation. Epictetus would say the event was identical. The sentence each attached to it was doing **all the work**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'His claim is blunt. What upsets you isn\u2019t **the thing** itself. It\u2019s **your opinion** of the thing. So when you\u2019re troubled, the useful place to look is your own judgement. It\u2019s the one part of the event you can rewrite.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The event is **the same for everyone** it touches. The sentence you attach is yours. Rewrite **the sentence**, and the part that keeps hurting changes.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Epictetus picks a heavy example: **death**. If death were terrible in itself, he says, Socrates would have found it terrible. He didn\u2019t. So where does the terror **actually live**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'His answer is that it lives in **the opinion**. The belief that death is terrible is the terrible part. That sounds like a trick, until you test it on **smaller things** you\u2019ve felt this week.'},{kind:'story',text:'Two people lose their jobs on the same morning. One calls it an inconvenience and books interviews. The other calls it a humiliation and hides for a month. Nothing about the redundancy differed. Only **the sentence** did.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now Tom\u2019s brother forgets his birthday. Tom\u2019s sentence is *he doesn\u2019t care about me.* A calmer reader of the same facts might guess his brother\u2019s had a rough month. Same silence, two very different evenings.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some events hurt before any sentence arrives: a broken arm, a real loss. The idea doesn\u2019t deny that. It says the pain that **keeps going** is usually the interpretation, and that part is yours.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When something troubles you, look first at **the opinion** you\u2019re holding. That\u2019s where the trouble lives. It\u2019s also the only part **you can rewrite**.'}]},enchiridion_3:{title:'Wish for What Happens',simple:{headline:'When a plan goes wrong, pick the next good step',body:'You spend two months planning an outdoor party, and on the day it rains. By noon you\u2019re still listing what you should have done differently. Epictetus gave odd advice for moments like this. Stop wishing things would go your way, and want them to go the way they go. He didn\u2019t mean giving up. **Put your effort in before the event, and your acceptance after it.** The rain has already happened. Moving everyone indoors and starting the music is still up to you. Save your fight for things you can still change.',doToday:'Pick something that went wrong this week and do the most useful next step today.'},card:{insight:'If you fight what is already done, you only feel worse.',tryThis:'Next time a plan falls apart, say out loud what happened. Then take one good next step.',skills:['psychology','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'It rains on the outdoor party you planned for two months. What now?',right:'Move the party inside and take the next step',wrong:'Go over what you should have done all day',why:'The rain is done, but your next step is still yours.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve planned a beach day for weeks. You wake up and it\u2019s **pouring**. You could spend the morning grumbling at the window. Or you could do something else. What\u2019s **the better move**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Grumbling at the rain won\u2019t move a single cloud. The gap between **what you wanted** and **what happened** is where the bad mood lives. Only one side of that gap can still change: what you do next.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Say what\u2019s **really happening**, out loud. Then pick **the next good step** from there. A rainy day can still be a good one.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two months organising an **outdoor party**, and the morning it arrives, it rains. By noon you\u2019re still listing everything you should have done differently. Is that list helping **anyone at all**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Epictetus gives an odd instruction. Don\u2019t wish for things to happen as you want. Wish for them to happen **as they do**. It sounds like giving up. He meant something closer to the opposite.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Put **the effort** before the event and **the acceptance** after it.',left:{title:'Before the event',items:['Plans and effort','A marquee in case of rain']},right:{title:'After the event',items:['Accept the day that arrived','The first useful step']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Distress is the gap between **what happened** and what you demanded. Only one side can still move. So accept the day that arrived, and take **the next useful step**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Epictetus asks for something that sounds like surrender. Don\u2019t want events to go **your way**. Want them to go **the way they go**. Can that really be advice for someone who wants to get things done?'},{kind:'explain',text:'It isn\u2019t surrender. It **moves the wish**. Effort goes into what you control, before the event. The wish goes onto the outcome, **after it arrives**. Distress is the gap between what happened and what you demanded.'},{kind:'story',text:'Two couples marry outdoors on the same rainy Saturday. One spends the day replaying the forecast. The other moves everyone under the trees and starts the music. Same weather. Only one **argued with it**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a manager hears the budget is cut by a third. She could spend the week arguing it shouldn\u2019t have happened. She writes the new number at the top of the plan and starts from there.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some things that happen should be fought: an injustice, a mistake you can still reverse. Accepting those too early is just giving in. The wish belongs on **what\u2019s already done**, not on what can still change.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Put your effort in **before** the event. Put your acceptance on it **after**. Then the only question left is the next useful step from where you actually are.'}]},enchiridion_4:{title:'Rehearse the Loss',simple:{headline:'Remember that the things you love can end',body:'Epictetus suggested a habit that sounds bleak at first. If you\u2019re fond of a mug, remind yourself that it\u2019s a mug, and mugs break. Then if it smashes, you\u2019re sad but not thrown. The same works for bigger things. Suppose one client brings in most of your income. **Say once, in plain words, that this client could leave.** Then carry on. You\u2019ll hold the work without clutching it, and you might answer two old enquiries that week. You may also value the work more, because you know it could end.',doToday:'Write one plain sentence naming something you rely on that could end, then carry on with your day.'},card:{insight:'Say once that a thing can end, and losing it will not shock you.',tryThis:'Pick one thing you love, like your home. Tell yourself once that it can end.',skills:['philosophy','psychology']},review:{prompt:'You love your job, and it pays all your bills. What should you do?',right:'Tell yourself once that it can end, then enjoy it',wrong:'Do not think about it, so you can stay happy',why:'Saying it once means losing it will not shock you.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your favourite mug has been on the shelf for **years**. One morning it slips and smashes. You\u2019re upset, but also surprised. Why does it feel like it was **never meant** to break?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Epictetus had **a simple trick**. When you like a jug, say to yourself, *this is a jug, and jugs break.* Then when it does break, you\u2019re not shocked. You knew **what it was** all along.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Good things **don\u2019t last forever**, and that\u2019s okay. Saying so once isn\u2019t gloomy. It helps you **enjoy them more** while they\u2019re here.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'One client brings in **most of your income**. Things are going well. You\u2019ve never once said that sentence out loud, even to yourself. What would change if you **said it plainly**, once?'},{kind:'story',text:'Epictetus offers a hard version. When you kiss your child, he says, remind yourself you\u2019re kissing **a mortal**. It sounds cruel. His point is accuracy: you\u2019re saying what kind of thing you have.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Things held as **permanent** get noticed dully, because nothing asks you to pay attention. Things held as **borrowed** get noticed. Saying the client could go doesn\u2019t make it happen. It makes you see the work clearly.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Say once, **plainly**, that the thing you rely on can end. Then carry on. You\u2019ll hold it **without clutching**, and notice it more.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re **kissing your child**, and a philosopher wants you to think: *this is someone who will die.* It sounds like terrible advice. Why would Epictetus want you to **spoil a happy moment**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'He isn\u2019t trying to spoil it. He wants the moment to stop resting on **a false premise**, that nothing ends. That premise gets corrected eventually. **Better to have known**, and to have paid attention while it lasted.'},{kind:'story',text:'Start smaller, as he does, with **a jug**. Say you\u2019re fond of a jug, and when it breaks you won\u2019t be thrown. You told yourself in advance what kind of thing it was. A cup is the easy place to practise.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a freelancer with **one big client**. She writes one sentence: *this client could leave next month.* She doesn\u2019t dwell on it. But that week she answers two old enquiries she\u2019d been ignoring.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Done as a mood instead of a practice, this turns into **rehearsing dread**. That\u2019s the opposite of the point. It\u2019s one clear statement of fact, said once, not an afternoon imagining the funeral.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Name what you have **accurately**, including that it ends. Say it once and move on. Held as **borrowed**, the things you love get noticed instead of taken for granted.'}]},enchiridion_5:{title:'Wool, Not Grass',simple:{headline:'Do the new habit, and do not talk about it',body:'Epictetus said sheep don\u2019t bring the shepherd grass to show how much they\u2019ve eaten. They turn it into wool and milk. Say you start running before work. The urge is to explain your whole plan to friends at dinner. **Talking about a habit feels like progress, so it can replace the doing.** Keep it to yourself for a while and let the results show. A friend who runs with you or checks in is different. You\u2019re asking for help there, not applause.',doToday:'Do ten minutes of a habit you\u2019ve been meaning to start, and tell nobody about it today.'},card:{insight:'When you talk about a new habit, it feels like you did it.',tryThis:'Next time you start a habit, tell no one. Do ten minutes of it each day instead.',skills:['habits','psychology']},review:{prompt:'You just started running every morning. What do you tell friends?',right:'Tell them very little and let the results show',wrong:'Tell them your whole plan so they cheer you on',why:'If friends cheer too soon, you get the good feeling without the work.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve decided to **practise piano** every day. On day one you tell the whole class all about it. Everyone says it\u2019s great. Strange thing, though: why do you suddenly feel **less like practising**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Telling people gave you **the good feeling** early, before any practice happened. So part of you feels the job is done. Epictetus said sheep don\u2019t show the shepherd how much grass they ate. They just **make wool**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t talk much about **the new habit** you\u2019re starting. Just **do it quietly** for a while. Let people notice the results, the way they\u2019d notice the wool.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve started something you\u2019re **quietly proud of**. A running plan, a savings rule, a new way of working. And you\u2019re already explaining **the system** to friends over dinner. What could be wrong with that?'},{kind:'story',text:'Epictetus was rude about this. At a dinner, he said, don\u2019t lecture on how people ought to eat. Just **eat as you ought**. Sheep don\u2019t bring the shepherd grass to prove they\u2019ve eaten. They make wool and milk.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Announcing a habit is **a substitute** that feels like progress. You get the credit for holding the principle now, without the work. Once you\u2019ve been credited, **the pressure drops**, and so does the effort.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Keep the habit **quiet** for a while. Let the **results** do the talking. Wool is proof that the grass was eaten, and nobody needs to see the grass.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A man at a dinner party explains, at length, how one should eat **in moderation**. Then he takes a third helping. Everyone notices. Why is talking about **a principle** so much easier than living it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'There are **two traps** here. First, announcing feels like progress, so it replaces progress. You get the credit early and the drive fades. Second, once you\u2019ve named your philosophy in public, failing at it is embarrassing, so you **defend the claim** instead.'},{kind:'story',text:'Sam tells everyone he\u2019s quitting sugar. For a week he gets praise at every lunch. By week two the praise has stopped, and so has he. The telling had already paid him, so the doing felt optional.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now Ana starts the same thing and says nothing. A month later a friend asks why she seems so well. She shrugs. That\u2019s **the wool**: the result shows up first, and nobody had to hear about the grass.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some habits need **other people**: a running partner, a coach, a friend who checks in. Telling them is support, not a show. The test is whether you\u2019re asking for help or for applause.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Talking about your principles is **intake**. Living them is **wool and milk**. Skip the announcement and you\u2019ll keep more of the drive.'}]},enchiridion_6:{title:'Play the Part Well',simple:{headline:'Play the part you got as well as you can',body:'Epictetus compared life to a play. Someone else picks your part, long or short, rich or poor. Your job is to play it well. You might be stuck in a job you didn\u2019t choose and can\u2019t leave yet. You could spend the year listing what\u2019s unfair about it. **The way you play the part is still yours, so work on that.** Learn the names of the people you deal with. Stay calm when the queue gets long. If the part is harming you, change it when you can.',doToday:'Pick one duty you didn\u2019t choose, and do it better today than you usually would.'},card:{insight:'You cannot pick your part, but how well you play it is up to you.',tryThis:'Pick one task you did not choose. Do it better than you did last time.',skills:['philosophy','career']},review:{prompt:'You are stuck in a job you did not pick. What do you do?',right:'Play the part well while you are there',wrong:'Do as little as you can until you leave',why:'Someone else picked the job, but how you do it is yours.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your class is doing **a school play**. You wanted to be the hero. Instead you\u2019re cast as the third tree on the left. You can sulk, or you can do **something else**. What?'},{kind:'explain',text:'You didn\u2019t pick the part. A teacher did. But **how you play it** is still all yours. A tree that stands still, on cue, can make a scene work. People notice **a part played well**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Lots of things in life get **picked for you**. What\u2019s left is how you play your part. So **play it well**, whatever it is.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You didn\u2019t choose this role. You can\u2019t leave it yet. The manager, the team and the budget were all **handed to you**. So is there anything here that\u2019s **still yours**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Epictetus uses the theatre. You\u2019re **an actor** in a play, and someone else chose the part. Short or long, rich or poor, your business is to act it well. Choosing it was never yours.'},{kind:'explain',text:'This splits **two judgements** people usually blur. How good was the part? How well was it played? Only the second is yours. It also **cuts both ways**: a great part played carelessly isn\u2019t saved by being great.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You didn\u2019t pick the market, the family or the health you started with. You do pick **how seriously** you play the part. That\u2019s **the only score** worth keeping.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture a stage. One actor has the lead. Another has **two lines** in act three. Which of them can give **the better performance** tonight, and what does that depend on?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Epictetus says you\u2019re that actor. Someone else wrote **the part**: its length, its wealth, its scenes. Your business is to **act it well**. Choosing the part belongs to another, and judging it is wasted effort.'},{kind:'story',text:'Meera works the night shift at a hospital reception. She didn\u2019t choose it. She learns every regular\u2019s name, keeps the queue calm and spots the frightened ones first. A small part, played with real care.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a son inherits his father\u2019s firm. Every advantage was handed to him. He coasts, misses meetings and blames the market. The **advantage** doesn\u2019t redeem him. A big part played carelessly is still played badly.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some parts can and should be changed: a job that harms you, an unfair rule. Read as **a reason to stay**, the idea turns into resignation. It covers what you can\u2019t change yet, not everything.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Keep two questions apart: **how good** is the part, and **how well** am I playing it? Only the second is yours, so spend your attention there.'}]},enchiridion_7:{title:'Two Handles',simple:{headline:'When your brother wrongs you, he is still your brother',body:'Epictetus said everything has two handles. You can carry it by one, but not by the other. His example is a brother who has wronged you. Grab it by the wrong, and you\u2019ll stew through every family dinner. Grab it by the fact that he\u2019s your brother and you grew up together, and you can talk to him. Both are true. **Of the true ways to see a problem, pick one you can act on.** If he owes you money, that might mean asking him for a repayment plan.',doToday:'Write a hard situation in one line, then rewrite it in a true way that points to a next step.'},card:{insight:'Most problems have more than one true side, and only one lets you act.',tryThis:'When someone bugs you, write down one other true thing about them. Then act on that.',skills:['thinking','relationships']},review:{prompt:'A coworker took credit for your idea. What do you do?',right:'Pick the true side that lets you talk it out',wrong:'Stay mad at the wrong until they say sorry',why:'The wrong is true, but you still work with them, so talk it out.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re trying to carry **a heavy bag** home. You grab it by the bottom, and it slips and spills. You grab it by **the handle**, and up it comes. Same bag. What changed?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Epictetus said problems have **two handles** too. If your sister breaks your toy, one handle is *she broke it.* That one just makes you angrier. Another is **she\u2019s my sister**, and that one helps you sort it out.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Both handles are **true**. Only one lets you **lift the problem**. So when something\u2019s hard, look for the handle that helps you do something.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your brother borrowed money a year ago and hasn\u2019t mentioned it since. Every family dinner, it\u2019s **all you can see**. You sit there going over it, getting angrier. Is there **another way** to pick this up?'},{kind:'story',text:'Epictetus says everything has **two handles**. One will carry it and one won\u2019t. If your brother wrongs you, the handle of his wrong won\u2019t lift. Try the handle that he\u2019s your brother, and you grew up together.'},{kind:'explain',text:'This isn\u2019t pretending. **The wrong is real**, and Epictetus never denies it. Among the true descriptions, some let you act and some only let you seethe. You get to **choose which one** you carry.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Before you react, check **which handle** you\u2019re holding. If it won\u2019t carry, gripping harder won\u2019t help. Pick up **the true handle** that lets you act.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Epictetus offers an odd test for any **hard situation**. Look for its handles. One of them will lift the thing. The other won\u2019t, no matter how hard **you pull**. How do you tell which is which?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The test is **practical**, not moral. Pick a description and ask: *can I do something from here?* If not, you\u2019re holding the handle that won\u2019t carry. **Gripping harder** doesn\u2019t help. Choosing another true one does.'},{kind:'story',text:'His own example is a brother who has wronged you. By the handle of the wrong, nothing lifts. By the handle that he\u2019s your brother and you were raised together, a conversation becomes possible.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a colleague takes credit for your idea in a meeting. One handle: *she took it.* Another true one: you work with her every day and need that to go well. The second lets you raise it calmly tomorrow.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Sometimes the only handle that lifts is the hard one. If someone keeps harming you, the useful description may be **the wrong itself**. Choosing a softer handle then is just spin.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Every situation has **more than one** true description. Some let you act, and some only let you seethe. Pick it up by **the handle that carries**.'}]},enchiridion_8:{title:'My Other Faults',simple:{headline:'When people gossip, joke about the faults they missed',body:'A friend tells you someone called you disorganised at lunch. Your first urge is to list every deadline you\u2019ve hit this year. Epictetus suggested a different reply. Say they must not know your other faults, or they\u2019d have mentioned those too. It\u2019s a joke with a method in it. **Once you admit you\u2019re flawed, the criticism has nothing left to attack.** The argument ends, and you keep the energy a defence would cost. If the criticism is fair, though, the next step is fixing it.',doToday:'Write two true faults of yours that your latest critic didn\u2019t mention, and send no defence today.'},card:{insight:'When you admit you have faults, a mean word has nothing left to hit.',tryThis:'Next time you hear someone spoke badly of you, write down one fault they missed. Then let it go.',skills:['communication','psychology']},review:{prompt:'A friend says someone called you lazy behind your back. What do you do?',right:'Laugh and say they missed your other faults',wrong:'Make a list of proof that you are not lazy',why:'If you admit your faults, the mean word has nothing to hit.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A kid at school tells everyone you **can\u2019t catch a ball**. Your face goes hot. You want to prove them wrong right now. But what if there\u2019s **a funnier answer**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Epictetus had one. Say, *you clearly don\u2019t know my other faults, or you\u2019d have mentioned them too.* Now there\u2019s **nothing to argue** about. You\u2019ve agreed you\u2019re not perfect, so **the teasing** loses its sting.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Nobody\u2019s perfect, and **that includes you**. When you happily admit it first, a mean comment has **nowhere to land**. Then you can just get on with your day.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Someone has said something **unflattering** about you, and a third person has kindly passed it on. You\u2019re already drafting your defence in your head. Is that defence really **worth writing**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Epictetus has a reply ready. Don\u2019t defend yourself. Just say the critic clearly didn\u2019t know your **other faults**, or they\u2019d have mentioned those too. It\u2019s a joke, but it has a method inside it.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The same remark leads to **two exchanges**, depending on what **you protect**.',left:{title:'Defending',items:['Keep up a flawless image','Gather your evidence','It runs for days']},right:{title:'Conceding',items:['Admit what\u2019s probably true','Add what they missed','Nothing left to attack']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Defending means keeping up a picture of yourself **without the flaw**. That\u2019s expensive, and usually false. Conceding costs nothing, and it **ends the exchange**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Word reaches you that a colleague called you **disorganised** at lunch. Your instinct is to list every deadline you\u2019ve hit this year. Epictetus would hand you **a stranger reply**. What would he say?'},{kind:'explain',text:'He\u2019d say: *clearly they didn\u2019t know my other faults, or they\u2019d have listed those too.* The move **concedes more** than was asked. There\u2019s nothing left to defend, because you\u2019ve already **surrendered the position**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Jo hears her manager thinks she talks too much in meetings. She tells him, smiling, that he hasn\u2019t seen her emails yet. The conversation that follows is about **what to fix**, not about who\u2019s right.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now Dev hears a neighbour called him rude. He spends a week gathering proof he isn\u2019t: cards he\u2019s sent, parcels he\u2019s taken in. The neighbour never hears any of it. Dev is the only one still **in the argument**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The reply only works because **it\u2019s true**: your faults really are many. Used to dodge a fair point, it\u2019s just a clever deflection. If the criticism is right, the next step is fixing it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You know **your faults** better than any critic does. Admit that first, and there\u2019s **no position** left to attack. You\u2019ll save the energy a defence would cost.'}]},enchiridion_9:{title:'The Blame Ladder',simple:{headline:'Fix your part and blame no one, not even you',body:'Epictetus described three steps. At first, you blame others when things go wrong. Once you start learning, you blame yourself. Once you\u2019ve learned, you blame neither. Your team misses a deadline, and your first sentence blames the client. Later you might blame yourself instead, which feels grown-up. **At the top, you skip the culprit and look at your part.** Check what you decided and how you responded, then fix those. The outcome doesn\u2019t need anyone to blame.',doToday:'Write one sentence about something that went wrong this week that blames nobody and names one thing you\u2019ll change.'},card:{insight:'It is good to stop blaming others, and best to blame no one at all.',tryThis:'After something goes wrong, write down one thing you will change. Blame no one, not even you.',skills:['psychology','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'Your team misses a big deadline. What should you do first?',right:'Write down what to change, and blame no one',wrong:'Tell the team it was all your own fault',why:'Blaming yourself is still blame, so fix what you can change.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your sandcastle gets knocked over by **a big wave**. Your little brother yells, *the sea did it on purpose!* Your older sister says it was **her fault** for building too close. Who\u2019s got it right?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Epictetus said there are **three steps**. First you blame others. Then you blame yourself. Last, you **blame nobody**, because a wave isn\u2019t a crime. You just ask what you\u2019ll build next, and where.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Blaming yourself is **better than blaming others**. But the top step is blaming nobody. Then you can just **get on** with fixing things.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A project goes badly. Your first sentence is **about the client**. A week later you\u2019ve moved on to **blaming yourself**, and it feels like growing up. Is that really the top of the ladder?'},{kind:'story',text:'Epictetus gives a three-rung test. The uninstructed blame others. Those who\u2019ve **begun learning** blame themselves. Those who\u2019ve finished blame **neither**, because they no longer treat the outcome as something that needs a culprit.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The middle rung is **the trap**. Self-blame feels mature, but it\u2019s the same error with the arrow reversed. You\u2019re still hunting **a verdict** about something you never controlled. Your part, though, is always worth assessing.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Blame neither, and look at **your part**: the judgement, the effort, the response. **The outcome** is weather, and weather doesn\u2019t need a culprit.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Most advice says the mature move is to **take the blame**. Epictetus agrees, up to a point. For him, blaming yourself is only **the middle rung**. So what\u2019s at the top?'},{kind:'explain',text:'At the top, you **blame neither**. Not out of indifference. You still assess **your part**: the judgement, the effort, the response. But the outcome is weather, and you stop hunting for a culprit.'},{kind:'story',text:'A young chef\u2019s supplier sends the wrong fish, and she rages at him. Ten years on, she blames herself for not checking. Twenty years on, she just asks what tonight\u2019s menu can do with cod.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a parent whose child fails a test. Rung one blames the teacher. Rung two blames their own parenting. Rung three asks what the child needs next week, and leaves the **verdict** out entirely.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Blame has real uses. Courts and workplaces need to know who did what, and **an apology owed** is still owed. The ladder is about your inner verdict on outcomes, not public responsibility.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Notice where **your first sentence** puts the fault, because that\u2019s your rung. The aim isn\u2019t a better culprit. It\u2019s **no culprit**, and a clear look at your part.'}]}}}},958,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'environment-beats-willpower',kind:'book',promise:'Fix your habits by changing your room',about:'Why do so many habits fail, even when you mean them? The Mindroll team wrote this short article about it. It says the fix is often in your room, not your willpower. Move a few things, and good habits get easier. Want to try it?',ideas:{'environment-beats-willpower_1':{title:'The Extra Step',card:{insight:'One extra step cuts a habit more than any promise does.',tryThis:'Pick one habit you want less of. Add one step to it, like unplugging the game console.',skills:['habits','psychology']},review:{prompt:'You want to watch less TV at night. What do you change?',right:'Add a step, like unplugging the TV each night',wrong:'Promise yourself you will turn it off by ten',why:'A step still works when you are tired, but a promise often breaks.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'The biscuit tin sits on the kitchen table. You grab one every time you walk past. Then someone moves the tin to **a high shelf**. Do you still eat **as many**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Mostly, you do **what\u2019s easiest**. One extra step, like opening a door and reaching up, makes you do a thing **less often**. Take a step away and you do it **more often**. It works even when you\u2019re tired.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Steps steer you more than **promises** do. So add a step to **what you want less**, and remove one from what you want more.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'On Sunday you delete a social app from your phone. By Wednesday you\u2019ve reached for it **a dozen times**. Each time it\u2019s gone, and you don\u2019t reinstall it. Were you **any stronger** on Wednesday?'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Priya, who loses most evenings to video games. The console sits under the TV, always plugged in. One Sunday she unplugs it and packs it in its box. By Friday she\u2019s playing far less, with no new rule at all.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s why it works. **Friction**, the number of steps between you and an action, predicts behaviour better than intention. A step asks nothing of you **at the moment** of choice. That\u2019s exactly when your resolve is **least reliable**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The lever runs both ways, and **adding steps** is the half people **often neglect**.',left:{title:'Want more',items:['Shoes by the door','Bag packed the night before']},right:{title:'Want less',items:['App deleted from the phone','Sweets on the top shelf']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Stop relying on resolving harder. Remove **a step** from what you want, and add one to **what you don\u2019t**. You\u2019ll feel no stronger, but you\u2019ll act differently.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Most people treat a habit that keeps failing as **a character flaw**. Not enough discipline, not enough grit. But what if the problem is simply **the layout**? What would you look at first?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The piece\u2019s claim is unflattering. Behaviour follows **the easiest path** more reliably than it follows intention. Default options beat chosen ones. Items at eye level outsell those on **low shelves**. One step removed changes **how many people** act.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Ben, who deletes a social app on Sunday. On Wednesday the urge arrives as usual. To give in, he\u2019d have to reinstall it, log in and wait. In that slower moment, he decides **not to bother**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a runner who lays out her kit the night before and leaves her shoes by the door. When the alarm goes, there\u2019s **nothing left** to decide. She\u2019s already dressed before the excuses arrive.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Friction works best on habits driven by **convenience**. A strong craving will often route round a single step. And the same lever can be **aimed at you**, like a subscription you can only cancel by phone.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Before blaming your character, **count the steps**. Make the good path **shorter** and the bad one longer. Then notice who else is setting your steps.'}]},'environment-beats-willpower_2':{title:'The Worst-Day Test',card:{insight:'A plan that needs willpower each day breaks on hard days.',tryThis:'Pick one habit you run on willpower. Set it up once so it still happens on your worst day.',skills:['habits','psychology']},review:{prompt:'You keep skipping your evening walk after long days at work. What helps most?',right:'Set it up once so it happens on your worst day',wrong:'Promise to push through with more willpower each night',why:'Use your effort once to set things up, not every day to fight.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'On Sunday you plan to practise piano every day after school. Monday goes well. On Wednesday you\u2019re tired, hungry and fed up. Does the piano **get played**, or does it **stay shut**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Some days you feel **strong**, and some days you don\u2019t. Trying hard is hardest when you\u2019re tired or upset. So set things up **once**. Leave the lid open and the music on the stand. Then a bad day still has **an easy way in**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A plan that needs you **feeling strong** breaks on your bad days. So plan for **your worst day**, not your best one.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your plan has you training **four mornings** this week. Monday is new and exciting. Thursday comes after a late, stressful night. Which morning is the plan **most likely** to fail on?'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Jonah, who wants a healthy packed lunch each day. Most evenings he means to cook, then he\u2019s too tired and orders in. So he cooks a big batch on Sunday afternoon. Now weeknights ask nothing of him.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Whether willpower runs down like a battery is **genuinely contested**. The research behind that idea has replicated poorly. But the practical point still holds. Self-control varies with **sleep, stress and hunger**, and it\u2019s lowest when life is hardest.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The design principle is to **spend willpower once**, on arranging conditions, not **every day**.',left:{title:'Daily resolve',items:['Paid every single day','Fails under load']},right:{title:'An arrangement',items:['Paid once','Works on your worst day']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t build a plan that only works on **good days**. Arrange the room, the phone or the calendar once. Then **your worst day** still gets the job done.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture a plan that works beautifully on **good days**. You sleep well, you\u2019re calm, and you do the thing. Now picture the week your child is ill and work goes wrong. What happens to **the plan**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The piece argues that willpower is **a poor daily input**. It varies with sleep, stress and hunger. It\u2019s lowest exactly when circumstances are hardest. So a plan needing it daily **fails under load**, the very condition it was meant to survive.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Amara, who wants to study every evening for a course. She relies on resolve, and it works for a fortnight. Then a hard month at work arrives. The books stay closed for **three weeks**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a nurse on rotating shifts who wants to drink more water. She can\u2019t rely on remembering. So she fills two bottles each night and leaves them by her keys. On the worst shift, they\u2019re **already packed**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Arranging things takes willpower too, just once, and some setups need upkeep. **Not everything** can be arranged. A hard conversation may still need effort in the moment. The point is **which input** you rely on.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Spend your willpower **once**, on arranging things. Don\u2019t spend it **every day** fighting them. The arrangement stays put, and the difference grows over months.'}]},'environment-beats-willpower_3':{title:'The One-Time Choice',card:{insight:'A choice you make once and keep beats the same choice made every day.',tryThis:'Pick one choice you make often, like when to work out. Make it a rule with a set time.',skills:['habits','productivity']},review:{prompt:'Each evening you go back and forth about going to the gym. What helps most?',right:'Make it a rule with set days and a set time',wrong:'Decide fresh each evening, based on how you feel',why:'Each time you choose again, the easy way gets one more chance to win.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Every morning you stand by the fridge and decide what to take for lunch. Some days it\u2019s fruit. Most days it\u2019s **crisps**. Why does the choice keep **sliding** the same way?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Each time you decide, you get **another chance** to pick the easy thing. And you often decide when you\u2019re rushed or tired. So decide **just once**. Fruit on school days, every week. Then there\u2019s **nothing to decide**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Choices you make **every day** keep sliding towards the easy option. Make the choice **once** and stick to it. Then you stop paying for it.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'At six each evening you ask the same question. **Gym or sofa?** You weigh it up, you feel tired, and the sofa wins again. What if the question **never came up**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Sofia, who used to decide each night whether to run the next day. She usually said no. So she set a standing rule: Monday, Wednesday and Friday, seven o\u2019clock, no exceptions. Now she runs three times a week.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s the reason. Each repeat is a fresh chance to **decide differently**. And you re-decide in worse conditions than you first decided: later, more tired, more pressured. So repeated choices drift towards **the easy option**. The sampling is **biased**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'A rule with exceptions **brings the decision back**. A rule without them **removes it**.',left:{title:'With exceptions',items:['A judgement every time','Drifts to the easy option']},right:{title:'No exceptions',items:['Nothing left to decide','Holds on tired days']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Turn **recurring choices** into standing arrangements. A fixed time, a set place, a rule with **no exceptions**. You\u2019ll have fewer moments where the outcome depends on your mood.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Think of a choice you make **over and over**. What to eat, when to exercise, whether to check email first. You make it fresh each time. Is each fresh decision really **a free choice**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The piece says a decision made once and **built into structure** beats the same one made repeatedly. Each repeat is a chance to decide again. And you usually re-decide **under worse conditions**, so the answers drift towards ease.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Leo, who saves a little each month, whenever he remembers. Most months, by the time he thinks of it, the money\u2019s gone. Then he sets up **a standing order** on payday. He hasn\u2019t had to decide since.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a writer who blocks nine to ten every weekday in her calendar. She doesn\u2019t ask whether she feels like writing. The block is already there, like a meeting. So the question **never gets asked**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Fixed rules stop responding to change. A rule set in January may be **wrong by June**, so review it deliberately, not in the moment. And a rigid rule can hurt, like **training through an injury** to keep it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Every choice you **settle once** is one you stop paying for. Build it into the week, and keep the rule **simple and firm**. Then fewer days hang on how you happen to feel.'}]},'environment-beats-willpower_4':{title:'Tie It to a Habit',card:{insight:'A new habit sticks when you tie it to one you never skip.',tryThis:'Pick a thing you do each day, like making coffee. Do the new habit right after it.',skills:['habits','productivity']},review:{prompt:'You forget to take your vitamins each morning. What helps most?',right:'Keep the bottle right by the coffee pot',wrong:'Try harder to remember them as soon as you wake',why:'Now you see the bottle each day, so you do not forget.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You want to stretch every night before bed. You mean it, too. But night after night, you **just forget**. You only remember once you\u2019re lying down. What\u2019s **missing** from the plan?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Most new habits fail because **you forget**, not because you don\u2019t want them. So tie the new habit to something **you already do**. After you brush your teeth, do one stretch. Brushing becomes **the reminder**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Hang your new habit on **an old one**. The old habit is the hook. Keep the new thing **tiny**, so the hook can hold it.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You buy vitamins and put them in a cupboard. You mean to take one **every morning**. Three weeks later the bottle is nearly full. Did you decide against them, or did you **never remember**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Hana, who moves the vitamin bottle out of the cupboard. She stands it beside the kettle, which she uses every morning without fail. Now reaching for the kettle means seeing the bottle. She hasn\u2019t missed a day since.'},{kind:'explain',text:'An established habit already has **a reliable trigger**. Attach something new and it **inherits the cue**. The kettle boiling, the laptop opening: each happens without being remembered. That fixes what kills most new habits, which is **forgetting**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The technique has **two requirements**, and missing either is why people **give up** on it.',left:{title:'Holds',items:['A routine you never skip','A tiny new step']},right:{title:'Breaks',items:['A routine you only sometimes do','Something big that crowds it out']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t just plan the habit. Plan **the cue**. Pick a moment that already happens **every day**, and hang something small from it.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'You design a new habit carefully. You know **what to do** and why it matters. Then the moment comes and goes, and you don\u2019t notice. Later you blame yourself. But **which part** did you never build?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The piece says people design the behaviour and the motivation, then **leave out the cue**. Anchoring fixes that. A new behaviour tied to a **stable routine** inherits a trigger that already fires. You don\u2019t have to **remember** anything.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Omar, who wants ten minutes of language practice a day. He used to set a vague evening slot and kept missing it. Now he practises on the train, **straight after** he sits down. He takes that train every workday.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take someone who ties twenty minutes of journalling to an evening run. Trouble is, the run only happens some weeks. Within a month, the journalling has stopped, and **the run** has started to wobble too.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The anchor must be **truly stable**, and the new habit small enough to fit. Anchors also move when life does. A new job or a holiday removes the cue, which can feel like **lost discipline**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Most new habits die from **forgetting**, not unwillingness. So borrow a cue from a routine **you never skip**. Keep the new step small enough not to crowd it.'}]},'environment-beats-willpower_5':{title:'The Morning After',card:{insight:'A missed day only hurts when you take it as proof you failed.',tryThis:'Write down now what you will do the morning after you next miss a day.',skills:['habits','psychology']},review:{prompt:'Your 41-day streak just broke because you were sick. What now?',right:'Treat it as one missed day and start again tomorrow',wrong:'Wait and start a brand new streak next Monday',why:'One miss does little harm, but calling it failure can make you quit.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Kai read every night for **twenty nights**. Then he went to a sleepover and missed one. The next night he thought, *what\u2019s the point now?* What do you think happened **next**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Kai didn\u2019t read again **for a month**. Missing one night didn\u2019t stop him. A thought did: *I\u2019ve failed*. One slip felt like proof, and that feeling did the damage, not the slip itself.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Everyone misses **a day** sometimes. What matters is what you do **the next day**. Make one missed day just one missed day, not the end of your habit.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your habit app shows a **41-day streak**. Then you\u2019re ill for two days and the number drops to zero. Nothing about the habit got harder. So why does starting again feel **so heavy**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Ruth, who draws every evening. After a bad week she misses two days, and reads it as proof she\u2019s **not an artist**. Two days become two months. The pencils didn\u2019t change. Her reading of the gap did.'},{kind:'explain',text:'A missed day becomes a missed week when you treat it as **evidence**. The lapse is a single event. **The interpretation** does the damage. So the useful question isn\u2019t how to never miss. It\u2019s what happens **when you do**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The fixes are **unglamorous**, and each one makes a lapse **harder to misread**.',left:{title:'Fragile',items:['Everything rides on the streak','No plan for a miss']},right:{title:'Sturdy',items:['A tiny worst-day version','A clear rule to resume']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t design for never missing. Design so **a miss stays small**. Plan the morning after now, while you\u2019re calm, and **one bad day** stays one bad day.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two people keep the same habit for six weeks, and both miss a day. One is back the next morning. The other is gone **for months**. The miss was identical. So what **really differed**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The piece puts the danger in **the interpretation**, not the lapse. Read a miss as proof that the attempt has failed, and you stop. So design to make that reading **hard to reach**: a tiny minimum version, and **a rule for resuming**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Dev, who meditates daily, with a fallback of **one minute** on the worst days. On the night his flight lands at two, he does sixty seconds on the hotel bed. It barely counts, yet the habit is still running.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture someone motivated by a long streak. While it\u2019s intact, it pulls her along. Then an ordinary missed day resets it to zero, and zero looks like **a reason to quit**. The streak that motivated her now ends the habit.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Small failure isn\u2019t the same as **no standard**. A minimum version can quietly become the whole habit. Some goals need the full dose. And repeated misses are **information** about your design, not noise you should ignore.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You will miss days. Build the plan so a miss **stays a single event**. Have a tiny version, a rule to resume, and **no streak** that carries the whole attempt.'}]},'environment-beats-willpower_6':{title:'The Book on the Pillow',card:{insight:'The things around you shape what you do more than your promises do.',tryThis:'Tonight, put a book on your pillow, or charge your phone in the hall.',skills:['habits','psychology']},review:{prompt:'You check your phone first thing every morning and want to stop. What helps?',right:'Charge the phone outside the bedroom tonight',wrong:'Swear you will leave it alone in the morning',why:'Move the phone once, and it keeps working with no promise.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You want to read more at bedtime. You promise yourself, again and again. Then one night you try something different: you put **a book** right **on your pillow**. What happens when you go to lie down?'},{kind:'explain',text:'To lie down, you have to **pick the book up**. So you open it. The things around you shape what you do **more than promises**. What you can reach and see **nudges you** all day, without you noticing.'},{kind:'moral',text:'To change a habit, change **the room**, not just your mind. Moving one thing works when **you want it**. It can\u2019t make you want something you don\u2019t.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two people want to **cook at home** more. One resolves to try harder. The other moves the takeaway apps off her home screen and shops for three meals on Sunday. Who do you think is **cooking more** a month later?'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Aisha, who wants to read more and has promised herself for years. One evening she puts a novel on her pillow. Every night after, it\u2019s in the way of lying down. She reads a chapter **almost every night**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The piece\u2019s strongest claim is that the environment **is the plan**. What\u2019s in reach, what\u2019s visible, what\u2019s already open and who\u2019s around all steer you. They act **continuously**, with no attention. A resolution needs **summoning every time**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'So stop asking what you\u2019ll **do differently**. Ask what you\u2019ll **change around you**.',left:{title:'Resolving harder',items:['Repeated every day','Fades over time']},right:{title:'Moving an object',items:['Done once','Works every night after']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Most people try to fix a habit by **resolving harder**. They never once **move an object**. Change the room, the phone or the calendar, and you\u2019ll have changed the plan.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Think of a habit you\u2019ve tried to fix by **resolving harder**. Maybe more than once. Now think about the room where it happens. Which object there have you **never moved**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Most advice treats your surroundings as a helper. The piece goes further: the environment isn\u2019t **support for the plan**. It **is the plan**. Reach, visibility, defaults and company shape behaviour more reliably than intention, and they need **no attention**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Marcus, who wanted to stop scrolling in bed. Promises lasted a night or two. Then he left his phone charging in the kitchen. Now the scrolling would mean **getting up**, and he rarely bothers.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a household that wants to eat more fruit. Nobody makes a promise. Someone simply puts a full bowl on the counter, where the biscuit tin used to be. Within a week, the fruit is going **first**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Design can\u2019t **manufacture motivation** for something you don\u2019t want. It turns weak intention into steady behaviour. It doesn\u2019t supply the intention. And some rooms, like a shared home or an office, **aren\u2019t yours alone**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Each change to your surroundings is **one act** with a lasting effect. A resolution is **a repeated act** that fades. So spend your intention once, on moving things.'}]}}}},959,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'franklin-autobiography',kind:'book',promise:'Franklin\u2019s ways to fix bad habits and make friends',about:'How do you fix a bad habit? Benjamin Franklin found a way. He was a printer who helped start the United States. For years, he marked each slip on a chart. He also learned to win people over. Which trick will you try first?',ideas:{'franklin-autobiography_1':{title:'One Virtue a Week',simple:{headline:'Fix one habit a week, not all at once',body:'Benjamin Franklin wanted thirteen good habits at once, and kept failing. While he watched for one fault, another caught him off guard. So he gave one habit a full week of attention and only noted slips in the rest. After thirteen weeks he started the round again, so each habit came up four times a year. Say you want to sleep earlier, exercise more and read more. **Give this week to bedtime alone, and only watch the other two.** Next week, exercise gets its turn.',doToday:'Pick one habit for this week, and write its rule so you can answer yes or no at bedtime.'},card:{insight:'Give one habit a whole week, and just mark slips in the rest.',tryThis:'Tonight, pick one habit for this week. Write a short rule you can check off each night.',skills:['habits','productivity']},review:{prompt:'You want to read more and go to bed earlier. Where do you start?',right:'Pick one habit this week, and save the other for next week',wrong:'Start both on Monday while you feel fired up',why:'Try to fix all your habits at once, and you slip on most.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'It\u2019s New Year. You write a list: read more, eat less junk, tidy your room, practise piano. By **week two** you\u2019ve kept none of them. What went **wrong** with the list?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Benjamin Franklin lived in America almost 300 years ago. He wanted **thirteen good habits**. He saw that trying them all at once wouldn\u2019t work. So he picked **one a week** and just noted slips in the others.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your attention only stretches so far. Give it to **one habit** at a time. Keep an eye on the rest, but **don\u2019t fix them** yet.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A manager asks her team to fix **four things** this quarter: punctuality, documents, code review and testing. Everyone agrees on Monday. By week three nothing has changed. Why do **big lists** fail you so reliably?'},{kind:'story',text:'In his memoir Franklin describes a scheme from his youth. He listed thirteen virtues, from Temperance to Humility, each with a one-line rule. Whenever he watched for one fault, a different one caught him off guard. Habit kept beating good intention.'},{kind:'explain',text:'So he didn\u2019t try harder. He changed the order of attack. Each virtue got **a full week** while he simply noted slips in the rest. Thirteen weeks made a cycle, so each came round **four times a year**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The team\u2019s **four goals** face **the same maths** as Franklin\u2019s thirteen virtues.',left:{title:'All at once',items:['Attention split four ways','Small failures every day','Quit by week three']},right:{title:'One a week',items:['Full attention on one','The rest only watched','Each gets regular turns']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Change comes **one behaviour** at a time. Give one habit your full attention and only watch the others. The **order of attack**, not willpower, does the work.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Franklin wrote the second part of his memoir in his late seventies. In it he looks back on **a scheme** from his youth: to reach **moral perfection**. Why does an old man spend pages on a plan that failed?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The plan was **thirteen virtues**, each with a rule short enough to check at bedtime. He gave one virtue a week of full attention and **only recorded** slips in the rest. The order mattered too.'},{kind:'story',text:'Temperance came first, because a clear head makes everything else easier. Silence came next, to leave room for learning. Each early virtue was meant to ease the next. He compared himself to a gardener clearing one bed at a time.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture yourself wanting to sleep earlier, move more and read more. Week one is only about bedtime. You notice the other two, but you don\u2019t chase them. Next week, exercise gets its turn.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Franklin never finished: Order beat him for life. And the rotation assumes habits are separate. Sleep, drink and temper often **move together**, so fixing one can fix three. The method rations attention. It doesn\u2019t prove change must be slow.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Willpower spread thirteen ways fails thirteen ways. Franklin\u2019s fix was **a sequence**: one virtue in focus, the rest **under watch**. Try it with your own list, one week at a time.'}]},'franklin-autobiography_2':{title:'The Black Spot',simple:{headline:'Mark each slip on a chart, and you slip less',body:'Benjamin Franklin kept a small notebook with a column for each day of the week. Each time he slipped on a habit, he marked a black spot. The first count shocked him, with far more faults than he\u2019d guessed. Over the weeks he watched the spots thin out. To buy fewer takeaway coffees, skip the rules for now. Make a tick in your phone\u2019s notes for each one you buy. **Knowing a mark is coming changes the moment before you buy.** Keep the record honest, even on bad days.',doToday:'Make a tick in your phone\u2019s notes each time you do the habit you want to cut, starting now.'},card:{insight:'Mark each slip as it happens, and soon you will slip less.',tryThis:'Today, pick one habit you want to cut. Make a mark in your phone each time you slip.',skills:['habits','psychology']},review:{prompt:'You want to drink less soda but hate strict rules. What do you do?',right:'Make a mark each time you have one',wrong:'Throw out all the soda in the house',why:'Each mark shows you a slip, so you start to slip less.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You want to eat fewer sweets. You make **no rules** at all. You just put a tick on a card each time you eat one. What do you think **happens next**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Franklin kept a small book with a box for each habit on each day. Each time he slipped, he put **a black dot** in the box. Before the book, he thought he was doing fine. The dots showed **far more** slips.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Counting a habit can **change it**, even with no plan. You think twice when **a mark is coming**. That\u2019s why the dots worked.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You install an app that logs every coffee and taxi. You set **no budget**. You just let it count. By week three you\u2019re walking past the caf\xe9, and you **never decided** to. Why?'},{kind:'story',text:'Franklin ruled a small book into days and virtues. Every fault earned a black spot. The first count was a shock: far more faults than he\u2019d ever guessed. Then, over the weeks, he got to watch the spots thin out.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Why did it work? Before the book he had **a vague sense** of doing well. The marks swapped that feeling for **a count**. And knowing a mark is coming changes the moment **before you act**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'So the tool **barely matters**. Paper, an app or tallies on a wall will all do. What matters is that you\u2019ll record it, every time. A crude record you keep beats **a clever one** you ignore.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Measuring is itself **a change**. You don\u2019t need a plan or a punishment. Just **record every slip** honestly, and the count starts working on you.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Franklin rubbed out marks in his little book so often that the paper **wore through**. So he moved his tables onto **ivory leaves** that wiped clean. Why would you care that much about a few black spots?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Each page had seven columns for the days and thirteen lines for the virtues. A **black spot** marked each slip. The weekly aim was to keep one line, that week\u2019s virtue, **completely clean**.'},{kind:'story',text:'His first surprise was the total. Before the book he\u2019d had a general sense of doing quite well. The count came out much worse. Things measured for the first time after years of guessing usually do.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a speaker who fills every pause with *um*. For one week he tallies each one and tries nothing else. By Friday there are fewer marks. Each um now costs something he can see.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The effect can fade once recording is routine: Franklin\u2019s courses grew rarer over the years, then stopped. A record improves **what\u2019s recorded**, not what matters. And for some people, close tracking feeds anxiety instead.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The count is **the intervention**. It works on the moment before the slip, which no resolution reaches. Just make sure you\u2019re counting **the right thing**.'}]},'franklin-autobiography_3':{title:'The Speckled Axe',simple:{headline:'If a habit works well enough, let it be',body:'Benjamin Franklin never managed to keep his papers in order. He told a story about it. A man asked a smith to grind his whole axe bright, and turned the grinding wheel himself. He got so tired that he decided he liked a speckled axe best. The edge already cut well. Say you\u2019ve chased an empty inbox for three years and never kept it for a week. Yet you still answer what matters. **Ask what more effort would give you, and stop where it gives little.** Be honest with yourself, because this can also be an excuse.',doToday:'Write down one habit you\u2019ve failed at for years, and what keeping it would give you.'},card:{insight:'If a habit does the job, more work on it will not add much.',tryThis:'Tonight, write one habit you keep missing. Next to it, write what fixing it would give you.',skills:['habits','thinking']},review:{prompt:'You never clear your email, but you answer what matters. What now?',right:'Leave it, since more work there will not add much',wrong:'Make a strict plan to empty it every day',why:'You get the job done, so more work on email adds little.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve tried to keep a **tidy desk** for two whole years. It\u2019s always a mess. But you never lose anything, and you\u2019re never late. Should you **keep trying**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Franklin tried for years to keep things tidy. He never managed it. He told a story about a man polishing an axe. The man got tired, and decided he liked **a spotty axe** best.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Be honest about the habit **you keep failing**. Ask if it\u2019s worth all that effort. If not, put **the effort** where it helps more.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve chased an **empty inbox** for three years and never held it a week. Yet you still answer everything that matters. Is the system failing you, or is it **the goal**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Of Franklin\u2019s thirteen virtues, Order gave him the most trouble: a place for everything, a time for each task. He explains why. His memory let him find things without tidiness. And a printer\u2019s hours were set by his customers.'},{kind:'explain',text:'His verdict came as a parable. A man wanted his whole axe as bright as its edge. He turned **the grindstone** until he was worn out. Then he decided he liked **a speckled axe** best.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The edge does **the cutting**. A few spots on the blade don\u2019t stop the axe working. So the honest question isn\u2019t whether you\u2019re perfect. It\u2019s whether more grinding **returns anything**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Effort has **diminishing returns**. Keep your record honest enough to show where it stops paying. Then stop grinding there, **not everywhere**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Franklin\u2019s memoir is mostly a story of methods that worked. Then he reaches **one virtue** that beat him for life, and he doesn\u2019t hide it. Why would a man describing his methods **admit the failure**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Order sat **third** on his list, placed early because it would free time for study. His reasons for failing were specific. He wasn\u2019t used to method, his memory covered the gaps, and **his customers** set his hours.'},{kind:'story',text:'Then the parable. A man asks a smith to grind his whole axe bright, and agrees to turn the wheel himself. He turns until he\u2019s exhausted. Finally he declares that he likes a speckled axe best.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a cook who keeps resolving to label every jar in her kitchen. She never keeps it up past a week. Yet she always finds what she needs. Another round of labelling would cost evenings and return almost nothing.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Diminishing returns is also **the oldest excuse**, and Franklin knew it: he suspected himself of the axe man\u2019s trick. And the trade can go stale. In old age, with his memory failing, he felt the lack keenly.'},{kind:'moral',text:'An honest record shows **where effort stops paying**. Stop grinding there, and check the verdict again as your life changes. Naming the failure is also why you can **trust the rest**.'}]},'franklin-autobiography_4':{title:'Humility You Can Hear',simple:{headline:'Sound less sure, and more people will agree',body:'A Quaker friend told Benjamin Franklin that people found him proud and bossy in arguments. He couldn\u2019t fix the feeling, so he fixed his words. He dropped \u201ccertainly\u201d and \u201cundoubtedly\u201d, and opened with phrases like \u201cI imagine\u201d or \u201cit seems to me\u201d. In a meeting, you\u2019re sure a plan will go over budget. Announce it as fact, and its author digs in. **Put it gently, and people can agree without losing face.** This habit, he said, won him much of his support, though he was a poor public speaker.',doToday:'Rewrite one firm opinion in a message or note so it opens with \u201cIt seems to me\u201d.'},card:{insight:'If you sound too sure, people argue back, so say how it seems to you.',tryThis:'Next time you don\u2019t agree, say \u201cit seems to me\u201d first. Then share your view.',skills:['communication','psychology']},review:{prompt:'You think a plan costs too much. How do you say so?',right:'It seems to me it costs too much, but I\u2019m not sure',wrong:'This plan costs way too much, and I am sure of it',why:'When you sound less sure, people can say yes and still feel good.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your friend says the film starts at six. You\u2019re **sure** it\u2019s seven. You could say *you\u2019re wrong*. But is there **a better way** to say what you think?'},{kind:'story',text:'A friend told Franklin that people found him proud and bossy. So he stopped saying words like certainly and no doubt. He stopped telling people flat out they were wrong. He started saying *it seems to me* instead.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Franklin never felt **truly humble**. But he learned to **speak humbly**, and people listened more. You can still say what you think, just softly.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'In a meeting you\u2019re **certain** the plan will overrun its budget. You say so, flatly. The room goes quiet, and the plan\u2019s author **digs in**. Being right didn\u2019t help. Why not?'},{kind:'story',text:'Franklin\u2019s list of virtues first had twelve. Then a Quaker friend kindly told him he was thought proud, overbearing in talk and sometimes rude in argument. Franklin added a thirteenth: Humility.'},{kind:'explain',text:'He couldn\u2019t manage the feeling, so he managed **the manner**. He dropped words like certainly and undoubtedly. Instead he\u2019d say *it seems to me* or I imagine. He began by **granting** where the other person might be right.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The same budget worry lands **very differently** depending on how you **frame it**.',left:{title:'Stated flatly',items:['They hear a challenge','Agreeing means losing face','They defend, even unsure']},right:{title:'Stated tentatively',items:['They hear a view','Agreeing costs them nothing','They weigh the point']}},{kind:'moral',text:'The room can\u2019t see **your inner state**. It can only hear how you speak. Put your view **tentatively**, and people can agree without first admitting defeat.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Franklin admits he spoke badly in public. He stumbled over words and was no orator. Yet he says **one habit** did most to win support for his public schemes. What could a poor speaker do that **outweighs eloquence**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The habit was **tentative phrasing**. He admits he never conquered pride, only its expression. Had he conquered it, he jokes, he\u2019d probably be proud of his humility. The joke is the argument: **the inner state** may be out of reach.'},{kind:'story',text:'He also says the soft manner felt forced at first, then turned natural. For decades afterwards, he claims, nobody heard him lay down the law. The performance had become the habit.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a junior analyst who thinks a senior colleague\u2019s forecast is wrong. She doesn\u2019t say so. She asks where one figure comes from, and says the trend seems to have turned. He checks, and revises it himself.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Performance without substance is also **the con man\u2019s method**. Tentative phrasing persuades whether or not you\u2019re right, so it makes you more effective, not more correct. It\u2019s defensible when you\u2019re seeking the truth together, not trying to win.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Some virtues are hard to tell from **their outward form**. Franklin performed a humility he didn\u2019t feel, and the room couldn\u2019t tell. Use the manner to **find the truth**, not to win.'}]},'franklin-autobiography_5':{title:'The Usefulness Test',simple:{headline:'Judge things by the job they do, not their name',body:'Philadelphia\u2019s round glass street lamps filled with smoke, and one knock smashed the whole globe. Benjamin Franklin suggested four flat panes with vents. The smoke escaped, and a knock broke only one pane. He argued for the lending library and the fire company the same way. They helped people. Your team must pick a famous method nobody has tried or a plain process that has worked three times. **Ask what each one has changed, and for whom.** You can check that answer, so the argument can end.',doToday:'Write down a choice you\u2019re weighing, and beside each option, what it would change and for whom.'},card:{insight:'Judge a thing by how well it does the job you need done.',tryThis:'Before you buy something, write down what you need it to do. Then pick the one that does it best.',skills:['thinking','productivity']},review:{prompt:'You need a new backpack for school. How do you pick one?',right:'Pick the one that does what I need it to do',wrong:'Pick the famous brand that all my friends have',why:'A famous name does not help if the thing does a bad job.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'One friend says to copy your notes out in **neat colours**. Another says to test yourself with **flash cards**. The first looks great, the second looks dull. Which one should you pick?'},{kind:'story',text:'Franklin helped start a library people could join and borrow from. He helped set up a team to fight fires. When he explained why, he didn\u2019t say it was noble. He said it worked and helped people.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Asking whether something is grand can start **a long argument**. Asking **does it work** is something you can check. So try it, and see what changed.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your team must pick between **a respected framework** nobody has used and a scrappy process that has shipped three projects. One sounds principled. One sounds ordinary. Which should **count for more**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Philadelphia\u2019s round glass street lamps from London filled with smoke and soon dimmed. One knock broke the whole globe. Franklin proposed four flat panes, with vents to carry the smoke away. A broken pane now cost one pane.'},{kind:'explain',text:'His case for the lamp was entirely about **what worked**. That\u2019s the pattern across the book: the library, the fire company, paved streets. He rarely appeals to duty. The reason given is that **people benefited**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Principles often leave a dispute open, because **both sides** can claim them. **Usefulness** can be checked.',left:{title:'Is it admired?',items:['Sounds principled','Hard to test','Arguments run on']},right:{title:'Does it work?',items:['Sounds ordinary','Can be checked','Settles the dispute']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Usefulness is **a fair test**, and one you can actually apply. Ask what **actually changed**, and for whom. That settles arguments principle leaves open.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'One mentor says build the product **properly**, on principle. Another says ship something rough and see whether anyone uses it. Both sound wise. How would you choose, when **principle alone** can\u2019t settle it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Franklin\u2019s answer runs through the whole memoir: ask **what it produced**, and for whom. He uses that test without apology, in a century that expected **nobler reasons**. It works because you can check it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take his street lamp. The London globes filled with smoke, and a single knock smashed one. His design used four flat panes and vents for the smoke. Smoke escaped, and a break cost only one pane.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a charity choosing between a grand awards dinner and a plain text reminder to donors. The dinner is admired. Ask which one actually raised more, and the choice gets clearer.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. A life tuned for usefulness can stop asking whether **the useful thing** is worth doing. Critics have called his approach shallow. So use his test for how, and supply the why yourself.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Usefulness is a test **you can check**, which is rare among values. Its strength is that it settles disputes. Its weakness is that it **never asks why**.'}]},'franklin-autobiography_6':{title:'The Junto Rule',simple:{headline:'Ask a question, and let people spot their mistake',body:'At 21, Benjamin Franklin started a weekly club of young tradesmen called the Junto. They debated politics and science. Members weren\u2019t allowed to contradict each other, and breaking the rule cost a small fine. A colleague states a wrong figure in a meeting. Tell them it\u2019s wrong in front of everyone, and they\u2019ll spend ten minutes defending it. **Ask where the figure comes from instead.** They can correct it themselves without losing face. Save blunt correction for errors that can\u2019t wait, like a safety risk.',doToday:'Find a recent message where you told someone they were wrong, and rewrite it as a question.'},card:{insight:'If you tell people they are wrong, they fight back, so ask a question.',tryThis:'Next time someone gets a fact wrong, ask where they heard it. Then let them check.',skills:['communication','leadership']},review:{prompt:'A coworker shares a fact you think is wrong. What do you say?',right:'Ask where they heard it, so they can check it',wrong:'Tell them right away that the fact is wrong',why:'A question lets them fix it on their own and not look silly.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'In class, a girl says the answer is 12. You call out, *No, that\u2019s wrong!* Everyone **turns to look** at her. What is she **most likely** to do next?'},{kind:'story',text:'When Franklin was young, he started a club with friends. They met each week to talk about big questions. The club had a rule. Nobody could tell someone flat out that they were wrong. Breaking it cost a small fine.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Telling someone *you\u2019re wrong* in front of others **traps them**. Ask a question instead. You\u2019ll **leave them a way** to change their mind.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Someone in a meeting states **a figure** that\u2019s plainly wrong. A colleague says so, flatly. For the next ten minutes nobody discusses the figure. They watch the first person **defend it**. What went wrong?'},{kind:'story',text:'At about twenty-one, Franklin formed a club of young tradesmen called the Junto. They met to debate questions of morals, politics and science. Confident assertion and direct contradiction were banned. Breaking the rule cost a small fine.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The rule wasn\u2019t about manners. Being told you\u2019re wrong in public makes retreat **expensive**, because backing down costs standing. So people defend positions they no longer hold. The Junto kept **the exit cheap**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Your meeting could have gone **two ways** once the wrong figure was **on the table**.',left:{title:'Contradiction',items:['That figure\u2019s wrong','They must lose to agree','A contest to watch']},right:{title:'Inquiry',items:['Where\u2019s that figure from?','They can revise freely','The figure stays central']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Rules of debate are **engineering**. Ban flat contradiction, and changing your mind in public costs almost nothing. Then the group **corrects itself** instead of splitting into sides.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Franklin\u2019s club met **every week** for nearly forty years. It was mostly young tradesmen, arguing about morals, politics and science. Most groups that meet to think end up meeting **to win**. How did this one avoid it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The Junto, founded in 1727, treated debate as **a search for truth**, not a contest. Dogmatic assertion and direct contradiction were banned. Each lapse cost **a small fine**, which made the rule real without making it grave.'},{kind:'story',text:'The fine worked like Franklin\u2019s virtue book. It turned a lapse into a cost everyone could see. Instead of the flat version, members put a view as a question, or tentatively. That costs nothing if you turn out wrong.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a product team where the newest engineer spots a flaw in the lead\u2019s design. She asks how the design handles that case. The lead works it through aloud and finds the gap himself. Nobody had to lose.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Soft framing can blur a real disagreement until nobody knows a claim is false. **Some errors** need saying plainly and fast, like a safety fault. And the rule assumes good faith from everyone in the room.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Make **changing your mind** cheap, and people will do it more often. That\u2019s what the Junto\u2019s fine bought. Keep **plain speech** for the errors that can\u2019t wait.'}]},'franklin-autobiography_7':{title:'The Borrowed Book',simple:{headline:'Ask for a small favor, and people like you more',body:'A new member of the Pennsylvania Assembly made a long speech against Benjamin Franklin. Instead of flattering his critic, he asked to borrow a rare book the man owned. It came at once, and he sent it back a week later with a note of thanks. They stayed friends for life. **Someone who has done you a favour is readier to help you again.** Having helped you, they have a reason to like you. With a cool colleague, ask their advice on one real problem. Keep it small and genuine, and use what they tell you.',doToday:'Send a short message asking for advice on one real problem to someone who\u2019s been cool towards you.'},card:{insight:'People often like you more once they help you with a small thing.',tryThis:'Today, ask someone who seems cold to you for help with one small thing.',skills:['relationships','psychology']},review:{prompt:'A coworker has been cold to you for weeks. What do you try?',right:'Ask for her help with one small, real thing',wrong:'Do her a big favor so she owes you one',why:'When people help you, they find a reason to like you.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A new kid in your class doesn\u2019t seem to like you. You could give them a sweet, or **ask to borrow** a pencil. Which one do you think would **warm them up**?'},{kind:'story',text:'A man who helped make the laws spoke out against Franklin. Franklin heard he owned a rare book. So he wrote and asked to borrow it. The man lent it, and after that they became friends.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When you help someone, part of you asks why. The easy answer is *I must like them*. So **a small favour** asked can **make a friend**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A colleague has been **cool towards you** for months. You could bring coffee, offer to cover a shift, be extra pleasant. Franklin would do **the opposite**. What would he try instead?'},{kind:'story',text:'In the Pennsylvania Assembly, a new member made a long speech against Franklin. Franklin didn\u2019t court him. He asked to borrow a scarce book from his library. It came at once. Franklin returned it about a week later, with a note of thanks.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The man was civil from then on, and they stayed friends **until his death**. Franklin credits an old maxim: people who\u2019ve helped you once will help again sooner than people **you\u2019ve helped**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Why not do him a favour? Because **debt isn\u2019t warmth**. A favour you give creates an obligation, and people resent creditors. A favour they give has to be explained, and the easiest explanation is **liking you**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'To turn someone round, **ask, don\u2019t offer**. Make it a small, genuine favour, like their advice. **Their own generosity** changes how they see you.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Franklin was clerk of the Pennsylvania Assembly when **a new member** spoke against his reappointment. The man was wealthy and educated. Franklin chose not to court him. So how did they end up **friends for life**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Franklin heard the man owned a rare book. He wrote asking to borrow it. The book came at once. Franklin sent it back about a week later, with a note of thanks. From then on, the man was friendly.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Why does receiving beat giving? A favour you give runs an obligation **towards you**, and debt can breed resentment. A favour they give needs explaining. Nobody imposes the answer. They supply it themselves: **they must like you**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a department head who\u2019s blocked your project twice. You don\u2019t argue again. You ask for her advice on one specific risk, and you use it. Having helped shape the plan, she now has reason to see it succeed.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. This shapes feelings without the other person knowing, which can slide into **manipulation**. It stays honest only if the request is real. Asked too often, or for something big, a favour reads as using someone.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A favour received can turn an opponent more reliably than **a favour given**. Keep it small and genuine, and **return the goodwill**. Franklin asked once, and gave the book back with thanks.'}]},'franklin-autobiography_8':{title:'Errata, Not Excuses',simple:{headline:'Say sorry with no excuses, then fix it',body:'Benjamin Franklin was a printer. A printer who sets a wrong letter lists it as an erratum. That\u2019s a short note saying what was wrong and what\u2019s right. He used the same word for the worst mistakes of his youth. Suppose you missed a deadline that cost a colleague their weekend. You could explain the changing brief and the pressure you were under. **Adding reasons turns an apology into a defence, and the matter stays open.** Say what you did and what it cost them, then put it right.',doToday:'Write a one-sentence apology for a recent mistake, with no reasons or excuses in it.'},card:{insight:'When you make a mistake, say it plainly, then fix what you can.',tryThis:'Tonight, write one mistake in a plain line. Then write how you will make it right.',skills:['psychology','communication']},review:{prompt:'You were late to pick up a friend. What do you say?',right:'Sorry I was late. Can I buy you lunch to make it right?',wrong:'Sorry, the traffic was really bad and my phone died',why:'Reasons make a sorry sound like an excuse, but a fix ends it.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You break your friend\u2019s mug. You say, *Sorry, but it was slippery, and you left it on the edge.* Your friend **frowns**. Why did your sorry **make it worse**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Franklin was a printer. When a printer sets a wrong letter, he marks it in a list called *errata*. When Franklin wrote his life story, he used that word for **his worst mistakes**. He named each one plainly.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Own up in **one plain sentence**. Don\u2019t add a long list of reasons. A plain sorry lets the matter **close** for good.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Years ago you let a friend down badly. When it comes back at night, you replay **the context**: the pressure, the bad advice, what they did first. All of it may be true. So why does the memory **keep returning**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Franklin, a printer by trade, logged the serious faults of his youth as errata. An erratum is a printer\u2019s correction notice: this was set wrong, here\u2019s the right text. It carries no plea.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Adding the circumstances turns **an admission** into a defence. A defence invites a verdict, and **the verdict** never quite arrives. So the trial keeps reopening, and the memory keeps coming back.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Here\u2019s the same **missed deadline**, written up as **a defence** and as **an erratum**.',left:{title:'A defence',items:['I missed it, but the brief changed','And nobody flagged the risk']},right:{title:'An erratum',items:['I missed the deadline','It cost you your weekend']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Record a mistake the way **a printer** would: what was wrong, and what\u2019s right. Leave out the circumstances. That\u2019s what lets **an old error** finally close.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Franklin opens his memoir with a printer\u2019s wish. He wouldn\u2019t mind living his life again, he says. His one request is **a second edition**, with the first one\u2019s faults corrected. What would **your corrections** be?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Through the book, his worst faults are logged as **errata**. An erratum states the error and the correction. It asks nobody, **including you**, to rule on whether you were a good person at the time.'},{kind:'story',text:'Among the faults he names: quitting his brother\u2019s printing house before his term was up. Neglecting the young woman he\u2019d courted while he was in London. Spending money he\u2019d been trusted to collect for a man named Vernon.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a company that finds it has charged customers twice. One statement explains the system migration and the vendor\u2019s error. Another says: we charged you twice, refunds go out today. Only the second closes the matter.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Franklin isn\u2019t always as bare as his method: of his brother he also records the beatings. And an erratum includes **the right text**. He later married Deborah Read and repaid Vernon. A confession with no correction isn\u2019t an erratum.'},{kind:'moral',text:'State what was wrong, then **supply the correction** where one is owed. Leave the plea out. That\u2019s how an old mistake stops **reopening the trial**.'}]},'franklin-autobiography_9':{title:'Be the Route',simple:{headline:'Share what you know, and people will come to you',body:'Benjamin Franklin designed a stove that heated rooms better on less fuel. The governor offered him a patent, so only he could sell it. He said no. He had gained from other people\u2019s inventions, so he let his own spread freely. He also helped start a lending library and ran the post office, so books and letters passed through him. **Being the person people come to is worth more than owning one thing.** Say you\u2019ve built a tool that saves your team hours. Teach it to other teams, and you become that person.',doToday:'Write a short how-to for one useful trick you know, and send it to a colleague who could use it.'},card:{insight:'When you share a good idea, people start to come to you for help.',tryThis:'At lunch, show one coworker a quick trick that saves you time.',skills:['career','leadership']},review:{prompt:'You learned a quick trick that saves you an hour a week. What now?',right:'Show it to a coworker so they can use it too',wrong:'Keep it to yourself so you are hard to replace',why:'When you share what you know, people start to come to you.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Think of **a bridge** over a river. It doesn\u2019t own a single cart that crosses it. Yet everyone knows where it is, and **everyone needs it**. What makes a bridge so important?'},{kind:'story',text:'Franklin made a better stove. He was offered a patent, a right to be the only one who could sell it. He said no. He also helped start a library and ran the post office, so books and letters passed through him.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Owning one good thing helps **only you**. If you share what you know, people **come to you** for help. And that keeps growing.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You built the reporting process your team relies on, and **nobody else** can run it. That makes you hard to replace. But is it really **the strongest position** you could hold?'},{kind:'story',text:'Franklin designed an iron stove that warmed rooms better on less fuel. The governor offered him a patent for its sole sale. He declined. He\u2019d gained from other people\u2019s inventions, he said, so his own should circulate freely.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Look at where he put himself instead. He helped found **a lending library**, improved the post as postmaster, and set up printers in other towns as partners. Each time he was **the route** things moved through.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Your reporting process can work as **a possession** or as **a route**.',left:{title:'Keep it',items:['You keep a day a week','Earns from one thing']},right:{title:'Teach four teams',items:['You\u2019re the person they consult','Grows with every use']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Owning an object earns from **one thing**. Being the route earns from **everything that passes**. So teach the process you\u2019ve kept to yourself.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'The governor of Pennsylvania offered Franklin **a patent** on his new stove: the sole right to sell it. Franklin turned it down in a few lines. Would you call that **pure generosity**, or something shrewder?'},{kind:'explain',text:'His stated reason was fairness: he\u2019d gained from others\u2019 inventions, so he\u2019d gladly let his own serve others. But the memoir shows, without saying so, what **that kind of choice** built. Ownership earns from one thing. **Position** earns from all that passes.'},{kind:'story',text:'The library gathered the town\u2019s readers into one subscription. The post office, he notes, improved his newspaper\u2019s news and reach. His printing partnerships set former journeymen up in other colonies, sharing profits with him. Each grew as it was used.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take two engineers who each build a useful process. One keeps hers private and stays hard to replace for a day a week. The other teaches his to four teams. He becomes the person they call whenever that problem appears.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. This is a reading of the record, not his motive. Declining didn\u2019t protect the idea either: a London ironmonger patented a version and made **a small fortune**. And being the route depended on posts he was appointed to.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You can own one valuable thing, or be **the point** things move through. The second compounds. Just remember that not everyone gets **to choose** the position.'}]}}}},960,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'gospel-of-wealth',kind:'book',promise:'Give your money where it does the most good',about:'Should rich people be ashamed to die rich? Andrew Carnegie thought so. He got rich making steel, then gave away most of his money. Here are his rules for giving, and where they fall short. Which ones do you agree with?',ideas:{'gospel-of-wealth_1':{title:'The Disgrace of Dying Rich',card:{insight:'A big gift can still be a tiny part of what someone has.',tryThis:'When someone gives a big gift, ask what part of their money it was.',skills:['money','thinking']},review:{prompt:'A rich man gives a big speech about giving back. What do you check?',right:'What share of his own money he has given away',wrong:'The total of the big gifts he has made so far',why:'The share he gave shows if he means what he says.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two friends promise to help the school fair. One has **lots of money** and gives a little. The other has a little and gives **most of it**. Who really meant it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Andrew Carnegie was one of the **richest men** who ever lived. He wrote that dying rich is **a disgrace**. Then he did something rare. He gave away about nine of every ten dollars he had. Could you do that?'},{kind:'moral',text:'Anyone can say **you should give**. Look at what share they gave themselves. It\u2019s **the share**, not the speech, that shows what someone believes.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A wealthy guest gives a speech about **giving back**. The room applauds. On the drive home, someone asks you a plain question. How much has the speaker **actually given**? Nobody in the car knows.'},{kind:'story',text:'In 1889 Andrew Carnegie, a steel magnate, published an essay with a blunt thesis. Dying rich, he argued, is a disgrace. He meant it as a duty, not a mood. Then, before he died, he gave away roughly ninety per cent of his fortune.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Plenty of people say the rich should give. Fewer do it. That\u2019s why **the share** matters more than the total. A big gift from a vast fortune can still be **a small fraction**. Carnegie\u2019s share was most of it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When someone argues for giving, **check the share** they gave. It tells you whether **they meant it**. Words are free, and proportions aren\u2019t.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture a steel magnate in 1889, one of the richest men alive. He sits down to write about **his money**. You\u2019d expect a defence of keeping it. What do you think **he argued** instead?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Carnegie\u2019s reasoning is simple. A fortune still undistributed at death is **a failed duty**. The job is to give it away **while you\u2019re alive**, and to do it with care. Leaving it to heirs or a will dodges the hard part.'},{kind:'story',text:'Carnegie didn\u2019t just argue it. Before he died he gave away roughly ninety per cent of his fortune. The money went to libraries, universities and research institutions. That\u2019s why the essay reads as a programme, not a pose.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture two donors at a charity dinner. One gives a gift that makes the papers, but it\u2019s a sliver of his fortune. The other quietly gives half of what she has. You know which one meant it.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Proportion tests **sincerity**, not merit. Giving away nearly everything says nothing about how the money was made. A man can give away ninety per cent of a fortune he should never have kept.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The share you give shows **what you believe**. It doesn\u2019t show whether the fortune was **fairly made**. Ask both questions, and keep them apart.'}]},'gospel-of-wealth_2':{title:'Not Yours Alone',card:{insight:'If many people helped you get rich, you owe part of it back.',tryThis:'Tonight, write down who helped you earn your money this year. Then pick one way to give back.',skills:['thinking','money']},review:{prompt:'Your business has a great year. A friend says you did it alone. Agree?',right:'Partly, since my workers helped me too',wrong:'Yes, since the hard work and the risk were mine',why:'Many people help you earn money, so part of it is owed back.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your class is saving for a trip, and you\u2019re picked to **mind the money**. It sits in a tin in your room. You could buy a game with it. But is it **really yours**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Carnegie said a rich person is like that money-minder, a **trustee**. He got rich from steel, but **not alone**. Thousands of workers made it. Railways carried it. Towns bought it. Laws kept his business safe.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Big fortunes are built by **many people** working together. So, Carnegie said, the money should **go back** to help them. It isn\u2019t just one person\u2019s to keep.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You land a great job and everyone congratulates you. You worked hard for it. But who built the school that taught you, and **the roads** you drove in on? How much was **really you**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Carnegie made his fortune in steel. But thousands of workers made the steel, railways moved it, markets bought it and laws protected his business. In his essay he drew the conclusion. The rich man is a trustee for the community.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The frame does two jobs at once. It **defends getting very rich**, since a trustee should be capable. It also **rejects inheritance**, since a trust isn\u2019t family property. The whole essay lives in the gap between those two.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your success arrived through **a system**, not just through you. Carnegie\u2019s answer was that part of it is **owed back**. You don\u2019t have to agree to find the question useful.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Today\u2019s giant foundations rarely explain why a rich founder should decide where the money goes. It\u2019s simply assumed. In 1889 Carnegie **wrote the assumption down**. Can you spot it before **you accept it**?'},{kind:'story',text:'The essay opens by defending its own age. Competition, Carnegie grants, is hard on individuals but good for society. It gathers wealth into a few hands. Having accepted that, he had to say what the few owe. Trusteeship was his answer.'},{kind:'explain',text:'A trustee holds **surplus wealth** for the community that made it possible. The duty ends only when it\u2019s **been given back**. And the person who gathered the money is the one who manages it. That last step does the heavy lifting.'},{kind:'story',text:'Think of a pension fund. Its trustees are appointed, they answer to members, and they can be removed if they fail. Your savings sit with people who are checked. That\u2019s what the word trustee usually means.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Carnegie\u2019s trustee **appoints himself**, picks the beneficiaries and judges his own work. Nobody can remove him. The word brings the weight of a legal duty without any of its checks.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Calling wealth **a trust** is a real moral claim. But a trust needs **someone to answer to**. When you hear the word, ask who checks the trustee.'}]},'gospel-of-wealth_3':{title:'The Coin and the Library',card:{insight:'Some gifts help for a day, and some build something that lasts for years.',tryThis:'Write down your last gift. Then mark if it helped for a day or for years.',skills:['money','thinking']},review:{prompt:'You want to help a town where many people lost jobs. What do you ask?',right:'Will this help for a day, or build something for years?',wrong:'Which plan helps the most people by tonight?',why:'Both kinds of gifts are good, but know which one you are giving.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A man on the corner asks you for **a coin**. You give him one, and you feel kind. Tomorrow he\u2019s back on the same corner. Did your coin **change anything**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Carnegie thought most giving in his day was **wasted**. A coin gets spent, and the problem stays. So he paid for **libraries** instead. A library lets people who are trying **help themselves**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A coin fixes **today**, and a library can change **tomorrow**. But what if someone\u2019s too hungry to read? Carnegie\u2019s plan didn\u2019t really answer that.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You drop a fiver into a collection tin on the high street. It takes two seconds and you **feel good** all morning. Do you know **what it did**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Carnegie was blunt about almsgiving. He reckoned that ninety-five per cent of the charity of his day was unwise. He\u2019d rather, he said, see a fortune thrown into the sea than spent in ways that encourage idleness.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'His sharpest point is about **the giver**, and what each kind of gift **actually buys**.',left:{title:'Alms',items:['Relieves the symptom','Buys the feeling of helping','Never checked']},right:{title:'Institutions',items:['Builds a capability','Buys an effect','Libraries, parks, halls, universities']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Before you give, ask whether you\u2019re **relieving a symptom** or **building a capability**. Both can be right. Mixing them up is how good money gets wasted.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A charity gala raises a fortune in one night. Everyone leaves glowing. A year later, nobody in the room could tell you **what changed**. Was the money spent on the problem, or on **the feeling**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Carnegie\u2019s claim is that **indiscriminate giving** relieves the symptom and keeps the condition alive. Worse, it lets the giver buy **the feeling** of helping, cheaply and at once. Whether anything changed is never checked.'},{kind:'story',text:'So he funded institutions that make self-improvement possible: libraries, parks, halls and universities. They were for people already trying to help themselves. He thought nearly all the charity of his day was unwise, and he said so plainly.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture two donors helping the same town. One hands out food parcels every winter. The other pays for an evening class in bookkeeping. You can see that each fixes **a different problem**, and neither replaces the other.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The scheme assumes the problem is **missing opportunity**. For some it is. For others it\u2019s illness, hunger or a family to care for. And the donor decides which case is which.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Relief and capability are **different jobs**. Carnegie was right that you shouldn\u2019t confuse them. But he never asked **who should judge** which one a person needs.'}]},'gospel-of-wealth_4':{title:'Big Gifts or Fair Pay',card:{insight:'A big gift later is not always better than fair pay now.',tryThis:'Next time a store gives a big gift, look up what it pays its staff.',skills:['thinking','money']},review:{prompt:'A company cuts pay, then pays for a big new museum. What do you ask?',right:'What would fair pay for the workers have done?',wrong:'How many people will come to see the new museum?',why:'You can\u2019t judge a gift without asking what fair pay would have done.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A girl runs a bake sale. Her helpers work all day and get **one biscuit** each. She keeps the rest. Then she gives **a big gift** to the school, and everyone claps. What would you ask?'},{kind:'story',text:'Carnegie\u2019s money came from steelworkers on twelve-hour shifts. In 1892, at his Homestead steelworks, their pay was cut and they went on strike. Guards were hired, a gunfight broke out, and people on both sides died.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Carnegie was sure **big gifts** did more good than **higher pay**. He never tested that on his own workers. Before you clap for a gift, ask what fair pay would\u2019ve done.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A firm squeezes its staff for years, then opens **a gleaming library** with its name over the door. You\u2019re invited to the opening. Everyone praises the gift. Is anyone asking about **the payslips**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Three years after the essay, a wage dispute at Carnegie\u2019s Homestead steelworks became a strike. Pinkerton agents, private guards, were brought in to retake the works. They fought the strikers, and men died. Carnegie was in Scotland.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The essay says wealth should serve **the community**. It also ranks a gift above paying the same money as wages. But it **never tests** that ranking against his own mills, with their twelve-hour shifts.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When you hear a surplus justified by **later giving**, ask the plain question. What would **paying more** at the time have done, and for whom? It\u2019s rarely asked.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'In 1889 Carnegie published an essay saying wealth should be **administered for the community**. Three years later, at his Homestead steelworks, men died in a fight over pay. How do you hold **both facts** at once?'},{kind:'story',text:'In 1892 a pay row at Homestead turned into a strike. Pinkerton agents were sent in to take the works back. In the battle with steelworkers, men on both sides died. Carnegie was in Scotland, and his manager Henry Clay Frick ran things.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The easy reading is **hypocrisy**. A sharper one says his doctrine **requires it**. If the rich manage wealth best, they should gather as much as they can. Any lawful means will do. The libraries were paid for by the wage bill.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a caf\xe9 owner who pays the legal minimum and gives generously to local causes. Her name is on the youth club. Her staff can\u2019t afford the rent nearby. You\u2019d praise one half and question the other.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**, in his favour. Carnegie argued that **large sums** on public institutions beat the same money scattered in small amounts, even as wages. And higher wages alone wouldn\u2019t have built libraries.'},{kind:'moral',text:'His reply has force, but he **never tested it** against his own payroll. A gift can\u2019t be judged alone. You also need to ask what **fair pay** would have done.'}]},'gospel-of-wealth_5':{title:'Read Their Best Case',card:{insight:'Read the best case for a view you dislike to find the part you reject.',tryThis:'Pick a view you think is wrong. Find the best case for it, and mark the one reason you reject.',skills:['thinking','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'You want to know why people hold a view you dislike. Where do you start?',right:'The best case for it, by someone who holds it',wrong:'A short summary by someone on your own side',why:'Only the best case shows you just where you disagree.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your class is holding a debate. You\u2019re told to argue **for school uniforms**, and you hate them. So you hunt for the other side\u2019s **best reasons**. What happens to your view?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Carnegie says plainly what he thinks. A few people getting very rich is fine, and **the rich know best** how to spend for others. You may think that\u2019s wrong. But he\u2019s **so clear** you can point to the spot.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When you disagree, read **the best version** of the idea. Then you can find **the exact spot** where you part ways. That\u2019s far better than a vague feeling.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Someone on your side sums up a view you dislike in one sneering line. You nod along. Later you meet a person who holds it, and **you can\u2019t answer** them. What went wrong with **your reading**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Carnegie\u2019s essay states its premises in the open. Concentrated wealth is inevitable and good. The rich administer surplus better than the state or the recipients. And the duty is done by giving it away during life.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Each premise is **contestable**, and the second is **the load-bearing one**. Better than the recipients, he says, because small sums spread thin do little lasting good. That\u2019s asserted, not shown, by the man who gains from the answer.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Read the essay as **an argument**, not a testimonial about a generous man. Its **clearest statement** lets you name the premise you reject. A vague unease can\u2019t do that.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Most writing about giving hides its assumptions behind **good works**. You admire the hospital wing and never see the premise. Carnegie\u2019s essay is different. Why would its **openness** be the most useful thing about it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Read as testimonial, the essay is **an advertisement**. The assumptions vanish into the life of a man who did give most of his money away. Read as argument, it\u2019s **unusually clear**. That\u2019s what makes disagreement possible.'},{kind:'story',text:'Strip away the biography and three claims remain. Fortunes will pool in a few hands, and should. A rich man spends spare money better than a government or the people it\u2019s for. Giving it away while alive pays the debt.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a student who rejects free markets. She reads only critics of them for a year. Then she reads one defender at full strength, and finds the **single premise** she really disputes. Her argument gets sharper overnight.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Reading a view at its strongest **isn\u2019t neutral**. A clean restatement can lend an argument dignity its results didn\u2019t earn. So read it beside its record: Homestead, the wage bill, the people missed.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Generous reading is **a method**, not a verdict. Read the strongest version, then read **the record** too. You\u2019ll know exactly what you reject, and why.'}]},'gospel-of-wealth_6':{title:'Three Ways to Give',card:{insight:'A gift in your will costs you nothing, since you can\u2019t keep the money.',tryThis:'Pick one gift you plan to give someday. Ask what waiting gains, then give part of it now.',skills:['money','thinking']},review:{prompt:'You plan to leave your savings to a cause in your will. What would Carnegie ask?',right:'What do I gain by waiting to give it?',wrong:'Is my will written clearly enough to work?',why:'Giving while you live costs you something, and you can see it used well.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve got a bag of sweets at break time. You could share them **right now**. Or you could promise them to friends **when you leave** school. Which one actually costs you something?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Carnegie saw **three ways** to pass on a fortune. Leave it to your family, and it spoils them. Leave it to good causes **when you die**, and it costs you nothing. You can\u2019t take money with you anyway.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Only the third way counts, Carnegie said: **giving it away** yourself while you\u2019re alive. That\u2019s the one that takes **real choices**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A retired business owner has a fortune and **three options**. Leave it to the children. Leave it to a hospital in her will. Or spend ten years **giving it away** herself. Which would you choose?'},{kind:'story',text:'Carnegie set out the same three ways. Leaving it to family, he said, harms the heirs. Leaving it to public causes at death is evasion. Given the choice, he added, those givers would have carried it off with them.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Only the third way involves **judgement** or **any risk** to the giver. The person who gathered the fortune is best placed to use it, he argues. A will names a destination. It can\u2019t run anything.'},{kind:'moral',text:'**Giving while living** is the version that tests you. Before you put off a gift, ask what **waiting** actually gains. Then ask who the delay serves.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A rich man leaves millions to a university in his will. The papers call it **generous**. Carnegie would have shrugged. Why would the timing of a gift matter more to him than **its size**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Carnegie names **three ways** to dispose of a fortune. Leave it to family, leave it for public purposes at death, or administer it yourself while alive. Only the third, he argues, needs **any judgement** from the giver.'},{kind:'story',text:'He saved his sharpest words for the bequest. A will, he argued, gives away only what the dead can no longer keep. Nobody gives up anything. So the papers may praise it, but it earns no credit.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a woman who means to give her old piano to a school, someday. Years pass. The school closes its music room. Her good intention expired while she waited for **the right moment**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Making a fortune and **spending one well** are different skills. Lifetime giving can be hasty, or bent to the giver\u2019s tastes. And Carnegie didn\u2019t finish the job himself: he placed much of what remained in a foundation.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The argument is consistent, and it **flatters its author**. It names the rich man the best person for a job he gave himself. Still, ask what **your waiting** gains.'}]},'gospel-of-wealth_7':{title:'Ladders, Not Handouts',card:{insight:'A free gift only helps people with the time and strength to use it.',tryThis:'Next time you help, ask who has no time or strength to use it.',skills:['leadership','thinking']},review:{prompt:'Your town opens a free night class. Who might it miss?',right:'People with no spare time or strength to come',wrong:'No one, since the class is free for everyone',why:'A free class only helps people who have the time and strength to come.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'When Carnegie was a working boy near Pittsburgh, he **couldn\u2019t afford books**. A local man, Colonel James Anderson, let working boys borrow from **his own library** on Saturdays. What would you do with that?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Carnegie grew rich and built **free libraries**, more than two and a half thousand. He saw them as **ladders**: things that let you climb by your own effort. Towns had to give the land and pay for upkeep.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A climbing wall is great if you\u2019re **strong and rested**. A tired kid can\u2019t use it. Ladders help people **with spare energy**, and Carnegie thought that was fine.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A town opens a free evening class in bookkeeping. It fills with people who already had **some schooling** and free evenings. The night-shift workers it was meant for **never come**. Nobody turned them away. So what happened?'},{kind:'story',text:'Carnegie\u2019s rule was to give ladders, not alms. Help those who will help themselves, he said. His library grants made the town supply the site and pay for upkeep. You couldn\u2019t just receive the gift. You had to climb a rung first.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The libraries worked, and many still stand. But the model assumes a reader like **the young Carnegie**: literate, ambitious, with evenings free. Climbing takes **spare time and energy**. Plenty of steelworkers on twelve-hour days had neither.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A ladder is a real gift, but it **excludes by design**. Before you build one, ask who **can\u2019t climb** it. Then decide if that\u2019s acceptable.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'As a telegraph messenger, Carnegie borrowed books from **Colonel James Anderson**, who lent them to working boys. Decades later he paid for **thousands of libraries**. Can you see the boy inside the policy?'},{kind:'story',text:'The library programme was that Saturday experience turned into policy. More than two and a half thousand were built. Each town had to find the site and fund the running costs. The conditions were the point, not paperwork.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The rule behind it is **ladders, not alms**. Give the willing **the means to rise**, and help only those who\u2019ll help themselves. A gift you must climb can\u2019t be passively received. That, he thought, filters out idleness.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now think of a carer looking after a sick parent. The new library down the road is free and open until eight. She\u2019d love to go. Her obstacle was never **the missing ladder**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The principle offers nothing to the exhausted, the sick or the carer. Carnegie treats that as **a feature**. Yet his own mills set the twelve-hour days that kept many workers off the ladder.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Ladders are among the best gifts **you can give**. Just remember who they\u2019re built for. Ask whose obstacle **isn\u2019t a ladder** at all.'}]},'gospel-of-wealth_8':{title:'A Tax Against Himself',card:{insight:'When people back a rule that costs them, they tend to mean it.',tryThis:'Next time someone argues for a rule, ask if it would cost them. If so, believe they mean it.',skills:['thinking','money']},review:{prompt:'A very rich woman asks for higher taxes on the rich. What does it show?',right:'A rule that would cost her shows she means it',wrong:'She must have a secret way to dodge the tax',why:'Asking for a rule that costs you is a strong sign you mean it.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Imagine the richest kid in school asking for **a new rule**. Whoever has the most sweets at home time hands **half of them** in. Why would anyone ask for that?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Carnegie did something like that. He said when a rich person dies **still rich**, the country should take a big share. The bigger the fortune, **the bigger the share**. It would push the rich to give while alive. Would you vote for that?'},{kind:'moral',text:'He was asking for a rule that **would cost him**. That\u2019s a strong sign he meant it. Talk is easy when it **costs you nothing**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019d expect the richest man in the world to hate death duties. Yet one paragraph of this essay **calls for them**, heavy and graduated. Why would a steel magnate want the state to **take his hoard**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Carnegie welcomed the growing willingness to tax large estates at death. He wanted the duties light on modest sums left to dependants, and severe on the largest fortunes. Of all taxes, he judged this one the wisest.'},{kind:'explain',text:'He gave **two reasons**. One is practical: a heavy tax at death makes giving during life the better option. The other is moral. By taking its share, the state **publicly condemns** a life spent hoarding.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Carnegie argued for **a rule** that would cost him and his kind. People rarely do that without **real conviction**. So when you see it, count it as evidence.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'The essay is often praised by people who **admire the giving**. Many of them would reject another part of it. Carnegie wanted heavy taxes on **large estates**. Can you keep one half and drop the other?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The two halves are **one argument**. If a fortune is held in trust, dying with it is **a default**. The state reclaiming it is the remedy, not an intrusion. Accept the trust, and you\u2019ve accepted the tax.'},{kind:'story',text:'The scale he wanted rose with the estate. A small sum left to a widow or child would barely be touched. The largest would lose a heavy share. The state\u2019s cut was a public verdict on a hoarder\u2019s life.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a head teacher who proposes that senior staff, herself included, take the first pay freeze. She could have exempted herself. She didn\u2019t. When she talks about fairness, the staff room listens differently.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. A tax meant partly to condemn a way of life is **a moral sentence** passed through the revenue. And if the rich manage wealth best, the state seems a poor heir to the job.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A rule that **costs its author** is strong evidence of belief. It isn\u2019t proof the rule is good. Judge **the argument** on its own terms too.'}]},'gospel-of-wealth_9':{title:'Leaving Kids Too Much',card:{insight:'Help that does all the work for someone can keep them from growing.',tryThis:'Next time you help someone, ask what work it takes away. Leave them some of it to do.',skills:['relationships','money']},review:{prompt:'You could pay off your grown son\u2019s debts tomorrow. What do you ask first?',right:'What work it takes away, and if that work helps him',wrong:'How much stress it would take off his mind',why:'Help can take away the very work that makes a person strong.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Imagine a parent who did all your homework for you, every night, for years. Your marks would look **amazing**. But what would you **never learn** to do?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Carnegie thought leaving a **huge fortune** to your children can harm them. If they never need to work, they may never find out **what they can do**. The help takes away the effort that makes them better.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Carnegie didn\u2019t say leave your family nothing. He thought a **modest amount** was fair. It was **huge fortunes** he was against, because of what they do to the person who gets one.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A friend inherits enough money at twenty-two **never to work** again. Ten years later, you meet up. He\u2019s comfortable, and a little lost. What did **the gift** take from him?'},{kind:'story',text:'Carnegie objected to great inheritances because of what they do to the heir. A fortune, he argued, usually dulls the heir\u2019s talents and drive. Without the need to work, the heir tends to drift into a smaller life.'},{kind:'explain',text:'He wasn\u2019t against all inheritance. He allowed **modest provision**, especially for wives and daughters, since women then had few ways to earn. The line falls between **a cushion** and a replacement for a working life.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Before you hand someone an advantage, ask what **effort it removes**. Some effort is just a burden. Some of it is **doing the work** of making them who they are.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture a new rowing boat, hung in a shed so it never gets wet. It stays **perfect** for years. It never goes anywhere either. Is that what **you want** for the people you love?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Carnegie\u2019s claim is that **great wealth** is a misfortune to the heir. Left to a child, a fortune tends to blunt ability and ambition. The heir ends up doing **less with his life** than he\u2019d have done without it.'},{kind:'story',text:'When the essay appeared in 1889, Carnegie had been married two years and had no children. His only child, a daughter, was born in 1897. He made the case in the abstract. It met his own family later.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now think of a parent who pays a student\u2019s rent but not her spending money. She takes a weekend job, and learns to budget. The cushion kept her safe. It didn\u2019t replace **her own effort**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. His evidence is anecdotal and his conviction total. The effect depends on **the amount**, on what the heir expects, and on what else is handed down. It\u2019s also convenient for a doctrine that needs fortunes elsewhere.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Still, this part of his doctrine **bound his own family**. That\u2019s a fair test of whether an argument is held or just used. Ask what **your advantages** remove.'}]}}}},961,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'how-couples-actually-argue',kind:'book',promise:'Fight with your partner and still stay close',about:'Why do some couples fight and still stay close? For years, scientists watched couples argue. Most of what they found is about how a fight starts and how it ends. One famous number from this work was much too high. Which part sounds like you?',ideas:{'how-couples-actually-argue_1':{title:'The First Sentence',card:{insight:'How a fight starts says a lot about how it will end.',tryThis:'Next time you are mad, wait until you are calm. Then say what they did and how you felt.',skills:['relationships','communication']},review:{prompt:'You are mad that the kitchen is a mess again. What should you do?',right:'Wait until you are calm, then say what happened and how you felt',wrong:'Bring it up now, while the mess is right there',why:'Your first words shape the fight, and they come out sharp when you are very mad.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You get home and the chocolate you were saving is **gone**. Your brother ate it. You march off to find him. What\u2019s **the first thing** you say?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Saying he\u2019s greedy is about **who he is**. He\u2019ll argue back to defend himself. Saying *you ate it, and I\u2019m upset* is about **what he did**. Now he can say sorry. Scientists who watched couples argue saw the difference almost at once.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The **first thing you say** in a row shapes the rest of it. Say what happened and how you feel. And if you\u2019re too cross, **wait a bit** first.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Tom books the weekend away **without asking** Maya. She\u2019s annoyed, and she opens with: *you never think about anyone but yourself.* How do you think **the next ten minutes** go?'},{kind:'story',text:'Tom hears an attack on his character, so he defends it. He lists every time he did think of her. Maya pushes back harder. Twenty minutes later they\u2019re arguing about who\u2019s selfish. Nobody has mentioned the weekend.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'In studies of couples, these two openings **split apart** almost at once. Only one gets **an answer**.',left:{title:'Names a flaw',items:['You never think of me','Invites a defence','Escalation follows']},right:{title:'Names a behaviour',items:['You booked without asking','I felt left out','Invites a reply']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Why not just phrase it better? Because the first sentence comes out at **peak irritation**, and the phrasing follows **your state**, not your plan. So the reliable fix is timing. Wait until you\u2019re calmer, then start.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your **first sentence** does more work than the next twenty. Name the behaviour and how you feel. If you\u2019re too cross to do that, **wait**, then start.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Researchers have spent decades watching couples argue. You might expect **the topic** to matter most: money, chores, in-laws. Yet one moment kept forecasting how a row went. Which **moment** was it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'It was **the opening**. Much of this work came from **John Gottman\u2019s lab**. There, the first minutes of a row said a good deal about its end. And how such rows go is linked to how **the relationship** fares.'},{kind:'story',text:'Maya opens with *you never think about anyone but yourself.* Tom hears a verdict on his character and defends it. She pushes harder. The trajectory was set in a sentence, long before either of them reached the weekend he\u2019d booked.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Sam, who comes home to find the bins missed again. He\u2019s furious, and he knows a soft opening is better. He tries to use one, and it still comes out sharp. **Good intentions** don\u2019t survive **peak irritation**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The phrasing follows **your state**, not your intention. So the dependable lever is **delay**. Raise it once the irritation has passed, and the gentler first sentence arrives almost on its own.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The opening forecasts a good deal, not everything, so this is **association, not destiny**. Much of the evidence comes from one research group. And delay can quietly turn into never raising it at all.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You can\u2019t control how **the row** ends, but you control **its first sentence**. Spend your effort there. If you can\u2019t say it gently yet, wait until you can.'}]},'how-couples-actually-argue_2':{title:'Talking Down',card:{insight:'A complaint can be answered, but an eye roll or mean joke cannot.',tryThis:'Next time you argue, listen to your own voice. Stop if you hear a sigh or a mean joke.',skills:['relationships','communication']},review:{prompt:'In a fight about money, which line talks down?',right:'Wow, let me explain this very slowly for you',wrong:'You spent more than we agreed on this month',why:'You can answer a complaint, but not someone who talks down to you.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your sister left her shoes on the stairs **again**. You could say so. Or you could roll your eyes and say *wow, so clever.* Which one does **more damage**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Saying *you left your shoes there* is **a complaint**. She can answer it: move them, or say sorry. Rolling your eyes says you think you\u2019re **better than her**. There\u2019s nothing to answer, so she can only give in or fight back.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Being cross is normal, and so is **complaining**. Making fun of someone, or talking down to them, is **a different thing**. Listen for it in your own voice.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two couples argue about money every month. Both get **angry**, and both complain. One couple has lasted twenty years. In the other, one partner keeps rolling their eyes. Which couple would **you worry** about?'},{kind:'story',text:'Priya and Dan are arguing about a bill. Dan says she spent more than they agreed. Priya can explain, apologise or push back. Then she sighs and offers to explain it very slowly for him. Dan has nothing he can answer.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Dan\u2019s line was **a complaint**. Priya\u2019s reply was **contempt**, and it works differently.',left:{title:'A complaint',items:['About what you did','Can be answered','Explain, fix or apologise']},right:{title:'Contempt',items:['About who you are','Can\u2019t really be answered','Submit or escalate']}},{kind:'explain',text:'In the research on couples, anger and complaint show up in **stable relationships** all the time. Contempt doesn\u2019t behave like them. Sarcasm, mockery, eye-rolling: it was the behaviour most linked to **poor outcomes**. Often **the tone** carries it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Arguing isn\u2019t the warning sign. **Speaking from above** is. Treat contempt as its own thing, not as strong criticism, and listen for it in **your own tone**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture a list of the worst things couples do in a row: getting angry, complaining, getting **defensive**. Researchers watching couples found all of those in relationships that lasted. So which behaviour **stood apart**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'It was **contempt**: sarcasm, mockery, name-calling, eye-rolling, and the tone for explaining things to someone slow. It signals **superiority**, not a complaint. In this research, it was the behaviour most strongly linked to **relationships ending**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Priya and Dan argue over a bill. He says she overspent. She can explain, fix it or apologise. Instead she offers to explain it very slowly for him. Now the claim is about his **standing**, and he can only submit or escalate.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Jess, who thinks her rows with Sam are fair because she never swears. Then she replays one in her head. The words were polite. The sighs and the slow, patient voice were not.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Teasing that both people enjoy isn\u2019t contempt, since **context and tone** decide. The finding comes mostly from one research group. And calling every complaint contempt is a handy way to dodge it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A complaint is about **what you did**, and it can be answered. Contempt is about **who you are**, and it can\u2019t. Listen for the tone, not the words.'}]},'how-couples-actually-argue_3':{title:'The Fight That Stays',card:{insight:'Many fights never get solved, so find a way to make them hurt less.',tryThis:'Write down one fight you have had more than five times. Then write one way to make it smaller.',skills:['relationships','communication']},review:{prompt:'You and your partner have fought about a messy home for years. What now?',right:'Find a way to make the fight hurt less',wrong:'Keep at it until one of you gives in',why:'These fights come from how two people differ, so make them hurt less.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You share a room with your sister. You like it **tidy** and she likes it **messy**. Every week you have the same row about socks on the floor. Will it **ever end**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Some rows come back forever because you\u2019re **built differently**. That\u2019s normal. So you agree: your half stays tidy, her half can be messy. The socks still annoy you sometimes. But now it\u2019s **a small moan**, not a big fight.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Not every row can be **solved**. When it keeps coming back, don\u2019t try to win it. Find a way to have it **more gently** each time.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Every party ends the same way. One of you wants to leave at ten. The other is just getting going. The drive home is **the same argument** as five years ago. Why hasn\u2019t it **been settled**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Because many arguments in long relationships are **perpetual**. They come from lasting differences in tempo, sociability, tidiness or money. Those aren\u2019t problems with a solution. They\u2019re two people **built differently**, and that\u2019ll still be true next year.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Couples who do well mostly **haven\u2019t resolved** these rows. They\u2019ve found **a cheaper version**.',left:{title:'Aim: settle it',items:['Each row is a battle','Recurring feels like failure','Costs rise each time']},right:{title:'Aim: costs less',items:['Each row is expected','Recurrence is normal','The cost stays low']}},{kind:'story',text:'So the couple stop arguing about when to leave. They take two cars, or the one who\u2019s tired goes home first. The difference between them is still there. The drive-home argument isn\u2019t.'},{kind:'moral',text:'If your row has come back for five years, **a settlement** isn\u2019t the realistic aim. Aim for a version that **costs less**. That\u2019s what couples who last tend to do.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'One partner saves, the other spends. They\u2019ve argued about money for **eight years**. Friends assume the marriage is in trouble, since nothing ever gets **resolved**. Would you agree?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Not necessarily. A large share of conflicts in long relationships are **perpetual**, rooted in lasting differences: tempo, sociability, tidiness, money. Couples who do well are mostly not the ones who resolved them. They found a way to argue **without escalating**.'},{kind:'story',text:'So the saver and the spender stop trying to convert each other. They agree a monthly sum each can spend without asking. The money row still flares now and then. It\u2019s shorter, and it no longer carries the last eight years.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Ana and Ben. She loves a full diary; he needs quiet weekends. They used to fight every Friday. Now they plan one social night, and she goes alone to the rest. He\u2019s no more sociable. The row is just cheaper.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Not every recurring row is perpetual: some are solvable, and calling them permanent is **avoidance**. Rows about harm or control aren\u2019t differences to live alongside. And some gaps, like whether to have children, may leave no path round.'},{kind:'moral',text:'For a lasting difference, the work moves from **solving** to **lowering the cost**. Expect the row to come back. Just make sure it costs you less each time.'}]},'how-couples-actually-argue_4':{title:'The Clumsy Peace Move',card:{insight:'A peace move works if it gets taken, even a clumsy one.',tryThis:'Next time a fight heats up, take the first peace move, even a clumsy one.',skills:['relationships','communication']},review:{prompt:'In a fight, your partner makes a bad joke about the moon. What now?',right:'Laugh at the joke, even though it was not funny',wrong:'Tell them it was not funny and keep going',why:'A peace move works when it is taken, even a clumsy one.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You and a friend are arguing over a board game, and it\u2019s getting **loud**. Then your friend pulls a silly face. It isn\u2019t very funny. Do you **laugh**, or tell them it\u2019s not funny?'},{kind:'explain',text:'That face was **a peace move**. It\u2019s like a wobbly pass in football. A wobbly pass still works if your teammate **catches it**. A perfect pass is wasted if they let it roll by. Peace moves work the same way.'},{kind:'moral',text:'In a row, a joke or a hug can calm things down, even a **clumsy one**. What matters is that the other person **takes it**. So when someone tries, let them.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Twenty minutes into a row about the holiday, your partner says, *shall we just go to the moon instead?* It **isn\u2019t funny**. You feel the urge to say so. What should you **do next**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'In observed couples, the difference is rarely **fewer arguments**. It\u2019s what happens mid-argument: a joke, a hand, a step back, an acknowledgement. These **repair attempts** are often graceless. The moon joke is one.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The observational work inverts **what most assume**. It\u2019s **the catch**, not the throw.',left:{title:'What we assume',items:['Fewer arguments','Well-chosen words','A perfect apology']},right:{title:'What matters more',items:['Repair during the row','Any move to calm it','The other person taking it']}},{kind:'explain',text:'That\u2019s good news, because it **removes the excuse**. You don\u2019t have to be articulate, calm or right. You have to make a move, and take one made at you. Refusing a repair for being clumsy is **itself an escalation**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The moon joke wasn\u2019t funny, but it was **a repair**. Take it. Whether a repair works depends on **how it\u2019s received**, far more than how well it was made.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'You probably assume the goal is **fewer arguments**. So you try to argue less, and judge yourself on every row. But what if the thing that matters most happens **inside the row**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The observed difference is rarely how often couples argue. It\u2019s whether **repair attempts** get made and taken: humour, a touch, a change of subject, an acknowledgement. Their **polish** matters much less than their **reception**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Kate and Joe are deep in a row about the holiday. Joe reaches across and pushes her mug towards her, slopping tea on the table. It\u2019s a graceless move. Kate could point at the spill. She takes the mug instead, and the temperature drops.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a couple where one partner says, mid-row, *okay, I may have been a nightmare.* It\u2019s clumsy and half a joke. The other snaps that it\u2019s not a proper apology. Now there\u2019s a fresh grievance on top of the old one.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. This is a pattern seen in observed conversations, **not a formula**. A joke can also brush off a real hurt, which nobody has to accept. Taking a repair should pause the escalation, not bury the issue.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Stop counting rows and start watching **what happens inside** them. Make a move, even a clumsy one. And when **one comes at you**, take it.'}]},'how-couples-actually-argue_5':{title:'The Famous Number',card:{insight:'A guess checked on the people it came from looks better than it is.',tryThis:'Before you share a post with a big score, ask how it was checked.',skills:['thinking','science']},review:{prompt:'An app says it picks good workers nine times in ten. What do you ask?',right:'Was it tested on people it never saw?',wrong:'How many people have used the app so far?',why:'A score tells you little until it is checked on new people.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your teacher hands out **the test questions** a week early. You learn every answer and score ten out of ten. Does that prove you **know the subject**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Not really. A **new test** would show the truth. Scientists once made rules to guess which couples would split up. Then they mostly checked the rules on **the same couples** they\u2019d built them from. So the score looked amazing.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A guessing rule usually looks better on the cases it was **built from**. The fair test uses **new cases** it\u2019s never seen. The things scientists saw about rows still hold up better than the score.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'For years one claim got repeated everywhere. Watch a couple talk for a short while, and researchers could predict divorce with **over ninety per cent** accuracy. It\u2019s the part of this research **everyone remembers**. Did it hold?'},{kind:'explain',text:'No. The models were largely **fitted and tested** on the same couples. A model built from a group bends to fit its quirks as well as any real pattern. Test it on that group and **the quirks** look like insight.'},{kind:'explain',text:'It\u2019s like painting the rings round darts that have **already landed**. Every throw is a bullseye. Check the model against couples it wasn\u2019t built on, and the accuracy **falls substantially**. Modest samples didn\u2019t help.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'So you should keep **two things** apart that usually get remembered **as one**.',left:{title:'Held up better',items:['Harsh openings escalate','Contempt predicts trouble','Repair matters']},right:{title:'Didn\u2019t hold',items:['The precision','The ninety per cent figure']}},{kind:'moral',text:'The famous figure came from testing models on **their own couples**. The behaviour findings have held up better. Keep them apart, and when you see an accuracy claim, ask: **was it tested** on new cases?'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'For years, one figure from couples research was repeated everywhere. From a **short conversation**, researchers could supposedly spot which couples would divorce. They\u2019d be right over **ninety per cent** of the time. Why didn\u2019t it hold?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The central problem: the models were largely **fitted and evaluated** on the same couples, not tested on new ones. That inflates apparent accuracy. Samples were also modest. And **the base rate** of divorce changes what any accuracy figure means.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a made-up sample where most couples stay together. A model that simply predicts *stays together* for everyone scores well. It knows nothing about any couple. The accuracy looks strong only because **the outcome** was rare.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a hiring firm whose tool picks good staff with very high accuracy. That figure was measured on the staff it was trained on. So it shows how well the tool fits its past. It says nothing about **the next applicant**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The correction can be overdone. Overfitting inflates a figure, but it doesn\u2019t make **the behaviours imaginary**. Still, much of the evidence for them comes from the same research group, so independent checks would settle it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The patterns are the useful part. The headline figure was **a method artefact**, and it\u2019s the bit you remember. Before you repeat any accuracy claim, ask whether it was tested on **new cases**.'}]}}}},962,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'how-to-disagree-well',kind:'book',promise:'Argue to learn, not just to win',about:'Do your fights go in circles? Often, two people are fighting about two different things. The Mindroll team, who make this app, wrote this article to help. It gives five ways to argue so you both learn. Which fight will you try them on first?',ideas:{'how-to-disagree-well_1':{title:'Say Their Side Back',card:{insight:'Say their side back until they say yes, then give your answer.',tryThis:'Next time you argue, say the other side back in your own words. Wait for a yes.',skills:['communication','thinking']},review:{prompt:'A friend at work says your plan will fail. What do you do first?',right:'Say his side back until he says yes',wrong:'Show him right away where he is wrong',why:'Once he says yes, you know what he means, and he will listen to you.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your sister wants **a scary film**. You want a funny one. She says yours is babyish. You say hers is dumb. Ten minutes later you\u2019re both cross, and **no film is on**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Try this instead. Before you argue back, **say her side** in your own words. Keep going until she says, *yes, that\u2019s what I mean*. Only then give yours. Now she\u2019s **listening**, not guarding.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A good waiter reads your order back **before cooking starts**. Do the same in an argument. Say their side back until they agree, and you\u2019ll argue about **the real thing**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A colleague argues against **your hiring plan**. You\u2019re already composing the reply. But the version of their objection in your head may not be **the one they hold**. Whose argument are you about to answer?'},{kind:'story',text:'So you try something else. You say their objection back: the risk is hiring before the money is certain. They say, not quite. It\u2019s that nobody\u2019s free to train the new person. You try again until they nod.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Restating their view does **two jobs at once**, and the second is the one **people underrate**.',left:{title:'For the argument',items:['Finds the real point of difference','Stops you attacking a phantom']},right:{title:'For the person',items:['Shows you were listening','Moves them from defending to assessing']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s why the second matters. Someone who\u2019s just heard their view **stated fairly** has nothing left to defend. They start weighing your reply instead. The piece argues that **weighing** is the state in which people change their minds.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t reply to the version **in your head**. Say their view back until they say yes. Then you\u2019re answering **what they think**, and they\u2019re ready to hear you.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Most arguments are aimed at **a version** of the other view that nobody actually holds. Both people talk. Neither reaches the real point of difference. How would you know if **you\u2019re doing it**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The test is to state the other position so well that **its holder agrees**. Not your own sense that you were fair, but their yes. That makes your summary something **they can check**, and gives them a reason to keep listening.'},{kind:'story',text:'Two neighbours argue about a new car park. One says it\u2019ll bring traffic. The other restates that, and hears: not traffic, *speed*, past the school gate. The real disagreement was narrower than either had thought.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now you restate a friend\u2019s case against your plan, and it comes out **stronger** than you\u2019d assumed. You can\u2019t un-know that. That\u2019s the cost the piece names, and why the practice is rare.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. State a case better than its holder did, and they may not **recognise it** as theirs. Restating is easy to fake, as a ritual before a reply that ignores it. And the claim about weighing is **argued, not shown**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Before you answer, **earn their yes**. It locates the real disagreement and moves them from defending to **weighing**. Use it where the disagreement matters, not on every passing comment.'}]},'how-to-disagree-well_2':{title:'Three Fights in One',card:{insight:'Facts end some fights, but others are about a word or what matters.',tryThis:'Next time you argue, agree on the facts first. Then ask what each word means to you both.',skills:['communication','thinking']},review:{prompt:'You both agree on the facts, yet you still argue. What is left?',right:'What a word means, or what matters most to you',wrong:'A fight about facts, so look up more of them',why:'When you agree on the facts, more facts will not end the fight.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two friends argue about **Pluto**. One says it\u2019s a planet, and the other says it isn\u2019t. They both know how big it is and where it goes. So **what are they** really fighting about?'},{kind:'explain',text:'It\u2019s only about the word *planet*. Arguments come in **three kinds**: about what\u2019s true, about what matters most, and about **what a word means**. Pluto is the third kind, so no fact will end it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Next time you argue, stop and ask **which kind** it is. Check the fact, agree on the word, and the fight gets **much smaller**. What\u2019s left is usually about what you each care about.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A team is forty minutes into arguing whether **remote work** is productive. Everyone has points. **Nobody has moved** an inch. You\u2019re starting to wonder what they\u2019re even arguing about. What\u2019s going on?'},{kind:'story',text:'One person means output per hour, and has the numbers. Another means whether junior staff learn as fast, which the numbers don\u2019t measure. A third simply prefers the office and grabs whichever argument is nearest.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'**Three conversations** are running in one meeting, and each is **settled differently**.',left:{title:'The dispute',items:['Facts: what\u2019s true','Values: what matters','Definitions: what a word means']},right:{title:'What settles it',items:['Evidence','An honest trade-off','Agreeing the meaning']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Treat a values dispute as factual and you get **an endless swap** of studies that persuade nobody. Treat a definition as real and two people disagree until someone notices **one word** is doing two jobs.'},{kind:'moral',text:'**Sort before you argue**: is this about what\u2019s true, what matters, or what a word means? Two of the three often dissolve. What\u2019s left is **a smaller, real disagreement** you can have honestly.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'The piece starts from a blunt claim. Most disagreements fail **before the substance** is ever reached, because people are answering different questions. Neither has noticed. Have you ever left an argument unsure **what it was about**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Evidence can **settle a fact**. It can\u2019t settle a clash of values, and trying only piles up studies that move nobody. A clash over a definition isn\u2019t a real clash at all. **One word** is covering two different things.'},{kind:'story',text:'A team argues about whether remote work is productive. The word *productive* is doing two jobs. Output per hour is factual, and numbers can settle it. Whether juniors learn as fast is factual too, but unmeasured. One person\u2019s office preference is a value.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now two managers argue about whether a new policy is fair. Both accept the same figures on who gains and who loses. So the facts aren\u2019t in dispute. What\u2019s left is a real disagreement about **what matters**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The three kinds don\u2019t always separate cleanly. What counts as good evidence can depend on values. And some definitions carry **real consequences**, like who qualifies for a benefit. Calling something *just semantics* can **wave away** a point that matters.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Sorting **shrinks** a disagreement. It doesn\u2019t promise the leftover is small. But it makes sure you\u2019re both in **the same conversation** before either of you tries to win it.'}]},'how-to-disagree-well_3':{title:'What Would Change Your Mind',card:{insight:'Say what would change your mind, and your fight turns into a test.',tryThis:'Pick one thing you are sure of. Write down what would prove you wrong.',skills:['thinking','communication']},review:{prompt:'You are sure the bus is late every day. Which line fits this idea?',right:'I will change my mind if it is on time all week',wrong:'I know it is late, and nothing will change that',why:'Saying what would change your mind shows you are thinking, not just holding on.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two friends argue about when **the shop shuts**. Ella thinks it\u2019s five. Max thinks it\u2019s six, and says it louder each time. Which of them do you think is **really thinking**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Ella says, *if the sign says six, I\u2019m wrong.* Now there\u2019s a way to find out. They walk over and read the sign. Shouting louder never gave them any way to settle it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Before you argue, say what would **change your mind**. If you can\u2019t think of anything, notice that. Maybe it\u2019s **just what you like**, not a fact at all.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re sure your supplier is **unreliable**. You\u2019ve said so in three meetings. Then a colleague asks a simple question: what would they have to do to change your view? You **can\u2019t answer**. What does that mean?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The question is **a test for you** before it\u2019s a way to persuade. If nothing could shift you, your view isn\u2019t really a belief about facts. It\u2019s **an identity**, or a preference.'},{kind:'story',text:'So you try. You\u2019d change your mind if they hit every deadline this quarter. Now your colleague knows what to look for. The argument shifts from attacking you to checking **the delivery dates**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'A stated condition turns your view from something you **defend** into something that **could be tested**.',left:{title:'A position',items:['Defended against attack','Nothing could change it']},right:{title:'A hypothesis',items:['Open to evidence','Says what would disprove it']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Say **what would move you**, and ask it early. Finding mid-argument that nothing would is **embarrassing**. Better to learn that about your view before the disagreement starts.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Naming the evidence that would move you sounds like **a concession**. It looks like you\u2019re admitting doubt before the argument starts. So why does the piece call it **the strongest signal** that you\u2019re reasoning?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Because it changes **what kind of thing** your view is. A position is something you defend. A hypothesis could be found wrong, and saying how is what makes it one. It also **changes the incentives** for the other side.'},{kind:'story',text:'A manager is sure a new rota has made staff less happy. Asked what would change his mind, he says: *if sick days hadn\u2019t risen.* Now the team has something to check instead of something to fight.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take someone who argues that their team is the best. Asked the same question, they say nothing ever could. That\u2019s honest, and useful. It isn\u2019t a factual belief. It\u2019s **loyalty**, and the argument was never about facts.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Not every view should have a condition. Values, like the wrongness of cruelty, **aren\u2019t hypotheses**, and holding them firmly isn\u2019t a failure. A condition can also be gamed: set **impossibly high**, it signals openness while ruling out change.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Before the argument, ask yourself **what would move you**. If the answer is nothing, you\u2019ve learned **what you\u2019re holding**. Then you can have the argument you\u2019re really in.'}]},'how-to-disagree-well_4':{title:'Answer the Best Reason',card:{insight:'Beating a weak reason proves little, so answer their best reason.',tryThis:'Pick a view you think is wrong. Write the best reason a careful person could give for it.',skills:['thinking','communication']},review:{prompt:'You want to argue against a new bike lane in town. How do you get ready?',right:'Find the best reason for it and answer that',wrong:'Find the loudest post for it and pick it apart',why:'Only beating the best reason tells you if your side holds up.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your team beats **the weakest player** on the other side in a quick game. Your friends cheer. But does that show your team is **better**? What would you really need to do?'},{kind:'story',text:'Your class argues about starting school later. One kid says yes, because school is boring. That\u2019s easy to beat. Another says yes, because tired kids find it hard to learn. That one\u2019s much harder to answer.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Every side has **a weak version** that\u2019s easy to beat, and beating it proves nothing. If you want to argue no, answer **the best reason** for yes.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You oppose **a four-day working week**. Online, its most visible champion says it would make everyone happier. You could take that apart in a paragraph. Should you? What would **that win** prove?'},{kind:'story',text:'Now suppose an economist argues it would cut sick leave and help firms keep staff. That\u2019s much harder to answer. You\u2019d need evidence, not a quip. Only one of those two replies would tell you anything.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Beating **a weak version** says nothing about the position, because the position was never that version. And the people most likely to be won over by your rebuttal are **already on your side**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Where you find **the other side** decides what your reply can **actually settle**.',left:{title:'Loudest advocate',items:['Chosen for reach','Easy to rebut','Persuades your own side']},right:{title:'Most capable advocate',items:['Chosen for rigour','Hard to rebut','Settles something real']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Before you answer an argument, find its **most capable advocate**, not its loudest. Answer that version. If you can\u2019t find one, you\u2019re **not ready** to reply.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'The piece names **two old models**. One is a scientist\u2019s chapter on the difficulties facing his own theory. The other is a dialogue where an opponent\u2019s case is restated **more forcefully** before it\u2019s answered. What could you copy from them?'},{kind:'story',text:'In *On the Origin of Species*, Darwin set aside a whole chapter for the difficulties facing his own theory. The objections weren\u2019t hidden in footnotes. They got the space of an argument.'},{kind:'story',text:'In Plato\u2019s *Republic*, Thrasymachus argues that justice serves the stronger. Before Socrates replies, Glaucon restates that case more forcefully than Thrasymachus did. Only then does Socrates answer, so his reply faces the best version.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The point is about **selection**. The visible advocates of any view are picked by audiences and platforms for **reach**. The best case is usually held by someone quieter and more careful. Finding them is the expensive part.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. It\u2019s slow, and there isn\u2019t always time. The audience in front of you may hold **the weak version**, and they\u2019re owed an answer. And building up a view nobody capable holds can lend it **unearned respect**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Beating a weak version is **performing a refutation**. Beating the strongest one is **making one**. Find the best case, wherever it\u2019s held, and then you answer that.'}]},'how-to-disagree-well_5':{title:'Know When to Stop',card:{insight:'Once you both know each other\u2019s side and still disagree, stop.',tryThis:'Pick a fight you keep having. Write one line on what you both agree on.',skills:['communication','relationships']},review:{prompt:'A fight with your uncle about phones has gone around three times. What now?',right:'Say what you agree on and what is still open, then stop',wrong:'Keep going until one of you finally gives in',why:'After that point, more pushing only makes both of you dig in.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You and a friend have argued about **the best book** for an hour. You keep saying the same things. So does she. Nobody\u2019s changing their mind. Should you **keep going**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Each time you say it again, it gets **harder** for your friend to change her mind in front of you. If you stop nicely, she can think it over **later, on her own**. That\u2019s often when minds change.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When you both understand each other and still disagree, **stop**. Say what you agree on and what you don\u2019t. Then **leave it there**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You and a colleague are on the same point **for the third time**. Each of you could recite the other\u2019s case. Neither is adding anything new. And the tone has **started to sharpen**. Now what?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Some disagreements are **stable**. You each understand the other\u2019s case, and you still differ. Past that point, carrying on turns the exchange into **a contest about status**. You\u2019re no longer finding anything out.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s why pushing backfires. Every lap raises **the cost** of changing position in public. So a final push hardens views. The piece argues that much real changing of minds happens **later, in private**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'There are **two ways** for an argument like yours to end, and only one **keeps the work**.',left:{title:'By exhaustion',items:['Someone gives in or walks out','Nothing is recorded']},right:{title:'On purpose',items:['Name what\u2019s settled and still open','They can revise later']}},{kind:'moral',text:'When the exchange starts repeating, say you understand each other and still disagree. Name **what\u2019s settled** and what\u2019s still open. Then **stop**, on purpose.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two people are still arguing, and both could now state the other\u2019s view perfectly. Positions are being **restated, not developed**. One is arguing mainly **to avoid conceding**. What\u2019s left for this argument to do?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Any one of those signs is a warning. Past that point, each repetition **raises the price** of changing position in public. So the argument hardens views instead of settling them. That\u2019s why the loop **rarely breaks itself**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a couple on their third lap about where to go on holiday. They could end it by exhaustion, or with one walking out. Instead, one says: *we both get it, and we still differ.* They list what\u2019s settled and stop.'},{kind:'story',text:'A week later, one of them quietly comes round on the dates. Nobody had to lose in front of anyone. The piece argues this is **where much updating happens**: afterwards, alone. An explicit ending leaves room for it.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some disagreements can\u2019t be left: a team still has to choose. Agreeing to differ can quietly hand the decision to **whoever\u2019s more stubborn**. Stopping can also be a dodge. And the private-updating claim is **the piece\u2019s judgement**, not shown.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Knowing when to stop is **a skill**, not a concession. Once you understand each other and still differ, name what\u2019s settled and **end it on purpose**. You leave the door open.'}]}}}},963,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'how-to-live-on-24-hours',kind:'book',promise:'Find time for you before and after work',about:'Where do your hours after work go? In 1908, an English writer named Arnold Bennett wrote a book about this. It was for office workers who felt they never had time. Each idea here is one of his tips. Ready to find your free hours?',ideas:{'how-to-live-on-24-hours_1':{title:'Twenty-Four Fresh Hours',simple:{headline:'You never have to make up missed time',body:'Arnold Bennett, writing in 1908, called time the one thing everyone gets in equal amounts. You can\u2019t save it up, and you can\u2019t borrow it. Each morning you wake with the same 24 hours, however you spent yesterday. You planned three study evenings last week and managed none. The urge is to plan six this week to catch up. **Missed hours aren\u2019t a debt, so you don\u2019t have to pay them back.** Plan this week as if it were the first one. Start with tonight.',doToday:'Cross out last week\u2019s missed goals, and write down what tonight\u2019s first free hour is for.'},card:{insight:'You get 24 new hours each day, even after a bad one.',tryThis:'When you miss a day of your plan, do not double up. Plan only the hours ahead of you.',skills:['productivity','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'You planned to play guitar three nights last week, but you did not. What now?',right:'Start fresh and plan only the hours ahead',wrong:'Play six nights this week to catch up',why:'Each morning brings new hours, so you never owe the missed ones.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You spent all of Saturday doing **nothing much**. You feel a bit bad about it. So when Sunday morning comes, how many hours do you get? **Fewer than usual**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Sunday brings **24 hours**, just like every day. Wasting Saturday didn\u2019t take any away. It didn\u2019t earn you extra ones either. A writer called Arnold Bennett said time is the one thing **everyone gets equally**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You can\u2019t **save up hours**, and you can\u2019t borrow them. Yesterday is gone either way. So the hours that matter are **today\u2019s**, and they\u2019re already here.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You meant to do something with the weekend. You **didn\u2019t**. On Monday morning, the temptation is to write off **the whole week** too and start properly next Saturday. Sound familiar?'},{kind:'story',text:'In 1908 Arnold Bennett, an English novelist, wrote a short book for office workers who never had time. He didn\u2019t open with a method. He opened with a count: each morning, you wake with twenty-four hours.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Bennett\u2019s point is that **money and time** follow **different rules**.',left:{title:'Money',items:['Can run short','Can be borrowed','Shared out unequally']},right:{title:'Time',items:['Same amount every morning','Can\u2019t be saved or borrowed','Identical for everyone']}},{kind:'explain',text:'So why do we waste it? Because waste is **never punished** by shortage. You squander a Tuesday and Wednesday still arrives full. Missed hours **aren\u2019t owed**, either. The only fresh question is what today\u2019s hours are for.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Every day arrives with **the same hours**, whatever you did with the last lot. Last week isn\u2019t a debt. So plan **today fresh**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture two people waking on the same Monday. One is **a millionaire**. The other has **two pounds** in her purse. Which of them has more hours ahead today? And why might that matter?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Bennett\u2019s claim is simple. Money can be **short, borrowed or saved**. Time can\u2019t. Everyone gets the same supply, and none of it keeps. He saw it as **the one equal thing** in an unequal world.'},{kind:'story',text:'His reader was a London clerk with fixed office hours. The clerk could waste an evening freely, because Thursday always came full. Nothing ran short in the moment. The loss only showed up years later, as plans that never happened.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Priya, who skipped her Spanish practice all week. On Sunday she plans to do seven sessions at once. But Sunday holds the same hours as ever. The missed week can\u2019t be repaid, only left behind.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The hours are only equal **on the clock**. A carer or a shift worker gets twenty-four, but far fewer to choose. Read harshly, equal hours make every shortfall look like **your fault**. Often it isn\u2019t.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You get **the same supply** as everyone, and you can\u2019t store it. So treat the day as **a budget to spend**, starting from today, not yesterday\u2019s deficit.'}]},'how-to-live-on-24-hours_2':{title:'The Sixteen-Hour Day',simple:{headline:'The hours after work belong to you',body:'Your job takes about eight hours of your day. Arnold Bennett pointed out that the other sixteen, sleep included, often end up serving it too. Mornings get you ready for work, and evenings help you recover from it. **Treat the hours after work as a day of their own.** If you finish at six, you could give two evenings a week to drawing or learning Spanish. The job stays the same. You stop handing it the time it doesn\u2019t pay for.',doToday:'Block one hour between work and bed tonight in your calendar, and name what it\u2019s for.'},card:{insight:'The hours after work are a second day, and that day is yours.',tryThis:'Tonight, pick one free hour after work, like seven to eight. Decide what it is for before dinner.',skills:['productivity','focus']},review:{prompt:'Your friend spends every night just resting up for work. What would Arnold Bennett say?',right:'Pick one free hour each night just for you',wrong:'Rest every night so you are fresh for work',why:'Work gets eight hours, but the hours after work are yours.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'School takes about **seven hours**. Sleep takes about nine. Add those up and take them from twenty-four. How many hours are **left over** every single day? More than you\u2019d guess?'},{kind:'explain',text:'That\u2019s **eight hours**, more than the whole school day. Arnold Bennett noticed that grown-ups called work **the real day**. The rest was just getting ready or getting over it. He thought that wasted most of a life.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The hours after school aren\u2019t just **a rest** from it. They\u2019re a big part of **your life**. You get to choose what they\u2019re for.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your job takes **eight hours** a day. That leaves **sixteen** that it doesn\u2019t touch, sleep included. So why does it feel as if the whole day belongs to **the office**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Bennett wrote for the London clerk of 1908. The clerk saw his office hours as the day. His mornings were for getting ready. His evenings were for recovering. The job had claimed hours it didn\u2019t even occupy.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Bennett proposed **turning it round**, so the job sits inside **your day**.',left:{title:'The job\u2019s day',items:['Work is the real day','Evenings are recovery','Mornings are preparation']},right:{title:'Your day',items:['Sixteen hours with a purpose','Work is one part','Evenings for something chosen']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s why it\u2019s cheap. It needs **no new job** and no permission. The work stays the same. Only the evenings change, because they now have **their own purpose**. That\u2019s why Bennett barely discusses which job you should do.'},{kind:'moral',text:'An evening spent only to be **fit for work** still belongs to the job. Give some of your sixteen hours **a purpose** you chose yourself.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Think of how people describe a weekday. They talk about **the working day**, then mention the evening as an afterthought. Which part of your waking life is actually bigger? And who decided **the evening** was the leftovers?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Bennett\u2019s sharpest point is about **prologue and epilogue**. If the morning prepares you for work and the evening recovers from it, the job owns both. It\u2019s claimed **all sixteen hours**, not just its eight.'},{kind:'story',text:'His reader was a salaried clerk with a train journey either side of the office. He felt life was happening somewhere else. Bennett told him to treat the sixteen hours as **a day in themselves**, with work inserted.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take an accountant who finishes at six. For years she\u2019s called the evening downtime. One week she gives two evenings to drawing. The job hasn\u2019t changed at all. Her evenings now answer to her.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Bennett\u2019s reader had **regular hours** and evenings mostly his own. A parent or shift worker has sixteen hours that are already full. For them, rest is a need, not **a failure** of purpose.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your job fills eight hours, not the whole day. Don\u2019t let it set **the purpose** of hours it never touches. Even a small share of **your evenings** can be yours.'}]},'how-to-live-on-24-hours_3':{title:'Three Evenings, Not Seven',simple:{headline:'Plan three nights a week, not every night',body:'In January you plan to study every evening, two hours a night. By week three the plan has collapsed, and you decide the whole idea was silly. Arnold Bennett saw this coming in 1908. He told readers to start with ninety minutes on three evenings a week, and leave the other four alone. **Size the plan for your worst week, not your best.** A small plan survives a late shift or a cold. A big one breaks on the first bad week, and then you blame the idea instead of the size.',doToday:'Put 90 minutes in your calendar on three evenings this week, and leave the other four free.'},card:{insight:'A small plan you can keep in a bad week beats a big plan.',tryThis:'Today, pick three nights this week for your goal. Leave the other four nights free.',skills:['habits','productivity']},review:{prompt:'A friend plans to study for two hours every night all year. What will go wrong?',right:'The plan is too big, so it breaks in a bad week',wrong:'She will quit because she does not try hard enough',why:'Big plans break in the first bad week, so start small.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You decide to get really good at guitar. **One hour**, every single day! Monday and Tuesday go well. On Thursday you\u2019re tired and skip it. By Sunday, **you\u2019ve stopped** completely. What went wrong?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The plan was **too big**. Arnold Bennett warned about this back in 1908. People start huge, quit fast, then decide the idea was silly. He said to begin with ninety minutes on **three evenings** a week.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A **small plan** you keep beats a big plan you drop. Leave room for **tired days**, and you\u2019ll keep going much longer.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'It\u2019s January. You draw up a plan: study **every evening**, two hours a night, starting Monday. It looks impressive on paper. Be honest. Where will **that plan** be by the third week?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Bennett expected exactly this. The ambitious reader tries too much, misses a few evenings, and decides **the whole idea** is impossible. The failure gets blamed on the idea, when it was really **the size** that broke.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The real limit is rarely **free hours**. It\u2019s **sustainable ones**.',left:{title:'Every evening',items:['Looks impressive','Breaks in a busy week','Kills the idea']},right:{title:'Three evenings',items:['Looks modest','Survives a bad week','Keeps the idea alive']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Begin with **ninety minutes**, three evenings a week, and leave four alone. A plan you can keep beats one you admire. **The smallness** is the design.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Bennett has just freed sixteen hours of your day. You might expect him to fill them all with **self-improvement**. Instead, he asks for **remarkably little**. Why would a book about time ask for so few of them?'},{kind:'explain',text:'His request is **ninety minutes**, three evenings a week, of deliberate mental effort. He also warns against starting with **too much energy**. The reader with grand resolutions, he notes, has usually stopped within weeks.'},{kind:'story',text:'Think of Tom, who wants to learn Italian. He signs up for an hour every night. A late shift breaks the run on day nine. He decides languages aren\u2019t for him and quits for good.'},{kind:'story',text:'His sister picks three evenings and keeps each session short enough for a bad week. Some weeks she manages two. A year on, she\u2019s still going. Her plan survived because it was **sized for bad weeks**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Small isn\u2019t automatically right. An exam next month may need more than ninety minutes three times a week. And a dose **too small** to show progress can die of boredom instead.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Size your plan for **your worst week**, not your best. The return comes from **keeping it** for years, so pick a programme small enough to survive.'}]},'how-to-live-on-24-hours_4':{title:'Fetch the Mind Back',simple:{headline:'When your mind wanders, bring it back to one thing',body:'Arnold Bennett gave office workers a plain exercise for the walk to the station. Pick one subject and keep your thoughts on it. They will wander within a minute. Bring them back without getting cross, then carry on. You sit down to think through a hard decision and catch yourself planning dinner. You haven\u2019t failed. **Each time you bring your mind back counts as one rep.** Like any skill, focus improves with practice, and wishing for it does nothing.',doToday:'Pick one question to hold on your next walk, and count each time you bring your mind back to it.'},card:{insight:'Your mind will drift, but each time you bring it back, your focus grows.',tryThis:'On your next quiet walk, think about one question. Count each time you bring your mind back.',skills:['focus']},review:{prompt:'You sit down to think about a job offer. Soon you are planning dinner. What now?',right:'Bring your mind back to the job offer and keep going',wrong:'Give up, since your brain cannot focus today',why:'Each time you bring your mind back, your focus gets a little stronger.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You take a puppy for a walk. It pulls off to sniff **a bush**. You bring it back. Ten steps later, it\u2019s off again. Do you get cross, or do you just **bring it back**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Your mind wanders just like **that puppy**. Arnold Bennett said each time you notice and fetch it back, it learns a little. The first tries feel silly. But there\u2019s **no clever trick**, only practice.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Focus is **a skill**, not something you\u2019re born with. Every time you bring your mind back, **that\u2019s the practice**. It gets easier each week.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'There\u2019s a decision you\u2019ve been **putting off** for weeks. You sit down to think it through. Within a minute you\u2019re checking **your phone**, then planning dinner. Does that mean you can\u2019t think properly?'},{kind:'story',text:'Bennett gave his clerk a plain exercise for the morning walk. Pick one subject and hold your thoughts on it. When they drift, and they will at once, fetch them back without irritation. Then carry on.'},{kind:'explain',text:'This is how **any faculty** trains. The first attempts are humbling, because they show how little control you have. The improvement comes from **repetition**, not insight. Each return to **the problem** counts as one rep.'},{kind:'explain',text:'It sounds like modern meditation: notice, return, repeat. But in 1908 Bennett sold it as **thinking better**, not feeling calmer. He wanted his clerk able to think hard about one thing on **the train**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The drift **isn\u2019t failure**. It\u2019s what the exercise works on. Calmly bring your mind back and carry on, and **the skill grows**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Bennett makes **a blunt claim**: you can\u2019t control your mind by wishing. He doesn\u2019t mean you\u2019re weak. He means something more useful. If wishing doesn\u2019t work, **what does**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'His answer is **training**, like any other faculty. Hold the mind on one subject. When it drifts, fetch it back **without irritation**. The first tries are humbling, and they show how little control you really have.'},{kind:'story',text:'His clerk had one stretch of quiet: the walk and the train each morning. So Bennett gave him one subject to hold for the whole journey. The training cost no extra time. It used a walk he took anyway.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture someone walking a dog that keeps heading for the hedge. She doesn\u2019t argue with it or punish it. She brings it back, calmly, every time. Over weeks it pulls less. Attention trains the same way.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Minds aren\u2019t equally trainable. A mind that keeps drifting may be signalling **tiredness or worry**, which needs care, not discipline. Real attention difficulties need proper help. And a busy road is no place to drift.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You can\u2019t wish your way into focus, so you **train it**, one return at a time. Forty returns on a walk aren\u2019t forty failures. They\u2019re **forty reps**.'}]},'how-to-live-on-24-hours_5':{title:'The Quiet Convert',simple:{headline:'Enjoy your new habit, but do not brag',body:'A friend starts getting up at five to run. A month later every dinner comes with a report, plus a gentle question about why you still scroll in bed. Arnold Bennett warned about this in 1908. A new routine can make you smug, tiring company. **Your routine is for your own good, and it doesn\u2019t make you better than anyone.** Preaching can also cost you the routine. Once your friends are tired of hearing about it, keeping it up gets harder. Mention it when someone asks, then change the subject.',doToday:'Get through today without mentioning your diet or routine unless someone asks you about it.'},card:{insight:'Your habit is for you, and it does not make you better than your friends.',tryThis:'Today, do not bring up your new habit first. Talk about it only when someone asks.',skills:['habits','communication']},review:{prompt:'You have run every morning for six weeks. A friend orders fries. What do you do?',right:'Let her eat, and do not bring up your habit',wrong:'Tell her how much better you feel without fries',why:'Your habit is for you, so let your friends eat in peace.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your friend has started **getting up early** to run. That\u2019s great, but now it\u2019s all she talks about. She sighs at your crisps, and people have started sitting **somewhere else**. What\u2019s gone wrong?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Running is good for her, but lecturing isn\u2019t. Arnold Bennett warned about this in 1908. A new plan can make people **annoying**. Once friends are fed up, people often **quietly give up** the plan as well.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A good plan is **for you**. It doesn\u2019t make you better than anyone. So keep doing it, and **skip the lecture**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A friend changed his mornings a month ago. Now every dinner comes with **a report**. There\u2019s the early alarm, the journal, and a gentle question about why you still scroll in bed. Is this **helping anyone**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Bennett gave part of a chapter to this danger. He warned against becoming **a prig**, someone insufferable about their programme. His worry was practical. The newly systematic mistake **their schedule** for a virtue, and their company suffers.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Then comes **the second cost**. Once friends tire of hearing about the routine, the social price is felt. And the routine is often **quietly dropped**. Performing it ends up costing you the thing itself.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your programme is for **your benefit**. It gives you **no standing** over anyone. Keep the routine, keep it to yourself, and let it do its work.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Advice books rarely admit their advice might make you **worse company**. This one does. Bennett sets out the danger plainly and treats it as **a design flaw**, not a matter of manners. What exactly is he worried about?'},{kind:'explain',text:'His sharper point is about motive. If you need your self-improvement **acknowledged**, you\u2019re doing it for a different reason than the one you give. The person visibly optimising their day often enjoys **the performance** more than the result.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Dan, who started journalling at dawn. Within a month it came up at every lunch. Colleagues began eating elsewhere. By spring he\u2019d stopped journalling too, and he couldn\u2019t quite say why.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Ana, who studies three evenings a week. When a friend asks how she finds the time, she answers plainly and changes the subject. Nobody feels behind. Her evenings carry on unnoticed, which suits them fine.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The warning can be over-learned. Telling a friend, or joining others with the same aim, can help a habit last. Total secrecy is its own kind of vanity. Bennett\u2019s target is **contempt and display**, not conversation.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A routine kept for **standing** fades when nobody admires it. One kept for **your benefit** survives being unnoticed. Mention it when asked, and never as proof you\u2019re ahead.'}]},'how-to-live-on-24-hours_6':{title:'The Journey Counts',simple:{headline:'Use your ride to work to think',body:'Forty minutes each way, five days a week, comes to over six hours. Most of it goes on scrolling or dozing. A commute feels like a gap between the real parts of the day. Arnold Bennett told London office workers to treat their train time as their own. **Choose one question before you set off, and think it through on the way.** Trains and waiting rooms suit this, because nobody interrupts you there. You\u2019ll arrive with a decision half made.',doToday:'Write one question on a note, then keep your phone in your pocket for your next commute or wait.'},card:{insight:'A train ride is real time, and it is a good time to think.',tryThis:'Before your next ride, pick one question to think about. Keep your phone in your pocket.',skills:['focus','productivity']},review:{prompt:'You have a long train ride and a hard choice to make. What should you do?',right:'Think about the choice and keep your phone away',wrong:'Scroll your phone, since a ride is not real time',why:'A quiet ride is real time, and it is good for hard thinking.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Say your bus to school takes **twenty minutes**. Twenty more to get home. Over a school week, how much time is that? And what do you usually do with it, besides **staring out** of the window?'},{kind:'explain',text:'That\u2019s **over three hours** a week. Trips feel like gaps between the real parts of the day. Arnold Bennett, writing in 1908, said they\u2019re **real time** too. He said to use them for thinking about one thing.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A trip isn\u2019t **empty time**. It\u2019s quiet, and nobody interrupts you. Pick **one thing** to think about, and the ride becomes yours.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Forty minutes each way, five days a week. Over a working year, that adds up to **a lot of hours**. Most of them go on staring, scrolling or dozing. Have you ever **counted yours**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Bennett\u2019s reader rode a suburban train into London every morning and home every evening. He treated the trip as a gap. Bennett saw a big daily block, **written off** out of habit, and told him to use it.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Why journeys? They\u2019re **predictable**, and nothing interrupts them. Those are the conditions hard thinking needs, and the working day rarely offers them. Queues and waiting rooms belong to **the same class** of time.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Bennett suggested **thinking, not reading**, because a train suits **reflection** better than study.',left:{title:'Written off as',items:['A gap between places','Vague recovery','Anything to fill it']},right:{title:'Bennett\u2019s view',items:['A daily block of time','One sustained activity','One question, held in mind']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Journeys feel like gaps, so you **write them off**. Yet those hours are as real as any others. Give the next trip a **single question**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A man sits on a train with **a closed book** beside him. He isn\u2019t reading or sleeping. He\u2019s staring at one spot, working something out. Is he **wasting the journey**, or using it better than anyone?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Bennett\u2019s claim is that journeys get written off because they **feel like transitions**. That\u2019s an error of filing, not a fact about the hours. Predictable, uninterrupted time suits **sustained attention** better than most of a working day.'},{kind:'story',text:'His clerk had the morning train, every working day. Bennett told him to skip reading and hold one hard subject in mind instead. It tied neatly to the walking exercise. Both need a quiet, unbroken stretch.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture Mei at the airport gate, with a speech to plan for her sister\u2019s wedding. She could scroll for an hour. Instead she builds the speech in her head, line by line. She boards with it ready.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The idea assumes a seat and **a quiet carriage**. Driving, cycling or standing in a crush leaves little room for hard thought. For some of you, the journey is the only rest you get.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your journeys are **real hours**, and they come with quiet built in. The modern trouble is that you fill them with a phone. Keep **one stretch** empty and think.'}]},'how-to-live-on-24-hours_7':{title:'Lapse, Not Collapse',simple:{headline:'Missing a few days does not end your plan',body:'You kept a new evening routine for two weeks. Then a late night at work and a cold took three evenings. You start to think it doesn\u2019t work for you. Arnold Bennett expected this. He said failing at a plan costs you little, because those hours would have gone on nothing much. **The costly part is giving up and feeling guilty about it.** So skip the new system and the fresh start date. Do the next session as normal, and don\u2019t count the gap.',doToday:'Do a short session today of one habit you dropped, without making a new plan first.'},card:{insight:'Missing a few days is a small slip, and it ends when you start again.',tryThis:'Today, pick up one habit you dropped. Do not make a new plan or wait for Monday.',skills:['habits','psychology']},review:{prompt:'You missed two weeks of Spanish practice. How do you start again?',right:'Pick it up again tonight, with no new plan',wrong:'Wait for Monday and start a strict new plan',why:'A few missed days do little harm, so just start again.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You were drawing every Monday, Wednesday and Friday. Then you **got ill**, and then there was a party. Two weeks slipped by, and now it feels **too late** to go back. Is it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'It isn\u2019t. Arnold Bennett said trying a plan **can\u2019t really hurt** you. If you drop it, you only lose hours you weren\u2019t using anyway. Feeling bad about it costs **more than the gap**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Missing some days **doesn\u2019t mean** the plan failed. Don\u2019t make a big deal of it. Just **start again** today, right where you left off.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You kept your new evening routine for **two weeks**. Then came a late night at work, a cold, a birthday. Three evenings gone. Somewhere in the fourth, a quiet thought forms: *this doesn\u2019t work for me.* Sound **familiar**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Bennett saw this coming and answered it **calmly**. Failing at the programme does you no harm, he argued, because those hours would have gone on nothing much. What does harm is **abandonment followed by guilt**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The same missed evenings can turn into **a lapse** or **a collapse**.',left:{title:'A lapse',items:['Some sessions missed','Restart at the next session','No fuss about the gap']},right:{title:'A collapse',items:['Some sessions missed','Read as proof it fails','Plan dropped, guilt kept']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Judging a plan on one bad fortnight is **bad evidence**. Expect lapses. Do **the next session** as normal, and skip the reckoning.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'You might expect a self-help book to promise **you won\u2019t fail**. Bennett does the opposite. He predicts you\u2019ll set a scheme, keep it briefly and drop it. Then he says something relaxed about that. What could make **failure harmless**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'His answer: the hours were **otherwise idle**. Trying costs almost nothing, so failing costs almost nothing too. The only expensive move is treating a lapse as **meaningful**, and abandoning the whole scheme with guilt.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture Kemi, who runs three mornings a week. A work trip knocks out ten days. She tells herself she\u2019s failed again, and the trainers go in the cupboard. A ten-day gap has become **a year off**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Her neighbour loses ten days to a bad cold. On the first free morning he simply runs. He doesn\u2019t count the gap or make a new plan. By summer, the lapse is forgotten.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Not every lapse is noise. A habit dropped again and again at the same point may be **badly designed**, and restarting it unchanged is stubbornness. And trying is only free when **the hours were idle**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Expect to lapse, and **don\u2019t read it** as a verdict on you. Restarting costs almost nothing. **Giving up** with guilt is the expensive part.'}]},'how-to-live-on-24-hours_8':{title:'The Book That Resists',simple:{headline:'Read books that make you think hard',body:'Arnold Bennett wrote novels for a living, yet he said your study evenings shouldn\u2019t go on novels. They go down too easily, so they leave little behind. He pointed readers to subjects with structure, like a serious history, where each part builds on the last. Two friends read three evenings a week for a year. One gets through forty easy books. The other works through one hard history, ten pages at a time, and can explain what caused what. **Pick material that makes you work, then check you can sum it up afterwards.**',doToday:'Read one page of a hard book or article you\u2019ve been avoiding, then write a one-sentence summary of it.'},card:{insight:'Easy books are fun, but only hard books make you think.',tryThis:'Tonight, read one hard page. Then close the book and write down what it meant.',skills:['thinking','focus']},review:{prompt:'You have one hour to learn about money. Which book should you pick?',right:'A hard book you can explain to a friend',wrong:'A fun, easy read you can finish fast',why:'Hard reading makes you think, and the thinking is what stays.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two kids want **stronger legs**. One rides a bike downhill every day. The other pedals **up the hill**, puffing all the way. Which one\u2019s legs will be stronger by the summer?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Minds work like legs. Arnold Bennett wrote novels for a living. Yet he said your study time **isn\u2019t for novels**. They\u2019re not bad. They just **go down too easily** to make you think hard.'},{kind:'moral',text:'If you want your mind to grow, pick things that are **a bit hard**. Easy things are fun. But they won\u2019t make you **any stronger**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two friends each read three evenings a week for **a whole year**. One gets through forty easy books. The other works slowly through **one serious history**. Ask each what they learned. Who has more to say?'},{kind:'story',text:'The first remembers enjoying most of the forty. The second can lay out a chain of causes, chapter by chapter. Both spent the same evenings. Only one of them came out of the year changed.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Bennett\u2019s reason is that the ninety minutes are for **the effort**, not the content. Pleasant reading fills the time without training anything. So he points you to subjects with **structure**: the causes of things, a serious history, a difficult poet.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Structure helps because each part depends on the last, so your mind has something to hold. And it gives you **a simple test**. Could you sum up what you did afterwards? If you can\u2019t, it **left nothing behind**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Choose **the book that resists** you. Ease feels like progress but builds nothing. The hard chapter leaves **something you can state**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Arnold Bennett made his name **writing novels**. So when he turns to how the reclaimed evenings should be spent, you\u2019d expect him to recommend them. He **rules them out**. Why would a novelist do that?'},{kind:'explain',text:'It isn\u2019t snobbery about fiction. Novels **go down too easily**, so they leave little behind. The ninety minutes are spent to **build a capacity**, and material that asks nothing of you builds nothing.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture Ravi, who spends his evenings on light reading. By December he has read a great deal. Ask him what changed in his thinking and he struggles. He consumed plenty, and changed in no respect he can name.'},{kind:'story',text:'His friend Lena picks a serious history of her city and reads it slowly. Some evenings she manages ten pages. But each week she can explain why one thing led to another. That residue is the point.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Difficulty isn\u2019t the same as value. Some hard books are obscure for no good reason. And a demanding novel can train attention as well as **a history**. The real test is effort that leaves **something statable**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t choose by genre. Choose by whether the material **makes you work** and leaves you able to say what you learned. If it does, **it\u2019s worth the evening**.'}]},'how-to-live-on-24-hours_9':{title:'The Conduct Audit',simple:{headline:'Check if your days match what matters to you',body:'Arnold Bennett asked readers to look back at what they had done and what came of it. You tell people that family comes first. Then you look at last month\u2019s evenings, and most went on email and late meetings. **Put what you did next to what you say matters, and look at the gap.** He wanted this done calmly, like routine upkeep, not as a trial. Once you see the gap, you can make a small change, like moving two evenings back home.',doToday:'Write two things you say matter most to you, and beside each, the hours you gave it this week.'},card:{insight:'Your words say what matters, but your hours show what you care about.',tryThis:'Tonight, on your way home, check if your day matched what matters most.',skills:['thinking','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'You say your health matters most. How can you check if that is true?',right:'Check how you spent your hours last week',wrong:'Tell yourself again how much you care about it',why:'Only what you did shows what you care about most.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You say being kind **matters to you**. On Monday you laughed when a friend tripped. On Tuesday you helped someone with homework. Which tells you more: what you say, or **what you did**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A mechanic doesn\u2019t guess why a bike squeaks. She checks **each part**, one at a time. Arnold Bennett said to do this with **your own week**. Look at what you did, and what came of it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Every so often, look back at **what you did** and what happened next. Then check whether it matches **what matters** to you. It\u2019s not about feeling bad.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Name **three things** you value. Now name three things you actually did last week. Put the two lists side by side. How well do they **line up**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Bennett asked for part of the reclaimed time to go on **your own conduct**. What did you do? What followed from it? Do your behaviour and your principles have the same shape? He treated this as **the foundation**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Why the foundation? Self-improvement by someone who\u2019s never looked at their actual conduct is **aimed blindly**. The direction was chosen without evidence. You can\u2019t fix a gap you\u2019ve **never compared**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'It\u2019s **an audit**, not a confession. You set your stated priority against **the record**.',left:{title:'Not this',items:['Penance: hunting for guilt','Reassurance: hunting for comfort']},right:{title:'This',items:['Priorities set against hours spent','Routine maintenance, done calmly']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Your beliefs describe what you intend. Only **the record** of your hours shows what you do. So check **your conduct** against your values, calmly and often.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Ask someone about their beliefs and they\u2019ll talk for **an hour**. Ask what they actually did last Tuesday, hour by hour, and most people **go quiet**. Why is the second question so much harder?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Bennett asked you to keep noticing **cause and effect** in your own life. It\u2019s his most demanding idea. The gap between belief and conduct is **invisible from belief**. It only shows when you set the record beside it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Bennett recommends Marcus Aurelius and Epictetus by name, two Stoic thinkers of the Roman world. The Stoics reviewed their days. His version is calmer than penance. It\u2019s an audit, done as **routine maintenance**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Jo, who says family comes first. She logs one month of evenings. Most went on email and late meetings. She doesn\u2019t punish herself. She simply moves two evenings back to home.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Reviewing yourself can tip into **rumination**, and the audit becomes a nightly trial. Cause and effect in a life is also messy. One week\u2019s record can mislead you, so wait a month before judging.'},{kind:'moral',text:'What you believe is easy to describe. What you do needs **a record**. Set the two side by side, calmly, and you\u2019ll see **the real gap**.'}]}}}},964,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'identity-not-outcomes',kind:'book',promise:'Set goals that last past the finish line',about:'What happens the day after you reach your goal? This article says goals built on a number feel like failing most of the time. Then they just end. These ideas show a better way. What will you still be doing next month?',ideas:{'identity-not-outcomes_1':{title:'Behind Until the Last Day',card:{insight:'A goal like a whole book says you are behind until the last day.',tryThis:'Pick one big goal. Add a small part you can finish before bed, like reading ten pages.',skills:['habits','psychology']},review:{prompt:'It is March. You have read six books, and your goal is thirty. What is wrong?',right:'The goal only says you did well on its last day',wrong:'You read too slowly and need to try much harder',why:'You keep going when you see progress, and this goal hides it until the end.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You plan to read **thirty books** this year. It\u2019s the end of March, and you\u2019ve read six. That\u2019s a lot of reading. So why does the goal make you feel **behind**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A goal like thirty books says **not yet** almost all year. It only says **well done** once, on the very last day. But you keep going when you can **see progress**, and this goal hardly ever shows you any.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A big goal is good at **pointing the way**. It\u2019s poor at getting you through **each day**. So add something small you can finish today.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You decide to **write a book** this year, and you write most evenings. Every night the goal reports the same thing: not done. It will say that on **364 days**. How long will you keep going?'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Dami, who aims to run **1,000 km** this year. By March she\u2019s running four times a week, yet she\u2019s felt behind since February. The running is real. The target can only tell her she hasn\u2019t done enough.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s the problem. You keep going on **progress signals**, and an outcome goal gives almost none. It\u2019s unmet for its whole length and met at a single point. Its one **good report** arrives when the work is over.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Outcome goals aren\u2019t useless. They\u2019re just built for **one job**, not **the other**.',left:{title:'Good at',items:['Setting a direction','Judging afterwards if it was worth it']},right:{title:'Poor at',items:['Keeping you going daily','Telling you today went well']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t throw out the big goal. Keep it for **direction**, and give each day **something to win**. Then the long middle stops feeling like failure.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Think about the **shape** of a goal, not what it\u2019s about. Thirty books, ten kilos, a finished novel. Each one is unmet for months, then met in a moment. Is that **a good shape** for keeping you going?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The article\u2019s point is **structural**, not moral. An outcome goal is failed until it\u2019s over. Motivation draws on **progress signals**, and this design supplies none until the end. That\u2019s exactly when you need them least.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a reader with a target of thirty books. By June she\u2019s read twelve, which is real reading. But measured against thirty, every one of those months has been **a shortfall**. The good news waits for December.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take someone aiming to lose ten kilos. Each week she eats better and moves more, and the scales slowly drift down. Yet measured against ten kilos, she falls short on every day until the very last.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Outcome goals still **set direction**, and they let you judge afterwards whether it was worth it. You can also split a far goal into milestones that report along the way. The claim is narrower than it sounds.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A single distant outcome is **a poor engine** for daily work. Use it to point and to judge. Let something you can **win today** carry you through the middle.'}]},'identity-not-outcomes_2':{title:'The Day After the Goal',card:{insight:'What you do the day after you reach a goal shows what it was for.',tryThis:'Pick a goal. Write what you want to still be doing a month after you reach it.',skills:['habits','thinking']},review:{prompt:'Two friends finish the same big race. One runs again that week. What does that show?',right:'For her, the running was the point, not the race',wrong:'She just trained harder, so she had more energy left',why:'If the running stops at the finish, the race was the whole goal.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two friends practise **penalties** every day before the cup final. The final comes and goes. A week later, one is out kicking a ball as usual. The other **never practises** again. Which one are you more like?'},{kind:'explain',text:'For the second friend, the **cup** was the point. Once it was over, the practice had **no reason** left. The first friend just likes playing. For her, the final was one day inside something she\u2019ll keep doing.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Watch what you do **after** you reach a goal. If you stop, the goal was the point. If you **carry on**, you\u2019ve found something worth doing.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two people cross the same **finishing line**, with the same training and the same medal. A year later, one runs most mornings. The other hasn\u2019t run since. What was **different** on race day?'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Tom, who trained for a marathon for six months. Early alarms, long Sundays, a careful plan. He finished, felt proud and put his shoes away. Within a month, the running had stopped completely.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Tom\u2019s running was **scaffolding** for the race. A target has **its end built in**, and everything organised around it inherits that end. So once you cross the line, the plan, the alarms and the habit all finish too.'},{kind:'explain',text:'You don\u2019t have to wait for the finish to know which kind of goal you have. Before you start, ask two things. What will you **still be doing** afterwards? And does the behaviour have **a reason** to continue once the target\u2019s gone?'},{kind:'moral',text:'A goal that ends was pointing at **the number**. Behaviour that continues was pointing at **a way of living**. So set the goal on what you want to keep doing.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Plenty of people **stop completely** just after a big success. The diet ends the week the weight comes off. The runner stops after the marathon. Why would effort **vanish** at the exact moment it\u2019s paid off?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The article\u2019s answer is that a target ends **by construction**. Everything organised around it inherits that end. So the revealing question about any goal is **the day after**. If the behaviour stops, it was scaffolding for the number.'},{kind:'story',text:'Two people finish the same marathon. One stops running within a month. The other is out again that week. She\u2019d already become **a runner**, and the race was one morning in that life.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture someone preparing for a language exam in June. If the goal is to pass, the study can stop the day results arrive. If the goal is to speak the language, you\u2019d expect June to be **a waypoint**, not the end.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Not every goal should continue. Someone who runs **one marathon** for a cause and then stops hasn\u2019t failed. The race was the point. The test shows what a goal was for, not what it should be for.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Before you chase a target, picture **the morning after** you reach it. If nothing would carry on, ask whether you\u2019re aiming at **the right thing**.'}]},'identity-not-outcomes_3':{title:'The Daily Win',card:{insight:'Set a goal as something you do, and you can meet it today.',tryThis:'Turn one goal into a thing you do, like a short walk. Then do it today.',skills:['habits','productivity']},review:{prompt:'You want to get fit this year. Which goal can you meet today?',right:'Take a short walk today',wrong:'Lose twenty pounds by summer',why:'A walk is done by tonight, and it is all up to you.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You want to play a whole song on the piano by the **summer concert**. It\u2019s April, and the song still sounds **wobbly**. How do you feel each time you sit down to practise?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Try a different goal: **play for ten minutes** after school. Each day, you did it or you didn\u2019t. You can **win that today**. And bit by bit, the song comes anyway, without you chasing it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A goal about **doing something** can be won every day. A goal about **the result** can only be won once. Pick the one you can win today.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You set a goal to **lose ten kilos** by summer. You eat well all week, then the scales don\u2019t move. Did you **fail this week**, or did the goal just ask the wrong question?'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Lena, who swaps her target for a process: **walk for twenty minutes** before dinner. By nine o\u2019clock each evening, she knows whether she did it. Most days she did. Her weight starts falling anyway, as a side effect.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'A process goal changes **the feedback** and **the control** you have.',left:{title:'Process goal',items:['Met or missed by tonight','Fully within your power','Feedback every day']},right:{title:'Outcome goal',items:['Met only at the end','Depends on luck and others','Feedback once']}},{kind:'explain',text:'There\u2019s a catch. A process can **drift** from the outcome it was meant to serve. Thirty minutes a day of the wrong exercise is faithfully done and useless. So check the process against **the result** now and then.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Hold yourself to **what you do**, not what you get. You can meet that today. The result usually follows **as a consequence**, not as a target.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Practising Spanish for **fifteen minutes** is met or missed by tonight. Being fluent by next summer isn\u2019t met for months. It\u2019s one ambition in **two shapes**. Which would you rather live with all year?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A process goal is defined by **action**, not result. That gives it two advantages. It reports success daily, which an outcome goal can\u2019t. And it\u2019s **fully yours**, while the outcome depends on luck, markets, bodies and other people.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Marta, learning Spanish for a move abroad. Her target was fluency by summer, and every week she felt behind. She switched to fifteen minutes a day. Now each evening ends with **a small win**, and the words keep coming.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a salesperson with a quarterly target. Much of it depends on budgets and timing she can\u2019t touch. So a bad quarter brings gloom but **no information**. The calls she makes each day are hers to count.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. A process can **drift** from the outcome it was chosen to serve. An hour of scales every day, never trying a real piece, is faithfully done and useless. So you still review the process against the result.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Run the work on **a process** you can meet today. Let the outcome be **a consequence**, checked now and then. That\u2019s where the big goal still earns its place.'}]},'identity-not-outcomes_4':{title:'Proof of Who You Are',card:{insight:'Say who you are becoming, and each small act is proof it is true.',tryThis:'Finish the sentence I am someone who. Then do one small thing today to prove it.',skills:['habits','psychology']},review:{prompt:'You want to cook more at home. Which way of saying it helps most?',right:'I am someone who cooks, so I will make dinner tonight',wrong:'I will cook a hundred meals at home this year',why:'If you are someone who cooks, each dinner is proof it is true.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two friends want to read more. One promises to read **thirty books**. The other says she\u2019s **a reader**. Then both have a busy month and read nothing. Which one do you think keeps going?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The first friend is now behind. The second is a reader who had **a busy month**. Every time she opens a book, it\u2019s **a small clue**: *I really am a reader.* The clues add up until you believe them.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Say **who you\u2019re becoming**, not what you\u2019ll have. But do the thing first, even a tiny bit. Then the words **feel true**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You decide to **cook more** at home. You could aim for a hundred home-cooked meals this year. Or you could say you\u2019re becoming **someone who cooks**. Does the wording really change anything?'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Sara, who wants to cook more. Under a target, Tuesday\u2019s takeaway is a meal lost from the hundred. Under an identity, Wednesday\u2019s dinner is one more piece of evidence. Same kitchen, same week, a different meaning.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The same dinner means something **different** under each framing, and that changes **what it does**.',left:{title:'Under a target',items:['A step towards a far total','Wasted if you miss it']},right:{title:'Under an identity',items:['Evidence of something already true','Counts on its own']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Why does it work? People tend to act in line with **how they describe** themselves. And the description is updated by what they do, not what they intend. So the order matters: **behaviour first**, the label second.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Say **who you\u2019re becoming**, then give it evidence. Each small action makes the claim easier to believe. Without the action, the claim **won\u2019t last**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'*I\u2019m not a maths person.* You\u2019ve probably heard someone say it, or said it yourself. Notice what that sentence does: it predicts **what you\u2019ll try** and **what you\u2019ll avoid**. Could the same force work for you?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The article\u2019s claim is about **what each action means**. Under a target, a run is a step towards something not yet done. Under an identity, it\u2019s **evidence** of something already true. People tend to act in line with how they describe themselves.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture two colleagues in April, after a bad fortnight with no reading. One set a target of thirty books, so she\u2019s now behind. The other described herself as a reader. She\u2019s simply **a reader** who had a bad fortnight.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take someone who announces he\u2019s a writer, then writes nothing for a month. The claim has no evidence under it, and that feels uncomfortable. So he quietly stops saying it. The label went first, and **the label lost**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. An identity can **harden**. A runner may keep running through injury to protect the label, or feel lost when forced to stop. And some goals really are just tasks, with no person to become.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Act first, then **describe yourself** by what you\u2019ve done. Each action becomes **evidence**, and evidence is what makes a label believable. Just don\u2019t let the label outrank the goal.'}]},'identity-not-outcomes_5':{title:'When Numbers Belong',card:{insight:'Use numbers to check your progress, not to push you each day.',tryThis:'Keep a number goal only for things with a real end date, like a test. Turn the rest into habits.',skills:['productivity','habits']},review:{prompt:'You save the same amount each month. How often should you check the balance?',right:'Once a month, to check that your plan still works',wrong:'Every day, so you know just where you stand',why:'A number jumps around each day, but once a month it shows where it is going.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Friday is the **spelling test**. It happens whatever you do, and then it\u2019s over. Your goal is to get **18 out of 20**. Is a number like that a good goal here?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Yes, because the test has **a real date** and a real end. Number goals fit things like that. But reading or **playing music** is meant to keep going. For those, a number goal is the wrong shape.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Numbers are great for things that **truly end**, like a test or a race. For things you want to keep doing, use them to **check progress**, not to push you.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A writer checks her **word count** every evening. Some days it jumps. Some days she cuts a chapter and it falls. Each evening feels like **a verdict**. Is the number helping her write, or getting in the way?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Outcome targets work when the deadline is **genuinely external** and the task genuinely ends: an exam, a race, a launch. The trouble starts when you give that shape to things meant to continue. That\u2019s **most of life**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Numbers do **two jobs**, and they\u2019re only good at **one** of them.',left:{title:'As measurement',items:['Shows if the process works','Catches a routine going wrong']},right:{title:'As motivation',items:['Unmet until the end','Largely outside your control']}},{kind:'story',text:'So the writer changes **the rhythm**. She writes every day, which is **the process**, and checks the total once a month. Daily, the number was noise. Monthly, it shows **the real trend**, and her evenings go back to writing.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Run your days on **process and identity**. Review the numbers on **a slow cycle**, as a check, not a scoreboard. Without that check, a faithful routine can run the wrong way for a year.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve started walking every day. It\u2019s going well. Then you weigh yourself **every morning**. Some days the number rises, some days it barely moves. Why does **watching closely** make you feel worse?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The article separates **measurement** from **motivation**. As motivation, an outcome is unmet until the end, largely outside your control and discouraging meanwhile. As measurement, it\u2019s indispensable. It tells you whether a faithfully kept process still points the right way.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take the daily weigh-in. Day to day the number wobbles for small reasons, so each morning feels like **a judgement**. Checked once a month, the same scale shows the trend. Same number, slower cycle, real signal.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a team launching a product on a fixed date. Here a number earns its place. The deadline is set from outside, and **the task really ends**. Hitting the date is the whole point.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The right cycle depends on how fast a number moves and how noisy it is. Some people find **frequent checks** useful. And many real deadlines sit inside things that continue, as an exam sits inside learning a subject.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Keep outcome targets for things with **a real deadline** that truly end. For everything else, run on process and review the numbers **slowly**. That way they\u2019ll guide you instead of grading you.'}]}}}},965,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'interpretation-of-dreams',kind:'book',promise:'See the hidden reasons behind what you do',about:'Do you know why you do what you do? In 1899, Sigmund Freud wrote a book about dreams. He said much of your mind works out of sight. Many of his ideas failed, but that one held up. Want to see it?',ideas:{'interpretation-of-dreams_1':{title:'Below the Waterline',card:{insight:'Much of your mind works out of sight, and it still helps you choose.',tryThis:'Before a big choice, write down one more reason you might want it.',skills:['psychology','thinking']},review:{prompt:'A friend says Freud was wrong about everything. What do you say?',right:'He got a lot wrong, but the mind does work out of sight',wrong:'Agree, since an idea with wrong parts is wrong all the way',why:'He can be right that it is there and wrong about how it works.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re walking home, humming a song. You don\u2019t know why. Then it clicks: it was playing in a shop an hour ago. **Who chose** that song? Not you. So **what did**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Think of a **swan** on a pond. On top, it glides and looks calm. Under the water, its feet paddle hard. You only see the top half. Freud said much of your mind is like that: **busy out of sight**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A lot of your mind **works out of view**. It still steers what you do. So when you give a reason, remember there may be **more you can\u2019t see**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You picked one flat over three others. Asked why, you say instantly: **the light**. It sounds true. But the same mind that chose also wrote the reason afterwards. How far should you **trust that answer**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Freud, a doctor in Vienna, published his dream book at the end of 1899. Its headline was that much of mental life happens out of view and steers behaviour quietly. Others had said it before. He made it impossible to ignore.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Most of his **details** fared badly. The **big claim** held up, found again by other methods.',left:{title:'Held up',items:['Much of the mind works unseen','It shapes choices and feelings']},right:{title:'Fared badly',items:['Every dream fulfils a wish','A fixed code of symbols']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Later, psychologists reached the hidden mind through **timed tests**, not dreams. People confidently explained choices they hadn\u2019t actually made. Their picture has no buried wishes. It kept the claim and **dropped the details**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Being right that something **exists** is different from being right about **how it works**. Judge the two apart. Your own reasons deserve the same doubt.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Vienna, 1899. A doctor publishes a book about dreams. It sells slowly at first. Today its vocabulary is **everywhere**, while most of its machinery lies **in ruins**. How can one book be both?'},{kind:'explain',text:'On one sound claim, Freud built **a specific machine**. Every dream fulfils a wish. A censor disguises it. The disguise follows a code an analyst can learn. These **particulars** are what fared badly under evidence.'},{kind:'story',text:'You tell a friend you turned down a job because of the commute. It\u2019s a good reason. But you also disliked the interviewer, and you haven\u2019t mentioned that, even to yourself. Both may have voted.'},{kind:'story',text:'Cognitive science came at it from the other side. Reaction times. Priming. Experiments where people gave confident reasons for choices they hadn\u2019t made. The result had no wishes and no censor, yet it found the hidden mind.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The claim wasn\u2019t Freud\u2019s alone, so calling him its discoverer overstates it. And his words smuggle his machine in. Calling a memory *repressed* **asserts a cause**, not just hidden processing.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Freud was right that **much of the mind** works unseen, and wrong about most of how. Keep the claim. Test **each mechanism** on its own.'}]},'interpretation-of-dreams_2':{title:'The Reason Behind the Reason',card:{insight:'The reason you hear first may not be the one that made the choice.',tryThis:'If a reason sounds odd, ask the person what else is under it.',skills:['thinking','psychology']},review:{prompt:'Your project gets cut for money, but a costlier one goes ahead. What now?',right:'Ask what else made the choice, besides the money reason',wrong:'Take the money reason as true and spend less next time',why:'The reason you are told is only the top layer.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You wake up from a dream about a **giant purple fish** in your school hall. Your friend has a dream book. It says a fish means money. Is that really **what your dream meant**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Freud said a dream has **two layers**. There\u2019s the dream you remember. Then there\u2019s the stuff it was **built from**: your own worries and memories. A dream book can\u2019t know yours. Only you can.'},{kind:'moral',text:'What you see first is **the top layer**. What made it can be **something else**. So when a reason sounds odd, look for the one underneath.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A project you led is **quietly shelved**. The official reason is budget. The same month, a costlier project with a more senior sponsor **sails through**. What do you make of that?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Freud split every dream in two. The **manifest content** is the dream as you remember it. The **latent content** is the material it was built from. The remembered dream is a product, not plain evidence.'},{kind:'explain',text:'That\u2019s why he mocked **dream dictionaries**. If a symbol meant the same for everyone, no analysis would be needed. His method depends on meaning that\u2019s **specific to the dreamer**, worked out backwards from their own material.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Apply the split to the shelved project: **what was said** versus **what decided**.',left:{title:'Stated reason',items:['Budget was tight','Priorities changed']},right:{title:'What may decide',items:['Whose sponsor carries weight','Who was in the room']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Budget is what was said. It may **not be what decided**. Whenever a stated reason and the **actual reason** could differ, treat the report as a product and work backwards.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'You remember a dream of a **locked drawer**. You could look it up in a dream book. Or you could ask what it brings to *your* mind. Freud bet everything on **the second route**. Why?'},{kind:'explain',text:'His central split is between the **manifest content**, the dream recalled, and the **latent content** behind it. The dream-work turns the second into the first. Interpreting means running that transformation backwards.'},{kind:'story',text:'You ask a colleague why she left the team. She says the commute. It\u2019s true, and it\u2019s tidy. Over coffee months later, she mentions the manager who never once asked her opinion.'},{kind:'story',text:'A council says a library closed for low footfall. Visits had in fact risen that year. The land it stood on had just been valued. Footfall is what was printed. Something else may have decided.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The theory beneath, that the latent content is **a disguised wish**, is disputed and largely unsupported. And later editions added fairly fixed symbols, close to the dream books he\u2019d mocked.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Whatever happens to his theory, the structure holds. A report is **a product**, not raw evidence. So read stated reasons **backwards**, toward what produced them.'}]},'interpretation-of-dreams_3':{title:'After the Neat Story',card:{insight:'People tell the easy part first, so the hard part comes out last.',tryThis:'Tonight, when someone says \u201cfine,\u201d ask one more question, then wait a bit.',skills:['thinking','psychology']},review:{prompt:'After a big mistake at work, one person tells a neat story. What next?',right:'Keep listening past the neat story for the parts left out',wrong:'Write down the cause they gave and end the review',why:'Most people skip the hard parts at first, so keep on asking.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You ask a friend about their holiday. They give you the **neat version**: sun, food, a boat trip. An hour later, chatting, they mention **the big argument**. Why didn\u2019t that come first?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Freud had a rule for his patients: say **everything** that comes to mind, even the silly or embarrassing bits. Those were often the bits that mattered. Most of us **trim our stories** without noticing.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The first story you hear is **the tidy one**. The important part often comes later. So **keep listening**, even after it seems finished.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A delivery went badly wrong. At the review, the one person who saw it gives a **clear five-minute account**. Everyone nods and writes down the cause. Was that **the whole story**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Freud\u2019s method was **free association**. The patient had to say everything that came to mind, especially what seemed trivial or embarrassing. His reasoning: what you\u2019re looking for is exactly what **ordinary filters** exclude.'},{kind:'story',text:'So he sat with one person for hours and treated their words as data. One dream could fill several sessions. He was honest about the cost. There\u2019s no short version, because a shortcut brings back the filtering.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The account at the review was honest. It was also **prepared**, and preparing is **a kind of filtering**.',left:{title:'Prepared account',items:['Five clear minutes','The cause, neatly stated']},right:{title:'Past it',items:['The detail that seemed irrelevant','What you\u2019d rather skip']}},{kind:'moral',text:'The filter works **before anyone decides** to hold back. Nobody has to be hiding anything. So keep listening **past the prepared account**, because length is the only reliable correction.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Before he listened, Freud tried other ways in. He used **hypnosis**. He pressed on patients\u2019 foreheads to summon memories. Then he settled on something slower: **letting them talk**. Why would slower work better?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The instruction was radical: report everything, including what seems **irrelevant**, and especially that. Any method that lets you **choose what to report** will systematically miss the material that filters exclude.'},{kind:'story',text:'In the dream book he turns the method on dreams. Each fragment becomes a starting point for the dreamer\u2019s own associations. One dream could take several sessions. He admits it plainly. There is no short version.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take your own team\u2019s retrospective. Each person brings a tidy summary written the night before. You could close after the summaries. Or you could ask for twenty more minutes, and hear the thing nobody had planned to say.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Doctors already took histories, and Josef Breuer had let a patient talk through her symptoms years before. And listening long doesn\u2019t make the listener **neutral**. Interpretation still decides what it all means.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Every self-report comes **pre-filtered**, before anyone chooses to hide a thing. Length is the fix. Keep going **past the tidy version**, and remember the listener still shapes the meaning.'}]},'interpretation-of-dreams_4':{title:'The Theory That Can\u2019t Lose',card:{insight:'An idea that no result could prove wrong is not being tested.',tryThis:'Next time you feel sure, write down one thing that would prove you wrong.',skills:['thinking','science']},review:{prompt:'Your boss says wins come from his plan and losses from the team. What now?',right:'Ask what result could ever show the plan was wrong',wrong:'Trust it, since his plan fits every result so well',why:'If no result could prove an idea wrong, it is not being tested.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You shoot an arrow at a fence. Then you walk over and **paint a target** round where it landed. Every shot is a bull\u2019s-eye. So are you **a great archer**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Some of Freud\u2019s ideas worked like that target. If a patient agreed with him, he was right. If they disagreed, that was **resistance**, so he was still right. He **couldn\u2019t lose**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'An idea that **can\u2019t lose** isn\u2019t strong. It just isn\u2019t risking anything. So ask yourself what would **prove it wrong**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your manager explains every success by **his strategy**. Every failure, he says, came from poor execution of it. He sounds right every single time. Is that **a good sign**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Freud\u2019s method had the same shape. If the patient agreed with an interpretation, it was confirmed. If they disagreed, that was **resistance**, which also confirmed it. **No answer** could ever count against him.'},{kind:'story',text:'The philosopher Karl Popper used this as his central example. Einstein\u2019s theory predicted something that could have come out wrong. Freud\u2019s could explain any behaviour at all. Its followers saw strength. Popper saw the flaw.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'A real theory **forbids** some outcomes. One that can\u2019t lose **forbids nothing**.',left:{title:'Real theory',items:['Names a result that would sink it','Gains trust by risking it']},right:{title:'Can\u2019t lose',items:['Every result fits','Feels like always being right']}},{kind:'moral',text:'A theory that can\u2019t lose has **stopped making claims**. So ask of any confident explanation, including yours: what result would **show it was wrong**? If nothing would, it\u2019s a belief.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture a theory **built so carefully** that every possible result supports it. Yes confirms it. No confirms it too. It has never once been wrong. Should that make you **trust it more**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The structure is simple. Agreement **confirms** the interpretation. Disagreement is resistance, which **also confirms** it. A theory arranged so nothing counts against it isn\u2019t strong. It has quietly stopped making claims.'},{kind:'story',text:'Karl Popper set Freud\u2019s psychology against Einstein\u2019s physics. Einstein predicted something that could have failed, and so earned trust by risking it. The psychology could explain any behaviour at all. Popper called that the flaw.'},{kind:'story',text:'Your manager credits every success to his strategy and every failure to how it was carried out. No result could ever reach the strategy. From inside, it feels like being right about everything.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Freud did answer this: late in life he said confirmation came from what the patient produced afterwards. And Adolf Gr\xfcnbaum argued some Freudian claims **are testable**, and fail. That\u2019s harsher, not kinder.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Before trusting a confident explanation, ask what would **prove it wrong**. If the honest answer is nothing, it\u2019s doing **something other** than explaining.'}]},'interpretation-of-dreams_5':{title:'The Telling Slip',card:{insight:'One slip can be luck, but the same slip again is a clue.',tryThis:'Today, write down one slip you keep making, and when it happens.',skills:['psychology','thinking']},review:{prompt:'You forgot to send the same report three times. What do you do?',right:'Ask why the slip keeps landing on this one report',wrong:'Set a louder alarm and try harder next time',why:'A slip that keeps coming back around one thing is a clue.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Tuesday is swimming day. For the third week running, you **leave your swim bag** at home. You never forget your football boots on Thursday. Is that **just bad luck**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Freud thought **slips aren\u2019t random**. Forgetting a name or saying the wrong word can point to something you **didn\u2019t want to say**. Maybe you don\u2019t enjoy swimming much. Your forgetting may be telling you.'},{kind:'moral',text:'One mistake can be **just tiredness**. The same mistake, again and again, is **a clue**. Notice where your slips keep landing.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve forgotten to send **the same report** three times. Each time you set a new reminder. Each time something else came up. Have you asked why it\u2019s **always this one**?'},{kind:'story',text:'On a journey in the late 1890s, Freud couldn\u2019t recall the name of the painter Signorelli. Other painters\u2019 names kept arriving instead. He traced the lapse to a subject he\u2019d just pushed out of a conversation: death.'},{kind:'explain',text:'His claim, developed mostly in a later book, was that slips are **not noise**. They happen at particular moments, around particular people. Even when one slip means nothing, **the pattern** is often readable.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Read the **forgotten report** two ways, and only one of them **tells you anything**.',left:{title:'As noise',items:['Set another reminder','Blame a busy week']},right:{title:'As data',items:['Ask why this report','Ask why this person']}},{kind:'moral',text:'A single slip may just be **tiredness**. A slip that keeps returning to one subject is **information** about that subject. You don\u2019t need Freud\u2019s mechanism to use it.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A traveller in the 1890s can\u2019t recall a famous painter\u2019s name. Wrong names keep arriving instead. It\u2019s **a tiny lapse**, the kind you\u2019d shrug off. Why would anyone treat it as **evidence**?'},{kind:'story',text:'The traveller was Freud, and the painter Signorelli. He traced the lapse to death, a subject he\u2019d just steered away from in conversation. He later made it the opening case of a whole book on everyday errors.'},{kind:'explain',text:'His argument is that slips are **overdetermined**, not random. A forgotten name, a misspoken word, a lost object: each is produced by something. Usually it\u2019s **an intention** that was never going to be stated.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now you. You keep mistyping one client\u2019s name in emails, always the one whose contract you privately think is unfair. Any single typo means nothing. Ten of them, around one person, might be telling you something.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Freud found hidden intentions almost everywhere, and **wasn\u2019t entitled** to many of his readings. Students of speech errors explain lots of slips by similar sounds. Fatigue explains plenty more.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t read **every slip**. Read the ones that **cluster**. A repeated error around one subject is honest information, often more honest than your intentions.'}]},'interpretation-of-dreams_6':{title:'The Filing Cabinet Dream',card:{insight:'When a small thing makes you very mad, look for a bigger worry.',tryThis:'When a small thing makes you mad, write down one bigger worry from that day.',skills:['psychology','thinking']},review:{prompt:'You snap at a coworker over one tiny typo. What do you check?',right:'What bigger worry the feeling might have come from',wrong:'Whether the typo was as bad as it felt',why:'A big feeling can move off a big worry onto a small thing.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You dream about your teacher. But she has your aunt\u2019s voice and your coach\u2019s whistle. She\u2019s **three people at once**. In the dream, it all seemed **perfectly normal**. How does that happen?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Freud said dreams do **two tricks**. They squash several people or things into one. And they move a strong feeling onto **something small**. That\u2019s why a dream about a pencil can feel scary.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Sometimes a feeling is about **something bigger**. It just landed on a small thing. When you\u2019re upset over a tiny thing, ask **where it came from**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You wake up shaken. You dreamt about **a filing cabinet**. Nothing happened in the dream. It was just a filing cabinet. Yet it\u2019s the most **upsetting thing** all week. Why?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Freud named two operations. **Condensation** fuses several things into one image, like a figure who\u2019s both your colleague and your brother. **Displacement** moves feeling off what matters onto something small and nearby.'},{kind:'story',text:'A couple argues about the bins. Within minutes it covers the holiday, the in-laws and last year\u2019s missed birthday. Three grievances have arrived fused into one complaint. The biggest one, the move abroad, never comes up.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'You can use both ideas without believing **anything about dreams**. They describe how people **talk about hard things**.',left:{title:'Condensation',items:['Several grievances, one complaint','The bins carry everything']},right:{title:'Displacement',items:['Big feeling, small target','The real issue unmentioned']}},{kind:'moral',text:'When a small thing produces **a large reaction**, the feeling may have **travelled** from somewhere bigger. Look for the source. Arguing about the bins won\u2019t settle it.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'In a dream, a stranger has your colleague\u2019s face and your brother\u2019s laugh. In another, a lost umbrella leaves you **in tears**. To Freud, neither was strange. Both were **signatures**. Of what?'},{kind:'explain',text:'He called them the two operations of **the dream-work**. Condensation packs several things into one image. Displacement shifts emotional weight off what matters onto something trivial. The strangeness is **the signature** of the compression.'},{kind:'story',text:'A manager is furious about a meeting room booked twice. The team is baffled by the heat of it. Months later it comes out: the same week, her promotion had been quietly withdrawn.'},{kind:'story',text:'Or you list complaints about a flatmate: the dishes, the noise, the heating. Each is small. Together they\u2019re one grievance in three outfits, about not feeling **considered**. Answering each separately gets you nowhere.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Used carelessly, both are **licences** to read anything into anything. An interpreter can reach whatever meaning they arrived with. They describe something real only when you can **check the source**, by asking.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Feelings get **packed together** and **moved sideways**. So when a reaction is out of scale, ask where it came from. Then check your guess, rather than trusting it.'}]},'interpretation-of-dreams_7':{title:'Freud\u2019s Own Dream',card:{insight:'Try a hard thing on yourself first, so people know you mean it.',tryThis:'Today, do one thing you tell others to do, and let them see you.',skills:['thinking','leadership']},review:{prompt:'You want your team to share mistakes at a weekly meeting. How do you start?',right:'Share your own mistakes first, even the ones that look bad',wrong:'Pick a clear mistake from someone else to show how it works',why:'Going first shows you will pay the cost you ask of others.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A cook brings you a new dish. She won\u2019t taste it herself. Another cook **tastes hers first**, right in front of you. Which dish would you **rather trust**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Sigmund Freud wrote a famous book about dreams. The first dream he picks apart in full is **his own**, about a patient called Irma. And it doesn\u2019t make him look good. He printed it anyway.'},{kind:'moral',text:'If you want people to try your idea, **try it on yourself** first. Show the **embarrassing bits** too. That\u2019s how they know you mean it.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re about to ask your team to **share their mistakes** in a new weekly review. Everyone is watching to see whose mistakes go first. Whose **should it be**?'},{kind:'story',text:'In July 1895 Freud dreamt of Irma, a patient whose treatment had only partly worked. His analysis showed the dream clearing him of blame and pinning it on a colleague. It was **unflattering**. He published it first.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The choice was **methodological**. He couldn\u2019t show patients\u2019 dreams without breaking confidence. And a stranger\u2019s dream is useless, since **only the dreamer** has the associations. His own was the one specimen with the inside visible.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Freud asked readers to accept nothing he hadn\u2019t **applied to himself**. So in your review, put **your own work** first, unflattering parts included.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A doctor writes a book claiming a new way to read dreams. For his first full example, he could pick any dream at all. He picks **his own**, and it makes him look **rather bad**. Why?'},{kind:'story',text:'The dream came in July 1895. In it, a colleague turns out to have given Irma an injection with an unclean syringe. Freud\u2019s analysis shows the dream acquitting him and blaming others. Then he publishes that.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The reason is **method**, not just courage. The evidence in his approach is the dreamer\u2019s associations. **Only the dreamer** has them, and patients\u2019 dreams were confidential. So he was the one case he could fully show.'},{kind:'story',text:'Say you\u2019re a team lead launching blameless post-mortems. You could open with a junior colleague\u2019s outage. Instead you write up your own worst call from last quarter, the part where you ignored a warning.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Analyst and subject are the same person, so nobody can check his associations. He admits he **left things out**, too. It sets a standard of candour. It proves nothing about dreams.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Going first on yourself doesn\u2019t make you right. It shows you\u2019ll **pay the cost** you\u2019re asking of others. That\u2019s why **the first case** should be yours.'}]},'interpretation-of-dreams_8':{title:'Yesterday\u2019s Leftovers',card:{insight:'In dreams and in fights, a dull thing can carry a big worry.',tryThis:'When you wake, write one dull bit from yesterday that was in your dream.',skills:['psychology','thinking']},review:{prompt:'A fight about one dirty cup goes on for an hour. What helps?',right:'Ask what worry the cup is carrying that went unsaid',wrong:'Decide once and for all who washes the cups',why:'A small fight can carry a big worry, so the cup will not fix it.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Last night you dreamt about **a red umbrella** you saw on the bus. You barely noticed it at the time. Of all the things that happened yesterday, why did your dream **pick that**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Carry a present in shiny paper and everyone asks what it is. Carry it in **a plain bag** and nobody looks. Freud thought dreams use **dull bits** of yesterday the same way, to carry something quietly.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Dreams are often built from **yesterday\u2019s leftovers**. Freud thought the boring bits were **a disguise**. In his view, that\u2019s why the dull umbrella got in.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'There\u2019s **an unwashed cup** by the sink. Within a minute you\u2019re in a row about who does everything in this house. It runs an hour. Was it **ever about the cup**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Freud noticed dreams are built from **the day\u2019s residue**: a face on a train, a newspaper phrase. He asked why the **trivial material** gets picked when the day held things that mattered.'},{kind:'explain',text:'His answer: trivial fragments **pass without objection**. They carry no charge of their own, so they can carry something that does. A dream built from big events would announce its subject. One built from rubbish **slips through**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When **a trivial occasion** sparks a big row, it\u2019s probably carrying something that couldn\u2019t be **raised directly**. Arguing about the cup won\u2019t resolve it. Ask what you\u2019re really arguing about.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A face glimpsed on a train. A phrase from a newspaper. Freud kept finding these **leftovers** in dreams, while the day\u2019s **real events** often stayed out. If you were hiding something, which would you use?'},{kind:'explain',text:'He called it **the day\u2019s residue**. His claim was that unimportant fragments get chosen because they **pass unremarked**. Carrying no charge of their own, they\u2019re free to carry something older that does.'},{kind:'story',text:'You dream of a stranger\u2019s green scarf from yesterday\u2019s bus. Nothing about the scarf mattered. On his account, that\u2019s exactly why it was chosen. It could carry an old worry quietly.'},{kind:'story',text:'Awake, the same pattern shows up. A team blows up over a **small process failure**, a form filed late. The real issue, that nobody trusts the new manager, can\u2019t be said. So everyone argues about the form.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Later research agrees that recent experience turns up in dreams. But emotional events may surface **more readily** than trivial ones. Nothing comparable supports the cover story. The sleeping brain may reuse fragments for **simpler reasons**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Yesterday\u2019s rubbish makes a good **disguise**, in dreams or in rows. The trivial occasion may be **carrying something** else. Ask what, rather than settling the trivial thing.'}]},'interpretation-of-dreams_9':{title:'The Hardest Case',card:{insight:'You learn the most from how an idea handles the case that does not fit.',tryThis:'Next time you make an excuse, ask if it would work no matter what.',skills:['thinking','science']},review:{prompt:'Your favorite idea meets a case it cannot explain. What do you do?',right:'Ask if your answer would fit the other result too',wrong:'Find a clever reading that makes the case fit after all',why:'If your answer would fit any result, it does not test the idea.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your friend says every dream is **a wish coming true**. Then you tell her about your nightmare: being chased through a dark school. Nobody wishes for that. What can she **possibly say now**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Freud faced this exact problem. His answer was that the wish belongs to a part of you that **you don\u2019t accept**. So when it comes true, it **feels like fear**. Clever, but notice something.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Some answers can explain **anything at all**, even the opposite. That makes them safe, not right. Watch how people handle **the hardest case**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'If every dream fulfils a wish, what about **the nightmare**? Nobody wishes for terror. Freud knew his own theory had raised **this problem**. So what would you say in his place?'},{kind:'explain',text:'His first answer was about the wish itself. It belongs to a part of the mind **the dreamer rejects**, so fulfilling it registers as dread. It\u2019s ingenious. It\u2019s also the kind of answer that can **absorb any counter-example**.'},{kind:'story',text:'The book went further. Even dreams that seemed to refute the theory were read as fulfilling a wish to prove Freud wrong. Then, twenty years on, came the traumatised soldiers, dreaming the same terror night after night.'},{kind:'explain',text:'This time he **conceded**. These dreams didn\u2019t fit, and he went looking for something beyond the pleasure principle. That later admission is **the most honest moment** in his handling of the theory.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Ask of any defence: would this **answer** have fitted **the opposite result** too? If so, it protects the theory without testing it. Your own beliefs deserve the same question.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Every theory has **a case it hates**. For Freud\u2019s wish theory, it was the nightmare. Nobody wishes for terror. So what does a thinker do when his own idea **turns on him**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'His first move was **ingenious**. The anxiety, he argued, belongs to a wish the dreamer finds unacceptable. Its fulfilment is felt as distress, not pleasure. The trouble is that this move can **absorb anything**.'},{kind:'story',text:'The book even handled dreams that seemed to refute it. A dreamer whose dream disproved the theory was read as wishing to prove Freud wrong. So the counter-example became more support. Nothing could get through.'},{kind:'story',text:'Two decades later, he faced the dreams of traumatised soldiers, reliving the same terror night after night. This time he admitted they didn\u2019t fit, and went looking for a mechanism **beyond pleasure**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The concession came twenty years on, in another book. And his replacement, a compulsion to repeat tied to a death drive, is criticised as **just as untestable**. A bigger patch is still a patch.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You learn more from how a theory treats **its worst case** than from its easy wins. Before defending your idea, check whether your answer would **fit the opposite** too.'}]}}}},966,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'laelius-on-friendship',kind:'book',promise:'Keep the friends who are worth keeping',about:'Why do some friends stay for life while others fade when the favors stop? Cicero was a leader in Rome long ago. He wrote a book about what real friends owe each other. Here are his best ideas. Which of your friends are worth keeping?',ideas:{'laelius-on-friendship_1':{title:'The Umbrella Friend',card:{insight:'Real friends like who you are first, and the help comes after.',tryThis:'Write down three people you see less this year. Next to each, write if you still like them.',skills:['relationships','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'You stop needing rides from a neighbor and stop visiting. What do you ask?',right:'Did the need fade, or did the liking fade too?',wrong:'Do I still owe her something for all those rides?',why:'If only the need is gone, you are still friends, so go visit.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You only grab an **umbrella** when it rains. On sunny days it sits in the corner, forgotten. Now think about a friend you only call **when you need help**. What does that make them?'},{kind:'story',text:'Sam helped you with maths all term. Now the test is over, and you don\u2019t need help any more. Do you still want to sit with Sam at lunch? Cicero, a thinker in ancient Rome, said that answer tells you everything.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Real friends aren\u2019t **useful tools**. You like who they are first, and the help comes after. If **the liking stays** after the help stops, it was real.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'It\u2019s been **a self-sufficient year**. You sorted out your money, learned to cook and stopped needing lifts. You also notice you see people **far less**. Nobody fell out, and nothing went wrong. So what happened?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Cicero\u2019s test is a prediction. If friendship grew out of **need**, the most self-sufficient people would have the fewest friends. He thought **the opposite** was closer to true. People who need little often make good friends, because nothing else is mixed in.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Cicero has nothing against **alliances**, which are respectable. They\u2019re just **a different thing**.',left:{title:'Friendship',items:['Begins with who they are','The help comes later','Outlasts the help']},right:{title:'Alliance',items:['Begins with what they give','The liking comes later','Ends with the help']}},{kind:'moral',text:'When someone can\u2019t give you **what they used to**, a deal ends. A friendship **doesn\u2019t**. So if you need someone less, check whether the liking went too.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Rome, 129 BCE. A great general called **Scipio** has just died. Days later, his lifelong friend Laelius sits down to talk about friendship. One question comes up early: did either man **ever need** the other?'},{kind:'story',text:'Laelius answers plainly. Scipio never needed him, and he never needed Scipio. Neither was looking for help. Each, he says, loved the other for his character, and the benefits followed from that.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Cicero is arguing against a popular view of his day. It said friendship grows from **weakness**: we look for friends to supply what we lack. His reply reverses the arrow. You don\u2019t love because you expect benefit. **Benefit follows** because you love.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a colleague you lunched with every day. She leaves for another firm, and the lunches stop. You liked her, but you haven\u2019t called. Was it the desk you shared, or the person? Only one of those is gone.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Most friendships begin in **shared use**: a desk, a team, a school run. And a friend who never helps when it matters fails Cicero too. The question isn\u2019t whether help flows. It\u2019s whether help is the reason.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Friendship starts with **who someone is**. The help is real, but it comes second. So when the need goes, look at **what\u2019s left**, and you\u2019ll know which kind you had.'}]},'laelius-on-friendship_2':{title:'The Nice Sickness',card:{insight:'A friend who only praises you feels nice but keeps the truth from you.',tryThis:'List the three friends you talk to most. Next to each, write the last time they disagreed with you.',skills:['relationships','communication']},review:{prompt:'Your two closest friends love every plan you share. What should you notice?',right:'You may only be going to friends who agree with you',wrong:'They are just good friends who believe in your ideas',why:'Praise only works on people who want it, so check who you go to.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You sing a solo at the school concert. One friend says it was **perfect**. Another says the high note wobbled, and you should breathe before it. Which friend was **nicer to hear**? Which one helped more?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Cicero, a thinker in ancient Rome, warned about **flattery**: only ever saying nice things. He called it a disease of friendship. It\u2019s sneaky, because it feels lovely. But you lose what a friend can give you: **the honest truth**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A friend who only says **nice things** can\u2019t help you get better. Look for **the honest one**, and be someone who\u2019s glad to hear the truth.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You tell a close friend your plan to quit and start a business. She **loves every part** of it, warmly. You leave dinner feeling wonderful. A week later, can you recall **a single question** she asked?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Of every threat to friendship, Cicero spends longest on **flattery**. He calls it the disease of friendship. A quarrel is plainly a problem, so it gets dealt with. Flattery is pleasant, so it never does. The **form** stays while the point goes.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Cicero is harder on the flattered than the flatterer. Flattery only works on **someone who wants it**. So look at the friends you\u2019ve chosen. If they\u2019re the ones who always agree, you\u2019ve **selected for flattery**. Nobody had to lie.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Put the dinner beside **an honest friend\u2019s** version, and see what **you\u2019d gain**.',left:{title:'The flatterer',items:['Loves every part','Feels wonderful tonight','Changes nothing']},right:{title:'The honest friend',items:['Asks the hard question','Stings a little','Might save the plan']}},{kind:'moral',text:'A flatterer keeps **the form** of friendship and removes the point: **an honest opinion**. You can\u2019t fix them. But you can be someone who welcomes the truth.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Cicero lists many dangers to friendship: quarrels, rivalry, **changes of fortune**. Yet he spends longest on something that feels lovely while it happens. Why would the most pleasant threat be **the worst one**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'His answer is that **a friend\u2019s value** lies in honesty. A friend may be the one person who\u2019ll tell you the truth. So a flatterer removes that, while keeping **the dinners** and warm messages. Nothing looks broken, so nobody repairs it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Tom shows his novel to two friends. One gushes. The other says the middle drags. Tom feels hurt and stops sending her chapters. Over the next year, he only shares with the gusher. The book never gets finished.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Cicero puts most of the blame on **the listener**. Flattery only takes hold where it\u2019s welcome. So the cure isn\u2019t mainly finding honest friends. It\u2019s becoming someone **others can be honest** with.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a manager whose team praises every idea she has. Meetings are warm and short. She starts to prefer the people who nod. Within a year, the doubters have stopped speaking. Nobody lied to her. She chose agreement.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some people call cruelty honesty and treat every kind word as suspect. **True encouragement** isn\u2019t flattery. A friend can be right to back your risky plan. What matters is whether their agreement is honest.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Flattery is dangerous because it **feels like friendship**. It keeps the warmth and drops the truth. So watch **whom you seek out**, and make yourself easy to be honest with.'}]},'laelius-on-friendship_3':{title:'The Early Word',card:{insight:'Speak up early and kindly, and listen well when it is your turn.',tryThis:'Pick one worry you are keeping from a friend. Write the kindest way to say it in one line.',skills:['communication','relationships']},review:{prompt:'A friend is about to sign a lease she cannot afford. When do you speak?',right:'Now, kindly, while she can still change her mind',wrong:'Later, once she sees the problem for herself',why:'Before she signs, a warning can help, but after, it is just blame.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your friend has **spinach in her teeth**. She\u2019s about to walk into her school photo. Telling her feels awkward. Saying nothing is easy. What would **a real friend** do right now?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Cicero, a thinker in ancient Rome, said friends must **tell each other** hard things. But kindly, and early, while it can still help. He said friends have a second job too: **listening** without getting cross. He thought that was harder.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Tell a friend the **hard thing** early, while it can still help. Say it **kindly**. And when they tell you something, don\u2019t get cross.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A friend is about to sign **a lease** on a flat she can\u2019t really afford. You\u2019ve seen her budget. Saying so now would sting. Saying nothing is easy. And it\u2019s **her decision**, isn\u2019t it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Cicero is specific about **timing**. A warning given while the decision is still open is information, and she can act on it. The same words after she\u2019s signed are **a reproach**. People resist reproaches.'},{kind:'explain',text:'He\u2019s also specific about **manner**. Frankness is required, but bitterness isn\u2019t. And he gives the listener a duty too: to take correction **without resentment**. He treats that as the harder half. A friend who bristles soon stops being told anything.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Staying silent feels **kind**. Cicero would say it\u2019s **a low opinion** in disguise.',left:{title:'Silence',items:['Feels considerate','Assumes she can\u2019t cope','Arrives as a verdict later']},right:{title:'An early word',items:['Stings a little now','Trusts her to decide','Arrives while it\u2019s useful']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Say the hard thing **while it\u2019s still open**, and say it without bitterness. Waiting turns a kind word into **an unkind one**. Then take correction the same way when it\u2019s your turn.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'You can see a friend\u2019s mistake coming, plainly. **Speaking now** would sting. Waiting feels gentler, and maybe it will sort itself out. Cicero had firm views on **which choice** is the kind one. Which would you pick?'},{kind:'explain',text:'His rule is to admonish **freely**, but never harshly, and to do it **early**. The reason is practical. While a matter is open, a warning is information. After the damage, the same warning is a reproach, and it\u2019s resisted.'},{kind:'story',text:'Priya notices her friend Dan\u2019s new business plan has no budget for tax. She mentions it over coffee, lightly, before he signs anything. He grumbles, then rewrites a page. A year later, it\u2019s a small footnote, not a crisis.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture the other half. Your friend says your temper in meetings is costing you. You feel the heat rise, and you want to argue. Cicero says this is **the harder duty**: taking it without resentment.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Both duties, he says, belong to friendship itself. Saying nothing quietly decides your friend **can\u2019t bear** the truth. That\u2019s **a low opinion** dressed as care. And bristling at correction teaches everyone to stop offering it.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Frankness is a licence, not a mandate. Some things **aren\u2019t yours** to judge, and a friend who corrects everything becomes a critic. Cicero\u2019s case is for a matter that\u2019s real, still open and fairly yours.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Silence can look like kindness, but it\u2019s **a verdict** that your friend can\u2019t cope. Speak **early and gently**. And when it\u2019s your turn to hear it, listen.'}]},'laelius-on-friendship_4':{title:'The Wrong Favor',card:{insight:'A friend who asks you to do wrong has already hurt the friendship.',tryThis:'Pick the last favor that made you uneasy. Write one honest line you could have said instead of yes.',skills:['relationships','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'A friend asks you to say she was at a meeting she missed. What now?',right:'Say no, because asking that hurts the friendship',wrong:'Say yes, because you stand by your friends',why:'Loyalty matters, but a good friend will not ask you to lie for her.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your friend forgot his homework. He wants to **copy yours** before the bell. *Come on, we\u2019re friends,* he says. Saying no feels mean. Saying yes feels wrong. So **what do you do**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Cicero, a thinker in ancient Rome, made this **a rule** of friendship. Only ask a friend for good things. Only do good things for a friend. Doing wrong for a friend **isn\u2019t an excuse**, he said.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A good friend **won\u2019t ask** you to do something wrong. If they do, the asking is the problem. You can **say no** and still be their friend.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A friend at work asks you to confirm she was at **a meeting** she missed. It\u2019s one line in an email. She says she\u2019d do the same for you. Why does refusing feel like **choosing a rule** over a person?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Cicero sets **one hard limit**. Ask friends only for what\u2019s honourable, and do only what\u2019s honourable for them. He puts the fault in **the asking**. Your friend is using the friendship against your interest: your honesty and your name.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The modern cases are rarely crimes. They\u2019re **small things**: covering for someone, signing off unchecked work, staying quiet when asked. Each one asks you to pay for the friendship in **the wrong currency**. That currency is your integrity.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Loyalty is real, but it **isn\u2019t the top value**. A friend who asks you to do wrong has already **broken something**. You can refuse and still care about them.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Rome, 133 BCE. The reformer **Tiberius Gracchus** has been killed. His devoted follower Blossius is questioned. Would he have done anything Gracchus asked? Yes, he says. Even **set fire** to the Capitol.'},{kind:'story',text:'Laelius tells this story in the dialogue, and he\u2019s not impressed. The Capitol was Rome\u2019s most sacred hill. To Blossius, burning it for a friend sounded like loyalty. To Laelius, it was friendship badly misunderstood.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Roman political friendship came with **an expectation**: support your friend, whatever the cause. Cicero rejects it. He points to factions and plots as where it leads. The breach, he argues, comes from **the one who asks**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a Friday at your desk. A friend asks you to sign off his figures without checking. It\u2019s small and he\u2019s in a hurry. Saying yes would spend your name to save his afternoon.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Cicero softens the rule himself. Where a friend\u2019s life or good name is at stake, he allows **a small step** off the path. It must stop short of disgrace. Where that line falls is left to judgement.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Loyalty matters, but it **isn\u2019t the highest value**. When a friend asks you to do wrong, the asking is **the injury**. Refusing protects you both.'}]},'laelius-on-friendship_5':{title:'Unstitch, Don\u2019t Tear',card:{insight:'A worn out friendship can fade slowly, with no need for a big fight.',tryThis:'Pick a friendship that has drifted. Write one line about what it gave you, with no blame in it.',skills:['relationships','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'An old friendship has stopped working. Nobody did anything wrong. What now?',right:'Let it fade slowly, with no big goodbye',wrong:'Have an honest talk and end it for good',why:'When no one did wrong, a slow fade keeps the good memories whole.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'In Year 3, you and Jo did **everything together**. Now you\u2019re in different classes and like different things. You still wave, but you hardly talk. Nobody did anything wrong. Do you need **a big goodbye**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Cicero, a thinker in ancient Rome, said some friendships just **wear out**. People grow in different ways, and that\u2019s no one\u2019s fault. So let it **fade slowly**, like stitches coming loose, not a big rip.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Friendships can **end gently**. If nobody did anything wrong, you don\u2019t need a fight or a speech. Let it fade, and **keep the good memories**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A long friendship has **quietly stopped working**. Nothing happened. Your calls feel like duty, and you both know it. You\u2019re rehearsing **a big conversation** about where it went wrong. Do you have to have it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Laelius passes on a saying from Cato, the stern old statesman. Friendships that have run their course should be **unstitched, not torn**. Let them loosen gradually. The exception is a real wrong, where **a clean break** fits.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The reason is about **what an ending says**. A public break declares the friendship was a mistake, which is usually false. A slow cooling only says that time passed. And open enmity hurts both of you **more than drift**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Your friend didn\u2019t betray you. You **changed at different speeds**, which is **nobody\u2019s failure**.',left:{title:'Tearing',items:['Demands an explanation','Turns time into a grievance','Rewrites the history']},right:{title:'Unstitching',items:['Lets the contact thin','Treats drift as ordinary','Keeps the history intact']}},{kind:'moral',text:'When a friendship fades without any wrong, **let it fade**. A gentle ending keeps **the shared history** whole. Not every ending needs a scene.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Cicero\u2019s dialogue mostly praises **friendship at its best**. Then, among its practical cases, comes a quieter question. What should you do when a friendship has simply **run its course**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Laelius repeats a saying of Cato\u2019s: such friendships should be **unstitched rather than torn**. Let them loosen bit by bit. He sets apart friendships ended by some **intolerable wrong**. There, a clean break is fitting.'},{kind:'story',text:'Two university friends drift after one moves abroad. Calls get rarer, then stop. Neither sends an angry message. Ten years on, they meet at a wedding and talk for an hour, easily. The history was never torn.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture two old friends who fall out over a slow drift. One sends a long email listing every grievance. The other replies in kind. Mutual friends take sides. What was an ordinary fading becomes a public feud.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Cicero\u2019s reasoning is about **reputation**. A public break says the friendship was a mistake, and that injures both people\u2019s standing. Drift says only that time passed. People change at **different rates**, so an ending is often nobody\u2019s failure.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Fading can be **a coward\u2019s exit**. If your friend still cares and asks what happened, silence can hurt more than a kind, clear word. Vanishing on someone isn\u2019t what Cicero meant.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Most endings are **nobody\u2019s fault**. Let those friendships loosen, and you keep **the history** whole. Save the clean break for a real wrong.'}]},'laelius-on-friendship_6':{title:'The Small Things Test',card:{insight:'Watch how someone acts before you trust them, since love can blind you.',tryThis:'Pick a new friend you like a lot. Write down one thing you know about how they treat people.',skills:['relationships','thinking']},review:{prompt:'You just met someone who already feels like a best friend. What now?',right:'Test them in small things before you fully trust them',wrong:'Trust the feeling and treat them as a best friend',why:'People show who they are slowly, so watch the small things first.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A new girl joins your class. She\u2019s funny, and she picks you **on day one**. By Friday she\u2019s your best friend. A month later, you find out she tells everyone **your secrets**. What went wrong?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Cicero, a thinker in ancient Rome, said most people do this **backwards**. They love first and look later. But once you love someone, it\u2019s hard to see them clearly. So **look first**, in small ways.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Before someone becomes **your best friend**, watch how they act in **little things**. Do they keep small secrets and treat others kindly? Then decide.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Many people have **a friendship** they wouldn\u2019t start again today. You met early and liked each other fast. Only later did you see how they treat people. By then, leaving felt like **breaking something**. Sound familiar?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Cicero states a rule people tend to run **backwards**: judge first, then love. Not love first, then judge. Much of the harm, he suggests, comes from **committing early**, then discovering who the person is when leaving hurts.'},{kind:'explain',text:'His method is to **test in small matters** first, small sums before large ones. The qualities that count are constancy and honesty under pressure. Those only show in situations that **haven\u2019t happened yet**. So the test is slow.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Once you\u2019re attached, your judgement **starts working for** the commitment, not **the truth**.',left:{title:'Love, then judge',items:['Feeling arrives first','Excuses pile up','Leaving is painful']},right:{title:'Judge, then love',items:['Small dealings first','Evidence piles up','Commitment is earned']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Affection won\u2019t wait, so watch **how someone acts** in small things while you can still see straight. Let the evidence arrive **before the commitment**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019d never lend **a stranger** a thousand pounds on a first meeting. Yet people hand over their trust, secrets and years after **one good evening**. Why are we so careful with money and so quick with friends?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Cicero\u2019s rule is **judge, then love**. Form a view of someone\u2019s character before the attachment forms. His complaint is that people reverse it. They commit, then discover who they\u2019ve chosen, when **judgement is compromised** and leaving hurts.'},{kind:'story',text:'Ella and Kate click at a new job. Within weeks they share everything: holidays, secrets, a flat. Then Kate starts taking credit for Ella\u2019s work. Ella spends two years explaining it away, because admitting it means losing her flatmate.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Cicero\u2019s method is to **test in small matters**, small sums before large ones. The qualities that count, like constancy and honesty under pressure, only show in situations **not yet arisen**. The test is slow because the evidence is.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Sam, who likes a new friend but waits a season. He notices she keeps small promises and talks kindly about absent people. When a real test comes, he already has some evidence.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Cicero admits **affection doesn\u2019t wait** for the assessment. Applied rigidly, the rule is cold: someone forever auditing others may never let anyone close enough to judge. What survives is a habit of noticing.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You can\u2019t stop yourself liking someone fast. But you can notice **what you actually know** about their conduct, apart from **their company**. Judge that, then commit.'}]},'laelius-on-friendship_7':{title:'Years of Salt',card:{insight:'Old friends have seen your good and bad days, which new friends cannot have yet.',tryThis:'Write the name of one old friend you have not called in a while. Then call them today.',skills:['relationships','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'You are busy, and you keep missing your oldest friend\u2019s calls. What now?',right:'Make time for the old friend, even for a few minutes',wrong:'Let it go, since your newer friends are more fun',why:'Only years can build that kind of trust, so an old friend is worth the call.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your oldest friend has known you **since you were five**. Someone new at school is more fun right now. Could the new friend ever **know you** the way the old one does?'},{kind:'explain',text:'An old friend has seen you on **bad days**. They know how you act when things go wrong. Cicero, a thinker in ancient Rome, said old friends are like **good wine**. They get better with time, and nobody can skip the years.'},{kind:'moral',text:'New friends are great, so **make them**. But old friends have something new ones **can\u2019t have yet**: years together. Look after them.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your oldest friend knew you **before you were anyone** in particular. You\u2019ve seen each other through a bad job and a funeral. Someone you met this year may be better company. So what does **the old friend** have?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Cicero compares friendships to **wine**: the ones that keep get better. A new friend may be warmer. But you haven\u2019t seen them when you failed, or when **loyalty cost them** something. Years supply that evidence. There\u2019s no shortcut.'},{kind:'explain',text:'He doesn\u2019t dismiss new friends, though. Promising ones should still be made, because **every old friendship** was new once. Someone has to **start the clock**. The two claims sit together, and both hold.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'So treat the two kinds of friendship as **different things**, not **rivals**.',left:{title:'Old friend',items:['Years of evidence','Can\u2019t be rebuilt','Needs maintaining']},right:{title:'New friend',items:['Warmth and promise','Starts the clock','Needs time']}},{kind:'moral',text:'If the value lies in **the years**, an old friendship needs maintaining, not comparing. Once spent, it **can\u2019t be rebuilt**. Neglect is how it usually goes.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Laelius repeats an old saying in the dialogue. Two friends must eat **many measures of salt** together before their friendship is complete. That\u2019s a lot of meals. What could all **that salt** be buying you?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The answer is **evidence**. Years bring shared history and behaviour that\u2019s been tested. You learn how a person acts when things go badly. Cicero compares lasting friendships to **wine that improves**. No amount of warmth can rush it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Mark lost his job at fifty. A newer friend sent a warm message and vanished. His friend of thirty years drove over, said little and came back the next week. Mark had known he would, long before.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Leila, who meets someone wonderful at a class. She doesn\u2019t drop her old friends to make room. She books the new friend for coffee anyway, because every old friendship was new once.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Age isn\u2019t proof of quality. Some old friendships survive on habit alone, the verdict **bent to fit** the years. Cicero\u2019s claim is about friendships that have kept, like wine that can bear age. Some bottles turn.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Make new friends, but don\u2019t trade old ones for them. Years of **tested trust** can\u2019t be bought or rebuilt, so **guard the friendships** that have kept.'}]},'laelius-on-friendship_8':{title:'The Second Self',card:{insight:'A true friend is a second you, and you care for them like yourself.',tryThis:'Write the advice you would give a friend with your problem. Then take it yourself.',skills:['thinking','relationships']},review:{prompt:'You have stayed a year at a job you hate. What helps?',right:'Ask what you would tell a friend in your place',wrong:'Wait until you feel sure enough to decide',why:'Your fears do not get in the way with a friend, so you see it clearly.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your friend is scared to try out for **the football team**. You tell her: just go, the worst is a no. Great advice. The next week, you\u2019re **too scared** to try out for the play. Why?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Cicero, a thinker in ancient Rome, said a true friend is like **a second you**. When it\u2019s their problem, you care, but you stand back a little. So you see it **plainly**. That\u2019s why your advice to them is so good.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Next time you\u2019re stuck, pretend **it\u2019s a friend\u2019s problem**. Ask what you\u2019d tell them. Then be brave and **take your own advice**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Last month you told a friend to leave **a job** that was making her miserable. You were clear, kind and right. Your own job is doing the same to you. You\u2019ve been finding **reasons to stay** for a year.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Cicero writes that looking at a true friend is like seeing **an image of yourself**. It\u2019s the essay\u2019s best-known idea. You can confide in them as safely as in yourself, and you weigh **their interests** as your own.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s a practical reading. Your own situation comes with **your fears** and excuses attached. A friend\u2019s comes with your care, minus the excuses. So you\u2019re close enough to care, yet **far enough** to see.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The same facts look different from **your chair** and from **a friend\u2019s place**.',left:{title:'Your own job',items:['Fears attached','Excuses ready','A year of staying']},right:{title:'Her job',items:['Care, no excuses','The answer is obvious','You told her to go']}},{kind:'moral',text:'A second self is close enough to care and far enough to see. So ask what you\u2019d **tell a friend** in your place, then **take that advice**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Cicero calls a true friend **a second self**. It sounds like a greeting-card line. But he wrote it while grieving, as a description rather than a compliment. So what would **taking it literally** mean for you?'},{kind:'explain',text:'He means it **structurally**. A true friend is someone you can confide in as safely as yourself. You weigh their interests as your own. And friendship, he says, makes success brighter and **lightens hardship** by sharing it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Ana tells her friend to leave a job that\u2019s crushing her. She\u2019s clear and kind, and she\u2019s right. Her own job has done the same to her for a year. She hasn\u2019t moved. She\u2019s too close to see it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Tom, whose closest friend is caught cheating at work. Tom did nothing wrong. Yet he feels the shame as if it were his own. People glance at him differently. A second self\u2019s disgrace lands on you too.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The demanding part is **symmetry**. The bond must run equally both ways, which rules out most of what people call friendship. Cicero says history remembers only **three or four** true pairs. The scarcity is the point, not gloom.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. A friend isn\u2019t literally another you. Treating them as one can **erase real differences**, or make every slight against them yours to avenge. And as a standard of rarity, it can sour into dismissing ordinary friendships.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The few friends who meet this standard deserve **a different order** of care. And when you\u2019re stuck, **borrow their view**: advise yourself as you\u2019d advise them.'}]},'laelius-on-friendship_9':{title:'Praise That Cost Something',card:{insight:'Advice about a risk counts most from someone who has paid its price.',tryThis:'Pick some advice you got about a risk. Write down if the person who gave it ever paid that price.',skills:['thinking','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'Two friends say start a business. One never has. One lost one. Whose advice counts?',right:'The one who lost one, because she paid the price',wrong:'Both the same, since they give you the same advice',why:'She lost a business and still says go, so she knows what it costs.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your best friend **moved away** last year. You miss him a lot, and it still hurts. Was it worth being **so close**, now that it\u2019s painful? Who could answer that better than you?'},{kind:'story',text:'Cicero, a thinker in ancient Rome, wrote about two lifelong friends. When Scipio died, Laelius was heartbroken. Yet days later he said the friendship was **the best thing** in his life. He wouldn\u2019t give it up to skip the pain.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Anyone can praise friends on a **happy day**. It means more from someone who\u2019s **lost one** and still says it was worth it. They know the cost.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two friends tell you to **start a business**. One has never tried. The other built one, lost it and is still paying off the debt. Both say go for it. Whose **go for it** counts more?'},{kind:'story',text:'Cicero sets his dialogue days after the death of Scipio, a great Roman general. His friend of a lifetime, Laelius, is asked how he\u2019s bearing it. He grieves for himself, he says, not for Scipio, who\u2019s beyond harm.'},{kind:'explain',text:'And Cicero wasn\u2019t writing from comfort. He wrote it in 44 BCE, a year after his daughter Tullia died. **The Republic** he\u2019d served was coming apart. The case for friendship comes from someone **paying its price**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'That frame is **the proof**. Laelius still says the friendship makes his life happy, and he wouldn\u2019t trade it to avoid the loss. A claim about risk is worth most from someone who\u2019s paid **the downside**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Weigh advice by **what it cost** the person giving it. Praise from someone who\u2019s only seen the upside is cheaper. The friend who **lost it all** and still urges you on is worth hearing.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Imagine writing a book in praise of friendship. Now imagine writing it the year after **your daughter died**, as your country falls apart. Cicero did exactly that. Why would grief make the case **stronger**?'},{kind:'story',text:'In the dialogue, Laelius is asked how he\u2019s bearing Scipio\u2019s death. He doesn\u2019t grieve for Scipio, he says, who\u2019s beyond harm, but for himself. Nothing fortune has given him compares with that friendship. Its memory still makes him happy.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s why **the frame** matters. The essay argues friendship is among the greatest goods. It\u2019s spoken from the condition that tests that claim hardest. If the claim were false, grief would expose it. Instead, **it holds**.'},{kind:'story',text:'The same test works far from Rome. A friend urges you to train for a marathon. She once tore a tendon racing and spent months recovering. She still says it was worth it. Her yes carries real weight.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Testimony isn\u2019t proof. Grief can idealise, and a mourner may be **least able** to see a friendship\u2019s faults. Laelius is also Cicero\u2019s creation. And people who regret a friendship rarely write dialogues about it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A case is worth most from someone **who\u2019s paid** for it. Laelius lost his friend and still called the friendship his greatest good. So check whether **your advisers** have felt the downside.'}]}}}},967,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'leisure-class',kind:'book',promise:'See what your spending says to others',about:'Why would anyone pay more for a bag just for its logo? In 1899, Thorstein Veblen wrote a book about how rich people spent money. He found that a lot of buying is about being seen. What is your spending saying?',ideas:{'leisure-class_1':{title:'The Logo on the Outside',card:{insight:'Some things cost more just so other people will see them.',tryThis:'Before you buy, ask what it would be worth if no one could ever see it.',skills:['money','psychology']},review:{prompt:'Two jackets are just as warm. The logo one costs $80 more. Why?',right:'Being seen in a jacket people know costs a lot',wrong:'Better stitching that keeps you much warmer',why:'When both work the same, the extra price pays for being seen.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two water bottles hold **the same water**. One is cheap. The other costs ten times more and has **a big logo** on the side. Which one do you think most kids at school want?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Some things are bought mainly so other people **will see them**. A writer called Thorstein Veblen called it *conspicuous consumption* in 1899. It only works if people can see the thing and **know it\u2019s costly**. A logo does both jobs.'},{kind:'moral',text:'If nobody could see it, a logo would **say nothing**. So ask what a thing is worth to you **with no one watching**. The rest of the price pays for being seen.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A designer handbag and a plain one carry **the same things** equally well. The designer one costs **far more**. Some of that might be stitching. So what\u2019s the rest of your money buying?'},{kind:'story',text:'Thorstein Veblen, an American economist, published The Theory of the Leisure Class in 1899. He studied the newly rich families of his day, with their mansions and lavish parties. He gave their habit a name: *conspicuous consumption*. It\u2019s still in use.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The signal only works when strangers can **see the bag** and **know its price**.',left:{title:'Signal works',items:['Logo on the outside','Price widely known','Carried in public']},right:{title:'Signal fails',items:['No visible mark','Nobody knows the cost','Kept at home, unseen']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Why must it be costly? A signal **anyone could send** tells nobody anything. If every stranger could afford your bag, carrying it would prove nothing. So the high price isn\u2019t a flaw in the display. It\u2019s what makes it **believable**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Some things are bought mainly **to be seen**. That only works if they\u2019re visible and **known to be expensive**. Ask what yours would be worth if nobody could see it.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'In 1899 a Chicago economist described America\u2019s rich **like an anthropologist** studying a distant tribe. The tone stung. What had Thorstein Veblen noticed about **their spending** that they hadn\u2019t?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Once survival isn\u2019t in question, wealth has to be **shown** to count. Showing it takes spending that others can see. Veblen called this **conspicuous consumption**. The good must be visible and known to be costly, or it says nothing.'},{kind:'story',text:'Think of a watch. It tells the time no better than a cheap one. But strangers can read **the brand** from across a room, and they know roughly what it cost. Both conditions hold, so the watch speaks for you.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a luxury brand drops its logo and sells plain designs. Does the signal die? Not quite. It still works, but only for viewers who can **recognise the item** and its price. Visible and costly always means: to someone.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Economists later formalised this as **costly signalling**. A signal is believable only when it\u2019s too expensive for pretenders to fake. Veblen got there in the 1890s **from observation**, with no mathematics.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Not every visible, expensive thing is a signal. A good coat is warm and also seen. A costly tool may simply work better. Motive is **hard to read** from outside, so ask the question of your own purchases first.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Wealth only counts once it\u2019s **seen**, and seen to be costly. So spending becomes **a message**. Ask which part of your last purchase was paid for the seeing.'}]},'leisure-class_2':{title:'Waste on Purpose',card:{insight:'Wasting money shows you have money to spare.',tryThis:'Price a plain version of something you want that does the same job. The gap is the show-off part.',skills:['money','psychology']},review:{prompt:'A wedding has five courses and flowers thrown out the next day. Why?',right:'To show the families have money to spare',wrong:'No reason, the planners just counted guests wrong',why:'Spending on what no one needs shows you have money to spare.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A family buys **three huge cakes** for ten guests. Most of it gets thrown away. Did they just count wrong? Or are three cakes **saying something** one cake can\u2019t?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Buying a useful thing proves nothing, since anyone who needs a coat might buy one. **Wasting money** is different. It shows you have **lots to spare**. Veblen used *waste* in his own way: spending that doesn\u2019t make anyone\u2019s life better.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Showing off works best when **some money is wasted**. And people rarely plan it. They just feel the costly thing is **nicer**, and you probably do too.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A wedding for a hundred and fifty guests. **Five courses**, and flowers that will be **binned tomorrow**. Nobody calls it a display of waste. Everyone calls it doing things properly. So what is all that excess for?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Veblen\u2019s claim is uncomfortable. The waste isn\u2019t a side effect of the display. It\u2019s **the thing being bought**. Anything useful could have been bought by someone who needed it. Only spending **without return** proves you can afford it.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Set **useful spending** beside **wasteful spending**, and see which one tells strangers anything.',left:{title:'Useful spending',items:['Might be a need','Proves nothing about means','Anyone might do it']},right:{title:'Wasteful spending',items:['Plainly not a need','Proves a surplus','Only the able can do it']}},{kind:'story',text:'Back at the wedding, nobody is being cynical. Veblen says the people involved aren\u2019t aware of any such motive. The families feel the lavish version is simply **nicer**. That feeling is how the signal **does its work**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'In a status purchase, the useless extra is **the point**. Nobody has to intend it. Price the plainest version that does the job, and **the gap** shows you what the signal costs.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'In the middle of his chapter on spending, Veblen stops to define **one word**: *waste*. He insists it carries **no moral blame**. Why would an economist need to say that?'},{kind:'explain',text:'For Veblen, waste means spending that doesn\u2019t serve human life or **wellbeing** on the whole. His claim is that this waste is **the thing purchased**. Only expenditure without return proves you could afford it, so the uselessness does the work.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a table set for far more than anyone can eat. A full platter sits pushed aside. The surplus isn\u2019t a flaw in the evening. It tells every guest the host has **more than enough**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a buyer choosing between two cars. Both do the school run. She picks the far dearer one and feels it\u2019s simply **better**. She isn\u2019t pretending. Veblen expects exactly that: the signal works through her sincere enjoyment.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Sincere taste and unconscious signalling look identical from outside, so the claim is **hard to test**. Calling something waste also assumes a view of wellbeing. And much costly spending has real returns: craft, durability, pleasure.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The useless part of a price is often **the message**. You don\u2019t have to be cynical to send it. Just ask how much of **your enjoyment** would survive a plainer version.'}]},'leisure-class_3':{title:'Showing Off by Not Working',card:{insight:'Long ago, the rich showed off by not working at all.',tryThis:'Next time you post a photo, ask what it tells strangers who do not know you.',skills:['psychology','history']},review:{prompt:'Why did showing off move from free time to things?',right:'In a city, strangers can\u2019t see your day, just your things',wrong:'People just got richer and had more to spend',why:'Strangers in a city can only judge you by what they see.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Long ago, how could you tell **who was rich**? Not by their phone. Look at their hands. If someone never had to dig, sew or carry, what did **soft hands** tell you?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Before people showed off by spending, they showed off by **not working**. Rich children spent years on things that earned nothing: old languages, fancy manners, special sports. **The years** were the point. They proved you had time to spare.'},{kind:'moral',text:'In a village, everyone sees **your whole day**. In a city, strangers only see **your things**. That\u2019s why showing off moved from free time to stuff.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'At a party, someone tells you twice how **slammed** they are: back-to-back calls, no weekends, barely slept. It sounds like a complaint. But is it really **landing** as one?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Veblen argued that before wealth was shown by spending, it was shown by **not working**. He called it **conspicuous leisure**. His evidence was the skills of the rich: dead languages, ceremonial sport, etiquette that took years to learn.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'So why did display move? Veblen put it down to **cities** and **anonymity**.',left:{title:'In a village',items:['Everyone knows you','Your day is seen','Leisure shows']},right:{title:'In a city',items:['Strangers all around','Nobody sees your day','Only your things show']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Strangers can\u2019t see how you spend your days. They can read **a purchased object** at a glance. So the evidence had to become portable. A later twist, not Veblen\u2019s own: for many professionals, **visible busyness** now signals being in demand.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Status goes to whatever is **scarce** and **easy to read**. Once it was idle time, then things, and now it\u2019s often busyness. So notice which signal you\u2019re sending.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'In Veblen\u2019s book, one chapter comes **before** conspicuous consumption. It\u2019s about people who prove their wealth by **doing nothing useful**. Why would idleness come first, and what does it tell you?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Leisure leaves **no product**, so it must leave evidence. Veblen read the skills of the rich as **receipts** for wasted time. Think of dead languages, correct spelling, ceremonial sport and animals bred for show. The slower to learn, the better the proof.'},{kind:'story',text:'In a village, this worked well. Everyone knew how you spent your day, so idleness was seen. Then people moved to cities, full of strangers. Veblen predicted that display would migrate **from time to things**, and roughly that happened.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now look at a holiday photo posted on a weekday. It turns the old leisure signal into something strangers can read on a screen. That fits his argument about anonymity: **the evidence travels** to wherever the viewers are.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Among many professionals, **visible busyness** now signals status better than idleness. Veblen\u2019s framework can absorb that, since busyness shows demand. But a theory that reads both idleness and busyness as display needs care.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Wealth was first shown by **not working**. Where nobody can see your day, the proof becomes **something you carry**. Ask what your own busyness is telling people.'}]},'leisure-class_4':{title:'The Moving Rung',card:{insight:'The people we compare ourselves to move up when we do.',tryThis:'Next time a raise or a win feels small, write down who you compare yourself with.',skills:['psychology','money']},review:{prompt:'Two months after a raise, nothing feels different. Where should you look first?',right:'At who you compare yourself with now',wrong:'At how much bigger your next raise must be',why:'Each gain changes who you look at, so the good feeling fades.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You save for months and buy **the bike** you wanted. For a week it feels great. Then a friend gets one with **better gears**. Your bike hasn\u2019t changed. So why does it feel worse?'},{kind:'explain',text:'We judge what we have by **the people just above** us. When we move up, they change too. It\u2019s like walking toward the line where sky meets land. However far you walk, **the line** moves back.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Veblen said this isn\u2019t **a flaw in you**. It\u2019s just how comparing works. If you want to be ahead, there\u2019s **always someone** further ahead.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You got **the raise** you\u2019d wanted for a year. Two months later, nothing feels different. The flat is fine, the car is fine. And somehow the people you notice are now **a rung higher**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Veblen\u2019s explanation is about **the reference point**. We measure ourselves against people slightly above us. That group moves when we do. So each gain **resets the comparison**, and the good feeling is temporary by design.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Why can\u2019t more money fix it? If what you want is **to be ahead**, being ahead is defined by comparison. When a whole society gets richer, the positions everyone was chasing **stay put**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'That\u2019s **lifestyle inflation**, described long before the phrase. A new salary moves you into a new circle. The new circle sets a new standard, and spending creeps up to meet it. It isn\u2019t **weak will**. The yardstick moved with you.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When a raise doesn\u2019t land, the number isn\u2019t the problem. **The comparison** is. So look at **who you\u2019re measuring** against before you chase the next rise.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Veblen called it **invidious comparison**: ranking people by standing. He said he meant *invidious* as a technical word, not blame. But why would comparison make **more wealth** feel like no gain at all?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Each class, Veblen argues, takes **its standard** from the class just above. So every gain resets the comparison. Satisfaction lasts only until the new circle comes into view. More wealth can\u2019t satisfy a desire whose object was **position**, not goods.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take someone whose pay doubles over five years. Nothing about her income fell. But she now lives among people who earn more again. Her sense of position is **where it started**, and so is her contentment.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a whole town getting richer together. Everyone\u2019s house gets bigger. The ranking, though, **stays exactly** where it was. If people wanted rank, the town as a whole is no more satisfied than before.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Later work fits this. Psychologists described people drifting back to a baseline after gains and named it **the hedonic treadmill**. Some research on income and happiness has found **relative position** matters. Veblen stated the mechanism decades earlier.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Not everything you want is positional. Food, health and safety improve lives whatever the neighbours have, and those gains last. The bleak conclusion holds for **wealth sought as rank**, not for every rise in income.'},{kind:'moral',text:'If **your goal** is rank, more money only moves the reference point. The way off isn\u2019t winning this round. It\u2019s **choosing** not to compete on rank.'}]},'leisure-class_5':{title:'The Painted Target',card:{insight:'If an idea fits all cases, it tells you little.',tryThis:'Next time an idea you like fits a new case, name one thing that would prove it wrong.',skills:['thinking','science']},review:{prompt:'A friend\u2019s idea explains both showing off and hiding money. What do you ask?',right:'What result would prove the idea wrong?',wrong:'How many more cases it can fit',why:'When both sides fit, the idea tells you nothing.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your friend has one answer for every problem: *you\u2019re just tired*. Sad, cross or bored, it\u2019s always tiredness. Sometimes **they\u2019re right**. But if it\u2019s always the answer, how much is it **really telling** you?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Veblen had a good idea: **some spending** is about being seen. But he used it to explain almost everything, even sport, religion and graduation gowns. He didn\u2019t test each case. He just **found a way** to fit it in.'},{kind:'moral',text:'An idea that explains **everything** can\u2019t be checked. So when someone has one answer for it all, ask **what would prove** them wrong. If nothing could, be careful.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'An archer\u2019s arrows all sit dead centre. Impressive, until you learn **the rings** were painted after each arrow landed. Every shot is a hit. So what has the archer **actually shown** you?'},{kind:'story',text:'Veblen explains academic gowns and university ceremony as **leisure-class display**. Maybe. But gowns also mark an occasion, tie graduates to a tradition, and are fun to wear. He doesn\u2019t weigh those reasons. He paints the rings around the case.'},{kind:'explain',text:'A useful theory tells you what you **shouldn\u2019t see**. If every case fits, whether people show off or hide their wealth, nothing could count against it. That isn\u2019t strength. It means the theory has **stopped making a claim**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'So split Veblen in two: **the narrow claim** and **the total claim**.',left:{title:'The narrow claim',items:['Some spending is signalling','Needs visibility and cost','Checkable case by case']},right:{title:'The total claim',items:['All culture is status rivalry','Every fact fits','Nothing could disprove it']}},{kind:'moral',text:'The narrow claim has held up well. The claim that **explains everything** doesn\u2019t, because it can\u2019t be tested. When **a theory you like** fits a new case, ask what would have counted against it.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'The Theory of the Leisure Class doesn\u2019t stop at spending. It reads religion, sport, the position of women and university ritual through **one lens**. That range is part of its fame. Is it also **its problem**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A claim that no observation could count against is called **unfalsifiable**. It may still be true, but it can\u2019t be checked, so it tells you **little about the world**. Critics say Veblen\u2019s total theory drifts this way.'},{kind:'story',text:'In the later chapters, the strain shows. His account of academic dress reads as **the theory applied**, not tested. The conclusion is fixed, and the case is made to fit. Each new development is read as the old pattern continuing.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now the part that survived. The core mechanism was later formalised as **costly signalling**. Economists call goods whose demand rises with their price *Veblen goods*. That\u2019s the honest legacy: a precise idea, applied where it can be checked.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The critique can be pushed too far as well. Rejecting the total theory doesn\u2019t mean rejecting **its examples**. Many of Veblen\u2019s cases, dress above all, hold up well on their own.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Take **the mechanism** seriously and decline the claim that it explains everything. When a theory you like fits a new case, name **what would have refuted** it.'}]},'leisure-class_6':{title:'The Silver Spoon Test',card:{insight:'Part of what looks pretty to us is the price, though it feels like taste.',tryThis:'When you love a thing, ask if you would still love it at a low price.',skills:['psychology','thinking']},review:{prompt:'You love a vase, then learn it is a cheap copy. It looks worse. Why?',right:'Knowing the price made it look pretty',wrong:'You suddenly spotted real flaws in how it was made',why:'Nothing about the vase changed but what you knew it cost.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two hoodies look almost **the same**. One has a famous name and costs three times more. Be honest with yourself. Does it really **look better**? Or do you just know it cost more?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Veblen noticed that a neat lawn looks lovely to us. A field with cows grazing on it would be **more useful**, and look much the same. But the lawn shows you can keep land that grows nothing. That\u2019s **part of its beauty**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'We often think costly things look nicer, just because we know **the price**. It feels like **our eyes** are deciding. Often it\u2019s the money.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'In a shop you run your hand over a table and think it\u2019s **beautiful**. Then you read the label: machine-made, a fraction of the price you\u2019d guessed. You look again. Why does it suddenly seem **a little flat**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Veblen\u2019s example is two spoons. One is hand-wrought silver, the other machine-made. In use they\u2019re the same. Yet the hand-made one is felt to be finer. That\u2019s not for any quality you can see. It\u2019s because it\u2019s known to cost more.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Veblen\u2019s claim is that **our sense of beauty** has absorbed the price. The marks of expense have soaked into taste so completely that they **no longer look** like expense. So a price judgement arrives feeling like an aesthetic one.'},{kind:'explain',text:'This isn\u2019t calling you **a snob**. The preference is **sincere**, which is why it\u2019s so hard to see. The test is whether your admiration survives learning the price. Often it doesn\u2019t, and that feels like being caught.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Taste and price are **tangled together**. You won\u2019t untangle them by deciding to. But you can ask, of anything that strikes you as beautiful, **what you know** about its cost.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Veblen\u2019s chapter on taste has a dry title about **money\u2019s rules for beauty**. Its sharpest example is a silver spoon. What could a spoon possibly reveal about **how you see**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Veblen called them **pecuniary canons of taste**: standards of beauty that have absorbed the demands of expense. *Pecuniary* means to do with money. By the end, the price is invisible, so the judgement feels like **pure perception**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Set a hand-wrought silver spoon beside a machine-made one. Both serve equally, and the hand-made one is admired more. Then the twist. If the admired spoon proves to be a skilled imitation, its value and pleasure fall, though nothing changed.'},{kind:'story',text:'His second case is the lawn. A close-cropped lawn is admired over a pasture. Yet a pasture with grazing animals would be more useful, and look much the same. The lawn shows land kept **producing nothing**. Its beauty and its cost arrive together.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some expensive things really are better made. Some beauty owes **nothing to cost**: a sunset, a cheap print you love. Veblen tends to treat all taste as captured, and that overreaches.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The test is useful because **the answer varies**. If your admiration survives learning the price, it wasn\u2019t only the price. If it collapses, **the invoice** was doing the work.'}]},'leisure-class_7':{title:'Dressed Not to Work',card:{insight:'Fancy clothes show that you spent a lot and do not do hard work.',tryThis:'Look in your closet and pick one thing you would not want if no one saw it.',skills:['psychology','money']},review:{prompt:'Your jacket from last year is still warm. Why buy this year\u2019s style?',right:'A new style shows you can throw out clothes that still work',wrong:'The new style is made much better than the old one',why:'Buying the new style shows you can afford to waste good clothes.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A long white dress, spotless, with **a train** that drags on the floor. You couldn\u2019t play football in it. You couldn\u2019t even eat **a messy lunch**. What do you think that\u2019s saying?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Fancy clothes say **three things** at once. *I spent a lot.* I don\u2019t do hard work. I can buy new ones when these go out of style. Clothes **go everywhere** with you, so they show off more than almost anything.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When a style gets cheap, **lots of people** copy it. Then it no longer shows you\u2019re rich. So the rich **move on**, and the old style starts to look wrong.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Most of what you buy stays at home. Clothes go out with you **all day**, in front of everyone you meet. That includes strangers who know **nothing else** about you. What are yours telling them?'},{kind:'explain',text:'That\u2019s why Veblen treats dress as **his best evidence**. One garment sends three signals. It was expensive. The wearer can\u2019t do manual work in it. And through fashion, **last year\u2019s spending** no longer counts.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Each signal needs **its own feature**, and one outfit can carry **all three**.',left:{title:'What it shows',items:['Expense','Unfitness for work','Being up to date']},right:{title:'How it shows',items:['Fine cloth, known labels','Heels, pale fabric, fragile cuts','A style that will soon pass']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Why must fashion **keep changing**? Once a style is cheap enough to copy widely, it **stops signalling**, so it must be replaced. On Veblen\u2019s account the churn is structural. It isn\u2019t a sign that taste keeps improving.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your clothes speak to **strangers** all day, about money, work and timing. Ask which things you own would stop **being worth having** if nobody ever saw them.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'In 1899, a rich man might wear **a tall shiny hat** and polished shoes. A rich woman might wear a corset. None of them could dig a ditch dressed like that. Was the discomfort **a cost** or the point?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Veblen\u2019s sharpest point is that the best garments prove **what you don\u2019t do**. A corset, a high heel, a spotless cuff can\u2019t be worked in. Wearing them shows you **don\u2019t have to**. The inconvenience is part of the message.'},{kind:'story',text:'Think of a pale shirt with a stiff collar. At a glance, it tells everyone the wearer isn\u2019t lifting, digging or fixing anything today. Veblen made the same point about the polished shoe of his own time.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now watch a style move through a town. First only a few can afford it, so it separates them. Then cheap copies appear. Soon the look **separates nobody**, and Veblen notes it starts to seem ugly a few years on.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Veblen gives little weight to dressing for **pleasure**, belonging or self-expression, which are real. Later, deliberate scruffiness became a status marker in some circles. His logic can stretch to cover it, but that stretch deserves suspicion.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Dress is the purest case of display: **expensive**, seen by strangers, and constantly renewed. Its best trick is proving **what you needn\u2019t do**.'}]},'leisure-class_8':{title:'Someone Else\u2019s Show',card:{insight:'Some showing off is done by one person for someone else.',tryThis:'Next time you see staff in fancy matching clothes, ask whose money it shows off.',skills:['psychology','history']},review:{prompt:'A kid does five clubs a week, and proud parents post each one. Whose show?',right:'Partly the proud parents\u2019, since the clubs show them off',wrong:'Only the kid\u2019s, since the kid does all the clubs',why:'One person puts on the show, and someone else gets the credit.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Long ago, a rich man kept **lots of servants**. They wore fancy matching coats. Some just stood by doors in silence. Visitors noticed them **right away**. But who do you think those coats were really about?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The coat wasn\u2019t about the servant. It showed off **the rich man** who paid for it. Veblen called this kind of showing off *vicarious*. That means **done by one person** for someone else\u2019s sake. You can still spot it today.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Sometimes **the showing off** is done by other people. Think of a kid sent to five clubs a week, with proud parents posting each one. Some of that may be **about the parents**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A hotel lobby. **Marble floors**, staff in matching uniforms, a doorman in a long coat. None of them chose those clothes. So whose **standing** are you looking at when you walk in?'},{kind:'story',text:'Veblen\u2019s main case was the wealthy household of his day. Servants in livery, more than the work needed. A wife in elaborate dress, with ceremonial duties. All of it, he argued, showed **the householder\u2019s means**, not their own.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Why hand the display on? Once the man of the house has to work, he **can\u2019t idle visibly** himself. So the wife and servants do it for him. Veblen stresses that this is **real work**, unpaid and unacknowledged.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Modern forms are easy to spot **once named**. In each pair, **the credit** goes across.',left:{title:'Performs it',items:['A child with five clubs','Staff in matching uniforms','A marble lobby']},right:{title:'Credited to',items:['The proud parents','The hotel','The firm']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Vicarious display is **produced by one person** and credited to another. So when comfort looks like a show, ask **whose standing** it builds. Then ask who actually has the choices.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Veblen\u2019s coldest observation is about **the household**. A wife spends her days in visible leisure. It looks like she\u2019s the one **enjoying it**. But what if her leisure isn\u2019t really hers?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Veblen\u2019s term is **vicarious consumption**: leisure and spending performed by one person on behalf of another. The effort is real and the leisure is real. Neither is **to the performer\u2019s credit**. The word carries the whole argument.'},{kind:'story',text:'His main case was the late-Victorian household. The wife\u2019s leisure was a performance required of her, unpaid and unacknowledged. The more visibly useless her pastimes, the better they served **her husband\u2019s standing**. He describes it in a flat, clinical tone.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a child whose week is packed with tutors and lessons. Or a firm whose marble lobby tells visitors how solid it is. In each case the display is produced by someone other than **the person it credits**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The model fits an old household and sits awkwardly on modern ones, where both partners work. It can also treat the performer as having **no motives** of their own. A child\u2019s lessons may be for the child.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When **you spot** a display, ask whose standing it builds. Then ask **who chooses**, before you decide whose display it really is.'}]},'leisure-class_9':{title:'The Best Seat',card:{insight:'People with the best seat fight change because of where they sit.',tryThis:'Next time someone fights a change, write down what the old way is worth to them.',skills:['thinking','leadership']},review:{prompt:'A club\u2019s longest members fight hardest against new members. What do you check first?',right:'What the change costs each of them',wrong:'Whether they are just mean and selfish',why:'Where people sit can explain why they fight, so check that first.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Say you have **the best seat** on the bus: by the window, near the heater. The driver asks if everyone wants to swap seats. Who\u2019s **least keen** on the idea?'},{kind:'explain',text:'You are. Not because you\u2019re mean. You just have **the most to lose**. Veblen said comfy people resist change for two reasons. Change costs them most, and they\u2019re **kept safe** from problems, so they feel them last.'},{kind:'moral',text:'People who are comfy with how things are often **don\u2019t want change**. That isn\u2019t because they\u2019re bad. It\u2019s because of **where they sit**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A members\u2019 club debates **opening up** to new members. The loudest objections come from the longest-standing members, who hold the best tables and the committee seats. Their arguments are about tradition. What\u2019s **their position** about?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Veblen explains this without calling anyone stupid or selfish. Those best served by **the present arrangement** have the least reason to change it. They\u2019re also **the most sheltered** from the pressures that force change. The resistance follows from the seating.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Veblen adds a twist. The leisure class sets **the standards** others copy, from manners to what counts as respectable. So its slowness spreads well beyond itself. It **holds back** everyone who takes their cues from it, perhaps including you.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'When the club\u2019s old guard pushes back, you can read it **two ways**. Veblen picks **the second**.',left:{title:'About character',items:['They\u2019re selfish','They\u2019re hypocrites','Change their minds']},right:{title:'About position',items:['They\u2019re sheltered','Any protected group would act alike','Change their exposure']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Resistance often follows from **the seat**, not the character. So when someone fights a change, first work out **what it costs them**. Then argue with their reasons.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture warm chairs by a fire. Outside, **the street** is being dug up. From inside, the trouble arrives only as **a view** through glass. How urgent would the roadworks feel to you?'},{kind:'explain',text:'For Veblen, institutions change under **the stress** of changing conditions. The wealthy are sheltered from that stress, so their views never demand an update. The news that something must change reaches them **last and weakest**.'},{kind:'story',text:'A members\u2019 club debates letting in newcomers. The members with the best tables and committee seats object loudest. They talk about tradition. Nobody needs to be a villain here. The old way simply suits **the people** sitting nearest the fire.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now spread it wider. The leisure class sets the standards of respectability that others imitate. What it treats as proper becomes proper for those below. So its slowness to change becomes **a drag** on everyone, perhaps on you.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Position isn\u2019t destiny. Some comfortable people champion reform, and some exposed groups fiercely resist change. The view is also easy to misuse: dismissing every argument as **mere interest** skips checking whether the reasoning is right.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Expect any **protected group** to change slowly, whatever it believes. Start with what the arrangement is worth to them. But **check their reasons** too, because your position might be shaping your view.'}]}}}},968,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'leviathan',kind:'book',promise:'Find out why people agree to be ruled',about:'Why would free people agree to be ruled? Thomas Hobbes asked this in 1651. A war was tearing his country apart. His answer is grim, but it still helps at work and at home. What would you give up for peace?',ideas:{leviathan_1:{title:'The First to Disarm',card:{insight:'When no one can make stopping safe, even kind people strike first.',tryThis:'When two sides will not back down, find someone both trust to set the rules.',skills:['thinking','leadership']},review:{prompt:'Two teams at work each hide their notes from the other. What fixes it?',right:'Someone both teams trust to make each side share',wrong:'A talk that asks both teams to be nicer',why:'Both teams stop hiding once someone they trust makes sharing safe.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two kids stand in the snow, each with a **big pile** of snowballs. Both are cold. Both want to go in. So why won\u2019t either of them **put theirs down**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'If you drop your pile first, the other kid **might still throw**. Even a kind kid can\u2019t be sure. Thomas Hobbes asked what happens when **nobody\u2019s in charge**. His answer: everyone stays scared, and nobody stops first.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The trouble isn\u2019t **mean people**. It\u2019s that nobody dares to go first. You need something both sides trust, like **a fair referee**, to make stopping safe.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two departments each keep their data **to themselves**. Each assumes the other does the same. Both would gain by sharing. Neither will go first. Who\u2019s **the villain** here?'},{kind:'story',text:'Thomas Hobbes wrote *Leviathan* in Paris, watching civil war tear England apart. It came out in 1651. He asked what\u2019s left when you remove every court, every officer, everyone whose word is final.'},{kind:'explain',text:'His answer was **mutual fear**. You can\u2019t tell who\u2019s peaceful, so you arm just in case. That alarms your neighbour, who arms too. Nobody is wicked. Each step is **sensible alone** and ruinous together.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'It\u2019s a **thought experiment**, not a history, so judge it by **the right test**.',left:{title:'A history claim',items:['What did happen?','Check the old records','Needs real evidence']},right:{title:'A what-if',items:['What would happen?','Think it through','Needs good reasons']}},{kind:'moral',text:'The departments don\u2019t need **better people**. They need something both can trust to **hold each side** to sharing. Then going first stops being a gamble.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Critics often say hunter-gatherer bands **weren\u2019t at war** all the time, so Hobbes was wrong. That sounds decisive. But was he ever really **writing history**, or asking you something else?'},{kind:'story',text:'Hobbes left England in 1640, fearing for his safety as king and Parliament quarrelled. He spent the next decade in Paris. There he wrote *Leviathan*, published in 1651, while civil war ran its course at home.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The state of nature is **a thought experiment**. Subtract every power that enforces anything, then ask what rational people do. Disarming alone is a risk you can\u2019t sensibly take. So everyone arms, and **mutual fear** follows.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture two firms that have sued each other for years. Each would happily drop its case. But whoever drops first is exposed to the other\u2019s. So both keep paying lawyers, and neither is being unreasonable.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Hobbes also pointed to some peoples of the Americas, which was prejudice, not observation. And the experiment removes too much. Reputation, kin and **repeated dealing** keep a lot of peace with no sovereign at all.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Judge Hobbes as a what-if, not a history. Fear is sensible for **each of you** and ruinous for all. The exit is **something both trust**, not better people.'}]},leviathan_2:{title:'Why People Stop Building',card:{insight:'People stop planting and building when what they make can be taken.',tryThis:'Tonight, back up one file or photo you worked hard to make, so it stays safe.',skills:['thinking','history']},review:{prompt:'In a town where deals are not kept, why do stores stay small?',right:'Owners will not build what can be taken from them',wrong:'The owners there just do not want to grow',why:'When what you build can be taken, you stop building for later.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You build a den in the woods. Every night, someone **smashes it**. You rebuild it twice. By the end of the week, **what do you** actually do?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Most people **stop building**. They\u2019re not lazy. There\u2019s just no point making what will be taken. Thomas Hobbes said a whole country works this way when **nobody keeps the peace**. No farms, no books, no towns.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Hard lives without rules don\u2019t come from **wild people**. They come from **no safe future**. When your work can be taken, you stop doing it.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re given a plot of land for free. The catch: anyone may take what grows, and nobody will stop them. Would you plant **an orchard** that fruits in **five years**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Almost nobody plants. You can\u2019t count on being **the one who picks**. So your horizon shrinks to today. Hobbes\u2019s famous grim list is really about this: **time horizons**, not savagery.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Before his five harsh words, he lists **what goes missing**: farming, sea trade, solid building, arts, letters, society. Every one of them **pays back slowly**. The misery at the end follows from that list.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Same people, same land. Only what they can **count on** changes, and so does **their horizon**.',left:{title:'With security',items:['Plant now, harvest later','Build to last','Study now, earn later']},right:{title:'Without it',items:['Eat today\u2019s food','Own what you carry','Learn only to survive']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Insecurity doesn\u2019t make people wicked. It makes them **short-sighted**, for good reasons. That\u2019s why Hobbes valued **an enforcing power** so highly: it lets you plan further ahead.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Hobbes\u2019s grim line about life without a ruler is **famous**. Most people quote only its ending. What happens to his argument if you read **the whole list**, in order?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Read it in order. It starts with farming and building, then **arts and letters**. Then society goes, and constant fear arrives. Only then come the five harsh words. The misery is **a consequence** of the absences.'},{kind:'story',text:'A farmer who plants fruit trees is planning **years** ahead. Someone who expects to be robbed tonight plans only for hours. Same skill, same land. What differs is how far ahead each can **sensibly plan**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a region where contracts are rarely enforced. Small firms stay small and seldom buy machinery. The owners aren\u2019t short of ambition. They just won\u2019t sink money into something that may not stay theirs.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Hobbes doubted this condition was ever general, and his claim about peoples of the Americas was prejudice. The argument is also used too fast. Needing **some enforcement** doesn\u2019t justify any particular coercion.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Hobbes\u2019s people aren\u2019t savages. They\u2019re rational, afraid and **unable to invest**. Give **your future** some security and the slow, building kind of work comes back.'}]},leviathan_3:{title:'Who Made This Rule',card:{insight:'People made most rules for a reason, so ask if it still holds.',tryThis:'Pick one rule at work or home. Ask someone why it was made.',skills:['leadership','thinking']},review:{prompt:'A rule has been in place for twenty years. What do you ask?',right:'Why it was made, and if that reason still holds',wrong:'Nothing, since a rule that old must be working',why:'People made the rule, so check its reason, not its age.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your class picks a captain for a football game. Everyone agrees to **follow her calls**. Is she in charge because she was **born to it**? Or is it something else?'},{kind:'explain',text:'She\u2019s in charge because **you all said yes**. Thomas Hobbes thought a country\u2019s ruler works the same way. Power doesn\u2019t come from God or birth. It comes from **a deal** the people make with each other.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Hobbes said you can\u2019t undo the deal. Later thinkers spotted **a twist**: if people made the power, people can **remake it**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your team never releases anything **on a Friday**. Nobody remembers why. Ask about it and you\u2019re told it\u2019s **always been so**. Is that a reason, or just a habit?'},{kind:'story',text:'Hobbes lived through a time when many said kings ruled **by God\u2019s choice** or by birth. He disagreed. In *Leviathan*, power comes from a covenant. Each person agrees with every other to give up the right to govern themselves.'},{kind:'explain',text:'He used this to defend **near-absolute rule**. Undo the covenant, he said, and you\u2019re back in the war of all against all. Later readers, including John Locke, kept the premise and **reversed the conclusion**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Hobbes built the premise for one conclusion. His own premise **permits another**, and it\u2019s **the one that lasted**.',left:{title:'Hobbes\u2019s conclusion',items:['Authority is fixed','You can\u2019t undo it','Rules are nature']},right:{title:'His premise allows',items:['Authority is agreed','Agreements can be reopened','Check the original reason']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Most rules you live under were **agreed by somebody**, once, for a reason. Find out what it was. If that reason **still holds**, keep the rule.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Imagine a club whose founders insist its rules can **never change**, because members joined on those terms. That sounds firm. But what if the founders\u2019 own logic is what lets you **reopen them**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'For Hobbes, the founding **covenant** is made by each person with every other, not with the ruler. So the ruler has promised nothing and **can\u2019t break it**. Subjects can\u2019t claim release because the ruler broke faith.'},{kind:'story',text:'John Locke and later thinkers of legitimate revolution took Hobbes\u2019s premise. If authority is built, they argued, it can be rebuilt. The American founders rested government on agreement too, for the opposite purpose.'},{kind:'story',text:'Back to the club. Its founders say members joined on fixed terms. Hobbes would agree and stop there. Locke\u2019s move is to keep the idea that rules are built, and ask whether these still serve.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The word covenant carries a **heavy load**. Hobbes counts submission to a conqueror, made in fear, as binding. Nobody alive actually signed. And a made rule isn\u2019t up for reopening whenever it\u2019s inconvenient.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Hobbes built his premise to make power **unbreakable**. It outlived him by doing the opposite. Anything you construct, **you can examine**, and sometimes remake.'}]},leviathan_4:{title:'One Word, Two Meanings',card:{insight:'Many long fights go on because one word means two things.',tryThis:'Next time a fight goes in circles, ask each side what the main word means.',skills:['communication','thinking']},review:{prompt:'Two sides argue for weeks but agree on every fact. What next?',right:'Ask each side what the main word means',wrong:'Gather more facts until one side gives in',why:'When both sides agree on the facts, the fight is about a word.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two friends argue for an hour about whether **a hot dog** is a sandwich. They agree on every fact about the hot dog. So why can\u2019t they **ever agree**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'To one, a sandwich is anything in bread. To the other, it needs **two slices**. They\u2019re using **one word** for two things. Thomas Hobbes said that before you argue, you should agree what your words mean.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Lots of fights aren\u2019t about facts at all. They\u2019re about **one word** meaning two things. Sort out **the word** first, and the fight often ends.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two colleagues have argued for **forty minutes** about whether a project is done. The code works. The guide isn\u2019t written. The client hasn\u2019t seen it. They agree on **every fact**. Can you see why it won\u2019t end?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Hobbes spent early chapters of *Leviathan* on **definitions**. He thought reasoning is a kind of **reckoning**, adding and subtracting names. If a word means one thing at the start and another at the end, the answer\u2019s wrong.'},{kind:'explain',text:'He compared words to **counters**, the tokens people once moved on a board to do sums. The wise use them to calculate. Fools treat them like coins, valuable in themselves, and take them **on trust**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'For the colleagues, *done* means two things, and that\u2019s the whole fight. Before **another round**, ask each side to **define** it. It\u2019ll cost you a minute.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'John Aubrey, who knew Hobbes, tells a story. At about forty, Hobbes found a copy of Euclid lying open in a library. He read one theorem and thought it **impossible**. What would you **do next**?'},{kind:'story',text:'He followed the proofs back, step by step, until **he was convinced**. From then on, Aubrey says, he loved geometry. It shaped *Leviathan*, which settles its key terms first, then reasons from them.'},{kind:'explain',text:'For Hobbes, reason is **reckoning with names**. A loose definition early on isn\u2019t a small slip. Every later step treats it as fixed, so the error **compounds**. The conclusion can be absurd while each step looks sound.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a team arguing for weeks about whether a new hire has been **a success**. Both sides cite the same numbers. Neither is lying. They\u2019ve simply never said what success would mean.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some disputes survive perfect definitions, because people **want different things**. And defining is a move: whoever fixes the terms often wins. Hobbes defines injustice as breaking a covenant, which does political work early.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When the facts are agreed and the fight goes on, check **the word**. Write your definition. Then ask if **the other side** would sign it.'}]},leviathan_5:{title:'The Line No Deal Crosses',card:{insight:'People make deals to stay safe, so no deal can make you risk your life.',tryThis:'Write down one thing no job or boss could ever make you do.',skills:['philosophy','career']},review:{prompt:'Your boss orders you up an unsafe ladder in high wind. What now?',right:'Say no, since no deal can make you risk your life',wrong:'Climb it, since you promised to do what he says',why:'You took the job to earn money, not to risk your life.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You join a club and promise to do what **the leader** says. Tidying up is fine. Carrying the bags is fine too. Then one day the leader says **jump off the roof**. Do you have to?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Thomas Hobbes gave rulers **nearly all the power**. But he said there\u2019s one thing no deal can take away. You never have to let yourself be killed. Staying safe was **the whole point** of the deal.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Every deal has a reason behind it. If a rule **breaks that reason**, it can\u2019t bind you. Knowing your line makes **smaller choices** easier.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Hobbes hands his ruler almost **unlimited power**. Speech, worship and property are all his to regulate. Yet he names **one right** that every subject keeps, whatever they agreed. Can you guess what it is?'},{kind:'explain',text:'It\u2019s the right to defend **your own life**. People submit to a ruler to stay alive. So nothing they agree can require them to die. That\u2019s not mercy. It\u2019s **Hobbes being consistent**.'},{kind:'story',text:'He follows the logic further than you\u2019d expect. A condemned man may resist the officers who come to execute him. Nobody is bound to accuse himself. These rights rarely work in practice, but they\u2019re never lost.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The logic runs the other way too. **Obedience was owed** in exchange for **protection**. A ruler who can no longer protect you has, on Hobbes\u2019s account, ended your duty to obey.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The limit comes from **the purpose** of the deal, not from a list of rights. So find your own line. Knowing it makes every **lesser bargain** easier.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A ruler is cruel and unjust, but keeps the country safe from invasion and civil war. On Hobbes\u2019s logic, do **you still owe** that ruler **your obedience**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Hobbes calls protection the **consideration** for obedience: what you get in return. A surrender made for safety can\u2019t coherently **require you to die**. So the right to resist your own destruction survives every covenant.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take a condemned man. His sentence is lawful, yet Hobbes says he may resist the executioners. He\u2019ll almost certainly fail. The right lets him struggle, not escape. But it hasn\u2019t been surrendered, because it can\u2019t be.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a worker whose contract says follow every instruction. A manager orders her onto **visibly unsafe** scaffolding. She took the job to live better, not to risk dying. The order defeats the reason she owed obedience.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The right is real and **nearly empty**. It covers life and body, not speech, worship or property. A ruler who protects you while oppressing you keeps your obedience. Royalists even feared it licensed submission to whoever had won.'},{kind:'moral',text:'So the cruel ruler keeps **your obedience**, uncomfortably. Protection, not justice, is the test. That\u2019s the argument\u2019s strength and **its sharpest limit**.'}]},leviathan_6:{title:'The Giant Made of People',card:{insight:'One picture shows the whole book, a giant made of people.',tryThis:'Before your next big talk, draw your main point as one picture. Show it first.',skills:['communication','thinking']},review:{prompt:'A friend says the giant on the front is crushing his people. Your reply?',right:'The giant is made of the people who join him',wrong:'Right, the people are trapped under his feet',why:'His strength comes from the people, so his body is made of them.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'At some big games, every fan holds up **one coloured card**. From far away, the cards make one giant picture. No single fan is the picture. So **where is it**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Thomas Hobbes put a picture like that at the front of his 1651 book. A **giant king** rises over the land. Look closely, and you\u2019ll see his body is made of **tiny people**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The picture tells **the whole book** before you read a word. The ruler isn\u2019t above the people. He\u2019s **made of them**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Before the first chapter, *Leviathan* opens with an engraving. A **crowned giant** rises over hills and a walled town. What do you notice when you look at **its body**?'},{kind:'story',text:'It\u2019s made of hundreds of small human figures, all **facing inward** towards the head. In one hand the giant holds a sword. In the other, a bishop\u2019s crozier. Civil and church power sit in one body.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The figures face the head, not you. They\u2019re not **being crushed** by power. They\u2019re what it\u2019s **made of**. The whole book argues that the ruler\u2019s authority comes from those it rules, pooled together.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Hobbes put his **thesis** on the cover. The sovereign is made of the people, and its power is **theirs, pooled**. If you can draw your argument, put the drawing first.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'The giant on Hobbes\u2019s cover holds **a sword** in his right hand. That\u2019s the power to make laws and keep the peace. What do you think he put in **his left**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A **crozier**: church authority held by the same body as the state\u2019s. It\u2019s Hobbes\u2019s most contested claim, and he put it on the title page. The sovereign is one **artificial person**, made of many natural ones.'},{kind:'story',text:'The name comes from the sea monster in the Book of Job, which no earthly power can match. Below the giant, paired panels set each civil power beside a church one: castle and church, cannon and excommunication.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a class poster where each pupil paints one small square. Step back and it\u2019s one image. No pupil is the poster, but without them there isn\u2019t one. That\u2019s Hobbes\u2019s sovereign in miniature.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. A picture persuades **without arguing**. This one shows perfect unity, every figure turned the same way. It can\u2019t show the dissenters, the conquered or those who never agreed. Hobbes\u2019s theory struggles with them too.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The engraving argues **before a word** is read. Authority is pooled from below, undivided at the top. If your argument fits one picture, ask **who it leaves out**.'}]},leviathan_7:{title:'The Sword Behind the Promise',card:{insight:'A promise holds when breaking it costs more than keeping it.',tryThis:'Before you sell to a stranger, use a site that holds the cash. Then ship it.',skills:['relationships','thinking']},review:{prompt:'You pay the whole bill. Your roommate pays half later. What makes it safe?',right:'A plan that makes breaking the deal cost more',wrong:'Trusting that you are both honest and mean it',why:'Honest people still slip, so make breaking the deal cost more.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You agree to swap with a girl from another class. Your football card for her **sticker book**. She says: give me yours today, and I\u2019ll bring mine **tomorrow**. Do you hand it over?'},{kind:'explain',text:'If you hand yours over first, she **might keep both**. She\u2019s probably honest, but you **can\u2019t be sure**. Now picture **a teacher** holding your card until her book arrives. Then nobody has to trust anybody.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Everyone agrees promises should be kept. The hard part is **going first**. Thomas Hobbes said a promise holds when breaking it costs **more than keeping** it.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two flatmates agree that one pays a **large bill** now and the other repays half next month. Both are honest. Both mean it. So why does the first one still **feel uneasy**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Hobbes lists **nineteen laws of nature**: seek peace, keep your agreements, don\u2019t be cruel, and more. He calls them theorems about what helps people survive, **not commands**. Then he adds a catch.'},{kind:'explain',text:'They bind your conscience always, but your actions only where **some power** makes keeping them safe. Whoever performs first is exposed. Without that power, he says, a promise is **only words**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Some online marketplaces hold a buyer\u2019s payment until the goods arrive. Neither side has to trust the other. Breaking the deal simply stops paying. That\u2019s Hobbes\u2019s answer **on a small scale**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Nobody needs to become trustworthy. You just need a check that makes **breaking the deal** cost more than **keeping it**. Build that before you sign.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two countries sign a treaty to **cut their weapons**. There are no inspections and no court above them. Both leaders shake hands. What would Hobbes predict happens **next**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'This is **the hinge** of the book. Rational people would agree to cooperate, Hobbes says. But agreement isn\u2019t the binding part. The sword binds, by changing **the payoff**, not anyone\u2019s character.'},{kind:'story',text:'Between the two countries there\u2019s no common power. Each gains by cheating if cheating is safe. So the treaty is only words, which is why such agreements usually come with inspections.'},{kind:'story',text:'Closer to home, you lend a friend money until payday. Your friend is honest. Hobbes wouldn\u2019t deny that pride and conscience keep people to their word. He\u2019d say they\u2019re just **too unreliable** to build a country on.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Most agreements are kept with no court in sight, through reputation, repeated dealing and habit. And someone must **hold the sword**. If the enforcer is party to no covenant, what keeps it honest?'},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t build on **good intentions** alone. Ask what makes keeping faith **safer than breaking** it, for both of you. Then you won\u2019t need to trust anyone\u2019s character.'}]},leviathan_8:{title:'Where the Rules Go Quiet',card:{insight:'Your freedom is what the rules leave out, so count the rules.',tryThis:'Before you sign a lease, count the rules about what you can do.',skills:['thinking','leadership']},review:{prompt:'A job handbook says you are free, then runs eighty pages. How do you check?',right:'Count the rules in it, not the nice words',wrong:'Trust it, since it says freedom comes first',why:'Your freedom is what the rules leave out, not what a page says.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two schools both say their pupils are **free**. One has five rules. The other has fifty, about your hair, your bag and your shoes. Which one gives **you more freedom**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Thomas Hobbes said your freedom is **the space** the rules don\u2019t cover. Where they say nothing, you choose. But the ruler could add **a new rule** tomorrow, and that bit of freedom would be gone.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t trust **the poster** that says you\u2019re free. **Count the rules** instead. Your real freedom is whatever they leave out.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'One firm\u2019s handbook opens with a page about **freedom and trust**, then runs to eighty pages of procedure. Another has six rules and no values page. Where would you have **more room**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'For Hobbes, a subject\u2019s liberty lies wherever **the law is silent**: whatever the sovereign hasn\u2019t regulated. His examples are buying, selling, making contracts, choosing your trade and **raising your children**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'That\u2019s far thinner than the later idea of **protected rights**, and Hobbes **meant it** that way.',left:{title:'Later liberal view',items:['A zone the state can\u2019t enter','Rights the ruler must respect']},right:{title:'Hobbes\u2019s view',items:['Whatever isn\u2019t legislated yet','Could be filled tomorrow']}},{kind:'moral',text:'So ignore the values page. Count what the eighty pages **actually specify**, and see **what\u2019s left**. That\u2019s how much room you have.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'An app has **only three rules**. One of them lets it ban any user, for any reason. By Hobbes\u2019s own measure, how free are **its users**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Hobbes defines liberty in general as **the absence** of outside obstacles to motion. Water walled in by a vessel can\u2019t spread. Water with no walls can. A subject\u2019s liberty is the room **the law\u2019s walls** haven\u2019t enclosed yet.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a constitution that declares **sweeping freedoms**. Its laws then regulate what you may print, where you may travel and whom you may meet. By Hobbes\u2019s count, those people aren\u2019t very free.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now the app. Three rules sounds like plenty of silence. But one rule giving **unlimited discretion** makes every silence provisional. The ban can arrive anywhere, so none of the unregulated space is really safe.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. If liberty is only what\u2019s not yet regulated, nothing stops **tomorrow\u2019s law** from taking it. Later liberal thought was spent building that missing protection. And one law can outweigh a hundred.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Hobbes\u2019s measure is useful: count what\u2019s specified, not **what\u2019s declared**. But weigh the rules **as well as** counting them. One open-ended rule can make all your silence **borrowed**.'}]},leviathan_9:{title:'Who Gets the Last Word',card:{insight:'When two people both claim the last word, being nice will not end the fight.',tryThis:'Find one choice you share with someone. Agree on who gets the last word.',skills:['leadership','history']},review:{prompt:'Two bosses can each stop a launch. They keep getting stuck. Why?',right:'Both can claim the last word, so settle who decides',wrong:'One of them is stubborn and needs to give in',why:'The fight ends only when one person gets the final say.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your mum says bedtime is **eight o\u2019clock**. Your gran, who lives with you, says nine. Both think they get the last word. Neither is being mean. So why do the rows **never stop**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'There are **two last words**, and that\u2019s the trouble. In Thomas Hobbes\u2019s England, church leaders and rulers both said they spoke for God. People didn\u2019t know **whom to obey**, and that helped start a war.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Even kind people clash when both get **the final say**. So Hobbes put the ruler above the church. Your fix is simpler: decide **who decides**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Roughly half of *Leviathan* is about **scripture**. Most readers skip it. Yet it\u2019s where Hobbes took **the real risk**. What made it so dangerous for him?'},{kind:'explain',text:'His argument was **structural**. A subject who obeys a church apart from the ruler has **two sovereigns**. Divided sovereignty leads back to war. So the public reading of religion must belong to the civil ruler.'},{kind:'story',text:'He was accused of atheism, and clergy attacked the book in print. After the monarchy returned in 1660, he was effectively barred from publishing on religion in England. He never took the argument back.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Why doesn\u2019t goodwill fix it? If two people can both **claim the last word**, every serious disagreement turns into a fight over who has it. Better people would still face **that question**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A dispute about **who decides** can\u2019t be settled by being nicer. Settle where **the final say** sits, before the next row rather than during it. You\u2019ll save yourself the war.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'An international court and a national supreme court both claim **the final say** over the same case. Each has good lawyers and good reasons. What would Hobbes say **the real dispute** is?'},{kind:'explain',text:'It\u2019s not what the law says. It\u2019s who has **the last word**. For Hobbes, a church obeyed apart from the sovereign is **a second sovereign**, and divided sovereignty returns you to war.'},{kind:'story',text:'To get there, Parts Three and Four reread scripture at length. Hobbes doubts Moses wrote every book attributed to him. He reads God\u2019s kingdom as a real, civil one, and denies the clergy any power of their own.'},{kind:'story',text:'In 1666 the House of Commons, debating a bill against atheism, asked a committee to look into *Leviathan*. Hobbes, in his late seventies, was alarmed enough to write on heresy law. He was never prosecuted.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Putting religion under the ruler lets the state dictate public worship and **punish dissenters**. The later liberal answer went the other way: separate church and state. And his own punishment proves little. Authorities punish critics who are wrong, too.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Where two bodies can **both claim** the last word, the fight is about who has it. You can\u2019t fix that with **goodwill**. Hobbes settled it, then paid for saying so.'}]}}}},969,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'lombard-street',kind:'book',promise:'See why bank panics start and what stops them',about:'How can a bank that owns plenty run out of cash fast? Walter Bagehot ran a paper about money in London. In 1873, he wrote this book about its banks. Here is what starts a panic and what stops it. Ready to see how?',ideas:{'lombard-street_1':{title:'When Everyone Asks at Once',card:{insight:'A bank can pay any one person, but not everyone at once.',tryThis:'Look for the FDIC sign on your bank\u2019s app. It means the government backs your savings.',skills:['money','thinking']},review:{prompt:'Your bank lends out most of the money people put in. Is that a problem?',right:'No, it can pay anyone who asks, just not everyone at once',wrong:'Yes, it means the bank has lost your money',why:'Lending is fine, and only a rush on one day can break the bank.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Ten friends each give Maya **\xa35** to look after. She lends \xa340 of it to her aunt until next month. What happens if **all ten** ask for it back today?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Maya has only **\xa310** in her hand. She can pay one or two friends, but not all ten. The money isn\u2019t lost. It\u2019s just with her aunt. A bank works the same way: it **lends most** of your money out.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A bank can pay **any one person** who asks. It can\u2019t pay **everyone at once**. That\u2019s fine, as long as you don\u2019t all ask on the same day.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You hear a rumour that your bank is in trouble. Walking past, you see **a queue** outside. You don\u2019t know if the rumour is true. Do you **join the queue**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Walter Bagehot, editor of The Economist, wrote *Lombard Street* in 1873. He started from a fact most readers avoided. A bank promises your money back on demand. Then it lends most of that money out. That isn\u2019t a flaw. It\u2019s the business.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The promise holds for **each of you** and fails for **all of you** together.',left:{title:'One depositor',items:['Can withdraw today','Is paid in full','Never sees the lending']},right:{title:'Everyone at once',items:['Can\u2019t all be paid','Front of the queue wins','Find most money lent out']}},{kind:'explain',text:'So yes, you join, and you\u2019re right to. Whoever waits gets what\u2019s left. On **ordinary days** withdrawals are unconnected. A panic connects them. That\u2019s why calm speeches fail. The cure must make **running first** pay nothing extra.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Banking runs on a promise that\u2019s **true for each** person and false for all. It works only while people **don\u2019t all ask** at once. Fix the arrangement, not the mood.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'London\u2019s money market did its business on one street, **Lombard Street**. In 1873 Walter Bagehot named a book after it. He opened on a fact most readers preferred **not to dwell on**. What was it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A bank owes **your money** on demand and has lent most of it out. That\u2019s fractional reserve banking, and it\u2019s ordinary, not a gamble. The promise is **true for each** depositor and false for all of them together.'},{kind:'story',text:'Think of an ordinary week. One person withdraws to buy a car, another to pay a bill. Then a doubt spreads about one bank. Now waiting is costly, because whoever waits may get nothing. So everyone withdraws together.'},{kind:'explain',text:'That last step isn\u2019t hysteria. Each person is being **sensible**. So no speech about calm can change the arithmetic of the queue. Bagehot wanted a **structural fix**: arrange things so withdrawing early confers no advantage.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a start-up that collects a year\u2019s fees upfront and spends them on growth. That\u2019s its business, as lending is a bank\u2019s. The danger isn\u2019t the spending. It\u2019s the one week when many customers ask for refunds together.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Calling something just the business can excuse **any fragility**. Some mismatches are simply bad practice, far larger than ordinary days justify. And removing the reason to run has a cost: a guarantee can encourage the risks it covers.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your bank\u2019s promise is **true for each** of you and false for all at once. A run is **sensible people** reacting to each other. So the cure changes the arrangement, not the mood.'}]},'lombard-street_2':{title:'Rich but Out of Cash',card:{insight:'Owning a lot does not mean you can pay a bill today.',tryThis:'Write down how much cash you could get this week, without selling things cheap.',skills:['money','thinking']},review:{prompt:'Your friend owns a big house and says cash is never a problem. Your reply?',right:'A house is not cash you can pay with today',wrong:'True, a big house means the bills get paid',why:'What you own and what you can pay with this week are not the same.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Leo owns a **huge card set** worth a lot of money. The ice-cream van stops outside his house. Leo has no coins at all. Can he **buy an ice cream**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Not today, he can\u2019t. Owning a lot isn\u2019t the same as **having cash now**. A bank can own more than it owes and still be stuck. It can\u2019t turn its things into cash **fast enough**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Being rich and **having cash** are two different things. You can have plenty of one and **none of the other**. Before you pay, check what you can use today.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A builder finishes **three houses** worth far more than she owes. None has sold yet. Her suppliers want paying **on Friday**. Is she rich, or is she broke? What would you call her?'},{kind:'story',text:'On paper she\u2019s well off. On Friday she can\u2019t pay. If she\u2019s forced to sell this week, the houses go for a fraction of their value. A timing problem turns into a real loss.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Bagehot split two problems that share **one symptom**: neither can **pay today**.',left:{title:'Insolvent',items:['Has lost the money','Owes more than it owns','Should be allowed to fail']},right:{title:'Illiquid',items:['Has the money tied up','Owns more than it owes','Fails only without help']}},{kind:'explain',text:'In a panic **nobody wants to buy**, so no asset has a reliable price. Sound and broken banks look alike. Bagehot\u2019s test was one a lender could use that week: lend against **good security**, judged by normal times.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Rich **on paper** isn\u2019t the same as able to **pay today**. Your net worth is what you own. Your liquidity is what you can pay with now.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'In May 1866 a huge London firm called **Overend, Gurney** collapsed. The Bank of England had refused to rescue it. The panic that followed hit **sound firms** too. Why would a healthy firm fall?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Because two different problems look **identical in a panic**. An insolvent bank has lost the money and should fail. An illiquid one has it **tied up**. Without help, it fails anyway, destroying value that existed.'},{kind:'story',text:'Bagehot watched that panic as editor of The Economist. When nobody wants to buy, no asset has a fair price. A sound bank forced to sell at panic prices can make itself insolvent. The rush to sell destroys the value.'},{kind:'explain',text:'So how does a lender sort them mid-crisis? Bagehot didn\u2019t ask anyone to measure true worth. His test was **good security**: lend against what would be sound collateral **in normal times**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture yourself owning a flat worth far more than your mortgage. The boiler breaks and the repair is due today. Your savings are spent. You aren\u2019t poor. You just can\u2019t pay this week without selling something cheap.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Judging collateral by normal times assumes they\u2019ll return. If prices have **really fallen**, the lender rescues the insolvent at its own cost. And a bank illiquid for long enough often becomes insolvent anyway.'},{kind:'moral',text:'**Cannot pay now** is a different problem from **cannot pay**. Mix them up and a scare becomes a collapse. Check your own liquidity, not just what you\u2019re worth.'}]},'lombard-street_3':{title:'Bagehot\u2019s Rule',card:{insight:'In a panic, lend a lot, at a high price, and hold something of value.',tryThis:'Next time you help with the same mess, ask to be paid back, plus a small cost.',skills:['leadership','money']},review:{prompt:'In a panic, a big bank lends to everyone for free. What went wrong?',right:'It left out the high price, so the help was too cheap',wrong:'Nothing, the rule is just to lend a lot',why:'Free help draws in banks that do not need it, so the price matters.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'It\u2019s exam morning and half the class has **forgotten a pen**. The teacher has a big box of spares. She hands them out slowly, one at a time. Soon kids **start to grab**. Why?'},{kind:'story',text:'They fear there won\u2019t be enough. So next time she holds up the box and says there are plenty. Anyone can take one. But you leave your library card with her and stay to tidy up after.'},{kind:'moral',text:'In a panic, **lend lots** so the fear stops. Make the help **cost something**, so only those in need ask. And hold **something good** until it\u2019s paid back.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Banks are short of cash and frightened. You run **the one institution** that can still lend. Ration it carefully, or hand it out cheaply to all? **What do you do**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Bagehot\u2019s rule has three parts. Lend **freely**, so the fear of running out stops. Charge **a high rate**, so only the needy borrow. Take **good security**, so you aren\u2019t absorbing bad bets.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Rationing feels prudent, but it **backfires**. When help comes grudgingly, every bank assumes it may be the one refused. So each hoards whatever it has. Half-measures don\u2019t save half the system. They **confirm the fear**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Cheap help has the opposite problem. A bank that borrows cheaply in every crisis has less reason to avoid the next. The **high rate** is the part that disciplines. It\u2019s also the part **most often dropped**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Lend freely, at a high rate, on good security. Drop **any one part** and the rule fails. Without the rate, your rescue becomes **a subsidy**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Every crisis lender faces **the same bind**. Lend too little and the panic wins. Lend too easily and you teach banks that **recklessness gets rescued**. Is there a way through?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The lender of last resort lends when **no one else will**, because it holds the final reserve. The idea is older than Bagehot. *Lombard Street* gave it **working rules**: freely, at a high rate, on good security.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture the Bank of England in a panic, doors wide open. Any bank with sound collateral can borrow as much as it needs. But the price is set high, and everyone can see it. Banks with other options stay away.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now think of someone rescued cheaply from a mess of their own making. A parent clears a teenager\u2019s overdraft, again, at no cost. Next term the overdraft is back. Cheap rescue taught exactly the wrong lesson.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. A rate high enough to deter opportunists can **mark borrowers** as weak, so they wait too long. Good security in a crash depends on prices nobody trusts. And under pressure, the high rate is easily dropped.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Freely and expensively **aren\u2019t opposites**, and you need both. Add good security and you end the panic without **rewarding recklessness**. That\u2019s why the rule still carries his name.'}]},'lombard-street_4':{title:'The Lifeboat in View',card:{insight:'People rush when they do not know if help will come.',tryThis:'Write down one plan your family needs, and share it on a calm day.',skills:['leadership','communication']},review:{prompt:'Your school may close for snow this winter. When should it share its plan?',right:'Now, on a calm day, so nobody has to rush',wrong:'On the morning it snows, once it knows more',why:'A plan known ahead of time means nobody has to rush.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Before you go into the theme park, your dad says: if we\u2019re split up, wait **by the big clock**. Later you lose sight of him. Your heart jumps. **What do you do**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'You don\u2019t panic, because you already know the plan. Banks work the same way. People rush a bank when they **don\u2019t know** if help will come. If they know **ahead of time**, most never rush.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Tell people the plan **before the trouble**. Not knowing is a big part of **what causes panic**. When help is certain, nobody needs to rush.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your company says job cuts are being considered. Then it says **nothing for three weeks**. By week two, your best colleagues have taken other offers. What did **the silence** cost?'},{kind:'story',text:'The cuts might have been small. The uncertainty did the damage on its own. Bagehot saw the same thing in banking panics. People run because they can\u2019t tell whether help will come, or on what terms.'},{kind:'explain',text:'So he insisted the Bank of England state its rule **in advance**, publicly. Then waiting becomes safe, and nobody needs to scramble. A market that knows help exists on **stated terms** rarely needs to use it.'},{kind:'explain',text:'**Won\u2019t people** take more risk if rescue is certain? Bagehot\u2019s answer was **the price**. Announce the support, and make it expensive. You remove the reason to run, not the reason to be careful.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A rule announced **before a crisis** prevents panics. A rule applied **during one** can only end them. So publish your plan while it\u2019s calm.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture a lifeboat hanging ready, **in plain view**, on a calm day. You don\u2019t need it. Yet its being seen is doing **real work**. What work?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Bagehot didn\u2019t only say what the Bank of England should do in a panic. He insisted it **say so publicly**, beforehand. Uncertainty about help is **itself a cause** of panic, so the announcement does most of the work.'},{kind:'story',text:'The Bank preferred to keep its options open. Central banks since have mostly agreed. They call it constructive ambiguity: keeping the terms of rescue unclear. Bagehot saw that silence as part of the disease.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture two schools on a snowy morning. One published its closure rule in autumn. Parents there just check the rule. At the other, everyone phones the office at once, and the lines jam.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The objection is **moral hazard**: a guaranteed rescue encourages the conduct that needs rescuing. Bagehot\u2019s reply was the penalty rate. Announce the support, and make it **expensive**. Clarity lowers the chance of panic but raises that risk.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. A price answers moral hazard only in part. A firm sure of surviving may still take **risks it would avoid** otherwise. And a promise written for the last panic may not fit the next.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Help everyone can see **before the storm** stops the rush. Your plan works by being known, **not by being used**. The argument over its cost still isn\u2019t settled.'}]},'lombard-street_5':{title:'Old Advice That Still Fits',card:{insight:'When old advice still fits, the cause of the problem has not changed.',tryThis:'Write down one problem that keeps coming back. Then write what has stayed the same.',skills:['thinking','history']},review:{prompt:'A note from ten years ago fits this month\u2019s problem at work. What now?',right:'Look for what has stayed the same since then',wrong:'Thank the person who wrote the old note',why:'A close fit after many years points to a cause that never changed.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'There\u2019s **a dip** in the school playground. Every time it rains, a puddle forms in the same spot. New kids splash in it every year. Why does the puddle **keep coming back**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Because **the dip** is still there. In 1873 Walter Bagehot wrote a book about London\u2019s banks. In 2008, people fixing a huge banking crisis kept **pointing to it**. A book from before cars still fitted.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Banks still make the **same promise**: your money back when you ask. While the promise stays the same, **the same trouble** keeps coming back.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'In 2008, people running banks Bagehot **couldn\u2019t have imagined** faced a panic. They reached for a book from 1873. Its words still described **their crisis**. Should that make you admire the book, or worry?'},{kind:'story',text:'Bagehot edited The Economist and knew the men who ran the City of London. His book isn\u2019t a theory. It reports how the money market worked: who held reserves, who lent overnight, where the chain would break.'},{kind:'explain',text:'He wasn\u2019t applying a doctrine. He asked what would stop **this failure** in this arrangement. A rule drawn from **how a promise breaks** still fits wherever the same promise is made.'},{kind:'moral',text:'That staying power is **a warning** as much as a compliment. The failure keeps returning because the promise **underneath hasn\u2019t changed**. When old writing fits you, look at what\u2019s stayed the same.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A step in an old building has a **hollow** worn by generations of feet. A brand-new boot lands **exactly in it**. What does the fit tell you: something about the boot, or the step?'},{kind:'story',text:'Bagehot was a banker\u2019s son who worked in the family bank. He edited The Economist from 1861 until his death in 1877. *Lombard Street* reads like reporting: how bills were discounted and who lent to whom overnight.'},{kind:'explain',text:'His rule was **derived, not applied**. He asked what would stop this failure: one reserve, overnight lending, cash owed on demand. Because the answer was tied to **the promise**, it outlived the institutions of 1873.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a safety report from the 1950s. It describes a modern factory accident almost exactly. You could admire its authors, or copy their rules. The useful question is what hasn\u2019t changed in the work itself.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Lasting isn\u2019t proof of being right. A vocabulary can survive because it **suits its users**. Critics argued that much of the 2008 lending ignored Bagehot\u2019s terms on rate and collateral, while borrowing his name.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Old writing that fits **your situation** is a prompt to check, **not a verdict**. Ask what\u2019s stayed the same underneath. That\u2019s usually where the trouble lives.'}]},'lombard-street_6':{title:'Run the System You Have',card:{insight:'Run the setup you have well, even if you would not have built it.',tryThis:'Find one thing everyone counts on that nobody looks after. Write down whose job it is.',skills:['thinking','leadership']},review:{prompt:'Your town\u2019s one hospital ended up on the far side of a river. What now?',right:'Plan around where it is, and run it well',wrong:'Argue first about whether it should be there',why:'A setup can be odd and still be worth running well.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'In your house, **one spare key** hangs by the door. Your mum uses it, and so does the neighbour who feeds the cat. Nobody decided this. What happens if **that key** is lost?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Everyone\u2019s **locked out**. In 1873, Walter Bagehot saw the same thing in England\u2019s banks. All the spare cash for emergencies sat in **one bank**, the Bank of England. Nobody chose this. It just grew.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Bagehot didn\u2019t ask whether it was **a good plan**. He asked how to **run it well**, since it\u2019s what you have. You can do the same with your spare key.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Over the years, one engineer has become **the only person** who understands your billing system. Nobody planned it. Is it wise? Does arguing about that **change anything** this quarter?'},{kind:'story',text:'Bagehot faced the same puzzle. England kept its entire last-resort cash reserve in one institution, the Bank of England. Nobody ever decided that. It accumulated. Nobody starting fresh would build it, but it\u2019s the system there was.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Bagehot kept **two questions** apart, and spent his book on **the second**.',left:{title:'Is it good?',items:['Would we design this?','Who\u2019s to blame?']},right:{title:'Running it well',items:['Admit what it is','Name who holds it','Plan around it']}},{kind:'explain',text:'His real complaint was **denial**. The Bank\u2019s directors long said the reserve wasn\u2019t their job. Yet the whole system relied on them. A duty nobody admits to is **a duty nobody prepares** for.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Whether an arrangement is good and how to run it are **separate questions**. Start with the second. Say out loud what your arrangement is, then **plan around it**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Imagine a vault where a dozen doors all open onto **one box**. From above the layout is obvious. From inside any single room, it\u2019s **easy to miss**. Who decided it should be built that way?'},{kind:'story',text:'Nobody did. The Bank of England was founded in 1694 as a private company lending to the government. Its privileges drew other banks to keep their spare balances there. By Bagehot\u2019s day it held the country\u2019s ultimate reserve.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Bagehot\u2019s target wasn\u2019t the concentration itself. It was **the denial**. The directors disclaimed the role while the system depended on them performing it. You **can\u2019t discharge a duty** well while denying you hold it.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Why not redesign it? He granted that many banks each holding their own reserve would be **more natural**. But he judged it out of reach. The confidence built around one reserve **couldn\u2019t be rebuilt** to order.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a small charity where the founder\u2019s spreadsheet quietly became the only record of every donor. Nobody chose it. Replacing it today would lose years of notes. The sensible step is naming who keeps it, and backing it up.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Accepting an arrangement can harden into **defending it**. Saying it\u2019s what we have can start good running, or excuse never reforming anything. An arrangement worth running may still be worth slowly changing.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Separate **whether it\u2019s good** from **how to run it**. Start by saying aloud what your arrangement already is. Then you can run it well, and change it slowly if you must.'}]},'lombard-street_7':{title:'Built Slowly, Lost Fast',card:{insight:'Trust takes years to build and can be lost in one day.',tryThis:'Name one person who trusts you with something. Write down how long that trust took to build.',skills:['relationships','money']},review:{prompt:'A store could switch to a cheap supplier that is often late. What should it weigh?',right:'A small saving against trust that takes years to build',wrong:'Just the money, since most shoppers will not see late orders',why:'Trust is lost fast but built slowly, so weigh the slow rebuild.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Sam has lent you **his football** all year, and you always gave it back. Then he finds out you lost his pencil case and **blamed someone else**. What happens next time you ask?'},{kind:'story',text:'He says no. A whole year of trust is gone in one afternoon. And saying sorry won\u2019t win it back by tomorrow. You\u2019ll need to be reliable for a long time again.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Trust **grows slowly**, from being reliable for a long time. It can be lost **much faster**. Banks run on trust too, and nobody can order people to trust a bank.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A restaurant has served the same town for **twelve years**. One evening, a photo of its kitchen spreads online. The bookings **empty in a weekend**. How long will it take to fill them again?'},{kind:'story',text:'Not a weekend. A new chef and a clean inspection won\u2019t do it. Customers need proof after proof, meal after meal. Destroying their trust quickly does nothing to speed up winning it back.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Bagehot said credit is **a set of opinions** about other people. It grows from long good conduct, and no authority can create it. An opinion built on years can be **overturned by one** piece of evidence.'},{kind:'explain',text:'That\u2019s why he urged care in changing the money market. Its value lay in **confidence**, which **can\u2019t be rebuilt** to order. Treat anything made of trust the same way, including your own good name.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your trust takes **years to build** and an afternoon to lose. The rebuild is slow, however fast the fall. So **price the downside** before you risk it.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A tall stack of tiles was built **one at a time**, to head height. Then one hand draws a tile **from the bottom**. How long did it take to build? How long to fall?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Bagehot called credit **a growth**, not a manufacture. It\u2019s a set of opinions about others, formed by experience. No decree can produce it. Building it takes decades, while **collapse can take** an afternoon.'},{kind:'story',text:'He used this to explain why England\u2019s one-reserve system had to be run, not replaced. The confidence placed in it had built up over generations. A better design on paper would start with none of that trust.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now think of a currency, or a brand. Each exists only in other people\u2019s expectations. A brand might take thirty years to earn its name and one recall to lose it. Treating the two speeds as equal is how such things get spent.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The asymmetry is a tendency, not a law. Confidence sometimes returns fast when **the cause is visible** and fixed. And the case for caution can be used to block every reform.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your trust rises slowly and **falls suddenly**. You can\u2019t order its rebuild. So give a small chance of losing it **more weight** than its odds suggest.'}]},'lombard-street_8':{title:'No One in Charge',card:{insight:'A big job needs one owner who stays long enough to learn it.',tryThis:'Name the one thing your home or work can\u2019t get wrong. Write down who owns it.',skills:['leadership','productivity']},review:{prompt:'All your customers pay on one site, and whoever is free fixes it. Problem?',right:'Yes, a job this big needs one named owner',wrong:'No, sharing the work keeps everyone fresh',why:'A big job with no named owner breaks when times get hard.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your class has a hamster. Each week **a different kid** feeds it. Nobody is in charge of the hamster overall. Who notices if it **looks ill**, or its cage needs cleaning?'},{kind:'story',text:'In 1873, Walter Bagehot spotted the same gap at the Bank of England. It held the spare cash for the whole country. Yet merchants took turns running it. The head changed every two years.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When a **big job** has no one named in charge, it goes wrong when things get hard. So you **give it a name** before the trouble comes. That\u2019s what Bagehot asked for.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Who owns the **one thing** your organisation simply can\u2019t get wrong? Try answering now. Notice **how long** the answer takes, and whether it\u2019s a name or a shrug.'},{kind:'story',text:'In Bagehot\u2019s day, the Bank of England held the nation\u2019s reserve. Its directors came from trade and took turns. The Governor changed about every two years. They were able and meant well, but none was chosen for banking skill.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Bagehot didn\u2019t demand their removal. He pointed out that the set-up guaranteed they\u2019d be **gone before the results** arrived. It was harmless when the Bank was one among many. It became **dangerous** once everything rested on it.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Nobody notices the day **that line is crossed**. So nothing changes by itself. His remedy was unglamorous: say the duty out loud, and add **one permanent expert** beneath the rotating heads.'},{kind:'moral',text:'An unnamed duty done by **rotating amateurs** fails under pressure. If something has become critical, give it **a named owner** now, before you need one.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture a boardroom where every chair is empty and **the nameplate** lies face down. The role is real. The duty is huge. So why is **nobody\u2019s name** on it?'},{kind:'story',text:'In the 1870s the Bank of England\u2019s directors were merchants, chosen from trade, not banking. They rose to Governor in turn and served about two years. The country\u2019s gravest money decisions sat with well-meaning amateurs.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Bagehot didn\u2019t call them foolish. His point was that **they\u2019d be gone** before the consequences came. And governance that suited a small bank **doesn\u2019t update itself** when the whole system leans on it.'},{kind:'explain',text:'His reform was **a permanent deputy** governor: an expert with continuity beneath the rotating figurehead. It wasn\u2019t a revolution. It gave **one lasting owner** to the duty the Bank still wouldn\u2019t admit.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture your local hospital, where the backup generator is tested by whichever manager is on shift. Nobody has owned it for twenty years. Each year looks like the last, until the night the power fails.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Naming an owner is necessary, **not sufficient**. A permanent expert can become a single point of failure. Rotation brings fresh eyes. And a name achieves nothing without the authority to act.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Where something critical has **no named owner**, the failure is already scheduled. Look at what **you can\u2019t afford** to get wrong. Put a name on it before the pressure arrives.'}]},'lombard-street_9':{title:'The Crowd at the Door',card:{insight:'A crowd of smart people can still jam, so someone must be in charge.',tryThis:'Pick one thing that could turn into a rush. Write what will be done and who decides.',skills:['leadership','thinking']},review:{prompt:'Everyone in your building is careful. No one owns the fire plan. What is likely?',right:'In a fire, careful people still jam the doors like a crowd',wrong:'Careful people will each get out fine on their own',why:'Some dangers come from the crowd, not from any one person in it.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'The fire bell rings. Thirty kids rush for **one door**. Each one is doing a sensible thing: getting out fast. But what happens when they **all arrive** at once?'},{kind:'explain',text:'They jam the door. That\u2019s why a teacher stands there and says **who goes first**. In 1873, Walter Bagehot said a money market in a panic acts like that crowd. It needs **someone in charge**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A crowd of **sensible people** can still get stuck. In a panic, you need someone in charge. And you need to know **ahead of time** what they\u2019ll do.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'After a concert, you and twenty thousand others head for **the same station**. Everyone is being sensible: catch a train before the crush. Nobody is directing anyone. What happens at **the gates**?'},{kind:'story',text:'The gates jam, and the platform overflows. Nobody did anything foolish. Bagehot made the same point about money. In 1873, many believed a market left alone would sort out a panic. He disagreed.'},{kind:'explain',text:'He wasn\u2019t against markets: he edited The Economist. His point was narrower. **In a crisis**, the market is a crowd. Each bank guarding its own cash is prudent. All of them doing it at once is **a panic**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Some traits belong to **the set-up**, not to anyone inside it. So a system of careful people can still need managing **on purpose**. That means a named party who\u2019s said in advance what they\u2019ll do.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Being careful one by one **doesn\u2019t stop** a crowd from jamming together. Your plan needs **a named party**. Write down what they\u2019ll do before anyone needs it.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A trading floor has emptied. Paper is everywhere. **One desk** is still lit, its ledger open. Whose desk is it, and **why are they** still there when everyone else has gone?'},{kind:'explain',text:'It belongs to the party whose **job is to stay**. Bagehot argued against the common view that the money market, left alone, would reach **a sound outcome** in a crisis. Its own history said otherwise.'},{kind:'story',text:'Put the book\u2019s pieces together. One reserve, held by an institution denying it held one, run by rotating amateurs. Such a system won\u2019t correct itself. England had panics in 1825, 1847, 1857 and 1866.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Today we\u2019d call a panic an **emergent property**: a feature of the whole that no single part controls. No bank chooses it. Every bank **contributes to it**. Rational actors can still need deliberate management.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a school trip coming home late. Forty parents arrive at the same minute. Each is sensible, wanting their child quickly. With nobody directing, the car park jams solid until a teacher steps out with a plan.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Not every market failure needs a manager, and a named party can be **wrong or slow**. A market that expects management may take risks it otherwise wouldn\u2019t. A plan works only if someone carries it out.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Some properties belong to **the arrangement**, not to anyone inside it. In a panic, you need **a named party** committed in advance. Your written plan is most of the management.'}]}}}},970,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'meditations',kind:'book',promise:'Stay calm when your day goes wrong',about:'What if one bad moment did not have to ruin your whole day? Marcus Aurelius ruled Rome long ago. He wrote notes to himself about how to stay calm. Here are his best tricks. Want to try them?',ideas:{meditations_1:{title:'The Story You Add',simple:{headline:'When someone upsets you, check if you are guessing',body:'Every upset has two parts. There\u2019s what happened, and there\u2019s the story you tell yourself about it. Your manager answers your long email with two words. That\u2019s the fact. The story, written in half a second, is that she thinks your work is poor. Marcus Aurelius kept reminding himself that **the hurt comes from your judgement, and you can change a judgement**. You can\u2019t undo the reply. You can treat the story as a guess and look for another explanation. She might be busy, or typing on her phone.',doToday:'Write down one thing that annoyed you today as a plain fact, then write the story you added beneath it.'},card:{insight:'Most of the time, your guess hurts you more than the facts do.',tryThis:'Next time you get upset, write down what happened. Then write the guess you added to it.',skills:['philosophy','psychology']},review:{prompt:'A coworker walks by and does not say hi. What do you do first?',right:'Write down what happened, then treat your guess as just a guess',wrong:'Decide she is mad at you and plan what to say back',why:'The only fact is that she walked by, and the rest is a guess.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A car cuts in front of you. Nobody is hurt. Nothing is even scratched. But forty minutes later, you\u2019re **still angry**. What\u2019s keeping that **feeling alive**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The car drove off long ago. What stayed is **the story** you told about it: *that driver did it on purpose.* You wrote that part yourself, in about **half a second**, and it\u2019s still running.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Every upset has two parts: **what happened** and **the story** you add. You can\u2019t change the first part. You can always check the second.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You spend an hour on an email. The reply is **two lines** long. Before you finish reading, you\u2019ve decided what it means: *they think my work is beneath them.* The afternoon is gone. Sound **familiar**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Marcus Aurelius ran the Roman Empire and still caught himself doing this. In his private notebook he kept writing the same reminder. The thing itself isn\u2019t the problem. Your judgement about it is.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Split any upset into **the fact** and **the verdict** you stacked on top.',left:{title:'The fact',items:['A two-line reply','A car pulls out','The meeting moved']},right:{title:'The verdict',items:['They think I\u2019m useless','He did it on purpose','I don\u2019t matter here']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Nothing in a two-line reply contains **contempt**. You added that part, fast. So say the plain fact first, then treat **your story** as a guess you can check.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Marcus Aurelius commanded Rome\u2019s armies on a cold frontier, writing notes nobody was meant to read. One instruction keeps coming back, **again and again**. Why would an emperor need to tell himself **the same thing**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The instruction is simple. If something outside you seems to hurt, the hurt sits in **your judgement** about it. And a judgement, unlike the event, is **yours to revise**. Agreeing is easy. Doing it mid-anger isn\u2019t.'},{kind:'story',text:'Your friend answers a long message with one word: *ok.* The fact is that one word arrived. The verdict is that she\u2019s angry with you. She might be, or she might just be busy. Nothing in two letters tells you which.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a driver cuts you off. The fact: a car changed lanes close to yours. The verdict: he did it on purpose, to you. The first takes two seconds. The second can follow you home and spoil dinner.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some verdicts are true. If your friend really was angry, checking the story won\u2019t change that. The practice doesn\u2019t deny real harm. It **separates the harm** from the extra pain you add.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You can\u2019t edit **what happened**. You can always question **the verdict** you stacked on it. That gap is the only place where you have any leverage.'}]},meditations_2:{title:'Reading the Terrain',simple:{headline:'When you get stuck, do the part you still can',body:'Your project is stuck for two weeks, waiting on one person\u2019s approval. You could chase them every day and keep refreshing your inbox. Marcus Aurelius ran an empire through war and plague, so he rarely had a clear road. He wrote that **the thing in your way can show you what to work on next**. Build the parts that never needed the approval. Waiting and pushing both leave you stuck. Working around the block keeps you moving, and you often find the weak spots in the plan.',doToday:'Spend 20 minutes on the part of a stuck task that doesn\u2019t depend on whatever is holding it up.'},card:{insight:'A problem can change your plan, but you can still do a lot.',tryThis:'When you get stuck today, write one part of the plan you can still do. Then start it.',skills:['thinking','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'You want to bake a cake, but you have no eggs until Monday. What now?',right:'Do the parts of the plan you still can, like the frosting',wrong:'Put the whole cake off until you buy eggs',why:'The missing eggs change the plan, but you can still do a lot.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re walking to a friend\u2019s house when a **fallen tree** blocks the path. Pushing it won\u2019t work, and waiting could take all day. So **what\u2019s the smart move**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Pushing wears you out, and waiting wastes the day. **Looking** tells you things: how big the tree is, and which way round is easiest. The block hasn\u2019t ended your walk. It has **changed the route** you take.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A thing that blocks you is also **telling you something**. Don\u2019t just push on it or wait. **Read it**, then keep going a new way.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'The one person who can **approve the plan** is away for two weeks. Everything now waits behind them. You spend three days chasing their deputy and refreshing your inbox. Is that really **the only move**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Marcus Aurelius wrote about this while running a war and steering an empire through a plague. He never had the option of waiting for a clear road. His notebooks keep coming back to it, because the temptation to wait kept coming back.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Read the same fortnight as an interruption or as **terrain**, and see what **keeps moving**.',left:{title:'As an interruption',items:['The work waits','You push on the block']},right:{title:'As terrain',items:['The work changes shape','You ask what it allows']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s **the test**. Ask what you\u2019re doing while blocked. If the only answer is chasing the blocker, the obstacle has **taken the work hostage**. If you\u2019re working on the part that never needed approval, you\u2019re reading it right.'},{kind:'moral',text:'An obstacle changes **the shape** of the work. It doesn\u2019t have to stop it. So ask what the block **makes possible**, and start there today.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'In his fifth notebook, Marcus makes a short argument. Other people can **block what he does**. They can\u2019t block **his intention**. So what happens to the work when the road closes?'},{kind:'explain',text:'His answer is that **the mind adapts**. It turns the obstacle to its own purpose, so what blocks the action **becomes the action**. The aim stays the same. Only the form of your next step changes.'},{kind:'story',text:'Say the one person who signs off is away for two weeks. Stoics added a clause to every plan: *unless something prevents it.* You spend the fortnight on the part that needed no approval, and find it was the weak part.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a supplier is late and your launch slips a month. You could email them every morning until they deliver. Or you could use the month to fix the onboarding you\u2019d planned to ship rough. Only one of those continues the work.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some obstacles are just obstacles, and the right move is to **remove them**. Read carelessly, the idea dresses a bad plan as fate. Marcus was a commander. He removed obstacles too.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Only **the route** was blocked. Your aim of acting well never is. So when the road closes, ask what **the block allows**, and do that now.'}]},meditations_3:{title:'The Lists of the Dead',simple:{headline:'Speak up, since most people soon forget',body:'Tomorrow you have to tell a senior colleague that their plan has a flaw. Your stomach turns each time you picture the room. Marcus Aurelius ruled Rome, yet he kept lists of famous people who had died. Everyone who had admired or feared them was gone too. He used the lists to remind himself that fame and other people\u2019s opinions both fade. **The people you fear are few, and most will forget by next month.** Then all that matters is whether your point is true and useful.',doToday:'Send one honest message you\u2019ve been putting off because you\u2019re worried about how it will look.'},card:{insight:'The crowd you fear is small, and it will soon forget.',tryThis:'Next time you are scared to speak up, count the people who will hear you. Then say it.',skills:['psychology','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'You need to tell your boss that her plan has a mistake. What helps most?',right:'Count the few people who will hear it, then say it',wrong:'Wait until you stop feeling scared, then say it',why:'Few people will hear it, and they will soon forget.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your friends dare you to do something you **don\u2019t want to do**. Your heart is thumping. All you can think is: *what will they think of me?* How could a dead emperor **help you here**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Marcus Aurelius ruled Rome, and he had **a trick** for moments like this. He reminded himself that nothing lasts. Not him, not the people he feared, not their opinion of him. From a plane, your school is **a tiny dot**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Most fear is about what **a few people** will think, and they\u2019ll soon forget. So don\u2019t let the fear decide. **Do the right thing** now.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Tomorrow you have to give a senior colleague **honest feedback** they won\u2019t like. You\u2019ve rehearsed the words all evening. They\u2019re fine. So why does your stomach still **turn over** every time you picture the meeting?'},{kind:'story',text:'The most powerful man in the Roman world sat down and wrote himself a list of the dead. Emperors, generals, philosophers. All famous, all admired or feared. All gone, along with everyone who admired or feared them.'},{kind:'explain',text:'His point wasn\u2019t gloom. It was **scale**. A hard conversation is rarely hard because of the words. It\u2019s hard because of **the audience**. Shrink that audience to **its real size**, and the thing you\u2019re avoiding gets smaller than the avoiding.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Picture the same meeting from **two distances**, and watch **the fear** change size.',left:{title:'Zoomed in',items:['This meeting, these five people','Every face, every reaction']},right:{title:'Zoomed out',items:['In a month, few remember','Most weren\u2019t paying attention']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Everyone whose opinion you\u2019re worried about is **temporary**, and so are you. That isn\u2019t a reason to stop caring. It\u2019s a reason to **say the true thing** while you can.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'The notebooks are **full of names**. Alexander, Pompey, Caesar, whole courts with their physicians and astrologers. Each time, Marcus writes the same note beside them. Gone. Why would an emperor keep **a list like that**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The mechanism is **altitude**. Most of what he fears is reputation: how an act will be seen and remembered. So he attacks the remembering. Those who remember will die, and those after them. **Lasting fame** is a chain of vanishing people.'},{kind:'story',text:'Your team keeps repeating the same mistake, and nobody will say so. You picture the raised eyebrows and the gossip afterwards. Then you ask who will still be thinking about it in a month. Almost nobody. So you raise it at the meeting.'},{kind:'story',text:'A critic tears into your work in a public thread. It stings all afternoon. Then you count: by Friday, they\u2019ll have moved on. So you answer the one fair point, plainly, and post the next piece anyway.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'The thought **turns easily** into an excuse. *Nobody will remember* can license carelessness as readily as courage. For someone already low, it can deepen the sense that **nothing counts**. Marcus always pairs it with a duty to act well now.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Hold your death, and **your audience\u2019s**, in view. Not to shrink what you do, but to **set its scale**. Fame and embarrassment are brief, so only the quality of the act is left.'}]},meditations_4:{title:'The Next Twenty Minutes',simple:{headline:'Pick one chore and give it twenty minutes',body:'You have a free afternoon and forty jobs on your list. You spend the first hour rereading and reordering the list, and you finish nothing on it. Marcus Aurelius noticed that the picture of the whole load is what crushes you. **Taken one at a time, each task is bearable.** So look at the list once, on purpose, and choose one job. Decide what you\u2019ll get done in the next twenty minutes, and do only that. If your mind runs ahead to the rest, bring it back to the job in front of you.',doToday:'Pick one task, set a 20-minute timer, and work on nothing else until it rings.'},card:{insight:'A long list looks like a wall, but one chore is easy to start.',tryThis:'Pick one chore and set a timer for twenty minutes. Keep your mind on that chore until it rings.',skills:['thinking','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'You are folding laundry, but you keep planning your next three chores. What now?',right:'Bring your mind back to the one chore you are doing',wrong:'Stop and plan the whole list again before you go on',why:'You can plan later, but for now keep your mind on this chore.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'It\u2019s Sunday night, and you\u2019ve got **a messy room**, a test on Monday and a project due. You look at all of it at once. Your stomach drops, and you do **none of it**. Why?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The pile isn\u2019t happening now. Only **one small job** is happening now. Most of the scary feeling comes from **picturing every job** at once. It\u2019s like stairs. You can\u2019t jump to the top, but you can always take one step.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t look at **the whole pile**. Pick the next thing and do it well. Then **stop and pick** the next one.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve got four free hours and **two hundred tasks**. So you spend the first hour rereading the list and moving things around. It feels like work. When the hour\u2019s gone, **nothing on it** has changed. Familiar?'},{kind:'story',text:'The man who wrote this was running a war, a plague and a bureaucracy. If anyone was entitled to feel overwhelmed, he was. Yet he had to write the instruction down again and again. It was a discipline, not a temperament.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Marcus\u2019s instruction has **two halves**. The obvious one is to do the task in front of you, and do it well. The harder one is **to stop**. Keep your mind from running into the remainder while your hands are on this task.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Put **the next twenty minutes** beside **the remainder**, and see where the dread lives.',left:{title:'Next twenty minutes',items:['Real','Nearly always manageable','Where your hands are']},right:{title:'The remainder',items:['Imagined','Never manageable in one go','Where the distress lives']}},{kind:'moral',text:'The stack behind the task **isn\u2019t real yet**. The next twenty minutes are. So **define those only**, do them, and then define the next twenty.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'An emperor keeps writing himself **the same instruction**. It\u2019s about one small thing: where his mind goes while he works. If it came naturally, **why repeat it**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'In the notebooks, Marcus tells himself not to be thrown by **picturing his life** as a whole. He\u2019s to ask of this moment only: what in *this* is unbearable? He\u2019d be **ashamed to answer**. He repeated it because it never came easily.'},{kind:'story',text:'You sit down to write a report. Ten minutes in, you\u2019re mentally drafting the three tasks after it, then the week. The report slows, and the dread grows. Nothing about the report changed. Only your attention moved.'},{kind:'story',text:'You\u2019re cooking dinner, but your head is answering tomorrow\u2019s emails. The onions catch and start to burn. Dinner was twenty minutes of easy work. The emails weren\u2019t even here yet, and they still spoiled the meal.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **the honest limit**. Somebody has to look at the whole list sometimes, or the next thing is chosen badly. Used all day, this turns into **drift**: plenty done, none of it right. Plan with the stack in view, briefly and on purpose.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Each task, taken alone, is **bearable**. The picture of the whole load is not. So look at the list on purpose, then work with the stack **out of view**.'}]},meditations_5:{title:'Nobody\u2019s Villain',simple:{headline:'Guess their reason before you get mad',body:'Each morning, Marcus Aurelius reminded himself that he\u2019d meet rude and ungrateful people that day. He wasn\u2019t dreading them. He wanted to remember that people act on their own idea of what\u2019s right. A colleague talks over you twice in a meeting. In their head, they were moving things along. **Working out their reason costs you a minute and cools most of the anger.** It doesn\u2019t excuse them. You can still ask them to let you finish next time, and you\u2019ll ask without an edge in your voice.',doToday:'Write one sentence, in their voice, saying why the last person who annoyed you did what they did.'},card:{insight:'Most people who act badly think they had a good reason.',tryThis:'Tonight, write why the last person who bugged you thinks they did the right thing.',skills:['psychology','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'A driver honks at you the second the light turns green. What do you do first?',right:'Guess his good reason, then calmly pick what to do',wrong:'Decide the driver is rude and honk right back',why:'Guessing his reason cools you down, and you still choose what to do.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A boy pushes past you to get off the bus, and he **doesn\u2019t say sorry**. You decide he\u2019s just mean. But can you **really tell** why he did it?'},{kind:'story',text:'Maybe he was about to miss his stop. Maybe he felt sick. In his own head, he wasn\u2019t being mean at all. He was just trying to get off. In his story, he\u2019s not the bad guy.'},{kind:'moral',text:'People who act badly nearly always think they have **a good reason**. Guessing it doesn\u2019t mean it was okay. It just **shrinks the anger**, so you can sort things out calmly.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A colleague **cut across you** in the meeting, twice. You\u2019ve spent the whole evening drafting replies in your head. Meanwhile, they went home thinking they\u2019d **helped things along**. Which of you is having the worse night?'},{kind:'story',text:'Marcus began each day by telling himself whom he\u2019d meet. The meddling, the ungrateful, the arrogant, the dishonest. He didn\u2019t do it to dread them. He did it to be unsurprised, and to remember why they act that way.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The idea comes from Socrates: **nobody chooses** what they believe is bad. The driver who cut in may be late and frightened. The colleague who takes credit may think they **earned it**. In their own account, they\u2019re responding.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Marcus keeps **understanding** and **approving** apart. That\u2019s the whole trick.',left:{title:'Understanding',items:['Cheap','Ends the anger','Costs you a minute']},right:{title:'Approving',items:['A separate decision','Still yours to make','May well be no']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Seeing someone\u2019s reason **doesn\u2019t excuse** them. It stops them **occupying your attention**. Then you can decide, calmly, what to do about it.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'First thing in the morning, Marcus runs **a rehearsal**. Today he\u2019ll meet the meddling, the ungrateful and the arrogant. He isn\u2019t dreading them. He reaches a stranger conclusion. **What could it be**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'They act this way, he says, from **not knowing** good from bad. It\u2019s a claim traced to Socrates: people pursue what seems good to them. So wrongdoing is **a mistake** about what\u2019s good, not a choice of what\u2019s bad.'},{kind:'explain',text:'His conclusion follows. Another person\u2019s mistake **can\u2019t harm** what matters in you. And people are made to work together, like **hands and feet**, or eyelids. Anger at your own kind makes little sense.'},{kind:'story',text:'A colleague takes credit for your work. To you, it\u2019s theft. To them, in that moment, it felt earned. Modern psychology broadly agrees here. People describe their own acts as responses to something, rarely as wrongs they set out to do.'},{kind:'story',text:'Or a driver cuts you up on the way home. You picture a bully. He may be late and frightened of something. You can still think he drove badly. You just don\u2019t have to carry him home with you.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Now the limit. Some people do act **in bad faith**, and know it. And *they know no better* can slide into contempt, or into tolerating what should stop. Treat it as a default that **buys you calm** while you find out.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Understanding why someone acted is cheap, and it **ends the anger**. Approving is a separate decision. Even with a liar, you still choose **the consequences**, calmly.'}]},meditations_6:{title:'The Archer\u2019s Job',simple:{headline:'Decide what good work looks like before anyone sees it',body:'You spend a week on a report, send it, and hear nothing back. By Thursday you\u2019ve decided it was bad. Marcus Aurelius wrote that praise doesn\u2019t make good work better, and going unpraised doesn\u2019t make it worse. Your readers might be busy, or they skimmed it. **You control the quality of your work, not whether anyone notices it.** The Stoics compared this to an archer, who can aim well but can\u2019t steer the wind. So write down what good looks like before you send, and judge the work against that. Feedback can still sharpen your aim.',doToday:'Write one line saying what would make your next piece of work good, before you send it.'},card:{insight:'You can control how well you do the work, but not if people clap.',tryThis:'Before you share your next work, write one line on what good looks like.',skills:['philosophy','psychology']},review:{prompt:'People clap for your talk, but you skipped the hard part. How did it go?',right:'Not that good, even though the people loved it',wrong:'It went great, since the whole crowd loved it',why:'Your own plan for good work counts more than the clapping.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You spent **all week** on a drawing. You showed it to everyone, and nobody said a thing. Now you\u2019re sure the drawing must be bad. But is it? **What does silence** actually tell you?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Silence tells you about **them**. Maybe they were busy, or didn\u2019t really look. It says nothing about the drawing. You\u2019re in charge of **how hard you try** and how careful you are. Whether people notice isn\u2019t up to you.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Judge your work by **the part that\u2019s yours**. Listen when a teacher or coach shows you something. Just don\u2019t let **claps or silence** decide if it was good.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You put **a week** into something, sent it off, and heard nothing back. By Thursday, you\u2019ve quietly decided it **wasn\u2019t very good**. Who made that decision, though?'},{kind:'explain',text:'If the response decides whether your work was good, **the verdict** belongs to whoever happened to read it. They read in whatever mood, with whatever attention. Usually that\u2019s **very little attention**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Split the work into **what\u2019s yours** and what belongs to **the room**.',left:{title:'Yours',items:['The care you took','Whether it was honest','Whether it was clear']},right:{title:'Not yours',items:['Whether they clap','Whether they notice','What mood they\u2019re in']}},{kind:'explain',text:'This isn\u2019t about ignoring feedback. Feedback shows you **things you can\u2019t see**. It\u2019s about where your standard comes from. Decide it before you send, and **write it down**. Then it survives silence, and applause too.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Reception is **noisy** in a way effort isn\u2019t. So set **your standard** first, and judge the work against that, not against the response.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'An emerald is **no worse** for going unpraised. A vine bears its grapes and **asks for nothing** more. Marcus wanted his own good acts to work like that. Can yours?'},{kind:'explain',text:'It comes from a Stoic split: what\u2019s **up to us**, and what isn\u2019t. Judgements, intentions and effort are ours. Reputation, reward and results are not. So praise adds nothing to **a good act**, and blame takes nothing away.'},{kind:'story',text:'A Stoic image, reported by Cicero, makes it sharp. The archer\u2019s task is to aim and release as well as possible. Once the arrow leaves the bow, wind and a moving target decide the rest. Hitting is preferred. Shooting well is the job.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now your report. You send it, and the reply is a thumbs-up from someone who clearly skimmed it. Had you written down *clear, honest, useful* beforehand, you\u2019d know how it went. Decided afterwards, your standard quietly becomes the thumbs-up.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Push this too far and it becomes **a refusal to learn**. Results are evidence: an archer who never hits should question the aim. And some work exists to be received. A teacher whose class learns nothing **can\u2019t hide** behind effort.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Shooting well is **your job**. Where the arrow lands is evidence, not **the verdict**. Set the standard before you send, and let results inform it, not define it.'}]},meditations_7:{title:'The Dawn Argument',simple:{headline:'Plan it tonight, then do it in the morning',body:'Marcus Aurelius ruled Rome, and some mornings he still didn\u2019t want to get out of bed. He didn\u2019t wait to feel like it. He reminded himself that he was made to do his work, like ants and bees doing theirs. You planned an early run. At six the alarm goes, it\u2019s dark, and the warm version of you starts making excuses. The version of you who planned it last night had the clearer view. **Decide the night before, and don\u2019t reopen it at dawn.** If you wake up ill, change the plan. If you only feel reluctant, go.',doToday:'Put a note by your bed with tomorrow\u2019s first task and the time you\u2019ll start it.'},card:{insight:'A bad mood in the morning should not change last night\u2019s plan.',tryThis:'Tonight, write what you will do first tomorrow, and when. Put the note by your bed.',skills:['psychology','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'You planned to send a hard email at nine. Now you want to wait. What now?',right:'Send it at nine, since last night\u2019s plan gets the vote',wrong:'Wait till you feel like it, so the email sounds good',why:'You made the plan with a clear head, and a mood cannot change it.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Marcus Aurelius ruled Rome and was **the most powerful man** in the world. Some mornings, he still didn\u2019t want to get out of bed. He wrote about it in his notebook. What do you think **he told himself**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'He never said he wanted to get up. He pointed out that ants and bees **get on with** their work, and he was made for his. How he felt **wasn\u2019t the point**. Getting up was his job.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You don\u2019t have to feel like doing something to do it. **Decide when you\u2019re calm**, the night before. Then the grumpy morning feeling **doesn\u2019t get a vote**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'It\u2019s six in the morning, dark and cold. You planned **a run** before work, and the alarm is going. The warm version of you is already **making its case**. Who should get to decide?'},{kind:'story',text:'Book Five of the notebooks opens with Marcus not wanting to leave his bed. He never claims he wants to. He asks whether he was made to lie warm under blankets, and points to ants and bees at work.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Put **the feeling** first, or put **the decision** first.',left:{title:'Feeling first',items:['Wait until you want to','The six o\u2019clock self decides','Lose most mornings']},right:{title:'Decision first',items:['Decide in advance','The rested self decides','The dread gets no vote']}},{kind:'explain',text:'This isn\u2019t **willpower**. Willpower is a fight at six in the morning, held on the bed\u2019s ground. Here the question is **who decides**. The cold version of you was never the one qualified to make the call.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Act on **the decision**, not the mood. Make the commitment while you can see clearly. Then notice the reluctance, and **do it anyway**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A court can rule on some cases and not others. That\u2019s **jurisdiction**: whose decision this is, before what it should be. Now your alarm goes at dawn. **Whose call** is it whether you get up?'},{kind:'story',text:'On Tuesday evening, you decide to start the tax forms at eight the next morning. At eight, everything in you would rather do anything else. You notice the feeling, sit down and open the first form. It came along, but it wasn\u2019t asked.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Most self-management tries to **change the feeling** first and act second. That hands the decision to whoever you are at six in the morning. Marcus, in Book Five, settles it by **what he\u2019s for**, like ants and bees at their work.'},{kind:'story',text:'On Sunday you agreed to send a difficult email first thing Monday. Monday comes, and you dread it. You could wait until you feel ready. But Sunday\u2019s you had the clear view. The dread can come along. It doesn\u2019t get to reopen the plan.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Where it stops: **the morning self** sometimes knows something. Illness, exhaustion and low mood aren\u2019t laziness, and Marcus admits rest is needed. A mood can\u2019t reopen a decision, but **a new fact** can. For depression, this is no substitute for help.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Settle the decision where you **can see clearly**. Then the reluctance at dawn has **nothing to rule on**. A fever can change the plan, but wanting to stay warm can\u2019t.'}]},meditations_8:{title:'The Unsent Reply',simple:{headline:'Stand up for yourself without being mean',body:'A colleague takes credit for your work in front of the team. You know something embarrassing about them, and it would land. Marcus Aurelius wrote that the best way to get back at someone is to refuse to act like them. Hit back, and the room sees two people doing the same thing. **You can answer a wrong without copying it.** Correct the facts once, in plain words. Raise it with your manager if you need to. Leave their character out of it, so you stay the person who didn\u2019t stoop to it.',doToday:'Draft your reply to someone who wronged you, then delete every line that attacks their character.'},card:{insight:'The best way to get back at someone is to not be like them.',tryThis:'Next time you are mad at someone, write your reply with just the facts. Cut every mean line.',skills:['philosophy','psychology']},review:{prompt:'A coworker tells the team a false story about you. What do you do?',right:'Calmly tell the facts and leave out anything mean',wrong:'Tell the team a true story that makes him look bad',why:'You can stand up for yourself without being mean like him.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Someone told the whole class **a silly thing** you said. You know an embarrassing thing about them too. You could post it in the group chat right now. It would **really work**. Should you?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Think of **throwing mud**. It might even hit them. But now your hands are covered in mud too, and everyone saw you throw it. Hitting back **turns you into** the thing you were upset about.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The best way to get back at someone is **to be unlike them**. You can still tell a teacher. That\u2019s **getting help**, not revenge.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A colleague takes **credit for your work** in front of the team. You know something unflattering about them. It\u2019s true, and it would land. You can already **hear yourself** saying it. What happens next?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Retaliation tempts because **it works**. But the moment you hit back, you\u2019re both playing **the same game**. You can be beaten at it now. Everyone watching reads every later exchange through that frame.'},{kind:'story',text:'Marcus kept noticing two things. The wrong someone does you damages them. Your anger about it damages you. Hit back, and two people are doing the same thing. Only one started it, and from outside it soon gets hard to say which.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'You can **answer a wrong** without **copying it**.',left:{title:'Answering',items:['Correct the facts plainly, once','Report it through the proper channel']},right:{title:'Copying',items:['Post their embarrassing secret','Reply in exactly their tone']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Hitting back spends **the one thing** you still hold: being plainly not the person who does this. So **answer the facts**, and leave the copying to them.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'In 175, Avidius Cassius, Rome\u2019s leading general in the east, **declared himself emperor**. It seems a false report said Marcus had died. Within about three months, his own officers killed him. **What would Marcus do** with the rebels left behind?'},{kind:'story',text:'Ancient historians record that Marcus dealt mildly with most of those who\u2019d joined the revolt. His line on revenge can\u2019t be dated against these events. But it was tested by them.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The line itself is short, in Book Six. Around it, the notebooks keep noting that **the wrongdoer harms himself** first. Your anger often costs you more than the wrong did. Retaliation adds a third harm: **a second wrongdoer**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Closer to home, a neighbour tells the street a false story about your family. You know something true about them that would land. Say it, and the street stops seeing who started it. It just sees a feud, and you can lose a feud.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Read badly, this becomes **passivity**: never naming a wrong, letting someone carry on. Being unlike them still allows **a firm reply**, a complaint, a consequence. And holding back just to look better is still playing to the audience.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Marcus isn\u2019t asking you to forgive. His point is **colder**: the one asset you still hold is that you don\u2019t do this. Retaliation **spends it** for a moment\u2019s satisfaction.'}]},meditations_9:{title:'Three Lines of Fact',simple:{headline:'Write down only the facts when bad news hits',body:'The flat you hoped to rent falls through because the landlord sells. Written as plain facts, it\u2019s short. The landlord sold, so you keep looking. Yet it feels huge. Marcus Aurelius liked to describe things in their plainest terms. A fancy dinner, to him, was a dead fish on a plate. Try that with the flat. **Most of the weight is the life you\u2019d already pictured living there.** That\u2019s worth grieving. Then picture yourself in the next place. The facts are smaller than they felt. Use this on your own setbacks, never on someone else\u2019s grief.',doToday:'Write a recent setback as three lines of plain fact, then list underneath what you\u2019d expected to happen next.'},card:{insight:'Most of the hurt from bad news is about the plans you lost.',tryThis:'Write one bad thing from this week in three lines, with just the facts.',skills:['psychology','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'A trip you planned for months gets canceled. What should you do first?',right:'Write just the facts, then make a new plan',wrong:'Tell yourself it was only a trip and forget it',why:'The facts are small, and you can always make a new plan.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your class trip to the beach is **called off**. Written as facts, it\u2019s three short lines: trip off, storm coming, maybe next month. So why does it feel **so much bigger** than that?'},{kind:'story',text:'You\u2019d already seen the day in your head. The bus ride with friends. Ice cream on the sand. A great story for Monday. That picture is what you lost. It was a plan, not a day you\u2019d had.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When a plan falls through, **the facts** are often small. Most of the sadness is for **the day you pictured**. And a picture can be drawn again.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your team is moved under another manager. On paper, **nothing\u2019s taken**: same pay, same title, same desk. Written as facts, it\u2019s three lines. Yet the week feels like **a loss** you can\u2019t quite name.'},{kind:'explain',text:'This is a diagnostic, **not a comfort**. It shows you where the loss sits. The facts are three lines. The feeling is about the next two years, in which the team was **still yours**. You\u2019d been living there already.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Put **the event** beside **the future you pictured**, and see which one can change.',left:{title:'The event',items:['Team moves under someone else','Pay and title unchanged','Can\u2019t be revised']},right:{title:'The expectation',items:['The team still yours in two years','Built your way','Can be revised']}},{kind:'moral',text:'The loss is real. It\u2019s just **somewhere else**. You\u2019re mourning an expectation, not an event, and an expectation can be mourned and then **rewritten**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Marcus describes a fine dish as **a dead fish**. A purple robe, to him, is sheep\u2019s wool dyed in shellfish blood. Why would anyone strip things down **that far**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'He uses the same move on upsets. Say no more than **first appearances** report. Someone spoke ill of you: that was reported. That you were harmed **was not**. What survives the stripping is usually tied to an expectation.'},{kind:'story',text:'A project you led is handed to another team. Written as facts, it\u2019s three lines. The weight comes from a future you\u2019d already furnished and moved into. Nobody took that future from you, because nobody had it yet.'},{kind:'story',text:'Or a holiday is cancelled after months of planning. The fact is one line: no trip. But you\u2019d been living in that week for months. That\u2019s what you\u2019re grieving, and it can be planned again.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'This **stops working** on some losses. A death, an injury or a betrayal isn\u2019t three lines with a story added. Stripping it down can feel cruel. Use this on **your own setbacks**, never on someone else\u2019s grief.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Events can only be **accepted**. Expectations can be mourned and **revised**. Mistaking one for the other is what keeps you stuck.'}]},meditations_10:{title:'The Sideways Glance',simple:{headline:'Keep your eyes on your own work',body:'You check a competitor\u2019s website, or a friend\u2019s follower count, four times before lunch. Each look takes a minute, so it feels free. Marcus Aurelius noted how much time you save by not watching what your neighbour says or does. **The bigger cost is that their pace starts to set yours.** You copy their topics and chase their numbers, though they\u2019re running a different race. Keep the checks that answer a real question, like a planned look at the market each month. Drop the ones you make out of a twinge of envy.',doToday:'Move the app you use most to check on other people off your home screen.'},card:{insight:'Each time you check on someone else, you start to copy their plan.',tryThis:'Today, take the app you use to check on others off your home screen.',skills:['thinking','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'After each run, your friend checks how fast a rival ran. What hurts her most?',right:'She starts to copy a plan made for someone else',wrong:'She loses a few minutes each time she checks',why:'The lost minutes add up, but running her rival\u2019s race is worse.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'It\u2019s sports day, and you\u2019re running **the big race**. You keep glancing at the girl in the next lane. Is she ahead, or faster? Each look is quick. So why does your run **feel worse**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Each look pulls you off **your own run**. It\u2019s like a dripping tap. One drip is nothing, but leave it all day and the bucket fills. Checking on other people works the same way. **The checks add up**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Every time you check on someone else, it costs you **a little time**. Those bits add up fast. Spend them on **your own work** instead.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'It\u2019s eleven in the morning. You check what a competitor shipped, for **the fourth time** today. Each check takes a minute or two. It hardly seems worth counting. **What\u2019s the real cost**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Watching others isn\u2019t one act. It\u2019s **a standing subscription**. Nobody experiences an hour of checking. They experience forty separate minutes that each felt like nothing. The checking never ends, so **the total** never shows up anywhere.'},{kind:'story',text:'In Book Four, Marcus asks how much time you gain by ignoring what a neighbour said or did. Instead, you\u2019d care only that your own acts are just. Then he adds an image: a runner keeping straight to his own line.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Each check has **a visible cost** and a **bigger hidden one**.',left:{title:'Visible',items:['A minute here','A minute there']},right:{title:'Hidden',items:['Attention slow to come back','A plan bent to their race','A target someone else set']}},{kind:'moral',text:'The lost minutes are **the smaller half**. The bigger loss is the pace you borrow from **someone else\u2019s race**. So keep your eyes on your own lane.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Book Four asks a question that **reads like this year**. How much time would you gain by not caring what **your neighbour** said or did? Most of us have never added it up.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Read to the end, the sentence isn\u2019t about productivity. The time saved is for making **your own acts** just. Watching neighbours moves attention off **the one thing** in your charge, your own conduct, and onto things that aren\u2019t.'},{kind:'story',text:'A friend started posting around the same time as you. You check her follower count most mornings. A twinge: ahead or behind? You start copying her topics, then check again to see if it worked. Each round is short, and the loop never ends.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a runner training for a marathon. After every session, she opens a rival\u2019s running app and adjusts her mileage to match. But the rival is training for a different race, over a different distance, for reasons she can\u2019t see.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Some watching is **necessary**. A business has to know its market, and a learner improves by watching people better than them. Marcus governed by knowing what others did. The target is curiosity that **serves no decision**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A scheduled look that answers **a real question** is fine. A subscription set off by a twinge isn\u2019t. Keep **your own line**, and spend the time on your own acts.'}]},meditations_11:{title:'Ground or Weather',simple:{headline:'Make a backup plan while things are going well',body:'Marcus Aurelius compared the world to a river. Hardly anything in it stays put. He wasn\u2019t being gloomy. He was describing what he\u2019d seen, leading Rome through war and plague. Take a small business that lives on one big monthly customer. That customer could leave with one email. **Name the one thing your plan assumes will stay the same.** Then decide, while you\u2019re calm, what you\u2019d do if it changed. The same goes for bad news. A rejected pitch on Friday is a change in the weather, not proof that you\u2019re bad at your job.',doToday:'Write down one thing your job or budget depends on, and the first step you\u2019d take if it stopped.'},card:{insight:'Most things change in time, so keep a backup plan ready.',tryThis:'Today, write down one thing you count on. Then write what you would do if it stopped.',skills:['thinking','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'Your small shop does well because one big buyer orders every month. What now?',right:'Write down what you would do if that buyer stopped',wrong:'Enjoy it and stop worrying, since it is going well',why:'Good times are like weather, so plan for the day they change.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'It\u2019s **sunny today**. Would you leave your coat at home for the whole year? And if it rains today, would you think it\u2019ll **rain forever**? Of course not. So why do we treat a bad week like it\u2019s forever?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Marcus Aurelius wrote that the world is **like a river**. It keeps flowing, and hardly anything in it stays put. Bad times don\u2019t last, and good times don\u2019t either. He didn\u2019t find that sad. He found it **useful**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Almost nothing stays the way it is now. So don\u2019t act as if **today is forever**, and give **every plan** a backup for when things change.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'The plan for next quarter **looks solid**. Read it again, though. It rests on one thing staying exactly as it is: one client, one manager, one price. **Nobody wrote that down**. Does anybody need to?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Marcus writes that everything flows **like a river**, with causes shifting endlessly and hardly anything standing still. He isn\u2019t being gloomy. He\u2019s describing the world he governed, **through war and plague**. It also describes any industry you\u2019ve worked in.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The same hidden assumption costs you **both ways**: in bad stretches and **good ones**.',left:{title:'Bad stretch',items:['This is just how things are','A hard quarter feels like a verdict']},right:{title:'Good stretch',items:['Weather treated as ground','Real shock when it moves']}},{kind:'story',text:'Take a small business that thrives because one big customer orders every month. It feels like ground. It\u2019s weather. The owner who decides now what happens if those orders stop won\u2019t be improvising in shock later.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Nothing is settled, so **name the condition** your plan quietly rests on. Then decide **what you\u2019ll do** when it moves, while you\u2019re still calm.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Marcus became emperor in 161. War with Parthia followed, then **a plague**, then war on the Danube. In 175 his leading general claimed the throne. What would **settled** even mean to him?'},{kind:'explain',text:'In Book Five, he describes substance as **a river** in continual flow. Actions keep changing, causes vary endlessly, and almost nothing stands still. The image echoes **Heraclitus**, the earlier Greek thinker of flux, whom Marcus names several times.'},{kind:'story',text:'Your pitch gets turned down on a Friday. By Sunday, you\u2019ve decided it means **you\u2019re not cut out** for this work. One rejection has become a verdict on your career. The hidden assumption is that today\u2019s arrangement is the permanent one.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a good stretch. Your job has been steady for years, and your whole budget quietly rests on it. Treated as ground, its loss would be a shock. Treated as weather, you\u2019d already know your first move.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'It has limits. Some things are **stable enough** to build on. Treating everything as weather becomes its own anxiety, or an excuse never to commit. And flux favours no direction: a bad stretch ending **doesn\u2019t promise** a good one.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The present arrangement **isn\u2019t the permanent one**, in bad stretches or good. Name one or two assumptions your plan rests on, and **decide now** what happens when they go.'}]},meditations_12:{title:'The Credit Test',simple:{headline:'Do the work that helps, even if no one sees',body:'You have time for one project. One has your name on it and is easy to show off. The other is a slow fix that customers need and nobody will notice. Marcus Aurelius pointed out that everyone who remembers you will die too, and so will the people after them. Chasing credit pulls you towards whatever is most visible. **Ask whether you\u2019d still choose this work if someone else got the credit.** Fair credit still matters for pay and promotion, so claim it. Don\u2019t let it pick the work.',doToday:'Do one useful job today that nobody will see or thank you for.'},card:{insight:'The work that helps most is often the work no one sees.',tryThis:'Before lunch, fix one small thing at work that no one will thank you for.',skills:['philosophy','psychology']},review:{prompt:'You can lead the big launch or fix a hidden bug. What do you ask?',right:'Would I still pick this work if no one sees it?',wrong:'Which job will people still talk about for years?',why:'If you would still pick it when no one sees, it is worth doing.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two jobs are left at the school fair. You could paint **the big sign** at the gate, with your name in the corner. Or you could fix **the wobbly stall** that keeps falling over. Which job needs you more?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Marcus Aurelius did **a simple sum** about being remembered. The people who remember you will die one day too. So will the people after them. In the end, **no one is left** to remember. It\u2019s like a name written in sand.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Do the job that **needs doing**, not the one that gets you noticed. Ask: would I still do it if **someone else** got the thanks?'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve got time for **one project**. One has your name on it, gets seen by everyone and is easy to show. The other is mostly invisible and slow, but it needed doing. Be honest. **Which one** are you leaning towards?'},{kind:'story',text:'In Book Four, Marcus sets out an argument that\u2019s almost a sum. Whoever wants fame after death forgets one thing. Those who remember him will soon be dead too. So will those after them, until the whole line of admirers is gone.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The motive **bends your choices**. It favours the visible over the useful, and the finishable over the important. It can **never be satisfied**, because the verdict arrives after you can\u2019t hear it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Marcus isn\u2019t against **a good name**. His target is work chosen *because of* how it\u2019ll be remembered. So ask whether you\u2019d still choose it if **the credit** went elsewhere.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Marcus argues against wanting **fame after death**. He doesn\u2019t call it sinful pride, and he doesn\u2019t say fame goes to the wrong people. His reason is closer to arithmetic. **Can you guess it**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Every admirer is **on the same schedule** as you. Then he adds a second step. Even if the memory lasted forever, what would the praise **be worth to you**? The same sum that shrinks fear is aimed here at desire.'},{kind:'story',text:'You can lead a launch with your name on it, or quietly fix the billing errors customers keep hitting. The launch will be remembered. The fix won\u2019t. If you\u2019d still pick the launch with the credit going elsewhere, fine. If not, notice that.'},{kind:'story',text:'At a club meeting, one volunteer gives the welcome speech and gets the applause. Another writes up the minutes nobody will thank her for. Ask each one whether they\u2019d still do it if the credit went elsewhere.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s where it **gets harder**. Credit pays rent, earns trust and decides who gets the next chance. For someone whose work is routinely claimed by others, the test can be unfair. Even Marcus valued a **name for justice**, because it does real work.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Claim **fair credit** for real work. Just don\u2019t let credit **choose the work**. The memory is real, but it\u2019s held by people on the same schedule as you.'}]}}}},971,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'meetings-that-earn-it',kind:'book',promise:'Hold fewer meetings and make each one count',about:'What does a one-hour meeting with eight people cost? It costs eight hours of work. The Mindroll team shows how to count it. Then they show how to make each meeting worth it. Is your next one worth its hours?',ideas:{'meetings-that-earn-it_1':{title:'The Hidden Workday',card:{insight:'A meeting costs its length times the number of people in it.',tryThis:'Before your next invite, write the hours times the people at the top.',skills:['productivity','leadership']},review:{prompt:'You plan a two-hour meeting with nine people. What do you do first?',right:'Count nine people times two hours, then see if it is worth it',wrong:'Cut it to one hour so the calendar looks lighter',why:'A meeting costs its length times the people, so do the math first.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Eight kids from your class give up their **lunch hour** to plan the school play. Each one loses an hour. So how much time did the plan **really cost**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'It cost **eight hours**, not one. When a group meets, every person in the room spends the hour. Nobody feels the total, because each kid only feels **their own hour**. So the big number stays hidden.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Before you call people together, **do the sum**: hours times people. Say the answer out loud. Then ask if it\u2019s **worth that much** to you.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You set up a **weekly hour** to keep a project on track. Eight people accept. The invitation took thirty seconds to send. Nobody, including you, asked what it would **actually cost**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Now **multiply** it out. One hour times eight people is eight hours, a full working day. Every week for a year, that\u2019s roughly **fifty working days**. Would anyone approve that as one line in a budget?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The sum is trivial, so why does nobody do it? The cost is **spread out**. Each person feels only their own hour, and nobody holds the total. As a calendar entry, the same fifty days is **approved every week**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A meeting costs its length **times the people** in it. Do that sum before you send the invitation, and decide if it\u2019s **worth the hours**. Most of the saving happens right there.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'The calendar says a meeting lasts **one hour**. Outside the door hang eight coats on eight hooks. Which of those two numbers is **the real price** of what happens inside?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A meeting is paid for by **the room**, not the calendar. Its cost is duration times attendees. That\u2019s a **distributed cost**: each person bears a small share, so nobody sees the total. Small shares are easy to approve.'},{kind:'story',text:'A team lead books a weekly hour for eight people. Thirty seconds to send. Over a year, it quietly spends about **fifty working days**. Nobody would sign that off as a budget line. As a recurring invite, nobody even asks.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now you\u2019re about to invite twelve people to a ninety-minute review. Before sending, you write the sum: *eighteen hours*. Seeing it, you cut the list to the five who decide. The meeting still happens. It just costs less.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The sum counts cost, not value. A day spent on a decision that saves a month is cheap. And used crudely, it tempts you to cut **someone who was needed**, which moves the cost into rework.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The calendar shows one hour. The room holds **that hour** once for every person in it. Do the sum before you invite, as **a question** about worth, not a number to shrink.'}]},'meetings-that-earn-it_2':{title:'The Broken Morning',card:{insight:'A meeting also costs the free time it breaks up around it.',tryThis:'Mark each gap under an hour in your plan for tomorrow. Then group your meetings.',skills:['productivity','focus']},review:{prompt:'You have three short meetings spread across your day. Why does your day feel wasted?',right:'The gaps between them are too short for real work',wrong:'Three meetings in one day is just too many',why:'A meeting costs its length plus the short gaps it leaves around it.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve got a free Saturday morning to build **a big model**. It takes three hours. But there\u2019s a swim lesson right **in the middle**. How far will the model get?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Think of **a long plank**. Cut one short piece from the middle. Now both ends are too short for a shelf. The swim lesson does the same to your morning. It **breaks the time** on each side.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A thing in the middle of free time costs **more than its length**. If you can, put it at **the start or end**, so the long stretch stays whole.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your diary shows **three half-hour meetings**: at ten, at noon and at three. That\u2019s only ninety minutes. So why do you reach six o\u2019clock with **nothing real** finished?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A meeting at eleven doesn\u2019t remove one hour. It splits the morning into **two blocks** too short for **real work**. The time before is too short to start anything. Then you need time to reassemble afterwards.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Same three meetings, same ninety minutes. Only the **placement** changes what\u2019s **left over**.',left:{title:'Scattered',items:['Ten, noon and three','Gaps too short to use','No block for deep work']},right:{title:'Clustered',items:['Back to back after lunch','One clear morning','Three hours for real work']}},{kind:'moral',text:'A meeting costs its length plus **the scraps** it leaves around it. So cluster meetings and **defend the block** they free. Spreading them out to be polite spends your day.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two people each have **three hours** of meetings today. One ends the day with nothing finished. The other finishes a hard piece of work. So what made **the difference**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A meeting\u2019s real cost is larger than its length. An hour at eleven **fragments the morning** into blocks too short for demanding work. That loss is **recorded nowhere**. No calendar shows the morning you didn\u2019t get.'},{kind:'story',text:'The first had meetings at ten, noon and three. Each gap was an hour or two. That\u2019s too short to start anything hard once you count settling in and **the reassembly** after.'},{kind:'story',text:'The other stacked all three meetings **back to back** after lunch. Her morning stayed whole, four clear hours. She didn\u2019t work harder or faster. She simply treated a clear morning as **the expensive thing**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The idea assumes work that needs **long stretches**. If your job is mostly coordination, short blocks may be the work. And stacking costs too: back to back, you\u2019ve no time to act on one meeting before the next.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Judge a meeting by **the time it spoils**, not just the time it takes. Cluster them, then **guard the block** you\u2019ve freed. It\u2019s only worth guarding if the hard work happens there.'}]},'meetings-that-earn-it_3':{title:'Four Jobs, One Hour',card:{insight:'Give each meeting one job first, like making a choice or sharing news.',tryThis:'Put the one job of your next meeting first in its invite.',skills:['communication','productivity']},review:{prompt:'Twelve parents meet to share news and pick a date. What goes wrong?',right:'Two jobs fight for one hour, so the news wins',wrong:'It needed more time on the calendar to talk',why:'Each kind of meeting needs its own people, so name the job first.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your club meets at lunch to **pick a name** for the team. First someone shares news about the trip. Then the chat drifts to new games. The bell rings, and the name is **still not picked**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Meetings do **different jobs**. Some decide things, some think up ideas and some just share news. Your club tried **all three at once** in one lunch. So the one job that mattered never got done.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Before a group meets, **say the job** out loud. Are you here to decide, to share ideas or to hear news? Once everyone knows, it\u2019s much easier to **stay on track**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Twelve people join **a launch sync**. Half the hour goes on updates. Then someone asks the real question: which date do we launch? Two of the people who must decide **aren\u2019t even there**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'There are **four jobs** a meeting can do: deciding, generating, aligning and informing. Each needs a different size, format and length. Most bad meetings are **two jobs fighting** for one hour. Your sync was an update someone hoped would end in a decision.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Deciding and generating sound alike, but they need **different rooms** and **different people**.',left:{title:'Deciding',items:['Only the people who decide','Options prepared in advance','Ends with a choice']},right:{title:'Generating',items:['Few enough to actually speak','No audience watching','Ends with options']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Name the meeting\u2019s job **before you book it**. The job sets who comes, how long it runs and **whether it\u2019s needed**. Put the type in the invitation\u2019s first word.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'An invitation reads *Project sync, 60 minutes*, and twelve people accept. Nobody knows if they\u2019re there to decide, brainstorm or listen. What happens when **twelve people** walk in with **four different** ideas of why?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Meetings do **different jobs**, and the job sets the format, the attendance and whether the meeting should exist. Deciding needs the deciders and prepared options. Aligning needs everyone whose work must fit. Informing **rarely needs** a meeting.'},{kind:'story',text:'At the sync, half the hour goes on updates. Then someone raises the real question: the launch date. Two people who must decide are absent. Nobody prepared the options. The room discovers this **forty minutes in**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a brainstorm held in front of an audience. Fifteen people are invited, but only three talk. The rest watch, so nobody risks a half-formed idea. It\u2019s a generating session run as **a presentation**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. In practice the types blur. A good brainstorm often ends in a decision. Applied rigidly, the labels become **stickers on invitations** that change nothing. The habit matters more: asking what the hour is for.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Most bad meetings are one type **run as another**. So before you book, ask what the hour is for and **who that job needs**. You\u2019ll spot the wrong guest list before the room fills.'}]},'meetings-that-earn-it_4':{title:'News Without a Room',card:{insight:'When news only goes one way, writing beats a meeting.',tryThis:'Pick one update you would give in a meeting. Write it as five lines you could send.',skills:['communication','productivity']},review:{prompt:'Your weekly meeting is just people reading out updates. What do you try?',right:'Write the updates down and keep a slot for real questions',wrong:'Keep the meeting, but give each update one minute',why:'If news goes one way, write it, but keep time for questions.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your whole class sits in the hall for **twenty minutes**. A teacher reads out the trip date, what to bring and the cost. By home time, **half of you** have forgotten. Was the hall needed?'},{kind:'explain',text:'That news only went **one way**. Nobody needed to talk back. A letter in your bag would\u2019ve done the job, and you could **read it again** at home. Nobody has to be in one place at once.'},{kind:'moral',text:'If a meeting is just to **pass on news**, write it down instead. A note is quick to make and **quick to read**. It\u2019s there whenever you need it.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Every Monday, you and nine colleagues join a call. One by one, they **read out updates**. Most listeners are half-checking email. Afterwards, nobody can say what anyone else said. Why does this meeting **keep happening**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'When information flows one way, **writing wins**. A written update is faster to produce and faster to read. It\u2019s searchable afterwards and works across time zones. You can read about **four times faster** than anyone speaks.'},{kind:'explain',text:'So why does the Monday call survive? Writing is harder than talking, and nobody checks that updates get read. But the call also gives people **a chance** to ask real questions and **bump into** each other.'},{kind:'moral',text:'If an update only flows one way, **write it down** and cancel the room. But name what the meeting quietly did for people, and **keep that part** on purpose.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Ten people give up an hour every Monday to hear each other **read out updates**. Written down, you could read the lot over coffee. So why does the meeting **survive** in so many teams?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Information that flows one way doesn\u2019t need **people in a room** together. Writing is faster to produce and to read, searchable afterwards, and free of time zones. The meeting persists because writing is harder and **reading isn\u2019t enforced**.'},{kind:'story',text:'A product team swaps its Monday call for a five-line written update. Each person writes theirs by ten. People read them at four times speaking speed, whenever suits. The hour comes back to **ten calendars**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a manager who calls you all in to announce a new holiday rota. Twenty minutes, no questions asked. A short note would have done it, and staff could check it again in March.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Status meetings often do quiet work: visibility, coordination, chats at the edges. Cancel them carelessly and **that\u2019s lost** too. And some news isn\u2019t one-way. With **bad news**, people\u2019s reaction is the point.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Put **one-way news** in writing, and give the room back. Then name what the meeting did besides informing, and **serve it on purpose**. Don\u2019t pretend it was never there.'}]},'meetings-that-earn-it_5':{title:'The Two-Line Ending',card:{insight:'A meeting is done once the choice and who will do it are written down.',tryThis:'Look back at your last meeting. Write the choice and who will do it in two lines.',skills:['leadership','communication']},review:{prompt:'Everyone nods at the end of a meeting, but nobody writes it down. What now?',right:'Type the choice and who will do it before anyone leaves',wrong:'Trust the nods and follow up by email next week',why:'A plan is real once it is written down with a name.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You and three friends plan **a picnic** for Saturday, and everyone happily agrees. You all go home excited. On the day, nobody has asked a parent and **nobody has brought food**. What went wrong?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Everyone agreed, but nobody said **who would do what**. So each friend thought someone else would sort it. A plan with no names is just **a nice feeling**. It isn\u2019t a plan yet.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A meeting isn\u2019t done until you say **what was decided** and who will do it. Write it down **before anyone leaves**, so nobody has to guess.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'The meeting ran well. Everyone nodded, and you all left feeling **aligned**. Three weeks later, two people are doing the same task and nobody has done the other. What did the room **never produce**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Ending a meeting properly feels **socially awkward**. Summing up sounds like taking charge. Assigning sounds like giving orders. So the room drifts apart on a general sense of agreement. That sense turns out to be **several different agreements**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The fix is a procedure, not a culture change. Use your **last two minutes** to restate the decision, the owner and the date. Then type it while everyone is still there. That **two-line summary** is the proof.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A meeting with no written decision and **no named owner** hasn\u2019t concluded. It has just stopped. So **type both lines** before anyone leaves the room.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Some meetings end with a clear sentence: *Sam owns this, by Friday.* Others just stop when the time runs out. From inside **the room**, both can feel **equally productive**. How would you tell them apart?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A meeting without a stated decision and **a named owner** hasn\u2019t concluded. It has stopped. Ending is awkward, so people leave on a vague agreement. The written summary turns that feeling into **an actual agreement**, or shows there wasn\u2019t one.'},{kind:'story',text:'A team spends an hour on the launch plan and leaves nodding. Nobody types a summary. Three weeks later, two people have built the same page. Nobody has written the emails. Each had heard **a different agreement**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture another team. In the last two minutes, someone tries to type the decision and gets stuck. There isn\u2019t one yet. That\u2019s awkward, but finding it out **in the room** is far cheaper than in three weeks.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Not every meeting should end in a decision. A brainstorm may rightly end with options, and forcing a choice makes a false one. An owner with no time or authority is **only a name**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Close every meeting by typing **the decision** and **the owner** while everyone is there. Read it back to the room. If you can\u2019t write it, you\u2019ve learned something cheaply.'}]}}}},972,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'melian-dialogue',kind:'book',promise:'See who really holds the power in a talk',about:'What happens when one side is much stronger? Long ago, strong Athens sent warships to Melos, a small island. Before the fight, the two sides talked. A Greek writer named Thucydides told the story of that talk. Would you give in, or fight?',ideas:{'melian-dialogue_1':{title:'A Case With No Prize',card:{insight:'To see why a rule exists, look at a time its reason is gone.',tryThis:'Pick one rule you follow. Then find a time when its reason is gone.',skills:['thinking','history']},review:{prompt:'A friend says he only cuts in line when he is late. How do you check?',right:'Watch what he does when he has no reason to rush',wrong:'Ask him to tell you the truth about it',why:'If he cuts in line when he is not late, being late is not his reason.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A girl in your class says she only pushes in the lunch queue on **pizza day**. Then you see her push in on **cabbage day** too. What does that tell you about her reason?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Long ago, strong Athens told the small island of Melos to **give in**. Melos had nothing Athens really needed. A writer called Thucydides told the story in full. With **no prize** to point to, you could see Athens\u2019 real reason.'},{kind:'moral',text:'If someone does a thing even when there\u2019s **nothing to win**, the prize was never the reason. Small cases show you **the real reason**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your gym says its no-guests rule is about **crowding**. Then, at six on a Sunday morning, staff turn away your friend from an **empty room**. Nobody else is there. So what is the rule really for?'},{kind:'story',text:'In 416 BCE Athens, at war with Sparta, sent a fleet to Melos. It was a small island outside the Athenian empire, with little Athens needed. Thucydides wrote the talks before the attack as a long dialogue, an unusual choice for him.'},{kind:'explain',text:'When a big prize is at stake, any harsh act can be blamed on **the prize**. Take the prize away and that excuse goes too. If the act still happens, **the principle** is doing the work. That\u2019s why Thucydides gives Melos so much space.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Compare a **high-stakes case** with one where **nothing big** hangs on it.',left:{title:'High stakes',items:['The prize explains everything','Harshness hides behind need','The principle stays hidden']},right:{title:'No prize',items:['Nothing to blame','The reason stands alone','The principle shows itself']}},{kind:'moral',text:'To see what really drives a rule, watch it where **nothing\u2019s at stake**. If it still holds there, the stated reason isn\u2019t **the whole story**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Thucydides wrote a long history of a great war between Athens and Sparta. Most of it is battles and speeches. Then, for one **minor episode**, he stops and writes a whole **dialogue**. Why spend so much on so little?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Melos works as **a controlled case**. Nothing strategic hangs on it, so no interest can soften what Athens says. You see the claim about power with **nothing in front** of it. It\u2019s the cleanest test you could design.'},{kind:'story',text:'In 416 BCE an Athenian fleet reached Melos, a small island first settled by Spartans. It had stayed outside the Athenian empire. The envoys talked with its leaders before any fighting began. With little to gain, Athens had to argue from power alone.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a firm that says its strict dress code is for meeting clients. Watch it on a Friday when no client visits. If people are still sent home to change, something besides clients is driving the rule.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Small isn\u2019t the same as irrelevant. Melos was a Spartan colony, and Athens had attacked it about ten years before. And a principle seen with **nothing at stake** may act differently when everything is.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Low stakes are where **reasoning is easiest** to read. When little depends on the outcome, the reasons you hear are closer to **what people believe**.'}]},'melian-dialogue_2':{title:'Justice Between Equals',card:{insight:'The strong only care what is fair when you can push back.',tryThis:'Next time a deal feels unfair, write down what the other side would lose.',skills:['thinking','leadership']},review:{prompt:'A big store you sell to says it will pay you less. What helps most?',right:'Find a second buyer, so you can push back',wrong:'Write a kind letter about why the cut is unfair',why:'If you can walk away, the big store has to listen.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A much bigger kid takes your **football** at break. You shout that it isn\u2019t fair. He just laughs and keeps playing. Why didn\u2019t **being right** help you?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Long ago, strong Athens told little Melos something shocking. People only argue about **fairness** when both sides are equally strong. Otherwise the strong side does **what it can**, and the weak side has to put up with it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Being right isn\u2019t always enough. So find a way to **push back**: friends who stand with you, or **a rule** the bigger kid must follow.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You run a small bakery. One big caf\xe9 chain buys **most of your bread**. It cuts the price it pays by a fifth. You write a careful letter about **fairness**. Why does nothing change?'},{kind:'story',text:'Athens opened its talks with Melos by refusing fine speeches. It wouldn\u2019t claim it had earned its empire by beating Persia. Then it said justice only arises between **equals in power**. Between unequals, the strong take what they can and the weak bear it.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Your letter assumed the two of you were **equals**. You weren\u2019t, and that **changes the rules**.',left:{title:'Between equals',items:['Both sides can walk away','A fair case gets heard']},right:{title:'Between unequals',items:['Only one side can walk','A fair case gets filed']}},{kind:'explain',text:'So the useful question isn\u2019t whether you\u2019re right. It\u2019s what the chain **loses by ignoring you**. A second buyer, other bakers acting together, a contract it must honour. Each one adds **leverage**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Fairness only binds when **both sides** can make the other listen. So build **leverage** first. Then your fair case finally has weight.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'The best-known line in the Melian Dialogue isn\u2019t shouted. The Athenians say it **almost wearily**, like a remark about the weather. Why might a calm tone make a hard claim **harder to answer**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The claim runs like this. Justice is a question only between **parties equal in power**. Between unequal ones, the strong do what their power allows. The weak bear **what they can\u2019t prevent**. It\u2019s offered as a description, not a boast.'},{kind:'story',text:'In the talks, the Melians kept returning to justice, the gods and Sparta. Each time, the calm opening had already answered them. Their case sounded naive rather than brave, however right it was.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a tenant whose landlord won\u2019t fix the heating. A polite note about what\u2019s fair goes unanswered. A note citing the landlord\u2019s legal duty, copied to the council, gets a reply within days.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The claim fits much of history, but it isn\u2019t a law. The strong need allies and citizens who **believe in them**. And if the weak stop making the fair case, nobody ever pays for ignoring it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When you hold little power, **being right** won\u2019t carry you alone. Build leverage, and keep **the fair case** too. Each one needs the other.'}]},'melian-dialogue_3':{title:'Hope Is Not a Plan',card:{insight:'Hope is fine when you have a plan, but it is not a plan.',tryThis:'Pick one thing you are hoping for. Write one real step you can take this week.',skills:['thinking','psychology']},review:{prompt:'You lost your job, and one company said maybe. What now?',right:'Keep hoping, but keep looking for other jobs too',wrong:'Just wait for that company to call you back',why:'Hope is fine on top of a plan, but it cannot be the plan.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your library book is due back tomorrow, and you\u2019ve lost it. You don\u2019t look. You just **hope** it turns up. What\u2019s really going to **find that book**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Long ago, small Melos faced the huge army of Athens. It didn\u2019t have enough soldiers. So it **hoped**: for luck, for help from the gods, for friends in Sparta. Athens said hope is fine **with a real plan**, and dangerous alone.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Hoping is fine if you\u2019re **doing something** too. But hope can\u2019t do the work for you. Name **one real step**, then take it.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your small business has **three months** of cash left. A big client has said they\u2019re *interested*. There\u2019s no contract and no date. You\u2019ve stopped looking for anyone else. How safe is your **month four**?'},{kind:'story',text:'The Melians faced Athens with too few soldiers. They said they trusted in fortune, in the gods, and in Sparta, which had founded their city. The Athenians answered each in turn. On hope, they were coldest of all.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Their point was about **position**, not optimism. Hope is a fair comfort if you\u2019ve got **other resources** behind it. If it\u2019s all you\u2019ve got, you only find out what it was worth after you\u2019ve lost. Your client is that kind of hope.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The same hope can be **harmless** or **ruinous**, depending on what stands behind it.',left:{title:'Hope plus margin',items:['You keep working','A miss hurts but passes']},right:{title:'Hope as margin',items:['Nothing stands behind it','A miss is final']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Hope is affordable when **you have a margin**. It\u2019s ruinous when it **is the margin**. So name what must turn up, and keep looking anyway.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A siege is coming. Your city is small and your army smaller. Your friends live across a sea **the enemy controls**. You still have **one thing left**. What is it, and is it enough?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The Athenians answered three appeals. Hope is **a luxury** of those with means. The gods, they said, rule wherever they can, as men do. And Sparta acts on its interest, so it won\u2019t risk much for colonists across **an Athenian sea**.'},{kind:'story',text:'They added a sharper point. Once real resources run out, people lean on **unseen ones**: prophecies and oracles. The Melians replied that their city was seven centuries old, and they wouldn\u2019t give up its freedom. It was noble, and it didn\u2019t answer the argument.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a student who skips revision because a friend says the exam is *always easy*. No notes, no past papers, just that one rumour. The worse the position gets, the more the rumour feels like evidence.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The argument is cold, and handy for the one making it. An empire telling its victim to drop hope **isn\u2019t neutral**. And hope sometimes pays: sieges get lifted, long shots come in. It just can\u2019t carry the plan.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Hope is a fair comfort when you have **a margin**. The danger grows just as your options shrink, which is when hope **feels most needed**.'}]},'melian-dialogue_4':{title:'Tell It Plainly',card:{insight:'Bad news told in plain words hits harder than news full of big words.',tryThis:'Next time you share bad news, write it once with no feeling words. Then read it back.',skills:['communication','history']},review:{prompt:'You must tell your team that a project failed. How do you write it?',right:'Say plainly what happened and what it cost, with no big words',wrong:'Start by saying how sad and sorry everyone feels',why:'Big words tell people how to feel, but plain facts let them feel it.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your friend tells you about a scary moment. She doesn\u2019t say *it was awful*. She just says the car slid, the wall came close, and then it stopped. Why does that **hit you harder** than **big words**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Melos said no to Athens. The island was surrounded and fell. The men were killed, and the women and children sold as slaves. Thucydides told it in **a few plain lines**. He didn\u2019t say how awful it was. He **just stopped**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When something terrible happens, you don\u2019t always need to say **how bad** it was. Say it plainly. Your reader will **feel it**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re writing up a project that failed and cost three people their jobs. One draft is full of *deeply unfortunate*. Another lists **what was decided**, when, and what followed. Which one is **harder to dismiss**?'},{kind:'story',text:'The Melians refused Athens. Athens besieged the island and took it. The men it captured were put to death and the women and children sold. Five hundred Athenian settlers later moved in. Thucydides tells it in **a few flat sentences**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'He uses **the same level voice** he used for the arguments. No comment follows. The Melians had trusted fortune, the gods and Sparta. None came. The outcome answers every appeal, so **the flat report** is the rebuttal.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Report the hard thing plainly and **let it land**. Your adjectives tell readers what to feel. **The bare facts** let them feel it themselves, and they\u2019re harder to wave away.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture a comic strip with two panels. In the first, a boy laughs at someone who slipped on ice. In the second, **the same boy** slips. There are **no words** at all. What has the strip just said?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Thucydides works the same way. The massacre at Melos is told in **the same register** as the debate before it. Then he places it between the Melian appeals it refutes and **an overreach** it seems to foretell. The arrangement is his comment.'},{kind:'story',text:'The siege dragged into winter, Athens sent more troops, and the town surrendered. The grown men taken were killed. The women and children were sold. Five hundred colonists were later sent. That\u2019s all he says before moving on.'},{kind:'story',text:'Right after Melos comes Sicily. Athens, sure of its power, sends a vast fleet far from home and is destroyed. Thucydides never links the two stories. You\u2019re left to notice that they sit side by side.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Restraint isn\u2019t neutrality. Choosing what sits next to what is **a strong editorial act**, and arrangement can steer you like comment. And some readers skim past a flat line a judgement would have stopped.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You don\u2019t need to tell people **how to feel**. Report the facts flatly, and let **what sits beside them** do the arguing.'}]},'melian-dialogue_5':{title:'Read What Comes Next',card:{insight:'A book can show how the strong talk without saying they are right.',tryThis:'When someone quotes a harsh old line, ask what happens next in the book.',skills:['thinking','history']},review:{prompt:'A coworker says this old book proves nice people lose. What do you say?',right:'The same book goes on to show Athens losing big',wrong:'He is right, since strong Athens won the argument',why:'The book shows how the strong talk, then shows them losing a huge fleet.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your friend reads half a story and says the sneaky fox **gets away with it**. So sneaking must pay, she says. But she put the book down early. What might she **have missed**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Athens won the argument with Melos and took the island. Then it sent **a huge fleet** to Sicily and lost it. Years later Athens lost **the whole war**, and its great walls were pulled down.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Thucydides shows how strong Athens talked, but he **doesn\u2019t cheer** it. Before you decide what a book says, **read to the end**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A speaker at a leadership day puts up a slide. The strong take what they can, it says, and the weak bear it. It\u2019s offered as **hard-headed wisdom**. Nobody mentions **what happened next**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Thucydides gives the Athenians the stronger argument, and it works as stated. Melos falls. The very next part of his history is **the Sicilian expedition**, where the same confidence ends in disaster. The war itself ended with Athens defeated.'},{kind:'explain',text:'So the dialogue shows how power talks when it **stops pretending**. Then it shows the state that talked that way **losing everything**. It describes realism. It doesn\u2019t tell you to admire it.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The dialogue gets read in **two ways**, and only one fits **the whole book**.',left:{title:'Read as praise',items:['The Athenians are right','The author agrees','Lesson: use your power']},right:{title:'Read whole',items:['This is how power talks','The book shows what follows','Lesson: power isn\u2019t shelter']}},{kind:'moral',text:'A harsh line in a classic isn\u2019t **the book\u2019s verdict**. When you hear one quoted as wisdom, ask **what happened** to the people who said it.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Courses on world politics often open with **the Melian Dialogue**. It\u2019s called the founding text of realism. Just as often, it\u2019s quoted as if Thucydides **agreed** with his Athenians. Did he?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The text supports **a narrower reading**. This is how power talks when it isn\u2019t performing. And those who talk this way **aren\u2019t protected** from what follows. Whether Thucydides meant more, he never says.'},{kind:'story',text:'He lets the Athenian argument win at Melos. The next part of the history is the disaster in Sicily. He wrote knowing Athens surrendered in 404 BCE, though his own account breaks off some years before.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a manager who quotes a ruthless line from a famous general in every team meeting. He never mentions that the general\u2019s army was later routed. You\u2019re hearing **half the story**, served as wisdom.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The speeches are composed, so you **can\u2019t separate** the Athenians\u2019 reasoning from Thucydides\u2019 arrangement. Sicily can be read as the price of arrogance, or just bad strategy. The ambiguity is probably deliberate.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The dialogue **describes** power without pretence. It doesn\u2019t **recommend** it. Read what comes next before you treat its harshest line as advice.'}]},'melian-dialogue_6':{title:'Whoever Sets the Terms',card:{insight:'The one who sets the rules of a talk often wins it.',tryThis:'When a talk starts with a rule, decide if you agree to it.',skills:['communication','thinking']},review:{prompt:'Your boss opens a pay talk by ruling out last year. What first?',right:'Ask yourself if you agree to that rule first',wrong:'Agree, so the talk can get to the point fast',why:'Say yes to the rules, and you may lose before you start.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Before a race in the garden, your sister sets **one rule**: the tallest runner starts a step ahead. You nod, because it sounds fine. Then you notice who\u2019s **the tallest**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Before Athens and Melos argued, Athens laid down **the rules**. Fairness was off the table. So was who was in the right. They\u2019d only talk about **who could do what**. Melos said yes, and that\u2019s where it lost.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Whoever sets the rules before a talk often **wins the talk**. So when someone says what can\u2019t be argued, stop and decide **if you agree**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your boss opens the pay review calmly. *Let\u2019s keep this to this year\u2019s numbers.* It sounds like **a note about process**. But last year you saved **a major client**. Where did that point just go?'},{kind:'story',text:'Before any position was argued, the Athenians laid down terms. They wouldn\u2019t claim a right to their empire. The Melians mustn\u2019t claim they\u2019d done no wrong. Both would discuss only what was possible. The Melians accepted.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Having accepted, they\u2019d lost. Justice was **ruled out by agreement**. Every later appeal, to the gods or to Sparta, had to become **a forecast about power**. On that ground Athens was always stronger.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Opening rules feel like **procedure**, but they\u2019re usually **substance** in disguise.',left:{title:'How it sounds',items:['Just keeping things tidy','A sensible start','Nothing to argue']},right:{title:'What it does',items:['Removes your best point','Fixes the ground','Decides the outcome']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Whoever sets the terms of a talk has **largely decided it**. So when an opening line rules something out, **decide whether you accept** it before you say anything else.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A meeting about a new road opens with one line from the chair: today is about **cost only**. Heads nod. You think the road is **a mistake**. Has the argument you came to make just vanished?'},{kind:'explain',text:'In the dialogue, **framing** did the heavy lifting. The gods turned into a forecast about whose side heaven takes, and Athens claimed heaven too. Sparta turned into **a forecast** about Spartan interest. Each appeal landed **where Athens was stronger**.'},{kind:'story',text:'It began before any of that. The Athenians set the rules. No claim of a right to rule, no Melian plea of innocence. Only what each side could do. The Melians agreed, and from then on they argued uphill.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a buyer who opens: let\u2019s keep to the numbers, not the account\u2019s history. It sounds like housekeeping. If that history is your **strongest point**, you\u2019ve just agreed to argue without it.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The Melians did half resist. They protested that the outcome was fixed, and argued that fair dealing served Athens too. And **refusing a frame** isn\u2019t free: the stronger side can simply stop talking.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Terms are usually set in an opening that **both sides call procedure**. Notice it. You won\u2019t always be able to refuse, but you can **decide on purpose**.'}]},'melian-dialogue_7':{title:'Staying Out Can Cost You',card:{insight:'To a strong side, staying out can look worse than saying no.',tryThis:'Before you stay out of a fight at work, write down who is watching.',skills:['thinking','leadership']},review:{prompt:'Your team backs a big change. You stay out. Why might the boss press you most?',right:'Others are watching, and staying out looks like a no',wrong:'People who stay out are just easier to pick on',why:'If one person stays out for free, others who watch may try it too.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A bossy group runs the playground, and everyone does what they say. One kid says, *I\u2019ll just stay out of it.* She isn\u2019t **against** anyone. So why is the group **so cross** with her?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Melos asked Athens if it could be friends and **join no side**. Athens said no. It ruled lots of islands. If tiny Melos stayed free, those islands might think **Athens was weak**, and try it too.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Staying out can look safe, but **others are watching**. When you sit something out, ask yourself who\u2019s looking and **what they\u2019ll think** it means.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A director is pushing a big reorganisation. Most people back it. You stay **neutral**: no objection, no support. You expect to be left alone. Instead she presses you harder than **the open critics**. What did your neutrality say?'},{kind:'story',text:'The Melians offered Athens friendship instead of war, as allies of neither side. The Athenians refused outright. Their subject islands, they said, would read a friendly Melos as a sign of weakness. Open hostility they\u2019d read as strength.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The argument isn\u2019t really about Melos. It\u2019s about **what everyone watching** will infer. Hostility can be explained by strength. **Tolerated neutrality** shows that defiance is free, and an empire pays for that in every relationship.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your neutrality isn\u2019t invisible. To a dominant side, it can read as **a dare**. Before you sit something out, work out **who\u2019s watching** and what they\u2019ll take it as.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A small country declares itself neutral between **two great powers**. It threatens nobody. It wants only to be left alone. Why might the stronger neighbour treat that as **worse than enmity**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The Athenians rejected Melian neutrality as **worse than hostility**. An open enemy confirms the order of things. **An unpunished neutral** questions it. Every subject island watching would learn that Athens could be defied for free.'},{kind:'story',text:'Melos had first taken neither side in the war. Thucydides says it turned openly hostile only after Athens ravaged its land. Then, in the talks, its offer of friendship was refused. Its harmless position was treated as the dangerous one.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a large firm that dominates a trade body. One small firm declines to sign its joint statement, without opposing it. The large firm reacts more sharply than to any open rival. You can probably guess why.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. This explains the Athenians. It **doesn\u2019t justify** them. Treating neutrals as rebels can turn bystanders into enemies. And neutral states have lasted for long periods beside great powers, so it\u2019s **a risk**, not a rule.'},{kind:'moral',text:'To a dominant power, your staying out is **a signal** to everyone else. Weigh **what it signals**, and to whom, before you assume neutral means safe.'}]},'melian-dialogue_8':{title:'Who Gets a Say',card:{insight:'Before you call a choice brave, ask if the people who pay got a say.',tryThis:'Next time you decide something for a group, tell them why first. Let them have a say.',skills:['leadership','communication']},review:{prompt:'Your captain pulls the team from the playoffs without asking. How do you judge it?',right:'Ask if the team who pays for it heard the case',wrong:'Cheer for her, since standing up alone is brave',why:'The people who pay for a choice should hear the case first.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your family is picking a holiday. Your dad and big brother talk it over **behind a shut door**. They choose two weeks of camping in the rain. You never heard **the reasons**. Is that fair?'},{kind:'explain',text:'When Athens came, the leaders of Melos didn\u2019t let the Athenians speak to **all the people**. The talks happened with just a few leaders there. Melos chose to fight. **The whole island** paid for it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'On Melos, a few people heard the case **for everyone**. The people who would suffer **never heard it**. Brave or not, that matters, and you can notice it.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A board decides to fight a takeover the staff would have accepted. The fight is long and costly. In the end, many of those staff **lose their jobs**. They were never shown **the offer**. Who was brave here?'},{kind:'story',text:'Early in the dialogue, Thucydides notes almost in passing that the Athenian envoys weren\u2019t brought before the people. Only the magistrates and **the few** heard them. He never raises it again. At the end, he records what happened to everyone.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The Melian leaders made a genuinely admirable choice about **liberty and honour**. But the people who paid for it never heard the case made. Thucydides sets **the two facts** side by side. You\u2019re left to draw the line.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Put the room and the island next to each other, and **the gap** is **hard to miss**.',left:{title:'Who heard it',items:['The magistrates and the few','Every Athenian argument']},right:{title:'Who paid',items:['The whole population','Killed or enslaved']}},{kind:'moral',text:'When you take a decision **on others\u2019 behalf**, check whether they\u2019ve **heard the case**. If they haven\u2019t, ask why not. It\u2019s easy to be brave with other people\u2019s lives.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture a meeting on a small island. A powerful enemy has sent envoys. The leaders hear them **in private**. The rest of the island **isn\u2019t in the room**. Everyone\u2019s life depends on the answer. Who should hear the case?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The Athenians claim to know why. The Melian leaders feared a crowd would be won over by **a persuasive case** heard without rebuttal. So the talks happened before the magistrates and the few. **Thucydides doesn\u2019t comment**.'},{kind:'story',text:'The case for refusing Athens risked every life on the island. Yet only a few heard it, on behalf of all. The refusal came, then the siege, the killings and the slavery. Thucydides leaves the facts side by side.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a government that commits a country to a costly war without any vote, and the cause proves just. You can still ask the Melian question. Did the people who bore the cost **ever hear the case**?'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. We don\u2019t know whether the final answer was put to the people. A free assembly might have refused too, with **the same result**. And leaders sometimes must act without full consultation. The point is consent, not prediction.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Courage is easier to admire when **you\u2019re not the one** paying. Before you praise a brave refusal, ask **who heard the case** and who bore the cost.'}]},'melian-dialogue_9':{title:'A Warning Too Far Off',card:{insight:'A warning about next year won\u2019t move someone judged on this year.',tryThis:'Next time you warn someone, find out what they are judged on. Tie your warning to that.',skills:['communication','thinking']},review:{prompt:'Your boss cuts safety classes to fit this year\u2019s budget. What works best?',right:'Tie the warning to a cost that hits this year',wrong:'Warn her it will hurt her good name one day',why:'If she is judged on this year, tie the warning to this year.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A kid in your class keeps taking other people\u2019s snacks. You warn him, *soon nobody will be your friend.* He just shrugs and takes **another crisp**. Why doesn\u2019t **your warning** work?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Melos warned Athens. If you crush us, other islands will learn that giving in **earns nothing**, and fight you harder. Athens didn\u2019t care. It wanted the islands it already ruled to be **scared right now**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A warning about later only works if someone **cares about later**. So when you warn someone, find a reason that matters to them **today**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You warn a sales director that pushing customers into **the wrong contracts** will hurt the brand in two years. Her bonus is paid **this quarter**. She thanks you and carries on. What did you miss?'},{kind:'story',text:'The Melians played the best card the weak usually hold. Destroy us, they said, and every other state learns what **submitting to Athens** is worth. The Athenians brushed it aside. They feared their own restless subjects more.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The warning was sound. Within a generation Athens had lost the war, and treating allies as subjects fed **the defections**. The Athenians didn\u2019t refute the argument. They were simply measuring on **a shorter clock**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Your warning to the director assumed **one clock**. She was running on **another**.',left:{title:'What you assumed',items:['She\u2019s judged on the long run','Later costs feel real now']},right:{title:'What was true',items:['She\u2019s judged this quarter','Later costs fall elsewhere']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Reputational costs are real, but they **arrive late**. Before you warn someone, find out **what period** they\u2019re judged on. Then tie your warning to a cost counted this year.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A regulator warns a bank that reckless lending will cost it public trust **in five years**. The bank\u2019s executives serve **two-year terms**. Everyone agrees the warning is correct. Why does nothing change?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Reputational costs are **spread out**, delayed and hard to trace to one act. Decisions get made under pressure by people answerable for **this month**. Each feature pushes the same way. The cost is real, but it falls outside the frame.'},{kind:'story',text:'At Melos the warning was clear: crush us, and every neutral learns submission earns nothing. Athens, fearing its restless islanders, brushed it aside. Within a generation it had lost the war, as allies defected when they could.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a restaurant manager who cuts portion sizes to hit this month\u2019s target. You warn her regulars will drift away by next year. She\u2019s judged on the monthly figures. The warning is right, and she can\u2019t hear it.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Hindsight makes it easy to call Athens short-sighted. Fear does work in the short run. Sometimes **the short run** is all there is. And tying Melos to later defections is an inference, not proof.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A correct warning can still be **irrelevant** to the person hearing it. Before you make one, check **which clock** they\u2019re measured on, and speak to that.'}]}}}},973,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'money-conversations',kind:'book',promise:'Talk about money before it costs you',about:'Why is money so hard to talk about, even with the people you live with? Staying quiet feels free, but it costs you. These five ideas show what it costs and how to start. Who will you talk to first?',ideas:{'money-conversations_1':{title:'The Quiet Bill',card:{insight:'Saying nothing about money costs a bit each month, and that adds up.',tryThis:'List three bills you pay that someone you live with may pay too.',skills:['money','communication']},review:{prompt:'Two roommates never talk about bills, and nothing has gone wrong yet. What happens?',right:'Small waste keeps adding up each month until someone talks',wrong:'Nothing, since no problem has shown up so far',why:'Nothing big goes wrong, but the small costs add up each month.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'It\u2019s your mum\u2019s birthday, so you save up and buy her **a nice mug**. Your brother does the same, from the same shop. Neither of you asked. What did **not talking** cost?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Nothing terrible happened. But money got **spent twice**, just because nobody wanted to start a chat. That kind of cost is **quiet**. It\u2019s like a dripping tap: one drip is nothing, but it drips all day, every day.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Not talking about money rarely causes **one big disaster**. It causes **small waste**, again and again. A short, awkward chat now saves you a lot later.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A couple each put money aside for **the next car**. They do it separately, for three years. Neither mentions it. Nothing goes wrong. So what has all that **polite silence** cost them?'},{kind:'story',text:'When it finally comes up, they have two half-built funds and two sets of paperwork. They also have an argument about why nobody said. No single day was a disaster. The cost arrived in small, steady pieces.'},{kind:'explain',text:'This is why silence feels free. Its cost is **paid continuously**, so nothing ever announces it. At home it shows up as doubled savings or unclaimed benefits. At work, it can leave you **underpaid for years**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Compare the two costs. One is **fixed and immediate**. The other **keeps compounding** while you wait.',left:{title:'Talking',items:['A few awkward minutes','Paid once','Easier early']},right:{title:'Silence',items:['Small waste every month','Paid again and again','Harder the longer it runs']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Silence about money isn\u2019t free. It sends you **a quiet bill** every month. Talk early, while **the cost is small** and the chat is still easy.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Ask people what it costs to avoid money talk and they\u2019ll picture **a crisis**: debt, a hidden loan, a blow-up. Usually it\u2019s nothing like that. So why does the bill still **keep growing**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The cost of silence is **real and continuous**. It never arrives as an event, so nothing prompts the talk. By the time you see it, the amount is large. That makes the talk **harder, not easier**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Two flatmates share a home for two years. Each quietly pays for the same streaming service and the same insurance cover. Neither asks. When they finally compare notes, the overlap is bigger than either guessed.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take work. Two colleagues do the same job, and neither has ever said what they earn. One is paid less for years. No single payday feels like a loss. Added up, it\u2019s a large one.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. This assumes two people of goodwill. If one partner uses money to control the other, open accounts **may not be safe**. And early can mean too early, weeks into a new relationship.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The discomfort of talking is **fixed**. The cost of silence **compounds**. So when you share money with someone you trust, the cheapest time to talk is now.'}]},'money-conversations_2':{title:'The Receipt on the Table',card:{insight:'Most money fights are about a feeling, like being left out.',tryThis:'Write down what bugged you in your last money fight, besides the price. Then ask the other person the same.',skills:['relationships','communication']},review:{prompt:'Your partner agrees to a cheaper gym but stays upset for days. Why?',right:'They felt left out when the gym was picked without them',wrong:'The cheaper gym still costs more than they wanted',why:'A lower price does not fix the feeling under the fight.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your sister gets **new trainers**. You don\u2019t. You shout that they cost far too much. Your mum offers you a pair next month. Are you happy now, or is something still **bothering you**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The trainers weren\u2019t really the problem. The feeling underneath was about **fairness**, or about **being asked**. Fixing the price doesn\u2019t touch that feeling. So the same fight comes back next time, about something else.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Fights about money are often **not about money**. Next time, ask what it **means to them**, not how much it cost. That\u2019s where the real fight is.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'One of you wants **a week abroad**. The other wants to save. You argue about the price all evening. You find a cheaper trip and agree. A week later, the **same row** returns. Why?'},{kind:'story',text:'This time it\u2019s about a sofa. Again you haggle over the amount, and again you settle it. But one of you still feels decisions get made **without them**. That feeling never came up, so it never got settled.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The amount was **a stand-in**. A row about a holiday is often a row about **who decides**, held in the only words to hand. Agree the figure and the real disagreement survives, unnamed. So it returns with the next purchase.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'So there are **two questions** you could ask. Only one reaches **the real subject**.',left:{title:'What it costs',items:['Haggle over the figure','Settle the number','Row returns next month']},right:{title:'What it means',items:['Ask what it says','Find the feeling underneath','Settle the real thing']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Money rows are rarely about **the amount**. They\u2019re about feeling **unheard or unsafe**. Ask what the money would mean, not what it would buy.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A partner objects to a gym costing \xa360 a month. They agree on a \xa330 one instead. Both have won something. So why is the house still **so tense** a week later? What did **the deal** miss?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Money is often **the only vocabulary** for a different disagreement. Underneath sit questions of **security, fairness**, freedom and whose work counts. Bargain over the number and you treat the proxy as the thing. Each side wins concessions, yet nothing is touched.'},{kind:'story',text:'Back to the gym. Asked what it meant, the objecting partner says it isn\u2019t the \xa360. It\u2019s that the decision was made before they heard about it. That\u2019s specific, and far easier to fix than a price.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take two siblings splitting the cost of a parent\u2019s birthday meal. One keeps querying the bill. The real issue is that she\u2019s always the one who books it. Once that\u2019s said, the bill stops mattering.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Sometimes it really is the number. If the money isn\u2019t there, exploring meanings **won\u2019t pay the bill**. And the question can become a dodge, asking what it means to avoid saying what it costs.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The number is usually **a stand-in**. Ask what the money would mean to the other person. Then you\u2019re arguing about **the real thing**, and you can actually settle it.'}]},'money-conversations_3':{title:'The Monthly Money Talk',card:{insight:'A money talk on a set day starts calm, since nothing is wrong yet.',tryThis:'Pick one day each month for a short money talk. Write down two things to cover.',skills:['habits','money']},review:{prompt:'A couple\u2019s money talks keep turning into fights. What change helps most?',right:'Set a short talk each month, before anything goes wrong',wrong:'Wait for a calm moment when both feel ready',why:'A talk on a set day starts calm, because no problem set it off.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Jas and her dad sit down for **ten minutes** every Sunday after tea. They look at her pocket money and what she\u2019s saving for. It\u2019s a bit boring. So why does it **work so well**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Starting a tricky chat is **the scariest bit**. With a set time, nobody has to decide to start. It just comes round, like PE on a Tuesday. And because nothing is wrong, **nobody feels blamed**. You can just talk.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Pick **a set time** to talk about money, like once a week. Then your chats aren\u2019t about a problem. They\u2019re just **the usual time**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Think about the last few times money came up in your home **without a plan**. What set it off? A bill, an overdraft, a big purchase? Each time, someone was **already upset**. Does that sound familiar?'},{kind:'story',text:'Sam and Ola used to talk money only after a surprise bill. Every talk began defensive. Now they meet for thirty minutes on the first Sunday of each month. The agenda is known. Most months it\u2019s dull.'},{kind:'explain',text:'A talk that only happens when someone\u2019s upset will always be **about being upset**. A fixed slot has the opposite traits. Nothing has gone wrong and nobody is accused. It also removes **the hardest step**, which is deciding to start.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t wait for a good moment. It **doesn\u2019t arrive**. Put **a short slot** in the calendar, so you never have to decide to start the talk.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'It feels sensible to save a money talk for **the right moment**: calm, relaxed, nobody tired. It sounds kind. But when did that moment last **actually arrive** in your home?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Unplanned money talks are almost always set off by **a problem**. So they start with someone defensive and something already wrong. That\u2019s **the worst condition** for the subject. A scheduled slot flips each of those. The agenda is known, and nobody\u2019s accused.'},{kind:'story',text:'Sam and Ola now meet monthly. They cover what came in, what went out, and what\u2019s coming. Neither has to say *we need to talk*. The slot simply comes round, and the talk happens because nobody had to start it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a parent and a teenager with a weekly ten-minute check on pocket money. It\u2019s short and fairly dull. But when a bigger request comes, there\u2019s already a place to raise it. Nobody has to ambush anyone.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. A slot can become a ritual: thirty minutes of figures and nothing real said. It\u2019s **no reason to sit** on an urgent problem until the date. And if one person books it to audit the other, it\u2019s an interrogation.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The good moment rarely comes, so **book it** instead. A fixed slot means **nothing is wrong** when you start. And you never face the hardest step, deciding to begin.'}]},'money-conversations_4':{title:'Say Your Number First',card:{insight:'Say your number first with friends, and they will often share theirs.',tryThis:'Next time you talk money with a friend, share your number first. Then ask for theirs.',skills:['communication','money']},review:{prompt:'You and a new roommate need a budget. Nobody wants to go first. What helps?',right:'Say your own number first, so sharing feels safe',wrong:'Ask what they can pay, then match your answer',why:'With friends, going first turns a test into an easy talk.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You and your cousin want to buy **a present** for your gran together. Nobody wants to say how much they have. It goes quiet. Who should **speak first**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Then you say, *I\u2019ve got \xa36 I can put in.* Your cousin smiles and says she\u2019s got \xa38. Sorted. Asking her straight out might have felt like a test. Going first made it feel like sharing.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When someone\u2019s **on your side**, say your own number first. It makes it easy for them to say theirs. With a shopkeeper, though, **let them go first**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two friends are about to **share a flat**. One asks the other what she can afford. The question lands like **a test**, and she goes vague. How could the first friend have opened instead?'},{kind:'story',text:'Try it the other way. The first friend says she can manage about \xa3700 a month, and she\u2019s nervous about her debts. Now nothing is being tested. Her flatmate relaxes and names her own figure straight back.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Volunteering a real figure turns **an interrogation** into **a disclosure**. And disclosure tends to be returned. Waiting for them to go first is a negotiating posture brought into a friendship. It reads as one, and they feel questioned, not joined.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'It flips standard advice, so it matters **which situation** you\u2019re in before you **speak first**.',left:{title:'Home and family',items:['Interests largely aligned','Your number is a disclosure','Go first']},right:{title:'Job or sale',items:['Interests opposed','The first number anchors','Let them go first']}},{kind:'moral',text:'With someone on your side, **say your figure** before you ask for theirs. It turns a test into sharing. Just know **which room** you\u2019re in first.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Standard negotiating advice says never name **the first number**. Let the other side anchor. It\u2019s sound advice across a desk. So why does it go so badly **at the kitchen table**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'In an **adversarial** talk, the first number anchors, so holding back makes sense. Between people whose interests **largely align**, the first number isn\u2019t a position. It\u2019s a disclosure that makes the whole exchange possible, and disclosure tends to be returned.'},{kind:'story',text:'A couple sit down to plan a budget. He waits, she waits, and each asks careful questions. It feels like an audit. Then she says what she earns and what she owes. Within minutes he\u2019s told her his.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now the same woman applies for a new job. The recruiter asks her current salary, and she volunteers it at once, the way she would at home. The offer lands right on that figure. She used the wrong template.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Interests are only largely aligned. Over an inheritance or a separation, a figure **can be used** against you. And disclosure only tends to be returned. Going first is a bet on goodwill, so make it where goodwill is real.'},{kind:'moral',text:'At home, **go first**, because your number is an offer, not a position. At work, the old rule still holds. People often get it backwards, so know **which situation** you\u2019re in.'}]},'money-conversations_5':{title:'Money Habits From Home',card:{insight:'We learn how to feel about money as kids, and it sticks with us.',tryThis:'Write down how your parents talked about money when you were small. Then ask your partner the same.',skills:['psychology','money']},review:{prompt:'Your partner will not spend savings, even on things you can easily pay for. What next?',right:'Ask what money was like in their home growing up',wrong:'Show them the numbers that prove you can pay for it',why:'Habits you learned as a kid do not change because of numbers.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You picked up **your accent** from the people around you. You never chose it. It sounds normal to you, and other accents **sound odd**. What else might you have picked up that way?'},{kind:'explain',text:'How you feel about money is mostly **learned at home**, when you\u2019re small. A child who grew up with little may find spending scary. A child who had plenty may feel calm. **Neither one** worked it out.'},{kind:'moral',text:'If talking about money feels awkward, the feeling may be **older than the chat**. It might not be about this friend at all. Ask where **their feelings** came from.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You and your partner earn about the same. A bonus arrives. One of you wants to **save every penny**. The other wants **a holiday at last**. Each view feels obvious to its owner. So who\u2019s right?'},{kind:'story',text:'Ask them where it comes from. The saver grew up in a house where money ran out before the month did. The spender grew up comfortable, where caution looked like worry. Neither reached their view by argument.'},{kind:'explain',text:'That\u2019s why these rows **won\u2019t settle**. Each of you argues as if the other chose their view. But you\u2019re arguing with **a formation**, and evidence doesn\u2019t reach it. Asking what money was like growing up can turn an odd position into one that makes sense.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Money attitudes are **mostly inherited**, absorbed before anyone could question them. So don\u2019t argue as if the other person chose theirs. Ask **where it came from**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture a child **on the stairs**. The bills are on the kitchen table, and the grown-ups talk behind **a shut door**. Nobody tells her money is private. Decades later, why does that view feel so obvious to her?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Attitudes to money are largely **inherited**, formed before they could be examined. That\u2019s why two people can hold **opposite views** while each finds theirs self-evident. Neither view was reasoned. Both were absorbed, so arguing as if it were a choice goes nowhere.'},{kind:'story',text:'Grown up, that child shares money with someone who talks about it openly. When he wants to go through the accounts, she goes quiet. He thinks she\u2019s hiding something. She isn\u2019t. She simply never learned that money talk is normal.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a different home, where money was only mentioned in anger. Someone raised there may flinch at any budget talk, however calm. Their partner reads it as **avoidance**. It\u2019s really an old alarm going off in a new room.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Inherited doesn\u2019t mean fixed, or **beyond question**. A childhood view can still be wrong. Its origin is where you start, not where you stop. And some money stories hurt. Nobody owes you theirs on demand.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When money talk feels awkward, the awkwardness may be **older than you both**. Ask what money was like growing up, then **answer it yourself**. That\u2019s where a real negotiation starts.'}]}}}},974,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'montaigne-essays',kind:'book',promise:'Be honest with yourself and fair to others',about:'What if being wrong did not feel like losing? Michel de Montaigne lived in France over 400 years ago. He wrote a book about himself, the good parts and the bad. Here are his best ideas. Want to try one?',ideas:{'montaigne-essays_1':{title:'The Level Scales',card:{insight:'Feeling sure is not the same as knowing, so check how you know.',tryThis:'Next time you feel sure in an argument, ask how you know.',skills:['thinking','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'Your friend says you have a fact wrong. What do you do first?',right:'Ask how you know before you defend your view',wrong:'Explain again why you are sure you are right',why:'Feeling sure is not proof, so check how you know first.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your friend says a whale isn\u2019t a fish. You\u2019re **completely sure** it is. You\u2019re ready to argue. But before you do, ask yourself one thing: **how do you know**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A whale breathes air and feeds its babies milk, so it isn\u2019t a fish. Feeling sure didn\u2019t make you right. A French writer called **Montaigne** had a medal made with **a pair of scales**. His motto was a question: what do I know?'},{kind:'moral',text:'Being sure isn\u2019t the same as **knowing**. Keep your mind like **level scales**, ready to tip. Then being wrong is easy: you just change your answer.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Someone questions **your plan** in a meeting. Before they finish the sentence, you\u2019re lining up reasons you\u2019re right. You haven\u2019t yet asked **whether they are**. Why does certainty arrive **so fast**?'},{kind:'story',text:'In 1576 Michel de Montaigne had a medal struck. It showed a pair of scales, hanging level. Beside them was his motto, in French: what do I know? It was a question, not a boast.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Montaigne held **a question**, not **a position**, and that changed what being wrong cost him.',left:{title:'A position',items:['Must be defended','Being wrong is defeat','Changing looks weak']},right:{title:'A question',items:['Can simply be revised','Being wrong is information','Changing is the point']}},{kind:'explain',text:'That\u2019s why he called his chapters **essays**. The French word means **an attempt**. He circles a subject, tries it from both sides, and leaves his contradictions in. So he could change his mind mid-page, and it reads as honesty.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Certainty is the state **most worth checking**. Before you defend a view, state **the strongest case** against it. Holding a question lets you revise at no cost.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A nobleman steps back from public office to read and write. He has **a medal** made, showing scales that hang perfectly level. Of all the mottoes he could choose, why pick one that **asserts nothing**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Montaigne saw **two settled ends**. Dogmatists claimed to know. Some sceptics claimed to know that nothing can be known, which is **a certainty too**. Both are positions you\u2019d have to defend. A question asserts nothing, so it can\u2019t trip over itself.'},{kind:'story',text:'In his long *Apology for Raymond Sebond*, he makes that point against the sceptics. And his Essays put the motto into practice. He tries a subject from one side, then another. When he contradicts himself, he leaves it in.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a scientist relying on a well-tested theory. She acts on it every day. But she keeps asking what new evidence would change her mind. When surprising data arrives, she isn\u2019t defeated. She revises.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Courts and hospitals can\u2019t run on open questions. At some point you act on **a best answer** and own it. Permanent doubt can become an elegant way to **dodge commitment**. Montaigne himself kept his religion and his duties.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The question governs **how you hold** a view, not whether you act on it. Keep **the scales level**, and evidence against you becomes news, not an attack.'}]},'montaigne-essays_2':{title:'Show the Flaws Too',card:{insight:'When you leave in the bad parts, people trust the rest of your story.',tryThis:'Before you send your next work update, add one thing that went wrong.',skills:['communication','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'One delay on your project was your fault. What do you put in your report?',right:'Put the delay in, so people trust the rest',wrong:'Leave it out, so the report shows only the wins',why:'When you show what went wrong, people believe what went right.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Imagine writing about yourself for people you\u2019ll **never meet**. Would you mention that you get carsick? That you\u2019re **scared of the dark**? Most people would quietly leave those bits out. Would you?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Over 400 years ago, a French writer called Montaigne wrote a book about himself. He put in his **painful kidney stones**, his sleep and his food. A gentleman wasn\u2019t meant to. But those awkward bits made readers **trust the rest**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'If someone only tells you **the good parts**, you wonder what\u2019s missing. Keep **the awkward bits** in. They\u2019re often where the real story is.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A project review lists **every milestone hit** and not a single problem. It looks polished. But you were there for the late nights and the near-misses. Do you **believe it**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Montaigne\u2019s subject was a human being, looked at closely. He had exactly one specimen with the inside visible: himself. So in went his kidney stones, his digestion, his poor memory and his short stature. No gentleman\u2019s book then held such things.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Leaving the awkward parts out doesn\u2019t make an account **cleaner**. It makes it **less believable**.',left:{title:'The polished account',items:['Only what went well','No doubt or discomfort','Hard to believe']},right:{title:'The honest account',items:['Includes what went badly','Names the discomfort','Easier to trust']}},{kind:'explain',text:'There\u2019s a quieter argument too. A philosophy written as if its author **had no body** describes someone who doesn\u2019t exist. It fails exactly when **the body speaks up**: in pain, fear or illness.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The part you\u2019d rather cut is usually **where the difficulty** lived. Leave it in, and **your reader** trusts everything else you write.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'In the 1570s, serious books were about God, history or doctrine. Then a French nobleman wrote a note to his reader. **His subject**, he said, was himself. Why risk looking **so vain**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'For Montaigne the frankness was **structural**, not a confession for colour. His subject was a person examined closely. Cut out sleep, food, illness and sex, and you lose **most of the evidence**. That\u2019s what the person does all day.'},{kind:'story',text:'In his forties he began to suffer from kidney stones. They troubled him for the rest of his life. He described the attacks in detail, and what living with that pain did to his thinking about death.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a team\u2019s post-mortem. One version lists the wins. Another notes that your own estimate was badly wrong in March. Only the second can teach anyone **what went wrong**, and only the second gets believed.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Candour can become **its own performance**: the unflattering detail chosen because it charms. Pascal, writing within a century, judged the whole project vain. And some things are private **for good reason**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The standard is **relevance**, not confession. Include what your account **depends on**, even when it\u2019s unflattering. Leave out what you\u2019d only add for effect.'}]},'montaigne-essays_3':{title:'Different Places, Different You',card:{insight:'You act one way here and another way there, so change the place.',tryThis:'Today, write down where you slip up most, and pick a new place.',skills:['psychology','thinking']},review:{prompt:'You are patient at work but snap at your family. What helps most?',right:'Look at what is different about each place, then change it',wrong:'Decide that the snappy one is the real you',why:'You are not just one or the other, and you can change the place.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'On Tuesday you stood up for a friend. On Thursday you **stayed quiet** while someone got teased. Same week, same you. So which one is **the real you**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A French writer called Montaigne watched himself very closely. He saw he could be **bold one day** and timid the next. Both were true. You can be loud at home and shy in class. It\u2019s **the same you**, in a different room.'},{kind:'moral',text:'People change from room to room **more than they admit**. That isn\u2019t fake. So notice **when you\u2019re brave**, and try to set up that room again.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'On Monday a colleague shares credit **generously** with the whole team. On Wednesday, in a budget meeting, they guard **every line** fiercely. You\u2019re left wondering: which one is **the real them**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Montaigne found nothing harder than making human actions hang together. They contradict each other so oddly they seem to come from different people. He didn\u2019t call it hypocrisy. He called it normal, and counted himself in.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Some modern personality research **points the same way**. A situation can explain more of behaviour than we tend to assume. People describe themselves consistently, but act less so. Your colleague didn\u2019t reveal a true nature. They met **two situations**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'That matters for change. Most self-improvement assumes **one stable self** being improved. Montaigne\u2019s picture is closer to **a committee**. So the useful question isn\u2019t what you\u2019re like. It\u2019s which member turns up in which room.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You\u2019re less a single character than **a committee**. So stop asking who you really are. Compare **the situations** and change the room you\u2019re in.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Someone overspends every time they shop with **one friend**, and never alone. They resolve, again and again, to be **stronger**. It never works. What if willpower was never the thing that needed changing?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Montaigne\u2019s essay on **the inconsistency** of our actions starts with a complaint. Nothing is harder, he says, than making our actions hang together. He doubts there\u2019s **a fixed self** underneath waiting to be found. He doesn\u2019t exempt himself.'},{kind:'story',text:'A person is brave one day and cowardly another. Montaigne\u2019s point is that neither is the real one. Both are the same person, meeting different moments. The brave one isn\u2019t a mask, and neither is the coward.'},{kind:'story',text:'Back to the shopper. The friend makes spending feel normal. So the lever isn\u2019t a firmer resolve. It\u2019s a different outing: a walk instead of the shops. Change the room, and **a different member** of the committee turns up.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Pushed too far, *it was the situation* becomes an excuse for anything. And traits that look weak in one moment show up across many. The anxious person is anxious **more often**. **Tendencies are real** too.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You\u2019re more like a committee than **a fixed character**. That moves the lever from willpower to **situations**. It doesn\u2019t remove you: you\u2019re still the one who pulls it.'}]},'montaigne-essays_4':{title:'Food Swallowed Whole',card:{insight:'An idea is yours only when you can say it in your own words.',tryThis:'When you read a good idea, put it in three lines of your own.',skills:['thinking','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'You read a great argument, and a friend pushes back. What shows you get it?',right:'You can argue with it and say it in your own words',wrong:'You can say it again, word for word, only louder',why:'Saying it twice does not mean you have worked it through.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your friend tells you the moon **makes the tides**. You say it in class, proud. Then your teacher asks you how. You open your mouth, and **nothing comes out**. What went wrong?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A French writer called Montaigne said ideas are like **food**. Food only feeds you once you **chew it** and take it in. You had the words about the moon, but you hadn\u2019t chewed them. Saying them again wouldn\u2019t help.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Knowing what someone clever said isn\u2019t **the same as thinking** it through. An idea is yours when you can explain it **in your own words**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve just read **an excellent argument**, and you can\u2019t wait to repeat it at dinner. Someone pushes back. You find you can only say it again, **a little louder**. Sound familiar?'},{kind:'story',text:'Montaigne was scathing about people who could recite the authorities but couldn\u2019t say what they themselves thought. They collected opinions like furniture. And they mistook **owning them** for understanding them.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Why doesn\u2019t repeating help? Each time you say a borrowed line, it **comes to mind faster**. Fast feels like sure. But it\u2019s only easier to reach for. **Availability** is easily mistaken for conviction, even by you.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Montaigne\u2019s test is whether the view has been **digested**, or is only **being stored**.',left:{title:'Stored',items:['Can be recited','Collapses when challenged','Fits only the original case']},right:{title:'Held',items:['You can argue against it','You can apply it to a new case']}},{kind:'moral',text:'A view you can only repeat isn\u2019t **yours yet**. Before you quote it, write **three sentences** of your own, and try to argue against it.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A manager quotes **the same business book** in every meeting. He knows its lines by heart. But ask him when its advice would fail, and he can\u2019t say. Does he **know the book**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Montaigne\u2019s complaint was that we can say what **Cicero** said about a thing, and not what **we ourselves** think. Memory of other people\u2019s judgements isn\u2019t a judgement of your own. It\u2019s only **stored**.'},{kind:'story',text:'In his essays on pedantry and on educating children, he uses two images. Undigested learning is food sitting unchanged in the stomach. Good learning is like bees, who visit many flowers but make honey that\u2019s entirely their own.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a student who reads a strong essay on free trade. One version of her quotes it. Another argues with it, finds a case it misses, and writes her own paragraph. Only the second has **made honey**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Nobody can digest everything. Most of what you know is taken **on trust** from better-placed people. Refusing to rely on experts until you\u2019ve checked it all is **its own mistake**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The test is for **opinions you voice** as your own, not every fact you rely on. If you can\u2019t argue with a view, extend it or apply it, **it isn\u2019t yours** yet.'}]},'montaigne-essays_5':{title:'The Fall From the Horse',card:{insight:'Talking to someone who has faced your fear helps more than facts.',tryThis:'Pick one fear. Ask someone who has faced it what it was like.',skills:['psychology','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'You are scared of a surgery you have never had. What helps most?',right:'Ask someone who has been through it what it was like',wrong:'Read all the facts until the fear goes away',why:'Hearing from someone who has been through it helps more than facts do.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'For years, a French writer called Montaigne was **very scared** of dying. Then one day he was thrown hard **from his horse**. He was knocked out and carried home. How do you think it felt?'},{kind:'story',text:'He thought he was dying. Yet afterwards he said it had felt gentle, almost like falling asleep. He\u2019d read wise books about not being afraid. They helped a bit. The fall helped more.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Fear of something you\u2019ve never been through is often **bigger than the thing**. So ask someone who\u2019s done it **what it\u2019s really like**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Montaigne argued that to philosophise is to **learn to die**. He was candid that his own fear was severe. Then a riding accident nearly killed him. Which do you think **helped more**: the argument or the fall?'},{kind:'story',text:'He was out riding near home when a servant on a powerful horse crashed into him at full gallop. He was thrown, knocked out and carried home, believing he was dying. Later he reported it had been gentle.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'He kept **both** in the book, side by side, and **the second** did more.',left:{title:'The argument',items:['We die a little daily','Afterwards is like before birth','Before birth troubled nobody']},right:{title:'The experience',items:['Thrown from his horse','Sure he was dying','Found it gentle']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s why the honesty is useful. A doctrine that says the fear **shouldn\u2019t exist** leaves you with the fear plus a sense of failure. Montaigne allows the fear, shows what eased it, and **admits** it wasn\u2019t solved.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Arguments help with fear, but **contact** often helps more. For something you dread and haven\u2019t experienced, ask someone **who\u2019s been through it** what it was actually like.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Someone afraid of flying has read **every safety statistic**. She can recite the numbers perfectly. She\u2019s still afraid. If the facts are on her side, why hasn\u2019t **the fear** moved?'},{kind:'explain',text:'One of Montaigne\u2019s early essays takes up an idea he credits to Cicero. To philosophise is **to learn to die**. His argument makes death **ordinary**, not noble. We die a little every day. Being dead is like not yet being born, which troubled nobody.'},{kind:'story',text:'In another essay he describes the fall. A servant rode into him at speed. Carried home unconscious, he recalled no terror, only a soft drifting, like sliding into sleep. It did more for his fear than the reasoning had.'},{kind:'story',text:'Back to the nervous flyer. The statistics are true, but they\u2019re abstract. What changes her is a short flight with a friend beside her. Then another. Like Montaigne, she needed **contact**, not more proof.'},{kind:'explain',text:'He tended to **add to** his essays rather than rewrite them. So the anxious reasoning and the calmer experience stand **side by side**. He didn\u2019t cut the first once the second did more. You can see him change.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. One near-death experience is **a single case**, and not everyone\u2019s is gentle. Making death ordinary helps with fearing being dead. It helps much less with grief, or with **fear of painful dying**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Fear often answers to **contact**, not proof. Montaigne offers **relief**, not a cure, and says so. Keep your arguments, but go and find out what the real thing is like.'}]},'montaigne-essays_6':{title:'Beyond Keeping Score',card:{insight:'Counting calls tells you about the count, but not about the friend.',tryThis:'Pick a friend you miss, and write what you still like about them.',skills:['relationships','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'A friend has not called much lately. How do you decide what to do?',right:'Stop counting, and write what you still like about them',wrong:'Count who called last and who showed up more',why:'A count tells you about calls, not about the friend.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Someone asks why your best friend is **your best friend**. You say you like the same games. But lots of kids like the same games. So you try again. Why don\u2019t the reasons **ever add up**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Over 400 years ago, Montaigne had a closest friend called \xc9tienne de La Bo\xe9tie. They were friends for only a few years before \xc9tienne died young. Montaigne tried to explain the friendship and gave up. It was just who they both were.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You can count who texts first, but that\u2019s **an answer about counting**. Stop keeping score, and ask what you **still like** about your friend.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A friendship has drifted. You start **a mental ledger**: who called last, who cancelled, who showed up for the birthday. The numbers give you **a clear verdict**. But is it a verdict about **your friend**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Montaigne and \xc9tienne de La Bo\xe9tie, a fellow magistrate in Bordeaux, were close for about four years. Then La Bo\xe9tie died in 1563. In his essay on friendship, Montaigne lists their shared reading, temperament and timing. Then he abandons the list.'},{kind:'explain',text:'He isn\u2019t being poetic. He\u2019s reporting that the reasons were true but **didn\u2019t add up** to the friendship. He refuses to pretend otherwise. His short answer simply points at **the two of them**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Your ledger gives **an answer**, but it\u2019s an answer about **the ledger**.',left:{title:'The ledger',items:['Who calls more','Who showed up','A tidy verdict']},right:{title:'What remains',items:['What\u2019s left when you stop counting','It may have no name']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Reasons can be arranged to **support any verdict** you already want. So stop counting. Ask what **remains between you** when you refuse to score, and decide on that.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Try to say why **your oldest friend** is your oldest friend. You\u2019re not allowed to mention where you met or what you did together. What\u2019s left, once **the circumstances** are gone?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Classical writers, Aristotle and Cicero above all, analysed friendship by **its grounds**: usefulness, pleasure, shared virtue. Montaigne knew that tradition well. His point is that even the highest ground didn\u2019t account for this friendship. **The analysis ran out** first.'},{kind:'story',text:'His essay spends a page explaining why he and La Bo\xe9tie were so close. Long after La Bo\xe9tie\u2019s death in 1563, Montaigne wrote a short answer in **the margin** of his own copy. It pointed only at the two of them.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take two old university friends who rarely speak. One keeps score of the missed calls. The other notices that, whenever they do meet, the old ease is **instantly back**. Only the second has looked past the ledger.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Bonds you can\u2019t explain **can still harm** you. The sense that something is beyond words keeps people in hurtful friendships. Sometimes a plain record of who keeps hurting whom is **exactly what\u2019s needed**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Some bonds **outrun their reasons**. The test tells you what remains when you stop counting. It doesn\u2019t tell you that **what remains is good**.'}]},'montaigne-essays_7':{title:'The Home Test',card:{insight:'When a custom far away seems awful, try the same words on home.',tryThis:'When a food or custom from far away seems gross, name one from home.',skills:['thinking','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'A friend calls a custom from another country cruel. What do you ask first?',right:'Would the same words fit the closest thing at home?',wrong:'Does that custom break the rules of our country?',why:'Using the same words at home shows if you meant cruel or just strange.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A new kid at school brings **a lunch** you\u2019ve never seen. Some kids pull faces and say it\u2019s gross. Is it **really gross**, or just new to them?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Montaigne heard about people in Brazil who **ate enemies** killed in war. His readers in France were horrified. He agreed it was wrong. Then he pointed at France, where people were **tortured alive** over religion. Which was worse?'},{kind:'moral',text:'When something far away seems awful, find **the nearest thing** at home. Would you use the same words? Often the bad feeling is about **strange**, not cruel.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You read about a country where thieves are **publicly flogged**. Barbaric, you think. Now try the same word on a prison at home that keeps people **alone in a cell** for years. Does it come as easily?'},{kind:'story',text:'Montaigne\u2019s essay on the cannibals of Brazil describes a practice that horrified his readers: eating enemies killed in war. Then he lists what France was doing at that very time. Torture and slaughter, in **the wars of religion**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'He isn\u2019t defending cannibalism. He thinks some things really are **worse than others**. His question is whether the disgust was tracking **cruelty** or only unfamiliarity. Those two can point in different directions.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Run your sentence about **the foreign custom** against **the nearest thing** at home.',left:{title:'If it survives',items:['It was measuring harm','Your judgement stands']},right:{title:'If it doesn\u2019t',items:['It was marking strangeness','The word just meant theirs']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Disgust is a badly set instrument when it\u2019s tuned to **strangeness**. Before you call a practice barbaric, aim the same words at **home**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Montaigne had a man in his household who\u2019d lived for years in **the French colony** in Brazil. He also says he once spoke with Brazilians brought to France. What did he **notice** that his readers missed?'},{kind:'story',text:'His essay on cannibals describes their customs with curiosity, including eating enemies killed in war. He grants **the horror** of eating the dead. Then he turns the description round on France.'},{kind:'explain',text:'He judges eating the dead **less barbarous** than what France did to the living. Torture, and burning by degrees, under **a pretext of piety**. The word barbarian, he suggests, tracks **unfamiliarity** more reliably than cruelty.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a friend who calls eating dogs cruel and disgusting, while happily eating pork. The home test doesn\u2019t tell her she\u2019s wrong. It asks her to aim **the same sentence** at the pork. It may survive. It may not.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The test can slide into **the relativism** he rejected, or into pointing at home to silence criticism abroad. And his portrait of the Brazilians is idealised. It says as much about **his complaints** against France.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your instrument may be set to **detect strangeness**, not harm. Aim your words at home first. Then you can judge abroad **with a true scale**.'}]},'montaigne-essays_8':{title:'The Back Room',card:{insight:'Keep one part of your life just for you, that no one else sees.',tryThis:'Pick one thing you enjoy, and keep it just for you this month.',skills:['psychology','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'Which of your hobbies counts as a back room in Montaigne\u2019s sense?',right:'The one you would keep doing if nobody ever knew',wrong:'The one you are best at and show your friends',why:'Once you show a thing to others, you start to shape it for them.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture a busy shop, full of customers and noise. Now look behind the counter. There\u2019s **a small door**. Behind it is a quiet room with **one chair** and a lamp. Who is that room for?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Montaigne, a French writer, was once mayor of a big city. He said everyone needs **a back shop**: a part of life nobody else sees. If you draw just for you, that\u2019s yours. If you **count the likes**, it\u2019s not.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Once you show a thing, you start shaping it **for others**. Keep one thing **just for you**. It\u2019s still there if you ever lose your team or your part.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Suppose every account you have **went dark** tomorrow, and nobody could see what you do. What part of your life would **carry on** exactly as before?'},{kind:'story',text:'Montaigne borrowed an image from trade: the back shop. It\u2019s the room behind the shop floor, not for sale and not on display. He said everyone needs one, held apart from role, reputation and audience.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s **the test**: would it survive **nobody knowing** about it? A hobby you post about is at the front of the house. So is an interest you mention because it makes you **sound interesting**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Why would **a busy man** need one? Montaigne served two terms as **mayor of Bordeaux**. His worry was that a person made only of roles has **nowhere to stand** when a role is taken away. That happens without warning.'},{kind:'moral',text:'This isn\u2019t an argument against **sharing**. Once a thing is shown, the showing **starts to shape** it. So keep one room you never show anyone.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A surgeon retires after thirty years. Within a month, she realises she has **no idea** who she is without the job. Her whole self was **the front counter**. What would Montaigne say went wrong?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The image comes in his essay **on solitude**. Keep family, possessions and health if you can, he says. But don\u2019t tie yourself so tightly that **losing them** takes you with them. The back shop is that reserve.'},{kind:'story',text:'Montaigne didn\u2019t stay in his tower. He was elected mayor of Bordeaux while travelling abroad, and served two terms. They ran through civil war and, at the very end, an outbreak of plague. The champion of the back shop worked the counter for years.'},{kind:'story',text:'Back to the surgeon. Suppose she\u2019d kept one thing apart, say birdwatching she never mentioned at work. When the job ended, that room would still be standing. She\u2019d have had **somewhere to stand** while she rebuilt the rest.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The back shop can harden into **withdrawal**: duties neglected, or nobody close let in. His advice assumes a household and an office to go back to. And a room of your own takes time and **freedom** not everyone has.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The back shop is **a retreat** kept inside a life, not a replacement for one. Keep it, so **you have somewhere** to stand when a role is taken away.'}]},'montaigne-essays_9':{title:'The Friend Who Argues',card:{insight:'When your friends all agree with your plan, you stop looking at it.',tryThis:'Before your next big choice, ask one friend to argue against your plan.',skills:['thinking','communication']},review:{prompt:'You want honest feedback on a plan before a big choice. What do you ask?',right:'Ask someone to argue against it',wrong:'Send it around and ask if anyone has thoughts',why:'When you ask people to argue, they feel free to say what is wrong.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You show your friends your plan for **a den**. Everyone says it looks great. You build it just as drawn. On the first windy day, **the roof blows off**. What went wrong?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Montaigne, a French writer, said he was **glad** when people argued with him. His complaint was that so few did it well. Most just nodded. Agreeing friends teach you little. Friends who **push back** make you think again.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Asking if it\u2019s good mostly gets you nods. Ask a friend to find **what\u2019s wrong** instead. That\u2019s when you hear **the useful stuff**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'How many people this year have told you, **to your face**, that you were wrong about something that mattered? If the answer is **almost nobody**, is that because you\u2019re always right?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Montaigne welcomed being contradicted. Agreement, he said, taught him nothing. Contradiction **roused his attention**. The point is about attention, not humility. Agreement lets you keep the position you arrived with, **unexamined**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The way you ask decides whether you get **real objections** or **polite edits**.',left:{title:'Asking for comments',items:['Any thoughts?','Small edits to the draft','The plan goes untested']},right:{title:'Asking for opposition',items:['Make the best case against','The real objections surface','The plan is tested']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Why does the second work? Calling the plan **a draft** you expect to abandon removes **the cost of disagreeing**. That cost is what was suppressing the objections. If nobody\u2019s told you you\u2019re wrong lately, suspect cost, not correctness.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Agreement lets your plan **sleep**. So don\u2019t ask for comments. Ask someone to make **the strongest case** against it, and look again.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A team reviews a strategy document. Everyone suggests **small tweaks**. Nobody questions the core idea. It ships, and fails for **the obvious reason** nobody raised. Was the review honest, or just **a ritual**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'In his essay on **the art of conversation**, Montaigne says contradiction rouses and exercises him. Agreement lets him settle. So his reason for welcoming it is **attention**, not a modest manner. A challenge forces you to look again.'},{kind:'story',text:'His complaint isn\u2019t that people disagree too much. It\u2019s that they do it badly. They soften, they defer, or they argue to win rather than to find out. He wanted partners who would press hard and fairly, and found few.'},{kind:'story',text:'Back to the team. Next time, the lead tells one colleague the plan is a draft she expects to abandon. She asks him for the strongest case against it. In ten minutes he names the flaw everyone had **politely ignored**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. A devil\u2019s advocate arguing by rote produces **theatre**, not truth. Constant challenge can drown quiet people and reward the loudest. And an objection is a reason to look again, **not proof** you were wrong.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Agreement leaves **your position** unexamined. Real contradiction is rare because disagreeing is costly. So ask for it in a way that **removes the cost**, then look again.'}]}}}},975,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'my-life-and-work',kind:'book',promise:'Find the hidden costs in how you work',about:'Where does the time in your work day go? Henry Ford made cars about 100 years ago. In 1922, he wrote a book about how he ran his car plants. Here are his best ideas and his blind spots. Want to look?',ideas:{'my-life-and-work_1':{title:'The Walking Nobody Counted',card:{insight:'In a job you repeat, most lost time goes to walking and fetching.',tryThis:'Time one chore today. Write how long you spent doing it and how long you spent walking.',skills:['productivity','leadership']},review:{prompt:'Clinic nurses are busy all day but see few patients. What do you check?',right:'How much of each shift goes to walking for supplies',wrong:'Whether each nurse could work faster with each patient',why:'Busy is not the same as fast, so time the walking first.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re packing **thirty party bags**. The sweets, balloons and stickers sit on three tables around the room. You\u2019re busy the whole time. So where is most of your time **really going**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Dropping a sweet in a bag takes a second. Walking to the next table takes ten. Over 100 years ago, **Henry Ford** saw this in his car factory. So he kept each worker still and **moved the car** past them instead.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The walking feels like work, so **nobody counts it**. But it\u2019s often where **most of the time** goes. Bring the job to you, not you to the job.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A warehouse picker fills orders **all shift**. Each order means a walk to the far shelves and back. She\u2019s busy every minute, and nobody calls her slow. Time her, though. Where does **the shift** actually go?'},{kind:'story',text:'At Highland Park in 1913, Ford\u2019s workers walked all day to fetch parts. So he built a **moving line**. The chassis travelled past, and each worker stayed at one station. Assembly of a Model T fell from over twelve hours to about **ninety minutes**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Why did nobody count the walking? A person fetching a part **looks busy**. Managers watched the doing: how fast a bolt went in. The walking sat between tasks, so it showed up in **no one\u2019s figures**. You\u2019ve probably got some too.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Split any shift into **the doing** and **the moving between**, and see where the time goes.',left:{title:'The doing',items:['Fitting the wheel','Packing the order','Easy to see']},right:{title:'The moving between',items:['Walking to fetch the part','Waiting between jobs','Hidden because it looks busy']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Busy isn\u2019t the same as productive. Before you try to **work faster**, measure the walking, fetching and waiting. That\u2019s usually **where the time** hides.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Ask most people what Ford invented and they\u2019ll say **splitting work** into small tasks. But that idea was old. Adam Smith described it in a pin factory in 1776. So what did Ford **actually change**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'His insight was narrower. **Bring the work** to the worker, not the worker to the work. A man walking to fetch a part isn\u2019t assembling anything. Move **the object** past people who stay put, and the gaps between operations collapse.'},{kind:'story',text:'Ford credits part of the idea to Chicago meat-packers. Their overhead trolleys carried carcasses past butchers who stayed in place. At Highland Park in 1913, he made the chassis travel instead. A Model T\u2019s assembly fell from over twelve hours to about ninety minutes.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a software team that ships slowly. Each feature passes through four people and waits days in each queue. The coding takes hours. The waiting takes weeks, and it appears in nobody\u2019s estimate. That queue is their walking.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Ford presents the gain and rarely the cost. Removing the walking also shrank a job to **one repeated motion**, at a pace the line set. Both are true, and the book never weighs them as a trade.'},{kind:'moral',text:'In any job you repeat, measure **the moving between**, not just the doing. That\u2019s usually where your time goes. Just count **the narrower job** as a cost too.'}]},'my-life-and-work_2':{title:'The Five Dollar Day',card:{insight:'Paying more can cost less if it keeps people from quitting.',tryThis:'Add up what it costs to hire and train one new worker. Then compare it with a raise.',skills:['leadership','money']},review:{prompt:'People keep quitting, so the boss plans a party. What should she check first?',right:'What each person who quits costs, compared with a raise',wrong:'Which fun party would cheer everyone up the most',why:'Each new hire costs a lot to train, so more pay can cost less.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'In 1914, Henry Ford roughly **doubled the pay** of his car workers. It went to five dollars a day. People were shocked. Was he just being kind, or was it **something else**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Imagine you run a car wash and pay your helpers very little. A new one quits every week, so you spend each morning **teaching again**. Ford\u2019s factory had that problem, only far bigger. Paying more made people **stay**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Ford said plainly it **wasn\u2019t charity**. Teaching new people over and over costs a lot. Sometimes paying more is **the cheaper choice**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A caf\xe9 pays the minimum and loses a barista **most months**. Each new one takes weeks to reach full speed. The manager blames morale and plans a team night out. Is morale really **the problem**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Ford faced the same problem in 1914, far bigger. The company was hiring several times its whole workforce in a year. Every newcomer had to learn the line\u2019s pace. So Ford roughly doubled pay to five dollars a day, and turnover collapsed.'},{kind:'explain',text:'His book says plainly it **wasn\u2019t charity**. He counts it among the company\u2019s best cost-cutting moves. Why? Turnover hides across the floor: recruiting, training, slow first weeks, **spoiled work**. Each looks small. Together they cost more than the pay rise.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'You can read the same wage as **a gift** or as **a fix**, and only one transfers.',left:{title:'Read as kindness',items:['A gift to workers','A cost the firm absorbs']},right:{title:'Read as operations',items:['A cure for turnover','A sum you can run']}},{kind:'moral',text:'When people keep leaving, **do the sum** before you buy a fix for a feeling. Price one departure. Then compare it with **higher pay**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'In January 1914, soon after the moving line came in, Ford announced **five dollars a day**. Most people remember it as generosity. Ford\u2019s own book calls it something else: **cost-cutting**. Which reading would you trust?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The problem was **labour turnover**: the rate at which people leave and must be replaced. Ford was hiring several times his workforce each year. Every newcomer had to be trained to the line\u2019s pace, then often **quit anyway**.'},{kind:'story',text:'So he roughly doubled the wage, and turnover collapsed. The book gives its own hiring figures to show the rise paid for itself in less retraining. He says plainly it wasn\u2019t charity.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a warehouse losing staff every few weeks. The manager proposes team-building days. Costed properly, each departure means recruiting, training and weeks at half speed. A pay rise might be cheaper than the next away day.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Much of the five dollars was **a profit share**. Qualifying depended on conduct the company checked, even through home visits. The work stayed just as repetitive. And Ford\u2019s volumes made it affordable. A thinner firm could sink.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Treat the five dollar day as **a sum to run**, not a promise. It worked on Ford\u2019s numbers. **Yours** may come out differently, so do the arithmetic first.'}]},'my-life-and-work_3':{title:'Pick One Way, Then Improve',card:{insight:'If you never do a job the same way, you can\u2019t tell what helped.',tryThis:'Pick one job you repeat. Write down each step, so you can test one change at a time.',skills:['productivity','thinking']},review:{prompt:'Three people pack boxes three ways. This month, mistakes drop. What did you learn?',right:'Nothing you can repeat, since each person packs their own way',wrong:'That the fastest packer\u2019s way must be the best one',why:'You can only judge a change if everyone starts from one set way.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Three friends bake biscuits for a sale, each **their own way**. One batch comes out brilliant. Nobody knows why. Was it the oven, the butter or the timing? How could you **find out**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'It\u2019s like **a fair test** in science. If you change three things at once, you can\u2019t tell which one mattered. Henry Ford ran his factory this way. He fixed **one way** to do each job, then improved it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Pick **one way** to do a job and stick to it. Then change **one thing** at a time. That\u2019s how you see what made it better.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Three people pack orders, each **their own way**, and all of them are good. This month, errors fall. Everyone\u2019s pleased. But whose method **made the difference**, and can you repeat it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'There\u2019s **no way to know**. Three methods ran at once, with no fixed starting point. Each result had many possible causes. A standard holds everything still except **the one thing** you change. Then the result tells you something.'},{kind:'story',text:'Ford fixed one way to do each job in his factory. He\u2019s often read as freezing methods forever. He wasn\u2019t. He called the standard a starting point. His objection was to changing a method before anyone understood it.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Compare a team with **no standard** to one with **a written way**.',left:{title:'No standard',items:['Everyone\u2019s own way','Results vary, nobody knows why','Every change is a guess']},right:{title:'A standard',items:['One written way','Variation shows up at once','Every change can be tested']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Fix one way first, and run it long enough **to learn from**. Then improve it. Without **a baseline**, change is just churn.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Ford\u2019s hostility to variety was extreme: **one model**, one chassis, as few options as he could manage. It sounds like a man who hated change. So why was he studied so closely **in Japan**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Under the rigidity sits **a method**. Fix a standard, run it long enough to learn from, then improve it. The reason is that **variation blocks learning**. If you do a job differently each time, you can\u2019t tell which difference mattered.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture three packers working their own ways. Errors fall one month, and the team celebrates. Then errors rise again, and nobody knows why. Nothing was held still, so neither month taught them anything they could repeat.'},{kind:'story',text:'In Japan a few decades later, Ford\u2019s approach was studied closely. There the second half of the formula, improve the standard, was taken more seriously. At Ford, the standard tended to harden instead.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. A standard can become the goal instead of the start, as Ford\u2019s **frozen Model T** showed. Standards imposed without the workers miss what they know. And in research or design, variation is often the point.'},{kind:'moral',text:'In anything you repeat, **fix the standard** first and improve against it. A standard is where your learning starts. It was never meant to be **the finish line**.'}]},'my-life-and-work_4':{title:'Count What You Throw Out',card:{insight:'Waste is more than trash, and someone always pays for it.',tryThis:'Pick one thing you throw out each week. Add up what it costs before you shrug it off.',skills:['productivity','money']},review:{prompt:'Your team throws out scraps each day. Sorting them is a pain. What do you do?',right:'Count what the scraps cost each week before you throw them out',wrong:'Let it go, since some scrap is a normal part of work',why:'Someone always pays for waste, so count it before you shrug it off.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'After a craft lesson, the floor is covered in **paper scraps**. Most go straight in the bin. But some are big enough to use again. How much are you **throwing away** without noticing?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Henry Ford hated **waste** of every kind. His factory took metal back out of scrap. It saved good wood from the crates parts arrived in. He even timed **every movement**, because wasted steps were waste too.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Waste isn\u2019t just rubbish. It\u2019s **lost time**, lost steps and **lost stuff**, and someone always pays for it. Check before you throw something away.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A workshop throws out a skip of offcuts **every week**. Sorting them seems fiddly, and the boss says scrap is just part of the job. Is it? Or is somebody **paying for it**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Ford went after waste everywhere. He recovered metal from scrap and redesigned parts to use less steel. He saved usable wood from shipping crates. He timed every movement on the line, decades before waste was an environmental cause.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Why so fierce? He saw waste as **theft from the customer**. Every scrap and wasted minute ends up in the price. So you pay for a factory you never saw. That turns salvage from thrift into **something like duty**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Ford defined waste **far more broadly** than the bin, and counted **four kinds**.',left:{title:'Material',items:['Metal in the scrap','Wood in old crates','Extra steel in a part']},right:{title:'Motion, time, skill',items:['Needless steps on the line','Wasted minutes','A skilled man on unskilled work']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Treat waste as **a failing**, not a fact of life. Count material, motion, time and skill. Then measure what you throw away before you decide **it\u2019s not worth** saving.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Ford counted everything: scrap metal, spare steel, **wood from crates**, every second on the line. Even skill spent on simple work counted as waste. So what did a man so careful **leave out**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'His categories, material, motion and time, are the roots of **lean manufacturing**. His reason was economic: **the customer pays** for every scrap in the price. The environmental framing came decades later. But you\u2019d recognise the practice he was already running.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a print shop under that rule. It trims margins to save paper and reuses packing boxes. It cuts the steps between press and guillotine. Each saving looks tiny. Across a year, they add up to a lower price.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now look at the man on Ford\u2019s line. He repeats the same motion, at the same pace, for forty years. Ford calls a skilled man on unskilled work a waste. This worker\u2019s **unused capability** never enters the ledger.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Any waste drive is limited by **what it counts**. Ford counted steel and skipped people once they were unskilled. What a ledger leaves out is usually paid for by someone with no say.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Count waste broadly: **material, motion, time** and skill. Then ask who your ledger leaves out. The waste you don\u2019t count still costs **somebody**.'}]},'my-life-and-work_5':{title:'What He Could Not See',card:{insight:'If you plan a job but never do it, you miss how hard it is.',tryThis:'Pick one task you give to others. Do it yourself for a day, and see how it feels.',skills:['leadership','thinking']},review:{prompt:'Your new phone script cuts call time in half. What do you check next?',right:'Whether you would want to use it every day, like your staff',wrong:'Whether you could cut the call time in half again',why:'The numbers can look good while the job gets worse for your staff.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A grown-up designs a new **school lunch queue**. On paper it\u2019s faster. But the grown-up never stands in it. The children wait in the rain at the end. Who knows **the real cost**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Henry Ford built a **moving line** for making cars. He said it saved workers from heavy lifting. But each worker did **one movement** all day, at the line\u2019s speed. Ford never did that job, so he didn\u2019t feel how dull it was.'},{kind:'moral',text:'If you plan a job for someone else, **picture doing it** yourself all day. Would **you** want to? If not, there\u2019s a cost your plan can\u2019t see.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A manager writes a call script that **halves handling time**. On the dashboard, it\u2019s a triumph. Within months, the best staff have gone. Nobody who wrote the script has ever **taken a call** with it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Ford\u2019s book is written with total confidence. It praises the line for freeing men from heavy labour. It gives the turnover figures that came before the five dollar day. It never connects that turnover to the line itself.'},{kind:'explain',text:'From above, a line is a set of **times and flows**. Tedium doesn\u2019t show up in them. It shows up in the person at one station, and in **how fast** people leave. Your numbers can improve while the job gets worse.'},{kind:'moral',text:'People who design a system rarely **work inside it**. So before you finalise a process, ask who will run it daily. Then ask **whether you would**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Ford\u2019s book is sure of itself on every page. The line frees men. High wages are sound. **The Model T** is the right car. Soon, part of that certainty would **turn against** him. Which part?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The blind spots are **structural**. Ford calls one motion, paced by a machine, work most men don\u2019t mind. He gives the turnover figures but never links them to the line. He designed **a system** he never had to work inside.'},{kind:'story',text:'The book also gives no hint that the Model T\u2019s twenty-year run was ending. Ford\u2019s greatest strength was refusing to change a working design. Soon that strength would cost him the market.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a rota planner who has never worked a night shift. On her spreadsheet, the new pattern covers every hour neatly. The nurses living it lose sleep for weeks. Her figures look better while their jobs get worse.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Hindsight makes this easy. Many people took the line **for the wage**, and its cheap cars reached buyers who\u2019d never owned one. Ford wasn\u2019t wrong to build it. His confidence just wasn\u2019t evidence about the work.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A designer\u2019s certainty tells you **nothing about the work**. Before you finalise any system, picture yourself inside it **every day**. If you wouldn\u2019t take that job, find out what it costs.'}]},'my-life-and-work_6':{title:'The Black Paint Rule',card:{insight:'A rule can stay long after the reason for it is gone.',tryThis:'Write down one rule you follow at work. Then write why it started, and if that is still true.',skills:['thinking','leadership']},review:{prompt:'Your team prints every report because an old computer once crashed. What do you ask?',right:'If the reason for the rule is still true',wrong:'How to make the printing faster and cheaper',why:'If the reason for a rule is gone, the rule can go too.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Henry Ford\u2019s famous car, the Model T, came in **one colour** only: black. Many people thought he just didn\u2019t care what buyers wanted. Was he being stubborn, or was there **a reason**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The usual story is about **drying paint**. A car couldn\u2019t move on until its paint was dry. Black paint dried faster than any other kind he had. So one colour kept **the whole line** moving quickly. Some historians aren\u2019t sure it\u2019s true.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Rules often start for **a good reason**. But reasons can change while the rule stays. Every so often, ask **why you** still do the things you do.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your office has a rule: every invoice is printed and **signed by hand**. Ask why, and people shrug. It\u2019s just how things work here. Someone once had **a good reason**. Does it still exist?'},{kind:'story',text:'Ford\u2019s famous line limited Model T buyers to black. It\u2019s usually read as arrogance. On the standard explanation, which some historians dispute, it was about paint. Black japan enamel dried fastest, and drying time was **the constraint** on the line.'},{kind:'story',text:'Then General Motors reasoned that buyers with rising incomes would **pay for choice**. Through Chevrolet, it offered variety, colours included, and took market share. Ford held his position too long. By then it was very hard to reverse.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s the pattern. Paint must dry, so black is best, so only black is offered. Soon black is simply **what Ford is**. The rule has hardened into **an identity**, and nobody inside checks whether the reason still holds.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A constraint adopted for a sound reason can **outlive the reason**. The organisation built around it is the last to notice. So write down **why each** of your rules exists, and check it.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'It\u2019s the **most quoted line** in Ford\u2019s book: Model T buyers could choose a colour, provided they chose black. Most readers hear contempt for customers. What if you\u2019re really reading **a sentence about paint**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The standard explanation, which **some historians dispute**, is throughput. Black japan enamel dried faster than the alternatives. At the speeds Ford\u2019s line had reached, drying time was the binding constraint. One colour lifted **the whole line\u2019s** pace.'},{kind:'story',text:'For years that was right. Then General Motors bet that buyers with rising incomes would pay for choice. It offered colours and variety, and took share. Ford had built a company around black, and he held on too long.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a shop that closes at four, because the old till needed an hour to balance by hand. The till went digital years ago. Customers now want evenings. Closing time never moved, and nobody remembers why.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Hindsight flatters this lesson. Black was right for years, and **a rule reviewed** every week isn\u2019t a rule. Constraint made the Model T cheap. The error was holding it after its reason changed.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t throw out **your constraints**. Just record which rules answer one, then check **once a year** whether it still exists. The rule should leave when its reason does.'}]},'my-life-and-work_7':{title:'Price First, Cost Second',card:{insight:'Set the price low first, then hunt for the savings that make it pay.',tryThis:'Pick one price you set. Write what a lower price might do to sales.',skills:['money','thinking']},review:{prompt:'Your candles are not selling. You want to raise the price. What do you check?',right:'Work out what a lower price would do to sales',wrong:'Raise it, since loyal buyers will pay a bit more',why:'A lower price can bring more buyers and push you to find savings.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You sell lemonade at **\xa31 a cup** and sell ten cups a day. What if you charged 50p instead? Would you **earn less**, or could you end up earning more?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Henry Ford **cut the price** of his Model T car almost every year. It fell from about 850 dollars to under 300. More people could afford one, so he sold far more. The low price also pushed him to **build it cheaper**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A lower price can mean **more buyers**, and more money in total. It also forces you to **find savings**. But it\u2019s only worth it if the savings are real.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Most sellers ask what the market **will bear**, then charge it. For about twenty years, Ford did the opposite on purpose. What happens to a business that keeps **cutting its price**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Ford cut the Model T\u2019s price almost every year, from around 850 dollars to under 300. He insists competitors didn\u2019t force him. He states it in the book as a policy. And the business grew the whole time.'},{kind:'explain',text:'His logic inverts the usual order. Setting a price from cost is **passive**: whatever costs are, customers pay them. A low target price **forces the hunt** for savings, because the price won\u2019t wait. You find things a comfortable margin would leave alone.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Set a price from cost, or set it low and **make cost follow**. They **build different businesses**.',left:{title:'Price from cost',items:['High margin, smaller market','Efficiency is optional']},right:{title:'Price first, low',items:['Thin margin, huge market','Efficiency is compulsory']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Before you raise a price, work out what **a lower one** would do to volume. Volume can return more than margin. And a low price makes **your savings** compulsory.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Imagine you run a factory and your cost per car is falling. The obvious move is to **keep the savings** as profit. Ford kept doing something stranger, year after year. What was **he chasing**?'},{kind:'story',text:'He cut the Model T\u2019s price almost every year, from around 850 dollars to under 300. Competitors weren\u2019t forcing him. He states it in his book as a deliberate policy, and the business grew throughout.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Two forces work together. Volume spreads **fixed costs** over more cars, so each costs less. And a price set below today\u2019s cost turns efficiency from a hope into **a deadline**. The profit then funds the next cut.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a gym that cuts its monthly fee to fill the quiet hours. To make the lower price pay, it has to find savings: fewer staffed desks, simpler classes. If enough members join, the total grows.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The loop needs a product **stable enough** to keep optimising. That same discipline left Ford unable to replace the car when it was beaten. And a cut without the savings is just a loss.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Price can be **a tool** for finding efficiency, not just a number you accept. Cut first, then hunt for savings. Just make sure **your market** keeps growing as the price falls.'}]},'my-life-and-work_8':{title:'Owning Every Step',card:{insight:'Doing every step yourself feels safe, but then it is hard to change.',tryThis:'Before you make a part yourself, ask what you would be stuck with if things change.',skills:['thinking','leadership']},review:{prompt:'A supplier keeps showing up late, so you think of making the part yourself. What now?',right:'What will I be stuck with if things change?',wrong:'How much faster will my own part show up each time?',why:'Doing it yourself gives you control but makes it hard to change later.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You love making pizzas. Then you decide to **grow your own** tomatoes, make your own cheese and even grind the flour. Every step is yours now. What happens when your friends **want tacos** instead?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Henry Ford did this with cars. He owned **mines**, forests, ships and a railway. Raw stuff went in one end of his giant factory, and cars came out the other. Nobody could slow him down, but **changing the car** was very hard.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Doing **every step** yourself means nobody can let you down. But it also means you **can\u2019t switch** easily. Control and freedom pull in opposite directions.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A caf\xe9 is tired of late deliveries from its bakery. So it **buys an oven** and hires a baker. No more waiting, and no supplier\u2019s margin. Then customers start wanting **gluten-free**. Now what?'},{kind:'story',text:'Ford took this further than almost anyone. He owned iron ore and coal mines, forests, a fleet of ships, a railway and glassworks. At his River Rouge plant, raw material entered at one end. Finished cars left the other.'},{kind:'explain',text:'His reasoning was simple. A supplier\u2019s delay is **your stoppage**, and a supplier\u2019s margin is your cost. Own the supplier and both vanish. But every change to the car now meant changes across **an owned chain**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Integration is **a trade**, and you only see **the price** when conditions change.',left:{title:'What it buys',items:['No supplier delays','No supplier margins','Control of timing']},right:{title:'What it sells',items:['Freedom to change','Freedom to drop a step','Focus on your strengths']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Owning every step buys **control** over cost and timing. It sells **flexibility**. So before you bring a job in-house, ask what you\u2019re really buying.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Ford wasn\u2019t content to buy steel. So he bought **the mine**, the ships that carried the ore and the railway that hauled it. What does a company gain when it **owns everything**? And what does it lose?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The gain is **control**. No supplier could stop his line or take a margin from it. At enormous volumes, with a stable product, that logic was powerful. The cost was **rigidity**, and you only see that when things change.'},{kind:'story',text:'When the market moved, every change to the car meant changes across mines, mills and ships. A company that buys parts can switch supplier. A company that owns the whole chain has to rebuild it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Then there was rubber. In the late 1920s, after the book appeared, Ford began a plantation in Brazil. It failed outright, at very great expense. He\u2019d bought the obligation to be good at tropical farming, and wasn\u2019t.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Integration isn\u2019t always a mistake: it paid for Ford while volumes were huge and the car stood still. But an owned supplier never loses the contract, so it **feels no competition**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Integration buys **control** and sells **flexibility**. Before you take a supplier in-house, ask what you\u2019re buying: reliability, or the duty to be good at something new.'}]},'my-life-and-work_9':{title:'No Chart, One Boss',card:{insight:'With no chart, one boss decides it all, and choices pile up.',tryThis:'Write down three choices at work that wait for one person. Ask what happens when they are away.',skills:['leadership']},review:{prompt:'Your company of twelve waits on the founder for every choice. What do you watch?',right:'When choices start to pile up on the founder\u2019s desk',wrong:'If adding job titles would slow the whole team down',why:'One boss can only decide so much, so share the small choices.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your club has no captain and no rules about who decides. Everyone just asks the teacher. With ten pupils, it\u2019s quick. What happens when there are **a hundred**, all **asking her**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Henry Ford was proud that his company had **no chart** showing who was in charge, and hardly any titles. If you needed something, you found the person who had it. But in truth, all the power **sat with Ford**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Having no boss on paper doesn\u2019t mean **nobody\u2019s in charge**. It can mean **one person** decides everything. That works until there\u2019s too much for one head.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A start-up of twelve runs on **the founder\u2019s say-so**, and it\u2019s fast. Three years later, at two hundred people, every decision still waits for her. Why has **the whole company** slowed to her pace?'},{kind:'story',text:'Ford writes with pride that his company had no organisation chart, few titles and no defined lines of authority. Meetings were mostly unnecessary, he says. Anyone who needed something simply found the person who could supply it.'},{kind:'explain',text:'There\u2019s something in his case. Formal structure can breed people who **defend positions** instead of doing work. But removing the chart didn\u2019t remove power. It made all of it **personal**, flowing from one man\u2019s attention and decisions.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'A flat structure works **below one person\u2019s limit**, and jams **above it**.',left:{title:'Twelve people',items:['The founder sees everything','Decisions take minutes']},right:{title:'Two hundred people',items:['Every decision queues for her','Nobody else may say yes']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Flat structures scale to the limit of **one person\u2019s attention**. Work out where that limit is before you reach it. Past it, you don\u2019t have freedom, just **an unchecked boss**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Ford\u2019s book boasts that his company had **no organisation chart** and no titles worth having. You might call that freedom. By the mid-1940s, the company was in deep trouble. What did **the missing chart** hide?'},{kind:'explain',text:'His case is that formal structure breeds people who **defend positions** rather than work. He isn\u2019t wrong that it can. But the absence of structure didn\u2019t remove power. It **removed any check** on it.'},{kind:'story',text:'The book\u2019s pride reads differently beside what\u2019s documented elsewhere. Ford\u2019s own executives were badly treated. An internal security operation grew under Harry Bennett. When his grandson took control in 1945, the company was near collapse.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a design agency of fifteen with no managers. The founder approves every pitch, hire and invoice. At forty staff, work piles up while she\u2019s travelling. Nobody else was ever allowed to say yes.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Structure is no cure: a hierarchy can be as unchecked as Ford\u2019s was. And flat doesn\u2019t always fail. Small firms that **genuinely share authority** can work well. What fails is flat in name only.'},{kind:'moral',text:'No chart doesn\u2019t mean no boss. It can mean **one unchecked boss**, whose attention sets the limit for everyone. Find **that limit** before you reach it.'}]}}}},976,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'nicomachean-ethics',kind:'book',promise:'Become a better person one act at a time',about:'What if being a good person is a skill you can learn? Aristotle taught in Greece long ago. He said we become good by doing good things, the way builders learn by building. Here are his best tricks. Want to try them?',ideas:{'nicomachean-ethics_1':{title:'Learn It by Doing It',card:{insight:'You become brave by doing brave things, even before you feel brave.',tryThis:'Today, do one small brave act before you feel ready. Ask one question at work.',skills:['habits','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'You want to speak up more at work. What should come first?',right:'Do one small brave act before you feel brave',wrong:'Wait until you feel brave enough to try',why:'You start to feel brave after you do brave things, not before.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You want to be **brave**, like the kids who speak up in class. So you wait to feel brave first. Weeks go by. The feeling **never shows up**. What\u2019s going wrong?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Nobody learns to swim by **reading about it**. You get in, splash and sink a bit. After lots of tries, it feels normal. Aristotle, a thinker in ancient Greece, said **being brave** is learned the same way.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You don\u2019t get brave and then act. You **act first**, in small ways, and the **brave feeling** grows from the doing. So put your hand up once this week.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You want to be someone who\u2019s **steady in a crisis**. So you wait for a crisis, hoping you\u2019ll find out you are. Is that really how **steadiness** gets made?'},{kind:'story',text:'Say you want to be someone who speaks up. This week you ask **one question** in the meeting, heart still thumping. Next week you ask another. You\u2019re not waiting for courage to arrive. You\u2019re building it.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Aristotle treated character like **a craft**. Builders learn by building, musicians by playing. Nobody becomes patient by being told patience is good. You become it by **doing the patient thing**, often enough that the pull shifts.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Character isn\u2019t something you have and then act from. It\u2019s **what\u2019s left** by your acts. So don\u2019t wait to **feel brave**: do the small brave thing first.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'In the second book of the Ethics, Aristotle sets a puzzle. To become just, you must do just acts. But surely doing them means **you\u2019re just already**? Which comes first, **the act** or the person?'},{kind:'explain',text:'His answer is that the virtues aren\u2019t in us **by nature**. We\u2019re able to take them on, and we complete them **by practice**. Early acts only look right. Full virtue comes later, when you choose the act for its own sake.'},{kind:'story',text:'Sam wants to be more generous, but says he\u2019ll start once he feels less anxious about money. On Aristotle\u2019s view, that\u2019s the wrong way round. A small gift this week is how **the ease** begins.'},{kind:'story',text:'In the same book he mocks people who talk about virtue instead of practising it. They\u2019re like patients who listen closely to the doctor, then **do nothing** they were told. Listening alone won\u2019t make them well.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Practice builds bad habits as readily as good ones, so you need judgement to pick the right acts. And **upbringing** weighs heavily. Aristotle says habits formed from youth make all the difference.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your character is **the residue** of what you do. The feeling arrives last, and it arrives **because of the acts**. So choose the act today, before you\u2019re ready.'}]},'nicomachean-ethics_2':{title:'Too Little, Too Much',card:{insight:'Most good habits sit between too little and too much, so watch both sides.',tryThis:'Today, write what too little and too much look like for giving.',skills:['thinking','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'A friend joked that you never pay. Now you pay for everyone. Why?',right:'You fixed too little and slid into too much',wrong:'You are still not generous enough with friends',why:'Most good habits have two ways to fail, so watch both sides.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your coach says you should **shoot more**. So you do. Now you shoot every time you get the ball, even when a friend is free by the goal. Why is your team **groaning**?'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Aristotle said most good traits sit between **too little** and **too much**.',left:{title:'Too little',items:['Never takes a shot','Never speaks up','Never shares a sweet']},right:{title:'Too much',items:['Shoots every single time','Talks over everyone','Gives away all their lunch']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Being good isn\u2019t about **doing more**. It\u2019s about finding **the space between** too little and too much. Watch for both mistakes, not just one.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A review told you to be **more assertive**. Six months on, you push in every conversation, including the ones that needed **a listener**. The advice worked. So why doesn\u2019t it feel right now?'},{kind:'story',text:'When the review was written, you really did go quiet. So you watched for timidity, every meeting. You never noticed the day you started **talking over** the new hire. The failure you feared was behind you.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Aristotle says each virtue sits between **two vices**: too much and too little of the same thing. Courage sits between cowardice and recklessness. Generosity sits between meanness and waste. So there are **two ways to fail**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Advice that names **one failure** can push you straight past it into the other. So name **both edges** first. Then ask which one you drift toward.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Aristotle notices something odd about cowards and hotheads. To the coward, the brave person looks **reckless**. To the reckless, the same person looks **timid**. So where exactly is the middle?'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Every virtue has **two neighbours**: an excess and a deficiency of **the same thing**, feeling or action.',left:{title:'Too little',items:['Cowardice','Meanness','Never angry, even at injustice']},right:{title:'Too much',items:['Rashness','Waste','Quick, excessive anger']}},{kind:'explain',text:'The extremes **distort your view**. Wherever you stand, the middle looks like the far edge. So people at one end call the virtue by **the other vice\u2019s** name. You can\u2019t trust where the centre feels.'},{kind:'story',text:'A manager known for being blunt gets told to **give more direct feedback**. The advice was written for someone shy. Handed to her, the same sentence pushes her further toward the edge she already lives on.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take someone who\u2019s always been generous to a fault. They lend money they can\u2019t spare, then quietly resent it. Aristotle\u2019s rule for them is **the bent stick**: bend it the other way to straighten it.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Not every trait has a neat pair of opposites, and Aristotle admits some extremes had no name. The pattern can be forced, too. Someone can invent **too much honesty** to excuse a lie.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Most virtues have **two ways to fail**. The one you\u2019re fleeing is rarely the one you\u2019re walking into. So learn **your own drift**, and lean against it.'}]},'nicomachean-ethics_3':{title:'When a Habit Feels Hard',card:{insight:'If a good habit still feels hard, you are early, not failing.',tryThis:'Tonight, rate how hard your new habit felt today, from one to ten.',skills:['habits','psychology']},review:{prompt:'Three weeks in, your new running habit still feels hard. What does that mean?',right:'You are still early, and the habit has not set yet',wrong:'It is not working, so you should try a new habit',why:'Hard means the habit is still new, so keep going.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Ana and Ben both practise piano **every day**. Ana groans and counts the minutes. Ben sits down with no fuss and forgets the clock. They do **the same thing**. What\u2019s different?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Aristotle, a thinker in ancient Greece, said **feelings tell the truth** about habits. Ben is glad to practise, so his habit has set. Ana still fights it, so she\u2019s **still learning**. If you\u2019re like Ana, you\u2019re not failing.'},{kind:'moral',text:'If a good habit still feels like **a fight**, you\u2019re early, not failing. Keep going. One day it\u2019ll feel **easy**, and that\u2019s how you\u2019ll know.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Three weeks of eating well, and it still takes **visible effort**. You walk past the biscuits with your jaw set. You decide it\u2019s not working. Are you **reading the sign** right?'},{kind:'story',text:'Aristotle takes self-mastery as his example. One person goes without a pleasure and is **glad to**. Another goes without and resents every moment. Their behaviour is identical. Only the feeling shows who has the trait.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The same good act can sit in **two different states**, and only **the feeling** tells them apart.',left:{title:'Held by effort',items:['Costs you every time','Fails when you\u2019re tired','Feels like restraint']},right:{title:'Formed into habit',items:['Costs little','Survives a bad week','Feels like relief']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Behaviour can be held in place by effort for a long time. So read **the cost** as a gauge. While it\u2019s high, you\u2019re early, and **the habit** isn\u2019t set yet.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two colleagues both stay calm in **tense meetings**. One finds it easy. The other walks out drained and needs ten minutes alone. From the outside, they look **exactly the same**. Are they?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Aristotle\u2019s test comes right after his claim that virtue is built by habit. If habit is **the method**, you need a way to read progress. He finds it in feeling: the pleasure or pain **that follows an act**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Back to the meeting. The colleague who finds calm easy has **formed the trait**. The one who leaves drained is holding it in place by effort. That\u2019s real progress, but it\u2019s exposed to the first exhausting week.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take your own new habit, say a morning run. In week two, you argue with the alarm every day. By month three, skipping it might feel odd. **Nothing changed** in the act. The feeling moved.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some right acts should stay painful. Telling a hard truth to someone grieving costs anyone. And ease can come from **never being tested**, not from a settled habit.'},{kind:'moral',text:'How an act feels afterwards is **a cheap instrument**. If it still costs you every time, the habit isn\u2019t formed. Keep going, and **watch the cost** fall.'}]},'nicomachean-ethics_4':{title:'Three Kinds of Friend',card:{insight:'Friends you keep for use or fun often fade when that ends.',tryThis:'Ask one work friend to dinner this week. Pick a place far from work.',skills:['relationships','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'Two coworkers grew close at work. Then one of them quits. What is likely?',right:'They may fade, since work was what they shared',wrong:'They will stay close, since they got along so well',why:'A friendship built on work often lasts only as long as the work.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re moving to **a new school** next term. You\u2019ve got lots of friends now. A year from today, which of them will you **still be seeing**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Aristotle, a thinker in ancient Greece, said friends come in **three kinds**. Some are useful, like a bus buddy. Some are fun, like a game buddy. Some like you **for who you are**. Only that kind lasts through big changes.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Useful friends and fun friends are **good friends** too. They just depend on the bus or the game. Friends who like **the real you** are the ones most likely to last.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re **leaving the company** on Friday. People have said kind things and promised to stay in touch. By spring, you\u2019ll know which friendships were about **the work**, and which were about you.'},{kind:'story',text:'Think of the colleague you\u2019ve had lunch with every day for three years. You share jokes about the same project and the same boss. Next week, the project is gone. Whatever\u2019s left is what the friendship was really built on.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Aristotle sorts friendships by what they\u2019re for: **usefulness**, pleasure, or the good of the other person. The first two end when **their basis** ends. Only the third survives a change of circumstances, and it takes years.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Sort the same friendships by **what they\u2019re built on**, and you can **predict which last**.',left:{title:'Use or pleasure',items:['A shared job or hobby','Forms quickly','Ends when the basis ends']},right:{title:'Character',items:['Who the other person is','Takes years','Survives a change of job']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Nobody does anything wrong when **a work friendship** fades. It\u2019s lost its basis. Most disappointment comes from expecting it to last **like the third kind**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A founder calls her co-founder **her closest friend**. In two years, they\u2019ve never once met outside work. Every dinner has been about the company. What would Aristotle **want to ask** her?'},{kind:'explain',text:'For Aristotle, a friendship takes its nature from **what it loves**. Love someone for what they bring you, and it ends when they stop bringing it. Love them **for who they are**, and it lasts while they stay that way.'},{kind:'story',text:'Back to the founder. If the company were sold tomorrow, what would they still share? Maybe a great deal. But two years of only working dinners can\u2019t tell her, and **that\u2019s the point**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture two old friends who met at university. They\u2019ve changed jobs, cities and partners since. What holds them is neither use nor fun. Each simply **wants good things** for the other, and that took years.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Why is the best kind **so slow**? A wish for friendship can arise quickly, Aristotle says, but friendship itself can\u2019t. You have to know **the other\u2019s character**, and that takes time and a shared life.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The best kind needs two good people alike in virtue, and Aristotle admits that\u2019s rare. Real friendships also **mix all three** motives. So the sorting is a lens, not a verdict.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Most hurt in friendship comes from **expecting the wrong kind** to last. Know what each friendship is built on. Then **spend your time** where it\u2019ll grow.'}]},'nicomachean-ethics_5':{title:'One Swallow',card:{insight:'One bad week cannot show if your whole life is going well.',tryThis:'Next time a week goes badly, write down the best thing from last year.',skills:['psychology','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'After a great few months, a friend says she has figured out life. What is true?',right:'A good few months cannot show a whole life',wrong:'She has clearly found the secret to a good life',why:'A life is judged by many years, so one good stretch proves little.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You got **a bad mark** in a test. You fell out with your best friend. You lost your bus pass. It\u2019s Friday night, and you think: *my whole life is going wrong.* Is it **really**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Aristotle, a thinker in ancient Greece, used an old saying. **One swallow** doesn\u2019t make a spring. A swallow is a bird that comes back when it\u2019s warm. But one bird, or one warm day, doesn\u2019t **mean spring is here**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You can\u2019t judge a whole life by **one bad week**. Or by one great week, either. A life is judged **as a whole**, over lots of days.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'It\u2019s been **a bad year**. You lost your job, the move fell through, and you\u2019re tired. Lately you\u2019ve started drawing conclusions about **your whole life** from it. Is twelve months enough to judge by?'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture the same year from ten years on. The lost job, the move, the tiredness. Judged against twelve months, it\u2019s a disaster. Judged against a decade, it\u2019s **a hard chapter** in a much longer life.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Aristotle says no day or short stretch makes a person happy, in his sense. **Flourishing** belongs to a complete life. As the old saying goes, **one swallow** doesn\u2019t make a spring, and neither does one day.'},{kind:'explain',text:'It **cuts both ways**. A bad month isn\u2019t proof your life is going badly. That\u2019s obvious, and rarely believed. Less comfortable: a good month isn\u2019t proof either. **Peak moments** settle very little.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A life is judged **whole**, not sampled. So stop auditing it every month. A bad stretch isn\u2019t a verdict, and **a good stretch** isn\u2019t one either.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Aristotle opens the Ethics by asking what **the highest good** for a person is. His answer comes with a condition that\u2019s easy to skip: it has to hold over **a complete life**. Why add that?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Because flourishing isn\u2019t a state you\u2019re in on a Tuesday. It\u2019s **the shape** of a whole life. So one season, good or bad, can\u2019t make or unmake it. **One swallow**, as he puts it, doesn\u2019t make a spring.'},{kind:'story',text:'After twenty good years, someone\u2019s business collapses at forty. They decide their whole life has been a failure. On Aristotle\u2019s view, that\u2019s **the wrong unit**. Twenty years of good work don\u2019t vanish because one chapter hurt.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a friend has had a superb few months. Promotion, new flat, new running habit. She tells you she\u2019s finally worked out **how to live**. It may be true. But a few months can\u2019t show it.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The long view can be used to wave away **real suffering**. A bad year hurts now, and perspective isn\u2019t a painkiller. Aristotle also admits that great, repeated disasters, like those of Priam, Troy\u2019s old king, can spoil a life.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Flourishing is **the shape** of a whole life, so stop reading it off one season. Judge a hard stretch against the decade. Then judge **a great stretch** the same way.'}]},'nicomachean-ethics_6':{title:'Milo\u2019s Portion',card:{insight:'The right amount is not the same for all, so look at the case.',tryThis:'Next time a rule does not fit, ask what a fair, wise boss would do.',skills:['thinking','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'Two workers come in late. One is late a lot, and one never is. Same answer?',right:'No, the fair answer depends on what happened',wrong:'Yes, one rule for all is the only fair way',why:'The fair call depends on the person and the case.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A big wrestler who trains **all day** and a small kid who\u2019s just started both need lunch. Should they eat **the same amount**? And who decides how much is right?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Aristotle, a thinker in ancient Greece, said **the right amount** isn\u2019t just the middle. It depends on **who you are** and what\u2019s going on. A good coach looks at you, not at a chart.'},{kind:'moral',text:'There\u2019s no single right amount for **everyone**. You find it by **good judgement**, in the moment. When you\u2019re stuck, ask what a person you look up to would do.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A client asks, **again**, for work outside what you agreed. How firm should you be? You search for **the correct level**, as if there were a dial with one right setting.'},{kind:'story',text:'Say it\u2019s the third request this month. This client sent you half your work last year. A flat no could lose them. A quiet yes teaches them to keep asking. With a newer client, you\u2019d answer differently.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Aristotle says the mean is **relative to us**. It\u2019s whatever a person of practical wisdom would land on in **exactly these circumstances**. Ethics can\u2019t have the precision of maths, he warns, and demanding it is a mistake.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The right amount **depends** on the case, so there\u2019s no rule to look up. The skill grows by making calls and watching them land. When stuck, borrow **a wise judge\u2019s** eyes.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Ten measures of food are **too much** for a training athlete, and two are too little. So a trainer prescribes six, **the midpoint**. Sounds fair. What could go wrong?'},{kind:'story',text:'This is Aristotle\u2019s own example, and he says a good trainer won\u2019t do it. Six measures may be **too little** for Milo, a famous wrestler, and too much for a beginner. The midpoint is the same for everyone. The right portion isn\u2019t.'},{kind:'explain',text:'So virtue is a settled habit of choosing the mean **relative to us**. A person of practical wisdom fixes it. It means anger at the right time, toward the right people, for the right reason. **None of that** can be settled in advance.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take a client who keeps asking you for extra work. The firm answer that fits a new client could wreck a ten-year relationship. Two managers might **answer differently** and both be right, because their cases differ.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The idea sounds circular. The mean is what the wise person chooses, and the wise person is whoever chooses the mean. For a beginner, that\u2019s **thin guidance**: you need a judge before you can become one.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The mean is **a judgement** made in the case, not a point on a scale. So stop hunting for the rule. Make the call, **watch it land**, and adjust.'}]},'nicomachean-ethics_7':{title:'No Right Amount',card:{insight:'Some acts, like stealing, are wrong at any size, so there is no fair middle.',tryThis:'When a friend wants to meet halfway, ask if any amount is OK.',skills:['thinking','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'A coworker took cash from the tip jar. Someone says he can pay back half. Fair?',right:'No, stealing is wrong in any amount',wrong:'Yes, meeting in the middle keeps the peace',why:'A middle works for how much, but stealing has no right amount.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A boy in your class wants to **copy your homework**. Your friend says, *meet in the middle, let him copy half.* It sounds fair. Does it **feel right** to you?'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'For lots of things the right amount is **in between**. Some things are **just wrong**.',left:{title:'A right amount',items:['How much to eat','How much to talk','How much to spend']},right:{title:'No right amount',items:['Stealing','Cheating','Being glad when others get hurt']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Copying half is **still cheating**. Aristotle said some things have **no right amount** at all. So when someone says meet in the middle, ask if it\u2019s that kind of thing.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A colleague\u2019s **expenses claim** is plainly faked. One person wants it reported. Another wants it ignored. Someone suggests a quiet word and **a partial repayment**. The room relaxes. Should you relax too?'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture the meeting. Reporting it feels harsh. Ignoring it feels lazy. The **partial repayment** sits neatly between them, so it feels wise. Nobody asks whether faking a claim is something that comes **in amounts**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Aristotle\u2019s mean governs **how much** of a thing like anger or generosity is right. But some acts are **bad in themselves**, he says, and theft is one. So the midpoint here isn\u2019t moderation. It\u2019s **a verdict** that the rule can bend.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Before you split a difference, check whether the thing **admits of degree**. If it doesn\u2019t, the midpoint isn\u2019t a compromise. It\u2019s **a choice** you\u2019re making.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Aristotle has just defined virtue as **a mean** between two extremes. In the very next breath, he adds a warning. He seems to know exactly how the idea **will be misused**. What does he see coming?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Not every act or feeling has a mean. Some have names that **already imply badness**. Among feelings he lists spite, shamelessness and envy. Among acts, adultery, theft and murder. **The wrongness** is in the description itself.'},{kind:'story',text:'A team finds that its **safety data** was falsified. The compromise on offer is to fix it quietly and tell no one. Framed as a dial between disclosure and silence, it looks moderate. Framed correctly, it\u2019s tolerating a falsehood.'},{kind:'story',text:'Or take a smaller case. You find a colleague has been quietly taking stock home. Someone suggests letting them keep the last box, if they stop now. It sounds like a compromise. It\u2019s still deciding that **some theft** is fine.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The test works only once **the description** is settled, and that\u2019s where the real argument lies. Is it borrowing, or theft? A tactful omission, or deceit? The principle can\u2019t decide that for you.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The mean sets **how much**, but never **whether**. Before you look for a middle, ask which question you\u2019re facing. On the wrong one, a midpoint is a verdict.'}]},'nicomachean-ethics_8':{title:'The Thrown Stone',card:{insight:'Your habits come from small choices you made, so you can change them.',tryThis:'Tonight, change one small thing you do before your bad habit starts.',skills:['habits','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'You have stayed up too late every night this month. Where is the fix?',right:'Change one small choice earlier in the night',wrong:'Try much harder to fall asleep at one',why:'Small choices built the habit, so change one of them early.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Sam is late for school **most days**. Every morning he says sorry and promises to be on time. Every morning he\u2019s late again. Now he shrugs: *that\u2019s just how I am.* Is he **right**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Partly. By the time he\u2019s running for the bus, **trying harder** won\u2019t help much. But Aristotle, a thinker in ancient Greece, said your habits come from **lots of small choices**. Sam\u2019s fix is earlier: packing his bag the night before.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You can\u2019t fix a habit in **one moment**. But you can change the small choices that build the next one. Look for **one step earlier** you could change.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve missed the **quarterly deadline** three times running. Each time, you swear you\u2019ll try harder. Each time, Thursday arrives and the work isn\u2019t done. Where is **the real failure** happening?'},{kind:'story',text:'Aristotle describes a man who made himself ill through **a long habit** of neglect, ignoring his doctors. He can\u2019t now simply choose to be well. But he could once have chosen differently, many times over.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Put the deadline in **two places**, and see **where your leverage** really sits.',left:{title:'At the moment',items:['Try harder on deadline day','Feel bad about Thursday']},right:{title:'Upstream',items:['Change one weekly act','Start the work on Monday']}},{kind:'moral',text:'By the deadline, the habit is **already formed**. Blaming yourself for Thursday aims at the wrong target. **The leverage** is upstream, in the small acts of the weeks before.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A colleague shrugs off another missed handover: *I\u2019m just disorganised, blame my nature.* It sounds honest. It might even be **partly true**. But does **being that way** let anyone off the hook?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Aristotle meets this objection in his third book. Everyone pursues what seems good to them, and how things seem depends on **character**. So, the objector says, nobody\u2019s responsible. Aristotle grants **much of the premise**.'},{kind:'story',text:'His image is **a thrown stone**. Once it leaves your hand, you can\u2019t call it back. But throwing it was up to you. So with a bad habit: early on, you could have avoided it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now the colleague. By the day of the missed handover, the disorder is largely set, and willpower is a poor tool against it. But it was set by **small choices**, made week after week. The next ones are still in reach.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Aristotle himself says habits formed in childhood make **all the difference**, and children don\u2019t choose their upbringing. The acts that formed a habit weren\u2019t always open to you.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Responsibility moves from **the moment** to the pattern. That\u2019s gentler on the day itself and harder on the weeks before it. You can\u2019t stop the thrown stone, but you can **choose the next throw**.'}]},'nicomachean-ethics_9':{title:'Virtue and Luck',card:{insight:'A good life needs good character, but it needs some luck too.',tryThis:'Before you judge someone, write down what hard things they were dealing with.',skills:['thinking','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'Your neighbor works hard, but she falls behind the year her husband gets sick. Why?',right:'She had bad luck, and good people need some luck too',wrong:'She must not be trying hard enough now',why:'Being good is needed for a good life, but luck matters too.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve trained hard **all season**. You\u2019re a great teammate. Then on match day, you wake up with **flu**. You can\u2019t play, and your team loses. Does that make you a worse player?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Aristotle, a thinker in ancient Greece, said being good is **needed** for a good life. But it isn\u2019t **enough on its own**. You also need some luck: health, a home, enough to live on, and friends.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When someone has a hard time, it may not be **their fault**. And when you do well, some of it **may be luck**. Being kind about that is just being accurate.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You manage two colleagues for **one year**. One had a parent in hospital and a team cut in half. The other had no crises and a full team. Their results differ. What do the numbers **really measure**?'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Set the two years side by side before you **read the results** as **character**.',left:{title:'Hard year',items:['A parent in hospital','A team cut in half','Weaker numbers']},right:{title:'Easy year',items:['No crises','A full team','Stronger numbers']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Aristotle refuses a comforting claim. Some said a good person is happy even under torture. He calls that **nonsense**. Virtue needs **something to act with**: friends, means, health. Stripped of them all, even a good person can\u2019t flourish.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Character is necessary for a good life, but **not sufficient**. So the colleague with the hard year may have **more virtue** and less to show for it. Judge them against their conditions.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Can a truly good person be **happy on the rack**, while being tortured? Some people said yes. Virtue, they argued, was **all you need**. What did Aristotle make of that?'},{kind:'explain',text:'He calls the claim **nonsense**. For Aristotle, flourishing is virtuous activity over a complete life, and activity needs **equipment**. Friends, wealth and power are tools that fine acts get done with.'},{kind:'story',text:'Your team has had **its best year**. It\u2019s tempting to read that as proof of your quality. Some of it is. Some was the market, a strong hire and nobody falling ill. Writing that down isn\u2019t modesty. It\u2019s accuracy.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now think of Priam, the old king of Troy in Homer. He lost his sons and his city in old age. Aristotle doesn\u2019t say Priam lacked virtue. He says **no character** could make that life a happy one.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. His list of what a full life needs includes things we\u2019d reject. He says the very ugly, the low-born and the childless are hardly happy. That\u2019s **a Greek citizen\u2019s** values, not a truth about flourishing.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Character is necessary, but **not sufficient**. Conditions shape what any life can show. So before you judge anyone, yourself included, **write down their conditions** first.'}]}}}},977,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'notes-on-changing-your-mind',kind:'book',promise:'Change minds, even your own, without a fight',about:'Why do people almost never change their minds in a fight? They do change. It just happens later, when they are alone. The Mindroll team wrote this short essay about how that works. Here are five ideas from it. Want to try one?',ideas:{'notes-on-changing-your-mind_1':{title:'Minds Change Later',card:{insight:'Most people do not change their minds in a fight, but they do later.',tryThis:'Next time you disagree with someone, make your point once. Then stop pushing, and give them time.',skills:['communication','psychology']},review:{prompt:'You made a strong point, and your friend will not agree. What now?',right:'Make your point once, then give it time',wrong:'Keep pushing until she says you were right',why:'People change their minds later, so pushing just makes them dig in.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You and your cousin argue about a board game. You both get **louder**. Nobody gives in. A week later she plays it **your way**, and never says a word. What happened?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Saying *you win* in the middle of an argument means **losing in front** of someone. That feels bad, and it isn\u2019t the same as being wrong. So people hold on. They do their **real thinking** later, alone.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Minds do change, just **not in the room**. So say your piece well, then **let it go**. You\u2019ll often never hear that you won.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You tell a colleague the weekly report wastes **everyone\u2019s time**. They defend it, point by point, and you leave feeling you **lost**. Did the conversation fail, or is it **too early** to tell?'},{kind:'story',text:'A month later, the report shrinks to **one page**. Your colleague never mentions your talk. Nothing moved while you argued. The change came later, in private, and nobody announced it. That\u2019s the usual shape.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Conceding in the room costs **two things**. Alone, later, it costs only **one**.',left:{title:'In the room',items:['You give up the view','You lose the exchange','They watch you lose']},right:{title:'Alone, later',items:['You give up the view','Nothing else']}},{kind:'explain',text:'So pressing for **an admission** backfires. It turns a quiet private update into a public position they **must defend**. The person who wins the argument often blocks the very change they wanted.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Silence in the room tells you **nothing**. Make your case clearly, then **stop**. The verdict arrives later, somewhere you won\u2019t see it.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'People plainly change their minds over the years. Yet almost nobody seems to change it **mid-argument**. Both are true. So why do so many people conclude that **minds never change**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The answer is **social cost**. Conceding mid-argument means giving up the view and also losing the exchange in front of someone. The second cost is **the bigger one**. Alone, weeks later, only the first remains.'},{kind:'story',text:'You tell a friend her fitness plan skips **rest days**. She argues hard and you drop it. Three weeks later she mentions, casually, that she\u2019s added two. She never says where the idea came from.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture the opposite. You press a colleague to **admit** a plan was wrong, in front of the team. Cornered, they defend it louder. A quiet rethink has become **a public stand** they can\u2019t drop.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. This is an observed pattern, not a measured one, and it says *rarely*, not never. It can also become an excuse. If silence never counts as failure, a **weak argument** can\u2019t ever fail.'},{kind:'moral',text:'No visible movement doesn\u2019t mean **no movement**. Argue well, then leave **room** for a private update. Winning the moment is often how you lose the change.'}]},'notes-on-changing-your-mind_2':{title:'Letting Friends Down',card:{insight:'People often keep a view so they do not let their friends down.',tryThis:'When facts bounce off someone, ask who would be let down if they changed.',skills:['psychology','communication']},review:{prompt:'Your uncle reads your facts and nods. Then he changes nothing. What do you ask yourself?',right:'Who would be let down if he changed his mind?',wrong:'Which new facts would get him to agree?',why:'When facts bounce off, look at the friends who want him to keep his view.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your friend has always said one football team is **the best**. Today they lose badly, and everyone saw it. Your friend still won\u2019t budge. Is it really about **the team**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Often the worry isn\u2019t about **being wrong**. It\u2019s about friends who expect the old view. Facts answer *Am I right?* But the question in the way is about **those friends**, and facts can\u2019t answer that.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When facts don\u2019t work, look at **the people** around a belief. Changing your mind can feel like **letting them down**. That\u2019s a real cost, even if you can\u2019t see it.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'For years your uncle has called the town\u2019s new bypass **a disaster**. You show him figures suggesting it worked. He reads them, nods, and **changes nothing**. Why didn\u2019t the evidence land?'},{kind:'story',text:'Think about what agreeing would cost him. He\u2019s the family\u2019s **critic**. He\u2019s won that argument at dinner for years, and his friends agree with him. Changing his mind means giving up a role, not just a fact.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'You think you\u2019re asking for **a small update**. He may be weighing **something bigger**.',left:{title:'What you ask',items:['Look at one new figure','Accept one fact changed']},right:{title:'What it costs',items:['A role he\u2019s known for','Standing with his allies','Years of past arguments']}},{kind:'explain',text:'That\u2019s why more evidence often **bounces off**. You\u2019re asking him to pay **a hidden bill**, one you can\u2019t see from outside. Adding facts doesn\u2019t shrink it. Noticing it at least tells you what you\u2019re up against.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The fear that keeps a belief in place is rarely **about being wrong**. It\u2019s about the people who expect it kept. Ask **who\u2019d be disappointed** before reaching for another fact.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A belief can be **load-bearing**. Take it out and something else wobbles: a friendship, a job, a place in the family. So when someone won\u2019t move, what are they **really protecting**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The essay\u2019s claim is about **fear**. It\u2019s rarely fear of being wrong, which you can survive in private. It\u2019s the **disappointment** of people who expect you to keep the view. That\u2019s why private changes so often go unannounced.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a manager who has backed one supplier loudly for years. A cheaper, better option turns up. The numbers are clear. But switching means admitting, in front of her team, that she **backed the wrong one**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Or take someone in a running club that swears by one training plan. She starts to doubt it. In private, the doubt costs nothing. Saying it aloud at the club could cost her **her place** there.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Social cost isn\u2019t always the obstacle. Some people just weigh evidence differently. Treat every disagreement as social pressure and you **never engage** their reasons. You\u2019re diagnosing them, not answering them.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Evidence fails on its own when the belief **holds something up**. Look for the bill you can\u2019t see. But stay open to the chance that they simply **disagree**.'}]},'notes-on-changing-your-mind_3':{title:'The Small Yes',card:{insight:'Big views start to change when people agree to one small point.',tryThis:'Next time you disagree, find one small point you both agree on. Ask for a yes on just that.',skills:['communication','thinking']},review:{prompt:'Your friend says all meetings are a waste of time. Where do you start?',right:'Ask her to agree on one small point, like one good meeting',wrong:'Prove to her that most meetings are worth it',why:'She guards the big claim the most, but a small yes is easy to give.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your friend says **all homework** is useless. You could argue with the whole idea. Or you could ask about **one thing**: the spelling list before the test she aced. Which works better?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A small point is **easy to give**. It doesn\u2019t feel like losing. But once she agrees one bit of homework helped, *all homework is useless* stops fitting. She has to **sort that out** herself.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Big changes of mind start with **a small yes**. Don\u2019t attack the biggest part of what someone thinks. Ask about **one small piece** you both can agree on.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your friend is sure that working from home **ruins teams**. You could take on that claim head-on. Or you could aim at something **much smaller**. Which one is likely to move them?'},{kind:'story',text:'You don\u2019t argue the big point. You ask about the one team they know that works well remotely. They agree: that one\u2019s fine. Now *working from home ruins teams* has **an exception** to explain.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Where you aim decides what you get: **a defence** or **a loosening**.',left:{title:'The central claim',items:['Most defended','Tied to who they are','Conceding it is a defeat']},right:{title:'The weakest support',items:['Least defended','Rarely part of identity','Conceding it costs nothing']}},{kind:'explain',text:'A cheap concession **doesn\u2019t stay cheap**. They now hold two views that don\u2019t fit. Settling it in the old view\u2019s favour gets harder as concessions **pile up**. Nothing is forced. The pressure is their own.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t aim at **the centre** of someone\u2019s view. Ask for **the narrowest agreement** you\u2019d genuinely accept. It\u2019s slow, but it\u2019s how positions actually loosen.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Almost nobody goes from **certain to reversed** in one step. So how does a firmly held view come apart? Where does the first crack appear, and **who puts it there**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'It starts with **a narrow point** the person can afford to give: one figure, one exception. That creates an inconsistency they then have to resolve. Each further concession makes the **old position** harder to keep.'},{kind:'story',text:'Your housemate insists the gym is a waste of money. You don\u2019t argue the whole claim. You ask about the month he went three times a week and slept better. He grants that month was worth it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take two old friends who\u2019ve argued about the same issue for **years**. Neither has ever won a single exchange. Yet each has quietly shifted, one small point at a time. Over years, small concessions add up.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. A small concession can be **fenced off**: that one\u2019s unusual, they say, and the main claim stays whole. And collecting small yeses to corner someone turns a method into a trick.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Aim at **the weakest support**, not the centre. Ask only for agreement you\u2019d genuinely accept. Then give it **time**, because one exchange rarely does it.'}]},'notes-on-changing-your-mind_4':{title:'The Way Out',card:{insight:'People change faster when they do not have to say they were wrong.',tryThis:'Next time someone comes around to your view, do not say you told them so.',skills:['communication','relationships']},review:{prompt:'Your sister finally agrees to get a cheaper phone plan. What do you say?',right:'Prices changed, so switching makes sense now',wrong:'I told you so last year, remember?',why:'A true reason to switch lets her change without saying she was wrong.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your project partner wanted to build **a volcano**. You wanted a weather chart. Now she sees the volcano **won\u2019t fit** on the table. She says the chart might be better. What do you say?'},{kind:'explain',text:'If you say *told you so*, she may **dig in** and defend the volcano. If you say the table is tiny, she can switch and still **feel okay**. You\u2019ve given her a way out.'},{kind:'moral',text:'People change their minds more easily when they **don\u2019t have to** say they were wrong. So when someone comes round, skip **the gloating**. Let them switch with their head up.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'For a year your housemate has insisted on a **fixed energy tariff**. Now prices have dropped and the fixed deal costs more. You were right all along. What\u2019s the **smart thing** to say?'},{kind:'story',text:'You could say *I told you so*. Or you could say prices have moved, so switching might make sense now. Only one of those is likely to **end in a switch**. The other turns it into a contest.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'A way out has to be **honest**, and some things **close it** fast.',left:{title:'Honest ways out',items:['New information arrived','Circumstances changed','Right concern, wrong conclusion']},right:{title:'What closes them',items:['Demanding the admission first','Reminding them who was right','Making it a public event']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Demand an admission first, and you **raise the price** of changing. It costs more than staying put. So they stay. Not because they still believe it, but because it\u2019s now **the cheaper option**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A face-saving route **isn\u2019t dishonest**. It removes the **humiliation tax** that keeps people defending things. The price is yours: you let them arrive without giving you credit.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Someone who has defended a view for years finally sees it\u2019s wrong. Changing now means saying so out loud. What would make that **easier**, and what would make it **impossible**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Successful persuasion nearly always leaves **a way out**. Only honest ones count: new information, changed circumstances, or a reframe. In the reframe, the person was right about **the concern** but wrong about the conclusion.'},{kind:'story',text:'A neighbour opposed a new crossing, afraid it would slow traffic. Months later she can see the road is safer. Her way out: the traffic worry was fair. The verdict against the crossing wasn\u2019t.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a colleague who backed a tool the team now hates. You could make him **admit** it was a mistake. Or you could point out the team has **doubled** since he chose it. Which one gets the tool replaced?'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. A way out can stretch into never admitting anything. Where a mistake did **real harm**, someone may need to own it. And giving up the credit is hard when you were right.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Make changing cheaper than staying. Offer **an honest route** out, then step back. You may never get **the credit**, but you\u2019ll get the change.'}]},'notes-on-changing-your-mind_5':{title:'Pick Your Test Early',card:{insight:'Decide what would change your mind before a fight starts.',tryThis:'Pick one view you feel sure about. Write what would prove it wrong, and put the date on it.',skills:['thinking','psychology']},review:{prompt:'You are sure your new boss will make things worse. What now?',right:'Write down what would change your mind, and date it',wrong:'Wait and see if you were right all along',why:'Before a fight starts, you have nothing to lose, so you can be fair.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You think your friend is the **best goalkeeper** in your year. A rival says no way. Before anyone argues, could you say what would **make you think again**?'},{kind:'story',text:'So you write it down before the season: *if he lets in more goals than Ava, I\u2019ll think again.* It takes ten seconds. Nobody is arguing with you yet, so it\u2019s easy to be fair.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Decide **before** an argument what would change your mind. In the middle of one, every test feels like **a trap**. Beforehand, it\u2019s just one honest sentence.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re sure the new manager will make your team worse. Over a drink, a friend asks what **would change** your mind. Every answer sounds like **ammunition**. So you say nothing would.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The question is **cheap** in advance and **nearly impossible** once you\u2019re defending.',left:{title:'Written in advance',items:['No position under attack','Nothing at stake','The answer can be honest']},right:{title:'Written mid-argument',items:['Any condition is a target','Any concession is a retreat','The answer bends']}},{kind:'story',text:'So in January you write it down: *more deadlines hit by June, and nobody good gone.* If that happens, you\u2019ll rethink. It\u2019s dated and specific. Now June decides, not your mood.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Writing it also **sorts your beliefs**. Sometimes you try and can\u2019t name any observation that would count against a view. Then it isn\u2019t really **a factual claim**. Better to find that out in private.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Decide what would change your mind **while nothing\u2019s at stake**. Write it down and **date it**. Later, the evidence gets to decide, not your pride.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'The other four ideas are about **other people**: why they don\u2019t move, and what makes moving cheaper. This one turns to **you**. When could you act before the pressure starts?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Writing down what would change your mind is the one move available **before any argument**. Done early, it\u2019s cheap: nothing is at stake. Done mid-disagreement, any condition you name becomes **a target**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take a view like *working from home is slowing our team*. The condition: if delivery times over two quarters match last year\u2019s, drop it. It\u2019s dull and specific. That\u2019s its strength: nobody\u2019s pride is involved.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now try it on a view you hold about **a person**: say, that a cousin is selfish. You try to write the test and stall. Nothing you can picture would count. That tells you something about the view.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Values and matters of taste rarely have a clean test. That makes them **a different kind** of claim, not a worthless one. And a vague condition can be quietly rewritten later. The date makes that harder.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A view with no possible test isn\u2019t **a factual claim**. Find that out in private, not mid-argument. And for the views that do have a test, **write it now**.'}]}}}},978,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'notifications-you-agreed-to',kind:'book',promise:'Check the old yeses that still cost you',about:'How many things in your life still run on a yes you gave long ago? Your phone buzzes with alerts. Apps you never use charge you each month. These ideas show you how to check each one. Which would you say yes to again today?',ideas:{'notifications-you-agreed-to_1':{title:'One Tap, Years of Alerts',card:{insight:'One tap on an alert is a yes that keeps running for years.',tryThis:'Open your alert settings. For each app, ask if you would turn it on today.',skills:['focus','habits']},review:{prompt:'A new app asks to send you alerts. How should you decide?',right:'Count how often the alert will buzz, not the one tap',wrong:'Say yes if it seems handy right now',why:'One tap sets up alerts that can buzz you many times a day.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You download a new game, and a box pops up asking to **send you alerts**. You want to start playing, so you tap yes. It takes **one second**, and you forget all about it.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Say the game sends **two alerts** a day. Over a year, that\u2019s more than 700 pings. You said yes once, but the yes **keeps running**, and each ping lands at a moment you never picked.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Saying yes to alerts isn\u2019t **one choice**. It\u2019s an order that keeps running **all year**. So before you tap, count how often it will knock.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You install a delivery app and allow its notifications, because you want **your order** to arrive. It takes a second. Two years later, what has **that one tap** actually cost you?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Say it fires **three times** a day. Over two years, that\u2019s more than 2,000 interruptions, each at a moment you didn\u2019t choose. Enabling it wasn\u2019t one decision. It was **a standing instruction**, carried out again and again.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The **decision** and its **cost** happen at very different moments.',left:{title:'When you decide',items:['One tap','During setup, in a hurry','The cost is invisible']},right:{title:'When it runs',items:['Dozens of times a day','In the middle of real moments','The cost is paid every time']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Most alerts on your phone were never **really chosen**. They were defaults you accepted during setup, when you only wanted to get into the app. So your settings reflect a hundred **quick taps**, not a view on what deserves to interrupt you.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t judge a notification by **the tap**. Judge it by **how often it fires**, in moments you haven\u2019t seen yet. Then audit the ones already running.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Think about how you decided which apps may **interrupt you**. You were probably setting up a phone, in a hurry, tapping yes to get going. Is that the moment you\u2019d pick to decide **how your days go**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The article\u2019s point is about **shape**. You decide about a notification once, in general. Then it plays out in specific moments. Switching it on costs one tap. What follows can go on for years. So price it by its **firings**, not the tap.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Ana, who allowed a news app\u2019s alerts while setting up her phone. It sends **five a day**. That\u2019s over 1,800 interruptions a year, from one second of attention at setup. She never decided that number.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Ben, who tries the usual advice: be stricter each time an app asks. But at install he wants the app, and the cost is invisible. Six months on, he knows which alerts he\u2019s never once acted on.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Counting firings isn\u2019t the whole price. One rare alert, like **an urgent message** from family, can outweigh a thousand others. And the audit isn\u2019t done once, because new apps keep arriving with new defaults.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Move the decision from **install time** to **a regular audit**. Ask of each alert whether you\u2019d switch it on today. You know far more now than you did then.'}]},'notifications-you-agreed-to_2':{title:'Who Set the Switches',card:{insight:'Most apps turn on alerts for you, since they make money when you look.',tryThis:'Turn off every alert on one app. After a week, turn back on only what you miss.',skills:['focus','habits']},review:{prompt:'A new app comes with fifteen kinds of alerts, all turned on. What now?',right:'Turn them all off, then turn back on what you miss',wrong:'Keep them on, since the app knows what is useful',why:'The app picked those settings, so starting from off gives the choice back to you.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Imagine school dinner comes with **chips** unless you ask for salad. At the end of the week, most kids have had chips every day. Did they all **choose** chips?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Not really. Chips was **the plan** unless someone spoke up. Apps work the same way. Once you tap yes, most switch on **nearly every alert**, because they make money when you look. And they know lots of people leave settings alone.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Too many alerts doesn\u2019t mean you\u2019re weak. Someone else **set them up** that way. You\u2019re allowed to **switch them off**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You set up a new phone and tap yes to get going. A week later it\u2019s **buzzing constantly**. You blame yourself for checking it too often. But **who decided** all those alerts should be on?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Most apps ship with alerts **switched on**, because your attention is what they sell. And few things shape **what people get** more than a default. The people who set them know that. It isn\u2019t an oversight.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The article calls the power of defaults well established. Enrolling people by default greatly raises **pension membership** and organ-donor registration. Whether it raises actual donation is more contested. And it works even when the choice is **in plain view**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'So read your settings screen as **a negotiation**, not a neutral **control panel**.',left:{title:'A control panel',items:['Neutral starting settings','Too many alerts means weak will']},right:{title:'A negotiation',items:['Positions set by the other side','Switching off is a counter-offer']}},{kind:'moral',text:'You\u2019re not fighting **your own weakness**. You\u2019re declining terms the other side set. So **start from off**, and switch back on only what you miss.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A new app arrives with **fifteen kinds** of notification, all switched on before you touched anything. Nobody asked you about fourteen of them. So whose choice is **the screen** you\u2019re looking at?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The article\u2019s answer: the makers\u2019. Alerts ship switched on because **your attention** is what\u2019s for sale, and defaults steer what people do. So the settings screen records **a negotiation** in which one side set every opening position. You accepted them by not acting.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take pension schemes. The article notes that enrolling people by default greatly raises how many join. It doesn\u2019t depend on hiding the choice. People can see the option to leave and still **stay put**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Joe, who tries the article\u2019s advice. He switches off every alert on his phone. Over the week, he notices missing two: messages from family and his bus times. Those go back on. **Everything else** stays off.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The week test has **a blind spot**. A fraud warning or a severe weather alert may fire once a year. You won\u2019t miss it in a week. Exempt those first. And some defaults are for your safety.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Every default on your phone was **somebody\u2019s choice**. Usually it wasn\u2019t yours. Start from off, and make each alert **earn its place** back.'}]},'notifications-you-agreed-to_3':{title:'Meetings That Never Die',card:{insight:'A meeting held each week goes on until someone asks to stop it.',tryThis:'Find one weekly meeting you set up. Give it an end date three months from now.',skills:['productivity','habits']},review:{prompt:'A weekly call for a finished project is still on the calendar. What helps most?',right:'Give it an end date, so keeping it needs a choice',wrong:'Keep it, since someone would end it if it were no help',why:'With an end date, it stops on its own unless someone keeps it.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your class started a **Friday practice** to get ready for the school play. The play was in March. It\u2019s now June, and everyone still turns up. **Nobody\u2019s sure why**. So why hasn\u2019t it stopped?'},{kind:'explain',text:'To keep it going, nobody has to **do anything**. To stop it, someone has to stand up and ask. That feels awkward. It can sound like saying the practice, or **the people in it**, were a waste.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Weekly things **keep going** on their own. So when you start one, give it **an end date**. If you still want it then, you can always say yes again.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A weekly check-in was set up for **a product launch**. The launch happened two years ago. Six people still meet every Tuesday at ten. Each of them privately suspects it could go. Why has **nobody said so**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The reason had **a duration**, but the meeting didn\u2019t. And it\u2019s nobody\u2019s job to notice that the launch is over. So the meeting carries on in silence, and the silence gets read as **agreement**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Creating and cancelling cost **very different amounts**, and that gap is **the whole problem**.',left:{title:'Creating it',items:['One invitation','Says nothing about anyone']},right:{title:'Cancelling it',items:['A conversation','Implies the hour was wasted']}},{kind:'explain',text:'The fix is **structural**. Give every recurring invitation an end date when you create it. Then continuing needs **a decision**, and stopping needs nothing. Renewing a meeting you value takes one click.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A recurring meeting **never dies** on its own. Put the expiry in at the start, and let **silence end it** instead of keeping it alive.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Look at your calendar for next week. Somewhere in it there\u2019s probably a **recurring meeting** nobody would create today. Nobody has cancelled it either. What keeps it **alive**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A recurring meeting has **no expiry** and no review. Creation costs one invitation, and cancellation costs a conversation. So the population of recurring meetings **only ever grows**, until someone pays the social price of stopping one.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take a team that set up a weekly hour to support a new hire. The hire is senior now, and the hour continues. Nobody decided to keep it. Nobody decided to stop it. It just runs.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture whoever finally suggests cancelling. Creating that hour implied nothing about anyone. Cancelling it implies **a judgement**: that the hour wasn\u2019t worth it, and perhaps the people in it weren\u2019t either. That cost falls on whoever speaks first.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. An end date moves the burden, but doesn\u2019t remove it. A valuable meeting can lapse because everyone assumed someone else would renew it. Sometimes the reluctance to cancel reflects **real value**. Expiry forces the question.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Set an **expiry date** whenever you create something recurring. Then silence ends it, and only **a real decision** keeps it going. It\u2019s the same move as starting your alerts from off.'}]},'notifications-you-agreed-to_4':{title:'The Monthly Yes',card:{insight:'A charge that comes each month is a yes you said just once.',tryThis:'Look through one month of your bank app. Write down each charge you cannot name.',skills:['money','habits']},review:{prompt:'You pay $6 a month for an app you stopped using. What now?',right:'Ask if you would still pick this monthly charge today',wrong:'Leave it, since six dollars is too small to matter',why:'Small charges add up to a big sum you never said yes to.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Say you pay **\xa32 a month** for extra lives in a game. You signed up once, back in the summer. By winter, you hardly play. Where is **the \xa32** still going?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Paying every month is a yes that **keeps running**, just like an alert. You decided once. Then the money goes out each month, and you stop noticing. Starting took one tap. Stopping can take **lots of steps**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A monthly payment is easy to **forget about**. So check what you\u2019re paying for, and ask if you **still use it**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You scroll a bank statement and count **eleven monthly charges**. Each is small, and you recognise most of them. Two you can\u2019t place at all. How did they get **past you**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A subscription has **the same shape** as a notification: decided once, executed every month and invisible after the first. Continuing needs nothing. Cancelling needs **an act**, and the business has little reason to make that easy.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Look at **where your attention is** at each end of **the deal**.',left:{title:'Signing up',items:['One click','Done in the moment','Your attention is on it']},right:{title:'Cancelling',items:['Often several steps','Sometimes a phone call','Attention long since moved on']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s the trick of the shape. Each charge sits **below the level** at which anyone would bother to act. Ten of them add up to a figure that would never pass as **a single decision**. But nobody asked for one.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Run the same audit on your subscriptions as on your alerts. Ask whether each one has **earned the right** to continue. Money has a number on it, so this audit is often **easier to face**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Imagine someone offered you one bundle: every subscription you pay for, at their total price, starting today. Would you **sign up**? If not, why are you **still paying** for it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The article treats a subscription as **a standing agreement**, much like a recurring meeting. You decide once, and it bills you monthly. Staying costs no effort, but leaving takes a step. Firms have **little reason** to smooth that step away.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Kemi, who signed up for a meal kit, a streaming service and a language app in one busy year. Each charge felt tiny. A year later she adds them up, and the total surprises her.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a newspaper subscription that took one click to start. Cancelling it takes several steps, and sometimes a phone call. So it carries on for months after the last read, one small charge at a time.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Usage isn\u2019t always the right test. A cloud backup or **insurance** works quietly, and you rarely touch it. Judge those on what would happen without them. And not every awkward cancellation is a deliberate trap.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Once a year, list **every recurring charge**. Ask whether each has earned the right to continue, not whether cancelling is worth the bother. The list is **almost always longer** than you expect.'}]},'notifications-you-agreed-to_5':{title:'The Sign Up Again Test',card:{insight:'Ask if you would start it today, not if you should quit.',tryThis:'Pick one thing you pay for. Ask if you would sign up today at this price.',skills:['thinking','habits']},review:{prompt:'You are two years into a side project you no longer enjoy. Which question helps?',right:'Would I start this today, knowing what I know?',wrong:'How much would I waste by quitting now?',why:'The time you spent is gone, so only today counts.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve gone to **chess club** every week for two years. You don\u2019t enjoy it much now. But each time you think of quitting, you think of **all those evenings**. What\u2019s the fairer question?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Try asking: *would I join today?* Those two years are gone, **whatever you choose**. The new question leaves them out and looks only at **right now**. That makes it much easier to answer honestly.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Quitting feels like **losing something**. Not starting feels like nothing. So for anything that keeps going, ask: **would I start this** today?'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You joined a gym **nineteen months** ago at a good price. You\u2019ve been four times since Easter. Each time you think about cancelling, you think about **what you\u2019ve paid**. What question are you really asking?'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The same membership gets **different answers** depending on **which question** you ask.',left:{title:'Should I cancel?',items:['Recalls what you\u2019ve already paid','Cancelling feels like a loss']},right:{title:'Start it today?',items:['Looks only at today\u2019s price','Not starting feels like nothing']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Why does it work? Asking whether to cancel makes you count **what\u2019s been spent** and what you\u2019d lose. Asking whether to start removes **the sunk cost**. There\u2019s nothing behind you, only the price and what you\u2019d get.'},{kind:'moral',text:'For anything that **keeps running**, ask: would I start this today, at this price, knowing what I now know? It\u2019s a harder question than cancelling, and **a fairer one**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two people look at the same membership at the same price. One is asked whether to **cancel** it. The other is asked whether to **take it up** today. Would you expect the same answer?'},{kind:'explain',text:'They ought to, but the article says they **can differ**. The cancel question brings in what\u2019s been spent and what would be lost. The start question weighs **only the current price**. That\u2019s a reason to be wary of the first.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take someone two years into a side project that no longer excites them. Asking about quitting drags in two years of evenings. Asking about starting weighs only **the next evening**, and whether it\u2019s worth giving.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Leo, who agreed to chair a weekly committee three years ago. Nobody has asked him since, and he\u2019s never asked himself. Would he take the role today, knowing the hours it takes? He\u2019s honestly not sure.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Sunk costs don\u2019t count, but **switching costs** do. Restarting might mean a joining fee or a lost place. And some commitments, to people above all, aren\u2019t renewable on preference. A promise doesn\u2019t lapse because you wouldn\u2019t make it today.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Anything that continues without a decision deserves **one now and then**. The renewal question is the cheapest version. Ask it of one arrangement **this week**, and count what restarting would really cost.'}]}}}},979,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'novum-organum',kind:'book',promise:'Catch the ways your own mind fools you',about:'Why do two smart people read the same facts and still disagree? Francis Bacon was an English lawyer and thinker. In 1620, he wrote about the ways our minds fool us. He also gave simple tests to catch them. Which one fools you most?',ideas:{'novum-organum_1':{title:'The Uneven Mirror',card:{insight:'Your mind bends facts toward your hopes, so check with a tool, not more effort.',tryThis:'Next time you and a friend disagree, write down what each of you hoped.',skills:['thinking','psychology']},review:{prompt:'You and a coworker read one report and disagree. What do you look for?',right:'What each of you hoped the report would say',wrong:'Which one of you read the report too fast',why:'Every mind bends facts toward its hopes, so look for the hope first.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Hold a piece of **very old glass** up to a window. The straight bars behind it suddenly look wavy. The bars haven\u2019t moved. So what **bent them**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The glass bent them on the way to **your eye**. Your mind works a bit like that glass. It bends what you see **before you think**, and it bends things the same way every time.'},{kind:'moral',text:'In 1620 Francis Bacon said **trying harder** won\u2019t straighten the glass. You need **a method**: steps that check your view from outside your head.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two capable colleagues read **the same report** this morning. One calls it a triumph, the other a warning. Neither of them was careless. So where did **two verdicts** come from?'},{kind:'story',text:'Each arrived hoping for something and fearing something. Those hopes decided which lines looked important, before either began to reason. In 1620 Francis Bacon described exactly this. He said the mind never sees by a neutral light. It\u2019s soaked in what we want.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Bacon\u2019s big move was to treat **error as a pattern**, not as **a personal failing**.',left:{title:'A failing',items:['Someone was careless','Fix: try harder','Blame the person']},right:{title:'A pattern',items:['Every mind bends this way','Fix: a check that runs','Look for the distortion']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s why effort fails. The bending happens **on the way in**, so harder reasoning starts from bent material. A careful thinker with a warped instrument still gets warped results. The fix has to sit **outside your mind**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When careful people disagree, look for **the distortion** before you hunt for the error. Then build **a check** that runs every time, whoever is in the room.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'In 1620 the Lord Chancellor of England published a book in Latin. Its title promised **a new instrument** for thinking. Yet it opened on the user, not the world. Why start with **the flaw** in you?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Bacon\u2019s claim is that the understanding works like **an uneven mirror**. It mixes its own shape into everything it reflects. And it does so in **regular patterns**. Patterns can be listed, so they can be corrected.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a team that keeps underestimating how long projects take, though everyone is experienced. Urging them to concentrate misses the point. What works is a rule: compare each estimate with how long past projects really took.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take two fans watching the same replay. One sees a clear foul. The other, backing the other side, sees a fair tackle. Neither is lying. Each saw the moment through the glass of what they wanted.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. If every view is bent, so is **the critic\u2019s**. Bacon\u2019s answer is procedure, not a clean mind. And not every disagreement is distortion. Sometimes one side simply knows more, or made a plain mistake.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your mind isn\u2019t a clean mirror, and **nobody\u2019s is**. So don\u2019t trust effort to fix it. Build **a procedure** that checks the bend for you.'}]},'novum-organum_2':{title:'Idols of the Tribe',card:{insight:'We all keep the hits and drop the misses, so go look for the misses.',tryThis:'Pick a pattern you trust today. Write down the times it did not work.',skills:['thinking','psychology']},review:{prompt:'Six months of sales numbers show a clear rise. What do you do first?',right:'Look for the times the pattern did not hold',wrong:'Build the plan on the rise while it is fresh',why:'We all see more order than is there, so check for the misses.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Look up at the clouds on a lazy afternoon. Soon you spot **a dragon**, then a face. The sky didn\u2019t draw them. So why does your mind **keep finding** shapes?'},{kind:'story',text:'Long ago, a man was shown pictures of sailors who prayed in a storm and were saved. People said praying had saved them. He asked one question. Where were the pictures of sailors who prayed and drowned?'},{kind:'moral',text:'Francis Bacon called these the **idols of the tribe**: mistakes every human makes. You can\u2019t switch them off. So **look for what** would show you\u2019re wrong.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve found a clear rising trend in **six months** of sales figures. You\u2019ve built a plan on it. Be honest. If the line had pointed down, would you have **spotted it** as fast?'},{kind:'story',text:'Bacon retells an old tale. A man is shown paintings in a temple of sailors who made vows in a storm and survived. Does he now believe in the gods? He asks where the paintings of the drowned are.'},{kind:'explain',text:'These are **idols of the tribe**, Bacon\u2019s name for errors built into human nature. The mind sees **more order** than exists. Once it holds an opinion, it drags everything in to agree and sets the exceptions aside.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Because everyone shares them, comparing notes won\u2019t help. The whole room bends **the same way**. So Bacon says don\u2019t wait to notice them. Presume they\u2019re at work, and **check for** the case that would prove you wrong.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A pattern you like isn\u2019t proof. Before you act on one, ask **what you\u2019d see** if you were wrong. Then go and **look for it**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your friend swears her lucky socks win matches. She can list **every win** she wore them for. Ask about the losses and she goes vague. Why does her memory keep **the wins** so neatly?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Bacon\u2019s **tribe** is the whole human race. Its idols come from **human nature**: seeing more order than exists, favouring confirmations over exceptions, believing what we wish. Because they\u2019re universal, they\u2019re also invisible.'},{kind:'story',text:'Bacon retells an ancient story. A man sees paintings of sailors who vowed to the gods mid-storm and lived. He\u2019s asked whether he now admits their power. His reply asks where the drowned who vowed are painted.'},{kind:'story',text:'The lucky socks work the same way. Wins in the socks get remembered and retold. Losses in them fade, or get explained away. Nobody painted **the drowned**, and nobody counts **the lost matches**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Seeing patterns isn\u2019t simply a defect. A mind that waited for complete evidence would learn nothing, and **many patterns** are real. The idol is trusting one unchecked. And naming a bias doesn\u2019t switch it off.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You can\u2019t climb out of **your own species**. So don\u2019t rely on feeling neutral. Make the search for **the exception** a step you always take.'}]},'novum-organum_3':{title:'Idols of the Cave',card:{insight:'Your past shapes how you see, so ask someone from outside to look.',tryThis:'Pick a view you hold strongly. Write down who first taught it to you, and when.',skills:['thinking','psychology']},review:{prompt:'You and a coworker have argued the same point for years. What helps?',right:'Ask where each of your views first came from',wrong:'Bring more facts until the other side gives in',why:'Each mind thinks from its own past, so find where each view began.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your coach says the best players **always pass**. Your cousin\u2019s coach says the best players **always shoot**. You\u2019ve both played for years. You\u2019re both sure. Who\u2019s right?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Francis Bacon said each of us thinks from inside **a private cave**. It\u2019s built from how we were raised, what we read and who we looked up to. From inside, you **can\u2019t see** its walls.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You can\u2019t hear **your own accent**, but a visitor hears it at once. Your cave is like that. Someone **outside it** can spot what you miss.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re certain the project needs **a detailed plan** up front. Your colleague is just as sure it needs a rough start and fast changes. You\u2019ve both done this for fifteen years. Why **won\u2019t either** budge?'},{kind:'story',text:'Ask each of you where the belief began. You learned planning from a first boss whose careful plans saved a launch. Your colleague learned speed early, watching a big plan collapse. Each lesson worked, once, somewhere.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Bacon called these **idols of the cave**. Each person\u2019s cave is furnished by their nature, schooling, reading and company. Two capable people can face **the same evidence** and see different things, because neither can see their own walls.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Unlike the tribe\u2019s errors, cave errors are **personal**, which means they\u2019re **visible from outside**.',left:{title:'Tribe errors',items:['Shared by everyone','Nobody can point them out']},right:{title:'Cave errors',items:['Yours alone','A different mind spots them']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Introspection won\u2019t find your cave, because it **works from inside** it. Trace where each view **came from early** on. The answer often explains a deadlock better than the arguments.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Plato told of prisoners who took shadows on a cave wall for the real world. Centuries later Bacon borrowed **the cave**, and changed one thing. What if there\u2019s **a cave** for each of you?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Beyond the errors all humans share, each mind has **its own**. Bacon lists how they form. Some minds fix on differences, others on resemblances. Some revere the old, others **love novelty**. Each errs its own way.'},{kind:'story',text:'His own example is William Gilbert, who studied **the magnet** with great care. Then Gilbert built a whole philosophy out of magnets. The subject he\u2019d worked hardest at became the lens for everything else.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take two managers who\u2019ve argued about planning for a decade. One rose under a careful boss. The other watched a big plan collapse early on. Neither is foolish. Each is reasoning from the walls of an early lesson.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Other minds only help if they\u2019re truly formed differently. A team hired from the same schools shares a cave and **calls it consensus**. And tracing a view to someone\u2019s past explains it. It doesn\u2019t refute it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Bacon\u2019s own rule is blunt. When an idea pleases you **unusually well**, treat that pleasure as a reason for doubt. Then let **a different mind** look at your walls.'}]},'novum-organum_4':{title:'Idols of the Marketplace',card:{insight:'Many fights are over one word, so say what it means first.',tryThis:'Next time you disagree, ask what the main word means to each of you first.',skills:['communication','thinking']},review:{prompt:'Your team argues for an hour about whether a plan is risky. What next?',right:'Ask each person what the word means to them',wrong:'Keep arguing until one side has the stronger case',why:'One word can hide two meanings, so say what it means first.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You and your brother argue for **twenty minutes**. Is a hot dog a sandwich? Then you notice something odd. You never agreed what *sandwich* means. So what were you **really fighting** about?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Francis Bacon called this kind of trap the **idols of the marketplace**. Our words grew up in quick talk, like trading at a market. They weren\u2019t made for **careful thinking**, so they can trip you up.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Some fights are about **a word**, not about the world. Before you argue, say what **you each mean**. Often the fight just disappears.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A meeting spends **forty minutes** on whether a project counts as *strategic*. Positions harden and voices rise. Nobody has said what the word means. Can you guess **how it ends**?'},{kind:'story',text:'At the end, someone finally asks each person to define strategic. Three people give three different meanings. One means long-term, one means big, one means close to the boss\u2019s heart. They\u2019d never really disagreed about the project.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Bacon thought these **idols of the marketplace** the most troublesome of all. Words are shaped for quick talk, so they divide the world where a crowd noticed a difference. Then we use them for **precise thinking**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Bacon saw **two kinds** of bad word, and both slip into **careful reasoning** unnoticed.',left:{title:'Empty names',items:['Name things that don\u2019t exist','Luck that can run out']},right:{title:'Loose names',items:['Name real things vaguely','A friend: daily, or trusted?']}},{kind:'moral',text:'A badly cut word **corrupts an argument** with no slip of logic. So when a dispute stalls, stop and define the key word. You may find you\u2019re **arguing about language**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Bacon named four idols of the mind. One of them, he said, was **the most troublesome**. It wasn\u2019t a flaw of temperament or upbringing. It was something you use **every minute** of the day. What was it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The answer is **language**. Words are fitted to ordinary understanding. They cut the world where a crowd saw a joint, not always where **one exists**. Once in use, they\u2019re taken for real divisions.'},{kind:'story',text:'Two friends argue about whether a mutual friend is *really* a friend. One means someone you see daily. The other means someone you\u2019d trust with a secret. Both are right, about different things. The word hid it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now an empty name. A gambler says his luck is **running out**, as if luck were a fuel tank. The phrase names something that isn\u2019t there. Yet he changes his bets because of it.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s the danger. If a word lumps together things that differ, every valid step **carries the lump** forward. The logic checks out. The conclusion is still wrong. That\u2019s partly why Bacon **distrusted the syllogism**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some disputes survive **a shared definition**, because they were about the world all along. Demanding definitions first can become a way of stalling. And no inquiry escapes ordinary words entirely.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Every argument you make runs on words, so **every argument** inherits their faults. Define the word that\u2019s **doing the work**. Then see what\u2019s left to argue about.'}]},'novum-organum_5':{title:'Idols of the Theater',card:{insight:'If a plan can never lose, it can never teach you a thing.',tryThis:'Pick one plan you follow. Write what would show it is not working, then check for it.',skills:['thinking','science']},review:{prompt:'Your team\u2019s method comes from a famous book. How do you judge it?',right:'Name the result that would show it is not working',wrong:'Trust it, since it has an answer for it all',why:'If a plan can fit any result, it does not test a thing.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'At the school play, the backdrop shows **green hills** and a bright sun. From your seat it looks real. But try walking into **those hills**. What would you find?'},{kind:'story',text:'Your friend says kids born in March are bossy. A bossy March kid proves it. A shy one is *bossy deep down*. A bossy May kid is just copying. His idea can never lose.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Bacon called ideas like this **idols of the theatre**: painted scenery we take as real. If an idea fits everything, it **checks nothing**. That\u2019s the warning sign.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your team follows a well-known method with **a dozen stages**. It has a name, a book and a certificate. Now ask the room which stages anyone has **actually tested**, here. What do you hear?'},{kind:'story',text:'Bacon was writing against the philosophy that ruled the universities of his day. It was built on Aristotle and refined over centuries of commentary. It could answer any question in its own terms. Yet it had taught almost nothing new about nature.'},{kind:'explain',text:'He called inherited systems **idols of the theatre**, like stage plays: invented worlds, staged to convince. A complete system hangs together and answers everything. It saves you reasoning case by case. Those are **comforts, not evidence**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The test is whether **any observation** could embarrass it. A useful framework **forbids something**.',left:{title:'Stage scenery',items:['Absorbs every outcome','Grows more impressive','Tells you less']},right:{title:'Working framework',items:['Rules some results out','Can be embarrassed','Tells you something']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t judge a framework by how much it explains. Name **the result** that would show it isn\u2019t working. Then check whether anyone has **looked for it**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Imagine a philosophy that can answer **every question** you put to it. Nothing ever catches it out. It has ruled the universities for centuries. Is that **its strength**, or its weakness?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Bacon likens received systems to **stage plays**: worlds of their own making, convincingly staged. He names **three kinds**. The sophistical leans on clever argument. The empirical generalises from a few experiments. The superstitious mixes theology into natural philosophy.'},{kind:'story',text:'His main target was the school philosophy built on Aristotle. It could dispute any question in its own terms. Each new fact was absorbed, not tested. In Bacon\u2019s judgement, it had added almost nothing to knowledge of nature.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a modern team method with a dozen stages. A project that succeeds proves the method works. One that fails was done wrong. Either way, the method wins. Nobody can say what result would count against it.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Every inquirer needs some framework, and Bacon underrated this. A framework may rightly survive an anomaly while it\u2019s repaired. And the charge is **easy to throw** at any view you dislike.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The fair test is simple. Say **in advance** what would embarrass your framework, then go and look. A system that can\u2019t lose has **stopped describing** the world.'}]},'novum-organum_6':{title:'Hunt the Exception',card:{insight:'Cases that fit are easy to find, so hunt for one that does not.',tryThis:'Take a belief you hold strongly. Write down what would change your mind.',skills:['thinking','science']},review:{prompt:'Nine stories back your idea of why people quit. What next?',right:'Hunt for one case that does not fit',wrong:'Collect more stories that back your idea',why:'One case that does not fit tells you more than ten that do.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You think your plant grows best **by the window**. It\u2019s tall and green, so you feel sure you\u2019re right. But how could you **really know** for certain?'},{kind:'story',text:'Francis Bacon would say: put **a twin plant** away from the window too. Give it the same water and soil. If it grows just as well, your idea is in trouble. That second plant is **the real test**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Anyone can find examples that **agree with them**. The useful hunt is for the one that could **prove you wrong**. You have to plan that hunt on purpose.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You have a theory about why some customers stay and others leave. **Nine examples** support it. They came easily. Should that **reassure you**, or worry you?'},{kind:'story',text:'Bacon\u2019s answer would be to worry. You haven\u2019t gone looking for the tenth case, the customer your theory says should stay, who left anyway. One such case can do what nine agreeing ones can\u2019t.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Bacon says a mind pleased with an opinion **drags everything in** to support it. Resolving to be open-minded won\u2019t change that. A step in the method that **requires the hunt** for contrary cases will.'},{kind:'explain',text:'He proposed three lists. Cases where a thing appears. Close cases where it **doesn\u2019t appear**. Cases where it varies in degree. The second list was **the new move**, because that\u2019s where a wrong explanation gets caught.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Confirmations are cheap for **any theory**. The case that could refute yours is worth more than all of them. So go and **hunt for it**, on purpose.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'In the second book of *Novum Organum*, Bacon sets out to learn **what heat is**. He lists hot things, as you\u2019d expect. Then he looks hard at a cold one. Why would **the moon** matter?'},{kind:'story',text:'First come cases where heat is present: the sun\u2019s rays, flame, friction. Then cases as close as he can find where it\u2019s absent. The moon\u2019s rays give light **without warmth**. So light alone can\u2019t be what heat is.'},{kind:'explain',text:'That\u2019s the logic of **exclusion**. Supporting cases are easy to gather for any theory. The decisive work is the deliberate search for instances that **rule an explanation out**. Bacon built it into the method as a required step.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture your own theory about why customers leave. Nine cases fit. The tenth would be someone it says should have stayed, who cancelled anyway. If you never look for that customer, you\u2019ve tested nothing.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. One odd case doesn\u2019t always sink a sound theory. It may be **a faulty measurement**, or an unseen factor. And Bacon underrated the bold guess, tested afterwards. Deciding which cases count takes judgement.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A claim is tested by **what it forbids**, not by what it can absorb. So for any belief you hold, find **the case** that would sink it. Then go and look.'}]},'novum-organum_7':{title:'Knowledge That Works',card:{insight:'If you know what causes a thing, you can make it happen.',tryThis:'Pick one thing you think you know about your work. Write what it lets you fix or predict.',skills:['thinking','career']},review:{prompt:'A coworker explains a tool well but cannot make it run. What does Bacon say?',right:'She does not know the cause well enough to make it work',wrong:'She knows it well, since she explains it so clearly',why:'You show you know a thing by making it work, not by talking.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two kids explain how a bike works. One uses **lots of long words**. The other says very little. Then the chain falls off. Which of them **really knows** bikes, do you think?'},{kind:'story',text:'The quiet kid kneels down and puts the chain back on. The bike works again. The talker is still explaining. Now you know who **understood** it. Talking well and doing aren\u2019t the same thing.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Francis Bacon said the test of knowing **why something happens** is whether you can make it happen. Judge an idea by **what it does**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve probably heard **knowledge is power** used to mean that information gives you an edge. Bacon\u2019s own point was **narrower and tougher**. What test was he really setting?'},{kind:'explain',text:'In *Novum Organum*, he treats knowing and doing as **one thing**. If you understand a cause, you can produce its effect. If you can\u2019t produce it, you **don\u2019t yet understand** it.'},{kind:'story',text:'The learning of his day could dispute any question at length. It was elaborate and consistent. What it hadn\u2019t done, he said, was produce works: things that light a room or cure a patient.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Bacon judged a doctrine **by its fruits**, not by how long it could **hold an argument**.',left:{title:'Disputation',items:['Wins debates','Explains at length','Produces nothing new']},right:{title:'Works',items:['Lights a room','Cures a patient','Can be tested']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Take a theory you hold about your work. Name the thing it should let you **build or predict**. If nothing comes to mind, it may only be **a debating position**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Printing, gunpowder and the magnetic compass **remade the world**, in Bacon\u2019s view. None of them came out of the universities\u2019 philosophy. What did that tell him about **what counts** as knowing?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Early in the book, he binds knowing and making **into one test**. If you can\u2019t name the cause, you can\u2019t bring about the effect. You master nature only by **following its rules**.'},{kind:'story',text:'The school learning of his day was elaborate, consistent and, to Bacon, **barren**. It could dispute anything. It couldn\u2019t generate works. The compass, printing and gunpowder came instead from experience and chance, outside the lecture halls.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a consultant with a detailed theory of why teams miss deadlines. Ask what it lets her predict about the next project. If she can only explain each miss afterwards, Bacon would doubt she understands the cause.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The test is strong in natural science and weak elsewhere. A historian can\u2019t produce the fall of Rome, and isn\u2019t ignorant for that. And remedies often worked **before anyone knew why**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Bacon\u2019s standard is **uncomfortable and clarifying**. Ask what your knowledge lets you build, cure or predict. If the answer is nothing, treat that as **a verdict** on the knowledge.'}]},'novum-organum_8':{title:'The Fingerpost Test',card:{insight:'The best clue fits one answer and not the other.',tryThis:'Pick a question you are not sure about. Write what you would see if each answer were true.',skills:['thinking','science']},review:{prompt:'Sales dropped in March. Two ideas fit the drop just as well. What helps?',right:'Find a clue that fits one answer and not the other',wrong:'Get more numbers each month until one story wins',why:'A fact that fits both answers cannot settle the fight.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'The biscuits are gone. You think **the dog** ate them. Your sister blames **your little brother**. You find crumbs on the floor. Do those crumbs help you decide?'},{kind:'story',text:'Crumbs fit both ideas, so they don\u2019t help at all. Then you spot the biscuit tin, back on its high shelf with the lid on. A dog can\u2019t do that. A boy can. One look settles it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Francis Bacon called this kind of clue **a fingerpost**, like a signpost where a road splits. The best clue is one where **each answer** expects something different.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Sales dipped in March. One person blames **the price rise**. Another blames a rival\u2019s launch. Every new monthly figure fits both stories. Three meetings later, where\u2019s **the argument** now?'},{kind:'explain',text:'It\u2019s exactly where it started. Most evidence is **compatible** with several explanations, so gathering more can leave a dispute untouched. The valuable evidence is what the explanations **predict differently**. Then the result can\u2019t fit both.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Bacon named this the instance of **the fingerpost**, after the signpost at a fork in the road. It doesn\u2019t tell you where you\u2019re going. It tells you **which road** you\u2019re not on.'},{kind:'story',text:'Back to March. If the price rise caused the dip, customers who kept the old price should have held steady. If the rival did it, they\u2019d have dropped too. That one comparison could split them.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Before you gather anything, ask **what result** would separate the explanations. Sometimes the honest answer is none. Then more data **won\u2019t settle it**, so argue about what it\u2019s really about.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Do heavy things fall because of **their own nature**, or because the earth pulls them? Bacon wanted a test that could only go one way. Where would you put **two clocks** to find out?'},{kind:'story',text:'His proposal used two clocks driven by lead weights. One would sit at the top of a tall steeple, the other deep in a mine. If the earth draws weights, their pull should change with distance.'},{kind:'explain',text:'That\u2019s **the crucial instance**. Rival explanations predict different outcomes, so the result has to eliminate one. Ordinary observation rarely decides anything. The crucial case is built so that **agreeing with both** is impossible.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take the March sales dip, blamed on a price rise or a rival\u2019s launch. Monthly totals fit both stories forever. A fingerpost asks what each story predicts differently, and goes and looks only there.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Crucial experiments are rarer than the name suggests. A theory can usually be **rescued** by adjusting an assumption. And there may be more than two roads, so killing one rival doesn\u2019t prove the other.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Before you gather evidence, write down **what each explanation** predicts. Where the predictions match, more data won\u2019t help you. Where nothing could separate them, **the dispute isn\u2019t** about evidence.'}]},'novum-organum_9':{title:'The World\u2019s Youth',card:{insight:'People long ago lived in the world\u2019s youth, so old does not mean right.',tryThis:'When someone defends a plan as the old way, ask what has been learned since.',skills:['thinking','history']},review:{prompt:'Someone says your plan should stay because it is the classic way. What do you ask?',right:'What have we learned since it became the classic way?',wrong:'Who came up with it first, and how famous were they?',why:'Respect old ideas, but weigh what has been learned since then.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your **first drawings** from nursery are still in a box somewhere. You keep them because you love them. Would you ask that **five-year-old you** for help with homework today?'},{kind:'explain',text:'We call the distant past *ancient*, as if it were old and wise. Francis Bacon said it was really **the world\u2019s childhood**. We\u2019re the older ones now, with much **more experience** behind us.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Love the people who **came before**, the way you love those drawings. But don\u2019t let them **settle a question** just because they\u2019re old.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'In an argument about how to run something, someone notes that the current way is **the classic one**. It\u2019s been used since the founders. The room goes quiet. Has **anything been proved**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Bacon turns the authority of age around. We credit old people with wisdom because of their **long experience**. Apply that to whole ages and it flips. The age with the most experience behind it is **the latest one**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Bacon kept his **reverence** for the ancients. What he removed was **their right** to settle questions.',left:{title:'Respecting them',items:['Read them closely','Costs you nothing']},right:{title:'Deferring to them',items:['Let age decide','Costs you the answer']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Classic isn\u2019t evidence. When an approach is defended for its age, ask what evidence **has built up** since. **Respect the source**, and weigh the evidence.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'The 1620 volume containing *Novum Organum* has a striking title page. A ship sails out between **the Pillars of Hercules**, the old limit of the known world. Why would a book about thinking open with **a voyage**?'},{kind:'story',text:'The Latin motto beneath promises that many will pass through and knowledge will grow. The point is that the old boundary was local. Treating it as final was the error. Bacon makes the same move with time itself.'},{kind:'explain',text:'He argues from naming. Our own age is **the truly old one**, since the world has lived longer now. The ancients are old only when we count backwards. That moves **the authority** of experience from past to present.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a team defending its process because the founders built it. Nobody says it works better. They say it\u2019s **the original**. Bacon would ask what the team has learned since the founders, and weigh that instead.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Everything else in the book needs this. If the ancients had **reached the summit**, there\u2019d be nothing left to build. No reason for new tables or instances. Knowledge has to **accumulate** for the method to matter.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Knowledge can be lost, and later ages can be worse at some things. Being later gives you access to more experience, **not its use**. And an old idea that survived long scrutiny may deserve weight.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The ancients are owed **your respect**, not the final word. You\u2019re the elder, with more behind you. So when age is offered as proof, ask **what\u2019s been learned** since.'}]}}}},980,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'on-liberty',kind:'book',promise:'Hear out the idea you are sure is wrong',about:'What if the ideas you are most sure of are the ones you know least? In 1859, John Stuart Mill wrote a book about being free. He said we find the truth when people speak and live their own way. Want to try his ideas?',ideas:{'on-liberty_1':{title:'The Trap of Being Sure',card:{insight:'When you shut down an idea, you act like you cannot be wrong.',tryThis:'Next time an idea gets laughed off, ask the person why they think so.',skills:['thinking','communication']},review:{prompt:'Your team laughs off a new idea and moves on. What do you do?',right:'Ask the person why, then answer the idea',wrong:'Move on, since everyone is sure it will not work',why:'Feeling sure is not proof, so hear the idea out and answer it.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'In class, Sam says the puzzle\u2019s answer is **12**. Everyone laughs, and the teacher moves on. Nobody asks Sam why. But what if Sam was **right all along**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'About 400 years ago, Galileo said the Earth moves round the Sun. The people in charge were **sure he was wrong**. They made him take it back. He was right. **Feeling sure** isn\u2019t the same as being right.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When you stop someone speaking, you act as if **you can\u2019t be wrong**. People just as sure have been wrong before. So let them talk, then **answer them**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A junior colleague says the new product will flop. Someone senior cuts in: **we\u2019ve been through this**. Nobody answers the point. The meeting moves on, and it feels efficient. Was anything **actually decided**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Mill published *On Liberty* in 1859. He noticed a pattern in history. Every generation silenced some view that a later one judged correct. And each did it with exactly the confidence you feel now, sure it couldn\u2019t be wrong.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The two mistakes aren\u2019t equal. **Silencing** costs most exactly when **you\u2019re most sure**.',left:{title:'Silence a truth',items:['The truth is lost','Nobody ever finds out']},right:{title:'Hear a falsehood',items:['Answering it takes minutes','The truth gets tested']}},{kind:'explain',text:'This isn\u2019t saying every view is as good as any other. Mill thought **some are false**. His claim is narrower. We\u2019re bad at knowing **in advance** which ones, so answering beats silencing.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Shutting a view down claims **you can\u2019t be wrong**. Nobody can claim that. So when a point gets silenced, **ask for the answer** instead.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Mill picks his examples carefully. The people doing the silencing aren\u2019t villains. One is **a city** famous for its thinkers. One is among **the best rulers** who ever lived. Why choose them?'},{kind:'explain',text:'His rule: to silence a view is to act as if you\u2019re **infallible**, unable to err. The case for hearing it doesn\u2019t need the view to be true. It needs only that **the silencers can err**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Athens condemned Socrates for impiety. The citizens who judged him weren\u2019t monsters. They acted on beliefs they had every reason to trust. Today almost nobody thinks they got it right.'},{kind:'story',text:'Marcus Aurelius was, in Mill\u2019s account, one of the best rulers who ever lived. He still persecuted Christians, in good conscience. If someone that wise could be that sure and wrong, so could you.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The argument protects opinion, not every act of speech. Mill says a claim fit to print can be punished when shouted at **an angry crowd**. And your doubt doesn\u2019t make **the dissenter right**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Confidence is **not evidence**. Being wrong feels exactly like being right from the inside. So hold your view, act on it, and **leave the challenge open**.'}]},'on-liberty_2':{title:'A Truth Gone Stale',card:{insight:'If no one argues with a true idea, it turns into words you just say.',tryThis:'Pick one view you hold, and write the best case against it in three lines.',skills:['thinking','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'Asked why cheating is wrong, Jake cannot give one reason. What is wrong?',right:'He knows the words, but not the reasons',wrong:'Nothing, since what he believes is right',why:'If you cannot say why it is true, one good point can knock it down.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your little cousin asks how you know **the Earth** goes round the Sun. You open your mouth. Nothing comes out. You\u2019re sure it\u2019s true. So why can\u2019t you **say why**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A team that only plays much younger kids thinks it\u2019s great. Then it meets **a real team** and falls apart. Ideas work the same way. One that\u2019s never argued with **goes weak**, even if it\u2019s true.'},{kind:'moral',text:'John Stuart Mill said an unargued truth becomes **a slogan**: words you repeat without the reasons. So test what you believe. Say the **best case against** it, then say why you still agree.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your team has held the **same strategy** for years. Everyone agrees with it. Then a new hire asks, in her first week, why it works. The room goes quiet. Does anyone **actually know**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Mill\u2019s claim is that a true belief, never contested, **decays**. The truth survives, but the understanding goes. You keep **the conclusion** and lose the argument, so you can\u2019t say where it stops.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'What you learn depends on whether you\u2019ve **met the objection** or **only the conclusion**.',left:{title:'Met the objection',items:['Knows why it holds','Knows where it stops','Spots a weak challenge']},right:{title:'Never challenged',items:['Knows only the phrase','Can\u2019t judge a challenge','Every question feels hostile']}},{kind:'moral',text:'An unchallenged truth is **fragile**. It gets defended badly, then gives way to the first **capable opponent**. If you can\u2019t state the case against your view, you hold a slogan.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A law student can recite her side of the case word for word. She has never once read **the other side\u2019s** brief. How well do you think she\u2019ll do in **the courtroom**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Mill gives several grounds for hearing dissent. The silenced view may be true, or partly true. The last ground is the boldest. Even a **wholly true** view, if never contested, ends up held as **prejudice**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Mill points to Cicero, the great Roman orator. Cicero studied his opponent\u2019s case as closely as his own. That wasn\u2019t generosity. It was preparation. He built the challenge in on purpose.'},{kind:'story',text:'The Catholic Church long did something similar. Before naming a saint, it appointed someone to argue against the case. The point was simple: the case had to survive its strongest objection first.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Manufactured doubt can be **a weapon**. Staging a debate about a well-tested finding can mislead an audience about the evidence. Mill\u2019s remedy is for understanding a belief, not pretending **a settled question** is open.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Agreement **isn\u2019t evidence**. A belief nobody argues with has stopped being examined. So if no critic shows up, **build one yourself**, and make your case survive it.'}]},'on-liberty_3':{title:'Two Halves of the Truth',card:{insight:'In most fights, each side holds part of the truth, but not all of it.',tryThis:'Next time you argue, first say one thing the other side gets right.',skills:['communication','thinking']},review:{prompt:'Two coworkers have argued about a launch for a month. What do you do?',right:'Find the part of the truth each side holds',wrong:'Pick the coworker who is right and back him',why:'Each side often has a good point, and if you pick a winner, you lose one.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You want a **film night**. Your sister wants a board game. You\u2019ve argued for ten minutes, and you\u2019re both getting louder. Is one of you **really wrong**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Listen closely and you hear two good points. You want to relax after a long week. She wants to do something together, not just stare at a screen. Both of you are right about something.'},{kind:'moral',text:'In most arguments, each side holds a **piece of the truth**. The point isn\u2019t always to win. If you win, the **other piece** gets thrown away.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two colleagues have argued for **a month**. One wants to ship fast and fix later. The other wants it right before anyone sees it. Which of them would you **back**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Each has watched the other\u2019s approach fail before. The fast one has seen perfect plans miss the moment. The careful one has cleaned up rushed launches. Each is right about why the other\u2019s way goes wrong.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Mill\u2019s point is that most disputes aren\u2019t **truth against error**. They\u2019re two **partial truths**, each mistaken for the whole. If you pick a winner, the loser\u2019s fragment goes with them.'},{kind:'explain',text:'He applied it to the politics of his day. A party of order and a party of progress were **both needed**, he argued. Each holds the half the other neglects, and **stops the other** going too far.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Treat a deadlock as **a joint job**, not a contest. Before deciding anything, say what each side is **right about**. Then build from both pieces.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture two parties at an election. One stands for **order**: keep what works. The other stands for **progress**: fix what doesn\u2019t. Each calls the other a danger. Which one do you think Mill backed?'},{kind:'story',text:'Neither, on its own. Mill argued a healthy political life needs both. Each covers what the other overlooks. And it\u2019s mostly the other side\u2019s pushback that stops each one going too far.'},{kind:'explain',text:'This is one of Mill\u2019s grounds for free discussion. Even **a false opinion** may carry a portion of truth the prevailing view lacks. The prevailing view is seldom the whole truth, so the missing part **arrives through dissent**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a smaller case. You and a colleague are deadlocked on a launch. You want to ship fast. They want it polished first. You\u2019ve each seen the other\u2019s way fail, and you\u2019re both right about why.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some disputes really are truth against falsehood, and halving them gives error a share it hasn\u2019t earned. It also invites **a lazy middle**. The true part may lie anywhere, rarely at the midpoint.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Splitting the difference **isn\u2019t assembly**. Find the true part on **each side**, wherever it sits, and build from those. Then nobody\u2019s fragment is wasted.'}]},'on-liberty_4':{title:'The Harm Principle',card:{insight:'You can stop a grown-up only if it keeps others from harm.',tryThis:'Before you tell someone to stop, ask who else their choice would hurt.',skills:['thinking','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'Your neighbor eats cake for breakfast every day. Should the town step in?',right:'Only if it would hurt others',wrong:'Yes, since it is plainly bad for her',why:'Her own good is not enough to force her, but harm to others is.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A woman lives alone in a flat with **thin walls**. She eats cake for breakfast. She also plays the drums at **three in the morning**. The town wants to stop both. Should it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The cake only affects her. The drums keep **the neighbours** awake. John Stuart Mill had a rule for this. You may stop a grown-up only to keep **other people** from harm. Their own good isn\u2019t enough.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Before you make someone stop, ask **who gets hurt**. If it\u2019s only them, Mill says **leave them be**. If others get hurt, stepping in can be fair.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A friend drinks too much, but only at home, alone. Another drives after drinking. You\u2019d happily see **the second** stopped by law. Would you stop **the first**? And on what grounds?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Mill fixed the line in one sentence. Power over an unwilling adult is justified only to prevent **harm to others**. Their own good, of body or of character, is **not enough**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Then he tested it. Poison kills rats, and people. Should its sale be banned? Mill said no, since most buyers have honest uses. But the seller may be made to label it and record each sale.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The hard part is applying it. What counts as **harm**? Does harm to yourself count if your family bears it? Is offence harm at all? Mill didn\u2019t claim to **settle these**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The rule is **easy to state**. The value is in testing it against the cases that strain it. So find **the awkward cases**, and let them show you where the line runs.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A man is about to step onto **a bridge** you know is unsafe. There\u2019s no time to warn him. Mill, the great enemy of interference, says **you may grab him**. Why?'},{kind:'explain',text:'His target wasn\u2019t only law. It was majorities, through law or opinion, enforcing their **likes and dislikes** on everyone and calling it morality. The harm principle draws **one line** for both.'},{kind:'story',text:'Back to the bridge. Stopping the man isn\u2019t overriding his choice. He wants to cross, not to fall in the river. You\u2019re serving his own aim. Once he knows the danger, the choice is his again.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a soldier who gets drunk on duty. Drinking, for Mill, is a private matter. But a soldier on duty owes something to others. The same act becomes other-regarding, and punishment is fair.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The rule needs a clean line between acts that touch only you and acts that touch others. **Almost nothing** is purely the first. And harm is left undefined, so the fight moves to who defines it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Harm to others is needed before anyone may interfere. It doesn\u2019t settle that they **should**. The principle **opens the question**, and the hard cases are where you learn its shape.'}]},'on-liberty_5':{title:'The Rule of Custom',card:{insight:'Customs can rule us more than laws, since no one asks for a reason.',tryThis:'Today, ask why one habit at your work is done that way.',skills:['thinking','psychology']},review:{prompt:'No one at work leaves before the boss, but no rule says so. Why?',right:'A rule no one wrote down, with no reason given',wrong:'Everyone chose to stay late on their own',why:'A custom never gives its reason, so no one thinks to push back.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'There\u2019s a big lawn in **the park**. No sign says keep off the grass. Nobody would tell you off for crossing it. Yet everyone walks **the long way round**. Why?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Some rules are **written down**. Others are just how things are done. John Stuart Mill thought the unwritten ones control us more. They **never give a reason**, so there\u2019s nothing to argue with.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When you do something only because everyone does, **stop and ask why**. If nobody knows, you\u2019ve found **a custom**, not a reason. You\u2019re allowed to question it.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Nobody leaves the office **before the manager** does. It\u2019s in no handbook. Nobody\u2019s ever been disciplined for it. Ask, and everyone says they\u2019re free to go. So why does **everyone stay**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Mill thought social pressure could control **more completely** than law. A law is visible, so you can oppose it in public. A custom presents itself as **how things are**. There\u2019s nothing to appeal against.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Put **a law** beside **a custom**, and see which one you can fight.',left:{title:'A law',items:['Written down and visible','Has a penalty','Can be argued with']},right:{title:'A custom',items:['Never written down','No visible penalty','Never has to explain']}},{kind:'explain',text:'His name for this is **the despotism of custom**. People stop asking what they prefer. They ask what people **in their position** usually do. Left unused, the first question withers.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A custom you\u2019ve never questioned isn\u2019t **a reason**. It\u2019s a habit that never had to explain itself. So ask for the reason, and **keep it** only if one turns up.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Mill opens the essay with an old fear: rulers against the people. Elected government had **partly answered** it. So why did he think a **newer danger** was worse?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The new danger was **the majority** itself, acting through law and through opinion. He judged this **social tyranny** the more formidable. It reaches into daily life and leaves you fewer ways to escape.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a team where nobody takes holiday in the busy quarter. No policy says so, and leave is rarely refused. But taking it costs a cooler manager and a quiet note at review. Nobody decided to impose that.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now one person books a week off in the busy quarter anyway. She isn\u2019t making a speech. But her colleagues see that nothing terrible happens. In Mill\u2019s terms, her refusal is a service: it shows the custom can be questioned.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Customs often carry knowledge nobody can now spell out. Some norms of courtesy and trust work **because nobody negotiates** them. And much nonconformity is only fashion.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t tear up every custom. **Ask for its reason** and keep it if a good one turns up. If nobody\u2019s ever heard one, you\u2019ve found **a quiet despot**.'}]},'on-liberty_6':{title:'Many Ways to Live',card:{insight:'No one knows ahead of time what life suits you, so you have to try.',tryThis:'Before you tell a friend to play it safe, ask if you know it suits her.',skills:['thinking','relationships']},review:{prompt:'Your friend, forty, wants to quit a safe job to build tables. What do you ask?',right:'Do I know the safe job suits her?',wrong:'How can I talk her out of it?',why:'Lots of people taking the safe path does not prove it suits her.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A farmer has **six kinds** of seed. She wants to know which grows best in her field. Guessing won\u2019t tell her. Reading won\u2019t either. So what\u2019s **the only way** to find out?'},{kind:'story',text:'She plants each seed in its own patch and watches what comes up. Now think of your aunt, who quits her job to grow food. Maybe she loves it. Maybe she goes back. Either way, everyone learns something real.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Nobody knows ahead of time which way of living **suits which person**. So let people try different ways, as long as they **don\u2019t harm others**. You learn from their results too.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A friend plans to leave **a secure job** at forty to train as a carpenter. The sensible advice writes itself. You\u2019re already drafting it. But **what do you know** about what suits them?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Mill\u2019s case for individuality isn\u2019t that self-expression feels nice. It\u2019s about **knowledge**. Nobody knows in advance which arrangements produce good lives. The only evidence comes from **people trying** them and the results being seen.'},{kind:'explain',text:'So he wanted different ways of life **permitted**, not merely tolerated. Suppress the variation and you **suppress the data**. Even a failed experiment teaches something, and the person who ran it paid the cost.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The secure job may suit your friend, or it may just be **common**. Those aren\u2019t the same. Before you give the sensible advice, check **which one you know**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'At the front of the essay, Mill set a passage from the German thinker **Wilhelm von Humboldt**. It praised people growing in **every possible variety**. Why open a book on liberty that way?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Because his case rests on it. Mill sets **a machine** against **a tree**. A machine is made from a blueprint to do one fixed job. A tree spreads out in all directions, driven by its own life. Human nature, he says, is the tree.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a town where everyone takes the same job, marries at the same age, lives the same way. It looks like proof that this way works. But nobody was ever allowed to try another, so it proves nothing.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now your friend leaves a secure job at forty for carpentry. If it fails, she\u2019s paid for the lesson herself. If it works, you\u2019ve learned a life like that can work. Either way, the data exists.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The protection holds only for conduct that concerns **yourself**, and few experiments stay inside that line. A household\u2019s experiment shapes its children. Results are slow and noisy, so a failure may not be one.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A society that forbids the attempt, then points to **the missing alternatives**, has made up its own evidence. Variation is **how we find out**. Failures are part of the data.'}]},'on-liberty_7':{title:'Who Gets Left Out',card:{insight:'When a rule leaves some people out, see who they are and ask why.',tryThis:'Next time you read a rule, look for who it leaves out.',skills:['thinking','history']},review:{prompt:'A company\u2019s fair pay rule leaves out the bosses. How do you read it?',right:'Treat who it leaves out as part of the rule',wrong:'Ignore it, since it is only a small note',why:'Who a rule leaves out is part of the rule, not a side note.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your class makes a rule: **everyone** gets a turn on the swings. Then the oldest kids add a line at the end. *Except the little ones.* Is it still **a rule for everyone**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'John Stuart Mill said grown-ups should be free to run their own lives. But he **left some people out**: children, and people in countries he called **backward**. He worked for a company that ruled much of India.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When a rule leaves people out, **look hard** at who, and why. You don\u2019t have to throw the rule away. You can keep **the good part** and say plainly where it\u2019s wrong.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'The most famous defence of liberty has **a paragraph** near the start. It quietly excludes **most of the people** alive when it was written. Would you call that a footnote?'},{kind:'story',text:'The year before *On Liberty* appeared, Mill retired from the East India Company. It governed much of India from London. He\u2019d worked in its head office for some thirty-five years, drafting many of its official dispatches.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The essay exempts a great deal from its own principle. It isn\u2019t for children, nor for what Mill calls **backward states of society**. That carve-out does **real work**: it puts whole peoples outside the protection.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'There are **two ways** you can read the exemption. Only one is **honest**.',left:{title:'As a footnote',items:['A product of its time','Skip it, keep the rest']},right:{title:'As evidence',items:['Part of the argument','Ask what it reveals']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t discard the essay, and don\u2019t skip the clause. Hold **the argument** and **the exemption** in view together. That\u2019s how Mill said contested truths should be read.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'In 1823, aged seventeen, Mill joined **the East India Company\u2019s** London office. In 1859 he published a famous defence of **liberty**. Where do you think the people the Company ruled fit in?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The principle, Mill says, applies only to people **in their maturity**. It excludes children. It also excludes societies he judged not yet able to improve **through free discussion**. For those, he allowed despotism, if it aimed at their improvement.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take a man who ruins himself, leaving his family to suffer. Mill admits that harms them. His answer is to punish the broken duty to his family, not the self-ruin. It\u2019s a careful move, and a telling one.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now the second. Mill spent some thirty-five years at the Company, drafting many of its dispatches. He retired in 1858, as the Crown took over its rule. A year later, the essay set whole peoples outside its protection.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**, for the critique too. The exemption for children is widely accepted. So the problem isn\u2019t exempting as such. It\u2019s **who decides** maturity, and on what evidence.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The argument survives the objection, **weaker and more honest**. That\u2019s Mill\u2019s own method turned on him. When you read any principle, read **who it leaves out** as part of it.'}]},'on-liberty_8':{title:'Who Knows Your Life Best',card:{insight:'You know your own life best, and you are the one who lives with it.',tryThis:'Before you push on someone\u2019s own choice, ask what you know that they do not.',skills:['relationships','thinking']},review:{prompt:'Your brother wants to leave a steady job to open a coffee shop. Now what?',right:'Ask what you know about his life that he does not',wrong:'Talk him out of it before he loses it all',why:'He knows his own life best and lives with it, so share facts, not orders.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your grown-up cousin wants to leave college and **train as a chef**. Her parents think it\u2019s a mistake. Who knows most about what she loves? And who will **live with the choice**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'John Stuart Mill said people often **choose badly**. But the person who steps in to choose for them **knows less**, and cares less. So they\u2019re no more likely to get it right, and they don\u2019t pay the price.'},{kind:'moral',text:'In your own life, you usually know the most and care the most. You live with what happens. So when it\u2019s someone else\u2019s life, **share what you know**, then **let them choose**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your brother wants to leave **a stable job** to open a caf\xe9. You\u2019re sure it\u2019s a mistake. You\u2019ve got the numbers ready. But who here is actually **better informed**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Mill\u2019s case against paternalism is about **evidence**, not manners. Your brother knows most about his situation, cares most about the outcome, and **bears the consequence**. You\u2019re working from general ideas about people like him.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Mill grants that people often choose badly. His claim is **comparative**. The person overriding them knows less and cares less, so there\u2019s no reason to expect **fewer errors**. And their errors land on someone with no say.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Before you override a choice about **someone\u2019s own life**, ask what you know that they don\u2019t. If the answer\u2019s nothing, **say your piece once**, then stop.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Society has a small, secondhand stake in how your life goes. You have the biggest one. Mill built an argument on **that gap**. What happens when someone **ignores it**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Interference runs on **broad assumptions**: what people like this usually want. These are often wrong. Even when they\u2019re right on average, they can miss **the person in front** of you. So outsiders meddling in private choices tend to get it wrong.'},{kind:'story',text:'Your brother plans a caf\xe9. You know caf\xe9s often fail. That\u2019s a presumption about caf\xe9s, not about him. He knows the street, the rent, his savings and how badly he wants it. You know an average.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a buyer asks for poison. Mill doesn\u2019t ban the sale. But the seller may have to label it, so the buyer knows what they\u2019re choosing. The aim is an informed choice, not a different one.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The argument assumes you have the facts and the capacity to use them. **Addiction**, compulsion and manipulation strain both. And supplying a fact versus steering a choice is blurrier in practice.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Mill doesn\u2019t claim people **choose well**. He claims the overrider is worse informed and less invested. So **supply the missing fact**, and leave the choice where it belongs.'}]},'on-liberty_9':{title:'Check the Result',card:{insight:'You can ask for a result without telling people how to get it.',tryThis:'When you need good work, write down the goal and how you will check it.',skills:['leadership','thinking']},review:{prompt:'You want your team\u2019s reports to improve. What do you set?',right:'The goal and a way to check it',wrong:'One form that everyone must use',why:'Set the goal and the check, and let people find their own way.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'To join the swim team, you must swim **two lengths**. The coach doesn\u2019t care whether a club, your mum or a summer lake taught you. So what is the coach **really checking**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'John Stuart Mill wanted the law to make sure **every child learns**. But he didn\u2019t want the government running all the schools. If one group ran every school, children might all **come out alike**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Mill\u2019s plan was **many kinds of school** and one test. Check what someone can do, not who taught them. That way you get the result **without the sameness**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Some people cite Mill as **against** compulsory schooling. Others cite him as a champion of state schools. Both camps are confident. Which of them has **read him right**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Neither. Mill wanted the law to **require** that every child be taught. He was against the state **doing all the teaching** itself. The state could compel, test by public exam and pay where families couldn\u2019t.'},{kind:'explain',text:'His reason was **uniformity**. One state system would press everyone into the same shape. And the shape would be set by whoever held power. Same teaching, same minds: **a tyranny of thought**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Think of your own team. You want reports that are clear. You could impose one template and one process. Or you could set the standard, check each report against it, and let people find their own way.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Requiring a result and **owning the machinery** are separate decisions. Set the **outcome and the test**. Owning the method is a larger power, so take it only on purpose.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture Mill, the great defender of liberty, asked about schooling. Should the law **force** every child to be taught? And should the state run **the schools**? What would you guess he said to each?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Yes to the first, and **mostly no** to the second. Mandate the outcome, test it publicly, fund it where needed, and **leave the teaching plural**. A state school could exist, but only as one experiment among many.'},{kind:'explain',text:'His worry was **who sets the mould**. Whoever held power, a king, a church, an old elite or the voting majority, would shape every mind. One national system of teaching, he argued, was **a factory for sameness**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Say you run a team and need reports a client can trust. You set the standard and check every report against it. How each person gets there is theirs. Some methods will turn out better, and everyone sees which.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture the opposite. Head office issues one template and one process for every team. Reports get more alike. So do the people writing them. Nobody can tell any more whether a better way existed.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Exams bring their own sameness: whoever sets the test **shapes the teaching**. Plural provision can deepen inequality where families can\u2019t judge or reach good schools. Mill himself allowed state schools where nothing else existed.'},{kind:'moral',text:'This is experiments in living, **applied to schooling**. Require the result and make it visible. Whether it would work is arguable, but **it follows** from everything else he believed.'}]}}}},981,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'on-the-shortness-of-life',kind:'book',promise:'Take back the hours you give away',about:'What if life only feels short because we waste it? Seneca was a thinker in Rome almost 2,000 years ago. He wrote this for a man who ran Rome\u2019s grain supply. Here are his best ideas about time. Where will you start?',ideas:{'on-the-shortness-of-life_1':{title:'Life Is Long Enough',card:{insight:'Life is long enough, but we waste it in small bits we never count.',tryThis:'Before bed, add up the minutes you lost today in small bits. Then pick one to win back.',skills:['habits','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'A friend says she would love to learn piano but has no time. What first?',right:'Add up where the hours of her week go',wrong:'Agree that some lives are too full for hobbies',why:'Most weeks have free hours that slip away in small bits.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You keep your pocket money in **a locked box**. If a kid at school asked for some, you\u2019d say no. But when someone wants an hour of **your time**, you just hand it over. Why?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Seneca was a thinker in Rome almost 2,000 years ago. He listed where people\u2019s time goes. Nothing on his list was big: **too much sleep**, showing off, jealousy, changing your mind. **Small leaks** add up, and you never see them go.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Life isn\u2019t **too short**. It feels short because we **give it away** in tiny bits. Guard your hours the way you guard your money.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You say you\u2019d read more, cook more, see friends more, if only there were **more hours**. Then the week ends and you can\u2019t say **where it went**. Sound familiar?'},{kind:'story',text:'Seneca, writing in Rome, compared a life to an estate. A careful manager makes a modest income go a long way. A spendthrift burns through a royal fortune in no time. Nobody says the spendthrift was underpaid.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Then he lists where the time actually goes. Drink, sleep, flattery, envy, **aimless study**. None of it is a disaster. Each item is ordinary and easy to defend. That\u2019s why **the total** of your hours never gets noticed.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You\u2019re not **short of time**. You\u2019re careless with it, in amounts too small to count. So look at **where the hours go** before you blame the clock.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Seneca advised the emperor Nero and grew very rich. He wrote this essay to Paulinus, who ran **Rome\u2019s grain supply**. It opens by attacking the most common complaint there is. Can you guess **which one**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The complaint is that **life is too short**. Seneca turns it round. The amount of time was adequate, he argues. The shortness was **manufactured** by the person living it. It\u2019s a report on spending, not on supply.'},{kind:'story',text:'Two people inherit the same sum. One spends with care, and it lasts for years. The other buys a little here and a little there, and it\u2019s gone by spring. Nobody blames the size of the gift.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take one ordinary Tuesday. Ten minutes on a call you didn\u2019t need. Twenty scrolling after lunch. Half an hour in a meeting where you said nothing. An hour replaying an argument. Together, that\u2019s about two hours.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Seneca wrote as a rich man to a rich friend. For someone working two jobs or caring for a sick parent, the hours aren\u2019t wasted. They\u2019re **taken**. And time given to people you love isn\u2019t lost.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Most lives aren\u2019t short. They\u2019re **spent small**, in leaks too ordinary to notice. Add yours up once, honestly, and the clock stops being **the culprit**.'}]},'on-the-shortness-of-life_2':{title:'The Busy Ones',card:{insight:'A busy week may not be yours, so count the hours you chose.',tryThis:'Tonight, mark each hour of today that you picked yourself. See how few there are.',skills:['productivity','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'Your week was full of meetings and errands. How do you judge it?',right:'Count how many of those hours you chose',wrong:'Count how much got done and feel proud',why:'A full week is only yours if you picked how the hours went.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Sam has football on Monday, piano on Tuesday and extra maths every other night. He\u2019s **never bored**. He\u2019s always rushing somewhere. But is Sam living **his own week**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Seneca, a thinker in Rome almost 2,000 years ago, pictured an old man adding up his life. He\u2019d been busy every day. But when he counted, very few years had been his own. The rest went on other people\u2019s plans.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Being busy isn\u2019t the problem. The question is **whose plan** your busy time belongs to. If **you chose it**, it\u2019s really yours.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your calendar is booked in **half-hour blocks**, nearly all of them set by other people. By Friday you\u2019re exhausted. Someone asks what you did that was yours. You **can\u2019t name** one thing.'},{kind:'story',text:'Seneca had a word for people like this: the **occupied**. Their days belonged to clients, patrons, lawsuits and social duty. They weren\u2019t lazy. They were the opposite. They were always about to start living, once this busy season ended.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Mark each block of your week by **who chose it**, and see what\u2019s **actually yours**.',left:{title:'Chosen',items:['The run you planned','Reading you picked yourself','An evening you kept free']},right:{title:'Assigned',items:['Meetings set by others','Errands you never questioned','Calls you said yes to']}},{kind:'moral',text:'A full diary **isn\u2019t a full life**. The measure is how many of your hours **you actually chose**. Start by counting them.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Seneca imagines an old man asked to **account for** his years. How many went to creditors, patrons, clients, quarrels and errands across the city? Subtract them all. How old is he **really**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The answer is **far fewer years** than his age suggests. He wasn\u2019t idle. He was industrious, and almost none of it was his. The trap is that **effort feels like progress**, so nobody stops to ask where it\u2019s going.'},{kind:'story',text:'Priya runs a busy team. Her diary is booked by others from nine to six, and she answers email at night. She means to plan the team\u2019s next year. It waits, every week, for a quieter month.'},{kind:'story',text:'Tom\u2019s weekends are packed: his kids\u2019 matches, a friend\u2019s move, a cousin\u2019s party. Each yes is kind. In December he realises he hasn\u2019t had a single Saturday that was his. Nothing went wrong. Nothing was chosen either.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Chosen hours are a luxury. Most people\u2019s days are assigned by work and care, and Seneca wrote to men rich enough to retire. And a duty you freely accept can be **the most yours** of all.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Activity **isn\u2019t life**. The occupied give their days to other people\u2019s purposes and promise themselves a start later. Ask **whose purpose** your hours serve, before the later runs out.'}]},'on-the-shortness-of-life_3':{title:'Later Is Not Promised',card:{insight:'Waiting for later costs years, and no one promised you more.',tryThis:'Write down each plan you said you would start when things calm down. Pick a date for one.',skills:['thinking','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'You planned to start running when work calmed down. That was two years ago. Now?',right:'Pick a date this month and start a little',wrong:'Wait for a quiet month, when you will have more energy',why:'Things rarely get calm, so the plan keeps moving to next year.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve got your coat on and your bag on your lap. **The door** is right there. You\u2019re going to leave **any minute now**. An hour later, you\u2019re still sitting there. What happened?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Saying *I\u2019ll start later* feels free. Nothing goes wrong, and nothing gets wasted that you can see. But **later isn\u2019t promised** to anyone. Seneca, a thinker in Rome long ago, saw people plan to **really live** only once they were old.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Putting things off is **the quiet way** time slips by. Later is rarely calmer. So **start a little** now, while the door is still right there.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ll start properly **after this project**: the running, the course, the book. You said the same about the last project, and the one before. Each time, the reason was **completely true**. So why does it feel wrong?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A bad day leaves a mark. A postponed thing **leaves nothing**. No hour was visibly wasted, and nothing was done badly. It just wasn\u2019t done, on the understanding that it would be later. That\u2019s why it **slips past you**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Seneca\u2019s remedy isn\u2019t panic. It\u2019s noticing **one clause** that has been **running your plans**.',left:{title:'Urgency',items:['Life is short, cram it in','Guilt about every idle hour']},right:{title:'Noticing',items:['When things calm down keeps renewing','Next year offers the same excuse']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Postponement **spends time** without seeming to spend anything. It draws on **a future** nobody has promised you. Don\u2019t wait for calm to start.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Seneca mocks men who plan to retire into **a better life** at fifty or sixty. They\u2019ve worked it all out. There\u2019s just one question he keeps asking them. Who **guaranteed them** those years?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Most waste is at least **visible**: an afternoon frittered, a night lost to drink. Postponement leaves no trace. It\u2019s the one habit that **spends time** while appearing to save it. That\u2019s why he makes it the essay\u2019s central target.'},{kind:'story',text:'Ana has wanted to learn Spanish since university. First it was exams, then a new job, then a move. Each reason was real. Twelve years on, she still means to start once things are quieter.'},{kind:'story',text:'A couple plan the long trip for when the mortgage is paid. They save, they wait, they talk about it every winter. The mortgage is paid at sixty-three. By then, one of them can\u2019t manage the flights.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some delay is just **good judgement**: you save before you buy, and train before you race. The target is the open-ended later, tied to no date. Pushed too far, the idea turns into recklessness or guilt.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Every plan to begin later is **a loan** on time you can\u2019t see. Nobody has shown you **the balance**. So stop letting one quiet clause run your years.'}]},'on-the-shortness-of-life_4':{title:'Learn From the Wise',card:{insight:'You get one life, but books add the years wise people spent.',tryThis:'Name one problem you keep coming back to. Look up one writer who spent years on it.',skills:['thinking','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'A new boss has to tell a worker some hard news. What helps first?',right:'Read a book by someone who spent years on it',wrong:'Keep trying new ways until one of them works',why:'On your own it takes years, but they already spent those years.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You and your friends have argued for **two weeks** about how to build the best den. Then you find a book by someone who built dens for **thirty years**. What\u2019s that book worth?'},{kind:'explain',text:'In one evening, you learn what took them **a lifetime**. Seneca, a thinker in Rome long ago, said you can\u2019t choose the family you\u2019re born into. But you can choose **wise people** from the past. None of them will say no.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You only get **one life** to learn from. Reading adds **other people\u2019s years** to yours. It\u2019s the cheapest way to grow your life.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your team has argued for a month about **how to give feedback**. Somebody spent thirty years on that exact question and wrote it down. What would their thinking **cost you**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Seneca frames it as **adoption**, not inheritance. You can\u2019t pick the family you were born into. You can adopt yourself into the family of **great minds**. Nobody refuses you, and nothing has to be paid back.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Reason from **your own life** alone, or from what **the many** spent years working out.',left:{title:'One life',items:['One experiment, run once','No comparison to check against']},right:{title:'Many lives',items:['Hundreds of finished experiments','Including the ones that ended badly']}},{kind:'moral',text:'You won\u2019t get **more years**. You\u2019ll get **more lives** to reason from, for the price of an evening. Read someone who spent a lifetime on your question.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'After pages attacking the busy, Seneca finally names the people who are **truly living**. It isn\u2019t the powerful or the rich. It\u2019s a group you could join **this evening**. Who are they?'},{kind:'explain',text:'His answer: those who make time for **philosophy**. They alone are at leisure, he argues, because they add **every past age** to their own. The busy have no time to look back. The reader has all of it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Leo is about to become a father and feels lost. He could work it all out alone, one sleepless mistake at a time. Instead he spends an evening reading someone who spent forty years thinking about raising children.'},{kind:'story',text:'You\u2019re weighing whether to leave a safe job. Your own experience is one career, lived once. A few evenings with good thinkers give you several finished lives. Some of them ended badly. That\u2019s useful too.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Seneca meant philosophy, not reading in general. He scorned scholars who spent leisure on trivia, like how many rowers Ulysses had. Reading can become **one more busyness**. And the dead have blind spots too.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The years you live are **your own**. The years others spent thinking are **open to anyone**, and free. Treat their finished lives as cases to learn from, not verdicts.'}]},'on-the-shortness-of-life_5':{title:'Free Time With a Plan',card:{insight:'An empty day fills with your phone, so decide what free time is for.',tryThis:'Pick tonight\u2019s free hour. Before it starts, write one line on what it is for.',skills:['focus','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'At the end of a free Sunday, how can you tell it was well spent?',right:'I can say what that free time was for',wrong:'I got enough rest to feel good on Monday',why:'If you can say what the time was for, you chose it.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Saturday afternoon is **totally free**. No homework, no plans. You flop onto the sofa. Three hours later you get up, and you **feel no different**. Where did that afternoon go?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Seneca, a thinker in Rome long ago, said free time comes in two kinds. One is **doing nothing**, which just fills the gap. The other is time you **decide how to use** before it starts.'},{kind:'moral',text:'If you can say what your afternoon was for, it was **real free time**. If you can\u2019t, it **just drifted**. So decide first.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You finally clear **a whole Saturday**, with no plans at all. By evening it\u2019s gone: your phone, a nap, some tidying, a show you half watched. You feel no different. Did you **fail at resting**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Seneca says no. You had **the wrong kind** of free time. An empty hour doesn\u2019t stay empty, because it fills with whatever is nearest. In Rome that was the games, grooming and **other people\u2019s opinions**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Two hours can look identical from outside. The difference is **a decision**, made **in advance**.',left:{title:'Idleness',items:['An empty slot','Fills with whatever\u2019s nearest','Can\u2019t say what it was for']},right:{title:'Leisure',items:['A slot with a purpose','Decided before it starts','Can say what it was for']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Leisure isn\u2019t **the absence of work**. It\u2019s time turned, on purpose, to something you chose. Ask yourself **what it was for**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Seneca is sharpest on people who **look** like they\u2019re at leisure. Their days are free of work. They spend them on their hair, their collections, the games. Why does he count them **among the busy**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Because idleness isn\u2019t leisure. Idleness is **a vacancy**. Leisure is a decision, made in advance, about what the vacancy is for. By his account it\u2019s **harder than work**, because nobody else is setting the task.'},{kind:'story',text:'Mark takes a week off work. He sleeps in, scrolls, fixes a shelf, watches a series. On Monday he\u2019s rested, but he can\u2019t say what the week was for. It was a vacancy, and it filled.'},{kind:'story',text:'Jen keeps one evening free each week. Before it starts she writes a single question to think about: where her work is heading. Some evenings she gets nowhere. She can still say **what it was for**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Seneca\u2019s leisure assumed a household run by others, many of them **enslaved**. After a double shift, rest isn\u2019t a failure. It\u2019s often what makes leisure possible. Treat this as a direction for some free time.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Rest **restores you**. Leisure is where you decide **what you\u2019re restored for**. Make that decision before the hour begins, or the hour will make it for you.'}]},'on-the-shortness-of-life_6':{title:'Time Has No Price Tag',card:{insight:'Lost money leaves a gap you can see, but lost time does not.',tryThis:'Before your next yes today, say its cost in minutes to yourself. Then decide.',skills:['productivity','psychology']},review:{prompt:'A coworker asks you to sit in on a meeting. What do you do first?',right:'Say its cost to yourself in minutes, then decide',wrong:'Say yes right away, since it would be rude not to',why:'Once you see the cost in minutes, your yes becomes a real choice.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Someone takes **a coin** from your desk. You\u2019d notice in a second. Someone takes **an hour** from your Saturday, ten minutes at a time. Would you notice that?'},{kind:'story',text:'A game asks if you want one more level. You tap yes, then again, then again. Each yes felt free. But the hour you meant for building your model has quietly gone.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Money leaves **a gap** when it goes. Time doesn\u2019t, so asking for it never feels like much. **Say the minutes** before you say yes.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019d notice instantly if **forty pounds** went missing from your wallet. You\u2019d check every pocket. But an hour went missing this morning, in favours and small chats. Did you **even look** for it?'},{kind:'story',text:'Seneca watched Romans go to court over **a boundary** and fight over a strip of land. The same people let anyone walk into their days. Nobody would hand an estate to a passer-by. Everyone hands over **their time**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The reason is **perception**, not character. Money declines where you can see it. A wasted afternoon leaves **no mark** on any account. So a request for half an hour feels like a social move, not a transaction.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Time has **no visible balance**, so you give it away without counting. **Make the cost visible**. Say the minutes to yourself first, then decide.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture a person counting coins at a table, **every single one**. Behind them, someone walks out of the door carrying **a clock\u2019s hands**. The counter never looks up. What\u2019s being stolen, and why doesn\u2019t anyone notice?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Seneca says people are **thrifty with possessions** and lavish with the one thing where thrift would be a virtue. The failure isn\u2019t mainly moral. It\u2019s about **what you can see**. Attention follows the visible, not the valuable.'},{kind:'story',text:'A colleague asks you to sit in on a meeting. Before answering, you price it: forty minutes, out of the four good hours you have today. Put that way, it\u2019s a real request. Sometimes you still say yes.'},{kind:'story',text:'A neighbour asks for help with a quick job on Saturday. You say the cost first: probably the whole morning. You still help, gladly. But now it\u2019s a gift you chose, not an hour that leaked away.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Pricing every request can turn generosity into **bookkeeping**. Friendship and care don\u2019t run well as transactions. Cost every favour and you may keep your hours but lose the people you\u2019d have spent them on.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The fix is **visibility**, not refusal. Say the price, then choose. Many requests survive the pricing, and that\u2019s fine, because now they\u2019re **real choices**.'}]},'on-the-shortness-of-life_7':{title:'The Ship in the Storm',card:{insight:'Busy days can feel like moving ahead, so ask what has changed.',tryThis:'Tonight, write one thing that is different now than a year ago. If nothing is, pick one to change.',skills:['thinking','productivity']},review:{prompt:'Your year felt very full. How do you check if it went anywhere?',right:'Ask what is different now than a year ago',wrong:'Add up how many things you got through',why:'Like a ship in a storm, you can move a lot and go nowhere.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A hamster runs on its wheel **all night**. It runs hard, and it gets tired. In the morning, where is it? In **exactly the same spot**. Does that sound like any of your days?'},{kind:'story',text:'Seneca, a thinker in Rome long ago, pictured a ship caught by a storm as it leaves harbour. It\u2019s blown round in circles. There\u2019s lots of motion, but no trip. Busy days feel full, so you never ask if you moved.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Being busy can **feel like getting somewhere**. The real test is simple: **what\u2019s different now**? If you can\u2019t name one thing, you were only spinning.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Someone says the year has **gone quickly**, and you agree. Now try to account for March. You were busy every day of it. What was different **at the end**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Seneca\u2019s image is a ship caught by a storm **leaving harbour**. It\u2019s driven round in circles, all motion and no voyage. Busyness works the same way. It produces **the feeling** of a full life, all day long.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Review the same month **two ways**, and see which list **tells the truth**.',left:{title:'What you did',items:['Forty meetings','Two hundred emails','Three trips']},right:{title:'What\u2019s different',items:['You run meetings without notes','One habit actually stuck']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Activity **isn\u2019t a voyage**. The idle at least know they\u2019re idle, but busyness defends itself. So judge a month by **what\u2019s different**, not by what you did.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Seneca warns against judging a life by **grey hair** and wrinkles. Someone can be around a long time, he says, without **living** much of it. What\u2019s the difference, and how would you tell?'},{kind:'explain',text:'His test looks back, and it\u2019s blunt. **What\u2019s different now** that wasn\u2019t before? If a summer gives no answer, the problem isn\u2019t that you lay down. You were almost certainly busy. **Busyness was the output**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Dan spends a year in back-to-back projects. He travels, presents, answers every message. In December his manager asks what he can do now that he couldn\u2019t in January. Dan opens his mouth and finds nothing there.'},{kind:'story',text:'Lena\u2019s year is quieter. She writes a little each morning and drops one commitment. At the end she can name two things that changed. She did less than Dan, and she got further.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Not every good year changes something. Caring for a dying parent can leave everything the same. There, **the sameness** is the achievement. The test catches drift, not duty.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Busyness **hides itself** by feeling full all day. Ask what\u2019s different while it\u2019s still early enough to change course. Later, **the account** is harder to settle.'}]},'on-the-shortness-of-life_8':{title:'Time at the Top',card:{insight:'You can earn money back, but not time, so count the time first.',tryThis:'Take one job or offer you are weighing. Write its time cost at the top, above the pay.',skills:['money','thinking']},review:{prompt:'A side job pays well but takes your evenings for six months. What first?',right:'Ask if six months of evenings is worth the pay',wrong:'Check if the pay is high enough for the work',why:'You can earn more money, but you cannot get those nights back.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your neighbour offers you **\xa35** every time you walk her dog. The catch is that it\u2019s every Saturday morning, **all summer long**. \xa35 sounds great. But what about the Saturdays?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Seneca, a thinker in Rome long ago, noticed something odd. People count **money**, effort and fuss. They leave time out. But you can earn money again. You can **never get back** last Saturday.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When you decide anything, **count the time first**. Money and effort can come back later. **Time you spend** never comes back, so it\u2019s worth the most.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A project pays **really well**. It\u2019ll also take most of your evenings for six months. You compare the fee with the effort, and it looks like **an easy yes**. Is that the right sum?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Seneca noticed an **odd ordering**. People prize salaries, gifts and favours, and hire out their labour to get them. The time they pay with is treated as **free**, because nobody can see it. The scarcest input gets left out.'},{kind:'story',text:'So you do the sum the other way round. At the top of the page you write **six months of evenings**: family, training, sleep. Only underneath do you write the fee. Suddenly the easy yes is a real question.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Everything else you lose can be **replaced**. Time can\u2019t. So put it **at the top** of the sum, and make the money justify itself.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'You can borrow money, rebuild a reputation and buy **another house**. Try to think of a shop that sells **last Tuesday**. There isn\u2019t one. So why does time come **last** in most sums?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Whatever goes first **anchors** the rest. A large, clear fee makes scattered evenings look small. They stay invisible until they\u2019re spent. Start from the time instead, and the fee becomes **the number that justifies** itself.'},{kind:'story',text:'A friend is offered a second job. It pays well and takes every Saturday for a year. Fee first, it\u2019s obvious. Time first, it\u2019s fifty-two Saturdays of her life. She might still take it, knowing the price.'},{kind:'story',text:'You\u2019re choosing between two builders for the kitchen. One is cheaper but needs eight weekends of your help. The other costs more and needs none. Price the weekends first, and the cheaper quote stops looking cheap.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Time isn\u2019t always the scarcest input. If you\u2019re in debt, money now can **buy back time** later. Hours differ too: a drained evening isn\u2019t an evening with your children. This corrects a neglect. It isn\u2019t a law.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Seneca isn\u2019t arguing **against work**. He\u2019s arguing about order. Put the input you can never recover **at the top**, and let the money explain itself.'}]},'on-the-shortness-of-life_9':{title:'The Smallest Real Version',card:{insight:'The right time to start rarely comes, so start small with what you have.',tryThis:'Pick something you keep waiting to start. Do the smallest real version of it today.',skills:['habits','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'You want to write a novel but are waiting for a free year. Now what?',right:'Write the smallest real version with the life you have now',wrong:'Keep saving until you can take the whole year off',why:'There will be good reasons to wait next year too.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You and a friend want to **start a band**. But you\u2019ll wait. Wait for a real drum kit. Wait till you\u2019re older. Wait till there\u2019s more time. When will **the band** actually start?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Seneca, a thinker in Rome long ago, saw people plan to start living **when they were old**. Many never got there. Your reasons to wait might be good. But next year there\u2019ll be **new good reasons**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t wait for **the perfect moment**. Nobody knows how much time they have. Start **a small version** now, with whatever you\u2019ve got.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ll start the business once the children are at school. It\u2019s **a good plan**. The money isn\u2019t there, and neither are the hours. You\u2019re right about both. So **when** does it begin?'},{kind:'story',text:'Then the children start school. The case for waiting is just as good, for brand new reasons. A tighter budget, a demanding boss, a tired winter. The plan moves again, and nobody decided to move it.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Seneca doesn\u2019t say the reasons are **excuses**. He grants they\u2019re often sound. His claim is narrower: the same sound reasoning will be there next year, and **it will be used**. Waiting becomes a policy, not a decision.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Stop asking if conditions are right. Ask what **the smallest real version** looks like under the conditions you have. A business becomes **one paying customer**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'It\u2019s dark, the path is rough, and nobody would call it **good digging weather**. A spade is leaning by the gate. You could wait for a brighter morning. Or could you **dig once**, now?'},{kind:'explain',text:'This is **the strongest form** of Seneca\u2019s argument, because it concedes everything. The conditions really are poor. He doesn\u2019t dispute that. He treats waiting for them as a policy, and a policy runs until **something stops it**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Someone has wanted to write a novel for ten years. They\u2019re waiting until they can take a year off. Under the life they actually have, the novel looks different. It looks like one page before breakfast.'},{kind:'story',text:'Kai wants to make films but has no camera. He could save for two years. Instead he shoots a two-minute film on an old phone this weekend. It\u2019s rough, but it\u2019s real, and it\u2019s started.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some things truly need conditions: medical training, a move abroad, a child. Starting badly can cost more than **waiting well**. And when money or illness forces a delay, the argument can harden into blame.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Postponement draws on **a future** nobody has guaranteed. The reasons may be sound every single year. So start **the first spadeful** under the conditions you actually have.'}]}}}},982,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'origin-of-species',kind:'book',promise:'Help people hear out a hard idea',about:'How do you share an idea that people are ready to hate? Charles Darwin had that problem in 1859. His book said living things change over time. These are the ways he made his case. Which one will you try first?',ideas:{'origin-of-species_1':{title:'Start on Common Ground',card:{insight:'Start with what people believe, then take them one small step at a time.',tryThis:'Before a big ask, write down two things you both agree on.',skills:['communication','thinking']},review:{prompt:'You want your team to drop a weekly meeting. How do you start?',right:'Start with what the team already says about it',wrong:'Open with your plan to cancel the meeting',why:'When you start where they agree, your idea looks like one small step.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You want to stay up later on Fridays. You ask, and your mum says no before you\u2019ve even finished. Now she\u2019s **guarding her answer**. Is she still **hearing yours**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Charles Darwin knew many readers would hate his idea. So he began with **pigeons**. Everyone agreed a keeper could choose which birds had chicks. Then he asked: what if **survival does the choosing**? That was one small step.'},{kind:'moral',text:'To win someone round, start with **what they believe**. Then show your idea is just **one more step**. It\u2019s much easier to walk than to jump.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You want your team to scrap a weekly meeting. You open with **your proposal**. Within a minute, everyone\u2019s defending it, even people who hate it. What went wrong in **that first sentence**?'},{kind:'story',text:'The book that changed biology doesn\u2019t open on wild animals. Its first chapter is about tame ones: pigeons, dogs, cattle and cabbages. Darwin bred fancy pigeons himself. Every farming reader knew breeders reshape animals by choosing which ones breed.'},{kind:'explain',text:'By the time **natural selection** arrives, readers have granted two things. Selection changes animals. Small changes add up. The only new claim is **who does the selecting**: survival instead of a farmer. The big step looks small.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Back at the meeting, compare **conclusion first** with **common ground first**.',left:{title:'Conclusion first',items:['People brace at once','Every point sounds hostile','You argue uphill']},right:{title:'Common ground first',items:['Start with what everyone already says','They nod before you propose','Cancelling looks like the next step']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t open with the conclusion people will resist. Start on **the ground** they already stand on, then extend it **one step** at a time.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'London, 1859. Darwin expects much of his audience to resist **his theory**. You\u2019d guess he\u2019d open with his strongest evidence. Instead chapter one is about **farm animals**. Why start somewhere so ordinary?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The method is to set up the mechanism on **ground already conceded**. Show how far it goes there. Only then extend it. By the time natural selection appears, you\u2019ve agreed to everything except **who holds the pen**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Darwin leans on what he calls unconscious selection. Each breeder keeps the best animals, with no grand plan. Over generations the breed still shifts. So lasting change with **no one intending it** is already granted before nature enters.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a council that doubts a cycle lane. You don\u2019t start with accident figures. You remind them they\u2019ve already approved safer crossings near schools. The lane becomes **one more step** along a road they chose.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The method persuades just as well when the extension is false. Start on agreed ground, then slip in a step that **doesn\u2019t follow**. Even Darwin\u2019s analogy strained, because a breeder chooses and nature doesn\u2019t.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Shared ground earns **a hearing**, not a conclusion. Open where your audience already stands. Then make sure **every step** you add really follows from the last.'}]},'origin-of-species_2':{title:'Four Plain Facts',card:{insight:'The best answer often rests on a few plain facts anyone can check.',tryThis:'Next time something breaks, write the plain facts first. Then test the answer that needs the fewest guesses.',skills:['thinking','science']},review:{prompt:'Sales at your store fall. Which answer should you check first?',right:'The one built on plain facts anyone can check',wrong:'The big one that explains every twist at once',why:'An answer built on plain facts is the hardest one to knock down.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A frog lays **a huge clump** of eggs in a pond. Hundreds of tiny tadpoles hatch. Come summer, how many of them do you think will **grow into frogs**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Only a few. Too many are born for all to live. No two are quite alike. Young ones **take after** their parents. And some differences help, like **swimming faster**. Put those four facts together and the pond slowly changes.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Each fact is **easy to check**. Nobody planned the change, and nobody had to. It\u2019s what **four plain facts** do when you put them together.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You run a small shop, and sales fall. One theory needs **a secret campaign**, a supplier plot and a change in taste. Another needs **two facts** from the till. Which do you check first?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Darwin\u2019s theory needs four facts. Living things **vary**. Some of that variation is **inherited**. Some of it helps survival. And more are born than can survive. Given those, change follows with no extra force and no purpose.'},{kind:'story',text:'The last fact came from an economist. In 1838 Darwin read Thomas Malthus on population, who argued people multiply faster than their food. Darwin saw the same sum applies to every species. He credits Malthus in the book.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s why it\u2019s strong. Each fact could be checked **on any farm**. So to reject the theory, a critic had to deny **a plain truth**. Back at your shop, the till facts win for the same reason.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A strong explanation **assumes little**. Start from facts **nobody disputes**, and you\u2019ll be far harder to knock down than the theory full of guesses.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Darwin picked **the slowest breeder** he knew of: the elephant. He worked out how many descendants one pair would leave if every calf lived. What do you think **the count** came to?'},{kind:'story',text:'Within some centuries, millions. Yet elephants aren\u2019t multiplying like that. So most must fail to breed, whatever anyone intends. Darwin\u2019s struggle for existence isn\u2019t a claim that nature is cruel. It\u2019s **arithmetic**, and it applies to every species.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Add three more plain facts. Offspring **vary**. Some variation is **inherited**. Some of it helps. Together with overproduction, they make differential survival unavoidable. No new force, no purpose. To reject it, you must deny something any farmer could see.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a car that won\u2019t start on a cold morning. One mechanic suspects a rare electrical fault. Another checks two plain facts first: the battery is old, and it\u2019s freezing. The plainer account is the one to test first.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Simple premises aren\u2019t complete ones. Darwin could see traits were inherited but not how. In 1867 Fleeming Jenkin argued that blending inheritance would **dilute any advantage** away. Genetics only answered him after 1900.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Darwin\u2019s case rests on **four plain facts**, and that\u2019s its strength. When you explain anything, start with what **nobody disputes**. Then notice what your premises still can\u2019t tell you.'}]},'origin-of-species_3':{title:'Your Own Hardest Critic',card:{insight:'Bring up the weak spot in your plan before anyone else does.',tryThis:'Before your next pitch, write the one question you dread most. Then write a short answer to it.',skills:['communication','thinking']},review:{prompt:'You pitch a cheaper supplier, but it is new and untested. What now?',right:'Bring up the weak spot yourself, with an answer',wrong:'Leave the risk out and hope nobody asks about it',why:'If you raise the weak spot first, you get to answer it your way.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re telling the class why the town needs **a skate park**. There\u2019s one big problem: it\u2019d be loud for the houses next door. You know someone will **bring it up**. Then what?'},{kind:'story',text:'Charles Darwin had a problem like this. He said living things change bit by bit. But what about the eye? It\u2019s so finely made. Darwin asked that himself and admitted it sounds silly at first. Then he pointed to **simpler eyes**, each one useful.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Find the **biggest problem** with your own idea. Say it out loud **before anyone else** does, and give your answer. You\u2019ll look honest, not caught out.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re proposing **a new supplier**. It\u2019s cheaper and faster, but it\u2019s never handled an order your size. You plan to leave that out. What happens when someone in the room **asks anyway**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Darwin faced worse. He gave a whole chapter, Difficulties on Theory, to the best objections against himself. Where were the transitional forms? How could small steps build an eye? He admits the eye sounds absurd at first, then answers it.'},{kind:'explain',text:'His answer was **the range of eyes** in living animals, each useful to its owner. Raising it himself let him **frame the question** and place his answer right beside it. Readers trust the rest more when you volunteer the hardest case.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Back to the supplier: the untested size is coming up **either way**. Who **raises it** decides how it lands.',left:{title:'They raise it',items:['In their words','When it suits them','It looks hidden']},right:{title:'You raise it',items:['In your words','Next to your answer','It looks honest']}},{kind:'moral',text:'State the **strongest objection** to your own case before anyone else can. Then answer it **on your terms**. That honesty buys trust for everything else you say.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Chapter six of the Origin opens with **a list of troubles**. Missing transitional forms. The eye. The honeybee\u2019s cell-building. Sterile hybrids. Darwin wrote every one himself. Why would you hand your critics **their best lines**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'It isn\u2019t modesty. It\u2019s **position**. An objection you raise comes in your wording, in your order, right **next to its answer**. And it takes the critic\u2019s best move off the board before anyone can play it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take the eye. Darwin grants that building it by selection sounds absurd at first hearing. Then he changes the question. Living animals show eyes at every grade, from a light-sensitive patch to **a lens**. Each grade is useful.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a student defending a thesis. She opens her talk with the two weaknesses she\u2019d attack if it were someone else\u2019s. The examiners\u2019 hardest questions are already on the table, and **already answered**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The move can be faked. You raise **a weak version**, knock it down and look candid while dodging the strong one. Darwin earned his credit because his difficulties were real. Some, like the missing fossils, stayed open for decades.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Be **your own hardest critic**, in public. State the objection as well as an opponent could, then answer it. Pick **the real one**, not the easy one.'}]},'origin-of-species_4':{title:'What Your Plan Needs',card:{insight:'Every plan rests on one thing being true, so show your proof for it.',tryThis:'Pick a plan you care about. Write the one thing it needs to be true, and your proof.',skills:['thinking','science']},review:{prompt:'Your plan needs customers to renew, but your boss doubts it. What do you do?',right:'Put that part of the plan first, with your proof',wrong:'Leave it out so no one questions it',why:'Say what your plan needs and show your proof, so no one thinks you hid it.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Look at a cliff made of **thin layers** of rock, stacked higher than a house. Each layer took ages to form. How old do you think **the whole cliff** must be?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Charles Darwin\u2019s idea needed **lots of time**. Tiny changes only add up over ages. But a top scientist worked out how fast the Earth cooled and said it was too young. Darwin couldn\u2019t prove him wrong. The rocks said old, so he **held on**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Darwin was right. Later, scientists found **hidden heat** inside the Earth that the sums had missed. If your idea needs something to be true, **say so**, and you\u2019ll know what to defend.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your business case says customers will **renew** after year one. Finance believes renewal in your sector is low. You could quietly cut that page. Or is there **a better move**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Darwin had the same problem on a bigger scale. Natural selection works by small changes, so it needs **immense time**. Arguing from rates of erosion, he claimed hundreds of millions of years. From 1862, Kelvin\u2019s physics said far fewer.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Darwin couldn\u2019t find a flaw in Kelvin\u2019s sums. But the rocks and fossils all pointed to **vast time**. So he named the clash as one of his **most serious objections** and kept the premise. He treated it as unsolved, not as a refutation.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The answer came from **another field**. Radioactivity, unknown to Kelvin, keeps adding heat inside the Earth. His cooling sums had **missed a heater**. Today the Earth\u2019s age is put at about four and a half billion years.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Find **the premise** your case can\u2019t do without. If people doubt it, put it on page one with **your evidence**. Don\u2019t hide it, and don\u2019t drop it untested.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'The Origin is about species, not **the Earth\u2019s age**. Yet nothing in it works unless the planet is very old. What do you do when your argument depends on something **outside its field**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Darwin tried to give readers a feel for deep time. He estimated how long rain and sea would take to wear away **the Weald**, in southern England. He got about three hundred million years. Critics attacked it, and he cut it from the third edition.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Then came physics. From 1862, William Thomson, later **Lord Kelvin**, treated the Earth as a cooling body. From the heat escaping its surface, he got an age **far too short**. Darwin counted it among his gravest objections and never answered it.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Radioactivity, discovered in 1896, settled it. It revealed **a heat source** inside the Earth that Kelvin\u2019s model left out. A discovery in **an unrelated field** decided what neither side could settle with its own tools.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a start-up whose plan depends on one thing: that customers come back for a second order. Investors doubt it. The founder puts that premise first, with her early numbers, and says what result would **prove her wrong**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The neat version says only radioactivity was missing. Historians also stress slow convection inside the Earth, raised by John Perry in 1895. And defying another field is **only a virtue** when your own evidence is strong. Usually it isn\u2019t.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Name the **hidden premise** your argument can\u2019t do without. Defend it with evidence, not hope. And hold out against another field only when **your own evidence** is strong.'}]},'origin-of-species_5':{title:'The Long Wait',card:{insight:'A long wait is good if it keeps adding new things.',tryThis:'Pick one thing you keep putting off. Write what the last month of waiting added to it.',skills:['productivity','thinking']},review:{prompt:'Your plan has been almost ready for a whole year. How do you judge it?',right:'Ask if the wait keeps adding something new',wrong:'Ask if you feel sure enough to send it',why:'Good waiting adds new things, but fear just checks the old ones again.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You wrote a story for a contest. It\u2019s **finished**. But you keep fixing tiny bits and never send it in. The deadline gets closer. What\u2019s really **stopping you**?'},{kind:'story',text:'In 1838 Charles Darwin worked out how living things slowly change. He waited **twenty years** to publish. Some think he was scared of angry readers. But he also spent eight years studying barnacles, small sea animals, and that made his case stronger.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Waiting can mean **making it better**, or it can mean **being scared**. Ask which one it is. If your fixes are tiny, it\u2019s probably fear.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'There\u2019s a plan you\u2019ve worked on for two years. It\u2019s **nearly ready**. It\u2019s been nearly ready for a year. Each time you think of sending it, you find **one more check**. Sound familiar?'},{kind:'story',text:'Darwin had natural selection by 1838. In 1844 he wrote a long essay and left instructions for his wife to publish it if he died. Then he spent eight years on barnacles. In 1856 he began **a huge book** on species.'},{kind:'story',text:'Then in 1858 a letter came from **Alfred Russel Wallace**, setting out the same mechanism. Friends arranged a joint announcement that summer. Darwin cut his big book down to what he called **an abstract** and published in 1859.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Rigour or dread? The test is what **the delay** keeps **producing**.',left:{title:'Signs of rigour',items:['New evidence keeps arriving','The work changes shape','You can name the gap']},right:{title:'Signs of dread',items:['The same checks, repeated','The changes are cosmetic','Nothing specific is missing']}},{kind:'moral',text:'A long delay isn\u2019t proof of fear or of care. Ask what it\u2019s **adding**: if **new evidence** keeps arriving, keep going. If you\u2019re only rechecking, send it.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'In 1844 Darwin wrote out his theory in full. Then he left his wife instructions to publish it **if he died**. He knew what he had. So why did **fifteen more years** pass before the book?'},{kind:'explain',text:'One reading is **rigour**. Eight years classifying barnacles, living and fossil, made him a **respected specialist**, not a speculator. It also showed him first-hand how much one species varies. Both counted when the book came under attack.'},{kind:'story',text:'The other reading is dread. In 1844 he told a friend that admitting species change felt like **confessing a murder**. That same year he arranged for the essay to appear without him. Then came the long quiet.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture someone who\u2019s rewritten **the same novel** for six years. Each draft changes commas, not chapters. Nothing new has come in since year two. Whatever it feels like from inside, the work stopped growing long ago.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The story fits either reading. Some historians say there was no real delay, since he was already writing his species book. And rigour has **no natural end**. Wallace gave Darwin the deadline he wouldn\u2019t set himself.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t judge **a long delay** by its length or your feelings. Judge it by **what it adds**. And if you\u2019re thorough by nature, you may need to set your own deadline.'}]},'origin-of-species_6':{title:'The Nearest Rival',card:{insight:'Your toughest rival is whoever needs the same things you need.',tryThis:'List who wants your exact customers. Then name one way you could be a little different.',skills:['thinking','science']},review:{prompt:'You run a small gym. Which rival should worry you most?',right:'The gym down the street that serves the same customers',wrong:'The big chain with ads all over TV',why:'The more two rivals need the same things, the harder they fight.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two kids try out for **the same part** in the school play. Both sing well. A third kid wants to **work the lights**. Who\u2019s the real threat to each singer?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The singers want exactly the same thing, so each is the other\u2019s **biggest rival**. Charles Darwin saw this in plants. Two plants of one kind in one patch drink the same water. If one grows **deeper roots**, the fight eases.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your hardest contest is with whoever needs **just what you need**. That\u2019s usually the one **most like you**. Being a little different is a way out.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'An independent bookshop worries about **a giant online seller**. It watches its prices and reads every headline about it. Meanwhile its customers are drifting somewhere else. Where do you think they\u2019re **actually going**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Two streets away is another bookshop. Same stock, same Saturday customers, same offer. That\u2019s the rival taking its sales. Darwin saw the same in plants. One at a desert\u2019s edge struggles with drought. Two varieties in one field struggle with each other.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Why is that harder? The two varieties need **the same light**, water and soil. Competition grows with **overlap**, not with size or ferocity. The fiercest pressure comes from whatever needs exactly what you need.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The escape is to **differ**. In nature, a variant with slightly different needs avoids the worst of the contest. The bookshop can do the same: a children\u2019s specialism, a caf\xe9, or **events** the other shop doesn\u2019t run.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your **real rival** is rarely the dramatic one. It\u2019s whoever serves **the same customers** with the same offer. Escape by needing something a little different.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Darwin warns that **struggle for existence** is a loose phrase. You might picture claws and teeth. But when he makes it precise, the fiercest struggle isn\u2019t with a predator at all. So **who is it** with?'},{kind:'explain',text:'It\u2019s with your own kind. The struggle is fiercest between members of **one species**, and nearly as bad between its varieties. They share districts, food and dangers. Competition is a function of **overlap**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Darwin points to a farming finding. A plot sown with several distinct grasses raises more plants than one sown with a single kind. **Different needs** mean less overlap, and less overlap means more room. He calls the drift apart divergence of character.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take two coffee carts parked side by side at a station. Same beans, same price, same commuters. Each morning they split one queue. When one switches to **hot food**, both have more room to grow.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Similarity is only a stand-in for overlap. Close relatives can split a resource by place or season. And very different creatures can **want the same thing**, as when birds and insects share one seed crop.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t ask who looks most like you. Ask who needs **exactly what you need**. That\u2019s your fiercest rival, and **differing from it** is your way out.'}]},'origin-of-species_7':{title:'Leave the Crowded Middle',card:{insight:'When everyone sells the same thing, move to one end of the crowd.',tryThis:'Next time rivals crowd you, write down one group of people they skip.',skills:['thinking','science']},review:{prompt:'Ten cleaners in town all offer the same service. How do you do well?',right:'Move to one end, away from the crowded middle',wrong:'Be a little better than the rest in the middle',why:'The middle has rivals on all sides, but an end has few.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Lie under a big tree and **look up**. Every branch splits and goes its own way. Can you find any two branches that **join back** together?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Charles Darwin drew life like that tree. Think of three kinds of bird. One eats big seeds, one small seeds. The **in-between one** has to fight both others for food. It gets **squeezed out**, and the ends drift apart.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Living things **split apart** like branches. They don\u2019t join back up. When a crowd is squeezing you, try moving **to the edge**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Ten agencies sell general marketing to small firms at **similar prices**. Most of them struggle. Two of them **thrive**. What do you think those two did differently?'},{kind:'story',text:'The Origin has only one figure: a branching diagram of lineages splitting over many thousands of generations. Most lines end. A few spread wide. Darwin used it because prose handles one point badly: life\u2019s pattern is **a tree**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'You might expect varieties to drift back towards the average. They don\u2019t. Forms in the middle compete with **neighbours on both sides** and get squeezed out. The extremes escape that crush, so branches **spread rather than merge**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Back to the agencies. The two that thrive are the one doing **only restaurants** and the one taking **only huge accounts**. They moved to the ends. Everyone in the middle overlaps with everyone else.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Life branches like **a tree**, not a ladder, and the branches that last are the ones that moved apart. In a crowded field, don\u2019t fight the middle. **Move to an end**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'In a private notebook in 1837, before he\u2019d found **the mechanism**, Darwin sketched a small branching tree. Twenty-two years later it became **the only figure** in his book. What was it there to show you?'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The figure argues against **a ladder** of life and for **a tree**.',left:{title:'Ladder',items:['One main line','Higher and lower forms','Progress built in']},right:{title:'Tree',items:['Many lines, none central','Different, not higher','Fit to local conditions']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Each band of the diagram is a long run of generations. The most different varieties tend to survive and branch again. **Intermediate forms**, squeezed from both sides, die out. **Gaps open** between groups.'},{kind:'story',text:'Those gaps explain something naturalists had long used. Species group into genera, and genera into families. On the tree, a group is just branches **sharing a fork**. The filing system turned out to be a record of common ancestry.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a crowded high street with five similar caf\xe9s. One turns into a quiet place for laptop workers. Another goes all in on fast takeaway. The three in the middle fight each other for the same passers-by.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Darwin wasn\u2019t wholly consistent. In places he hints later forms are higher, and readers keep bending the tree into a ladder. Branches **sometimes rejoin**, too, through hybrids and genes swapped between microbes.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Life is **a branching tree** with no main line and no top. The branches that last tend to be the ones that **moved furthest apart**. If you\u2019re crowded, look for your end.'}]},'origin-of-species_8':{title:'The Missing Pages',card:{insight:'If proof is easy to lose, not finding it tells you little.',tryThis:'When someone says there is no proof, ask how hard it would be to find.',skills:['thinking','science']},review:{prompt:'No customers have complained about the new checkout. What does that tell you?',right:'Not much, since upset people may just leave',wrong:'That people must be happy with it',why:'If upset people just leave, you hear nothing at all.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You find an old photo of your gran **as a baby**, and one of her at your age. Every photo in between is lost. Did she **jump from baby** to eleven overnight?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Of course not. The photos just didn\u2019t last. Charles Darwin had the same problem with **fossils**. Where were all the in-between animals? He said fossils are **rare to form**, easy to destroy, and hard to find.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When something\u2019s missing, ask first: how likely was it **to last**, and to be found? If the odds were low, missing tells you **very little**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your new checkout has run for a month, and **not one complaint** has come in. Your boss calls it a success. But what would you see if unhappy customers **just left**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Darwin faced a bigger silence. If species changed gradually, where were **the intermediate fossils**? He called it perhaps the most obvious and serious objection to his theory, and gave it **a whole chapter**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'His answer was **the odds**. Most living things leave nothing. A fossil needs quick burial in the right sediment. Erosion destroys much of what\u2019s buried. What survives must still be exposed, and then **found by someone** looking.'},{kind:'explain',text:'He compared the rocks to **a history** of the world. Only the last volume survives, and only **a few scattered pages** of that. Your checkout data works the same way. If unhappy customers leave quietly, silence proves nothing.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Absence counts as evidence only when the thing was **likely to survive** and be found. So before you trust a silence, ask whether **complaints were likely** to be recorded at all.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two years after the Origin, a quarry in **Bavaria** gave up a strange fossil. It had feathered wings, a long bony tail and clawed fingers. Was this the kind of find Darwin said **might never come**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'It was. In his chapter on **the imperfect record**, Darwin had argued that missing intermediates proved little. Few creatures are buried well. Most rock layers are later eroded. And only **a small part** of the Earth had been explored.'},{kind:'story',text:'The fossil was named Archaeopteryx. It put feathers on an animal with a bony tail and clawed fingers. That\u2019s just the in-between form Darwin\u2019s theory said should have existed. The gap had been in **the search**, not in history.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a small drug trial where nobody gets a rare side effect. It\u2019s tempting to call the drug safe. But an effect that strikes one person in thousands had little chance of showing up in so few. Its absence tells you little.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. As stated, the defence can\u2019t lose: any gap can be blamed on poor preservation. Darwin was asking for credit. It was repaid by **later evidence**: transitional fossils, and molecular comparisons between living species.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Missing evidence matters only when it was **likely to survive** and be found. You can use that argument, but don\u2019t hide behind it. A defence that never meets **new evidence** is just an excuse.'}]},'origin-of-species_9':{title:'The Word He Avoided',card:{insight:'People forget why, but they repeat your words, so pick them with care.',tryThis:'Before you share big news, look at its main word. Ask what people will picture.',skills:['communication','science']},review:{prompt:'You are naming a new team that fixes urgent problems. What matters most?',right:'Check what the name makes people picture',wrong:'Pick a catchy name and explain the job later',why:'People repeat the name, so it should not suggest the wrong thing.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Darwin wrote **a famous book** about how living things change. When it first came out, one word you\u2019d expect wasn\u2019t in it at all. Can you guess **which word** he left out?'},{kind:'explain',text:'It was *evolution*. Back then, that word sounded like **climbing a ladder** towards something better. Darwin didn\u2019t think life works like that. Living things just change to **fit where they live**. A polar bear\u2019s thick coat is no good in a hot forest.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Words carry **hidden meanings**. Darwin chose his with care, to keep a wrong idea out. People often forget your reasons, but they **remember your words**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'The **last word** of the Origin\u2019s first edition is *evolved*. It\u2019s the only time any form of that word appears. Why would Darwin keep **the word** out of his own book?'},{kind:'explain',text:'In 1859, evolution suggested **unfolding** towards a set end, the way an embryo unfolds into an adult. Darwin\u2019s mechanism has **no set end**. So he wrote descent with modification instead: species descend and change along the way.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Your choice of word can **build in** a picture your argument **doesn\u2019t contain**.',left:{title:'What evolution suggests',items:['A ladder with a top','Higher and lower forms','Change towards better']},right:{title:'What selection does',items:['A tree with no top','Fit to local conditions','Change that reverses too']}},{kind:'story',text:'The word won anyway. By the sixth edition it was the theory\u2019s common name, and it carried the ladder in with it. Higher organisms, advanced species, humans at the summit: the misreading lives **in the word**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'People read **your argument** once, but they repeat **your words** for years. Before you make a claim that matters, check what its one key word implies.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Darwin\u2019s own phrase was **descent with modification**. The word evolution never appears in his first edition, though *evolved* closes the book. You might call that a footnote. What if the missing word is **the argument**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'In 1859, evolution belonged largely to embryology. There it meant **unrolling** a structure already present in the germ. So it implied **a destination**. Selection has none: a trait is favoured only for present circumstances.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take fish living in **dark caves**. Over many generations they\u2019ve often lost working eyes. Eyes are no use in total darkness and may cost something to build. In the language of progress, that looks like going backwards.'},{kind:'explain',text:'In Darwin\u2019s terms it\u2019s simply **fit to local conditions**, which is all selection produces. Every lineage alive today has been descending and changing for **exactly as long** as every other. No branch is higher.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a company announcing **a restructure** to staff. One memo calls it streamlining. Another calls it cuts. The plan is identical. But each word is what people repeat in the corridor, and each carries **its own verdict**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Darwin used higher and lower himself, wavered on progress, and adopted the word later. Vocabulary alone didn\u2019t cause the misreading. It also suited **what readers wanted** to believe about themselves.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Darwin\u2019s care over a word was **a claim** in itself: life has no built-in direction. The word won anyway. When your idea matters, choose **its name** as carefully as you build the case.'}]}}}},983,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'plutarch-lives',kind:'book',promise:'Learn who people are from small moments',about:'How can you tell what a person is like? Plutarch was a Greek writer who lived about 1,900 years ago. He wrote the life stories of famous Greeks and Romans. He looked for small moments, not just big wins. Want to see his way?',ideas:{'plutarch-lives_1':{title:'Small Moments Tell More',card:{insight:'A small moment can tell you more about a person than big wins.',tryThis:'Tonight, write down one small moment that showed you what a friend is like.',skills:['thinking','history']},review:{prompt:'Two babysitters have great reviews. What tells you the most about them?',right:'A small moment, like how they act when a kid spills juice',wrong:'The long list of past jobs at the top of their profile',why:'One small moment can tell you more than a long list of wins.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You can watch a footballer\u2019s **best goals** on a highlights clip. But what about after the match, when the cameras are off? What would tell you **what they\u2019re like**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Plutarch was a Greek writer who lived about 1,900 years ago. He wrote the life stories of famous leaders. He said plainly he\u2019d skip **most battles**. He was hunting for **small moments**, like a joke, that showed who they were.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A big win shows **what happened**. A small moment shows **who someone is**. So when you want to know a person, watch how they act when nobody\u2019s looking.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two candidates apply for the same job with **equally strong CVs**. One is curt with the receptionist on the way in. Nobody mentions it. But hasn\u2019t the panel just learned **something new**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Plutarch was a Greek writer working around 100 CE. He opens his Life of Alexander with a warning. He\u2019s writing lives, not histories. He\u2019ll shorten the campaigns and linger on a passing remark or a joke.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'A history and a life ask **different questions**, even about the same man.',left:{title:'A history asks',items:['What happened?','Who won, and how?']},right:{title:'A life asks',items:['What was this person like?','What did they do **unguarded**?']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Plutarch compares himself to a **portrait painter**. The painter takes the likeness from the face and eyes, where character shows. He gives less care to the rest. Plutarch does the same, dwelling on the **signs of character**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your record tells people **what happened**. Your small, unguarded moments tell them **who you are**. When you judge someone, give those moments real weight.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'The Life of Alexander is crowded with wars. Yet Plutarch opens it by asking readers **not to complain** when he cuts battles short. Why would a biographer of a conqueror **skip the conquests**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'His reason is stated plainly. He\u2019s writing **lives, not histories**. Great exploits often show little of virtue or vice. A slight remark or a jest can reveal **far more** than a battle where thousands die.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a portrait painter at work. He studies the face and the eyes closely, because that\u2019s where character shows. The hands and the clothes get a few quick strokes. Plutarch says his Lives work the same way.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture you\u2019re choosing a builder. Two have glowing reviews. One arrives early and tidies up after the quote. The other is rude to his own apprentice. You\u2019ve just seen what no review could show you.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. If you want to know what happened, Plutarch is **a poor guide**. He cuts whole campaigns short. And he picks which moments count, so you see **his choice** of evidence, never the full record.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Plutarch told you **in advance** which question his book answers: what a person was like. Judge him by that. And when you judge people, look for **the unguarded moment**.'}]},'plutarch-lives_2':{title:'Side by Side',card:{insight:'Set two people in the same spot side by side to see what each chose.',tryThis:'Pick a choice you are judging. Find someone who faced the same choice, and see what they did.',skills:['thinking','history']},review:{prompt:'A friend\u2019s bold bet paid off. How do you judge her choice?',right:'Compare her with someone who faced the same choice',wrong:'Admire the result, since it worked out so well',why:'Next to a second case, you can tell the choice from the luck.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two kids take a penalty in the **same match**. One blasts it after a long run-up. One walks up calmly and places it. Same goal, same keeper, same crowd. So what\u2019s **the difference**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Plutarch wrote his life stories **in pairs**: one Greek leader, one Roman. When you put two lives side by side, the shared bits cancel out. What\u2019s left is **down to the person**, not luck.'},{kind:'moral',text:'One story alone can make **luck look like talent**. Put two side by side and you\u2019ll see **what each one chose**. That\u2019s how you learn what was really them.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A friend\u2019s start-up takes off and everyone calls her **brilliant**. Another friend tries almost the same idea and it flops. Was the first one brilliant, or just **lucky**? How would you tell?'},{kind:'story',text:'Plutarch\u2019s Parallel Lives sets a Greek beside a Roman. Theseus sits with Romulus, both legendary founders. Demosthenes sits with Cicero, both great orators. Many pairs end with a short essay weighing the two men.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Read a life alone and you judge by **the outcome**. Read two together and **choices stand out**.',left:{title:'One life alone',items:['Success looks like talent','Luck stays invisible']},right:{title:'Two lives together',items:['Shared conditions cancel out','You see the person']}},{kind:'explain',text:'It works like a **simple experiment**. Hold the situation roughly steady across two lives. Then whatever still differs is more likely **character than circumstance**. That\u2019s the question a single biography lets you dodge.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t judge a choice by its result alone. Find someone who **faced the same choice** and chose differently. The comparison shows **what was the person**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Plutarch could have written each life on its own. Instead he set **every Greek** beside **a Roman**, and often added a short essay comparing them. Why build a whole book in pairs?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A single life tempts you to **judge by the outcome**. But outcomes depend heavily on circumstance. Put two people in similar spots side by side. Whatever still differs is more plausibly **the person**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Alexander the Great and Julius Caesar, one of Plutarch\u2019s pairs. Read alone, Alexander\u2019s daring is easy to admire. Set beside Caesar\u2019s, the same trait worked under different limits and led to different results.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take two managers who both inherit a struggling team. One cuts the budget, the other rebuilds trust first. The market and the team are the same. So the gap in results tells you about them.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. No two lives share conditions closely, so the experiment is **loose**. Some pairs are matched for neat symmetry, like two founders. Not every comparison survives. The device shows you where to look, not what\u2019s proved.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Pairs don\u2019t prove what a person was. But a **second case** holds the setting still, so **the choice shows**. That\u2019s worth more than any single story.'}]},'plutarch-lives_3':{title:'When No One Is Watching',card:{insight:'Habits show most in small moments, when no one is watching.',tryThis:'Next time you meet someone, watch how they treat a waiter who brings the wrong food.',skills:['psychology','relationships']},review:{prompt:'A new coworker is warm in meetings but rude to the janitor. What counts more?',right:'Small moments, like how she treats the janitor',wrong:'How she acts in meetings, where it counts the most',why:'Habits show in small moments, and those are hard to fake.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'On prize day, two kids both **clap loudly** for a friend. At lunch, one saves a seat for the new kid. The other doesn\u2019t even notice. Which moment tells you **more about them**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Who you are is built from **what you repeat**. Anyone can be kind for one big moment. Being kind every day takes **a habit**, and habits are hard to fake. They just pop out.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Plutarch, a Greek writer, loved tiny stories about leaders. He knew **small moments** don\u2019t lie as easily. Watch what people do when **nobody\u2019s watching**, and you\u2019ll see who they are.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A project lead has just watched her pitch **collapse**. In the corridor, she can blame the junior who built the slides. Or she can say the plan was hers. **Nobody senior** is there. What happens next?'},{kind:'story',text:'She says the plan was hers, and it was wrong. Nobody will ever praise her for it. That\u2019s the point. It wasn\u2019t a performance for anyone. It was a habit, showing itself when attention was elsewhere.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Plutarch\u2019s stories are small on purpose. He tells what a leader did with **a messenger**, or how he ate on campaign. He notes what was said once **the matter seemed closed**. None of it is trivia.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'A **staged moment** and an **unguarded one** reveal very different things about you.',left:{title:'The staged moment',items:['High stakes, full attention','One effort can produce it']},right:{title:'The unguarded moment',items:['Low stakes, attention elsewhere','Needs years of habit']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Character is built by repetition, so it leaks out when **nothing\u2019s at stake**. Give those moments **more weight** than the performance you\u2019re shown.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A general can rehearse a speech before a battle. Can he rehearse how he speaks to a **tired messenger** at midnight? Which of the two would you trust to show **the real man**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Plutarch was a Platonist. He shared a view common in Greek ethics since Aristotle: virtue is formed **by practice**, like a skill. If so, character should show most where a person **isn\u2019t choosing** how to appear.'},{kind:'story',text:'So his Lives are full of small scenes. A leader with a messenger. A meal on campaign. A remark made after the matter seemed settled. He isn\u2019t gathering gossip. He wants the habit, caught off guard.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now think of two colleagues up for promotion. Both shine in the big review. But one quietly fixes other people\u2019s mistakes every week, unasked. Years of that can\u2019t be faked for an audience.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Anecdotes are the **least checkable** evidence, and the likeliest to improve with retelling. A story that fits a character perfectly may fit because someone made it fit. Trust traits many witnesses saw.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A great act can come from **one big effort**. A small habit has to hold up across a lifetime of witnesses. That\u2019s why **low-stakes evidence** is harder to fake, and worth more to you.'}]},'plutarch-lives_4':{title:'Know When You\u2019ve Won',card:{insight:'Each win makes you want a bigger one, so stop and see what you have.',tryThis:'Write down your last big win and what it gave you. Then ask what the next one would add.',skills:['leadership','psychology']},review:{prompt:'Your side business just hit its goal. What is your first move?',right:'Write down what the win gave you before you plan more',wrong:'Set a goal twice as big while things go well',why:'Stop and see what you have before you reach for more.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You build a tower of blocks. It\u2019s already **the tallest** in the class. You add one more, then another. Each one worked, so surely the next will too. What happens with **one block too many**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Wanting to win isn\u2019t bad. The danger is **never stopping** to see that you already have. Each win makes the next try bigger. And when you\u2019re winning, nobody says **be careful**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Plutarch wrote about **very able leaders** who kept reaching for more and lost everything. Know **when you\u2019ve won**. Enjoy it before you reach again.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A king is planning a war against Rome. His adviser asks one simple question: **and then what**? The king has an answer every time. Can you guess **where it ends**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Plutarch tells of Pyrrhus, a Greek king, and his adviser Cineas. After Italy, Sicily. Then Carthage and Africa, then Greece. Then, at last, rest and enjoyment. Cineas asks why they can\u2019t rest now. Pyrrhus goes to war anyway.'},{kind:'explain',text:'That\u2019s the failure Plutarch keeps finding. It isn\u2019t **wickedness**. It\u2019s capable people who can\u2019t tell **when they\u2019ve won**. Ambition magnifies whatever judgement is already there, so drive makes a rash person dangerous.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The failures come **late**, not early. Each success widens the next plan and removes a few of the doubts that would have checked it. Eventually the scale outruns **untested judgement**, and one decision undoes a record.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your ambition isn\u2019t the problem. Success quietly **removes your checks**. So after each win, stop and name what you\u2019ve done before you **reach further**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Alcibiades was brilliant at speaking, at war and at charm. Athens followed him again and again. Yet nobody **trusted him** for long, and he **fell more than once**. How does so much talent end like that?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Plutarch\u2019s answer is that ambition is **a multiplier**, not a vice. It magnifies the judgement already there. The same drive that makes a capable person decisive makes **a rash one** catastrophic.'},{kind:'story',text:'Alcibiades urged Athens into a vast expedition against Sicily, already dreaming of Carthage beyond it. Recalled to face charges, he went over to Sparta. Then he went to the Persians. He came back to lead Athens, and fell again.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a founder whose first three products all succeed. Each win brings a bigger launch and fewer people willing to question her. The fourth launch is the largest yet, and nobody pushes back.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The pattern is easiest to see **backwards**. Knowing the ending, every earlier success looks like a step to ruin. Many bold people expanded and never fell. Ambition doesn\u2019t always fail. Success just stops testing it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your drive will magnify **whatever judgement** you bring. Winning won\u2019t test it for you. So after each win, **test it yourself** before you reach for more.'}]},'plutarch-lives_5':{title:'A Story, Not a Report',card:{insight:'An old story may not be exact, but it shows what people thought.',tryThis:'Next time you hear a great old story, ask who told it first, and why.',skills:['thinking','history']},review:{prompt:'A perfect story about a king from long ago goes viral. How do you read it?',right:'As a clue to what people thought of him',wrong:'As an exact report of what happened that day',why:'Old stories show what people thought, more than what took place.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A friend tells you a brilliant story about someone at another school. It\u2019s funny and it fits them perfectly. But your friend wasn\u2019t there. How much of it **really happened**? Does it **still matter**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Plutarch lived about **400 years** after Alexander the Great. He read older books that are now lost, and some took sides. He also loved a story with **a lesson in it**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'So read Plutarch to learn **what people were like**. Don\u2019t read him for exact dates or words. His stories are **good lessons**, not news reports.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You read that a famous general said **something perfect** just before a battle. It\u2019s vivid and it sums him up. It was written centuries later. So **what are you reading**: a fact, or a portrait?'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Plutarch is more careful than his reputation, but he still **slips**.',left:{title:'His care',items:['Reports rumours as **rumours**','Gives rival versions','Admits when he can\u2019t tell']},right:{title:'His slips',items:['Repeats claims later contradicted','Prefers the more revealing story']}},{kind:'story',text:'Take his Alexander. Plutarch wrote around four hundred years after the king died. He worked from older accounts now lost, and some were already partisan. For several figures, he\u2019s **the main surviving source**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'So you **can\u2019t simply check** him elsewhere. The honest way is to read him as **a witness** to what people believed and told. His sources are gone. His judgement about character is still here.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Treat a vivid old story as **an argument about character**. Don\u2019t treat it as **a report of events**. That\u2019s how Plutarch is most useful to you.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'For some ancient figures, almost everything we know comes from **one writer**. He lived centuries after them and liked a story with a moral. What do you do when you **can\u2019t go elsewhere**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'In Plutarch\u2019s favour, he **flags rumours** as rumours. He sets out rival versions and sometimes admits he can\u2019t decide. Against him, he repeats material later evidence contradicts. His **teaching aim** gives him a motive to prefer revealing stories.'},{kind:'story',text:'Plutarch wrote his Alexander some four centuries after the king\u2019s death. He leaned on earlier writers whose books haven\u2019t survived, and several were partisan. For some subjects, his is the main account still standing.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a modern leader whose biography was written by an admiring aide. You can\u2019t interview the dead. But you can ask what the aide wanted you to believe, and weigh each scene with that in mind.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The opposite error is just as easy: dismissing everything as **moral fable**. He had sources we lack, and much of what he reports is supported elsewhere. Weigh each story. Don\u2019t bin them all.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Read Plutarch as **a witness** with sources you\u2019ll never see. Hold his motives in mind, story by story. That\u2019s **reading biography**, which is what he asked for.'}]},'plutarch-lives_6':{title:'The Horse\u2019s Shadow',card:{insight:'When all agree on why a thing fails, go look for yourself.',tryThis:'Pick one thing that keeps going wrong. Watch it happen for five minutes before you agree.',skills:['thinking','leadership']},review:{prompt:'The printer at work jams every day, and everyone says it is old. What first?',right:'Watch it jam a few times before I agree',wrong:'Buy a new one, since everyone agrees on why',why:'Once they all agree, people stop looking, so go see for yourself.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Long ago, a king named Philip was shown **a fine horse**. It reared and kicked, and no rider could get on. The king told his men to take it away. Would **you give up** too?'},{kind:'story',text:'His young son, Alexander, had been watching closely. He saw the horse wasn\u2019t wild. It was scared of **its own shadow**. So he turned it to face the sun, and the shadow fell behind. The horse calmed, and he climbed on.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Everyone else saw **a wild horse**, but the boy saw what scared it. When something keeps going wrong, **watch it yourself** before you guess.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A report fails most nights. The team has **an agreed reason**: the server\u2019s overloaded, and the upgrade is overdue. A new hire asks to **simply watch** it run once. Is she wasting your time?'},{kind:'story',text:'Plutarch tells of a horse brought to King Philip of Macedon. Nobody could mount it, and Philip ordered it led away. His young son said they were losing **a fine animal** through inexperience. Then he turned its head to the sun.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The boy had seen it shying at **its own shadow**. Everyone else had seen **a problem horse**.',left:{title:'Everyone else',items:['Agreed on the reason','Tried harder, then gave up']},right:{title:'The boy',items:['Watched before deciding','Changed one condition']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Plutarch picks out **observation**, not courage. The grown men were brave. But once experienced people agree on a cause, they **stop looking**, and each failure seems like more proof. A newcomer who watches isn\u2019t cleverer. They\u2019re just outside the story.'},{kind:'moral',text:'An agreed reason can hide **the real cause**. Before you accept it, **watch it happen** yourself. Ten minutes of looking can beat months of certainty.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A crowd of experienced horsemen has given up on an animal nobody can mount. A boy says they\u2019re losing it through **inexperience**. What could he possibly see that **they\u2019ve missed**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Plutarch tells it in his Life of Alexander. Philip of Macedon has ordered the horse led away. The boy Alexander has noticed it fears its own shadow. He turns it towards the sun, and mounts.'},{kind:'explain',text:'This is Plutarch\u2019s method **in action**. The preface promised a small scene over a battle. A battle mixes a general with luck, terrain and thousands of men. A boy and a horse isolate **one trait**: looking.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a clinic where patients keep missing appointments. Staff agree they\u2019re just careless. A new receptionist sits in the waiting room for an afternoon. She sees the reminder letters arrive a day too late.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Plutarch writes with hindsight, picking the boyhood scene that foreshadows the conqueror. The telling may have improved. And often **the agreed view** is right. Look before you accept it, but don\u2019t assume it\u2019s wrong.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Certainty makes people **stop looking**. Spend a few minutes watching, and you may see **what\u2019s really happening**, as the boy saw the shadow.'}]},'plutarch-lives_7':{title:'The Man Who Never Bent',card:{insight:'If you never bend, even on small things, you can make things worse.',tryThis:'Write down the few rules you will not bend on. If the list is long, cut it.',skills:['philosophy','leadership']},review:{prompt:'Your team will back your plan if you give up one small point. What now?',right:'Ask if that small point is worth losing the plan',wrong:'Say no, since giving in on one thing means you lose',why:'Never bending on small things can bring a worse result.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your team must pick **a game plan**. You think yours is best, and maybe it is. But you won\u2019t give an inch on anything. What happens when **nobody joins you**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Cato was a Roman leader about 2,000 years ago. He was famous for being honest. Nobody could bribe him. But he hardly ever made deals, even to stop something worse from happening. The side he fought for lost.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Sticking to what\u2019s right is **a good thing**. But if you **never bend at all**, you can make things worse, even when you\u2019re right.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re right about the problem, and everyone knows it. A rival offers a deal that would mean **bending a little**. You refuse. Months later, **the worst outcome** arrives. Were you still right?'},{kind:'story',text:'Pompey, Rome\u2019s most famous general, sought marriage ties with Cato\u2019s family. Cato refused to be bound to him. So Pompey married Julia, the daughter of Julius Caesar. The alliance Cato blocked was replaced by a worse one.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Plutarch admires Cato\u2019s **integrity**, and still records **what it produced**.',left:{title:'Cato was right',items:['Caesar threatened the republic','Principles shouldn\u2019t be for sale']},right:{title:'His refusals',items:['Pushed Pompey towards Caesar','Drove away moderate allies']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Plutarch doesn\u2019t settle it. He records Cato\u2019s death at Utica, after Caesar won, with **deep respect**. He doesn\u2019t explain the damage away either. He leaves you a hard question: is a virtue that **can\u2019t bend** still a virtue?'},{kind:'moral',text:'Integrity is a virtue. But when a situation needs alliances, refusing to bend can **work as a defect**. Ask which principles are **worth the outcome**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Cato the Younger couldn\u2019t be bribed and wouldn\u2019t compromise. He was right about **the danger** Caesar posed. Yet his side lost, and his own choices helped. How can being right **make things worse**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Plutarch\u2019s Cato is a study of **integrity as a limit**. Incorruptibility is a virtue. But in a situation that requires alliances, a virtue that **cannot bend** can function as a defect. Plutarch admires it and shows the cost.'},{kind:'story',text:'Pompey sought a marriage tie with Cato\u2019s family, and Cato refused. Pompey married Caesar\u2019s daughter Julia instead. Cato\u2019s obstruction on principle drove moderates away. The republic he defended fell, and his rigidity was among the causes.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a union negotiator who won\u2019t trade on any clause. Each is defensible on its own. Talks collapse, and the employer imposes terms worse than every compromise offered. She was right, and her members pay.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The portrait is easily misused against **all principle**. Plutarch never says to deal with anyone. Cato was right about the danger. And Pompey, who did deal with Caesar, ended at war with him anyway.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The test isn\u2019t flexibility for its own sake. Ask which of **your principles** you\u2019d hold at the cost of the outcome. Then check **that list** is short enough to be honest.'}]},'plutarch-lives_8':{title:'Caesar\u2019s Tears',card:{insight:'We all race someone, and who we race decides if we feel behind.',tryThis:'Write down the name of the person you measure yourself against. Then ask if you picked them.',skills:['psychology','career']},review:{prompt:'You are thirty and doing well, but you feel behind. What do you check first?',right:'Who I am racing, and if I picked that person',wrong:'How much harder I need to work to catch up',why:'How far behind you feel depends on who you are racing.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Julius Caesar became one of the **most famous** Romans ever. In his thirties, he sat reading about Alexander the Great. Then he **began to cry**. Why would a successful man weep over a book?'},{kind:'explain',text:'By Caesar\u2019s age, Alexander had won **a huge empire**. Caesar felt he\u2019d done nothing people would remember. But Alexander had been dead for over 250 years. Caesar was racing **a dead man**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Most of us **measure ourselves** against someone. The person you pick can push you or make you miserable. So it\u2019s worth knowing **who it is**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Caesar is in his late thirties, governing a Roman province in Spain. He\u2019s reading about Alexander. Suddenly he **bursts into tears**. His friends ask what\u2019s wrong. What could **upset a governor** like this?'},{kind:'story',text:'His answer is short. At his age, Alexander had ruled so many peoples. He himself had done nothing remarkable. Plutarch reports the moment and adds **no comment**. He trusts the scene to do the work.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Caesar wasn\u2019t **measuring himself** against his peers. He used **a far stranger yardstick**.',left:{title:'The usual measure',items:['Romans of his own age','A normal successful career']},right:{title:'Caesar\u2019s measure',items:['A long-dead Macedonian king','Fame across the world']}},{kind:'explain',text:'A person\u2019s reference point is usually **invisible**, even to them. Here it surfaces in one moment. Once you know Caesar was **keeping time** against Alexander, much of his later career becomes easier to read. Who are you measuring against?'},{kind:'moral',text:'Most ambition has **one person** underneath it. You rarely name them, even to yourself. Find **your yardstick**, and you\u2019ll understand your own drive.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A Roman official in his late thirties, already a man of standing, reads about a king dead for centuries. He weeps. What is he **really measuring**, and **against whom**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Caesar told his friends that Alexander had conquered **so many nations** by his age. He himself had done nothing memorable. Plutarch adds no comment. The tears place the engine of a career in **a comparison** with a dead man.'},{kind:'story',text:'Plutarch pairs the Life of Alexander with the Life of Caesar. So the reader is invited to run the very comparison Caesar ran. The book\u2019s structure repeats the man\u2019s own habit of mind.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a junior lawyer who feels like a failure at thirty. Her peers are doing fine. But her mother was made partner at twenty-nine. That\u2019s the clock she\u2019s been racing, and she\u2019s never said so aloud.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. One reported moment is **thin ground** for a whole psychology. Suetonius, writing near Plutarch\u2019s time, has Caesar moved by a statue of Alexander, not a book. Ambition has many causes. Treat this as a clue, not a diagnosis.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your reference point is usually **out of sight**. Name it, and much of **your own drive** becomes easier to read. Then decide whether to keep it.'}]},'plutarch-lives_9':{title:'A Rule Broken Once',card:{insight:'A rule you break for a good cause stays broken for bad ones too.',tryThis:'Before you bend a rule, write down who could use it next.',skills:['leadership','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'Your boss will skip a sign-off just for your good project. What do you ask?',right:'Who else could use this next, and for what?',wrong:'How fast can we get this good project going?',why:'Once a rule breaks for a good cause, worse ones can use it too.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your team has a rule: everyone gets **the same time** on the pitch. For the cup final, the coach breaks it and you win. What happens at **next week\u2019s match**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Next week, she does it again in an ordinary game. Then the other coaches start doing it too. The rule was broken once, for a good reason. Now it\u2019s broken for everyone.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A rule you break for a good reason **stays broken**. Others can use it for worse reasons. So before you bend one, ask **who else will**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A reformer is sure he\u2019s right, and most people agree. A colleague blocks his plan using a perfectly **legal veto**. There\u2019s one way through, but no one has ever used it. Should he **take it**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Tiberius Gracchus was a tribune, elected to protect ordinary Romans. He proposed giving public land to the poor. A fellow tribune vetoed it. Tiberius had the assembly vote him out of office. That had never been done. The law passed.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Plutarch is **sympathetic** to the cause and **unsparing** about the method.',left:{title:'The cause',items:['Land piled up with the rich','Lawful routes to reform closed']},right:{title:'The method',items:['An official removed from office','Open to whoever came next']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Rome\u2019s system rested on limits that held because **nobody broke them**. Once one fell for a good cause, it lay open to **worse causes**. Within months, Tiberius himself was killed by a crowd led by senators.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The exception you make for a good cause **doesn\u2019t stay attached** to it. Before you break a rule, ask **who else** will now break it, and for what.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'The poor are losing their land, and every lawful route to reform is **blocked**. You could fix it today by breaking a rule that\u2019s held for centuries. Would you, knowing **anyone could follow**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Plutarch holds **two things** together. The reform was necessary: land had piled up with the rich, and the poor were suffering. The method set **a precedent** that people with worse intentions used later.'},{kind:'story',text:'Tiberius Gracchus brought a law giving public land to poor citizens. His colleague Octavius vetoed it, so Tiberius had the assembly depose him. The law passed. Before the year was out, Tiberius and many supporters had been killed.'},{kind:'story',text:'Plutarch sets the Gracchi beside Agis and Cleomenes, kings of Sparta. They too tried to restore land and the old equality by overriding the existing order. The pairing makes the pattern itself the subject.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The argument can become a defence of **any injustice**: never break a rule, because someone might copy you. Plutarch doesn\u2019t say that. The cost is real even when it\u2019s worth paying.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A remedy that damages **the means of remedy** is still a remedy. It\u2019s still damage too. Count **both costs** before you break the rule.'}]}}}},984,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'power-of-concentration',kind:'book',promise:'Build your focus a little each day',about:'Can you sit still for five minutes without grabbing your phone? Over 100 years ago, a writer who went by Theron Q. Dumont wrote a book on training your focus. Some of his tips still work. Want to see which ones?',ideas:{'power-of-concentration_1':{title:'The Attention Muscle',card:{insight:'Focus grows like a muscle, so build it a little each day.',tryThis:'Tonight, read for five minutes with your phone in the next room. Tomorrow, add one more minute.',skills:['focus','habits']},review:{prompt:'Sam says he is just not a focused person. What is the better view?',right:'He can build his focus a little each day',wrong:'Some people are born with focus, and he is not',why:'Focus grows when you use it, so a slow start is normal.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'The first time you try a **pull-up**, you might not get up at all. Nobody says you were born weak. So why do people say they were **born unfocused**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A book from the 1910s called The Power of Concentration says focus works **like a muscle**. You build it by using it **a little**, again and again. People who focus well mostly **practised**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Focus isn\u2019t something you **have or lack**. It\u2019s something you **build**. Start with five minutes, then add a little more each week.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You sit down to read a report. Ten minutes later you\u2019re on your phone, again. You tell yourself you\u2019re **just not** a focused person. Is that **really the problem**?'},{kind:'story',text:'The Power of Concentration was written in the 1910s for clerks and salesmen. Its one big premise is that attention is **trained**, not summoned. People who can hold it have usually practised, not been born able.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Which story you tell yourself decides **what you do** about a **bad session**.',left:{title:'A character problem',items:['Not a focused person','Try harder','Fails, and proves it']},right:{title:'A fitness problem',items:['Not trained yet','Short sessions, then longer','Fails, and sets a target']}},{kind:'explain',text:'So train it the way you\u2019d train **any muscle**. Practise for **short sessions**, then extend them slowly. Later research has broadly agreed that sustained attention improves with practice. The gain is **specific**, though, to what you practise.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A bad session doesn\u2019t prove you\u2019re **unfocused**. It shows **what to train** next. Treat focus as a skill you haven\u2019t built yet.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two clerks share **the same office**. One can stay on a ledger for an hour. The other drifts within minutes. Is the difference something **they were born** with, or built?'},{kind:'story',text:'The book was written for clerks and salesmen in the 1910s. Its drills are short and repeated. It treats focus as built by **repetition**, not summoned by **wanting it**. So you practise, then practise a little longer.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Dumont\u2019s premise is that attention behaves like **a capacity**, not a mood. That turns a failure to focus into **a fitness problem**, not a flaw of character. And that changes **what you do** next.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture someone who can\u2019t read for more than ten minutes. She sets a timer for ten and stops there. Each week she adds two minutes. By spring she reads for half an hour without noticing.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The muscle picture is imperfect. Research suggests the gains are **specific**: practising one kind of focus doesn\u2019t make you focused at everything. And some trouble with attention isn\u2019t a matter of training at all.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The book\u2019s premise holds even where its era doesn\u2019t. Treat focus as **untrained**, not missing. Then train **the kind you need**, in short sessions, on purpose.'}]},'power-of-concentration_2':{title:'The Second Hand',card:{insight:'Fun things hold your focus for you, so train it on dull things.',tryThis:'Today, watch the second hand of a clock for two minutes. Think of nothing else.',skills:['focus']},review:{prompt:'You want to train your focus. What should you practice on?',right:'Something dull, like the second hand of a clock',wrong:'Something fun, so it is easy to stick with',why:'Fun things hold you, so only dull things make your focus work.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You can watch a **good film** for two hours without blinking. But try watching a clock\u2019s **second hand** for two minutes. Why is one so easy and the other so hard?'},{kind:'explain',text:'When something is **exciting**, it holds you. You aren\u2019t doing the work. The film is. Dumont\u2019s drills use **boring things** on purpose, like a ticking second hand. Then your focus has to do all the lifting.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Fun things train your focus **very little**. Boring things make you **do the holding**. So practise on something dull, a few minutes at a time.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You decide to build your focus by reading **a gripping novel**. Hours vanish. It feels like real concentration. Then Monday\u2019s **spreadsheet** arrives and you can\u2019t last ten minutes. What went wrong?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The book\u2019s exercises are **deliberately tedious**. Watch the second hand of a clock. Hold one object in mind. The reasoning is simple. When material is engaging, **interest does the work** for you, so nothing gets trained.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The novel and the spreadsheet test **different things**, though both **feel like** focus.',left:{title:'Gripping material',items:['The material holds you','Feels like focus','Trains very little']},right:{title:'Dull material',items:['You hold the material','Feels like effort','Trains the real skill']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Most real work happens when **nothing pulls you in**. That\u2019s the condition you need to train under. It\u2019s like strength training: you use **resistance**, not convenience. The dull object is the weight.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Focus on gripping material isn\u2019t training. **The material** is doing it. Practise on **something dull**, and you build the skill your work actually needs.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A salesman in the 1910s opens a manual on concentration. He expects secrets. Instead he\u2019s told to watch a clock\u2019s **second hand** and do nothing else. Why would anyone **prescribe boredom**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Dumont\u2019s drills are tedious by design. Watch the second hand go round. Hold a single object in mind. Nothing about them is meant to be **interesting** at all, and that\u2019s the point.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Attention on something engaging isn\u2019t **attention being exercised**. The material does the lifting. The capacity that matters is the one you use when **nothing pulls you in**. That\u2019s the condition most real work happens under.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a student who trains her focus with puzzles she loves. She can solve them for hours. Yet her revision notes still lose her in minutes. The practice never happened under **the conditions** she needed.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Dull practice trains you for dull work, but the gain may stay **fairly narrow**. And the book\u2019s own reasons are weaker than the advice. The instruction survives, even where its explanation doesn\u2019t.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Interest is a crutch when you\u2019re training. Pick material that gives you **no help**, and your attention has to **carry itself**. That\u2019s the skill dull work asks for.'}]},'power-of-concentration_3':{title:'Tomorrow\u2019s Job Tonight',card:{insight:'If you pick your main job in the morning, you spend your best hour choosing.',tryThis:'Tonight, write tomorrow\u2019s one main job on a note. Put it where you will start work.',skills:['productivity','focus']},review:{prompt:'You open your laptop and spend twenty minutes choosing what to do. What helps?',right:'Pick the main job the night before, so you start right away',wrong:'Wait until the morning, when you feel fresh, to choose',why:'If you choose the night before, your fresh morning goes to the work.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'It\u2019s Saturday and you\u2019ve got **lots to do**. You spend half the morning **deciding** where to start. By lunch, you\u2019ve barely begun. Where did the morning go?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Dumont gave a simple rule: pick tomorrow\u2019s **main job** tonight. Then in the morning you don\u2019t have to choose anything, so you just begin. Your **freshest time** goes on doing, not deciding.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Choosing takes **energy** too, so do it the night before, when it\u2019s easy. Then your morning can start with **the real work**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You sit down at nine with a clear head. By half past, you\u2019ve checked email and **reshuffled your list** twice. You still haven\u2019t started. What did that **first half hour** buy you?'},{kind:'story',text:'The Power of Concentration tells its readers to settle the next day\u2019s **main task** in advance. Its reason is plain. Choosing in the morning spends your freshest attention. And it spends it on a question you could answer the night before.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Same task, same person. Only **the timing** of **the choice** changes.',left:{title:'Decided at nine',items:['Fresh attention spent choosing','Open to renegotiation','Starts late, if at all']},right:{title:'Decided last night',items:['Fresh attention spent working','Already settled','Starts at once']}},{kind:'explain',text:'There\u2019s a second effect. A plan made calmly last night is carried out by **a morning self** who didn\u2019t make it. That self has less standing to **reopen the question**. So the debate never starts.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Deciding costs attention, so don\u2019t spend **your best hour** on it. Settle tomorrow\u2019s **main task** tonight, while the choice is cheap. Then you start at once.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Nearly every modern productivity book tells you to plan **tomorrow tonight**. A manual from the 1910s said it too. When advice keeps **being rediscovered**, what is it actually doing?'},{kind:'story',text:'Dumont presents the rule as a concentration technique: settle the next day\u2019s main task in advance. His explanation for why it works is **vague**. The rule itself has held up far better than **his reasons**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'It\u2019s better understood as a guard against **decision fatigue**. A task chosen in advance is begun without **the period of choosing**. That period is where most of your mornings are lost.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a writer who leaves one line on her desk each night: tomorrow, draft the second chapter. At eight she sits and starts typing. She doesn\u2019t argue with the note, because **someone calmer** wrote it.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some days the plan is wrong by morning: a crisis lands, or new facts arrive. Treating last night\u2019s note as untouchable then costs you. The rule closes debate, which is useful **until the facts change**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The night-before rule protects **your freshest attention** and closes the morning debate. Keep it, but leave **a door open** for real news. Decide early, then start.'}]},'power-of-concentration_4':{title:'The Nagging Item',card:{insight:'Undone jobs pull at you until you do them or give them a day.',tryThis:'Today, do one quick job you put off. Write a day next to each bigger one.',skills:['productivity','focus']},review:{prompt:'Three undone jobs keep popping into your head at work. What do you do?',right:'Do the quick ones now, and write a day for the rest',wrong:'Push them away and try harder to focus on work',why:'Undone jobs keep tugging at you, but a plan with a day calms them.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re trying to read, but you keep remembering your **unpacked bag**. Then the email you didn\u2019t send. Then the **library book**. Why do these little things keep popping up?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Jobs you haven\u2019t finished keep **tugging** at your mind. Dumont\u2019s fix was blunt: **finish them**. Later research adds a gentler fix. When you can\u2019t finish, write down exactly when you will. That often calms the tugging.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Little **unfinished jobs** quietly steal your focus. Finish the small ones. For the rest, write down exactly **when** you\u2019ll do them.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re halfway through a report. The **dentist appointment** you haven\u2019t booked surfaces. Then the unreturned call. You push each one aside, and each one **comes back**. What\u2019s going on?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The book noticed that **unfinished business** keeps drawing attention. It degrades focus on whatever\u2019s in front of you. The cost of an open item isn\u2019t paid once. It\u2019s paid **continuously**, for as long as it stays open.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Its fix is unsentimental: **finish small things** rather than letting them pile up. Later studies suggest a second route. A **specific plan** for when you\u2019ll do a task can ease much of the intrusion without finishing it.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Each nagging item is either **closed** or **given a plan**.',left:{title:'Done in minutes',items:['Do it now','Off your mind']},right:{title:'Bigger job',items:['Write the next action','Add a date','Then let it go']}},{kind:'moral',text:'The nagging comes from **ambiguity**, not the task itself. For each open item, write **the next action** and its date. Then your attention can come back.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'You close the laptop at six with **four things** half done. On the train home, they replay one by one. You weren\u2019t working on them. So why are they still **using your attention**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Dumont saw this a century ago. Unfinished business, he wrote, keeps pulling at the mind. His answer was **completion**: finish small things instead of letting them gather. Every open item costs a little, all the time.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Much later, experiments tested the effect. Some found **unfinished tasks** were recalled more readily than finished ones. But the finding hasn\u2019t **replicated consistently**. So treat it as a plausible pull, not a law.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take a manager with a dozen loose ends. She can\u2019t finish them tonight. So she writes each one down with a next step and a day. On the train, the list stays quiet. Nothing\u2019s done, but each has **a place**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The book only offers completion, which often **isn\u2019t available**. That overstates what you must do. Some later studies suggest a specific plan eases much of the intrusion. The plan, not the finish, may do the work.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Open items **tax your attention** while they stay vague. Finish the small ones. Give the rest **a next action** and a date, and let them wait.'}]},'power-of-concentration_5':{title:'The Loud Claim',card:{insight:'A claim said loudly and often feels true, even when it is not.',tryThis:'Today, pick one sure claim you hear. Ask how the person knows it.',skills:['thinking']},review:{prompt:'A book says the same thing five times, with no proof. How do you judge it?',right:'Skip the loud parts and test the part you can check',wrong:'Trust it more, since it sounds so sure and clear',why:'Saying a thing loudly and often does not make it true.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A cereal box shouts **super strong** in huge letters, three times. There\u2019s a big cartoon muscle on the front. Does shouting it make the cereal **any stronger**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Old self-help books could be like that box. The Power of Concentration says things **very loudly**, again and again. It never says **how it knows**. But under the noise, you\u2019ll find a few tips you can really test.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Loud isn\u2019t the same as **true**. When a book shouts, look for **the testable part**. Keep that, and leave the noise.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A bestseller promises to fix your life in ten short chapters. Every page sounds **completely sure**. There isn\u2019t a single source. By the end you feel convinced. But **convinced of what**, exactly?'},{kind:'story',text:'The Power of Concentration came out in the 1910s, in a commercial self-improvement series. The format shaped it: short chapters, confident claims, no citations. It also implies your difficulties are **entirely within your control**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Strip **the packaging** off and only **a small core** remains.',left:{title:'The core',items:['Attention is trainable','Practise on dull material','Decide in advance']},right:{title:'The packaging',items:['Will and magnetism','Claims without sources','Repetition for emphasis']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Why does the loud part persuade? **Repetition** with emphasis reads as conviction. But it adds **no information**. Certainty, brevity and no qualifications are exactly the signs a claim **hasn\u2019t been tested**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t let a confident voice stand in for **evidence**. Strip the packaging off, then **test the part** you can check. Often it\u2019s small, and worth having.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two books make the same claim. One hedges, cites studies and admits doubts. The other states it **five times** in capitals. Which one do most readers **find more convincing**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Dumont\u2019s book belonged to a commercial self-improvement series of the 1910s. Its chapters are short, its claims confident, its sources absent. Throughout, it implies that the reader\u2019s difficulties are **entirely their own** to fix.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The useful material is a **small portion**: attention is trainable, practise on dull things, decide in advance. The rest is filler of a particular kind. It\u2019s **assertion repeated** with emphasis, which sounds like conviction and says nothing new.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a modern productivity thread. Ten bold lines, no sources, every one stated as fact. You share it because it feels **so certain**. Its features match Dumont\u2019s format exactly, a century on.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Plain, confident writing isn\u2019t proof of a weak claim either. Some true advice is simply **stated well**. The signs tell you to check, not to throw the book away.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Certainty, brevity and no qualifications are **a format**, not evidence. Treat them as **a cue to check**. Then keep whatever survives the test.'}]},'power-of-concentration_6':{title:'The Still Chair',card:{insight:'A still body helps your mind stay put, so sit still before hard work.',tryThis:'Before your next hard task, sit still for two minutes. Notice how much you want to move.',skills:['focus','habits']},review:{prompt:'You keep getting up for a snack when your taxes get hard. What helps?',right:'Sit still for two minutes first, then stay seated',wrong:'Get up when it is hard, since a break clears your head',why:'Each time you stand up, your mind gets a way to run off too.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Here\u2019s a strange challenge. Sit on a chair and **don\u2019t move** at all for five minutes. No scratching, no shifting, no looking round. Is it easy, or **much harder** than it sounds?'},{kind:'explain',text:'When your mind wanders, your body often **wanders too**. You tap, shift or reach for a snack. Moving is **easier to spot** than thinking. So Dumont starts with the body: keep it still first.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Before you try to **still your mind**, still your body. Sit for two minutes with **hands on your knees**. Then starting the hard job feels easier.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your report gets hard, so you get up to make tea. Back at the desk, it gets hard again. You **get up again**. Is the problem the report, or the **getting up**?'},{kind:'story',text:'The book\u2019s first exercises are **physical**. Sit in a chair and don\u2019t move for five minutes. Hold one hand out level, without a tremor. Keep your eyes fixed on one point. The first is far harder than it sounds.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The book explains it with **nervous energy**, which is nonsense. A better account has two parts. Restlessness and distraction travel together, so stilling one closes **a route** for the other. And sitting down on purpose is **a small commitment**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your fidgeting is the **visible part** of a wandering mind. Sit still for two minutes, then **stay seated**. You\u2019ll find much of the difficulty was restlessness.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A man sits square in an empty room, **hands on his knees**, feet flat on the floor. He isn\u2019t working yet. He isn\u2019t even thinking hard. Why does a book on concentration **start here**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Dumont\u2019s drills begin with the body. Stillness in a chair for a set time. A hand held out level, with no tremor. The eyes fixed on a single point. Holding attention on an object comes **only later**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'His explanation, about controlling a vital force, doesn\u2019t hold. The practice survives it. Sitting deliberately is a **commitment device**: getting up now means breaking something you chose. Like putting on **a uniform**, the arrangement shapes what you do next.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take someone who opens a spreadsheet and reaches for her phone within a minute. She tries two still minutes first, phone face down. When she starts, getting up feels **more expensive**. She stays longer.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The effect is modest, and restlessness going with distraction doesn\u2019t prove stillness causes focus. Some people **think better moving**, on a walk or at a standing desk. For some with ADHD, enforced stillness may cost attention.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Begin with the body, because it\u2019s **easier to correct** than the mind. Treat stillness as **an opening trick**, not a law. If moving helps you think, keep moving.'}]},'power-of-concentration_7':{title:'Counting the Returns',card:{insight:'Your mind will drift, and each time you bring it back is practice.',tryThis:'Look at one plain mug for five minutes. Count each time you bring your mind back.',skills:['focus']},review:{prompt:'Twenty seconds into the drill, your mind drifts to lunch. What now?',right:'Bring your mind back calmly, and count it as one rep',wrong:'Stop, since a wandering mind means you are doing it wrong',why:'Each time you come back is one rep, so drifting is part of it.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'There\u2019s one plain **button** on an empty table. Your job is to look at it for five minutes. That\u2019s all. After ten seconds, your mind has gone off to think about **lunch**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Did you fail the drill? Not at all. Noticing you drifted and **coming back** is really the whole point. Each come-back is one **training rep**, like one lift of a weight.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your mind will wander, and that\u2019s fine. **Bring it back** calmly, every time. **The coming back** is what builds your focus.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You set a timer for five minutes and stare at **a coin**. By second twenty, you\u2019re planning dinner. You drag yourself back, annoyed. Then it happens **again**. Are you just bad at this?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The book\u2019s core drill has **three parts**. One object, so there\u2019s one clear place to return to. A fixed time, decided in advance, so you don\u2019t quit when it\u2019s uncomfortable. And **the return**, done without frustration.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Two people drift at twenty seconds. What they **make of it** decides who\u2019s **still going** in week three.',left:{title:'Wandering is failure',items:['Thinks I\u2019m bad at this','Gives up by day three']},right:{title:'The return counts',items:['Notices, and comes back','Counts each return calmly']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Practising on something you find interesting is like **training for a hill** on flat ground. It feels good and prepares you for little. **The dull object** is the hill, and each return is a step up.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A wandering mind isn\u2019t doing the drill **badly**. Each time you notice and come back calmly, you\u2019ve done **one rep**. So count your returns, not your failures.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture a quiet room, a timer and **a plain cup**. For five minutes you\u2019ll look at nothing else. The instructions say your mind will leave. They also say **what to do** when it does. What is it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The drill has **three working parts**. A single object, chosen to be dull. A duration settled beforehand. And the instruction to **return without frustration** each time attention wanders. That last part is the one the book gets right.'},{kind:'story',text:'Two people start the drill on the same day. Both drift within twenty seconds. One decides she\u2019s bad at it and stops by day three. The other treats each return as a rep and is **still going** in week three.'},{kind:'story',text:'The same structure turns up in **contemplative traditions**, where people rest attention on the breath. Dumont knew that world well, and under another pen name wrote books on yoga. Modern mindfulness teaching stresses the same calm return.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The drill trains staying with one dull object, and how far that carries into other work is **less certain**. A session can turn into self-criticism, which defeats it. Some people find long inward focus distressing.'},{kind:'moral',text:'If you read wandering as failure, you\u2019ll quit. If you read **the return** as the rep, you\u2019ll carry on. Keep sessions **short**, and count every return.'}]},'power-of-concentration_8':{title:'The Shared Bookshelf',card:{insight:'Most people know the same tips, so what sets them apart is what they do.',tryThis:'Pick one tip you already know. Use it for ten minutes before you read anything new.',skills:['habits','productivity']},review:{prompt:'You have read five books on focus and still cannot focus. What next?',right:'Pick one tip you already know and use it today',wrong:'Find a sixth book with better tips on focus',why:'You know the tips, so use one of them instead of reading more.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Everyone on your football team knows that **practice** makes you better. The coach has said it a hundred times. But only **some players** practise at home. Who gets better?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The ones who practise, of course. They didn\u2019t **know more** than the others, but they **did more**. Dumont\u2019s book says most people already know the same tips. Just knowing isn\u2019t what changes things.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You don\u2019t need **the next tip**. You need to **use the one** you have. Pick it, and do it today and tomorrow.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve read **five books** on focus. You could teach the tips: single-task, plan ahead, put the phone away. And yet your afternoons still dissolve. What\u2019s **actually missing**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Dumont\u2019s underlying observation is plain. People in the same trade have read the same material. What separates them is staying on one thing long enough to matter. And then doing it again.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'**Knowledge** is widely shared. **Application** isn\u2019t, and that\u2019s where people differ.',left:{title:'Knowing',items:['Read the tip','Nod along','Read one more tip']},right:{title:'Doing',items:['Pick one tip','Do it today','Do it again tomorrow']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s the awkward part for **a book**. Its advice wasn\u2019t new then and isn\u2019t new now. You probably knew it already. So the limit is rarely information, and **more reading** can\u2019t fix a failure to do.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You don\u2019t lack **information**. You lack the doing. Pick one tip you **already know**, and do it today instead of reading another.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A bookshop window holds the same title stacked **a dozen times**. Hundreds will read it. Behind the stacks, one person is working on a single sheet. Which of them will the book **actually change**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Dumont claims the difference between people isn\u2019t what they know but **what they consistently do** with it. It\u2019s overstated, and it points at **something real**. In most trades, the core advice is common property.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take two salespeople who sat the same training. Both know to follow up within a day. One does it every time. The other means to. A year later, their results look like **different jobs**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now look at the book itself. Having said knowing isn\u2019t the constraint, it keeps supplying **more knowing**. That\u2019s what the format rewards. A chapter can be sold. An instruction to stop reading and practise can\u2019t.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Sometimes the problem really is information: a wrong method practised hard stays wrong. And calling everything **an application problem** can turn into blame, when the real obstacle is time, health or circumstance. Ask which constraint binds.'},{kind:'moral',text:'In most trades, **everyone knows** roughly the same things. What you consistently do is the difference. So check **which constraint** binds you, then stop reading and practise.'}]},'power-of-concentration_9':{title:'Right Once, Not Always',card:{insight:'A person can be right about one thing and wrong about the next.',tryThis:'Today, pick one claim from someone you trust. Look for proof of it on its own.',skills:['thinking']},review:{prompt:'A book\u2019s focus drill works for you. It also says thoughts move people. Now what?',right:'Check that claim on its own before you believe it',wrong:'Trust it too, since the book was right before',why:'Being right about one thing does not prove the next claim.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A friend says there\u2019s a **test on Friday**. She\u2019s right. Then she tells you a dog **drove her car**. Do you believe the dog part too?'},{kind:'explain',text:'This book has good tips, like training focus with **short, dull drills**. You can test those. But it also says strong thoughts can **move other people** from far away. Nobody has ever shown that\u2019s true.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Some books mix true and false claims in **the same sure voice**. So check each claim **on its own**, even when you trust the book.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'On one page, a clear drill: sit still, hold attention on one object, bring it back. You try it, and **it helps**. A few chapters on, the same voice explains **thought at a distance**. Do you believe it?'},{kind:'story',text:'The Power of Concentration does exactly this. Beside its sound exercises sit passages on **thought transference**, **personal magnetism** and influencing others from afar. They\u2019re written in the same confident tone as the breathing drills.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s how the mix works. The drill is **checkable**, and it works, so your trust rises. Then the unsupported claims arrive in the same tone and **inherit that trust**. Nothing in the voice warns you that the kind of claim has changed.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Read side by side, the book holds **two different kinds** of claim. Only **one kind** survives checking.',left:{title:'Checkable',items:['Practise attention on a dull object','Sit still before working']},right:{title:'Unsupported',items:['Thought moves people at a distance','Will reshapes your health']}},{kind:'moral',text:'A source that\u2019s right about one thing **earns a hearing**, not blanket trust. Check each claim on its own, and ask if it\u2019s **a different kind**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two weights sit side by side and look **identical** from the front. Lift one and it\u2019s solid. Turn the other and you see it\u2019s **hollow**. How would you tell them apart without turning them?'},{kind:'story',text:'Alongside the drills, Dumont claims concentrated thought can influence people directly. He says will can shape physical health, and that enough mental effort produces material results. That last is the prosperity doctrine of the period.'},{kind:'explain',text:'These aren\u2019t **dated phrasings** of defensible ideas. They were unevidenced then and have been refuted since. Their use now is as **a contrast**. They show exactly where a text stops being supported and starts asserting.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a podcast host who gets one sleep tip exactly right. You try it, and you sleep better. Next week he recommends a supplement with no evidence behind it. Your trust arrives **before you check**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The lesson can be **over-learned**. One false claim doesn\u2019t make the rest false. Throwing out the drills because the book sells magnetism loses something real. Check both directions.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A source earns credibility with **something checkable**, then can spend it on something that isn\u2019t. Judge **each kind of claim** on its own evidence. That works in both directions.'}]}}}},985,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'principles-of-psychology',kind:'book',promise:'Make your habits and your mind work for you',about:'Why do some days run on their own while others wear you out? In 1890, a Harvard teacher named William James wrote a big book on the mind. Here are his best ideas for using yours. Which one will you try first?',ideas:{'principles-of-psychology_1':{title:'The Habit Flywheel',card:{insight:'A habit is hard to start, but then it carries you on hard days.',tryThis:'Tonight, list your weekly habits. Mark the ones you would pick again.',skills:['habits','psychology']},review:{prompt:'You have walked to work every day for five years. Why is it easy now?',right:'The habit you started long ago now carries you',wrong:'You are just a tougher person than most people',why:'The hard push came at the start, and now the habit does the work.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You brush your teeth **every morning**. You never stop to decide. Even on a sleepy, grumpy day, it just happens. So what\u2019s **doing the work** when you\u2019re not trying?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A habit is like a **heavy wheel** in a machine. It\u2019s hard to get spinning. Once it turns, it keeps going on its own. William James, writing in 1890, called habit a **flywheel**. It carries you on days you don\u2019t feel like trying.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The hard push comes **at the start**. After that, the wheel does the work. But it spins whichever way you set it, so **check your wheels**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve done your job the same way for **six years**. Most mornings you don\u2019t feel motivated at all. Yet you turn up, every day. How much of that did you **actually decide**?'},{kind:'story',text:'William James gives two examples. A fisherman keeps going to sea through the winter. A miner keeps going down into the dark. James isn\u2019t praising their grit. The decision was made long ago, and the wheel is still turning.'},{kind:'explain',text:'A **flywheel** is the heavy wheel in an old engine. It stores energy while the engine pushes. Then it carries the machine when nothing pushes. Habit does that for a day. **Momentum** built while you were keen carries you through the rest.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The same **effort** buys very different amounts, depending on **when** you spend it.',left:{title:'Spent early',items:['Goes into a forming habit','Buys years without deciding','Paid once']},right:{title:'Spent later',items:['Goes into daily arguments with yourself','Buys one day at a time','Runs out']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Habit is a **flywheel**: hard to start, then it runs by itself. Spend your effort **early**, while a habit forms. And remember it turns whichever way it was set.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Think of a fisherman heading out to sea on a **freezing winter** morning. The wind is bitter. He goes anyway, year after year. What\u2019s carrying him, if it **isn\u2019t grit**?'},{kind:'story',text:'William James taught at Harvard. In 1890 he published *The Principles of Psychology*, after about twelve years of work. Its chapter on habit argues that habits are physical. Repetition reshapes the nervous system, so the body does alone what once took a decision.'},{kind:'explain',text:'James called habit the **flywheel of society**. It stores the push of old decisions and keeps lives turning long after anyone chooses. The fisherman isn\u2019t running on grit. He\u2019s running on **momentum**, set when his choice was fresh.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a team that runs a weekly meeting nobody remembers designing. Nobody likes it, and nobody cancels it. It isn\u2019t anyone\u2019s choice any more. It\u2019s a flywheel, still spinning from a push someone gave years ago.'},{kind:'explain',text:'That\u2019s why James meant it as **praise and warning** at once. The wheel doesn\u2019t care which way it spins. Your best routines and your worst ones run on **the same mechanism**, and both feel like who you are.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. A fisherman may stay at sea because there\u2019s **no other work**, not because of momentum. Momentum explains why a life persists. It doesn\u2019t justify it, and habits can still change, at a cost.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Most of your week runs on **old decisions**. That\u2019s a gift when they were good ones. So check **which wheels** you\u2019d set spinning again today, and which you\u2019re only maintaining.'}]},'principles-of-psychology_2':{title:'The Ball of String',card:{insight:'When a habit is new, one slip undoes more than a good day adds.',tryThis:'Pick your newest habit. Keep it every day this week, even on a bad day.',skills:['habits']},review:{prompt:'Two weeks ago, you stopped bringing your phone to bed. Is one night off fine?',right:'No, keep the rule, since a slip undoes a lot early on',wrong:'Yes, one night out of fourteen is too small to matter',why:'While a habit is new, one slip costs more than one good night earns.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve started practising guitar **every day**. It\u2019s day three, and you\u2019re tired. Your friends want you to come out. Surely **one day off** can\u2019t matter much, can it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Picture winding string into a **neat ball**. It takes lots of slow turns. Then you drop it, and it unrolls across the floor. William James used this picture for **new habits**. One slip undoes many turns.'},{kind:'moral',text:'While a habit is new, **don\u2019t skip** a single day. On a bad day, do a tiny bit. Once it\u2019s strong, a missed day is **just a missed day**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve started writing for twenty minutes **before work**. On day four the train comes early and the plan collapses. It\u2019s only one morning. Does skipping it really cost you **just one day**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'James borrowed an image from the Scottish psychologist **Alexander Bain**. Winding a ball of string takes many careful turns. Drop it once, and it unrolls further than many turns win back. That **lopsided cost** is the whole argument.'},{kind:'explain',text:'So the rule is strict: allow **no exceptions** while a new habit is forming. An early lapse undoes more than a good day builds. But the strictness is **front-loaded**. Once the habit is rooted, nothing unwinds.'},{kind:'story',text:'Back on the platform, you don\u2019t skip. You write for five minutes while you wait. The habit still gets its repetition. And *today is different* never gets the chance to become a rule.'},{kind:'moral',text:'In the first weeks, a lapse is **expensive**. So decide the smallest version that still counts, and keep the streak. The strictness is **temporary**, and that\u2019s exactly why it\u2019s worth it.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Near the end of his chapter on habit, James stops describing and starts **giving orders**. There are a handful of practical rules. One of them sounds **almost harsh**. Why would a careful scientist get so strict?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The first rule: start a new habit with as **strong a start** as you can. The second, taken from Alexander Bain: allow **no exception** until the new habit is securely rooted. Each lapse costs more than a repetition earns.'},{kind:'story',text:'Sam has walked the dog every evening for one week. Tonight his friends ask him out to play football. He walks the dog first, quickly, then goes. At one week old, the habit might not survive a night off.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Why does a lapse cost so much? The new habit is up against an **old tendency** with a head start. Every win for the old one strengthens it. Early on, the new habit has little stored up, so **never lose a battle**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture someone who has run every morning for two years. She wakes with a cold and stays in bed. Nothing unwinds. Next morning she runs as usual. For her, a missed day is only a missed day.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Later research on everyday habits is kinder: missing a single day seemed to do **little lasting harm**. Rigid all-or-nothing rules can backfire too. When the streak breaks, people often abandon the whole effort.'},{kind:'moral',text:'James\u2019s absolute rule **overreaches**. But its shape survives: early lapses deserve far more care than late ones. So guard **your newest habit** hardest, and don\u2019t panic over one missed day later.'}]},'principles-of-psychology_3':{title:'The Price of Attention',card:{insight:'When you pick one thing to focus on, you let the rest go.',tryThis:'Next time you sit down to work, pick one thing. When your mind drifts, bring it back.',skills:['focus','psychology']},review:{prompt:'Forty small things broke up your morning. What would James say happened?',right:'The loudest thing chose each of the forty for you',wrong:'You have bad focus and need to try harder',why:'You did pay attention, but you let the loudest thing choose each time.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You sit down to do your **maths homework**. Your phone buzzes, the TV is on next door, and the dog wants to play. Twenty minutes later, the maths **isn\u2019t done**. Where did the time go?'},{kind:'explain',text:'William James said paying attention means **picking one thing** out of many. When you pick one, you let the others go. If you don\u2019t pick at all, **the loudest thing** picks for you.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your mind will wander. That\u2019s normal. The real skill is **bringing it back**, again and again, to the thing **you chose**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your morning is a blur of **small interruptions**: emails, messages, a quick look at the news. By lunch you\u2019ve done nothing you planned. Did you lack focus? Or was something else **choosing for you**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'William James defined attention as the mind taking hold, clearly and vividly, of **one thing** out of several possible ones. The cost is inside the definition. To hold one thing, you **let the rest go**.'},{kind:'story',text:'So look again at that morning. You didn\u2019t lack focus. You attended, vividly and in turn, to forty things. Each one was chosen for you by whatever was loudest at the time.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Distraction isn\u2019t **an absence** of focus. It\u2019s a run of choices **left unmanaged**.',left:{title:'Left to chance',items:['The loudest thing wins','Forty objects, one after another','Focus feels missing']},right:{title:'Chosen early',items:['You pick the object first','You notice each drift','You bring it back']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Every minute of attention is **bought** with everything you didn\u2019t attend to. So choose the object **early**, before anything else does. Then practise the return.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'James opens his chapter on attention with a cheeky move. He says **everyone already knows** what attention is. Then he spends pages pinning it down. Why define something so **obvious**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Because the definition hides **a price**. Attention is the mind seizing one of several things that seem open to it at once. Focusing on one means **withdrawing** from the others. You never get the rest for free.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a morning of small interruptions. You\u2019d call it a morning without focus. James would disagree. You attended, clearly and in turn, to forty things. Someone else, or something loud, chose each one.'},{kind:'explain',text:'He also noticed that attention to one **unchanging object** can\u2019t be held for more than a few seconds. What we call sustained attention is **repeated return**. He thought good judgement and strong character grow from that power to come back.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take someone practising a simple breathing exercise. Her mind drifts to lunch, then to an email. Each time she notices, she comes back. She isn\u2019t failing at the exercise. The coming back *is* the exercise.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. A mind that lets nothing in can miss **the unexpected**, like the smell of smoke. Some work, such as caring for a small child, is interrupted by design. Choosing early can\u2019t change that.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You can\u2019t fence out the world, and you shouldn\u2019t try. But you can **own more** of the choices. Pick your object early, and treat every return as **the skill itself**.'}]},'principles-of-psychology_4':{title:'The Stream, Not the Chain',card:{insight:'Your thoughts flow like a stream, so a break costs more than a pause.',tryThis:'When someone breaks in on your work, see how long it takes to start again.',skills:['focus','psychology']},review:{prompt:'A coworker says a quick break in your work only costs a minute. Is that true?',right:'No, because the stream breaks and needs time to flow again',wrong:'Yes, you just pick up right where you left off',why:'Thought flows like a stream, so a break means you must get it moving again.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'On the way home, you think about lunch, then **the smell of chips**, then a beach trip last summer. Then your cousin pops up. Where did one thought stop and the **next one start**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'William James said your mind isn\u2019t a **row of beads**, one thought after another. It\u2019s a **stream** that keeps flowing. That\u2019s why being stopped mid-puzzle hurts. The flow breaks, and it takes time to get going again.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your thoughts flow like **a river**, not a chain. When someone breaks the flow, you **don\u2019t just pause**. You have to start the current again.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Try to remember **the exact moment** you decided to leave a job, or drift from a friend. Most people can\u2019t find it. There **wasn\u2019t one**. So where did the decision come from?'},{kind:'story',text:'In James\u2019s day, many psychologists pictured the mind as a chain of separate ideas, linked end to end. James thought nothing in experience is jointed like that. Your decision was a long run of feelings and half-thoughts.'},{kind:'explain',text:'His word was the **stream of thought**. Thought flows, and the transitions between thoughts are **part of the content**. A chain hides those transitions. Yet that\u2019s where much of the interesting work happens.'},{kind:'explain',text:'This has a cost for **your working day**. An interruption isn\u2019t a pause after which you pick up the same link. The **current breaks**, and it has to re-form. That\u2019s why four unbroken hours can beat eight broken ones.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your mind is **a stream**, not a chain of separate links. Protect long stretches of **continuity**. The real input isn\u2019t the hours but the unbroken flow.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re trying to recall **a name**. It\u2019s on the tip of your tongue. Someone suggests one, and you know at once it\u2019s wrong. How can **an empty gap** tell right from wrong?'},{kind:'explain',text:'James used this exact test. The gap where the name should be **isn\u2019t empty**. It\u2019s active, rejecting wrong names the moment they appear. A gap that can judge has content, and no **chain of ideas** can explain it.'},{kind:'story',text:'In James\u2019s time, many psychologists were hunting for the basic units of experience, the way a chemist looks for elements. James argued the units were never there. There was only the flow, and he named it the stream of thought.'},{kind:'explain',text:'If thought is **a current**, an interruption doesn\u2019t pause it. It breaks it, and the current has to **re-form** before the work can continue. Many people notice that four unbroken hours produce what eight broken ones can\u2019t.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a writer whose phone pings every ten minutes. Each ping takes seconds to check. But each time, she has to find her thread again. By evening, she\u2019s worked eight hours and written very little.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The stream describes how experience **feels**, not how the brain makes it. Psychology later made real progress by measuring parts anyway. And the office lesson is **an extension**: James wasn\u2019t studying work.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your experience is **a stream**, and its transitions carry meaning. Treat **continuity** as something worth guarding. When you protect the flow, you protect the work it makes possible.'}]},'principles-of-psychology_5':{title:'Routine as a Budget',card:{insight:'Each small choice you hand to habit leaves more of your mind for what counts.',tryThis:'Pick one choice you make every day, like breakfast. Make it the same all week.',skills:['habits','focus']},review:{prompt:'You spend ten minutes each morning picking what to wear. What would James say?',right:'Hand it to habit, so your mind is free for what counts',wrong:'Keep choosing each day, since it only takes ten minutes',why:'Hand the small choices you make each day to habit, and your mind is free.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'By bedtime, you\u2019ve made **lots of little choices**. What to wear, what to eat, which shoes, which way to walk. None of them was hard. So why do you feel **so tired**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Each choice uses **a little attention**. William James said your brain and nerves learn from whatever you repeat. So turn jobs that are **the same each day** into habits, like packing your bag.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your body will learn **whatever you repeat**, good or bad. So teach it on purpose. Then your mind is free for the things that **really need it**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'It\u2019s ten in the morning and **you\u2019re tired**. You\u2019ve done nothing yet except decide things: what to wear, which train, what to eat, which email first. None was hard. So what **wore you out**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'James\u2019s instruction was to make **the nervous system** your ally, not your enemy. It will act automatically whatever you do. So the only question is **what you\u2019ve trained** it to do. Hand routine to habit, and attention is left over.'},{kind:'explain',text:'He saw habit as physical: **repetition changes** the pathway. So every repetition is a deposit, including the ones you never meant to make. There\u2019s **no neutral day**. You either choose your deposits or drift into them.'},{kind:'story',text:'On Sunday you resolve to start running. Monday passes with no run. You haven\u2019t just failed to build a habit. You\u2019ve practised resolving without acting, and trained the wrong pathway. James\u2019s rule: act at the very first chance.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Routine isn\u2019t the enemy of **a thoughtful life**. It\u2019s the budget for one. Train your nervous system on purpose, and act on each resolution **straight away**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'James describes a person for whom **nothing is habit** except indecision. Every cup, every bedtime, every start of work is a fresh debate. He could think of few people **more miserable**. Why?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Because half that person\u2019s time goes on deciding, or regretting, what should be **automatic**. James\u2019s fix is to make **the nervous system** your ally. It will act on its own whatever you do. You only choose what it learns.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take a man who resolves on Sunday to start running. Monday comes and goes with no run. He thinks nothing has happened. In fact he\u2019s rehearsed deciding without doing, and taught his body they\u2019re unrelated.'},{kind:'explain',text:'James\u2019s **fourth maxim** is stranger. Do something hard each day for no reason except that you\u2019d **rather not**. He compared it to insurance on a house. It costs a little now and pays out when a real test comes.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture someone who packs her bag the night before, in the same order, every time. After a few weeks she never thinks about it. Her mornings start calm, with her attention free for the day\u2019s real work.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. James couldn\u2019t see pathways change, so his physiology was **an educated guess**. The popular claim that each decision drains a fixed store of willpower has proved hard to repeat. And what\u2019s automated stops being examined.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your nervous system **will learn** something today either way. Choose the deposits instead of drifting into them. Automate the small things, then spend **your attention** where it counts.'}]},'principles-of-psychology_6':{title:'Open Loops',card:{insight:'Jobs you leave undone keep tugging at you, even when you rest.',tryThis:'Write down every job you have left undone. Then finish the easiest one right now.',skills:['focus','productivity']},review:{prompt:'Your week felt heavy, but nothing in it was hard. What wore you out?',right:'The undone jobs still pulling at your mind',wrong:'The hard jobs, since hard work is what tires you',why:'Many small undone jobs can tire you more than one hard job.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'It\u2019s Saturday and nothing hard is going on, but **you feel heavy**. You still owe a friend a text, and your library book is late. Why does a day with **nothing in it** feel so tiring?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Each job you **haven\u2019t done** sits at the back of your mind. Now and then it pops up, and each pop costs a little. **None of them** goes away by itself.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Lots of small undone jobs can tire you more than **one big job** that\u2019s done. So close **a tiny one**, and feel the difference.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your week felt crushing. Yet when you look back, **nothing in it** was hard. An unanswered message, a decision put off, a talk you haven\u2019t had. Why did such **small things** weigh so much?'},{kind:'explain',text:'This idea is **often credited** to William James, though it isn\u2019t in *The Principles*. It says mental fatigue tracks **unfinished business** more than hard work. A day of hard, finished tasks tires you. A day of easy, unfinished ones flattens you.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Each **open loop** keeps a claim on your attention. The claim charges you continuously, whether or not you\u2019re working on it. Its weight grows with **how long** it\u2019s been open and how often it resurfaces.'},{kind:'story',text:'So you answer the message. It takes two minutes. The relief is far bigger than the task. That looks irrational, but it isn\u2019t. You didn\u2019t finish a small job. You stopped paying a standing charge.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Count your **open loops**, not your tasks. None of them closes by itself. Close the cheap ones and you\u2019ll feel **lighter** than the work deserves.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture two days. On the first, you finish **three hard tasks**. On the second, you touch **ten easy ones** and finish none. Which evening leaves you flatter, and why?'},{kind:'explain',text:'An observation **often credited** to James, though not made in *The Principles*, says the second. Fatigue doesn\u2019t follow how hard the work was. It follows **what\u2019s unresolved**, because each open thing keeps drawing on attention.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take a manager with a light week on paper. But there\u2019s an email she\u2019s avoided, a hiring decision she\u2019s deferred, a conversation she keeps postponing. None is difficult. Together they leave her exhausted by Wednesday.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The weight of a loop isn\u2019t set by **difficulty**. It grows with how long the loop has been open and how often it resurfaces. Each is cheap alone. None closes **by itself**, so the load quietly builds.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture someone who finally replies to a two-line email she\u2019s dodged for a month. It takes ninety seconds. The relief is out of all proportion. She hasn\u2019t just finished a small task. She\u2019s stopped a standing charge.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Experiments on unfinished tasks came later, starting with Bluma Zeigarnik in the 1920s, and the results are **mixed**. And some decisions deserve to stay open until you know more. A written plan can quiet them.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Count **your open loops**, not just your tasks. Close the cheap ones fast. For the rest, a written plan for when you\u2019ll deal with them can **ease their claim**.'}]},'principles-of-psychology_7':{title:'The Self-Esteem Fraction',card:{insight:'You feel better about yourself if you do more or claim less.',tryThis:'Name one thing you say you do but do not. Do it this week, or stop saying it.',skills:['psychology']},review:{prompt:'You still say you play guitar, but you have not played in two years. Now what?',right:'Play again, or stop saying that you play',wrong:'Keep saying you play, and plan to start again someday',why:'Doing more and claiming less both help, but claiming without doing hurts.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You tell everyone **you play piano**. But you haven\u2019t practised in months, and it nags at you every time you walk past it. Is there a way to **stop the nagging**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'William James wrote how you feel about yourself as **a fraction**. On top is what you manage. Underneath is what you claim. A fraction grows if the top grows. It **also grows** if the bottom shrinks.'},{kind:'moral',text:'There are **two honest fixes**. Practise again, or say *I used to play* and **feel lighter**. Both make the fraction bigger.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A guitar has stood in the corner for **two years**. You still call yourself someone who plays. Every time you walk past it, there\u2019s **a small stab** of guilt. Where\u2019s it coming from?'},{kind:'explain',text:'William James set self-esteem out as **a fraction**: success divided by pretensions. Success is what you achieve. **Pretensions** are what you claim for yourself. A fraction moves in both directions equally, and that\u2019s the part people skip.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The guitar leaves you **two honest exits**, and one position that only **costs**.',left:{title:'Honest exits',items:['Practise, and raise the top','Stop claiming to be someone who plays']},right:{title:'The costly middle',items:['Keep the claim','Skip the hours','Pay in guilt daily']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Because self-esteem is a fraction, **dropping a pretension** works as well as fulfilling one. Holding the claim without the hours is **the only losing** position.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Imagine being the **second-best** boxer in the world. You can beat everyone on earth except one person. James says such a man may feel **deeply ashamed**. How can nearly winning feel like losing?'},{kind:'explain',text:'James wrote self-esteem as **success over pretensions**. The boxer has staked himself on beating that one rival. So his pretension is huge, and the fraction stays small. Beating **everyone else** counts for nothing.'},{kind:'story',text:'James admits the same of himself. He\u2019d staked himself on psychology, so he felt mortified when others knew more of it. Yet he was perfectly content to know no Greek. He\u2019d never claimed it.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Because it\u2019s a fraction, **lowering the bottom** works exactly as well as raising the top. James noticed how light people feel the day they stop striving to be young or slender. And **how rarely** they think of it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a dusty guitar in your corner. You haven\u2019t played in two years but still call yourself a player. Each glance brings a stab of guilt. You\u2019re paying for the claim daily and getting nothing back.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The fraction is a figure of speech, not a measurement. Some pretensions pull you to grow. Others are **duties**, not vanities: a parent can\u2019t lower the claim to keep a child safe.'},{kind:'moral',text:'For **optional claims**, both halves of the fraction are open to you. Either do the work or drop the claim. For duties, only **the top** can move.'}]},'principles-of-psychology_8':{title:'Act Your Way In',card:{insight:'You cannot choose to feel brave, but you can choose how you move.',tryThis:'Next time you dread something, take a two-minute walk first. Then do the hard thing.',skills:['psychology','health']},review:{prompt:'You sit still and dread a job interview that starts in ten minutes. What helps?',right:'Stand up and walk, so your body leads the feeling',wrong:'Sit still and go over every way it could go wrong',why:'Your body is a big part of how you feel, so move it first.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'It\u2019s five minutes before **the school play**. You\u2019re sitting in a corner, going over your lines and **feeling sick**. The more you worry, the worse it gets. What could you do instead?'},{kind:'explain',text:'William James said your body is **a big part** of how you feel. Sitting and worrying keeps your body tense. Then the tense body feels like proof you should worry. Moving **breaks the circle** from the outside.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You can\u2019t just decide to **feel brave**. But you can stand tall, breathe slowly and move. The feeling often **follows the body**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Ten minutes before **a difficult call**, you sit still and rehearse the dread. Your heart races. Then the racing heart feels like proof that **you\u2019re not ready**. Sound familiar?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Common sense says you see a bear, feel afraid, then run. William James turned that around. The body responds first, he argued, and **the feeling** is your sense of that response. The bodily change isn\u2019t a result of the emotion. It\u2019s **much of it**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'You can\u2019t order a feeling, but you can **change its inputs**, and those are **in your hands**.',left:{title:'Out of reach',items:['Deciding to feel calm','Arguing yourself into confidence']},right:{title:'In reach',items:['Stand up and walk','Slow your breathing','Speak like the confident you']}},{kind:'moral',text:'To change a feeling, **change the body** first. Stand, walk, slow your breathing, speak up. The feeling follows the posture **more readily** than the reverse.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'You see a bear in the woods. Common sense says you **feel afraid**, then you run. William James thought that order was **backwards**. What if the body moves first?'},{kind:'story',text:'James first set out the idea in an essay in 1884, then gave it a chapter in *The Principles*. The Danish physician Carl Lange reached a similar view at about the same time. So it\u2019s known as the James\u2013Lange theory.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The claim is that **bodily change** isn\u2019t a consequence of emotion but much of its substance. The feeling is your perception of the body\u2019s response. So **the lever** on a feeling is what your body is doing.'},{kind:'explain',text:'James drew **practical rules** from it himself. Give a strong feeling no outward outlet, and it tends to fade. To grow a mood you lack, copy **its outward signs**: sit tall, relax your face, lift your voice.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a woman waiting to give a hard piece of news at work. Instead of sitting and rehearsing, she walks the corridor and talks through the first line aloud. Her breathing slows. The dread doesn\u2019t vanish, but it loosens.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The strong version didn\u2019t survive. In the 1920s Walter Cannon objected that different emotions share **similar bodily changes**. Later posture studies found small, inconsistent effects. And acting cheerful can bury real grief.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your body\u2019s state is **part of the feeling**, even if not all of it. You can\u2019t command a mood. But you can change its inputs, and that\u2019s **often enough** to help.'}]},'principles-of-psychology_9':{title:'The Threads of Memory',card:{insight:'Rereading helps less than tying each new fact to what you know.',tryThis:'After your next class or meeting, link one new fact to two things you know.',skills:['thinking','psychology']},review:{prompt:'You have a test next week. Which study plan fits James best?',right:'Tie each fact to things you know, then explain it',wrong:'Reread your notes five times until they stick',why:'The memory you are born with stays the same, so more links help most.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve got a test on **volcanoes** tomorrow. You could read the same page five times. Or you could do something else. Which way will make the facts **actually stick**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'William James thought the memory you\u2019re born with **can\u2019t grow**. What can grow is the number of **links**. Shaking a fizzy drink, then opening it, is a bit like a volcano. Tell your brother about it, or draw it. Now the fact has many threads.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You can\u2019t make your memory **bigger**. But you can tie each new fact to **lots of things** you already know. More links means more ways to find it again.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You read a chapter **three times** before an exam. It feels familiar every time. A week later, you can barely recall a page. Where did **all that reading** go?'},{kind:'explain',text:'William James was blunt: the **raw retentiveness** of a mind can\u2019t be trained. What changes is how many connections a fact is tied into. A fact attached to nothing has **one thread**. A fact attached to ten things has ten.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Re-reading feels like progress, but it adds **exposure**, not **threads**.',left:{title:'Adds exposure',items:['Re-reading the chapter','Feeling it look familiar']},right:{title:'Adds threads',items:['Explain it to someone','Link it to what you know','Argue against it']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Memory is mostly the residue of **how hard you thought**. So don\u2019t re-read. Explain it, apply it, link it to something you **already know**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Can you train your memory like a muscle? William James decided to **test it on himself**. He picked two poems and timed himself. What do you think he **found**?'},{kind:'story',text:'First he timed himself learning lines of a Victor Hugo poem. Then for several weeks he learned part of Milton\u2019s *Paradise Lost* every day. Then he timed himself on more Hugo. He wasn\u2019t faster. If anything, he was slower.'},{kind:'explain',text:'His conclusion: **native retentiveness** is fixed. What improves memory is how you record facts, weaving each into **a system** of relations. People who remember a lot rarely have better hardware. They\u2019ve tied each fact to more.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take two students on the same course. One re-reads her notes every night. The other explains each topic to a flatmate and links it to last year\u2019s work. On James\u2019s view, the second has given the facts far more threads.'},{kind:'explain',text:'That makes forgetting mean **something different**. If memory isn\u2019t a faculty you can drill, then forgetting a course usually shows the thinking **was never done**. You keep what you thought hard about.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. His test was one man timing himself, **too crude** to settle anything. Well-learned facts still fade with time. And testing yourself, rather than re-reading, strengthens memory even without new links.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You can\u2019t upgrade **the hardware**, but you can add threads. Explain, apply, argue and test. Each one gives a fact **another way** back to you.'}]}}}},986,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'reading-in-public',kind:'book',promise:'Keep reading when a book feels too hard',about:'What do you do when a book feels too hard? This short essay by the Mindroll team says most good books feel that way at first. It gives ways to keep going, from reading fast to talking back. Which hard book will you try again?',ideas:{'reading-in-public_1':{title:'Lost at the Start',card:{insight:'Feeling lost early in a hard book is normal, so keep reading.',tryThis:'Pick a book you quit. Read one more chapter and see if things start to fit.',skills:['thinking','habits']},review:{prompt:'Twenty pages into an old classic, you feel lost. What now?',right:'Keep reading and see if things start to fit',wrong:'Put it back, because it is too hard for you',why:'Feeling lost early in a hard book is normal, and later pages help.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You start a book your gran loved at your age. Chapter one is full of **strange names** and places. You can\u2019t tell who anyone is. Is this book **just not for you**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A text message is made to be understood **straight away**. A hard book often isn\u2019t. Its first pages set up words and ideas that **make sense later**. So feeling lost early is how hard books begin, not proof you\u2019re failing.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Being lost at the start is **normal**. Being lost and **going nowhere** is different. If nothing fits after a few chapters, that\u2019s the time to stop.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A friend recommends a classic. Twenty pages in, you\u2019ve met **terms you can\u2019t define** and an argument you can\u2019t follow. You put it back on the shelf. Was that **the right call**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Priya, who starts a hard book on economics. Most of what she reads each day is news and messages, written to be grasped at once. So when chapter one won\u2019t open up, she assumes the fault is hers.'},{kind:'explain',text:'But a serious book expects you to **reach the end**. Its first chapters introduce terms that pay off much later. So Priya\u2019s confusion tells her about **the book\u2019s difficulty**. It says nothing about **her ability**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The real question isn\u2019t whether you\u2019re confused. It\u2019s whether **the confusion** is **going somewhere**.',left:{title:'Keep going',items:['Lost, but pieces start fitting','Terms return with meaning']},right:{title:'Time to stop',items:['Lost, and nothing fits','Chapters pass with no shape']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Expect not to follow a hard book\u2019s opening, and **keep reading** anyway. Quit only when the confusion is **going nowhere**. Telling the two apart is a skill you build by pushing on.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Most of what you read in a day is built to be **understood at once**: news, emails, messages. Then you open a serious book, and it isn\u2019t. What has that daily diet **trained you** to expect?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Being confused early is **exactly right**. A serious book is written for someone who\u2019ll reach the end. Its early terms only **come clear later**. Read the fog as failure and you\u2019ll **drop the very books** that had most to teach you.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture someone opening a classic of philosophy. By page twenty she has met words she can\u2019t define. She reads on anyway. Around chapter four, those early terms start turning up again, now with their meaning filled in.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a second reader with a different book. Five chapters in, nothing has started to fit. No term has come back clearer, and no direction has appeared. His confusion feels different from hers. It isn\u2019t going anywhere.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some confusion is the writer\u2019s fault, and the rule can excuse **a badly written** book. And some books need background you don\u2019t have yet. Pressing on won\u2019t supply it, so **a simpler book** first might.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Expect a hard book\u2019s opening to lose you. Judge it by whether the confusion is **going somewhere**, not by whether it\u2019s there. That judgement is **a skill**, and you learn it by continuing.'}]},'reading-in-public_2':{title:'Read Fast the First Time',card:{insight:'Read a hard book once, fast, to see its shape before you go slow.',tryThis:'Open a book you got stuck in. Mark the hard spot, then read past it for ten minutes.',skills:['habits','thinking']},review:{prompt:'You are forty pages into a hard book, stuck on one word. What next?',right:'Mark it and keep reading, so later pages can explain it',wrong:'Stop and read it again until that one word makes sense',why:'Later pages often explain the early ones, so keep going on the first read.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Before you start a jigsaw, you look at **the picture** on the box. You don\u2019t know where each piece goes yet. So why does that picture **help so much**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A hard book works the same way. Read it all the way through once, **quite fast**, even if you miss a lot. Then you know **its shape**. The second time, every hard bit has a place to go.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t stop at **every hard bit** the first time through. Mark it and **keep going**. Later pages often explain the earlier ones.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re **forty pages** into a dense book. A key term on page twelve still makes no sense. You go back and read it again, then again. Is that **careful reading**, or a trap?'},{kind:'story',text:'Think of Tom, reading a big history book for a course. He refuses to move on until each page is clear. Three weeks later he\u2019s still in chapter two. The chapter that explains everything sits unread at the end.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s why. In a hard book, **later chapters** often explain the early ones, and terms gain meaning as they recur. You only see the argument\u2019s shape **from the end**. So slow work at the start happens **without the context** it needs.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The essay credits this advice to Mortimer Adler\u2019s *How to Read a Book*. Go through once **at speed**, and accept you\u2019ll miss a lot. That quick pass gives you **a rough map**. Your slower second read then knows where each piece belongs.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your first pass through a hard book isn\u2019t for understanding. It\u2019s for **shape**. Finish it once **at speed**, then slow down for the hard parts.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Most careful readers treat **page one** as the place to work hardest. Every term pinned down, every sentence understood. It sounds like good discipline. So why do careful readers so often **never finish**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A difficult book is often not built to be understood **in order**. Later sections explain earlier ones. Terms settle as they\u2019re used. The essay credits the fix to Mortimer Adler: read it through once quickly, accepting **you\u2019ll miss** much.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a reader with a dense book on economics. On her first pass she skims, marks what puzzles her, and reaches the end in a week. On the second, half her marks answer themselves.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a colleague given the same book. He insists on mastering each page before turning it. A month on, he knows chapter one in detail. He has never seen the chapter that explains it.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some texts build step by step, like **maths or law**, where each part needs the last. A fast pass through them leaves no map. And a careless first read can leave **a wrong map** you\u2019ll have to undo.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Careful attention at the start is paid **before the context** arrives. So read once fast to see the shape, then slow down. You\u2019ll need to **tolerate not understanding** for a while.'}]},'reading-in-public_3':{title:'Argue in the Margin',card:{insight:'Writing where you disagree as you read helps a book stick.',tryThis:'Next time you read, write three words in the margin where you disagree. Later, check if you were right.',skills:['thinking','habits']},review:{prompt:'You are reading a hard chapter with a pencil. Which mark helps most?',right:'A few words where you disagree with the book',wrong:'A neat line under every sentence that seems important',why:'A mark helps when it holds a thought of your own.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your class reads an article saying school should **start an hour later**. You think: *but then it ends later too!* What should you **do with that thought**?'},{kind:'story',text:'You write it on a sticky note beside the words. Two paragraphs on, the writer answers your exact point. You were wrong, but now you know why. And that answer is more likely to stick.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When you read, **argue back**. Write your objection next to the words. Even when you\u2019re wrong, **finding out why** is how you learn.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You finish a hard book and flip back through your copy. The pages are **spotless**, or covered in **neat underlining**. A month later, which of its arguments could you still answer?'},{kind:'story',text:'A book argues that small teams move faster. In the margin, you write: *what about coordination?* Two chapters later, the author deals with exactly that. Your objection was wrong, and now you know precisely why.'},{kind:'explain',text:'It works like **explaining** does. You can\u2019t object to a claim until you\u2019ve understood it well enough to spot **its weak point**. Just following along asks far less of you. Doing the harder thing tends to make **the reading stick**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'**Underlining** feels like engagement. The test is whether a mark holds **anything you produced**.',left:{title:'Feels like work',items:['Highlighting a passage','Underlining every key line']},right:{title:'Holds a thought',items:['I disagree here','Where\u2019s the evidence?','Contradicts chapter two']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Argue with a hard book **in the margin**. Many of your objections will be wrong, and working out why is **the real learning**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Pick up a secondhand copy of a hard book. Some owners leave it **spotless**. Others fill the margins with **arguments**. Which reader do you think got more from it, and why?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The essay\u2019s claim is that arguing in the margin turns reading from **listening into a conversation**. Objection works like explanation. You can\u2019t say where a claim fails without grasping it first. That\u2019s **a far higher bar** than following along.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a reader working through a book on cities. Beside one confident chapter she writes: no evidence given. Beside another: contradicts page forty. Each mark took her ten seconds, and each one holds a thought of her own.'},{kind:'story',text:'Her friend reads the same book with a highlighter. Whole pages glow yellow. It felt like hard work at the time. But none of those marks holds anything he produced. Highlighting asked for no thinking at all.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The essay offers tendencies, not measured results. And the habit can harden into **reading to refute**, objecting before you understand. So you must **check later** whether you were right.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A spotless copy of a hard book has often **barely been read**. Write your objections **as you go**, then find out why they were wrong, or whether they were.'}]},'reading-in-public_4':{title:'Explain It Out Loud',card:{insight:'Explaining a chapter out loud, with the book shut, shows what you missed.',tryThis:'After you read a chapter today, shut the book and explain it out loud. Note where you get stuck.',skills:['thinking','habits']},review:{prompt:'You just read a chapter with care. How do you find what you missed?',right:'Shut the book and explain it out loud',wrong:'Read your highlights again and check they still make sense',why:'When you talk, you cannot stop to fix things, so the gaps show.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You read a chapter about **volcanoes**. You feel sure you get it. Then you shut the book and explain it to your dog. Halfway through, you stop: *and then the lava\u2026 um.* What does that **um** tell you?'},{kind:'explain',text:'That **um** is the bit you didn\u2019t really understand. When you write, you can stop and fix things. When you **talk out loud**, you have to keep going. So the gaps show up **straight away**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'After you read, **shut the book** and say it out loud. Where you get stuck is the part to **read again**. Finding out now beats finding out in a test.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve just read a chapter **attentively**, pen in hand. It all made sense on the page. Now close the book and **say what it claimed**. How far do you get before you stall?'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Ana, who reads a chapter on sleep for a course. Book shut, she explains the main claim easily to an empty room. Then she reaches the evidence and stops. She realises she never took it in.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Speaking is harder than writing a summary. Writing lets you **pause, hedge** and rearrange, so a gap can be quietly routed around. Speech runs **in real time**. The point where you falter is often **not where you thought**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The full version asks three things. What did the chapter claim? What was **the evidence**? Where was the argument **weakest**? Say it to a person if one\u2019s around, or to an empty room if not.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Close the book and **explain it aloud**. Where you stall marks what you **didn\u2019t retain**. Better to learn that today than on the day you need it.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Most people check their reading by **writing a summary** or rereading their notes. Both feel thorough. But there\u2019s a harder and faster test that needs no paper. What happens when you **say it aloud**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The essay calls speaking the argument aloud, without the book, **a demanding test**. A written summary permits pausing and hedging. Speaking leaves no time to cover up, so it **exposes gaps** at once. Few other checks find them as quickly.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a manager reading a chapter of a business book on the train. At her desk, book closed, she tries to state its claim, evidence and weakest point. The claim comes easily. The evidence doesn\u2019t come at all.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a student explains a history chapter to his empty bedroom. He\u2019s fluent from start to finish, with no stalls at all. He feels ready. But one date and one cause are simply wrong, and nobody is there to say so.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The claim that few checks are as quick is offered **without evidence**. Being questioned or working problems can do the same. And **fluency isn\u2019t accuracy**: an empty room can\u2019t correct you.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Saying a chapter aloud with the book shut **finds the gap** fast. Early tries feel humbling, and **that\u2019s useful to know**. Just remember that your smooth explanation can still be wrong.'}]},'reading-in-public_5':{title:'Two Books Beat One',card:{insight:'A second author on the same subject teaches more than one book read twice.',tryThis:'Pick a book that lost you. Write down one other author on the same subject to read next.',skills:['thinking','habits']},review:{prompt:'You loved a book about sleep. How can you learn the subject better?',right:'Read a second author on the same subject and compare',wrong:'Read the same book again, more slowly this time',why:'Two books show which parts people argue about, and one book cannot.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two friends tell you about a football match. Both say it ended **2\u20131**. But one says the winning goal was a foul, and one says it wasn\u2019t. Which part should you **think hardest** about?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Books work the same way. One book says everything in **the same sure voice**, so you can\u2019t tell what everyone agrees on. A **second book** by someone else shows you which parts people argue about.'},{kind:'moral',text:'If a book on a subject is hard, try **a different book** on it. Where they agree, **relax**. Where they disagree is the tricky part.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You finish a popular history of money and come away **sure of its story**. It all hangs together. Do you now understand the subject, or just **one writer\u2019s account** of it?'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Ben, who then reads a second book on money by a different author. It tells parts of the story differently and stresses other causes. Suddenly he can see which parts are settled and which were one writer\u2019s view.'},{kind:'explain',text:'With only one book, you can\u2019t separate **the subject** from one writer\u2019s take on it. Every claim comes with **the same confidence**. So nothing tells you which points are agreed and which are **still argued over**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Reading a second author beats rereading the first, because **disagreements** show **where the difficulty lives**.',left:{title:'Rereading one book',items:['Same voice, same confidence','Framing taken whole']},right:{title:'A second author',items:['Disputes become visible','Agreement lets you relax']}},{kind:'moral',text:'When a book defeats you, don\u2019t just reread it. Find **a different author** on the same subject. Their disagreements mark **the hard part**, and where they agree, you can relax.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'You finish a persuasive book on sleep. Every claim lands with **equal confidence**: the firm findings, the guesses, the author\u2019s pet theories. Reading it twice won\u2019t settle it. So how can you tell **which is which**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The essay\u2019s answer is a second book by someone else. One book can\u2019t pull **the subject** apart from its author\u2019s view. Two can. Where they clash, you see what\u2019s disputed, what hangs on **an assumption**, and where support is weak.'},{kind:'story',text:'Imagine two books on training a dog. Both say to reward good behaviour, so that part is probably settled. One says use a crate and the other says don\u2019t. Now you know where to think hardest.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a reader who finished one book on management and adopted its whole framing. Every problem at work now looks like the author\u2019s favourite problem. She had no other lens to hand while reading, so she took this one.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Two books can share a framing, from the same school or sources, so **agreement isn\u2019t proof**. Pick authors who start from **different places**. And for a beginner, two clashing accounts can confuse before they clarify.'},{kind:'moral',text:'One book can\u2019t show you **the subject** apart from its author. A second one can, and its disagreements show **where to dig**. Choose your second author to start somewhere different.'}]}}}},987,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'reading-that-sticks',kind:'book',promise:'Remember more of what you read',about:'Why does a book you finished last month feel like you never read it? This short essay by the Mindroll team says reading it again barely helps. These six ideas show what works instead. It takes more work. Ready to keep what you read?',ideas:{'reading-that-sticks_1':{title:'The Familiar Feeling',card:{insight:'A page can feel easy to read and still be gone a week later.',tryThis:'Next time you finish a chapter, shut the book. Then say what it said, and see what is missing.',skills:['thinking','psychology']},review:{prompt:'You read a chapter twice, and now it feels easy. What next?',right:'Shut the book and say what it said first',wrong:'Move on, since the easy feeling means you know it',why:'A page can feel easy and still not stick, so test yourself.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You read a page about **sharks** three times. By the third time, it feels **really easy**. Every line looks familiar. But does that easy feeling mean you\u2019ll remember it tomorrow?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Reading something again makes it **familiar**. Familiar feels like knowing, but it isn\u2019t the same. It only shows the words are easy to see. To find out what you\u2019ve kept, **shut the book** and try to say it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Easy to read isn\u2019t the same as **remembered**. The only real test is **trying to say it** with the page closed. What you can\u2019t say back, you haven\u2019t kept yet.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You finish a chapter on negotiation and close the book **feeling sure**. A week later a colleague asks what it said. You remember the title and that it was good. What happened to **the argument**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Maya, revising for an exam. She reads her notes three times the night before. By the last pass, every page **glides past**. She goes to bed confident. In the exam, she can\u2019t produce a single definition.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Maya\u2019s confidence was real. It was just **measuring ease**, not **memory**.',left:{title:'What fluency tracks',items:['How smoothly words go down','Whether the page looks familiar','Rises fast with re-reading']},right:{title:'What memory needs',items:['Producing it with the book closed','Recall days later','Barely moved by re-reading']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Fluency comes from clear writing, familiarity and **recent exposure**. None of those shows anything was stored. So test the feeling instead of trusting it. Close the book and **reconstruct** the chapter. It\u2019ll be worse than you expect, and that gap is useful.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The smooth second read feels like learning. Mostly, it\u2019s just **familiarity**. Trust **what you can produce** with the book shut, not how easily the page went down.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Re-reading is probably the most common way people study. It\u2019s also among the **least effective**. If it works so poorly, why does almost everyone **keep choosing it**? The answer is in how it feels.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The feeling you get while reading is called **fluency**: text going down smoothly. Re-reading raises it a lot and raises retention **barely at all**. The second pass feels far better than the first. The gain is in smoothness, not recall.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture Tom, a manager who rereads a book on feedback before a hard meeting. Each page feels obvious now. In the meeting, he reaches for the method and finds only a **vague sense** that it was good.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Ana, learning a new language. She reads yesterday\u2019s word list and every word looks known. Then she covers the English and tries to say each one. Half of them **won\u2019t come**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Fluency isn\u2019t always a false signal. When you truly know something, it also feels easy. So ease **can\u2019t tell you** either way. That\u2019s why you need the test, not the feeling.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A page that reads easily has only shown you it\u2019s **familiar**. To learn what you\u2019ve stored, **close the book** and reconstruct. The shortfall you find is the useful part.'}]},'reading-that-sticks_2':{title:'Write It From Memory',card:{insight:'Trying to remember a page helps more than reading it again.',tryThis:'After your next idea, close the app. Say its main point out loud before you open it again.',skills:['habits','psychology']},review:{prompt:'You have twenty minutes before a test tomorrow. What helps more?',right:'Close your notes and write what you recall',wrong:'Read your notes one more time, slowly',why:'Each time you reach for a fact, it gets easier to find.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You have ten spelling words to learn. Your sister reads the list **over and over**. You cover it and try to write them out. Who will remember more **next week**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Probably you will. Covering the list feels harder, and you might get only six right. But **reaching for a word** makes the path back to it stronger. Even a **half-right try** helps. Reading it again doesn\u2019t do that.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Trying to remember is **how you remember**. It feels harder than reading again, and that\u2019s fine. The **effort of reaching** is what does the work.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve got twenty minutes left before a meeting where you\u2019ll present a report. You could **read it again**. Or you could turn it face down and **rebuild it** from memory. Which feels better?'},{kind:'story',text:'Sofia tries both on two reports. The first she reads twice, and it goes down smoothly. The second she turns over and **reconstructs** in her notes, slowly, with gaps. Days later, the second is the one she can explain.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The method that **feels better** is the one that **does less**.',left:{title:'Reading it again',items:['Feels productive','Raises familiarity','Hides the gaps']},right:{title:'Rebuilding it',items:['Feels uncomfortable','Strengthens the path back','Shows you the gaps']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Why does the attempt work? Reaching for something **strengthens the path** to it. That\u2019s true even when you **partly fail**. It\u2019s one of the more reliably repeated results in learning research, for many ages and subjects.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A second reading feels like progress. **Closing the book** and trying is what actually builds memory. When the attempt **comes up short**, you\u2019ve learned something too.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Close a book after a chapter and try to rebuild its argument. It\u2019s **uncomfortable**. It feels less productive than reading the chapter again. So why does this awkward attempt **beat the reread**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'This is **retrieval practice**. It does more for retention than a second reading, and the margin is wide when you\u2019re **tested days later**. Straight away, the two can look similar. The difference shows up with time.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a nurse learning a new drug chart. Each evening she reads it once, then covers it and lists the doses she can recall. The misses are **obvious and specific**. Those are what she checks next.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take this app. After each idea, you stop before moving on. You say in one sentence **what it claimed** and what you\u2019d do with it. If nothing comes, give it one more read and try again.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Recall needs something there to reach for. If you never understood the chapter, rebuilding it just **produces blanks**. Then one careful reading comes first, and the attempt comes after.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Reading again shows you what\u2019s familiar. **Trying to recall** shows what\u2019s actually there, and strengthens it. A shortfall isn\u2019t a failure but **the information** you need.'}]},'reading-that-sticks_3':{title:'The Right Kind of Hard',card:{insight:'Study that feels slow can stick better, if it is not too hard.',tryThis:'Mix two topics you are learning. Quiz yourself on both before you check.',skills:['thinking','habits']},review:{prompt:'One study night felt smooth, and one felt hard. Which taught more?',right:'The hard one, if the work was recalling',wrong:'The smooth one, since it went so well',why:'Slow work that makes you recall leaves more in your head later.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re practising times tables. One way, you do all the sevens, then all the eights. Another way, they\u2019re **all mixed up**. The mixed way feels harder. Is harder **a bad sign**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Not always. Some hard ways work well: leaving **gaps between practice**, mixing topics up, and trying to remember instead of reading again. They feel slow, but **more stays** with you later. Hard that makes you think is good hard.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Feeling stuck doesn\u2019t always mean you\u2019re **doing it wrong**. The right kind of hard **helps you remember**. But too hard, like a book far beyond you, doesn\u2019t help.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two colleagues learn the same software. One works through each feature in order, **smooth and steady**. The other mixes them up and keeps testing herself. She feels **far less sure**. Who knows more next month?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Spacing sessions apart, **mixing related topics** and recalling instead of reviewing all feel slower. But they tend to leave more behind. Each makes you **pull things back** and compare them, work that a smooth review never asks of you.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Both kinds of difficulty feel like struggling. Only **one kind** is doing **the work**.',left:{title:'Useful difficulty',items:['Recalling before you look','Telling one topic from another','Effort goes into retrieval']},right:{title:'Wasted difficulty',items:['Material far beyond you','Effort goes into decoding']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s the trap. Going through one topic at a time and re-reading **feels productive**. Spacing, mixing and recalling feel like **struggling**. So if you judge a session by how smooth it felt, the weaker method wins.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t grade your study by **how it felt**. Effort spent recalling or telling topics apart is **doing the work**. Effort spent decoding something far beyond you isn\u2019t.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A student revises for an exam two ways. One week she re-reads, and it\u2019s smooth. The next she spaces sessions, mixes topics and tests herself, and it **feels awful**. Which would you call the **better week**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Most of us pick the smooth one. Yet study that **feels harder** often leaves more behind later. How it feels in the moment **misleads you**. So if smoothness is your test, you\u2019ll keep dropping the methods that work.'},{kind:'story',text:'Think of a learner driver. Practising only reversing for an hour feels smooth by the end. Mixing reversing, junctions and hill starts feels clumsy. But on test day she has to **choose the move** herself.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take someone learning history. Instead of finishing one period before the next, he mixes them. He keeps having to ask **which era** a fact belongs to. It\u2019s slower, and that comparison is the point.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Not every struggle helps. Wrestling with something **far beyond you** isn\u2019t the useful kind. The difficulty has to come from recalling or comparing, not from being lost.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Hard study isn\u2019t automatically good study. But **the right size** of difficulty, spent recalling and comparing, leaves more behind. Judge your methods by **what remains later**.'}]},'reading-that-sticks_4':{title:'The Second Look',card:{insight:'One look back after a gap does more than two right after you read.',tryThis:'Pick one thing you read today. Look back at it tomorrow, and again next week.',skills:['habits','thinking']},review:{prompt:'You just finished an article you want to keep. When should you look back?',right:'A day later, and again next week',wrong:'Right away, while it is still fresh',why:'A look after a gap does more than one while it is fresh.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You learn a magic trick on Monday. You could practise it **five times** right away. Or you could try it once now, and **once on Tuesday**. Which way will you still know on Saturday?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Straight away, it\u2019s all still **fresh**. Doing it again is easy, so it doesn\u2019t do much. After a gap, you\u2019ve forgotten a bit. **Pulling it back** takes effort, and that effort seems to help it stick.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Coming back **later** beats going over it **right away**. One well-timed return can do more than several in a row. A little forgetting first is fine.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You finish an article you\u2019d like to remember, and it feels clear. So you **read it again** at once, while it\u2019s fresh. It goes down easily. Did that second read **do anything**?'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The same second look, at **two different times**, does **very different work**.',left:{title:'Straight after',items:['Everything still active','Easy and quick','Tells you little']},right:{title:'After a gap',items:['Partly forgotten','Slow and effortful','Recall does the work']}},{kind:'story',text:'Kemi reads a report on Monday. On Tuesday, before opening it, she jots down what she remembers. A week later she does it again. Each time she checks what she missed, rather than **rereading from the top**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Why does the gap help? One idea is that a little forgetting makes the return need **real retrieval**, which strengthens memory. Researchers still dispute the exact reason. Whether spacing **works** isn\u2019t in dispute.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t reread straight away. Come back **about a day later**, then about a week later, and **recall before you look**. The slower pass is the one doing the work.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Imagine reading a chapter twice, **back to back**. Now imagine reading it once, then returning **a week later**. The second plan feels lazier and less efficient. Why might it leave you knowing more?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Coming back after a gap beats coming back at once, and the gap can be **longer than you\u2019d think**. A reread straight away is easy, since it\u2019s all still active. That ease makes it **uninformative**: it can\u2019t show you what\u2019s gone.'},{kind:'story',text:'A trainee lawyer reads a new rule on Monday. On Tuesday she writes out what she recalls before checking. The next Monday she tries again. Each pass is **slower** than a reread, and each shows her a gap.'},{kind:'story',text:'Compare a colleague who reads the same rule three times on Monday afternoon. By the third pass it feels thoroughly learned. Three passes in a row, yet **one return** a day later would likely have done more.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Nobody fully knows **why spacing works**. And there\u2019s no exact rule for the gap, just a rough guide: a day, then a week. You\u2019ll have to judge your own timing.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A second look **straight away** feels efficient and does little. Wait a day, then a week, and **recall before you look**. The effort of the delayed pass is the point.'}]},'reading-that-sticks_5':{title:'The Listener Test',card:{insight:'Explaining a book in your own words shows you where your gaps are.',tryThis:'Pick one idea you read this week. Write three lines about it for a friend who has not read it.',skills:['communication','thinking']},review:{prompt:'You think you understand a book. How can you check fast?',right:'Explain it in your own words, as if to a friend',wrong:'Read the parts you marked one more time',why:'To explain it, you must put it together, so the gaps show.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You watched a great show about volcanoes. At dinner, your little brother asks **how they work**. You start to answer and get stuck. You knew it while you watched. **Where did it go**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'When you watch or read, you can **nod along**. When you explain, you must choose what comes first and **what the point is**. If you don\u2019t know, you notice straight away. That\u2019s why explaining is such a good test.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Telling someone about it shows **what you really know**. A real listener helps most. But even **writing it out** for a pretend friend does a lot of the work.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A friend asks about the book you finished last week. You start in the middle, double back, and realise you **can\u2019t say** what the main claim was. Her puzzled look tells you **exactly where** you lost it.'},{kind:'explain',text:'You **can\u2019t fake** an explanation. Recognising each sentence is enough to follow a text. It isn\u2019t enough to rebuild the argument for someone who\u2019ll ask questions. So the gaps show up **right away**, and you see exactly where.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Explaining also makes you **find the structure**, not just borrow the **author\u2019s phrasing**.',left:{title:'Reading',items:['Follows the author\u2019s order','Lets vagueness pass']},right:{title:'Explaining',items:['You decide what comes first','You choose what to leave out','Confusion shows at once']}},{kind:'moral',text:'To check you understand something, **explain it** in your own words. Write it as if for a friend who hasn\u2019t read it. **The gaps** will show up at once.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Putting something in your own words for a real person is about the **toughest check** you can run. That\u2019s exactly why it **tells you so much**. What does explaining reveal that reading never can?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Following a text needs only **recognition**. Explaining it needs you to reorganise it: decide what comes first, what can go, and **what the point is**. That\u2019s **building the structure**, and reading on its own never asks you to.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture an engineer explaining a new system to a colleague. Halfway through, the colleague frowns at one step. The engineer realises she\u2019s been **skipping it herself**. She\u2019d read past it every time.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a student alone at her desk. She writes a summary of a chapter as if a friend will read it and ask questions. Writing for them, she spots **two vague paragraphs**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The imagined listener does most of the work, but not all. A summary nobody reads lets vagueness slide. A **real person\u2019s confusion** points at exactly which part you didn\u2019t understand.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Explaining can\u2019t be faked, so it shows **your real gaps**. Writing for an imagined friend gets you most of the way. **A real listener** gets you the rest.'}]},'reading-that-sticks_6':{title:'Five Books Kept',card:{insight:'A few books you can recall are worth more than many you forgot.',tryThis:'Pick the last book you finished. Write down three things from it you could use.',skills:['habits','thinking']},review:{prompt:'You could rush through six books this month, or learn two well. Which?',right:'Two books you can recall and use later',wrong:'Six, because a bigger count means more learning',why:'What you can still use counts more than how many you finished.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your friend says she read **thirty books** this year. You read five. She seems way ahead. But what if you can tell someone about all five, and she **can\u2019t remember** most of hers?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Thirty books **read and forgotten** leave you with almost nothing. Five books you can remember and use leave **five books\u2019 worth** of ideas. That\u2019s five more than thirty forgotten ones. The number on the list isn\u2019t the point.'},{kind:'moral',text:'It\u2019s not about **how many** books you finish. It\u2019s about **how much stays**. Read fewer, and spend the time remembering them.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A colleague\u2019s reading app shows **thirty books** finished this year. Yours shows five. It\u2019s hard not to feel behind. But ask each of you to explain a book from March. Who **can actually do it**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Why chase the total? The count is **easy to see**, and what you\u2019ve kept isn\u2019t. You can say thirty and compare it with a friend\u2019s. What you could use from those books **never gets a number**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Run the arithmetic and the **unfashionable answer** wins **by a mile**.',left:{title:'Thirty, forgotten',items:['Impressive count','Roughly nothing left to use']},right:{title:'Five, kept',items:['Read with recall and spacing','Tested by explanation','Five books\u2019 worth to use']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Recalling a book costs little next to the **hours you spent** reading it. Your reading speed was never **the holdup**. So treat finishing a book as **the start** of the work, not its end.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Five books you can use beat thirty you\u2019ve forgotten. Read **fewer, more slowly**, and do the remembering. **What you keep** is the only total that counts.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Many readers set a yearly target and track every book they finish. The total climbs, and it feels like **progress**. But how much of last year\u2019s list could you **actually use** today?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Do the sum out loud. Thirty books you\u2019ve forgotten give you **close to nothing** to use. Five you\u2019ve recalled, revisited and explained give you **five books\u2019 worth**. So the smaller pile wins outright.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture Leo, who reads a book a week and remembers almost none of them. When a friend asks for a recommendation, he names titles. When she asks **what they said**, he\u2019s got only impressions.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture Grace, who reads one book a month. After each chapter she recalls it, comes back a week later, and explains it to her sister. She reads less. She can **use what she read**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Not all reading is for keeping. A novel read for pleasure or a quick skim for **one fact** doesn\u2019t need this effort. The rule is for what you want to use.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Speed was never **the problem**. What matters is **how much you keep**. Treat finishing a book as the beginning of the work, and your pace will change for good reason.'}]}}}},988,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'repair-not-victory',kind:'book',promise:'Come back closer after a fight',about:'Do you worry that you and someone you love fight too much? This short essay by the Mindroll team says the number of fights matters less. What you do after a fight matters more. Here are five ways to make up. Ready to try one?',ideas:{'repair-not-victory_1':{title:'The Minutes After',card:{insight:'What you do after a fight tells you more than how often you fight.',tryThis:'After your next fight, be the first to try to make up, even with a cold drink.',skills:['relationships','psychology']},review:{prompt:'Your friend and her husband argue most weeks. What should she watch?',right:'Whether one of them tries to make up, and the other lets them',wrong:'How many times a week the two of them argue',why:'Making up after a fight says more than how often you fight.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You and your brother fight over a video game. You both **storm off**. Ten minutes later he\u2019s back in the doorway, holding **a plate of toast**. What happens next?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Families and friends who stay close **still argue**. The big clue is what comes after. Does someone **move back** towards the other? And does the other person let them? The toast is your brother moving back. So what you say next matters.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Counting fights tells you **very little**. The minutes after a fight tell you far more. So **take the toast**, and be the one who offers it sometimes.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'One couple you know **almost never** argues in public. Another bickers about the route, the heating and the dishwasher. If you had to bet on which pair lasts, you\u2019d pick the quiet one. Is that **a good bet**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Take the bickering pair on a bad evening. Words fly about the heating. Then Sam mutters a joke about the thermostat, and Priya half laughs. The row fizzles out. That small move back, and its acceptance, is the whole story.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The research behind this essay **watches couples** disagree. How often they fight predicts **less than you\u2019d think**. What separates lasting pairs is the minutes after: does someone move back, and is the move **accepted**?'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Two ways to judge a relationship give you **very different** answers about the **same couple**.',left:{title:'Counting fights',items:['How often they argue','Quiet looks safe','Says little about recovery']},right:{title:'Watching repair',items:['What happens straight after','Does someone move back','Is the move taken']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Disagreement is normal between people who share a life. What matters is **the move back**, and whether it\u2019s **accepted**. Watch the minutes after, not the tally.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'This essay draws on research that **watches couples** argue in real life. You might expect the pairs who last to be the ones who **fight least**. Is that what the watching actually shows?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Any two people who share a life will disagree. So conflict alone **can\u2019t tell** one couple from another. What varies is recovery. Repair has **two halves**: someone moves back, and the other accepts the move.'},{kind:'story',text:'Two flatmates row about rent. Within the hour one knocks on the other\u2019s door with a question about dinner. The other answers, a little stiffly. Nothing is settled yet, but the evening is saved. That\u2019s repair in miniature.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a couple who rarely raise their voices. When something does go wrong, each waits for the other to make the first move. Neither does. The silence lasts days, and the next quarrel starts from there.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. This research is **observational**, and some of its more precise claims haven\u2019t replicated well. The broad repair finding has held up best. Read it as **a tendency**, not a forecast for your couple.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Fights tell you **less than you\u2019d think**. The way back tells you more. So when something cracks, look for **the first move**, and make sure it\u2019s answered.'}]},'repair-not-victory_2':{title:'The Clumsy Peace Offer',card:{insight:'Tries to end a fight are often clumsy, but they still count.',tryThis:'Next time someone makes a clumsy try at peace, like a joke, say yes to it.',skills:['relationships','communication']},review:{prompt:'In a fight about money, your husband makes a dumb joke. What is happening?',right:'He may be making a clumsy try to end the fight',wrong:'He is trying to dodge the money talk so he can win',why:'A clumsy try to end a fight still counts, so take it.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You and your dad just had **a row** at the kitchen table. You\u2019re both still cross. Then he slides a mug of **hot chocolate** across to you. He hasn\u2019t said sorry. What\u2019s going on?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Your dad is trying to **end the fight**. People rarely make up with a perfect apology. They tell a bad joke, offer a drink or change the subject. What counts is **the direction**: they want out of the row.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Making up is often **clumsy**. Don\u2019t wait for a perfect sorry. When someone reaches out badly, it still counts, so **take it anyway**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re in the middle of **an argument** about money. Out of nowhere, your partner says the neighbour\u2019s cat is **on the shed roof** again. What do you hear?'},{kind:'story',text:'You hear a dodge, and you say so. Your partner bristles. The argument runs on for another hour, louder than before. Afterwards, it\u2019s hard to remember what the cat remark was for. It may have been the exit, offered badly.'},{kind:'explain',text:'A **repair attempt** is any move to calm things mid-conflict. The ones that work are often clumsy. What decides the outcome is whether **the other person** accepts it, not **how well it\u2019s made**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'That gives you **two jobs**. Make your own attempt without waiting until you can make **a good one**. Waiting lets the row grow. And learn to spot a poor olive branch from the other side.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The signal isn\u2019t the content but **the direction**. Someone is trying to leave the fight. Refusing a clumsy attempt **on quality grounds** is how a fixable row stops being fixable.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture the middle of a row. One person offers **a cup of tea**, another makes **a joke** that falls flat. Neither looks like an apology. So how would you know if either was an attempt to make peace?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The essay\u2019s answer: a repair attempt\u2019s **quality** barely matters. What matters is **its direction**, away from the fight, and whether it\u2019s accepted. So the offer can be clumsy. The acceptance has to be generous.'},{kind:'story',text:'A couple argue about a holiday. He stops halfway and says, flatly, that he\u2019s hungry. She hears him changing the subject and rolls her eyes. He tries once more, then gives up. The row lasts all evening.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take two colleagues after a tense meeting. One sends a slightly awkward message asking if the other wants a coffee. It doesn\u2019t mention the meeting at all. The other says yes. By the afternoon, they\u2019re talking properly.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Not every change of subject is a reach. A joke that always arrives when one issue is raised may be **plain evasion**. And accepting a repair ends the escalation, **not the issue**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Make **your attempt** without waiting for a good one. When someone reaches towards you clumsily, **take the reach**. You can return to the issue once you\u2019re calm.'}]},'repair-not-victory_3':{title:'Always and Never',card:{insight:'Complain about what they did, not who they are, so they can fix it.',tryThis:'Next time you complain, leave out the words always and never.',skills:['communication','relationships']},review:{prompt:'Your husband forgot to call the restaurant again. What do you say?',right:'Complain about what he forgot this time, so he can fix it',wrong:'Tell him he never does what he says he will do',why:'Talk about one thing he did, so he has something to fix.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your friend forgot to bring **your book** back. You could tell her she forgot it today. Or you could tell her she **always forgets** things. Which one starts **a bigger fight**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The first is about **one day**. She can fix it by bringing the book tomorrow. The second is about **who she is**. There\u2019s nothing she can do to fix that, so she\u2019ll just fight back.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Talk about **what happened**, not about the person. Watch for **always** and **never**. Those words turn a small problem into one nobody can fix.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your partner said they\u2019d **book the table** for Friday. They didn\u2019t, and now there\u2019s nowhere to eat. You open your mouth to say something. Which **sentence** comes out?'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The same frustration can come out as **a complaint** or as **a criticism**.',left:{title:'A complaint',items:['You didn\u2019t book the table','About one event','Something they can fix']},right:{title:'A criticism',items:['You never do what you say','About their character','Nothing left to fix']}},{kind:'story',text:'Say it the first way and your partner can apologise and book somewhere else. Say it the second way and they\u2019re left defending their whole character. The table gets forgotten. The row is now about who they are.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The essay draws on Gottman\u2019s work here. Criticism attacks the person, and it often hides in **always** or **never**. **Contempt** goes further, adding superiority through sarcasm or mockery. He found it the strongest single predictor of a break-up.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Complaining more often is survivable. The **critical version** of the same complaint is far less so. Keep the sentence about **the event**, and watch your tone.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two sentences can carry **the same grievance**. One leads to an apology. The other leads to a long, bitter evening. Often the difference is **a few words** and a tone. Which words?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The essay separates **three acts**. A complaint is about a behaviour: this happened, and it affected me. Criticism is about the person. **Contempt** adds superiority, in sarcasm, mockery and a tone of talking down.'},{kind:'story',text:'A woman\u2019s partner leaves the washing-up again. She says it irritated her to come home to it. He grumbles, then does it. Next week she says he never lifts a finger. He argues instead of washing anything.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a team lead. She tells a colleague the report was late and cost the client call. Fair, and fixable. A week later she says the same thing with a sigh and an eye-roll. The colleague hears contempt and stops listening.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. A rule against always can be overdone, since **a real pattern** is fair to raise. Raise it as a run of events. And a sentence kept to the event, said with a sneer, is **still contempt**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Police **the register**, not the frequency. You can complain often and survive it. Turn the complaint into a verdict on **who they are**, and you\u2019ve left them nothing to fix.'}]},'repair-not-victory_4':{title:'The Hollow Win',card:{insight:'When you win a fight, the other person often gives in, so it comes back.',tryThis:'Next time you are sure you are right, ask what the fight is really about.',skills:['relationships','communication']},review:{prompt:'You have texts that prove it was her turn to call the plumber. Now what?',right:'Ask her what the fight is really about',wrong:'Read her the texts so she has to give in',why:'Proof makes her give in, but the same fight will come back later.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You and your best friend argue about **the best footballer**. You know all the facts. You win, and she says *fine, you\u2019re right*. Then she **walks home** on her own. Did you really win?'},{kind:'explain',text:'She **gave in**, but she didn\u2019t agree. Next week the same row will come back about something else. When you care about someone, being right and **staying close** can pull in different directions.'},{kind:'moral',text:'With people you care about, **winning** isn\u2019t the point. Try to understand **what each of you** needs instead. Then you can both come back to it.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re arguing, again, about **whose turn** it was to call the plumber. You have **the texts**. You read them out. Your partner concedes. What did you just win?'},{kind:'story',text:'Ten days later, the same argument breaks out again. This time it\u2019s about the car insurance. The subject is new, but the heat is the same. The plumber point was settled. Whatever sat underneath it never was.'},{kind:'explain',text:'A won argument produces a resolved point and **a concession** made under pressure. A concession **isn\u2019t agreement**. It usually gets taken back later, which is why the same conflict returns **in different clothes**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The alternative isn\u2019t **giving in**. It\u2019s changing **what you aim at**.',left:{title:'Aiming to win',items:['More evidence','A point resolved','A concession that comes back']},right:{title:'Aiming to understand',items:['Ask what it\u2019s really about','A shared account of needs','Both can raise it again']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Here\u2019s the test. Could they **revisit it** afterwards without reopening a defeat? If not, the issue is **still live**, and now harder to name.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'You end an argument holding **every fact**. The other person says fine, have it your way. It feels like a victory. So why does the **same fight** keep coming back in a new form?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The essay says treating a disagreement as **a contest** gets the objective wrong. What\u2019s really at stake is **the relationship\u2019s ability** to handle the next one. Proving a point can cost exactly that.'},{kind:'story',text:'Two brothers argue over who should host Christmas. The elder lists every year he\u2019s done it, and the younger gives way. In spring, a row about their mother\u2019s birthday turns oddly bitter. Nobody mentions Christmas.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a couple deciding where to live. Instead of trading evidence, they each say what they need: one wants quiet, the other wants friends nearby. The choice stays open. Neither has lost, so both can raise it again.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some arguments are about facts that matter, like a debt or **a child\u2019s safety**. There, being right isn\u2019t vanity. And this assumes good faith. It\u2019s not advice to yield to someone who **wins by pressure**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Aim for **a shared account** of what happened and what each of you needs. It may leave the point open. That\u2019s fine, as long as **both of you** can raise it again.'}]},'repair-not-victory_5':{title:'Pause, Then Return',card:{insight:'A break in a fight only helps if you come back to it.',tryThis:'Pick one fight you dropped, and set a time to talk about it.',skills:['relationships','communication']},review:{prompt:'You both agree to stop a fight for now. What makes the break work?',right:'Set a time to come back to it, and then come back',wrong:'Leave it alone until it goes away on its own',why:'A break helps only when you come back at the time you set.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Last week you and your sister had **a row** about the TV remote. You both stopped, and nobody mentioned it again. Today she leaves her bag on your bed, and **you shout**. Why so loud?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The remote row didn\u2019t go away. It was **stored**, not sorted, and it came back stuck to the bag. Stopping a fight is good when you\u2019re both cross. But you have to **come back** to it later.'},{kind:'moral',text:'It\u2019s fine to **take a break** from a fight. Just say when you\u2019ll talk again. Then **keep that promise**, so the old row doesn\u2019t sneak back.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'On Sunday you both said, *let\u2019s not do this now*. Sensible. It\u2019s Thursday, and **neither of you** has mentioned it. Tonight a small remark about the washing-up lands **far harder** than it should. Why?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Pausing is wise. Mid-escalation, you\u2019re both **worked up** and thinking narrowly, which is poor for accuracy and for generosity. So stop and resume later. The catch is that **later has to arrive**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'A pause that turns into avoidance **stores** the disagreement. It doesn\u2019t resolve it. You also learn that this subject **can\u2019t be discussed**. So it returns attached to the next unrelated thing, like that washing-up remark.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The difference between the two is **a single sentence**, said as **you stop**.',left:{title:'An escape',items:['Let\u2019s not do this now','No time named','Quietly never resumed']},right:{title:'An appointment',items:['Let\u2019s come back after dinner','A time both agree','Kept, even when awkward']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Call the pause, then **name the return**. That turns an escape into **an appointment**, and it\u2019s the only version that repairs anything.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'An argument breaks out over **towels** left on the floor. It\u2019s ferocious, far out of proportion. Halfway through, the real cause surfaces. It\u2019s **something said** at a family lunch a month ago. How did it get here?'},{kind:'story',text:'That afternoon at lunch, both had agreed to leave it for now. It was a sensible pause. But neither picked it up again. So it waited, and the towels gave it somewhere to land.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Returning when calm is the step **most often skipped**, and it\u2019s where resolution happens. A pause that becomes permanent leaves the issue open. It also teaches you both that **it can\u2019t be discussed**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take two colleagues who clash in a meeting. One says they should stop and pick it up at nine tomorrow. At nine, they do. It\u2019s awkward for a minute, then useful. The pause was an appointment.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. A named time **isn\u2019t magic**. Come back while one of you is still worked up and the row restarts, so move it openly. And a **truly trivial** matter can be dropped, if you both agree.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Pause when you\u2019re both too heated to think. But **say when** you\u2019ll come back, then come back. A pause with **no return** is just avoidance.'}]}}}},989,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'republic',kind:'book',promise:'Be fair, even when no one is watching',about:'What would you do if no one could ever see you? Plato wrote about this long ago in Athens, Greece. His book, the Republic, is one long talk about being fair. Here are its best ideas. Which one will you try first?',ideas:{republic_1:{title:'Beat the Best Case',card:{insight:'To win an argument, beat the other side at its best.',tryThis:'Pick a view you disagree with. Write its best case, the way its biggest fan would.',skills:['thinking','communication']},review:{prompt:'You want to argue against a plan at work. Which version do you answer?',right:'The best case for it, as its biggest fan would say it',wrong:'Its weakest point, so everyone sees it fall fast',why:'A win over a weak case proves little, so answer the best case first.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You and your sister argue about who should pick the film. You could repeat her **silliest reason** back in a funny voice. Everyone would laugh. But would you have **won the argument**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'About 2,400 years ago in Athens, Plato wrote a book called the *Republic*. It asks what **justice** is. One man in it says justice is just whatever helps **the strong**. Plato lets him argue hard and well, with no easy jokes against him.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Before you argue against an idea, say it in its **strongest form**. If you can beat that, you\u2019ve **really won**. If you can\u2019t, you\u2019ve learned something.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re about to pitch a new plan to your team. You list the objections you expect and **knock each one down**. They all look flimsy. Is that because the plan is strong, or because **you wrote them**?'},{kind:'story',text:'The *Republic* opens with a simple question: what is justice? Thrasymachus breaks in with a hard answer. Justice, he says, is whatever serves **the stronger**. Rulers write laws for their own benefit. Plato lets him make that case with real force.'},{kind:'story',text:'Then something stranger happens. Socrates seems to win, but Glaucon, Plato\u2019s own brother, isn\u2019t satisfied. He calls the win **mere rhetoric** and restates the case better than Thrasymachus did. The rest of the book answers **that stronger version**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s why it matters for your pitch. A weak objection gives you **a weak win**. The critic you\u2019ll actually meet brings **the strong one**, and you haven\u2019t prepared. Writing their best case first is how you **get ready**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Answer an argument at **its strongest**, not its weakest. State the other side until someone who holds it would say **you got it right**. Then decide what you think.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Plenty of writers make their opponents look silly. Plato did the opposite. He gave the case he meant to defeat **the best speech** in his opening book. Why would anyone hand **the other side** such good lines?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The reason isn\u2019t generosity. A weak version of an objection produces **a weak refutation**. Then the reader who later meets the strong version is **unprotected**. Plato is building an argument that can survive the real opponent, not a puppet.'},{kind:'story',text:'In Book I, Thrasymachus argues that justice serves the stronger. The just man always comes off worse, he says, and injustice on a grand scale gets admired. Socrates answers him. Then Glaucon objects that the answer only sounded like a win.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture Priya, writing up a decision to move her team\u2019s office. She adds a paragraph called *the case against*. She writes it as the move\u2019s fiercest critic would. Then she shows it to that critic and asks if it\u2019s fair.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some views don\u2019t deserve a best version, because their best version is still built on a lie. Building them up can lend **false weight**. And you can spend so long on the other side that you never decide.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Give the view you mean to beat **its best form** first. If your answer survives that, it\u2019ll survive **the real critic**. If it doesn\u2019t, you\u2019ve found out cheaply.'}]},republic_2:{title:'Large Letters First',card:{insight:'When a problem is too close to see, look at a bigger copy of it.',tryThis:'Name a habit you keep repeating. Picture a whole team doing it, and write what you would tell them.',skills:['thinking','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'You keep dropping side projects and cannot see why. What might help most?',right:'Picture a team that starts all its jobs and ends none',wrong:'Promise yourself you will try much harder next time',why:'You can spot the same problem in a big group much faster.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your little brother writes you a note in **tiny letters**. You squint and can\u2019t read it. Then you spot the same words painted **huge** on a sign across the street. Which one would you read first?'},{kind:'explain',text:'In Plato\u2019s *Republic*, the friends talking can\u2019t see **justice** inside one person. It\u2019s too small and too close. So they imagine **a whole city** instead. They read the big version first, then look for the same pattern in one person.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When something is **too close** to see, find a bigger copy of it. The same pattern is often **easier to spot** when you aren\u2019t standing inside it.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve started **five side projects** this year. None is finished. Each time, you blame a busy month. You can\u2019t quite see the pattern from where you\u2019re standing. So where else could you **look for it**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Early in the *Republic*, Socrates hits the same wall. Justice is hard to see in one person. So he suggests reading it in **a whole city** first, the way you\u2019d read large letters before small ones. Then he checks the individual.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Try it on your projects. Picture a team that **starts everything** and finishes nothing. You\u2019d spot the fault in a minute: no one picks **what to drop**. That isn\u2019t a metaphor. It\u2019s **the same mechanism**, at a scale you can see.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When a problem is **too close** to read, find a larger case with **the same structure**. Read it there, where you aren\u2019t inside it. Then bring the answer home.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Socrates and his companions are stuck. They want to know what makes **one person** just, and they can\u2019t see it. So Socrates proposes something odd: set the person aside and build **a city**. Why would that help?'},{kind:'explain',text:'His image is **large letters**. If you had to read small print far away, you\u2019d look first for the same words written big. A city should show justice **at scale**. Then you check whether one soul has the same pattern.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take someone who can\u2019t name what\u2019s wrong at home. Then she reads about an office where nobody may say **the obvious thing**. She recognises it at once. It\u2019s her kitchen table, at a scale where she isn\u2019t standing inside it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Or take a manager who misses every deadline she sets herself. Then she inherits a department that does the same. Watching it, she sees the cause in days: every plan assumes nothing will go wrong.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. A city and a soul might not share **one structure** at all. Readers have argued for two thousand years about the rigid city Plato builds. And a bigger case can mislead you if the match is **only a likeness**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When you\u2019re **too close** to see a pattern, look for **a bigger instance** of it. Just check that it really is the same mechanism, not a pleasing echo.'}]},republic_3:{title:'Three Parts at War',card:{insight:'When you skip a plan, one part of you beat another part.',tryThis:'Next time you skip a plan, say which part of you won. Then call on your pride.',skills:['psychology','habits']},review:{prompt:'You meant to go for a run but stayed on the couch. What now?',right:'Say which part won, then call on your pride',wrong:'Tell yourself you just have weak willpower',why:'Knowing better is weak alone, but pride can help your plan win.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'It\u2019s a hot day and you\u2019re **really thirsty**. Someone hands you a fizzy drink. Part of you wants to gulp it. Another part says **no, not now**. How can one person want two things at once?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Plato said that inside each of us are **three parts**. **Appetite** wants things, like food or drinks. Reason thinks ahead. And **spirit** is the proud part that gets cross when you let yourself down. They often pull different ways.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When you don\u2019t do what you meant to, it isn\u2019t just **weak willpower**. One part of you **won the argument**. Name which one, and you\u2019ll know what to do next.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You decided on Sunday to stop checking your phone in bed. It\u2019s Tuesday, half past midnight, and you\u2019re **still scrolling**. You know it\u2019s bad for you. So why doesn\u2019t **knowing** make any difference?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Plato splits a person into **reason**, **spirit** and **appetite**. His evidence is ordinary conflict. A thirsty man who refuses to drink must hold something wanting and something refusing. One part can\u2019t do both at once.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Spirit is the part people miss. It\u2019s the seat of **anger and pride**. Plato pictures a man furious at himself for giving in. That\u2019s spirit taking **reason\u2019s side** against appetite. And it\u2019s the part that actually moves you.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'At midnight, **knowing better** loses to appetite. **Self-respect** has a fighting chance.',left:{title:'Reason alone',items:['It\u2019s bad for my sleep','I\u2019ll stop tomorrow']},right:{title:'Spirit joins in',items:['I\u2019m not someone who does this','I keep my word to myself']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Most failures to act aren\u2019t **ignorance**. They\u2019re one part of you wanting something else, loudly. Name the part that won, and recruit **your pride** to side with reason.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'You promised yourself one biscuit. Now the packet\u2019s empty, and you\u2019re **furious with yourself**. Not with the biscuits. With you. But if you\u2019re both the eater and the angry one, **who\u2019s angry** at whom?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Plato\u2019s answer is that you\u2019re **three parts**. Reason calculates. Appetite wants. Spirit is the seat of anger, honour and indignation. His test is conflict: one thing can\u2019t want and refuse **the same thing** at once. So something in you must be divided.'},{kind:'story',text:'His own example is a man furious with himself for giving in to a craving. That fury isn\u2019t appetite, which wanted the thing. It isn\u2019t cool reason either. It\u2019s spirit, taking reason\u2019s side, too late this time.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Sam, who\u2019s tried to quit smoking for years. Health warnings never worked. What finally did was his daughter asking why he smelled of smoke. Pride did what knowledge couldn\u2019t. He didn\u2019t want to be **that kind of dad**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. As psychology, three parts is **crude**. Real minds don\u2019t split so neatly, and pride can side with appetite too. Plato\u2019s own aim was arrangement, not suppression: each part doing **its own work**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Knowing better has **little force** on its own. Self-respect is what moves you. So when you slip, name the part that won, and bring **your spirit** in on reason\u2019s side.'}]},republic_4:{title:'The Return to the Cave',card:{insight:'People will doubt a new idea, so bring proof that they can check.',tryThis:'Pick one idea you want your team to try. Write one thing they could check for themselves.',skills:['communication','leadership']},review:{prompt:'Your team shrugs at a better way you learned in a class. What next?',right:'Expect the doubt and bring something they can check',wrong:'Say it again, but louder, until they get it',why:'People doubt news from outside, so plan for doubt and bring proof.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Imagine prisoners who\u2019ve lived their whole lives in **a dark cave**. All they\u2019ve seen are **shadows** moving on a wall. They get good at guessing which comes next. If you were one, what would you think the world was?'},{kind:'story',text:'One prisoner is set free. He climbs out into **bright sunlight**. At first it hurts his eyes. Slowly he sees real things, then the sun itself. When he goes back down to the others, he stumbles in **the dark**. They don\u2019t believe a word.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Coming back with **something new** is the hard part. People won\u2019t believe you at first. So **expect that**, and bring something they can see for themselves.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Maya comes back from a conference with **a better way** to run the team\u2019s projects. She\u2019s seen it work. At Monday\u2019s meeting she explains it, then explains it again. The room just **looks at her**. What went wrong?'},{kind:'story',text:'Plato tells of prisoners chained facing a wall, watching shadows cast by a fire behind them. They\u2019re **skilled at predicting** the shadows. One is freed and dragged into daylight. Blinded at first, he slowly sees real things, then the sun.'},{kind:'story',text:'Then he goes **back down**. In the dark his eyes are useless, and he stumbles. The others decide the journey ruined him. He\u2019s now worse at the shadow game, and his only evidence is **his own word**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'That\u2019s Maya\u2019s Monday. The disbelief isn\u2019t a flaw in **the room**. It follows from where they stand. So don\u2019t explain harder. Expect to look clumsy at the old game for a while, and bring them **something they can check**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Everyone remembers **the climb** out of the cave. The part that matters is **the return**. When you bring news back, you\u2019ll be doubted, so plan for it.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Plato\u2019s most famous image starts in **darkness** and climbs to **the sun**. Most people stop the story there, at the bright part. But it doesn\u2019t end in daylight. What happens when the freed man walks back down?'},{kind:'story',text:'Prisoners chained since childhood face a wall. Objects carried before a fire behind them cast moving shadows. The prisoners take the shadows for the world. One is freed, dragged up, blinded, and slowly learns to see things, then the sun.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Then he returns. His eyes fail in the dark and he stumbles. The others conclude the trip **ruined him**. Socrates adds that they\u2019d kill anyone who tried to free them. Their disbelief isn\u2019t bad character. It\u2019s **their position**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take an engineer back from six months at a rival firm. She\u2019s seen a faster way to ship. Back home she\u2019s slower at the old tools than before, and her only proof is a story. Her team quietly decides she\u2019s gone soft.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The image flatters whoever feels like the freed one. But plenty of people come back from a course or a book **simply wrong**. Being doubted isn\u2019t proof you saw the sun. Sometimes the cave is right.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When you bring back **a new view**, the doubt is predictable. Expect to look clumsy for a while. Then bring **something they can check**, because your word alone won\u2019t carry it.'}]},republic_5:{title:'The Leader Who Holds Back',card:{insight:'If you want to lead too much, you may want it for the wrong reason.',tryThis:'Before you pick a leader, ask each one what they hope to get from the job.',skills:['leadership','thinking']},review:{prompt:'Two coworkers could lead the new project. One badly wants it. What do you do?',right:'Ask what each one hopes to get, then pick',wrong:'Pick the one who wants it most, since that shows they care',why:'How much they want the job can hint at what they want it for.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your class needs a team captain. Jake **really, really** wants it and keeps asking. Leila doesn\u2019t ask at all, but she\u2019s fair and good at the game. Who would **you pick**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Plato thought the best leaders are often the ones who **don\u2019t want** the job. Someone who wants power badly usually wants it **for something**: to be important, or to get their own way. That can get in the way of being fair.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Wanting a job badly doesn\u2019t make you **good at it**. When you choose a leader, ask what each person hopes **to get**. The quiet, fair one might be your best captain.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two people apply to run the new project. Dan has wanted it for a year and **lobbies everyone**. Ruth is capable, but she\u2019d rather keep doing her own work. She only applied because **someone asked**. Who should get it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Plato says the people fit to govern are those **least eager** to. His reason is motive. Anyone who wants power wants it for something: honour, wealth, the thrill of it. When that **clashes with the job**, you can guess which one wins.'},{kind:'explain',text:'So why would the reluctant serve at all? In Plato\u2019s city, the philosophers have seen **something better** and treat ruling as a duty. They serve because the penalty for refusing is **being ruled** by someone worse.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'A selection process that rewards **ambition** measures the wrong thing: **appetite for the role**.',left:{title:'Dan',items:['Wants it most','Lobbies for a year','Expects status from it']},right:{title:'Ruth',items:['Capable and reluctant','Applied when asked','Expects only the work']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Wanting power is **evidence** about what someone would use it for. So don\u2019t read reluctance as weakness. When you pick a leader, ask what each person expects **to get** from it.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Most job adverts look for **hunger**: people who really want the role. Plato thought that, for ruling at least, this gets it backwards. What if wanting the job badly is **the warning sign**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'His reasoning is about **motive**. Someone who wants power wants it for something: honour, wealth, or the exercise itself. When that interest conflicts with the job, it gets served first. The reluctant ruler has **no rival interest** competing.'},{kind:'story',text:'In Plato\u2019s city, the philosophers have seen something better than governing. They treat ruling as a duty interrupting it. They take their turn because refusing means being ruled by someone worse, and because they owe the city their upbringing.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a residents\u2019 committee. The chair everyone dreaded was the man who campaigned hardest. The one who did it well was a retired nurse who agreed only after three people asked. She wanted nothing from it but a working lift.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. A system that installs the unwilling has to **make them serve**. Plato\u2019s answer is compulsion, and readers still argue whether that\u2019s enough. And reluctance alone isn\u2019t competence. Some people refuse because they\u2019d be bad at it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You can\u2019t read skill from reluctance. But you can read motive from **eagerness**. When you choose who leads, ask what each person expects to get, and weigh **the answer** heavily.'}]},republic_6:{title:'The Ring of Gyges',card:{insight:'What you do when nobody can see shows who you really are.',tryThis:'Pick one good thing you do each day. Ask yourself if you would still do it unseen.',skills:['philosophy','psychology']},review:{prompt:'Nobody ever checks your work receipts. A dinner with friends could slip through. What now?',right:'Claim only what you would claim if someone could see',wrong:'Claim it, since no one will ever find out',why:'What you do unseen shows who you are, not just how you look.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Imagine you found **a magic ring**. Put it on and you turn **invisible**. Nobody could ever see what you did or tell on you. What would you do first, honestly?'},{kind:'story',text:'In Plato\u2019s book, a man called Glaucon tells a story. A shepherd finds a ring that makes him invisible. He doesn\u2019t use it for good. He uses it to take over a whole kingdom. Glaucon says anyone would do the same.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Being good when people are watching is **the easy part**. What you do when nobody can see shows **who you really are**. That\u2019s the test that counts.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your company never checks expense claims. Last month a friend\u2019s birthday dinner could have gone through as **a client meal**. Nobody would ever have known. You\u2019ve thought about it since. What, exactly, was **stopping you**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Glaucon, Plato\u2019s brother, tells of a shepherd who finds a ring of invisibility. He uses it to seduce the queen, kill the king and take the kingdom. Glaucon\u2019s claim is blunt. Given that ring, **anyone** would behave the same.'},{kind:'explain',text:'He isn\u2019t being cynical for sport. He\u2019s demanding that Socrates defend justice as **good in itself**, not good for **your reputation**. If honesty is only for show, the ring would end it. The rest of the book answers him.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Plato\u2019s answer is that the unjust man **isn\u2019t getting away** with anything. The damage is inside. He\u2019s let appetite rule him, and a soul in that state is **wretched**, watched or not. The cost is paid privately.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your expense claim is **a small ring**. What you do when nobody can check describes what you are. What you do when watched shows only **your image**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Imagine a ring that makes you **invisible** whenever you like. No camera, no witness, no consequence. Plato\u2019s Glaucon asks a simple question about it. Would anyone, given that ring, **stay honest**?'},{kind:'story',text:'His story: a shepherd finds such a ring. He uses it to seduce the queen, kill the king and seize the kingdom. Glaucon\u2019s claim is that a just man would do the same. Justice, he says, is practised **only for reputation**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Plato\u2019s reply takes **the whole book**, and it\u2019s structural. Injustice damages the arrangement of **your own soul**. A person ruled by appetite is wretched whether anyone\u2019s watching or not. So the invisible man isn\u2019t getting away with it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture Tom, filling in an anonymous staff survey. His name is nowhere on it. He could settle a score with his manager, or be fair. Nothing outside him decides. Whatever he writes is a small, private portrait of Tom.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Plato\u2019s answer only persuades if you accept his picture of the soul. Plenty of people seem to cheat unseen and **sleep soundly**. For them, the inner damage is a claim, not something they feel.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The ring is any moment **nobody can check**. What you do there isn\u2019t a lapse or an exception. It\u2019s the clearest picture of **who you are**.'}]},republic_7:{title:'The Noble Lie',card:{insight:'A rule built on a story can break the day someone checks it.',tryThis:'Name one rule at work or home that rests on a story. Ask what happens if someone checks it.',skills:['thinking','leadership']},review:{prompt:'A team rule rests on a story nobody has ever checked. What do you ask?',right:'What happens on the day someone checks the story?',wrong:'Does the story keep the team calm and happy?',why:'If a rule rests on a story, it falls apart once someone looks.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A teacher tells the class that every seat was picked by **a magic hat**. Really, she chose them herself. Everyone sits quietly and nobody argues. But what happens the day you **find out**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'In Plato\u2019s ideal city, the rulers tell everyone **a story**. People are born with gold, silver, or iron and bronze inside them. That metal decides **their place**. Plato admits it\u2019s false. He wants it believed so the city stays calm.'},{kind:'moral',text:'If a rule only works because **you believe a story**, ask what happens when someone checks. A fair rule can **survive the truth**. A rule built on a fib can\u2019t.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A company tells new staff that pay bands are set by **a strict formula**. Nobody has ever seen it. Everyone accepts their band, and things run smoothly. What happens the day someone asks **to see the formula**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Plato\u2019s founders do something similar. They tell citizens they were formed from the earth with **gold, silver**, or iron and bronze mixed in. The metal sets each person\u2019s class. Plato calls it a falsehood and proposes it anyway.'},{kind:'explain',text:'His aim is **stability**: a story that makes the order feel natural, not imposed. The trouble is what it does to checking. Once rulers may deceive for your good, **you can\u2019t test** whether they\u2019re serving you. The test needs the truth.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Every arrangement runs on **some story**. The question is whether yours could survive **being checked**. If the order depends on nobody looking, it\u2019s already fragile.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Plato builds his ideal city with great care. Then, at its founding moment, he asks every citizen to believe **something untrue**. He says so openly. Why would a philosopher who prizes truth plant **a lie** at the base?'},{kind:'story',text:'The myth says citizens grew from the earth with a metal mixed into each: gold, silver, or iron and bronze. That metal fixes their class. Plato is candid it\u2019s false, and candid he wants it believed.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The justification is **stability**. The story makes the social order feel natural. But grant that rulers may deceive for the good of the ruled, and **no check** is left. Checking whether a lie serves you would require the truth.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a start-up whose founders tell staff they\u2019re **one big family**. It keeps people loyal through long nights. Then layoffs arrive by email. Overnight, the story stops working, and so does the trust it was holding up.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **the best defence**. Plato thinks every society already runs on founding stories. He\u2019s proposing to choose one on purpose rather than inherit one. That explains the move. It doesn\u2019t **dispose of** the objection.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Every order leans on **a story**. Ask whether yours could survive **someone checking**. If it can\u2019t, you\u2019re not keeping the peace, only borrowing it.'}]},republic_8:{title:'Sending the Poets Away',card:{insight:'The answer you hoped was wrong is often your most honest one.',tryThis:'Pick one fact you wish were not true. Write down why you still believe it.',skills:['thinking','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'Your numbers say your favorite plan will not work. You hate that answer. What now?',right:'Trust the answer more, since it goes against what you want',wrong:'Look for reasons it is wrong until the plan holds up',why:'If you wish an answer were wrong, your wish did not make it up.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Imagine you love a TV show **more than anything**. Then you work out it makes you grumpy and mean. Would you be brave enough to **switch it off**, even though you\u2019d miss it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Plato loved the poems of **Homer** all his life. But he decided poetry could train our feelings **the wrong way**. So in his ideal city, most poets had to leave. He admitted the argument went against his own love.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Sometimes the right answer is one **you don\u2019t like**. That\u2019s often a sign it\u2019s honest, not wishful. Follow the reasons, even when they **cost you** something.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve spent months on a plan **you love**. Then the numbers come in, and they say it won\u2019t work. You check them twice. They still say no. Do you trust the numbers or **your heart**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Plato faced this with poetry. He confesses a lifelong love of Homer. Yet his argument banishes most poets from the ideal city. Only hymns to the gods and praise of good men stay. He enforces a conclusion he plainly **doesn\u2019t enjoy**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'His serious charge is about **training**. Watching grief performed exercises the part of you that indulges emotion, where indulging costs nothing. Repeat it and it **shapes your character**. That\u2019s roughly what we now assume about media.'},{kind:'explain',text:'And he leaves **the door open**. He invites anyone to defend poetry in prose, and says he\u2019d gladly be persuaded. That\u2019s the mark of someone following an argument, not **a preference**. He hopes to lose, and checks anyway.'},{kind:'moral',text:'An argument you\u2019d rather lose is **hard to fake**. Your wishes are pulling the other way. So when your plan\u2019s numbers say no, the sting is **a reason** to trust them.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Plato had loved Homer all his life. Then his own argument told him to send **the poets away** from his ideal city. He followed it, and said it pained him. Why obey **an argument** that hurts?'},{kind:'explain',text:'His first charge is that imitation sits **three steps** from truth. A carpenter copies the idea of a bed. A painter copies the carpenter\u2019s bed. So a poem about life is a copy of **a copy**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The second charge is the serious one. Watching grief performed exercises **the part of you** that indulges feeling, at no cost. Repeat it often and it shapes **your disposition**. We assume much the same about media today.'},{kind:'story',text:'Yet he doesn\u2019t slam the door. He keeps hymns to the gods and praise of good men. And he invites poetry\u2019s friends to plead its case in prose, saying he\u2019d gladly be won back. People have taken him up on it ever since.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a researcher whose favourite theory fails her own test. She\u2019d love to tweak the test until it passes. Instead she writes up the failure. It\u2019s the least enjoyable paper she\u2019s written, and probably her most reliable.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. An unwelcome conclusion isn\u2019t automatically true. Some people enjoy grim answers and mistake gloom for rigour. And most readers now accept his training worry without **expelling anyone**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Notice when **you\u2019re arguing** for something you wish weren\u2019t true. Those arguments are your least enjoyable. They\u2019re also **your most reliable**, because your wishes were pulling against them.'}]},republic_9:{title:'The Boss They Asked For',card:{insight:'If a group cannot say no, one boss can take it over.',tryThis:'Pick a group you belong to. Name one thing it still says no to.',skills:['leadership','history']},review:{prompt:'A messy club gladly hands one eager member full control. What should worry you?',right:'Once he is in charge, nobody can say no to him',wrong:'He might not do as good a job as he promised',why:'A messy group invites a boss, and then it cannot tell him no.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Imagine a classroom where **nobody** has to listen to anyone. No rules, no teacher in charge. At first it\u2019s fun. Then it gets loud and messy. Who do you think everyone **turns to**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Plato thought too much freedom with **no one in charge** can tip over. When things get messy, people want someone strong to fix it. So they **hand power** to a person who promises to protect them. Nobody forced it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A group that can\u2019t say **no** to anything gets messy. Then it often **invites** a boss who says no to everyone. Keep a few fair rules, and you won\u2019t need one.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A volunteer group drops its rules one by one. Nobody wants to seem **bossy**. Meetings run for hours and nothing gets decided. Then one member offers to sort it all out. You\u2019re **relieved**. What could go wrong?'},{kind:'explain',text:'In Book VIII, Plato traces how cities decay. Democracy\u2019s great good is **freedom**. But a society built around it starts treating **any constraint** as oppression. The father fears his son and the teacher his pupil. Nobody can impose anything.'},{kind:'story',text:'Then the tyrant arrives, not as an invader but as **a champion**. He presents himself as the people\u2019s protector against the wealthy. He\u2019s given a bodyguard. By the time the danger is visible, he holds **the only organised force**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Disorder makes people **want a protector**, and the protector is usually invited. If your group can\u2019t say no to anything, it can\u2019t say no to **whoever takes charge**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture a city that prizes **freedom** above everything else. No army is at its gates. Later, one man rules it outright. Nobody conquered it. So **what happened**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'In Book VIII, Plato traces constitutions decaying in sequence, ending in **democracy** and then tyranny. His version is mechanical, not moralising. Freedom becomes the only value, so every constraint starts to feel like **oppression**. Even fathers fear their sons.'},{kind:'story',text:'Into that disorder steps a champion. He stands up for the people against the wealthy, and they give him a bodyguard. When the danger is finally visible, he commands **the only organised force**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a company that drops every process because process feels like bureaucracy. Within a year, nothing ships and nobody decides. A new chief arrives promising to fix it, and staff cheer as she takes **every decision** for herself.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Plato was hostile to democracy and didn\u2019t describe Athens fairly. His sequence **isn\u2019t a prediction**. What survives is narrower: protectors tend to be invited after disorder.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When a group can\u2019t refuse **anything**, it can\u2019t refuse whoever offers to take over. Watch for the moment you\u2019d welcome a protector. That\u2019s when **your no** matters most.'}]}}}},990,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'richest-man-in-babylon',kind:'book',promise:'Save part of your pay and help it grow',about:'Why do so many people get paid more and still save nothing? A writer named George Clason told stories about it. They take place in Babylon, a rich city long ago. Each one has a plain rule about money. Will you keep them?',ideas:{'richest-man-in-babylon_1':{title:'Pay Yourself First',simple:{headline:'Save some of your pay first, then spend the rest',body:'In George Clason\u2019s book, a young scribe in Babylon asks a rich moneylender how to become wealthy. The answer is plain. Keep at least a tenth of everything you earn, and take it first. Your pay lands on the 28th, and you plan to save whatever is left. By the 27th, almost nothing is. Small purchases use up whatever stays in your main account. **Move your tenth out on payday, before you spend anything.** The rest of the month will fit around what\u2019s left. If a tenth is too much right now, start smaller.',doToday:'Set up an automatic payday transfer of a tenth of your pay into a separate savings account.'},card:{insight:'Put part of your pay into savings first, then spend what is left.',tryThis:'Today, set up your bank to save a dime of each dollar you earn.',skills:['money','habits']},review:{prompt:'You try to save what is left each month, but none is left. What now?',right:'Save some on payday, before you spend any',wrong:'Keep a closer eye on what you spend each day',why:'If you save first, you cannot spend that money.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You get pocket money every Saturday, and you plan to save what\u2019s left. By Friday the jar is empty again. It\u2019s **always empty**. So where does all **that money go**?'},{kind:'story',text:'In a very old story, a young man called Arkad asks a rich moneylender for his secret. The answer is plain. Keep one coin of every ten you earn. And keep it first, before you buy a single thing.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Money you plan to save **at the end** gets spent along the way. So save **first**, the day it arrives. Then spend what\u2019s left.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your pay lands on the 28th. Rent, the phone and a card bill all leave that week. You\u2019ll save **whatever survives**, like every month. By the 27th, it\u2019s about **forty pounds**. Sound familiar?'},{kind:'story',text:'Clason\u2019s parable puts this in ancient Babylon. Arkad, a young scribe, asks a rich moneylender how to become wealthy. The answer is plain. You pay the baker and the sandal-maker, so pay yourself too. Keep at least a tenth of all you earn.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'With the same income and the same bills, only **which claim** comes **first** changes.',left:{title:'Save what\u2019s left',items:['Spending gets first claim','Savings are the leftover','Usually near zero']},right:{title:'Save on payday',items:['Saving comes off first','Spending fits what\u2019s left','The month bends around it']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Why does order beat willpower? Money left at month\u2019s end is whatever survived **every small decision**. Each one saw room in the balance and used some. Take the saving out first, and each decision sees **less room**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Saving what\u2019s left yields **almost nothing**, at almost any income. Take your tenth **on payday**, and let the month fit around it.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A young scribe in ancient Babylon asks a rich moneylender how to get wealthy. The answer isn\u2019t a trade, a tip or **a lucky deal**. It costs **nothing to learn**. So why does almost nobody do it?'},{kind:'story',text:'That scribe is Arkad, and he grows up to be the richest man in Babylon. Clason has him teach seven cures for a lean purse. The first is plain. Of every ten coins you put in your purse, take out only nine.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The mechanism is about **order, not willpower**. Spending grows to fill the money you can see. So the limit that binds is what\u2019s in the account, not what you earn. Take the tenth out first and **the rest adjusts**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Two friends earn the same and both mean to save a tenth. One saves the morning she\u2019s paid. The other saves at month\u2019s end. A year later, only the first has savings. Nothing differed but the order.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The rule assumes a margin. If your income doesn\u2019t cover rent and food, a tenth taken first returns as costly borrowing. And the tenth is **a convention**, not a law.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The amount you save matters less than **when you save it**. Take your share first, and **spending becomes** the leftover instead.'}]},'richest-man-in-babylon_2':{title:'The Rising Shelf',simple:{headline:'Sort your needs from your wants to save more',body:'George Clason noticed that what people call necessary spending grows to match their income, whatever they earn. You get a pay rise. Within a year there\u2019s a nicer coffee each morning and a taxi home on Fridays. Each one started as a treat and now feels like a need, yet your savings haven\u2019t moved. **Before payday, list your spending and mark each item as a need or a want.** Wants are fine. Choose them on purpose, so habit doesn\u2019t choose them for you.',doToday:'List five things you paid for this week, and write N for need or W for want beside each one.'},card:{insight:'When your pay grows, your wants grow too and use up the extra.',tryThis:'Tonight, list what you bought this week. Mark each one as a need or a want.',skills:['money','psychology']},review:{prompt:'Your pay went up last year, but your savings did not. Why?',right:'Your wants grew until they used up the extra pay',wrong:'Prices went up, and there was nothing you could do',why:'Each new want starts to feel like a need, so the extra gets spent.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'When you first buy extra lives in a phone game, it feels like **a treat**. A few months later, you buy them every week. When did that turn into **part of the game**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Nobody **decided** that. It just crept up. Things you buy often start to feel normal, and then they feel needed. That\u2019s why **more pocket money** rarely feels like enough. Your wants grow to fit it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Wants aren\u2019t bad, but you should **see which is which**. Write your **money plan down** before the money comes. Then spending is a choice, not a habit.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your pay rose eighteen months ago. Your savings didn\u2019t. You can\u2019t name **one big purchase** that ate the difference. So where did the rise go, if **nobody chose** to spend it?'},{kind:'story',text:'Usually it went on small upgrades. A better phone plan, more takeaways, a gym you still pay for. Each began as a treat and became regular. Within months it felt normal, and now it reads as a need.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Arkad\u2019s second cure is to **control your expenditures**. Clason splits necessary expenses from desires. Then he makes an odd claim: desires will always **outrun income**, however high it rises. He doesn\u2019t call that greed. It\u2019s just how wanting behaves.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Write the list **before payday**, and every regular cost lands in **one column**.',left:{title:'Needed',items:['Rent and food','Getting to work','Clothes that fit']},right:{title:'Wanted, by habit',items:['The pricier phone plan','Weekly takeaways','The gym you skip']}},{kind:'moral',text:'You\u2019ll never fund **every desire**, at any income. So choose **in writing** which ones this month will pay for. A want you chose is fine.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two shelves hold **the same jars**. The lower shelf is what felt necessary last year. The upper one is this year, after a pay rise. You never decided to need more. So **who moved them**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Nadia gets a pay rise. Soon there\u2019s a nicer coffee each morning, taxis home on Fridays, a second streaming service. Each began as a treat. A year on, she lists them all as necessary, and her savings haven\u2019t moved.'},{kind:'explain',text:'That\u2019s Arkad\u2019s **second cure**: control your expenditures. Clason separates necessary expenses from desires, and says desires always outrun income, at any level. So the question isn\u2019t how to fund every wish. It\u2019s **which ones** you\u2019ll fund.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take someone earning far more than Nadia. Her list has a bigger house, a second car and private school fees. It feels just as tight. Different items, same shape: the needs rose to meet the money.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some costs really do become necessary: a child, a longer commute, care for a parent. Treat every rise as drift and a budget becomes **a guilt list**. And if income can\u2019t cover real needs, desire isn\u2019t the problem.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A written budget isn\u2019t there to **ban things**. It makes the sorting visible, so you can **change it**. Fund the desires you chose, and question the ones that just arrived.'}]},'richest-man-in-babylon_3':{title:'Gold That Works',simple:{headline:'Leave what your savings earn in the bank to grow',body:'Money sitting in an account that pays nothing loses ground as prices rise. Clason\u2019s hero Arkad makes a different mistake. He lends his savings to a craftsman who pays him for it, then spends each payment as it arrives. His pile never grows. His teacher tells him he has eaten the coins that should have been earning for him. **Keep savings where they earn, and leave the earnings in.** Then the earnings earn too, and the pile grows on itself. No return is guaranteed, so learn before you choose.',doToday:'Look up the interest rate on your savings today, and write it next to the balance.'},card:{insight:'When your savings earn money, leave it in so it can earn more.',tryThis:'Today, check what your savings earned last month. Leave it in the bank.',skills:['money']},review:{prompt:'Your bank paid you $5 for your savings this month. What do you do?',right:'Leave the $5 in the bank so it can earn too',wrong:'Spend the $5, since your savings are still safe',why:'Money you leave in can earn more, so your pile grows fast.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You keep a seed in a drawer for a year. When you open the drawer, what do you find? **One seed**, exactly as before. What would have happened if you\u2019d **planted it**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Saved money is like that seed. In a jar, **\xa3100 stays \xa3100**, while prices slowly creep up. A bank can pay you a little extra for keeping it, called **interest**. Leave that extra in, and it can earn too.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Saving is **step one**. Step two is letting your savings **earn more money**, then letting that money earn as well. A coin in a jar doesn\u2019t grow.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Say you and a friend each save a tenth for thirty years. You leave yours in an account earning nothing. She puts hers somewhere **modest and boring**. Same habit, same deposits. Where do **the two piles** end up?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Your idle pile is just the deposits, minus what **rising prices** took. Hers earns a modest return, and **its earnings** earn too. Depending on the rate, hers can end up **roughly twice** the size, or more.'},{kind:'story',text:'In Clason\u2019s parable, Arkad finally lends his savings to a craftsman who pays for the use of them. Arkad spends each payment as it arrives. His teacher tells him he has eaten the very coins that should have been earning for him.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Saving is only **the first step**. The second is sending your saved money out to earn, then sending **its earnings** out too. It\u2019s never guaranteed, but idle money doesn\u2019t stand still.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Arkad has saved at last, and his savings are out on loan, earning. Each time a payment comes back, he spends it. His pile **isn\u2019t growing**. What has he **got wrong**?'},{kind:'story',text:'His teacher, the moneylender Algamish, shows him the fix. Each payment that comes back must go straight out to work as well. Only once Arkad does that does his wealth start to grow on itself.'},{kind:'explain',text:'That loop has a name: **compounding**. You earn on past earnings as well as the first sum. Early gains look too small to bother with. Over decades, most of the gap opens **in the later years**, after many people have given up.'},{kind:'story',text:'Then there\u2019s Tom. He\u2019s saved steadily for ten years, all of it earning nothing. He\u2019s waiting until he understands investing perfectly. Meanwhile rising prices nibble at it. His waiting feels like caution, but it\u2019s a choice too, and it has a cost.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Clason says nothing about risk, diversification or fees. Sending money out to work is exactly where it **can be lost**, and no return is guaranteed. He answers whether to invest, not what in. That needs better sources.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Idle money **loses ground**, slowly and quietly. Money sent to earn, with **its earnings** sent out again, can grow on itself. So once you\u2019ve saved, don\u2019t stop there.'}]},'richest-man-in-babylon_4':{title:'The Brickmaker\u2019s Jewels',simple:{headline:'Get money tips from people who are good with money',body:'Clason tells of Arkad handing his first year of savings to a brickmaker travelling to buy jewels. The brickmaker is honest, but he knows bricks. The jewels turn out to be worthless glass. Today the brickmaker might be a cousin who works at a bank, or a confident friend with a crypto tip. Being near a subject can look like knowing it. Before you act on a tip, **ask what that person has done with their own money**, and for how long. If they can\u2019t answer, treat the tip as a guess.',doToday:'Write down one money tip you\u2019re following and what the person who gave it has done with their own savings.'},card:{insight:'Being good at one thing does not make you good with money.',tryThis:'Next time you get a money tip, ask if it worked for that person.',skills:['money','thinking']},review:{prompt:'Your uncle, who fixes cars, gives you a money tip. What do you do first?',right:'Ask if the tip worked with his own money',wrong:'Use it, since he is a smart and honest man',why:'A kind, smart person can still give a bad money tip.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your bike chain keeps slipping off. One friend talks about bikes **all day long**. Another says less, but she fixes bikes every weekend. You can only ask one. **Who do you** ask?'},{kind:'story',text:'In Clason\u2019s story, young Arkad saves money for a whole year. A brickmaker he knows is going on a trip to buy jewels. Arkad gives him all his savings. The brickmaker is honest. But the jewels turn out to be worthless.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Knowing about bricks doesn\u2019t mean **knowing about jewels**. Ask someone who has **really done it**, not just someone who sounds sure. So ask your friend who fixes bikes.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'At a family lunch, your cousin talks about money with **total confidence**. She works at a bank and handles other people\u2019s accounts all day. She tells you exactly where to put your savings. Surely **she knows**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Clason tells of Arkad\u2019s first year of savings. He hands them to a brickmaker travelling to buy jewels. The brickmaker is honest and knows nothing about jewels. The stones are worthless. Arkad\u2019s teacher asks why anyone would trust a brickmaker about jewels.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'From outside, being **near a subject** looks just like being **good at it**.',left:{title:'Near the subject',items:['Works with it daily','Knows the words','Sounds sure']},right:{title:'Competent in it',items:['Did it with their own money','Over years, not luck','Results you can see']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Why is the mistake so common? **Confidence** is easy to see. A track record isn\u2019t, because you\u2019d have to ask, and asking feels rude. So ask anyway. What have they done with their own money, and **for how long**?'},{kind:'moral',text:'Closeness to money isn\u2019t **skill with money**. Before you act on advice, ask what that person has **actually done**, in this exact area. Ask the brickmaker about bricks.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Three people offer to advise you on your savings. A loan officer who handles money all day. A commentator with **strong views**. A friend whose savings have grown steadily for decades. Whom would **Arkad\u2019s teacher** trust?'},{kind:'story',text:'Arkad trusts his first year\u2019s savings to a brickmaker buying jewels abroad. The jewels are worthless. His teacher, the moneylender Algamish, isn\u2019t sympathetic. Advice is handed out freely, he says, so take only the advice that\u2019s worth having.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The test isn\u2019t **a job title**. It asks three things. Has this person done the thing, with **their own resources**, for long enough to rule out luck? That\u2019s a higher bar than most advice you\u2019ll ever hear.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a brilliant surgeon at a dinner party, sharing tips on which shares to buy. She\u2019s honest and clever and very sure. In money, though, she\u2019s the brickmaker. Her skill is real, just in another field.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. A track record can be luck that hasn\u2019t run out yet. People who failed at the same thing rarely give advice. And the test can wrongly dismiss **a trained adviser** bound to act in your interest.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Excellence in one field says **nothing about another**. Ask what someone has done, with **their own money**, and for how long. Then hold even a good answer loosely.'}]},'richest-man-in-babylon_5':{title:'The Unbroken Run',simple:{headline:'Save each time you get paid, with no breaks',body:'Clason\u2019s rules are plain, like saving a tenth and guarding what you save. Most people agree with them. Far fewer keep them going. A couple saves every month for three years, then pauses for a holiday and means to restart in spring. The pause turns into a year. The results come from keeping the rule without a break. **A gap costs more than the months you miss.** You also lose what that money would have earned later, and the habit gets harder to restart.',doToday:'Pick one money rule you already agree with, and mark today on your calendar as day one of keeping it.'},card:{insight:'If you stop saving, you lose that money and what it would have earned.',tryThis:'Today, check that part of your pay still goes into savings.',skills:['habits','money']},review:{prompt:'You have saved for three years. A big trip comes up. What now?',right:'Pay for the trip and keep saving each payday',wrong:'Stop saving for a year, then start again',why:'A gap costs you the money and what it would have earned.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You walk across a patch of grass once, and nothing changes. You walk the same way every day, all year. One morning there\u2019s **a path**. Which walk **made it**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'No single walk made it. **All of them** did, and money works the same way. Saving \xa32 a week sounds tiny, but kept up for a year it\u2019s \xa3104. The danger is **the gap**. Skip one week, and skipping the next gets easier.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You don\u2019t need a **clever trick** with money. You need a few **plain rules**, kept going for a long time, with no gaps.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A couple has saved a tenth every month for **three years**. Then a holiday comes up. They pause the saving, meaning to restart in the spring. You can probably guess what happens **next**.'},{kind:'story',text:'The pause becomes a year. Nothing about their plan was wrong. They knew the rule and agreed with it. They\u2019d even proved they could keep it. It simply wasn\u2019t kept, and that one gap cost more than a year.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Most money trouble isn\u2019t **a knowledge problem**. The rules are few and **widely known**.',left:{title:'What people expect',items:['A secret insight','One brilliant move','The newest trick']},right:{title:'What actually works',items:['A few plain rules','The same dull rule','Years without a break']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Why does a gap cost so much? The book\u2019s rules **work through time**. Saving first pays only if it happens every payday. Money at work only compounds if it\u2019s left alone. Drop a year, and you also lose **its later earnings**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A thin purse is **fixable**, and the fix is a plan, not luck. Run one **plain rule** without a break, for years. That\u2019s the whole secret, and it\u2019s rare.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Save a tenth. Guard your gold. Own your home. Read the book quickly and it looks like **a list of maxims**, nice to agree with. What if that\u2019s **the wrong way** to read it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The maxims are secondary. The frame of the whole book is that a lean purse is **a fixable condition**. The fix is a plan, followed for years, not **a stroke of luck**. The hard part is repetition, not insight.'},{kind:'story',text:'Even Arkad doesn\u2019t get it right at once. His first savings vanish with the brickmaker. His next earn, and he spends what they earn. The book\u2019s answer isn\u2019t a cleverer move. It\u2019s the plan, kept.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now two savers in our time. Both know the same rules. One saves the same tenth every month for six years. The other has read every book on money and saves when inspired. Only one of them has savings worth counting.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Why is a break so costly? **Growth builds on growth**. So a plan kept for **twenty years** ends well over twice the size of one kept for ten. Stop for a year, and you lose that year and everything it would have earned.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The plan needs a surplus, and the book is silent about incomes too low to make one. It\u2019s written for someone **misspending a margin**. Said to someone with none, it can sound like a verdict on their character.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Agreeing with the rules costs **nothing**. Keeping them for years is **the real work**, and the real result. Which rule do you know but have never run unbroken?'}]},'richest-man-in-babylon_6':{title:'The Arithmetic of Ruin',simple:{headline:'Keep your money safe before you try to grow it',body:'An old friend at a wedding tells you about a scheme paying three per cent a month. The payments have always arrived, he says. Clason\u2019s Babylon is full of people ruined this way, chasing earnings that looked too good. The sums show why caution comes first. **Lose half, and the rest must double to get you back to where you were.** So before you ask what it pays, ask how you\u2019d get your money out if it failed. If nobody can give you a clear answer, keep your money where it is.',doToday:'List where each part of your savings sits, and how fast you could get it back.'},card:{insight:'If you lose half your money, the rest has to double just to get back.',tryThis:'Before you put money in a deal, ask how you would get it back.',skills:['money','thinking']},review:{prompt:'A friend says his plan pays a lot each month. What do you ask first?',right:'How would I get my money back if it fails?',wrong:'How much more could I make if I put more in?',why:'If you lose half, the rest has to double to win it back.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You had \xa320. You lose half, so now you have \xa310. How much does your \xa310 have to grow to get you **back to \xa320**? Half again? Or **something bigger**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Half of \xa310 is only \xa35, which gets you to \xa315. To reach \xa320, your \xa310 must **double**. So losing is quick, and **winning it back** is slow. That\u2019s why safety comes first.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Before you think about **how much** money could grow, ask one question. If it goes wrong, **how would I** get it back?'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'At a wedding, an old school friend tells you about a scheme paying **three per cent** a month. The payments have always arrived. You\u2019ve got your first few thousand saved. He can get you in **this week**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Clason\u2019s Babylon is full of people ruined the same way. They chased earnings that looked too good, or put gold into things they didn\u2019t understand. Every one went straight to the yield and skipped the question of safety.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Losses and gains don\u2019t balance. Lose half of \xa34,000 and you\u2019re left with \xa32,000. To get back to \xa34,000, you now need **a hundred per cent** gain, not fifty. So guarding the money is **arithmetic**, not nerves.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Ask the questions **in order**. Every ruin in the book starts in **the left column**.',left:{title:'Return first',items:['How much could I make?','How fast?']},right:{title:'Safety first',items:['How would I get it back?','Who has done this before?','What if it fails?']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Guard **the money** before you look at **the return**. A loss needs a bigger gain to repair it, so avoiding one is worth more than chasing an equal win.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your \xa3100 falls by half, then rises by half. The two moves are **the same size**. So you\u2019re back where you started, right? **Do the sums** before you answer. Where does the money end up?'},{kind:'explain',text:'It ends at **\xa375, not \xa3100**. The fall takes half of a larger sum than the rise has to work with. That\u2019s why a loss needs **a bigger gain** to repair it. Avoiding losses beats chasing gains of the same size.'},{kind:'story',text:'A fund loses 40% one year and gains 40% the next. Its manager tells investors they\u2019re back where they started. They aren\u2019t. \xa3100 fell to \xa360, and 40% of \xa360 only lifts it to \xa384.'},{kind:'explain',text:'That\u2019s why Arkad tells you to **guard your treasure** from loss. The book\u2019s laws of gold warn that money flees anyone chasing **impossible earnings**. Study each investment first, and consult people experienced in handling it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture Lena, who took years to save her first small surplus. One avoidable loss wouldn\u2019t just shrink the sum. It could break the habit that built it. For her, survival comes before any question of return.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Nothing is perfectly safe, and safety costs something too. Money kept where it can\u2019t fall usually earns little, and rising prices nibble at it. And fraudsters **advertise safety** loudly. A promise of safety isn\u2019t proof.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Ask how you\u2019d **get it back** before you ask what it pays. The sums are unkind to losses, so **the principal** comes first.'}]},'richest-man-in-babylon_7':{title:'The Roof You Own',simple:{headline:'Buy a home only if you will stay for years',body:'Clason told readers to own their home, since rent buys shelter and nothing more. He was right that housing is usually the biggest bill a household pays. He was too quick to say buying always wins. Say you expect to move for work in two years. The fees for buying and selling, plus repairs, could cost more than two years of rent. If you\u2019ll stay for ten years, buying may well come out ahead. **Put every cost of both choices on one page before you decide.** Renting also buys you the freedom to move.',doToday:'Write your monthly housing cost at the top of a page, then list three costs that owning would add.'},card:{insight:'A home pays off if you stay for years, but not if you move soon.',tryThis:'Tonight, write how many years you will stay. Then add up what buying would cost.',skills:['money','thinking']},review:{prompt:'You will move for work in two years. Should you buy a home now?',right:'Not if the fees cost more than two years of rent',wrong:'Yes, because rent is money thrown away for good',why:'If you move soon, the fees to buy and sell can cost more than rent.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You hire a bike every Saturday for \xa33. After a year, you\u2019ve paid **\xa3156**, and you still don\u2019t own a bike. Renting a home works the same way. Is **owning** always better?'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'A writer named Clason said **rent is gone**, but owning **builds up**. Both have costs.',left:{title:'Renting',items:['The money is gone','The owner fixes the roof','Easy to move']},right:{title:'Owning',items:['You keep what you build','You pay to fix the roof','Hard to move']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Rent money is gone once it\u2019s paid, so Clason was **partly right**. But owning a home costs money too. So neither is **always better**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'For most households, one regular payment is bigger than food, transport or energy. You hand it over every month and **own nothing** for it. What is it, and was Clason right about **what to do**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'It\u2019s housing, usually by a distance. Clason\u2019s fifth cure says **own your home**. Rent buys a year of shelter and nothing more, while payments toward ownership **build an asset**. And someone secure in their own home plans further ahead.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The comparison has **two sides**, and the book weighs **only one**.',left:{title:'What it counts',items:['Rent paid and gone','Payments building an asset','The security of owning']},right:{title:'What it ignores',items:['Repairs and insurance','Buying and selling costs','Prices that can fall']}},{kind:'explain',text:'What survives is narrower. Housing decisions shape your finances **more than** any investment choice. But Clason\u2019s conclusion, therefore buy, doesn\u2019t follow automatically. **It depends** on prices, interest, buying and selling costs, and how long you\u2019ll stay.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Clason was right that housing **matters most**. He was too quick to say **always buy**. Count every cost on both sides before you decide.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Rent is dead money, so buying is always prudent. You\u2019ve heard it a hundred times. It\u2019s also the advice in Clason\u2019s **fifth cure**. So why is it the book\u2019s **weakest chapter**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Arkad presents owning as **a double gain**. Payments that would have gone to a landlord build an asset. And a man secure in his own home, he says, thinks differently about his work. The second claim is fair. **The first** carries the weight.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The chapter assumes property **rises in value**. It ignores repairs, insurance, the costs of buying and selling, and being tied to one place. It treats one big purchase on **borrowed money** as plainly prudent. Written in 1926, that confidence hasn\u2019t travelled well.'},{kind:'story',text:'Sam expects to move cities for work within two years. A friend tells him rent is dead money, so buy now. But buying and selling costs could outweigh two years of rent. For Sam, renting may be cheaper.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Ruth, who plans to stay in one town for many years. Where she lives, buying costs less than renting over that span, repairs included. For her, Clason\u2019s advice may well be right. Same rule, opposite answer.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The idea fails wherever its assumptions fail: falling prices, a short stay, high interest, big repair bills. Its real error is turning **a conditional judgement** into a rule. Rent buys flexibility, and hands the price risk to someone else.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Housing is usually **your biggest cost**, so the choice matters. But whether to buy **depends on you**: how long you\u2019ll stay, and what each side really costs.'}]},'richest-man-in-babylon_8':{title:'Seventy, Twenty, Ten',simple:{headline:'Pay a little to each person you owe',body:'In one of Clason\u2019s stories, Dabasir the camel trader owes money across Babylon and hides from the people he owes. His plan splits every ten coins he earns. Seven go on living, two are shared among the people he owes, and he keeps one. **Each person he owes gets something every time, so he can stop hiding.** You might owe a card company and a friend. Fix your split before payday and send each a share. If one debt charges much higher interest, pay more towards that one.',doToday:'List everyone you owe, and write beside each name a small amount you can pay them on your next payday.'},card:{insight:'Pay a bit on each debt when you get paid, and keep a bit too.',tryThis:'Tonight, list everyone you owe. Write what you can pay each one on payday.',skills:['money','habits']},review:{prompt:'You owe a bank and a friend. Payday comes. What do you do?',right:'Pay each one a little, and keep a little too',wrong:'Pay the one who asks the loudest, in full',why:'When each one gets a share, nobody is left out.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Dabasir is a camel trader in old Babylon, in one of Clason\u2019s stories. He owes money to lots of people. He\u2019s so ashamed that he **hides from them** in the market. What can he **possibly do**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'He makes a plan. Of every **10 coins** he earns, 7 are for living. 2 are shared among **everyone he owes**, and 1 is kept. Say you owe your brother and a friend. You\u2019d give each a coin, every time.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When everyone you owe gets **a little each time**, nobody is left out. You never have **to hide**. And you still keep one coin in ten.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You owe four people: a card company, a relative, an old landlord and a friend. You pay **whoever shouts loudest**. When you spot the friend\u2019s car outside the shop, you **wait until** it\u2019s gone.'},{kind:'story',text:'Clason tells this through clay tablets about Dabasir, a camel trader of Babylon deep in debt. His plan has three parts. Seven-tenths of everything he earns is for living. Two-tenths is divided among every creditor. One-tenth he keeps.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Why does the **twenty** matter so much? Retellings often keep only the saving. But save a tenth while ignoring debts, and you end up borrowing to cover the gap. The debt share is what makes **the arithmetic** hold together.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'It also changes how you live, because **nobody** is being **avoided** anymore.',left:{title:'Loudest first',items:['The loudest gets paid','The rest hear nothing','You dodge half the street']},right:{title:'Everyone, every time',items:['Each creditor gets something','Each knows the plan','Nobody to hide from']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Fix the split **before the money** arrives, and keep to it. Every creditor gets something every time, so you can **walk the market** again.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'One chapter opens with clay tablets, dug up and translated. They tell how a camel trader of Babylon climbed out of **deep debt**. A modern professor, in debt himself, decides to **try the plan**. What does it say?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The trader, Dabasir, splits every payment the moment it arrives. **Seventy per cent** to live on, twenty shared among creditors, ten kept. The value isn\u2019t in the numbers. It\u2019s in **fixing them in advance**, so they\u2019re never renegotiated under pressure.'},{kind:'story',text:'Dabasir had been hiding from the people he owed, keeping to the edges of the market. Under the plan, every creditor gets something each time. Nobody is being avoided. He can walk down the middle of the market again, head up.'},{kind:'explain',text:'It\u2019s the **same mechanism** as paying yourself first. Every share comes off each payment at once, so the month adjusts to seven-tenths. Whether seventy is right for you is **plainly arguable**. The structure is what transfers.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Kim. She owes a friend \xa3500 with no interest, and a card company \xa33,000 at a high rate. Dabasir\u2019s split sends both a share. It keeps the friendship whole. But every pound sent to the friend isn\u2019t cutting the costly debt.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Where one debt charges far more interest, clearing it first usually costs less overall. Some debts carry heavier penalties or **legal priority**. And if seven-tenths can\u2019t cover living costs, the plan can\u2019t even start.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Decide the split once, apply it to **every payment**, and revisit it rarely. Its best work is human: it **ends the hiding**. Weigh that against the interest you\u2019ll pay.'}]},'richest-man-in-babylon_9':{title:'The Seventh Cure',simple:{headline:'Get better at your job so you earn more',body:'Most of Clason\u2019s rules divide the money you already earn. His last rule grows it. Arkad tells his students to keep learning their craft and get more skilful at it. You and a colleague both save a tenth. You stay at the same level for years. She spends two evenings a week getting better at her job. Within a few years she earns a third more. **Her tenth grew because she grew the pay it comes from.** Keep the saving habit first, or a bigger income turns into bigger spending.',doToday:'Practise one skill your pay depends on for 20 minutes today.'},card:{insight:'Saving uses the pay you have, but more skill can make your pay grow.',tryThis:'Today, spend ten minutes on one skill your job pays more for.',skills:['career','money']},review:{prompt:'Your pay has not gone up in years. What should you try first?',right:'Get better at a skill your job pays more for',wrong:'Save two dollars in ten to make up for it',why:'More skill grows the pay that your savings come from.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You wash cars on your street for **\xa33 each**. At first one car takes you a whole hour. Then you learn a few tricks. Soon it takes half an hour, and it **looks better** too. What changed?'},{kind:'explain',text:'**You** did. The price didn\u2019t change, but the earner did. Now you can wash **twice as many** cars, and earn more. And one coin in ten of a bigger pile is a bigger coin.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Saving is about the money you have. This idea is about **getting better** at what earns it. Learn to save first, though, or more money just means **more spending**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two people each save a tenth of their pay. One earns the same for ten years. The other spends a few evenings a week **getting better** at her work. Ten years on, whose tenth is **bigger**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Within a few years she earns a third more. Her tenth is now a third larger, with no change in the habit at all. She didn\u2019t save harder. She grew the number the saving is taken from.'},{kind:'explain',text:'That\u2019s Arkad\u2019s seventh and final cure: **increase your ability** to earn. Study, become more skilful, learn more of your craft. It\u2019s the only cure that raises **the number** every other rule takes a share of.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Why put it last? A larger income without the saving habit simply becomes **larger spending**. So the habit comes first. Then your effort goes into **the earner**, the thing that produces the money.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The other cures divide **your income**. This one grows it. Put the same steady effort into **one skill** that you put into saving.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Every rule in this book about saving is **a percentage** of something. A tenth here, seven-tenths there. Only one rule changes **the something** itself. Which one is it, and why does Clason save it for last?'},{kind:'explain',text:'It\u2019s Arkad\u2019s seventh cure: grow your **ability to earn**. He treats skill as the asset that makes the surplus everything else needs. The one who keeps learning a craft, and works at doing it better, is **richly rewarded**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture Dan. He\u2019s saved a tenth faithfully for six years, but his pay hasn\u2019t moved. He could push his saving to a fifth, or chase higher returns. Both work on the same fixed sum. Neither changes what he earns.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture Mara, on a good income that rises every year. She saves nothing, because each pay rise turns into spending. For her, more skill would only mean more spending. The saving habit has to come first.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. It\u2019s the book\u2019s thinnest chapter. It never says which skills to build, or what to do when **a craft dies out**. And if you can\u2019t yet cover basic needs, this cure comes first, not last.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The other cures divide a fixed sum. Growing **the earner** changes the sum. Keep the habit, then put that same steady effort **into your skill**.'}]}}}},991,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'science-and-hypothesis',kind:'book',promise:'Know which rules were found and which were picked',about:'Which rules in science were found in nature, and which did people just pick? Henri Poincar\xe9 was a French math expert. He asked that in a book in 1902. These ideas help you sort facts from choices. Which of your rules were picked?',ideas:{'science-and-hypothesis_1':{title:'Picked, Not Found',card:{insight:'Some rules were not found in nature but picked to make work easy.',tryThis:'Pick one rule your team follows. Ask if it was found or picked, and if it still helps.',skills:['thinking','science']},review:{prompt:'Two teams argue about the right way to number their files. What do you ask?',right:'Which way makes the work easy for everyone',wrong:'Which way is the one true, correct way',why:'People picked how to number the files, so judge how well it works.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'In Britain, cars drive on the **left**. In France, they drive on the **right**. Nobody is lost or confused. So which side is the **true side** to drive on?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Neither side is true. Each country **picked one**. But the choice still matters, because drivers picking at random would crash. A thermometer works the same way. Someone chose where **zero goes**, and the water doesn\u2019t care.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Some rules in science were **picked, not found**. People chose them because they make the work easier. When you meet a rule, ask if **you could have** picked another.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A new colleague asks why the team writes dates as **year, month, day**. One person says it\u2019s simply correct. Another says it was agreed, so files sort in order. Which answer tells you **what the rule is**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Henri Poincar\xe9, a French mathematician, wrote a book on this in 1902. His word for rules like the date format was convention. Nobody found year, month, day in nature. The team chose it, for a reason: the files sort.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'A single theory usually holds **both kinds**. The skill is **telling them apart**.',left:{title:'Discovered',items:['Found by looking','Could have turned out otherwise','Can be shown false']},right:{title:'Agreed',items:['Chosen by people','Kept because it helps','Judged by how well it works']}},{kind:'explain',text:'A convention is **free, but not random**. Pick a clumsy one and every sum after it gets harder. And experiment never tests it alone. It tests **the whole package**: the convention plus the laws written in its terms.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Some principles aren\u2019t discoveries at all. They\u2019re **conventions**, kept because they make the rest simpler. So judge yours by **how well they work**, not by whether they\u2019re true.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Paris, 1902. One of the great mathematicians of his age writes a book for **the public**. Its question sounds simple. Which parts of science did we **learn from the world**, and which did we put there?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Poincar\xe9\u2019s answer was **conventions**. These are principles neither found by experiment nor forced by logic. You\u2019re free to choose them, guided by experience. They can\u2019t be true or false. They can only be **more or less convenient**.'},{kind:'story',text:'His main example was geometry. You could keep any geometry you liked, he argued, and adjust the physics to match. Experiment checks the two together, never one alone. So the choice of geometry is a choice about **bookkeeping**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a thermometer. One scale puts freezing at 0, another at 32. No test could show one scale is right. But a scale with odd, uneven marks would make **every reading** harder. Convenience is a real limit.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Calling something a convention also **shields it** from evidence. So the label has to be argued for, case by case. And spotting conventions in a real theory is far harder than on the page.'},{kind:'moral',text:'This isn\u2019t a licence to believe anything. Experiment still judges **the whole system**. But inside it, some parts are **your choices**, and you should know which.'}]},'science-and-hypothesis_2':{title:'A Heap of Stones',card:{insight:'Facts mean little until a guess tells you which ones count.',tryThis:'Before you look anything up today, write your best guess. Then collect only what tests it.',skills:['thinking','science']},review:{prompt:'Your team tracks forty numbers and still cannot explain a sales drop. What next?',right:'Make a guess about the drop, then collect what tests it',wrong:'Track more numbers until the answer shows up',why:'More facts make a bigger pile, but a guess shows which facts count.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You have a project on birds. You write down **sixty facts**: wingspans, colours, nests. Then you sit down to write, and you\u2019ve got **nothing to say**. What went wrong?'},{kind:'story',text:'Now start again with a question: why do some birds fly south in winter? At once you know what to look for. Food, cold, how far they go. Most of your sixty facts can stay in the notebook.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Facts are like **stones**. A heap of them is not a house. You need **a plan first**, and in a project that plan is your question.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your team tracks **forty numbers** about customers. At every review, someone asks why sales dipped. Someone else says you need more data. A year later, you\u2019ve got far more data and **the same question**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Poincar\xe9 compared this to building. Science is made of facts the way **a house** is made of stones. But **a heap** of stones isn\u2019t a house, and a pile of facts isn\u2019t a science. Something has to organise them.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Facts are endless, and most are worthless. The ones worth having **recur**: they tell you about more than themselves. Only a hypothesis tells you which facts will do that. So **the frame** comes before the data.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Run the same review two ways, **collecting first** or **framing first**.',left:{title:'Collecting first',items:['Gather whatever can be measured','Wait for a pattern','More data, same confusion']},right:{title:'Framing first',items:['Name what might explain the dip','Gather what would settle it','Fewer facts, each doing work']}},{kind:'moral',text:'When you\u2019ve gathered plenty and still can\u2019t decide, you\u2019re missing **a frame**, not more facts. Write the question first. Then collect **only what answers it**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture a perfect **heap of stones**, sorted and stacked, beside the plan of a house nobody has started. The heap grows every day. Will it ever **become the house**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Poincar\xe9\u2019s point was about **selection**. Facts are infinite, and most say nothing beyond themselves. The useful ones **recur**, so one observation predicts others. Deciding which facts will recur is already a guess about how the world works.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take a pupil with sixty bird facts and nothing to write. The facts aren\u2019t wrong. They just don\u2019t answer anything. Once she asks why birds fly south, a few facts matter and the rest can wait.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a team with forty customer numbers. Every review ends with a call for more data. When they finally ask whether the dip follows a price change, they know **exactly which two** numbers to pull.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Theory first has its own trap. A frame chosen **too early** decides what you notice, and can hide the fact that would have overturned it. When a field is young, open looking has its place.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Facts are the stones, but **a question** is the plan. Gather with one in mind, and hold it loosely enough to **change it** when you must.'}]},'science-and-hypothesis_3':{title:'The Answer That Fits Everything',card:{insight:'An idea that fits all results can\u2019t tell you what comes next.',tryThis:'Pick one idea you count on. Write down one result that would prove it wrong.',skills:['thinking','science']},review:{prompt:'Your boss explains every good or bad month by the weather. What is the problem?',right:'It fits every month, so it tells you nothing',wrong:'It is a strong idea, since it fits every month',why:'If an idea could fit any month, it takes no risk and tells you nothing.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Jo says her socks are **lucky**. Her team wins: the socks worked. Her team loses: the socks were tired. Her team draws: the socks were half lucky. Jo is **never wrong**. Is that good news for her socks?'},{kind:'explain',text:'An idea that fits **whatever happens** can\u2019t tell you anything ahead of time. So ask: what would **show it\u2019s wrong**? If Jo said the socks only work at home, an away win would sink her idea. Now you can test it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A good idea says what **should not** happen. If it fits everything, it says **nothing at all**. Ask what would prove yours wrong.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A manager explains every quarter by **market sentiment**. Good results mean positive sentiment, and bad ones mean negative. For three years the phrase has fitted **every result**. Why should that worry you?'},{kind:'story',text:'Poincar\xe9 saw this in the physics of his day. Scientists built models of light from hidden wheels, springs and fluids. Several matched the known results equally well. He noted that once something has one such explanation, it has **infinitely many**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'When many models fit, **the fit** can\u2019t choose between them. Worse, it flatters each one. A model that would fit any data has learned nothing. The real question is **what it rules out**: which result would finish it?'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Every explanation is either **a claim** that can fail or **a vocabulary** that can\u2019t.',left:{title:'A claim',items:['Names results that would sink it','Can fail','Predicts']},right:{title:'A vocabulary',items:['Absorbs every result','Cannot fail','Describes after the fact']}},{kind:'moral',text:'A theory earns its keep by **what it forbids**. When an explanation fits every result, it isn\u2019t strong, it\u2019s empty. Ask what **would prove yours wrong**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'In Poincar\xe9\u2019s day, physics had **too many theories** that worked. Models built of hidden wheels, springs and fluids each matched what was known. Most people would call that success. Poincar\xe9 saw **a warning**. Why?'},{kind:'explain',text:'His reasoning: if many models fit equally, the fit is evidence about **the models\u2019 flexibility**, not about nature. A hypothesis earns its place by **being at risk**. It should name results that, if they turned up, would finish it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take a manager who explains every quarter by market sentiment. Up, down or flat, the phrase fits. In three years it has never once said in advance what would happen. It isn\u2019t a theory. It\u2019s a **vocabulary**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now Jo and her lucky socks. Wins, losses and draws all prove the socks work. But suppose she says they only work at home. One away win would sink her. Her idea finally **says something**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Real science bends. When a result clashes with a theory, it\u2019s often fair to adjust a side assumption and keep the core. Where **rescue ends** and evasion begins is a judgement, not a rule.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A theory you can\u2019t lose is a theory that **tells you nothing**. So for every explanation you rely on, name **the result** that would finish it.'}]},'science-and-hypothesis_4':{title:'The Triangle on the Ball',card:{insight:'When two rules both work, ask which is handier, not which is true.',tryThis:'Find one argument about the right way today. Ask which way is handier.',skills:['thinking','science']},review:{prompt:'Two coworkers argue over whether meters or feet are true. What do you say?',right:'Neither is true, so ask which one is handier for the work',wrong:'Meters are the true way, since scientists use them',why:'No way to measure is true, but one can be more handy.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'At school you learn that a triangle\u2019s angles **add up to 180** degrees. Now draw a big triangle on **a beach ball**. Add up its angles. Is your school rule wrong?'},{kind:'explain',text:'On the ball, the angles add up to **more than 180**. Neither rule is a mistake. They\u2019re **two sets of rules** about shapes, and both work. Poincar\xe9 said asking which one is true is the wrong question. Ask which is handier.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Some questions sound deep but **don\u2019t make sense**. Asking which rules about shapes are true is like asking if **grams or ounces** are true. Pick the one that helps you.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'For two thousand years, Euclid\u2019s geometry was taken as **the truth** about space. Then mathematicians built other geometries, just as free of contradiction. Parallel lines behaved differently in them. So which one describes **your world**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Poincar\xe9\u2019s answer was that the question **has no meaning**. Experiment measures bodies: rods, light rays, the paths of things. It never measures **bare space**. So a strange measurement can always be read two ways.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture measuring a vast triangle with light rays, and finding its angles don\u2019t add to 180. You could say space is curved. Or you could keep Euclid and say **light bends**. The data fit both.'},{kind:'explain',text:'That doesn\u2019t make geometry a matter of taste. Experiment still judges **geometry and physics** together. Within that, picking a geometry is like picking the metric system over older measures. Neither is **truer**. One may be far handier.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When **two systems** fit the same facts, stop asking which is true. Ask which is **more convenient** for the work you\u2019re doing, and choose it on purpose.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Imagine asking whether **the metric system** is true and the old measures false. It sounds like an empty question. Poincar\xe9 thought a famous question about **space** was just as empty. Can you guess which one?'},{kind:'explain',text:'His argument rests on what experiment touches. It measures **bodies**, never space itself. Any result that seems to refute a geometry can be absorbed by **revising the physics** instead. So geometry works like a set of definitions.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take the light-ray triangle again. Its angles come out over 180 degrees. One physicist says space is curved. Another keeps Euclid and says the light bent on its way. Both accounts **predict the same** readings.'},{kind:'story',text:'Then came general relativity. Poincar\xe9 had expected Euclid to stay the handiest choice. Instead, a **curved geometry** became the convenient way to describe gravity. The answer changed, but arguably on **his terms**: convenience for the whole system.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Whether relativity proved him right is **still argued**. Einstein later granted the point in principle. But he held that in practice, geometry can be treated as something experiment tests. Poincar\xe9 also backed the wrong choice.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Poincar\xe9 didn\u2019t predict the physics. He described **the question\u2019s shape**. When you\u2019re asked which system is true, check whether **you\u2019re choosing** rather than finding.'}]},'science-and-hypothesis_5':{title:'The Step Onto the Bus',card:{insight:'Good ideas often pop up after hard work, but still need a check.',tryThis:'Write down one hunch you feel sure about today. Next to it, write how you will check it.',skills:['thinking','science']},review:{prompt:'After days stuck on a problem, the answer pops into your head. What now?',right:'Check it with care, because feeling sure is not proof',wrong:'Trust it, since a strong hunch is not often wrong',why:'Hard work brings the idea, but feeling sure is not proof.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re stuck on a hard puzzle for **days**. You give up and go out. Then, stepping onto **a bus**, the answer pops into your head. Where did it come from?'},{kind:'story',text:'That happened to Henri Poincar\xe9, a great French maths thinker. He felt sure at once. But feeling sure isn\u2019t proof. When he got home, he **worked it all out** on paper. Only then did he know.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Good ideas often start as **a guess**. The guess only comes after hard work, and then **you check it**. Hunch first, proof after.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Science lessons make discovery look like **a recipe**: question, method, result. Follow the steps and you\u2019ll reach the answer. But is that how **the big ideas** actually arrive?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Poincar\xe9 was **unusually frank** about this. Discovery involves guessing, he said. The guesses come from taste and long immersion, not from method. Method arrives afterwards, **to check them**. Teach only the checking, and science looks inhuman.'},{kind:'story',text:'In a later book, he described days of fruitless work on a maths problem. He broke off for a trip. As he stepped onto a bus, the solution arrived whole. He felt certain. He still checked it at home.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The flash sits between **two stretches** of graft, and **neither one** is optional.',left:{title:'Before',items:['Days of conscious effort','Nothing seems to work','Then a break']},right:{title:'After',items:['Careful checking','Certainty isn\u2019t evidence']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Your best ideas will often arrive as **a guess**. Do the hard work that earns it, then check it, because **feeling sure** isn\u2019t proof.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A mathematician works for days and gets **nowhere**. He leaves on a trip and forgets the problem. Then, stepping onto a bus, the answer arrives **complete**, with total certainty. What was his mind doing while he wasn\u2019t looking?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Poincar\xe9\u2019s guess, from **a later book**, was that the unconscious keeps combining possibilities. Something like **a sense of beauty** filters them. Only the ones with the right form reach awareness. So, he thought, the useful ideas are also the elegant ones.'},{kind:'story',text:'His own case was his work on a family of mathematical functions. The days of effort weren\u2019t wasted. They loaded the problem. The bus step only came because he\u2019d already **done the graft**, and he verified the answer afterwards.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture you, stuck on a work problem all week. On Saturday, mid-walk, the fix arrives and **feels obvious**. His account says enjoy it, then test it on Monday. The flash feels just as sure when it\u2019s wrong.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. This is one exceptional mind **looking inward**, and that\u2019s weak evidence about how thinking works. A mind that sorts by beauty is his hypothesis, not a finding. Flash stories also hide the flashes that were wrong.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The romantic moment is real, but it\u2019s **bracketed by discipline**. Hard work comes before the guess. **Checking** comes after, because your certainty isn\u2019t evidence.'}]},'science-and-hypothesis_6':{title:'The Smooth Curve',card:{insight:'A simple answer is a handy pick, not proof that it is true.',tryThis:'When you pick the simple answer, say out loud that you chose it.',skills:['thinking','science']},review:{prompt:'Two answers fit your facts. Someone says the neat one must be true. Your reply?',right:'We pick the simple one since it is easy to use',wrong:'Agreed, a neat answer is a strong sign it is true',why:'We pick simple rules to make work easy, not to find the truth.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'The biscuit tin is **empty**. Answer one: your brother ate them. Answer two: a mouse got in, a bird took some, and Gran had a few. Both could be true. Which do you **check first**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'You check **the simple one** first, because it\u2019s easier to check. That\u2019s a smart choice. But it\u2019s still **a choice**. Simple answers are sometimes wrong, and scientists have had simple rules that later needed fixing.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Picking the simple answer is **a good habit**. Just remember you picked it. That way you stay **ready to change** your mind.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Plot ten measurements on a graph. You draw **a smooth curve** through them and call the small misses error. It feels like reading the data. But are you reading it, or **deciding** something?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Poincar\xe9\u2019s point: **infinitely many curves** pass through any set of points. Choosing the smooth one is a decision, not a deduction. We prefer simple laws because complicated ones are **unusable**, not because they\u2019re shown false.'},{kind:'story',text:'History backs him up. Laws that looked simple have often been replaced by **messier ones**. Nature was never obliged to be tidy. The tidy version was simply the one scientists could **work with** at the time.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The same preference can be held as **a rule** or mistaken for **evidence**.',left:{title:'As a rule',items:['Chosen because it\u2019s usable','Stated as a choice','Revised without fuss']},right:{title:'As evidence',items:['Read as a sign of truth','Reported as a finding','Defended when data turn']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Keep preferring **the simpler explanation**. It\u2019s a good rule. But say you\u2019re **choosing it**, rather than reporting it as the one the evidence favoured.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A physicist finds a law so **elegant** it seems it must be right. Colleagues say the same. Nobody says so in print, but the beauty feels like **proof**. Is it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Poincar\xe9 said the preference for simplicity **can\u2019t be justified** by evidence. Through any set of points pass infinitely many curves. We pick the smooth one and blame the misses on error. That\u2019s a **decision**, made because complicated laws are unusable.'},{kind:'story',text:'The history of science is part of his case. Laws that looked simple were often replaced by more complicated ones. Nature, he says, is **not obliged** to be simple. The tidy law was the one people could use.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now your own graph. Ten points, a smooth line, a few misses put down to noise. You could draw a wiggly line that hits every point. You don\u2019t, because you **couldn\u2019t use** it to predict the eleventh.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some argue simplicity does carry weight: a model with fewer adjustable parts is **less able** to fit noise. And simple depends on notation. A law that\u2019s short in one language can be long in another.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Simplicity is **a precondition** of the work, not a result of it. Choose it freely, and say that **you\u2019re choosing**, so elegance never passes for evidence.'}]},'science-and-hypothesis_7':{title:'Rules That Define Words',card:{insight:'A rule that no test could ever break tells you what a word means.',tryThis:'Pick one rule you count on at work. Write what result could prove it wrong, if any.',skills:['thinking','science']},review:{prompt:'Your team finds that active users come back each week. Active means coming back weekly. So?',right:'The finding is just what active means, so it tests nothing',wrong:'It is strong proof that active users keep coming back',why:'If no result could prove it wrong, it just says what a word means.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your school says a pupil is **on time** if they arrive before the bell. Could that rule ever turn out to be **false**? Could anyone go out and test it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'No, because it just tells you what **on time** means. Science has rules like that too. A harder push makes a thing speed up more. But how do you measure a push? By how much it **sped up**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Some rules **can\u2019t be wrong**, because they set out what words mean. That\u2019s useful. Just don\u2019t treat them as if **a test** had proved them.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A team defines an engaged user as one who **returns within a week**. A year later, someone presents a finding: engaged users come back within a week. The room nods. Did anyone notice **the circle**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Poincar\xe9 found the same circle in mechanics. Newton\u2019s second law says force equals mass times acceleration. It looks like **a finding**. But we can\u2019t measure force or mass apart from that very relation. So the law defines what a force is.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Ask of any principle what **observation** could count against it. The answer **sorts it**.',left:{title:'A finding',items:['Something could count against it','Tested against the world','Usable as evidence']},right:{title:'A definition',items:['Nothing could count against it','Judged by usefulness','Organises the work']}},{kind:'explain',text:'That **isn\u2019t a scandal**. A definition that organises useful work is doing real work. It just isn\u2019t doing **empirical** work. Confuse the two, and you\u2019ll go looking for evidence where none is possible.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A statement can look like a discovery and be **an agreement** about words. For any principle you rely on, ask **what would count** against it. If nothing would, label it a definition.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Poincar\xe9 took the founding principles of mechanics **one at a time**. Of each he asked: is this from experiment, from logic, or **something else**? His answer unsettled readers. What do you think he found?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Several, he argued, were **neither**. They were definitions, so no experiment could refute them. The test is simple. Ask what observation would count against the principle. If nothing could, it\u2019s **part of the framework**, not a report on the world.'},{kind:'story',text:'His main case was the second law: force equals mass times acceleration. To test it, you\u2019d need to measure force and mass some other way. There isn\u2019t one. So nothing can catch the law out. It fixes what **a force** means.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now the product team. They define engagement as a weekly return, then a year later report that engaged users return weekly. The finding was the definition. Nobody lied. They just **mistook vocabulary** for discovery.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The test is cleaner on the page than in a lab. Whether a principle is a definition can depend on how **the rest** of the theory is set up. And calling a claim a definition can shield a real assumption.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A definition that organises good work is **worth keeping**. Just don\u2019t search for evidence it can\u2019t have. Ask of each principle you hold whether anything **could count against it**.'}]},'science-and-hypothesis_8':{title:'The Tiny Flick',card:{insight:'A lot of luck is a tiny cause you cannot see that grows big.',tryThis:'Pick one thing that keeps going wrong at random. Write where a small change might grow big.',skills:['thinking','science']},review:{prompt:'A machine at work fails at random times, and nobody knows why. Where do you look?',right:'For a small change that grows into a big problem',wrong:'Nowhere, since random failures have no cause to find',why:'Random often means a small cause you missed grew big.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You flip a coin. **Heads or tails?** You call it luck. But the coin just follows **the flick** of your thumb. So where does the luck come in?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Flick a **tiny bit** harder and the coin lands the other way up. The difference is far too small for you to feel. So you **can\u2019t predict** it, even though nothing about it is magic.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A lot of **luck** is just tiny causes you can\u2019t see. Some things turn **small differences** into big ones. That\u2019s what makes them hard to guess.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A roulette wheel is **pure mechanics**: a spin, a ball, some friction. Nothing in it happens without a cause. Yet nobody can predict the number. So where, exactly, is **the chance**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Poincar\xe9\u2019s answer: a difference in the spin **far too small** to notice changes where the ball lands. The system is fully determined. But the precision you\u2019d need is **beyond reach**, and the error grows instead of staying small.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Systems come in two kinds, by what they do with **a small error**. Watch **which kind** you\u2019re in.',left:{title:'Forgiving',items:['Small errors stay small','A rough measurement is enough','Forecasts hold']},right:{title:'Sensitive',items:['Small errors grow','No measurement is precise enough','Forecasts decay']}},{kind:'story',text:'Take an office printer that jams at random. Call it bad luck and the search stops. Poincar\xe9\u2019s view starts one. Somewhere, perhaps in damp paper or an uneven stack, a small difference is being **amplified**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Calling something **random** usually ends the search. Treat it as **a diagnosis** instead. Find where a small difference grows, and you may make the system less sensitive.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Poincar\xe9 studied **three bodies** pulling on each other by gravity. It sounds like a tidy sum. Instead, their paths tangled beyond any simple formula. What made three bodies **so hard**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The orbits were **extremely sensitive** to where the bodies started. A tiny starting error became an enormous one later. From that, he drew **a general account** of chance, set out fully in later work. Small unmeasured causes get amplified into effects you can\u2019t ignore.'},{kind:'story',text:'His examples were everyday. A cone balanced on its point must fall, but nobody can say which way. The weather turns on details no one measures. A roulette ball lands by **a spin** too small to notice.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now your own late train, or the printer that jams at random. Each is fully caused. You just can\u2019t see **the cause**, because the system magnifies something you never measured. That\u2019s a place to look, not a shrug.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Not all chance is hidden detail. Quantum physics, which came after him, seems to involve randomness that isn\u2019t. And some unpredictability comes from **many small causes** adding up, as in a gas. Poincar\xe9 discussed that kind too.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Chance, on this account, isn\u2019t **an absence of cause**. It\u2019s the gap between the precision **you have** and the precision the outcome needs.'}]},'science-and-hypothesis_9':{title:'Three Kinds of Guess',card:{insight:'Only a few guesses in a plan can sink it, so test those first.',tryThis:'Pick one plan you care about. Circle the guesses that could sink it, and test those first.',skills:['thinking','science']},review:{prompt:'A coworker says your sales plan should use weeks, not months. Your reply?',right:'Months is a free choice, so look at the risky guesses',wrong:'Switch to weeks so the plan becomes more accurate',why:'A free choice cannot sink a plan, so test the guesses that can.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your friend says your class trip plan is bad, because you wrote the times in the **24-hour clock**. Is that really **the problem**? Which part of a plan can actually go wrong?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Guesses come in **three kinds**. Safe ones: things very far away hardly matter here. Handy ones, like grams or ounces, which change nothing you can see. And **real claims**, like *the coach leaves at nine.* Only those can let you down.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Before you argue about a plan, sort its parts. **Handy choices** don\u2019t matter much. Check the **real claims**, because they\u2019re the ones that can fail.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A sales forecast assumes the shop opens on weekdays. It\u2019s laid out **in months**. And it assumes customers will pay **ten per cent more** without leaving. A critic attacks the months. Is that where you\u2019d aim?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Poincar\xe9 sorted hypotheses into **three kinds**. Some are natural, like ignoring very distant bodies. Some are **indifferent**: swap them and nothing you can observe changes. And some are real generalisations, which experiment can confirm or destroy.'},{kind:'story',text:'In the forecast, weekday opening is natural. Months versus weeks is **a free choice**. The price rise is the real generalisation, and the only one carrying **real risk**. The critic attacked the scaffolding. The forecast stands or falls on the price.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Mixing up the kinds goes wrong in **two directions**, and both waste **an argument**.',left:{title:'Category error',items:['Attacking a free choice as unproven','Demanding evidence for bookkeeping']},right:{title:'Evasion',items:['Calling a real claim mere convention','Shielding a risk from evidence']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Before you defend a model, **sort its assumptions**. Some you could change freely. The result depends on the others, so **test those**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Critics of a theory often aim at **the wrong part**. They demand proof for a choice that **couldn\u2019t be proved**, or let a risky claim hide. Why do arguments about theories so often miss?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Poincar\xe9\u2019s answer is that hypotheses **aren\u2019t one kind**. Natural ones we barely notice making. Indifferent ones are scaffolding: swap them and no prediction changes. Real generalisations can be confirmed or destroyed, and they alone **carry risk**.'},{kind:'story',text:'His natural example was that **very distant bodies** have a negligible effect. Nobody tests that before every experiment. His indifferent examples were two ways of picturing the same calculation. Choosing between them changes nothing anyone can observe.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now your forecast. Weekday opening is natural. Months or weeks is indifferent. Customers paying ten per cent more without leaving is the real claim. If a critic goes after the months, you can **point them** at the price.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The boundaries move. For decades many physicists treated **atoms** as a convenient picture, until experiment made them a real claim. And the sorting is itself a judgement, open to the same evasion it\u2019s meant to catch.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Know which of your assumptions are **load-bearing**, which are scaffolding, and which are just **your notation**. Then aim your tests at the first kind.'}]}}}},992,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'science-of-getting-rich',kind:'book',promise:'Keep the good advice and skip the rest',about:'What if a famous money book is only partly true? Wallace Wattles wrote it in 1910. He said you could think your way to being rich. He gave no proof. But some of his advice is good. Want to sort it out?',ideas:{'science-of-getting-rich_1':{title:'The Lucky Sock Test',card:{insight:'If no result could ever prove a plan wrong, then it is not a science.',tryThis:'Pick one plan you follow. Write down what result would show that it does not work.',skills:['thinking','science']},review:{prompt:'A course says anyone who fails just did not believe enough. What do you ask?',right:'What result would show this course does not work?',wrong:'How many people say the course changed their lives?',why:'If a plan blames you each time it fails, no one can test it.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Say you own a **lucky sock**. Your team wins, so the sock worked. Your team loses, so you didn\u2019t believe in it enough. Can the sock **ever be wrong**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'In 1910 Wallace Wattles wrote a book called *The Science of Getting Rich*. It works **like the sock**. Get rich, and the method worked. Stay poor, and you **didn\u2019t believe** hard enough. Nothing was ever tested.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A real science can **be proved wrong**. If no result could ever count against a method, it\u2019s **not a science**. It\u2019s a claim.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A sales course promises results to **anyone who follows it**. The people it fails are told they lacked commitment. The course is never at fault. What\u2019s going on with **a promise like that**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Wallace Wattles published his short book in 1910. He wrote within New Thought, a movement that taught the mind shapes material reality. He borrowed the word science for **the prestige** it carried. He borrowed none of its methods.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Look at how the book is built. It offers **total certainty** and a repeatable formula. When it fails you, the fault is yours: you doubted. So success proves the method, and failure proves **your lapse**. No result could count against it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Before you trust a method, ask what result **its author** would accept as proof it fails. If the answer is none, it\u2019s **a claim**, not a finding.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'The title promises **a science**. Open the book and you\u2019ll find no experiments, no measurements and no data. Yet the word has worked for over a century. What exactly has it **been doing**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Three parts carry the book: **certainty**, a repeatable formula, and failure blamed on **your belief**. The third keeps the other two safe. Every failure gets relabelled as the reader\u2019s lapse. So the method can be confirmed but never refuted.'},{kind:'story',text:'Wattles wrote in 1910, inside the New Thought movement. His central claim is that a clear picture of wealth, held with faith, draws wealth to you. He offers no evidence for it. If it doesn\u2019t work, you held the picture badly.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a wellness coach. She says her method heals anyone who trusts it fully. When someone relapses, she says it shows hidden doubt. It\u2019s the same shape as Wattles. Nothing that happens could ever count against her.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Calling the method unscientific doesn\u2019t make **every sentence false**. Some practical advice in the book is ordinary and sound. It just stands on its own grounds, not his. Don\u2019t throw it out with the title.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A method that explains away **every failure** can\u2019t be tested. Ask what result would count against it. If nothing would, treat it as **a claim**, not a science.'}]},'science-of-getting-rich_2':{title:'The Plan That Can\u2019t Fail',card:{insight:'A plan feels safe since it cannot fail, so take a real step today.',tryThis:'Write one thing you plan to do. Take its first real step before bedtime.',skills:['productivity','habits']},review:{prompt:'Sam has redone his business plan four times and has no customers. What next?',right:'Take the first real step with the plan he has',wrong:'Fix the plan once more so it is ready to launch',why:'A plan cannot fail until it is tried, so doing beats more planning.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You have a project due on Friday. On Monday you make a plan, and on Tuesday you make **a neater plan**. On Wednesday you pick new colours for it. It\u2019s Thursday night. **Have you started**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Planning feels like work, but it\u2019s **safe**. A plan can\u2019t go wrong, because nobody has tried it yet. Doing is scarier, because **you might fail**. So people keep on planning instead of starting.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Pick one clear thing you want to do. Then take **the first step** today, **where you are**. Getting ready forever isn\u2019t the same as starting.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two people want to start a newsletter. One spends **three months** on the plan. The other sends **a rough first issue** this week. A year from now, which of them has a good one?'},{kind:'story',text:'Sam wants to start a business. He has rewritten his plan four times. Each version is neater than the last. He hasn\u2019t yet spoken to a single customer. He feels busy. Nothing has met reality.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s the trap. Planning feels **productive**, and it carries no risk of visible failure. A plan never meets reality, so it can never be shown not to work. That\u2019s why it slides into **the place of acting**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Underneath Wattles\u2019s odd claims is **sound advice**. Form one specific intention, then act on it today, with the plan you have. You learn from **the first step**, not the fifth draft.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Most of this book says a picture held in the mind **draws wealth**. Buried under that is one line of advice **readers keep**. What part could survive when the theory around it doesn\u2019t?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The advice is about **decisiveness**. Act now, in your present circumstances, rather than waiting for better ones. Act on **the current plan**, rather than revising it again. Better conditions usually arrive because you acted, not before.'},{kind:'story',text:'Sam has rewritten his business plan four times. Each draft is tidier. He still hasn\u2019t spoken to a customer. The plan feels like progress, and it can\u2019t fail, because it\u2019s never been tried.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a woman who wants to learn guitar. She has watched twenty videos about which guitar to buy. She owns none. One cheap guitar and ten minutes today would teach her more than video twenty-one.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some work really does reward preparation. Acting today on a dangerous or **irreversible plan** isn\u2019t decisiveness. And you don\u2019t need the book\u2019s claim about thought shaping reality. The advice is correct without it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A plan can\u2019t fail, which is exactly **why it\u2019s comfortable**. Form one clear intention and **act on it** today. Only the step you take can teach you anything.'}]},'science-of-getting-rich_3':{title:'Make, Don\u2019t Fight',card:{insight:'Make what no one sells, and you will not have to fight for buyers.',tryThis:'Today, ask three people what they wish someone sold near them.',skills:['career','thinking']},review:{prompt:'Lots of designers bid on the same logo jobs as you. What is the better move?',right:'Make a thing people want that no one else makes',wrong:'Bid a little lower than all the others on each job',why:'Cutting prices starts a fight, but a new thing has no rival.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'At the school bake sale, **six tables** sell chocolate cake. Each one sells a little. It\u2019s a hot day. Which table do you think has **the longest queue**?'},{kind:'story',text:'The busiest table belongs to one kid selling **cold lemonade**. She isn\u2019t fighting the cakes for buyers. She\u2019s selling something nobody else brought. On a hot day, everyone wants it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You can fight for **a share** of what\u2019s already there. Or you can **make something new**. Making something new often means you don\u2019t have to fight.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You want to open a caf\xe9. The street already has **four**. Your plan is to be slightly cheaper than the cheapest. Every sale you make, someone else is **fighting to keep**. How does that end?'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Wattles split ambition into two minds: one **takes a share**, the other **makes something new**.',left:{title:'Competing',items:['Takes a share of what exists','Fights incumbents on their terms','Wins only what others lose']},right:{title:'Creating',items:['Makes what didn\u2019t exist','Has no rival yet','Wins without taking']}},{kind:'explain',text:'On strategy, he has a point. Fighting for an **established market** means meeting rivals where they\u2019re strongest. That\u2019s their customers, their prices, their scale. Making something new avoids that fight. It needs **nothing mystical** to work.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When a market feels **crowded**, stop asking how to take a slice. Ask what **isn\u2019t being made**. That\u2019s often where you\u2019ll find room.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Ten freelancers chase **the same job**. Each one cuts their price a little to win it. The winner earns less than last time. Is there **another game** they could be playing?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Wattles separates **two minds**. The competitive mind seeks a share of what already exists. The creative mind brings into being what didn\u2019t. He tells you to be a creator and **never a competitor**.'},{kind:'story',text:'A freelance designer is tired of bidding against dozens of others for logos. She notices local shops asking for menus nobody designs well. She starts there. For now, she has no rival.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a town with three gyms, all chasing the same members on price. A newcomer opens a class for older beginners, people none of the gyms served. Its members weren\u2019t taken from anyone.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Wattles rests this on a claim that **supply is unlimited**. That isn\u2019t true of most things people want. And creating takes capital, time or freedom to fail. He writes as if every reader has them.'},{kind:'moral',text:'His reason is wrong, but **the strategy holds**. If you can, don\u2019t fight for a slice. Ask what **nobody is making** yet.'}]},'science-of-getting-rich_4':{title:'Count What Works',card:{insight:'When you count what went well, you see the whole picture first.',tryThis:'Tonight, write down two things that went well today and why each one worked.',skills:['psychology','focus']},review:{prompt:'Your team missed one deadline but met three others. How do you start the review?',right:'List what went well and why, then look at the miss',wrong:'Spend the whole review on the missed deadline',why:'If you skip what worked, you might break it while you fix the miss.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your team lost on Saturday. All the way home, you think about **the goals** you let in. You replay every one. But what about **the rest** of the game?'},{kind:'story',text:'Your passing was the best it\u2019s been all year. If you forget that, you might change the very thing that was working. You\u2019d be fixing a game using only **half the facts**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Being thankful for what\u2019s **going well** won\u2019t bring good luck. But it shows you **the whole picture** of what you have, so you choose better.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A quarter ends. Sales fell in **one region**, and the whole review is spent on it. Nobody mentions that two other regions grew. What happens to **those two** next quarter?'},{kind:'story',text:'Next quarter, everything gets reorganised, including the regions that were working. Nobody asked why they grew. The fix for one problem quietly dismantled two successes. No one meant to break them.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Wattles gives gratitude **a chapter** and claims it draws more good to you. That part has no evidence. But the habit underneath is sound. Attention is limited. If yours always sits on the gap, you\u2019re deciding on **half the inventory**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Make counting **what works** a deliberate habit. Not because thanks attract anything, but because a choice made on **half the facts** is a worse choice.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two managers get the same report. One reads only the **red numbers**. The other reads the red and the green. Both are smart and both care. Which of them will **decide better**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Wattles presents gratitude as **a mechanism**. It brings your mind into harmony with a creative energy, he says. So it attracts more. That mechanism is unsupported. The practice isn\u2019t. It moves attention from **the gap** to the position.'},{kind:'story',text:'The first manager sees a falling region and reorganises everything. Two growing regions get broken in the shake-up. Nobody had looked at why they were growing. He decided on an incomplete inventory.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now someone with a small business. She lists three things that went well this week and why. One is a regular client she\u2019d planned to drop for a bigger one. She keeps both.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Later studies of gratitude suggest **modest benefit**, and the evidence is mixed. Nothing shows thanks attract wealth. And the habit fails if it becomes a reason **not to look** at what\u2019s wrong.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Counting what works isn\u2019t optimism. It **completes the record**. You\u2019re adopting an attention habit with plausible effects, not engaging **a law of nature**.'}]},'science-of-getting-rich_5':{title:'Spot the Old Claim',card:{insight:'Once you see an old claim in its plain form, you can spot it anywhere.',tryThis:'Next time you see a money tip, write its main claim in one line. Ask what would prove it wrong.',skills:['thinking','history']},review:{prompt:'A course mixes good budget tips with the idea that wishing makes you rich. What now?',right:'Find its main claim and ask what would prove it wrong',wrong:'Trust it all, since the budget tips make good sense',why:'Good tips can hide a weak claim, so find the main claim first.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Think of a cartoon character you love. Its newest version is shiny and detailed. Have you ever seen **its first drawing**? What might the **simple sketch** show you?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Wallace Wattles said an **unseen stuff** makes up all things. Hold a clear thought, and the stuff takes its shape. So picturing riches would make you rich. There\u2019s **no proof**. But many later books copy this claim.'},{kind:'moral',text:'We read this book to see **how it works**, not to follow it. Once you\u2019ve seen the **first plain version**, you can spot it inside every new one.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A podcast guest says wealth begins with **a clear vision**, held daily. Effort matters too, they add. Mindset is only part of it. It sounds measured. But what **exactly** are they claiming?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Wattles states the claim with **no hedges**. All things come from a thinking stuff. A thought held in it takes form. So wealth follows from thought. Every method in the book flows from that **one premise**, which has no evidence.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Later versions add caveats that make the claim **harder to pin down**. The original has none, so you can see the premise in plain view. Once you\u2019ve seen it bare, you\u2019ll **spot it** under the hedges.'},{kind:'moral',text:'This book belongs here for **its influence**, not its soundness. Read it as **an origin document**. Then find the central claim in any modern version, and ask what would disprove it.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Today\u2019s wealth books talk about **mindset**, vision and energy, always with caveats. They\u2019re hard to argue with. Where did their **central idea** first appear without any caveats at all?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Wattles posits **a thinking stuff** from which all things are made. Hold a clear picture of wealth, with faith and gratitude, and you impress it on the stuff. Then you receive it. That premise carries **every method** in the book.'},{kind:'story',text:'A course promises that aligning your energy with abundance builds wealth. It also teaches sensible budgeting. The budgeting is real. The tips make the course feel trustworthy, and the energy claim rides along with them.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now open Wattles. The same claim sits there with **no budgeting** around it. Thought impresses the stuff, and wealth follows. Seen bare, it\u2019s easy to recognise. You can then spot it inside the course.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Influence isn\u2019t soundness. A claim repeated for a century shows **its appeal**, not its truth. And the risk runs both ways: dismiss the whole genre, and you may miss **ordinary advice** it carries.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Studying the original isn\u2019t endorsing it. It shows **the premise** without disguise. Learn its shape here, and you\u2019ll recognise it **under the hedges** later.'}]},'science-of-getting-rich_6':{title:'Grow Where You Are',card:{insight:'You move up by doing the job you have now as well as you can.',tryThis:'Today, pick one plain task at work and do it as if your next boss could see.',skills:['career','habits']},review:{prompt:'A worker thinks her job is too small, so she does the least. What happens?',right:'The people who pick who moves up never see her best work',wrong:'Her boss will see her skill once a better job opens',why:'People judge you for the next job by how you do this one.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A hermit crab lives in **a borrowed shell**. One day it moves into a bigger one. But what has to happen **before it moves**? Do you think it moves first, or grows first?'},{kind:'story',text:'You get put on the B team. You could sulk and play half-hearted until the coach sees sense. Or you could play every B team game as hard as you can.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t wait for a better team before you **try your hardest**. Do your best **where you are now**. That\u2019s how you grow out of it.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re stuck in a job that feels **too small**. You\u2019ll do your real work, you tell yourself, once the right role comes. Until then, the minimum will do. Who\u2019s **watching** in the meantime?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Wattles calls this acting in **your present place**. Do what can be done now, with what\u2019s to hand, in the job you have. You move on by being **larger than your place**. You get larger by filling it completely.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s why it works. If you wait for the right position, the people deciding about it **never see** your best work. Your next role is judged on **what you do now**. The habit is the qualification.'},{kind:'moral',text:'It\u2019s the least exciting line in the book, and **the most useful**. It needs nothing mystical. Fill your present place to **the edges**, so you\u2019re ready for the next.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two people start in the same **junior role**. One treats it as a waiting room. The other treats it as **the job**. Five years on, they\u2019re in very different places. What made the gap?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Wattles argues against waiting for the right position **to begin**. You move on only by being larger than your present place. You become larger by doing **everything in it** fully. Better circumstances come after the action, not before.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture an analyst who feels her role is beneath her. She does the minimum until a promotion comes. It doesn\u2019t. The people who\u2019d decide never saw what she could do, because she never showed them.'},{kind:'story',text:'Wattles isn\u2019t alone here. Marcus Aurelius, the Roman emperor, kept urging himself to do the task in front of him well. Most serious accounts of how work gets done say the same. None of them need magic.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The book also says thought alone produces wealth. If that were true, **present action** would be pointless, and Wattles never squares the two. And filling a role well can\u2019t fix **unfair promotion** or a dead end.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Readers remember this chapter and **quietly drop** the doctrine. That\u2019s the right trade. Do your present work fully, because it\u2019s **the only evidence** anyone has for your next role.'}]},'science-of-getting-rich_7':{title:'Do Small Things Well',card:{insight:'Big work is made of small tasks, and each one is done well or badly.',tryThis:'Pick one small task you do each day, and do it with care today.',skills:['productivity','habits']},review:{prompt:'A writer does nothing for weeks, then works all night before the deadline. What helps more?',right:'Small tasks done well every day, not one big push',wrong:'A bigger push that is planned better next time',why:'Small tasks done well each day add up, and a late push leaves mistakes.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two kids are learning the piano. One plays for **ten careful minutes** every day. The other plays nothing all week, then **two hours** on Sunday. After a month, who do you think plays better?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Usually it\u2019s the **ten-minute** player. Big wins are made of small things done well, one after another. Wattles also said do all you can today, but **don\u2019t do tomorrow\u2019s** work too. Steady beats cramming.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Every small thing you do is done **well or badly**. None of them counts **for nothing**. Do the little ones properly, and they add up.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Think about **your average day**. How much of it is made of big moments? Most of it is emails, small tasks and routine jobs. So where does **your real output** come from?'},{kind:'story',text:'A writer does almost nothing for a month. Then, before the deadline, she writes for thirty hours straight. It feels heroic. The next week, she\u2019s exhausted and writes nothing at all.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Wattles says every act is either **efficient or not**. Most days are made entirely of small acts. So the quality of your **ordinary hours** is the quality of your work. He adds: do all you can today, but not tomorrow\u2019s work.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The writer\u2019s month and a steady month can hold **the same hours**, spent **very differently**.',left:{title:'The heroic sprint',items:['Long stretches of nothing','One huge push','Borrows from next week']},right:{title:'Ordinary hours',items:['Small acts, done well','Every day','A pace you can keep']}},{kind:'moral',text:'It sounds trivial, and **it isn\u2019t**. Your output is limited by your ordinary hours, not by **missing heroics**. Do the small acts well, every day.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture a bench of **small parts**, all made to one standard. One sits slightly out of line. In a row of a hundred, why does that **one part** stand out?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Wattles claims success isn\u2019t a few **great acts**. It\u2019s ordinary ones done in a successful way. Each act is efficient or inefficient, and an inefficient one is **a failure**, however small. Nothing in your day is neutral.'},{kind:'story',text:'A writer idles for a month, then works thirty hours straight before her deadline. The sprint feels like proof of effort. It also borrows from next week, which she spends recovering. Her month held very few good hours.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a nurse who checks every chart the same careful way each shift. No single check is impressive. Over a year, those small acts are the whole of her competence. You\u2019d trust her over a hero.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Wattles ties this to his doctrine that right thought draws wealth. That has **no evidence**. And not every act matters equally. Treat each one as pass or fail, and you\u2019ll slide into **perfectionism**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'This is **the book\u2019s best** passage. Most people\u2019s output is limited by the quality of **their ordinary hours**, not by a lack of extraordinary ones. Do today\u2019s small acts well.'}]},'science-of-getting-rich_8':{title:'Don\u2019t Look Away',card:{insight:'Hiding from a problem lets it grow, and then you cannot fix it.',tryThis:'Name one thing you have been putting off. Open it today and pick one next step.',skills:['thinking','psychology']},review:{prompt:'A friend stops opening her bank letters because money makes her nervous. What helps?',right:'Open them today and pick one next step',wrong:'Keep them shut until she feels calm about money',why:'Bills you do not look at keep growing and get harder to fix.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You get a note to take home from school. You\u2019re pretty sure it\u2019s **bad news**. So it stays at the bottom of your bag. What happens to the problem **while it waits** there?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Wallace Wattles told readers **not to think** about being poor. Don\u2019t read about it, he said, or talk about it, even about other people. He claimed thinking about a thing makes it happen. There\u2019s **no proof** of that.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Not looking at a problem doesn\u2019t **make it go away**. It just means you **can\u2019t help fix it**, whether it\u2019s yours or someone else\u2019s.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'There\u2019s a pile of **unopened letters** on your table. Some are from the bank. Opening them makes you anxious, so you\u2019ve stopped. Every week the pile grows. Is anything in it **getting smaller**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Wattles tells his readers to give no attention to poverty, illness or failure. Don\u2019t study them, discuss them or dwell on their causes, he says, even in other people\u2019s lives. His reason: attending to a thing brings it about.'},{kind:'explain',text:'There\u2019s no evidence for that, and the advice **does real harm**. It targets exactly what a problem needs to be fixed: the unpaid bill, the failing venture, the symptom. Obey it, and you learn of trouble **later, when it\u2019s larger**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'One narrow part survives, and the line between it and **the harm** is **worth drawing**.',left:{title:'Discipline',items:['Stop replaying your own setback','Learn from it, then work']},right:{title:'Looking away',items:['Ignore someone else\u2019s situation','Leave the letters shut']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Not dwelling on **your own setbacks** can be discipline. Refusing to look at a problem, or at **other people\u2019s lives**, is something else. Open the letters, and decide one next step.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A passenger on a bright train pulls the blind **most of the way** down. The street outside hasn\u2019t changed. Only **the view** has. What has the passenger actually achieved?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Wattles forbids attention to poverty, illness and failure, **including other people\u2019s**. He presents it as mental hygiene: what you attend to, you attract. There\u2019s **no evidence** for that. In practice, it\u2019s advice to stop noticing.'},{kind:'story',text:'A small business owner sees sales slipping. Following the book, she refuses to study the numbers or talk about it. Six months later the trouble is far larger. The information she avoided was the information she needed.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a comfortable reader who walks past a struggling neighbour. The book tells him not to dwell on it, for his own sake. A way of managing his own mood becomes a licence not to see someone else.'},{kind:'explain',text:'It also protects itself. If the method fails, the book has a reason ready: **you attended** to the wrong things. Every failure becomes the reader\u2019s lapse. That\u2019s **unfalsifiable and cruel** in the same move.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. A narrow version is worth keeping: once you\u2019ve learned from **your own setback**, don\u2019t replay it forever. That\u2019s discipline. It just doesn\u2019t stretch to ignoring a bill, a symptom or a neighbour.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Pulling the blind changes **the view**, not the street. Read this chapter as **an object lesson** in how a system shields itself from bad news. Then look plainly at what you\u2019ve been avoiding.'}]},'science-of-getting-rich_9':{title:'Move Your Own Pieces',card:{insight:'You can steer your own choices, but not what others decide.',tryThis:'Next time you push someone to choose, write down what you will do either way.',skills:['relationships','psychology']},review:{prompt:'Your brother keeps leaving a mess in the room you share. What works?',right:'Decide what you will do either way, then do it',wrong:'Keep arguing with him until he gives in',why:'You can steer what you do, but pushing others mostly fails.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'In chess, your hand can move **your own pieces**. It can\u2019t reach across and move the other player\u2019s. So what happens if you spend the game **wishing** they\u2019d move differently?'},{kind:'story',text:'Your friend wants to play a different game from you. You could nag until they give in, and they\u2019d sulk. Or you could decide what you\u2019ll do: play theirs first, or play yours with someone else.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Wallace Wattles wrote a book full of **claims with no proof**. But this rule is sensible. Use your willpower on **what you do**, not on making others choose.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You want a client to sign. You send reminders, rework the pitch, and **picture the call** where they agree. Weeks go by. Where has **all that effort** actually gone?'},{kind:'story',text:'The client\u2019s decision was always theirs. Every reminder and every rewrite went into something you don\u2019t control. Meanwhile, your own next move sat undecided: chase other clients, or wait one more month.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Wattles forbids using **will power** on other people or outside things. He says it\u2019s wrong and doesn\u2019t work. Given where the genre went, that\u2019s **a notable limit**. It\u2019s the one part of his system a person can actually do.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Effort on **your own conduct** is effort you control. Pressure on others mostly fails. So when you catch yourself pushing someone to decide, **decide what you\u2019ll do** either way.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A book promising wealth through **the power of thought** has a whole chapter on will. You\u2019d expect it to teach you to bend the world. What does it **actually say**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Apply will **only to yourself**, Wattles says: to your own attention and conduct. Never to other people or outside things. His reasons are metaphysical. The conclusion is **sensible anyway**, because their decisions are theirs.'},{kind:'story',text:'A parent wants his adult son to take a steady job. He argues the case at every dinner. The son digs in harder each time. The father has spent a year on a decision that was never his to make.'},{kind:'story',text:'A freelancer waits weeks for a client to sign, sending reminder after reminder. Then she switches questions. Instead of how to make them agree, she asks what she\u2019ll do either way. She sets a date and moves on.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The rule sits oddly in a book whose central claim is that thought shapes the world. That\u2019s willing outside things **rather directly**. And some goals rightly need you to persuade others. Confined to yourself, effort can become **passivity**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Of everything in the book, this is the part that matches **something you can do**. Move your own pieces with care. Leave the other player\u2019s **moves to them**.'}]}}}},993,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'self-help-smiles',kind:'book',promise:'Keep what works from the first self-help book',about:'What if the first self-help book left half the story out? In 1859, a Scottish writer named Samuel Smiles wrote true stories of people who worked hard and won. Some of his tips still work. Some do not. Want to see which ones?',ideas:{'self-help-smiles_1':{title:'The Ones Who Lost',card:{insight:'A pile of winners cannot show you what made them win.',tryThis:'Next time a success story makes you want a plan, stop. Ask how many people tried it and lost.',skills:['thinking','history']},review:{prompt:'A talk has ten speakers who quit safe jobs and won. What do you ask?',right:'How many people quit safe jobs and are not on stage?',wrong:'Which of the ten speakers took the biggest risk?',why:'Winners show what they did, not how often the same bet failed.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your school puts up a photo of every **spelling bee** winner. Each one says they practised every night. So does practice **make you win**? What would you need to know?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Lots of kids practised every night and **still lost**. Their photos aren\u2019t on the wall. In 1859 Samuel Smiles wrote a book of **true life stories** about people who kept trying and won. He never counted the ones who lost.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A wall of winners **can\u2019t tell you** what made them win. For that, you need the ones who **tried and lost** too.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re thinking of leaving a steady job to start something. You read **five founder memoirs**. Every author bet everything and **kept going**. By the fifth, the case feels closed. Should it?'},{kind:'story',text:'Smiles built *Self-Help* from hundreds of short lives: engineers, potters, surgeons, the self-taught. Each person persists through obscurity and is rewarded. Read in a run, the lives start to feel like a law. Nobody in the book persisted and lost.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Every memoir on your shelf is **perfectly true**. The trouble is **what\u2019s missing** from it.',left:{title:'On the shelf',items:['Bet everything, and won','Wrote a memoir','Easy to find']},right:{title:'Off the shelf',items:['Bet everything, and lost','Wrote nothing','Impossible to count']}},{kind:'explain',text:'This is **survivorship bias**: judging by the cases that came through a filter and forgetting the filter. The missing number is **the base rate**. Of everyone who quit a safe job to build something, how many made it?'},{kind:'moral',text:'A collection of winners shows **what they did**. It can\u2019t show **what made them win**, because the people who did the same and failed aren\u2019t in it.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Read *Self-Help* in one sitting and something odd happens. Life after life, somebody persists and wins. By the last chapter it feels like **a law of nature**. But **what kind of proof** is a pile of lives?'},{kind:'story',text:'Smiles gathered hundreds of short lives: engineers, potters, surgeons, the self-taught. Each one keeps going through years of obscurity. Each one is rewarded in the end. The book grew from talks he gave to young working men in Leeds.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Each life is true. The problem is **the filter**. Failure rarely produces a biography, so the people who persisted and lost **left no record**. Another success story adds nothing on that point. It only makes you feel surer.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a survey of a hundred people who lived past 100. Most drank coffee daily. It sounds like a finding. But you\u2019d need the coffee drinkers who died at seventy, and they weren\u2019t asked.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The critique shows the book **can\u2019t prove** persistence works. It doesn\u2019t show persistence is useless. And it can turn into a lazy reflex that dismisses every success story unheard. Ask how big **the failure pile** is.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Read Smiles as **testimony**, not evidence. He shows what successful people believed mattered. Before a pile of winners convinces you, ask **who isn\u2019t in it**.'}]},'self-help-smiles_2':{title:'The Fourth Try',card:{insight:'You cannot pick your talent, but you can pick how long you keep at it.',tryThis:'Pick something that failed three times. Change one step and try once more.',skills:['habits','career']},review:{prompt:'Your sister fails math twice. She says she is bad at it. What should she do?',right:'Change one step and try the class once more',wrong:'Accept it and pick work that fits her talent',why:'Two fails tell you little, so change one step and go again.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two kids learn to juggle. One gets it on **day one**, then gets bored. The other drops the balls all the time but **practises every day**. Who\u2019s better after a month?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Samuel Smiles wrote a book of real lives in 1859. His inventors aren\u2019t shown as geniuses. They\u2019re shown failing, failing again, and **keeping going**. You can\u2019t choose how clever you are. You can **choose to keep** trying.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Plain hard work, kept up, often **beats being clever** now and then. But it\u2019s **not a promise**. Some people try hard and still lose.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve failed the same driving test **three times**. Your friend passed first go. It\u2019s tempting to decide you just **don\u2019t have it**. Is three failures a verdict, or just a normal start?'},{kind:'story',text:'*Self-Help* grew from talks Smiles gave to young working men in Leeds. They were teaching themselves in the evenings, with little schooling. He wasn\u2019t speaking to the gifted. He wanted them to believe their effort would count.'},{kind:'explain',text:'So his engineers **aren\u2019t shown as clever**. They\u2019re people who went again after the third failure, and the fourth. One plodder beating a gifted rival is an anecdote. Hundreds, across **trades and centuries**, are harder to wave away.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Every case is **persistence that worked**, so the honest claim is **narrower**.',left:{title:'His claim',items:['Application beats brilliance','Failure means too little effort']},right:{title:'What survives',items:['Application is necessary','Not enough on its own']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Persistence is needed, but it\u2019s **not enough** on its own. Of talent and application, **only application** is yours to raise. So a fourth try, done differently, comes before any verdict.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture the engineers in a Victorian bestseller. You might expect **flashes of genius**. Instead, Smiles shows men who failed, and failed again, and **went back** after the fourth time. Why would he leave the genius out?'},{kind:'story',text:'The book began as talks to young working men in Leeds, teaching themselves at night. They had little schooling. A book about genius would have offered them nothing. A book about application gave them a reason to begin.'},{kind:'explain',text:'His claim is that **ordinary ability**, applied steadily, beats brilliance applied in fits. The strength is accumulation. The weakness is **selection**, and it\u2019s severe. Every case is persistence that worked, drawn from a crowd that also held **persistence that failed**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take someone who\u2019s tried learning Spanish twice and quit both times. He decides he has no ear for languages. The honest version of Smiles says go again before deciding. Two attempts tell you almost nothing about aptitude.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Persistence in the **wrong direction** is just waste, and the book says little about when to stop. And talent versus application is too clean. Steady practice is often **how ability grows**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You can\u2019t choose your talent. You can choose **your application**, and it\u2019s the one input you can raise. Just don\u2019t mistake it for **a guarantee**.'}]},'self-help-smiles_3':{title:'The Extra Five Dollars',card:{insight:'Each small honest choice builds the habit you will need for big ones.',tryThis:'Name the smallest thing you always let slide. Then do it well today.',skills:['habits','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'A cashier hands you $5 too much, and no one would know. What do you do?',right:'Hand it back, since small choices build the habit for big ones',wrong:'Keep it, since only big choices show who you are',why:'Small choices build the habit, so they decide the big ones too.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re about to leave the room. A drawer is **open a little**. Nobody will notice, and nobody will care. You go back and **shut it anyway**. Why might that tiny move matter?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Walk across a field once and the grass springs back. Walk the same way every day and **a path forms**. Samuel Smiles thought character grows the same way. It\u2019s built from **small things**: honesty, being on time, finishing jobs.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Each time you do the small right thing, it **gets easier**. Then, when a big choice comes along, **you\u2019re ready** without thinking.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A shop hands you **20p too much** in change. Pointing it out feels silly. It\u2019s 20p. But what are you **practising** when you pocket it, or when you don\u2019t?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Smiles argues that character is built from **small refusals**, not large decisions. Honesty in trivial matters. Punctuality. Finishing what you started. Each one is easy and produces almost nothing alone. What builds up is **a disposition**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'So the 20p isn\u2019t neutral. Keeping it quiet is **a rehearsal** of letting things slide. You\u2019re building a habit either way. The only choice is which one, and it\u2019s made in moments that feel **too small to count**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Big moments don\u2019t build your character. They **reveal** what the small ones built. So attend to **the small cases**, because the large ones will be settled there.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Which builds more character: one dramatic act of courage, or **a thousand refusals** of a tiny temptation? Most of us would pick the drama. Smiles **bets on the thousand**. Why?'},{kind:'explain',text:'His answer is cumulative. Each refusal is trivially easy and does almost nothing alone. What builds up is **a disposition** that works **without deliberation**. By the time something big arrives, your answer has largely been built in advance.'},{kind:'story',text:'This is an old argument. Aristotle held in the *Nicomachean Ethics* that we become just by doing just acts. Smiles restated it for Victorian readers, with clerks, apprentices and mechanics who were honest in trivial things.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a manager who rounds his expense claims up by a few pounds each month. He says it hardly matters. In money, he\u2019s right. But every month he\u2019s practising **the small dishonesty**, and getting better at it.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The idea turns harsh easily. Victorians readily read small lapses as proof of bad character, especially **in the poor**. A habit built in calm can still give way under fear or need. And punctuality is partly **just a convention**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your character isn\u2019t decided in the big moments. It **builds up** in small ones you barely notice. Pick **your small refusals** with care, and be slower to judge other people\u2019s.'}]},'self-help-smiles_4':{title:'What Else Was True',card:{insight:'Every win is part hard work and part luck or help.',tryThis:'Next time you read a success story, believe the work. Then write down what else helped.',skills:['thinking','history']},review:{prompt:'A friend out of work for a year is told to try harder. What is missing?',right:'What else is in his way besides effort',wrong:'Proof that he tried as hard as he says',why:'Hard work counts, but so do luck and help.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two girls run the same race. One has **good trainers** and a dad who takes her to the track every week. The other runs in **school shoes**. The first one wins. Everyone says she tried harder. Would you agree?'},{kind:'explain',text:'In 1859 Samuel Smiles wrote a book of true stories about people who worked hard and did well. Hard times are in it, but **they\u2019re always beaten**. Nobody in the book is **stopped for good**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When someone wins, **hard work** is part of it. Luck and the life they were born into are part too. So when you hear a winner\u2019s story, believe the effort, then ask **what else** helped.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A magazine profile of a founder dwells on the **dawn starts** and the rejections. One sentence near the end mentions **a family loan** that covered the first year. Which part do you remember?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Smiles\u2019s lives leave out **luck and circumstance**. That isn\u2019t simple forgetting. His method **couldn\u2019t see it**. He collected people who got through, so every obstacle in the book is one that was overcome. Obstacles that won were never written up.'},{kind:'explain',text:'He also wrote for and about people for whom effort **really did** turn into advancement. For them, the book was largely true. Stretched into a law, it reads anyone else\u2019s failure as **a flaw of character**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Back to the profile. The rejections are **effort**. The loan is **circumstance**, and it may be most of the difference.',left:{title:'In the story',items:['Dawn starts','Rejections','Refusing to quit']},right:{title:'Worth asking',items:['Who paid the rent?','Who opened doors?','What was the timing?']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Believe **the effort** in a success story you read. Then ask **what else was true**: money, timing, health, who was already in the room. Effort counts, but it\u2019s never the only thing.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'In a book of several hundred success stories, how often would you expect **circumstance** to decide an outcome? Count them in *Self-Help* and you\u2019ll find a **strange number**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The book\u2019s central omission is **luck**, and it\u2019s structural. A collection of successes can\u2019t show circumstance deciding anything. Every single obstacle in it was beaten, or there\u2019d simply be **no biography**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Think of who Smiles was writing about. Engineers and tradesmen in a period when, for many of them, effort **really did** turn into advancement. For them, the book was largely true. The trouble came when it was stretched into a law.'},{kind:'explain',text:'As a law, it reads failure as **too little effort**. That was politically convenient. It put the cause of poverty in the poor, and its remedy there too. That\u2019s the reading the book **lends itself to**, and it\u2019s false.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a girl who wins a chess contest. She trains hard every day. Her grandad is also a chess coach who plays her every evening. Both facts are true. Smiles would tell you about the training.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The correction can overshoot. Saying luck decides everything is as untestable as saying effort does. It also throws away **the one input** you control. Smiles is right that effort matters. He\u2019s wrong that it\u2019s **the whole story**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Read success stories with **the missing people** in mind. Then a moral claim becomes a useful one: effort is **the part you control**, not the whole cause.'}]},'self-help-smiles_5':{title:'Gears Under the Cover',card:{insight:'Take the good tool from an old book, and leave its old lesson behind.',tryThis:'Write down one plain step from the last advice book you read. Test that, not the famous names.',skills:['thinking','habits']},review:{prompt:'A book on waking early is full of famous early risers. How do you read it?',right:'Pull out one plain step and test it yourself',wrong:'Copy what the most famous one does each day',why:'The plain step is worth a test, but famous names prove very little.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Some old clocks have a **fancy carved cover**. Lift it off and you\u2019ll find **plain gears** inside. The cover is the bit everyone admires. Which part do you think keeps the time?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Samuel Smiles wrote *Self-Help* in 1859. Parts of it are **out of date**. He thought people who stayed poor hadn\u2019t tried hard enough, and that\u2019s wrong. Underneath is something useful: **plain practice**, done again and again, makes you better.'},{kind:'moral',text:'An old book can hide **a useful idea** under an old-fashioned lesson. Lift off the old lesson, and **keep the gears** that still work.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re halfway through a business bestseller. Chapter one was **a founder** rejected again and again. Chapter two was an athlete training before dawn. By chapter ten you\u2019re convinced. But **convinced of what**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'That format goes back to *Self-Help* in 1859. Smiles built an argument from **hundreds of concrete cases**, in ordinary language, for readers with no formal education. It was genuinely new. It\u2019s why the book sold enormously and **founded a genre**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Its **morals** have aged badly. Its **method** hasn\u2019t, so it\u2019s worth separating the two.',left:{title:'The moral',items:['Failure means too little effort','Victorian improvement']},right:{title:'The method',items:['Many concrete cases','Deliberate, plain repetition']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Reading it this way teaches **a useful skill**. The book feels overwhelming as evidence and proves very little. Learn to feel both at once, **persuaded and unproven**. Then carry that into every book that copied its structure.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t judge an advice book by **its parade of names**. Strip off the dated moral and **test the method** underneath. That\u2019s usually the only part worth keeping.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Why read a Victorian self-help book whose morals are **dated** and, in places, wrong? Because it\u2019s **the first specimen** of a format you\u2019ve met a hundred times since. What can the original teach you?'},{kind:'story',text:'Smiles\u2019s technique was new in 1859. He built an argument from hundreds of concrete cases, in plain language, aimed at readers with no formal education. The book sold enormously. It also set the pattern for a whole genre.'},{kind:'explain',text:'What does that much evidence prove? **Very little**. Selected cases show what the successful did and believed, not what caused their success. The book is a clear specimen of a genre that **stops checking** its own arithmetic.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now pick up a modern business book. Chapter after chapter of chosen success, usually with less range than Smiles. The selection problem is the same, and it\u2019s still unaddressed. The structure is his, almost exactly.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Method and moral don\u2019t always separate cleanly. Choosing only successes is what makes failure **look like a vice**. So the method carries some of the moral inside it. Strip the frame, then check **what came off** with it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Read *Self-Help* as a study in method. Notice how **persuasive** piled-up stories feel, and how **little they prove**. Then take the plain repetition and leave the moral.'}]},'self-help-smiles_6':{title:'Two Kinds of Help',card:{insight:'Doing it yourself builds skill, but people in need still need help.',tryThis:'Before you say someone must do it alone, stop. Ask if they need a hint or real help.',skills:['leadership','relationships']},review:{prompt:'A city council member uses Smiles to fight free school lunches. What does this idea say?',right:'Kids need food, and being fed does not stop learning',wrong:'Free lunches will keep kids from learning to help themselves',why:'Doing it yourself builds skill, but hunger is not a skill to learn.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your big brother does your **maths homework** for you. It\u2019s right, and it\u2019s done fast. Then next week there\u2019s **a test**. Who\u2019s going to sit it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Samuel Smiles said **help you give yourself** makes you strong. Help from others, he said, often makes you weak. For learning, he had a point. But some people used his idea to say **poor families** shouldn\u2019t get help.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Doing a thing yourself is **how you learn it**. But a hungry family needs food, not a lesson. **Help with need** doesn\u2019t stop anyone learning.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A new hire asks you to **just fix** their bug. You could do it in five minutes. Later, a neighbour tells you they\u2019ve lost their job and **can\u2019t pay rent**. Does the same rule apply to both?'},{kind:'explain',text:'*Self-Help* opens with its doctrine. Help you give yourself **builds you up**, Smiles says. Help from outside often **weakens you**. As a point about skill, it holds. A problem solved for you leaves you unable to solve the next one.'},{kind:'explain',text:'But Smiles stretched it into politics: laws and outside help **do little** for people. His readers turned that against **relieving poverty**. It doesn\u2019t follow. Being fed isn\u2019t a skill, and food stops nobody practising anything.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'So the new hire and the neighbour need **different things**, and only one needs **hints**.',left:{title:'Learning a skill',items:['Let them try first','Give hints, not answers']},right:{title:'Real need',items:['Food, rent, safety','Help is just help']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Help from within builds **capability**. That\u2019s a useful truth about learning. It\u2019s **no argument** against helping people in need, so don\u2019t let one claim smuggle in the other.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'The book takes its name from one doctrine. Help from within builds you up, Smiles says, and help from without **wears you down**. Half of it is true about learning. What happens when you **use it for everything**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Two claims travel as one. The first is about **how capability forms**. Solving a problem builds the skill, and the skill is worth more than the answer. Good teachers, managers and parents **work on this** every day.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a parent whose child asks for help with a puzzle. Solving it for her is quick. Giving a hint is slower, and she solves the next one alone. That\u2019s the half of Smiles that holds.'},{kind:'story',text:'The second claim is political. Smiles argues that laws and institutions do little to raise people. National progress, he says, is the sum of individual effort. In the nineteenth century, his readers used this against relieving the poor.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The second claim **doesn\u2019t follow** from the first. A skill is built by practising it. Having enough to eat isn\u2019t a skill, and food stops nobody practising. If anything, need **eats the time** that learning takes.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Even the capability claim has edges. Help that\u2019s never offered isn\u2019t self-reliance but **neglect**. Learners need models, scaffolding and feedback, all from without. The question is **the kind of help**, never whether to give any.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Keep the half that holds: let people **solve their own** problems when skill is the point. Don\u2019t let it carry **the other half** in. Need isn\u2019t a lesson.'}]},'self-help-smiles_7':{title:'Who Did the Work',card:{insight:'The boss often gets the credit for work that others did.',tryThis:'When you tell how something got done, stop. Name the people who did the work.',skills:['leadership','history']},review:{prompt:'Your team\u2019s project wins an award, and only the boss is named. What is missing?',right:'The names of the people who did the work',wrong:'Nothing, since the person in charge speaks for the team',why:'Credit belongs with the people who did the work, not just the boss.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Think of a **school play**. Who gets the clapping at the end? Now think about who built the set and **ran the lights**. Did you clap for them?'},{kind:'story',text:'George Stephenson grew up poor in the north of England. He worked the engines at a coal mine. At eighteen he **still couldn\u2019t read**, so he paid for lessons at night. Later he built some of the first railways.'},{kind:'moral',text:'In 1859, history books were mostly about **kings and lords**. Samuel Smiles said working people like Stephenson **changed Britain** more. When you hand out credit, look for who did the work.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture a Victorian history shelf: **kings, lords and generals**. Then Smiles fills a book with a potter, an instrument maker and a coal-pit engineman. What\u2019s he **arguing** by choosing them?'},{kind:'story',text:'His cast is the argument. Watt the instrument maker. Wedgwood the potter. George Stephenson, who tended engines at coal pits and couldn\u2019t read at eighteen. He paid for night school, then built some of the first public railways.'},{kind:'explain',text:'This part has **held up well**. Many key industrial advances came from people who made things by hand, not men of theory. Often they had **little schooling**. On who did the work, he beat the histories of his day.'},{kind:'explain',text:'He drew a further lesson, and it\u2019s weaker. He implied the same road was **still open** to his readers. But what let a self-taught mechanic remake a trade in 1780 wasn\u2019t what a young worker faced in 1859. **The door had narrowed**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Smiles\u2019s choice of subjects **was the argument**: tradesmen, not gentlemen, remade Britain. When you tell how anything got done, check whether you\u2019re naming **the doers** or just the people present.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'In 1859, who did British history say had made the country? Mostly **men of rank**: kings, lords, generals. Samuel Smiles wrote a book that **argued back**. You can tell from the people he chose.'},{kind:'story',text:'His pages are full of potters, instrument makers, millwrights and enginemen. Stephenson tended steam engines at coal pits in the north-east and couldn\u2019t read at eighteen. He paid for night school. He went on to build early public railways.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The choice is **polemical**. Smiles argues that working men of ordinary origin, not their patrons, transformed the country. The record broadly supports him. Major advances clustered among **practitioners rather than theorists**, many with little formal schooling.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a company history that credits its success to three founders. An unnamed team of engineers designed the product. The founders were present, and they matter. But the history repeats the mistake Smiles attacked: crediting rank over work.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Smiles offered old openings to new readers **without saying so**. Conditions around 1780 weren\u2019t those of 1859. And his cast is a selection too: those who reached the record, **not every artisan** who helped.'},{kind:'moral',text:'On who did the work, Smiles has **largely held up**. On whether the road stayed open, he hasn\u2019t. Next time you credit a success, **look past the title** to the hands.'}]},'self-help-smiles_8':{title:'The Burned Furniture',card:{insight:'Most wins come after many fails, but a fail is no sign you will win.',tryThis:'Before you start a hard goal, write how many tries you will give it.',skills:['habits','career']},review:{prompt:'A writer gets two books turned down and calls it hopeless. What does this idea add?',right:'Two fails is a normal start, so set a number of tries',wrong:'Two fails are a clear sign to give up now',why:'Most wins come after fails, so decide on a number of tries first.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Bernard Palissy was a French potter in the 1500s. He wanted a **shiny white glaze** for his pots. He tried for years and kept failing. Then his oven ran short of fuel. What did he **burn**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Smiles tells how Palissy fed **his own furniture** into the fire to keep it hot. The white glaze is the famous part. The years of failing are most of the story, but you rarely hear them.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Failing is **the normal way** to get somewhere. It isn\u2019t a sign you\u2019re bad at it. Most famous wins come after **many fails** nobody hears about.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A colleague seems to have succeeded fast: a new role, a launch, an award. Your own progress feels **painfully slow** by comparison. What if her record is missing **the years before**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The public record shows the outcome and **squeezes the years** before it into a sentence. So every success looks faster than it was. And your own slow progress looks **abnormal**, when it\u2019s actually the usual shape.'},{kind:'story',text:'Smiles puts the missing years back, case after case: bankruptcies, rejected patents, decades of work that came to nothing. Palissy, a sixteenth-century French potter, burned his own furniture to keep his kiln going. The glaze came last.'},{kind:'explain',text:'There\u2019s **one catch**. Every failure in the book was followed by success, or there\u2019d be no biography. Failure that stayed failure is absent. So the book shows failure **comes before** success, not that it predicts it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Failure is **the ordinary route**, not the exception. But it\u2019s no promise. So set **a budget of attempts** before you start, and stop when you\u2019ve spent it.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Smiles held that we learn **more wisdom** from failure than from success. Is that comfort for people who lose, or a description of **how things go**? His biographies suggest the second.'},{kind:'story',text:'Bernard Palissy, a sixteenth-century French potter, spent years chasing a white enamel glaze. Smiles tells how, out of fuel, he **fed his kiln** with his own furniture. The glaze is the famous part. The years are most of the story.'},{kind:'explain',text:'In nearly every life, the achievement is **the last item** on a long list of failed attempts. The record hides that list. Smiles restores it, case after case, until you see **the pattern**: success rarely came without it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a founder pitching investors. Every great company failed many times first, he says. We\u2019ve failed many times, so we\u2019re on track. But stories like Smiles\u2019s only contain failures that ended well. His logic runs **backwards**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Taken as a promise, the idea becomes a reason **never to stop**. Some failures are information: the design is wrong, or nobody wants the thing. So you need **a budget**, set in advance.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Failure is the usual road, and the record hides it. But it **precedes success** without predicting it. Decide how many attempts you\u2019ll fund, and what result would **tell you to stop**.'}]},'self-help-smiles_9':{title:'The Front Desk Test',card:{insight:'How you treat someone who can do nothing for you shows who you are.',tryThis:'Today, watch how you treat the person who can do the least for you.',skills:['relationships','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'A new coworker charms the boss but ignores the janitor. Which shows more?',right:'How she treats the person with the least power',wrong:'How she treats the boss who can help her',why:'Being kind when it cannot pay off shows who you are.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Back in Victorian times, who got called **a gentleman**? A rich lord? A poor worker? Most people would\u2019ve said the lord, without even thinking. One writer **disagreed**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Samuel Smiles said a gentleman isn\u2019t someone **born rich**. It\u2019s someone who\u2019s honest and kind, even to people who can do nothing for them. So a poor worker could be one. A lord could **fail to be** one.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Watch how someone treats the dinner staff, or **the new kid** nobody talks to. That\u2019s where you see **what they\u2019re like**, and where others see you.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A candidate is charming to the **interview panel**. On the way out, he snaps at the receptionist and walks past the cleaner like she\u2019s furniture. Which do you think **tells the panel more**: that, or his answers?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The last chapter of *Self-Help* takes **the most class-bound** word in English. It cuts the word loose from birth, money and manners. A gentleman, Smiles says, is **a way of behaving**: truthful, considerate, never taking unfair advantage.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Why is the receptionist the real test? Courtesy to someone who can promote you **proves little**, because it pays. Courtesy to someone who can do nothing for you has **no motive** but the conduct itself. So it\u2019s clear evidence.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A gentleman, for Smiles, is defined by **conduct, not rank**. It\u2019s open to a labourer and can be missing in a lord. Watch how you treat **the least powerful** person in the room.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Some words seem welded to **rank**. In Victorian Britain, gentleman was one of them: a matter of birth and money. What happens when a writer tries to **pry the word loose**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Smiles\u2019s final chapter defines the gentleman by **conduct**: truthfulness, consideration, self-respect and refusing to take unfair advantage. Above all, it\u2019s how he treats people who can do **nothing for him**. Rank doesn\u2019t come into it.'},{kind:'story',text:'His examples fit the argument. Working men who behaved well under pressure sit beside men of rank who didn\u2019t. His readers were artisans and clerks, shut out of the rank for good. Now the standing it named was **within their reach**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture two equally strong job candidates. Both are polite to the panel. One is curt with the receptionist on the way out. By Smiles\u2019s standard, that one moment tells you more than **the whole interview**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The content of the conduct has dated: Victorian **respectability** isn\u2019t quite our ideal. And the thought that nobility lies in conduct is older than Smiles. What he gave it was **a mass working readership**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Smiles turned a word of rank into **a word of conduct**, and it stuck so well we no longer notice. Judge yourself the same way, by how you treat those who **can\u2019t repay you**.'}]}}}},994,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'self-reliance',kind:'book',promise:'Trust your own mind, even when no one agrees',about:'Do you ever drop an idea because it seems too plain? Long ago, a writer named Ralph Waldo Emerson said to trust your own mind. He was from America. He wrote an essay on it called Self-Reliance. Which of your ideas needs a second look?',ideas:{'self-reliance_1':{title:'The Thought You Threw Away',card:{insight:'Your own ideas can seem dumb just because they are yours.',tryThis:'Write down one idea you held back today. Read it again tomorrow, and test it.',skills:['thinking','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'An idea comes to you in a meeting, but it seems too plain. What now?',right:'Write it down and test it before you drop it',wrong:'Stay quiet, since someone else must have thought of it',why:'You toss your own ideas too fast, so give each one a fair test.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your teacher asks a question. An answer pops into your head, but it seems **too easy**, so you keep quiet. Then a classmate says it and gets praised. How does **that feel**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A writer called Ralph Waldo Emerson wrote about this in 1841. In great books, he said, we keep meeting **our own thoughts**, ones we\u2019d thrown away. The ideas weren\u2019t bad. We\u2019d dropped them **because they were ours**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your ideas can seem plain **just because they\u2019re yours**. Don\u2019t throw them out so fast. Keep them **long enough** to test them.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You read a book everyone is praising. On page forty you meet an idea **you had last year**. You dropped it for being **too obvious**. Now it\u2019s in print, looking brilliant. What happened?'},{kind:'story',text:'Emerson described exactly this. In great work, he wrote, we meet our own rejected thoughts. They come back to us strange and grand. The thought didn\u2019t improve on the way. Only the owner changed.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s the mechanism. You apply **a discount** to anything you produce, and it runs **before you examine** the idea. So you never catch it. You only hear its verdict: obvious, or surely said before.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Emerson doesn\u2019t ask you to trust **every passing notion**. He asks you to **give it a hearing**.',left:{title:'Dropping it',items:['Feels obvious, so it goes','Judged by who thought it','Never tested']},right:{title:'Holding it',items:['Written down anyway','Judged on its merits','Tested before it\u2019s dropped']}},{kind:'moral',text:'The discount isn\u2019t about the idea. It\u2019s about **who thought it**. Hold your next obvious thought **long enough to test** it, with good humour rather than defiance.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'In 1832 Emerson resigned as a Boston minister. He couldn\u2019t in conscience keep giving communion. Nine years later he published an essay on **trusting your own mind**. Where does that trust **usually fail**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'His answer is surprising. It fails **on arrival**. A thought shows up, and you mark it down because it\u2019s yours. That discount runs **before any examination**, so all you notice is the verdict.'},{kind:'story',text:'A designer sketches a simple fix for a confusing form. It seems too plain to share, so she bins it. Six months later a rival ships the same fix. Everyone calls it clever, including her.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a junior analyst. In a meeting, a senior colleague says the point he\u2019d decided not to raise. The room nods. The thought didn\u2019t gain weight on the way. Only **the speaker** changed.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Most dismissed thoughts **deserved it**. The essay gives no test for telling an overlooked insight from the merely obvious. And memory flatters the argument: you recall the one that came true, not the hundred that didn\u2019t.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Emerson doesn\u2019t promise **your thoughts are right**. He asks you to give them **a fair hearing** before you drop them. The habit costs a line in a notebook.'}]},'self-reliance_2':{title:'Change Your Mind Out Loud',card:{insight:'Changing your mind when the facts change is a good thing.',tryThis:'Tell a friend about a time you changed your mind, and say what changed it.',skills:['thinking','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'You backed a plan in March. By July, the numbers say it is failing. What now?',right:'Say you changed your mind, and show the numbers',wrong:'Keep backing it so the team sees you are steady',why:'Follow the facts, not your old words, and say why you changed.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'On Monday you tell your friends a new game is **boring**. On Friday you try it properly, and you **love it**. But you said it was boring. Can you take that back?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Ralph Waldo Emerson, an American writer, said yes. Keeping an old view **just to look steady** is silly, he argued. What you think **now** matters more than what you said before.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You\u2019re allowed to **change your mind**. Just say what you think now, and **say why**. Learning something new isn\u2019t the same as being unreliable.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'In March you argued hard for **the new system** in front of the whole team. It\u2019s July. The numbers are in, and you think you were wrong. Yet you\u2019re **still defending it**. Why?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Emerson\u2019s famous line about consistency is nearly always **cut short**. He had no quarrel with consistency itself. His target was a **foolish** kind: agreeing with your own past statements purely for the sake of agreeing.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Both look steady from outside. The difference is whether your view follows **the evidence** or **the record**.',left:{title:'Integrity',items:['Follows the evidence','Says why it changed','Keeps its promises']},right:{title:'Foolish consistency',items:['Follows the record','Hides that it changed','Keeps its old positions']}},{kind:'explain',text:'His alternative: say what you think today, **firmly**. Tomorrow, say what you think then, even if it contradicts today. Firm means considered, not careless. It\u2019s refusing to let **the record vote**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t hold a view just **because you said it**. Follow **the evidence**, and say plainly when it moved you. That\u2019s not weakness, it\u2019s the point.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'It\u2019s one of the most quoted lines Emerson wrote, about consistency and **little minds**. People use it to excuse being unreliable. But the line has a word in it they **usually drop**. Which one?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The word is **foolish**. Emerson attacks a foolish consistency, not consistency itself. He means agreeing with your past statements for the sake of agreement. Consistency with **the evidence** is integrity. Consistency with your record is something else.'},{kind:'story',text:'A manager champions a new rota in spring. By summer, the data says it\u2019s hurting the team. Reversing it would look like a U-turn, so she keeps it. Each month, the team pays for her record.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a friend who said she\u2019d never move to the city. Five years on, a job there fits her life. Her old words aren\u2019t a promise to anyone. They were just her view at the time.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The essay underrates **being predictable**. Other people plan around your word. Someone who reverses often is costly to work with, even when each reversal is right. The test is whether the evidence changed, or only your mood.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Keep your promises, but don\u2019t keep **your opinions** out of pride. When the evidence moves, **say so plainly**, and say why. The record shouldn\u2019t get a vote.'}]},'self-reliance_3':{title:'Your Own Patch of Land',card:{insight:'A copy of a hit will always come in second.',tryThis:'Write down one thing you can make from what you know that your friends cannot.',skills:['career','thinking']},review:{prompt:'A friend\u2019s newsletter is a big hit. How do you start your own?',right:'Build it around what you know and she does not',wrong:'Copy her style and do it a bit better',why:'A copy comes in second, so make what only you can make.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture a big garden where everyone gets **one small patch**. Your patch only grows food if you dig it. So what happens if you spend all day **digging next door**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Sam\u2019s comic is a hit at school, so you draw one just like it. Friends say yours is fine, just not as good. Your own idea, about your grandad\u2019s pigeons, stays in a drawer. That\u2019s the one nobody else could draw.'},{kind:'moral',text:'If you copy someone, you make **a weaker version** of something already there. The thing **only you could make** never gets made. Emerson\u2019s advice: dig your own patch.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A rival\u2019s newsletter is **thriving**. You could copy its format and write it slightly better. Or you could build something **they can\u2019t**. Which one feels safer? And which one is actually worth more?'},{kind:'story',text:'Priya has spent ten years working in the trade the rival only reports on. Copying their format would be easy to start. Building hers around **what she knows** feels smaller. But only she can write it.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Emerson\u2019s complaint about copying isn\u2019t that it\u2019s dishonest. It\u2019s that it\u2019s **unproductive**. Effort spent reproducing what exists is effort not spent on **the one thing** that exists only in you. Nobody else was going to make that.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Copying feels like **the safe bet**. Your own angle feels **smaller at first**.',left:{title:'Copying',items:['Quick to start','Measured against the original','One more of the same']},right:{title:'Your own angle',items:['Slow to feel right','Measured on its own terms','Something that was missing']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Before you copy what works, ask what **only you** could add. It will feel less impressive from the inside. That\u2019s usually a sign **it\u2019s yours**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'In one passage Emerson sets two blunt verdicts side by side. One is about **envy**. The other calls **imitation** a kind of self-destruction. Why would copying someone be that serious?'},{kind:'explain',text:'His argument is about **supply**. What you could contribute exists only in you. If you spend your effort reproducing someone else\u2019s work, your contribution isn\u2019t made by **anybody**. Nobody else was going to make it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Emerson\u2019s image is a field. The world is full of good things, he says. Yet the corn that feeds you only grows on **the plot** you were handed to work. You didn\u2019t choose it, and you can\u2019t swap it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a session musician who spends years sounding exactly like a famous guitarist. He\u2019s good, and he\u2019s booked. But the songs only he could write never get written. His own plot stays bare.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Nearly everyone learns by imitation first: apprentices copy masters, writers copy writers they love. Emerson leaves no room for that stage. And he can sound like he\u2019s promising everyone **a hidden genius**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Copy to learn, if you must. But don\u2019t make **a copy** your life\u2019s work. The thing only you could add is **the one thing** nobody else will supply.'}]},'self-reliance_4':{title:'Staying Quiet to Fit In',card:{insight:'Each time you stay quiet to fit in, you give up a little freedom.',tryThis:'Count each time today you hide what you think, just to keep things smooth.',skills:['communication','psychology']},review:{prompt:'Your friends pick a place to eat that you do not like. What now?',right:'Say once what you think, then let the group decide',wrong:'Go along without a word, since it is only one night',why:'Small silences add up, so say what you think once, in a kind way.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your friends pick a film. You think it looks bad, and you\u2019d rather see **the other one**. But you say, *Sure, that\u2019s fine.* Nobody asks again. Why was that **so easy**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Ralph Waldo Emerson, an American writer, said groups push us **to fit in**. We swap a little of what we think for being liked. We do it so often that we **don\u2019t notice** the swap.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Each time you hide what you think, you pay **a tiny price**. The tiny prices **add up**. And most of the time, saying it kindly would have been fine.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'In the meeting you had **an objection**. The plan had a hole in it. You let it go, the meeting moved on, and nobody will ever know your point **existed**. What did that silence cost?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Emerson pictured society as **a joint-stock company**. Each member gives up some independent judgement, and in return gets a surer share of bread. Nobody signs this deal. It\u2019s made **by default**, one comfortable silence at a time.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Each **instalment** buys something. Look at **what it bought**.',left:{title:'The instalment',items:['Nodding at a plan you doubt','Keeping the objection back','Ordering what everyone ordered']},right:{title:'What it bought',items:['A smoother five minutes','No awkward pause','Almost nothing']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Emerson overstates the case. Nobody functions **without some deference**, and he knew it. Read as a rule, his essay is unlivable. Read as **a corrective**, it lands: most of those instalments bought almost nothing.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You don\u2019t have to refuse every convention. Just say **the true thing** once a day, when it would be easier not to. Kept small, it\u2019s **a practice** that compounds.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Emerson claimed society everywhere **works against** the independence of its members. That sounds paranoid. But think about your last week. How many opinions did you **soften** before saying them?'},{kind:'explain',text:'His image is a joint-stock company. Members hand over liberty and judgement for a surer share of bread. The price goes unnoticed because each instalment is **too small** to register. It\u2019s softened **before it\u2019s spoken**.'},{kind:'story',text:'A new hire notices the launch date can\u2019t work. Everyone senior seems relaxed, so he stays quiet. Three weeks later the date slips, exactly as he expected. Nobody knows he saw it coming.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a dinner with friends. You order what everyone orders and laugh at a joke you didn\u2019t like. Neither moment felt like a choice. Each one bought a smooth evening, so it felt like **courtesy**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Much deference is **earned**: to expertise, to shared rules, to people you love. Refusing all convention is unlivable and can excuse plain rudeness. And speaking up costs some people far more than others.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Read Emerson as an audit, not a manifesto. Count **the instalments** you pay. Then, once a day, say the true thing **kindly and plainly**.'}]},'self-reliance_5':{title:'What Envy Tells You',card:{insight:'Envy points at something you want, not at what is wrong with them.',tryThis:'Next time you feel envy, write down what you want. Then write one step toward it.',skills:['psychology','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'A friend gets the job you wanted, and you feel envy. What comes first?',right:'Name what you want, and one step toward it',wrong:'Decide she got lucky and did not earn it',why:'Envy tells you about what you want, so use it to find your next step.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your friend\u2019s birthday party looks **amazing** in the photos. Yours was just pizza at home. Suddenly you feel cross with her, and **a bit sad**. What\u2019s that feeling trying to tell you?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Ralph Waldo Emerson, an American writer, said envy comes from **not knowing**. You only see the best bit of her party. And you haven\u2019t asked yourself **what you really want**. Maybe it\u2019s just more friends round.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Envy is **a sign** that you want something. It isn\u2019t a reason to think badly of anyone. Work out **what you want**, then do something about it.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Someone you started out with announces **the promotion** you quietly wanted. You spend the evening comparing your whole life with their **one paragraph** of news. Does that comparison seem fair?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Emerson treats envy as **ignorance**, not sin. That makes it usable. You\u2019re comparing your whole life, known from inside, with the **edited version** of theirs. The comparison is rigged before it starts.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Envy carries **real information**, but it\u2019s badly addressed. It fixes on **the person**, not the thing.',left:{title:'A verdict',items:['They didn\u2019t deserve it','Comfortable, and closed','Nothing changes for you']},right:{title:'A signal',items:['What exactly do I want?','Uncomfortable, and open','A next step appears']}},{kind:'explain',text:'So move the feeling from the person to **the thing**. Is it the work, the freedom, the recognition? And are you **pursuing it**? Usually the honest answer is no. The envy was the first sign.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Envy reliably points at **something you want**. Don\u2019t spend it judging them. Name **the exact thing**, then ask whether you\u2019re doing anything toward it.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Emerson pairs two verdicts in one passage. One is about copying. The other says envy is **ignorance**. Not wickedness, not weakness. So what is the envious person **ignorant of**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Two things, in fact. First, **the other life**: you only see its edited front room. Second, **yourself**. Envy points at a want you\u2019re not pursuing, and you haven\u2019t noticed. Emerson stresses the second.'},{kind:'story',text:'Two friends start at the same firm. Years later, one posts that she\u2019s been made a partner. The other reads it on the bus and feels sick. He hasn\u2019t seen her hours, her doubts or her cost.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a teacher who envies a friend\u2019s book deal. She asks what exactly she envies. It isn\u2019t the fame. It\u2019s that her friend writes, and she stopped. That\u2019s her **own ground**, left untilled.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some envy points at **injustice**, not a private want: unequal pay, a rigged process. Treat every case as self-knowledge and you can talk yourself out of a fair complaint.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When envy arrives, ask **what exactly** it points at. Then ask what you\u2019d have to give up for **the whole** of that life. The answer is usually about you.'}]},'self-reliance_6':{title:'You Bring Yourself Along',card:{insight:'A new place changes what is around you, but you come along too.',tryThis:'Next time you dream of moving, write what the move would fix. Then write what would come with you.',skills:['thinking','career']},review:{prompt:'A job in another city looks like a way out. What do you check first?',right:'What the move would fix, and what would come with you',wrong:'Whether the new city looks more fun than here',why:'A move changes the place, but your habits come with you.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve had **a rotten week** at school. You think, *If only I lived somewhere else, everything would be better.* Would it? What would actually **come with you**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Some things really are **about the place**. If a bully\u2019s in your class, a new class can fix that. But being shy, or quick to get cross, **comes along** in your bag.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Ralph Waldo Emerson, an American writer, said you take **your same self** wherever you go. A new place changes **what\u2019s around you**. It doesn\u2019t change you.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your job has been **grinding you down**. A post comes up in another city, and you feel lighter just reading the advert. Before you pack, ask one thing. What can a move **actually deliver**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Emerson called travelling a fool\u2019s paradise. You pack, cross the sea and wake up in Naples. And there beside you is the same sad self you were fleeing. He wasn\u2019t against travel. He was against asking it to fix you.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'A move changes **the inputs**, not **the thing** processing them.',left:{title:'Left behind',items:['A genuinely bad manager','A long commute','A market with no work']},right:{title:'Packed with you',items:['How you handle uncertainty','Taking on too much','The feeling you\u2019re escaping']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Before a big move, name **the specific thing** the new place removes. If the only answer is *this feeling*, the feeling has **bought a ticket** too.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Emerson had been to Europe himself. Yet in his essay he calls travel **a fool\u2019s paradise**. Why would a man who travelled warn you against it? And what exactly was he **warning against**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Not travel itself. He allows that a journey for study or art can serve you well. His target is the traveller who goes abroad to find **what he lacks** at home. He finds he\u2019s **packed his trouble** too.'},{kind:'story',text:'In Emerson\u2019s picture, you pack your trunk, say goodbye and cross the sea. You wake in Naples. Beside you is the very self you were fleeing, sad and **unchanged**. It came in the luggage.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take someone who moves cities to escape an exhausting job. The new office has a kinder manager, which helps. But within a year she\u2019s said yes to too much again. That habit travelled with her.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Sometimes the place really is the problem: a hostile town, a dead job market, an unsafe home. And new surroundings can make **old routines** easier to break. Don\u2019t use the line to stay stuck.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Travel changes **the inputs**, not the one processing them. So ask what a move can remove, and what will **come along**. Then it can do the job it\u2019s good at.'}]},'self-reliance_7':{title:'What Would Still Be Yours',card:{insight:'A job can be taken away, but your skills stay with you.',tryThis:'List three skills that would still be yours if your job ended. Circle one to grow.',skills:['career','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'Two coworkers have the same job and pay. Who is safer if the job ends?',right:'The one whose skills would still be hers anywhere',wrong:'The one who has worked at the company the longest',why:'What you know how to do stays with you when the job goes.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve been **team captain** all year. You wear the armband at every match. Then a new coach picks someone else, and the armband\u2019s gone. So what\u2019s **still yours**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Ralph Waldo Emerson, an American writer, said leaning on **what you have** isn\u2019t the same as trusting yourself. The armband was nice. But how well you pass, and friends who like you anyway, are **really you**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Badges and nice things are **good to have**. But they aren\u2019t you. You find out which one you were leaning on **when they go**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You have **a steady job**, decent savings and a title people respect. It all feels solid. Now imagine the job ends tomorrow. What would **still be yours** by Friday?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Emerson draws a line the title tends to hide. Depending on property, position or an institution is **reliance**. It works. **Self-reliance** is what remains when those are withdrawn. From inside, the two feel identical.'},{kind:'story',text:'Two colleagues share the same title and salary. One has **skills** that would transfer anywhere and friends who aren\u2019t just contacts. The other is the role. On an ordinary Tuesday, you can\u2019t tell them apart.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The difference shows in **how they negotiate**. The first can say no, or ask for more, because losing the job wouldn\u2019t mean losing herself. Oddly, that tends to make the arrangement **safer**, not riskier.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Keep the savings and the steady job. They\u2019re **real security**. Just don\u2019t mistake them for you, and keep adding to **what would remain**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Emerson says something startling about property. A cultivated person, he argues, comes to be **ashamed** of it. Why would anyone be ashamed of what they\u2019ve earned? And what does he **want instead**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'His point is that what you have is **an accident**. It can be taken. What you are can\u2019t. So leaning on property, or the governments that guard it, shows **a lack** of self-reliance, not proof of it.'},{kind:'story',text:'A senior manager has the corner office, a strong salary and a title everyone knows. Then a merger removes her role. Within a month she realises most of her contacts belonged to **the job**, not to her.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a carpenter in the same town who loses his biggest client. It hurts, and money is tight. But his skill, his name for good work and his regulars are **still his**. He starts again.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Emerson underrates how much of anyone\u2019s self is **built from circumstance**: health, schooling, money that bought time to learn. Read harshly, the idea blames people for needing supports they never chose.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The supports are real, and you\u2019re right to value them. The error is **mistaking them** for yourself. Take stock of **what would remain**, and add to it.'}]},'self-reliance_8':{title:'Kindness Close to Home',card:{insight:'It is easy to be kind far away, but close by it costs you time.',tryThis:'Write the name of someone close by who needs your time, and one thing you will do.',skills:['relationships','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'You give to charity each month, but you never call your brother. What now?',right:'Keep giving, and call your brother this week too',wrong:'Skip the call, since your giving does enough good',why:'Easy giving should not take the place of the kind that costs you time.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Zara puts her pocket money in **the charity box** at school. That\u2019s kind. At home, her little brother keeps asking her to play, and she always says no. Which kind thing would **cost her more**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Ralph Waldo Emerson, an American writer, saw people who cared about **faraway causes** but were unkind at home. Caring far away cost them little. Caring close by cost them **time and effort**. Do you know anyone like that?'},{kind:'moral',text:'Being kind to people far away can be easy, because **nothing in your day** changes. Kindness close by is **harder**. So do both.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your **monthly donations** go out like clockwork, and they do real good. Meanwhile there\u2019s a difficult call to your brother that you\u2019ve put off **since the spring**. Which one is really costing you?'},{kind:'story',text:'Emerson pictures a hard, uncharitable man who parades his tenderness for people **a thousand miles** away. Emerson tells him to go home. Be kind to your own child, he says, and to the man who chops your wood.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s the lesson about cost. Giving to strangers is easy because **nothing in your life** has to change. **Proximity** is what makes virtue expensive. And the risk is that the cheap act quietly stands in for the costly one.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Keep **the donations**. They do real good. But check whether your giving ever asks you to be **present**, and make **the nearby call** too.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Emerson imagines a zealot arriving with the latest news about **a distant cause**. The man is hard and uncharitable at home. Yet he\u2019s wrapped in tenderness for **strangers** a thousand miles off. What\u2019s wrong with that picture?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Emerson\u2019s reply is to love **your own child** and the man who chops your wood. Love kept far off, he says, can turn into **unkindness at home**. Distance makes virtue cheap. Proximity makes it cost something.'},{kind:'story',text:'A few lines on, Emerson admits he sometimes gives to popular charities he doesn\u2019t believe in. He calls each such dollar wicked. The fault he names is **giving under pressure**, a quiet kind of conformity.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a busy professional with three standing orders to good causes. She\u2019s proud of them, rightly. But her ageing father has asked twice for help sorting his papers. That would take a whole Saturday.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Emerson\u2019s own position is **harsher** than this lesson. He grudges money given to people he feels no bond with. And distant giving can do more good than anything you could do nearby.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t stop giving. Ask whether **your generosity** ever needs you to be present, available or uncomfortable. If it all runs by standing order, notice **what it\u2019s standing in** for.'}]},'self-reliance_9':{title:'The Day After the Win',card:{insight:'Lasting peace comes from living by what you believe, not from a win.',tryThis:'Write one line on how the last goal you reached felt a week later.',skills:['philosophy','psychology']},review:{prompt:'You finally get the big promotion, and by the afternoon you feel let down. Why?',right:'You hoped the win would bring lasting peace, and it did not',wrong:'It was the wrong goal, so you need a much bigger one',why:'A win is real, but it cannot give you the peace you hoped for.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'For weeks you wait for **your birthday present**. It arrives, and it\u2019s great. But a week later, you feel just like before. Where did **that happy feeling** go?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Ralph Waldo Emerson, an American writer, ended his essay on this idea. Nothing outside you can give you **lasting calm**, he said. Only you can, by living by **what you believe**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Getting what you wanted **feels great**. But it doesn\u2019t keep you calm for long. That kind of calm has to come **from you**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'The promotion you worked **two years** for is announced. There\u2019s a round of congratulations. By mid-afternoon you\u2019re at your desk feeling **oddly flat**, and slightly ashamed of it. Is something wrong with you?'},{kind:'explain',text:'No. The flatness is **the ordinary case**, not a malfunction. For two years the anticipation was doing the work. Anticipation ends **on arrival**. So the thing you finally got can\u2019t keep supplying it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Emerson closes his essay on luck. A political win, a rise in rents, a sick friend recovering: some good turn lifts your spirits. You think good days are coming. Don\u2019t believe it, he says.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The usual fix is to set **the next target** straight away. It works, because it restores the anticipation. It also guarantees this same flat afternoon again, **one rung up**, again and again.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Peace was never stored **in the outcome**, so no outcome will supply it. That sounds bleak, but it\u2019s freeing. The next promotion **doesn\u2019t have to** carry that weight.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Emerson ends *Self-Reliance* not on effort but on **fortune**. He lists the turns of luck people pin their hopes on. Each one lifts your spirits. Why does he tell you **not to trust** them?'},{kind:'explain',text:'His claim has two halves. Peace comes **from yourself**, which is about location: it isn\u2019t stored in outcomes. And it comes from **your principles**, which is about content. No event can award those, or take them away.'},{kind:'story',text:'A runner trains for a marathon for a year. She finishes, cries at the line, and celebrates. By Tuesday she feels hollow, so she books a faster race. The hollowness waits for her there too.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a founder whose company finally sells. The money is real, and it changes a lot. But the calm he expected doesn\u2019t come with the cheque. He still wakes at four, worrying.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some outcomes really do change how a life feels: a recovery, a safe home, **enough income**. Telling someone in real need that peace is internal is cold. The claim fits best once basic needs are met.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Want the goal, work for it and enjoy it. Just don\u2019t ask it to deliver **your peace**. That comes from living by **what you hold true**.'}]}}}},995,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'seven-minutes-with-the-doctor',kind:'book',promise:'Get more out of a short doctor visit',about:'What if a short doctor visit could answer the question you came with? The team behind this app wrote this short essay. It shows a few small things to say and ask. Ready for your next visit?',ideas:{'seven-minutes-with-the-doctor_1':{title:'The Worry at the Door',card:{insight:'Say what you fear it is, not just what hurts.',tryThis:'Before your next visit, write the sentence you are most scared to say. Say it first.',skills:['communication','health']},review:{prompt:'You have headaches and fear it is what your dad had. How do you start?',right:'Say where it hurts, then say what you fear it is',wrong:'Talk about the headaches and wait for the doctor to ask',why:'The worry is often why you came, so say it first.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your tummy has hurt for a week. At the doctor\u2019s, you say **where it hurts** and when. But the thing you really want to ask is different. Is it **what put your cousin** in hospital?'},{kind:'explain',text:'If you only say where it hurts, the doctor answers that. If you also say **what scares you**, they can check for it and talk it through. Otherwise you go home **still wondering**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Say what\u2019s wrong, then say **what you\u2019re scared** it might be. The worry is often **the real reason** you came. It\u2019s fine if it feels silly.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'The appointment goes smoothly. You describe **the headaches**, the doctor asks about sleep and work, and suggests some changes. You stand and reach for the door. Then you turn back with **the real question**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Meet Tom. His headaches are the **symptom**. His worry is that they\u2019re what his father had. He saves that for the doorway, because it\u2019s the hardest sentence to say. By then, there\u2019s **no time left**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'A doctor given **a symptom** answers a clinical question. Given **the worry**, they can answer yours.',left:{title:'Symptom only',items:['Gets a clinical answer','Fear raised at the door']},right:{title:'Symptom and worry',items:['Addresses why you came','Changes what gets examined']}},{kind:'explain',text:'The fix is **one sentence** at the start, in two parts. Here\u2019s what\u2019s happening. And here\u2019s what I\u2019m **afraid it is**. It costs nothing, and the appointment gets spent on the right question.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The symptom is what you report. **The worry** is why you came. So open with **the sentence** you were most nervous about saying.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Doctors know this pattern well. A patient presents **a minor complaint**, and the real concern comes out at the door, or **not at all**. Why do so many visits end on the question they were for?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A symptom and a worry are **two different questions**. A symptom asks what\u2019s causing this. The worry asks whether it\u2019s the thing you fear. Say only the first, and the doctor answers **the wrong question** well.'},{kind:'story',text:'Tom describes his headaches clearly. The doctor asks about sleep and work and suggests changes. Hand on the door, Tom asks if it could be what his father had. That was **the whole reason** he came.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Ana, who\u2019s found a lump. Her first words are what she\u2019s noticed, and then that she\u2019s scared it\u2019s what her sister had. The doctor knows straight away **what she\u2019s asking**, and the time goes there.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Saying the worry doesn\u2019t mean it will be tested for, or should be. Sometimes the feared thing is **simply unlikely**, and explained. And the worry goes alongside the symptom, never instead of it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Give the doctor **both questions** in your first sentence: what\u2019s happening, and what you\u2019re afraid it is. Then the visit is spent on **why you came**.'}]},'seven-minutes-with-the-doctor_2':{title:'When the Doctor Cuts In',card:{insight:'Doctors cut in to test a guess, so say the big thing first.',tryThis:'Next time a doctor cuts in, answer the question. Then say you have one more thing.',skills:['communication','health']},review:{prompt:'Thirty seconds in, the doctor cuts in on your second point. What now?',right:'Answer, then say you have one more thing',wrong:'Stop and let the doctor lead from there',why:'The doctor has a guess to check, so answer, then go on with your list.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You tell the bike mechanic about **a squeak**, a wobble and the gears. He stops you after the squeak and grabs a tool. Is he **ignoring** the rest?'},{kind:'explain',text:'He heard the squeak and **had a guess**. He wants to check it now. Doctors cut in early for the same reason. They\u2019re busy, so they test a guess fast. That\u2019s not rude, but **the rest** can get missed.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When a doctor cuts in, **you\u2019re not being ignored**. Say **the big thing first**. And if you have more, say so.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re **thirty seconds** into explaining, and the doctor cuts across your second point to ask about your diet. It feels like **a brush-off**. Is it, and should you let it go?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Studies of consultations keep finding that patients are interrupted early, often **within the first minute**. It\u2019s usually not rudeness. The doctor has formed **a hypothesis** and wants to test it. That\u2019s efficient when the guess is right.'},{kind:'story',text:'It\u2019s costly when it\u2019s wrong. Kim builds up to her main worry, the dizzy spells, after the back pain and the sleep. The interruption comes at the back pain. The dizzy spells **never get mentioned**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The fix is **structural**, not confrontational. Say the main thing first, and **name the shape**.',left:{title:'Building up',items:['Background first, point last','The point may never land']},right:{title:'Front-loading',items:['Main point first','Say: there are three things']}},{kind:'moral',text:'An early interruption is **a guess being tested**, not a dismissal. So put the important thing first. Then answer, and say you\u2019re **not finished** yet.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A mechanic, a tutor, a doctor: experts under time pressure all **cut in early**. They\u2019re matching your story to a pattern before you\u2019ve finished. What happens to **the part** you hadn\u2019t reached yet?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Studies of consultations keep finding interruptions, often **well within** the first minute. The doctor forms an early hypothesis and tests it at once. That pays off when **the hypothesis** is right. When the key item was still unsaid, it\u2019s lost.'},{kind:'story',text:'Kim saves her dizzy spells for last, after the back pain and poor sleep. The doctor cuts in at the back pain and follows it. The appointment ends there. The thing she most wanted checked was **never said**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Raj. He opens with: there are three things, and the chest pain is first. The doctor still interrupts. But afterwards, going back to item two is **the agreed plan**, not grabbing the floor.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The kind reading is usually right, **not always**. Some interruptions are dismissive, and you\u2019re entitled to say so plainly. And if you don\u2019t know which symptom matters, you can\u2019t easily put it first.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Expect **the early interruption** and plan for it. Put the most important thing first. Then **name the shape** of your list, so coming back to it feels natural.'}]},'seven-minutes-with-the-doctor_3':{title:'Three Lines on a Card',card:{insight:'Bring three things on paper, with the most important first.',tryThis:'The night before your next visit, write three lines. Put the one that scares you at the top.',skills:['communication','health']},review:{prompt:'Your list has four things, and the visit is short. What do you do?',right:'Cut it to three and put the scariest first',wrong:'Bring all four and see how far you get',why:'A short visit fits about three things, so put the big one first.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Mia goes to the doctor about **an itchy rash**. She also means to ask about her headaches and her sore ankle. In the room, her mind **goes blank**. When does she remember?'},{kind:'story',text:'She remembers both in the car on the way home. That\u2019s not bad luck. When you\u2019re nervous, it\u2019s easy to forget. Lots of people think of their **main question** after they leave.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Write down **three things** before you go, most important first. A short visit only fits about three. And **paper doesn\u2019t go blank**, even when you do.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You have a short appointment tomorrow and **ten minutes** to prepare. You could write out the whole story, from the first symptom on. Or you could do something **much smaller**. Which helps more?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Arrive with **three items**, most important first. Three, because that\u2019s roughly what fits. In order, because the appointment may end early. An unordered list makes **the ending arbitrary**. An ordered one cuts only the least important.'},{kind:'story',text:'Mia meant to raise a rash, headaches and a sore ankle. In the room she went blank. She remembered the headaches in the car park. Next time she writes three lines, with **the scariest** at the top.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Writing it down is worth **the small awkwardness**. Recall under stress is poor. Reading all three out at the start helps too. Your doctor can say straight away that item three needs **its own appointment**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Three lines, **in order**, on paper. Read them out first. It\u2019s the most useful preparation **you can do**, and it takes ten minutes.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Some patients arrive **thoroughly prepared**, with a full written account of every symptom since spring. It feels responsible. But in a short appointment, that thoroughness can work **against them**. How?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A full account won\u2019t fit, and wherever it\u2019s cut off decides **what gets missed**. A short list fixes that. Three items, because that\u2019s roughly what fits. Ordered, so if time runs out, it ends on **the least important**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Mia went in about a rash, meaning to mention headaches too. Her mind went blank, and she remembered in **the car park**. That\u2019s a predictable failure, not bad luck. Recall under stress is poor, and paper fixes it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Leon, who reads his three lines out **before anything else**. The doctor hears them and says the third needs a separate appointment. Leon books it on the way out. Nothing gets discovered too late.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Three is a **rule of thumb**, not a finding. Some problems won\u2019t squeeze into one line. And most important and most frightening can differ. What scares you isn\u2019t always what matters most clinically.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You can\u2019t control how long the appointment lasts. You can control **what it reaches**. Three lines, ordered and written, make sure time runs out on **the small stuff**.'}]},'seven-minutes-with-the-doctor_4':{title:'The Question Before You Leave',card:{insight:'Ask what would change the doctor\u2019s mind, and you get clear signs to watch.',tryThis:'Before you leave your next visit, ask what change should bring you back.',skills:['communication','thinking']},review:{prompt:'The doctor says your cough is likely a virus. What do you ask before leaving?',right:'What change would make you think it was something else?',wrong:'Are you really sure that it is just a virus?',why:'The answer tells you just what change should bring you back.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A smoke alarm doesn\u2019t just say **be careful of fire**. It goes off at one clear sign: smoke. You never have to guess when to act. Can a doctor\u2019s answer **work like that**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'It can, if you ask **one question** before you leave. *What would make you think it was something else?* Then you aren\u2019t just told to come back if it\u2019s worse. You get **one clear sign** to watch for.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Asking how the doctor worked it out **isn\u2019t rude**. Most doctors are happy to explain. And you leave knowing **exactly what to watch**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'The doctor says it\u2019s probably **a viral infection**. One patient leaves with *come back if it gets worse.* Another asks one question first. She leaves knowing which **two changes** would mean something else. What did she ask?'},{kind:'explain',text:'She asked **what would change** the doctor\u2019s mind. The answer shows what the diagnosis rests on and what would prompt a second look. That\u2019s exactly what you need to know **when to come back**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The question turns **a vague instruction** into **a specific trigger** you can act on.',left:{title:'Vague',items:['Come back if worse','Worse is never defined']},right:{title:'Specific',items:['Come back if the fever returns','Defined before you leave']}},{kind:'explain',text:'It isn\u2019t **adversarial**. You\u2019re not challenging the conclusion or saying the doctor is wrong. You\u2019re asking for the reasoning behind it, which most clinicians are willing to give but **rarely volunteer** under time pressure.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Before you leave, ask what would **change their mind**. You\u2019ll swap a vague verdict for **a clear sign** to watch at home.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Most patients leave an appointment with **a verdict**: it\u2019s probably this. A verdict tells you what the doctor thinks now. It doesn\u2019t tell you **when to doubt it**. So who\u2019s watching for that at home?'},{kind:'explain',text:'One question fixes that: what would make you think **it\u2019s something else**? It turns a judgement into **a specification**. It shows what the diagnosis rests on, which changes matter, and when to return.'},{kind:'story',text:'Nina\u2019s doctor says her cough is probably viral. Before leaving, she asks what would point to something else. The doctor names **two specific changes**. Now she knows exactly what would send her back, and what wouldn\u2019t.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a manager signing off a risky plan. She asks the team what result would make them **change course**. That gives everyone a trigger to watch. The question works anywhere a judgement is made under uncertainty.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The answer is only as good as the time and candour behind it. Some diagnoses have **no clean edges**. A list of warning signs can feed anxiety. And something new and alarming counts, even off the list.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A verdict tells you **what to believe**. Its edges tell you **what to watch**. Ask for the edges before you leave, and you\u2019ll know when to come back.'}]},'seven-minutes-with-the-doctor_5':{title:'Bring Someone to Take Notes',card:{insight:'Fear makes you hear less, so bring someone whose job is to write.',tryThis:'If a visit may bring hard news, ask a friend to come and take notes.',skills:['communication','relationships']},review:{prompt:'Your friend goes with her mom to hear test results. What helps most?',right:'Agree first that your friend writes while her mom asks',wrong:'Your friend comes along to keep her mom company',why:'Fear makes people hear less, so a second person who writes saves more.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your mum goes with Gran to the doctor. Gran\u2019s nervous about **the results**. Afterwards, at home, Gran can only remember one word. Mum can tell her **everything** the doctor said. How?'},{kind:'explain',text:'When you\u2019re scared, your mind **fixes on the fear**. You stop hearing the rest. Mum wasn\u2019t caught in the same way, and she was **writing it down**. So she heard what was said, not what was feared.'},{kind:'moral',text:'If a visit might bring scary news, take someone **to listen**. Decide the jobs first: **one asks, one writes**. Then you\u2019ll go home with the real answer.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'The consultant explains the results, the options and the next steps. You nod throughout. In the car, your partner asks what she said about **the second scan**. You realise you heard **one word** clearly.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Recall after a consultation is often poor, and **worse when it\u2019s frightening**. Anxiety **narrows attention**. So the moment you most need to listen is the moment you\u2019re least able to.'},{kind:'story',text:'Next time, you ask your sister along. You agree **beforehand**: you ask the questions, and she writes. She isn\u2019t caught in your fear, so she hears what was said. She even asks the question you forgot.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Without **agreed roles**, the second person tends to join in rather than **record**.',left:{title:'Just company',items:['Asks, reassures, reacts','Notes nothing down']},right:{title:'A listener',items:['One asks, one writes','Hears what was said']}},{kind:'moral',text:'When the news might be hard, **don\u2019t go alone**. Take someone to hear it, and agree first that **their job** is to write.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A patient and a companion sit side by side, hearing **the same words**. An hour later, they remember very different conversations. One recalls the fear. The other recalls **what was said**. Why the gap?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Studies generally find patients forget **a good deal** of what they\u2019re told. Distress makes it worse, because anxiety **narrows attention**. The essay claims a companion likely improves recall. It doesn\u2019t claim a measured amount.'},{kind:'story',text:'Grace takes her daughter to hear biopsy results. They agree the roles in the car park: Grace asks, her daughter writes. At home, the notes show **two next steps** Grace hadn\u2019t taken in at all.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Pete, whose brother comes along without a plan. He asks his own questions, reassures Pete and reacts to the news. It\u2019s kind, but nobody writes anything down. Pete gets **company**, and not much else.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. A companion isn\u2019t always possible, or wanted. Some patients need **privacy**. The person who comes may bring their own fear, or answer for you. And notes of an unclear explanation keep the unclear version.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The moment you most need to listen is when **you\u2019re least able**. So bring someone to hear it for you, and settle **who writes** before you walk in.'}]}}}},996,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'sleep-and-the-next-day',kind:'book',promise:'Get past a bad night without making it worse',about:'How much does one bad night hurt you? The Mindroll team wrote this short essay about sleep. It looks at the most popular sleep tips and which ones work. Where will you start tonight?',ideas:{'sleep-and-the-next-day_1':{title:'Just One Bad Night',card:{insight:'One bad night hurts you a little, but worry can make it worse.',tryThis:'Next time you sleep badly, take it easy. Go to bed at your usual time.',skills:['health','habits']},review:{prompt:'You slept four hours before a busy day. What do you do tonight?',right:'Keep your usual bedtime and let the short night pass',wrong:'Go to bed at eight to catch up fast',why:'One short night passes on its own, and trying to fix it feeds the worry.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You stayed up **far too late** at a sleepover. Now it\u2019s morning and your head feels **foggy**. You start to worry. Is the whole day going to be ruined?'},{kind:'explain',text:'One short night makes you **a bit slower**. You get grumpy more easily, and paying attention takes more work. That\u2019s real, but it\u2019s small. After **a normal night**, it fades. Worrying about it can cause a lot of the harm.'},{kind:'moral',text:'One bad night isn\u2019t **a big problem**. You don\u2019t need a plan to fix it. Take the day **a bit easier**, then go to bed at your usual time.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You slept **four hours** before a big day. By lunch you\u2019re planning a nap, an early night and a new **sleep app**. Which of those plans will actually help?'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Priya, who slept badly before a presentation. She drinks extra coffee, naps at lunch and goes to bed at eight. At eight she lies awake, checking the clock. Now she\u2019s worried about tomorrow too.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The night itself does **modest harm** that fades. Your **response to it** is what can last.',left:{title:'The night',items:['Slower reactions','A shorter temper','More effort to focus']},right:{title:'The worry',items:['Checking how you slept','Trying hard to sleep','Anxiety at bedtime']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Every fix treats the night as **a problem to solve**. That framing brings checking, effort and worry about sleep. These are widely thought to help turn odd bad nights into **lasting insomnia**. Left alone, a bad night stays one night.'},{kind:'moral',text:'One short night costs you **a little**, and it recovers. So lower the day\u2019s ambition and keep **your usual times**. The fix is to carry on.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A night of **broken sleep**, and a long day ahead. The usual advice is a list of repairs. This essay asks a quieter question first. How much has one night **really cost** you?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The essay\u2019s answer is **modest and real**. Reactions slow, mood is harder to steady, and focus takes more effort. These effects **recover** if the next nights are normal. One bad night isn\u2019t a medical event.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture someone who sleeps badly once and decides to fix it. She tracks her sleep, naps on a schedule and goes to bed early. Bedtime turns into a test she might fail. The next night is harder.'},{kind:'explain',text:'What builds up isn\u2019t the lost sleep. It\u2019s **the interpretation**. Treating each poor night as a problem brings checking, effort and bedtime worry. These are widely thought to be part of how bad nights become **lasting insomnia**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take her flatmate, who has the same short night. He expects a slower day, skips the hard jobs and has no extra coffee. That night he goes to bed at his usual time. By midweek, he\u2019s forgotten it.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The claim covers **one night**, not short nights that keep repeating. Slower reactions matter more on some days, so a long drive deserves care. And poor sleep that persists is worth taking to a doctor.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The night costs you **something modest**, and it pays itself back. Your worry about it may not. So skip the repairs and **carry on** as usual.'}]},'sleep-and-the-next-day_2':{title:'The Weekend Repair',card:{insight:'A long sleep on the weekend pays back only part, and it moves your clock.',tryThis:'This weekend, set your alarm close to your work time. Do not sleep in for hours.',skills:['health','habits']},review:{prompt:'You had five short nights this week. What does sleeping until noon on Saturday do?',right:'It pays back part of the lost sleep and moves your body clock',wrong:'It pays back the whole week, so Monday starts fresh',why:'Sleeping in helps a bit, but it makes Monday hard.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You went to bed **late all week**. On Saturday you sleep until eleven, and it feels wonderful. You\u2019re sure you\u2019ve **caught up** now. Have you?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A lie-in pays back **some** lost sleep, so you do feel more awake. But it doesn\u2019t pay back all of it. It also moves your **body clock** later. So on Sunday night you\u2019re not sleepy, and Monday feels hard.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A lie-in helps **a little**, but it can\u2019t fix the whole week. The **real fix** is going to bed on time on school nights.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Five short nights in a row. The plan is simple: sleep until eleven on **Saturday and Sunday**, then start the week even. At seven on Monday, it doesn\u2019t feel **even at all**. Why not?'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Dan, who works long days all week. Every weekend he sleeps late, and on Saturday he feels great. Then Sunday night he lies awake until one. Monday starts short, and the debt **begins again**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Studies of weekend recovery sleep generally find **partial repair**, not **a clean reset**.',left:{title:'What returns',items:['How alert you feel','Some performance']},right:{title:'What doesn\u2019t',items:['The rest of the debt','A clock pushed later','A harder Sunday night']}},{kind:'explain',text:'The lie-in feels like repayment because you **do** wake more alert. But sleeping hours later moves **your body clock**. That shift is sometimes called **social jetlag**. So the repair creates part of next week\u2019s debt.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Sleep debt isn\u2019t a balance **one Saturday clears**. Take a modest lie-in if you need it. But protect **your weeknights**, because that\u2019s where the debt is made.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Many people treat sleep like **a bank account**. Miss hours in the week, pay them in on Saturday, and the balance is restored. It\u2019s a tidy picture. Does **your body** keep the books that way?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The essay says it doesn\u2019t. Weekend recovery sleep tends to restore **how alert you feel** and some performance. Other things don\u2019t fully return. Meanwhile, a big shift in timing moves **the body clock**, and Monday pays for it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a teacher after a week of late-night marking. She sleeps until noon on Saturday and Sunday. She feels better, but on Sunday night she can\u2019t drop off. Monday\u2019s early start finds her short again.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take her neighbour, after the same kind of week. He gets to bed earlier on Thursday and Friday. At the weekend he wakes near his usual time. He repays less on Saturday, but Monday doesn\u2019t catch him out.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The essay doesn\u2019t call lie-ins useless, and it gives **no figures** for how partial the repair is. Shift workers and new parents may not control their weekdays. For them, a lie-in may be the best on offer.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Recover what you can at the weekend, and stop expecting **the account to settle**. The part you can fix is **the weekday schedule**, where the debt begins.'}]},'sleep-and-the-next-day_3':{title:'Same Time Every Morning',card:{insight:'Getting up at the same time each day can help as much as more sleep.',tryThis:'Pick one time to get up that you can keep every day, weekends too. Set the alarm tonight.',skills:['health','habits']},review:{prompt:'You can change one thing about your sleep. What should you change first?',right:'Get up at one time each day, and let bedtime follow',wrong:'Aim for an extra hour in bed every night',why:'You cannot force sleep, but you can always set the time you get up.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Lily gets up at seven for school. At weekends she wakes whenever she likes, sometimes at eleven. Her bedtimes jump around too. She always **feels tired**. Her mum says she needs **more sleep**. Is that the answer?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Your body has **a clock inside it**. It likes things at the same time each day. You can\u2019t make yourself fall asleep on time, but you can **get up** on time. Morning light helps set the clock too.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Getting up at **the same time** every day, weekends too, keeps your clock steady. Then sleep comes **more easily**. You still need enough hours in bed, though.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two people sleep for **about the same hours** each night. One wakes at the same time every day. The other\u2019s times **swing by hours**. Which one do you think sleeps better?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Studies of sleep regularity generally find that **irregular schedules** go with worse outcomes. That holds even allowing for how much sleep people get. So timing appears to matter **at least as much** as hours.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'You often **can\u2019t choose** your hours, but you can usually choose **your timing**.',left:{title:'Duration',items:['Set by work and children','Hard to change','What people try first']},right:{title:'Timing',items:['Often more adjustable','Keeps your clock steady','Its morning end is yours']}},{kind:'explain',text:'So fix **the wake time** first, weekends included, and let **bedtime follow**. You can\u2019t force sleep at ten, but you can get up at seven. That, with morning light, helps anchor your body clock.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A steady clock makes the sleep you get **easier to keep**. But a fixed wake time with late nights just means **short sleep**. Hold the morning, and give bedtime room to follow.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Ask people how to sleep better and most say the same thing: **more hours**. It\u2019s the obvious lever. But your hours are often set by **work and children**. What if there\u2019s a lever you\u2019re ignoring?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The essay\u2019s claim is careful. Consistent timing **appears to matter** at least as much as total duration. And it\u2019s usually **the usable lever**, because the timing of your window bends where hours won\u2019t.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture Marcus, who wakes at six on weekdays and ten at weekends. He sleeps decent hours overall. Yet he\u2019s groggy every Monday and restless most Sunday nights. His total is fine. His timing swings.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Why start with waking? Sleep won\u2019t arrive on command, but an alarm will. Wake time, and **the morning light** that comes with it, helps anchor the clock. It\u2019s **the end you control**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Aisha, a parent whose hours are set by a toddler. She can\u2019t add sleep. She can fix her alarm at half six, every day. The hours stay short, but they come more easily.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. A fixed wake time with a late bedtime simply gives you **short sleep**. This is a scheduling rule, not a swap for enough hours. Rotating shifts can\u2019t hold one alarm. And the evidence says *appears*, not proves.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Hours are often **out of your hands**. The timing of your morning usually isn\u2019t. Fix it first, give bedtime room, and **let the clock** do the rest.'}]},'sleep-and-the-next-day_4':{title:'The Bed That Wakes You',card:{insight:'Lying awake in bed teaches your brain that bed is for being awake.',tryThis:'Next time you lie awake too long, get up. Do something dull in dim light.',skills:['health','psychology']},review:{prompt:'You have been lying awake for an hour, and you are getting mad. What now?',right:'Get up and do a dull task in dim light until you are sleepy',wrong:'Stay in bed, because lying there still counts as rest',why:'Your brain links your bed with whatever you do in it most nights.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'It\u2019s late, and you\u2019ve been in bed **for ages**. You turn over, check the clock and turn over again. Your brain **won\u2019t switch off**. What should you do now?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A dog sees its lead and gets excited, because the lead **always means a walk**. Your bed works the same way. If bed keeps meaning lying awake and cross, your brain learns that **bed means awake**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'If you can\u2019t sleep, **get up** for a bit. Do something **boring** in dim light. Go back to bed when you feel sleepy.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Three in the morning, and you\u2019ve been awake **for an hour**. Getting up feels like giving in. So you stay put, and tell yourself that **lying here** is still rest. Is it?'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Ruth, who often lies awake for hours. To use the time, she starts reading **work email** in bed. Within a few weeks, simply lying down makes her feel **more alert**. Her bed has learned a new job.'},{kind:'explain',text:'This is **simple conditioning**. The bed becomes a cue for whatever you reliably do in it. An hour spent awake and frustrated is a very good lesson. So every long, cross night teaches **bed means alert**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The same hour awake can teach **two different things**, depending on **where you spend it**.',left:{title:'Stay and fight',items:['Bed paired with frustration','Lying down wakes you']},right:{title:'Get up',items:['Something dull, low light','Back when sleepy','Bed kept for sleep']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Getting up at three is **unappealing**. It\u2019s also better supported than most of what\u2019s sold for sleep. So get up, keep it dull and dim, and come back **when sleepy**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Nobody wants to leave **a warm bed** in the middle of the night. Staying feels sensible, even restful. But what is your bed learning while **you lie there**, wide awake and getting cross?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The essay\u2019s answer is **conditioning**. Your brain links a place with what usually happens there. It\u2019s the same learning that makes a smell from the kitchen **make you hungry**. Fret in bed often enough and the bed calls up fretting.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture Tom, who lies awake most nights for an hour or more. He tries harder each time: breathing tricks, counting, willing himself under. Each effortful hour pairs the bed with strain. Soon, getting into bed wakes him up.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The instruction is deliberately plain. Get up, do something **dull in low light**, and return when sleepy. Something absorbing would **reward being awake**. The aim isn\u2019t to use the time well. It\u2019s to move it elsewhere.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Nia, who wakes at four most nights. She starts getting up when it drags on. In the next room she folds laundry under one lamp, then goes back when her eyes feel heavy.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The essay gives **no clock time** for a long stretch, and watching the clock can feed alertness. One night of getting up proves little. And insomnia that persists is worth taking to a doctor.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your bed learns **what you do** in it. Keep the wakeful hours somewhere else, dull and dim. It\u2019s unappealing advice, and **better supported** than most.'}]},'sleep-and-the-next-day_5':{title:'The Hour Before',card:{insight:'Fix your last hour and your day before you buy a sleep aid.',tryThis:'Tonight, change one thing in your last hour before bed. Turn the lights down low.',skills:['health','habits']},review:{prompt:'You sleep badly and want to buy sleep gummies. What do you try first?',right:'Change the light or what you do in your last hour',wrong:'Buy the gummies and give them a fair try',why:'Fix your last hour for free before you spend money on sleep.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Chloe has been sleeping badly for weeks. She wants to buy **a sleep spray** and a sleep app. But every night she plays a bright, **exciting game** right up until bedtime. Where would you look first?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Look at **the hour before bed** first. How bright is it, how warm is the room, and what are you doing? These probably matter more than anything you buy. Changing them **costs nothing**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Before you buy anything for sleep, look at **your last hour**. It\u2019s cheap to change. And **the whole day** counts too, like no caffeine late on.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A friend has **a sleep tracker**, a shelf of supplements and a careful wind-down routine. They still sleep badly. Every afternoon at five, they have **a coffee**. Where would you start looking?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The essay\u2019s first step is cheap. **Light, warmth** and what you do in the last hour likely matter more than anything taken or tracked. That hour is **a better place** to look than the supplement aisle.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'But the hour before bed **isn\u2019t the whole story**. The firmest support is **spread across the day**.',left:{title:'The last hour',items:['Light','Warmth','What you\u2019re doing']},right:{title:'The whole day',items:['Steady timing','Morning daylight','Caffeine timing']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Why does advice crowd into that hour? It\u2019s where you **feel the problem**, so it\u2019s where you look. The causes often sit in the **sixteen hours before**, and those aren\u2019t felt at all.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Start with **your last hour**, since it costs nothing to change. Then look further back, at **caffeine and daylight**. A perfect routine can\u2019t fix a cause sitting at five.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Most sleep advice is about **the hour before bed**: screens, temperature, a routine. That\u2019s better than the supplement aisle. But is it looking where **your problem** actually starts?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The essay makes **two claims** at once. The final hour, with its light, warmth and activity, likely matters more than pills or trackers. Yet it\u2019s still **not the whole story**, and treating it as the whole story is its own mistake.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture Jo, who gives her evenings real care. Dim lamps from nine, a cool room, a book, no screens. She still sleeps badly. Every afternoon at four, she has a large coffee at her desk.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The essay explains the pull of that hour. It\u2019s where the problem **is felt**: lying there, not sleepy. So it\u2019s where people look. But the causes often sit **earlier in the day**, where nothing feels wrong.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Sam, who has never had a routine. He scrolls in bed, wakes at a different time each day and drinks cola late. For him, the last hour is **a fine first step**. It just won\u2019t be the last.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The essay ranks without measuring: it says *likely* and firmest, but gives **no figures**. It judges supplements in general, not any one product. And some poor sleep has medical causes no routine will reach.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Look at **the last hour** before you buy anything. Then look further back, at timing, daylight and caffeine. A perfect evening can\u2019t fix **a problem located** somewhere else.'}]}}}},997,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'small-talk-is-not-small',kind:'book',promise:'Make small talk easy and worth your time',about:'Do you skip small talk to get to the real stuff? Small talk does a job most of us miss. The Mindroll team wrote this short essay about that job. Here are five ideas from it. Ready to make your next chat easier?',ideas:{'small-talk-is-not-small_1':{title:'The Knock on the Door',card:{insight:'Small talk is a quick check that you will both be kind and polite.',tryThis:'Next time someone talks about the weather, add one small thing back.',skills:['communication','relationships']},review:{prompt:'A neighbor chats about the rain. You want to ask about the fence. What first?',right:'Answer the small talk about rain, then ask about the fence',wrong:'Skip the rain and ask about the fence right away',why:'A minute of small talk shows you are both kind before you ask.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your friend\u2019s mum says hello and adds, *cold today, isn\u2019t it?* You don\u2019t care about **the weather**. Neither does she. So why does everyone **say this stuff**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The weather isn\u2019t the point. The chat is **a quick check** that you\u2019ll both be friendly and polite. It\u2019s like **knocking on a door**. The knock says nothing, but it makes it okay to come in.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Small talk is **a safety check**, not a waste of time. Once you both pass it, you can talk about **real things**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'It\u2019s your first day in a new job. You skip the commute chat and ask the person beside you **what they really think** of the manager. They answer **in one word**. What went wrong?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Small talk isn\u2019t a weak form of real talk. It has **a different job**. It checks that both of you will follow the rules, respond kindly and **not impose**. The content is dull on purpose, because the exchange is the test.'},{kind:'story',text:'Your new colleague had no basis yet for being candid with you. You\u2019d asked for **disclosure** before the check had run. Two minutes on the commute and the coffee machine would have shown you were safe to be honest with.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Skipping small talk doesn\u2019t **save time**. It removes the step that makes the next conversation possible. So answer the weather properly, and **pass the check**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Lots of people say they **hate small talk**. They\u2019d rather skip the weather and get to something real. It sounds honest. So why do conversations that skip it so often **never get going**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Small talk is **a protocol**. It establishes that a conversation is possible at all. The topic is **low-stakes** on purpose, so both people can test the rules without risking anything. Seen that way, its triviality is a feature, not a flaw.'},{kind:'story',text:'At a wedding, you\u2019re seated beside a stranger. You open by asking about her marriage. She smiles politely and turns to her other neighbour. You asked for something personal before either of you knew the other was safe.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a neighbour you\u2019ve nodded to for months. One morning you chat about the rain for a minute. The next week, it\u2019s easy to mention the shared fence. The rain did the groundwork.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The check is **cultural**. What counts as a proper opening varies between places, and some people find the ritual a barrier. It can also become a hiding place. A friendship stuck on the weather has passed the test and stopped.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Treat small talk as **a doorway**, not a waste. A minute on the weather tells both of you **it\u2019s safe** to go further. Just don\u2019t stay in the doorway forever.'}]},'small-talk-is-not-small_2':{title:'The Second Question',card:{insight:'People like it when you ask more about what they just told you.',tryThis:'When a friend shares news, ask one more question. Then share yours.',skills:['communication','relationships']},review:{prompt:'A coworker says she spent the weekend camping. What do you say?',right:'Ask her a second question about the trip',wrong:'Tell her about a camping trip you took last year',why:'A second question shows you heard her answer.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your friend says she went to **the seaside**. You could say that\u2019s nice and tell her about your trip to the park. Or you could ask her something. Which reply would make her **happiest**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Anyone can ask **a first question** without listening. But you can\u2019t ask a second one unless you heard the answer. So asking *what did you find?* shows you **really listened**. It\u2019s rare, so people remember it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'People who are **good to talk to** ask a second question. They ask about **the answer** they just heard, before they talk about themselves.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'At a party, someone says they\u2019ve just spent **a month in Portugal**. You say *lovely*. Then, because it\u2019s been ready for a while, you mention **your own trip**. Why does the chat go nowhere?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A first question is **a formality**. You can ask it without listening at all. A second question needs you to have heard the first answer. It proves the answer was heard and **worth pursuing**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Try the party again. This time you ask what took them to Portugal. They tell you about a cousin\u2019s wedding in Porto. Now you ask how it went, and the conversation finally has somewhere to go.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Most people are **braced** for small talk to stay formal. **The follow-up** tells them it needn\u2019t.',left:{title:'Waiting your turn',items:['Hear the answer','Plan your own story','Change the subject']},right:{title:'Following up',items:['Hear the answer','Ask about it','Stay on their topic']}},{kind:'moral',text:'The rare move is **the follow-up**. Take the last specific thing they said and **ask about it**. That needs no skill, only listening.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Think of the person you most enjoy **talking to**. What do they actually do in a conversation? They\u2019re probably not the funniest. Chances are they do **one small thing** most people skip.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The one reliable marker of a good talker is **the second question**. They ask about the answer they just got, instead of moving on. The effect is **disproportionate**, because it\u2019s rare and hard to fake without real attention.'},{kind:'story',text:'A new parent at the school gate mentions she\u2019s just started a night course. Most people say *good for you* and move on. One asks what she\u2019s studying. That parent is the one she seeks out next week.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a manager in a one-to-one. His report mentions a client call that went oddly. He could move to his agenda. Instead he asks what was odd about it, and hears about a problem nobody had flagged.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. A follow-up can be **counterfeit**: a stock *tell me more*, asked while you plan your own story. It can become an interrogation if it never stops. And some short answers mean the person wants to stop there.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The hard part isn\u2019t wit. It\u2019s **listening to the answer** instead of preparing yours. Do that, and **the second question** almost asks itself.'}]},'small-talk-is-not-small_3':{title:'The Thing They Mentioned',card:{insight:'Ask about their own week, and you will get a real answer.',tryThis:'Before you see a friend, write down one thing they told you last time.',skills:['communication','relationships']},review:{prompt:'You see an old friend twice a year. Which question works best?',right:'Ask how the move they told you about went',wrong:'Ask what their big dream in life is these days',why:'It is easy to answer, and it shows you listened last time.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Last week, Leo told you **his gran** was coming to stay. Today you could ask if he had a good weekend. He\u2019ll probably say *fine*. Is there **a better question**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Try asking, *did your gran come?* Now Leo has **a real answer** to give. It shows you **remembered** what he told you. That isn\u2019t about being funny or cool. It\u2019s about remembering one thing.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A question about **something they told you** works better than a general one. It\u2019s easy to answer. And it shows **you remembered**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'It\u2019s Monday. You ask a colleague **how her weekend was**. She says *fine, thanks*, and you both go back to your screens. Nothing was learned. What question would have **got a real answer**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Last Friday she\u2019d mentioned she was **moving house** on Saturday. So try again: ask how the move went. Now she tells you about the van, the stairs and the boxes still unopened. It\u2019s a real conversation.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Specific questions beat **interesting ones**. A clever question asks **for a performance**, and people answer blandly.',left:{title:'Interesting',items:['Your philosophy of work?','Demands a performance','Gets something generic']},right:{title:'Specific',items:['How did the move go?','Answerable at once','Gets real news']}},{kind:'explain',text:'A specific question carries **proof**. Nobody could ask it without having listened last time. That makes the exchange feel **personal**, not procedural. So specificity isn\u2019t a charisma problem. It\u2019s a memory one.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Skip the clever question. Ask about **the thing they mentioned** last time. You don\u2019t need charm, just **a good memory** for one detail.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Some people collect **clever questions** for parties: what would you do with unlimited money, what\u2019s your philosophy of work? They seem like a way past boring chat. So why do they so often get **such bland answers**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'An interesting question **arrives without warning** and asks for a performance. Most people handle that by producing something generic. A specific question about their real circumstances can be **answered at once**. It also produces real information.'},{kind:'story',text:'At a dinner, a guest asks the host what he\u2019d do with unlimited money. He gives a joke answer. Later another guest asks how the new team at his work is settling in. He talks for ten minutes.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now two friends who meet twice a year. One always opens with *so, what\u2019s new?* The other remembers the house move from last time and asks about the new kitchen. Only one of those questions could be asked of anyone.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Specific can **overstep**. Asking about a medical appointment mentioned in passing may raise more than someone wants, especially in company. When you barely know someone, a remembered detail can feel like surveillance. And some people love the big question.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A question only this person could answer makes **the talk personal**. You don\u2019t need to be clever. You need to **remember one thing** they told you.'}]},'small-talk-is-not-small_4':{title:'The Good Audience',card:{insight:'We most enjoy talking to the people who ask us things.',tryThis:'Before your next party, write down two questions to ask. Do not plan stories.',skills:['communication','psychology']},review:{prompt:'You dread a work dinner because you have nothing fun to say. What helps?',right:'Plan a few questions to ask instead of stories',wrong:'Get a few funny stories ready to tell',why:'People like to talk about their lives, so ask more than you tell.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A school show is more fun with **a good crowd**. The crowd doesn\u2019t tell a single joke. But they laugh and lean in. So who makes **the show** go well?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Talking with people works the same way. Most people **like talking** about themselves. And it\u2019s rare for someone to really listen. So when you ask and listen, you\u2019re **giving them** something nice.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You don\u2019t need **great stories** to be good company. You need **good questions**. People enjoy talking to the ones who ask them things.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Think of the person you **most enjoy** talking to. In your last conversation with them, who did most of the talking? For most people, the answer is a little **surprising**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Two guests leave a dinner. One told stories all night. The other mostly asked questions. Ask the people seated beside them who was better company. The one asking questions tends to win, though she\u2019d said far less.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Studies of conversation generally find that people who ask more questions, especially **follow-ups**, are liked better. Why? Talking about yourself is **rewarding**. And attention is scarce, so whoever gives it is offering something valuable.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your worry about having **something interesting** to say is aimed at the wrong thing. Being interested is what reads as interesting. So **prepare questions**, not stories.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Before a party, you might **rehearse a few stories**, hoping to seem interesting. It\u2019s a common worry: what if I\u2019ve got nothing to say? But what if you\u2019re preparing **the wrong thing**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Being interested reads as **being interesting**. Studies of conversation generally find a pattern. People who ask more questions, follow-ups most of all, are **liked better** by the person they talked to. The pattern is strange, but it\u2019s fairly dependable.'},{kind:'story',text:'At a work dinner, a new hire worries she has nothing to offer. So she asks the woman beside her about her project, then about the setback she mentions. Afterwards, that colleague tells others the new hire is great company.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now think of a grandparent at a family gathering who asks each grandchild about their week. He says little about himself. Yet every child wants to sit next to him. His questions are the gift.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Questions asked as **a technique**, to be liked rather than to learn, slide into interviewing. The pattern is about being liked, not being known. If you only ever ask, the other person learns little about you.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Good company is mostly **good attention**. Spend your preparation on questions, not material. The person who **talked the most** usually leaves sure it went well.'}]},'small-talk-is-not-small_5':{title:'The Clean Exit',card:{insight:'You remember how a talk ends, often more than how it starts.',tryThis:'Next time a chat is going well, end it with what you enjoyed.',skills:['communication','relationships']},review:{prompt:'Coffee with someone new is going well. When do you leave?',right:'End it on purpose while it is still going well',wrong:'Stay until you both run out of things to say',why:'If you leave while it is fun, you both remember the fun part.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re chatting with a friend at the park. It\u2019s fun. Then you both **run out** of things to say. Someone mumbles *well*, and you drift off. Why does the whole chat **feel flat** later?'},{kind:'explain',text:'How a chat ends changes **how you remember** it. So end on purpose, with one kind line. Say you\u2019ve got to go, that it was fun, and to tell you **how the match goes**. Then there\u2019s something to talk about next time.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Stop while there\u2019s **still more to say**. End with **one clear line**. Then next time, you can just pick up where you left off.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve had **twenty good minutes** with someone at a conference. Then the topics thin out. You both glance at your phones, and one of you escapes **to the coffee**. How will you remember that chat?'},{kind:'story',text:'Now replay it. Before the topics run dry, you say you should let them get on. You name the point of theirs you found useful. You promise to send the article you mentioned. Same twenty minutes, different memory.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The drift is **the common failure**. Its awkward ending attaches itself to the whole conversation afterwards. Most people put all their effort into the opening and **none into the exit**. Yet the ending often shapes the memory more.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t let a good conversation **drift to a stop**. Close it on purpose, **with energy left**: say what you enjoyed, or what happens next.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'People rehearse **how to open** a conversation: the first line, the right question. Hardly anyone rehearses how to leave. Yet which part do you remember afterwards, the opening or **the way it ended**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'How a conversation ends shapes **how it\u2019s remembered**, often more than how it began. A deliberate close does three things. It says you\u2019ll let them get on, names what you enjoyed and, where it fits, **proposes a next contact**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Two old classmates meet by chance at a station. They talk until the material runs out and the pauses grow long. One finally mutters about a train. Both walk away remembering the awkward end, not the good middle.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a first coffee with a possible new friend. Halfway through a funny story, she checks the time and says she has to go. She asks to hear the rest next week. The unfinished story becomes the reason to meet again.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The idea is a tendency, with no measure of how much endings outweigh openings. A close that\u2019s **too polished** can feel like a dismissal. And some talks only reach what matters once the easy material runs out.'},{kind:'moral',text:'End while there\u2019s **still energy**. An unfinished conversation is a better thing to leave someone with than a finished one. And it makes **the next one** easy to start.'}]}}}},998,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'souls-of-black-folk',kind:'book',promise:'Make people see a wrong they would rather ignore',about:'How do you get people to see a wrong they would rather ignore? In 1903, a Black American writer named Du Bois wrote about life after slavery. He mixed facts with true stories. Here are nine of his ideas. Which will you try first?',ideas:{'souls-of-black-folk_1':{title:'Double Consciousness',card:{insight:'When people look down on you, you start to watch yourself through their eyes.',tryThis:'Today, when you feel watched, name it as a second job.',skills:['psychology','history']},review:{prompt:'You are the only woman on a team that expects less. Why are you so tired?',right:'You are also watching yourself through their eyes',wrong:'You are nervous, like anyone at a new job',why:'Seeing yourself through the eyes of people who look down on you is hard work.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Imagine playing a match while the whole crowd wants you to lose. You play your best. But you can **hear them** too. Soon you\u2019re watching yourself the way they do. What happens when that crowd **never goes home**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'In 1903 a Black American writer named Du Bois gave this feeling **a name**: *double consciousness*. Slavery had ended less than forty years before. Black people were still treated as less. So many saw themselves **twice**: as they were, and as others saw them.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When people look down on you, you can end up **watching yourself** through their eyes. That\u2019s tiring **extra work**. Giving it a name helps you spot it.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re the first in your family in this job, or the only woman on the panel. You answer a question. Meanwhile you **hear your answer** as the doubters will. Who else at the table **is doing that**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Du Bois wrote *The Souls of Black Folk* in 1903, a generation after slavery ended. He described a split inside one person. He wanted to be both Black and American, in a country that treated those as opposites. He wouldn\u2019t give up either.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s the cost. You keep **a copy** of other people\u2019s judgement running in your head. You check each move against it. That copy was put there by **contempt**, not earned by any evidence about you. It\u2019s **a second job**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Double consciousness is the forced habit of seeing yourself through **doubting eyes**. Du Bois named it for a particular history. Naming it is how you start to **put it down**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Millions of people had lived with a feeling that almost nobody had **put into words**. In 1903 one scholar finally named it. Why would a name matter so much, when the **feeling itself** was old?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Du Bois first set out the idea in an 1897 magazine essay. He revised it into the book\u2019s opening chapter. Living under contempt, he argued, forces **two views** of yourself: your own, and the world\u2019s. Keeping the second one going is **a real cost**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Think of a Black girl in 1903 who loves to read. She knows she\u2019s clever. She also knows many white people assume she isn\u2019t. So part of her mind keeps checking how she looks to them. That checking never stops.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a woman who\u2019s the first in her family to become a surgeon. She does the operation. She also watches herself doing it through the eyes of colleagues who expect less. The second job tires her more than the first.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Stretch the idea to every outsider and it loses **its history**: slavery, Jim Crow laws and racial violence. And it isn\u2019t only a wound. Du Bois also wrote of a **second sight** that came with it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You can borrow the shape of the idea, but **keep its source** in view. Spotting **the second job** is the first step to setting it down.'}]},'souls-of-black-folk_2':{title:'Numbers Beside the Story',card:{insight:'Facts show a problem is big, and a true story shows who it hurts.',tryThis:'Next time you make a case, add one true story next to your facts.',skills:['communication','thinking']},review:{prompt:'Your report on late buses has strong numbers, but nobody acts. What do you add?',right:'One true story of a real rider next to the facts',wrong:'More charts, so the facts are too big to ignore',why:'The facts show how big it is, and the story shows who it hurts.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your class wants a crossing outside school. You show the head teacher a chart of cars. She nods and does nothing. What could make her **actually care**, when the **numbers alone** didn\u2019t?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Du Bois studied how people live using **facts**. He went from house to house in Philadelphia and turned answers into numbers. But numbers alone changed few minds. So his 1903 book also told **a true story**: his own baby son\u2019s death.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Numbers show a problem is **common**. A true story shows it\u2019s **real people**. If you\u2019re trying to win someone over, give them both.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve made a careful case at work. The figures are solid. Everyone agrees with them. Then **nothing changes**. You try again with louder figures. Why does a case that\u2019s **accepted** still get ignored?'},{kind:'story',text:'Du Bois had studied in Berlin. In 1899 he published a house-by-house survey of Black Philadelphia. It was careful, original social science. Four years later, he put work like that beside memoir, history and grief in one book.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The order is deliberate. Chapters on land, rent and debt in rural Georgia come first. Later comes **his son\u2019s death**. Meet the grieving father and you know he\u2019s no exception. Read the next table and you know **what it\u2019s counting**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Each half alone gives the reader **an easy exit**. Together they **close both exits**.',left:{title:'Only facts',items:['Accepted, then ignored','Held at arm\u2019s length']},right:{title:'Only story',items:['Felt, then dismissed','Called mere anecdote']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Evidence without feeling gets **nodded at**. Feeling without evidence gets **brushed off**. So when your case isn\u2019t landing, add **the missing half**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A scholar spends years counting homes, jobs and rents. His work is careful and **widely respected**. Yet the country he\u2019s describing barely changes. What should he do with **all that data** now?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Du Bois\u2019s answer was **a mixed book**. Its fourteen chapters move between history, sociology, memoir and one short story. Each kind of writing **blocks the exit** the reader took from the one before. Rigour and force aren\u2019t rivals here.'},{kind:'story',text:'Du Bois later wrote that a lynching in Georgia in 1899 changed his work. A Black man, Sam Hose, was murdered by a white mob. Du Bois decided calm, detached study alone couldn\u2019t answer a country where such killings went on.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a charity asking for funds. Its leaflet is full of figures on child hunger. Donations stay flat. Then it adds one boy\u2019s morning, skipping breakfast so his sister can eat. The same figures start to move people.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The method can be **turned round**. One vivid case can stand in for a pattern that doesn\u2019t exist. Du Bois\u2019s pairing works because **his data was real** and could be checked. Remove that half and it\u2019s only persuasion.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Put **the count** and **the witness** side by side. The figures show it\u2019s general, and the story shows it\u2019s someone. But keep your data honest, or you\u2019re just persuading.'}]},'souls-of-black-folk_3':{title:'The Unasked Question',card:{insight:'If a talk keeps going around one question, ask it out loud.',tryThis:'Next time a talk goes in circles, say the question no one is asking. Then answer it.',skills:['communication','thinking']},review:{prompt:'Every meeting about your team is about cutting its hours. What do you do?',right:'Say out loud the question no one is asking, then answer it',wrong:'Argue about each line of the budget, one by one',why:'Once you say the question no one asks, the whole talk can change.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Imagine every teacher gets a note about you that says *trouble*. They\u2019re all **very polite**. But they all read the note first. What would you want to **say out loud**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Back in 1903, leaders and newspapers often talked about Black Americans as **a problem**, something to sort out. Some white people chatted politely to Du Bois. But under every chat sat **one question**: what\u2019s it like to be treated as a problem?'},{kind:'moral',text:'When a hidden idea sits under every talk, **say it plainly** and answer it. Du Bois did. Then people could see **what it did** to real lives.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture a conversation where everyone is **perfectly polite**. They mention people they admire. They regret the bad news. Yet something sits under every sentence that nobody says. Can you **feel it** in the room?'},{kind:'story',text:'Du Bois opens his book with exactly that scene. White acquaintances approach him with careful sympathy. They praise a Black man they know, or deplore cruelty in the South. They never put plainly the question on their minds.'},{kind:'explain',text:'That question was what it\u2019s like **to be a problem**. The phrasing is the analysis. A problem is **a category**, managed and legislated about. The whole debate could run in Congress without **Black experience** ever entering it.'},{kind:'explain',text:'By asking it aloud, Du Bois turned the subject of the debate into **its witness**. He answered from inside: what it does to you to be **discussed, not heard**. Once said plainly, the frame could be seen and questioned.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Some frames work because **nobody says them**. Say the hidden assumption out loud, then answer it. You\u2019ll change **what the talk** is about.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A schoolroom, years before the book. Children are swapping little visiting cards. One girl takes a look at a boy\u2019s card and **refuses it**, coldly. What does that boy suddenly **understand**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'That boy was Du Bois. He saw he was **shut out** of her world. The book builds on that moment. Its organising question is the one never put directly: what it\u2019s like to be **a problem**.'},{kind:'story',text:'As an adult, he describes white acquaintances who approach him with careful sympathy. They mention a Black man they admire. They deplore cruelty in the South. They never ask the thing they mean. He takes that silence as a sign they know.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a hospital where the night staff are only ever called the cover problem. Meetings discuss rotas, costs and gaps. Nobody asks what the shifts are like to work. One nurse finally asks it aloud, and the meeting changes.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Naming the frame changes what a discussion is about. It doesn\u2019t change **laws, wages or votes**. Du Bois knew this, and spent decades organising. And naming can become **a substitute** for engaging with the actual policy.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Every debate rests on a frame you can **say aloud**. Name it, answer it, and you\u2019ve changed the conversation. Then the **real work** still has to follow.'}]},'souls-of-black-folk_4':{title:'Arguing With an Ally',card:{insight:'To disagree with a friend, praise what is right, then name one flaw.',tryThis:'Next time you disagree with a friend, say one thing they got right first. Then name your one point.',skills:['communication','leadership']},review:{prompt:'Your team lead, who you respect, backs a plan you think will fail. What now?',right:'Praise what is right in the plan, then name the one flaw',wrong:'Stay quiet, since pushing back would split the team',why:'Give real praise first, then name one flaw, and your friend can hear you.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your best friend is the team captain. You think her plan for the match will **lose the game**. Say nothing and you\u2019ll lose. Shout at her and the team splits. Is there **a third way**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Du Bois faced this with a leader called Washington. Washington told Black people to learn trades and stop pushing for the vote, for now. Du Bois agreed **skills mattered**. But people with no vote **can\u2019t protect** what they earn.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You can disagree with a friend **without being mean**. Say what they get right first. Then name **the one place** you think they\u2019re wrong.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A leader you admire, on **your side**, is following a plan you think will fail. Stay silent and it fails. Attack them and your side splits. Most people pick one of those two. What\u2019s **the other option**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Washington, born into slavery, was the most powerful Black leader in America. In 1895 he offered white Southerners a bargain. Black Americans would set aside the vote and civil equality, for now. In return they\u2019d get room to build skills and wealth.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Du Bois **states the case** for Washington first. He credits the achievement and the hard conditions that made the plan look sensible. Then he finds **the flaw**: a group without votes can\u2019t protect its gains. What was given up would have secured the rest.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Disagree with an ally by stating their case **at its strongest**. Give real credit. Then place the disagreement at **one exact point**, with no talk of bad motives.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Imagine arguing in print with the most powerful person on **your own side**. He has rich patrons and the ear of **the White House**. You think his strategy is failing. How do you say so?'},{kind:'explain',text:'That was Du Bois\u2019s position with Washington. As Du Bois sets it out, Washington asked Black Americans to give up three things for now. They were **political power**, the push for civil rights, and **higher education** for their young.'},{kind:'story',text:'In return they\u2019d get peace with the white South and room for trade training. Du Bois credits the achievement. Then he points to what followed in those years. Votes were stripped away, inferiority was written into law, and Black colleges lost support.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture two scientists on one team. One thinks the other\u2019s method will waste a year. She writes his case out so well that he nods. Then she marks the single step she doubts. They argue about that step, not each other.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The even tone didn\u2019t keep things even: within a few years the two camps were **in open conflict**. Historians found Washington quietly funded court challenges too. And this courtesy is for allies, not **owed to oppressors**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Argue with allies **on substance**, not on motive. State their case so they\u2019d recognise it, then mark **the one point** where you part. It won\u2019t guarantee peace, but it keeps the argument honest.'}]},'souls-of-black-folk_5':{title:'The Veil',card:{insight:'When a line splits people, one side must watch the other to get by.',tryThis:'Pick a line between groups you know. Ask which side has to learn about the other.',skills:['thinking','history']},review:{prompt:'A night cleaner knows every manager\u2019s habits, but they do not know his name. Why?',right:'Only one side of the line has to study the other',wrong:'He is just a more curious person than they are',why:'Across a line like this, one side has to look and the other does not.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture a thin curtain hung across a room. It\u2019s **not a wall**. You can see shapes moving on the other side. But everything looks **blurred**. Which side of the curtain would you want to be on?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Du Bois called the line between Black and white America **the veil**. Black people had to watch the white world closely, just to stay safe. Many white people **hardly looked** through it. Most didn\u2019t notice it was there.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A veil lets **both sides** see a little. But only one side **has to look**. When you study any divide, ask who\u2019s forced to watch.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A wall is easy to spot. Everyone on both sides can see it. But what about a barrier that **lets light through**? Who notices it, and who walks past without **seeing it** at all?'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a Black shopkeeper in a Southern town in 1903. To stay in business, and safe, he must know his white customers\u2019 moods and unwritten rules. Few of them know anything of his life beyond the counter. The knowledge runs one way.'},{kind:'explain',text:'In 1903 the veil was enforced by **law and violence**: separate schools, stripped votes, killings left unpunished. Du Bois\u2019s image adds something. It shows what the separation **does to sight**. One side must be expert in the other. The other needn\u2019t look.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A veil is a separation **that isn\u2019t a wall**. Both sides see dimly, but only one is **obliged to look**. When you describe a divide, describe what it does to **your sight**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'There\u2019s an old folk belief that a baby born with **a veil** over its face will have **second sight**. Du Bois opens his book with it. Why would a trained scholar borrow a belief you\u2019d expect him to dismiss?'},{kind:'explain',text:'He says Black Americans are born with such a veil. The gift **cuts both ways**. They see the other world clearly. But they see themselves only as **it reflects them**. The image is a membrane, not a wall.'},{kind:'story',text:'Those within must know the world outside in detail to survive it. A shopkeeper in a Southern town must read every white customer\u2019s mood. Those customers know almost nothing of his life. To them, the veil is just part of the scenery.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a new cleaner in an office tower. Within weeks he knows every manager\u2019s moods and routines. Ask the managers about him and most can\u2019t give his name. The same one-way sight shows up.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The veil can be misread as a barrier **without authors**. In 1903 it had many: lawmakers who passed segregation laws, officials who struck off voters, mobs that killed unpunished. People built it, and people **can take it down**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Everyone sees a wall. A veil is seen mostly by **those it confines**. When you describe one, show **what it does** to sight, and who hung it.'}]},'souls-of-black-folk_6':{title:'The Color Line',card:{insight:'The best guesses come from one change you see in many places.',tryThis:'Tonight, write one big change you expect at work in ten years. Put today\u2019s date on it.',skills:['thinking','history']},review:{prompt:'Your boss asks what will matter most at work in ten years. What helps?',right:'Find one pattern you see in many places, then write down the date',wrong:'Pick the loudest story in this week\u2019s news and hope it lasts',why:'Look for a pattern in many places, and write your guess down.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'It\u2019s 1903. The new century has **only just begun**. Nobody knows what\u2019s coming. If you had to guess the century\u2019s **biggest problem**, what would you say?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Du Bois guessed **the colour line**: people with light skin ruling people with dark skin. He meant **the whole world**. Back then, European countries ruled most of Africa and much of Asia. In the US South, laws kept Black people apart.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Du Bois saw **one pattern** in many places, and he turned out to be right. Spotting **the big problem** early is hard. It\u2019s worth trying anyway.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture writing down, in 1903, the single conflict that would shape **the next hundred years**. The world wars hadn\u2019t happened. Empires still ruled much of Africa and Asia. Would you dare **commit** to one answer?'},{kind:'story',text:'In the opening pages of his book, Du Bois did just that. He named the colour line as the century\u2019s central problem. The century was barely two years old. The end of empires and the civil rights movement all lay ahead.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The scope is **often missed**. Du Bois didn\u2019t mean only segregation in the American South. He meant darker and lighter peoples across Asia, Africa, the Americas and the islands. It was **one structural pattern**, found in many places.'},{kind:'explain',text:'It wasn\u2019t **prophecy**. His training let him see a pattern as structural rather than local. His position as a Black scholar let him **see it at all**. He was the best-placed observer, and also trained to measure.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Big forecasts come from **structure**, not headlines. Look for one pattern across many places, then **write it down** and date it. You\u2019ll learn whether your eye is any good.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'In 1900, at a conference in London, a young scholar addressed the nations of the world. Three years later he put the **same claim** on a book\u2019s first pages. What was so important it had to **open the book**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The claim was that **the colour line** would be the century\u2019s central problem. Du Bois meant the whole order of empire. In 1903 he set it at the front of a book on American life. **A conference statement** became its frame.'},{kind:'story',text:'His training let him see segregation in Georgia and colonial rule in Africa as one structure. His position meant he was looking where most white scholars weren\u2019t. The forecast held through world wars, the end of empires and the civil rights era.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a nurse who notices that every ward she\u2019s worked on is short of staff. She writes that staffing will define her profession for a decade. She dates it. Years later, she can check her eye.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Forecasts that land are remembered and misses are forgotten, so **judge the method**. And the colour line wasn\u2019t the only great conflict. Class, nationalism and the Cold War shaped the century too. The claim was **organising, not exclusive**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Training without position misses the pattern. Position without training **can\u2019t prove it**. When you have both, risk **an early name** for your field\u2019s central problem.'}]},'souls-of-black-folk_7':{title:'Proof From One Home',card:{insight:'A big problem feels far off until you see it in one home.',tryThis:'Pick a problem that feels far away. Write two lines on how it shows up in one home.',skills:['thinking','history']},review:{prompt:'A report on moldy apartments feels far away to you. How do you see its cost?',right:'Picture how it shows up inside one home',wrong:'Read the city totals until the size sinks in',why:'Big problems are felt one home at a time, so look at one home.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A chart can tell you that many families suffer. But can a chart **make you feel** what it\u2019s like inside **one home**? What could do that instead?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Du Bois had a baby son. When the boy was about eighteen months old, he fell ill and died. Du Bois wrote a chapter **full of love** and grief. Near the end, he admits **part of him** was glad his son would never face racism.'},{kind:'moral',text:'No loving father wants that thought. Racism put it there, and that shows **how deep** it went. One true story from one home can prove what **numbers can\u2019t**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve read a dozen reports on a problem. The figures are grim. You nod, and then **forget them** by lunch. What would it take for the problem to **stay with you**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Du Bois\u2019s first child, a son, died in Atlanta at about eighteen months old. Du Bois gives him a chapter of his own, in the middle of a book of history and economics. It\u2019s full of love and grief.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Near its end, the chapter turns. Du Bois reports, with **clear discomfort**, a thought he can\u2019t push away. His son will never have to live **behind the veil**. A loving father is half-glad his child is gone.'},{kind:'explain',text:'That admission is **the evidence**. It shows what the system does inside a home: it can put relief into mourning. No survey can capture that. Du Bois isn\u2019t seeking sympathy. He\u2019s closing **a gap** in his proof.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Structures can be described forever **in the abstract**. Their cost is felt at the scale of **one household**. So when a problem seems distant, find the home where it\u2019s lived.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A book of land records, debt figures and political argument. Then, in the middle, a short chapter about **a baby boy**. Why would a sociologist put his own **private grief** among the tables?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Here the argument stops being statistical without becoming **less rigorous**. A father\u2019s grief, honestly reported, is **evidence too**. It shows that the structures described elsewhere have **an interior**. You can\u2019t get that from a survey.'},{kind:'story',text:'He writes of the birth, the child\u2019s short life and his death in Atlanta. Then comes a thought he reports with discomfort: relief that the boy escaped life behind the veil. He doesn\u2019t excuse it. He sets it down.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a housing report full of figures on damp homes. Readers skim it. Then it adds one family drying a toddler\u2019s clothes by the oven, because the bedroom walls are wet. Suddenly the figures have a home in them.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. One private grief can\u2019t establish a general claim alone. It leans on the surveys around it. It can be misread as **despair**. Du Bois didn\u2019t think that: he spent a long life fighting. And testimony like this **costs the witness**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When a problem seems abstract, find **the one household** where it\u2019s lived. That\u2019s the size it\u2019s actually felt at. But don\u2019t **demand such testimony** from everyone who suffers before you\u2019ll believe them.'}]},'souls-of-black-folk_8':{title:'Songs Beside Poems',card:{insight:'How you lay out your work makes a point before anyone reads your words.',tryThis:'Before you send your next email or slides, check that the layout makes your point.',skills:['communication','thinking']},review:{prompt:'You say both teams are equal, but your slides always list one first. Why care?',right:'The layout makes a point too, and people see it first',wrong:'Only the words count, since nobody looks at the layout',why:'People see how your work looks first, so the layout makes a point too.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Open the book to any chapter. At the top sit **two things**: a few lines of poetry, and some printed music with **no words**. Why would a writer start every chapter that way?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The poems are often by famous European poets. The music comes from **spirituals**, songs made by enslaved people. Many weren\u2019t allowed to read or write, so they **kept their songs** and passed them on. Du Bois called them America\u2019s most beautiful music.'},{kind:'moral',text:'By putting the songs **beside great poems**, Du Bois said they were great art too. How you lay something out can **make a point** before you say a word.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two things sit at the top of a page, in **the same type**. One is a famous poem. The other is a tune passed on only by voice. What are you told before **a word of argument**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Each chapter of Du Bois\u2019s book opens with two epigraphs. One is poetry, often by Tennyson or Schiller. Below it is a bar of music from a spiritual, printed as notes, with no words and no title.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The pairing is **an argument** about whose culture counts. A spiritual sits beneath famous verse, in the same place and type. That claims **equal standing** before you\u2019ve read a word. In the last chapter, Du Bois makes the case outright.'},{kind:'explain',text:'He reads the sorrow songs as **a record** kept by people forbidden to keep records. In many slave states it was **a crime** to teach enslaved people to read. The songs carried memory and faith anyway, voice to voice.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The form of a work can carry **part of its argument**. Readers notice the form first. So check whether your layout is **supporting your point** or arguing with it.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Plenty of readers can\u2019t read written music. So picture opening a chapter and meeting a line you **can\u2019t access**, set just below one **you can**. How does that moment feel?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Every chapter of Du Bois\u2019s book does this. A passage of poetry sits above **a bar of music** from a spiritual, printed as notes with no words. The form claims **equal standing** for both before the prose begins.'},{kind:'story',text:'The last chapter makes the case openly. Du Bois calls the sorrow songs the country\u2019s finest artistic expression and its true spiritual inheritance. He traces what they carried: grief, faith, and a hope of freedom held under slavery.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a museum that hangs folk quilts in the same frames and lighting as its oil paintings. No label argues the point. Visitors simply treat the quilts as art, because the room has already told them to.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Not every epigraph is European: some are American, one\u2019s from the Bible, and the last is itself a spiritual. Reading the wordless bar as **a deliberate barrier** is an interpretation. Du Bois never says so. And many readers **simply skip** it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Before a reader meets your argument, they meet **your form**. Make sure it\u2019s **making the same case**. Du Bois\u2019s pages argued for the songs before he wrote a word about them.'}]},'souls-of-black-folk_9':{title:'Name Your Own Mistakes',card:{insight:'People trust what you say went wrong when you name your own side\u2019s mistakes too.',tryThis:'Next time you write up a mistake, add what your own side got wrong.',skills:['communication','history']},review:{prompt:'Your team\u2019s project failed, partly because of another team. How do you write it up?',right:'Say what worked and what your own side got wrong',wrong:'Focus on the other team\u2019s mistakes, since they caused most of it',why:'Name your own side\u2019s mistakes too, and people will believe the rest.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your team lost the final. You could blame **the referee**. You could say your team was perfect. Or you could tell **the whole truth**. Which story would make people believe you?'},{kind:'explain',text:'When the US Civil War ended in 1865, about **four million** people were freed from slavery. Most had no land, money or schooling. The government set up the **Freedmen\u2019s Bureau** to help. Du Bois wrote about what it did well and badly.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Du Bois said the Bureau did good work, made mistakes, and was **shut down too soon**. Telling all of it made him **easy to trust**. Don\u2019t leave out your own side\u2019s mistakes.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A project you believed in **has collapsed**. Everyone wants the story. You could call it a noble effort betrayed. Or you could call it a mess from the start. Is there **a third version**?'},{kind:'story',text:'As the Civil War ended, Congress created the Freedmen\u2019s Bureau to help some four million newly freed people. It ran schools, settled disputes and oversaw labour contracts across the South. Writing in 1903, Du Bois gave it a chapter.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Du Bois fills **both columns**. He credits what worked and names what failed, **without softening**.',left:{title:'What it achieved',items:['Schools for freed people','Courts where none existed','Free labour contracts']},right:{title:'Where it failed',items:['Land promises abandoned','Corruption and underfunding','Ended with work half done']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Then comes **his verdict**. The failure wasn\u2019t fate. It was chosen in Washington, by people who **lost interest** while the work was half done. That charge is believable only because he\u2019s already admitted the Bureau\u2019s own faults.'},{kind:'moral',text:'An honest account of a lost cause credits **what worked** and names **what failed**, including on your side. Refusing the easy story is what makes you believable.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Around 1900, an account gaining ground in American universities told one story about the years after slavery. It called them **a corrupt disaster** forced on the South. How do you answer a story like that without **simply flipping it**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Du Bois\u2019s chapter on the Freedmen\u2019s Bureau answers it **without inverting it**. He grants corruption and incompetence where he finds them. That\u2019s exactly why his credit counts when he lists **schools, courts** and labour contracts.'},{kind:'story',text:'His verdict is pointed. Land redistribution was abandoned. Money and authority were withheld. Then the Bureau was ended with its work half done, by people in Washington who\u2019d lost interest. Each was a decision, not a law of nature.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a manager writing up a failed product launch. She lists the market\u2019s surprises, then her own team\u2019s missed deadlines. Because she named those, the board believes her when she says the budget was cut too early.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. This chapter is short and early. Du Bois\u2019s full reckoning came in a 1935 book that put **freed people** at the centre. And balance can become **false balance**: two full columns don\u2019t mean equal weight.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A lost cause is believed when **its advocate** names its own mistakes. So when you tell what went wrong, give **both columns**, and weigh them honestly.'}]}}}},999,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'spacing-effect-evidence',kind:'book',promise:'Remember more from the same study time',about:'Should you trust the advice to space out your study? It is one of the best-tested ideas about how we learn. But it has limits. These ideas show what the tests prove, and where the proof stops. Want to see what it means for you?',ideas:{'spacing-effect-evidence_1':{title:'Spread Out Your Study',card:{insight:'Spread the same study time over a few days, and you will remember more.',tryThis:'Split your next hour of study into three short parts on three days.',skills:['habits','science']},review:{prompt:'You have ten hours to study for a test in three weeks. What is your plan?',right:'Spread the same ten hours across the three weeks',wrong:'Save all ten hours for the last weekend',why:'The same hours, spread out, stick better than one long night.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Sam and Mia both have **one hour** to learn their spellings. Sam does the whole hour on Sunday night. Mia does fifteen minutes on four days. Who\u2019ll **remember more** next week?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Mia will, most likely. They spent **the same time**. She just spread hers out. A scientist called Ebbinghaus wrote about this in the 1880s. People have tested it **again and again** since, and you get the same answer.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Spreading out your study costs **no extra time**. It just changes your calendar. And it\u2019s one of the **best-tested ideas** about how you learn.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your exam is in **three weeks**, and you\u2019ve got about ten hours to give it. The plan forming in your head is the usual one. Clear the final weekend and **do it all** then. Will it work?'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture two students, each with ten hours. Priya does all ten on the final weekend. Tom spreads his ten across the three weeks, a little at a time. Same total time. Weeks later, Tom remembers more of it.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Why trust this one? Psychology has a **replication problem**: many findings fail when the study is run again. Spacing was first described in the 1880s and has **held up** across ages, subjects and cultures since. The effects are big, not marginal.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Spacing asks for **no extra effort**. Only **the calendar** changes.',left:{title:'Massed',items:['All ten hours at the end','Feels intense and productive','Less is left later']},right:{title:'Spaced',items:['The same ten hours, spread','Feels calmer, less dramatic','More is left later']}},{kind:'moral',text:'The same hours, spread out, leave **more in your memory**. It costs you nothing extra. Yet deadline pressure pushes almost everyone **the other way**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Every week brings **a new claim** about how your mind works. This one is different. It dates from the 1880s and has been retested ever since. So how much should you **trust it**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The claim is **narrow**. Give two people the same total study time. The one who spreads it out keeps more than the one who packs it into one session. The evidence for that is **unusually strong** for behavioural science.'},{kind:'story',text:'Ebbinghaus documented it in the 1880s. Over the next century and more, researchers tested it with new materials, new ages and new fields. It held. It was extended. And the effects came out substantial, not marginal.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture Leila choosing how to revise. A friend swears by a trick from one new study. Her teacher points to spacing, tested for over a century. Leila picks the record over the headline, and she\u2019s right to.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Age alone proves nothing, since old ideas can be wrong. What counts is age plus **varied replication**. And the record backs one narrow claim. It doesn\u2019t settle why spacing works, or how far it reaches.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When you hear that a finding is solid, ask **how old** it is and where it\u2019s held. Spacing passes **both tests**. You can lean on it with real confidence.'}]},'spacing-effect-evidence_2':{title:'Nobody Knows Why Yet',card:{insight:'Spacing works, but nobody knows for sure why it works yet.',tryThis:'Pick one thing you are learning. Test yourself on it now, then again in two days.',skills:['science','thinking']},review:{prompt:'An app says its schedule has the one perfect timing. What do you think?',right:'Be wary, since nobody knows why it works yet',wrong:'Trust it, since spacing has lots of tests behind it',why:'The best timing depends on why it works, and no one knows that yet.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A magnet holds your drawing on **the fridge**. You use it every day. Could you explain how a magnet actually works? And does the drawing **fall down** if you can\u2019t?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Spacing out your study is like that magnet. Scientists **all agree** it works. They still argue about **why it works**. Some think each new day gives your memory a fresh way in. Others think the effort of remembering after a gap does it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You don\u2019t need the why to **use the trick**. Space out your study now. The scientists can **keep arguing** while you remember more.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A study app promises it has found **the perfect gap** between reviews. Its algorithm, it says, beats every other. You\u2019re tempted. But what would it need to know to **make that claim**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Dev uses that app to learn Spanish. The app is right about one thing: spacing his reviews out works. That part is settled. Whether its timing is truly the best is another question, and nobody can answer it yet.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'There are **two questions** here, and only one has **a firm answer**.',left:{title:'Settled',items:['Spaced beats massed study','It holds across ages and subjects']},right:{title:'Still argued',items:['Why it works','So how to time gaps']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Two explanations compete. One says each spaced session happens in **a new context**, so you store it in more ways. The other says partial forgetting makes recall effortful, and **the effort** strengthens it. Both may be operating.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The mechanism decides the best schedule, and it isn\u2019t **settled yet**. So you can use spacing now. But be **wary of anyone** selling a single perfect timing.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Every effect raises **two questions**. Does it happen? And why does it happen? You\u2019d think the second must be answered before you trust the first. For spacing, it **hasn\u2019t been**. So should you still use it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The first question has more than **a century** of replication behind it. The second has **competing answers** and no agreement. Running the two together causes errors in both directions, so it helps to keep them apart.'},{kind:'story',text:'One account points to **varied encoding**. Say you review French verbs on Monday at your desk, then Thursday on the bus. Each session lays the memory down in a slightly different setting and mood.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Ana revising chemistry. She leaves a week between sessions, so some formulas fade. The other account says pulling them back is the point: **the effort** makes them stick. Both may be operating at once.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Because the mechanism sets the schedule, any precise timing rests partly on **a bet**, software included. But the opposite mistake is just as real. An unsettled why is no reason to doubt the whether.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Keep **whether** and **why** apart. Use spacing now, because you don\u2019t need the mechanism for that. Just don\u2019t trust anyone who claims to have its one perfect schedule.'}]},'spacing-effect-evidence_3':{title:'Gaps That Fit Your Deadline',card:{insight:'If you need it for a long time, your gaps can be long too.',tryThis:'Count the days to your next test, then wait one day for every ten.',skills:['habits','science']},review:{prompt:'You need the facts for a talk in ten days. When do you first review?',right:'Review after a day or two, a tenth of the time',wrong:'In ten days, right before you give the talk',why:'A good first gap is about a tenth to a fifth of the time you have.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'This week you\u2019ve got **two jobs**. Learn ten spellings for a test in ten days. And learn your times tables, which you\u2019ll need **for years**. Should you practise them the same way?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Not quite. The **best gap** between practice times depends on how long you need something. For the spelling test, go back after **a day or so**. For times tables you\u2019ll use for years, the gaps can grow to weeks.'},{kind:'moral',text:'If you need it soon, use **short gaps**. If you need it for years, use **longer ones**. Close enough is fine.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re giving **a presentation** next Thursday. You\u2019re also starting a language for a trip **next year**. You know both should be spaced out. But how far apart should your reviews be?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The literature offers **a rough rule**. Set the gap at about ten to twenty per cent of your **horizon**, the time until you need it. Longer horizons call for longer gaps, roughly in proportion.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Put **real dates** on it and the rule becomes **a schedule**.',left:{title:'The talk',items:['Needed in a week','First review after a day']},right:{title:'The language',items:['Needed in a year','First review after a month or more']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Don\u2019t fuss over the exact figure. The precise ratio varies by study and material, and fine-tuning gains little. Getting from **massed to spaced** is the big win. Tuning the gap is **a refinement**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Advice to space it out gives you **a direction**. The horizon rule gives you **a date** for your calendar. Decide when you need it, then work back.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Most study advice stops at **space it out**. That tells you which way to go. It doesn\u2019t tell you when to come back. So how would you **pick a date**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The answer starts from your goal. The best gap scales **roughly in proportion** to how long you need the material. The literature\u2019s rule of thumb is about **ten to twenty** per cent of that horizon.'},{kind:'story',text:'Kemi has a driving theory test in a week. A tenth to a fifth of seven days is about a day. So she reviews tomorrow, then again before the test. Short horizon, short gaps.'},{kind:'story',text:'Her brother is learning anatomy terms he\u2019ll need for years as a nurse. On the same rule, daily reviews would be far too tight. His gaps should stretch to a month or more.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The ratio is a rule of thumb, not a constant. It varies by study and material. And tuning within **a sensible range** gains little. Agonising over twelve versus fifteen per cent gets your priorities upside down.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Start from **the horizon**, not a default schedule. Set the gap at a rough fraction of it. Then stop tuning, because spacing at all is **the big win**.'}]},'spacing-effect-evidence_4':{title:'Mostly Tested on Simple Facts',card:{insight:'Spacing is well proven for facts, and a good bet for skills.',tryThis:'Next time you read a claim about learning, ask what the tests were done on.',skills:['thinking','science']},review:{prompt:'A coach says science proves spacing will make anyone great at chess. Fair?',right:'It may help, but most tests used simple facts',wrong:'Yes, spacing is proven for all kinds of learning',why:'Spacing is well proven for facts, but less tested for skills like chess.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You test a raincoat under **the shower**, and you stay dry. Great. Now a big storm is coming. Do you know for sure it\u2019ll **keep you dry** out there?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Spacing is a bit like that coat. Most tests used **simple things**, like word pairs and facts. Those are easy to check: you know it or you don\u2019t. For big ideas and skills, it **probably helps** too. There\u2019s just less proof.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Use spacing for anything made of **facts**. Try it on harder things too. Just remember that **less proof** isn\u2019t the same as no proof.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve read that spacing is one of the **best-tested findings** in psychology. Now you want to use it to get better at **writing essays**. Is the evidence about essays, though?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Mostly, it isn\u2019t. A large share of spacing studies use **simple material**: word pairs, vocabulary and facts. They\u2019re used because they test cleanly. A word pair is either recalled or not, so **the result** is clear.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The **evidence** is lopsided, and it tracks **what\u2019s easy** to measure.',left:{title:'Strong evidence',items:['Word pairs and vocabulary','Simple facts','Clean right-or-wrong tests']},right:{title:'Thinner evidence',items:['Conceptual understanding','Problem-solving','Skills such as essay writing']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Thinner doesn\u2019t mean **absent**. Spacing may well help your essays. But outcomes like understanding are harder to define, so effects there are measured **less consistently**. Your confidence should drop as the material gets more complex.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Spacing is very well established for **simple facts**. For complex skills it\u2019s plausible, not proven. So use it freely, and be wary of **precise schedules** for learning to think.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A course promises **a precise schedule** for learning to think. It cites the spacing effect, a finding with a century of support. Should that record make you **trust the course**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Look at what the century tested. Most spacing studies use **discrete material**, such as word pairs and vocabulary, because it can be tested cleanly. That\u2019s **the main limit** on stretching the finding further.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Jonah, learning a hundred Spanish words. That\u2019s exactly the kind of task the studies used. When he spaces his recall, he\u2019s on very solid ground. The evidence fits his task closely.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take his sister, learning to solve geometry proofs. Spacing plausibly helps her too. But outcomes like problem-solving are harder to define and measure. So the studies behind her are fewer, and their effects less consistent.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The limit cuts both ways. Thinner evidence isn\u2019t **evidence of absence**, so dismissing spacing for complex learning is as unjustified as calling it universal. The honest view sits between.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Match the evidence to **your material**. For facts, spacing is one of the sturdier findings. For thinking skills it\u2019s **a good bet**, not a law, whatever the course says.'}]},'spacing-effect-evidence_5':{title:'Eyes Off the Page',card:{insight:'Shut your notes and recall it after a day, a week, then a month.',tryThis:'Close your notes on one thing you learned this week, then write down all you can.',skills:['habits','science']},review:{prompt:'You read a report on Monday for a meeting next month. What next?',right:'Close it later and write its main points from memory',wrong:'Read it again later so it all feels fresh',why:'Recall with the page shut helps much more than reading it again.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your teacher taught you **the eight planets** in order today. Right now, you can rattle them all off. Will they **still be there** when the holidays come?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s the trick. Tomorrow, cover your notes and **say them from memory**. Do it again next week, then next month. Reading them again feels easier, but **trying to remember** helps much more.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Going back at all matters more than **the perfect day**. A day late is fine. Just keep **your eyes** off the page when you try.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You read a long report on Monday for a meeting **next month**. On Friday, you read it again, and it feels **reassuringly familiar**. Has that second reading actually helped you remember it?'},{kind:'story',text:'Your colleague Ruth does it differently. On Friday she closes the report and writes its five main points from memory. It feels worse. She gets two wrong. But it\u2019s Ruth\u2019s version the evidence backs.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Spaced rereading is **much weaker** than spaced recall, and the two get **mixed up**.',left:{title:'Rereading',items:['Eyes on the page','Feels easy and familiar','Helps a little']},right:{title:'Recalling',items:['Eyes off the page','Feels harder, even clumsy','Builds the memory more']}},{kind:'explain',text:'The schedule can be simple: about a day, then a week, then a month. Software can track the dates. But no algorithm is the magic, and **clever interval maths** gains little over **a plain schedule**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Try to **remember it**, at growing intervals, **without looking**. That\u2019s the whole finding. Doing it at all matters far more than getting the timing perfect.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Someone spends **a whole weekend** choosing a flashcard app and importing hundreds of cards. Then, in every review, they flip each card, glance at the answer and mark it **as known**. What went wrong?'},{kind:'explain',text:'They optimised **the wrong part**. Software helps with one thing: tracking when each review is due. The effect doesn\u2019t depend on any particular algorithm. What it depends on is **actual recall**, and a glance at the answer isn\u2019t that.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture the same cards, done differently. Before turning each one, you say the answer out loud. Some you get wrong, which feels bad. You do this after a day, then a week, then a month.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Omar, learning the bones of the hand for an exam. On the train he could reread his diagram. Instead he covers it and names each bone. It\u2019s slower, and he misses a few. That\u2019s the version that sticks.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. A day, a week, a month is a rough rule, not **the best schedule** for every goal. Something you need for years wants longer gaps. Still, the gain from tuning it is modest.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The core is one instruction: try to remember it, at intervals, **without looking**. Everything else is **optimisation**. The technique works with or without a clever app.'}]}}}},1000,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'tao-te-ching',kind:'book',promise:'Get more done by pushing less',about:'What if pushing harder is why things go wrong? The Tao Te Ching is a short, old book from China. People say a wise man named Laozi wrote it. Here are its best ideas. Want to try them?',ideas:{'tao-te-ching_1':{title:'The Unseen Leader',card:{insight:'The best leaders set things up so people can do the work themselves.',tryThis:'Today, let someone at work make a small call you would make.',skills:['leadership','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'Your team does great work and says they did it alone. What does that mean?',right:'You set things up so well that they did the work',wrong:'You did not make your own part clear enough',why:'The best leaders set things up, then people feel they did it alone.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two team captains. One **shouts orders** all game, and everyone thanks her. The other sets things up before kick-off, then says little. Her team plays brilliantly. Which one is **the better captain**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'An old Chinese book, the Tao Te Ching, ranks leaders. The worst are **hated**, then feared, then loved. The best are **hardly noticed**. When they\u2019re done, the people say *we did it ourselves.* That\u2019s the second captain.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You don\u2019t need to be **seen** to lead well. Set things up, then **step back**. If your team feels proud of itself, you did your job.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your manager joins every thread and makes every small call. The team is grateful. Then she takes **a week off**, and nothing moves at all. What was **all that help** really building?'},{kind:'story',text:'Chapter seventeen of the Tao Te Ching ranks rulers in four tiers. Lowest is the one people despise. Next is the one they fear, then the one they love. Highest is the one they barely know is there.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Why rank being loved lower? **Incentives**. A leader who needs thanks keeps stepping in, because stepping in earns thanks. So the team grows **grateful and dependent** together. The day she\u2019s away, every decision waits for her.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The **quiet manager** works just as hard. You just can\u2019t see **where it goes**.',left:{title:'Works on credit',items:['Makes every small call','In every thread','Team stalls without her']},right:{title:'Works on conditions',items:['Hires well, sets priorities','Clears blockers weeks early','Team ships, nobody notices']}},{kind:'moral',text:'The best leader puts the effort into **the conditions**, not **the credit**. If your team says they did it themselves, you\u2019ve done it right. Being barely noticed is the goal.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Much of the Tao Te Ching speaks to rulers, in an age of **rival states** and rival advisers. Most advice told rulers how to be loved. Why did this book put **something above** that?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Love has to be **earned in view**. A ruler who wants it must keep acting where he\u2019s seen, so the people learn to wait. The best ruler acts **early and quietly**. What the people feel is their own skill.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a manager who answers every question within minutes. Her team adores her. Then she\u2019s off sick for a week. Nothing ships, because every small call was hers to make. The gratitude and the dependence grew together.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a teacher who plans a group task with care. Each group knows its job, and the right things sit on every table. She steps back. At the end, the class feels proud of **its own work**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Being unseen can hide **plain neglect** as easily as skill. It leaves good work unprotected, since credit decides budgets and promotions. And in a crisis, people often need a leader they can see.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The chapter isn\u2019t telling you to disappear. It\u2019s telling you where **your effort** goes: into the conditions, not **the applause**. When people say they did it themselves, you\u2019ve led well.'}]},'tao-te-ching_2':{title:'The Empty Part',card:{insight:'The empty part of a thing is often the part you use.',tryThis:'Today, leave one hour of your day open, and plan nothing for it.',skills:['productivity','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'Your boss wants your only free hour this week. What do you do first?',right:'Keep that empty hour open for surprises',wrong:'Say yes, since a free hour is just wasted time',why:'An empty hour is where you fix surprises or rest.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Think about **a cup**. It has a handle, a base and walls. Now fill it right up with clay, so it\u2019s solid all the way through. Is it **still a cup**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'You can\u2019t drink from it any more. The Tao Te Ching points this out. A wheel turns on **the hole** in its middle. A room is useful for **the space** inside. The empty part does the job.'},{kind:'moral',text:'We make the solid parts, so those are what we notice. But **the empty part** is often what you use. Keep **some space** free in your day.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your week is booked solid, meeting after meeting. Then a real problem lands on Tuesday. There\u2019s **nowhere to put it**. Your diary looks full of work. So why can\u2019t you **deal with it**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Chapter eleven of the Tao Te Ching gives three examples. Thirty spokes meet at a hub, and the wheel turns on its empty centre. A clay pot holds things in its hollow. A room is used for its space.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'In each case we **make the solid** part, but we **use the empty** one.',left:{title:'What we make',items:['Spokes and hub','Clay walls','Back-to-back meetings']},right:{title:'What we use',items:['The hole in the hub','The hollow of the pot','An hour to think']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s why it\u2019s **not mystical**. Someone made the solid part, so that\u2019s what gets valued. But the job lives in **what was left out**. Your free hour is doing work: it\u2019s the room for whatever comes.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A diary with no gaps is **a cup filled in**: more stuff, no use. Before you add anything, ask what **the gap** is doing.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A potter spends hours shaping **clay walls**. A carpenter cuts doors and windows into a room. Both work hard on the solid parts. Yet when you use what they made, what are you **actually using**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Chapter eleven ends on one idea, in our words: we work with what\u2019s there, but **we use what isn\u2019t**. The solid part and the empty part **depend on each other**. We tend to see only one.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take a nurse on a ward where every minute is planned. When an emergency arrives, there\u2019s no slack to absorb it. Everything else slips. The schedule looked efficient. It had no hollow, so it had no use.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a writer who leaves the first hour of each morning blank. Nothing is booked there. Some days it holds a stray idea, some days a problem. The empty hour is where her best work starts.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Emptiness only works when **something solid** shapes it. A hollow with no walls isn\u2019t a cup. A calendar of nothing is drift, not slack. The verse doesn\u2019t say the solid part is worthless.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You need both: **the walls** and **the hollow**. Build the solid parts well, then protect the space they shape. That space is what you\u2019ll use.'}]},'tao-te-ching_3':{title:'Soft Water, Hard Stone',card:{insight:'A calm question you keep coming back to moves people more than a shove.',tryThis:'Next time you argue, say their view back first. Then ask one calm question.',skills:['communication','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'A coworker will not budge on a bad plan. How do you help her change?',right:'Say her view back, then ask one calm question',wrong:'Push back hard with every flaw you can find',why:'When you push, people dig in, so go around like water.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A river runs into **a big rock**. The water can\u2019t push the rock out of the way. It just goes round, again and again. Come back in a thousand years. Where\u2019s **the rock** now?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The Tao Te Ching says nothing is **softer than water**. Yet nothing is better at wearing down hard things. Water doesn\u2019t fight. It finds the easy way and **keeps coming back**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When you push hard, people **push back**. Try going round instead, gently, and keep at it. You\u2019ll often move more than **a shove** ever could.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your friend says something you\u2019re sure is wrong. You tell her why, **point by point**. By the end she\u2019s holding her view even tighter. You had **better arguments**. So what happened?'},{kind:'story',text:'Chapter seventy-eight of the Tao Te Ching calls water the softest, weakest thing there is. Yet nothing is better at wearing down what\u2019s hard and strong. Everyone knows this, the text adds. Almost nobody acts on it.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Pushing straight at someone\u2019s view **hardens it**, because now they must defend it in front of you. Water works differently. Restate their view well. Admit **what\u2019s true** in it. Then move sideways to the case it misses.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'A river doesn\u2019t argue with **the rock**. It goes round, and comes back, and **wears it away**.',left:{title:'Pushing',items:['Attack their position','They defend it harder','Nothing moves']},right:{title:'Like water',items:['Restate it fairly','Ask about the gap','They shift themselves']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Force against the grain makes people **dig in**. Steady pressure along the softer line tends to move them. So don\u2019t push next time: **go round** and keep coming back.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A river meets a rock in its path. It doesn\u2019t push and it doesn\u2019t stop. It **goes round**, comes back, and goes round again. Centuries later, the rock is gone. **What did the work**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The chapter\u2019s claim isn\u2019t that gentleness is **morally better**. It\u2019s practical. Persistence along the line of **least resistance** outlasts force against the grain. Water wins by never giving the stone anything to push against.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture two colleagues arguing over a plan. One lists every flaw, and the other defends each point harder. Then the first stops. She restates his view so well he nods, and asks about one case it misses. He starts rethinking.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a new rule at work that everyone ignores. Shouting about it fails. Someone instead finds the one team already half doing it and helps them first. The change spreads from **the soft spot** outwards.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Water works on the timescale of rivers, and you don\u2019t always have one. A decision due Friday can\u2019t wait for erosion. And yielding is easily confused with **avoidance**. Water returns. Avoidance doesn\u2019t.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your pushing gives the other side **something to resist**. Water gives it nothing, and **keeps coming back**. Be soft in method and stubborn in persistence.'}]},'tao-te-ching_4':{title:'Stop Before It Spills',card:{insight:'Most good things have a best point, and more work past it makes them worse.',tryThis:'Before you start a task today, write down when you will stop.',skills:['productivity','thinking']},review:{prompt:'Your report is good, but you want one more night on it. What now?',right:'Decide where more work would start to make it worse',wrong:'Keep fixing it, since more work makes it better',why:'Past its best point, more work makes a thing worse.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You fill a glass of juice **right to the top**. Now carry it across the room without spilling a drop. Can you? Would it be easier with **a little less** in it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The Tao Te Ching gives pictures like this. A **full cup** spills. A blade sharpened too far goes blunt faster. A house stuffed with gold can\u2019t be guarded. Each one went **past its best**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'More isn\u2019t always better. Most things have **a best point**, and going past it makes them worse. Learn to **stop there**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve rewritten a report six times. Each draft feels **a bit better**. On the seventh, you cut a paragraph that actually mattered. The direction never changed. So when did **improving** turn into **damage**?'},{kind:'story',text:'The Tao Te Ching warns that a cup filled to the brim spills easily. A blade honed to its sharpest edge blunts soonest. A hall full of gold and jade can\u2019t be guarded. Each describes **an optimum passed**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The trouble is that **the direction** of improvement stays **the same** past the peak.',left:{title:'Before the peak',items:['More effort, better result','Feels like progress']},right:{title:'After the peak',items:['More effort, worse result','Still feels like progress']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Nothing announces **the top**, so people usually spot it afterwards, from the wreckage. The text\u2019s answer is to **decide the stopping point** while you\u2019re calm. In the moment, you\u2019ll always find a case for one more.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Fullness, sharpness and wealth aren\u2019t the problem. **Going past the peak** is. So name the point where more effort makes your work **worse**, before you start.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Three images sit together in one short chapter: a **brimming cup**, an overhoned blade and a hall of treasure. None of them is bad in itself. So what do the three **have in common**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Each one is **an optimum passed**. Fullness, sharpness and wealth all work, up to a point. After it, the same quality starts **working against itself**. The cup spills, the edge chips, the treasure needs guarding.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a runner training for a race. Each extra mile makes her faster, until one week it doesn\u2019t. She keeps adding miles, because more has always worked. The injury tells her where the peak was.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a designer who sets a rule before starting: three rounds of changes, then ship. On round three she\u2019s tempted to keep going. The rule, made **while calm**, decides for her.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some things have **no early peak**. Skill, savings and care often keep improving long after they feel like enough. And a rule to stop early can become your excuse to quit when it gets hard.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You won\u2019t feel the peak when you reach it. So **set the stopping point** in advance, while you can still think clearly. Then trust it over **the urge** for one more.'}]},'tao-te-ching_5':{title:'Steer, Don\u2019t Drag',card:{insight:'If a habit falls apart when you stop pushing, you forced it.',tryThis:'Tonight, put your shoes by the door. Take a short walk after work tomorrow.',skills:['habits','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'Your new morning plan only works when you push yourself hard. What does that mean?',right:'You are forcing it, so it falls apart when you stop pushing',wrong:'You just need more willpower to keep it going',why:'If it breaks when you stop pushing, you were forcing it.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two kids want to get a little boat across a pond. One grabs the rope and **drags it** through the reeds. The other sits in it and **steers with the wind**. Who gets there less tired?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The Tao Te Ching has a phrase, **wu wei**. People often think it means doing nothing. It doesn\u2019t. It means not **pushing against** how things want to go. You still act, but you steer instead of drag.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Hard work isn\u2019t always the right work. Before you push, look for **the easier line**. If it keeps going when you stop, you\u2019ve found **the steering**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You want to practise piano every night. So you set alarms, nag yourself and **feel guilty** when you skip. It works for a fortnight. Then one busy week, it all **stops at once**. Why?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The Tao Te Ching\u2019s **wu wei** is often translated as non-action. It doesn\u2019t mean doing nothing. The sage acts without straining, yet everything gets done. The contrast is with **forcing**: working against the grain.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Your piano habit was being **dragged**. Here\u2019s what **steering** it might look like instead.',left:{title:'Dragging',items:['Practise late, when tired','Fight about it nightly','Stops when nobody nags']},right:{title:'Steering',items:['Practise after breakfast','Leave the music out','Keeps going by itself']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s **a simple test**. Does it hold up when you stop paying attention? If it collapses the moment you look away, you were **forcing it**. If it carries on, you set it up along the grain.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Forcing shows results faster, but it needs you forever. Steering is **slower to show**, then **runs without you**. So before you push harder, look for the way round.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two rowers face the same river. One fights **the current** all morning and arrives exhausted. The other waits for the tide, then barely lifts an oar. Both reached the far bank. Which one **was working**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'**Wu wei** is regularly mistranslated as doing nothing. The text means action that doesn\u2019t **work against the grain**. Forced action needs constant energy to keep going. Unforced action is set up so the situation carries it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a manager who chases every report by hand each Friday. It works while she chases. The week she\u2019s on holiday, nothing arrives. Every piece of it depended on her pushing.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a colleague who moves the report into a meeting people already attend. Nobody has to be chased. It\u2019s slower to set up, but once it\u2019s running, it carries on without her.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some things worth doing run against the grain and stay that way. Stopping an injustice or learning something hard needs **sustained effort**. Calling that forcing just gives you an excuse to quit.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Ask whether your effort is **steering or dragging**. If it falls apart when you look away, you were forcing it. Find the version that **runs without you**.'}]},'tao-te-ching_6':{title:'The Name and the Thing',card:{insight:'Names stay the same, but the things they name can change.',tryThis:'Tonight, write down one name you call yourself, then check if it is true.',skills:['thinking','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'Your coffee shop\u2019s sign says it has the best coffee in town. What now?',right:'Look past the name and check the coffee itself',wrong:'Trust the sign, since it has said that for years',why:'A name points at a thing, so check the coffee and not the sign.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your class picks a team name: **the Fast Foxes**. Everyone loves it. A month later, you\u2019re all still saying it. But has anyone checked whether the team is **actually fast**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The Tao Te Ching starts in a strange way. Its first lines warn that any way you can put into words **isn\u2019t the whole way**. Words point at things. They **aren\u2019t the things** themselves.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A name is **a pointer**, like a signpost on a road. Don\u2019t stare at the sign. Keep **looking at the thing** it points to.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A company names its strategy: **customer first**. It goes on posters, slides and every all-hands meeting. Two years on, the phrase is everywhere. Is anyone still checking **what customers** actually get?'},{kind:'story',text:'The Tao Te Ching opens by undercutting itself. In our words, the way you can describe isn\u2019t the lasting way. The name you can give isn\u2019t the lasting name. Everything after is offered as approximate.'},{kind:'explain',text:'This isn\u2019t a claim that **nothing can be said**. It\u2019s a warning about what happens after. Once something has a name, **the name** gets the attention. The thing it pointed at quietly stops being examined.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The company started **optimising the phrase** and stopped **inspecting the work**.',left:{title:'Tending the name',items:['Polish the slogan','Defend the label','Repeat it more']},right:{title:'Watching the thing',items:['Check what\u2019s actually happening','Ask if it still fits','Change the word if needed']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Names are useful, but they start **doing your thinking** for you. When you find the right word, keep **looking past it** at what\u2019s actually there.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Most books open by telling you **what they\u2019ll teach**. The Tao Te Ching opens by warning that its own words **will fall short**. Why would a text undercut itself in its very first line?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The warning is about **attention**. A name is useful at first. Then it becomes the object you look at, and the thing behind it **stops being examined**. The word starts doing the thinking.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a team that names its strategy and prints it everywhere. Soon meetings are about whether work fits the phrase. Nobody asks whether the phrase still fits the market. The name has become the thing being maintained.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take someone who once called herself *a night owl*. Years later she defends the label, staying up late and tired. She never checks whether it still fits. The label is running her evenings.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Taken too far, this excuses **vagueness**: if nothing can be pinned down, nothing can be tested. People need names to work together at all. The warning is about attention, not naming itself.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The book keeps **changing its images**: water, a valley, an uncarved block. None of them is allowed to harden. Use your names, but **keep checking** the thing they point at.'}]},'tao-te-ching_7':{title:'Don\u2019t Rush to Be First',card:{insight:'Go first and you get the bugs, so see if second is good enough.',tryThis:'Before you buy the next new thing, wait one month. See what goes wrong first.',skills:['thinking','leadership']},review:{prompt:'Your whole office wants the new phone first. What do you ask?',right:'What going first costs, and if second is almost as good',wrong:'How fast I can get one before they sell out',why:'Going first means you hit the problems, so check the cost before you rush.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two kids race across **a frozen pond**. The first one out has no idea where the ice is thin. The second one watches and **follows the safe path**. Which one would you rather be?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The Tao Te Ching names **three treasures**. Being kind, and being careful with what you have. The third is strange: **not daring** to be first. Going first is risky. Everyone sees your mistakes.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Being first isn\u2019t always **the prize**. It can mean being the one who **falls through**. Before you rush ahead, ask what second place would give you.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two caf\xe9s plan to open on the same new street. One rushes to be first, before the road is even finished. The other waits **three months**. Which do you think has **the better first year**?'},{kind:'story',text:'The Tao Te Ching lists three treasures: compassion, frugality, and not daring to be first in the world. The first two are familiar. The third is the strange one. The text argues for it hardest.'},{kind:'explain',text:'It isn\u2019t about **timidity**. Going first makes you **the exposed position**: the target, the precedent, the one whose mistakes are public. The first caf\xe9 opens into the roadworks. The second watches and learns, cheaply.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'A lot of chasing first place is about **status**, not **real advantage**.',left:{title:'First for status',items:['All the exposure','Mistakes in public','Little extra return']},right:{title:'Second on purpose',items:['Learns from others','Cheaper and safer','Nearly as good']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Restraint can be **a strong position**, not just a polite one. Before you rush to lead, **price what leading costs**, and check if second is nearly as good.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Most advice tells you to get there first: first to market, first to speak, first in line. An old Chinese book calls **not daring** to be first one of its **three treasures**. Why?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The other two treasures are compassion and frugality. The third, the text claims, makes a person **fit to lead**. Being first means being **the exposed one**: the target and the precedent. Your mistakes become public and hard to undo.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture the first person to try a new process at work. Every flaw in it lands on her desk. She fixes them in public, slowly. The colleague who goes second gets the fixed version for free.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take someone who always speaks first in meetings, to be seen leading. Half the time she\u2019s working from **half the facts**. The person who speaks third has heard the objections and lands the point.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Sometimes **the first mover** takes a lasting position and the cautious never catch up. When a market tips to one winner, second can mean nothing. Applied wholesale, the advice becomes an excuse for timidity.'},{kind:'moral',text:'What survives is narrower and still useful. People often chase first place for **status**, which brings all the exposure and none of the return. So **price the lead** before you take it.'}]},'tao-te-ching_8':{title:'Give Away What You Know',card:{insight:'A skill you teach keeps working, but a skill you hide ties you down.',tryThis:'Today, write down the steps for one job only you know how to do.',skills:['career','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'Only you know how to do the big report at work. What now?',right:'Teach it, so it keeps working without you',wrong:'Keep it to yourself, since it keeps you needed',why:'What you teach keeps working, while what you hide needs you there.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re the only kid in class who knows **a great trick** for long division. You could keep it secret, so everyone needs you. Or you could **teach it**. Which gives you more?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The last chapter of the Tao Te Ching says the wise person **doesn\u2019t pile things up**. The more they do for others, the more they have. A trick you teach keeps helping people **without you**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Things you keep need **guarding**. Things you give away **keep working**. Sharing often brings back more than hoarding ever could, even if it feels backwards.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'One engineer is the only person who understands the billing system. She\u2019s **impossible to replace**, and she knows it. She also can\u2019t take **a real holiday**. Who is really holding whom?'},{kind:'story',text:'The closing chapter of the Tao Te Ching says the sage doesn\u2019t hoard. Doing for others, it claims, leaves him with more. Giving leaves him richer. Read as economics, that sounds false.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Read it as a claim about **attention** instead. Everything you hold needs guarding, and the guarding becomes **your job**. The engineer\u2019s knowledge has started to set her schedule. Her holdings run her days.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The giving half is less mystical. Capability you give away **compounds**. What she teaches keeps working when she\u2019s not there. What she hoards is only worth something **while she\u2019s present**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Hoarding feels safe, but **it ties you down**. Teach what you know, and it **keeps working without you**. That\u2019s how standing really builds.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'An earlier chapter warns that **a hall of gold** and jade can\u2019t be guarded. The closing chapter goes further: the sage **keeps nothing back**. Is this a book telling you to own nothing?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Not quite. It\u2019s a claim about what holding does to you. Everything held needs **guarding**, and past a point the guarding becomes the occupation. Your holdings start **setting your schedule**. Thoreau would describe the same trap centuries later.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture an expert who keeps her methods secret to stay needed. Every question comes to her. Every deadline waits on her. Her knowledge is valuable, but only while she\u2019s in the room.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a colleague who writes down everything she knows and teaches it freely. Her methods spread across the team. People trust her and bring her the interesting problems. Her standing grows while she gives.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. This is a claim about **a threshold**, not a case for owning nothing. A household with no savings isn\u2019t wise, just exposed. And people can exploit your generosity, taking and never giving back.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The text is against **hoarding as a reflex**, not against provision. Keep what you need. Give away the skill you\u2019ve been **guarding**, and let it work without you.'}]},'tao-te-ching_9':{title:'While It\u2019s Still Small',card:{insight:'Problems are easiest to fix early, when they still look small.',tryThis:'Today, pick one small problem at home. Spend five minutes on it before it grows.',skills:['productivity','thinking']},review:{prompt:'You see a small drip under your kitchen sink. What do you do?',right:'Fix it this week, while it is still small',wrong:'Wait and see if it gets any worse',why:'A fix costs less when it is small, even if it does not look needed.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'There\u2019s a tiny weed in your garden, smaller than **your finger**. You could pull it out in a second. Or you could leave it. What will it look like **next summer**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The Tao Te Ching says a tree **too big to hug** started as a tiny shoot. A long journey starts **right under your feet**. So deal with things while they\u2019re small, when it\u2019s easy.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Small problems are **easy to fix**. Big problems started small. So when you spot something tiny going wrong, **deal with it now**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A new hire seems a little lost in **their first week**. Nobody says anything, because it\u2019s probably fine. Three months later, they quit. Could you have **seen it coming**?'},{kind:'story',text:'The Tao Te Ching says to handle hard things while they\u2019re easy. Act on great things while they\u2019re still small. A tree too wide to hug grew from a shoot. A tall terrace began with a basket of earth.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The famous line about a long journey is usually quoted as **persistence**. It\u2019s really about **timing**. Early is when acting is cheap. It\u2019s also when you\u2019re least sure it\u2019s needed, so it looks like overreacting.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The same problem costs **very different amounts** depending on when you **step in**.',left:{title:'Week one',items:['A ten-minute chat','Easy to fix','Looks unnecessary']},right:{title:'Month three',items:['A resignation letter','Hard to undo','Obvious to everyone']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Nobody thanks you for **the crisis** that never happened. Act anyway. Deal with things **while they\u2019re small**, when fixing them is still cheap.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Everyone knows the line about a journey of a thousand miles. It\u2019s usually quoted as **a call to persevere**. But read the chapter around it, and is it **really about** that?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The chapter is about **timing**. What\u2019s at rest is easy to hold. What hasn\u2019t appeared yet is easy to plan for. What\u2019s brittle is easy to break. So act **before things grow**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a team lead who notices one sharp email between two colleagues. She has a quiet word with both that afternoon. It takes ten minutes. Nothing more happens, so nobody ever knows what she prevented.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a homeowner who spots a small crack in the ceiling and decides to wait. A year later it\u2019s a stain, then a leak. The fix that once cost an afternoon now costs a month.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Acting on every weak signal is **its own failure**. Most small things stay small, and someone who jumps on all of them is exhausting. The text gives no rule for telling which shoots become trees.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Early action looks like overreaction, and the wins stay invisible. You won\u2019t get thanked for it. Still, the cheapest time to fix **your problem** is **while it\u2019s small**.'}]}}}},1001,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'the-argument-you-keep-having',kind:'book',promise:'Stop having the same fight over and over',about:'Do you keep having the same fight with someone you love? The Mindroll team wrote this short essay about why. It uses the work of John Gottman, a scientist who studied couples. Want to stop trying to win and start getting along?',ideas:{'the-argument-you-keep-having_1':{title:'The Pillar You Keep Pushing',card:{insight:'Some fights come back because you are not alike, so stop trying to win them.',tryThis:'Write down the fight you have most often. Then ask if it has an answer at all.',skills:['relationships','communication']},review:{prompt:'You and your husband have fought about money for years. What do you do first?',right:'Ask if the fight has an answer, or if you just differ',wrong:'Find the one reason that proves you are right',why:'Some fights never end, so learn to live with them instead of trying to win.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You share a room with your brother. He likes it **messy** and you like it **neat**. You argue about it every single week. What do you think next week\u2019s argument will change?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Some fights have **an answer**, like whose turn it is to wash up. Others keep coming back because two people are **just different**. No answer ends those. Trying to win them only makes everyone crosser.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When a fight is about **being different**, stop trying to win it. Ask how you can **share the room** and still get on. That\u2019s a goal you can reach.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'It starts over **the heating**. One of you is always cold, the other always warm. You both know the lines by now. Someone will say *you always do this*, and it\u2019ll end **where it ended** last month.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Each round is treated as **the chance** to settle it. So each round that fails feels like a failure, and that failure gets carried into the next one. The difference stays the same size. **The resentment** grows.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Naming it **as permanent** doesn\u2019t fix anything. It **changes the goal** instead.',left:{title:'Winning',items:['Convert the other person','Settle it for good','Not on offer']},right:{title:'Managing',items:['Live well around it','Can actually be done']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Research on couples, most prominently **John Gottman\u2019s**, has put the share of permanent disagreements at **roughly two thirds**. Treat that as one group\u2019s estimate. The useful part is the category itself.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Some arguments aren\u2019t waiting for **a solution**. Ask whether yours is solvable at all. If it isn\u2019t, stop trying to win and **start managing it**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two people push against **a pillar** for years. The scrape marks round its base show how hard they\u2019ve tried. It hasn\u2019t moved, because it holds up **the house**. What are they really trying to do?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The essay starts from a distinction. Some disagreements are **problems**: a decision or a fact would end them. Others are **permanent differences** of personality or need. The costly mistake is treating the second kind as the first.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a couple who\u2019ve argued about money for a decade. She feels safe with savings. He wants to enjoy it now. Every round, each tries again to **convert** the other. Ten years on, neither has moved.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Naming it as permanent changes nothing about **the difference** itself. What it changes is the goal, from winning to managing. Winning was never on offer for that couple. **Managing** is, and that\u2019s its whole advantage.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take two flatmates. One likes quiet evenings, the other has friends round most nights. They stop debating who\u2019s right. Instead they agree two quiet nights a week. Neither has changed, but the rows have shrunk.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Calling something permanent can be **a way to dodge** a fixable problem. Think of a debt, a broken promise or unkindness. Some differences cost one of you far more. And cruelty isn\u2019t a difference of temperament.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Before you spend another year solving it, ask if it\u2019s **solvable at all**. If it isn\u2019t, you can stop pushing the pillar. Then **learn to live** around it.'}]},'the-argument-you-keep-having_2':{title:'The Root Under the Weed',card:{insight:'If the same fight keeps coming back, look for one worry under it.',tryThis:'When a fight comes back, ask what it would say about you if they were right.',skills:['relationships','psychology']},review:{prompt:'You fight about dishes, then about being late, then about the car. What now?',right:'Look for the worry that stays the same each time',wrong:'Make a fair chore chart so the dishes stop coming up',why:'The topic may change, but the worry under it does not.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your mum asks you to clear the table. You say, *why is it always me?* Last week it was **the bins**. The week before, it was the dog. Why does it feel like **the same fight**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Think of a weed. You pull off the top, and next week it\u2019s back, because **the root** stayed in the ground. *Why is it always me* might really mean: **does anyone see** how much I do?'},{kind:'moral',text:'A fight that keeps coming back has **a root**. The job changes, but the worry underneath **stays the same**. Find what\u2019s the same each time.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Monday, the dishes were left. Thursday, you were **twenty minutes late**. Saturday, you took the car without asking. Three different rows in one week. Why does each one feel **exactly the same**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Leah and Sam, who argue about chores. They draw up a rota, and it works. A month later the argument is back, now about lateness. Then it\u2019s the car. Each topic gets settled. The argument doesn\u2019t.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The **surface topic** changes weekly. The **real one** stays the same round after round.',left:{title:'The surface',items:['Dishes, lateness, the car','Changes every week','Has a fair answer']},right:{title:'The subject',items:['Does my time count?','Same question every round']}},{kind:'explain',text:'To find it, look for **what stays the same**. Then ask the essay\u2019s question: what would this mean about me if you were right? If the honest answer is *I don\u2019t value your time*, you\u2019ve **found the subject**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Settling the topic leaves **the real question** untouched. That\u2019s why the chores rota settled nothing. Talk about **the question** directly, and the argument has somewhere to go.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A couple argue about his phone at dinner. Then a missed call. Then him **working late**. Each row has a fair answer, and each answer gets reached. So why are they **still arguing**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The essay\u2019s claim is that the content of a recurring argument **isn\u2019t its subject**. Under a changing surface sits a stable question, such as **whose time counts**. Settling the surface leaves that question exactly where it was.'},{kind:'story',text:'Back to the couple. A phone rule settles dinner. A promise to call settles the missed call. But underneath all three rows runs one question: *do I come first?* No rule reaches that.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The essay draws on **John Gottman**, who separates solvable problems from perpetual ones. He reports that most conflicts in long relationships are perpetual. In the healthy version, both people can discuss them with **some humour** and without it escalating.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take two colleagues who clash over **meeting notes**, then deadlines, then who answers the client. Each fix holds for a week. Then one of them asks what it would mean if the other were right. The honest answer is about **respect**, not notes.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. This can become **a licence** not to listen. Sometimes the dishes really are about the dishes, and a rota would end it. Settle the surface fairly first. If it keeps coming back, then you look underneath.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When the topic keeps changing, the subject is **the part that doesn\u2019t**. Ask what it would mean about you if they were right. That\u2019s where **the real argument** lives.'}]},'the-argument-you-keep-having_3':{title:'The First Minute',card:{insight:'How a fight starts often shapes how the whole fight ends.',tryThis:'Before a hard talk, write your first line with what happened and what you want.',skills:['communication','relationships']},review:{prompt:'Your roommate left the kitchen a mess again, and you are mad. What now?',right:'Wait until you are calm, then say what happened and what you want',wrong:'Bring it up right away, while the mess proves your point',why:'How you start shapes the talk, and harsh words come out first when you are mad.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your sister took your hoodie **again**, without asking. You could say, *you\u2019re so annoying, you always do this*. Or you could say something else. Which start do you think **goes better**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Start with blame, and the other person **defends** themselves. Then you both get crosser. Start with what happened and what you want: *can you ask before you borrow it?* Now **a real talk** is much more likely.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The way you **start** shapes how it ends. You\u2019ll want to speak when you\u2019re most cross, so wait a bit. Then start **soft**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You waited **an hour** for a friend who never texted. You could open with *you\u2019re so selfish*. Or you could say you waited, and ask to agree times. Which start gets you **a better evening**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'How the first minute goes tends to **set the course** of what follows. An accusation gets defence, and defence gets **escalation**. Open the same complaint as a request, and you often get a different conversation entirely.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The same complaint can open **two ways**, and each tends to **set a different path**.',left:{title:'A harsh start',items:['Says what they are','Gets defence','Then escalation']},right:{title:'A soft start',items:['Says what happened','States what you want','Can be agreed to']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s the catch. You deliver the opening when you\u2019re **most annoyed**, which is exactly when the harsh version comes easiest. So the useful move is often to **delay the conversation**, not polish your wording mid-heat.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your first sentence matters more than **your whole case**. Start with what happened and what you want, without blame. And if you\u2019re furious, **wait first**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve rehearsed the whole case in the shower: dates, examples, the time it happened in March. Then you walk in and your **first sentence** is *you never listen*. How do you think **the next ten minutes** will go?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The essay\u2019s claim: how a disagreement **opens** is a good guide to how it ends. Research on couples, Gottman\u2019s in particular, reports the first few minutes forecast the outcome **far better than chance**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture Ana raising the bills with her partner. She opens with *you\u2019re hopeless with money*. He defends himself, she hits back, and an hour later nobody remembers the bills. The content barely entered into it.'},{kind:'explain',text:'A **softened start-up** has a near-mechanical shape. Describe the situation, state a feeling and make a specific request, without blame. Begin with **what happened**, not what they are, and ask for what you want.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Kofi, whose housemate keeps playing music late. He waits until Saturday morning, when he\u2019s calm. Then he says he\u2019s been sleeping badly and asks for quiet after eleven. They sort it out over breakfast.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. A soft start lowers the odds of escalation, but it doesn\u2019t make **the other person** reasonable. Some serious wrongs should be said plainly. And delay has a limit: postponed forever, a complaint simply goes unmade.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your opening sets the path more than **your argument** does. The harsh version comes easiest when you\u2019re angry. So **delay the talk**, then start with what happened and what you want.'}]},'the-argument-you-keep-having_4':{title:'The Agreed Pause',card:{insight:'All close couples fight, but the ones that last have a way back.',tryThis:'Pick one word that means you need a break, and agree on when you will come back.',skills:['relationships','communication']},review:{prompt:'You both need a break in the middle of a fight. What helps most?',right:'A stop word you both picked while you were calm',wrong:'Walking out of the room once it gets too hot',why:'A break you agreed on while calm will not look like giving up.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Lots of people think **good friends** never fight. But think of your closest friend, or your family. Have you **ever had a fight** with them? What happened after it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Say two brothers pick a word, like *pause*, while they\u2019re calm. Either one can say it in a fight, and they take twenty minutes apart. Storming off looks like **sulking**. A word you **agreed first** doesn\u2019t.'},{kind:'moral',text:'All close friends and families **fight sometimes**. The ones that work have **a way back**. Pick your pause sign while you\u2019re calm, before you need it.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Imagine a household rule. Whoever sits in **a particular chair** is asking to stop, and the other can\u2019t read anything into it. It sounds absurd. Why might it be **the smartest thing** in the house?'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'No close relationship runs without conflict. What matters is how **you stop**, and **when you agreed** it.',left:{title:'Improvised mid-row',items:['Walking out','Going quiet','Reads as a tactic']},right:{title:'Agreed in advance',items:['A word for twenty minutes','Nothing decided after eleven','Reads as a pause']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Anything done during a row gets read as **part of the row**. Walking out may be an honest need for air. But in the moment, it can\u2019t be told apart from a tactic. An agreement made earlier **takes the pause out** of the fight.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The skill isn\u2019t never fighting. It\u2019s having **a route out**, agreed while nobody is angry. Then using it says **nothing about who\u2019s winning**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Think of the worst row you\u2019ve had with someone close. At some point, one of you **walked out** or went quiet. Did the other person read it as a break, or as **a move**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The essay starts from a plain premise: **no close relationship** runs without conflict. What marks the ones that work isn\u2019t avoiding rupture. It\u2019s having **a reliable route** out of it, settled before anyone needs it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture Grace and Tom mid-row. Tom goes quiet because he\u2019s overwhelmed. Grace reads the silence as contempt, and pushes harder. Neither can see the other\u2019s intention from outside. The row goes somewhere neither meant.'},{kind:'explain',text:'That\u2019s the problem a prior agreement solves. Its forms are **unglamorous**: a word meaning *I need twenty minutes*, a rule that nothing gets decided after eleven. Each is dull on purpose. **Its meaning** was fixed earlier.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take two old friends who keep clashing about politics. Years ago they agreed a joke phrase that means *enough for tonight*. Either can say it. They change the subject, and the friendship carries on.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The essay says plainly this comes from how rows tend to go, **not a measured finding**. It leaves the underlying difference untouched. And a pause can be abused, called whenever one person is losing and never picked up again.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A pause only works if **you agreed it** while calm, and if it includes **a way back** to the subject. Settle both tonight, before you need them.'}]},'the-argument-you-keep-having_5':{title:'Heard, Not Fixed',card:{insight:'Often, people want to be heard before you fix things.',tryThis:'Next time someone tells you a problem, say back what you heard. Then ask what they want.',skills:['communication','relationships']},review:{prompt:'Your wife says her long drive to work wears her out. What comes first?',right:'Say back what you heard before you offer a fix',wrong:'Tell her about a faster way to drive to work',why:'When someone wants to be heard, a quick fix can feel like you do not care.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your friend says, *maths was awful, I got it all wrong*. You say, just ask the teacher for help. Your friend **goes quiet**. You were only **trying to help**. So what went wrong?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Your friend didn\u2019t want **a fix** yet. They wanted you to see the day was hard. When you jump straight to the answer, it can feel like **you skipped them**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Often people don\u2019t want you to **fix the problem**. They want you to see how hard it is. So **show you get it** first, then ask if they want ideas.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your partner says the commute is **wearing them down**. You suggest an earlier train and a good podcast. It\u2019s a sensible plan. They go **cold**. What just happened?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Often the other person doesn\u2019t want the thing changed. They want it **acknowledged as costly**. Offer a fix where a description was wanted, and it **reads as dismissal**. That\u2019s how a good solution makes things worse.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The two requests often arrive **in identical words**, which is why they\u2019re **so often confused**.',left:{title:'Wants a fix',items:['Wants it changed','Hears a solution as help']},right:{title:'Wants understanding',items:['Wants the cost seen','Hears a solution as dismissal']}},{kind:'explain',text:'So show you understand **before you respond**. Restate their position until they confirm you\u2019ve got it right. That isn\u2019t agreeing. You can state a view **accurately** and still reject it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'If your fix makes things worse, that\u2019s **the tell**. They wanted to be understood. Say back **what you heard** before offering any fix.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A colleague tells you, for **the third time**, that the new system is slowing their work. You have a good workaround ready. Do you **share it** now, or does something need to come first?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The essay\u2019s claim is that in a recurring argument, the real request is very often **to be understood**. It isn\u2019t to be agreed with. The two get **routinely confused**, and a good fix offered too soon lands badly.'},{kind:'story',text:'Back to the colleague. You hand over the workaround, and they go flat. To them, it said the cost wasn\u2019t worth dwelling on. Your answer was good. It just **skipped the part** they were asking for.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The practice is to **show comprehension** first. Restate their position until they confirm it\u2019s right, conceding nothing. The test is **their confirmation**, not your own sense of having listened. Only then respond.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a father whose teenage daughter says school feels pointless. He holds back the speech about exams. Instead he says back what he heard, and asks if that\u2019s it. She nods, then keeps talking for twenty minutes.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Sometimes the person **does want it changed**, and endless sympathy becomes its own dismissal. A restatement used as a technique is soon spotted. And the essay says this is often the ask, not always.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Stating a view accurately **isn\u2019t agreeing** with it. So show you understand before you answer, and **ask what they want**. Many arguments no fix could touch end right there.'}]}}}},1002,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'the-art-of-war',kind:'book',promise:'Win more by fighting less and planning first',about:'What if the smart way to win is to fight less? The Art of War is a very old book from China. It is said to be by a general, Sun Tzu. His ideas still help at work. Which will you try first?',ideas:{'the-art-of-war_1':{title:'Win Without a Fight',card:{insight:'The best win gets you what you need with no fight at all.',tryThis:'Before your next big meeting, call one person who may say no. Ask what worries them.',skills:['leadership','communication']},review:{prompt:'Two people at work do not like your plan, and you vote next week. What now?',right:'Call each one alone and ask what worries them',wrong:'Get your best points ready to win in the room',why:'Fix what they fear first, and then there is no fight left to have.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You and your brother both want the TV at seven. You could win a **big row** about it tonight. Or there\u2019s another way. Which result is **best for you**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Win the row, and you get the TV. But your brother is cross all night, and next time he fights harder. Sort it out at lunch instead, and you get the TV **and the peace**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Winning a fight is good. Getting what you need with **no fight** is better. A fight always **breaks a bit** of what you wanted.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'The meeting tomorrow will settle your plan, and two colleagues already disagree. You\u2019re up late **sharpening arguments**, rehearsing how to beat them in the room. Is that really **the best result** you can get?'},{kind:'story',text:'Say you win. The plan goes through. But the team now knows you **argue to win**, and next time they arrive armed. The win goes on the record. The damage rarely shows up on the same page.'},{kind:'story',text:'So try a different day before. You have two short talks, **one-to-one**. One colleague has a worry you can meet **early**. The other wants a change you can live with. Tomorrow you walk in with **nothing left to win**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Why does nobody do this? A **famous victory** is easy to see. A fight that never happened **leaves no trace**, so nobody praises it. That\u2019s why Sun Tzu rates the general who never needed a siege above one who won a famous siege.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Every fight **damages the prize** a little. So the best version of tomorrow is decided today, **quietly and early**. Aim to walk in with nothing left to fight over.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A war book, you\u2019d think, would praise the general who wins **every battle**. Sun Tzu\u2019s third chapter opens with a ranking instead. Where do you think the famous **unbeaten general** lands on it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Taking a state whole beats shattering it. Capturing an army intact beats destroying it. Then the turn: winning every battle **isn\u2019t the summit**. The summit is breaking the other side\u2019s resistance **without fighting** at all.'},{kind:'explain',text:'This is **accounting, not pacifism**. Sun Tzu still wants the other side subdued. His point is that a fight\u2019s cost is real but **booked somewhere else**. So you rarely see it next to the win.'},{kind:'story',text:'You squeeze a supplier hard and win the negotiation. The price is great on paper. Before long they\u2019re quietly looking for the exit, and your best terms leave with them.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a neighbour and a new shared fence. Before any formal request, you knock and ask what worries them about it. It\u2019s the light in their garden. You move the fence a little, and there\u2019s nothing left to argue about.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. A colleague isn\u2019t an enemy, so only **real agreement** counts, never quiet pressure. Settle everything in private and the meeting becomes theatre. Some disagreement **needs airing**, because it\u2019s information.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A won fight is **second best**, because it spends what you fought for. The higher skill gets the result **with the prize intact**, and nobody ever notices.'}]},'the-art-of-war_2':{title:'Guard Your Side First',card:{insight:'Not losing is up to you, but winning waits for their mistake.',tryThis:'Write the one weak spot that would hurt most if it broke this month. Then fix it.',skills:['career','thinking']},review:{prompt:'You plan to ask for a raise next month. What do you do first?',right:'Fix any weak spot in your work your boss could point to',wrong:'Wait for a day when your boss is in a great mood',why:'You can fix your own weak spots, but you cannot pick your boss\u2019s mood.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re playing draughts with a friend. You spot a **clever jump** and go for it. Then your friend takes three of your pieces in one go. What did you **forget to check**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'There are **two jobs**, in order. First, guard your own pieces. That part is **all yours**, and you can do it now. Winning needs your friend to slip up. You can\u2019t make that happen, so it waits.'},{kind:'moral',text:'First make sure **you can\u2019t lose**. Then keep your eyes open and wait for **their mistake**. Waiting like that isn\u2019t doing nothing.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve spent all week on a bold pitch to a **new client**. It\u2019s good. But you haven\u2019t once looked at your **biggest client\u2019s contract**, which runs out next month. Where should this week have gone?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Sun Tzu splits any contest into **two separate jobs**. First, put yourself **beyond defeat**. That job is entirely in your hands. Second, wait for a chance to win. That one only the other side can give you, through their own mistake.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Most people pour effort into **what\u2019s theirs** and leave **what\u2019s yours** wide open.',left:{title:'In your hands',items:['Your weakest point','Your cash runway','Your own worst argument']},right:{title:'In theirs',items:['Their mistake','Their bad moment','The opening you need']}},{kind:'story',text:'So you **close the gap** first. You call the big client, fix the two things they\u2019ve grumbled about, and renew. Now the bold pitch is a bonus. If it fails, nothing breaks.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Not losing is **your job**, and you can start it now. Winning waits for **their slip**. Do the part you own first, then watch.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture an opponent who is careful and **never slips**. Sun Tzu\u2019s fourth chapter has a blunt answer about what you can do against them. Can you guess **what it is**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'He describes the skilled fighters of old doing **two things in order**. First they made themselves **impossible to defeat**. Then they waited until the enemy could be beaten. The first lies in your own hands. The second comes from the enemy\u2019s errors.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take a career. Priya chases a flashy side project to get noticed. Meanwhile her core work slips. When a restructure comes, she\u2019s the easy one to cut. She tried to be **hard to ignore** before she was **hard to remove**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a small business. One owner spends on growth with almost no cash in the bank. Another builds **runway first**, then grows. When a slow month hits, only one of them is still around to keep growing.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Waiting has a cost: the book itself warns that long campaigns drain everything. The rival may **never slip**. And most people you deal with aren\u2019t enemies, so a colleague\u2019s mistake isn\u2019t an opening to use.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Against a careful rival, you **can\u2019t force** a win. You can still make sure you **don\u2019t lose**, and that half carries over to almost everything.'}]},'the-art-of-war_3':{title:'Won Before It Starts',card:{insight:'Most wins are decided by the work you do before the day.',tryThis:'Before your next big buy, look up the price. Then write down the most you will pay.',skills:['thinking','career']},review:{prompt:'Your talk with a car seller starts in an hour. What now?',right:'Look up the price and pick the most you will pay',wrong:'Trust yourself to find a good deal once you are there',why:'Most of the deal is won by the work you do first.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'It\u2019s **sports day**. Two kids line up for the same race, on the same track, in the same shoes. The whistle blows. Do you think the race is really **where it\u2019s decided**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Sam learns the spelling words all week. Jo opens the test paper on Friday and hopes the words will come. Most of them don\u2019t. The test didn\u2019t decide it. The week did.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Do the work that **decides it** before the day comes. Then the test, the game or the race just **shows the win** you already made.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'The negotiation starts in an hour. You know what you want. You don\u2019t know your **walk-away point**, or what happens to them if you leave. Your plan is to **read the room**. Will that be enough?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Sun Tzu says the winner **seeks battle** only once the victory is secured. The loser fights first, then looks for a way to win. It sounds like a riddle. It\u2019s really **a description** of a process.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Position, resources, information and timing are all settled **before it begins**. So the negotiator who lost rarely lost in the room. They lost the week before, when they **skipped the thinking**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Here\u2019s **the test**: can you say **why you\u2019ll win** before you start?',left:{title:'A plan',items:['You can say why you\u2019ll win','Built the week before','Survives a bad hour']},right:{title:'A hope',items:['You\u2019ll find a way','Built in the room','Needs a good hour']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Improvising feels like courage, but it\u2019s usually **missing preparation**. If you can\u2019t say why you\u2019ll win, **you\u2019re not ready** to begin.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Sun Tzu\u2019s fourth chapter sets **two generals** side by side. One is destined to win, the other to lose. Only **one thing** differs between them. What could it be?'},{kind:'explain',text:'It\u2019s **the order**. The winner secures victory first, then seeks battle. The loser seeks battle first, then looks for victory. By the time armies meet, position, supply, information and timing have **already decided** most of it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take a negotiation that goes badly. The person who lost arrived with no walk-away number and no idea of the other side\u2019s options. They planned to think on their feet. The meeting just showed a result settled the week before.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now you\u2019re pitching a new process to a sceptical team. You learn every objection first and answer each one quietly. When you finally propose it, the meeting is short. The deciding happened before anyone sat down.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some contests can\u2019t wait until you\u2019re sure: you\u2019re attacked, or the window closes. In new fields, **starting small** is how you learn if you can win at all. And a fluent reason isn\u2019t proof.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The battle **reveals** what was decided before it. So commit only when the earlier work **favours you**, and treat a small test as part of that work.'}]},'the-art-of-war_4':{title:'Make No Big Mistakes',card:{insight:'You win long games by making no big mistakes.',tryThis:'Look at your next big plan. Write the one mistake in it you could not undo.',skills:['thinking','career']},review:{prompt:'Your team must pick between two plans. Which question should you add?',right:'Which plan could hurt us in a way we cannot undo?',wrong:'Which plan could win us the most if it works?',why:'Judge a plan by the worst that could happen, not just the best.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two goalkeepers try out for your team. One makes **amazing diving saves**, but lets in a silly goal most games. The other never dives, and never lets in a silly goal. Who would **you pick**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The diving looks great, and people cheer. But one silly goal can **lose the game**. It\u2019s like a tower of blocks. Building it took an hour. **One clumsy knock** undoes it in a second.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You often win by **not making big mistakes**, not by doing amazing things. The calm goalie wins more games, even though **nobody claps**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'The bold plan is on the table, and the room is excited. It could **double the business**. Nobody has asked what happens if it lands badly, because asking feels like **the wrong mood**. Would you?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Sun Tzu describes his ideal general flatly: he wins by **making no mistakes**. No brilliance, no famous move. Over a long game, your results depend less on your best decision than on **your worst**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The sums are lopsided, so sort mistakes into **two kinds** before you **commit to a plan**.',left:{title:'You can recover',items:['A launch that slips','A pitch that lands flat']},right:{title:'You can\u2019t undo',items:['Betting the whole budget','Losing your key client']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Why does this lose arguments? Avoiding errors **sounds unambitious** in a room that wants to impress. Yet one serious error can **erase a run** of good calls. Recovering usually costs more than the original gain.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The bold plan is only open to someone who **hasn\u2019t already lost** their position. So ask what each plan could win, and which mistake you **couldn\u2019t undo**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Think of the commanders history remembers. They\u2019re famous for **brilliant moves**. Sun Tzu\u2019s ideal general has none. He earns no name for wisdom and no **praise for courage**. Why would that be the one he rates highest?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Because he wins by **making no mistakes**, and nothing dramatic happens. It fits chapter four. You make yourself unbeatable and wait for their error. But your opponent is **waiting for yours** too. Give them none.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture two people doing the same job for years. One makes a few dazzling calls and one ruinous one. The other makes steady, careful calls and no disasters. Only the first gets talked about. Only the second is **still standing**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a hillside. Lots of walkers charged straight up and slid back down, leaving long scars in the grass. One walker skips the dramatic climb. She looks for the route with **no fall in it**, and reaches the top.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Avoiding mistakes isn\u2019t **avoiding risk**. A commander who never moves has made a mistake too, just a slow one. And most of life forgives more than a battlefield. Learning needs small, **recoverable errors**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Guard hardest against mistakes you **can\u2019t undo**. Accept the small ones as **the price** of acting. Then let your record be quietly, boringly free of disasters.'}]},'the-art-of-war_5':{title:'First on the Field',card:{insight:'How you show up depends on when you leave.',tryThis:'Pick the biggest thing in your next two weeks. Put time to get ready for it on your calendar now.',skills:['productivity','career']},review:{prompt:'A big pitch is ten days away, and this week is packed. What now?',right:'Put time to get ready on your calendar now',wrong:'Handle this week first, then get ready once things calm down',why:'Busy weeks stay busy, so save that time now.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Mia and Leo are catching the same bus for a **school trip**. Mia leaves home early and walks. Leo gets up late and **runs all the way**. Who do you think enjoys the first hour more?'},{kind:'story',text:'Leo is hot and out of breath, and he\u2019s forgotten his lunch. Mia is calm and ready for anything. It\u2019s the same bus and the same trip. But they\u2019re starting it in **two different states**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Getting there **early and ready** gives you a big edge. It\u2019s decided by when you set off, **long before** the thing begins.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two people walk into the same meeting with the same skills. One has been there **twenty minutes** and read everything. The other arrives running and skims the papers on the way in. Are they in the **same meeting**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Sun Tzu opens chapter six with two armies heading for the same battle. Whoever holds the field first and waits will be **fresh**. Whoever arrives second and has to hurry will be **exhausted**. Same ground, same numbers.'},{kind:'story',text:'Two candidates, one job. One spent an evening on the company: its products, its problems, the person interviewing. The other read the advert on the train. Neither difference shows on the timetable. Both decide the interview.'},{kind:'explain',text:'So why do you keep arriving late? Preparing for something that **hasn\u2019t happened yet** always looks less urgent than something on fire. So it loses, every week, unless you **protect it on purpose**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'This isn\u2019t about being a good person. It\u2019s about **the state you\u2019re in** when it starts. Book the early start **before the week fills**, and guard it.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture a camp. The kit was laid down hours ago and a cloak is spread out to rest on. On the horizon, **a line of dust** is still coming. Which side would **you rather be**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Sun Tzu goes further than **being punctual**. The clever fighter, he says, makes the enemy come to him rather than being made to go. Arriving first gives you that choice. You **pick the ground** and rest.'},{kind:'story',text:'At a weekend market, one trader sets up her stall before dawn. She takes the corner spot by the entrance and has coffee before anyone comes. Another arrives with the crowd, hurries to unpack, and gets the last pitch behind the bins.'},{kind:'story',text:'Or two football teams playing an away match. One travels the day before and sleeps near the ground. The other drives through the night and walks straight onto the pitch. Same rules, same field. Different legs.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. First isn\u2019t always best. Arriving early can mean committing before you know where the contest is, or **showing your hand**. And springing a meeting on someone to tire them turns the idea into **a trick**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The point isn\u2019t earliness for its own sake. It\u2019s the state **you arrive in**. Set off early enough that you\u2019re **rested when it starts**.'}]},'the-art-of-war_6':{title:'Keep Them Guessing',card:{insight:'If they cannot guess your next move, they must guard it all.',tryThis:'Before your next deal, pick one thing to keep to yourself. Say it only when it helps you.',skills:['communication','thinking']},review:{prompt:'A recruiter asks early what pay you would take. What do you do?',right:'Keep your number to yourself until they name one',wrong:'Name a fair number right away so you seem open',why:'Once you say your number, each new offer starts there.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You always kick your penalties to the left. The goalie has noticed. You run up, you kick, and the goalie **dives left**. What just happened, and how could you **stop it**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'If the goalie knows your side, they **guard just one**. If they don\u2019t know, they must guard both, so each side is weaker. It\u2019s like rock, paper, scissors. Always play rock, and your friend **always wins**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When the other side **can\u2019t guess** your next move, they have to watch everything. In a game, that\u2019s fair. Lying about **real things** is different.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A recruiter asks, **early and pleasantly**, what salary you\u2019d accept. You want to seem open, so you **name a number**. From that moment, where does the rest of the conversation happen?'},{kind:'story',text:'Below it. Every offer now starts from your number and works down. You gave away **the one thing** they most wanted to know, for nothing, just to seem friendly. Once said, it\u2019s spent.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Sun Tzu measures skill by what the other side **can\u2019t work out**. If they don\u2019t know where you\u2019ll act, they must cover every place at once. So they\u2019re weaker everywhere, though **you haven\u2019t grown** at all.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'This isn\u2019t lying. It\u2019s simply **not giving away** what **you value most**.',left:{title:'Fair to hold',items:['Your walk-away number','Which term matters most','The trade you\u2019d make first']},right:{title:'Not fair',items:['Inventing a rival offer','A deadline you don\u2019t have','Faking anger to scare them']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Most people give their intentions away **for free**, out of a wish to seem open. Hold your priorities back until **a trade** is on the table. Then spend them.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture a valley at night. On the far side, **rows of fires** burn beside rows of tents. Nobody is sitting at any of them. If you were the enemy, **where would you attack**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'In chapter six, Sun Tzu defines skill in attack as leaving the enemy **unsure what to defend**. Skill in defence leaves them unsure what to attack. Strength, speed and numbers **aren\u2019t even mentioned**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'It pairs with his blunt claim in chapter one that warfare **rests on deception**. Outside war, the useful core is narrower. You control what can be **read about your intentions**, and nothing obliges you to publish it early.'},{kind:'story',text:'Every empty fire is a place the enemy must still watch. They spread their troops across the whole valley to cover them all. **Each place gets less**. The army they finally meet is a divided one.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a supplier asks which of three terms matters most to you. You discuss all three without ranking them. They have to bargain hard on each, and when a trade finally comes, **your ranking** still has value.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Withholding and misleading are different acts. Not volunteering your priorities is fair. Building **a false picture** for someone who relies on you is lying. And among friends and colleagues, a name for being unreadable **costs trust**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Ambiguity **divides their strength** without adding to yours. Hold back what you value, but **never invent** what isn\u2019t true. That line is the whole idea.'}]},'the-art-of-war_7':{title:'Many Against Few',card:{insight:'You do not need to be bigger, just bigger where it counts.',tryThis:'List what you are spreading your time over this week. Pick one to go all in on.',skills:['productivity','leadership']},review:{prompt:'You run a small team up against a much bigger rival. What is your plan?',right:'Be the best at one thing the rival does badly',wrong:'Try to match the big rival on all it does',why:'You cannot be bigger in all things, but you can win at one.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re watering the garden with a hose. On spray, it wets the whole lawn **a little**. Then you twist the nozzle to jet. Why can the **same water** suddenly blast mud off a wall?'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture your week. You have a model to build, a poem to learn, a room to tidy and a song to practise. You do ten minutes of each, every day. By Friday, **none of them** is done.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You don\u2019t have to be strong **everywhere**. Put most of your effort in **one place**, and let some things wait. That one thing gets done.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your team has **six priorities** this quarter. Each gets a sixth of the week, and each one is easy to defend. Everyone is busy. So why is **none of them** finished?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Sun Tzu\u2019s answer is to keep your force **in one body** while the enemy splits up. Then your whole meets **a fraction** of theirs. You don\u2019t need more than a rival in total. You need more where the contest happens.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a small bakery up against a supermarket. It can\u2019t match the range or the prices. So it becomes the best in town at **one thing** the supermarket does badly: proper bread. On that point, it\u2019s the bigger force.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The hard part isn\u2019t understanding this. It\u2019s choosing to be **visibly weak** somewhere, on purpose. Leaving one thing underserved so another can be overwhelming **feels like negligence**. It only reads as focus afterwards.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Spreading effort evenly is the surest way to be **weak everywhere** while feeling busy. Choose your point, and choose **what you leave thin**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A wall stands with ladders heaped beneath its **strongest stretch**, abandoned. A lone figure walks past them, calmly, towards a gap further along. Why would anyone **give up** on the ladders?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Chapter six sets two thoughts side by side. Keep your force **as one body** while the enemy is made to divide. And like water running downhill, avoid what\u2019s strong and **strike what\u2019s weak**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Neither side\u2019s total changes. Only the sums at **the point of contact** do. Being hard to read makes them spread out to cover everything. Concentration then meets **one of their parts** with all of yours.'},{kind:'story',text:'The ladders were built for the strongest part of the wall, where the defenders massed. The attacker leaves them. He takes everything he has to the gap, where only a few can stand against him.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a start-up with three markets in view. It could enter all three thinly. Instead it puts everyone into one, and lets the other two wait. In that one market, it now fields a bigger team than any rival.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Concentration is a bet. Whatever you leave thin is exposed, and pick the wrong point and you\u2019ve **lost everywhere at once**. Some things can\u2019t be left thin: safety, the law, **your people**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Win **where contact happens**, not in the totals. Being unreadable divides them, and concentration uses the division. Just **choose on purpose** what you leave thin.'}]},'the-art-of-war_8':{title:'The Price of Delay',card:{insight:'Time is not free, and each day you wait costs you.',tryThis:'For one project, write what one more month would cost and what it would add.',skills:['productivity','thinking']},review:{prompt:'Your team wants one more month to polish a report. What do you ask first?',right:'What will the extra month cost, and what will it add?',wrong:'How much better will the report be with more time?',why:'More time always costs something, so count the cost too.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re making a poster for a class project. You redo the title **four times** to get it just right. By Thursday you\u2019re bored of it. What\u2019s happened to **the rest** of the poster?'},{kind:'explain',text:'It\u2019s still blank. The longer a job drags on, the more it **uses up**: your time, your energy, your interest. It\u2019s like an ice lolly you can\u2019t decide how to bite. All the while, **it\u2019s melting**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A good job that **gets done** can beat a perfect one that never does. That doesn\u2019t mean rush. It means **time isn\u2019t free**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You can launch today at **eighty per cent**, or in three months at ninety. The team leans towards waiting. It feels like the careful choice. What are you **not counting**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Sun Tzu\u2019s second chapter is about **how long** a war lasts. Slow victory dulls weapons and **sags spirits**, he argues, and a long campaign drains the state. He knows of no country that gained from a long war.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Put both sides of the delay in **the same units** before you decide what **it\u2019s worth**.',left:{title:'What it adds',items:['A more polished product','Fewer known flaws']},right:{title:'What it costs',items:['Three more months of salaries','A team gone stale','A window a rival takes']}},{kind:'explain',text:'So should you **just hurry**? No. He grants that foolish haste exists. His claim is narrower: duration is **a cost** like any other, and most planning treats it as free.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Enthusiasm, money and attention all **run down** with time. So a better, slower plan can lose to a worse one that **actually finishes**. Price the delay before you take it.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Before a single battle, chapter two **adds up the bill**: chariots, armour, supplies and a heavy daily outlay of silver. Then it keeps counting. What do you think happens to that bill **every extra day**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Every day the campaign lasts is **paid for**. The money, the weapons and the soldiers\u2019 spirit all wear down. And the slower progress gets, the longer it runs, so **the delay feeds itself**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take one more month of polish on a product. It\u2019s paid from the account that funds the launch: budget, team energy, the patience of people waiting. Priced that way, the month often costs more than **the polish is worth**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Or a deal you keep stalling to get slightly better terms. Month after month you ask for one more change. The other side\u2019s patience wears thin, a rival steps in, and **the window closes**. You were still negotiating a deal that no longer existed.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some work **rewards patience**: research, trust, anything that compounds. A drug trial wanting another year of safety data should get it. Rushing there passes the cost to others. And the idea easily excuses **sloppy work**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A long effort eats **the very means** it depends on. Delay is never free, and neither is haste. Before you wait, **price both sides**.'}]},'the-art-of-war_9':{title:'Weigh Both Sides First',card:{insight:'You think better in a calm hour before than in the rush.',tryThis:'Before your next big choice, list what each side has going for it. Go one point at a time.',skills:['thinking','leadership']},review:{prompt:'Your boss has a hard talk with a client on Friday. What helps her most?',right:'Listing what each side has going for it the day before',wrong:'Trusting herself to think on her feet in the room',why:'People decide better in a calm hour than in the rush.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your team has a **big match** tomorrow. You could plan what to do while you\u2019re running about on the pitch. Or you could plan tonight, **sitting somewhere quiet**. Which plan do you think works better?'},{kind:'story',text:'Tonight you write two lists. One says what your team is good at. The other says what their team is good at. You make a plan that uses both. You\u2019re not cleverer at home, just **not rushed**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Do your **big thinking** early, somewhere quiet. In the moment, you\u2019re rushed and people are watching. Before, **you have time** to see clearly.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'The difficult conversation is on Friday. It\u2019s Tuesday, and nothing is written down. You figure you\u2019ll **read the room** on the day. When will your judgement about it actually be **at its best**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Today, while nothing is happening. Judgement under pressure **is unreliable**. Judgement in advance isn\u2019t. You\u2019re not smarter on Tuesday. You\u2019re just **not being rushed**, which is most of the difference.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Sun Tzu ends his first chapter on this. The winning general, he says, does **many calculations** in a temple before the battle. Earlier he lists them: five constant factors, then **seven comparisons** between the two sides.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The checklist moves **the slow work** to a calm hour, so you can **act fast** later.',left:{title:'In the moment',items:['Rushed','People watching','Easy to panic']},right:{title:'Beforehand, in quiet',items:['Time to think','No audience','Easy to compare']}},{kind:'moral',text:'The question isn\u2019t how well you **think on your feet**. It\u2019s how much you\u2019ve arranged **not to have to**. Weigh it up while nothing is happening.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Before any campaign, Sun Tzu pictures the general **in a temple**. No enemy, no field, no hurry. Just a quiet room and **two sides** to weigh. Why would a war book begin there, before any tactics?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Because the general who calculates much wins, and the one who calculates little loses. He lists **five constant factors**: unity of ruler and people, weather and seasons, terrain, the commander\u2019s qualities, and discipline. Then come **seven comparisons**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a manager facing a tough budget meeting. On Tuesday she sits alone and writes two columns: her case and theirs, factor by factor. She spots **her weakest point** and plans for it. On Friday, she barely has to think.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a team captain before a final. He lays out **one stone per factor** for each side on a table: fitness, form, the pitch, the weather. Seeing them side by side, he changes the plan before anyone kicks a ball.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. A checklist weighs only what\u2019s on it, and a neat comparison built on **poor information** is still wrong. Calculation can turn into delay. And before a hard talk with a friend, weigh **the problem**, not your strengths against theirs.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You\u2019re not smarter **in the temple**. You\u2019re just not rushed, and that\u2019s most of the gain. So decide in advance, in quiet, by **plain comparison**.'}]},'the-art-of-war_10':{title:'Ready Either Way',card:{insight:'If your plan needs bad luck to stay away, it is just a hope.',tryThis:'Find one thing your plan counts on going right. Write what you would do if it went wrong.',skills:['thinking','career']},review:{prompt:'A small business gets most of its money from one loyal client. What now?',right:'Plan what you would do if that client went away',wrong:'Keep that client so happy they would not think of leaving',why:'A happy client can still leave, so have other work ready.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve played a computer game for **weeks**. You\u2019ve never kept a spare copy of your save, because it\u2019s never been lost. Then one morning you switch it on, and the screen says **no save found**. Now what?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Weeks of play, gone, and a copy would have taken a minute. Thinking *it probably won\u2019t happen* can be right for ages, then wrong once. **The once** is what hurts. Being ready **works either way**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t count on the bad thing **not happening**. Count on **being ready** if it does. That part is always up to you.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your whole pipeline runs through one person who has **never once** let you down. Nobody else knows how their part works. You\u2019ve never needed anyone else to. So **what\u2019s the risk**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Sun Tzu\u2019s eighth chapter says what a general **may rely on**. Not the chance the enemy stays away, but his own readiness to meet them. Not hope they won\u2019t attack, but a position that **can\u2019t be taken**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'A forecast and a preparation can look alike, but only **one of them** is **yours to control**.',left:{title:'A forecast',items:['Right for years','Wrong once, and it counts']},right:{title:'A preparation',items:['Works whatever happens','Survives the once']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Look for the bets you didn\u2019t notice placing. No backup, because the drive probably won\u2019t fail. No second supplier, because prices probably **won\u2019t rise**. Each is **a quiet bet** that the world stays ordinary.'},{kind:'moral',text:'If your plan needs someone else **to hold back**, it\u2019s a hope. If it can **survive them acting**, it\u2019s a plan. Build for the second kind.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'The sky is clear and the sea is flat. The forecast couldn\u2019t be better. Yet a man is setting **the last stone** into his storm wall. Is he wasting **a perfect day**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Sun Tzu would say no. **Confidence belongs** in preparations, not forecasts. A forecast can be right for years and then wrong once. A wall **doesn\u2019t care** whether you guessed the weather right.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take a shop with one supplier and a strong contract. The contract makes walking away unlikely. It doesn\u2019t make it impossible. A second supplier, already tested, works **whether or not** the first one stays.'},{kind:'story',text:'Or your income. Your main contract will probably renew, so you spend what comes in. It renews for years. Then one year it doesn\u2019t, and there\u2019s nothing set aside. The forecast was right **every time but one**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. You can\u2019t make every position unassailable. Readiness costs money and attention, and a plan defended against everything **does nothing else**. Pushed too far, the idea becomes paranoia and treats every partner **as an enemy**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You only own **one kind** of confidence: the kind that survives being wrong. So choose **which storms** are bad enough to build for, and build for those.'}]},'the-art-of-war_11':{title:'Ask Someone Who Knows',card:{insight:'Your past shows what used to be true, not what is true now.',tryThis:'Write the one guess your plan leans on most. Then ask the people it is for what they need.',skills:['thinking','leadership']},review:{prompt:'A city plans a bus route from ten-year-old travel numbers. What should it do?',right:'Ask the people who will ride it where they need to go',wrong:'Trust the old numbers, since people do not change much',why:'Old numbers are a guess, and the riders know where they need to go.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re starting at a **new school**. You want to know which teacher is strict. You could guess from the teachers at your old school, or **wait and see**. Is there a better way to find out?'},{kind:'story',text:'Ask a kid who\u2019s been there a year. Your old school tells you what teachers are often like. It can\u2019t tell you about *this* teacher. The kid who\u2019s sat in her class knows, and asking takes a minute.'},{kind:'moral',text:'To know what\u2019s **really going on**, ask a person who knows. Your past tells you what usually happens. It **can\u2019t tell you** what\u2019s happening now.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re about to commit a whole quarter to what the market **probably wants**. The plan rests on guesses made inside the building. Everyone agrees with it. What\u2019s **missing** from that room?'},{kind:'story',text:'The customers are. A conversation with five actual customers was available **the whole time**. Nobody had it, because the team trusted **its own experience**. That experience said what usually happens, not what these people want now.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Sun Tzu\u2019s final chapter makes the same point about war. Knowledge of the enemy **can\u2019t come** from spirits or from comparing past battles. It only comes from **people who actually know** the situation.'},{kind:'explain',text:'He\u2019s scathing about the economics. Armies face each other for years over a victory **settled in a day**. Yet commanders grudge the cost of finding out. The information is almost always **cheaper than the ignorance**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your experience tells you what usually happens. For what **this situation** holds this week, ask **someone who knows**. It\u2019s almost always cheaper than guessing.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'On the table lies a fine printed map of the ground ahead. Beside it, someone has dropped a **small muddy sketch**, drawn this morning. They disagree about **the river**. Which one would you trust?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The map shows the ground **as it usually is**. The sketch shows it as someone saw it today. Sun Tzu\u2019s last chapter turns on this. He calls it **foreknowledge**: knowing the enemy\u2019s situation before you act.'},{kind:'explain',text:'It can\u2019t come from spirits, from comparing past events, or **from calculation**. The chapter is about spies, sorted by how they\u2019re recruited. The principle underneath is broader: knowledge of the other side **comes from people**.'},{kind:'story',text:'A founder is sure customers want a feature, because her last company\u2019s customers did. That\u2019s inference by comparison. It tells her what customers are often like. Only **these customers** can tell her what they want.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now you\u2019re choosing a restaurant from a review written ten years ago. It praises the chef and the quiet room. A friend ate there last week and is one message away. Which one knows the place **as it is now**?'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. People can be wrong, partial or lying, and Sun Tzu knew it. Part of his chapter is about **feeding false reports** to the enemy. So treat a source as evidence to weigh. And ask people openly. **Don\u2019t spy** on them.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Comparison with the past tells you what\u2019s usual. Only **a person who knows** can tell you what\u2019s true **right now**. Asking is nearly always cheaper.'}]},'the-art-of-war_12':{title:'Pull Back Without Shame',card:{insight:'Decide based on what is happening, not on how it will look.',tryThis:'Name one thing you keep doing just because stopping looks bad. Ask if you would stop if no one knew.',skills:['leadership','career']},review:{prompt:'Your big project is failing, and everyone knows you pushed for it. Which test helps?',right:'Would I make this call if nobody knew who made it?',wrong:'Will ending it now look worse than ending it later?',why:'Take the crowd away, and only the facts are left to decide on.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You told everyone you\u2019d do a **hard magic trick** at the school show. In practice it keeps going wrong. An easier trick would work fine. But then everyone would know you **gave up**. What do you do?'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'There are **two traps** here, and both are about **the people watching**.',left:{title:'Showing off',items:['I want people to clap','So I pick the flashy thing']},right:{title:'Saving face',items:['I don\u2019t want to look silly','So I keep doing what fails']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Go for things because **they\u2019re worth it**. Stop things when they **stop working**, even if people see. Decide as if nobody would ever know.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You **argued publicly** for a project. It\u2019s now clearly failing, and everyone can see that you know. The work already spent is gone either way. So what\u2019s **really keeping** it alive?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Sun Tzu prizes the general who moves forward **without chasing glory** and pulls back **without dreading shame**. His only concern is protecting the country and serving it well. Such a commander, he says, is a treasure.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The two halves name **two failures**, and the second is **more common**.',left:{title:'Advancing for fame',items:['Fights because winning looks good','Picks fights not worth winning']},right:{title:'Fearing disgrace',items:['Continues because stopping shows','Pays to avoid admitting it']}},{kind:'explain',text:'It\u2019s sunk cost **with a reputation**. Once quitting has a social price, people keep paying a real one to avoid it. The audience is usually real. But how a call looks **can\u2019t be an input** to it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Decide on **the situation**, not the audience. Advance because the prize is worth it. Retreat when you should, **even if it\u2019s seen**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A climber is a short way below a bare summit. A storm is rolling in. The flag is still rolled on her pack, and planting it **would look great**. Instead she turns **and heads down**. Was that a loss?'},{kind:'explain',text:'In chapter ten, after listing how a general\u2019s faults ruin an army, Sun Tzu names the one **worth most**. He advances without seeking glory and retreats without fearing shame. He thinks only of **protecting the people**.'},{kind:'story',text:'The climber\u2019s retreat is visible. Everyone below will see the flag come back unplanted. She **decides on the storm** anyway, not on the story people will tell. That\u2019s the choice Sun Tzu is describing.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a manager who launched a product line **with fanfare**. It\u2019s losing money every month. Closing it would be visible, so it continues. The losses grow, which makes closing look **even worse**, so it continues again.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Sometimes reputation is real: a retreat can embolden an opponent or **cost trust** you\u2019ll need. Then how it looks is part of the situation. And a commitment others rely on is more than face. Quitting can **let people down**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A commander deciding for appearances **decides worse**, and loses the reputation too. So ask yourself one thing: would you make **this same call** if nobody ever learned who made it?'}]}}}},1003,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'the-attention-budget',kind:'book',promise:'Give your best focus to what matters most',about:'Do you have time for your work, but not the focus? This short article from the Mindroll team treats focus like money you get each day. These five ideas show where it leaks away. Where is your focus going today?',ideas:{'the-attention-budget_1':{title:'The Four Good Hours',card:{insight:'You get only a few sharp hours a day, so save them for hard work.',tryThis:'Tonight, find your three sharpest hours. Put your hardest task in them before anything else.',skills:['focus','productivity']},review:{prompt:'Your mind is sharpest from eight to eleven. What goes in those hours?',right:'Your hardest task, before email gets those hours',wrong:'Clearing your email first, so your head is clear for the hard task',why:'Your few sharp hours should go to hard work, since easy work can wait.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You have a whole Saturday to build a **tricky model kit**. You start at nine and keep going. By teatime the pieces don\u2019t fit and you\u2019re **making mistakes**. What went wrong?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Your brain has only **a few strong hours** of hard thinking each day. People who kept careful notes on their work found about **three to five**. After that, you can keep going, but the work gets sloppier.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your strong hours are like **pocket money**. Once they\u2019re spent, they\u2019re gone. So spend them on **the hardest thing** first, and save easy jobs for later.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'It\u2019s three in the afternoon. You\u2019ve answered forty emails and sat through **two meetings**. Now you open the hardest thing on your list, and **nothing comes**. Where did your good thinking go?'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Priya, who writes reports for a living. She spends each morning on email, because it feels urgent and quick. She starts the big report after lunch. By four she\u2019s rereading the same page and getting nowhere.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Writers, researchers and composers who kept records show a pattern. They manage **three to five hours** of demanding work a day, then hit **diminishing returns**. Research on violinists found practice clustering near the same ceiling. Wanting it more doesn\u2019t seem to stretch it.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Priya\u2019s day had **the same hours** either way. Only the **allocation** changed.',left:{title:'Her old day',items:['Good hours on email','Tired hours on the report','Report done badly']},right:{title:'A budgeted day',items:['Good hours on the report','Tired hours on email','Email barely suffers']}},{kind:'moral',text:'For hard work, the limit isn\u2019t time but **attention**. You get a few good hours, not a whole day. Ask what gets them, and give them to **the hardest task**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Most productivity advice tries to give you **more hours**: earlier starts, tighter calendars, faster habits. Yet the hard work still stalls by mid-afternoon. What if time was never **the real limit**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The article\u2019s claim is that demanding work draws on **a budget**, not a clock. You get roughly three to five good hours a day. One suggested reason is **depletion**, though that idea is contested. So the question is allocation, not extension.'},{kind:'story',text:'Think of the violinists in the research the article leans on. However ambitious or disciplined they were, their daily **deliberate practice** clustered around the same ceiling. Writers, researchers and composers who kept records show a similar pattern.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Sam, who builds software. For years he cleared messages first and coded after lunch. Then he swapped them. The code got his fresh mornings, and the messages got the tired afternoons. Same hours, new allocation.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The evidence is strongest for **demanding cognitive work**, and much of a working day isn\u2019t that. The ceiling comes mainly from violinists, so treat four hours as a planning estimate. It doesn\u2019t mean nobody should work longer.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You can\u2019t stretch **the good hours** by wanting them more. You can only choose **where they go**. Give them to the hardest thing before anything else claims them.'}]},'the-attention-budget_2':{title:'The Switching Fee',card:{insight:'When you switch tasks, part of your mind stays on the last one.',tryThis:'Today, close your email for one full hour while you work. Then check it once.',skills:['focus','productivity']},review:{prompt:'You glance at a message in the middle of writing. What costs you most?',right:'The minutes it takes to find your place again',wrong:'The few seconds you spend reading the message',why:'A look takes seconds, but finding your place takes minutes.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re reading a book for school. Your phone **buzzes**, and you look for a few seconds. Then you go back, and you read **the same line** three times. Why won\u2019t it stick?'},{kind:'explain',text:'When you switch, part of your mind **stays behind** on the first thing. Looking takes seconds. But finding your place again takes **a few minutes**. No clock shows those minutes, because you never left your chair.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Each switch costs **more than it seems**. Twenty quick looks can eat a whole afternoon. So keep the phone away and check it **once, at the end**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You were at your desk from **nine to six**. You answered things, joined calls and kept busy all day. Yet when you try to name what you **actually made**, there\u2019s nothing to point to. How?'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture Dan writing a tricky paragraph. A message pings, and he glances for three seconds. When he turns back, he has to find his place and **rebuild the argument** in his head. By the article\u2019s estimate, that takes closer to three minutes.'},{kind:'explain',text:'It\u2019s sometimes called **attention residue**. Move on before a task is finished, and part of your attention stays with it. The lab effect is well documented. Its size across a real day is **harder to measure**, but the direction isn\u2019t in dispute.'},{kind:'explain',text:'So twenty small interruptions don\u2019t cost twenty small amounts. They cost **the long stretch** that would have produced the work. Trying harder inside the fragments won\u2019t help. The fix is **fewer fragments**, with switches batched into a few set moments.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Each switch feels instant, so you **never see the fee**. Across a day it adds up to busy hours with nothing made. Batch your switches, and **long stretches** can exist again.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Count every glance at a message or a tab in **two hours** of work. Most people guess a handful. Each one felt like nothing at the time. Now ask what each one **really cost** you.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The article\u2019s claim is that switching charges **an invisible fee**. Leave a task unfinished and part of your attention stays with it, so the next task suffers. The return then costs minutes, not seconds. Across a day, **the fees compound**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Ana, drafting a report. She checks one email, then a chat, then a news tab. Each look takes seconds. Each return means finding her place and **rebuilding the thought**. By lunch she\u2019s written two paragraphs, and she\u2019s exhausted.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a team that agrees to answer messages at **three set times** a day. Between those times, nobody expects a reply. The switches still happen. They\u2019re just batched, so the long stretches between them can exist.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The three minutes is the article\u2019s estimate, not a **measured constant**. Some work is short by nature and loses little. And batching has a cost: a message that needed you now waits, which suits some jobs and not others.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The fee lives in **the switch**, not the glance. So concentrating harder inside each fragment changes little. Cut the number of fragments, and **your long stretches** come back.'}]},'the-attention-budget_3':{title:'Turn Most Alerts Off',card:{insight:'Each alert you keep lets someone else pick when you stop.',tryThis:'Tonight, turn off every alert. Then turn back on only the few you would pay for.',skills:['focus','habits']},review:{prompt:'A new app asks to send you alerts. What should you ask yourself?',right:'Would I pay to let this alert stop me?',wrong:'Will I miss something good if I say no?',why:'Each alert can stop you at any time, so few should get a yes.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Imagine telling everyone in town: **ring my bell** any time, and I\u2019ll come to the door. At dinner, during homework, in the middle of a film. Would you **really agree** to that?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Turning on an alert is **that same promise**. You made it once, with one tap. Now it gets used many times a day, at moments **you didn\u2019t pick**. A game pings, and suddenly your drawing has stopped.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Every alert lets someone else **choose the moment**. So keep only the ones that **really matter** to you. The rest can wait until you check.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Months ago, setting up a new phone, you tapped **Allow** on a dozen prompts in a minute. Each tap felt like nothing. So why are those taps still **interrupting you** today?'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture Lena, deep in a budget forecast. Her phone lights up with a sale from a shop she used once. She looks, and the numbers slip away. The shop chose that moment. It couldn\u2019t see what she was doing.'},{kind:'explain',text:'A notification is **a decision made early**, by somebody else, to fire at a moment they can\u2019t see. The real cost isn\u2019t reading it. It\u2019s **the re-entry**: rebuilding the state you were in, which takes far longer.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Your settings feel like **a preference**. They work more like **a budget**.',left:{title:'A preference',items:['Allow by default','Set once, then forgotten','Every sender gets a claim']},right:{title:'A budget',items:['Off by default','Each alert must earn it','Almost nothing qualifies']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Each alert you allow is a **standing claim** on your attention. Phones ship with almost everything on. Flip the default: keep only **the alerts** you\u2019d gladly pay for.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'One tap on a settings screen, months ago. It took a second and **felt like nothing**. Today that tap will fire again and again, each time at a moment **you didn\u2019t choose**. Who\u2019s really running your day?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The article frames a notification as **pre-committed attention**. You agree once, in the abstract, to hand over your focus to whoever asks next. That agreement then runs dozens of times a day. The cost is **the re-entry**, not the reading.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Omar, writing a tricky proposal. A delivery update pings, then a group chat, then a sale. Each takes seconds to read. Each time, though, he has to rebuild the argument he was holding. None of those senders knew he was busy.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Jess, who switches every alert off one evening. Then she turns back on only **the few** she\u2019d pay for: calls from family, one work channel. Her phone goes quiet, but the important things still reach her.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some alerts are worth their cost, like a call from your child\u2019s school. For some jobs, being reachable **is the work**. And switching everything off can backfire if you then check by hand every few minutes.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Treat your settings as **a budget**, not a preference. Every enabled alert is a claim on a future minute. Let almost nothing qualify, and make **your own checking** deliberate too.'}]},'the-attention-budget_4':{title:'Leave Some Time Empty',card:{insight:'Empty time is when your mind joins up what you already took in.',tryThis:'Today, take one walk with your phone out of reach. See what ideas pop up.',skills:['focus','thinking']},review:{prompt:'You have been stuck on a problem for two hours. What should you do next?',right:'Take a walk and leave your phone out of reach',wrong:'Keep pushing at your desk until the answer comes',why:'When you stop pushing, your mind can join up what you took in.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re stuck on a riddle. You give up and walk to the shop. Standing in the queue, **the answer pops** into your head. You weren\u2019t even trying. So **what did the work**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'While you\u2019re not busy, your mind **sorts things out**. It joins up ideas you met earlier. It\u2019s like tidying a room. Only when you stop throwing new things in is there **time to sort** them.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Bored time isn\u2019t **wasted time**. It\u2019s when your mind connects things. So leave some gaps empty, with **no screen**, and see what turns up.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You spent the afternoon stuck on a problem at your desk. That evening, hands in **the washing-up**, the answer arrives whole. You weren\u2019t thinking about it at all. Why did it come **then**?'},{kind:'story',text:'This isn\u2019t only your experience. Henri Poincar\xe9, the French mathematician, described it from his own work. Solutions that wouldn\u2019t come at the desk arrived later, in undemanding moments away from it. Most people know the feeling from a shower or a walk.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Undirected time seems to do **its own work**. The mind consolidates, connects and retrieves material that focused effort can\u2019t reach directly. Fill every gap with a feed, and you don\u2019t lose time. You lose **that processing**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The gaps that once did this, like queues, commutes and waiting, are now **reliably filled**. The filling is pleasant, so you never notice what\u2019s missing. That\u2019s a case for **putting the phone down** that doesn\u2019t rely on guilt.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Protect some time where nothing is being consumed. Treat it as **a different mode** of work, not rest. The test is whether you reach **conclusions you weren\u2019t seeking**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Think of the queue, the commute, the wait for the kettle. Those gaps used to be **simply empty**. Now a feed fills almost every one. What did **those empty minutes** used to do for you?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The article\u2019s claim is that **undirected attention** does real work. It consolidates, connects and retrieves things you took in earlier. Filling every gap doesn\u2019t steal time. It steals **the processing** that makes what you read useful.'},{kind:'story',text:'Henri Poincar\xe9, the French mathematician, wrote about where his solutions came from. Often they arrived **away from the desk**, in a moment when he wasn\u2019t working on the problem. It\u2019s a famous account of something most people have felt.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Kemi, who listens to podcasts on every walk. One week she leaves her phone at home. On the third walk, a fix for a work problem surfaces, one she hadn\u2019t been looking for.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Empty time makes **nothing from nothing**. It connects material you\u2019ve already taken in through focused effort. The article only says such gaps appear to help, not that they always do. And some gaps, like driving, aren\u2019t for drifting.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Leave some gaps **truly empty**, as a different mode of work. Then use the article\u2019s own test. Do you ever reach **a conclusion** you weren\u2019t looking for?'}]},'the-attention-budget_5':{title:'Decide the Night Before',card:{insight:'Pick your main task tonight, so you can start on it first thing.',tryThis:'Before you stop work today, write tomorrow\u2019s main task on paper. Leave it on your desk.',skills:['productivity','habits']},review:{prompt:'Last night you chose to write the report first. Two emails can wait. What now?',right:'The report, since you already made that choice last night',wrong:'The emails, so your head is clear for the report',why:'A choice made last night should not be undone by things that can wait.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'It\u2019s Saturday morning and you wake up **full of energy**. You could do your project, practise piano or tidy up. You check a message. Half an hour later, you **still haven\u2019t started**. What happened?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Choosing is **hard work** for your brain. If you choose in the morning, you use up your fresh time just deciding. But if you choose **the night before**, you can wake up and get straight on with it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your fresh morning is **precious**. Don\u2019t spend it on choosing. Pick tomorrow\u2019s main job tonight, and write it down **before bed**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You arrive with a clear head and twelve new emails. Two are marked urgent. By the time you\u2019ve **decided what to do**, the best hour of your day is **already gone**. Where did it go?'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture Raj, who plans each morning over coffee. Overnight messages are waiting, so the plan bends towards whatever looks urgent. The proposal that matters slips again. His freshest hour went on **choosing**, not doing.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Morning choosing is expensive for **two reasons**. It happens when attention is freshest, so it eats the best hour. And it\u2019s done under the pull of whatever arrived overnight, which favours **the urgent** over the important.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Decided **the night before**, the choice has the whole day in view and **no pressure**.',left:{title:'At dawn',items:['Fresh hour spent choosing','Urgent beats important']},right:{title:'Last night',items:['Fresh hour spent doing','Already made, hard to reopen']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Choose tomorrow\u2019s one task before you finish today. Then your morning starts with **doing**, not deciding. And the tempted you has to **overturn a decision**, which is a higher bar than making one.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'You wake with a clear head. Overnight messages are waiting. You spend the next half hour weighing what matters most, and **urgent wins**. Your sharpest hour has gone on **a decision**. Could it have gone anywhere else?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The article\u2019s point is that **choosing** is the morning\u2019s most expensive activity. It burns the freshest attention, under the pull of whatever came in overnight. A choice made the evening before is made with context and **no incoming pressure**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Mia, a nurse who starts early. Before leaving her shift, she writes one line for tomorrow\u2019s study: *chapter four, first.* Next morning she opens the book without weighing anything. The choosing was already done.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a writer who decided last night to draft the proposal first. In the morning, an easier task tempts him. To switch, he\u2019d have to **overturn a decision**, not just make one. That\u2019s a meaningfully higher bar.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Your evening self has context but **not the news**. Sometimes what arrives overnight really changes the priority, and the plan should give way to a fact. Judgement can also be poor after a draining day.'},{kind:'moral',text:'So keep the evening decision **small**: one task, not a timetable. Let a new fact reopen it, but **never a temptation**. Then your fresh hour goes on doing.'}]}}}},1004,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'the-case-against-notes',kind:'book',promise:'Write fewer notes and learn more from them',about:'How many of your notes have you opened again this year? The Mindroll team wrote this short essay against most note taking. It also says which notes are still worth keeping. Which of yours would you keep?',ideas:{'the-case-against-notes_1':{title:'The Unopened Notebook',card:{insight:'You will not read most notes again, so the help comes when you write them.',tryThis:'Count how many of your notes you opened to use this year. Give an honest number.',skills:['thinking','productivity']},review:{prompt:'A coworker sorts old notes into new folders each Friday. What is wrong?',right:'Most notes are not read again, so folders help little',wrong:'Her folders are still not sorted well enough to find things',why:'A note helps you when you write it, and filing adds little.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Think of a notebook you **filled last year**. Every page is neat. Now be honest. How many times have you **opened it since**, just to use it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Most notes are **never read again**. That doesn\u2019t mean writing them was useless. When you put something in **your own words**, you have to think about it. That thinking is the part that helps.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The page you keep **matters less** than you think. The good part of a note is **what writing it** did to your head. So don\u2019t fuss over neat pages.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You set up a note app with **folders, tags** and templates. Three years on, it holds thousands of notes. Now name the last time you opened one **to use it**, not to file it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Maya, who spends Friday afternoons moving her notes into a better folder structure. She\u2019s proud of the system. When she finally counts, she\u2019s reopened a handful of notes in two years. The rest just sit there.'},{kind:'explain',text:'We tend to **take for granted** that note-taking pays off, and we rarely check. The essay asks you to test it on yourself. Not whether notes could be useful, but whether **yours have been**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'If a note will **never be reopened**, its length, folder and searchability stop mattering. What\u2019s left is what producing it did to **your understanding**. That\u2019s a different job, and it calls for a different kind of note.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Most notes are never reopened, so most note-taking is **ceremony**. The point isn\u2019t to stop. It\u2019s to judge a note by **what writing it** did for you.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Elegant note systems get admired for their **structure**: links, tags, tidy templates. They\u2019re rarely tested on one plain question. How many of your notes have you **returned to** in the past year?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Answer honestly, and for many people it\u2019s **close to zero**. The essay concludes that most note-taking practice is **ceremonial**. Any value lies in the act of writing, not in the artefact you keep.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a researcher with three thousand linked notes. She spends an hour a week maintaining them. When she counts the ones she reopened to use, not to tidy, she finds a dozen in two years.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture her colleague, who scribbles rough notes in meetings and bins them weekly. He never rereads them either. But writing them forced him to put each point in his own words, and that part did its work.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The count measures **how often** notes are used, not what a use is worth. One reopened contract or reference can outweigh **a thousand idle** notes. The essay itself concedes this later.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Count your reopened notes honestly, and you\u2019ll likely find **very few**. That doesn\u2019t mean stop writing. It means **judge each note** by what making it did to your thinking.'}]},'the-case-against-notes_2':{title:'The Busy Hand',card:{insight:'Copying feels like learning, but your hand works while your mind rests.',tryThis:'Next time you read something worth keeping, close it first. Then write only what you remember.',skills:['psychology','thinking']},review:{prompt:'You study for a work test by copying the manual. What should change?',right:'Close the manual first, then write what you remember',wrong:'Copy it more slowly and with more care, so it goes in',why:'You can copy with your mind at rest, so a tired hand proves nothing.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'The teacher fills the whiteboard. You copy **every single word** into your book. Your hand is busy the whole time. Your mind is on lunch. At the end, how much is **in your head**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A **photocopier** can copy a whole book. It doesn\u2019t learn one word of it. When you copy without thinking, you\u2019re being a photocopier. Your hand feels busy, so it **feels like learning**. But very little sticks.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Copying feels like learning, but **the busy hand** fools you. Shut the book first. Then write **what you remember**, in your own words.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re studying for a work exam. Each evening you copy **key paragraphs** from the manual into a notebook. It takes hours, and you feel thorough. So why does **the practice test** go so badly?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Copying can be done **without comprehension**. The words travel from page to notebook through your hand. The feeling of effort comes from the transcribing, not from thinking. And because effort usually means learning, you **trust the feeling**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a colleague in the same spot who tries something different. She reads a paragraph, closes the manual, then writes what she remembers. It\u2019s slower and harder. Some nights she can barely write a line.'},{kind:'explain',text:'That difficulty is **the point**. Whatever she manages to write has come from memory, through **her understanding**, which is how material gets retained. Your copied pages never had to pass that test.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Copying gives you **the feeling** of learning with little of the substance. Write less, and never copy. Close the source first, so **writing costs you** some thought.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Copying a passage out by hand is slow and **tiring**. Tiredness feels like effort, and effort feels like learning. So after an hour of it, you feel sure you\u2019ve **learned something**. Have you?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The essay links this to **re-reading**: the same trick, at a higher cost. Copying needs no comprehension. Your hand keeps working while **your mind rests**, and the effort you feel comes from the transcribing itself.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a student who copies whole paragraphs from a textbook before an exam. Her notebook is beautiful. In the exam, one question asks her to apply an idea to a new case. She has nothing to say.'},{kind:'explain',text:'There\u2019s **some evidence** here. Studies of student note-taking have found something telling. Students taking word-for-word notes sometimes did worse on **conceptual questions**. Those writing fewer notes, in their own words, did better. The essay adds that **the findings are mixed**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a lawyer copying out a clause word for word. She needs the exact wording, so copying is right. And she copies slowly, stopping to ask what each phrase commits her client to.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Copying has honest uses. Sometimes **exact wording** matters: a definition, a formula, a clause. And slow copying can be close reading if you stop to think. A busy hand just isn\u2019t **proof** the mind is working.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Copying feels like deep engagement but leaves **very little memory**. Unless the exact words matter, close the source before you write. What survives has been **through your understanding**.'}]},'the-case-against-notes_3':{title:'The Thinking Note',card:{insight:'A good note is one you could only write if you got the point.',tryThis:'Next time you read something, close it first. Then write one doubt or question of your own.',skills:['thinking','psychology']},review:{prompt:'You must share notes from a work class. Which note shows you understood?',right:'One line in your own words, with a question',wrong:'The best slide, copied down word for word with care',why:'Anyone can copy a slide, but your own words take thought.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You read a page about **how bees make honey**. Then you shut the book. Could you write one line about it **without peeking**? What would that line prove?'},{kind:'story',text:'You write: *Bees drink sweet juice from flowers, carry it home, and dry it out in the hive.* It\u2019s just one line. But you could only write it because you understood the page.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Ask yourself: could someone write this note **without getting it**? A copied line, yes, but a line **in your own words**, no. That\u2019s the useful kind.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You read a paragraph arguing that **towns grow faster** when rents are cheap. You reach for your notebook. What\u2019s the most **useful thing** you could write down?'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Two notes on **the same paragraph**. Only one **needed understanding** to write.',left:{title:'Note A',items:['Copies the best sentence','Anyone could write it']},right:{title:'Note B',items:['Or does slow growth keep rents cheap?','Needs the point understood']}},{kind:'explain',text:'The useful note is one you **had to think** to write: a restatement, an objection, a link, an open question. You can\u2019t make any of these without working through the material. The note is **a by-product** of thinking, not a stand-in.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The test: could someone who **hadn\u2019t understood** write it? A quotation, yes. That\u2019s why it\u2019s worth writing **with the book closed**. And it\u2019s why such notes are short: real processing yields a sentence or two.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A note is useful in proportion to **the thinking** it took. So close the book and write **in your own words**. A short note is often the sign the work was done.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'The essay has argued that most notes go unread and that **copying teaches little**. So should you stop writing notes altogether? Or is there **one kind of note** that earns its keep?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Its answer is the note that **couldn\u2019t exist** without thought. Writing in your own words, without looking, is **retrieval practice**: pulling an idea back out, which builds memory. The app and the layout don\u2019t matter. What matters is whether the book was shut.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture someone finishing a dense article on sleep. Page closed, she writes two lines restating its argument, then one question it never answered. It takes four minutes. Nobody who skimmed the article could have written them.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a colleague asked to share notes from a training day. He rewrites each slide in his own words by swapping synonyms. The notes look original. But he could have made them without grasping a single point.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. A paraphrase can be made **mechanically**, by swapping words, and a note can be short because it\u2019s lazy. Own words reliably show thought mainly when they **say something new**: an objection, a link, a question.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Judge a note by one test: could someone who **hadn\u2019t understood** have written it? A quotation passes for anyone. Aim for the note that **only your thinking** could produce.'}]},'the-case-against-notes_4':{title:'Notes Worth Keeping',card:{insight:'Notes to learn can be rough, but notes you must find again need care.',tryThis:'Write down three things you will need again but cannot remember. Keep them where you can find them.',skills:['productivity','thinking']},review:{prompt:'Your team picks a new company to buy from. What should you write down?',right:'The choice and its reasons, kept where you can find them',wrong:'Nothing, since you will not read most notes again',why:'You may need to find this later, so the record counts.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your friend gets a **new phone number**. It\u2019s eleven digits long, and you\u2019ll need it next week. Should you try to **learn it by heart**, or just write it down?'},{kind:'explain',text:'You\u2019re not trying to *learn* that number. You just need to **find it** later. So writing it down is right. Some things are for **keeping, not learning**: numbers, dates, plans, and why you chose something.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The case against notes is only about **notes made to learn**. Things you\u2019ll **need to find** again deserve a proper record. So before you write, ask which kind you\u2019re making.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your team picks one supplier over another after a long meeting. Six months later, someone asks **why**. Nobody wrote it down. How will you **rebuild the argument**?'},{kind:'story',text:'If the reasons had been written at the time, the answer would take a minute. Instead, the team rebuilds it from memory. Nobody can show what was actually decided, or why.'},{kind:'explain',text:'This is **where the case** against notes stops working. Some things won\u2019t fit in your head and you\u2019ll **need them again**: references, numbers, decisions and their reasons. For these, the record matters as much as the writing.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Writing things down isn\u2019t **the problem**. Mistaking a record **for learning** is.',left:{title:'Notes to understand',items:['Rarely reread','Judge them by the writing']},right:{title:'Notes to retrieve',items:['References and decisions','Kept and findable']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Ask what each note is for. Notes **to understand** can stay rough. Notes **to retrieve** need more care, and mixing the two serves neither.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Having argued against notes for three ideas, the essay turns on **its own case**. Where does a provocation like this **stop being true**? And what should you still write down?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The argument fails for material you must **retrieve**, not just understand. Reference information, commitments, decisions and their reasons all deserve **a durable record**. So does anything where being wrong is expensive. For these, the artefact matters.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture an engineer three years into a long building project. The context now runs to thousands of choices, far more than anyone could hold in memory. Her written log of decisions is what keeps the work coherent.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a charity treasurer asked by auditors what was agreed last spring. Remembering isn\u2019t enough. What was decided has to be shown to someone else. Only a record made at the time can do that.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The line between the two kinds isn\u2019t always **visible when you write**. A note made to understand can turn out to be **the only record** of a decision. You rarely know which page will matter.'},{kind:'moral',text:'So the case against notes is **much narrower** than it first sounds. Notes to understand should be made for the writing. Notes to retrieve need **a careful record**, and confusing the two serves neither.'}]},'the-case-against-notes_5':{title:'One Sentence, Later',card:{insight:'One sentence a day later does more than a long set of notes.',tryThis:'Pick a book you finished yesterday. With it shut, write one sentence on what it changed for you.',skills:['habits','thinking']},review:{prompt:'You finish a two-day course on Friday. Which plan fits this idea?',right:'On Saturday, with your notes shut, write one line on what changed',wrong:'On Friday night, write full notes while it is fresh',why:'Waiting a day makes you recall it and pick what mattered.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A friend asks about **a film** you saw last week. You don\u2019t describe every scene. You pick **the one thing** that mattered most. How did you know which thing to pick?'},{kind:'explain',text:'To pick one thing, you had to **understand the film**. That\u2019s the trick. A day after you finish a book or lesson, write **one line** on what it changed for you. Choosing that line makes you think.'},{kind:'moral',text:'One line, **a day later**, is enough. It\u2019s so small that you\u2019ll **really do it**. And picking the one big thing is the part that helps you remember.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You finished a good book last month and meant to **write it up** properly: chapter summaries, key passages. The template is **still half empty**. You\u2019ve since finished two more books. Sound familiar?'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Leo, who finishes a book on Sunday night and puts it back on the shelf. On Monday, over breakfast, he writes one sentence about what it changes for him. That\u2019s all. It takes two minutes.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The delay makes him **recall it**, since the book is back on the shelf. The one-sentence limit forces **a judgement** about what the book was really about. Choosing what to leave out means seeing how it fits together.'},{kind:'explain',text:'It also **survives busy weeks**. Big systems tend to be dropped within months, and then they record nothing. One sentence is small enough to keep doing. A year of them becomes **a useful document**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Keep the practice small. A day after you finish something, write **one sentence** on what it changes for you. It\u2019s recall after a gap, and **small enough** to last.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'After arguing against most note-taking, the essay keeps **just one habit**. It takes a minute, needs no system, and happens **after you finish**, not during. Why would that be the one worth keeping?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The habit is **a single sentence**, written some time after finishing. The delay forces retrieval, because you can\u2019t copy what\u2019s gone. The limit forces **a judgement** about what the thing was actually about. That judgement is the valuable part.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a manager who finishes a two-day course on Friday. On Saturday morning, without opening her notes, she writes: *I\u2019ve been giving feedback too late to be useful.* One sentence, and it shapes her next month.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture her colleague, who builds an elaborate template for every book: summaries, quotes, tags. By March he\u2019s stopped using it. Since then it has recorded nothing, while her sentences keep piling up.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. One sentence suits something read **for its ideas**. It doesn\u2019t suit material you must retrieve in detail, which needs a different tool. And the delay is loose: leave it **too long** and little may be left.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Twelve months of single sentences adds up to **something worth reading**. Twelve months of dropped systems doesn\u2019t. Write **one line**, a day or so later, on what the thing changed for you.'}]}}}},1005,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'the-cost-of-being-reachable',kind:'book',promise:'Keep your focus and still be easy to reach',about:'Why does answering messages fast feel like doing your job well? This article looks at who gains from your fast replies, and who pays. These ideas show how to stay easy to reach and still get work done. What do fast replies cost you?',ideas:{'the-cost-of-being-reachable_1':{title:'Free to Send',card:{insight:'A message takes seconds to send, but it makes you lose your place.',tryThis:'Next time a message comes in while you work, leave it. Read it at a time you pick.',skills:['focus','communication']},review:{prompt:'Coworkers keep sending you quick messages all day. Who can fix it?',right:'You can, by choosing a time to read them',wrong:'They can, if you ask them to send fewer',why:'The people who send never feel it, so the fix is yours.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re doing homework. A friend sends a **funny video**. Then another friend asks about the weekend. Each message took them **two seconds** to send. So why does your evening feel chopped into bits?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Sending is easy, so your friends send. Nobody is being mean. But each message makes **you stop** and find your place again. They never feel that cost, so asking them to send less rarely works. What you **can choose** is when you read.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A message is **cheap to send** and costly to get. You can\u2019t make everyone send less. But you can **pick the time** you read them.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'By lunch you\u2019ve had **thirty messages**. Each was polite, short and reasonable. None of the senders did anything wrong. Yet your morning is **in pieces**. Who did this to it?'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Hannah, a project manager. A colleague needs a date, so he messages her. Ten seconds for him. Hannah drops her budget mid-thought, answers, then rebuilds where she was. Multiply that by everyone who writes to her.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The same message looks **very different** from **each end**.',left:{title:'The sender',items:['Seconds to write','Gets an answer sooner','Never sees the switch']},right:{title:'The recipient',items:['Drops a task mid-thought','Rebuilds it afterwards','Multiplied by every sender']}},{kind:'explain',text:'For each sender, sending is **the rational move**. It\u2019s free and it gets an answer. But many reasonable choices add up to a day in fragments. Asking senders to stop means asking them to carry a cost they **never feel**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Nobody is to blame for the flood. Sending is free, so volume grows to fill your speed. The fix sits with you: **choose when you read**, not **how much** others write.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture an office where everyone is **considerate**. Nobody shouts across the room. They just send short, polite messages. Somehow everyone\u2019s day is **still fragmented**. How can good manners produce that?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The article\u2019s answer is **an asymmetry**. A message costs the sender almost nothing and costs the recipient a context switch. The structure pushes the cost onto the person receiving. **No sender** ever sees it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Felix, a designer people love working with because he answers in minutes. The quicker he replies, the more people write. Nobody plans it. His speed simply teaches everyone that **sending works**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a team that asks everyone to **send less**. For a week it holds. Then deadlines bite, and each person decides their one message is fine. It is fine, on its own. The volume returns.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The claim that volume rises to fill your speed is argued from incentives, **not measured**. And the sender\u2019s end isn\u2019t hopeless: a team can agree shared norms that change what gets sent.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Every sender is reasonable, and the **sum still hurts** you. Since senders never feel the cost, change what you control: **when you read**.'}]},'the-cost-of-being-reachable_2':{title:'Speed Gets Noticed',card:{insight:'Fast replies are easy to see, but hard work is not, so speed gets thanks.',tryThis:'Look back at yesterday. List what you were thanked for, and mark each as speed or hard work.',skills:['career','focus']},review:{prompt:'Your manager praises how fast you reply. Why is checking less so hard?',right:'Speed is easy to see, and people thank it',wrong:'You just don\u2019t have the willpower to check less',why:'People can see fast replies and thank them, so the pull is real.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your friends say you\u2019re **a great friend** because you always reply fast. You worked hard on a drawing for three days too. Nobody mentioned that. Which one **gets noticed** more?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A fast reply is **easy to see**. Anyone can tell you answered in a minute. Hard work over three days is quiet until it\u2019s finished. So people **praise the speed**, and that praise is real. That\u2019s why stopping feels hard.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Fast replies get praised because **they\u2019re easy to see**. That\u2019s not in your head. So choose on purpose **how much speed** you give.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'At review time, a colleague is praised for being **so responsive**. She answers everything within minutes. Your hardest work this month gets **one line**. What is the organisation really rewarding?'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Marco, who decides to check email twice a day. His work improves, but quietly. Meanwhile colleagues who reply in minutes keep getting thanked. Within a month he\u2019s back to checking constantly.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'One kind of work is **easy to judge**, the other **hard to see**.',left:{title:'A fast reply',items:['Visible in minutes','Anyone can judge it','Thanked at once']},right:{title:'Slow hard work',items:['Invisible until done','Hard to judge outside','Noticed late, if ever']}},{kind:'explain',text:'So organisations reward **the visible signal**. People notice this and conclude, correctly, that responsiveness gets them credit. The incentive **isn\u2019t imagined**. That\u2019s why the habit holds even when you know it costs you.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Fast replies earn **real credit**, so \u201cjust check less\u201d asks you to pay alone. Treat speed as **a signal** you supply. Then decide how much of it to give.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'The standard advice for a flooded inbox is simple: **check less**. Almost everyone has heard it. Almost nobody keeps it up. Is that weak willpower, or are you **reading the room** correctly?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The article\u2019s claim is that responsiveness is **legible**. A quick reply is visible and easy to judge. Three days of difficult work is neither. So organisations reward the signal they can see, and **people infer** correctly what gets noticed.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Asha, an analyst who spends a week on a model that saves her team hours. It gets a nod in a meeting. The same week, a colleague is thanked six times for **quick answers**. Asha notices which one counted.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Joel, who decides to check email twice a day, alone. His colleagues keep replying in minutes. He swaps a recognised signal for an unrecognised one and takes **the reputational cost** by himself. He lasts a fortnight.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. This explains the habit, but it doesn\u2019t make fast replies worthless. In some roles, **responsiveness is the job**. And one person opting out pays alone, so quiet withdrawal is the wrong fix.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You\u2019re rewarded for **a proxy**: speed stands in for reliability. Don\u2019t drop it by accident or supply it by default. **Decide deliberately** how much to give.'}]},'the-cost-of-being-reachable_3':{title:'Set Your Reply Times',card:{insight:'Reply times you share look like a plan, but silence looks like you forgot.',tryThis:'Post your reply times where people will see them. Say how to reach you if it is urgent.',skills:['communication','focus']},review:{prompt:'You want fewer messages, but you don\u2019t want to look flaky. What now?',right:'Tell people your reply times and how to reach you if urgent',wrong:'Stop answering, and reply when you have time',why:'People need to know when you will reply more than they need speed.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Kai wants to finish his homework without his phone **buzzing all evening**. If he just ignores his friends, they\u2019ll keep messaging, wondering what\u2019s wrong. How can he get quiet **without upsetting** anyone?'},{kind:'story',text:'Kai **tells his friends**: I\u2019ll read messages at six. If something\u2019s really urgent, call me. Now they know when to expect him, and emergencies **have a way through**. But it only works if he sticks to it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Going silent makes people **worry and chase** you. Telling them **when you\u2019ll reply** makes them relax. Then keep your word.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You decide to stop checking email every few minutes. You don\u2019t tell anyone. Within a week, people are **following up**, copying in your manager and phoning your desk. Why did going quiet **make it worse**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Unannounced silence reads as **unreliability**. Senders can\u2019t tell a delay from a dropped ball, so they escalate and route around you. Volume rises and relationships take the damage. Going quiet **moves the cost**, rather than removing it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Grace, an engineer. She adds a line to her email signature: I reply at ten and three; for **anything urgent**, call me. Within a fortnight the follow-ups fade. People know when she\u2019ll answer and how to reach her.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Most senders don\u2019t need **speed**. They need to **know when**. A stated window gives them that, and a separate route for urgent things answers the usual objection. It only holds if you honour it every time.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Silence reads as **unreliability**. A stated window reads as **a system**. Say when you\u2019ll reply, give emergencies a route, then keep to it.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two colleagues both answer email **twice a day**. One is seen as disorganised. The other is seen as **admirably clear**. They reply at the same speed. What\u2019s the difference?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The article\u2019s answer: one **announced it**. A stated pattern, with set times and an urgent route, supplies predictability. And predictability is what senders need. Most don\u2019t require speed. They require **knowing when**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Leon, who quietly stops replying until evening. His clients can\u2019t tell a delay from a dropped ball. They chase, escalate and copy in his boss. His **inbox grows**, and two relationships cool. Silence moved the cost.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Priti, who posts her pattern: replies at eleven and four, urgent things by phone. For a month she sticks to it exactly. People stop testing it. The **follow-ups fade**, because there\u2019s nothing left to check.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. A window followed unreliably is **worse than none**, because people learn to test it. And not everyone can publish one. Some roles need constant cover, and a junior person may need agreement first.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your silence leaves people guessing, so they chase. **Your stated window** lets them plan. Announce it, give emergencies **their own route**, and honour it.'}]},'the-cost-of-being-reachable_4':{title:'The Short Urgent List',card:{insight:'Very few things are urgent, and you can list them ahead of time.',tryThis:'List the three things at work that can\u2019t wait four hours. Have those come by phone.',skills:['productivity','communication']},review:{prompt:'Every message in your inbox is marked urgent. What do you do first?',right:'Check it against your short list of things that can\u2019t wait',wrong:'Treat each one as urgent, since the sender says so',why:'What is urgent depends on the facts, not on how the sender feels.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A message pops up from your friend, marked **urgent**. You drop everything to read it. It\u2019s about which film to watch on Saturday. How often is urgent **really urgent**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Ask yourself: what **truly can\u2019t wait** a few hours? Someone is hurt or unsafe. The plan for getting home has changed. That\u2019s about it. The list is **short**, and it barely changes from week to week.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Feeling urgent isn\u2019t **being urgent**. Make a short list of what can\u2019t wait, and agree those things **come by phone**. The rest can be a message.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Plenty of the messages you get are flagged **urgent**, or written as if they were. So you answer everything at once, just in case. But how many of them **truly needed** an answer within the hour?'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Owen, an operations lead. He goes back through a month of urgent messages. Very few needed an answer within the hour, and those few fell into **the same categories**. A system was down. A deadline with a client was today.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The article offers this as **something to test** on your own messages, not a measured rate. If it holds, write the list: things that can\u2019t wait four hours. It\u2019s usually short, and **the same** every quarter.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'A list helps, but only once **urgent things** have **their own route**.',left:{title:'Definition only',items:['Everything arrives by email','Every message looks pressing','Urgency stays a feeling']},right:{title:'Definition plus route',items:['Urgent things come by phone','Silence elsewhere is safe','Urgency is a fact']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Almost nothing is urgent, and the exceptions are **predictable**. Write **the short list**, give it a channel you watch, and the rest can wait for your window.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Every team has a word that **jumps the queue**: urgent. Once someone uses it, everything else stops. So who decides when it applies? Is it the situation, or just **the sender\u2019s mood**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The article suggests that **almost nothing** is urgent, and the exceptions can be named ahead. Production is down. Someone is unsafe. A deadline with an outside party lands today. Write that list and urgency becomes **a shared definition**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Ines, who runs a small support team. They agree a list of three urgent cases and send those **by phone only**. Everything else goes to email, read at set times. Now silence in the inbox feels safe.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a firm that writes the same list but adds **no channel**. Every message still arrives by email, looking equally pressing. People skim everything just in case. The definition exists, but nothing has changed.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The article gives no figures, so test the claim on **your own messages**. A list fitted to last quarter can miss a new crisis. And once the phone means urgent, some people will call about anything.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Name **the few things** that can\u2019t wait, and give them a route you watch. Then urgency is a fact about the situation, and **you can relax** about the rest.'}]},'the-cost-of-being-reachable_5':{title:'Work Nobody Asked For',card:{insight:'Being easy to reach all day hurts the big work no one is waiting for.',tryThis:'Pick one task nobody is asking you for. Block an hour for it tomorrow, and keep it like a meeting.',skills:['productivity','career']},review:{prompt:'Your week fills with requests, and your big project hasn\u2019t moved. Why?',right:'No one waits for it, so it loses each hour',wrong:'The requests just matter more than it does',why:'Work no one asked for has no one waiting, so block time for it first.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You want to build **a treehouse** this summer. Nobody is asking you to. Meanwhile your mum wants help, a friend wants a game, and homework is due. By August, **how much treehouse** exists?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Small jobs have **someone waiting**. Finish one and you\u2019re thanked straight away. Big jobs are slow, and each time you stop, you have to **find your place** again. So the big thing is always the easiest to put off.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The big thing **loses every time** unless you give it a spot. Pick **a fixed time** for it each week, like a club. Then keep that time for it.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Think back over the past year. You answered thousands of messages and **kept up** with everything. Now name the one big thing you **built or finished**. Is there anything to point to?'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Rosa, who wants to rewrite her team\u2019s onboarding guide. Nobody has asked for it. Every hour she plans for it, someone is waiting on a reply. The reply wins, every time. A year on, the guide is untouched.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Interruption doesn\u2019t hurt **every task** equally. It hits **unrequested work** hardest.',left:{title:'Requested work',items:['Someone is waiting','Thanked at once','Little to rebuild']},right:{title:'Unrequested work',items:['Nobody is waiting yet','Noticed late, if ever','Much to rebuild']}},{kind:'explain',text:'So you drift toward **reactive work**, without ever deciding to. Intention alone loses to the person currently waiting. The fix has to be structural: **block time** in advance for the unrequested work, and defend it like a meeting.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Being always available has a cost, and it lands on **the work nobody** is asking about. That\u2019s the work that adds up. Give it **protected hours** before the requests arrive.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'At the end of a busy year, two people compare notes. One has **kept up** with everything. The other has **made something**: a tool, a guide, a new skill. Which year would you rather have had?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The article\u2019s claim is that interruption hurts long, **unrequested work** most. It has two disadvantages at once. It has the most state to rebuild after each break. And nobody is waiting, so it **loses every contest** for the same hour.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Sunil, a developer who knows the old billing code needs rewriting. Nobody has asked. Each week, requests with someone waiting take every hour he meant to give it. No single choice caused this. The drift did.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Clara, a teacher who wants to redesign a course. She blocks Thursday mornings for it and treats them like **a meeting**. When a colleague asks for that slot, she offers another. By summer, the course is rebuilt.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some work goes unrequested because it **isn\u2019t worth doing**. Blocked time can also become a way to dodge colleagues who really need you. And the fix assumes you control enough of your calendar.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The cost of being reachable is **never on a list**, because it falls on work nobody tracks. Good intentions lose to whoever is waiting. **Book the hours**, and defend them.'}]}}}},1006,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'the-cost-of-fees',kind:'book',promise:'See what a small fee costs you over time',about:'Does a 1% fee sound small? It comes out of all your money, every year. In thirty years, it can take a fourth of what you save. Here is how to see the cost, and where fees hide. Want to check yours?',ideas:{'the-cost-of-fees_1':{title:'The Fee on Everything',card:{insight:'A yearly fee comes out of all your money, not just your gains.',tryThis:'Tonight, look up the fee on your fund. Then see what it costs you in dollars.',skills:['money']},review:{prompt:'Your fund did not grow at all this year. Its fee is 1%. What happens?',right:'The fee still comes out of all your money',wrong:'No fee comes out, since nothing grew',why:'The fee is a cut of all you hold, even in bad years.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your savings pot holds **\xa3100**. This year, it didn\u2019t grow a single penny. Yet when you check, **\xa31 is missing**: the yearly fee. Why would they charge you in a year like that?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Most yearly fees are **a slice** of all the money you hold. They come out in good years and bad years. If your money earns 5p for each \xa31, a 1p fee takes **a fifth** of it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A fee on everything you hold doesn\u2019t care **how the year went**. It sounds tiny next to your savings. Compare it with **what they earn** instead.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You hold **\xa350,000** in a fund charging 1% a year. The markets go nowhere and your return is zero. Then you check **the statement**. What do you think the fund took?'},{kind:'story',text:'It took \xa3500, exactly 1% of your balance. The next year the markets fall and you lose money. The fee is **still taken**, adding to the loss. It never once asked how the fund did.'},{kind:'explain',text:'A management fee is charged on **the whole balance**, every year. The return it pays for rises and falls, but the fee doesn\u2019t. It\u2019s quoted as a share of assets, and **deducted, not paid**, so you rarely see it in pounds.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The same 1% looks **very different** depending on **what you measure** it against.',left:{title:'Against the balance',items:['One per cent of what you hold','Sounds trivial']},right:{title:'Against real returns',items:['Of a 4 or 5% real return','A fifth to a quarter','Every year, compounding']}},{kind:'moral',text:'A fee on the balance is **taken whatever** the year does. Measured against what your money actually earns, one per cent is **a big slice**. Read it in pounds.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'One fund charges **1% of your balance**. Another takes 15% of any gain. The first number looks far smaller. But which would you **rather pay** in a year when nothing grows?'},{kind:'explain',text:'In a flat year, the gain fee costs **nothing**. The balance fee still takes 1% of everything. That\u2019s the key detail. A fee on the balance is **independent of performance**, while the return it pays for isn\u2019t.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Grace, with savings in a fund charging 1%. Over many years she expects a real return of about five per cent. So each year the fee quietly takes a fifth of her outcome, compounding.'},{kind:'story',text:'Her friend Tom expects only four per cent after inflation. For him, the same 1% fee takes a quarter of the outcome. Neither ever hands over a payment. The fee is simply deducted, so neither sees it in pounds.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. A balance fee isn\u2019t always the larger one. In a strong year, 15% of a 10% gain costs more than **1% of the balance**. Its real harshness is that it\u2019s certain, whatever the year does.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t compare a fee with **your balance**. Compare it with **what your money earns**. Seen that way, a small-sounding percentage takes a large, certain slice every single year.'}]},'the-cost-of-fees_2':{title:'Do the Math Once',card:{insight:'In thirty years, a 1% fee can take a fourth of your money.',tryThis:'Use a free tool online to see what your fee will cost you.',skills:['money','thinking']},review:{prompt:'A coworker says a 1% fee hardly matters. What do you say?',right:'In thirty years, it can take a fourth of the money',wrong:'That is true, since 1% a year is very small',why:'Once a fee takes money, that money stops growing.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two pots each start with **\xa3100** and grow at the same rate each year. One has a tiny fee taken every year. The other has a fee five times bigger. After thirty years, how **far apart** are they?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Much further than **you\u2019d guess**. Each year the fee takes a bit of your pot, and that bit is gone for good. It can\u2019t grow any more. So **the gap** gets bigger every single year.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A small fee taken every year from **all your money** adds up fast. Do the sum once and **you\u2019ll see** how big it gets.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your fund charges **1% a year**. Your money grows about 7% a year. Next to seven, one looks tiny. So how much could **that one per cent** really cost you over thirty years?'},{kind:'story',text:'Take \xa310,000 growing at 7% for thirty years. With no fee, it reaches about \xa376,100. With a 1% fee, it reaches about \xa357,400. That\u2019s roughly three-quarters of the no-fee result.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The fee was **one per cent** a year. The cost was **a quarter** of the money.',left:{title:'A tiny fee',items:['A fifth of 1% a year','You keep roughly 94%']},right:{title:'A 1% fee',items:['One per cent a year','You keep about 75%']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Why is it so easy to miss? The fee is quoted in **the same units** as the return, so it looks small beside seven. But it\u2019s a slice of the whole balance, including **all the growth** from every earlier year.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The fee compounds **against you** exactly as the return compounds for you. The sum takes **two minutes**, and almost nobody does it. Do it before your next fund choice.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Leave a pound alone at 7% a year, and in thirty years it grows **more than sevenfold**. Knock just one point off the return, and it grows less than sixfold. Where did **all that money** go?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The ratio of the two is about **three-quarters**, whatever the starting balance. That\u2019s why a 1% fee can cost a quarter of the money. Each year\u2019s slice also removes **all the growth** it would have had.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture Amira and Ben, each investing \xa310,000 for thirty years at 7%. Amira picks a fund charging a fifth of 1%. Ben\u2019s charges a full 1%. At the end she has about \xa372,000. He has about \xa357,400.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Chloe, who runs her pension through a calculator twice: at her fee, and at a low one. It takes two minutes. Seeing the two totals side by side, she can\u2019t un-know them.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. These are lump-sum figures at a steady 7%. If you pay in regularly, later money is charged for fewer years, so **the share lost** shrinks. A lower return changes the numbers too.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The exact figures move, but **the shape** doesn\u2019t. The gap widens with time, because the fee eats money that would have kept compounding. So **do the sum** for your own balance.'}]},'the-cost-of-fees_3':{title:'Sure Fee, Maybe Gain',card:{insight:'A high fee comes out for sure, but the extra gain is a guess.',tryThis:'Before you pay a high fee, ask what a cheap fund would cost.',skills:['money','thinking']},review:{prompt:'A fund costs more and says it will beat the market. What is the deal?',right:'A sure fee now for a gain that may never come',wrong:'A smart team that will earn its fee back',why:'The fee is sure, but the extra gain is only a guess.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A boy at school offers you a deal. Give him **two sweets** now, and his secret trick will help you win more at **the fair**. Should you take it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The two sweets are **gone for sure**. The win is **only a maybe**. Some money helpers work the same way. They charge a bigger fee and say they\u2019ll earn you more. The fee is certain, but the extra isn\u2019t.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A bigger fee **isn\u2019t always bad**. Sometimes it\u2019s worth it. But it has to **prove itself**, so don\u2019t pay more just because someone promises more back.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A fund manager shows you **a glossy brochure**. His fees are higher, he admits, but his team will **beat the market** and earn them back. It sounds reasonable. What exactly are you buying?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Look at the two halves of the deal. The fee is **in the contract** and will be charged. The better return is **a forecast**, and it may never arrive. You pay a known amount now for an unknown gain later.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The brochure asks **the wrong question**. Here\u2019s **the real one**.',left:{title:'The marketing asks',items:['Is this manager good?','Will they beat the market?']},right:{title:'You should ask',items:['Is a hope worth a certainty?','What would the cheap option cost?']}},{kind:'explain',text:'The evidence here is consistent. Most actively managed funds **do worse** than their benchmarks over long periods, after fees. And the few that do better rarely **keep it up** for long.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Active management **isn\u2019t always wrong**. But the burden of proof sits **with the fee**. Compare it with the low-cost option, not with doing nothing.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A brochure says its fund turned \xa310,000 into \xa318,000 over ten years, so the fee **paid for itself**. It\u2019s a tidy story. What would you need to know **before believing** it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The usual sales argument is that a good manager beats the market. That frames the choice as **good versus bad**. It\u2019s really a contractual cost against **a forecast**, and the fee carries the burden of proof.'},{kind:'story',text:'Back to the brochure. The fund grew, but so did the market. A cheap tracker of the same market might have done as well, or better. That\u2019s the comparison the brochure leaves out.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Raj. One fund charging 2% has beaten its benchmark for a decade. Raj is tempted, and you might be too. But persistence among winners is weak, so past success is a poor guide to the next decade.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Active management is **sometimes justified**, and a higher fee isn\u2019t proof of a bad deal. The argument doesn\u2019t decide every case. It moves **the burden of proof** onto the fee, where it belongs.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When a higher fee is sold on returns, name the trade: **a certainty** for a hope. Then compare it with the cheap option you could buy instead. Make the fee **earn its place**.'}]},'the-cost-of-fees_4':{title:'Where Fees Hide',card:{insight:'The fee you see is often not the whole cost, so add up every fee.',tryThis:'Add up all the fees on one account for the year. Write that one number down.',skills:['money']},review:{prompt:'No one can tell you the total yearly cost of your account. What now?',right:'Take it as a warning sign, since that is rarely good news',wrong:'Guess it is low, since no one said there were extra fees',why:'A cost no one can tell you is rarely good news.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A toy costs **\xa310**. At the checkout you see \xa32 for postage. Then a \xa31 service fee. Then \xa31 for paying by card. What does the toy **really cost** you now?'},{kind:'explain',text:'It costs \xa314. Each extra charge was small, so you barely noticed it. Money pots work like this too. The fee you\u2019re told about is **often not all**. Small charges hide elsewhere, and together they can **match the main** fee.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t trust the price **you\u2019re first told**. Add up every little charge until you reach **the real total**, the one you actually pay.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You picked a fund because **its charge was low**. Then you notice the platform takes a fee of its own. And the fund trades, and you hold it in **another currency**. Has anyone added this up?'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Mei\u2019s account. The fund\u2019s management charge is modest. On top sit a platform fee, trading costs inside the fund and a currency conversion charge. Each one looks reasonable. Nobody ever showed her the total.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Some costs sit **in plain view**. Others are **built in**, where you\u2019d rarely look.',left:{title:'Usually visible',items:['Management charge','Platform fee']},right:{title:'Usually embedded',items:['Trading costs inside the fund','Bid-offer spreads','Currency conversion']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Pension and insurance products are often **the least transparent**. Their total can be hard to work out even when you try. Treat that difficulty as **information**. A cost nobody can state on request is rarely good news.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Headline fees **aren\u2019t the whole cost**. Stacked together, the extras can rival or exceed them. So find **the all-in figure**, not the advertised one.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two funds sit side by side. One advertises **a tiny charge**. The other advertises a charge three times bigger. You\u2019d pick the first without thinking. But is the advertised number **the one you pay**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Cost is **a stack**, not a number. The management charge sits on top. Below it come platform fees, trading costs, spreads and currency conversion. Each layer is defensible alone. Together they can rival or exceed **the headline**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Back to the two funds. The cheap one sits on a platform charging its own fee, and it trades heavily. The dearer one sits on a platform with no charge. Added up, the gap between them may vanish.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a fund that charges a performance fee. In a year when the market rises, it rises too, and the fee is taken. But the fund only tracked the market. You paid extra for a gain a cheap tracker would have given you.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some hidden costs **can\u2019t be avoided**: any fund that trades pays spreads, cheap ones included. The aim is to know the total, not to reach zero. And how the stack splits varies by account.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Read **the total**, not the label. Find the all-in yearly figure for your own account. If nobody can give it to you, that silence is **your answer**.'}]},'the-cost-of-fees_5':{title:'Check Fees First',card:{insight:'Fees are one of the few money choices where you do not have to guess.',tryThis:'List each account you have and its yearly fee. Circle the highest one.',skills:['money']},review:{prompt:'You have one free afternoon for your money. What is the best use?',right:'Cut your fees, since you do not have to guess',wrong:'Read up on which stocks will go up next year',why:'A lower fee on the same stocks leaves you more, with no risk added.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture a wall of **dials** controlling your savings. One sets how fast prices rise. One sets whether it\u2019s a good year. Nobody can turn those. But is there **one lever** you can pull?'},{kind:'explain',text:'There is: **the fee**. You can\u2019t control how much your money grows. But you can choose a smaller fee. Two pots holding the same things will grow alike, and the cheaper one **leaves you more**, for sure.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Checking fees feels like **a boring chore**. The prize shows up years later, so it\u2019s easy to skip. But the prize is real, so **look at fees first**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve got **one free afternoon** for your money. You could research which shares might rise. You could read market forecasts. Or you could do something **far less exciting**. Which would pay more?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Most money choices trade **risk against return**, and sensible people disagree. Cutting fees doesn\u2019t. For the same holdings, a lower fee raises your expected return **without adding risk**. The gain is arithmetic, not a forecast.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'That makes fees **close to unique** among the choices **you can make**.',left:{title:'Most choices',items:['Trade risk for return','Depend on forecasts','Reasonable people disagree']},right:{title:'Cutting fees',items:['Adds no risk','Benefit is certain','Better option known in advance']}},{kind:'explain',text:'So why does it go undone? The gain is invisible and arrives decades later. The effort arrives **this afternoon**. Nobody feels the extra balance in thirty years. Everybody feels the hours **comparing platforms**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Expensive products **aren\u2019t always wrong**. But the fee is the one variable you fully control. Treat checking it as a one-off task with a very high **hourly rate**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Almost every investing question asks you **to predict** something. Which fund will win? Where will markets go? Is there any decision where you know **the better option** before anything happens?'},{kind:'explain',text:'There\u2019s one. Fees are among the few variables you control **completely and at once**. Lowering them raises expected return without adding risk. The improvement is **arithmetically certain**, so nothing has to be predicted correctly.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Hannah, with \xa350,000 in a fund charging 1%. She moves it to a fund holding the same market for a fifth of that. One afternoon\u2019s work. Over the following decades, the gain compounds without any forecast.'},{kind:'story',text:'Her neighbour Sam means to do the same. But the saving is invisible and decades away, while the effort is today. Every month he puts it off. He never feels what it\u2019s costing him.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The certainty only holds **like for like**. A cheaper fund holding different assets is a different bet. Switching can cost money through exit charges or tax. And cheap can lack things worth paying for, such as advice.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Like for like, a lower fee is **a sure gain**. That\u2019s rare enough that it deserves to come first. Look at fees **before you pick** anything else.'}]}}}},1007,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'the-exercise-findings-that-held',kind:'book',promise:'Find out which exercise advice holds up',about:'Which exercise tips are true? The Mindroll team checked which claims held up when studies were done again. Some tips are dull but true. Others were sold as much bigger than they are. Which ones do you follow?',ideas:{'the-exercise-findings-that-held_1':{title:'The Steepest Step',card:{insight:'The biggest health gain comes from going from nothing to a little.',tryThis:'If you hardly move, walk ten minutes today. Count it as a win.',skills:['health','habits']},review:{prompt:'A friend who never works out asks where to start. What do you say?',right:'A ten-minute walk most days, counted as a win',wrong:'A full plan of five gym days every week',why:'The biggest gain comes at the very start, so a small start counts most.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two people each add **one short walk** a day. One never moves at all. The other already runs every morning. Which of them gets the **bigger health boost**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'It\u2019s the one who never moved. Scientists tracked the risk of dying early. It fell most for people going from **nothing to a little**. After that, each extra bit still helped, but **less and less**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'If you hardly move, **a small start** is where you\u2019ll gain the most. You don\u2019t need a huge plan. **Ten minutes** most days is a real win.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Dan hasn\u2019t exercised in years. On New Year\u2019s Day he signs up for **five gym sessions** a week, an hour each. By the third week he\u2019s stopped. What did his plan **get wrong**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Dan\u2019s plan was built for someone already fit. To him it felt like a wall. After two weeks of aching, he decided exercise just wasn\u2019t for him. He went back to **doing nothing** at all.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s what the research says. The biggest drop in the risk of dying early comes from going from **nothing to a little**. That\u2019s well short of the usual guidelines. After that, the curve **flattens out**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The review calls this **the least exciting** finding, and **one of the best** supported.',left:{title:'Dan\u2019s plan',items:['Five hours a week','Made for the fit','Dropped by week three']},right:{title:'The steep step',items:['Ten minutes, most days','Made for starting out','The biggest gain']}},{kind:'moral',text:'If you do nothing, you\u2019re the one with **the most to gain**. And the amount you need is small. So start **unimpressively**, and count it.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture a graph of exercise against **risk of early death**. You might expect a steady slope, where each extra hour helps the same. It doesn\u2019t look like that. So where is the line **steepest**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'It\u2019s steepest **at the very bottom**. The largest drop in risk comes from going from nothing to a little, well below the usual targets. Then the curve **flattens considerably**. The review calls this the field\u2019s most **practically important** fact.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Dan, who hadn\u2019t exercised in years. He signed up for five hour-long gym sessions a week. By week three he\u2019d stopped, sure that exercise wasn\u2019t for him. The target was pitched at someone already fit.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take June, who has run five mornings a week for years. She adds a sixth. It still helps, but only a little. A daily ten-minute walk would have done far more for Dan than that run does for her.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The curve describes **whole populations**, not a promise to you. It\u2019s about the risk of dying early, not fitness or performance. And flatter isn\u2019t flat: the gains shrink, but they don\u2019t stop.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The biggest gain sits with **the person doing nothing**, and it costs very little. If that\u2019s you, skip the programme. **Ten minutes, most days**, is the useful target.'}]},'the-exercise-findings-that-held_2':{title:'Who Chose to Exercise',card:{insight:'People who choose to work out differ in other ways, so gains look too big.',tryThis:'When you read a health claim, ask if people chose it or were picked.',skills:['science','thinking']},review:{prompt:'A headline says runners are much healthier than other people. What do you ask first?',right:'Did they choose to run, or were they picked by chance?',wrong:'How many miles a week do the runners go?',why:'People who choose to run differ in other ways, so the gap looks too big.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Kids in an after-school **running club** are healthier than kids who aren\u2019t. So running makes you healthy, right? Maybe. But first ask **who joined** the club.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Kids who pick a running club might **sleep more** and eat better already. Some of their good health came with them. A fairer test picks people **by chance**, like names from a hat. Those tests find smaller gains.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Exercise still helps you. But big claims often mix in **everything else** about the people who chose it. So you should trust the tests where people were **picked by chance**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A report says people who cycle to work are **far healthier** than people who drive. It sounds like proof. But before you believe **the size** of that gap, ask one question.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture the cyclists. Many earn more, sleep better and eat well. They\u2019re more likely to see a doctor. Some of the gap in the report was there **before anyone** got on a bike.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Good studies try to subtract these differences. They **adjust** for income, say. But they can only adjust for what they measured, and that\u2019s **never all of it**. Whatever was missed travels with the exercise and gets counted as its effect.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Two kinds of study, and the **headlines** nearly always carry **the first**.',left:{title:'Watching',items:['Studies people who chose','Adjusts for what it measured','Bigger, flattering numbers']},right:{title:'Randomised trial',items:['Assigns exercise by chance','Groups start out alike','Smaller trials, less flattering results']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Exercise works. But the headline numbers are **inflated** by everything that comes with being someone who runs. You\u2019ll get a truer picture from **the randomised trials**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve read that people who exercise are far healthier than people who don\u2019t. Many of those claims come from **watching people** who already exercise. So how much of the gap is the exercise, and how much is **who they are**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'People who exercise **by choice** differ from people who don\u2019t. They tend to differ in income, sleep, diet and how often they see a doctor. Observational studies adjust for what they measured, which is **never all of it**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take the cyclists in a report on commuting. They\u2019re far healthier than drivers. But many of them also earn more and sleep better. That part of the gap was there **before the bike**, and the report counts it anyway.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a randomised trial. Researchers assign exercise **by chance**, so the two groups start out alike. Exercise still shows a benefit. But the trials tend to be smaller, and the gains look **less flattering**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. None of this means exercise fails. The inflation is in **the size**, not the existence, of the effect. And trials have limits too: smaller studies give less precise answers. You weigh the evidence, you don\u2019t bin it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Before you trust an effect size, ask **how the groups** came to be. Weight the **randomised trials**. They\u2019re less exciting, and they\u2019re closer to the truth.'}]},'the-exercise-findings-that-held_3':{title:'Strength Joins the List',card:{insight:'Muscle and bone fade with age, but muscle grows with hard work at any age.',tryThis:'Today, do wall push-ups until the last few feel hard.',skills:['health','habits']},review:{prompt:'Your uncle is seventy and thinks he is too old to get stronger. What is true?',right:'Muscle still grows with hard work at any age',wrong:'Old people should stick to slow walks to stay safe',why:'Muscle grows with hard work at any age, so guides now ask for strength work.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your gran carries a heavy crate of logs up her front steps **every week**. She\u2019s never been to a gym. Is that exercise? And does it count **as much** as a run?'},{kind:'explain',text:'For years, exercise advice was mostly about **your heart**. Now strength work is part of it too. As people age, muscles and bones get weaker, and falls get likelier. But muscles grow stronger with **hard work**, at any age.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Gran\u2019s crate counts. **Strength work** is now part of the advice, because the evidence changed. You don\u2019t need a gym, just something that feels **hard by the end**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'For a long time, the exercise message was mostly about **your heart rate**. Strength work sat with the optional extras. Then the major guidelines changed their minds. What made them **move it**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Much of the push came from research on ageing. Muscle mass and grip strength turned out to predict who **stays independent** later in life. And both respond to loading, even late on. That tied strength to something everyone wants.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The review says the **stimulus** matters, not **the equipment**.',left:{title:'What held',items:['Twice a week','Major muscle groups','Hard final repetitions']},right:{title:'What didn\u2019t',items:['It must be a gym','It must be free weights']}},{kind:'explain',text:'The modest version is **twice a week**, covering your major muscle groups. The load should feel genuinely hard by the last few repetitions. Carrying bags, climbing stairs or wall press-ups can all count. What matters is **the version you\u2019ll do**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Strength work **isn\u2019t optional** any more. It\u2019s one of the clearer cases of advice changing because **the evidence changed**. Two hard sessions a week is the line you\u2019re aiming for.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Most of this review is about findings that **shrank** when people looked closer. This one went the other way. Strength work moved from optional extra to **explicitly recommended**. Why did it grow when so much else shrank?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The drivers are **muscle mass** and **bone density**. Both decline with age, and both matter for later outcomes, especially falls and frailty. Muscle also responds to loading at any age. So the guidance had a clear reason to change.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture Rosa, seventy-five, carrying her shopping up three flights of stairs. No gym, no free weights. Under the newer guidance, that load counts, if it\u2019s hard by the top. She\u2019s working the strength that predicts later independence.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Sam, who has no gym nearby and dislikes weights. He does wall press-ups and carries heavy bags, twice a week, until the last few reps burn. The evidence backs **the stimulus**, so his plan fits it.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Grip strength predicting independence doesn\u2019t prove training secures it. Strength may partly be **a sign** of general health. The review calls the evidence reasonably strong. And twice a week is where guidelines settled, not a measured best.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Strength work earned its place because **the evidence changed**. You don\u2019t need a gym. Load your **major muscles** twice a week, hard by the final reps, in whatever way you\u2019ll keep doing.'}]},'the-exercise-findings-that-held_4':{title:'The Small Afterburn',card:{insight:'The extra burn after a workout is real, but it is small.',tryThis:'Take the last fitness claim you believed. Ask if it is real, and as big as they said.',skills:['science','thinking']},review:{prompt:'An app says its short workout beats an hour of walking, because of the afterburn. True?',right:'The extra burn is real, but it is small',wrong:'A short, hard workout burns lots more for hours',why:'The afterburn is real, but it is far too small to make up the gap.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Turn off a **cooker ring** and hold your hand above it. It\u2019s still warm. That heat is real. But could it **boil a kettle** of water?'},{kind:'explain',text:'After very hard exercise, your body keeps using **extra energy** for a while. People call it the afterburn. Some ads said it burned hundreds of calories. Careful tests mostly found **tens**, not hundreds.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The afterburn is real, like the warm ring. It\u2019s just **much smaller** than you were told. Hard workouts are still worth doing, just **not for that**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A gym poster promises that a short, intense class keeps you **burning calories** for hours afterwards. It\u2019s the reason you picked that class over a longer ride. How big is that **afterburn**, really?'},{kind:'story',text:'Meet Priya. She swapped her hour-long Sunday ride for a twenty-minute interval class. The instructor told her the afterburn would make up the difference, and more. She believed it, because it sounded scientific, and it **partly was**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Extra energy use after intense exercise **does exist**, and it can be measured. But in most controlled work it\u2019s **tens of calories**, not the several hundred that were sold with interval training. It\u2019s largest after work few people sustain.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Nobody invented the afterburn. A **real effect** was stretched to **sell a workout**.',left:{title:'What was sold',items:['Hundreds of extra calories','Short beats long on energy']},right:{title:'What tests found',items:['Tens of calories, mostly','The numbers don\u2019t show it']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Priya\u2019s class is still worth doing. It just won\u2019t out-burn the ride. Pick between them on what **you enjoy** and **what you\u2019ll keep**, not on the afterburn.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Interval training was sold with a promise: lots of **extra burning** after you stop. The studies behind it were real. The effect was real. So what went wrong between **the lab** and the advert?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The afterburn is **excess oxygen use** after exercise. Measured properly, it\u2019s a modest fraction of the session itself, usually **tens of calories**. It\u2019s largest after very intense work, the kind few people sustain week after week.'},{kind:'story',text:'Priya swapped her hour-long ride for a twenty-minute interval class. She was told the afterburn would close the gap. To do that, it would need to be large. It isn\u2019t, so **the claim fails** even though the effect exists.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Tom, who picks his class by which one promises the biggest afterburn. He hates intervals and goes twice, then stops. He chose on a rounding error and lost the thing that mattered: turning up.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. This is a verdict on one argument, not on interval training, which **is worth doing**. A small effect isn\u2019t nothing. And the mistake runs both ways: you can also dismiss a real effect because it was once oversold.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Of any fitness claim, ask if it\u2019s real and if it\u2019s **big enough**. The afterburn is real. It just **fails the second test**, so don\u2019t let it pick your workout.'}]},'the-exercise-findings-that-held_5':{title:'The Plan You Keep',card:{insight:'The best workout plan is the one you will still be doing in a year.',tryThis:'Ask if you would still do your workout on a cold, tired day.',skills:['habits','health']},review:{prompt:'A friend who hates mornings joins a dawn boot camp a study rated best. What now?',right:'Pick a plan he will still be doing in a year',wrong:'Stick with the boot camp, since a study rated it best',why:'Plans differ a bit, but the ones who keep going do far better.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your cousin buys **the best running shoes** in the shop and finds a perfect plan online. He runs hard for two weeks. Then it rains. Will he **still be running** next winter?'},{kind:'story',text:'He stops when it rains, and he never starts again. His friend just joins a **Saturday football game** with her mates. It\u2019s not a clever plan. A year later, she\u2019s still playing every week.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The best plan is **the one you keep**. Pick something you enjoy, that fits your week and that you\u2019d do on **a wet day**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two people start exercising in January. One researches **the best programme** and follows it to the letter. The other joins a friend for a fairly hard weekly session. Who\u2019s **better off** by next January?'},{kind:'story',text:'Alex, the researcher, is flawless until March. Then work gets busy, the mornings stay dark, and the programme stops. Jo\u2019s session is nothing special. But she likes her friend, it fits her week, and she\u2019s **still going** in January.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Studies comparing training programmes mostly find **small differences**. The gap between people who keep going and people who stop is **far larger**. So a decent plan done for years beats a perfect one dropped in March.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Keeping going depends on **your life**, not **your physiology**.',left:{title:'The trial asks',items:['Which protocol wins?','What does physiology say?']},right:{title:'You should ask',items:['Do I enjoy it?','Does it fit my week?','Does it feel like me?']}},{kind:'moral',text:'The best programme is mostly the one **you\u2019ll still do** a year from now. So choose for **bad weather** and tired days, not for the best study.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Fitness writers love to argue about **the best protocol**: intervals or steady runs, three sets or five. The research does find differences. They\u2019re just small. So what actually **separates** the people who get results?'},{kind:'explain',text:'It\u2019s **adherence**: whether you keep going. The gap between people who continue and people who stop dwarfs the gap between programmes. A moderately effective plan done for years beats **an optimal one** abandoned in March.'},{kind:'story',text:'Alex picks the top-rated programme and follows it perfectly, until March. Jo joins a friend\u2019s weekly session that\u2019s merely decent. By next January, only one of them is still exercising. On paper Alex chose better. In practice Jo did.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Ben, who hates early mornings. He joins a dawn bootcamp because a trial rated it best. He misses four of the first six sessions. The best protocol, for him, is one he\u2019ll actually attend.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The review admits the evidence is **weakest here**. Keeping going clearly matters. How to make people keep going is far less clear. And an easy plan can still miss something, like strength work.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Choose the version you\u2019d do **in bad weather**, not the one with the best study. Tuning a protocol is easy to publish. **Keeping going** is what changes your results.'}]}}}},1008,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'the-meeting-that-was-a-decision',kind:'book',promise:'Make the call instead of holding a meeting',about:'Why do so many weekly meetings end with nothing decided? Often, the group meets so no one has to choose alone. This short article by the Mindroll team shows how to spot that. Which of your meetings does this?',ideas:{'the-meeting-that-was-a-decision_1':{title:'Nobody Owns the Choice',card:{insight:'When a whole group decides, no one owns the choice or the blame.',tryThis:'Pick one weekly meeting. Write down who would get the blame if its last choice went wrong.',skills:['leadership','productivity']},review:{prompt:'No one can say who owns a choice from the weekly meeting. What is going on?',right:'The meeting spreads the choice so no one owns it',wrong:'The team shares the choice fairly, as it should',why:'If no name comes up, the meeting is spreading out the blame.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your class must choose **a theme** for the end-of-term party. The class rep could just pick one. Instead, everyone talks about it for **three lunchtimes**. What do you end up with?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A theme **nobody much wanted**. Why does this happen? If the rep chooses alone and it goes wrong, it\u2019s her fault. If the whole class chooses, it\u2019s **nobody\u2019s fault**. That feels safer, so choices drift to the group.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When everyone decides, **nobody does**. Talking it over feels safe, but it takes much longer. And groups often pick **the safe, boring** choice, not the best one you could have had.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Every Tuesday, eight people meet to **review** a project. Decisions get discussed, and things move on. But ask who you\u2019d blame if last week\u2019s call goes wrong. **Nobody** can name anyone. What is that meeting for?'},{kind:'explain',text:'It\u2019s often **distributing responsibility**. A call one person could make is brought to a group, and the outcome becomes **nobody\u2019s in particular**. Nobody planned this. Everyone gains locally, because a decision made alone can be wrong in a way a group one can\u2019t.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The cost is **speed and quality**. Groups drift toward what\u2019s **defensible**, not what\u2019s correct.',left:{title:'Group-owned call',items:['Weeks of meetings','Drifts to the defensible option']},right:{title:'One owner',items:['Decided in a day','Free to pick the right option']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Spreading the weight of a call is **a real function**, and you may sometimes want it. But it\u2019s an expensive way to buy cover. Ask **whose name** is on each decision.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture **eight pairs of hands** on one heavy block. Each touches it so lightly that none is really taking the weight. From across the room, the block looks **carried**. Is it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'That\u2019s many **standing meetings**. A decision one person could own is brought to a group. The group discusses it, and the outcome is attributable to no one. Each person gains **cover**, and nobody pays a visible price.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take a weekly review that has run for a year. It\u2019s billed as an update, so nobody questions it. But ask whose name is on its last decision, and the room goes quiet. **No name** is available.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a supplier choice that one manager could make in a day. Brought to a committee, it takes **three weeks**. The committee picks the option nobody could be blamed for, not the one the manager thought best.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some decisions **genuinely need a group**, because the facts are spread across people. A call that commits everyone may need everyone\u2019s agreement. And cover is sometimes worth buying. The objection is to buying it by habit.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Shared ownership **looks like** teamwork. Often it means nobody carries the call. Ask who you\u2019d hold accountable, and if **no name comes**, you know what the meeting is doing.'}]},'the-meeting-that-was-a-decision_2':{title:'Who Could Decide Alone',card:{insight:'If one person could make the call, ask why there is a meeting.',tryThis:'Look at your next weekly meeting. Write down who could make the call alone.',skills:['leadership','productivity']},review:{prompt:'A team lead is not sure she may hire alone. What helps?',right:'Her boss tells her in writing she can decide alone',wrong:'Keep a group for every hire, so the team backs it',why:'If she is not sure she may decide, her boss can just say so.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your family spends **a whole hour** choosing between two days out. Mum could have just picked one and told everyone. So why might she **ask everybody** instead? Can you think of a reason?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Maybe she doesn\u2019t want **the blame** if it rains. Maybe she wants to hear ideas. Maybe she isn\u2019t sure it\u2019s **her choice** to make. Each is a real reason, and each has a quicker fix than an hour.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Before a group talks over a choice, ask: **who could just decide** this? Very often someone could. You only need **everyone together** when each person knows a different piece.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Six people spend **forty minutes** choosing between two print suppliers. Everyone agrees the call belongs to the office manager. She holds the budget and the contracts. She opens by asking **what everyone thinks**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The most useful question about any meeting is simple. Could **one person** have made the call and told everyone? If yes, the meeting is **buying them cover**. Sometimes that\u2019s worth it. It should be a purchase, not a habit.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Usually a name exists, and the reason they didn\u2019t decide points to **the fix**. Cover needs explicit delegation. Input can be gathered in writing. Uncertainty about who decides is fixed by **stating authority**. None of these is a meeting.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The question splits meetings into **two kinds**, and only **one kind** justifies the room.',left:{title:'Needs the room',items:['Facts spread across people','Assembled live, together']},right:{title:'Needs one owner',items:['A name is available','Decide, then tell everyone']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Ask who could have **decided alone**. If you find a name, find out why they didn\u2019t, and fix that instead. If you can\u2019t, **you may need** the room.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture **one chair** pulled up to a table, with one sheet and one pen. The other chairs are **stacked by the wall**. How many of your meetings could have looked like that?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Ask who could have decided alone. The question needs **no audit** and no data, and it fits any recurring meeting. In most cases a name is available. The interesting part is why that person **didn\u2019t simply decide**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take a product lead who could set a feature\u2019s priority alone. She keeps calling a meeting because she wants engineering\u2019s view. That\u2019s a need for **input**, and it can be gathered in writing, without the hour.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a team lead who takes every hire to a weekly panel. She isn\u2019t after input. She\u2019s unsure she\u2019s **allowed** to decide. One line from her boss stating her authority could end the panel.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The question can strip out **consultation that matters**: people affected may deserve a voice. A name being available doesn\u2019t mean that person knows enough. And delegation only gives cover if bosses honour it later.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Most calls need only **the person who owns** them. When you find that name, ask why they didn\u2019t decide. Cover, input or doubt about authority each has **a cheaper fix**.'}]},'the-meeting-that-was-a-decision_3':{title:'The Room That Only Grows',card:{insight:'A seat in the room shows you count, so the list keeps growing.',tryThis:'On your next invite, say in writing who can skip it. Send the notes to everyone.',skills:['leadership','communication']},review:{prompt:'A weekly meeting has grown to sixteen people and stopped deciding. What helps most?',right:'Shrink the room and send the notes to everyone',wrong:'Ask the newer people to stop coming',why:'The notes keep everyone in the know, so the room can shrink.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You plan **a small party** for five friends. Then you think: if I leave out Sam, he\u2019ll feel bad. And Sam\u2019s best friend. Soon there are **fourteen**. Will the game you planned still work?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Being invited shows **you matter**. So inviting someone feels kind, and taking them off the list feels mean. That means lists grow and **almost never shrink**, until the group is too big to do its job.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Here\u2019s a kind fix. Keep **the planning group** small, then **tell everyone** the plan. Nobody misses the news, and the small group can get on with it.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A project meeting starts with **five people**. Over a year, a few more get added, and nobody ever leaves. By now it\u2019s fourteen. **Nothing gets decided**. How did you get here without anyone choosing it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Attendance is **a status signal**. Being in the room reads as mattering, and being left out reads as the opposite. People read it correctly. So inviting is generous and uninviting is **a demotion**. The asymmetry only points one way.'},{kind:'explain',text:'And nothing pushes back, because **the cost is hidden**. It\u2019s spread across salaries instead of showing as one line. So the list grows until the meeting can\u2019t do its job. A discussion with fourteen people **isn\u2019t a discussion**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Removing people is a hard sell. **Separating the signal** from the seat is **much easier**.',left:{title:'Cutting the list',items:['Reads as a demotion','People resist it']},right:{title:'Publishing the notes',items:['Everyone still gets the news','The room shrinks to working size']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Keep the room **small enough to work**, and publish the notes to a wider group than the attendees. You keep **everyone included** in the news without removing anyone.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture **a full room**, and a queue still pressing through the door. Nobody in the queue is asking what the meeting is for. So what are they **really queuing for**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'They\u2019re queuing for **the signal**. Being in the room shows you\u2019re relevant, and people aren\u2019t simply being vain. Being left out **really does** signal the opposite. That\u2019s why asking them to decline is a hard sell.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take a steering group that has grown to sixteen. Nobody will volunteer to leave, and nobody wants to cut a colleague. So it meets weekly and **decides nothing**. The cost, spread across salaries, never appears anywhere.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now the chair tries something else. She marks most invitees optional and sends the notes to all sixteen. Five keep coming. Everyone still hears what was decided, and the five can **finally decide**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Notes aren\u2019t a perfect substitute: what gets written is filtered, and reading isn\u2019t being there. Some status is about **influence**, not information. And optional only feels safe if nobody is quietly marked down for it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'People join meetings for **the signal**, not the work. Give them the signal through the notes, and you can **shrink the room** without demoting anyone you work with.'}]},'the-meeting-that-was-a-decision_4':{title:'Write the Plan First',card:{insight:'Send a plan first, so the group only has to find what is wrong.',tryThis:'Send the plan and your pick before the next meeting. Ask people what is wrong with it.',skills:['communication','leadership']},review:{prompt:'You are calling a meeting to choose a new booking system. What do you send first?',right:'A written plan with your pick, asking what is wrong',wrong:'A note that asks each person to bring ideas',why:'Groups fix a plan on paper much faster than they make one.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Imagine your class writing **a story from nothing**, all shouting ideas at once. Now imagine your teacher hands out a story and asks **what\u2019s wrong** with it. Which goes faster, do you think?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Fixing a story is **much faster**. Building one from nothing is slow, and the loudest voices win. There\u2019s a bonus too. Some people won\u2019t say *I disagree* out loud, but they\u2019ll **write a note** on the page.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Before a group meets to decide, **write the plan down** and share it. Then the group only has to find **what\u2019s wrong**. Sometimes writing it solves the problem first.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You call a meeting to choose **a new booking system**. Nobody has written anything down. So the hour goes on listing options, in whatever order they come up. Who do you think **wins that room**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Whoever **speaks most fluently**. Building options live is slow, and the discussion is shaped by the order points were raised. None of that has anything to do with **which option** is best.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'A written proposal sent first changes **what the room** is doing, from **generation** to critique.',left:{title:'No proposal',items:['Options built live','Favours fluent speakers','Doubts get smoothed over']},right:{title:'Proposal sent first',items:['Options already exist','Room hunts for flaws','Objections go on the page']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Critique is a task groups are **genuinely good at**. People will annotate an objection they\u2019d never voice across a table. And writing the proposal often **settles the question** before anyone meets, which saves the hour entirely.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Send the proposal **and a recommendation** before the room meets. Ask for **objections**, not thoughts, so you keep the hour for finding what\u2019s wrong.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A team must settle **a contested question**. A junior member thinks the favoured plan has a flaw. In a spoken round-table, will she say so? What if the plan were **on paper** first?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Without a proposal, a meeting must **generate** options live. With one, its job is to **critique** options that already exist. Generating in the room is slow and skewed. Testing what\u2019s written is something groups do well.'},{kind:'story',text:'Back to the junior member. Across the table, the round-table smooths her doubt away before she speaks. Given the written plan a day early, she marks the flaw in the margin. Now the room has to deal with it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a manager who sits down to write up a reorganisation, with reasons. Halfway through, the reasoning stops holding together. She drops the plan **before anyone meets**. The writing did the work the meeting would have done worse.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Whoever writes the proposal **frames the options**, and critique tends to stay inside that frame. A recommendation can anchor the room. Writing favours fluent writers. And some problems are open, where building together is the point.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Put the decision **on the page** before the room meets. You\u2019ll hear objections that talk would smooth over, and you\u2019ll often find **no meeting** was needed.'}]},'the-meeting-that-was-a-decision_5':{title:'The Month Off Test',card:{insight:'Stop a meeting you doubt for a month, and what people need will come back.',tryThis:'Pick one weekly meeting you doubt. Stop it for a month and list what comes up.',skills:['productivity','leadership']},review:{prompt:'After a month off, the only thing missed was a deadline check. Now what?',right:'Keep the meeting off and meet that one need a cheaper way',wrong:'Bring the full weekly meeting back to be safe',why:'The test shows what was needed, and a small fix can cover it.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your club has met **every Friday lunch** for a year. Nobody is sure it helps. So you stop it for a month. What do you think **happens next**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The things people **really needed** pop back up, often smaller and easier to sort. Asking what a meeting is for rarely works, because much of what it does is small and unplanned. When it stops, people **notice what\u2019s missing**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Not sure a meeting is useful? **Stop it** for a month. If it was needed, you can **start it again**, so trying costs very little.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A team has an **hour-long status meeting** every Monday. Nobody can say exactly what it produces, and nobody wants to be the one to kill it. You could argue about it for months. Or **could you test it**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Say the team suspends it for a month. In week one, nobody knows the release date, and a two-line weekly post fixes it. In week two, a designer misses a check with engineering and books fifteen minutes. **Nothing else** comes up.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Why not just ask? A meeting\u2019s functions are **mostly undocumented**: coordination in the margins, visibility, the thing someone mentions at the end. Suspending it makes them **visible by absence**. Then they can be named, and served more cheaply.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t debate a doubtful meeting. **Suspend it** for a month and watch what surfaces. The risk is low, because **you can reinstate it**, and arguing settles far less.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'The **Thursday review** has been off for a month. Nobody has missed the review itself. But two small questions **have come up** that nobody can answer. What is that telling you?'},{kind:'story',text:'On day nine, someone asks who\u2019s tracking supplier dates. On day twenty, a manager asks how she\u2019ll now hear about slips. Two gaps, **both specific**. Neither was on any list of what the review was for.'},{kind:'explain',text:'That\u2019s the point of the test. Much of what a meeting produces is **incidental**, so nobody can list it in advance. Ask what it\u2019s for and you get its stated purpose. Its absence reveals **its working ones**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a weekly sync is suspended, and **nothing at all** surfaces in a month. What wasn\u2019t missed probably wasn\u2019t being used. The team keeps it off and gets an hour a week back.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some functions surface slowly, or only in a crisis. A meeting kept for emergencies can look useless for a month. Newer staff may feel gaps nobody else notices. And **trust built** in the room may have no smaller replacement.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A month off is **a test**, not a proof. Still, it beats arguing: **you can reinstate** the meeting, and what you learn is specific enough to act on.'}]}}}},1009,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'the-money-conversation',kind:'book',promise:'Talk about money with the ones you love',about:'Why is it so hard to tell someone close what you earn or owe? Most of us use soft words instead. That can cost a lot later. These ideas show how to talk about money calmly. What number have you been afraid to say?',ideas:{'the-money-conversation_1':{title:'Say the Number',card:{insight:'Soft money words feel safe, but they let people guess wrong for years.',tryThis:'Next time you would say fine about money, say the real number.',skills:['money','communication']},review:{prompt:'Your husband asks if you can pay for a vacation this year. What do you say?',right:'Say the real number you can spend on it',wrong:'Say you are fine, so he does not worry',why:'A real number lets you both plan, so no one has to guess.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You and your brother are buying Mum a present. You ask how much he has. He says **loads**. So you pick a nice scarf. At the till, loads turns out to be **\xa33**. What went wrong?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Words like loads or not much feel safe. But they **hide the number**, so the other person has to guess. Saying the real number feels odd for a moment. Then you\u2019re both **working from the truth**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A vague word **leaves people guessing**. Say the **real number** instead. It feels awkward for a second, and often the other person is just relieved.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Asked about money, you\u2019ve said **fine** for years. So has your partner. Then one of you loses a job, and you both reach for the savings. Whose savings were you **counting on**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Ana and Joe. Each quietly assumed the other\u2019s savings would cover a gap. Neither ever said a figure out loud. When Joe\u2019s job went, they compared notes for the first time. Both had been leaning on the same thin cushion.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Vagueness isn\u2019t neutral. It lets two people hold **assumptions that don\u2019t match** for years. And it\u2019s comfortable in every single moment, so there\u2019s never a reason to end it today. That\u2019s why it needs **a set occasion**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Ana and Joe used **soft words**. A **real figure** would have shown the gap years earlier.',left:{title:'The word',items:['Fine','Comfortable','A bit tight']},right:{title:'The number',items:['\xa32,000 in savings','Covers three months\u2019 rent','\xa30 spare until payday']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Vague money words feel safe, but **they aren\u2019t neutral**. Swap one for **the real number**. The moment is awkward, and what comes back is often relief.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Most homes run on words like **comfortable**, **tight** and fine. Nobody\u2019s lying. Nobody\u2019s hiding much. So why would a habit that feels this harmless turn out to be **so expensive**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The cost is **deferred**. Vague words let plans rest on the other person\u2019s money behaving in ways **nobody confirmed**. The bill arrives at the worst moment: a redundancy, an illness, a separation. Then every assumption is tested at once.'},{kind:'story',text:'Ana and Joe said *fine* for six years. Each assumed the other had savings to fall back on. When Joe was made redundant, they finally compared figures. The cushion they\u2019d both been counting on barely covered a month.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a job. You and your manager never discuss pay in figures. You assume a raise is coming this year. She assumes you\u2019re happy where you are. Nobody finds out until review season, when it\u2019s too late to plan.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. A number isn\u2019t always safe to share. Where one partner **controls the other**, a figure can become a weapon. And a number only settles what it measures. You can agree the figure and still disagree about it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Vagueness is comfortable in every moment, which is **why it lasts**. Don\u2019t wait to feel ready. Give **the real number** a set occasion, before a crisis tests it for you.'}]},'the-money-conversation_2':{title:'Who You Compare To',card:{insight:'Money shame comes from feeling behind where you think you should be.',tryThis:'When money talk makes you tense, name the people you compare yourself to.',skills:['money','psychology']},review:{prompt:'A friend with good pay feels sick when people ask what she earns. What helps?',right:'Help her name who she compares her money to',wrong:'Tell her that her pay is really quite good',why:'She feels behind someone, so praise for her pay will not help.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Sam gets **\xa32 pocket money** a week and says so. Jo gets \xa310, but lets her friends think she gets \xa320. When pocket money comes up, who feels **worse**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Jo does. The bad feeling isn\u2019t about **how much** she has. It\u2019s about the gap between what she has and **what people think**. Each time you pretend, it gets harder to stop, like standing on tiptoe for too long.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Feeling bad about money is often about **the gap**, not the amount. Ask where your idea of *should* came from. It\u2019s often **just a few people**, not everyone.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two friends earn very different salaries. One earns modestly and has never hidden it. The other earns well, but **less than people assume**. Which of them dreads the question **what do you earn**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Leah, the second friend. For years she\u2019s kept up the restaurants and holidays her friends expect. Her salary isn\u2019t the secret any more. The secret is the **performance** around it, and each month it runs, dropping it means admitting more.'},{kind:'explain',text:'So the shame doesn\u2019t **track income**. It measures the distance from a standard. That\u2019s why telling Leah her salary is fine won\u2019t help. **The amount** was never the problem. The comparison is.'},{kind:'explain',text:'What helps is **naming the standard**. Ask what you\u2019re measuring against and where it came from. Often it\u2019s inherited, or drawn from **a few friends** whose spending you can see. Said aloud, it loses its grip.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Money shame is about **the gap**, not the figure. Reassurance about the amount can\u2019t reach it. **Name the standard** you\u2019re using, and it often shrinks.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019d expect people who earn little to feel the **most shame** about money. Often it\u2019s the other way round. Someone on a **good salary** can dread the subject far more. Why would that be?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Money shame grows from **the gap** between where you are and where you think you ought to be. So it tracks distance from **a standard**, not the figure. Raise the standard high enough and a good salary can feel like failure.'},{kind:'story',text:'Leah earns well, but less than her friends assume. She\u2019s spent years matching their restaurants and holidays. Now the secret isn\u2019t her pay. It\u2019s the whole performance, and every month it runs makes stopping cost more.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Tom, who feels behind at forty. Asked where his *should* comes from, he names his father\u2019s career and three university friends. Said out loud, that sample sounds thin. The feeling loosens more than any pep talk managed.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Sometimes the amount really is the problem. If you can\u2019t pay the rent, questioning your standards won\u2019t pay it. And sometimes **the comparison is fair**, and the gap is worth acting on.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The shame is rarely about **the amount**. It\u2019s about a standard you may never have chosen. Say it out loud, ask **where it came from**, and see how much is left.'}]},'the-money-conversation_3':{title:'The Quiet Week',card:{insight:'Money talks go badly after a bill and well in a calm week.',tryThis:'Put a money talk on your calendar for a calm week. Set it to repeat each month.',skills:['relationships','money']},review:{prompt:'Your monthly money talk falls in a week with no bills. What now?',right:'Have the talk anyway, since a calm week is best',wrong:'Skip it this month, since there is nothing to fix',why:'A calm week is the best time to talk, so keep the date.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your school practises **fire drills**. But it never waits for a real fire to do it. It picks an ordinary morning, with no smoke and no panic. Why choose **a normal day**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'On a calm day, everyone can **think clearly**. Money talks work the same way. Don\u2019t wait for a problem, when someone\u2019s already upset. Pick a day ahead, like **the first Sunday** of the month. Then the date is the reason.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Money talks go best when **nothing is wrong**. Since there\u2019s never a reason on a calm day, **fix the date** in advance. Then you won\u2019t need one.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'The **card statement** lands, higher than either of you expected. Within a minute someone asks what that was for. An hour later, you\u2019re both tired and nobody\u2019s mentioned **what you want**. How did it go so wrong?'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The same conversation changes with **its timing**. Held during a problem, it\u2019s **a negotiation**.',left:{title:'After a bill',items:['Someone\u2019s already defensive','Something\u2019s already lost','The aim is damage control']},right:{title:'A quiet week',items:['Nobody has anything to defend','Nothing\u2019s lost yet','Disagreeing costs nothing']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Everyone agrees the calm version is better. It still **doesn\u2019t happen**, because raising money needs a reason, and an ordinary week supplies none. So the first reason that arrives is a bill. The fix isn\u2019t **more willpower**. It\u2019s a date.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Mei and Raj. They booked the first Saturday of each month, over coffee, and set it to repeat. Most months nothing\u2019s wrong. They talk about plans instead, and nobody has to win.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Hold the money talk when **nothing is wrong**. A quiet week will never give you a reason, so **let the calendar** give one. Book it, and set it to repeat.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two couples have the same money talk. One has it the night **a bill arrives**. The other has it on an ordinary Sunday, with **nothing due**. Same people, same numbers. Why do they get such different results?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A talk prompted by a problem starts with someone **defensive** and something already lost. Its job is damage control. A talk in an ordinary week can **explore** instead. With nothing at stake, a disagreement costs you nothing.'},{kind:'story',text:'Mei and Raj used to talk money only after a shock: a car repair, a big bill. Every talk became a row. Then they booked the first Saturday of each month. Now most of those Saturdays are dull, and that\u2019s the point.'},{kind:'story',text:'Or take a small team that only discusses its budget when it\u2019s overspent. Each meeting is a hunt for who\u2019s to blame. A review booked every quarter, whatever the numbers, turns the same figures into plain information.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Problems don\u2019t wait for the calendar. When a bill lands, deal with it. A fixed date can **harden into ritual**, where the real subjects get skipped. And if one of you dreads every meeting, a date won\u2019t fix that.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A calm week turns money talk into **plain information**. The trouble is that a calm week never gives you a reason. So don\u2019t rely on deciding, and **let a repeating date** choose for you.'}]},'the-money-conversation_4':{title:'Safe or Free',card:{insight:'Most money fights are about what the money is for, not how much.',tryThis:'Pick a money fight you have had twice. Write what each of you wants the money to do.',skills:['relationships','money']},review:{prompt:'You and your husband fight every month about how much to save. What first?',right:'Ask what each of you wants the money to do',wrong:'Make a budget to find the right amount to save',why:'The fight is about two goals, so name what the money is for.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You and your sister share **\xa340 of birthday money**. She wants to keep it in the jar. You want a day out. Every week you argue, and neither of you budges. Who\u2019s **in the right**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Maybe **both of you**. Your sister likes knowing there\u2019s money there if something breaks. That\u2019s **feeling safe**. You want fun while you can have it. That\u2019s feeling free. You just want the money to do **different jobs**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Two people can **both be right** and still disagree about money. Before you argue about how much, ask **what it\u2019s for**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A couple have argued **about savings** for years. Every row is about a number: how much to put away this month. They\u2019ve tried budgets and spreadsheets. Why does the argument **keep coming back**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Call them Nina and Ben. Nina wants a bigger cushion before they spend anything. Ben wants to use the money while it can still change their lives. Each hears the other as either anxious or careless.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Neither is wrong. They\u2019re **solving for different things**, and they\u2019ve **never said so**.',left:{title:'Security',items:['Money as a cushion','Afraid of a sudden shock','Calm when it\u2019s saved']},right:{title:'Freedom',items:['Money as choices','Afraid of wasted years','Calm when it\u2019s used']}},{kind:'explain',text:'So change the question. Ask what **this money is protecting** against. Nina says losing a job with nothing in reserve. Ben says years going by with nothing done. Now **both goals** are on the table, and you can plan for each.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Arguments about amounts are **rarely about amounts**. When a money fight keeps coming back, ask **what it\u2019s for**. You\u2019ll usually settle it faster than any spreadsheet could.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two people look at the same savings account. One sees **a locked box** against disaster. The other sees **a packed bag** for a life not yet lived. Neither will give way. So why do they keep fighting?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Each figure someone proposes is the output of **a goal** they haven\u2019t stated. Argue the output and the goal stays hidden. So each side hears the other as careless or anxious, not as **solving a different problem**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Nina and Ben argued about how much to save. Then they asked what the money was protecting against. She named a sudden loss of income. He named a life never lived. They agreed a fixed cushion first, then money to spend.'},{kind:'story',text:'Or take two siblings who inherit a house. One wants to sell, the other to keep it. Ask what each is protecting, and one says a safety net, the other a family place. Now there\u2019s something to plan around, not a stand-off.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Naming goals doesn\u2019t remove the trade-off: a pound saved is still a pound unspent. And not every goal is sound. *Freedom* can label spending you **can\u2019t afford**, and security can mask control.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A spreadsheet answers **how much**. Many money rows are really about **what for**. Name the goal on each side first, and you\u2019ll often find room for both.'}]},'the-money-conversation_5':{title:'Judge After Three',card:{insight:'Your first money talk is the worst one, and the next is usually easier.',tryThis:'Pick a day for your next money talk. Do it even if the last one was bad.',skills:['communication','relationships']},review:{prompt:'Your first money talk with your boyfriend was stiff and awkward. What now?',right:'Plan the next money talk, and judge it after three',wrong:'Stop, since it shows you two cannot talk about it',why:'The first talk is the hardest, so one bad try tells you little.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You sit down with your dad to talk about **pocket money**. Nobody knows what to say. You both go quiet, and it feels weird. You decide you **never want** to do that again. Should you stop?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Think about **your first day** at a new school. You didn\u2019t know where anything was, or who anyone was. By the second week, it was just school. The first money talk is like that. Everything\u2019s new **all at once**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The first talk is **the hardest one**, and it gets easier fast. So don\u2019t judge it after one try. **Judge it after three**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You and your partner finally sit down to **talk about money**. It\u2019s clumsy. One of you gets defensive, the other goes quiet. Afterwards you agree it didn\u2019t work. Is that **a fair verdict**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The first conversation is the worst for **three reasons**. Everything is unsaid at once. Neither of you knows the other\u2019s position. And you haven\u2019t yet **found shared words**, like what counts as spare or what saving means.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'So **the first talk** is a poor guide to **the practice**.',left:{title:'Stop after one',items:['Pay the cost of starting','Decide it doesn\u2019t work','Miss the easy ones']},right:{title:'Judge after three',items:['Pay the cost once','Second one is easier','Routine by the fourth']}},{kind:'moral',text:'The first money talk is **meant to be awkward**. That\u2019s the cost of starting, and you pay it once. Book the second, and **judge it after three**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Most people try a money talk **exactly once**. It goes badly, so they conclude the subject can\u2019t be discussed. That\u2019s a reasonable-sounding verdict. But what if **the first attempt** was never a fair test?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The cost of starting is paid **in the first talk**. Everything\u2019s unsaid, positions are unknown, and there\u2019s no shared vocabulary. None of that carries into the second in the same form. So the benefit **keeps recurring** while the cost doesn\u2019t.'},{kind:'story',text:'Priya and Sam had their first check-in at the kitchen table. She got defensive, he went quiet, and they nearly called it off. The second was shorter and calmer. By the fourth, it felt like any other chore.'},{kind:'story',text:'It\u2019s like learning to drive. Your first lesson is stalls and nerves, and nobody judges driving by it. Yet that\u2019s exactly how many people judge money talks: one stiff hour, then a lifelong rule.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Awkward isn\u2019t the same as harmful. If the first talk brought **contempt or threats**, more practice isn\u2019t the answer. And three is a rule of thumb. For some couples it stays hard, and they need outside help.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Expect the first money talk to be **clumsy**. That tells you about starting, not about the practice. Don\u2019t stop after one; give it three before **you decide**.'}]}}}},1010,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'the-number-you-say-first',kind:'book',promise:'Know what to say when pay comes up',about:'What do you say when a boss asks how much pay you want? Most people never practice that talk. The Mindroll team looked at what the research says about it. Here are five plain tips. Want to try them?',ideas:{'the-number-you-say-first_1':{title:'Say the First Number',card:{insight:'The first fair number said pulls the final deal toward it.',tryThis:'Pick a fair number for your pay. Then say it out loud once today.',skills:['money','communication']},review:{prompt:'At the end of a job interview, they ask what pay you want. What now?',right:'Name a fair number, so the talk starts from yours',wrong:'Say you are open and ask to hear their number first',why:'If they say a number first, the deal starts from theirs.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re selling your old bike to **a neighbour**. She asks what you want for it. You could name a price, or ask what she\u2019d pay. Which start usually gets you **a better price**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Someone guesses the sweets in a jar: *about 500?* Now everyone\u2019s guesses **creep towards 500**, even if the first was wild. People start from the first number and **move only a little**. Prices work the same way.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The first number said **pulls the final one** towards it. It isn\u2019t a sneaky trick. So when someone asks what you want, have **a fair number** ready, and say it.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Ten minutes into the final interview, the recruiter asks **what salary** you\u2019re looking for. You say you\u2019d rather hear what they had in mind. It feels safe. What have you **just handed** them?'},{kind:'story',text:'In classic demonstrations, people watched **a wheel of fortune** spin to a number. Then they made an unrelated estimate. Their estimates shifted towards the number on the wheel. It was obviously arbitrary, and it still moved them.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s why. People find a settlement by starting from a number and **adjusting**, and they stop too soon. So the first credible number **shapes the range**. It works on experts, and knowing about it barely weakens it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Anchoring isn\u2019t a trick that needs **a naive target**. It\u2019s how everyone searches for a deal. So if you\u2019re asked for a number, **give one**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Someone tells you anchoring is a cheap trick. Good negotiators **see straight through** it, they say. Know the effect, and it can\u2019t touch you. Is that true? What happens to **the experts**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Anchoring is one of the sturdier findings in **judgement research**. An initial number shifts later estimates, among experts too. It holds when the anchor is **obviously arbitrary**. And knowing about it doesn\u2019t much reduce the effect.'},{kind:'story',text:'In one classic setup, people watched a wheel of fortune being spun. Then they gave an estimate about something unrelated. Their answers leaned towards the wheel\u2019s number. Nobody was fooled at any step, and the number **still pulled**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a candidate asked for her expected salary. She says she\u2019s flexible and would like to hear their range first. It feels polite. But she\u2019s let them **state the first number**, and the deal will form around theirs.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The anchor has to be **credible**. A figure far outside the plausible range can end the talk instead of framing it. And real negotiations add walk-away options and pay rules that lab estimates don\u2019t.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The effect comes from how everyone searches for a settlement, so seeing it **doesn\u2019t cancel it**. That makes **who opens** matter more than how. Have a credible number ready, and say it.'}]},'the-number-you-say-first_2':{title:'The Bottom of the Range',card:{insight:'Most people hear the low end of a range, so start it at your price.',tryThis:'Before your next quote, write the lowest price you would take. Start your range there.',skills:['money','communication']},review:{prompt:'You would take $20 an hour, and a form asks for a range. What now?',right:'A range with $20 at the bottom, not in the middle',wrong:'A range from $18 to $22, so you meet them halfway',why:'People tend to hear the low end of a range, so put your price there.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You ask for more pocket money. You say: *between \xa35 and \xa38 a week?* Your parents smile and say \xa35 sounds fine. What happened to **the \xa38**? Did anyone even **talk about it**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'When you give a range, people often hear **only the lowest number**. They\u2019d rather pay less, so why pick the top? Saying 5 to 8 tells them **5 is fine**. The 8 is easy to ignore.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A range is often heard as **its bottom number**. So if you give one, make the bottom a number you\u2019d be **happy with**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You tell an employer you\u2019re looking for **sixty to seventy thousand**. It feels flexible and cooperative. Then the offer arrives. Where do you think it\u2019s most likely to land, and **why there**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The other side has **no incentive** to consider the top. Saying sixty to seventy tells them sixty is acceptable. The talk carries on from there. You\u2019ve **conceded the bottom** before anyone has negotiated anything.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take an application form that insists on a band. Say sixty-five is what you\u2019d actually accept. Write sixty-five to seventy-five, not fifty-five to seventy-five. The number you\u2019d take belongs at **the bottom**, not in **the middle**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'To show flexibility, you don\u2019t need a range. **One number** plus what would move it **keeps the anchor**.',left:{title:'A loose range',items:['Sixty to seventy','Sixty sounds fine','Bottom conceded early']},right:{title:'Number plus terms',items:['Seventy thousand','Movable for a later start','Anchor stays intact']}},{kind:'moral',text:'A range can **give away** its lowest figure before talks begin. If you must give one, put the number you\u2019d accept **at the bottom**. Better still, give one number and say what would move it.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A freelancer would accept **\xa3500** for a job. The client asks for a quote. Should she say \xa3500, \xa3500 to \xa3600, or \xa3400 to \xa3600? One of those quietly **costs her money**. Which?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A range offers **two numbers**, and the listener can start from whichever suits them. That\u2019s usually the lower one. You\u2019ve also declared it **acceptable**, so the top costs them nothing to ignore.'},{kind:'story',text:'Back to the freelancer. \xa3400 to \xa3600 puts her real figure in the middle, so the client is likely to hear \xa3400. \xa3500 to \xa3600 keeps her number at the bottom, which can do as well as one figure.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture someone who wants to seem flexible in a pay talk. Instead of a range, he names one figure and adds what would change it, such as **the start date**. The anchor stays where he put it.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The case comes from **incentives**, not a study of ranges, so a range only *tends* to be heard this way. Where published pay bands are normal, a single number may just be fitted into the band.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You can\u2019t make a listener weigh **the top** of your range. Treat the bottom as your offer, and set it where **you\u2019d say yes**. Or give one figure and name what would move it.'}]},'the-number-you-say-first_3':{title:'A Number With a Reason',card:{insight:'A number with a true reason is much harder to turn down.',tryThis:'Find one true fact to back your next ask, like what the same job pays nearby.',skills:['communication','career']},review:{prompt:'Two coworkers ask for the same raise. Only one gives a reason. Who likely gets it?',right:'The one with a reason, since saying no needs a reason too',wrong:'The one who asks in the boldest, surest voice',why:'It is hard to say no to a number with a good reason.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two kids ask to stay up later. Ana asks: *can I stay up till nine?* Leo asks the same, then adds that **swim club** now ends at eight. Who\u2019s more likely to **get a yes**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Ana gave **a wish**. It\u2019s easy to say no to a wish. Leo gave **a reason**. To say no to him, his parents need a reason of their own. Often you\u2019ll find they don\u2019t have one.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A number with a reason is **harder to turn down**. So next time you ask for something, bring **one fact** that backs it up, and say it with your ask.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two colleagues ask for **the same raise**. One explains how her role has grown this year. The other says he feels he deserves it. Same number, same manager. Which request do you think **survives**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'An unexplained figure is **a preference**, and it can be declined without argument. The same figure with a basis needs **a counter-argument** to refuse. Frequently there isn\u2019t one. That\u2019s why her request is far harder to wave away.'},{kind:'explain',text:'There\u2019s a second reason. Her manager often can\u2019t approve a raise alone. A reason gives him **something to take** to whoever does. Market data, a grown role, a similar job elsewhere: **her request** becomes his case.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Preparing for a pay talk isn\u2019t mostly about **choosing the number**. It\u2019s about **building the basis**. Gather the evidence first, and you\u2019ll find the number largely follows from it.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A team wants a bigger budget next year. One plan is to wait and see what finance offers. Another is to gather **last year\u2019s results** and a comparable team\u2019s spend. Which do you think gets **taken seriously**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The reason attached to a number decides how it\u2019s received, more than **its size** does. Requests with a reason land differently, **even weak reasons**. So the work of preparing moves from picking a figure to assembling its basis.'},{kind:'story',text:'Back to the team. Waiting for finance hands them the first number. Bringing results and a comparable gives the figure a basis, so refusing it **needs an argument**. Often finance won\u2019t have one ready.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take someone asking for a high figure. With no basis, it reads as **opportunism**, and it\u2019s dismissed. With market data behind it, the same figure reads as **a position**. Positions get negotiated, not waved away.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Weak reasons cut both ways: a thin one may win a hearing, then **fail under scrutiny**. A basis invites a counter-basis, so cherry-picked data can backfire. And no reason creates budget that isn\u2019t there.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A figure without a basis is just **a wish**. With one, it becomes the other side\u2019s problem to answer. So spend your preparation on **the evidence**, and let the number follow.'}]},'the-number-you-say-first_4':{title:'The Starting Snowball',card:{insight:'Each raise builds on what you make now, so your start shapes years of pay.',tryThis:'Before you take your next job offer, ask once for a bit more pay.',skills:['money','career']},review:{prompt:'A friend says to skip asking now and ask at your first review. Is that wise?',right:'No, raises build on your starting pay, so ask now',wrong:'Yes, a strong first review can easily fix a low start',why:'Each raise is a part of your pay now, so a low start keeps you behind.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two snowballs roll down **the same hill**. One starts **a bit bigger**. Both pick up snow as they roll. At the bottom, how big do you think the gap between them will be?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Say you get \xa350 a month and your cousin gets \xa360. Each year you both get **a tenth more**. You go to \xa355. Your cousin goes to \xa366. The \xa310 gap is now \xa311, and **it keeps growing**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Each rise is built on **what you get now**. So the starting amount matters most, and you shouldn\u2019t rush it. A few **awkward minutes** can count for years.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two people join **the same team** on the same day. One starts on \xa340,000, the other on \xa342,000. Both get 3% a year. Does that \xa32,000 gap **stay the same**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'After the first raise, the gap is already **\xa32,060**. It widens with every raise that follows, because each one is **a percentage** of the current figure. So the starting number compounds for as long as you stay.'},{kind:'explain',text:'It can follow you, too. Your current pay can become **the reference point** for your next offer. And inside a job, raises capped as percentages are a **poor way** to correct a low start.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The entry talk is short and awkward, so people treat it as **the relaxed one**. It\u2019s the opposite. An hour\u2019s conversation sets **a trajectory**, not just a year.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture two offers for the same job. One has a higher **starting salary**. The other pays the same extra sum once, as **a bonus** in year one. Over several years, which is worth more?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A bonus is paid once. A higher entry figure is paid **every year**, and each percentage raise is applied to it. So the difference persists and grows. Much of your total pay can be **set at entry**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take two people hired on the same day, on \xa340,000 and \xa342,000. Both get 3% a year. After one raise, the gap is \xa32,060. It widens every year after, for as long as they both stay.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture someone who starts low and stays. Each year\u2019s raise is **a capped percentage** of a small figure, so he barely catches up inside the firm. That\u2019s why the largest single jumps often come from **changing employer**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The case needs a long stay and raises that really are percentages. **Fixed pay scales**, promotions and flat awards loosen the link. Where offers are fixed, pushing hard at entry gains little.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Every raise multiplies whatever **the start** was, so a gap at entry is multiplied too. That\u2019s why your first number deserves **the effort**, even when the conversation feels like the one to relax about.'}]},'the-number-you-say-first_5':{title:'Let the Silence Run',card:{insight:'A pause after your price is often just them doing math, so let it run.',tryThis:'Next time you name a price, say it once. Then wait for them to speak.',skills:['communication','money']},review:{prompt:'You quote a client $200 for a job. She looks down and pauses. What now?',right:'Stay quiet and wait for her to speak first',wrong:'Offer a small discount to end the awkward quiet',why:'She may just be doing math, and a price cut now gives money away.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re selling your old comics. A boy asks how much, and you say **\xa33**. He looks at the comics and says nothing. A few seconds pass. What do you **feel like** doing?'},{kind:'story',text:'You blurt out, *or \xa32, if you like.* He hadn\u2019t even answered. He might have been counting his coins. His silence felt like a no, so you lowered your own price for nothing.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When someone goes quiet, they\u2019re usually **just thinking**. So after you say a number, **stop and wait**. Let them speak first.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You name **your figure** for the role. The hiring manager looks down at her notes and says nothing. Five seconds. Ten. What\u2019s the urge you\u2019re **fighting** right now?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The urge is to **fill the pause**. And filling it almost always means softening the number, adding a qualification, or offering a cut nobody asked for. That\u2019s **the unforced concession**, and it\u2019s the most common failure.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Meanwhile, she\u2019s probably doing arithmetic, checking a band or composing a reply. **Her silence** carries no message. It only feels like one from **your side** of the table, because you\u2019re waiting for a verdict.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Letting the pause run isn\u2019t **a power play**, and it needs no confidence. It only needs you to see the silence as **neutral**. Say your number, then let them speak next.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve just named a number you prepared carefully, with **a solid basis** behind it. The other side goes quiet. Four seconds feels like a minute. Which of you is **about to move**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Whoever has just named a number is **listening for a verdict**, so a pause sounds like one. The other side is usually just working something out. **The message** exists on one side of the table only.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a freelancer who quotes \xa32,000. The client studies the proposal in silence. She adds, *I could do \xa31,800.* Nobody had pushed back. She\u2019s **given away** \xa3200 to end a silence that meant nothing.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a candidate who names her salary figure, then simply waits. It\u2019s uncomfortable for a few seconds. The manager does the sums and replies with a counter close to **her number**. The anchor survived **because she stayed quiet**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Silence isn\u2019t always neutral. Sometimes it\u2019s **a deliberate tactic**, and sometimes your number really is out of range. Letting it run doesn\u2019t mean refusing to talk once they respond. Norms about pauses differ, too.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The silence after an offer is usually neutral. **Interpreting it** is what costs money. So say your number, let the pause run, and wait for **them to speak**.'}]}}}},1011,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'the-people-you-stop-calling',kind:'book',promise:'Win back friends you never meant to lose',about:'Why do so many friendships end without a fight? Most just fade, and both people feel it. These ideas show why no one reaches out first. They also show how small the fix is. Who would you call first?',ideas:{'the-people-you-stop-calling_1':{title:'The Drift Nobody Chose',card:{insight:'Most friendships fade without a fight, so there is nothing to fix.',tryThis:'Think of one friend you lost touch with. If you cannot name a fight, send a hi.',skills:['relationships','psychology']},review:{prompt:'An old friend went quiet a year ago, with no fight. What happened?',right:'Your friendship just faded with no fight',wrong:'She decided she did not want you around',why:'Most friendships fade with no fight, so the quiet does not mean she is mad.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your best friend moves to a new school across town. At first you text each other every day. Then a reply takes four days, then eleven. Nobody had a fight. So **what happened** to **the friendship**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Most friendships don\u2019t end with a fight. They **fade slowly**, and nobody ever chooses it. When a friend goes quiet, you might think *they don\u2019t like me any more.* So you go quiet as well, and **the gap** grows.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Nothing broke, so there\u2019s **nothing to fix** between you. The silence doesn\u2019t mean your friend is cross. You can just **say hello** again whenever you like.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You realise you haven\u2019t spoken to **an old flatmate** in two years. You try to remember the row that ended it. You can\u2019t find one. So why does getting back in touch feel **so hard**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Ana and Ruth, flatmates for three years. Ruth moved for a job, then Ana had a baby. Replies slowed from days to weeks. Nobody was wronged and nobody chose it. The gaps just got longer until they were the friendship.'},{kind:'explain',text:'A row leaves you **something to do**: apologise, explain, make up. A drift leaves only a gap. Worse, each of them reads **the silence** as a verdict, and pulls back further. That\u2019s how an ordinary pause becomes permanent.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Most lost friendships weren\u2019t lost **over anything**. There\u2019s nothing to resolve, so **the barrier** to resuming is lower than it feels. You\u2019re not reopening a dispute, just ending a pause.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Ask people why a friendship ended and they\u2019ll **hunt for the moment**: the cancelled dinner, the missed birthday. Most can\u2019t find one. What if the missing reason is **the whole story**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Adult friendships rarely end in **a rupture**. They end in a lengthening interval that nobody decided on. Because there was no event, there\u2019s **nothing to apologise for** and nothing to resolve. You\u2019re left with a gap that has grown conspicuous.'},{kind:'story',text:'Ana and Ruth shared a flat for three years. Then came a job in another city, a baby, a reply that took four days, then eleven. Each began to read the quiet as a judgement. So each waited a little longer.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take two colleagues who sat side by side for years. One changes teams and the lunches stop. Months later, he wonders what he did wrong. The honest answer is nothing: the desk moved, not the friendship.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some friendships do end over something: a betrayal, a real hurt. Calling that drift skips what needs saying. And some people have **genuinely grown apart**, so a quiet ending may be the right one.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The usual ending has **no cause**, and the silence carries no message. Stop searching for the incident. If you can\u2019t find one, you\u2019re free to **simply get in touch**.'}]},'the-people-you-stop-calling_2':{title:'The Ball in Both Courts',card:{insight:'When friends go quiet, both think it is the other\u2019s turn.',tryThis:'Pick one person you think owes you a call. Call them first, even if it feels unfair.',skills:['relationships','psychology']},review:{prompt:'You are sure it is your cousin\u2019s turn to call. What do you do?',right:'Call her first, since your score may be wrong',wrong:'Wait, since it really is her turn this time',why:'You remember your own calls best, so you may forget some of hers.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You and a friend haven\u2019t messaged for months. You\u2019re sure you sent **the last one**. Somewhere across town, your friend is sure of exactly the same thing. **Who\u2019s right**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'You remember your own message well, because **you were there** when you sent it. Their last message feels fuzzier. They feel the same about yours. Nobody is lying, but **both memories** lean the same way.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When it feels like **their turn**, it might not be. So don\u2019t wait to find out who owes whom. Just **send the message** yourself.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You think of an old friend and feel **a small sting**. You suggested dinner last spring, and he never followed up. The ball is in his court. Or **is it**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Across town, Joe is thinking almost exactly the same about you. He remembers sending a birthday message you never answered. He\u2019s sure the next move is yours. Both of you are sincere, and both of you are waiting.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s why. You remember **your own efforts** more clearly than anyone else\u2019s. So both people honestly believe they reached out last. The result is **a stable deadlock**, held in place by a mild grievance on each side.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Your record of **who owes whom** isn\u2019t just unreliable. It\u2019s **tilted in your favour**.',left:{title:'The ledger',items:['I reached out last','It\u2019s their turn now','Silence reads as an answer']},right:{title:'The reality',items:['You both think that','Nobody actually knows','Silence is shared hesitation']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Your memory leans **one known way**, so don\u2019t consult it. Assume you\u2019ve misremembered and **send it anyway**. Whoever notices first is best placed to act.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two old friends, two cities, **one silence**. Ask either of them and you\u2019ll hear the same story: *I was the last to reach out.* They can\u2019t both be right. So what\u2019s **going on**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'In lapsed friendships, both people commonly believe **the other owes** the next contact. If memory erred at random, it would sometimes favour your friend. It doesn\u2019t. It leans towards **crediting yourself**, on both sides at once.'},{kind:'story',text:'Mia suggested a dinner last spring and her friend Joe never followed up. Joe sent a birthday message that Mia never answered. Each holds a small, sincere grievance. Neither wants to concede the point by writing first.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take two brothers who stopped calling after a busy year. Each tells his partner that he always makes the effort. Both say it honestly. The silence between them isn\u2019t an answer. It\u2019s the same hesitation, running twice.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Sometimes the ledger is right. If one person has done all the reaching out for years, that imbalance is **real information**. This idea ends a lapsed deadlock. It isn\u2019t a reason to carry a one-sided friendship forever.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A record biased in **a known direction** should be discounted, not consulted. There\u2019s no point to concede. The person who notices first is simply **better placed** to act.'}]},'the-people-you-stop-calling_3':{title:'The Two-Line Message',card:{insight:'The note that brings a friend back is short and asks for nothing.',tryThis:'Send an old friend a photo. Add one line about a day you shared.',skills:['relationships','communication']},review:{prompt:'You want to reach a friend after two quiet years. What do you send?',right:'A photo with one line about a fun day',wrong:'A long note that says sorry for the silence',why:'A small note asks for nothing, so it is easy to answer.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You find a photo of you and an old friend at **a birthday party**. You want to send it. But you feel you should write something long first. So you wait. Then **a month goes by**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The long message feels like the right one, but it often **never gets sent**. A photo with one line is easy. It asks for nothing, so your friend can send **a tiny reply**. Then you can chat a bit more.'},{kind:'moral',text:'To get back in touch, **small is enough**. You don\u2019t have to fix everything at once. Send **the little message** today, not the perfect one someday.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve owed an old friend a message for **two years**. In your head it has to explain the gap, apologise and suggest a proper catch-up. It\u2019s been half-written for months. Why is it **so hard** to send?'},{kind:'explain',text:'You\u2019ve imagined **the debt** as large, so the message has to be large too. But the size of the debt is **the thing that\u2019s wrong**. Usually nobody is waiting for an explanation. They\u2019d just like to hear from you.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The long message feels **more respectful**. The short one is the one that **actually arrives**.',left:{title:'The big letter',items:['Explains and apologises','Proposes a long meeting','Often never sent']},right:{title:'The small note',items:['A photo and one line','Asks for nothing','Easy to answer']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Make it short, specific and **unburdened**: something remembered is enough. It lets the exchange restart **at a low level**. You can rebuild from there, a reply at a time.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture **two parcels** you\u2019ve made for the same old friend. One is a careful letter, still sitting in the hall after a month. The other is a card with one line. Which one **gets through** the door?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The message that restores contact is much smaller than you\u2019d assume. It\u2019s short, specific and **unburdened**. There\u2019s no accounting for the silence and no request. **Something remembered** is enough to begin.'},{kind:'story',text:'Sara drafts a long note to a university friend. It explains the gap, apologises and proposes a weekend away. Each part adds weight for both of them. Writing it becomes a project, so it never gets sent.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Ben, who passes the caf\xe9 where he and an old colleague used to meet. He sends a photo of the window and one line. She replies within the hour. They\u2019ve swapped three messages by the evening.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. If the silence followed **a real hurt**, a breezy photo can look like pretending it never happened. Then something more direct is owed. And a small message restarts contact, but it doesn\u2019t rebuild depth on its own.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Small works because it **permits a small reply**. The exchange can resume low and rise from there. So send **the two-line version** today, rather than your good version never.'}]},'the-people-you-stop-calling_4':{title:'Being Near Did the Work',card:{insight:'A lot of friendship comes from seeing each other without planning it.',tryThis:'Pick one friend you used to see every day. Ask them to meet at a set time each month.',skills:['relationships','habits']},review:{prompt:'Your closest friend moves three hours away. What helps most?',right:'A call at the same time each month',wrong:'Trust that true friends stay close on their own',why:'Seeing each other made the plans for you, so now you need a set time.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Last year you sat next to your best friend **every single day**. This year you\u2019re in different classes. You still like each other just as much. So why does staying friends feel **harder now**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Before, you met **without trying**, because you were in the same room. Now you have to ask, pick a time and turn up. You\u2019ve never had to do that before, so it\u2019s **a new skill**, not a failure.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Being near each other did **the hard work**. When that stops, plan **a regular time** to meet. Saturday mornings in the park will do.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'For four years you had lunch with a colleague **most days**. Then she moved jobs. You both promised to keep in touch, and meant it. Six months on, you\u2019ve met twice. What **went missing**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Each of those two meetings took a dozen messages to arrange. Before, nobody arranged anything. You were both in the same building at one o\u2019clock. The friendship hadn\u2019t changed. The structure around it had simply gone.'},{kind:'explain',text:'In classic studies of housing, how close people lived **strongly predicted** who became friends. Repeated, unplanned contact does much of the work. People in a friendship consistently **underestimate** how much. Your lunches were that kind of contact.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A friendship that fades after a move isn\u2019t **a verdict** on it. Goodwill never produced the contact, so it won\u2019t replace it. Set up **a standing arrangement** instead, like a monthly call.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Think of your closest friends. Where did you meet them? Most people name **a school**, an office, a team or a street. Very few name a place they **chose to keep visiting**. Why does that matter?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Most durable adult friendships are built somewhere with **forced repeat contact**. The structure does the work, and the people in it rarely notice. When it goes, the friendship must be maintained **deliberately**, often for the first time.'},{kind:'story',text:'Studies of housing found something stark. How close together people lived strongly predicted who became friends. Picture neighbours on one landing, meeting daily without planning to. Unplanned, repeated contact did much of what we credit to affinity.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a team that moves from one office to fully remote work. Nobody falls out. But the chats by the kettle stop, and within a year several close pairs have quietly faded.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The housing studies are about who becomes friends, not how friendships **survive a move**. Applying them to upkeep is the essay\u2019s own extension. And a standing date can harden into an obligation you both dread.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Being bad at keeping friends is **a missing skill**, not a moral failing. Goodwill won\u2019t replace proximity. Give the friendship **a deliberate substitute**, like a standing call.'}]},'the-people-you-stop-calling_5':{title:'Say the Awkward Sentence',card:{insight:'Saying out loud that you drifted ends the guessing for you both.',tryThis:'Send one friend a short note. Say you drifted, it was nobody\u2019s fault, and you want to meet.',skills:['communication','relationships']},review:{prompt:'You run into a friend you drifted from at a wedding. What do you say?',right:'We drifted, it was nobody\u2019s fault, and I miss you',wrong:'Just hi, and act like nothing ever happened',why:'Saying it plainly ends the guessing you have both been doing alone.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You go to knock on the door of a friend you haven\u2019t seen **for ages**. Your hand stops in the air. What if they don\u2019t want to see you? Then **the door opens**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'When friends stop talking, both of them **wonder** what **the silence** means. Saying it out loud clears that up. Now it\u2019s just **a plain fact**: we got busy and drifted. A fact is far easier than a guess.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You can just say it: **we drifted**, and it was nobody\u2019s fault. It feels scary. But your friend has often been **feeling the same**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You see an old friend\u2019s name on your phone and feel **a small knot**. It\u2019s been two years. You could send a breezy hello and pretend nothing happened. Or you could **say the thing**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Leah writes three sentences to her friend Kate. They\u2019ve drifted, and she doesn\u2019t think either of them meant to. She\u2019d like to fix it. Is Kate free for a walk on Sunday week?'},{kind:'explain',text:'It works because it removes **the ambiguity**. The silence meant something to both, but neither knew what. Naming it collapses that into **an ordinary fact**. Keep it brief and **without blame**, with one concrete plan.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The awkward sentence is dreaded far more than it deserves. Say that you drifted, that it was **nobody\u2019s fault**, and that you\u2019d like **it back**. Often the other person feels relief.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two old friends both know the friendship has **gone quiet**. Each wonders what the other thinks it means. Neither says a word, so each keeps guessing alone. What would happen if one of them just **named it**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The most direct way to restart things is **the awkward sentence**. It\u2019s brief and non-accusatory. It says that it\u2019s been a long time, that you\u2019d like to fix it, and proposes **something specific**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Leah sends Kate three lines: they\u2019ve drifted, neither meant to, and how about a walk on Sunday week. There\u2019s no list of who stopped calling whom. Kate replies that she\u2019d been meaning to write for months.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Tom, who meets an old friend at a wedding. He says it plainly: we lost touch, nobody\u2019s fault, let\u2019s fix it. The silence they\u2019d both been reading stops being a question.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. If your friend has clearly chosen to end things, naming it again is **pressure, not repair**. If there was a real hurt, *nobody\u2019s fault* isn\u2019t true. Saying it skips what needs saying.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The fear that they don\u2019t want it back is real, but probably **rarer than it feels**. Either way, **the guessing ends**. And most often, what comes back is relief.'}]}}}},1012,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'the-prince',kind:'book',promise:'See how power works without turning cruel',about:'Why do people still quote a book about rulers from 500 years ago? Machiavelli wrote down how the rulers of his day really acted. Some of it can help you. Some of it would hurt you. Which part will you keep?',ideas:{'the-prince_1':{title:'He Wrote What He Saw',card:{insight:'Writing down how power works is not the same as saying it is good.',tryThis:'Next time someone quotes an old book, ask one thing. Does it say what happens, or what to do?',skills:['thinking','leadership']},review:{prompt:'A coworker says Machiavelli would back his sneaky plan. What do you check first?',right:'If the book tells what happens or tells you what to do',wrong:'If his plan will make the company more money',why:'Showing how people act is not telling you to act that way.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A scientist spends all summer watching wasps. She writes down exactly **how they sting**. Does that mean she thinks stinging is good? Or is she just trying to **understand wasps**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'About 500 years ago, Niccol\xf2 Machiavelli worked for the government of Florence, in Italy. He watched rulers **up close**. Then he wrote *The Prince*, about how they **really act**. People often think he\u2019s telling you to be cruel.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Saying **what happens** isn\u2019t the same as saying it\u2019s good. When someone tells you how the world works, ask if they\u2019re **describing or advising**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'In a meeting, a senior colleague defends a round of **quiet cuts**. Machiavelli would approve, he says. It lands as a clever point. Nobody in the room has read the book. Should you **believe him**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Machiavelli served the Florentine republic for fourteen years as an official and envoy. When the Medici family returned in 1512, he lost his post. He was briefly jailed and tortured. Then, on his farm, he wrote down what he\u2019d seen.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Older advice books painted **an ideal prince**. Machiavelli wrote about **real ones** instead.',left:{title:'What he observes',items:['New rulers often break promises','Here\u2019s why it tends to happen']},right:{title:'What readers hear',items:['Break your promises','Here\u2019s how to do it']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Splitting **what happens** from **what should happen** is the first step in understanding anything. Skip it and you go wrong. You either deny ugly facts or you obey them as rules.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Machiavelli mostly wrote **a forecast**, not a blessing. So when someone quotes him at you, ask the plain question. Was he **describing it** or recommending it?'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'In 1513, a sacked official sits on his farm outside Florence. Earlier that year he was jailed and tortured. Now he\u2019s writing **a short book** for rulers. Why would anyone call it **a weather report**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'In his fifteenth chapter, Machiavelli says he\u2019s leaving **imagined kingdoms** to other writers. The gap between how people live and how they ought to is huge. Study only the second, he argues, and you\u2019ll **ruin yourself**.'},{kind:'story',text:'So when he notes that a new prince will usually have to **break faith**, read it as a forecast. This tends to happen. Here\u2019s why. Here are the mechanics. The verdict is left to the reader.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a reporter who writes up how a gang recruits teenagers. She explains the pitch step by step. Nobody thinks she wants more recruits. Her account is what lets parents and teachers spot the pitch early.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. He often slides from *this happens* to a prince must, and many chapters do prescribe. Choosing what to describe, and for whom, is already a choice. A cold account can still **work as a manual**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Keep **what happens** and **what should happen** in separate piles. It protects you more than it protects him. Then you can learn from his eye without borrowing his verdicts.'}]},'the-prince_2':{title:'Pain Fast, Gifts Slow',card:{insight:'Hard news hurts less all at once, and good news feels better bit by bit.',tryThis:'Next time you have hard news to share, share it all at once.',skills:['psychology','leadership']},review:{prompt:'You have two bad changes and two good ones to share. How?',right:'Share the hard news at once and the good news bit by bit',wrong:'Share one change each week so it feels gentle',why:'Each new bit of bad news makes the hurt start over again.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your coach has **three new rules** for the team. She could tell you all three on Monday. Or she could add one a week for three weeks. Which way would **sting less**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Think of a plaster. One **quick pull** hurts once, then it\u2019s over. Peeling it slowly hurts for ages. Treats work **the other way**. One sweet a day feels nice all week. A whole bag is gone by tea time.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Machiavelli noticed this 500 years ago. **Bad news** hurts less all at once, and **good news** feels bigger bit by bit. Watch for anyone using it on you.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re a manager with **three unwelcome changes** and two good ones to announce. Your instinct is to spread them all out evenly, one a month. Is that **the kind choice**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Machiavelli saw an asymmetry. Harm that repeats keeps people **bracing**, because each new blow revives the last. Benefit given in one lump is absorbed and becomes **the new normal**. So timing changes how a decision feels.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The same cuts can land as **one shock** or as **a long dread**.',left:{title:'Cuts each quarter',items:['Everyone keeps bracing','Each round revives the last']},right:{title:'One restructure',items:['Painful, then over','People settle and move on']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Deliver the hard news **together**, and spread the good news out. But notice what you\u2019re doing. You\u2019re managing **how it feels**, not whether it\u2019s right.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A tyrant has just seized a city by having its **leading citizens** killed. Now he needs the rest to live with him. What does Machiavelli say about **the timing** of what comes next?'},{kind:'story',text:'The rule sits in a chapter on men who rose **through crime**. His case is Agathocles, who made himself ruler of Syracuse that way. Machiavelli separates cruelty done once, then turned to the subjects\u2019 benefit, from cruelty that keeps returning.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The mechanism is about time. Repeated harm **compounds**: each blow revives the earlier ones, so fear never settles. A big benefit in one go **decays**: it\u2019s absorbed and becomes the baseline. Small ones are felt again each time.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a firm that gives you a pay rise. Paid in one jump, it soon feels normal. The same amount in two steps gets noticed twice. Nothing about the money changed, only how you met it.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Read in its chapter, the injuries are killings and the benefits are **a usurper buying quiet**. The rule makes wrongdoing easier for victims to swallow. It measures how harm feels, never whether it\u2019s just.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Timing changes **the experience**, not the substance. Use it to soften news you\u2019re sure is fair. And when it\u2019s used on you, judge **the decision itself**.'}]},'the-prince_3':{title:'The Whole Sentence',card:{insight:'Fear may be safer than love, but you must never be hated.',tryThis:'Before you quote a famous line, read what comes right after it. The end may change it.',skills:['thinking','leadership']},review:{prompt:'Someone says Machiavelli taught that fear beats love. What did they leave out?',right:'His warning that you must never be hated',wrong:'Nothing, since fear works better than love for leaders',why:'He said fear is safer only if people do not hate you.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve probably heard it: it\u2019s better to be **feared than loved**. People say Machiavelli wrote that 500 years ago. But what if they\u2019ve only heard **half of it**?'},{kind:'story',text:'A team captain grabs the best kit for himself and bosses everyone about. Soon the whole team hates him. After that, nobody listens to him, however tough he acts. That\u2019s the part of the warning people forget.'},{kind:'moral',text:'He said it\u2019s best to be **loved and feared**, and never **hated**. So before you repeat a famous line, check the whole sentence.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A new boss tells you she\u2019d **rather be feared** than liked, and credits Machiavelli. She seems proud of it. Did he actually say that, or is she quoting **a broken sentence**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'His claim is that it\u2019s best to be both. Since the two rarely go together, **fear is safer**. People break the bond of love whenever breaking it pays. Fear rests on **dread of punishment**, which doesn\u2019t depend on their mood.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Then comes **the condition** that does most of the work: **avoid being hated**.',left:{title:'As quoted',items:['Fear beats love','Be as harsh as needed']},right:{title:'As written',items:['Best to be both','Fear is safer than love','Never become hated']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Hatred, he says, comes from **taking property** and meddling with people\u2019s families. Avoid those and a ruler can be feared without being hated. A ruler who is hated is **finished**, however feared.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The honest version is narrower than the quote. Be **reliable rather than liked**, and be careful what you take. Affection is optional, but **resentment** can sink you.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Is it better to be **loved or feared**? It\u2019s one of the most quoted questions in politics. Most people know only the answer. Fewer know **the story** Machiavelli tells just before it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Cesare Borgia was **thought cruel**, yet his harshness brought order to the Romagna. Florence, afraid of seeming cruel, held back and let Pistoia tear itself apart. Machiavelli uses the pair to frame his question.'},{kind:'explain',text:'His answer: best to be both, but fear is safer if you must choose. **Love depends** on your subjects, who drop it when it costs them. **Fear depends** on you. The overriding rule is never to be **hated**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a strict head teacher. Pupils fear her detentions, but she punishes only with clear cause and never singles anyone out. Her rival shames pupils in assembly. Both are feared. Only one is hated.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The reliability argument assumes subjects **who can\u2019t leave**. Where people can walk away, fear buys quiet exits, not loyalty. And restoring the missing half makes the line accurate. It doesn\u2019t make it humane.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The famous line is **half a sentence**. The whole of it says be reliable, take nothing that breeds hatred, and **never be hated**. Read the paragraph before you quote it.'}]},'the-prince_4':{title:'The Exit Door',card:{insight:'Harsh rule works on people who are stuck, and your team can leave.',tryThis:'Before you get tough with your team, ask if they could just walk away.',skills:['leadership','career']},review:{prompt:'A new manager wants to rule her team like a harsh prince. Is that smart?',right:'No, her team can walk away and tell others why',wrong:'Yes, tough bosses always get the most work done',why:'His harsh tricks worked on people who were stuck, and most teams can leave.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A new captain takes over **your football club**. He shouts, picks favourites and makes the younger kids carry all the kit. By half term, what do you think **the players do**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Five of them join **another club**. Machiavelli wrote about rulers whose people **couldn\u2019t leave**. In a club or a class, people can walk away and tell others why. So a prince\u2019s harsh tricks stop working.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Some of what he noticed is still true. People remember **who was kind** and who took from them. Noticing that helps you, but copying **his harsh tricks** doesn\u2019t.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A newly promoted manager keeps **The Prince** on her desk and quotes it in one-to-ones. Within a year, her two strongest people have gone. What did she **miss in the book**?'},{kind:'story',text:'The stories about why they left reached her next employer before she did. Machiavelli\u2019s prince faced something very different. He couldn\u2019t resign, his subjects couldn\u2019t leave, and losing usually meant **exile or death**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'**The game** has changed, so **the winning moves** have changed too.',left:{title:'His world',items:['Subjects can\u2019t leave','Defeat is final','Nobody hears what you did']},right:{title:'Your workplace',items:['People can resign','Disputes go higher','Your reputation travels']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Exit and reputation turn his one-off contests into **repeated games**. In those, harshness gets punished next round. What still transfers is **his eye**: how favours are remembered and debts resented, and how promises fade under pressure.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Keep his observations and leave **his prescriptions**. They were written for a game you\u2019re not playing. Before you borrow a tactic, ask whether people **can walk away**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Italy in 1513 was a patchwork of **small states**, foreign armies and violent successions. A prince who lost didn\u2019t find **another post**. What would good leadership look like there?'},{kind:'story',text:'He was usually exiled or killed. Under those conditions, Machiavelli\u2019s advice made sense: strike first, don\u2019t count on goodwill, be severe early. These were answers to a world with **no exit** and no appeal.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Your world adds **two things** his lacked. People can leave, and reputations travel. That turns his one-off contests into **repeated games**, where harshness gets punished in the next round, and the one after.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a team lead who rules by fear. Everyone complies in meetings. Then the best people quietly apply elsewhere, and their reasons travel with them. Fear bought her **compliance**, but not loyalty.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Exit isn\u2019t always real. Where people can\u2019t afford to leave, or **one employer** runs the town, the old logic returns. That\u2019s a reason to watch for closed systems, not to use their tactics.'},{kind:'moral',text:'His descriptions of people under pressure **still hold**. His prescriptions were built for **a closed world**. Ask whether your people can leave, and you\u2019ll know which half to use.'}]},'the-prince_5':{title:'Build Before the Flood',card:{insight:'Luck runs half of what happens, so get ready while things are calm.',tryThis:'Name one problem you can see coming. Spend five minutes today on its first small step.',skills:['productivity','thinking']},review:{prompt:'Your lease ends in eight months. Nothing is wrong yet. When do you get ready?',right:'Now, while things are calm and you still have time',wrong:'Closer to the time, when the problem is clear',why:'Get ready now, while things are still calm and you have time.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your big test is **three weeks away**. Right now nothing feels urgent, so it\u2019s easy to put off. Then it\u2019s the night before. Can you learn **three weeks of work** in one evening?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Machiavelli said luck is like **a river in flood**. Nobody can stop it once it rises. But a bank built in **calm weather** decides how much it wrecks. Those quiet weeks were your time to build.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Luck decides **about half** of what happens, he thought. The other half is yours, but only if you **start early**, while things still feel calm.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You can see a problem coming: a key contract ends in **eight months**. Nothing is wrong yet, so nothing is being built. Each week it gets **easier to put off**. Sound familiar?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Machiavelli pictured fortune as **a river in flood** that sweeps everything away. Once it rises, nobody can stop it. But in calm weather people can build **dykes and banks**, so the water runs in a channel.'},{kind:'explain',text:'His estimate is deliberately modest. Fortune rules **about half** of what we do, he says, and leaves the other half to us. That\u2019s enough for preparing to matter, but not enough to **guarantee anything**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You can\u2019t steer a flood while it\u2019s happening. You can shape what it does to you **months earlier**, in **calm weather**, exactly when preparing feels least necessary.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'In 1512 a Spanish army forced **the Florentine republic** to fall. Machiavelli had served it for fourteen years. Soon he\u2019d lost his post and been jailed. What did he then write about **fortune**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'He says fortune decides **roughly half** our actions and leaves the rest to us. Her power shows where **no preparation** was made. So the half that\u2019s ours has to be used early, before anything goes wrong.'},{kind:'story',text:'His picture is a river in flood. In calm weather, people can build dykes so the next flood runs in a channel. He turns it on Italy, a country with no such banks, unlike Germany, Spain and France.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a small caf\xe9 whose landlord hints at selling. Nothing has changed yet. The owner spends one quiet month listing other premises and saving a buffer. When the sale comes, it\u2019s a move, not a closure.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The same chapter ends on an uglier image of fortune, one that **rewards boldness** over patience. And preparing has costs too. You can build for floods that never come and **starve the present**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You can\u2019t stop the river. You can decide, **in calm weather**, how much it takes from you. Build **one bank** now, for the flood you can already see.'}]},'the-prince_6':{title:'Your Own Army',card:{insight:'What your work runs on should be yours, so no one can change the rules.',tryThis:'List three things your work cannot run without. Mark any you do not own.',skills:['career','thinking']},review:{prompt:'All your sales come from one website, and it works great. Is that safe?',right:'No, the site can change the rules any time',wrong:'Yes, a site that works this well is safe to use',why:'The site works for its owners, so they can change the deal at any time.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Every game at the park uses **Sam\u2019s ball**. One day, Sam\u2019s side starts losing. Sam picks up the ball and goes home. The game is over. Who was **really in charge**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'In Machiavelli\u2019s Italy, rulers often **hired soldiers**. But pay isn\u2019t a good reason to risk your life, so they often ran. Others **borrowed a friend\u2019s army**, which obeyed its real owner. Both left the ruler stuck.'},{kind:'moral',text:'If you depend on something that belongs to **someone else**, they\u2019re in control. Machiavelli said a ruler needs **his own army**. You need your own ball.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A small firm sells software built entirely on **one supplier\u2019s platform**. The platform is excellent. Then the supplier **doubles its prices**. How many ways out does the firm have?'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Machiavelli distrusted **two kinds** of borrowed strength, for **different reasons**.',left:{title:'Mercenaries (hired)',items:['Paid, so no reason to die','Run when it gets hard']},right:{title:'Auxiliaries (borrowed)',items:['Loyal to someone else','Lose, and you\u2019re undone','Win, and you\u2019re their prisoner']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s the trap. A rented capability is **good at its job** and loyal to its owner\u2019s interests. It performs, and every success makes it harder to replace. The danger isn\u2019t that it fails you. It\u2019s that **it works**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Rented strength serves **its owner**, not you. Rent what you like, but for anything you depend on absolutely, ask what happens **if the terms change**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Pope Julius II was let down by his hired troops at Ferrara. So he **borrowed an army** from the king of Spain instead. It sounded safer. Was it **really safer**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Those troops were beaten at Ravenna. He avoided being at the mercy of enemies and allies alike only because Swiss troops arrived. They drove the French out. Machiavelli calls that luck, not policy.'},{kind:'explain',text:'His rule: mercenaries fail you because **pay isn\u2019t a reason** to die. Auxiliaries fail you even when they win, because then you\u2019re **their prisoner**. A state resting on either is never secure.'},{kind:'story',text:'He practised what he preached. As a Florentine official he built **a citizen militia**. It helped bring Pisa back under Florence in 1509. In 1512 it was routed at Prato, and the republic fell.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Your own arms aren\u2019t automatically **good arms**, as Prato showed. And in business, renting most things is sensible. The real test is narrower: which dependencies **could end you**?'},{kind:'moral',text:'Rent freely, but own what you **can\u2019t lose**. Anything your work depends on absolutely belongs **in your hands**. Ask of each supplier what happens if it changes its terms.'}]},'the-prince_7':{title:'When Nice Is Not Kind',card:{insight:'If you stay soft no matter what, you help a bully hurt the rest.',tryThis:'Today, talk to the person on your team who keeps being mean.',skills:['philosophy','leadership']},review:{prompt:'A coworker bullies your team, and your boss never steps in. What happens?',right:'Her soft touch helps the bully and hurts the rest',wrong:'Her calm slowly wins the bully over',why:'If no one stops a bully, the good ones leave.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A playground monitor never tells anyone off. She\u2019s **always gentle**, even when one kid keeps pushing the little ones over. She hopes it\u2019ll stop. Does **her kindness** help the little ones?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Machiavelli watched rulers in Italy 500 years ago. He saw that **a soft ruler** who lets trouble grow can do **more harm** than a firm one. Sometimes, he thought, you have to be hard to protect others.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Being gentle is good. But being gentle **no matter what** can let bullies win. Know which of your rules **never bend**, and which ones can.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A manager prides herself on **never confronting** anyone. One person on her team bullies the others, and she hopes it\u2019ll pass. A year later, who has **paid for her kindness**?'},{kind:'story',text:'The good people have left. The bully is still there. Her kindness has cost everyone except the bully. In Machiavelli\u2019s terms, fixed gentleness is a strategy, with **a known failure**: people who don\u2019t share it exploit it.'},{kind:'explain',text:'He says a ruler must be **capable of harshness**, and use it or not as necessity requires. He doesn\u2019t say be bad. He says the ability must **exist and be ready**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'So sort your principles into **two kinds** before anyone **tests them**.',left:{title:'Absolute',items:['Never broken, whatever happens','Few, and chosen on purpose']},right:{title:'Default',items:['Usually kept','Can bend when necessity is real']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Gentleness is a fine default, but a fixed one gets **exploited**. Decide now which principles are **absolute** and which can bend. Being unclear is what gets used against you.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Machiavelli has just promised to write about how rulers **really live**. Then he draws his conclusion, and it\u2019s the line that made the book **notorious**. What could a ruler possibly need to unlearn?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Someone who insists on being good among so many who aren\u2019t will **come to ruin**, he says. So a prince must be able **not to be good**, and use that only as necessity requires. Philosophers now call this **dirty hands**.'},{kind:'story',text:'His examples are about office. A generous ruler empties the treasury, then has to squeeze his people. A merciful one lets disorder grow, which ends up crueller than severity that prevents it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture the head of a charity who won\u2019t lay anyone off. Funding drops, and she keeps everyone on. Within a year the charity closes. Everyone loses their job, and the people it helped lose their support.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. This is his most dangerous sentence, the one most easily borrowed to excuse what someone wanted anyway. **Necessity** is judged by the person who gains from claiming it. He offers **no outside check**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Take the conflict seriously: private virtue and **the duties of office** can clash. But treat every claim of necessity, **including your own**, as suspect until you\u2019ve shown it.'}]},'the-prince_8':{title:'Go and Live There',card:{insight:'From far off, you hear of trouble only once it is big.',tryThis:'Pick one thing that is new to you. Spend five minutes looking at it up close today.',skills:['leadership','focus']},review:{prompt:'You take over a team in another city. How do you keep track?',right:'Spend the first month close to the team, not reading reports',wrong:'Ask for weekly reports so nothing slips past you',why:'Reports show trouble once it is big, but up close you catch it small.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re in charge of **the class plants**. If you look at them every morning, you spot one yellow leaf. If you check once a month, what do you **find instead**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Probably **a dead plant**. Machiavelli said a ruler who takes over a new land should **go and live there**. Up close, you see small problems while they\u2019re easy to fix. From far away, you hear about them too late.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Small problems are **cheap to fix**, but only if you\u2019re near enough to see them. So stay **close** to what you look after.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You take over a team **in another city**. The weekly reports look fine. Then one says a key client has gone. Everyone in that office saw it coming **for months**. Why didn\u2019t you?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Machiavelli\u2019s first remedy for holding new territory was simple: the ruler should **go and live there**. Close by, you see disorders as they start. Far away, you hear of them when they\u2019re **beyond repair**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'He ranked **his remedies**, and the soldiers came **last**, not first.',left:{title:'Living there',items:['See trouble early','Harder for officials to cheat','People can reach you']},right:{title:'A garrison',items:['Costly to keep','Resented by locals','His last resort']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Distance doesn\u2019t just delay news. It **filters** it, removing the small early signals that were cheap to act on. A report arrives once something is reportable. By then the **useful window** has usually closed.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Presence isn\u2019t a gesture. It\u2019s **an information channel**, faster than any report. For anything newly yours, spend **the first month** close to it, not reading about it.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'The Ottoman sultan conquered the Greek lands, then **went to live** in them. Without that, Machiavelli says, he **couldn\u2019t have kept them**. Why would where a ruler sleeps matter so much?'},{kind:'story',text:'The case comes from his chapter on **mixed principalities**. Lands of a different language and custom were the hardest kind to hold. His best remedy for them wasn\u2019t force at all. It was the ruler\u2019s presence.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Living there, you see disorders **as they spring up**. Away, you hear of them when they\u2019re great. Distance doesn\u2019t just delay. It **filters**, passing on only the problems that have already grown expensive.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a coach who watches every practice. He spots the player who\u2019s limping. Another coach only reads the match scores. She learns about the injury when that player can\u2019t play at all.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. His second remedy, settlers, is cheap because it takes homes from a few locals too poor to resist. That\u2019s **dispossession** dressed as economy. And an owner who hovers can **crowd out** the people doing the work.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Be near **what\u2019s newly yours** while its problems are small. Presence picks up what no report contains. Just don\u2019t stand **so close** that nobody else can work.'}]},'the-prince_9':{title:'Who Tells You the Truth',card:{insight:'If nobody brings you bad news, look at how you acted last time.',tryThis:'Write down how you reacted the last time someone told you bad news.',skills:['leadership','communication']},review:{prompt:'You lead a team, and nobody has raised a problem in months. Why?',right:'The way you reacted taught them bad news costs them',wrong:'Things are going well, so there is nothing bad to report',why:'People stop bringing bad news to a boss who gets mad.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Maya is in charge of **a group project**. She gets cross whenever anyone points out a mistake. Soon nobody does. The project looks fine to her. What happens when **the teacher marks it**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'People in charge often hear **only good news**, because others are scared to bring bad news. To **flatter** means saying nice things that aren\u2019t true, just to please. Machiavelli saw that flattery left rulers blind.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Pick **a few people** you trust to tell you the truth. Then, when they do, **say thanks**, even if it stings. That\u2019s how you keep hearing it.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'The more senior you get, the more **carefully edited** the news reaching you becomes. Nobody lies, exactly. They just leave things out. How would you even know **what\u2019s missing**?'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Machiavelli saw **a real bind**. Every ruler has to decide how freely **people may speak**.',left:{title:'Everyone speaks freely',items:['Respect for you drains away']},right:{title:'Nobody speaks freely',items:['The truth stops reaching you']}},{kind:'explain',text:'His answer is **a narrow channel**. Choose a few wise advisers. Let only them speak candidly, and only on what you ask. Listen patiently, decide alone, and make sure **speaking freely** costs them nothing.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Then the detail most people miss. He holds the ruler responsible for **the advice** he gets. If you\u2019re not being told things, that\u2019s a fact **about you**: how you reacted last time, and who saw it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Bad news reaching you is **an output** of your own behaviour. So pick your few truth-tellers, then **take it well** when they deliver, especially where others can see.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Most people assume a ruler is wise because he has **good advisers**. Machiavelli turns that **upside down**. What if the advice you get is only ever as good as you are?'},{kind:'explain',text:'An unwise prince **can\u2019t tell** good advice from bad. With several advisers, he can\u2019t reconcile them. With just one, that one soon **takes his state**. Good counsel grows from the prince\u2019s own judgement.'},{kind:'story',text:'In his design, the prince picks a few wise advisers. Only they may speak truthfully, on what he asks. He listens patiently, decides alone, then holds firm once he\u2019s decided.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a head of department who praises a junior for flagging a flaw, in front of everyone. Next month, two more people raise problems early. Nothing in the process changed. **Her reaction** did.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The ruler still decides what may be discussed. Advisers speak only when asked, so **the question nobody asked** has no way in. And a small fixed circle can harden into **a court** of its own.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The quality of what you hear depends on **you**. Build a narrow channel for the truth. Then keep it open **with your reactions**, and ask the question nobody else will.'}]}}}},1013,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'the-quiet-hours',kind:'book',promise:'Keep your first hours for your own work',about:'Why does your quiet morning hour slip away so fast? This app\u2019s team wrote this short essay about it. It shows how to keep that hour, and who waits while you do. Which hour will you keep?',ideas:{'the-quiet-hours_1':{title:'Claim the First Hour',card:{insight:'Your first quiet hour is the one everything wants, so claim it first.',tryThis:'Tonight, write one line that says what your first hour tomorrow is for.',skills:['focus','productivity']},review:{prompt:'Your early hour keeps getting lost to email. What helps you keep it?',right:'Decide the night before what the hour is for',wrong:'Try harder to ignore your email when it comes in',why:'Lots of things want that hour, so give it a job before they arrive.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'It\u2019s Saturday, and you wake up early. The house is **completely quiet**. You plan to work on your drawing. Then you check a game, and your brother wakes up. Where did **the quiet time** go?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Early morning feels like **spare time**, but it isn\u2019t. It\u2019s the one quiet stretch, so **everything wants it**: games, family, noise. If you don\u2019t decide what it\u2019s for, the first thing to arrive simply takes it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Quiet time isn\u2019t left over. It\u2019s **wanted time**, and you have to **claim it first**. Decide the night before what your quiet hour is for.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You set your alarm for six to write before work. By half past, you\u2019ve answered **three messages** and read the news. The hour is gone again. Was it ever **really free**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Lena, who wants to finish a course in the mornings. She tells herself she\u2019ll use the early hour when she gets up. Most days a message arrives first. Whatever reaches the hour first gets it.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The essay says the morning isn\u2019t **free time**. It\u2019s **contested time**.',left:{title:'Free time',items:['What\u2019s left after obligations','Used when you get round to it']},right:{title:'Contested time',items:['Claimed before the claims arrive','Booked the night before']}},{kind:'explain',text:'The difference is rarely **willpower**. It\u2019s whether the hour was **allocated before it began**. So schedule it rather than just intend it. And start before the first message, not after you\u2019ve glanced at one.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your first quiet hour isn\u2019t what\u2019s left over. Everything wants it, so you **claim it** the night before. Then the first message finds **the hour taken**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Most advice treats the early morning as **spare time**, the bit before the day properly starts. So why does it vanish so reliably? The essay\u2019s answer starts by asking **what kind of time** it is.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Free time is what remains after your obligations. **Contested time** is different: it has to be claimed before the claims arrive. The first two hours are the only **reliably uninterrupted** block. That\u2019s exactly why everything else wants them.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture two people who both rise at six. One has written down the night before what the hour is for. The other plans to decide when she sits down. By seven, only one of them has started.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now think of a team lead who blocks eight till ten every day. He starts before opening his inbox. His colleagues soon learn the hours are taken. Keeping them is a refusal, repeated daily, that people notice.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The first two hours are quiet in a typical office life, not in every life. Parents of small children and **shift workers** may find their quiet block late at night, or nowhere at all.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The morning isn\u2019t free time waiting for you. It\u2019s **contested**, so you claim it **before the claims arrive**. Wherever your own quiet block falls, the rule is the same.'}]},'the-quiet-hours_2':{title:'The Boring Rule',card:{insight:'A dull rule keeps a habit going, since you have no choice to make.',tryThis:'Tonight, pick one set time and one set place for tomorrow\u2019s work.',skills:['habits','focus']},review:{prompt:'Your morning drawing time keeps slipping. Which change fits the idea?',right:'Same time and place each day, with your phone in another room',wrong:'Pick a new time each morning, once you see how the day looks',why:'A set time and place leave you nothing to decide.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Some days you practise **piano** at four. Some days at six, or after TV. Each day you ask yourself whether to start now. How often is the answer **later**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Mia has a different plan. When she gets home from school, her phone goes in a box in the hall. Then she plays piano for twenty minutes. It\u2019s the same every day, so there\u2019s nothing to decide.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A habit lasts when it\u2019s **boring**. Same time, same place, same first step. When there\u2019s **nothing to decide**, there\u2019s nothing for you to argue about.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You plan to start each morning with an hour of writing. Monday you check email first, just quickly. Tuesday there\u2019s **an early call**. By Thursday, every morning opens with **a debate**. Sound familiar?'},{kind:'explain',text:'What keeps a morning isn\u2019t willpower. It\u2019s **a dull rule**: no meetings before ten, phone in another room, the work chosen the night before. Each variation brings back a decision. And **every decision** is a place for the morning to slip.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'A **resolve** gets decided again each morning. An **arrangement** gets decided once.',left:{title:'A resolve',items:['I\u2019ll write first thing','Reopened every morning','Loses to the easy option']},right:{title:'An arrangement',items:['Writing starts at half seven','Settled once, in advance','Nothing to decide at eight']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s the honest trade. A boring routine gives you **steady output** and **no stories**. That suits anything you want to still be doing in a year. It doesn\u2019t suit anything you mainly want to enjoy describing.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A rule you have to think about is a rule you\u2019ll **negotiate with**. So make yours **boring**. The dullness isn\u2019t a flaw, it\u2019s the part doing the work.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'You could run your mornings with judgement: read the day, respond to what it needs, keep things **fresh**. It sounds wiser than a fixed routine. So why does the **interesting version** so often fall apart?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The essay\u2019s answer is that **variation reintroduces decisions**. Each one is a small renegotiation of the rule. The easy option gets another chance to win. A morning practice survives on **sameness**: same time, same place, same first action.'},{kind:'story',text:'Think of a writer who decides each day when to start, depending on her mood. Some mornings she starts at seven. Others, the post arrives first, then a call. Her best weeks and worst weeks look nothing alike.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a designer with three dull rules. No meetings before ten. Her phone stays in another room. And she picks tomorrow\u2019s task before stopping tonight. She never wonders whether today counts, so the work usually gets done.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some lives can\u2019t be made this regular: a sick child, an on-call job, a week away. A rigid rule can also **outlast its purpose**. And the essay asserts this mechanism rather than proving it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A boring routine buys you steady work and costs you **the stories**. For anything you\u2019ll still want doing in a year, that\u2019s **the right trade**.'}]},'the-quiet-hours_3':{title:'Who Waits While You Work',card:{insight:'When you block time to focus, someone else has to wait for you.',tryThis:'Today, tell one person at work when your quiet time will end.',skills:['productivity','communication']},review:{prompt:'You keep eight to ten for focus work. What do you owe your coworkers?',right:'Tell them ahead of time when you will be free',wrong:'Nothing, since focus time costs no one but you',why:'Someone waits while you focus, so tell them when you will be free.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You shut your door to practise **guitar** for an hour. Your little brother wants help with his model. Your mum wants a hand with dinner. Who\u2019s really paying for **your quiet hour**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'They are. While you play, they **have to wait**. That can be fine. But be honest about it, and tell them **when you\u2019ll be free**. Waiting is much easier when you know how long it lasts.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Time just for you **costs someone** a wait. You don\u2019t have to stop taking it. Just **say when it ends**, then keep to that time.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your eight-to-ten block is **protected**. At quarter past nine, a colleague needs one answer before sending a quote to a client. They wait until ten. So does the client. Who paid for **your focus**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Most advice about deep work talks about **the time you gain**. The essay adds the part it leaves out. That time comes from somewhere, usually **other people\u2019s convenience**. Someone waits whether you apologise or not.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Either way **the cost is real**. Saying so changes **how it lands**.',left:{title:'Going quiet',items:['No warning given','Every wait is a surprise','Feels like a refusal']},right:{title:'Naming the cost',items:['Times told in advance','People plan around it','Reads as an arrangement']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Your quiet hours aren\u2019t free. **Someone waits** for them. So admit the cost, tell people **when you\u2019re free**, and keep those times.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Advice on focus usually treats your protected morning as **a private choice**. It seems to cost nobody but you. But picture the person at your shut door with a question. What is **your quiet hour** costing them?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The essay calls this the part most advice leaves out. **Being unavailable** means someone waits. You might stop apologising, or find you can\u2019t. Neither changes the fact. It\u2019s **a trade**, and colleagues carry the other side.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a designer who needs one answer from you at nine. She can\u2019t send her drawings until she has it. She waits until ten. Her client waits too. Your quiet hour was partly paid for in her morning.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a manager who announces she\u2019s offline from eight to ten. Every morning her team sits blocked, waiting for her approvals. The window is honest and predictable. The cost is still large, and it lands on them.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The essay gives no way to **weigh the cost**. A senior person\u2019s quiet hour can cost a junior one far more than the reverse. Saying the cost aloud makes it honest. It doesn\u2019t make it fair.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Keeping a morning is **a trade**, not a private choice. Someone else pays part of it. So name the cost, make the window **predictable**, and weigh it when the bill is large.'}]},'the-quiet-hours_4':{title:'Keep the Rule Small',card:{insight:'A small rule you keep each day beats a big one you often break.',tryThis:'Take a rule you keep breaking. Make it small enough to keep every day.',skills:['habits','productivity']},review:{prompt:'Your rule of no meetings all morning broke three times this week. What now?',right:'Shrink it to the first hour or two, and keep that every day',wrong:'Keep the whole morning and stick to it harder',why:'When a rule breaks, people learn it can bend, so keep yours small.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You make a big rule: **no screens** all Saturday. At eleven you play one quick game. Then you think, *well, that\u2019s ruined.* What happens to **the rest** of the day?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Think of a **thin fence** round your whole garden. It gets knocked down in a week. A short, **strong wall** by the gate stays up. Small rules work the same way: you can keep them every day.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t try to guard your whole day. Guard **a small part**, like the first half hour, **every single day**. A small rule you always keep is a real rule.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two rules. One protects **nine till twelve** every day. The other protects nine till half past ten, **no exceptions**. Which do you think will still be working in a month?'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Omar, who blocks the whole morning for his thesis. On Tuesday a meeting lands at ten. The plan feels broken, so he answers email until lunch. By Friday the rule has quietly gone.'},{kind:'explain',text:'A big rule doesn\u2019t bend. It **snaps**, and the rest gets written off. Worse, each exception teaches everyone, you included, that the rule is **negotiable**. A narrow rule can be kept perfectly, so it\u2019s never up for discussion.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Why **the start**? The first block comes before most demands can reach you. It\u2019s also often your **freshest attention**, so a narrow claim catches much of the value. The essay suggests two hours, or less if two won\u2019t hold.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t defend the whole day. Defend **the first block**, every day, **without exception**. Shrink the claim until you can keep it, then stop shrinking.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'How big should a rule about your time be? Defending **the whole day** sounds most ambitious. Defending one short block sounds timid. The essay bets on **the timid one**. Why would smaller hold better?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The argument is about **scope**. A rule with a large scope tends to break early and then get **abandoned entirely**. An interruption at ten spoils a plan for nine to twelve. A narrow claim survives contact with the day.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a student who promises himself no phone all morning. At half nine he checks one message. *That\u2019s the morning gone*, he decides, and gives up for the day. The rule didn\u2019t bend. It snapped.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a translator who keeps just the first hour of each day, with no exceptions at all. Her colleagues learn not to ask. Nobody argues with a rule that has never once been broken.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Not everyone is freshest in the morning, so your best block may come later. Two hours is a rough suggestion, not a measured figure. And you can **shrink too far**: a rule too short for real work protects nothing.'},{kind:'moral',text:'It\u2019s **the exceptions**, not the size, that teach people a rule can be argued with. So make yours small enough to keep perfectly, and **stop shrinking** once it holds the work.'}]},'the-quiet-hours_5':{title:'The Night Before',card:{insight:'Your morning is set the night before, by when you stop and what you pick.',tryThis:'Tonight, before you go to bed, write down the one task you will start with.',skills:['productivity','habits']},review:{prompt:'Your early mornings get little done. Which change fits the idea?',right:'Stop at a set time, and write down tomorrow\u2019s one task first',wrong:'Set the alarm half an hour earlier to gain time',why:'Your night sets up your morning, so stop on time and pick one task.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Saturday morning, homework time. You sit down and spend **fifteen minutes** choosing which bit to do first. By the time you start, you\u2019re already **a bit bored**. What could you have done the night before?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Choosing takes time and energy. If you choose in the morning, you spend your **freshest time** just picking. So pick **one job** the night before and leave it on your desk. Then you can start straight away.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A good morning starts **the night before**. Pick one thing, go to bed on time, and you\u2019ll **start fast**. Getting up early but going to bed late just makes you tired.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You sit down at eight with good intentions. You glance at the inbox, rewrite **your list**, then reorder it. At twenty past, you finally start. Where did **the best minutes** of your day go?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Two decisions shape a morning, and both happen **the evening before**. When you stop sets when you sleep, and so whether the early hour is usable. Choosing tomorrow\u2019s task tonight removes **deciding**, the morning\u2019s costliest job, from your freshest time.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Grace, who writes reports. Before she shuts her laptop each evening, she writes one line: the section she\u2019ll start on tomorrow. In the morning there\u2019s nothing to choose. She opens the file and begins.'},{kind:'moral',text:'This is really **an evening practice** with a morning payoff. Getting up earlier without moving the evening just makes you **more tired**. So stop on time, and choose tonight.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A protected morning falls apart. You\u2019d probably blame **eight o\u2019clock**: the inbox, the slow start, the wandering mind. The essay looks somewhere else. When does the failure **really happen**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Its answer is **the evening before**. That\u2019s when you decided how late to stop, which sets your sleep. It\u2019s also when you did or didn\u2019t choose the next day\u2019s work. The morning only **shows the result**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture someone who sets the alarm an hour earlier but goes to bed at the usual time. For a week she\u2019s up at six, foggy and slow. She has more morning and less to spend in it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a builder who stops at six each evening. Before he goes, he leaves one job out on the bench. Next morning he walks in and picks up the tool. There\u2019s nothing left to choose.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Evenings aren\u2019t always yours: children, shifts and other people shape them. Something urgent can overtake the task you chose, so treat it as **a default**, not a vow. And the essay gives no figures on sleep.'},{kind:'moral',text:'An earlier alarm without an earlier evening is a resolution **to be tired**, not a plan. Move the evening instead. That\u2019s where **the morning is decided**.'}]}}}},1014,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'the-salary-conversation',kind:'book',promise:'Get paid more without acting tough',about:'Do you think a raise takes a lot of nerve? Mostly, it takes facts and good timing. It also helps to know what your boss needs. The Mindroll team, which makes this app, wrote this article. What will you bring to your next pay talk?',ideas:{'the-salary-conversation_1':{title:'The Ally Across the Desk',card:{insight:'Your boss often does not set the pay, and may be on your side.',tryThis:'Before your next pay talk, ask your boss who sets the pay and what proof would help.',skills:['career','communication']},review:{prompt:'Your boss says the raise is not up to her. What do you do?',right:'Ask what proof would help her ask for you',wrong:'Push her harder until she gives in and says yes',why:'Your boss is often on your side, so give her what she needs.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your class wants a **trip to the zoo**. You ask your teacher, and she says she\u2019ll see. You feel cross, as if she\u2019s saying no. But is she really **the one who decides**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Your teacher doesn\u2019t hold the money. The **head of the school** does. Your teacher likes your class and would enjoy the trip too. So she isn\u2019t your enemy. She\u2019s **your helper**, if you give her good reasons.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The person you ask often has to **ask someone else**. Treat them as **a helper**, not an enemy. Then give them what they need to ask well.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You walk into your pay review **ready for a fight**. Your manager is across the desk, and you\u2019ve rehearsed every argument against them. But who are you **really arguing with**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Maya, a designer asking for a raise. Her manager, Tom, works within a **pay band** set by finance. He didn\u2019t choose the limit and doesn\u2019t keep the difference. He wants to keep Maya. He just needs a case to take upstairs.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Maya can treat Tom as **the obstacle** or as **her advocate**. The second gets her further.',left:{title:'The obstacle',items:['Argue against him','Give him nothing','Waste your ally']},right:{title:'The advocate',items:['Ask what he needs','Hand him evidence and comparables','Give him a specific figure']}},{kind:'explain',text:'It changes **timing** too. If pay is fixed in a planning cycle, the talk that matters comes **before that cycle**. Then your manager can still carry your case into the room where it\u2019s decided.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The person opposite is usually **not the counterparty**. Treat them as **your ally**. Ask what they need to make your case, before you offer a number.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Most pay advice is about **confidence**: sit up straight, ask big, hold firm. Yet a raise is often won or lost by someone who **isn\u2019t in the room**. So who are you actually talking to?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The manager in the meeting is usually **an agent**, not a principal. They work within a band set elsewhere. Your job is to **equip them** with evidence, comparables and a specific figure they can take upward.'},{kind:'story',text:'Maya walks in braced to argue. Instead she asks Tom what would make her case easy to win upstairs. He names two things: recent results and market data. She sends both that week. Tom takes them to finance.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Sam, who treats his manager as the enemy. He argues that she\u2019s being unfair. She\u2019d happily back him, but he\u2019s given her nothing to show anyone. The meeting ends politely and changes nothing.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Sometimes the person opposite **is the decider**: a founder, or a recruiter paid to hold costs down. And a manager can want to keep you while being judged on staying in budget.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Treat the person opposite as **an ally** by default, not a rule. Find out **who really decides**. Then hand your manager the case they\u2019ll need upstairs.'}]},'the-salary-conversation_2':{title:'Show Your Work',card:{insight:'Facts your boss can check are harder to turn down than a number.',tryThis:'Write down the raise you want. Next to it, write one fact your boss can check.',skills:['career','money']},review:{prompt:'You want a $4,000 raise. What should you bring to the talk?',right:'Facts your boss can check, like what your job pays',wrong:'The biggest number you can say with a straight face',why:'Your boss can say no to a number, but facts need an answer.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'In maths, your teacher asks for the answer **and your working**. Two pupils both write *42*. One shows every step, and the other shows nothing. Which answer is **harder to mark wrong**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Asking works the same way. Wanting more pocket money is just **a wish**. Saying you now do the washing up every night is **a reason**. A reason is much harder to wave away, so it\u2019s worth finding one.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t just ask for a number. Tie it to something **people can check**. The reason does **the persuading**, and the number is where it lands.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You ask for **\xa34,000 more**. Your manager nods, says it\u2019s noted, and the meeting moves on. Nothing happens. A colleague asks for a similar raise and gets it. What did **she bring** that you didn\u2019t?'},{kind:'story',text:'Your colleague, Priya, brought three things. A **market range** for her role and city. A record of what she\u2019d delivered that year. And a comparable role elsewhere she could name. Her figure sat on top of all three.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s why it works. Declining **a bare number** is easy. Declining a documented case needs **a counter-argument**. If nobody has one, the position tends to move. And your manager now has something to take upstairs.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A number without **a checkable basis** is a wish. The basis does **the persuading**. It won\u2019t create money that isn\u2019t there, but without one you\u2019ll usually hear no.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two people ask for the same raise. One names **the biggest figure** she can defend. The other names a smaller one, with **a market range** attached. Which request do you think is harder to turn down?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Evidence turns a request from **a preference** into a position. It works by shifting **the burden**. Without a basis, the person opposite needs no reason to say no. With one, they need an answer to your evidence.'},{kind:'story',text:'Priya asks for a raise with a market range for her role and city. Her manager can\u2019t simply decline. Finance has to explain why she\u2019s paid below the range. Nobody can, so the figure moves.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Dan. He asks for the top of the band because he feels he deserves it. There\u2019s money in the budget that year. But with no reason given, saying no costs his manager nothing.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. A basis can\u2019t create money in **a fixed budget**. Market data can be cherry-picked, and a sceptical reader will check it. A colleague\u2019s pay may be confidential and awkward to cite.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A basis is **necessary, not sufficient**. It won\u2019t beat a truly fixed budget. But without one, you\u2019ll usually fail **even when** the money was there.'}]},'the-salary-conversation_3':{title:'Ask Before Pay Is Set',card:{insight:'Pay is often set weeks before you hear, so ask before then.',tryThis:'Ask your boss what month pay is set where you work. Then plan to ask a month before.',skills:['career']},review:{prompt:'Pay at your job is set each June. When should you ask?',right:'In May, a month before the pay gets decided',wrong:'In June, once you have had more time to get ready',why:'Pay is often set before you hear, so a late ask can\u2019t win.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'In June you ask to go to **summer camp**. You ask nicely and give good reasons. But your family planned the whole summer back in March. What chance does your **perfect request** have now?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The days were booked and the money spent **months ago**. So the answer was no before you spoke. It\u2019s also easier to ask **just after** something good, like a great report, while people remember it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Big choices are often made **before anyone tells you**. Find out when the choice happens. Then ask **before that day**, not after it.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You prepare for weeks. In the review you make **your case** well, and your manager agrees with every word. Then they mention that next year\u2019s budget was settled **a month ago**. What was left to win?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Pay is usually decided in **a budgeting window** that closes weeks before anyone is told. A conversation after that can\u2019t succeed, however well it goes. Finding out **when the window is** may matter more than your words.'},{kind:'story',text:'Jess works out that her firm sets pay in **October**. So she books her conversation for early **September**, straight after her team ships a big project. Her results are fresh and the budget is still open.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Most of the outcome is set **before you speak**. Find the window and **work back from it**. Ask soon after a visible win, while it\u2019s still fresh.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two people make the same request with the same evidence. One gets a raise. The other gets a sympathetic nod. Nothing they said was different. So what decided it **before either** of them **walked in**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The piece names three timing forces. **The budget cycle** fixes what\u2019s available. **Recency** changes how your evidence is judged. And **an alternative**, if you have one, changes your position. None of them is the meeting itself.'},{kind:'story',text:'Omar prepares a strong case and asks in November. His manager agrees with all of it. But the October cycle has closed, and there\u2019s nothing left to allocate. He was persuasive and three weeks too late.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Lena. She asks the same month her team ships a project everyone saw. Six months later, the same request would be judged on memory. Her contribution hasn\u2019t changed. The evidence is just **immediate**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Not every employer has **a fixed cycle**. Small firms often decide case by case, and some keep money back for exceptions. And a request timed only to a fresh win can look opportunistic.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Much of the answer is fixed **before you speak**. Ask when the cycle runs, then **work back** from it. Your words still matter, but only while the window is open.'}]},'the-salary-conversation_4':{title:'Name It and Stop',card:{insight:'Once you say your number, each word you add can bring it down.',tryThis:'Next time you name a price, say it plainly. Then stay quiet for ten seconds.',skills:['communication','career']},review:{prompt:'You name your number and your boss goes quiet. What do you do?',right:'Stay quiet and let your boss answer first',wrong:'Say that you would also take a bit less',why:'If you talk into the quiet, you give your boss a reason to pay less.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You want your friend\u2019s **shiny card**. You say you\u2019ll swap two of yours for it. He goes quiet, and it feels awful. So you add: *or just one is fine.* What **just happened**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'He never said no. You lowered **your own offer** to fill the quiet. Quiet feels like a no, so you want to fix it. But it\u2019s usually just the other person **thinking it over**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When you make an offer, say it once and **stop talking**. Let the other person **answer first**. Filling the quiet usually makes your offer worse.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You name your figure: **\xa352,000**. Your manager looks at the paper and says nothing. Two seconds pass, then four. The quiet feels like **a rejection**. What do you say next?'},{kind:'story',text:'Ben was in that seat. He filled the pause: *I mean, I\u2019m flexible, around fifty would be fine.* His manager hadn\u2019t objected to anything. Ben had just negotiated against himself, and lost two thousand pounds.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Silence isn\u2019t **a power move**. It\u2019s arithmetic. Once a figure is on the table, whoever speaks next tends to move. The only thing you can add is a reason to **offer less**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The silence works with a second habit: say **the figure** without **softening clauses**.',left:{title:'Softened',items:['I was hoping, maybe, around fifty','Already a concession']},right:{title:'Plain',items:['Fifty-two thousand','Then nothing at all']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Name your number **plainly**, then **stop**. Silence isn\u2019t a trick to unsettle anyone. It simply stops you from **bidding against yourself**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Negotiation books love the **long pause**. Name a number, they say, then sit in silence until the other side cracks. It\u2019s sold as **a test of nerve**. But is nerve really what\u2019s going on?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The mechanism is simpler. With a figure on the table, anything you add can only **qualify, justify or reduce** it. Silence feels like rejection, so you want to repair it. Repairing it means **bidding against yourself**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Ana asks for **\xa348,000** and her manager reaches for a calculator. Ana panics and adds that the figure is negotiable. Her manager was only checking the band. Ana opened the bidding against herself.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Kofi. He says his figure without a single softening clause, then waits. The pause runs long. His manager asks how he reached it, so he explains his basis. Then he waits again.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Whoever speaks next moves is **a tendency**, not a law. A long silence can read as hostile, especially in a warm relationship. And it\u2019s no excuse to dodge questions. Answer them, then stop.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The pause isn\u2019t **a contest of nerve**. Anything you add can only **lower your figure**. So say it plainly, answer real questions, and let them speak first.'}]},'the-salary-conversation_5':{title:'The Rest of the Package',card:{insight:'When pay will not move, ask for other things, like a better title.',tryThis:'Tonight, write three things besides pay you would like from your job. Put them in order.',skills:['career','money']},review:{prompt:'Your boss says your pay is at the top for your job. What now?',right:'Ask for a better title or more time off',wrong:'Thank her and agree that the talk is over',why:'A no on pay is a no to one thing, not to everything.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You ask for more **pocket money**, and the answer is no. The family budget is tight this year. You\u2019re about to give up and walk off. Is that **really the end** of it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Pocket money comes from the family\u2019s money. A later bedtime on Fridays **doesn\u2019t cost** a penny. Nor does choosing the film. So a parent who can\u2019t give more money may happily say yes to **something else**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'If the money can\u2019t change, **other things** often can. Know which ones you **really care about**. Then ask for those instead of giving up.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your manager has pushed for you, but the salary **won\u2019t move**. It\u2019s at the top of the band. You thank them and stand up to leave. What did you just **walk away from**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Salary is one part of pay, and often **the least adjustable**. Title, scope, holiday, a training budget and a review at six months are **real compensation** too. Many of them come from a different budget.'},{kind:'story',text:'Ruth hits the same ceiling. But she\u2019d ranked her list beforehand. She asks for a **six-month review** and a training budget. Her manager can\u2019t move the salary, but can say yes to both on the spot.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A no on salary isn\u2019t **a no to everything**. Ask which **constraint** you\u2019ve hit. Then give your manager a way to say yes, by naming what you value.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A manager wants to keep you. You want to stay. The salary is **genuinely fixed**. Both of you leave the meeting with nothing, despite **goodwill on both sides**. How does that happen?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Pay is **a bundle**, and its parts come from different budgets. A band at its ceiling says nothing about the title, the review date or the holiday. So ask which **constraint** you\u2019ve actually hit.'},{kind:'story',text:'In that meeting, Tariq hadn\u2019t thought about anything but salary. When it stalled, he had nothing else to ask for. His manager had three things she could move. She just didn\u2019t know which ones he wanted.'},{kind:'story',text:'Mei prepared differently. She ranked an earlier review first, then remote days, then title. When the salary stuck, she named all three. Her manager, finally able to offer something, closed the deal that afternoon.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Not everything is worth what it looks like. A title without pay can be a cheap way to close you out. And base salary tends to **anchor future raises** in a way a perk doesn\u2019t.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When salary stops, **the conversation** doesn\u2019t have to. Rank what else you value **before you go in**. It gives your manager a way to say yes.'}]},'the-salary-conversation_6':{title:'The Line Drawn at Home',card:{insight:'Pick your lowest pay at home, since saying no during the talk is hard.',tryThis:'Before your next pay talk, write down the least you would take. Add your backup plan.',skills:['career','thinking']},review:{prompt:'Your pay talk is next week. When should you pick your lowest number?',right:'Tonight at home, while you are calm, and write it down',wrong:'During the talk, once you have heard their offer',why:'During the talk, saying no feels too hard, so pick your number before.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You go to the shops with **\xa35** and a list. By the till sit rows of **bright sweets**. You hadn\u2019t planned on any. What usually happens next?'},{kind:'explain',text:'If you decided at home, you **stick to the list**. If you decide in the shop, the sweets usually win. Saying no is hard **in the moment**. It\u2019s much easier when you\u2019re calm and nobody\u2019s watching.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Before a deal, decide **the least** you\u2019ll accept. Write it down **at home**. Only pick a line you\u2019d truly keep if they say no.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'The offer is **\xa33,000 below** what you wanted. Your manager is friendly and waiting. The process has taken weeks. Saying no feels rude and **hard to face**. Do you say yes?'},{kind:'explain',text:'In the room, three things push you to accept. There are **the weeks** already spent and the relationship. And there\u2019s the sheer discomfort of refusing. None of them were part of your thinking **when you were calm**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Alex wrote a note the night before: *\xa350,000, or I take the other offer.* In the room, Alex only had to check the figure against the note. It was a decision made in writing, not under pressure.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Decide your walk-away **before**, not during. Write it down with **the alternative** behind it. Then the room only asks you to compare, not to choose.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'People often leave a negotiation having accepted something they\u2019d **privately ruled out** the night before. They weren\u2019t tricked. Nobody lied. So what changed **between the kitchen table** and the meeting room?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The claim is strong: the walk-away **can\u2019t be decided** in the room at all. There, you feel the sunk cost of the process, the relationship and the discomfort of refusing. Written down beforehand, it becomes **a commitment**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Noor writes the night before: fifty thousand, or the other offer stands. The meeting is warm and long. When the offer comes in at forty-eight, Noor checks the note, thanks them, and declines.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Chris, whose line was a bluff. There was no other offer and no plan to leave. When the employer held firm, Chris gave way. The line had protected nothing, because it **was never real**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. A fixed line can be **too rigid**. New facts, like a bigger scope or a better title, can change what an offer is worth. Pressure shouldn\u2019t move your line, but information can.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your position is set by **your alternative**. Fix your walk-away while you\u2019re calm, and make sure you\u2019d act on it. If you have no alternative, **better to know** now.'}]}}}},1015,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'the-second-in-command-problem',kind:'book',promise:'Do the job that keeps a team running',about:'Who turns a boss\u2019s big idea into a real plan? Often, it is the person just below the boss. Few people ever learn that person\u2019s name. This essay shows what that job is and how to do it well. Is that person you?',ideas:{'the-second-in-command-problem_1':{title:'Turning Ideas Into Plans',card:{insight:'A big idea is not a plan until it has steps and dates.',tryThis:'Take one big goal you were given. Write its first three steps, each with a person and a date.',skills:['productivity','leadership']},review:{prompt:'Your boss says the team should use less paper this year. What do you do?',right:'Write the first steps and give each a date',wrong:'Wait for her to send a full plan to follow',why:'Someone has to write the steps and dates to make it a plan.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your friend has a great idea for a class trip to the seaside, and everyone cheers. But who books the coach, and who brings the lunches? By **which day**? Somebody has to **work it out**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'A big idea isn\u2019t **a plan** yet. Someone has to turn it into who does what, in what order, and by when. When that\u2019s done well, nobody notices. It looks like the idea **just happened** by itself.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Turning an idea into **a real plan** is a job of its own. It\u2019s where things most often go wrong. So when you hear a big idea, **offer to write** the list.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'The founder of a bakery chain tells everyone: *next year we open in three new cities.* There\u2019s applause. The next morning, someone has to decide **which city first**, who moves there and when. Would you call that **just admin**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Priya is the founder\u2019s deputy. She works out that the second city needs a lease first, then a manager, then staff. She sets a date for each and checks who can really move. Nobody calls it strategy.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Administration carries out a plan. This job **produces the plan**, which takes judgement about what can happen, in what order, with whom. That\u2019s why it\u2019s nearer to **translation** than paperwork. And when it\u2019s done well, the direction looks self-executing.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'It looks like paperwork, because **its outputs** are calendars. The real difference is **who decides**.',left:{title:'Administration',items:['Follows a finished plan','Knows the function','Can come from outside']},right:{title:'This role',items:['Makes the plan','Knows this organisation','Hard to fill from outside']}},{kind:'moral',text:'A direction isn\u2019t a plan until someone **converts it** into order, people and dates. That\u2019s where **most failures happen**. When you\u2019re handed a vague goal, draft the plan yourself.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Every institution that lasted had **someone one rung** below the person it\u2019s named after. You rarely know their name. So what did that person actually do all day, and why does **nobody remember**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The founder supplies **the direction**. Someone else turns it into sequence, dependencies and dates. That conversion is where most failure happens. It gets filed as admin because its outputs are calendars, but it\u2019s closer to **translation**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Priya\u2019s founder announces three new cities. Priya sees that the second city can\u2019t open without a lease. And the lease can\u2019t be signed until the first one is profitable. She orders the steps and dates them. From outside, it seems to run itself.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a hospital that hires an outside project manager to replace a retiring deputy. He knows planning methods well. But he doesn\u2019t know which ward can really absorb more patients, or which consultant always runs late. The schedules start slipping.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. In some places the founder does the conversion too, and there\u2019s no deputy role to fill. And if a direction is **genuinely incoherent**, quietly patching it into a plan hides a problem you should raise.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A direction does nothing until someone **converts it** into a schedule. Done well, that work is **invisible**, and it rests on knowing one organisation deeply. That\u2019s why it\u2019s so hard to replace.'}]},'the-second-in-command-problem_2':{title:'The Hard Number',card:{insight:'If a date is too soon, bring a plan that fits it.',tryThis:'Pick one deadline you doubt. Write one line on what you could finish by that date.',skills:['communication','leadership']},review:{prompt:'Your boss wants a launch in six weeks. It needs four months. What do you say?',right:'Say four months, and show what fits in six weeks',wrong:'Agree to six weeks and hope it works out',why:'A hard number with a plan beside it sounds like help.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your class wants to build a huge model castle for the school fair. The fair is in one week, and you can tell it\u2019ll take three. Everyone\u2019s excited. Do you **say so**, or **keep quiet**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'If you say it\u2019ll take three weeks, you might sound grumpy. But if nobody says it, the castle **won\u2019t be ready**. The trick is to bring **a smaller plan** too, like one tower that fits the week.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Sometimes your job is to say **the hard number**. Say it kindly, and bring **a plan that fits** it. Then people hear help, not grumbling.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'The leadership team wants the new app out **in six weeks**. Heads nod around the table. You\u2019ve looked at the work, and it needs **four months**. Everyone else is sincerely hopeful. What do you say?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Saying the unwelcome number is **the core duty**, not loyalty. Everyone else in the room has a reason to be optimistic, and they mean it. So if you don\u2019t say the date can\u2019t be met, **nobody will**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Dev, the deputy, says it plainly: four months. Then he adds that in six weeks they could run a trial with two customers, testing the riskiest part first. The room still hears the bad news. But now it comes with a plan.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The objection alone reads as **obstruction**, however accurate. An objection with an option **reads as help**.',left:{title:'Just the no',items:['Six weeks won\u2019t work','Sounds like attitude','Filed as negative']},right:{title:'No plus option',items:['Four months, or a trial','Sounds like planning','Heard as help']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Your value is saying the number **nobody wants**, again and again. You stay heard by bringing **the version that fits** it. Don\u2019t just say what can\u2019t be done by that date.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'In most planning meetings there\u2019s one person who says *that\u2019s four months, not six weeks.* Anyone can say it once. So why do so few people **keep saying it** without being labelled **the obstacle**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The skill isn\u2019t loyalty. It\u2019s stating **the unwelcome number** in the room, repeatedly, without being reclassified as an obstacle. This duty can\u2019t be handed off, because everyone else\u2019s optimism is **sincere**, which makes it more persuasive, not less.'},{kind:'story',text:'Dev tells the board the launch needs four months. In the same breath, he offers a six-week trial with two customers that tests the riskiest part. The objection is still there. This time, it arrives with a plan beside it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a head nurse whose ward is asked to absorb a new clinic. She says the team can\u2019t take it on without dropping something. She names which task could pause for a month. Her manager agrees to the swap.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Sometimes no version fits the number, and the honest answer is just no. Bringing options can slide into **accepting a target** you should refuse. And you can be wrong, since caution isn\u2019t accuracy.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Being reliably right about limits looks **just like negativity** in the moment, and the vindication rarely gets credited. So never bring the objection alone. Bring **what can be done** by that date instead.'}]},'the-second-in-command-problem_3':{title:'The Crisis That Didn\u2019t Happen',card:{insight:'When you stop a problem early, no one sees it, so no one thanks you.',tryThis:'Write down the two or three people who see your work. Then ask if that is enough for you.',skills:['career','psychology']},review:{prompt:'Your boss gets praised for a smooth year you made happen. What now?',right:'Be proud of the work and the people who see it',wrong:'Make sure everyone hears how much you did',why:'No one can see a problem that never came, so be proud of the work.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'When an umbrella works, you stay dry and never think about it. You only notice it when it **blows inside out** and you get soaked. Who ever says **thank you** to an umbrella?'},{kind:'story',text:'The school trip goes perfectly, and everyone thanks the head teacher. Nobody thanks Mrs Hale. She rang the coach firm twice and packed plasters for the rocky beach. Nothing went wrong, so nobody saw her work.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Some jobs stop bad things happening, and you **can\u2019t see** a problem that never came. So don\u2019t do them for thanks. Be **proud of the job** itself.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Nobody resigned this year, the big supplier stayed and the launch didn\u2019t slip. At the annual party, the chief executive gets the applause. Her deputy claps too. What exactly is **there to praise**, if **nothing happened**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'This role mostly **produces absences**: the crisis that didn\u2019t happen, the hire who didn\u2019t leave. Absences can\u2019t be attributed to anyone. And outcomes go to the person whose direction it was, which **isn\u2019t unfair**, since the direction was theirs.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Chasing credit tends to **harm the work**. The people who last **look elsewhere**.',left:{title:'Chasing credit',items:['Making the difficulty visible','Letting things briefly fail','Narrating it to others']},right:{title:'Finding it elsewhere',items:['A few who see it','The outcome itself','The work\u2019s own satisfaction']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Credit is **structurally unavailable** here, and expecting it will corrode you. The people who last find their satisfaction in the work and **a few witnesses**. If that\u2019s not enough for you, do something else.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Think of a smooth year where you work. The **disasters that didn\u2019t happen** leave nothing to point at. So how would anyone ever know who prevented them, and what does that do to **the person** who did?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The second-in-command\u2019s best work is **absences**. Outcomes get credited to whoever set the direction, and that\u2019s fair as far as it goes. What stays hidden is that the direction would have **produced nothing** without the conversion.'},{kind:'story',text:'Lena ran operations for a charity for six years. When donors praised the director, she started mentioning her own role at dinners. Then she let a deadline slip, just to show what happened without her. The team paid for it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Tom does the same job at a theatre. He shows two colleagues what he\u2019s doing and why. When the season runs smoothly, their nod and the full house are enough. He\u2019s still there ten years later.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. *Structurally unavailable* can become an excuse for leaders never to look, and a good one should credit the deputy. Needing recognition is **ordinary**, not a flaw. And the claim about who lasts is a pattern, not a measured fact.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You can\u2019t be credited for what didn\u2019t happen, and chasing it **corrodes the work**. Find recognition in **a few people** who see it, and in the outcome. If that\u2019s not enough, choose another role.'}]},'the-second-in-command-problem_4':{title:'The Wrong Next Boss',card:{insight:'Being a great helper does not prove you can pick the goals.',tryThis:'Write one goal for your team that nobody handed you. If you get stuck, that is worth knowing.',skills:['leadership','career']},review:{prompt:'A great helper is up for the top job. What should the owner check?',right:'Whether she can pick the goals, not just plan them',wrong:'Her long record of running things well for years',why:'It takes one skill to pick a goal and another to plan it.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'The vice-captain has been brilliant all year, turning the captain\u2019s ideas into training plans. Now the captain is leaving. Everyone says the vice-captain should **take over**. Will they be **a great captain**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A helper turns someone else\u2019s idea into a plan. A leader has to **come up with** the idea. These are **nearly opposite** jobs. You can be great at one and find the other really hard.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Being a brilliant helper doesn\u2019t prove you\u2019ll be **a brilliant leader**. They\u2019re **different skills**, and both are good ones. Ask which one you enjoy more.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Marcus has been the finance director\u2019s deputy for eight years and knows every process inside out. When she retires, the board promotes him. In his first month he sits waiting. **No direction arrives**. What\u2019s **gone wrong**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The deputy\u2019s job is **narrowing** a wide intention into a plan. The top job is **generating** the intention in the first place. These skills are close to opposite. So excellence at the first is weak evidence for the second.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Promotion looks like the obvious reward, but the deputy\u2019s record **tests only one** of **two jobs**.',left:{title:'The deputy\u2019s job',items:['Receives a direction','Narrows it into steps','Judged on delivery']},right:{title:'The top job',items:['Sets the direction','Starts from nothing','Judged on choices']}},{kind:'moral',text:'The best deputy is often **the wrong successor**. Good institutions tend to **promote sideways**, giving more scope to the skill you\u2019ve actually seen. Check for direction-setting before you hand over the top job.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A deputy has run your organisation day to day for years and earned a reward. Promotion to the top job looks like **the obvious fit**. So why do so many deputies **struggle** once they\u2019re in it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'One role narrows a wide intention into **a plan**. The other generates the intention. They\u2019re close to opposite. The deputy\u2019s record is a record of narrowing someone else\u2019s direction, with almost **no observations** of generating one.'},{kind:'story',text:'Marcus was the finance director\u2019s deputy for eight years. Promoted on her retirement, he spends his first month waiting for a direction to plan. None comes, because setting it is now his job. Nobody had ever watched him do that.'},{kind:'story',text:'Compare a museum whose deputy director is superb at turning plans into exhibitions. Instead of promoting her, the trustees widen her remit to every schedule in the building. She thrives, and they find a new director elsewhere.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The pattern is *often*, not always: some people have both skills, and setting direction **can be learned**. A deputy also knows the organisation deeply, which an outsider lacks. And endless sideways moves can make the role a ceiling.'},{kind:'moral',text:'If you\u2019re weighing a deputy for the top job, look for evidence they can **set a direction**. Their record of delivery won\u2019t show it. Promoting sideways is **often wiser**.'}]},'the-second-in-command-problem_5':{title:'Two Weeks Away',card:{insight:'If things break when you are gone, the steps were only in your head.',tryThis:'List what would break if you were gone for two weeks. Write down the steps for the top one.',skills:['productivity','career']},review:{prompt:'Your coworker is proud that only she can pay the staff. What do you think?',right:'It is stuck on her, so write the steps and train someone',wrong:'It keeps her job safe, so she should keep it that way',why:'A job only one person can do keeps that person stuck in it.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re in charge of the class fish tank. Only you know how much food to give and when to change the water. Then you\u2019re ill for **a whole week**. What happens to **the fish**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'If something only works when you\u2019re there, it\u2019s **stuck on you**. Writing the steps on a card fixes that. Then anyone can **take over**, and you\u2019re free to try new jobs too.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Being the only one who knows feels important, but it **traps you**. **Write it down** and teach someone else. Then things keep going when you\u2019re away.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A deputy takes her **first proper holiday** in three years. While she\u2019s away, a supplier isn\u2019t paid, the newsletter never goes out, and nobody can open the safe. What did **her absence** reveal?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A function that only works when **you\u2019re present** isn\u2019t a function. It\u2019s **a dependency**. And a dependency can\u2019t be improved, promoted out of or safely scaled. Feeling indispensable is comfortable, but it quietly **becomes a ceiling**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The same job can be done **as a person** or **as a system**.',left:{title:'A dependency',items:['Lives in one head','Breaks when you\u2019re away','Keeps you stuck']},right:{title:'A system',items:['Written down','A trained deputy','Runs while you rest']}},{kind:'moral',text:'The best operators make themselves **briefly unnecessary** on purpose. Take two weeks off, and treat whatever breaks as **your real job** description. Then write it down and train someone.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Being the one person who can run payroll feels like **security**. People need you. Nobody can move you. So why do the best operators work so hard to make themselves **briefly unnecessary**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A deputy whose absence stops the organisation has built **a dependency**, not a system. The good ones document the process, train a deputy, take leave and let **the structure run**. Then they watch what happens.'},{kind:'story',text:'Sara runs operations for a small hotel and takes her first holiday in three years. An invoice sits unsigned, next month\u2019s rota never appears, and nobody knows the alarm code. She comes home to a list of everything she never wrote down.'},{kind:'story',text:'Compare Jon, who keeps a running manual for the warehouse he manages. Before his leave, his assistant runs the place for a week while he watches. When Jon goes, two small things wobble. He writes both down.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. In some workplaces, being indispensable really **is protection**, and being replaceable makes you easier to cut. Not everyone can take two weeks off. And some know-how is tacit, so it resists being written down.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The person who can\u2019t be replaced **can\u2019t be moved**, so the role becomes a ceiling. Indispensability is **job security**, not a career. Treat what breaks in your absence as the work still to do.'}]}}}},1016,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'the-two-lists',kind:'book',promise:'Say no kindly and keep your free time',about:'How did your week get so full? Most of us got there one small yes at a time. The team behind this app wrote these ideas. They show what each yes costs and how to say no kindly. Which yes will you check first?',ideas:{'the-two-lists_1':{title:'The Unnamed No',card:{insight:'Each yes takes time from something else you have not named yet.',tryThis:'Next time someone asks a favor, name one thing you would give up to say yes.',skills:['productivity','thinking']},review:{prompt:'A coworker asks you to join a call on Thursday night. What do you ask first?',right:'Ask what I would give up by saying yes',wrong:'Ask if the call is important enough to join',why:'Each yes takes the place of something else, so name it first.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your friend asks you to help at her **cake stall** on Saturday morning. It\u2019s only two hours, so you say yes straight away. It feels like it **costs nothing**. But does it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Saturday morning was going to be something, like your match or a lie-in. Your week is like a **full lunchbox**. To fit in an apple, something has to come out. A yes doesn\u2019t make the week bigger. It **swaps one thing** for another.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Saying yes to one thing means **saying no** to another. You just can\u2019t see the no yet. So **give it a name** before you agree.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Thursday is your **only free evening** this week. Someone asks you to join a planning call then. It\u2019s a good cause and it\u2019s only an hour. So why does saying yes feel **so easy**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Priya, who says yes to the call on the spot. The request had a face, a task and a reason. The cost had nothing. Thursday arrives, and she realises she\u2019s **given up** the one evening she rests.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'When you\u2019re asked, one side of the trade is **vivid** and the other is **hidden**.',left:{title:'What you see',items:['The person asking','A clear task','A good reason']},right:{title:'What you don\u2019t',items:['The project that slips','The evening that goes','The slack in your week']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Agreeing doesn\u2019t add **any capacity**. It pushes out whatever would have used that time. So name the trade plainly: the call, or **your rest**. The call is worth doing. It just isn\u2019t worth that evening.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A yes feels free because its cost has **no name** yet. Ask what you\u2019re **giving up** by agreeing. If you can\u2019t answer, you don\u2019t know the price.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Most people who are badly over-committed never made **one bad decision**. They said yes, reasonably, again and again. Each answer looked fine at the time. So where did **the whole year** go?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Every acceptance spends time that was free for **something else**. That something else is rarely named when you agree. So the request is concrete and the cost is **deferred and anonymous**. You weigh something against nothing, and something wins.'},{kind:'story',text:'Priya is asked to join a Thursday planning call. Named plainly, the choice is the call or her only free evening. The call is worth doing on its own. It isn\u2019t worth what it costs, so she declines.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Ben, asked to review a friend\u2019s draft on Sunday. He names what it displaces: a lazy morning he wouldn\u2019t miss much. The trade is cheap, so he says yes gladly. Naming the cost changed nothing, and that\u2019s fine.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The claim that many requests fail the comparison is an observation, not a measured finding. Some requests outrank what they push out. Pushed too far, the habit becomes **accounting** that refuses every favour.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Naming the cost is meant to **inform a yes**, not forbid one. Before you agree, say what you\u2019re declining. Then you\u2019re **weighing two things**, not one.'}]},'the-two-lists_2':{title:'The Tomorrow Test',card:{insight:'A yes for next month feels free, but that week will be full too.',tryThis:'Next time you are asked to help next month, act as if it is tomorrow. Then answer.',skills:['productivity','thinking']},review:{prompt:'In January, a friend asks you to give a talk in March. What do you ask?',right:'Would I say yes if it began tomorrow?',wrong:'Is March still open on my calendar right now?',why:'March will be as full as this week, so act as if it is tomorrow.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'In May, Sam\u2019s asked to make a costume for the school play in June. June feels **ages away**, so he says yes. What can\u2019t he see yet about **that month**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'June arrives. Sam has tests, football practice and now a costume too. Far-off weeks look empty only because **nothing\u2019s planned yet**. When they come, they\u2019re **just as busy** as this one. Future you is just you, later.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Plans far away feel easy because you **can\u2019t see** how busy you\u2019ll be. So ask yourself one thing: would I say yes if it was **tomorrow**?'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'In January a friend asks you to give **a short talk** in March. March is blank in your diary, so you say yes. It feels **almost weightless**. Why?'},{kind:'story',text:'March arrives, and you\u2019re writing slides at eleven at night. It\u2019s a week that looks exactly like every other week. Nothing about the talk changed. Only the date moved from far away to **right now**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The same request feels different depending on **the tense** you **answer it in**.',left:{title:'Answered for March',items:['A blank diary','A rested, imagined you','No visible cost']},right:{title:'Answered for tomorrow',items:['This week\u2019s real load','You as you are','The full price']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s the test. Ask whether you\u2019d say yes if it **began tomorrow** morning. The calendar six weeks out is empty because **nothing\u2019s booked**, not because you\u2019ll be free. It\u2019s the same commitment either way.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A distant date isn\u2019t a **lighter week**. It\u2019s a week you can\u2019t see yet. So answer **in the present tense**, the way you\u2019ll actually live it.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A request for **six weeks away** arrives. You check your diary and find the week **wide open**. Saying yes seems to cost nothing at all. What is that empty diary actually telling you?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Two things make a distant yes feel weightless. The cost falls on **a future self** you can\u2019t picture in detail. And the weeks around the date are blank. Neither is **evidence of free time**.'},{kind:'story',text:'In January, Ola agrees to give a talk in March. By March she\u2019s writing slides late at night, in a week like any other. Asked for tomorrow, she\u2019d have seen that load. Asked for March, she saw a blank square.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Dev, asked in May to join a committee starting in autumn. Autumn looks free. He pictures the first meeting as tomorrow evening, squeezed between work and dinner. He realises he\u2019d say no, and says so now.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Sometimes the future really is lighter, because an exam season ends or a project ships. Then the test is too harsh. It can also overweight a bad day. And it measures **cost, not value**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The distance is doing **much of the work** when a yes feels easy. Move the date to tomorrow and see what\u2019s left. Some things are still **worth a heavy Thursday**.'}]},'the-two-lists_3':{title:'The Slow No',card:{insight:'When you know the answer is no, a quick no is kinder.',tryThis:'Find one ask you have been putting off that you know is a no. Answer it today.',skills:['communication','relationships']},review:{prompt:'A neighbor asks you to feed her cat next month. You will be away. What now?',right:'Say no today so she can ask someone else',wrong:'Say you will check and get back to her',why:'A slow no costs you both a week, so answer once you know.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your friend invites you to her party on Saturday, and you know you **can\u2019t go**. But you say *maybe*, so all week she counts you in. What happens when you finally tell her on **Friday**?'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Waiting doesn\u2019t make a no **softer**. It just makes it land **later and harder**.',left:{title:'A quick no',items:['She knows today','She can ask someone else','You stop worrying']},right:{title:'A slow no',items:['She waits all week','She thinks you\u2019re coming','You dread telling her']}},{kind:'moral',text:'If you already know your answer is no, **say it now**. A quick no lets the other person **make a new plan**. That\u2019s a kindness, even if it doesn\u2019t feel like one.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A neighbour asks if you can **feed their cat** while they\u2019re away next month. You know you\u2019ll be away too. So you say, *let me check and come back to you.* Where does **that answer** leave them?'},{kind:'story',text:'They hear a likely yes, so they don\u2019t book anyone else. A week later you tell them no. Now they\u2019re scrambling, with less time to find someone. Your gentle delay has made the refusal land harder.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The delay costs you too. An undecided request **doesn\u2019t sit quietly**. It comes back each time you think of it, and often takes more attention than the task would. The discomfort isn\u2019t avoided. It\u2019s **spread across the week**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'So answer quickly, **whichever way** it goes. A prompt no is more useful than a slow one. It\u2019s far more useful than **a yes given** only to dodge the discomfort of saying no.'},{kind:'moral',text:'*Let me check* feels softer than no, but it\u2019s usually worse. It costs them **a week of uncertainty** and costs you **a week of dread**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Putting off a refusal is usually seen as **the polite thing**. You\u2019re sparing someone\u2019s feelings, at least for now. But who is actually paying for **that delay**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The other person pays first. While they wait, they **can\u2019t make other plans**. Many of them read the delay as **a soft yes**. So the eventual no arrives later and lands harder than a quick one would.'},{kind:'story',text:'A neighbour asks you to feed her cat next month, and you know you\u2019ll be away. You say you\u2019ll check. She stops looking for anyone else. A week later your no reaches her, with far less time left.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Kofi, invited to help organise a reunion he knows he won\u2019t attend. He leaves the message unanswered for ten days. Every time he opens his inbox, it nags at him. The reply, when he sends it, takes one minute.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The idea applies when you **already know** the answer. If you really need to check, say when you\u2019ll reply and keep to it. A large request may deserve real thought first. And people hear a quick no differently.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When you know the answer, **speed is the courtesy**. A prompt no lets everyone move on. It\u2019s far kinder than **a yes** you only gave to avoid saying no.'}]},'the-two-lists_4':{title:'One True Reason',card:{insight:'Say no to the ask, not the person, and give one true reason.',tryThis:'Write your next no, then cut every reason but the truest one. Read it out loud.',skills:['communication','relationships']},review:{prompt:'Your team asks you to lead the office move, but you cannot. What do you say?',right:'Give one true reason, then offer what you can',wrong:'List all the ways you are busy so they see you mean it',why:'Each extra reason is one more thing they can fix, so give just one.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your friend asks you to help build **a huge model** on Saturday. You can\u2019t, because you have swimming. You could list every reason you\u2019re busy. Or you could **keep it short**. Which works better?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Every extra reason is **a problem to fix**. You say you have no lift, and your friend says, *my dad can drive you!* Now you need a new reason. **One true reason** is enough.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Say what you can\u2019t do, give **one reason**, and offer something else if you can. Then stop talking. You\u2019re saying no to **the plan**, not your friend.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re asked to **lead the office move**. You can\u2019t take it on. You start drafting a reply, and it grows to a paragraph of apologies and reasons. Is **all that explaining** helping?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Each reason turns your refusal into **a problem to solve**. Too busy this week? They\u2019ll offer next week. So the talk becomes **a negotiation**. A long justification also reads as guilt, as if your no isn\u2019t settled.'},{kind:'story',text:'Here\u2019s the whole reply instead. I can\u2019t take this on, because I\u2019m committed to the audit until June. I can pass on my notes from the last move. There\u2019s nothing in it to solve, and nothing personal.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Decline **the request**, not the person. Say what you can\u2019t do, give **one true reason**, and offer what you can. Then stop.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Most people expect a refusal to **hurt the relationship**. Often it doesn\u2019t. The request gets turned down and life goes on. So what is it that actually **leaves a sting**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'What stings is feeling **declined personally**, as if asking was the mistake. Much of the difference is in the phrasing. Talk about **the task** and your own capacity. Don\u2019t talk about them, or whether they should have asked.'},{kind:'story',text:'Asked to lead the office move, Lena replies in two lines. She can\u2019t, because the quarter-end accounts take all of her time until June. She offers to brief whoever does it. Her manager has nothing to argue with.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Joe, who refuses the same job with five reasons and a vague maybe. His manager solves two reasons on the spot. Neither of them is sure what was decided. A week later, Joe\u2019s leading the move.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. One reason only works if **it\u2019s true**: a convenient excuse gets solved or found out. Some people are owed more explanation, like a partner. And phrasing can\u2019t remove real consequences when the asker has power over you.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Be clear, give **one true reason**, and offer something real if you can. Then stop. You can refuse the request and **keep the person**.'}]},'the-two-lists_5':{title:'Check Your Old Yeses',card:{insight:'Old yeses keep going on their own, so ask if you would say yes today.',tryThis:'Pick one thing you do every week. Ask if you would agree to it today as a new request.',skills:['productivity','thinking']},review:{prompt:'You have written a monthly newsletter for three years. How should you judge it now?',right:'Ask if you would agree to it today as a new request',wrong:'Keep going, since you have already given it three years',why:'Old yeses go on by themselves, so judge each one as if it were new.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You joined **chess club** two years ago because your best friend did. She left last year. You still go every Tuesday. Did you ever actually **decide to stay**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Starting something needs a yes. Stopping it needs you to **do something**. Keeping on needs **nothing at all**. So things carry on long after their reason has gone, like toys piling up in a box.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Old yeses **keep going** on their own. So every now and then, ask about each one: if I was asked today, **would I say yes**? If not, it\u2019s time to talk about it.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Think about your week. How much of it is **new requests**, and how much is **old ones** you agreed to years ago? Which part do you think is quietly filling it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The recurring meeting, the committee, the newsletter: each was agreed **for a reason**. That reason may have expired. Each one continues because ending it **takes an act**, and continuing takes nothing.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The same commitment gets **a different answer** depending on **how you ask**.',left:{title:'Should I stop?',items:['Weighs years already given','Feels like letting people down']},right:{title:'Would I start?',items:['Weighs only the cost ahead','Just a fresh decision']}},{kind:'story',text:'You\u2019ve mentored a former colleague monthly for three years. Asked fresh, you wouldn\u2019t start it, because she\u2019s outgrown it. So you raise it gently. She admits she kept coming partly so as not to let you down.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Most over-commitment isn\u2019t new requests but **old ones** that never ended. So re-ask each standing yes: would I **agree to this today**, as a new request?'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'You may know the move with subscriptions. You ask: would I **sign up today**, at this price? If not, you cancel. So why not ask the same about **your commitments**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Asked as a new request, a commitment **loses two weights**. One is **the sunk cost** of what you\u2019ve already given. The other is the social weight of withdrawing. Without them, it\u2019s just a decision about the cost from now.'},{kind:'story',text:'Ama joined her street\u2019s residents\u2019 committee four years ago to fight a planning decision. That fight ended long ago. Asked fresh, she wouldn\u2019t join now. When she steps down, two others admit they\u2019d stayed only because she had.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a team\u2019s weekly status meeting, set up for a launch long since finished. Nobody chose to keep it. Ending it would take an act, so it continued. Re-asked as a new request, it lasts **one more week**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some commitments matter **because they last**: caring for a relative, a long friendship, a promise meant to be kept. Re-asking them often erodes the reliability that makes them worth it. So re-ask now and then, not constantly.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Standing commitments **renew silently**, so they need a periodic re-ask. Would you agree to this today, knowing **what you know now**? Often the honest answer is no.'}]}}}},1017,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'up-from-slavery',kind:'book',promise:'Get things done when the odds are against you',about:'What can you do when almost every door is shut to you? Booker T. Washington was born enslaved. He grew up to build a big school from almost nothing. Here are his tricks, and where they fell short. Want to see them?',ideas:{'up-from-slavery_1':{title:'The School Before the Speech',card:{insight:'Talk ends when the crowd goes home, but what you build keeps working.',tryThis:'Pick one plan you keep talking about. Today, start a small, real version with what you have.',skills:['leadership','history']},review:{prompt:'Your street wants a place to swap books. What would Washington do?',right:'Build a small shelf this week with what you have',wrong:'Write the city a long letter asking for one',why:'A small, real shelf helps now, and it keeps working after the talk ends.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two friends want a treehouse. One spends the summer **arguing** that it can be done. The other starts **nailing boards** to the tree. By next summer, which of them has something to show?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Washington was born enslaved in Virginia. In 1881 he was asked to start a school for Black students at Tuskegee, in Alabama. It had **no proper buildings** and almost no money. So he and his students **built it themselves**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'An argument only works on **people listening now**. A thing you build **keeps working** after they stop. If you\u2019ve got a big plan, start a small real version of it.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your town has nowhere for teenagers to go after school. You could spend **a year writing** to the council. Or you could open a hall two nights a week, with **borrowed tables**. Which would you choose?'},{kind:'story',text:'Washington was born enslaved and freed as a child. In 1881 he was asked to open a school for Black students in Alabama. It began with almost nothing. Up From Slavery, published in 1901, tells what came next: buildings, a brickyard, farms and donors.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'His reason was **durability**. An argument has to be won again, but **an institution** keeps going.',left:{title:'An argument',items:['Persuades whoever is listening','Must be made again','Stops when the speaker stops']},right:{title:'An institution',items:['Works whoever is convinced','Trains people who teach others','Outlasts its founder']}},{kind:'explain',text:'It was also a choice made **under constraint**. In the South of the 1890s, demanding rights in public could be dangerous. Building something white donors would fund was **one of the few** roads left open. Critics said that came at a price.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Arguments have to be **made again** for every new audience. A school you\u2019ve built **keeps running** either way. So start the smallest real version of your plan, and count the terms you accept.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Up From Slavery came out in 1901. You might expect a book by a man born enslaved to be **an argument**. Instead it\u2019s mostly about **building**: walls, a brickyard, farms. Why would he tell it that way?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Washington\u2019s method was to **build institutions** rather than win debates. An argument persuades the people already listening. It has to be **remade each generation**. A working school produces graduates, who teach others, whoever happens to be convinced.'},{kind:'story',text:'Tuskegee opened in 1881 with no proper buildings and almost no money. The students raised the buildings themselves. Then came a brickyard, farms and a curriculum. Each step made the next one easier to fund. None of it waited on winning a debate.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a charity that spent years lobbying for a free legal clinic. One spring it opens **a small clinic** instead, one evening a week, with volunteer lawyers. People get help **that week**. The case for clinics is now standing in the room.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Building was chosen because few other roads were open, and donors set **the terms**. Du Bois said those terms get built in with the walls. And the rights Washington set aside were won later by **protest and courts**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A thing **you build** keeps working when the debate moves on. But **the funders\u2019 terms** are built in too. Count them as part of the price you pay.'}]},'up-from-slavery_2':{title:'The Bet on Being Needed',card:{insight:'Being needed gives you power, but it does not make people fair.',tryThis:'Write one fair thing you hope hard work will earn you. Then ask for it this week.',skills:['thinking','history']},review:{prompt:'You\u2019re told to accept low pay until the company needs you. What\u2019s the risk?',right:'There is no promise of fair treatment, even once they need you',wrong:'Hard work always earns fair treatment in the end',why:'Being needed gives you power, but no one has to treat you fairly.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'After slavery ended, Black people in the South were still treated **very unfairly**. Washington had a plan to change that. Do you think he wanted to **ask loudly** for fair treatment, or try something else first?'},{kind:'explain',text:'His bet was to **be needed first**. If a town can\u2019t do without your skills, he thought, it must treat you better. He didn\u2019t think rights had to be earned. He thought this was **the order** they\u2019d arrive in.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Washington\u2019s plan was about **order**, not about whether rights were owed. Another writer, Du Bois, said it gave up **too much now**. You can learn from both.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A manager tells you to work hard and **keep quiet** for five years. By then, she says, the firm will depend on you so much it **must treat you** fairly. Would you take that deal?'},{kind:'explain',text:'That deal is Washington\u2019s strategy in small. He bet that **economic indispensability** would win rights that demands alone couldn\u2019t. Learn skills the region needs. Own land and run trades. Then **the dependence** does the arguing for you.'},{kind:'story',text:'In 1903 Du Bois replied in The Souls of Black Folk. He also valued work and property. But Black Americans were being asked to set aside the vote, civil rights and higher education for now. All three were being taken from them in those years.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Neither man doubted the rights were **owed**. They disagreed about **the order** of winning them.',left:{title:'Washington',items:['Build economic footing first','Avoid open confrontation','Rights follow need']},right:{title:'Du Bois',items:['Demand rights now','Protest openly and keep going','Without votes, gains get taken']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Being needed can bring **real leverage**. But ask what guarantees **the last step**, from need to rights. If nothing does, you may be giving up the present for a promise.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two Black leaders looked at the same South around 1900. Both thought the rights were **owed**. Yet one answered the other in public, at length. What could two people on **the same side** disagree about?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The answer was **sequence**. Washington\u2019s theory wasn\u2019t that rights had to be deserved. It was a prediction about **leverage**. A region that depends on your skills and trade has reason to concede what it would refuse an appeal.'},{kind:'story',text:'Du Bois answered in 1903, in The Souls of Black Folk. He agreed that work and property mattered. His charge was about timing. While Black Americans were urged to wait, the vote was being stripped away and segregation written into law.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a worker who takes low pay for years, sure the firm will come to rely on her. It does. Then a new owner arrives and **cuts her job** anyway. Being needed gave her leverage, but **no guarantee**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Economic footing did matter, and many Tuskegee graduates built it. But leverage can be **cancelled by force**. In Tulsa in 1921, white mobs destroyed a prosperous Black community. Without political power, what you\u2019ve earned can be taken.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Washington\u2019s bet was about **the order** rights arrive in. Du Bois asked what **guarantees the last link**. Before you bet on being needed, ask the same question.'}]},'up-from-slavery_3':{title:'Respect for Work Done Well',card:{insight:'Respect a job for how well it is done, not for its title.',tryThis:'Next time you judge someone by job title, ask how well they do the work.',skills:['career','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'A store gives its cleaners a fancy new title but nothing else. Is the work better?',right:'No, the work is only as good as how well it is done',wrong:'Yes, a better title makes the work count for more',why:'A new title cannot make the work better, but doing it well can.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two people fix bikes in your street. One calls himself a **bike engineer** but rushes every job. The other just says she fixes bikes, and they **never break again**. Whose work would you respect more?'},{kind:'explain',text:'At Washington\u2019s school, the students **built the buildings**, grew the food and made the bricks. He made them learn each job **properly**. He believed a job done badly can\u2019t make you proud, whatever it\u2019s called.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Pride comes from **doing a job well**, not from **its name**. A careful farmer can be prouder than a careless poet. That\u2019s what Washington taught.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'At a party someone asks what you do. You say you\u2019re **an electrician**. You watch them file you below the lawyer beside you. They\u2019ve never seen **either of you** work. What are they really judging?'},{kind:'story',text:'At Tuskegee the students built the buildings, grew the food and made the bricks. Washington insisted they learn each trade properly. In his view, work done badly can\u2019t carry dignity, whatever attitude you bring. He distrusted schooling sought only for status.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Washington put dignity in **the doing**, not in **the category** a job belongs to.',left:{title:'From the title',items:['Comes with the job name','Survives poor work','Handed out by others']},right:{title:'From the doing',items:['Comes from how well','Earned again each time','Visible in the work']}},{kind:'explain',text:'There\u2019s **a tactical half** too. Skills the local economy needs give a community leverage no appeal can. Critics answered that praising manual work, in a society set on **confining Black Americans** to it, risked supplying the excuse.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Judge work by **how well it\u2019s done**, including your own. A new title can\u2019t add dignity to careless work. And a humble name **can\u2019t take it** from careful work.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'In 1895 Washington spoke to a crowd in Atlanta. A people won\u2019t get ahead, he argued, until it honours **good farm work** as highly as **writing poetry**. Why start there?'},{kind:'explain',text:'His answer: dignity is **in the doing**. Skilled work done properly carries it, whatever its standing. Work done badly can\u2019t, whatever its title. So the category of a job tells you **very little**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Washington had been shaped by work. As a boy, freed after 1865, he laboured at a salt furnace and in a coal mine. Later a broom got him into Hampton. At Tuskegee, students made the bricks and raised the buildings.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture two kitchens. In one, a head chef with a grand title sends out careless plates. In the other, a cook without one gets every dish right. By Washington\u2019s test, the dignity sits in the second kitchen.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. In a society set on confining Black Americans to manual trades, praising those trades **risked supplying the argument** for confinement. Du Bois insisted a people also needs **scholars and leaders**. Both positions can be defended.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Washington meant it as belief and as **a tactic**. Respect work for **how well it\u2019s done**, including yours. But notice when someone uses that idea to keep you in your place.'}]},'up-from-slavery_4':{title:'The Calm Public Voice',card:{insight:'People write to fit their reader, so a calm voice may be a choice.',tryThis:'Next time a story seems too calm, write down who it was written for.',skills:['communication','history']},review:{prompt:'A company\u2019s yearly report is cheerful but skips a big mess. What should you do?',right:'Ask who the report was written for and what they needed',wrong:'Trust it, since a cheerful report means a good year',why:'A report is made for its readers, so the calm tone may be a choice.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Imagine you need money from someone for your club. You write them a letter. Would you fill it with **complaints**, or keep it **warm and polite**? Now imagine your whole school depends on that letter.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Washington\u2019s book is **very calm**. He was born enslaved, yet he barely complains. But he wrote it mostly for **white readers**, and he needed their money for his school. So the calm voice was partly a careful choice.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When writing sounds odd, ask **who it was for**. The answer can change **what you read** in it. It\u2019s also a clue to what was left out.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Here\u2019s a book by a man born enslaved. It has almost **no bitterness** in it. Former owners get credit, and grievances are played down. What explains **a tone** like that?'},{kind:'story',text:'Up From Slavery first ran in a magazine, then appeared as a book in 1901. Its readers were mostly white: Northern donors, and Southern whites whose tolerance Tuskegee needed. Washington was among the most skilled fundraisers of his day.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Read it as **temperament**, or read it as **strategy**. You get two different books.',left:{title:'As temperament',items:['A gentle man writes gently','It seems sentimental','Its silences look innocent']},right:{title:'As strategy',items:['A careful man writes for funders','It becomes intelligible','Its silences become choices']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s **what was left out**. The book was written as lynching peaked and Southern states finished stripping Black men of the vote. Almost none of that appears. **The tone bought** the school, and it bent the record.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A calm voice can be **a policy**, not the whole person. When a tone puzzles you, ask **who it was for**. Then ask what that audience needed you not to hear.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Washington was the best-known Black American of his day. In his life story he refuses, as a matter of principle, to **hate anyone**. Is that a portrait of the man, or **a tool** he chose?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The book\u2019s conciliatory tone is **a decision**, not a disposition. Read as temperament, it seems sentimental. Read as strategy, it makes sense, because every paragraph is aimed at **readers who could withdraw** their support.'},{kind:'story',text:'It first ran in a magazine in instalments, then as a book in 1901. The readers were mostly Northern donors and Southern whites. The calm voice reassured them. Their money and tolerance kept Tuskegee open and growing.'},{kind:'story',text:'Then there\u2019s the second record. Historians later went through his private papers. They found he had **secretly funded** court challenges to segregation and to the loss of the vote. In public he said little. The voice was a policy, run beside private resistance.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. As testimony about the South around 1900, the book **misleads**. It was written as lynching peaked, and almost none of that appears. And what he wouldn\u2019t say in public, **the public never heard**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A tone tells you about **its audience** as much as its author. The calm voice bought a school and **bent the record**. Before you rely on a text, ask who it was for.'}]},'up-from-slavery_5':{title:'Working Inside the Walls',card:{insight:'Before you fight a limit, find out what it still lets you do.',tryThis:'Write one limit you cannot change this year. Then list what it blocks and what it still allows.',skills:['thinking','leadership']},review:{prompt:'A new manager gets no money to spend this year. What should she do first?',right:'List what the limit still lets her do, then use all of it',wrong:'Spend the year asking her boss for the money',why:'She cannot change the limit this year, so she starts with what is left.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re stuck indoors on a rainy day. You can\u2019t go to the park. You could **sulk at the window** all afternoon. Or you could look around and ask what **this room** still lets you do.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Washington faced **hard limits** on what he could say. In 1895 he told a story about sailors dying of thirst. Another ship told them to **lower their buckets** right where they were. They had drifted into a river\u2019s mouth, and the water was fresh.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t spend all your strength on **the walls**. First find out what the space inside still allows. Then **use all of it**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your new job comes with **a frozen budget** and a board that doubts you. You can\u2019t change either this year. Do you spend the year **fighting the freeze**, or find out what\u2019s still possible?'},{kind:'story',text:'In Atlanta in 1895, speaking to a largely white Southern crowd, Washington told of a ship lost at sea. Its crew was dying of thirst. A passing ship signalled them to lower their buckets where they were. They\u2019d drifted into a great river\u2019s mouth.'},{kind:'explain',text:'His point was **build here**, with what\u2019s at hand. Then came an image of fingers on one hand. Socially, the races could stay apart like those fingers, yet work together for progress. That gave the crowd **the reassurance** it wanted.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Was **the price** too high? Critics said that accepting separation in public helped segregation look acceptable. That\u2019s the core of the quarrel with **Du Bois**. Both sides have real answers. What isn\u2019t disputed is the skill.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You can\u2019t move every wall this year. So **map it exactly**: what it forbids, and what it still allows. Then take **the most** that space will give.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Imagine every move you make is watched by people who can **shut you down**. Some words are banned in some rooms. Some plans won\u2019t be funded. How do you get anything done **inside walls** like that?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Washington\u2019s answer was **precision, not defiance**. He worked out what would be funded, what tolerated, and what could be said in which room. Then he took **the maximum** from that space, rather than spending himself on the walls.'},{kind:'story',text:'The 1895 Atlanta address is the clearest case. He told a white audience the races could live apart **like fingers**, yet work together for progress. They got their reassurance. He got support for Black schooling and work.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a nurse on a ward with no spare staff and no new budget. She can\u2019t change either. So she lists **what\u2019s still hers**: the handover, the rota order, the supply cupboard. She makes each one work perfectly.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Working skilfully inside a limit can look like **endorsing it**. The year after Atlanta, the Supreme Court upheld segregation in the Plessy case. Critics said his public acceptance made segregation **easier to defend**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Be **exact about the limit**, then use everything inside it. But watch the cost: you can end up **lending it your name**. Know which you\u2019re doing.'}]},'up-from-slavery_6':{title:'The Broom Test',card:{insight:'The small first task you are given is often the real test.',tryThis:'Pick the smallest task someone gave you this week. Finish all of it, even the parts no one checks.',skills:['career','habits']},review:{prompt:'On your first day, you must sort a messy shared folder. How do you treat it?',right:'As the real test, so you finish even the parts no one checks',wrong:'As busywork to get through before the real job starts',why:'The first small task is often the test, since it shows how you work alone.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A boy travels about 500 miles, partly on foot, to reach a school. He has almost **no money**. The teacher isn\u2019t sure about him. Instead of a test paper, she hands him **a broom**. Why?'},{kind:'explain',text:'That boy was Washington, and the school was Hampton. She told him to **sweep a room**. He swept it three times and dusted it four. She wiped the woodwork with her handkerchief and found **no dust**. He was in.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You can\u2019t fake a clean room. So the broom showed how **careful** he was with nobody watching. When you\u2019re given a small job, treat it as **the real test**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'It\u2019s your first week at a new job. Your manager asks you to tidy **a shared drive** nobody\u2019s touched in years. Is that a chore, or **the real assessment**?'},{kind:'story',text:'In 1872 Washington reached Hampton Institute after about 500 miles, partly on foot, with almost no money. The head teacher hesitated, then told him to sweep the room next door. He swept it three times and dusted it four.'},{kind:'explain',text:'She ran **her handkerchief** over the woodwork and found nothing. He calls it his college examination. It was **unfakeable**: the only way to get a clean room is to clean it. And no one stood over him.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Most exams measure **preparation for the exam**. This one couldn\u2019t be studied for. It measured **thoroughness** with no supervision. The story also did a job: it flattered readers who prized character shown through work.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The small first task is usually **the real test**. It shows how you work when **nobody is checking**. So finish the parts that nobody will look at.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture two tests for a place at a school. One is a written paper you can **cram for**. The other is a broom and an empty room. Which one tells the teacher **more about you**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A test you can only pass **by doing the thing** measures something an exam can\u2019t. It can\u2019t be faked or studied for. It shows **the habit itself**, under no supervision. That\u2019s rarer than it sounds.'},{kind:'story',text:'Washington reached Hampton in 1872, worn and dirty from the road. He wasn\u2019t admitted at once. The head teacher sent him to sweep a recitation room. He swept it three times, dusted it four, and passed her handkerchief test.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a new volunteer at a food bank, asked to sort donated tins by date. She checks every label, even at the back of the shelf. A month later, she\u2019s the one **trusted with the stockroom**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Unfakeable tests aren\u2019t automatically fair. Who gets the broom and who gets the paper **is a choice**. Critics said the Hampton model steered Black students toward labour, not learning. And small tasks favour **people already trained**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Treat the first small job as **the assessment**, because it usually is. Do it as if someone will check. But don\u2019t assume **every assessor** is fair to you.'}]},'up-from-slavery_7':{title:'The Name at Roll Call',card:{insight:'A form that asks for what some people lack will leave them out.',tryThis:'Look at one form or sign-up list you run. Mark each box some people cannot fill.',skills:['thinking','history']},review:{prompt:'A bank needs three years of past addresses to open an account. What should it notice?',right:'Some people do not have what the form asks for',wrong:'Three years is a fair rule, since it is the same for all',why:'When a rule expects the same thing from all, some get left out.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'It\u2019s your first day at a new school. The teacher calls the register, and every child answers with **two names**. You\u2019ve only ever had **one**. What do you say when she reaches you?'},{kind:'explain',text:'That happened to Washington after slavery ended. The law hadn\u2019t recognised enslaved families, and he had **no surname**. So when the teacher asked, he chose one on the spot: **Booker Washington**. He kept it for life.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Some everyday rules **expect things** not everyone has. A name on a list can show what someone **lost**. Notice who your rules leave out.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A form asks for your previous addresses and **your parents\u2019 names**. For most people it takes ten minutes. For someone just out of care, **every box** asks about something they never had. Who designed it?'},{kind:'story',text:'Freed as a child, Booker started school in Malden, West Virginia. The other children had two names. He\u2019d only ever had one. Asked his full name, he answered Booker Washington, chosen on the spot. He later learned his mother had named him Taliaferro.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Why no surname? A surname records **a family line**. Slavery refused to recognise enslaved families. Their marriages had no legal standing, and children could be **sold apart** from parents. Washington writes that he never knew who his father was.'},{kind:'explain',text:'He tells it **without comment**, and that makes it land harder. A routine moment, a teacher writing down a name, shows what slavery took. Any process built on an assumption **tests for it**, meaning to or not.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Every process **assumes something** about the people arriving. When someone lacks it, the process quietly fails them. Look at **what yours assumes**, and who arrives without it.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A teacher writes down a child\u2019s name. It\u2019s the most **routine moment** in any school. So why would a book about slavery spend time on it? What could **one roll call** reveal?'},{kind:'story',text:'In a schoolroom in Malden, West Virginia, a boy called only Booker heard the others answer with two names. When the teacher reached him, he said Booker Washington. Newly free, he was supplying from nothing what the register assumed.'},{kind:'explain',text:'A surname is **a record of lineage**. American slavery gave enslaved families no standing in law. Marriages had no legal force, and families could be **divided by sale**. Washington tells the scene flatly. The flatness is the argument.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a college that requires a parent\u2019s signature on every scholarship form. It looks like routine paperwork. But a student without a parent in the picture **hits a wall** at once. You\u2019ve just found what the form tests for.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The scene is easily misread as cheerful self-invention. That reading **erases the loss** it records. And many enslaved families kept names among themselves that the law ignored. The point is what **the institution refused** to see.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A process that assumes everyone has something will **quietly fail** those who don\u2019t. Washington showed it with one name. Ask what your own processes take **for granted**.'}]},'up-from-slavery_8':{title:'The Fourth Kiln',card:{insight:'To change how people see you, make something they need.',tryThis:'List three things your group could make that the people near you need. Start the easiest one today.',skills:['leadership','career']},review:{prompt:'Store owners see the teen club on their street as a pest. What would help?',right:'Help the stores with something they need on busy days',wrong:'Give a strong speech about why the teen club is good',why:'Making things people need can change their minds more than speeches can.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your school needs new buildings, and there\u2019s no money. So you decide the students will **make the bricks**. The first kiln fails. So does the second, and **the third**. What would you do next?'},{kind:'explain',text:'That\u2019s what happened at Washington\u2019s school, Tuskegee. With no money left, he **pawned his watch** to pay for a fourth try. It worked. Later the school sold bricks to white neighbours, and **a new tie** grew between them.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Some things you only learn **by failing** at them first. And making something people need can **change their view** of you.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Shopkeepers in your area see the local youth group as **a nuisance**. The group could give speeches about how good it is. Or it could find something **the shops need**. Which would change minds faster?'},{kind:'story',text:'Tuskegee needed buildings, and Washington decided the students would make the bricks. The first three kilns failed. With no money left, he pawned his watch to fund a fourth. That one worked, and the brickyard became a lasting part of the school.'},{kind:'explain',text:'He keeps **the failures** in on purpose. A brick that holds depends on clay, heat and repetition. The students who finally made good ones knew something **no lecture** could give them. You learn it with your hands.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Then the yard **sold bricks** to the white community nearby. That created a trading tie where none had existed. Washington says it did more for relations in the county than **any speech** could. Need did the work argument couldn\u2019t.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Some skills only come **through failure**. And supplying **what others need** can change how you\u2019re seen. But goodwill in a county isn\u2019t equal rights in law.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Three kilns of bricks fail, one after another. The money is gone. Most people would **give up** or blame the clay. One man **pawns his watch** instead. What does he know that you might not?'},{kind:'story',text:'Tuskegee\u2019s buildings were raised by students, from bricks they made themselves. Washington funded the fourth kiln with his pawned watch, and it worked. The yard then sold bricks to the surrounding white community. A commercial tie now existed where none had.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The brickyard is **his whole method** in small. First, skill learned through failure: knowledge that lives in hands that have watched kilns fail. Then that skill turned into **a relationship**, because the neighbours needed what it made.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a small design team the sales staff ignore. The team stops pitching itself and builds the slide templates sales keep asking for. Within weeks, **sales are asking** for its advice. The need did the persuading.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Need is leverage only while it lasts and as far as it reaches. White neighbours could buy Tuskegee\u2019s bricks. They could still back **the 1901 constitution** that stripped Black Alabamians of the vote. Commerce changed a county, not **the law**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Supply something **people actually need**, and the need can do what argument can\u2019t. Just know its limit. Goodwill you\u2019ve earned won\u2019t protect **your rights in law**.'}]},'up-from-slavery_9':{title:'The Toothbrush Rule',card:{insight:'When big things are out of your hands, keep one small rule each day.',tryThis:'Choose one rule so small you cannot fail it, like making your bed. Keep it today.',skills:['habits','history']},review:{prompt:'After losing a job, you feel nothing is in your control. What helps first?',right:'Keep one small rule each day, no matter what',wrong:'Wait until you can make a big change again',why:'A small rule you can keep helps good habits grow.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Imagine starting at a school with **no money** and almost nothing of your own. You can\u2019t change much. But there\u2019s **one small thing** you can do perfectly every morning. What could it be?'},{kind:'explain',text:'At Washington\u2019s school, every student had to keep **a toothbrush** and use it. He wanted baths, clean shirts and tidy rooms too. His old teacher, General Armstrong, called this **the gospel** of the toothbrush.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A toothbrush was a rule **any student** could keep, even with no money. Keeping one small rule **builds a habit** of order. Then bigger habits can grow from it.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve just lost your job. The market, the replies and the timing are **out of your hands**. Nothing feels like yours to steer. Is there anything at all **you fully control**?'},{kind:'story',text:'At Tuskegee, Washington made every student keep a toothbrush and use it, along with baths and clean clothes. He called it the gospel of the toothbrush, crediting the phrase to Armstrong of Hampton. Cleanliness came first because a penniless student fully controlled it.'},{kind:'explain',text:'His reasoning was that **habits of order** travel. A student who keeps their person in order keeps their work in order. He credits the toothbrush with changing students\u2019 habits, and **through them** their families.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The same rule can be read **two ways**, and the text supports **both readings**.',left:{title:'Self-command',items:['A standard you fully control','Builds the habit of order','Dignity under constraint']},right:{title:'Acceptability',items:['Meeting others\u2019 idea of decent','Answering prejudice by conforming','The burden falls on the wronged']}},{kind:'moral',text:'When nothing big is in your hands, choose **one small standard** and hold it absolutely. That habit becomes **a base** for others. Just know whose standard you\u2019re keeping.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A political leader writes his life story. You\u2019d expect speeches, fights and laws. Instead he spends pages on **toothbrushes**, baths and clean shirts. Why would a man with **so much at stake** care about that?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Washington\u2019s reasoning was about **control**. A penniless student can\u2019t choose much. But a toothbrush, a bath and a clean shirt are **entirely theirs**, from the first day. And habits of order, he argued, **travel home** to families.'},{kind:'story',text:'At Tuskegee every student had to keep a toothbrush and use it. In that period, washing and sanitation marked **class and race**. Respectability was one of the few protections open to Black Americans. A clean shirt there was a standard held under pressure.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture someone after a long illness, when work, plans and energy are all uncertain. They make the bed every morning, without fail. It\u2019s tiny. But **it\u2019s theirs**, and after a month they add a short walk.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Respectability protected less than it promised. Clean, educated, property-owning Black Americans were still lynched and still lost the vote. And the rule can read as **teaching the wronged** to please those who wronged them.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Holding one small standard can **steady you** when nothing else is yours. But ask whether you keep it **for yourself** or for someone watching. Washington\u2019s text supports both answers.'}]}}}},1018,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'walden',kind:'book',promise:'Want less and get your time back',about:'What if the things you buy cost you hours of your life? In the 1840s, Henry David Thoreau built a cabin by a pond. He wrote down every cost, down to half a cent. Here is what he learned. Want to try it?',ideas:{walden_1:{title:'The Price in Hours',card:{insight:'Each thing you buy costs hours of your life, not just dollars.',tryThis:'Next time you want a thing, divide its price by what you make an hour. Is it worth those hours?',skills:['money','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'A couch costs $900, and you make $15 an hour. What do you ask?',right:'Is this couch worth sixty hours of my life?',wrong:'Is it cheaper than other couches at the store?',why:'A price tag shows dollars, but you pay for it in hours of your life.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A new game costs **\xa330**. You earn \xa35 for every car you wash, and each car takes an hour. So what does that game **really cost** you?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Six cars means **six hours** of your Saturdays. That\u2019s the real price. In 1845 a writer called Henry David Thoreau moved into a **small hut** by a pond. He priced everything he needed in time, not money.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Every price tag hides a **number of hours**. Turn the money into time and you\u2019ll see **the real trade**. Some things are worth it, and that\u2019s fine.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A flat with a second bedroom costs **\xa3300 more** a month. You take home \xa320 an hour. It looks like a small step up from the rent. Is it really **that small**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Thoreau built a one-room house by Walden Pond, near Concord, Massachusetts. He kept his accounts **to the cent**. About six weeks of paid work a year covered his needs. The rest of the year, by his reckoning, was his own.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Now convert the flat. \xa3300 divided by \xa320 is **fifteen hours** a month. Over a year, that\u2019s close to **a working month**. The bedroom isn\u2019t competing with other flats any more. It\u2019s competing with a month of your life.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The price tag only compares money with money. Count it in **hours of your life** instead. Sometimes you\u2019ll still say yes, but you\u2019ll be answering **the real question**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Walden was published in 1854. You might expect it to open on the pond. Instead its first long chapter is called **Economy**, and it\u2019s full of **sums**. Why would a book about the woods start with accounts?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Thoreau\u2019s rule is simple. Price anything by the hours of your life it takes, whether you pay them now or later. Money is only **the go-between**. So every purchase is **a trade**, and you should judge it as one.'},{kind:'story',text:'At the pond, from 1845 to 1847, he lived on very little. He found that about six weeks of labour a year paid for everything. He treated the rest as his own. The number fitted one man with no family.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take someone who picks a cheaper flat so she can work four days a week. The smaller rent buys back a day of her life, every week. She hasn\u2019t spent less for its own sake. She\u2019s bought time.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The rule assumes you can trade hours at the margin. Many people can\u2019t work less, so a cheaper choice never gives them time back. And **not every hour** is loss: work you enjoy isn\u2019t pure cost.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Every price is paid in **your hours**. The rule doesn\u2019t forbid spending. It makes **the trade visible**, so whatever you buy, you\u2019ve chosen it with open eyes.'}]},walden_2:{title:'Stuff in the Way',card:{insight:'Some nice things need so much care that they eat your free time.',tryThis:'Pick the most costly thing you own. Add up the hours of care it takes each year.',skills:['money','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'You bought a camera for your walks. Now you spend them cleaning and fixing it. Why?',right:'The care it needs is eating your free time',wrong:'You just need to get faster at fixing it',why:'A thing gets in your way when its care eats the time it was for.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You wanted a **big fish tank** so you could watch the fish. Now it needs feeding every day and cleaning every week. Where are your **Saturday mornings** going?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Thoreau had a word for this: a **hindrance**. That means something in your way. Nice things need **upkeep**, or looking after, and that time comes out of the fun they were meant to bring. The tank eats your watching time.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Thoreau wasn\u2019t against **nice things**. He knew people need warmth and food. But after a point, each new thing **costs more time** than it gives back.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You move to a **bigger home** so you\u2019ll finally have room for your own projects. A year on, you\u2019re working longer hours. The projects haven\u2019t started. So what did the **extra room** buy?'},{kind:'story',text:'Thoreau looked at the people he admired most: the ancient philosophers of China, India, Persia and Greece. By choice, they were poorer in outward things than anyone. If comfort made clear minds, he argued, the pattern would run the other way.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Thoreau chose his word on purpose. Most luxuries aren\u2019t **indulgences**, he said, but **hindrances**.',left:{title:'An indulgence',items:['A harmless extra','Costs you once','Enjoy it or ignore it']},right:{title:'A hindrance',items:['Something in the way','Costs you every week','Demands care, used or not']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s where the time goes. Every comfort has **upkeep**: it must be paid for, stored, insured, repaired and replaced. Those hours come from whatever it was meant to **free you** for. So the bigger home ate your evenings.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t just ask what a thing costs to buy. Ask what its **upkeep** will take from you. Past some point, comforts get in the way of **the life** they promised.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Each thing you buy is meant to make life easier: a bigger house, a better car, a boat. So why do so many people end up with **more comfort** and **less time**? Thoreau thought he knew.'},{kind:'explain',text:'His answer: most luxuries aren\u2019t harmless extras but **positive hindrances**. The reason is **upkeep**. Each comfort must be paid for, stored, insured, mended and replaced. Past a **threshold**, each addition costs more attention than it returns.'},{kind:'story',text:'His evidence was the ancient philosophers of China, India, Persia and Greece. They chose to be poorer in outward things than the poor of his own town. Yet they were the minds he admired above all.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a boat bought for relaxing weekends. It needs paying for, storing and mending. Before long you\u2019re spending most Saturdays fixing it. The boat hasn\u2019t given you weekends. It\u2019s taken them.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The argument is easier for someone without children. A family car can be exactly what makes **a shared life** work. And his evidence is selective: a few admired thinkers prove simple living is possible, not that comfort causes muddle.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Thoreau isn\u2019t against warmth, shelter or food. His test is whether **your comfort\u2019s upkeep** eats the thing it was bought for. Most of us never notice **the threshold**.'}]},walden_3:{title:'Clear Out for a Reason',card:{insight:'Clear things out for a reason, or the clutter will come back.',tryThis:'Before you clean today, write down what the free space or time is for.',skills:['focus','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'Leo quits two clubs for free time. A month later, he is just as busy. Why?',right:'He never said what the free time was for',wrong:'He did not quit enough clubs to make real space',why:'Free time with no plan fills up again, so first decide what it is for.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'On Saturday you tidy your **whole room**. It looks great. Two weeks later, it\u2019s a mess again, just like before. Nothing else in your days changed. So **why did the mess** come back?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Thoreau didn\u2019t think having less was good on its own. He cleared things away so he had **time to think**, read and write. He said a life should hold **two or three** main things, not a hundred.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Before you clear things out, decide what **the space is for**. Clear with no reason and it soon **fills up again**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You clear the whole flat: bags to the charity shop, **bare shelves**, a weekend of work. It looks wonderful. Four months later it\u2019s **exactly as it was**. Where did it all come from?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Thoreau\u2019s famous line repeats one word three times: simplicity. People rarely quote **the next part**. It asks you to cut your affairs down to two or three, not hundreds. That\u2019s **a number**, and a small one.'},{kind:'explain',text:'His diagnosis is that life gets **frittered away** by detail. Not ruined, but wasted in pieces too small to notice. So more effort across a hundred affairs gives you **a hundred tidier affairs**, and the same fritter.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Your flat refilled because you cleared **the shelves** but kept **every commitment**.',left:{title:'A mood',items:['Clear the shelves','Keep every commitment','A tidier drift']},right:{title:'A purpose',items:['Name what it\u2019s for','Cut to two or three','Time goes there']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Simplicity is **a tool**, not a virtue. Name what the freed time is for, then cut your affairs to **two or three**. Otherwise the clutter simply returns.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture a team with **forty open projects**. To cope, they buy a new tool that tracks every one of them. Everyone feels more organised. What would Thoreau say about **that purchase**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'In the chapter on where he lived and what he lived for, Thoreau repeats **simplicity** three times. The practical half follows: cut your affairs down to two or three. The **count itself** is the problem.'},{kind:'story',text:'The team\u2019s tool tends the forty. It doesn\u2019t ask why there are forty. More effort across that many projects gives forty better-kept projects and the same fritter. Thoreau\u2019s cure is **subtraction**, not management.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a friend who drops three standing commitments to simplify. For a fortnight her evenings feel huge. Then new ones creep in, because she never named a use for them. Thoreau\u2019s test: could she state her affairs **on a thumbnail**?'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Not every life can shrink to two or three affairs. Caring for children or parents comes as a hundred small duties you **can\u2019t cut**. And simplicity can turn into a project of its own, with gear and rules.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Reducing your affairs only matters because it **frees attention** for something you chose. So don\u2019t manage the forty. **Cut the count**, then give the time to what it was for.'}]},walden_4:{title:'Look Closely on Purpose',card:{insight:'Looking hard is a skill that grows when you use it on plain things.',tryThis:'Spend three minutes looking hard at one plain thing nearby. Then write down one new thing you see.',skills:['focus','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'You have walked the same street to work for years. How do you practice looking?',right:'Pick one plain thing on it and look closely on purpose',wrong:'Wait for something on it to catch your eye',why:'You get better at looking when you choose what to look at.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You walk the same way to school **every day**. Hundreds of times. But what\u2019s in the third garden on the left? Can you **picture it** right now?'},{kind:'story',text:'Thoreau lived by a pond for two years and watched it in every season. People said the pond had **no bottom**. So he cut holes in the ice and measured it. It did have one.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Looking closely is **a skill**, like a muscle. Most people only look at things that shout. You can choose **plain things** and look on purpose.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve walked the same route to work **hundreds of times**. Without looking, how much of its left-hand side could you describe? Most people manage **almost nothing**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Locals said Walden Pond had no bottom. Thoreau didn\u2019t argue. He cut holes in the winter ice and let down a weighted line. It was a little over **a hundred feet** at its deepest. Much of the book is like that: a pond, watched closely.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Why bother? Thoreau called his aim **living deliberately**, which meant choosing where his attention went. He didn\u2019t want to reach the end and find he\u2019d never really been there. The fear isn\u2019t death. It\u2019s being busy for years and **never present**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Attention gets **pulled** by alarms and screens, or **placed** on purpose by you.',left:{title:'Already running',items:['Started before you chose','Grabbed by whatever\u2019s loudest','Busy all day']},right:{title:'Deliberate',items:['Chosen right now','Aimed at what you picked','Present for some of it']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Attention is **a skill** that grows with use. Most of yours goes wherever it\u2019s pulled, so choose one ordinary thing and **look closely**, on purpose.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'On 4 July 1845, Thoreau moved into his cabin at Walden Pond. He stayed **two years**, two months and two days. Much of what he wrote there is about ice, birds and depth. Why spend **so long** watching a pond?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Thoreau named his aim **living deliberately**. It wasn\u2019t a feeling of calm. It was a decision about **where attention goes**. What scared him was reaching the end of his life busy the whole way, but **never there**.'},{kind:'story',text:'So he practised on plain things. Locals said the pond had no bottom, so he sounded it and mapped it. He watched its ice, its birds and its visitors. Nothing there demanded his attention. He **chose** to give it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a commuter with no cabin. She picks one tree on her route and studies it daily for a month. She notes the bark, the birds, the changes in weather. Her commute is the same. One part of it is now chosen.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. This assumes you have **spare attention**. A carer or a shift worker short of sleep may have none to place. And close watching can turn into hiding, studying a pond instead of the people who need you.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You don\u2019t need a cabin. Decide where **your attention** goes, and give some of it to **plain things**. The pond was his setting, not his method.'}]},walden_5:{title:'Living Close to Town',card:{insight:'Walden was a test of living on less, run close to town.',tryThis:'For one week, buy only what you need. Then write down what you did not miss.',skills:['thinking','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'A friend says no one can live in the woods. So is Walden no use?',right:'It was a test of living on less, close to town',wrong:'True, most people could not live alone in the woods',why:'Thoreau lived close to town, so you can try his test where you live.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture Thoreau\u2019s hut. Do you see a **wild forest**, miles from anyone, with a lonely man inside? That\u2019s the picture most people have. But look at the path to his door. Whose **footprints** are those?'},{kind:'explain',text:'His hut stood about a mile and a half from town. He walked in **most days**, and friends visited often. He wasn\u2019t surviving in the wild. He was testing **how little** he needed to live.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Thoreau changed **one thing**: how much he spent. He kept his town and friends close. So you could try **a small piece** of it too.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A cabin in the woods, as promised. But **the village** is in plain sight, the path is covered in footprints, and three chairs wait by the door. Is this really **a hermit\u2019s home**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Walden Pond was about a mile and a half from Concord. Thoreau walked into town most days and had a steady stream of visitors. His family is often said to have done some of his washing. The land belonged to his friend Emerson.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Which version **you believe** decides whether the book is any **use to you**.',left:{title:'The myth',items:['A hermit in the wilderness','Survival against nature','Out of reach']},right:{title:'The arrangement',items:['A cabin near town','An experiment in spending less','Repeatable on a small scale']}},{kind:'explain',text:'The hermit version lets you wave the book away: *I could never live in the woods.* The real version makes **a precise claim**. Want less, and you can buy back much of your time. You can test that **almost anywhere**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Walden wasn\u2019t a survival story. It was **an experiment** in spending less, run close to town, so **you can repeat** it in a small way.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Ask people to picture Walden and they\u2019ll describe **a lonely man** deep in the wild, living off the land. It\u2019s a romantic picture. But what if the real story is **more useful** than the legend?'},{kind:'story',text:'In 1846, on one of his ordinary walks into Concord, Thoreau was arrested for refusing to pay a tax. He spent the night in the town jail. No hermit deep in the wilds gets picked up on **a stroll to town**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'He **wasn\u2019t a hermit**. The book itself is open about this. It has whole chapters on his **visitors** and on the village, and he walked into town most days. The hermit is the legend, not the text.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a founder whose story says she built her company **alone in a garage**. You hear it and think you could never manage that. But what support and money did **the arrangement** really have? The honest version is usually smaller.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Critics who mock him for having help from home overcorrect, since he never claimed to be cut off. But his test did rest on lent land, no dependants and family nearby. **Repeatable** isn\u2019t the same as available to all.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Read as a feat, Walden only needs **admiring**. Read as an experiment, it can be **tested**, by you, in a small way. Check the arrangement before you measure yourself against the myth.'}]},walden_6:{title:'The Half-Cent List',card:{insight:'A claim about what life costs needs a list, or it is just a feeling.',tryThis:'Write down each thing you bought yesterday and what it cost. Then read the list back.',skills:['money','thinking']},review:{prompt:'You want your partner to agree to cancel some apps you pay for. What first?',right:'List each app with what it costs a month, then add them up',wrong:'Tell them that a simpler life tends to be happier',why:'You can check real numbers, so they are hard to argue with.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'When Thoreau built his hut by the pond, he wrote down **every single cost**: boards, nails, hinges, chalk and bricks. He even kept track of **half a cent**. Why would anyone bother?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The total came to twenty-eight dollars and twelve and a half cents. That\u2019s **a list**, not a feeling. Anyone can **check it**. If you only say *it didn\u2019t cost much*, nobody can check anything.'},{kind:'moral',text:'If you say something costs little, **show the list**. A claim with no numbers is only **a feeling**. Real numbers can be checked.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two friends both say a household could live on less. One makes the case with **big principles**. The other brings **a list** of every cost. Which one stands a chance of changing your mind?'},{kind:'story',text:'Thoreau didn\u2019t just say a person could live on very little. He listed the house: boards, nails, lime, hinges, chalk and transport. The total was twenty-eight dollars and twelve and a half cents. Then he listed his food, item by item, for months.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Why keep the half-cent? A **specific number** invites attack, and either survives it or doesn\u2019t. A vague claim about simplicity can\u2019t be attacked, so it **convinces nobody** new. People have argued over Thoreau\u2019s figures ever since. They can.'},{kind:'story',text:'At home, saying *we spend too much* starts a fight about values. Bring three months of statements, totalled by category, and you\u2019re arguing about \xa3140 a month on takeaways. That argument can actually be settled.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A principle sounds wise but can\u2019t be checked. **A receipt** can be argued with, and that\u2019s its strength. Before you make the case for spending less, **list the numbers**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Most books praising simple living offer **a principle**. Walden\u2019s first chapter offers something stranger: **a ledger**. The cost of the house, line by line, down to the half-cent. Why would a philosopher show you his sums?'},{kind:'story',text:'The house account lists boards, shingles, laths and two second-hand windows. Then a thousand old bricks, lime, hair, nails, hinges, a latch, chalk, transport. The total is twenty-eight dollars and twelve and a half cents. That bought a plastered room about ten feet by fifteen.'},{kind:'explain',text:'An argument about what a life costs is only as strong as **its accounts**. Itemised, it can be attacked and can survive. Left as a general claim, it can\u2019t be refuted, so it **persuades nobody** who wasn\u2019t already persuaded.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture two founders asking investors for money. One says *we\u2019re growing fast*. The other brings the monthly numbers, line by line. The first claim can only be believed or doubted. The second can be **checked**, and so it can persuade.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. A list proves what was spent, not what was **needed**. Thoreau\u2019s land was lent and his axe borrowed, and neither is on his list. A precise list can lend false authority to an incomplete one.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Give your case **real numbers**, so it can be checked and can survive. Then ask what **your list leaves out**. Some costs, like care and risk, never reach a receipt.'}]},walden_7:{title:'Say It in the Room',card:{insight:'If all of you think it but no one says it, it stays the same.',tryThis:'Pick one thing your group agrees on but never says. Write one plain line you could say out loud.',skills:['communication','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'Everyone on your team thinks the due date is too soon. What do you do?',right:'Say it plainly at the next team meeting, once',wrong:'Work late and hope the due date gets moved',why:'Silence looks like you agree, so a shared view has to be said out loud.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your school brings in a **new lunch rule**. Everyone thinks it\u2019s silly, and at the table they all say so. Then a teacher asks how it\u2019s going. **Nobody says** a word. What happens next?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The rule stays. The teacher heard **silence**, and from the outside, silence looks just like **agreeing**. Thoreau wrote about people living with quiet unhappiness. One way to read it: they felt it, but hid it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When everyone thinks the same thing but keeps quiet, **nothing changes**. If you say it out loud, once, calmly, **you can change** it.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Over coffee, everyone on the team agrees **the new process** is worse. In **the review meeting**, someone asks how it\u2019s going. A pause. *Fine,* says someone. Everyone nods. What just happened?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Thoreau thought most people live with a **quiet unhappiness**. On one reading, the working word is *quiet*. His complaint wasn\u2019t that people held wrong views. It was that they **hid them**, and acted as if they didn\u2019t.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s how it keeps going. Everyone privately disagrees. Nobody says so in the meeting. Silence reads as **agreement**, so the process continues every week, with **your silent vote** counted in its favour.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Saying it costs **less than you fear**. You\u2019re not bringing a new view into the room. You\u2019re making public one **the room already holds**. The people who might object are the people who agree.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Private agreement changes nothing while **the room** stays quiet. So in the next meeting, say it **plainly, once**, without softening it.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Thoreau has a famous line about people living in a quiet kind of despair. It\u2019s usually read as **a lament** about unhappiness. But what if the word **doing the work** is *quiet*?'},{kind:'explain',text:'On this reading, Thoreau is pointing at **concealment**. People privately know their arrangement is wrong, and act in public as if it weren\u2019t. The arrangement survives because **nobody says it**, and silence can\u2019t be told apart from agreement.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a team that privately agrees the new process is worse. Each person holds that view, but none can be sure the others do. Then one says it in the meeting, once. Now everyone knows that **everyone knows**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Thoreau\u2019s own remedy was drastic. He rearranged his whole life around his doubts, then published his reasons, at length. Few people can do that. The smaller version is open to you: state plainly, once, what the room already believes.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. This reading leans hard on one word of a line that also speaks of real despair. And speaking up **isn\u2019t always cheap**. If dissent is punished, or you\u2019ve misjudged the room, the cost falls on you.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your silence isn\u2019t **neutral**. It counts as a vote. But check that the private agreement is **real** before you speak for the room.'}]},walden_8:{title:'Morning at Any Hour',card:{insight:'Morning is when you feel fresh inside, at any hour of the day.',tryThis:'Tonight, pick one job to do first, before you check your phone.',skills:['focus','productivity']},review:{prompt:'A student has a free hour before class. Her phone is full of alerts. What first?',right:'Her own work, before she opens any message',wrong:'A quick look at her alerts, just to check',why:'Texts bring in other people\u2019s plans, so do your own work first.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You wake up on Saturday with **a great idea** for a story. There are also **new messages** on your phone. Which do you pick up first?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The messages pull you into other people\u2019s plans. By the time you\u2019re done, the idea feels far away. Thoreau said **morning isn\u2019t a time** on the clock. It\u2019s the **fresh feeling** before other people\u2019s stuff gets in.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Morning is **a fresh state**, not an hour. You can feel it at four in the afternoon. Keep it by doing **your own thing** first.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You have **forty minutes** before anyone needs you. You open your messages, just to check. Half an hour later the block is technically still there. So why does it feel **worth so little**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Thoreau said morning isn\u2019t an hour but **a condition**: not yet having picked up yesterday. Checking messages imports yesterday **all at once**. The time is still there, but now you\u2019re responding instead of thinking.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a night-shift nurse who writes for an hour before she checks her phone. Her neighbour gets up at six and clears his inbox first. Only one of them kept Thoreau\u2019s morning, and it isn\u2019t the early riser.'},{kind:'explain',text:'This isn\u2019t advice to **get up earlier**. Thoreau loved rising early, but the clock isn\u2019t his point. He said few people are awake enough for real thinking, and the clock **doesn\u2019t decide** who.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Morning ends when **the world\u2019s business** comes in. So keep the order: do **your own work** before opening any message. The hour doesn\u2019t matter.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A writer starts work at two in the afternoon, after **a slow lunch**. Her phone stays off until four. Is she working **in the morning**? By the clock, clearly not. But what would Thoreau say?'},{kind:'explain',text:'For Thoreau, morning is a condition: being awake and **not yet resumed** yesterday. It\u2019s morning whenever you\u2019re **fresh inside**, whatever the clock says. It ends when you re-enter the world\u2019s business.'},{kind:'story',text:'So the writer at two is in morning, if nothing of the day\u2019s business has reached her. Compare a manager up at six who answers every message first. He\u2019s up early, but **already resumed**. His morning ended at six.'},{kind:'story',text:'Or take an analyst who means to plan her week first thing. She opens email *for one minute*. An hour later she\u2019s answered everyone and planned nothing. Nobody interrupted her. Her own first click ended **the morning**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Thoreau backed this with a bold claim. Millions are awake enough for **physical work**, he said. Only a tiny few are awake enough for **real thinking**. Being awake was the scarce thing, and **the clock** didn\u2019t hand it out.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Many people don\u2019t control their first hour. Parents, carers and shift workers often start the day already resumed, and calling that **a failure** is unfair. And some thinking is better after contact, sparked by someone else\u2019s problem.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Morning is **a condition**, not a time. If you have the choice, put **whatever needs you fresh** before anyone else\u2019s agenda.'}]},walden_9:{title:'The Different Drummer',card:{insight:'Slow can mean stuck, or it can mean a different plan.',tryThis:'Pick someone you have judged as behind. Write down what they might be aiming for instead.',skills:['thinking','relationships']},review:{prompt:'A friend has not settled down at forty, unlike the rest. What should you do?',right:'Ask what they are aiming for before you judge',wrong:'Guess they are stuck and give them some advice',why:'Pace alone cannot tell you what someone wants, so ask first.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your friend is always **the last to finish** the class reading. Is she slow? Is she not trying? Or is something else going on that **you can\u2019t see**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Sam never joins the football at break. Some kids say he\u2019s no good at it. But Sam is learning chess, and he plays every break with a friend. He isn\u2019t behind. He\u2019s busy with something else.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Thoreau said someone out of step might hear **a different drummer**, meaning their own beat. From outside, behind and different **look the same**. So ask before you judge.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A colleague is **a year behind** their peers on promotion. You probably have a theory about why. But what does that fact alone **actually tell you**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'At the end of Walden, Thoreau says a person out of step may be hearing **a different drummer**. It\u2019s usually read as **permission to be odd**. On our reading, it makes a sharper point about **measuring people**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The colleague\u2019s slowness fits **both stories**, so the facts can\u2019t pick **between them**.',left:{title:'Falling behind',items:['Slower than peers','Same goal, less progress']},right:{title:'Another pace',items:['Slower than peers','Different goal, steady progress']}},{kind:'explain',text:'So where does **your confidence** come from? Not from the facts, since they fit both. It comes from **your assumptions**: what the work is for, and which race you think everyone\u2019s in. That\u2019s worth checking before drawing a conclusion.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Behind and differently paced **look identical** from outside. Before you judge, ask what someone\u2019s **aiming at**. Then ask yourself the same thing.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Near the end of Walden, Thoreau pictures someone out of step. They may be hearing **a different drummer**, music others can\u2019t hear. It\u2019s usually read as a licence to be **eccentric**. Is that all it says?'},{kind:'explain',text:'On our reading, it describes **a measurement problem**. Falling behind and keeping another pace produce **the same data**. So if you feel sure which one you\u2019re seeing, that certainty came from somewhere other than the data.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take a colleague a year behind on promotion. She might be struggling. She might be choosing depth over speed, or carrying obligations you can\u2019t see. From your desk, all three look exactly alike.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now turn it on yourself. You might judge yourself behind in a race **you never chose**. Or you might excuse real slipping as a different drum. Either way, ask: what am I keeping pace with, and is it what I meant?'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Sometimes the usual reading is right, and the person **really is behind**. Calling it a different drum can be kind flattery that withholds help. The idea pauses judgement. It doesn\u2019t forbid it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Pace alone can\u2019t tell you **which race** someone is running. So ask what sets the pace, for them **and for you**, before you decide.'}]},walden_10:{title:'The Old Clothes Test',card:{insight:'If a new plan needs new stuff before it can start, watch out.',tryThis:'Pick one thing you keep meaning to start. Do three minutes of it now, with what you own.',skills:['habits','productivity']},review:{prompt:'You want to start a podcast. Which first step passes the old clothes test?',right:'Start today with what you own, and record a short one',wrong:'Buy a good mic first so the sound is right',why:'Buying gear can feel like starting, but only doing the thing counts.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You decide to start running. First you buy **new trainers**, then a water bottle, then a cool watch. Three weeks later you\u2019ve got it all. So how many runs have you **actually done**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Thoreau warned about plans that begin with **new clothes**, or new kit of any kind. If it\u2019s real, you can start it **as you are**. Getting ready feels like starting, but it isn\u2019t. The kit can come later, if you still want it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'If a plan needs new stuff before you start, **be careful**. Try it first **with what you\u2019ve got**, even your old trainers.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'For three weeks you\u2019ve been about to start **a side project**. So far you have a domain name and two fonts. It feels like progress. But have you **actually started**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'In his first chapter, Thoreau warns against plans that start with **a shopping list**. His image is new clothes. Begin **in what you have**, he says, then ask what **the outfit** is doing for you.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Buying the kit, the software, the domain name: each is **a real action**, and each feels like starting. None of them is the work. A purchase is also the one step that **never risks** finding out you\u2019re bad at it.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Thoreau adds that outward change can\u2019t lead. New clothes won\u2019t fit **an unchanged person**. The change happens in you first. Then the outward things follow, or turn out **not to be needed**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Preparation is the comfiest way to **avoid starting**. So do **the smallest real version** today, with what you own, and buy nothing.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Someone means to **write a novel**. They buy writing software, a desk and a course, and set up a website in their name. Months pass. Each purchase made sense. So why is there still **no chapter**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Thoreau\u2019s image is new clothes, and his test is about **the costume**. Be wary of any venture whose first step is **buying the outfit**. If it needs you to look different before it begins, **the looking** may be most of what\u2019s on offer.'},{kind:'story',text:'Back to the novelist. Every purchase was defensible, and each one felt like a start. But the test asks a single question: could they have written a chapter **in a notebook** on the first day? They could have.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a would-be painter who owns every brush but hasn\u2019t painted. Compare a runner who ran for a year, then bought better shoes. Both bought kit. Only the painter\u2019s shopping has **replaced** the painting.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some work does need equipment first: a pilot needs an aircraft, a lab needs instruments. And for some people, new kit is an **honest signal** of commitment. The test asks about avoidance. It doesn\u2019t ban preparing.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The order matters. **Change first**, then the outward things follow or turn out unneeded. If you could start today **with what you own**, ask whether the shopping was the whole enterprise.'}]},walden_11:{title:'The Tools of Their Tools',card:{insight:'Each tool brings jobs that keep going, until the tool runs your week.',tryThis:'Pick one app you keep up. If it were gone tonight, would you set it up again, or feel relieved?',skills:['philosophy','productivity']},review:{prompt:'A team\u2019s new report system now eats most of every Friday. What is the test?',right:'Ask if they would set it up again, or feel relieved to lose it',wrong:'Ask if it is the best report tool they could buy',why:'Ask if the tool still gives you more than the work it takes.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You plant **a few tomatoes** so you\u2019ll have a summer snack. They grow well, so you plant more. Now you water them every evening and weed every weekend. Who\u2019s **in charge** here?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Thoreau said people become **tools of their tools**. You get a thing to help you. Then it needs **looking after**, and those jobs never stop. You can\u2019t even go away, because the plants now decide your summer.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Each step was **a good idea**. But every tool brings jobs, and the jobs **keep going**. That\u2019s how your stuff ends up bossing you.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A team adopts **a reporting system** to save time. It works. Before long, most of every Friday goes to keeping it running. **Nobody chose** that. So how did it happen?'},{kind:'story',text:'The system needed someone to keep the data clean. Then someone to run the reports, and someone to train new staff. Each job made sense. Now the tool meant to **free time** eats most of a Friday, every week.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Thoreau saw the same chain. The man who picked fruit when hungry **becomes a farmer**. The man who sheltered under a tree ends up **keeping a house**. Each step was better. Now the farm must be worked, hungry or not.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'You make **the choice** once, but you pay **the service** every single week.',left:{title:'The choice',items:['Made once','Made for a reason','Easy to see']},right:{title:'The service',items:['Paid every week','Outlasts its reason','Never really chosen']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Every tool arrives with **upkeep** that outlives the reason you chose it. So here\u2019s the test: if it vanished tonight, would you **rebuild it**, or feel relieved?'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'In his first chapter, Thoreau traces a chain. A hungry man picks fruit, then plants a field, then works it every day. In the end he works it **hungry or not**. When did **the tool** take charge?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Thoreau\u2019s phrase for this: **tools of their tools**. Each sensible choice brings obligations that **outlive the reason** for it. Nobody ever chooses to serve. They choose the tool, and the service comes bundled.'},{kind:'story',text:'A system adopted to save time now needs an administrator. Nobody decided to hire a servant for a tool. Each step was justified, so there was **no single moment** to object. Now the system sets the schedule.'},{kind:'story',text:'Or take an audience built to support someone\u2019s work. At first it helps. Over time it starts to dictate **what the work** can be. Neither the system nor the audience was a mistake when it was chosen.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some service to tools is the price of **things worth having**: farming fed people that gathering couldn\u2019t. Most of us rely on shared tools we can\u2019t drop alone. The test can show you an inversion. It can\u2019t always free you.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Nothing was stolen, and every step was **an improvement**. Yet at the end, the tool **sets the schedule**. Ask whether you\u2019d still choose the bargain, knowing its upkeep.'}]},walden_12:{title:'Rich in Leaving Things Alone',card:{insight:'You are rich in the things you can walk past and not miss.',tryThis:'List what you keep up with each week. Mark one you could drop tomorrow and not miss.',skills:['money','philosophy']},review:{prompt:'You are offered a fun extra job that pays well. What does Thoreau ask?',right:'Could you walk past it, and what would it take from you?',wrong:'Does it pay enough to be worth the extra hours?',why:'Being rich means you are free to say no, not only how much you earn.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Ali has lots of games but feels he must play every new one. Bea has a few and **happily skips** the new ones. Who do you think is **richer**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Thoreau would pick Bea. Every game Ali feels he must keep up with **pulls at him**. It\u2019s taking his time and worry, every day. Bea\u2019s time is **still her own**, because she can leave things alone.'},{kind:'moral',text:'For Thoreau, being rich means you can **leave things alone**. The fewer things you feel you must have, the **freer you are**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two people have **the same savings**. One runs four side projects, two subscriptions and a long feud. The other runs none of these. Are they **equally rich**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'By Thoreau\u2019s measure, no. For him, wealth isn\u2019t what you hold. It\u2019s what you can **walk past** without it costing you. The one with no extra claims is **richer**, though the bank can\u2019t see it.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Every held thing is **a standing claim** on your attention: a subscription, a side project, an argument you haven\u2019t dropped. You pay it all the time, whether or not it gives you anything. **Count the claims**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Thoreau caught this in himself. He spent pages imagining buying farms around Concord, walking them and pricing them in his head. The farms he couldn\u2019t let alone were the ones he\u2019d already started owning in his imagination.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Wanting is where **the claim** begins, before anything is bought. So your real wealth is the list you could **drop tomorrow** without flinching.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'In the chapter on where he lived, Thoreau describes imagining farms all around Concord. He **comes close** to buying one before the deal falls through. Then he turns them all down. What did he **see**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'His conclusion turns wealth around. Net worth counts what you hold. Thoreau counts **what holds you**: every possession, project or grudge that claims your attention. The fewer of those you carry, the **richer you are**.'},{kind:'story',text:'He walked those farms in his head and priced them, field by field. Nothing had been bought, yet **the claim** had already started. The daydreams were already spending his attention. Wanting, not buying, was where the cost began.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take someone with a side project that once excited her. It\u2019s been months since it gave her anything. It still claims an evening a week, and her thoughts on the bus. Letting it go would make her richer.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The definition flatters people who already have enough. If you\u2019re poor, you **can\u2019t walk past** rent or food. And some claims, like children or friends, are what a life is for, not a cost to it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your wealth is the set of things **you can let alone**. Owning little isn\u2019t the same as being free of **their claims**. And the claim starts the moment you begin to want.'}]}}}},1019,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'walking-as-thinking',kind:'book',promise:'Get better ideas from a plain walk',about:'Why do good ideas so often come when you are away from your desk? A walk helps some kinds of thinking, but not all. These five ideas show which jobs a walk helps, and how to use it. Where will you walk today?',ideas:{'walking-as-thinking_1':{title:'The Blank Wall',card:{insight:'A walk helps with jobs that have many good answers, not just one.',tryThis:'Split your problem into the part with many answers and the part with one. Walk with the first.',skills:['thinking','health']},review:{prompt:'You need party ideas and must also check the cost. Which goes on the walk?',right:'The party ideas, since there are lots of good answers',wrong:'The cost, since a walk makes you sharp at every job',why:'A walk helps most when a job has many good answers, not one.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You have two jobs for homework. One is thinking up **ideas for a story**. The other is **a long sum**. If you went for a walk first, which one would it help?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Walking helps most when there are **lots of possible answers**, like story ideas or names for a pet. It does much less for a sum, which has **one right answer**. And it doesn\u2019t make you smarter at everything.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A walk is great for **thinking up ideas**. It\u2019s not much help with sums or spelling. So walk when you need ideas, and **save the sums** for your desk.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re planning **a product launch**. Two jobs sit on your list: brainstorming angles for the campaign, and checking the budget adds up. You\u2019ve heard a walk helps you think. Which job should **come with you**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Walking reliably improves tasks where you **generate many answers**. Dozens of campaign angles could work, so that job is open. Checking the budget is closed: **one answer** is right. Walking does much less for that kind.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Is it just the **fresh air**? Studies found the boost held outdoors and also on a treadmill **facing a blank wall**. That argues against scenery. Something about the walking itself seems to matter.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Walking doesn\u2019t make you **smarter at everything**. It helps with **open problems** that have many answers. So take the open part for a walk, and do the closed part at the desk.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Almost everyone has solved a problem **on a walk**. It\u2019s more than folklore. But the usual explanations are **more confident** than the evidence. So what does the evidence actually support, and what doesn\u2019t it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The finding is specific. Walking reliably helps with tasks that involve **generating many answers**. It does much less for tasks with **one correct answer**. The popular version, that walking makes you smarter, overstates a narrow and useful result.'},{kind:'story',text:'You\u2019re planning a launch. Brainstorming campaign angles is open, with dozens of workable answers. Checking that the budget adds up is closed, with one. You take the brainstorm out on a walk and leave the spreadsheet for later.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture the studies. Some walkers were outdoors. Others walked on a treadmill, facing a blank wall. If scenery caused the effect, the wall should have removed it. It didn\u2019t. That points to the walking itself, though not to how.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Walking helps you generate options, not judge them. **Choosing between them** is a closed task, and may go better at the desk. And knowing that walking helps isn\u2019t knowing how it helps.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Use a walk for **open problems**, where many answers could work. Don\u2019t expect it to fix **closed ones**. The effect is real, just narrower than people claim.'}]},'walking-as-thinking_2':{title:'Breaking the Stare',card:{insight:'Staring keeps you trying the same thing, and a walk breaks that loop.',tryThis:'Next time you are stuck for forty minutes, take the problem on a walk. Leave your phone behind.',skills:['focus','thinking']},review:{prompt:'You have been stuck on one email for forty minutes. What helps most?',right:'A walk without your phone, with the problem still in your head',wrong:'A walk while you scroll your phone to clear your head',why:'A walk helps when the screen goes away and the problem stays in your head.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve been stuck on the same **tricky puzzle** for ages. You keep trying the same few moves. Then you take the dog out, and halfway round the block **the answer pops up**. Why then?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Staring at a problem is like pushing a bike through mud on **the same line**. You make a deep rut. A walk lifts you out of the rut, but the problem **stays in your head**. Now you can try a new line.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When you\u2019re stuck, **get up and move**. Take the problem with you, but **leave the screen** behind. That\u2019s often when a new answer shows up.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve spent forty minutes on **one paragraph**. Each attempt is a small variation on the last. You get up for a walk, mostly out of frustration. Ten minutes in, **the right structure** arrives. What happened?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A problem you look at directly is one you keep **approaching the same way**. Movement interrupts that loop **without making you drop** the problem. That\u2019s why the answer tends to arrive during the walk, not after it.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The same walk can **remove the screen** or **keep it** in your hand.',left:{title:'Phone out',items:['Movement, yes','The screen stays','Benefit mostly lost']},right:{title:'Eyes up',items:['Movement, yes','The screen goes','The problem still carried']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Why the screen? Staring at **a bright rectangle** up close is a narrow, demanding way of looking. A walk gives the opposite: distance, movement and **change at the edges**. Your attention loosens, and new links can surface.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Staring keeps you on **the same track**. A walk breaks the loop while the problem **stays loaded**. But a walk spent on your phone keeps the very thing it was meant to remove.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Some of your best ideas probably arrived **away from the screen**. Not at the desk, where you were trying hardest. Not after you\u2019d given up, either. So what is a walk doing that **staring can\u2019t**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Looking straight at a problem keeps you **approaching it one way**. Movement interrupts that loop. Crucially, it doesn\u2019t ask you to **drop the problem**. That\u2019s the difference from a complete break, and why answers come during the walk.'},{kind:'story',text:'A writer spends forty minutes on a paragraph that won\u2019t come right. Each attempt is a small variation on the last. She walks out in frustration. Ten minutes in, the structure arrives, while she\u2019s still turning the problem over.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a colleague who also walks out stuck, but scrolls his phone the whole way round. He gets the movement. He keeps the screen. He comes back exactly as stuck as he left.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The account of why is **the shakiest part**. The blank-wall treadmill finding sits awkwardly with a story about distance and changing views. So hold the screen explanation loosely. The practical point survives.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A walk works by **loosening how you look**, not by making you quit. Keep the problem loaded. And **leave the phone**, or you\u2019ve brought the desk with you.'}]},'walking-as-thinking_3':{title:'One Question, No Headphones',card:{insight:'A thinking walk needs one question in your head and nothing else.',tryThis:'Next time you walk, pick one question to carry. Leave the headphones at home.',skills:['focus','thinking']},review:{prompt:'You want a walk to help with a work problem. What do you take?',right:'One question to carry, and no headphones',wrong:'A podcast about the problem, to get you going',why:'A walk brings ideas when one question is all you carry.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You head out for a walk with your **headphones in** and a show playing. It\u2019s a lovely walk. But you come back with **no new ideas**. What filled the space they needed?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Go out with **an empty basket** and you can pick up what you find. Go out with a full one, and there\u2019s nowhere to put anything new. So take **one question** with you, and carry it loosely.'},{kind:'moral',text:'For ideas, take **one question** and leave the music behind. A walk just to rest, with music on, is **fine too**. It\u2019s just a different kind of walk.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re stuck on why customers keep cancelling. You go for a walk with **a podcast on**. You come back better informed about something else. But you\u2019re **no less stuck**. Why didn\u2019t the walk work?'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The podcast **fills the channel** the walk was supposed to **free up**.',left:{title:'With a podcast',items:['A pleasant, good walk','The channel is full','Time becomes listening']},right:{title:'With one question',items:['Nothing competing for space','The question is loaded','Time becomes thinking']}},{kind:'story',text:'Next time, you frame one question before leaving: *what\u2019s the real reason customers cancel?* You don\u2019t grind at it. You just carry it. Twenty minutes in, a remark from last month\u2019s support call surfaces and connects.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Hold the question **loosely**. Working hard at it on the walk brings back **the desk mode**. Carrying it gives your loosened attention something to attach to, without forcing it. Too little and nothing sticks.'},{kind:'moral',text:'What you bring **decides the walk**. Take one question and leave the input at home. A podcast walk is still a good walk, but it\u2019s **a different activity**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two people walk the same path for twenty minutes. One comes back with **an answer**. The other comes back relaxed but **no further on**. Same route, same weather. What did each one bring?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The condition that **seems to matter** is having the problem loaded and **nothing else competing**. A podcast fills the channel the walk would free. It turns the time into consumption, which is the mode you were already in at your desk.'},{kind:'story',text:'The first walker framed one question before leaving: why do customers cancel? She didn\u2019t work at it. She just carried it. Near the park gate, a complaint she\u2019d half forgotten came back and explained it.'},{kind:'story',text:'The second walker listened to an interesting episode the whole way. He enjoyed it and learned something. But the channel was full from start to finish, so nothing of his own had room to land.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. A walk purely for rest is legitimate. Turning every walk into a thinking session is **its own optimisation**. And notice the hedge: the condition *seems* to matter. It\u2019s a practical rule, not a verdict on podcasts.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Bring **one question**, held loosely, and leave the input behind. That gives your attention a target without forcing it. Then you decide **which kind of walk** you\u2019re on.'}]},'walking-as-thinking_4':{title:'The Five-Second Catch',card:{insight:'Ideas from a walk leave fast, so write them down before you judge them.',tryThis:'Put a card and pencil in your coat now. On your next walk, write ideas down before you judge them.',skills:['productivity','thinking']},review:{prompt:'A great first line for a speech comes to you on a bridge. What now?',right:'Write it down right away, before you decide if it is good',wrong:'Trust yourself to remember it, since it feels so clear',why:'How clear an idea feels does not tell you if it will last.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'On the walk home, you think of **the perfect idea** for your story. It\u2019s so clear you\u2019ll never forget it. By the time your shoes are off, **it\u2019s gone**. Where did it go?'},{kind:'explain',text:'An idea can feel **very sharp** when it arrives. That doesn\u2019t mean it will stay. Two more thoughts come along and push it out. So keep a **card and pencil** in your pocket, ready to write one line.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Walk ideas **come fast** and go fast. Carry something to catch them with. Then use it **straight away**, before you decide if the idea is any good.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Crossing a bridge, you find **the exact opening line** for a talk you\u2019ve been dreading. It\u2019s so obviously right that writing it down feels **unnecessary**. What do you think you\u2019ll have by the time you get home?'},{kind:'story',text:'By the time you\u2019re home, you remember having a great line. You can\u2019t remember the line. Two more thoughts came along on the way and pushed it out. Feeling sure on the bridge didn\u2019t protect it at all.'},{kind:'explain',text:'How clear an idea feels when it arrives **doesn\u2019t predict** whether it will last. And the next convenient moment to write it down usually comes **too late**. So capture has to be faster than deciding if it\u2019s worth capturing.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Anything that catches a sentence in **five seconds** will do. Anything slower **loses the race**.',left:{title:'Works',items:['A voice memo','A card and a pencil']},right:{title:'Fails',items:['Stopping to type in an app','Planning to remember','Waiting until you sit down']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Arrange your capture **before the walk**. Record first and judge at home, because judging is when ideas slip. Throwing some away is **the price of keeping** the good ones.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'An idea arrives mid-walk, so clear it feels **impossible to forget**. You decide to note it down later. It\u2019s a familiar move. It usually ends with **a lost idea**. So why does certainty fool you so reliably?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The feeling of certainty is **a feeling about now**. It says nothing about whether the thought will last. Meanwhile the walk keeps producing thoughts, and the next one **pushes the last** one out. The competition is the problem.'},{kind:'story',text:'Crossing a bridge, a speaker finds the perfect opening line for a talk she\u2019s been dreading. It seems too obvious to need writing down. At home she has the feeling of having had it. The line itself is gone.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a walker with a voice memo ready. A thought arrives and he records it in five seconds, without stopping or looking at a screen. At home he deletes most of what he caught. One clip is worth keeping.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The habit can turn into a walk spent waiting to capture, which is **directed attention** by another route. And recording everything just moves the sorting later. A pile of unheard memos is no better than a forgotten thought.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Record first and **judge at home**. Your capture has to be faster than **your hesitation**. But capture is only the first step, not the result.'}]},'walking-as-thinking_5':{title:'Ideas Are the Bonus',card:{insight:'A walk is good for your mood and sleep, so any idea is a bonus.',tryThis:'Take a five-minute walk today with no goal at all. See how you feel after.',skills:['health','habits']},review:{prompt:'A friend says walks only count if you get ideas. What do you say?',right:'Walks are worth it for mood and sleep, and ideas are a bonus',wrong:'She is right, so skip the walk on days you need no ideas',why:'Walking helps mood and sleep, so any good ideas are extra.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Think of a cereal box with **a toy inside**. You\u2019d buy the cereal anyway, because you like it. The toy is just **a bonus**. What does that have to do with a walk?'},{kind:'explain',text:'There\u2019s strong evidence that walking often helps you **feel better** and **sleep better**. It\u2019s free, and you can do it almost anywhere. Lots of thinking tricks are hard, so people stop. Walking is easy, so you keep going.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Walk because it helps **your mood** and your sleep. Any good ideas on the way are **the toy** in the box. You\u2019d want the walk anyway.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve read that walking might boost your thinking, and you\u2019re **not convinced**. Fair enough. But suppose the thinking part turned out to be weak. Would there still be a reason to walk **most days**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Yes. Separately from thinking, the evidence that regular walking helps **mood and sleep** is strong. It needs no equipment and no plan. It works in **ordinary weather**, in an ordinary place. That\u2019s the unglamorous benefit.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Cheapness is **an argument** in itself. Most techniques for producing insight are demanding, and demanding techniques get dropped. Walking costs almost nothing to start or keep up. **A modest habit** kept for years can matter more than a strong one dropped in a week.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Walk for **mood and sleep**, where the evidence is firm. Treat any thinking that comes with it as **a bonus**. You\u2019re already doing something worth doing.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Nietzsche, Dickens, Wordsworth and Darwin all relied on walking, and said so. Darwin had **daily circuits**. That\u2019s an impressive list. But how much should **famous walkers** really persuade you?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Each one is **weak evidence** on its own. Together they\u2019re reasonably suggestive, which is the right weight. Famous walkers are remembered partly **because they were famous**. Walkers who achieved little left no record at all.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture someone who tries an intense morning routine for better ideas. It takes an hour and a list of rules. She keeps it up for a week, then drops it. The ideas stop with it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture her neighbour, who walks for forty minutes most evenings. It needs no kit and no plan. Years later she\u2019s still doing it. Her mood and sleep benefit, and the odd idea arrives as a bonus.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Walking isn\u2019t a productivity technique with **a lab-proven mechanism**. And its low downside assumes a safe place to walk and a body that walks easily. Not everyone has those. For them, any cheap movement kept up is the point.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Walking is a very cheap practice with **a long record** and a plausible account. Its case for **mood and sleep** stands on its own. That\u2019s enough for something that costs you forty minutes.'}]}}}},1020,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'walking-is-the-underrated-one',kind:'book',promise:'Walk more, and make the habit last',about:'Why do you hear so little about walking? It is free, and it does a lot for your body and mind. But nobody sells it. These ideas show why, and how to keep walking. Where could your next walk start?',ideas:{'walking-is-the-underrated-one_1':{title:'Nobody Sells Walking',card:{insight:'The tips you hear most often come with something to buy.',tryThis:'Take the last health tip you heard. Write down who gets paid if you believe it.',skills:['thinking','health']},review:{prompt:'A vitamin shows up in every ad you see. What does that tell you?',right:'Mostly that someone gets paid if you believe it',wrong:'That it must work, or it would not be everywhere',why:'Ads show you who pays for a tip, not if it works.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You see adverts for **sports drinks**, trainers and fitness apps every day. You almost never see an advert for **going for a walk**. Why do you think that is?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Walking is free. There\u2019s **nothing to buy**, so no company pays to tell you about it. That says something about money. It says **very little** about how good walking is for you.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The tips you hear most are often **the ones that sell**. So when a tip is everywhere, ask **who gets paid**. Then think of one free thing that works.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'In one week your phone shows you **a protein powder** and a rowing machine. Then comes a ring that scores your sleep. Nobody suggests **a thirty-minute walk**. Why not?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Walking has **no equipment**, no subscription and no supplement. No company gains when you do it. The channels that carry health advice are mostly funded by **things you can buy**. So walking rarely reaches you.'},{kind:'explain',text:'That\u2019s a filter worth using everywhere. How often you hear advice reflects **its commercial backing**. It says little about **its evidence**. The two can come apart entirely, and walking is the plainest case.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Before you weigh a tip, ask **who benefits** if you believe it. If the answer is nobody, it reached you **without promotion**. That\u2019s weak evidence for it, but better than none.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Walking is one of the **best-supported things** you can do for body and mind. Yet it\u2019s one of the **least discussed**. If it works so well, why does so little advice mention it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The answer lies in how advice travels. It reaches you **in proportion** to what it sells. Walking has no kit, no subscription and no content built around it. So nobody pays to **put it in front** of you.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture one week of your feed. A protein powder, a home rowing machine, a fitness app and a sleep ring all appear. Each has a company behind it. In the same week, nobody suggests a walk.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a new supplement that every creator seems to recommend. Its fame tells you someone is paying for reach. It doesn\u2019t tell you the pills work. Asking who benefits turns that noise into a useful fact.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Not being sold doesn\u2019t prove something works, and plenty of unpromoted ideas are simply wrong. Heavy marketing doesn\u2019t make a product bad. And walking isn\u2019t **wholly unsold**: step counters exist.'},{kind:'moral',text:'How loud a tip is tells you **who funds it**, not whether it works. Use the filter to decide **where to look**. Don\u2019t use it to decide what\u2019s true.'}]},'walking-is-the-underrated-one_2':{title:'Where Ten Thousand Came From',card:{insight:'Ten thousand steps began as a name to sell a step counter.',tryThis:'Check your daily step goal. If you rarely reach it, set it to a number you hit most days.',skills:['health','thinking']},review:{prompt:'Your friend skips her walk because she can\u2019t reach ten thousand. What do you say?',right:'Fewer steps still help, and ten thousand was a sales name',wrong:'She is right, since a short walk does not do much',why:'Ten thousand was a name to sell a gadget, so fewer steps count.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Lots of watches and phones tell you to walk **ten thousand steps** a day. It sounds like a scientist worked it out. So where did **that number** really come from?'},{kind:'explain',text:'In the 1960s, a step counter was sold in Japan. Its name meant *ten-thousand-step meter*. The number was easy to remember, so it **helped sell** the gadget. Nobody had **tested it** first.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Ten thousand steps was **a name**, not a test you\u2019ve got to pass. Later research found walking helps **well below it**. So every step you take still counts.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'On a busy day you manage **six thousand steps**. Your watch shows the ring unfilled, and it feels like **a failed day**. But who decided ten thousand was the line?'},{kind:'story',text:'A Japanese pedometer sold in the 1960s was branded *manpo-kei*, meaning ten-thousand-step meter. The number is usually said to have been picked because it was memorable. Its written character reportedly looked like a walking figure, too.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Later research found benefits building **well below** ten thousand. Substantial gains start far lower. The point where extra steps stop helping much **differs between studies**, and it\u2019s often under ten thousand.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Ten thousand was **marketing first**, and evidence came later. It\u2019s not a pass mark. Your six thousand steps **still counted**, so don\u2019t let a round number turn a benefit into a shortfall.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Some numbers seem to be everywhere: on devices, in advice, in conversation. **Ten thousand steps** is one. Most people assume a study set it. What if it **came from an advert** instead?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The figure began as **a product name**. A 1960s Japanese pedometer was sold as the ten-thousand-step meter. The number was memorable, which made it good marketing. Evidence came afterwards, **in that order**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Later research looked at real step counts. Benefits built up well below ten thousand, with big gains starting far lower. The point where more steps stopped adding much varied between studies. It was often under ten thousand.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a colleague on a busy day. He says there\u2019s no point walking, because he\u2019ll never reach ten thousand. The target has turned a real benefit into a failure. So he skips the steps that would have counted.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The number isn\u2019t harmful, and for someone who likes **a clear target** it may help. The origin story is reported rather than settled, too. The walking-figure detail is what\u2019s usually said, so hold it loosely.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A figure repeated everywhere can have **no tested origin**. Once embedded, it gets cited as though it did. Ask where **your round numbers** came from before you treat them as rules.'}]},'walking-is-the-underrated-one_3':{title:'Walking Lasts a Bad Week',card:{insight:'Walking keeps going in a bad week, since it needs so little.',tryThis:'Pick your floor for a bad week now, like a short walk from your door.',skills:['habits','health']},review:{prompt:'Your gym pass runs out in a hard month at work. What now?',right:'Keep a short walk going as your floor',wrong:'Stop working out until you can get back to the gym',why:'The simplest workout lasts best when life gets hard, so keep walking.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'It\u2019s a bad week. You have two tests, your **swim class moves**, and your football boots are lost. By Friday you\u2019ve done **no sport at all**. What could have kept going?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Some sports need a lot: kit, a lift, a booked time. Each of those can **go wrong**. Walking needs almost nothing. So in a hard week, it\u2019s the thing **most likely** to keep going.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Think of walking as **the bottom rung** of a ladder. On bad days you can still stand on it. Keep **a short walk** going, even when the rest stops.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You plan **a demanding routine** that does a lot each session. By spring it\u2019s gone. Your friend just walks every day, and **never stops**. Over a whole year, which plan does more?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Every requirement is **a point of failure**: kit to pack, a trip to the gym, a class to book. Under stress, one of them gives. The more an exercise needs, **the sooner** it\u2019s dropped.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a hard month at work. The bike stays in the hall and the gym bag never opens. But the ordinary shoes by the door still go out most evenings, for ten minutes at a time.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Walking **scales down** without feeling like failure. Ten minutes on a bad day is still the habit, not a broken version of it. That\u2019s **the property that counts** when everything else has gone wrong.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Durability decides outcomes more than effort per session. So hold walking as **your floor**, not your goal. In a bad week, protect it and **let the rest go**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Plenty of people start the year with **a gym plan**. Plenty of them have quietly stopped by spring. The walk they took on the way home often **kept going**. Why does the humble option last?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Each thing an exercise needs can fail: kit, travel, a booking, recovery time. Under stress, **one of them gives**, and the routine goes with it. Walking has almost **no failure modes**, so it\u2019s what remains.'},{kind:'story',text:'A woman starts a hard training plan in January. It does a lot per session. Then work gets heavy, her class times shift, and by March she\u2019s stopped. Over the year, she manages a few good months.'},{kind:'story',text:'Her neighbour only walks, ten minutes on bad days and longer on good ones. Each session does less. But it never stops, so by December he\u2019s done far more. The one still happening wins the year.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. This is about durability, not effect. For some goals, walking is **no substitute** for harder training. And it isn\u2019t free of barriers for everyone: injury, illness, unsafe streets or long hours can remove it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t judge exercise by what one session does. Judge it by what you\u2019ll still be doing **in a bad week**. Make walking **your floor**, and defend it.'}]},'walking-is-the-underrated-one_4':{title:'Walk With the Problem',card:{insight:'A walk helps you think of new ideas, but mostly while you walk.',tryThis:'Next time you are stuck for ideas, take the problem on a walk.',skills:['thinking','focus']},review:{prompt:'You must check a report and write a slogan. Where does a walk help?',right:'The slogan, so write it while you walk',wrong:'The report, so check it after you walk',why:'A walk helps you think of new ideas, and the help comes as you walk.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You need an idea for **a class story**. You sit at your desk and nothing comes. Twenty minutes go by, and you have **one line** you don\u2019t even like. What now?'},{kind:'explain',text:'In tests, people thought of **more new ideas** while walking than while sitting. It worked indoors and outdoors. The help came mostly **during the walk**, so take the problem with you.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A walk can help when you need **new ideas**. The help is real but **small**. It isn\u2019t magic, so keep the problem in your head as you go.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You need **a name** for a new project. You\u2019ve stared at the same document for forty minutes. Every option is **a variation** on the first one. Would a break help, or something else?'},{kind:'explain',text:'In experiments, people came up with more **alternative uses** for objects while walking than while seated. It held indoors as well as out. The effect lasted only **briefly afterwards**. That\u2019s the well-supported part.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The help suits **generating** options, not **executing** a set task.',left:{title:'Generating',items:['A new project name','A slogan to invent','Ways round a problem']},right:{title:'Executing',items:['A report to proofread','A page of sums','A form to fill']}},{kind:'moral',text:'The effect is **real and modest**, and it arrives while you walk. So when you\u2019re stuck generating options, don\u2019t take a break from the problem. **Take it walking**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Walking is sometimes sold as **a brain hack**: sharper focus, a better memory, a smarter you. The evidence is **much narrower** than that. So what does it actually support?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The best-known finding is about **divergent thinking**. In experiments, people generated more alternative uses for objects while walking than while seated. It held indoors as well as out, so it\u2019s **not just scenery**.'},{kind:'story',text:'You\u2019re stuck on three ways out of a scheduling knot. Every idea is a version of the first. You leave the desk and walk with the question. Partway round, a fourth option turns up.'},{kind:'story',text:'Your colleague takes a break instead. He leaves the problem at his desk and chats in the kitchen for a while. It\u2019s pleasant enough. But the help comes from walking while thinking, and he did neither.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The finding is about generating options in controlled tasks. It doesn\u2019t show walking cracks hard problems or **improves thinking** long term. A walk is likely to help, not certain to.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Treat walking as **a working tool**, not a health claim. When you\u2019re stuck generating, take the problem along. The help arrives **during the walk**, so the problem has to come too.'}]},'walking-is-the-underrated-one_5':{title:'Give Your Walk a Hook',card:{insight:'A walk tied to a daily habit lasts as long as that habit.',tryThis:'Pick one thing you do every day, like lunch. Add ten minutes of walking right after it.',skills:['habits','health']},review:{prompt:'You want to walk more during a busy month. Which plan will last?',right:'Walk right after lunch, since you eat lunch each day',wrong:'Fit in a walk at some point each day',why:'A walk tied to something fixed happens when that thing does.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Ava and Ben both want to walk more. Ava will walk **at some point** each day. Ben will get off the bus **one stop early**. Who do you think is still walking in a month?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Most likely Ben. Ava\u2019s walk has to fight homework, games and friends **for a spot**, and it usually loses. Ben\u2019s bus ride home happens every day anyway, so his walk **comes with it**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A walk on its own is **easy to skip**. Hook it onto something you\u2019re already doing every day. Then the walk happens **when that happens**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'The walk you meant to fit in **at some point** today hasn\u2019t happened again. Nothing stopped it. Everything else simply arrived first, and each had **a time attached**. Sound familiar?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A walk scheduled in the abstract competes with everything and **usually loses**. Tie it to an existing event with reliable timing, and it **borrows that reliability**. That\u2019s how an intention becomes a habit.'},{kind:'story',text:'Good anchors already happen at fixed times: after lunch, after the school run, the end of the working day. They happen whether or not you feel like walking. So the walk gets pulled along.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The strongest form is **a call on foot**. If a regular call needs no screen, take it walking. No new time has to be found. You\u2019re **converting time** you\u2019d already committed.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t ask the day to be longer. **Attach the walk** to something fixed, and it inherits that thing\u2019s reliability. Anything that **needs no new time** is far more likely to survive.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two people want to walk more. One relies on **good intentions**. The other ties the walk to her commute. A month on, only one is still walking. What did **the commute** give her?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A standalone walk competes with **everything else** in the day, and usually loses. A walk tied to a fixed event **inherits its reliability**. Tying a new behaviour to an existing cue is well supported in habit research.'},{kind:'story',text:'You have a weekly call that needs no screen. You start taking it on foot. No new time had to be found. Half an hour you\u2019d already committed became half an hour of walking.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take someone whose walk was tied to her commute. It held for a year. Then she began working from home, and within a fortnight the walks had stopped. The anchor went, so the walk went too.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. An anchor lends only **the reliability it has**. If your routine changes, the walk can vanish with it. And the evidence covers cue-linked habits in general. That the call is strongest is reasoning, not a tested result.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t schedule a walk in the abstract. **Attach it** to something that already happens, and check the anchor when your life changes. Ideas that need **no extra time** survive best.'}]}}}},1021,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'wealth-of-nations',kind:'book',promise:'Learn what Adam Smith said about money and work',about:'Was Adam Smith really a fan of greed? This thinker from Scotland wrote The Wealth of Nations in 1776. He liked markets. But he also warned about sellers who team up against buyers. Here are both sides. Which one will surprise you?',ideas:{'wealth-of-nations_1':{title:'The Pin Factory',card:{insight:'Small steps get more done, but people who do one step can stop learning.',tryThis:'Today, ask a coworker to show you one step of their job.',skills:['career','productivity']},review:{prompt:'Your team splits into five small jobs and gets more done. What should you add?',right:'Time for people to learn each other\u2019s steps',wrong:'Nothing, since the team now gets more done',why:'Splitting work helps, but people still need to learn the whole job.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your class is making cards for the school fair. At first, each of you makes **a whole card**. It\u2019s slow going. Then someone says: let\u2019s **split the jobs**. What happens next?'},{kind:'explain',text:'One child folds, one draws, one adds glitter. The pile grows fast. In 1776 Adam Smith saw this with **pins**. But he saw **the cost** too. If you only add glitter all day, you never learn to make a whole card.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Splitting work makes **far more things**. Doing one tiny job all day can leave you **knowing less**. Smith said both are true, and you should plan for both.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A support team is reorganised so each person owns **one narrow step**: sorting, refunds or escalation. Tickets close faster than ever. A year later, the refund system changes. Who on the team can **redesign it**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Smith opens The Wealth of Nations in a small pin works. A pin takes about eighteen operations. Ten workers, each doing a few, make tens of thousands a day. One person doing every step could scarcely make twenty.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Hundreds of pages later, Smith returns to **the worker**. Someone kept to a few simple tasks never has to use **their understanding**, and loses the habit. He didn\u2019t undo the split. He proposed public schooling to offset it.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Your support team didn\u2019t get worse. It made **a trade**, and only **one side** shows up in the numbers.',left:{title:'The gain',items:['Tickets close faster','Each step done well']},right:{title:'The cost',items:['Nobody sees the whole','No one can redesign it']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Narrow roles multiply output and shrink **understanding**. Keep the split, but pay for **the cost** on purpose. Plan rotation or a shared review, so you can still redesign the whole.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'The pin factory sits in the **first chapter** of Smith\u2019s book. Most quotations stop there. But in the fifth book, he comes back to the people making pins. What did he **find there**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Smith credits the **division of labour** with three gains. Repetition builds skill. Nobody wastes time switching tasks. And simple motions are easy to hand to **machines**. That\u2019s why he made it the engine of rising wealth.'},{kind:'story',text:'In the pin works, ten people split about eighteen steps. Together they make tens of thousands of pins a day. One person alone could scarcely make twenty. That\u2019s the gain, and it\u2019s enormous.'},{kind:'explain',text:'In Book V comes **the warning**. A worker kept to a few simple operations meets no problem that needs thought. He loses the habit of thinking, Smith argues. His remedy was **public schooling**, paid for on purpose.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a firm that narrows each engineer to one service. It ships faster. Two years on, nobody understands how the services fit together. When the market moves, you can\u2019t find anyone who sees the whole system.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Smith pictured the factory hand of his century. Much narrow work today is skilled and absorbing. And specialists can take knowledge **deeper** than any generalist could.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Splitting work is **the engine** of output. Carried far, it quietly dulls the people inside it. Keep the engine, but budget **the remedy** into your own plan.'}]},'wealth-of-nations_2':{title:'Why the Baker Bakes',card:{insight:'People help most when it helps them too, so build plans that do.',tryThis:'Today, pick one weekly chore and make it pay off for the helper.',skills:['thinking','money']},review:{prompt:'Parents keep skipping the snack sign-up, even after kind notes. What would Smith suggest?',right:'Make the plan help the parents who keep it',wrong:'Send an even kinder note to the parents',why:'Kindness comes and goes, so a plan holds when it helps people too.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You buy a bun at the bakery on Saturday. The baker has **never met you**. So why was she up at four in the morning, making **your bun**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Selling buns is how the baker **earns a living**. So there are buns every day, for anyone who comes in. Adam Smith saw this as a kind of **machine**. He didn\u2019t say being selfish is good.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You can count on a stranger\u2019s **own interest** more than their kindness. That\u2019s why the shelves are full. It\u2019s **a mechanism**, not a reason to be greedy.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your bread is on the shelf this morning. The shop\u2019s fridge is full of milk. Nobody who made them has **ever thought** about you. So why is your dinner **there anyway**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Smith\u2019s point is **reliability**. Kindness is real, but it\u2019s unevenly spread, and a stranger can\u2019t count on it. Interest is steady. If supplying you pays the baker, the bread turns up. A system can be **built on that**.'},{kind:'story',text:'A charity\u2019s volunteer rota keeps collapsing. The organiser sends warmer and warmer appeals. Nothing changes. Then she lets volunteers pick their own shifts and swap freely. Now the rota serves the people keeping it, and it holds.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Smith wrote another book first, on **sympathy**. So read the baker passage as **engineering**, not ethics.',left:{title:'Ethics',items:['What makes a good person','Kindness and sympathy']},right:{title:'Engineering',items:['What reliably turns up','Interest between strangers']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t lean on goodwill alone. Make sure the arrangement **plainly serves** the people keeping it. And remember it\u2019s only **a mechanism**: it works under competition, not without it.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'It\u2019s one of the most quoted passages in economics: the butcher, the brewer and the baker. It\u2019s usually read as **praise of greed**. What if it\u2019s really describing **a machine**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Smith\u2019s claim is about what you can **count on**. Benevolence is real but uneven, and strangers can\u2019t rely on it. Interest is **dependable**. So a system that serves strangers has to be built on interest.'},{kind:'story',text:'Smith wrote The Theory of Moral Sentiments first, a book largely about sympathy and moral judgement. He kept working on it. The man who described the baker never stopped being a moralist.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a town has one baker. She\u2019s just as self-interested as before. But with no rival, her interest points elsewhere: higher prices, smaller loaves. The motive hasn\u2019t changed. The conditions have.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Interest isn\u2019t the only thing holding a society together. Trust, conscience and law do work prices can\u2019t. And quoted as a virtue, the passage gets used to **defend monopolies** Smith attacked.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Self-interest is **a mechanism**, not a virtue. It serves you only when sellers compete and **can\u2019t collude**. Remove those conditions and the same motive builds monopoly.'}]},'wealth-of-nations_3':{title:'Sellers in One Room',card:{insight:'When a rule helps the sellers who asked for it, look twice.',tryThis:'Next time you read about a new rule, look up who asked for it.',skills:['thinking','history']},review:{prompt:'Taxi owners ask the city to cap new taxis for safety. What would Smith ask?',right:'Who asked for the cap, and who gains from it',wrong:'Nothing, since the cap keeps riders safe',why:'Look twice at a rule that helps the people who asked for it.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Three stalls sell lemonade at the school fair. Each wants your money, so each **keeps prices low**. Then the three owners have a quiet chat **behind the tent**. What do you think happens next?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Now all three charge **double**. You\u2019ve nowhere else to go. Adam Smith wrote about this in 1776. Sellers of the same thing, he said, rarely meet without **plotting** against buyers. Where they stand tempts them, not who they are.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When sellers **team up**, buyers lose. So when a group asks for a new rule, ask **who it helps** most. If it helps the group that asked, look twice.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A trade body asks for a costly new licence for anyone **joining the trade**. It says this is for safety. The safety case may be real. So why might you **read it twice**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'People quote Smith to defend business. Yet he\u2019s **hard on merchants**. He warns that people in one trade seldom meet, even socially, without the talk turning to **raising prices**. Any proposal from them deserves suspicion, he adds.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'It\u2019s about structure, not character. One side is **organised**, and the other is **scattered**.',left:{title:'Producers',items:['Few, and know each other','Each gains a lot','Get organised and heard']},right:{title:'Consumers',items:['Many and scattered','Each loses a little','Rarely heard at all']}},{kind:'story',text:'Back to the licence. It may well make things safer. It also keeps rivals out, and every customer, including you, pays a little more. Smith\u2019s test isn\u2019t refusal. It\u2019s two questions: who proposed it, and who gains?'},{kind:'moral',text:'A rule proposed by **those it protects** deserves a closer look. It may still be right. So find out who asked for it, and **read their case** twice.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Defenders of business quote Smith all the time. Yet he wrote that traders rarely gather, even for fun, without a **scheme against the public**. So whose side was he **really on**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'At the close of Book I he goes further. Any law proposed by merchants, he says, deserves **close suspicion** first. Their interest is never quite the public\u2019s. And they\u2019re **far better placed** to press it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Smith\u2019s target was what he called the mercantile system: import duties, export bounties and monopoly trading companies. He argued merchants largely designed these policies. Consumers quietly paid for them, through higher prices.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a modern case. Established taxi firms lobby for a cap on new licences. They cite passenger safety, and they may be sincere. But the cap also protects their fares. Smith would ask you to look twice.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Smith was just as scathing about rulers, so this isn\u2019t a plea for **more government**. And consumers do sometimes organise. A rule an industry proposes can still be right. Suspicion means a closer look, not a no.'},{kind:'moral',text:'His target wasn\u2019t government itself. It was **government captured** by the sellers it favours. So when a rule comes from **those it protects**, you read their case twice.'}]},'wealth-of-nations_4':{title:'Toil and Trouble',card:{insight:'A price pays for the work and skill behind it, not just the time.',tryThis:'Next time a price shocks you, write down what it might pay for.',skills:['money','thinking']},review:{prompt:'A plumber charges a lot for a quick fix. What do you look at first?',right:'The years of skill and work behind the fix',wrong:'That a quick fix should not cost much',why:'A price pays for the skill and work behind the job, not just the time.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two pairs of wool socks hang side by side. One pair costs **five times more** than the other. They look almost the same. Before you shout **rip-off**, what might explain the gap?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Maybe someone **knitted them** by hand. Maybe the wool is hard to get. Adam Smith said the real cost of a thing is the **toil and trouble** of getting it. Toil means hard work. That\u2019s what a price counts.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Behind most prices sit **work** and **scarcity**. So when something costs a lot, don\u2019t assume you\u2019re being cheated. First ask what went into it.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A handmade chair costs **ten times** as much as a flat-pack one. Your first thought is that someone\u2019s overcharging. A better second thought is a question. What **went into** each of them?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Smith treats labour as **the real measure** of value. What a thing costs is the toil and trouble of getting it. The theory has been superseded. But its question survives: what **work and scarcity** sit behind this price?'},{kind:'explain',text:'He complicates it at once. A price must also pay for **capital and land**. Skilled work counts for more than unskilled. And the day\u2019s price moves around a **natural level**, pushed by supply and demand.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now the plumber who charges a lot for twenty minutes. It looks steep. But those minutes rest on years of skill that few people have. You\u2019re paying for the years, and for the scarcity.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A price is a **compressed record** of work and scarcity. It isn\u2019t proof of fairness either way. Treat it as **a question**: what went into this?'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'On a take-home wage of \xa315 an hour, a \xa3600 phone costs **forty hours**. Smith would say that\u2019s its real price. Is he right that **labour** is what things truly cost?'},{kind:'explain',text:'In Book I, Smith calls labour **the real measure** of exchangeable value. A thing costs the toil of getting it. It\u2019s worth the toil it saves you. Money is only **the usual way** of counting.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take a hand-thrown pot from a small workshop. It costs far more than a factory bowl. Behind it sit a potter\u2019s skill, the kiln, and many slow hours. The price is all of that, compressed.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now turn it on yourself. That phone is forty hours of your working life. The hours are the honest figure, because you can\u2019t borrow them back. Spending looks different in that unit.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. As a theory of price, it\u2019s superseded. A rare stamp or a plot of city land can be worth a fortune with **little labour** in it. And a year spent on something nobody wants is worth little.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Labour is **a measure** of cost, not the whole story of price. Use it as a question, never as **proof** that a price is fair. What went into this, and what did it cost you?'}]},'wealth-of-nations_5':{title:'The Missing Rival',card:{insight:'Sellers try hard when they have rivals, and that keeps prices low.',tryThis:'Next time a bill goes up, count how many rivals the seller has.',skills:['thinking','money']},review:{prompt:'Your town has one cable company. Its prices keep going up. What do you check?',right:'Whether the sellers have any rivals at all',wrong:'Whether the people who run it are greedy',why:'Markets work best with rivals, so look for the piece that is missing.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A football match needs **a few things** set up first. Lines on the pitch. A referee. The same number of players on each side. Take those away, and what happens to **the game** you\u2019re watching?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A market is like that. It works well only when **some things** are true. There\u2019s more than one seller. Buyers know what they\u2019re getting. And sellers **don\u2019t team up**. Your school\u2019s one tuck shop can charge what it likes.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When a market goes wrong, don\u2019t blame **buying and selling**. Look for the missing piece. Adam Smith said someone has to **draw the lines** first, and sometimes that\u2019s the government.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A town has a **single broadband provider**. Prices rise and service slips. Everyone shrugs and says markets just don\u2019t work here. Before you agree, what would you **check first**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Smith\u2019s case for markets is **conditional**. Competition must be real. Buyers must be able to judge what they buy. Sellers must not collude. These are **prerequisites**, not guarantees, and setting them up is somebody\u2019s job.'},{kind:'story',text:'Run the checklist on the town. There\u2019s no rival. There\u2019s no cheap way for a new firm to lay cables. There\u2019s no easy way to compare deals. The mechanism never had its conditions in the first place.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Where conditions fail, Smith expects **the state** to act. He backed roads, bridges and **public schooling**, and rules on how banks issue notes. He never suggests the conditions arise by themselves.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A market that fails may be **missing a condition**. So before you call it broken, ask: are there rivals, can buyers judge, is collusion ruled out? Name **the missing one** first.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Smith is often remembered as the man who said markets **fix themselves**. Yet he backed public schools, roads and banking rules. Was he contradicting himself, or is **the slogan** wrong?'},{kind:'explain',text:'His case was always **conditional**. Markets are the best available mechanism when competition is real, entry is open, buyers can judge and sellers can\u2019t collude. Where those fail, he expects **public action**.'},{kind:'story',text:'His clearest case is schooling. He\u2019s just described how narrow factory work dulls the people doing it. He doesn\u2019t leave that harm to the market that caused it. He proposes publicly supported schools instead.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture patients who can\u2019t compare clinics\u2019 prices or results. Fees keep climbing. You might blame greedy owners. Smith would check the conditions first. Buyers who can\u2019t judge can\u2019t discipline sellers, whoever they are.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The conditions are never all fully met. Applied loosely, the framework can justify intervening **almost anywhere**. And the state that sets the conditions can be captured by the sellers it oversees.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Markets are a tool with **prerequisites**. When one fails, name the missing condition. Then ask whether your fix **restores it**, or just replaces one problem with another.'}]},'wealth-of-nations_6':{title:'Who Can Wait Longer',card:{insight:'In a deal, the side that can wait longer gets the better price.',tryThis:'Before your next big deal, write down how long each side could wait.',skills:['communication','career']},review:{prompt:'Your landlord wants a big rent increase. Which fact matters most?',right:'How long each side could go without a deal',wrong:'How strong your reasons against the increase sound',why:'Good reasons help a little, but the side that can wait wins.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You want your friend\u2019s **rare card**. She wants one of yours. But her mum is picking her up **in two minutes**. You\u2019ve got all afternoon. Who do you think gives in first?'},{kind:'explain',text:'When two sides make a deal, the one who **can wait longer** is stronger. Adam Smith noticed this in 1776. Bosses had money saved and could last **a year or two**. Many workers couldn\u2019t last a week.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Clever words help **a little**. But if you need the deal right now, the other side can just wait. So first ask: who needs this deal **more and sooner**?'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your firm\u2019s biggest client says they\u2019re **very relaxed** about renewing. Their emails are warm. Your boss wants the deal signed this month. What should you weigh **most** right now?'},{kind:'story',text:'Smith describes wage bargaining without sentiment. Masters want to pay little and workmen want much, and both combine. But masters could live a year or two on their stock. Many workmen couldn\u2019t last a week without pay.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Anyone can claim to be relaxed. It costs nothing, so it **tells you nothing**. What informs is **structure**: who has an alternative ready, and whose plans break if this lapses. The arguments sit on top of that.'},{kind:'explain',text:'So work on your position **between deals**. Nothing helps you in the room like **an alternative** arranged months early. That might be a second supplier, a cash buffer or another offer.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The outcome is mostly decided by **who can wait**. So before you prepare arguments, write how long each side could comfortably go **without a deal**. Negotiate from that.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two people are haggling over a price. One of them has **rent due** on Friday. The other doesn\u2019t. Neither has said a word about it. Who do you think is **already winning**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'In his chapter on wages, Smith asks what decides pay. Workers want much and masters little. It isn\u2019t hard, he says, to see **which side** usually wins. His reason is **holding power**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Masters were fewer, so they combined easily. The law of his day banned workers\u2019 combinations but left masters\u2019 alone. And masters could live a year or two on savings. Many workers couldn\u2019t last a week.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a startup negotiating with its only big customer. It has three months of cash left. No pitch will hide that. The real work is to stretch the runway or find a second customer first.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Smith described an unequal world where workers had no savings and no legal right to combine. Holding power isn\u2019t fixed. And treating every deal as a siege poisons **long relationships** where both sides gain.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Arguments act only at **the last step**. Holding power has usually settled things before you speak. So change **how long** you can wait, and you change the deal.'}]},'wealth-of-nations_7':{title:'One Famous Phrase',card:{insight:'A famous line can grow much bigger than what its writer meant.',tryThis:'Next time a famous line ends a talk, ask what it says about this talk.',skills:['thinking','communication']},review:{prompt:'A friend says the invisible hand will stop price fixing. What do you ask?',right:'What the phrase says about this exact case',wrong:'Nothing, since a famous man said it long ago',why:'A famous line cannot stand in for what is true about this case.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your friend ends every argument the same way: *my dad says so.* And then **everyone stops**. But what did his dad actually say, and **about what**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Adam Smith wrote a very long book in 1776. One line in it mentions an **invisible hand**. Today that line is famous. People use it to end arguments it was **never about**. It\u2019s like a trailer for a different film.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A famous phrase can grow **much bigger** than what its writer meant. When someone uses one to end an argument, you can ask: **what does it say** about this?'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A debate about a new rule is going nowhere. Then someone says the market will sort it out: **the invisible hand**. People nod. The talk stops. But has anyone said **a thing** about your rule?'},{kind:'story',text:'The phrase appears **once** in The Wealth of Nations. Smith is asking why merchants tend to invest at home, not abroad. Each seeks his own safety. Yet he ends up helping home industry **without meaning to**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'That\u2019s **one step** in a case about limits on trade. It isn\u2019t a general theory. And around it, the book is full of **self-interest going wrong**: price-fixing, monopolies, merchants writing laws for themselves.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Once a famous phrase is in the room, you\u2019re arguing about **its reputation**. So bring it back to earth. Ask what the principle claims about **this specific case**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'It\u2019s probably the best-known image in economics: **an invisible hand** guiding markets. You\u2019d expect a whole chapter on it. So how often does it appear in **Smith\u2019s book**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Once. It sits in Book IV, in a chapter against **restraints on imports**. A merchant who prefers home investment for his own security is steered, unintentionally, towards supporting **his country\u2019s industry**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a meeting. A colleague waves off a plan to check suppliers for price-fixing. The invisible hand will handle it, he says. Yet the book behind that phrase expects sellers to collude.'},{kind:'story',text:'Or a friend who wins every argument by citing a famous study. Nobody asks what it found, or about whom. The name ends the talk. The claim itself was never checked against the case at hand.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The correction can be overdone. Smith did believe self-interest, **under the right conditions**, often serves the public unintentionally. Dismissing that idea because the phrase is rare is the same error in reverse.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The invisible hand is **one image** in one argument. Its fame now does work the passage can\u2019t support. So when you hear it, ask **what it claims** here.'}]},'wealth-of-nations_8':{title:'The Harm Nobody Counts',card:{insight:'A change can help and hurt at once, so plan the fix too.',tryThis:'Before you change how people work, write down who might lose something.',skills:['leadership','productivity']},review:{prompt:'A hospital gives each nurse one task to speed things up. Who plans the fix?',right:'Whoever made the change, as part of the same plan',wrong:'Nobody, since the nurses will get used to it',why:'The harm will not fix itself, so whoever made the change must plan for it.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your school speeds up lunch. Now each kitchen helper does **one job**. One scoops peas. One hands out trays. The queue is faster, and everyone\u2019s pleased. So **who pays** a cost?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The helpers do one dull thing **all day**. That cost doesn\u2019t show in the faster queue. Adam Smith saw this with factory work in 1776. He said the factory would **never fix it**, so the country should pay for schools.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A change can do **real good** and real harm at once. The harm can land on people who didn\u2019t choose it. If you don\u2019t **plan the fix**, it stays.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A firm splits its claims team so each person handles **one narrow step**. Claims fly through, and **the dashboard** proves it. A year later, two good people leave. Where did that show up in your numbers?'},{kind:'story',text:'Over that year, staff lost the overall picture. Nobody could handle an unusual case any more. People got bored and left. None of it appeared in the figure that justified the change. Nobody had planned for it.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Smith admired the division of labour, then wrote grimly about its cost. A worker kept to a few simple tasks **stops using his mind**. He didn\u2019t call that a fair price. The system causing it **would never correct it**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'So every honest proposal carries **two columns**, and you\u2019ve designed the change, so **both are yours**.',left:{title:'The gain',items:['Faster claims','Shows on the dashboard','Measured every week']},right:{title:'The cost',items:['Lost overview','Shows up in resignations','Measured by nobody']}},{kind:'moral',text:'A real gain can cause **a real harm** that no mechanism will price. So whoever designed the change carries the remedy. Put rotation or training **into the same plan**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Smith is the great admirer of **splitting work**. Yet he wrote some of the harshest lines about what it does to a mind. Why would its biggest fan **sound the alarm**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'In every advanced society, he argues, narrow work will dull **most working people**. That will happen unless government **deliberately acts**. It isn\u2019t a fair price for growth. It\u2019s a harm that needs a remedy.'},{kind:'story',text:'His model was the parish schools of Scotland. A small school in every district taught reading, writing and arithmetic cheaply. Teachers were paid partly by the public, partly by fees, so they stayed diligent.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now an automation project strips the varied parts out of a job. What\u2019s left is monitoring a screen. Output rises. The person doing it slowly loses the skills that made them useful. No figure records the loss.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Schooling in childhood may do little for a mind dulled at forty. And blaming a designer for **every effect** can stall useful reform. The claim is only about costs the system won\u2019t price.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Efficiency can create harms that land on people **who didn\u2019t choose** them. No market will price those. So if you make the change, you **carry the remedy**.'}]},'wealth-of-nations_9':{title:'The Empty Plate',card:{insight:'Judge your work by what it does for the people it is for.',tryThis:'Today, ask one person your work is for what would help them most.',skills:['productivity','leadership']},review:{prompt:'A city brags that it fixed more potholes than ever. Which question fits Smith\u2019s test?',right:'What changed for the people who use those roads?',wrong:'How many more potholes can it fix next year?',why:'Counting work tells you how much you made, not who it helped.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your class bakes **200 cupcakes** for the bake sale. Everyone\u2019s proud of the big number. But they\u2019re far too sweet, and most end up **in the bin**. Was the sale a success?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Adam Smith wrote that the whole point of making things is for **people to use** them. He said it was so plain it hardly needed saying. Then he showed how often rulers **forgot it**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'How much you make isn\u2019t the point **on its own**. What matters is what it does for **the people** it\u2019s for. Ask who your work helped.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'An IT team reports it closed **far more tickets** this year than last. Everyone\u2019s pleased. But the same problems keep coming back. What would you **ask next**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Smith states it flatly: consumption is **the only purpose** of production. Producers deserve a hearing only while they serve **the consumer**. Closing tickets is output. Working computers for your users are the purpose.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Why do the two come apart? Output is **easy to count**, and it\u2019s inside the team\u2019s control. The effect on users is harder and sits outside it. So the measurable one becomes **the defence**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Smith saw policy doing the opposite. Under the mercantile system, he argued, the consumer\u2019s interest was almost always sacrificed to the producer\u2019s. Import duties and export bounties made goods dearer at home.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Output can be fully met while **the purpose** goes unmet. So when your team defends itself by output, ask what changed for **the people** it was for.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A university department defends its budget by the **papers it publishes**. The count rises every year. Nobody asks who read them, or what changed. Would Smith be impressed by **the number**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Near the end of Book IV, Smith calls one principle **too obvious** to need proof. Producing anything is only for someone to consume. Producers deserve attention only as far as they **serve consumers**.'},{kind:'story',text:'His target was the policy of his day. Bounties paid producers to make or export goods. Consumers paid twice, he argued: through the taxes that funded them, and through higher prices at home.'},{kind:'story',text:'Back to the department. Paper counts answer a producer\u2019s question: how much did we make? Smith\u2019s standard asks the consumer\u2019s: what did the work do for the people it was meant to serve?'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Work matters to **the people doing it**, as Smith knew when he worried about dulling factory jobs. And what you want this minute isn\u2019t always your interest. Don\u2019t use the test to dismiss producers.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Production is the means and consumption **the end**. Output can be maximised while the purpose stays empty. So judge your own work by **what it did** for its users.'}]}}}},1022,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'what-a-study-shows',kind:'book',promise:'See what a study shows before you trust it',about:'Have you ever believed a study you saw in the news? Most of us hear about studies from a headline. The person who wrote it may never have read the study. Six plain questions help you check. Which will you ask first?',ideas:{'what-a-study-shows_1':{title:'Small Groups Can Fool You',card:{insight:'Check how many people were in a study, and who they were.',tryThis:'Next time someone shares a study with you, find out how big the group was.',skills:['science','thinking']},review:{prompt:'A morning habit helped forty students at one school focus. What now?',right:'Treat it as a maybe until a bigger study checks it',wrong:'Change your whole morning, since a study found it',why:'Forty students at one school is too few to change your day.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Zara asks **five friends** from her football team which sport is best. All five say football. So she tells the whole school that **every kid** loves football. Would you believe her?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Two things went wrong. Five is **very few**, and luck can make small groups look odd. And they were **all footballers**, so of course they picked football. She\u2019d need lots of kids, of all sorts.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Before you believe a study, ask **how many people** were in it. Then ask if they were **like the people** the claim is about.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A headline says a simple **morning habit** makes people far more focused. Deep in the article, you find the study. It tested **forty undergraduates** at one university, for one afternoon. What does that let you conclude?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Start with **how many**. Small groups give **unstable results**. By luck alone, some will show big effects, and those are the ones that get written up. An effect found in thirty people may vanish in three hundred.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Then ask **who they were**. Students at one university aren\u2019t everyone. A result from one country, or from patients, **may not transfer**. Psychology has often drawn big claims from a narrow slice of people.'},{kind:'explain',text:'So back to the headline. Forty students, one afternoon, one campus. That\u2019s a fine reason to run **a bigger study**. It isn\u2019t a reason for you to rebuild **your mornings** around it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A striking result from a few similar people is **a starting point**, not a verdict. Find **the sample size** before you read the conclusion. If nobody says, that tells you something.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A finding lands in your feed. It came through **a headline**, written by someone who probably never read the paper. Before you share it or act on it, what\u2019s **the cheapest question** you could ask?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A sample limits a study **twice over**. Its size decides how stable the estimate is. Its make-up decides **who the result** applies to. A study can pass one test and fail the other.'},{kind:'story',text:'Flip a coin four times. Nearly one time in three, you\u2019ll get three or four heads. It looks like a pattern, but it\u2019s **just luck**. Small studies flip that coin constantly, and the lucky streaks get published.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture a huge survey of the users of one fitness app. The numbers barely wobble, because the sample is vast. But everyone in it chose to download a fitness app. It answers a question about them, not you.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Small isn\u2019t the same as wrong. Some small studies are careful, and some effects are big enough to show in few people. One small study is **weak evidence**, not none.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Ask **how many** and **who** before you read the conclusion. Size can\u2019t fix the wrong people, and the right people can\u2019t fix a tiny sample.'}]},'what-a-study-shows_2':{title:'Names From a Hat',card:{insight:'If people pick their own group, a study cannot show a cause.',tryThis:'When you hear that a habit helps, ask if the people chose it.',skills:['science','thinking']},review:{prompt:'A news story says people who eat a certain cereal live longer. What do you ask?',right:'Did they choose the cereal, or did chance pick?',wrong:'How much longer do the cereal eaters live?',why:'When people pick their own group, a study shows a link, not a cause.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Kids who join the **chess club** get better maths marks than kids who don\u2019t. So does chess **make you better** at maths? Or could something else be going on?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The kids **chose to join**. Kids who love puzzles might pick chess, and also be good at maths. To find out, put every name **in a hat**. Draw half for chess lessons, and half for none.'},{kind:'moral',text:'If people pick their own group, you **can\u2019t tell** what caused the difference. When **chance picks** the groups, puzzle lovers land in both, and you can.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A report says people who eat **a certain cereal** live longer. Nobody told them what to eat. They **chose it** themselves. Before you switch breakfasts, what\u2019s the one thing you\u2019d want to know?'},{kind:'story',text:'Think about who picks that cereal. They may differ from other people in money, exercise and smoking, long before the first bowl. Any of those could explain the longer lives. The cereal just gets the credit.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Who **decided who got what**? That one question tells you **which kind** of study you\u2019re reading.',left:{title:'Assigned by chance',items:['Researcher picks the groups','Groups differ by luck','Can show a cause']},right:{title:'People choose',items:['Participants pick for themselves','Groups differ in why','Shows an association']}},{kind:'explain',text:'This is why findings about **diet and habits** flip so often. People who do the healthy thing aren\u2019t a random group. Their differences also shape the outcome, so **the habit** takes credit that belongs to the person.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Observational studies can **suggest a cause**, but they can\u2019t prove one. So ask **who chose** the groups. If the people did, read the claim as an association.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture a careful study of **thousands of people** who chose to take a daily vitamin. They\u2019re healthier than those who didn\u2019t. The study is big and well run. Can you say the vitamin **works**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'In a randomised trial, the researcher **assigns** each person by chance. Everything else that might matter, even things nobody measured, gets **spread by chance** too. So a difference in outcome can be credited to the treatment.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a gardener testing a new feed. She **draws lots** to decide which plants get it. No plant can steer where it goes, so sickly and sturdy ones land in both beds. Now the beds can be compared.'},{kind:'story',text:'Back to the vitamin takers. They chose it, and people\u2019s reasons are bound up with money, health and habit. Researchers can adjust for what they **measured**, not for what they missed. So size and care can\u2019t turn association into cause.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Observational work is often the only ethical or practical option, and it suggests causes **worth testing**. Trials have limits too. They\u2019re often small or short, so you still ask how many, and who.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Assignment by chance is what lets a study **claim a cause**. When people chose for themselves, you\u2019ve learned **an association**. It may be a clue, but it isn\u2019t yet a cause.'}]},'what-a-study-shows_3':{title:'Compared With What',card:{insight:'A win only counts when you know what it beat.',tryThis:'Find one ad that says something works. Ask what it was compared with, and if that was fair.',skills:['science','thinking']},review:{prompt:'A new drug beat a sugar pill in its test. What should you ask next?',right:'How does it compare with the drug doctors use now?',wrong:'Did most people in the test feel better?',why:'The new drug still has to beat the one doctors use now.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'An advert says kids used **a new spelling app** for a term. They got ten more words right on their test. It sounds great. Can you spot **what\u2019s missing**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Kids learn spellings over a term **anyway**. What if kids with no app gained ten words too? Then the app added nothing. To know, you need **a group without it**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A result only means something **next to a comparison**. So when anything claims to work, ask **better than what**? Without that answer, you can\u2019t tell.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Three trials of a new medicine all report a win. One beat a sugar pill. One beat **the usual treatment**. One beat a rival given **at a weak dose**. Would you treat these as the same result?'},{kind:'explain',text:'An effect only exists **relative to something**. Beating nothing shows the medicine does something. Beating **what doctors already use** is the strong result. Beating a rival that was weakened on purpose means close to nothing.'},{kind:'explain',text:'And with **no control group** at all? You see that people improved, not why. Many complaints ease with time, and people may feel better just for **being treated**. A control shows what would have happened anyway.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A win is only as good as **what it beat**. So before you accept that something works, ask what it was **compared with**. Then ask if you\u2019d have picked that comparison yourself.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture a headline: *new drug cuts symptoms*. Every word is **accurate**. The trial was real and the numbers are right. So what **one question** would show you how much it\u2019s worth?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The comparison **decides the claim**. Beating nothing is weak. Beating **the current standard** is strong. Beating a deliberately weak rival is close to meaningless. So the first question about any effect is simple: compared with what?'},{kind:'story',text:'Back to the headline. The trial compared the new drug with an older one, given at a dose nobody uses in practice. The summary doesn\u2019t mention the dose. Nothing was faked. The bar was simply **set low**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a different trial uses a sugar pill as its control. But the real pills taste bitter, and the sugar pills don\u2019t. Patients can tell which group they\u2019re in, so the groups now differ in **what they believe**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Sometimes beating nothing is **the honest test**: where no treatment exists, a placebo is the right comparison. The fault isn\u2019t a weak rival as such. It\u2019s one chosen to flatter, then left out.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Every effect is measured **against something**. Find out what, and whether it was credible. Then ask if you\u2019d have chosen **that comparison** yourself, before you saw the result.'}]},'what-a-study-shows_4':{title:'Real but Too Small',card:{insight:'A result can be real and still far too small to matter.',tryThis:'When the news shows a risk, ask how many in a hundred get it.',skills:['science','thinking']},review:{prompt:'A food doubles the risk of a rare illness. What do you want to know?',right:'How many get it out of a million',wrong:'If the result is real or just luck',why:'Doubled can still be tiny, so ask for the real count.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A study finds that **new trainers** make kids run faster. Scientists call the result *significant*. That sounds like a lot. So how much faster do you think they **actually run**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'One tenth of a second, over a **long race**. It\u2019s real, but nobody would notice. To scientists, *significant* means it probably isn\u2019t luck. It doesn\u2019t mean **big**. Ask how much, in things you can picture.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A result can be **real but tiny**. So always turn it into something you could **notice**: minutes, marks, or kids out of a hundred.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A press release says **a common food** doubles the risk of a rare illness. The finding is statistically significant. Both claims are true. Should you **stop eating** it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Start with **significant**. It means the effect is probably not zero. It says **nothing about size**. With a big enough sample, even a trivially small effect comes out significant. The word answers whether it\u2019s there, not whether it matters.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The same result, told **two ways**. Both are true, but only one helps **you decide**.',left:{title:'Relative',items:['Risk doubles','Sounds alarming','The press release\u2019s pick']},right:{title:'Absolute',items:['One to two per million people','Sounds like what it is','What you actually weigh']}},{kind:'explain',text:'So ask how big, in units **you can picture**: minutes, pounds, percentage points, people out of a hundred. If the risk goes from one in a million to two, it\u2019s doubled. It\u2019s also **barely there**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Significant means **probably real**, not big. Before you act on a finding, translate it into **a unit** you\u2019d notice in your own week. Then decide if it\u2019s worth it.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A trial with **a huge sample** reports a statistically significant benefit. The abstract says so twice. Nowhere does it say **how big** the benefit is. What would you need to see before it changed anything you do?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Significance says an effect is **probably not zero**. The more people a study includes, the more precisely it detects a difference. So a big enough sample flags even a trivial effect. **Precision** about a tiny effect is still a tiny effect.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a press release about a common food. It reports a doubled risk of a rare illness: one in a million becomes two. Both figures are true. Only one helps you, and the release leads with the other.'},{kind:'story',text:'Back to the huge trial. Dig out the number, and the benefit might be a fraction of a point on some scale. It\u2019s real and precisely measured. You\u2019d still never notice it in your own week.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Small isn\u2019t always negligible. A tiny effect across **a whole population** can add up to many people. So a health body may care about what you can ignore. And an absolute figure needs its baseline too.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Significance asks **is it there**. Size asks **does it matter**. Ask for both numbers, relative and absolute, in units you\u2019d notice, before you let a finding move you.'}]},'what-a-study-shows_5':{title:'Wait for a Second Team',card:{insight:'Trust one study a bit, and trust it more when others find it too.',tryThis:'Pick a finding you often repeat. Look up whether a second team has found it too.',skills:['science','thinking']},review:{prompt:'A famous study in a best-selling book was never done again. How much should it count?',right:'A little, until a second team finds it too',wrong:'A lot, since it is famous and well written',why:'One study should move you just a bit, even a famous one.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your friend uses **a new pencil** in a test and gets top marks. Now she calls it her **lucky pencil**. She wants to use it in every test from now on. Is she right?'},{kind:'explain',text:'One good test could be **plain luck**. If the pencil works again and again, for lots of different kids, that\u2019s worth noticing. Scientists check results **the same way**: other people do the test again.'},{kind:'moral',text:'One result on its own is **a maybe**, not a fact. You can trust it more when **other people** test it again and find the same thing.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Five labs run **the same experiment**. At one bench, the flask turns orange. At the other four, **nothing happens**. Guess which result makes the news. So what should you make of it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A single result is **a hypothesis** with evidence attached. When researchers set out to repeat published findings carefully, famous ones included, **a large share** didn\u2019t hold up. So one study should shift your beliefs only a little.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Usually **nobody cheated**. Journals favour new, positive findings. Flexible analysis offers many paths to a significant result. Results that find nothing are **hard to publish**. Each nudge is small, but together they tilt the record.'},{kind:'explain',text:'So when a striking finding reaches you, ask if **another team** finds it again. Best of all is a team that would have been **pleased to find nothing**. If even they find it, it\u2019s starting to earn trust.'},{kind:'moral',text:'One result is where checking begins, **not where it ends**. Keep your real confidence for findings that **other teams** have found again.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A famous finding sits in **bestselling books** and on posters in offices. It\u2019s been retold for years. Then a careful team tries to repeat it, and finds **nothing at all**. What did the original actually show?'},{kind:'explain',text:'**Replication** is what turns a result into a finding. A single study is a result. It should move your beliefs only slightly. When famous findings were retested with care, **a substantial fraction** failed to reproduce.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture the original lab. The team tried a few outcomes and a few subgroups, and dropped some odd cases. Each choice was defensible. One path reached significance, and that\u2019s the one they wrote up. Nobody lied.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture five teams running one experiment on five benches. One flask turns and four don\u2019t. The four null results sit in drawers, hard to publish. The one that turned is what you read about.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'So where should **your weight** go when you meet **a new claim**?',left:{title:'Weight lightly',items:['One striking paper','A famous name on it','A great story']},right:{title:'Weight heavily',items:['Meta-analyses','Pre-registered replications','Teams with no stake']}},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Replication isn\u2019t a magic seal. A meta-analysis of biased studies **inherits their bias**, and a failed repeat can be too small itself. Some decisions can\u2019t wait, and then one good study is the best you have.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Treat a lone study as **a hypothesis**, and let it move you a little. Save real confidence for results found again by people **with no stake** in them.'}]},'what-a-study-shows_6':{title:'Who Paid for It',card:{insight:'Who paid for a study shows how closely to check it.',tryThis:'Next time a study backs a product, find who paid for it. Then check how it was done.',skills:['science','thinking']},review:{prompt:'A drug company\u2019s own test says its drug works well. What is your first step?',right:'Check how it was done and what it beat',wrong:'Throw it out, since the company paid for the test',why:'Who paid tells you how closely to check, not whether it is true.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'A crisp company runs **a taste test**. It says kids like its crisps best. But the company wants **you to buy** them. Does that mean the test is wrong?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Not by itself. But it\u2019s **a reason to look**. Nobody has to lie. The company could pick a rival crisp nobody likes, or share only the tests that went well. So ask **how they tested**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When someone gains if you believe a result, **look harder** at it. Don\u2019t just swallow it, and **don\u2019t just bin it** either.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A study says a new snack bar **boosts energy**. At the bottom of the paper, in small print, you find the funder: the company that **sells the bar**. What do you do with that?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Funding shapes research **before any paper** is written, by deciding what gets studied. It also tracks results. Industry-funded trials report **favourable outcomes** more often than independent ones on the same questions.'},{kind:'explain',text:'That doesn\u2019t mean anyone **faked anything**. The tilt runs through **ordinary choices**: the design, the comparison, and how the outcome is defined. Then there\u2019s what never gets published. Each is defensible on its own.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'There are **two opposite mistakes** here, and both feel like being **sensible**.',left:{title:'Ignoring the funder',items:['Funding treated as irrelevant','A friendly design goes unchecked']},right:{title:'Dismissing on funding',items:['Funding treated as proof','Sound work thrown out unread']}},{kind:'moral',text:'A conflict of interest is **a prompt**, not a verdict. When a finding favours its funder, **look harder** at the design and the comparison. Don\u2019t reject it unread.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'At a market stall, the seller **weighs your apples** on her own scale. She\u2019s probably honest. You still glance at the public scale by the gate. Why? And what does that have to do with **reading research**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Asking **who benefits** isn\u2019t cynicism. It\u2019s a question about **which studies exist**. Funding decides what gets investigated in the first place. So before you read any single paper, interest has already shaped what there is to read.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a trial paid for by the maker of a new drug. Nothing is invented. But the rival chosen is weak, and the outcome is defined generously. An earlier trial that went badly was never published.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture someone who dislikes a finding. They spot the funder and dismiss the study unread. That feels like scepticism, but it\u2019s the same mistake in reverse. **Funding alone** proves nothing either way.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Interest isn\u2019t only money. Researchers have careers, reputations and **favourite theories**, and independent funders have agendas too. Independent isn\u2019t the same as disinterested, so no study escapes the question.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Treat interest as **a prior**, not a verdict. A finding that favours its funder earns **a closer look** at its design. Then weigh it on a scale nobody at the stall owns.'}]}}}},1023,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'what-changed-my-mind',kind:'book',promise:'Help people change their minds without a fight',about:'Why does a good argument so rarely change a mind right away? This short essay from the Mindroll team looks at how minds change. Most change later, when people are alone. A person they respect can help too. What would change yours?',ideas:{'what-changed-my-mind_1':{title:'Plant It and Wait',card:{insight:'People rarely change their minds during an argument, but some do later, alone.',tryThis:'Next time you disagree, make your best point once. Then stop and give them time.',skills:['communication','psychology']},review:{prompt:'A coworker turns down your idea in a meeting. What should you do next?',right:'Make your point once, then let it work later',wrong:'Keep pushing until they admit you are right',why:'People change their minds later and alone, so one clear point is enough.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You and your friend argue about **a film**. You think it\u2019s great. She thinks it\u2019s boring. You both dig in, and you go home **sure you\u2019re right**. Is that the end of it?'},{kind:'story',text:'A week later, alone in bed, you think about what she said. One of her points was quite good. Maybe the middle really was slow. You don\u2019t text her. You just think a little differently now.'},{kind:'moral',text:'People hardly ever change their minds **mid-argument**. Giving way then feels like losing. The change comes **later, on their own**, so say your point kindly and let it go.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You argue with a relative for an hour. Your points are good. They concede **nothing at all**. You drive home sure it was **a waste of time**. Was it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Changing your position in front of the person arguing **costs twice**. You give up the point, and you\u2019re seen to **lose the exchange**. Alone, only the first cost is left. So people defend at the table and reconsider in the car.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'It changes what an argument is for. You can **harvest** or you can **plant**.',left:{title:'Harvesting',items:['Wants a visible yes','Reads silence as failure','Pushes harder']},right:{title:'Planting',items:['Leaves something to think about','Expects silence at the time','Stops once the point\u2019s made']}},{kind:'moral',text:'No concession in the room tells you **very little**. The rethink, if it comes, comes later and alone. Make **your point once**, then leave it to work.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Think of a belief you\u2019ve actually dropped. Where were you when it went? Probably not **mid-argument**, facing someone who was pushing you. Is that **the usual shape** of a change of mind?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The position is defended at the time, then shifts weeks or months later, alone. Conceding in front of an opponent gives up the point **and the exchange**. The second is **a social loss** with a witness, and it\u2019s the expensive one.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a manager pitching a new process to a sceptical team. Nobody budges in the meeting, so she pushes harder. The room digs in. She concludes argument never works, and the private rethink grows less likely.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a friend who says one careful thing about your diet, then changes the subject. You bristle at the time. Three weeks later, alone at the shop, you find yourself thinking about it.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. It\u2019s a tendency, not a law: some people change on the spot when little is at stake. And planting has **its own trap**. Silence isn\u2019t proof your point was any good.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Judge a conversation by **what it planted**, not by the concession you got. Expect **no signal** at the time. The real change, if it comes, happens without an audience.'}]},'what-changed-my-mind_2':{title:'A Person You Respect',card:{insight:'People often change their minds because someone they respect holds the other view.',tryThis:'Write down one view you laugh at. Then ask if anyone you respect holds it.',skills:['psychology','thinking']},review:{prompt:'You want a doubtful friend to take your view seriously. What helps most?',right:'Being someone they respect who holds the view',wrong:'Sending them the strongest argument you can find',why:'When someone they respect holds a view, they cannot write it off as silly.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You think **chess is dull**, and only dull kids play it. Your mum explains why it\u2019s fun. Your teacher gives you a list of reasons. Nothing changes. What would actually **change your mind**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Then you find out that Jas, the funniest kid in your class, plays chess every lunchtime. Nobody gave you a new reason. But you can\u2019t keep telling yourself that only dull kids play.'},{kind:'moral',text:'People often change their minds because of **a person**, not a reason. When someone you like thinks the other way, you can\u2019t say only silly people do. So you **look again**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve long assumed people who home-school their children are **cranks**. Then you learn a colleague you admire does exactly that. Nobody has argued with you. Yet **something has shifted**. Why?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Most opposing views are never really considered. We file them as **what fools believe**, or what spiteful people believe. A respected person holding the view **breaks the filing**. They don\u2019t bring a better argument. They make the view worth thinking about.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'That\u2019s why **contact** can matter more than **debate**.',left:{title:'Being right loudly',items:['A clear public argument','Easy to file away','A weak intervention']},right:{title:'Being present',items:['Patient and plainly not contemptible','Hard to dismiss','A strong intervention']}},{kind:'moral',text:'A shifted opinion often has **a relationship** behind it, not new evidence. So being right in public moves people less than you\u2019d hope. Being **someone they respect** moves them more.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture someone who\u2019s dropped a belief they held for years. You ask what did it. You\u2019re expecting **a killer fact** or a better argument. What if **the answer** is a name?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The essay\u2019s claim is about **a filter** that runs before any argument. Opposing views are sorted in advance as what stupid or malicious people think. Arguments rarely get past that sorting. A **respected holder** does, because the sorting has nowhere to put them.'},{kind:'story',text:'Think of a man sure that people who go vegetarian are preachy. Then his brother-in-law, whom he likes, quietly stops eating meat. There\u2019s no lecture. But now the label has to cover someone he likes.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a public health team trying to reach sceptics about a safety measure. More data and louder warnings get filed away. A trusted local figure who backs it may get a hearing the leaflets never did.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Respect is a reason to take a view seriously, not to believe it. The same route carries **bad ideas** through admired people just as easily. And it can stop halfway, with the filter broken but nothing examined.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Being clearly and publicly right is **a weak intervention**. Being present, patient and plainly not contemptible is **a strong one**. Let the relationship do what your argument can\u2019t.'}]},'what-changed-my-mind_3':{title:'When Mocking Backfires',card:{insight:'When you mock someone, the fight becomes about them, so they dig in.',tryThis:'Before you correct someone, ask if they could agree without looking like a fool.',skills:['communication','psychology']},review:{prompt:'A friend shares a false fact in the group chat. What will change her mind?',right:'A kind, private message that makes agreeing easy',wrong:'A public correction so everyone sees the facts',why:'Mocking and a crowd make backing down feel like losing, so keep it private.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Sam says a whale is **a fish**. Leo laughs and calls him dumb. The others laugh too. Sam goes red and says it again, louder. Why is Sam now **even more sure**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Leo was right about whales. But he turned **a question about whales** into a question about Sam. Is Sam dumb? Sam will fight that one hard. Giving way now feels like agreeing **he\u2019s dumb**, so he won\u2019t. Would you?'},{kind:'moral',text:'Calling someone stupid makes them **hold on tighter**. If you want someone to change, make it **easy to change**. A quiet, kind word beats a laugh in front of everyone.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You see a post repeating a claim you know is false. You reply with **the facts**, plus a line about how anyone could believe this. Your reply does well. So why does the poster **dig in**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Contempt changes the question. It was *is this true?* Now it\u2019s **am I a fool**? People will reconsider the first. They defend the second far harder, because dropping the belief now feels like **dropping themselves**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'An **audience** makes it worse. Witnesses raise the stakes for who the person is, which is exactly what you need low. Backing down in private is a change of mind. Backing down in public is **a defeat**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The scornful version of **a correct argument** can be worse than silence. It hardens the belief and adds a reason to distrust you. If you want someone to move, **make it cheap**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'You can probably recall **an encounter** that hardened you. Someone called you stupid for believing something. Did you drop the belief? Or did you hold on harder, and **trust them** less afterwards?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The mechanism is **identity protection**. An attack on a belief that also judges the believer changes the question. It stops being what\u2019s true and becomes **what kind of person** you are. People defend the second far harder than the first.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a colleague who corrects a teammate\u2019s mistake in a team meeting, with a sigh and an eye-roll. The facts are right. The teammate defends the error for weeks, and starts double-checking everything that colleague says.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a father whose teenage son believes a dubious fitness claim. He doesn\u2019t mock it in front of the family. He asks about it on a drive, just the two of them. Now agreeing costs the son far less.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some views deserve no respect, and nobody\u2019s saying error must be flattered. The claim is narrower: scorn rarely moves **the person scorned**. Onlookers are a separate audience, and a public correction may still inform them.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Firmness is fine. **Contempt** and an audience are what backfire. Keep the facts as hard as they are, but make it **cheap to move**.'}]},'what-changed-my-mind_4':{title:'Give Them a Way Out',card:{insight:'People change more easily when they can keep their reasons and drop the old view.',tryThis:'When someone starts to come around, say what they were right about first.',skills:['communication','relationships']},review:{prompt:'A friend fought a new bike lane over traffic jams. The jams were small. Your reply?',right:'You were right about the risk, just wrong about its size',wrong:'So you admit you were wrong about the whole thing',why:'A way out that keeps their reasons makes changing easier than holding on.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Ben said your team should play with **five defenders**, to be safe. It went badly. You could say, *you were wrong.* Or you could say something else. Which helps Ben **change his plan**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Try this instead: you were right that we needed to be safe, but five was just too many. Now Ben keeps his good reason. He only has to drop the number, and that\u2019s a much smaller step.'},{kind:'moral',text:'People find it easier to change when they **don\u2019t have to say** they were wrong. Find the part they **got right**, and say it first. Then talk about the rest.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A manager resisted **remote working**, fearing it would hurt communication. Six months in, communication is mostly fine. You could tell her she was wrong. Is that **the reply** that helps her move?'},{kind:'explain',text:'What a person changing their mind needs is **somewhere to go**. That\u2019s a version of the new view that lets them keep their reasons and drop **only the conclusion**. Right about the risk, wrong about its size, is a step they can take.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Demanding **an admission** first changes the price of **changing your mind**.',left:{title:'Closing the exit',items:['Admit you were wrong first','Changing costs too much','The belief survives']},right:{title:'Supplying the exit',items:['Keep your reasons, drop the conclusion','Change becomes a small step','The belief can go']}},{kind:'explain',text:'The exits are familiar. **New information** that wasn\u2019t available before. **Changed circumstances**, so the old view was reasonable then and wrong now. Or a reframing, where they were right about what they cared about most.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t demand a confession before you accept the change. That makes changing cost more than **keeping the belief**. Supply **the exit** instead: you were right about the risk, wrong about its size.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Watch someone start to come round to your view. You feel the urge to say it: *so you admit you were wrong.* It\u2019s satisfying. But what does **that demand** do to **their next step**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Most beliefs change **gradually**, with the earlier position still defensible on what was known. So people need a route that doesn\u2019t require **admitting they were wrong**. Demanding a confession misdescribes the change.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a neighbour who said the new cycle lane would cause jams. A year on, the jams have been minor. One reply rubs it in. Another says she was right to worry about traffic, and the effect was smaller.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a doctor who advised against a treatment, then sees new trial results. She can switch without disgrace, because the information wasn\u2019t there before. Her old advice was reasonable then. It\u2019s just out of date now.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Exits can be abused. A reframing that keeps a false claim under **a new name** isn\u2019t a change of mind at all. And when an old view did real harm, an exit shouldn\u2019t quietly cancel an apology.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Demanding an admission makes **the change** cost more than the belief. That\u2019s a reliable way to keep the belief alive. So **supply the exit**, and let it save their reasons, not excuse the harm.'}]},'what-changed-my-mind_5':{title:'Let Them Find It',card:{insight:'People trust what they find out themselves more than what they are told.',tryThis:'Take one claim you want someone to accept. Turn it into a question they can check.',skills:['communication','thinking']},review:{prompt:'A coworker says a weekly meeting is a must. You disagree. What do you try?',right:'Suggest a month without it so they can see for themselves',wrong:'Show them the numbers that prove it wastes time',why:'When people find the answer themselves, they have nothing to doubt or give up.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your friend says the pool **isn\u2019t cold** at all. You don\u2019t believe her. She could show you a chart. She could promise. What would **actually convince** you?'},{kind:'story',text:'You dip your hand in the water. It\u2019s warm. Nobody had to win an argument, and you don\u2019t have to say anyone else was right. You just know now, because you checked it yourself.'},{kind:'moral',text:'What changes minds most is what people **find out themselves**. So instead of telling someone, **ask a question** that helps them check.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your partner is sure the weekly takeaway comes to **about \xa320**. You think it\u2019s nearer \xa350. You could show them the bank statement. Is that the move most likely to **change their mind**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Instead you ask what they reckon it came to last month, and suggest looking together. The figures are the same either way. But this time they find the total themselves, and nobody has to concede anything.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'**Same figures**, different owner. That\u2019s the whole difference between **telling** and asking.',left:{title:'A statement',items:['Has a source to suspect','Accepting it concedes to you','Easy to reject']},right:{title:'A question',items:['Leads to their own answer','Nothing to concede to anyone','They own the conclusion']}},{kind:'explain',text:'A conclusion you reached yourself has **no source** to be suspicious of. Defending it concedes nothing to anyone. So the two usual obstacles, distrust of the messenger and **the cost of losing**, simply aren\u2019t there. People can see for themselves.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The strongest evidence is **their own**. So don\u2019t hand someone the argument. **Offer the experiment**, and let them run it.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Again and again, the same obstacle turns up: changing your mind **costs something** in front of someone. So what happens if you take **the someone** away? What kind of evidence is left to move you?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The essay\u2019s answer: what moves people is usually **something they saw** personally, not something they were shown. That\u2019s why **questions** often beat assertions. A question about their own case produces a conclusion they own. A statement produces a claim to reject.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture a dad who thinks the new route to school is slower. His daughter doesn\u2019t argue. She suggests they time both routes this week. If he times it himself, he\u2019ll believe the clock.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a team lead convinced a weekly meeting is essential. A colleague suggests a month without it, then a look back. The lead might find it was wasted time. Or might find it mattered. Either way, the verdict\u2019s theirs.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. It\u2019s slow, and it **can\u2019t be aimed**. You can set up the conditions, but you can\u2019t decide what they\u2019ll conclude. A question built to allow one answer is just a statement in disguise.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The persuader\u2019s job shrinks to **setting up conditions**. You give up control over the answer. That loss is **the price** of the method working at all.'}]}}}},1024,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'what-friendship-studies-found',kind:'book',promise:'See what long studies say about friends',about:'How many friends do you need to be happy and healthy? Some studies have followed the same people for many years. Here is what they found, and what they cannot tell us. Who could you call for help at night?',ideas:{'what-friendship-studies-found_1':{title:'One Close Friend',card:{insight:'One friend you can count on seems to matter more than many.',tryThis:'Write the name of one friend you could count on. Then plan a lunch with just them this week.',skills:['relationships','health']},review:{prompt:'Your friend just moved and plans to join five clubs. What helps most?',right:'Find one friend to count on, not a crowd',wrong:'Join all five, since more people means more help',why:'One friend you can count on helps more than a big crowd.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Ava has **thirty friends** online. Sam has two, and one of them would help him with anything, any time. Who do you think has **more of what matters**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'One famous study followed a group of men for most of their lives. Its leader said one thing stood out. Happiness with **close relationships** at fifty told you more about **health at eighty** than cholesterol did.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Having **one person** you can really count on seems to matter more than **lots of friends**. These studies watch people, so we can\u2019t be sure what causes what. But the pattern is worth knowing.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two people move to a new city. Lena joins five clubs and fills her diary. Omar has fewer contacts but **one friend** he could rely on in a crisis. By **the usual count**, Lena wins. Does she?'},{kind:'story',text:'The Harvard Study of Adult Development followed the same men for decades. Its director has reported that satisfaction with relationships at fifty predicted health at eighty better than cholesterol did. The variable was satisfaction, not count.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'So the real question is **where your effort goes**, and most of us **spread it thin**.',left:{title:'A wide network',items:['Many contacts kept warm','Effort spread thin','Judged by how many']},right:{title:'A few, deeper',items:['Someone to rely on','Effort concentrated','Judged by reliance']}},{kind:'explain',text:'One caution. These are **observational findings**. People in better health may simply keep up better relationships, so which causes which **isn\u2019t settled**. The pattern recurs across studies, though the evidence isn\u2019t uniform.'},{kind:'moral',text:'One person you could rely on in a crisis seems to count for more than **a wide network**. So put **your effort** into deepening a few ties, not keeping many warm.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Social health usually gets measured by **counting**: friends, contacts, events a month. The long cohort studies suggest you might be counting **the wrong thing**. So what should you be measuring instead?'},{kind:'explain',text:'In much of the literature, the **quality** of close relationships predicts health and wellbeing better than **their number**. Whether someone reports a person they could rely on in a crisis tends to predict more than network size.'},{kind:'story',text:'The best-known case is the Harvard Study of Adult Development, which followed the same men for decades. Its director has reported that relationship satisfaction at fifty beat cholesterol as a predictor of health at eighty.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Omar, newly moved, with a choice of how to spend his evenings. He could keep fifty contacts warm with likes and quick replies. Or he could call **one friend** properly, every week. The research hints that the second returns more.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. These are **observational findings**. People in better health may sustain better relationships, so health could produce the satisfaction. And a director\u2019s summary of one cohort of men isn\u2019t a measured effect for everyone.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Stop judging **your social life** by its size. Ask whether there\u2019s someone you could rely on. The pattern is real, even if **its cause** isn\u2019t settled.'}]},'what-friendship-studies-found_2':{title:'Which Came First',card:{insight:'Being sick can leave you alone, and being alone can make you sick.',tryThis:'When you read that being alone makes you sick, ask if being sick came first.',skills:['thinking','health']},review:{prompt:'A headline says making friends adds a set number of years. What do you think?',right:'The link is real, but the exact number claims too much',wrong:'It came from a study, so the number must be right',why:'The link is real, but no study that just watches people can be that sure.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Rosa was ill for a **whole term**. She missed school and saw her friends less. Then she felt lonely, which made her feel worse. If you met her at the end, could you tell **which came first**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Being alone goes with **worse health**. But being ill also makes people more alone. Being short of money can push on both at once. So the arrow points **both ways**, and nobody can fully pull them apart.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When you hear that being alone **makes you ill**, don\u2019t forget the arrow. The link is real, but it runs **both ways**. A simple story sounds surer than the studies are.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A headline says isolation raises your risk of illness by **an exact percentage**. Behind it is a study that watched one group of people over time. The number travels well. Does **the study\u2019s design** travel with it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s the tangle. Isolation predicts **worse health**. Poor health also produces isolation. And income, mobility and depression predict **both of them**. A study that only watches people can\u2019t assign anyone to be isolated, so it can\u2019t fully separate these.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Studies **adjust** for the factors they measure, as far as they can. That removes some of the tangle, not all. Anything measured badly, or **not measured**, stays inside the result. So an exact causal figure claims more than the design supports.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'None of this means **the link** is fake. You can hold **both columns** at once.',left:{title:'Why it stands',items:['Effects survive adjustment','The mechanisms are plausible']},right:{title:'Why numbers wobble',items:['Illness can cause isolation','Shared causes push on both']}},{kind:'moral',text:'The link between isolation and health is **real**. But the arrow runs both ways, so treat **any clean figure** with suspicion. You\u2019re hearing more confidence than the design can give.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'The long studies on connection and health share **one problem**. Nobody can assign people to be isolated for twenty years and wait. So the studies watch, measure and adjust. What can that design **never quite tell you**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'**The direction** of cause. Isolation predicts worse health, poor health produces isolation, and income, mobility and depression predict both. That third kind of factor is called **confounding**. Adjustment removes the parts that were measured, and leaves the rest.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Mr Hale, who loses his job at sixty. Money gets tight, he drives less, and he stops seeing friends. His health slips too. A study sees an isolated man in poor health. It can\u2019t see that **one cause** drove both.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a colleague who quotes a study on isolation and health. She says making friends adds a set number of years to your life. The study found a real link. That neat figure is more confidence than its design supports.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Used carelessly, the arrow argument dismisses every observational finding, and **much health knowledge** rests on them. Here the effects survive adjustment and the mechanisms are plausible. The doubt targets the precision, not the existence.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Treat the link as **real** and the circulating numbers with **suspicion**. When you meet a clean causal figure, look for the arrow back. You\u2019ll usually find it.'}]},'what-friendship-studies-found_3':{title:'Lonely in a Full Room',card:{insight:'You can see lots of people and still feel lonely.',tryThis:'Tonight, tell one close friend how you felt this week, not just what you did.',skills:['relationships','psychology']},review:{prompt:'A nursing home adds a weekly group, but one woman stays lonely. Why?',right:'It gave her more people, but she still did not feel known',wrong:'The group was too small to give her enough company',why:'She has lots of company, but she needs someone to know her.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'The party is **packed**. Music, games, your whole class. You\u2019re right in the middle of it all. And you feel **completely on your own**. How can that be?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Being **alone** is about how many people you see. You can count it. Feeling **lonely** is how you feel inside, when you want more closeness than you have. You can be in a crowd and lonely, or on your own and fine.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Lonely and alone **aren\u2019t the same**. So adding more people doesn\u2019t always help a lonely friend. What helps more is feeling that someone **really knows you**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Mia has drinks with friends twice a week, a close team at work and family nearby. She tells you she\u2019s **lonely**. Your first thought is that it **can\u2019t be true**. Is it?'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'It can be true, because these are **two different variables**, measured separately and only **moderately linked**.',left:{title:'Isolation',items:['Seen from outside','How much contact you have']},right:{title:'Loneliness',items:['Felt from inside','The gap between had and wanted']}},{kind:'explain',text:'So Mia\u2019s contact is high, while her sense of **being known** isn\u2019t. Both variables predict poor health, somewhat independently. That suggests they work in **different ways**, and neither can stand in for the other.'},{kind:'explain',text:'That\u2019s why adding contact doesn\u2019t reliably fix loneliness. A scheme can add time with people without adding **the sense** of being known. Then it moves one variable and not the other. Programmes that only add contact have tended to **underperform**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Isolation is a count. Loneliness is **a felt gap**. When someone with a full diary says they\u2019re lonely, believe them, and ask **what\u2019s missing**, not who.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A council launches a loneliness scheme. Volunteers make **hundreds of visits**, and the council reports the scheme a success. Then someone asks the residents how lonely they feel. Why might **the answer** be disappointing?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Because isolation and loneliness are measured separately and only **moderately correlated**. Isolation is objective: how much contact you have. Loneliness is subjective: the gap between **the contact you have** and the contact you want.'},{kind:'story',text:'The council counted visits, which moves isolation. It never measured whether residents felt known. So it reported one variable under the other\u2019s name. The visits may have been kind and still left the loneliness untouched.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Arthur, a widower who sees almost nobody. He walks, reads and writes long letters to a brother abroad. By any count he\u2019s isolated. Ask him, and he says he isn\u2019t lonely. The variables have **come apart**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The two do correlate, if only moderately, so for many people more contact and **less loneliness** go together. A felt measure also depends on expectations. And the claim is that contact-only programmes tend to underperform, not that contact never helps.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Both predict poor health, so **both matter**. But if you want to help loneliness, measure the felt gap, not the visits. **Being known** is the variable.'}]},'what-friendship-studies-found_4':{title:'Friendship on a Schedule',card:{insight:'Friends fade when you stop seeing them each day, so plan time to meet.',tryThis:'Name two friends you lost touch with after a move or new job. Pick a day to call each.',skills:['relationships','habits']},review:{prompt:'A close friend moves far away. What gives your friendship the best chance?',right:'Plan a set time to talk instead of waiting',wrong:'Trust that a real friendship will last on its own',why:'When you stop seeing a friend each day, you have to plan to talk.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'At school you saw Ben **every day** without trying. Then he moved to a new school. You both meant to stay friends. A few months later, you **hardly ever** talk. What went wrong?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Nothing went wrong between you. **School** was doing the work: it put you in the same room every day. When that stopped, nothing kept you in touch. It\u2019s like a plant that wilts because **nobody watered it**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Friendships fade when you stop being in touch, and that\u2019s **nobody\u2019s fault**. So when life moves you apart, keep in touch **on purpose**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You and a colleague were close for **years**. Then one of you changed jobs. You both promised to keep in touch. Two years on there have been three messages, all about birthdays. **What happened**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Adult friendships **decay without maintenance**, and the decay is the default, not a failure. How often you\u2019re in touch is one of **the better predictors** of whether it survives a move.'},{kind:'story',text:'The office was doing the work. It put you both by the kettle every morning, so contact happened without either of you deciding. When the job changed, that structure went. Nothing in the friendship broke. The thing holding it up left.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Studies of network turnover find **substantial replacement** over several years, driven largely by **shared context** changing.',left:{title:'Spontaneous contact',items:['Needs a shared place','Lost with a move','Feels natural']},right:{title:'Scheduled contact',items:['Not tied to a place','More dependable','Feels unromantic']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Spontaneous contact is exactly what a move removes. So don\u2019t wait for it to happen. Schedule **regular contact**, and treat it as **maintenance**, not a test of the friendship.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Think back to your closest friends at school or university. You didn\u2019t schedule them. You didn\u2019t need to. So why does keeping **adult friends** feel like **so much effort**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Because **the structure** was doing the work. A school, an office or a street produced contact as **a by-product**, with nobody arranging it. Studies of network turnover find substantial replacement over several years, driven largely by changes in that shared context.'},{kind:'story',text:'Kofi and Dan sat together at school for six years. Then they went to different universities. Each meant to stay close. Without the shared classroom, their messages thinned out within a term. The friendship didn\u2019t go bad. The classroom just stopped.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Hana, who moved for work and kept **two old friends**. She didn\u2019t wait to feel like calling. She put a recurring call in her diary with each of them. It felt unromantic. It also kept happening.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. That contact predicts survival doesn\u2019t prove it **causes** it: people who care more may just keep in touch more. Some friendships lapse for good reasons. And a reminder can produce contact without closeness.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Adult friendship is closer to **a maintained system** than a stable state. Since spontaneous contact is what the loss of shared context removes, the replacement has to be **deliberate**.'}]},'what-friendship-studies-found_5':{title:'The 3 a.m. Question',card:{insight:'Knowing who you could call for help at night says more than counting friends.',tryThis:'Write the name of one person you could call for help at night. If you pause, plan a call.',skills:['relationships','psychology']},review:{prompt:'Your friend cannot name anyone she could call for help at night. What does it mean?',right:'She has a gap to fix, so she should plan a call',wrong:'It means nothing, since no one makes that call',why:'You may never make the call, but no name shows a gap you can fix.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Imagine something goes **really wrong** in the middle of the night. You\u2019re scared and you need help. Who could you call? Does **a name** come to you straight away?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Counting friends is **easy to fake**. You can add anyone to the list. But this question is hard to fool yourself with. Either a name comes straight away, or it doesn\u2019t. **Nobody** has to make the call.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The best question about friends is simple: **who could you call**? If a name comes quickly, that\u2019s good news. If it doesn\u2019t, that\u2019s **worth knowing** too.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You could count your friends, your followers, your **busy weekends**. Or you could ask one question: is there someone you could call **at 3am**? Which tells you more?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Researchers on social support sometimes ask a version of that question. It works because it\u2019s **unambiguous**. A count of friends can be padded, and a full diary can hide a lot. This answer **can\u2019t be padded**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'It tests **three things** at once. Is someone close enough that you could bother them? Would the call be welcome? Are they actually around? If any one fails, **the name** doesn\u2019t come.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Leah, who has a busy social life. She tries the question and stalls. She thinks of three names and doubts each one. Almost nobody makes the call. But that **hesitation** is **the finding**, and it\u2019s actionable.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You don\u2019t need to make the call. You need to know **whether you could**, without hesitating. If you can\u2019t name someone, that\u2019s **the result**, and you can act on it.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'These ideas began with studies quoted constantly and read rarely. After the doubts about cause and measurement, what can **one person** honestly take from them? The answer fits on **a single card**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'It\u2019s a question sometimes used in social support research. Is there someone **you could call** in the middle of the night? It\u2019s **diagnostic, not literal**. Almost nobody makes the call. What matters is naming someone without hesitating.'},{kind:'story',text:'Leah has a full diary and dozens of friends. She tries the question and stalls between three names, unsure any would welcome the call. Her count looks healthy. Her answer shows where the gap is.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Tariq, who sees few people since retiring. He answers at once: his old neighbour, who\u2019d drive over in his slippers. By the count, Tariq looks thin on support. By the question, he\u2019s **covered**.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The question is used in research, but nothing here says **how well it predicts**. A confident answer may misjudge the welcome. And someone with no single name may have real support spread across several people.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A count of friends can be padded. **Your answer** to the 3am question can\u2019t. Treat any hesitation as **a prompt**, not a verdict, and act on it.'}]}}}},1025,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'what-predicts-saving',kind:'book',promise:'Find out which saving tricks really work',about:'Why do so many saving tips sound right but do so little? The Mindroll team looked at ten years of studies on saving. One trick worked again and again. Others did less than people hoped. Which ones do you use?',ideas:{'what-predicts-saving_1':{title:'Saving by Doing Nothing',card:{insight:'If saving runs on its own, you still save when you do nothing.',tryThis:'Set your bank to put money into savings each payday. Pick an amount small enough to keep.',skills:['money','habits']},review:{prompt:'You keep forgetting to put money into savings each month. What do you change?',right:'Set your bank to save each payday by itself',wrong:'Set a phone reminder to do it yourself each month',why:'Anything that needs a new choice each month will get skipped one day.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Priya must **decide each week** to put pocket money in her money box. Tom\u2019s dad puts some aside for him first, unless Tom says no. Who do you think **saves more**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Most likely Tom. Deciding takes **effort**, so some weeks Priya forgets or doesn\u2019t bother. Tom doesn\u2019t have to do anything. When grown-ups\u2019 pensions switched to this, **far more people** saved, and nobody was talked into it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Set saving up **once**, so it happens by itself. Then even doing nothing means **you save**. Few money tricks have better tests behind them.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Every month you **mean to** move money into savings. Some months you do. Then the car needs a tyre, or payday slips by. At year\u2019s end, the account holds **far less** than you planned.'},{kind:'story',text:'Pension schemes once made workers opt in. Then many flipped the default, so workers were enrolled unless they opted out. Participation rose **dramatically**. The money, the rules and the information stayed the same. Only **the default** changed.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Same incentives, same information. Only **what happens** when you do **nothing** changed.',left:{title:'Opt-in',items:['Doing nothing means not saving','Each month needs a decision','Many never start']},right:{title:'Opt-out',items:['Doing nothing means saving','Skipping a month changes nothing','Many never leave']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Why does it hold so well? It rests on **no contested theory**. Acting takes effort and not acting doesn\u2019t. That\u2019s about as safe as behavioural science gets. Anything needing **a monthly decision** eventually misses one.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t rely on **remembering** to save. Make saving what happens **when you do nothing**. A standing transfer covers the months you\u2019d have missed.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two schemes offer workers the **same pension**, with the same money and the same leaflet. One asks them to sign up. The other signs them up unless they say no. How different could **the results** be?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A **default** is what happens if you take no action. Under opt-in, doing nothing means not saving. Under opt-out, it means saving. Either way, **many people** never move from it. So the default does the deciding.'},{kind:'story',text:'When pension schemes in many countries flipped to opt-out, participation **jumped**. Nobody was persuaded of anything. The result has held across countries, schemes and populations. It\u2019s among the most replicated findings in applied behavioural work.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Dev, who means to save on the first of each month. In March a boiler repair wins. In June he simply forgets. A standing transfer on payday would have saved in **both months** without him lifting a finger.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The finding is about joining, not about saving enough. A default sets **how much**, too, so who picks it matters. And with no slack, an automatic transfer can just move your shortfall elsewhere.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Willpower loses to **inertia** in the end. So put inertia **on your side**. Make saving your default, at an amount you can really keep.'}]},'what-predicts-saving_2':{title:'Knowing Is the Easy Part',card:{insight:'Money classes teach a lot, but they change little of what you do.',tryThis:'Pick one saving step you keep putting off. Set it to run by itself on payday.',skills:['money','habits']},review:{prompt:'Your friend signs up for a money class so she will save. What should she expect?',right:'She will learn a lot, but her saving may change only a little',wrong:'Once she knows more, she will start saving on her own',why:'Most people know they should save, so the hard part is doing it.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Max did **a whole class** on money. He can explain saving better than anyone. But by Saturday his pocket money is **all gone**, again. How can you know so much and still do this?'},{kind:'explain',text:'You know you should brush your teeth for **two minutes**. Knowing is easy. Doing it when you\u2019re tired and late is hard. A talk about teeth won\u2019t fix that. **A timer** on the brush might.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Facts help you **know** more, but they don\u2019t do the saving. What helps is a nudge **at the moment** you spend, like money that moves to savings by itself.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A company runs a **well-reviewed course** on saving. Afterwards, staff score far higher on a money quiz. A year on, how much more do you think they\u2019re **actually saving**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Some reviews of money courses found **clear gains** in knowledge but small changes in behaviour, fading within months. Later and larger reviews found **modest** positive effects. That\u2019s better, but still modest. Quiz scores move. Saving barely does.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Why so little? Most people already know they **should save**. The hard part is doing it when **other demands** want the money. Information doesn\u2019t touch that part. So knowledge is rarely the thing holding saving back.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'A course works on **what you know**. Saving happens in **what you do**.',left:{title:'Knowledge',items:['Rises clearly after a course','Easy to deliver','Shows up on a quiz']},right:{title:'Behaviour',items:['Shifts a little, then fades','Needs help at the moment','Shows up in the account']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Take the course if you like, but don\u2019t expect it alone to **change what you do**. Aim at **the moment** of decision: defaults, automatic transfers, prompts on payday.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Teaching people about money is **easy to deliver**, and a whole industry is built on it. It sounds obviously right. So why does the evidence leave it **looking modest**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The research **splits**. Some meta-analyses find clear knowledge gains, little effect on behaviour, and effects that **decay within months**. Later and larger ones find modest positive effects. Neither reading makes knowledge the main thing holding saving back.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Ana, who aces an evening course on budgeting. She can explain compound interest to anyone. Then her car fails its test, a friend\u2019s wedding comes up, and **the transfer** she planned never happens. Her knowledge was never the gap.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture her colleague, who never took the course. His employer enrolled him in a pension by default, and a standing transfer leaves on payday. He couldn\u2019t explain how it works. He saves every month anyway.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The finding is about education alone changing behaviour. It doesn\u2019t say **understanding is worthless**. Where knowledge is truly missing, it may matter, and it can help you choose well once a default exists.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Knowing you should save is rarely **the binding limit**. Doing it under pressure is. So aim your effort at **the moment** money moves, not at more facts.'}]},'what-predicts-saving_3':{title:'Check the Pay First',card:{insight:'Habits help, but how much you earn and how steady it is count for more.',tryThis:'Before you judge how someone saves, look at their pay first. Then do the same for yourself.',skills:['money','thinking']},review:{prompt:'Your cousin\u2019s hours change each week, and she saves little. What do you check first?',right:'How much she earns, and how steady her income is',wrong:'How careful she is and how she spends',why:'Your pay, and how steady it is, matters more than your habits do.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Ella and Sam both want to save. At the end of the month, Ella has **saved more**. Is she just **better at saving**, or is something else going on? What would you guess?'},{kind:'story',text:'Ella gets pocket money every week. Sam gets less, and some weeks none at all. This month Sam also had to pay for **a lost library book**. Ella\u2019s habits may be no better. She just has more coming in, and it\u2019s steady.'},{kind:'moral',text:'How much money **comes in**, and how steady it is, matters more than habits. So before you blame someone\u2019s willpower, **look at the money** first. Blaming them would be unkind.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two people both save **nothing**. One has a steady salary. The other has low, irregular pay and frequent surprise bills. Both get the same **well-designed nudge** to save. Who do you think it helps?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Behavioural factors explain **a modest share** of who saves. Income, how stable it is, and unexpected expenses explain more. So the nudge likely helps the salaried person. It needs **slack** to work on: money left once essentials are paid.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The **same tools** land very differently, for reasons that have **nothing to do** with character.',left:{title:'Steady, some slack',items:['Defaults have money to move','Nudges can bind']},right:{title:'Irregular, no slack',items:['Nothing left to move','Nudges have nothing to act on']}},{kind:'explain',text:'This **isn\u2019t fatalism**. Behaviour still explains part of who saves, and the tools still work. The point is **where to aim** them: at people with room to save. Treating a structural problem as discipline is wrong, and unkind.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When someone isn\u2019t saving, look at **income and stability** before habits. Then aim your tools **where they can bind**, starting with your own budget.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Savers often look **disciplined**. They budget, they plan, they resist the sale. So it seems obvious that discipline makes savers. But what if the arrow partly **runs the other way**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'This is the confusion between what **predicts** saving and what **causes** it. Discipline is easier to keep with steady income and slack. So a habit that goes with saving may partly reflect the income behind it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture Leah, on a steady salary with a little left each month. A payday transfer works well for her. She looks like **a model saver**, and her habits do help. But the slack came first.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now picture Jay, on shift work with hours that change weekly and surprise bills. He gets the same transfer. In thin months he cancels it to cover rent. Same tool, same intent, a very different result.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. This can be misread as **giving up**. Behaviour still explains part of who saves, so habits still matter. And the evidence here gives no exact shares. Treat modest and more as directions, not sizes.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Before you judge **anyone\u2019s saving**, your own included, look at what comes in and how steady it is. Then aim your habits **where there\u2019s room**.'}]},'what-predicts-saving_4':{title:'Name Your Savings',card:{insight:'Give your savings a clear job, and you will spend less of it.',tryThis:'Give one account a name that shows its job, like January Rent.',skills:['money','psychology']},review:{prompt:'Which savings account will you spend from less?',right:'One named for a clear goal, like January Rent',wrong:'One just called Savings, for any use at all',why:'To spend a named account, you have to give up its goal first.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Zoe has **two money jars**. One is labelled savings. The other is labelled new football boots. When she wants sweets, which jar do you think she **dips into**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'She takes from **the savings jar**. The boots jar stays full. A pound buys the same from either jar, so labels shouldn\u2019t matter. But to spend the boots money, Zoe has to **give up the boots**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Give your savings **a clear job**, like bike by summer, not just savings. A pot with a job is **harder to raid**, even though money is money.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You keep a **holiday fund** of a few hundred pounds. You also carry a balance on a credit card. On paper, that **makes no sense**. Yet people keep set-ups like this, and the fund survives the year.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Money is **fungible**: any pound can do the job of any other. So labelling pots should change nothing. It reliably does. People with **a named account** for a named purpose raid it less.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Why does a label work? A labelled pot **has a purpose**. Spending it means overriding that purpose, not just deciding to spend. That\u2019s where the saving happens. The irrational bit **is the mechanism**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'A **broad label** gives little to override. A **specific one** gives more.',left:{title:'A broad label',items:['Emergency','Easy to call anything urgent']},right:{title:'A specific label',items:['January rent','A clear thing to give up']}},{kind:'moral',text:'A named pot is **irrational and useful** at once. Name your savings after **a specific purpose**, and spending them becomes a choice to give that purpose up.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Someone has **\xa32,000** in a pot called wedding. They also owe \xa32,000 on a high-interest credit card. Strict economics calls this **a mistake**. Is it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'**Mental accounting** means treating money in different pots as if it weren\u2019t interchangeable. In strict terms that\u2019s irrational. In practice, people with a named pot for a named purpose **raid it less**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Zara, saving for a flat deposit in a pot she named after it. A weekend away tempts her. Spending that pot means **giving up the flat**, a little. So she skips the trip, and the pot keeps growing.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now back to the wedding pot. The card charges more in interest than the pot earns, so the label is **costing money**. Using the pot to clear the card would likely leave them better off.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. A named pot is **a workaround** that exploits a bias, not a cure for one. Next to high-interest debt, it can cost more than it protects. And preferring specific names is a suggestion, not a measured result.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Keep **named pots** where they help you save. Drop them where the inefficiency **gets expensive**, which usually means high-interest debt. Judge the trick by what it costs you.'}]},'what-predicts-saving_5':{title:'Easy In, Slow Out',card:{insight:'Make it easy to put money in and a bit slow to take it out.',tryThis:'Check how fast you can empty your savings. If it takes one tap, find a bank that takes a day.',skills:['money','habits']},review:{prompt:'You are building a savings account to help people save. Where should a small wait go?',right:'On taking money out, like a wait of one day',wrong:'On putting money in, so each choice to save feels real',why:'Put the wait on taking money out, since that choice is made in a rush.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Picture a money box with **a wide slot** on top, so coins drop in easily. The door to get them out has **a stiff, slow handle**. Why would anyone build it like that?'},{kind:'story',text:'Gita keeps her savings with her mum, not in her room. To get them, she has to ask and **wait until tomorrow**. Quite often, by tomorrow, she doesn\u2019t want the toy any more.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Make saving **easy** and taking money out **a little slow**. You choose to save when you\u2019re calm. A short wait gives your calm self a say before you spend.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'It\u2019s late, a sale **ends at midnight**, and your savings are **one tap away**. Months ago, calmly, you decided what that money was for. Now you\u2019re deciding again, under pressure. Who wins?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Schemes that made saving **effortful** failed. Schemes that made saving automatic and taking money out **mildly inconvenient** did better. A small, well-placed obstacle tended to beat encouragement. It\u2019s the principle most often got backwards.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The two decisions happen **in different states**, so the friction belongs on **the worse one**.',left:{title:'Paying in',items:['Decided calmly, in advance','Should take no effort']},right:{title:'Taking out',items:['Decided under pressure','Should take a day']}},{kind:'explain',text:'How big should the hassle be? Not big. **A day\u2019s delay**, or savings held at a separate bank, is enough. It turns an impulse into **a decision**. When you truly need the money, a day costs almost nothing.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Put the friction **on withdrawal**, not deposit. Make paying in automatic, and **taking money out** a little slow. One day is enough to let your calm decision win.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Many savings accounts let you **empty them** from your phone in seconds. That sounds like good service. So why might the easiest account to use be **the hardest** one to save in?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Paying in and taking out are decided **in different states**. Depositing is chosen calmly and in advance. Withdrawing is chosen under pressure, when something\u2019s gone wrong or something is wanted. **Friction** belongs on the pressured one.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Omar, whose savings sit in an app he can empty with one tap. A sale ends at midnight. By morning the money is gone, spent in **the worst state** to decide anything. Nothing stood in the way.'},{kind:'story',text:'His sister Mina moved her savings to a separate bank that takes a day to transfer. The same sale tempts her. By the time the money could arrive, the impulse has passed. The delay forbade nothing.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. A day\u2019s delay works against impulse, **not against need**. When money is short, friction on the way out may push you toward costlier borrowing. And heavier locks are a different thing, with real emergency costs.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t make saving the hard part. Make it automatic, and put **a small delay** on the way out. A day is enough to turn **an impulse** into a choice you\u2019d stand by.'}]}}}},1026,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'what-sleep-debt-does',kind:'book',promise:'Know when short sleep is hurting you',about:'How tired are you after a week of short nights? You may feel fine while your thinking slows down. In this short article, the Mindroll team explains what short sleep does to you, and what helps. Want to know when to trust the feeling?',ideas:{'what-sleep-debt-does_1':{title:'The Broken Gauge',card:{insight:'After short nights, how you feel stops telling you how you are doing.',tryThis:'Before something big today, count the hours you slept this week. Trust that number, not your mood.',skills:['health','psychology']},review:{prompt:'After a week of short nights, you feel fine to drive. What do you check?',right:'The hours you slept this week, not the feeling',wrong:'Whether you feel sharp enough right now to drive',why:'Short sleep breaks the feeling you would use to check yourself.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve gone to bed late **every night** this week. You feel only a little tired, nothing serious. So how well is your brain **really working** right now?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Picture a car whose **fuel gauge** sticks at half full. The tank keeps emptying, but the gauge keeps saying half. Short sleep does that to **your sense** of how you\u2019re doing. The feeling levels off. Your real sharpness keeps dropping.'},{kind:'moral',text:'After short nights, **how you feel** isn\u2019t a good guide. Count your hours of sleep instead. That number **doesn\u2019t get tired**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'It\u2019s your third five-hour night this week. You feel a bit tired, **no worse** than on Tuesday. You decide you\u2019ve adjusted, and offer to **drive a friend** to the airport. Have you?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Restricted-sleep studies keep finding the same pattern. Performance drops, as you\u2019d expect. But people\u2019s ratings of their own alertness **stop dropping** with it. They settle at a bit tired while measured performance **keeps sliding**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Hunger **gets louder** the longer you ignore it. Sleep debt **goes quiet**.',left:{title:'Hunger',items:['Gets louder over time','Hard to ignore','Pushes you to act']},right:{title:'Sleep debt',items:['Goes quiet over time','Easy to overlook','Feels like adapting']}},{kind:'moral',text:'The tool you\u2019d use to check yourself is **the first to break**. So before anything that matters, don\u2019t ask how you feel. Ask **how much you slept**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Hunger gets louder the longer you wait. Sleep loss seems to do **the opposite**. After a few short nights, the tiredness levels off. Is that your body **adapting**, or something else?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The essay separates **feeling tired** from **being impaired**. In restricted-sleep studies, self-rated alertness plateaus while measured performance keeps falling. The gap between the two is the dangerous part, because you can\u2019t see it from inside.'},{kind:'story',text:'Sam is a junior doctor finishing a week of short nights. By Friday she feels no worse than Wednesday. She takes that as proof she\u2019s **fine to drive** home. The feeling is the one reading she can\u2019t trust.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a student before a big exam after a week of late nights. He feels sharp enough to skip the early night. The quiet in his head reads as **readiness**. It may be the debt talking.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Counting hours is a proxy too. People need different amounts, and the essay gives no threshold. And it **cuts one way**: feeling fine is weak evidence, but feeling badly impaired is worth trusting.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Sleep debt goes quiet, and **the quiet** feels like coping. Don\u2019t let it vote on whether you\u2019re fit for something important. Let **the hours** decide.'}]},'what-sleep-debt-does_2':{title:'The Partial Reset',card:{insight:'Sleeping in on the weekend pays back some sleep, but not all of it.',tryThis:'Count this week\u2019s short nights. Plan next week from the hours you slept, not from how Sunday felt.',skills:['health','habits']},review:{prompt:'You slept six hours a night all week, then ten each weekend night. Now what?',right:'Count it as only part of a fix, and change your week',wrong:'Count it as a full reset, and keep the same week',why:'You feel awake soon, but your focus takes longer to come back.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'All week you went to bed late. On Saturday and Sunday you sleep **until lunch**. By Sunday night you feel great. Are you really **back to normal**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Your phone is nearly flat, so you charge it for **a few minutes**. The warning goes away, but the battery\u2019s still only part full. A lie-in is like that. Feeling awake **comes back first**. Focus takes longer.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A lie-in **helps**, but it doesn\u2019t fix everything. You **feel caught up** before you really are. So try not to plan on paying it all back at the weekend.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Six hours a night all week, then **ten on Saturday** and Sunday. By Monday you feel reset. It feels like a fair trade. But does the weekend **really clear** the debt?'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Recovery sleep doesn\u2019t bring **everything** back at **the same speed**.',left:{title:'Recovers fairly well',items:['How alert you feel','Some performance measures']},right:{title:'Slower, or unclear',items:['Sustained attention, in several studies','Metabolic markers: evidence mixed']}},{kind:'explain',text:'There\u2019s also **a timing cost**. A big lie-in tends to push your body clock later. On Sunday night you\u2019re not sleepy at your usual time, so Monday **starts short**. The repair brings back part of the problem.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Weekend sleep is **partial recovery**, not a reset. Treating it as a full one licenses **the same week** again. Plan from what you actually slept.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two long nights at the weekend and you feel **brand new**. On short weeknights, the feeling ran ahead of the facts. Could it be **doing the same** now?'},{kind:'explain',text:'It is. Recovery sleep restores **felt alertness** fairly quickly. Sustained attention comes back more slowly, and in several lab studies hadn\u2019t fully returned after a weekend. So the **sense of being restored** isn\u2019t proof of it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Priya sleeps six hours a night from Monday to Friday, then ten each weekend night. Every Monday she feels caught up. So the weekday pattern **never changes**, and its costs never quite clear.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now consider the clock. After a Saturday lie-in until noon, Tom isn\u2019t sleepy at his usual time on Sunday. He lies awake until two. Monday starts **short**, and part of the debt he repaid is back.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Recovery sleep **isn\u2019t pointless**, and some of the evidence is genuinely mixed. For markers like insulin sensitivity, some studies find recovery after a couple of long nights and others don\u2019t.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A lie-in pays back **part** of what you owe. Use it, but don\u2019t let it **license** the same short week. Plan from the hours you actually slept.'}]},'what-sleep-debt-does_3':{title:'The Missing End',card:{insight:'An hour cut from the end of the night is not an average hour.',tryThis:'Next time an early alarm cuts your night, write down which end you lost.',skills:['health','science']},review:{prompt:'An early flight means waking two hours sooner. What have you mostly lost?',right:'Mostly REM sleep from the end of the night',wrong:'A bit of each kind of sleep, spread evenly',why:'Sleep comes in parts, so the end you cut changes what you lose.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You usually sleep until seven. For a school trip, you get up **at five**. You lost two hours. But **which two**? A little from all over the night, or the last part?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A night has **parts**. Early on, you get lots of very deep sleep. Near morning, you get more **REM sleep**, when most vivid dreams happen. So an early alarm mostly takes away the dreaming part.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Not every hour of sleep is **the same**. A short night isn\u2019t just a smaller full night. It matters **which end** got cut.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your alarm goes off **an hour early** for a flight. You tell yourself it\u2019s only an hour, a small slice of a long night. But is the hour you lost **an average hour**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Sleep **isn\u2019t uniform**. Deep slow-wave sleep is concentrated early in the night. The share of REM rises **towards morning**. So an hour cut from the end removes mostly REM-rich sleep, not a little of everything.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Where the cut falls changes **what you lose**, and one side is **less clear**.',left:{title:'Early alarm',items:['Cuts the end of the night','Mostly REM-rich sleep lost','Fairly clear']},right:{title:'Late bedtime',items:['Cuts into the start','What\u2019s lost is less clear-cut','Often overstated in popular accounts']}},{kind:'moral',text:'The stages appear to **do different work**. So a short night isn\u2019t a scaled-down full one. Don\u2019t count it as **a full night** minus a little.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'It\u2019s easy to treat the night as **one block** of rest. Lose an hour and you\u2019ve lost an hour. But what if the night is built in **layers**, and an alarm only shaves one end?'},{kind:'explain',text:'**Sleep architecture** varies across the night. Deep slow-wave sleep comes early. REM comes later, in **longer stretches** toward morning. Cut an hour off the end and you remove mostly REM-rich sleep, not an even slice.'},{kind:'story',text:'Ellie normally wakes at seven. Twice this week an early train pulls her up at five. On paper she lost two hours a night. In practice she lost mostly **the REM-rich end**, both mornings.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Marcus, who stays out late and sleeps until his usual alarm. He also loses two hours, but from **the start**. What that removes is less clear-cut, despite the confident claims you may have read.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Both stages have been **linked** to memory, and REM to emotional processing. But the mapping from stages to jobs is less clean than popular accounts suggest. Don\u2019t diagnose a single night from it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The structural point survives the caveats. An hour **isn\u2019t just an hour**, and which end it came from matters. So stop treating your hours as **interchangeable**.'}]},'what-sleep-debt-does_4':{title:'Keep One Wake Time',card:{insight:'Steady sleep times are easier than more sleep, and they help on their own.',tryThis:'Pick a wake time you could keep on Saturday. Set it tonight for every day this week.',skills:['health','habits']},review:{prompt:'Your evenings are full, so you cannot add an hour of sleep. What helps?',right:'Pick one wake time and keep it on weekends too',wrong:'Sleep in on weekends to make up the hours',why:'Steady times help on their own and use hours you already sleep.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'On school days you get up **at seven**. At weekends you stay up late and sleep until ten. Every Monday morning feels **awful**. What\u2019s going on?'},{kind:'explain',text:'There are two ways to sleep better. One is to sleep **for longer**, which is hard when clubs and homework get in the way. The other is to sleep **at the same times** every day. That\u2019s much easier.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Pick a wake-up time and **keep it**, even on Saturday. An alarm can do that for you. Steady times help **on their own**, without extra hours.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your commute means leaving at seven, and your evenings are **already full**. Advice to sleep an extra hour sounds like one more thing you **can\u2019t do**. Is there a lever you can actually pull?'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Duration is often set by others. **Timing** is often **more adjustable**.',left:{title:'More sleep',items:['Needs hours you may lack','Often set by others','Asks for a sacrifice']},right:{title:'Regular times',items:['Uses hours you already sleep','Often more adjustable','Asks for a schedule']}},{kind:'explain',text:'The instruction is to **anchor the wake time**, weekends included, and let the rest follow. Waking is the end of the night most **under your control**. An alarm can set it. Falling asleep can\u2019t be ordered.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Regularity counts **on its own**, and the evidence for it has grown. It asks for a schedule, **not a sacrifice**. That\u2019s why it survives a busy life.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A parent with a baby can\u2019t simply decide to sleep longer. Neither can a nurse on shifts. So is all sleep advice **out of reach** for them, or is there **another lever**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'**Duration** is often set from outside: a child, a shift, a commute. The timing of whatever sleep is available is often **more adjustable**. And the evidence suggests regularity helps independently of total hours.'},{kind:'story',text:'Dan has a toddler and gets about six hours whatever he does. He can\u2019t add an hour. But he can get up at **half six** every day, Sunday included. That costs him nothing extra.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Aisha, who works long weeks and sleeps until eleven on Saturdays. She moves her weekend alarm closer to her weekday one. It\u2019s a rearrangement of hours she already sleeps, not a sacrifice.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. For some people, timing is no freer than duration. Rotating shifts and night feeds set both. And regularity doesn\u2019t replace **enough sleep**. A regular short night is still a short night.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When you can\u2019t sleep longer, **sleep regularly**. Fix the wake time first, because it\u2019s the end you control. It asks for **nothing extra**, so it holds up.'}]},'what-sleep-debt-does_5':{title:'Advice That Can\u2019t Fail',card:{insight:'Trust a sleep tip less if no result could prove it wrong.',tryThis:'Pick one sleep tip you follow. Write down what result would show it is not working.',skills:['thinking','health']},review:{prompt:'A bedtime routine did not help, and you are told you did it wrong. What now?',right:'Ask what result would show that the routine fails',wrong:'Try the routine again and trust it more',why:'If no result can count against a tip, you cannot test it.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your cousin says **a special stretch** at bedtime will help you sleep. You try it and still sleep badly. She says you must have **done it wrong**. Can the stretch ever fail?'},{kind:'explain',text:'A **fair test** can come out either way. If every bad night is blamed on you, the tip can never lose. And a tip that can\u2019t lose **can\u2019t be checked** at all.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Try sleep tips, but **don\u2019t trust them** too much. The best-backed advice is dull: **enough hours**, at the same times each day.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'An article promises better sleep: **a cooler bedroom**, no screens before bed, a wind-down ritual. Each comes with a convincing reason it should work. What\u2019s **missing** from every one?'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Much of the advice offers **a mechanism**. Very little of it offers **an outcome**.',left:{title:'Mechanism',items:['A story about why','Easy to write','Says nothing about size']},right:{title:'Outcome',items:['A test showing it works','Slow and dull','Could come out against']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s **the tell**. Ask what result would count against the advice. If sleeping badly after the routine gets blamed on doing it wrong, **no result** ever can. That should lower your confidence, not raise your effort.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Wind-down routines and screen curfews have mostly **small, noisy effects**. They\u2019re worth trying, not worth believing hard. If you want the safe bets, they\u2019re **the dull ones**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'The essay makes its own claims carefully. Then it turns to the much larger pile of **sleep advice** around it. How far should you trust a tip that **comes with a story**, but no test?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Much sleep advice is stated so it **can\u2019t be tested**. Routines, temperatures and curfews come with a plausible mechanism, not a trial. When no result could count against a claim, **lower your confidence** in it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Jo follows a strict wind-down hour and still sleeps badly. A friend says she started too late. Next week, too early. Every bad night gets explained away, so the routine **never fails**. Nor can it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Kit, who skips the rituals. He sticks to the dull items the essay rates higher: timing, light, caffeine and enough time in bed. They\u2019re **boring** to read about. That, roughly, is why so little advice is about them.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Untestable as stated isn\u2019t the same as useless. Small, noisy effects **can still be real**, and trying them costs little. And a claim that sleep advice is worthless would need its own test.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The target is **the confidence**, not the practice. Try the tips if you like. Just believe hard only in the dull things: **enough hours**, at consistent times.'}]}}}},1027,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'what-sleep-does',kind:'book',promise:'Sleep better with what the studies show',about:'What does sleep do for you, and how much of what you hear is true? The Mindroll team wrote this article to find out. It shows what studies back up and which claims go too far. Want to sleep better tonight?',ideas:{'what-sleep-does_1':{title:'Sleep on It',card:{insight:'Things you learn right before sleep stay with you better.',tryThis:'Tonight, go over the one thing you most want to know just before bed. Test yourself in the morning.',skills:['health','habits']},review:{prompt:'You have a test tomorrow. When should you go over the hardest part?',right:'Right before bed, so sleep can help you keep it',wrong:'At lunch, then stay up late studying even more',why:'You keep more of what you study when you sleep on it.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You have a **spelling test** tomorrow. You could stay up late, going over the words again and again. Or you could read them once more and **go to bed**. Which choice helps you more?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Scientists teach people something new. Then some sleep, and some stay awake **for the same time**. When they\u2019re tested, the sleepers **usually do better**. Your brain seems to keep working on the day\u2019s learning while you sleep.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Sleep isn\u2019t a break from learning. It\u2019s **part of it**. So read the words once more tonight, then **sleep on them**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'It\u2019s the night before a big presentation. You could rehearse until two in the morning, or run through it once and **sleep**. Staying up feels like **more practice**. Is it?'},{kind:'story',text:'In the studies, people learn something new: a list of words, say, or a skill with their hands. Then some sleep and others stay awake **for the same stretch**. On the test that follows, those who slept tend to do better.'},{kind:'explain',text:'This is called **consolidation**. Sleep is when your brain seems to file the day\u2019s material, stabilising it rather than just resting. Disrupt particular sleep stages and particular kinds of memory suffer. That\u2019s **hard to explain** as rest.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Learning carries on **after you stop**. So the timing of study, relative to sleep, matters in its own right. Review what counts **shortly before bed**, and let sleep do its part.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Most advice about studying is about **effort**: more hours, better notes, harder focus. This idea is about **timing** instead. What if the learning you did today isn\u2019t finished until you\u2019ve slept?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The article ranks this among the **better-established findings** about sleep. Material learned before sleep is kept measurably better than material learned before the same time awake. Sleep stabilises and integrates **the day\u2019s learning**. It isn\u2019t a pause in it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Two kinds of result back it up. People improve more on **physical skills** and on remembered facts after sleep than after equal hours awake. And when particular sleep stages are disrupted, particular kinds of memory get worse.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Leah, a student with exams next week. She used to revise her hardest topic at lunch and scroll before bed. Now she swaps them. Same total study time, but the hard topic gets **slept on** every night.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The mechanism is **still argued over**, and effect sizes vary a lot by task. So there\u2019s no fixed gain to promise. And it doesn\u2019t mean more sleep brings proportionally more learning.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The advice holds **whichever mechanism** wins: study then sleep beats study without it. So don\u2019t trade sleep for extra hours. **Schedule your learning** around it.'}]},'what-sleep-does_2':{title:'Steady Sleep Times',card:{insight:'When you sleep may matter as much as how long you sleep.',tryThis:'Pick one wake-up time you can keep all seven days, weekends too. Set your alarm for it tonight.',skills:['health','habits']},review:{prompt:'You sleep six hours on work nights and ten on weekends. What helps most?',right:'Keep one wake-up time each day, weekends too',wrong:'Sleep in more to raise your weekly total',why:'The times you sleep seem to matter, not just the total hours.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'On school nights you go to bed at ten. On Friday and Saturday you **stay up late**, then sleep in. It feels like **catching up**. Does your body see it that way?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Your body has **a clock inside it**. It makes you sleepy at night and helps you wake. Studies suggest it pays attention to **your pattern**, not only to your hours. A steady pattern is easier for it to follow.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When you sleep **may matter** as much as how long. A lie-in doesn\u2019t fully make up for short nights. Try keeping **the same times**, weekends too.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Six hours on each work night, then **nine and a half** on Friday and Saturday. Add it up and you average **seven a night**. On paper, it\u2019s a respectable week. Is it?'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture Tom, who works that exact pattern. His total looks fine on a tracker. But his bedtime swings by hours each weekend, and Monday always feels rough. He protects the total and lets **the timing drift**.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Studies that track sleep variability find **irregular schedules** predict worse health outcomes, even when total sleep is adequate. That suggests your body clock responds to **the pattern**, not only the amount. So a steady seven is likely better than an erratic eight.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Most people optimise **the total**. This idea says fix **the window** first.',left:{title:'Protect the total',items:['Late nights in the week','Lie-ins to repay them']},right:{title:'Fix the window',items:['Same wake-up time daily','Weekends included']}},{kind:'moral',text:'How long you sleep is often **out of your hands**: a commute, a baby, a shift. When your window falls is **more adjustable**. Start there, and add hours later.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two people each sleep **seven hours** on average. One goes to bed at the same time every night. The other swings by hours from weeknight to weekend. Would you expect **the same health**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The article treats **regularity** as an independent factor, not just a stand-in for getting enough. If that\u2019s right, the pattern matters as well as the amount. It reverses the usual order: **fix the window** first, then add hours.'},{kind:'story',text:'The evidence comes from studies that track how much people\u2019s sleep times vary. Irregular schedules predicted worse outcomes even when duration was adequate. From that, the article infers, cautiously, that the **body clock** responds to pattern.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Grace, a nurse on early shifts. She can\u2019t add hours, because her rota fixes the start. On days off, she can **hold a wake-up time** near her working one. That part is hers to set.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. These studies are **observational**. Irregular sleep might harm health, or ill health and chaotic lives might cause irregular sleep. That\u2019s why the wording stays careful: may matter, likely better, not certainly.'},{kind:'moral',text:'You often can\u2019t choose **how long** you sleep. You can usually choose **when**. Hold that window steady, weekends included, before you chase extra hours.'}]},'what-sleep-does_3':{title:'Two Signs You Slept Enough',card:{insight:'Eight hours is an average, and how you wake shows your own need.',tryThis:'For a week, skip the watch. Each night, write down if you woke on your own and felt sharp.',skills:['health','thinking']},review:{prompt:'Your watch says you slept six and a half hours. Is it enough?',right:'Check if you woke before your alarm and stayed sharp',wrong:'Compare it with eight hours and aim for more',why:'Your need shows in how you wake and feel, not in a number.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Lots of people say everyone needs **eight hours** of sleep. Your friend sleeps seven and feels great. You sleep eight and still yawn **all afternoon**. So who\u2019s getting enough?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Eight hours is **an average** for grown-ups. Some people need a bit more, and some a bit less. A better test is **how you feel**. Do you wake up on your own? Do you stay sharp all afternoon?'},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t chase **a number**. Worrying about it can keep you awake. Just check **two things** each day: waking on your own, and feeling sharp.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your watch says you slept seven hours and twelve minutes, with **a poor slice** of deep sleep. You felt fine when you woke. Now you\u2019re **not so sure**. Should you be?'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture two friends. Ama sleeps seven hours, wakes before her alarm most days and stays alert all afternoon without coffee. Ben sleeps eight, needs the alarm and leans on caffeine by three. Only one of them is short of sleep.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The eight-hour figure is **a population average**, not a personal prescription. The useful signals are whether you **wake without an alarm** and stay alert through the afternoon without caffeine. Ama passes both on seven hours. Ben fails both on eight.'},{kind:'explain',text:'And the tracker? It\u2019s reasonable at estimating **total sleep** and poor at staging it. The light, deep and REM breakdown is inferred from movement and heart rate. Validation studies find it **disagrees substantially** with lab measurement.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Treat the total as **a rough guide** and the stages as entertainment. Then ask two questions: did you wake before the alarm, and did you stay alert? Those are **your real score**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Many people lie awake worrying they won\u2019t **reach their number**. The worry keeps them up, so the number gets worse. What if the number was **never the point**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The article\u2019s claim is that individual needs **vary meaningfully**, so eight hours describes a group, not you. Judge rest by its effects instead. Waking without an alarm and staying alert without caffeine test **what sleep is for**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Nina, who wakes naturally and is sharp all afternoon on seven hours. By the article\u2019s test, she isn\u2019t sleep-deprived. Her colleague does neither on eight, and he is. **The hours** didn\u2019t decide it. The two signals did.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Joe, who checks his tracker every morning. It says his deep sleep was low, though he felt rested. But a wrist device doesn\u2019t see sleep stages. It infers them from movement and heart rate, and lab comparisons disagree substantially.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The two questions need **honest conditions**. Daily caffeine or a fixed early start can make them hard to read. And the average isn\u2019t useless: it\u2019s a fair starting point, just not a target.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Your need is measured **on you**, not on a chart. Trust the total roughly and the stages barely. Let **waking and alertness** tell you whether you\u2019re rested.'}]},'what-sleep-does_4':{title:'Coffee Stays for Hours',card:{insight:'Coffee at four is still in you at bedtime, and it can cut deep sleep.',tryThis:'Today, have your last coffee eight hours before bed. See how you wake up tomorrow.',skills:['health','habits']},review:{prompt:'You drink coffee at four and still fall asleep fine. Is it harmless?',right:'No, it can still cut deep sleep you do not feel',wrong:'Yes, falling asleep fast shows the coffee is gone',why:'Coffee can cut deep sleep in a way you cannot feel.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your mum has **a big mug** of coffee at four o\u2019clock. At bedtime she falls asleep fine. So the coffee must be **all gone** by then. Right?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Not quite. In grown-ups, about **half the caffeine** is still there five or six hours later. It can cut down **your deepest sleep**. But you may not notice. You wake up and think you slept fine.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Caffeine **stays a long time**. A drink after lunch can still be working at bedtime. So it\u2019s best to stop about **eight hours before bed**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You have a coffee at **four in the afternoon**. You\u2019re asleep by midnight, no trouble at all. You wake up feeling fine. So the coffee **did no harm**, didn\u2019t it?'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture Zoe, who drinks that same coffee every day. Bed at midnight is eight hours later, about one and a half half-lives. Roughly a third of the caffeine, or a little more, is still circulating when she turns out the light.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Caffeine\u2019s **half-life** is roughly five to six hours in most adults. The harm usually isn\u2019t trouble falling asleep. People with high tolerance often sleep fine. It\u2019s a measurable cut in **slow-wave sleep**, the deepest kind.'},{kind:'explain',text:'That\u2019s the portion most linked to **filing what you learned**. So the damage can be invisible from inside. Your hours stay the same, the quality drops, and **nothing tells you** anything happened.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Set a **cutoff time**, not a limit on cups. Make it about eight hours before bed, **earlier than most expect**. Even then, some remains, so it reduces the residue rather than removing it.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Plenty of people drink coffee at four and sleep **like a log**. They\u2019d swear it does nothing. Yet their sleep may still be **quietly thinner**. How could you lose something you never feel?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The article\u2019s claim is that caffeine\u2019s cost falls on **sleep quality**, not on falling asleep. Its half-life is roughly five to six hours in most adults. It tends to reduce **slow-wave sleep**, often without changing the hours at all.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Ravi, a heavy coffee drinker with high tolerance. He reports sleeping fine, and in a sense he does: his hours don\u2019t change. But slow-wave sleep, the part most tied to **consolidation**, can still shrink. Nothing inside him flags it.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Hana. She moves her last coffee to eight hours before bed, for **two weeks**. That cuts the leftover caffeine without removing it. Then she judges the difference for herself.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. People\u2019s caffeine metabolism **varies a lot**, so five to six hours and the eight-hour cutoff are starting points. And a self-test can\u2019t see slow-wave sleep. It only shows whether you wake and stay alert differently.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Caffeine\u2019s **long tail** reaches your bed even when you feel fine. So use a **cutoff time**, about eight hours before bed. Then test it on yourself, because bodies differ.'}]},'what-sleep-does_5':{title:'Claims That Go Too Far',card:{insight:'Sleep matters, but some bold claims say more than the studies can show.',tryThis:'When a sleep claim sounds sure, ask if the study could show what caused what.',skills:['thinking','science']},review:{prompt:'A headline says people who sleep less get sick more. What do you ask?',right:'Could the study show which one caused the other?',wrong:'How many hours should I sleep to stay well?',why:'A link cannot show what caused what, so ask what else could explain it.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Imagine a headline: *Kids who sleep less get worse marks.* It sounds as if short sleep **causes** bad marks. But wait. Could **something else** explain it?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Two things can **go together** without one causing the other. Ice cream sales and sunburn both rise in summer. Ice cream doesn\u2019t cause sunburn. **The sun** causes both. You can\u2019t tell which way it goes from the pattern alone.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Sleep **really does matter**. But some famous claims say more than the facts show. When someone sounds **very, very sure**, you\u2019re allowed to ask how they know.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A friend tells you, with total confidence, that short sleep causes **a long list** of diseases. They read it in a popular book, stated as **settled fact**. Can you tell if it\u2019s true?'},{kind:'story',text:'Look at the claim closely. Short sleepers have worse health outcomes: that\u2019s a correlation. So short sleep causes them: that\u2019s a different, causal claim. Between the two sit **rival explanations** the data can\u2019t rule out.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Short sleep might cause illness. Illness might cause short sleep. Or **a third factor**, like stress or shift work, might drive both. Correlational data fit all three, so on their own they **can\u2019t show direction**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Popular sleep writing overreaches in **familiar ways**. Watch for **the gap**.',left:{title:'Confident version',items:['One study called settled','Correlation sold as cause','Effect sizes without uncertainty']},right:{title:'Careful version',items:['Several studies weighed','Direction left open','Confidence intervals shown']}},{kind:'moral',text:'The core findings **aren\u2019t wrong**. The confident version is just stronger than the evidence. Knowing that leaves you **better placed** to act on what\u2019s actually established.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Sleep is genuinely important. So why would a careful article spend **a whole section** on the ways popular sleep writing **gets it wrong**? Wouldn\u2019t you rather hear that sleep fixes everything?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Its answer is that sleep matters enough to be **described accurately**. Some widely repeated claims about sleep and disease rest on **correlational data** that can\u2019t establish direction. And a few well-known figures have been criticised for overstating them.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take the line that short sleepers have worse outcomes, so short sleep causes them. Short sleep might cause the harm. Ill health might cause short sleep. Or stress might drive both, which is **confounding**. The data fit all three.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take the eight-hour figure. It\u2019s a population average, yet popular accounts present it as **a personal requirement**. Add single studies reported as settled, and effect sizes stated without confidence intervals. Each step adds certainty the evidence never had.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. One misuse is to dismiss sleep research, when the core findings stand. The other is to apply this **unevenly**, doubting claims you dislike and waving the rest through. The regularity evidence is observational too.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The core findings survive, but **the certainty** doesn\u2019t. Test every confident claim the same way, including ones you like. Could the data show **which way** the arrow points?'}]}}}},1028,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'what-the-room-rewards',kind:'book',promise:'Learn what really gets people ahead at work',about:'Does your job reward what it says it does? Most jobs hang up nice signs. But the people who move up show the real rule. These ideas show you how to read it, and when to leave. What does your job reward?',ideas:{'what-the-room-rewards_1':{title:'Promotions, Not Posters',card:{insight:'What a job rewards shows in who moves up, not on its signs.',tryThis:'List the last five people who moved up near you. Write down what they share.',skills:['career','thinking']},review:{prompt:'A sign at your job says customers come first. How do you learn what gets rewarded?',right:'Look at what the last five people who moved up share',wrong:'Read the sign on the wall and the staff rule book',why:'A sign shows what a job hopes, but who moves up shows what it picks.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your coach always says **effort matters most**. Then the team for the big match goes up. You think back over who got picked the last five times. What do **those players** have in common?'},{kind:'explain',text:'They aren\u2019t the hardest workers. They\u2019re **the top scorers**. The coach\u2019s words are what he hopes the team is like. **The picks** are what he actually chooses. When the two don\u2019t match, the picks tell you more.'},{kind:'moral',text:'To learn what a place **really rewards**, don\u2019t go by what it says. Look at **who gets picked**. What they share is the real rule.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You join a firm whose wall poster promises **collaboration**, candour and **customer focus**. You\u2019d like to be promoted one day. So where should you look to find out what that **actually takes**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Take the last five people promoted. Four of them led internal launches that senior leaders watched closely. None worked much with customers. That\u2019s not proof of hypocrisy. It tells you what\u2019s being rewarded: launches leaders can see.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The poster and the record describe **different things**. Where they clash, **the record wins**.',left:{title:'The poster',items:['How it wants to be seen','An intention','Easy to believe']},right:{title:'The promotions',items:['What it actually chooses','A decision','Rarely added up']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Look at what was **legible** about each person, not their qualities. Which projects, how visible, and **who backed them**. Anyone can see who got promoted. Almost nobody puts the pattern together, so the poster goes on being believed.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The posters describe **an intention**. The promotions describe **a decision**. Read the last five, and you\u2019ll know the real job spec.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Every company has **a values page**. Few employees could tell you how it matches the last round of promotions. Yet only one of those two things decides careers. Which one would you **bet on**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Stated values describe how an organisation **wants to look**. The promotion record is its **revealed preference**. Where the two clash, the record is what actually counts. Abstract qualities like drive can be read into anyone afterwards.'},{kind:'story',text:'Dev\u2019s firm says it prizes mentoring junior staff. He lists the last five promoted. All ran high-profile projects with a senior sponsor, and none did much mentoring. He keeps mentoring, but stops expecting it to get him promoted.'},{kind:'story',text:'Or take a school that says it values innovation. Look at who became head of department lately. If each one had the best exam results and no new ideas, you\u2019ve learned what that school really counts.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Five is **a small sample**. A pattern may be chance, or one manager\u2019s habit, so look wider first. And what was visible isn\u2019t always what decided it. The method describes the room, not whether to play to it.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Believe **the promotions** over the posters. The record is public, and almost nobody adds it up. Just look **wide enough** that one manager\u2019s taste doesn\u2019t pass for the rule.'}]},'what-the-room-rewards_2':{title:'Work Nobody Sees',card:{insight:'If nobody sees your work, it counts as if you never did it.',tryThis:'When you finish a job, send a short note. Send it to the one person who needs to know.',skills:['career','communication']},review:{prompt:'You fixed a problem that kept coming back, and nobody noticed. What now?',right:'Send a short note on what broke and what you changed',wrong:'Let the work speak for itself and wait to be noticed',why:'Work nobody sees counts for nothing, so a short note helps it count.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You and Max make a **poster about volcanoes**. You find all the facts at home. Max holds it up in class, and the teacher says *great work, Max*. Where did **your part** go?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Your part happened. **Nobody saw it**. Work people can see often gets more credit than good work they can\u2019t. That isn\u2019t fair, but it\u2019s **what usually happens**. Knowing it just stops your work from going missing.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Good work that nobody sees **doesn\u2019t get counted**. So when you finish something, **tell the person** who needs to know. One short line is enough.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You spent **three weeks** fixing a report that kept breaking. Now it just works, so nobody thinks about it. At review time, your manager praises someone\u2019s **shiny slide deck**. What happened to your three weeks?'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'There are **two lessons** you could draw here, and only one of them **is useful**.',left:{title:'The cynical lesson',items:['Quality doesn\u2019t matter','Do less, announce more','Pick work for looks']},right:{title:'The useful lesson',items:['Quality still matters','Unseen work scores zero','Report it in a note']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Visible work beats good work **more often** than anyone admits. That\u2019s not how it should be. But you can believe that and still act on how it is. Describing what\u2019s rewarded **isn\u2019t endorsing it**.'},{kind:'story',text:'So you send your manager a four-line note. What was broken, what you changed, and what it now saves each week. It takes five minutes. Next review, your three weeks are on the record.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Work nobody can see is **scored as zero**. Don\u2019t do worse work loudly. Send **a short note** when something finishes, to the person who\u2019d never otherwise know.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two engineers do equally good work. One spends a week on **a launch** the whole floor sees. The other quietly keeps **the payment system** from falling over. Which one gets mentioned at promotion time?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The cynic\u2019s conclusion is that quality doesn\u2019t matter, only appearance. The narrower truth: quality still matters, but **only work someone sees** enters the scoring. So the remedy changes **what\u2019s reported**, not what\u2019s done.'},{kind:'story',text:'The quiet engineer starts sending a note whenever a piece of maintenance finishes. Written at the time, so it\u2019s accurate. Sent to one person, so it\u2019s a report, not a broadcast. By review season, her manager has a file.'},{kind:'story',text:'Or take an office manager whose supplier contracts never lapse. Because nothing ever goes wrong, nobody notices her work at all. A short note each time a contract renews puts it on the record.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. A place that scores only visible work ends up with **too little maintenance**. If everyone narrates, maybe nobody maintains. And once visibility starts choosing your work, you\u2019ve adopted the value. It\u2019s still worth arguing for better scoring.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Accept the description **without endorsing it**. Keep choosing work for what it\u2019s worth, then **make it visible** to the one person who\u2019d otherwise never know.'}]},'what-the-room-rewards_3':{title:'Missed, Not Rejected',card:{insight:'When quiet people get skipped, it is mostly because no one knew their work.',tryThis:'Write two lines on what you finished last month and what it changed.',skills:['career','communication']},review:{prompt:'A quiet coworker who does good work gets skipped again. What is the most likely reason?',right:'Her work never reached the people doing the judging',wrong:'Her boss has decided she is not good enough',why:'Bosses pick from what they know, so unknown work does not come up.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Every week after school, you tidy **the art cupboard**. Nobody asks you to. Then your teacher picks a new class helper, and it **isn\u2019t you**. Was she saying your tidying wasn\u2019t good enough?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Probably not. She **never knew** you did it. It\u2019s like leaving your homework on the kitchen table, so your teacher marks it missing. She isn\u2019t saying it was bad. She just **never saw it**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When quiet people get missed, it\u2019s **usually not on purpose**. The people choosing just don\u2019t know. So **help them know** what you did.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Someone on your team solved a problem that **kept coming back**, then told no one. Eight months later, their manager finds out **by chance**. Has she been ignoring them all this time?'},{kind:'explain',text:'No, she **had no way** of knowing. Decisions get made in rooms you aren\u2019t in, from what the people there can recall. Unnarrated work gives them **nothing to recall**. It isn\u2019t judged and dismissed. It just never reached the people doing the judging.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'So the fix isn\u2019t **becoming self-promoting**. It\u2019s making your work **legible** to people who weren\u2019t there.',left:{title:'Self-promotion',items:['Talking yourself up','Aimed at an audience','Hard for quiet people']},right:{title:'Legibility',items:['A record of results','Aimed at the decider','Easy to bear']}},{kind:'story',text:'Take Ana, a quiet engineer. She keeps a short running record: rewrote the weekly import, and it hasn\u2019t failed since March. Before each review, she hands it to her manager. That isn\u2019t politics. It\u2019s the input the process needs.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Being passed over is usually **missing information**, not a verdict. You don\u2019t have to talk yourself up. Just **write down** what you did and what changed, and hand it over.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A talented engineer is passed over **for the third time**. She decides her manager must **dislike her**. It\u2019s the obvious explanation, and it feels true. But is it the likeliest one?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Being overlooked is usually **an absence of information**, not a judgement. If it were a judgement, you\u2019d need to change the judge\u2019s mind. If it\u2019s missing information, you just **supply it**. That\u2019s a far more bearable task.'},{kind:'story',text:'So she starts a running record. Rewrote the weekly import; it hasn\u2019t failed since March. Fixed the flaky test suite; releases no longer stall. At the next promotion round, her name comes up for the first time.'},{kind:'story',text:'Or picture a volunteer who\u2019s kept a charity\u2019s rota running for years without complaint. When trustees choose a new coordinator, they pick someone louder. Nobody weighed her work and found it wanting. Nobody in the room knew it existed.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Sometimes the overlooking **is deliberate**. Bias and favouritism exist, and a record won\u2019t cure them. And supplying information takes time and confidence that aren\u2019t shared evenly, so the process favours whoever reports.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Before you assume **a verdict**, assume missing information. The process needs input it doesn\u2019t otherwise get. **Supply it in writing**, to whoever will be in the room.'}]},'what-the-room-rewards_4':{title:'Ask for the List',card:{insight:'Ask what it would take, and you get a list instead of a kind word.',tryThis:'Before your next review, write down this question. What would have to be true for me to move up?',skills:['career','communication']},review:{prompt:'You want to reach the next level in a year. Which question do you ask?',right:'What would have to be true for me to reach that level?',wrong:'Am I doing well enough to be ready for it?',why:'Asking if you are ready gets a kind word, but this gets a list.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You want a **speaking part** in the school play. After rehearsal you ask your teacher, *am I doing okay?* She smiles and says **keep it up**. You feel good. So what do you practise tomorrow?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Try **a different question**: what would I need to do to get a speaking part? Now she gives you a list. Learn lines fast, speak up, and come to every rehearsal. A list is something you can **work on**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Asking if you\u2019re doing okay gets you **a kind word**. Asking what you\u2019d need to do gets you **a list**. You can\u2019t work on the first, but you can work on the second.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You ask your manager if you\u2019re ready for **the next level**. She says you\u2019re doing really well, it\u2019s on the cards, these things take time. You leave smiling. A week later, what will you **do differently**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Asking if you\u2019re ready invites a yes or no, and usually **gets a hedge**. A hedge costs the person answering nothing. So ask what would have to be true for you to reach that level in a year. That **needs a list**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Say your manager names three things. Lead one project end to end, mentor a new starter, and present to leadership. You write them down. Each one can be worked on, checked and brought back next quarter.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Writing the list down matters **as much as** asking. A spoken list gets **quietly renegotiated**.',left:{title:'Kept in heads',items:['Agreed in the room','Shifts without notice','Usually downward']},right:{title:'Written down',items:['Agreed and recorded','Changes are visible','Something to point at']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t ask if you\u2019re doing well. That gets **reassurance**, which feels nice and changes nothing. Ask what would have to be true, then **write the list down**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two people ask their managers about promotion on the same day. One asks if she\u2019s **ready**. The other asks what would have to be true **within a year**. A week later, which of them knows what to work on?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The second question **converts a judgement** into a specification. It moves the work of being specific onto **whoever holds the standard**. And it tests them. A manager who can\u2019t name anything specific is telling you how the decision gets made.'},{kind:'story',text:'Jo asks the second question. Her manager names three conditions, and Jo emails them back that afternoon as a summary. Six months on, she\u2019s met all three. When a fourth appears, she has something to point at.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a trainee cook who asks the head chef if he\u2019s ready to run the pass. He gets a shrug. When he asks what would have to be true, she names two skills to practise this month.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. A list **isn\u2019t a promise**. Budgets, reorganisations and other candidates can change the outcome even when every condition is met. And a list can narrow you, so you meet its letter and miss what the role needs.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Turn the judgement into **a specification**. Ask what would have to be true, write the answer down, and **return to it**. A list you\u2019ve recorded is hard to shift quietly.'}]},'what-the-room-rewards_5':{title:'When the Room Is Wrong',card:{insight:'Seeing what a job rewards does not mean you must please it, or stay.',tryThis:'Name one thing your job rewards. Write down if it helps anything the job needs.',skills:['career','thinking']},review:{prompt:'Your job rewards neat reports you find dull, but they help the team decide. What now?',right:'Learn the skill, since it helps what the job needs',wrong:'Leave, since a rule you dislike means the job is wrong',why:'Leave if what gets praised helps nothing the job needs, not if you hate it.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'At your football club, the coach cheers loudest for kids who **argue with the ref**. You could learn to do it, and you\u2019d get picked more. But is that what you want **to get better at**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Some rules you just **don\u2019t like**. If the coach wants more passing and you love dribbling, passing still wins games. That\u2019s worth learning. But arguing with the ref **helps nobody play**. That rule is simply wrong.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Seeing what a place rewards **doesn\u2019t mean** you must do it. If what it rewards is wrong, **leaving is fair**. Just leave because you looked carefully, not because you were cross one day.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019ve read your workplace carefully. What gets rewarded is **long hours** and agreeing with the director in meetings. The people shipping the real work are **rarely mentioned**. It\u2019s been this way under three department heads. Now what?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Those are the **three signs** of a room that\u2019s wrong. The rewarded behaviour has no link to results. The people doing the work stay invisible. And the pattern **survives new leaders**. When all three hold, adapting won\u2019t fix it.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The hard part is telling **a wrong room** from rules you merely **dislike**.',left:{title:'Rules you dislike',items:['Clear and legible','Linked to what it needs','Learn them']},right:{title:'A wrong room',items:['Clear and legible too','Linked to nothing it needs','Consider leaving']}},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s the cost of staying to play. Getting good at a game unrelated to results means **years on a skill** that transfers badly. The next place probably won\u2019t reward it, and those years **don\u2019t come back**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Reading the room **doesn\u2019t oblige you** to satisfy it. Where the rewards link to nothing the place needs, leaving is **a fair answer**, not a failure. Just leave on information, not frustration.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A new director arrives, **the third** in five years. Within months, the same people are praised for the same thing: being seen busy late. The team keeping the service running goes unmentioned. And you notice **you\u2019ve started** staying late too.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The earlier ideas taught you to **read the room** and act on it. This one sets the limit. Adapting is right when the rules are legible and merely disliked. It\u2019s wrong when **the room rewards** the wrong things.'},{kind:'story',text:'Back to the late nights. Being seen busy has **no link to results**. The people doing the work stay invisible. The pattern has outlasted three leaders. All three signs hold, and staying late is a skill that transfers badly.'},{kind:'story',text:'Compare a firm that rewards polished strategy papers. You find the writing tedious. But decisions really do improve after a good paper. That\u2019s a rule you dislike, not a wrong room. There, the honest move is to learn it.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The test is easy to fail **in your favour**. Frustration makes a disliked rule look wrong, which is why all three signs matter. Leaving also costs income, and sometimes a wrong room can be changed from inside.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Learn the rules **where they serve** what the place needs. Where they don\u2019t, adapting is just years spent on the wrong skill. Most people take **far too long** to see it.'}]}}}},1029,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'why-feedback-fails',kind:'book',promise:'Say hard things so people can hear them',about:'Why does so much careful advice change so little? The Mindroll team, who make this app, wrote this essay about it. Often, the words are fine. What goes wrong is how and when people say them. Want to see what to change?',ideas:{'why-feedback-fails_1':{title:'Why People Stop Listening',card:{insight:'When words feel like an attack, people stop listening, even if the words are true.',tryThis:'Before your next hard talk, pick a quiet spot and say you want to help.',skills:['communication','leadership']},review:{prompt:'A coworker keeps ignoring your true advice. What should you check first?',right:'Whether your words feel like an attack to him',wrong:'Whether your words could be more exact',why:'People who feel attacked fight back, so how you say it decides if they hear it.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your best friend says your goal kick was **a bit weak**. Later, a kid who teases you says the same words, **in front of everyone**. Which time do you actually listen?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Same words, but with the teaser you feel **under attack**. You get ready to fight back, and you **stop hearing** the bit about the kick. You\u2019re like a scared hedgehog, rolled up tight, ignoring even the best food.'},{kind:'moral',text:'When advice feels like **an attack**, people defend instead of listening. So **how you say it** decides whether it gets through. Kind and private beats clever and loud.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Over coffee, a peer you trust says your report **buried the key number**. You fix it that afternoon. A month later a director makes the same point **in a team meeting**. What do you do this time?'},{kind:'story',text:'You spend the evening drafting a defence. You can recall the director\u2019s tone exactly. You can\u2019t quite recall the point. The report stays as it is. The sentence was the same both times. Only the standing at stake changed.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Feedback that feels like **a threat** to **your standing** triggers defence. A person who is defending isn\u2019t processing content. They hear the first criticism, then remember **the tone**, and produce an argument instead of a question.'},{kind:'explain',text:'So the framing matters **more than accuracy**. Correct feedback delivered into a threat accomplishes nothing and **costs the relationship**. The fixes are unglamorous: privacy, a warning, a stated wish to help, and time. None improves the content.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The relationship decides **whether anything lands**. Before hard feedback, ask if this one **can carry it**. If it can\u2019t, lower the threat first, or start with the relationship.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Organisations put a great deal into feedback, and get **remarkably little** change in behaviour back. The usual fix is better wording. But what if the problem isn\u2019t **the words** at all?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The essay\u2019s first answer is **the listener\u2019s state**. Feedback that triggers a threat response often isn\u2019t processed as information. A plausible reason: the state that follows may **narrow attention** onto the social danger, not the content.'},{kind:'story',text:'A manager gives two team members the same accurate criticism. One acts on it within a day. The other argues, then changes nothing. The words were identical. What differed was what each relationship made **the words mean**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now think of feedback you once rejected. You probably remember its tone clearly. Its substance is hazier. That\u2019s the threat state at work: you were guarding yourself, so the content never fully arrived.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The idea can become an excuse never to say anything uncomfortable. Careful framing lowers the threat but **can\u2019t guarantee** a listener drops their guard. And no framing rescues feedback that\u2019s simply wrong.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Accuracy still matters, but it only counts **once it arrives**. Framing decides whether it does. So **lower the threat** before you deliver the point, every time.'}]},'why-feedback-fails_2':{title:'Name the Moment',card:{insight:'Name one moment and what it caused, then kindly say what to try next.',tryThis:'Think of advice you gave that named no moment. Name the moment and what to try.',skills:['communication','leadership']},review:{prompt:'A coworker\u2019s talk lost the crowd. Which note helps her most?',right:'The crowd tuned out in section three, so make that section shorter',wrong:'It was a bit unclear, so be clearer next time',why:'A clear moment and what it caused give her something to fix.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your teacher writes *nice work* on your story. Your friend\u2019s story gets a different note. The part where **the dog runs off** was funny, so add more like that. Which of you knows **what to do** next?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Vague words like *try harder* **don\u2019t help**. Useful advice says **what you did**, what it caused and what to try next. It\u2019s easier to hear, too, because it\u2019s about one thing you did, not about you.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Clear advice tells someone **exactly what** to change. But clear advice said meanly still feels like an attack. So be **clear and kind** together.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your manager tells you to **be more proactive**. You nod. On Monday morning you sit at your desk and wonder what, exactly, you\u2019re meant to **do differently**. Sound familiar?'},{kind:'story',text:'Now try a different note. In Tuesday\u2019s meeting, raising the risk before the decision would have helped. That gives you a moment, an effect and a different move. Same concern. Only this version can be acted on.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Vague praise and vague criticism are **equally useless**. What changes behaviour is a specific action, its effect, and **an alternative**. *You were unclear* gives nothing to fix. Section three lost the room gives a place to start.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Specific also **takes the sting out**. A remark about a thing done is easier to accept than a verdict on who you are. But it **doesn\u2019t replace care**. Precise criticism delivered carelessly still feels like a threat.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Name **the moment**, **the effect** and the alternative, not the person. Then deliver it with care. Specific alone gives feedback that\u2019s accurate, usable and **still not heard**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Two notes on the same talk. One says *you were unclear*. The other names **the section** that lost the room and what the audience seemed to think it meant. Which note could **you act on** tomorrow morning?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Specificity is the most **reliable improvement** to feedback. It works for two reasons. First, actionability: you can fix a section, not a trait. Second, it removes **the identity charge**. A point about a thing done is easier to accept.'},{kind:'story',text:'Tom is told he\u2019s **not a team player**. He hears a verdict on his character and spends a week proving otherwise. Had he been told he skipped two handovers, he\u2019d have fixed the handovers. Same point, different weight.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a manager who has learned to be precise. She names the exact slide, the exact effect, the exact fix. But she does it **in front of everyone**. Her feedback is accurate and actionable. It still bounces.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Specificity can be overdone into a list of every small fault, which reads as **a case being built**. And some feedback is about a pattern. Then you\u2019d give two or three instances, not a vague label.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Specificity improves **what is said**. Care decides **whether it\u2019s heard**. You need both, because each one fails on its own.'}]},'why-feedback-fails_3':{title:'The Praise Sandwich',card:{insight:'Hiding bad news between two kind words teaches people to tune out the kind words.',tryThis:'Today, praise one thing a friend did well, and stop there.',skills:['communication','leadership']},review:{prompt:'A great worker on your team keeps missing deadlines. How do you bring it up?',right:'Talk about the deadlines plainly, and praise her on another day',wrong:'Say a nice thing, then the deadlines, then a nice thing',why:'Praise and bad news are both heard better when each one comes alone.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your dad says you\u2019re **such a good helper**, and you tense up. You know what\u2019s coming next: a *but*, then your room or your shoes. Why do **nice words** make you nervous?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Some people use a trick for hard news. Say something nice, then **the problem**, then something nice again. It\u2019s called **a praise sandwich**. But if you always do it, people stop trusting the nice parts.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Hiding a problem between **two nice things** makes people wait for the but. Say the nice thing when it\u2019s true, **on its own**. Say the problem kindly, at another time.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your review begins. Your manager says you\u2019ve done **some really strong work** this quarter. Be honest. Do you feel pleased, or do you **brace yourself** for what comes next?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Many people brace. They\u2019ve learned the pattern: praise, criticism, praise. So opening praise is heard as **a warning**, and a true compliment stops working. Worse, people learn to **discount praise** everywhere, not just inside sandwiches.'},{kind:'explain',text:'The sandwich **hides the message** too. To fit the shape, the criticism gets softened. People routinely leave thinking the talk **went well**. You believe you made the point. They mostly heard the bread.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The answer isn\u2019t brutality. It\u2019s **separating the two**, so each **means something**.',left:{title:'The sandwich',items:['Praise as a warning','Criticism softened to fit','Both get lost']},right:{title:'Kept apart',items:['Praise when earned','Criticism given directly','Both are heard']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Direct and kind **aren\u2019t in tension**. Give praise separately, when it\u2019s earned, and raise problems **directly**. The sandwich tends to manage neither.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'It\u2019s one of the most widely taught feedback techniques: **praise, criticism, praise**. Managers learn it and pass it on. So why does this essay think it **often backfires**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'It has **two costs**. The first falls on praise. Once you learn the pattern, opening praise sounds like a preamble to bad news. The second falls on the criticism, softened until you leave thinking it **went well**.'},{kind:'story',text:'A team lead needs to raise missed deadlines with a strong performer. She says the work is great, mentions the dates in passing, and ends on how valued he is. He leaves pleased. The deadlines keep slipping.'},{kind:'story',text:'Next quarter she splits them. On Monday she thanks him for a specific piece of work, and nothing follows. On Thursday she raises the deadlines plainly. This time **both messages** land, because neither is propping up the other.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Splitting them assumes there\u2019ll be another occasion for praise. Where feedback is rare, it may never come. And naming a real strength that\u2019s **relevant to the point** isn\u2019t a sandwich. The problem is the formula.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The sandwich exists because **criticism threatens**. Its fix is to dilute the message. A better fix keeps the message whole, lowers the threat another way, and lets **praise be praise**.'}]},'why-feedback-fails_4':{title:'Ask Before You Tell',card:{insight:'Ask how they think it went, and they often name your point first.',tryThis:'Next time you must give advice, ask how they think it went first.',skills:['communication','leadership']},review:{prompt:'A project went badly, and you have three points to make. How do you start?',right:'Ask how they think it went, and hear the whole answer',wrong:'List your three points clearly, then ask for questions',why:'A problem people name themselves does not feel like an attack.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your friend\u2019s team just lost. You were going to tell her she stayed **too far back**. Instead, you ask one question: *how do you think it went?* What do you think **she says**?'},{kind:'story',text:'She says she stayed too far back. Now you\u2019re on the same side, looking at the same thing. Then she adds that the coach told her to stay back. You didn\u2019t know that. Asking showed you.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Before you tell someone what went wrong, **ask what they think**. Often they\u2019ll say it first, and people don\u2019t argue with **their own words**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A project went badly, and you\u2019ve got **three points** to raise. You could list them. Instead you open with one question: *how do you think it went?* Then you **stop talking**. What happens next?'},{kind:'story',text:'Your colleague names two of your three points. Then they add one you\u2019d missed: the deadline was moved without anyone telling them. You listen to the whole answer. Your third point now looks different.'},{kind:'explain',text:'A problem someone names themselves **isn\u2019t a threat**. It\u2019s a shared observation, from a source they won\u2019t argue with. Their answer also shows whether the gap is **awareness or capability**. Those need different responses.'},{kind:'explain',text:'There\u2019s **one failure mode**. If you ask only to steer them to your answer, it\u2019s a rhetorical question. People spot it quickly. It\u2019s worse than telling directly, because now they feel **managed** as well as criticised.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Open with **their view**, and listen to the whole answer before adding anything. Most of your feedback arrives on its own. Just make sure **the question is real**.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A manager asks, *so how do you think the launch went?* Her tone makes it clear there\u2019s **a right answer**. Her employee guesses at it, guesses wrong, and is then told. How do they **leave the room** feeling?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Asking first changes **the mechanics** of feedback, but only if the question is real. A genuine question does three jobs. It lets them name the problem. It shows **what kind** of problem it is. It surfaces what you couldn\u2019t see.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take a designer whose mock-up was late. Asked how it went, she says the brief changed twice. She\u2019s already set up a check-in to stop it happening again. The point you came to make has been made, and fixed.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now an analyst whose report missed a key figure. Asked how it went, he names the gap at once. So awareness isn\u2019t the problem. Capability is, and that needs a **different response** from a lecture.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The essay names its own failure mode: a rhetorical question is **worse than telling**. Asking can also misfire with someone anxious and unaware. If a long silence feels like a test, saying it plainly may be kinder.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Ask before you tell, and mean the question. Their answer turns a verdict into **a shared observation**. If you only want to hear **your own point**, just say it.'}]},'why-feedback-fails_5':{title:'A Little Every Week',card:{insight:'Rare feedback feels like big trouble, but a weekly note is just a chat.',tryThis:'Pick one day each week to give a small note, even when nothing is wrong.',skills:['leadership','communication']},review:{prompt:'A boss gives one worker notes every day for two weeks, then stops. What happens?',right:'It feels like trouble, so the worker gets more scared',wrong:'It calms the worker down, since the notes came often',why:'Notes only feel calm when they are a habit that never stops.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'One coach says nothing all term. Then she calls you in for **a big talk**, and your heart races before she starts. Another coach gives **one small tip** at every practice. Which one is easier to hear?'},{kind:'explain',text:'When a talk only happens once in a while, being called over **tells you** something is wrong. You spend the whole time wondering, *how much trouble am I in?* With a tip every week, each one is small, and **mistakes get fixed** early.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Rare advice feels like **a scary event**. Advice every week is **just a chat**. That\u2019s why little and often is easier to hear.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'A meeting called *quick chat* appears in your calendar. It\u2019s from a manager who hasn\u2019t given you feedback **in five months**. You spend two days reading the title for clues. What do you **actually hear** in the meeting?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Mostly the framing. When feedback is rare, every instance is **an event**. Being pulled aside is itself a signal. So you read the tone for how much trouble you\u2019re in, before **any content** gets through.'},{kind:'story',text:'Compare a team where feedback happens weekly, a sentence or two at a time. Corrections are small and early. Nobody prepares. A comment about a slide is just a comment about a slide, not a verdict.'},{kind:'explain',text:'That\u2019s why **cadence** may beat phrasing. Raising the rate asks nobody to get better at delivery, and it tends to **lower the temperature** on its own. The catch: short bursts read as a campaign, which raises the threat.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Often the most useful change isn\u2019t **better phrasing**. It\u2019s **a steadier rhythm**. Make feedback weekly and informal, and keep it going when nothing is wrong.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'You may change **one thing** about how a manager gives feedback. Better wording, better timing, or simply **more often**. Which would you pick, and why?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The essay favours **cadence**. How often feedback happens decides what each instance means. Rare feedback is an event, read for how much trouble you\u2019re in. Routine feedback is **low stakes**, small and early.'},{kind:'story',text:'Two people get the same sentence about a missed figure. One hears it in a weekly catch-up, among other small notes. The other hears it after five silent months. For the second, it\u2019s **a verdict** before a word is said.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now a manager after a bad quarter. She suddenly gives one person daily feedback for two weeks, then stops. To him it doesn\u2019t feel like routine. It feels like **a campaign**, and it\u2019s more threatening than her old silence.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The routine must be genuine, or it backfires. And frequency **doesn\u2019t rescue** bad content. Weekly vague praise is still a mood, and weekly sandwiches still teach people to discount praise.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Feedback usually fails for **structural reasons**, and cadence is the clearest. Raise the rate and keep it steady. The other ideas still decide **what you say**.'}]}}}},1030,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BOOK",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={sourceKey:'why-you-reopen-the-same-tab',kind:'book',promise:'Get back to work without starting over',about:'Why can a quick look at your phone cost you so much time? This essay looks at what happens when you leave a task and come back. These ideas show where the time goes. What keeps pulling you away?',ideas:{'why-you-reopen-the-same-tab_1':{title:'The Rebuild Cost',card:{insight:'A short break costs more than it looks, since you must find your place again.',tryThis:'Before you look away from a task, write down your next step.',skills:['focus','productivity']},review:{prompt:'A coworker says quick questions are fine, since they take ten seconds. What does she miss?',right:'The time it takes to find your place again after each one',wrong:'The few seconds it takes her to ask each question',why:'The question is quick, but finding your place takes a lot longer.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re halfway through **a hard sum**. You know the next step. Then someone calls you to help carry the shopping. It takes **one minute**. When you sit back down, how long until you\u2019re really back?'},{kind:'explain',text:'You stare at the page. *What was I doing?* Why did I write that? You have to **work it out again**, and that takes longer than the break. It also looks just like working, so **nobody notices**, not even you.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A break costs **more than the break**. The slow part is **finding your place** again. So before you stop, write down what you were about to do.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You\u2019re checking figures that **don\u2019t add up**. You\u2019ve ruled out two causes and have a hunch about the third. A message lights up your phone. You glance for **four seconds**. What did that glance cost?'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Maya, an accountant doing exactly this. She turns back to the sheet and stalls. Which two causes did she rule out? What was the hunch? She spends **several minutes** rebuilding where she was. The message took four seconds.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Coming back means rebuilding your **working state**: what you were doing, why, and what came next. That job is the same size whether you left for four seconds or four minutes. So the cost **doesn\u2019t shrink** with the break.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Worse, you **never see it**. Rebuilding feels exactly like working. You\u2019re at the desk, looking at the document. So a day of short interruptions can **produce very little** while feeling completely full, and nothing tells you why.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The glance is the **cheap part**. The expensive part is **rebuilding** where you were, and it hides inside time that feels like work. Write one line before you look away.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Most people count a distraction by **the time spent** on it. Four seconds on a message, thirty on a headline. By that sum, your day barely suffered. So why does it feel like **nothing got done**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The essay\u2019s claim is that the glance is **the cheap part**. Returning to complex work means rebuilding a working state, and that takes much longer than the break. Estimates of the size vary. **The direction** doesn\u2019t.'},{kind:'story',text:'Picture Tom, an engineer chasing a fault. He\u2019s holding three suspects in his head, one already ruled out. A colleague asks a quick question. Ten seconds later Tom is back, staring at the logs, rebuilding the list he just had.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take two days on a team. One has a single **ninety-minute** meeting. The other has thirty three-minute check-ins. The clock says they match. But the second day asks for **thirty rebuilds**, and you pay for every one.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. The cost depends on how much state you were holding. **Routine work** has little to rebuild, so this bites hardest on complex tasks. And not every interruption is waste. Some are worth the price.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A short break doesn\u2019t mean **a short cost**. You pay to rebuild **your working state**, and that bill hides inside time that looks like work. Know the price before you glance.'}]},'why-you-reopen-the-same-tab_2':{title:'The Open Loop',card:{insight:'If you keep going back to a page, a choice is often still open.',tryThis:'Next time you reopen something, name the choice you are putting off. Make it, or pick a time to.',skills:['focus','psychology']},review:{prompt:'You have read the same email four times and not answered. What do you do?',right:'Decide how to answer it, or set a time to decide',wrong:'Close the email so it stops pulling you back',why:'Closing the email does not make the choice, so the pull comes back.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'There\u2019s a pair of trainers online you **keep looking at**. You close the page. An hour later you open it again. Then again after tea. Why do you **keep going back**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'It\u2019s not just habit. You haven\u2019t **decided** yet: buy them, or forget them? Your mind keeps returning to the **unfinished question**. Closing the page doesn\u2019t answer it, so the pull comes straight back.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The page keeps opening because **a choice** is still waiting. Make the choice, or pick **a time** to make it. Then the pull stops.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'There\u2019s an email you\u2019ve **read four times** this week. You haven\u2019t answered it. Each time you open it, skim it, and close it again. What are you **actually doing** when you go back?'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Ravi. A supplier has emailed a new price, and he can\u2019t decide whether to accept it. So he keeps checking the message, then the comparison sheet, then the message again. Each return tries to settle what the last one didn\u2019t.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'Ravi can **close the tab** a dozen times. Only **closing the loop** stops the returning.',left:{title:'Closing the tab',items:['Removes the reminder','Leaves the decision open','The urge returns']},right:{title:'Closing the loop',items:['Makes or schedules the decision','No reason to return','The tab can go']}},{kind:'explain',text:'There are **two ways out**. Make the decision, or **schedule it** with a set time and the question to answer then. The essay says scheduling seems to work nearly as well as deciding, though the studies behind that are few.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A tab you keep reopening is **a symptom**, not a distraction. Find the decision underneath. Make it, even badly, or **book a time** for it.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Think of what **you return to** most. A message you\u2019ve read four times. A document you keep checking. A comparison you\u2019ve studied again and again. Is that **really distraction**?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The essay reads the reopening as **a symptom**. Each return is an attempt to settle something the earlier returns didn\u2019t. So closing the tab does nothing. The **unresolved item** simply generates the urge again.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Jo, who has a job offer to answer. She rereads it every evening and checks the salary sheet again. Nothing changes. On Friday she writes: *decide Monday at nine, yes or no*. The checking stops that night.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take a shopping basket left full for a week. You visit it daily, adding nothing. The question underneath is small: is this worth the money? Answer it, even badly, and **the visits end**. The basket was never the problem.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Sometimes you go back because **new information** arrived, and looking again is the work. Deciding badly is cheap for a reply, costly for a choice you can\u2019t undo. There, scheduling is the honest move.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Each return is your mind **knocking on** an open question. Answer it, or give it **a fixed time**. A vague plan to think later only leaves the loop open.'}]},'why-you-reopen-the-same-tab_3':{title:'The Breaks You Start',card:{insight:'Many breaks start with you, and they come right when the work gets hard.',tryThis:'Today, each time you leave a task, jot down what you were doing just before.',skills:['focus','psychology']},review:{prompt:'You keep leaving a report to read the news. What should you check first?',right:'What you were doing just before you left',wrong:'Which alerts to turn off on your phone',why:'You start these breaks yourself, so the spot where you left tells you more.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'You sit down to do homework. **Nobody calls you**. No phone buzzes. Ten minutes later you\u2019re in the kitchen, looking in the fridge. **Who interrupted** you?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Often, you did. Lots of breaks start **inside your own head**. And they come at a particular moment: **the hard bit**. The sum stops making sense, and suddenly a snack feels like a very good idea.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Not every break comes from outside. Many start **when it gets hard**, which is when you learn the most. Notice **what you were doing** just before you left.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You switch off notifications, close the door and put your phone in a drawer. An hour later you\u2019ve checked **the news** three times. Nothing called you there. So **what did**?'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Lena, writing a tricky proposal. She reaches a paragraph where the argument goes vague. Within seconds she\u2019s on a news site. It doesn\u2019t feel like escape. It feels like a sensible decision about what to do next.'},{kind:'explain',text:'In studies of knowledge work, a **large share** of interruptions are self-made. They cluster at moments of difficulty, ambiguity or minor tedium. So the switch arrives **just as the work** stops being fluent, which is when it starts to matter.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Most focus tools target **outside noise**: alerts, open offices, meetings. None of them sees this kind. But your trigger tends to be **the same each time**. Spot it by noting what you were doing just before you left.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Many interruptions **come from you**, right at the hard part. A general resolve to concentrate gives you nothing to act on. **Your trigger** does.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'A whole industry targets **outside interruptions**: notifications, open-plan noise, meetings. Yet people still leave their work again and again. With all that handled, **what\u2019s still pulling** them away?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The essay\u2019s answer is **self-interruption**. In observational studies it makes up a large share of all interruptions. You leave before anything prompts you, usually at a point of difficulty. And it feels like **a choice**, not an escape.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Omar, debugging code. Each time an error makes no sense, he opens his messages. It feels like a reasonable next thing to do. Only when he logs his exits for a day does he see the pattern: **every one** followed confusion.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Bea, a designer. Her exits come at a different moment: the **dull bits**, like resizing images. Her trigger isn\u2019t difficulty but tedium. Same habit, different cause, so she needs a different fix from Omar.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Not every exit is an escape. Sometimes leaving is a real judgement that **something matters more**. The trouble is that an escape always looks like that judgement. And the trigger lasts seconds, so it\u2019s easy to miss.'},{kind:'moral',text:'The interruptions no tool can block **are your own**. They gather where the work turns hard, unclear or dull. Find **your trigger**, because a trigger repeats and resolve doesn\u2019t.'}]},'why-you-reopen-the-same-tab_4':{title:'The Blocker Trap',card:{insight:'Site blockers fail because the urge to leave comes from the task, not the site.',tryThis:'Before you start work, write your next step in plain words. Then begin.',skills:['focus','productivity']},review:{prompt:'Last month you got an app that blocks websites. Now it is off. What helps more?',right:'Making the next step clear before the hard part starts',wrong:'A stronger blocker that is much harder to turn off',why:'The urge to leave comes from the task, so make the hard part clear.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Your mum hides the games console so you\u2019ll **finish your project**. It works for a day. By Wednesday you\u2019re drawing on your hand and staring out of the window. Why didn\u2019t **hiding it** work?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The console was never the real reason. You wanted to stop because **the project felt hard**, or you didn\u2019t know what to do next. Hiding one escape just sends you to another. **A clear next step** helps far more.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Blocking the fun thing **doesn\u2019t fix** the hard thing. Make your next step small and clear, like *write three facts about frogs*. Then there\u2019s **less to run from**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You install a blocker that **locks you out** of news sites during work hours. The first week is wonderful. By the third, you\u2019ve **switched it off**. What went wrong?'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Kit, a student with an essay due. He blocks every distracting site. Then he hits a section he doesn\u2019t understand, and the urge to leave arrives anyway. He tidies his desk instead. Later he disables the blocker. It takes one click.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'A blocker acts on **where you go**. The urge comes from **the task itself**.',left:{title:'What blockers touch',items:['The destination','What\u2019s within reach','One click to undo']},right:{title:'What drives leaving',items:['The task','Difficulty or ambiguity','This is hard, make it stop']}},{kind:'explain',text:'So the fix sits **upstream**. Most self-interruption happens when the next step is unclear. Make that step specific, and there\u2019s **no ambiguity** to escape from. Instead of working on the essay, you *list three points for section two*.'},{kind:'moral',text:'A blocker guards the door, but **you hold the key**. The lasting fix changes the moment of difficulty. Make **the next step** so clear there\u2019s nothing vague to flee.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'You can lock yourself out of any site in seconds. Blockers do it well, and they **work for a while**. Then, sooner or later, they get **switched off**. If the tool does its job, why doesn\u2019t the habit end?'},{kind:'explain',text:'The essay\u2019s answer: blockers treat **the symptom**. The urge to leave comes from the task, from difficulty, ambiguity or distaste. Block one destination and the urge finds another. Or you disable the tool, which takes **one click**.'},{kind:'story',text:'Take Nadia, writing a grant bid with every site blocked. She reaches the budget section, which she dreads. She can\u2019t reach the news, so she reorganises her files instead. The blocker worked. The escape still happened.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Ben, facing the same kind of bid. Before starting, he writes his next action: *copy last year\u2019s staff costs into row four*. When the budget section comes, there\u2019s nothing vague to flee. He just starts.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Blockers do help in **a narrow case**: when the habit is the main problem and the work itself is fine. And in a truly open problem, finding the next step is the hard part.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Locking the exit leaves **the reason to leave** in place. Most people flee when the next move is unclear. So **fix the task**, not the destination, and you\u2019ll have less to escape.'}]},'why-you-reopen-the-same-tab_5':{title:'Stop at an Easy Spot',card:{insight:'Stop when you know what comes next, and the next day starts fast.',tryThis:'Today, stop one step early at an easy spot. Write the next step down before you leave.',skills:['productivity','habits']},review:{prompt:'You are tired, but you know what comes next. When do you stop?',right:'Now, with the next step written down',wrong:'After you finish the whole part, however long it takes',why:'When you stop where you know what comes next, starting again is easy.'},basics:[{kind:'hook',text:'Some mornings you sit down to your homework and **just can\u2019t start**. You stare at a blank page, unsure where to begin. Other days you **dive straight in**. What makes the difference?'},{kind:'explain',text:'Imagine stopping tonight **one step early**, at an easy bit. You stick a note on your book: next, draw the map. Tomorrow you open it, read the note and **start drawing**. No staring, no wondering.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Finishing everything means **starting from nothing** next time. Stop where you know what comes next, and write it down. Then starting is **easy**.'}],standard:[{kind:'hook',text:'You finish a section at six, tired but pleased. The desk is clear. Next morning you open the file and **face a blank**. Twenty minutes pass before you write anything. Why is **starting again** so slow?'},{kind:'story',text:'Writers have used a fix for about a century. Hemingway would end his day **mid-flow**, while the writing still came easily and he could see what came next. So each morning began from a known point, not from nothing.'},{kind:'explain',text:'Here\u2019s why it works. The cost of returning is mostly working out **where you were**. A note stating the next step removes most of it. Writing that note takes **about fifteen seconds**.'},{kind:'contrast',lead:'The difference is **where you stop**, not **how much** you get done.',left:{title:'A clean finish',items:['Stop when it\u2019s done','Start from a blank','First move unknown']},right:{title:'A loose end',items:['Stop at an easy point','Next step written down','First move ready']}},{kind:'moral',text:'Don\u2019t stop at the end of something hard. Stop at an **easy point**, with **the next step** written down. Tomorrow you\u2019ll start from somewhere.'}],deep:[{kind:'hook',text:'Most of us stop work when we\u2019re **spent**: the section done, or the energy gone. It feels like the natural place to end. What if it\u2019s **the worst place** to stop?'},{kind:'explain',text:'This idea completes the first one. If returning mostly costs you finding your place, the cheapest fix is to **record your place** before leaving. A note naming the next action does it. It\u2019s **an instruction** from the only person who knows.'},{kind:'story',text:'Writers have relied on this for about a century. Hemingway stopped for the day **while it flowed**, with the next move already clear to him. That way the following day picked up from a known place, not a blank.'},{kind:'story',text:'Now take Dee, an analyst, at the end of a long day. Instead of fighting through the last tricky query, she stops before it. She writes: *join the two tables on customer ID*. Next morning she\u2019s working within a minute.'},{kind:'explain',breaks:!0,text:'Here\u2019s **where it breaks**. Some work has little state to rebuild, so a clean finish costs you nothing. Stopping early also gives up a fluent stretch that may not return on cue. And a vague note, like carry on, **names no action**.'},{kind:'moral',text:'Stopping exhausted means restarting at **the hard part**, with no plan. That\u2019s where the tab gets reopened. Stop **mid-stride** instead, and leave yourself the next step.'}]}}}},1031,[]);
