__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"LESSON_PLANS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}}),e.planFor=function(o){if(!o)return null;const n=o.startsWith('card_')?o.slice(5):o;return t[n]??null};const t={meditations_1:{opening:'misconception',misconception:{belief:'The Stoic move is to stop feeling things \u2014 to be unbothered.',reality:'Marcus never asks for that. He asks you to notice the verdict you added to the event, because the verdict is the part you can change.'},visual:{kind:'contrast',left:{title:'The event',items:['Two-line reply','Cut up in traffic','Meeting moved']},right:{title:'The verdict',items:['\u201cThey think I\u2019m beneath them\u201d','\u201cHe did that on purpose\u201d','\u201cI don\u2019t matter here\u201d']}},limits:'This does not make every reaction a mistake. Some verdicts are correct \u2014 the reply was contemptuous. The practice is to check, not to assume you were wrong to feel it.',reflect:'What was the last thing that stung you? Write the bare event in one line. Which words did you have to leave out?',recap:['An upset arrives as an event plus a verdict.','The verdict is yours and can be inspected.','Name the bare fact first; the feeling usually shrinks.']},meditations_2:{opening:'problem',example:{text:'The one person who can approve the plan is away for two weeks. Read as an interruption, the work stops and you chase them. Read as terrain, you spend the fortnight on the part of the plan that never needed approval \u2014 and find it was the weak part.',caption:'The same fortnight, two readings'},misconception:{belief:'\u201cThe obstacle is the way\u201d means every setback is secretly good for you.',reality:'Marcus is not consoling. He is instructing: an obstacle changes the shape of the work, and the work continues in the new shape \u2014 now.'},limits:'Some obstacles are just obstacles, and the honest response is to remove them. The test is whether you are doing anything while blocked besides pushing on the block.',reflect:'What is blocking you this week? What does the block make possible that a clear road would not?',recap:['A block is information about the terrain.','Work continues in a different shape, not later.','If you are only chasing the block, it has taken the work hostage.']},meditations_3:{opening:'problem',misconception:{belief:'Remembering you will die is morbid \u2014 a way of making everything feel pointless.',reality:'Marcus uses it as altitude. Seen from high enough, the audience you fear is small and temporary, and the difficult thing becomes sayable.'},visual:{kind:'spectrum',low:'Zoomed in: this meeting, these five people, this week',high:'Zoomed out: none of them will remember; you will not either',note:'Courage lives further right than you usually stand.'},limits:'The tool is for courage, not for shrugging off responsibility. \u201cNobody will remember\u201d is not a reason to do the careless thing; it is a reason to do the right one without fear.',reflect:'Name the audience you are performing for right now. How many of them are actually paying attention?',recap:['Most fear is about status in a small group.','The group is temporary, and so are you.','Use the thought to say the true thing, not to say nothing.']},meditations_4:{opening:'problem',visual:{kind:'steps',title:'The instruction, in order',steps:['Look at the task in front of you','Do it well','Stop \u2014 do not run ahead into the remainder','Take the next one']},misconception:{belief:'This is advice to work harder and not rest.',reality:'Half of it is an instruction to stop. The distress is in the imagined pile behind the task, and the pile is not real yet.'},limits:'Someone has to look at the whole list occasionally, or the next right thing is chosen badly. The discipline is about where your attention lives while your hands are busy.',reflect:'What is the next twenty minutes of the thing you are avoiding? Only that.',recap:['Do the task in front of you, well.','Do not let the mind run into the remainder.','The remainder is where the distress lives, and it is not real yet.']},meditations_5:{opening:'decision',misconception:{belief:'Understanding why someone acted badly means excusing it.',reality:'Marcus keeps the two apart. Understanding is cheap and stops the anger; approving is a separate decision you still get to make.'},limits:'Some people do act in bad faith and know it. The idea is a default, not a law; it buys you calm while you find out which case you are in.',reflect:'Think of the last person who annoyed you. Finish their sentence: \u201cFrom where I stood, I was only\u2026\u201d',recap:['People experience themselves as responding, not initiating.','Anger is expensive; understanding is cheap.','Understanding and approving are different acts.']},meditations_6:{opening:'misconception',misconception:{belief:'If the work was not recognised, it was not good.',reality:'You control the quality of the attempt. Recognition is decided by people who were mostly not paying attention.'},reflect:'Where are you currently letting an audience grade work they did not watch you do?',recap:['Judge the attempt, which is yours.','The reception belongs to others.','Tying the two together hands the verdict to the inattentive.']},meditations_7:{opening:'problem',reflect:'What did you not want to do this morning, and what would have happened if wanting had been the criterion?'},meditations_8:{opening:'decision',misconception:{belief:'The best revenge is a better, quieter revenge.',reality:'The instruction is to be unlike the person \u2014 which means no revenge at all, because revenge is the thing you would be copying.'},reflect:'Who are you tempted to answer in kind? What would \u201cunlike them\u201d look like tomorrow?',recap:['Answering in kind makes you the thing you resent.','The only clean response is to be different.','That is harder and it is the whole point.']},meditations_9:{opening:'example',reflect:'Take something that is worrying you and write it as three lines of fact. What disappears?'},meditations_10:{opening:'problem',reflect:'How much of your week goes to watching other people \u2014 and what would you do with the hour you got back?'},meditations_11:{opening:'misconception',misconception:{belief:'A settled mind is one that has finished deciding.',reality:'Marcus treats nothing as settled \u2014 he re-argues the same points every morning, because a decision made once decays.'}},meditations_12:{opening:'problem',reflect:'Whose good opinion are you working for this month? Will they hold it in a year?'},'how-to-disagree-well_1':{opening:'decision',visual:{kind:'steps',title:'Before you answer',steps:['Say their view back: \u201cSo your view is\u2026\u201d','Adjust until they say \u201cyes, that\u2019s it\u201d','Only now respond \u2014 to that version']},misconception:{belief:'Restating their view is a courtesy you offer before winning.',reality:'It is a test you run on yourself. If their view turns out stronger than you assumed, you have learned the most important thing in the room.'},limits:'It costs time, and in a fast exchange it can read as stalling. Use it where the disagreement matters, not on every passing comment.',reflect:'Pick a view you disagree with. Write it so its holder would say \u201cyes, exactly.\u201d Could you?',recap:['State their case until they agree you have it.','It locates the real disagreement.','It changes what they are doing: assessing, not defending.']},'how-to-disagree-well_2':{opening:'example',example:{text:'A team argues about whether remote work is productive. One means output per hour, and has numbers. One means whether juniors learn as fast, which the numbers do not measure. One prefers the office and reaches for whichever argument is nearest. Three conversations, one meeting, none of them winnable.',caption:'Three disagreements wearing one name'},visual:{kind:'contrast',left:{title:'Kinds of disagreement',items:['About what is true','About what matters','About what a word means']},right:{title:'How each is settled',items:['Evidence','Not by evidence \u2014 by values, openly','By noticing you meant two things']}},reflect:'The argument you keep having \u2014 is it about facts, values, or a definition? Say which.',recap:['Most arguments mix three kinds of dispute.','Only the factual kind is settled by evidence.','Sort before you argue; two of the three usually dissolve.']},'how-to-disagree-well_3':{opening:'problem',misconception:{belief:'Saying what would change your mind is a concession.',reality:'It is the strongest signal that you are reasoning. It also tests whether such evidence exists \u2014 and if nothing would, you were holding an identity, not a belief.'},limits:'Some positions are values, and no evidence should move them. The honest answer there is \u201cnothing \u2014 this is a value\u201d, which is also worth knowing.',reflect:'For one belief you hold strongly: what, specifically, would change your mind? Write it down before the next argument.',recap:['Name the evidence that would move you.','It converts a position into a hypothesis.','If nothing would, notice what you are actually holding.']},'how-to-disagree-well_4':{opening:'decision',misconception:{belief:'Beating the version of the argument in front of you counts as winning.',reality:'It establishes nothing, because the position was never that version. Only the strongest form is worth engaging.'},reflect:'Who is the most capable person who holds the view you disagree with? Not the loudest \u2014 the best.',recap:['Every position has a weak form that is easy to beat.','Beating it changes nobody\u2019s mind.','Find the strongest advocate and answer them.']},'how-to-disagree-well_5':{opening:'problem',visual:{kind:'steps',title:'Signs the exchange is done',steps:['Positions are being restated, not developed','Someone is arguing to avoid conceding','Repetition has started']},reflect:'Which conversation in your life has reached the stable point? What would naming it, kindly, sound like?',recap:['Some disagreements are stable: both understand and still differ.','Pushing past that point produces entrenchment.','Name what was settled and what remains, and stop.']},'environment-beats-willpower_1':{opening:'example',example:{text:'Two people want to read more. One resolves to read every evening. The other moves the book to the pillow and the phone to the kitchen. In March, only one of them is still reading, and it is not the one who resolved harder.',caption:'Friction decides, not resolve'},visual:{kind:'spectrum',low:'More friction: the thing you want less of',high:'Less friction: the thing you want more of',note:'Move objects, not intentions.'},reflect:'What is one object you could move tonight that would change tomorrow?',recap:['Behaviour follows friction more reliably than intention.','Add friction to what you want less of; remove it from what you want more of.','Move objects.']},'environment-beats-willpower_2':{opening:'misconception',misconception:{belief:'Successful people simply have more willpower.',reality:'Whether willpower depletes is contested; what is not is that plans needing it every day fail on the days you are tired, stressed or hungry \u2014 the days that decide.'},limits:'Willpower is not useless \u2014 it gets the structure built in the first place. It is a poor thing to run on daily.',reflect:'Which habit of yours currently runs on willpower alone? What would it take for it to happen on your worst day?',recap:['Self-control varies with sleep, stress and hunger.','A plan that needs it daily fails under load.','Use willpower to build structure, not to run it.']},'environment-beats-willpower_3':{opening:'problem',visual:{kind:'contrast',left:{title:'Deciding daily',items:['Decided late, tired','Each time a chance to slip','Decays toward the easy option']},right:{title:'Deciding once',items:['Decided fresh, in advance','No re-decision moment','Runs without you']}},reflect:'Which recurring decision could you settle for the month, in writing, today?',recap:['Every repeat is a chance to decide differently.','Re-decisions happen in worse conditions than the first one.','Convert repeats into standing arrangements.']}}},2952,[]);
