__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={analects_1:{pictureCaption:'Two pots on one sill. The dry one gets the water; the one already standing wet gets none. Same care, opposite treatment.',basics:{title:'The right advice depends on who asks',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TWO FRIENDS, ONE QUESTION',prompt:'Two friends ask you: should I try out for the team? One always rushes in. One always hangs back. Should they get the same answer?',options:['Yes. Fair is fair.','No. They need different answers.'],answer:1,reveal:'The one who rushes needs to hear: slow down. The shy one needs to hear: go for it. Same question, two different people, two answers.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'CHINA, ABOUT 2,500 YEARS AGO',text:'Confucius was a famous teacher in China. Two of his students asked him the same thing. Should we act right away on what we learn? He told one to wait and ask his family first. He told the other to go ahead.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why he did it',text:'A third student asked why. Confucius said the first one always rushed, so he held him back. The second always held back, so he pushed him on. Each got what he was short of.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Good advice is not the same for all. First look at the person. Then tell them what they need, even if it is the opposite of what you told someone else.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE TWO POT PLANTS',picture:!0,text:'Two plants sit on a windowsill. One has dry, cracked soil. The other is wet. You water the dry one and leave the wet one alone. Same care, opposite fixes.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time a friend asks for advice, first ask yourself: what do they already do too much of? Then help them with the other side.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Two kids are learning to swim. Mia jumps in without looking. Sam won\u2019t leave the steps. What would Confucius tell them?',options:['Mia: look first. Sam: jump in.','Both of them: look before you jump.','Both of them: be brave and jump in.'],answer:0,why:'Each one needs a push away from what they already do too much of. One rule for both would help one and hurt the other.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'ONE QUESTION, TWO ANSWERS',text:'Two students asked Confucius the same thing: should I act at once on what I have just learned? Zilu, the bold one, was told to consult his father and elder brother first. Ran Qiu, the hesitant one, was told to act now. A third student asked why.'},{kind:'explain',title:'His answer',text:'Ran Qiu holds himself back, Confucius said, so I pushed him forward. Zilu charges ahead, so I held him back. The contradiction was not a slip. It was the method.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Water goes to the pot with cracked, dry soil, and none to the one already wet. A single rule for both would drown one or parch the other.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Advice is a diagnosis, not a rule. The right instruction depends on who is standing in front of you, and it is often the opposite of what the next person needs.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Pulled out as a list',items:['Be careful','Be bold','Two rules that cancel']},right:{title:'Kept with the person',items:['Careful, for the one who rushes','Bold, for the one who hesitates','Two diagnoses that fit']},note:'This is why the book resists summary. The answers only make sense attached to whoever asked.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE ALL-HANDS SLIDE',text:'A manager ends the meeting with one line: *move faster, but don\u2019t cut corners.* The colleague who already rushes hears *move faster.* The one who already dithers hears *don\u2019t cut corners.* Both nod. Both leave doing exactly what they did before.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Distrust general advice, including yours',text:'Each listener filters general advice and keeps the half that confirms their habit. Advice that changes anything has to be aimed at the habit, which means you have to know the habit first.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before you next give advice, ask one question first: what does this person already do too much of? Then aim at that.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A friend who overthinks every choice has spent a month deciding whether to accept a job offer. What would Confucius\u2019s method suggest?',options:['Push her to decide: hesitation is her habit, not rashness','Tell her to take more time, since big choices need care','Give her the standard advice you give everyone about jobs','Refuse to advise, since only she can know what she wants'],answer:0,why:'The general rule says be careful. She already is, to a fault. The useful advice targets what she overdoes.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'HOW THE BOOK IS MADE',text:'The Analects were compiled by his students and their students after Confucius died. They record answers given to particular people. When several students ask about the same virtue, each gets a different reply, and the book seldom says why. One passage is a rare exception.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'THE EXCEPTION',text:'Zilu and Ran Qiu each ask whether to act at once on what they hear. Zilu is told to defer to his father and elder brother; Ran Qiu, to go ahead. Asked to explain, Confucius says one hangs back and needed urging, the other overreaches and needed restraining.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Nobody here asks what plants need in general. Each pot is looked at first: water for the cracked soil, nothing for the wet. That is diagnosis.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Confucius taught by diagnosis. The same question got opposite answers because the right instruction depends on what the asker already overdoes, and a rule detached from its listener goes wrong.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHY GENERAL ADVICE FAILS',nodes:['One rule for everyone','Each listener filters it','Each keeps the half they like','Habits get stronger, not weaker'],note:'The general version does not merely fail to help. It hands each person a reason to carry on.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the method needs',text:'It assumes the teacher knows the student. Confucius taught his students closely over years, and the book is full of his remarks on their temperaments. Diagnosis was possible because he had watched them for a long time.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Most advice-givers lack that knowledge. Aimed at a stranger, the opposite of the general rule is only a guess, and a bad guess pushes the rash further. The method can also become a licence to tell anyone whatever you like. Without real acquaintance, the general rule is the safer default.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Whose advice changed you most? Was it general, or aimed at a habit of yours that they had noticed?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these misuses the method?',options:['Telling a stranger online the reverse of what everyone else told them','Telling a cautious junior colleague to ship before it feels ready','Telling a reckless friend to sleep on a big decision overnight','Giving two of your children opposite advice about the same choice'],answer:0,why:'Diagnosis needs knowledge of the person. Reversing the usual advice for someone you do not know is not tailoring. It is guessing.'}]}},analects_2:{pictureCaption:'He holds the door so it cannot swing back. He needs no idea what the stranger likes, only what he himself would hate.',bridge:'Idea 1 fitted the advice to the person. This is the one rule he offered for everyone, and it is deliberately small.',basics:{title:'Start with what you would hate',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'A BIRTHDAY SURPRISE',text:'You love loud games. So for your friend\u2019s birthday you plan a huge, noisy surprise party. But your friend is shy. She hates being stared at. The party you would love is her worst day.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Two ways to say the rule',text:'One way says: treat others the way you want to be treated. But people want different things. Another way says: don\u2019t do to others what you would hate done to you. Confucius, a teacher in China long ago, used the second.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Don\u2019t do to other people what you would not want done to you. It is easier to know what you would hate than to guess what someone else would like.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'What I\u2019d like',items:['A big surprise party','Jokes about me','Loud music in the car']},right:{title:'What I\u2019d hate',items:['Being laughed at','Being left out','Having my things taken']},note:'People like very different things. Hardly anyone likes the things on the right.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'He holds the door so it won\u2019t swing back in the next person\u2019s face. He does not need to know what they like. He knows what he would hate.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before you tease someone today, ask: would I hate this if it were me? If yes, skip it.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A new boy joins your class. You don\u2019t know what he likes yet. How can this rule help?',options:['Don\u2019t do what you would hate on your first day','Do what you would most enjoy if you were new','Wait until you know just what he likes'],answer:0,why:'You may not know what he likes. You do know what feels bad on a first day, like being stared at or left out.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A NEW COLLEAGUE',prompt:'You want to welcome a new colleague. Which rule is safer to work from?',options:['Do for them what you would enjoy','Don\u2019t do to them what you would resent'],answer:1,reveal:'You might enjoy a welcome lunch with the whole team. They might find it an ordeal. But you can be fairly sure they would resent being ignored, talked over or left off the invitation. The second rule rarely misfires.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'ONE WORD FOR LIFE',text:'A student, Zigong, asked Confucius for a single word he could practise for his whole life. The answer was reciprocity, and Confucius defined it in the negative: what you do not want for yourself, do not impose on others.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When unsure how to treat someone, work from what you would resent rather than what you would like. Restraint needs no guess about another person\u2019s tastes.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'The positive rule',items:['Do what you would want done','Assumes your tastes are shared','Can impose your preferences']},right:{title:'The negative rule',items:['Don\u2019t do what you would resent','Assumes only shared dislikes','Asks only for restraint']},note:'What people resent overlaps far more than what they enjoy, which is why the negative form is easier to use.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Holding a door is not a guess about what a stranger wants. It is refusing to do what anyone would resent: letting it swing shut in their face.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where it stops',text:'It is a floor, not a programme. It will keep you from being cruel, but on its own it will not make you useful. Confucius offered it as a place to begin, not as the whole of goodness.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before your next piece of feedback, joke or favour, ask: would I resent this in their place? If so, stop there.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Your flatmate is grieving. In their place, you would want to be taken out and distracted. What does the negative rule suggest?',options:['Avoid what you would resent, such as being pressed to talk','Take them out, since that is what you would want yourself','Leave them entirely alone, since you cannot know their wishes','Ask the other flatmates what they would want in the same case'],answer:0,why:'What you would enjoy may be wrong for them. What you would resent, being pushed or crowded, is a much safer guide.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'A STUDENT CORRECTED',text:'In one exchange Zigong announces that what he does not want others to do to him, he does not want to do to them. Confucius tells him that this is not yet within his reach. In another, he names the same principle as the one word to practise for life.'},{kind:'term',word:'Shu',meaning:'The word Confucius gave Zigong, usually translated reciprocity or consideration: using yourself as the measure, so that what you would not accept you do not impose on others.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Do not impose on others what you would not want yourself. The negative form asks for restraint rather than knowledge of other people\u2019s tastes, which makes it both safer and easier to apply.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the negative is safer',text:'A positive rule, to do for others what you would want, quietly assumes your preferences are everyone\u2019s. It licenses the generous bore and the zealous helper. The negative rule assumes only that the things people resent overlap, which they mostly do.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it is still hard',text:'Applying it means honestly imagining yourself in the other position at the moment you are the one able to impose. That is why he told Zigong it was beyond him. The rule is simple to state and slow to practise.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Holding the door is restraint, not a guess at the stranger\u2019s tastes. He only declines to do what he would resent himself.'},{kind:'limits',text:'It is a floor, not a programme: it prevents specific harms and does not by itself make you useful. Your own resentments are still the measure, so a person who would not mind harshness can excuse giving it. Elsewhere the Analects add an active duty: to help others stand as you would wish to stand.'},{kind:'recap',points:['The negative form asks for restraint, not mind-reading.','It is easy to state and hard to practise honestly.','It is a floor. Helping others is a further duty.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HONEST OBJECTION',prompt:'A manager says: \u201cI\u2019d want blunt criticism, so I give it to everyone.\u201d What does the negative rule add?',options:['It asks what he would resent, such as humiliation, which is widely shared','Nothing: the manager is already following the rule as stated','It forbids all criticism, since nobody enjoys receiving it','It requires asking each person exactly how to treat them'],answer:0,why:'The positive rule follows the manager\u2019s taste. The negative rule asks what would be resented, and being humiliated in front of others is resented almost universally.'}]}},analects_3:{pictureCaption:'A tall stack of copied pages, and a blank sheet with the pen lying untouched. Plenty taken in. Nothing yet thought through.',basics:{title:'Read it, then use it',beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'If I read a lot, I will learn a lot.',reality:'Only if you use what you read. Confucius, a great teacher in China long ago, said reading without thinking is wasted work. And thinking without reading is risky.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'AFTER THE VIDEO',text:'You watch a video on how to do a skateboard trick. It all makes sense. You feel like you get it. Then you step on the board and fall straight off.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Learning and thinking need each other. Reading without thinking leaves you full of facts you can\u2019t use. Thinking without reading leaves you sure of things that are wrong.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Only reading',items:['You know lots of facts','You never test them','You can\u2019t use them']},right:{title:'Only thinking',items:['You make up your own answers','You never check them','You are sure, and wrong']}},{kind:'picture',caption:'A big pile of copied pages. Next to it, a blank page and a pen no one has picked up. Lots of reading. No thinking yet.'},{kind:'try',text:'After the next thing you learn, do one thing with it. Explain it to someone at home, or try it out.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Sam reads ten books about chess but never plays a game. What is he missing?',options:['Trying it out for himself','Reading even more chess books','A harder book about chess'],answer:0,why:'He has done the reading. He has not tested any of it. Playing is where the reading turns into skill.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'FORTY BOOKS A YEAR',text:'You read forty books last year, plus the summaries and the podcasts. Now name one decision any of them changed. If the answer is one or none, you have been consuming, not learning.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Study that is never tested against your own judgement makes you well-read and useless. Judgement that never studies makes confident nonsense. You need both, in turns.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the first trap is pleasant',text:'The feeling of being informed comes from the consuming, not from understanding. So it arrives whether or not you have understood anything, and it is enjoyable. Nothing warns you that the learning has stopped.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the second is so old',text:'The other failure is reasoning from first principles about a field you have not bothered to learn. You arrive, with great confidence, at mistakes the field corrected long ago.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Pages and pages copied out, and a blank sheet with the pen untouched. That is the first trap: taking in without thinking.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Alternate',steps:['Read or listen to one thing.','Before reading more, apply it to a real decision.','Write down the strongest objection to it.','After your own reasoning, check what people who studied the question concluded.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A friend has worked out, from scratch, a new theory of nutrition. He has read nothing on the subject. Which failure is he at risk of?',options:['Confident nonsense: thinking without learning','Wasted labour: learning without thinking','Neither, since original thought is always valuable','Both at once, since he has neither read nor tested'],answer:0,why:'He has done plenty of thinking and no study, so he risks repeating errors the field has already corrected.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',word:'Xue and si',meaning:'The two words in the passage. *Xue* is learning: studying what others have handed down. *Si* is thinking: reflecting on it for yourself. Confucius says each fails without the other.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Learning without thinking is wasted labour, and thinking without learning is dangerous. Each corrects the other: study gives thought its material, and thought tests what study supplies.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Two failures, two people',text:'The saying is a pair, and each half describes a different person. One has read everything and can do nothing with it. The other has read nothing and is sure of everything. Most of us lean one way, and the half we need is the other one.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE CORRECTING LOOP',nodes:['Study supplies material','Thought tests it on a real case','The test exposes a gap','The gap sends you back to study'],loop:!0,note:'Break the loop at either point and you get one of his two failures.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The stack is learning without thinking: copied, kept, never tested. The untouched pen is the half that would make the stack worth having.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The modern version',text:'Summaries, talks and feeds make the first failure cheap. They produce the feeling of being informed at speed, and the feeling is pleasant enough to pass for understanding. The test is whether anything you took in has changed a decision.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The pair is not symmetrical in his own practice. Elsewhere he says he once spent a whole day without eating and a night without sleeping, just thinking, and gained nothing: better to learn. He leaned towards study. And no fixed ratio exists: some fields need years of study before judgement is worth much.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What is the last thing you read and agreed with entirely? Name one decision it should change, or one objection to it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A team reads every report on its competitors but never tests a single claim against its own customers. Which half of the saying applies?',options:['Learning without thinking: material gathered, never tested','Thinking without learning: confident guesses about the market','Neither: reading competitor reports is already a kind of thinking','Both halves equally, since the team has achieved nothing'],answer:0,why:'They have done the study. What is missing is judgement applied to a real case, which is where the reading would be tested.'}]}},analects_4:{pictureCaption:'The last bowl set carefully in place before anyone sits. Nobody is watching. A small act done with care, night after night, teaches the care.',bridge:'Idea 3 said study counts only when it is used. This turns from study to conduct: what you practise is what you become.',basics:{title:'Do it on purpose, and you change',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THANK YOU FOR DINNER',text:'Your family says thank you to whoever cooked, every night. You used to mumble it. One week you say it properly, and look at the cook. By Friday you notice you really do feel thankful.'},{kind:'term',word:'Ritual',meaning:'A small action done the same way each time. A handshake. Saying thank you for a meal. Standing up when a guest comes in.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The things you do again and again shape who you become. Doing a kind act on purpose, even before you feel it, can teach you to feel it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why Confucius cared',picture:!0,text:'Confucius was a teacher in China about 2,500 years ago. He spent a lot of time on manners: how to greet people, how to share a meal. Not for show. He thought good manners trained the heart.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE HABIT GROWS',nodes:['You do the kind thing','You do it again and again','The kind feeling grows','The kind thing gets easier'],loop:!0},{kind:'myth',belief:'If I don\u2019t feel it, doing it is fake.',reality:'Doing it with no care at all is empty. But doing it with care, before the feeling comes, is how the feeling grows. It is practice, like learning a song.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one small thing you do each day, like saying hello to your teacher. This week, do it on purpose and look at them. See if it feels different.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Ben thinks saying thank you is pointless unless he feels grateful. What would Confucius say?',options:['Say it with care, and the feeling will grow','Only say it on days you really mean it','Saying it is enough. Care does not matter.'],answer:0,why:'Saying it with no care is empty. Saying it with care is how the thankful feeling gets trained.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Rituals and manners are empty forms. Sincerity is what counts.',reality:'Confucius agreed that form without feeling is worthless. But he did not say drop the form and wait for sincerity. He said the form, practised with attention, is how sincerity is trained.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The forms you go through repeatedly become the disposition you have. Ritual is not a costume over character. Practised attentively, it is how character is built.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Behaviour runs both ways',text:'Everyone accepts that feeling produces conduct: you care, so you call. The claim here is the reverse as well: conduct produces feeling. It sounds hollow until you have tried it.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'STANDING UP',text:'Standing when someone enters a room is not a belief about rank. It is a rehearsal of attention: for a moment your body stops what it was doing and turns to the person. Repeated, the turning becomes a habit of mind.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Nobody has sat down and nobody is watching, yet the last bowl still goes down square. Attentive form, done for its own sake, becomes who you are.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Hollow form',items:['Done on autopilot','Proves nothing','Changes nothing']},right:{title:'Attentive form',items:['Done on purpose','Still not proof of caring','Makes caring reliable']},note:'The difference at the start is attention, not sincerity.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The modern version',text:'The stand-up you attend properly. The message you answer the same day. These are not proof that you care. They are the mechanism by which caring becomes reliable rather than occasional.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one small ritual you perform without thinking, such as how you greet a colleague. Do it deliberately for a week, and notice whether the form changes the feeling.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A new manager feels awkward holding weekly one-to-ones and wants to wait until they feel natural. What would Confucius advise?',options:['Hold them attentively now. The ease comes from the practice.','Wait, since a forced meeting is worse than no meeting at all','Hold them, but treat them as a box to tick each week','Replace them with an open-door policy for the whole team'],answer:0,why:'Waiting for the feeling gets the order backwards. The attentive form is what trains it, and the box-ticking form trains nothing.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',word:'Li',meaning:'Usually translated ritual or propriety. It covers ceremonies such as mourning and sacrifice, and also everyday forms: how to greet, how to sit, how to eat in company.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'A WHOLE BOOK OF CONDUCT',text:'One book of the Analects records almost nothing he said. It describes how Confucius behaved: how he stood, ate, dressed and greeted people. That it was recorded beside his sayings shows how seriously the form was taken.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Ritual is formative, not decorative. The forms you repeat build the disposition you have, so attentive form is how sincerity is trained, not a mask over its absence.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Against two positions',text:'One position keeps the forms and lets them go hollow. Confucius asks bluntly what ritual can mean to someone without humaneness. The other drops the forms and waits for sincerity. His answer to both: keep the form, and practise it with attention.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The table laid before anyone sits, the last bowl set square. Neither hollow routine nor waiting for the feeling: the form, attended to, doing its slow work.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE TWO-WAY CLAIM',nodes:['Attentive form','Repeated over years','A settled disposition','Form that comes easily'],loop:!0,note:'Feeling produces conduct, and conduct produces feeling. The second direction is the one the Analects insist on.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The claim can be turned into a demand for conformity: if form makes character, enforce form. Confucian ritual also encoded rank, between ruler and subject, father and son, husband and wife, and was used for centuries to keep those ranks fixed. A form performed under compulsion may train resentment, not care.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which of your daily forms has gone hollow? What would doing it with attention look like tomorrow?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'Someone objects: a ritual performed before you feel it is just hypocrisy. What is the Confucian reply?',options:['Practised with attention, the form trains the feeling rather than faking it','Hypocrisy is fine, since only outward conduct affects other people','The objection is right, so the form should wait for sincerity','Ritual has nothing to do with feeling; it simply keeps order'],answer:0,why:'He agrees hollow ritual is worthless. His claim is that the attentive form comes before the feeling and helps produce it.'}]}},analects_5:{pictureCaption:'An old crack, a failed repair chipped out, and a hand pressing in fresh filling. The crack is not the fault. Leaving it would be.',bridge:'Idea 4 was about building good habits. This one is about what to do with a fault you already have.',basics:{title:'Mistakes are normal. Fixing them is the job.',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE SAME SPELLING',text:'Every week your teacher marks the same word wrong in your work. You know it is wrong. You keep meaning to learn it. Six weeks later, you still spell it the old way.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'What is the real mistake here?',options:['Spelling the word wrong the first time','Knowing it is wrong and not fixing it'],answer:1,reveal:'Getting it wrong once is normal. We all do. The real mistake is the one you keep making each week, after you know.'},{kind:'idea',text:'We all make mistakes. The real mistake is knowing about one and not fixing it. Confucius cared much less about the slip than about what you did next.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Who said this',text:'Confucius was a teacher in China about 2,500 years ago. He did not ask his students to feel bad about their faults. He asked one plain question: have you fixed it yet?'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A crack in a wall, and a hand pressing new filling into it. The crack is not the problem. Leaving it there would be.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of one thing you know you keep getting wrong. Today, do one small thing to fix it.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Ella forgot her friend\u2019s birthday. She feels awful for days but says nothing. What would Confucius say?',options:['Feeling bad is not enough. Say sorry and make it right.','Feeling bad for days shows she cares, and that is enough.','Forgetting was the big mistake, so now it is too late.'],answer:0,why:'The slip was forgetting. The real fault would be leaving it. Feeling bad does not fix anything.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE BUDGET SHEET',text:'There is a formula in the team\u2019s budget sheet that you have known was wrong since spring. You meant to fix it. Every month you work around it. It has now been wrong for longer as a choice than it ever was as a mistake.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Having a fault and not correcting it is the real fault. Error is forgivable; refusing to revise is not. The weight moves from the slip to what you do next.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'The mistake',items:['A fact about a moment','Usually forgivable','Already over']},right:{title:'Leaving it',items:['A choice, made daily','Hard to excuse','Still going on']},note:'This changes what you are accountable for.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Light on guilt, heavy on repair',text:'The Analects barely ask anyone to feel worse. Confucius asks whether the thing has been fixed, and treats the feeling as beside the point. Energy spent managing how the fault looks is energy not spent on the fault.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The old repair chipped out, new filling pressed into the crack. Less tidy than never cracking, and far better than living with it.'},{kind:'try',text:'Name one thing you have known was wrong for over a month. The knowing is not the work. Take the first step of fixing it today.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You discover you gave a client a wrong figure last week. Which response fits the idea?',options:['Correct it with the client now, before anything else','Hope nobody notices, since the meeting is over','Spend the afternoon on why it happened first','Apologise at length to your manager about it'],answer:0,why:'The original slip is done. What you are accountable for now is whether the wrong figure stays in the world.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'A SHORT, POINTED LINE',text:'The saying is brief: to have a fault and not amend it is what truly deserves the name. Elsewhere the book tells students not to be afraid of changing once they see a fault. Together they set the standard at revision, not at a clean record.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The real fault is not the mistake but the failure to amend it. A mistake is a fact about a moment. Leaving it uncorrected is a choice made again every day.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the reframing helps',text:'It moves accountability from the past, which cannot be altered, to the present, which can. It also weakens the motive to hide. If error is expected, admitting one costs less, and correction gets cheaper.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE STUDENT HE PRAISED',text:'Asked which student loved learning, Confucius named Yan Hui and gave two reasons. Yan Hui did not take out his anger on others, and he never made the same mistake twice. Not that he never erred: he never erred the same way again.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The failed repair has been chipped out, not painted over. Amending means going back into the crack, which is less comfortable than covering it.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Some faults cannot be amended, only acknowledged: the harm is done and the person is gone. The line can also be misread as permission to feel nothing about a wrong once it is patched. Where someone was hurt, amending includes making it right with them, not only fixing the process.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What fault have you known about for a year? What has stopped you amending it: cost, fear, or how it would look?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these misreads the idea?',options:['Treating a public apology as the fix and leaving the cause untouched','Correcting an error quickly even though admitting it is embarrassing','Forgiving a junior colleague\u2019s first slip but checking it was fixed','Spending less time on guilt and more on repairing the damage'],answer:0,why:'The apology manages how the fault looks. The idea is about amending the fault itself.'}]}},analects_6:{pictureCaption:'A jar of chillies wears a pear tag. Trusting it, a hand tips them onto sliced pears: the wrong name spoils the dish.',basics:{title:'Call things by their right names',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE PLAYGROUND GAME',prompt:'In a game, half the class thinks *out* means off the pitch. The other half thinks it means caught. What happens?',options:['The game goes fine','Each play ends in an argument'],answer:1,reveal:'Nobody is lying. Nobody is cheating. They are using one word for two things. So each call sounds wrong to half the players.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'A STRANGE FIRST STEP',text:'Confucius was a teacher in China about 2,500 years ago. A student asked him: if you were put in charge of a country, what would you do first? He said he would make sure things were called by their right names.'},{kind:'idea',text:'If words don\u2019t match the things they name, people get mixed up. Plans go wrong, and no one knows what to do. So fix the words first.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The tag says pear. The jar holds chillies. The hand goes by the tag, and the bowl of pears is spoiled. Nobody lied. The name was wrong.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW IT GOES WRONG',nodes:['A word means the wrong thing','What people say stops being true','Plans fail','No one knows what to do']},{kind:'example',kicker:'AT HOME',text:'Your mum says your room is *tidy* when the floor is clear. You think it is tidy when the bed is made. Every Saturday you fight about it. The fight is really about one word.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you argue with someone, ask: are we using one word for two things? Then say what you each mean by it.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your group says its project is *nearly done*. Really, half of it is not started. What would Confucius want?',options:['Say how much is really left, so the words fit','Keep saying nearly done so no one worries','Work fast and hope it gets nearly done'],answer:0,why:'If the words do not fit the facts, each plan built on them goes wrong. Fix the words, or fix the work.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'HIS FIRST ACT IN OFFICE',text:'His student Zilu asked what he would do first if a ruler put him in charge of government. Rectify the names, Confucius said. Zilu told him this was wide of the mark. Confucius called him uncultivated, and explained.'},{kind:'idea',text:'If names are not correct, language does not fit the facts, and nothing built on that language can succeed. Before anything else, make the word match the thing.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Every other tag on the shelf matches its jar, so this one is trusted too. The hand reaching for pears gets chillies, and the bowl it was adding to is ruined.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE CHAIN',nodes:['A name does not fit','True-sounding statements are false','Plans built on them fail','People cannot tell how to act'],note:'No one has to lie for this to happen. The fault is in the vocabulary.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'GREEN THAT IS AMBER',text:'A project status says green. Everyone close to it knows it is amber. Decisions above it are made on green. When it fails, the review blames communication. The real fault was a word that had stopped meaning what it said.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'The name',items:['Head of Quality','Status: green','We value openness']},right:{title:'The thing',items:['Spends the week on sales','Two weeks behind','Bad news is unwelcome']},note:'Each gap is a corrupted name, and each produces confusion no one can locate.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The repair',text:'It is unglamorous: make the word match the thing, or change the thing to match the word. Either works. Leaving the gap open does not, however good everyone\u2019s communication is.'},{kind:'try',text:'When a project is going wrong in several places at once, check whether two people are using one word for two different things.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Two teams keep missing handovers. Both say they define *done* clearly. What is the first thing to check?',options:['Whether each team means something different by done','Whether one of the teams is simply working too slowly','Whether the handover meetings should happen more often','Whether the managers of the two teams get along'],answer:0,why:'Repeated failures with no obvious culprit often trace back to one word carrying two meanings.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'THE STATE OF WEI',text:'Zilu asks what Confucius would do first if the ruler of Wei gave him charge of government. Commentators have long read the answer against a dispute there, in which a son held the throne his exiled father claimed. Which names fitted whom was a live question.'},{kind:'term',word:'Zhengming',meaning:'Rectifying the names: making titles and words match the realities they name. In Confucian thought it covers roles above all, so that a ruler acts as a ruler and a father as a father.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Government starts with correct names. When a word does not match its reality, statements become false without anyone lying, affairs fail, and people no longer know what to do.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HIS CHAIN, LINK BY LINK',nodes:['Names are incorrect','Speech does not fit facts','Affairs fail','Rites and punishments go astray','People cannot act'],note:'Each link is short, which is what makes the chain persuasive. It runs from vocabulary to paralysis.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it is administrative',text:'Nothing here is mystical. A system runs on shared meanings. When a title, a status or a stated value stops matching what it names, every decision built on it inherits the error, and the confusion gets blamed on communication rather than vocabulary.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Nobody here is lying. The shelf runs on its tags, one tag has drifted from what its jar holds, and the error passes straight into the dish built on it.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The doctrine cuts two ways. It can demand that a ruler actually rule well, or insist that everyone keep to an assigned station, and it has often been used the second way. And some words are vague by necessity: forcing a fixed definition can hide a real disagreement rather than settle it.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which word where you work has drifted furthest from what it names? Would it be cheaper to fix the word, or the thing?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A company keeps the title senior engineer for staff who now mostly manage. Which consequence does the doctrine predict?',options:['Plans and hiring built on the title go wrong without anyone lying','Nothing, provided everyone privately knows what the job involves','Only the job holders suffer, since titles are a personal matter','Morale improves, since prestigious titles keep people motivated'],answer:0,why:'A name that no longer fits its reality corrupts every statement and decision that relies on it, even when everyone is honest.'}]}},analects_7:{pictureCaption:'A board of tools, each with one job and its own hook. The shadow of a hand falls among them: the one thing here that chooses.',bridge:'Idea 1 showed a teacher fitting advice to each student. This asks what kind of person all that teaching was meant to make.',basics:{title:'You are more than one skill',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'A SPOON',text:'A spoon is great at one job. But it can\u2019t decide what soup to make. It can\u2019t tell if the soup is any good. Someone else has to pick it up and use it.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'FOUR CHARACTERS',text:'Confucius was a teacher in China about 2,500 years ago. Chinese is written with signs called characters. One of his sayings is just four characters long. It says a good person is not a tool.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A tool does one job, and someone else decides how to use it. A person should be able to think for themselves and handle things they were not trained for.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A wall of tools, each made for one job. The shadow of a hand falls among them. The hand is what picks the right tool.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'So it is bad to be really good at one thing.',reality:'No. Confucius wanted his students to be very good at things. He just did not want them to be only that one thing.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of something you are great at. Now name one thing you could still do well if that skill was not needed.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Leo is the best goalie in school, but he only ever thinks about being in goal. What would Confucius tell him?',options:['Keep being a great goalie, and grow in other ways too','Stop playing in goal so he is not just one thing','Spend all his time on goalkeeping to be the best'],answer:0,why:'Being great at one thing is fine. Being only that thing is what Confucius warned against.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE BEST ANALYST',text:'She is the best data analyst in the building, and every review says so. Then the company changes direction, the reports she built stop being wanted, and she finds she has no view on what should replace them. She was excellent, and wholly dependent on someone wanting that one thing.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A utensil has one function and is valued for it. An educated person should be able to meet a situation they were not trained for, and to judge what their skill should be used on.'},{kind:'explain',title:'It is a claim about judgement',text:'A tool is applied by somebody who decides where it goes. It cannot ask whether the job is the right job. Confucius wanted an education that produced the person who decides, not the instrument that executes.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'This is an argument against expertise.',reality:'He demands real skill. The argument is against being only expertise. A person defined by one skill has no standpoint from which to judge how it is used.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Each tool hangs in its place with one job. The hand\u2019s shadow falls among them, the one thing on the board that decides what any tool is for.'},{kind:'try',text:'Ask what you would be worth in a room where your own speciality was not needed. Then pick one thing to build outside it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A brilliant engineer is asked to build a feature she believes will harm users. What does the idea say she should bring?',options:['Her judgement on whether it should be built, not only her skill','Her skill alone, since the decision belongs to someone else','A refusal to specialise further, so she is never used this way','A request to move to a team where her skills are valued more'],answer:0,why:'A utensil executes without judging the job. The point of not being one is having a standpoint from which to ask whether the job is right.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'A STUDENT ASKS WHAT HE IS',text:'His student Zigong once asked what Confucius made of him. A vessel, Confucius said. Which vessel? A precious one, used for grain offerings in the ancestral temple. Praise and a limit in a single answer.'},{kind:'term',word:'Junzi',meaning:'Often translated gentleman. Originally a ruler\u2019s son, a man of noble birth. Confucius made it a matter of character: the cultivated person, whatever their birth.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The junzi is not a utensil. A tool has one function and cannot judge its own use. Cultivation is what lets a person decide where any skill should go, their own included.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Four characters, no gloss',text:'The saying is four characters long and comes with no explanation. Its force is in the contrast. A vessel is defined by what it holds or does. A cultivated person is defined by judgement, which no single function contains.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Tools in their places, each defined by its job. The hand is not hung on the board. It is the judgement that takes the tools down.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The career reading',text:'Deep specialism is valuable, and it makes you a utensil in exactly his sense: dependent on somebody continuing to want that function. The protection is not giving up the depth. It is having something outside it.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The ideal was shaped for a small educated class whose work was governing, and it could look down on craft and trade. Confucius said he had learned many humble skills only because he was poor when young. Societies need people superb at one thing, and the saying can be used to sneer at them.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HARD CASE',prompt:'A world-class surgeon reads nothing outside medicine. On this idea, what is missing?',options:['A standpoint from which to judge how her skill is used','More surgical skill, since depth is what defines a person','Nothing, since excellence in one craft is the whole aim','A second specialism to fall back on if the first fails'],answer:0,why:'He is not asking for less skill or a backup skill. He is asking for the judgement that sits above any single function.'}]}},analects_8:{pictureCaption:'Two piles on a desk, and one sheet moved onto its own mat. Sorting what you know from what you are assuming is the work.',bridge:'Idea 3 warned against thinking without learning. This is the check against it: knowing exactly where your knowledge stops.',basics:{title:'Know what you don\u2019t know',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE QUIZ',text:'In a team quiz, you write an answer you are not sure of, as if you were sure. Your team trusts you. You lose the point. If you had said you weren\u2019t sure, someone else might have known.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Real knowing has two parts. Know what you know. And know what you don\u2019t know. Mixing the two up is where big mistakes come from.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'A LESSON FOR A BOLD STUDENT',text:'Confucius was a teacher in China about 2,500 years ago. He had a student called Zilu, who was brave and quick to act. Confucius told him that real knowing means being honest about both parts.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two piles of paper. The hand is moving one sheet to a place of its own: the pile of things I am not sure about.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A MAP',text:'A good map shows where the land ends and the sea begins. A map with no edge would walk you into the water. What you know needs an edge too.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before a test or a big choice, make two lists: things I am sure of, and things I am guessing. Then check the guesses.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Zara is asked the way to the station. She thinks it is left, but she is not sure. What should she say?',options:['I think it\u2019s left, but I\u2019m not sure','It\u2019s left, said firmly so she sounds helpful','I don\u2019t know, though she has a good idea'],answer:0,why:'She marks her guess as a guess. That way the person can check it, and she has still helped.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TWO ADVISERS',prompt:'Which adviser is more dangerous?',options:['One who knows little and says so','One who knows a lot but cannot tell where it stops'],answer:1,reveal:'Ignorance you have marked is safe: you ask, check and hedge. Ignorance you have not marked gets acted on at full confidence. That is where the expensive mistakes live.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Knowledge includes an accurate map of its own edges: holding what you know as known, and what you do not know as unknown. The danger is not ignorance but unmarked ignorance.'},{kind:'explain',title:'An accounting rule',text:'It looks like modesty. It is closer to bookkeeping. If you cannot keep the two piles apart, you will draw on the second without noticing, and spend confidence you do not have.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One sheet moved off the big pile onto a mat of its own. Not known, only assumed, and now marked as such.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it is hard',text:'The boundary is invisible from inside. Not knowing something usually includes not knowing that you do not know it. So this is a discipline, practised again and again, not a state you reach once.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Before a decision',steps:['Draw two columns: sure, and assuming.','Put each claim the decision rests on in one of them.','For each assumption, ask what happens if it is wrong.','Check the ones that would hurt most.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Planning a launch, you realise the forecast rests on a customer survey you never read. What does the idea ask?',options:['Move the forecast to the assumed column and read the survey','Trust the forecast, since a survey was done at some point','Scrap the forecast entirely, since it is not certain','Present it confidently so the team stays motivated'],answer:0,why:'The fault is not lacking certainty. It is acting on an assumption as if it were known.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Admitting what you do not know is a matter of humility.',reality:'Confucius makes it a matter of definition. Knowledge itself includes knowing the edge of what you know. A person with many facts and no sense of that edge is not, on this account, knowledgeable.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'SAID TO ZILU',text:'The definition is addressed to Zilu, the student the Analects portray as brave, blunt and quick to act. Confucius offers to teach him what knowing is, and gives it in two halves: hold the known as known, and admit the unknown as unknown.'},{kind:'idea',text:'To know is to hold what you know as known and to allow what you do not know as unknown. Knowledge includes an accurate map of its own edges.'},{kind:'explain',title:'A definition with two layers',text:'It folds a second claim into the first. You must know things, and also know which things you know. The second layer is what lets you act safely on the first, because it tells you where to ask, check or hedge.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Most sheets stay on the large pile. One is moved, deliberately, to its own place. The definition lives in that small movement.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Marked ignorance',items:['You ask','You check','You hedge']},right:{title:'Unmarked ignorance',items:['You assert','You act','You are surprised']},note:'The failure is not the size of the second pile. It is not knowing which pile a belief is in.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The boundary is invisible from inside, so the instruction can never be finished. It can also be overplayed: someone who labels everything unknown never commits, and false modesty can be a way of dodging responsibility. The aim is an accurate edge, not a retreating one.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these misuses the idea?',options:['Calling every claim uncertain so you never have to commit to a view','Telling a client which parts of your estimate rest on assumptions','Asking a colleague to check a figure you are not sure of','Writing down what a plan assumes before you approve it'],answer:0,why:'The definition asks for an accurate edge. Marking everything unknown is as inaccurate as marking everything known.'}]}},analects_9:{pictureCaption:'The lowest steps are worn deep by use. The rail runs beside them and stops before the top, where the climber no longer needs it.',bridge:'The earlier ideas were practices. Here Confucius says what a lifetime of them added up to.',basics:{title:'A whole life in one sentence',beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'HIS LIFE IN STAGES',text:'Confucius was a teacher in China about 2,500 years ago. When he was old, he told his whole life as one short list of stages. After thirty, each stage took ten years.'},{kind:'timeline',points:[{when:'15',what:'He set his heart on learning.'},{when:'30',what:'He could stand on his own feet.'},{when:'40',what:'He was no longer muddled by doubts.'},{when:'50 and 60',what:'He knew what heaven asked of him. Then he could listen calmly.'},{when:'70',what:'He could do what he wanted, and it was right.'}],note:'The last stage is the surprise.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Being good takes a lifetime. At first you have to push yourself to do the right thing. Slowly, you start to want the right thing, and the pushing stops.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE RIDING A BIKE',text:'At first you hold on tight and think about each push. Then one day you just ride. You did not give up. The riding became part of you.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The rail helps on the low steps. Near the top it stops. By then the climber does not need to hold on.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of one good habit, like brushing your teeth. Do you still have to push yourself, or do you just do it? That shows how far along you are.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Two kids both tidy their rooms. One has to force herself each time. The other just does it. Who is further along, by this idea?',options:['The one who just does it, with no fight','The one who forces herself, since that is harder','Neither, since they both tidy the room'],answer:0,why:'For Confucius, the goal was not strong self-control. It was wanting the right thing, so the fight goes away.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'The goal of a good life is iron self-control.',reality:'In Confucius\u2019s account, self-control is a middle stage. The destination is no longer needing it: wanting what is right, so that the discipline becomes unnecessary.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Confucius told his life as stages ending, at seventy, in following his heart\u2019s desire without overstepping what was right. The aim is to want the right thing, and it arrives very slowly.'},{kind:'timeline',kicker:'FIFTY-FIVE YEARS',points:[{when:'15',what:'Set on learning'},{when:'30',what:'Standing firm'},{when:'40',what:'Free from doubts'},{when:'50 and 60',what:'Knowing heaven\u2019s decree; an ear that took in truth readily'},{when:'70',what:'Following his heart without overstepping'}],note:'Fifteen years to the first, then a decade each. None is skipped.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the timescale matters',text:'The honest part is the length: fifty-five years, stages in an order that cannot be skipped, and no claim that he reached seventy by deciding to. Set it beside any programme promising transformation in a quarter.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The rail runs beside the worn lower steps and stops short of the top. Near the end of the climb, nothing needs holding.'},{kind:'explain',title:'A better measure',text:'If the goal is wanting the right thing, managing effort well is not the score. The score is how much of the right behaviour still needs effort at all. Less effort means further along.'},{kind:'try',text:'List three things you do because you should. For each, note whether it still takes effort. That is your progress, not how well you manage the effort.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A manager is proud that she now keeps her temper in tense meetings by sheer force of will. By this idea, what would show further progress?',options:['No longer feeling the urge to snap in the first place','Holding her temper by even stronger force of will','Avoiding tense meetings so the effort is not needed','Letting her temper out, since forced calm is fake'],answer:0,why:'More discipline is the middle stage. The final stage is not needing it, because the wanting itself has changed.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'timeline',kicker:'FIFTEEN TO SEVENTY',points:[{when:'15',what:'Bent on learning'},{when:'30',what:'Established, standing firm'},{when:'40',what:'Free from doubts'},{when:'50',what:'Knowing the decrees of heaven'},{when:'70',what:'Desire and what is right coincide'}],note:'At sixty, between these, his ear received truth readily. The list is told from the far end of a life, so it reads as a report, not a plan.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The end point of cultivation is not perfect self-control but no longer needing it. By seventy, what he desired and what was right had become the same, after fifty-five years in order.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the last stage is the point',text:'Every earlier stage involves effort against something: ignorance, doubt, resistance to hearing. The last describes the absence of that friction. Discipline was the means. The aim was a character in which it had nothing left to do.'},{kind:'keyline',text:'Self-control is the scaffolding. The building is wanting the right thing.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The lower steps are hollowed by countless climbs; the rail stops before the smoother upper ones. Support is for the stages that need it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The middle stages are places to live',text:'The list treats each decade as somewhere you live, not a corridor to hurry along. That corrects promises of quick transformation, and it links back to ritual: long repetition of attentive form is what does the slow work.'},{kind:'limits',text:'It is one man\u2019s retrospect, and the stages may be partly idealised. It is not a schedule, and no reason for complacency at forty. Nor does the last stage license trusting every impulse: the claim is that his desires had been trained over decades, not that desire is a reliable guide.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which use of the passage gets it wrong?',options:['Doing whatever feels right, on the grounds that the heart is a sound guide','Judging progress by how much right behaviour still needs effort','Expecting deep change of character to take years, not weeks','Treating a long middle stage as a place to live, not a failure'],answer:0,why:'At seventy his heart could be followed because fifty-five years had trained it. Untrained desire is exactly what the earlier stages work on.'}]}}}},2955,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'art-of-being-right_1':{pictureCaption:'A fencer turns from the bout and raises a glove to the crowd. Winning their cheer and scoring the point are different skills.',basics:{title:'Winning is not the same as being right',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'IN CLASS',prompt:'Sam says loudly that a whale is a fish, and the class laughs at Priya. Priya says quietly that it is a mammal. Who looks like the winner?',options:['Sam','Priya','Nobody'],answer:0,reveal:'Sam does. But Priya is right. The one who looked like the winner was not the one who was right.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Being right means what you say is true. Winning means people think you are right. These are two different skills. Some people are good at winning even when they are wrong.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Being right',items:['What you say is true','You check the facts','Works even if you are quiet']},right:{title:'Winning',items:['People think you are right','You sound sure and quick','Can work even if you are wrong']},note:'We hope these go together. Often they don\u2019t.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO NOTICED THIS',text:'Arthur Schopenhauer was a German thinker who lived about 200 years ago. He wrote down 38 tricks people use to win arguments they are losing. He was not cheering the tricks on. He was writing down what people do.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'PLAYING TO THE CROWD',caption:'The fencer turns to the crowd and lifts a hand, as if he has won. But a cheer is not a point. The two can come apart.'},{kind:'explain',title:'So why learn the tricks?',text:'A trick works best when nobody sees it. Once you can spot it and name it, it loses most of its power. These lessons are about spotting tricks, not using them.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'In a class debate, Leo talks fast and sounds very sure. Mia speaks slowly and has the real facts. Most people clap for Leo. What does this show?',options:['Sounding sure can win, even without the facts','Leo must be right, because most people agreed','Mia lost, so her facts must be wrong'],answer:0,why:'The clapping shows who won the room, not who had the facts.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE MEETING RAN LONG',text:'Your colleague has the figures wrong, and you know it. But he answers every point quickly and with a smile, the room laughs at his line about your spreadsheet, and the decision goes his way. Nobody checked the figures.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Winning an argument and being right are separate skills, and they come apart constantly. Schopenhauer called the first dialectic, the art of appearing to be right, and catalogued its moves because people use them whether or not they ever studied them.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The fencer has turned from the bout to raise a glove to the crowd. Whether they cheer and whether he scored are separate questions, settled by separate skills.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the two come apart',text:'His explanation is about people, not logic. We defend a position because we said it, and our pride outlasts the evidence. So a dispute gets decided by technique, by the audience and by stamina. The truth wins only when it happens to line up with those.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW TECHNIQUE TAKES OVER',nodes:['You state a position in public','The evidence turns against it','Pride says: don\u2019t back down','Technique replaces truth'],note:'Nobody needs to have read a book of tricks to use them. Pride finds them on its own.'},{kind:'explain',title:'So why learn the moves?',text:'Each move works by not being seen as a move. A stretched claim works only if nobody notices the stretch. Once you can name what just happened, most of its force is gone. That is the honest use of the book, and the one Schopenhauer himself gives.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of an argument you lost but still believe you were right about. Write down what decided it. If it was not the facts, give the something else a name.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A colleague wins a budget argument by sounding certain and making the room laugh, though his numbers were wrong. What would Schopenhauer say happened?',options:['He was good at dialectic, which is a different skill from being right','His numbers must have been close enough to the truth to pass','The room weighed the evidence and found his case stronger','Laughter is a fair sign that an argument has really landed'],answer:0,why:'The room\u2019s verdict measured technique and mood. Whether the numbers were right is a separate question it never asked.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'LEFT IN HIS PAPERS',text:'Arthur Schopenhauer, the German philosopher, drafted this short treatise but never published it. It appeared after his death. It sets out thirty-eight stratagems for coming off best in a dispute, whether or not you are in the right.'},{kind:'term',word:'Dialectic, in his sense',meaning:'The art of disputing so as to come off best. Schopenhauer keeps it apart from logic, which asks only what is true and what follows. His fuller name was eristic dialectic, from the Greek word for strife.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Dialectic, the art of appearing to be right, is a different skill from logic, the art of being right, and the two part company constantly. The catalogue exists because the first is in use whether anyone studied it or not.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The argument from vanity',text:'His diagnosis is about human nature rather than reasoning. People defend a position because they asserted it. Vanity makes being wrong in public hard to bear, so the defence goes on after the evidence has turned. A dispute is then decided by technique, audience and stamina, and truth prevails only when it happens to coincide with them.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Logic asks',items:['Is the claim true?','Does the conclusion follow?','Indifferent to who is speaking']},right:{title:'Dialectic asks',items:['Who comes off best?','Will the room accept it?','Turns on nerve and timing']},note:'His point is that these are different questions, not that the second should win.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Turned away from the bout, glove raised, the fencer is working the room. That is dialectic. Whether a touch actually landed is the question logic asks.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Description, not recommendation',text:'He is explicit that he is recording what people do. The value of the record is defensive. Each stratagem works by passing unnoticed as a move, so seeing it is most of the defence.'},{kind:'limits',text:'A catalogue of tricks can be read as a manual, and that reading is the obvious misuse. A subtler one is calling every strong opposing point a trick, so as never to engage with it. Naming a move is a defence only when the move was really made. And a fluent, confident opponent is sometimes also right.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of an argument you lost and still think you were right about. What decided it, if not the merits?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these is a misuse of Schopenhauer\u2019s distinction?',options:['Dismissing every persuasive opponent as merely good at dialectic','Noticing that a debate was won on stamina, not on evidence','Checking a confident speaker\u2019s figures after the meeting ends','Admitting that a fluent opponent may also be in the right'],answer:0,why:'The distinction lets you ask whether a win tracked the truth. Used to wave away every loss, it becomes one more way of never being wrong.'}]}},'art-of-being-right_2':{pictureCaption:'The small square in the middle is what was actually said. Everything stretched out around it is the version being attacked.',bridge:'Idea 1 said each trick works by going unnoticed. This is the commonest one, and the easiest to miss.',basics:{title:'When someone makes your words bigger',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'AT DINNER',text:'You say, \u201cI don\u2019t like broccoli much.\u201d Your brother says, \u201cSo you hate all vegetables? That\u2019s silly. Carrots are great!\u201d Everyone nods. Now you are stuck defending something you never said.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A stretched claim is a trick. Someone makes what you said bigger than it was. Then they knock down the bigger version. The small thing you really said never gets an answer.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The small square in the middle is what you really said. The big, stretched net around it is the version someone chose to attack.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it works',text:'People don\u2019t go back and check your first words. They just hear the new, bigger version. And if you start to argue for the big version, you look silly. So the trick wins, unless you catch it early.'},{kind:'steps',title:'When your words get stretched',steps:['Listen for words like *all*, *never* or *always* that you did not say.','Stop. Don\u2019t argue for the big version.','Say what you really said, in the same words.','Then talk only about that.']},{kind:'try',text:'Next time someone starts with \u201cSo you think\u2026\u201d, check. Is that what you said, or something bigger?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You say, \u201cSome homework is too long.\u201d A friend says, \u201cSo you think homework is pointless?\u201d What is going on?',options:['Your words were stretched into something bigger','Your friend said your words back exactly','You have changed your mind about homework'],answer:0,why:'You said *some* homework is too long. *Pointless* is a much bigger claim, and it is not yours.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE REPLY OPENS',prompt:'You say some regulation is counterproductive. The reply: \u201cSo you think all regulation should go.\u201d What is the best first move?',options:['Explain why some rules are still useful','Restate exactly what you said, and no more','Point out that they are being rude'],answer:1,reveal:'The first option feels natural, but it accepts their version and starts defending it. You have already given up the ground. The cure is to put your claim back to its real size first.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The stretched claim restates your position more broadly than you made it, then refutes the broader version. It is the first stratagem in Schopenhauer\u2019s list, and probably the commonest move in any argument.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it works',text:'Audiences rarely recheck the original wording. Once the wide version is on the table, you are left defending a statement you never made, and any attempt to narrow it again looks like retreat.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'What you said',items:['Some regulation backfires','This film is too long','I\u2019m wary of this hire']},right:{title:'What gets attacked',items:['All regulation should go','You hate long films','You never trust anyone new']},note:'Each claim on the right is easier to knock down. That is the whole reason for moving to it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The square in the middle is the claim as made. The gaping net round it has been stretched until it will tear easily.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'SCHOPENHAUER\u2019S COUNTER',title:'Timing matters',text:'His defence is exact: restate your proposition within its original limits, and refuse to argue anything wider. Do it on the first exchange. Three exchanges in, the stretched version has become what the room believes you said.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'In a meeting you say a new tool has slowed one team down. Someone replies, \u201cSo you think we should never adopt new tools?\u201d What is the best response?',options:['\u201cI said it slowed one team down. Let\u2019s look at that.\u201d','\u201cNew tools often fail, so caution is wise in general.\u201d','\u201cI didn\u2019t mean to criticise anyone\u2019s choice of tools.\u201d','\u201cFine, let\u2019s talk about how we adopt new tools.\u201d'],answer:0,why:'Restating the original, narrow claim stops the stretch before it sets. The others either defend the wide version or back away from your own.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'STRATAGEM ONE',text:'Schopenhauer puts this trick first. He calls it extension: carrying an opponent\u2019s assertion beyond its natural limits, reading it as generally as possible, and refuting it in that exaggerated form. In our description, a claim about some cases is answered as if it were a claim about all.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A claim pushed beyond the limits its speaker gave it becomes easy to refute. The refutation then passes for an answer to the original, which was never touched.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE STRETCH SETS',nodes:['A limited claim is made','It is restated more widely','The wide version is refuted','The room remembers the wide version'],note:'Each step is small. By the last, narrowing your claim looks like backing down.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why denial is costly',text:'Once the wide version is in play, the speaker must either defend it, which means defending something they never claimed, or disown it, which sounds like retreat. Either way the move has worked. The audience is following the exchanges, not rereading the first sentence.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The terracotta square at the centre is the claim as stated. The frame has been worked outwards until the weave gapes and is ready to tear.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The counter he gives',text:'Restate the proposition precisely, within its original limits, and decline to discuss anything wider. It is unglamorous, and it is the only counter that does not accept the stretched frame. It must come early, before the wide version hardens into the record.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Not every broadening is a trick. Sometimes a claim really does commit you to more than you said: if your reason for one case applies equally to all, pointing that out is fair argument, not extension. The counter can be abused too. A speaker who retreats to ever narrower wording after each objection is running the reverse move.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'FAIR OR STRETCHED?',prompt:'Someone argues that phones should be banned in class because they distract. Which reply is fair argument rather than extension?',options:['\u201cYour reason would ban laptops too. Do you accept that?\u201d','\u201cSo you think technology has no place in schools at all.\u201d','\u201cYou clearly want to go back to slates and chalk.\u201d','\u201cThen you must be against every kind of new idea.\u201d'],answer:0,why:'The laptop question follows the speaker\u2019s own reason to its consequences, which they must answer. The others swap their claim for a wider one they never made.'}]}},'art-of-being-right_3':{pictureCaption:'A hand turns the lamp towards the jacket on the empty chair. The light moves from the page to the person.',bridge:'The stretched claim bends what you said. This trick stops talking about what you said at all, and talks about you.',basics:{title:'When they talk about you, not the point',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE SWITCH',belief:'If someone starts talking about you, you must have said something wrong.',reality:'Often it is the other way round. People switch to you when they are losing on the real question. They have nothing left to say about it.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'WHOSE TURN IS IT?',picture:!0,text:'You and Jake argue about whose turn it is. You show him the rule card. Jake looks at it, then says, \u201cYou always cheat anyway.\u201d The rule card is still on the table. Nobody is looking at it now.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When someone can\u2019t win on the question, they may attack the person instead. They talk about you, not the point. That switch is a clue that your point was strong.'},{kind:'flow',nodes:['You argue about the point','They run out of answers','They talk about you instead','The point gets forgotten'],note:'Notice *when* the switch came. It came right after they ran out of answers.'},{kind:'explain',title:'But what if it\u2019s true?',text:'Maybe you did cheat once, last year. That still doesn\u2019t tell you whose turn it is now. A thing can be true about you and have nothing to do with the question.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What to say',text:'Stay calm. Say, \u201cMaybe, but that\u2019s not what we were talking about.\u201d Then point back at the rule card. You don\u2019t need to say mean things back.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You show your friend a map that proves her shortcut is longer. She says, \u201cYou just like being a know-it-all.\u201d What does that tell you?',options:['She has switched from the map to you','The map must be wrong after all','You need to prove you are not a know-it-all'],answer:0,why:'Whether you are a know-it-all has nothing to do with the map. The switch suggests she had no answer to it.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'TWENTY MINUTES IN',text:'You have been arguing that the project is over budget, with the numbers in front of everyone. The reply is not about the numbers. It is that you have always disliked this project, and that your own report was late last quarter. Both may be true. The numbers have not changed.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When an argument goes badly, the losing side often shifts from the question to the arguer: your motives, your character, your tone. Schopenhauer treats this as the last resort, which makes it a diagnostic. If the subject has changed to you, you were probably winning.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'WHERE THE LIGHT GOES',caption:'The page with the argument stays on the table while a hand swings the lamp towards the jacket on the chair. The question has not moved; the attention has.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it works',text:'An audience will accept the swap if it is made smoothly. And the personal charge is often true, which makes it hard to wave away. But true and relevant are different things. A late report says nothing about whether this budget is overspent.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'About the question',items:['Are the numbers right?','Does the plan work?','Is the claim true?']},right:{title:'About the arguer',items:['Why do you care so much?','What\u2019s in it for you?','Why so aggressive?']},note:'Questions on the right can be fair in their place. Asked instead of the left, they are an exit.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE TELL',title:'Read the timing',text:'Don\u2019t start by judging whether the charge is fair. Ask when it arrived. A switch to you that comes just after a strong point went unanswered is the stratagem. Noticing that is more useful than defending yourself.'},{kind:'try',text:'When the subject moves to you, say so plainly: that may be true, but it doesn\u2019t answer the point about the numbers. Then restate the point.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You show, with invoices, that a supplier is overcharging. The reply is that you have a grudge against them. What is the most useful response?',options:['Name the switch, and bring it back to the invoices','Prove at length that you hold no grudge at all','Point out the other person\u2019s own past grudges','Drop it, since a grudge would undermine your case'],answer:0,why:'The invoices are the question. Defending your motives or attacking theirs both accept the new subject.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'WHEN DOES IT COME?',prompt:'In a long dispute, when does a switch to attacking the person usually arrive?',options:['At the start, to set the tone','When one side is losing on the substance','At random, whenever tempers fray'],answer:1,reveal:'Schopenhauer puts it last in his list: the move of someone who sees they will come off worse on the matter itself. The timing is the evidence.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A speaker who is losing on the substance can abandon the question and turn on the person, and audiences often accept the swap. Seen from the other side, that swerve marks the moment the argument was being won.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The last resort',text:'Schopenhauer ends his list here. When one side sees the other is stronger on the matter, it leaves the subject and makes the person the target. He describes it as turning personal, even rude. Its smoother form is not an insult but a question about motive, interest or conduct, put as though it were relevant.'},{kind:'term',word:'Ad hominem',meaning:'Latin for \u2018to the person\u2019. Today, answering a claim by attacking whoever made it instead of their reasons. Schopenhauer called this last trick *ad personam*, keeping *ad hominem* for turning an opponent\u2019s own words against them.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The argument stays on the table. The hand moves only the lamp, towards the jacket on the empty chair. Nothing on the page has changed except where the light points.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the charge sticks',text:'It works because the personal charge is often true. The speaker may well have an interest, or a poor record. Its truth makes it hard to deny and easy to mistake for an answer. But a true charge about the arguer leaves the claim exactly where it was.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Not every remark about a person is this move. A witness\u2019s interest in the outcome bears on how far to trust their testimony, and an expert\u2019s record bears on their judgement. The fault is using the person in place of the argument where the argument stands on its own. And timing is only a tell: people also get personal because they are tired or hurt.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'When did someone last answer your point by talking about you? What had you just said?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'RELEVANT OR A SWERVE?',prompt:'In which case is a point about the speaker fair, not a swerve?',options:['A paid spokesman says, with no evidence, that his firm\u2019s product is safe','A critic shows data that a policy failed, and is told they never liked it','A colleague\u2019s costed proposal is met with a reminder that they were late','A student presents a proof, and is told they just want to look clever'],answer:0,why:'When a claim must be taken on trust, the speaker\u2019s interest is evidence. Where the reasons are on the table, motives change nothing.'}]}},'art-of-being-right_4':{pictureCaption:'Dozens of envelopes forced through the door at once. The hand ignores the flood and picks up one, the terracotta one, to deal with first.',bridge:'Idea 3 changed the subject to you. This trick keeps to the subject, but buries you in questions about it.',basics:{title:'Too many questions at once',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'FIVE BALLS AT ONCE',text:'Someone throws five balls at you at the same time. You drop them all. Then they say, \u201cSee? You can\u2019t catch.\u201d But nobody could catch five at once. The test was unfair, not you.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Some people ask lots of questions at once, or ones that need a long answer. Then any short reply looks weak. It does not mean you are wrong. There was just no time to show you are right.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'PETS',text:'You say dogs make better pets than cats. Your friend fires back: Why? Who says? What about walks? Have you even had a cat? You start on the first one. They laugh and say you can\u2019t answer.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A pile of letters pushed through the door all at once. The hand does not panic. It picks up one and starts there.'},{kind:'steps',title:'What to do',steps:['Don\u2019t try to answer them all at once.','Pick one question. Answer it properly.','Say you will come back to the rest.','Then come back to them.']},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it feels like losing',text:'A question takes one breath to ask. A good answer can take a minute. People watching like quick and short. So the one asking looks smart, even when the answers are on your side.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'In class, someone asks you four quick questions about your project at once. What does this lesson say to do?',options:['Answer one properly, then say you will get to the rest','Answer all four in one quick sentence','Admit that your project must be wrong'],answer:0,why:'One good answer shows you know your stuff. Rushing all four makes every answer look thin.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'If you can\u2019t answer a question on the spot, you probably don\u2019t have an answer.',reality:'Most true claims cannot be fully justified in thirty seconds. A demand for an instant definition, source or mechanism tests recall and composure, not whether you are right.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Asking many questions at once, or questions that need long answers, makes any single reply look evasive. The tactic rests on an asymmetry: asserting takes a sentence, answering properly takes a paragraph, and audiences reward brevity.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'AT THE TOWN MEETING',text:'You argue that the town should build a cycle lane. The reply comes fast: what is your definition of safe? Where is your data? What about deliveries? Who pays? Each is a fair question. Together, in fifteen seconds, they cannot be met, and your pause is read as doubt.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'The questioner',items:['One sentence per question','Can ask five in a breath','Looks sharp']},right:{title:'The answerer',items:['A paragraph per answer','Can answer one at a time','Looks evasive']},note:'The gap is in the format, not in the facts.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The letters come through all at once. The hand takes one, and starts there. That is the whole defence.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The honest response',text:'Name what has happened. Say the questions are fair, that answering properly takes longer than this allows, and then answer one of them fully. It is less satisfying than a quick comeback. It is also honest, and it denies the tactic the pause it needs.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you face a barrage, say: good questions; let me answer the one about cost properly, and come back to the others. Then come back to them.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'In a review, a manager fires five pointed questions at your proposal in a row. Which response uses the idea?',options:['Answer the most important one fully, and offer to take the rest next','Give a one-line answer to each, so that none goes unanswered','Say the questions are unfair, and decline to answer any of them','Promise a written reply later, and say nothing more for now'],answer:0,why:'One full answer shows the claim can be defended. Five thin ones confirm the impression the barrage was built to create.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'DEFINE IT. NAME IT. NOW.',text:'A speaker says a new treatment is safe. The reply: define safe, precisely. Name the trials. Explain the mechanism. Each demand is legitimate. Made together, in front of an audience, they turn a debate about the treatment into a test of the speaker\u2019s memory.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A demand that cannot be met in the time available is presented as one that cannot be met at all. Inability to answer on demand passes for inability to answer.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What is really being tested',text:'Most true claims cannot be fully justified in thirty seconds. So the demand tests recall and composure, not correctness. Several of Schopenhauer\u2019s stratagems are built from questions, and this family works best before an audience, where hesitation reads as doubt and a crisp question reads as a hit.'},{kind:'term',word:'Burden of proof',meaning:'The duty to support a claim. Fairly placed, it falls on whoever asserts. A barrage quietly moves it, so that one side may ask freely while the other must answer everything at once.'},{kind:'flow',nodes:['Several demands at once','Each needs a long answer','A pause, or a thin reply','The room reads it as doubt'],note:'No step in this chain needs the claim to be false.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Everything arrives through the slot at once. The defence is in the hand: take one envelope and deal with it fully.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Some questions deserve an instant answer. If a speaker cannot name a single source for a claim made with confidence, the pause is evidence. The defence can also become a dodge: saying a question needs more time is honest once, and evasive if you never come back to it. The test is whether you do.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'DODGE OR DEFENCE?',prompt:'Which response to a barrage of questions is a dodge rather than the honest defence?',options:['Calling every question too complex, and never coming back to any','Answering the cost question fully, and promising to take the rest next','Saying the questions are fair but need more time than this allows','Asking which question matters most, then answering that one properly'],answer:0,why:'The defence admits the questions are fair, answers what it can and comes back to the rest. Never coming back turns it into the evasion the barrage accused you of.'}]}},'art-of-being-right_5':{pictureCaption:'At the coconut shy he cheers, but the coconut he aimed at still sits on its stand. He knocked over the one next to it.',bridge:'Like the stretched claim in idea 2, this swaps your point for another. Here the swap is sideways, to a neighbour, rather than outwards.',basics:{title:'Beating the point next door',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'BREAK TIME',prompt:'Ella says the class should get a longer break, because everyone is tired. Tom says breaks don\u2019t make you smarter. Everyone cheers for Tom. Did Tom answer Ella?',options:['Yes, he won the point','No, he answered a different point'],answer:1,reveal:'Ella never said breaks make you smarter. Tom beat a point next door to hers. Then he acted as if he had beaten hers.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Sometimes a person knocks down a point that looks like yours, but isn\u2019t. Then they act as if they have won. Your real point is still standing.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE THE WRONG TARGET',picture:!0,text:'At the fair, you throw at one coconut. You hit the one next to it, and you cheer. But the one you aimed at is still there. Hitting *something* is not the same as hitting the right thing.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'What Ella said',items:['We need a longer break','Because we get tired']},right:{title:'What Tom beat',items:['Breaks make you smarter','He says that isn\u2019t true']},note:'The right side lost. The left side was never touched.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why people miss it',text:'In an argument, people watch who seems to be winning. They don\u2019t check which point was won. So the switch slips past, unless someone says it out loud.'},{kind:'try',text:'If an argument moves on and your point never got an answer, say, \u201cThat\u2019s not what I said.\u201d Then say your point again.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You say your team needs more practice. Your friend says, \u201cPractice can\u2019t fix bad luck,\u201d and everyone nods. What happened?',options:['Your friend beat a different point, not yours','Your friend proved you need less practice','You said something about luck by mistake'],answer:0,why:'You never talked about luck. Your point about practice is still waiting for an answer.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE NEW ESTATE',text:'A resident argues that a new housing estate needs a bus route. The developer replies with figures showing the estate will have plenty of parking, thanks everyone, and moves on. The parking figures are sound. Nobody had questioned them. The bus route has quietly left the agenda.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Someone refutes a point that resembles the one at issue, then behaves as though the matter is settled. It works because audiences track momentum rather than logic, and because correcting it means the tedious work of restating what was actually claimed.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'A HIT, BUT NOT THAT ONE',caption:'Arms up for a hit, while the coconut he aimed at still sits on its stand. Its neighbour lies in the straw. Something was won, just not the thing in dispute.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Three versions',text:'Schopenhauer describes several. Proving a related proposition instead of the disputed one. Establishing a general principle, when its application to this case was the real question. Or showing one instance and claiming the rule. Each wins something, just not the thing being argued about.'},{kind:'flow',nodes:['The real question is asked','A neighbouring point is won','Momentum says someone won','The real question is dropped'],note:'The substitution happens gradually, which is why nobody objects at the time.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'KEEP IT WRITTEN DOWN',title:'The defence',text:'Keep the original question on paper, or restate it aloud at intervals. When a conclusion is drawn, compare it with the question. If they do not match, say so plainly: that is not the claim I made.'},{kind:'try',text:'At your next contentious meeting, write the actual question at the top of your notes. Check any conclusion against it before you agree to anything.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You argue that a training course did not improve sales. A colleague shows that staff enjoyed the course, and says that settles it. What is the best reply?',options:['\u201cEnjoying it isn\u2019t the same as it improving sales.\u201d','\u201cStaff enjoyment is very hard to measure fairly.\u201d','\u201cI agree it was enjoyable, so let\u2019s keep it.\u201d','\u201cYou only defend it because you chose it.\u201d'],answer:0,why:'The enjoyment point was won, but nobody disputed it. Naming the gap brings back the real question. Saying they only defend it because they chose it is the personal switch from idea 3.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Winning a point in an argument means winning the argument.',reality:'Only if it was the point at issue. Schopenhauer describes several ways of winning a neighbouring point and presenting it as the disputed one. They work because the audience counts exchanges won, not which exchange mattered.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Proving something adjacent to the disputed claim, and treating the dispute as settled, wins a real victory over a point nobody contested.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'THE QUESTION\u2019S NEIGHBOURS',left:{title:'What was disputed',items:['Does the rule apply here?','Is the rule true?','Does the policy work?']},right:{title:'What gets proved',items:['A general principle','One case where it held','That its aim is good']},note:'Each item on the right is a real proof. None is a proof of its partner on the left.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it is invisible',text:'The substitution is gradual. Each exchange moves a little away from the original question, and each is won fairly on its own terms. By the time the conclusion is drawn, the room has usually forgotten what was first asked. That forgetting is what the stratagem depends on.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The thrower celebrates a real hit. The coconut in the straw fell fairly; it just was not the one he aimed at, which still sits untouched on its stand.'},{kind:'term',word:'Ignoratio elenchi',meaning:'Latin, roughly \u2018ignorance of the refutation\u2019. An old logicians\u2019 name for proving a conclusion other than the one in dispute. The reasoning may be valid. It is simply about something else.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The counter',text:'Keep the original question in writing, or restate it before any conclusion is accepted, and compare the two. The objection itself is short: that is not the claim I made. It is tedious to make, which is part of why the trick works.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The objection can be abused. Some speakers meet every rebuttal with a claim that they meant something else, quietly moving their position so it can never be pinned down. And sometimes the neighbouring point really does settle the question, because the original claim depended on it. The test is whether the disputed claim actually follows.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'DOES IT FOLLOW?',prompt:'In which case does winning a neighbouring point actually settle the dispute?',options:['The plan relied on a cost estimate that has just been shown false','A critic of a new law turns out to have backed a similar one','A new rule\u2019s aim is shown to be good, so the rule must work','One school improved under a policy, so it works everywhere'],answer:0,why:'If the claim rested on the estimate, defeating the estimate defeats the claim. The others win a nearby point and borrow its momentum.'}]}},'art-of-being-right_6':{pictureCaption:'A thick book of numbered lists, read to the end and pressed shut. One hand closes it. The other rests empty on the desk.',bridge:'Idea 1 made the case for learning the tricks. This is what Schopenhauer suggests doing once you know them.',basics:{title:'Spot the tricks. Don\u2019t use them.',beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'AT THE END OF THE LIST',text:'Arthur Schopenhauer wrote down 38 tricks people use to win arguments. Near the end he gave some plain advice. Don\u2019t argue with people who can never admit they are wrong. Argue with people who listen to reasons.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Learn the tricks to spot them, not to use them. The best defence of all is to care more about what is true than about winning. That means saying \u201cI was wrong\u201d when you are.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Saying \u201cI was wrong\u201d means you lost.',reality:'You lose the argument. But you get the true answer, which was the point all along. And next time, people know you say what you really think.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'This book of tricks has been read to the end. Now one hand is closing it. Knowing the tricks is enough.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Naming a trick takes its power',text:'Every trick works because nobody sees it. Once you can say, \u201cYou made my point bigger,\u201d or, \u201cThat\u2019s about me, not the question,\u201d the trick mostly stops working.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Worth arguing with',items:['Listens to your reasons','Can say \u201cI was wrong\u201d']},right:{title:'Not worth it',items:['Only wants to win','Never backs down']},note:'His advice: argue with people on the left. And try to be one of them.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you find out you are wrong in an argument, say so right then. Then notice what happens next.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You are arguing about a football fact. Halfway through, you check and see you were wrong. What does this lesson say?',options:['Say so straight away: \u201cI was wrong about that\u201d','Keep going, so you don\u2019t look silly','Switch to a different point you can win'],answer:0,why:'Caring about what is true means saying so the moment you see it. Switching points would be one of the tricks.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'MID-ARGUMENT',text:'Ten minutes into a disagreement about a deadline, you check the calendar to prove your point and see you had the date wrong. You have three choices: say so, change the subject, or find a new angle and press on. Two of those are on Schopenhauer\u2019s list.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The catalogue is a field guide, not a manual. Its honest use is to name moves when they are made on you. The only real defence is to care more about the truth than about winning, and to say you were wrong when you were.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why naming works',text:'Each stratagem depends on going unnoticed as a move. Extension works only if nobody notices the stretch. The personal attack works only if the switch goes unremarked. Saying what just happened, in one sentence, removes most of the force.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'MANUAL OR FIELD GUIDE',left:{title:'Read as a manual',items:['Which move wins here?','Use it before they do','Winning is the point']},right:{title:'Read as a field guide',items:['Which move was just made?','Name it, then return to the point','The truth is the point']},note:'The same thirty-eight entries. The difference is which side of them you stand on.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Choose your opponents',text:'His surest defence is to decline most contests. Argue with people who listen to reasons and can bear to be shown wrong. With people who cannot, no counter-move will help, and the dispute is not worth winning.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The book of moves, read to its last page and pressed shut. Knowing the list was the point. Using it was not.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you realise mid-argument that you are wrong, say so at once, in one sentence, before you have time to find a new angle.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Halfway through a disagreement you see that your figures were wrong. Which response is the honest use of the book?',options:['Say so now, and argue the rest on the corrected figures','Shift to a related point where your figures still hold','Ask why the other side checked your figures so closely','Let it pass, since your conclusion may still be right'],answer:0,why:'Shifting ground and questioning their motives are tricks from earlier lessons, and letting it pass quietly avoids the point. Saying so at once is the move that serves the truth.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'timeline',kicker:'A BOOK HE DID NOT PUBLISH',points:[{when:'1788',what:'Arthur Schopenhauer is born in Danzig.'},{when:'Around 1830',what:'He drafts the catalogue of thirty-eight stratagems.'},{when:'1860',what:'He dies in Frankfurt, the catalogue still unpublished.'},{when:'1864',what:'It is published from his papers.'}],note:'What we read is a manuscript he left behind, not a book he saw through the press.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The stratagems are patterns to recognise, not techniques to adopt. Naming one removes most of its force, and the surest defence is to argue only with people capable of being wrong, and to be one of them.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why recognition is most of the defence',text:'Every stratagem in the catalogue works by being invisible as a move. Extension needs nobody to notice the stretch; the personal attack needs the switch to go unremarked. Name the move in the room and a trick becomes a claim, which can be examined like any other.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The advice at the end',text:'Schopenhauer closes with advice he takes from Aristotle: do not dispute with just anyone. Choose people who reason, who will not defend absurdities, and who can bear to be in the wrong when the truth is on the other side.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The book of numbered lists, pressed shut with its ribbon showing. It has been read. The empty hand beside it is what the reader does with it.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Schopenhauer was not hopeful. He thought vanity made honest disputing rare, and observed that people cling to a losing position partly in the hope that a better argument will occur to them later, which is sometimes justified. So conceding at once has a limit: failing to answer an argument now is not proof that it is right. Asking for time is honest too.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which of the moves in these lessons have you used yourself, without naming it to yourself at the time?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HARD CASE',prompt:'Mid-debate, an opponent makes an argument you cannot answer, though you still suspect you are right. What fits the honest use of the book?',options:['Say you can\u2019t answer it yet, and take time to check','Concede at once that you were wrong all along','Move to a related point where you stand firmer','Ask why they care so much about winning this'],answer:0,why:'Honesty means not pretending to an answer you lack, nor to a change of mind you have not had. Changing the subject and questioning their motives are moves from the list.'}]}},'art-of-being-right_7':{pictureCaption:'One mug, two cards. The hand fixes one card to the plinth and leaves the other lying flat. Visitors will read the card before they look.',bridge:'Idea 6 said naming a trick takes away its force. This trick is naming itself: choosing the word before anyone argues.',basics:{title:'The word that wins before anyone argues',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'SAME BOY, TWO WORDS',text:'Max hardly talks in class. One teacher calls him *shy*. Another calls him *rude*. Max has done nothing different. But the word you hear first changes how you see him.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The word picked for something can win an argument before it starts. Nobody gave a reason. A word was chosen, and people reacted to the word.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'ONE THING, TWO NAMES',left:{title:'Kind word',items:['Careful','Saving','Brave']},right:{title:'Unkind word',items:['Scared','Stingy','Reckless']},note:'Each pair can describe the very same act. Only the word changed.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One mug, two cards. Whichever card the hand sticks on is what people will read first.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it works so well',text:'A name comes before any reasons. It sounds like plain describing, so nobody argues with it. And once a name has stuck, it is hard to take back.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What you can do',text:'If someone gives your idea an unfair name, don\u2019t argue under that name. First say what you mean in plain words: *I want to save some of my pocket money.* Not *stingy.* Then talk about it.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You want the class to try a new way to pick teams. Someone calls it \u201ca messy experiment.\u201d What should you do first?',options:['Say plainly what the new way is','Argue about whether it is messy','Call their way \u201cboring and old\u201d'],answer:0,why:'Arguing about *messy* keeps their word in charge. Calling theirs a name back is the same trick. Plain words come first.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TWO HEADLINES',prompt:'A council plans to close one road to cars on Sundays. Which headline is already arguing?',options:['Council trials Sunday road closure','Council bans drivers in Sunday crackdown','Neither, both just describe it'],answer:1,reveal:'Both describe the same plan. The second has also judged it: *ban* and *crackdown* do the arguing before a single reason is given. Even *trials* leans a little. Names are rarely neutral.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Whoever fixes the name has often settled the outcome. The same policy is caution or cowardice, a reform or an experiment, and the audience responds to the word. Names arrive before reasons and are much harder to withdraw.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it goes uncontested',text:'Naming looks like description, so nobody objects. It happens early, before the argument proper. By the time reasons arrive, the room is weighing them under a word that has already passed judgement.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One mug, two cards. The hand fixes one to the plinth and leaves the other lying flat. Visitors read the card before they look at the mug.'},{kind:'steps',kicker:'THE COUNTER',title:'When your position has been named for you',steps:['Notice the name, before you answer anything.','Restate the position in plain description.','Offer a neutral name, if one is needed.','Then argue the substance.'],note:'Do it at once. Rejecting a name later sounds like evasion.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'IN PRACTICE',text:'Your proposal to delay a launch is introduced as *getting cold feet*. Before defending it, say what it is: a two-week delay to fix a known fault. Now the room is weighing the delay, not your nerve.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A colleague calls your suggestion to review one supplier contract a \u201cwitch hunt.\u201d What is the strongest first move?',options:['Describe it plainly: a routine check of one contract\u2019s terms','Insist it is not a witch hunt and explain your motives','Call their opposition a cover-up in return','Argue the merits now and let the label fade by itself'],answer:0,why:'Plain description replaces the frame. Denying the label keeps it centre stage, and a counter-label just plays the same game.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'CONTROLLING THE VOCABULARY',text:'Several of Schopenhauer\u2019s stratagems concern the words a dispute is conducted in. His observation is that when a thing must be named, the side that picks the name can pick one that already carries its verdict. His examples come from the religious and political quarrels of his day. The mechanism is not tied to them.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The name under which a thing is discussed carries the judgement. Whoever fixes the terminology has often settled the outcome, because the fixing happens early and looks like description.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'SAME THING, OPPOSITE VERDICTS',left:{title:'Favourable',items:['Reform','Devout','Cautious','Flexible']},right:{title:'Unfavourable',items:['Innovation','Zealot','Timid','Unprincipled']},note:'*Innovation* is praise now. For a long time it was an accusation. The words drift; the mechanism does not.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the timing matters',text:'The name is fixed before any argument and rarely contested, because contesting a word seems pedantic. Later it is worse: objecting to the label once the debate is under way looks like changing the subject because the substance went badly.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The object stays the same. The hand chooses which card goes up and which lies flat, and the card is read first.'},{kind:'term',word:'Loaded language',meaning:'Words that carry a verdict while appearing only to describe. A test: could someone on the other side accept the word as a fair description? If not, the word is doing an argument\u2019s work.'},{kind:'limits',text:'There is no fully neutral vocabulary. Every name selects, and demanding perfect neutrality can itself become a stalling tactic. Some judging names are also simply accurate: calling a fraud a fraud is no stratagem if it is one. The counter is not to purge judgement from words but to make the judgement argue for itself.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'LABEL OR DESCRIPTION?',prompt:'Which use of a charged word is not the naming stratagem?',options:['Calling a scheme a fraud after showing its falsified accounts','Calling a proposed change an experiment on our children','Calling a cautious colleague timid before they have spoken','Calling a rival\u2019s plan radical to open the discussion'],answer:0,why:'There the word comes after the reasons and sums them up. In the others it arrives first and does the reasons\u2019 work.'}]}},'art-of-being-right_8':{pictureCaption:'A single page slid into a drawer that already has someone else\u2019s label on it. Once it is filed there, nobody reads the page.',bridge:'Idea 7 chose a flattering or damning name. This trick goes further and files your view under something people already reject.',basics:{title:'The wrong label on your idea',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE CLASS TRIP',text:'You suggest the class trip goes to the science museum. Someone says, \u201cThat\u2019s just what the teachers want.\u201d A few kids groan. Nobody has said one thing about the museum.'},{kind:'idea',text:'This trick puts your idea in a group people already don\u2019t like. Then nobody looks at your idea itself. They just react to the group.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A YUCK STICKER',text:'Say someone puts a *yuck* sticker on your lunchbox. Your lunch is fine. But now nobody wants to try it. They never even look inside.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Someone is filing a page in a drawer that already has a label. Once it is in there, nobody reads the page. They read the label.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why \u201cI\u2019m not!\u201d doesn\u2019t work',text:'If you shout that it is not a teacher idea, you sound upset. And now everyone is talking about the teachers, not the museum. That is just what the trick wanted.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What to do instead',text:'Don\u2019t argue about the label. Just say your idea again. Give your reasons. *The museum has a hands-on room, and it is free.* Let the label fall away.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You say your family should get a cat. Your brother says, \u201cThat\u2019s such a little-kid idea.\u201d What is the best reply?',options:['Give your real reasons for getting a cat','Insist loudly that you are not a little kid','Call his idea a baby idea back'],answer:0,why:'Arguing about *little-kid* keeps the label in charge. Your reasons are what the question is really about.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'picture',kicker:'FILED, NOT READ',caption:'A page slid into a drawer that already carries someone else\u2019s label. From now on it will be found, and judged, by the label.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE PROPOSAL',text:'You propose letting staff choose their own working hours. The reply: that is exactly what the old management tried. People wince. Nobody has discussed your proposal. It has been filed.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Declaring that a position belongs to some discredited category leaves the claim itself untouched. The association does the work, and the person who made the claim spends their time distancing themselves instead of arguing.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Only a resemblance is needed',text:'The link need only be plausible, not real. The burden then shifts: you must now show that you are not the thing you have been called, which was never the question.'},{kind:'flow',nodes:['Your view is named after a rejected one','You protest that it is different','Protesting sounds defensive','The debate is now about the box'],note:'Every step after the first is spent on the label, not the view.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Refuse the frame, not the label',text:'Don\u2019t argue about whether you belong in the category. State your actual position again, specifically, without reference to it. Arguing about the box concedes that the box is the question.'},{kind:'try',text:'When your view is filed under something disreputable, restate it in one concrete sentence that does not mention the category at all.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You suggest a four-day-week trial for your team. A manager replies that it is the kind of idea that sank the old firm. What is the best response?',options:['Restate the trial: one team, three months, judged on output','Explain at length how you differ from the old firm','Point out that the manager worked at the old firm too','Drop it, since the association will now stick'],answer:0,why:'A specific restatement ignores the box. Distancing yourself argues inside it, and turning it on the manager is the personal switch from idea 3.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',word:'Guilt by association',meaning:'Discrediting a claim by linking it to a person, group or doctrine the audience already rejects. The link is offered in place of an argument against the claim itself.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE ODIOUS CATEGORY',text:'Schopenhauer describes the move as placing an opponent\u2019s thesis in a detested category: calling it by the name of a doctrine the audience already rejects. His examples are heresies and schools of thought his readers knew, Manichaeism among them. Only a loose resemblance is needed.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Filing a view under a name the audience already detests makes refutation look unnecessary. The claim goes unexamined, and its holder is left distinguishing themselves from a position they never held.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why denial is weak',text:'Anyone protesting that they are not the thing they have just been called sounds defensive, whatever the truth. So the stratagem wins twice: once through the association, and again through the denial it provokes.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The page is going into a drawer someone else has already labelled. Once filed, it will be judged by the drawer, not read.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'WHERE THE LINE FALLS',low:'A shared mechanism, shown',high:'A surface resemblance, asserted',note:'Comparisons near the left can be fair argument. The stratagem lives at the right, where the link is asserted, not shown.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Sometimes the resemblance is real and relevant. If a proposal repeats the mechanism of a scheme that failed, saying so is evidence, not smear, provided the shared mechanism is shown. The counter also has a cost: refusing every comparison can shield a view from the history it ought to answer to.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SMEAR OR EVIDENCE?',prompt:'Which comparison is fair argument rather than the stratagem?',options:['This repeats the pricing rule that failed last year, for the same reason','That is just socialism dressed up in new clothes','That is exactly what our competitors would say','That sounds like the sort of thing cranks believe'],answer:0,why:'The pricing comparison names a shared mechanism that can be checked. The others ask the room to reject a view for the company it keeps.'}]}},'art-of-being-right_9':{pictureCaption:'Raised hands, a full hall, and on the lectern a single blank sheet. The performance is large. What is written down is nothing.',bridge:'Like the personal attack in idea 3, this move tends to arrive just when the argument is being lost.',basics:{title:'Big words, nothing inside',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE BIG WORDS',prompt:'An older kid explains why his team is the best. He uses lots of long words. You don\u2019t follow any of it. Whose fault is that?',options:['Yours, for not being clever enough','Maybe his, for not making sense','Nobody\u2019s, it was just too hard'],answer:1,reveal:'Most people blame themselves. But sometimes there was nothing to follow. Big words can hide the fact that nothing was said.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When some people start losing an argument, they switch to big, foggy words and sound very sure. Listeners think, *I must be slow.* Often nothing real was said.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Lots of arm-waving, and a hall full of people. But look at the paper on the stand. It is blank.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How to spot it',text:'Watch *when* it starts. If someone was losing, and then suddenly gets deep and hard to follow, that is a clue. A real idea doesn\u2019t need fog.'},{kind:'steps',title:'What to say',steps:['Say, \u201cSorry, I didn\u2019t follow that.\u201d','Ask, \u201cCan you say it in simple words?\u201d','If it is still foggy, ask once more.'],note:'A real point can be said simply. A foggy one usually can\u2019t.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A FANCY BOX',text:'A huge box in fancy paper can still be empty. You only find out by opening it. Asking for simple words is how you open the box.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'In a group project, your partner answers your question with a long, foggy speech. You didn\u2019t follow it. What should you do?',options:['Ask them to say it again in simple words','Nod, since it sounded very smart','Decide you are just not clever enough'],answer:0,why:'Asking costs you a moment. If the idea is real, it can be said simply.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'If I can\u2019t follow a confident speaker, the failure is mine.',reality:'Sometimes it is. But an audience will often assume it has failed to understand rather than that nothing was said, especially when the speaker seems calm and has standing. That assumption is what this trick runs on.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When the argument goes against them, some speakers turn unintelligible and confident. Listeners blame themselves for not following. It is the cheapest of the tricks, and the easiest to spot afterwards.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'IN THE REVIEW',text:'You ask why the forecast missed by a third. The answer is three minutes on structural headwinds, strategic realignment and second-order effects. Everyone nods. Afterwards, nobody can say what the reason was.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The timing tell',text:'The profundity arrives at a particular moment: just after a point went unanswered. That is the same timing as the personal attack. A speaker who was clear while winning and turned opaque when losing has told you something.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Raised hands, a full hall, and on the lectern a single blank sheet. The performance is large. The content is not.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The counter',text:'Ask for the claim in ordinary words, and keep asking. A real position survives being restated simply. This one does not, which is why it is a last resort and not an opening move.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you lose the thread, ask: I didn\u2019t follow that; what is the claim, in one sentence? The cost of asking is almost always smaller than it feels.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A senior colleague answers your specific objection with a long, abstract reply you can\u2019t follow. What does the idea suggest?',options:['Ask for the main claim in a sentence, and ask again if needed','Assume they are right, since they clearly know the field','Reply in equally abstract terms to show that you can','Drop the objection, to avoid looking slow in the room'],answer:0,why:'If there is a real answer, it will survive plain words. Matching the fog, or deferring to it, lets it work.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'A LIFELONG GRIEVANCE',text:'Obscurity was a sore point for Schopenhauer. For decades he attacked Hegel, the most celebrated German philosopher of the day, accusing him of passing off unintelligible writing as depth. He knew how readily an audience mistakes difficulty for profundity.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Confident, abstract language that cannot be assessed can rescue a losing position, because audiences often assume the failure of understanding is theirs rather than conclude that nothing was said.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What he observed',text:'The observation is about audiences. Faced with confident words they cannot follow, people frequently conclude that they have missed something, not that nothing was there, particularly when the speaker has standing. Admitting incomprehension feels like admitting inferiority, so few do.'},{kind:'flow',nodes:['The argument turns against the speaker','Abstract, confident language arrives','Listeners assume their own failure','Nobody asks what was claimed'],note:'Break the third link and the rest fails. One plain question does it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The gestures fill the hall. The sheet on the lectern is blank. To judge the speech, look at the sheet, not the hands.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The test',text:'Ask for the claim in ordinary words, and keep asking. A real position survives being restated simply. One that dissolves under the request was never a position, only a performance.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Some true things are hard. Physics, law and philosophy contain claims that cannot be made simple without becoming false, and a demand for plain words can be a way of dismissing difficulty itself. The fair demand is for the claim, not for the whole argument in a sentence. And a speaker who is merely bad at explaining is not running a stratagem.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'FOG OR DIFFICULTY?',prompt:'Which case is most likely the stratagem rather than real difficulty?',options:['A speaker, clear until stuck, turns abstract and won\u2019t restate the claim','A physicist says the full answer needs maths, but states the claim plainly','A lawyer explains a technical point slowly, checking that you follow','A teacher admits a question is hard and offers to go through it later'],answer:0,why:'Real difficulty can still name its claim. The stratagem is marked by its timing and by refusing the plain restatement.'}]}}}},2956,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'as-a-man-thinketh_1':{pictureCaption:'A small plant, well kept, on the sill. Outside, a storm no care could stop. Habit can shape the plant, not the weather.',basics:{title:'Do your thoughts make your life?',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'AN OLD BOOK SAYS SO',prompt:'A book says your whole life comes from the thoughts you keep having. How much of that is true?',options:['All of it','None of it','Some of it'],answer:2,reveal:'Some of your life grows from your habits. A lot of it does not.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO WROTE IT',text:'James Allen wrote this short book in England in 1903. It is called *As a Man Thinketh*. *Thinketh* is an old way to say *thinks*. He said the life you have shows the thoughts you keep having.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Allen said your thoughts make your life. That is not true of everything. But some parts of your life do grow from your habits, and those parts are worth a look.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Can grow from your habits',items:['How good you get at a skill','How you treat your friends','How tidy your bag is']},right:{title:'Does not',items:['Where you were born','If you catch a cold','If it rains on sports day']}},{kind:'picture',caption:'You can look after the plant on the sill. You cannot stop the storm outside. Your life has both kinds of thing in it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it matters which one',text:'If all of it were true, a kid who got ill must have had bad thoughts. That is wrong, and it is unkind. Bad luck is not a mark against you. Nobody earns it.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'LATE FOR SCHOOL',text:'You keep being late for school. Maybe it is a habit. You stay up late on your tablet, so you can\u2019t get up. That part you can change. But if the bus breaks down, that is not your thinking. It is the bus.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Which of these could grow from your own habits?',options:['Your spelling getting better after daily practice','A storm that stops the school trip','Your family moving to a new town'],answer:0,why:'Practice is a habit you chose. The storm and the move came from outside you.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'A BOOK FROM A FRIEND',text:'A friend lends you a slim old book: change your thinking and your life will follow. You like it. Then you think of your neighbour, laid off when the factory shut, who was as hopeful and hard-working as anyone you know.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Allen claims your circumstances are the visible result of your habitual thinking. As literal cause and effect that is false. As a description of the share of your life that follows from your habits, it points at something real.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Two versions of one sentence',text:'The strong version says thought causes circumstance, all of it. The narrow version says part of your situation follows from settled habits: how you spend your evenings, handle money, speak to people. The narrow one can be checked against a life. The strong one cannot.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'HOW MUCH FOLLOWS FROM HABIT',low:'Nothing in your life follows from your habits',high:'Everything in your life follows from your thoughts',note:'Both ends are false. The useful claim sits in between, and where it sits differs from one life to the next.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The plant on the sill is the share habit can reach. The storm beyond the glass is not, however hopeful or hard-working the person tending the pot.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the strong version is cruel',text:'If circumstance follows from thought, then the laid-off, the ill and the robbed thought their way there. Held as an absolute, the idea says everyone deserves what happened to them. The book is often sold in exactly this form.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one part of your situation that bothers you. Split it: what grew from things you do again and again, and what arrived from outside? Work on the first part only.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A colleague says your money worries are just a mindset problem, since thoughts create reality. What is the best response?',options:['Some money trouble follows from habits; much comes from pay, prices and luck','Agree, since the right mindset is what makes money arrive','Disagree, since habits have no effect on money at all','Agree, but only if the worries began fairly recently'],answer:0,why:'Keep the narrow claim and reject the literal one. Habits explain some of a situation, never all of it.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'ENGLAND, 1903',text:'James Allen published this book in 1903. Its title borrows a line from the biblical Book of Proverbs about a person being as they think in their heart. Its argument is that outer conditions grow out of inner life, the way a plant grows from its seed.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Read literally, Allen says circumstance is caused by thought, and that is false. Read narrowly, he describes the share of a situation that follows from settled dispositions, and that share is real.'},{kind:'term',word:'Disposition',meaning:'A settled tendency to think, feel or act in a certain way: quick to anger, careful with money, slow to trust. It is the part of thinking that leaves tracks in a life.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'The literal claim',items:['Thought causes circumstance','Holds for every life','Cannot be tested','Makes suffering deserved']},right:{title:'The narrow claim',items:['Habit shapes part of a situation','Varies by person and case','Can be checked against a life','Leaves room for luck and injustice']}},{kind:'picture',caption:'Allen\u2019s image is a plant growing from its seed. Keep the window in the frame: tending reaches the pot, and no disposition reaches the storm.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the line is easy to cross',text:'Both versions use the same words. The narrow one earns its plausibility from ordinary experience: people who save tend to have savings. The literal one borrows that plausibility and stretches it over things no habit touches, an accident, an epidemic, a war. A reader who accepts the first rarely notices accepting the second.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Even the narrow claim needs care. Habits are themselves shaped by upbringing, money and health, so following from your dispositions does not mean entirely your doing. Turned on other people, it slides back towards blame. It is best used about yourself, looking forward, not about others, looking back.'},{kind:'recap',points:['The literal claim, that thought causes circumstance, is false.','The narrow claim, that some of a life follows from habit, is real.','Use the narrow claim on yourself, forward, never as a verdict on others.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE SLIDE',prompt:'Which statement has slid from the narrow claim into the literal one?',options:['She lost her home in the flood because her thinking was disordered','His habit of skipping revision partly explains his exam results','Her quick temper has cost her more than one friendship','His careful saving made the lean year easier to get through'],answer:0,why:'No habit of mind raises a river. The other three describe ordinary ways that settled habits shape part of a situation.'}]}},'as-a-man-thinketh_2':{pictureCaption:'The grey rows were planted on purpose. The orange shoots came uninvited, and the hands are back pulling them, again.',bridge:'Idea 1 kept the narrow claim: some of your life follows from habit. This is Allen\u2019s picture of how habits of mind form.',basics:{title:'A mind is like a garden',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'YOUR ROOM, AGAIN',text:'You tidied your room on Saturday. It looked great. By Thursday there were clothes all over the floor again. You never chose to make it messy. It just happened, bit by bit.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'ALLEN\u2019S GARDEN',text:'James Allen wrote a short book in 1903. He said a mind is like a garden. If you plant nothing, it does not stay empty. Weed seeds blow in and grow all by themselves.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Habits of thinking grow whether you pick them or not. So looking after your mind is not one big job. It is a small job you keep doing.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The grey rows were planted on purpose. The orange weeds just turned up. The hands are pulling them out, again.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What are the weeds?',text:'Weeds are habits that grow when you are not watching. Grabbing your phone every few minutes. Telling yourself you are bad at maths. They grow from whatever is nearest.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one weed you want gone, like grabbing a screen as soon as you wake up. Pull it every day this week, not just once.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'On New Year\u2019s Day, Mia decides to stop moaning so much. By February she moans as much as ever. What would Allen\u2019s garden say?',options:['She weeded once, then stopped looking after it','She is just a moaner and can never change','She picked the wrong day to start'],answer:0,why:'One big weeding is not enough. Weeds come back, so the job has to keep going.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE JANUARY RESET',belief:'Sort your attention out properly once, and it stays sorted.',reality:'Allen\u2019s central image says otherwise. A mind is a garden: left alone it does not stay tidy, it fills with whatever seeds land there. The work is never done once.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'SIX WEEKS LATER',text:'In January you sorted your attention out: notifications off, phone out of the bedroom, an hour of reading a night. It is March. The notifications are back, the phone is on the pillow, and you cannot say when it happened.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The mind is a garden: if nothing useful is planted, something useless grows anyway. Attention is not set once. It is maintained, or it reverts.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it reverts',text:'Habits of thought form whether or not you choose them, and the ones that form by default are shaped by whatever is most available: the phone in reach, the worry most often repeated. Neglect is not neutral. It hands the plot to whatever is nearest.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Attention as a decision',items:['Sorted once, in January','A lapse feels like weakness','Big resets, long gaps']},right:{title:'Attention as maintenance',items:['Tended every week','A lapse is expected','Small checks, no gaps']}},{kind:'picture',caption:'The grey rows were planted on purpose. The orange shoots came uninvited, and the hands are back pulling them, again.'},{kind:'try',text:'Put a ten-minute weekly check in your diary: what has grown in my attention this week that I did not plant? Pull one thing out.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Which plan fits the garden image best?',options:['A short review of your habits every Sunday','One thorough digital detox each January','Waiting until things feel bad, then a big reset','Deleting every app so there is nothing left to tend'],answer:0,why:'The garden image says the work is continuous. Small regular upkeep matches it; one big act of decision does not.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'THE CONTROLLING IMAGE',text:'Allen\u2019s central image is the mind as a garden, which can be cultivated with intelligence or left to run wild. If no useful seeds are put in, useless ones fall in anyway and go on producing their own kind. The ground is never simply empty.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The alternative to cultivating the mind is not an empty plot but whatever grows unattended. So tending attention is continuous work, not a single act of decision.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW A DEFAULT TAKES ROOT',nodes:['No habit deliberately set','Attention goes to what is nearest','Repetition makes it a habit','The habit makes it nearer still'],loop:!0,note:'This is the defensible part: defaults form whether anyone selects them or not, and they form around what is available.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the image gets right',text:'It makes the work ongoing. Most resolutions treat attention as a switch: decide once and it stays decided. The garden predicts what actually happens, the slow return of the old pattern, and treats that return as weather to be expected rather than as a moral failure.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The grey rows were planted on purpose. The orange shoots came uninvited, and the hands are back pulling them, again.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The image also carries a claim the book relies on and never establishes: that you control your mind as fully as a gardener controls a plot. You do not. Sleep, illness, mood and circumstance all seed the ground. At this point the metaphor quietly does the work that evidence should be doing.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What is growing in your attention this month that you never planted?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'WHERE THE IMAGE OVERREACHES',prompt:'Which conclusion does the garden image *not* support?',options:['Anyone with persistent low mood simply failed to weed','Habits of attention drift back unless maintained','Defaults form around whatever is most available','Small regular upkeep beats one large reset'],answer:0,why:'That conclusion rests on the unearned part of the image, the gardener\u2019s full control. The other three follow from the defensible part.'}]}},'as-a-man-thinketh_3':{pictureCaption:'The press squeezes the block, and what comes out at the sides is the same stuff the block was made of all along.',bridge:'Idea 2 said habits of mind grow whether or not you choose them. Pressure is where you find out which ones grew.',basics:{title:'Hard moments show your habits',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE LAST MINUTE',prompt:'Two players miss a goal in the last minute. One shouts at the ref. One jogs back and gets ready. Why are they so different?',options:['One is just a better person','Each did what they usually practise','The ref was unfair to one of them'],answer:1,reveal:'When things get hard, there is no time to think. People do what they have done many times before. The hard moment did not make them. It showed what they had practised.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When life gets hard, you see what you usually do. Hard times don\u2019t just happen to you. They also show your habits.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The press squeezes the block. What comes out at the sides is the same orange stuff the block was made of.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'A BAD WEEK',text:'You had a horrid week. You snapped at your little brother three times. It is easy to say, *that was not really me.* But it is worth a look. It shows a habit you can work on.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'If hard things happen to you, it must be your fault.',reality:'No. Lots of hard things just happen: illness, money trouble, unfair people. They are not your fault. How you act when they come is the part you can practise.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of the last time you lost your temper. What did you do? Pick one calmer thing to practise for next time.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Sam\u2019s dog is ill, and Sam is grumpy with his friends all week. What does this idea say?',options:['His grumpiness shows a habit, but the illness is not his doing','His grumpy mood is what made the dog ill','A bad week tells you nothing about anyone'],answer:0,why:'The hard thing was not Sam\u2019s fault. How he acted is still worth a look, because that part he can practise.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'WHICH WEEK TELLS YOU MORE',prompt:'You want to know your real habits. Which tells you more?',options:['An ordinary, quiet week','A week when everything went wrong','Neither; habits cannot be seen'],answer:1,reveal:'In a quiet week you have time to choose how to act. Under strain you get the response you have rehearsed most. That is why it is the better reading.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE WEEK YOU DISCOUNT',text:'A deadline moved forward and a parent went into hospital in the same week. You were short with your partner and curt with a colleague, and you have been calling it *not really me* ever since.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Pressure does not only impose on you. It shows what you habitually are. How you respond under strain is a reading of what you have practised, and it is the part most open to change.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'What the strain imposed',items:['The moved deadline','The hospital visits','Less sleep']},right:{title:'What the strain revealed',items:['Snapping when tired','Going silent instead of asking for help','Dropping the routines that steady you']},note:'The left column was not your doing. The right column is where the work is.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The press squeezes the block, and what comes out at the sides is the same stuff the block was made of all along.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'So a hard week is a test of character, and the hardship is your fault.',reality:'No. The illness and the deadline were not your doing, and treating them as a verdict on you is false and cruel. Only the response is yours to work on.'},{kind:'try',text:'Write down one thing you did in your last hard week that you would not have predicted. Treat it as a reading of your habits, not an exception.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Two people are laid off on the same day. One spends a month in bed; the other rings everyone they know. What can you conclude?',options:['Each response says something about the person; the layoff itself does not','The one in bed must have brought on the layoff by thinking badly','Responses under strain are random and tell you nothing','The one who rang round has simply had better luck so far'],answer:0,why:'The setback was imposed on both. The difference in response is the informative part, and the part each could work on.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'ALLEN\u2019S STRONGER CLAIM',belief:'Allen only means that pressure shows what a person is like.',reality:'He claims more. Circumstance, he argues, does not make a person but reveals them, and someone is pushed about by conditions only so long as they believe themselves the creature of those conditions.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Circumstance reveals as well as imposes. Read narrowly: how you respond under strain is a reliable readout of what you have practised, and the response is more open to change than the setback.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'The defensible reading',items:['Same setback, different responses','The difference is informative','The response can be worked on']},right:{title:'The indefensible reading',items:['The setback itself reveals character','Poverty and illness as verdicts','The conditions were your doing']}},{kind:'explain',title:'The slide',text:'The book moves between these readings without marking the change, and reads as though the strong claim had been shown. Writing in 1903, Allen has no framework for separating what conditions do to people from what people bring to conditions. The narrow reading depends on exactly that separation.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The press squeezes the block, and what comes out at the sides is the same stuff the block was made of all along.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Circumstances plainly do impose. Poverty, illness and injustice are not mainly revelations of character, and treating them as such is false and cruel. The narrow reading has its own limit: a heavy enough load tells you about the load as well as the person. Use it to read your own practice, not to grade other people\u2019s suffering.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which piece of your behaviour from your last hard stretch have you been filing under *not really me*?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'USE IT ON THE RIGHT PERSON',prompt:'A manager says an employee\u2019s poor work during her divorce simply shows who she really is. What is wrong with this?',options:['It treats a heavy imposed load as a pure verdict on her character','Nothing: pressure always reveals a person\u2019s true character in full','Strain never tells you anything at all about a person','A divorce is a private matter and so cannot be discussed'],answer:0,why:'The defensible reading is a tool for examining your own practice. Turned into a verdict on someone else\u2019s hardship, it becomes the indefensible one.'}]}},'as-a-man-thinketh_4':{pictureCaption:'An empty boat, its one oar trailing loose in the water. Nobody is steering, so the current is.',bridge:'Idea 2\u2019s garden filled with weeds when nothing was planted. Allen says the same of a life with no aim.',basics:{title:'No plan is still a plan',beats:[{kind:'picture',kicker:'NOBODY ROWING',caption:'No one is rowing this boat. The oar just trails in the water. So the water decides where it goes.'},{kind:'idea',text:'If you do not pick something to aim at, you still end up somewhere. Small worries and whatever is nearby choose for you.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE SUMMER HOLIDAY',text:'Six weeks of summer, and no plans. You meant to build something, or learn something. Then it is the last week. The time went on games, scrolling and being bored.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why does that happen?',text:'Your mind always holds on to something. If you have not picked a thing, it grabs the nearest thing. Small worries are always nearest. So they fill the gap.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'A SMALL AIM IS FINE',text:'James Allen wrote about this in 1903. He said your aim does not have to be big. For you it could be learning to swim a length, or reading one long book. It just has to be picked.'},{kind:'try',text:'Write one line: *This month I am trying to\u2026* Make it small enough to really do.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Leo says he won\u2019t pick a hobby, so he can keep his options open. What does this idea say?',options:['Not picking is a choice too, and small stuff will fill the time','He is right, since keeping options open is always wise','He should wait until he finds one big passion'],answer:0,why:'Leaving the space empty does not keep it empty. Whatever is nearest moves in.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'KEEPING YOUR OPTIONS OPEN',text:'You have no particular plan for the next six months, and you call it keeping your options open. Meanwhile the weeks are filling up with email, small worries and whatever anyone else asks of you.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Without a defined aim you drift into whatever currents are nearby. Having no aim is not neutral openness. It is a state in which the trivial is certain to fill the space.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE SPACE FILLS',nodes:['Nothing chosen','Attention settles on the nearest thing','Small worries are always nearest','The months fill with them'],note:'Allen\u2019s point is about capacity. Attention has to rest on something.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'An empty boat, its one oar trailing loose in the water. Nobody is steering, so the current is.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'You need to find your calling before you can aim at anything.',reality:'Allen says the aim need not be grand. Any defined object does the job: it gives attention somewhere to go other than the nearest worry. You do not have to have found a calling to begin.'},{kind:'try',text:'Write one sentence naming what the next six months are for. If you cannot, write that you have decided to drift, and see whether you agree with it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A friend between jobs is deliberately setting no goals, to stay open. On Allen\u2019s argument, what is most likely?',options:['Small worries and other people\u2019s requests will fill the time','They will drift calmly towards the right opportunity','They will find their true calling faster without aims','Nothing much will happen, since no time is committed'],answer:0,why:'Attention does not wait. With nothing chosen, it attaches to whatever is nearest, and that is rarely what matters.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE CHAPTER ON PURPOSE',text:'Here Allen is at his bluntest. People with no central purpose, he argues, fall easy prey to petty worries, fears and troubles, and these lead towards failure. It is the sharpest observation in the book, and it has survived the century better than the metaphysics around it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Aimlessness is itself a choice. A person without a defined object does not stay open; they drift into whatever currents are nearby, and the nearest are always the petty ones.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The mechanism is capacity',text:'Attention will attach to something. If nothing substantial has been designated, it settles on whatever is nearest, and small anxieties are always nearest. So vagueness is not a neutral wait. It is a decision, usually unmade, to let the trivial set the agenda.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'HOW FAR YOU NEED TO MOVE',low:'No defined aim: the nearest worry steers',high:'One fixed, lifelong calling',note:'Allen does not ask for the right-hand end. A modest, defined aim moves you far enough from the left.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'An empty boat, its one oar trailing loose in the water. Nobody is steering, so the current is.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Some open periods are chosen and useful: recovering, exploring, waiting on information. The test is whether the openness was decided, with an end, or simply happened. And an aim fixed too early or held too rigidly has its own cost. It can blind you to better ones.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Attention always attaches to something.','With no aim set, the nearest worries take it.','A small defined aim is enough to change that.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'TELL THEM APART',prompt:'Which kind of openness is the one Allen\u2019s argument warns against?',options:['A year of drift described as keeping your options open','Three months, chosen in advance, to try out two fields','Pausing a plan for a month while waiting on test results','Holding a modest aim loosely so a better one can replace it'],answer:0,why:'The other three are decisions with a shape. Only the year of drift leaves the space for whatever is nearest to fill.'}]}},'as-a-man-thinketh_5':{pictureCaption:'One small, plain original in the case. On the walls, larger, glossier copies of the same pot. The case is where you see what came first.',bridge:'The first four ideas pulled usable parts out of the doctrine. This one asks why the doctrine is worth meeting in its original form.',basics:{title:'Find the first version',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A SONG COVER',text:'You love a new pop song. Then you find out it is a cover of a very old song. The old one is short and plain. Hearing it shows you what the new band added.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Lots of modern advice about thinking your way to a better life comes from this small 1903 book. Reading the old version helps you spot what was added later to sell it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What got added?',text:'James Allen\u2019s book is partly about religion, and partly about habits of thinking. Many newer books drop the religious part. Then they make bigger promises. Think the right way and you will get rich, or famous.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The small orange pot in the case came first. The big shiny ones on the wall are copies, made larger.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'ON A VIDEO',text:'A video says, *Believe it and you will get it!* Now you know the idea is old. You can ask a good question. Which part was in the first book, and which part did the video add?'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you hear that thinking positive gets you anything you want, ask: what was the first, plainer version of this?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Why read the old book if newer books say the same thing?',options:['To see which parts are old and which were added','Because older books are always more right','Because newer books never leave anything out'],answer:0,why:'The old book shows you the idea before anyone made it bigger. Then you can judge each part.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'SPOT THE DIFFERENCE',left:{title:'Allen, 1903',items:['Thought shapes character and conditions','Set in a religious frame','Patient self-control over years']},right:{title:'Added later',items:['Bigger, faster promises','The religion taken out','Disclaimers in the small print']}},{kind:'idea',text:'Much modern self-improvement is this 1903 text with the theology removed and the promises enlarged. Reading the original lets you see which parts are old and which were added to sell it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the source is the cheaper route',text:'The book is short enough to read in an hour, so you can see the whole argument, including the claim everything else rests on. Most people meet the idea scattered across a hundred later versions, each hedged differently. An hour with the original lets you judge that claim directly.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One small, plain original in the case. On the walls, larger, glossier copies of the same pot. The case is where you see what came first.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick a genre you read or listen to: productivity, money, fitness. Find one origin text and read it. Note one idea that was already there and one that was added.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A podcast says your thoughts attract wealth. What does reading Allen\u2019s original help you do?',options:['Trace the claim to its source and judge the core of it directly','Confirm the claim, since a book from 1903 makes it too','Dismiss it, since anything that old must be out of date','Skip the source, since only the newest version matters'],answer:0,why:'The original is a way to see the load-bearing claim plainly. Its age neither proves nor disproves it.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'timeline',kicker:'THE SAME CLAIM, RESTATED',points:[{when:'1903',what:'James Allen publishes *As a Man Thinketh*.'},{when:'1937',what:'Napoleon Hill\u2019s *Think and Grow Rich* ties getting rich to thought and desire.'},{when:'1952',what:'Norman Vincent Peale publishes *The Power of Positive Thinking*.'},{when:'2006',what:'Rhonda Byrne\u2019s *The Secret* presents thoughts as attracting what they dwell on.'}],note:'Each restates some version of the claim that thought shapes circumstance. Which drew on Allen directly is a separate question.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The book matters less for what it establishes than for what came after. Reading the origin of a genre shows its load-bearing claim directly, before later versions hedged it and enlarged its promises.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why fragments are harder to judge',text:'Met in its later forms, the doctrine arrives with qualifications attached: results may vary, effort required. Each disclaimer makes it harder to see what is actually being claimed. In Allen\u2019s short original nothing is hedged, so the foundation is in plain view, and so is its lack of support.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One small, plain original in the case. On the walls, larger, glossier copies of the same pot. The case is where you see what came first.'},{kind:'limits',text:'An origin is not a refutation. Showing that an idea is old, or was later sold hard, does not show it is wrong, and some later work tests narrow claims about habit and attention that Allen only asserted. Nor is the oldest version the purest. Allen\u2019s own title borrows from a far older text.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which piece of advice you follow could you trace to an earlier source? What changed on the way?'},{kind:'recap',points:['Much later self-improvement restates Allen\u2019s central claim.','The short original shows that claim unhedged.','Knowing where an idea came from is not the same as judging it.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'ORIGIN IS NOT A VERDICT',prompt:'Someone argues that all mindset advice is nonsense because it descends from a 1903 book. What is the best reply?',options:['The origin shows you the core claim, but each claim still needs judging','Agree: where an idea comes from settles whether it is true','Disagree: the modern versions are tested science, unlike Allen','Agree: anything rewritten later in order to sell must be false'],answer:0,why:'Reading the source is a way to see the claim clearly. Whether it holds is a separate question, answered claim by claim.'}]}},'as-a-man-thinketh_6':{pictureCaption:'The path runs smooth, then crosses onto broken ground with no sign. The only marker is the stone at the point where it changes.',bridge:'Idea 1 warned that the literal claim makes suffering deserved. Here is where Allen applies it to illness, and the warning becomes concrete.',basics:{title:'When an idea goes too far',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'A WRONG IDEA',belief:'If you get ill, it is because you were thinking bad thoughts.',reality:'That is not true. Germs, genes and accidents make people ill. Kind, happy people get ill too. Getting ill is not a punishment.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHERE IT COMES FROM',text:'James Allen wrote a short book in 1903. A lot of it is about good habits of thinking. But in one part he went too far. He said that sickness in the body comes from wrong thoughts.'},{kind:'idea',text:'An idea can be partly useful and then go too far. Allen was wrong to say thoughts cause illness. That idea blames people who are ill, and they did nothing wrong.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The path is fine, then it turns to broken ground. There is no sign. Only the orange stone marks the spot.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'IN THE PLAYGROUND',text:'Your friend\u2019s grandad is very ill. Someone says, *He should have been more cheerful.* Now you can spot it. That is the idea gone too far. It blames him for something he did not cause.'},{kind:'explain',title:'So what is still true?',text:'Feeling calm can help you cope when you are ill. But doctors and medicine are what treat the illness. And being ill never means you thought the wrong way.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Which of these goes too far?',options:['You got flu because you had sad thoughts','Being kind to yourself can help you feel better','Practising every day helps you get better at a skill'],answer:0,why:'Flu comes from a germ, not from sad thoughts. The other two are about habits, which is where the book is useful.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'A PARCEL IN THE POST',text:'A friend with a serious diagnosis is sent a book by a well-meaning relative: change your thoughts and heal your body. She reads it late at night and starts to wonder what she did wrong.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Allen extends his claim from circumstance to the body: disordered thinking produces disease. Here a book of useful metaphors turns harmful, because it makes illness a verdict on the person who is ill.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'FOUR SMALL STEPS',nodes:['Thought shapes habits','Thought shapes circumstance','Thought shapes the body','The ill made themselves ill'],note:'Each step sounds like a small extension of the one before. The first is defensible. The last is false and cruel.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the doctrine removes',text:'If illness follows from thinking, there is no reason to look for causes outside the person: infection, genes, poverty, pollution. That removes compassion and diagnosis in one move. The patient is left to correct their thoughts, and to blame themselves when that fails.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The path runs smooth, then crosses onto broken ground with no sign. The only marker is the stone at the point where it changes.'},{kind:'try',text:'When you meet an idea about mindset that has been stretched to physical illness, stop and ask one question: what does this imply about the person who is ill?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY THE TEST',prompt:'A wellness post says illness is stored resentment that positive thinking can release. What is the main problem?',options:['It implies the ill caused their illness, and steers them away from real causes','It is fine as long as patients keep taking their medicine','It is a harmless metaphor that cheers some people up','It is wrong only because resentment is hard to measure'],answer:0,why:'Ask what the claim implies about the person who is ill. Here it implies they did it to themselves, which is false and does harm.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'A WORD TO HAVE READY',word:'Unfalsifiable',meaning:'Describes a claim that no possible evidence could count against. That is not a sign it is true. It means the claim has been built so that it cannot lose.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE CHAPTER ON THE BODY',text:'Having argued that circumstance grows from thought, Allen applies the same rule to health. Disease, he says, is rooted in thought, and suffering is the effect of wrong thinking. On this account the sick, the poor and the harmed produced their condition by thinking badly.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The doctrine becomes dangerous where it makes circumstance and health a straightforward product of thought: false, unfalsifiable, and harmful wherever it is believed.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it survives',text:'Every counterexample is absorbed. The cheerful person who falls ill had hidden negativity; the patient who recovers proves the method; the one who does not had not yet got their thinking right. Nothing can count against it. That is what makes it durable, and what should make you suspicious.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Defensible',items:['Stress affects how you feel','Mood colours how illness is lived','Attention shapes habits']},right:{title:'The doctrine',items:['Thought causes disease','Illness shows wrong thinking','Recovery depends on mindset']},note:'The left column describes partial influences among many causes. The right makes thought the cause.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The path runs smooth, then crosses onto broken ground with no sign. The only marker is the stone at the point where it changes.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The strongest objection is that mind and body do interact: stress, mood and expectation affect symptoms and recovery, and this is studied seriously. But that is a partial, two-way influence among many causes. Allen asserts one-way causation without exception, and the blame follows from that. His useful points about attention and purpose do not need it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'FIND THE CROSSING',prompt:'Which sentence marks the point where an idea about attitude has crossed into the harmful doctrine?',options:['Her cancer came back because she let negative thoughts back in','Stress can make it harder to sleep well and recover','A settled purpose makes petty worries less consuming','Calm under pressure is a habit that can be practised'],answer:0,why:'It treats a disease as the product of thought, and so the patient as its author. The other three stay on the defensible side.'}]}},'as-a-man-thinketh_7':{pictureCaption:'One drawing, pinned to the board and left there. Its soft, worn edges show how long it has been looked at.',bridge:'Idea 4 said have a defined aim. This one says keep the same aim long enough for the work to add up.',basics:{title:'Keep the same picture for a long time',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THREE NEW HOBBIES',text:'In September you started guitar. In October you switched to drawing. By December it was coding. Each one was fun. But you are still a beginner at all three.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Pick a picture of what you want to build, and keep it for a long time. Sticking with one aim lets your small efforts add up.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A JIGSAW',text:'Think of doing a jigsaw, but with a new picture on the box every week. You would never know where the pieces go. Keep the same box, and every piece finds its place.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHAT ALLEN SAID',text:'James Allen wrote in 1903 that you should hold on to your dream, and not drop it when it gets hard. He wrote as if holding on would make the dream come true. That part is not true. Holding on is the useful part.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One drawing, pinned down for a long time. Its edges are soft from being looked at again and again.'},{kind:'try',text:'Write one line about what you want to build this year. Stick it inside a door you open every day.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Ava wants to be a good swimmer. What would the useful part of Allen\u2019s idea tell her?',options:['Keep that aim for months, so each practice adds up','Just picture it well, and she will swim well','Switch sports when practice gets boring'],answer:0,why:'Holding the aim helps because it keeps her practising the same thing. Picturing alone does not teach her to swim.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TWO PROJECTS, FIVE YEARS',prompt:'Two people start side projects with the same hours a week. One picks a new idea every few months. One keeps the same one for five years. Who is likely further along?',options:['The one who kept changing','The one who kept the same aim','About equal, since the hours were equal'],answer:1,reveal:'Same hours, different result. Effort spent on one aim builds on itself. Effort spread across restarts mostly resets to zero.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Hold a picture of what you are building steadily, and long enough to work on it. A persistent goal changes what you notice and practise over years, not weeks.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it works without any magic',text:'A stable aim gives every decision something to be measured against. With a shifting one, each choice is reasonable on its own and the sum makes no sense. Restarting also throws away the compounding that makes long efforts pay.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'A stable aim',items:['Choices measured against one thing','Skills build on each other','You notice what serves it']},right:{title:'A shifting aim',items:['Each choice judged alone','Skills reset at each restart','Attention scatters to the new']}},{kind:'myth',belief:'Picture your goal vividly enough and it will come to you.',reality:'That is Allen\u2019s mystical version. The sound version is narrower: the picture matters because it keeps directing what you notice and practise, year after year. The work still has to be done.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One drawing, pinned to the board and left there. Its soft, worn edges show how long it has been looked at.'},{kind:'try',text:'Write in one sentence what you are trying to build. Put it where you will read it by accident: a lock screen, the inside of a notebook.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Which habit fits the sound version of the idea?',options:['Checking weekly choices against one written aim kept for years','Visualising success each morning so that it arrives','Choosing a fresh goal each January to stay motivated','Keeping your aims vague so that no chance is missed'],answer:0,why:'The aim earns its keep by being stable and by being used to judge decisions. Visualising alone does none of that work.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO CLAIMS IN ONE CHAPTER',left:{title:'Allen\u2019s claim',items:['Holding the image brings it about','Mind acts on the world directly','Cannot be tested']},right:{title:'The sound claim',items:['Holding the image directs attention','You act on the world','Can be checked in a life']}},{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE CHAPTER ON DREAMERS',text:'Allen praises dreamers and tells the reader to cherish a vision and not abandon it at the first difficulty. He states it mystically, as if a picture held steadily in mind will itself bring the thing about. Underneath is a claim that needs no mysticism at all.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Stripped of the mysticism, the chapter is right: a persistent picture of what you are building directs attention and practice over years, and changing target again and again forfeits that.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The mechanism',text:'A stable objective lets decisions be evaluated against something. A shifting one makes every decision locally reasonable and collectively incoherent. And a project abandoned and restarted accumulates none of the compounding that makes long efforts pay.'},{kind:'keyline',text:'The weaker version is true and sufficient. The book would be better if it argued only that.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One drawing, pinned to the board and left there. Its soft, worn edges show how long it has been looked at.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Persistence is not always a virtue. Some aims deserve to be dropped: the evidence says they will not work, or you have changed. The idea guards against abandoning a goal out of boredom or at the first difficulty, not against revising it for good reasons. Holding on to a failing vision is its own way of losing years.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'KNOW WHEN TO LET GO',prompt:'On the sound version, which is a good reason to change a long-term aim?',options:['Years of evidence show it cannot work as planned','The work has just hit its first real difficulty','A newer idea feels more exciting this month','Progress has been slower than you pictured it'],answer:0,why:'The idea protects an aim from boredom and early setbacks. It does not ask you to ignore evidence that the aim is wrong.'}]}},'as-a-man-thinketh_8':{pictureCaption:'Papers heaped at one corner, other hands waiting all round the edge. One pair rests flat and still: everyone has gathered at that desk.',bridge:'Idea 3 said strain reveals what you have practised. Allen\u2019s last chapter adds that other people read it too.',basics:{title:'People trust the calm one',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE SCHOOL TRIP',prompt:'The coach breaks down on a school trip. Kids are shouting. Who do people go to?',options:['The loudest grown-up','The calmest grown-up','The tallest grown-up'],answer:1,reveal:'People go to the calm one. Calm tells them something: *this person can handle it.*'},{kind:'idea',text:'Being calm is a skill you can build, not just something you are born with. Other people can see it, and they trust you more because of it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One person sits still, hands flat on the desk. All round the edge, other hands wait. They have come to the calm one.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why calm earns trust',text:'When things go wrong, people want someone who will not panic next time either. Seeing you calm today is how they guess about tomorrow.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO SAID IT',text:'James Allen wrote about this at the end of his 1903 book. He said calm comes from a long time of practising self-control. It is not just something you are born with.'},{kind:'steps',title:'How to practise calm',steps:['When something goes wrong, stop.','Take one slow breath.','Let others speak first.','Then say one calm, useful thing.']},{kind:'question',prompt:'In a group project, it all goes wrong the night before. Who will the group most likely turn to?',options:['The one who stays calm and thinks','The one who shouts the loudest','The one who blames everyone else'],answer:0,why:'Calm shows you can be trusted when things go wrong. That is when people need someone.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'FRIDAY, FOUR O\u2019CLOCK',text:'The system goes down late on a Friday. Watch where people take the problem. It is rarely to the most senior person in the room. It is to the one who did not raise their voice last time.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Calm is a competence other people can see. It is built through long self-control rather than handed out as a temperament, and people bring their problems to whoever has it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why calm reads as reliable',text:'Composure under pressure is visible, and it is hard to fake for long. So it works as evidence about how you will behave in the next difficulty, which is exactly what anyone deciding whether to depend on you is trying to find out.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Some people are just calm by nature, and the rest of us are not.',reality:'Allen treats serenity as the result of long and patient self-control. Temperament may give some people a head start, but calm can also be built slowly, by practice.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Papers heaped at one corner, hands waiting all round the table. One pair rests flat and still, and that is where everyone has gathered.'},{kind:'try',text:'In the next situation that goes wrong, be the slowest person in the room to speak. Then notice who is asked.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A new manager wants her team to bring problems to her early. What does this idea suggest she do?',options:['Stay visibly composed whenever problems arrive','Tell everyone that her door is always open','React strongly so people see she cares','Fix problems herself before anyone notices'],answer:0,why:'People bring problems to whoever has shown they will not make them worse. An open door matters less than what happens inside it.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE LAST CHAPTER',text:'Allen ends the book on serenity, and it is his best chapter. He treats calm as the fruit of long, patient effort in self-control, and observes that the calm person is loved and relied upon, because people instinctively feel they can depend on them.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Calm is a competence others can detect and act on, not only a private state. Because it is built rather than possessed, it serves as evidence of how you will behave in the next difficulty.'},{kind:'term',word:'Signal',meaning:'In economics and biology, a visible behaviour that carries information about something hidden. A signal is credible when it is costly, or hard to fake over time.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW TRUST GETS ALLOCATED',nodes:['Repeated calm in difficulty','Others observe it','They infer future reliability','Problems are brought to you'],note:'Allen means it spiritually. The chain works without that frame.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One pair of hands lies flat and still. Every other pair waits at the edge of that desk, one pinning down loose papers. Trust has gathered where the calm is.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Calm can be mistaken for competence. A composed person can be composed and wrong, and organisations do sometimes reward the look of control over the substance. Suppressed distress is not serenity either: someone who never shows strain may simply not be asking for help they need. The claim is about composure earned and tested over time.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Who do you take problems to? What have you watched them do that earned it?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'WHAT MAKES IT CREDIBLE',prompt:'Why does Allen\u2019s observation hold better for calm shown over years than for calm shown once?',options:['Composure kept up over years is hard to fake, so it is better evidence','A single calm moment usually means the person does not care','People forget single events and remember only the most recent','Calm only counts once someone holds a senior position'],answer:0,why:'A signal is credible when it is hard to fake. One calm moment could be luck or acting; years of it tell people what to expect.'}]}},'as-a-man-thinketh_9':{pictureCaption:'One small piece, finished to a high standard under the lamp. The rest of the bench is dark, and nothing there needs deciding yet.',bridge:'Idea 4 told you to choose an aim, however small. This is Allen\u2019s answer for the reader who still cannot find one.',basics:{title:'No big dream? Do today\u2019s job well',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'WHAT DO YOU WANT TO BE?',text:'Your class is asked what they want to be. Some say vet, or footballer. You have no idea at all. It feels like you are already behind.'},{kind:'idea',text:'If you don\u2019t know your big aim yet, that is fine. Do the job in front of you really well. Bigger aims tend to show up while you work.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One small thing, made really well under the lamp. The rest of the desk is dark. It can wait.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO SAID IT',text:'James Allen wrote a short book in 1903. He knew some readers had no big dream. His advice to them was simple. Do your small, everyday jobs as well as you can.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'A SMALL JOB',text:'Say you are asked to sort the class library. Dull? Maybe. But you sort it so well that anyone can find a book. Next term, the teacher asks you to run it.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one small job this week, like a chore or a bit of homework. Do it better than you ever have. Don\u2019t ask what it is for.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Zak feels bad because he has no big dream. What would Allen tell him?',options:['Do this week\u2019s jobs really well, and stop worrying','Stop everything until he finds his dream','Pick any dream at all, even one he does not want'],answer:0,why:'You don\u2019t need a big dream to start. Doing the job in front of you well is always open to you.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE PURPOSE TRAP',belief:'You need to find your purpose before your work can mean anything.',reality:'Allen\u2019s answer runs the other way. For the reader with no grand aim, meaning starts in the work already in front of them, done as well as it can be done.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'THREE BOOKS IN',text:'You have read three books on finding your purpose. You still cannot name yours, and now, on top of not knowing, you feel bad about not knowing.'},{kind:'idea',text:'With no great purpose in view, fasten on doing the immediate duty faultlessly, however small. Capacity shows at whatever scale is available, and for people who do that well, the scale tends to grow.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why this escapes the trap',text:'Advice about finding your purpose often produces paralysis: nothing seems worth starting until the purpose is found. This instruction asks for nothing you lack. The duty in front of you is always available, so there is always somewhere to begin.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW A DIRECTION FORMS',nodes:['The task at hand, done well','Others notice and trust you more','Larger tasks are offered','A direction starts to form'],note:'Nothing in the chain needs the destination known first.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One small piece, finished to a high standard under the lamp. The rest of the bench is dark, and nothing there needs deciding yet.'},{kind:'try',text:'If you have no answer to what you are for, do this week\u2019s ordinary work unusually well, and stop asking the question for a month.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A graduate in a dull first job keeps signing up for courses to find themselves. What would Allen advise?',options:['Do the current job unusually well while a direction forms','Quit and travel until a clear purpose appears','Keep the job but do the minimum until inspired','Write a detailed life plan before doing anything else'],answer:0,why:'Allen\u2019s answer needs no purpose to be found first. The work in front of you is where capacity shows and a direction forms.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A READER WITH NO AIM',prompt:'Allen has told the reader that thought shapes circumstance and that a central purpose is essential. What can he offer someone who cannot find one?',options:['Keep searching until it appears','Do the immediate duty perfectly','Accept a life of drift'],answer:1,reveal:'He tells them to give their whole attention to doing the duty in front of them perfectly, however small it looks, until a larger purpose forms.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Where no great purpose can be found, do the immediate duty faultlessly. Capacity is shown at whatever scale is available, and that scale tends to grow for those who do the available thing well.'},{kind:'explain',title:'An answer to his own doctrine',text:'A reader told that everything follows from right thought, who cannot name a grand aim, has nowhere to go. This instruction gives them somewhere. It is the oldest advice in the tradition, do the next right thing, and it is the part of Allen most worth keeping because it asks nothing of the metaphysics.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Waiting for purpose',items:['Nothing seems worth starting','Skill stays untested','Attention on the missing answer']},right:{title:'Doing the duty',items:['The task at hand is enough','Skill shows at the current scale','Attention on the work']}},{kind:'picture',caption:'One small piece, finished to a high standard under the lamp. The rest of the bench is dark, and nothing there needs deciding yet.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The scale does not always grow. Good work often goes unrewarded, especially where it is unseen or opportunity is scarce, and a careful worker can be kept small precisely because they are useful where they are. The instruction is a way out of paralysis, not a promise. A month of it is a good test; a decade without review is drift.'},{kind:'recap',points:['No grand purpose is needed to begin.','Do the duty in front of you, and do it well.','Review now and then: diligence is not a direction.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE LIMIT IN PRACTICE',prompt:'Someone has done an unrewarding job faultlessly for eight years, with no change in scale. What does the idea, read honestly, suggest?',options:['It was a way to start, not a reason never to review','Keep going, since faultless work always grows in time','They must have done the work less well than they think','Purpose cannot be found, so stop asking the question'],answer:0,why:'The instruction answers paralysis. It does not promise growth, and years without it are a reason to look up and choose again.'}]}}}},2957,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'base-rates_1':{pictureCaption:'One carved reader under a lamp; behind it, a tray of hundreds of plain pegs. The detail draws the eye. The tray is the count.',basics:{title:'First ask: how common is it?',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'WHO IS SAM?',prompt:'Sam is quiet, tidy and loves puzzles. Is Sam more likely to be a librarian or a farmer?',options:['A librarian','A farmer'],answer:1,reveal:'Most people say librarian, because Sam sounds like one. But there are many times more farmers than librarians. Lots of farmers are quiet and tidy and love puzzles too.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Before you judge one case, ask how common each answer is. A good story about one person can make you forget the plain count. Start with the count. Then let the story nudge it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the story wins',text:'Quiet. Tidy. Puzzles. The details feel like clues. They paint a picture, and the picture looks like a librarian. But a picture is not a count. Often the details are just colour.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Your eye goes to the one carved reader with a lamp. But look at the tray behind it. It holds hundreds of plain pegs. That is the count.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A JAR OF SWEETS',text:'A jar holds 90 red sweets and 10 blue ones, all the same shape. You grab one in the dark. You have a hunch it is blue. But 9 out of 10 sweets are red, so it is still most likely red.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you guess something about a person, first ask: how many people are like that in general? Then look at the clues.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A new boy at school is very tall. Is he more likely to play basketball for a club, or not to play for a club at all?',options:['Not to play, since most kids don\u2019t play for a club','To play, since tall kids are good at it','To play, since being tall is such a strong clue'],answer:0,why:'Being tall nudges the guess a little. But most kids do not play for a club, and that count is where to start.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE INTERVIEW',text:'A candidate interviews brilliantly: fluent, warm, sharp. You leave certain you have found the one. But brilliant interviews predict job performance only weakly, so most of what impressed you says little about how they will actually do the job.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Given a vivid description, people judge likelihood by how well it fits a stereotype and largely ignore how common the category is. The detail feels like information. Often it is only atmosphere.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'A CLASSIC DEMONSTRATION',text:'People are given a sketch of someone quiet, tidy and fond of puzzles, and asked: librarian or farmer? Most say librarian, because the sketch fits. But farmers outnumber librarians many times over, and the sketch fits plenty of farmers too. The count should win. The sketch beats it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The carved reader is the vivid description. The tray of hundreds of plain pegs is the base rate. The figure holds the eye, and the tray holds most of the answer.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Atmosphere',items:['Sounds like a librarian','Interviewed brilliantly','A symptom matching a rare disease']},right:{title:'Base rate',items:['Far more farmers than librarians','Interviews predict performance weakly','The common cause is far commoner']},note:'The left column is what you notice. The right column is where the estimate should start.'},{kind:'explain',title:'When people do better',text:'This is among the more robust findings in judgement research, though its size varies a lot with how the question is framed. People do better when frequencies are given as counts, like 3 in 100, rather than as probabilities.'},{kind:'steps',title:'The boring question first',steps:['Say the base rate out loud: how often does this happen in general?','Only then look at the details of this case.','Let the details adjust the figure, not replace it.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A friend with a headache reads online that it matches a rare brain condition. What does the idea suggest?',options:['Start from how common each cause is: the common one is likelier','Take the rare one seriously, since the symptoms match so closely','Ignore the symptoms entirely, since details are only atmosphere','Assume the worst, since rare conditions are often missed'],answer:0,why:'A symptom that matches a rare disease is still, usually, the common one. The match should adjust the base rate, not replace it.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE NUMBER PEOPLE SKIP',text:'This piece makes one claim and tests it from several sides. When judging how likely something is, the most useful fact is usually how often it happens in general, and it is the fact most reliably ignored. This first idea names the mechanism.'},{kind:'term',word:'Base rate',meaning:'How common something is in the relevant population, before you know anything about the particular case. In probability it supplies the prior: the figure you start from before weighing the evidence.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Judging by resemblance quietly swaps the question *how likely is this?* for the question *how typical is this?* A vivid sketch answers the second, says almost nothing about the first, and still overrides the base rate.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the sketch wins',text:'The description does fit librarians better than farmers. That is true and nearly beside the point, because farmers outnumber librarians many times over and the sketch fits plenty of them. Fit is evidence, but weak evidence. The base rate should dominate, and people let the fit stand in for the whole answer.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Resemblance and frequency side by side: one richly detailed figure that looks the part, and a tray packed with hundreds of plain ones. The judgement goes to the figure. The probability sits in the tray.'},{kind:'equation',kicker:'THE RIGHT ORDER',parts:['Base rate','Adjustment for the details'],result:'Your estimate',note:'The error is to drop the first term and treat the second as the whole sum.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How robust, and the defence',text:'The pattern is among the more robust in judgement research, though its size varies a lot with framing, and people do better when frequencies are stated as counts rather than probabilities. The defence is to ask the boring question first: before this story, how common is this thing?'},{kind:'limits',text:'The base rate is a starting point, not a verdict. Some details are strongly diagnostic and should move you a long way; the error is ignoring the count, not using the case. And a base rate is only as good as its population. Which group counts as cases like this one is itself a choice, a problem idea 5 takes up.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE PANEL',prompt:'A hiring panel dismisses base rates as cold statistics: this candidate\u2019s brilliant interview speaks for itself. What is the strongest reply?',options:['Brilliant interviews predict performance weakly, so start there and let the interview adjust it','The panel is right: a case in front of you beats an abstract figure','Interviews are worthless and should be dropped from hiring altogether','Base rates describe groups, so they cannot apply to one person'],answer:0,why:'The interview is evidence, but weak evidence. Starting from how such candidates generally perform, then adjusting, uses both.'}]}},'base-rates_2':{pictureCaption:'Before measuring anything, the hands fix the tape to one known peg. Every other measure starts from there. How things usually go is the peg.',bridge:'Idea 1 showed the count being ignored in a guess about a person. This one applies the same count to plans and forecasts.',basics:{title:'Ask how long it usually takes',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE SCIENCE FAIR',text:'Your group is making a model volcano for the science fair. You all agree it will take one afternoon. It sounds easy. You have every step planned out.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'What is the best clue to how long it will really take?',options:['How long your plan says each step will take','How long other groups\u2019 volcanoes took last year'],answer:1,reveal:'Last year\u2019s groups are your best clue. If most of them took a week, yours will probably take about that long too, even if your plan looks good.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Before you plan your own thing, ask how long things like it usually take. Start from that number. Then think about what makes yours different.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Before measuring, the hands fix the tape to one peg. Every other measure starts from there. How long things usually take is your peg.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why plans feel faster',text:'When you plan, you picture each step going right. You don\u2019t picture the glue that won\u2019t dry, or the friend who is off sick. Other groups had problems like that too. Their times already include them.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you guess how long your homework will take, first remember how long it took last time. Start your guess from there.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your family plans to paint the shed. Dad says it will take two hours. Last time it took all day. What does this idea say?',options:['Start from all day, then adjust','Trust Dad, since he has a plan','Forget last time, since this is a new year'],answer:0,why:'Last time is the best clue to how long it really takes. Start there, and change it only for a real reason.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'KAHNEMAN\u2019S TEXTBOOK',text:'The psychologist Daniel Kahneman once led a team writing a textbook. They estimated it would take about two years. It took eight. One member had seen similar projects and knew the typical figure, but it played no part in the team\u2019s estimate.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Start from what usually happens to cases like this one, before considering anything specific about yours. That number is the anchor. Your specifics should move you from it, not replace it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why specifics mislead',text:'The specifics of your case are what produce overconfidence. Detail feels like information but mostly works as a story, and the story makes your case feel unlike the class of cases it belongs to.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Inside view',items:['Starts from your plan','Rich in detail','Feels unique','Two years']},right:{title:'Outside view',items:['Starts from similar cases','Thin, but tested','Feels pessimistic','Typically far longer']}},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the order matters',text:'Get the numbers for similar cases before any planning. Once the plan exists, the base rate reads as pessimism about your plan rather than as information about the category, and it gets argued away.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A surveyor fixes the tape to a known peg before measuring anything else. The outside view is that peg.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before your next estimate, write down how long this normally takes for anyone. Then adjust for your case, and write down why.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Your team estimates a website rebuild at three months. What does the outside view ask you to do first?',options:['Find out how long similar rebuilds have usually taken','Break the plan into tasks and add up their times','Add a safety margin of a few weeks to the plan','Ask the team how confident they feel about it'],answer:0,why:'Breaking down the plan and padding it are both still the inside view. The outside view starts from the record of similar projects.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'TWO VIEWS',word:'Outside view',meaning:'Forecasting a case from the record of similar cases, before considering its particulars. The inside view, its opposite, builds the forecast from the case\u2019s own details and plan.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Estimate from the reference class first and treat your specifics as adjustments. Most people never establish the anchor at all, and once a plan exists the anchor no longer sounds like information.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'THE CANONICAL CASE',text:'Kahneman\u2019s own team estimated their textbook at about two years. It took eight. One member had seen similar projects and knew what was typical, yet that figure played no part in the estimate. The knowledge existed. The estimate was simply not built from it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The mechanism',text:'Detail is what produces overconfidence. It feels like information and mostly functions as a story, which makes the case feel unlike the reference class it belongs to. The richer the plan, the more unique the project seems, and the further the estimate drifts from what usually happens.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE ANCHOR GETS LOST',nodes:['A plan built from specifics','The case feels unique','The base rate arrives late','It reads as pessimism'],note:'Hence the instruction: get the class\u2019s numbers before any planning begins.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Hands fixing a tape to a datum peg before measuring anything else. The reference class is the datum. The plan is measured from it.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The outside view needs a reference class that genuinely fits, and choosing one is a judgement that can go wrong, by accident or on purpose; idea 5 is about exactly that. It also says what typically happens, not what must. A project can beat the base rate. The demand is that you argue for the deviation rather than assume it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'WHERE IT WENT WRONG',prompt:'A team plans a launch in detail, then hears that similar launches usually take twice as long. They decide their plan is more thorough than most. What has happened?',options:['The base rate arrived after the plan, so it read as pessimism','They used the outside view correctly by checking similar launches','They were right, since a detailed plan makes overruns unlikely','The base rate was wrong, since launches differ too much to compare'],answer:0,why:'Hearing the number is not enough. Arriving after the plan, it was weighed against the plan instead of used to build it, which is the failure the piece predicts.'}]}},'base-rates_3':{pictureCaption:'Hundreds of ordinary fish, one rare one. Any check that sometimes mistakes an ordinary fish for the rare kind will mostly pull out ordinary ones.',bridge:'Idea 1 ignored the count in a guess about a person. Here it is ignored in a test result, and the arithmetic shows how far that misleads.',basics:{title:'When a good test is still mostly wrong',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE CHEAT CATCHER',text:'A game has a cheat catcher. Real cheats are rare: 1 player in 1,000. The catcher always spots a real cheat. But it also flags 5 in every 100 honest players by mistake.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'The catcher flags your friend. How likely is it that your friend really cheated?',options:['Very likely, about 95 in 100','About half and half','Not likely, about 1 in 50'],answer:2,reveal:'Count 1,000 players. One is a cheat, and gets flagged. The other 999 are honest, but about 50 of them get flagged by mistake. So about 51 are flagged, and only 1 really cheated.'},{kind:'equation',parts:['1 real cheat','About 50 mistakes'],result:'About 51 flagged',note:'Only 1 of the 51 really cheated.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When something is very rare, even a good test is wrong more often than right when it says yes. The mistakes come from the huge crowd of ordinary cases.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A net full of ordinary fish, and just one rare orange one. Say a checker grabs a plain fish by mistake now and then. Most of what it grabs will be plain.'},{kind:'explain',title:'So what do you do?',text:'Don\u2019t treat one flag as the answer. Check again, in a different way. A second check can help sort the one real cheat from the honest players.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'The school smoke alarm sometimes goes off when someone burns toast. Real fires are very rare. When the alarm goes off, what is most likely?',options:['Something like burnt toast, not a fire','A real fire, since alarms are well made','There is no way to tell at all'],answer:0,why:'Fires are rare and burnt toast is common, so most alarms are false ones. You still leave the building every time, because a real fire matters so much.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'BEFORE THE ARITHMETIC',prompt:'A condition affects 1 in 1,000 people. A test for it catches every real case and wrongly flags 5% of healthy people. You test positive. Roughly how likely is it that you have the condition?',options:['About 95%','About 50%','About 2%'],answer:2,reveal:'About 2%. People reliably answer far higher, trained clinicians included, because attention goes to the test\u2019s accuracy and not to how rare the condition is.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When a condition is rare, even an accurate test produces mostly false positives, because its small error rate is applied to a vastly larger pool of people who do not have it.'},{kind:'steps',kicker:'COUNT IT OUT',title:'Imagine 1,000 people',steps:['1 has the condition, and the test catches it.','999 do not. The test wrongly flags 5% of them: about 50.','So about 51 people test positive.','Only 1 of those 51 has the condition: roughly 2%.'],note:'A test like this is often called 95% accurate. That figure says nothing about how rare the condition is.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Not just medicine',text:'Any screening for something rare works the same way: fraud checks, security alerts, sifting job candidates. It will generate far more false alarms than hits, and a process designed without that in mind will spend most of its effort on the false alarms.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A net of hundreds of ordinary fish and one rare one. A check that sometimes mistakes an ordinary fish for the rare kind will, most often, come up with an ordinary one.'},{kind:'try',text:'When a check flags something rare, ask two things: how rare is it, and how often does this check raise false alarms? Treat the flag as a reason to test again, not as a conclusion.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A bank\u2019s fraud filter catches almost every fraud and wrongly flags 1% of genuine payments. Fraud is very rare among its payments. Your payment is flagged. What is the sensible reading?',options:['Probably a false alarm: worth checking, not a verdict','Almost certainly fraud, given how good the filter is','Exactly a 1% chance that the payment is genuine','Nothing at all can be concluded from a flag'],answer:0,why:'With fraud very rare, the 1% of genuine payments wrongly flagged can easily outnumber the real frauds. A flag is a reason to look again.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE ACCURACY TRAP',belief:'If a test is 95% accurate, a positive result is 95% likely to be right.',reality:'Not unless the condition is common. For one affecting 1 in 1,000, a test that catches every case but wrongly flags 5% of the healthy gives a positive that is right roughly 2% of the time.'},{kind:'idea',text:'What a positive result means depends on prevalence as much as on accuracy. Screen for something rare and the false positives, drawn from the huge unaffected pool, swamp the true ones.'},{kind:'term',word:'Prevalence',meaning:'How common a condition is in the group being tested. It is the base rate of idea 1, under its medical name.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The arithmetic, as counts',text:'Take 1,000 people. One has the condition and tests positive. Of the 999 without it, 5% test positive anyway: about 50. So a positive comes from a healthy person about 50 times for every once it comes from a sick one. Set out as counts, the answer is hard to miss, as idea 1 predicted.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHAT 95% ACCURATE HIDES',left:{title:'Hit rate',items:['Catches the one real case','Matters less when cases are rare']},right:{title:'False-alarm rate',items:['Wrongly flags 5% of the healthy','Dominates when cases are rare']},note:'One accuracy figure blends two rates. For rare conditions, the false-alarm rate is the one to ask about.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One rare fish in a net of hundreds. Any check that sometimes mistakes an ordinary fish for the rare kind will mostly pull out ordinary ones.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The 2% applies to someone drawn from the general population. A person tested because of symptoms belongs to a different reference class, where the condition is commoner and a positive means more. And a retest helps only if it can fail differently. Repeating a check that errs for the same reason repeats the error.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'An airport scanner flags 1 traveller in 20 for a search. Real threats are vanishingly rare. A security chief wants every flagged traveller treated as a likely threat. What is the flaw?',options:['Nearly every flag is a false alarm, so effort goes on innocent people','None: a flag from a good scanner is strong evidence of a threat','The scanner must be faulty, since it flags so many people','Only that the scanner ought to flag more people than it does'],answer:0,why:'With threats so rare, almost every flag comes from the huge innocent pool. A process built without that in mind spends most of its effort on false alarms.'}]}},'base-rates_4':{pictureCaption:'Every other house on the street is buried in overrunning building work. One owner looks calmly at a clear garden, sure theirs will go differently.',bridge:'Idea 2 started your own plans from the count. This one is about why that is so hard: your own case always feels like the exception.',basics:{title:'The usual rules apply to you too',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'IT WON\u2019T HAPPEN TO ME',belief:'Other people\u2019s plans go wrong. Mine won\u2019t, because I know what I\u2019m doing.',reality:'Everyone feels like that about their own plans. The people whose plans went wrong felt it too.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE SCHOOL PLAY',text:'Every year, the school play starts late on the big night. This year you are helping to run it. You have a great plan, so you feel sure it will start on time.'},{kind:'idea',text:'It is easy to think the usual rules are for other people. They are for you too. Start from what usually happens, even when the plan is yours.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why your own plan feels special',text:'You know every detail of your own plan. You know why it should work. You don\u2019t know the inside of other people\u2019s plans. You only hear how they ended. So yours always looks better.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'All the other houses have building work that goes on and on. One owner looks at a clear garden, sure their job will be quick.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Make it a rule',steps:['Write down what usually happens.','Write one real reason yours will be different.','If you can\u2019t find one, go with what usually happens.']},{kind:'question',prompt:'Every year your team\u2019s car wash raises less money than planned. This year you are running it. What does this idea say?',options:['Expect less than you hope, like other years','Expect lots, since you are better at this','Don\u2019t plan at all, since it never works'],answer:0,why:'Other years are your best guide, even with you in charge. Change the guess only if you have a real reason.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE KITCHEN EXTENSION',text:'Everyone you know whose building work ran over had an excuse. Yours will not run over, because you have found a good builder. So, almost certainly, did they.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Base rates feel as though they apply to other people. The inside view of your own case has all the detail and none of the track record, which is exactly why it is more confident and less accurate.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'AN UNEVEN CONTEST',left:{title:'Your case',items:['Known from the inside','Full of reasons it is different','Vivid']},right:{title:'The reference class',items:['Known only as statistics','No reasons visible','Abstract']},note:'A vivid particular against an abstraction. The particular wins every time.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why trying harder does not fix it',text:'The comparison stays unequal however hard you try: you still see your own reasons and nobody else\u2019s. What helps is not willpower but procedure, steps that force the general figure into the decision.'},{kind:'steps',title:'The procedure',steps:['Write down the reference-class figure first.','State, explicitly, why your case should deviate from it.','If the argument falls apart once written, use the figure.']},{kind:'picture',caption:'A street wrapped in overrunning building work, and one owner calmly facing a clear garden. Theirs, they are sure, will be different.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Suppose most people who start a side business like yours give it up within a year. You are confident yours will last. What does the idea ask of you?',options:['Start from that figure and state why you would differ','Ignore it, since you know your own situation best','Drop the idea, since the odds are against you','Work harder than the others to make sure you beat it'],answer:0,why:'The figure is the starting point, not a ban. The work is to say, in writing, why your case should deviate, and to notice when you can\u2019t.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE ASYMMETRY',prompt:'You are comparing your renovation with others that overran. What do you actually know about each?',options:['Yours in detail, theirs only as outcomes','Both in about the same detail','Theirs in detail, yours only roughly'],answer:0,reveal:'You know the inside of your own case: the reasons it is different. The cases in the reference class reach you only as statistics. That asymmetry is the whole mechanism.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Base rates feel like other people\u2019s business, because the comparison is always between a vivid particular, your own case, and an abstraction. The particular wins, so the remedy has to be a procedure rather than resolve.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where this sits in the piece',text:'The first three ideas showed base rates being ignored in judgements about people, plans and test results. This one names the hardest version: applying them to yourself, where your own case feels exempt. The inside view has all the detail and none of the track record, which is precisely why it is more confident and less accurate.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why procedure, not willpower',text:'Resolve cannot fix an unequal comparison. A procedure can: write the reference-class figure down first, then require an explicit argument for why this case should deviate. Most of the time the argument cannot be made once it has to be stated, which is the point of making it a step.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A street of overrunning building sites, and one owner calmly regarding a clear garden. The vivid particular, standing in front of the statistics.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Some cases really are different, and the procedure allows for it: a stated, checkable reason can justify moving off the base rate. The opposite misuse is a ritual in which every case passes. A reason that would also have applied to the cases that overran is no reason at all.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Base rates feel like other people\u2019s problem.','You see your case from inside, and theirs only as statistics.','Write the figure first, then argue for any deviation.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'TEST THE ARGUMENT',prompt:'Your firm is starting a project of a kind that usually overruns. Which argument for beating the base rate is strongest?',options:['Something absent from the overrunning projects that removes their usual cause','Our team is unusually talented and motivated this time','We have a detailed plan and a good project manager','We have learned from the mistakes we have heard about'],answer:0,why:'Talent, plans and good intentions were present in the overrunning projects too. Only a reason those cases lacked can justify moving off their rate.'}]}},'base-rates_5':{pictureCaption:'Rows of comparable case files, and at the end an empty shelf where this case\u2019s comparisons would sit, if any existed.',bridge:'Ideas 2 and 4 depended on finding the right comparison. This one asks what happens when there is none, or when it is picked to suit.',basics:{title:'Compare with the right group',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A NEW KIND OF THING',prompt:'Old phone batteries lasted one day. A brand-new kind of battery has just come out. How long will it last?',options:['One day, like the old ones','The old ones can\u2019t really tell you'],answer:1,reveal:'The new battery is a different kind of thing. The old record does not fit it. Using it would sound sure, and could still be wrong.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Starting from what usually happens only works if you compare with the right group. If your case is truly new, or a different kind of thing, the old numbers can trick you.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Shelves full of files about cases that are like each other. At the end, an empty shelf. For a truly new case, there may be nothing to compare.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'PICKING THE GROUP',text:'You want to guess how long a new puppy will take to learn to sit. Do you compare with all dogs? With all puppies? With puppies of the same kind? Each group gives a different answer. Picking the group is the hard part.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Watch out for picking to win',text:'It is easy to pick the group that gives the answer you want. If you want a short guess, you pick the fastest group. So say out loud which group you picked, and why.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you compare, finish this sentence out loud: things like this, done by people like us, usually\u2026'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your class is the first to go on a brand-new kind of school trip. A friend says it will be boring, because the old trips were. What is the best reply?',options:['This trip is new, so old trips may not tell us much','Old trips were boring, so this one will be too','Trips are always fun, whatever happened before'],answer:0,why:'The old trips were a different kind of thing. When the group does not fit, its numbers can mislead.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'ANY NUMBER WILL DO',belief:'The outside view means finding a statistic and trusting it over your own judgement.',reality:'It means finding the right reference class. A statistic about the wrong population is not the outside view. It is confident misinformation.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The outside view needs a genuine reference class. Where something structural has changed, or the case belongs to a different category, base rates mislead. The discipline is to state which class you are using, and why.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO WAYS IT FAILS',left:{title:'The class is wrong',items:['The technology has been superseded','The market has changed','The rules are new']},right:{title:'The class is chosen badly',items:['By accident: the obvious class','On purpose: the flattering class']},note:'The first is a fact about the world. The second is a fact about the person choosing.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'A SHIFTED BASE RATE',text:'A firm forecasts how long customers will take to pay, using ten years of its own records. But the payment rules changed last spring. The old figure is precise, well documented, and now describes a world that no longer exists.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Rows of comparable case files, and an empty shelf at the end where this case\u2019s comparisons would be, if any existed.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The honest procedure',text:'State the class out loud, argue for it and let others challenge it: projects like this, run by teams like ours, usually take this long. Most of the error enters in that sentence, so it belongs in the open.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A manager cites how long launches took at your firm a decade ago, before you moved to a very different way of building. What is the best response?',options:['Ask whether that class still fits, and argue for a better one','Accept it, since any outside view beats an inside view','Drop base rates altogether, since the past no longer applies','Average the old figure with the team\u2019s own estimate'],answer:0,why:'The discipline is neither to abandon the outside view nor to apply it blindly. State the class, argue for it, and replace it when something structural has changed.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'TWO FORECASTERS',text:'Two forecasters estimate an unusual project. One finds a statistic for something superficially similar and calls it the outside view. The other names the class she is using, explains why it fits and invites challenge. Both use base rates. Only the second uses them honestly.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The outside view is only as good as its reference class. It fails when the class is genuinely wrong and when it is chosen badly, so the class must be named, argued for and left open to challenge.'},{kind:'term',word:'Reference class',meaning:'The population of past cases you treat as comparable to this one, and whose record you start from. Choosing it is a judgement, not a lookup.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Failure one: the world moved',text:'A base rate drawn from a superseded technology, market or regulatory regime is confidently misleading. The numbers are real, but they describe cases that no longer resemble yours. A case can also belong to a different category from the obvious one.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Failure two: the class was chosen',text:'A class can be chosen badly by accident, and more easily on purpose: wanting a short estimate, you find the class of fast projects. Because every class is a choice, naming the population you claim to belong to is where most of the error actually enters.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A corridor of comparable case files ending at an empty shelf. For a genuinely novel case, the comparisons may not exist.'},{kind:'limits',text:'This idea is itself easy to misuse. Claiming that a case is truly novel is the quickest way to escape every base rate, and the inside view of idea 4 makes almost everything feel novel. Real novelty is rarer than it feels. The test is the same: state why the usual class does not fit, and let someone argue.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MOVE',prompt:'A founder says their start-up is so novel that no failure statistics apply to it. Which reply best follows the piece?',options:['Name the closest genuine class, argue for it, and let it be challenged','Agree: genuinely new ventures have no base rate at all','Use the failure rate for all businesses, whatever the field','Ignore the statistics, since founders know their own case best'],answer:0,why:'Novelty is a claim to be argued, not a pass. Applying any statistic blindly fails too. The discipline lies in naming and defending the class.'}]}}}},2958,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'being-ill-among-other-people_1':{pictureCaption:'One person in bed, and hands reaching in from every side. Each one is somebody who needs telling, answering and reassuring.',basics:{title:'When you are ill, everyone needs you',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'HOME FROM HOSPITAL',picture:!0,text:'You break your arm at football. Your gran rings and cries. Your aunt wants the whole story. Your friends text all night. To each one you say the same thing: *I\u2019m fine, don\u2019t worry.* By bedtime you are worn out.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When someone is ill, lots of people need to be told. Each one is upset, and the ill person ends up calming them all down. That is real work, and it can be handed to someone else.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why does this happen?',text:'People care, but they do not know what to do with the feeling. So they bring it to you. You are the one in the middle, so you end up looking after them. And you are the most tired person there.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A SHOP WITH ONE TILL',text:'Picture a shop with a long queue and one person at the till. Every customer needs serving. Now picture that the person at the till has the flu. That is what being ill can feel like.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE FIX',text:'Your mum says: *I\u2019ll tell everyone. I\u2019ll answer the calls.* Now Gran rings Mum, not you. Your aunt gets the story from Mum. You get to rest. Nobody cares less. The job just moved.'},{kind:'try',text:'If a friend is off ill, don\u2019t send them lots of questions. Ask their parent or a close friend how they are instead.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your dad is in hospital for a week. Lots of people want news. Which plan helps him most?',options:['Your mum passes on the news to everyone','Everyone visits him to hear it for themselves','Everyone texts him so he can reply when he likes'],answer:0,why:'Visits and texts all land on your dad. One person passing on the news takes that job off him.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE WEEK AFTER A DIAGNOSIS',prompt:'Once the news is out, who ends up doing the most work to manage it?',options:['The doctors','The close family','The person who is ill'],answer:2,reveal:'Every person who hears needs telling, and then needs their reaction absorbed. That lands on the one person they all came to see.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A diagnosis creates a queue of people to tell, and each telling means absorbing their reaction and reassuring them. The work falls on the one person with the least capacity for it, so plan for it.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW ONE CALL GOES',nodes:['You share the news','They react with real feeling','You comfort them','Next person in the queue'],loop:!0,note:'Multiply by a family, a workplace and a group of friends.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the ill person becomes the host',text:'There is no settled script for this. People arrive with real feeling and no idea what to do with it. The ill person is the only one in the room with an obvious role, so they slip into it: host, reassurer, the one who keeps things light.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Hand the job over',steps:['Pick one person who is calm and organised.','Give them the list of who needs to know.','Let them send updates and field the questions.','Point everyone else to them, kindly and firmly.'],note:'Almost nobody offers this kind of help, because it does not occur to people. It is worth asking for.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of the person you would trust with this job if you needed it. If someone near you is ill now, offer to be that person for them.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A colleague is starting treatment and dreading telling the team. What would help her most?',options:['Her manager agrees what to say and answers the team\u2019s questions','She tells each person herself, so nobody feels left out','She sends one long email and replies to every answer','Nobody mentions it, so she is never asked about it'],answer:0,why:'Telling everyone herself or answering every reply keeps the work with her, and silence leaves the team guessing. A named person carrying the questions takes most of it off her.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHAT THE ESSAY ARGUES',text:'Illness is usually described as something that happens to a body. This essay argues that much of its difficulty is social and administrative: the telling, the handling of other people\u2019s reactions, and the slow sorting of who turns up. This first idea is the plainest case.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The ill person ends up managing everyone else: reassuring visitors, absorbing their distress, and performing a version of themselves that is easier to be around. The work is invisible, substantial, and done when capacity is lowest.'},{kind:'term',word:'The host role',meaning:'The part the ill person falls into by default. With no script for anyone, they are the only one present with an obvious job, so other people\u2019s feelings become theirs to manage.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it goes unseen',text:'None of it looks like work. A phone call, a visit, a cheerful face. But each one asks the ill person to take in someone\u2019s shock and hand back comfort. The performance is part of the cost: being the patient other people can bear to visit.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'No coordinator',items:['Every call comes to the patient','The news told again and again','Each reaction absorbed in person']},right:{title:'One coordinator',items:['Calls go to one named person','News told once, then passed on','Reactions land with someone who has capacity']},note:'The essay calls the relief disproportionate, and notes that almost nobody offers it.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Some people need to tell certain people themselves, and the telling can be part of taking the news in. A coordinator who decides what others may know becomes a gatekeeper. The essay presents this as a common pattern, not a rule for everyone, and the right arrangement is whatever the ill person chooses.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Who in your life would be the right coordinator, and do they know it? Who would you be the coordinator for?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'After a death, the widow spends each day answering calls and comforting callers. Which response applies this idea?',options:['A relative takes the calls, passes on arrangements, and shields her from repeats','Friends stop calling altogether so that she can grieve in peace','She lets each call go to voicemail and replies when she can','Callers keep calls brief so that each one asks less of her'],answer:0,why:'Silence leaves her alone, and shorter calls still leave her as host. Moving the coordination to someone with capacity is the move the essay recommends.'}]}},'being-ill-among-other-people_2':{pictureCaption:'A covered dish held in at an open door. Nobody had to ask for it. A specific offer arrives as help, not as a question.',bridge:'Idea 1 showed the work illness hands to the ill person. Here is more of it, hidden inside kind offers.',basics:{title:'Offer one thing, not anything',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'YOUR FRIEND HAS A BROKEN LEG',prompt:'He is stuck at home for a month. Which offer is most likely to help him?',options:['\u201cTell me if you need anything!\u201d','\u201cI\u2019ll bring your homework on Tuesday.\u201d'],answer:1,reveal:'Both are kind. But the first one leaves him to think of a job, ask for it and feel okay asking. The second one is already done.'},{kind:'idea',text:'\u201cLet me know if you need anything\u201d sounds kind, but it hands the work back to the ill person. Real help names one thing and one day.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why is asking so hard?',text:'To ask, you have to know what you need. You have to believe the offer was real. And you have to feel okay being the one who asks. When you are ill, all three are hard.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Open offer',items:['\u201cNeed anything?\u201d','He has to think of a job','He has to ask']},right:{title:'Real offer',items:['\u201cHomework, Tuesday, okay?\u201d','The job is already picked','He only has to say yes']},note:'Both friends care just as much. Only one is holding the job.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'AT THE DOOR',caption:'The door opens, and a hand holds out a warm dish. Nobody had to ask for it. It just came. That is what a real offer looks like.'},{kind:'try',text:'Know someone who is ill or having a hard week? Offer one thing on one day. For example: *I\u2019ll walk your dog on Saturday, is that okay?*'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your neighbour has just had a baby. Which offer follows this idea?',options:['\u201cI\u2019ll bring you soup on Friday night.\u201d','\u201cShout if there is ever anything at all.\u201d','\u201cWe\u2019re always here for you, you know.\u201d'],answer:0,why:'The other two are kind but leave the work with your neighbour. The soup has a day and a job already.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'TWELVE KIND MESSAGES',text:'Your friend\u2019s partner is in hospital. She has twelve messages saying *let me know if you need anything.* She needs plenty: the dog fed, a lift on Wednesday, someone to sit with her. She asks nobody. A week later the messages have stopped.'},{kind:'idea',text:'An open offer is sincere and does almost no work, because it hands the task of identifying, asking and coordinating to the person least able to do it. The people who help name a specific thing and a specific time.'},{kind:'equation',kicker:'WHAT ONE REQUEST COSTS',parts:['Knowing what would help','Believing the offer','Being willing to ask'],result:'One request',note:'Each part takes effort, and the last is uncomfortable. An open offer asks for all three.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Flip the default',picture:!0,text:'A specific, pre-committed offer works differently: *I am bringing dinner on Thursday. Tell me if that does not suit.* Now the default is help arriving. Accepting costs nothing, and declining is the effortful choice.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'REWRITING THE MESSAGE',text:'Instead of *anything I can do?*, try: *I can take the dog Monday to Wednesday. I\u2019ll pick her up at six unless you say otherwise.* The care is the same. What changed is who has to think.'},{kind:'try',text:'Find the last time you said *let me know if you need anything.* Send a follow-up that names one thing and one day.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A friend has just been made redundant. Which message applies the idea?',options:['\u201cI\u2019ll look over your CV on Sunday morning. Send it when you can.\u201d','\u201cSo sorry. Let me know if there\u2019s any way at all I can help.\u201d','\u201cLet me know when you\u2019re ready to talk about what comes next.\u201d','\u201cI know people. Ask me whenever you want an introduction.\u201d'],answer:0,why:'Each of the others waits for your friend to decide and ask. Only one already holds the task.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'People who never take up offers of help must not really need any.',reality:'The essay argues the opposite reading. The offers fail because of their form: they hand the decision back to the person with the least capacity. The difference between helpful and unhelpful offers is not sincerity. It is who is holding the task.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Open offers transfer the work of identifying, requesting and coordinating to the ill person. Specific, pre-committed offers keep that work with the helper, which is where it belongs.'},{kind:'term',word:'Decision cost',meaning:'The effort of working out what would help, choosing it, and asking for it. The essay\u2019s general principle is that whoever has more capacity should carry it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Three costs, not one',text:'A request needs knowledge of what would help, belief that the offer was meant, and willingness to take the position of asker. The first two are effort. The third is discomfort, and for many people it is the one that stops them.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'WHO HOLDS THE TASK',low:'\u201cLet me know if you need anything\u201d',high:'\u201cDinner Thursday, tell me if not\u201d',note:'Moving right shifts the work from the person receiving to the person giving. At the far end, accepting is the easy option and declining takes effort.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'THE FAR RIGHT END',caption:'A dish already held in at the open door. The decision cost has stayed with the giver, so accepting takes no effort at all. The way out has to be just as cheap.'},{kind:'limits',text:'A pre-committed offer can impose: dinner someone cannot eat, a visit when they want quiet. The exit must be real and cheap, and the offer has to fit the person. Pushed too far, carrying the decision for someone becomes deciding for them.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Where else does an open offer hand work back to the person with less capacity? Think of a new parent, a carer, a colleague drowning in a deadline.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE FLAW',prompt:'Which offer looks specific but still leaves the decision cost with the ill person?',options:['\u201cI could do a meal, a lift or some shopping. Which would help?\u201d','\u201cI\u2019m dropping soup round on Friday. Text if Friday is bad.\u201d','\u201cI\u2019ll do the school run Tuesday and Thursday unless you say no.\u201d'],answer:0,why:'A menu still leaves the choosing and the asking with the ill person. The other two default to help arriving.'}]}},'being-ill-among-other-people_3':{pictureCaption:'The worn track to the visitor\u2019s chair does not come from the door you would expect. Help arrived from somewhere else.',bridge:'The first two ideas were about how help is given. This one is about who turns out to give it.',basics:{title:'The friends who turn up may surprise you',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'A MONTH OFF SCHOOL',text:'You are ill at home for a month. Your best friend sends one text, then goes quiet. But a girl from your class, who you hardly know, drops round with a card every week. You feel confused and hurt.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Being able to show up when things are hard is its own skill. It is not the same as how much someone likes you. So the people who turn up may not be the ones you expected.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'If my best friend did not come, they never really cared.',reality:'Often they did care. They just did not know what to say. And every week they said nothing, it got harder to start.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE SWIMMING',text:'Lots of people love the beach but cannot swim. Liking the sea and being able to swim in it are two different things. Caring about you and coping with hard times are two different things too.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Look at the worn track to the chair. It does not come from the door. Help came from a way you were not looking.'},{kind:'try',text:'Did a friend go quiet when things were hard for you? When it is over, say hello to them. The friendship can start again.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your cousin was in hospital. Her closest friend never visited. What is the most likely reason?',options:['The friend did not know what to say or do','The friend was never really her friend','The friend was secretly glad she was ill'],answer:0,why:'Mostly, friends who stay away do not know what to do. That is a missing skill, not missing love.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Illness shows you who your real friends are.',reality:'It shows you who can be near difficulty. That is a specific capability, and it lines up only loosely with how close you are. Reading the gaps as a verdict on character is mostly a mistake.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Illness often reveals that the categories you sorted your relationships into were wrong. Who shows up tracks tolerance for difficulty, not affection, and the two are not distributed the way you assumed.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW A CLOSE FRIEND VANISHES',nodes:['Doesn\u2019t know what to say','Says nothing this week','The silence becomes awkward','Harder to break next week'],loop:!0,note:'Not indifference. Awkwardness that compounds.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why acquaintances turn up',text:'The people who appear unexpectedly have often been through something similar. They know what to bring, how long to stay, what not to say. That knowledge is what makes them good at it, not how long they have known you.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The worn track to the visitor\u2019s chair comes in from an unexpected direction. That is often where the help comes from.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of someone you went quiet on during a hard time of theirs. Send a short, plain message now. It does not need to explain the silence.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'After your surgery, an old school friend never called but a neighbour came daily. What reading does the essay suggest?',options:['The neighbour copes well with difficulty; the friend may not','The neighbour is a truer friend and should replace the old one','The old friend has shown they never valued the friendship','Closeness is a myth, and only the people who act count'],answer:0,why:'Showing up is one capability among many. It does not cancel the old friendship or prove the neighbour closer.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'THE SORTING',left:{title:'Who you expected',items:['Closest friends, every week','Acquaintances, a card at most']},right:{title:'Who came',items:['Some close friends, silence','Some acquaintances, every week']},note:'The essay calls this a revelation that the categories were wrong.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Showing up under difficulty is a specific capability, distinct from affection, and it correlates only loosely with closeness. Read who appears as a fact about tolerance for difficulty, not a verdict on who cares.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Two traits, not one',text:'The usual model has one dial: how much someone cares, which predicts how much they do. The essay splits it in two. Affection is one trait. The ability to sit near illness without freezing is another. Someone can be high on the first and low on the second.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The common conclusion, and the better one',text:'The common conclusion is that the friends who vanished were never real. The essay\u2019s account is less dramatic: they did not know what to say, and each week of silence made it harder. The ones who came often knew the territory already.'},{kind:'keyline',text:'Knowing this in advance makes the absence less of an injury, and leaves the friendship somewhere to resume.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Some absences really are verdicts. A friend may withdraw out of indifference, and the reframe does not oblige you to forgive everything. It can also be borrowed by the absent friend as an excuse. And the reframe works best afterwards; during the illness, the absence may simply hurt.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'\u201cIf they loved me, they would have found a way.\u201d How would the essay answer this?',options:['Love does not supply the skill of being near difficulty, so its absence is weak evidence','They may not have loved you, and it is healthier to accept that and move on','Love and showing up are one and the same, so their failure tells you everything','It does not matter why they stayed away, because only actions count in a crisis'],answer:0,why:'The essay separates affection from the capacity to show up. A missing capacity is a poor guide to missing affection, though not proof of it.'}]}},'being-ill-among-other-people_4':{pictureCaption:'Walking out of the hospital into an empty car park. The crowd that came during the crisis has already gone.',bridge:'Idea 3 was about who turns up. This one is about when they stop.',basics:{title:'Better is not the same as over',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE FIREWORKS',text:'At a fireworks show, everyone gathers while it is loud and bright. When the last one fades, they all go home. But being ill is not like fireworks. The hard part can carry on after the bright part ends.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE WEEK AFTER',picture:!0,text:'You leave hospital after an operation. In the first week you got cards and visits. Now you are home and nobody calls. But you still can\u2019t play sport. You still get tired. And you still feel a bit wobbly inside.'},{kind:'idea',text:'People gather round when someone is very ill. When the person looks better, they drift away. But that is often when it is hardest, so it is a good time to check in.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why do people drift away?',text:'It is not because they are mean. People notice a crisis, so they come. They notice the person looks well, so they stop. But looking well and being well are not the same.'},{kind:'try',text:'Know someone who was ill a while ago? Ask a parent to help you set a reminder for a few weeks from now. Then send them a card or a message.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend\u2019s grandad came home from hospital two months ago. When could a visit help most?',options:['Now, even though everyone thinks he is fine','Only on the day he first came home','Not at all, since he is better now'],answer:0,why:'By now most people have stopped visiting. That makes a visit now worth more, not less.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'BACK AT WORK',text:'A colleague comes back after three months off. The team says *great to have you back* and moves on. She is still tired by two o\u2019clock, still has appointments, still taking in what happened. Nobody asks again.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Attention gathers around the acute phase and withdraws sharply once someone looks well, often while they are least able. The gap between the crisis ending and life resuming is the loneliest stretch.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'During the crisis',items:['Cards, visits, meals','Everyone asks how you are','Heavy load, widely shared']},right:{title:'Weeks after',items:['Messages slow, then stop','Everyone assumes it is over','Work, limits, and taking it in']},note:'Support falls away while much of the need remains.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Structural, not callous',text:'Support is mobilised by a visible crisis and stood down by visible resolution. Neither signal tracks the real need. Nobody decides to abandon anyone. The signal they were following simply went quiet.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the person won\u2019t say so',text:'The story around them has already ended with *and then she got better.* Saying it is not over means contradicting everyone. So the person is often the last one allowed to say it.'},{kind:'try',text:'If someone you know has been ill, put a note in your calendar for two months after it ended. When it comes up, ask how they are really doing.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A friend finished treatment six weeks ago and says she is fine. What does this idea suggest?',options:['Check in anyway, lightly; this is when most support has gone','Take her at her word and let her move on from it','Wait for her to ask, since she knows best what she needs','Avoid the subject, so she is not defined by her illness'],answer:0,why:'The essay says the person is often the last allowed to say it is not over. A light check-in gives them the opening.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHAT SUPPORT RESPONDS TO',nodes:['Visible crisis','Support mobilised','Visible recovery','Support stood down'],note:'Every step reads a signal. None reads the need.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Recovery is not the end of it socially. Support follows visible crisis and visible resolution, and neither tracks the real need, which is often heaviest just after attention has withdrawn.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the gap contains',text:'The essay names three loads that arrive as the crowd leaves: returning to work, managing what limitation remains, and absorbing what happened. The first two are practical. The third is the hardest to see from outside.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The doors open onto an empty car park. Whatever crowd was here during the crisis has gone; the person walking out has not finished.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The concluded story',text:'Once everyone has told themselves the episode is over, the ill person is the last who is allowed to say otherwise. Saying it is not over means correcting a story other people have settled on, and asking them to re-mobilise.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Two months is a rule of thumb from the essay, not a clinical timeline. Some people want the episode closed and resent check-ins that keep them cast as the ill one. The check has to be light and easy to wave off, an offer and not an inspection.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Who in your life looked well some months ago and has not been asked since? What would a light check-in sound like?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE COMPLAINT',prompt:'A friend says people stopped calling once she was home, so they must have stopped caring. What is the essay\u2019s reading?',options:['Support followed the visible crisis and stood down when that signal ended','They still cared, but assumed she wanted quiet to recover in peace','They were never as close to her as she had believed they were','Recovery at home simply needs less support than a hospital stay'],answer:0,why:'The essay calls the pattern structural, not callous: support answers visible signals, and those signals stop before the need does.'}]}},'being-ill-among-other-people_5':{pictureCaption:'Two hands take a cup and simply hold it. No waving it away, no apology. That is the skill.',bridge:'Idea 2 was about offering help well. This is the other side: receiving it well.',basics:{title:'Learning to say yes to help',beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Saying \u201cno thanks, I\u2019m fine\u201d is the polite thing to do.',reality:'Sometimes it is. But if you really need help, saying no leaves the other person stuck. They wanted to do something kind, and now they can\u2019t.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Letting people look after you is a skill. Some people find it hard, because they are used to doing things alone. But it is worth getting better at.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'IN BED WITH THE FLU',picture:!0,text:'Your little brother brings you a hot drink. He spilled a bit on the way. You could say sorry five times. You could get up and do it yourself. Or you could just take it, say thanks, and drink it.'},{kind:'steps',title:'How to take help well',steps:['Say thank you.','Don\u2019t say sorry for needing it.','Let them do it their way, even if it is a bit wrong.']},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it matters',text:'When someone you love is ill, you feel worried. Helping gives that worry somewhere to go. So when you let someone help you, you are giving them something too.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time someone offers to help you, say yes and thank you. Don\u2019t add *sorry*.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend offers to carry your bag because your ankle hurts. What shows the skill?',options:['Say thanks and hand it over','Say sorry and carry it anyway','Say no so they don\u2019t have to bother'],answer:0,why:'The other two leave your friend with nothing to do. Taking the help is kind to both of you.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE CAPABLE ONE',text:'You are the one who organises things. After an operation, a friend comes to cook. You apologise twice, hover in the kitchen, and end up doing the washing-up yourself. She leaves feeling she was in the way.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Being looked after is a skill, and most people reach the point of needing it without practice. Accepting help without apologising is hard for anyone whose sense of themselves runs on self-sufficiency.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Receiving badly',items:['Apologise for needing it','Pay it back at once','Redo what they did']},right:{title:'Receiving well',items:['Say what you need, plainly','Let it stay unbalanced for now','Let it be done imperfectly']},note:'The essay also names a fourth: tolerating being the one who receives.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why refusing is not kind',text:'The people around you have feeling with nowhere to go. Help gives it an outlet. Refuse it, and you are not sparing them. You leave them helpless alongside you, and the refusing exhausts both of you.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The failure nobody notices',text:'The risk is not one refusal. It is declining, kindly and repeatedly, until nobody is offering any more. People drift into it without noticing.'},{kind:'try',text:'Accept the next offer of help without explaining or apologising for needing it. Notice how long the urge to apologise lasts.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Your brother offers to do your shopping while you recover. He will buy the wrong brands. What applies the idea?',options:['Give him a list, then accept what comes back without fuss','Thank him, but do the shopping yourself so it is right','Accept, but insist on paying him back for his time','Accept, and apologise for putting him to the trouble'],answer:0,why:'Saying what you need is part of the skill. Redoing it, repaying it or apologising for it are the habits the skill replaces.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'BEFORE THE ARGUMENT',prompt:'A friend recovering from illness refuses every offer so as not to be a burden. What happens to the people offering?',options:['They feel relieved','They feel helpless','They feel nothing'],answer:1,reveal:'The essay\u2019s claim: refusal does not spare them. Their feeling has nowhere to go, so they are left helpless alongside the person they wanted to help.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Receiving care is a skill most people have never practised. Accepting help competently is itself a contribution, because it gives other people something to do with feeling that otherwise has no outlet.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Four specific difficulties',text:'The essay is precise about what is hard: accepting without reciprocating, saying what you need without apologising, letting a thing be done imperfectly, and tolerating being the recipient in a relationship that used to be balanced. Each runs against a habit of competence.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Who finds it hardest',text:'People who have always been the capable one. Their refusals look like consideration, but they exhaust both sides: the helper keeps offering, the ill person keeps deflecting. Left alone, it can end with nobody offering at all.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two hands take the cup and hold it. Nothing is waved away, nothing is apologised for. That plainness is the skill.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Not every offer should be accepted. Some help takes control the person wants to keep, and some costs more to receive than it gives. Independence can matter a great deal to someone\u2019s sense of themselves. And the reframe must not become pressure to accept help for the helper\u2019s sake.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Receiving well is a skill, and the capable find it hardest.','Refusing leaves helpers helpless, not spared.','The skill is accepting plainly, not accepting everything.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these is a misuse of the idea?',options:['Telling an ill friend she must accept your help because refusing hurts you','Accepting a lift to hospital without listing reasons you are sorry','Letting your partner cook badly rather than taking over the kitchen','Saying plainly that you need someone to collect a prescription'],answer:0,why:'The idea is a skill for the receiver to practise, not a lever for the helper. Pressing it on someone turns care into a demand.'}]}}}},2959,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'beyond-good-and-evil_1':{pictureCaption:'The grand model takes the eye. Behind it sits one small, cramped room. Nietzsche reads a grand system from the room it was planned in.',basics:{title:'Big ideas start with a person',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'AFTER THE GAME',text:'Sam has lost at chess three times this week. Now Sam says chess is a dull game for dull people. Sam has lots of reasons, and some of them are quite clever.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'Where did Sam\u2019s view of chess really come from?',options:['From careful thought about chess','From how losing made Sam feel'],answer:1,reveal:'The reasons came last. First came the losing, and a need for chess not to matter. Then Sam found reasons to fit.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A big idea often tells you about the person who holds it. So before you ask *is it true?*, ask *why would this person need it to be true?*'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO SAID THIS',text:'Friedrich Nietzsche was a German thinker. In 1886 he wrote a book called *Beyond Good and Evil*. In it he said the great thinkers of the past were each telling their own life story. They just did not know it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The big, grand model is what you see first. But look behind it. A small, cramped room sits there. The grand building was planned from that room.'},{kind:'try',text:'The trick only works fairly if you use it on yourself too. Pick a view you argue for a lot. Ask: what would I lose if it were wrong?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Mia says school uniforms are a great idea. Which question uses Nietzsche\u2019s trick?',options:['What would Mia lose if uniforms were a bad idea?','How many schools have uniforms these days?','Has Mia given at least three good reasons?'],answer:0,why:'The trick asks what the person needs to be true. Maybe Mia hates picking clothes each day. That does not make her wrong, but it is worth knowing.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A DINNER ARGUMENT',prompt:'Your uncle insists money never made anyone happy. What is most useful to know before you argue back?',options:['What the research on money says','What it would cost him to be wrong','Whether he has ever been rich'],answer:1,reveal:'Evidence rarely moves him. If he once turned down a bigger salary for work he loved, the view is holding up a life. That is why he defends it so hard.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Every philosophy is partly a memoir its author did not mean to write. Before asking whether a view is true, ask what kind of person would need it to be true.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Not an accusation of lying',text:'Nietzsche is not calling thinkers dishonest. He says the conclusion often comes first, from temperament, and the reasoning arrives afterwards to justify it. The author sincerely believes the reasoning came first. That is what makes the memoir involuntary.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE ORDER HE SUSPECTS',nodes:['A temperament and its needs','A conclusion that suits them','Reasons built to support it','A system that looks impartial'],note:'In public, a system seems to run from the reasons down. Nietzsche reads it from the bottom up.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The grand model takes the eye. Behind it, on the same bench, is the small room it was planned from. Nietzsche looks there first.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'TURNED ON YOURSELF',text:'Suppose you often argue that ambition is overrated. Ask what it would cost you if ambition mattered after all: perhaps a decision, years ago, to stop competing. That does not make your view false. It tells you where to check it hardest.'},{kind:'try',text:'Take a view you defend often. Write down, honestly, what it would cost you personally if it were false.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A colleague who has never managed anyone argues that managers add nothing. How would Nietzsche\u2019s method treat the claim?',options:['Ask what he would lose if managers mattered, and look hardest there','Treat his motive as proof that the claim must be false','Take the claim at face value, since he has no stake in it','Judge only the logic, since motives never tell you anything'],answer:0,why:'The method points you to what a view is doing for the person who holds it. A motive is a place to look, not a verdict.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'THE OPENING PART, 1886',text:'Early in *Beyond Good and Evil*, Nietzsche says what he has come to think every great philosophy has been: the personal confession of its author, a memoir written without meaning to. It sets the terms for a book that reads moral claims as having authors.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A philosophy reports its author\u2019s needs more reliably than it reports the world. Its moral aims are the seed the whole plant grew from; the reasoning came later, to justify what temperament had already decided.'},{kind:'term',word:'Rationalisation',meaning:'Reasoning produced after a conclusion, in order to defend it, which its author sincerely experiences as the route by which the conclusion was reached.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The method that follows',text:'Instead of asking whether a doctrine is true, ask what kind of person would need it to be true. A philosophy praising detachment may come from someone temperamentally unable to attach. The question is uncharitable by design, and it finds what charity misses.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A grand domed model, and behind it the small cramped room it was planned from. Nietzsche reads every system for its back room.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The objection he accepts',text:'The obvious reply is that this applies to Nietzsche too: his philosophy is also a confession. He anticipates it and accepts it. That does not refute the observation. It means the tool must be turned on whoever holds it, which is the only honest way to use it.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Where a belief comes from does not settle whether it is true; logicians call that confusion the genetic fallacy. A bitter person can be right about bitterness. Used only on opponents, the method becomes a way to dismiss any argument by diagnosing its author. It earns its keep as a guide to where to look, not as a refutation.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these is a *misuse* of Nietzsche\u2019s method?',options:['Rejecting a rival\u2019s economic theory because he grew up poor','Asking what your view of marriage would cost you if wrong','Noticing a writer who praises detachment seems unable to attach, then rereading him','Checking hardest the beliefs you would least like to give up'],answer:0,why:'A motive tells you where to look. Treating it as a refutation, and only for opponents, is the genetic fallacy wearing Nietzsche\u2019s clothes.'}]}},'beyond-good-and-evil_2':{pictureCaption:'Spectacles lie on the sill before a sharp view. Everything is seen through them, and nobody looks at them. That is the will to truth.',bridge:'Idea 1 asked what a philosopher needs to be true. This one asks why anyone needs truth at all.',basics:{title:'Why do you want the truth?',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A QUESTION NOBODY ASKS',prompt:'Why do people want to know the truth, and not a nice lie?',options:['Because truth is always best','Because lies get you in trouble','I have never asked myself'],answer:2,reveal:'Most of us pick an answer fast. But if we are honest, we never really asked. We want the truth so much that we forget it is a choice.'},{kind:'idea',text:'We all want the truth, but almost nobody asks *why*. Nietzsche did not say lies are better. He said the wish for truth should be looked at, not hidden.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO ASKED',text:'Friedrich Nietzsche was a German thinker. His book *Beyond Good and Evil* came out in 1886. It opens with an odd question. Why do we want truth? Why not a nice lie, or not knowing? The great thinkers before him built on truth without asking.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE YOUR OWN GLASSES',picture:!0,text:'If you wear glasses, you look through them all day. You almost never look *at* them. Some things we care about are like that. We see with them, so we forget they are there.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'AT HOME',text:'In some homes, everyone eats dinner at the table. In others, people eat when they like. Each family thinks its way is just normal. Nobody chose it. It was handed down, like an old chair.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one rule you live by without thinking. Bedtime, chores, how you spend Saturday. Ask: who did I get this from? Would I choose it?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Lily has always thought being busy is good and doing nothing is lazy. What would Nietzsche ask her?',options:['Where did you get that idea, and would you choose it?','Why are you not busier than you are now?','Why not just stop being busy at all?'],answer:0,why:'He does not tell her to drop the idea. He asks her to look at it, so it is a choice and not just a habit.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'A WASTED EVENING',text:'You spend Sunday evening on the sofa watching a series, and by ten you feel faintly guilty. Nobody told you to. Somewhere you learned that an evening with nothing to show for it is wasted, and you have never asked who taught you that.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Your strongest values are often ones you never chose. Nietzsche\u2019s complaint is not that they are wrong. It is that they are invisible: held so deep that nobody notices they are preferences at all.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where it starts',text:'The book opens by asking what in us really wants truth, and why not untruth, uncertainty or even ignorance. Philosophers, he says, built everything on the assumption that truth is worth having, without once examining it. He does not conclude that we should prefer lies.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'SEEN THROUGH, NEVER SEEN',caption:'The mountains are sharp because of the spectacles on the sill. You look through them all day and never at them. A value held that deep works the same way.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'FURNITURE OR CHOICE',left:{title:'Held as furniture',items:['Inherited, never examined','Feels like plain reality','Easier to spot in others']},right:{title:'Held as a choice',items:['Traced to where it came from','Its costs are known','Kept, or dropped, on purpose']},note:'The value can be identical in both columns. What changes is whether you know you hold it.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'IN A COMPANY',text:'A team that prizes speed above everything has usually never asked what it trades for it: care, testing, the quiet colleague who needs a day to think. Speed may still be right. But nobody decided it was.'},{kind:'try',text:'Write down one value you hold without question, about work, money or how to spend an evening. Ask who you got it from and what it costs you. Decide whether you would choose it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A friend has always believed a serious person owns a home by forty. What response follows Nietzsche here?',options:['Ask where the belief came from and what it costs, then decide','Drop it, since anything inherited is probably mistaken','Keep it, since a value held this long has proved itself','Leave it alone, since values cannot be argued about'],answer:0,why:'Examining a value is not abandoning it. The aim is to hold it as a choice rather than as furniture.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE FIRST SECTION',title:'The question the book opens with',text:'*Beyond Good and Evil* begins by turning on the thing philosophers prize most. Granted that we want truth, Nietzsche asks, why not untruth instead, or uncertainty, or ignorance? He calls this the problem of the value of truth, and suggests it has hardly been posed before.'},{kind:'term',word:'The will to truth',meaning:'Nietzsche\u2019s name for the drive behind inquiry: the wish to know what is so, whatever it costs. He treats it as a drive with a history and a purpose, not as the neutral starting point of thought.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Every detail of the view depends on the spectacles, and they lie unexamined on the sill. Nietzsche turns round to look at the lens rather than the landscape.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Philosophers assumed truth is worth having and built on the assumption without examining it. Nietzsche\u2019s objection is not to truth but to the preference being invisible.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it generalises',text:'Most strongly held values work the same way. What counts as a good job, a serious person or a wasted evening is usually inherited, not chosen, and far easier to see in other people. A family in which money is never discussed holds a value nobody decided on.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'HOW A VALUE IS HELD',low:'Furniture: there before you, never looked at',high:'Choice: traced, costed, kept on purpose',note:'Nietzsche asks for movement along this line, not necessarily for a different value at the end of it.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The question is easily misheard as relativism, as if truth stopped mattering once its value was questioned. Nietzsche does not say that, and his own book is relentless in pursuit of uncomfortable truths. Nor can every value be reopened at once: a life spent auditing every preference has no ground to stand on. The exercise is one value at a time.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which value would you defend without a moment\u2019s hesitation? Who handed it to you, and what does it cost?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'CORRECT THE MISREADING',prompt:'A student says: \u201cNietzsche showed that truth is no better than lies.\u201d What is the best correction?',options:['He asked why we prefer truth, objecting that the preference went unexamined','He showed that lies are more useful than truth in ordinary life','He argued that truth cannot exist, so the preference is empty','He held that only scientists, not philosophers, should seek truth'],answer:0,why:'The book questions the value of truth without concluding that falsehood is better. The complaint is about invisibility, not about truth.'}]}},'beyond-good-and-evil_3':{pictureCaption:'Two coats. One pocket holds a map that gets taken out and checked. The other has been sewn shut, so nothing new can go in.',bridge:'Idea 2 found a value nobody examines. This one finds the belief that cannot be examined, because it feels like knowledge.',basics:{title:'Being sure is not the same as being right',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'WHO IS WORSE?',belief:'A liar is always a bigger problem than someone who is just very sure.',reality:'Friedrich Nietzsche, a German thinker, said the very sure person can be the bigger problem. A liar knows the truth and hides it. The very sure person has stopped checking at all.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'ON THE WAY TO THE PARK',text:'Your friend Ben is sure the park is left at the corner. He does not look at the map. He walks fast and tells everyone to follow. Twenty minutes later, you are all lost.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A liar still knows the truth. A person who is very sure has stopped checking. So being sure can block the truth even more than a lie does.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Look at the two pockets. One has a map that gets taken out and checked. The other is sewn shut. Nothing new can get in.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why being sure is sneaky',text:'When you are sure, it does not feel like a guess. It feels like a fact. So you never think to check. And when someone says you are wrong, it feels like an attack.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of one thing you are totally sure of. Ask: how would I know if I were wrong? If you can\u2019t say, go and check.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Jo tells the teacher he checked his sums, but he knows he did not. Kai is sure every sum is right, so he will not look. Who is less likely to find a wrong sum?',options:['Kai, because he feels sure and won\u2019t look again','Jo, because telling a fib is always worse','Neither, because both of them made mistakes'],answer:0,why:'Fibbing is still wrong. But Jo knows the truth, so he can go back and check. Kai feels sure, so he won\u2019t. Being sure stops the checking.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO BE WRONG',left:{title:'The liar',items:['Knows what is true','Says something else','Can take it back']},right:{title:'The convinced',items:['Believes the false thing','Experiences it as knowledge','Has nothing to take back']},note:'One false belief is under supervision. The other is not.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Convictions are more dangerous enemies of truth than lies. A liar still holds the truth privately. Someone convinced has stopped checking, and their sincerity makes the error last.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The reasoning is about detection',text:'A lie can be withdrawn, because the liar knows it is a lie. A conviction has no such supervisor. Its holder experiences it as knowledge, which switches off every way it might be checked.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHY SINCERITY PROTECTS IT',nodes:['A belief feels like knowledge','Checking seems pointless','Challenges feel like attacks','Others read the certainty as proof'],note:'Each step makes the belief harder to dislodge, and none of them involves lying.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The map in the left pocket is worn from use. The right pocket is sewn shut. A conviction is the pocket nothing new can enter.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'TWO PEOPLE ON ONE PROJECT',text:'One colleague fudged a figure in a report and knows it. Another is certain the project will succeed and has stopped reading the numbers. The first can be caught and corrected. The second will defend the project in good faith until it fails.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you deal with someone utterly certain, treat them as the harder problem, not the person you caught out. Ask what evidence would change their mind, and yours.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'On Nietzsche\u2019s view, who is the hardest problem for the truth?',options:['A manager sure the plan is sound, who no longer looks at results','An employee who invents an excuse and knows it is invented','A salesman who exaggerates, and admits it when pressed','A friend who tells a polite lie about your cooking'],answer:0,why:'The others all still know the truth. The manager has stopped checking, so nothing will correct the error.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'AN OLDER APHORISM',text:'Nietzsche first put this in a one-line aphorism in *Human, All Too Human* (1878), and returned to it in later writing. The suspicion behind it, that certainty is a symptom to be read rather than evidence to be trusted, runs all through *Beyond Good and Evil*.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Convictions are more dangerous enemies of truth than lies, because a lie is under supervision and a conviction is not. Strength of belief carries no information about truth, and may carry negative information.'},{kind:'term',word:'Conviction',meaning:'In Nietzsche\u2019s sense, not just a belief but a belief its holder has closed to examination: experienced as knowledge and defended as part of who they are.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why sincerity makes it worse',text:'The liar keeps the truth privately and can withdraw the lie. The convinced person has no private copy of the truth to return to. So sincerity makes a false belief more durable: argument is processed as attack, and certainty reads to observers as evidence.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The negative information',text:'The beliefs held most fiercely tend to be the ones doing the most work for the believer\u2019s sense of themselves. So fierceness tells you less about the world than about how much the belief is carrying. This is idea 1\u2019s question, aimed at a single belief.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One pocket holds a well-used map, taken out and checked. The other is sewn shut. The danger is less the false map than the closed pocket.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The comparison is a provocation, not a measurement. Lies do real damage, and liars often come to believe themselves. Some certainty is earned: you are right to be sure the stove is hot. The claim is that felt certainty is not itself evidence, not that every confident person is dangerous or that lying is the lesser vice.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HARD CASE',prompt:'A scientist is completely certain of a well-tested result. Does Nietzsche\u2019s point count against her?',options:['Only if her certainty has replaced the testing, rather than come from it','Yes, since strength of belief always signals an error somewhere','No, since scientists are trained never to hold convictions','Yes, since a lie would do less harm than her certainty does'],answer:0,why:'The danger is a belief closed to checking. Certainty that comes from testing, and stays open to it, is not a conviction in his sense.'}]}},'beyond-good-and-evil_4':{pictureCaption:'A sculptor carves a boxer, and on the wall his own shadow has taken the same guard. Long attention shapes the one who gives it.',bridge:'Idea 3 warned about a belief that stops checking. This one warns about a fight that slowly reshapes the fighter.',basics:{title:'Careful what you fight, and what you stare at',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A TUG OF WAR',text:'In a tug of war, how you lean depends on how the other team pulls. Your feet, your arms, your whole body are set by them. Pull for long enough and you forget how to stand any other way.'},{kind:'idea',text:'If you spend a long time fighting something, you can start to become like it. And what you look at a lot changes you, whether you mean it to or not.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO SAID IT',text:'Friedrich Nietzsche was a German thinker. In his 1886 book *Beyond Good and Evil*, he wrote a short warning. People who fight monsters should take care not to turn into monsters. And if you stare into a deep, dark pit for long, it stares back.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'IN THE PLAYGROUND',text:'Ava hates how Zoe bosses people around. So Ava starts a group to stop Zoe. Soon Ava is telling everyone who can join and who can\u2019t. She is bossing people, just like Zoe.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The second half',text:'The pit part means this. What you look at again and again shapes you. Watch angry videos every day, and you get angrier. Not because they are evil. Because you looked so much.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'He carves a small boxer, day after day. Look at his shadow. It has raised its fists too.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of something you are against. Now write down one thing you are *for*. Spend some time on that today.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Leo\u2019s class set out to stop a group that teases kids. Now Leo\u2019s class teases that group. What has happened?',options:['They have become like what they were fighting','They have won the fight fair and square','They are just giving the teasers a taste of it'],answer:0,why:'They took on the other side\u2019s ways in order to fight it. That is the trap Nietzsche warned about.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'TEN YEARS OF OPPOSITION',text:'A campaign group forms to fight a company\u2019s secrecy. Ten years on, it vets its members, briefs against rivals and keeps its own decisions quiet. Nobody chose this. Each step was taken to win the fight.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Fight something long enough and you tend to resemble it; watch something long enough and it reshapes you. Nietzsche\u2019s famous saying is a warning to anyone whose work is opposition.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why opposition is formative',text:'To fight an opponent you adopt their terms, their tactics and their tempo. You answer their moves, in their arena, on their timetable. Those habits stick. An organisation defined by what it fights comes to resemble it, because it took its shape from the fight.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE SHAPE TRANSFERS',nodes:['You define yourself against an opponent','You copy their tactics to compete','The tactics become habits','You resemble what you fought'],note:'No single step feels like a betrayal. That is why the end point surprises people.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The stranger half',text:'The line about gazing into the abyss is less about contemplating evil than about attention. What you look at persistently reorganises what you become, whatever it is. So the real question is where you spend your attention, and whether you chose it.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'THE SHADOW ON THE WALL',caption:'The sculptor has spent so long on his boxer that his own shadow now stands in the same guard. Shape something long enough and it shapes you back.'},{kind:'try',text:'If your position is mostly defined by what you are against, write down what you are for. Then check how much of your week goes to each.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Which team is most at risk from Nietzsche\u2019s warning?',options:['A team whose whole strategy is answering one rival\u2019s every move','A team that studies its rival once a year, then sets its own plan','A team that ignores competitors and builds what users ask for','A team that copies one good idea from a rival and moves on'],answer:0,why:'A team defined by one opponent takes its shape from them. The risk grows with how much of your attention the fight takes.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE MOST QUOTED LINE',belief:'The abyss saying is a gothic warning against thinking about evil.',reality:'Read with care, it is about method and attention. Opposition makes you adopt your opponent\u2019s shape, and prolonged attention reshapes whoever gives it, whatever the object.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'SECTION 146',text:'The saying sits in the book\u2019s fourth part, a run of short sayings with no argument around them. That is partly why it is quoted to the point of meaninglessness: it travels easily. Its two halves make two different claims.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Sustained opposition shapes you into the thing you oppose, and sustained attention is formative regardless of its object. Spend attention deliberately, because it will shape you either way.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO CLAIMS IN ONE LINE',left:{title:'The monster half',items:['About method','You adopt their terms and tactics','The risk grows with the fight']},right:{title:'The abyss half',items:['About attention','What you watch reorganises you','The risk grows with the gaze']},note:'The second is the stranger and more general claim.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The hands shape the boxer; the hours of looking have shaped the sculptor\u2019s shadow into the boxer\u2019s guard. Nobody chose that second change. It came with the work.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it bites on opposition',text:'Opposition means meeting an opponent on their terms and at their tempo. A movement defined only by its enemy is trained by that enemy every day, and has little to say about itself once the enemy is gone. The habits outlive the cause.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The saying is easily turned against anyone who opposes a real wrong, as if resisting cruelty made you cruel. Some fights must be fought, and fighting is not the same as copying. And attention shapes; it does not determine. A historian who studies tyranny for a lifetime does not become a tyrant. The warning is about drift, not destiny.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What do you spend most attention opposing? What has that attention been teaching you?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which is a *misuse* of the saying?',options:['Telling anti-bullying campaigners they are bullies for campaigning','Noticing a rivalry has led your team to copy the rival\u2019s worst habits','Limiting the time you spend reading outrage you cannot act on','Writing down what your group is for, not only what it opposes'],answer:0,why:'The warning is about drifting into the opponent\u2019s methods. Opposing a wrong is not, in itself, the drift.'}]}},'beyond-good-and-evil_5':{pictureCaption:'A site cleared flat, with one small potted plant left untouched. In a book that knocks things down, notice what is left standing.',bridge:'Idea 4 warned what opposition does to you. Here, briefly, the demolition stops, and you can see what Nietzsche valued.',basics:{title:'The line where the knocking down stops',beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'A BOOK THAT KNOCKS THINGS DOWN',text:'Friedrich Nietzsche was a German thinker. His 1886 book *Beyond Good and Evil* attacks one big idea after another. But in the middle there is one short, gentle line. It says that what we do out of love is beyond good and evil.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Everything here has been knocked flat, except one small plant. In a book full of knocking down, that plant is the line about love.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When you do something out of love, you don\u2019t stop to ask *is this right?* You just do it. Nietzsche noticed that rules come in when the love is missing.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'AT HOME',text:'Your little brother falls off his bike. You run to help. You don\u2019t think, *it is my duty to help him.* You just run. No rule was needed.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO DO A GOOD THING',left:{title:'Out of love',items:['You just do it','No rule needed','It feels easy']},right:{title:'Because of a rule',items:['You stop and think','Someone says you must','It can feel hard']}},{kind:'myth',belief:'If you love someone, anything you do for them is fine.',reality:'No. Nietzsche was saying how love feels from the inside. He was not giving a free pass. A thing done out of love can still hurt people. It can still be wrong.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Grace helps her friend tidy up without being asked. Tom helps only because his mum told him to. What would Nietzsche notice?',options:['A rule was needed only where the wish to help was missing','Tom was the better helper, because he did as he was told','Grace did something wrong by not waiting to be asked'],answer:0,why:'Grace helped from love, so no rule came into it. For Tom, the rule did the job the wish did not.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'A LINE OFTEN MISREAD',belief:'Nietzsche says anything done out of love is justified.',reality:'He is not saying love excuses harm. He is saying that acts done from love are not experienced, by the person doing them, as moral choices at all.'},{kind:'idea',text:'What is done from love happens beyond good and evil. Nobody deliberates about helping someone they love; the machinery of duty, weighing and restraint never starts.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'AT THREE IN THE MORNING',text:'Your child is ill in the night. You are up before you have decided anything. There is no weighing of obligations, no sense of doing your duty. If someone later praised you for being dutiful, it would sound slightly wrong.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why this matters to his argument',text:'If moral vocabulary drops out exactly where behaviour is best, then morality is doing something other than producing good conduct. It is what we reach for when the motive is absent and the behaviour has to be compelled.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A site cleared flat, with one small potted plant left untouched. In a book that knocks things down, the line about love is where it stops.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Reading for the gaps',text:'The book is relentless. This line is unguarded, almost generous, and it sits oddly among the rest, which is the point. Nietzsche is not building a system, so his alternative shows where the demolition pauses.'},{kind:'try',text:'In the next relentless piece of writing you read, mark the moment the attack stops. Ask what the author is protecting there.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A friend says he covered up a colleague\u2019s fraud \u201cout of love\u201d. What does Nietzsche\u2019s point imply?',options:['Love explains why he did not deliberate, not whether he acted rightly','Love places the act beyond criticism, so the question is closed','The act cannot have been loving, since it turned out to be wrong','Love is beside the point, since only duty counts at work'],answer:0,why:'The line describes how loving acts feel from inside. It is not a licence, and others may still judge the act.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'SECTION 153',title:'The title doing work',text:'Among the short sayings of the fourth part is one that gives the book its title: acts done from love happen beyond good and evil. It is one of the few unguarded lines in a book otherwise given over to demolition.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Acts done from love are not experienced as moral choices at all. If the moral machinery switches off exactly where conduct is best, morality is what we reach for when the motive is missing.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE INFERENCE',nodes:['Loving acts involve no moral deliberation','Yet they are often our best conduct','So morality does not produce the best','It compels conduct when the motive is absent'],note:'The saying is evidence for the book\u2019s larger case against morality\u2019s view of itself.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Cleared ground and one untouched pot. In a book of demolition, the places where it stops are where the alternative shows.'},{kind:'explain',title:'A way of reading him',text:'Nietzsche does not lay out a positive system. His alternative appears in the gaps, where the attack pauses. Reading him well means marking those moments, because they say more about what he valued than his attacks do.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The line has been read, and used, as permission for anything done with enough feeling. Possessive parents, jealous partners and fanatics all act from something they call love, and the harm is still harm. Nietzsche describes where moral language applies, not what is allowed. The line is easily borrowed as an excuse, and should not be.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of something good you did without deliberating at all. What, if anything, would a rule have added?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'Someone objects: \u201cSo a jealous husband is beyond judgement.\u201d What is the best reply?',options:['The line says loving acts are not felt as moral choices; it does not shield them','He is right: Nietzsche held that feeling justifies whatever it produces','Jealousy is not love, so the line has nothing to say about the case','The line only covers acts of love that turn out well in the end'],answer:0,why:'Describing how an act feels from inside is not excusing it. The objection reads a description as a licence.'}]}},'beyond-good-and-evil_6':{pictureCaption:'The clay has no shape of its own, only the print of the key. A good built against an enemy is shaped by that enemy.',bridge:'Idea 5 found good conduct that needs no moral rule. This one asks where moral vocabularies come from in the first place.',basics:{title:'Two ways to build what you call good',beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO KINDS OF GOOD',left:{title:'Starts from you',items:['\u201cI want to be brave\u201d','\u201cI want to be strong\u201d','Bad is what I am not']},right:{title:'Starts from them',items:['\u201cThey are cruel\u201d','\u201cThey are proud\u201d','Good is what they are not']},note:'Both lists talk about good. They start in different places.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Some ideas of good start with what you admire and want to be. Others start with someone who hurt you, so good means *not like them*.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO SAID IT',text:'Friedrich Nietzsche was a German thinker. In his 1886 book *Beyond Good and Evil*, he asked where our ideas of good come from. Some start from pride. Some start from a hurt.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'TWO TEAMS',text:'One team says: we pass well and we never give up. Another team says: we are not cheats like the Rovers. The first team knows what it is. The second team needs the Rovers to know who it is.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it matters',picture:!0,text:'If your idea of good is only *not like them*, you always need a *them*. Take away the enemy, and you lose what you stand for. Good that starts from what you love stands on its own.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Nietzsche said the first kind is good and the second kind is bad.',reality:'It is not that simple. He said the second kind made people deeper and cleverer in some ways. You can see the two are built differently without taking his side.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Ruby says a good friend is someone who isn\u2019t fake like Chloe. Where does Ruby\u2019s idea of good start?',options:['From someone she is against','From something she admires','From a rule her teacher set'],answer:0,why:'Ruby says what a good friend is *not*. Ask her what a good friend *is*, and see if she has an answer of her own.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'TWO STATEMENTS OF VALUES',text:'One small firm writes down what it values: careful work, plain dealing, finishing what it starts. Another writes that it is not like the big banks: it does not exploit, it does not hide fees. Both sound principled. Only one would still make sense if the big banks disappeared.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Nietzsche separates a morality that starts from *good and bad*, the strong naming what they admire in themselves, from one that starts from *good and evil*, defined first by what is done to you.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHERE THE VALUE STARTS',left:{title:'Good and bad',items:['Begins by affirming itself','Good means strong, capable','Bad comes almost as an afterthought']},right:{title:'Good and evil',items:['Begins with the other','Evil is what the powerful are','Good is defined as its opposite']},note:'One starts from itself. The other starts from an enemy, so it cannot exist without one.'},{kind:'explain',title:'He is not simply choosing a side',text:'Nietzsche grants that the second morality produced subtlety, depth and psychological insight the nobles never developed. His objection is to its being made universal: a vocabulary built by and for the powerless, presented as morality itself, with its origin forgotten.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why you can use it without agreeing',text:'You do not have to accept his verdict to see that the two are different structures. A value built from what you admire can be described without mentioning anyone else. A value built from a grievance cannot, and it carries the grievance with it.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'A SHAPE TAKEN FROM ANOTHER',caption:'The clay holds a clear shape, but every edge of it came from the key beside it. Describe the clay and you have described the key.'},{kind:'try',text:'Take a strong moral position you hold. Describe it without mentioning anyone who has wronged you. If you can\u2019t, notice which kind it is.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Which statement of values is built the second way, in Nietzsche\u2019s terms?',options:['We are honest, unlike the cynics who run this town','We are honest, and keep our word even when it costs us','We aim to be honest, though we do not always manage it','We value honesty because our founders taught us to'],answer:0,why:'The line about the cynics defines its good against an opponent. The others name what they admire, whatever anyone else does.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'TWO WORDS FOR NOT GOOD',word:'Bad and evil',meaning:'In Nietzsche\u2019s account, *bad* is the noble\u2019s word for what is lowly, coined almost in passing. *Evil* is the word of those on the receiving end for the powerful, coined first, with *good* defined as its opposite.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE LAST PART OF THE BOOK',text:'In the final part, on what is noble, Nietzsche says that across the many moralities he has examined, two basic types keep recurring: master morality and slave morality. He develops the contrast at length the following year, in *On the Genealogy of Morality*.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Master morality begins by affirming itself and calls what it is not bad. Slave morality begins with the other, calls the powerful evil and defines good as the negation. The difference is where value originates.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW SLAVE MORALITY IS BUILT',nodes:['Someone has power over you','Their qualities are named evil','Good is defined as their opposite','The value now needs its enemy'],note:'Master morality runs the other way: good is named first, and bad follows as an afterthought.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The clay has a definite form, yet every edge of it was given by the key. That is the structure Nietzsche describes: a good that is the impression of someone else\u2019s shape.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What he grants, and what he objects to',text:'He credits slave morality with subtlety, depth and psychological insight the nobles never developed. His objection is to its universalisation: a vocabulary made by and for the powerless, presented as morality as such, with its origin forgotten.'},{kind:'limits',text:'His verdict is contested, and his side of it should not be softened. Nietzsche writes with open contempt for equality and for the weak, and his terms were later borrowed for causes uglier than anything in the book. Morality built from grievance has also driven just reform: the wronged are often right. You can use the structural distinction without accepting his ranking.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Pick a moral position you hold strongly. Is it built from something you admire, or from something done to you?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A reform movement defines justice entirely as the reverse of what an old regime did. On Nietzsche\u2019s analysis, what risk does it carry?',options:['Its idea of the good may not survive the regime\u2019s disappearance','Its claims must be false, since they were born of a grievance','It is bound to become exactly as cruel as the old regime','It cannot reach any insight that the old regime lacked'],answer:0,why:'A value defined by an enemy needs the enemy. That is a structural risk, not a refutation: Nietzsche himself grants such moralities depth.'}]}},'beyond-good-and-evil_7':{pictureCaption:'A mirror held up so it reflects itself, the frame repeating inside the frame. A method that judges others has to survive being pointed back.',bridge:'Idea 1 said every philosophy confesses its author. Here Nietzsche asks the same of his own.',basics:{title:'Use your own rule on yourself',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE REFEREE',text:'Max tells his team the ref was unfair, because she is friends with the other coach. Then it turns out Max\u2019s mum will ref the next game. Max says that is totally different.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'Is it different?',options:['Yes, because Max\u2019s mum is fair','No, Max\u2019s own rule applies to him too'],answer:1,reveal:'If friends make a ref unfair, then mums do too. A rule you use on others has to work on you. If not, it is not a rule. It is just a weapon.'},{kind:'idea',text:'If you have a rule for judging other people, use it on yourself too, and say so out loud. Nietzsche did this with his own big ideas.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHAT NIETZSCHE DID',text:'Friedrich Nietzsche was a German thinker. He said physics, the science of how things move, is just one way of seeing the world. Then he saw the catch: his own view was one way of seeing too. He agreed, and said that was all the better.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'This mirror shows itself, again and again. A rule for judging others should be turned back on you too.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you say someone is biased, ask: could the same be said of me? Then say your answer out loud.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Ella says people only like a band because it is popular. What would Nietzsche want her to ask?',options:['Do I only dislike it because it is popular?','How many people like the band right now?','Is the band really any good at all?'],answer:0,why:'Her rule says being popular shapes what people like. So it might shape her too. Turning it on herself is the honest move.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'PHYSICS, AND THEN HIMSELF',text:'Early in the book Nietzsche argues that physics is an interpretation of the world. It arranges the world but does not explain it. Some sections later he offers his own reading of nature. He expects the reply that this too is only an interpretation. His answer is that this would be all the better.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Take any critique of bias, motive or self-interest. Either it applies to the person making it, or it is itself an example of the thing it attacks. Nietzsche says this about himself, rather than hoping nobody notices.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Not modesty',text:'He is not modest anywhere in the book. He is being consistent. Having said every philosophy is a confession, he accepts that his own is one too. A tool that spares its user is not a tool. It is a weapon with a handle on one side.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A mirror turned on itself, the frame repeating inside the frame. A critique has to survive being pointed back at its maker.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'THE SAME ARGUMENT, TWO WAYS',left:{title:'Exempting yourself',items:['Their view is driven by interest','Silence about your own','Wins until someone notices']},right:{title:'Applying it out loud',items:['Their view is driven by interest','So, probably, is mine','Survives being noticed']},note:'The second is a weaker weapon and a far stronger argument.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'IN A FORECAST MEETING',text:'You argue the sales forecast is optimistic because the team\u2019s bonuses depend on it. Before anyone says it, add that your own estimate is cautious partly because caution protects your department. Now the argument is about the numbers, not about who caught whom.'},{kind:'try',text:'When you make a sceptical argument, apply it to your own position out loud before someone else does.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A critic says all economic research is shaped by its funders. What would Nietzsche\u2019s standard require of her?',options:['That she ask how her own work is shaped by who funds it','That she stop citing any research that had a funder','That she prove her own research has no funder at all','That she accept no claim can be true or false'],answer:0,why:'The critique applies to its maker. Saying so openly is what separates a tool from a weapon.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'SECTIONS 14 AND 22',title:'The move',text:'Nietzsche argues that physics is an interpretation and arrangement of the world, not an explanation of it. Later he reads nature itself in terms of power, and anticipates the objection that this too is only interpretation. He accepts it, and says it would be all the better.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Nietzsche turns his method on himself. A critique of motivated reasoning must apply to its author, or it becomes an instance of what it attacks. He says so openly, which few critics do.'},{kind:'term',word:'Self-application',meaning:'Testing an argument about how beliefs are formed, such as that all views are interpretations or are shaped by interest, against the beliefs of the person making it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Two readings',text:'Read one way, conceding that his view is only interpretation is a collapse: he has given up any claim to be right. Read another, it is the most honest move in the book: he refuses to exempt himself from his own tool. Both readings have serious defenders.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'HOW FAR TO TURN IT',low:'Weapon: applied to opponents only',high:'Tool: applied to everyone, author first',note:'Most sceptical arguments sit near the left and hope nobody notices. Nietzsche moves to the right, and says so.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One mirror turned on itself, frame inside frame. The test of a method is whether it still works with its user inside it.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Self-application can become a dodge. If every objection is simply welcomed, nothing could count against the view, and a view nothing could count against says very little. Conceding that you are biased also does not show you are right. The standard is necessary, not sufficient.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HONEST OBJECTION',prompt:'What is the strongest objection to Nietzsche welcoming the charge against him?',options:['Welcoming every objection can make a view immune to criticism','He was being falsely modest to win the reader over','Physics really is an explanation, so the charge is unfair','Applying a method to yourself is always a sign of weakness'],answer:0,why:'If no objection can hurt the view, the concession costs nothing. That is the collapse reading, and it deserves an answer.'}]}},'beyond-good-and-evil_8':{pictureCaption:'A sieve holds back the pebbles and lets only fine sand through. Nietzsche made his writing hard to sort readers the same way.',bridge:'Idea 7 showed him pointing his method at himself. This one is about how he wrote, and which readers the writing was meant to keep out.',basics:{title:'Hard to read on purpose',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'A SECRET CLUB',text:'Your friends make a club with a secret password. Anyone can join, but only if they work out the clue first. The clue is hard on purpose. It keeps out kids who don\u2019t really care.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Some writing is hard because the writer was not very good. Some is hard on purpose, to keep out readers who will not slow down. Nietzsche wrote the second way.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO DID THIS',text:'Friedrich Nietzsche was a German thinker. He wrote *Beyond Good and Evil* in short, strong bits, not one long, smooth chain of reasons. You can\u2019t skim it. You have to slow down and put the pieces together yourself.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why would anyone do that?',text:'Nietzsche said deep thinkers need a mask. He even said they fear being understood more than being got wrong. Hard writing was his mask. It was meant to keep out the wrong readers.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'TWO KINDS OF HARD',low:'Hard because the writer got it wrong',high:'Hard on purpose, to sort out readers',note:'Before you give up on something hard, ask where on this line it sits.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Only fine sand falls through the sieve. The big stones stay on top. Nietzsche wanted his hard writing to sort readers the same way.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'WHAT HAPPENED NEXT',text:'It did not work well. His short, strong lines are easy to copy and share. People quote them without the rest of the book. So lots of people now repeat him without knowing what he meant.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You find a poem very hard to read. What would this idea tell you to ask first?',options:['Is it hard by mistake, or hard on purpose?','Is the poet just showing off?','Should I skip it and read something easy?'],answer:0,why:'Some hard writing is a test you can pass by slowing down. Some is just badly done. Work out which it is first.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A DIFFICULT REPORT',prompt:'A report you have been sent is almost impossible to follow. What is the most likely reason?',options:['The writer could not do better','It is hard on purpose, to filter readers','You can\u2019t tell without looking closer'],answer:2,reveal:'Most hard writing is just poor writing. But some is hard by design, and the two call for different responses. The skill is telling which before you decide.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Nietzsche held that a deep thinker needs a mask, and fears being understood more than being misunderstood. His broken-up style is a deliberate filter against the casual reader.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How the filter works',text:'The book is built from numbered fragments, short and overstated. You cannot extract the argument by reading the first line of each paragraph, because no argument is laid out to extract. There are positions, and the reader has to assemble them. Whoever will not go slowly gets nothing usable.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'WHAT GETS THROUGH',caption:'Most of what goes into the sieve stays caught on top; only the fine sand reaches the bowl. The fragments do that to readers. Only the patient ones get through.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'FAILURE OR FILTER',left:{title:'Hard by failure',items:['The writer is confused','Slowing down does not help','Contradictions lead nowhere']},right:{title:'Hard by design',items:['The writer is selecting readers','Slowing down pays','Pieces fit once assembled']}},{kind:'explain',title:'It backfired',text:'The style meant to obstruct casual reading made him one of the most quoted philosophers there is. His lines now circulate stripped of exactly the context the form was meant to enforce. His fear of being understood by the wrong people was well placed, and poorly defended against.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time something is written obscurely, ask whether the obscurity is failure or filter before deciding what to do about it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A famous Nietzsche line appears on a gym poster about pushing through pain. What does this idea predict about it?',options:['It has probably lost the context the book\u2019s form was meant to protect','It is likely a fair summary, since he chose short forms himself','It shows his filter worked, since only keen readers quote him','It must be invented, since he never wrote anything short'],answer:0,why:'The fragments were meant to force slow reading. Lifted out, they travel easily and arrive without the argument.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'A COMMON COMPLAINT',belief:'Nietzsche wrote in fragments because he could not sustain an argument.',reality:'The fragmentation is a strategy. He says outright that the highest insights should sound like folly to readers not made for them. The style is a filter, and he knew it.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'SECTIONS 40 AND 290',text:'Two passages frame it. In one, he says that deep things, and deep people, seek out disguises. In the other, near the end, he says a deep thinker fears being understood more than being misunderstood: misunderstanding hurts his vanity, but being understood hurts his sympathy for the reader.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Nietzsche writes to be hard to take in quickly. Short, overstated, awkward fragments make the reader assemble the argument, and repel anyone unwilling to. Difficulty is used as a filter.'},{kind:'term',word:'Esoteric writing',meaning:'Writing with two audiences: a surface for any reader and a deeper sense kept for those who read slowly and closely. Nietzsche discusses the old split between exoteric and esoteric teaching in this book.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why fragments filter',text:'You cannot skim an aphoristic book for its argument, because none is laid out. There are positions, often at odds on the surface, which only cohere when the reader does the work. The reader who will not go slowly gets nothing usable, as intended.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The sieve holds back most of what is poured in and passes only the finest grains. Nietzsche built his fragments to sort readers that way, by how slowly they would go.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The filter failed comprehensively. The style meant to obstruct casual reading made him the most quotable of philosophers, and his lines circulate stripped of the context the form was built to enforce. And the defence of deliberate difficulty is easily abused: it can shield muddled writing from criticism. Obscurity has to earn the benefit of the doubt.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of a writer you find hard. What evidence would tell you whether the difficulty is failure or filter?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which is a *misuse* of the idea that difficulty can be a filter?',options:['Excusing your own unclear report as deliberately demanding','Rereading a dense passage slowly before judging it','Noticing that a famous line reads differently in context','Asking whether a hard text rewards a second reading'],answer:0,why:'Deliberate difficulty has to be shown, not claimed. Used as a shield for muddle, it is failure dressed up as a filter.'}]}},'beyond-good-and-evil_9':{pictureCaption:'A jar with a grand label and nothing inside. A name can fill the glass and still leave it empty.',bridge:'Idea 8 asked whether difficulty hides an argument. This one finds explanations that sound complete and contain nothing.',basics:{title:'An answer that is only a name',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'WHY DOES IT WORK?',prompt:'Why does a sleeping pill make you sleep? Someone says: because it has a sleepy power. Did they explain it?',options:['Yes, that explains it','No, they just said it again'],answer:1,reveal:'A sleepy power just means it makes you sleep. They said the same thing twice, with new words. You still don\u2019t know how it works.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Some answers sound like they explain a thing, but they only give it a name. A name is not a reason. Ask what the answer tells you that you did not know.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'AN OLD JOKE',text:'Long ago, a French writer called Moli\xe8re wrote a funny play. In it, a man training to be a doctor is asked why a drug makes people sleep. He says it has a power to make them sleep. Friedrich Nietzsche, a German thinker, used this joke in his 1886 book.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'This jar has a big, fancy label. But look inside. It is empty. Some answers are like that: all label.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'AT SCHOOL',text:'Why is Jack good at maths? *Because he has a maths brain.* But what is a maths brain? Just being good at maths. The answer went round in a circle.'},{kind:'try',text:'When someone gives you a reason today, ask: what does this tell me that I didn\u2019t know? If nothing, it was just a name.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Why do cats chase mice? Which answer just gives it a name?',options:['Because cats have a mouse-chasing nature','Because cats hunt small things that move fast','Because wild cats needed mice to eat'],answer:0,why:'A mouse-chasing nature only means cats chase mice. The other two at least tell you something new.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'IN THE REVIEW MEETING',text:'A project failed, and the review concludes the team lacked resilience. Everyone nods. But how do you know they lacked resilience? Because the project failed. The explanation has named the failure again, in a different register, and closed the question.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Beware explanations that only rename what they explain. They have the grammar of an answer and none of the content: a noun standing in for a mechanism.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'THE SLEEPING DRUG',text:'Nietzsche borrows a joke from Moli\xe8re\u2019s comedy *The Imaginary Invalid*. A medical candidate is asked why opium puts people to sleep, and answers that it has a sleep-making power. The examiners applaud. Nietzsche aims the joke at Kant, who had explained a kind of knowledge by a faculty for it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A jar with a grand label filling the glass, and nothing inside. The answer is all label.'},{kind:'steps',kicker:'THE TEST',title:'Explanation, or name?',steps:['Write the explanation as a single noun or phrase.','Ask what it predicts that the thing itself did not already show.','Ask how you would know it was present.','If only by the effect it explains, you have been given a name.']},{kind:'try',text:'When you are given an explanation this week, ask what it predicts. If the answer is nothing, you have been handed a name.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Sales fell this year. Which explanation actually has content?',options:['A rival cut its prices by a fifth in March','The business lost its sales momentum this year','The market simply lost its appetite for us','The brand lost the magic it used to have'],answer:0,why:'Only the rival\u2019s price cut names a cause you could check apart from the fall itself. The others describe the fall again in other words.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'SECTION 11',text:'Kant had asked how certain judgements, known independently of experience yet adding to our knowledge, are possible. His answer, as Nietzsche tells it, was: by means of a faculty. Nietzsche compares this to Moli\xe8re\u2019s medical candidate, for whom opium sends people to sleep because it has a sleep-making power.'},{kind:'term',word:'Virtus dormitiva',meaning:'Latin for sleep-making power: the mock-learned answer in Moli\xe8re\u2019s play to why opium causes sleep. It has become a standard name for an explanation that merely restates its effect.'},{kind:'idea',text:'An explanation that renames its object adds no information. The test is whether it predicts anything the phenomenon did not already show. If not, the vocabulary has grown and nothing has been learned.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The wider class',text:'The faculties of older psychology are the classic case: a will to explain willing, a memory to explain remembering. Modern instances are numerous and comfortable. A behaviour put down to culture, an outcome to mindset, a failure to a lack of resilience: each names the pattern again and closes the question.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A jar with an elaborate label and nothing inside. The label fills the glass, which is what a name can do to a question.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the closing is for',text:'Nietzsche\u2019s sharper point is that closing the question is what such explanations are for. They let inquiry stop without anything being answered, and they feel like knowledge while they do it.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Not every name is empty. A name for an unknown cause can be an honest placeholder that points inquiry somewhere: biologists named the unit of heredity long before they knew what it was made of. The fault is treating the placeholder as the answer. And a good redescription can organise evidence before anyone can explain it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TELL THEM APART',prompt:'Which of these is an honest placeholder rather than an empty explanation?',options:['Calling an unknown factor X while designing tests to find it','Saying a student failed because she lacked academic ability','Saying a city thrives because of its entrepreneurial spirit','Saying a drug works because it has therapeutic power'],answer:0,why:'A placeholder admits it is a name and drives a search. The others present a renaming as if it were the cause.'}]}}}},2960,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'carnegie-autobiography_1':{pictureCaption:'Everyone on the furnace floor is guessing, hands spread. One person holds a sheet of real figures, and only he knows where he stands.',basics:{title:'Know what each one costs you',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE BAKE SALE',text:'You and a friend sell cakes at the school fair. You charge 50p each. You sell them all, and it feels like a great day. But did you make any money? You never added up the flour, the eggs and the icing.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Know just what it costs you to make one of a thing. Then you know which price is safe, and which price loses you money.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'AMERICA, OVER 100 YEARS AGO',text:'Andrew Carnegie made steel, the strong metal used for railways and bridges. He kept asking one thing: what did one ton of his steel cost to make? His rivals could not say. They only found out at the end of the year if they had made money.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The others are guessing, hands up. One man holds the real numbers. He is the only one who knows if a price is safe.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it helped in hard times',text:'Some years, few people wanted steel, so prices had to drop. Carnegie knew how low he could go and still be fine. His rivals did not. If they matched his price, they might lose money on every ton.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one thing you make or sell, even a birthday card. Add up what one costs you. Was your guess right?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You sell bracelets for \xa31 each. The beads for one cost 80p. The string costs 30p. What is true?',options:['You lose 10p on each one','You make 20p on each one','You make \xa31 on each one'],answer:0,why:'One bracelet costs you \xa31.10 to make. If you never added it up, you would think you were doing fine.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TWO FIRMS, ONE BAD YEAR',prompt:'Demand collapses and prices have to fall. Two firms make the same product. Which is better placed to survive the price war?',options:['The bigger one','The one that knows its cost per unit','The one with the better product'],answer:1,reveal:'A price war turns on one question: how low can you go? A firm that knows its unit cost can answer. A firm that sees only a yearly total is guessing.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Carnegie could say what a ton of steel cost him to make, and his rivals could not. That dull fact let him price with confidence, find the operations losing money, and judge every improvement against a real figure.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where he learned it',text:'Before steel, Carnegie worked on the Pennsylvania Railroad, which had built detailed cost systems because it had to. He carried the habit into steel, where firms knew only their results at the end of the year. He records asking again and again what a process cost, and finding that competitors could not answer.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'A yearly total',items:['Profit or loss, once a year','No idea which step loses money','A price cut is a gamble']},right:{title:'A cost per ton',items:['A figure for each process','The losing step is visible','You know your floor price']},note:'Both might report the same profit. Only one knows where it came from.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Every hand on the floor is spread in a guess. The one sheet of real figures belongs to the man who knows his floor price.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why rivals did not copy it',text:'A new machine can be seen, bought and copied. A cost system works inside the books, out of sight. Rivals cannot see what you know, so they feel no pressure to catch up. That is why an advantage in measurement often outlasts an advantage in technology.'},{kind:'try',text:'Take one thing you produce. Write down your guess at its unit cost, then work it out from the real inputs. Note the gap.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A caf\xe9 owner knows the shop made a small profit last year. Which step would give her Carnegie\u2019s advantage?',options:['Work out what each item on the menu costs to make','Raise every price a little to widen the margin','Compare her yearly profit with the caf\xe9 next door','Buy a better coffee machine than her rivals have'],answer:0,why:'A yearly total hides which items earn and which lose. A cost per item shows where she can cut prices safely and what to drop.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'THE PENNSYLVANIA RAILROAD',text:'Carnegie\u2019s training was not in steel. As a young man he worked for the Pennsylvania Railroad, one of the largest businesses in America, which had developed detailed cost systems out of necessity. When he moved into iron and then steel, he found an industry run on year-end totals.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Precise unit costs, kept process by process, gave Carnegie a decisive and unglamorous edge. He priced, cut and invested on figures while his rivals worked on estimates.'},{kind:'term',word:'Unit cost',meaning:'What it costs to make one unit of output, such as a ton of steel, counting materials, labour and fuel. Unlike a total profit, it cannot hide a losing process inside a winning year.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHAT THE NUMBER BOUGHT',nodes:['Know the cost of each ton','See which process loses money','Fix it, and measure the fix','Cut prices where rivals cannot follow'],note:'Each step depends on the one before. Without the first, the rest is guesswork.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Every figure on the floor is guessing, hands spread. One sheet holds exact numbers. In a downturn, that sheet decides who can afford to keep selling.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The durable part',text:'A technical lead is visible: rivals see the new furnace and order one. A measurement lead sits in the books and in the habit of asking. Competitors who do not measure cannot see what they are missing, so it is seldom copied. That claim reaches well beyond steel.'},{kind:'limits',text:'A cost sheet records what is easy to count. The low costs in Carnegie\u2019s mills rested partly on twelve-hour days and on wage cuts, which the sheet records simply as savings. Measurement shows where cost lives, not whether lowering it is right. And a precise figure can still mislead if shared costs are divided up badly.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What do you produce whose unit cost you have only ever estimated? How confident are you in that estimate?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'FIND THE REAL ADVANTAGE',prompt:'Two rival firms have identical machines. Which difference is most like Carnegie\u2019s edge, and least likely to be copied?',options:['One tracks the cost of each process weekly; the other reads an annual total','One has a larger factory and can buy its materials in bulk','One has a famous brand that its customers trust','One pays its sales team better commissions'],answer:0,why:'Size, brand and pay are visible and can be matched. A habit of measurement is hidden in the books, so rivals rarely know to imitate it.'}]}},'carnegie-autobiography_2':{pictureCaption:'Every egg there is, in one basket, with both hands round it and all the attention on it. Without the watching, it is only risk.',bridge:'Knowing your costs, as in idea 1, takes close attention. This idea is about where to point that attention.',basics:{title:'One basket, watched closely',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'A SAYING YOU MAY KNOW',belief:'Never put all your eggs in one basket. Always spread things out.',reality:'Andrew Carnegie, a rich steel maker from long ago, said the opposite. Put all your eggs in one basket, he said, and then watch that basket. Both can be right. It depends on what you are doing.'},{kind:'idea',text:'If you are running something yourself, pick one thing and watch it closely. Your attention is what makes it work, and attention does not split well.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'All the eggs are in one basket. Two hands hold it, and two eyes watch it. Nothing gets dropped while someone is watching.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'AT THE SCHOOL FAIR',text:'You run a stall at the fair. You could run three stalls at once. But you can only stand at one. The other two get messy, or run out of change. One stall you watch does better than three you keep leaving.'},{kind:'explain',title:'So when should you spread out?',text:'Say you are just saving your pocket money. You are not running anything, and you can\u2019t check on it every day. Then it is wiser to keep it in more than one place. If one goes wrong, you don\u2019t lose it all.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'You run it',items:['One basket','Watch it closely']},right:{title:'You just keep it',items:['More than one basket','Spread the risk']},note:'The trick is to know which one you are doing.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend wants to be great at the drums. She also signs up for guitar, piano and violin. What would Carnegie say?',options:['Pick the drums and give them your full attention','Good idea, four is safer than one','Drop all four and wait a year'],answer:0,why:'She is doing the work herself, so her attention is what counts. Split four ways, it helps none of them much.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO DIFFERENT JOBS',left:{title:'The investor',items:['Puts money in, does not run it','Cannot watch every holding','Spreads to survive surprises']},right:{title:'The operator',items:['Runs the business day to day','Can watch one thing closely','Concentrates to make it succeed']},note:'Carnegie\u2019s advice was written for the right-hand column. Read from the left, it becomes dangerous.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Put all your eggs in one basket, and watch that basket. If you are building something yourself, attention is the scarce input, and spreading your stake spreads the attention that would make any one venture succeed.'},{kind:'explain',title:'His argument',text:'The usual counsel is to spread your bets. Carnegie turns it round. Diversification, in his view, is a strategy for people who cannot watch. He addresses the man building a business, and the watching is not a detail. It is the condition the whole advice rests on.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'All the eggs there are, held by two hands with the attention fixed on them. The basket is safe only because it is watched.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'SPREADING THE WRONG THING',text:'A baker with a growing business opens a caf\xe9 and a catering arm to spread her risk. Each now gets a third of her week. The bakery\u2019s costs drift, and a supplier problem goes unnoticed for a month. Her risk did not spread. Her attention did.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where it reverses',text:'Your savings are a different case. You are not running those companies and cannot watch them. Concentrating there buys no extra attention, only extra exposure. Advice that is sound for an operator is poor advice for someone protecting capital.'},{kind:'try',text:'List where your working attention goes this week. Is anything on the list there to spread risk, when really it is spreading you?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Who is best placed to follow Carnegie\u2019s advice?',options:['A woman running her own engineering firm full time','A retired teacher investing her savings for income','An employee holding shares in firms he never visits','Someone placing small bets across many start-ups'],answer:0,why:'Only she can do the watching the advice depends on. For the others, concentrating adds risk without adding attention.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE CONTRARIAN LINE',belief:'Carnegie thought diversification was simply a mistake.',reality:'His target is narrower. He treats diversification as a strategy for people who cannot watch, and aims his advice at the man building a business. Concentrate, then watch. The second half is the condition, not an afterthought.'},{kind:'idea',text:'For an operator, attention is scarcer than capital. Concentrating your stake concentrates your attention, which is what makes a venture succeed. The advice holds only for as long as the watching does.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The mechanism',text:'An operator\u2019s returns depend on noticing things: costs drifting, a better process, a weak manager. Each extra venture takes a slice of that noticing. Carnegie\u2019s own edges, from cost figures to scrapping old plant, came from watching one industry very closely.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One basket, all the eggs, two hands and undivided attention. The picture is safe because of the watcher, not the basket.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'WHICH ARE YOU, HERE?',low:'Passive investor: cannot watch, should spread',high:'Full-time operator: can watch, may concentrate',note:'Most people sit at different points for different things: an operator in their work, an investor with their savings.'},{kind:'term',word:'Survivorship bias',meaning:'Drawing conclusions only from the cases that survived to be seen. Those whom concentration ruined did not write memoirs, so this advice reaches us already filtered.'},{kind:'limits',text:'As a way to preserve capital, concentration is poor: one bad turn and everything goes, however closely you watch. Carnegie does not separate building a fortune from keeping one, and his book cannot show how many followed this advice and failed. Use it for where your effort goes, not automatically for where your savings sit.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Where are you concentrated without watching, or watching something you are barely invested in?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which is a misuse of the advice?',options:['Putting all your retirement savings in one share you cannot follow','A founder turning down side ventures to focus on her main firm','An engineer spending years mastering one industry\u2019s processes','A shop owner closing a second branch she could not supervise'],answer:0,why:'The advice depends on watching. Concentration without the ability to watch keeps all the risk and loses the reason for taking it.'}]}},'carnegie-autobiography_3':{pictureCaption:'A boy at the telegraph key, head tilted to listen. The messages of the people who decided things passed under this hand.',bridge:'Before the costs and the steel there was a first job. This idea goes back to where Carnegie started.',basics:{title:'Where you stand matters',beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'PITTSBURGH, AROUND 1850',text:'Andrew Carnegie came to America from Scotland as a poor boy. At about fourteen he got a job taking telegrams round the city. A telegram was a message sent down a wire as clicks, then written out and carried by hand.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the job gave him',text:'He took messages to the most important people in town, so he got to know who they were. He learned to read the clicks by ear, which few could do. People noticed. Later a railway boss called Thomas Scott hired him.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A boy at the telegraph key, listening hard. The messages of busy, important people passed right under his hand.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Where you stand can matter as much as how hard you work. Carnegie worked hard. But his job also put him right next to the people who made the big choices.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'AT THE SCHOOL PLAY',text:'You could paint the set at the back of the hall. Or you could run notes for the teacher in charge. Painting is fun. But the runner hears every plan, meets everyone, and gets asked to help again next time.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'He did well just because he worked so hard.',reality:'He did work hard. But he was also lucky to be in that office at that time, and his own story shows how much the job mattered. Both parts were real.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'At lunch you can help in the school office, or in the library, where it is quiet and you get more free time. What does this idea say to think about?',options:['Which one puts you near the people who decide things','Which one gives you the most free time','Which one your best friend picks'],answer:0,why:'Free time is nice. But being near where choices get made can lead to much more later.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TWO OFFERS',prompt:'One job pays 15% more. The other pays less but sits beside the people who make the firm\u2019s decisions. Which is likely to matter more in ten years?',options:['The higher salary','The seat near the decisions','Neither; only your skills matter'],answer:1,reveal:'Not always, but often. Salary compounds slowly. A seat near decisions brings information, people who know your work, and chances that never appear in a job description.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A telegraph job put a boy beside the people who decided things, and Carnegie is honest that the position did more for him than any decision he made afterwards. When choosing a role, weigh nearness to decisions against the pay.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'THE TELEGRAPH OFFICE',text:'As a messenger and then an operator in Pittsburgh, Carnegie learned to take messages by ear, and learned the names and business of the city\u2019s leading men. Thomas Scott of the Pennsylvania Railroad noticed him and made him his own telegrapher. The railway, the investments and eventually steel came from there.'},{kind:'flow',nodes:['Messenger in the right office','A rare skill: reading the wire by ear','Noticed by people who decide','A place in their network'],note:'The skill was his. The office, and the timing, were partly luck.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The boy listens rather than reading the tape. Every message between the people deciding things passes under his hand.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why nearness pays',text:'Near decisions you see how they are really made, who makes them and what they need. Those people see you too. Ability that no one important sees is easily overlooked. Ability beside the decision-makers gets used, and then trusted.'},{kind:'try',text:'How many steps are you from where the main decisions in your organisation are made? Name one move that would bring you a step closer.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A graduate must choose between a well-paid analyst job in a distant back office and a lower-paid role assisting the chief executive. By this idea, what should weigh most?',options:['How close each role sits to where decisions are made','Which starting salary will compound into more money','Which job title will look better on her CV','Which role makes the fewest demands on her time'],answer:0,why:'Pay matters, but the idea is that position shapes what comes next. The assistant sees decisions being made.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'READ BESIDE SELF-HELP',text:'Samuel Smiles\u2019s Self-Help, a Victorian bestseller, taught that success comes from character and persistence, and tended to leave the luck out of its stories. Carnegie\u2019s account of his start is more bracing. The persistence is there. So is the plain accident of being in that office at that moment.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A messenger boy learned to read the wire by ear, was noticed, and entered a network of people who decided things. By his own account, the position counted for more than any decision he made afterwards.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'PITTSBURGH, THE 1850S',picture:!0,text:'Carnegie began by carrying telegrams to the city\u2019s businesses. He learned to take messages by ear, became an operator, and was hired by Thomas Scott of the Pennsylvania Railroad as his personal telegrapher. Scott\u2019s backing brought him into railway management and his first investments.'},{kind:'equation',parts:['Real skill','The right room','The right moment'],result:'A start like Carnegie\u2019s',note:'A Smiles-style story reports only the first term. Carnegie\u2019s account keeps all three.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why position compounds',text:'Information moves along a network. A boy handling every message learns who is doing what before almost anyone. Being seen by the people deciding turns ability into opportunity, and each opportunity brings more nearness. The early position keeps paying long after the job itself is gone.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Position is not destiny. Many boys carried telegrams; skill and drive were also needed, and Carnegie\u2019s later decisions were far from trivial. Hindsight flatters too: a post looks decisive because we know the ending. And taken too far, the lesson becomes courting the powerful instead of becoming good at anything.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which early position did more for you than the decisions you made afterwards? Would you have said so at the time?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE SELF-MADE OBJECTION',prompt:'Someone says Carnegie\u2019s story proves anyone can rise through hard work alone. What is the best reply from this idea?',options:['His own account credits position and timing alongside the effort','His story shows that hard work did not really matter at all','Telegraph jobs were unusually well paid in his day','He admitted he had little talent for telegraphy'],answer:0,why:'The account keeps both parts: real persistence, and the luck of being in that office then. Dropping either misreads it.'}]}},'carnegie-autobiography_4':{pictureCaption:'A slump: the cranes are still and the yards are shut. One gate stands open and material is going in, because that yard kept its cash.',bridge:'Idea 1\u2019s cost figures told Carnegie what price he could survive. This idea is about what he did with that in a slump.',basics:{title:'Buy when it is cheap, if you saved',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE THE JANUARY SALES',text:'After the holidays, shops cut their prices. The coat that cost \xa360 in December costs \xa330 in January. But only someone who still has money can buy it. Everyone else spent theirs already.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When times are hard, things get cheap, and the one who saved can buy. First you need the money. Then you need the nerve to spend it.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'AMERICA, THE 1870S',text:'Andrew Carnegie made steel. In the 1870s, business got very bad. Most firms stopped building. Carnegie built a huge new steel works. Building was cheap, because no one else was doing it. When good times came back, he was bigger than ever.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Hard times at the docks. The cranes are still. One yard\u2019s gates are open, and it is busy. That yard saved its money.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why most people don\u2019t',text:'In hard times everyone is scared. Spending feels wrong. So even people with money keep it. Carnegie spent his. That took nerve: being brave when others are scared.'},{kind:'steps',title:'His two rules, in order',steps:['In good times, save. Don\u2019t borrow too much.','In hard times, use what you saved to grow.'],note:'Rule one has to come first. Without savings, nerve is no help.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Footballs are half price in the summer sale. Who can get a bargain?',options:['Sam, who saved some pocket money for it','Jo, who really wants one but spent it all','Ali, who plans to save up by winter'],answer:0,why:'A sale only helps someone who has the money when it happens. Jo wants one, but wanting is not enough.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'TWO FIRMS IN A RECESSION',text:'Two firms enter a recession. One has borrowed heavily and paid out its profits. The other has little debt and cash in the bank. Building and equipment costs fall sharply. The first spends the slump cutting back. The second buys a rival\u2019s plant for a fraction of what it cost.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Expand when everyone else contracts. It takes two things almost nobody has at once: cash at the bottom of the cycle, and the nerve to spend it. Get them in that order.'},{kind:'timeline',kicker:'HIS PATTERN',points:[{when:'1873',what:'A financial panic starts a long depression'},{when:'1875',what:'Carnegie\u2019s new Edgar Thomson steel works opens, built through the slump'},{when:'1893',what:'Another panic. Carnegie builds again while rivals retrench'}],note:'Each time he came out with capacity bought cheaply, and a larger share of a recovering market.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where the cash came from',text:'Carnegie kept debt low and ploughed profits back into the business instead of paying them out. In good years that looked conservative, even timid. In a slump it was what made aggression possible. The cash is the part usually left out of the story.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Liquidity',items:['Cash at the bottom of the cycle','Built in the good years','Decides whether you can act']},right:{title:'Nerve',items:['The will to spend it','Tested when every signal says save','Decides whether you will act']},note:'Nerve without cash is bravado. Cash without nerve is a missed chance.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Every other yard is shut and every crane still. One gate is open and the work goes in. Its owner prepared for this in the good years.'},{kind:'try',text:'If a downturn arrived next month, what could you actually afford to buy or build? Write that figure down before you ask whether you would dare.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'In a slump, a small firm sees a rival\u2019s equipment for sale cheaply. What should it settle first?',options:['Whether it has the cash to buy without endangering itself','Whether its managers feel confident about the recovery','Whether other firms are also buying during the slump','Whether the equipment would impress its customers'],answer:0,why:'Liquidity first, nerve second. Confidence without cash can turn a bargain into a collapse.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'AGAINST THE CYCLE',text:'Carnegie\u2019s rule was to expand hardest in downturns, when construction and equipment were cheapest and competitors were retrenching. He built through the depressions of the 1870s and 1890s, acquiring capacity at a fraction of its cost in good years, and came out of each with a larger share of the market.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Counter-cyclical expansion needs liquidity and nerve, in that order. The nerve gets the credit in the telling. The liquidity, built quietly in good years, is what made it possible.'},{kind:'term',word:'Counter-cyclical',meaning:'Moving against the business cycle: building when the economy shrinks and holding back when it booms. Most firms do the opposite, because they feel rich at the top and poor at the bottom.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW GOOD YEARS FUND BAD ONES',nodes:['Good years: low debt, profits reinvested','Slump: the price of plant collapses','Cash buys capacity cheaply','Recovery: larger share, lower costs'],loop:!0,note:'The bigger profits of the recovery are reinvested, ready for the next slump.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Cranes idle, yards shuttered, one gate open. The open gate was paid for years earlier, in profits that were kept rather than paid out.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why so few do it',text:'The logic is simple and widely understood. What is rare is having both halves at once. Firms that borrowed to grow in the boom have no cash at the bottom. Firms that do have cash are run by people reading the same frightening signals as everyone else.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Survivors tell this story. Some who expanded into a slump ran out of cash before the recovery, or bought capacity for a market that never came back. A downturn is known to be temporary only afterwards. The rule also assumes the industry itself is sound, which in a declining trade it is not.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'If prices in your field halved tomorrow, would you be a buyer or a seller? When was that decided?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE ORDER MATTERS',prompt:'A founder says that in the next downturn the company will be bold and expand. What is the most useful question to ask her?',options:['What cash will you hold at the bottom, and how are you building it now?','How will you keep your nerve when the news turns frightening?','Which of your competitors do you expect to fail first?','How quickly do you expect the recovery to arrive?'],answer:0,why:'Boldness is the second requirement. Without cash built in advance, the plan cannot be carried out however brave it is.'}]}},'carnegie-autobiography_5':{pictureCaption:'A row of frames, and one taken down. The nail and the unfaded patch show where it hung. What is missing is part of the story.',bridge:'The ideas so far take Carnegie\u2019s method at his word. This one asks how far to trust the book itself.',basics:{title:'Notice what a story leaves out',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'AFTER THE ARGUMENT',text:'Your little brother tells your mum about a fight. He says you took his game. He says you shouted. All true. He leaves out that he broke your model first.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When people tell their own story, they choose what goes in. Notice what is left out, and how much. The gaps can tell you as much as the words.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'HOMESTEAD, 1892',text:'Andrew Carnegie, one of the richest men in America, wrote the story of his life. In 1892, at his steel works in a town called Homestead, the workers and the bosses fell out. Armed guards hired by the firm fought the workers. People died.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What his book does',text:'His book says only a little about this. It puts the blame on the bosses who were there, and Carnegie was far away in Scotland. The workers who stood up to the firm hardly get a word. Their long, hard days in the steel works get little space either.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'THE MISSING FRAME',caption:'A row of frames, and one taken down. Nothing on the wall is false. But the nail and the bright patch show that something hung there.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time a friend tells you about a fight, ask yourself: whose side is missing? What would they say?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A football star writes a book. It has ten pages on his best goal and one line on the year he was banned. What is the smart way to read it?',options:['Enjoy it, but notice how little space the ban gets','Trust all of it, since he was there','Throw it away, since it must all be lies'],answer:0,why:'He may be honest about the goals. The tiny space for the ban is a clue that part of the story is being kept short.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'An autobiography is the most reliable account of a life, because the author was there.',reality:'The author was there, and also has the most at stake. An autobiography is a case being made. Where its space goes, and where it does not, is part of what it tells you.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Read an account of a life as a case being made. Notice the size of the gaps and what sits in them. They are evidence too.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'HOMESTEAD, 1892',text:'At Carnegie\u2019s Homestead works a dispute became a lockout. Armed guards from the Pinkerton detective agency, hired by the company, fought the workers, and men were killed. The autobiography handles this briefly and at a distance. Responsibility rests with the officials and partners on the spot, and Carnegie himself was away in Scotland.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Told at length',items:['How costs were measured','How partners were chosen','How expansion was timed']},right:{title:'Told briefly, or not at all',items:['The lockout and the deaths','The wage cuts before it','Twelve-hour days in the mills']},note:'The left column is candid and useful. The right is where a reputation is being managed.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'THE BARE NAIL',caption:'A row of frames down a hallway, and one bare nail. The wallpaper behind it never faded, so the patch shows exactly where something hung. A gap in a life story can be read the same way.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Two kinds of trust',text:'The passages on method are detailed, and he had little reason to bend them. The passages on labour were written late in life by a man managing a legacy. Reading both with the same trust is the mistake the book invites.'},{kind:'try',text:'Take a memoir, a company history or a CV you know well. Find an eventful stretch that gets a single line. Ask what that line is doing.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A former chief executive\u2019s memoir gives forty pages to a merger and one paragraph to the factory closures that followed. How should you read it?',options:['Use the merger detail, and treat the closure account as one side','Distrust the whole book, since one part is clearly slanted','Accept both parts equally, since the author was in charge','Ignore the closures, since the memoir is about his career'],answer:0,why:'Candour in one area and careful management in another can sit in the same book. The gap is the clue to which is which.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'HOMESTEAD, PENNSYLVANIA, 1892',text:'A dispute at the Homestead steel works became a lockout. Henry Clay Frick, running the company, brought in armed Pinkerton guards. The battle that followed left men dead on both sides, and the state militia was sent in. Carnegie was in Scotland. No episode did more damage to his name.'},{kind:'idea',text:'An autobiography is a case being made: candid where candour costs little, brief where the record is damaging. Noticing the size of the gaps, and what sits in them, is part of reading it honestly.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the book does with it',text:'Homestead is handled briefly and at a distance, with responsibility placed on the officials and partners on the spot, and Carnegie absent abroad. The wage reductions that preceded it are not examined. Conditions in the mills, including the twelve-hour day behind the cost figures he reports with pride, get little attention.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the method passages still hold',text:'Gaps do not make everything else false. His accounts of costing, timing and partnership are detailed and cost him little to tell. The task is calibration: a different level of trust for different passages, not one verdict on the whole book.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Reading an account of oneself',steps:['Map where the pages go: what gets a chapter, what gets a line.','Note what you know happened that is missing.','Ask what the author had at stake in each part.','Set your trust passage by passage, not for the whole book.']},{kind:'picture',kicker:'EVIDENCE, NOT PROOF',caption:'A frame has come down, and the nail and the unfaded patch record that it hung there. They do not record what it showed. Finding that out takes other sources.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Reading for gaps can become its own bias. Brevity is not always evasion: an author may say little from regret, or from ignorance of events he did not see. Absence is evidence, not proof, and it needs outside sources before it becomes a conclusion. The aim is a fairer reading, not a cynical one.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of the account you give of your own working life. What gets a paragraph, what gets a line, and why?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'CALIBRATE',prompt:'A historian uses the autobiography for two chapters, one on Carnegie\u2019s cost methods and one on labour at Homestead. What is the best approach?',options:['Lean on it for methods, and test the labour passages against other sources','Reject it for both, since a self-serving book cannot be trusted','Use it for both, since he had first-hand knowledge of each','Use it only for labour, since his methods are already well known'],answer:0,why:'The book is candid where candour costs little and managed where it costs much. Trust should follow that line.'}]}},'carnegie-autobiography_6':{pictureCaption:'The first walker went straight out and found the thin ice. The second learned from the hole and took a wide arc round it.',bridge:'Like buying in a downturn in idea 4, this is a rule about timing: when to move, and why not first.',basics:{title:'Let someone else go first',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A NEW GAME COMES OUT',prompt:'A brand new video game comes out. It costs a lot, and people say it keeps crashing. What is the smart move?',options:['Buy it on the first day','Wait for the fixed version, then play lots','Never buy new games at all'],answer:1,reveal:'The first players pay the most and find all the bugs. Wait, and you get a game that works, often for less.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Being first costs a lot, because the first one finds all the problems. Let others go first. Learn from what goes wrong. Then go big once it works.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The first walker went straight out and found the thin ice. The second walker saw the hole and went round it.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'STEEL, LONG AGO',text:'Andrew Carnegie made steel in America. A new way to make steel, called the Bessemer process, was invented in England. Carnegie did not rush in. He waited until others showed it worked. Then he built with it, bigger and faster than anyone.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The catch',text:'Waiting only works if you can move fast once you know. Carnegie had the money and the people to build quickly. If you wait and then go slowly, you are not being clever. You are just late.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time something new comes out, ask: do I need to be first? Or can I wait and learn from the first ones?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your class tries a new game in the park. The first team keeps tripping on a hidden root. What would Carnegie do?',options:['Watch where they trip, then play it well','Rush in first so you win before anyone','Never try a new game in case you fall'],answer:0,why:'The first team found the root for you. You learn from their falls, then play better than they did.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'THE BESSEMER PROCESS',text:'The Bessemer process, a British invention, made steel far more cheaply than older methods. Carnegie did not back it early. He waited while others took the risk of making it work. Once it was proved, he adopted it at scale faster than anyone.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Pioneering does not pay. The first mover pays to discover what fails. The second buys the proven version with the failures removed, and the profit goes to whoever industrialises the thing.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'The inventor of a technology is the one who gets rich from it.',reality:'Often not. Inventors bear the cost of the failures. The large profits usually go to whoever later builds it at scale, cheaply and reliably. That describes most technologies since Carnegie\u2019s day.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One set of prints ends at the hole in the ice. The second follows a careful arc round it. The second walker paid nothing to learn where the ice was thin.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The half he does not state',text:'Following is only an advantage if you can outbuild the pioneer once the proof exists. Carnegie could commit enormous sums quickly. A firm that cannot move decisively is not a fast follower. It is simply late.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Funding the discovery',items:['Pays for the failures','Learns first','Rarely keeps the profit']},right:{title:'Buying the debugged version',items:['Pays for something proven','Learns from others\u2019 costs','Must build fast to profit']},note:'Both are strategies. Only one is usually priced honestly.'},{kind:'try',text:'Name one new tool your field is excited about. Are you funding its discovery or waiting for the debugged version? If you wait, could you move fast?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A firm decides to wait until a new manufacturing method is proven elsewhere. When is that sound?',options:['When it can adopt the method quickly and at scale once it is proven','Whenever the method is expensive, since waiting is always cheaper','When its competitors have also chosen to wait and see','Only if the firm itself invented the method first'],answer:0,why:'Waiting pays only if you can then outbuild the pioneer. Otherwise you have merely arrived late.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'A DELIBERATE RULE',text:'Among the book\u2019s most useful and most contrarian rules is that pioneering does not pay. Carnegie let others be first with new processes, watched what it cost them, and adopted at scale once the faults were known. He held to it consistently, and says so plainly.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The first mover bears the cost of finding out what fails; the second buys proven technology more cheaply. But following pays only if you can outbuild the pioneer once the proof exists.'},{kind:'term',word:'Fast follower',meaning:'A firm that lets others pioneer a technology, then adopts it quickly and at large scale once it works. The word that matters is *fast*: the strategy depends on speed after the wait.'},{kind:'flow',nodes:['Pioneer pays to find the faults','Proof arrives; risk falls','Follower builds at scale, quickly','Follower takes the market'],note:'Carnegie with Bessemer steel: wait for the demonstration, then outbuild everyone.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The pioneer\u2019s prints end at the hole. The follower\u2019s arc round it is wide and sure. It is only an advantage if the follower then keeps walking, fast.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The unstated condition',text:'Carnegie does not spell out the qualification: capacity. His version worked because he could deploy huge sums rapidly once a process was proven. For a firm without that capacity, waiting yields no advantage. It just concedes the field to someone who can build.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Some markets reward being first: where customers lock in, where a network grows more valuable with size, or where patents block followers. Waiting there can mean never getting in. The rule also needs pioneers to exist; if everyone follows, nothing is proved. And hindsight hides the followers who waited too long.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'In your field, who is paying right now to discover what fails? Could you follow quickly if they succeed?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'WHERE IT BREAKS',prompt:'In which case is Carnegie\u2019s rule weakest?',options:['A market where the first product to win users keeps them for good','A heavy industry where a new process is costly to perfect','A field where early versions of a tool are unreliable','A business with the capital to build fast once a method is proven'],answer:0,why:'Where being first captures customers for good, waiting means the follower may never get in, however fast it later moves.'}]}},'carnegie-autobiography_7':{pictureCaption:'A machine taken apart, every piece still good. Behind it the better one is already running. It was scrapped for being worse, not broken.',bridge:'Idea 6 was his caution about new processes. This is the other side: once one was proven, he moved without sentiment.',basics:{title:'Compare it with the best, not with nothing',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE WAY TO SCHOOL',prompt:'Your way to school takes 30 minutes. It works fine. A friend shows you a way that takes 15. Should you keep your old way?',options:['Yes, it still works','No, it costs you 15 minutes a day'],answer:1,reveal:'The old way is not broken. But next to the new way, it wastes time every single day. *It still works* was the wrong test.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Don\u2019t ask if a thing still works. Ask if it beats the best one you could have. Something that works can still be costing you.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'OVER 100 YEARS AGO',text:'Andrew Carnegie made steel. When a better machine came along, he ripped out his old ones, even when they still worked and still made money. Other steel makers thought he was mad.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'This machine was taken apart. Every piece is still fine. The better machine is already working behind it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why that was smart',text:'The new machines made steel for less. His rivals kept their old ones, because they still worked. So Carnegie could sell his steel for less than them, and they could not keep up.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one thing you do the same way every day. Don\u2019t just ask if your way works. Ask: is there a better way?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your team practises in a way that works OK. The coach shows a new drill that helps much more. Some players say: why change, ours works. What would Carnegie say?',options:['Compare the old way with the new drill, not with doing nothing','Keep the old way, because it still works','Do both, so nothing goes to waste'],answer:0,why:'*It works* compares the old way with nothing. The real choice is between the old way and the best way you have.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'IT STILL WORKS',text:'Your team\u2019s ordering system is ten years old, long since paid for, and it still does the job. A newer one would halve the time each order takes. Someone says what everyone is thinking: why throw away something that works?'},{kind:'idea',text:'Compare what you have against the best alternative, not against nothing. Against nothing, anything that still works looks like profit. Against the best option, it may be costing you every day.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'WHAT HIS RIVALS SAW',text:'Carnegie repeatedly tore out machinery that was profitable and not worn out, to install better equipment. His competitors regarded this as madness. They kept their working plant, and were undercut by the man who had written his off.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Against nothing',items:['Old machine earns \xa3100 a day','So keep it','Feels prudent']},right:{title:'Against the best',items:['A new one would earn \xa3300','So the old one costs \xa3200 a day','Feels wasteful, but is not']},note:'The figures are invented. The arithmetic is the point.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why so few do it',text:'The accounting is simple; the psychology is not. Scrapping something that works feels like destroying value. The loss is concrete and immediate, while the gain is only projected. Most people protect the thing they can see.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'An intact machine laid out piece by piece, and the new plant already standing behind it. A working asset, written off on purpose.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one tool, process or habit you keep because it still works. Name the best alternative, and estimate what the gap costs you each month.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A firm\u2019s delivery vans are old but reliable. Newer vans would cut fuel and repair bills sharply. Which comparison should decide it?',options:['The old vans\u2019 running costs against the new vans\u2019 running costs','The old vans\u2019 running costs against having no vans at all','What the old vans cost to buy against what they would sell for','What competitors are currently using for their own deliveries'],answer:0,why:'Against nothing, working vans always look worth keeping. The decision turns on the gap between them and the best option.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'THE ECONOMIST\u2019S WORD',word:'Opportunity cost',meaning:'The value of the best alternative you give up by choosing what you chose. A machine\u2019s opportunity cost is what the best available machine would have earned in its place.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A plant earning money can still be costing money against the alternative. Carnegie scrapped profitable, unworn machinery because the comparison that mattered was with the best available, not with nothing.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why rivals called it madness',text:'Their comparison was the natural one: the machine works, earns and is paid for. Writing it off feels like destroying value, and the loss is concrete while the gain is projected. Carnegie took the visible loss to avoid a larger one that nobody was measuring.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A sound machine dismantled on the yard floor, its replacement already running. The rivals would have kept it, and paid the difference every day.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What made it possible',text:'The cost accounting of idea 1. He could know what the old plant cost per ton and what the new one would, so the comparison was real rather than a guess. Without those figures, scrapping working machinery is a gamble that merely looks bold.'},{kind:'equation',parts:['A better process','Exact cost figures'],result:'Confident scrapping',note:'Take away the figures and the same act is recklessness.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The rule needs the new option to be truly better and the switch costed honestly: disruption, retraining, the risk that the new plant underperforms. Constant replacement can chase small gains at real cost. And the people tied to the old plant carry losses that this comparison leaves out.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What do you keep mainly because it already exists and still works? What is its real comparison?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE ERROR',prompt:'A manager argues against replacing a working machine: we paid a fortune for it, so we must get our money\u2019s worth. What is wrong with this?',options:['The price already paid is gone either way and should not decide it','The machine may break soon, so it is not worth keeping anyway','Working machines should always be replaced as soon as possible','The manager has ignored what competitors are doing with theirs'],answer:0,why:'What was paid cannot be recovered. The only live comparison is between the old machine\u2019s future and the best alternative\u2019s.'}]}},'carnegie-autobiography_8':{pictureCaption:'Late at night, a manager watches the works, hand on the books and a key beneath it. A share made the place partly his.',bridge:'Idea 2 said attention is the scarce input. This is how Carnegie got other people to give him theirs.',basics:{title:'Give people a real share',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE LEMONADE STAND',text:'You run a lemonade stand, and your friend helps. You could pay her \xa32 for the day. Or you could give her half of whatever you make.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'Which deal makes her care more about not wasting lemons?',options:['\xa32 for the day','Half of what you make'],answer:1,reveal:'With \xa32, wasted lemons are your problem. With half, every wasted lemon costs her too. So she keeps an eye on them.'},{kind:'idea',text:'If you want someone to care as if it were theirs, give them a real share. Then their good and bad choices cost or earn them money too.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'HOW CARNEGIE DID IT',picture:!0,text:'Andrew Carnegie owned big steel works. He did not just pay his best managers a wage. He gave them a part of the business. Some started with no money at all and ended up rich.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The catch',text:'The deal had rules, and Carnegie wrote them. If a partner left, the firm could buy back his share at a low price. So the partners shared the work and the worry. Carnegie kept the control.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'A wage',items:['Same pay whatever happens','Waste is the boss\u2019s problem']},right:{title:'A share',items:['Pay grows if the business grows','Waste costs you too']}},{kind:'question',prompt:'You sell comics with your cousin. She keeps forgetting to collect the money. Which deal would most likely change that?',options:['She gets half of every sale','You pay her \xa31 a day, whatever happens','You remind her every morning'],answer:0,why:'With half of each sale, every payment she forgets costs her too. That is what makes her care.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THREE WAYS TO PAY A MANAGER',prompt:'You need a manager to cut costs at a plant you own. Which pay deal produces the most careful cost-watching?',options:['A high fixed salary','A salary plus a bonus for hitting targets','A share of the business itself'],answer:2,reveal:'A salary pays the same whatever happens. A bonus rewards the target, not the business. A share means every wasted pound is partly the manager\u2019s own.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Carnegie paid his best managers in partnership interests rather than large salaries. They watched costs like owners because they were owners, and they did not leave for rivals. It cost him almost no cash.'},{kind:'explain',title:'His reasoning',picture:!0,text:'It was about attention and retention. A partner watches costs in a way an employee does not, because the waste is partly his. And a partner whose wealth is tied up in the firm does not walk to a competitor. Carnegie presents the system as central to his success.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'CHARLES SCHWAB',text:'Charles Schwab started as a labourer at one of Carnegie\u2019s works and rose to run the company. Through the partnership he became enormously wealthy. A young man of no fortune could end up rich, and the men running the mills carried the consequences of their own decisions.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Salary',items:['Paid the same whatever happens','Waste is the owner\u2019s problem','Easy to leave for a rival']},right:{title:'Partnership share',items:['Gains and losses are shared','Waste costs the partner too','Leaving means leaving wealth behind']},note:'Same manager. A different stake in the result.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Read the terms',text:'The structure was severe. The firm could buy a partner\u2019s interest back at book value, which kept control concentrated and denied departing partners the rise in its worth. The alignment was real, and it ran in one direction. The terms were always Carnegie\u2019s.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of someone you want to decide like an owner. List what part of the outcome they actually carry. If the answer is none, expect an employee\u2019s decisions.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A sales manager on a fixed salary keeps overspending on travel. Which change best applies Carnegie\u2019s idea?',options:['Give her a stake in the profits her spending affects','Remind her of the travel budget every month','Raise her salary so that she feels more valued','Cap her travel spending with a strict new rule'],answer:0,why:'A rule or a reminder treats the symptom. A stake makes the waste partly hers, which is what turned Carnegie\u2019s managers into cost-watchers.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'ONE CLAUSE',prompt:'Carnegie\u2019s firm could buy out a departing partner at book value. In a fast-growing firm, who gains most from that clause?',options:['The partner who leaves','The partner who controls the firm','The firm\u2019s customers'],answer:1,reveal:'Book value tracks what assets cost, less wear, not what the business would fetch. In a fast-growing firm the gap is large, and it stays with whoever controls the buy-back.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Partnership interests made Carnegie\u2019s managers carry the consequences of their decisions, buying attention and loyalty at almost no cash cost. The terms were written so that the alignment ran mostly one way.'},{kind:'term',word:'Book value',meaning:'What a business\u2019s assets are worth on its own books: roughly what they cost, less wear. In a growing, profitable firm it can sit far below what a buyer would pay.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What it bought',text:'Attention and retention. A partner watches costs because waste comes partly out of his own pocket, and he stays because his wealth is tied up in the firm. Men like Charles Schwab, who began as a labourer, became enormously wealthy through it. For Carnegie the cash cost was almost nothing.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE TERMS WORKED',nodes:['Manager earns a partnership interest','Watches costs like an owner','The firm grows; the stake gains value','If he leaves, bought back at book value'],note:'Control, and the growth in a departing partner\u2019s stake, stayed with Carnegie.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'WHOSE TERMS',caption:'A manager on the gallery at night, his hand on the ledger and a key beneath it, watching the floor like an owner. The key is his while he stays. What it is worth when he leaves was written by someone else.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The idea is sound, and the design is instructive for how much the designer kept. Alignment can be partial: partners carried the effort and the downside, while the buy-back capped what they could take away. Ownership also aligns managers with the owner, not with the workforce. Partners watching cost per ton were watching wages too.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Where do you ask someone to think like an owner? What share of the outcome do they carry, and on whose terms?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'READ THE DESIGN',prompt:'A start-up gives staff equity that the founders can buy back at the original price if anyone leaves. Which reading fits Carnegie\u2019s system best?',options:['Real alignment while staff stay, with the growth kept by the founders','A gift with no strings, since the staff become full owners','A pure trick, since the staff carry nothing of the outcome','A salary in disguise, since the shares can never rise in value'],answer:0,why:'Like Carnegie\u2019s terms, it makes staff carry the outcome while they stay, but the buy-back decides who keeps the growth.'}]}},'carnegie-autobiography_9':{pictureCaption:'A hand slides a key across to an empty chair, the works running outside. His skill was filling that seat with someone better than himself.',bridge:'Idea 8 showed how he kept able people. This is the line that sums him up, and it is about them.',basics:{title:'Know what you are really good at',beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'A WORD FIRST',word:'Epitaph',meaning:'A short line about a person after they die, often cut into their gravestone. It says how they should be remembered.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'THE LINE ABOUT HIM',text:'Andrew Carnegie got very rich making steel. But he was not an expert in steel. A line said to be his epitaph sums him up. It says he knew how to get people better than him to work for him.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Carnegie\u2019s real skill was finding people better than him at the job, and making them want to stay. That is a true skill, not just being modest.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'THE EMPTY CHAIR',caption:'A key slides to an empty chair. The skill is finding someone better for that seat, and keeping them.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE CLASS PROJECT',text:'In a group project, Mia can\u2019t draw and is not great at maths. But she knows Sam draws brilliantly and Leo loves numbers. She gets them both on board and keeps them happy. Their project is the best in the class.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'If you are not the expert, you are not really good at anything.',reality:'Some skills are not about doing the job yourself. Spotting talent, and making people glad to stay, is a skill too. You can get better at it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'But it is not the whole story',text:'The line is true. But a line like this only shows the good side. It leaves out the hard parts of his life, like the fight at his Homestead works.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your team captain is not the best player. But she picks a great team, and everyone wants to play for her. What would Carnegie say?',options:['That is a real skill, and she should grow it','She should feel bad for not being the best','She should try to play every position herself'],answer:0,why:'Like Carnegie, her skill is in the people she brings together. That counts.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE INTERVIEW QUESTION',text:'You are asked what you are good at. You are not the best engineer or analyst in the building. You are the person who gets the best ones working together, and keeps them. You hesitate, as if that were a confession.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'THE LINE CREDITED TO HIM',text:'The epitaph usually credited to Carnegie is about other people. It praises a man who knew how to bring into his service men better than himself. It is usually read as modesty. It is closer to a job description.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Carnegie was not a metallurgist and did not pretend to be. His skill was identifying people who were, and making it worth their while to stay. The epitaph credited to him is a claim about method.'},{kind:'equation',parts:['Spot the best people','Make staying worth it'],result:'Carnegie\u2019s actual skill',note:'His other strengths, cost figures, capital and timing, all worked through the people he kept.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'A SEAT FOR SOMEONE BETTER',caption:'A key slid across the desk to an empty chair. The works run outside the window. The seat is for someone better at the job than the person offering the key. The key is the reason to stay.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What it looked like',text:'He did not run the furnaces. He found operators who could, Charles Schwab above all. He tied them to the firm through partnership. His own part was cost accounting, the use of capital and timing. The technical excellence came from the people.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'If you are not the expert, your role is somehow lesser.',reality:'Spotting and keeping excellent people is a craft of its own. It means knowing what good looks like in work you do not do, and setting terms that make people stay. It can be practised and improved.'},{kind:'try',text:'Write down, in one line, the thing you are actually good at. If it is not a technical skill, stop apologising for it. Decide how to get better at it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A hospital\u2019s chief executive is not a doctor. Which reading of the epitaph credited to Carnegie applies to her best?',options:['Her job is finding and keeping excellent clinicians, and that is a real skill','She should defer on every decision to the most senior doctor','She should train in medicine so she can lead with authority','Her role matters less because she cannot treat any patients'],answer:0,why:'Carnegie\u2019s line names a skill in its own right: recognising excellence you do not have, and making it stay.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'MODESTY OR METHOD?',belief:'The epitaph credited to Carnegie is a gracious piece of modesty.',reality:'It is a real claim about method. He was not a metallurgist or an engineer. His contributions were cost accounting, capital allocation, timing, and finding and keeping exceptional operators. The line describes the last of these.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The epitaph credited to Carnegie sums him up as a man who enlisted better men than himself. That is true, and it names his actual skill. It also suits a man managing how he would be remembered.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The skill, taken seriously',text:'Enlisting better people takes judgement about excellence in a craft you do not practise, and terms that make staying worth it. Schwab is the clearest case: picked young, promoted fast, and bound in through the partnership interests of idea 8.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'TWO HALVES OF THE SKILL',caption:'A key pushed across to a chair nobody yet sits in. The skill has two halves: judging who is good enough for the seat in a craft you do not practise, and setting terms that make them stay.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The line as performance',text:'Most of his self-presentation does a job, and this line does too. Where the line first appeared is uncertain, but it fits the rest of his self-portrait: a man remembered for Homestead and the twelve-hour day, summed up by a sentence that is true and incomplete. Idea 5 asked what the book leaves out. Here the gap fits in a single line.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'What the line includes',items:['Spotting talent','Keeping able men','Crediting others']},right:{title:'What it leaves out',items:['Homestead','The twelve-hour day','Terms that kept control his']},note:'True and incomplete at once.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Taken at face value, the line flatters him; dismissed as spin, it undersells a real skill. Nor does the lesson generalise neatly. Not being technical is no virtue in itself. The claim holds only because he was strong at the non-technical things he did instead.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'If you wrote one line to sum up your working life, what would it include, and what would it leave out?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'READ IT BOTH WAYS',prompt:'Which reading of the epitaph is most accurate?',options:['A true account of his skill that also omits what he wished forgotten','Pure modesty from a man who undervalued his own contribution','Pure public relations with no basis in how he worked','An admission that he had no real skill of his own'],answer:0,why:'The line is accurate about his method and selective about his legacy. Reading it well means holding both.'}]}}}},2961,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'chemical-history-candle_1':{pictureCaption:'One lit candle on a bare bench, the seats behind it waiting. Six lectures of chemistry started from nothing more than this.',basics:{title:'Start with a real thing',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO START',text:'Your teacher writes a long word on the board. You copy it down. By lunch it is gone. The next week she brings in a real bird\u2019s nest and asks how it was built. Hands go up all over the room.'},{kind:'idea',text:'To explain something, start with one real thing people can see. Let the big ideas come later, when the thing makes people ask for them.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'LONDON, ONE CHRISTMAS',picture:!0,text:'Michael Faraday was a famous scientist in London. One Christmas he gave six talks to young people. He did not start with hard words. He put one lit candle on the table. He told them there was no better way into science.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why a candle?',text:'A candle looks dull. But a lot is going on in it. Wax melts. Liquid climbs. Air moves. A flame glows. Each thing you spot makes you ask why. Each answer is a bit of science.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A DETECTIVE',text:'A good detective does not start with a list of rules. She looks hard at one clue. The questions come out of the clue, one by one, in the right order.'},{kind:'try',text:'Teach a friend a game. Don\u2019t read out the rules first. Play one turn and let them ask why.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You want to show your little brother what a magnet does. Which start uses Faraday\u2019s idea?',options:['Hand him a magnet and a pile of paper clips','Tell him what the word magnet means','Read him a page about how magnets work'],answer:0,why:'He sees it work first. Then he asks why, and the words have something to stick to.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TEN MINUTES WITH A NEW HIRE',prompt:'You have ten minutes to explain your team\u2019s billing system to a new starter. What do you open with?',options:['A diagram of how the whole system fits together','One real invoice, followed from start to finish','The key terms they will hear every day'],answer:1,reveal:'The diagram and the glossary answer questions they have not asked yet. One real invoice makes them ask, and each part of the system arrives when they need it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Open with one concrete instance and let the principle arrive late. A real object raises the questions in the right order, so each idea turns up because it is needed.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A bare bench, one candle, an empty room. Faraday started six lectures of chemistry from here, and never needed a definition first.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the order matters',text:'An abstraction introduced before the thing it describes is just a word to memorise. Introduced after, it names something the listener has already noticed. Faraday\u2019s candle holds capillary action, combustion, convection and the make-up of the air, all of it happening on the table.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE QUESTIONS ARRIVE',nodes:['A real thing on the table','Someone notices something','They ask why','An idea arrives to answer it'],loop:!0,note:'Each answer sends the eye back to the object, which raises the next question.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The second gain',text:'Every claim can be checked on the spot. Nobody has to take the speaker\u2019s word, because the evidence is burning in front of them. That is the difference between being taught science and being shown it.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you explain something at work, bring the real thing: the actual email, the spreadsheet, the broken part. Start there, and hold the summary back.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You are training staff on a new safety rule. Which opening follows Faraday?',options:['Walk through one real near-miss and ask what went wrong','Read the rule aloud and explain each of its clauses','Hand out a one-page summary for everyone to read first','Start with the law that the new rule is based on'],answer:0,why:'The near-miss is the candle. People notice, ask why, and the rule arrives as the answer to their question.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'THE ROYAL INSTITUTION, LONDON',text:'Faraday gave six Christmas lectures for young people, published in 1861. He opened by setting one candle before the room and saying there was no better door into natural philosophy, the period\u2019s name for the study of the physical world.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A concrete object generates the right questions in the right order. Start there, and every abstract principle enters as the answer to something the audience has already wondered about.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Syllabus order',items:['Definitions first','Concepts arrive because they are next','Claims taken on authority','Remembered as words']},right:{title:'Object order',items:['The thing first','Concepts arrive because they are needed','Claims checked on the bench','Remembered as events']},note:'The content can be identical. The order decides whether it is understood or memorised.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What one candle holds',text:'Across the lectures the candle leads to capillary action, combustion, convection, the glow of hot carbon, the make-up of water and air, and finally breathing. None of it is imported from a textbook. Each topic is dragged out of the object because the previous answer raised it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Nothing on the bench but one candle. The empty seats matter: the questions will come from the people who fill them.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Not every subject has a candle. Some ideas, in mathematics or law, have no single object that contains them, and a forced example can mislead more than a definition would. The method also costs time: an hour of questions may cover what a page of definitions covers in five minutes. It pays when understanding matters more than coverage.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of something you explain often. What is its candle: the one real case that contains most of it?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE IMITATION',prompt:'Which of these only *looks* like Faraday\u2019s method?',options:['Opening a talk with a vivid photo, then reading out the definitions anyway','Teaching tax by working through one family\u2019s real return, line by line','Teaching grammar from a sentence a pupil actually wrote that morning','Teaching statistics from a survey the class has just run on itself'],answer:0,why:'A picture used as decoration is not the method. The object has to generate the questions that the concepts then answer.'}]}},'chemical-history-candle_2':{pictureCaption:'A glass tube draws vapour from the dark middle of one flame, and a second flame burns on what it carries.',bridge:'Idea 1 put a real candle on the bench. Here Faraday looks inside its flame, and lets the flame make the argument.',basics:{title:'Show it, don\u2019t just say it',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'LOOK INTO A FLAME',prompt:'What is in the middle of a candle flame?',options:['The hottest, brightest fire','Fuel that has not burned yet','Nothing at all'],answer:1,reveal:'Most people guess the hottest fire. In fact the middle is dark. It is wax that has turned to gas, waiting for air. The burning happens round the outside, like a shell.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'FARADAY\u2019S TRICK',picture:!0,text:'Michael Faraday did not just tell children this. He pushed a thin glass tube into the dark middle of a flame. Gas came out at the far end of the tube. He lit it, and a second flame popped up.'},{kind:'idea',text:'If you want people to remember something, show it happening. Being told is easy to forget. Seeing it is not.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why only the outside burns',text:'Fire needs fuel and air in the same place. The middle of the flame has lots of fuel but not enough air. Only the outside edge has both. So the outside burns, and the middle waits its turn.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Told',items:['\u201cThe flame is hollow.\u201d','You nod','Gone by next week']},right:{title:'Shown',items:['A tube, then a second flame','You gasp','You tell your family that night']},note:'The fact is the same. Only one of them sticks.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend does not believe that ice floats. What is the best way to change her mind?',options:['Drop an ice cube in a glass of water','Tell her again that it is true','Show her a page about it in a book'],answer:0,why:'Seeing it happen is hard to argue with, and hard to forget.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'If you explain something clearly enough, people will remember it.',reality:'Clear telling is easily nodded at and easily lost. What sticks is a moment when you watched your own first impression proved wrong in front of you.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When you want something remembered, show it happening rather than stating it. Faraday did not say a flame is hollow. He drew the fuel out of its middle and lit it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A glass tube in the dark centre of one flame. At its far end, a second flame burns on the vapour it carried out.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the tube proves',text:'The middle of a flame is wax that has turned to vapour but has not met enough air to burn. Burning happens at the boundary, where fuel and oxygen are both present. Draw the vapour off, light it somewhere else, and the claim proves itself.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Why it works as a lesson',steps:['Let the audience form a first impression: a flame is solid light.','Make that impression visibly wrong.','Let the explanation follow from what they have just seen.'],note:'Nobody is asked to believe anything. They watch the vapour come out.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before your next presentation, find the one claim people will doubt. Ask what you could show them, live, that would make the argument unnecessary.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You need managers to accept that the customer sign-up form is too long. What would Faraday do?',options:['Have them fill it in on a phone, timed, during the meeting','Present a chart of how many users drop out at each step','Read out what customers have said about it in surveys','Summarise the published research on form length and abandonment'],answer:0,why:'The others are reports about the problem. Filling it in is the problem happening in front of them, with nothing left to take on trust.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'A FLAME FROM A FLAME',text:'Faraday\u2019s audience had already looked closely at a flame and noticed a dark region in its middle. He then passed a glass tube into that dark centre. Vapour flowed along it, and he lit it at the far end: fuel carried out of one flame to make another.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A demonstration can carry an argument by itself when it overturns a first impression in plain view. The correction is seen, so the explanation that follows is remembered as a consequence, not a claim.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHERE THE BURNING HAPPENS',nodes:['Heat turns wax to vapour','Vapour fills the centre','Air reaches only the edge','Burning happens in a thin shell'],note:'The dark middle is fuel waiting for oxygen. The bright part marks the boundary, not the bulk.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The anatomy of a model demonstration',text:'Three things line up. The audience\u2019s first impression is wrong: a flame looks like a solid body of light. The correction is visible, not inferred. And the explanation follows the correction instead of preceding it. Remove any one and the demonstration shrinks to an illustration of something already said.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Fuel drawn from the centre of one flame and burning at the end of the tube. Nobody watching has to be told the flame is hollow.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Some truths cannot be shown on a bench: the very slow, the very large, the statistical. A vivid demonstration can also mislead, because it feels like proof of more than it shows. The tube proves the centre holds unburnt fuel. It does not, on its own, establish everything about how a flame is structured.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What do you believe mainly because you once saw it demonstrated? What, exactly, did the demonstration show?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A teacher wants pupils to see that a heavy ball and a light ball fall at the same rate. Which plan has all three parts of Faraday\u2019s demonstration?',options:['Collect predictions, drop both balls together, then explain why they land at once','Explain the physics carefully first, then drop two balls to illustrate it','Show a film of a hammer and a feather dropped together on the Moon','State the rule clearly and ask the pupils to test it at home'],answer:0,why:'It draws out the wrong first impression, corrects it in plain view, and lets the explanation follow. Explaining first skips the prediction, so there is nothing to correct.'}]}},'chemical-history-candle_3':{pictureCaption:'The wick stands whole inside the flame. The bright pool of melted wax around it is the real fuel, on its way up.',bridge:'Idea 2 found that a flame burns vapour, not solid wax. This asks where the vapour comes from, and why the supply never runs wild.',basics:{title:'The wick is a straw, not the fuel',beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'A candle burns its wick, a bit like a fuse.',reality:'The wick hardly burns at all. It works like a straw. It lifts melted wax up to the flame, and the flame burns the wax.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A candle makes its own fuel as it goes. Heat melts the wax. The wick lifts it up. The flame burns it. So it keeps going for hours.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'See the pool of melted wax? The wick stands in it, still whole. It lifts the wax up to the flame.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'ROUND AND ROUND',nodes:['Flame heats the wax','Wax melts into a pool','Wick lifts it up','Flame burns it'],loop:!0,note:'Each step feeds the next one. That is why the flame stays the same size.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it does not flare up or die',text:'The wick can only lift so much wax at a time. So the flame can only get so big. And while it burns, it keeps melting more wax for itself. The candle keeps itself steady. Nobody has to fix it.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What else at home keeps itself going with no help? What do you think keeps it steady?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Why can a candle burn for hours on such a thin wick?',options:['The wick lifts up melted wax, and the wax is the fuel','The wick is made to burn very, very slowly','The flame is too small to burn the wick'],answer:0,why:'The wick is just the way up. The wax is what burns.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A THIN STRING, HOURS OF LIGHT',prompt:'A candle burns for hours with only a thin string at its heart. What is the flame actually burning?',options:['The wick, very slowly','Solid wax, near the flame','Wax vapour, fed up the wick'],answer:2,reveal:'Heat melts the wax, the wick draws the liquid up, and the flame burns it as vapour. The wick is the delivery route, and it is used up only slowly.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A candle is a small self-regulating machine. It melts, lifts and burns just enough fuel to stay steady, and the wick is its supply line, not its fuel.'},{kind:'equation',parts:['Heat melts wax','Wick lifts liquid','Flame burns vapour'],result:'Hours of steady light',note:'Each part sets the pace of the next, so nothing needs adjusting by hand.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it does not flare or die',text:'The flame\u2019s heat sets how fast wax melts. The wick sets how fast liquid can rise. The flame can only burn what arrives. So the system settles at one rate and holds it, while the heat trims the wick\u2019s tip as the candle shortens.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The wick stands whole inside the flame. The pool of melted wax around it is the fuel, waiting its turn to climb.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'AT WORK',text:'A weekly report has gone out on time for two years without anyone touching it. Then the person who quietly fixed the data every Monday leaves. The report was never running itself. Something was regulating it, and nobody had asked what.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one process at work that runs fine without attention. Write down what keeps it steady. If you cannot, find out before you depend on it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A team\u2019s shared inbox stays manageable though nobody manages it. What does the candle suggest you do first?',options:['Find out what is keeping it steady before you rely on it','Leave it alone, since it is plainly working well','Add a formal process so that it is properly controlled','Assume it will fail soon and replace it now'],answer:0,why:'Steady running means something is regulating it. Knowing what lets you protect it, or notice when it stops.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'FARADAY\u2019S PUZZLE',text:'Faraday points out that the wick is not the fuel. A candle that used up its wick as fast as its wax would not last. So what is the wick for, and how does the whole thing hold steady for hours?'},{kind:'term',word:'Self-regulating',meaning:'Able to hold a steady state without outside adjustment, because its own output limits its own input. A thermostat does it by design. A candle does it by physics alone.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A candle is a self-regulating apparatus: heat melts the wax, the wick lifts it, the flame burns it as vapour, and each step sets the pace of the next. A simple-looking object hides a stable mechanism.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Nobody designed it in one step',text:'The balance of wax, wick and flame was reached by centuries of small adjustment. Faraday\u2019s real lesson is a habit of looking: take an ordinary object and ask how it manages not to fail. The answer is usually a mechanism nobody notices until it breaks.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The wick stands unburnt inside the flame. The bright pool is the fuel, and the wick is only its route up.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'TRANSFERRED',text:'A nightly data job runs for years untouched. Then it fails, and it turns out it only ever worked because one upstream table stayed small. It looked self-regulating. It was lucky. The candle test is to name the mechanism, not to admire the record.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The title overstates. A wick does burn, slowly: its tip is consumed as the candle shortens, and older wicks had to be trimmed by hand or they smoked. The point is the rate, not immunity. And steadiness is not proof of regulation; a system can run unattended for years on luck.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HONEST LIMIT',prompt:'A colleague says a system must be well designed because it has run untouched for years. What is the candle\u2019s answer?',options:['Long running shows that it is stable, not why; name the mechanism before trusting it','Agreed: years of steady running is the best proof of sound design','Systems that run untouched for years are usually close to failing','Only a system somebody designed on purpose can regulate itself'],answer:0,why:'The candle is steady for a reason you can name. Faraday\u2019s habit is to find that reason, because a good record alone can hide luck.'}]}},'chemical-history-candle_4':{pictureCaption:'One glass of lime-water with two tubes in it: one from a funnel over the candle, one carrying someone\u2019s breath. Either clouds it the same way.',bridge:'For five lectures Faraday tested the candle. In the last one he turns the same test on the people watching it.',basics:{title:'You and a candle do the same thing',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'UNDER A JAR',text:'When a glass jar is put over a lit candle, the flame soon goes out. It has run short of something in the air that it needs. You need that same thing to live. It is called oxygen.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When you breathe, your body does a slow, gentle version of what a candle does. Both take in oxygen. Both give out water and a gas called carbon dioxide.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'THE LAST TALK',picture:!0,text:'Faraday used a test liquid called lime-water. It turns cloudy when carbon dioxide gets into it. Gas from a candle made it cloudy. In his last talk, breath was blown through it. It went cloudy in just the same way.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'A candle',items:['Takes in oxygen','Gives out carbon dioxide and water','Fast, bright and hot']},right:{title:'You',items:['Take in oxygen','Give out carbon dioxide and water','Slow, steady and warm']},note:'Same in, same out. Only the speed is different.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the test matters',text:'Faraday did not just say people are like candles. He checked. He used the same test on the people as on the candle. If they were not alike, the test would have shown it, in front of the whole room.'},{kind:'try',text:'Sit still and count your breaths for one minute. Then jog on the spot for a minute and count again. Your body is using its fuel faster, so it needs more oxygen.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A candle under a jar goes out. What does that candle have in common with you?',options:['Both need oxygen from the air','Both give out oxygen as they go','Both need water to keep going'],answer:0,why:'A candle and a person both take oxygen from the air. Without it, neither can keep going.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'THE FINAL LECTURE',picture:!0,text:'Lime-water turns milky when carbon dioxide passes through it. Faraday had already shown that a candle\u2019s fumes do this. In the last lecture, breath was blown through the same lime-water, and it clouded in exactly the same way.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Breathing and burning are the same chemistry at different speeds: both use oxygen and give off carbon dioxide and water. Faraday showed it with one test, run on the candle and on the people watching it.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'The candle',items:['Takes in oxygen','Gives off carbon dioxide and water','Fast, bright and hot']},right:{title:'The body',items:['Takes in oxygen','Gives off carbon dioxide and water','Slow, steady and warm']},note:'Same inputs, same outputs. The difference is the pace.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why one instrument matters',text:'A comparison stated is a metaphor. A comparison tested with the same instrument on both sides is a finding. If breath had not clouded the lime-water, the idea would have failed in front of the room. It did, so nobody had to take it on trust.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Recently won',text:'The chemistry of breathing had been worked out less than a century before Faraday spoke. He was not handing down a fact from antiquity for the audience to accept. He was showing them something recently won, and letting them watch the evidence for themselves.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one claim you accept about how your body or your habits work. Name a simple test you could run on yourself this week. Then run it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A colleague says afternoon meetings drain the team\u2019s focus. What is the Faraday move?',options:['Measure the same output on afternoons with and without meetings','Agree, since most people say they feel tired after meetings','Find a published study on meetings and attention and share it','Ask the team in a survey whether meetings leave them drained'],answer:0,why:'He used one test on both sides of a comparison. Measuring the same thing with and without the meeting does that, here and now.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',word:'Lime-water',meaning:'A clear solution of calcium hydroxide in water. Carbon dioxide turns it milky by forming fine particles of chalk. It was Faraday\u2019s detector for carbon dioxide in the lectures.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE INSTRUMENT, TURNED ROUND',text:'Lime-water had shown the carbon dioxide in a candle\u2019s fumes. In the sixth lecture the same detector is pointed at the audience. Breath clouds it just as the candle\u2019s fumes did, and the object on the bench becomes a model of the people looking at it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One detector, two inputs: a funnel over the flame and a tube carrying breath. The lime-water cannot tell which is the candle and which the person, and that is the point.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Respiration and combustion are one chemistry at two speeds. The deeper lesson is method: test the familiar with the instrument you used on the strange, and let a comparison earn its place by surviving the test.'},{kind:'equation',parts:['Fuel','Oxygen'],result:'Carbon dioxide, water, energy',note:'For the candle the fuel is wax; for the body, food. The candle releases the energy in a rush, the body in small controlled steps.'},{kind:'explain',title:'More than a pleasing ending',text:'The body is not on fire, but it does oxidise its fuel, and the inputs and outputs match. Proving that with lime-water turns a nice likeness into a claim that could have failed. That is the habit Faraday leaves behind: if a comparison is real, it should survive being run on yourself.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The likeness is in the balance sheet, not the mechanism. A flame oxidises fuel in one hot rush. The body does it in many small steps, driven by enzymes at body temperature, capturing much of the energy instead of shedding it as heat and light. Pressed too far, the analogy teaches that cells hold tiny fires.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which explanation about yourself have you accepted without ever testing it? What would your lime-water be?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'WHERE IT HOLDS',prompt:'Which statement keeps Faraday\u2019s comparison accurate?',options:['Body and candle share inputs and outputs but reach them by very different routes','The body burns its food in a slow, cool flame deep inside each cell','Breath differs from candle fumes because only the candle makes carbon dioxide','The likeness is purely poetic, since breath and flame share no chemistry'],answer:0,why:'Lime-water shows the same products. It says nothing about the route, and the routes are very different.'}]}},'chemical-history-candle_5':{pictureCaption:'Real flasks and stands, nothing toy-sized, and a hand ready to guide. The route is made easy. The science is not made smaller.',bridge:'Ideas 1 and 2 showed how Faraday opened and how he proved things. This is the rule underneath both: change the route, never the truth.',basics:{title:'Slower, not smaller',beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO EXPLAIN',left:{title:'Smaller',items:['Leaves out the hard part','Easy now','Wrong later']},right:{title:'Slower',items:['Keeps the hard part','One step at a time','Still true later']},note:'Faraday always picked slower.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When you explain something to someone younger, go slower. Don\u2019t make it smaller. A simple answer that is wrong does not teach. It gets in the way later.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'TALKS FOR CHILDREN',text:'Faraday\u2019s candle talks were for children. But he used the real words of science, like oxygen and carbon. He did not leave out the hard parts. He showed each one on the table, so it was easy to follow.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'AT HOME',text:'Your little cousin asks why it gets dark at night. You could say the sun goes to sleep. That is easy, but wrong. Or you could shine a torch on a ball and turn the ball slowly. It takes longer. But now she knows the real reason.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Real glass and real tools, nothing made as a toy. Faraday made science easy to follow. He never made it smaller.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time someone younger asks you a question, give the true answer. Just give it in small steps, and show them if you can.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A small child asks where rain comes from. Which answer is slower, not smaller?',options:['Show how breath on a cold window turns into drops','Say that the clouds are crying','Say it is too hard until she is older'],answer:0,why:'It keeps the real reason and makes it easy to see. The other two are wrong, or they give up.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'NEAR ENOUGH',text:'A new colleague asks how your pricing works. You say it is basically cost plus a margin. It is not, quite. Discounts and contract terms change everything. A month later they quote a client on that basis, confidently and wrongly.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Slow down; do not dilute. An explanation that leaves someone with a comfortable wrong model has not taught them. It has made the right model harder to accept later.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Explaining to beginners means simplifying the content until it is easy.',reality:'Faraday\u2019s lectures were for children. He kept the content whole and the vocabulary real. What he changed was the route. Every concept arrived attached to something on the bench.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO MAKE IT EASIER',low:'Shallower: less of the truth',high:'Slower: all of the truth, in smaller steps',note:'Most explaining for non-specialists moves towards the left. Faraday stayed at the right end.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why a wrong model is worse than none',text:'Someone with no model knows they do not know. Someone with a tidy wrong one thinks the matter is closed. The true version later has to dislodge it first. A simplification that sacrifices truth does not save effort. It moves the effort to later and makes it bigger.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Full-size laboratory equipment, nothing toy, set out for a young audience. The steps were made smaller. The science was not.'},{kind:'try',text:'Take one explanation you give often. Ask of each simplification in it: is this slower, or is it shallower? Replace any shallow one with an extra step.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You are explaining compound interest to a nervous first-time saver. Which approach is slower, not shallower?',options:['Work through three years of one real balance, line by line','Tell them it simply means their money grows faster over time','Skip the arithmetic and tell them which account to open','Give them the formula and a calculator and let them try'],answer:0,why:'It keeps the whole mechanism and breaks it into steps they can follow. Saying it simply grows faster is true but leaves them unable to reason about it.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Faraday\u2019s lectures were simplified science, pitched down for a young audience.',reality:'They carry real content: the products of combustion, the composition of water, the behaviour of gases, in the proper vocabulary. Adults still read them because nothing was removed to make them easy.'},{kind:'term',word:'Inoculate',meaning:'Borrowed from medicine: to give a weak dose so the body resists the real thing. A friendly false model inoculates a listener, who then resists the true one, believing they already understand.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The whole craft is the difference between slower and shallower. Keep the difficulty and supply the means of working through it. Remove it to deliver a conclusion, and the comfortable wrong model inoculates the listener against the right one.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'The usual method',items:['Removes the difficulty','Delivers a conclusion','Listener can repeat it','Ends the questions']},right:{title:'Faraday\u2019s method',items:['Keeps the difficulty','Supplies the means through it','Listener can reason with it','Starts the questions']},note:'Same audience, same hour. Only one leaves them able to go further alone.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Respect as method',text:'He addressed children as able to be wrong and corrected. He invited predictions, and when one failed he treated the failure as the interesting part. That assumes the listener can bear a difficulty, which is the opposite of talking down, and it is why the lectures still work on adults.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Full-sized glassware and stands, nothing toy. The hand is placed to guide, not to swap in something smaller.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Every explanation simplifies; the question is whether each simplification is true at its own scale. Saying a flame burns wax vapour leaves out detailed chemistry but is not false. Some audiences also lack time for the slower route, and a short, honest partial answer beats a long one never finished. The rule forbids false steps, not missing ones.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE SHALLOW ONE',prompt:'Which simplification would Faraday reject?',options:['Electrons circle the nucleus like planets, and that is the whole story','A candle flame burns wax vapour, not solid wax','Air is mostly nitrogen, with about a fifth oxygen','Plants make sugar from carbon dioxide and water using light'],answer:0,why:'The others leave out detail but stay true. The planet picture, sold as the whole story, is a comfortable wrong model that later has to be undone.'}]}},'chemical-history-candle_6':{pictureCaption:'A strip of cloth hangs into a dish, and the liquid creeps up it with nothing pushing. The same pull lifts melted wax up a wick.',bridge:'Idea 3 said the wick lifts the wax. This is how it does it, and why Faraday explained it with a towel.',basics:{title:'Wax climbs a wick like water climbs a towel',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'BATH TIME',text:'Leave the corner of a towel hanging in the bath. Come back later, and the towel is wet far above the water. Nothing pumped the water up. It climbed by itself.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Wax climbs a candle wick in the same way water climbs a towel. Something that looks special is often a thing you have seen before.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'See how the wet part creeps up the cloth, with nothing pushing it. Wax climbs a wick in just this way.'},{kind:'term',word:'Capillary action',meaning:'When a liquid creeps up tiny gaps by itself, even uphill. It happens in towels, in tissues and in candle wicks.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'FARADAY\u2019S TOWEL',text:'Faraday told the children how a towel left hanging in a basin can empty the water onto the floor. Then he showed the same thing in a candle wick. The children had seen it at home. Now they had a name for it.'},{kind:'try',text:'Dip one corner of a paper towel into a glass of water. Watch the water climb. That is how wax gets up a wick.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend thinks a wick is made of special stuff that sucks up wax. What would Faraday say?',options:['It is the same pull that wets a towel in the bath','Yes, wicks are made to suck up wax','No, the heat of the flame pushes the wax up'],answer:0,why:'Wax climbs a wick the way water climbs cloth. Nothing special is needed, just lots of tiny gaps.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'A WET FLOOR',text:'A tea towel slips off the edge of the sink, one end left in the washing-up water. An hour later there is a puddle on the floor. No pump, no tilt, no heat. The water climbed the cloth, crossed the rim and came down the other side.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Wax reaches a candle\u2019s flame by capillary action, the same pull that draws water up a hanging cloth. Faraday\u2019s method was to show the exotic process as an ordinary one the audience had already seen.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The same thing, not just like it',text:'This is not an analogy. A towel and a wick are both bundles of fine fibres with narrow gaps between them, and liquid is drawn into narrow gaps. Faraday shows it in cloth, in a wick, and in a column of salt drawing coloured liquid upward. One effect, three settings.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Defined first',items:['\u201cCapillary action is\u2026\u201d','A term to memorise','Gone in a week']},right:{title:'Seen first',items:['The towel that flooded the floor','A memory the term attaches to','Hard to lose']},note:'The explanation is complete before the physics is named.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Cloth hanging into a dish, the liquid climbing it with nothing pushing. A wick is the same thing, carrying wax instead of water.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time something at work looks like a special case, ask where you have already seen the ordinary version of it. Explain it from there.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A new manager treats the team\u2019s growing backlog as a unique crisis. What is the Faraday move?',options:['Show it as a familiar queue: work arriving faster than it leaves','Treat it as unique and design an entirely fresh process','Give it the correct technical name from the field','Wait and see whether it clears itself before acting'],answer:0,why:'A backlog is an ordinary queue. Seeing the everyday version makes it explainable, and usually points to the fix.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'A TOWEL IN A BASIN',text:'In his first lecture Faraday explains how wax reaches the flame. He reminds his audience of a towel left with one end in a basin, which carries the water over the rim and onto the floor. Then he shows the same effect in a wick, and in a column of salt drawing coloured liquid up.'},{kind:'term',word:'Capillary action',meaning:'The rise of a liquid through narrow spaces, driven by its attraction to the solid it wets and by surface tension, until the weight of the column balances the pull.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Faraday explains capillary action not with an analogy but with the identical effect in a familiar setting. The definition then attaches to a memory the listener already has.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'UP THE WICK',nodes:['Liquid wax wets the fibres','It is drawn into narrow gaps','The column rises','The flame burns the top away'],loop:!0,note:'In an upright strip of cloth the column stops when its weight balances the pull. In a candle the top is burnt as fast as it arrives, so the rise continues.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it is not decoration',text:'An analogy says one thing is like another and hopes the likeness holds. Faraday\u2019s towel is the same process, so nothing has to be translated back. That is why it lasts: the listener does not remember a comparison. They recognise a mechanism they have already seen at work.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Liquid climbing a strip of cloth against gravity. Not a picture of something like a wick: the same physics, in a dish.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Not every special case is an ordinary one in disguise. Some processes are new, and insisting on a familiar version produces a false match: electricity taught as water in pipes misleads at the points that matter. Look for the everyday version first, then check that it is the same mechanism and not just a resemblance.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What in your field do newcomers find exotic? Where have they already seen it, without knowing its name?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SAME OR SIMILAR?',prompt:'Which of these is Faraday\u2019s method, rather than an analogy?',options:['Explaining a wick\u2019s draw with a paper towel soaking up a spill','Explaining voltage as water pressure pushing through a pipe','Explaining the immune system as an army defending a fort','Explaining memory as a filing cabinet full of records'],answer:0,why:'The paper towel is the same physics as the wick. The others compare different mechanisms, and hold only in part.'}]}},'chemical-history-candle_7':{pictureCaption:'Seen from above, the melted wax sits in a cup whose rim is still solid wax. Air drawn up past the sides keeps that rim cool.',bridge:'Idea 3 called the candle self-regulating. This is the strangest part of that: the flame builds the cup that holds its own fuel.',basics:{title:'The flame builds its own cup',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'WHY NO DRIPS?',prompt:'The flame is hot enough to melt all the wax. So why does a good candle not drip?',options:['The wax is a special kind','Cool air keeps the edge solid','The flame is not that hot'],answer:1,reveal:'The flame pulls air up past the sides of the candle. That air cools the edge. The edge stays solid and makes a little cup. The cup holds the melted wax.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A candle\u2019s flame makes a cup for its own fuel. The air it pulls in keeps the edge cool, so the melted wax has somewhere to sit.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Look down into the candle. The middle has melted. The pale ring around it has not, so it holds the pool like a cup.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE CUP IS MADE',nodes:['Flame heats the air above it','Hot air rises','Cool air flows up the sides','The edge stays solid'],note:'The same air that feeds the flame also builds the cup.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'BY AN OPEN WINDOW',text:'Near an open window, a draught pushes the flame over to one side. That side of the cup melts. Wax runs down the candle, and the flame flickers. Break one part, and the whole thing goes wrong.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Is there a part of your day that seems useless but might be holding something else up?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A candle in a windy room has wax running down one side. What has gone wrong?',options:['The wind spoiled one side of the cup','The wax was too cheap to work','The flame has run out of air'],answer:0,why:'Wind pushes the flame and the air to one side. That part of the rim gets too warm, and the cup leaks.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE BACK DOOR',text:'A candle on the table burns all evening without a drip. Someone opens the back door, and within minutes a channel has melted in one side and wax is running down onto the cloth.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The flame draws air up past the candle\u2019s sides, and that air keeps the rim cool and solid. The flame builds the vessel that holds its own fuel, and the vessel fails when the draught is disturbed.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'A LOOP OF DEPENDENCIES',nodes:['Flame heats the air above','Rising air pulls cool air upward','Cool rim holds a cup of wax','Cup feeds a steady flame'],loop:!0,note:'Each part produces the conditions for the next.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What Faraday did with it',text:'He asked how the cup survives under a flame hot enough to consume it. Then he pointed to what a draught does: the cup fails, the wax runs, the candle gutters. A feature nobody would notice turned out to be holding the whole arrangement together.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A pool of wax held in a ring of wax that has not melted. The ring stays solid because the flame keeps pulling cool air past it.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'AT WORK',text:'A weekly meeting runs long, and everyone wants to cut it. Nobody notices that it is also the only place where two departments hear each other\u2019s problems. Cut it, and those problems arrive later, bigger. The awkward feature was doing work.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before you remove the most annoying step in a process, ask what would break without it. Ask the person who would notice first.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A shop removes its slow queue barriers to speed things up. Soon customers are pushing in and arguing. What does the candle suggest the barriers were doing?',options:['Keeping order, which nobody noticed until they went','Nothing; the customers were just impatient that day','Slowing the queue, which was why they had to go','Decorating the shop long after they stopped being useful'],answer:0,why:'Like the candle\u2019s rim, the barriers were quietly holding the system together. Their awkwardness was the price of that.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'A candle holds its melted wax because wax is hard to melt.',reality:'Wax melts readily, and the flame is hot enough to melt all of it. The cup survives because the air drawn into the flame cools the rim as it passes. Take the airflow away and the cup fails.'},{kind:'term',word:'Convection',meaning:'Heat carried by a moving fluid. Air warmed by the flame rises, and cooler air flows in below and up the candle\u2019s sides to replace it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The convection current that feeds the flame also cools the candle\u2019s rim, so the flame builds the vessel that contains its own fuel. It is a system maintaining the conditions of its own operation.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Even draught',items:['Rim cooled all round','Cup holds','Flame steady']},right:{title:'Disturbed draught',items:['One side overheats','Wax runs down','Candle gutters']},note:'Faraday shows the dependency by pointing to what a draught does, not by arguing about it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'A system, not a heap of parts',text:'The candle is not simply burning. It produces the airflow that shapes the container that supplies the fuel. Interrupt any one link and the whole arrangement collapses. That interdependence is what Faraday wants his audience to see in an object they had thought too simple to study.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A pool held by a rim of the same wax, unmelted. The rim is not a wall the candle came with. The draught builds it.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE AWKWARD FEATURE',text:'An engineer wants to replace a slow overnight batch job with real-time updates. Nobody mentions that finance reconciles against the stable snapshot the batch leaves behind. The job is replaced, and the month-end figures stop agreeing.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Not every awkward feature is quietly essential. Some are leftovers, and the belief that everything does hidden work becomes an argument against any change. The rule is investigative, not conservative: find out what a feature does, as a draught shows what the rim does, and then decide.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE RIGHT TEST',prompt:'You suspect an odd step in a process is doing hidden work. Which approach follows the lesson of the draught?',options:['Disturb it in a small, safe trial and watch what fails','Keep it, since odd features usually exist for good reasons','Remove it, since nobody can explain it on paper','Ask whoever added it what they meant it to do'],answer:0,why:'Faraday did not argue about the rim. A draught upsets it and the cup fails, which shows its job. A small, controlled test shows what a feature is holding up.'}]}},'chemical-history-candle_8':{pictureCaption:'The flame drawn as what makes it bright: countless specks of carbon, each heated until it glows, then burnt away.',bridge:'Idea 2 found the burning at the flame\u2019s outer edge. This explains why that edge shines, and it is not what you would guess.',basics:{title:'A candle shines because of soot',beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'A flame is bright because the fire is burning so well.',reality:'The bright part is full of tiny bits of soot. They get so hot that they glow, like the wires in a toaster. Then they burn up before they leave the flame.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A candle gives light because tiny specks of soot glow in its heat. Without them, the flame would be much harder to see.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The flame drawn as tiny glowing specks. Each one is a bit of soot, so hot that it shines.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'THE SOOT TEST',text:'Faraday held something cold in the flame. Black soot built up on it. That soot was the same stuff that had been glowing. The cold had caught it before it could burn.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE SPARKS',text:'Think of the sparks from a sparkler. Each spark is a tiny hot bit, glowing as it flies. A candle flame is full of glowing bits too. They are just far smaller.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The strange part',text:'Give a flame more air, and the soot burns up faster. It has no time to glow. So the flame gets hotter, but dimmer. That is why a gas cooker flame is blue and gives so little light.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A camping stove burns with a blue flame that gives off little light. Why?',options:['It burns its fuel so well that little soot is left to glow','It is not hot enough to make any light','Blue flames are a cold kind of fire'],answer:0,why:'Little soot means little glow. The flame is hot. It just is not bright.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'BRIGHT OR CLEAN?',prompt:'Which flame is burning its fuel more completely?',options:['A bright yellow candle flame','A pale blue gas flame','They burn equally completely'],answer:1,reveal:'The blue flame. The candle\u2019s yellow light comes from unburnt carbon glowing in the heat. Brightness is a sign of incomplete burning, not of a good fire.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A candle is bright because it is slightly dirty. Solid carbon particles are heated white inside the flame and burnt before they leave it. A hydrogen flame, with no particles, is almost invisible.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Not a solid body of light but a cloud of specks, each a grain of carbon glowing white-hot before it burns.'},{kind:'steps',title:'How Faraday showed it',steps:['A cold surface held in the flame collects soot: the glowing carbon, caught before it burns.','A flame given more air burns hotter and gives less light.','So the light was unburnt fuel at high temperature.']},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it reverses the intuition',text:'We read brightness as vigour. Here it is a by-product of inefficiency. A perfectly efficient flame would be nearly dark, and Faraday produced one on the bench. The feature everyone notices is the imperfection doing the work.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'IN A PRODUCT',text:'An app\u2019s best-loved feature is a slightly slow animation that a developer wants to optimise away. Users like it because the pause tells them their tap registered. Engineer out the imperfection and the thing people valued goes with it.'},{kind:'try',text:'Name something in your work that performs unusually well. Before you fix any flaw in it, check whether that flaw is what makes it work.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Someone proposes feeding a candle flame more air to make it burn better. What happens to its light?',options:['It falls, because the carbon burns before it can glow','It rises, because the fuel now burns more completely','It stays the same, since the light comes from the wax','It turns redder and brighter as the flame cools down'],answer:0,why:'More air consumes the carbon sooner. The flame grows hotter but darker, as Faraday showed.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE QUESTION HE ASKS',text:'Why does a candle flame give so much light when a hydrogen flame, though very hot, is barely visible? Faraday\u2019s answer is that the candle\u2019s fuel contains carbon, and the hydrogen flame has nothing solid in it to glow.'},{kind:'term',word:'Incandescence',meaning:'Light given off by a solid because it is hot, as in a lamp filament or a poker in a fire. Hot gas alone gives little of it; solid particles give a great deal.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The yellow light of a candle comes from solid carbon particles made incandescent by the heat and then consumed. Brightness signals incomplete combustion: the visible glow is unburnt fuel at high temperature.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE LIFE OF A PARTICLE',nodes:['Wax vapour breaks down in heat','Carbon particles form','They glow white-hot','They burn at the outer edge'],note:'Catch them early on a cold surface and they are soot. Give the flame more air and they burn before they can glow.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A flame resolved into specks: each one a carbon particle at white heat, about to be burnt away.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The reversal',text:'Faraday then produces a nearly dark flame by supplying more air. It is hotter and gives less light. The demonstration overturns the natural reading, that a bright flame is a good one, and replaces it with a mechanism the audience has watched.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The lesson about imperfection does not generalise freely. Here the flaw is the function. More often a flaw is just a flaw, and the claim that every inefficiency is secretly useful is a way of defending waste. Even here the glow comes at a price: a sooty flame is bright but dirty.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A team\u2019s most productive meetings overrun because people stay on to argue. Management wants strict end times. What does the candle suggest?',options:['Check whether the overrun produces the results before cutting it','Enforce the end times, since overruns are always waste','Let every meeting run long, since overruns cause success','Ignore it, since meetings and candles share nothing useful'],answer:0,why:'Like the soot, the flaw may be doing the work. The rule is to look first, not to assume either way.'}]}},'chemical-history-candle_9':{pictureCaption:'The apparatus is pushed aside, finished with. One candle burns at the front: fully understood now, and ready to stand for something more.',bridge:'Idea 4 made the candle a model of the audience\u2019s bodies. The ending makes it a model of their lives.',basics:{title:'Save the big message for the end',beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'THE LAST WORDS',text:'Faraday spent six talks showing children how a candle works. At the very end he said something new. He hoped they would be like a candle: giving light to the people around them, and doing their duty well.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A big message works best at the end, after the facts. Said first, it is just nice words. Said last, people know what it means.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The tools are pushed back. One candle is left, burning at the front. Now the children know how it works, it can stand for more.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Said first',items:['Be like a candle!','Nice, but what does it mean?','Soon forgotten']},right:{title:'Said last',items:['Be like a candle','You know how it burns and gives light','Full of meaning']}},{kind:'example',kicker:'AT A MATCH',text:'A coach who says \u201cnever give up\u201d before the game is just talking. A coach who says it after you came back from three goals down is saying something true. The words are the same. The order changes them.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You are giving a talk about your grandad, who was a nurse. When should you say what he taught you about kindness?',options:['At the end, after the stories about him','At the very start, before anything else','It does not matter where it goes'],answer:0,why:'The stories make the lesson real. Then the big line means something.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE KEYNOTE',text:'A conference talk opens on a slide that says the audience can change the world. The room nods politely. Forty minutes later nobody could say what change was meant, or why they should believe it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Put the sentiment after the evidence, not before it. Where a moral sits decides whether it reads as a platitude or a conclusion.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'THE LAST PARAGRAPH',text:'Faraday ends his sixth lecture with a wish: that his listeners might be like a candle, giving light to those around them and doing their duty to others. It is the series\u2019 closing moral, and it comes after hours of chemistry.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the position does the work',text:'By the end the audience knows what a candle does. It consumes itself to give light and heat, steadily, while keeping up the conditions of its own burning. The metaphor now has content. Said in the first minute, the same words would have been decoration.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The apparatus pushed aside and finished with. One candle left at the front, understood, and ready to stand for something.'},{kind:'flow',nodes:['Evidence builds understanding','Understanding gives the image content','The sentiment lands as a conclusion'],note:'Reverse the order and the sentiment arrives with nothing to hold it up.'},{kind:'try',text:'Take your next presentation or note. Move the inspiring line from the top to the bottom, then check that what comes before it actually earns it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You are closing a project review that went well. Where should the line about what the team proved it can do go?',options:['At the end, after the results that show it','At the start, to set a positive tone','On its own slide, before the agenda','Nowhere, since a review has no room for sentiment'],answer:0,why:'After the results it is a conclusion people can check. Before them it is a claim they must take on trust.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'SAME WORDS, TWO PLACES',left:{title:'As an opening',items:['A wish about candles and duty','Unsupported','Heard as moralising']},right:{title:'As a close',items:['The same wish','Backed by six lectures','Heard as a conclusion']},note:'Nothing in the sentence changes. Its position does.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Faraday\u2019s closing wish works because it arrives last. Six lectures have made the candle fully understood, so the metaphor is earned rather than asserted. A moral placed after the evidence becomes a conclusion; placed before it, a platitude.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Read cynically, then in context',text:'Taken alone, the ending is Victorian moralising. In context it completes the course. Faraday has shown that a candle consumes itself to produce light and heat, burns in an orderly way, and keeps up the conditions of its own operation. The last sentence asks the audience to read that understood object as a figure for a life.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The apparatus is pushed aside and used. One candle burns alone at the front edge: the object, now understood, becoming a figure.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Earned and cheap metaphors',text:'A metaphor about a candle is cheap: everyone knows candles give light. A metaphor about a candle whose chemistry you have just watched is dense, because each feature now carries a fact. The image is unchanged. What changed is what the listener knows.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Position cannot rescue a moral the evidence does not support. Chemistry shows how a candle works, not how anyone should live. The step from one to the other is a choice, not a deduction, and Faraday\u2019s own religious and Victorian sense of duty shapes it. Good ordering earns the sentiment attention, not proof.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of a value you want a team or a child to take from something. What would have to come first for it to land as a conclusion?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HONEST OBJECTION',prompt:'A critic says Faraday\u2019s ending smuggles a moral into a science course. What is the fairest reply?',options:['The ordering gives the image content, but the moral is still a choice, not a finding','The chemistry itself proves that people should behave like candles','It is pure decoration, and the course would lose nothing without it','Morals belong at the start, where listeners can judge them fairly'],answer:0,why:'Placement makes the metaphor meaningful. It does not make the ethical claim follow from the science, and an honest reading says so.'}]}}}},2962,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'common-sense_1':{pictureCaption:'An open pamphlet among tankards and a work glove. The fine leather book sits shut on the shelf above. The pamphlet was written for the table.',basics:{title:'Talk to the one who decides',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE WRONG LISTENER',text:'You want a later bedtime. You make a great case to your little brother. He nods at every word. Then you see the problem. He does not decide your bedtime. Your mum does.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Before you try to win someone over, work out who really decides. Then talk to that person, in words they already use.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'AMERICA, 1776',text:'In 1776, America was not yet its own country. It was ruled by the king of Britain, far across the sea. A man named Thomas Paine wrote a short booklet called *Common Sense*. It said the people there should rule themselves.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHO IT WAS WRITTEN FOR',left:{title:'Most booklets then',items:['Long, fancy words','Bits of Latin and Greek','Written for rich, schooled men']},right:{title:'Paine\u2019s booklet',items:['Plain, short words','Bible stories people knew','Written for farmers and shopkeepers']},note:'Paine did not need the rich men. He needed everyone else.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'An open booklet on a busy table, by mugs and a work glove. The fine book stays shut on the high shelf. Paine wrote for this table.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it worked',text:'Most of the ideas in it were not new. What was new was who he wrote for. He wrote so a farmer could follow every line. Lots of people read it, or heard it read out, and talked about it.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you want something, ask two things. Who decides this? What words does that person use? Then make your case to them.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your class wants a new football for break time. Which plan follows Paine\u2019s idea?',options:['Write a short, clear note to the head teacher','Make a long speech to your class about it','Use the longest words you know, to sound smart'],answer:0,why:'The head teacher decides, so the note goes to her. Your class already agrees, and long words help no one follow.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE USUAL ASSUMPTION',belief:'A strong argument will win, as long as it is strong enough.',reality:'Most arguments fail for a duller reason. They are pitched at people who were never going to decide, in language the people who do decide do not use.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Before making a case, name the specific person who has to be convinced, and write to them. In *Common Sense* the content was not what was new. The audience was.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'PHILADELPHIA, 1776',picture:!0,text:'Political pamphlets of the day were written for men with classical educations, and cited classical authors to match. Thomas Paine, a recent arrival from England, wrote for everybody else: plain words, short sentences, and Bible references his readers already knew.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the audience was the argument',text:'If independence was a matter for gentlemen, gentlemen would settle it. By writing for farmers, craftsmen and shopkeepers, Paine treated them as the people deciding. The pamphlet did not only persuade its readers. It told them the decision was theirs.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'AT WORK',text:'You want funding for a new tool. You write a careful case about how elegant it is and send it to your team, who already love it. The person who signs off is the finance lead, who cares about one thing: what it saves. Same tool, wrong reader, no money.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Before you write',steps:['Name the one person whose yes you need.','List what they already care about, and the words they use for it.','Cut every sentence written to impress somebody else.','Read it back as them.']},{kind:'try',text:'Take a case you are making this week. Write at the top the name of the person who decides. Then delete one paragraph that was written for someone else.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Parents want a safer crossing outside a school. The council\u2019s highways officer decides. Which approach follows the idea?',options:['A short note to the officer, in the terms their decisions use','A passionate post in the parents\u2019 group that everyone shares','A long letter on why child safety matters to every family','A speech at the school gate to rally the other parents'],answer:0,why:'The officer decides, so the case is written to them in their terms. The other three mostly persuade people who already agree.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE PAMPHLETS OF THE 1770S',title:'What political writing sounded like',picture:!0,text:'Colonial pamphlets on the quarrel with Britain were mostly addressed to an educated few. They argued from law and charters and decorated the argument with classical authors. Paine dropped all of that. His references were to Scripture and to daily life, which his readers already shared.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The decision about whom an argument is for can matter more than the argument. Name the party who will actually decide, then write to them, in their references and their register.'},{kind:'term',word:'Register',meaning:'The level of formality a piece of writing assumes, and with it the reader it assumes. A register built for the learned quietly tells everyone else the matter is not theirs to settle.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why this is the transferable part',text:'Most arguments do not fail on strength. They fail because they are pitched at people who were never going to be the deciding party. Paine\u2019s content was largely familiar. What was new was treating the ordinary colonist as the person whose mind had to change, and so as the person whose choice it was.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO LOSE',left:{title:'Strong case, wrong reader',items:['Wins admiration','Changes no decision','Feels like progress']},right:{title:'Right reader, wrong register',items:['Reaches the decider','Is not followed','Reads as not meant for them']},note:'Paine avoided both: the readers who would decide, in the words they used.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Plainness has costs. A case cut down to what everyone can follow loses its qualifications, and a register built to stir carries a weak argument as easily as a strong one. Some allies, John Adams among them, thought the pamphlet\u2019s plan for government far too simple. Writing to the decider is a method. It says nothing about whether the case is sound.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of an argument you lost recently. Who actually decided it, and was anything you said written for them?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A scientist wants a town to ban a pesticide near its river. The decision will be made by a public vote. Which mistake is the idea warning against?',options:['Writing the case as a technical paper for other scientists','Using examples from the town\u2019s own farms and river','Explaining the risk in short sentences anyone can follow','Treating the voters, not the experts, as the ones to persuade'],answer:0,why:'The voters decide, so a paper pitched at experts is aimed at the wrong party. The other three are the method itself.'}]}},'common-sense_2':{pictureCaption:'Neighbours handing things across open doors, and beside them one chained gate. The helping is society. The lock is government.',bridge:'Idea 1 was about whom Paine wrote for. This is the first move he made for them, on the pamphlet\u2019s opening pages.',basics:{title:'The group and its rules are two things',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A NEW CLUB',prompt:'You and your friends start a club. At first there are no rules. Why might you need some later?',options:['Every club has to have rules','As it grows, a few people stop playing fair','Rules make a club more fun'],answer:1,reveal:'At first everyone helps, because they all want the club to work. As more people join, a few slack off or cheat. The rules are for them.'},{kind:'idea',text:'People helping each other is one thing. The rules over them are another. The helping is good in itself. The rules are only needed because some people do not play fair.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'THOMAS PAINE, 1776',text:'Thomas Paine wrote a short book in 1776, when the king of Britain ruled America. It starts with this split. People living and working together is a good thing, he said. Rulers are something we need, but should not love.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A BIKE LOCK',text:'Nobody loves locks. But you need one on your bike, because a few people steal. Paine thought rulers were like that. Useful, but only because not everyone is good.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'On one side, neighbours hand things to each other. On the other, a locked gate. The lock is there only because not everyone can be trusted.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why split them?',text:'Once you split them, you can say the rules are bad without saying the people are bad. Paine could attack the king\u2019s rule and still stand up for his own people. And if rules are just a tool, a bad one can be swapped.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your class has a new rule: no talking at lunch. You think it is unfair. Which line uses Paine\u2019s idea?',options:['I like our class. I just think this rule should go.','Our class is awful, so of course the rule is too.','Rules are always bad, so we should have none.'],answer:0,why:'You kept the group and the rule apart. That lets you fix the rule without attacking the class.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'ON THE WARD',text:'You believe in the hospital you work for. A new rota policy is wearing the ward down. Each time you raise it, someone hears an attack on the hospital. The talk turns to loyalty instead of the rota.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Separate the thing from the way it is run. Paine set society, which our needs produce, against government, which our failings make necessary. Once they are apart, you can criticise the running without attacking the thing.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How Paine opens',text:'*Common Sense* begins before Britain is even mentioned. Imagine a few settlers in an empty land, he says. They cooperate because they need each other. Only as their numbers grow and their sense of duty slackens do they set up some government. It is a practical convenience, not a sacred order.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Society',items:['Produced by our needs','Good in itself','The country you belong to']},right:{title:'Government',items:['Made necessary by our failings','Good only if it works','A tool, and replaceable']},note:'Even at its best, Paine says, government is an evil we cannot do without.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Neighbours handing things across open doors: society. One chained gate: government, there because some people cannot be trusted.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it does the work',text:'If government is a tool, it earns its place by results. Then any one arrangement can be judged and replaced, and saying so is not treason. Monarchy had been carefully shielded from just that question: does this work?'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you criticise how something you value is run, first say plainly what you are not criticising. Then make the point.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A fan wants the club\u2019s owners to change the ticket policy without sounding disloyal. Which opening applies Paine\u2019s move?',options:['The club is ours; the ticket policy is the owners\u2019, and it is failing us','The club has lost its way, and the fans deserve far better than this','Real fans should keep quiet and back the owners in public','Clubs are just businesses now, so loyalty is beside the point'],answer:0,why:'It names the thing and the running of it separately, so the criticism lands on the policy, not the club.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE FIRST PAGES',title:'Where the pamphlet begins',text:'Paine opens by pulling apart two things habitually run together. Society comes from what we need from one another; government comes from our failure to behave well. The first is a blessing in any condition. The second, at its very best, is an evil we cannot do without.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Government is a tool made necessary by human failings, not the community itself. A tool earns its place by results, so any arrangement of it can be judged and replaced without disloyalty to the country.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'PAINE\u2019S THOUGHT EXPERIMENT',nodes:['Settlers cooperate from need','Numbers grow, duty slackens','Some government becomes useful','Government is judged by results'],note:'The last step is the one the rest of the pamphlet depends on.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Open doors and passing hands: society. A single chained gate: government, the lock a community adds when trust runs short.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What it unlocks',text:'Monarchy had been insulated from the question of whether it works, because it was presented as an order rather than a convenience. Once government is a tool, Paine can ask of this one what anyone asks of a tool, and treat replacing it as practical rather than treasonous.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The split is cleaner on the page than in life. Law helps create the trade and property that society runs on, so the two are entangled. The line is also borrowed to argue that government should do as little as possible, yet Paine himself later proposed public provision for the old and the poor, in *Rights of Man* and *Agrarian Justice*.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Society comes from need; government from our failings.','A tool is judged by results, not revered.','Name what you are not criticising before you criticise.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which use of Paine\u2019s distinction stretches it past what he argued?',options:['Arguing that since government is an evil, every public service is suspect','Criticising a council\u2019s housing policy while praising the town itself','Asking whether an institution\u2019s structure still produces results','Separating loyalty to a country from approval of its rulers'],answer:0,why:'Paine called government necessary and judged it by results. Treating every public function as suspect drops the word *necessary*.'}]}},'common-sense_3':{pictureCaption:'A fine violin handed to hands far too big and rough to play it. Nobody asked whether they could. It was simply their turn.',bridge:'Idea 2 made government a tool to be judged by results. Here Paine judges one: rule passed down by birth.',basics:{title:'Born to the job is not good at the job',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'A NEW CAPTAIN',text:'Your team captain moves away. The coach says the new captain will be her little brother. Not because he is good. Just because he is her brother. He has never played.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'KINGS, LONG AGO',text:'Many countries once picked rulers this way. When a king died, his son became king, good at it or not. In 1776 a writer named Thomas Paine said this was a silly way to choose.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Being born into a job tells you nothing about whether you can do it. So picking leaders by birth is a lucky dip.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A LUCKY DIP',text:'At a fair, you put your hand in the lucky dip. Sometimes you pull out a prize. Sometimes you pull out junk. And with kings, you could not put the junk back.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A violin handed to big, rough hands. Nobody asked if they could play it. It was just their turn.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why this was clever',text:'People can argue for hours about what is fair. Paine asked a plainer question. Does this way of choosing get good leaders? That is hard to argue with. A brave, wise father does not always have a brave, wise son.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A shop owner hands her shop to her son. He has never worked in a shop. What would Paine point out?',options:['Being her son does not mean he can run it','He must be good at it, or he would not get it','Shops run themselves, so it does not matter'],answer:0,why:'Who your parents are tells you nothing about what you can do. That was Paine\u2019s whole point.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'HOW TO BEAT A SYSTEM',belief:'To bring down a bad system, prove that it is unjust.',reality:'Arguments about justice can stall for ever. Paine\u2019s sharpest attack on kings asked something duller: is this a good way to pick someone who can do the job?'},{kind:'idea',text:'Inheriting an office has no connection with being able to do it. Paine framed hereditary rule as a lottery, a practical objection that needs no theory of rights to land.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHY THE LOTTERY FAILS',nodes:['An able ruler','The office passes to his son','The ability does not','In time, an unfit ruler'],note:'And nothing in the system can remove him. That is the second half of the problem.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'A FAMILY FIRM',text:'A founder builds a firm on her own judgement. She retires and her son takes over. He may be capable or he may not. Nothing about how he got the job tells you which, and if the answer is no, who on the board is going to say so?'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A fine violin passed to hands that cannot play it. They got it by their place in the queue, not by their skill.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the practical form is stronger',text:'A moral objection invites a moral defence: tradition, duty, divine order. A competence objection asks only whether the selection method is likely to produce a capable holder. That question is very hard to answer with yes.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of a role near you filled by seniority or family. Ask the competence question: does this method reliably pick someone able to do it?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A club always makes its longest-serving member chair. You think the rule is bad. Which argument follows Paine?',options:['Length of service does not show who can chair a meeting well','It is unfair that newer members never get a turn to lead','Long-serving members already have more than enough say','The chair should be drawn at random to give everyone a chance'],answer:0,why:'It attacks the selection method on competence, which is harder to dismiss than an appeal to fairness.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'THE DONKEY AND THE LION',text:'In the section on monarchy and hereditary succession, Paine calls nature as a witness against inherited rule. Nature, he says, so often turns it into a joke, handing people a donkey where they expected a lion, that she plainly disapproves of it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Hereditary rule fails a plain test: nothing carries a ruler\u2019s abilities to an heir. Framed as competence rather than justice, the objection needs no theory of rights at all.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The point is statistical',text:'Whatever makes a ruler capable, nothing transmits it to a son. A system that fills the office by birth is therefore certain to produce incompetent holders at intervals, and, having no means of removing them, to keep them.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The sharper point',text:'He adds a historical observation. Every royal line began with somebody who took power by force. Trace a dynasty back and its sanctity turns out to be decoration, added afterwards to a seizure. For England he points to William the Conqueror.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'An instrument handed on by order of the queue, not by skill. The hands are wrong for it, and the line has no way to notice.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The argument is strongest against a monarch who actually governs; where the crown is ceremonial, competence matters far less. Nor is any method safe. Elections and appointments produce incompetents too. Paine\u2019s claim is comparative: birth is a method with no link at all to ability, and with no way to correct its failures.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Where in your own world is a role filled by a method with no link to the ability the role needs?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'A defender of monarchy replies that heirs are trained for the role from birth, so ability does pass. What is the best answer in Paine\u2019s terms?',options:['Training cannot guarantee aptitude, and an unfit heir still cannot be removed','Kings are unjust whatever their training, because all people are created equal','Training is irrelevant, because a ruler only needs to be obeyed','Most heirs have in fact been trained badly, so the reply fails'],answer:0,why:'The competence argument has two halves: no reliable transfer of ability, and no way to remove failure. Training touches only the transfer of ability, and only partly.'}]}},'common-sense_4':{pictureCaption:'The harbour gate is open and the tide is turning. The man with the loaded cart can go now, or wait and find it shut.',bridge:'Ideas 2 and 3 argued whether to change. This one argues when, and why waiting is never free.',basics:{title:'Waiting is a choice too',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'SWAPPING SEATS',prompt:'You want to change where you sit in class. When is it easiest?',options:['On the first day of term','In the summer, once you have thought it over','It is just as easy any time'],answer:0,reveal:'On day one, nobody has settled in. By summer, everyone has friends nearby. Moving then means breaking things up. The chance shrank while you waited.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Some changes get harder the longer you wait. So waiting is not the safe choice. It is a choice, and it has a cost.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'AMERICA, 1776',text:'In 1776, the king of Britain ruled America. Thomas Paine wrote a booklet saying America should rule itself. Lots of people half agreed, but said: not yet. Paine spent a big part of his booklet on one word: now.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why now?',text:'Paine said America had just enough to do it. It had enough people for an army. It had wood for ships. It owed no big debts. As it grew bigger and richer, he said, it would be harder to bring together.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The gate is open, but the tide is turning and the gate will close. The man with the cart can go now, or wait and find it shut.'},{kind:'steps',title:'How to answer \u201cnot yet\u201d',steps:['Say what you want to change.','Say what waiting will cost.','Say when the chance runs out.']},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friends want to start a band. The school will lend you the music room free this year. But they say: let\u2019s wait till next year. What would Paine do?',options:['Point out that the free room may be gone next year','Agree, since next year you will all play better','Give up, because they do not want it enough'],answer:0,why:'Paine\u2019s move is to make the cost of waiting plain. Then *not yet* has to beat a real price.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'NOT THIS QUARTER',text:'Everyone agrees the old system should go. Nobody argues for keeping it. And every quarter the switch is pushed back, because this quarter is busy. Two years later it is still running, bigger and harder to unpick.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Arguments for change usually lose to arguments for waiting unless the cost of waiting is made concrete. Paine argued about *now* rather than *whether*: a window exists, it will close, and delay is itself a decision.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Paine\u2019s case for now',text:'Roughly a quarter of *Common Sense* is about timing. America, he argued, had enough people to raise an army, enough timber and shipping to build a navy, and no national debt. Growth would erode those advantages. A larger, richer, more settled country would be harder to unite.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'THE SAME DECISION, TWICE',left:{title:'Now',items:['Less to protect','Fewer interests to settle','The means are already here']},right:{title:'Later',items:['More built on the old way','More people with a stake in it','The same means, more resistance']},note:'What makes change worth doing and what makes it possible decay together.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'An open harbour gate on a turning tide. The cart is loaded. Every minute spent deciding is part of the decision.'},{kind:'try',text:'Take one change you keep postponing. Write one line: what waiting costs, and the date by which it costs it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Your team keeps putting off a move to a better system. Which argument makes Paine\u2019s case?',options:['Each month adds records to move; by spring the switch costs twice as much','The new system is clearly better, so we should move to it at some point','Other teams have already switched, and we will look behind the times','We should wait for a quiet month so that the move goes smoothly'],answer:0,why:'It makes the cost of waiting concrete and dated. The others argue whether, or argue for waiting.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'OF THE PRESENT ABILITY',title:'Where the timing argument sits',text:'The fourth of the pamphlet\u2019s main sections turns from whether to separate to whether America could. Paine treats timing as a real question, not a flourish, and builds his answer from an inventory of what the colonies had at that moment.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The capacity to change direction is highest when there is least to protect. The conditions that make change worth doing are the ones that make it possible, and both decay together.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The inventory',text:'Enough men to raise an army. Timber and shipbuilding skill for a navy. No national debt. Each item was an asset that growth would dilute or tie up, and the present, he argues, is the true time to act, because the window narrows as the thing being defended grows.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE WINDOW CLOSES',nodes:['The colonies grow','More trade, property and settled interests','More people with something to lose','Harder to unite, harder to move'],note:'Delay is not a pause in the decision. It is a slow vote for the status quo.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A gate on a turning tide, and a man who has stopped to think. The thinking is not neutral: the gate is already moving.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Urgency is the oldest device in persuasion, and a manufactured deadline looks just like a real one. Paine\u2019s is credible because each item could be checked. His forecasts were also optimistic. The colonies never built a navy to match Britain\u2019s, and the decisive fleet at the war\u2019s end was French. A window argument is only as good as its inventory.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What decision are you treating as postponed that is in fact being made, every week, by default?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'REAL OR MANUFACTURED?',prompt:'Which of these is a genuine window argument in Paine\u2019s sense, rather than mere urgency?',options:['The lease ends in June; after that, moving means a new lease and fees','This offer is only open to the first hundred customers who sign today','Everyone else is already doing it, so we cannot afford to wait','If we do not act now, we may lose our nerve completely'],answer:0,why:'It names a dated, checkable change in conditions that raises the cost of acting later. The others create pressure without an inventory.'}]}},'common-sense_5':{pictureCaption:'An unsigned parcel on a wall, and strangers\u2019 hands reaching from every side. No name to judge it by, and nothing stopping it travelling.',bridge:'This overlaps with idea 1, which chose the reader. Here the subject is delivery: how the pamphlet physically reached that reader.',basics:{title:'No name, low price, easy to share',beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'JANUARY 1776',text:'A short booklet came out in the city of Philadelphia, in America. It said America should break away from the king of Britain. It did not give the writer\u2019s name. It said only that an Englishman wrote it.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'Why might the writer leave his name off?',options:['He was shy','So people would judge the idea, not him','He forgot'],answer:1,reveal:'The writer was Thomas Paine. Hardly anyone knew his name. With no name on it, readers had one thing to go on: did the reasons make sense?'},{kind:'idea',text:'How an idea gets around is part of the idea. Make it easy to judge on its own, and cheap and easy to pass on.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Made to be passed on',text:'The booklet was cheap enough to buy and hand round. It used plain words. In inns, people read it out loud to friends who could not read. One copy could reach a whole room.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A parcel with no name on it, and many hands reaching for it. It is easy to take and pass on.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE IDEAS BOX',text:'Your school has a box for ideas, and you can leave your name off. An idea in that box is judged on what it says, not on who said it. And a short, clear note gets read by more people.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'No name means you can say anything.',reality:'Leaving your name off is a way to let a good idea be judged fairly. It is never a cover for being mean.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You want your class to try a new game at break. Which plan follows Paine?',options:['Put the rules on one short page and make lots of copies','Write ten pages of rules and keep the only copy','Tell just your best friend and hope it spreads'],answer:0,why:'Short, cheap and easy to share is how Paine\u2019s booklet spread. An idea nobody can get hold of does nothing.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE WRITER\u2019S ASSUMPTION',belief:'Writing the case is the work. Getting it read is someone else\u2019s problem.',reality:'Paine designed the distribution of *Common Sense* as carefully as its argument. A case nobody can afford to read is a case that has not been made.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Publish so that the reasoning is judged on its own and can travel. Paine left off his name and kept the pamphlet cheap enough to pass around, and its effect cannot be separated from that design.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why no name',text:'An argument attached to a known name is judged partly by the name. Paine, a recent immigrant, had no name worth using. Signed only as the work of an Englishman, the pamphlet left readers nothing to assess but the reasoning.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why cheap and plain',text:'Price and prose followed the same logic. It was cheap enough to pass hand to hand, and written so a farmer could follow it. In taverns it was read aloud to people who could not read, so one copy could reach a room.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'No signature on the parcel, many strangers\u2019 hands. The design is in what is missing: no name to judge, no barrier to taking it.'},{kind:'try',text:'Take something you want people to act on. Cut it to the length people will actually forward, and remove anything that only works if they know who you are.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A nurse has a simple way to avoid a common dosing error and wants nurses everywhere to use it. Which approach follows Paine?',options:['A one-page checklist anyone can copy, shared freely between wards','A detailed report sent only to her own ward manager','A paid training course that nurses can sign up to attend','A long journal article that sits behind a subscription'],answer:0,why:'It is cheap, easy to pass on, and works whoever wrote it. That is the design that let *Common Sense* travel.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'PHILADELPHIA, JANUARY 1776',text:'*Common Sense* appeared with its author given only as an Englishman. It was reprinted widely and read aloud in taverns to listeners who could not read. The design that carried it there was as deliberate as the argument.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Distribution is part of the argument. Remove what lets readers judge the author instead of the case, and remove what stops the case being passed on.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHAT EACH CHOICE REMOVED',left:{title:'The choice',items:['No name','A low price','Plain prose','Reading aloud']},right:{title:'What it took away',items:['Judging the case by its author','The cost of passing it on','The need for a classical education','The need to read at all']}},{kind:'explain',title:'The prose was a claim too',text:'Pamphlets addressed to gentlemen assumed the decision belonged to gentlemen. Writing without classical quotation or legal vocabulary, in language a farmer could follow, made the opposite claim before any argument began: the decision was the reader\u2019s.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'An unsigned bundle on a public wall, taken by strangers. Nobody controls who reads it, which was the point.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Anonymity removes accountability as well as prejudice. It shelters a smear as readily as an honest case, and leaves nobody to answer for errors. The famous sales figures are also hard to verify, and some historians think they were inflated. The design mattered; how much is less certain than the legend.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Unsigned, a case is judged on its reasoning.','Cheap and plain, it can be passed on and read aloud.','Distribution is designed, not left to chance.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'WHERE IT FAILS',prompt:'In which case does Paine\u2019s anonymity work against the reader?',options:['An unsigned claim that a named official took bribes','An unsigned case for a new park, with its costs set out','An unsigned guide to fixing a common bicycle fault','An unsigned essay arguing for a shorter school day'],answer:0,why:'An accusation needs someone to answer for it. Without a name the reader cannot weigh the source, and the accused has nobody to reply to.'}]}},'common-sense_6':{pictureCaption:'A continent filling the sheet, and a small island off its edge holding it by one thread. Side by side, the arrangement argues against itself.',bridge:'Like the lottery argument in idea 3, this wins on arrangement rather than grievance. Here the trouble is size and distance.',basics:{title:'Too small and too far to steer',beats:[{kind:'picture',kicker:'LOOK AT THE SIZES',caption:'A huge land, and a tiny island at the edge. The island holds the big land on a thread. Something looks wrong before anyone says a word.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'THOMAS PAINE, 1776',text:'In 1776 the king of Britain ruled America. Britain is an island. America is a huge land far across the sea. A writer named Thomas Paine said America should rule itself.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Sometimes you do not need a long argument. Just show the thing at its real size. Paine said it made no sense for a small island to rule a whole continent for ever.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'A HEAD TEACHER OVERSEAS',text:'Imagine your school is run by a head teacher who lives across the ocean. You write to ask for a new door. Months later, she writes back. By then the door has fallen off and the rain got in. She is not mean. She is just too far away.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it worked',text:'Paine did not have to prove the king was bad. He just showed the set-up could not work. That is easy to see, and hard to argue with.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time something feels unfair, say it at its real size first. *One kid decides for thirty.* Let that sink in before you argue.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your football club is run by a parent who has never come to a game. Which line makes Paine\u2019s kind of point?',options:['One person who never watches decides for the whole team','That parent is lazy and does not care about us','Our team is the best, so we should be in charge'],answer:0,why:'It just says the set-up plainly, at its real size. The other two pick a fight about who is bad or best.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'SIGN-OFF FROM HEAD OFFICE',text:'Your branch runs every decision past a head office in another time zone. Approval takes a week. By the time it arrives the situation has moved on. Nobody there is careless. The arrangement is.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When an arrangement is indefensible, describe it plainly at its actual size before arguing against it. Paine set a continent beside an island and let the disproportion argue for him.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Built from distance',text:'Government has to respond. A petition crossing the Atlantic and back took months, and by the time an answer came the situation had changed. However well-meaning the authority, it could not govern well from there.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO ARGUE',left:{title:'From grievance',items:['The king is wicked','Must be proved, case by case','Can be met with concessions']},right:{title:'From arrangement',items:['The set-up cannot work','Visible at a glance','No concession fixes distance']},note:'Paine needed only the right-hand column.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A continent filling the sheet, an island at its edge holding it by one thread. You see the problem before reading a word.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick an arrangement you think is broken. Describe it in one sentence with real sizes and distances and no adjectives. See whether it argues for itself.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Tenants want repairs handled locally, not by a landlord abroad. Which line makes Paine\u2019s move?',options:['Every leak waits six weeks for a reply from four thousand miles away','The landlord does not care about any of us and never has','Other buildings on this street have far better repair services','We have a right to decent housing, and the law says so'],answer:0,why:'It shows the arrangement at its real size and lets the distance argue. The others rely on grievance, comparison or principle.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TWO CHARGES',prompt:'Which charge would a defender of British rule find harder to answer?',options:['The king has treated the colonies unjustly','An island cannot keep governing a continent'],answer:1,reveal:'Injustice can be denied, explained or met with concessions. A ratio of sizes cannot. Paine\u2019s best-known image moves the argument out of grievance and into arrangement.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Separation can rest on the configuration rather than on anyone\u2019s wickedness. Show that the arrangement cannot work at its actual size, and no reform within it answers you.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The two supports',text:'Paine backs the image twice. From distance: an appeal crossing the ocean and back took months, so the authority could not respond in time. From nature: nowhere, he says, is a satellite larger than the planet it circles, yet Britain and America reverse that order.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A vast landmass, a small island off the edge of the sheet, one taut thread between them. The proportion is the argument.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it resists concession',text:'A grievance can be met: repeal the tax, recall the governor. A configuration cannot be met with goodwill, because the problem is nobody\u2019s intent. That is why the passage made separation look like a matter of arrangement rather than of rebellion.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Vivid proportion can stand in for argument; a picture of mismatch persuades whether or not the mismatch matters. Distant rule was not literally impossible, either. Britain went on to govern far larger and more distant territories for generations. The real claim is narrower: rule from that distance could not be responsive, or rest on consent.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What arrangement around you is defended by pointing at the good intentions of the people in it, when the trouble is its shape?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which use of the scale argument is weakest?',options:['The smaller party should never decide for the larger, simply because it is smaller','An office overseas cannot handle emergencies that need answers within hours','A board that meets once a year cannot run a ward\u2019s daily rota','A volunteer living abroad cannot organise a local food bank\u2019s deliveries'],answer:0,why:'Paine\u2019s image worked because distance had a mechanism: delay. Size alone, with no account of why it breaks the arrangement, is the image without the argument.'}]}},'common-sense_7':{pictureCaption:'A cracked jug, filled as if nothing had happened. The water runs straight out along the crack. Going back does not mend the break.',bridge:'Idea 4 put a price on waiting. This one prices the option everyone called safe: patching things up.',basics:{title:'Keeping things the same is a choice too',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE SAFE CHOICE',belief:'Keeping things as they are is always the safe choice.',reality:'Not always. If the way things are caused the problem, keeping it means the problem comes back. That has a cost too.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE SHARED LOCKER',text:'You and a friend share a locker. Every week you fight about the mess. Every week you make up and keep sharing. And the next week, it is the same fight.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When people call one choice the safe one, check it. Ask what it will really bring, not just what it avoids.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'AMERICA, 1776',picture:!0,text:'By 1776, America and Britain were already fighting. Many people wanted to make up and go back to how things were. A writer named Thomas Paine said that hope was the fantasy. Going back would bring back the same problems.'},{kind:'explain',title:'His reasons',text:'People would still buy what America grew, he said, whoever was in charge. Being tied to Britain pulled America into Britain\u2019s wars. And once people have been killed, you cannot act as if nothing happened.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Make up',items:['Sounds safe','Same set-up as before','Same fights come back']},right:{title:'Change things',items:['Sounds risky','A new set-up','New problems, not the old ones']},note:'Both choices have risks. Only one was being counted.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your group project keeps going wrong because one person does all the talking. Someone says: let\u2019s just carry on. What would Paine ask?',options:['What will carrying on really bring us?','Who started this problem in the first place?','Why not just let the teacher decide?'],answer:0,why:'Carrying on sounds safe. Paine would count what it really brings, which here is the same problem again.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'EARLY 1776',prompt:'Fighting with Britain had begun the year before. Which option did cautious colonists see as the realistic one?',options:['Reconciliation: patch things up with Britain','Independence: break away for good'],answer:0,reveal:'Reconciliation looked like safety and independence like a gamble. Paine\u2019s sharpest move was to turn that round and call reconciliation the fantasy.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The option called realistic is not always the one that has been priced. Reconciliation, Paine argued, would restore the very relationship that had produced the conflict. It was a risk dressed as the status quo.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Counting what it delivers',text:'Trade, he argued, would find America whatever flag flew, because people buy what they need. So the supposed commercial dependence was imaginary. Meanwhile the connection drew America into Britain\u2019s wars, with no protection worth the name in return.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'PROMISED AND DELIVERED',left:{title:'What it promised',items:['Safety','Trade','Peace']},right:{title:'What it delivered',items:['The structure that caused the conflict','Trade that would come anyway','A share in Britain\u2019s wars']},note:'Paine asks what an option brings, not only what it avoids.'},{kind:'explain',title:'And no way back',picture:!0,text:'Blood had been shed. After that, he argued, the two sides could not resume their former footing. The relationship people wanted to restore no longer existed, so restoring it was never really on offer.'},{kind:'try',text:'Find a decision where staying put is called the cautious choice. Write down three things it will actually bring in the next year.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A firm keeps renewing a supplier whose delays cause its outages. The board calls renewal the safe option. What is Paine\u2019s challenge?',options:['Renewal keeps the cause of the outages, so price that risk too','Switching suppliers is always too dangerous during a crisis','The board should wait for the supplier to improve on its own','Any change is better than keeping a supplier nobody likes'],answer:0,why:'He does not say change always wins. He says the safe option has to be priced like any other.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'timeline',kicker:'BEFORE THE PAMPHLET',points:[{when:'April 1775',what:'Fighting begins at Lexington and Concord.'},{when:'July 1775',what:'Congress sends the King a petition seeking reconciliation.'},{when:'August 1775',what:'The King proclaims the colonies in rebellion.'},{when:'January 1776',what:'*Common Sense* is published.'}],note:'Reconciliation was still the respectable position when Paine attacked it.'},{kind:'term',word:'Status quo',meaning:'Latin for the existing state of affairs. In argument it is often treated as costless by default, so only the alternatives are asked to justify themselves.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The cautious course is a decision with consequences. Continuing an arrangement that generated the problem, and calling that neutral, usually means its consequences have not been counted.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The inversion',picture:!0,text:'Independence was supposed to be the gamble. Paine argues that reconciliation restores a relationship whose structural faults produced the conflict and would produce it again. The choice is not between safety and risk but between two risks, one disguised as the status quo.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The pricing',text:'He counts what reconciliation delivers. Commerce: America\u2019s goods would sell whatever flag flew. Security: the tie brought Britain\u2019s wars without real protection. Feeling: after killing, the old footing could not be resumed, any more than innocence can be restored once lost.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The inversion is easily abused. Anyone can call the cautious option a fantasy, which shifts the burden without doing the counting. Paine\u2019s version persuaded because he counted. But a pamphlet written to persuade gives its own option the gentler audit, and independence brought years of war whose costs he did not price with the same rigour.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Staying put is a decision, with consequences.','Price what an option delivers, not just what it avoids.','Count both options with the same strictness.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'COUNT BOTH SIDES',prompt:'A town debates patching an ageing bridge or closing it to rebuild. Which response applies Paine\u2019s point and respects its limit?',options:['Cost ten years of patching, and cost the rebuild just as strictly','Rebuild, because patching is the status quo and so a fantasy','Patch, because the cautious option needs no defence of its own','Keep patching until the bridge visibly fails, then decide'],answer:0,why:'Paine\u2019s move is to price the option called safe. The limit is to price the alternative with the same rigour, not to assume change wins.'}]}},'common-sense_8':{pictureCaption:'A crown resting on a written page. Paine\u2019s proposal: give the law the place a king had, so a document rules, not a person.',bridge:'Idea 2 made government a tool. Here Paine says what should hold its authority once the king is gone.',basics:{title:'Let the rules be the boss',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'WHO MAKES THE RULES?',text:'In a playground game, the biggest kid makes the rules. When he is losing, the rules change. When he is winning, they stay the same. Everyone else just has to put up with it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'It is fairer when the rules are the boss, not a person. Write them down, and they hold for everyone, even the strongest player.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'THOMAS PAINE, 1776',text:'In 1776, a writer named Thomas Paine wanted America to stop being ruled by a king. People asked: then who will be in charge? His answer was the law. In some lands the king is the law. In a free land, he said, the law should be king.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A crown sitting on a page of written rules. Paine\u2019s idea: the rules wear the crown, not a person.'},{kind:'explain',title:'A crown for a page',text:'Paine even suggested a show. Write the rules down. Put a crown on them, so everyone can see who is in charge. Then break the crown up, so no one can take it for himself later.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A RULE BOOK',text:'A good board game comes with a rule book. If you argue, you look it up. Nobody wins just by being loudest. And if a rule is bad, you can all agree to change it.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your class keeps arguing about who gets the computer. Which fix follows Paine\u2019s idea?',options:['Write a turn list that everyone has to follow','Let the oldest kid decide who goes next each day','Whoever gets there first keeps it all day'],answer:0,why:'The written list is the boss, so nobody gets more just by being bigger or faster.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'A PROPOSAL IN A PAMPHLET',text:'Asked who would rule an America without a king, Paine proposed a ceremony. Draw up a charter. Crown it in public, so the world can see that in America the law is king. Then break the crown up, so that no one can claim it afterwards.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Put authority in a document rather than a person, and make the difference visible. Under absolute rule the king is the law. In a free country, Paine argued, the law should be king, and there should be no other.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What a crown is for',text:'Paine understood that a crown is a device for making authority visible and obeyed. He did not propose abolishing that function. He proposed handing it to a text, because people need something to be loyal to, and a text can hold that loyalty.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'The king is law',items:['Authority sits in a person','Shifts with his will','Passes to his heir']},right:{title:'The law is king',items:['Authority sits in a text','Binds the rulers too','Can be amended openly']},note:'The same need for something to obey. A different thing to obey.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A crown on a written page. The ceremony makes the transfer visible: the page now holds the place a person used to.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'A TEAM\u2019S UNWRITTEN RULES',text:'A team\u2019s rules live in its founder\u2019s head. Whatever she says goes, and it varies by the week. Writing the rules down, and having her follow them too, moves authority from the person to the page.'},{kind:'try',text:'Find one rule in your team or household that exists only as somebody\u2019s preference. Write it down where everyone, that somebody included, can see it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A volunteer group keeps splitting over whatever its long-time leader wants. Which change applies Paine\u2019s idea?',options:['Adopt a written constitution that binds the leader too','Replace the leader with someone more reasonable','Hold a vote each time a disagreement comes up','Let the leader keep deciding, but explain each decision'],answer:0,why:'Replacing the person keeps authority in a person. Paine moves it to a document that binds whoever leads.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'A WORD FROM POLITICS',word:'Sovereignty',meaning:'The final authority in a political community: whatever, or whoever, has the last word. Under a monarchy it sits in the person of the king.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'THOUGHTS ON THE PRESENT STATE',title:'The passage',text:'Answering the charge that America would have no king, Paine proposes a day on which a charter is brought out and crowned, so the world will know that in America the law is king. To prevent misuse afterwards, the crown is then to be broken up and scattered among the people.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Sovereignty can move from a person to a written text. Keep the visible apparatus of authority, but point it at a document that binds rulers too and can be amended.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Theatre with a purpose',text:'The ceremony is not decoration. A crown makes authority visible and obeyed, and people need something to give their loyalty to. Paine transfers that function rather than abolishing it. A document can hold the loyalty, and unlike a person it can be amended.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A crown resting on a page. What a crown does, making authority visible, is kept. Only what it sits on has changed.'},{kind:'limits',text:'A document does not enforce itself; courts, officials and citizens willing to obey it do. The law can be king and still be unjust: the Constitution later adopted protected slavery. The rule of law constrains rulers without guaranteeing good rules. And American constitutional practice drew on many sources, not on this passage alone.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Authority can sit in a text rather than a person.','Keep the ceremony; change what it points at.','A written law binds rulers, but must itself be just.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HARD CASE',prompt:'A new leader announces that the constitution will now mean whatever the leader says it means. In Paine\u2019s terms, what has happened?',options:['The king has become the law again, whatever the text says','Nothing important, so long as the text itself is unchanged','The law is still king, because a constitution still exists','The document has been amended in the ordinary way'],answer:0,why:'Paine\u2019s point is where authority sits. A text that means whatever one person says has put a person back on the throne.'}]}},'common-sense_9':{pictureCaption:'The keystone goes in last. It holds only because every plain stone beneath it was laid first, as Paine\u2019s big claim rests on his counting.',bridge:'Idea 4 counted armies, timber and debt. This closing claim is what all that counting paid for.',basics:{title:'Save the big claim for last',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'TWO SPEECHES',text:'Two kids give speeches to be class rep. The first starts: *I will change this school for ever!* People roll their eyes. The second gives three plain plans first. Then she says: *this could be our best year ever.* People believe her.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Save your biggest claim for the end. And only make it once your plain reasons have earned it.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'THOMAS PAINE, 1776',text:'Thomas Paine wrote a booklet in 1776 saying America should stop being ruled by Britain\u2019s king. Most of it is plain reasons: people, ships, wood, money. Only near the end does he make a huge claim.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The huge claim',text:'Near the end, Paine says they now had the chance to start the world again. Nothing like it, he says, had happened since Noah and the great flood in the Bible. It is a bold line. It works because of all the plain pages before it.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A BLOCK TOWER',text:'You can put a flag on top of a tower of blocks. But the flag only stands up high because of all the blocks under it. Put it on the floor first, and it is just a flag.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Making a big point',steps:['Give your plain reasons first.','Check that each one is true.','Then, and only then, say the big thing.']},{kind:'picture',caption:'The top stone of an arch goes in last. It only holds because every plain stone under it was laid first. A big claim works the same way.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You want your family to get a dog. When should you say: it will make us all happier?',options:['At the end, after your plan for walks and food','At the start, before any boring details','Never, because big claims are always silly'],answer:0,why:'The big claim lands once the plain reasons have paid for it.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO ORDERS',left:{title:'Big claim first',items:['Sounds like a pitch','Every detail after it is doubted','Nothing has earned it']},right:{title:'Big claim last',items:['Sounds like a conclusion','Every detail before it supports it','The reader has done the counting']},note:'The same claim. A different order, and a different reception.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Save the largest claim for the end, and make it only if the pages before it have paid for it. Paine argued practically for some forty pages before saying the colonists could begin the world over again.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the claim says',text:'In its closing pages *Common Sense* says the colonists had it in their power to begin the world over again, that nothing like this had happened since Noah, and that a new world was about to be born. It is not argument. It is scale.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it had been earned',text:'By then the reader had been through navies, timber, debt, distance and the failures of inherited rule. The vast claim arrives as the sum of checkable points, not as a substitute for them. Placed first, it would have read as a sermon.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The keystone goes in last and holds only because every plain stone beneath it is already in place. Set first, it would simply fall.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'A FUNDING PITCH',text:'A charity asks a council for money. Opening with how it will transform the town loses the room. Opening with the waiting list, the costs and the pilot results, then closing on what the town could become, gets a hearing.'},{kind:'try',text:'Take your next proposal. Move its most ambitious sentence to the end, then check: does everything before it actually support it?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A head teacher wants support for a new timetable. Where should the line *this could change how our school works* go?',options:['Last, after the evidence on attendance and results','First, to grab attention before the details begin','In the middle, to wake readers up halfway through','Nowhere, since big claims always weaken a case'],answer:0,why:'The large claim persuades when the plain evidence has already done the work. Without it, the line is a slogan.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE CLOSING PAGES',title:'The largest claim available',text:'In an appendix added to a later edition, the pamphlet changes register. The colonists, Paine says, have it in their power to begin the world over again; nothing like this has happened since the days of Noah; a new world is about to be born. After forty pages of practical argument, this is pure scale.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A claim of the largest scale persuades only when the argument before it has paid for it. Paine spends the pamphlet counting, then spends the count on one passage of vision.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Specific, not sentimental',text:'His case for exceptionalism is concrete. A new government is normally built out of an old one, inheriting its structures and debts. The colonies had enough organisation to function and no entrenched order to dismantle. That combination is what he claims is rare.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'WHERE THE PAMPHLET SITS',low:'Plain counting: navies, timber, debt',high:'Vision: begin the world over again',note:'Nearly all of it sits at the left. The right-hand end is reached once, near the close, and is believable because of the rest.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A keystone set last locks the arch; set first, it falls. The passage of vision is the keystone, and the pages of counting are the arch beneath it.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Such claims are rarely true, and most calls to begin again come from people who have not counted what they would have to keep. Paine\u2019s new world was also not new for everyone. Native nations already lived there, and the republic that followed kept slavery. The fresh start was offered to some and not to others.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What is the biggest claim you want to make this year? List the plain points that would have to come before it.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Large claims persuade when the argument has paid for them.','Paine\u2019s exceptionalism rested on specifics, not sentiment.','Ask whom a fresh start is fresh for.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which use of the closing move would Paine\u2019s method reject?',options:['A founder who opens by promising to reinvent an industry, then gives no figures','A report that ends on a bold vision after ten pages of costed evidence','A speech that saves its widest claim for after the practical case','An essay that cuts its grand finale because the evidence was too thin'],answer:0,why:'The method is to earn the claim first. A grand promise with nothing counted beneath it is the claim without the payment.'}]}}}},2963,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'compound-interest-slowly_1':{pictureCaption:'The hands rule a careful straight line. The real growth curves up and off the table, far past where the ruler points.',basics:{title:'Growth that grows',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A BANK PAYS 7P FOR EVERY \xa31',prompt:'You put \xa3100 in. Each year the bank adds 7p for every \xa31 in there. You leave it for 30 years. About how much is there now?',options:['About \xa3310','About \xa3500','About \xa3760'],answer:2,reveal:'It is about \xa3760. Most people guess near \xa3310. That guess adds \xa37 a year, every year. But the bank pays on the whole pot, and the pot keeps getting bigger.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When money grows, the growth starts to grow too. So it speeds up over time. Our gut guesses it grows by the same amount each year, and guesses far too low.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Your \xa3100, ten years at a time',steps:['Start: \xa3100.','After 10 years: about \xa3200.','After 20 years: about \xa3390.','After 30 years: about \xa3760.'],note:'It roughly doubles every ten years. Each doubling is bigger than the last.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A FAMILY TREE',text:'You have 2 parents. They had 4 parents between them. Those 4 had 8. Go back ten steps and there are over 1,000 names. Each step doubles the one before, so it gets huge fast.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The hands draw a straight line, the way our gut thinks. The real growth curves up and runs right off the table.'},{kind:'try',text:'Ask a grown-up to help you on a phone. Type 100. Then times 1.07, and press equals. Do that again, 30 times in all. Watch the jumps get bigger.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Two friends each put \xa350 in the same bank. One takes it out after 10 years. One waits 20. Which is true?',options:['The one who waits has about twice as much','The one who waits has a little more','They end up with the same'],answer:0,why:'The money roughly doubles every ten years. Ten more years is one more doubling, so the pot about doubles again.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'TWO SAVERS, SAME RATE',text:'One saver puts in \xa31,000 a year from 25 to 34, then stops for good. Another starts at 35 and puts in \xa31,000 every year until 65. Both earn seven per cent. At 65 the first has about \xa3112,500. The second has about \xa3101,000, after paying in three times as much.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Growth on growth is not intuitive. We picture a steady climb, so we badly underestimate long compounding, and the cost of waiting never feels like anything.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'SEVEN PER CENT, THIRTY YEARS',left:{title:'What the gut does',items:['Adds 7% thirty times','About 210% gained','Ends near three times']},right:{title:'What the sum does',items:['Doubles about every ten years','Three doublings','Ends near 7.6 times']},note:'The gap between the columns is the gap that makes waiting feel free.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why waiting costs half, not a third',text:'In the straight-line picture, ten years out of thirty is a third of the result. In the real sum, starting ten years late drops the last doubling, and the last doubling is the biggest. Thirty years turns \xa3100 into about \xa3760. Twenty turns it into about \xa3390.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The ruler gives the line your gut draws. The terracotta curve is the arithmetic, already off the edge of the table.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The fix is not a better gut',text:'Nobody really develops an intuition for this. The piece\u2019s advice is plainer: run the number once. Seeing the eightfold figure on a screen does what being told about compounding never quite does.'},{kind:'try',text:'Put your current monthly saving into any compound calculator at seven per cent, once to 65 and once starting ten years later. Look at the difference between the two final figures.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A friend says starting their pension at 40 instead of 30 only loses a quarter of the 35-year result. What has gone wrong?',options:['They pictured a straight line; the delay removes the biggest doubling','They forgot that later contributions are usually larger','They are right, since ten years is less than a third of thirty-five','They ignored inflation, which makes the late start cost nothing'],answer:0,why:'The straight-line picture treats each year as equal. Under compounding the years missed shorten the series at its expensive end, so the loss is about half, not a quarter.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHAT THE PIECE TAKES ON',text:'Everyone has heard that compounding is powerful. The piece starts from the observation that hearing it changes almost nothing. People can repeat the slogan and still put off saving, because nothing in their sense of time registers what a decade of delay removes.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Intuition is linear and compounding is not. The shortfall between the two is not a curiosity: it is the exact mechanism by which delay feels free while costing about half the outcome.'},{kind:'term',word:'Doubling time',meaning:'How long a growing sum takes to double. At seven per cent a year it is a little over ten years. Thinking in doublings rather than percentages is the quickest way to see a long series.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THIRTY YEARS AT SEVEN PER CENT',nodes:['\xa3100 today','About \xa3200 at year ten','About \xa3390 at year twenty','About \xa3760 at year thirty'],note:'The linear guess for year thirty, about \xa3310, is passed in year seventeen.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the early saver wins',text:'Pay \xa31,000 a year from 25 to 34 and stop, and at seven per cent you have about \xa3112,500 at 65. Pay the same from 35 to 64 and you have about \xa3101,000. Each early pound compounds for over thirty years, through three or more doublings. Most late pounds get far fewer. It sounds like a trick. It is the sum working through.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Every figure here assumes a steady seven per cent, which no real investment delivers. Returns arrive unevenly, and for anyone still paying in, a bad decade near the end hurts more than one at the start. The early-saver result holds at seven per cent; at six or below, the late saver\u2019s thirty years of payments win. The calculation shows the shape of the cost of delay, not a promise of the amount.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What are you postponing on the grounds that a few years cannot make much difference? Which kind of growth is it?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A debt grows at seven per cent a year and nothing is paid. Someone plans to deal with it in ten years. What does the piece\u2019s logic say?',options:['The debt will roughly double, so the delay costs far more than it feels','The debt will grow by about seventy per cent, as ten times seven suggests','Delay is neutral, since the rate is the same whenever you pay','Compounding only applies to savings, so the comparison fails'],answer:0,why:'The mathematics does not care which side of the account it is on. Seven per cent for ten years is close to a doubling, and the linear guess of seventy per cent is the same intuition error.'}]}},'compound-interest-slowly_2':{pictureCaption:'A row of jars, one per stretch of years. Most hold a few coins; the last overflows. A long series does its work at the end.',bridge:'Idea 1 showed that growth on growth outruns the gut. This one asks when that growth arrives: mostly at the end.',basics:{title:'The last years do the most',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'YEAR THREE',text:'You have saved for three years. The bank has added a few pounds. That is all. It feels like nothing is happening. You think about taking it all out and buying a bike.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Growth that grows on itself is slow at first and fast at the end. The quiet early years are not wasted. They build the pile that the big years grow from.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'\xa3100 AT 7P PER \xa31, 30 YEARS',left:{title:'First 20 years',items:['Grows by about \xa3290','Slow, year after year']},right:{title:'Last 10 years',items:['Grows by about \xa3370','Fast, because the pile is big']},note:'Ten years at the end beat twenty at the start.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'JAR BY JAR',caption:'One jar for each stretch of years. Most jars hold a few coins. The last one spills over. The end of the row is where the growth is.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A SNOWBALL',text:'Roll a small snowball. Each turn picks up a thin layer, so it grows slowly. Once it is big, each turn picks up a lot, because there is more of it touching the snow.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why people give up',text:'The early years show the least. So right when sticking with it matters most, it looks the most pointless. That is why most people who fail at saving do not make a mistake. They just stop.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of a pile you are building: savings, a skill, a big project. Write down why the slow part still counts.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Sam saves for five years and the pot has barely grown. Sam wants to quit. What does this idea say?',options:['The slow years build the base the fast years need','Five slow years show the plan is not working','Sam should wait for it to go faster, then add more'],answer:0,why:'Growth on growth always starts slow. The big jumps come later, and only if the pile is still there.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'If five years of saving have barely moved the balance, the plan is not working.',reality:'That is what a working plan looks like at year five. In a long compounding series the visible growth comes late, because each year\u2019s growth is applied to a larger base.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The last decade does most of the work. At typical long-run returns, the final ten years of a thirty-year run add more than the first twenty combined, so the early years matter for the base they build.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'\xa31,000 A YEAR AT 7%, 30 YEARS',left:{title:'Years 1 to 20',items:['\xa320,000 paid in','About \xa323,900 of growth']},right:{title:'Years 21 to 30',items:['\xa310,000 paid in','About \xa347,200 of growth']},note:'Half the deposits, twice the growth. Most of it is growth on the base laid down earlier.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'THE SHAPE OF THE SERIES',caption:'One jar per stretch of years. Seven near-empty jars, then one overflowing. Most of the absolute growth sits at the end, because that is where the base is largest.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the common failure is quitting',text:'The returns are least visible exactly when persistence matters most. So the evidence in front of you at the moment you decide argues for stopping. The piece\u2019s point is that most people do not get the sums wrong. They abandon the series before it reaches the part that pays.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'WHAT YOU SEE, WHEN',low:'Early years: little to see, most structural work',high:'Late years: most growth, built on the early base',note:'The years that look emptiest are laying the foundation.'},{kind:'try',text:'Find the first year of any long series you are in, savings or otherwise. Write down what it has built so far that the later years will grow from.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Two people will each invest the same \xa35,000 once. One does it now, the other in ten years. At seven per cent, what does this idea predict at the thirty-year mark?',options:['The early \xa35,000 ends up about twice the size of the late one','The late \xa35,000 catches up, since the rate is identical','They differ by about a third, the share of years missed','The late one is ahead, because it misses the slow years'],answer:0,why:'The early money gets one more doubling, and it comes at the expensive end where each doubling is largest.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'ONE SUM, THIRTY YEARS AT 7%',prompt:'Which adds more to a single \xa3100 invested once: the first twenty years, or the last ten?',options:['The first twenty','Roughly the same','The last ten'],answer:2,reveal:'The first twenty years add about \xa3287. The last ten add about \xa3374. Each year\u2019s growth sits on a larger base, so the end of the series outweighs its much longer beginning.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A compounding series is back-loaded. Its absolute gains cluster at the end, while its structural work is done at the start, in years that feel like nothing is happening.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'WHERE THE GAIN SITS',caption:'Jars for successive periods of the same series. The early ones barely change; the last overflows. The gain is back-loaded because each period grows a larger base.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The argument it supports',text:'The piece uses this to explain why starting early beats contributing heavily later. A late start does not trim the series evenly. It removes doublings, and the doublings it removes are the ones you would have collected at the large end.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW SERIES GET ABANDONED',nodes:['Early years show little','Quitting looks reasonable','The base is withdrawn','The big late years never come'],note:'Nothing here is a miscalculation. Each step follows from what is visible at the time.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Beyond money',text:'The piece suggests the same shape turns up in skills and in any asset that accumulates on itself. Where a thing really does build on what is already there, the early stretch will look unrewarding, and that look is not evidence against it.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The pattern depends on the rate. For one lump sum, the last ten of thirty years beat the first twenty only above about five per cent a year. It also assumes the late years arrive: money needed early, or a crash near the end, cuts off the payoff. And many skills run the other way, with quick early gains that later slow.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Absolute growth clusters in the final years.','The early years build the base those years grow from.','The usual failure is quitting during the quiet part.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which use of this idea stretches it past what it supports?',options:['Staying in a failing venture because results always come at the end','Keeping a pension running through ten quiet years','Starting a small regular saving now rather than a bigger one later','Expecting the visible gains of a long series to come late'],answer:0,why:'Back-loading is a property of things that genuinely build on themselves. It cannot tell you that a venture which is not compounding will pay off if you wait.'}]}},'compound-interest-slowly_3':{pictureCaption:'A small hole near the base, and a puddle far bigger than the hole suggests. A small yearly fee, left for decades, drains like this.',bridge:'The arithmetic that makes returns grow on themselves works just as well on the fee taken from them.',basics:{title:'Small fees grow too',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'JUST 1P PER \xa31',belief:'A fee of 1p for every \xa31 a year only ever costs you a tiny bit.',reality:'It is taken every year, from the whole pot. And each penny taken never gets to grow. Over a long time, that adds up to a big slice.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A fee is taken from your money every year. So it grows the same way your money does, just in the wrong direction.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'\xa3100 FOR 30 YEARS',text:'Your \xa3100 grows by 7p per \xa31 each year. With no fee, after 30 years you have about \xa3760. With a fee of 1p per \xa31, it only grows by 6p. After 30 years you have about \xa3570. The small fee took about a quarter.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The hole in the barrel is tiny. The puddle is huge. A small drip, left long enough, makes a big mess.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it is easy to miss',text:'You never hand the fee over. It is taken out for you, quietly. And 1p sounds like nothing. So it never feels like you paid.'},{kind:'try',text:'If you have a savings account, ask a grown-up to help you find out what it pays and what it costs each year.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Two banks both grow your money by 7p per \xa31. One takes 1p per \xa31 a year as a fee. After 30 years, what happens?',options:['The one with the fee ends up about a quarter smaller','The one with the fee ends up just a tiny bit smaller','They end up the same, as both grow by 7p'],answer:0,why:'The fee comes off the whole pot every year, and what it takes never grows. Over 30 years that is about a quarter.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A 1% FEE, THIRTY YEARS',prompt:'A fund grows at seven per cent and charges one per cent a year. Over thirty years, about how much of the final balance does the fee remove?',options:['About 1%','About 10%','About a quarter'],answer:2,reveal:'About a quarter. \xa310,000 at seven per cent becomes about \xa376,000. At six per cent, after the fee, it becomes about \xa357,400.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A percentage fee compounds against you on exactly the mathematics that makes returns compound for you. Every pound it takes is a pound that never compounds.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why one per cent is not one per cent',text:'The fee is charged on the whole balance every year, not on the gain. So it scales up as the balance grows, and each year\u2019s fee also removes all the growth that money would have earned for the rest of the period.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'THE SAME FEE, TWO WAYS',left:{title:'As a share of assets',items:['1% a year','Sounds negligible']},right:{title:'As a share of the gain',items:['About 14% of a 7% return','Every year']},note:'Same fee. One of these numbers is the one worth looking at.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why nobody feels it',text:'One per cent sounds small, it is quoted per year, and it is deducted automatically. You never make a payment, so it is never experienced as one. The presentation, more than the size, is what keeps it out of sight.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A hole too small to worry about, and a puddle that runs off the edge of the frame. Time turns the one into the other.'},{kind:'try',text:'Find the total annual cost of each fund or account you hold. The figure is usually published. Then divide it by the return you expect, to see it as a share of your gain.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Fund A charges 0.2% a year, Fund B charges 1.2%, and both hold similar assets. How should you think about the gap?',options:['As a seventh of a 7% gain each year, compounding','As one per cent a year, which is too small to matter','As a one-off cost, paid once when you first invest','As irrelevant, because the dearer fund will earn it back'],answer:0,why:'A one-point gap against a seven per cent return is about fourteen per cent of the gain each year, and it compounds like everything else.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'flow',kicker:'\xa310,000 OVER THIRTY YEARS',nodes:['No fee at 7%: about \xa376,100','1% fee: about \xa357,400','1.5% fee: about \xa349,800'],note:'One per cent takes about a quarter of the final balance; one and a half takes about a third.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE SAME SUM, REVERSED',text:'After two ideas spent on what compounding does for you, the piece turns the formula around. Nothing about the mathematics changes when the percentage is a charge. It still applies to the whole balance, every year, for as long as the money is invested.'},{kind:'idea',text:'An annual fee is a negative rate of return applied to the whole balance. Over decades it removes a fraction of the final total far larger than its headline percentage.'},{kind:'term',word:'Total annual cost',meaning:'Everything an investment charges in a year, expressed as a percentage of what you hold. It is the figure to compare, rather than any single headline charge.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Reframing the fee',text:'The piece\u2019s practical move is to express the fee as a share of the expected return, not of assets. One per cent of a balance growing at seven is about fourteen per cent of the gain. Seen that way, the size of the claim the fee makes on your money is plain.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A small hole and a vast puddle. The hole is the headline rate; the puddle is what thirty years of it removes.'},{kind:'limits',text:'A fee is wasted only if it buys nothing. A dearer arrangement that keeps someone invested through a fall may be worth it, and the cheapest option is not automatically right. The quarter also depends on the seven per cent: returns are uncertain, while the fee is not. That asymmetry strengthens the point rather than weakening it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'FIND THE FLAW',prompt:'An adviser says their 1.5% fee hardly matters, because in a good year you barely notice it. What is wrong with this?',options:['The fee is taken in every year and compounds whether you notice it or not','The fee is only charged in the years when returns happen to be positive','A 1.5% fee is too small to have any effect over long periods','Bad years matter less than good years in a long series'],answer:0,why:'Not noticing a charge is a fact about presentation, not about its cost. The fee comes off the whole balance every year and removes all the growth that money would have earned.'}]}},'compound-interest-slowly_4':{pictureCaption:'Nothing has been taken from any sack, yet each holds less than the last. Cash under inflation keeps its number and loses its contents.',bridge:'A fee at least shows up on a statement. Inflation compounds against you with no statement at all.',basics:{title:'Money that shrinks without moving',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'A \xa31 BAR OF CHOCOLATE',text:'A bar costs \xa31 today. Say prices go up by 3p for every \xa31 each year. Next year the bar is \xa31.03. Keep going. After 24 years the same bar costs about \xa32.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When prices go up, the same money buys less. Money just sitting there does not stay the same. It shrinks in what it can buy, a bit more each year.'},{kind:'term',word:'Inflation',meaning:'Prices across the shops going up over time. When there is inflation, each \xa31 buys a little less than it did before.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Nobody took anything out of these sacks. But each one holds less than the one before. Money that sits still is like this.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why you don\u2019t notice',text:'You still have \xa320 in your money box. Nothing was taken out. Nobody sent a note. But the things you want now cost more. The number stayed the same. What it buys got smaller.'},{kind:'explain',title:'So is cash bad?',text:'No. You need some money you can grab fast, for a broken phone or a trip. That should be cash. But money you won\u2019t need for years shrinks if it just sits.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your gran keeps \xa3100 in a drawer for 24 years. Prices rise 3p per \xa31 each year. What has happened?',options:['It is still \xa3100, but buys about half as much','It is still \xa3100, and buys just the same','It has shrunk to about \xa350 in the drawer'],answer:0,why:'The notes did not change. Prices about doubled, so the same \xa3100 now buys about half of what it did.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE BALANCE THAT NEVER MOVES',text:'You have \xa310,000 in a current account paying nothing. You check it every month for years. It is always \xa310,000. Nothing has been deducted, and no statement shows a loss. It feels like the one safe part of your finances.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Inflation applies the compounding formula in reverse to what your money buys. Cash that earns less than inflation is not static. Its purchasing power is shrinking, compounded, which is why a return can look positive and still be a real loss.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The arithmetic',text:'At three per cent inflation, prices roughly double in twenty-four years. Your \xa310,000 then buys about what \xa34,900 buys today. Over a forty-year working life it loses more than half its purchasing power, without any visible event.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it is underweighted',text:'The loss has no transaction. Nothing is deducted, the balance never changes, and only what the balance buys has moved. People react to losses they see, and this one, by its nature, is never seen.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO KINDS OF RISK',left:{title:'Cash',items:['No ups and downs','A slow, near-certain real decline']},right:{title:'Invested',items:['Real ups and downs','A chance of growth above prices']},note:'Holding cash is a choice of which risk to take, not a way of avoiding risk.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Every sack untouched, every sack smaller than the last. That is a balance that never moves, measured in what it buys.'},{kind:'try',text:'Work out the cash buffer you genuinely need for emergencies. Then name, honestly, what any cash beyond that is for and how long it will sit.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Your savings account pays 2% while prices rise 3%. What is happening to the money in real terms?',options:['It is losing about 1% of its buying power a year','It is growing at 2%, so it is quite safe','It is holding steady, since the balance keeps rising','It is losing 3% a year, the full rate of inflation'],answer:0,why:'The balance rises, but prices rise faster. The real return is roughly the interest minus inflation, here about minus one per cent a year.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE SAFE OPTION',belief:'Money kept in cash is safe, because the balance can never go down.',reality:'The balance is the wrong measure. What it buys falls every year that prices rise faster than any interest it earns, and that fall compounds.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Inflation is compounding in reverse. Cash that earns less than inflation eliminates volatility and in exchange guarantees a slow real decline, so choosing it is choosing a risk, not avoiding one.'},{kind:'term',word:'Nominal and real',meaning:'A nominal amount is the number on the statement. A real amount is what that number can buy, after inflation. A nominal gain can be a real loss.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The piece\u2019s figures',text:'At three per cent, prices roughly double in twenty-four years, so cash held that long buys about half what it did. Over a forty-year working life it buys under a third. None of this ever appears as a deduction, which the piece argues is why it is so consistently underweighted.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A shelf of sacks, none of them opened, each slumped lower than the last. The loss is real and there is no moment at which it happened.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What cash is for',text:'The piece is explicit that cash has a real and important role: a buffer, so that you are never forced to sell investments at a bad moment. Its claim is narrower. Cash is a poor place for money you will not need for twenty years.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Interest offsets part of the decline, and in some periods cash has paid close to inflation or above it. Inflation is not a steady three per cent. And the alternative is not free: investments can fall sharply just when money is needed. The point is the trade-off, not a rule that all cash is waste.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'WEIGH IT',prompt:'Someone will need \xa320,000 for a house deposit in eight months and has it in cash. By the piece\u2019s argument, is that a mistake?',options:['No: a short, known need is what cash is for, despite slow inflation loss','Yes: any cash loses value, so it should all be invested at once','Yes: inflation will have halved its value by the time they buy','No: cash cannot lose value as long as the balance stays the same'],answer:0,why:'The piece objects to long-term money in cash. Over eight months the inflation loss is small, while a market fall could wreck the deposit.'}]}},'compound-interest-slowly_5':{pictureCaption:'The lever is simple. The hand is clenched. What makes this hard is the person at the switch, not the mechanism.',bridge:'Idea 2 warned that people quit during the quiet years. This one is about how to stop that happening.',basics:{title:'Knowing is easy. Keeping going is hard.',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'SEEDS IN A POT',text:'You plant seeds. A week later nothing shows. So you dig one up to check. Then another. The ones you dig up can\u2019t grow. The hard part was never planting. It was leaving them alone.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The sums behind growing money are easy to learn. What is hard is to keep going when it looks like nothing is happening, or when it goes down for a while.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The lever is easy to pull. Look at the fist. The hard bit is the person, not the lever.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why we stop',text:'Saving can look like it does nothing for months. Sometimes the pot even drops. Each time, it feels smart to stop or change the plan. But each stop breaks the growth that was building.'},{kind:'steps',title:'How to make it easy',steps:['Set it up so money goes in by itself each month.','Write your plan down before things go up or down.','Don\u2019t check every day. Once in a while is fine.'],note:'These work because they take the choice away.'},{kind:'try',text:'If you get pocket money, ask a grown-up if some of it can go straight into savings each week, before you can spend it.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Mia saves \xa32 each week. For two months the pot hardly grows. What does this idea say helps most?',options:['Make the \xa32 go in by itself, so she does not have to choose','Learn more maths, so she sees why it works','Check the pot every day, so she knows how it is going'],answer:0,why:'The sums are not the hard part. The risk is that she stops, so making the \xa32 go in by itself takes the choice away.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'EIGHTEEN MONTHS OF NOTHING',text:'You have paid into a fund every month for a year and a half. It is worth slightly less than you put in. The news is gloomy. Pausing for a few months, until things settle, seems like the obviously sensible thing to do.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The behaviour is harder than the maths. Most of the return from a long strategy is the reward for not interrupting it. And interrupting it is what feels most urgent at the worst moments.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Locally reasonable, collectively destructive',text:'Holding through a fall. Paying in while returns are negative. Not moving your money around after a good year. Each break from these feels sensible on its own day. Added up, they break the series. The piece points to evidence that investors earn less than the funds they hold, and calls the gap large and persistent.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Structural fixes',steps:['Make the contribution automatic.','Write a policy in advance: what you hold, and when you would change it.','Check the balance less often.'],note:'None of these improves the decision. They remove it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A lever that needs one pull a month, and a hand clenched over it. The mechanism is trivial. The hesitation is the whole problem.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why not just learn to decide better?',text:'Because this is a decision you will reliably get wrong, and knowing that does not stop it. Education leaves the decision in place. Structure takes it away. Then carrying on needs nothing, and stopping needs deliberate effort.'},{kind:'try',text:'Set your saving up as a standing order or automatic payment. Then carrying on needs no decision, and stopping needs a deliberate one.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A friend keeps pausing contributions after bad months. Which advice fits the piece best?',options:['Automate the payment and check the balance far less often','Read more about compounding until the logic feels convincing','Watch the market closely so you can pause at the right time','Contribute only in months when returns have been positive'],answer:0,why:'The friend\u2019s problem is not knowledge. Removing the monthly decision stops the interruptions in a way that understanding does not.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHERE THE PIECE ENDS UP',text:'After four ideas of arithmetic, the piece lands on its real claim. The mathematics of compounding can be taught in an afternoon. The behaviour it demands, doing nothing when doing something feels most urgent, is what almost nobody manages.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Compounding rewards the absence of interruption, and the moments that invite interruption are exactly the ones that matter. So the useful fixes remove the decision rather than try to improve it.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE INTERRUPTION LOOP',nodes:['A fall, or months of nothing','Acting feels urgent','Pause, sell or switch','The series is broken','Next fall feels worse'],loop:!0,note:'Every step is locally reasonable. The loop is what destroys the return.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The evidence it cites',text:'The piece points to comparisons of investor returns with fund returns: people earn less than the funds they own. It describes the gap as large and persistent, and reads it as the cost of those well-meant interventions. It does not give figures, so the size is the piece\u2019s judgement.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why structure, not education',text:'Automatic contributions, a written policy and fewer checks share one property: they work without the investor deciding well in the moment. For a decision you reliably get wrong, removing it is more dependable than improving it.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Automation locks in whatever plan you set, including a bad one. Circumstances change: a lost job, a new need, a buffer that has run out. A written policy should say when it is reviewed. The rule is that market mood cannot reopen the plan. Changes in your own life still should.'},{kind:'recap',points:['The arithmetic is easy. Not interrupting it is hard.','Each interruption feels sensible on the day.','Remove the decision: automate, write it down, look less.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'MOOD OR CIRCUMSTANCE?',prompt:'Which of these should be allowed to change an automated monthly contribution?',options:['Losing your job and needing the money for rent','A fall in the market that made the news this week','A sense that prices are about to drop further','A year of returns well below what you hoped for'],answer:0,why:'A lost income is a real change in your situation. The other three are the moods the structure exists to keep out of the decision.'}]}}}},2964,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'deciding-without-knowing_1':{pictureCaption:'Dice resting on a careful plan. Whatever happens next came from both, and afterwards you cannot lift the dice off to see which did what.',basics:{title:'A good choice can still go wrong',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE COAT',prompt:'The forecast says rain, so you take your coat. It stays sunny all day, and you carry the coat for nothing. Was taking it a bad choice?',options:['Yes, because it did not rain','No, it was smart with what you knew'],answer:1,reveal:'You could not know the sky would clear. With rain on the forecast, taking the coat was a good choice. It just turned out you did not need it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'How a thing turns out is part choice and part luck. So a good choice can end badly, and a silly one can end well. Judge the choice, not just the result.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Dice sit on top of a careful plan. What happens next comes from both. Afterwards, you can\u2019t tell which part was luck.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'ON THE PITCH',text:'Your friend shoots from the halfway line instead of passing. It goes in! Everyone cheers. But try that shot again and it will most likely miss. The goal was luck. Passing was the better choice.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why this matters',text:'If you only look at results, you learn the wrong things. You start to copy lucky choices. And you stop making good choices that just happened to go wrong.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time something goes wrong, ask two things. Was my choice sensible, with what I knew then? Or was I just unlucky?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You take the safe path home. A tree has fallen across it, and you are late. What should you learn?',options:['The choice was fine. The tree was bad luck','Never take that path home again','Safe choices never really work out'],answer:0,why:'You could not have known about the tree. One bad result does not make the choice a bad one.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'TWO JOB OFFERS',text:'Two friends each choose a job. One researches carefully and picks the stable firm; it folds a year later. The other picks on a whim; the start-up thrives. At the next dinner, one is called naive and the other brilliant.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Outcomes mix the quality of a decision with luck, and the mixture cannot be separated after the fact. Grade only the result and you learn to avoid decisions that went badly, not decisions that were badly made.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'FOUR CASES, NOT TWO',left:{title:'Sound process',items:['Good result: deserved','Bad result: unlucky']},right:{title:'Poor process',items:['Good result: lucky','Bad result: deserved']},note:'Judge by result alone and four cases collapse into two. The lucky get praised and the unlucky get blamed.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What grading on results rewards',text:'Over time, outcome-based review rewards risk-taking that happened to pay and punishes sound judgement that happened not to. Nobody intends this. It is what you get when the one thing you measure is partly luck.'},{kind:'steps',title:'What to grade instead',steps:['What did I know at the time?','What did I assume?','What alternatives did I consider?','Was the process reasonable, given all that?'],note:'This part can be assessed, and it is the only part that carries over to the next decision.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Dice resting on a careful plan. The result will come from both, and nobody can lift the dice off afterwards to see which did what.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick a decision that ended badly this year. Write one line on how it was made and one on how it turned out. Draw lessons from the first line only.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A colleague\u2019s risky, unchecked bet pays off handsomely. Your manager wants everyone to copy their approach. What does the idea say?',options:['Check the reasoning first, since a good result may have been luck','Copy it, since the result shows the approach works','Reject it, since risky bets always end badly in time','Copy it, but only for decisions of a similar size'],answer:0,why:'A good result does not certify the process. If the reasoning was poor, copying the success teaches the wrong lesson.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE NATURAL VIEW',belief:'If it went well it was a good decision, and if it went badly it was a bad one.',reality:'This Mindroll piece argues that the result is contaminated. Any decision worth agonising over involves uncertainty, so the outcome is part judgement and part chance, and the two cannot be separated after the fact.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Judge a decision by the reasoning available at the time, not by its outcome. Grading on outcomes teaches you to avoid decisions that went badly rather than decisions that were badly made.'},{kind:'term',word:'Decision quality',meaning:'How well a choice was made, given what could be known then: the information gathered, the assumptions made, the alternatives weighed. It is a separate question from how the choice turned out.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW BAD LESSONS ARE LEARNED',nodes:['Grade only on results','Lucky risks get praised','Sound, unlucky calls get punished','Judgement drifts towards risk'],note:'Nobody chooses this drift. It follows from measuring the one thing that is partly chance.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why grade the reasoning',text:'The piece makes two claims for it. The reasoning can be assessed: what was known, what was assumed, which alternatives were considered. And it is the only part that transfers. The luck in this outcome will not recur; the way you reasoned will.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Dice resting on a plan. The outcome is a mixture of the two, and no review after the fact can lift the dice back off.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The separation is easier to state than to do. From one decision you often cannot tell skill from luck, and a run of bad outcomes may be evidence that the reasoning was worse than it looked. A process judged sound by the person who ran it also needs an honest second reader. Outcomes are not the verdict, but they remain evidence.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'Someone objects: \u201cIn the real world, results are all that count.\u201d What is the best reply?',options:['Results count, but one result cannot show whether the reasoning was good','Results do not count at all; only the effort behind them does','Results count at work but not in decisions about personal life','Results always match the quality of the reasoning in the end'],answer:0,why:'The piece does not say outcomes are unimportant. It says a single outcome is a poor grade on the decision, because luck is mixed into it.'}]}},'deciding-without-knowing_2':{pictureCaption:'The boat has only just set out, and the prediction is already sealed and tucked behind the clock, to be opened once it is back.',bridge:'Idea 1 said to grade the reasoning, not the result. But memory quietly rewrites what your reasoning was, so it has to be on paper first.',basics:{title:'Write it down before you know',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'AFTER THE FINAL',text:'Your team loses the final. On the way home your friend says, *I knew we would lose.* But before the match he said you would win easily. He is not lying. He really does remember it that way now.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Memory changes',text:'Your memory is not like a video. Each time you remember, you build the memory again. Once you know what happened, you build it with that inside. So it feels as if you knew all along.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Once you know how things turned out, your memory quietly changes what you thought before. The way to stop it is to write your guess down first.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A MARK ON THE DOOR',text:'A pencil mark on the door frame shows how tall you were last year. Without it, you could only guess. A written guess is a mark like that, for what you thought.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Three lines',steps:['What do I think will happen?','Why do I think so?','What would show I was wrong?'],note:'Write them before you find out. Read them after.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The boat is only just setting off. The guess is already sealed up behind the clock. It gets opened when the trip is over.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before your next test, write down the mark you expect, and why. Check it when the test comes back.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Before a race, you think you will come fifth. You come first. Now you feel sure you always knew you would win. What is going on?',options:['Your memory has changed to fit what happened','You really did know all along that you would win','You were not telling the truth before the race'],answer:0,why:'Memory often does this, to all of us. Only a guess written down before the race would show what you really thought.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A YEAR LATER',prompt:'A project you led has failed. What will your memory most likely tell you about how confident you were at the start?',options:['Roughly what you really thought','That you had your doubts all along','That you were sure from the start'],answer:1,reveal:'The piece describes memory of an earlier belief as rebuilt after the fact. It is often tainted by what happened. You recall having expected the outcome, with confidence. You have no sense that anything was revised.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Memory rewrites itself. Afterwards you recall having expected whatever happened. The only reliable defence is a short written record, made before the outcome, of what you expected and why.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why this blocks learning',text:'To learn from a decision you compare what you expected with what happened. If the expectation has been quietly changed to match, the comparison tends to say you were roughly right. Feedback on your judgement disappears. That is because the thing you compare against has already been changed.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Remembered expectation',items:['Rebuilt after the fact','Shaped by what happened','Revised without your noticing']},right:{title:'Written expectation',items:['Fixed before the outcome','Blind to what happened','Cannot be revised silently']}},{kind:'picture',caption:'Sealed while the boat is still leaving harbour. Whatever happens on the voyage, this record of what was expected cannot be quietly revised.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Add a number',text:'The piece suggests adding a probability: seventy per cent, say. Over many predictions that builds a record of calibration. Do the things you call seventy per cent likely happen about seventy per cent of the time? Without a record, there is no way to know.'},{kind:'try',text:'For your next big decision, write three lines: what you expect, why, and what would show you were wrong. Date it and keep it where you will find it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Your team is launching a new service. Which habit will give you honest feedback on your judgement later?',options:['Writing down now what you expect, and how sure you are','Holding a careful review once the results are in','Asking the team afterwards what they had expected','Trusting your memory of how you felt on launch day'],answer:0,why:'Afterwards, everyone\u2019s memory has been shaped by the result. Only a record made before the outcome is clean.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'THE PROBLEM',word:'Hindsight',meaning:'Knowing how things turned out. The trouble is what that knowledge does to memory: once the outcome is known, your recollection of what you expected beforehand is rebuilt to include it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Write down what you expect, why, and how likely you think it, before the outcome is known. Memory of a prior belief is rebuilt afterwards to fit what happened. A written prediction cannot be revised silently.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The mechanism',text:'The piece says memory of a prior belief is reconstructed after the fact and often contaminated by what actually happened. People recall having expected the outcome they observed, with confidence and with no awareness of the revision. Nothing feels wrong, which is why good intentions cannot fix it.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE RECORD IS LOST',nodes:['You form an expectation','The outcome arrives','Memory rebuilds the expectation','\u201cI knew it all along\u201d'],note:'The last step feels like accurate recall. That is why only a record outside your head helps.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A record outside your head, sealed while the boat is still leaving harbour. It will hold the expectation that memory would otherwise rebuild.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What a record buys',text:'Calibration over time. If you write seventy per cent against many predictions, you can check whether about seventy per cent of them happened. That is feedback on judgement itself, which the piece calls unavailable in principle without a record, because the benchmark has already been altered.'},{kind:'limits',text:'A record defeats silent revision, not self-deception at the moment of writing. People hedge, write vaguely, or skip the prediction they are least sure of. Calibration also needs many predictions; a handful tells you little. For a one-off decision, the record helps you review the reasoning but cannot score it.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of a recent outcome that nobody around you claims to have been surprised by. Could any of them show you what they expected beforehand?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE FLAW',prompt:'Which of these records still lets memory revise itself?',options:['A note written the day after the result, saying what you had expected','A dated email to yourself before the vote, with a probability','A journal line before the interview: \u201c60%, because the brief fits me\u201d','A written prediction sent to a friend before the match started'],answer:0,why:'Once the result is known, even an honest note is written by a memory that has already been revised. The record has to come first.'}]}},'deciding-without-knowing_3':{pictureCaption:'One part held by a pin that slides out, one post welded down for good. The hand weighs the pin: how hard is this to undo?',bridge:'Ideas 1 and 2 were about judging a decision afterwards. This one is about how much care a decision deserves before you make it.',basics:{title:'Can you undo it?',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'PENCIL OR PEN',text:'Some choices are like writing in pencil. If it is wrong, you rub it out and try again. Others are like writing in pen. Once it is down, it stays.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'Which of these choices deserves the most time to think?',options:['Which film to watch tonight','Whether to cut your long hair very short','Which crisps to buy at the shop'],answer:1,reveal:'Hair grows back, but slowly. A bad film or bad crisps are over by tomorrow. The choice that is hard to undo gets the thinking time.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Before you think hard about a choice, ask: if I get this wrong, how hard is it to undo? Easy to undo? Decide fast. Hard to undo? Slow down.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One part is held by a pin that slides out. The other is fixed down for good. The hand is asking: how easy is this to undo?'},{kind:'explain',title:'The usual mistake',text:'People often do it backwards. They spend ages picking a pencil case they could swap next week. Then they rush a big choice, just because someone wants an answer now.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you can\u2019t decide, ask: could I change this next week? If yes, just pick one and go.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'At a sleepover, your friend has spent twenty minutes choosing which game to play first. What would help?',options:['Pick one fast, since you can switch games later','Keep thinking until she is totally sure','Have everyone vote on each game twice'],answer:0,why:'Picking the wrong game costs a few minutes. That is easy to undo, so a fast choice is the right one.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE HABIT',belief:'Big decisions need careful thought, so think hard about all of them.',reality:'Decisions differ hugely in what a mistake costs. A choice you can undo deserves speed. One you cannot undo deserves the slow thought people spend on both out of habit.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Let how easily a choice can be undone decide how much thought it gets. Make choices that are cheap to undo fast, on partial information. Save the slow process for the ones you cannot take back.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'SORT FIRST',low:'Undone in a day, for almost nothing',high:'Cannot be undone at any price',note:'Where a decision sits on this line should set how long you spend on it, not how urgent it feels.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why speed suits reversible choices',text:'If a choice can be undone, being wrong costs only the undoing plus the time. Weeks of slow thought can cost more than that. So a fast decision on partial information is the better process here, not the lazy one.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The common failure',text:'The same slow care for every choice. A team agonises over a choice it can undo, like the wording of a trial page. Then it rushes one it cannot undo, like signing a long lease, because it came in urgent. Urgency chose the process. And urgency is a poor guide.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A part held by one removable pin beside a post welded for good. The hand weighs the pin, which is the question to ask first.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before you next deliberate, ask: if this is wrong, what would undoing it cost in six months? Let the answer set how long you spend.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Which of these decisions most deserves slow, careful thought?',options:['Selling the family home to fund a new business','Trying a new layout for the weekly newsletter','Choosing a supplier on a one-month trial','Picking a project tool with free cancellation'],answer:0,why:'Selling the house would be costly or impossible to undo. The others can be reversed cheaply, so speed serves them better.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'WHERE DOES THE WEEK GO?',prompt:'A team has a week to choose a logo for a trial campaign and to sign a five-year lease the landlord needs by Friday. Where does time usually go?',options:['Mostly to the lease','Mostly to the logo','Evenly to both'],answer:1,reveal:'The piece names this as the common failure: agonising over the reversible choice while the irreversible one is made quickly because it arrived urgently.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Sort decisions by reversibility before deciding how to decide. Reversible ones should be made fast, on incomplete information; irreversible ones warrant the slow process.'},{kind:'term',word:'Cost of error',meaning:'What being wrong would cost. For a reversible decision it is the price of reversing plus the time lost. For an irreversible one it is the whole consequence, with no way back.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The argument',text:'Deliberation has a price: time, attention, delay. It is worth paying only where it prevents an expensive error. Where error is cheap to fix, deliberating costs more than it saves. So the piece puts one question first, what undoing this would cost in six months, and calls that the cheapest improvement available to most decision-making.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Reversible',items:['Decide fast','Accept incomplete information','Learn by doing, then adjust']},right:{title:'Irreversible',items:['Decide slowly','Gather what can be known','Weigh alternatives before acting']}},{kind:'limits',text:'Reversibility is easy to misjudge. Many choices are reversible on paper and sticky in practice: a hire, a public promise, a product customers come to rely on. A string of small reversible choices can add up to an irreversible drift. And a reversal that is cheap for you may be expensive for someone else.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One part pinned, one welded. The hand weighs the pin before anything else: the cost of undoing comes first.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISJUDGEMENT',prompt:'Which decision is most likely being wrongly treated as easy to reverse?',options:['Announcing a price cut to all customers as a trial','Testing two versions of a web page for one week','Ordering one sample before a large purchase','Trying a new route to work for a few days'],answer:0,why:'You can raise the price again on paper, but customers remember the cut. Undoing it costs far more than it appears to.'}]}},'deciding-without-knowing_4':{pictureCaption:'The builder has knocked the model down on purpose, while it is still only a model, and is searching the pieces for the joint that gave.',bridge:'Idea 2 wrote down what you expect. The pre-mortem goes further: it assumes the worst has already happened and asks you to explain it.',basics:{title:'Pretend it already went wrong',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE CAMPING TRIP',text:'Your family plans a camping trip. Someone asks, *What could go wrong?* Everyone says, *Not much.* Then someone says, *Imagine it is Sunday and the trip was a disaster. What happened?* Now everyone has ideas. The tent leaks. The car is too full. Nobody packed the torch.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Before you start something, pretend it has already gone wrong. Then explain why. You will often spot more problems than if you just ask what *might* go wrong.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it works',text:'*What might go wrong?* feels gloomy, so people give a short, polite list. *It went wrong. Why?* is a puzzle to solve. People seem to be better at explaining what happened than at guessing what will.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The builder knocked the model down on purpose. Now the hands search for the weak joint, while it is still only a model.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Try it on a plan',steps:['Pick a plan, like a school project.','Say: it is finished, and it went badly.','Write down every reason why.','Fix the worst reason now.']},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your class is planning a bake sale. Which question uses this idea?',options:['Imagine the sale flopped. Why did it?','What do we need to buy for the sale?','Will the sale be a big success?'],answer:0,why:'Pretending it already failed helps people name real problems, while there is still time to fix them.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE LAUNCH MEETING',text:'A team reviews a launch plan. The leader asks for risks, and two polite, minor ones come up. Then she says: it is a year from now and the launch failed. Take five minutes and write down why. The list runs to a page, and on it is the problem three people had privately doubted.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Before committing, state that it is a year later and the thing has failed, then explain why. The change of tense tends to surface objections that asking what might go wrong does not.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO FRAMINGS',left:{title:'What might go wrong?',items:['A hypothetical','Pessimism sounds disloyal','A short, polite list']},right:{title:'It failed. Why?',items:['A fact to explain','Pessimism is the task','Specific, concrete causes']},note:'The piece\u2019s reason: people seem better at explaining a fact than at predicting one.'},{kind:'explain',title:'It makes dissent safe',text:'In an ordinary review, someone who suspects the plan is flawed has to challenge the group. In a pre-mortem they are simply doing the exercise. That is a much lower bar, and it brings out information that would otherwise stay private.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How good is the evidence?',text:'The piece says the technique has some evidence behind it. The finding usually cited is that imagining an outcome as having already happened produces more reasons for it. That shows more problems get named. It does not show that plans reviewed this way go better.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A model knocked down on purpose by its builder, and hands searching the pieces for the joint that gave, while failing is still cheap.'},{kind:'try',text:'At your next planning meeting, say: it is a year from now and this failed. Give everyone five minutes to write down why, on their own, before anyone speaks.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You are planning an office move. Which instruction runs a pre-mortem?',options:['Tell everyone it is next spring, the move failed, and ask them why','Ask everyone which risks to keep an eye on during the move','Invite anyone worried about the move to speak up now','Agree to list what went wrong once the move is over'],answer:0,why:'Only *it failed, tell me why* states the failure as a fact and asks for the explanation. That framing is what licenses people to be specific.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE TECHNIQUE',text:'Before committing to a plan, the group is told it is a year later and the plan has failed. Each person writes the explanation. It is a post-mortem moved to the one moment when its findings can still change anything.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Assume the project has already failed and explain why. Stating failure as fact licenses pessimism, so people give specific causes instead of a polite list, and doubters can speak without challenging the group.'},{kind:'term',word:'Pre-mortem',meaning:'A review held before a project starts, imagining it has already died. The name contrasts it with a post-mortem, the examination after a death that establishes its cause.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Running one',steps:['Brief the plan as it stands.','State: it is a year later, and this failed.','Each person writes the reasons, alone.','Share them, and fix the worst before committing.']},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the framing matters',text:'There are two mechanisms on the piece\u2019s account. One is cognitive: people seem better at explaining a fact than at predicting one, so the change of tense tends to produce concrete causes. The other is social: dissent becomes compliance with the exercise rather than a challenge to the group, which is a far lower bar.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the evidence covers',text:'The piece says the technique has some evidence behind it. The finding usually cited is that imagining an outcome as having already happened produces more reasons for it. It gives no figures and names no study. Notice what that covers: more problems named, not better outcomes delivered.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Surfacing a problem is not solving it. A pre-mortem can produce a long list nobody acts on, or become a ritual a team performs and then ignores. It can favour vivid, imaginable failures over dull, likely ones. And if the leader is visibly attached to the plan, the safety it offers doubters is weaker than it looks.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these defeats the purpose of a pre-mortem?',options:['The project lead reads out their own reasons first and asks for agreement','Everyone writes their reasons privately before any discussion','The failure is set a year ahead and stated as a plain fact','The worst reasons lead to changes before the plan is signed off'],answer:0,why:'The point is to bring private doubts into the open. If the leader frames the reasons first, people drift back to agreeing with the group.'}]}},'deciding-without-knowing_5':{pictureCaption:'The finished frame is pushed into the corner. The maker is checking the measurements that produced it, which is where the lesson is.',bridge:'Idea 1 warned against grading by results. This is the review itself: what to ask once it is over, and why a good result can mislead most.',basics:{title:'Check how you chose, not just how it went',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'WHEN IT GOES WELL',belief:'If it worked out, I must have chosen well.',reality:'Not always. Sometimes you choose badly and get lucky. That is the time to be most careful, because luck makes a bad choice feel smart.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE SPELLING TEST',text:'You did not practise for a spelling test. You guessed, and you got full marks! The words just happened to be easy ones. Next week you think, *I don\u2019t need to practise.*'},{kind:'idea',text:'When something is over, don\u2019t just ask how it went. Ask: was my choice sensible, with what I knew then? Sometimes the honest answer is: good choice, bad luck.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Good choice, bad result',items:['Bad luck','Change nothing']},right:{title:'Bad choice, good result',items:['Good luck','Careful: fix the choice']},note:'The second one is sneaky. Winning hides the mistake.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The finished box is pushed into the corner. The maker is checking the measuring instead. That is where the lesson is.'},{kind:'try',text:'After your next game or test, ask: what did I do, and was it sensible? Then ask: how much of it was luck?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You forget your football boots, but the match is called off because of rain. What should you learn?',options:['Pack my boots next time. I just got lucky','Forgetting my boots does not really matter','Rain will always save me in the end'],answer:0,why:'The good result was luck. Not checking your bag was still a mistake.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'TWO REVIEWS',text:'A campaign beats its target. Its review lasts ten minutes, all congratulations. Another campaign, planned with more care, misses. Its review lasts two hours and ends with a new sign-off process. Nobody asks whether either decision was sound.'},{kind:'idea',text:'After an outcome, ask whether the decision was sound given what was knowable at the time. Being able to say *well made and unlucky* without flinching is the difference between learning and superstition.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The dangerous case',text:'A bad result after sound reasoning is variance and should change nothing. A good result after poor reasoning is a warning, not a proof. The second is where the damage is done. Success rewards whatever produced it, recklessness included. And you cannot see it happen.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW LUCK BECOMES HABIT',nodes:['A careless call','It happens to work','The habit is rewarded','The careless call is repeated'],loop:!0,note:'Each turn of the loop feels like evidence. It is luck being mistaken for skill.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Keep review and blame apart',text:'The piece argues that a post-mortem which also decides blame cannot produce honest reasoning. That is because the pull to retell events in your own favour is too strong. Hold the review on its own, apart from any talk of who is to blame.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The finished frame pushed aside, and the measurements that made it laid out and checked. The result is set aside; the method is reviewed.'},{kind:'try',text:'Take your most recent success. Write down the reasoning behind it as it stood at the time. Would you make that call again, knowing it might not have worked?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A trader breaks the firm\u2019s risk rules and makes a large profit. What should the review conclude?',options:['The profit is a warning, since the reasoning broke the rules','The profit shows that the risk rules are too strict','The breach can be ignored, since nothing went wrong','The trader should be rewarded, and the rules quietly tightened'],answer:0,why:'A good result after poor reasoning is the case that does the damage. Rewarding it teaches the recklessness.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'TWO LESSONS, BOTH FROM RESULTS',text:'A shop owner skips a safety check and has a record year, so the check gets dropped for good. A ward rewrites a careful rota after one bad night. In both cases the result wrote the lesson. Neither asked whether the original decision was sound given what was known.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Review the reasoning available at the time, not the result. A bad result after sound reasoning is variance and should change nothing; a good result after poor reasoning is a warning, not a validation.'},{kind:'term',word:'Variance',meaning:'The spread of outcomes that sound decisions produce simply because chance is involved. A single bad result inside that spread says nothing about whether the decision was good.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why success does the damage',text:'Failure tends to prompt review; success often does not. So reckless processes that happened to work are reinforced, and nobody sees it happen. The piece calls this the discipline everything else rests on: written expectations and the pre-mortem both feed a review that must judge reasoning, not results.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Review and blame',text:'The piece argues that a post-mortem which also determines blame cannot produce honest reasoning, because the incentive to reconstruct favourably is overwhelming, and it gives that as one reason post-mortems often yield little. Its remedy is structural: separate the review from the accountability conversation.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Accountability does not vanish because it is postponed, and some failures, such as harm to others, cannot wait for it. That post-mortems often yield little is the piece\u2019s judgement, not a finding it cites. And *well made and unlucky* is easy to abuse: file every failure as variance and the review becomes its own superstition.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The finished frame is pushed into the corner while the measurements are checked. The object is the result; the measurements are the reasoning.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HARD CASE',prompt:'Your third sound-looking decision in a row ends badly. What does the idea suggest?',options:['Re-examine the reasoning closely, since repeated misses can be evidence','Change nothing, since bad results after sound reasoning are variance','Abandon the approach, since three failures prove it was wrong','Stop reviewing, since luck makes every review meaningless'],answer:0,why:'One bad result can be variance. A pattern may mean the reasoning was less sound than it looked, and the review must stay open to that.'}]}}}},2965,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'decisions-that-look-obvious-afterwards_1':{pictureCaption:'The old guess is being rubbed out halfway along the line. A new mark already stands by the flag where things ended.',basics:{title:'Why the ending makes it look obvious',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'BEFORE THE MATCH',text:'Before the big match, you think your team might win. Maybe. You are not sure at all. Then they win three nil.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'A week later, a friend asks what you thought before the match. What do you remember?',options:['That you were not sure','That you were fairly sure they would win'],answer:1,reveal:'People tend to remember being more sure than they were. Knowing the end changes the memory. It happens quietly, and the new memory feels true.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Once you know how something ended, your memory of what you expected moves towards it. You can\u2019t feel it move. So the past looks more obvious than it was.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A RUBBED-OUT GUESS',picture:!0,text:'Picture writing your guess in pencil. When the answer comes, your brain rubs out the guess and writes it again, closer to the answer. Then it forgets it ever used the rubber.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Knowing about it does not stop it',text:'Even when people are told about this, it still happens. They say, \u201cNo, I really did know.\u201d They are not lying. The new memory feels real to them.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time someone says \u201cI knew it all along\u201d, ask: did they say so before? Try the same test on yourself.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend picked the wrong answer in a quiz. Now everyone says the right one was obvious. What does this idea say?',options:['It only looks obvious because we know the answer now','Your friend was silly not to see it','Quiz answers are always clear if you think hard'],answer:0,why:'Knowing the answer changes how easy it seems. Before, it may have been a real puzzle.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE INTERVIEW',prompt:'Last month you gave yourself even odds of getting a job. You got it. Asked now, what do you say your odds had been?',options:['Even, exactly as you thought','Better than even, looking back','Worse than even, looking back'],answer:1,reveal:'People who learn a result tend to recall their earlier expectation as much closer to it, say so with confidence, and deny that they have moved. Being told about the effect does not undo it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Learning an outcome rewrites your memory of what you expected, and you cannot see the rewrite from inside. So every past decision looks either obvious or inexplicable, and neither reading can be trusted.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW A MEMORY MOVES',nodes:['You expect something, unsure','You learn the outcome','Memory shifts towards it','You feel you saw it coming'],note:'The last step feels like remembering. It is reconstructing.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The old mark rubbed out mid-line, a new one pencilled beside the finish. Memory does both steps, and leaves no crumbs you can see.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it wrecks self-assessment',text:'To know whether you judged well, you compare what you expected with what happened. But the only record most people have of what they expected is a memory, and that memory has already been edited by the outcome. The thing being assessed has contaminated the evidence.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What follows',text:'The essay draws the consequence plainly: a review of a past decision conducted from memory will conclude that the outcome was foreseeable, whether or not it was. That includes your own reviews of your own decisions.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of a past decision that now looks obvious, yours or someone else\u2019s. Ask whether there is any record, not a memory, of what was expected at the time.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'After a project fails, the team agrees the warning signs were obvious all along. What should this idea make you suspect?',options:['That knowing the outcome made the signs look clearer than they were','That the team ignored signs that everyone could plainly see','That the project leader should have been replaced much earlier','That the warning signs were simply invented after the fact'],answer:0,why:'The signs may have been there, but how clear they now seem is not reliable evidence. Knowing the ending sharpens them.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE ESSAY\u2019S STARTING POINT',text:'Historical decisions read as either inevitable or idiotic. The essay\u2019s claim is that both readings come from the same place: we already know how it ended. Its first move is to show that this knowledge does not sit beside your memory of the odds. It reshapes it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Knowing an outcome reliably alters your memory of what you expected, and the alteration is not visible to introspection. Without a record of the prior belief, honest self-assessment of judgement is close to impossible.'},{kind:'term',word:'Hindsight bias',meaning:'The usual name for this effect: after learning an outcome, people see it as having been more predictable than they did beforehand, and recall their own earlier expectations as closer to it.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'What a fair review needs',items:['Your prior belief','The outcome','The gap between them']},right:{title:'What you actually have',items:['A memory of the prior belief','The outcome','A memory already moved by it']},note:'The outcome has contaminated the record you would judge it with.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The prior belief, rubbed out mid-line; a new mark beside the flag where it ended. A record made at the time is the one mark this eraser cannot reach.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why warnings fail',text:'If the shift were a choice, knowing about it would let you refuse it. It is not. In the experiments people report their altered recall with confidence and reject the suggestion that it moved. You cannot correct for a change you cannot detect.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The effect does not mean every outcome was unforeseeable. Some decisions really were bad, and some warnings really were ignored. Hindsight makes those cases impossible to tell apart from memory alone, not impossible to tell apart. The fix is evidence from the time. And the suspicion cuts both ways: a decision that looks idiotic deserves it as much as one that looks obvious.'},{kind:'keyline',text:'Distrust how obvious the past looks, most of all when it looks obvious to you.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HARD CASE',prompt:'You doubted a plan that later failed. You now remember being sure it would fail. What is the most defensible view of your foresight?',options:['Unknown, unless you wrote down what you expected at the time','Good, since your memory and the outcome agree with each other','Poor, because memory always runs against your real views','Good, because doubting plans is usually the right instinct'],answer:0,why:'Your memory has been edited by the outcome, in the direction that flatters you. Only a record made beforehand can say.'}]}},'decisions-that-look-obvious-afterwards_2':{pictureCaption:'A desk as it stood on one day: a file, a phone, a notepad, and nothing that arrived later. The decision was made from this.',bridge:'Idea 1 showed that memory cannot be trusted to judge the past. This one says what to use instead.',basics:{title:'Judge what they knew, not how it ended',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'NO COAT',prompt:'In the morning the sky is clear and the forecast says sunny. You leave your coat at home. At lunch it pours. Was that a bad choice?',options:['Yes, you got soaked','No, it was a fair choice'],answer:1,reveal:'With what you knew that morning, leaving the coat made sense. The rain was bad luck. To judge a choice, look at what was known when it was made.'},{kind:'idea',text:'To judge a choice, don\u2019t ask if it worked. Ask what the person knew at the time, how sure they could be, and how long they had to decide.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'This desk shows only what was on it that day. Nothing from later. That is all the person had to decide with.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Three questions to ask',steps:['What did they know then?','What could they not know yet?','How much time did they have?']},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE TEAM CAPTAIN',text:'Your class picked Sam as team captain. Then Sam was ill on the day of the big game, and you lost. People say it was a silly pick. But no one knew Sam would be ill. On the day you picked, Sam was a good choice.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Sometimes it looks worse',text:'The check can go the other way too. Sometimes you find that people did know something important and chose to ignore it. Then the choice really was a poor one.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend made a move in a board game, and it lost her the game. What is the fair way to judge the move?',options:['Ask what she could see on the board at the time','Say it was bad, because she lost','Say it was good, because she tried'],answer:0,why:'Losing does not prove the move was bad. What she could see when she chose it is what counts.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE REVIEW MEETING',text:'A supplier you chose went bust six months later, and the review is under way. Someone says the warning signs were obvious. Nobody has opened the file showing what was known when the contract was signed.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The honest question about a past decision is not whether it worked. It is what was on the desk on the day, how reliable it was, and how long there was. Reconstruct the information, not the outcome.'},{kind:'steps',title:'What to reconstruct',steps:['What was known, and what was unknown.','What could reasonably have been found out.','What the alternatives were.','What the arguments for the rejected options were.']},{kind:'explain',title:'Documents, not memory',text:'Doing this properly usually needs the record: the emails, the data as it stood, the version of the forecast that existed then. Memory of the state of knowledge is exactly what hindsight corrupts, so a review that relies on it has already been decided by the outcome.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A desk as it stood on one day: a file, a phone, a notepad, and nothing that arrived later. The decision was made from this.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it pays',text:'Judged by outcome, you learn about luck, which does not transfer. Judged by information and process, you learn something you can use next time. Decisions that look absurd usually stop looking absurd once the desk is restored. A few look worse, and that is worth knowing too.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before you judge any past decision, write down what was actually knowable at the time. Then judge.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You are asked to explain a hiring decision that went badly. What should you bring to the review?',options:['The CVs, notes and references as they stood when you decided','A clear account of everything that went wrong after the hire','Your honest memory of how confident you felt at the time','A list of the warning signs that came to light later on'],answer:0,why:'The documents from the day show what was knowable. Memory and later signs have both been shaped by the outcome.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'A WORD FROM MEDICINE',word:'Post-mortem',meaning:'A review held after a project or decision has played out, to learn from it. The word is borrowed from medicine, where it means an examination after death.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A useful post-mortem rebuilds the information available at the time: what was known, unknown and findable, the alternatives, and the case for the options rejected. Then the assessment concerns process, which transfers, rather than outcome, which does not.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Reconstruct the outcome',items:['Start from what happened','Work back to the mistake','Rely on memory','Lessons about luck']},right:{title:'Reconstruct the information',items:['Start from what was on the desk','Ask what was knowable','Rely on documents','Lessons about process']},note:'Only the right-hand review produces something that carries over to the next decision.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why documents',text:'The method depends on records made then: emails, the data as it stood, the forecast in its original version. Memory will not do, because memory of what was known is exactly what idea 1 showed hindsight rewriting. A review built on recollection has quietly imported the outcome.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The payoff',text:'Only this method can identify a good process that produced a bad result. That matters, because such a process should be repeated, and a review by outcome would tell you to abandon it. The method also cuts the other way: restore the desk, and a few decisions look worse than they did.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Everything that arrived later has been taken off this desk. What is left is what the decision was actually made from.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Documents are not neutral either. What got written down was chosen by people with their own aims, and what mattered may never have been recorded. Reconstruction can also become a defence for anything: every decision looks reasonable if you are generous enough about what could not be known. Asking what could reasonably have been found out keeps that honest.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE FLAW',prompt:'A board reviews an acquisition that failed. Which step most undermines the review?',options:['Asking the executives to recall what they believed at the time','Collecting the valuation models as they stood before the deal','Listing the alternatives that were on the table that month','Writing up the strongest case made against the deal back then'],answer:0,why:'Recollection of what was believed is exactly what knowing the outcome corrupts. The other steps use the record from the day.'}]}},'decisions-that-look-obvious-afterwards_3':{pictureCaption:'The braced tower was knocked down by a stray ball. The tall, wobbly stack beside it survived, and it gets the rosette.',bridge:'Idea 2 turned the review towards process. This one shows why process and outcome must be judged apart.',basics:{title:'A good choice can still go wrong',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE SCIENCE QUIZ',text:'Ali revises for the quiz all week. On the day she has a bad cold, can\u2019t think straight, and gets 5 out of 10. Ben does no revision. The quiz is multiple choice, so he guesses, gets lucky, and scores 9.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A good choice can end badly. A silly choice can end well. So don\u2019t judge a choice only by how it turned out.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Good choice, bad luck',items:['Ali learned all week','She fell ill on the day','She got 5']},right:{title:'Bad choice, good luck',items:['Ben learned nothing','His guesses were lucky','He got 9']}},{kind:'picture',caption:'One tower was built with care, and a ball knocked it down. The wobbly one stayed up by luck. Now it gets the prize.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why this matters',text:'If you only praise the 9, you teach the class to be like Ben. You teach them to take silly risks. Next time, Ben\u2019s way will most likely go wrong. Ali\u2019s way will most likely go right.'},{kind:'scale',low:'All down to luck',high:'All down to the choice',note:'Most results sit somewhere in the middle. The hard part is that you only get to see the result.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you win or lose a game, ask two things. Was my plan good? Was I lucky? Answer each one on its own.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend crossed the road without looking and was fine. What does this idea say?',options:['It was still a bad choice; he was just lucky','It was a good choice, because he was fine','It does not matter, because nothing happened'],answer:0,why:'A good ending does not make a risky choice a good one. Next time the luck may not be there.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'IT WORKED, DIDN\u2019T IT?',belief:'If a decision worked out, it was a good decision.',reality:'A well-made decision can fail and a reckless one can succeed. The outcome is evidence about a decision, not the verdict on it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Good process and good outcome come apart constantly. Organisations that reward outcomes alone teach people to gamble, and separating the two is hard because the outcome is the part everyone can see.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW GAMBLING GETS TAUGHT',nodes:['A reckless bet happens to pay off','It is praised and rewarded','Others copy the bet','The odds catch up'],note:'The careful colleague whose sound plan met bad luck learns the same lesson: care is not what gets rewarded here.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The careful tower fell to a stray ball; the wobbly stack stayed up. The rosette goes on the one still standing, and everyone watching learns to build wobbly.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'TWO PROJECT LEADS',text:'One lead researches the market, tests the plan and launches into a sudden downturn. Another skips the testing, bets on a hunch and catches a lucky wave. Judged by results alone, the first is passed over and the second promoted. Everyone watching has just learned which method to use.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How to separate them',text:'Review decisions on what was known and what was considered, and write that review before the result arrives. Once the outcome is in, it colours everything. A judgement of the process made beforehand is the one it cannot rewrite.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick a decision you are making this week. Before you know how it turns out, write two lines on why it is sound.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A trader made a large bet against the advice of the risk team, and it paid off handsomely. How should the firm assess it?',options:['As a poor process with a lucky result, not something to reward','As a good decision, since the profit speaks for itself','As proof that the risk team\u2019s advice was far too cautious','As irrelevant, because only the long-run average matters'],answer:0,why:'The result was good, but the process was a gamble. Rewarding it teaches everyone else to gamble too.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TWO DECISIONS',prompt:'A careful decision that failed, and a careless one that succeeded. Which deserves more criticism?',options:['The careful one: it failed','The careless one: it was a gamble','Neither: only results count'],answer:1,reveal:'The careless one was a bad decision that met good luck. The essay\u2019s point is that most systems of judgement get this backwards, because the result is the only thing in plain view.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Judge process and outcome separately. A good process can fail and a bad one succeed, and an organisation that rewards outcomes alone ends up rewarding gambles.'},{kind:'equation',parts:['Quality of the process','Luck'],result:'The outcome you see',note:'Only the result is visible. Reward it alone and you reward the luck as much as the process.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Process and luck side by side: a braced tower felled by a stray ball, a wobbly stack still up. Judge only what stands, and the rosette goes to luck.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why separation is hard',text:'The outcome is public, vivid and final. Process is private and has to be reconstructed. So even people who know the two differ end up grading on results, because results are the evidence in plain view. And once the result is in, idea 1 applies: memory of the process has already drifted towards it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The practical version',text:'Review decisions on what was known and what was considered, and do it in writing before the result arrives. A review written beforehand is the one assessment of process that the outcome cannot reach.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Outcomes are not irrelevant. Across many decisions, results are the best evidence about whether a process is actually good; a method that keeps failing is probably flawed, whatever it looked like on paper. The separation matters most for judging a single decision, where luck is large. Pushed further, it becomes a way to excuse any failure as bad luck.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Process and outcome come apart constantly.','Rewarding outcomes alone rewards gambling.','Write the process review before the result arrives.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'A manager objects that talk of process is an excuse, since in the end only results pay. What is the best reply?',options:['Results matter over many decisions; one result can still be mostly luck','Results are luck, so they should be ignored when judging people','Process is what matters, so results never need to be checked','Agreed: a good process is simply one that produces good results'],answer:0,why:'The idea concedes that results are evidence. It denies only that one result is the verdict on one decision.'}]}},'decisions-that-look-obvious-afterwards_4':{pictureCaption:'The critic points at the dish they should have ordered. It was crossed off the menu that night.',bridge:'Idea 2 restored what was known at the time. This one restores what could actually have been done.',basics:{title:'You can only pick from what was there',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THEY SHOULD HAVE\u2026',belief:'If a choice went wrong, they should just have picked something better.',reality:'Often the better thing did not exist yet, or was not allowed, or no one knew about it. You can only choose from what is really on offer.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE SCHOOL TRIP',text:'Your class trip was to a museum. It rained, and it was dull. Now someone says you should have gone to the new water park. But the water park opened last week. When the trip was booked, it was not there to pick.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When you judge a choice, only count the options that were really there at the time. People often compare a choice with one that never existed.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'He is pointing at the meal they should have picked. But look: it was crossed off the menu that night.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A SHOP AT CLOSING TIME',text:'You pop out for bread at the end of the day. The shop has only plain loaves left. Your sister says you should have got the nice seeded one. There was no seeded one left to get.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you hear \u201cthey should have\u2026\u201d, ask: could they really have done that, back then?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your grandparents had no mobile phones as children. Someone says they should have just texted their friends. What is wrong?',options:['Texting was not an option back then','Texting is worse than talking face to face','Phone calls cost too much back then'],answer:0,why:'You can\u2019t judge a choice against something that did not exist yet.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE OBVIOUS FIX',text:'Reading about a firm that kept its records on paper for years, you think: why not just put it all on a computer? Then you notice the date. The cheap software that would have made it easy had not been built yet.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The most common hindsight error is judging a decision against an option that was never available: a technology not yet built, an ally not yet willing, a fact not yet known.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the menu looks wide afterwards',text:'Constraints fade from the story. The information, the deadline, the budget and the politics pressed hard at the time and are invisible now. Only the choice remains on the record, so it seems it could have been anything, and it gets compared with an option nobody could have taken.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The critic points at the dish they should have ordered. It was crossed off the menu that night.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Restore the menu',steps:['List what could actually have been done that week.','By these people, with this budget.','With the information they had then.','Only then compare the choice with the real options.'],note:'The genuine alternatives are usually far fewer than the retrospective account suggests.'},{kind:'try',text:'When you next hear that someone should have done X, check whether X was really on the menu at the time.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A critic says a town should have built its flood barrier ten years earlier. What should you check first?',options:['Whether the funding, approval and design existed ten years ago','Whether the flood that came was as bad as was reported','Whether the critic has any real expertise in engineering','Whether the barrier has worked well since it was built'],answer:0,why:'The criticism assumes the earlier option was available. Restore the constraints, and it may not have been.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE WIDE MENU',belief:'Looking back, it is clear what they should have done instead.',reality:'What is usually clear is an option that did not exist at the time. After the fact the option space looks wide, because the constraints that shaped it have faded and only the choice remains visible.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Most confident hindsight compares a decision with an alternative that was never available. Restore the constraint set, and the genuine options are usually far fewer than the retrospective account suggests.'},{kind:'term',word:'Counterfactual',meaning:'A \u201cwhat if\u201d: an imagined course of events that did not happen, used to judge the one that did. The question to ask of it is whether anyone could actually have chosen it.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE ERROR IS MADE',nodes:['Constraints operate at the time','They fade from the record','Only the choice stays visible','It is judged against an impossible option'],note:'Each step is natural. Together they measure the choice against an option nobody could have chosen.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The corrective',text:'Rebuild the constraint set before evaluating: what could actually have been done that week, by these people, with this information. Technology, willing allies, known facts, deadline, budget, political reality. The essay\u2019s claim is that the honest list is usually short, and that the decision frequently looks reasonable once it is written out.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The critic is pointing at a crossed-out line. Whatever it was, it was not on the menu the night the choice was made.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Restoring the menu can be done dishonestly. Narrow it enough and any decision looks forced. Some alternatives really were available and were dismissed, and some constraints were chosen, like a deadline set for convenience. The test is whether an option could have been taken, not whether it would have been comfortable to take.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A historian argues that a general should have used aircraft to scout a battlefield. Which fact would most damage the argument?',options:['The battle was fought before practical aircraft existed','The general later admitted the battle was poorly planned','Other generals of the time made very similar choices','Aircraft proved decisive in wars fought decades later'],answer:0,why:'If the option did not exist, the criticism compares the decision with an alternative nobody could have chosen.'}]}},'decisions-that-look-obvious-afterwards_5':{pictureCaption:'The prediction goes into the box before anything has happened. Later it will show what was really expected, whatever memory says.',bridge:'Idea 1 showed that memory rewrites what you expected. This is the one record it cannot rewrite.',basics:{title:'Write your guess down first',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE SPORTS DAY GUESS',text:'Before sports day, you write on a scrap of paper: *I think Red team will win. I am fairly sure.* You fold it and put it in a drawer. Then Blue team wins.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why bother?',text:'Remember the first lesson? Once you know the end, your memory of your guess moves. In a week you might think you always said Blue. The paper does not move. It tells you what you really thought.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Before you find out how something ends, write down what you think will happen and how sure you are. Put the date on it. Don\u2019t change it later.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The guess goes into the box before anything happens. Later, the box will show what was really expected.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Make a guess card',steps:['Write what you think will happen.','Write how sure you are, from 1 to 10.','Add the date. Put it away.','Look again when you know the end.']},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it can sting',text:'Lots of your guesses will be wrong. That is not fun to see. But it is how you learn when to feel sure and when not to.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You think you will pass your swimming test. How could you check later if you were right to feel so sure?',options:['Write your guess and how sure you are now, with the date','Remember how you felt, and think about it after','Ask a friend after the test what you had said'],answer:0,why:'Your memory will move once you know the result, and so will your friend\u2019s. A dated note will not.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHAT DEFEATS HINDSIGHT',text:'Idea 1 showed that your memory of what you expected moves towards whatever happened. Knowing this does not stop it. Effort will not fix that. Only a note written before the outcome, dated and unrevised, can tell you later what you actually thought.'},{kind:'idea',text:'For any big call, write down what you expect, how sure you are and why, before the outcome. Date it and do not edit it. It is the only reliable defence against rewriting your own reasoning.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'ONE SENTENCE IS ENOUGH',text:'*12 March. I think the new hire will still be here in a year: 70%. Strong references, but she seemed unsure about the travel.* That is the smallest useful version: a prediction, a probability, a reason. It takes a minute.'},{kind:'term',word:'Calibration',meaning:'How well your confidence matches your accuracy. If the things you call 70% likely happen about 70% of the time, you are well calibrated.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the record gives you',text:'Over time the notes add up to something no amount of reflection can produce. It is a calibration record. You find out whether your confident calls come true more often than your hesitant ones. You also see where you are consistently overconfident.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why almost nobody does it',text:'The results are unflattering and the benefit comes later. Yet only someone with the record can see where their estimates go wrong. That is the first step to correcting them.'},{kind:'try',text:'For your next big call, write one paragraph on what you expect and why, with a probability. Date it, and do not edit it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You are about to decide whether to expand into a new market. Which record would let you judge this call fairly in two years?',options:['A dated note of your forecast, confidence and reasons, left unedited','The final board paper, kept updated as the project develops','Your memory of the meeting, backed by your colleagues\u2019 memories','A summary written once the first year\u2019s results have come in'],answer:0,why:'Only a note fixed before the outcome escapes hindsight. Updated papers, memories and later summaries have all been touched by the result.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE DRAWER',text:'You find a note you wrote two years ago about a decision you now remember as an easy call. In your own handwriting it says 55%, with two worries you had completely forgotten. The decision was not easy. You had simply lost the record of its being hard.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A dated record of what you expected, written before the outcome and never revised, is the only instrument that defeats hindsight. Kept over time, it shows whether your confidence tracks your accuracy.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why only a record works',text:'Idea 1 established that the shift in memory cannot be seen from inside or undone by warning. So no amount of honesty or reflection will recover a prior belief. The belief has to be stored outside the mind before the outcome arrives, where the outcome cannot reach it.'},{kind:'term',word:'Calibration',meaning:'The match between confidence and accuracy. Of all the calls you rated 70% likely, roughly 70% should come true. Only ratings recorded beforehand can show whether they do.'},{kind:'steps',title:'The minimum useful version',steps:['Write what you think will happen.','Give it a probability.','Say why, in a sentence.','Date it, and never edit it.']},{kind:'picture',caption:'The prediction goes into the box before anything has happened. Later it will show what was really expected, whatever memory says.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The record judges calibration over many calls, not one: a 70% call should fail about three times in ten, so a single miss proves little. It is also easy to game, by writing only safe predictions, or vague ones that cannot be scored. And it records what you expected, not whether the decision was right; that still needs the reconstruction of idea 2.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these defeats the purpose of the record?',options:['Updating last month\u2019s note when new information arrives','Writing down a probability you feel unsure about','Recording a prediction that later proves badly wrong','Keeping notes on decisions that seem fairly minor'],answer:0,why:'A revised note has been touched by later knowledge. New information belongs in a new dated note, with the old one left as it was.'}]}}}},2966,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'deliberate-difficulty_1':{pictureCaption:'Notes highlighted line by line until they feel easy. Beside them, a blank sheet, and a hand with nothing to write.',basics:{title:'Easy to read is not the same as learned',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE NIGHT BEFORE A TEST',prompt:'You read your notes three times. Now they feel easy. Do you know them?',options:['Yes. They feel easy, so I know them','Not sure yet. Easy to read is not the same as known'],answer:1,reveal:'The notes got easy because you have seen them so much. That tells you about the notes. It does not tell you what you can say with the notes shut.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When notes feel easy to read, it seems like you have learned them. Often you have not. The easy feeling comes from the page, not from your memory.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A SONG YOU KNOW',text:'A song comes on and you sing along. Easy. Now turn the music off and sing the second verse on your own. Most people get stuck. Knowing it when you hear it is not the same as knowing it.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO REVISE',left:{title:'Reading again',items:['Feels easy','You feel sure','Test goes worse']},right:{title:'Testing yourself',items:['Feels hard','You feel less sure','Test goes better']},note:'Studies of this keep finding the same thing. The way that feels good teaches less.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the easy feeling tricks you',text:'Each time you read a page, it gets smoother to read. Your brain notices that and says, *I know this.* But the test will not show you the page. It asks you to bring the answer up on your own.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The notes feel easy. The blank page shows what you really know.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time your notes feel easy, shut them. Write down all you can. Then check. The gap is how much the easy feeling fooled you.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Sam reads his spelling list five times. It feels easy now. What is the best way to check he knows it?',options:['Cover the list and try to write the words','Read the list a sixth time to be sure','Ask himself if the words look right'],answer:0,why:'Only writing them from memory shows what he knows. Reading again just makes the list feel easier.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE THIRD READ-THROUGH',picture:!0,text:'It is the evening before an exam. You are on your third pass through the highlighted notes, and every line looks familiar. You feel ready. The next morning the paper asks you to explain one of those lines from scratch, and nothing comes.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Ease of reading feels like learning, but it belongs to the material in front of you, not to your memory. People reliably mistake one for the other, and choose the study method that teaches them least.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Confidence and competence part ways',text:'In the experiments the essay describes, students who re-read rated their mastery higher and did worse on a delayed test than students who spent the same time testing themselves. The testers felt less sure and scored better. The feeling and the result moved in opposite directions.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE TRAP CLOSES',nodes:['Re-read the notes','They feel familiar','You conclude you know them','You stop, under-prepared'],note:'Every step feels reasonable. The mistake is treating the second step as evidence.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'OUTSIDE THE EXAM HALL',text:'You are driven to a new office every day for a week, and by Friday the route feels obvious. The first time you drive it yourself, you miss the turn. Seeing something repeatedly builds familiarity, not the ability to produce it unaided.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time revision feels easy, stop and test yourself cold. Mark it honestly. The gap between how it felt and how you scored is the size of the illusion.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Two colleagues prepare for a certification exam. One rereads the manual until it feels obvious. The other answers practice questions and gets many wrong. Who is probably better prepared?',options:['The one getting questions wrong, because recall is being built','The one rereading, because the manual now feels obvious','Whoever feels more confident on the morning of the exam','Both equally, since they spent the same amount of time'],answer:0,why:'Feeling sure came from familiarity with the manual. The wrong answers are uncomfortable, but they are practice at what the exam will demand.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE ESSAY\u2019S TARGET',belief:'A study session that felt smooth and confident was a good session.',reality:'The essay argues that this belief is usually backwards. Smoothness comes from repeated exposure to the page, so it rises fastest under the method that builds least.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Fluency is a signal about the material, not about your memory. Because learners read it as evidence of learning, they reliably choose methods that produce the feeling over methods that produce the result.'},{kind:'term',word:'Recognition',meaning:'Knowing something when it is in front of you. Recall, its partner, is producing it when it is not. Re-reading trains recognition; tests and real use nearly always demand recall.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The evidence it leans on',text:'The experiments compare re-reading with self-testing over the same time. Re-readers rate their mastery higher and perform worse on a delayed test. Self-testers rate it lower and perform better. The essay calls this a strong and repeated finding, and draws one conclusion from it: confidence and competence can move in opposite directions.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why they come apart',text:'The method that builds competence is unpleasant in the moment; the method that builds confidence is not. So the feeling of a session tracks its comfort. Hence the essay\u2019s rule: distrust ease. If studying feels smooth, you are probably rehearsing recognition rather than building recall.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'RECOGNITION, NOT RECALL',caption:'Every line of the notes has been marked and re-read. The blank sheet beside them asks for recall, and the hand has nothing to put on it.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Ease is not always a lie. Once you can recall something cold, it will also feel easy, and that ease is earned. What matters is where the ease came from: from seeing the page again, it proves little; after unaided recall, it means more. Read as \u201chard always means good\u201d, the idea is misused, and the essay\u2019s fourth idea draws that line.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Fluency belongs to the material, not to your memory.','Re-reading builds recognition; tests demand recall.','Distrust ease that came only from seeing the page again.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'WHICH SIGNAL COUNTS',prompt:'Which of these is the best evidence that you have learned a chapter?',options:['You can write its main argument from memory a few days later','Every page looks familiar when you flick back through it','You read it quickly and never felt confused at any point','You highlighted the key passages and reread them twice'],answer:0,why:'Only writing it from memory measures recall without the page. The others measure fluency, which the material itself supplies.'}]}},'deliberate-difficulty_2':{pictureCaption:'A full bucket stands by the well, ready to use. The hands haul their own up from the dark instead. The hauling is the practice.',bridge:'Idea 1 showed that easy re-reading misleads. This is the remedy: pull the material out of memory instead of putting it back in front of you.',basics:{title:'Try to remember before you look',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'SPELLINGS FOR FRIDAY',text:'You have ten words to learn by Friday. You could read the list again. Or you could cover it and try to write the words. You try. You get four wrong. That feels bad.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Trying to remember a thing helps you learn it more than reading it again. This is true even when you get it wrong. The hard try is doing the work.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The full bucket is the answer, sitting in your notes. The hands pulling the rope are you, pulling it up from memory.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A PATH IN THE GRASS',text:'Walk across a field the same way each day, and a path appears. Trying to remember is like walking the path. Reading the answer is like being carried across. Only the walking makes the path.'},{kind:'explain',title:'But what if I get it wrong?',text:'That is fine. A try that fails still helps, as long as you check the answer after. It just does not feel like it is helping. That is why most people skip it.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Cover and try',steps:['Read it once, and make sure you get it.','Cover it up.','Write or say all you can.','Uncover it and check what you missed.']},{kind:'try',text:'Tonight, close the book before you feel ready. Write what you can on a scrap of paper. Then look.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Mia has a history quiz. She has read the chapter once and she gets it. What should she do next?',options:['Shut the book and write what she can','Read the chapter two more times','Copy the chapter out in her best writing'],answer:0,why:'She already gets it. Now trying to bring it back will help most, even if she misses some.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TWENTY MINUTES FREE',prompt:'Next week you must present a report you have just read. You have twenty minutes now. Which use of them helps more?',options:['Read the report through again','Close it, write out its main points, then check'],answer:1,reveal:'Reading again feels safer and goes smoothly. Writing from memory is slower and leaves gaps. The attempt, gaps and all, is what makes the points available to you in the room next week.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Trying to recall material before checking it makes it stick much better than re-reading, even when the attempt fails. The struggle appears to be doing the work. It is also less pleasant. That is why almost nobody chooses it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why pulling beats looking',text:'Retrieving something appears to strengthen the route back to it. Re-reading strengthens only familiarity. Familiarity gives confidence without access. You feel you know the report. But in the meeting you cannot bring it up.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The full bucket is the page you could reread. The hands are hauling their own water up from the dark: slower, and the only one that is practice.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Testing yourself is for checking what you know once the learning is done.',reality:'Here the attempt is the learning. Recalling appears to strengthen the memory. So it belongs in the middle of studying, not only at the end.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The honest caveat',text:'It works best once you have understood the material at least once. Testing yourself on something you never followed produces frustration, not learning. So the order is: understand it, then recall it, then repeat.'},{kind:'try',text:'Close the book earlier than feels right. Write what you remember before you look anything up. Expect it to be incomplete.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You are learning the key terms for a new job. Which plan uses the idea best?',options:['Read the glossary once, then quiz yourself daily with it hidden','Read the glossary every morning until it feels second nature','Quiz yourself on day one, before reading the glossary at all','Keep the glossary open beside you while you work'],answer:0,why:'Reading once, then quizzing daily with the glossary hidden, puts understanding first and recall after. Quizzing before reading anything skips the understanding that recall depends on.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'DIAGNOSIS, THEN TREATMENT',text:'The essay\u2019s first idea was a diagnosis: re-reading builds fluency and fools you. This one is the treatment, and the claim is comparative and strong. Recall attempts beat review, the gap is large, and it has been well replicated.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Recall before review. Trying to reconstruct material without looking strengthens access to it in a way that re-reading does not, and the benefit holds even when the attempt fails.'},{kind:'term',word:'Retrieval practice',meaning:'Deliberately trying to bring material back from memory, without looking, as a way of learning it rather than only of checking it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The proposed mechanism',text:'Each act of retrieving appears to strengthen the path to the memory; re-reading strengthens only familiarity. Familiarity is the trap, because it yields confidence without access. That is why re-reading feels effective and tests badly. Note the word *appears*: the mechanism is offered as an explanation, held less firmly than the result it explains.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The full bucket is the answer handed over. The hauling hands are retrieval: slower, harder, and the only one of the two that is practice.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE SEQUENCE',nodes:['Understand it once','Close the book and reconstruct','Check the gaps','Repeat later'],note:'The essay is explicit about the order: comprehension, then retrieval, then repetition.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Retrieval has a prerequisite. Testing yourself on something you never understood produces frustration rather than learning. The idea is misused when it becomes a reason to skip comprehension, or when a failed attempt is never followed by checking the answer. And because it is markedly less pleasant, almost nobody chooses it voluntarily: knowing the idea is not the same as practising it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these misapplies the idea?',options:['Quizzing new students on a topic nobody has explained to them yet','Writing a summary from memory, then comparing it with the text','Pausing a reading to list the points so far, unaided','Answering practice questions and checking every wrong answer'],answer:0,why:'Retrieval strengthens access to something already understood. Without comprehension first there is nothing to retrieve, only frustration.'}]}},'deliberate-difficulty_3':{pictureCaption:'Above, the cards sorted into three neat piles. Below, the same kinds shuffled into one row, so the hand must decide what each card is.',bridge:'Idea 1 said your feel for a session misleads you. Mixing practice is the clearest case: it feels like getting worse while it makes you better.',basics:{title:'Mix it up, even when it feels worse',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A REAL MATCH',text:'In a football match, you never know what comes next. A pass, then a header, then a shot. Nobody shouts which one it will be. You have to see it and pick.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Two ways to practise',text:'Say you are learning three kinds of sum: add, take away and times. You could do ten of one kind, then ten of the next. Or you could mix all thirty up.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'Which way makes you better on the test next week?',options:['Ten of each kind, in a row','All of them mixed up'],answer:1,reveal:'Mixed up. But here is the odd part. While you practise, mixing feels worse. You make more mistakes. Most people think it is not working.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Mixing kinds of practice makes you worse while you practise and better later on. It feels like it is not working. It is.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why mixing helps',text:'When ten sums in a row are all adding, you never ask *which kind is this?* You just add. In a mix, you have to pick each time. Picking is the part the test needs.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'SORTED OR MIXED',caption:'At the top, the cards are sorted. Below, they are mixed. Now each card asks the hand: which kind are you?'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you practise sums or spellings, shuffle the kinds together. Expect more mistakes. Keep going.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Leo is practising tennis. Which plan uses this idea?',options:['Mix forehands, backhands and serves','Hit fifty forehands, then fifty backhands','Only practise the shot he is best at'],answer:0,why:'Mixing makes him choose the shot each time, like in a real game. It will feel harder, and work better.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE PRACTICE LOG',belief:'If my scores in practice are falling, the practice is not working.',reality:'With mixed practice, lower scores during the session go with better results later. The drop is the cost of deciding, every time, what kind of problem you are facing.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Practising several related skills in a mixed order makes you worse during practice and better later than practising each in a block. The hard part is keeping going while it feels like you are getting worse.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'A SPREADSHEET COURSE',text:'You are learning three kinds of formula. Blocked, you do twelve lookups, then twelve date sums, then twelve conditionals, and the last few of each set are effortless. Mixed, every question first asks *which tool is this?*, and you often get that wrong.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What blocking hides',text:'In a block you apply the same procedure again and again without ever choosing it. The worksheet chose for you. Real problems do not arrive labelled. Deciding which procedure applies is the part that transfers, and only mixing makes you practise it.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'NOBODY SORTED THESE',caption:'The sorted piles are the worksheet choosing for you. In the mixed row, the hand has to work out which kind each card is before it can do anything with it.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'THE SAME HOUR, TWO WAYS',left:{title:'Blocked',items:['Smooth, fast, few errors','Feels effective','Weaker on later tests']},right:{title:'Mixed',items:['Slower, more errors','Feels less effective','Stronger on later tests']},note:'Learners consistently rate mixed practice as less effective, even though they do better on the later test.'},{kind:'try',text:'Mix related practice into one session, in no fixed order, and accept feeling worse while you do it. Judge it by a test a few days later, not by how the session felt.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A sales trainee is practising answers to three kinds of customer objection. Which plan applies the idea?',options:['Shuffle all three kinds into one session of role-plays','Master the first kind fully before moving to the next','Drill the hardest kind until the scores stop rising','Repeat whichever kind felt smoothest the last time'],answer:0,why:'Shuffling forces the trainee to recognise which objection is in front of them, which is exactly what a real customer will demand.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'BEFORE THE RESULT',prompt:'Two groups practise the same three problem types for the same time. One works in blocks, the other mixed. Which scores higher during practice?',options:['The blocked group','The mixed group','Neither; they score the same'],answer:0,reveal:'The blocked group, reliably. On a later test the order flips. The essay cares about that reversal, and about what it says on judging your own practice.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Interleaving lowers performance during practice and raises it later. The larger lesson is that your sense of how well a session went is a poor instrument, biased towards whatever felt fluent.'},{kind:'term',word:'Interleaving',meaning:'Mixing problem types within one practice session, instead of finishing all of one type before starting the next. The second arrangement is called blocking.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The mechanism: discrimination',text:'Blocked practice lets you apply one procedure repeatedly without deciding which procedure applies. Interleaving forces that decision on every item. Choosing the procedure is the part that transfers to later problems, and it is the part blocking quietly removes.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'A DECISION ON EVERY ITEM',caption:'Sorted piles at the top, a shuffled row below. Only the row makes the hand tell the kinds apart, which is the step blocking leaves out.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHY THE FEELING LIES',nodes:['Blocked: same method, item after item','Errors fall, speed rises','The session feels productive','The choosing was never practised'],note:'Every signal you can feel improves. The one that matters later was never trained.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The finding worth carrying',text:'The essay treats interleaving as one instance of the point it opened with. Felt effectiveness and actual effectiveness diverge, and the bias runs one way, towards fluency. Any method that makes a session smoother deserves suspicion until a later test confirms it.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The benefit comes from choosing between skills that could be confused, which is why the essay speaks of related skills. By the essay\u2019s own mechanism, shuffling unrelated material would add switching without adding that decision. And mixing sharpens the choice between procedures you can already do. If you cannot yet do any of them, the essay\u2019s fourth idea applies: difficulty from a missing prerequisite only impedes.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'Staff say a new training scheme feels harder and less useful, but their error rates on the job have fallen. What does the essay suggest?',options:['Keep it: results on the job outrank how the sessions feel','Drop it: staff are the best judges of their own training','Revise it until the sessions feel as useful as they are','Offer it only to those staff who report enjoying it'],answer:0,why:'Felt effectiveness is biased towards fluency. When it disagrees with a later measure of performance, the later measure is the better guide.'}]}},'deliberate-difficulty_4':{pictureCaption:'Both halves of the wall are hard. The holds are hard in a way that builds a climber. The smooth half builds nothing.',bridge:'Ideas 1 to 3 all praised difficulty. This one sets the limit: only some kinds of difficulty teach.',basics:{title:'Good hard and bad hard',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'HARDER IS BETTER?',belief:'If hard practice helps you learn, then the harder the better.',reality:'No. Some hard things help you learn. Other hard things just get in the way. The trick is to tell them apart.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Hard is good when the hard part is the thing you are learning. If it is hard because of something else, like noise or a bad explanation, it just slows you down.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Both sides of this wall are hard. The side with holds makes you a better climber. The smooth side just makes you fall.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO KINDS OF HARD',left:{title:'Good hard',items:['Testing yourself on spellings','Sums mixed up, not in groups','A climb that is hard but possible']},right:{title:'Bad hard',items:['Messy writing you can\u2019t read','A loud room','Work far too hard for you yet']},note:'Good hard is in the thing you are learning. Bad hard is all around it.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'AT HOME',text:'Your brother is learning his times tables. Saying them without looking is good hard. Doing it with the TV on loud is bad hard. So turn off the TV. Keep the no-looking part.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time homework feels hard, ask: is the hard part the work, or something around it? Fix the thing around it.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your teacher hands out a worksheet. Which kind of hard should you ask to fix?',options:['The print is so faint you can\u2019t read it','You must answer without your notes','The sums are mixed up, not in groups'],answer:0,why:'Faint print has nothing to do with the sums. The other two are the good kind of hard.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'SPANISH AFTER WORK',text:'One evening you quiz yourself on verbs without the book. It is hard, and useful. The next evening your course gives an explanation you cannot follow. You are in a noisy caf\xe9, and the topic is two units ahead of you. Also hard. You learn almost nothing.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Harder is not always better. Difficulty that engages the process you are trying to strengthen helps. Difficulty from bad instructions, poor conditions or missing groundwork just gets in the way. Telling them apart is the whole skill of designing practice.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The test',text:'Ask where the difficulty sits. If it is in the thing being learned, such as recalling, choosing or rebuilding, it is productive. If it is in the delivery, such as the room, the handwriting, an unclear explanation or a missing step, it is friction.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'WHERE THE DIFFICULTY SITS',low:'In the conditions: noise, jargon, missing steps',high:'In the skill: recalling, choosing, rebuilding',note:'Keep the difficulty at the right-hand end. Strip it out at the left.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The holds are set hard but possible: that is the climbing. The smooth half is hard too, and teaches nobody anything.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the slogan is risky',text:'*Struggle is good* is easy to misapply. Make things needlessly hard and you get the discomfort of learning without the learning. You also reliably discourage the person on the receiving end.'},{kind:'try',text:'Take one thing you are practising and list what makes it hard. Mark each item *the skill* or *the conditions*. Remove one item from the second list this week.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You are training a new colleague on a system. Which difficulty should you remove?',options:['Instructions full of jargon they have not learned yet','Asking them to do the task once without your notes','Mixing three kinds of request in one practice run','Making them explain the steps back to you unaided'],answer:0,why:'The jargon is difficulty in the delivery. The other three put the effort into the skill itself.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'A WELL-MEANING TUTOR',text:'A tutor has heard that struggle helps learning. So she hands out a smudged, hand-copied worksheet, pitched a year ahead, in a noisy hall. Her students struggle a great deal. They learn little, and they come back less often.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Only difficulty that engages the processes producing learning is worth having. Difficulty in the conditions around the material adds load without engaging anything, and the slogan that struggle is good is easily used to justify it.'},{kind:'term',word:'Desirable difficulty',meaning:'The essay\u2019s term, from learning research, for difficulty that engages the processes that produce learning. The adjective does the work: it implies that other difficulty is not desirable at all.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'IN THE MATERIAL OR AROUND IT',left:{title:'Desirable',items:['Recall without notes','Spacing practice out over time','Mixed problem types']},right:{title:'Friction',items:['Poor handwriting','A noisy room','A needlessly obscure explanation','Material far above the learner']},note:'Difficulty in the material is productive. Difficulty in the delivery is friction.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the distinction is the point',text:'The first three ideas could be summed up as *struggle is good*, and the essay sees the danger in that. Applied loosely, it licenses making things gratuitously hard. The result is the discomfort of learning without the learning, and it reliably discourages the person subjected to it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Hard but possible holds on one half: difficulty in the climbing. A smooth wall on the other: difficulty in the conditions, which no climber learns from.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The line is not always clean. A noisy room is friction for most students but the very skill for someone training to work in noise: what counts as the thing being learned depends on the goal. And how far above a learner is too far varies by person, so the test has to be applied to a learner, not to a worksheet.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these misuses the idea that struggle helps learning?',options:['Writing practice questions in deliberately confusing wording','Asking students to recall last week\u2019s topic before revising it','Mixing old and new problem types in one homework','Spacing practice of one skill out over several weeks'],answer:0,why:'Confusing wording puts the difficulty in the delivery, not the skill. It produces the discomfort of learning without the learning.'}]}},'deliberate-difficulty_5':{pictureCaption:'The answer card is held back for a moment while the other hand is still working. The pause protects the attempt.',bridge:'Idea 2 said the attempt to recall does the work. This one protects that attempt from a correction that arrives too soon.',basics:{title:'Wait a moment before you help',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'HELPING WITH HOMEWORK',text:'Your little sister is stuck on a sum. She opens her mouth to answer. Before she can, you tell her the right answer.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'Did that help her learn it?',options:['Yes, now she knows the answer','Not much. She never got to try'],answer:1,reveal:'She heard the answer. But she never came up with her own, so she had nothing to check it against. The try is the part that teaches.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When someone is learning, wait a moment before you put them right. Let them have a go first. Then show them the right answer.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The answer card is held back for a moment. The other hand has to have a go first.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Should you always wait?',text:'No. Say someone is learning a move, like a tennis swing. Fix a mistake there straight away. If you wait, they keep doing it wrong, and the wrong way turns into a habit.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Count to five',steps:['Ask the question.','Let them say their answer.','Wait. Count to five in your head.','Then show them the right one.']},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend is learning the names of countries on a map. When should you tell him if he is right?',options:['Once he has said his answer','Before he has a chance to guess','Never. He should find out on his own'],answer:0,why:'Let him try, then tell him. Telling him first stops him trying. Never telling him leaves the mistake in place.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE EAGER TRAINER',text:'You are teaching a new hire the product range. You ask what a feature does, and as they draw breath you supply the answer. They nod. A week later they cannot tell you either.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Immediate correction can stop the learner producing an answer at all. A short delay keeps their attempt alive while still correcting it, and in some settings appears to give better long-term retention, though the effect is modest.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the pause protects',text:'The proposed mechanism: to learn from a correction, you hold your own attempt in mind and compare it with the right answer. Instant correction removes the need to hold anything. A short delay keeps the comparison, and the comparison is part of what makes the correction stick.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The answer exists, just held back. For a few seconds the learner\u2019s own attempt is the only one in play.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHEN TO WAIT, WHEN NOT TO',left:{title:'Pause briefly',items:['Facts and names','Concepts and explanations']},right:{title:'Correct at once',items:['Physical technique','Procedures repeated again and again','Any error that would become a habit']},note:'The essay is plain that the principle does not survive being applied everywhere.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you help someone learn a fact or an idea, count to five before you correct them. Let the attempt finish first.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Which of these mistakes should be corrected immediately?',options:['A trainee nurse repeating the wrong technique on a practice dummy','A colleague guessing the wrong year for a company milestone','A student giving the wrong capital city in a quiz','A new hire getting a product\u2019s name wrong in training'],answer:0,why:'An error in a repeated procedure gets practised and becomes a habit. For a one-off fact, a brief delay costs nothing.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE MOST QUALIFIED CLAIM',text:'The essay ends on its most hedged idea, and says so. Delaying feedback slightly appears to improve retention in several experimental settings. But the effect is smaller and less robust than retrieval or spacing, and it depends on what is being learned.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Correction that comes slightly late can beat correction that comes at once, because it leaves the learner\u2019s own attempt standing long enough to be compared with the answer. The benefit is real but bounded.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE PROPOSED MECHANISM',nodes:['The learner attempts an answer','Holds the attempt in mind','The correction arrives','Compares the two, and it sticks'],note:'Instant correction removes the second step, and with it part of what consolidates the correction.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The card is withheld for a beat while the learner\u2019s hand is still working. The delay is there to protect the attempt.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Retrieval, from the other side',text:'This is idea 2 seen from the teacher\u2019s chair. The attempt to recall does the work; an instant answer pre-empts the attempt. The learner receives the content without ever retrieving it, which is re-reading in another form.'},{kind:'limits',text:'For procedural skills, where a wrong step would be practised again and again, immediate feedback is clearly better: the error becomes a habit. So the rule is bounded. For factual and conceptual learning a short delay is probably beneficial and certainly not harmful. Applied everywhere, the principle fails.'},{kind:'recap',points:['A short delay preserves the attempt and the comparison.','The effect is smaller and less robust than retrieval or spacing.','Where an error would become a habit, correct it at once.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE BOUNDARY',prompt:'A driving instructor reads about delayed feedback. What should she conclude?',options:['Correct a missed mirror check at once, since it would become a habit','Save every correction for a debrief at the end of the lesson','Delay all corrections slightly, as the research recommends','Stop correcting, since learners must produce their own answers'],answer:0,why:'Driving is procedural: an uncorrected error is practised every time. The essay limits the delay to factual and conceptual learning.'}]}}}},2967,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'democracy-in-america_1':{pictureCaption:'Two houses, nearly the same. The only difference is one band of brick over a door, and that is where he is looking.',basics:{title:'Why small gaps sting the most',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'WHICH BUGS YOU MORE?',prompt:'Which would bother you more?',options:['A king far away lives in a huge palace','Your friend gets a bit more pocket money than you'],answer:1,reveal:'Most people pick the friend. The king is far off and nothing like you. Your friend is just like you. So the small gap feels like it says something about *you*.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When people are nearly equal, small gaps hurt more, not less. A gap you could have closed starts to feel like your fault.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO NOTICED THIS',text:'Alexis de Tocqueville came from France in 1831 to look at the United States. In France, some people were born lords. In the United States, no one was. He saw that this changed how a gap feels.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO KINDS OF GAP',left:{title:'Born into it',items:['A lord has more than you','You could never be a lord','It feels like the weather']},right:{title:'Equal on paper',items:['A friend has a bit more','You could have had it too','It feels like a mark against you']}},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two houses, almost the same. One has a little extra brick over the door. Look where the man is staring.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'ON THE TEAM',text:'Everyone on your team gets the same kit. Then one kid is picked as captain. It is only an armband. But it can sting more than a big prize at some other school, because it went to someone just like you.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your class all sat the same test. Your friend got one mark more than you. Why might that sting so much?',options:['It is so close it feels like your fault','One mark is a very big gap','Your friend must have cheated'],answer:0,why:'The gap is tiny. It stings because you were so close that it feels like it says something about you.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE PAY REVIEW',text:'Everyone on your team is paid within a narrow band. You find out a peer got a slightly bigger rise than you. The amount is trivial. You think about it for a week.'},{kind:'idea',text:'As conditions become more equal, the gaps that remain feel more galling, not less. Under fixed rank a difference is a fact. Among nominal equals, the same difference reads as a verdict on you.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Fixed rank',items:['The gap is a fact of the world','Nobody could have closed it','Nobody takes it personally']},right:{title:'Nominal equality',items:['The gap is a verdict','In principle, you could have closed it','So it is about you']},note:'Same gap. What changed is what it seems to say.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why closeness hurts',text:'Tocqueville, a French aristocrat who toured the United States in 1831, saw that equality changes what a difference means. A lord\u2019s wealth told a peasant nothing about the peasant. A colleague\u2019s rise seems to tell you something, because you were in the same race.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two near-identical houses. The only difference is a single band of brick, and that band is where his eyes go.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHY IT HAS AGED WELL',title:'Where you see it now',text:'It explains why comparison is fiercest in places that are, by any historical standard, remarkably level: a team on similar pay, a feed full of people broadly like you. A glimpse of the very rich stings less than a friend\u2019s slightly better holiday.'},{kind:'try',text:'When a small gap stings, ask: does it sting because it is large, or because it is close enough to feel like my fault? Name which. The second kind loosens once it is named.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A friend on the same salary as her flatmate is upset that the flatmate bought a slightly nicer car. What does Tocqueville\u2019s idea suggest?',options:['The sting comes from closeness, so naming that may ease it','The gap must really be large, or it would not hurt','Equal salaries should have prevented the feeling entirely','The flatmate must be showing off on purpose'],answer:0,why:'Equality does not remove the sting; it sharpens it. Seeing that the hurt comes from closeness is most of the relief.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'FRANCE TO AMERICA, 1831',text:'Alexis de Tocqueville came from a noble family that had suffered in the French Revolution. In 1831 he sailed to the United States with his friend Gustave de Beaumont, officially to study prisons. He came back with a book about what equality does to people.'},{kind:'term',word:'Equality of conditions',meaning:'Tocqueville\u2019s central phrase. Not equal wealth, but the absence of fixed rank: nobody is born above anybody else, and any position is, in principle, open to anyone.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Equality does not end comparison. It sharpens it. Once rank is gone, every remaining gap could in principle have been closed, so a small difference stops being a fact and becomes a judgement on you.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW A GAP BECOMES A VERDICT',nodes:['Fixed rank is abolished','Any gap could have been closed','So a gap implies a failure','Small, close gaps sting most'],note:'The closer the comparison, the harder it is to blame anything but yourself.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it has aged so well',text:'He argued that the desire for equality grows as equality grows, because each remaining inequality stands out against a level background. That explains narrow pay bands fought over fiercely, and feeds of near-peers that corrode more than any glimpse of the very rich.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two near-identical houses, one band of brick apart. Under fixed rank he would not look twice. Among equals he cannot look away.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The diagnosis can be turned on people with real grievances: call a complaint mere comparison and you need not check whether the gap is unjust. Some gaps are large, and some are rigged. And the contrast flatters the old order. Peasants did not always take a lord\u2019s wealth like weather, as the revolts of his own country showed.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of a gap that stung recently. How large was it, really? Would it have stung if the other person were nothing like you?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these is a *misuse* of Tocqueville\u2019s idea?',options:['Dismissing a pay discrimination complaint as envy among near-equals','Noticing that a colleague\u2019s small rise stings because you were so close','Expecting comparison to sharpen as a team\u2019s pay becomes more equal','Choosing to spend less time comparing yourself with near-peers online'],answer:0,why:'The idea explains why small gaps hurt. It does not show that every gap is small or fair. That still has to be checked.'}]}},'democracy-in-america_2':{pictureCaption:'Many hands pull plain chairs into a circle, and one more is carried in from outside. Nobody asked permission. They are just doing it.',basics:{title:'Get a few people together and do it',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE MESSY PARK',text:'The park by your school is full of litter. You could wait for the council to clean it up. Or you and two friends could bring bags on Saturday and do it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When something near you needs doing, get a few people together and do it. The more often people do this, the better they get at it.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHAT A VISITOR SAW',text:'Alexis de Tocqueville came from France to visit the United States almost 200 years ago. He was amazed. People there made groups for everything: to build a church, to start a hospital, to hand out books. In France, the state did those jobs.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Lots of hands pull chairs into a circle. One more chair is being brought in from outside. No one told them to.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it matters',text:'One person on their own can\u2019t do much against a big, strong government. A group can. And a group that has cleaned up a park knows how to work as a team next time something big comes up.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE RIDING A BIKE',text:'Nobody learns to ride a bike by reading about it. You learn by riding, and you stay good by riding. Working in a group is the same. Stop doing it, and you forget how.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'The school library is short of books. Which choice is most like what Tocqueville saw?',options:['A few pupils start a book swap themselves','Everyone waits for the head to fix it','One pupil writes a sad note and waits'],answer:0,why:'A few people joined up and did it. That is the habit he saw, and doing it makes the next time easier.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHO BUILDS THE HOSPITAL?',left:{title:'In France or England',items:['France: the government does it','England: a great nobleman does it']},right:{title:'In the United States',items:['A group of citizens forms','They raise the money and build it']},note:'Tocqueville visited in the 1830s. He thought this political skill set the country apart.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The habit of forming voluntary groups is what stops individuals facing the state alone. It is a skill, and like any skill it lasts only as long as people practise it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it is political',text:'One person on their own is powerless against a centralised authority. An association is the one body in between that can resist it without itself becoming a state. That is why he cared about church committees and road committees: they are where the muscle is kept.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'FROM ROADS TO LAWS',text:'Neighbours who once organised to fix a road have learned to call a meeting, divide the work and hold each other to it. When a bad law is proposed, they already know how to organise against it. People who have never done this tend to stay passive, whatever their rights on paper.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Hands pull plain chairs into a circle, and one more is carried in from outside. Nobody is in charge of this. That is the skill.'},{kind:'try',text:'When something local needs doing, find two other people and do it, rather than writing to someone who might.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A town has strong legal rights but no clubs, committees or local groups. What would Tocqueville predict?',options:['Its people will struggle to act together when it counts','Its rights on paper will keep it free and active','It will be run more fairly, since nobody is lobbying','Its government will shrink, with no groups making demands'],answer:0,why:'He thought rights without the habit of joining together leave people passive in politics. The skill, not the paperwork, is what lets them act.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHAT KEPT SURPRISING HIM',text:'Tocqueville returns to it again and again: Americans form associations not only for trade and politics but to build churches, distribute books, found hospitals and send missionaries. Where France would send an official and England a great lord, Americans simply formed a society.'},{kind:'term',word:'Intermediate body',meaning:'Any group standing between the lone individual and the central state: a club, a church, a township, a society. Strong enough to resist, small enough not to become a state itself.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Voluntary association is a democracy\u2019s main defence against centralised power, and it is learned only by doing it. Without the practice, formal rights leave a people inert.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why equality makes it necessary',text:'In an aristocracy, he argues, a few powerful men can act on their own and pull others along. In a democracy everyone is independent and individually weak. Unless they learn to combine freely, they are helpless, and the state steps in to do what they cannot.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE SKILL IS BUILT',nodes:['A small shared problem','Citizens organise to fix it','They learn to meet, plan, persuade','They can resist bigger power'],note:'Each round of practice makes the next one cheaper. Stop practising and the chain runs backwards.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A circle of chairs pulled together by many hands, with no one directing. The last seat is carried in from outside, by choice.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Associations can be closed, sectarian or hostile: the skill that founds a hospital can also organise a mob or keep outsiders out. And some needs are too large or too unequal for volunteers. Groups tend to serve those who already have time and confidence, which is one reason states do things too.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Associations stand between the individual and the state.','The skill is learned by practising on small things.','It can serve exclusion as easily as freedom.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A government wants to improve run-down neighbourhoods. Which approach would Tocqueville favour?',options:['Fund residents\u2019 groups to choose and run small projects themselves','Send skilled officials to fix every problem quickly and well','Hold a national vote on which neighbourhoods get the money','Hire a private firm to deliver the improvements on contract'],answer:0,why:'The officials might do a better job this year. Only funding residents to run projects builds the habit that lets citizens act for themselves next time.'}]}},'democracy-in-america_3':{pictureCaption:'A room of hands all raised together. One hand has come halfway up and stopped. Nothing visible is holding it down.',bridge:'Idea 2 showed citizens acting together. This one shows what the many, acting together, can do to the one who disagrees.',basics:{title:'When nobody says the thing',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'WHAT A TYRANT LOOKS LIKE',belief:'A tyrant is always one cruel king who throws people in jail.',reality:'Alexis de Tocqueville, a French visitor to the United States long ago, saw another kind. It is when most people agree, and the few who don\u2019t just stay quiet.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE CLASS VOTE',text:'Your class votes on where to go for the trip. Hands shoot up for the zoo. You want the museum. Nobody would punish you for saying so. But you keep your hand down.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'All these hands go up together. One hand started to rise and stopped. Nothing is holding it down, except the crowd.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The danger is not that most people make bad rules. It is that they make it feel too hard to disagree. No one is punished. No one says the thing.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why is that so bad?',text:'If nobody says the other view, the group never hears it. Maybe it was a good idea. Maybe it would have changed minds. Now nobody will ever know.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time a group agrees very fast, ask yourself: was there something nobody said? Why did it feel hard to say?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your team is picking a name. Everyone loves one, but you think it is a bit mean. What was Tocqueville warning about?',options:['You stay quiet just because everyone else agrees','The coach bans you from picking names','The team picks a name you do not like'],answer:0,why:'Nobody stopped you. The crowd made it feel too hard to speak. That silence is the danger he saw.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE MEETING',text:'The proposal is popular. Everyone senior has backed it. You can see a flaw, and nobody has mentioned it. Nothing would happen to you if you raised it. You leave the meeting having said nothing.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Tocqueville\u2019s tyranny of the majority is not about bad laws. It is about opinion: a democratic majority can make dissent socially impossible without punishing anyone.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The circle around thought',text:'In America, he argued, the majority draws a boundary around what may be thought. Inside it you are free. Step outside and you are not jailed. You are simply unread, unelected and avoided. No law is needed, which is what makes it so complete.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO KINDS OF CONTROL',left:{title:'An absolute king',items:['Strikes at the body','Bans the book','The idea survives in secret']},right:{title:'Majority opinion',items:['Leaves the body alone','Lets the book go unread','The idea survives, and nobody hears it']},note:'He meant the comparison to shock. The second, he thought, reaches further.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Every hand up together, and one that started to rise and stopped. Nothing visible is holding it back. That is the point.'},{kind:'try',text:'After your next meeting, ask: was there a view in the room that nobody voiced? Then ask why it was not sayable.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Which situation is closest to what Tocqueville meant by tyranny of the majority?',options:['A writer with an unpopular view is not banned, just never published or invited','A parliament passes a law that most voters strongly dislike','A government jails the journalists who criticise it','A small faction outvotes a larger one through a loophole'],answer:0,why:'No law and no punishment, just a social cost that makes the view unsayable. That is the pressure he thought no monarch could match.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'BEFORE YOU READ ON',prompt:'Which did Tocqueville think controls thought more completely?',options:['An absolute monarch with censors','A democratic majority with none','They are about the same'],answer:1,reveal:'He chose the majority, deliberately, to shock. A king strikes the body and the mind escapes. Majority opinion leaves the body alone and goes for the mind: the dissenter keeps his views, and nobody hears them.'},{kind:'idea',text:'In a democracy the majority\u2019s power is less over law than over opinion. It need not punish dissent. It makes dissent socially pointless, which reaches further than censorship ever did.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why nothing stops it',text:'In the first volume he argues that American majority opinion has no rival power. Public opinion, the legislature, the executive and even the jury all answer to it, so a dissenter has nowhere to appeal. He claimed to know of no country with less real independence of mind.'},{kind:'explain',title:'His remedies',text:'He does not ask the majority to restrain itself. He looks for structures that slow it: courts able to set laws aside, the jury, strong local government, and a legal profession with habits of its own. Obstruction, not virtue.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A room of hands raised in one direction. One hand has risen halfway and stopped. Nothing visible holds it. Nothing needs to.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Being disagreed with is not being silenced, and the idea is easily borrowed by anyone who wants an unpopular view treated as persecuted. He also overstated the case: Americans of the 1830s argued fiercely, and abolitionists spoke out, though they met mobs for it. The test is whether a view can be voiced at all, not whether it wins.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Majority tyranny works on opinion, not law.','It succeeds by social cost, not punishment.','Its remedies are structures that slow the majority down.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'Someone whose view is widely criticised calls that tyranny of the majority. What is the best reply?',options:['Criticism is not silence; the question is whether the view can be voiced at all','They are right, since criticism by many people is itself a form of tyranny','Majority tyranny only concerns laws, so their complaint does not apply','Unpopular views are usually wrong, so the pressure is doing its job'],answer:0,why:'Tocqueville\u2019s worry is a view that cannot be heard, not one that loses the argument. Open criticism is the opposite of the silence he described.'}]}},'democracy-in-america_4':{pictureCaption:'Every shelf in the larder is full. The hand is already holding up a shopping basket, ready for more.',bridge:'Idea 1 said equality makes the gap beside you feel personal. This is the same logic pointed upwards, at the next rung.',basics:{title:'Plenty, and still not enough',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE NEW BIKE',text:'You saved for months and got the bike you wanted. You loved it. Two weeks later, a friend got a faster one. Now yours feels a bit old.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When anyone can get to the top, there is always a next step to want. Not getting it feels like your fault. So people with plenty still feel they don\u2019t have enough.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The shelves are full. But the hand is already holding a basket, ready to go and get more.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHAT HE SAW',text:'Alexis de Tocqueville came from France to visit the United States almost 200 years ago. People there were better off than most people anywhere. Yet he saw them rushing and worried, never at rest. He wanted to know why.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE THE HORIZON',text:'Walk towards the horizon. However far you go, it stays just as far ahead. A ladder with no top rung is the same. You never reach the last step.'},{kind:'try',text:'Write down one thing you wanted a year ago and now have. Did getting it stop the wanting, or did a new want take its place?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Mia gets into the top maths group. A week later she is upset she is not top of that group. What would Tocqueville say?',options:['There is always a next step, so the worry moves up too','She is just greedy and should be ashamed','She should not have tried for the top group'],answer:0,why:'It is not a fault in Mia. When there is always a step above, the worry climbs with you.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'AMERICA, THE 1830s',text:'Tocqueville describes the free, well-off citizens of what he thought the luckiest country on earth. He finds them anxious. Serious even at play. Hurrying, as if afraid of missing the shortest road to happiness.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A society that removes the ceilings also removes the excuses. When any place is open to you, failing to reach it is personal. And there is always another rung above the one you reached.'},{kind:'equation',parts:['No ceiling','No excuse'],result:'Permanent restlessness',note:'Neither part is bad on its own. Together they turn open horizons into pressure.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE NEXT RUNG',text:'By any outside measure you have done well: a better job than your parents had, a home, some savings. But you are not comparing yourself with where you started. You are comparing yourself with the next rung, and it is always in view.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A larder full on every shelf, and a hand already holding up the basket for the next trip.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the diagnosis helps',text:'Tocqueville offers no cure. But knowing that the restlessness belongs to the situation, not to some flaw in you, changes the question. It is no longer why you are never satisfied. It is how much of the race you actually want to run.'},{kind:'try',text:'Write down the rung you are reaching for, and the one you would have been delighted with five years ago. If you have passed the second, ask what the first is actually for.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A friend has just been promoted and already feels behind. What does Tocqueville\u2019s idea suggest?',options:['The feeling comes from an open ladder, not from failing','They should aim higher, since feeling behind means they are','They are ungrateful and should count their blessings','They will feel settled once they reach the very top'],answer:0,why:'The restlessness belongs to a ladder with no top. Seeing that lets them decide how far to climb, instead of feeling it as a personal failure.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'A COMMON READING',belief:'Tocqueville thought ambition was a vice, and that Americans should want less.',reality:'He condemns nothing here. He diagnoses. The restlessness is not a moral fault but a property of a society without fixed ranks, and it would afflict anyone placed in it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Open horizons are felt as pressure rather than freedom. Where any position is possible, falling short is personal, and the horizon recedes as you approach it.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE SECOND VOLUME',title:'What he saw',text:'In a chapter of the second volume he asks why Americans are so restless in the midst of their prosperity. He describes a man who builds a house for his old age and sells it before the roof is on, and who plants a garden and leases it before the trees bear fruit.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'TWO SOCIETIES',low:'Fixed ranks: falling short is fate',high:'Open ranks: falling short is your fault',note:'The further a society moves to the right, the more prosperity and anxiety rise together.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The two halves of the mechanism',text:'First, comparison: equals measure themselves against each other, not against where they began. Second, time: a person set on worldly comfort, with one short life to win it in, is always in a hurry. Together they keep the comfortable anxious.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A larder full on every shelf, and the hand reaching past it with a basket. The plenty is real. So is the reaching.'},{kind:'limits',text:'A diagnosis is not a cure, and it can become an excuse: if restlessness is the situation\u2019s fault, nothing need change. The same restlessness also drove real invention and wealth. And he is describing free, comfortable citizens. For the enslaved and the poor of the same country, anxiety was about survival, not the next rung.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which rung are you reaching for now? What would you have thought, five years ago, of where you stand today?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A company scraps all fixed grades so anyone can rise to any role. On Tocqueville\u2019s logic, what should it expect?',options:['More drive, and more staff who feel personally behind','Less anxiety, since nobody is held back any longer','Less ambition, since there is no ladder left to climb','Little change, since restlessness is down to personality'],answer:0,why:'Removing ceilings removes excuses. Openness brings energy, and it also makes every shortfall feel personal.'}]}},'democracy-in-america_5':{pictureCaption:'A full basket passed over the fence. A kind act and a sensible one: next month it may come back the other way.',bridge:'Idea 2 showed Americans joining in. This is how they explained to themselves why they bothered.',basics:{title:'Helping others helps you too',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'NEXT DOOR',prompt:'Sam carries his neighbour\u2019s shopping. Asked why, he says *one day I might need her help.* What do you think?',options:['It doesn\u2019t count, since he wants something back','It is fine, and it means he will keep doing it','It is the best reason anyone could have'],answer:1,reveal:'Alexis de Tocqueville, a French visitor to the United States long ago, heard reasons like Sam\u2019s all the time. He thought they were not grand. But they made people help again and again.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Helping others often helps you too. That reason is not as noble as pure kindness. But far more people will act on it, every day.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A basket handed over the fence. It is kind. It is also smart. Next month, the basket might come back.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO RULES',left:{title:'Be a hero',items:['Give up a lot for others','A few people manage it','Most people don\u2019t']},right:{title:'Help, since it helps you too',items:['Small, everyday help','Almost everyone can do it','People keep it up']}},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE TAKING TURNS',text:'In a game, you let others have their turn. You do it partly to be nice. You also do it because then they let you have yours. So the game keeps going for everyone.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What it can\u2019t do',text:'Some good things cost you a lot and pay back nothing, like owning up when it gets you in trouble. *It helps me too* won\u2019t get you there. For that, you need something more.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your class wants the room kept tidy. Which plan would Tocqueville trust most?',options:['A rota, so each turn helps you too','Hope one very kind kid does it all','Tell everyone to be better people'],answer:0,why:'The rota does not need anyone to be a hero. It works because a tidy room helps everyone, you included.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE PARCEL',text:'Your neighbour asks you to take in a parcel while they are out. You do it without thinking. Asked why, you would probably say something like: it is useful to be on good terms, and one day I will need the favour.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Self-interest properly understood: serving others serves you too, so decent conduct is useful rather than heroic. Less noble than virtue, Tocqueville thought, and far more reliable.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why he backed the humbler doctrine',text:'A morality that demands heroism gets heroism from a few and nothing from the rest. A morality that asks only for enlightened calculation gets decent conduct from nearly everyone, every day. He thought a democratic age could not count on the first.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Morality of sacrifice',items:['Asks for great acts','A few rise to it','Most fall short']},right:{title:'Interest properly understood',items:['Asks for small, habitual acts','Almost everyone manages','It holds, year after year']},note:'One produces heroes. The other produces a reliable neighbourhood.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A full basket passed over the fence. A kind act and a sensible one: next month it may come back the other way.'},{kind:'try',text:'Look at one arrangement you rely on: a rota, a shared kitty, a favour system. Does it need anyone to be exceptional? If it does, redesign it so it doesn\u2019t.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A volunteer food bank keeps collapsing whenever its one devoted organiser burns out. What would Tocqueville suggest?',options:['Design it so ordinary members gain from helping run it','Find another organiser who is just as devoted','Appeal more strongly to people\u2019s better nature','Accept that good causes depend on rare people'],answer:0,why:'An arrangement that needs a hero fails when the hero tires. One that rewards ordinary people for taking part keeps going.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'HIS NAME FOR IT',word:'Self-interest properly understood',meaning:'Tocqueville\u2019s name for a doctrine he found almost all Americans holding: that by serving others you serve yourself, so virtue is a matter of enlightened calculation rather than sacrifice.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A morality built on enlightened self-interest produces few heroes but a great many reliable small acts. In a democratic age, he argued, that is the better bargain, though it fails exactly where sacrifice is required.'},{kind:'explain',title:'He was not admiring it',text:'He is candid that the doctrine is unheroic. It inspires no great self-sacrifice. He even notes that Americans often do themselves an injustice, explaining by calculation acts that came from real generosity. His case for it is comparative: it is what can actually be secured.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it fits a democracy',text:'In aristocratic ages, he says, moralists praised the beauty of virtue to a class raised to it. A society of equals has no such class. What reaches everyone is the argument that honesty, thrift and helpfulness pay over time, and so become habits.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'DESIGNING FOR IT',text:'A shared allotment can rely on one saint who waters every plot, or it can give each holder a watering day and a share of the harvest. The second never produces a hero. It also never collapses when the saint moves away.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A basket passed over the fence, with hands on both sides of it. Generosity and interest, in one gesture.'},{kind:'limits',text:'He admits the doctrine inspires no great sacrifice, and the limit follows. It is powerless when calculation and duty come apart: when doing right costs you and will never be repaid. A society that holds nothing higher will not know what to ask of people then, whether in war, in a crisis or in a lone act of honesty.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Enlightened interest gets small good acts from almost everyone.','Heroic virtue gets great acts from a few.','Where duty costs and never pays, interest goes silent.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'WHERE IT BREAKS',prompt:'In which case does self-interest properly understood give the weakest support?',options:['Reporting fraud at your firm when it will cost you your job','Keeping a promise to a customer who returns every week','Paying a neighbour back promptly for a favour','Being honest with a supplier you will deal with for years'],answer:0,why:'In the others, decency pays over time. Reporting the fraud costs you and may never repay you, which is where Tocqueville admits the doctrine falls silent.'}]}},'democracy-in-america_6':{pictureCaption:'A ring of plain chairs around a table with a local map. One chair is pushed back: someone has just stood up to have their say.',bridge:'Idea 2 praised the habit of joining. This is where Tocqueville thinks the habit is first learned.',basics:{title:'Learn to run big things by running small ones',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'THE SHALLOW END',text:'Nobody learns to swim in the deep sea. You start in the shallow end. You can make mistakes there and be fine. Then you are ready for deeper water.'},{kind:'idea',text:'People learn to run a country by first running small things near them, like a road or a school. Small things are real, but safe to get wrong.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'NEW ENGLAND TOWNS',text:'Alexis de Tocqueville came from France to visit the United States almost 200 years ago. In the north-east, in New England, he saw towns run by the people who lived there. They met in a hall and decided things together.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Chairs round a table with a map of the town on it. One chair is pushed back. Someone has stood up to speak.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'AT YOUR SCHOOL',text:'Say your school lets pupils choose where the new benches go. You argue, you vote, and you see how it turns out. Next time you will be better at it. That is how you learn to help run things.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why not let someone far away decide?',text:'A boss far away could choose for you, and might choose well. But then you never learn how. Tocqueville thought people who never practise end up just doing as they are told.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A club wants its new members to learn to lead. What would Tocqueville suggest?',options:['Give them one small, real job to run','Let them watch the leaders for a year','Give them a book about how to lead'],answer:0,why:'You learn to run things by running something small and real, where a mistake is not a disaster.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE USUAL PICTURE',belief:'Democracy is mainly voting in national elections every few years.',reality:'Tocqueville thought that was not nearly enough. Nobody learns to govern by voting once every four years. People learn on a road, a school and a local rate, decided with their neighbours.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Self-government is a skill, and skills need practice on something small enough to fail safely. The New England township, Tocqueville argued, is where Americans got that practice.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Primary schools for liberty',text:'His comparison: town meetings are to liberty what primary schools are to science. They bring it within reach and teach people to use and enjoy it. Without such local bodies, he says, a nation may have a free government and still lack the spirit of liberty.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Liberty at national level',items:['A distant idea','Seldom used','Easy to leave to others']},right:{title:'Liberty in the township',items:['A road, a school, a rate','Used often','Your neighbours expect you there']}},{kind:'picture',caption:'A ring of plain chairs around a table with a local map. One chair is pushed back: someone has just stood up to have their say.'},{kind:'try',text:'If you want people to take charge at scale, give them something small and real to decide first. Which one decision could you hand over this week?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A charity wants its volunteers to start shaping its national strategy. What would Tocqueville advise first?',options:['Let each local branch decide something real for itself','Give every volunteer a vote on the national plan at once','Send everyone a detailed report on the strategy','Leave strategy to experts and keep volunteers on tasks'],answer:0,why:'Taking charge at scale grows from practice on something small and real. A vote alone gives the right without the habit.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'SMALLEST UNIT FIRST',word:'Township',meaning:'In New England, a small unit of local government run largely by its residents, who met in town meetings to choose officials and decide on roads, schools and local taxes.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why he starts small',text:'Before Congress or the presidency, the first volume of Democracy in America describes the New England township. Tocqueville thought the whole system could only be understood from there, and his reason was educational rather than administrative.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Liberty held in principle is not liberty practised. Local institutions turn rights into habits, and without those habits a free constitution produces subjects rather than citizens.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW A CITIZEN IS MADE',nodes:['A small, real decision','Mistakes that can be survived','Skill and appetite grow','A people able to govern'],note:'Cut out the first step and the chain never starts, however good the constitution.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The warning',text:'He separates centralised government, which a nation needs, from centralised administration, which runs the details of local life from the capital. The second may be efficient. It also leaves rights on paper that nobody has the competence or the appetite to use.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A ring of chairs around a table and a parish map. One seat is pushed back. A neighbour has stood to argue about their own road.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Local self-rule is also local power. New England towns could be narrow, conformist and closed to outsiders, and women had no voice in the meeting. In American history, local majorities have often used local control to deny rights that only national action secured. Practice in self-government is not the same as justice.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which decisions in your organisation could be handed to the people closest to them, even if they might get them a little wrong?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A country adopts an excellent constitution but runs every local matter from the capital. What would Tocqueville predict?',options:['Rights on paper that few people know how to use','A free and active people, protected by good law','A rapid growth of local clubs to fill the gaps','Corruption, since local officials go unsupervised'],answer:0,why:'He thought the spirit of liberty is learned locally. A good constitution without that practice produces subjects, not citizens.'}]}},'democracy-in-america_7':{pictureCaption:'One window glows warm, curtains half drawn. The street outside, which everyone shares, is tidy and completely empty.',bridge:'Ideas 2 and 6 were the cure. This is the illness they treat: a quiet retreat into private life.',basics:{title:'Staying in your own little world',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE STREET PARTY',text:'Your street wants to plan a party. Nobody comes to the meeting. Everyone is at home, warm and happy with their own family. No one is being mean. But the party never happens.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Some people pull back into their own small circle of family and friends. It is not selfish. It looks sensible. But then no one looks after the world we all share.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One window is warm and bright. The street outside, which belongs to everyone, is empty.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'NOT THE SAME THING',left:{title:'Selfish',items:['Takes from others','Only thinks of me','Very old, and easy to spot']},right:{title:'Pulling back',items:['Kind to family and friends','Just stays at home','New, and easy to miss']}},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHY IT WORRIED HIM',text:'Alexis de Tocqueville visited the United States from France long ago. He feared this. If everyone stays home, someone else ends up running things for them. And people forget how to work together.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time something near you needs fixing, notice if your plan is just to make your own room nicer. Could you do one small thing outside it?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'The park near you is getting run-down. Which family is doing what Tocqueville warned about?',options:['They buy a nice swing for their own garden','They drop litter in the park on purpose','They join a group to fix up the park'],answer:0,why:'They are not being mean. They just made their own space nicer and left the shared one alone.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'A NEW WORD IN THE 1830s',word:'Individualism',meaning:'Tocqueville\u2019s word, then new in French, for a calm and thought-out feeling. It leads each person to withdraw into family and friends. The wider society is left to itself.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Individualism is not selfishness. It is a retreat into private life that looks reasonable. And a people who have retreated will accept a state that manages everything for them.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Selfishness',items:['A vice as old as the species','Loves self above all','Corrupts every virtue']},right:{title:'Individualism',items:['A product of democratic ages','Loves its own small circle','First drains public life']},note:'The second is harder to spot because it looks like a decent, quiet life.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHERE THE RETREAT LEADS',nodes:['Each retires into a private circle','Public life is left to whoever remains','Habits of acting together fade','Alone before a central power']},{kind:'picture',caption:'One warm window with its curtains half drawn, and a well-kept street outside with nobody in it.'},{kind:'try',text:'Notice when you meet a public problem by making your own circle more comfortable. That is the move Tocqueville describes.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Which of these is individualism in Tocqueville\u2019s sense?',options:['Responding to rising local crime by fitting better locks and staying in','Cheating the neighbours to get ahead in business','Joining a residents\u2019 group to press for better street lighting','Refusing to share anything with your own family'],answer:0,why:'Nothing about it is selfish or wrong. It is a sensible private retreat. That is just why it is easy to miss.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'A WORD AND A WARNING',text:'In the second volume Tocqueville stops to define a word he says is recent. Earlier generations, he notes, had a word only for selfishness. Individualism is something else: a mature and calm feeling, not a passion, and democracy produces it as naturally as aristocracy produced obligation.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Equality dissolves the chains of obligation that tied people together, and each withdraws into a small private world. The withdrawal is reasonable. Its end point is citizens who let a central power manage their common life.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why equality produces it',text:'Aristocracy bound everyone into a chain, from peasant to king, each owing something to the next. Democracy breaks the chain. Nobody owes anybody, which is a real gain, but it removes the structures that made acting together automatic. Joining now has to be chosen.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where it ends',text:'Later in the same volume he imagines a new kind of despotism: an immense, mild power that spares citizens the trouble of running their own affairs and keeps them in a permanent childhood. Individualism is how a people walks into it, with nobody forcing them.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A row of houses at dusk: one window warm, curtains half drawn, and a street everyone shares, well kept and deserted.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The remedy',text:'He does not answer with sermons. Local government and voluntary associations drag people back out, by giving them things that can only be done together. That is why townships and associations matter so much to him.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Private life is not a failing. For many people, caring for a family takes all the time they have, and a theory that reads that as civic decline can turn into scolding. His contrast also flatters the old order, whose chains of obligation were often chains of domination. The warning is about a society, not a verdict on anyone.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Individualism is a calm retreat, not selfishness.','Equality breaks the old chains, so joining becomes a choice.','The remedy is shared work, not sermons.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'Someone objects that time spent with family is a good thing, not a danger. What is Tocqueville\u2019s best reply?',options:['It is good, but if everyone stops there, public life falls to a central power','It is selfish, and people ought to feel ashamed of retreating','It is harmless, because a democracy can run itself without citizens','It is a danger only for those who hold public office'],answer:0,why:'He agrees it is not a vice. His fear is the sum of many such sensible retreats, and his remedy is institutions that draw people out.'}]}},'democracy-in-america_8':{pictureCaption:'A corridor of past rulings, floor to ceiling. One volume is marked for checking. Nothing in this place moves fast, and that is its use.',bridge:'Idea 3 described a majority that nothing seemed able to stop. This is the brake Tocqueville found for it.',basics:{title:'The slow part that keeps things safe',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A PUZZLE',prompt:'Alexis de Tocqueville came from France to study the United States. There, most people could change things fast. He looked for something to slow them down. What did he find?',options:['Kings and lords','The army','Lawyers'],answer:2,reveal:'Lawyers! There were no lords to do the job. But lawyers are trained to check what was decided before. That makes them slow and careful.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A group that can change things fast needs something slow to check it. Tocqueville found that slow part in lawyers. Not because they were good people, but because of how they were trained.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE BRAKES ON A BIKE',text:'Brakes don\u2019t make a bike go. They slow it down. But nobody would ride down a steep hill without them. Brakes are what make going fast safe.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Shelves and shelves of old rulings. One book is marked. Before anything new, someone checks what was decided before.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why are lawyers slow?',text:'Lawyers learn to look at what was decided in the past. They like rules and proper steps. So when a crowd wants to change something today, they ask: what does the rule say? What did we do last time?'},{kind:'explain',title:'Are they always right?',text:'No. Sometimes they slow down a good change too. And, we would add, they may like rules partly because rules give them work. Tocqueville did not say lawyers were perfect. He just thought a fast system needs some slow part.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your club can change its rules with a quick show of hands. What would Tocqueville want?',options:['A rule that big changes wait a week','No rules, so the club can move fast','One leader who decides it all'],answer:0,why:'Waiting a week is a brake. It gives time to think before a quick mood becomes a rule.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE EMERGENCY MOTION',text:'Your residents\u2019 association is furious about a decision and wants to vote tonight to change its rules. One member asks to check the constitution and wait for the next meeting. Everyone groans. She may be the most useful person in the room.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Tocqueville looked for an aristocracy in a country that had abolished one. He found it in the legal profession. Its habits of form and precedent pull against sudden popular enthusiasm. A democracy needs something that does.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Habits made by training',text:'Lawyers pick up a taste for order and formality. They also gain an instinctive attachment to precedent. That makes them conservative in a specific sense: they prefer what has been established, whatever their politics. He does not say they are virtuous. He says their training makes them slow.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'The majority',items:['Impatient','Changeable','Moved by the mood of the day']},right:{title:'The legal class',items:['Slow','Procedural','Moved by what was decided before']},note:'And lawyers filled the courts and much of the legislature. Through the jury, their habits reached ordinary citizens.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A corridor of past rulings, floor to ceiling, one volume marked. Nothing in this place moves fast, and that is its use.'},{kind:'try',text:'In any system you run that can move fast, find what is structurally slow. Before removing it, ask what it is protecting you from.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A start-up wants to drop its two-person sign-off on large payments because it slows things down. What does Tocqueville\u2019s idea suggest?',options:['Keep it: its slowness is a brake on rash decisions','Drop it: speed is the main advantage of a small firm','Keep it only until the founders trust each other','Replace it with an all-staff vote on every payment'],answer:0,why:'The step is slow on purpose. Removing every brake leaves a fast system with nothing to check its enthusiasms.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'A SEARCH FOR A BRAKE',text:'Having described a majority with almost no rival, Tocqueville goes looking for anything in America that might temper it. Aristocracy had been abolished. In a chapter of the first volume he concludes that the nearest thing left is the legal profession.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Lawyers are the closest thing a democracy has to an aristocracy: trained to love form and precedent, present throughout public life, and instinctively slow. That slowness is the counterweight a majority needs.'},{kind:'term',word:'Precedent',meaning:'A past decision treated as a guide for later cases. A mind trained in precedent asks first what was done before, and needs a reason to depart from it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it reaches so far',text:'In America, he notes, almost every political question sooner or later becomes a judicial one. So the lawyer\u2019s habits spread: through judges who can set laws aside, through the many lawyers in legislatures, and through the jury, which teaches ordinary citizens to think a little like judges.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A corridor of bound rulings, floor to ceiling. One volume is marked for checking. Nothing here moves quickly, which is what it is for.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The brake is not disinterested. Lawyers, we would add, profit from the complexity they maintain, and the same instinct that blocks a dangerous change blocks a necessary one. Precedent has preserved injustice as faithfully as liberty. He is not praising lawyers. He is pointing at the only brake left in a system that had removed the others.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What is the slowest, most procedural part of a system you work in? What would happen in its first month without it?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HARD CASE',prompt:'A court blocks a popular reform on procedural grounds. On Tocqueville\u2019s view, how should we judge it?',options:['The brake may be useful or obstructive; slowness alone does not settle it','It is always right, since slowing the majority is the court\u2019s job','It is always wrong, since the majority should rule in a democracy','It proves the lawyers are acting purely in their own interest'],answer:0,why:'He defends having a brake, not every use of it. And the legal instinct can obstruct necessary change as readily as dangerous change.'}]}},'democracy-in-america_9':{pictureCaption:'The fence is long gone. Only its post holes are left. But the worn path still turns at the old line and never crosses it.',bridge:'The earlier ideas describe equality among white citizens. Here Tocqueville looks hard at the people that equality shut out.',basics:{title:'A harm that would outlast the law',beats:[{kind:'timeline',kicker:'WHAT HAPPENED WHEN',points:[{when:'1831',what:'A young Frenchman, Alexis de Tocqueville, tours the United States.'},{when:'1835',what:'He writes that slavery is the worst danger the country faces.'},{when:'1861',what:'A civil war starts. Slavery is at its heart.'},{when:'1865',what:'Slavery is ended by law.'}],note:'He also said the trouble would not end there.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHAT SLAVERY WAS',text:'In the southern states, millions of Black people were owned by others. They were forced to work for no pay. They could be sold, and their families split up. It was deeply wrong, and the law allowed it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Tocqueville saw that ending a deep wrong by law would not end the harm. Racism, treating people as worse because of their race, would last long after the law.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How could he tell?',picture:!0,text:'In most northern states, slavery had already ended. But Black people there were still shut out and looked down on. He thought it was, if anything, worse there. A new law had not changed minds.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'When a bad law ends, the harm ends too.',reality:'He was right. After slavery ended, southern laws kept Black people apart from white people and stopped most from voting. Violence backed those laws up. They lasted until the 1960s. People had to fight all over again.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where he went wrong',text:'He could not picture Black and white people living as equals. Others in his time could, Black Americans above all, and fought for it. Seeing a problem is not the same as seeing the fix.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Why did Tocqueville think ending slavery would not end the problem?',options:['Racism would last after the law changed','The law would soon be brought back','Most people did not want it to end'],answer:0,why:'He saw racism even where there was no slavery. So he knew a new law would not change minds on its own.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'WHAT HE SAW, 1831\u201332',text:'On his journey Tocqueville crossed between slave states and free states, and compared them. He also watched Choctaw families, forced off their land, crossing the Mississippi in winter. The last long chapter of his first volume is about what he saw.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Slavery, Tocqueville argued in 1835, was the gravest danger to the Union. Its effects would outlast it, because the prejudice it bred ran deeper than any law could reach.'},{kind:'explain',title:'His evidence',text:'He noticed that racial prejudice seemed stronger in states that had ended slavery than in those that kept it. Free Black people in the North had some rights on paper and were shut out in practice. So, he concluded, law could end the institution but not the attitude.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The fence came down long ago and its post holes are filled in. The path worn beside it still turns at the line. The law went; the habit stayed.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Was he right?',text:'The Civil War came about twenty-five years later, and slavery was abolished in 1865. Legal segregation and the loss of the vote followed. In much of the South they lasted until the 1960s. The effects outlasted slavery, as he said they would.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'A person who sees a problem clearly will also see the fix.',reality:'Tocqueville saw the fault with rare precision. Yet he could not imagine a shared, equal society, and expected the races either to separate or to clash. His analysis and his remedy came apart. That is why the chapter is worth reading.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of an injustice built into the base of something you know, and later removed on paper. Which of its effects are still running?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A city ends a decades-old rule that kept one group out of certain neighbourhoods. What does Tocqueville\u2019s analysis predict?',options:['The pattern will persist long after the rule is gone','The neighbourhoods will mix within a few years','Ending the rule shows the prejudice has already gone','Nothing will change, so ending the rule was pointless'],answer:0,why:'Removing the injustice is needed, but it is not enough. The habits and gaps it created keep running until they are tackled directly.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE END OF VOLUME ONE',text:'Democracy in America mostly describes equality among white citizens. Its last, long chapter turns to the others: Indigenous nations being driven west, some of whose removal Tocqueville saw himself, and the millions of enslaved Black people. Here his tone darkens.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Writing in 1835, Tocqueville named slavery as the gravest threat to the Union and predicted that its consequences would long outlast it. He was right about the danger and poor on the remedy.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HIS ARGUMENT',nodes:['Slavery creates a racial caste','Prejudice grows around the caste','Law abolishes the institution','Prejudice survives the law'],note:'His evidence for the last step was the North, where prejudice seemed stronger than in the slave states.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The last step of his argument, seen from above: the fence is gone, and the path still keeps to its line.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The prediction',text:'He did not foresee the exact course: civil war about a quarter of a century later, abolition in 1865, then segregation and the loss of the vote, lasting into the 1960s. But that course has the shape his argument implied. Removing the institution did not remove what it had built.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'The analysis',items:['Named the central fault precisely','Saw prejudice outlasting law','Used the North as evidence']},right:{title:'The prescription',items:['Saw no path to equality','Speculated about separation','Shared his era\u2019s assumptions']},note:'An observer can diagnose a society precisely and still fail to imagine its cure.'},{kind:'limits',text:'His framing often treats the presence of Black Americans, rather than slavery and white prejudice, as the problem, which is the very assumption he failed to escape. Others in his time, Black abolitionists above all, did imagine an equal, shared society. And in France he supported the brutal conquest of Algeria. His clarity about America did not reach his own country\u2019s empire.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What injustice was built into the foundation of an institution you know? Which of its consequences outlived the formal fix?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A country abolishes a caste system in law. Using Tocqueville\u2019s chapter, which expectation is best founded?',options:['Discrimination will persist long after the change in the law','The law will end discrimination quickly, since attitudes follow law','Discrimination will be worst where the caste system was strongest in law','Outside observers will see the remedy more clearly than the problem'],answer:0,why:'Law can end an institution without reaching the attitude, as Tocqueville argued from the North. Strength in law is no guide to where prejudice will be worst.'}]}}}},2968,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={enchiridion_1:{pictureCaption:'A few cards kept close, a great heap pushed to the far side. The near few are yours to handle. The heap never was.',basics:{title:'Two piles: yours and not yours',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE NIGHT BEFORE THE MATCH',text:'It is the night before a big match. You worry it will rain. You worry the ref will be unfair. You worry the other team is bigger. You lie awake for an hour.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO SAID THIS',text:'A man named Epictetus taught this idea in Greece, about 1,900 years ago. He had been born a slave. He knew a lot about things he could not control.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Some things are up to you: what you think, what you try, what you choose. Some things are not: the weather, other people, what they say. Sort which is which before you get upset.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Up to you',items:['How hard you try','Whether you warm up','What you say to your team']},right:{title:'Not up to you',items:['The rain','The ref','How big the other team is','The final score']},note:'Most of the worry is on the right. Most of what you can do is on the left.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The hands keep a few cards close and push the big heap away. Most worries belong in the far heap.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why sort first?',text:'Once you are upset, it is hard to think straight. So sort early, while you are calm. Ask: is this mine? If yes, do something. If no, put it down.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time a worry comes, ask one question first: is this up to me? If yes, do one thing about it. If no, let it go.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You are worried a friend will not like your birthday present. Which part is up to you?',options:['Choosing the present with care','Whether your friend likes it','What the other guests think of it'],answer:0,why:'You can choose with care. How your friend feels about it is up to them.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'A SLAVE WHO BECAME A TEACHER',text:'Epictetus was born a slave in the Roman Empire in the first century, was later freed, and became one of its most respected teachers. His student Arrian wrote the teaching down in a short handbook. This idea comes first.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Some things are up to you: your judgements, your intentions, what you pursue and avoid. Your body, your reputation, your position and other people are not. Most distress comes from treating the second list as if it were the first.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Up to you',items:['What you judge to be true','What you aim at','What you go after or avoid']},right:{title:'Not up to you',items:['Your health and body','Your reputation','Your job title','What anyone else does']},note:'He is not saying the right-hand column does not matter. He is saying it is not yours to command.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why before the feeling',text:'Once distress has arrived, you are arguing with it, and usually losing. Done in advance, the sorting is a filing decision: this one I can act on, that one I cannot. It costs a minute and can save an evening.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The cost of getting it wrong',text:'Treat what is not yours as yours, he warns, and you will be blocked, you will grieve, and you will blame everyone in sight. Not because the world is cruel, but because you staked yourself on something you never controlled.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A few cards kept close, the great heap pushed to the far side. The near pile is small, and it is the only one you can work with.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before your next decision that matters, write the two columns. Put each worry where it belongs. Then spend your effort only on the left.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You have a job interview tomorrow and cannot sleep. Which of these belongs in the first column?',options:['How well you prepare your answers tonight','Whether the panel takes to you','Whether a stronger candidate has applied','Whether the role will still exist next year'],answer:0,why:'Your preparation is an act, and acts are yours. The panel, the other candidates and the company\u2019s future are not.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE HANDBOOK\u2019S FIRST CUT',text:'The Enchiridion is a pocket summary of Epictetus\u2019s teaching, compiled by his student Arrian in the second century. It does not open with a definition of virtue or happiness. It opens with a division: some things are up to us, and some are not. The rest of the book assumes this cut.'},{kind:'term',word:'Up to us',meaning:'The usual English for the Greek *eph\u2019 h\u0113min*. It covers opinion, the impulse to act, desire and aversion: the workings of your own judgement. Body, property, reputation and office fall outside it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Your judgements, intentions, desires and aversions are up to you. Your body, reputation, position and other people are not. Distress is what follows from staking yourself on the second group as if it belonged to the first.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE MISTAKE RUNS',nodes:['You want something not up to you','You treat it as yours to secure','The world does not oblige','Blocked, grieving, blaming'],note:'His warning in the opening chapter: you will end by blaming gods and men alike. The fault was in the first step.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it comes before the feeling',text:'He tells the student to meet each harsh impression with a test: is this among the things up to me? If not, be ready to say it is nothing to you. Done in advance, this is a quick sorting. Done in the middle of distress, it is an argument you are already losing.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A few cards kept close, the heap pushed away. The sorting itself is quick. What it saves you from is not.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The line is cruder than life. Health, exam results and friendships are partly shaped by what you do. A common modern repair is to put the effort in the first column and the outcome in the second. Misused, the division becomes a reason to shrug off what you could change, or what others suffer. He meant it to discipline wanting, not to excuse inaction.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Take a worry you carried today. Which parts of it were up to you, and which part did you spend most of the day on?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these misuses the division?',options:['Not voting, because the election result is not up to you','Preparing hard for a match, then accepting the score','Apologising, while leaving forgiveness to the other person','Saying what you think, without needing others to agree'],answer:0,why:'Casting a vote is an act, and acts are yours. Only the result belongs in the second column.'}]}},enchiridion_2:{pictureCaption:'One street seen through two lenses: plain, a bus pulling away; tinted, a storm. The street is the same. The judgement is the lens.',bridge:'Idea 1 split the world into what is yours and what is not. This one shows why that helps: the hurt sits in your judgement, which is yours.',basics:{title:'It\u2019s not the thing. It\u2019s what you think about it.',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'SAME SPILL, TWO KIDS',prompt:'Two kids spill juice on their shirts at lunch. One laughs. One cries. What was different?',options:['The juice','What each one told themselves','The shirts'],answer:1,reveal:'The juice was the same. One kid thought, *oops, it will wash out.* The other thought, *everyone saw, I look silly.* That thought made the tears.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Things that happen do not upset you on their own. What upsets you is what you think about them. And what you think is something you can look at again.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'A TEACHER FROM LONG AGO',text:'A teacher named Epictetus said this about 1,900 years ago. He said even big, scary things are not bad all on their own. The thought *this is terrible* is what does the harm.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW AN UPSET IS MADE',nodes:['Something happens','You tell yourself what it means','You feel it'],note:'You can\u2019t always change step one. You can look again at step two.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Same street. Same bus. Through the clear lens, a bus is just leaving. Through the tinted lens, the whole street looks like a storm. Your thought works like that lens.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'So I should just pretend bad things are fine.',reality:'No. The thing still happened. You do not have to like it. You just check if your thought about it is fair, or much too big.'},{kind:'try',text:'When something stings, say your thought out loud. Then ask: would a calm friend think the same thing?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You are not picked for the school play. Which part can you still change?',options:['Your thought that this means you are no good','The list of who got picked for each part','The teacher\u2019s choice of who plays the lead'],answer:0,why:'The list is done. Your thought about what it means is still yours to check.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'SAME DAY, SAME TEAM',text:'Two people are made redundant on the same morning from the same team. One tells it as a humiliation and cannot look at job listings for a month. The other tells it as an inconvenience and has interviews the following week.'},{kind:'idea',text:'You are disturbed not by what happens but by your judgement about what happens. The event is fixed. The judgement is the part that keeps hurting, and it is yours to revise.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'What happened to both',items:['The job ended','Same date, same team','Same terms']},right:{title:'What each one said',items:['\u201cI have been humiliated\u201d','\u201cThis is an inconvenience\u201d']},note:'Everything that differed between the two people is in the right-hand column.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the sentence does the work',text:'The event happened once. The sentence you attached to it repeats every time you think about it, and each repetition brings the feeling back. That is how an old event can still hurt years later: you are still telling it the same way.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Hold both lenses up to the same street and you get two different afternoons. The bus left either way. What differs is the glass, and the glass is yours to change.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'This means pretending nothing bad happened.',reality:'Epictetus does not deny the event. He separates it from the verdict. The redundancy happened. Whether it is a humiliation is a judgement, and a judgement can be examined.'},{kind:'try',text:'When something stings, write down the sentence you have attached to it. Ask whether a calmer person, given the same facts, would have written the same sentence.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A friend cancels dinner at the last minute. Which response uses the idea?',options:['Notice the sentence \u201cthey don\u2019t value me\u201d and test it against the facts','Tell yourself the cancellation does not matter in the slightest','Decide the friend is unreliable and stop inviting them round','Wait for the hurt to fade before you think about it at all'],answer:0,why:'The idea is not to deny the event or to bury the feeling. It is to find the judgement doing the hurting and check it.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'CHAPTER FIVE',text:'The handbook states it flatly: people are disturbed not by things but by their opinions about things. The example is death. If death were terrible in itself, it would have seemed so to Socrates, who met it calmly. What is terrible is the opinion that death is terrible.'},{kind:'idea',text:'An event and your judgement of it are two different things. Only the judgement disturbs you, and only the judgement can be revised, so that is where the work is done.'},{kind:'term',word:'Impression',meaning:'The Stoic word (*phantasia*) for how a thing first appears to you, often with a verdict already built in. Epictetus teaches that an impression can be examined before you agree with it.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHERE THE WORK HAPPENS',nodes:['An event','An impression with a verdict','You assent, or you hold back','Disturbance, or none'],note:'The event and the impression arrive unasked. The third step is the one he calls yours.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Both lenses show the same street and the same bus. The tinted one hands you a storm along with the view, as an impression arrives with its verdict built in. Holding back assent is noticing the tint.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'A TEST CASE',text:'Two people lose the same job. Strip both accounts to what a stranger would accept: a role ended on a given date. Everything else, humiliation in one telling and inconvenience in the other, was added by the person telling it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Not a modern discovery',text:'Cognitive therapy rests on the same structure: situations do not produce emotions directly; interpretations do, and interpretations can be tested. Albert Ellis, one of its founders, cited this teaching of Epictetus as a source.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Some judgements are accurate. A lost job can be a real harm, and grief is not a reasoning error. Epictetus goes further than most readers will, holding that only your own choices are truly good or bad. Yet he also says that when someone is grieving you should sympathise, even lament with them outwardly. The idea is a discipline for your own judgements, not a lecture for others.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'An objector says: \u201cOn this view, a bereaved person is simply thinking badly.\u201d What is the best reply?',options:['Examine your own judgements; do not hand verdicts to the grieving','Yes, and they should be told at once to correct their thinking','The idea only applies to small things, like missing a bus','Grief is a feeling, so judgements have nothing to do with it'],answer:0,why:'Epictetus himself tells you to sympathise with someone in grief. The practice is turned on your own impressions, and examining a judgement is not the same as condemning it.'}]}},enchiridion_3:{pictureCaption:'Rain on a table laid for a party, and someone already carrying the food indoors, unhurried. No argument with the weather, just the next step.',bridge:'Idea 2 was about your judgement after something happens. This one is about the wish you carried into it.',basics:{title:'Want what is really happening',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'SPORTS DAY',text:'You have waited all term for sports day. You wake up and it is pouring with rain. Sports day is off. You spend the whole morning saying, *it should not have rained.*'},{kind:'explain',title:'What that does',text:'Saying *it should not have rained* changes nothing. The rain is already here. But the moaning uses up your whole morning. You are fighting something that has already won.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Don\u2019t wish things were different from how they are. Wish for what is really happening. Then you can start doing something with it.'},{kind:'steps',title:'When a plan falls through',steps:['Say what has really happened.','Say it again, with no *should* in it.','Ask what you can do from here.','Do the first small thing.']},{kind:'myth',belief:'This means giving up and not caring.',reality:'No. You still try hard at the things that are up to you. You just stop arguing with things that have already happened. That frees you up to act.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'BACK TO SPORTS DAY',text:'The rain is real. So, what now? You could set up a table tennis game in the hall. You could race your friends at a board game. The day is not the one you wanted, but it is still a day.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Rain has spoiled a party outside. Someone is already carrying the food in. They are not shouting at the sky. They are just doing the next thing.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your best friend is away on your birthday. What does this idea say to do?',options:['Accept she is away and plan a fun day anyway','Keep wishing she had picked a different week','Call off your birthday until she gets back'],answer:0,why:'Her trip is what is happening. Starting from there means you can still have a good day.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'TWO MONTHS OF PLANNING',text:'You have spent two months organising an outdoor party. On the day, it rains. You spend the afternoon working out what you should have done differently, while your guests stand around in the kitchen.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Do not wish events to go as you want. Wish them to go as they do. Effort belongs to what you control beforehand. Acceptance belongs to the outcome, once it has arrived.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where the distress lives',text:'Distress is the gap between what happened and what you demanded should happen. After the fact, only one side of that gap can still move: the demand. Drop it and the gap closes, and attention is free for the next step.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Before the event',items:['Effort','Plans','A marquee, in case of rain']},right:{title:'After the event',items:['Acceptance','The first useful step','No argument with the weather']},note:'Two jobs, done in the right order. Most people do the argument first.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The same rain on the same party table. This time someone is already carrying the food indoors, at a walk. The argument with the weather has been skipped.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'This is resignation dressed up as wisdom.',reality:'Resignation stops acting. This removes the argument with reality that usually comes first and helps nothing, so that the acting can start sooner.'},{kind:'try',text:'The next time a plan falls through, say out loud what has actually happened before you say what you wanted. Then take the first useful step from where you are.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Your train is cancelled and you will miss the start of a meeting. Which response follows the idea?',options:['Tell the organiser now and join the meeting late','Spend the wait replaying how you should have left earlier','Accept it and decide the meeting no longer matters','Complain to the staff until another train appears'],answer:0,why:'Accept what happened, then act from there. Deciding the meeting no longer matters accepts but stops acting, which is the misreading.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Wishing for what happens means accepting whatever comes and doing nothing.',reality:'Epictetus relocates the wish; he does not abolish effort. You act fully on what is yours. The wish that things had gone otherwise is what you drop, once the outcome is in.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Want events to happen as they do, not as you would have them happen. Then your peace no longer depends on the one thing you cannot adjust after the fact.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'CHAPTER EIGHT',text:'The handbook promises that if you wish things to happen as they actually happen, your life will go smoothly. The claim rests on the first chapter. Outcomes are not up to you, so a wish fixed on outcomes is a wish fixed on something you do not own.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'WHERE THE WISH SITS',low:'Demand: it must go my way',high:'Consent: it went this way',note:'Distress grows with distance from the right-hand end. Effort, made beforehand, is the same at both ends.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Rain on a table laid for a party, and the food already going indoors at a walk. The effort went into the party. Once the rain came, consent looks like this unhurried step.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The jug, and the harder cases',text:'In the handbook\u2019s third chapter the same thinking is applied to a jug you are fond of: know it is a jug, and its breaking will not overthrow you. He then applies it to a child or a wife, which is where most readers stop. Idea 4 takes that up.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Applied to injustice, the formula is dangerous. Wishing the world to be as it is can slide into consent to wrongs that should be resisted, and the passage has been read as a recipe for passivity. The defensible version covers only what has already happened and cannot be changed. What can still be changed belongs to effort, not to acceptance.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Distress is the gap between what happened and what you demanded.','After the fact, only the demand can move.','Accept the past outcome; keep acting on what is still open.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A patient learns that a treatment has failed. Which response best fits the idea, properly read?',options:['Accept the result, then discuss the next option with the doctor','Keep going over what should have happened instead of what did','Accept the result as fate and stop seeking any further treatment','Blame the doctor until the outcome is somehow put right'],answer:0,why:'The idea accepts what has happened and keeps effort on what is still open. Stopping all treatment is the passive misreading.'}]}},enchiridion_4:{pictureCaption:'The cup is whole and in use. Beside it, someone has drawn it broken, on purpose, long before anything happened to it.',bridge:'Idea 3 dealt with the wish after something goes wrong. This one does its work in advance, before anything has gone wrong.',basics:{title:'Remember that good things end',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A LIBRARY BOOK',text:'A library book is yours for three weeks. You know it has to go back. So you read it now, while you have it. And you don\u2019t cry when you take it back. You knew what kind of thing it was.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When you love something, remind yourself plainly that it can break or end. Not to make yourself sad. So you enjoy it more now, and the end does not knock you flat.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'YOUR FAVOURITE MUG',text:'Epictetus, a teacher from long ago, used a cup to explain this. If you love a cup, tell yourself: *this is a cup, and cups can break.* Then, if it breaks one day, it does not knock you over. You knew.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The cup is whole. Next to it, someone drew it broken, on purpose, long before. That is the trick: know it now.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it helps you notice',text:'When we think something will last forever, we stop looking at it. When we remember it won\u2019t, we look again. That is why the last day of a holiday can feel so bright.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'This means thinking sad thoughts all the time.',reality:'No. You say it once, plainly, and get on with your day. Sitting and picturing sad things for ages is the wrong way to do it.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You love it when your grandad comes to stay. How would you use this idea?',options:['Remember visits don\u2019t last, and enjoy this one fully','Stop looking forward to visits, so you can\u2019t be let down','Spend the visit worrying about when he will go home'],answer:0,why:'The point is to notice and enjoy what you have, not to worry or to pull away.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'ONE CLIENT',text:'One client is most of your income. You know it, and you have never once said it out loud, because saying it would make it real. So you plan as though that client will be there for ever.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When something matters to you, name plainly what kind of thing it is: a cup can break, a contract can end, a person can die. Affection built on the belief that nothing ends will be shattered by the ordinary.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Picturing loss in advance is morbid.',reality:'Epictetus treats it as accuracy. You remind yourself what kind of thing you have, so the moment does not rest on a false premise that life will one day correct.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it sharpens attention',text:'Things we treat as permanent fade into the background, because permanence removes the need to look. Things we know are on loan get noticed. Said plainly, the fragile fact makes the present more vivid, not less.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A cup in use, and beside it the same cup in pieces, drawn deliberately in advance. The hand sets it down and carries on.'},{kind:'try',text:'Name one thing you rely on that is genuinely fragile. Say it once, plainly. Then notice that you carried on with your day.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'DONE PROPERLY',prompt:'Which of these is the practice done properly?',options:['Saying once that the contract could end, then planning sensibly','Spending an evening imagining the day the contract is lost','Refusing to depend on any client, so nothing can be lost','Never thinking about the contract, so it cannot worry you'],answer:0,why:'One clear statement of fact, then a plan. An evening of imagining is rehearsing dread, refusing every client is pulling away, and never thinking of it keeps the false premise.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',word:'Premeditation of evils',meaning:'Thinking through possible losses before they come. Cicero discussed it under the Latin *praemeditatio*. Epictetus\u2019s version is brief: a plain statement of what a thing is, not a long imagining.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Say plainly what the things you love are: breakable, mortal, on loan. Then you can hold them without clutching, and their loss, when it comes, does not overturn what you believed.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'CHAPTER THREE',title:'From jug to child',text:'He starts with something trivial. If you are fond of a jug, say it is a jug you are fond of, and its breaking will not disturb you. Then, with no change of tone, he applies the same rule to kissing your child or your wife: say you are kissing a human being.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'CHAPTER ELEVEN',title:'Returned, not lost',text:'Elsewhere he completes the thought. Never say you have lost something; say you have given it back. Possessions and people alike were lent. Look after them as a traveller looks after an inn: carefully, and knowing it is not yours.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The cup is whole and in use. The outline of its breaking was drawn in advance, and the hand still sets it down with care.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Done as a mood rather than a practice, this becomes rehearsal of dread, the opposite of its intent. It is one clear sentence, said once, not an afternoon picturing the funeral. And applied to people, his claim that a death should not disturb you goes further than most will follow. Grief at a death is not a mistake about what the person was.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Name one thing you hold as if it were permanent. What changes if you say, once, what kind of thing it really is?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these is a misuse of the practice?',options:['Keeping friends at a distance so that losing them cannot hurt','Telling yourself once that a parent is mortal, then calling them','Planning for a key contract ending, then working on it fully','Enjoying a holiday more because you know it is the last week'],answer:0,why:'The practice lets you hold things without clutching. Refusing to hold them at all is avoidance, and it loses the attention the practice was meant to give.'}]}},enchiridion_5:{pictureCaption:'Down the table, every pair of hands is explaining. One pair, at the front, is simply eating, doing what the others only describe.',bridge:'The first four ideas are private practices. This one says keep them private: do them, and do not describe them.',basics:{title:'Do it. Don\u2019t talk about it.',beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Telling everyone your plan helps you stick to it.',reality:'An old teacher, Epictetus, warned against it. Saying it out loud can feel good, as if you have already done some of it. Then you may do less.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Don\u2019t tell people how good you are going to be. Just be it. Let what you do show it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Everyone at this table is busy explaining. One pair of hands is just eating. Those hands are doing what the rest are talking about.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'HIS OWN EXAMPLE',text:'Epictetus said sheep do not bring their grass back to the shepherd to show how much they ate. They eat it, and later they give wool and milk. Show the wool, not the grass.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'A NEW HABIT',text:'Say you decide to read every night. You could tell the whole class about it. Or you could just read, every night, for two weeks. If it is going well, people will see it. You won\u2019t need to tell them.'},{kind:'try',text:'Start one small good habit today. Tell nobody for two weeks. See how it goes.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend says, \u201cFrom now on I\u2019m going to be really kind!\u201d What would Epictetus say?',options:['Show it by being kind, not by saying it','Tell more people, so you have to stick to it','Kindness only counts if people know about it'],answer:0,why:'The kindness is in what she does. Saying it first can feel like enough, and then less gets done.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THREE WEEKS IN',prompt:'You have started running and are quietly proud of it. At a dinner, which feels better right now?',options:['Explaining your training plan in detail','Thinking about tomorrow\u2019s run'],answer:0,reveal:'Explaining feels better, and that is the problem. Describing the plan wins you the credit of having one, at once, without the run.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Do not explain your principles. Act on them. At a dinner, do not tell people how one ought to eat. Eat as one ought.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why announcing backfires',text:'Stating a principle gets you the social credit of holding it, immediately, without the work. Having been credited, you feel less pressure to do the work. The announcement is a substitute that feels like progress.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The second trap',text:'Once you have publicly named your philosophy, failing at it becomes embarrassing. Energy goes into defending the claim instead of improving the practice. You end up arguing about your diet rather than eating well.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Down the table, every pair of hands is explaining. One pair, at the front, is simply eating, doing what the others only describe.'},{kind:'try',text:'Tell nobody about the habit you are starting for two weeks. If someone notices a change, let the change answer for you.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A colleague has begun a course on leadership. Which behaviour fits Epictetus\u2019s advice?',options:['Using what they learn in meetings without mentioning the course','Summarising each week\u2019s lesson for the team in a long email','Correcting colleagues who still lead in the old way','Waiting until the course is over before trying any of it'],answer:0,why:'The learning shows in the conduct. Describing it, or policing others with it, is the talk he warns about.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'THE SHEEP AND THE SHEPHERD',text:'Sheep do not bring their fodder to the shepherd to show how much they have eaten. They digest it, and produce wool and milk. Near the end of the handbook Epictetus offers this as the model for a student of philosophy: show the results, not the intake.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Do not call yourself a philosopher or lecture others on principles. Digest the principles and let them show in conduct, because talking about them can stand in for practising them.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'HIS MODEL WAS SOCRATES',text:'The same chapter points to Socrates, who avoided display so thoroughly that people came to him asking to be introduced to philosophers. He took them along to others, and put up with being overlooked.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Intake',items:['Reading the books','Explaining the principles','Calling yourself a Stoic']},right:{title:'Wool and milk',items:['Eating moderately','Bearing an insult quietly','Keeping a promise nobody checks']},note:'Only the right-hand column is evidence that the left-hand one happened.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Two mechanisms',text:'Announcing pays out early: you receive credit for the principle before doing anything, and the pressure to act drops. It also fixes a position. Having declared, you must defend, so effort goes into the claim rather than the practice.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Silence is not always better. Teaching requires saying things, and Epictetus spent his life lecturing. Some commitments are rightly public, such as a promise others rely on. And never naming your values can become a pose of its own. The target is talk that replaces practice, not talk as such.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A table of explaining hands, and one pair quietly eating. Of everyone at the table, only that pair is showing the principle.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'An objector says Epictetus talked about philosophy for a living, so the advice is hypocritical. What is the best reply?',options:['He aimed at talk that replaces practice, not at teaching itself','He never lectured, and only wrote his teaching down','The advice applies only to dinner parties, not to schools','Philosophers are exempt, because talking is their practice'],answer:0,why:'Teaching others is a practice in its own right. The warning is against talk that stands in for doing, which a teacher can fall into like anyone.'}]}},enchiridion_6:{pictureCaption:'One costume left on the rail, and someone taking it down to put on. The part was chosen elsewhere. Wearing it well is theirs.',bridge:'Idea 1 put your body, position and circumstances in the not-yours column. This one asks what is left for you to do inside them.',basics:{title:'You didn\u2019t pick the part. You pick how you play it.',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE SCHOOL PLAY',prompt:'You wanted to be the hero in the school play. You get cast as a tree. What can you still decide?',options:['Nothing, the part is set','How well you play the tree','Whether you get to be the hero'],answer:1,reveal:'The part was picked for you. But you can still be the best tree on that stage. You can learn when to sway. You can stay still for the whole scene.'},{kind:'idea',text:'You don\u2019t get to choose every part you play in life. You do get to choose how well you play it. That part is all yours.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO SAID THIS',text:'Epictetus was born a slave, almost 2,000 years ago. He did not choose that part. Later he was set free and became a famous teacher. He said life is like a play, and someone else hands out the parts.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One costume left, and someone putting it on. They did not choose it. They can still wear it well.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Picked for you',items:['Your family','Your town','How tall you are']},right:{title:'Picked by you',items:['How kind you are at home','How hard you try','How you treat new kids']}},{kind:'myth',belief:'So you must put up with anything you are given.',reality:'No. If something is unfair, or someone is hurting you, telling a grown-up you trust is part of playing well. Playing well never means just putting up with harm.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You are made goalkeeper, but you wanted to score goals. What does this idea say?',options:['Be the best goalkeeper you can be','Play badly so they move you up front','Sit out until you get the spot you want'],answer:0,why:'Where you play was picked for you. How well you play there is up to you.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Accepting your role means giving up on your life.',reality:'Epictetus is pulling apart two judgements people usually merge: how good the part is, and how well it is played. Only the second is yours, so only the second is worth assessing.'},{kind:'idea',text:'You are an actor in a play whose parts were handed out by someone else. Long or short, grand or humble, your business is to play your part well. Choosing it was never yours.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'NOBODY VOTED',text:'You are the one in the family who lives nearest your ageing parent. Nobody decided it. The role simply arrived. You cannot currently leave it, and how you do it is still entirely yours.'},{kind:'explain',title:'It cuts both ways',text:'If a modest part played excellently is a success, an advantageous part played carelessly is not redeemed by the advantage. By this measure, a gifted, well-placed person who coasts is doing worse than a struggling one doing their best.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One costume left on the rail, and someone taking it down without looking for another. The part was chosen elsewhere. Wearing it well is theirs.'},{kind:'try',text:'Ask two questions about where you are now: how good a part is it, and how well am I playing it? Spend your attention only on the second.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'One manager inherited a strong team and coasts. Another took over a failing team and is steadily rebuilding it. By this idea, who is doing better?',options:['The rebuilder, since only the playing of the part is assessed','The first manager, since their team\u2019s results are better','Neither can be judged until next year\u2019s results are in','Both equally, since neither of them chose their team'],answer:0,why:'The part each was handed is not theirs to claim credit or blame for. How each is playing it is.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'A SLAVE WHO TAUGHT FREEDOM',text:'Epictetus was born a slave and spent years as the property of a powerful freedman at the imperial court in Rome before he was freed. When he tells students that another chooses their part, he is not speaking from comfort.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Your part, short or long, poor or powerful, was chosen by another. What is yours is to play it well. Judge yourself on the playing, never on the part.'},{kind:'term',word:'Role',meaning:'For Epictetus a role carries its own duties: son, brother, citizen. Elsewhere in the handbook he says duties are measured by these relationships, not by how well the other person plays theirs.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'CHAPTER SEVENTEEN',title:'The playwright',text:'The handbook names only an author of the play, but in Epictetus\u2019s wider teaching that is God, who orders the whole. That is why the part can be accepted without bitterness. A reader who does not share his theology has to ask whether the image works without an author. It largely does: the part was assigned by circumstance, if not by design.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A bare rail with one costume, taken down without a look along the rail for another. The choosing happened elsewhere. The wearing happens here.'},{kind:'limits',text:'This is the idea most open to abuse. Told to people in unjust positions, \u201cplay your part well\u201d has served those who benefit from the parts staying as they are. The defensible reading governs how you act inside a role while you are in it. It is not an argument against changing roles, or against ending unjust ones.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Name a part you did not choose. Then, as a separate question, how well are you playing it?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which use of the idea is a misuse?',options:['Telling an underpaid worker to stop asking for fair pay','Doing a temporary job well while applying for a better one','Caring well for a parent, a role nobody else could take on','Judging your year on effort, not on the team you were given'],answer:0,why:'The idea governs how you play a part, not whether unjust parts should stand. Asking for fair pay is itself part of playing well.'}]}},enchiridion_7:{pictureCaption:'Two good handles at the top, and the hand has seized the jar by its base instead. Gripped there, it only tips.',bridge:'Idea 2 put the hurt in your judgement. This one shows there is usually more than one true judgement to choose from.',basics:{title:'Pick it up by the handle that lifts',beats:[{kind:'picture',caption:'This big jar has two good handles. But the hand has grabbed it by the bottom. Gripped like that, it will only tip over.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Most problems can be picked up in more than one way. Some ways let you do something. Some just make you angrier. Pick the true way that lets you lift it.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'YOUR BROTHER',text:'Your brother breaks your game. One way to hold it: *he broke my stuff.* That just makes you mad. Another true way: *he is my brother, we share a room, I want us to get on.* That way lets you sort it out.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO SAID THIS',text:'Epictetus, a teacher from almost 2,000 years ago, used this same picture. He said each thing has two handles. One lifts. One does not. His own example was a brother, too.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'This means pretending the bad thing did not happen.',reality:'No. Your brother really did break it. You can still tell him so. Both handles are true. You are just choosing which true thing to hold first.'},{kind:'try',text:'Cross with someone? Say one more true thing about them. Does it help you see what to do next?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend forgot your birthday. Which true thing is the handle that lifts?',options:['She has been my good friend for years','She forgot my birthday this year','Nobody ever remembers me'],answer:0,why:'She did forget, and she is a good friend. Both are true. Only the friendship gives you a way to carry on together. *Nobody ever remembers me* is not even true.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE LOAN',text:'Your brother borrowed money two years ago and has never mentioned it since. Every time you see him, you pick the relationship up by that fact. Every visit goes the same way: polite, tight, and over early.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Everything has two handles: one by which it can be carried and one by which it cannot. Among the true descriptions of a situation, choose one you can act from.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Will not carry',items:['He owes me and ignores it']},right:{title:'Will carry',items:['He is my brother','We grew up together','I want him at my table']},note:'Both columns are true. Only one leads to anything you can do.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Choosing, not pretending',text:'Epictetus does not deny the wrong. Among the true descriptions available, some let you act and some only let you seethe. You choose which to pick the situation up by. From the right-hand column you might even raise the money, calmly.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two good handles, and the hand has seized the base instead. Pulled from there, the jar tips and never leaves the ground.'},{kind:'try',text:'Name the handle you are holding a difficult situation by. Then name one other true handle it has, and ask what you could do from there.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A colleague got the promotion you wanted. Which handle carries?',options:['They are now someone I need to work well with','They were promoted over me, which was unfair','They must have played politics to get there','I should never have trusted the process'],answer:0,why:'*Unfair* may even be true, but it gives you nothing to do. That you now need to work well with them is also true, and it gives you a next step.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'CHAPTER FORTY-THREE',text:'The handbook\u2019s own case: your brother wrongs you. Do not take it by the handle of his wrongdoing, which will not carry. Take it by the other: that he is your brother, that you were brought up together. Then it can be lifted.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A situation has more than one true description. Some can be acted on and some can only be resented. Which true description you pick it up by is in your power.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why this is not spin',text:'The wrong is still real; he never denies it. Spin replaces a true description with a false, kinder one. This chooses between two true descriptions, by asking which one makes action possible. The test is practical, not moral.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE PRACTICAL TEST',nodes:['Pick a true description','Is there something I can do?','No: wrong handle, choose again','Yes: act from there'],note:'Holding the wrong handle harder does not help. Changing handles does.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The jar has handles made for carrying, and the hand has gone for the base. The jar is real either way. Only one grip moves it.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Choosing a handle is not choosing to forget. Some wrongs have to be picked up by the wrong itself, because only that handle leads anywhere: reporting fraud, leaving someone abusive. The kinder handle can also be used to dodge a confrontation that is needed. Ask whether your handle carries the problem, or only carries it out of sight.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What have you been carrying by the handle that does not lift? Name another true handle it has.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HARD CASE',prompt:'A business partner has been quietly falsifying the accounts. Which use of the idea is sound?',options:['Hold it by the partnership: protect the business by acting on the fraud','Hold it by the friendship, and say nothing about the accounts','Hold it by his dishonesty, and spend the year resenting him','Decide no handle applies, since his conduct is not up to you'],answer:0,why:'The handle that carries is the one that lets you act well. Here that means dealing with the fraud, not swallowing it or stewing in it.'}]}},enchiridion_8:{pictureCaption:'A handful of pebbles put down by someone else, and your own full sack of the same. Their list of your faults is the shorter one.',bridge:'Idea 7 found a true handle for someone else\u2019s wrong. This one finds one for someone\u2019s criticism of you.',basics:{title:'When someone says something mean about you',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'IN THE PLAYGROUND',text:'A friend tells you that Sam said you are bad at football. Your face goes hot. You want to find Sam right now and prove him wrong.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When you hear that someone said something bad about you, you don\u2019t have to fight it. You can calmly agree you are not perfect. Then there is nothing left to fight about.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'A JOKE FROM LONG AGO',text:'Epictetus, a teacher from long ago, had a funny way to answer this. Something like: *if he knew me better, he would have a much longer list!* It is a joke. But it works.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it works',text:'To fight back, you have to act as if you have no faults. That is hard work, and it is not true. Everyone has faults. Once you say so, the mean words have nothing to push against.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Think of each pebble as a fault. Sam found a small handful. You know about a whole sack. So his few pebbles are nothing to fight about.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'So I should put myself down whenever someone is mean.',reality:'No. This is not about saying you are bad. It is about being calm with being a normal person who gets things wrong. If someone keeps on being cruel, tell a grown-up.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time someone teases you, try a light, true answer, like: *yep, and I can\u2019t sing either.* See what happens.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Someone says your drawing is messy. Which answer uses this idea?',options:['\u201cTrue, and you should see my handwriting!\u201d','\u201cIt is not! Yours is way worse!\u201d','Say nothing, and stay upset all day'],answer:0,why:'A light, true answer ends it. Fighting back keeps it going.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'KINDLY PASSED ON',text:'Someone said something unflattering about you at a party, and a third person has kindly passed it on. You are already drafting the reply: the context, the evidence, the reasons they are wrong.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When told that someone speaks badly of you, do not defend yourself. Concede more than was asked: they plainly did not know your other faults, or they would have mentioned those too.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Defending',items:['Keep up an image without the flaw','Gather the evidence','The exchange runs on']},right:{title:'Conceding',items:['Admit what is probably true','Add what they missed','Nothing left to attack']},note:'The second costs nothing, because you know your faults better than any critic does.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the joke works',text:'It disarms the criticism by giving up the position being attacked, and from strength, not weakness. It only works because it is true. This is not performed modesty. Your faults really are many, and a stranger\u2019s list will be incomplete.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The critic\u2019s handful of pebbles, and your own sack of the same. Conceding their few costs nothing when you already carry far more than they found.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What it saves',text:'Defending requires you to maintain a picture of yourself as someone without the flaw. That picture is expensive to keep up and usually false. Conceding ends the exchange before it has cost you an evening.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you hear a criticism of yourself second-hand, write down two worse and truer things before deciding whether to respond at all.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You hear that a colleague called you disorganised. Which response applies the idea?',options:['Agree it is fair, and mention a worse habit they missed','Send round your calendar to show how organised you are','Ask the person who passed it on to defend you next time','Point out that the critic is just as disorganised'],answer:0,why:'Conceding, and adding to it, leaves nothing to defend. The other three all keep the exchange going.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A REPORT REACHES YOU',prompt:'You are told a rival has been criticising you. Which response protects you best?',options:['A careful rebuttal of each point','Silence','Cheerful agreement, with additions'],answer:2,reveal:'A rebuttal commits you to defending an image. Silence leaves the criticism standing. Agreeing, and adding to it, leaves nothing to attack. That is the handbook\u2019s advice.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Answer reported criticism by conceding it, and more. A self you have not claimed cannot be wounded, and the critic\u2019s list of your faults is always shorter than yours.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'CHAPTER THIRTY-THREE',title:'Where it sits',text:'The advice comes in a long chapter of rules for conduct among other people: be mostly silent, avoid gossip, do not laugh much or loudly. Among them: if told someone speaks ill of you, do not defend yourself. Reply that they plainly did not know your other faults.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'HOW MUCH THERE IS TO HIT',low:'A flawless self-image to defend',high:'Nothing claimed, faults admitted',note:'The further right you stand, the less a critic can reach. Self-knowledge, not self-contempt, is what moves you there.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it is stronger than it looks',text:'The move gives up the position under attack, and does so from strength, since nobody knows your faults better than you. It also follows from the first chapter. Reputation is not up to you, so defending it is effort spent on the wrong column.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A stranger\u2019s handful of pebbles beside the owner\u2019s full sack. The concession is made from the sack, which is why it comes from strength and not from weakness.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Some criticism is false and damaging, and must be answered: a lie about your honesty told to an employer, a false accusation. Conceding there is not humility; it abandons the truth. The joke also turns sour if it becomes self-contempt. It works when the faults are real and the stakes are small.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which second-hand criticism are you still answering in your head? What would you add to their list?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which use of the advice goes wrong?',options:['Agreeing with a false claim of theft, made to your employer','Laughing off a remark that you talk too much in meetings','Adding a worse fault when told you are a messy cook','Letting a rumour that you are vain pass without rebuttal'],answer:0,why:'The advice is for true faults and small stakes. A false accusation that can do real harm is a matter of truth, and has to be answered.'}]}},enchiridion_9:{pictureCaption:'A dropped bowl in pieces, and a pair of hands calmly sweeping them up. Nobody is pointing at anybody. That is blaming neither.',bridge:'Idea 2 came from the same chapter, which ends on this ladder. Where your blame lands shows how far the sorting from idea 1 has gone.',basics:{title:'Where does your blame go?',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'SPILT MILK',prompt:'You knock over a glass of milk at breakfast. What is your first thought?',options:['My sister put it in a silly place','I am so clumsy, I always do this','Oops. Where is a cloth?'],answer:2,reveal:'All three are normal. Epictetus, a teacher from long ago, said they show how far you have learned. The first blames others. The second blames you. The third just gets the cloth.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When things go wrong, beginners blame other people. People who are learning blame themselves. People who have really learned blame no one. They just deal with it.'},{kind:'steps',title:'The three steps',steps:['Bottom step: *it\u2019s their fault.*','Middle step: *it\u2019s my fault, I\u2019m useless.*','Top step: *it happened. What do I do now?*']},{kind:'picture',caption:'A bowl got broken. Nobody is pointing at anyone. The hands just sweep up the bits. That is the top step: it happened, so what now?'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Blaming yourself is the grown-up thing to do.',reality:'It is better than blaming others. But it is only the middle step. You can still say sorry and fix it. That is not blame. That is doing your part.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'AFTER A LOST MATCH',text:'Your team loses. The bottom step says the ref cheated. The middle step says you are the worst player there. The top step says: we lost, so let\u2019s practise corners on Tuesday.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time something goes wrong, notice where your first thought puts the blame. That tells you which step you are on.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You forget your homework at home. Which thought is on the top step?',options:['I forgot it. I\u2019ll tell my teacher and bring it tomorrow.','Mum should have reminded me this morning.','I\u2019m so stupid. I never get anything right.'],answer:0,why:'It says what happened and what to do next. It blames no one, and it still takes care of your part.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE USUAL TOP RUNG',belief:'Taking the blame yourself is the mark of a mature person.',reality:'For Epictetus it is the middle rung. Self-blame is progress on blaming others, but it makes the same error with the arrow reversed: hunting for a culprit for something outside your control.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The untaught blame others, beginners blame themselves, and those whose education is complete blame neither. Where your blame lands shows how far you have come.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE LADDER',nodes:['Blame others','Blame yourself','Blame neither'],note:'Most self-improvement stops at the middle rung and calls it maturity.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'A FAILED LAUNCH',text:'A launch flops. Rung one: marketing botched it. Rung two: I should have seen it coming, I am not up to this job. Rung three: the plan rested on a guess about demand that was wrong, so what do we test before the next one?'},{kind:'explain',title:'Neither is not indifference',text:'The third rung still assesses your part: the judgement, the effort, the response. What it drops is the search for a culprit for the outcome, which behaves more like weather. You can learn from weather. You cannot usefully blame it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A broken bowl, and a pair of hands sweeping it up. No finger pointed at anyone else, and none at themselves. The third rung looks like this: the next job, done.'},{kind:'try',text:'After the next thing that goes wrong, notice where your first sentence puts the fault. That is your position on the ladder.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'After a missed deadline a manager says, \u201cIt\u2019s on me, I failed the team.\u201d Where is that on the ladder?',options:['The middle rung: better than blaming others, still seeking a culprit','The top rung, because the manager has taken full responsibility','The bottom rung, because it is really a dig at the team','Off the ladder, since managers are meant to take the blame'],answer:0,why:'Self-blame is progress, not the destination. The top rung would ask what went wrong in the plan and what to change.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE END OF CHAPTER FIVE',text:'Right after saying that judgements, not things, disturb us, the handbook draws a consequence. When we are hindered or troubled, we should blame nobody but our own judgements. Then it ranks people by where their blame goes.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Blaming others marks the untaught; blaming yourself, the beginner; blaming neither, the one whose education is complete. Where you put the blame is the measure of progress.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'WHERE THE ARROW POINTS',low:'Outward: blame others',high:'Nowhere: blame neither',note:'Self-blame sits in the middle. It feels like the far end, which is why so many stop there.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the middle rung is a trap',text:'Self-blame feels like responsibility, so it is mistaken for the destination. But it repeats the first error with the arrow reversed: a verdict sought about an outcome that was not up to you. It also tends to punish rather than to correct.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the top rung keeps',text:'Blaming neither is not shrugging. Your own judgement, effort and response remain fully open to assessment, and should be assessed. What drops away is the search for a culprit for the result, which on Stoic terms was never the kind of thing that could be at fault.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A broken bowl on a kitchen floor, swept into the pan. No verdict is being passed in either direction, and the response is already under way.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Blame has real uses. Courts, schools and workplaces need to know who did what, and an apology owed is owed. Read carelessly, *blame neither* lets whoever caused harm off the hook, or dresses carelessness up as calm. The ladder describes your inner verdict about outcomes, not the public accounting of who is responsible.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Blame others, blame yourself, blame neither: three stages.','Self-blame is progress, and still the same error reversed.','The top rung still judges your part, never the weather.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A friend crashes your car and apologises. What does the top rung look like?',options:['Accept the apology, arrange the repair, and let resentment go','Refuse the apology, since nobody is ever really to blame','Blame yourself for lending them the car in the first place','Stay angry with them until the repair has been paid for'],answer:0,why:'Practical matters still get settled. What the top rung drops is the hunt for a culprit, in either direction.'}]}}}},2969,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'environment-beats-willpower_1':{pictureCaption:'One small block set before one doorway, and the worn track in the carpet already bends to the other. One step changed the route.',basics:{title:'Make it easy, or make it hard',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE BISCUIT TIN',prompt:'The biscuits move from the kitchen table to a high shelf in the cupboard. What happens?',options:['You eat about the same','You eat fewer'],answer:1,reveal:'You still like biscuits. But now there is a step in the way: open the door, reach up. One small step is often enough.'},{kind:'idea',text:'We mostly do what is easiest. One extra step makes us do a thing less. One less step makes us do it more.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A block sits in front of one door. Look at the carpet. People now walk to the other door instead.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'IT WORKS BOTH WAYS',left:{title:'Want to do it more?',items:['Take a step away','Swim bag packed the night before','Water bottle filled and by the door']},right:{title:'Want to do it less?',items:['Add a step','Games console unplugged and in a box','Sweets on the top shelf']}},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it works',text:'A step works even when you are tired or cross. You don\u2019t have to be strong right then. The step does the work for you.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one thing you want to do less. Add one real step to it. Not a rule. A step, like unplugging it and putting it away.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You want to stop watching videos in bed. Which plan uses this idea?',options:['Charge the tablet in the kitchen at night','Promise yourself you will not watch','Only watch one video, then stop'],answer:0,why:'Getting up to fetch the tablet is a real step. A promise asks you to be strong at bedtime, when it is hardest.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE APP YOU DELETED',text:'On Sunday you delete a social app from your phone. By Wednesday you have reached for it a dozen times, found it gone, and not reinstalled it. You were no stronger on Wednesday than on Sunday. There was simply a step in the way.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Friction, the number of steps between you and an action, predicts behaviour more reliably than intention. Remove steps from what you want to do. Add steps to what you don\u2019t.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why friction beats intention',text:'It asks nothing of you at the moment of decision, which is exactly when your resources are least reliable. Intention has to be summoned each time. A step simply sits there, every time, whether you feel strong or not.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE EVERYDAY EVIDENCE',text:'The piece points to ordinary patterns. Default options tend to win over ones people must actively choose. Items at eye level sell more than items on low shelves. Removing a single step from a process can substantially change how many people complete it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A single block before one door, and the worn path in the carpet has already moved to the other.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Making things harder for yourself is a gimmick. Real discipline would not need it.',reality:'Adding steps is the half people neglect, and often the stronger half. A deleted app has to be reinstalled, and the reinstalling is where the decision actually gets made, slowly, with time to think.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one habit you want less of and add one physical step to it. Not a rule: a step.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You want to snack less while working from home. Which change does the idea favour?',options:['Keep snacks in a high cupboard in another room','Set a firm rule of no snacks before noon','Note down every snack in an app as you eat it','Eat a bigger breakfast so you stay full for longer'],answer:0,why:'A rule asks for resolve at the moment of temptation. Distance adds steps that work whether or not you feel resolved.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'THE WORD THE PIECE USES',word:'Friction',meaning:'The number of steps between you and an action. Not physical resistance, but the clicks, reaches, walks and waits that stand between wanting to do a thing and doing it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Behaviour follows the path of least resistance more reliably than it follows intention. That is unflattering, and it is the strongest lever you have, because it asks nothing of you at the moment of choice.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the piece is arguing against',text:'The piece opens against the habit of diagnosing failure as a character problem. Its alternative is a design diagnosis: if the action you want takes more steps than the one you don\u2019t, the steps will usually win, however sincere the intention.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHERE THE DECISION MOVES',nodes:['App deleted from the phone','The urge arrives as usual','Reinstalling takes deliberate steps','The choice is made slowly, not on reflex'],note:'Friction does not remove the choice. It moves it to a point where you are thinking.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One block before one doorway. Nobody forbade that door; the worn path simply went elsewhere.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Friction works best on behaviour driven by convenience. A strong craving will often route round a single step. The same lever also works against people: a subscription that can only be cancelled by phone is friction aimed at a customer. And the evidence the piece cites is broad patterns, not a promise about any one person or habit.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Steps predict behaviour better than intentions do.','Remove steps from what you want; add them to what you don\u2019t.','Adding steps moves the decision to a slower, clearer moment.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'WHO IS IT AIMED AT?',prompt:'Which of these is friction being used against the person it affects?',options:['A subscription that can only be cancelled by phone in office hours','Leaving your running shoes by the front door the night before','Logging out of a shopping site so buying takes a few more steps','Keeping the guitar on a stand instead of in its case'],answer:0,why:'It is the same lever, set by the seller against the customer\u2019s own choice. The idea is a tool for arranging your own path, and worth recognising when someone arranges yours.'}]}},'environment-beats-willpower_2':{pictureCaption:'Two identical machines. One is wired to the wall and runs whenever needed. The other runs only if somebody turns the crank today.',bridge:'Idea 1 showed what a single step can do. This one shows why leaning on willpower instead is a weak plan.',basics:{title:'Plan for your worst day',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'WEDNESDAY',text:'On Sunday you plan to practise piano every day after school. Monday goes well. On Wednesday you are tired, hungry and fed up after a bad day. The piano stays shut.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Some days you feel strong. Some days you don\u2019t. A plan that only works on strong days will fail on the others. So plan for your worst day.'},{kind:'explain',title:'When is it hardest?',text:'Making yourself do things is harder when you are tired, hungry or upset. Those are the same days when life is hard. So that is when a plan built on trying hard breaks.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two machines. One is plugged in, so it runs by itself. The other only runs if someone turns the handle. Today, nobody is turning it.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'A BETTER PLAN',text:'Don\u2019t try hard every day. Set things up once. Leave the piano lid open with your music on the stand. Now a tired day still has an easy way in.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of one thing you only do when you feel like it. Ask: what could I set up so I do it even on a bad day?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Mia wants to drink more water at school. Which plan will work best on bad days?',options:['Pack a full bottle in her bag each night','Tell herself to try harder each day','Drink only when she feels thirsty'],answer:0,why:'The bottle is set up once and works even on a bad day. Trying harder depends on how she feels.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'YOUR WEEKLY PLAN',prompt:'Your plan has you training four mornings this week. On which day is it most likely to fail?',options:['Monday, when it is new','The day after a late, stressful night','Saturday, when you have more time'],answer:1,reveal:'Self-control is variable. It correlates with sleep, stress and hunger, and it is lowest exactly when circumstances are hardest.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A plan that needs you to be reliably motivated works on good days and fails on the days that matter. Spend willpower once, on arranging conditions, rather than daily on overcoming them.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Whatever the theory',text:'Whether self-control is a resource that runs down is genuinely contested, and the research behind that idea has replicated poorly. The practical point does not depend on it. Motivation varies, and the bad days arrive whatever the mechanism turns out to be.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The plugged-in machine runs whatever kind of day it is. The crank machine waits for someone with the energy to turn it.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Daily resolve',items:['Paid every day','Fails under load','Has to be found again tomorrow']},right:{title:'An arrangement',items:['Paid once','Works on bad days too','Still there tomorrow']},note:'The difference compounds over months.'},{kind:'try',text:'Find one habit you run on willpower alone. Ask what would have to be true about the room, the phone or the calendar for it to happen on your worst day.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You keep failing to make a healthy lunch on weeknights. Which response follows the idea?',options:['Cook a batch on Sunday so weeknight lunches take no effort','Commit more firmly, and remind yourself every evening','Accept that you lack discipline and stop trying','Wait for a calmer month before trying again'],answer:0,why:'The batch spends effort once, at a good moment. The weeknights then no longer depend on how you feel.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE CONTESTED PART',belief:'Willpower is a fuel tank that empties through the day, and the science has proved it.',reality:'Whether self-control depletes like that is genuinely contested, and the depletion research has replicated poorly. The piece does not need it to be true. Its argument rests on something less disputed: self-control varies.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Treating willpower as the main daily input builds a system that fails under load, the very condition it was meant to survive. Spend willpower once on arranging conditions, not daily on overcoming them.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The argument without the theory',text:'The piece sidesteps the debate. Whatever the mechanism, self-control is variable, it correlates with sleep, stress and hunger, and it is lowest exactly when circumstances are hardest. A plan that needs it every day is therefore weakest on precisely the days it exists for.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The crank machine can work, and on a good day someone turns it. The wired one never needed a good day.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHY THE GAP COMPOUNDS',nodes:['Willpower spent once, on arranging','The arrangement persists','Bad days are covered too','Months of steady behaviour'],note:'Daily resolve must be paid again each morning. An arrangement keeps working after the effort is spent.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Arrangements take willpower too, once, and some need upkeep. Not everything can be arranged: a hard conversation or a temptation met in company may still need effort in the moment. The framing can also become an excuse, if every lapse is called a design flaw. The claim is about which input to rely on, not that effort never matters.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which of your plans only works on a good day? What would it need to survive your worst one?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HONEST OBJECTION',prompt:'A critic argues that since the depletion research replicated poorly, the case against relying on willpower collapses. What is the best reply?',options:['The case never rested on depletion, only on self-control varying day to day','The depletion research will probably be confirmed by larger studies','Willpower is clearly a fuel tank, whatever the studies say','The critic is right, so daily resolve is a sound plan after all'],answer:0,why:'The piece deliberately rests on the less disputed claim: motivation varies and is lowest when things are hardest. That holds whatever the mechanism.'}]}},'environment-beats-willpower_3':{pictureCaption:'A switch thrown once and taped down, so the choice can never present itself again.',bridge:'Idea 2 said to spend willpower once. This is how: turn a decision you keep remaking into one already made.',basics:{title:'Decide once, then stop deciding',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A TAPED SWITCH',picture:!0,text:'Picture a switch you keep flicking on and off. Each time, you have to choose. Now flick it once and tape it down. You never have to choose again. The tape does it for you.'},{kind:'idea',text:'If you make the same choice again and again, make it once instead. Same time, same place, ready to go. Then your mood on the day does not decide.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why choosing each day goes wrong',text:'Each time you choose again, you might choose something else. And you often choose again when you are tired or rushed. Tired choices pick the easy thing. So over time, the easy thing wins.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'HOMEWORK',text:'Every day, Leo decides when to do his homework. Often it ends up late at night, or not at all. So he makes one choice for the whole term: five o\u2019clock, at the kitchen table. Now there is nothing to decide.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'A rule is fine as long as you allow a few exceptions.',reality:'Each exception means you must choose again: is today special? That brings back the very choice you wanted to stop making. A rule with no exceptions feels strict, but the choice stays made.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one thing you decide every day, like when to practise. Choose once for the whole month. Write it down.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Ava decides each morning whether to walk to school or ask for a lift. Most days she asks for a lift. What does this idea suggest?',options:['Choose once: she walks every dry day','Decide each morning, by how she feels','Ask her parents to pick for her each day'],answer:0,why:'One choice made ahead stops the daily vote. Whether it is dry is a fact to check, not a fresh choice.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'LUNCH, AGAIN',text:'Every working day at half past twelve you decide what to have for lunch. You start the week with good intentions. By Thursday, tired and busy, you take whatever is nearest. Your resolve did not change. You kept re-deciding in worse conditions.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A decision made once and built into an arrangement beats the same decision made again and again. Each repetition is a chance to decide differently, usually in worse conditions.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why repeats drift',text:'You first decide when calm and clear. You re-decide later in the day, more tired, more pressed. So the repeats come from your worse moments, and they drift towards the easy option. Not because resolve fails, but because of when the deciding happens.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Ways to remove the recurrence',steps:['A standing order.','A scheduled block that repeats itself.','A recurring delivery.','A rule with no exceptions.']},{kind:'explain',title:'Why no exceptions matters most',text:'It is the least comfortable item and the most important. A rule with exceptions needs a judgement each time: does today count? That puts back exactly the decision you were trying to remove.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A switch thrown once and taped down. The choice cannot come up again.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one recurring decision, such as lunch or when to exercise, and settle it for the month in writing. Notice how the week feels without it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You want to put money aside each month but keep deciding the amount on payday. What does the idea suggest?',options:['Set up a fixed transfer that runs the day after payday','Decide the amount carefully each payday, with a budget','Put aside whatever happens to be left at the month\u2019s end','Promise yourself you will be stricter about spending'],answer:0,why:'A standing transfer removes the monthly re-decision. The other options all keep a choice in the month, made in whatever state you are in.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'WHICH WAY IT DRIFTS',prompt:'You plan to exercise four times a week, and re-decide each evening. Over a month, which way do the evening decisions drift?',options:['Towards exercising more','No drift either way','Towards the easy option'],answer:2,reveal:'Each repeat is taken later, more tired and more pressed than the first decision. The drift is not a failure of resolve. It reflects where the decisions are being taken from.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Every recurring decision converted into a standing arrangement is one you stop paying for. Repeated decisions decay toward the easy option, because the moments you re-decide in are worse than the one you first decided in.'},{kind:'term',word:'Biased sampling',meaning:'Drawing from an unrepresentative set. Here, your repeat decisions are drawn mostly from tired, pressed moments, so they do not represent what you would choose when thinking clearly.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The rule with no exceptions',text:'The piece calls this the most important arrangement and the least comfortable. An exception clause sounds reasonable, but it needs a judgement each time: is this one of the exceptions? That judgement is the recurring decision again, under a new name.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Thrown once and taped. Not locked: the tape can come off, but only on purpose.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Fixed arrangements stop responding to change. A rule set in January may be wrong by June, so it needs a review, made deliberately and not in the moment. A rule with no exceptions can also turn harmful, as when someone trains through an injury to keep it. The aim is to remove casual re-deciding, not to stop thinking.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Each repeat is a chance to decide again, in worse conditions.','Standing arrangements remove the repeat.','Review the rule on purpose, never in the moment.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE LEAK',prompt:'Which of these keeps the very decision it was meant to remove?',options:['A rule to run every weekday unless you do not feel up to it','A standing order that moves savings out on payday','A weekly delivery of the same basic groceries','A fixed hour blocked out for writing every morning'],answer:0,why:'Whether you feel up to it is a judgement made each morning, in exactly the state the rule was meant to bypass. The exception brings the daily decision back.'}]}},'environment-beats-willpower_4':{pictureCaption:'The pill bottle stands right beside the kettle. The hand that reaches for the kettle every morning cannot miss it.',bridge:'Ideas 1 to 3 arranged the room and the calendar. This one arranges the cue: the moment a new habit starts.',basics:{title:'Hook a new habit onto an old one',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'WHY HABITS GET DROPPED',belief:'If you keep forgetting a new habit, you just don\u2019t care enough.',reality:'Most new habits fail because you forget, not because you don\u2019t want them. Nothing reminds you. The fix is a reminder that is already part of your day.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Tie a new habit to something you already do every day. After you brush your teeth, do the new thing. The old habit reminds you.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'TEETH, THEN STRETCH',text:'You want to stretch every night, but you keep forgetting. So you decide: after I brush my teeth, I do one stretch. You brush every night anyway. Now the stretch has a place to hang.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A COAT HOOK',text:'A coat dropped on the floor gets lost. A coat on a hook is always there. Your old habit is the hook. Hang the new one on it.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'PILLS BY THE KETTLE',caption:'Someone keeps their pills right by the kettle. Each morning they reach for the kettle, and there are the pills.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Keep the new thing small',text:'Pick a hook you never skip. And keep the new thing tiny. If it is too big, it gets in the way, and you may start to skip the old habit too.'},{kind:'try',text:'Finish this line: after I ___, I will ___. Use something you already do every single day.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Zara wants to feed her fish every morning but keeps forgetting. Which plan uses this idea?',options:['Feed them right after she pours her cereal','Try hard to remember every morning','Feed them when she happens to walk past'],answer:0,why:'Pouring cereal happens every morning anyway. It becomes the reminder.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE FULL BOTTLE',text:'You buy vitamins and put them in a cupboard. You mean to take one every morning. Three weeks later the bottle is nearly full. You never decided against them. You just never once remembered at the right moment.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A new behaviour survives better when anchored to a reliable existing one: after the kettle boils, before the laptop opens. The anchor gives the cue. That is the part people forget to design.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it works',text:'An established habit already has a reliable trigger. The new behaviour inherits it. The kettle boiling, the laptop opening: each already happens without being remembered. That solves what kills most new habits. It is forgetting, not unwillingness.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Scheduled on its own',items:['Every morning, at some point','Has to be remembered','Easily missed for weeks']},right:{title:'Anchored',items:['As the kettle boils','The kettle reminds you','Missed only if the kettle is']}},{kind:'picture',kicker:'OUT OF THE CUPBOARD',caption:'The bottle from the scene, moved to stand beside the kettle. The kettle is used every morning without fail. Now it reminds you of the pills as well.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Choosing an anchor',steps:['Pick something you really do every day, without fail.','Make the new behaviour small enough to fit beside it.','Name the exact moment: after this, I do that.']},{kind:'try',text:'Name the exact existing moment your new habit will hang from. Write it as one sentence: after ___, I ___.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You want ten minutes of language practice a day. Which anchor is most likely to hold?',options:['Straight after your morning coffee, which you never skip','Whenever a spare ten minutes turns up during the day','After your gym session, which happens about twice a week','Before bed, once everything else is finished'],answer:0,why:'The coffee happens every day without being remembered, and ten minutes fits beside it. A twice-weekly session is too unstable to anchor to.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE DIAGNOSIS',text:'The piece makes a specific claim about why new habits die: mostly from forgetting, not unwillingness. People design the behaviour and the motivation, then leave out the cue. When the moment passes unnoticed, they blame themselves for a part they never built.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Attaching a new behaviour to a stable existing routine is more reliable than scheduling it on its own, because the new behaviour inherits a trigger that already fires without being remembered.'},{kind:'term',word:'Cue',meaning:'The event that sets a behaviour off. An established habit has a reliable cue that needs no remembering: the kettle clicks, the laptop opens. A new habit with no cue depends on memory.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE CUE IS INHERITED',nodes:['An existing routine fires reliably','New behaviour placed directly after it','The old cue now triggers both','Nothing needs remembering']},{kind:'picture',kicker:'A BORROWED CUE',caption:'Something small and new, placed beside a routine that never fails. The hand reaching for the kettle meets the bottle, so the kettle\u2019s cue now sets off both.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The two requirements',text:'The anchor must be genuinely stable, and the new behaviour small enough to fit without displacing it. Miss either and the technique fails in a predictable way, which the piece gives as the usual reason it is tried and abandoned.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Attach something demanding to a fragile routine and both break: the new behaviour does not happen, and the old routine starts to wobble. Anchors also move when life does. A new job, a move or a holiday removes the cue and the habit with it, which can feel like a loss of discipline when it is a loss of trigger.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which of your routines happen without fail, even on bad days? Those are the only ones worth hanging anything from.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE DIAGNOSIS',prompt:'Someone anchored twenty minutes of journalling to an evening run they manage only some weeks. Within a month both have stopped. What is the likeliest explanation?',options:['The runs were not stable enough, and the add-on made them heavier still','Journalling is a habit that cannot be anchored to anything at all','They lacked the willpower to keep two habits going at once','Anchoring only works for habits that are done in the morning'],answer:0,why:'A demanding addition on an unreliable anchor is the failure the piece describes: the add-on weighs on the routine and both collapse.'}]}},'environment-beats-willpower_5':{pictureCaption:'One stone is the missed day. The walker strides straight on to the next one: no fall, no turning back.',bridge:'The first four ideas make a habit easier to keep. This one is about the day you do not keep it anyway.',basics:{title:'One missed day is just one day',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE BROKEN RUN',text:'Kai read every night for twenty nights. Then he went to a sleepover and missed one. The next night he thought: what\u2019s the point now? He did not read again for a month.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Everyone misses a day sometimes. What matters is what you do next. Make a missed day just one missed day, not the end.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What really went wrong',text:'Missing one night did not stop Kai. A thought did: I have failed. One slip felt like proof. That thought did the damage, not the slip.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One stone in the path is the missed day. The walker just steps on to the next stone and keeps going.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Plan for a missed day',steps:['Choose a tiny version for bad days, like one page.','Decide now: after a miss, start again the next day.','Don\u2019t keep a count that one miss can wipe out.']},{kind:'question',prompt:'You draw every day, then miss two days when you are ill. What does this idea say?',options:['Start again tomorrow, like any other day','Give up, since the run is broken now','Wait until next month to start fresh'],answer:0,why:'Two missed days are just two days. Starting again the next day keeps the habit alive.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE WRONG QUESTION',belief:'The goal is never to miss a day.',reality:'Everyone misses. The useful design question is what happens when you do. A structure where one missed day stays one missed day is the one still running in March.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Design so that failure is small. A missed day becomes a missed week when it is read as proof the attempt has failed. The reading does the damage, not the lapse.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW A DAY BECOMES A WEEK',nodes:['One missed day','Read as: it has failed','The next day feels pointless','The habit quietly ends'],note:'Every step after the first is interpretation, not circumstance.'},{kind:'steps',title:'The unglamorous fixes',steps:['A minimum version small enough to do on your worst day.','An explicit rule about resuming after a miss.','No streak count that makes breaking it mean something.']},{kind:'picture',caption:'A different stone in the path, and a stride that simply carries on to the next one.'},{kind:'try',text:'Write down, now, exactly what you will do the morning after you miss a day.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Your habit app shows a 41-day streak, and you have just missed a day. Which response does the idea favour?',options:['Do the minimum version tomorrow and treat the count as noise','Do double tomorrow to make up for the day you missed','Restart properly next Monday with a fresh plan','Pause until you have time to do it properly again'],answer:0,why:'Resuming at once, small, keeps one miss as one miss. Make-up sessions and fresh starts treat the lapse as a bigger event than it was.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'DAY FORTY-TWO',text:'A long run is broken by one ordinary missed day. The behaviour is no harder than it was yesterday. What has changed is the meaning: the number that kept you going is now zero, and zero looks like a reason to stop.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Systems survive not by never failing but by keeping failure small. The danger is the interpretation of a lapse, so the design should make that interpretation hard to reach.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The piece\u2019s argument',text:'A missed day becomes a missed week when the lapse is read as evidence that the attempt has failed, not as a single event. So the fixes are structural: a minimum version small enough for the worst day, an explicit rule about resuming, and no streak accounting that makes a break meaningful.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'STREAKS CUT BOTH WAYS',left:{title:'While intact',items:['Motivates','Gives each day a stake','Feels like progress']},right:{title:'Once broken',items:['Supplies a reason to quit','Wipes out the visible progress','Makes the next day feel pointless']},note:'A system that depends on an unbroken run is fragile by construction.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The odd stone is the lapse. The stride over it is the design: the path was built to be walked past one bad step.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Small failure is not the same as no standard. A minimum version done every day can quietly become the whole habit. Some goals need the full dose to work, and repeated misses are information about the design, not noise to ignore. Streaks do motivate while intact; the claim is that they should never carry the meaning of the whole attempt.'},{kind:'recap',points:['The lapse is an event. Reading it as failure does the damage.','Plan a minimum version and a rule for resuming, in advance.','Never let a counter carry the whole attempt.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'DESIGN CHECK',prompt:'By the piece\u2019s argument, which design is the most fragile?',options:['A reward unlocked only after sixty unbroken days','A five-minute minimum for days that go badly','A written rule to resume the morning after any miss','A weekly target met by any five days out of seven'],answer:0,why:'A reward that depends on an unbroken run gives one missed day the power to end the whole attempt. The others keep a lapse small.'}]}},'environment-beats-willpower_6':{pictureCaption:'A book left across the pillow of a made bed. Nobody can lie down tonight without picking it up first, and no promise was needed.',bridge:'Idea 1 added or removed one step. This one widens the lens: everything around you, taken together, is the plan.',basics:{title:'Move one thing tonight',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE BOOK ON THE PILLOW',picture:!0,text:'You want to read more at night. You promise yourself, again. Then you try something else. You put a book right on your pillow. At bedtime you have to pick it up to lie down. So you open it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The things around you shape what you do more than promises do. To change a habit, change the room, not just your mind.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO READ MORE',left:{title:'Trying harder',items:['Promise again','Hope you remember','Same room as before']},right:{title:'Changing the room',items:['Book on the pillow','Tablet left in the kitchen','Done once, works every night']}},{kind:'explain',title:'What counts as the room',text:'What you can reach. What you can see. What is already open. Who is with you. Each one nudges you all day, and you don\u2019t even notice.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What it can\u2019t do',text:'This works for things you already want to do. It can\u2019t make you want something. If you really don\u2019t want a plan, moving things round won\u2019t save it.'},{kind:'try',text:'Tonight, move one thing. A book on the pillow. Shoes by the door. Then see what happens tomorrow.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Noah wants to play his guitar more. Which change follows this idea?',options:['Take it out of its case and put it on a stand','Write a promise to practise on his wall','Ask a friend to tell him off if he skips'],answer:0,why:'Out of the case, the guitar is easy to see and grab. A promise on the wall leaves the room just the same.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TWO PLANS',prompt:'Two people want to cook at home more. One resolves to try harder. The other moves the takeaway apps off the home screen and shops for three meals on Sunday. Who is cooking more a month later?',options:['The one who resolved','The one who changed the surroundings','Neither: it comes down to character'],answer:1,reveal:'The resolution has to be summoned every evening. The changes act every evening without being summoned.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The environment is not support for the plan. It is the plan. What is in reach, what is visible, what is already open, who is present and what the defaults are shape behaviour more reliably than intention.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it outperforms resolving',text:'Surroundings act continuously and need no attention. A resolution needs remembering and summoning every time. So the productive question is not what you will do differently, but what you will change about the room, the phone, the calendar or the company. Each change is one act with a lasting effect.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Five places to look',steps:['Reach: what is nearest to hand?','Sight: what can you see from where you sit?','Open: what is already running?','Company: who is around when it happens?','Defaults: what happens if you do nothing?']},{kind:'myth',belief:'Moving a book is too small a change to matter.',reality:'It sounds trivial, and the piece calls it the highest-yield idea of the set. Most people try to fix behaviour by resolving harder and never once move an object.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'ONE OBJECT, MOVED ONCE',caption:'One book, moved once, onto the pillow. Every night from now on it is in the way of lying down, and it asks for no resolve at all.'},{kind:'try',text:'Tonight, move one object: the book onto the pillow, the phone out of the bedroom, the running shoes by the door. Judge it by tomorrow, not by how small it felt.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You check your phone first thing every morning and want to stop. Which change applies the idea?',options:['Charge it in another room and use a cheap alarm clock','Set a firm intention each night not to look at it','Set a reminder that tells you to wait until later','Allow yourself five minutes, then put it down'],answer:0,why:'Moving the phone changes what is within reach at the moment the habit fires. The others all rely on resolve at that moment.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO DIAGNOSES',left:{title:'A character problem',items:['I lack discipline','Fix: resolve harder','Effort every day']},right:{title:'A design problem',items:['The room favours the habit','Fix: move things','Effort once']},note:'The whole piece is a case for the right-hand column.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The environment is not support for the plan; it is the plan. Reach, visibility, defaults and company shape behaviour more reliably than intention, and they work without attention.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The strongest version',text:'Earlier ideas treated surroundings as levers: a step added, a switch taped, a cue borrowed. Here the piece goes further: surroundings are not there to help an intention along. They are the plan itself, and the useful thing to do with an intention is to spend it rearranging them.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why one-time acts win',text:'Each change to the room, the phone, the calendar or the company is a single act with a persistent effect. A resolution is a repeated act with a fading one. Over months, that difference compounds.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'A SINGLE ACT THAT LASTS',caption:'The book was put on the pillow once. Every night after, it does the work a resolution would have to be summoned for.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The piece states its own limit: design cannot manufacture motivation for something you do not want. It converts weak intention into consistent behaviour; it does not supply the intention. A system built to force something genuinely unwanted will be dismantled. And some surroundings, such as a shared home or a workplace, are not yours alone to redesign.'},{kind:'keyline',text:'Surroundings turn a weak intention into steady behaviour. They cannot supply an intention that is not there.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Name one habit you have tried to fix only by resolving harder. Which object have you never moved?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE LIMIT',prompt:'A student fills their room with study cues but still never studies, because they no longer want the course. What does the idea say?',options:['Design cannot supply the missing want; the course itself needs rethinking','The cues are too weak, so add stronger and more visible ones','Willpower now has to take over where the room has failed','The idea is simply wrong, since nothing changed at all'],answer:0,why:'The environment converts weak intention into behaviour. With no intention to convert, more cues will be ignored or taken down.'}]}}}},2970,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'franklin-autobiography_1':{pictureCaption:'Thirteen nails, one for each virtue. This week only one is wound with thread. The other twelve are watched, not worked on.',basics:{title:'One habit a week',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'NEW YEAR\u2019S LIST',text:'It is New Year. You write a list. Read more. Eat less junk. Tidy your room. Be kind to your brother. Practise piano. By week two, you have kept none of them.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'A MAN WITH THIRTEEN',title:'Franklin had the same problem',text:'Benjamin Franklin lived in America almost 300 years ago. As a young man he made a list of thirteen good habits he wanted. He saw that trying all of them at once would not work.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Work on one habit at a time. Keep an eye on the rest, but don\u2019t try to fix them yet. Then move on to the next one.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Thirteen nails, one for each habit. This week, only one gets the thread wound round it. The rest hang there and wait.'},{kind:'steps',title:'How Franklin did it',steps:['Week one: work hard on one habit.','Just notice the other twelve. No fixing yet.','Week two: move to the next habit.','After thirteen weeks, start the list again.']},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE LEARNING A SONG',text:'When you learn a hard song, you don\u2019t fix it all at once. You work on one tricky bit until it is easy. Then the next bit. You still play the rest. You just don\u2019t fix it yet.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You want to read more, go to bed on time and stop biting your nails. What would Franklin do?',options:['Pick one for this week and just notice the others','Start all three today, while you feel keen','Give up two of them and keep only one'],answer:0,why:'One at a time gives each habit real care. The others are not dropped. Their turn will come.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'FIVE RESOLUTIONS, ONE MONDAY',prompt:'You start five new habits on the same Monday. What usually happens by the third week?',options:['Most stick, if you really mean them','One or two survive, more or less by chance','All five slip, more or less together'],answer:2,reveal:'Each habit needs attention at the moment of choice, and you only have so much. Spread across five, none gets enough, and they tend to fail together.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Work on one behaviour at a time and merely observe the rest. Attention is the scarce input, so ration it on purpose.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'A YOUNG PRINTER\u2019S PLAN',text:'Franklin drew up thirteen virtues, from Temperance to Humility, each with a one-line definition. He gave each a week of full attention while keeping a daily record of slips in all of them. Thirteen weeks made a cycle, so each virtue came round four times a year.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the definitions matter',text:'Each definition was short enough to check at bedtime. *Be temperate* cannot be scored. *Did not eat until I felt dull* can. A rule you cannot check at the end of a day cannot be recorded, and the whole method rests on the record.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'All thirteen at once',items:['Attention split thirteen ways','Thirteen small failures a day','Quit by week three']},right:{title:'One a week',items:['Full attention on one','The rest only watched','Each gets four turns a year']},note:'Nothing is abandoned in the right-hand column. It is only queued.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one habit. Write its rule so you could answer yes or no at bedtime. Work only on that for a week, and simply note the others.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A manager wants her team to improve punctuality, documentation, code review and testing this quarter. Which plan follows Franklin?',options:['Push one each fortnight while logging slips in the rest','Launch all four at once, with one shared target','Pick the most important and drop the other three','Wait until the team has spare time for all four'],answer:0,why:'Franklin rationed attention: one gets the push, the rest stay visible through the record. Nothing is dropped, and nothing competes.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'THE BOLD PROJECT',text:'In the second part of his memoir, written in his late seventies, Franklin describes a scheme from his youth: to arrive at moral perfection. He soon found that while he guarded against one fault, another surprised him, and that habit kept beating good intention.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Change comes one behaviour at a time. Give one virtue full attention, keep the others under observation only, and rotate. The sequence, not the willpower, is the engineering.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How the cycle was built',text:'Thirteen virtues at a week each made a thirteen-week course, four courses a year. He ordered the list on purpose. Temperance came first, because a clear head makes the rest easier. Silence came next, to leave more room for learning. Each early virtue was meant to ease the next.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'HIS OWN COMPARISON',text:'Franklin likens himself to a gardener who does not try to pull every weed at once, which would be beyond him, but clears one bed and then the next. Each cleared bed is a visible gain, and the gain encourages the rest.'},{kind:'term',word:'Operational definition',meaning:'Defining a quality by an action you can observe and check. Not *be orderly* but *every thing has its place*. Franklin\u2019s one-line definitions are an early example, and they are what made a daily record possible.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Thirteen nails in a row. One is wound thick with this week\u2019s thread. The other twelve are not abandoned, only waiting their turn.'},{kind:'limits',text:'He did not finish. Order beat him for life, and he admits he never reached perfection. The method also assumes habits are separate. Some are not: sleep, drink and temper move together, and fixing one can fix three. The rotation rations attention. It does not prove change must be slow.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'Someone runs a full thirteen-week cycle and sees no change at all. By Franklin\u2019s design, what is the likeliest flaw?',options:['The rules were too vague to mark each evening','Thirteen weeks is too short for any habit to form','Watching the other twelve should have been skipped','All thirteen should have been worked on together'],answer:0,why:'The rotation only works if each day can be scored. A rule that cannot be marked leaves no record, and the record is what keeps the other twelve in view.'}]}},'franklin-autobiography_2':{pictureCaption:'A hand adds today\u2019s mark to a wall of tallies. The method is the mark itself, and knowing it is coming.',bridge:'Idea 1 was the plan. This is the part of it that did the most work: the small daily mark.',basics:{title:'Counting changes what you do',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE SWEETS JAR',prompt:'You want to eat fewer sweets. You make no rules. You just put a tick on a card each time you eat one. What happens?',options:['Nothing. Ticks are just ticks.','You eat fewer, without trying.'],answer:1,reveal:'Often you eat fewer. Just before you take one, you think of the tick you will have to make. That small pause does the work.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Writing down each slip can change you, even with no plan to change. You think twice when you know a mark is coming.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'FRANKLIN\u2019S LITTLE BOOK',text:'Benjamin Franklin lived almost 300 years ago. He kept a small book to track his good habits. It had a box for each habit on each day. Each time he slipped, he put a black dot in the box.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the dots were a shock',text:'Before the book, Franklin thought he was doing fine. The book swapped that feeling for a count. The count was worse than he thought. That is what often happens the first time you count something.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A hand adds today\u2019s mark to the wall. It is easy to slip when no one counts. It is harder when you know a mark is coming.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one thing you want to do less, like saying *um* or checking a screen. Make a tick each time, for one week. Don\u2019t try to stop. Just count.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Maya wants to stop cutting in when people talk. Which plan uses Franklin\u2019s idea?',options:['Mark a dot each time she cuts in','Promise very hard to never do it again','Ask her friends to tell her off each time'],answer:0,why:'The dot is the method. Knowing she will have to mark it makes her stop and think before she speaks.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE SPENDING APP',text:'You install an app that logs every coffee and taxi. You set no budget. You just let it count. By the third week you are walking past the caf\xe9, and you never decided to.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Recording a behaviour, without punishing it, changes the behaviour. The mark that is coming changes the moment before you act, not the moment after.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'A DIRTIER PAGE THAN EXPECTED',text:'Franklin ruled a small book into days and virtues and put a black spot against every fault. He reports his surprise at finding himself far fuller of faults than he had imagined, and the satisfaction of watching the spots thin out over time.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHERE THE MARK DOES ITS WORK',nodes:['You are about to slip','You remember the mark','A small pause','Sometimes, a different choice'],note:'The analysis afterwards changes little. The knowledge at the moment of acting changes a lot.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why almost any tracker works',text:'The tool barely matters: paper, an app, tallies on a wall. What matters is that you know, in the moment, it will be written down. That is why a crude record you actually keep beats a clever one you never look at.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Today\u2019s mark going on to the wall. It is not the total at the end that changes things. It is knowing this mark is coming.'},{kind:'try',text:'Choose one thing you want to change. Record it every day for a month, and deliberately do nothing else about it. Then look at the trend.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Four people want to snack less. Which one is using Franklin\u2019s mechanism?',options:['Logging each snack as it happens, with no target set','Reading a weekly snack summary that an app sends out','Promising on Sunday never to snack again after Monday','Adding up at the end of each week how many snacks there were'],answer:0,why:'The record works at the moment of acting. A summary afterwards or a promise beforehand misses the moment that matters.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Franklin\u2019s method worked because his resolutions were strong.',reality:'Franklin found that being convinced he should do better did not stop the slips. What the method added was the book: a mark for every fault, made daily, with no penalty attached. The record, not the will, is the active part.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Measurement is itself an intervention. Knowing that a lapse will be recorded alters the moment before the lapse, which nothing else in the method reaches.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The design of the book',text:'Each page had seven columns for the days and thirteen lines for the virtues. A black spot went wherever he had slipped. The aim each week was to keep one line, that week\u2019s virtue, clean. When rubbing out marks wore holes in the paper, he moved the tables to ivory leaves that wiped clean.'},{kind:'term',word:'Reactivity',meaning:'Psychologists\u2019 name for the way that recording a behaviour changes it. It is studied under self-monitoring: the act of observing yourself, with no reward or penalty, shifts what you do.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the count came out worse',text:'Before the book Franklin had a general sense of doing reasonably well. Estimates flatter; counts do not. Almost anything measured for the first time after years of being guessed comes out worse, and that surprise is part of what moves people.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The effect can fade once recording becomes routine. Franklin himself went from weekly courses to yearly ones, then stopped. A record improves what is recorded, not what matters: count the wrong thing and you improve the wrong thing. And for some people close tracking of food or faults feeds anxiety rather than change.'},{kind:'recap',points:['A record changes behaviour before any analysis is done.','It works at the moment of acting, through anticipation.','Choose what you count with care, because it will move.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'PREDICT THE RESULT',prompt:'A company starts counting how many tickets each support agent closes, hoping to improve service. What should it expect?',options:['Ticket counts rise, whether or not service improves','Little change, since a low count is never punished','Better service, because tracking always improves it','No effect, because agents ignore numbers they did not choose'],answer:0,why:'Recording changes the behaviour recorded. If the count is not the thing you care about, you get more of the count.'}]}},'franklin-autobiography_3':{pictureCaption:'Everything else in the room is in order. The desk never will be. Franklin wrote that down instead of hiding it.',bridge:'The record in idea 2 showed Franklin where he was improving. It also showed him, plainly, where he was not.',basics:{title:'The habit he never got',beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'FRANKLIN\u2019S WORST HABIT',text:'Benjamin Franklin made a list of thirteen good habits. One was Order. That meant a place for each thing and a time for each job. He tried for years. He never got it. His papers and things stayed in a mess.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The rest of the room is neat. The desk is not, and never was. Franklin put that in his book rather than pretend.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Be honest about the habit you keep failing at. Then ask if it is worth all the effort. If not, stop pushing there and use the effort where it helps more.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'THE SPOTTY AXE',text:'Franklin told a story. A man wanted his whole axe shiny, not just the sharp edge. To polish it, he had to turn a heavy stone wheel. He got tired. In the end he said he liked a spotty axe best.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Was Franklin just giving up?',text:'He wondered that too. He said he might be like the axe man, fooling himself. But it is also true that the sharp edge does the cutting. A few spots don\u2019t stop the axe from working.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of one habit you have failed at again and again. Ask: what would really change if I got it? Write down your honest answer.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Sam has tried for two years to keep a neat locker. It is always a mess, but he never loses things or is late. What fits Franklin\u2019s idea?',options:['Maybe stop pushing here and work on something else','Keep trying until the locker is perfect each day','Tell people the locker is neat when they ask'],answer:0,why:'Being honest shows Sam the mess costs him very little. His effort may help more somewhere else.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Admitting a failure undermines everything else you claim.',reality:'Franklin\u2019s plain admission that he never mastered Order is what makes the rest of his account believable. A report with no failures in it reads as a sales pitch.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Keep the record honest enough to show where effort has stopped paying, and stop grinding there rather than everywhere.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'A PLACE FOR EVERYTHING',text:'Order, a place for each thing and a time for each task, gave Franklin more trouble than any other virtue. He explains why: his memory was good enough to find things without tidiness, and a printer\u2019s hours were set by his customers, not by his schedule.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'THE SPECKLED AXE',text:'A man asks a smith to grind his whole axe as bright as its edge. The smith agrees, if the man turns the grindstone. He turns until he is worn out, then decides he likes a speckled axe best. Franklin admits he suspected himself of the same.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'WHERE THE RETURN STOPS',low:'The edge: the part that does the work',high:'Every speck polished: hard labour, little gain',note:'Most habits have an edge worth grinding and a blade that can stay speckled. An honest record tells you which is which.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The room is in faultless order; the desk is not. Franklin did not tidy the desk for the reader. He reported it.'},{kind:'try',text:'Name the one habit you have failed to build for years. Write down what it would actually return. If the answer is little, drop it in writing and move the effort.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You have chased an empty inbox for three years and never held it a week. You still answer everything that matters. What does Franklin\u2019s example suggest?',options:['Admit it in writing and spend the effort where it returns more','Try a stricter system, since failing means the system was weak','Keep it as a goal, because dropping goals becomes a habit','Stop opening the inbox so the failure stops bothering you'],answer:0,why:'The honest record shows the grinding costs more than it returns. Dropping it openly is different from pretending it is done.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE THIRD VIRTUE',text:'Order sat third in Franklin\u2019s list, placed early because it would free time for his studies. It is the one he reports never mastering. His reasons are specific: he was not used to method, his memory covered for the lack, and a tradesman\u2019s hours belong to his customers.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Self-improvement has diminishing returns, and an honest record is how you find where they begin. Refusing to hide his worst failure is also what makes the rest of Franklin\u2019s account credible.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The parable, and his doubt about it',text:'He tells of a man who wanted his whole axe bright, tired at the grindstone, and settled for a speckled one. Then he turns it on himself. Something posing as reason told him perfect neatness might be moral fussiness, and might make him envied. He suspects the excuse even as he records it.'},{kind:'term',word:'Diminishing returns',meaning:'The point after which each extra unit of effort buys less than the one before. Here: the edge of the axe repays grinding; the last specks on the blade barely do.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Everything in the room is in order except one desk. The failure is local, and Franklin recorded it as local.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the confession earns trust',text:'An account in which every method succeeds gives a reader nothing to calibrate against. Franklin names the virtue that beat him and gives his reasons. That lets you believe him about the others, and tells you what kind of person the method suits.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Diminishing returns are also the oldest excuse there is, and Franklin knew it: the speckled axe comes with a suspicion attached. The judgement can go stale, too. In old age, with his memory failing, he says he felt the lack of Order keenly. A trade-off that held when his memory was sharp stopped holding.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TELL THEM APART',prompt:'In which case is the speckled axe used honestly?',options:['A writer drops a daily word count after records show it adds nothing','A student stops revising because top marks take a lot of effort','A driver skips mirror checks because accidents have been rare','A manager stops giving feedback because people sometimes dislike it'],answer:0,why:'Honest use rests on a record showing the return has stopped. The others drop effort where the return is real, and call it a trade-off.'}]}},'franklin-autobiography_4':{pictureCaption:'He ducks far lower than the door needs. Nobody watching can tell what he feels inside. They only see the bow.',bridge:'Humility was the last virtue Franklin added. Like Order in idea 3, he never mastered it. Unlike Order, he found a way round.',basics:{title:'Speak softly and people listen',beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'If you are right, you should say it loud and firm.',reality:'Franklin found that people listen better when you say *I think* or *it seems to me*. Being loud makes them dig in, even when you are right.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'A FRIEND\u2019S WARNING',text:'Benjamin Franklin lived in America almost 300 years ago. A friend told him that people found him proud and bossy. So he added one more habit to his list: being humble. That means not acting as if you are better than others.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What he changed',text:'He stopped saying *certainly* and *no doubt*. He stopped telling people flat out that they were wrong. He started saying *I think* and *it seems to me*. He still said what he thought. He just said it softly.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Franklin never felt truly humble. But he learned to speak in a humble way. People listened more, and changed their minds more often.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'He bows much lower than the door needs. People can\u2019t see inside his head. They only see the bow.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Bossy',items:['You are wrong.','That will never work.','It is this, of course.']},right:{title:'Humble',items:['I might be missing something.','I wonder if it would work.','It seems to me it is this.']},note:'Both sides say what you think. Only one lets the other person agree without feeling silly.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend says the film starts at six. You are sure it is seven. What would Franklin say?',options:['I think it might be seven. Shall we check?','No, you are wrong. It is seven, not six.','Nothing. Let them find out when they are late.'],answer:0,why:'You still say what you think. You just leave room for your friend to agree without feeling silly.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'THE THIRTEENTH VIRTUE',text:'Franklin\u2019s list first had twelve virtues. Then a Quaker friend kindly told him he was thought proud: overbearing in conversation, and sometimes insolent in argument. Franklin added a thirteenth: Humility.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Franklin could not manage humility itself, so he managed its appearance, and says so. The appearance did what the virtue was supposed to do: people listened and changed their minds.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The specific changes',text:'He gave up flat contradiction and words such as *certainly* and *undoubtedly*. In their place came softer forms: *I imagine*, *it appears to me at present*. When someone said something he thought wrong, he began by granting the cases where they might be right.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Stated flatly',items:['The listener hears a challenge','Agreeing means conceding','They defend, even if unsure']},right:{title:'Stated tentatively',items:['The listener hears a view','Agreeing costs them nothing','They weigh it on its merits']}},{kind:'explain',title:'What it bought him',text:'Franklin calls himself a poor speaker, hesitant and never eloquent. Yet he credits this habit with much of his weight when he proposed new institutions. The room could not tell whether his humility was felt. It could hear how he spoke.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A bow far deeper than the doorway needs. From outside, felt humility and performed humility look the same. Only the bow is visible.'},{kind:'try',text:'In your next disagreement, replace *you\u2019re wrong because* with *I may be missing something, but*. Notice whether the other person moves.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'In a meeting you are confident a plan will overrun its budget. Which opening follows Franklin?',options:['Grant where it could work, then say the costs seem different to you','State firmly that it will overrun, as you have seen it many times','Keep quiet and let the final numbers prove you right later','Say that everyone knows plans like this always overrun'],answer:0,why:'He still says what he believes. He frames it so the other side can agree without having to admit defeat.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'BEFORE YOU READ ON',prompt:'Franklin says he achieved only the appearance of humility, not the thing. Does that make his advice a con?',options:['Yes: it teaches people to fake a virtue','No: the appearance did the virtue\u2019s work','It depends on what the appearance is used for'],answer:2,reveal:'Franklin\u2019s case is that the performance produced what the virtue was for: people listened and agreed more readily. Whether that is admirable depends on whether you are steering people toward the truth or toward your interest.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Some virtues are, in their effects, hard to tell from their outward form. Franklin performed a humility he did not feel, and the room, where the consequences land, could not see the difference.'},{kind:'explain',title:'His confession',text:'He reports little success with the reality and a good deal with the appearance. He adds that he never conquered pride, only its expression, and that had he conquered it he would probably have been proud of his humility. The joke is also the argument: the inner state may be out of reach.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The performance became the habit',text:'He also reports that the manner, put on at first against his nature, grew so easy that for decades no dogmatic phrase escaped him. That is the strongest form of the claim: practising the outward form can, over years, make it your own.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The bow is deeper than the doorway requires. It is a performance, and it still does what a bow is for.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Performance without substance is also the con man\u2019s method. Tentative phrasing persuades whether or not you are right, so the habit makes you more effective, not more correct. It can also turn into false modesty that hides what you actually think. It is defensible when used to find the truth together, not to win.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of a view you hold strongly. How would you put it so someone could agree with you without first having to admit they were wrong?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'A critic says performed humility is simply dishonesty. What is the strongest reply from Franklin\u2019s own account?',options:['The form did the virtue\u2019s work, and with practice it became habit','Nobody is truly humble, so performing it is no worse than usual','Persuading people matters more than honesty in public life','He only performed it with people who were proud themselves'],answer:0,why:'He claims two things: the effects were the virtue\u2019s effects, and the manner became natural. Neither licenses deceiving people about facts.'}]}},'franklin-autobiography_5':{pictureCaption:'Every tool on the board is worn and mismatched, because it gets used. The perfect boxed set has never done a job.',bridge:'Ideas 3 and 4 judged a habit by what it returned. Here that test becomes a principle Franklin applied almost everywhere.',basics:{title:'Ask: does it work?',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO REVISE',text:'One friend says to copy out your notes in neat colours. It looks great. Another says to test yourself with flash cards. It looks dull. Which should you pick? Ask one plain question: which one helps you learn more?'},{kind:'idea',text:'When you choose between two ways of doing something, ask which one works. Don\u2019t just pick the one that looks good or sounds grand.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'WHAT FRANKLIN BUILT',text:'Benjamin Franklin lived in America almost 300 years ago. He helped start a library people could join and borrow from, and a team to fight fires. When he said why, he did not say it was noble. He said it worked and helped people.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The tools on the board are old and scuffed, because they get used. The shiny set in the box has never done a job.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why this question is handy',text:'*Is it good?* is hard to answer. People can argue for ages. *Does it work?* is a question you can check. Try it, then look at what changed.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'If something works, that is all that counts.',reality:'Franklin was great at asking *how*. You still need to ask *why*. A plan can work very well at something that should not be done.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Two kids want to save pocket money. One makes a pretty chart. One puts coins in a jar each week. How would Franklin choose?',options:['See which one ends up with more money saved','Pick the chart, because it looks so nice','Pick the one the teacher likes the best'],answer:0,why:'Franklin\u2019s test is what works. Here, that means which plan really saves the money.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO JUDGE ADVICE',left:{title:'Is it admired?',items:['Sounds principled','Hard to test','Arguments run on']},right:{title:'Does it work?',items:['Sounds ordinary','Can be checked','Settles disputes']},note:'Franklin used the right-hand column without apology.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When two approaches compete, weigh whether each works as openly as whether it is admired. *Useful* is a test you can actually apply, because you can check what changed for somebody.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE STREET LAMPS',text:'Philadelphia\u2019s round glass lamps from London filled with smoke and soon dimmed, and one knock broke the whole globe. Franklin proposed four flat panes, with vents to carry the smoke out. A broken pane now cost one pane. His case for the design was entirely about what worked.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it settles arguments',text:'Principles often leave a dispute open, because both sides can claim them. *Does it work?* asks what changed for somebody, and that can be checked. Franklin applied it to libraries, fire companies, the post and his own habits.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Worn, mismatched tools that do the work, above a perfect set never unboxed. Franklin judged by the board, not the box.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Judging by usefulness is shallow.',reality:'It is narrow, which is different. It is an excellent answer to *how* and a thin one to *why*. Use it to choose between methods once you know the aim is worth having.'},{kind:'try',text:'Take two approaches you are choosing between. Write what each would change, for whom, and how you would know. Then notice which one you were leaning towards, and why.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Your team must choose between a respected framework nobody has used and a scrappy process that has shipped three projects. What would Franklin weigh first?',options:['What each one has actually delivered, and for whom','Which one experts in the field admire more','Which one sounds better when presented upward','Which one cost more to develop in the first place'],answer:0,why:'Franklin\u2019s test is the result. Admiration is evidence too, but not the deciding kind.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'TWO PIECES OF ADVICE',text:'One mentor says build the product properly, on principle. Another says ship something rough and see whether anyone uses it. Both sound wise, and principle alone cannot settle it. Franklin would ask what each approach had actually produced, and for whom.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Usefulness is a legitimate test, and one you can apply. Its strength is that it can be checked. Its weakness is that it can stop asking whether the useful thing is worth doing.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The pattern in the book',text:'Again and again Franklin explains a choice by its effect: the subscription library, the fire company, paved and lit streets, the stove he would not patent. He rarely appeals to nobility or duty. The reason given is that it worked and people benefited.'},{kind:'term',word:'Pragmatic test',meaning:'Judging a belief or practice by its practical consequences rather than by its source or its nobility. American philosophers made a theory of it about a century after Franklin. He simply practised it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The worn tools are worn because they work. The matched set is admired and idle. The picture argues for the board.'},{kind:'limits',text:'A life tuned for usefulness can stop asking whether the thing being made useful is worth doing. Franklin is fairly charged here: later critics, the novelist D. H. Lawrence among them, read his morality as a ledger. Read him as a method, a superb answer to *how*, and supply the *why* yourself.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Ask what an approach changed, and for whom.','Usefulness settles disputes that principle leaves open.','It answers how; it does not answer why.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these is a misuse of Franklin\u2019s test?',options:['Defending a cruel policy because it reliably meets its targets','Choosing the cheaper method after both were tested and worked','Dropping a respected habit that showed no effect in a year','Picking a plain tool over a beautiful one that kept failing'],answer:0,why:'Usefulness judges means to an end. It cannot tell you whether the end, or its cost to people, is acceptable.'}]}},'franklin-autobiography_6':{pictureCaption:'Hands round a club table, and one coin going into the fine box: the small, visible price of telling someone flat out they were wrong.',bridge:'Idea 4 was one man\u2019s way of speaking. The Junto built the same habit into a group\u2019s rules. This lesson is about why groups need it.',basics:{title:'Leave a way to change your mind',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'IN FRONT OF THE CLASS',prompt:'In class, a girl says the answer is 12. You call out: *No, that\u2019s wrong!* Everyone looks at her. What is she most likely to do?',options:['Say thanks and change her answer','Stick with 12, even if she now has doubts'],answer:1,reveal:'Changing her mind now feels like losing in front of the whole class. So she sticks with 12. Your call made it harder for her to change.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'FRANKLIN\u2019S CLUB',picture:!0,text:'When Benjamin Franklin was young, he started a club with friends. They met each week to talk about big questions. The club had a rule. No one could tell someone flat out that they were wrong. If you did, you paid a small fine.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Telling someone *you\u2019re wrong* in front of others makes it hard for them to change their mind. Ask a question instead, and leave them a way out.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A DOOR LEFT OPEN',text:'Think of a room with one door. If you block the door, the person inside will fight to get past. If you leave it open, they can walk out calmly. A soft word leaves the door open.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Shuts the door',items:['That\u2019s wrong.','You don\u2019t get it.']},right:{title:'Leaves it open',items:['Are you sure it\u2019s 12?','It seems to me it\u2019s 15.']},note:'You still say what you think. You just let them change without losing.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you disagree with a friend, start with a question: *Could it be\u2026?* See if they change their mind more easily.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend says the bus comes at three. You think it is half past. Which line follows the club\u2019s rule and still says what you think?',options:['Are you sure? I thought it said half past.','No. You have got it wrong again.','Fine, have it your way, then.'],answer:0,why:'A question lets your friend check and change their mind without feeling beaten.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE MEETING',text:'Someone in a meeting states a figure that is plainly wrong. A colleague says so, flatly. For the next ten minutes nobody discusses the figure. They watch the first person defend it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Direct contradiction makes changing your mind expensive, because retreating in public costs standing. The Junto\u2019s rule against it was not manners. It kept the exit cheap, so the group could correct itself.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'PHILADELPHIA, 1727',picture:!0,text:'Franklin, then about twenty-one, formed a club of young tradesmen for mutual improvement, called the Junto. Members brought questions on morals, politics and science to debate. Confident assertion and direct contradiction were banned, on pain of a small fine.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW A CORRECTION HARDENS',nodes:['A flat contradiction, in public','Retreat now costs face','The person defends the claim','The group gains an opponent'],note:'The rule cuts the chain at the first link.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What to say instead',text:'The rule fined the flat version. The alternative was Franklin\u2019s own habit: put a position as a question, or tentatively, as *it appears to me*. That costs the speaker nothing if they turn out to be wrong, which is exactly why they say it more often, and why the room keeps correcting itself instead of hardening.'},{kind:'try',text:'In your next disagreement, swap a flat contradiction for *it appears to me*, or for a real question. Watch whether the other person moves.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A teammate presents a plan built on a wrong assumption, in front of the whole team. Which response keeps the exit cheap?',options:['Ask what happens to the plan if that assumption does not hold','Point out the error clearly, so that nobody else repeats it','Say nothing now and email a correction to the whole team','Agree for now, to protect their standing in the room'],answer:0,why:'A question lets them find the flaw and revise in public without having been beaten. The error still gets fixed.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'A CLUB FOR INQUIRY',text:'The Junto, founded in 1727, met weekly for nearly forty years. Its members, mostly young tradesmen, took turns proposing questions. Franklin records that debate was meant as a search for truth, not a contest to win, and that dogmatic assertion and direct contradiction were fined.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Rules of debate are engineering. Ban direct contradiction and positive assertion, and the cost of changing your mind in public falls toward nothing, so a group corrects itself rather than splitting into sides.'},{kind:'term',word:'Face',meaning:'The public standing a person stakes on a claim. Once a claim has been contradicted in front of others, retreating spends face, so people defend positions they have privately stopped believing.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why a fine',picture:!0,text:'A small money penalty made the rule real without making it grave. It turned a lapse into a cost the whole room could see, much as Franklin\u2019s virtue book turned a fault into a mark. Both act on the moment before the slip.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'THE SAME CORRECTION, TWICE',left:{title:'Contradiction',items:['That figure is wrong','Speaker must lose to agree','Room watches a contest']},right:{title:'Inquiry',items:['Where does that figure come from?','Speaker can revise freely','Room watches the figure']}},{kind:'limits',text:'Soft framing can blur real disagreement until nobody knows a claim is false. Some errors need saying plainly and fast: a safety fault, a fraud. And the rule assumes good faith. A member pushing a known falsehood exploits a room that never says *no*. The Junto relied on members who shared the aim.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of a group you belong to. What does it cost someone there to say *I was wrong*? Who pays that cost most often?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'A critic says the Junto\u2019s rule simply made people timid. What is the best reply?',options:['It lowered the cost of conceding, so more errors got corrected','Timidity is a fair price to pay for a pleasant evening','Members who disagreed strongly could always leave the club','The rule governed manners only, never the arguments made'],answer:0,why:'The rule did not stop disagreement. It changed its form, so the losing side could move without a public defeat.'}]}},'franklin-autobiography_7':{pictureCaption:'A rare book handed down from the shelf. In Franklin\u2019s story, asking for one, not giving one, turned an opponent into a friend.',bridge:'Idea 6 kept disagreements from creating enemies. This one deals with an enemy who already exists.',basics:{title:'Ask for help to make a friend',beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'A MAN WHO DISLIKED HIM',text:'Benjamin Franklin worked for the group that made the laws where he lived. A new member spoke out against him. Franklin heard the man owned a rare book. So he wrote and asked if he could borrow it.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'What do you think happened next?',options:['The man said no','The man lent it, and became a friend'],answer:1,reveal:'He sent the book at once. Franklin sent it back a week later with a thank-you note. Next time they met, the man spoke to him kindly. They stayed friends for life.'},{kind:'idea',text:'People who do you a kind turn often start to like you more. So asking a small, real favour can warm up someone who is cold to you.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why would that work?',text:'When you help someone, part of you asks why you did it. The easy answer is: *I must like them.* So after helping, people often feel warmer to the one they helped.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One hand lends a rare book. The other takes it. It was the lending that made the man feel kind.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'So any favour will do, even a made-up one.',reality:'Franklin asked partly to win the man over. But the favour was small, and he gave the book back with thanks. Asking for fake favours to use people is not kind.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A new kid in class seems not to like you. Which is most like Franklin\u2019s idea?',options:['Ask to borrow a pencil you really need','Give them your best snack every day','Ignore them until they come to you'],answer:0,why:'A small, real favour lets them be kind to you. Lots of gifts can feel like a debt they owe.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A COLD COLLEAGUE',prompt:'A colleague has been cool towards you for months. Which move is more likely to warm things up?',options:['Do them a favour','Ask them for a small favour'],answer:1,reveal:'Most people pick the first. Franklin found the second worked better. A favour you do them can feel like a debt. On the modern account, a favour they do you has to be explained, by them.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'THE BORROWED BOOK',text:'In the Pennsylvania Assembly, a new member made a long speech against Franklin. Rather than court him, Franklin asked to borrow a scarce book from his library. It came at once. Franklin returned it within a week with a note of thanks. The man was civil from then on, and they stayed friends until his death.'},{kind:'idea',text:'To win over someone hostile, ask for a small favour rather than offering one. The person who did you a kindness has to account for it, and the easiest account is that they think well of you.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why not do them a favour?',text:'Debt is not warmth. A favour you do them puts them under an obligation, and people can resent a creditor. Asking lets them be generous, and their own generosity changes how they see you from the inside, which being pleasant from outside cannot do.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A rare book handed down from the shelf. Franklin was the one asking, and that is what turned the man.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The name it has now',text:'Franklin puts it down to an old saying: someone who has done you one kindness is readier to do another than someone you have helped. Psychologists now call it the Ben Franklin effect, and explain it as people bringing their feelings into line with what they have done.'},{kind:'try',text:'With someone who has been cool towards you, ask a small, genuine favour: their advice, a recommendation, a book. Make sure it is something you will really use.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You need the support of a department head who has blocked you twice. Which approach follows Franklin?',options:['Ask for their advice on a problem they know well','Offer to take on some of their workload for a month','Send a gift to show there are no hard feelings','Go over their head so their support is not needed'],answer:0,why:'Asking for their expertise lets them be generous. Doing them a favour creates a debt, and a debt is not warmth.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'The way to win over an opponent is to do them a kindness.',reality:'Franklin found the reverse more reliable. The one who gives the kindness, not the one who receives it, is the one whose feelings change.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A favour received can turn an opponent more reliably than a favour given, because the giver has to explain their own generosity, and the simplest explanation is goodwill.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'THE ASSEMBLY CLERK',text:'Franklin was clerk of the Pennsylvania Assembly when a new member, a man of fortune and education, spoke against his reappointment. Franklin borrowed a rare book from him, returned it with thanks, and found him friendly ever after. Franklin explains it by an old maxim: one who has done you a kindness is readier to do another than one you have obliged.'},{kind:'term',word:'Cognitive dissonance',meaning:'The discomfort of holding beliefs that clash with your own actions. It is the usual modern account of the Ben Franklin effect: having helped someone you disliked, you ease the clash by liking them more.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why receiving beats giving',text:'A favour given creates an obligation running toward you, and people can resent a creditor. A favour received runs the other way. Nobody imposes an explanation on the giver. They supply it themselves, which is why it holds.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The hand that lends, not the hand that takes, is the one whose view of the other changes.'},{kind:'limits',text:'This shapes someone\u2019s feelings without their knowing, and Franklin asked precisely to win the man over: influence of a kind that can become manipulation. It stays honest only if the request is real and the goodwill is returned. Asked too often, or for something large, a favour reads as using someone and can deepen dislike. Franklin\u2019s case was one small request, returned with thanks.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which use of the idea crosses from influence into manipulation?',options:['Inventing a need so a rival will help you, then using their goodwill','Asking a sceptical colleague to review a draft you want reviewed','Borrowing a tool from a neighbour you argued with, and returning it promptly','Asking an old critic for advice in their field, and acting on it'],answer:0,why:'Franklin\u2019s request was small and honoured: he borrowed the book and returned it with thanks. A fake need used to exploit goodwill keeps the mechanism and drops the honesty.'}]}},'franklin-autobiography_8':{pictureCaption:'Hands reset a line of type. The wrong letter has been lifted out and set apart: corrected, not argued over.',bridge:'Idea 3 was honest about a habit he never built. This is how he was honest about conduct he regretted.',basics:{title:'Say what you did wrong. Then stop.',beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'When you say sorry, explain all the reasons it was not your fault.',reality:'Reasons turn sorry into an argument. Then people argue back. A plain *I got that wrong* lets it end.'},{kind:'term',word:'Erratum',meaning:'A printer\u2019s word. It is a note saying something in a book was printed wrong, and what it should say. More than one is called errata.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'A PRINTER\u2019S WORD',text:'Benjamin Franklin was a printer almost 300 years ago. When he wrote the story of his life, he called his worst mistakes *errata*. He named each one, the way a printer marks a mistake in a book.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Own up to a mistake in one plain sentence. Don\u2019t add a long list of reasons. A plain owning-up lets the matter close.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Hands fix a line of print. The wrong letter is taken out and put to one side. No fuss. Just a fix.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Sorry with excuses',items:['I broke it, but it was slippery.','And you left it on the edge.']},right:{title:'Plain sorry',items:['I broke your mug.','I will get you a new one.']},note:'The plain one also says how you will put it right. A printer fixes the mistake, too.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You forgot your friend\u2019s birthday. Which is most like an erratum?',options:['I forgot your birthday. I\u2019m sorry.','I forgot, but I had so many tests.','You forgot mine once, so we\u2019re even.'],answer:0,why:'It names the mistake and stops. The other two turn sorry into an argument.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE OLD MISTAKE',text:'Years ago you let a friend down badly. When it comes back to you at night, you replay the context: the pressure you were under, the bad advice, what they did first. All of it may be true. The memory keeps coming back anyway.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Record a mistake the way a printer records an error: what was wrong, and what is right. Adding the circumstances turns an admission into a defence, and a defence invites a verdict that never quite arrives.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why a printer\u2019s word',text:'An erratum is a printer\u2019s correction notice: this was set wrong, here is the right text. It carries no plea. Franklin, a printer by trade, used the word for the serious faults of his youth.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHY THE MEMORY RETURNS',nodes:['Admission plus context','Context invites a judgement','You argue the case again','The trial stays open'],loop:!0,note:'An erratum drops the context, so there is no case to argue.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A line of type being reset. The wrong letter sits apart, removed. The fix is the whole of the response.'},{kind:'try',text:'Write one thing you did wrong in a single sentence, with no clause about the circumstances. Stop there. Notice the urge to add *but*.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You missed a deadline that cost a colleague their weekend. Which message is an erratum?',options:['I missed the deadline and it cost you your weekend. I\u2019m sorry.','I missed it, but the brief changed twice and nobody warned me.','Sorry about the deadline. It has been a hard month for all of us.','I missed it, though I think the original date was unrealistic.'],answer:0,why:'It names the fault and its cost, with no clause in its own defence. Each of the others slips in a plea.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'A SECOND EDITION',text:'Franklin opens his memoir by saying he would gladly live his life over again, if he could have what authors have in a second edition: the chance to correct the faults of the first. Through the book, his worst faults are logged as errata.'},{kind:'idea',text:'An erratum is a form of self-accounting that closes. It states the error and the correction, and asks nobody, yourself included, to rule on whether you were a good person at the time.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'HIS ERRATA',text:'Among the faults he names: leaving his brother\u2019s printing house before his apprenticeship was up, by exploiting a technicality; neglecting the young woman he had courted while he was away in London; and spending money he had been trusted to collect for a man named Vernon.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The temptation it resists',text:'The context around an old mistake is usually mitigating, and usually true. But adding it converts the admission into a defence. A defence invites a verdict, and the memory keeps returning because the trial keeps being reopened.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A line of type reset by hand, the faulty letter lifted out and set aside. The correction is the response.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Franklin is not always as bare as the method. Of his brother\u2019s case he also records the beatings that drove him away. And a printer\u2019s erratum comes with the right text: Franklin later married Deborah Read and repaid Vernon. An admission with no correction, where one is owed, is not an erratum. It is only a confession.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which old mistake do you still argue about with yourself? Write it as an erratum: what was wrong, and what the right text would be.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A company finds it charged customers twice. Which statement follows the erratum model most fully?',options:['We charged you twice on 3 May. Refunds go out today.','We charged some customers twice due to a supplier fault.','We regret any confusion caused by recent billing changes.','We charged you twice on 3 May. We are very sorry.'],answer:0,why:'An erratum states the error and the right text. Naming the fault without the correction is only half of one.'}]}},'franklin-autobiography_9':{pictureCaption:'Worn paths cross the field from every side and meet at one post box. It owns none of the letters, yet everyone\u2019s way runs through it.',bridge:'Idea 5 read the stove patent as usefulness. Here the same refusal is read another way: what being the route, not the owner, gave him.',basics:{title:'Share it, and people come to you',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A BRIDGE',text:'Think of a bridge over a river. The bridge does not own the carts that cross it. But all of them pass over it. So everyone knows where the bridge is, and everyone needs it.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'THE STOVE HE DID NOT LOCK UP',text:'Benjamin Franklin made a better stove almost 300 years ago. He was offered a patent. That is a right to be the only one who can sell a thing. He said no. Other people\u2019s ideas had helped him, so he let his idea help others.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Owning one good thing helps only you. Being the one that books, letters and know-how pass through makes you useful to lots of people. That grows over time.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE LIBRARY AND THE POST',picture:!0,text:'Franklin helped start a library that its members could borrow from. Later he ran the post office in his city, so letters passed through his hands. He did not own the books or the letters. But he was in the middle of it all.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Keep it',items:['You know a great trick.','Only you can use it.','Only you are helped.']},right:{title:'Share it',items:['You teach it to friends.','Now they come to you.','You are the one they ask.']}},{kind:'try',text:'Know a trick that helps you, like a way to learn spellings? Teach it to one friend this week.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Leo is the best in class at fixing bike chains. Which choice fits Franklin\u2019s idea?',options:['Show others how, and be the one they ask','Keep it a secret so only he can do it','Charge money each time he helps someone'],answer:0,why:'Sharing makes Leo the one people come to. Keeping it secret helps only him.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE PROCESS NOBODY ELSE KNOWS',text:'You built the reporting process your team relies on, and nobody else can run it. That makes you hard to replace for a day a week. A peer teaches a similar process to four teams, and becomes the person consulted whenever that kind of problem appears.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Owning one object pays rent from one thing. Being the route things travel through gives you standing over everything that travels, and that compounds.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'THE STOVE AND THE PATENT',text:'Franklin designed an iron stove that warmed rooms better on less fuel. The governor of Pennsylvania offered him a patent for its sole sale. He declined: he had gained from other people\u2019s inventions, and thought it fair to let his own circulate freely.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where he put himself instead',picture:!0,text:'Franklin helped found a lending library, served as postmaster and improved the post, and set up printers in other towns as his partners. In each case he was not the owner of one valuable item but the point things moved through: books, letters, trade.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW BEING THE ROUTE COMPOUNDS',nodes:['You share the method','Others use it','They bring you new problems','You see more than anyone'],loop:!0,note:'An owned object earns from itself. A route earns from everything that passes along it.'},{kind:'try',text:'Take a process you have kept to yourself and teach it to one other team this month. Note who comes back to you, and with what.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You have built a tool that saves your team hours each week. Which move follows Franklin?',options:['Document it and help other teams adopt it','Keep the code private so others must ask you','Charge other teams an internal fee to use it','Use it quietly and let your results speak'],answer:0,why:'Spreading it makes you the person that whole class of problem routes through. Guarding it earns from one thing.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'A REFUSAL, IN ONE PARAGRAPH',text:'When the governor offered Franklin a patent on his stove, Franklin declined briefly and on principle: having gained from the inventions of others, he should be glad to serve others freely by his own. The memoir also shows, without saying so, what that kind of choice built.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Ownership earns from one thing; position earns from everything that passes. Franklin repeatedly chose to be the route, through the library, the post and his printing network, rather than the owner of a single asset.'},{kind:'term',word:'Network position',meaning:'Where you sit among the flows between people: who comes to you, and what passes through you. A central position gains value as the network grows, which a single owned item cannot.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'WORN BY OTHER PEOPLE\u2019S FEET',caption:'Every path across the field was worn by someone else\u2019s errand, and all of them end at the post box. Its worth lies less in the box than in what passes through it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The cases',text:'The library gathered the town\u2019s readers into one subscription. The post office, he notes, improved his newspaper\u2019s news and reach. His printing partnerships set former journeymen up in other colonies, sharing profits with him. None is a possession; each grew as it was used.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Owning the object',items:['Earns from one thing','Value fixed at the start','Rivals copy and compete']},right:{title:'Being the route',items:['Gains from all that passes','Value grows with use','Others\u2019 use adds to it']}},{kind:'limits',text:'This is a reading of the record, not Franklin\u2019s stated motive: his reason was fairness. Nor did declining protect the idea. He reports that a London ironmonger patented a version of the stove and made a small fortune. And being the route depended on posts he was appointed to. Not everyone can choose that position.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Owning pays from one thing; being the route compounds.','Franklin\u2019s own stated reason was fairness to other inventors.','Declining ownership left the stove open for others to patent.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HARD CASE',prompt:'An engineer can patent a small tool or release it openly to her industry. What does Franklin\u2019s case most honestly show?',options:['Openness can build standing, though someone else may profit from the object','Openness always pays more than ownership in the end','Patents are unfair, since every invention borrows from others','Ownership is safer, since the London copy shows sharing fails'],answer:0,why:'Franklin gained position and lost the stove\u2019s profits to a copier. Both are in the record, and the trade-off is real.'}]}}}},2971,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'gospel-of-wealth_1':{pictureCaption:'A strongroom swept almost bare, its door propped open to the street. Carnegie judged a fortune by how little of it was left at the end.',basics:{title:'A rich man who said: give it away',beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'FROM SCOTLAND',text:'Andrew Carnegie was born in Scotland in 1835. His family was poor. They moved to the United States when he was twelve. Later he made steel, the strong metal used for railways and bridges. He became one of the richest men who ever lived.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'In 1889 he wrote about what rich people should do with their money. What did he say?',options:['Keep it safe for your children','Give most of it away before you die','Spend it on whatever you like'],answer:1,reveal:'He said that dying rich is a disgrace: something to be ashamed of. A rich person has a job to do. The job is to give the money away, and to do it well.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Carnegie said a rich person who dies still rich has failed at their job. The job is to give the money away, with care, while you are alive.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A safe for money, swept almost empty, with the door left open. Carnegie thought a rich person\u2019s safe should look like this at the end.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Did he do it?',text:'Lots of people say the rich should give. Carnegie really did. He gave away about nine out of every ten dollars he had. He paid for libraries, schools and places where people do science.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'AT THE SCHOOL FAIR',text:'Two friends both say they will give to the school fair. One has lots of money and gives a little. The other has a little and gives most of it. The share they gave tells you who meant it.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A famous singer says rich people should share their money. How can you tell if she means it?',options:['Look at how much of her own money she gave','Listen to how strongly she says it','Count how many people agree with her'],answer:0,why:'Anyone can say it. What she gave, out of what she had, shows whether she believes it.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'AFTER THE SPEECH',text:'A wealthy guest gives a speech about giving back. The room applauds. On the way home somebody asks the obvious question: how much has the speaker actually given? Nobody in the car knows.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Carnegie argued that dying rich is a disgrace. He meant it as an obligation, not a sentiment. In our gloss, a fortune still undistributed at death is evidence of a failure of nerve or imagination.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE CHAIN OF HIS ARGUMENT',nodes:['Great wealth grows out of social conditions','So the fortune is held in trust','So it must be given back','By the owner, while still alive'],note:'Each step follows from the one before. The last is the step most fortunes never reach.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the essay reads as a programme',text:'Plenty of people have said the rich should give. What sets this essay apart is that its author then did it. Carnegie gave away roughly ninety per cent of his fortune before he died, on libraries, universities and research institutions. The argument came first, and most of the giving followed it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'An emptied strongroom with the door propped open to the street. One coin is left on the floor. That was the shape of success, on his terms.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time someone argues that wealth should be given away, look up what share of their own they have given. Not the total. The share.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Two donors each give $10 million. One had $20 million; the other has $5 billion. By Carnegie\u2019s test, what tells you more?',options:['The share of each fortune that was given away','The size of the gift, since the two are equal','Whether each gift was announced in public','Which donor speaks more often about giving'],answer:0,why:'His standard is distribution of the fortune. Half of one fortune is a programme. A sliver of the other is a gesture.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'timeline',kicker:'A LIFE IN FIVE DATES',points:[{when:'1835',what:'Born in Dunfermline, Scotland, to a weaver\u2019s family.'},{when:'1848',what:'The family emigrates to Pennsylvania.'},{when:'1889',what:'Publishes the essay *Wealth*, soon reprinted as The Gospel of Wealth.'},{when:'1901',what:'Sells his steel business and turns to giving full time.'},{when:'1919',what:'Dies, having given away most of his fortune.'}],note:'The essay comes before most of the giving. That is why it can be read as a plan he then carried out.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A fortune still undistributed at death is not a private matter but a failure of duty. The obligation is to give it away during life, competently, rather than leave it to heirs or to bequest.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What he was arguing against',text:'Two habits of the rich of his day: passing great fortunes to children, and leaving them to public causes in a will. Both, he argued, avoid the hard part, which is deciding well while you are alive to be judged on it. The thesis about dying disgraced is his verdict on both.'},{kind:'term',word:'Operational',meaning:'Meant as a working rule that can be checked, not as a mood. An operational claim says what to do and how failure would show: here, in the size of the estate at death.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the record matters to the reading',text:'He gave away the great majority of his fortune, which is more than most people making the same argument. So the essay cannot be waved off as rhetoric. That is also what makes its contradictions worth taking seriously: they belong to a man who acted on his views.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Proportion is a test of sincerity, not of merit. Giving away nearly all of a fortune says nothing about how the fortune was made, and the doctrine has no place to ask. A man can give away ninety per cent of money he should never have kept. Later ideas in this course take that up.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Dying rich was, for Carnegie, a failure of duty.','The duty is to distribute during life, with judgement.','He largely did it, which is why the essay is a programme.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A founder pledges to give her whole fortune to charity in her will. How would Carnegie\u2019s thesis judge the pledge?',options:['As short of the duty, which is to distribute it competently while alive','As meeting the duty, since the money still reaches public causes','As better than lifetime giving, since it allows time for advice','As beside the point, since only the size of a fortune matters'],answer:0,why:'The thesis is about timing and competence, not only destination. A bequest leaves the judgement undone.'}]}},'gospel-of-wealth_2':{pictureCaption:'Open palms under a heavy sack, supporting it rather than clutching it, as if minding it for somebody else.',bridge:'Idea 1 set out the duty. This is the reason he gave for it: the fortune was never wholly his.',basics:{title:'Looking after money that isn\u2019t yours',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE CLASS TRIP TIN',text:'Your class is saving for a trip. You are picked to look after the money. It sits in a tin in your room. You could buy a game with it. But you know it is not really yours.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Carnegie said a rich person is like someone minding money for other people. The money came from many people working together. So it should go back to help them.'},{kind:'term',word:'Trustee',meaning:'Someone trusted to look after money or things for other people. A trustee keeps it safe and uses it for them, not for themselves.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Look at the hands. They are not gripping the sack. They hold it flat on open palms, as if they are carrying it for someone else.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why wasn\u2019t it all his?',text:'Carnegie got rich from steel. But he did not make it alone. Thousands of workers made the steel. Railways carried it. Towns bought it. Laws kept his business safe. All of that helped the money grow.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The odd part',text:'Carnegie still thought it was fine to get very rich. He just said you must not keep it all, or hand it to your children. People still argue about whether those two ideas fit together.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You score the winning goal. Which thought is most like Carnegie\u2019s idea?',options:['My team set me up, so the win is partly theirs','I scored, so the win is all mine','Goals are luck, so nobody earns the credit'],answer:0,why:'The goal was yours. But the passes, the coach and the team helped it happen. That is what Carnegie said about his money.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'A fortune belongs entirely to the person who built it.',reality:'Carnegie, who built one of the largest, said no. The laws, markets, labour and public works that made it possible were not his creation. So the surplus was held in trust for the community.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The rich man is a trustee for the community. The fortune arrived through a social system and is owed back to it. The person who gathered it is the one who manages it.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHAT THE FRAME ALLOWS',left:{title:'It defends',items:['Piling up an enormous fortune','The rich man choosing where it goes']},right:{title:'It rejects',items:['Passing the fortune to heirs','Keeping it to the grave']},note:'Both columns follow from the same idea. That tension runs through the whole essay.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why read the source',text:'Giving on a grand scale still runs on this argument: the person who made the money is the right person to decide where it goes. Today the assumption usually goes unspoken. Carnegie states it openly. So you can see the premise before deciding whether to accept it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The hands support the sack rather than grip it. Carnegie\u2019s rich man is meant to carry the fortune, not to own it.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick something that went well for you this year. List what arrived through a system rather than through you: a school, a recommendation, a road, a law, a market.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A successful founder says, \u201cI built this alone.\u201d Which reply uses Carnegie\u2019s trustee argument?',options:['Customers, staff, laws and markets made it possible, and you made none of them','Nobody builds anything, since success is entirely a matter of luck','Founders should hand their companies straight over to the state','You should leave the company to your children to keep it safe'],answer:0,why:'The argument does not deny the founder\u2019s work. It says the conditions that turned work into a fortune were social. So the surplus is owed back.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'HOW THE ESSAY OPENS',text:'The essay begins by defending the age that produced men like him. Competition, he grants, is hard on individuals but good for society as a whole, and it gathers wealth into a few hands. Having accepted that, he must say what the few owe. Trusteeship is his answer.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Surplus wealth is a trust, not a possession. The rich man administers it for the community that made it possible, and the duty ends only when it has been given back.'},{kind:'term',word:'Trusteeship',meaning:'Holding property for the benefit of others. In law a trustee answers to the beneficiaries and can be removed for mismanagement. Carnegie borrows the word, and its moral weight.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Two jobs for one idea',text:'The frame lets him defend enormous accumulation, because a trustee should be capable and the capable gather wealth. It also lets him reject inheritance, because a trust is not family property. Most of the essay lives in the space between those two.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Palms open under the sack, carrying rather than clutching. The posture of a trustee. Notice that nobody else is in the picture to check on him.'},{kind:'limits',text:'A real trustee is appointed and answers to someone. Carnegie\u2019s trustee appoints himself, chooses the beneficiaries, judges his own performance and cannot be removed. The word brings the moral weight of a legal duty without any of its checks. Modern philanthropy inherited that assumption, and it is easier to see stated plainly here.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Who were the beneficiaries of Carnegie\u2019s trust, and what say did they have in how it was run?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'FIND THE GAP',prompt:'Which feature of a legal trust is missing from Carnegie\u2019s version?',options:['Beneficiaries who can hold the trustee to account','A duty to use the property for other people','Property held by one person for the good of many','A rule against keeping the property for yourself'],answer:0,why:'He keeps the duty and drops the accountability. His trustee answers only to his own judgement.'}]}},'gospel-of-wealth_3':{pictureCaption:'Hands reach towards a stall that hands out a ladder and tools, not food. Carnegie\u2019s kind of giving, and its question: who can use a ladder?',bridge:'Ideas 1 and 2 said the fortune must go back to the community. This one is about how, and the kind of giving he refused.',basics:{title:'Help for a day, or help that lasts?',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'MONEY TO HELP A TOWN',prompt:'Carnegie had money to help a town. Which do you think he chose?',options:['Hand out coins to anyone who asks','Build a free library anyone can use'],answer:1,reveal:'He chose the library. He thought coins help for a day, and then the need comes back. A library lets people learn, and maybe get a better job.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Carnegie did not like giving coins to whoever asked. He liked building things, like libraries, that help people help themselves.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The hands reach out. The stall does not give them food. It gives them a ladder and tools, so they can build something.'},{kind:'explain',title:'His reason',text:'Carnegie thought most giving in his day was wasted. A coin gets spent, and the problem stays. He also thought givers often just wanted to feel kind. They did not check if anything got better.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Help for today',items:['A hot meal','A warm coat']},right:{title:'Help that lasts',items:['A library','Swimming lessons']},note:'Both kinds are good. The mistake is mixing them up.'},{kind:'explain',title:'But wait',text:'What if someone is too sick or too hungry to read? A library does not feed them. Some people need help today, not a ladder. Carnegie\u2019s plan did not really answer that.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A boy in your class finds reading hard. Which is help that lasts?',options:['Practising reading with him each week','Reading his book out loud for him today','Telling him the answers in class'],answer:0,why:'Reading to him helps today. Practising builds something he keeps. Both can be kind, but only one lasts.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Any money given to people in need is money well spent.',reality:'Carnegie thought most charity in his day was wasted, and some of it did harm. He reckoned that of every thousand dollars given, about nine hundred and fifty was spent unwisely.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Indiscriminate giving relieves the symptom and sustains the condition. Carnegie preferred institutions that make self-improvement possible for those willing to use them.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the giver is buying',text:'His sharpest point is about the giver. Money handed over in passing buys the feeling of having helped, at once and cheaply. Whether anything changed is never checked. Libraries, parks, halls and universities, he argued, buy an effect instead.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO KINDS OF HELP',left:{title:'Relieving a symptom',items:['Food for tonight','Rent for this month','Works at once']},right:{title:'Funding a capability',items:['A course, a library, a tool','Pays off over years','Needs spare time and energy']},note:'Both are valid. Confusing them is not.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The blind spot',text:'The scheme assumes the problem is a lack of opportunity. For some people it is. For others it is illness, hunger or a family to look after, and a library does nothing for them. And the donor is the one who decides which case is which.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of the last thing you gave money to. Was it relieving a symptom or funding a capability? Name which, and whether that was what you meant to do.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A charity asks you to fund either winter coats for a shelter or a job-training course. What does the distinction tell you?',options:['They do different jobs, so decide first which job you mean to fund','Only the course counts, because coats merely relieve a symptom','Only the coats count, because need always comes before opportunity','They are the same, since both help people who are in hardship'],answer:0,why:'Carnegie would pick the course. The lesson worth keeping is narrower: know which you are funding, and do not mistake one for the other.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'HIS COLDEST PAGES',text:'Carnegie is blunt about almsgiving. He would rather, he says, see a rich man\u2019s millions thrown into the sea than spent in ways that encourage idleness. He estimates that ninety-five per cent of the charitable giving of his day was unwise.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Giving without discrimination relieves the symptom and keeps the condition alive. The better use of wealth is to build what helps people who are already trying to help themselves.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HIS CASE AGAINST ALMS',nodes:['Money given without inquiry','The need is met for a day','The cause of it continues','The giver feels he has helped'],note:'On his account the last step is the real fault: it ends the giver\u2019s interest at exactly the point where nothing has changed.'},{kind:'term',word:'The deserving poor',meaning:'A common nineteenth-century sorting of people in need into those thought to have earned help and those thought not to. Carnegie\u2019s scheme rests on a version of it, made by the giver.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The stall hands out a ladder and tools, not food. The whole programme in one image, including the hands it may never reach.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The argument has force: relief can become a way of not looking. But it assumes the recipient\u2019s problem is opportunity rather than circumstance, which is true for some and false for others. It also puts the donor in charge of deciding which. The essay never asks who is qualified to judge that, though the whole scheme rests on it.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'When you give, who decides what the person receiving it needs: you, or them?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'An admirer of Carnegie says that alms only keep people where they are. What is the strongest reply?',options:['It depends whether their obstacle is opportunity or circumstance, and the giver may not know which','Alms are always better, because they reach people in need straight away','Libraries never helped anyone, so his alternative fails on its own terms','Giving should be left to the state, which can judge need impartially'],answer:0,why:'The reply grants what is right in the point and names its blind spot: the scheme assumes one kind of problem and lets the donor decide who has it.'}]}},'gospel-of-wealth_4':{pictureCaption:'A grand new building, and at the foot of its steps the worn boots of the people who built it, lined up and waiting outside.',bridge:'Idea 2 said the fortune came through other people\u2019s labour. This idea asks what those people were paid.',basics:{title:'Where the money came from',beats:[{kind:'timeline',kicker:'THREE YEARS APART',points:[{when:'1889',what:'Carnegie writes that the rich must use their money for the good of all.'},{when:'1892',what:'At his steelworks in Homestead, a fight over pay ends with men dead.'}],note:'Both things are true about the same man.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What happened at Homestead',text:'The steelworkers there worked shifts of twelve hours. Their pay was cut. They went on strike. Guards were hired to take back the steelworks, and a gun fight broke out. People on both sides died. Carnegie was away in Scotland. His manager, Henry Clay Frick, was in charge.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Carnegie gave away a huge fortune. But the money came from workers on long shifts and cut pay. He was sure big gifts did more good than higher pay. He never tested that on his own workers.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A grand building, paid for by a rich man. In front of it, the worn-out boots of the workers. Who really paid for it?'},{kind:'example',kicker:'AT THE BAKE SALE',text:'A girl runs a bake sale. Her helpers work all day and get one biscuit each. She keeps the rest of the money. Then she gives a big gift to the school, and everyone claps. The helpers might ask: why not pay us fairly first?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Which question does the bake sale make you ask about Carnegie?',options:['Would fair pay have done more good than gifts later?','Did he give enough of his money to libraries?','Should he have been at home when the fight began?'],answer:0,why:'The gifts were real. But they came out of money the workers did not get. He was sure gifts were better, but he never tested that against paying his own workers more.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'HOMESTEAD, PENNSYLVANIA, 1892',text:'Three years after the essay, a wage dispute at Carnegie\u2019s Homestead steelworks became a strike. The company brought in Pinkerton agents, private guards, to retake the works. They and the strikers fought, and men died. Carnegie was in Scotland. His manager, Henry Clay Frick, ran the company\u2019s response.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The essay says wealth should be administered for the good of the community. It assumes, and never tests against his own works, that giving the money away later does more good than paying the workers more at the time.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'ONE MAN, TWO RECORDS',left:{title:'What the essay says',items:['Wealth is held in trust','Use it for the community','Give it away while living']},right:{title:'How it was made',items:['Twelve-hour shifts','Wages cut','A strike defeated at Homestead']},note:'The libraries on the left were paid for by the wage bill on the right.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Hypocrisy, or something more useful?',text:'The easy reading is that he preached one thing and did another. A sharper reading is that his doctrine requires it. If the rich are the best people to manage wealth, then gathering as much as possible, by any lawful means, is the first step of the philanthropy.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The building is the gift. The boots at the foot of the steps are where the money came from. The essay ranks the building above the boots without asking them.'},{kind:'try',text:'When someone justifies a large surplus by what they will give later, ask the plain question: what would paying more at the time have done, and for whom?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A company holds wages down and funds a celebrated scholarship scheme. What question does this idea tell you to ask?',options:['Would higher pay now do more good than the scholarships later?','Are the scholarships generous enough to justify the profits?','Is the scheme publicised widely enough to be noticed?','Would the founder give more if taxes were a little lower?'],answer:0,why:'That is the comparison Carnegie\u2019s essay settles in favour of the gift without testing it. A gift can be real and still not be the best use of the money.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Homestead simply shows that Carnegie was a hypocrite.',reality:'Perhaps. The more useful reading is that his doctrine requires what happened. Hypocrisy is a flaw in a man. This would be a flaw in the argument, and arguments outlive their authors.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The contradiction lies between how the fortune was made and what the essay says about its use. The framework assumes, and has no way to test, that giving it away later beats paying more at the time.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'HOMESTEAD, 1892',text:'The essay was three years old when a wage dispute at Homestead became a strike. Pinkerton agents hired to retake the works fought the steelworkers, and men on both sides died. Carnegie was in Scotland; Henry Clay Frick, his manager, handled it. The strike was defeated.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHY THE DOCTRINE NEEDS IT',nodes:['The rich administer wealth best','So more surplus means more good','So keep costs, wages included, low','Libraries are paid from the wage bill'],note:'Each step is lawful and, inside the doctrine, virtuous. The objection has nowhere to get in.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The finished building, the worn boots lined up below it. Carnegie\u2019s framework ranks the first above the second and never tests the ranking.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Two honest qualifications. Carnegie had a general reply: he argued that large sums spent on public institutions do more good than the same money scattered in small amounts, even as wages. And higher wages alone would not have built libraries. But he never tested that reply against his own payroll, or asked the people whose pay it was.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Who is best placed to judge whether a worker\u2019s extra pay or a donor\u2019s library does more good?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A tech firm squeezes contractor pay and then funds a famous foundation. Which criticism follows Carnegie\u2019s contradiction most closely?',options:['The foundation\u2019s budget came from pay the contractors did not get, and nobody asked them','The foundation ought to support different causes from the ones it has chosen','Foundations are always run less efficiently than ordinary companies are','The founder is plainly insincere about wanting to help anyone at all'],answer:0,why:'The point is structural, not about sincerity. The surplus was taken first and handed out later, and the people it came from had no say.'}]}},'gospel-of-wealth_5':{pictureCaption:'One document mid-table, chairs drawn up equally on both sides. Set out as a case to argue, not a tribute to admire.',bridge:'After the contradiction in idea 4, the question is what to do with the essay. This idea\u2019s answer: argue with it.',basics:{title:'Hear the other side at its best',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE CLASS DEBATE',text:'Your class is having a debate. You are told to argue for school uniforms. You hate school uniforms. So you have to find the best reasons the other side has. It is hard. It is also the first time you really get why they think it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When you disagree with an idea, read the best version of it, by someone who believes it. Then you can see the exact spot where you disagree.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One page in the middle of a table. Chairs on both sides, the same number. It is ready to be argued about, fairly.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why Carnegie is good for this',text:'Carnegie says plainly what he thinks. It is fine for a few people to get very rich. And the rich know best how to spend money for others. You may think that is wrong. But he says it so clearly that you can point to the part you disagree with.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Reading it as praise',items:['A kind rich man','He gave lots, so he was right']},right:{title:'Reading it as an argument',items:['What does he take for granted?','Is each part true?']},note:'The first way, you just clap. The second way, you think.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You think video games are good for kids. What is the best thing to read?',options:['The best case against games, by someone who means it','A list of reasons why games are great','A funny post making fun of people who hate games'],answer:0,why:'You learn most from the best version of the side you don\u2019t agree with. Then you know where you really stand.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'BEFORE YOU READ IT',prompt:'What is the most useful way to read an essay by a famous donor?',options:['As the life story of a generous man','As an argument with premises you can test','As propaganda, to be dismissed'],answer:1,reveal:'Read as a life story, it becomes an advert. The assumptions vanish into the man\u2019s life. Dismissed, you never see them at all. Read as an argument, they are on the table.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Read the essay as the clearest statement on offer of a view you may well reject. The view is that letting wealth gather in a few hands, then giving it freely, serves people better than not letting it gather at all.'},{kind:'steps',kicker:'HIS PREMISES, IN ORDER',steps:['Wealth gathering in a few hands is bound to happen, and on balance good.','The rich are better placed to manage surplus than the state or the recipients.','The duty is met by giving it away during life.'],note:'Each is open to dispute. The second holds up the rest.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why clarity helps the critic',text:'Most writing about giving hides its assumptions behind good works. Carnegie states his. That makes disagreement possible. You can name the premise you reject, instead of feeling a vague unease about a generous man.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One document in the middle, chairs drawn up equally on both sides. A case set out to be argued, not a tribute to be admired.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick a view you reject. Find its clearest statement by someone who holds it, not a summary by your own side. Write its premises down, one line each.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A friend argues that since Carnegie gave away most of his money, his view of wealth must be sound. What is the best response?',options:['His giving is a fact about him; his premises still need testing','He was a hypocrite, so nothing in the essay is worth reading','He gave so much that his opinions should count as settled','The essay is too old now to be relevant to anything'],answer:0,why:'That is reading the essay as a testimonial. The giving was real. It settles nothing about whether the rich are the best people to manage the surplus.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'A TOOL FOR READING',word:'Load-bearing premise',meaning:'The assumption an argument cannot do without. Remove it and the conclusion falls, however sound the other steps are.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Read the essay as an argument whose structure can be assessed, not as a testimonial about a generous man. Its premises are stated plainly, and the load-bearing one is that the rich are the best administrators of surplus.'},{kind:'steps',title:'The premises, stated plainly',steps:['Concentration of wealth is inevitable and beneficial.','The rich are better qualified to administer surplus than the state or the recipients.','The duty is discharged by distribution during life.'],note:'Grant the first and third and reject the second, and the essay no longer reaches its conclusion.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Testing the second premise',text:'Better than the recipients, he says, because small sums spread among many do little lasting good. The comparison is asserted rather than shown, and the person making it is the one who gains from the answer.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Argument or testimonial',text:'Read as testimonial, the essay becomes an advertisement: the assumptions disappear into the biography of a man who did in fact give away most of his money. Read as argument, it is unusually clear about what it assumes. That is rare in the genre, and it is what makes disagreement possible.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One document, chairs pulled up equally on both sides. The essay set out as a case to be argued, which is the only fair way to reject it.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Reading a view at its strongest is not neutral either. Restating an argument at its best can lend it a dignity its consequences did not earn. The clean argument needs reading beside its record: Homestead, the wage bill, the people the libraries did not reach. Generous reading is a method, not a verdict.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which of the three premises would you reject first, and what would you put in its place?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which is a misuse of the advice to read the clearest statement of a view you reject?',options:['Reading only Carnegie\u2019s essay and treating his record as beside the point','Reading his essay first, before reading what his critics said of it','Listing his premises and then testing them one at a time','Rejecting one of his premises while granting the other two'],answer:0,why:'The point of reading the strong version is to argue with it properly. Using it to screen out the record turns a method into a defence.'}]}},'gospel-of-wealth_6':{pictureCaption:'Three envelopes, three ways to dispose of a fortune. Only the middle one has a pen across it: the one that still needs the giver\u2019s judgement.',bridge:'Idea 1 said give it away while you live. This is his full case, made against the only two alternatives.',basics:{title:'Three ways to pass on a fortune',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'IF YOU WERE OLD AND RICH',prompt:'Carnegie said there are three things a rich person can do with their money. Which one did he like?',options:['Leave it all to your family','Leave it to good causes when you die','Give it away yourself while you are alive'],answer:2,reveal:'He liked the third one. It is the only one where you have to think hard, choose well, and see what happens.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Three envelopes for three choices. Only one has a pen on it. That is the one where you still have to decide.'},{kind:'idea',text:'There are three ways to pass on a fortune. Carnegie said only one really counts: giving it away yourself, while you are alive and can choose with care.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What was wrong with the other two?',text:'Leaving it all to your family, he said, spoils them. Leaving it to good causes when you die costs you nothing. You can\u2019t take money with you. So giving it then is not a real choice.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE SHARING SWEETS',text:'Sharing your sweets at break time is a real choice. You give up something you could have eaten. Saying \u201cyou can have them when I leave school\u201d costs you nothing today.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Grandad has a big stamp collection. Which choice is most like what Carnegie praised?',options:['Giving it to a museum now, and helping set it up','Writing in his will that a museum gets it','Keeping it in a box for the family to sort out'],answer:0,why:'He chooses, he gives it up while he could still enjoy it, and he helps it get used well.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE SAME MONEY, THREE WAYS',text:'A retired business owner has a fortune and three options. Leave it to the children. Leave it to a hospital in her will. Or spend the next ten years giving it away herself, choosing the projects and seeing them through. The money is the same each time. What it asks of her is not.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Carnegie names three ways to dispose of a fortune: to family, by bequest at death, or by administering it yourself while alive. Only the third involves any judgement, or any risk to the giver.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'WHAT EACH COSTS THE GIVER',low:'Nothing: left to family, or left in a will',high:'Judgement and risk: given away while alive',note:'He says the gift at death earns no credit. Those who leave vast sums would not have left them had they been able to take them along.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Competence, not virtue',text:'His case for the third way is practical. The person who gathered the fortune is, he argues, best placed to deploy it, and deploying it takes judgement that cannot be exercised from the grave. A will names a destination. It cannot run anything.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Three envelopes. Two are finished business. The one with the pen across it is the gift that still asks something of the giver.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of something you mean to give away eventually: money, time, things, a skill. Ask what is gained by waiting, and who the delay serves.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Why would Carnegie decline to admire a large bequest to a university?',options:['The giver held on to it until it was of no more use to him','Bequests are nearly always smaller than lifetime gifts are','The money ought to have gone to the giver\u2019s family first','Universities cannot spend very large sums of money well'],answer:0,why:'For Carnegie the gift at death involves no sacrifice and no judgement, so it earns no credit.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'HIS GROUNDS',prompt:'Carnegie rejects two of the three ways to dispose of a fortune. On what grounds does he prefer the third?',options:['It is the most virtuous','It needs judgement only the living can exercise','It avoids tax on the estate'],answer:1,reveal:'His reasoning is about competence rather than virtue. That matters, because competence is the ground on which he turns out to be uniquely qualified.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Of the three ways to dispose of a fortune, only administration during life counts. Inheritance harms the heir, bequest is evasion, and only the living owner can bring judgement to the task.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Three options, three verdicts',steps:['Left to family: harmful to the heirs.','Left for public purposes at death: evasion, since it could not be taken along.','Administered by the owner in life: the only one that needs judgement.']},{kind:'explain',title:'An argument with an interest',text:'The reasoning is internally consistent. It also has a visible interest in its own conclusion. It makes the rich man uniquely qualified for a task he has assigned himself, and the qualification is the very thing it rewards: having gathered the fortune.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two envelopes finished, one with a pen across it. Carnegie\u2019s argument is that the third alone requires a living hand.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Making a fortune and spending one well are different skills, and nothing shows that one predicts the other. Lifetime giving can be hasty or bent to the giver\u2019s tastes. And Carnegie did not finish the task himself: he placed much of what remained in the Carnegie Corporation, a foundation set up in 1911 to keep giving after his death.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What are you holding back to give \u201clater\u201d? What would giving it now ask of you?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'Someone objects that making a fortune and giving it away well are different skills. Which part of the argument does this hit hardest?',options:['That the one who gathered it is best placed to deploy it','That leaving great wealth to heirs tends to harm them','That a bequest at death costs the giver nothing at all','That a fortune can be disposed of in only three ways'],answer:0,why:'His case for the third way rests on competence. If earning and giving are separate skills, the qualification he relies on does not carry over.'}]}},'gospel-of-wealth_7':{pictureCaption:'A reading room with a ladder up to the high shelves. Through the window, a mill chimney: where the readers would be coming from.',bridge:'Idea 3 was the giving he refused. This is what he funded instead, and who it was built for.',basics:{title:'A ladder only helps if you can climb',beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'A BOY WHO WANTED BOOKS',text:'When Carnegie was a working boy near Pittsburgh, he could not afford books. A local man, Colonel James Anderson, let working boys borrow from his own library on Saturdays. Carnegie borrowed books there. He never forgot it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Carnegie wanted to give ladders, not handouts. A ladder is something that lets people climb up by their own effort. For him, the best ladder was a free library.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Thousands of libraries',text:'When he was rich, he paid for free public libraries. He paid for more than two and a half thousand of them. But there was a rule. The town had to give the land, and promise to pay to keep it running.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A library with a tall ladder up to the top shelves. Out of the window, a mill chimney. The readers would come here after work.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A CLIMBING WALL',text:'A climbing wall at the park is great if you are strong and have time to practise. But a kid with a broken leg can\u2019t use it. It is not a bad wall. It just isn\u2019t for everyone.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Who gets left out?',text:'A library helps people with spare time and energy. Many steelworkers worked twelve hours a day. After that, who has the strength to read? Carnegie\u2019s plan helped some people and not others. He thought that was fine.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your town starts a free coding club after school. Who might it leave out?',options:['Kids who mind a little brother after school','Kids who already like computers','Kids who live close to the club'],answer:0,why:'The club is a ladder. It only helps those with time to climb. Kids with jobs at home don\u2019t have that time.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'A FREE EVENING CLASS',text:'A town opens a free evening class in bookkeeping. It fills with people who already had some schooling, some free evenings and someone at home to mind the children. The night-shift workers it was partly meant for never come. Nobody turned them away.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Carnegie\u2019s rule for giving was to provide ladders that the willing can climb, and to help only those who will help themselves. The principle works, and it excludes by design.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'LADDER OR RELIEF',left:{title:'A ladder',items:['A library, a park, a hall','Needs spare time and energy','Lasts for years']},right:{title:'Relief',items:['Food, rent, a coat','Asks nothing of the person','Lasts until it is used up']},note:'Carnegie chose the left column on purpose, and said so.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the conditions mattered',text:'His library grants required the town to supply the site and commit to paying for upkeep. The gift could not simply be received; the community had to climb a rung first. The conditions were the point, not paperwork.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A reading room, a ladder to the high shelves, and through the window the mill chimney. The ladder is there. The question is who has the evening free to climb it.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before you fund or organise something, ask: does using this need spare time and energy? Who in the group I mean to help has neither?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A charity gives laptops to families so children can study online. Who is this kind of ladder most likely to miss?',options:['Children with no quiet place or spare time to study','Children who already have a computer at home','Children whose parents work in an office','Children who prefer reading on paper'],answer:0,why:'A ladder only helps whoever has the circumstances to climb it. A laptop does nothing for a child whose obstacle is not the lack of a laptop.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'equation',kicker:'THE CARNEGIE FORMULA',parts:['Carnegie\u2019s money for the building','The town\u2019s land and upkeep'],result:'A free public library',note:'The grants required the community to provide the site and commit to running costs. The gift could not be passively received.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The best use of wealth is to provide ladders, not alms: provision that helps those willing to climb. The conditions are deliberate, and so is the exclusion.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'ALLEGHENY, 1850s',text:'As a young telegraph messenger, Carnegie used the private library of Colonel James Anderson, who lent books to working boys on Saturdays. He later credited it with opening the world of books to him. The library programme is that experience turned into policy.'},{kind:'explain',title:'An effective intervention',text:'The libraries worked. More than two and a half thousand were built, and many of the buildings still stand. But the model assumes a reader much like the young Carnegie: literate, ambitious and with evenings to spare.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The reading room, the ladder, and the mill chimney through the window. The gift and the working day that decided who could use it, in one frame.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The principle excludes by design. It works for the person with the capacity and circumstance to use it, and offers nothing to the person whose obstacle is not the absence of a ladder: the exhausted, the sick, the carer. Carnegie treats that as a feature. His own mills set the twelve-hour days that made the ladder hard to climb.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of a programme you support. What does using it assume about people\u2019s time and energy?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HARD CASE',prompt:'A steelworker on twelve-hour shifts lives two streets from a new Carnegie library. On Carnegie\u2019s principle, what does the library owe him?',options:['Access and nothing more: the ladder is there if he can find time to climb','Shorter hours at the mill, so that he has time to use the library','Relief payments until he is in a position to start reading','Books chosen to suit his own trade and his particular interests'],answer:0,why:'That is the principle working as designed. Whether it is enough is exactly what its critics questioned.'}]}},'gospel-of-wealth_8':{pictureCaption:'Beside an empty armchair, shares lifted onto trays: none from the smallest, some from the middle, a heap from the largest. A graduated estate tax.',bridge:'Idea 6 rejected the bequest. This is what he wanted the state to do to fortunes kept until death.',basics:{title:'A rich man who wanted to tax the rich',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'WHAT YOU MIGHT EXPECT',belief:'Rich people always want taxes on the rich to be low.',reality:'Carnegie was one of the richest men alive. He wanted a big tax on the money rich people leave when they die.'},{kind:'term',word:'Estate',meaning:'Everything a person owns when they die: money, houses, land. An estate tax takes part of it for the country before the rest is passed on.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Carnegie said that when a rich person dies still rich, the country should take a large share. The bigger the fortune, the bigger the share.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'HOW THE TAX GROWS',low:'A small sum left to a family: little or no tax',high:'A giant fortune: a very big share taken',note:'The tax rises as the amount rises. That kind of tax is called graduated.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The chair and stick are left behind. Their owner is gone. The trays show the tax: none from a small sum, some from a bigger one, a big heap from the biggest.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why would he want that?',text:'He thought dying rich was a disgrace. A big tax would push the rich to give their money away while they were alive. It would also show that the country thought sitting on a pile of money until you die was wrong.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it matters',text:'He was asking for a rule that would cost people like him. That is a strong sign he really believed it. Talk is easy when it costs you nothing.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A boy says limits on screen time are a good idea, even for him. Why might you trust him more?',options:['He wants a rule that costs him something','He is older than the other kids','He said it in a loud, sure voice'],answer:0,why:'It is easy to want rules for other people. Wanting a rule that costs you is strong proof you mean it.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A STEEL MAGNATE ON TAX',prompt:'What did one of the richest men in the world say about taxing large estates at death?',options:['It is theft and should be abolished','It should be light, to protect families','It is the wisest tax of all'],answer:2,reveal:'He called heavy, graduated duties on estates the wisest form of taxation, and welcomed the growing willingness to impose them.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Carnegie argued for a heavy, graduated tax on large estates. The state should take a substantial share of a fortune kept until death, both to push the rich to give during life and to mark public disapproval of a selfish one.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHY IT FOLLOWS',nodes:['The fortune is held in trust','Keeping it till death is a default','The state reclaims what was owed','So the rich give early instead'],note:'The tax is not an intrusion on his doctrine. It is the doctrine\u2019s remedy.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Two purposes',text:'One is practical: a heavy tax at death makes giving during life the better option. The other is moral. By taking its share, he argues, the state publicly condemns a life spent hoarding.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'THE SHARE GROWS',caption:'The armchair and walking stick are left behind. A share is lifted onto a tray from each fortune, and it grows with the fortune: nothing from the smallest, a heap from the largest.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The half people leave out',text:'The essay is often cited by people who admire the philanthropy and would reject the tax. But the two halves are one argument. Accept that the fortune is held in trust, and the tax comes with it.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time someone argues for a rule, ask whether it would cost them. If it would, treat that as evidence that they mean it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A politician who owns several rental flats campaigns for tighter rules on landlords. What does this idea suggest?',options:['A rule that costs its advocate is strong evidence they believe in it','It is probably a trick, since nobody acts against their own interest','Their view counts for less because they are personally involved','Who proposes a rule tells you nothing about how they really think'],answer:0,why:'Arguing for a rule that would cost you is the strongest available sign of conviction. It does not make the rule right, but it tells you the belief is real.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'ONE ESSAY, TWO HALVES',left:{title:'The half admirers quote',items:['Give it away while living','Libraries, not alms','The rich as trustees']},right:{title:'The half they skip',items:['Heavy duties on large estates','The state takes a large share','Hoarding publicly condemned']},note:'Both columns come from the same essay, and from the same premise.'},{kind:'term',word:'Graduated',meaning:'Rising in steps with the amount. A graduated duty takes little or nothing from modest estates and a rising share as estates grow larger.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Nothing on the smallest tray, a few coins on the next, a heap on the largest, all beside a chair its owner has left. Graduation, drawn: the share rises with the estate.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Heavy, graduated duties on large estates are the wisest of taxes. A fortune kept until death is a default on the trust, and the state reclaiming it is the remedy, not an intrusion.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Not an aside',text:'He welcomes the spreading willingness to tax large estates heavily. He wants the duties graduated, light on modest sums left to dependants and severe on the largest fortunes. And he presents the state\u2019s share as a public verdict on a selfish rich man\u2019s wasted life.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it is strong evidence',text:'Here a steel magnate argues for a rule that would cost him and his kind. People rarely do that without conviction. It shows the trust doctrine was held, not merely deployed.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Two strains show. A tax designed partly to condemn a way of life is a moral sentence passed through the revenue, which many reject in principle. And it sits awkwardly with his load-bearing premise: if the rich administer wealth better than anyone, the state is a poor heir to the job. His answer is that the rich should never let it come to that.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which rules have you argued for that would cost you something, and which cost only other people?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISREADING',prompt:'A think tank cites The Gospel of Wealth to argue that private philanthropy makes estate taxes unnecessary. What is wrong with the citation?',options:['Carnegie argued for heavy estate taxes as part of the same doctrine','Carnegie opposed private philanthropy in favour of state spending','Carnegie wrote nothing at all about inheritance or about estates','Carnegie thought that taxes should fall only on wages and trade'],answer:0,why:'The essay supports both lifetime giving and a severe estate tax, and treats them as one argument. Citing the first against the second cuts it in half.'}]}},'gospel-of-wealth_9':{pictureCaption:'A rowing boat hung in slings above the water, hull perfect, oars unmarked. Kept safe from the one thing it was built for.',bridge:'Idea 6 dismissed leaving a fortune to family as harmful. This is his reason: what it does to the person who receives it.',basics:{title:'Can a big gift do harm?',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'A BOAT THAT NEVER SAILS',text:'Picture a new rowing boat. Someone hangs it up in a shed so it never gets wet. It stays perfect. But it never goes anywhere. It never does the thing it was made for.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The boat hangs above the water on straps. Not a scratch on it. And no one has ever rowed it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Carnegie thought leaving a huge fortune to your children can harm them. If they never need to work, they may never find out what they can do.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'AT HOME',text:'Imagine a parent did all your homework for you, every night, for years. Your marks would look great. But you would never learn to work things out. The help would take away the effort that was making you better.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What he did allow',text:'He did not say leave your family nothing. He thought wives and daughters should get a modest amount. In his day, women had few ways to earn money. It was huge fortunes he was against.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A rich aunt offers to pay for everything, so you never need a job. What might Carnegie warn?',options:['You might never find out what you can do','You would spend it all much too fast','Your friends would all be jealous of you'],answer:0,why:'His worry was not the money itself. It was the effort the money would take away, and what that effort does for you.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'ask',kicker:'A THOUGHT EXPERIMENT',prompt:'Suppose the next twenty years of your work were made optional tomorrow. Be honest: what would you become?'},{kind:'idea',text:'Carnegie objects to great inheritances because of what they do to the heir. A fortune usually dulls the heir\u2019s talents and drive. It draws him into a less useful life than work would have given him.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HIS CHAIN OF HARM',nodes:['A fortune arrives, unearned','The need to work disappears','Talents go unused','A less useful life'],note:'His concern is the person, not the money. The fortune removes an effort that was doing something for them.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Not against all inheritance',text:'He allows modest provision, especially for wives and daughters, since women then had few ways to earn. What he objects to is fortunes. The line falls between a cushion and a replacement for a working life.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A boat in slings above the water, hull perfect, oars unmarked. Protected from everything, including the use that would have made it worth having.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before giving someone an advantage, ask what effort it removes, and whether that effort was doing something for them.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Parents could pay their adult son\u2019s rent with no end date while he works out what to do. What would Carnegie\u2019s argument ask them to weigh?',options:['Whether the support removes an effort that was building his abilities','Whether the rent is a fair price for the flat he is living in','Whether other parents they know are doing the same thing','Whether the money would grow faster if it were invested'],answer:0,why:'His objection is to what an unearned cushion does to the person who gets it, not to the money itself.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'AN ARGUMENT BEFORE AN HEIR',text:'When the essay appeared in 1889, Carnegie had been married two years and had no children. His only child, a daughter, was born in 1897. The case against leaving great fortunes to heirs was made in the abstract, and met his own family only later.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Great wealth is a misfortune to the heir. Left to a child, a fortune tends to blunt their abilities and ambition, and leaves them living less usefully than they would have done without it.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHERE HE DRAWS THE LINE',left:{title:'What he objects to',items:['Fortunes left to sons','Wealth that makes work optional']},right:{title:'What he allows',items:['Moderate provision for wives and daughters','A cushion, not a replacement']},note:'His target is fortunes, not inheritance as such.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The evidence',text:'His evidence is anecdotal and his conviction total. The claim has some support and considerable qualification since: the effect depends heavily on the amount, on what the heir expects, and on what else is handed down with the money.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The boat hangs clear of the water, flawless and unused. Carnegie\u2019s heir, as he imagined him: kept from harm and from purpose at once.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The claim is about the heir, but it is also convenient for a doctrine that needs fortunes to go elsewhere. It measures the heir by usefulness as Carnegie defines it. Still, it is the part of his doctrine that bound his own family, which is a fair test of whether an argument is held or merely deployed.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What advantage were you given that removed an effort? Was that effort doing something for you?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'Which case is closest to Carnegie\u2019s worry about heirs?',options:['A gifted pupil handed every answer, who never learns to work a problem through','A pupil whose scholarship covers the fees but leaves all the work to do','A pupil who inherits a modest sum and spends it on training for a trade','A pupil who receives no help at all and leaves school early to work'],answer:0,why:'His objection is to an advantage that removes the effort that was building the person. A modest cushion that still leaves the work to do is what he allows.'}]}}}},2972,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'how-couples-actually-argue_1':{pictureCaption:'The ball has only just left the hand, and its whole line down the lane is already set. An argument\u2019s first sentence works the same way.',basics:{title:'How you start a row decides a lot',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE MISSING CHOCOLATE',text:'You get home. The chocolate you were saving is gone. Your brother ate it. You are cross, and you go to find him. What is the first thing you say?'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Start one',items:['\u201cYou\u2019re so greedy.\u201d','\u201cYou always take my stuff.\u201d']},right:{title:'Start two',items:['\u201cYou ate my chocolate.\u201d','\u201cI\u2019m upset. I was saving it.\u201d']},note:'Start one is about who he is. Start two is about what he did and how you feel.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The first thing you say in a row shapes the rest of it. Say what happened and how you feel, not what is wrong with the other person.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Once the ball leaves your hand, its path is set. Your first words in a row are like that ball.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why is it so hard?',text:'You say the first thing when you are most cross. So the words come out sharp, even if you meant to be fair. The best fix is to wait until you are calmer. Then start.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you are cross with someone, wait ten minutes before you say anything. Then start with what they did and how you feel.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend borrowed your game and lost a piece. Which start will most likely go well?',options:['\u201cA piece is missing, and I\u2019m really sad about it.\u201d','\u201cYou\u2019re so careless. You lose everything.\u201d','\u201cI knew I shouldn\u2019t trust you with it.\u201d'],answer:0,why:'The other two are about what kind of person your friend is. That makes them defend themselves instead of helping.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'WATCHING COUPLES ARGUE',prompt:'In studies that watched couples disagree, what forecast a good deal of how the disagreement went?',options:['What it was about','How it was opened','Who was more upset'],answer:1,reveal:'The opening. An opening that names a behaviour and a feeling runs differently from one that names a character flaw, and the difference shows almost at once.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The first minute of a disagreement predicts much of the rest. The first sentence is the move with the highest return. And it is wholly in the hands of whoever raises the subject.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW A HARSH OPENING RUNS',nodes:['A harsh first sentence','The other person defends','Both escalate','The real issue never reached'],note:'The direction is set before the substance arrives.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE SAME COMPLAINT, TWICE',text:'*You never think about anyone but yourself* names a flaw, and invites a defence of character. *You booked the weekend without asking me, and I felt left out* names a behaviour and a feeling, and invites a reply about the weekend.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why you cannot just phrase it better',text:'The first sentence usually comes out when you are most annoyed. Good intentions do not survive that. The phrasing follows your state, not your plan. So the reliable fix is timing. Raise it later, once the state has passed, and the better sentence comes more easily.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before raising the next thing that annoys you, wait until you are calmer, then write your first sentence down. Check it names a behaviour and a feeling.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Your partner has left the kitchen in a mess again, and you are furious. What does this idea suggest?',options:['Raise it later, starting with the mess and how it makes you feel','Raise it now, while the evidence is fresh and you can point to it','Say nothing at all, since any complaint will only start a row','Raise it now, but keep your voice low and your words polite'],answer:0,why:'The opening sets the course. When you are most annoyed, the opening comes out harsh, however polite you try to be. Waiting is the fix.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHAT THE ESSAY COVERS',text:'Decades of observational research on couples in conflict, much of it from John Gottman\u2019s laboratory, produced a handful of findings that have held up reasonably well. The essay notes that most are about how an argument opens and ends, not what it is about. This is the first.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The opening minutes of a conflict discussion say a good deal about how it ends, and in Gottman\u2019s work, how such discussions go is associated with how the relationship fares.'},{kind:'term',word:'Harsh start-up',meaning:'Opening a disagreement by naming a character flaw rather than a behaviour and a feeling. The essay treats it as the first link in an escalation chain.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The mechanism',text:'Escalation dynamics. A harsh opening provokes defensiveness; defensiveness provokes escalation. The trajectory is set before either person reaches the substance. The divergence between a harsh and a soft opening appears almost immediately, rather than building over time.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The ball has barely left the hand and its line is already fixed. The essay\u2019s claim is that a first sentence behaves like this release.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The practical twist',text:'If the first sentence matters most, the obvious advice is to phrase it better. The essay argues that this is unreliable, because the sentence is produced at peak irritation, and phrasing follows state. The dependable lever is delay.'},{kind:'limits',text:'This is association, not destiny: the opening forecasts a good deal, not everything. Much of the evidence comes from one research group, and the essay\u2019s last idea explains where its predictive claims overreached. Delay can also slide into never raising the subject at all.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISREADING',prompt:'Someone concludes: \u201cSo if our arguments open badly, our relationship is doomed.\u201d What is wrong with this?',options:['The finding is an association, and the opening is the part you can change','The finding was about the topic of arguments, not how they opened','The finding showed openings matter only in couples who are already unhappy','The finding has been withdrawn, so no conclusion can be drawn from it'],answer:0,why:'The essay reports an association, not a sentence passed. Its practical point is the reverse of doom: the opening is fully in your control.'}]}},'how-couples-actually-argue_2':{pictureCaption:'Most of the marks on this wall are ordinary dents. One has eaten right through. Contempt is that one.',bridge:'Idea 1 was about how an argument opens. This one picks out the single behaviour the research treats as different in kind.',basics:{title:'The one thing that is worse than shouting',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'IN A ROW WITH YOUR SISTER',prompt:'Which of these does the most harm?',options:['Getting cross','Saying what bugs you','Rolling your eyes at her'],answer:2,reveal:'Being cross is normal. Saying what bugs you is fair. But rolling your eyes says: *you are stupid, and I am better than you.* That is a different thing.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Most fights have anger and moaning in them, and that is normal. Making fun of someone, or acting like you are above them, is a different and much worse thing.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why is it worse?',text:'If you say *you left your shoes on the stairs*, she can answer. She can move them or say sorry. But if you sneer and say *wow, so clever*, there is nothing to answer. She can only give in or fight back harder.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Lots of small dents, and one hole that goes right through. Most rows leave dents. Making fun of someone burns a hole.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How to spot it',text:'Look out for eye-rolling, making fun, calling names, and the slow, bored voice people use to talk down to someone. Often it is the tone, not the words.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you argue with someone at home, listen to your own voice. If it sounds like you are talking down to them, stop and say what is really bugging you.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Which of these is the kind of thing this lesson warns about?',options:['\u201cOh well done, genius,\u201d with a big sigh','\u201cI\u2019m cross that you took my pen\u201d','\u201cPlease stop kicking my chair\u201d'],answer:0,why:'The other two say what is wrong, and can be answered. \u201cWell done, genius\u201d puts the other person down.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Contempt is just criticism turned up loud.',reality:'It is a different kind of thing. Criticism complains about something you did. Contempt speaks from above you. In this research it was the behaviour most consistently linked to poor outcomes.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Anger, complaint and even defensiveness are common in stable couples. Contempt works differently. It is worth watching for as its own thing, not as strong criticism.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'A complaint',items:['About something you did','Can be answered','Reply: explain, fix, apologise']},right:{title:'Contempt',items:['About your standing','Cannot really be answered','Reply: submit, or escalate']},note:'That is why it is different: it leaves only two moves, and both make things worse.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What it looks like',text:'Sarcasm, mockery, name-calling, eye-rolling, and the tone people use to explain something to someone they assume is slow. The words can be perfectly polite. The tone carries the message.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The easiest one to catch',text:'From the outside, contempt is the easiest of these behaviours to see. That also makes it the easiest to find in yourself, if you are willing to look. Listen to how you sound, not what you said.'},{kind:'try',text:'In your next disagreement, notice whether you sigh, roll your eyes or slow your speech. Count those, rather than counting how often you argue.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Which line from an argument about money is contempt rather than complaint?',options:['\u201cLet me explain this slowly, since budgets seem to confuse you.\u201d','\u201cI\u2019m worried, because we went over budget again this month.\u201d','\u201cYou spent a lot on the car, and I wish we\u2019d talked first.\u201d','\u201cI\u2019m angry. We agreed on a limit, and it wasn\u2019t kept.\u201d'],answer:0,why:'The others name a behaviour or a feeling and can be answered. *Let me explain this slowly* speaks from above, about the person\u2019s ability.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'THE CATEGORY',word:'Contempt',meaning:'Behaviour that communicates superiority rather than a complaint: sarcasm, mockery, name-calling, eye-rolling, the tone used for someone assumed to be slow.'},{kind:'idea',text:'In this research, contempt was the behaviour most strongly associated with relationships ending. It should be treated as a separate category, not as an extreme form of criticism.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the observations show',text:'The essay\u2019s starting point is that the obvious negatives are common in stable couples. Anger, complaint and defensiveness appear throughout relationships that last. Contempt is the one that behaves differently in the data.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it cannot be answered',text:'A criticism is a claim about conduct, and conduct can be explained, fixed or apologised for. Contempt makes a claim about the other person\u2019s standing. There is no answer to that on its own terms. The available responses are submission or escalation.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Ordinary dents and scuffs, and one place where something corrosive has eaten through. The essay\u2019s point is that this mark is a different kind of damage.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The finding is an association, mostly from one research group, and the essay\u2019s last idea warns against reading its numbers too precisely. Teasing that both people enjoy is not contempt; context and tone decide. And labelling a partner\u2019s every complaint as contempt is a way of dodging it.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of your last argument. Was there a moment where the tone, rather than the words, carried a message about who was above whom?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'\u201cContempt is just honesty without the politeness.\u201d What is the strongest reply from the essay\u2019s argument?',options:['Honesty makes a claim the other can answer; contempt makes one they can only submit to or fight','Honesty is always kinder than contempt, so the two can never be confused in practice','Contempt is less honest, because people who feel it tend to exaggerate their complaints','Contempt is fine in private, so long as it is never expressed in front of other people'],answer:0,why:'The essay\u2019s distinction is structural: a complaint about conduct can be answered, a claim about standing cannot.'}]}},'how-couples-actually-argue_3':{pictureCaption:'A rock too heavy to move, and paths laid round it instead of through. That is how couples who do well handle a lasting difference.',bridge:'Ideas 1 and 2 were about how one argument goes. This one is about the argument you keep having.',basics:{title:'Some rows come back forever, and that is fine',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'A ROCK IN THE GARDEN',picture:!0,text:'Picture a huge rock in the middle of a garden. It is far too heavy to move. So the paths go round it. Nobody trips on it any more.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE SHARED BEDROOM',text:'You like your room tidy. Your sister likes it messy. You share. Every week you have the same row about socks on the floor. It never really ends.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Lots of rows between people who spend years together never get solved, because they come from real differences. The aim is not to win. It is to have the row without it getting bigger each time.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'A PATH ROUND THE ROCK',text:'You agree: your half can be messy, my half stays tidy. The socks still annoy you sometimes. But now it is a small moan, not a big fight. You did not change her. You made the row cheaper.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of a row you keep having. Instead of trying to win it, ask: how could we have this one more gently next time?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You and your friend always argue about which game to play. What does this idea suggest?',options:['Take turns choosing, so the row stays small','Keep arguing until one of you finally wins','Stop being friends, since you can\u2019t agree'],answer:0,why:'You like different games, and that may not change. Taking turns is a path round the rock.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'FIVE YEARS, ONE ARGUMENT',text:'Every party ends the same way. One of you wants to leave at ten. The other is just getting going. The drive home is the same argument it was five years ago, and it has got a little more bitter each time.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A large share of conflicts in long relationships are perpetual. The couples who do well are mostly not the ones who resolved them, but the ones who found a way to have the same argument without it escalating.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What they are about',text:'The essay lists the usual ones: differences in tempo, sociability, tidiness, money. These are not problems with a solution. They are two people being differently built, and that will still be true next year.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Aim: settle it',items:['Each row is a battle to win','Every recurrence feels like failure','Cost rises each time']},right:{title:'Aim: cheaper version',items:['Each row is a known quantity','Recurrence is expected','Cost stays low']},note:'If a disagreement has recurred for five years, the realistic aim is not a settlement.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE PARTY, REVISED',text:'They stop trying to agree on the right time to leave. They take two cars, or agree the leaver goes home first. The difference is still there. The drive-home argument is not.'},{kind:'try',text:'Name one argument you have had more than five times. Write down what a less costly version of it would look like, rather than what winning would look like.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'One partner is a saver and the other a spender. They have argued about it for years. What does the idea recommend?',options:['Accept the difference and design a version of the argument that costs less','Keep discussing it until one partner sees why the other is right','Treat years of arguing as a sign the relationship is not working','Avoid the topic entirely, since it can never be resolved'],answer:0,why:'The difference is likely permanent. The aim is to live alongside it, which is neither winning nor avoiding.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Couples who do well are the ones who have resolved their big differences.',reality:'The essay reports that they mostly are not. Many of their conflicts are never resolved. What distinguishes them is that the same argument recurs without escalating.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Many recurring arguments in long relationships are perpetual, and that is normal. The realistic goal is not settlement but a version of the conversation that costs less.'},{kind:'term',word:'Perpetual conflict',meaning:'A disagreement rooted in a lasting difference between two people, such as tempo, sociability, tidiness or money, which recurs because the difference does not go away.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why this reframes the goal',text:'If you believe every disagreement has a solution, a recurring one is evidence of failure, and each round carries the frustration of the last. Accept that it is perpetual, and the same round becomes routine. The work moves from solving to lowering the cost.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'WHERE TO AIM',low:'Each round escalates on the last',high:'The same round, at low cost',note:'Settlement is not on this line. For a perpetual difference, the useful movement is along it.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Not every recurring argument is perpetual. Some are solvable, and calling them permanent is avoidance. Some recurring conflicts are about harm or control, which no one should live alongside. And some differences, such as whether to have children, may not leave room for paths round them.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which of your recurring disagreements is a lasting difference, and which is a problem you have been calling permanent to avoid it?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SORT THE CASES',prompt:'Which recurring argument is the essay\u2019s idea least suited to?',options:['One partner keeps checking the other\u2019s phone and limiting who they see','One partner likes a busy social diary and the other prefers quiet evenings','One partner leaves dishes overnight and the other wants them done at once','One partner runs ten minutes late and the other is always early'],answer:0,why:'The others are differences in tempo, sociability or tidiness. Control is not a difference to live alongside.'}]}},'how-couples-actually-argue_4':{pictureCaption:'A mug pushed across, slopped over the rim. Clumsy, but a peace offer, and it works only if the other hand takes it.',bridge:'Idea 3 said the argument may never be settled. This is what stops it escalating: repair, made and taken mid-argument.',basics:{title:'A clumsy sorry still counts',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'IN THE MIDDLE OF A ROW',text:'You and your friend are arguing over a board game. It is getting loud. Then your friend pulls a silly face. It is not very funny. You could laugh. Or you could say, *that\u2019s not funny.*'},{kind:'idea',text:'In a row, small moves to calm things down really help: a joke, a hug, a step back. It does not matter if they are clumsy. What matters is that the other person takes them.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A PASS IN FOOTBALL',text:'A wobbly pass still works if your teammate catches it. A perfect pass is wasted if they just let it go by. A peace move in a row is the same. It works when it is caught.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'A WOBBLY OFFER',caption:'One hand pushes a mug across the table. It spills a bit. Not a neat move, but it is a peace move. Now it is up to the other hand to take it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it is good news',text:'You do not have to be clever or calm or right. You just have to try. And when someone tries with you, you have to let them. Saying *that was a rubbish joke* keeps the row going.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time someone makes a silly joke in the middle of a row, laugh, even a little. See what happens to the row.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Mid-argument, your brother says, *Okay, I may have been a bit of a nightmare.* What is best?',options:['Take it, and calm down a bit too','Say he was a big nightmare, not a bit','Wait until he says sorry properly'],answer:0,why:'It was clumsy, but it was a peace move. Taking it is what makes it work.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHAT PEOPLE AIM FOR',left:{title:'What most assume matters',items:['Fewer arguments','Calm, well-chosen words','Getting the apology right']},right:{title:'What the observation suggests',items:['Repair during arguments','Any move to de-escalate','The other person taking it']},note:'The essay says this inverts what most people assume they should be optimising.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The observable difference is rarely fewer arguments. It is what happens mid-argument: a joke, a hand, a step back. These moves are often graceless, and whether they are taken matters far more than how well they were made.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What a repair looks like',text:'Humour, a touch, a change of subject, an acknowledgement such as *fair point*. A bad joke in the middle of a row can do the same work as a good one, provided the other person takes it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why this removes the excuse',text:'You do not have to be articulate, calm or right. You have to make a move, and take one when it is made at you. Refusing a clumsy repair because it was clumsy is itself an escalation.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'SLOPPED, BUT OFFERED',caption:'A mug pushed across the table mid-row, slopped over the rim. A graceless repair. The other hand is still flat on the wood, and what it does next decides whether the move works.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE AWKWARD JOKE',text:'Twenty minutes into a row about the holiday, your partner says, *Shall we just go to the moon instead?* It is not funny. You feel the urge to say so. That urge is the moment this idea is about.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time someone makes an awkward joke mid-argument, take it. Refusing it because it was awkward is a decision to keep arguing.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'In a heated row, your partner reaches over and touches your arm without saying anything. What does the idea suggest?',options:['Accept it as a repair, even though the issue is unresolved','Pull away, since a touch is not a real apology','Ask them to put what they mean into proper words','Ignore it and finish making your point first'],answer:0,why:'A touch is a repair attempt. Its reception matters more than its polish, and taking it does not mean dropping the issue.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'BEFORE THE FINDING',prompt:'In observed arguments, which mattered more for a repair attempt?',options:['How skilful it was','Whether it was taken'],answer:1,reveal:'The observational work suggests polish matters much less than reception. A clumsy repair that is accepted does the work; an elegant one that is refused does not.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The presence of repair appears to matter more than the absence of conflict. Repair attempts work mainly through their reception rather than their quality, so clumsy ones still count.'},{kind:'term',word:'Repair attempt',meaning:'Any move to de-escalate during a conflict: humour, a touch, a change of subject, an acknowledgement. Defined by what it tries to do, not by how well it does it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What gets inverted',text:'Most people try to optimise for fewer arguments. The essay suggests the more useful target is what happens inside them. Two couples can argue equally often; the difference can lie in whether de-escalating moves are made and accepted.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'TWO WAYS A REPAIR GOES',nodes:['A clumsy joke mid-row','Taken: the temperature drops','Refused for being clumsy: a new grievance'],note:'Refusal is not neutral. The essay counts it as an escalation of its own.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'RECEPTION, NOT POLISH',caption:'A slopped mug pushed across the table: a repair attempt, defined by what it tries to do, not by how neatly it is done. Whether it works rests with the flat hand opposite.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The essay itself says to treat this at the strength the evidence supports: a pattern seen in observed conversations, not a formula. A joke can also be a way of brushing off a serious hurt, and nobody is obliged to accept a repair that dismisses one. Taking a repair should pause the escalation, not bury the issue.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Repair matters more than having fewer arguments.','Reception counts for more than polish.','Refusing a clumsy repair is itself an escalation.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these is a misuse of the idea?',options:['Insisting your partner laugh at a joke made about something that deeply hurt them','Accepting an awkward hand on your shoulder in the middle of a row','Answering an ill-timed joke with a small smile and a softer tone','Offering a clumsy \u201cfair point\u201d even though you are still annoyed'],answer:0,why:'The idea asks you to take clumsy repairs. It does not turn a dismissive joke into a repair the other person owes you.'}]}},'how-couples-actually-argue_5':{pictureCaption:'Every dart sits dead centre, because the rings were painted round each one after it landed. Checked against its own throws, any score looks perfect.',bridge:'Ideas 1 to 4 came from watching couples argue. This one asks which part of that research held up, and which part did not.',basics:{title:'A score that looked too good',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'THE PRACTICE TEST',text:'Your teacher gives you the test questions a week early. You learn the answers. You get ten out of ten. Does that prove you know the subject? Not really. A new test would show the truth.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What happened with couples',text:'Scientists watched couples argue. They made rules to guess which couples would split up. Then they checked the rules on the same couples they made them from. So the score looked amazing.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Every dart is in the middle. That is because the rings were painted after the darts landed. A rule checked on its own cases looks perfect too.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A guessing rule usually looks better on the cases it was built from. The only fair test is on new cases it has never seen.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Tested on the same couples',items:['Like the practice test','Score looks very high']},right:{title:'Tested on new couples',items:['Like a surprise test','Score drops a lot']},note:'The famous number came from the left side. People remembered it anyway.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What still holds',text:'The big number was much too high. Other things the scientists saw hold up better. Starting a row gently seems to help. Putting people down seems to do harm. Peace moves seem to matter.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A new app says it can guess your exam grades. What should you ask first?',options:['Was it tested on students it had never seen?','How many people have downloaded it?','Do lots of famous people use it?'],answer:0,why:'Anything can look clever on the cases it was built from. New cases are the real test.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'A FIGURE THAT TRAVELLED',text:'For years a striking claim was widely repeated. From a short observed conversation, researchers could predict which couples would divorce, and be right over ninety per cent of the time. It is the part of this research most people remember. It is also the part that did not hold up.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The famous prediction figure was overstated. That is because the models were fitted and tested on the same couples. The findings about behaviour have held up better than the prediction claim. The two are worth keeping apart.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the same couples inflate the score',text:'A model built from a set of couples bends to fit their quirks as well as any real pattern. Test it on those same couples and the quirks look like insight. Check it against couples it was not built on, and the score falls a long way.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Rings painted round darts that had already landed, so every throw is a bullseye. Fit a model to some couples and test it on the same ones. Its score is drawn the same way.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Better supported',items:['Harsh openings escalate','Contempt predicts poor outcomes','Repair matters']},right:{title:'Did not hold',items:['The precision','The ninety per cent figure']},note:'The essay adds that much of the evidence for them comes from the same research group.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE SAME TRAP ELSEWHERE',text:'A firm says its hiring tool picks good staff with very high accuracy. Say that was measured on the staff it was trained on. Then the number tells you how well it fits its past. It does not tell you how it will do with the next person who applies.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you see an impressive prediction figure, look for one thing: was it tested on cases the model had never seen?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'What is the right conclusion from the overstated divorce-prediction figure?',options:['Keep the behavioural findings, and distrust the headline precision','Discard the whole body of research on couples in conflict','Trust the figure, since the behaviours behind it were real','Treat the figure as roughly right but slightly rounded up'],answer:0,why:'The essay separates the two. The patterns are better supported. The precision came from how the models were tested.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'If a research programme\u2019s famous number collapses, its findings collapse with it.',reality:'The essay argues for separating them. The prediction figure was overstated for methodological reasons. The behavioural observations about openings, contempt and repair are better supported, though not beyond question.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Widely repeated claims of predicting divorce from short observations, with figures above ninety per cent, came from models largely fitted and evaluated on the same samples. The precision was an artefact; the patterns are the useful part.'},{kind:'term',word:'Out-of-sample testing',meaning:'Checking a model against cases it was not built on. It is the test that shows whether the model learned a real pattern or just fitted the quirks of its data.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'In-sample accuracy, drawn plainly: the target is painted round darts already thrown, so every one scores. Only darts thrown at rings painted beforehand test the aim.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Three problems, not one',text:'The essay names the central one: fitting and evaluating on the same samples rather than testing on new couples, which inflates apparent accuracy. Sample sizes were also modest. And the base rate of divorce changes what any given accuracy figure means.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the base rate matters',text:'Take a made-up sample where most couples stay together. A model that simply predicts *stays together* for everyone scores well without knowing anything. A raw accuracy figure means little until you know how common the outcome was to begin with.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The correction can be overdone. Overfitting inflates a figure; it does not make the underlying behaviours imaginary. But the essay\u2019s own caution applies the other way too: much of the evidence for those behaviours comes from the same research group, so independent confirmation is what would settle them.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which statistic do you repeat without knowing how it was tested? What would you need to check?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A school says its test identifies pupils who will drop out with ninety per cent accuracy. Which question matters most?',options:['Was it checked on pupils it was not built from, and how rare is dropping out?','How many pupils took the test when the figure was first calculated?','Did the researchers who built the test have a strong reputation?','Has the test been used for long enough to become widely trusted?'],answer:0,why:'Out-of-sample testing and the base rate are the two things that decide what a headline accuracy figure means.'}]}}}},2973,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'how-to-disagree-well_1':{pictureCaption:'A model, rebuilt with care, slid back across the table for its owner to check. That is their view, handed back for their yes.',basics:{title:'Say their side back first',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'FILM NIGHT',text:'Your sister wants a scary film. You want a funny one. She says yours is babyish. You say hers is dumb. Ten minutes later you are both cross, and no film is on.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Before you argue back, say the other person\u2019s side in your own words. Keep going until they say, yes, that is what I mean. Only then give your side.'},{kind:'steps',title:'How to do it',steps:['Say: so you think\u2026 and finish it fairly.','Ask: have I got that right?','If not, try again.','When they say yes, give your side.']},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE ORDERING FOOD',text:'A good waiter reads your order back to you before taking it to the kitchen. That way the right food comes out. Saying someone\u2019s side back works the same way.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it works',text:'First, you stop fighting a side they never had. Second, they can see you were really listening. People who feel heard stop guarding and start thinking. Then they will listen to you too.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One person hands the other\u2019s model back, made with care. It is as if to say: is this yours? Did I get it right?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend says the new game rule is unfair. What should you do first?',options:['Say what you think she means, and ask if you have it right','Tell her why the rule is fair before she says more','Agree with her so that the two of you don\u2019t fall out'],answer:0,why:'First make sure you know her side. Saying it back shows you listened, and stops you arguing with the wrong thing.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A MEETING ABOUT A BUDGET CUT',prompt:'A colleague argues against your plan. What usually stops the two of you getting anywhere?',options:['You disagree too strongly','You are each answering a version the other does not hold','One of you lacks the facts'],answer:1,reveal:'Most arguments are conducted against a version of the other view that nobody actually holds. The exchange never reaches the real point of difference.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Before you respond, summarise the other position until its holder agrees you have it right. Then reply to that version, not to the one in your head.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHAT THE SUMMARY DOES',left:{title:'To the argument',items:['Finds the real point of difference','Stops you attacking a phantom']},right:{title:'To the other person',items:['Shows you were listening','Moves them from defending to assessing']},note:'One step, two effects. The second is the one people underrate.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why their posture changes',text:'Someone who has just heard their view stated fairly has nothing left to defend. They start weighing your reply instead. The piece argues that this weighing posture is the one in which people actually change their minds.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The cost',text:'You may find the position is stronger than you assumed. Having stated it well, you cannot un-know that. This is why the practice is rare, and why it matters.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A model rebuilt and slid back across the table for its owner to inspect. Nothing moves on until they say it is theirs.'},{kind:'try',text:'In your next disagreement, open with: *so your view is\u2026* Finish it, and do not reply until they say yes.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A colleague says your hiring plan is risky. You restate their objection and they say, not quite. What now?',options:['Try the summary again until they agree it is their view','Reply to your version, since it is close enough','Drop the summary, because it is clearly not working','Ask them to restate your plan before you go on'],answer:0,why:'The rule is to continue until they say yes. A near miss means you would still be answering a view they do not hold.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Restating the other side is a courtesy that slows a debate down.',reality:'The piece treats it as the step that makes the disagreement locatable at all. Without it, two people argue past each other at versions neither holds.'},{kind:'idea',text:'State the opposing position so well that its holder endorses your version. This finds the real difference and changes the other person from defending to assessing.'},{kind:'term',word:'Restatement test',meaning:'Stating a view so faithfully that the person who holds it would sign your version. Building the strongest version of a view, the steelman, is a different step: idea 4.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The test is their agreement',text:'The piece\u2019s check is not your own sense that you were fair. It is the other person saying yes. That makes the summary something they can audit, and gives them a reason to keep listening.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The model slid back across the table for its owner to check. The owner\u2019s yes is the test, not the builder\u2019s confidence.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The idea\u2019s title and its test pull apart. State a case better than its holder did, and they may not recognise it as theirs. Restating is also easy to fake, as a ritual before a reply that ignores it. And the piece\u2019s claim that assessing is the only state in which anyone changes their mind is a strong one; it is argued, not shown.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of a view you reject. Could you state it so that someone who holds it would sign your version?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE FAKE',prompt:'Which of these only looks like the practice?',options:['Summarising their view in a sentence, then replying without waiting for their answer','Restating their view twice because the first try missed a key condition','Discovering, while restating it, that their position is stronger than you thought','Refusing to reply until they confirm your summary matches their view'],answer:0,why:'The practice is defined by the other person\u2019s agreement. A summary they never get to confirm is a ritual, not a check.'}]}},'how-to-disagree-well_2':{pictureCaption:'Three ropes in one tangle. Two have been set aside, and the hand holds up the single knot that is really left.',bridge:'Idea 1 made sure you know their view. This one asks what kind of disagreement you are actually having.',basics:{title:'What are we really arguing about?',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'IS PLUTO A PLANET?',text:'Two friends argue about Pluto. One says it is a planet. One says it is not. They both know how big Pluto is and where it goes. So what are they fighting about? Only the word *planet*.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Arguments come in three kinds. Some are about what is true. Some are about what matters most. Some are about what a word means. Work out which kind you are in.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'THREE KINDS',left:{title:'The kind',items:['What is true','What matters most','What a word means']},right:{title:'How to sort it',items:['Look it up or check','Each say why you care','Agree what you both mean']}},{kind:'picture',caption:'Three ropes got tangled up. Pull two away, and only one knot is left. That knot is the real argument.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why sorting helps',text:'Often two of the three kinds were never a problem. Once you check the fact and agree on the word, the fight is much smaller. What is left is usually about what each of you cares about.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you argue, stop and ask: is this about what is true, what matters, or what a word means?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Two friends argue: is a hot dog a sandwich? Both agree on what is in a hot dog. Which kind of argument is it?',options:['About what a word means','About what is true','About what matters most'],answer:0,why:'They agree on the facts. They just mean different things by *sandwich*.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THREE PEOPLE, ONE MEETING',text:'A team is arguing about whether remote work is productive. One person means output per hour, and has the numbers. Another means whether junior staff learn as fast. A third simply prefers the office and grabs whichever argument is nearest. Forty minutes in, nobody has moved.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Most arguments mix disputes about facts, values and definitions, and are run as if they were one kind. Separate them, and two of the three often turn out not to be in dispute.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'HOW EACH KIND ENDS',left:{title:'The dispute',items:['Facts: what is true','Values: what matters','Definitions: what a word means']},right:{title:'What settles it',items:['Evidence','Not evidence: an honest trade-off','Agreeing the meaning, then it is gone']}},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the meeting stalls',text:'Treat a values dispute as factual, and you get an endless exchange of studies that persuade nobody. Treat a definitional one as real, and two people disagree until someone notices they are using one word for two things.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The tangle on the table was three ropes. Two came away cleanly. What is left in the hand is the knot worth working on.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before your next argument, sort it out loud: are we disagreeing about what is true, what matters, or what a word means?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Two managers argue about whether a new policy is fair. Both accept the same figures on who gains and who loses. What is the likely dispute?',options:['Values: they weigh the same gains and losses differently','Facts: one of them must have misread the figures','Nothing real: it will dissolve once they recheck the data','Evidence: they need a study on how fair the policy is'],answer:0,why:'With the facts agreed, what remains is how much each outcome matters. More data will not settle that, but naming it lets them argue it honestly.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE MEETING, SORTED',text:'First, the word *productive* is doing two jobs, which is a definitional split. Output per hour is a factual claim: the numbers can settle it. Whether juniors learn as fast is factual too, but the numbers on the table do not measure it. And a plain preference for the office is a value, dressed up in whichever argument is nearest.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Facts, values and definitions are settled in different ways. Run them as one dispute and none can be won. Sort them first, and what usually remains is a smaller, real disagreement about values.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'SORT BEFORE YOU ARGUE',nodes:['One tangled argument','Definitions agreed: they vanish','Facts checked: many settle','A values difference remains'],note:'The last node is not a failure. It is the disagreement you can now have honestly.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the piece is against',text:'The piece starts from the claim that most disagreements fail before the substance is reached, because people are answering different questions without noticing. Sorting is its cure for that: it makes sure both people are in the same conversation before either tries to win it.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The three kinds are not always cleanly separable. What counts as good evidence can itself depend on values, and some definitions carry real consequences, such as who qualifies for a benefit. The sort can also be misused: calling a dispute just semantics to wave away a point that matters. Sorting shrinks a disagreement. It does not guarantee the residue is small.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Recall an argument that went round in circles. Which of the three kinds was each person actually in?'},{kind:'recap',points:['Facts are settled by evidence; values are not.','Definitional disputes vanish once the meaning is agreed.','What is left is usually a real values difference.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which response misuses the sorting move?',options:['Dismissing a dispute over who counts as a carer as only about words','Noting that two people mean different things by productive','Asking for evidence when the dispute is about what actually happened','Saying plainly that a remaining difference is about values'],answer:0,why:'Some definitions decide real outcomes, like who gets support. Calling that semantic hides a dispute that matters rather than dissolving one that does not.'}]}},'how-to-disagree-well_3':{pictureCaption:'Is the shelf straight? Rather than argue, the hands bring a spirit level: the check that would settle it, named before the argument.',bridge:'Idea 2 found the factual part of a disagreement. This idea is the test for whether you really hold your side as a fact.',basics:{title:'What would change your mind?',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TWO FRIENDS, ONE ARGUMENT',prompt:'Two friends argue about when the shop shuts. Which one is really thinking?',options:['The one who says it louder each time','The one who says what would prove her wrong'],answer:1,reveal:'Ella says: I think it shuts at 5. If the sign says 6, I\u2019m wrong. Now there is a way to find out. Shouting louder gives no way at all.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Before you argue, say what would change your mind. It shows you are thinking, not just trying to win. It also checks that you could ever be wrong.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO HOLD A VIEW',left:{title:'Thinking',items:['I could be wrong','Here is what would show it','Let\u2019s check']},right:{title:'Just defending',items:['I am right','Nothing could change it','Let\u2019s keep arguing']}},{kind:'picture',kicker:'GET THE LEVEL',caption:'Is the shelf straight? No need to shout about it. Hold the level up to it. The bubble shows who is right.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What if nothing would change it?',text:'Sometimes you ask yourself and find no answer. That tells you something. Maybe it is not really about facts. Maybe it is just what you like, like a best colour. That is fine, but it is not a fact.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick something you are sure of. Finish this line: *I would change my mind if I saw\u2026*'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Leo says his team is the best. You ask what would change his mind. He says nothing ever could. What does that tell you?',options:['It is more about what he likes than about facts','He must be right, because he is so sure','You should find more facts to show him'],answer:0,why:'If nothing could change it, it is not a claim that facts can test. It is more like a choice, such as a favourite.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Saying what would change your mind weakens your position before the argument starts.',reality:'It is the strongest signal there is that you are reasoning rather than defending. And it hands the other person something concrete to meet instead of something to attack.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Name, in advance, the evidence that would move you. It turns your position into a hypothesis. And if no such evidence exists, you have learned something uncomfortable about the view.'},{kind:'explain',title:'A test for you first',text:'The question is a test for you before it is a way to persuade. If nothing could change your mind, you are not holding a factual belief. You are holding an identity or a preference. And the argument you are about to have is not the one you think.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHAT A STATED CONDITION DOES',nodes:['You name what would move you','They have a target to meet','Effort turns from attack to evidence'],note:'You change the other person\u2019s incentives as well as your own.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'THE CHECK, NAMED FIRST',caption:'Whether the shelf is straight will not be settled by who argues longer. The hands bring the level: a check either side could lose, chosen before the argument.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Ask it early',text:'The honest answer is often embarrassing. Many strongly held views turn out to have no condition at all. Finding that out mid-argument is hard to take. That is why the piece says to ask before the disagreement, not during it.'},{kind:'try',text:'Choose a view you will defend this week. Answer, out loud: what would I have to see to change my mind here?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You insist a supplier is unreliable. Which statement best applies the idea?',options:['If their on-time rate this quarter is above ninety per cent, I am wrong','I have dealt with them for years, so I simply know they are unreliable','Show me any evidence you like and I will consider it','Everyone in the team agrees with me, so the point is settled'],answer:0,why:'Only the ninety per cent test names a specific condition the other side could meet. An open offer to consider anything commits you to nothing.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'POSITION INTO HYPOTHESIS',text:'The piece\u2019s claim is that naming a disconfirming condition changes what kind of thing your view is. A position is something you defend. A hypothesis is something that could be found wrong, and saying how is what makes it one.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Stating what would change your mind is the clearest sign that you are reasoning, not defending. Its first use is on yourself: a view with no possible disconfirming evidence is not a factual belief.'},{kind:'term',word:'Disconfirming evidence',meaning:'Evidence that would count against a claim if it turned up. A factual claim always has some, at least in principle. If you cannot name any, the claim may not be factual.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'HOW A VIEW IS HELD',low:'No condition could move it: identity or preference',high:'A named, meetable condition: a hypothesis',note:'Many strongly held views turn out to sit further left than their holders assumed.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'A QUESTION A BUBBLE CAN ANSWER',caption:'A spirit level brought to a shelf. The dispute has been turned into one the bubble can settle, which is what a named condition does to a view.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The effect on the other side',text:'A stated condition is something the other party can meet. That redirects their effort from attacking you toward finding evidence, which is the part of an argument that can actually settle anything.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Not every view should have a disconfirming condition. Values, like the wrongness of cruelty, are not hypotheses, and holding them firmly is not a failure, only a different kind of disagreement. The condition can also be gamed: set impossibly high, it signals openness while ruling out change. And evidence that meets a condition can still be cherry-picked.'},{kind:'recap',points:['A named condition turns a position into a hypothesis.','No condition means it is not a factual belief.','Ask yourself before the argument, not during it.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE GAME',prompt:'Which condition only pretends to apply the idea?',options:['I will change my mind if every study ever done agrees I am wrong','I will change my mind if next month\u2019s sales fall below last year\u2019s','I will change my mind if the trial group recovers faster than the control group','I will change my mind if the bridge survey finds no cracking'],answer:0,why:'A condition that can never be met looks open but rules out change. The others name evidence that could realistically turn up.'}]}},'how-to-disagree-well_4':{pictureCaption:'The straw dummy by the wall is untouched. The walker is heading for the full suit of armour, the version worth testing yourself against.',bridge:'Idea 1 had you restate their view fairly. This one goes further: find the best version of it, even if they did not make it.',basics:{title:'Take on the best version',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A FOOTBALL MATCH',text:'Say you beat the weakest player on the other team. Does that show your team is better? No. To know that, you have to play their best players.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Every side of an argument has a weak version that is easy to beat. Beating it proves nothing. Find the best version of the other side, and answer that.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The straw dummy is easy to knock over, so the walker leaves it. He goes to the armour. That is the real test.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'SHOULD SCHOOL START LATER?',text:'One kid says yes, because school is boring. That is easy to beat. Another says yes, because tired kids find it hard to learn. That is much harder to answer. If you want to argue no, you must answer the second one.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where to find the best version',text:'Not from the loudest person who thinks it. From the one who has thought about it the most. Loud people are good at being heard. That is not the same as being right.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick a view you disagree with. Ask yourself: what is the best reason someone could have for it?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your class argues about having pets in school. You think no. Which reason for yes should you answer?',options:['Looking after a pet can teach kids to be kind and careful','Pets are cute and everyone likes looking at them','My friend\u2019s cousin had a class pet and loved it'],answer:0,why:'Teaching kids to be kind and careful is the strongest reason for yes. If you can answer it, you have really made your case.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE EASY TARGET',text:'You oppose a four-day working week. Online, its most visible champion says it would make everyone happier. You could take that apart in a paragraph. Now suppose an economist argues it would cut sick leave and help keep staff. That is harder. Only one of those replies would tell you anything.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Attacking the strongest version of an argument is the only attack that settles anything. Beating a weak version changes nobody\u2019s mind and uses up the conversation.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the weak win is empty',text:'The position was never its weak version. So beating that version says nothing about the position. And the people most likely to be won over by the rebuttal are the ones who already agreed with you.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHOSE VERSION TO ANSWER',left:{title:'Most visible advocate',items:['Chosen for reach','Easy to rebut','Rebuttal persuades your side']},right:{title:'Most capable advocate',items:['Chosen for rigour','Hard to rebut','Rebuttal settles something']}},{kind:'picture',caption:'The straw dummy stands untouched by the wall. The walker passes it for the armour, the only opponent that would test him.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before you answer an argument, find its most capable advocate, not its loudest. Answer that version. If you cannot find one, you are not ready to reply.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You want to argue against a new city cycling scheme. Which preparation fits the idea?',options:['Read the transport planner\u2019s case for it and answer her best points','Collect the angriest online posts in favour and rebut them','Answer the scheme\u2019s slogan, since that is what most people saw','Ask your friends who oppose it for their strongest objections'],answer:0,why:'The planner is the most capable advocate. Rebutting slogans or angry posts would only stage a rebuttal.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'THE PIECE\u2019S TWO EXAMPLES',text:'The piece names Darwin and Plato. In *On the Origin of Species*, Darwin set aside a chapter for the difficulties facing his own theory. In Plato\u2019s *Republic*, Thrasymachus\u2019 case that justice serves the stronger is restated more forcefully by Glaucon before Socrates answers it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Only the strongest version of a view is worth engaging. Beating a weaker one is the difference between having refuted a view and having performed a refutation.'},{kind:'term',word:'Straw man',meaning:'A weak version of an opposing view, built to be knocked down. Beating it looks like winning the argument, but the real position is untouched.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Reach is not rigour',text:'The piece makes a point about selection. The visible advocates of any position are chosen, by audiences and platforms, for reach. The best case for it is usually held by someone less visible and more careful, and finding them is the expensive part.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How it differs from idea 1',text:'Idea 1 asked you to restate the view the other person holds, until they agree. This asks for more: the best version of the view, wherever it is held, even if the person in front of you never made it.'},{kind:'limits',text:'It is slow, and sometimes there is no time to find the best advocate. Answering only the strongest version can also miss the audience you face, who may hold the weak one and are owed an answer to it. And for views with no capable advocate at all, insisting on the best version can lend them a respectability they have not earned.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'For a view you reject, who is its most capable advocate? Have you read them, or only its loudest?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'WHICH IS PERFORMANCE?',prompt:'Which of these is performing a refutation rather than making one?',options:['Rebutting a viral clip of a view to an audience that already disagrees with it','Answering the most careful published defence of a view before replying','Spending a chapter on the hardest objections to your own theory','Restating an opponent\u2019s case more strongly before answering it'],answer:0,why:'Beating a viral, weak version for people already on your side establishes nothing about the view. The others engage its strongest form.'}]}},'how-to-disagree-well_5':{pictureCaption:'All four hands rest flat. The thing between them has been turned over from both sides, and now it is simply being left there.',bridge:'Ideas 1 to 4 make a disagreement as clear as it can be. This one is about what to do when it is clear and still there.',basics:{title:'Know when to stop',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE LAST WORD',belief:'If you stop arguing first, you lost.',reality:'Sometimes you both get each other and still think different things. Going on just turns it into a fight about who wins. Stopping then is a skill.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When you both understand each other and still disagree, stop. Say what you agree on and what you don\u2019t. Then leave it there.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Signs it is time to stop',steps:['You keep saying the same things.','Nobody is adding anything new.','Someone is just trying not to give in.']},{kind:'picture',caption:'Hands flat and calm. The thing in the middle has been looked at from both sides. Now they just leave it there.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why stopping helps',text:'Each time you say it again, it gets harder for the other person to change their mind in front of you. If you stop nicely, they can think it over later, on their own. That is often when people change their minds.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time an argument goes round in circles, say: I think we get each other and still disagree. Shall we leave it there?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You and a friend have argued about the best book for an hour. You keep saying the same things. What should you do?',options:['Say what you each think, then leave it','Keep going until one of you gives in','Walk off without saying anything else'],answer:0,why:'Saying where you each stand, then leaving it, ends things nicely. It keeps things friendly, and leaves room to think it over later.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE THIRD TIME ROUND',text:'You and a colleague are on the same point for the third time. Each of you can now recite the other\u2019s case. Neither is adding anything. Both of you are still going, and the tone has started to sharpen.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Some disagreements are stable: both sides understand each other and still differ. Past that point, carrying on turns an exchange into a contest about status. Naming the difference and stopping is a skill, not a concession.'},{kind:'steps',title:'The signals',steps:['The exchange has begun repeating.','Positions are restated, not developed.','Someone is arguing to avoid conceding, not to establish anything.'],note:'Any one is a warning. Together they mean the useful part is over.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why pushing backfires',text:'Every repetition raises the cost of changing position in public. So the final push produces entrenchment, not resolution. The piece argues that much real changing of minds happens later and in private, and a final push makes that less likely.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Four hands at rest, and the object between them left where it lies. Both sides have turned it over. Nobody is grabbing.'},{kind:'try',text:'When you see the signals, say: I think we understand each other and still disagree. Shall we leave it there? Then sum up what was settled.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'An argument with your partner about a holiday has gone round three times. Which ending fits the idea?',options:['Say what you agree on and what is still open, and pause it','Keep going, since one more round might finally settle it','Give in, so that the argument is over and you can move on','Leave the room once it gets heated, without saying anything'],answer:0,why:'An explicit ending names what was settled and leaves room to revise later. Giving in or walking out ends it without that.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'WHERE MINDS CHANGE',prompt:'According to the piece, where does a lot of genuine changing of minds happen?',options:['In the heat of the argument','Later, in private','When one side runs out of points'],answer:1,reveal:'The piece\u2019s claim is that much updating happens afterwards, alone. A disagreement ended well protects that. One ended by a final push or by someone storming off works against it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Once a disagreement is stable, continuing produces entrenchment, not resolution. Name what was established and what remains open, and stop on purpose.'},{kind:'term',word:'Stable disagreement',meaning:'A disagreement in which both sides understand each other\u2019s position accurately and still differ. More argument cannot clarify it further.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE ENTRENCHMENT LOOP',nodes:['A point is restated','Conceding gets more public','Each side digs in','The point is restated again'],loop:!0,note:'Every lap raises the price of changing position, which is why the loop rarely breaks itself.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Ending, not abandoning',text:'The piece separates an explicit ending from stopping by exhaustion or by someone walking out. The explicit one records what was settled, so the work is not lost, and leaves the other person free to revise without having to lose in front of you.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Some disagreements cannot be left: a team still has to choose, and agreeing to differ can quietly hand the decision to whoever is more stubborn. Stopping can also be a dodge, used to escape a point you are losing. And the claim that much updating happens in private is the piece\u2019s judgement; it gives no evidence for it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'WHEN STOPPING FAILS',prompt:'A committee has a stable disagreement on which of two sites to build on, and must decide this week. What does the idea offer?',options:['Stop the debate, record the settled points, and decide by an agreed rule','Agree to differ, and leave the site question open indefinitely','Keep arguing, since a decision is needed and someone must concede','Let whoever argued longest choose, since they care the most'],answer:0,why:'Stopping ends the argument, not the decision. Naming what was established clears the way to settle the choice by a fair procedure rather than by endurance.'}]}}}},2974,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'how-to-live-on-24-hours_1':{pictureCaption:'Yesterday\u2019s bottles go back unused. Today\u2019s identical delivery is already on the step. Nothing carries over, in either direction.',basics:{title:'A fresh day, every day',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A LAZY SATURDAY',prompt:'You spent all of Saturday doing nothing much. How many hours do you get on Sunday?',options:['Fewer, as a punishment','The same 24 as always','More, to make up for it'],answer:1,reveal:'Sunday comes with 24 hours, like every day. Wasting Saturday did not cost you any of them. It did not earn you extra ones either.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Everyone gets the same 24 hours each day. You can\u2019t save them up, and you can\u2019t borrow more. So the hours that count are today\u2019s.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'MONEY AND TIME',left:{title:'Pocket money',items:['You can save it','You can borrow some','Some kids get more']},right:{title:'Time',items:['You can\u2019t save it','You can\u2019t borrow any','Everyone gets 24 hours']},note:'Time is the one thing that is shared out the same for all.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Long ago, milk was left at the door each day. Yesterday\u2019s bottles go back unused. Today\u2019s new ones are already waiting.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Who said this?',text:'Arnold Bennett was an English writer. In 1908 he wrote a short book about time. He saw that rich and poor people get the same 24 hours. That is why he thought time was worth taking seriously.'},{kind:'try',text:'Had a lazy day yesterday? Don\u2019t try to make up for it. Pick one good thing to do with today\u2019s hours, and do it.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Sam wasted the first week of the holidays. What does this idea say?',options:['Use today well. Last week is gone either way.','The holidays are ruined, so why bother now?','Work twice as hard to win back the lost week.'],answer:0,why:'The lost week can\u2019t be won back, and it can\u2019t be taken from today. Today is the part Sam can still use.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'MONDAY, AFTER A LOST WEEKEND',text:'You meant to do something with the weekend. You did not. On Monday morning the temptation is to write off the week as well, and start properly next Saturday.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Every day arrives with the same twenty-four hours, whatever you did with the last lot. Time cannot be saved, borrowed or lost in advance, so the only useful question is what today\u2019s hours are for.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'THE ASYMMETRY',left:{title:'Money',items:['Can run short','Can be borrowed','Can be saved','Shared out unequally']},right:{title:'Time',items:['Same amount every morning','Cannot be borrowed','Cannot be saved','Identical for everyone']},note:'Bennett\u2019s point: time is the one possession everyone is given in the same quantity.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it gets wasted',text:'Waste is never punished by shortage. You squander a Tuesday and Wednesday still arrives full. Because the supply never runs low in the moment, nothing feels urgent. The cost only shows up added together, years later, as the things you meant to do and did not.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Yesterday\u2019s bottles go back untouched. Today\u2019s identical delivery is already on the step. Nothing carried over, in either direction.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time a day goes badly, resist planning a heroic tomorrow to make up for it. Ask only what today\u2019s remaining hours are for.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You planned to study on three evenings last week and managed none. What does the idea suggest for this week?',options:['Plan this week on its own terms: last week\u2019s hours are gone, not owed','Study six evenings this week to make up the missing three','Wait for a quieter week so you can start with a clean record','Accept that evenings are simply not workable for you'],answer:0,why:'Hours do not carry over in either direction. Missed time is not a debt to repay, only a week that is finished.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'LONDON, 1908',text:'Arnold Bennett, a prolific English novelist and journalist, published a short and rather impatient book for the office worker who felt he never had time. It opens not with a method but with an inventory: each morning you wake with twenty-four hours in your possession.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Time is the one resource supplied to everyone in identical quantity, and it cannot be stored. Treat the day as a budget to allocate, not a flow to endure, and start from today rather than from yesterday\u2019s deficit.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The asymmetry with money',text:'Money can be short, borrowed, saved or inherited. None of that applies to time. The supply is fixed, it cannot be accumulated, and waste is not punished by shortage: tomorrow brings the same amount again. Bennett treats this as the one genuinely equal thing in an unequal society.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHY IT IS SQUANDERED',nodes:['The supply never runs short today','So no urgency is felt','The hours drift','The loss shows only over years'],note:'A shortage that appears only in aggregate is one nobody responds to in the moment.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the frame changes',text:'The rest of the essay depends on this. A budget is allocated in advance, line by line. A flow is merely got through. Once the day is a budget, the question stops being how to get through the evening and becomes what the evening is for.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Yesterday\u2019s bottles leave untouched; an identical set already waits. The supply is renewed whether or not the last was used.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The equality holds only on the clock. Someone with long shifts, children or a relative to care for gets twenty-four hours but far fewer to allocate, and Bennett\u2019s reader had regular office hours and evenings largely his own. Read harshly, equal hours make every shortfall look like a personal failing. Often it is not.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'A critic says \u201ceveryone has the same 24 hours\u201d is a comfortable line for people with free evenings. What is the fairest reply?',options:['The clock is equal but free hours are not; budgeting still applies to the hours truly yours','It is no line at all: careful budgeting finds the same free hours for anyone','The critic is right, so the idea has nothing to offer people short of time','Bennett meant it only as a figure of speech, not as a claim about time'],answer:0,why:'Bennett\u2019s equality is literal only on the clock. The useful part, allocating rather than enduring, works on however many hours you actually control.'}]}},'how-to-live-on-24-hours_2':{pictureCaption:'Dusk at the kitchen table, work done, and a sketchbook being opened. The evening is not leftover time. It has a purpose of its own.',bridge:'Idea 1 called the day a budget. This one asks which of its hours you have been leaving out of the sum.',basics:{title:'Your day doesn\u2019t end when school does',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'AFTER SCHOOL',belief:'School is the real day. The hours after it are just for flopping on the sofa.',reality:'Those hours are part of your life too. There are a lot of them, and they are yours to choose what to do with.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'DO THE SUM',text:'School takes about seven hours. Sleep takes about nine. That still leaves eight hours. That is more than the whole school day, and you get it every day.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The hours outside school are not just a rest from it. They are a big part of your life, and you get to choose what they are for.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'NOT OVER YET',caption:'It is getting dark, but the day is not done. He opens a sketchbook. These hours are his to choose.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE THE CRUSTS',text:'Lots of people treat those hours like the crusts on a sandwich: the bit you leave. But here the crusts are bigger than the filling. Leave them and you miss most of the sandwich.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What Bennett noticed',text:'Arnold Bennett wrote a book about time in 1908. He saw that grown-ups called their work hours the real day. The rest was just getting ready for work, or getting over it. He thought that wasted most of a life.'},{kind:'try',text:'Tonight, count the hours you had outside school that were yours. Then pick one thing you would like a few of them to be for.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Maya gets home at four and says, \u201cThe day is over. Now I just rest.\u201d What would Bennett say?',options:['She still has hours left that she can choose to use','She is right, because school is the main part of the day','She should do extra homework so school goes better'],answer:0,why:'For Bennett, the hours after school are a day of their own. Rest can be part of it. But what they are for is her choice.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A WORKING DAY',prompt:'If your job takes eight hours, how many hours of the day does it not take?',options:['About four, once commuting and chores are done','Sixteen','None that really count'],answer:1,reveal:'Sixteen. Sleep takes about half of them, but that still leaves as many waking hours outside the job as in it. Most people spend them as if they did not count.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Most people treat the working day as the day, and the hours around it as recovery. Bennett proposes the reverse: the sixteen hours are a day in their own right, with the job inserted into it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Prologue and epilogue',text:'If the evening is spent getting over today\u2019s work and the morning getting ready for tomorrow\u2019s, the job has claimed hours it does not occupy. The evening is not free time if its purpose is to make you fit for the office.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'The job\u2019s day',items:['Work is the real day','Evenings are recovery','Mornings are preparation']},right:{title:'Your day',items:['Sixteen hours with their own purposes','Work is one part, inserted','Evenings are for something you chose']},note:'Same hours, same job. Only the question of who owns the evening has changed.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'AFTER SIX',caption:'Evening at the kitchen table, the light going outside. Work is over and the sketchbook is open: an hour after the job, given a purpose the job did not set.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it needs no new job',text:'Bennett spends little time on what work you should do. The reframing changes nothing about the job and everything about the hours around it, so it is open to anyone, whatever they do for a living.'},{kind:'try',text:'For one week, note the hours between finishing work and going to sleep. Add them up on Sunday. That total is the number worth deciding about.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A friend says their evenings vanish because work leaves them drained. Which response is closest to Bennett?',options:['Treat even a few evening hours as yours, with a purpose chosen in advance','Change jobs first, since nothing else is going to help','Rest every evening, so that work goes better the next day','Work longer hours now in order to free up time later'],answer:0,why:'Bennett\u2019s move is to stop spending the evening on the job\u2019s behalf. Resting only to be fit for work is exactly the habit he targets.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE READER HE HAD IN MIND',text:'Bennett was writing for the salaried London clerk: fixed office hours, a train journey either side, and a vague sense that life was happening somewhere else. His sharpest observation concerns how that reader divided his own day.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Treating the working day as the real day hands the whole day to the job, including the hours it does not occupy. The sixteen hours outside work are a day of their own, and the work is inserted into it.'},{kind:'term',word:'Prologue and epilogue',meaning:'How Bennett\u2019s reader treated the hours around work: time before it spent preparing, time after it spent recovering. Neither has a purpose of its own. Both belong to the job.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE JOB TAKES THE EVENING',nodes:['Work is called \u201cthe day\u201d','Evening becomes recovery','Recovery serves tomorrow\u2019s work','The job owns all sixteen hours'],note:'No employer asked for this. The reader concedes it in the way he names his hours.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'THE EPILOGUE RECLAIMED',caption:'Dusk at a kitchen table, and a sketchbook being opened. The hour after work is not spent recovering for tomorrow. It has been given a purpose of its own.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the move is cheap',text:'It needs no change of job, no promotion, no permission. That is why the essay barely discusses what work you should do. The reframing is open to anyone with any hours outside work at all, and everything else in the book depends on it.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Bennett\u2019s imagined reader had regular hours and evenings largely his own. For a shift worker, a parent or a carer the sixteen hours are not empty, and recovery is a need, not a failure of purpose. The reframe still helps on a smaller budget. It should never be read as a reproach to the exhausted.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Name one thing you do in the evening mainly to be fit for work tomorrow. Is that a purpose you would have chosen?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISREADING',prompt:'Which of these misreads Bennett\u2019s idea?',options:['Every hour outside work should be put to productive use','The evening needs a purpose that is not set by the job','Work is one part of the day, not the whole of it','Hours spent only recovering for work still belong to the job'],answer:0,why:'Bennett asks for a modest share of the evenings, not all of them. His objection is to letting the job set the purpose of hours it does not occupy.'}]}},'how-to-live-on-24-hours_3':{pictureCaption:'A vast plan, drawn out in full and abandoned. In the middle, one small block actually laid. Only the block exists.',bridge:'Idea 2 found the hours. This one is about how few of them to claim at first.',basics:{title:'Start small enough to keep going',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'A NEW PLAN',text:'You decide to get really good at guitar. One hour, every single day! Monday goes well. Tuesday too. On Thursday you are tired, so you skip it. By Sunday you have stopped.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'Why did the plan stop?',options:['You are no good at guitar','The plan was too big to keep','Guitar is just too hard'],answer:1,reveal:'The plan asked for too much, so it broke. Then it felt like *you* had failed. A smaller plan might still be going.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A small plan you keep beats a big plan you drop. Bennett said to start with ninety minutes on three evenings a week. Not every evening.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A huge plan lies on the table, never built. In the middle sits one small brick, put in place. That brick is the only real part.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why three days, not seven?',text:'Arnold Bennett wrote a book about time in 1908. He knew people start with big plans and quit fast. Then they think the whole idea was silly. Three days leaves room for tired days, so you keep going.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one thing you want to get better at. Choose three days this week for it. Keep the time short enough to do even on a bad day.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Leo wants to read more. Which plan would Bennett like best?',options:['Read for a set time on three days a week','Read for two hours every single day','Read only when he is in the mood'],answer:0,why:'Three set days is small enough to keep. Two hours a day will likely break. Waiting for a mood is not a plan at all.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TWO PLANS',prompt:'Which plan is more likely to still be running in a year?',options:['Two hours every evening','Ninety minutes, three evenings a week'],answer:1,reveal:'The first looks more serious and fails sooner, and one bad week turns into a verdict on the whole idea. The second leaves four evenings alone, which is what lets it survive.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Begin with ninety minutes, three evenings a week. The smallness is the design: a programme you can keep beats one you admire.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW AMBITION KILLS A PLAN',nodes:['A plan for every evening','A busy week; evenings missed','\u201cThis is unrealistic\u201d','The whole idea is dropped'],note:'The conclusion is drawn about the idea, from evidence about the plan\u2019s size.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The real constraint',text:'Bennett\u2019s point is that the limit on self-improvement is almost never the number of available hours. It is the number of sustainable ones. Many people could find seven free evenings on paper. Very few could keep all seven for a year.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'An enormous plan, drawn in full and abandoned. In the centre, one small block actually laid. Only one of these exists.'},{kind:'try',text:'Choose your three evenings now, by name, and the ninety minutes on each. Leave the other four alone on purpose.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A colleague announces a plan to study every night for a professional exam. What would Bennett warn them about?',options:['A missed week may convince them the whole plan was unworkable','Evening study is less effective than study in the morning','Exams are a poor use of time set aside for improvement','A plan this serious needs a written timetable for each night'],answer:0,why:'The danger is not laziness but scale. When an oversized plan breaks, the failure gets blamed on the idea instead of on the size.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHAT HE ACTUALLY PRESCRIBED',text:'Having freed the sixteen hours, Bennett asks remarkably little of them: ninety minutes of deliberate mental effort on three evenings a week. He also warns against starting with too much energy. The reader with grand resolutions, he observes, has usually stopped within a few weeks.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The constraint on self-improvement is not available hours but sustainable ones. Choose a programme small enough to survive, because the return comes from keeping it for years.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why small protects the idea',text:'An oversized plan that fails does double damage. You lose the evenings, and you also lose the belief that evening study can work at all. A modest plan that holds keeps the idea credible long enough for the results to arrive, and those results come from accumulation.'},{kind:'keyline',text:'Size the plan for your worst week, not your best one.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'HOW MUCH TO ASK',low:'Too little to change anything',high:'Too much to keep up',note:'Bennett aims at the largest amount that still survives a bad week: three evenings, with four left untouched.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The ambitious plan is drawn in full and abandoned. The single block in the centre is modest, and it is the only part that exists.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Small is not automatically right. Some goals have deadlines that ninety minutes three times a week cannot meet, and a dose too small to show progress can die of boredom instead of exhaustion. The principle is to size the plan to what survives. That is a question for each person, not a fixed number from 1908.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A team wants to adopt a new practice. Which rollout follows Bennett\u2019s reasoning?',options:['One short session a week, kept for a quarter, then reviewed','A daily hour for everyone from Monday, with no exceptions','An optional session whenever people feel they have time','A full week of training, then nothing until next year'],answer:0,why:'It is sized to survive and judged on a long run. The daily hour is the oversized plan, and the optional one is no plan at all.'}]}},'how-to-live-on-24-hours_4':{pictureCaption:'The dog keeps heading off the path. The walker brings it back, again, without a fuss. That patient return is the whole exercise.',bridge:'Idea 3 set aside ninety minutes. This one trains the skill those minutes depend on: keeping the mind where you put it.',basics:{title:'Your mind can be trained like a puppy',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'A PUPPY ON A WALK',picture:!0,text:'You take a puppy for a walk. It pulls off to sniff a bush. You bring it back. Ten steps later it pulls off again. You don\u2019t get cross. You just bring it back. That is how puppies learn.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Your mind wanders, just like that puppy. Each time you notice and bring it back, it gets a bit better at staying. Focus is something you can practise.'},{kind:'steps',title:'The walking game',steps:['Pick one thing to think about on a walk.','Your mind will drift. That is normal.','When you notice, bring it back. Don\u2019t be cross.','Count how many times you bring it back.']},{kind:'myth',belief:'If my mind keeps drifting, I am just bad at focus.',reality:'Every mind drifts. The bringing back is the practice. Each time you do it is one more go, like a rep in sport.'},{kind:'explain',title:'An old idea',text:'Arnold Bennett wrote about this in 1908. He said the first tries feel silly, because the mind drifts at once. But it gets better with practice. There is no clever trick.'},{kind:'try',text:'On your next walk to school, pick one question, like what to do at the weekend. Count how often you drift off. Try again next week and compare.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Ava tries to think about her story on the bus. Her mind keeps going to a game. What should she do?',options:['Notice, then bring it back to the story, again and again','Give up, since her mind just can\u2019t stay on one thing','Get cross with herself so that she tries harder'],answer:0,why:'Bringing it back is the exercise. Each time she does it, the next time gets a little easier.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE DECISION YOU KEEP CIRCLING',text:'There is a decision you have been putting off for weeks. Each time you sit down to think it through, within a minute you are checking your phone, planning dinner or replaying a conversation. You conclude you cannot think about it properly.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Concentration is not something you have or lack. It is a skill, trained by repetition: notice the mind has drifted, bring it back without irritation, carry on.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Bennett\u2019s exercise',steps:['Take one subject with you on a walk.','Hold your thought on it.','When it drifts, and it will at once, notice.','Fetch it back without annoyance, and continue.','Repeat for the whole walk.'],note:'He promises the first attempts will be humiliating. Improvement comes from repetition, not insight.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Older than the wellness industry',text:'These are the mechanics meditation teachers describe now: notice, return, repeat. But in 1908 Bennett sold it as a way to think better, not to feel calmer. He wanted his clerk able to think hard about one thing on the train, because that was the time he had.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The dog keeps heading off the path. The walker brings it back, again, without a fuss. That patience is the exercise.'},{kind:'try',text:'Tomorrow, take that decision on a walk. Hold it for the whole route and count the returns. The number will be large. Write it down, and compare in a week.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Ten minutes into thinking about a problem, you notice you have been planning your weekend. What does the exercise ask?',options:['Return to the problem calmly; noticing the drift is part of it','Stop, and try again when you are less distracted','Conclude the problem is too dull to hold your attention','Tell yourself off firmly, so the drift happens less often'],answer:0,why:'Each return is one repetition of the skill. Bennett asks for it without irritation, because the drift is expected, not a fault.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'SAME MECHANICS, TWO PROMISES',left:{title:'Bennett, 1908',items:['Sold as mental discipline','Aim: think hard about one thing','Done on the walk or the train']},right:{title:'Meditation now',items:['Sold as wellbeing','Aim: feel calmer','Often done sitting still']},note:'Notice, return, repeat. The method is the same; only the promise changed.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The mind cannot be controlled by wishing, only trained. Hold it on one subject, fetch it back without irritation each time it drifts, and the capacity grows with repetition, as any faculty does.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The case against wishing',text:'Bennett\u2019s starting claim is blunt: the mind is not governed by resolve. He treats it like any other faculty in training. The first attempts are humiliating, because they show how little control you have, and the improvement comes from repetition rather than from insight.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'THE LEAD, NOT THE LECTURE',picture:!0,text:'A dog on a lead keeps heading for the hedge. You do not argue with it or punish it. You bring it back, every time, calmly. Over weeks it pulls less. Bennett asks you to treat your attention the same way.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it matters to the programme',text:'The ninety minutes of idea 3 are worth little if attention leaves after five. The serious reading and the reflection Bennett prescribes both depend on this skill. And the training costs no extra time: it uses a walk you were taking anyway.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Repetition improves the skill, but minds are not equally trainable, and a mind that keeps drifting is sometimes signalling tiredness or worry that needs attention, not discipline. For real attention difficulties a walk and resolve are no substitute for proper help. And a busy road is no place to be lost in thought.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Concentration is trained, not wished into being.','The exercise is the return, made without irritation.','Bennett sold it for thinking better, not feeling calmer.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISREADING',prompt:'Which conclusion misreads Bennett\u2019s exercise?',options:['A walk with forty returns was a failed session','Counting the returns gives a baseline to improve on','The drifting is expected, especially at first','Attention training is far older than the apps selling it'],answer:0,why:'Forty returns is forty repetitions. The drift is what the exercise works on, not evidence that it failed.'}]}},'how-to-live-on-24-hours_5':{pictureCaption:'A running shoe set out on the bench for show, and a half-eaten sandwich where a friend sat. Show off the habit, and people leave.',bridge:'Ideas 1 to 4 built a programme. This one is about what the programme can do to the way you treat other people.',basics:{title:'Don\u2019t show off about your plan',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'AT LUNCH',picture:!0,text:'Your friend has started getting up early to run. That\u2019s great. But now it is all she talks about. She asks why you don\u2019t run. She sighs at your crisps. People have started sitting somewhere else.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'What has gone wrong?',options:['Running was a bad plan','She has started to show off about it','Her friends are jealous of her'],answer:1,reveal:'Running is fine. The problem is how she treats people now. She acts as if her plan makes her better than them.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A good plan is for you. It does not make you better than anyone. So keep doing it, and don\u2019t lecture people about it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What Bennett warned',text:'Arnold Bennett wrote a book in 1908 about using free time well. He took care to warn about this. He knew a new plan can make people annoying. And once friends are fed up, people often quietly give the plan up.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Keeping it quiet',items:['You do the plan','Friends stay friends','You keep going']},right:{title:'Showing off',items:['You talk about the plan','Friends get fed up','The plan often stops']}},{kind:'try',text:'Started something new, like a sport or an instrument? Keep it to yourself for a month. Just do it. See if it still feels worth doing.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Jay has started reading every night. Which is the Bennett way to handle it?',options:['Keep reading, and don\u2019t boast about it','Tell his brother he is lazy for not reading','Stop reading so no one thinks he is a show-off'],answer:0,why:'The reading is good for Jay. Making others feel small about it is the part Bennett warns against.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'A disciplined routine shows you are more serious than people without one.',reality:'Bennett\u2019s point is that the programme is for your benefit and gives you no standing over anyone. Treat it as a credential and you have started doing it for a different reason.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'DINNER WITH A CONVERT',text:'A friend reorganised his mornings a month ago. Now every dinner includes a report: the early alarm, the journal, a gentle question about why you still scroll in bed. The routine is real. It is also, clearly, being performed.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Do not become insufferable about your programme. The newly systematic tend to mistake their schedule for a virtue, which damages their company and, once that cost is felt, quietly ends the programme too.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE CONVERT LOSES IT',nodes:['New routine, real benefit','The routine becomes a topic','Other people tire of it','The social cost is felt','The routine is quietly dropped']},{kind:'picture',kicker:'THE EMPTY END',caption:'The running shoe is set out on the bench like an exhibit. At the far end, a half-eaten sandwich: whoever was listening has had enough and gone. The routine is real. The audience did not stay.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The sharper point',text:'If you need your self-improvement acknowledged, you are doing it for a different reason than the one you give. Bennett\u2019s instinct is that the person visibly optimising their day is often enjoying the performance more than the result.'},{kind:'try',text:'Say nothing about your new routine for a month, and keep doing it. Notice whether it still feels worth doing when nobody knows.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You have kept your study evenings for six weeks, and a friend asks how you find the time. What fits Bennett\u2019s warning?',options:['Answer plainly and briefly, then move on','Explain the whole system so they can adopt it','Point out how much time they lose on their phone','Deny it, to avoid any appearance of pride'],answer:0,why:'Bennett does not ask for secrecy, only that the programme earn you no standing. A plain answer to a real question is neither a lecture nor a performance.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'BENNETT\u2019S WORD',word:'Prig',meaning:'Someone smugly sure of their own correctness, and tiresome about it. Bennett warns that his reader may become one: insufferable about the new programme and contemptuous of people who have none.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A programme of self-improvement confers no standing over anyone. Treat it as a virtue to display and you damage your company, and in time the programme as well.'},{kind:'explain',title:'An unusual warning',text:'Advice books rarely concede that their advice might make the reader worse company. Bennett sets this danger out explicitly, and treats it as a failure mode to design against rather than a question of manners.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO REASONS TO KEEP A ROUTINE',left:{title:'For the benefit',items:['Done whether seen or not','Mentioned when asked','Survives going unnoticed']},right:{title:'For the standing',items:['Done partly to be seen','Raised unprompted','Fades when nobody admires it']},note:'Bennett\u2019s implied test: does the programme need an audience?'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'NEEDING AN AUDIENCE',caption:'A shoe placed on the bench to be seen, not worn, and a half-eaten sandwich at the far end where the audience has already left. Kept for standing, a routine ends up with nobody to show it to.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Practical, not polite',text:'The point is not etiquette. A routine worn as an identity in company acquires a social cost, and when the cost is felt the routine tends to go with it. Contempt also bends the purpose: the programme becomes evidence of superiority instead of a means of living better.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The warning can be over-learned. Telling a friend what you are doing, or joining others with the same aim, can help a practice last, and total secrecy is its own kind of self-regard. Bennett\u2019s target is contempt and display, not conversation. The difficulty is that from the inside the two can feel the same.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which of your own habits do you mention more often than anyone needs to hear? What would you lose if nobody knew about it?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'WHERE IS THE LINE',prompt:'Which of these crosses into what Bennett warns against?',options:['Mentioning your routine mainly so others feel behind','Telling a friend about a hard book you are working through','Joining a club of people who share the same aim','Answering honestly when someone asks about your evenings'],answer:0,why:'The others share or explain. Only making others feel behind uses the programme to claim standing over someone, which is the prig\u2019s move.'}]}},'how-to-live-on-24-hours_6':{pictureCaption:'A grey blur outside, a book shut on the seat. The traveller is not filling the journey. He is using it to think.',bridge:'Idea 4\u2019s exercise needs a stretch of time with nothing else in it. Bennett finds one in the daily journey.',basics:{title:'Trip time is real time',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE BUS TO SCHOOL',text:'Say your bus to school takes twenty minutes. And twenty minutes home. That is forty minutes a day. Over a school week, that is more than three hours. Most of it you spend staring out of the window.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Trips feel like gaps between the real parts of the day. But they are real time. You can use them to think about one thing.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'He looks out of the train window. His book stays shut on the seat. He is not reading. He is thinking hard about one thing.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What Bennett said',text:'Arnold Bennett wrote about time in 1908. The people he wrote for went to work by train. He said the trip was not wasted time. Use it to think hard about one thing, in your head.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Trips don\u2019t count. They are just getting from one place to the next.',reality:'A trip is quiet, and you know when it will come. That makes it good for thinking. It only feels empty because we call it a gap.'},{kind:'try',text:'On your next trip, pick one thing to think about: a problem, a story or a plan. Don\u2019t pick up a screen until you have thought about it for ten minutes.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your car ride to a match takes half an hour. Which way uses Bennett\u2019s idea?',options:['Think through how you will play, all the way there','Just wait, since car rides don\u2019t really count','Play any game, since the time is wasted anyway'],answer:0,why:'The ride is real time, and it is quiet. Holding one thing in your head turns it into time you used.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'DO THE ARITHMETIC',prompt:'Forty minutes each way, five days a week, for a working year. Roughly how much time is that?',options:['About forty hours','About a hundred hours','About three hundred hours'],answer:2,reveal:'Around three hundred hours, depending on holidays: close to two months of working days. Most of it is filed as getting there rather than as time.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The commute is not dead time. It is a predictable, uninterrupted block, and Bennett proposes using it for one difficult thing held in the head.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why journeys suit thinking',text:'Journeys, queues and waiting rooms are unusual. You know when they will happen, and nothing interrupts them. Those are the conditions sustained attention needs, and the working day rarely provides them.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Written off as',items:['A gap between places','Time for vague recovery','Something to fill with anything']},right:{title:'Bennett\u2019s view',items:['A daily block of real time','Time for one sustained activity','Best spent on reflection']}},{kind:'explain',title:'Thinking, not reading',text:'Bennett suggests concentrated reflection on the train rather than reading, on the grounds that the conditions suit thinking better than study. The arithmetic has not changed since 1908. What has changed is how much is now available to fill the time with.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A grey blur outside, a book closed on the seat. The traveller has chosen to use the journey, not to fill it.'},{kind:'try',text:'Choose one question to carry on tomorrow\u2019s journey. Open nothing, no phone and no book, until you have spent ten minutes on it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You face a twenty-minute wait at the doctor\u2019s, and a decision about a job offer. What would Bennett suggest?',options:['Use the wait to work through the decision in your head','Read a magazine, since waiting rooms are for resting','Clear some emails so the wait is not completely lost','Save the decision for a free evening, to think properly'],answer:0,why:'A waiting room is predictable and uninterrupted, the same class of time as the commute. It suits one sustained piece of thinking.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'THE SUBURBAN TRAIN',text:'Bennett\u2019s reader travelled into London by suburban train, morning and evening. Bennett treats that journey as a substantial daily block that was habitually written off, and uses it as his example of time hiding in plain sight.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Time is written off when it feels like a transition rather than time. That is an error of filing, not a fact about the hours, and journeys suit sustained attention better than most of the working day.'},{kind:'term',word:'Categorisation error',meaning:'Putting something in the wrong mental box. Here: filing a predictable, uninterrupted block as \u201cgetting there\u201d, and so never asking what it is for.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why he chose reflection',text:'He suggests concentrated reflection rather than reading on the train, on the grounds that the conditions there suit thinking better than they suit study. That ties this idea to the concentration exercise of idea 4, which needs exactly this kind of uninterrupted stretch.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Landscape blurred, book closed beside him. Nothing in the compartment is being consumed. The journey is being used.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The argument assumes a seat and a quiet carriage. Driving, cycling or standing in a crush leaves little room for hard thought, and for some people the journey is the only rest they get. The modern problem is also the reverse of his: the journey is no longer empty but full of a phone, which makes deliberate use harder.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'List the predictable, uninterrupted blocks in your week: journeys, queues, waits. Which have you never thought of as time?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'Which of these is the same class of time Bennett meant?',options:['A daily fifteen-minute wait at the school gate','An hour of back-to-back meetings at work','A long dinner out with a group of friends','A shift answering phones at a busy desk'],answer:0,why:'It is predictable and uninterrupted, and usually written off as waiting. The others are already full, or constantly interrupted.'}]}},'how-to-live-on-24-hours_7':{pictureCaption:'The footprints stop, the ground lies untouched for a stretch, then the prints carry on in the same line. The gap did not end the walk.',bridge:'Idea 3 kept the plan small so it would survive. This one is about what to do when it lapses anyway.',basics:{title:'Missed a day? Just start again',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE BROKEN STREAK',belief:'If I miss a few days, I have failed. So I might as well stop.',reality:'Missing days is normal. The only big loss is stopping for good. You can just start again.'},{kind:'scene',text:'You were drawing every Monday, Wednesday and Friday. Then you got ill. Then there was a party. Two weeks went by. Now it feels too late to go back.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Missing some days does not mean the plan has failed. Don\u2019t make a big deal of it. Just start again today.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The footprints stop. There is a gap. Then they carry on in the same line. The gap did not end the walk.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it costs nothing',text:'Arnold Bennett wrote about this in 1908. He said trying a plan can\u2019t really hurt you. If you drop it, you only lose hours you weren\u2019t using anyway. Feeling bad about it costs more than the missed days.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of something you stopped doing. Do it once today. Don\u2019t count the days you missed. Don\u2019t make a new plan first.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Ruby practised football skills for a week, then stopped for ten days. What should she do?',options:['Start again today, and not worry about the gap','Give up, because ten days is too long a break','Wait until next month for a fresh start'],answer:0,why:'The gap is over. Starting again today is all it takes. Waiting for a fresh start only makes the gap longer.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE THIRD MISSED EVENING',text:'You kept your new evening routine for two weeks. Then a late night at work, a cold, a birthday. Three evenings gone. Somewhere in the fourth, a quiet conclusion forms: this does not work for me.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Expect to lapse, and treat a lapse as unimportant. Restart without ceremony, because abandonment followed by guilt costs far more than any missed session.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'LAPSE OR COLLAPSE',left:{title:'A lapse',items:['Some sessions missed','Restart at the next scheduled one','No accounting for the gap']},right:{title:'A collapse',items:['Some sessions missed','\u201cSo it doesn\u2019t work\u201d','Programme dropped, guilt kept']},note:'The missed evenings are identical. Only the conclusion drawn from them differs.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The bad inference',text:'Someone who misses three evenings and decides the scheme is unworkable has judged a scheme on a fortnight\u2019s evidence. Bennett adds that the downside of trying is close to zero: the hours lost were ones you were not going to use anyway.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The prints stop, the ground is untouched for a stretch, then the prints continue in the same line. The gap is marked, not mourned.'},{kind:'try',text:'Restart something you abandoned, today. No new system, no new start date, and no reckoning with the missed weeks.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You missed a fortnight of your language practice. Which restart fits Bennett?',options:['Do tonight\u2019s session as planned and say nothing about the gap','Do double sessions until the missed fortnight is covered','Wait for the first of the month to begin again cleanly','Redesign the whole plan so this can never happen again'],answer:0,why:'Each of the others treats the lapse as significant, with a debt, a ceremony or a new system. Bennett\u2019s point is that it is not.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'TWO WORDS FOR ONE GAP',word:'Lapse and collapse',meaning:'A lapse is missed sessions in a practice that continues. A collapse is the practice ending because the misses were read as a verdict. The same gap can become either.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Failing at the programme is unimportant and should be expected. Since the hours were otherwise unused, trying has almost no downside, and the only expensive response to a lapse is to treat it as meaningful.'},{kind:'explain',title:'His unusually relaxed answer',text:'Bennett anticipates the collapse before it happens and answers it calmly. The great merit of the whole scheme, he argues, is that failing at it does you no harm, because those hours would otherwise have gone on nothing in particular. What does harm is abandonment followed by guilt.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW A LAPSE BECOMES A COLLAPSE',nodes:['A few evenings missed','The misses read as evidence','\u201cThe scheme is unworkable\u201d','Abandoned, with guilt'],note:'The break happens at the second step: a fortnight treated as a verdict.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it reads as modern',text:'Later writing on habits built a whole vocabulary around this distinction. Bennett states it plainly in 1908 without any of it. The practices that survive for years are the ones whose lapses are not taken as information about the person.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Not every lapse is noise. A practice dropped again and again at the same point may be badly designed, too large or aimed at the wrong thing, and restarting it unchanged is then stubbornness. And trying costs nothing only when the hours really were idle. Time taken from sleep or from other people has a price.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Expect lapses; they are not verdicts.','Restart without ceremony or accounting.','A repeated collapse at one point is a design question.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'A friend says: \u201cIf I keep restarting, I\u2019m just failing over and over.\u201d What is the best reply from Bennett\u2019s view?',options:['Each restart costs little; only reading lapses as a verdict ends a practice','True, so choose a plan you are certain you will never miss','Restarting once is fine, but a second lapse proves the point','Failing is fine as long as you feel bad enough not to repeat it'],answer:0,why:'Each restart recovers the practice at almost no cost. The expensive outcome is the conclusion that it cannot work.'}]}},'how-to-live-on-24-hours_8':{pictureCaption:'A tall stack of easy reads, and one thick volume set apart with its bookmark barely inside. The hard one is the one that builds something.',bridge:'Idea 3 set aside ninety minutes and idea 4 trained the attention. This one is about what to point that attention at.',basics:{title:'Pick the book that makes you work',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'IN PE',prompt:'Which one makes your legs stronger?',options:['Walking on flat ground','Running up a hill'],answer:1,reveal:'The hill is harder, and that is why it works. Your legs have to push. Your mind is the same.'},{kind:'idea',text:'If you want your mind to grow, pick things that are a bit hard for you. Easy things are fun, but they don\u2019t make you stronger.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A tall pile of easy books, and one big book set apart. Its bookmark is only just inside. That is the hard one, and it has been started.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What Bennett said',text:'Arnold Bennett wrote novels, which are long made-up stories. Yet he said your special study time is not for novels. They are not bad. They are just too easy to make you think hard.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'So reading for fun is bad.',reality:'No. Read for fun as much as you like. Bennett means the special time you set aside to grow. That time needs something harder.'},{kind:'try',text:'After you read something hard, say what it was about in two sentences. If you can, it worked. If you can\u2019t, read that bit again.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Kai has set aside time to learn something. Which would Bennett pick for it?',options:['A book on how volcanoes work','His best comic, read again','A funny story he knows well'],answer:0,why:'The volcano book makes him work to follow it. That effort is what makes his mind stronger.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Bennett\u2019s advice to keep novels out of study time is literary snobbery.',reality:'He was a novelist himself. His point is about the ninety minutes. They exist to build a capacity. Material that asks nothing of you builds nothing, however good it is.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Spend planned study time on something that resists you. The point is the effort, not the content. Pleasant reading fills the hours without training anything.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'TWO READERS, ONE YEAR',text:'Two people each read three evenings a week for a year. One reads whatever is pleasant and gets through forty books. The other works slowly through one serious history. Ask each what they learned. One can lay out a chain of causes. The other mostly remembers enjoying it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why structure helps',text:'Bennett points to subjects with structure: the causes of things, a serious history, a difficult poet. Structure is what makes sustained attention possible. Each part depends on the last, so the mind has something to hold.'},{kind:'equation',parts:['Ninety minutes','Material that resists'],result:'A capacity built',note:'Swap the second term for something easy and the result is only ninety minutes spent.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A tall stack of easy reads, and one thick volume set apart, its bookmark barely past the cover. The hard one is the one that changes you.'},{kind:'try',text:'For your next planned session, pick the book you have been avoiding because it is hard. Afterwards, summarise it in three sentences.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You have set aside time to understand economics. Which choice fits Bennett?',options:['A textbook chapter you have to reread to follow','A lively podcast you can enjoy while cooking','A novel set in a bank during a crash','A set of short, breezy newspaper columns'],answer:0,why:'The chapter takes effort. It leaves something you can state afterwards. The others pass pleasantly and leave little behind.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'A NOVELIST AGAINST NOVELS',text:'Arnold Bennett made his living and his name as a novelist. So it is striking that, when he turns to what the reclaimed evenings should be spent on, he rules novels out. His reason is not their quality. It is that they go down too easily.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The ninety minutes are spent for the exertion, not the content. Choose material that resists you, because material that asks nothing of your attention leaves nothing behind.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What he recommends instead',text:'Subjects with structure: the causes of things, a serious history, a difficult poet. Structure is what makes sustained attention possible. The test he implies is whether you could summarise what you did afterwards. Effortful material leaves a residue that can be stated; pleasant material often does not.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'HOW MUCH RESISTANCE',low:'Passes agreeably, leaves nothing',high:'Too hard to follow at all',note:'Bennett wants material well past comfortable, but still within reach of sustained effort.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Identical easy volumes stacked high. One heavy book set apart, barely begun. The pile measures consumption; the bookmark measures effort.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Difficulty is not the same as value. Hard material can be obscure for no good reason, and a reader can struggle through it and keep as little as from something easy. Nor are novels effortless: a demanding one can train attention as well as a history. The real criterion is effort that leaves a statable residue, not a genre.'},{kind:'recap',points:['The study time is for exertion, not consumption.','Choose material with structure that resists you.','Test: can you state what you got from it?']},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY THE TEST',prompt:'Which session best meets Bennett\u2019s standard?',options:['Two hours on a hard history, after which you can explain three causes','Four hours on a classic novel, enjoyed and soon half forgotten','Two hours on a difficult philosophy text, finished but not understood','An hour of lectures watched at double speed, without notes'],answer:0,why:'Effort alone is not the test. The session should leave something you can state afterwards, and only the history session does.'}]}},'how-to-live-on-24-hours_9':{pictureCaption:'A mechanism taken apart and laid out in order, one cog turned over for a closer look. Inspection, done calmly, part by part.',bridge:'Idea 6 used the journey for reflection. This one gives the reflection its subject: your own conduct.',basics:{title:'Check what you did, not just what you say',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'A SMALL CHECK',text:'You say being kind matters to you. On Monday you laughed when a friend tripped. On Tuesday you helped someone with homework. What happened after each one? What you did tells you more than what you say.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Every so often, look back at what you did and what happened next. Then check: does it match what you say matters to you?'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Someone has taken a machine apart to see how it works. Every piece is laid out. One cog is held up for a close look.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A BIKE MECHANIC',text:'A mechanic does not guess why a bike squeaks. She looks at each part, one at a time. Your week is the same. Look at what you did, one thing at a time.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Who said this',text:'Arnold Bennett wrote a book about time in 1908. He said to spend some of your free time on this. Not to feel bad. Just to see what you did, and what came of it.'},{kind:'try',text:'Once a week, write down one thing you did and one thing that happened because of it. Don\u2019t decide anything yet. Just notice.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Noor says friends matter most to her. Which check would Bennett suggest?',options:['Look at what she did with friends this week','Ask her friends to tell her she is kind','Write a list of good things to believe'],answer:0,why:'What she did shows if it matches what she says. Praise and beliefs can\u2019t show that.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'ask',kicker:'BEFORE YOU READ ON',prompt:'Name three things you value. Now name three things you did last week. How well do the two lists line up?'},{kind:'idea',text:'Use some of the reclaimed time to examine your own conduct: what you did, what followed, and whether your behaviour and your stated principles are the same shape.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it comes first',text:'Bennett treats this as the foundation, not an extra. A programme of self-improvement run by someone who has never looked at their actual conduct is improving in a direction chosen without evidence.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE PERCEPTION HE MEANS',nodes:['What you did','What followed from it','Does it match what you value?'],note:'He ranks the steady perception of cause and effect in your own life above every other.'},{kind:'explain',title:'An audit, not a confession',text:'It is not penance and not reassurance. It is a comparison of stated priorities against the record of how the hours were spent, which is the only place the gap between the two shows up.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A mechanism laid out in order. One cog held up and turned over. Inspection, not blame.'},{kind:'try',text:'Once a week, write down one thing you did and one thing that followed from it. Draw no conclusion for a month. Then read the entries together.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You say your health is a priority. Which review fits Bennett\u2019s idea?',options:['Check last week\u2019s record of meals, sleep and exercise, and what followed','Reread your health goals to remind yourself why they matter','Forgive yourself for last week and promise to do better','Ask a friend whether they think you seem healthy lately'],answer:0,why:'Only the record of conduct shows whether the priority is real. Goals, promises and opinions describe intention, not behaviour.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'A DEBT HE NAMES',text:'Bennett recommends Marcus Aurelius and Epictetus by name, two Stoic philosophers of the Roman world. The debt shows in his most demanding prescription: that part of the reclaimed time go on reviewing your own conduct, much as the Stoics reviewed their days.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Spend part of the time on the continual perception of cause and effect in your own life. Without an honest record of conduct, self-improvement aims in a direction chosen without evidence.'},{kind:'term',word:'Evening review',meaning:'A Stoic practice: at the end of the day, going back over what you did, what went well and what did not, as routine rather than in a crisis.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'What it is not',items:['Penance: a search for guilt','Reassurance: a search for comfort','A dramatic reckoning in a crisis']},right:{title:'What it is',items:['An audit of the record','Stated priorities set against hours spent','Routine maintenance, done regularly']}},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the record, not the beliefs',text:'Most people can describe their beliefs in detail and their conduct hardly at all. The gap between the two is invisible from the side of belief. It only appears when the actual record of the hours is set beside what you claim to value.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The mechanism stripped and laid out in order, one cog turned over for inspection. Maintenance, done calmly, part by part.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Reviewing conduct can tip into rumination, especially for anxious people, and the audit becomes a nightly trial. Cause and effect in a life is also harder to read than in a machine: outcomes have many causes, and one week\u2019s record can mislead. That is why it is wise to wait a month before drawing any conclusion.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE DRIFT',prompt:'Which of these has drifted from Bennett\u2019s practice?',options:['A nightly hour listing every fault, ending in resolutions','A weekly note comparing your diary with your priorities','Noticing that late nights keep coming before poor mornings','Recording what you did and what followed, without judging yet'],answer:0,why:'That is penance, not an audit. Bennett\u2019s review sets the record against your priorities calmly, as maintenance.'}]}}}},2975,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'identity-not-outcomes_1':{pictureCaption:'A long chart of boxes, every one marked short of the goal. Only the very last box counts as done.',basics:{title:'A big goal feels like losing until the end',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THIRTY BOOKS',prompt:'You plan to read thirty books this year. It is the end of March and you have read six. How does the goal make you feel?',options:['Like you are winning','Like you are behind'],answer:1,reveal:'Six books is good reading! But the goal says thirty. So for most of the year, the goal only says one thing: not yet.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A goal like thirty books is not met for nearly the whole year. You only win on the last day. That makes it hard to keep going.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Look at all the boxes marked not yet. Only the last box is done. That is how a big goal feels from inside.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why that matters',text:'We keep going when we can see we are getting somewhere. A big goal far away hardly ever tells you that. It says well done once, at the very end, when you need it least.'},{kind:'explain',title:'So are big goals bad?',text:'No. A big goal shows you which way to go. It helps you look back and ask: was it worth it? It is just not good at getting you through each day.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one big goal. Add something small you can do and finish today, like reading ten pages.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Ben wants to save \xa350 for a game by the summer. Which plan gives him a win he can feel this week?',options:['Put \xa32 in his jar every Saturday','Keep checking how far he is from \xa350','Wait until summer to see if he made it'],answer:0,why:'He can do that each Saturday and know he did it. The \xa350 only says yes at the very end.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE BOOK YOU ARE WRITING',text:'You set out to write a book this year, and you write steadily. Every evening the goal reports the same thing: not done. It will say that on three hundred and sixty-four days, and something else on one.'},{kind:'idea',text:'An outcome goal is unmet for its whole duration and met only at the end. The feedback it gives you is negative for almost the entire time you are working on it.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE SHAPE OF THE FEEDBACK',nodes:['Day one: not yet','Day one hundred: not yet','Day three hundred: not yet','The last day: done'],note:'Motivation draws on signs of progress. This design supplies none until the end, when they are least needed.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Box after box marked short of the goal, and one box done at the far end. That is the feedback an outcome goal gives.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHAT IT IS FOR',left:{title:'Outcome goals are good at',items:['Setting a direction','Judging afterwards if it was worth it']},right:{title:'They are poor at',items:['Sustaining the work in between','Telling you today went well']},note:'Sustaining the behaviour is a different job, and it needs a different tool.'},{kind:'try',text:'Replace one outcome target with something you can succeed at today.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A friend aims to run 1,000 km this year and has felt behind every week since February. What does the idea suggest?',options:['Keep the target for direction, and add a weekly run they can tick off','Drop every target, since numbers only ever cause stress','Raise the target so that it creates more urgency','Check the running total daily to stay focused on it'],answer:0,why:'The outcome sets direction. Something that can be met this week supplies the progress signal the yearly total never will.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE PIECE\u2019S CLAIM',text:'The piece starts from a structural observation, not a moral one. An outcome goal has a shape: unmet for its whole length, met at a single point at the end. Whatever the goal is about, the shape is the same, and it is close to the worst shape there is for keeping someone going.'},{kind:'idea',text:'An outcome goal is failed until it is over. It supplies negative feedback for almost its whole duration, and its one positive signal arrives when it is least needed.'},{kind:'term',word:'Progress signal',meaning:'Any sign, during the work, that the work is going somewhere. The piece\u2019s premise is that motivation draws on these. A goal that reports only at the end gives none along the way.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Box after box marked short, and a single box done at the very end. Everything before it reads as falling short.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The signal comes at the wrong time',text:'The one positive report comes at completion, when the work is over and motivation is no longer required. During the long middle, when it is needed most, the goal can only say not yet. A ten-kilo target or a finished book behaves the same way.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The piece does not call outcome goals useless: they set direction and let you judge afterwards whether the effort was worth it. A distant goal can also be broken into milestones that supply signals along the way. The claim is narrower: a single far-off outcome, on its own, is a poor instrument for sustaining daily behaviour.'},{kind:'recap',points:['An outcome goal is unmet until the end.','So its feedback is negative almost throughout.','Use it for direction and review, not daily motivation.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE STRONGEST REPLY',prompt:'Someone says a big number on the wall keeps them going. What is the best response, by the piece\u2019s argument?',options:['It may set direction well, but daily signals will carry the long middle better','They are mistaken, because outcome goals never motivate anyone','They should raise the number until it feels exciting again','Numbers only motivate people with unusual discipline'],answer:0,why:'The piece keeps outcome goals for direction and review. Its claim is about sustaining the behaviour in between, not that a target can never help.'}]}},'identity-not-outcomes_2':{pictureCaption:'The morning after the race: tape on the ground, course empty, shoes left behind. For some runners, the running ended at that line.',bridge:'Idea 1 looked at the long stretch before the finish. This one looks at the morning after it.',basics:{title:'What happens after you win?',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'AFTER THE CUP FINAL',text:'Two friends practise penalties every day before the cup final. The final comes and goes. The next week, one of them is out kicking a ball as usual. The other never practises again.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Watch what someone does after they reach a goal. If they stop, the goal was the point. If they carry on, the thing itself was the point.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The race is over. The tape is on the ground. Someone has left their running shoes behind. Will they run tomorrow?'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO KINDS OF GOAL',left:{title:'Stops at the finish',items:['Win the cup','Read one big book','Pass the swim test']},right:{title:'Keeps going',items:['Be someone who plays','Be a reader','Be a swimmer']}},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it matters',text:'If a goal has an end, all you did for it can end too. So before you start, ask: what do I want to still be doing after?'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of a goal you have. Ask: what do I want to still be doing a month after I reach it?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Ella learns to swim a length to get a badge. What would show that swimming was the point, not just the badge?',options:['She keeps going to the pool after she gets it','She gets the badge on her very first try','She puts the badge where all can see it'],answer:0,why:'The day after tells you. If she keeps swimming, the badge was just one step along the way.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE MORNING AFTER',prompt:'Two people finish the same marathon. One is running again within a week. What best predicts which one?',options:['Who finished faster','Who trained harder','What the running was for'],answer:2,reveal:'For one, the race was the point, and the running was scaffolding for it. For the other, the race was an event inside something they already were: a runner.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The revealing question about any goal is what happens the day after it is achieved. If the behaviour stops, it was scaffolding for the number, not the point.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why effort evaporates after success',text:'A target ends by construction, and everything organised around it inherits the ending. The training plan, the routine, the early alarm: all of it was built to reach the line, so all of it is finished once the line is crossed.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Broken tape, an empty course, a pair of shoes left at the line. What happens next tells you what the race was for.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Visible in advance',text:'You do not have to wait for the finish to see which kind of goal you have. Ask two things before you start: what is the state afterwards, and does the behaviour have any reason to continue once the target is gone?'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick a goal you are working on. Ask what you want to still be doing the month after you hit it. Make that the goal.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You are preparing for a language exam in June. Which version of the goal would survive the day after?',options:['Being someone who reads a little in the language every day','Passing the June exam with the best mark you can get','Learning two thousand words before the exam date','Finishing every chapter of the course book in time'],answer:0,why:'The other three all end in June. A daily habit has a reason to carry on once the exam is over.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'TWO FINISHERS',text:'Two people cross the same finishing line. A year later one runs most mornings and the other has not run since. On race day they looked identical: same distance, same training, same medal. The difference was in what the race was for.'},{kind:'idea',text:'What happens the day after a goal is achieved shows what it was pointing at. A goal that ends was pointing at the number. Behaviour that continues was pointing at a way of living.'},{kind:'explain',title:'An ending built in',text:'The piece\u2019s point is structural. A target has its end built in, and everything organised around it inherits that end. So effort that was scaffolding for the number tends to evaporate once the number is reached, which can look like a strange collapse just after success.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The tape broken, the course empty, the shoes left at the line. The race ended. The question is whether the running did.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why this is more than a slogan',text:'This is why the piece treats framing a goal as an identity as more than a matter of words. Calling yourself a runner gives the behaviour a reason that outlives the race. A target cannot, because its reason expires with it. Idea 4 develops this.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Not every goal should continue. Someone who ran one marathon for a cause and then stopped has not failed: the race was the point, and that is fine. The test shows what a goal was for; it does not say every goal should be for continuing. The piece says the test predicts durability well, but offers that as an observation, not a measured result.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Pick a goal you are chasing. What will you be doing on the morning after you reach it?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE FAIR CASE',prompt:'A friend learns to drive, passes the test and stops taking lessons. Does the day-after test count this against the goal?',options:['No: passing was a genuine endpoint, so stopping lessons is the right result','Yes: stopping the moment the goal is met shows it was pointed wrongly','Yes: they should keep taking lessons so the habit continues','No: the day-after test only applies to sport and fitness'],answer:0,why:'The test reveals what a goal was for. Where the task truly finishes, stopping is the right outcome, the case idea 5 returns to.'}]}},'identity-not-outcomes_3':{pictureCaption:'A timer set and already running, the hand just leaving it. A goal that can be met, in full, today.',bridge:'Ideas 1 and 2 showed what outcome goals do badly. This is the first replacement: a goal you can meet today.',basics:{title:'A goal you can win today',beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'SAME WISH, TWO GOALS',left:{title:'Read a big book by summer',items:['Not done today','Not done tomorrow','Done one day, maybe']},right:{title:'Read for 15 minutes',items:['Done today','Done tomorrow','A win most days']},note:'Same wish to read. Only one gives you a win tonight.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Turn a big goal into something you do, like read for fifteen minutes. You can win at that today. And the big result often comes anyway.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A timer, wound up and ticking. When it rings, you have done it. That is a goal you can win today.'},{kind:'explain',title:'It is up to you',text:'You can choose to read for fifteen minutes. You can\u2019t choose how long a book takes, or who wins a prize. A goal you are in charge of is fairer to you.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE PIANO SONG',text:'Don\u2019t make the goal play the whole song by the concert. Make it play for ten minutes after school. Each day you did it or you didn\u2019t. And bit by bit, the song comes.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one big goal. Say it as something to do today: read for fifteen minutes, or practise for ten.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Which of these goals can you win today?',options:['Practise my times tables for ten minutes','Get top marks in the next maths test','Know all my times tables by the end of term'],answer:0,why:'You can do ten minutes today and know you did. The other two end later, and they don\u2019t depend only on you.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE USUAL WORRY',belief:'Process goals are a way of lowering the bar.',reality:'Same ambition, different instrument. Write for thirty minutes is met or not met by this evening. Finish the novel is not. The outcome usually still arrives, as a consequence rather than a target.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Process goals, defined by action rather than result, can be met today. That changes the feedback: you can succeed daily, and no deadline can make you miss.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Process goal',items:['Met or missed by tonight','Fully within your power','Feedback every day']},right:{title:'Outcome goal',items:['Met only at the end','Depends on luck and other people','Feedback once']},note:'Holding yourself to what you do not control produces discouragement that tells you nothing.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The catch',text:'A process can drift from the outcome it was chosen to serve. Thirty minutes a day of the wrong activity is faithfully done and useless. So check the process against the outcome now and then. That check is where the outcome goal earns its place.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A timer set and running, the hand already leaving. Once it rings, today\u2019s goal is met, whatever the final result turns out to be.'},{kind:'try',text:'Restate one goal as something that happens today rather than something that completes later.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You want to get fitter. Which of these is a process goal?',options:['Walk briskly for twenty minutes after lunch','Be able to run five kilometres by spring','Lose four kilos before the summer holiday','Beat last year\u2019s time in the charity race'],answer:0,why:'Only the walk can be done, and known to be done, today. The others are results that arrive later and depend on more than you.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'TWO KINDS OF GOAL',word:'Process goal',meaning:'A goal defined by an action you take, such as write for thirty minutes, rather than a result you reach. It is met or not met within the day, by you alone.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Restated as a process, the same ambition can succeed daily and cannot be missed by a deadline. The outcome usually arrives anyway, as a consequence rather than a target.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Two advantages',text:'First, feedback: a process goal reports success daily, which is what idea 1 found missing. Second, control: the process is fully yours, while the outcome depends on metabolism, markets, luck and other people. Holding yourself to what you do not control produces demoralisation that carries no information.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE OUTCOME STILL ARRIVES',nodes:['A daily action, met or missed','Daily signals of success','The action keeps being done','The result follows as a by-product'],note:'Usually, not always. The piece says the outcome tends to arrive, not that it must.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The timer is running and the hand is already leaving. Nothing more is needed today for the goal to be met.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The piece names the caveat itself: a process can drift from the outcome it was meant to serve. Thirty minutes a day of the wrong activity is faithfully executed and useless. So the process has to be reviewed against the outcome from time to time. That review is where outcome goals keep their place, and idea 5 develops it.'},{kind:'recap',points:['A process goal can be met today, by you alone.','Daily success supplies the signal outcome goals lack.','Review the process against the outcome, or it can drift.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE DRIFT',prompt:'Which of these shows the weakness the piece warns about?',options:['An hour of scales daily for a year, never once trying a real piece','Thirty minutes of writing each morning, and a draft by autumn','Twenty minutes of walking daily, with fitness checked every few months','Fifteen minutes of reading nightly, and more books than planned'],answer:0,why:'The process was done faithfully but never checked against the outcome it was meant to serve. That is the drift a periodic review would catch.'}]}},'identity-not-outcomes_4':{pictureCaption:'Where a sign might show a number, an apron hangs on a hook: not a total, but something somebody puts on every day.',bridge:'Idea 3 turned the goal into an action. This one turns it into a description of who you are, and changes what each action means.',basics:{title:'Be a reader, not a book counter',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'WHAT A GOAL SAYS',belief:'Saying *I am a reader* is just a nice slogan.',reality:'It changes what each day means. Read ten pages, and you are not just one step nearer a far-off total. You have shown, today, that you are a reader.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Say who you are becoming, not what you will have. *I am someone who reads* works better than *I will read thirty books.*'},{kind:'picture',caption:'No number on this door. Just an apron that someone puts on every day. That says who works here.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE CLUES',text:'Each time you read, it is a small clue that *I am a reader.* One clue proves nothing. But the clues add up, and soon you believe it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Do it first, then say it',text:'Saying *I am a runner* when you never run feels fake. Soon you stop saying it. So do the thing first, even a tiny bit. Then the words feel true.'},{kind:'try',text:'Finish this line: I am someone who ___. Then do one small thing today that shows it.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Which goal sounds like who you are becoming?',options:['I am someone who draws every day','I will fill a whole sketchbook by June','I will win the art prize this year'],answer:0,why:'It describes a kind of person. Each drawing is proof, not a step towards a far-off number.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO SAY IT',text:'Two colleagues start reading more. One says she will read thirty books this year. The other says she is becoming someone who reads. In April, after a bad fortnight, one of them is behind. The other is a reader who had a bad fortnight.'},{kind:'idea',text:'State an identity rather than a target. Under a target, each action is a step towards something not yet achieved. Under an identity, it is evidence of something already true.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The mechanism',text:'People act in line with how they describe themselves, and the description is updated by what they do, not by what they intend. Each action is a small piece of evidence about who you are. As the evidence builds up, the identity becomes believable to you.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'An apron on the hook where a number might go. It is worn every day, and the wearing is what makes it true.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Declare the identity first, and the behaviour will follow.',reality:'That is the usual failure. A claim with no behaviour behind it is uncomfortable to hold. The unease is usually settled by dropping the claim. The order runs the other way: behaviour first, description second.'},{kind:'try',text:'Describe your goal as the kind of person you are becoming. Do one thing today that supports it, and see whether it survives the week better.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You want to cook more. Which approach follows the idea?',options:['Cook one simple meal this week, then start calling yourself someone who cooks','Tell everyone today that you have become a keen cook','Set a target of a hundred home-cooked meals this year','Buy a set of good knives so that you feel like a cook'],answer:0,why:'Behaviour first, description second. The meal is the evidence that lets the description stick.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHAT ONE ACTION MEANS',left:{title:'Under a target',items:['A step towards a distant total','A reminder you are not there yet','Wasted if the total is missed']},right:{title:'Under an identity',items:['Evidence of something already true','A clue to who you are','Counts on its own']}},{kind:'idea',text:'Framing a goal as the kind of person you are makes each action a piece of evidence, not a step. The evidence builds a description you believe, and people tend to act in line with how they describe themselves.'},{kind:'term',word:'Consistency',meaning:'The tendency to act in line with how you describe yourself. The piece rests the identity framing on it, adding that the description is updated by behaviour, not by intention.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'No count on the door, only an apron someone puts on each day. Who works here is shown by what they do.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the order matters',text:'If the description is updated by behaviour, then declaring an identity you have no evidence for is the wrong way round. The claim sits unsupported, which is uncomfortable, and the usual way out is to drop it. So the piece reverses the usual order: behaviour first, description second.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Identity can harden. Someone who is a runner may keep running through injury to protect the label, or feel lost when they must stop. The same mechanism also works against people: *I am not a maths person* is consistency pointed the wrong way. The framing does real work, but it is still a frame, and some goals really are just tasks.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What do you already do often enough to count as evidence? What would you call the person who does it?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE FAILURE MODE',prompt:'Someone announces they are now a writer, writes nothing for a month, and quietly stops saying it. What went wrong, by the piece\u2019s account?',options:['The description came before any behaviour, so it had nothing to rest on','Writing is a talent, so that identity was never open to them','They should have set a word-count target instead of an identity','They announced it too publicly, and the pressure made them stop'],answer:0,why:'An unsupported claim is uncomfortable, and dropping it is the usual fix. The piece\u2019s order is behaviour first, then the description.'}]}},'identity-not-outcomes_5':{pictureCaption:'An exam hall, a fixed clock and one paper. A deadline set from outside, for a task that truly ends.',bridge:'Ideas 1 to 4 argued against running on outcomes. This one marks where numbers still belong.',basics:{title:'When a number is the right goal',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE SPELLING TEST',text:'Friday is the spelling test. It will happen whatever you do, and then it is over. A goal like *get 18 out of 20* makes sense here. There is a real date and a real end.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Number goals work for things that truly end on a set date, like a test or a race. For things you want to keep doing, a number goal is the wrong shape.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The clock on the wall won\u2019t wait. The test ends when it ends. That is when a number goal makes sense.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHICH SHAPE FITS?',left:{title:'Ends on a date',items:['A test','A race','The school play']},right:{title:'Meant to keep going',items:['Reading','Being a good friend','Playing music']}},{kind:'explain',title:'Numbers still help',text:'Numbers also show if your plan is working. But don\u2019t check too often. Measure your height every day and it seems stuck. Measure it every few months and you see the change.'},{kind:'try',text:'Look at a goal you have. Does it end on a real date? If not, swap the number for something you do each day.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Which goal fits a number best?',options:['Finish the school fun run in under twenty minutes','Practise the piano three hundred times this year','Read a hundred books, then keep on reading'],answer:0,why:'The fun run has a set date, and then it ends. Piano and reading are meant to keep going.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO USES OF A NUMBER',left:{title:'As measurement',items:['Shows whether the process works','Catches a routine heading the wrong way','Checked on a slow cycle']},right:{title:'As motivation',items:['Unmet until the end','Largely outside your control','Discouraging in the meantime']},note:'Indispensable on the left. Poor on the right.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Outcome targets work when the deadline is genuinely external and the task genuinely finishes: an exam, a race, a launch. For things meant to continue, use numbers to measure, not to motivate.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The arrangement that works',text:'Run the behaviour on process and identity. Review outcomes on a slow cycle, as a diagnostic, not as a daily scoreboard. Without the review, a faithfully kept routine can run for a year in the wrong direction.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE WORD COUNT',text:'A writer checks her total word count every evening. Some days it jumps; some days she cuts and it falls, and each evening feels like a verdict. Checked monthly, the same number shows the real trend, and the evenings are left for writing.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A fixed clock over an exam hall. The deadline is set from outside, and the task ends when it says so. Here a number belongs.'},{kind:'try',text:'List your current targets. Keep the ones with a genuine outside deadline. For the rest, swap the number for a process and put a monthly review in the calendar.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You are building savings with a fixed transfer each month. How does the idea say to use the balance?',options:['Review it monthly to check the plan is working','Check it every day to keep yourself motivated','Ignore it entirely, since only the process counts','Set a dated target and judge each week against it'],answer:0,why:'The balance is a diagnostic. Checked monthly it shows the trend; checked daily it mostly shows noise.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'WHICH ONE NEEDS A NUMBER?',prompt:'Which of these is best run as an outcome target?',options:['Keeping fit','A product launch on a fixed date','Being a better listener','Reading more'],answer:1,reveal:'The launch has an external deadline and genuinely finishes. The others are meant to continue, which is most of life, and that is where the piece says outcome targets go wrong.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Numbers are indispensable as measurement and poor as motivation. Keep outcome targets for things with a real external deadline that genuinely end, and review everything else on a slow cycle, as a diagnostic.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Measurement versus motivation',text:'This idea gathers the earlier ones. As motivation, an outcome is unmet until the end, largely outside your control and demoralising meanwhile. As measurement, it is the check that a process, faithfully done, still points the right way. Idea 3\u2019s caveat is answered here.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'HOW OFTEN TO LOOK',low:'Daily: mostly noise',high:'Monthly: mostly signal',note:'The piece argues that a slow cycle keeps the number\u2019s diagnostic value without turning it into a daily verdict.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A fixed clock over an exam hall. Here the deadline is real, set from outside, and the task ends when it strikes.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The piece states the daily-versus-monthly contrast firmly, but the right cycle depends on how fast a number moves and how noisy it is, and some people find frequent checks useful. External deadlines that can be renegotiated behave more like internal ones. And a task that genuinely ends often sits inside one that continues, as an exam sits inside learning a subject.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Outcome targets fit external deadlines and tasks that finish.','Elsewhere, numbers measure; process and identity motivate.','Review outcomes on a slow cycle, not as a daily scoreboard.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A team sets a target of a thousand users by December to keep everyone motivated. By the piece\u2019s argument, what is the better arrangement?',options:['Run the work on weekly practices and review the user count monthly','Check the user count every morning so the target stays urgent','Drop the number entirely, since numbers only demoralise','Raise the target so that it feels more inspiring'],answer:0,why:'Growing a product is meant to continue, so the number belongs in review. The work itself runs better on practices the team can meet each week.'}]}}}},2976,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'interpretation-of-dreams_1':{pictureCaption:'Above the water the swan glides, still. Below, its feet work hard. Much of the mind, Freud said, is like that: busy out of sight.',basics:{title:'Your mind works where you can\u2019t see',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'ON THE WAY HOME',text:'You are humming a song. You don\u2019t know why. Then it comes to you: it was playing in a shop an hour ago. You did not choose to hum it. Part of your mind did that without telling you.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A lot of what your mind does happens out of sight. It still steers what you do, and it does not tell you first.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO SAID THIS',text:'Sigmund Freud was a doctor in Vienna, a city in Austria. More than 120 years ago he wrote a famous book about dreams. His big claim was that a great deal of the mind works out of view.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A SWAN',picture:!0,text:'A swan glides across a pond. On top, it looks calm and still. Under the water, its feet paddle hard. You only see the top half. The half you can\u2019t see does the steering.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'What held up',items:['Much of the mind works out of view','It still changes what you do']},right:{title:'What did not',items:['Each dream is a wish come true','Dream pictures are a secret code']},note:'Later, scientists found the hidden mind with tests of their own. They kept his big claim and dropped the rest.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time someone asks why you did something, give your reason. Then add: *but I\u2019m not fully sure.* See how often that last part is true.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You pick the blue cup each morning. You think it is because blue is nice. What does Freud\u2019s big idea say?',options:['There may be a reason you can\u2019t see, like it is always at the front','You picked it for just the reason you think, and no other','Your choice of cup is a secret code for a hidden wish'],answer:0,why:'The big idea is only that reasons can work out of sight. The secret code part is the bit that did not hold up.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'WHY THAT ONE?',prompt:'You chose one flat over three others you viewed. Asked why, you say: the light. How far should you trust that answer?',options:['Fully: you were there','Partly: it is a guess about yourself'],answer:1,reveal:'Your reason may be true. But it was put together after the choice, by the same mind that made it. Experiments have caught people giving confident reasons for choices they had not in fact made.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A great deal of mental life happens out of view and steers behaviour without announcing itself. That is the claim Freud made famous, and it survived. Most of what he built on it did not.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Above the waterline, stillness. Below it, the work that steers.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Two routes to one claim',text:'Freud reached the hidden mind through dreams and his patients\u2019 talk. Much later, psychologists reached it through timed tests and priming studies, where a word flashed too briefly to notice can still speed up recognition of a related one. Their hidden mind has no buried wishes and no censor. It simply works faster and wider than awareness.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Survived',items:['Much of the mind works out of view','It shapes choices and feelings','Our own explanations can miss it']},right:{title:'Fared badly',items:['Every dream fulfils a wish','A fixed code of dream symbols','A censor guarding the door']},note:'Most people use the left column every day without knowing where it came from.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How to judge an influential book',text:'Separate the claim that something exists from the account of how it works. A thinker can be right about the first and wrong about nearly all of the second. The words they leave behind carry both, which is why a word like *repressed* can sound more proven than it is.'},{kind:'try',text:'The next time you explain why you did something, add one honest sentence: what would I say if I had to admit I don\u2019t fully know? Notice how often that sentence is the truer one.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A friend says Freud has been disproved, so the unconscious is a myth. What is the best reply?',options:['His details were dropped, but later research strongly supports hidden mental processing','Freud was right, and modern science has confirmed his theory of dreams','The unconscious cannot be studied, so neither side can be shown right','Only his dream theory failed; repression and the censor were confirmed'],answer:0,why:'Later work kept the general claim and dropped the particulars. Judging those two separately is the whole lesson.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'VIENNA, 1899',text:'Freud, a Viennese neurologist, published his dream book at the end of 1899, dated 1900 on the title page. It sold slowly at first. Its headline was that much of mental life goes on out of view and steers what we do. Philosophers and physiologists had proposed unconscious processes before him. He made the idea impossible to ignore.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Freud was right that much of the mind works out of view, and wrong about most of how. The claim survived by being rediscovered, by other methods, without his machinery.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The swan is the surviving claim: work out of view, steering what shows. It carries none of his machinery: no wish, no censor, no code.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What he built on it',text:'On that foundation he built a specific machine. Every dream fulfils a wish. The wish is disguised by a censor. The disguise follows a symbolic code an analyst can learn to read. These particulars are the parts that have fared badly under evidence.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The second route',text:'Cognitive science reached unconscious processing from the other side: reaction times, priming, and experiments in which people confidently explained choices they had not actually made. Its picture has no repressed wishes and no censor. It vindicated his headline and discarded his particulars.'},{kind:'keyline',text:'Being right that a thing exists is a different achievement from being right about how it works.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Two cautions. The claim was not Freud\u2019s alone, so saying he discovered the unconscious overstates it. And his vocabulary still smuggles his mechanism in. Calling a memory *repressed* asserts a cause, a motivated pushing-down, not merely that something is processed out of view. The everyday word carries the discarded theory along with the surviving claim.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of a choice you made this month. Which of your reasons for it were assembled afterwards?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'Which of these applies the lesson of this idea to another influential thinker?',options:['Accept that a pattern they named is real, while testing each proposed mechanism separately','Reject the whole body of work once its central mechanism is shown to be wrong','Accept their mechanisms, since the pattern they named turned out to be real','Judge the work by how widely its vocabulary is now used in ordinary speech'],answer:0,why:'Existence and mechanism are separate claims with separate evidence. Freud was vindicated on one and not the other, and his vocabulary carries both.'}]}},'interpretation-of-dreams_2':{pictureCaption:'On stage, a small painted cottage. In the wings, the ropes and weights that produce it. Freud cared about the wings.',bridge:'Idea 1 said much of the mind works out of view. This is Freud\u2019s tool for getting at it: separate what is shown from what produced it.',basics:{title:'What is shown, and what made it',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE BOOTS',text:'Your brother says he wants to quit football. He says his boots hurt. So he gets new boots. He still wants to quit. Then you find out that a boy on the team keeps teasing him.'},{kind:'idea',text:'What you are shown and what made it can be two different things. Freud said dreams work like this. The dream you remember is the show. Something else built it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The crowd sees a little cottage on a stage. Behind the curtain, ropes and weights hold it up. The cottage is the show. The ropes made it.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'TWO NAMES',text:'Sigmund Freud was a doctor who studied dreams. He called the dream you remember the *manifest* part. That means the part on show. He called what it was built from the *latent* part. That means the hidden part.'},{kind:'myth',kicker:'NO DREAM DICTIONARY',belief:'Each thing in a dream has one meaning you can look up in a book.',reality:'Freud mostly said no. The same picture can come from quite different things in different people. To find out, you ask the dreamer.'},{kind:'try',text:'When someone gives a reason that seems a bit odd, ask yourself: what would this look like if that was not the real reason?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend says she can\u2019t come to your party because of homework. But it is a Saturday, and she loves parties. What does this idea suggest?',options:['The reason she gave might not be the thing that made her say no','She must be lying to you on purpose to hurt you','Homework is always the real reason people stay at home'],answer:0,why:'The shown reason and the real one can differ. That does not mean anyone is lying. She may not know the real reason herself.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE STATED REASON',text:'A project you led is quietly shelved. The official reason is budget. The same month, a costlier project with a more senior sponsor sails through. Budget is what was said. It may not be what decided.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Separate what is presented from what produced it. Freud called the remembered dream the manifest content. He called the material it was built from the latent content. He treated the first as a product, not as evidence.'},{kind:'term',word:'Dream-work',meaning:'Freud\u2019s name for the process that turns the latent material into the dream you remember. To interpret a dream, for him, is to run that process backwards. It is not to read the images as they stand.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The audience sees a modest cottage. In the wings hang the ropes and counterweights that produce it. Freud\u2019s claim is that every dream has wings like these.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why he scorned dream books',text:'If a symbol meant the same for everyone, you could read a dream straight off a list. There would be nothing to analyse. Freud\u2019s method assumes the opposite. The meaning lives in what each image brings to the dreamer\u2019s mind, so only the dreamer can supply the key.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where the tool still works',text:'Whether dreams really work this way is disputed. Much of the theory has not survived. The distinction has. Look anywhere people report on themselves, from exit interviews to the stated reasons for a decision. What is presented is the product of something else.'},{kind:'try',text:'When a decision is justified oddly, ask what it would look like if the stated reason were not the reason. Then go and look for that.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A team keeps rejecting a new tool, each time for a different technical reason. Which response applies the distinction?',options:['Treat the reasons as the product, and look for what keeps producing them','Fix each technical objection in turn until none of them are left','Look up which objections usually signal resistance to change','Accept the objections, since the team knows its own reasons best'],answer:0,why:'When the reasons keep changing and the verdict does not, the reasons are manifest content. Looking up what objections usually mean is the dream-book mistake. It uses a fixed key instead of this team\u2019s material.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE CENTRAL DISTINCTION',text:'Freud divides every dream in two. The manifest content is the dream as the dreamer remembers and reports it. The latent content is the material he claims it was constructed from: thoughts, wishes and memories that do not appear in it directly.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The remembered dream is a product, not evidence. To interpret it you must reverse the process that made it, not read its images directly.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE DIRECTION OF TRAVEL',nodes:['Latent content: the dream-thoughts','The dream-work transforms them','Manifest content: the dream recalled','Interpretation runs it backwards'],note:'Skip the middle step and you are reading a product as if it were the source.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Against the dream books',text:'This is why he is scathing about the popular decoding method, where each image has a fixed meaning. If the key were universal, no transformation would be needed, and no analysis either. His method stands or falls on the meaning being specific to the dreamer\u2019s own material.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The audience gets the cottage. The wings hold the rigging that produced it. Interpretation, for Freud, means going backstage.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Two problems. The theory beneath the distinction, that the latent content is a disguised wish, is disputed and largely unsupported. And Freud did not hold his own line: later editions added a section on symbols with fairly fixed meanings, uncomfortably close to the dream books he had mocked. What survives is the structure: a report is a product.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of a report you rely on: a survey, a review, a colleague\u2019s explanation. What process turned the real material into the version you received?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these misuses the distinction between manifest and latent content?',options:['Deciding a colleague\u2019s stated reason is false because you have already guessed the real one','Asking what process produced a survey result before reading the numbers','Treating an exit interview as shaped by what leavers can safely say','Asking a dreamer what each image brings to mind before proposing a meaning'],answer:0,why:'The distinction licenses a question, not a verdict. Replacing the stated reason with your own guess skips the person\u2019s own material, the step the method depends on.'}]}},'interpretation-of-dreams_3':{pictureCaption:'The listener sits still, the chair pulled close. The clock is the cost: this method cannot be hurried.',bridge:'Ideas 1 and 2 said the material that matters is out of view. This is how Freud tried to reach it: by letting one person talk, at length.',basics:{title:'Keep listening past the first answer',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE WALK HOME',text:'Your friend says school was fine. You keep walking. Ten minutes later she says lunch was boring. Twenty minutes later she tells you someone was mean to her. That was the real news.'},{kind:'idea',text:'People don\u2019t say the big thing first. If you want the truth, let them talk for a long time, and take each bit seriously.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'A NEW KIND OF DOCTOR',text:'Sigmund Freud was a doctor in Vienna over a hundred years ago. He asked his patients to say whatever came into their heads. Even silly things. Even things that made them blush. Those most of all.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the silly bits?',text:'We all have a filter. It throws out the bits that seem silly or awkward before we speak. We don\u2019t even notice it working. Freud thought the hidden answers were in just those bits. So he asked people to switch the filter off.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The listener keeps still and keeps the chair close. The clock shows the cost. This kind of listening takes a long time.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time a friend seems upset, don\u2019t rush to fix it. Ask one question, then just listen. Wait longer than feels normal before you speak.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your little cousin says she is fine, but she seems sad. What does this idea tell you to do?',options:['Stay with her and let her keep talking','Ask her lots of quick yes or no questions','Take her word for it and talk about something else'],answer:0,why:'The real thing often comes out late. Quick questions let her pick what to say, and moving on stops the talk too soon.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE REVIEW MEETING',text:'A delivery went badly wrong. The one person who saw it unfold gives a clear five-minute account at the review. Everyone nods, writes down the cause and moves on. The account was honest. It was also prepared, and preparing it was a kind of filtering.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Whatever the theory\u2019s faults, the practice was new: sit with one person for hours and treat everything they say as data, especially what seems irrelevant. A person\u2019s own account is evidence, if you get past the prepared version.'},{kind:'term',word:'Free association',meaning:'Freud\u2019s method. The patient reports everything that comes to mind, without choosing, including what seems irrelevant, trivial or embarrassing.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHY THE FILTER MATTERS',nodes:['You ask what happened','They filter, without meaning to','The prepared account arrives','What was filtered stays missing'],note:'The filter works before anyone decides to hide anything. Let the speaker choose what to report and you will miss what it removes.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why there is no short version',text:'Freud was frank that this took hours, and that a single dream could fill several sessions. A shortcut would mean letting the speaker select again, which is the very thing the method exists to remove. Length is not a side effect. It is the correction.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Hands still, chair pulled close, and a clock that says what this costs. In this method the cost is the point.'},{kind:'try',text:'When only one person saw a problem first-hand, book an hour with them instead of surveying ten others. Keep going after their prepared account ends.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A customer cancels after five years, and only she knows why. Which approach follows this idea?',options:['A long, open conversation that continues past her first explanation','A short survey with a box for her main reason','Comparing her answer with a hundred other cancellations','Asking the three questions that best predict cancellations'],answer:0,why:'Short formats let her choose what to report, so they collect the filtered version. Only length gets past the account she prepared.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',word:'The fundamental rule',meaning:'Freud\u2019s later name for the instruction at the centre of free association: say everything that comes to mind, without selecting, and above all what seems irrelevant, trivial or embarrassing.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'VIENNA, THE 1890S',text:'Freud reached the method by giving up others. He had tried hypnosis, and pressing on patients\u2019 foreheads to summon memories. He settled on letting them talk while he listened. In the dream book he turns it on dreams: each fragment of a dream becomes a starting point for the dreamer\u2019s associations.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The material worth finding is exactly what ordinary filters exclude. Any procedure that lets the speaker select will miss it, and the only reliable correction is length.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The logic of the instruction',text:'The rule targets the filter, not the liar. People drop what seems trivial or embarrassing before they decide to withhold anything. The instruction reverses the filter\u2019s priorities: report precisely what you would leave out. There is no short version, because any shortcut reintroduces selection.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Stillness, a chair drawn close, and a clock. Here time is not overhead. It is how the filter is worn down.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Do not overstate the novelty. Doctors already took histories, and Freud\u2019s older colleague Josef Breuer had let a patient talk through her symptoms in the early 1880s. And listening at length does not make the listener neutral. What a long account means still depends on who interprets it, which is where the standard objection to Freud lands.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Where in your work does one person\u2019s report carry a decision? How long does anyone actually listen to them?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A hospital wants to learn from a near-miss that only one nurse witnessed. Which design best applies the method\u2019s logic?',options:['An unhurried interview that continues well past her first, prepared account','A structured form listing the ten most common causes of near-misses','A brief written statement from her, taken straight after the shift','A ward discussion where every member of staff offers a view'],answer:0,why:'The form and the brief statement let her select, and the ward discussion adds people who did not see it. Length with the one witness gets beneath the prepared account.'}]}},'interpretation-of-dreams_4':{pictureCaption:'The arrow landed first. Now a target is being painted round it. An answer made to fit after the fact can never miss.',bridge:'Idea 3\u2019s method leaves a lot to the listener\u2019s reading. The objection: if every answer counts in the analyst\u2019s favour, the theory cannot lose.',basics:{title:'An idea that can never lose',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE LUCKY SOCKS',prompt:'Sam wears his lucky socks and wins. Next week he wears them and loses. He says: *I\u2019d have lost by more without them.* Could anything prove the socks don\u2019t work?',options:['Yes, losing proves it','No, he has an answer for anything'],answer:1,reveal:'Win, and the socks worked. Lose, and they stopped it being worse. Each result agrees with him. That is a sign his idea says nothing.'},{kind:'idea',text:'If an idea can explain every result, even opposite ones, it is not strong. It has stopped saying anything you could check.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE CHARGE AGAINST FREUD',text:'Sigmund Freud told his patients what he thought their dreams meant. His critics said: if a patient agreed, Freud took it as proof. If the patient said no, he called it *resistance*, which means pushing back against the truth. So that was proof too.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'An idea that can lose',items:['Says what should happen','Could be shown wrong','Tells you something']},right:{title:'An idea that can\u2019t lose',items:['Fits any result','Can never be shown wrong','Tells you nothing']},note:'The left one takes a risk. That risk is what makes it worth trusting.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A PAINTED TARGET',picture:!0,text:'You shoot an arrow at a fence. Then you paint a target round it. Every shot is a bull\u2019s-eye. That says nothing about your aim.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time someone is very sure why something happened, ask: *what would show you were wrong?* If the answer is nothing, be careful.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend says her star sign reading is always right. When it fits, it\u2019s right. When it doesn\u2019t, she says she read it wrong. What is the problem?',options:['Nothing could ever count as the reading being wrong','Star sign readings are right some days and wrong others','She needs to read the reading more carefully each day'],answer:0,why:'The trouble is not a few misses. It is that she can explain away each miss, so the idea can never be tested.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE STRATEGY THAT CANNOT FAIL',text:'A manager says every success comes from his strategy. Every failure, he says, comes from the team carrying it out badly. Sales up: the strategy works. Sales down: the team did not follow it. Over three years no result has touched the strategy. From inside, this feels like being right about everything.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A theory arranged so that nothing could count against it is not strong. It has stopped making claims. This is the standard criticism of Freud. The same pattern turns up everywhere.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The Freud version',text:'If a patient accepted an interpretation, it was confirmed. If the patient rejected it, critics said, that was read as *resistance*, the mind guarding itself against the truth. So it was confirmed too. Yes and no pointed the same way.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'HOW MUCH IT RISKS',low:'Forbids nothing: fits any result',high:'Forbids a lot: one result could sink it',note:'The philosopher Karl Popper argued that a scientific theory earns our trust by forbidding outcomes and risking them. Psychoanalysis was one of his main examples of a theory that risked nothing.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it feels like strength',picture:!0,text:'Each new case fits, so confidence grows. But fitting was guaranteed in advance. The test is not whether the explanation fits what happened. It is whether it would have fitted the opposite just as well.'},{kind:'try',text:'Take the next confident explanation you hear, or give. Ask out loud: what result would show this was wrong? If the answer is nothing, treat it as a belief, not a finding.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A consultant says a reorganisation worked: morale rose, and where it fell, that was healthy adjustment. What is the strongest response?',options:['Ask what outcome would have shown it failed, before looking at more results','Accept it: both explanations describe reasonable things to happen','Ask for a larger survey so the morale figures are more reliable','Reject it: consultants always claim their own work succeeded'],answer:0,why:'Rise and fall both count as success, so more data cannot settle it. The useful question is what would have counted as failure.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE OBJECTION, PRECISELY',belief:'The objection to psychoanalysis is that its claims were tested and found false.',reality:'That is a different objection. The standard one is that, as practised, nothing could count against it: a patient\u2019s yes confirmed an interpretation, and a no was resistance, which confirmed it too.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A real theory forbids certain outcomes and gains its credibility by risking them. A theory that absorbs every result has not won. It has stopped making claims.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'POPPER\u2019S CONTRAST',text:'Karl Popper, the Austrian-born philosopher of science, set the psychologies of Freud and Alfred Adler against Einstein\u2019s physics. Einstein\u2019s theory predicted something that could have come out wrong. The psychologies could explain any behaviour at all, and their followers took that as strength. Popper took it as the flaw.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A target drawn before the shot can be missed. This one is painted afterwards, round wherever the arrow fell, and so forbids nothing.'},{kind:'term',word:'Falsifiable',meaning:'Able to be shown false by some possible observation. Not the same as false: a falsifiable claim may well be true. It simply says something definite enough to be caught out.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE SAME SHAPE TODAY',text:'A manager credits every success to his strategy and every failure to poor execution. No possible quarter could reach the strategy. It feels, from inside, like being right about everything, which is what makes the shape so durable.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The objection needs precision in both directions. Freud did answer it: late in life he argued that neither a yes nor a no was taken at face value, and that confirmation came from what the patient produced afterwards. And the philosopher Adolf Gr\xfcnbaum argued that some Freudian claims are testable, and fail. That is a harsher verdict than Popper\u2019s, not a kinder one.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Pick one explanation you rely on at work. What result, next quarter, would make you abandon it?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE STRUCTURE',prompt:'Which of these explanations is unfalsifiable in exactly the way described?',options:['If the child behaves, the method worked; if not, the parents applied it badly','The new drug will lower blood pressure more than the old one in trials','Customers will leave if the price rises by more than ten per cent','This bridge design will carry forty tonnes without measurable bending'],answer:0,why:'The other three forbid outcomes and could be caught out. The parenting claim credits success to the method and failure to its users, so no result can reach it.'}]}},'interpretation-of-dreams_5':{pictureCaption:'A hand on the door, the lunchbox left behind again. The ring worn under it says this is a pattern, not a one-off.',bridge:'Ideas 1 and 2 said behaviour has sources out of view. Slips are the everyday case: the moments a hidden source shows through.',basics:{title:'A mistake that keeps happening is a clue',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'EVERY TUESDAY',text:'Tuesday is swimming day. For the third week in a row, you leave your swim bag at home. You never forget your bag on Thursday. That is football day, and you love football.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'Is it just bad luck?',options:['Yes, people forget things all the time','Maybe the forgetting is telling you something'],answer:1,reveal:'One lost bag could be bad luck. Three, always on swim days, is a pattern. Maybe part of you does not want to go.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A lunchbox left by the door again. See the worn ring under it? It has been left there many times. That is a pattern, not bad luck.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Mistakes are not always random. When the same slip keeps happening around the same thing, the pattern is a clue about how you feel.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO NOTICED THIS',text:'Sigmund Freud, a famous doctor, collected slips. A name you forget. A wrong word that pops out. A key you lose. He thought each one pointed to something the person did not want to say.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Each slip has a secret meaning.',reality:'Freud often went too far. Sometimes you forget a name just because you are tired. One slip tells you very little. The same slip again and again is worth a look.'},{kind:'try',text:'Is there a thing you keep forgetting or getting wrong? Instead of fixing it again, ask: what do all these times have in common?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You keep losing your piano music, but only in the week of a lesson. What does this idea suggest?',options:['Ask yourself how you really feel about piano lessons','Buy a brighter folder so the music is easy to find','Nothing: losing things is just random bad luck'],answer:0,why:'A brighter folder fixes one time. The pattern, always in lesson week, is the clue worth listening to.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE THIRD REMINDER',text:'You have forgotten to send the same report three times. Each time you set a new reminder. Each time something else came up. You have not yet asked why it is always this report, for this person.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Errors are not always noise. A repeated slip around one subject is information about that subject. Patterns in your mistakes can be more honest than your intentions.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Hand on the door, lunchbox left behind, and a ring worn into the varnish where it has sat before. One lapse is noise. The ring is the pattern.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Freud\u2019s claim',text:'Freud argued that a forgotten name, a misspoken word or a mislaid object is produced by something, usually an intention that was not going to be stated. He set this out most fully in a book published soon after the dream book, *The Psychopathology of Everyday Life*.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Fixing the instance',items:['Set another reminder','Blame being busy','Same slip next month']},right:{title:'Reading the pattern',items:['Ask what the slips share','Notice the person or topic involved','Deal with that']},note:'The right-hand column needs no theory of the mind, only the willingness to treat your own errors as evidence.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The honest caveat',text:'Freud overread his examples. Sometimes a name goes because you are tired, and he was not entitled to every interpretation he offered. The defensible version is weaker: one slip means little, but errors that cluster around one subject are telling you something about it.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick the mistake you have fixed most often this year. Instead of fixing it again, note when it happens, and what or who it is near.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You keep double-booking meetings with one particular client, and never with anyone else. Which response uses this idea?',options:['Ask what about this client you might be avoiding','Switch to a calendar app with better clash alerts','Conclude it is chance, since double-booking is common','Decide the slip proves that you dislike the client'],answer:0,why:'The pattern points at the client, which is worth asking about. Deciding it *proves* dislike is Freud\u2019s overreach: a slip raises a question, it does not settle one.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'A NAME HE COULD NOT FIND',text:'On a journey in the late 1890s, Freud could not recall the name of the painter Signorelli, and other painters\u2019 names kept arriving instead. He traced the lapse to a subject he had just pushed out of a conversation, death, and later made it the opening case of *The Psychopathology of Everyday Life* (1901).'},{kind:'idea',text:'Slips are overdetermined, not random. A forgotten name or a wrong word is produced by something, often an intention that was not going to be stated, and the pattern is readable even when a single instance is not.'},{kind:'term',word:'Overdetermined',meaning:'Freud\u2019s word for an event with more than one cause converging on it. A slip can come from tiredness and from an unstated wish at once. Finding one cause does not rule out the other.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where the signal is',text:'The argument does not rest on any single slip. Slips happen at particular moments, around particular people. One instance is ambiguous, but a set of instances is legible: errors cluster non-randomly, and the cluster points at its subject.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One forgotten lunchbox is ambiguous. The ring worn into the varnish is not: it records a cluster, and the cluster points at what is being left behind.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'WHERE TO STAND',low:'Every slip reveals a hidden intention',high:'Every slip is random noise',note:'Freud sat near the left end. The defensible position lies between: single slips are ambiguous, clusters carry information.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Freud overread his examples, finding a hidden intention almost wherever he looked, and he was not entitled to many of the interpretations he offered. Students of speech errors, before and since, explain many slips by sounds and words that resemble each other, and fatigue explains many more. What remains defensible is the pattern, not his mechanism.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What error have you corrected more than twice this year? What, or who, was nearby each time?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'A sceptic says most slips are just tiredness, so Freud was wrong about slips. What is the best reply?',options:['Granted for single slips, but repeated errors around one subject still carry information','Tiredness is itself a sign of a hidden wish, so the slip still counts','Freud showed that no slip is ever caused by tiredness alone','The sceptic is right, so patterns in errors can safely be ignored'],answer:0,why:'The defensible claim concedes the point about single slips and keeps the weaker one about clusters. Calling tiredness a hidden wish is the cannot-lose move from idea 4.'}]}},'interpretation-of-dreams_6':{pictureCaption:'Several engraved plates stacked under one sheet. Whatever prints will be all of them at once. That is condensation.',bridge:'Idea 2 named the dream-work, the process that turns hidden material into the dream you remember. These are its two main tools.',basics:{title:'Squashed together, and moved over',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'A STRANGE DREAM',text:'You dream about your teacher. But she has your aunt\u2019s voice and your coach\u2019s whistle. She is three people at once. In the dream, that seemed quite normal.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Freud said dreams do two tricks. They squash several things into one picture. And they move a big feeling onto something small.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'TWO BIG WORDS',text:'Sigmund Freud, a doctor who studied dreams, gave the tricks names. Squashing is *condensation*. Moving a feeling is *displacement*. You can use both words without taking on all his ideas about dreams.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Several printing plates are stacked under one sheet of paper. What prints is all of them at once. That is trick one.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'TRICK TWO, AWAKE',text:'Your sister shouts at you for using her hairbrush. It is only a hairbrush. But she has had a bad week at school. The big feeling from school moved onto the small hairbrush.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time a small thing makes you very cross, ask: is this feeling really about this? Or did it come from somewhere else?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend cries hard when his pencil snaps. You know his dog is ill. What might be going on?',options:['The sad feeling about his dog moved onto the pencil','He is upset because it was his best pencil','He thinks a snapped pencil means bad luck'],answer:0,why:'A big feeling can move onto a small thing nearby. The pencil is where the feeling landed, not where it came from.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'ONE COMPLAINT, FOUR GRIEVANCES',text:'A colleague says the team never answers his messages. Ask more and the story grows. There was the missed promotion. There was the moved desk, and a meeting he was left out of. And yes, some messages did go unanswered. Four grievances, arriving fused as one.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The dream-work has two main moves. Condensation packs several things into one image. Displacement moves the feeling from what matters onto something trivial nearby.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Several plates under one sheet: what prints is all of them at once. That is condensation, and why a dream figure can be two people.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Displacement',text:'Displacement moves the weight of feeling off the thing that matters. It lands on something small and close by. That is why dreams so often feel loaded with meaning about nothing. It is also why the most upsetting dream of your week can be about a filing cabinet.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Condensation',items:['Several sources, one image','A figure who is two people','Many grievances, one complaint']},right:{title:'Displacement',items:['Feeling moved sideways','Dread about a trivial object','Fury at a small, nearby issue']}},{kind:'explain',title:'Why Freud needed them',text:'Together they let him say the dream you remember is compressed and misdirected. It is not just strange. The strangeness is the mark of the compression. The concepts have outlived that theory. You can use either one without accepting anything about dreams.'},{kind:'try',text:'When a small thing has caused a large reaction, in you or in someone else, ask where the feeling might have travelled from.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'After a tense quarter, your manager erupts over a typo in a slide. Which reading uses displacement?',options:['The feeling belongs to something larger, and landed on the typo','Typos simply matter more to her than to most people','She is fusing several separate worries into one image','The outburst shows the slides were badly prepared'],answer:0,why:'Displacement is feeling moving from a large issue to a small, nearby one. Fusing several worries into one is condensation, the other move.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',word:'Condensation',meaning:'One element of the remembered dream standing for several hidden ones at once: a figure who is simultaneously your colleague and your brother, carrying features of both.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The dream-work compresses and misdirects. Condensation fuses several things into one image; displacement moves emotional weight from what matters onto something adjacent and trivial. The strangeness of dreams is the signature of both.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Plates stacked under one sheet. What prints is a single image made of all of them, which is condensation exactly.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Displacement',text:'The second operation moves the charge. Feeling that belongs to something important is transferred onto something adjacent and trivial, which is why a dream can feel intensely significant about nothing. Freud reads the mismatch between feeling and content as a clue to where the feeling came from.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How the concepts escaped',text:'Both outlived the theory by escaping it. Several grievances arriving fused into one complaint is condensation. Disproportionate anger at a small issue while the large one goes unmentioned is displacement. Displacement passed into later psychology as one of the standard defence mechanisms.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Used carelessly, both are licences to read anything into anything. If any image can stand for several things, and any feeling can have come from elsewhere, an interpreter can reach whatever meaning they arrived with. The concepts describe something real when the source can be checked, by asking. They do not prove a hidden source exists.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of the last disproportionate reaction you had. What larger thing was nearby in time?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TELL THEM APART',prompt:'Which of these is condensation rather than displacement?',options:['A dream figure with your boss\u2019s voice and your father\u2019s coat','Dread in a dream about a misplaced stapler','A row about a cup on the day of redundancy news','Panic over an email\u2019s tone the day of a diagnosis'],answer:0,why:'Condensation fuses several sources into one element. The other three move a large feeling onto a small, nearby object, which is displacement.'}]}},'interpretation-of-dreams_7':{pictureCaption:'The patient\u2019s couch is empty. The open notebook lies on the doctor\u2019s own chair: in the first case, the doctor is the subject.',bridge:'Idea 3 described the method. Here Freud demonstrates it, and his first specimen is himself.',basics:{title:'He tested it on himself first',beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'SUMMER, 1895',text:'In 1895, Sigmund Freud had a dream about a patient he called Irma. In his book, it is the first dream he picks apart in full. And it does not make him look good.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHAT HE FOUND',text:'Irma was not fully well. Freud was worried he had treated her badly. In the dream, it turns out to be another doctor\u2019s fault. Freud saw that the dream let him off the hook. Then he put that in his book.'},{kind:'idea',text:'If you want people to trust your method, try it on yourself first. Show them what it found, even the parts you would rather hide.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The couch for patients is empty. The open notebook sits on the doctor\u2019s own chair. This time, the doctor is the one being studied.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A COOK',text:'A cook who won\u2019t eat her own food makes you wonder why. A cook who tastes it first, in front of you, makes you trust the dish.'},{kind:'try',text:'Got a new trick for doing homework faster? Try it on your own homework first. Then tell your friend what went wrong, as well as what went right.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Mia has a new way to learn spellings. How can she best show that it works?',options:['Try it on her own spellings first, and share how it went','Pick the best speller in class and test it on him','Tell people it works, since it worked in her head'],answer:0,why:'Testing it on herself first means nobody can say she picked an easy case. Sharing the bad parts too is what Freud did.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'BRAVE, OR CAREFUL?',belief:'Freud put his own dream first as an act of courage.',reality:'It took courage, but it was just as much a matter of method. In his approach the dreamer\u2019s associations are the evidence, and only the dreamer has them. The one specimen with the inside visible was himself.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When you introduce a method, demonstrate it on your own material first. It is the demonstration least open to the charge of picking a convenient case, and most convincing when it shows you at your worst.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'IRMA\u2019S INJECTION',text:'The book\u2019s first full worked example is a dream Freud had in July 1895 about a patient he calls Irma, whose treatment had not fully succeeded. Analysing it, he finds the dream clearing him of blame and pinning it on a colleague. Then he publishes that.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Demonstrate on someone else',items:['A patient\u2019s case breaches confidence','A stranger\u2019s dream comes without associations','Readers may suspect an easy case']},right:{title:'Demonstrate on yourself',items:['Nothing confidential to breach','Every association is available','An unflattering result is hard to fake']}},{kind:'picture',caption:'An empty couch, and the notebook open on the doctor\u2019s own chair. The first case in the book is the author.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before you ask a team to adopt a process, run it on your own last project in front of them, including what it shows you got wrong.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You want your team to start running blameless reviews of failed projects. What is the strongest first demonstration?',options:['Run one on a project of yours that went badly, in front of them','Run one on a project that went well, so it feels safe','Share a well-known company\u2019s review as a model to copy','Run one on a colleague\u2019s failure, with their permission'],answer:0,why:'Your own failure is the case nobody can say you chose for convenience, and it shows you will accept what the method finds.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'THE SPECIMEN DREAM',text:'In July 1895 Freud dreamt of Irma, a patient whose treatment had only partly worked. In the dream a colleague turns out to have given her an injection with an unclean syringe. Freud\u2019s analysis, the book\u2019s first worked example, shows the dream acquitting him and blaming others, and he publishes it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'He demonstrates the method on himself first, publishing the self-serving content of his own dream. It sets a standard rather than proving a result: readers are asked to accept nothing he has not applied to himself.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it had to be him',text:'The choice is methodological. A patient\u2019s dream could not be laid out in full without breaching confidence. A stranger\u2019s dream could not be analysed at all, because the associations are the evidence and only the dreamer has them. The only specimen with the inside visible was his own.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The couch is empty and the notebook lies open on the analyst\u2019s chair. Analyst and subject are one person, which is the strength and the flaw.'},{kind:'keyline',text:'An unflattering result is the hardest kind to accuse of having been chosen.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Analyst and subject are the same person, which Freud acknowledges and cannot escape. Nobody else can check his associations, so the interpretation cannot be tested. He also says openly that he has not reported everything the dream brought up, so even this specimen was edited. It sets a standard of candour. It proves nothing about dreams.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which method do you ask others to follow that you have never visibly applied to your own work?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'A critic says the Irma analysis proves nothing, since Freud was both analyst and subject. What is the fairest response?',options:['Granted: it shows the method and his candour, not that the theory is true','Wrong: self-analysis is more objective, because he knew all the facts','Wrong: publishing an unflattering dream proves the reading is right','Granted: so demonstrating on yourself has no value for a method'],answer:0,why:'The critic is right about proof and wrong if they conclude it is worthless. Its value is as a standard of candour, not as evidence.'}]}},'interpretation-of-dreams_8':{pictureCaption:'One scrap lifted from a basket of yesterday\u2019s rubbish. Freud thought dreams were built from scraps like this, chosen because nobody would notice them.',bridge:'Idea 6 showed feeling moving onto trivial things. This is where the trivial things come from: yesterday.',basics:{title:'Dreams borrow from yesterday',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TONIGHT\u2019S DREAM',prompt:'Freud asked what tonight\u2019s dream is most likely to use. What did he think?',options:['The biggest thing that happened today','A small thing you barely noticed today'],answer:1,reveal:'He thought small bits, like a dog you passed or a word on a poster. Dreams do borrow recent bits. Whether small ones win over big ones is less sure.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Dreams are built from leftovers of the day before. Freud thought the small bits were picked for a reason: they could carry a big feeling without being noticed.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'A NAME FOR IT',text:'Sigmund Freud was a doctor who studied dreams. He called these leftovers the *day\u2019s residue*. Residue means what is left over, like crumbs on a plate after tea.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A hand picks one scrap out of a bin full of rubbish. Dreams, Freud said, are made from scraps like this.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A PLAIN BAG',text:'Carry a present in shiny paper and everyone asks what it is. Carry it in a plain old shopping bag and nobody looks twice. Freud thought dreams use dull bits the same way, to carry something quietly.'},{kind:'try',text:'For a week, jot down what you dream. Then think back to the day before. Which small thing turned up?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You dream about a red bike you walked past yesterday. What would Freud say?',options:['The bike is a small leftover from your day, maybe carrying something else','You secretly want a red bike more than anything','The dream means nothing, because the bike was not big news'],answer:0,why:'The bike is the day\u2019s residue. For Freud, a small leftover like this was a carrier. The interesting question is what it carried.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE CUP',text:'There is an unwashed cup by the sink. Within a minute it has become a row about who does everything in this house. The row runs an hour. Nobody mentions the cup again, and nothing is settled.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Freud saw dreams as built from yesterday\u2019s trivial leftovers. His claim is that trivial material is chosen precisely because it carries no charge of its own. That makes it a safe carrier for something that does.'},{kind:'term',word:'The day\u2019s residue',meaning:'Freud\u2019s name for the recent, neutral impressions that dreams are mostly built from. Think of a face on a train, a phrase in a newspaper, a half-noticed detail of the day before.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHY THE TRIVIAL GETS PICKED',nodes:['A charged thought cannot appear openly','It borrows a trivial fragment','The fragment passes unremarked','The dream gets through'],note:'A dream made of weighty material would give away its subject. One made of yesterday\u2019s rubbish arrives looking random.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The same pattern, awake',text:'The cup was never the subject. A trivial occasion carried something that could not be raised directly. That is why arguing about the cup settles nothing. Outsized irritation at a small process failure often works the same way.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One scrap lifted out of a basket of the day\u2019s rubbish and held to the light. The question is not what it is, but what it is carrying.'},{kind:'try',text:'For a week, note which fragment of the day before turns up in your dreams. The pattern is more interesting than any single dream.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'The week after a painful round of job cuts, a team\u2019s review meeting spends forty minutes on the office printer. What does this idea suggest?',options:['The printer may be carrying feeling that cannot be raised directly','The printer is plainly the team\u2019s most pressing problem','The team has moved on and is focusing on practical matters','Review meetings should ban talk about office equipment'],answer:0,why:'A trivial topic taking up far too much time, right after something large, is the residue pattern. Fixing the printer will not end the discussion.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE OBSERVATION',text:'Freud notes that dreams draw heavily on the day before the dream, and especially on its trivial, half-noticed impressions rather than the things that mattered. That recent material turns up is an observation anyone can check against their own nights. That it is mostly trivial is less certain.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Dreams borrow yesterday\u2019s indifferent fragments because those fragments carry no charge of their own. That makes them available as cover for something older that does.'},{kind:'explain',title:'His question',text:'Why the trivial, when the day held things that mattered? His answer: unimportant fragments are precisely what can pass without objection. The apparent randomness is a feature. A dream built from significant material would announce its subject. One built from rubbish gets through.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A single scrap held up from a basket of rubbish. The observation is the basket. The theory is that this scrap was chosen as cover.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO CLAIMS, TWO VERDICTS',left:{title:'The observation',items:['Dreams use recent material','Easy to check, and holds up','That it is mostly trivial is less sure']},right:{title:'The explanation',items:['Trivial bits are selected','They are cover for older material','Hard to test, weakly supported']}},{kind:'limits',text:'Later dream research broadly confirms that recent waking experience turns up in dreams, though emotionally significant events may surface more readily than trivial ones. It offers no comparable support for the second claim, that trivial material is selected as cover. Simpler accounts are available: the sleeping brain may reuse loose recent fragments for reasons that have nothing to do with disguise. Keep the observation; hold the explanation loosely.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of the last row you saw about something small. What larger thing could not be raised that day?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SEPARATE THE CLAIMS',prompt:'A friend dreamt about a stranger\u2019s umbrella from her train, and concludes the dream must be hiding something deep. What is the most accurate response?',options:['The umbrella fits the day\u2019s residue; that it hides something is Freud\u2019s unproven extra step','She is right: trivial dream material always conceals something deeper','The umbrella cannot be day\u2019s residue, since it meant nothing to her','Dreams are random, so recent material turning up in them is coincidence'],answer:0,why:'That recent material appears in dreams holds up. That it is chosen as cover is the part that remains unproven.'}]}},'interpretation-of-dreams_9':{pictureCaption:'A plank patched, then patched again over the patch. A theory can absorb its hardest case this way, for a while.',bridge:'Idea 4 was the charge that the theory could not lose. This is the case where Freud came closest to losing, and what he did about it.',basics:{title:'The case that does not fit',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A PUZZLE FOR FREUD',prompt:'Sigmund Freud said each dream is a wish coming true. Which dream is hardest for that idea?',options:['A dream about flying','A nightmare','A dream about lunch'],answer:1,reveal:'Nobody wishes to be scared stiff. So if each dream is a wish, bad dreams are a real problem. Freud knew it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'You learn the most about an idea from the case it can\u2019t explain. What counts is what you do when that case turns up.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'HIS FIRST ANSWER',text:'Freud\u2019s first answer was clever. He said the wish belongs to a part of you that the rest of you does not agree with. So when that wish comes true in a dream, it feels scary.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A plank is patched, then patched again over the patch. A clever answer can keep covering a problem like this. The hole is still there.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'HIS LATER ANSWER',text:'Years later, after a terrible war, Freud learned of soldiers who dreamt about the fighting, night after night. Their dreams were not wishes at all. Freud said so. He admitted these dreams did not fit, and went looking for a new idea.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you argue for something, ask yourself: would my answer still work if the facts were the other way round? If so, it is a patch.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Ava thinks the bus is always late on Mondays. This Monday it comes early. What is the honest thing to do?',options:['Admit her rule was wrong this time and look again','Say it was early because the driver was new','Say early counts as late, because it was not on time'],answer:0,why:'The other two save the rule by bending the facts. Those are patches. Freud\u2019s best moment was admitting a case that did not fit.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE CASE IN THE ROOM',text:'You argue that remote work raises output. A colleague mentions the team whose output fell. You could say they did remote work badly. Notice that this answer would work against any team, whatever had happened.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A theory is tested by what its author does with the case it cannot explain. For Freud\u2019s wish theory that case was the anxiety dream, and how he handled it is worth more than most of his interpretations.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The first answer',text:'If every dream fulfils a wish, nightmares are a problem: nobody wishes for terror. Freud\u2019s first answer was that the wish belongs to a part of the mind the dreamer is at odds with, so its fulfilment registers as dread. Ingenious, and able to absorb almost any counter-example.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Patch over patch. Each repair holds, and each makes the next one easier to justify. That is how a theory absorbs a case it should lose to.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The second answer',text:'After the First World War he had to account for soldiers whose dreams replayed the same horror night after night. He conceded that these did not fit the wish theory, and looked for a different mechanism, beyond what he called the pleasure principle.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Patching',items:['Redefine the claim to fit','Every result now agrees','The theory stops risking anything']},right:{title:'Conceding',items:['Admit the case does not fit','Look for another mechanism','The theory keeps its meaning']}},{kind:'try',text:'When you defend an idea against its hardest case, check whether your answer would have accommodated the opposite result too. If it would, it is a patch.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Your pricing model predicted a discount would raise sales. Sales fell. Which response is the honest equivalent of Freud\u2019s concession?',options:['Say the model does not explain this case, and look for what does','Say sales would have fallen further without the discount','Say customers valued the discount but did not show it yet','Redefine success as brand awareness rather than sales'],answer:0,why:'The other three would fit any outcome. Only admitting the model does not explain this case lets the result count against it.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'timeline',kicker:'TWO ANSWERS, TWO DECADES',points:[{when:'1899',what:'The dream book: every dream is a wish fulfilled, distressing dreams included'},{when:'1914\u201318',what:'The First World War leaves soldiers with dreams that replay their terror'},{when:'1920',what:'In *Beyond the Pleasure Principle*, Freud concedes these dreams are an exception'}],note:'The concession is in a later book, not the dream book. It shows what he did when the hard case would not go away.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The anxiety dream is the strongest objection to the wish theory, and the theory raises it itself. Freud first absorbed it. Later, faced with dreams he could not absorb, he admitted the exception.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The first answer',text:'If the wish belongs to a part of the mind the dreamer rejects, its fulfilment would feel like dread. The answer is ingenious, and it is the kind that can absorb any counter-example: pleasure confirms the wish, and so does terror. That is the structure of idea 4, applied by Freud to his own theory.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A patch over a patch. Each repair keeps the water out, and each makes the theory a little less able to lose.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The concession',text:'The repetitive dreams of traumatised soldiers would not yield. Freud did not redefine the wish until they fitted. He admitted they did not, and proposed a compulsion to repeat that operated beyond the pleasure principle. That is the intellectually honest moment in the work.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Do not overstate the honesty. The concession came two decades on, in another book, and the replacement, a compulsion to repeat tied in the same book to a proposed death drive, has been criticised as just as hard to test as what it replaced. Admitting an exception is honest. Answering it with another untestable mechanism is still a patch, only a bigger one.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What is the hardest case against an idea you hold? Would your usual answer to it have fitted the opposite result too?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'JUDGE THE DEFENCE',prompt:'A theory predicts that a training programme raises scores. Scores fall. Which defence is the concession rather than the patch?',options:['Our theory does not cover this group; we need a different explanation for it','Scores fell because the programme exposed hidden weaknesses, which confirms it','The fall shows the programme working at a level tests cannot detect','Scores would have fallen further without it, exactly as the theory implies'],answer:0,why:'The other three would fit a rise as well as a fall. Only saying the theory does not cover this group lets the result count against it.'}]}}}},2977,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'laelius-on-friendship_1':{pictureCaption:'Two chairs drawn up to a bare table. Nothing to trade, nothing to work on. Whatever brings these two here, it is not a deal.',basics:{title:'Friends are not tools',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'YOUR BEST FRIEND',prompt:'Think of your best friend. Why are you two friends?',options:['Because they help me out','Because I like who they are'],answer:1,reveal:'Friends do help each other. But the help is not why you are friends. You liked them first. The help came after.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Real friends are not people you keep because they are useful. You like who they are. The help comes after the liking, not before it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two chairs at a bare table. No homework, no game, no swap. Two people sit here just to be near each other.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'A ROMAN WHO NOTICED',text:'Cicero was a famous thinker in Rome over 2,000 years ago. He spotted something odd. If we made friends only to get help, the people who need the least help would have the fewest friends. But they often have lots.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'NOT AN UMBRELLA',text:'You only pick up an umbrella when it rains. On sunny days it sits in the corner. If you only call a friend when you need something, you are treating them like an umbrella.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'AFTER THE TEST',text:'Sam helped you with maths all term. Now the test is over and you don\u2019t need help. Do you still want to sit with Sam at lunch? If yes, that was a real friendship. If not, it was more like a deal.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Maya has moved up a class. She doesn\u2019t need her old reading buddy now. What would show it was a real friendship?',options:['She still plays with her buddy at break','She thanks her buddy and moves on','She finds a new buddy in her new class'],answer:0,why:'A real friendship does not stop when the help stops. The liking was there all along.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'A GOOD YEAR, A QUIET ONE',text:'It has been a self-sufficient year. You sorted out your money, learned to cook, stopped needing lifts. You also notice you see people a lot less. Nobody fell out. The diary just emptied.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Friendship does not come from need. It comes from seeing something in another person worth being near. The benefits follow the friendship. They do not cause it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Cicero\u2019s test case',text:'If friendship grew out of need, the most self-sufficient people would have the fewest friends. Cicero thought the opposite was closer to true. People who need little are often good at friendship, because nothing else is mixed into it.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Friendship',items:['Starts with liking who they are','Help follows the liking','Survives when the help stops']},right:{title:'Alliance',items:['Starts with what they provide','Liking follows the help','Ends when the help ends']},note:'Cicero has nothing against alliances. They are respectable. They are just a different thing.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What a change reveals',text:'When someone can no longer give you what they used to, a relationship built on that is not damaged. It is revealed. The test runs the other way too. If you need people less and see them less, ask which has really changed: the need, or the liking.'},{kind:'try',text:'Name three people you see less this year. For each, ask: did I stop needing them, or stop liking them? If it was only the need, get in touch this week.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A neighbour drove you to work for years. Your new job is a walk away, and you have stopped dropping in. What does Cicero\u2019s test ask?',options:['Whether the friendship was ever about more than the lifts','Whether you still owe them for all those years of driving','Whether they now need a favour from you in return','Whether it is normal to lose touch after changing jobs'],answer:0,why:'If only the need has gone, the friendship has not, and it deserves a visit. If the lifts were all of it, it was an alliance, and that is worth knowing too.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'ROME, 129 BCE',text:'The dialogue is spoken mostly by Gaius Laelius, days after the death of his lifelong friend Scipio, the general who destroyed Carthage. Laelius asks whether Scipio ever needed him. Not at all, he answers, and he did not need Scipio. Each loved the other for his character.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Friendship arises from goodness recognised in another person, not from usefulness. We do not love because we expect benefit. Benefit follows because we love.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What he is arguing against',text:'A view current in his day held that friendship grows from weakness: we seek friends to supply what we lack. Cicero\u2019s reply is a prediction. If that were so, the most self-sufficient would have the fewest friends. The reverse is closer to the truth.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE DIRECTION OF THE INFERENCE',nodes:['You recognise good character','Affection forms','Benefits follow'],note:'Reverse the arrows, so that expected benefit produces the affection, and you have an alliance. Respectable, and a different thing.'},{kind:'term',word:'Virtue',meaning:'For Cicero here, goodness of character in its everyday form: honesty, constancy, decency. Not a sage\u2019s perfection. It is what you recognise when you admire someone, as opposed to what they can do for you.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two chairs at a bare table, nothing exchanged and nothing being worked on. What holds these two is not a transaction.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The line is cleaner on paper than in life. Most friendships begin in shared use: a desk, a team, a school run. And a friend who never helps when it matters fails Cicero\u2019s standard too, since benefits are meant to follow. The test is not whether help flows, but whether help is the reason.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE ALLIANCE',prompt:'Which case shows an alliance rather than a friendship in Cicero\u2019s sense?',options:['Two colleagues stop speaking the week one of them leaves the firm','Two friends who first met because they shared a desk at work','A friend lends you money, and you pay it back promptly','A wealthy man with no needs who keeps several close friends'],answer:0,why:'It ended the moment the use did. Meeting through work, or trading help, fits friendship well enough. Ending with the use is what reveals an alliance.'}]}},'laelius-on-friendship_2':{pictureCaption:'A hand holds up a mirror curved to slim and straighten whoever looks in. Pleasant to look into. Not true.',bridge:'Idea 1 said a friend is valued for who they are. Here is the commonest way that value is quietly emptied out.',basics:{title:'A friend who always agrees',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'AFTER THE CONCERT',text:'You sing a solo at the school concert. One friend says it was perfect. Another says the high note wobbled, and you should take a breath before it. The first one was much nicer to hear.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'Which friend helped you more?',options:['The one who said it was perfect','The one who told you about the note'],answer:1,reveal:'The first friend made you feel good for a minute. The second gave you something you can use. Next time, the note won\u2019t wobble.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Flattery means only saying nice things, true or not. It feels like friendship. But it takes away the best thing a friend can give you: the truth.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'This mirror is bent to make you look taller and thinner. It is nice to look into. It just isn\u2019t true.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'A good friend always agrees with you.',reality:'A good friend tells you the truth, kindly. Anyone can agree with you. Only a real friend will tell you when you are wrong.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'A ROMAN WARNING',text:'Cicero, a thinker in old Rome, called flattery a disease of friendship. It is sneaky, because it feels so nice. He also said it only works on people who want it. So part of the cure is up to you.'},{kind:'try',text:'Ask a friend one thing you could do better, in a game or a sport. Then just say thanks. Notice how hard it was to ask.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend shows you a story she wrote. It is good, but the ending is muddled. What would a real friend do?',options:['Say what you liked, and that the ending lost you','Say it is perfect so that she feels happy','Say nothing at all so you don\u2019t upset her'],answer:0,why:'Kind and true can go together. Saying only nice things would leave her ending muddled.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THREE FRIENDS, ONE PLAN',prompt:'You are thinking of quitting your job to start a business. Which friend do you find yourself calling first?',options:['The one who will be thrilled for you','The one who will ask hard questions','The one who knows the trade best'],answer:0,reveal:'Most of us call the one who will be thrilled. Nobody asked them to lie. We simply chose the friend most likely to agree.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Flattery is the disease of friendship because it feels like friendship while it happens. It keeps the warmth and removes the one thing only a friend will give you: an honest second opinion.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it outranks a quarrel',text:'A quarrel is visibly a problem, so it gets dealt with. Flattery is pleasant, so it never does. The friendship keeps its form, the dinners and warm messages, while what made it worth having has been taken out.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'The flatterer',items:['Agrees with the plan','Leaves you feeling wonderful','Costs you nothing today']},right:{title:'The friend',items:['Asks where it could fail','Leaves you a little uneasy','Saves you something later']},note:'Both are good company. Only one is doing a friend\u2019s job.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Cicero blames the listener',text:'He is harder on the flattered than on the flatterer. Flattery only works on someone who wants it. If the agreeable friends are the ones you seek out, you have selected for flattery without asking anyone to lie.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The mirror is held up obligingly, and curved to slim and straighten whoever stands before it. That is the service a flatterer offers.'},{kind:'try',text:'Ask one person close to you: what do you think I am getting wrong at the moment? Then only listen. Notice how hard the question was to ask.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Every friend you showed your plan to loved it. By Cicero\u2019s test, what should you check first?',options:['Whether you showed it to anyone likely to disagree','Whether the plan is as good as they all said','Whether your friends know enough about the field','Whether you have thanked them all for the support'],answer:0,why:'Praise from everyone may say more about whom you asked than about the plan. Cicero\u2019s test looks at your choice of listeners.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHERE THE ESSAY LINGERS',text:'Of all the threats to friendship, Cicero spends longest on flattery, and calls it the disease of friendship. Quarrels, rivalry and changes of fortune all get less of his attention. The reason lies in how each one feels while it is happening.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Flattery is the failure that passes for friendship. A friend\u2019s value lies largely in being the one person who will tell you the truth, so a friend who flatters keeps the form and removes the substance.'},{kind:'term',word:'Flattery',meaning:'Not praise, which may be true. Cicero means agreement offered because it pleases: telling people what they want to hear in order to stay in their favour.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the flattered are to blame',text:'Cicero puts most of the fault on the listener. Flattery only takes hold where it is welcome, in someone who enjoys hearing it. The remedy is less a matter of finding honest friends than of becoming a person to whom honesty can be spoken.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW NOBODY HAS TO LIE',nodes:['You enjoy agreement','You seek out the agreeable','Critics are seen less','Only praise reaches you'],loop:!0,note:'No one in this loop is dishonest. Your choice of company does all the work.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The test can be abused. Some people call cruelty honesty and treat all warmth as suspect. Encouragement is not flattery when it is true, and a friend can be right to back a risky plan. The question is whether their agreement survives their honest opinion, not whether they ever agree.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Whose opinions do you seek most often? When did each of them last tell you something you did not want to hear?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TELL THEM APART',prompt:'Which of these is flattery in Cicero\u2019s sense?',options:['Praising a friend\u2019s draft you privately think is weak, to keep things easy','Praising a friend\u2019s draft that you honestly think is excellent','Pointing out a flaw in a friend\u2019s draft without any harshness','Backing a friend\u2019s risky plan after weighing it honestly'],answer:0,why:'Flattery is agreement cut loose from your honest judgement. True praise and honest support are not flattery, however warm.'}]}},'laelius-on-friendship_3':{pictureCaption:'A small hard stone, held out on a flat, open hand across the table. Not thrown. That is how a hard truth should be passed.',bridge:'Idea 2 showed why a friend\u2019s honesty matters. This one is about how to give it, and how to take it.',basics:{title:'Tell them early, tell them kindly',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'BEFORE THE BIG GAME',prompt:'Your friend has been practising a kick the wrong way. The game is on Saturday. When should you tell him?',options:['Now, while he can still fix it','After the game, so he isn\u2019t upset now','Never, it is not your business'],answer:0,reveal:'Told now, he can still fix it. After the game, it is just a telling-off about a thing he can\u2019t change. The same words feel very different.'},{kind:'idea',text:'If a friend is getting something wrong, tell them. Tell them early, while they can still fix it. And tell them kindly, not meanly.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Told early',items:['Feels like help','He can still fix it','He feels looked after']},right:{title:'Told late',items:['Feels like blame','Too late to change it','He feels told off']}},{kind:'picture',caption:'A hard little stone, held out on a flat, open hand. Not thrown. That is how to pass a friend a hard truth.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The other half',text:'Cicero was a thinker in old Rome. He said friends have two jobs here. One is telling. The other is listening when a friend tells you. He thought listening without getting cross was the harder job.'},{kind:'steps',title:'How to say a hard thing',steps:['Say it soon.','Say it when you are alone together.','Talk about the thing, not the person.','Say how they could fix it.']},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend tells you that your joke upset a girl in your class. What would Cicero say to do?',options:['Listen, and don\u2019t get cross with your friend','Tell your friend to mind her own business','Laugh it off so that it isn\u2019t awkward'],answer:0,why:'Taking advice well is the other half of the job. Your friend told you because she cares.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'YOU HAVE SEEN THE BUDGET',text:'A friend is about to sign a lease on a flat she cannot really afford. You have seen her budget. Saying so now would sting. Saying nothing is easy, and you tell yourself it is her decision.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Frankness is the obligation; harshness is not. Give advice freely but without bitterness, and early, while the matter is still open. Take advice patiently, without resentment.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why timing changes the message',text:'A warning given while the decision is open is information, and can be acted on. The same warning after the damage is a reproach, and reproaches are resisted. Waiting turns a kind word into an unkind one.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Keeping quiet is the kind thing to do.',reality:'Cicero sees it the other way. Silence quietly decides that your friend cannot handle being told. That is a low opinion of them, dressed up as consideration.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The stone passes openly across the table, on a flat hand, early and without ceremony. A hard thing, softly delivered.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The harder half',text:'Cicero gives the listener a duty too: to take correction without resentment. He treats it as the harder of the two, and the two halves usually fail together. A friend who bristles at advice soon stops being given any.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of one thing you have noticed and not said to a friend. If it still matters, say it this week, in private, about the thing rather than the person.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You have read your friend\u2019s best man speech. One joke will hurt the bride. The wedding is a month away. What does Cicero\u2019s advice suggest?',options:['Tell him now, plainly and kindly, while he can still rewrite it','Say nothing, since it is his speech and his friendship','Wait until after the wedding, then explain the problem','Tell him bluntly, since frankness matters more than tact'],answer:0,why:'Early makes it information rather than reproach. Kindly keeps it frank without being harsh.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'TWO DUTIES, ONE WORD',word:'Admonition',meaning:'A friendly warning or correction: telling someone, for their sake, that they are going wrong. Cicero treats giving it and receiving it as two duties of the same friendship.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Admonish freely but without harshness, and early rather than after the damage. Receive admonition without resentment. Both belong to what friendship is; neither is an optional extra.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Manner and timing',text:'Cicero is specific. Frankness is required; bitterness is not. And the moment matters. A warning while the matter is open can be used. The same words afterwards are a verdict on something done, and are resisted as one.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE SILENCE THAT LOOKS KIND',nodes:['You see the mistake','You fear the sting','You stay silent','The damage lands','Only reproach is left'],note:'Each silent step feels considerate. The last step is the very thing you were trying to avoid.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why silence is not kindness',text:'Not saying the hard thing quietly decides that the other person cannot bear to be told. That is a low opinion dressed as consideration. On the other side, the friend who resents correction teaches everyone to stop offering it.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Frankness is a licence, not a mandate. Some things are not yours to judge, and a friend who corrects everything has become a critic. Early warning on every passing worry is only anxiety shared. Cicero\u2019s case is for a matter that is real, still open, and fairly yours to raise.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Frankness is a duty; harshness is not.','Early, it is information. Late, it is reproach.','Taking correction well is the harder half.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'Someone argues that their friends are adults, so it is not their place to point out mistakes. What is Cicero\u2019s reply?',options:['Warning a friend, and being warned, is part of what friendship is','They are right, since advice between adults is always a form of control','Advice is owed only when a friend asks for it in so many words','Mistakes teach better than warnings, so silence is the wiser course'],answer:0,why:'For Cicero frankness is a duty of friendship, not an intrusion on it. Withholding it treats a friend as fragile rather than as an adult.'}]}},'laelius-on-friendship_4':{pictureCaption:'A pace short of the line at a dark doorway, he stops with one hand raised. Friendship brings you this far, and no further into wrong.',bridge:'Idea 3 asked you to tell a friend the truth. This one draws the line a friend may not ask you to cross.',basics:{title:'A friend can\u2019t ask you to do wrong',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'WHAT LOYAL MEANS',belief:'A real friend will do anything you ask.',reality:'A real friend will do a lot for you. But not something wrong. And a real friend would not ask.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'BEFORE CLASS',text:'Your friend forgot his homework. He asks to copy yours before the bell. *Come on, we\u2019re friends,* he says. You feel stuck. Saying no feels mean.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Being a good friend never means doing wrong for them. If a friend asks you to, the asking is the problem, not your no.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'AN OLD RULE',text:'Cicero was a thinker in Rome long ago. He made this the first rule of friendship. Only ask a friend for good things. Only do good things for a friend. Doing wrong for a friend is no excuse, he said.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Fine for a friend',items:['Help them study','Lend them your pen','Stick up for them']},right:{title:'Not fine, even for a friend',items:['Let them copy','Lie to cover for them','Join in being mean']}},{kind:'picture',kicker:'THE LINE ON THE FLOOR',caption:'He stops one step before the line, hand up. He would go a long way for a friend, but not into the dark room past it. A friend who asks you to step over is using the friendship against you.'},{kind:'try',text:'Practise a kind no out loud: *I can\u2019t do that, but I can help you another way.* Saying it once makes it easier when it counts.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your best friend broke a window playing ball. She asks you to tell her mum it was someone else. What does Cicero\u2019s rule say?',options:['Say no, and offer to go with her to own up','Say yes, because friends stick together','Say nothing, and let her mum work it out'],answer:0,why:'You don\u2019t do wrong for a friend. But you can still help her do the right thing.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'A FAVOUR, FRAMED AS LOYALTY',text:'A friend at work asks you to confirm she was at a meeting she missed. It is one line in an email. She says she would do the same for you. Refusing feels like choosing a rule over a person.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Friendship stops where wrongdoing starts. Ask of friends only what is honourable, and do for them only what is honourable. Having done it for a friend is no defence.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The request is the breach',text:'Cicero puts the fault in the asking. A friend who asks you to do wrong is using the relationship against your interest: your honesty, your record, your name. The injury happened before you said yes or no.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'HOW FAR LOYALTY RUNS',low:'Loyal in nothing: a fair-weather friend',high:'Loyal in everything: an accomplice',note:'Cicero places friendship well along the line, then draws a hard stop at wrongdoing. Past that point, loyalty is not the highest value in play.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'THE HARD STOP',caption:'He stops a pace short of the line, weight back, one hand raised. He came this far willingly. The dark room beyond is where loyalty would turn into taking part in a wrong.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Ordinary, not dramatic',text:'The modern cases are rarely crimes. They are covering for someone, signing off on something unchecked, staying quiet when asked. Each is small. Each asks you to pay for the friendship in the wrong currency: your integrity.'},{kind:'try',text:'Prepare one sentence for the next time loyalty is used as leverage: I will help you with this, but not that way. Then offer what you can honestly do.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A friend asks you, as their referee, to say they led a project they only joined late. What does Cicero\u2019s rule suggest?',options:['Decline that claim, and offer an honest reference instead','Agree, since a friend\u2019s whole career may be at stake','Agree, but keep the wording vague enough to deny later','Refuse to act as anyone\u2019s referee from now on'],answer:0,why:'The line falls at the wrongdoing, not at the friendship. You can still do everything honourable for them.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'AFTER TIBERIUS GRACCHUS',text:'Laelius recalls Gaius Blossius, a devoted follower of the reformer Tiberius Gracchus, who was killed in 133 BCE. Questioned afterwards, Blossius said he would have done whatever Gracchus wished, even, if asked, set fire to the Capitol, Rome\u2019s most sacred hill. Laelius holds this up as friendship badly misunderstood.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Friendship does not extend to wrongdoing. Ask of friends only what is honourable, do for them only what is honourable, and never offer \u201cI did it for a friend\u201d as a defence.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What he is arguing against',text:'Roman political friendship carried an expectation of support whatever the cause. Through Laelius, Cicero rejects it, and points to factions and plots against the state as where it leads. Loyalty is real, but it is not the highest value in play.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the asking is the injury',text:'To ask a friend to do wrong for you is to spend their integrity for your benefit. It turns the bond against their interest. So the breach comes from the one who asks, before the friend has decided anything.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Loyalty he endorses',items:['Time, effort and risk for a friend','Defending them when they are right','Telling them when they are wrong']},right:{title:'Loyalty he rejects',items:['Joining a friend\u2019s wrongdoing','Covering for it afterwards','Treating the bond as an excuse']}},{kind:'picture',kicker:'SHORT OF THE THRESHOLD',caption:'Feet planted a pace short of the line, one hand raised flat. Not a retreat from the friendship, which came this far willingly, but a refusal to follow it into the dark room beyond, as Blossius said he would have.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Cicero softens the rule himself: where a friend\u2019s life or good name is at stake, he allows a small step off the straight path, short of disgrace. Where that line falls is left to judgement. And his examples come from public life; for someone whose safety depends on a group, refusing can cost far more.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'When has loyalty been used as leverage on you? What were you actually being asked to spend?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which appeal to friendship would Cicero reject?',options:['A friend asks you to sign off a safety check you have not done','A friend asks you to defend them against a false accusation','A friend asks you to say honestly where they went wrong','A friend asks you to give up a weekend to help them move'],answer:0,why:'Signing off unchecked work is doing wrong for a friend. The others cost time or comfort, which friendship may rightly ask.'}]}},'laelius-on-friendship_5':{pictureCaption:'Hands unpicking a rope strand by strand, the shears closed and unused beside them. It comes apart without anything being cut.',bridge:'Idea 4 drew the line at wrongdoing. That line returns here: a real wrong can end a friendship cleanly, and this is how Cicero would end the rest.',basics:{title:'Some friendships just fade',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'YOUR OLD BEST FRIEND',text:'In Year 3 you and Jo did everything together. Now you are in different classes. You like different things. You still wave, but you hardly talk. Nobody did anything wrong.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'What should you do?',options:['Tell Jo you are not friends any more','Let it be, and stay friendly','Try hard to make it like Year 3 again'],answer:1,reveal:'There is nothing to fix and no one to blame. You both just changed. A big talk would only make it hurt.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Some friendships end because people change, not because anyone was bad. Let those ones fade slowly and kindly. Don\u2019t rip them apart.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The hands are untwisting the rope bit by bit. The scissors lie shut. Nothing is being cut.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A SEWN-ON BADGE',text:'When a badge has to come off your school bag, you unpick the stitches one by one. If you rip it off, you tear the bag as well. Rip a friendship, and you tear the good times too.'},{kind:'explain',title:'When it is different',text:'Cicero was a thinker in Rome long ago. He said one case is different. If a friend does something really bad to you, it is fine to end it clearly. Fading is for friendships where nobody did wrong.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You and your camp friend used to write every week. Now it is once a year. What does this idea say?',options:['That is fine. Let it fade and stay kind','Send a note saying the friendship is over','Decide she must not care about you now'],answer:0,why:'Nobody did wrong. Letting it fade keeps the good memories safe.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'NOTHING HAPPENED',prompt:'A long friendship has quietly stopped working. No fight, no betrayal. What is the usual instinct?',options:['To have it out, and find what went wrong','To let it cool without comment','To ignore it and hope it revives'],answer:0,reveal:'Most people feel an ending needs an explanation. Cicero thought that instinct does more harm than the drift. It demands a reason where there is only time.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A friendship that has run its course can be unstitched rather than torn: allowed to fade gradually, unless there has been a real wrong. Not every ending needs a scene.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Unstitched',items:['Contact thins over months','The history stays intact','Both keep their good name']},right:{title:'Torn',items:['A showdown, or a public break','The past is recast as a mistake','Others take sides']},note:'A clean break is right after a genuine wrong. For mere divergence it costs more than it settles.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What an ending says',text:'A public break declares that the friendship was a mistake, which is usually false and hurts both people\u2019s standing. A slow cooling says only that time passed. And a friendship turned into open enmity costs both sides more than the drift would have.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Strand by strand, patiently, with the shears lying closed beside. The rope comes apart without anything being cut.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Divergence is not betrayal',text:'People change at different speeds and in different directions. A friendship ending is often nobody\u2019s failure. Treating it as betrayal turns an ordinary fact into a grievance, and asks the other person to answer for time.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of a friendship that has thinned. If nothing wrong happened, drop the question of what went wrong. Send one warm, easy message and expect nothing back.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A university friend has stopped replying much, and you find it hurtful. Which response fits Cicero\u2019s advice?',options:['Keep things warm and occasional, and let it find its level','Write to say you feel abandoned, and ask what you did','Unfollow them everywhere, so the ending is at least clear','Ask mutual friends what they have been saying about you'],answer:0,why:'Nothing wrong has happened, only drift. Unstitching keeps the history intact. The others force a scene where there was only time.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'An honest ending means telling someone plainly that the friendship is over.',reality:'For Cicero honesty is not the issue. A public break makes a claim, that the friendship was a mistake, which is usually false. Where nobody has done wrong, letting it fade is the more truthful ending.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Friendships that have run their course should be unstitched rather than torn: let them cool gradually, unless a real wrong makes a clean break right. Public enmity damages both people more than drift would.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where it sits',text:'The advice comes among the dialogue\u2019s practical cases. Laelius repeats a saying he heard from Cato, the stern elder statesman: friendships should be unstitched, not torn. He sets apart those ended by some intolerable wrong, where a clean break is fitting.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW A DRIFT BECOMES A FEUD',nodes:['Two people change','Contact thins','One demands an explanation','Each assigns blame','Open enmity'],note:'The drift is no one\u2019s fault. The feud starts at the third step, when time is treated as an offence.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Unpicking, not cutting: the strands come loose one by one while the shears stay closed. The rope\u2019s history is left undamaged.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Fading can be a coward\u2019s exit. Where one person still cares and asks what happened, silence can hurt more than a kind, clear word, and vanishing on someone is not what Cicero meant. He wrote for a world of public reputations. In private life a short, honest conversation is sometimes the gentler cut.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Most endings are drift, and drift is nobody\u2019s failure.','Unstitch, don\u2019t tear: let it cool rather than declaring it over.','A genuine wrong is the exception, and justifies a clean break.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'WHERE THE RULE BENDS',prompt:'On Cicero\u2019s own terms, which case calls for a clean break rather than a slow fade?',options:['A friend has been secretly lying about you to win a promotion','A friend has moved abroad and now writes only rarely','A friend\u2019s new interests leave you with little in common','A friend has had children and has no spare evenings'],answer:0,why:'His exception is a real wrong. The rest are divergence, which he would let fade without a scene.'}]}},'laelius-on-friendship_6':{pictureCaption:'Scales that have done their weighing, and a seal beside them. In Cicero\u2019s order the weighing comes first, the seal after.',bridge:'Idea 5 was about ending friendships gently. This one is about starting them carefully, so that fewer need ending.',basics:{title:'Get to know them first',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE NEW GIRL',text:'A new girl joins your class. She is funny, and she picks you on day one. By Friday she is your best friend. A month later you find out she tells everyone your secrets.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Before someone becomes your best friend, see how they act. Watch them first. Then let yourself get close.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the order matters',text:'Once you love a friend, it is hard to see them clearly. You make excuses for them. And if you need to step back, it hurts a lot. So it is smart to look first.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'First the weighing, on the scales. Then the red seal, which stamps it for good. Look first, then promise.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Small tests',steps:['Lend something small. Does it come back?','Tell a small secret. Does it stay secret?','Watch how they treat other kids.','See if they stick by you on a bad day.']},{kind:'myth',belief:'Watching a new friend like this is mean.',reality:'You can still be kind and have fun. You just don\u2019t hand over your biggest secrets on day two. Cicero, a thinker from old Rome, said this is hard, because liking someone happens fast.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You met Ali at camp two days ago. He wants to be best friends and swap secrets. What does this idea say?',options:['Have fun, and get to know him before big secrets','Say no, because you only just met him','Tell him all your secrets so he trusts you'],answer:0,why:'You don\u2019t have to push him away. You just let time show you who he is first.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'STILL HERE, OUT OF HABIT',text:'Many people have a friendship they would not start again today. You met early, liked each other fast, and only later saw how they treat people. By then it was years old, and leaving felt like breaking something.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Judge first, then love. Form a view of someone\u2019s character before the attachment forms, not after. Once it forms, it bends judgement and makes leaving painful.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE USUAL ORDER',nodes:['Quick liking','Early commitment','Late discovery of character','Years of adjusting the verdict'],note:'Cicero\u2019s complaint is the order: character found out after the commitment. The years of adjusting are our point, not his.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Test in small matters',text:'Cicero advises trying people in small things first. The qualities that count are constancy and honesty under pressure. They only show in situations that have not happened yet. A season of ordinary dealings lets some of them happen.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The scales come first; the seal comes after. Weigh the character, then commit, not the other way round.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Judging a friend is cold and calculating.',reality:'Cicero knew it sounds cold. He thought the other way costs more. In what we have seen, that is often years spent excusing someone you would never have chosen with clear eyes.'},{kind:'try',text:'For one promising new friend, write down what you actually know of their conduct, apart from how you feel with them. Wait a season before calling it settled.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You click at once with a new colleague. They suggest going into business together next month. What does Cicero\u2019s order suggest?',options:['Enjoy the friendship, and see how they handle small matters first','Agree now, while the trust between you is fresh and strong','Decline, since friends should never go into business together','Ask colleagues to vouch for them, then decide this week'],answer:0,why:'Character shows over time and in small dealings. Commit after the evidence, not before it.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE REVERSED ORDER',text:'Cicero states a rule that people tend to run backwards: judge, then love; not love, then judge. Much of the harm in friendship, he suggests, comes from committing early and only then discovering who the other person is, when leaving is painful.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Form the judgement before the attachment. Once affection has formed, judgement is compromised and withdrawal is painful, so the estimate of character has to come first.'},{kind:'term',word:'Constancy',meaning:'Steadiness of character: being the same person under pressure as at ease, and holding to a friend when it costs something. Cicero counts it among the qualities a friend most needs. No first meeting can show it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The method',text:'He recommends testing in small matters first, small sums before large ones. The difficulty is that the decisive qualities only show in circumstances that have not yet arisen. The test is slow because the evidence is.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Scales that have done their weighing, and a seal lying beside them. In Cicero\u2019s order the weighing always comes before the seal.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Cicero concedes the rule is hard to keep: affection does not wait for the assessment to finish. Applied rigidly it is also cold, and someone forever auditing others may never let anyone close enough to judge. What survives is a weaker, useful habit: notice what you know of someone\u2019s conduct, apart from how you enjoy their company.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which friendship would you not enter today? What did you know of their conduct before you were attached, and what only after?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HONEST CONCESSION',prompt:'Which difficulty does Cicero himself concede?',options:['Affection tends to form before the assessment is complete','Character cannot be judged by anyone outside the person','Old friendships must be ended once judged unsound','Testing friends is dishonest and spoils the friendship'],answer:0,why:'He admits the order is hard to keep because liking runs ahead of judgement. The rule survives as a habit of noticing, not a gate.'}]}},'laelius-on-friendship_7':{pictureCaption:'Rows of bottles grown dusty with age, and one new one just placed on top. Every old bottle on this rack was new once.',bridge:'Idea 6 asked you to give a new friendship time. This one is about what that time, once spent, becomes.',basics:{title:'Old friends and new friends',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A TREE',text:'A tree you planted when you were five is big now. It gives shade. A tree you plant today can\u2019t give shade for years. But every big tree was once a tiny one that someone planted.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Old friends have something new friends can\u2019t have yet: years together. But keep making new friends too. Every old friend was new once.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the years give you',text:'An old friend has seen you on bad days. They know how you act when things go wrong, and you know how they act. A new friend might be great fun. But no one can skip the years.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Cicero, a thinker in old Rome, said good friendships get better with age, like wine. Here are old bottles, and one new one just put in.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Old friend',items:['Knows your bad days','Has shown they stick around','Can\u2019t be swapped for anyone']},right:{title:'New friend',items:['Fun and exciting','Not tested yet','Could be an old friend one day']}},{kind:'try',text:'This week, do one thing with an old friend and one thing with a new friend. One can\u2019t stand in for the other.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your oldest friend has been a bit dull lately. A new kid is really fun. What would Cicero say?',options:['Keep both. They give you different things','Drop the old friend for the fun new one','Stay with the old friend and ignore the new kid'],answer:0,why:'The old friend has years you can\u2019t get back. The new one might be an old friend one day.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'TWENTY YEARS IN',text:'Your oldest friend knew you before you were anyone in particular. You have seen each other through a bad job, a breakup, a funeral. Someone you met this year may be better company. They cannot have that for twenty years.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Old friendships are a different substance from new ones. Time supplies what nothing else can. Yet promising new friendships must still be made, because the old ones were new once.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What time supplies',text:'A new friendship may be warmer. It still lacks evidence: you have not seen this person when you failed, when they were tempted, when loyalty cost them something. Cicero compares good friendships to wines that improve as they keep. There is no shortcut.'},{kind:'equation',parts:['Shared history','Tested behaviour'],result:'An old friendship',note:'Neither part can be bought or hurried. Both only come from time.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Aged rows below, one bottle just placed on top. Both claims at once: the old cannot be hurried, and the new must start somewhere.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Preserve, don\u2019t optimise',text:'If the value lies in accumulated time, an old friendship should be maintained, not judged against newer company. Once spent it cannot be rebuilt, and nothing newer can stand in for it. Neglect is the usual way it gets spent.'},{kind:'try',text:'Put one hour into an old friendship this month, and one into a new one. They are not substitutes, so don\u2019t let either stand in for the other.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You have moved city and made lively new friends. Calls with your oldest friend are down to twice a year. What does Cicero\u2019s view suggest?',options:['Protect the old friendship on purpose, while still building the new','Let the old one go, since the new friends are close at hand','Pull back from the new friends to stay loyal to the old one','Wait and see which friendships turn out most rewarding'],answer:0,why:'The old friendship holds what cannot be rebuilt, and the new ones may become old. They are not substitutes.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TWO CLAIMS, ONE PASSAGE',prompt:'Cicero ranks old friendships above new ones. What do you expect him to conclude about making new friends?',options:['Be wary of them; the old are better','Make them, if they show promise','Treat old and new exactly alike'],answer:1,reveal:'He refuses the obvious conclusion. Old friendships are better, and promising new ones should still be made, because every old friendship began as a new one. Somebody has to start the clock.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Old friendships are not the same substance as new ones. Like wines that can bear age, the best grow sweeter, and time is the only ingredient. New friendships that could grow old should still not be refused.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What only time supplies',text:'The argument is about evidence. Years bring shared history, behaviour that has been tested, and knowledge of how a person acts when things go badly. Laelius repeats an old saying: friends must eat many measures of salt together before friendship has done its full work.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'HOW TO TREAT AN OLD FRIEND',low:'Optimise: judge it by what it offers now',high:'Preserve: protect what time has built',note:'Cicero\u2019s view points firmly to the right. What time built cannot be rebuilt if spent, and nothing newer substitutes for it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Aged rows beneath, a single new bottle placed on top. The rack holds both of Cicero\u2019s claims: age cannot be faked, and it has to start.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Age is not proof of quality. Some old friendships survive on inertia, the trap idea 6 describes: years of adjusting the verdict to fit the commitment. Cicero\u2019s claim is about friendships that have kept, like wine that can bear age. Not every old bottle has. Some turn.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Time supplies history and evidence that nothing else can.','Preserve old friendships; their value cannot be rebuilt.','Still start new ones: every old friend was new once.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'A friend reasons that since old friendships are better, investing in new ones is a waste. What is Cicero\u2019s answer?',options:['Old friendships were new once, so refusing new ones ends the supply','He agrees that past a certain age new friendships seldom matter','New friendships are warmer, which outweighs the value of history','Old and new friendships turn out to be equal in every way'],answer:0,why:'Time is the ingredient, so the clock has to start somewhere. Promising new friendships are the old friendships of later.'}]}},'laelius-on-friendship_8':{pictureCaption:'A stone and its reflection, held at exactly the same size. A true friend is another you: an accurate likeness, not a flattering one.',bridge:'Idea 2 warned against a mirror that flatters. Cicero\u2019s image for a true friend is a reflection that does not.',basics:{title:'A friend is like another you',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'GOOD ADVICE, NOT TAKEN',text:'Your friend is scared to try out for the team. You tell her: just go, the worst you get is a no. Great advice. The next week, you are too scared to try out for the play.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Cicero, a thinker in old Rome, said a true friend is like a second you. You care about their life as you care about your own. And, we think, you often see their problems more clearly than yours.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A stone and its reflection, the same size and shape. A true friend is a bit like that: another you, not a bent copy.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it\u2019s easier for a friend',text:'When it is your own problem, you are too scared or too close to see it. When it is a friend\u2019s, you stand back a little. So you see it plainly. That is why your advice to them is so good.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A HEAVY BAG',text:'A heavy bag feels lighter when two people carry it. A good joke is better when two people laugh. Cicero said friends work like that. They split the bad times and make the good times bigger.'},{kind:'try',text:'Stuck on something? Ask: what would I tell my best friend to do? Then do that.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You are nervous about a test, so you keep putting off revising. What does the second-you idea suggest?',options:['Think what you would tell a friend, then do it','Wait until you stop feeling nervous first','Ask a friend to do the work for you'],answer:0,why:'You can see a friend\u2019s problem clearly. So borrow that view for your own.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Calling a friend a second self is just a warm way of saying you are close.',reality:'On our reading, it is practical as well as warm. It explains why their disgrace lands on you, why you can predict their answer, and why advising them is so much easier than advising yourself.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A true friend is a second self: someone you can confide in as safely as in yourself, whose interests you weigh as your own. Shared, good fortune grows brighter and bad fortune lighter.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE ADVICE YOU GAVE',text:'Last month you told a friend to leave a job that was making her miserable. You were clear, kind and right. Your own job is doing the same to you, and you have been finding reasons to stay for a year.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the view is clearer',text:'Your own situation comes with your fears and excuses attached. A friend\u2019s comes with your care for them, and without the excuses. A second self is close enough to care and far enough away to see.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Borrow the clearer view',steps:['Write your situation down as if it were a close friend\u2019s.','Write the advice you would give them.','Put it away for an hour.','Read it as though somebody had handed it to you.']},{kind:'picture',caption:'The stone and its reflection, exactly the same size. Not the flattering mirror of idea 2: a likeness that does not bend.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Your closest friend makes a public mistake at work and is embarrassed. You feel it almost as if it were yours. On the second-self reading, this is\u2026',options:['What a second self means: their fortunes register as yours','A sign you are too dependent and should step back','Ordinary sympathy, the same as for any colleague','A failure to keep proper boundaries between you'],answer:0,why:'Cicero\u2019s second self is structural: you weigh their interests as your own. On our reading, that is why their disgrace and success land on you.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE BEST-KNOWN IMAGE',text:'To look at a true friend, Cicero writes, is to look at a kind of image of yourself. It is the essay\u2019s best-known idea and its most demanding. He wrote it in mourning, which is why it is meant as a description rather than a compliment.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A true friend is a second self: someone in whom you may confide as safely as in yourself, and whose interests you weigh as your own. The relationship has to run the same way in both directions.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What follows from it',text:'On our reading, practical consequences follow. Shared fortune: Cicero says friendship brightens success and lightens adversity by dividing it. Shared exposure: a friend\u2019s disgrace becomes yours. And a clearer view: you see their situation without the excuses that cloud your own.'},{kind:'term',word:'Symmetry',meaning:'The requirement that the same treatment runs both ways. Each confides in the other; each weighs the other\u2019s interests as their own. One-sided devotion, however sincere, is not what Cicero is describing.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why so few qualify',text:'Symmetry rules out most of what people call friendship. Cicero treats true friendship as extremely rare: across all of history, he says, only a handful of famous pairs are remembered. The scarcity is the point. Those few that meet the standard deserve a different order of attention.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A stone and its reflection, exactly the same size. Not a flattering likeness but an accurate one, which is what makes a second self useful.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The image can mislead. A friend is not literally another you, and treating them as one can erase real differences, or make every slight against them yours to avenge, which idea 4 forbids. Read as a standard of rarity, it can also sour into dismissing ordinary friendships, which have their own worth.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY THE STANDARD',prompt:'Which relationship falls short of Cicero\u2019s second self, however warm it is?',options:['One friend confides everything; the other keeps their own life closed','Two friends who disagree often but tell each other everything','Two friends who live far apart and speak every few months','Two friends whose fortunes differ widely, one rich and one poor'],answer:0,why:'The second self requires symmetry. Distance, disagreement and unequal fortune do not break it. Confiding that runs only one way does.'}]}},'laelius-on-friendship_9':{pictureCaption:'One end of the couch worn by use, the other untouched, a cup by the used end. One friend is still here.',bridge:'The first eight ideas are the argument. This one is about who is making it, and when.',basics:{title:'Was it worth it?',beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'ROME, LONG AGO',text:'Cicero, a thinker in old Rome, wrote about two best friends, Laelius and Scipio. Scipio died. Days later, Laelius talked about friendship. He was very sad. He still said the friendship was the best thing he ever had.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'Would Laelius rather never have known Scipio, so that he would not feel this sad?',options:['Yes, so he would not be sad now','No, the friendship was worth the sadness'],answer:1,reveal:'He would not swap it. He said he was not sad for Scipio, who was past all harm. He was sad for himself. And remembering the friendship made him happy.'},{kind:'idea',text:'This talk about friendship comes from a man who has just lost his friend. That is what makes it count. He is paying the full price, and he still says it was worth it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One end of the sofa has been sat in. The other end is empty and neat. The cup is by the used end. One friend is still here.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why that matters',text:'Anyone can say friends are great on a happy day. It means more from someone who is sad because they lost one. They know the cost. And they still say yes.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'WHO KNOWS BEST',text:'Your best friend moved away last year. You miss him a lot. Was it worth being so close, now that it hurts? You know the answer best, because you have felt both parts.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Two people say a long climb up a mountain is worth it. Whose word counts for more?',options:['The one who did the climb, sore legs and all','The one who has only seen photos of the top','Neither, because nobody can really know'],answer:0,why:'Like Laelius, the climber has paid the cost. So when they say it was worth it, you can trust it more.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TWO PEOPLE, ONE CLAIM',prompt:'Two people tell you a close friendship is worth any cost. Whose word should count for more?',options:['A happy person with many friends','A person grieving a friend just lost','A philosopher who has studied it'],answer:1,reveal:'The grieving person is paying the full price of the claim right now. If they still make it, it has passed the hardest test there is.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The dialogue\u2019s case for friendship is made by someone in mourning. That is not background. It is the proof: the claim is being tested by its full price as it is spoken, and it holds.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'THE SETTING',text:'The dialogue is set days after the death of Scipio, a great Roman general. His friend of a lifetime, Laelius, talks with his two sons-in-law. He says he grieves not for Scipio, who is beyond harm, but for himself, and that the memory of the friendship still makes his life happy.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Cicero\u2019s own position',text:'Cicero wrote it in 44 BCE, in his last years. His daughter Tullia had died the year before, and the Roman Republic he had served was coming apart. The book reads less like philosophy about friendship than testimony from someone who has paid for his view.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One end of the couch worn, the other untouched, the cup still by the used end. The cost of a friendship, set out plainly.'},{kind:'explain',title:'A general test for advice',text:'The structure carries well beyond friendship. A claim about something risky is worth most from someone who has paid its downside. Advice from people who have only seen the upside is cheaper, and should be weighed that way.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you take advice on a risk, ask: has this person ever paid the downside they are describing? Weigh what they say accordingly.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You are deciding whether to start a business. Whose encouragement does this idea say to weigh most?',options:['A founder whose company failed and who would still do it again','A friend who has read widely about successful start-ups','A founder who succeeded early and has never had a setback','An adviser who is paid whether or not you go ahead'],answer:0,why:'They have paid the downside and still back the choice. Like Laelius, their claim has survived its full price.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'timeline',kicker:'TWO GRIEFS, ONE BOOK',points:[{when:'129 BCE',what:'Scipio dies suddenly. The dialogue is set days later.'},{when:'106 BCE',what:'Cicero is born.'},{when:'45 BCE',what:'Cicero\u2019s daughter Tullia dies.'},{when:'44 BCE',what:'Cicero writes On Friendship, the year Caesar is assassinated.'},{when:'43 BCE',what:'Cicero is killed in the political purges that follow.'}],note:'The grief in the dialogue belongs to Laelius. Its author was grieving too.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The case for friendship is made by a man paying its full price. Laelius, days after Scipio\u2019s death, would not trade the friendship to avoid the loss, and that is the book\u2019s strongest argument.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'WHAT LAELIUS SAYS',text:'Asked how he is bearing the loss, Laelius says he does not grieve for Scipio, who is beyond harm, but for himself. Of everything fortune or nature has given him, he says, nothing compares with that friendship, and its memory makes his life a happy one.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the frame is the proof',text:'An essay arguing that friendship is among the greatest goods is spoken from the condition that tests the claim most severely. If it were false, grief would expose it. It holds: the speaker would accept the loss again for the friendship. The setting does what no argument alone could.'},{kind:'keyline',text:'An argument for something is worth most from someone who has just been charged for it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A couch with one end worn and the other untouched, a cup beside the used end. The price, in a room.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Testimony is not proof. Grief can idealise, and a mourner may be the least able to see a friendship\u2019s faults. Laelius is also Cicero\u2019s creation, so the frame is a literary choice as well as a witness. And the test runs one way: people who regret a friendship after losing it rarely write dialogues about it.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which of your own convictions have you paid for? Which have you only ever held at no cost?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'A critic says a grieving man\u2019s praise of friendship is bias, not evidence. What is the strongest reply?',options:['Grief should push towards regret, so praise that survives it is telling','Grief always makes people see more clearly than they otherwise would','Cicero was a famous orator, so his judgement deserves trust','Laelius was a real person, so the dialogue must be accurate'],answer:0,why:'Loss is when a friendship\u2019s cost is plainest. Praise that holds there has passed a hard test, though not an infallible one.'}]}}}},2978,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'leisure-class_1':{pictureCaption:'Curtains open, lamp lit, one vase turned to face the street. It was placed for the people walking past, not for the room.',basics:{title:'Buying things to be seen',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'TWO WATER BOTTLES',text:'Two bottles hold the same water. One costs a little. The other costs ten times more and has a big logo on the side. Lots of kids at school want the second one.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Some things are bought mainly so that other people will see them. This is called *conspicuous consumption*.'},{kind:'term',word:'Conspicuous consumption',meaning:'*Conspicuous* means easy to spot. *Consumption* means buying and using things. Put them together: buying things so that people will notice.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Two things must be true',text:'For this to work, people must be able to see the thing. And they must know it costs a lot. If no one can see it, it says nothing. If no one knows the price, it says nothing.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The vase sits in the window, facing the street. It was put there for people walking past, not for the room.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO NOTICED THIS',text:'Thorstein Veblen was an American who wrote a book about this in 1899. He studied how very rich families showed off. His name for it is still used today.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of something you really want. Ask: would I still want it if no one else could ever see it?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Which of these is most likely bought to be seen?',options:['Trainers with a huge, famous logo on the side','Warm socks that no one sees under your boots','A plain pencil case that is cheap and strong'],answer:0,why:'The logo lets others spot the trainers and know the price. The socks and the pencil case are bought to be used.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TWO BAGS',prompt:'A designer handbag and a plain one carry the same things equally well. What is the extra money mostly for?',options:['Better stitching','Being recognised by strangers','Lasting longer'],answer:1,reveal:'Some of it may buy quality. But often much of it buys recognition: a bag that strangers can identify, and price, at a glance.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Conspicuous consumption is buying whose purpose is to be seen buying. The good must be visible and must be known to be expensive, or the signal fails.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Signal works',items:['Logo on the outside','Price widely known','Carried in public']},right:{title:'Signal fails',items:['No visible mark','Nobody knows what it cost','Kept at home, unseen']},note:'This is why the logo is on the outside of the bag.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it has to be costly',text:'A signal anyone could send tells nobody anything. If everyone could afford the bag, carrying it would prove nothing. So the cost is not an unfortunate side effect of the display. It is what makes the display believable.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'AMERICA, 1899',text:'Thorstein Veblen, an American economist, published the book in 1899, drawing on the newly rich families of his day and their mansions and lavish parties. He named the habit precisely enough that the term is still in ordinary use.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Curtains open, lamp lit, the vase turned to the street. The owner is not the audience here. The passer-by is.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before your next large purchase, ask what it would be worth to you if nobody could ever see it. The difference is what you are paying for the signal.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Two versions of the same jacket are equally warm. The one with a large logo costs \xa380 more. Who is most clearly paying for the signal?',options:['Someone who picks the logo version though both keep out the cold','Someone who picks the plain version because it fits them better','Someone who picks the logo version as it was the last in their size'],answer:0,why:'The extra \xa380 buys nothing the wearer can feel, only something others can see. That is the signal.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'CHICAGO, 1899',text:'Thorstein Veblen was teaching economics at the University of Chicago when he published The Theory of the Leisure Class. It described the American rich of the Gilded Age in the detached tone of an anthropologist studying a distant tribe, which is part of why it stung.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Once survival is not in question, wealth has to be shown to count, and showing it takes spending that others can see. The good must be visible and known to be costly, or it says nothing.'},{kind:'term',word:'Conspicuous consumption',meaning:'Veblen\u2019s term for consumption whose purpose is to be observed: goods acquired less for their use than as evidence of the capacity to acquire them.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE SIGNAL WORKS',nodes:['Wealth is invisible','Spending makes it visible','Cost makes it credible','Standing is granted'],note:'Break either middle link, so that the spending is unseen or cheap, and nothing is communicated.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The later, formal version',text:'Economists and biologists later formalised the same structure as costly signalling: a signal is believable only when it is too expensive for those without the underlying quality to fake. Veblen reached it in the 1890s from observation, without the mathematics.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The curtains are left open on purpose and the vase faces outward. A display needs a viewer, and this one is placed for the street.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Not every visible, expensive thing is a signal. A good coat is warm and also seen; a costly tool may simply work better. Motive is hard to read from outside, and the label slides easily into a sneer at other people\u2019s taste. The question is most honest when asked of your own purchases.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Name the last thing you bought partly because it would be seen. Which part of the price was for the seeing?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TEST THE CONDITIONS',prompt:'A luxury brand drops its logo and starts selling its goods in plain designs. By Veblen\u2019s logic, what happens to the signal?',options:['It still works, but only among people who can recognise the item and its price','It disappears entirely, because a signal cannot function without a visible logo','It grows stronger for everyone, because rarity always impresses any stranger','It stops mattering, because the goods are now bought only for their use'],answer:0,why:'Both conditions can still hold, but only for viewers who can read the item. Visible and known to be costly always means: to someone.'}]}},'leisure-class_2':{pictureCaption:'Far more food than anyone could eat, and a full platter pushed aside. The surplus is not a flaw in the display. It is the display.',bridge:'Idea 1 said the signal has to be costly. This one goes further: the uselessness is what is being bought.',basics:{title:'Wasting on purpose',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A BIRTHDAY PARTY',prompt:'A family has three huge cakes for ten guests. Most of the cake is thrown away. Why might they do that?',options:['They counted wrong','To show they can waste it'],answer:1,reveal:'Maybe they did count wrong. But three cakes say something one cake can\u2019t: *we have so much, we don\u2019t need to finish it.*'},{kind:'idea',text:'Showing off works best when money is wasted. Buying something useful proves nothing. Wasting money proves you have lots to spare.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A PEACOCK\u2019S TAIL',text:'A peacock drags a huge tail around. It is heavy, and it makes him slow. Some scientists think that is why it impresses. Only a strong bird can carry all that and still be fine.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Far too much food, and a full plate pushed away. The extra food is the message: *we have more than we need.*'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why useful things don\u2019t work',text:'Anyone who needs a coat might buy a coat. So a coat proves nothing. But spending on what you plainly don\u2019t need shows you have money left over. That is the proof.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'People who buy fancy things know they are showing off.',reality:'Usually they don\u2019t. Veblen said they just feel the costly thing is nicer. The showing off happens underneath, without anyone planning it.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'A SPECIAL MEANING',text:'Veblen used the word *waste* in his own way. He did not mean it as an insult. He meant money spent on things that do not make anyone\u2019s life better.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'By Veblen\u2019s idea, which of these shows off money best?',options:['A huge car that is hard to park and burns lots of fuel','A small car that is cheap to run and easy to park','An old bike that gets you to school quickly'],answer:0,why:'The big car costs far more than it needs to. That extra, doing nothing useful, is what shows the money.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Luxury goods cost more because they are better made.',reality:'Sometimes they are. Veblen\u2019s claim is sharper: the part of the price that buys nothing useful is not a by-product of the display. It is the thing being purchased.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Spending works as a signal precisely because it is wasteful. Anything useful could have been bought by someone who needed it. Only plainly unnecessary spending proves you could afford to be unnecessary.'},{kind:'equation',parts:['What it does for you','What it costs beyond that'],result:'What you pay',note:'In a status purchase, Veblen says, the second part is the point.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE WEDDING',text:'Five courses for a hundred and fifty guests, flowers that will be binned tomorrow. Nobody plans it as a display of waste. Everyone experiences it as doing things properly. Yet a cheap, practical wedding would say something quite different about the families.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Nobody has to be cynical',text:'Veblen notes that the people involved are not aware of any such motive. They find the costly thing genuinely more desirable. That is the uncomfortable part: the mechanism works best when it is felt as taste, and the signal runs underneath.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A table set for far more than anyone can eat, and a full platter pushed aside. The surplus is the message.'},{kind:'try',text:'For your next large purchase, price the plainest version that does the same job. The gap between the two is what the signal costs.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A family keeps a vast formal dining room they use twice a year, and says it is simply how a proper home should be. What would Veblen say?',options:['They likely mean it; the signal works through their sense of what is proper','They know it is only for show, and are pretending to themselves otherwise','They are right, since a large room is more practical for any family'],answer:0,why:'Veblen does not accuse anyone of pretending. The preference is felt as real, which is exactly how the signal does its work.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'A WORD, DEFINED',text:'In the chapter on conspicuous consumption Veblen pauses to say that *waste* is a technical word for him. It carries no moral blame. It means expenditure that does not serve human life or wellbeing on the whole.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The waste is not an unfortunate by-product of display. It is the thing purchased, because only expenditure without return proves that you could afford it.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Useful spending',items:['Might be a need','Proves nothing about means','Anyone might do it']},right:{title:'Wasteful spending',items:['Plainly not a need','Proves a surplus','Only the able can do it']},note:'The right-hand column is the one that carries information.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why sincerity helps',text:'Veblen says the people involved are not conscious of signalling; they experience the expensive thing as better. That is not a gap in the theory but part of it. A display felt as taste is more convincing than one performed knowingly, and nobody has to be a hypocrite for it to run.'},{kind:'keyline',text:'Nobody has to intend the waste for the waste to do its work.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The claim is hard to test, because sincere taste and unconscious signalling look identical from outside. Calling a purchase waste also smuggles in a view of what serves wellbeing, which Veblen presents as neutral but rarely is. And much costly spending has real returns: craft, durability, pleasure. Not every surplus is a signal.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What in your home costs far more than its function requires? Would you call it taste or signal, and could you tell the difference?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'A critic says people buy luxury goods because they enjoy them, not to signal. What is Veblen\u2019s best reply?',options:['Enjoyment is how the signal is felt, so the two are not rivals','Nobody truly enjoys luxury goods; they only pretend that they do','Enjoyment is beside the point, since only the price matters to buyers','The critic is right, and the theory should be dropped entirely'],answer:0,why:'Veblen expects the preference to be sincere. The signalling works through the enjoyment, not instead of it.'}]}},'leisure-class_3':{pictureCaption:'A great lawn, perfectly kept and never used. One chair for sitting; the tools that did the work are pushed out to the edge.',bridge:'Before spending could show wealth, Veblen argues, not working did. This is the older signal behind idea 1.',basics:{title:'Showing off by not working',beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'LONG AGO',text:'Long ago, rich people had another way to show off. Not by buying things. By *not working*. If you never had to dig, sew or carry, people could tell you were rich.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Before people showed off by spending, they showed off by not working. Free time, spent on useless things, was proof that you were rich.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A huge lawn, kept perfect, used by no one. The rake and fork are pushed to the edge. The work is kept out of sight.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'SKILLS THAT EARN NOTHING',text:'Rich children learned things that took years and earned nothing. Old languages no one speaks. Fancy manners. Special sports. The point was the years. They proved you had time to spare.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHY IT CHANGED',left:{title:'In a village',items:['Everyone knows you','They see your whole day','Free time shows']},right:{title:'In a city',items:['Strangers all around','No one sees your day','Only your things show']},note:'So the showing off moved from your time to your things.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time someone says how busy they are, notice it. Today, being busy can be a way to show you matter.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A new girl joins your school. No one knows her family. What would show fastest that they are rich?',options:['Her costly new phone','How much free time her parents have','Her family\u2019s long past in the town'],answer:0,why:'Strangers can\u2019t see how anyone spends their days. They can see a phone at once. That is why things took over from free time.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'TWICE IN ONE CONVERSATION',text:'At a party, someone tells you twice how slammed they are: back-to-back calls, no weekends, barely slept. It sounds like a complaint. It lands as something else.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Before status was shown by visible spending, it was shown by visible idleness. That meant not needing to work, and supporting others who did not. Spending took over when people could no longer see each other\u2019s days.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The evidence of idleness',text:'Veblen points to the skills of the leisure class: dead languages, ceremonial sports, manners that took years to learn. Each proves time spent on something with no productive return. The more useless and slow to acquire, the better the proof.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHY THE SIGNAL MOVED',nodes:['Village: everyone sees your day','City: strangers see nothing','Evidence must be portable','Owned objects replace idleness'],note:'Veblen puts it down to anonymity. Display moves to whatever strangers can read at a glance.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A perfect lawn that nobody plays on, its tools hidden at the edge. Land kept to produce nothing is leisure, on display.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The modern inversion',text:'For many professionals today the display has flipped. Busyness signals being in demand. That twist is a later observation, not Veblen\u2019s own. But it runs on his logic. The signal is whatever is scarce and easy to read.'},{kind:'try',text:'When somebody tells you twice how busy they are, notice which old signal is being sent. Then ask whether you send it too.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Which prediction follows from Veblen\u2019s account of why the signal moved?',options:['As people know less about each other, display shifts to things seen at once','As cities grow, people will go back to showing status through visible idleness','As societies grow richer, people will stop displaying their status altogether'],answer:0,why:'Veblen ties the shift to anonymity. Wherever strangers judge strangers, the evidence has to be portable and quick to read.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',word:'Conspicuous leisure',meaning:'Veblen\u2019s term for the visible abstention from productive work: time spent, and seen to be spent, on things that earn nothing. In the book it comes first, a chapter before conspicuous consumption.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Wealth was first displayed not by spending but by not working. The shift to consumption came with anonymity: where nobody can see your day, the evidence has to be something you carry.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Receipts for wasted time',text:'Leisure leaves no product, so it must leave evidence. Veblen reads the accomplishments of the leisure class as that evidence: dead languages, correct spelling and etiquette, ceremonial sport, animals bred for show. Each is a receipt for years spent unproductively.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Leisure signal',items:['Needs witnesses who know you','Shown by how time is spent','Works in a village']},right:{title:'Consumption signal',items:['Readable by strangers','Shown by what is owned','Works in a city']},note:'The prediction: as anonymity rises, display migrates from time to things.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The prediction, checked',text:'That is roughly what happened as people moved into cities. It seems to be happening again as display migrates to whatever is legible on a screen. A holiday photo posted on a weekday is leisure made portable.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The inversion is the hard case. Among many professionals, visible busyness now signals status better than visible idleness. Veblen\u2019s framework can absorb this, since busyness signals being in demand. But that flexibility is a warning: a theory that reads both idleness and busyness as display needs care before it explains anything.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which of your accomplishments mainly prove that you once had time to spare?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A remote worker posts photos of a quiet weekday at the beach. Using Veblen\u2019s analysis, what is happening?',options:['Leisure is being made portable, so strangers can see how time is spent','Consumption has fully replaced leisure as the only signal of status','The photos carry no signal, since nobody can see what was paid','Busyness is being displayed, which is the modern inversion at work'],answer:0,why:'The photos turn the old leisure signal into something strangers can read, as his argument about anonymity predicts.'}]}},'leisure-class_4':{pictureCaption:'One step reached, and the flight still climbs into the light. Whichever step you stand on, there is always one above to look at.',bridge:'Ideas 1 to 3 were about showing status to others. This one is about what the comparison does to you.',basics:{title:'Why more is never enough',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE NEW BIKE',text:'You save for months and buy the bike you wanted. For a week it feels great. Then a friend gets one with better gears. Your bike is the same bike. But now it feels a bit worse.'},{kind:'idea',text:'We judge what we have by the people just above us. When we move up, they change too. So the good feeling from getting more never lasts long.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'He has reached the orange step. But look up. There is always one more step above, and that is where his eyes go.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE THE HORIZON',text:'It is like walking toward the line where the sky meets the land. You walk and walk. But that line moves back just as fast as you go.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Not your fault',text:'Veblen wrote about this in 1899. On his view, it is not a flaw in you. It is how comparing works. If what you want is to be *ahead*, there is always someone further ahead.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you want something new, ask: who am I comparing myself with? Would I still want it if they did not have one?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Mia gets more pocket money. A month later she feels just as short of money as before. Why, by this idea?',options:['She now compares herself with friends who have more','She has started spending her money carelessly','Pocket money always buys less as time goes on'],answer:0,why:'Her money went up, but so did the people she measures against. So the gap felt the same.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'TWO MONTHS AFTER THE RAISE',text:'You got the raise you had wanted for a year. Two months later nothing feels different. The flat is fine, the car is fine, and somehow the people you notice now are a rung further up.'},{kind:'idea',text:'We measure ourselves against people slightly above us, and that group moves when we do. Each gain resets the reference point, so the satisfaction it brings is temporary by design.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE LOOP',nodes:['You compare with people just above','You gain and catch up','The comparison group moves up','The gap is back'],loop:!0,note:'Veblen treats this as a structure, not a personal weakness.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why more cannot fix it',text:'If what you want is to be ahead, more money cannot satisfy it for everyone, because being ahead is defined by comparison. When a whole society grows richer, the positions everyone was chasing stay exactly where they were.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One step reached, and the flight still climbs into the light. Each step you reach shows you the next one.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Lifestyle inflation, explained',text:'Spending creeps up with pay. Veblen\u2019s account says why: the new income moves you into a new circle, and the new circle sets a new standard. It is not weak will. It is the reference point moving with you.'},{kind:'try',text:'When a raise does not land, look at who you are comparing yourself with, rather than at the number.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'After a promotion, Sam moves to a richer neighbourhood. He can easily afford it, yet he feels poorer than before. What best explains it?',options:['His comparison group moved up along with him','He is spending more than his new salary allows','Richer areas make people less grateful by nature'],answer:0,why:'Nothing about his income fell. The people he measures himself against changed, and so did his sense of position.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'People who are never satisfied with more money simply lack self-control.',reality:'Veblen\u2019s claim is structural. If the goal is relative position, satisfaction is temporary by construction, whatever the person\u2019s character.'},{kind:'idea',text:'What wealth is sought for is standing above others. Standing cannot be achieved by everyone, because it is defined by comparison, so rising wealth does not bring rising satisfaction.'},{kind:'term',word:'Invidious comparison',meaning:'Veblen\u2019s phrase for comparing people in order to rank them by worth or standing. He says he uses *invidious* as a technical word, not as blame, though its everyday hint of envy is hard to shake.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The moving reference point',text:'Each class, Veblen argues, takes its standard from the class just above it. So every gain resets the comparison. Satisfaction lasts only until the new circle comes into view, which is soon. More wealth cannot satisfy a desire whose object was position rather than goods.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Climbing does not shorten the flight above. Each step reached becomes the floor from which the next is measured.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Later evidence',text:'Psychologists later described people drifting back to a baseline after gains, and named it the hedonic treadmill. Some research on income and happiness has also found that relative position matters. Veblen stated the mechanism decades before either line of work.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Not everything people want is positional. Food, health and safety improve lives whatever the neighbours have, and gains in them do last. The bleak conclusion holds for wealth sought as rank. Read as saying no rise in income can improve anyone\u2019s life, it overreaches.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Who exactly is in your comparison group? When did it last change, and what moved it?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'FIND THE EXIT',prompt:'On Veblen\u2019s logic, which change would most likely take one person off the treadmill?',options:['Choosing a measure of a good life in which rank is not the point','Earning enough to pass everyone in their present circle','Moving to a richer area to raise their sense of position','Spending less on display while still ranking people by wealth'],answer:0,why:'More wealth only moves the reference point. The way off is to stop competing on rank, not to win the current round.'}]}},'leisure-class_5':{pictureCaption:'Arrows land at random, and a brush paints a bullseye around each one afterwards. Every shot looks perfect, which is why the hits prove nothing.',bridge:'Ideas 1 to 4 show the mechanism at its strongest. This one asks where Veblen pushed it further than it will go.',basics:{title:'When one idea explains too much',beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'If an idea explains everything, it must be a great idea.',reality:'Not always. An idea that explains everything can\u2019t be checked. Whatever happens, it just says *I told you so*.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Veblen\u2019s main idea is good: some spending is about being seen. But he used it to explain almost everything. That went too far.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE ONE ANSWER FOR ALL',text:'Think of a friend who says *you\u2019re just tired* to every problem. Sad? Tired. Cross? Tired. Sometimes they are right. But if it is always the answer, it stops telling you much.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where he went too far',text:'Veblen said sport, religion, schools and even graduation gowns were all about showing off. Some of that may be true. But he did not test it. He just found a way to fit each one in.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Each arrow sits right in the middle of its rings. But the rings were painted after it landed. So it always looks like a hit.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Keep this',items:['Some spending is about being seen','Showing off can make costly things popular']},right:{title:'Drop this',items:['*Everything* is about showing off','Whatever happens proves him right']}},{kind:'try',text:'Next time someone has one answer for everything, ask: what would show this is wrong? If nothing could, be careful.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Leo says kids only play football to show off. What is the best reply?',options:['Some might, but lots play because it is fun','He is right, because everything is showing off','He is wrong, because no one ever shows off'],answer:0,why:'Showing off is real. But it is one reason among many, not the only one.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE GOWN AND THE CAP',text:'Veblen explains academic gowns and university ceremony as leisure-class display. Maybe. But gowns also mark an occasion, tie a graduate to a tradition and are fun to wear. He does not weigh those reasons. He fits the case in.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Veblen reads almost everything as status competition, and at that width the theory explains too much to be tested. The narrow claim, that some consumption is signalling, has held up well.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why explaining everything is a weakness',text:'A useful theory tells you what you should *not* see. If every case fits, whether people show off or hide their wealth, then no observation could count against it. That is not strength. It means the theory has stopped making a claim.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Every arrow looks like a bullseye, because the rings are painted round it after it lands. A theory that draws its target afterwards can never miss, and so proves nothing.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'The narrow claim',items:['Some spending is signalling','It needs visibility and cost','It can be checked case by case']},right:{title:'The total claim',items:['All culture is status rivalry','Every fact fits','Nothing could disprove it']},note:'The first has been formalised and tested. The second has not survived.'},{kind:'explain',title:'No way to lose',text:'Critics add that every new development can be read as the same pattern continuing. A theory that can absorb any change in fashion, manners or institutions is hard to falsify in practice, however sharp its best observations.'},{kind:'try',text:'When a theory you like explains a new case, ask what result would have counted against it. If you cannot name one, hold the explanation loosely.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A friend argues that a neighbour\u2019s plain old car is also status display, because it signals being above display. What is the problem?',options:['If plain and flashy both count as display, the claim can no longer be tested','Nothing; Veblen showed that every single purchase is a form of display','The neighbour probably cannot afford a newer car, so it is not display'],answer:0,why:'When opposite behaviours both confirm a theory, it has stopped predicting anything. That is the overreach.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE REACH OF THE BOOK',text:'The Theory of the Leisure Class does not stop at spending. It reads religion, sport, the position of women and university ritual as products of the same leisure-class logic. The range is part of its fame, and part of its problem.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The narrow claim, that some consumption is signalling and behaves oddly on price, has held up. The total claim, that leisure-class rivalry accounts for nearly everything, explains too much to be tested.'},{kind:'term',word:'Unfalsifiable',meaning:'Said of a claim that no possible observation could count against. Such a claim may still be true, but it cannot be checked, so it tells you little about the world.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How the strain shows',text:'In the later chapters the explanations become strained. His account of academic dress and university ritual reads as the theory being applied rather than tested: the conclusion is fixed, and the case is made to fit. Every development can be read as the pattern continuing.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The rings go up after the arrow lands, so every shot is a hit. That is the strained case: the conclusion fixed, and the target drawn around wherever the evidence fell.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What survived',text:'The core mechanism was later formalised as costly signalling, and economists call goods whose demand rises with their price *Veblen goods*. That is the honest legacy: a precise mechanism, applied where it can be checked.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'HOW FAR THE CLAIM REACHES',low:'Some spending is signalling',high:'All culture is status rivalry',note:'Veblen is strongest at the left end. The further right he goes, the less the claim can be checked.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The critique can be pushed too far as well. Rejecting the total theory does not mean rejecting its examples: many of Veblen\u2019s cases, dress above all, hold up well on their own. The fault is the claim that the mechanism explains everything, not the mechanism.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which idea do you use to explain almost everything? What would count against it?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE OVERREACH',prompt:'Which use of Veblen keeps the narrow claim rather than the total one?',options:['Testing whether demand for a handbag rises when its price is raised','Explaining a friend\u2019s love of chess as a hidden display of leisure','Reading every new fashion as fresh proof that the theory is right','Treating all charity as a competition for social standing'],answer:0,why:'The handbag case is specific and could come out either way. The others fit any outcome, which is the overreach.'}]}},'leisure-class_6':{pictureCaption:'Two spoons, the same shape, the same shine. Only one has a price tag, and that is the one the eye starts to prefer.',bridge:'Idea 2 said the preference is felt as taste. This one shows taste itself absorbing the price.',basics:{title:'Does the price change what looks nice?',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TWO SPOONS',prompt:'Two spoons look just the same. One was made by hand and cost a lot. One was made by a machine and cost little. Which do most people call nicer?',options:['The handmade one','The machine one','Neither'],answer:0,reveal:'Veblen said most people pick the handmade one, even when they can\u2019t tell them apart. Knowing the cost changes what they see.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two spoons, the same in every way you can see. Only one has the price tag. Does it look nicer to you now?'},{kind:'idea',text:'We often think costly things look nicer, just because we know they cost more. It feels like our eyes are deciding. Often it is the price.'},{kind:'explain',title:'A lawn and a field',text:'Veblen gave another example. A neat lawn looks lovely to us. A field of grass with cows on it would be more useful, and look much the same. But the lawn shows you can keep land that grows nothing.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'AT THE SHOPS',text:'Two hoodies look almost the same. One has a famous name and costs three times more. Be honest. Does it *look* better? Or do you just know it cost more?'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time something looks lovely to you, ask: would I still think so if I did not know the price?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Ana loves a vase. Then she finds out it is a cheap copy that looks the same. Now she likes it less. What changed?',options:['Only what she knew about the price','The shape and colour of the vase','How well the vase holds flowers'],answer:0,why:'The vase did not change at all. Only what she knew changed. So the price was part of why she liked it.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE CARVED TABLE',text:'In a shop you run your hand over a table and think it is beautiful. Then you read the label: machine-made, a fraction of the price you had assumed. You look again, and somehow it seems a little flat.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Our sense of beauty has absorbed the price. We find costly things finer, then experience the judgement as purely aesthetic. The verdict arrives already carrying the invoice.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The spoon',text:'Veblen\u2019s example is a hand-wrought silver spoon beside a machine-made one. In use they are the same. Yet the hand-made one is felt to be finer, not for any quality you can perceive, but because it is known to have cost more.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two spoons, the same shape, the same shine. Only one carries a price tag, and the eye starts to prefer it.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'If you like expensive things, you are just a snob.',reality:'Veblen is not accusing anyone of pretending. The marks of expense have been absorbed into taste so completely that they no longer look like expense. The preference is sincere, which is why it is hard to see.'},{kind:'steps',title:'The test he implies',steps:['Notice something you find beautiful.','Ask what you know about its cost.','Imagine it was cheap and identical in every useful way.','See whether the admiration survives.'],note:'Often it does not, and that feels like being caught rather than like changing your mind.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time something strikes you as beautiful, ask what you know about what it cost, and whether you would say the same not knowing.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A collector\u2019s favourite painting turns out to be an excellent copy. She now finds it less beautiful. What does Veblen\u2019s idea suggest?',options:['Part of her admiration was for its cost and rarity, not its look','The copy must look different, and she has now noticed the flaws','She was never really interested in the painting in the first place'],answer:0,why:'Nothing visible changed, only what she knew about its cost. That is the price inside the aesthetic judgement.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE SPOON, EXAMINED',text:'Veblen sets a hand-wrought silver spoon beside a machine-made one. Both serve equally; the hand-made one is admired more. Then the twist: if the admired spoon proves to be a skilful machine imitation, its value, and the pleasure of owning it, falls sharply, though nothing about it has changed.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Taste is not independent of expense. The marks of costliness have been absorbed into the standard of beauty so completely that they are no longer visible as such, and a price judgement is experienced as an aesthetic one.'},{kind:'term',word:'Pecuniary canons of taste',meaning:'Veblen\u2019s name, and his chapter title, for standards of beauty that have absorbed the requirements of expense. *Pecuniary* means to do with money; *canons* are accepted rules.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The lawn',text:'His second case: a close-cropped lawn is admired over a pasture, though a pasture with grazing animals would be more useful and look much the same. The lawn shows land kept without producing anything. Its beauty and its cost arrive together.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE PRICE DISAPPEARS',nodes:['Costly things mark status','Status marks feel desirable','Desirable comes to feel beautiful','Beauty is judged sincerely'],note:'By the last step the price is invisible, which is why the judgement feels like pure perception.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Some expensive things really are better made, and some beauty owes nothing to cost: a sunset, a cheap print you love. Veblen tends to treat all taste as captured, which overreaches. The test is useful precisely because the answer varies. When admiration survives learning the price, it was not only the price.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Name an object you find beautiful. If you learned it cost a tenth of what you thought, what would change?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'RUN THE TEST',prompt:'Which finding would count most against Veblen\u2019s claim in a particular case?',options:['People keep preferring an item after learning it is a cheap, identical copy','People pay more for hand-made goods than for machine-made ones','People describe expensive items as beautiful rather than as costly','People admire lawns more than they admire working pastures'],answer:0,why:'If the preference survives the price being removed, the price was not doing the work. The other findings are what Veblen predicts.'}]}},'leisure-class_7':{pictureCaption:'A gown whose train sweeps the floor: nobody could scrub, carry or hurry in it. One loose thread, and it already needs replacing.',bridge:'Dress puts ideas 1, 2 and 3 into one object: visible spending, waste and proof of not working.',basics:{title:'What fancy clothes are saying',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'A WHITE PARTY DRESS',text:'A long white dress, spotless, with a train that drags on the floor. You could not play football in it. You could not even eat a messy lunch. That is part of what it says.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Fancy clothes say three things at once. *I spent a lot. I don\u2019t do hard work. I can buy new ones when these go out of style.*'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A gown that sweeps the floor. No one could work in it. That is part of the message.'},{kind:'equation',parts:['Costs a lot','Can\u2019t work in it','Soon out of style'],result:'The perfect show-off',note:'Clothes are worn in front of everyone you meet, all day. So they show off more than almost anything.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why styles keep changing',text:'When a style gets cheap, lots of people can copy it. Then it no longer shows that you are rich. So the rich move on to a new style, and the old one starts to look wrong.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'IN VEBLEN\u2019S DAY',text:'Veblen wrote about clothes in 1899. Back then, rich men wore tall shiny hats. Rich women wore tight corsets. No one could do hard work in them, and that was the point.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Bright white trainers are worn only for walking around town, never for sport. What do they show?',options:['That you don\u2019t need them for anything hard','That they are the best shoes for running in','That you like to keep your sports kit clean'],answer:0,why:'Spotless shoes show they are not used for hard work. Veblen saw the same message in spotless shirts.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE PUREST CASE',title:'Why clothes?',text:'Most purchases stay at home. Clothes go out with you all day, in front of everyone you meet, including strangers who know nothing else about you. That is why Veblen treats dress as his best evidence: it does the whole job of display at once.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Dress sends three signals in one garment: it was expensive, the wearer cannot do manual work in it, and through fashion, last year\u2019s spending no longer counts.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'What it shows',items:['Expense','Unfitness for work','Being up to date']},right:{title:'How it shows it',items:['Fine cloth, known labels','Heels, pale fabric, fragile cuts','A style that will soon pass']}},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE WHITE SHIRT',text:'A pale shirt with a stiff collar shows at once that the wearer is not lifting, digging or fixing anything today. Veblen made the same point about the polished shoe and the tall hat of his own time.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why fashion has to churn',text:'Once a style is cheap enough to copy widely, it stops signalling, so it must be replaced. On Veblen\u2019s account the constant change is structural, not a sign that taste keeps improving. Last season\u2019s look is retired because it no longer separates anyone.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A gown with a train that sweeps the floor. Nobody could work in it, and that is a large part of what it says.'},{kind:'try',text:'Look at what you own and ask which items would stop being worth having if nobody ever saw them.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A style of trainer becomes cheap and common. Soon its first buyers stop wearing it. What does Veblen\u2019s logic say?',options:['It stopped signalling once anyone could copy it','It wore out faster than the more costly kinds','Its first buyers simply grew bored of the look'],answer:0,why:'On his account fashion churns because a widely copied style no longer separates anyone. The shoe did not change; its signal did.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'THREE SIGNALS, ONE GARMENT',left:{title:'The requirement',items:['Expense','Incapacity for work','Novelty']},right:{title:'In 1899 dress',items:['Fine cloth, often renewed','Tall hat, corset, patent shoe','This season\u2019s cut']},note:'Dress meets every requirement of display at once, and is seen by strangers all day.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Dress is the purest case of display: expensive, visible to strangers and constantly renewed. The most effective clothing proves not only expense but that the wearer does not need to work.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Proving a negative',text:'The sharpest point is that the best garments prove what the wearer does not do. A corset, a high heel, a spotless cuff cannot be worked in. Wearing them shows you do not have to. The inconvenience is not a cost of the fashion. It is part of the message.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why fashion churns',text:'Once a style is cheap enough to copy, it stops separating anyone, so it must be replaced. Veblen also notes that styles admired one season look ugly a few years on, which he takes as evidence that taste follows the signal rather than leading it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A gown whose train sweeps the floor, made for someone who never has to bend, carry or hurry.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Veblen gives little weight to dressing for pleasure, belonging or self-expression, all of which are real. The century after him complicates things further: casual clothes and deliberate scruffiness became status markers in some circles. His logic can stretch to cover them, but that stretch is the overreach of idea 5.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which of your clothes would you still wear if nobody would ever see you in them?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'Expensive trainers designed to look worn and dirty sell well. How does Veblen\u2019s analysis handle them best?',options:['The known price carries the signal, and insiders learn to read the look','It cannot: clothes that look worn disprove the whole idea of display','Buyers want to look like manual workers, so no status is involved','Price is irrelevant here, since only the look is visible on the street'],answer:0,why:'The display still needs a cost that viewers know about. Once insiders know the price, a worn look can carry it, though that flexibility deserves suspicion.'}]}},'leisure-class_8':{pictureCaption:'A costly, splendid coat on a man who did not choose it. It is there to say something about whoever employs him.',bridge:'Ideas 3 and 7 showed leisure and dress as signals. Here they are performed by one person on behalf of another.',basics:{title:'Showing off through someone else',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'A BIG HOUSE, LONG AGO',text:'Long ago, a rich man had lots of servants. They wore fancy matching coats. Some just stood by doors in silence. Visitors noticed them right away.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Sometimes the showing off is done by other people. The servant\u2019s fine coat was not about the servant. It was about the rich man who paid for it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A costly coat, and the man wearing it did not choose it. It tells visitors about the person he works for.'},{kind:'term',word:'Vicarious',meaning:'Done by one person for someone else\u2019s sake. Veblen called this showing off *vicarious*: the servants did the showing, but the rich man got the credit.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Wives too',text:'Veblen said the same about rich men\u2019s wives in his time. Their fancy dresses and free days showed off the husband\u2019s money. He said this was real work, and it was not paid.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'TODAY',text:'Think of a kid sent to five clubs a week, with proud parents posting about each one. Some of that is for the kid. Some of it may be showing off the parents.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A fancy hotel buys smart matching coats for all its staff. Who is the show mostly about?',options:['The hotel and its owners','The staff who wear them','The guests who see them'],answer:0,why:'The staff wear the coats, but the message is about the hotel. That is showing off through other people.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'A rich man\u2019s wife living in idle luxury is the one enjoying the leisure.',reality:'Veblen argues her leisure is not leisure. It is a performance required of her, showing off his means. The more visibly useless her pastimes, the better they serve him.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Vicarious consumption is display performed by one person for another. The leisure and the spending are real. But the status they produce belongs to someone else.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it is delegated',text:'Once the man of the house has to work, he can no longer show idleness himself. So the display is handed on. It goes to the wife\u2019s clothes and calendar, the servants\u2019 livery, the entertaining. Veblen stresses that this is work, unpaid and unacknowledged.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A splendid coat on a man who did not choose it. The livery shows off the household, not the person inside it.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE DISPLAY IS HANDED ON',nodes:['The householder must work','Display is handed on','Others perform leisure and spending','Status goes to the householder']},{kind:'example',kicker:'MODERN FORMS',text:'The child whose packed timetable of tutors and lessons shows off the parents\u2019 means. The lobby whose marble tells visitors how solid the firm is. In each case the display is produced by someone other than the person it credits.'},{kind:'try',text:'Notice when someone\u2019s visible comfort is mainly evidence about someone else\u2019s standing. Then ask who actually has the choices.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'An executive\u2019s assistant is expected to wear costly clothes. The job does not need it, and the pay is modest. Who is the display for?',options:['The executive and the firm whose standing it signals','The assistant, who gains status from the clothes','Customers, who need to see staff dressed formally'],answer:0,why:'The assistant produces the display, but it credits someone else. That is what makes it vicarious.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',word:'Vicarious consumption',meaning:'Veblen\u2019s term for consumption and leisure performed by one person on behalf of another, whose standing it establishes. The leisure is real, the effort is real, and neither is to the performer\u2019s credit.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When the head of a household can no longer idle visibly, the display is delegated. Wives and servants consume and idle on his account, and the more useless their occupation, the better it serves.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'THE HOUSEHOLD IN 1899',text:'Veblen\u2019s primary case is the wealthy household of his day: servants in livery, more of them than the work required, and the wife\u2019s elaborate dress and ceremonial duties. All of it, he argues, demonstrates the householder\u2019s means rather than their own.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the word cuts',text:'*Vicarious* carries the argument. The wife\u2019s leisure is not leisure but a performance of it, required of her, unpaid and unacknowledged. Veblen describes this in a flat, clinical tone, which makes it one of the most cutting passages in the book.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Costly livery, plainly not chosen by the man inside it. The splendour is real, and it belongs to someone else.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHO PERFORMS, WHO GAINS',left:{title:'Performs the display',items:['Wife','Servant','Child','Receptionist']},right:{title:'Gains the standing',items:['Husband','Household','Parents','Firm']},note:'In each pair the performer bears the cost; the credit goes across.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The model is built on the late-Victorian household and sits awkwardly on modern ones, where both partners work and choices are shared. It also risks treating the performer as having no motives of their own: a child\u2019s lessons may be for the child, a spouse\u2019s style their own. Ask who chooses before deciding whose display it is.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'WHOSE DISPLAY',prompt:'A firm has junior staff host clients in a showpiece office, in costly clothes, late into the evening. Which reading is most in Veblen\u2019s spirit?',options:['The staff perform the display, but the standing it buys goes to the firm','The staff are enjoying a luxury lifestyle that the firm pays for','The display is wasteful, so it cannot be doing anything for anyone','The clients are the ones whose status the evening establishes'],answer:0,why:'Vicarious display is produced by one party and credited to another. Asking whose standing it builds is the point of the term.'}]}},'leisure-class_9':{pictureCaption:'Deep chairs by a warm fire. Outside, the street is being dug up. From in here, the upheaval is only a view through glass.',bridge:'The leisure class sets the standards that ideas 1 to 8 describe. This one asks why those standards change so slowly.',basics:{title:'Why the comfy don\u2019t want change',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'THE BEST SEAT',text:'Say you have the best seat on the bus: by the window, near the heater. The driver asks if everyone wants to swap seats. Who is least keen? You are. Not because you are mean. You just have the most to lose.'},{kind:'idea',text:'People who are comfy with how things are often don\u2019t want change. This is not because they are bad. Change costs them the most, and problems reach them last.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Warm chairs by a fire. Outside, the street is being dug up. From inside, the trouble is just something you see through glass.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Two reasons',text:'Veblen gave two reasons. First, people who are doing well have the most to lose if things change. Second, they are kept safe from problems. So they are the last to feel that anything needs fixing.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'PICKING TEAMS',text:'The captain picks the teams every week. A teacher says to pick teams by drawing names from a hat. The captain argues hardest against it. Is the captain bad? Not really. The old way just suits the captain best.'},{kind:'try',text:'When someone fights a change, ask: what would they lose if it happened? Ask that before you argue.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Kids at the front of the class get the best view. The class votes on swapping seats. Who is most likely to vote no?',options:['The kids who sit at the front now','The kids who sit at the back now','The new kids who just joined the class'],answer:0,why:'The front-row kids lose the most from a change. That, not meanness, is why they push back.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE CLUB VOTE',text:'A members\u2019 club debates opening up to new members. The loudest objections come from the longest-standing members, who hold the best tables and the committee seats. Their arguments are about tradition. Their position is about the tables.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Those best served by existing arrangements have the least reason to change them, and are the most sheltered from the pressures that force change. The resistance follows from the seating, not the character.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHERE PRESSURE ARRIVES',nodes:['Pressure builds in society','The exposed feel it first','The sheltered feel it last','Change is slowed at the top'],note:'Institutions change under stress. The comfortable are, by definition, those under the least.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it spreads',text:'Veblen adds a twist. The leisure class sets the standards others copy, from manners to what counts as respectable. So its slowness to change spreads well beyond itself, holding back everyone who takes their cues from it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Deep chairs by the fire, and the street being dug up outside. Inside, the upheaval arrives as a view through a window.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'People resist change because they are stupid or selfish.',reality:'Veblen avoids both explanations. Any protected group would be slower to change, whatever its members believe, because the signal that change is needed reaches them last and weakest.'},{kind:'try',text:'When somebody resists a change, work out what the present arrangement is worth to them before you argue with their reasoning.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Senior staff at a firm resist a move to remote work that most junior staff want. What does Veblen\u2019s idea suggest you do first?',options:['Ask what the current setup gives them that remote work would take away','Assume they are simply out of touch with how work has now changed','Accept that their long experience must make them right about it'],answer:0,why:'Start with position: what the arrangement is worth to them. That explains the resistance before you judge their reasons.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'WHO MOVES LAST?',prompt:'A new way of working threatens long-standing arrangements. On Veblen\u2019s account, which group adapts most slowly?',options:['The most educated','The most comfortable','The most ambitious'],answer:1,reveal:'The comfortable, because they are sheltered from the pressure. Veblen adds that the very poor are slow to change too, being too worn down by daily need to spare the effort.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The leisure class is conservative from position, not principle. Change costs most to those most invested in the present, and they are the group least exposed to the pressures that force anyone to reconsider.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The mechanism is exposure',text:'For Veblen, institutions change under the stress of changing material conditions. The wealthy are sheltered from that stress, so their views are formed under conditions that never demand an update. The news that something must change reaches them last and weakest.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Warm chairs, a fire, and through the window the street being dug up. The pressure is real, and it stays on the other side of the glass.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it spreads',text:'Because the leisure class sets the standards of respectability that others imitate, its conservatism travels. What it treats as proper becomes proper for those below, so its slowness becomes a drag on everyone.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'A complaint about character',items:['They are selfish','They are hypocrites','Change their minds']},right:{title:'A claim about position',items:['They are sheltered','Any protected group would act alike','Change their exposure']},note:'Veblen\u2019s claim is the right-hand column. It predicts the pattern without judging anyone.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Position is not destiny. Some comfortable people champion reform, and some exposed groups resist change fiercely when it threatens them. The structural view is also easily used to dismiss every argument from the comfortable as mere interest, which skips the step of checking whether their reasoning is right.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Where do you have the best seat? What change would you be the last to notice was needed?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'Someone says wealthy people opposing a reform must be acting from greed. What is the correction Veblen\u2019s analysis offers?',options:['Position predicts their resistance whatever their motives, so judge the arguments separately','They are acting from greed, which is exactly what Veblen meant by position','Wealthy people are better informed, so their opposition should settle it','Their opposition shows that the reform is probably harmful to society'],answer:0,why:'Veblen explains the pattern by exposure and stake, not vice. That predicts resistance, but it does not settle whether the reform is right.'}]}}}},2979,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={leviathan_1:{pictureCaption:'A diagram chalked on a floor, still being drawn. It is not a map of anywhere: it is a model drawn to reason with.',basics:{title:'What if nobody was in charge?',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'A SNOWBALL FIGHT',text:'Two kids each have a big pile of snowballs. Both are cold. Both want to stop and go in. But neither puts their pile down first. Why not? If you drop yours, the other kid might still throw.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Thomas Hobbes asked a *what if*. What if nobody was in charge? No rules, no police, no one to sort out fights. His answer: everyone would be scared of everyone. Not because people are bad, but because no one dares to stop first.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO WAS HOBBES',text:'Thomas Hobbes lived in England almost 400 years ago. A war inside his own country was tearing it apart. In 1651 he brought out a book about power called *Leviathan*.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO KINDS OF QUESTION',left:{title:'A history question',items:['What did happen?','You check old records','It needs real facts']},right:{title:'A what-if question',items:['What would happen if\u2026?','You think it through','It needs good reasons']},note:'Hobbes was mostly asking the second kind.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A hand draws in chalk on the floor. It is not a map of a real place. It is a drawing to help you think, like Hobbes\u2019s *what if*.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why even kind kids keep a pile',text:'Say you would never throw first. The other kid does not know that. So they keep their pile, just in case. Now you must keep yours too. Nobody is mean. Both are just careful. That is what keeps the fight going.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A friend says Hobbes was wrong, because people never lived in one big war. What is the best reply?',options:['He mostly asked what would happen if no one was in charge','He was right, because long ago everyone fought all the time','He meant that all people are mean deep down'],answer:0,why:'Hobbes\u2019s question was a *what if*, not a story about the past. And his point was fear, not meanness.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'A COMMON COMPLAINT',belief:'Hobbes thought early humans lived in constant war, and prehistory proves him wrong.',reality:'He was not mainly doing prehistory. He was asking what remains when enforcement is removed: no courts, no police, nobody whose word is final. That is a thought experiment, and it has to be judged as one.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Take away anyone who can enforce a rule, and even peaceful people must arm, because no one can afford to be the only one who disarms. The problem is not wickedness. It is fear that makes sense.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW PEACEFUL PEOPLE ARM',nodes:['You cannot tell who is peaceful','So you arm, just in case','Your arming alarms your neighbour','So your neighbour arms too'],loop:!0,note:'Each step is sensible on its own. Together they produce exactly the danger everyone feared.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'AT WORK',text:'Two departments each keep their data to themselves, because each assumes the other does. Both would be better off sharing. Neither will go first. Nobody in either team is wicked. Each is being reasonably careful, and the result is bad for both.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The way out',text:'Hobbes\u2019s exit is not persuading people to be nicer. It is a power everyone can rely on to punish whoever breaks the peace first. Once that exists, disarming stops being a gamble. That, on his account, is why people would submit to a sovereign at all.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A chalk sketch on a floor, not a map of anywhere. Hobbes\u2019s state of nature is drawn the same way: to reason with, not to record.'},{kind:'try',text:'When someone dismisses an argument as bad history, ask which it is: a claim about what happened, or a test of what would happen. Then judge it by the right standard.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Two firms have sued each other for years. Each would like to drop its case, but whoever drops first is exposed to the other\u2019s. What does Hobbes\u2019s analysis point to?',options:['A settlement that a court will enforce on both at once','A long meeting where each side promises to behave better','Waiting until one firm runs out of money and gives up','Deciding which firm is the selfish one and blaming it'],answer:0,why:'Neither side lacks goodwill. Each lacks assurance. Something both can rely on to hold the other to the deal makes stopping safe.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'PARIS, THE 1640S',text:'Hobbes left England in 1640, afraid for his safety as the quarrel between king and Parliament grew. He spent the next decade in Paris, and wrote *Leviathan* there while the civil war ran its course at home. It was published in 1651. The state of nature was written by a man watching one from across the Channel.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The state of nature is a thought experiment: subtract every power able to enforce anything, and ask what rational people would do. Hobbes\u2019s answer is mutual fear, because disarming alone is a risk no one can sensibly take.'},{kind:'term',kicker:'A MODERN NAME',word:'Security dilemma',meaning:'A term from the study of international relations. The steps one side takes to feel safe make the other side feel less safe, so both arm, and both end up less secure than before.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What he actually claims',text:'Hobbes says he does not believe this condition was ever general across the world. Much of his evidence is present, not prehistoric: rulers facing each other armed and watchful, civil war, and the citizen who locks his doors and chests even with laws and officers in place. What, he asks, does that say about his opinion of his neighbours?'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A diagram in chalk, the hand still drawing it. A model built to reason with, which is how the state of nature asks to be read.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Two limits. Hobbes also pointed to some peoples of the Americas as living this way, which was European prejudice, not observation. And the experiment removes too much. Reputation, kinship and repeated dealing enforce a great deal without a sovereign; Elinor Ostrom\u2019s work showed communities governing shared resources for generations by rules they made and enforced themselves. Fear is real, but it is not all that is left when the courts go.'},{kind:'recap',points:['It is a thought experiment, so judge it as one.','Mutual fear is sensible for each person and ruinous for all.','The exit is something both sides can trust, not better people.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'A critic says: early hunter-gatherer bands were not at constant war, so Hobbes is refuted. What is the strongest response?',options:['It targets a history claim he mostly did not make; ask what enforced their peace','It refutes him completely, since his whole argument depends on the distant past having been violent','It is beside the point, because Hobbes never mentioned any real people or places at all','It confirms him, because those bands lived without any fear of one another'],answer:0,why:'Hobbes asks what is left when enforcement is gone. Bands with custom, kin and reputation still had enforcement, and that is the real pressure on his argument.'}]}},leviathan_2:{pictureCaption:'A bare field with nothing built on it, and one figure wrapped tight against it. Nothing was destroyed here. Nothing was ever started.',bridge:'Idea 1 set up the thought experiment. This one shows what Hobbes said it costs, and why the cost is poverty before it is violence.',basics:{title:'Nobody builds what can be taken',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE DEN IN THE WOODS',prompt:'You build a den in the woods. Every night someone smashes it. After a week, what do you do?',options:['Build it bigger and better','Stop building it at all','Build it again, just the same'],answer:1,reveal:'Most people stop. Not because they are lazy. There is just no point in building what will be taken away.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Thomas Hobbes said that when no one keeps the peace, nobody builds, plants or learns. Why work for something that can be taken? That, not wild people, is what makes life so hard.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The part people skip',text:'Hobbes ended a list with five harsh words. Life would be lonely, poor, nasty, brutal and short. People remember those words. But first he listed what would be missing. No farms. No good houses. No books, no art, no towns where people live side by side.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Nothing has been built on this field, because nothing built here could be kept. So one person stands alone, wrapped up tight.'},{kind:'flow',nodes:['No one keeps the peace','What you make can be taken','So nobody plants or builds','So everyone stays poor'],note:'Each step makes sense. The end is a life with almost nothing in it.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A MONEY JAR',text:'Saving your pocket money only makes sense if the jar is safe. If someone can empty it any time, you spend it today. Hobbes thought a whole country works the same way.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A town market gets robbed every week, and no one stops it. What would Hobbes expect to happen next?',options:['Fewer people bother to grow things and sell them','People work harder to grow even more things','Nothing changes, because people still need food'],answer:0,why:'When work can be taken, people stop doing it. The town gets poorer, even though no one there is lazy.'}]},standard:{title:'What goes first without security',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A FREE PLOT OF LAND',prompt:'You are given land for nothing. The catch: anyone may take what grows there, and no one will stop them. Do you plant an orchard that fruits in five years?',options:['Yes, an orchard is a sound long-term investment','No. You plant nothing that pays back slowly','Yes, as long as you can fence it yourself'],answer:1,reveal:'Almost nobody plants. You are not lazy or wicked. You simply cannot count on being the one who picks the fruit, so the sensible horizon shrinks to today.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Without a common power, the return on anything slow can be taken, so nobody plants, builds or studies. Hobbes\u2019s famous grim list is about time horizons, not savagery.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The clauses that get dropped',text:'Before the five famous words, Hobbes lists what goes missing: farming, trade by sea, solid building, knowledge of the earth, the reckoning of time, arts, letters, society. Every one of them pays back slowly. The grim ending is the result of that list, not a separate verdict on human nature.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'SAME PEOPLE, TWO HORIZONS',left:{title:'With security',items:['Plant this year, harvest in five','Build a workshop, use it for decades','Study now, earn later']},right:{title:'Without it',items:['Eat what you have today','Own only what you can carry','Learn only what keeps you safe']},note:'The people are the same in both columns. Only what they can count on has changed.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A field with nothing built on it: no wall, no tool, no house. Not because nobody could build, but because nothing built could be kept.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Not monsters',text:'Hobbes\u2019s people are not savages. They are rational and reasonably afraid, and they cannot afford to invest. The poverty follows from the horizon, not from malice. That is why he values an enforcing power so highly: it lengthens how far ahead anyone can plan.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one rule you resent because it is enforced. Picture the alternative honestly, hour by hour, with nothing enforcing it. What would you stop bothering to do?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'In a region where contracts are rarely enforced, small firms stay small and seldom buy machinery. What is Hobbes\u2019s explanation?',options:['When returns can be taken, slow investment stops being worth making','The owners there are less ambitious than owners elsewhere','Machinery costs too much when local markets are small','People there simply prefer trading to making things'],answer:0,why:'The horizon shrinks before the effort does. Nobody puts money into what may not still be theirs when it pays back.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'CHAPTER 13',title:'Where the passage sits',text:'The famous description closes a chapter on what Hobbes calls the natural condition of mankind. He argues that people are roughly equal, since even the weakest can kill the strongest, and that three things set them against each other: competition, distrust and the desire for glory. The grim list is where that argument lands.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Life without a common power is miserable chiefly because nothing cumulative is possible. Every investment needs a future you can count on, and fear takes the future away first.'},{kind:'term',word:'Time horizon',meaning:'How far ahead a person can sensibly plan. A farmer planting trees has a horizon of years; someone expecting to be robbed tonight has a horizon of hours. Hobbes\u2019s list of absences is a list of long horizons.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Read the list in order',text:'The passage moves from industry, farming and building, to knowledge, arts and letters, to society, then to continual fear of violent death, and only then to the five famous words. The miseries at the end are consequences of the absences at the start. Quote the ending alone and the argument disappears.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Bare ground in every direction, and one figure wrapped against it. Nothing here was destroyed; nothing was ever begun.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Hobbes doubts this condition was ever general and offers it as an analysis of what law removes. But his evidence is mixed: rulers facing each other armed, civil war, and a claim about peoples of the Americas that was prejudice, not observation. And the argument is often used too quickly. That some enforcement is necessary does not show that any particular coercion is.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Take one institution you rely on and dislike. Describe the next day without it, hour by hour. Which of your plans would shorten first?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISREADING',prompt:'Which reading of the passage would Hobbes reject?',options:['People without government are savage by nature, so harsh rule is what they deserve','The worst cost of insecurity is that slow, cumulative work stops happening','Rational people stop investing once the returns can simply be taken','Rulers with no common power above them live partly in this condition'],answer:0,why:'His people are rational and afraid, not savage. The misery comes from the missing horizon, not from a vicious human nature.'}]}},leviathan_3:{pictureCaption:'Many hands, each adding one small piece to a crown. No single hand could make it. It exists because they built it.',bridge:'Ideas 1 and 2 showed life with no one in charge. This is Hobbes\u2019s account of how someone comes to be in charge.',basics:{title:'Who gives a ruler power?',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'AN OLD ANSWER',belief:'Kings rule because God chose them, or because they were born to it.',reality:'Many people in Thomas Hobbes\u2019s time believed that. Hobbes said no. A ruler\u2019s power comes from the people under it. They agree to hand it over.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Power is something people make. Lots of people agree to let one ruler decide, so they can live in peace. The power comes from that deal, not from God or from birth.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Lots of hands build one crown, piece by piece. No one hand could make it. It only exists because they all add a piece.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'IN THE PLAYGROUND',text:'Your class picks a captain for a football game. Everyone agrees to follow her calls. Is she in charge because she was born to it? No. She is in charge because you all said yes.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Can you take it back?',text:'Hobbes said no. Once the deal is made, breaking it brings back the fear and the fighting. But later thinkers saw a twist. If people made the power, people can remake it. They used his own idea to argue the other way.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your club has always had its oldest member as leader. Someone asks why. What does this idea let you say?',options:['People made that rule, so people can look at it again','Old rules are the best, so it should never change','The oldest member is always the wisest, so it is fair'],answer:0,why:'A rule people made is a choice, not a law of nature. So it is fair to ask if it still makes sense.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE RULE NOBODY CHOSE',text:'Your team never releases anything on a Friday. Nobody remembers why. The rule is defended on the grounds that it has always been so. Somebody, once, agreed it for a reason. Whether that reason still holds is a question nobody asks.'},{kind:'idea',text:'In Hobbes, the sovereign\u2019s power comes from an agreement among the governed, not from God or blood. He used that to justify near-absolute rule. Later readers used the same premise to justify removing it.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO SOURCES OF POWER',left:{title:'Divine right',items:['Power comes down from God','Passed on by birth','To question it is sin']},right:{title:'Hobbes\u2019s covenant',items:['Power comes up from the people','Made by each agreeing with each','To question it is politics']},note:'Hobbes kept the strong ruler. What he changed was where the strength comes from.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why Hobbes said you cannot undo it',text:'Each person agrees with every other to give up the right to govern themselves, on condition that everyone else does the same. Having made that covenant, you cannot unmake it without dissolving the one thing standing between you and the war of all against all.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What later readers did',text:'They kept the premise and reversed the conclusion. If authority is built, it can be rebuilt. John Locke, the American founders and later theories of legitimate revolution all rested government on agreement, a premise much like the one Hobbes had built for the opposite purpose.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Many hands, each adding a piece. The crown exists because they built it, and what hands built, hands can take apart.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one rule you live under at work or at home. Find out who agreed it, when, and why. Then ask whether that reason still holds.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A manager defends an old sign-off process by saying it has been in place for twenty years. Which response uses Hobbes\u2019s premise?',options:['Ask what it was agreed for, and whether that reason still applies','Accept it, since rules that last have proved themselves by lasting','Quietly ignore it, since nobody now on the team agreed to it','Push to scrap it, since every old rule is probably a bad one'],answer:0,why:'A constructed rule can be examined. Its age is no reason either way; the reason it was made is what to check.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'timeline',kicker:'A PREMISE CHANGES HANDS',points:[{when:'1651',what:'Hobbes grounds sovereign power in a covenant among the governed, to defend near-absolute rule.'},{when:'1689',what:'John Locke publishes his Two Treatises, grounding government in consent that can be withdrawn.'},{when:'1776',what:'The American Declaration of Independence rests government on the consent of the governed, and claims a right to replace it.'}],note:'The premise was much the same. The conclusion was turned round.'},{kind:'idea',text:'For Hobbes, authority is constructed by a covenant among those who will be ruled. He built that premise to make the sovereign unbreakable. It outlived his conclusion, because anything constructed can be examined and remade.'},{kind:'term',word:'Covenant',meaning:'In Hobbes, an agreement in which at least one party performs later and must be trusted meanwhile. The founding covenant is made by each person with every other, not with the ruler.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the ruler is not a party',text:'Because subjects covenant with one another and not with the sovereign, the sovereign has promised nothing and so cannot break the agreement. Subjects cannot claim release on the ground that the ruler broke faith. This is the clever and troubling part of the design: it makes the power irrevocable by construction. Idea 5 shows the one exit he does allow.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Many hands assembling one crown from small pieces. Hobbes needed it built that way. His successors noticed that what is built can be rebuilt.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The word covenant carries a heavy load. Hobbes counts submission to a conqueror, made out of fear, as just as binding as an agreement freely made, which stretches consent almost to nothing. Nobody alive actually signed. And the premise cuts both ways: that a rule was made does not mean it should be reopened whenever it is inconvenient.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Hobbes moved the source of authority from God or blood to agreement.','He used it to make rule unbreakable; others used it to justify revolution.','A constructed thing can be examined, which is not the same as discarded.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A club\u2019s founders insist its rules can never change, because members joined on those terms. Which reply uses Hobbes\u2019s premise against his conclusion?',options:['Rules made by agreement can be remade by agreement, so the terms can be reopened','The founders are right, since a covenant once made can never be undone','Members can leave whenever they like, so the rules hardly matter','Rules bind only when they were handed down by some older authority'],answer:0,why:'That is the move Locke and the revolutionaries made: keep the idea that authority is built, and drop the claim that it can never be rebuilt.'}]}},leviathan_4:{pictureCaption:'Two hands point firmly at the same block, and each means something different by it. Until they say what, they can argue forever.',bridge:'A step back from the politics: before any of it, Hobbes spends chapters on what words mean, because he thought arguments go wrong there first.',basics:{title:'Many fights are about one word',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'IS A HOT DOG A SANDWICH?',prompt:'Two friends argue for an hour about whether a hot dog is a sandwich. They agree on every fact about the hot dog. Why can\u2019t they agree?',options:['One of them has the facts wrong','They mean two things by *sandwich*','One of them just likes to argue'],answer:1,reveal:'To one, a sandwich is anything in bread. To the other, it needs two slices. They are not fighting about the hot dog. They are using one word for two things.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Lots of fights start because people use the same word for two things, and don\u2019t notice. Thomas Hobbes said: before you argue, agree what your words mean.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two hands point at the same block. Each person means something different by it. Until they say what, they can argue forever.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHY HOBBES CARED',text:'Thomas Hobbes wrote a famous book about power in 1651. Before the politics, he spent whole chapters on words. He thought thinking is a bit like doing sums. If your words keep changing what they mean, the sum comes out wrong.'},{kind:'steps',title:'When an argument goes round and round',steps:['Stop arguing for a moment.','Find the word you both keep using.','Each of you says what it means to you.','Agree on one meaning, then carry on.']},{kind:'question',prompt:'You and your brother argue about who did a fair share of the chores. You both agree on what each of you did. What should you try?',options:['Ask what each of you means by *fair share*','Make a longer list of every chore you did','Ask a parent to say which of you is right'],answer:0,why:'You agree on the facts, so the fight must be about the word. Sort out what *fair* means and the fight may end.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'IS IT DONE?',text:'Two colleagues have argued for forty minutes about whether the project is done. They agree on every fact: the code works, the guide is not written, the client has not seen it. Each grows more frustrated that the other cannot see the obvious.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Most stuck disputes are two definitions colliding: one word doing two jobs, unnoticed. Hobbes fixes his definitions early in Leviathan because reasoning on shifting words is worthless, however careful.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Reason as reckoning',text:'Hobbes thought reasoning is a kind of adding and subtracting: you combine names and work out what follows. If a name means one thing at the start and another at the end, every step can be valid and the answer still wrong. The error hides, because each step followed from the last.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'COUNTERS AND COINS',text:'Hobbes compares words to counters, the tokens people once moved on a board to do sums. The wise use them to calculate and remember they stand for something else. Fools treat them like coins, valuable in themselves, and accept them on the authority of whoever spent them.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two hands, one block, two meanings. Neither is pointing wrongly. They have just not said what they are pointing at.'},{kind:'try',text:'In your next stuck argument, stop and ask each side to define the central word in one sentence. Do it before the next round, not after.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A team argues for weeks over whether a new hire has been a success. Both sides cite the same numbers. What is the cheapest next step?',options:['Ask each side what it means by *success*','Gather more numbers until the case is clear','Let the most senior person present decide','Wait another quarter and then look again'],answer:0,why:'When the facts are agreed and the argument goes on, the dispute is almost always about the word. Defining it costs a minute.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'A BOOK LEFT OPEN',text:'John Aubrey, who knew Hobbes, tells how at about forty Hobbes came across a copy of Euclid lying open in a gentleman\u2019s library. He read one theorem, thought it impossible, and followed the proofs back step by step until he was convinced. From then on, Aubrey says, he was in love with geometry.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Reason is reckoning with names, so a reckoning is only as good as the definitions it starts from. Hobbes settles his key terms first, as geometry does, which is why Leviathan reads as definitions before it reads as politics.'},{kind:'term',word:'Absurdity',meaning:'For Hobbes, not mere silliness but a technical failure: words joined so that they signify nothing, such as a round square. He thought it comes chiefly from reasoning without settled definitions.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How errors multiply',text:'A loose definition early on is not a small error. Each later step treats it as fixed, so the mistake is carried forward and compounded, and the conclusion can be absurd while every inference looks sound. Hobbes even thought people with plain common sense fare better than those who reason carefully from bad definitions.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two people point at the same object and mean different things by it. No argument between them can finish until that is said aloud.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Not every dispute is verbal. Some survive perfect definitions, because people want different things. And defining is itself a move: whoever fixes the terms often wins. Hobbes is a case in point. He defines injustice as breaking a covenant, so where there is no power to make covenants safe, nothing can be unjust. The definitions do political work before the politics begins.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which word sits at the centre of a disagreement you keep having? Write your definition. Would the other side sign it?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these is a misuse of Hobbes\u2019s advice to define terms first?',options:['Insisting on a definition of fairness that settles the dispute in your favour','Asking both sides to say what they mean by fairness before going on','Noticing that two people agree on every fact and still disagree','Checking whether a key word shifts meaning during an argument'],answer:0,why:'Defining is meant to expose a disagreement, not to win it in advance. A definition the other side would reject only hides the real dispute.'}]}},leviathan_5:{pictureCaption:'A hand signs over a whole bundle of keys but keeps one back, closed in the other fist. Everything is surrendered except the reason for surrendering.',bridge:'Idea 3\u2019s covenant hands the ruler almost everything. This is the one thing Hobbes says it cannot hand over.',basics:{title:'The one thing no deal can make you do',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE CLUB RULE',text:'You join a club. You promise to do what the leader says. Tidy up? Fine. Carry the bags? Fine. Then one day the leader tells you to jump off the roof. Do you have to?'},{kind:'idea',text:'Thomas Hobbes gave rulers nearly all the power. But he said one thing no deal can take away. You never have to let yourself be killed. Staying safe was the whole point of the deal.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One hand signs and hands over a big bunch of keys. The other hand keeps one small key back. That key is your own life.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHY THE LINE IS THERE',nodes:['People want to be safe','So they give a ruler power','The ruler must keep them safe','An order to die makes no sense'],note:'The line is not a kind extra. It comes from why the deal was made.'},{kind:'explain',title:'He went further',text:'Hobbes said even a person who has been sentenced to death may fight back. And nobody has to tell on themselves. He knew this would hardly ever work. But he said the right is still there.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of one thing no friend, team or club could ever make you do. Knowing your line makes the smaller choices easier.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A team captain says you must play on with a bad head injury, because you promised to follow orders. What would Hobbes say?',options:['No promise can make you accept being badly hurt','You promised, so you must do what you are told','Only the coach can say when you may stop'],answer:0,why:'You joined to be part of the team, not to get badly hurt. Staying safe was the point of the deal.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'NEARLY EVERYTHING, EXCEPT',prompt:'Hobbes hands the sovereign almost unlimited power. Which right does he say a subject keeps?',options:['To speak freely against the ruler','To choose their own public religion','To defend their own life'],answer:2,reveal:'Not speech, not religion. Only this one, and it comes from his own logic. People submit to a sovereign to stay alive. So nothing they agreed to can require them to die.'},{kind:'idea',text:'No covenant can bind you to submit to your own destruction. The exception is small but load-bearing. It admits that authority is justified by protection, and fails without it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Logic, not mercy',text:'The reasoning comes from inside his system, not from a moral principle. Every right is given up in exchange for security. A surrender that puts you in danger defeats its own purpose. So no agreement can require it. Hobbes is not being kind. He is being consistent.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'HOW FAR HE TAKES IT',text:'A condemned man may resist the officers who come to execute him, even though the sentence is lawful. Nobody is bound to accuse himself. These rights are useless in practice, since resisting is unlikely to work. But Hobbes insists they are not lost.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A signature hands over the whole bundle of keys, bar one, kept in the other fist. Everything goes except the reason for giving it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'When protection stops',text:'The same logic runs the other way. Obedience was owed in exchange for protection. A sovereign that can no longer protect you has, on Hobbes\u2019s account, ended your duty to obey it. The limit comes from the purpose of the deal, not from a list of rights attached to it.'},{kind:'try',text:'Write down the one thing no contract, employer or loyalty could make you do. Knowing where your line is makes every lesser bargain easier.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A contract says staff must follow all instructions. A manager orders someone onto scaffolding that is visibly unsafe. How does Hobbes\u2019s reasoning apply?',options:['An agreement made for your benefit cannot require you to accept your own destruction','The contract was signed freely, so every instruction under it binds','The worker may refuse only if a union backs the refusal','Work contracts fall outside anything Hobbes\u2019s reasoning covers'],answer:0,why:'The limit comes from the purpose of the deal. A job is taken to live better. So an order that risks your life defeats the reason obedience was owed.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE USUAL CHARGE',belief:'Hobbes\u2019s sovereign is unlimited, and subjects keep no rights at all.',reality:'He gives the sovereign nearly everything, then names one thing it cannot have. Nobody can be obliged to give up the right to defend his own life, because preservation was the point of the covenant.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The right to resist your own destruction survives every covenant, because a surrender made for the sake of safety cannot coherently require you to die. Authority is justified by protection, and obligation lapses when protection fails.'},{kind:'term',kicker:'A WORD FROM CONTRACT LAW',word:'Consideration',meaning:'What each side receives in return for what it gives. In Hobbes\u2019s scheme, protection is the consideration for obedience. Remove it and the obligation has nothing left to rest on.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The extensions',text:'Hobbes follows the logic further than readers expect. A condemned man may resist his executioners. No one is bound to accuse himself, and a covenant not to defend yourself against force is void. The obligation of subjects lasts as long as the power able to protect them, and no longer.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Everything signed away bar one small key, kept in the fist. The sovereign gets the whole bundle except the reason for handing it over.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The right is real and nearly empty. It lets the condemned man struggle, not escape, and it covers life and body only, not speech, worship or property. A sovereign that protects you while oppressing you keeps your obedience. And the protection clause cut awkwardly in his own day: royalists suspected it licensed submission to whichever regime had won.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Every right is surrendered for security, except security itself.','The condemned may resist; no one must accuse themselves.','Obedience lasts only as long as protection does.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HARD CASE',prompt:'A ruler is cruel and unjust but keeps the country safe from invasion and civil war. On Hobbes\u2019s logic, do subjects still owe obedience?',options:['Yes: protection is what obedience was owed for, and it is still delivered','No: cruelty breaks the covenant between the ruler and the subjects','No: any injustice by the ruler restores the full right of rebellion','Yes, but only from the subjects who personally made the covenant'],answer:0,why:'The exception is about protection, not justice. That is the argument\u2019s strength and its most uncomfortable limit. Nor is the ruler a party to the covenant, so there is no contract for cruelty to break.'}]}},leviathan_6:{pictureCaption:'A crowned giant whose whole body is made of small human figures. The ruler is not set above the people. The ruler is made of them.',bridge:'Idea 3 said authority is built by the people under it. The book\u2019s title page shows that before you read a word.',basics:{title:'A giant made of people',beats:[{kind:'picture',kicker:'LOOK CLOSELY',caption:'Look at the giant\u2019s body. It is not one big person. It is made of hundreds of small people, all packed together. Only the crown sits on top.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE FRONT OF THE BOOK',text:'Hobbes\u2019s book *Leviathan* came out in 1651. At the front is a famous picture, a bit like the one you just saw. A giant king rises over the land. Look closely, and his body is made of tiny people.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The picture tells you the whole book before you read a word. The ruler is not above the people. The ruler is made out of them. His power is their power, put together.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE CARDS IN A STADIUM',text:'At some big games, every fan holds up one coloured card. From far away the cards make one giant picture. No single fan is the picture. But without the fans, there is no picture at all.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What is in his hands?',text:'In one hand the giant holds a sword. That stands for the power to make laws and keep the peace. In the other he holds a bishop\u2019s staff. That stands for power over the church. Hobbes wanted both in one ruler.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your class makes one big poster. Each person paints one small square. How is that like Hobbes\u2019s giant?',options:['The big picture is made of everyone\u2019s small part','The best painter gets to sign the whole poster','The teacher paints it while the class watches'],answer:0,why:'The giant is made of the people, just as the poster is made of all the squares.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'THE TITLE PAGE, 1651',text:'Before the first chapter, *Leviathan* opens with an engraving. A crowned giant rises over hills and a walled town, holding a sword and a bishop\u2019s crozier. Look closer and its body is made of hundreds of small human figures, all turned inward towards its head.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The frontispiece is the book\u2019s thesis in one image. The sovereign is not above the people; it is composed of them, and its authority is theirs, pooled.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A crowned figure built from small human shapes. Not the original engraving, but its idea: the ruler\u2019s body is the people.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'THE PANELS BELOW',left:{title:'Civil power',items:['A castle','A cannon','A battle']},right:{title:'Church power',items:['A church','The thunderbolts of excommunication','A formal disputation']},note:'Each power is paired with its instrument. Hobbes wants both columns held by the same hands.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Constituting, not crushed',text:'The small figures face the head, not the viewer. They are not being trampled by power; they are what it is made of. The whole book argues that the sovereign\u2019s authority is authorised by those it rules, and the picture makes the claim before a word is read.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before you next present an argument, try drawing it as one image. If you can, put it first. If you cannot, ask whether the argument is as clear as you think.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'CORRECT THE READING',prompt:'A colleague says the frontispiece shows a tyrant towering over a crushed people. What is the best correction?',options:['The people are the giant\u2019s body: its power is theirs, pooled','The giant is a god, showing that kings are chosen from above','The figures are prisoners, showing the price paid for order','The giant is the church, shown standing above the king'],answer:0,why:'Hobbes\u2019s sovereign is composed of its subjects. The image shows authority built from below, not imposed from above.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE OTHER HAND',prompt:'The giant holds a sword in his right hand. What did Hobbes put in his left?',options:['A book of laws','A bishop\u2019s crozier','A pair of scales'],answer:1,reveal:'A crozier: spiritual as well as civil authority, held by a single body. It is Hobbes\u2019s most contested claim, and he put it on the title page.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The engraving argues the thesis visually: the sovereign is one artificial person made of many natural ones, holding sword and crozier together. Authority is pooled from below and undivided at the top.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Reading the details',text:'The name comes from the sea monster in the Book of Job, a creature no earthly power can match, and a line from Job runs across the top. The body is made of subjects facing the head. Below, paired panels set each civil power beside a spiritual one: castle and church, cannon and excommunication, battle and disputation.'},{kind:'term',word:'Artificial person',meaning:'Hobbes\u2019s term for one who acts in the name of others. Many people authorise a single sovereign to act for them all, so the sovereign\u2019s acts count as their own.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A crowned figure whose body is all small human shapes. The drawing keeps the engraving\u2019s central claim: there is no ruler apart from the ruled.'},{kind:'limits',text:'A picture persuades without arguing. This one shows perfect unity: every figure in its place, every one turned the same way. It cannot show the dissenters, the conquered or those who never agreed, and Hobbes\u2019s theory struggles with them too. An argument that fits in one image may have left out whatever does not fit.'},{kind:'recap',points:['The sovereign is composed of the people, not set above them.','Sword and crozier: civil and church power in one body.','The image shows the claim, and also hides the dissent.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'TEST THE IMAGE',prompt:'Which feature of the engraving would a critic point to as the weakest part of its case?',options:['Every figure faces the head, so the picture leaves no room for dissent','The sword stands for civil power, which most readers already accepted','The landscape shows a real town, which grounds the giant in daily life','The crown stands for sovereignty, which every theory of rule assumes'],answer:0,why:'The unanimity is both the argument and its flaw. A real people includes those who did not agree, and the picture simply leaves them out.'}]}},leviathan_7:{pictureCaption:'A signed agreement, two seals, and a blade laid across it. The seals record the promise. The blade is what makes it hold.',bridge:'Idea 1 ended on a power everyone can rely on. This is why Hobbes thought agreement alone could never supply it.',basics:{title:'A promise needs something behind it',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE SWAP',text:'You and a girl from another class agree to swap. Your football card for her sticker book. She says: give me yours today, and I\u2019ll bring mine tomorrow. Do you hand it over?'},{kind:'idea',text:'Everyone agrees promises should be kept. The hard part is going first. Thomas Hobbes said a promise only holds when something makes breaking it cost more than keeping it.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A REFEREE',text:'The rules of football are written down. But a game stays fair because there is a referee. Take the referee away and the rules are still on paper. The players just stop trusting each other to follow them.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A signed promise with a blade lying across it. The paper says what was agreed. Something else makes people keep it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Good rules, with a catch',text:'Hobbes wrote down rules that help people live together. Seek peace. Keep your promises. Don\u2019t be cruel. He said everyone should want to follow them. But you can only follow them safely when others have to as well.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your class agrees that everyone will bring a snack to share on Friday. What makes the plan most likely to work?',options:['The teacher checks who brought one','Everyone says they really mean it','You hope people remember on the day'],answer:0,why:'A check makes keeping the promise the easy choice. Meaning it is nice, but it is not the part that holds.'}]},standard:{title:'Promises with nothing behind them are only words',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE HONEST-PEOPLE THEORY',belief:'If people are honest enough, agreements will hold on their own.',reality:'Hobbes does not deny that honour and conscience exist. He denies they are reliable enough to build on. Whoever keeps a promise first is the one exposed, and the structure fails if good faith lapses once at scale.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Agreement is not the binding part. A promise with nothing to enforce it is only words; what makes it hold is a power that makes breaking it cost more than keeping it.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE FIRST-MOVER PROBLEM',nodes:['We agree, and you go first','I gain if I then defect','You foresee this, so you wait','Nobody performs at all'],note:'The sword does not make anyone trustworthy. It changes the payoff, so going first stops being a gamble.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Theorems, not commands',text:'Hobbes lists nineteen laws of nature: seek peace, keep your agreements, do not be cruel, and more. He is careful to call them theorems about what helps people survive, not commands. They bind in conscience always, but in action only where some power makes keeping them safe.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'BUYING FROM A STRANGER',text:'Some online marketplaces hold a buyer\u2019s payment until the goods arrive. Neither side has to trust the other. Breaking the deal simply stops paying. That is Hobbes\u2019s solution on a small scale: change the cost of breaking faith, not the character of the people.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A signed agreement with two seals, and a blade laid across it. The seals record the promise. The blade is what makes it hold.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before you agree to anything that matters, ask what makes keeping it safer than breaking it, for both sides. Build that before you sign.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Two flatmates agree that one pays a large bill now and the other repays half next month. On Hobbes\u2019s logic, what makes the deal safest?',options:['A written agreement either of them could enforce in court','A sincere promise from the second flatmate to repay','An understanding that friends need no written terms','The first flatmate\u2019s trust in the other\u2019s good character'],answer:0,why:'Sincerity and character are real but unreliable. Something that makes defaulting costly is what lets the first flatmate safely go first.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'TWO COURTS',word:'In foro interno',meaning:'Latin, roughly \u201cin the inner court\u201d: binding on desire and conscience. Hobbes says the laws of nature always bind this way, but bind outward action only where keeping them is safe.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The laws of nature are theorems about survival, not commands. They oblige you always to want peace, but to act on them only when a power makes performance safe. Without that power, agreements are only words.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The hinge of the book',text:'Hobbes has argued that rational people would agree to cooperate. Now he points out that agreement is not the binding part. Whoever performs first is exposed, since the other gains by defecting if defecting is safe. The sword solves this by changing the payoff, not the sentiment. Nobody needs to become trustworthy.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why not honour?',text:'Hobbes does not deny that pride or conscience keep some people to their word. He denies they are reliable enough to build on. A structure meant to hold a country together fails if they lapse once at scale. Fear of a common power is the passion he thinks can be counted on.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A sealed agreement with a blade laid across it. The paper records the promise; on Hobbes\u2019s account, the sword is what makes it hold.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Enforcement is not the only glue. Most agreements are kept with no court in sight, through reputation, repeated dealing and habit. And the sword raises its own problem: someone must hold it, and Hobbes\u2019s sovereign is party to no covenant. What makes keeping faith safe from the enforcer? His critics pressed that question hardest.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Agreeing was never the hard part. Performing first is.','The laws of nature bind conscience always, action only where it is safe.','Enforcement changes the payoff, not the person.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'Two countries sign a treaty to cut their weapons, with no inspections and no court above them. What would Hobbes predict?',options:['Each will hesitate to disarm first, since the other gains by cheating','The treaty will hold, since both sides sincerely agreed to it','The treaty will hold, since breaking a promise is plainly wrong','Both will disarm quickly, since war is so costly to each side'],answer:0,why:'Between sovereigns there is no common power to make performance safe. Without some check, the treaty is only words, which is why such agreements usually come with inspections.'}]}},leviathan_8:{pictureCaption:'A wall of regulations, densely lettered, and one blank panel at the end. On Hobbes\u2019s account, that blank is the whole of a subject\u2019s liberty.',bridge:'Idea 5 found the one right no covenant removes. This is the rest of a subject\u2019s freedom, and how thin Hobbes makes it.',basics:{title:'Your freedom is what the rules leave out',beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'HOUSE RULES',left:{title:'In the rules',items:['Bed by nine','No phones at dinner','Homework before games']},right:{title:'Not in the rules',items:['What you draw','Which friend you call','What you build in your room']},note:'Everything on the right is up to you. Hobbes would call that your freedom.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Thomas Hobbes said your freedom is the space the rules don\u2019t cover. Where the rules say nothing, you choose. Where they speak, you don\u2019t.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A big board full of rules. One part at the end is still empty. That empty part is where you are free.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The catch',text:'Hobbes said the ruler could fill that empty space at any time. One new rule tomorrow, and that freedom is gone. So for him, freedom was not a wall the ruler could not cross. It was just the part not yet written.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'COUNT THE RULES',text:'Two schools both say their pupils are free. One has five rules. The other has fifty, about your hair, your bag and your shoes. Which gives you more freedom? Hobbes would say: don\u2019t read the poster. Count the rules.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A new club says its members are totally free. Then you read its rule book, and there is a rule for everything. How free are you, by Hobbes\u2019s idea?',options:['Not very, because the rules cover nearly everything','Totally free, because the club says so','Free, as long as you like the rules'],answer:0,why:'Hobbes measured freedom by what the rules leave out. Saying you are free does not make it so.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'TWO HANDBOOKS',text:'One firm\u2019s staff handbook opens with a page about freedom and trust, then runs to eighty pages of procedure. Another has no statement of values and six rules. Which gives its people more room to decide for themselves? The values page will not tell you. The page count might.'},{kind:'idea',text:'For Hobbes, the liberty of subjects lies wherever the law is silent: whatever the sovereign has not regulated. It is a thin definition, but it makes liberty something you can count rather than feel.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What his list includes',text:'He gives examples: to buy, sell and make contracts with one another, to choose where you live, what you eat and your trade, to bring up your children as you think fit. None is a right the sovereign must respect. Each is simply something the sovereign has so far left alone.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A wall of regulations with one blank panel at the end. That blank, on Hobbes\u2019s account, is the whole of a subject\u2019s liberty.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Liberty means areas of life the state is forbidden to enter.',reality:'That is a later liberal view. For Hobbes, liberty is only what has not yet been legislated, and the sovereign could legislate it tomorrow. He was not embarrassed by this. His aim was to show why authority must be undivided.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick a system you belong to: a workplace, a school, an app. Ignore what it says about freedom. Count what it actually specifies, and see what is left.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A constitution declares sweeping freedoms, but its laws regulate what you may print, where you may travel and whom you may meet. By Hobbes\u2019s measure, how free are its people?',options:['Only as free as the unregulated space left over, which is small','Very free, since their constitution guarantees their freedoms','Impossible to say without knowing whether rulers are elected','Free in spirit, whatever the detailed rules happen to say'],answer:0,why:'Hobbes reads freedom off the silence of the law, not its declarations. Count what is prescribed, and the declaration matters little.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO IDEAS OF LIBERTY',left:{title:'Hobbes',items:['Whatever the law has not yet regulated','No boundary the ruler must respect','Shrinks with every new law']},right:{title:'Later liberals',items:['A protected sphere around the person','Limits the state may not cross','Defended by rights against the ruler']},note:'Hobbes\u2019s version is thinner, and deliberately so.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Liberty, for Hobbes, lies in the silence of the laws: whatever the sovereign has passed over, where each may act as his own reason suggests. It is no territory protected from authority, only a gap not yet filled.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Liberty in general',text:'In the same chapter Hobbes defines liberty in physical terms: the absence of external obstacles to motion. Water walled in by a vessel is not free to spread; water with no walls is. A subject\u2019s liberty applies the same idea to law. It is the room the law\u2019s walls have not yet enclosed.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What he gains',text:'Coherence. Hobbes never has to say where the boundary between authority and liberty sits, or who settles disputes about it, because there is no boundary, only a silence which may be filled. That suits his larger aim, which was never to fence off a private domain but to show why sovereignty cannot be divided.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A long board of regulations with its last panel left blank. On Hobbes\u2019s account that panel is the subject\u2019s whole liberty, and nothing stops it being filled.'},{kind:'limits',text:'What he loses is any protection once the silence ends. If liberty is only what has not yet been regulated, nothing stops tomorrow\u2019s law from taking it, and much later liberal thought was spent building that missing protection. Counting also misleads: one law against criticising the ruler can weigh more than a hundred rules about trade.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'COUNT, THEN WEIGH',prompt:'An app has only three rules, but one lets it ban any user for any reason. By Hobbes\u2019s measure, how free are its users, and what does that measure miss?',options:['Largely free by count, but the measure misses how much one rule can weigh','Completely free, because only three rules are written down','Not free at all, because any rule at all cancels liberty','Free only if the users voted for the three rules themselves'],answer:0,why:'Hobbes\u2019s measure counts silence. It cannot see that one rule giving unlimited discretion makes every silence provisional.'}]}},leviathan_9:{pictureCaption:'A church door and a courthouse door side by side, with one bolt fixed across both. Two institutions, one final authority.',bridge:'Idea 6\u2019s giant held a bishop\u2019s crozier as well as a sword. This is the argument behind that second hand, and what it cost Hobbes.',basics:{title:'Two bosses means a fight',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'TWO BEDTIMES',text:'Your mum says bedtime is eight. Your gran, who lives with you, says nine. Both think they get the last word. Neither of them is being mean. The trouble is that there are two last words.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When two people both get the last word, the fights never stop. Thomas Hobbes said the church and the ruler can\u2019t both be the boss. He put the ruler in charge of both.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHY HOBBES THOUGHT THIS',text:'In Hobbes\u2019s time, a war split England. Part of the fight was about religion. Church leaders and rulers both said they spoke for God. People did not know whom to obey. Hobbes thought that was a big cause of the war.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A church door and a court door side by side. One bar is fixed across both. Hobbes wanted one ruler to hold both doors.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'HE GOT IN TROUBLE',text:'Half of Hobbes\u2019s book is about the Bible. It made many people angry. Some said he did not believe in God at all. Later he was stopped from putting out more books on religion. But he never took back what he wrote.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Two team captains both think they choose the plays. They keep clashing. What would Hobbes say is the real problem?',options:['Two people both claim the last word','One of the captains is just being bossy','The players are not listening hard enough'],answer:0,why:'Even two kind captains will clash if both get the final say. The fix is to decide who has it.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE HALF NOBODY READS',text:'Roughly half of Leviathan is about scripture: who wrote the books of the Bible, what the kingdom of God means, who may interpret it. Most readers skip it. Yet it is where Hobbes took the real risk.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Public interpretation of religion must belong to the civil sovereign. Rival authorities, each claiming to speak for God, are what produced the war Hobbes had fled. He paid for saying so for the rest of his life.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW TWO MASTERS MAKE A WAR',nodes:['A church commands in God\u2019s name','The ruler commands in the law\u2019s name','Subjects must choose whom to obey','Divided obedience becomes civil war'],note:'Neither side need be wicked. The structure alone is enough.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A church door and a courthouse door, with one bolt fixed across both. Hobbes\u2019s answer: a single authority holding both.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'WHAT IT COST HIM',text:'He was accused of atheism. The book was attacked by clergy and in print. After the monarchy was restored in 1660, he was effectively barred from publishing on religious subjects in England. He never withdrew the argument.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why goodwill does not fix it',text:'If two authorities can both claim the last word, every serious disagreement becomes a contest over who has it. Better people would still face that question. The dispute is structural. So the answer has to be structural too: settle where the last word sits.'},{kind:'try',text:'Find one decision at work or at home where two people can both claim the last word. Agree who actually has it, before the next disagreement rather than during it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A company\u2019s product head and sales head can each veto launches, and they keep deadlocking. What is Hobbes\u2019s diagnosis?',options:['The structure gives two people the last word, so it will keep clashing','The two heads need to build a better working relationship','Sales should always defer, because the product matters more','Deadlock is healthy, because it forces careful decisions'],answer:0,why:'No amount of goodwill settles a dispute about who decides. The fix is deciding where the final say sits.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'LONDON, 1666',text:'In 1666 the House of Commons, debating a bill against atheism and profanity, asked its committee to look into Leviathan. Hobbes, then in his late seventies, was alarmed enough to write on the law of heresy. He was never prosecuted, but his later writings on religion went unlicensed in England.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A subject who obeys a church independently of the sovereign has two sovereigns, and divided sovereignty returns you to war. So Hobbes puts the public interpretation of scripture under the civil power, and his own fate became evidence for the thesis.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How he gets there',text:'Parts Three and Four reinterpret scripture at length. Hobbes doubts that Moses wrote all the books attributed to him. He reads the kingdom of God as a real, civil kingdom, once in ancient Israel and again after Christ\u2019s return, not a church ruling now. And he denies the clergy any power independent of the sovereign, attacking the papacy above all.'},{kind:'term',word:'Erastianism',meaning:'The view that the state should hold authority over the church, including in religious disputes. It is named after Thomas Erastus, a sixteenth-century Swiss theologian; Hobbes is among its most thorough defenders.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Church door and court door in one wall, one bolt across both. Hobbes\u2019s settlement: two institutions, one final authority.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Putting religion under the sovereign hands the state power to dictate public worship and punish dissenters, which Hobbes accepted so long as private belief was left alone. The later liberal answer to the same structural problem went the other way: separate church and state. And his own punishment fits his thesis neatly but proves less than it seems. Authorities punish critics who are wrong, too.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Where in your own life do two authorities both claim the last word? Is the clash about the issue, or about who decides?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'An international court and a national supreme court each claim the final say over the same case. On Hobbes\u2019s analysis, what follows?',options:['The clash is structural, and goodwill between the judges will not settle it','The dispute will settle once both courts read the law carefully','The court with the older tradition will naturally win out','Two final authorities are safer, since each will check the other'],answer:0,why:'Where two bodies can both claim the last word, the real question is who has it, not what the law says. Hobbes thought only settling that ends the dispute.'}]}}}},2980,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'lombard-street_1':{pictureCaption:'More tokens on the counter than coats on the hooks, because the rest are out on loan. Fine, until every token comes back at once.',basics:{title:'Why a bank can\u2019t pay everyone at once',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'MAYA KEEPS THE MONEY',prompt:'Ten friends each give Maya \xa35 to keep. She lends \xa340 of it to her aunt till next month. Can Maya pay her friends back?',options:['Yes, on any day they ask','Yes, but not if they all ask on one day','No, not ever'],answer:1,reveal:'Maya has \xa310 in hand. She can pay one or two friends. She can\u2019t pay all ten today. The money is not lost. It is just with her aunt.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A bank keeps only a bit of your money in its safe. It lends the rest out, and earns a little extra when it is paid back. That works because people don\u2019t all ask for their money on one day.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Lots of tokens. Only a few coats on the hooks. The rest are out on loan. That only works if people don\u2019t all come back at once.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW A RUN STARTS',nodes:['A rumour: the bank is in trouble','Some people take their money out','Others see, and rush too','The bank runs out of cash'],note:'This rush is called a run. Each person in it is being sensible. If you wait, there may be none left.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why \u201ckeep calm\u201d does not work',text:'If the others will rush, rushing is smart. So asking people to stay calm won\u2019t stop a run. Walter Bagehot wrote a book on London\u2019s banks in 1873. His fix: make sure that rushing first gets you nothing extra.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your club keeps everyone\u2019s money, and has lent most of it to the school fair till next week. Why could one rumour cause trouble?',options:['If all ask at once, there is not enough on hand','The club has lost the money for good','Lending money out is always a mistake'],answer:0,why:'The money is safe at the fair. The trouble is timing. The club can pay a few people, but not all of them on one day.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'A QUEUE OUTSIDE THE BANK',text:'You hear your bank may be in trouble. Walking past, you see a queue outside. You have no idea whether the rumour is true. You join the queue anyway, and you are right to: if it is true, the people at the front get paid and the people at the back may not.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A bank lends out money it has promised to return on demand. That is not a flaw to be fixed; it is the business. It works because depositors don\u2019t all ask at once, and it fails within days when they do.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TRUE FOR ONE, FALSE FOR ALL',left:{title:'Any one depositor',items:['Can withdraw today','Is paid in full','Never notices the lending']},right:{title:'All depositors at once',items:['Cannot all be paid','The front of the queue wins','Find most of the money lent out']},note:'The promise is true for each of you, and false for all of you together.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the queue is not stupid',text:'On an ordinary day withdrawals are unconnected: one person buys a car, another pays a bill. A panic connects them. Everyone reasons the same way, and each is right to, because whoever waits gets what is left. A run is sensible people reacting to each other.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'More tokens than coats on the hooks, because the rest are on loan. Each token can be honoured. All of them at once cannot.'},{kind:'explain',title:'So what fixes it?',text:'Not speeches about calm. Walter Bagehot, editor of The Economist, argued in *Lombard Street* (1873) that the cure has to change the arrangement, not the mood: make sure that withdrawing early gives nobody an advantage. Remove the reason to run, and the run has nothing to feed on.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A gym sells far more memberships than it has machines, because most members rarely come. Which view fits this idea?',options:['That is the business, and it fails only if everyone turns up at once','The gym is cheating its members and should sell fewer memberships','Members should be persuaded to come in less often than they do','The gym is badly run and will almost certainly fail within a year'],answer:0,why:'Like a bank, the gym\u2019s promise holds because use is spread out. The weak point is the day everyone comes together, not the overselling itself.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'LONDON, 1873',text:'Walter Bagehot named his book after Lombard Street, the City of London street where the money market did its business. It starts from a fact most readers preferred not to dwell on: a bank owes its depositors money on demand, and has lent most of that money out.'},{kind:'term',word:'Fractional reserve',meaning:'Keeping only part of what depositors are owed as cash, and lending the rest. It is the ordinary form of banking, not a special gamble taken by careless banks.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Banking rests on a promise that is true for each depositor and false for all of them together. The mismatch is not a defect to engineer away. It is what a bank is, and it breaks when withdrawals stop being independent.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW INDEPENDENCE BREAKS',nodes:['Ordinary days: withdrawals unrelated','Doubt about one bank','Waiting becomes costly','Everyone withdraws together'],note:'The last step is not hysteria. A depositor who waits while others withdraw may get nothing, so running is the individually sensible choice.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why calm cannot be ordered',text:'If running is rational, persuasion fails: no speech changes the arithmetic of the queue. So the remedies in *Lombard Street* are structural. Bagehot is not trying to make depositors braver. He wants an arrangement in which withdrawing early confers no advantage, which removes the reason to run.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A cloakroom that has issued more tokens than it holds coats. Each token is good. The arrangement depends on them not all arriving together.'},{kind:'limits',text:'\u201cThat is just the business\u201d can be stretched to excuse any fragility. Some are only bad practice: a mismatch far larger than ordinary days could ever justify. And removing the reason to run has its own cost, because a guarantee can encourage the very risks it protects against. Ideas 3 and 4 are Bagehot\u2019s attempt to handle that.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A start-up takes a year\u2019s fees upfront and spends them on growth. What does Bagehot\u2019s idea say to ask first?',options:['What happens if many customers ask for refunds in the same week?','How do we stop taking payment upfront, since that is the risk?','How do we persuade customers never to ask for a refund?','Has the founder been honest about where the money went?'],answer:0,why:'Spending prepayments is the business, as lending is a bank\u2019s. The danger is correlated demand, so plan for the week the refunds arrive together.'}]}},'lombard-street_2':{pictureCaption:'A cellar full of goods worth a fortune, and an empty purse. Rich on paper is not the same as able to pay today.',bridge:'Idea 1 showed how a run starts. This one asks which banks deserve help when it does.',basics:{title:'Rich, but no cash today',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE ICE-CREAM VAN',text:'Leo has a huge card set. It is worth a lot of money. The ice-cream van stops outside. Leo has no coins. He can\u2019t pay for an ice cream with a card set. Not today, anyway.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Owning a lot is not the same as having cash right now. A bank can own more than it owes and still be stuck, because it can\u2019t turn what it owns into cash fast enough.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A cellar packed with good things to sell. An empty purse. This person is not poor. They just can\u2019t pay today.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO KINDS OF STUCK',left:{title:'Lost it all',items:['The money is gone','Waiting will not help','Should close']},right:{title:'Short of cash today',items:['The money is tied up','Fine in a few months','Needs a short loan']}},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it is hard to tell',text:'In a panic, people are scared. Nobody wants to buy things. So even good things seem worth less. For a while, a bank that is fine and a bank that is broke can look just the same.'},{kind:'explain',title:'A simple test',text:'Walter Bagehot wrote about London\u2019s banks in 1873. His test was simple. Lend to a bank that can hand over good things to hold until it pays back. Things that would be worth a lot on a normal day.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Mia\u2019s family owns a big house. Their car breaks down, and the repair must be paid today. They have no cash. What is their problem?',options:['Short of cash today, not poor','They have lost all their money','Their house is now worth nothing'],answer:0,why:'The house is still worth a lot. It just can\u2019t pay a bill today.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'A GOOD YEAR, NO CASH',text:'A builder finishes three houses worth far more than she owes. None is sold yet. Her suppliers want paying on Friday. On paper she is well off. On Friday she cannot pay, and if she is forced to sell this week, the houses go for a fraction of their value.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A bank can own more than it owes and still fail, because its assets cannot be sold today at anything like their value. Confusing \u201ccannot pay now\u201d with \u201ccannot pay\u201d is how a scare becomes a collapse.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Insolvent',items:['Owes more than it owns','The money is gone','Should be allowed to fail']},right:{title:'Illiquid',items:['Owns more than it owes','The money is tied up','Sound in three months, if it survives']},note:'Two different problems with one symptom: neither can pay today.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the difference vanishes in a panic',text:'In a panic nobody wants to buy, so no asset has a reliable price, and every institution looks doubtful at once. An illiquid bank forced to sell at panic prices can make itself insolvent. Value that existed is destroyed by the rush to sell it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A cellar full of goods and an empty purse in hand. Not poor. Just unable to pay today.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Bagehot\u2019s sorting rule',text:'Bagehot did not ask lenders to judge each bank\u2019s true worth mid-crisis. He gave them a test they could apply that week: lend against collateral that would be sound in normal times. It is imperfect and usable under pressure, which is what a crisis rule has to be.'},{kind:'try',text:'List what you could pay with on Tuesday: cash, and anything you could sell at a fair price within a day. Set it against what falls due this month. That is your liquidity, not your net worth.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A friend says: \u201cI\u2019m worth a lot on paper, so I can\u2019t have a cash problem.\u201d What is the best reply?',options:['Worth on paper and cash on Tuesday are different things','Then sell everything at once, so the risk is gone','Paper value is not real, so you are actually poor','A cash problem only happens to careless people'],answer:0,why:'Net worth measures what you own. Liquidity measures what you can pay with now. A person, like a bank, can have plenty of one and none of the other.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'LONDON, MAY 1866',text:'Overend, Gurney and Company, one of the largest discount houses in London, failed in May 1866. The Bank of England had refused to rescue it. The panic that followed fell on sound firms as well as unsound ones. Bagehot watched it as editor of The Economist, and his book is shaped by what he saw.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Insolvency and illiquidity are different problems that look identical in a panic. One bank has lost the money and should fail. The other has it tied up, and fails anyway without help, destroying value that existed.'},{kind:'term',word:'Illiquid',meaning:'Holding assets that cannot be turned into cash quickly without a heavy loss. An illiquid bank may own more than it owes. An insolvent one does not.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW A SCARE BECOMES A COLLAPSE',nodes:['Depositors withdraw','The bank must sell assets now','Panic prices: assets fetch little','Losses make it insolvent'],note:'The forced sale turns a timing problem into a real loss. Help that arrives in time stops the loss from ever existing.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The sorting problem',text:'The lender would like to save the illiquid and let the insolvent go. But in a panic no asset has a reliable price, so true worth cannot be measured. Bagehot\u2019s answer is the good-security test: lend against what would be sound collateral in normal times, and let that do the sorting.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The goods are real, and so is the empty purse. A lender who values the cellar at panic prices lets this household fail.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The test is rough. Collateral judged by normal times assumes normal times will return. If the panic reflects a real fall in values, lending against old prices rescues the insolvent at the lender\u2019s cost. And the line blurs: a bank illiquid for long enough often becomes insolvent. Bagehot chose a rule that works under pressure, not one that is exact.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'A critic objects that in a panic nobody can tell solvent banks from insolvent ones, so the distinction is useless. What is Bagehot\u2019s answer?',options:['You need not tell them apart directly: lend on good collateral and let it sort them','You can tell them apart, if you audit every bank before lending anything','Then lend to every bank on any terms, since mistakes cost less than collapse','Then lend to none of them, since a wrong rescue costs more than a failure'],answer:0,why:'The good-security test swaps an impossible judgement for one that can be made in the week it is needed. It is imperfect, and it is operable.'}]}},'lombard-street_3':{pictureCaption:'The strongroom door is wide open and the hands are welcome. But the tag at eye height says this money has a price.',bridge:'Idea 2 asked which banks to help. This is Bagehot\u2019s rule for how to help them.',basics:{title:'Three rules for helping in a panic',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'EXAM MORNING',text:'It is exam day. Half the class has forgotten a pen. The teacher has a big box of spares. But she hands them out slowly, one by one. Soon kids start to grab. They fear there won\u2019t be enough.'},{kind:'idea',text:'In a panic, the helper should lend lots, and fast, so the fear stops. But the help should cost something. And the helper should hold something good until it is paid back.'},{kind:'equation',parts:['Lend lots, fast','At a high price','Holding something good'],result:'A panic that stops',note:'Leave out any one part and the rule breaks.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why each part matters',text:'Lend lots: if you are stingy, people get more scared. A high price: then only those who really need help will ask. Something good to hold: then the helper is not left with the cost of someone else\u2019s mess.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The door is wide open, so no one needs to push. But look at the tag. The help has a price.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'BACK TO THE PENS',text:'So the teacher holds up the box and says there are plenty. Anyone can take one. But you leave your library card with her, and you stay after to tidy up. Kids who have their own pen keep using it.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'On sports day lots of teams run out of water. You run the big water tank. Which plan follows the rule?',options:['Plenty for any team, for a high fee, if they leave a bag till they pay','One small cup for each team, so that the tank lasts all day long','As much as anyone wants, for free, with no questions asked at all'],answer:0,why:'Plenty stops the panic. The fee means only teams in need come. The bag means you are not left out of pocket.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'YOU HOLD THE CASH',prompt:'Banks are short of cash and frightened. You are the one institution that can lend. What do you do?',options:['Lend a little, carefully, to make it last','Lend as much as needed, at a high rate, against good collateral','Lend as much as needed, cheaply, to anyone'],answer:1,reveal:'Rationing feels prudent and confirms the fear. Cheap and unconditional ends this panic and pays for the next one. Bagehot\u2019s rule sits between them, and needs all three of its parts.'},{kind:'idea',text:'In a panic, a central bank should lend freely, at a high rate, on good security. Freely ends the fear of running out. The high rate means only the needy borrow. Good security keeps the lender out of bad bets.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'A rescue',items:['Plenty of cash','At a rate that stings','Only against sound collateral']},right:{title:'A subsidy',items:['Plenty of cash','Cheap','Against anything offered']},note:'Both stop this panic. Only one makes the next panic less likely.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why rationing backfires',text:'When help is handed out grudgingly, every bank assumes it may be the one refused, and hoards whatever it has. Half-measures do not save half the system. They confirm the fear that caused the run.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The door is wide open, so nobody needs to fight their way in. The tag at eye height is the price, and it is meant to be seen.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The part that disciplines',text:'The high rate stops the rescue being a reward. A bank that can borrow cheaply in every crisis has less reason to avoid the next one. At a penalty rate, help is there, but nobody plans on needing it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'In a panic, a central bank lends to every bank at a low rate against any collateral. What has it got wrong?',options:['It ends this panic but rewards the risks that cause the next','Nothing: lending freely is the whole of the rule','It should have lent sparingly, to the strongest banks only','It should have waited for the market to settle first'],answer:0,why:'Freely is one part of three. Without the high rate and good security, a rescue becomes a subsidy.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'THE ROLE BEING DEFINED',word:'Lender of last resort',meaning:'The institution that lends when no one else will, because it holds the final reserve. The idea is older than Bagehot. *Lombard Street* gave it working rules.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Lend freely, at a high rate, on good security. Each clause is load-bearing: together they supply cash in a panic without rewarding the recklessness that produced it.'},{kind:'steps',title:'What each clause is for',steps:['Freely: half-measures confirm the fear and spread the run.','At a high rate: only the genuinely needy borrow, and the facility is not used opportunistically.','On good security: the lender does not absorb the losses of firms that deserve to fail.'],note:'Drop any one and the rule becomes either useless or a subsidy.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The difficulty in one sentence',text:'Every crisis lender faces the same bind. Lend too little and the panic wins. Lend too easily and you teach institutions that recklessness will be rescued. Bagehot\u2019s rule answers both at once, which is why central banks still argue about it by his name.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'An open door with a price on it. Freely and expensively are not opposites. The rule needs both in the same doorway.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Each clause is harder than it sounds. A rate high enough to deter opportunists can mark borrowers as weak, so they stay away until it is too late. Good security in a crash depends on prices nobody trusts. And under political pressure the high rate is easily dropped, which turns a rescue into a subsidy that invites the next crisis.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of someone rescued cheaply from a problem of their own making. Did the rescue make the same problem more likely next time?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these claims to follow Bagehot but does not?',options:['Lending without limit, at a low rate, against any collateral','Lending without limit, at a high rate, against sound collateral','Charging the same high rate to every borrower who comes','Refusing a loan to a firm whose collateral is worthless'],answer:0,why:'Lending freely is one clause of three. Without the rate and the security it is the subsidy the rule was built to avoid.'}]}},'lombard-street_4':{pictureCaption:'A lifeboat hangs ready in full view on a calm day. Help that everyone can see before trouble starts is what stops the rush.',bridge:'Idea 3 gave the rule. This one says when to tell people about it: before anyone needs it.',basics:{title:'Tell the plan before the trouble',beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'You only need a plan once the trouble starts.',reality:'A plan that people know in advance can stop the trouble from starting at all. Just knowing help is there is often enough.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'AT THE THEME PARK',text:'Before you go in, your dad says: if we get split up, go to the big clock and wait there. Later you lose sight of him. Your heart jumps, but you don\u2019t panic. You know just what to do, and what he will do.'},{kind:'idea',text:'If people know ahead of time that help will come, most panic never starts. Not knowing is a big part of what makes people panic.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A boat hangs ready on a calm day, in plain view. Knowing it is there keeps people calm.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Plan told before',items:['You know where to go','You stay calm','You may not need help']},right:{title:'Plan made up during',items:['You don\u2019t know what happens','You rush and grab','The panic spreads first']}},{kind:'explain',title:'Back to the bank',text:'People run on a bank because they don\u2019t know if help will come. Walter Bagehot wrote about London\u2019s banks in 1873. He said the bank in charge should tell its plan to all, early. Then there is no reason to rush.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Won\u2019t people get lazy?',text:'If help is sure, some might take silly risks. Bagehot had an answer. The help should cost a lot. It is there, but nobody wants to need it.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'On day one your teacher says: stuck on homework? I help at lunch, but you lose ten minutes of break. What does telling it early do?',options:['Kids stop worrying, and only come if they need to','Nobody will ever get stuck on homework again','Every kid will now come along at lunch'],answer:0,why:'Knowing help is there takes away the worry. The cost means only kids who need it come.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THREE WEEKS OF SILENCE',text:'Your company says job cuts are being considered, then gives no details for three weeks. By the second week your best colleagues have taken other offers. The cuts might have been small. The uncertainty did the damage on its own.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A rule known in advance prevents panics that a rule applied afterwards can only end. If everyone knows the lender of last resort will act, and on what terms, most reasons to run disappear.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'UNCERTAINTY AS A CAUSE',nodes:['Will help come? Nobody knows','Each bank guards its own cash','Lending between banks freezes','The feared shortage arrives'],note:'Nothing in this chain needs a real loss. Doubt about the rescuer is enough.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the announcement does the work',text:'People run because waiting might leave them with nothing. Announce, before any crisis, that support exists and on what terms, and waiting becomes safe. A market that knows help is there on stated terms rarely needs to use it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The lifeboat hangs uncovered in plain view on a calm day. Passengers who can see help is ready have no reason to rush for it.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Promising a rescue just encourages people to need one.',reality:'That is the serious objection, usually called moral hazard. Bagehot\u2019s answer is the price: the support is announced, and it is deliberately expensive. You can count on it, and you will not want to use it.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one thing at work or at home that could go wrong suddenly. Write down now what will happen and who decides, and tell the people it affects.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A school expects snow closures this winter. Which approach follows Bagehot?',options:['Publish now how closures will be decided and announced','Decide each morning, depending on how bad it looks','Keep the policy vague, so that parents stay flexible','Promise never to close, so that nobody worries'],answer:0,why:'A known rule removes the scramble of uncertainty. Deciding on the day leaves everyone guessing at the same moment.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE PART INSTITUTIONS RESIST',text:'Bagehot did not only say what the Bank of England should do in a panic. He insisted that it say so publicly, beforehand. The Bank preferred to keep its options open. Bagehot regarded that silence as part of the disease.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Uncertainty about whether help will come is itself a cause of panic. A rule announced in advance does most of its work without being used. Announced only in the crisis, it can end a panic but not prevent one.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Help shown before any storm: the lifeboat hangs ready where the whole deck can see it. Its work is done by being seen, not by being launched.'},{kind:'term',word:'Constructive ambiguity',meaning:'Leaving it unclear whether, or on what terms, a rescue will come, so that institutions cannot rely on one. Central banks have generally preferred it to Bagehot\u2019s clarity.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Announced in advance',items:['Fewer panics start','Support rarely drawn on','Risk-taking may grow']},right:{title:'Kept ambiguous',items:['Doubt can start panics','Support drawn in a rush','Risk-taking held in check']},note:'Bagehot named the trade precisely: clarity lowers the chance of a panic and raises the chance of the behaviour that causes one.'},{kind:'explain',title:'His answer to moral hazard',text:'The objection is that a guaranteed rescue encourages the conduct that needs rescuing. Bagehot\u2019s reply is the penalty rate from idea 3: announce the support, and make it expensive. The guarantee removes the reason to run without removing the reason to be careful.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The price answers moral hazard only in part. A firm sure of surviving any panic at a cost may still take risks it would avoid if failure were possible. Nor can every crisis be described in advance; a promise drawn for the last panic may not fit the next. After a century and a half the argument is unsettled.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which rule in your organisation exists only in someone\u2019s head, to be decided in the crisis? What would writing it down change?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'A regulator says: \u201cIf we promise support, banks will take more risk.\u201d What is Bagehot\u2019s strongest reply?',options:['Promise it, on terms expensive enough that nobody plans on using it','Banks never change their behaviour in response to promised support','Risk-taking stops mattering once the panic itself has been prevented','Keep the promise secret, so that only the central bank knows of it'],answer:0,why:'He accepts the objection and answers it with price. Whether the price is enough is the part that has never been settled.'}]}},'lombard-street_5':{pictureCaption:'Generations of feet wore this hollow into the step. The newest boot lands in exactly the same place.',bridge:'Ideas 1 to 4 set out the mechanism and the rule. This one asks why both still fit, 150 years on.',basics:{title:'An old book that still fits',beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'LONDON, 1873',text:'In 1873 a man named Walter Bagehot wrote a book called *Lombard Street*. That is a street in London where lots of banks were. He ran a paper about money, called The Economist. He knew the bankers he wrote about.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What happened in 2008',text:'In 2008, banks all round the world got into deep trouble at once. The people trying to fix it kept pointing to Bagehot\u2019s book. A book from before cars and phones still fit.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Bagehot\u2019s book still fits because banks still make the same promise: your money back when you ask. While the promise stays the same, the same trouble keeps coming back.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Feet have worn a dip in this old step. The newest boot lands in the very same dip.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A PUDDLE AT SCHOOL',text:'There is a dip in the playground. Each time it rains, a puddle forms in the same spot. New kids splash in it every year. The puddle keeps coming back because the dip is still there.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'If an old rule still works, that is just good news.',reality:'It is good to have the rule. But it also tells you the problem never went away. The same weak spot is still there.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A school corridor has flooded every winter for 50 years. A note from a past head says just what to do. What does that tell you?',options:['The leak was never fixed, so the trouble comes back','The past head must have been a weather expert','Floods happen much less now than they used to'],answer:0,why:'When old advice still fits so well, it is because the cause of the trouble has not changed.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO WROTE IT',text:'Walter Bagehot edited The Economist and knew the men who ran the City of London. *Lombard Street* is not a theory of banking. It describes how the money market really worked. It shows who held reserves, who lent to whom overnight, and where the chain would break.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Bagehot described one institution in one century. Yet 2008 was fought in his terms. That staying power is a warning as much as a compliment. The failure keeps coming back because the promise underneath has not changed.'},{kind:'timeline',points:[{when:'1694',what:'The Bank of England is founded.'},{when:'1866',what:'Overend, Gurney and Company fails, and panic follows in London.'},{when:'1873',what:'Bagehot publishes *Lombard Street*.'},{when:'2008',what:'A global banking panic. Central bankers reach for Bagehot\u2019s rule.'}],note:'The institutions changed beyond recognition. The promise underneath did not.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the rule survived',text:'Bagehot was not applying a doctrine. He asked what would stop this failure in this arrangement, working from the mechanics. A rule drawn from how the promise breaks still fits wherever the same promise is made.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A step hollowed by generations of feet. The new boot fits the old hollow exactly. That says more about the step than the boot.'},{kind:'try',text:'Find the oldest piece of advice that still describes a problem you have. Ask what has stayed the same that keeps making it fit.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A team finds a ten-year-old incident report that describes this month\u2019s outage exactly. How should they read it?',options:['As a warning that the cause was never removed','As proof that the old team understood the system well','As chance: outages often look alike by coincidence','As a reason to reapply the old fix unchanged'],answer:0,why:'An exact fit after a long gap means the underlying arrangement has not changed. That is what to look at, rather than admiring the old report.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'A BANKER WHO EDITED A PAPER',text:'Bagehot was a banker\u2019s son who worked in his family\u2019s bank before editing The Economist, from 1861 until his death in 1877. *Lombard Street* reads like reporting: how bills were discounted, who held the reserves, who lent to whom overnight, and where the chain would snap.'},{kind:'idea',text:'*Lombard Street* lasted because its rule was derived from the mechanics of a promise, not from a doctrine. The promise has not changed, so neither has the failure. That is the uncomfortable reason it is still quoted.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Derived, not applied',text:'Bagehot asks what would stop this failure in this arrangement: a single reserve, a market lending overnight, depositors entitled to cash on demand. His answer was general enough to survive the arrangement changing, because it was tied to the promise rather than to the institutions of 1873.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Many feet over many years made the hollow. The modern boot sits in it exactly. The fit tells you about the step.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Read as a compliment',items:['Bagehot saw further than others','The rule is timeless','We know what to do']},right:{title:'Read as a warning',items:['The promise is unchanged','The failure keeps recurring','We keep needing to do it']},note:'Both readings are true. Only the second tells you where to look.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Durability is not proof of correctness. A vocabulary can survive because it suits the people using it, and invoking Bagehot can lend a rescue authority it has not earned: critics argued that much lending in 2008 ignored his terms on rate and collateral. Old writing that fits is a prompt to check, not a verdict.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which old warning in your own field do people still quote? What has stayed the same that keeps it true?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A safety report from the 1950s describes a modern factory accident almost exactly. What is the right first inference?',options:['The conditions that caused it have persisted, and need finding','The authors of the old report were unusually far-sighted','Accidents are random, so the close match is a coincidence','The factory should adopt the old report\u2019s rules unchanged'],answer:0,why:'When old writing fits exactly, the useful question is what has not changed. Admiring the authors or copying their rules skips it.'}]}},'lombard-street_6':{pictureCaption:'Every door in the vault opens onto one box. Nobody planned the room this way. It is the room there is.',bridge:'Idea 5 said the promise has not changed. This one looks at the odd arrangement Bagehot found built around it.',basics:{title:'Nobody planned it, but it is ours now',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE ONE SPARE KEY',text:'In your house, one spare key hangs by the door. Your mum uses it. So does your brother, and the neighbour who feeds the cat. Nobody decided this. It just grew, bit by bit. Now if that key is lost, all of you are locked out.'},{kind:'idea',text:'In 1873 Walter Bagehot wrote about England\u2019s banks. All the spare cash for emergencies sat in one bank, the Bank of England. Nobody chose this. It just grew. His advice: stop asking if it is good, and learn to run it well.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Lots of doors, and each one opens onto the same box. That box is the only spare cash for all of them.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why one place is risky',text:'If all the spare cash is in one place, that place has a big job. When other banks are in trouble, they all turn to it. If it says no, there is nowhere else to go.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The problem Bagehot saw',text:'The people who ran the Bank of England said this job was not theirs. But the whole country leaned on them anyway. Bagehot said a job that nobody owns up to is a job that nobody gets ready for.'},{kind:'try',text:'Look for one thing at home or school that everyone needs but nobody looks after. Say out loud whose job it should be.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your class always borrows one girl\u2019s calculator. Nobody planned that. What would Bagehot say?',options:['It is how things are, so agree how to look after it','Ban the calculator until there is a better plan','Say nothing, and hope that it keeps working'],answer:0,why:'He would not stop to ask if the plan was good. He would ask how to run it well, since it is what you have.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'If a system is badly designed, the first job is to replace it.',reality:'Bagehot granted that nobody would choose England\u2019s banking system. He also saw that it could not simply be swapped out. The useful question was how to run the system that existed.'},{kind:'idea',text:'England kept its entire last-resort cash reserve in one institution, and nobody ever decided that. It accumulated. Bagehot\u2019s point: stop judging the arrangement and start running it, which first means admitting what it is.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A dozen doors, one box. Every bank depended on a single reserve that nobody had set out to create.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The unadmitted duty',text:'The Bank of England\u2019s directors long denied that they held the reserve for everyone. Yet the whole system depended on them acting as if they did. A duty nobody admits to having is a duty nobody prepares for.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Is this arrangement good?',items:['A question for designers','Answer: probably not','Leads to argument']},right:{title:'Given it, what now?',items:['A question for operators','Answer: run it well','Leads to preparation']},note:'Bagehot kept the two questions apart, and spent his book on the second.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'AT WORK',text:'Over the years, one engineer has become the only person who understands the billing system. Nobody planned it. Debating whether that was wise changes nothing this quarter. Writing down that she holds the role, and planning around it, does.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A town\u2019s only large hospital grew up, by accident, on the wrong side of the river. What is the Bagehot-style response?',options:['Accept it is the hospital, and plan bridges and capacity around it','Refuse to invest in it until it can be rebuilt somewhere better','Hold an inquiry into who chose to put it on that side of the river','Treat the location as a deliberate choice that must have had reasons'],answer:0,why:'Whether it is in the right place and what to do given where it is are separate questions. Only planning around it helps a patient this year.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'HOW IT ACCUMULATED',text:'The Bank of England was founded in 1694 as a private company lending to the government. Its privileges drew other banks to keep their spare balances with it. By Bagehot\u2019s day it held the ultimate cash reserve of the whole country, though nobody had ever set out to give it that role.'},{kind:'term',word:'One-reserve system',meaning:'Bagehot\u2019s description of the English arrangement: other banks kept their reserves at the Bank of England, so one institution held the cash the whole country would fall back on in a panic.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Nobody designed the one-reserve system, and nobody starting fresh would choose it. But it is the system there is. The question is how to run it, and that begins with saying out loud what it already is.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One box, a dozen doors. From above the arrangement is obvious. From inside any one room it was easy not to see it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The complaint is about denial',text:'Bagehot\u2019s target is not concentration as such. It is the directors disclaiming the role while the system depended on them performing it. His book is partly an attempt to force a description onto a reality: you cannot discharge an obligation well while denying you hold it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why not redesign it?',text:'He granted that many banks each keeping their own reserve would be the more natural system. He judged it out of reach. The confidence built up around one reserve could not be rebuilt to order, a point idea 7 takes up.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Accepting an arrangement can harden into defending it. \u201cIt is what we have\u201d is sometimes the start of good operation and sometimes an excuse never to reform anything. Bagehot separated the two questions; he did not abolish the first. An arrangement worth running may still be worth slowly changing.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these misuses Bagehot\u2019s distinction?',options:['Refusing to discuss any reform because this is the system there is','Writing down who actually holds a duty that nobody formally assigned','Planning carefully around a single supplier you would not have chosen','Admitting a role you have quietly performed for years without a title'],answer:0,why:'Separating the questions is not the same as closing one. Running the arrangement well is compatible with working, slowly, to change it.'}]}},'lombard-street_7':{pictureCaption:'A tall stack built one tile at a time, and one hand drawing out the bottom tile. Slow to build, quick to fall.',bridge:'Idea 6 asked why nobody simply rebuilt the system. This is Bagehot\u2019s answer: the trust inside it cannot be built to order.',basics:{title:'Trust is slow to build, quick to lose',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'SAM\u2019S FOOTBALL',text:'Sam has lent you his football all year. You always gave it back. Then he finds out you lost his pencil case and blamed someone else. Now he says no to the ball. A year of trust, gone in one afternoon.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Trust grows slowly, from a long time of being reliable. Nobody can just make it. And it can be lost far faster than it was built.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'This stack was built one tile at a time. Pull one tile from the bottom, and it all comes down at once.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'TWO SPEEDS',low:'Building trust: months and years',high:'Losing it: one afternoon',note:'The two speeds are not the same. And losing it fast does not make winning it back any quicker.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What this has to do with banks',text:'Walter Bagehot wrote about London\u2019s banks in 1873. He said banks run on trust. People trust a bank because it has paid them back for years. No king and no law can just order people to trust a bank.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of someone who trusts you with something. What would it take to lose that trust? How long would it take to win it back?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A shop has been honest for twenty years. One week it is caught cheating. What is most likely to happen?',options:['People stop trusting it fast, and it takes years to win back','People forget all about it after a few days','Twenty good years mean one bad week won\u2019t count'],answer:0,why:'Twenty years built the trust. One week can knock it down. Building it again is slow.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'TWELVE YEARS, ONE WEEKEND',text:'A restaurant has served the same town for twelve years. One evening a photo of its kitchen goes round online. The bookings empty in a weekend. A new chef and a clean inspection will not fill them again in a weekend.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Credit is a set of opinions about other people, built slowly by long good conduct. No authority can create it. Because an opinion can be revised much faster than it was formed, credit is slow to gain and quick to lose.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Building credit',items:['Years of reliable conduct','Each proof adds a little','Cannot be rushed']},right:{title:'Losing credit',items:['One bad week','One doubt recasts everything','Rebuilding is no faster']},note:'Destroying it quickly does nothing to speed up the reconstruction.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the speeds differ',text:'Trust is a forecast: this person will pay, this bank will keep its promise. A forecast built on many good years can be overturned by one piece of evidence that the years were misleading. Building needs proof after proof. Doubting needs only one reason.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Built tile by tile to head height. One hand at the bottom is enough. Credit falls far faster than it rises.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Bagehot\u2019s caution',text:'This is why Bagehot advised altering the English money market only carefully. Its value lay in accumulated confidence, which could not be reproduced on demand. Not conservatism as a creed: caution where the thing at risk cannot be rebuilt to order.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A firm could switch to a cheaper supplier that sometimes ships late to its long-standing customers. What does this idea say to weigh?',options:['Years of reliability could be lost to a handful of late orders','Mainly the savings, since loyal customers forgive and forget','Nothing extra: reputation recovers as fast as it falls','Only whether the new supplier is cheaper than the old one'],answer:0,why:'The saving is small and steady. The possible loss is large and slow to repair. The asymmetry is what makes it a bad trade.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'When confidence collapses, a strong enough authority can restore it by decree.',reality:'Bagehot denies it. Credit is an opinion formed by long experience of reliability. A government can guarantee or command, but it cannot make people hold an opinion they have not formed.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Credit is a growth, not a manufacture: opinions about others, built slowly by experience and not producible by decree. The asymmetry follows. Accumulation takes decades; collapse can take an afternoon.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where it sits in the argument',text:'Bagehot uses this to explain why the one-reserve system of idea 6 must be run rather than replaced. The confidence the English market placed in its arrangements had been built over generations. A better design on paper would start with none of it.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE ASYMMETRY',nodes:['Years of reliability','An opinion: they will pay','One sign they may not','The opinion revised overnight'],note:'The fall is not the rise run backwards. Nothing in the rebuilding goes faster because the collapse was quick.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A stack raised tile by tile, undone from the bottom by one hand. Credit in one image: slow up, sudden down.'},{kind:'explain',title:'It transfers',text:'The same holds for any asset that exists only in other people\u2019s expectations: a reputation, a currency, a brand. Each is built at one rate and lost at another. Treating the two rates as symmetrical is how such assets get spent.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The asymmetry is a tendency, not a law. Confidence sometimes returns quickly when the cause of a shock is visible and fixed, and long-trusted names sometimes survive scandal. The case for caution can also be used to block every reform of a trusted institution. Bagehot\u2019s claim is narrower: price the downside, because the rebuild cannot be ordered.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'PRICE THE DOWNSIDE',prompt:'A bank can save money by cutting a service customers have relied on for decades, with a small chance of a public failure. How should the idea shape the decision?',options:['Weigh the rare failure heavily, since lost confidence cannot be rebuilt on demand','Treat the chance of failure at face value, like any other cost','Ignore it, since decades of trust will easily absorb one bad event','Make the cut, then rebuild confidence quickly if anything goes wrong'],answer:0,why:'Gain and loss run at different speeds. A small chance of losing something that cannot be rebuilt to order deserves more weight than its odds suggest.'}]}},'lombard-street_8':{pictureCaption:'Every chair is empty, and the one nameplate lies face down. The role exists. Nobody\u2019s name is on it.',bridge:'Idea 6 found a duty nobody admitted to. This one asks who was actually performing it.',basics:{title:'Who is in charge of the big job?',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE CLASS HAMSTER',prompt:'Your class has a hamster. Each week a new kid feeds it. Nobody is in charge of the hamster overall. What is most likely to go wrong?',options:['Nothing, it will be fine','Something slips and no one notices','It gets far too much food'],answer:1,reveal:'Each kid does their week. But who checks if the cage needs a clean, or if the hamster looks ill? Nobody. That job has no name on it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When a really big job has no one named in charge, it tends to go wrong just when things get hard. So give the job a name before the trouble comes.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'THE BANK OF ENGLAND',text:'Walter Bagehot wrote about London\u2019s banks in 1873. Back then, the Bank of England held the spare cash for the whole country. It was run by merchants, people who bought and sold goods. They took turns. The head changed every two years. None was picked for knowing banks.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'All the chairs are empty. The name card lies face down. The job is real, but no name is on it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What Bagehot asked for',text:'Bagehot did not say they were silly, or that they should go. He asked for two things. First, say out loud that the Bank has this big job. Second, add one expert who stays for years, so someone always knows how things work.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A new parent washes the team kit each week. Kit keeps going missing. What would Bagehot suggest?',options:['Name one person who stays in charge of the kit','Stop washing the team kit at all','Swap parents every day, not every week'],answer:0,why:'Taking turns is fine for the washing. But someone named has to own the whole job, and stay long enough to notice what goes wrong.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'ask',kicker:'BEFORE YOU READ ON',prompt:'Who owns the one thing your organisation simply cannot get wrong? Notice how long the answer takes.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The Bank of England held the nation\u2019s reserve. But its board of merchants took turns and was not chosen for banking skill. Bagehot did not demand their removal. He demanded the duty be named: an unnamed duty done by amateurs fails under pressure.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'THE COURT OF THE BANK',text:'The Bank was run by directors drawn from trade. They took turns. The Governor changed about every two years. They were able and meant well. But the set-up made sure they would be gone before the results of their choices arrived.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A full set of chairs, all empty, and a nameplate lying face down. A key role, with nobody\u2019s name standing on it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it does not fix itself',text:'The set-up was harmless when the Bank was one bank among many. It became dangerous once the whole system rested on it. But nobody notices the day that line is crossed. So nobody changes how it is run.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Bagehot\u2019s remedy, applied',steps:['Say the duty out loud.','Name one person who owns it.','Give them continuity: a permanent expert beneath the rotating heads.'],note:'Unglamorous, and the point is that it is in place before the pressure arrives.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A start-up\u2019s payment system now carries every customer. Each week it is looked after by whoever is free. What does Bagehot\u2019s complaint suggest?',options:['Name an owner with continuity before the next outage','Hire a better-known chief executive to oversee it','Keep rotating, since everyone should share the load','Wait for an outage to show who ought to own it'],answer:0,why:'The system has quietly become critical while its ownership stayed casual. Naming an owner before the pressure arrives is the whole remedy.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'THE GAP HE POINTED AT',left:{title:'What the Bank was',items:['Holder of the nation\u2019s reserve','Last resort in every panic','Central to the whole system']},right:{title:'How it was run',items:['Rotating merchant directors','A Governor every two years','No banking expertise required']},note:'Bagehot\u2019s complaint lives in the gap between the two columns.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Governance that was harmless when an institution was small becomes dangerous once the system depends on it, and it does not update itself. Where something critical has no named owner, the failure is already scheduled.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'THE COURT, 1870s',text:'The directors were merchants, chosen from trade rather than banking, who rose to Governor in turn and served about two years. Bagehot did not call them foolish. He said the arrangement ensured the country\u2019s gravest financial decisions were taken by amateurs who would be gone before the consequences came.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The unglamorous reform',text:'His proposal was a permanent deputy governor: an expert with continuity beneath the rotating figurehead. Not a revolution in the Bank\u2019s constitution. One named, lasting owner for the duty the Bank still declined to admit it had.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'An empty boardroom and a nameplate face down. The duty is real; the arrangement leaves no one\u2019s name on it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the threshold goes unnoticed',text:'No single day marks the moment an institution becomes systemically important. Each year looks like the last. The rules written for a smaller body carry on, and the gap between duty and governance widens without anyone deciding it should.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Naming an owner is necessary, not sufficient. A permanent expert can become a single point of failure, or bend the role to private ends. Rotation has virtues Bagehot gave little weight to: fresh eyes, less entrenchment. And naming a duty achieves nothing if the person named lacks the authority to act.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'For twenty years a hospital\u2019s backup generator has been tested by whichever manager is on shift. Which change follows Bagehot\u2019s reform most closely?',options:['One named engineer owns the generator and its testing, for years','A new rota, so that every manager tests it equally often','A committee that meets once a year to review generator policy','A sign beside the generator reminding staff to test it'],answer:0,why:'His remedy is a named owner with continuity. Fairer rotation, an annual committee or a reminder all leave the duty with nobody in particular.'}]}},'lombard-street_9':{pictureCaption:'The floor has emptied and paper is everywhere. One desk is lit, its ledger open: someone whose job it is to stay.',bridge:'Ideas 4, 6 and 8 each asked for a duty to be named in advance. This is the principle that joins them.',basics:{title:'A panic needs someone in charge',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE FIRE BELL',text:'The fire bell rings. Thirty kids rush for one door. Each one is doing a sensible thing: get out fast. But all at once, they jam the door. That is why a teacher stands there and says who goes first.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A crowd of sensible people can still get stuck. In a panic, someone has to be in charge. And everyone has to know, ahead of time, what that person will do.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Bagehot and the money market',text:'In 1873, many people thought money markets would sort themselves out. Walter Bagehot ran a paper about money, and he was not against markets. But he said that in a panic, a money market acts like a crowd at a jammed door.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Everyone has left, and paper is all over the floor. One desk still has its lamp on. Someone is in charge here.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'On a calm day',items:['People make their own choices','Things sort themselves out','No one needs to lead']},right:{title:'In a panic',items:['Everyone rushes the same way','Things jam','Someone must lead, with a plan']}},{kind:'question',prompt:'After a school trip, lots of parents arrive at once and the car park jams. What would Bagehot say?',options:['Pick one person to direct cars, and tell parents the plan first','Let the parents sort it out, as they are all sensible','Ask the parents to stay calm and drive more slowly'],answer:0,why:'Each parent is sensible, but together they jam. A named person with a plan told in advance is what clears it.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Bagehot\u2019s warning is an argument against free markets.',reality:'He edited The Economist and did not distrust markets in general. His point is narrower. This market had a feature, a single reserve, that no one in it had the job of keeping up.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Money does not manage itself, however sensible each person handling it may be. In a crisis the market is a crowd. A crowd needs a named party who has said in advance what they will do.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Sensible parts, unmanaged whole',text:'A system can be made only of sensible people and still need to be managed on purpose. That is because some traits belong to the set-up, not to anyone in it. Each bank guarding its own cash is prudent. All banks doing it at once is a panic.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A trading floor gone dark and littered. One desk lit, ledger open. It belongs to somebody whose job it is to be there when everyone else has gone.'},{kind:'equation',parts:['A single reserve','Denied by its holder','Run by amateurs'],result:'Recurring panics',note:'Bagehot\u2019s case in one line, drawn from ideas 6 and 8. Left alone, this set-up would not fix itself. And it had not.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one situation at work or at home that could turn into a panic. Write down what will be done and who decides. Share it before anyone needs it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A block of flats is full of careful residents, but nobody owns the fire plan. What does Bagehot\u2019s principle predict?',options:['Sensible residents may still jam the exits without a named lead','Careful residents will coordinate among themselves well enough','A plan matters only if the residents happen to be careless','The risk is low, since nobody in the building wants a fire'],answer:0,why:'The danger belongs to the set-up, not to any one resident. Being careful one by one does not stop a crowd from jamming together.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'WHAT WAS HE ARGUING AGAINST?',prompt:'Bagehot argued that money will not manage itself. What was his target?',options:['Markets in general','The belief that this market would right itself in a panic','The greed of bankers'],answer:1,reveal:'The editor of The Economist was no enemy of markets. His target was the prevailing view that the money market, left alone, would reach a sound outcome in a crisis. Its history said otherwise.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Some properties belong to an arrangement rather than to anyone inside it. A system of rational actors can still need deliberate management, and in a panic that means a named party committed in advance.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The case he assembles',text:'Put the book\u2019s observations together: a single reserve, held by an institution denying it holds one, governed by rotating amateurs. Such a system will not correct itself. It will produce recurrent panics, as England\u2019s had in 1825, 1847, 1857 and 1866.'},{kind:'term',word:'Emergent property',meaning:'A modern term for a feature of a whole system that no single part has or controls. A panic is one: no bank chooses it, and every bank contributes to it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The crowd has gone and left its paper behind. One lamp, one open ledger: the party that answers for the whole floor.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the writing matters',text:'A named party that has said in advance what it will do gives the crowd something to act on other than fear. Bagehot\u2019s rule, announced beforehand, is that party\u2019s script. Much of the management he asks for is a commitment made public before it is needed.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The principle can justify too much. Not every market failure needs a manager, and a named party can be wrong, slow or captured. Management also changes behaviour: a market that expects to be managed may take risks it otherwise would not, the moral hazard of idea 4. A written plan works only if someone can and will carry it out.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HONEST OBJECTION',prompt:'Which objection does the principle have to take seriously?',options:['A party expected to manage panics may encourage the risks that cause them','Markets made up of rational actors can never produce a panic at all','Bagehot opposed markets, so his view is simply a matter of ideology','Writing plans down in advance has no effect on anyone\u2019s behaviour'],answer:0,why:'The other three are false. Encouraged risk is the real cost of a manager: the promise of management changes what people inside the system do.'}]}}}},2981,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={meditations_1:{pictureCaption:'The street is calm. The grip on the wheel is not. All the heat in this picture is in the driver.',basics:{title:'What happened, and the story you add',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'IN THE CAR',picture:!0,text:'A car cuts in front of you. Nothing was hit. No one was hurt. But forty minutes later you are still mad about it.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'What is keeping you mad?',options:['The other car','The story in your head about it'],answer:1,reveal:'The car left long ago. What stayed is the story: *that driver did it on purpose.* You wrote that part yourself.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Every upset has two parts. Part one is what happened. Part two is the story you tell about it. Most of the hurt is in part two.'},{kind:'equation',parts:['What happened','Your story about it'],result:'How bad it feels',note:'You can\u2019t change the first part. You can check the second.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'TRY IT ON A TEXT',text:'Your friend texts back one word: *ok.* What happened? One word came. The story? *She is mad at me.* Maybe she is. Maybe she was just busy. You do not know yet.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO TAUGHT THIS',text:'Marcus Aurelius ruled Rome almost 2,000 years ago. He kept a private notebook. In it he told himself this lesson again and again, because it is easy to forget when you are upset.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you are mad, say what happened in one plain line. No guessing what anyone meant. Then see how much of the feeling is left.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your teacher hands back your work and says nothing. Which part is *what happened*?',options:['She handed it back without a word','She thinks my work is bad','She does not like me'],answer:0,why:'Only the handing back is a fact. The other two are stories you would be adding.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'TWO LINES',text:'You spend an hour on an email. The reply is two lines long. Before you have finished reading it you have decided what it means: *they think my work is beneath them.* The afternoon is gone.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Almost everything that upsets you arrives in two parts: what happened, and the verdict you attached to it. The verdict is the part that hurts, and the only part you can change.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'The event',items:['A two-line reply','A car pulls out','The meeting is moved']},right:{title:'The verdict',items:['\u201cMy work is beneath them\u201d','\u201cHe did that on purpose\u201d','\u201cI don\u2019t matter here\u201d']},note:'Nothing on the left contains contempt. Contempt is something you inferred, in about half a second.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the gap matters',text:'You cannot un-send the email or un-move the meeting. You can check an inference. Marcus returns to this throughout his notebooks, because the gap between event and verdict is the only place you have any leverage.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Stoics stop themselves from feeling things.',reality:'Marcus never asks for that. The practice is to slow the inference down: say what happened in words a stranger would accept, then notice how much of the feeling was riding on the words you added.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Look at the street: calm, ordinary. Now look at the hands. Everything hot in this picture was added by the driver.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you are annoyed, write the bare fact in one sentence a stranger would accept, with no adjectives. Then ask what the adjectives were doing.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A colleague walks past your desk without saying hello. Which response applies the idea?',options:['Note the fact, they walked past, and treat \u201cthey are ignoring me\u201d as a guess to check','Decide that what colleagues think of you simply does not matter','Assume the best: they certainly did not see you sitting there','Stop them at once and ask why they are upset with you'],answer:0,why:'The move is not to swap a bad verdict for a kind one. It is to see that any verdict is an inference, and an inference can be checked.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'THE DANUBE FRONTIER, 170s',text:'Marcus wrote these notes in Greek, at least partly in camp, while commanding Rome\u2019s armies on the northern frontier. Nobody else was meant to read them. One instruction recurs more than almost any other, which tells you how often he needed it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'If something outside you seems to hurt, the hurt is in your judgement about it, and a judgement is yours to revise. Take the judgement away and what is left is only an event.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE STOIC SEQUENCE',nodes:['An event','An impression: \u201cthis is bad\u201d','Assent: you agree with it','Distress'],note:'Stoic psychology puts a step between the impression and the feeling: your assent. That step is where Marcus aims the whole practice.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the practice is',text:'He does not argue with the feeling. He describes the event again in bare terms until the judgement has nothing to hold on to: a reply of two lines; a man who pulled out. The test is a description a stranger would accept.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it sounds modern',text:'Cognitive therapy rests on the same structure: situations do not produce emotions directly. Interpretations do, and interpretations can be examined. Its founders named the Stoics as forerunners. Marcus was there about eighteen centuries earlier.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Some verdicts are correct. The reply really was contemptuous; the injustice is real. The practice is to check the inference, not to assume you were wrong to feel it. Taken as a rule that every hurt is your own doing, it becomes self-blame, and an excuse for other people\u2019s conduct.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What was the last thing that stung you? Write the bare event in one line. Which words did you have to leave out?'},{kind:'recap',points:['An upset arrives as an event plus a verdict.','The verdict is an inference, and inferences can be checked.','Checking is not suppressing. Some verdicts survive the check.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'CORRECT THE MISREADING',prompt:'A friend says: \u201cSo Stoicism means deciding nothing is ever really bad.\u201d What is the best correction?',options:['It means treating \u201cthis is bad\u201d as a claim to examine, which may turn out to be true','It means bad events stop mattering once you have become calm about them','It means feelings are errors of thought and should be argued away','It means only physical harm should count as a real harm'],answer:0,why:'Marcus asks for the judgement to be inspected, not for a fixed answer. A verdict that survives inspection is still yours to act on.'}]}},meditations_2:{pictureCaption:'The walker is not climbing the fallen tree. They are reading it: the torn roots show which way the storm came.',bridge:'Idea 1 was about what you tell yourself when something goes wrong. This one is about what you do next.',basics:{title:'A blocked path is telling you something',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'YOU ARE WALKING TO A FRIEND\u2019S',prompt:'A fallen tree blocks the path. What is the smart first move?',options:['Push the tree until it moves','Wait for someone to clear it','Look at it and learn something'],answer:2,reveal:'Pushing wears you out. Waiting wastes the day. Looking tells you things: how big it is, which way round is easy, what the storm did up ahead.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'This walker has stopped to *read* the fallen tree. The torn roots show which way the wind blew.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A thing that blocks you is also telling you something. Don\u2019t just push on it or wait. Read it, then keep going a new way.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE WATER IN A STREAM',text:'Water meets a rock. It does not stop. It does not fight the rock. It finds the way around, and it keeps moving.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'AT SCHOOL',text:'You need a library book for your project, but it is checked out for two weeks. You could sit and wait. Or you could start the part that needs no book: the drawing, the plan, the questions. The block showed you what to do first.'},{kind:'try',text:'Stuck on something today? Ask one question: what can I do *now* that does not need the stuck part?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your team can\u2019t practise because the field is flooded. Which answer uses this idea?',options:['Use the gym to work on passing','Cancel practice until the field dries','Complain to the coach about the rain'],answer:0,why:'The flood changed the shape of practice. It did not have to stop it.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'STUCK BEHIND ONE PERSON',text:'The one person who can approve the plan is away for two weeks. Everything is now queued behind them. You spend the first three days chasing their deputy and refreshing your inbox.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'\u201cThe obstacle is the way\u201d means every setback is secretly good for you.',reality:'Marcus is not consoling you. He is giving an instruction: an obstacle changes the shape of the work, and the work continues in the new shape, now.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A blocked path tells you something about the terrain. Treat interference not as an interruption of the work but as the material the work is now made of.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Read as an interruption',items:['Work stops until it moves','You push on the block','Two weeks of chasing']},right:{title:'Read as terrain',items:['Work continues, differently','You ask what it makes possible','Two weeks on the part that needed no approval']},note:'Same fortnight. Only the reading changed.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'WHO IS SAYING THIS',text:'Marcus wrote this while running a war and an empire through a plague. He never had the option of waiting for a clear road. The notebooks keep coming back to it because the temptation to wait kept coming back.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The test',text:'Ask what you are doing while blocked. If the only answer is \u201cchasing the thing that is blocking me\u201d, the obstacle has taken the work hostage. If the answer includes something the block made possible, you are reading it as terrain.'},{kind:'try',text:'Ask of your current obstacle: what does this let me practise, fix or see that a clear road would not?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Your launch slips a month because a supplier is late. Which response treats the delay as terrain?',options:['Use the month to fix the onboarding you had planned to ship rough','Email the supplier every morning until they deliver','Tell the team that things happen for a reason','Pause the project so nobody wastes any effort'],answer:0,why:'The delay made something possible that the original schedule did not. That is the work continuing in a new shape.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE ORIGINAL ARGUMENT',text:'In the fifth notebook Marcus makes a compact argument. Other people can obstruct what he does, but not his intention or his attitude, because the mind adapts and turns any obstacle to its own purposes. What blocks the action becomes the action.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The obstacle is not a pause in the work. It is the work\u2019s new material. What changes is not the aim, only the form the next action takes.'},{kind:'term',word:'The reserve clause',meaning:'A Stoic habit of adding \u201cunless something prevents it\u201d to every plan. You commit fully to the action and hold the outcome loosely, so a blocked plan is not a defeat. It was always allowed for.'},{kind:'example',text:'The approver is away for two weeks. With the reserve clause already in place there is nothing to mourn. You spend the fortnight on the part of the plan that never needed approval, and discover it was the weak part.'},{kind:'keyline',text:'Only the route was blocked. The aim of acting well never is.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Some obstacles are just obstacles, and the right response is to remove them. Read carelessly, the idea becomes a way of putting up with what should be fought, or of dressing a bad plan as fate. Marcus was a commander. He removed obstacles too.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What is blocking you this week? Name one thing the block makes possible that a clear road would not.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these is a *misuse* of the idea?',options:['Accepting a fixable injustice at work because \u201cthe obstacle is the way\u201d','Working on the unblocked half of a project while approval is pending','Using a cancelled meeting to write the memo properly instead','Changing the route to a goal after a supplier fails to deliver'],answer:0,why:'The idea redirects effort. It does not ask you to tolerate what you could and should remove.'}]}},meditations_3:{pictureCaption:'A city of lit windows, each one somebody else\u2019s evening. Almost none of them are thinking about you.',bridge:'The first two ideas dealt with a bad moment. This one is a tool for a frightening one.',basics:{title:'Zoom out, and the scary thing shrinks',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'BEFORE YOU SPEAK',text:'Your friends want you to do a dare. You do not want to. Your heart is thumping. What will they think of you?'},{kind:'explain',title:'A strange trick',text:'A Roman emperor named Marcus Aurelius had a trick for moments like this. He reminded himself that nothing lasts. Not him. Not the people he was scared of. Not what they thought of him.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Most fear is about what a few people will think. Those people will soon forget. So do the right thing now, and do not let the fear decide.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A VIEW FROM A PLANE',text:'From a plane, your whole school is a tiny dot. The kids who might laugh are too small to see. The problem did not vanish. You just saw its real size.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Each lit window is someone busy with their own night. Almost none of them are thinking about you.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Thinking about how nothing lasts is just sad.',reality:'Marcus did not use it to feel sad. He used it to feel brave. If it will all be forgotten soon, there is less to be scared of today.'},{kind:'try',text:'Scared of what people will think? Ask: will anyone remember this in a month? Then do the thing you think is right.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You are afraid to ask a question in class. What would Marcus\u2019s trick tell you?',options:['In a week, no one will remember that you asked','Stay quiet so that no one can laugh at you','Nothing matters, so do not bother asking'],answer:0,why:'The trick shrinks the fear so you can act. It is not a reason to give up.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'AN EMPEROR, IN PRIVATE',text:'The most powerful man in the Roman world sat down and wrote himself a list of the dead: emperors, generals, philosophers. All famous. All feared or admired. All gone, along with everyone who feared or admired them.'},{kind:'idea',text:'You are temporary, and so is everyone whose opinion you are worrying about. The point is not gloom. It is scale. Most anxiety is about status in a small group. Seeing how brief that group is makes the difficult thing sayable.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Remembering that you will die is morbid, and makes everything feel pointless.',reality:'Marcus uses it as altitude. Seen from high enough, the audience you fear is small, temporary and mostly not paying attention.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A city of lit windows, each one somebody else\u2019s evening. Stand at this height and ask how many of them are thinking about your meeting.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What makes a hard conversation hard',text:'Rarely the words. Usually the audience: the colleagues who will hear about it, the reputation it might dent. Shrink the audience to its real size and the thing you are avoiding is often smaller than the avoiding.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'TWO DISTANCES',low:'Zoomed in: this meeting, these five people',high:'Zoomed out: in a month, almost nobody remembers',note:'Courage lives further to the right than you usually stand.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before a conversation you are dreading, ask: who exactly is the audience I am afraid of? How much will any of them remember in a month?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You have to give a senior colleague honest, unwelcome feedback. How does this idea help?',options:['It shrinks the imagined audience, so saying the true thing feels possible','It shows that honesty is pointless, since everything is forgotten','It tells you to wait until the feeling of dread has passed','It proves that their opinion of you has no effect on anything'],answer:0,why:'The idea is a tool for courage. It does not say opinions have no effect, only that the fear is far larger than the stakes.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'THE LISTS OF THE DEAD',text:'The notebooks are full of names. Alexander, Pompey, Caesar, whole courts with their physicians and astrologers. Marcus writes them down and notes the same thing each time: gone, and so is everyone who admired or feared them.'},{kind:'term',word:'Memento mori',meaning:'Latin for \u201cremember that you will die.\u201d A later name for an old exercise: keeping your own death in mind on purpose, in order to decide better how to act now.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Hold your own death, and your audience\u2019s, steadily in view. Not to shrink what you do, but to set its scale: fame and embarrassment are brief, so only the quality of the act is left to care about.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The mechanism is altitude',text:'Most of what he fears is reputational: how an act will be seen and remembered. So he attacks the remembering. Those who remember will die too, and those after them. Lasting fame is a chain of people who are themselves vanishing.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Misread as',items:['Nothing matters','Why bother?','Withdraw']},right:{title:'Used as',items:['Little is worth fearing','Do it now, and well','Say the hard thing']},note:'The same fact, pointed in opposite directions.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The thought turns easily into an excuse. \u201cNobody will remember\u201d can license carelessness as readily as courage, and for someone already low it can deepen the sense that nothing counts. Marcus pairs it, every time, with a duty to act well now. Without that half it is only gloom.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Name the audience you are performing for at the moment. How many of them are actually watching?'},{kind:'recap',points:['Most fear is fear of a small, brief audience.','The audience is temporary, and so are you.','Use the thought to act well now, never to excuse not acting.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which use of the idea would Marcus reject?',options:['Skipping a promise because \u201cnone of this will matter in a century\u201d','Raising an awkward truth because the embarrassment will not last','Caring less about a critic who will have forgotten by Friday','Doing careful work that nobody is ever likely to notice'],answer:0,why:'He uses mortality to remove fear, not duty. The obligation to act well now is the half that keeps it from being an excuse.'}]}},meditations_4:{pictureCaption:'One joint, finished properly, in the light. The rest of the frame waits in the dark, and it can.',basics:{title:'Do one thing. Then the next.',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'SUNDAY NIGHT',text:'You have a messy room, a test on Monday and a project due. You look at all of it at once. Your stomach drops. You do none of it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Don\u2019t look at the whole pile. Pick the next thing. Do it well. Then stop and pick again.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A STAIRCASE',text:'You can\u2019t jump to the top of the stairs. But you can always take one step. And once you are on it, the next step is easy to see.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Look where the light is: on one small piece. The big frame behind it is still dark. It can wait.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the pile feels so bad',text:'The pile is not happening now. Only one small job is happening now. Most of the scary feeling comes from picturing all the jobs at once.'},{kind:'steps',title:'When you feel swamped',steps:['Pick one small job.','Set a timer for 20 minutes.','Do only that job.','Stop. Then pick the next one.']},{kind:'question',prompt:'You have ten chores and you feel stuck. What does this idea say to do?',options:['Pick one chore and do just that one','Make a plan for all ten first','Wait until you feel ready'],answer:0,why:'One chore is doable right now. The other nine are not here yet.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'FOUR HOURS, TWO HUNDRED TASKS',prompt:'What is the most tempting way to spend the first hour?',options:['Start the most important task','Reread and reorder the list','Start the easiest task'],answer:1,reveal:'The list is where the dread is, and looking at it feels like working. An hour goes, and nothing on the list has changed.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Attend to the task in front of you, do it well, and do not let your mind run ahead into the enormous unfinished remainder.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Two instructions, not one',text:'The obvious half is *do the task.* The harder half is *stop*: keep the mind from running into the remainder while your hands are still on the current thing.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'The next 20 minutes',items:['Real','Nearly always manageable','Where your hands are']},right:{title:'The remainder',items:['Imagined','Never manageable in one go','Where the distress lives']},note:'The stack behind the task is not real yet.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'WHO NEEDED TO HEAR IT',text:'The man writing this was running a war, a plague and a bureaucracy. He was entitled to feel overwhelmed, and he had to write the instruction down again and again. It is a discipline, not a temperament.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One joint, finished properly, in the light. The rest of the frame waits in the dark, and it can.'},{kind:'try',text:'When the whole project feels unmanageable, define the next twenty minutes only. Write it down, do it, then define the next twenty.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Mid-task, you catch yourself mentally drafting the three tasks after this one. What does the instruction ask of you?',options:['Bring your attention back to the task your hands are on','Stop and re-plan the whole list properly','Switch to whichever task is worrying you most','Work faster so the remainder shrinks sooner'],answer:0,why:'The second half of the instruction is to stop the mind running ahead. Planning has its place, but not while the hands are busy.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'This is advice to work harder and never rest.',reality:'Half of it is an instruction to stop. The target is not idleness but imagination: the mind\u2019s habit of picturing everything that remains, all at once, and being crushed by the picture.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Do what is in front of you, and refuse to let the mind assemble the whole remaining load into one picture. Each task, taken alone, is bearable. The picture is what is not.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The argument in the notebooks',text:'Marcus tells himself not to be thrown by imagining his life as a whole. He is to ask of the present moment only: what in *this* is unbearable? Put that way, he says, he would be ashamed to answer.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE LOOP HE IS CUTTING',nodes:['One task in hand','Mind runs to the whole stack','Distress about the stack','The task in hand gets worse'],loop:!0,note:'The loop is fed by the second step, so that is where he cuts it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it is credible',text:'This is not a calm man describing his temperament. It is an emperor at war, during a plague, repeating an instruction because it did not hold the first time. Repetition in a private notebook is evidence of difficulty, not of mastery.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Somebody has to look at the whole list sometimes, or the next thing is chosen badly. The discipline governs where attention lives while the hands are busy. It does not replace planning. Used all day, it turns into drift: plenty done, none of it the right thing.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'WHERE ATTENTION SITS',low:'All of it on the stack',high:'All of it on the task',note:'Plan with the stack in view, briefly and on purpose. Work with it out of view.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Do the task in front of you, well.','Do not let the mind build the remainder into one picture.','Look at the whole list on purpose, at set times, not while working.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HONEST OBJECTION',prompt:'Which objection does the idea have to concede?',options:['Without planning now and then, \u201cthe next thing\u201d may be the wrong thing','People who feel overwhelmed are simply working too slowly','Imagining the future has no practical use at all','Marcus found the practice easy, so it may not transfer'],answer:0,why:'The instruction is about attention during work. Choosing what to work on still needs a view of the whole.'}]}},meditations_5:{pictureCaption:'A hand raised to knock in anger, stopped. Under the door, someone is having an ordinary evening, with no idea they are the villain.',bridge:'Idea 1 checked your story about an event. This one checks your story about a person.',basics:{title:'Nobody thinks they\u2019re the bad guy',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'ON THE BUS',prompt:'A boy pushes past you to get off the bus. He does not say sorry. Why did he do it?',options:['He is mean','He likes to push people','You can\u2019t tell yet'],answer:2,reveal:'Maybe he was about to miss his stop. Maybe he felt sick. In his own head he was not being mean. He was just trying to get off.'},{kind:'idea',text:'People who act badly nearly always think they have a good reason. In their own story, they are not the bad guy. They are just reacting to something.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'ONE MOMENT, TWO STORIES',left:{title:'Your story',items:['She took my seat','She did it to annoy me']},right:{title:'Her story',items:['The seat looked free','I was tired, so I sat down']},note:'Both stories feel true from the inside.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why this helps you',text:'When you think someone hurt you on purpose, you stay mad for a long time. When you can guess their reason, the mad feeling gets smaller. You get your day back.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'If I see why they did it, I\u2019m saying it was okay.',reality:'No. You can see why and still say it was wrong. You can still ask them to stop. You just do it without the big angry feeling.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A hand about to knock, hard. But look under the door. Someone in there is just having a normal night.'},{kind:'try',text:'When someone bugs you, finish this line for them: *I was only trying to\u2026* Then decide what to do.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your sister takes your charger without asking. What does this idea say to do first?',options:['Guess what she thought she was doing','Decide she did it just to be mean','Never say a word to her about it'],answer:0,why:'She had a reason that made sense to her. Knowing it helps you sort things out calmly. You can still ask her not to do it.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'AFTER THE MEETING',text:'A colleague cut across you in the meeting, twice. You have spent the evening composing replies. They went home thinking they had helped move things along.'},{kind:'idea',text:'People who behave badly almost always believe they are responding reasonably to something. Seeing that does not excuse them. It stops them occupying your attention.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'HIS MORNING ROUTINE',text:'Marcus began the day by telling himself whom he would meet: the meddling, the ungrateful, the arrogant, the dishonest. Not to dread them. To be unsurprised, and to remember that they act this way because they cannot tell good from bad.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Older than Marcus',text:'The idea comes from Socrates: nobody chooses what they believe to be bad. A driver who cuts in may be late and frightened of something. A colleague who takes the credit may believe, in the moment, that they earned it.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Understanding',items:['Cheap','Ends the anger','Costs you a minute']},right:{title:'Approving',items:['A separate decision','Still yours to make','May well be no']},note:'Marcus keeps the two apart. That is the whole trick.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A hand raised to knock in anger, stopped. Under the door, someone is having an ordinary evening, with no idea they are the villain.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before deciding somebody acted badly on purpose, finish this sentence for them: \u201cFrom where I stood, I was only\u2026\u201d Then decide what to do.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A neighbour\u2019s builders start drilling at 7 a.m. Which response applies the idea?',options:['Work out what they think they are doing, then ask calmly for a later start','Accept the noise, since they must have had a good reason for it','Assume it is deliberate and reply in kind tomorrow morning','Say nothing at all, because anger is always a mistake'],answer:0,why:'Understanding removes the anger, not the request. You still decide what to do, and you do it calmly.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Understanding why someone acted badly means excusing it.',reality:'Marcus keeps the two apart. Understanding is cheap and stops the anger. Approving is a separate decision, and it is still yours to make.'},{kind:'term',word:'No one errs willingly',meaning:'A claim traced to Socrates. People pursue what seems good to them, so wrongdoing is a kind of mistake about what is good, not a choice of what is bad.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Those who wrong you are acting on a mistaken view of what is good. Another person\u2019s mistake cannot harm you in what matters, and people are made to work together, like hands and feet, so anger at them makes little sense.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How he uses it',text:'The second notebook opens with a rehearsal. Today he will meet the meddling, the ungrateful and the arrogant. They are so, he says, from not knowing good from bad. He concludes that none of them can harm him, and that he cannot be angry with his own kind, made to work together like hands, feet and eyelids.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What psychology adds',text:'Modern psychology broadly agrees with the observation. People describe their own actions as responses to something, and rarely as wrongs they set out to do. Marcus is asking you to take that inside view of somebody else.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Some people do act in bad faith, and know it. And \u201cthey know no better\u201d can slide into contempt, or into putting up with conduct that should be stopped. The idea is a default that buys you calm while you find out which case you are in. It is not a verdict, and it does not replace consequences.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of the last person who annoyed you. Finish their sentence: \u201cFrom where I stood, I was only\u2026\u201d'},{kind:'recap',points:['People experience themselves as responding, not starting it.','Anger is expensive. Understanding is cheap.','Understanding and approving are different acts.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HARD CASE',prompt:'Someone lies to you repeatedly and clearly knows it. What does the idea still offer?',options:['Calm while you act: it removes the anger, not the consequences','Proof that they are not really lying, once you see their side','A reason to keep trusting them until they learn better','Nothing at all, since it only covers honest mistakes'],answer:0,why:'Even in the bad-faith case the practical gain is the same: you decide what to do without anger doing the deciding.'}]}},meditations_6:{pictureCaption:'The bowl is good. Every chair is empty and turned away. Both are true, and only one of them is the potter\u2019s business.',bridge:'Idea 3 shrank the audience you fear. This one takes away its power to grade you.',basics:{title:'Did you do it well? That part is yours',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'NO ONE SAID A WORD',text:'You spent all week on a drawing. You showed it. Nobody said a thing. Now it feels like the drawing must be bad.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'Is the drawing bad?',options:['Yes, because no one liked it','You can\u2019t tell from that'],answer:1,reveal:'Silence tells you about *them*. Maybe they were busy. Maybe they did not look. It tells you nothing about the drawing.'},{kind:'idea',text:'You are in charge of how hard you try and how well you do it. You are not in charge of whether people notice. So judge your work by the first part.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Up to you',items:['How hard you tried','How careful you were','Whether you finished']},right:{title:'Not up to you',items:['If they clap','If they notice','What mood they are in']}},{kind:'picture',caption:'The bowl is good. The chairs are empty and turned away. The potter made a good bowl anyway.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Should you ignore what people say?',text:'No. A teacher or a coach can show you things you can\u2019t see. Listen to that. Just don\u2019t let *silence* or *claps* decide if your work was good.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before you show your work, tell yourself what would make it good. *It is neat. It is my best. It is done.* Check that list, not the clapping.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You play your best game ever. Your team loses and no one cheers. What does this idea say?',options:['You played well, and that part was yours','You played badly, because the team lost','It only counts if someone cheers for you'],answer:0,why:'How you played was up to you. The score and the cheers were not.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'SILENCE',text:'You put a week into something, sent it, and heard nothing back. By Thursday you have quietly concluded that it was not very good.'},{kind:'idea',text:'You control the quality of your attempt. You do not control whether it is recognised, rewarded or even noticed. Tie the verdict to the response and you hand it to people who were not paying attention.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE STANDARD DRIFTS',nodes:['You send the work','The response is thin','\u201cSo it was not good\u201d','Next time you aim at applause'],note:'Nobody decided this. The standard quietly became whatever the response was.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where the verdict sits',text:'If the response decides whether the work was good, the judgement belongs to whoever happened to be reading, in whatever mood, with whatever attention. Most of the time that is very little attention.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'So feedback should be ignored.',reality:'No. Feedback tells you things you cannot see. The point is narrower: reception must not be the *definition* of quality, because reception is noisy in a way that effort is not.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The bowl is good. Every chair is empty and turned away. Both are true, and only one of them is the potter\u2019s business.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before you publish, send or present anything, decide privately what would make it good work whatever the reaction. Write it down. It has to survive silence.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Your talk gets warm applause, but you know you skipped the hard part of the argument. By this idea, how did it go?',options:['Below your standard: the applause does not change what you skipped','Well: the audience is the only judge that a talk can have','Impossible to say until some written feedback arrives','Well enough, since nobody in the room noticed the gap'],answer:0,why:'The idea cuts both ways. Praise is as noisy as silence, and the standard you set beforehand outranks both.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHERE THIS COMES FROM',text:'The Stoics divided the world in two: what is up to us, and what is not. Our judgements, intentions and efforts are ours. Reputation, reward and results are not. Marcus applies that division to work.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The good of an act lies in doing it rightly. Praise adds nothing to it and blame takes nothing away, so neither can be the measure of whether you did well.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'HIS OWN COMPARISONS',text:'Marcus points to things that are fine in themselves. An emerald is no worse for going unpraised. A vine bears its grapes and asks for nothing more. He wants his own good acts to be like that.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE STOIC ARCHER',text:'A Stoic image, reported by Cicero, makes the point sharply. The archer\u2019s task is to aim and release as well as possible. Once the arrow has left the bow, wind and a moving target decide the rest. Hitting is preferred. Shooting well is the job.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The practical form',text:'Decide the standard before you send the work. Written down in advance, a standard such as *clear, honest, useful to the reader* survives silence. Decided afterwards, it quietly becomes whatever the response was.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Carried too far, this becomes a refusal to learn. Results are evidence about your effort: an archer who never hits should question the aim. And some work exists to be received. A teacher whose class learns nothing cannot shelter behind effort. The claim is about what defines doing well, not about ignoring outcomes.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Where are you letting an audience grade work they did not watch you do?'},{kind:'recap',points:['Effort and judgement are yours. Reception is not.','Set the standard before you send the work.','Treat results as evidence, not as the verdict.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HONEST OBJECTION',prompt:'Which objection does the idea have to take seriously?',options:['Repeated bad results may show the effort itself was badly aimed','Praise is the only reliable sign that work was done well','Effort cannot be known, so only outcomes can be judged','Marcus never did work that other people had to assess'],answer:0,why:'Outcomes are not the measure, but they are evidence. Ignoring them entirely turns a discipline into an excuse.'}]}},meditations_7:{pictureCaption:'The warm blanket is still sliding off. The bare feet are already on the cold floor. Getting up did not wait for wanting to.',basics:{title:'You don\u2019t have to feel like it',beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'AN EMPEROR IN BED',text:'Marcus Aurelius ruled Rome almost 2,000 years ago. He was the most powerful man in the world. Some mornings he still did not want to get up. He wrote about it in his notebook.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'What do you think he told himself?',options:['That he would feel better once he was up','That getting up was his job, wanted or not','That he could stay in bed just this once'],answer:1,reveal:'He never said he wanted to get up. He said ants and bees get on with their work, and he was made for his. How he felt was not the point.'},{kind:'idea',text:'You do not have to feel like doing a thing to do it. Decide when you are calm. Then the grumpy feeling later does not get a vote.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The blanket is still sliding off the bed. The feet are already on the cold floor. They did not wait to feel like it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why not decide in the morning?',text:'In the morning you are sleepy and the bed is warm. That is the worst time to make a choice. So make the choice the night before, when you can think clearly.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A PROMISE TO A FRIEND',text:'You said you would help a friend on Saturday. On Saturday you would rather stay home. But the promise was made on a day you were thinking straight. So you go.'},{kind:'steps',title:'The night-before trick',steps:['At night, pick one thing for the morning.','Say it out loud, or write it down.','In the morning, do it first.','Notice the feeling. Don\u2019t ask it what to do.']},{kind:'question',prompt:'You planned to practise piano before school. In the morning you really don\u2019t feel like it. What does this idea say?',options:['You decided last night, so you play anyway','Skip it, since practice only works if you want to','Wait and see if the feeling has gone by lunch'],answer:0,why:'The choice was made when you were calm. The sleepy feeling can come along. It just does not get to decide.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'SIX A.M., ALARM GOING',prompt:'You planned a run before work. The alarm goes, and it is dark and cold. Who should decide whether you go?',options:['You, now, since you know how you feel','You, last night, when you made the plan','Whichever argument sounds better from bed'],answer:1,reveal:'The six o\u2019clock you is warm, groggy and arguing for the bed. It is the worst negotiator you have. Last night\u2019s you had a clear view, and already decided.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Act on the decision, not the mood. Make the commitment when you can see clearly, and treat how you feel at the moment of doing it as having no vote.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How Marcus argued himself up',text:'Book Five of his notebooks opens with him not wanting to leave his bed. He never once claims he wants to. He asks whether he was made to lie warm under blankets, and points to ants and bees getting on with their work. The feeling is noted, then set aside.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Feeling first',items:['Wait until you want to','The six o\u2019clock self decides','Lose most mornings']},right:{title:'Decision first',items:['Decide in advance','The rested self decides','The feeling is noted, not consulted']},note:'Most self-management fails because it tries to change the feeling before the action.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Not willpower',text:'Willpower is a fight at six in the morning, held on the bed\u2019s ground. This is a different question: who gets to decide? The cold version of you was never the one qualified to make the call, so there is no fight to win.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Warm blanket still sliding, bare feet already on cold stone. The getting up did not wait for the wanting to.'},{kind:'try',text:'Tonight, decide one thing tomorrow morning does, and when. Write it down. In the morning, notice the reluctance and do the thing anyway.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'On Sunday you agreed to send a difficult email first thing Monday. Monday comes and you dread it. Which response applies the idea?',options:['Send it: the dread is real, but it was never the one deciding','Wait until you feel ready, since a forced email will read badly','Reopen the decision to see whether it still feels right today','Find a way to feel enthusiastic about it before you start'],answer:0,why:'The decision was made with a clear view. Monday\u2019s dread can come along. It does not get to reopen the plan.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'A WORD FROM LAW',word:'Jurisdiction',meaning:'The authority to decide a particular kind of question. A court may rule on some cases and not others. Asking who has jurisdiction asks whose decision this is, before asking what it should be.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'BOOK FIVE, THE OPENING',title:'The dawn passage',text:'Book Five begins with Marcus reluctant to get up. He argues with himself, but never by claiming to want to. Was he made for pleasure, to lie under the covers? Plants, birds, ants and bees all do their own work. He is for his, and warmth was never part of the arrangement.'},{kind:'idea',text:'How you feel at the moment of acting was never the criterion. Settle the decision where you can see clearly, and the reluctance at dawn has nothing left to rule on.'},{kind:'explain',title:'A question of who decides',text:'Most self-management tries to change the feeling first and act second. That hands the decision to whoever you are at six in the morning. Marcus settles it by appeal to what he is for. The modern form moves it to the evening before, made by a rested self. Either way the cold self is never consulted, and a question of who decides is far easier to win than a contest of willpower.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHERE THE DECISION SITS',nodes:['Evening: decide, with a clear view','Dawn: reluctance arrives','Reluctance is noted, not consulted','The work starts'],note:'The feeling still happens. It has simply been given no case to hear.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Warm blanket sliding, bare feet on cold stone. The reluctance in this room is real. It just was not asked.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The morning self sometimes knows something. Exhaustion, illness and low mood are not laziness, and Marcus himself concedes that rest is needed, only that he takes more than nature asks. A plan made the night before can also be wrong. The rule is that a mood cannot reopen a decision, not that a new fact cannot. And for depression it is no substitute for help.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Make the commitment in advance, with a clear view.','How you feel at the moment of acting gets no vote.','New facts can reopen a decision. Moods cannot.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'MOOD OR FACT?',prompt:'You committed last night to train at dawn. Which of these should be allowed to reopen the plan?',options:['Waking with a fever and a sore throat','Waking warm and very much wanting to stay put','A strong sense that today is just not your day','The thought that one missed session hardly matters'],answer:0,why:'A fever is a new fact about the situation. The other three are the reluctance itself, which was never given a vote.'}]}},meditations_8:{pictureCaption:'Their letter lies on the desk. The reply in the hand carries the same seal, and it is going in the bin unsent.',bridge:'Idea 5 took the anger out of being wronged. This one is about what you do next, when hitting back is right there.',basics:{title:'Don\u2019t become the one who hurt you',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'GETTING EVEN',belief:'If someone is mean to you, being mean back makes it fair.',reality:'It does not make it fair. It makes two mean people instead of one. And now you are one of them.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'IN THE GROUP CHAT',text:'Someone told the whole class a silly thing you said. You know an embarrassing thing about them, too. You could post it right now. It would really work.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The best way to get back at someone is to not be like them. Hitting back turns you into the thing you were upset about.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE THROWING MUD',text:'You can throw mud at someone. It might even hit them. But now your hands are covered in mud too, and everyone saw you throw it.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO SAID IT',text:'Marcus Aurelius was a Roman emperor long ago. He wrote that the best revenge is not to be like the person who hurt you. He saw that hitting back hurts the one who does it, too.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A letter came, with a red seal. The reply in this hand has the same red seal. It is going in the bin, not the post.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Is that just letting them win?',text:'No. You can still say, \u201cThat was not okay.\u201d You can still tell a teacher or a parent. That is not revenge. It is getting help. What you skip is doing the same mean thing back.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A boy trips you on purpose in PE. What does this idea say to do?',options:['Tell the teacher, and don\u2019t trip him back','Trip him back so he learns how it feels','Pretend it never happened and say nothing'],answer:0,why:'Telling someone is fair and smart. Tripping him back would make you the one who trips people.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE REPLY IS READY',text:'A colleague has taken credit for your work in front of the team. You know something unflattering about them. It is true, and it would land. You can already hear yourself saying it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The best revenge is not to be like the person who wronged you. Retaliation works, which is why it tempts. But it spends the one thing you still hold: being plainly not the person who does this.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What retaliation actually buys',text:'The moment you hit back, the two of you are playing the same game. You can now be beaten at it, and everyone watching reads every later exchange through that frame. A situation where you were wronged has become one where you are a participant.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW YOUR POSITION CHANGES',nodes:['They wrong you','You are the injured party','You hit back, and it lands','Now you are both players'],note:'The hit lands, and the one advantage you had goes with it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What Marcus kept noticing',text:'He returns to it often: the wrong someone does you damages them, and your anger about it damages you. Retaliate and two people are doing the same thing. Only one of them started it, and from outside it soon becomes hard to say which.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Their letter on the desk. Your reply in your hand, sealed the same way. It is going in the bin, not the post.'},{kind:'try',text:'When someone wrongs you, write down what retaliating would cost you specifically: which relationship, which reputation, which future exchange. Decide with that list in view.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A rival posts a misleading account of a dispute you had with them. Which response follows the idea?',options:['Correct the facts plainly, once, and leave their character out of it','Post the embarrassing thing you know about them, since it is true','Ask friends to post a slanted account of your own in return','Reply in exactly their tone, so they feel what it was like'],answer:0,why:'You can answer without becoming like them. Correcting the record is not retaliation. Copying their move, directly or through friends, is.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'THE EASTERN REVOLT, 175',text:'In 175 Avidius Cassius, Rome\u2019s leading general in the east, declared himself emperor, apparently after a false report that Marcus had died. Within about three months his own officers killed him. Ancient historians record that Marcus dealt mildly with most of those who had joined the revolt. His line on revenge cannot be dated against these events, but it was tested by them.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The best revenge is to be unlike the person who wronged you. It reads as a moral instruction, but it is closer to a warning about what retaliation does to the one retaliating.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The line and its neighbours',text:'The sentence itself is short, in Book Six. Around it the notebooks keep making two observations. The wrongdoer harms himself first. And your anger at a wrong often costs you more than the wrong itself did. Retaliation adds a third harm: it makes you a second wrongdoer.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO POSITIONS',left:{title:'Wronged',items:['The injury is theirs','Onlookers can see who started it','Every option is still open']},right:{title:'Retaliating',items:['The injury is shared','It reads as a feud','You can be beaten at their game']},note:'The retort may well be true, and may well land. That is exactly why it is expensive.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two letters with matching seals: theirs on the desk, yours in hand. The reply that would make them a pair goes in the bin.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Not forgiveness',text:'Marcus is not asking you to forgive, or to feel warmly. His point is narrower and colder. The one asset you still hold is that you are not the kind of person who does this. Retaliation spends it, and buys only a moment\u2019s satisfaction.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Read badly, this becomes passivity: never answering, never naming a wrong, letting someone carry on. Being unlike them still allows a firm reply, a complaint, a consequence. And if you hold back only to look better than them, you are still playing to the audience. Marcus\u2019s concern is what you become, not how you appear.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'WHERE IS THE LINE?',prompt:'Which of these still counts as being unlike the person who wronged you?',options:['Reporting their conduct through the proper channel, with the facts','Spreading a true but unrelated secret of theirs to even things up','Matching their tone exactly, so they feel what it was like','Staying silent while they repeat it, to show you are above it'],answer:0,why:'A firm, factual response is not retaliation. Copying their method makes you like them, and a silence that lets it continue is not what Marcus asks either.'}]}},meditations_9:{pictureCaption:'An empty room, and one patch of the colour already chosen for it. The room is the fact. The patch is the life pictured there.',bridge:'Idea 1 split an event from your verdict on it. This one finds what you are grieving: often not the event, but a future you expected.',basics:{title:'Sad about what happened, or what you hoped for?',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE TRIP IS OFF',text:'Your class trip to the beach is called off. Write down just the facts. *The trip is off. A storm is coming. It might move to next month.* That is it. Three lines.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'So why does it feel so much bigger than three lines?',options:['The facts are worse than they look','You had already pictured the day'],answer:1,reveal:'You had seen it in your head. The bus. The ice cream. Your friends in the waves. That picture is what you lost. It was a plan, not a day you had.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When a plan falls through, the facts are often small. Most of the sadness is for the day you had pictured. Know which one you are sad about.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'AN OLD HABIT',text:'Marcus Aurelius was a Roman emperor long ago. When something upset him, he wrote it down as plain facts. The facts were often small. The rest was stuff he had added himself.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO LISTS',left:{title:'What happened',items:['The trip is off','A storm is coming','It may move to next month']},right:{title:'What you had pictured',items:['The bus ride with friends','Ice cream on the sand','A great story for Monday']},note:'It is fine to be sad about the second list. That list is the one you can change.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'THE ROOM IN HIS HEAD',caption:'The room is empty. That is the fact. The orange patch is the colour he had picked for it. That is what he will miss. And a colour can be picked again.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your best friend can\u2019t come to your party after all. What does this idea suggest?',options:['See that you are sad about the day you pictured, then picture a new one','Decide the whole party is ruined and call it off','Tell yourself it does not matter at all'],answer:0,why:'The fact is small: one friend can\u2019t come. The big feeling is for the picture. And a picture can be redrawn.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'A REORGANISATION',text:'Your team is moved under another manager. On paper nothing is taken from you: same pay, same title, same desk. Written as facts, it is three lines. Yet the week feels like a loss you cannot quite name.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Strip an upsetting event to what literally happened and it is usually small. What remains is a future you had already furnished and moved into. That is what you are grieving.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'THE FACT AND THE PATCH',caption:'Written as facts, this room is one line: empty, and not going to be yours. The orange patch is the future you had already started living in.'},{kind:'explain',title:'A diagnostic, not a comfort',text:'This is not a trick for feeling better. It shows you where the loss sits. In the reorganisation the facts are three lines. The feeling is about the next two years, in which the team was still yours. You had been living there.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'EVENT AND EXPECTATION',left:{title:'The event',items:['The team moves under someone else','Pay and title unchanged','Cannot be revised']},right:{title:'The expectation',items:['The team was still yours in two years','You would build it your way','Can be revised']},note:'Events cannot be changed. Expectations can. Mistaking one for the other is what keeps you stuck.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'If the facts are small, the loss is not real.',reality:'The loss is real. It is just somewhere else. You are mourning an expectation, not an event, and an expectation can be mourned and then rewritten.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Two pages, not one',steps:['Write the event as bare facts, in a few lines.','On a separate page, write what you assumed would follow.','Let yourself grieve the second page.','Then draft a new expectation you can actually build on.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A flat you had already furnished in your head falls through when the landlord sells. Which response applies the idea?',options:['Separate the fact, no lease, from the life you pictured there, and grieve that','Tell yourself it was only a flat and the sadness is silly','Go over the landlord\u2019s decision until it finally makes sense','Refuse to picture any future home until a lease is signed'],answer:0,why:'The facts are short and fixed. The sadness belongs to the life you had imagined, which is a real loss and open to revision.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE OBVIOUS OBJECTION',belief:'Reducing a loss to bare facts is a way of pretending it did not hurt.',reality:'It is a way of finding where the hurt lives. Marcus holds that nearly all of it sits in the commentary you supplied, and commentary, unlike fact, can be examined.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'HOW HE DOES IT',text:'He describes things down to their materials: a fine dish is a dead fish, a purple robe is sheep\u2019s wool dyed in shellfish blood. Elsewhere he tells himself to say no more than first appearances report. Someone spoke ill of you: that was reported. That you were harmed was not.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Strip an upsetting event to what literally occurred. Whatever large feeling survives the stripping is usually attached to an expectation, and an expectation, unlike an event, can be revised.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Same tool, different target',text:'Idea 1 used the bare description to catch a verdict: *they despise my work.* Here it catches something quieter. The facts of a reorganisation are three lines. The weight comes from a future you had already furnished, which nobody took from you, because nobody had it yet.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'WHERE THE WEIGHT SITS',caption:'The fact here is a bare room that was never yet his. The weight sits in the single patch of colour: an expectation he had already begun to live in.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHY CONFUSION KEEPS YOU STUCK',nodes:['An expectation is lost','It is mistaken for the event','You argue with what cannot change','The grief has nowhere to finish'],note:'Named as an expectation, the loss can be mourned and then rewritten. An event can only be accepted.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Some losses are events through and through. A death, an injury or a betrayal is not three lines of fact with a story added, and stripping it down can feel cruel. Use this on your own setbacks, never on someone else\u2019s grief. And an expectation that was promised to you, then broken, is an event in its own right.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of a plan that fell through this year. What, exactly, had you already started living in?'},{kind:'recap',points:['Stripped to facts, most setbacks are short.','What remains is usually an expectation, not an event.','Expectations can be mourned and revised. Events can only be accepted.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'WHERE IT DOES NOT BELONG',prompt:'For which of these is the three-lines exercise least appropriate?',options:['Consoling a friend whose parent has just died','Missing out on a promotion you had assumed was yours','A holiday cancelled after months of planning it','A project you led being handed to another team'],answer:0,why:'The exercise is for your own setbacks, where a story has been added. A bereavement is an event in full, and someone else\u2019s grief is not yours to strip down.'}]}},meditations_10:{pictureCaption:'Eyes on the lane ahead. Whatever is going on past the fence, the runner is spending none of his time on it.',bridge:'Idea 6 took the verdict away from the people watching you. This one counts what it costs you to keep watching them.',basics:{title:'Stop looking at the next lane',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'SPORTS DAY',text:'You are running the big race. You keep looking at the girl in the next lane. Is she ahead? Is she faster? Each look is quick. But each one pulls you off your own run.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'NOW LOOK AT HIM',caption:'This runner does the other thing. His eyes stay on his own lane, never the next one. All of his time goes into his own run.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Every time you check what someone else is doing, it costs you a little time. The checks add up. Spend that time on your own work instead.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO SAID IT',text:'Marcus Aurelius was a Roman emperor long ago. He wrote that you save so much time when you stop watching what other people say and do. You can put that time into doing your own part well.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A DRIPPING TAP',text:'One drip from a tap is nothing. But leave it dripping all day and the bucket fills up. Checking on other people is like that. Each check is tiny. The bucket of lost time is not.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'IN A GAME',text:'You keep checking your friend\u2019s score in a game you both play. Ten times a day. Each check feels like nothing. But that time could make you better. And soon you are playing her way, not yours.'},{kind:'try',text:'Tomorrow, make a tally mark each time you check what someone else is doing. Don\u2019t tell yourself off. Just count.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend is reading a long book faster than you. What does this idea say to do?',options:['Read your own book at your own pace','Keep asking her what page she is on','Skip pages so you can catch her up'],answer:0,why:'Checking her page costs you time, and racing her changes how you read. Your book is the one in your hands.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'ELEVEN IN THE MORNING',prompt:'You check what a competitor shipped. It is the fourth check today, each a minute or two. What is the real cost?',options:['A few minutes, nothing more','The minutes, plus a pace that is no longer yours','Nothing, since knowing the field is useful'],answer:1,reveal:'The minutes are the smaller half. Each check also nudges you to adjust to someone running a different race, over a different distance, for reasons you cannot see.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Watching what others do is not one act but a standing subscription. It costs time you never notice spending, and it pulls your pace towards a race that is not yours.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the cost is invisible',text:'Nobody experiences an hour of checking. They experience forty separate minutes that each felt like nothing. Each check is short and the checking never ends, so the total never shows up anywhere.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'BOOK FOUR',title:'The runner',picture:!0,text:'Marcus asks how much time a person gains by not caring what a neighbour has said or done, and caring only whether his own acts are just. He follows it with an image: running straight along your own line, not looking about.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHAT A CHECK COSTS',left:{title:'Visible',items:['A minute here','A minute there']},right:{title:'Invisible',items:['Attention that is slow to come back','A plan bent towards their distance','A target someone else set']},note:'The lost time is the smaller half of the damage.'},{kind:'try',text:'For one week, notice each time you check what a peer or competitor is doing. Count the checks rather than judging them.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A friend training for a marathon checks a rival\u2019s running app after every session. By this idea, what is the larger cost?',options:['Pacing to the rival\u2019s plan, which was built for a different race','The few minutes spent opening the app every day','Feeling bad about being slower than the rival','Giving the rival\u2019s profile more attention than it deserves'],answer:0,why:'Marcus\u2019s image is a runner glancing sideways. The time matters, but setting your pace by someone else\u2019s race does more damage.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'BOOK FOUR',title:'A question that sounds recent',picture:!0,text:'Book Four asks how much leisure a person gains by not caring what a neighbour said, did or attempted, and caring only that his own acts are just. It reads as if written this year. Then comes an image: running straight along the line rather than looking about.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Every glance at what others are doing is a small payment of the thing Marcus treats as scarce: time. The larger loss is the pace you borrow from a race you are not running.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW A GLANCE BECOMES A PACE',nodes:['A quick check on a peer','A twinge: ahead or behind?','You adjust your plan to theirs','Another check, to see how it went'],loop:!0,note:'Each round is short. The loop itself has no end, which is why its total never shows.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the time is for',text:'Read to the end, the sentence is not about productivity. The time saved is to be spent making your own acts just. Watching neighbours is wrong for Marcus because it moves attention off the one thing in your charge, your own conduct, and onto things that are not.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO KINDS OF LOOKING',left:{title:'Scheduled',items:['Chosen in advance','Answers a real question','Ends']},right:{title:'Subscription',items:['Set off by a twinge','Answers nothing new','Never ends']},note:'The limit is not on knowing. It is on checking that has stopped being a decision.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Some watching is necessary. A business has to know its market, a learner improves by watching people better than them, and Marcus governed by knowing what others did. The idea does not ban looking. It targets curiosity that serves no decision, and the habit of setting your pace by someone whose distance and reasons you cannot see.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Whose progress do you check most often? Which decision has any of those checks actually changed?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'Which of these does Marcus\u2019s point leave untouched?',options:['A quarterly look at competitors, done once to inform a pricing decision','Refreshing a rival\u2019s profile several times a day to see what is new','Changing your training each week to match a friend\u2019s mileage','Reading a colleague\u2019s messages to see what they say about you'],answer:0,why:'A scheduled look that serves a decision is not the target. The other three are open-ended watching that spends time and borrows someone else\u2019s pace.'}]}},meditations_11:{pictureCaption:'A hand in the river cannot hold it still. The water runs on past the fingers, the leaf with it. Nothing here stays put.',bridge:'Idea 2 read an obstacle once it had arrived. This one expects the ground to move before it does, and builds plans that survive it.',basics:{title:'Nothing stays the same for long',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE THE WEATHER',text:'If it is sunny today, you would not leave your coat at home for the whole year. And if it rains today, you would not think it will rain forever. Weather changes. So does nearly everything else.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Almost nothing stays the way it is now. Bad times don\u2019t last. Good times don\u2019t either. So don\u2019t act as if today is how it will always be.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'A ROMAN EMPEROR SAID IT',picture:!0,text:'Marcus Aurelius ruled Rome long ago. He wrote that the world is like a river. It keeps on flowing. Hardly anything in it stays put. He did not find this sad. He found it useful.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO MISTAKES',left:{title:'When things are bad',items:['\u201cIt will always be like this\u201d','\u201cI am just bad at maths\u201d']},right:{title:'When things are good',items:['\u201cMy team will always win\u201d','\u201cThis group will never change\u201d']},note:'Both forget the same thing. It will change.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'A PLAN WITH A BACKUP',text:'You plan to walk to school with your friend every day. That plan needs one thing to stay the same: your friend. What if she moves house? A good plan says what you will do then, too.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one plan you have. Ask what it needs to stay the same. Then think of one thing you would do if that changed.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You lost three games in a row. It feels like you will never win again. What would Marcus say?',options:['Things change, and this bad run will not last forever','You are bad at this game, so stop playing it','Don\u2019t think about what comes next at all'],answer:0,why:'He thought hardly anything stays settled. That includes a losing run.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'A MISREADING',belief:'If nothing is settled, there is no point making plans.',reality:'Marcus is not arguing against plans. He is arguing against one hidden assumption inside them: that the current arrangement is the permanent one. Plans should be built to survive their conditions changing.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'NEXT QUARTER',text:'The plan for next quarter looks solid. Read it again and it rests on one thing staying exactly as it is today: one client, one manager, one price. Nobody wrote that assumption down. Nobody has to, until it moves.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Almost nothing is settled. Much anxiety, and many bad plans, rest on an unspoken belief that today\u2019s arrangement is permanent. Build as if the conditions will change, because they will.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'ONE ERROR, BOTH WAYS',left:{title:'In a bad stretch',items:['\u201cThis is just how things are now\u201d','A hard quarter read as a career verdict']},right:{title:'In a good stretch',items:['Weather treated as if it were ground','Real shock when it moves']},note:'One assumption, two costs.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why he keeps saying it',picture:!0,text:'Marcus writes that everything is like a river in continual flow, with causes shifting in endless ways and hardly anything standing still. He is not being gloomy. He is describing the world he governed, through war and plague. It is also a fair description of any industry you have worked in.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Stress-test one plan',steps:['Name the one condition the plan silently assumes will hold.','Ask how likely it is to change while the plan runs.','Decide now what you will do when it does.'],note:'A decision made calmly now beats one improvised in shock later.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Your small business does well because one large customer orders every month. What does the idea suggest?',options:['Decide now what you would do if that customer stopped ordering','Enjoy it, since worrying about change is just pessimism','Turn down new work so the big customer gets full attention','Keep plans vague, since none of them will survive anyway'],answer:0,why:'The good stretch is weather, not ground. Naming the assumption and planning for its loss is what Marcus\u2019s point asks of you.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'timeline',kicker:'A REIGN WITHOUT A SETTLED YEAR',points:[{when:'161',what:'Marcus becomes emperor, sharing power with Lucius Verus. War with Parthia follows.'},{when:'c. 165',what:'A plague spreads through the empire, carried back by the armies from the east.'},{when:'169',what:'Lucius Verus dies. Marcus rules alone, with war on the Danube.'},{when:'175',what:'His leading general in the east, Avidius Cassius, claims the throne.'},{when:'180',what:'Marcus dies while still on campaign in the north.'}],note:'This is the man writing that nothing is settled.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Nothing is settled: things, actions and causes are all in flux. The assumption under most anxiety, that the present arrangement is the permanent one, is false in both directions.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'BOOK FIVE',title:'The river',picture:!0,text:'In Book Five he describes substance as a river in continual flow, actions always changing, causes varying in countless ways, and almost nothing standing still. The image echoes Heraclitus, the earlier Greek thinker of flux, whom Marcus names several times in the notebooks.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'GROUND OR WEATHER',left:{title:'Treated as ground',items:['Built on directly','Never named in the plan','Its loss is a shock']},right:{title:'Treated as weather',items:['Built around','Named as an assumption','Its loss was allowed for']},note:'The same client, manager or price can be either. The difference lies only in the planning.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the reserve clause is rational',text:'Idea 2 introduced the Stoic habit of adding *unless something prevents it* to every plan. Flux is the reason that habit is rational rather than timid. If conditions are always moving, a plan without the clause is a bet that this time they will not.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Some things are stable enough to build on, and treating everything as weather becomes its own anxiety, or an excuse never to commit. Flux also favours no direction: a bad stretch ending does not promise a good one. The useful version names one or two load-bearing assumptions. It does not try to plan for everything.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Hardly anything is settled. The present arrangement is not the permanent one.','The error runs both ways: despair in bad stretches, complacency in good ones.','Name the assumption a plan rests on, and decide now what happens when it goes.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'WHICH READING HOLDS UP?',prompt:'Who is using Marcus on flux well?',options:['A founder who names the one supplier the plan depends on, and finds a second','A student who will not choose a course, since everything will change anyway','A manager who expects a bad quarter to fix itself, since nothing lasts','Someone who avoids close friendships, since people always drift apart'],answer:0,why:'Flux is a reason to build plans that survive change. It is not a reason to avoid commitment, or to wait for change to do the work.'}]}},meditations_12:{pictureCaption:'One candle for each person who remembers. Far down the row they have already gone out. The nearest still burns, for now.',bridge:'Idea 3 used death to shrink the audience you fear. This one turns it on the praise you hope for, and what that hope makes you choose.',basics:{title:'Would you still do it if no one knew?',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE SCHOOL FAIR',prompt:'Two jobs are left. Paint the big sign at the gate, with your name in the corner. Or fix the wobbly stall that keeps falling over. Which job most needs doing?',options:['The sign, because everyone will see it','The stall, because it keeps falling over'],answer:1,reveal:'The sign is nice. But the stall is the job that needs you. It is easy to pick the job people will see and remember.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Do the job that needs doing, not the job that gets you noticed. Being remembered is a poor reason to pick what you do.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Marcus\u2019s sum',picture:!0,text:'Marcus Aurelius was a Roman emperor. Some people wanted to be famous after they died. He did a simple sum. The people who remember you will die one day too. So will the people after them. In the end, no one is left to remember.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A NAME IN THE SAND',text:'Writing your name in the sand is fun. Then the waves come in. Being remembered is a bit like that. It is real, but it does not stay.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The test',text:'Ask one question. Would I still do this if someone else got the thanks? If yes, the job is worth doing. If no, you may have picked it for the thanks.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Wanting to be thanked makes you a bad person.',reality:'No. Everyone likes to be thanked. Marcus only says don\u2019t let it choose for you. Pick the job that needs doing. If thanks come too, good.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You helped clean up the classroom, but the teacher thanks someone else. What does this idea say?',options:['The room is clean, and that was the point','You should have made sure she saw you','Next time, only help when people are watching'],answer:0,why:'The job needed doing, and it got done. The thanks were extra.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO PROJECTS, ONE SLOT',left:{title:'Has your name on it',items:['Seen by everyone','Easy to finish and show','Will be remembered']},right:{title:'Needed doing',items:['Mostly invisible','Slow, maybe never finished','Will be forgotten']},note:'You have time for one. Be honest about which you are leaning towards, and why.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Choosing work for how it will be remembered quietly bends your decisions. And the remembering is held by people who will soon be gone themselves.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HIS ARITHMETIC',nodes:['You do the remembered thing','Others remember it','They die, as you will','Those after them die too'],note:'Book Four sets it out almost as a sum. The memory is real. It is simply held by people on the same schedule as you.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'THE SUM, IN CANDLES',caption:'Each candle is someone who might remember your work. They go out in order, and the dark keeps coming up the row.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the motive does to choices',text:'It favours the visible over the useful, the finishable over the important, and the thing with your name on it over the thing that needed doing. And it can never be satisfied, because the verdict arrives after you can no longer hear it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Not against reputation',text:'Marcus is not saying a good name is worthless while you live. He cared about governing justly, and a good name is what lets people trust you. His target is narrower: work chosen *because of* how it will be remembered.'},{kind:'try',text:'Take the thing you are proudest of doing this year. Ask whether you would have done it if someone else had got the credit.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You can lead a launch with your name on it, or quietly fix the billing errors customers keep hitting. Which question does the idea tell you to ask?',options:['Would I still choose this if the credit went to someone else?','Which one will look better in my annual review?','Which one will people still talk about in ten years?','Which one can I finish before the deadline?'],answer:0,why:'The credit test separates work you want done from work you want to be seen doing. The other questions are the motive Marcus warns against.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'BEFORE THE ARGUMENT',prompt:'Marcus argues against wanting fame after death. What is his main reason?',options:['Fame is sinful pride','Fame usually goes to the wrong people','The people who remember you are mortal too'],answer:2,reveal:'His case is almost arithmetic. Those who remember you will soon be dead, then those who follow them, until the whole line of admirers is gone. No sermon is needed.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Reputation after death is borrowed from the dying: every admirer is on the same schedule as you. It is a poor motive, and work chosen for it is quietly worse work.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'THE WHOLE LINE OF ADMIRERS',caption:'The far candles are out and the dark is advancing. The one still burning is on the same schedule as the rest.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'BOOK FOUR',title:'Where it sits',text:'The argument comes in Book Four, among notes on how fast names fade. He adds a second step: even if the memory lasted forever, what would the praise be worth to you? Idea 3 used the same arithmetic against fear. Here it is aimed at desire.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The narrower target',text:'He is not arguing that reputation is worthless while you live. He cared about governing justly, and a ruler\u2019s name for justice does real work. The target is work chosen *because of* how it will be remembered. That motive favours the visible, the finishable and the signed, and it can never be satisfied.'},{kind:'term',word:'By-product',meaning:'Something produced along the way while you aim at something else. The implied test turns on it: is the reputation a by-product of real work, or is the work a by-product of wanting reputation?'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'WHICH IS THE BY-PRODUCT?',low:'Done for the credit; usefulness incidental',high:'Done because it was needed; credit incidental',note:'The test is whether you would still do it if the credit went to someone else. Most real work sits somewhere along this line.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Credit is not only vanity. It pays rent, earns trust and decides who gets the next chance, so the test can be unfair to someone whose work is routinely claimed by others. And the arithmetic shows that fame is brief, not that it is nothing. The argument targets a motive, not the wish to be paid and promoted fairly.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'A colleague objects that if they never claim credit, someone else takes it and they are never promoted. What is the best reply from the idea?',options:['Claim fair credit; the idea warns against letting credit choose the work','Credit is vanity, so let it go to whoever wants it most','Promotion is a kind of fame, so it is equally worthless','Work quietly and trust that good work is always noticed'],answer:0,why:'Marcus targets work chosen for how it will be remembered. Being paid and promoted fairly for real work is a different matter.'}]}}}},2982,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'meetings-that-earn-it_1':{pictureCaption:'Eight coats on eight hooks outside one door. Whatever happens in that room is costing eight people\u2019s time, not one.',basics:{title:'One hour can cost eight',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE CLASS PLAY',prompt:'Eight kids give up their lunch hour to plan the class play. How much time did the plan cost?',options:['One hour','Eight hours'],answer:1,reveal:'Each kid gave one hour. There were eight kids. So the plan cost eight hours of time, not one.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When a group meets, the time is not one hour. It is one hour for each person in the room. Add it up before you call everyone in.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Eight coats on eight hooks, outside one door. That means eight people\u2019s time is being used in there, all at once.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'EVERY MONDAY',text:'Say your team meets every Monday for an hour, just to talk. There are eight of you. That is eight hours each week. In ten weeks, it is eighty hours.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why nobody adds it up',text:'Each person only feels their own hour. Nobody feels all eight. So the big number stays hidden. It only shows up when someone does the sum out loud.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time a group wants to meet, do the sum. Hours times people. Say the answer out loud. Then ask if the plan is worth that much.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A teacher keeps all thirty kids back for ten minutes. The news is about lost coats, and it is for two kids. What does the sum show?',options:['It costs five hours of time, so just tell the two','It costs ten minutes, so it is no big deal','It costs thirty minutes, one for each kid'],answer:0,why:'Ten minutes times thirty kids is three hundred minutes. That is five hours, spent on news for two people.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'A WEEKLY INVITATION',text:'You set up a weekly hour to keep the project on track. Eight people accept. The invitation took thirty seconds to send, and nobody, including you, thought about what it would cost.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A meeting costs its length multiplied by the people in it. An hour with eight people is a working day. Do that sum before you send the invitation and it changes which meetings happen.'},{kind:'steps',title:'The sum, done out loud',steps:['One hour times eight people is eight hours: a working day.','Every week for a year, that is roughly fifty working days.','Ask whether anyone would approve fifty days as one line in a budget.']},{kind:'explain',title:'Why nobody does the sum',text:'The arithmetic is trivial. It goes undone because the cost is spread out: each person feels only their own hour, and nobody feels the total. Fifty days as a budget line would be questioned. The same fifty days as a calendar entry is approved every week without a thought.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Eight coats outside one door. The calendar says one hour. The hooks say eight.'},{kind:'try',text:'Put the cost in your next invitation: 1 hour \xd7 8 people = one working day. Then read the guest list again.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You are about to invite twelve people to a ninety-minute review. What does the idea ask you to do first?',options:['Work out that it costs eighteen hours, and decide if it is worth that','Cut it to an hour so it takes up less of the calendar','Send it anyway, since anyone not needed can decline','Book it later in the week, when calendars are emptier'],answer:0,why:'The cost is length times people: eighteen hours. Once that number is on the table, the guest list and the meeting itself get decided differently.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHERE THE PIECE STARTS',text:'This piece starts from a claim about price: a meeting is paid for by the room, not the calendar. The calendar shows one hour. The room contains that hour once for every person in it. This first idea is the arithmetic. The next adds the time around the meeting.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The cost of a meeting is its duration multiplied by its attendees. Stated as a single figure at the moment of inviting, it changes which meetings happen at all.'},{kind:'term',word:'Distributed cost',meaning:'A cost split across many people, so each bears a small share and nobody sees the total. Small shares are easy to approve. The total, if anyone saw it, often would not be.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHY THE SUM GOES UNDONE',nodes:['Invitation sent: one hour','Each person loses their own hour','Nobody holds the total','The entry recurs, unquestioned'],note:'Nothing in a calendar ever shows the eight-hour figure, so nothing prompts the question.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where the saving happens',text:'The piece claims most of the reduction comes at the moment of inviting, not from later reforms of process. A plausible reason: once a meeting exists it has a slot, a habit and people who expect it. Before it exists, the number is only a question about whether to spend it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Eight coats on the hooks and one closed door. Inside, the calendar entry says one hour.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The sum counts cost, not value. A working day spent on a decision that saves a month is cheap. Hours are not all equal either: one that blocks someone\u2019s best work costs more than one they would have spent on email. Used crudely, the number pushes people to cut attendees who were needed, which moves the cost into rework later.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these misuses the idea?',options:['Dropping the one engineer who can say if the plan is feasible, to cut the cost','Noting in an invitation that the session will cost about a working day','Making a weekly hour fortnightly after working out its yearly cost','Asking whether a decision really needs eight people before booking it'],answer:0,why:'The sum is a question about whether the time is worth it, not a number to drive down. Removing the person the decision depends on cuts the visible cost and creates a larger hidden one.'}]}},'meetings-that-earn-it_2':{pictureCaption:'One short block set in the middle of a long plank. The offcuts either side are too short to build anything with.',bridge:'Idea 1 counted the hour inside the meeting. This one counts the time it spoils around it.',basics:{title:'The time around it gets broken',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'SATURDAY MORNING',text:'You have a free morning to build a big model. It will take three hours. But you have a swim lesson right in the middle. You can\u2019t get far before it. After it, you are tired and wet. The model waits.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A thing in the middle of your free time costs more than its own length. It breaks the time on each side into bits too short to use.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A LONG PLANK',picture:!0,text:'Take a long plank. Cut one short piece out of the middle. Now the two ends are short bits, and each is too short for a shelf. One small piece spoilt the whole plank.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'SAME LESSON, TWO TIMES',left:{title:'Lesson in the middle',items:['A short bit before','The lesson','A short bit after']},right:{title:'Lesson at the start',items:['The lesson','Then one long free stretch']},note:'Same lesson. Same length. Only one morning has room to build.'},{kind:'try',text:'Look at your next free day. If one thing sits in the middle, can it move to the start or the end?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You need two hours to finish a big drawing. A friend wants to play for half an hour. When is best?',options:['Right at the start or right at the end','Right in the middle, as a little break','It makes no difference at all'],answer:0,why:'In the middle, it splits your two hours into bits too short to finish in. At the start or end, the long stretch stays whole.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TWO DAYS',prompt:'Day A has three half-hour meetings spread across it. Day B has one two-hour meeting first thing. Which usually leaves more room for demanding work?',options:['Day A: only ninety minutes of meetings','Day B: the rest of the day is clear'],answer:1,reveal:'Day A has fewer meeting minutes, but every gap is broken at both ends. Day B spends more time in the room and leaves one long stretch intact.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A meeting\u2019s real cost includes what it does to the time around it. An hour in the middle of a morning splits it into two blocks too short for demanding work, and that loss is recorded nowhere.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where the time goes',text:'The hour before a meeting is too short to start anything real, so it goes on small tasks. The time after goes on reassembly: finding your place and reloading what you were thinking. Neither shows on the calendar, which records only the meeting.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Spacing meetings out is kinder. It gives everyone room to breathe.',reality:'It feels polite, but it is usually the more expensive arrangement. Every gap becomes an offcut. Clustering meetings leaves the rest of the day whole, which is kinder to anyone who needs to think.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A short job in the middle of a long plank. The two offcuts are what a mid-morning meeting leaves you.'},{kind:'steps',title:'What to do',steps:['Cluster meetings against each other rather than spreading them out.','Protect long clear blocks on purpose.','Treat a request for the middle of a clear morning as the expensive kind.','Offer the edges of the day first.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A colleague asks for thirty minutes. Your morning is clear and you have a meeting at two. Which slot does the idea favour?',options:['Half past one, next to the meeting at two','Half past ten, to break up the morning','Any time: half an hour barely registers','Two short calls, to keep things light'],answer:0,why:'Placing it against the existing meeting keeps the clear morning whole. Half an hour at half past ten would split it into two shorter blocks.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO RECORDS OF ONE MORNING',left:{title:'What the calendar shows',items:['11:00 to 12:00, meeting','The rest: free']},right:{title:'What actually went',items:['The hour before: too short to start','The meeting itself','The time after: getting back in']},note:'Only the left column is ever written down.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A meeting fragments the time around it. Its true cost is its length plus the blocks it leaves too short for demanding work, which is why scattered meetings can consume a day out of proportion to their length.'},{kind:'term',word:'Fragmentation',meaning:'Splitting a long stretch of time into pieces. The total time is unchanged, but each piece is too short for work that needs a long run at it, so the usable time falls.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The mechanism',text:'Demanding work has a start-up cost: loading the problem, getting back to where you were. A block barely longer than that cost is worth little. So the measure that matters is not hours spent in meetings but long blocks left over. By that measure, three short meetings can cost more than one long one.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The block in the middle is the meeting. The offcuts either side are the morning it leaves behind.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why calendars fill the wrong way',text:'A clear morning looks like the easiest place to put something, so it fills first, and politeness spreads meetings out. The piece argues the reverse: price the middle of a clear block highest, stack meetings against each other, and defend the block that results.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The idea assumes work that needs long unbroken stretches. For someone whose job is mostly coordination, short blocks may be the work, and clustering matters less to them, though it may matter to the people they meet. Stacking has its own cost: back-to-back meetings leave no time to act on one before the next. And a defended block is only worth defending if the demanding work happens in it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HARD CASE',prompt:'A manager says their day is all short calls anyway, so fragmentation does not apply to them. What is the best reply?',options:['Partly fair, but their calls may be breaking up other people\u2019s long blocks','Wrong: every meeting costs double its length, whatever the job','Right: once you are used to switching, fragmentation costs nothing','Wrong: they should cancel the calls to create long blocks anyway'],answer:0,why:'Fragmentation costs most where work needs a long run-up. Their own day may have none, but the people on the other end of the calls may.'}]}},'meetings-that-earn-it_3':{pictureCaption:'Several tools are crammed onto one hook, while a hook beside them hangs empty. That is one hour asked to do every job.',bridge:'The first two ideas priced a meeting. This one asks what job it is for, which decides who should be in it.',basics:{title:'What is this meeting for?',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'A COMMON MIX-UP',belief:'A meeting is a meeting. Everyone sits down and talks.',reality:'Groups meet to do four different jobs. Each job needs different people and a different plan. Mix them up and the meeting goes wrong.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Before you call a meeting, name its job. Is it to decide, to think up ideas, to get everyone on the same plan, or to share news?'},{kind:'explain',title:'The four jobs',text:'Deciding: pick one choice. Only the people who choose need to come. Ideas: think up new things. Keep it small, so all can speak. Agreeing: get everyone on the same plan. Sharing news: tell people things. That one often needs no meeting at all.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Look at the middle hook. Lots of tools are crammed on it, and a hook nearby is empty. That is one meeting trying to do every job.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE CLUB NAME',text:'Your club meets to pick a name. First someone shares news about the trip. Then the talk drifts to new games. Twelve kids, lots of chat. The bell rings. Still no name.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time your group meets, say the job out loud first. We are here to decide. Or: we are here for ideas.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your class needs to pick a day for the bake sale. Who should be in that meeting?',options:['The few people who will pick the day','The whole class, so everyone hears','Anyone with an idea for a cake'],answer:0,why:'This is a deciding job. It needs the people who choose, with the days ready to pick from.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE \u201cQUICK SYNC\u201d',text:'Twelve people are invited to a sync on the launch. Half the hour goes on updates. Then someone raises the real question, which date to launch, and the room discovers that two of the people who must decide are absent and nobody prepared the options.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Deciding, generating, aligning and informing are different activities with different sizes and formats. Most bad meetings are two of them fighting for one hour. Name the type before you book it.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'THE JOB DECIDES THE ROOM',left:{title:'If the job is\u2026',items:['Deciding','Generating','Aligning','Informing']},right:{title:'It needs\u2026',items:['The deciders, with options prepared','Few enough people that all can speak','Everyone whose work must fit together','Usually no meeting at all']}},{kind:'explain',title:'Why mixing fails',text:'Each type wants a different room. A decision tried with twelve people has an audience where it needs deciders. A generating session held in front of an audience goes quiet. An informing meeting that someone hoped would end in a decision has neither the right people nor the options.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Several tools crammed onto one hook, with an empty hook beside them. One hour, asked to do every job.'},{kind:'try',text:'Put the type in the first words of your next invitation, such as *Decision:* or *Update:*. Then check the guest list against it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A colleague wants to get everyone together to finalise the budget, and invites fifteen people. What does naming the type reveal?',options:['It is deciding, so it needs the deciders and prepared options, not fifteen people','It is informing, so a fuller agenda will help the hour run smoothly','It is generating, so the more people there are the better the ideas','It is aligning, so it should simply run until everyone agrees'],answer:0,why:'To finalise is to decide. Once that is named, fifteen attendees and no prepared options look wrong before the room is booked.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'READ THE INVITATION',prompt:'An invitation says *Q3 roadmap*, twelve attendees, one hour. What type of meeting is it?',options:['A decision','A generating session','An update','Nobody can tell, including the attendees'],answer:3,reveal:'That is the problem the piece targets. Each attendee arrives expecting a different meeting, and the mismatch surfaces halfway through the hour instead of before it was booked.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Meetings do different jobs, and the job sets the format, the attendance and whether the meeting needs to exist. Most dysfunction is one type being run as another.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The piece\u2019s argument',text:'The claim is structural. Deciding needs the people who decide and options prepared in advance. Generating needs few enough people to speak. Aligning needs everyone whose work must fit together, with time to surface conflicts. Informing usually needs no meeting. Get the type wrong and the attendance is wrong with it.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHY NAMING WORKS',nodes:['The type is named in the invitation','The wrong attendance becomes visible','It is fixed before the room is booked'],note:'Left unnamed, the same mismatch is found during the meeting, when the hour is already spent.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Several tools forced onto one hook. Each would hang properly on its own. Crammed together, none comes off cleanly.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The categories blur in practice: a good generating session often ends in a decision, and aligning can shade into informing. Applied rigidly, types become labels stuck on invitations that change nothing. The value lies less in the exact list than in the habit of asking, before booking, what the hour is for and who that job needs.'},{kind:'recap',points:['The job decides the room: who, how many, what format.','Most bad meetings are two jobs fighting for one hour.','Name the type in the invitation, before anyone accepts.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'WHY IT DID NOT WORK',prompt:'A team now labels every meeting\u2019s type, yet meetings still drift. Which explanation fits the idea best?',options:['The labels are added, but attendance and preparation never change to match','Naming a type has no effect, because people never read invitations','There are really more than four types, so the labels are wrong','Meetings drift by nature, and no change of format can prevent it'],answer:0,why:'Naming works by exposing the wrong attendance and the missing preparation. A label that changes neither is ritual, not the intervention.'}]}},'meetings-that-earn-it_4':{pictureCaption:'Through the doorway, rows of empty chairs nobody needed. In front, a hand delivers the news in an envelope, to be read whenever suits.',bridge:'Idea 3 named four kinds of meeting. This one takes the informing kind and asks whether it needs a room at all.',basics:{title:'News can be written down',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE TRIP DATE',text:'Your whole class sits in the hall for twenty minutes. A teacher reads out the trip date, what to bring and what it costs. By home time, half of you have forgotten. A letter in your bag would have done the job.'},{kind:'idea',text:'If a meeting is just to pass on news, write it down instead. A note is quick to make and quick to read. Nobody has to be in one place at one time.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO SHARE NEWS',left:{title:'All in one room',items:['Everyone must be free at once','You hear it once, then it is gone','It goes at talking speed']},right:{title:'Written down',items:['Read it when you can','Look at it again later','Read it as fast as you like']}},{kind:'picture',kicker:'THE CHAIRS CAN STAY EMPTY',caption:'Nobody needs to sit in those chairs. The news goes in a letter, and each person reads it when they can.'},{kind:'explain',title:'So why do people still meet?',text:'Writing a good note is harder than talking. And no one checks that a note was read. So people call a meeting, because it feels safer.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHAT YOU MIGHT LOSE',text:'But meetings do some small jobs on the side. You chat with friends before it starts. You find out who else is going. If you stop meeting, find another way to do those jobs.'},{kind:'try',text:'Got news for your team or your family? Try writing it in the group chat, not calling everyone round.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your coach wants to tell the team the times for next week\u2019s games. What does this idea say?',options:['Send the times in a message all can check','Call a team meeting so it feels important','Tell the captain and hope it gets round'],answer:0,why:'This is just news. A message is quick, and you can look back at it later.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE USUAL OBJECTION',belief:'People don\u2019t read written updates, so the meeting has to stay.',reality:'Usually true, and a different problem. It is about whether the update is worth reading and whether anyone is accountable for it. Solve that, and the room is not needed to pass the news on.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When information flows one way, write it down and cancel the room. Writing is faster to produce and to read, searchable afterwards, and needs nobody to be free at the same time.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'The status meeting',items:['Everyone free at once','Moves at speaking speed','Gone when the hour ends','Hard across time zones']},right:{title:'The written update',items:['Read when convenient','Moves at reading speed','Searchable later','Open to anyone, anywhere']}},{kind:'picture',kicker:'THE ROOM, NOT NEEDED',caption:'The meeting room stands set out and empty. The update arrives at the door in writing, to be read at reading speed whenever its reader is free.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the meeting survives',text:'Writing well is harder than talking, and reading is not enforced. A meeting is easier to produce and guarantees an audience, so it lasts even when everyone in it would rather have read the thing in five minutes.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The honest exception',text:'Status meetings often do a second job: informal coordination, being seen, the conversations around the edges. Cancel them without replacing that and something real is lost. The answer is to name the second job and serve it on purpose.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Moving an update into writing',steps:['Write the update, and make one person answerable for it.','Make it worth reading: short, specific, what changed.','Name what the meeting was doing on the side.','Serve that side job deliberately, rather than pretending it was not there.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A weekly status meeting is replaced by a written update that everyone reads. A month later, two teams find they have built the same thing. What went wrong?',options:['Its side job, informal coordination, was dropped with nothing in its place','Status cannot be written down, so the meeting has to come back','The updates were too long, so nobody found time to read them','The two teams were not invited to enough other meetings'],answer:0,why:'The news still moved. What vanished was the coordination that happened around the edges, which needed to be named and served on purpose.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'A WORD FIRST',word:'Synchronous',meaning:'Happening for everyone at the same time. A meeting is synchronous: it needs everyone free at once. A written note is asynchronous: each person reads it when they can.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Information that flows one way does not need people in a room together. The meeting version persists mainly because writing is harder and reading is not enforced.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The piece\u2019s argument',text:'The case for writing is a list of plain advantages: quicker to produce, quicker to take in, searchable afterwards, open to people in other time zones. Against them the meeting offers mainly a guaranteed audience. The piece puts their survival down mostly to that guarantee, and to writing being harder.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Answering the objection',text:'People do not read updates. The piece concedes this and reframes it: an unread update was either not worth reading or nobody was answerable for it. A meeting covers that problem by forcing attendance. It does not solve it.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'WHEN A ROOM EARNS IT',low:'One way: news, figures, status',high:'Two ways: questions, reactions, disagreement',note:'The further left a meeting sits, the stronger the case for writing it down instead.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'ONE WAY, IN WRITING',caption:'News that flows one way does not need the rows of chairs behind. The envelope at the door carries it, and nobody has to be free at the same time.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The piece names its own exception: status meetings often carry informal coordination, visibility and the conversations at the edges, and cancelling them carelessly loses that too. Some information is also less one-way than it looks: bad news, or a change people will resist, where the reaction is the point. Writing those down can be a way of avoiding a room that was needed.'},{kind:'recap',points:['One-way information belongs in writing.','Unread updates are a problem of quality and accountability.','Name what the meeting did on the side, and serve it deliberately.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HONEST OBJECTION',prompt:'A team lead says the daily stand-up is not really for status: it is the only time the team talks. What does the idea suggest?',options:['Name that function and serve it directly, perhaps without the status ritual','Cancel it anyway, because its information belongs in writing','Keep it unchanged, since any meeting with a side benefit is justified','Make it longer, so the talking happens alongside the updates'],answer:0,why:'The piece admits meetings can carry a real secondary function. The move is to name it and serve it on purpose, not to deny it or to keep the status ritual as its cover.'}]}},'meetings-that-earn-it_5':{pictureCaption:'The room has emptied. One hand is still writing a single line with a name under it. That line is what the hour bought.',bridge:'Ideas 1 to 4 decide whether a meeting should happen. This one is about how the ones that do happen should end.',basics:{title:'End with who does what',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'AFTER THE PLANNING',text:'You and three friends plan a picnic. Everyone says yes. It sounds great. You all go home. On Saturday, nobody has asked a parent and nobody has brought any food.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A meeting is not done until you say what was decided and who will do it. Write it down before anyone leaves.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it goes wrong',text:'Saying *so, you bring the food* can feel bossy. So people leave on a happy feeling instead. But each one heard a slightly different plan. Nobody was sure it was their job.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Everyone has gone. One hand is still at the table, writing one line and a name. That line is the plan.'},{kind:'steps',title:'The last two minutes',steps:['Say what you decided.','Say who will do it.','Say by when.','Write it where all of you can see it.']},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your group picks a topic for a class project. What should happen before you all leave?',options:['Write the topic, who does each part, and when','Just go, since you all agreed out loud','Wait for the teacher to hand out the jobs'],answer:0,why:'Writing it shows the plan is real. If you can\u2019t write it, you find out now that you did not really agree.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW A GOOD MEETING FAILS',nodes:['The hour ends warmly','Everyone leaves agreed','Each agreed something different','Three weeks later, nothing has moved'],note:'Nobody did anything wrong. The meeting simply stopped instead of concluding.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A meeting that ends without a written decision and a named owner has produced a feeling of progress, not progress. End by typing both while everyone is still there.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why rooms break up on a feeling',text:'Ending is socially awkward. Summarising sounds like asserting authority, and assigning sounds like giving orders. So the room disperses on a general sense of agreement, which later turns out to have been several different agreements.'},{kind:'steps',title:'The closing two minutes',steps:['Restate what was decided, in one sentence.','Name the person who owns it.','Say by when.','Type it into the invitation or the channel before anyone stands up.']},{kind:'picture',caption:'The chairs are empty and one hand is still writing: a line, and a name under it. The only proof the hour bought something.'},{kind:'try',text:'In your next meeting, offer to write the summary. Two lines: the decision, the owner and the date. Read it out before people leave.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You start to write the closing summary and find you can\u2019t: nobody is sure what was decided. What does that tell you?',options:['The meeting reached no decision, and it is cheaper to learn that now','The summary is too formal for this kind of meeting','Someone should write it up from memory later in the week','The decision was clear, but nobody wants to be its owner'],answer:0,why:'A summary that cannot be written is a diagnosis. Finding out in the room costs a few minutes. Finding out three weeks later costs far more.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE OBJECTION',belief:'Closing summaries are bureaucracy. Everyone was there and heard it.',reality:'Everyone was there, and each heard something slightly different. The two-line summary is the only thing that proves the hour bought something, and a test of whether the room really agreed.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A meeting without a stated decision and a named owner has not concluded; it has stopped. The written summary turns a feeling of agreement into an actual one, or shows there was none.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Concluded or stopped',text:'The piece distinguishes a meeting that concludes from one that merely stops. Both end on time and both feel productive. Only one leaves behind something a person can act on. The difference is hard to see in the room, which is why it has to be made visible in writing.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Procedure, not culture',text:'The piece puts the fix in procedure rather than culture. The awkwardness of summarising and assigning is real and will not be argued away. A fixed last two minutes, the same every time, sidesteps it: following the routine is not the same as taking charge.'},{kind:'keyline',text:'If the summary cannot be written, the meeting did not decide.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'An empty room and one hand still writing. Everything the hour produced has to fit on that line.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Not every meeting should end in a decision. A generating session may rightly end with options, and forcing a choice there produces a false one. A summary is only as good as what follows: an owner with no time or authority is a name, not an owner. And whoever writes the summary shapes it, so it should be read back to the room.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HARDER CASE',prompt:'A brainstorming session ends with twenty ideas and no choice. What should its closing summary contain?',options:['What it did decide: which ideas go forward, who takes them, and by when','Nothing, since the rule only applies to meetings called to decide','One idea picked on the spot, so that the meeting counts as concluded','A full record of every idea, so nobody can dispute it later'],answer:0,why:'Even a generating meeting decides something: what happens next and who carries it. Forcing a single choice would fake the very decision the summary is meant to test.'}]}}}},2983,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'melian-dialogue_1':{pictureCaption:'One small marker alone on a vast, bare table. With nothing else on the board, you can see exactly what is being done to it.',basics:{title:'Why tell the story of a tiny island?',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'AT THE PARK',text:'An older kid grabs the ball in a big match. Maybe he just wants to win. Then he grabs it again in a kickabout that does not matter at all. Now you know something. It was never about winning.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'GREECE, 2,400 YEARS AGO',text:'Athens was a strong city in ancient Greece. It had a huge navy and ruled many islands. It was at war with another strong city, Sparta. Melos was a small island that had not joined Athens.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why Melos?',text:'Athens sent ships and told Melos to give in. Melos had nothing Athens badly needed. Thucydides, a writer from Athens, told this small story in full. He wanted you to see what Athens said when there was no prize to point to.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Thucydides chose a place where Athens had nothing big to win. So when Athens spoke, you could see its real reason. It was simply this: we are strong, so we take.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One small marker on a big, empty table. There is nothing else to hide behind. You can see just what is going on.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'AT HOME',text:'Your brother says he took the last biscuit because he was starving. Then he takes the last one again, just after a huge dinner. Now you know. Being hungry was never the reason.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend says she only cheats at cards when the prize is big. How could you test that?',options:['Watch if she cheats when there is no prize','Ask her if she is telling the truth','Only play her when the prize is big'],answer:0,why:'With no prize, her excuse is gone. If she still cheats, the prize was never the reason.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'CHOOSE YOUR CASE',prompt:'A historian wants to show how great powers really reason. Which case shows it most clearly?',options:['A war over a rich port both sides need','A threat to a small island nobody needs'],answer:1,reveal:'At the rich port, every harsh move can be blamed on the prize. At the small island there is no prize, so whatever reason is given has to stand on its own.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Thucydides tells the story of Melos in full because Melos hardly mattered. With nothing strategic at stake, the Athenian argument has no cover, and what is left is a bare claim about power.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'THE AEGEAN, 416 BCE',text:'Athens, at war with Sparta, sent a fleet to Melos, a small island first settled by Spartans that had stayed outside the Athenian empire. Before attacking, the Athenian envoys talked with the island\u2019s leaders. Thucydides wrote the talks as a dialogue, a form he uses nowhere else at such length.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why small stakes help',text:'When something big is at stake, a harsh act can always be explained by the prize. Take the prize away and that explanation goes with it. If the act still happens, the principle behind it is doing the work. Melos is how Thucydides takes the prize away.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A vast empty table and one small marker. Nothing else is on the board, so nothing can hide what is being done.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE \xa33 COFFEE',text:'A manager says a strict expenses rule is about saving money. Watch how it is applied to a \xa33 coffee, where the saving is not worth the argument. If it is still enforced to the letter, something besides cost is doing the work.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick a rule you are told exists for an important reason. Find a case where that reason does not apply, and see whether the rule is still enforced.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A school says its phone ban is there to protect exam results. Which observation best tests that claim?',options:['Whether the ban is enforced as strictly once exams are over','Whether exam results went up after the ban came in','Whether most parents say they support the ban','Whether other local schools run a similar ban'],answer:0,why:'With exams over, the stated reason is absent. If the ban is enforced just as hard, something else is doing the work.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'A CHANGE OF FORM',text:'Thucydides, an Athenian general turned historian, wrote the history of the long war between Athens and Sparta. Most of it is campaigns, and speeches before assemblies. For Melos, a minor episode of 416 BCE, he switches to a dialogue between two delegations and lets it run at length.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Melos works as a controlled case. Because nothing strategic hangs on it, no interest can be cited to soften the Athenian position, and the reader sees the claim about power with nothing in front of it.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'A high-stakes case',items:['A vital port or ally','Harshness can be blamed on the prize','The principle stays hidden']},right:{title:'Melos',items:['A small island with little to gain','No prize to blame','The principle stands alone']},note:'Thucydides gives the reader the second kind of case, so the argument has to show itself.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Even the room was small',text:'The staging repeats the point. The Melian leaders would not let the envoys address the people, so the talks happened among a handful of magistrates. A decision affecting everyone was argued in a room. The Athenians suggest the leaders feared a crowd would be swayed by the case. Thucydides does not comment.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One marker alone on a bare table. Strip a case down like this, and whatever happens next is plainly the principle at work.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Small is not the same as irrelevant. Melos was a Spartan colony in waters Athens claimed, and Athens had already attacked it about ten years earlier. So the case is cleaner than most, not perfectly clean. And a principle seen where nothing is at stake may behave differently where everything is.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which rule in your own life is always justified by what is at stake? Where could you watch it with the stakes removed?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A country says it imposes sanctions only to protect its vital interests. Which case would best test that claim?',options:['How it treats a tiny state that threatens none of its interests','How it treats a rival that threatens its main trade routes','What its own published statement of sanctions policy says','How it treats a large ally that has broken a treaty'],answer:0,why:'Where no vital interest is at stake, the stated reason is absent. Whatever the country does there shows which principle is really at work.'}]}},'melian-dialogue_2':{pictureCaption:'A latch bent off its screws and a large boot print on the threshold. A latch, like fairness, stops only those who choose to stop.',bridge:'Idea 1 cleared the table. This is the claim about power that was left standing on it.',basics:{title:'When \u201cnot fair\u201d is not enough',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE BIGGER KID',prompt:'A much bigger kid takes your ball. You shout, \u201cThat\u2019s not fair!\u201d What usually happens?',options:['He says sorry and gives it back','He shrugs and keeps it'],answer:1,reveal:'Often, nothing changes. *Not fair* only works if the other person cares. Or if someone can make them care.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'WHAT ATHENS SAID',text:'Long ago, the strong city of Athens told the tiny island of Melos to give in. Then Athens said something shocking. It said people only argue about fairness when both sides are equally strong.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Athens said fairness only counts between equals. When one side is much stronger, it does what it is able to do. The weaker side has to put up with it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Was Athens boasting?',text:'Not quite. Athens said it in a tired way, like a fact about the weather. It was not saying this is good. It was saying this is how things go.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'If you are right, being right is enough.',reality:'Sadly, not always. Being right matters. But someone who does not care about fairness will not stop just because you are right. You also need something that makes them stop.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A bent latch and a big boot print. A latch only stops people who choose to stop.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'WHAT HELPS INSTEAD',text:'So the smaller side looks for a way to push back. Friends who stand with you. A teacher who can step in. A rule the bigger kid has to follow. Each of these makes your side stronger.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Some older kids always take the best lunch table. What is most likely to change that?',options:['Ask the lunch staff to set up a fair table rota','Tell the older kids again that it is not fair','Wait for the older kids to decide to share'],answer:0,why:'Saying it is unfair has not worked. A rule with an adult behind it gives your side some strength.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'A COMMON READING',belief:'The Athenians are boasting that might makes right.',reality:'They say something colder. Justice, they say, is a question only between equals in power. Between unequals, the strong take what their power allows and the weak bear what they cannot prevent. They offer it as a description, almost wearily.'},{kind:'idea',text:'An argument from fairness only binds when both sides are strong enough to make the other listen. Without that, the strong act and the weak absorb it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it comes first',text:'The Athenians say this at the start, before either side has argued anything. They refuse to make fine speeches about having earned their empire, since both sides know such speeches persuade nobody. Everything the Melians say afterwards has to fight that opening.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Between equals',items:['Each side can hurt the other','Fairness is worth arguing','A deal has to suit both']},right:{title:'Between unequals',items:['Only one side can hurt the other','Fairness alone changes little','The strong set the terms']},note:'The Athenian claim in two columns. It is grim, and in many rooms it is accurate.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The latch has been bent off its screws, and a large boot print sits on the threshold. A latch, like a plea of fairness, stops only those willing to be stopped.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE SMALL SUPPLIER',text:'A small firm sells most of its output to one giant retailer. The retailer cuts the price it pays. The firm writes a careful letter about fairness and long partnership, and nothing changes. What would change things is a second buyer, suppliers acting together, or a contract the retailer must honour.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before you next argue that something is unfair, ask what the other side loses if they ignore you. If the answer is nothing, work on that first.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A landlord refuses to fix a tenant\u2019s broken heating. Which move follows the lesson?',options:['Find out what the law requires and who can enforce it','Write a longer letter explaining why it is unfair','Wait for the landlord to see it from your side','Accept it, since the stronger side always wins anyway'],answer:0,why:'A legal duty with someone to enforce it gives the tenant leverage. Fairness alone gives the landlord nothing to lose.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'MELOS, 416 BCE',text:'The Athenian envoys open by declining to argue their right to rule. They will not claim their empire was earned by defeating Persia, nor that they come to avenge a wrong. They ask the Melians, in turn, not to plead that they have done Athens no harm. Then comes the claim the dialogue is remembered for.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Justice is a question that arises only between parties equal in power. Between unequal ones, the strong take what their power allows and the weak bear what they cannot prevent. The Athenians present this as how the world runs.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE CLAIM WORKS',nodes:['Fairness needs a listener','A listener needs a reason','The reason is mutual harm','No mutual harm, no hearing'],note:'Read this way, justice is not denied. It is made conditional on a rough balance of power.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The latch is twisted off and a boot print marks the threshold. The latch never had the strength to stop anyone. It held only while the stronger side chose to respect it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the weariness matters',text:'A boast can be answered: a boaster can be shamed. A tired description is harder to answer. By presenting the claim as plain fact rather than pride, the Athenians make every later appeal sound naive rather than brave. The tone is part of the argument.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the weak can do',text:'If the claim holds, the useful question for the weaker side is not whether it is in the right, but how to change the balance: allies, delay, costs it can impose, things the stronger side needs from it. The Melians keep returning instead to justice, the gods and Sparta.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The claim fits a great deal of history, but it is not a law. Weak parties sometimes win on legitimacy, because the strong need allies, subjects and their own citizens to keep believing in them. And treating fairness as pointless fulfils itself: if the weak stop making the case, nobody ever pays for ignoring it. Build leverage; do not drop the argument.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of a dispute where you hold little power. Apart from being right, what could you bring to it?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'Someone says: \u201cSo arguing from justice is pointless.\u201d What is the best reply?',options:['Justice persuades when it has some leverage behind it, so build that too','Justice always wins out in the end, so the Athenians were simply wrong','The weak should accept the terms they are offered and avoid resistance','Justice belongs in law courts and never applies between states'],answer:0,why:'The claim is that fairness alone does not move the stronger side. That is a reason to add leverage, not to abandon the case.'}]}},'melian-dialogue_3':{pictureCaption:'Signalling from a jetty into fog, towards a ship nobody has seen. Hope is all this figure has, and that is the danger.',bridge:'Idea 2 said fairness alone would not move Athens. So the Melians reached for something else: hope.',basics:{title:'Hope is not a plan',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE NIGHT BEFORE THE TEST',text:'You have a spelling test tomorrow. You have not studied. You think, *Maybe it will be easy. Maybe the teacher will be off sick.* You go to bed hoping.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What Melos hoped',text:'Long ago, the small island of Melos faced the huge army of Athens. Melos did not have enough soldiers. So it hoped. It hoped for luck. It hoped the gods would help. It hoped its friends in Sparta would come.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Hope is fine when you have a real plan too. It is dangerous when hope is the only plan you have.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Someone waves out to sea for a ship. But there is no ship in sight. Hope is all they have left.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A LUCKY CHARM',text:'Hope is like a lucky charm in your pocket on race day. It is nice to carry if you have also trained. But it cannot run the race for you.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of something you hope will just work out. Name one real thing that would make it happen. If you can\u2019t name one, make a plan.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your bike has a flat tyre, and the race is on Saturday. Which is hope used well?',options:['Fix the tyre, and hope for good weather too','Hope the tyre will be fine by Saturday','Hope that someone will lend you a bike'],answer:0,why:'Hope is fine on top of a real plan. On its own, it is not a plan.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THREE MONTHS OF CASH',text:'A small business will run out of cash in three months. The plan for month four is a big client who has said they are *interested*. There is no contract and no date. The owner is hopeful, and has stopped looking for anything else.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Hope is affordable when you have a margin to fall back on. When hope is the margin, it is ruinous, and you only see it clearly once it has failed you.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'THE ATHENIAN REPLY',text:'The Melians said they trusted in fortune, in the gods, and in Sparta, which had founded their city. The Athenians answered each in turn. On hope they were coldest: a comfort to those with other resources, they said, but those who stake everything on it learn what it is only when they are ruined.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Hope on top of a plan',items:['A margin exists','Disappointment hurts, but you survive','You keep working']},right:{title:'Hope as the plan',items:['Nothing behind it','Disappointment is final','Work stops while you wait']},note:'The feeling is the same in both columns. The position is not.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Waving a cloth at an empty horizon in fog. It is all this figure has, and nothing out there has answered.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The turn to the invisible',text:'The Athenians add a sharper point. When real means fail, people turn to invisible ones: prophecies, oracles and the like. The worse the position, the more these feel like evidence.'},{kind:'try',text:'Take a plan you are counting on. Mark the part that depends on something turning up. Name what that is, who would do it, and why. If you cannot, it is not yet a plan.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Which of these plans has the Melian problem?',options:['A job hunt whose only lead is a recruiter who might call','A job hunt with five applications out, hoping one goes well','A savings plan with a budget, hoping interest rates rise','A race plan with months of training, hoping for a cool day'],answer:0,why:'In the other plans, hope sits on top of real means. With the lone recruiter, hope is the only thing holding the plan up.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'NOT ABOUT OPTIMISM',belief:'The Athenians are sneering at optimism.',reality:'Their point is about position. Hope does little harm to those with resources to spare. For those who stake everything on it, it is ruinous, and its real nature shows only after the ruin.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Hope is a reasonable comfort when you have a margin, and fatal when it is the margin. The danger rises exactly as the alternatives disappear, which is when hope feels most necessary.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Three appeals, three answers',text:'The Melians appeal to fortune, to the gods and to their Spartan kin. The Athenians answer each. Hope is a luxury of those with means. The gods, as far as anyone can tell, rule wherever they can, just as men do, so Athens expects heaven\u2019s favour too. Sparta acts on its interest, and will not run great risks for colonists across a sea Athens controls.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW HOPE BECOMES THE PLAN',nodes:['Real options shrink','Hope fills the gap','Hope starts to feel like evidence','No new options are sought'],loop:!0,note:'Each turn makes hope more central and the position weaker. That is why the danger peaks when the need is greatest.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A figure signals from a jetty into fog, and no ship is there. Hope has become the whole plan.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The Melian answer',text:'The Melians reply that they will not, in a moment, take away the freedom of a city settled seven hundred years before. It is a fine statement of who they are. It is not an answer to the argument, which is the failure the Athenians predicted.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The argument is cold, and it is convenient for the one making it: an empire telling its victim to give up hope is not a neutral adviser. And hope sometimes pays. Sieges have been relieved and long shots have come in. The point is not that hope is foolish, but that it cannot be counted on, so it should not carry the whole plan.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which is a misuse of the Athenian point about hope?',options:['Telling a well-trained team that hoping to win is foolish','Asking what a plan does if the hoped-for help never comes','Keeping a fallback in place while a funding decision is pending','Naming exactly who would rescue the project, and why they would'],answer:0,why:'The point targets hope used instead of means. Hope on top of real preparation costs nothing, and taking it away helps no one.'}]}},'melian-dialogue_4':{pictureCaption:'An empty square at noon, every door open, one shawl dropped on the stones. Nothing is explained. Like the history, the scene speaks for itself.',bridge:'Idea 3 ended with the Melians trusting hope. This is what followed, and how plainly Thucydides tells it.',basics:{title:'Saying a hard thing plainly',beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'WHAT HAPPENED TO MELOS',text:'Melos said no to Athens. So the Athenian army surrounded the island. After a long wait, the island fell. The Athenians killed the men they caught. They sold the women and children as slaves.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How Thucydides told it',text:'Thucydides wrote this in a few short, plain lines. He did not say how awful it was. He did not tell you what to feel. He just said what happened, and stopped.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When something terrible happens, you do not always need to say how bad it was. Say it plainly. The reader will feel it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'An empty square. Every door is open. A shawl lies dropped on the stones. Nobody tells you what happened, but you can feel it.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'IN A COMIC',text:'Panel one: a boy laughs at a kid who slipped on the ice. Panel two: the same boy slips. No words are needed. Putting two things side by side can say more than a speech.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'SIDE BY SIDE',text:'Right after Melos, Thucydides tells how Athens sent a huge fleet far away to Sicily. That trip ended in disaster for Athens. He never says the two are linked. He just puts them next to each other.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You are writing about the day your town flooded. Which line follows this idea?',options:['The water came up to the windows. School shut for a month.','It was the most awful, terrible, saddest day ever.','Everyone should feel very sorry for our poor town.'],answer:0,why:'Plain facts let the reader feel it for themselves. Telling them how to feel often makes them feel less.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO TELL IT',prompt:'Which account of a hard event usually hits a reader harder?',options:['One full of words like *horrific* and *tragic*','One that states plainly what happened, then stops'],answer:1,reveal:'Strong adjectives do the reader\u2019s feeling for them, and readers push back when told what to feel. Plain facts leave the judgement to the reader, and a judgement you reach yourself lands harder.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'MELOS, WINTER 416 BCE',text:'The Melians refused. Athens besieged the island and took it. The men they captured were put to death, the women and children sold into slavery, and five hundred Athenian settlers were later sent to live there. Thucydides tells it in a few flat sentences.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Thucydides reports the destruction of Melos in the same level voice he used for the arguments, without comment, and lets its placement do the arguing.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the outcome answers',text:'The Melians had trusted in fortune, the gods and Sparta. The outcome answers every appeal without a word of rebuttal. No fortune came, no god intervened, no Spartan ship arrived. The flat report is the counter-argument.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'An empty square at midday, every door open, a shawl dropped on the cobbles. Nobody is shown and nothing is said. It is enough.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE WRITE-UP',text:'You are writing up a project that failed and cost three people their jobs. One version is full of *regrettable* and *deeply unfortunate*. Another lists what was decided, when, and what followed. The second is harder to read, and much harder to dismiss.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you have to report bad news, write it once with no adjectives and no instructions about how to take it. Read it back before you add anything.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A charity appeal describes a famine. Which approach follows Thucydides\u2019 method?',options:['Say what happened to one village, in order, with no adjectives','Open with *heartbreaking* and *unimaginable suffering*','Tell the readers plainly how guilty they ought to feel','Leave out the details so as not to upset anyone'],answer:0,why:'Plain, specific facts let readers reach the feeling themselves. Being told what to feel tends to make them resist.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'timeline',kicker:'WHAT SURROUNDS MELOS',points:[{when:'431 BCE',what:'War between Athens and Sparta begins'},{when:'416 BCE',what:'Athens besieges Melos; the dialogue'},{when:'415 BCE',what:'Athens sends a great fleet to Sicily'},{when:'413 BCE',what:'The Sicilian expedition is destroyed'}],note:'In the history, the fall of Melos is followed directly by the decision to sail for Sicily. Thucydides never links the two aloud.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The massacre is reported in the same register as the arguments, without editorial, and placed between appeals it refutes and an overreach it foreshadows. The arrangement is the comment.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'THE REPORT',text:'The Melians refused. The siege dragged on into winter, Athens sent more troops, and the town surrendered. The Athenians put to death all the grown men they took, sold the women and children as slaves, and later settled five hundred colonists there. That is all he says.'},{kind:'term',word:'Juxtaposition',meaning:'Placing two things side by side without stating the connection, so that the reader makes it. An argument by arrangement rather than by assertion.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Answered by events',text:'The dialogue ends with the Melians trusting fortune, the gods and Spartan honour. The narrative answers each, not by argument but by outcome. Then comes Sicily, where Athens, confident in its power, overreaches and is destroyed. Thucydides states neither lesson.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'An empty square, every door open, one shawl on the stones. The scene carries the weight without a word, as the history does.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Restraint is not neutrality. Choosing what sits next to what is a strong editorial act, and a reader can be steered by arrangement as firmly as by comment. Plain reporting also has a cost: some readers pass over a flat sentence that a stated judgement would have stopped them at. And the link to Sicily is the reader\u2019s inference; he never makes it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE STEER',prompt:'A report lists a company\u2019s record profits and then, in the next paragraph and without comment, its mass layoffs. What is the fairest description?',options:['A judgement made by placement, as real as a stated opinion','Neutral reporting, since no opinion is expressed anywhere','Careless writing that happens to put two facts together','Plain fact, which cannot steer a reader in any direction'],answer:0,why:'Facts set side by side invite the reader to connect them. That is an argument made by arrangement, and it should be read as one.'}]}},'melian-dialogue_5':{pictureCaption:'A victory wreath still hangs on the last wall of a ruined hall. The win was real, and so was what came after it.',bridge:'Idea 4 set Melos just before Sicily. This idea follows that placement to what it does to the dialogue\u2019s most famous reading.',basics:{title:'Showing is not cheering',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'A COMMON MISTAKE',belief:'If a book shows someone winning by being cruel, the book thinks cruelty is good.',reality:'No. A book can show how cruel people think, so that you understand them. Then it can show what happened to them next.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'WHAT HAPPENED TO ATHENS',picture:!0,text:'Athens won the argument at Melos. It took the island. Then it sent a huge fleet to Sicily, and lost it. Some years later, Athens lost the whole war. Its great walls were pulled down.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Thucydides shows clearly how strong Athens talked. He does not say Athens was right. He shows the city that talked that way losing everything later.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A FILM VILLAIN',text:'In a good film, the villain\u2019s plan can sound clever. That does not mean the film wants you to copy him. Watch the end of the film before you decide what it is saying.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'READ TO THE END',text:'Some people say, *The strong do what they like. Even an old Greek book says so.* But they stopped reading too early. In that same book, the strong city falls.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A classmate says a book proves bullying works, because the bully wins in chapter three. What should you ask first?',options:['What happens to the bully later on?','How many chapters does the book have?','Did the bully have good reasons?'],answer:0,why:'One chapter is not the whole book. To know what a book is saying, see how it ends.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE STRATEGY DAY',text:'A speaker puts up a slide with a line from the Athenians. The strong take what they can, it says, and the weak bear what they must. It is offered as hard-headed wisdom. Nobody in the room mentions what happened to the city that said it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The dialogue shows how power talks when it stops pretending. Then it shows the state that talked that way losing everything. It describes realism. It does not recommend it.'},{kind:'term',word:'Political realism',meaning:'The view that states act mainly on power and interest, not law or morals. Analysis, on this view, should start from that fact. The Melian Dialogue is often called its founding text.'},{kind:'timeline',kicker:'WHAT HAPPENED NEXT',points:[{when:'416 BCE',what:'Athens wins the argument and destroys Melos'},{when:'415\u2013413 BCE',what:'The Sicilian expedition sails and is destroyed'},{when:'404 BCE',what:'Athens surrenders and its long walls are pulled down'}],note:'Thucydides\u2019 own account breaks off before the end of the war. But he wrote knowing how it ended.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Description is not endorsement',picture:!0,text:'Thucydides gives the Athenians the stronger argument and lets it work as stated: Melos falls. The next part of his history is the expedition to Sicily. There the same confidence ends in disaster. It is hard to read that order as simple praise.'},{kind:'try',text:'When someone quotes a harsh line from a classic as wisdom, ask what happens next in the same book.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A colleague cites the Melian Dialogue to argue that ethics has no place in negotiation. What is the most accurate response?',options:['The text shows that reasoning winning, then shows its users ruined','The text proves the Athenians were wrong about how power works','The text is about Greek history and has no modern use at all','The text agrees, since the Athenians got exactly what they wanted'],answer:0,why:'The dialogue shows power talking frankly. The history around it shows that frankness did not protect Athens from what came next.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'A FOUNDING TEXT',text:'Courses in international relations often begin with the Melian Dialogue as the founding text of political realism: states pursue power, and talk of justice is a luxury of equals. It is just as often cited as if Thucydides agreed with his Athenians.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The text supports a narrower reading: this is how power talks when it is not performing, and those who talk this way are not thereby protected from consequences. Whether Thucydides meant more, he never says.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'The endorsement reading',items:['The Athenians state the truth','Thucydides agrees with them','Lesson: act on power']},right:{title:'The narrower reading',items:['The Athenians state how power talks','Thucydides shows what follows','Lesson: power is no shelter']},note:'Both readings fit the dialogue. The second fits the history around it better.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The arrangement',picture:!0,text:'He gives the Athenians the better argument and lets it win at Melos. The next part of the history is the Sicilian disaster. He wrote knowing that the war ended in Athenian surrender in 404 BCE, although his own account breaks off some years before that.'},{kind:'term',word:'Composed speeches',meaning:'Thucydides says he wrote his speeches as the speakers would most likely have spoken, keeping to the general sense of what was said. The words are his arrangement, not a transcript.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The narrower reading has its own difficulty. Because the speeches are composed, we cannot separate the Athenians\u2019 reasoning from Thucydides\u2019 arrangement of it, and he never says which reading he intends. Sicily can be read as the price of arrogance, or simply as bad strategy. The ambiguity is probably deliberate, and it has certainly lasted.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of a hard-nosed line you have heard quoted as wisdom. Do you know how things went for the people who first said it?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'A critic says: \u201cAthens lost through bad strategy, not realism.\u201d What is the strongest reply?',options:['Granted; the narrow reading only claims power gave no protection','Wrong; Thucydides says outright that Sicily was punishment for Melos','Wrong; Sicily came before Melos, so the order cannot be a comment','Granted; so the dialogue simply endorses the Athenian position'],answer:0,why:'The narrow reading does not need Sicily to be a punishment. It only needs frank reasoning to have offered no protection, which the history shows.'}]}},'melian-dialogue_6':{pictureCaption:'A chalk line drawn across the table, and the papers pushed beyond it. Those arguments were out of play before anyone spoke.',bridge:'Idea 2 gave the Athenian claim about power. This idea looks at the move that made it stick: setting the rules first.',basics:{title:'Whoever makes the rules',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'BEFORE THE GAME',text:'You and your cousin sit down to play a board game. Before you start, he says, *No take-backs, and the oldest goes first.* You shrug and say okay. He is the oldest.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Whoever sets the rules before a talk often wins the talk. So when someone says what may not be argued, stop and decide if you agree.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'THE FIRST MOVE',text:'Before Athens and Melos argued at all, Athens set the rules. No talk about what is fair. No talk about who was in the right. Only talk about who could do what. Melos said yes. That was the mistake.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A line is drawn across the table. The papers have been pushed to the far side. Those points are out of the game before it starts.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why that lost it',text:'Melos was the weaker side. In a talk only about strength, the weaker side cannot win. Every good point Melos had was about fairness. And fairness had already been ruled out.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time someone starts with *Let\u2019s not talk about\u2026*, stop. Ask yourself if that is a point you need. You can say no to the rule.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Before a class vote, one kid says, \u201cNo one can talk about last year.\u201d Last year his idea went badly. What should you do?',options:['Ask why last year is off limits before you agree','Agree, since rules make things fair for everyone','Say nothing, and just vote the way you want'],answer:0,why:'The rule is not neutral. It hides the one point that hurts his side. Decide about the rule before the talk starts.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE FIRST EMAIL',text:'A buyer opens a negotiation: *Let\u2019s keep this to the numbers and not revisit the history of the account.* It reads as a sensible note about process. If last year\u2019s history is your strongest point, you have just agreed to argue without it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Whoever sets the terms of a discussion has largely decided its outcome. The terms are usually set in an opening exchange. Both sides treat it as procedure, not substance.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'THE ATHENIAN OPENING',text:'Before any position is argued, the Athenians lay down rules. They will not claim a right to their empire. The Melians must not claim they have done no wrong. Both will discuss only what is possible, given that the powerful take what they can. The Melians accept.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE FRAME DECIDES',nodes:['Opening rules feel like procedure','The weaker side accepts them','Its best arguments are now out of bounds','It argues on the stronger side\u2019s ground'],note:'Every later Melian appeal is either ruled out or turned into a forecast about power. And on power, Melos is weaker.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The chalk line went down before anyone spoke. Everything pushed past it is out of play. Whoever drew it chose which papers those were.'},{kind:'try',text:'In your next negotiation, note any opening line that rules something out. Before you reply on substance, decide whether you accept that rule.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A landlord opens a rent review: \u201cLet\u2019s stick to market rates, not the flat\u2019s condition.\u201d What is the best first move?',options:['Decline that limit, since the flat\u2019s condition is your best point','Agree for now, then raise the flat\u2019s condition later on','Research market rates thoroughly before replying at all','Agree, since market rates are the most neutral standard'],answer:0,why:'Accepting the frame first and objecting later is how the Melians lost. Decide on the terms before arguing inside them.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'WHEN WAS IT DECIDED?',prompt:'At which point did the Melians lose the argument?',options:['When their appeal to the gods was dismissed','When they accepted the Athenians\u2019 opening terms','When Sparta failed to send help'],answer:1,reveal:'Every later appeal was made inside terms they had already accepted. Justice was out of bounds, so their best arguments had to become predictions about power, where they were weaker.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The Athenians win by setting the terms: no claims of right, no pleas of innocence, only what each side can do. Once the Melians accept that frame, everything they say afterwards is either inadmissible or weak.'},{kind:'term',word:'Framing',meaning:'Setting the terms of a discussion: what counts as relevant, which standard applies, which questions may be asked. It is usually treated as procedure, though it often settles the substance.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How each appeal was turned',text:'Justice was ruled out by agreement. The gods became a forecast about whose side heaven takes, and the Athenians claimed heaven too. Sparta became a forecast about Spartan interest, which the Athenians read more coldly. Each appeal had to be made as a prediction about power, on ground where Athens was stronger.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A chalk line across a bare table, the papers pushed past it. Nothing has been argued yet, and those papers have already lost.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The Melians did half resist. They protested at the start that the outcome was fixed in advance, and they argued that fair dealing was in Athens\u2019 own long-term interest. Refusing a frame is not free either: the stronger side can simply stop talking. The lesson is to notice the frame and decide, not that a weaker side can always reject the terms.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'When did someone last open by ruling a kind of argument out? Was it the argument you most needed?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A committee chair opens: \u201cWe\u2019re here to discuss cost, not wisdom.\u201d You think the plan is unwise. What does the Melian lesson suggest?',options:['Contest the limit at once, before any cost is discussed','Argue about cost now, and raise wisdom at the vote','Accept it, since a chair\u2019s ruling on scope is final','Show the plan is unwise by showing it costs too much'],answer:0,why:'Once the cost frame is accepted, your objection must be translated into cost, which may be weaker ground. Decide on the frame first.'}]}},'melian-dialogue_7':{pictureCaption:'Every boat moored inside the chain, and one small boat outside it, untouched. Everyone inside can see it, and that is the problem.',bridge:'Justice was ruled out in idea 6. Here the Melians try the modest option left, staying neutral, and learn why it is refused.',basics:{title:'Why staying out can look like a dare',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'IN THE PLAYGROUND',prompt:'A bossy group runs the playground, and everyone does what they say. One kid says, \u201cI\u2019ll just stay out of it.\u201d Why might the group mind?',options:['They don\u2019t mind at all','Others will see he said no and got away with it'],answer:1,reveal:'The group is not scared of one quiet kid. They are scared of what everyone else learns by watching him.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Staying out of a fight can look safe. But a strong side may see it as a dare. Others are watching, and they learn they can say no too.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'WHAT MELOS OFFERED',text:'Melos said to Athens: let us be friends, but not join either side. Athens said no. Athens ruled lots of islands. If tiny Melos could stay free, those islands might think Athens was weak.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'All the boats are inside the chain. One small boat sits outside it, free. Every boat inside can see it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Unfair, but real',text:'Melos was not hurting anyone. It is not fair that staying out made Athens angry. But it helps to know how the strong side thinks, so you are not caught by surprise.'},{kind:'try',text:'When you stay out of something, ask two things. Who is watching? And what will they think it means?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your class splits over which game to play, and you stay out of it. Why could that still cause trouble?',options:['Both sides may read your choice as a message','Staying out is always the safest thing to do','Nobody ever notices the things you don\u2019t do'],answer:0,why:'Not choosing is still seen. People decide what it means, even if you meant nothing by it.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE QUIET ABSTAINER',text:'A director is pushing a reorganisation hard. Most people back it. You stay neutral: no objection, no support. You expect to be left alone. Instead you are the one the director keeps pressing, more than the people who openly disagree.'},{kind:'idea',text:'To a dominant power, neutrality can be more threatening than hostility. Hostility can be explained by strength. Neutrality tolerated shows everyone watching that defiance is free.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'THE OFFER REFUSED',text:'The Melians offered to be friends to Athens and allies of neither side. The Athenians refused. To their subject islands, they said, Melian friendship would be proof of Athenian weakness, while Melian hatred would be proof of Athenian power.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHAT THE SUBJECTS INFER',nodes:['Melos stays free and neutral','Subjects see it go unpunished','Athens can be defied','Every other hold weakens'],note:'The danger is not anything Melos could do. It is what a free Melos would show.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Every boat inside the chain, and one outside it, untouched. The empire\u2019s problem is not that boat. It is everyone watching it.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before you sit something out, work out what your not taking a side will be taken as evidence of, and by whom.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A large firm dominates a trade body. One small firm declines to sign its joint statement, without opposing it. Why might the large firm react strongly?',options:['Other members may conclude that dissent costs nothing','The small firm has become a serious rival in the market','Members of a trade body are legally bound to sign','The statement is invalid without every signature'],answer:0,why:'The refusal matters less in itself than as an example to others. That is the Athenian reasoning about Melos.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE SAFE OPTION',belief:'Neutrality is the safe position for a small state.',reality:'It can be, but not before a power whose rule depends on looking irresistible. To such a power, a neutral that is tolerated is a public demonstration that defiance costs nothing.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The Athenians reject neutrality as worse than enmity. Hostility from Melos would display Athenian power. Friendship from a free Melos would display weakness to subjects who might rebel.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The audience is elsewhere',text:'The argument is hardly about Melos at all. It is about what other islands will infer. An empire pays for being defied in every relationship it holds, so it treats each case as a signal to all the others.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The chain holds every boat but one. That small boat, outside and unchallenged, is a message to all the rest.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Beyond antiquity',text:'The logic is ugly and internally sound, and the pattern outlived Athens. Dominant powers often treat neutrality as more threatening than opposition, because an open enemy confirms the order of things, while an unpunished neutral calls it into question.'},{kind:'limits',text:'This explains the Athenians; it does not justify them. Treating neutrals as rebels can backfire by turning bystanders into enemies, which is exactly the Melian counter-argument. And neutral states have survived for long periods beside great powers, so the pattern is a risk to weigh, not a rule.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Where are you currently declining to take a side? Who is watching, and what might they take it as evidence of?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HARD CASE',prompt:'A small country declares neutrality between two great powers. On the Athenian logic, what makes that most dangerous?',options:['One power rules allies who are watching it for signs of weakness','Both powers are evenly matched and neither rules any allies','The small country trades equally with both of the powers','The small country lies far from either power\u2019s territory'],answer:0,why:'The danger comes from an audience. Where a power\u2019s rule depends on looking irresistible, a tolerated neutral is a signal it cannot afford.'}]}},'melian-dialogue_8':{pictureCaption:'A few figures round a table in a back room, a bolt on the door. A whole island\u2019s fate is being argued in here.',bridge:'Idea 6 showed the Athenians setting the terms of the talks. This idea asks who on Melos was allowed to hear them.',basics:{title:'The people who never heard',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE CLASS TRIP',text:'Your class must choose. Take a cheap trip now, or save for a big one next year that might not happen. Three kids on the class council decide for everyone. Nobody else even hears the choice.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'ON MELOS',text:'When Athens came, the leaders of Melos did not let the Athenians speak to all the people. They met them in a small room, just a few leaders. Melos chose to fight. The whole island paid for it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'On Melos, a few leaders heard the case for everyone. The people who would suffer never heard the argument. Brave or not, that matters.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A few people sit at a table in a back room. There is a bolt on the door. Everyone else is outside.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Was it brave?',text:'Melos chose freedom over giving in. That sounds brave. But the people who paid for it never heard the case. Is it still brave if other people pay the price?'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Have a few people ever chosen something for your whole group? Did everyone get to hear why?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your team captain wants to give up a match to protest a rule. What should happen first?',options:['The whole team hears why, and gets a say','The captain decides, because she is captain','The coach decides without telling anyone'],answer:0,why:'Everyone on the team will live with the result. So everyone should hear the case before the choice is made.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'A STAGE DIRECTION',text:'Early in the dialogue, Thucydides notes almost in passing that the Melians did not bring the Athenian envoys before the people, only before the magistrates and the few. He never raises it again. At the end, he records what happened to everyone.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The Athenian case was heard by a few on behalf of everyone, and those who paid for the refusal never heard it. Thucydides sets the two facts side by side and says nothing.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Who heard it',items:['The magistrates and the few','Heard every Athenian argument','Answered the envoys']},right:{title:'Who paid',items:['The whole population','Heard none of it','Killed or enslaved']},note:'The most damning detail in the piece, delivered as a stage direction.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A few figures round a table in a back room, a bolt on the door. The whole island\u2019s fate is being argued in here.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE BOARDROOM',text:'A board decides to fight a takeover that the staff would have accepted. The fight is long and costly, and in the end many of those staff lose their jobs. The board acted on principle. The staff were never shown the offer.'},{kind:'try',text:'When a decision is being taken on other people\u2019s behalf, ask whether those people have heard the case. If they have not, ask why not.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'An elder refuses a buyer\u2019s offer for the family farm on principle, without telling the rest of the family. Which concern does the Melian detail raise?',options:['Those who will bear the cost never heard the offer','The elder\u2019s principle must have been a mistaken one','Refusals made on principle always turn out badly','The buyer should have gone to someone else instead'],answer:0,why:'The point is not that refusing was wrong. It is that those who would pay were never consulted.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'WHY A SMALL ROOM',text:'The Melian leaders kept the envoys away from their people. The Athenians say they know why: the leaders feared a crowd would be won over by a persuasive case heard without rebuttal. So the talks happened before the magistrates and the few.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The case for refusing Athens, and so risking everyone\u2019s life, was heard by a few on behalf of all. Thucydides records who heard it and what happened to everyone, and leaves the two facts adjacent.'},{kind:'term',word:'The few and the many',meaning:'Greek shorthand for a city\u2019s small ruling group and its whole citizen body. Which of the two held power was a central dividing line in Greek politics.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The restraint',text:'Thucydides records the make-up of the meeting at the start and the outcome at the end. He draws no line between them. The reader has to, and the line raises a question about Melian courage that the text will not answer.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A council in a back room, a bolt on the door. Inside, a few people are hearing the case for an island that cannot hear it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Two open questions',text:'Was it courage, given who bore the cost? And was the Athenian distrust of arguments made in small rooms, in this instance, warranted? Athens was a democracy, and the scene invites a comparison with a hearing kept to a few, without settling it.'},{kind:'limits',text:'We do not know whether the final answer was put to the people, or that they would have chosen differently. A free assembly might have refused too, with the same result. The point is about consent, not a prediction. And leaders sometimes must act without full consultation. The question is whether a decision that risked every life on the island was one of those times.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HARD CASE',prompt:'A government commits a country to a costly war without any vote, and the cause proves just. What does the Melian detail still let you ask?',options:['Whether those who paid had any chance to hear the case','Whether any just cause can ever be worth a war','Whether the war could have been won more quickly','Nothing, since a just outcome settles the question'],answer:0,why:'The Melian question is about consent, not about the cause. A good decision taken for others can still leave them unheard.'}]}},'melian-dialogue_9':{pictureCaption:'This year\u2019s page is full. Next year\u2019s is blank, and that is where the cost of today\u2019s decision will be written.',bridge:'Idea 7 showed Athens fearing what its subjects would infer. Here the Melians turn that same audience into an argument, and lose.',basics:{title:'A warning about later',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE SNACK TAKER',prompt:'A kid keeps taking other kids\u2019 snacks. You warn him, \u201cSoon nobody will be your friend.\u201d Why might he not care?',options:['He thinks you are wrong about it','Soon feels far away, and the snacks are right now'],answer:1,reveal:'The cost you warned him about is real. But it comes later. He is only thinking about today.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'WHAT MELOS SAID',text:'Melos warned Athens. If you crush us, other islands will learn that giving in to you earns nothing. They will fight you harder. Athens did not care. It wanted the islands it already ruled to be scared now.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A warning that something will hurt someone later only works if they care about later. People in a hurry often only care about now.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'This year\u2019s page is full of writing. Next year\u2019s page is blank. That blank page is where the cost will show up.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Was Melos right?',text:'In the end, partly yes. Some years later, Athens lost its war. One reason was that the cities it ruled turned against it when they got the chance.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you warn someone, ask when the cost will hit them. If it is far off, find a reason that matters to them today.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend copies homework every day. Which warning is most likely to work?',options:['The teacher checks it with a test this Friday','One day this will catch up with you','You will be sorry when you are grown up'],answer:0,why:'Friday is soon and real. A cost that feels far away is easy to ignore.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'THE STRONGEST CARD',text:'The Melians played the best card the weak usually hold. Destroy us, they said, and every neutral state learns what submission to Athens is worth. You will make them enemies. The Athenians brushed it aside. They feared their own restless subjects more, and an example served that fear now.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Reputational costs are real but arrive late, so people under pressure, answerable for this month rather than this decade, consistently give them too little weight.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'This year\u2019s page is crowded with entries. Next year\u2019s is blank. The reputation bill is written there, and nobody deciding now has to read it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Right about the cost, wrong about the clock',text:'The Melian argument was sound. Within a generation Athens had lost the war, and its habit of treating allies as subjects fed the defections that made defeat possible. The Athenians did not refute the argument. They were simply measuring on a shorter clock.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'What the warning assumes',items:['The listener is judged on the long run','Later costs feel real now']},right:{title:'What is often true',items:['The listener is judged this quarter','Later costs fall on someone else']},note:'The gap between the columns is why sound warnings fail.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE QUARTERLY BONUS',text:'You warn a sales director that pushing customers into unsuitable contracts will damage the brand in two years. Their bonus is paid on this quarter. The warning is correct and irrelevant to them. Tie it to something counted now, such as cancellations, and it may land.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before you warn someone that an action will cost them later, find out what period they are judged on.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You want to stop a manager cutting safety training to hit this year\u2019s budget. Which argument follows the lesson?',options:['Show that the cut will raise this year\u2019s insurance costs','Explain that the cut may harm the firm\u2019s name in time','Argue that cutting safety training is simply wrong','Point out that a future manager will blame them for it'],answer:0,why:'A cost inside the period they are judged on gets weighed. A cost after it usually does not.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'A WORD FIRST',word:'Discounting the future',meaning:'Giving a cost or benefit less weight the further away it lies. Some discounting is rational, because the future is uncertain. Too much, and distant costs barely register.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The Melians argued that destroying them would cost Athens its standing with every other state. The argument was not wrong, only wrong about the timescale the Athenians were managing.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'THE EXCHANGE',text:'The Melians warn that crushing them will turn every neutral into an enemy. The Athenians reply that free states on the mainland worry them little. What they fear are islanders, both those outside their rule and subjects already chafing at it. A demonstration of what happens to the defiant serves that fear directly.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A full ledger page, and a blank one for a later year, with a ruler between them. What is decided on the left is paid for on the right.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What happened later',text:'Within a generation Athens had lost the war. After the Sicilian disaster, allies defected when they could, and the habit of treating them as subjects made defection easier to choose. The Melian warning came true, after the Athenians had collected their short-term return.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the cost is underweighted',text:'Reputational costs are spread out, delayed and hard to trace to any one act. Decisions are made under pressure by people answerable for this month. Each feature pushes the same way: the cost is real, but it falls outside the frame in which the decision is judged.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Hindsight makes it easy to call the Athenians short-sighted. Fear does work in the short run, and sometimes the short run is all there is: a power that falls this year has no decade to protect. And tying Melos to the later defections is an inference about many causes, not a single traceable one.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A regulator warns a bank that aggressive lending will cost it trust in five years. Its executives serve two-year terms. Which change would make the warning bite?',options:['Hold back executive pay until the five-year results are in','Make the warning longer and much more strongly worded','Send the same warning to the executives more often','Ask the executives to think about their long-term reputation'],answer:0,why:'The warning fails because the cost falls outside the period anyone is judged on. Moving the judgement to match the cost fixes that.'}]}}}},2984,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'money-conversations_1':{pictureCaption:'Two of nearly everything on these shelves, bought separately and never compared. Nothing here is a disaster. It just quietly cost twice.',basics:{title:'Not talking costs a little, again and again',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'MUM\u2019S BIRTHDAY',text:'It is your mum\u2019s birthday. You saved up and bought her a nice mug. So did your brother. Same shop, same mug. You never asked each other. Now she has two.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Small, and easy to miss',text:'Nothing terrible happened. But money was spent twice, just because nobody wanted to start a chat. That kind of cost is quiet. It does not feel like a problem, so nobody fixes it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Not talking about money rarely causes one big disaster. It causes lots of small waste that nobody notices, again and again, until it adds up.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two of nearly everything, bought by two people who never checked with each other. No disaster. Just paying twice, quietly.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A DRIPPING TAP',text:'A dripping tap never floods the house. One drip is nothing. But it drips all day, every day. By the end of the year, that is a lot of water.'},{kind:'explain',title:'So why talk early?',text:'Talking feels awkward, but only for a few minutes. The waste keeps growing for as long as nobody talks. The longer you wait, the bigger and harder the chat gets.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You and your sister are both saving up for the same game to share. What does this idea say to do?',options:['Talk now, so you only buy it once','Keep quiet and save on your own','Wait to see who buys it first'],answer:0,why:'A short chat now stops you paying twice. Waiting only lets the waste grow.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'TWO HALF-BUILT FUNDS',text:'A couple each put money aside for the next car, separately, for three years. Neither mentioned it. When it finally came up, they had two half-built funds, two sets of paperwork, and an argument about why nobody had said.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The cost of not discussing money is paid continuously, in small amounts, which is what makes it invisible. No event announces it. It simply accumulates.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'The discomfort of talking',items:['Fixed','Paid once, up front','Over in an hour']},right:{title:'The cost of avoiding it',items:['Grows over time','Paid every month','Harder to raise the longer it runs']},note:'That asymmetry is the whole case for having the conversation early.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where the cost hides',text:'At home: duplicated provision, mismatched assumptions about retirement or risk, and resentments that attach to other subjects because the real one is off limits. At work: people underpaid for years compared with colleagues doing the same job, because nobody compared.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A cupboard with two of nearly everything. Nobody chose waste. Nobody compared, either.'},{kind:'try',text:'Spend one hour with the person you share costs with, listing what each of you pays for. Do it before anything has gone wrong.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Two flatmates have never discussed bills, and nothing has gone wrong. Which view fits this idea?',options:['The awkwardness is fixed; the cost of silence keeps growing','Talking now will stop them ever disagreeing about money','Silence costs nothing until something visibly goes wrong','They should wait until a bill gives them a reason to raise it'],answer:0,why:'Nothing has to go wrong for silence to cost something. The discomfort stays the same size; the cost does not.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHAT THE ESSAY IS ABOUT',title:'The cost nobody sees',text:'This short essay asks why talking about money with the people closest to you is harder than almost anything else. It opens on cost. The usual cost of silence, it argues, is not disaster but years of small, avoidable inefficiency: duplicated accounts, unclaimed benefits, two people saving for the same thing separately.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The cost of avoiding money conversations is real, continuous and invisible. Because nothing arrives as an event, nothing prompts the talk, and by the time the cost shows, it has made the talk harder.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE TRAP',nodes:['Nobody starts the conversation','Small costs accumulate unseen','The total grows large','Raising it now feels like blame','Even less reason to start'],loop:!0,note:'The longer the silence runs, the more it costs to break.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The asymmetry',text:'The discomfort of talking is fixed and immediate. The cost of avoiding it compounds. On paper that is an easy comparison. The trouble is that the growing cost never presents itself as a choice, so the comparison is rarely made.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Resentment in disguise',text:'One of the costs is not financial. When the real subject is unavailable, resentment attaches to other subjects: the dishes, the holiday, a tone of voice. The row is about something else because the actual one cannot be had.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Every pair on these shelves was a small, sensible purchase. Together they are the bill for never comparing.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The argument assumes two people of goodwill whose interests largely align. Where one partner uses money to control the other, a full, open accounting may not be safe, and silence can be self-protection. Early can also mean too early: detailed money talk weeks into a relationship may cost more trust than it saves.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'Which situation shows the same mechanism outside the household?',options:['Colleagues who never compare pay, and one is underpaid for years','A company that takes one large, sudden loss in a market crash','A shop that raises its prices once and loses many customers','A person who overspends badly on a single impulse purchase'],answer:0,why:'The mechanism is a small, continuous cost that never arrives as an event. The others are single, visible events.'}]}},'money-conversations_2':{pictureCaption:'The receipt sits in the middle of the table, and neither person is looking at it. The clenched fist is about something else.',bridge:'Idea 1 counted the cost of not talking. When the talk does come, this is why it so often goes wrong: the number is not the subject.',basics:{title:'It\u2019s not really about the money',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE TRAINERS',prompt:'Your sister got new trainers. You didn\u2019t. You shout, \u201cThey cost too much!\u201d What are you really upset about?',options:['The price of the trainers','Feeling it isn\u2019t fair','The colour of the trainers'],answer:1,reveal:'You don\u2019t really care what they cost. You care that she got something and you didn\u2019t. The price was just the easiest thing to shout about.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Fights about money are often not about the money. They are about a feeling underneath, like *that\u2019s not fair* or *nobody asked me.*'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The receipt is right there in the middle. But nobody is looking at it. The fight is about something else.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why fixing the number doesn\u2019t work',text:'Say your mum offers to buy you trainers next month too. Problem solved? Not quite. If the real feeling was *she always comes first,* it is still there. It will come back next time.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'What it sounds like',items:['They cost too much','Why did you buy that?','What a waste of money']},right:{title:'What it might mean',items:['It\u2019s not fair','Nobody asked me','I\u2019m worried we can\u2019t afford it']}},{kind:'try',text:'Next time there\u2019s a fight about money, ask: *what does this mean to you?* Not *how much was it?*'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Two friends argue about sharing the cost of a pizza. One says, \u201cYou always choose!\u201d What is the fight really about?',options:['Who gets to decide','The exact price of the pizza','Which toppings taste best'],answer:0,why:'*You always choose* is not about money. It is about who decides. Sorting out the price would not fix that.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE HOLIDAY, THEN THE SOFA',text:'One of you wants a week abroad. The other wants to save. You argue about the price for an evening, find a cheaper version, agree on it, and a week later have the same argument about a sofa.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Money arguments are rarely about the amount. They are about what the spending says: that someone feels unheard, unsafe, or left out of a decision. Settling the number leaves that untouched.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the number keeps coming back',text:'The amount was a proxy. A dispute about a holiday is often a dispute about who gets to decide. It is held in the only words to hand. Agree the number and the real disagreement survives, intact and unnamed. So it returns, attached to the next purchase.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A receipt in the middle of the table. Neither person is looking at it. Whatever this is about, it is not the receipt.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Asking the better question',steps:['Notice the argument has narrowed to one amount.','Put the number down for a moment.','Ask what the money would mean, not what it would buy.','Listen for security, fairness, freedom or being recognised.','Deal with that, then come back to the number.'],note:'The answers tend to be specific, and easier to address than the surface question.'},{kind:'try',text:'In your next money disagreement, ask *what would this mean to you?* before you dispute a single figure.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A partner objects to a \xa360-a-month gym, accepts a \xa330 one instead, and stays tense for days. What is the likeliest explanation?',options:['The number was never the real disagreement','\xa330 is still more than they wanted to spend','They need more time to adjust to the new cost','They are simply annoyed at losing the argument'],answer:0,why:'Settling the amount and staying tense is the sign of a proxy. Something underneath, such as security or who decides, is still unnamed.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE OBVIOUS READING',belief:'A money argument is a disagreement about an amount, and it settles when the amount is agreed.',reality:'The essay argues the amount is usually a proxy. Underneath are questions of security, fairness, autonomy and whose work is being recognised, which is why such arguments are so hard to resolve on their own terms.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Money is often the only vocabulary available for a disagreement about something else. Ask what the money would mean, not what it would buy, and the real subject becomes discussable.'},{kind:'term',word:'Proxy',meaning:'Something that stands in for something else. Here, a figure argued over in place of the question actually at stake, such as who decides or who feels safe.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'SURFACE AND SUBJECT',left:{title:'The surface',items:['The holiday costs too much','Why did you buy that?','We can\u2019t afford it']},right:{title:'A possible subject',items:['Who gets to decide','A decision made without me','I don\u2019t feel secure']},note:'The essay\u2019s claim is that the right column is more specific, and easier to address, than the left.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why bargaining over the number fails',text:'Negotiating the amount treats the proxy as the thing. Each side can win concessions, both can agree, and the actual disagreement is left intact and unnamed. That is also why the surface question keeps returning: its cause was never touched.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A receipt between two people, and neither is looking at it. The essay\u2019s point in a single object.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Sometimes it really is the number. If the money is not there, no amount of exploring meanings will pay the bill, and treating a real shortfall as a feelings problem is its own avoidance. The question can also be used as a dodge: asking what it means instead of answering what it costs.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these misuses the idea?',options:['Answering \u201chow much do we owe?\u201d by asking what money means to them','Asking what a holiday would mean before arguing over its cost','Noticing that a row about a sofa is really about who decides','Naming fairness as the issue after the same row returns three times'],answer:0,why:'A question about what is owed is about the number itself. Steering it into meanings dodges a real problem, which is the misuse.'}]}},'money-conversations_3':{pictureCaption:'An ordinary month on the wall. One short slot is marked, in a week when nothing is due and nothing is wrong.',bridge:'Ideas 1 and 2 showed why the money talk is costly to skip and easy to derail. This one fixes when it happens.',basics:{title:'Talk at a set time, not when it goes wrong',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'WAITING FOR THE RIGHT MOMENT',belief:'The best time to talk about something tricky is when the moment feels right.',reality:'The right moment hardly ever comes. Money talks that are not planned nearly always start when something has gone wrong. Then everyone is cross before you begin.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Pick a set time to talk about money, like once a month. Then the talk is not about a problem. It is just the usual time.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One small square on the calendar. Nothing is due that week. Nothing is wrong. That is why it is a good time.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'TEN MINUTES ON SUNDAY',text:'Jas and her dad have ten minutes every Sunday after tea. They look at her pocket money, what she is saving for, and what is coming up. It is short and a bit boring. That is the point. Nobody ever has to say, \u201cWe need to talk.\u201d'},{kind:'explain',title:'The hardest part is starting',text:'Starting a tricky chat is the scariest bit. With a set time, nobody has to decide to start it. It just comes round, like PE on a Tuesday.'},{kind:'try',text:'Ask a grown-up at home if you could have a set time each week to talk about your pocket money. Keep it short.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You and your brother share the cost of a game you both play. You only talk about it when one of you is cross. What would help?',options:['Pick a time each month to check how it\u2019s going','Only talk about it when it feels unfair','Never bring it up, so there are no fights'],answer:0,why:'A set time means you talk before anyone is cross. Waiting for a problem means every talk starts with one.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'HOW IT USUALLY STARTS',prompt:'Think of the last few times money came up at home unplanned. What usually set it off?',options:['A calm moment with time to spare','Something had gone wrong','A plan to review things'],answer:1,reveal:'Unplanned money talk is almost always triggered by a problem. So it starts with someone defensive and something already wrong: the worst condition for the subject.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A scheduled money conversation beats waiting for a good moment, because the good moment does not arrive. A talk that only happens when someone is upset will always be a talk about being upset.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Triggered by a problem',items:['Something is already wrong','Someone feels accused','Nobody knows what is coming']},right:{title:'A fixed appointment',items:['Nothing has gone wrong','Nobody is being accused','The agenda is known in advance']},note:'Same people, same money. Only the conditions change.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The step it removes',text:'The hardest part is starting. Someone has to decide to raise it, and that decision is most of what stops the conversation from happening at all. A recurring slot means nobody has to decide.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One short slot in an ordinary month, in a week when nothing is due. It sounds joyless. That is why it works.'},{kind:'try',text:'Put thirty minutes in the calendar, monthly. Agree a short agenda in advance: what came in, what went out, what is coming up.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A couple\u2019s money talks keep turning into rows. Which change does this idea point to?',options:['A short monthly slot, booked whether or not anything is wrong','Waiting until both are calm before raising any money issue','Raising each problem the moment it arises, so it stays small','Leaving money to whichever partner is better with it'],answer:0,why:'Waiting for calm rarely produces it, and raising problems as they arise means every talk opens on a problem. A fixed slot is not triggered by one.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'scale',kicker:'WHEN THE TALK HAPPENS',low:'Only when something has gone wrong',high:'At a fixed time, whatever is happening',note:'The essay puts the working arrangement at the far right: monthly, brief, with an agenda. It sounds joyless, it says, and it works.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Schedule the money conversation. A fixed slot removes the two things that ruin it: a triggering problem, and the decision to start.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The argument',text:'Spontaneous money discussions are almost always triggered by a problem, the essay argues, so they start with someone defensive and something already wrong. A fixed appointment has the opposite properties: nothing has gone wrong, nobody is accused, the agenda is known. And it removes the step of initiating, which is most of what prevents the talk.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE SLOT WORKS',nodes:['The date arrives','Nobody had to raise it','Nothing is wrong yet','Small issues surface while still small'],note:'Set beside idea 1, the slot also catches costs before they have had time to compound.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A grid, a single marked square, an ordinary week. The design is the absence of any reason to have the conversation.'},{kind:'limits',text:'A slot can become a ritual that avoids the real subject: thirty minutes of figures, nothing said. It is no reason to sit on an urgent problem until the date comes round. And it presumes both people come willingly. Booked by one to audit the other, it becomes the interrogation it was meant to prevent.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What would go on the agenda of your first thirty minutes? Which item would you be most tempted to leave off?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HARD CASE',prompt:'A large unexpected bill arrives two weeks before the monthly slot. What does the idea imply?',options:['Deal with the bill now; the slot is for what is not urgent','Hold it for the slot, so it is not raised in a moment of stress','Cancel the slot, since problems will come up on their own anyway','Treat the bill as proof that scheduling the talk does not work'],answer:0,why:'The slot exists because good moments do not arrive, not to delay urgent ones. Its value lies in the talks that happen when nothing is wrong.'}]}},'money-conversations_4':{pictureCaption:'One card laid face up first, without being asked. The other hand is just about to turn its own.',bridge:'Idea 3 gets everyone to the table. This one is about the first thing to say once you are there.',basics:{title:'Go first',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'A PRESENT FOR GRAN',text:'You and your cousin want to buy a present for your gran together. Nobody wants to say how much they have. It goes quiet. Then you say, \u201cI\u2019ve got \xa36 I can put in.\u201d Your cousin smiles. \u201cI\u2019ve got \xa38.\u201d Sorted.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When you talk about money with someone on your side, say your own number first. It makes it easy for them to say theirs.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One card is face up on the table. That person went first. Now the other hand is turning its card over too.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why going first works',text:'If you ask, \u201cHow much have you got?\u201d, it can feel like a test. If you say your own number first, it feels like sharing. And when someone shares, people often share back.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Asking first',items:['Feels like a test','They feel put on the spot','Often goes quiet']},right:{title:'Sharing first',items:['Feels friendly','They feel it is safe','Often they share back']}},{kind:'explain',title:'Only with people on your side',text:'This is for friends and family, when you all want the same thing. It is not for someone trying to sell you something. With them, let them say a number first.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You and two friends want to split the cost of snacks for a film night. Nobody says anything. What helps most?',options:['Say how much you can put in first','Wait for someone else to go first','Ask each friend to say their amount'],answer:0,why:'Saying your number first turns a test into sharing. Then the others can easily join in.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE NEGOTIATOR\u2019S HABIT',belief:'Never name your number first. Let the other side reveal theirs.',reality:'That is a habit from negotiating with opponents. With a partner or family member whose interests largely match yours, the first number is not a position. It is a disclosure, and it makes the exchange possible.'},{kind:'idea',text:'In a personal money conversation, volunteer a real figure first: what you earn, owe or are frightened of. It turns a grilling into a disclosure. And disclosure tends to be returned.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO KINDS OF TABLE',left:{title:'A negotiation',items:['Interests opposed','The first number anchors','Waiting first is sensible']},right:{title:'A household',items:['Interests largely shared','The first number is a disclosure','Waiting first reads as a posture']}},{kind:'explain',title:'Why holding back backfires at home',text:'Waiting for the other person to speak first is a bargaining posture brought into a relationship. And it reads as one. They feel questioned rather than joined. A figure of your own changes the shape of the exchange.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One card laid face up, unasked. The other hand is already reaching to turn its own.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'ONE SENTENCE',text:'Two friends are about to share a flat. Instead of asking what the other can afford, one says: *I can manage about \xa3700 a month, and I\u2019m nervous about my debts.* The question is no longer a test. The other\u2019s figure follows.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before your next money conversation with someone close, choose one real figure you will volunteer, and say it before you ask for theirs.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'WHICH TEMPLATE?',prompt:'This week you discuss pay with a prospective employer, and the household budget with your partner. Where does this idea apply?',options:['With your partner, not with the employer','With the employer, not with your partner','With both, since openness always helps','With neither, since money is private'],answer:0,why:'The essay limits the idea to household and family conversations. A job offer is closer to a negotiation, where a different template applies.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'FROM NEGOTIATION',word:'Anchoring',meaning:'The tendency of a first number to pull the discussion that follows towards it. It is why negotiating advice so often turns on who names a figure first.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Between people whose interests largely align, name your own figure first. The negotiator\u2019s habit of letting the other side go first is the wrong template there.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the rule inverts',text:'In an adversarial negotiation, the essay notes, the first number anchors, and the common advice is to let the other side go first. Between partners the first number is not a position to be exploited. It is a disclosure, and disclosure tends to be reciprocated. Withholding it is a posture, and reads as one.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHAT GOING FIRST DOES',nodes:['You volunteer a real figure','Interrogation becomes disclosure','They feel joined, not examined','They reciprocate'],note:'The essay counts fears as figures too: what you are frightened of is part of the number.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A face-up card on a table usually means a game. Here it is the move that ends the game.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The prior question',text:'The qualification matters as much as the advice. It applies to household and family conversations, not to employment. Knowing which situation you are in comes first, and people often apply the wrong template to both: guarded at home, candid with an employer.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Interests are only largely aligned. Within families there can be real conflicts, over an inheritance or a separation, where a disclosure can be used against you. And disclosure only tends to be returned: someone may take your figure and offer nothing back. Going first is a bet on goodwill, worth making where the goodwill is real.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'WHICH TEMPLATE?',prompt:'In which of these should you most probably not name your figure first?',options:['Agreeing a fee with a new client','Planning a joint budget with a partner','Telling a sibling what you can give a parent','Comparing savings goals with a flatmate'],answer:0,why:'A new client\u2019s interests are partly opposed to yours, so it is closer to a negotiation. The other three are household or family talks, where disclosure opens the conversation.'}]}},'money-conversations_5':{pictureCaption:'A child on the stairs, bills on the hall table, and the grown-ups behind a shut door. This is where money silence is learned.',bridge:'Idea 2 found a feeling under the number. This one goes a layer further down: where feelings about money come from.',basics:{title:'We learn money feelings at home',beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO HOUSES',left:{title:'Ali\u2019s house',items:['Money is never mentioned','Prices are a secret','Ali feels odd asking']},right:{title:'Bea\u2019s house',items:['Money comes up at tea','Bea knows what things cost','Bea asks easily']},note:'Neither child chose this. They picked it up.'},{kind:'idea',text:'How we feel about money is mostly learned at home, when we are small. So when talking about money feels awkward, the feeling may be older than the chat.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The bills sit on the table. The grown-ups talk behind a shut door. The child on the stairs is learning that money is not talked about.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it matters',text:'Say Ali and Bea share money one day. Bea wants to talk it through. Ali goes quiet. Bea thinks Ali is hiding something. But he is not. He just never learned that money talk is normal.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE AN ACCENT',text:'You did not choose your accent. You picked it up from the people around you. It sounds normal to you, and other accents sound odd. Money feelings work a bit like that.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Saving and spending',text:'A child who grew up with little money may feel that saving is a must and spending is scary. A child who grew up with plenty may feel calm about spending. Neither one worked it out. Both just picked it up.'},{kind:'try',text:'Ask a grown-up at home: what was money like in your house when you were young? Then just listen.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend goes quiet whenever you talk about what things cost. What is a kind way to think about it?',options:['Maybe money was never talked about at home','They must be hiding something from you','They are being rude to you on purpose'],answer:0,why:'Feelings about money are often learned at home. Asking kindly helps more than guessing the worst.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'ONE BONUS, TWO VERDICTS',text:'You earn about the same. A bonus arrives. One of you thinks *save it, all of it.* The other thinks *at last, a holiday.* Each finds their own view simply obvious, and the other\u2019s faintly alarming.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Attitudes to money are largely inherited, often formed before they could be examined. That is why two people can hold incompatible views while each finds theirs self-evident.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Absorbed, not reasoned',text:'Someone raised in insecurity may treat saving as non-negotiable and spending as danger. Someone raised in comfort may read the same caution as anxiety. Neither reached that view by argument. Both absorbed it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Nobody tells this child that money is private. The shut door and the bills tucked out of the way say it. Decades later, that view will simply feel obvious.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'If I explain my reasons clearly enough, they will see sense.',reality:'Arguing as though the other person\u2019s position were a choice will not resolve it. You are arguing with something formed in childhood, and evidence does not reach it. Understanding where it came from is where progress starts.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Where to begin',steps:['Ask what money was like when they were growing up.','Answer the same question yourself.','Notice which of your *obvious* views came from there.','Negotiate from there, not from the surface positions.'],note:'The answers are usually specific, and often surprising.'},{kind:'try',text:'Ask the person you share money with what money was like in their home growing up. Then answer it yourself, out loud.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Your partner refuses to touch your savings, even for things you can easily afford. What is the most useful next step?',options:['Ask what money was like in their home growing up','Show them the figures proving you can afford it','Agree a fixed sum to spend and stop discussing it','Accept that they are simply more careful than you'],answer:0,why:'If the caution was absorbed rather than reasoned, figures will not reach it. Learning where it came from turns it into something you can negotiate with.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A STUBBORN ARGUMENT',prompt:'One partner wants to bank a windfall; the other wants to spend part of it. Either is affordable. Why won\u2019t the argument settle?',options:['One of them is being irrational','Each is defending a view absorbed, not chosen','They have not yet seen the right figures'],answer:1,reveal:'The essay\u2019s answer is formation. Views about money are often learned before they could be examined, so each feels self-evident to its holder, and no spreadsheet reaches it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Attitudes to money are largely inherited. Arguing as though the other person chose their position will not resolve it; asking where it came from can turn an apparently irrational view into an intelligible one.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The essay\u2019s argument',text:'The discomfort of money talk often traces to a childhood household where money was never mentioned, or was mentioned only in anger. That matters, because it means the awkwardness is often not about this relationship at all. It arrives already formed.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A household where money is kept behind a shut door. The lesson is learned on the stairs, long before it could be examined, which is why it later feels self-evident.'},{kind:'term',word:'Formation',meaning:'A view or habit shaped by upbringing rather than reached by reasoning. You can argue against a conclusion. A formation is not one, so evidence does not address it.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'ARGUING WITH A FORMATION',left:{title:'Treating it as a choice',items:['Bring better evidence','Expect them to update','Read refusal as stubbornness']},right:{title:'Treating it as a formation',items:['Ask where it came from','Expect a story, not a reason','Read refusal as history']},note:'The essay\u2019s claim is that only the right column leads somewhere an actual negotiation can begin.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Inherited does not mean fixed, or exempt. A view formed in childhood can still be wrong and still need changing; its origin is where the negotiation starts, not where it ends. The claim that neither view is reasoned is also too tidy, since people do revise money habits through experience. And some childhood money stories are painful. Nobody owes them on demand.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Where did you learn to be uncomfortable about money? Could you say it out loud to the person you share money with?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HONEST OBJECTION',prompt:'Someone says: knowing my partner\u2019s caution comes from childhood doesn\u2019t change our budget. How does the idea answer?',options:['It doesn\u2019t settle the budget; it makes negotiating it possible','It shows the cautious partner should simply give way','It means both views are equally right, so nothing changes','It replaces the budget talk with a talk about the past'],answer:0,why:'Knowing the origin turns an apparently irrational position into an intelligible one. That is where the negotiation begins, not a substitute for it.'}]}}}},2985,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'montaigne-essays_1':{pictureCaption:'A lamp held up in a crowded loft. It lights the nearest crates and no further. The stacks go back much deeper than the light.',basics:{title:'What do I really know?',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A FACT YOU ARE SURE OF',prompt:'Your friend says a whale is not a fish. You are sure it is. What is the best next move?',options:['Tell her she is wrong','Ask yourself how you know','Change the subject'],answer:1,reveal:'A whale is not a fish. It breathes air and feeds its babies milk. Being sure did not make you right. The question *how do I know?* would have caught it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Feeling sure is not the same as knowing. So keep asking: what do I know? Ask it again about each new thing.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO ASKED IT',text:'Michel de Montaigne was a writer in France about 450 years ago. He had a medal made with a pair of scales on it. Next to the scales was his motto. It was a question: what do I know?'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A PAIR OF SCALES',text:'Scales that hang level have not picked a side. Put a new fact on one side, and they tip. Montaigne tried to keep his mind like that. It was always ready to tip.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The lamp lights the nearest boxes. The rest stay dark. What you know is the lit part. There is always more in the dark.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it helps when you are wrong',text:'If you hold a fixed view, being wrong feels like losing. So you fight for it. If you hold a question, being wrong is easy. You just change your answer.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you feel very sure, say the best reason the other side might have. Then make up your mind.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Two friends disagree about the fastest way to school. Who is acting like Montaigne?',options:['Sam: I think it is the park way, but let\u2019s time both','Ali: It is the park way. I have always said so','Jo: Nobody can ever know which way is faster'],answer:0,why:'Sam has a view and still tests it. Jo sounds humble, but has made a new rule that ends the question.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'IN THE MEETING',text:'Someone questions the plan you proposed. Before they have finished the sentence, you are lining up the reasons you are right. You have not yet asked whether they are.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Montaigne\u2019s motto was a question, not a claim: what do I know? Treat certainty as the state most worth checking, and keep the question open for each new case.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Holding a position',items:['Must be defended','Being wrong is a defeat','Changing your mind looks weak']},right:{title:'Holding a question',items:['Can simply be revised','Being wrong is new information','Changing your mind is the point']},note:'This is why Montaigne could change his mind halfway through an essay and it reads as honesty, not weakness.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'So he believed that nothing can be known.',reality:'That would be a doctrine too, as settled as the certainty it attacks. Montaigne kept the scales level instead: no final verdict, and the question asked again of each new thing.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A lamp held up in a crowded loft. It reaches the nearest crates and no further. Certainty is forgetting how far back the stacks go.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why he called them essays',text:'The French *essai* means an attempt. Montaigne circles a subject, tries it from one side and then another, and when he contradicts himself he leaves the contradiction in. The form of the book is the motto put into practice.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before you commit to something you feel sure of, state the strongest version of the opposing case, well enough that someone who holds it would accept your summary.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A colleague says she has concluded that nobody can know whether any strategy works, so she never defends one. How does this compare with Montaigne?',options:['It is a settled doctrine, not an open question, so it is not his view','It is exactly his view: real knowledge is impossible','It is his view, applied a little more strictly than he did','It is the opposite: he defended his own views firmly'],answer:0,why:'Montaigne kept asking. Deciding that nothing can be known closes the question as firmly as certainty does.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'A MEDAL, 1576',text:'Michel de Montaigne, a French nobleman who had withdrawn from public office to read and write, had a medal struck. It showed a pair of scales, hanging level, with his motto in French: Que sais-je? What do I know?'},{kind:'idea',text:'Certainty is the most suspicious state a mind can be in. Montaigne\u2019s answer is not a rival doctrine but a standing question, asked again of each new thing, which lets him revise at no cost.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What he was arguing against',text:'Dogmatists claimed to know. Some sceptics claimed to know that nothing can be known. In his long *Apology for Raymond Sebond*, Montaigne notes that the second claim trips over itself, since it asserts a certainty too. A question asserts nothing, so he takes the question as his motto.'},{kind:'term',word:'Essai',meaning:'French for a trial or attempt. Montaigne used it for his chapters: tries at a subject, not verdicts on it. The English word essay, for a piece of writing, comes from his use.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'TWO SETTLED ENDS',low:'The dogmatist: I know',high:'The doctrinaire sceptic: nothing can be known',note:'Both ends are positions to defend. Montaigne declines to stand anywhere on the line. What he holds is a question, put to each case as it comes.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it pays',text:'A position must be defended, so evidence against it arrives as an attack. A question can absorb the evidence and move. That is why he can contradict himself in mid-essay without loss, and why the book reads as honest rather than weak.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Courts and hospitals cannot run on open questions. At some point a person has to act on a best answer and own it, and permanent doubt can become an elegant way of dodging commitment or waving away strong evidence. Montaigne himself kept his religion and his duties while he doubted. The question governs how you hold a view, not whether you act on one.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A scientist relies on a well-tested theory. Which stance best fits Montaigne\u2019s motto?',options:['Act on it, while asking what new evidence would change it','Refuse to rely on it until it is proven beyond all doubt','Treat it as settled, since it has been tested so well','Hold that no theory can ever really be known at all'],answer:0,why:'The motto concerns how a view is held: open to revision. It does not forbid acting on the best answer available.'}]}},'montaigne-essays_2':{pictureCaption:'A bathroom shelf with nothing tidied away: flannel, ointment, pills, one bottle open. The parts of a life most self-portraits leave out.',bridge:'Idea 1 asked what he really knows. This one shows the evidence he used: himself, body included.',basics:{title:'He put the awkward parts in',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'WRITING ABOUT YOU',text:'Imagine writing about yourself for people you will never meet. Would you put in that you get carsick? That you are scared of the dark? Most people would leave those out.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A writer called Montaigne wrote about his own body: his illness, his sleep, his food, his fears. He kept in the parts most people hide. That is why we can trust the rest.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO WAS HE',text:'Michel de Montaigne wrote a big book about himself in France, over 400 years ago. Back then, a gentleman did not write about his body. Montaigne did anyway. He wrote about painful stones in his kidneys, and about what he liked to eat.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Nothing on this shelf is hidden in a cupboard. Montaigne\u2019s book is like that. The plain, awkward things are left out where you can see them.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A SCHOOL PHOTO',text:'A school photo shows you neat and smiling. It is you, but not all of you. A friend who has seen you sick, muddy and grumpy knows you better. Montaigne wanted to be known like that.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the awkward bits matter',text:'If someone tells you only the good parts, you start to wonder what they left out. When they tell you the awkward parts too, you believe the rest. And the hard parts are often where the real story is.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you write about your day, keep in one thing you would rather hide.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Three players write about a match their team lost. Whose report can you trust most?',options:['Mia, who says she was tired and missed easy shots','Leo, who says the ref was unfair all game','Kai, who only lists the goals he scored'],answer:0,why:'Mia kept in the part that does not make her look good. That makes the rest of her report easier to believe.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Writing about your digestion and your illnesses is just oversharing.',reality:'For Montaigne it was method. His subject was a human being examined closely, and he had exactly one specimen with the inside visible. Leaving out the body would have meant leaving out most of the evidence.'},{kind:'idea',text:'An honest account of a person includes the undignified parts. Leave out the body and you describe a character, not a human being, and the reader stops trusting the rest.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'WHAT HE PUT IN',text:'The kidney stones that tormented his later years. His digestion and his sleep. His short stature and his poor memory. His sexual habits. In the sixteenth century, none of this belonged in a book by a gentleman.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The quieter argument',text:'Philosophy is often written as though the author had no body. Montaigne\u2019s point is that such a doctrine describes someone who does not exist, and it fails exactly when the body asserts itself: in pain, in fear, in illness.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The ointment, the pills, the bottle with its cap off, all left out on the shelf. The things a polished account would put away.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'The polished account',items:['Only what went well','No doubt, no discomfort','Hard to believe']},right:{title:'The honest account',items:['Includes what went badly','Names the discomfort','Easier to trust']},note:'The omission is usually where the real difficulty lives.'},{kind:'try',text:'In the next thing you write about your own work, a report or a review, keep one detail you were going to cut for being unflattering.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A project review lists every milestone hit and no problems at all. What would Montaigne make of it?',options:['It describes something other than the work that happened','It is admirably focused on what was achieved','It is fine, because reviews are not about feelings','It needs more detail about each of the successes'],answer:0,why:'Real work involves friction. An account with none in it has left out the part where the difficulty lived.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'A BOOK WITH ONE SUBJECT',text:'In his note to the reader, Montaigne says that he himself is the matter of his book. That was a strange choice in the 1570s. Serious books were about God, history or doctrine, and a nobleman describing his own habits risked looking vain.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Including the undignified is what makes a self-portrait trustworthy. The body is not a confession added for colour. It is most of the evidence about a human being.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it is structural',text:'His subject is a person examined closely, and he has exactly one specimen whose inside he can see. Excluding sleep, food, illness and sex would cut away much of what that specimen does all day. The frankness follows from the method.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'THE STONE',text:'In his forties Montaigne began to suffer from kidney stones, and they troubled him for the rest of his life. He writes about the attacks in detail, and about what living with that pain did to his thinking about pain and death.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Nothing tidied into the cabinet: flannel, ointment, pills, one bottle open. A self-portrait with the bathroom shelf left in.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The argument against philosophy',text:'A doctrine written as though its author had no body describes nobody. It holds while the body is quiet and fails when the body speaks. Montaigne tested ideas against a body that spoke often and painfully, and kept what survived.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Candour can become its own performance: the confession offered to look honest, the unflattering detail chosen because it charms. Pascal, writing within a century, judged the whole project of painting himself to be foolish vanity. And some things are private for good reason. The standard is relevance: include what the account depends on, not everything you could disclose.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which is a *misuse* of Montaigne\u2019s standard?',options:['Adding an embarrassing anecdote to a report purely to seem relatable','Noting in a post-mortem that your own estimate was badly wrong','Mentioning that your illness slowed the project in March','Recording in a diary that you were afraid before the exam'],answer:0,why:'The standard is to include what the account depends on. Disclosure chosen for effect is just another way of posing for the portrait.'}]}},'montaigne-essays_3':{pictureCaption:'One peg, several coats, every one the same size and the same person\u2019s. None of them is the real one; each fits a different day.',bridge:'Look at yourself as closely as Montaigne looked at his body, and the next thing you notice is that you do not hang together.',basics:{title:'Brave on Tuesday, scared on Thursday',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TWO DAYS, ONE YOU',prompt:'On Tuesday you stood up for a friend. On Thursday you stayed quiet when someone was teased. Which one is the real you?',options:['Tuesday','Thursday','Both, and neither'],answer:2,reveal:'Both were you. Neither is the whole of you. On those two days you met two different moments.'},{kind:'idea',text:'People change from one place to the next much more than they admit. That is normal. It is not fake.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO NOTICED',text:'Michel de Montaigne wrote in France over 400 years ago. He watched himself very closely. He saw that he could be bold one day and timid the next. He decided both were true.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One peg, lots of coats. Each one is for a different kind of day. But they all belong to the same person.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'AT HOME AND AT SCHOOL',text:'At home you might be loud and bossy with your little brother. In class you might be shy and never put your hand up. Same you. Different room.'},{kind:'explain',title:'So what can you do?',text:'Look at the room, not just at yourself. When are you brave? Maybe when a good friend is next to you. Then you know a trick. Bring the friend.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of a time you were braver than usual. Who was there? Where were you? That is a clue to how to be brave again.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Kim is chatty at football but silent in class. What would Montaigne say?',options:['Both are Kim. Each place brings out a different side','The silent Kim is the real one, deep down','Kim is faking it at football to fit in'],answer:0,why:'Neither side is fake. Different places bring out different parts of the same person.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE SAME COLLEAGUE',text:'On Monday a colleague shares credit generously in front of the whole team. On Wednesday, in a budget meeting, the same person guards every line as if it were theirs. You wonder which one they really are.'},{kind:'idea',text:'People are far less consistent than they believe. Montaigne treated this as the ordinary condition, not hypocrisy. So ask which version of you shows up in which room, and arrange the rooms.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What he observed',text:'Montaigne found nothing harder than making human actions hang together. They contradict each other so oddly that they seem to come from different people. He did not call this hypocrisy. He called it normal, and he included himself.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE USUAL MISTAKE',nodes:['A situation','One side of you shows up','You act','You call it your nature'],note:'The last step is the error. The colleague did not reveal a true nature on either day. They met two situations.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One peg, several coats: for work, for weather, for an evening out. All one person\u2019s, all one size. Each fits a different room.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why this matters for change',text:'Most self-improvement assumes a stable self that is being improved. If you are more like a committee, effort aimed at \u201cwho I am\u201d misses. The lever is the situations: which ones bring out the member you want.'},{kind:'try',text:'Name one trait you claim for yourself. List three times this month you acted otherwise. What did those situations have in common? That is the lever.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You are patient with clients and short-tempered with your family. Which response fits Montaigne?',options:['Notice what differs between the two settings, and change the setting','Decide which of the two is your true character','Accept that, at heart, you are an impatient person','Treat the patience at work as a mask you put on'],answer:0,why:'Neither side is the real you. The useful question is what each setting does to you, because settings can be rearranged.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'BOOK TWO, CHAPTER ONE',text:'Montaigne\u2019s essay on the inconsistency of our actions opens with a complaint. Those who study human conduct, he says, find nothing harder than making it hang together, because our actions contradict each other so strangely. He does not exempt himself.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Montaigne doubts there is a fixed self underneath waiting to be found. A person is brave one day and cowardly another, and neither is the real one. Inconsistency is the ordinary human condition.'},{kind:'term',word:'Situationism',meaning:'The view, argued by some psychologists, that the situation a person is in explains more of their behaviour than we usually assume, and fixed character traits explain less.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What research adds',text:'Some modern personality research points the same way: a single situation can explain more of behaviour than we tend to assume. People are often more consistent in how they describe themselves than in how they act. The self-description stays put. The conduct moves with the room.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Several coats on one peg, every one the same size: one person, dressed by circumstance. The peg is the only constant.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE COMMITTEE',text:'Montaigne\u2019s picture suggests you are less a single character than a committee. The practical question shifts from \u201cwhat am I like?\u201d to \u201cwhich member turns up in which room?\u201d Someone who overspends whenever they shop with one friend may do better changing the outing than resolving to be stronger.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Pushed too far, this dissolves responsibility, and \u201cit was the situation\u201d becomes an all-purpose excuse. Psychologists also still argue about it. Traits that look weak in a single moment show up clearly across many: the generally anxious person is anxious more often. Inconsistency is real, and so are tendencies.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Our actions contradict each other more than we admit.','Neither the brave day nor the timid one is the \u201creal\u201d you.','Change the situations, not only the resolve.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'A friend objects that if there is no fixed self, nobody is responsible for anything. What is the best reply in Montaigne\u2019s spirit?',options:['You still choose your rooms, and answer for what you do in them','True: behaviour is set by the situation, so blame makes no sense','There is a fixed self after all, but it is hidden from view','Responsibility applies only to people who behave consistently'],answer:0,why:'The idea moves the lever from willpower to situations. It does not remove the person who pulls the lever.'}]}},'montaigne-essays_4':{pictureCaption:'A whole meal on the plate, the knife and fork clean beside it. It looks like dinner, but nobody has eaten any of it.',bridge:'Idea 1 kept a question open. This one is about opinions that were never questioned at all, only collected.',basics:{title:'Saying it again does not make it yours',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE FOOD',text:'Michel de Montaigne was a French writer from long ago. He said ideas are like food. Food only feeds you once you chew it and your body breaks it down. Food you never take in does you no good.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Knowing what a clever person said is not the same as thinking it through. An idea becomes yours when you can explain it in your own words and use it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The food is all there, and the knife and fork are clean. It looks like a meal. But nobody has eaten a bite.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'IN CLASS',text:'Your friend tells you that the moon makes the tides. You say it in class. The teacher asks you how. You go quiet. You had the words, but not the idea.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why saying it again does not help',text:'Each time you repeat a line, it gets easier to say. That can feel like knowing it. But it is only easier to say. You still might not know why it is true.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Is this idea mine yet?',steps:['Say it in your own words.','Give an example the book did not give.','Say what someone might argue against it.'],note:'If you can do all three, you have chewed it.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You read a great book about space. Which shows the ideas are now yours?',options:['You can explain one to your little sister with a new example','You can say the best line word for word','You have read the same page five times'],answer:0,why:'Explaining it with your own example shows you have chewed it. Saying the words back only shows you stored them.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'BEFORE DINNER',text:'You have just read an excellent argument and you are looking forward to repeating it at dinner. Someone pushes back. You find you can only say the argument again, a little louder.'},{kind:'idea',text:'An opinion you can quote but cannot defend, extend or apply is stored, not held. Repeating it makes it easier to reach for, not better understood.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Montaigne\u2019s complaint',text:'He was scathing about people who could recite what the authorities said but could not say what they themselves thought. They collected opinions the way people collect furniture, and mistook owning them for understanding them.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A plate with the whole meal on it, the cutlery clean. Learning that has not been digested looks like nourishment and provides none.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW REPETITION FOOLS YOU',nodes:['You read a good line','You repeat it','It comes to mind faster','Fast feels like sure'],note:'Availability is easily mistaken for conviction, including by the person doing the repeating.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The digestion test',text:'Can you argue against the view? Can you extend it? Can you apply it to a case its author never saw? If not, it has not become yours yet, however fluently you can say it.'},{kind:'try',text:'After reading something good, write three sentences of your own about it before you quote anyone.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A manager quotes a famous business book in every meeting but cannot say when its advice would fail. By Montaigne\u2019s test, what is going on?',options:['The view is stored, not held: it has not been digested','The view is well understood, since it is quoted so often','The book must be wrong, or he would be able to defend it','Quoting experts is always a sign of weak thinking'],answer:0,why:'Frequent repetition makes a view available, not understood. The missing piece is being able to test it.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'If you can recite the best authorities on a subject, you know the subject.',reality:'Montaigne complains that we can say what Cicero said about a thing and cannot say what we ourselves think. Memory of other people\u2019s judgements is not a judgement of your own.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Borrowed learning stays borrowed until it is digested: argued with, extended, applied to cases its author never saw. Repetition only makes it easier to produce, which is easily mistaken for conviction.'},{kind:'explain',title:'His two images',text:'In his essays on pedantry and on the education of children, Montaigne compares undigested learning to food that sits in the stomach unchanged. Good learning is like the work of bees, which take from many flowers but make honey that is entirely their own.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A meal served whole, knife and fork untouched. Montaigne\u2019s image for learning that is carried about but never digested.'},{kind:'term',word:'The illusory truth effect',meaning:'A well-replicated finding in psychology: statements people have heard before are judged more likely to be true, partly because familiar things are easier to process. Ease is felt as truth.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Stored',items:['Can be recited','Collapses when challenged','Fits only the original case']},right:{title:'Held',items:['Can be argued against','Survives the objection, or changes','Extends to new cases']},note:'The honest check: state the strongest objection to a view you hold. If you cannot, the work on it has not been done.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Nobody can digest everything. Most of what anyone knows is taken on trust from people better placed to know, and refusing to rely on experts until you have checked it all yourself is its own mistake. The test is for opinions you voice as your own, not for every fact you rely on.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which is a *misuse* of Montaigne\u2019s point?',options:['Ignoring a doctor\u2019s advice because you have not reasoned it out yourself','Writing your own summary of an argument before quoting it','Trying to state the strongest objection to a view you hold','Testing a borrowed principle on a case its author never considered'],answer:0,why:'He targets opinions worn as your own without thought. Relying on real expertise you cannot check is a different matter.'}]}},'montaigne-essays_5':{pictureCaption:'A calm horse on the road, grass pressed flat where a rider lay, a glove left behind. Montaigne\u2019s fall was gentler than his fear.',bridge:'Idea 4 asked you to test what you have read. Here Montaigne\u2019s reading on death meets something that actually happened to him.',basics:{title:'The fear, and what really happened',beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'A FALL FROM A HORSE',text:'Michel de Montaigne lived in France over 400 years ago. For years he was very scared of dying. Then one day he was thrown hard from his horse. He was knocked out and carried home.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'He thought he was dying. How do you think it felt to him?',options:['Terrible and full of fear','Strangely soft and calm'],answer:1,reveal:'He wrote that it felt calm, like drifting off to sleep. It was far gentler than his fear had been.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The grass is flat where the rider fell. The horse stands calm. Montaigne said the fall felt calm too.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Fear of something you have never been through is often bigger than the thing. For Montaigne, what really happened taught him more than all his clever thinking.'},{kind:'explain',title:'He kept both',text:'Montaigne had read wise books about not being afraid. They helped a bit. The fall helped more. He did not pretend the fear was gone for good. He wrote all of it down.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A JAB AT THE DOCTOR',text:'Before your first jab, you might picture something awful. Then it is a quick pinch. Your mind had made it bigger. Next time, you know what it is really like.'},{kind:'try',text:'Scared of something new, like a first sleepover or a big test? Ask someone who has done it what it was really like.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You are scared of your first swim in the deep end. What would Montaigne suggest?',options:['Ask a friend who has done it what it was like','Read about it until you stop being scared','Tell yourself you should not be scared at all'],answer:0,why:'For Montaigne, what really happened helped more than thinking about it. A friend who has done it can tell you what it is like.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'A COUNTRY ROAD',picture:!0,text:'Montaigne was out riding near home when a servant on a powerful horse crashed into him at full gallop. He was thrown, knocked out and carried home, believing himself to be dying. Later he reported that the experience had been gentle, close to falling asleep.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Montaigne argued that to philosophise is to learn to die, and admitted his own fear was severe. The argument helped. Coming close to death helped more. He presents neither as a cure.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO THINGS THAT HELPED',left:{title:'The argument',items:['We die a little every day','Afterwards is like before birth','Before birth troubled nobody']},right:{title:'The experience',items:['Thrown from his horse','Believed he was dying','Found it gentle']},note:'He kept both in the book, and the second did more.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Making death ordinary',text:'He did not try to make death noble. He tried to make it ordinary. A day ending is a small version of it, and the state afterwards is the state before birth, which bothered nobody. Familiar things frighten less.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the honesty is useful',text:'A doctrine that says the fear should not be there leaves you with the fear and a sense of failure as well. Montaigne\u2019s account allows the fear, shows what reduced it, and admits it was not solved. That is something you can use.'},{kind:'try',text:'For something you fear and have never experienced, find someone who has been through it and ask what it was actually like.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You dread an operation you have never had. Which approach fits Montaigne?',options:['Ask people who have had it what it was actually like','Tell yourself firmly the fear is irrational and should go','Avoid thinking about it at all until the day comes','Read about everything that could go wrong, to be ready'],answer:0,why:'For Montaigne, contact with the real thing did more than argument. Someone who has been through it is the nearest you can get.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'AN OLD SAYING',text:'One of Montaigne\u2019s early essays takes its title from an old idea he credits to Cicero: to philosophise is to learn how to die. In it he admits his fear was severe, and sets out to tame it by keeping death constantly in mind.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The fear of death is eased less by proving it should not be felt than by making death ordinary and, where possible, by contact with it. Montaigne keeps the argument and the experience side by side.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The argument',text:'He makes death familiar rather than noble. We die a little continuously, and the end of each day is the same kind of thing. Being dead is like not yet being born, a state that troubled nobody. Fear feeds on strangeness, so he removes the strangeness.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'THE ACCIDENT',picture:!0,text:'In another essay he describes being thrown when a servant rode into him at speed. Carried home unconscious and thought to be dying, he recalled no terror, only a soft drifting, like sliding into sleep. It did more for his fear than the reasoning had.'},{kind:'term',word:'Exposure',meaning:'In modern treatment of fears, gradual contact with the feared thing, which for many fears does more than reasoning about it alone. What changed Montaigne was closer to exposure than to argument.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why he kept both',text:'He tended to add to his essays rather than rewrite them, so earlier and later thoughts stand side by side on the page. He did not cut the anxious reasoning once experience had done more. The visible revision is part of the method.'},{kind:'limits',text:'One near-death experience is a single case, and not everyone\u2019s is gentle; it would be wrong to promise his calm to anyone else. Making death ordinary helps with the fear of being dead, much less with grief or with fear of a painful dying. He offers relief, not a solution, and says so.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What do you fear that you have never experienced? Who could tell you what it is actually like?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'Someone afraid of flying has read every safety statistic and is still afraid. What does Montaigne\u2019s case suggest?',options:['Contact with the feared thing may help where reasons have not','They simply have not yet found the right argument','The fear shows that the statistics must be wrong','A fear that survives good reasons cannot be eased'],answer:0,why:'His reasoning helped a little; the experience helped more. Fear often answers to contact rather than proof.'}]}},'montaigne-essays_6':{pictureCaption:'Two chairs at a table, one cushion worn through by years of sitting. The wear shows the friendship happened. It does not show why.',bridge:'Idea 5 found experience did more than argument. Here argument fails outright: reasons cannot explain his closest friendship.',basics:{title:'Why is your best friend your best friend?',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'TRY TO EXPLAIN IT',text:'Someone asks why your best friend is your best friend. You say you like the same games. But lots of kids like the same games. You try again. The reasons never quite add up.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'TWO FRIENDS',text:'Michel de Montaigne lived in France over 400 years ago. His closest friend was a writer called \xc9tienne de La Bo\xe9tie. They were friends for only a few years, and then \xc9tienne died young. Montaigne missed him for many years after.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Some friendships can\u2019t be explained by a list of reasons. Montaigne tried to explain his best friendship and gave up. In the end he said it was just because of who they both were.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two chairs at a table. One cushion is worn right through from years of sitting. You can see the friendship. You can\u2019t see why.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why keeping score goes wrong',text:'You can count who texts first, or who said sorry last. Counting will give you an answer. But it is an answer about the counting, not about your friend.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of a friend. Don\u2019t count anything. Ask: what do I still like about them, even when I stop keeping score?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend has been busy and has not called much. What fits Montaigne\u2019s idea?',options:['Ask what you still like about your friend','Count the calls to see who is the better friend','Make a list of reasons to stay friends'],answer:0,why:'Keeping score only tells you about the score. Montaigne looked at what was left when the counting stopped.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'YOUR OLDEST FRIEND',prompt:'Try to say why your oldest friend is your oldest friend, without describing where you met or what you did together. What happens?',options:['The reasons come easily','The reasons run out fast','The reasons turn back into circumstances'],answer:2,reveal:'For most people the list either slides back into circumstances or stops. Montaigne hit the same wall, and decided the wall was the finding.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Montaigne could not explain his friendship with La Bo\xe9tie by reasons, and said so. With people, what survives when you stop counting is what matters.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'MONTAIGNE AND LA BO\xc9TIE',text:'\xc9tienne de La Bo\xe9tie was a fellow magistrate in Bordeaux and a writer. He and Montaigne were close for only about four years, until La Bo\xe9tie died in 1563. In his essay on friendship Montaigne lists the shared reading, the temperament, the timing, and then abandons the list.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The giving-up is the argument',text:'He is not being poetic. He is reporting that the reasons he produced did not add up to the friendship, and refusing to pretend otherwise. The title of this lesson is how he put it.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'The ledger',items:['Who calls more','Who showed up','Gives a clear verdict','About the ledger']},right:{title:'What survives it',items:['What remains when you stop counting','May have no name','About the friendship']},note:'Reasons can be arranged to support whatever verdict you already wanted.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two chairs, one cushion worn through by years of use. The wear is evidence that something was there. It is not an explanation of it.'},{kind:'try',text:'With a friendship that has drifted, stop scoring it. Ask what remains when you refuse to count, and decide on that.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A friendship has drifted and you are deciding whether to invest in it. Which step fits Montaigne?',options:['Set aside who did more, and ask what remains','List who made the effort over the past year','Write out pros and cons and follow the totals','Let it go, since you cannot say why it matters'],answer:0,why:'The ledger answers a question about the ledger. What remains when you stop counting tells you about the friendship.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'A LINE ADDED BY HAND',text:'Montaigne\u2019s essay on friendship spends a page explaining why he and \xc9tienne de La Bo\xe9tie were so close. Long after La Bo\xe9tie\u2019s death in 1563, writing in the margin of his own copy of the book, he added the short answer this lesson is named after.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Some bonds outrun their reasons. Montaigne\u2019s failure to explain his friendship is the argument: the reasons were true but did not add up to the thing, and he declined to pretend they did.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What he was arguing against',text:'Classical writers, Aristotle and Cicero above all, analysed friendship by its grounds: usefulness, pleasure, shared virtue. Montaigne knew that tradition well. His point is that even the highest ground did not account for this friendship. The analysis ran out before the thing did.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two chairs, one cushion worn through. Years of evidence that the friendship was real, and not one clue as to why.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Using it on a friendship now',text:'Reasons are always available on both sides, and can be arranged to support any verdict you already want. The ledger, who calls and who turns up, gives an answer about the ledger. The test is what survives when the ledger is set aside.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'WITH THE LEDGER SET ASIDE',low:'Nothing remains',high:'Something remains that you cannot name',note:'Both ends teach you something. The second is what his sentence exists to describe, and it is not a failure of the friendship.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Bonds that cannot be explained can still be bad ones. The sense that something is beyond words is also what keeps people in friendships that harm them, and a plain record of who keeps hurting whom is sometimes exactly what is needed. The test tells you what remains. It does not tell you that what remains is good.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which is a *misuse* of Montaigne\u2019s idea?',options:['Staying close to a friend who keeps lying because it \u201cjust works\u201d','Keeping a friend you value even though you cannot list why','Ignoring who phoned last when deciding about a drifting friendship','Admitting you cannot fully explain why a friendship matters'],answer:0,why:'He describes what reasons cannot capture. That is not permission to ignore reasons that show real harm.'}]}},'montaigne-essays_7':{pictureCaption:'Far from home, in a busy market, he turns a magnifying glass on his own street: the same close look, aimed at home.',bridge:'Idea 1 turned doubt on his own certainty. This one turns it on a whole country\u2019s disgust.',basics:{title:'Is it wrong, or just strange?',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A NEW LUNCH',prompt:'A new kid at school eats food you have never seen. Some kids say it is gross. Is it?',options:['Yes, it is gross','No, it is just new to them'],answer:1,reveal:'The food harms nobody. It is just not what those kids are used to. The word *gross* really meant *not ours*.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When something from far away seems awful, ask: would I use the same words about something like it at home? Often the bad feeling is about *strange*, not about *cruel*.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'He stands in a busy market far from home. But his magnifying glass shows his own street. Look at home just as closely.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'FRANCE, 450 YEARS AGO',text:'Michel de Montaigne heard about people in Brazil who ate enemies they had killed in war. His readers in France were horrified. Montaigne agreed it was wrong. Then he pointed at France itself.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What he pointed at',text:'At that time, people in France were fighting over religion. They tortured people who were still alive. Montaigne asked a hard question. Which is worse: to eat someone already dead, or to hurt someone still living?'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'SAME WORDS, BOTH SIDES?',left:{title:'Far away',items:['They eat enemies killed in war','Called savage in France']},right:{title:'At home',items:['People tortured over religion','Done in the name of God']},note:'Montaigne thought the word *savage* fitted the right side at least as well.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time something seems weird or wrong, find the closest thing at home. Would you use the same words about it?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your cousin says people who eat with their hands are rude. What would Montaigne ask?',options:['Does it hurt anyone, or is it just not how we do it?','Who taught them such bad manners?','Why don\u2019t they just use forks like us?'],answer:0,why:'Eating with your hands hurts no one. Montaigne would want to know if *rude* means harmful, or just new to us.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'A NEWS STORY',text:'You read about a country where thieves are publicly flogged, and you think: barbaric. Now write the same sentence about something at home, say a prison system that holds people alone in a cell for years. Does the word come as easily?'},{kind:'idea',text:'Before calling a foreign practice barbaric, try the same words on the nearest equivalent at home. If the sentence will not survive the trip, it was marking the practice as theirs, not measuring its cruelty.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'THE ESSAY ON CANNIBALS',picture:!0,text:'Montaigne\u2019s essay on the cannibals of Brazil describes a practice that horrified his readers: eating enemies killed in war. Then he lists what was being done in France at that very time, torture and slaughter in the wars of religion, and asks which is worse.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Not a defence',text:'He is not defending cannibalism. He thinks some things really are worse than others. His question is whether his readers\u2019 disgust was tracking cruelty or only unfamiliarity. Those two can point in different directions.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE TEST',nodes:['A foreign custom shocks you','You form a sentence: barbaric','You aim it at home','Does it survive?'],note:'If it does not, the word was measuring distance from you, not harm.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'So Montaigne thought every culture\u2019s customs are equally fine.',reality:'That is relativism, and it is not his conclusion. He thinks some practices are genuinely worse. His point is that you cannot find out which while your instrument is set to detect strangeness rather than harm.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time a foreign practice strikes you as barbaric, write the same sentence about the nearest thing at home.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A friend calls eating dogs, common in some countries, cruel and disgusting, while happily eating pork. What does Montaigne\u2019s test ask?',options:['Would the same words fit how pigs are raised and killed here?','Is the practice legal in the countries where it happens?','Should every culture\u2019s customs simply be left unjudged?','Is a strong gut reaction the best guide here?'],answer:0,why:'The test is to aim the same sentence at home. It may survive, or it may turn out to have been about strangeness.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'NEWS FROM BRAZIL',text:'Montaigne had in his household a man who had lived for years in the French colony in Brazil, and says he once spoke with Brazilian visitors brought to France. His essay on cannibals describes their customs with curiosity, including eating enemies killed in war, then turns the description round on France.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The word barbarian tracks unfamiliarity more reliably than cruelty. To test a judgement of a foreign practice, apply the same words to the nearest equivalent at home and see whether they survive.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The reversal',picture:!0,text:'He grants the horror of eating the dead. Then he judges it less barbarous than what was being done to the living in France: torture, and burning by degrees, under the pretext of piety, in the religious wars of his own day.'},{kind:'term',word:'Calibration',meaning:'Whether an instrument reads true. A scale that adds a kilo to everything foreign is badly calibrated. Montaigne suspects his readers\u2019 disgust is set to strangeness, so it cannot measure harm.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'What the sentence claims',items:['This practice causes harm','Harm is what offends me']},right:{title:'What it may be doing',items:['Marking the practice as theirs','Strangeness is what offends me']},note:'The home test separates the two. Only the left column is a moral judgement.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The test can be misused both ways. It can slide into the relativism he rejected, where nothing may be judged, or into whataboutism: pointing at home to silence criticism abroad. And his own picture of the Brazilians is idealised in places, a portrait of noble simplicity that says as much about his complaints against France as about Brazil.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which foreign custom strikes you as most obviously wrong? What is its nearest equivalent where you live?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which is a *misuse* of Montaigne\u2019s test?',options:['Answering any report of abuse abroad with \u201cwe do bad things too\u201d','Asking whether a practice at home deserves the word you used abroad','Concluding after the test that a foreign practice really is worse','Revising a judgement once you see it was about unfamiliarity'],answer:0,why:'The test improves judgement. Used to shut down criticism, it becomes the relativism Montaigne rejected.'}]}},'montaigne-essays_8':{pictureCaption:'A busy counter out front, and behind it a door left ajar onto a small room with one chair and one lamp: the back shop.',bridge:'Idea 3 found a different self in every room. This one keeps one room that no role or audience reaches.',basics:{title:'A room that is just yours',beats:[{kind:'picture',kicker:'BEHIND THE COUNTER',caption:'The front of the shop is busy and full. Now look at the door behind it. There is a quiet room with one chair and a lamp.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Keep one part of your life just for you. Not to show anyone. Not to be good at. One French writer called it a back shop.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO SAID THIS',text:'Michel de Montaigne lived in France over 400 years ago. He had a busy public life. For a time he was mayor of a big city, Bordeaux. But he said that each of us needs a back room no one else sees.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'FRONT OR BACK?',text:'You love to draw. If you post each drawing and count the likes, drawing is in the front of the shop. If you draw in a notebook no one sees, just because you like it, that is your back shop.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it matters',text:'Once you show a thing, you start to shape it for others. Then you are always putting on a show. The back room is the one place you don\u2019t have to. And if you ever lose your team or your part, it is still there.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one thing you enjoy. Keep it to yourself for a month. Notice if it feels different.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Which one is a back shop?',options:['Building a model at home that no one will see','Practising a dance to post online','Reading a book so you can tell the class'],answer:0,why:'A back shop is for you alone. The other two are done with someone watching in mind.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'ask',kicker:'IF IT ALL WENT DARK',prompt:'Suppose every account you have went dark tomorrow, and nobody could see what you do. What part of your life would carry on unchanged?'},{kind:'idea',text:'Keep a back shop: a part of life held wholly apart from role, reputation and audience. It is a retreat kept within a life, not a replacement for one. It is what lets you survive public life.'},{kind:'term',word:'Arri\xe8re-boutique',meaning:'French for the back shop: the room behind the shop floor, not for sale and not on display. Montaigne\u2019s image for the part of a life kept wholly your own.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The test',text:'Would it survive nobody knowing about it? A hobby you post about is in the front of the house. So is an interest you mention because it makes you sound interesting. Once a thing is shown, the showing starts to shape it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The counter is cluttered and busy. Through the door behind it: one chair, one lamp, and nobody to perform for.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why a busy man needed it',text:'Montaigne held public office: he served two terms as mayor of Bordeaux. His worry was that a person made wholly of public roles has nowhere to stand when a role is taken away. And that happens without warning, in his century and ours.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one thing you enjoy. On purpose, never mention it to anyone for a month.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Which of these is a back shop in Montaigne\u2019s sense?',options:['Running alone at dawn with no app tracking it','A side project you post progress updates about','A book club you joined to meet useful people','A skill you practise for your next promotion'],answer:0,why:'Each of the others is shaped by an audience or a role. The back shop survives nobody knowing about it.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',word:'Back shop',meaning:'Montaigne\u2019s arri\xe8re-boutique: a room behind the shop, kept entirely free and entirely one\u2019s own, where a person can be in their own company and hold their real liberty.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Everyone needs a part of life held apart from role, reputation and audience. He calls it his principal retreat, but it is a room kept inside a life, not a replacement for one: the place to stand when the world takes a role away.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where it sits',text:'The image comes in his essay on solitude. Keep family, possessions and health if you can, he says, but do not tie yourself to them so tightly that losing them takes you with them. The back shop is that reserve.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'BORDEAUX, 1581 TO 1585',text:'Montaigne did not stay in his tower. Elected mayor of Bordeaux while travelling abroad, he served two terms, through civil war and, at the very end, an outbreak of plague. The man who argued for a back shop spent years at the front counter.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A cluttered counter, and a door ajar onto one chair and one lamp. Not a replacement for the shop, but the thing that keeps it bearable.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW SHOWING CHANGES A THING',nodes:['You do it for itself','You start to show it','The showing shapes it','It becomes another role'],note:'This is not an argument against sharing. It is an observation about what sharing does.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The back shop can harden into withdrawal: duties neglected for the private room, or a self kept so apart that nobody close is let in. Montaigne\u2019s advice assumes someone who still has a household and an office to return to. And a room of one\u2019s own takes some time, space and freedom, which not everyone has.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A surgeon retires and finds she has no idea who she is without the job. What would Montaigne say went wrong?',options:['She had no back shop: every part of her was a role','She retired too early and should have kept working','She was too attached to private hobbies to adjust','She shared too little of her private life with others'],answer:0,why:'His worry was exactly this: a self made only of roles has nowhere to stand when a role is taken away.'}]}},'montaigne-essays_9':{pictureCaption:'A fencer ready on the floor, and nobody opposite. The second blade leans against the wall, unused. You cannot train against agreement.',bridge:'Idea 1 kept your own question open, and idea 4 tested your opinions alone. This one asks other people to do the testing.',basics:{title:'Why it helps when people disagree',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE DEN',text:'You show your friends your plan for a den. Everyone says it looks great. You build it. On the first windy day, the roof blows off.'},{kind:'idea',text:'People who agree with you teach you very little. People who push back make you think again. So it helps to be told you are wrong.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'A STRANGE WISH',text:'Michel de Montaigne lived in France over 400 years ago. He said he was glad when people argued with him. His complaint was that so few did it well. Most just nodded.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A fencer is ready to practise, but no one is there to fence. The second sword leans on the wall. You need someone who pushes back.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A GOALKEEPER',text:'Taking shots at an empty goal feels great. Every shot goes in. But you learn nothing. A keeper who tries to stop you makes you better.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How to get real pushback',text:'Asking \u201cis it good?\u201d mostly gets you nods. Instead, ask a friend to find what is wrong with it. Now they are allowed to disagree. That is when you hear the useful stuff.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You wrote a story for a contest. Which question will help it most?',options:['What is the weakest part of this?','Do you like it?','Is it good enough to win?'],answer:0,why:'Asking for the weak part lets your reader push back. Asking if they like it mostly gets you a nod.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'COUNT THEM',prompt:'How many people this year have told you, to your face, that you were wrong about something that mattered?',options:['Several','One or two','None I can remember'],answer:2,reveal:'For most people the honest count is low. That is not because they have been right. Disagreeing costs something, and most people decline to pay.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Contradiction wakes the mind up; agreement lets it sleep. Montaigne welcomed being contradicted, and his complaint was that almost nobody did it properly.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why agreement teaches nothing',text:'Agreement lets you keep the position you arrived with, unexamined, because nothing required you to look at it. The point is about attention, not humility: a challenge forces you to look again.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A fencer on guard, facing an empty floor. The other blade rests by the wall. Most of us practise like this, with no one opposite.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO ASK',left:{title:'Asking for comments',items:['\u201cAny thoughts?\u201d','Small edits to what exists','The plan goes untested']},right:{title:'Asking for the opposite case',items:['\u201cMake the best case against this\u201d','The real objections surface','The plan is tested, or changed']},note:'The second removes the cost of disagreeing, which is what was suppressing it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'His standard',text:'Montaigne wanted people who would press, not people who would soften. By that standard, a lot of modern review is a ritual in which both parties agree the document exists, and nothing in it is tested.'},{kind:'try',text:'When circulating a plan, ask one person to make the strongest case against it, rather than asking for comments.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You want honest pushback on a proposal before a big decision. Which request is most likely to get it?',options:['\u201cI expect to drop this draft. Argue the opposite case.\u201d','\u201cAny thoughts at all before I send this on?\u201d','\u201cLet me know if anything jumps out at you.\u201d','\u201cI think this is strong, but happy to hear views.\u201d'],answer:0,why:'Calling the draft disposable and asking for the opposite case removes the cost of disagreeing. The others invite small edits.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Welcoming criticism is a matter of humility.',reality:'Montaigne\u2019s reason is about attention. In his essay on the art of conversation he says contradiction rouses and exercises him, while agreement lets him settle. The gain is a mind kept awake, not a modest manner.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Agreement leaves a position unexamined; contradiction forces you to look at it. Real contradiction is rare because disagreeing is costly, so it has to be asked for in a way that removes the cost.'},{kind:'explain',title:'His complaint',text:'Montaigne does not complain that people disagree too much. He complains that they do it badly: they soften, defer, or argue to win rather than to find out. He wanted partners who would press hard and fairly, and found few.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A fencer on guard against an empty floor, the second blade unused by the wall. Without an opponent, nothing in the stance is ever tested.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Getting contradiction on purpose',steps:['Say the plan is a draft you expect to abandon.','Name one person to argue the opposite case.','Ask for the strongest objection, not for comments.','Answer it on its merits, or change the plan.']},{kind:'keyline',text:'If nobody has told you that you are wrong lately, the likeliest reason is cost, not correctness.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Contradiction helps only when it is aimed at the truth. A devil\u2019s advocate arguing by rote produces theatre, and a culture of constant challenge can drown quiet people and reward whoever argues loudest. And an objection is a reason to look again, not proof that you were wrong.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which is a *misuse* of Montaigne\u2019s idea?',options:['Treating any objection as proof that the plan is wrong','Asking a sceptical colleague to argue against your plan','Calling a draft disposable so people feel free to criticise','Rechecking a view you hold after a sharp challenge'],answer:0,why:'Contradiction is valuable because it makes you look again. Looking again can confirm the plan as well as sink it.'}]}}}},2986,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'my-life-and-work_1':{pictureCaption:'Chalked footprints show where a worker used to walk between benches. The rail now runs along that path and carries the work instead.',basics:{title:'Bring the job to the worker',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'PACKING PARTY BAGS',prompt:'You are packing 30 party bags. Sweets, balloons and stickers sit on three tables round the room. Where does most of your time go?',options:['Putting things in the bags','Walking from table to table'],answer:1,reveal:'Dropping a sweet in a bag takes a second. Walking to the next table takes ten. The walking eats the time. But it feels like work, so you never count it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Don\u2019t walk the job around. Keep the worker still and bring the job to them. Most lost time hides in the walking between jobs.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO WORKED THIS OUT',text:'Henry Ford made cars over 100 years ago. His workers used to walk to fetch parts all day long. So he built a moving line. The car slid past, and each worker stayed in one spot.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The chalk marks show where a worker used to walk, back and forth. Now the rail runs that way and carries the work instead.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A LUNCH LINE',text:'At lunch, you slide your tray along. One cook adds pasta. The next adds peas. The cooks stay put. The tray does the moving, so nobody has to walk.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What changed',text:'Most of the time saved was the walking. Putting one car together fell from more than twelve hours to about an hour and a half.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you do a job with lots of bits, like making sandwiches, put every bit within reach first. Then see how much faster it goes.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your class is making 100 cards for a fair. Which plan uses Ford\u2019s idea?',options:['Sit in a row and pass each card along','Each child fetches glue and glitter for every card','Everyone tries to work twice as fast'],answer:0,why:'Passing the cards along means nobody walks to fetch things. Working faster does not cut the walking at all.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE STOCKROOM',text:'A warehouse picker fills orders all shift. Each order means a walk to the far shelves and back. She is busy every minute, and nobody would call her slow. Time her, though, and most of the shift turns out to be walking.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Bring the work to the worker, not the worker to the work. In a repeated process the time is usually lost in the moving between tasks, and the loss is invisible because it looks like work.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why nobody counted it',text:'A person walking to fetch a part is plainly busy. Managers watched the doing: how fast a bolt went in, how well a wheel was fitted. The walking sat between tasks, belonged to none of them, and so appeared in no one\u2019s figures.'},{kind:'equation',parts:['Time doing','Time moving between'],result:'A shift',note:'Ford attacked the second part. That is where most of the time was.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'HIGHLAND PARK, 1913',text:'Ford\u2019s answer was the moving assembly line. The chassis travelled past workers who stayed at their stations, each doing one operation. Assembly time for a Model T fell from over twelve hours to about ninety minutes.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Chalked footprints mark where a worker once walked between benches. The rail now runs along that path, and the walking has stopped.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one job you repeat every week. Time it once, splitting the doing from the fetching, searching and walking. Then move the materials, not yourself.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A clinic\u2019s nurses are run off their feet but see few patients. What would Ford check first?',options:['How much of a shift goes on walking to fetch supplies','Whether the nurses could work faster with each patient','Whether more nurses should be hired for each shift','Whether patients are being booked into the wrong slots'],answer:0,why:'Busy is not the same as productive. Ford would measure the moving between before touching the doing.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE COMMON STORY',belief:'Ford\u2019s breakthrough was splitting work into small, specialised tasks.',reality:'The division of labour was old: Adam Smith described it in a pin factory in 1776. Ford\u2019s insight was narrower. Moving the object past stationary workers removed the walking, and the walking was most of the time.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The gain came from eliminating motion nobody had been counting. Bring the work to the man, and the time between operations collapses.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'WHERE THE LINE CAME FROM',text:'Ford credits the idea partly to the overhead trolleys Chicago meat-packers used to move carcasses past butchers. Applied at Highland Park in 1913, it made the chassis travel while the worker stayed. The book telling this, written with the journalist Samuel Crowther, appeared in 1922.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE CHAIN',nodes:['Chassis moves, worker stays','No fetching, no walking','Gaps between operations collapse','Twelve hours becomes ninety minutes'],note:'Each step follows from the one before. The saving came from the gaps, not from faster hands.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The general rule',text:'In any repeated process, measure the moving between rather than the doing. Fetching, waiting, searching and hand-offs rarely appear in anyone\u2019s job description, so they escape scrutiny. Later operations thinking treats them as waste for exactly that reason.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Ford presents the gain and rarely the cost. The change that removed the walking also reduced a job to one repeated motion, performed at a pace set by the line. Both halves are true: an enormous productivity gain, and a narrower job. Ford rarely acknowledges this as a trade, and the book never weighs it.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'In your own work, what is the walking: the fetching, waiting or hand-off that looks like work and appears in nobody\u2019s figures?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A software team ships slowly. Each feature passes through four people and waits days in each queue. What is the Ford-style diagnosis?',options:['The time is in the waiting between people, not the coding','The engineers need to write their code a good deal faster','The team needs more specialists, each with a narrower task','The features are too large and should simply be cut'],answer:0,why:'Like walking between benches, queue time looks like part of the work and appears in nobody\u2019s estimate. It is usually where the time goes.'}]}},'my-life-and-work_2':{pictureCaption:'Every slot holds a worn card, and the blank cards for new starters sit unused. The pay envelope is what kept the rack full.',bridge:'Idea 1 made the line fast. This one is about the people who kept leaving it, and what Ford paid to keep them.',basics:{title:'Paying more to spend less',beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'DETROIT, 1914',text:'Henry Ford made cars over 100 years ago. In 1914 he did something that shocked people. He roughly doubled what his factory workers were paid, to five dollars a day.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'Why do you think he did it?',options:['To be kind to his workers','To save his company money','Both of those'],answer:1,reveal:'Ford\u2019s book says plainly it was not charity. He called it one of his best ways to cut costs, and his own sums show it paid for itself.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When people keep leaving a job, training new ones costs a lot. Paying more so people stay can cost less than paying less.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why leaving costs so much',text:'Each new worker has to be taught. While they learn, they are slow and make mistakes. Then, just as they get good, they quit. So you start again. That is the hidden bill.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE CHAIN',nodes:['Pay is low','People quit fast','New people need teaching','Work is slow, and mistakes cost money'],note:'Ford broke the chain at the very first step.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Workers clock in with these cards. Every slot holds an old, worn card, so people stayed. The new cards sit unused. The pay packet did that.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE CAR WASH',text:'You and your friends run a car wash. You pay your helpers very little, so a new one quits every week. Each time, you spend the morning showing them how. Pay a bit more, and one helper stays all summer. You get your mornings back.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A shop keeps losing staff. Every month the owner trains someone new. What would Ford check first?',options:['Whether better pay would make people stay','Whether the new staff are lazy','Whether to hire even more people'],answer:0,why:'Training new people over and over costs money. Paying more might cost less than that.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE USUAL READING',belief:'The five dollar day was Ford being generous, a cost he chose to bear.',reality:'His own book says plainly it was not charity, and counts it among the company\u2019s best cost-cutting moves. Its turnover and hiring figures show it paid for itself in reduced retraining. It was an operations decision.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Raising pay can be the cheapest option, when the money saved on constant hiring and retraining is larger than the money added to wages.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The cost that hides',text:'Turnover does not appear as one line in the accounts. It is spread across the floor: recruiting, training, the slow first weeks, the spoiled work, the experienced colleague who stops to show a newcomer. Each looks small. Together they are a real cost.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'THE RACK STAYS FULL',caption:'Every slot holds a worn, long-used card, while the blank cards for new starters sit untouched. The envelope going in is the cheaper side of the sum.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Read as fairness',items:['A gift to the workers','A cost the firm absorbs','Admired, rarely copied']},right:{title:'Read as operations',items:['A fix for turnover','Pays for itself in retraining','A sum anyone can run']},note:'Both readings are true. The second is the one that transfers.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'A CAF\xc9, TODAY',text:'A caf\xe9 pays the minimum and loses a barista most months. Each new one takes weeks to reach full speed, and the manager spends hours training. Add those hours, the slow weeks and the wasted stock, then compare them with a pay rise. The rise does not always win, but it is often closer than it looks.'},{kind:'try',text:'If people keep leaving somewhere you have a say, cost one departure: hiring time, training hours, weeks at half speed. Then compare it with the cost of paying more.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A warehouse manager says staff keep leaving because morale is low, and proposes team-building days. What would Ford ask first?',options:['What each departure costs, against the cost of higher pay','Whether the team-building days could be held more often','Whether new hires should be screened harder for loyalty','Whether the same work could be done by fewer people'],answer:0,why:'A retention problem framed as a morale problem may be a pay problem. Do the arithmetic before buying a fix for a feeling.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'THE KEY WORD',word:'Labour turnover',meaning:'The rate at which workers leave and have to be replaced. A firm that hires several times its whole workforce in a year has turnover of several hundred per cent.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The five dollar day is a cost that looks like generosity and functions as efficiency. Ford says plainly it was not charity, and his own figures show it paid for itself.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The problem behind it',text:'The wage was announced in January 1914, soon after the moving line came in. Turnover had become severe: the company was hiring several times its workforce in a year, and every newcomer had to be trained to the line\u2019s pace.'},{kind:'flow',nodes:['The line makes work repetitive','Workers leave in large numbers','Constant hiring and retraining','Pay doubled, turnover collapses'],note:'The book gives the turnover figures and the fix. It never connects them to the first step.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the book says',text:'Ford presents the wage as sharing profit, and says plainly it was not charity: he counts it among the company\u2019s best cost-cutting moves. What transfers is that: a retention problem described as a morale problem is often a pay problem.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A full rack of worn time cards and an untouched stack of blank ones: low turnover, made visible. The envelope slipped into a slot is the wage that bought it.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The five dollars was not a plain wage. Much of it was paid as a profit share, and qualifying depended on conduct the company checked, including home visits by its Sociological Department. The rise also treated a symptom: the work stayed as repetitive as before. And Ford\u2019s volumes made it affordable; the same rise could sink a thinner business.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Where have you seen a cost that looked like generosity but worked as efficiency, or the reverse?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISREADING',prompt:'Which conclusion does the five dollar day actually support?',options:['Check whether higher pay costs less than constant replacement','Raise pay, and any retention problem will disappear','Generosity to staff always pays for itself in the end','Turnover is a morale problem, best fixed by culture'],answer:0,why:'The lesson is a calculation to run, not a promise. Ford\u2019s case worked on his numbers; yours may come out differently.'}]}},'my-life-and-work_3':{pictureCaption:'A board of matching spanners, one held against the master. The master is the baseline every part, and every change, is measured against.',bridge:'Idea 1 cut out wasted motion. This one is how you know whether a change is an improvement at all.',basics:{title:'Do it one way, then make it better',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE BAKE SALE',text:'Three friends bake cookies for a sale. Each one does it their own way. One batch comes out great. Nobody is sure why. Was it the oven? The butter? The time?'},{kind:'idea',text:'Pick one way to do a job and stick to it. Then change one thing at a time. Now you can see what made it better.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A FAIR TEST',text:'In science you change just one thing in a test. If you change three things at once, you can\u2019t tell which one mattered. Doing any job you repeat works the same way.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Every spanner is checked against one master spanner. Without the master, there is no way to tell which ones are right.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO SAID THIS',text:'Henry Ford made cars over 100 years ago. He fixed one way to do each job in his factory. But he said that fixed way was only a start. The next step was to make it better.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Getting better at a thing you repeat',steps:['Choose one way to do it.','Do it that way a few times.','Change one small thing.','Check: is it better or worse?','Keep the better way as your new one.']},{kind:'question',prompt:'You want to make the best paper plane in your class. Which plan follows this idea?',options:['Make one design, then change one thing each time','Make a totally new design every time','Copy the plane that flew best last week'],answer:0,why:'Changing one thing at a time shows you what made the plane fly further. A new design each time can\u2019t tell you that.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THREE WAYS, ONE JOB',prompt:'Three people pack orders, each their own way, all competently. This month errors fall. Whose method caused it?',options:['The fastest packer\u2019s','The most careful packer\u2019s','There is no way to know'],answer:2,reveal:'Three methods were running at once, against no fixed starting point. Any of them, or none, could explain it. The month taught you nothing you can repeat.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Fix one way of doing a repeated task, run it long enough to learn from, then improve it. Without a standard there is no baseline, and without a baseline change is just churn.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why variation blocks learning',text:'If a process is done differently every time, each result has many possible causes, and you cannot tell which difference mattered. A standard holds everything still except the one thing you change, so the result tells you something.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Standardising means freezing a method so nobody may change it.',reality:'Ford is explicit that the standard is a starting point, not a conclusion. His objection is to changing a method before it has been understood, because then the change cannot be judged.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A board of matching spanners, one held against the master. The master is the baseline every part, and every change, is measured against.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'No standard',items:['Everyone\u2019s own way','Results vary, nobody knows why','Changes are guesses']},right:{title:'A standard',items:['One written way','Variation shows up at once','Changes can be tested']}},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one task you do often. Write down exactly how you do it now. Do it that way for a week before changing anything, then change one thing.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A new manager wants to improve onboarding, which every trainer runs differently. What comes first?',options:['Agree one onboarding process and run it as written','Try several promising new ideas all at once','Let each trainer keep refining their own version','Survey new hires about what they disliked most'],answer:0,why:'Until there is one process, there is nothing to measure an improvement against. The survey becomes far more useful once there is a baseline.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE ARGUMENT',text:'Ford\u2019s hostility to variety was extreme: one model, one chassis, as few options as he could manage. Under it sits a method that has outlived him. Fix a standard, run it long enough to learn from, then improve it. He is explicit that standardisation is where improvement begins.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Variation is the enemy of learning. A process performed differently each time tells you nothing about which difference mattered, so fix the standard first and improve against it.'},{kind:'term',word:'Baseline',meaning:'A fixed, measured starting point that later results are compared with. Without one, a change in results cannot be credited to anything.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE CYCLE',nodes:['Fix one way','Run it and measure','Change one thing','Keep what works as the new standard'],loop:!0,note:'The loop only turns if the last step happens. Ford was strong on the first step and weaker on the last.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where it went next',text:'The method fed into what became industrial quality control, and Ford\u2019s approach was studied closely in Japan a few decades later. There the second half of the formula, improve the standard, was taken more seriously than it was at Ford, where the standard tended to harden.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One part held against the master. The template is not the goal; it is what makes the next improvement measurable.'},{kind:'limits',text:'A standard can become the goal instead of the starting point, and Ford\u2019s own refusal to change the Model T shows how. Standards imposed without the people who do the work tend to miss what those people know. And where variation is the point, as in design or research, a fixed method can stop discovery. The claim is about repeated processes.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these is a misuse of the idea?',options:['Keeping a process unchanged for years because it is the standard','Writing down the current method before trying to improve it','Changing one step of a routine and comparing the results','Asking the people who run a process to propose changes'],answer:0,why:'The standard exists to make improvement measurable. Treating it as finished keeps the first half of Ford\u2019s formula and drops the second.'}]}},'my-life-and-work_4':{pictureCaption:'Bins sorted by material, and one pair of hands lifting a usable piece out of the scrap. Nothing leaves until someone has checked it.',bridge:'Idea 1\u2019s walking was one kind of waste. Here Ford names the rest, and misses one.',basics:{title:'Waste is more than rubbish',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'WHAT COUNTS AS WASTE?',belief:'Waste is just the stuff you throw in the bin.',reality:'Henry Ford, who made cars over 100 years ago, saw more kinds. Lost time. Extra steps. And wasted skill, like asking your best player to just fetch the balls.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Before you throw something away, check if it can still be used. And look for the hidden waste too, like time and steps.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Every bin is sorted. One worker pulls a good piece out of the scrap pile. Even the heap gets a second look.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO KINDS',left:{title:'Waste you can see',items:['Scraps of metal','Old wooden boxes']},right:{title:'Waste you can\u2019t see',items:['Time spent waiting','Extra steps','Skill not being used']}},{kind:'explain',title:'What Ford did',text:'His factory saved metal from scraps. It used the wood from old crates. It made parts with less steel. He said waste was like taking money from the buyer, who paid for it in the price.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'IN ART CLASS',text:'Your class cuts big circles out of card. The corners go in the bin. But those corners could be stars for the next project. Saving them costs nothing, and means less card to buy.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your class throws away half-used paper at the end of each lesson. What would Ford do first?',options:['Check how much is thrown out, and if it can be used','Buy cheaper paper so the waste costs less','Ask everyone to use less paper and hope'],answer:0,why:'Ford measured what was thrown away before deciding it was no use. Often it still was.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'SCRAP, CRATES AND STEEL',text:'Ford\u2019s examples are relentless. Metal recovered from scrap. Parts redesigned to use less steel. Usable wood salvaged from shipping crates. Every movement on the line timed. This was decades before anyone framed waste as an environmental problem.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Treat waste as a failing, not a fact of life, and define it broadly: material, motion, time, and skilled people doing unskilled work.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why he took it personally',text:'Ford presents waste as theft from the customer. Every scrap, wasted minute and needless step ends up in the price, so somebody who never saw the factory pays for it. That turns salvage from thrift into something closer to a duty.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Bins sorted by material, and one pair of hands lifting a usable piece out of the scrap. Nothing leaves until someone has checked it.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'HOW FAR TO GO',low:'Scrap is scrap',high:'Every scrap is examined',note:'Ford sat at the far end. The habit worth borrowing is nearer the middle: measure what you throw away before deciding it is worthless.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one routine: a weekly shop, a report, a workshop. List what it throws away, time included. Measure the biggest item before deciding recovery isn\u2019t worth it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A restaurant bins a quarter of its bread each night and calls it a cost of doing business. What would Ford say?',options:['Measure it first: that waste is being charged to customers','Bread is cheap, so its waste is not worth attention','Waste is unavoidable in food, so accept it and move on','Buy cheaper bread so that the loss matters less'],answer:0,why:'To Ford, waste is a cost passed on to the buyer. You decide recovery is not worth it after measuring, not before.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHAT HIS ACCOUNTING COUNTS',left:{title:'Counted as waste',items:['Metal in the scrap','Wood in the crates','Steel in the design','A skilled man on unskilled work']},right:{title:'Left out',items:['A person on one motion for forty years','Capability the job never uses']},note:'The right-hand column is the category he cared most about, applied to a different kind of worker.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Ford treats waste as close to a moral failing, defines it broadly, and frames it as theft from the consumer. The framing produced real results and one blind spot.'},{kind:'term',word:'Lean manufacturing',meaning:'A later approach, developed largely from Toyota\u2019s methods, that treats every form of waste as something to remove: excess material, motion, waiting, transport and, in later versions, unused talent.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The direct ancestor',text:'Ford\u2019s categories, material, motion and time, are recognisably lean\u2019s. His reason was economic: the customer pays for every scrap in the price. The environmental framing came decades later, but the practice it describes is the one he was already running.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Bins sorted by material, one hand lifting a usable piece from the heap. Ford\u2019s eye for recovery, turned on everything except the person sorting.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Ford\u2019s own category catches him. He calls a skilled man on unskilled work a waste, then designs jobs that hold people to one motion for decades, and does not count it. Human capability drops out of the ledger once the worker is unskilled. Any waste drive is limited by what it counts, and what it leaves out is usually paid for by someone with no say. The book appeared in 1922; from 1920 to 1922, Ford\u2019s own newspaper, the Dearborn Independent, ran an antisemitic series.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'In a process you know, what is thrown away that nobody measures? Include people\u2019s time and skill.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY HIS OWN TEST',prompt:'Ford counts skilled people on unskilled work as waste. Applied consistently, what would that say about his line?',options:['That a lifetime on one motion wastes capability too','That repetitive work is no waste, since it is efficient','That only material waste is measurable, so only it counts','That the line wasted nothing, because it saved time'],answer:0,why:'His own category, applied to every worker rather than only skilled ones, catches the cost his accounting leaves out.'}]}},'my-life-and-work_5':{pictureCaption:'A perfect line seen from above, and one square where the same hands make the same motion all day, with no way out.',bridge:'Ideas 1, 2 and 4 each brushed against a cost Ford did not count. This one names it.',basics:{title:'Would you do the job you planned?',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE CHORE CHART',text:'You make the chore chart for your family. It looks great on paper. Your little brother gets the bins, every day, all year. You would hate that job. You just didn\u2019t think about it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When you plan how other people will work, ask: would I want to do this job every day? Planners often miss what the job feels like.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'FORD\u2019S MOVING LINE',text:'Henry Ford\u2019s factory made cars fast. Each worker stood in one spot and did one small move, over and over. The line set the speed. Ford was sure this was good for workers. He wrote that most of them did not mind doing the same thing all day.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Everything on this line moves along smoothly. But look at the bright square. One pair of hands does the same small job there, all day.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO VIEWS',left:{title:'From above',items:['Neat','Fast','Every part in place']},right:{title:'From the spot',items:['Same move all day','Can\u2019t stop to rest','Bored stiff']}},{kind:'explain',title:'Did it matter?',text:'Yes. So many workers quit that Ford had to keep hiring new ones. In the end he paid a lot more to make them stay. The dull job had a cost after all.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your team plans a bake sale. One person will stand at the till alone for three hours. What does this idea say?',options:['Ask if you would want that job, then share it out','Keep the plan, since the till is the easy job','Give it to whoever moans the least'],answer:0,why:'The planner should picture doing the job. Three hours alone at a till looks easy on paper and feels long in real life.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHAT THE BOOK MISSES',left:{title:'The book says',items:['The line frees men from heavy labour','High wages are sound policy','The Model T is the right design']},right:{title:'It plays down or misses',items:['One motion all day is a cost too','The line drove the turnover','The Model T was about to lose']},note:'These are not slips. Each one comes from where Ford stood.'},{kind:'idea',text:'People who design a system rarely work inside it, and their confidence can hide its costs. Before you finalise a process, ask who will run it daily, and whether you would.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the designer cannot see it',text:'From above, a line is a set of times and flows. Tedium does not show up in them. It shows up in the person at one station, and in how quickly people leave. The designer\u2019s numbers can improve while the job gets worse.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A perfect line seen from above, and one square where the same hands make the same small motion all day. There is no way out of it.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'A CALL SCRIPT',text:'A manager writes a call script that halves handling time. On the dashboard it is a triumph. The staff read the same lines hundreds of times a day, and within months the best of them have gone. Nobody who wrote the script has taken a call with it.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before rolling out any process, run it yourself for one full session at the real pace. Note what you would change if you had to do it every day.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A hospital redesigns its shift handover to save ten minutes. What check does this idea add?',options:['Have the planners run the new handover themselves, on a real shift','Measure the minutes saved over the first month','Ask senior managers whether the design looks sound','Compare it with the handovers at other hospitals'],answer:0,why:'The other checks all look from above. The idea is to stand where the person running it stands.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'timeline',kicker:'WHAT THE BOOK KNEW',points:[{when:'1908',what:'The Model T goes on sale'},{when:'1913',what:'The moving line starts at Highland Park'},{when:'1914',what:'The five dollar day'},{when:'1922',what:'My Life and Work is published'},{when:'1927',what:'The Model T is replaced'}],note:'The book was written near the top, and it reads with that confidence.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The book\u2019s blind spots are structural, not incidental. Ford designed a system he never had to work inside, and he could not see the costs that fell on the people who did.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Three things it does not see',text:'It praises the line for freeing men from heavy labour, and waves off one motion, paced by a machine, as work most men do not mind. It gives the turnover figures and calls the wage cost-cutting, not charity, but never connects the turnover to the line. And it gives no hint that the Model T\u2019s run was ending.'},{kind:'explain',title:'His own category would have caught it',text:'Ford treats a skilled man on unskilled work as waste. Applied to human capability rather than only to steel, that category would have registered a working life spent at one station. He applied it in one direction only.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One station on a perfect line: the same hands, the same motion, no way out. The view from above is the designer\u2019s view.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Hindsight makes this easy. Many people took the line for the wage, and its cheap cars reached buyers who had never owned one. The point is not that Ford was wrong to build it. It is that a designer\u2019s confidence is no evidence about the work, and that his greatest strength, refusing to change a working design, was about to cost him the market.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which system do you design or manage that you have never worked inside at the real pace?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HARDER CASE',prompt:'A designer says: \u201cNobody has complained, so the process is fine.\u201d What does Ford\u2019s case suggest?',options:['Complaints may arrive as people leaving, not as feedback','Silence from the people using it shows it is working','Only the designer can judge a process without bias','If output is high, the work cannot be costly to anyone'],answer:0,why:'Ford\u2019s workers did not need to argue. They left, in numbers large enough to force a doubling of wages.'}]}},'my-life-and-work_6':{pictureCaption:'A row of identical dark car bodies, and one can of colour at the end of the line, still unopened.',bridge:'Idea 3 fixed a standard to learn from. This one shows a standard outliving its reason.',basics:{title:'Why every car was black',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A PUZZLE',prompt:'Henry Ford made millions of cars over 100 years ago. For years you could only buy his car in black. Why?',options:['He just liked black best','Black paint dried fastest','Black was the only paint there was'],answer:1,reveal:'His cars were made on a moving line. Each car had to wait for its paint to dry. The usual answer is that black dried faster, so the line could go faster. Some historians doubt it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Some rules start for a good reason. Then the reason goes away, but the rule stays. So check old rules to see if the reason is still there.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'All the cars are black. A pot of a new colour sits at the end, still shut. Nobody thought to open it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What went wrong',text:'Later, a rival car maker called Chevrolet sold cars in colours. People liked having a choice. But Ford had built his whole firm around one black car. It was very hard to change in time.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'AT HOME',text:'Your family always eats at five. It began because your mum had to leave for work at six. She changed jobs last year. Dinner is still at five, and no one knows why now.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of one rule at home or at school. Ask why it started. Is that reason still true today?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your club only meets in the small room. That began when the big hall was being fixed. The hall is fine now. What does this idea say?',options:['Ask if the reason still holds, and maybe move','Stay in the small room, since that is the rule','No rule should last more than a year'],answer:0,why:'The rule had a good reason once. The reason has gone, so it is worth checking.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE FAMOUS LINE',belief:'Ford offering any colour so long as it was black was arrogance: buyers would take what he gave them.',reality:'On the standard explanation, which some historians dispute, it was a production decision. Black japan enamel dried faster than anything else available, and at the speeds Ford\u2019s line had reached, drying time was the constraint.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A rule adopted for a sound technical reason can outlive the reason, and the organisation built around it is usually the last to notice.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'CONSTRAINT INTO IDENTITY',nodes:['Paint must dry before the next step','Black dries fastest','Only black is offered','Black is simply what Ford is'],note:'By the last step, nobody is asking about drying times.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'THE RIVAL',text:'General Motors reasoned that customers with rising incomes would pay for choice, and offered variety, colours included, through Chevrolet. It took market share. Ford held his position too long, and by then it was very difficult to reverse.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Identical dark bodies down the line. The can at the end holds the choice the line was built to leave out.'},{kind:'try',text:'List three rules where you work. Beside each, write the constraint it answers. Check whether that constraint still exists, and put a date in the diary to check again next year.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A team releases software only once a month, a rule set when testing took weeks. Testing now takes an hour. What does this idea suggest?',options:['Check whether the monthly rule still answers a real constraint','Keep the rule, since it has worked well for years','Release daily, since slow releases are always a mistake','Ask customers whether they like the monthly releases'],answer:0,why:'The rule was not wrong when it was made. Its reason may have gone, and nobody has checked.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'AN OPERATIONS WORD',word:'Binding constraint',meaning:'The one limit that sets the pace of a whole process. Speed up anything else and output does not change. On the usual account of Ford\u2019s line, it was the time paint took to dry.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Read as arrogance',text:'The sentence about any colour so long as it is black is the most quoted in the book, usually read as contempt for customers. The standard explanation, which some historians dispute, is throughput: black japan enamel dried faster than the alternatives available, so one colour lifted the speed of the whole line.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A constraint adopted for a sound reason tends to outlive the reason. It hardens into an identity, and the organisation built around it is the last to notice.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'As a production decision',items:['Justified by drying time','Raised line speed','Right for years']},right:{title:'As a strategy',items:['Held after the market changed','Ruled out choice','Let General Motors take share']},note:'The same rule. The facts around it moved.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Identical dark bodies run into the distance. The one can of colour waits, unopened, at the end of the line.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Hindsight flatters this lesson. Ford\u2019s single colour was right for years, and a rule reviewed every week is not a rule. Nor is constraint the problem: constraint made the Model T cheap. The error was holding it after its reason had changed. The useful discipline is narrower: record which rules answer a constraint, so they can be checked.'},{kind:'recap',points:['On the usual account, the black car was a throughput decision.','Rules born of a constraint outlive the constraint.','Write down the reason, and check it on a schedule.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A hospital insists on paper forms, a rule set when its computers kept failing. The systems are now reliable. What is the best diagnosis?',options:['A rule has outlived the constraint that justified it','Paper was always the wrong choice for a hospital','The rule protects patients, so it should never change','Staff prefer paper, so it is only a matter of taste'],answer:0,why:'The rule was a sound response once. The question is whether the constraint still exists, and nobody has been asked to check.'}]}},'my-life-and-work_7':{pictureCaption:'Coin stacks for each year\u2019s price, each lower than the last, and a hand still taking coins off. Outside, the line of cars it sells.',bridge:'Idea 6 was a rule that outlived its reason. Ford\u2019s price cuts leaned on the same unchanging car.',basics:{title:'Cut the price, sell far more',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE LEMONADE STAND',text:'You sell lemonade at 50p a cup, and ten people buy. That makes \xa35. Next day you try 30p a cup. Now thirty people buy. That makes \xa39. Each cup is cheaper, but you end up with more money.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Cutting the price on purpose can bring in far more buyers. Selling lots more can earn more in total, and make each one cheaper to make.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'FORD\u2019S CAR',picture:!0,text:'Henry Ford made a car called the Model T over 100 years ago. At first it cost about $850. He cut the price again and again, on purpose. In the end it cost under $300. And his business kept growing.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE CIRCLE',nodes:['Cut the price','Many more people buy','Make far more cars','Each car costs less to make'],loop:!0,note:'That saving pays for the next price cut. Then the circle turns again.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Price first',text:'Most sellers work out what a thing costs to make, then add a bit. Ford did it the other way round. He set a low price first. Then his team had to find ways to make the car for less.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you sell something, like at a school fair, try two prices. Count how many you sell at each. Which made more in total?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'At the school fair, cakes cost \xa32 and lots are left over. What would Ford try?',options:['A lower price, to sell far more cakes','A higher price, to make more on each one','Fewer cakes next time, at the same price'],answer:0,why:'Ford bet that a lower price brings in many more buyers, and more money in total.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'NO RIVAL IN SIGHT',prompt:'Ford\u2019s car sells well at about $850, and no competitor is forcing his hand. What does he do?',options:['Hold the price and take the margin','Raise it, since demand is strong','Cut it, and keep cutting it'],answer:2,reveal:'He cut it almost every year, on purpose, from around $850 to under $300. The business grew the whole time.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Set the price low first, then find the cost reductions that make it profitable. Volume can return more in total than margin, and a low price forces efficiencies you would not otherwise look for.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'LOWER EVERY YEAR',caption:'The coin stacks step down, and a hand is taking still more off the lowest. Through the window runs the line of identical black cars that the low price sells.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why price-first works',text:'Setting a price from cost is passive: whatever the costs are, the customer pays them. Ford argues that a low target forces the organisation to hunt for savings, because the price will not wait. The hunt finds things a comfortable margin would have left alone.'},{kind:'flow',nodes:['A lower price','A wider market','Higher volume','Lower cost per car'],loop:!0,note:'The volume funds the next reduction. It worked for two decades.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Charge what it will bear',items:['Price set from cost','High margin, smaller market','Efficiency is optional']},right:{title:'Ford\u2019s inversion',items:['Price set first, and low','Thin margin, huge market','Efficiency is compulsory']}},{kind:'try',text:'Before your next price rise, work out what a lower price would do to volume. Do the arithmetic even if you then decide against it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A small software firm wants more revenue. Following Ford, what should it calculate first?',options:['How far volume would rise at a lower price','How much its current customers would pay','What competitors charge for something similar','How much its own costs rose over the year'],answer:0,why:'Ford started from the price that would widen the market, then made the costs fit. The arithmetic comes first, whatever you decide.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'scale',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO PRICE',low:'Cost first: add a margin to it',high:'Price first: force the cost down to fit',note:'Most firms sit near the left. Ford moved deliberately to the right and stayed there for twenty years.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Price can be a tool for finding efficiency. Cut it first, widen the market, and let volume and the hunt for savings make it profitable.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'THE MODEL T\u2019S PRICE',text:'The Model T fell from around eight hundred and fifty dollars to under three hundred while the business grew. Ford insists this was deliberate rather than forced by competitors, and he states it in the book as a policy.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Stacks falling in steps, a hand trimming the newest one further, and outside a line of identical cars running out of sight. The price is cut first; the volume follows.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The mechanism',text:'Two things work together. Volume spreads fixed costs over more cars, so each costs less. And a price set below today\u2019s cost turns efficiency from a hope into a deadline. Ford argues that pricing by cost is passive: it lets costs stay where they are.'},{kind:'flow',nodes:['Cut the price','Market widens','Volume rises','Cost per car falls','Profit funds the next cut'],loop:!0,note:'The loop needs a design stable enough to keep optimising.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The strategy depends on a product stable enough to keep optimising. The same discipline that produced the cost curve left Ford unable to replace the car when it was finally beaten. It also assumes demand keeps growing as price falls; a saturated market, or buyers who want something else, breaks the loop. And a cut without the savings is just a loss.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'In something you sell or price, what would a much lower price force you to find?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HIDDEN DEPENDENCY',prompt:'A firm copies Ford, cutting prices every year on a product it redesigns every season. What is the likely problem?',options:['Constant redesign stops the cost savings building up','Customers distrust a product whose price keeps falling','Lower prices always shrink profit, whatever the volume','Yearly cuts are too slow to change demand much'],answer:0,why:'Ford\u2019s loop ran on a design stable enough to optimise for years. Each new design resets the cost curve.'}]}},'my-life-and-work_8':{pictureCaption:'Ore arrives by barge at one end of the works, and finished cars leave by rail at the other. One owner holds every step between.',bridge:'Idea 7\u2019s price cuts needed costs Ford controlled. This is how far he went to control them.',basics:{title:'Owning every step',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'THE PIZZA PLAN',text:'You love making pizza. But the shop keeps running out of cheese. So you buy a cow. Then you grow tomatoes, and wheat for the flour. Now no shop can let you down. But what if you want to make tacos?'},{kind:'idea',text:'Owning every step of making something means no one can let you down. But it also makes it very hard to change what you make.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHAT FORD OWNED',text:'Henry Ford made cars over 100 years ago. He did not want to wait for anyone else. So he owned mines for iron and coal. He owned forests, ships, a railway and glassworks too.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Rock comes in by boat at one end of the works. Finished cars roll out at the other. Ford owned every step in between.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'THE TRADE',left:{title:'What you get',items:['No waiting for others','No paying their profit']},right:{title:'What it costs',items:['Hard to change','You must be good at many jobs']}},{kind:'story',kicker:'THE RUBBER FARM',text:'Ford also started a huge rubber farm in Brazil, in South America. Making cars did not make him good at growing rubber. The farm failed completely, and it cost a great deal.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your class makes a magazine. A friend says: let\u2019s make our own paper and ink too. What is the catch?',options:['You would have to get good at new, hard jobs','Making your own paper is always cheaper','Then nobody could ever stop the magazine'],answer:0,why:'Owning every step gives you control. But each step is a new job you must be good at.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'THE RIVER ROUGE',text:'At Ford\u2019s River Rouge plant, raw material entered at one end and finished cars left the other. Behind it stood iron ore mines, coal mines, forests, a fleet of ships, a railway and glassworks, all owned by Ford.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Owning your whole supply chain buys control over cost and timing, and sells flexibility. The price of that trade only shows when conditions change.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Ford\u2019s reasoning',text:'A supplier\u2019s delay is your stoppage, and a supplier\u2019s margin is your cost. Own the supplier and both disappear. At Ford\u2019s volumes, with a stable product, that logic was powerful.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Ore arrives by barge; finished cars leave by rail. Every step between belongs to one owner, and so does every problem.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'BOUGHT AND SOLD',left:{title:'What integration buys',items:['No supplier delays','No supplier margins','Control of quality and timing']},right:{title:'What it sells',items:['Freedom to change the product','Freedom to drop a weak step','Focus on what you do best']}},{kind:'explain',title:'Where it broke',text:'Every change to the car meant changes across an owned supply chain, which is part of why Ford could not respond quickly when the market moved. His rubber plantation in Brazil failed outright, at very great expense.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before taking any supplier in-house, write down what you are buying: reliability, or the obligation to be good at something new.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A bakery chain, tired of late flour deliveries, considers buying a mill. What should it ask first?',options:['Whether it wants to get good at milling as well as baking','Whether the mill would make its bread a little cheaper','Whether its flour supplier would agree a lower price','Whether owning a mill would impress its customers'],answer:0,why:'Integration fixes the delays and hands you a new business to run well. That obligation is the real cost.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'THE JARGON',word:'Vertical integration',meaning:'Owning the earlier and later stages of your own supply chain, from raw material to finished product, instead of buying from independent suppliers.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Integration buys control and sells flexibility. Ford pursued it further than almost anyone, and the price became visible only when the product had to change.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How far he went',text:'Iron ore and coal mines, forests, ships, a railway, glassworks, and the River Rouge plant, where raw material entered and finished cars left. The reasoning was control: no supplier could stop his line or take a margin from it.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'HOW MUCH TO OWN',low:'Buy everything from others',high:'Own every step',note:'Ford sat at the far right. Where you should sit depends on how stable your product is and how large your volumes are.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One building takes in ore at one end and releases cars at the other. Control in a single image, and rigidity too.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it turned rigid',text:'Every change to the car implied changes across the owned chain. When the market moved, Ford had to move mines, mills and ships with it. A company that buys can switch supplier. A company that owns has to rebuild.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The Brazilian rubber plantation, begun in the late 1920s after the book appeared, failed outright: Ford had bought the obligation to be good at tropical farming, and was not. An owned supplier also never loses the contract, so it feels no competition. And integration paid while volumes were enormous, which most businesses never reach.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE REAL PURCHASE',prompt:'A start-up plans to make its own chips to escape supplier delays. By Ford\u2019s case, what is it really buying?',options:['Reliability, plus the duty to be good at making chips','Lower costs, since supplier margins simply disappear','Speed, since it will no longer depend on anyone','Flexibility, since it will control every component'],answer:0,why:'Control comes with an obligation, and flexibility is what gets sold. Ford\u2019s rubber venture shows the cost when the new skill never arrives.'}]}},'my-life-and-work_9':{pictureCaption:'A bare wall where a chart might hang, one pin left in the plaster. The structure is gone; the person deciding is not.',bridge:'Ideas 6 to 8 showed systems that could not bend. This one shows what sat at the top of them.',basics:{title:'No chart, one boss',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE CLASS PLAY',text:'Your class puts on a play. Nobody has a set job. When anyone needs something, they ask the teacher. With five kids, that works fine. With thirty, there is a long queue. Then the teacher is off sick.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Having no set jobs can feel fast and free. But then one person ends up deciding everything. It only works while that person can keep track of it all.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'FORD\u2019S WAY',text:'Henry Ford ran a huge car company over 100 years ago. He was proud that it had no chart of who was in charge of what. Few people had titles. If you needed something, you found the person who could help.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A wall where a chart of who does what might hang. It is empty, except for one pin. Ford liked it that way.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE JUGGLING',text:'One person can juggle three balls. Maybe five. Keep adding balls and they all drop. A company run from one head is the same. It works until there are too many balls.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What happened',text:'Ford grew old, and he would not let anyone else decide. Some of his choices were wrong, and no one could stop him. Years later, his company came close to failing.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your club has no jobs and one leader who decides everything. It grows from 6 kids to 40. What is the risk?',options:['The leader can\u2019t keep up, and no one else can decide','Having no jobs makes the club fair for all','More kids will always make the club more fun'],answer:0,why:'With no jobs, everything lands on one person. That works for six kids, not for forty.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE FLAT COMPANY',belief:'A company with no organisation chart has no hierarchy.',reality:'Ford\u2019s company had no chart, few titles and no defined lines of authority. That did not remove power. All of it became personal, flowing from one man.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Rejecting formal structure works while one person can hold the whole business in their head. Past that limit, it leaves authority concentrated and unchecked.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Ford\u2019s case against charts',text:'He argues that formal structure produces people who defend positions rather than do work, and that meetings were largely unnecessary: a man who needed something found the man who could supply it. There is something in this. Titles can become territory.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A bare wall where a chart might hang, one pin left in the plaster. The structure is gone; the person who decides is not.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'THE LIMIT',low:'Small enough for one head',high:'Larger than one person\u2019s attention',note:'Flat works on the left. Ford kept the same arrangement long after the company had moved to the right.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'A GROWING START-UP',text:'A start-up of twelve runs on the founder\u2019s say-so, and it is fast. At two hundred, every decision still waits for her. Nobody else is allowed to decide, so her queue sets the speed of the whole company.'},{kind:'try',text:'If you work somewhere flat, list the decisions only one person can make. Then ask what happens to each when that person is away for a month.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A founder boasts that her company has no managers, just her and the team. It now has 300 people. What is the risk?',options:['Every decision depends on her attention, with no check if she is wrong','Flat teams are always slower than teams with managers','Staff will leave because they want impressive job titles','Without managers, nobody will know what work to do'],answer:0,why:'Ford\u2019s case shows that a flat structure scales only to one person\u2019s attention, and removes any check on that person.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'AFTER THE BOOK',text:'The book\u2019s pride in having no structure reads differently beside what is documented elsewhere: Ford\u2019s treatment of his own executives, an internal security operation under Harry Bennett, and a company near collapse by the time his grandson took control in 1945.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The absence of structure did not remove power. It removed any check on it. Flat organisation scales to the limit of one person\u2019s attention, and fails worst when that person is wrong.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What he argued',text:'Ford writes with pride that the company had no organisation chart, no titles of consequence and no defined lines of authority. His case is that formal structure breeds people who defend positions instead of doing work. He is not wrong that it can.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A bare wall, the outline of where a chart might hang, and one pin. Taking down the chart left all the authority in one place.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'What structure costs',items:['Titles that become territory','Meetings','People defending positions']},right:{title:'What structure provides',items:['Decisions that outlast one person','Someone allowed to say no','A check on the top']},note:'Ford counted the left column and not the right.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Structure is no cure. A hierarchy can be as unchecked as Ford\u2019s was, and his objection to defensive titles still lands on many organisations. Nor does flat always fail: small firms that genuinely spread authority can work well. What fails is flat in name, with all power held by one person, which is what Ford described as freedom.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'In a group you belong to, who would make the decisions if the person at the top vanished tomorrow?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE REAL PROBLEM',prompt:'Which description best fits the risk in Ford\u2019s arrangement?',options:['Authority was personal, so nothing could correct his mistakes','Too few meetings meant the staff were badly informed','A lack of titles left workers feeling unappreciated','Without a chart, departments duplicated one another\u2019s work'],answer:0,why:'The danger was not untidiness. All power flowed from one man, and nothing could check him when he was wrong.'}]}}}},2987,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'nicomachean-ethics_1':{pictureCaption:'A doorstep worn hollow by years of feet. No single step made the dip. All of them did, and now each foot settles into it.',basics:{title:'You get brave by doing brave things',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'WHICH COMES FIRST?',prompt:'You want to be brave. What has to come first?',options:['Feeling brave','Doing a brave thing'],answer:1,reveal:'Most people wait to feel brave. Aristotle was a thinker in Greece more than 2,300 years ago. He said it works the other way round. You do the brave thing first, while you are still a bit scared.'},{kind:'idea',text:'You are not born brave, kind or patient. You become those things by doing them, again and again. The feeling comes after the doing.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE LEARNING TO SWIM',text:'Nobody learns to swim by reading about it. You get in the water. You splash and sink a bit. After lots of tries, swimming just feels normal. Being brave or kind is learned the same way.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'No one step wore this dip in the stone. Thousands of steps did. Your habits are made the same way.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'AT SCHOOL',text:'Say you want to stop being shy in class. Waiting to feel bold does not work. So this week you put up your hand once. Next week, twice. It is scary at first. Then it gets a bit less scary each time.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one thing you want to be, like kind. Do one small kind thing today, even if you don\u2019t feel like it. Then do one more tomorrow.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Mia wants to be more patient with her little brother. What would Aristotle tell her?',options:['Act patient with him a little, again and again','Wait until she feels more patient inside','Read about patience until she gets it'],answer:0,why:'Patience grows from doing patient things. The calm feeling comes after lots of practice.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE USUAL ORDER',belief:'To change how you act, first change how you feel. Get confident, then speak up.',reality:'Aristotle runs it the other way. You become brave by doing brave acts, and fair by doing fair ones. Character is what your actions leave behind, not what you start from.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Character is not something you have and then act from. It is the residue of your actions. You become patient, fair or brave by doing those things, often enough that the inclination shifts.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW A TRAIT FORMS',nodes:['A small act, against inclination','Repeated, it gets easier','The inclination shifts','The act now comes naturally'],note:'The feeling arrives last, and it arrives because of the acts.'},{kind:'explain',title:'A craft, not a subject',text:'Aristotle compares it to learning a trade: builders learn by building, musicians by playing. You do not become patient by being persuaded that patience is good, any more than you learn carpentry from a book about wood.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A step worn hollow by years of feet. No single footfall did this. The pattern did, and now each foot settles into the dip.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE QUESTION IN THE MEETING',text:'You want to be someone who speaks up. So this week you ask one question in the meeting, heart still going. Next week, another. You are not waiting for courage to arrive. You are building it, one slightly frightening act at a time.'},{kind:'try',text:'Choose one trait you want. Do the smallest act of it today, before you feel like it: the call instead of the email, the honest reply. Repeat tomorrow.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Sam wants to be more generous, but says he will start once he feels less anxious about money. What would Aristotle say?',options:['Give in small amounts now; the easier feeling follows the acts','Wait, since generous acts without generous feelings do not count','Change his beliefs about money first, and the acts will follow','Make one very large gift to break the habit of meanness at once'],answer:0,why:'On Aristotle\u2019s view the feeling is built by the acts. Waiting for it first reverses the order that actually works.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'WHAT HE WAS ARGUING AGAINST',belief:'If you truly know what is good, you will do it. Virtue is a kind of knowledge.',reality:'Socrates, as Plato presents him, held something close to this. Aristotle thought it missed how character is made. Virtue of character comes from habit, so it is trained like a skill rather than taught like a subject.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The virtues are not in us by nature, nor against nature. We are able to take them on, and we complete them by practice: we become just by doing just acts, brave by doing brave ones.'},{kind:'term',word:'Hexis',meaning:'Aristotle\u2019s word for a settled state. Not a passing feeling and not a bare ability, but a formed tendency to feel and act in certain ways, built by repeated activity of the same kind.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The puzzle he answers',text:'If you must do just acts to become just, must you not be just already? Aristotle replies that early acts only look right. Full virtue also needs knowing what you do, choosing it for its own sake, and acting from a firm state. Practice is how the look becomes the thing.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The patient who only listens',text:'In the same book he mocks people who talk about virtue instead of practising it. They are like patients who listen carefully to the doctor and then do nothing they were told. Theory alone will not make them well.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A hollow worn into stone by repeated steps. The state was produced by the activity, and now it shapes each step that follows.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Practice entrenches bad habits as readily as good ones, so the acts must be the right ones, and choosing them takes judgement. Upbringing also weighs heavily: Aristotle says the habits formed from youth make all the difference, and nobody chooses their childhood. The method is strongest where you already have some say over what you repeat.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Character is the residue of repeated acts, not their source.','Early acts only resemble virtue; repetition makes them real.','Knowing what is good does not, alone, make you good.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'A friend says: \u201cActing kind when you don\u2019t feel kind is just faking it.\u201d What is Aristotle\u2019s best reply?',options:['The early acts are how the real disposition gets built','Faking it is fine, because only the behaviour ever counts','He agrees: acts without the feeling have no moral value','Feelings play no part in character, so the worry is idle'],answer:0,why:'Aristotle grants that the early acts are not yet full virtue. They are the training that produces it, and the feeling follows.'}]}},'nicomachean-ethics_2':{pictureCaption:'Pour too much and it spills across the bench. Too little and it is barely there. The right pour sits between two different mistakes.',bridge:'Idea 1 said you build a trait by practice. This one asks what you are aiming at when you practise.',basics:{title:'Too much and too little',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'ON THE PITCH',text:'Your coach says you should shoot more. So you do. Now you shoot every time you get the ball, even when a friend is free right in front of goal. Your team is not happy.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Most good traits sit between two mistakes: too little and too much. Brave sits between being scared of everything and being reckless, which means taking silly risks.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO GET IT WRONG',left:{title:'Too little',items:['Never takes a shot','Never shares a sweet','Never speaks up']},right:{title:'Too much',items:['Shoots every single time','Gives away all their lunch','Talks over everyone']},note:'The good spot is in between: shoot when it makes sense.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One glass spilled over. One has hardly anything in it. The middle one was poured just right.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why this matters',text:'Aristotle was a thinker in Greece more than 2,300 years ago. His idea helps with a trap. People often watch for one mistake only. If you try hard not to be shy, you may not see yourself getting bossy. The other mistake sneaks up on you.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick something you want to get better at. Say what too little looks like. Then say what too much looks like. Aim for the space between.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Leo was told to be more helpful. Now he takes over whenever a friend tries to build something. What went wrong?',options:['He went past helpful into too much','Nothing, he is finally helpful enough','Helping is good, so more is always better'],answer:0,why:'Leo only watched for being unhelpful. He missed the other mistake: doing so much that it stops helping.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'ADVICE THAT BACKFIRED',text:'A review told you to be more assertive. Six months on, you push in every conversation, including the ones that needed a listener. The advice was right for the person you were. It is wrong for the person you have become.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Each virtue sits between two vices: an excess and a deficiency of the same thing. So more is not automatically better, and there are two ways to fail, not one.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'ONE TRAIT, TWO EDGES',low:'Cowardice: too little nerve',high:'Recklessness: too much',note:'Courage sits between. Generosity sits the same way, between meanness and waste.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why advice misfires',text:'\u201cBe more assertive\u201d helps the person who errs toward too little and harms the one already at too much. The same sentence is handed to both. Advice that names only one failure points in a direction without knowing where you stand.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Too much spilled on the bench, too little left in the bottom. The hand stops at the line, and the line is at neither end.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Before you adjust',steps:['Name what too little looks like here.','Name what too much looks like here.','Ask which edge you usually drift toward.','Lean away from that edge, not just away from the one you were warned about.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A manager already known for being blunt is told to give more direct feedback. What does the idea suggest?',options:['Check first whether he already errs toward too much','Follow it, since more directness is always more honest','Ignore it, since feedback is best kept to a minimum','Aim halfway between blunt and silent in every case'],answer:0,why:'The same advice helps one person and harms another. Knowing which edge you are near comes before any adjustment.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'THE SHAPE OF EVERY VIRTUE',left:{title:'Deficiency',items:['Cowardice','Meanness','Never angry, even at injustice']},right:{title:'Excess',items:['Rashness','Prodigality, or waste','Quick, excessive anger']},note:'Courage, generosity and good temper sit between. In the second book Aristotle walks through virtue after virtue in this shape.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Every virtue of character is flanked by an excess and a deficiency of the same feeling or action. Failure has two directions, and the vice you are fleeing is rarely the one you are walking into.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the extremes mislead',text:'Aristotle notes that each position looks extreme from the others. To the coward the brave person seems rash; to the rash, cowardly. So where you stand distorts where you think the middle is, and people at one edge call the virtue by the name of the other vice.'},{kind:'explain',title:'His practical rule',text:'Because the target is hard to hit, he advises steering away from the extreme you are more prone to, as people straighten a bent stick by bending it the other way. Notice what you are drawn to, and pull against it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The spill and the dregs are opposite errors. The hand that stops at the line had to watch for both at once.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Not every trait has a neat pair of opposites, and Aristotle admits some extremes had no name. The pattern can also be forced, inventing a \u2018too much honesty\u2019 to excuse a lie. And the structure does not say aim for the exact centre. Where the right point falls is a judgement, which is a separate problem.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'For the trait you most want to improve, which edge do you drift toward? What would overcorrecting into the other edge look like?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which is a misuse of the two-failure structure?',options:['Calling cowardice \u201cprudence\u201d because rashness is the only danger','Naming both extremes of generosity before choosing a gift','Leaning toward boldness because you know you tend to be timid','Noticing that \u201cspeak up more\u201d may be the wrong advice for you'],answer:0,why:'Treating one vice as the only danger lets you relabel the other as a virtue. Aristotle warns that people at an edge misname the middle.'}]}},'nicomachean-ethics_3':{pictureCaption:'Lower down, the holds are ringed with the marks of a struggle. Higher up, the climber barely grips. The effort shows how far along you are.',bridge:'Idea 1 said habits are built by repeated acts. This one gives you a way to tell how far the building has got.',basics:{title:'How it feels shows how far you\u2019ve got',beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO KIDS AT THE PIANO',left:{title:'Ana',items:['Practises every day','Groans each time','Counts the minutes']},right:{title:'Ben',items:['Practises every day','Sits down with no fuss','Forgets the clock']},note:'They do the same thing. It feels very different to each of them.'},{kind:'idea',text:'How a good habit feels tells you how far along you are. If it still feels like a fight, you are early. If it feels easy, the habit has set.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Who said so',text:'Aristotle was a thinker in Greece long ago. He said you can learn what someone\u2019s habits really are by how they feel. Two people can act the same. The one who is glad to do it has the habit. The one who hates it is still learning.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Low on the wall, the holds have scrape marks round them. Each one was a fight. Higher up, the climber holds on lightly. It got easier.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'If it still feels hard, I must be doing it wrong.',reality:'Not true. Hard just means it is early. Ana is not failing. She is still building the habit. If she keeps going, the groan gets smaller.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of a good habit you are working on, like making your bed. Does it still feel like a fight? Then you are early, not failing. Keep going.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Jay has read every night for two weeks. It still feels like hard work. What does this tell him?',options:['He is still early, and the habit is forming','Reading is not for him, so he should stop','He has the habit now, so he can stop trying'],answer:0,why:'The hard feeling shows where he is. It does not mean it isn\u2019t working. Easy comes later.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'WEEK THREE',text:'Three weeks of eating well, and it still takes visible effort every day. You walk past the biscuits with your jaw set. You conclude it is not working, and that people who eat well must simply be built differently.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The pleasure or pain that follows an act shows what state you are actually in. Same behaviour, different state: the person who still feels the pull is earlier in the process than the one who does not.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Aristotle\u2019s own example',text:'He takes self-mastery over bodily pleasures. One person goes without and is glad to. Another goes without and resents every moment. The behaviour is identical. Only the feeling shows that the first has the trait and the second is still holding it in place.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'THE SAME ACT, TWO STATES',left:{title:'Held by effort',items:['Costs you every time','Fails when you are tired','Feels like restraint']},right:{title:'Formed into habit',items:['Costs little','Survives a bad week','Feels like relief']},note:'The left column is not failure. It is an earlier point on the same road.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The lower holds are ringed with the marks of a struggle. Higher up, the same route is taken almost lightly.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one thing you do well but at a cost. Rate the effort today from one to ten, and again in a month. The number is a reading of where you are, not a verdict on the approach.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'READ THE GAUGE',prompt:'Two colleagues both stay calm in tense meetings. One finds it easy; the other is drained afterwards. What follows?',options:['The second is earlier in forming the habit, though both act well','The second is more virtuous, since the calm costs her more','They are at the same point, since their behaviour is identical','The first was simply born calm, so habit plays no part'],answer:0,why:'Aristotle reads the state off the feeling. Same act, different state, and continuing effort marks the earlier one.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',word:'Continence (enkrateia)',meaning:'Aristotle\u2019s name for the state of someone who has strong wrong desires and overrules them. It deserves praise, but it is not yet virtue, in which the wrong desires have themselves been reshaped.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Take the pleasure or pain that follows your acts as a sign of the state you have formed. Someone who abstains gladly has the virtue; someone who abstains grudgingly does not have it yet.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where it sits',text:'The test comes in the second book, just after the claim that virtue is built by habit. If habit is the method, he needs a way to read progress, and he finds it in feeling. Being rightly trained, as Plato had said, means being pleased and pained by the right things.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THREE STATES',nodes:['Acting badly, and glad to','Acting well, grudgingly','Acting well, gladly'],note:'From outside, the middle state looks like the last. Only the feeling tells them apart.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the reading matters',text:'Behaviour can be held in place by effort for a long time, so behaviour alone tells you little. While the act still costs you, the disposition has not formed, and the act is exposed to fatigue and the first bad week.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The struggle marks on the lower holds are not failure. They are the record of the stage before ease.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The test can mislead. Some right acts should stay painful: telling a hard truth to someone grieving costs anyone. Ease can also come from never having been tested. And many later moralists prized the person who struggles and wins over the one who never feels the pull. Read feeling as evidence, not proof.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HARD CASE',prompt:'Someone has been honest at work for years and still finds every awkward truth painful. On Aristotle\u2019s test, what is most reasonable?',options:['The pain suggests the state is unfinished, unless the situation explains it','Their honesty does not count, since only effortless acts are virtuous','They have full virtue, since continuing effort shows moral seriousness','The test fails here, because feelings reveal nothing about character'],answer:0,why:'The feeling is a sign to be read, not a verdict. First ask whether the pain comes from the state or from the circumstances.'}]}},'nicomachean-ethics_4':{pictureCaption:'Two hooks hold things that belong to the job and will stay with it. The scarf was brought from outside, by choice.',bridge:'The first ideas were about building your own character. Aristotle gives two whole books to what joins people to each other.',basics:{title:'Three kinds of friend',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'MOVING SCHOOLS',prompt:'You move to a new school. Which friends will you still see next year?',options:['All of them, the same as now','Some of them, and it depends why you were friends'],answer:1,reveal:'Some friends were really about the bus ride or the team. When that ends, they drift away. Nobody did anything wrong.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Aristotle, a thinker in Greece long ago, said friends come in three kinds. Some are useful. Some are fun. Some like you for who you are. Only the third kind lasts through big changes.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The three kinds',text:'A useful friend: you sit together because you share a bus. A fun friend: you play the same game. A true friend: you like each other, and want good things for each other. The first two are fine. They just depend on the bus or the game.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two hooks hold work clothes. They belong to the job and stay with it. The scarf was brought from home, on purpose.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'If a friend drifts away, one of you did something bad.',reality:'Often no one did. The thing you shared ended, like a club or a class. So the friendship ended too. That is normal.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of your friends. For each, ask: would we still be friends if we were not in the same class? Both answers are fine. It just tells you what kind of friend they are.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Zara and Kim were best mates on the swim team. Zara quits swimming, and they stop talking. What kind of friendship was it?',options:['A fun one, built on swimming together','A true one, where each liked who the other was','Not a real friendship, just pretending'],answer:0,why:'It rested on the thing they shared. When swimming stopped, so did the friendship. It was still real while it lasted.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'LAST DAY ON FRIDAY',text:'You are leaving the company on Friday. People have said kind things and promised to stay in touch. By spring you will know which of these friendships was about the work, and which was about you.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Aristotle sorts friendships by what they are for: usefulness, pleasure, or the good of the other person. The first two end when their basis ends. Only the third survives a change of circumstances, and it takes years.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHAT IT RESTS ON',left:{title:'Utility or pleasure',items:['Built on a shared job or hobby','Forms quickly','Ends when the basis ends']},right:{title:'Character',items:['Built on who the other person is','Takes years','Survives a change of job']},note:'Neither column is fake. They are different kinds of thing.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Nobody behaved badly',text:'Aristotle does not condemn the first two kinds; he describes them. A friendship of utility lasts as long as the usefulness, and dissolves without anyone doing wrong. Most of the disappointment comes from expecting the durability of the third kind from the first.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two hooks hold the job\u2019s clothes, which stay with the job. The scarf was brought from outside on purpose, and will leave with its owner.'},{kind:'try',text:'List the five people you see most. Next to each, write what would still connect you if your job changed tomorrow. Then decide where your spare evenings go.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'PREDICT IT',prompt:'Two people became close running a project together. The project is cancelled. What does Aristotle\u2019s account predict?',options:['It may fade, unless something beyond the project has grown between them','It will fade, because work friendships are never real friendships','It will last, since closeness built under pressure is the deepest kind','It depends only on how often they promise to keep in touch'],answer:0,why:'A friendship built on usefulness ends with the use, unless it has become something more. That takes time, but it can happen.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE COMMON MISREADING',belief:'Aristotle thought friendships of utility and pleasure were fake.',reality:'He calls them friendships, lesser ones, because they resemble the best kind. What differs is what each friend loves: the use, the enjoyment, or the other person for their own sake.'},{kind:'term',word:'Philia',meaning:'The Greek word usually translated as friendship. It is wider than ours, covering family, partners in business and fellow citizens: any bond of goodwill that both sides return and recognise.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A friendship takes its nature from what it loves. Love someone for what they bring you, and it ends when they stop bringing it. Love them for who they are, and it lasts as long as they remain so.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the best kind is slow',text:'A wish for friendship can arise quickly, he says, but friendship cannot. You must know the other person\u2019s character, and that takes time and shared life. He cites the old saying that people cannot know each other until they have eaten a good deal of salt together.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'HOW LONG EACH LASTS',low:'Utility: as long as the use',high:'Character: as long as the character',note:'Pleasure sits between. He notes that the young make and lose friends quickly, because what pleases them changes.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two hooks hold what belongs to the workplace. The scarf came from outside by choice. Only that one leaves with its owner.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The best friendship, for Aristotle, needs two good people alike in virtue, and he admits that is rare. Real friendships mix all three motives, so the sorting is a lens, not a verdict. And parts of these books rest on hierarchies we reject: he treats husband and wife, or ruler and ruled, as naturally unequal.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A founder calls her co-founder her closest friend. In two years they have never met outside work. What would Aristotle ask?',options:['What, beyond the company, each one loves in the other','Whether two years is long enough to count as friendship','Whether the company is succeeding, since that decides it','Nothing: working closely together is the highest friendship'],answer:0,why:'The test is the object of the love, not its length or intensity. If the company is all they share, it may end with the company.'}]}},'nicomachean-ethics_5':{pictureCaption:'A long cloth, its pattern changing along its length. The card window shows one plain patch, and that patch tells you nothing about the whole.',bridge:'Idea 1 said character is built over many acts. Aristotle judges a life the same way: over its whole length.',basics:{title:'Don\u2019t judge it by one week',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'A ROTTEN WEEK',text:'You got a bad mark in a test. You fell out with your best friend. You lost your bus pass. It is Friday night and you think: my whole life is going wrong.'},{kind:'idea',text:'You can\u2019t judge a whole life by one bad week. Or by one great week. Aristotle, a thinker in Greece long ago, said a life has to be judged as a whole.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'ONE SWALLOW',text:'Aristotle used an old saying. A swallow is a bird that comes back in spring. But one swallow does not mean it is spring yet. One warm day doesn\u2019t either. You need lots of days.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The cloth is long, with lots of patterns. The little window shows just one patch. You can\u2019t tell the whole cloth from that.'},{kind:'explain',title:'It works both ways',text:'A great week doesn\u2019t mean everything is sorted, either. Scoring the winning goal feels amazing. But it does not make you the best player forever. Good weeks and bad weeks both pass.'},{kind:'try',text:'Had a bad day? Think back to this time last year. What was worrying you then? Most of it passed. Today is one patch of a long cloth.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Raj won a prize this week. He says his life is perfect now. What would Aristotle say?',options:['One great week can\u2019t tell you about a whole life','He is right, since winning shows his life is good','He should worry, since good weeks never last'],answer:0,why:'Good weeks count, but they are one patch. You judge the cloth by all of it.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'A BAD YEAR',belief:'A bad year is good evidence about how your life is going.',reality:'For Aristotle the unit is a whole life. Flourishing is a shape a life has, not a state you are in on a given Tuesday, so no single year settles it, in either direction.'},{kind:'idea',text:'No day or short period makes a person happy, in Aristotle\u2019s sense. Flourishing belongs to a complete life, and cannot be read off any single sample of it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The uncomfortable direction',text:'A bad month is not proof your life is going badly. That is obvious, and rarely believed. Less comfortable: a good month is not proof either. Peak experiences seem to settle the question, and they settle very little.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A card window over one plain patch of a long, complicated cloth. However carefully you study the patch, it will not show you the pattern.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'HOW OFTEN YOU CHECK',low:'Every week: mostly noise, plenty of alarm',high:'Every decade: the shape shows',note:'Checking more often does not sharpen the reading. It just catches more noise.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE YEAR YOU LOST',text:'Redundancy, a move, an illness in the family. Judged against twelve months, the year is a disaster. Judged against ten years, it is a hard chapter in a life that also holds the decade before it and whatever you build next.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time a bad stretch has you drawing conclusions, write the ten-year view: what the last decade held, and what the next might. Judge the stretch against that.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'After a superb few months, a friend says she has finally worked out how to live. What does Aristotle\u2019s point suggest?',options:['A good stretch is no more conclusive than a bad one','She is right, since peak periods reveal what a life is','She should brace herself, since a bad stretch must follow','Happiness is a feeling, so if she feels it, it is settled'],answer:0,why:'The unit is the whole life. A good sample is still only a sample, which is the less comfortable half of the idea.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE FIRST BOOK',text:'Aristotle opens the Ethics by asking what the highest human good is. His answer is an activity of the soul in accordance with virtue. Then he adds a condition that is easy to skip: in a complete life.'},{kind:'term',word:'Eudaimonia',meaning:'Usually translated happiness, better as flourishing or living well. Not a mood but the way a whole life goes, which is why a moment cannot have it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Flourishing is not a state you occupy for a while. It is the shape of a whole life, so one season, good or bad, cannot make or unmake it. One swallow, as he says, does not make a spring.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Solon\u2019s puzzle',text:'He takes up an older saying, credited to the Athenian lawgiver Solon, that no one should be called happy until he is dead. Aristotle resists it: then a happy person could never be called happy while it was true. His answer is that virtuous activity is the most stable thing in a life, and it carries a person through change.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One patch, framed and isolated, against a cloth whose pattern runs its whole length. The window is the error; the cloth is the unit.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The long view can be misused to wave away real suffering: a bad year hurts now, and perspective is a judgement, not a painkiller. Aristotle also concedes that great and repeated disasters, like those of Priam, the old king of Troy in Homer, can ruin a life\u2019s blessedness. Some patches are large enough to change the cloth.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which recent stretch have you been treating as a verdict on your life? What does the ten-year view say about it?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HARD CASE',prompt:'After twenty good years, someone\u2019s business collapses at forty. They say their life has been a failure. What does Aristotle\u2019s view allow?',options:['The blow is real damage, but one stretch is not the verdict','Nothing external can touch a good life, so the loss is irrelevant','They are right, since a life is judged by how it is ending','The twenty good years guarantee that the life was a success'],answer:0,why:'Aristotle holds both: misfortune does real harm, and the unit is still the whole life. Judging a life by how it is ending is close to the saying he resisted.'}]}},'nicomachean-ethics_6':{pictureCaption:'A tailor pins the hem on the wearer, tape measure left hanging. The right length is judged on this person, not read off a scale.',bridge:'Idea 2 placed each virtue between two failures. This one corrects the obvious misreading: that the right spot is the halfway point.',basics:{title:'The right amount depends',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'LUNCH FOR TWO',prompt:'A big wrestler who trains all day and a small kid both need lunch. Should they eat the same amount?',options:['Yes, the middle amount suits everyone','No, the right amount depends on the person'],answer:1,reveal:'Aristotle, a thinker in Greece long ago, used an example like this. Too little food for one person is too much for another. A good coach picks the amount for each.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The right amount is not just the middle. It depends on who you are and what is going on. You find it by good judgement, not by a rule.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'WHEN A FRIEND IS SAD',text:'Your friend is upset. How much should you talk? If she wants to let it all out, you mostly listen. If she wants ideas, you talk more. The right amount changes. A good friend reads the moment.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Aristotle said always pick the middle.',reality:'No. He said pick what is right for this person, now. Sometimes that is a lot. Sometimes it is very little. A wise person can tell which.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A NEW COAT',picture:!0,text:'A tailor makes clothes. To get a coat the right length, a tailor does not pick a middle size. The tailor pins the bottom while you wear it. A tall person gets a longer coat than a short one. The tailor judges by eye, on you.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you are not sure how much to do, ask: what would a person I look up to do here, right now?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Two kids ask how long to practise piano each day. One is new. One has a big contest soon. What is the best answer?',options:['It depends on each kid and what they need','Both should do the same middle amount','Both should do as much as they can'],answer:0,why:'The right amount fits the person. A wise teacher picks it for each case.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE MODERATION MYTH',belief:'The doctrine of the mean says: always take the middle path.',reality:'Aristotle says the right amount is relative to the situation and the person, and is fixed as someone of practical wisdom would fix it. That last clause does all the work.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The mean is not a position on a scale. It is whatever a skilled judge would land on in exactly these circumstances. So there is no fixed correct amount of firmness, generosity or anger.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'PINNED, NOT MEASURED',caption:'The tailor pins the hem on the person wearing the coat, and the tape measure hangs unused. There is a right length, but it is found on this wearer, by a trained eye.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE CLIENT WHO KEEPS ASKING',text:'A client asks, again, for work outside the agreed scope. How firm should you be? It depends on the relationship, on whether this is a pattern, on the precedent you set and on what you can sustain. A rule that works with one client fails with the next.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why there is no rule',text:'Aristotle says ethics cannot have the precision of mathematics, and that demanding it is itself a mistake. The right act depends on the time, the people, the reason and the manner. That many variables do not reduce to a formula.'},{kind:'steps',title:'How the skill grows',steps:['Make the call as well as you can.','Watch how it lands.','Adjust next time.','Ask how someone you respect would have judged it.'],note:'Trainable, but not something you can look up. That is why he treats virtue as a craft.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you catch yourself hunting for the correct level of firmness, stop. Ask what someone you respect would do in exactly these circumstances.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Two managers each receive a late report from their team. One gives a formal warning; the other lets it go this time. Could both be right?',options:['Yes, if their circumstances differ in ways that matter','No, the mean is one fixed point for everyone','No, the halfway answer is a mild warning, so both erred','Yes, because in ethics any choice is as good as another'],answer:0,why:'The mean is relative to the case. Different histories and stakes can make different responses right, though not any response.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'MILO\u2019S DINNER',text:'Aristotle\u2019s illustration: if ten measures of food are too much and two too little, a trainer will not prescribe six. Six may be too little for Milo, a famous wrestler, and too much for a beginner. The arithmetic midpoint is the mean of the thing, not the mean relative to us.'},{kind:'term',word:'Phronesis',meaning:'Practical wisdom: the ability to see what a particular situation requires and to act on it. Aristotle makes it the standard for the mean.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Virtue is a settled state of choosing the mean relative to us, fixed by reason as the practically wise person would fix it. The mean is a judgement made in a case, not a midpoint found on a scale.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'THE MEAN RELATIVE TO US',caption:'The tape hangs unread while the hem is pinned on the wearer, by eye. A right length exists, but only this case and a trained judge can fix it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the judge attends to',text:'To feel fear, anger or pity at the right times, about the right things, toward the right people, for the right reason and in the right way: that is the mean. It is a list of particulars. None of it can be settled before the case arrives.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The demand for precision',text:'Early in the first book he warns that each subject allows only so much precision. Demanding proofs in ethics is as misplaced as accepting mere persuasion from a mathematician. The looseness is not a defect of his theory; it belongs to the subject.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The obvious objection is circularity. The mean is what the wise person chooses, and the wise person is the one who chooses the mean. For a beginner that gives little guidance: you need a judge before you can become one. Aristotle\u2019s answer is training under good laws and good examples, which presumes you have them.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Whose judgement do you borrow when you are unsure what a situation needs? What makes it trustworthy, rather than merely familiar?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'A critic says the mean is empty: it just tells you to do the right amount. What is the strongest reply?',options:['It says where to look: both errors, and a trained judge\u2019s eye','It is meant literally: the right act is the arithmetic middle','The critic is right, and Aristotle offers no guidance at all','It is useful because it forbids ever responding strongly'],answer:0,why:'Aristotle never claims a formula. The doctrine tells you what to attend to and whose judgement to train toward, which is guidance, though not a rule.'}]}},'nicomachean-ethics_7':{pictureCaption:'One pan has been taken off the scales. With nothing on the other side, there is no balance to find, only the stone.',bridge:'Ideas 2 and 6 were about finding the right amount. This one asks first whether there is a right amount at all.',basics:{title:'Some things have no right amount',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'MEET IN THE MIDDLE?',belief:'In any argument, the middle answer is the fair one.',reality:'Not always. Some things are wrong at any amount. There is no right amount of stealing. Half-stealing is still stealing.'},{kind:'idea',text:'For lots of things, like how much to eat or talk, the right amount is in between. But some things are just wrong. Aristotle, a thinker in Greece long ago, said they have no right amount at all.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE HALF DEAL',text:'A boy in your class wants to copy your homework. Your friend says, \u201cMeet in the middle. Let him copy half.\u201d It sounds fair. But copying half is still cheating. There is no right amount of cheating.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One side of the scales is gone. There is nothing to weigh the stone against. So there is no middle to find.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO KINDS OF QUESTION',left:{title:'Has a right amount',items:['How much to eat','How much to talk','How much to spend']},right:{title:'No right amount',items:['Stealing','Cheating','Being glad when others get hurt']},note:'First ask which kind of question it is.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time someone says \u201cmeet in the middle\u201d, ask: is this a thing with a right amount? Or is it just wrong?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your team plans to trip the other team\u2019s best player, \u201cjust a little bit\u201d. What does this idea say?',options:['Tripping on purpose is wrong at any amount','A little trip is the fair middle way','It is fine if the other team does it too'],answer:0,why:'There is no right amount of a foul meant to hurt someone. A little is still wrong.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE EXPENSES CLAIM',text:'A colleague\u2019s expenses claim is plainly falsified. One person wants it reported; another wants it ignored. Someone suggests a quiet word and a partial repayment. It sounds moderate, and the room relaxes.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The mean governs how much of a thing like anger or generosity is right. Some actions are bad in themselves and have no right amount. Aristotle names theft among them.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the midpoint really decides',text:'Splitting the difference between enforcing a rule and ignoring it is not moderation. It is a decision that the rule is negotiable, which is larger than the decision on the table. The midpoint looks neutral and is in fact a verdict.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'With one pan removed, there is nothing to weigh against the stone. Splitting a difference needs two sides, and here there is only one.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Before splitting a difference',steps:['Ask whether the thing comes in degrees at all.','If it does, look for the right amount for this case.','If it does not, treat the proposed midpoint as a verdict, and judge it as one.']},{kind:'try',text:'The next time someone proposes splitting the difference, ask out loud: is this a question of how much, or a question of whether?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SORT IT',prompt:'Which of these is a question the mean cannot answer?',options:['Whether to keep quiet about a colleague\u2019s faked results','How strongly to object to a colleague\u2019s rude email','How much to put towards a leaving present','How long to let a useful meeting overrun'],answer:0,why:'Faking results is not a matter of degree. The other three are questions of how much, where the mean applies.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE SAME CHAPTER',text:'Immediately after defining virtue as a mean, Aristotle blocks the obvious abuse. Not every action or feeling admits of a mean. Some have names that already imply badness: spite, shamelessness and envy among feelings; adultery, theft and murder among actions.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Some acts and feelings are bad in themselves, not by being too much or too little of something. There is no right amount of them, so asking where to set the dial is already a mistake.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the names matter',text:'You cannot do these things at the right time, with the right person, in the right way, he says. The wrongness is in the description. Nor is there a mean of an excess or a deficiency: the extremes are not dials either.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A scale with one pan gone. The stone cannot be balanced against anything, so the question of where the beam settles never arises.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE NEGOTIATED FRAUD',text:'A team discovers falsified safety data. The compromise on offer is to correct it quietly, with no disclosure. Framed as a dial between full disclosure and silence, it looks moderate. Framed correctly, it is a decision to tolerate a falsification, and the dial was never there.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The test works only once the description is settled, and that is usually where the real argument lies. Is this borrowing, or theft? A tactful omission, or deceit? The principle cannot settle those questions for you. And Aristotle\u2019s own list reflects his society; some of its entries would be argued over today.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of a compromise you were offered recently. Was it a question of how much, or of whether?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which is a misuse of the doctrine of the mean?',options:['Treating a proven bribe as a matter of how much to tolerate','Debating how sharply to answer a rude remark in a meeting','Asking how generous to be to a friend who is struggling','Weighing how much risk to take on with a new venture'],answer:0,why:'A bribe is not too much of something good. Treating it as a dial setting assumes it admits of degree, which it does not.'}]}},'nicomachean-ethics_8':{pictureCaption:'The stone has left the hand and is gathering others as it falls. Letting go was a choice; calling it back is not.',bridge:'Idea 1 said character is built by repeated acts. This one draws the consequence: so you are answerable for the character too.',basics:{title:'Is it just how you are?',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'A STONE DOWN A HILL',picture:!0,text:'Picture letting go of a stone at the top of a steep hill. Once it is rolling, you can\u2019t call it back. But letting go was up to you. Aristotle, a thinker in Greece long ago, said habits work a bit like that.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'LATE AGAIN',text:'Sam is late for school most days. Every morning he says sorry and promises to be on time. Every morning he is late. Now he says, \u201cThat\u2019s just how I am.\u201d'},{kind:'idea',text:'Your habits come from lots of small choices. So you can\u2019t fix a habit in one moment. But you can change the small choices that build the next one.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where Sam can fix it',text:'By the time he is running for the bus, it is too late. Trying harder then won\u2019t help much. The fix is earlier: packing his bag the night before, and going to bed on time. Small choices, made again and again.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW A HABIT GROWS',nodes:['A small choice','Made again and again','It becomes a habit','The habit steers the next choice'],loop:!0,note:'To change the habit, change the small choice early on.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of one thing that keeps going wrong for you. Don\u2019t just promise to try harder at the bad moment. Find one small step earlier that you could change.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Ella keeps forgetting her PE kit. Saying sorry each time hasn\u2019t worked. What would help most?',options:['Packing her kit the night before, every night','Trying very hard to remember each morning','Deciding she is just a forgetful person'],answer:0,why:'The fix lives earlier, in a small step she repeats. That is how a new habit forms.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE THIRD MISSED DEADLINE',prompt:'You have missed the quarterly deadline three times running. Where is the best place to fix it?',options:['Deadline week, by trying harder','The weeks before, in what you do routinely','Nowhere: some people are just like this'],answer:1,reveal:'By deadline week the pattern is largely formed, and willpower is a poor tool against it. Blaming yourself for Thursday is aiming at the wrong target.'},{kind:'idea',text:'You are responsible for your character, because it was formed by repeated acts that were open to you. The leverage is never where the failure becomes visible. It is upstream, in the pattern.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The objection he takes seriously',text:'If character determines action, how can anyone be blamed for acting in character? Aristotle grants much of the premise: by the time it shows, the disposition is largely set. But it was set by acts you chose, again and again, so you share responsibility for it.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'HIS EXAMPLE',text:'A man makes himself ill through a long habit of neglect, ignoring his doctors. He cannot now simply choose to be well. But he could once have chosen differently, many times over. The illness is his doing, though not today\u2019s.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The stone is gone and gathering others. You cannot call it back. But letting go was up to you, and so is the next stone.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHERE TO AIM',left:{title:'At the moment',items:['Try harder on deadline day','Feel bad about Thursday','Willpower against a formed habit']},right:{title:'Upstream',items:['Change one weekly act','Start the work on Monday','Build the next disposition']},note:'More forgiving about the moment. More demanding about the pattern.'},{kind:'try',text:'Stop resolving to try harder next deadline. Choose one repeated act in the weeks before it and change that.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A colleague says: \u201cI\u2019m just disorganised. Blame my nature.\u201d How would Aristotle respond?',options:['Your nature was shaped by habits, and the next ones are still yours','Fair enough: nobody can be blamed for acting in character','Try much harder at the moments when organisation matters','Organisation is a talent, so focus on what you do well'],answer:0,why:'He accepts that character drives action now, then moves responsibility upstream, to the acts that form the next disposition.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'BOOK THREE',text:'Aristotle\u2019s third book asks what is voluntary, and so open to praise and blame. Near its end he meets an objection: everyone pursues what seems good to them, and how things seem depends on character. So, the objector says, no one is responsible for what they pursue.'},{kind:'idea',text:'We are in part the causes of our own dispositions, because they arise from repeated activities that were in our power. So we answer for acting from them, even when, in the moment, we could not act otherwise.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The thrown stone',text:'His image is a stone. Once you have thrown it you cannot get it back, though throwing it was up to you. So with the unjust or self-indulgent person: at first they could have avoided becoming so. Having become so, they cannot simply stop.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The release was voluntary. The fall, and everything it gathers, is no longer in the hand.'},{kind:'explain',title:'No plea of ignorance',text:'He adds that only a thoroughly senseless person could fail to see that states come from repeated activity of the same kind. The person who drifts into a habit cannot plead ignorance of how habits form.'},{kind:'keyline',text:'Responsibility moves from the moment to the pattern: gentler on Thursday, harder on the weeks before it.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The argument is weakest where it matters most. Aristotle himself says habits formed in childhood make all the difference, and children do not choose their upbringing. The acts that formed a disposition were not always available. Used carelessly, the idea blames people for conditions they did not author. It applies to the reach you actually had.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'A critic says: if early habits came from upbringing, responsibility just moves back to the parents. What is the fairest response?',options:['Partly true: responsibility is shared, and grows as your own reach does','Wrong: every adult freely chose every habit they now have','True: so no one is ever responsible for their character','Irrelevant: only the act in the moment can ever be judged'],answer:0,why:'Aristotle says we are partly the causes of our states, not wholly. The more a habit was in your power, the more it is yours.'}]}},'nicomachean-ethics_9':{pictureCaption:'A fine tool, skilled hands, and nothing to cut. Without material, the best tool and the best hands make nothing.',bridge:'Idea 5 judged a life as a whole. This one admits that how the whole goes is not entirely up to you.',basics:{title:'Being good isn\u2019t always enough',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'MATCH DAY',text:'You have trained hard all season. You are a great teammate. Then on match day you wake up with flu. You can\u2019t play. Your team loses without you.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'Does this mean you are a worse player than you thought?',options:['Yes, you let the team down','No, bad luck got in the way'],answer:1,reveal:'Your skill did not change overnight. The flu did not care how hard you trained. Aristotle, a thinker in Greece long ago, saw that luck matters too.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Being a good person is needed for a good life. But it is not enough on its own. You also need some luck: health, a home, enough to live on, and friends.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A great tool and skilled hands. But there is no wood to work on. So nothing gets made.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why this is kind, not sad',text:'Some people say good people always win. That isn\u2019t true, and Aristotle said so. It means that when someone has a hard time, it may not be their fault. And when you have a great time, some of it may be luck.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you judge someone, ask: what is going on for them? Are they ill, or new, or having a hard time at home?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Two kids get very different marks in a test. One was ill all week. What does this idea say?',options:['The marks alone can\u2019t show who worked harder','The higher mark always means the better student','Being ill is no excuse for a low mark'],answer:0,why:'Being ill is bad luck. It can pull a mark down without saying anything about effort.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO COLLEAGUES, TWO YEARS',left:{title:'Hard year',items:['A parent in hospital','A team cut in half','Fewer results']},right:{title:'Easy year',items:['No crises','A full team','More results']},note:'The difference in output is not a clean reading of character.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Good character is necessary for a flourishing life, but not sufficient. Health, resources, friends and luck also matter, and their absence does real damage.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The claim he refuses',text:'Some said a good person is happy even while being tortured. Aristotle calls that nonsense. Virtue needs something to act with and on: friends, means, health. A good person stripped of all of them cannot flourish, whatever their character.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The tool is excellent and the hands are ready. With nothing to work on, neither can show what it is worth.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Saying circumstances matter is just making excuses.',reality:'Aristotle thought the opposite. Pretending conditions do not matter is not toughness or generosity. It is a factual error, and it leads you to misjudge people in both directions.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'YOUR OWN GOOD STRETCH',text:'Your team has had its best year. It is tempting to read that as proof of your quality. Some of it is. Some of it was the market, a strong hire and nobody falling ill. Writing that down is not modesty. It is accuracy.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before you next judge someone\u2019s performance, write down their conditions first. Judge them against those, rather than against each other.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A teacher\u2019s results drop in the year her school loses half its staff. What would Aristotle say?',options:['Her results may fall even if her skill and care have not','Results are results; character always shows in the outcome','Her character must have slipped, since the drop is real','Circumstances never matter to those who are truly good'],answer:0,why:'Conditions are part of the outcome. Character is necessary for good work, not a guarantee of good results.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'ON THE RACK',prompt:'Can a truly good person be happy while being tortured?',options:['Yes, virtue alone is enough for happiness','No, some misfortunes damage even the best life'],answer:1,reveal:'Aristotle says no, and calls those who say yes talkers of nonsense. The Stoics later took the other side, holding that virtue alone suffices. The quarrel runs through ancient ethics.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Flourishing is virtuous activity over a complete life, and activity needs equipment. Character is necessary, but external goods are needed too, and their loss can spoil a life that no character could save.'},{kind:'term',word:'External goods',meaning:'Aristotle divides goods into those of the soul, those of the body, and those outside both, such as wealth, friends and good fortune. Flourishing consists chiefly in the first, but needs the others.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Two kinds of need',text:'Some goods are tools: friends, wealth and power are means by which fine acts get done. Others mar happiness when missing outright. He thinks of a life struck by disaster, like that of Priam, the king of Troy in Homer, who lost his sons and his city in old age.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The tool is excellent and the hands are skilled, but there is nothing to work. Activity needs material, and some of that is luck.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Still not a lottery',text:'Aristotle does not hand happiness over to chance. A good person, he says, bears misfortune with dignity and will never do hateful acts, so cannot become truly wretched. Character still governs how a life is lived. It cannot govern everything that happens in it.'},{kind:'limits',text:'His list of what a full life needs includes things we would reject: he says the very ugly, the low-born and the solitary and childless are hardly happy. That reflects the values of a Greek citizen, not a truth about flourishing. Keep the structure, necessary but not sufficient, and question the inventory.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A company ranks staff by output alone and calls the ranking a measure of character. What is Aristotle\u2019s objection?',options:['Output mixes character with conditions, so it cannot read character cleanly','Output says nothing whatever about how anyone has behaved','Character cannot be judged at all, so no ranking is possible','Only intentions matter, so output should be ignored entirely'],answer:0,why:'Character is necessary to good work but not sufficient. Ranking by output alone credits and blames people for their conditions.'}]}}}},2988,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'notes-on-changing-your-mind_1':{pictureCaption:'Hands busy at the sink, mind somewhere else. This is often where a point made weeks ago finally lands: alone, far from the argument.',basics:{title:'The change comes after the argument',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE BOARD GAME',text:'You and your cousin argue about a board game. You say the best plan is to buy every square you land on. She says it is smarter to save your money. You both get louder. Nobody gives in.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'TWO WEEKS LATER',text:'Your cousin is at home, helping with the dishes. Out of nowhere, she thinks about what you said. Maybe you had a point. She never tells you. But her mind has moved a little.'},{kind:'idea',text:'People almost never change their mind while someone is arguing with them. If they change it, they do it later, on their own, often while doing something else.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why not right away?',text:'Giving in during an argument means losing in front of the other person. That feels bad, and it is not the same as being wrong. So people hold on in the moment. They do their real thinking later.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Washing up, alone, thinking about something else. This is often where a mind finally changes.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you disagree with someone, make your point once, as well as you can. Then stop. Don\u2019t wait for them to say you won.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You tell a friend why the book is better than the film. She just shrugs and says no. What does this idea tell you?',options:['She may still think about it later, on her own','Your reason must have been a bad one','You should keep going until she agrees'],answer:0,why:'People rarely give in while you are still arguing. If the change comes, it comes later, when nobody is watching.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE USUAL CONCLUSION',belief:'Arguing is pointless, because nobody ever changes their mind.',reality:'People do change position. They almost never do it while being argued with. Expecting to win the moment is the main reason people decide that minds never change.'},{kind:'idea',text:'An argument can plant something, but the shift happens later, alone, often during something unrelated. No movement in the room tells you nothing about whether the conversation worked.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the room is the wrong place',text:'Conceding in the moment costs two things. You give up the view, and you lose the exchange in front of the other person. The second is the more expensive. Alone, weeks later, only the first cost is left, so that is when the change tends to happen.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE WEEKLY REPORT',text:'You tell a colleague the team\u2019s weekly report is a waste of everyone\u2019s time. They defend it point by point. A month later they quietly cut it to one page. They never mention your conversation.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW WINNING BACKFIRES',nodes:['You press for a concession','Conceding now means losing in public','They defend the view out loud','Now the view is theirs to protect'],note:'The person who wins the argument often prevents the change they were arguing for.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Nobody here is arguing. Hands busy, mind elsewhere, alone: the setting in which a point made weeks ago tends to land.'},{kind:'try',text:'In your next disagreement, make your strongest case once, clearly. Then stop, and do not ask for agreement. Judge the conversation by whether you made the point well.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You make a careful case to your manager for changing a process. They push back on every point and the meeting ends. What should you conclude?',options:['Nothing yet: if it shifts, it will likely shift later and in private','The case failed, because they conceded nothing in the room','You should book a follow-up and press until they agree','The manager is closed-minded and the case was wasted'],answer:0,why:'No visible movement is no evidence either way. Pressing for agreement would turn a possible private rethink into a position to defend.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE FIRST NOTE',title:'Two things that seem to clash',text:'People plainly do change their minds over time. Yet almost nobody seems to change it while being argued with. The essay\u2019s answer is that both are true, and that the second has misled people about the first.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Belief change rarely happens during the argument. The argument plants something; the shift comes afterwards, alone, sometimes weeks later and often unacknowledged. Expecting to win the moment is what convinces people that minds never change.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO PRICES FOR ONE CHANGE',left:{title:'Conceding in the room',items:['You give up the view','You lose the exchange','The other side watches you lose']},right:{title:'Updating alone, later',items:['You give up the view','Nothing else']},note:'The essay treats these as separate costs, and the second as the more expensive. That is why the change waits.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The uncomfortable corollary',text:'Pressing for acknowledgement turns a possible private update into a public position the person must now defend. So whoever wins the argument, in the sense of forcing a visible retreat, frequently prevents the change they were arguing for.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The kitchen sink, weeks later. No opponent, no audience. On the essay\u2019s account, this, not the argument, is where the shift tends to happen.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The essay offers an observed pattern, not a measured one. It cites no data, and it says *rarely*, not never. Some people do concede on the spot, and the essay\u2019s own reasoning suggests it is easiest on small matters with nobody watching. And the idea can become an excuse: if silence never counts as failure, a weak argument can never be shown to have failed.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of a view you have changed. Where were you when it actually moved, and was the person who argued for it there?'},{kind:'recap',points:['Minds change, but rarely in the argument itself.','Conceding in public costs more than changing your view.','Pressing for a visible win can block the private one.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these misuses the idea?',options:['Repeating a weak argument for years because no response proves nothing','Making a case once and then letting the subject drop','Not reading a friend\u2019s silence as proof the point failed','Skipping the victory lap when a colleague\u2019s view seems to shift'],answer:0,why:'Silence is not evidence of failure, but that does not make every argument immune to failing. A weak case still needs improving.'}]}},'notes-on-changing-your-mind_2':{pictureCaption:'A warm, crowded room, and one person at the door looking out into the cold. Leaving a belief can mean leaving the room that holds it.',bridge:'Idea 1 was about the cost of giving way to an opponent. This one is a different cost: the people on your own side.',basics:{title:'The real reason people don\u2019t change',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE NEW GAME',prompt:'You and your friends all said a new game was rubbish. Then you played it at your cousin\u2019s, and now you think it is great. What makes it hard to say so?',options:['You might be wrong about the game','Your friends expect you to agree with them','You have not played it for long enough'],answer:1,reveal:'Most people are not scared of being wrong about a game. They are scared of what their friends will think. That is the real thing in the way.'},{kind:'idea',text:'What keeps someone stuck on a view is often not the fear of being wrong. It is the people who expect them to keep it.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A TENT POLE',text:'A belief can be like the pole in a tent. Pull it out, and the tent falls on everyone inside. So people leave the pole in, even when it is a bit bent.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A warm room full of people, and one person at the door. Changing your mind can feel like stepping out into the cold.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why more facts don\u2019t help',text:'If the real worry is your friends, more facts will not fix it. Facts answer the question *Am I right?* But the question in the way is *Will they still like me?*'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time someone will not change their mind, ask yourself: who would be upset if they did? The real reason may not be about facts at all.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend keeps saying one band is the best ever, but she seems less sure now. Her big sister loves that band. Why might she not change?',options:['She does not want to let her sister down','She has not heard enough of their songs yet','She cannot tell good songs from bad ones'],answer:0,why:'Often what keeps a view in place is a person, not a fact. Here that person is her sister.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE FAMILY CRITIC',text:'For years your uncle has been the one in the family who says the town\u2019s new bypass was a disaster. You show him figures suggesting it worked. He reads them, nods, and changes nothing.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The fear that keeps a belief in place is usually not about being wrong. It is about the people who expect you to keep holding the view. The belief is load-bearing for a friendship, a job, a family position.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the figures bounced off',text:'You were asking him to update a fact. He would be paying for something else: his role as the family critic, the arguments he has won at dinner, the people who agree with him. That is a bill you cannot see from outside, and more evidence does not shrink it.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'THE ASK AND THE BILL',left:{title:'What you think you are asking',items:['Look at this new figure','Accept one fact is different','A small update']},right:{title:'What it may cost them',items:['A role they are known for','Standing with people who agree','Years of past arguments']},note:'Evidence speaks to the left column. The decision is made on the right.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The room is warm, full and expecting him to stay. The cold is outside the door. That is what a belief can be holding up.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time someone will not move despite good evidence, stop adding evidence. Ask instead who would be disappointed if they changed their mind.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A colleague privately agrees your team\u2019s software is outdated but defends it in every meeting. Their manager chose it. What does the idea suggest?',options:['The obstacle is their manager, not the facts about the software','They need a longer document comparing the two products','You should mention in the meeting that they agreed in private','They must genuinely believe the old software is better'],answer:0,why:'More evidence would not touch the real cost. Seeing who would be disappointed tells you what actually stands in the way.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE MISSING-FACTS VIEW',belief:'People hold on to a wrong belief because they have not yet seen the right evidence.',reality:'The essay argues that evidence so often fails on its own because the obstacle is somewhere else. Changing the view would cost the person something with the people around them.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A belief that will not move is usually being held in place socially, not factually. It is load-bearing for a friendship, a job or a family role, so changing it means paying a bill the persuader cannot see.'},{kind:'term',word:'Load-bearing',meaning:'A builder\u2019s word for a wall that holds up the floors above it. Take it out and more than the wall comes down. The essay borrows it for beliefs that hold up relationships.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What people are actually afraid of',text:'The essay\u2019s claim is about fear. It is usually not fear of being wrong, which a person can survive in private. It is the disappointment of the people who expect them to keep the view. This also explains why the private update of idea 1 so often goes unannounced: announcing it is what sends the bill.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHY EVIDENCE BOUNCES',nodes:['New evidence arrives','Accepting it changes a public view','Other people rely on that view','The view is defended instead'],note:'The evidence answers a question that was never the one in the way.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The room behind him expects him to stay; only the doorway is lit. On the essay\u2019s account, leaving a belief is often leaving a room.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Social cost is not always the obstacle. Some people simply weigh the evidence differently, and treating every disagreement as social pressure is a way of never engaging their reasons. Asking who would be disappointed can also slide into condescension: diagnosing the other person instead of answering them. The essay says *usually*, and offers an observation, not a study.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A researcher has defended one theory for a whole career and now faces strong contrary results. What does the idea lead you to expect?',options:['Resistance driven partly by colleagues, students and a reputation built on the view','Quick acceptance, since researchers are trained to follow the evidence','Resistance only if the new results are weaker than they look','No resistance at all once the full data have been published'],answer:0,why:'On the essay\u2019s account, the more people rely on someone holding a view, the bigger the hidden bill for changing it. Training in evidence does not cancel that bill.'}]}},'notes-on-changing-your-mind_3':{pictureCaption:'One brick eased out of a wall. Nothing has fallen yet, but every brick above it now rests on a little less.',bridge:'Idea 2 showed why a whole position is costly to give up. This one shows how it usually goes instead: a small piece at a time.',basics:{title:'Big changes start with a small yes',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'THE BLOCK TOWER',text:'Think of the game where you pull blocks out of a tower. You don\u2019t pull a block that holds everything up. You take one loose block that slides out easily. Then the whole tower gets a bit wobbly.'},{kind:'idea',text:'People almost never flip from sure to the opposite in one go. First they agree to one small point. Then the rest of the view slowly starts to come loose.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'ALL HOMEWORK IS USELESS',text:'Your friend says all homework is useless. You don\u2019t argue with all of it. You ask about the spelling list she learned before the test she did well on. She says, okay, that one helped. That small yes is the start.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the small point works',text:'A small point is easy to give. It does not feel like losing. But once she says one bit of homework helped, *all homework is useless* does not quite fit any more. She has to sort that out in her own head.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One brick slides out. The wall still stands. But the bricks above it now have less to sit on.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you disagree, don\u2019t go for the whole thing. Ask about one small point you really think is true. Then leave it there.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend says every old film is boring. What is the best first step?',options:['Ask if one old film he liked was boring','Tell him he is wrong about all of them','Show him a long list of great old films'],answer:0,why:'One small yes costs him nothing. After it, the big claim no longer quite fits.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'WHERE TO AIM',prompt:'A friend is sure that working from home ruins teams. You think they are wrong. Where is a change of mind most likely to start?',options:['With their main claim, argued hard','With one small point they can afford to give','With the most dramatic counterexample you know'],answer:1,reveal:'The main claim is the most defended part, and the most tied to who they are. A narrow point, one exception, costs nothing to concede. Positions tend to loosen from there.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Almost nobody goes from certain to reversed. A person first concedes a narrow point they can afford, and the structure comes apart from there. The small concession is not a trick. It is how a position actually loosens.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW A POSITION LOOSENS',nodes:['A narrow point is conceded','It clashes with the main view','The clash needs resolving','Each new concession makes the old view harder'],note:'Nobody attacked the main claim. It loosened from underneath.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the centre is the wrong target',text:'The central claim is the most defended part of a position and the most tied to identity. Attack it and you get a defence. The weakest supporting claim is different: offered so it costs nothing to accept, it can be given up without losing anything.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'ONE TEAM, ONE EXCEPTION',text:'Your friend concedes that one team they know works well from home. A small point. But now *working from home ruins teams* has an exception, and they have to decide what it means. Over months, the claim may become *it depends on the team*.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One brick eased out of the wall. Nothing has fallen, but the bricks above now rest on less. Positions loosen like this.'},{kind:'try',text:'In your next disagreement, ask for the narrowest agreement you would genuinely accept, not the whole position. Then let it sit.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A colleague insists that every meeting is a waste of time. Which opening follows the idea?',options:['Ask whether last week\u2019s planning meeting saved anyone time','Explain why meetings are essential to any working team','List every meeting this quarter that went well','Leave it, since nobody gives up a strong view'],answer:0,why:'It asks for one narrow, cheap concession. The others aim at the central claim, or give up on the slow mechanism that actually works.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'WHAT A POSITION RESTS ON',word:'Supporting claim',meaning:'A smaller belief that a main position rests on. *Working from home ruins teams* might rest on *people slack at home* and *ideas need corridors*. Each can be questioned on its own.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Belief change typically starts with conceding a minor point, not the central one. The concession is cheap, but it creates an inconsistency the person has to resolve, and each one makes the old position harder to keep.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The mechanism',text:'A cheap concession does not stay cheap. The person now holds two things that do not fit, and resolving that in favour of the old view gets harder as concessions accumulate. Nothing is forced on them. The pressure comes from their own need to make their views fit together.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO TARGETS',left:{title:'The central claim',items:['Most defended','Tied to identity','Conceding it is a defeat']},right:{title:'The weakest support',items:['Least defended','Rarely part of who they are','Conceding it costs nothing']},note:'The essay\u2019s advice follows directly: aim where the cost of agreeing is lowest.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What it explains',text:'The essay says plainly that this is slow. It is also why arguments carried on for years between people who know each other tend to work, while single exchanges rarely do. A small concession needs time, and a next conversation, to become anything.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One brick out of the wall. The essay\u2019s picture of change: nothing falls at once, but whatever sat on that brick is now less secure.'},{kind:'limits',text:'A small concession can also be absorbed. People often fence off an exception (*that team is unusual*) and keep the central claim whole, so the loosening is a tendency, not a law. The method can also be turned into a trick: collecting small yeses to corner someone. The essay\u2019s version asks only for agreement you would genuinely accept.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which use of the idea departs from the essay?',options:['Lining up small agreements to trap someone into a conclusion','Asking a friend to grant one exception, then leaving it','Questioning the weakest support for a view, not the view','Keeping a slow disagreement going for years with a friend'],answer:0,why:'The essay says plainly that the small concession is not a trick. Used as a trap, it makes each concession expensive, and people defend accordingly.'}]}},'notes-on-changing-your-mind_4':{pictureCaption:'A stile built into the fence: a way to the other side without tearing anything down in front of everyone.',bridge:'Ideas 1 and 2 named what changing your mind costs. This one is about lowering that cost for somebody else.',basics:{title:'Leave them a way out',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'OWNING UP',belief:'If someone changes their mind, they should have to say, \u201cI was wrong.\u201d',reality:'Asking for that makes changing much harder. Many people would rather keep a view than say they were wrong out loud. So let them change without saying it.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE SCIENCE PROJECT',text:'Your project partner wanted to build a volcano. You wanted a weather chart. Now she sees the volcano will not fit on the table. She says, \u201cOkay, the chart might be better.\u201d'},{kind:'explain',title:'What you say next matters',text:'If you say, \u201cHa! I told you so,\u201d she may dig in and say the volcano is fine after all. If you say, \u201cYes, the table is tiny,\u201d she can switch and still feel okay.'},{kind:'idea',text:'It is much easier to change your mind when no one makes you say you were wrong. Giving someone a way out is not a lie. It just takes away the shame.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Honest ways out',text:'You can say: *That\u2019s new.* Or: *Things have changed.* Or: *You were right to worry about that part.* All three can be true. None of them makes your friend say they were wrong.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A fence with some steps built in. You can climb over without breaking the fence while everyone watches.'},{kind:'try',text:'When a friend comes round to your view, say nothing about being right. Just carry on together.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend finally agrees your way to the park is faster. What is the best thing to say?',options:['\u201cGreat, let\u2019s go that way, then.\u201d','\u201cSee? I told you I was right.\u201d','\u201cSay you were wrong first.\u201d'],answer:0,why:'Saying you were right makes changing feel like losing. Letting it go makes it easy.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE ENERGY TARIFF',text:'For a year your housemate has insisted on a fixed energy tariff. Prices drop and the fixed deal now costs more. You could say *I told you so*. Or you could say *prices have moved, it might be worth switching*. Only one of those is likely to end with the switch.'},{kind:'idea',text:'People change position far more easily when they can do it without admitting they were wrong. A face-saving route is not dishonesty. It removes the humiliation tax that keeps people defending things they no longer believe.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'OPEN OR CLOSED',left:{title:'Honest ways out',items:['New information arrived','Circumstances changed','Right concern, wrong conclusion']},right:{title:'What closes them',items:['Demanding the admission first','Reminding them who was right','Making the switch a public event']},note:'Every route on the left is true. That is what separates it from flattery.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why demanding the admission backfires',text:'Insist that someone says they were wrong before you accept their new view, and you raise the price of changing above the price of keeping the belief. They will keep the belief. Not because they still hold it, but because it is now the cheaper option.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A stile in the fence: a way across that leaves the fence standing. The person can cross without anyone watching it come down.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The cost falls on you',text:'The uncomfortable part is for the persuader. You have to let the other person arrive at your position without credit, and often without acknowledging you were involved at all.'},{kind:'try',text:'When someone is coming round to your view, say nothing about having been right. Offer the honest route instead: the new fact, or the concern they were right about.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A colleague who resisted your plan now says, \u201cWith the new budget, your plan might work.\u201d Which reply applies the idea?',options:['\u201cYes, the new budget changes things. Let\u2019s go ahead.\u201d','\u201cI said that three months ago, but I\u2019m glad you agree.\u201d','\u201cSo you accept your original objection was wrong?\u201d','\u201cThe budget is irrelevant. The plan was always right.\u201d'],answer:0,why:'They have taken an honest route out: changed circumstances. Accepting it costs you only the credit.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'ONE REPLY, TWO OUTCOMES',prompt:'Someone who argued against you for months says they now agree. You reply: so you admit you were wrong? What is the likeliest result?',options:['They confirm it, and the change sticks','They back away and defend the old view again','Nothing changes either way'],answer:1,reveal:'On the essay\u2019s account, demanding the admission makes the change more expensive than the belief. Faced with that price, people keep the belief.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Successful persuasion nearly always includes a face-saving route: a way to change position without conceding you were foolish. Insisting on the admission closes that route.'},{kind:'term',word:'Humiliation tax',meaning:'The essay\u2019s name for the extra cost of changing your mind when doing so means being seen to have been wrong. It is paid on top of giving up the belief itself.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Only honest routes count',text:'The essay limits itself to three. New information: the facts really did change. Changed circumstances: the situation did. Or a reframing in which the person was right about the underlying concern and wrong about the specific conclusion. Each is true, which is why it works without being flattery.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'A FAIR WORRY',text:'A neighbour opposed a new crossing because she feared it would slow traffic. Months later she concedes it has made the road safer. Her route out: the worry about traffic was fair; the conclusion that the crossing was a mistake was not. She keeps her reasons and drops the verdict.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A fence with a stile. Crossing leaves the fence standing, which is the point: nobody had to tear down their position in public to get over.'},{kind:'limits',text:'A way out can be stretched into never saying anything was wrong. Where a mistake did real harm, someone may need to own it, and endless face-saving can shield people from accountability. And the cost lands on the persuader, who must give up the credit. That is easy advice to give, and hard to take when you were right and paid for it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HONEST OBJECTION',prompt:'Someone objects that offering a way out is just flattery. What is the essay\u2019s best answer?',options:['The routes it allows are true: new facts, new circumstances, a fair concern','Flattery is acceptable whenever it gets someone to the right view','People who change their minds deserve to be praised for it','A way out matters more than whether the reason given is honest'],answer:0,why:'The essay allows only honest routes. They work because they remove the humiliation, not because they deceive.'}]}},'notes-on-changing-your-mind_5':{pictureCaption:'One line written on a card and pinned up before anything has happened. Written now, it costs almost nothing to write honestly.',bridge:'The first four ideas were about other people\u2019s minds. The last turns to your own, before any argument starts.',basics:{title:'Decide now what would change your mind',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE BEST GOALKEEPER',text:'You think your friend Leo is the best goalkeeper in your year. Before the season starts, you write: *If he lets in more goals than Ava this term, I\u2019ll think again.* It takes ten seconds. Nobody is arguing with you yet.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Decide what would change your mind before anyone argues with you. Write it down. Later, in the middle of an argument, it is almost impossible to be honest about it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why before is easier',text:'Before an argument, nothing is at stake. You can be fair. In an argument, any test you name can be used against you. So it is very hard to name one.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHEN TO WRITE IT',left:{title:'Before',items:['Nobody is arguing','Easy to be honest','Takes ten seconds']},right:{title:'During',items:['You are defending','Every test feels like a trap','Almost impossible']}},{kind:'picture',caption:'One short line on a card, pinned up before anything happens. Now it is there to check later.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one thing you feel sure about. Write: *I would change my mind if\u2026* Add the date. Keep it somewhere safe.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Mia is sure her bean plant grows faster with music. When should she decide what would prove her wrong?',options:['Before she tests it','After she sees how it grew','When her brother says she is wrong'],answer:0,why:'Before the test, she has nothing to defend. After it, she might bend the rule to fit what she hoped.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE NEW MANAGER',text:'You are sure the new manager will make the team worse. Over a drink, a friend asks what would change your mind. Every answer you think of sounds like handing them ammunition, so you say nothing would.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Decide what evidence would change your mind before you need it. Written in advance, while you are not invested, it is a cheap sentence. Written while defending the view, it is close to impossible to write honestly.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'THE SAME SENTENCE, TWICE',left:{title:'Written in advance',items:['No position under attack','Nothing at stake','The answer can be honest']},right:{title:'Written mid-argument',items:['Any condition is a target','Any concession is a retreat','The answer bends to defend']}},{kind:'example',kicker:'WHAT IT LOOKS LIKE',text:'Written in January: *I will change my mind about the new manager if, by June, the team hits more deadlines than last year and nobody good has left.* Dated, specific, checkable. Now June can decide, not your mood in June.'},{kind:'explain',title:'It also sorts your beliefs',text:'Sometimes you try to write the condition and cannot. A view with no possible disconfirming observation is not really a factual claim. Better to discover that about one of your positions in private than in public, while defending it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One line on a card, pinned at eye level while the wall is still bare. Nothing has happened yet, which is why the line can be honest.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick a current conviction. Write the observation that would falsify it, and date it. Put it somewhere you will see it again.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You believe a new staff rota will cut overtime. When should you write down what result would show you were wrong?',options:['Now, before the first month\u2019s figures come in','After the first month, once a trend shows','Only if a colleague openly challenges you','Never, since figures can be read either way'],answer:0,why:'Before the figures arrive nothing is at stake, so the condition can be honest. Afterwards, any condition you name is shaped by what you are defending.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE ESSAY\u2019S LAST MOVE',title:'From them to you',text:'The first four notes are about other people: why they do not change, and what lowers the cost. The last turns inward. Everything the essay says about the pressure of defending a view applies to you too, and there is one point where you can act before that pressure starts.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Writing down now what would change your mind is the one intervention available before any argument starts, and the most useful. In advance it is cheap and honest. During a disagreement it is nearly impossible.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHY LATER IS TOO LATE',nodes:['You hold a view','Someone challenges it','Any condition you name becomes a target','So you name none'],note:'This is the cost of conceding in public from idea 1, turned on yourself.'},{kind:'explain',title:'A filter, not just a promise',text:'The exercise also sorts your beliefs. If you cannot write any observation that would count against a view, the essay argues it is not a factual claim at all. That is far more useful to find out in private than mid-argument, where the discovery would feel like losing.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'A WORKED CONDITION',text:'View: *working from home is making our team slower.* Condition: *if delivery times over the next two quarters match last year\u2019s, I drop this.* The second sentence is dull and specific. That is its strength: it can be checked without anyone\u2019s pride being involved.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A line on a card, pinned to a bare wall long before anything happens. The essay\u2019s practice: set the test while you have no stake in passing it.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Not every belief has a clean disconfirming observation. Values and judgements of taste usually do not, which makes them a different kind of claim, not a worthless one. A condition can also be written too vaguely ever to trigger, or quietly rewritten when the evidence arrives. The date makes that harder, not impossible.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Decide what would change your mind before you need to.','In advance it is cheap. Mid-argument it is nearly impossible.','A view with no such condition is not a factual claim.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these defeats the purpose of the exercise?',options:['Rewording the condition after the evidence has arrived','Writing the condition before anyone has challenged you','Finding that one of your views has no possible condition','Dating the card so that you can check it later on'],answer:0,why:'The value lies in setting the test before you have a stake in the result. Rewriting it afterwards brings back the very pressure it was meant to avoid.'}]}}}},2989,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'notifications-you-agreed-to_1':{pictureCaption:'One signature on one sheet, and a long roll of slips running off the desk. The yes was given once. The copies keep coming.',basics:{title:'One tap, hundreds of pings',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'A NEW GAME',text:'You get a new game. A box pops up: *Allow alerts?* You want to start playing, so you tap yes. It takes one second, and you forget it.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'Say the game sends you two alerts a day. How many is that in a year?',options:['About 20','About 200','About 700'],answer:2,reveal:'Two a day for a year is over 700 pings. You said yes once. The game keeps going all year, until you say no.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Saying yes to alerts is not one choice. It is an order that keeps running. Each ping can break into your day, at times you never picked.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The hand signs just once. The long roll keeps running off the desk. That is what one yes to alerts looks like.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A KNOCK ON THE DOOR',text:'Imagine telling a friend, *Knock on my door any time you like.* You said it once. Now they knock at dinner, in the middle of homework, and at bedtime.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why so many pile up',text:'You tapped yes when you were busy and in a hurry. You were not thinking about the hundreds of pings to come. So most alerts on a phone were never really chosen. They just piled up, one quick tap at a time.'},{kind:'try',text:'With a grown-up, look at the alerts on a phone or tablet. For each one, ask: would I say yes to this today?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A new app asks if it can send you alerts. What is the best way to think about it?',options:['Think how often it will ping, not just about this one tap','It is only one tap, so it hardly matters','Say yes, since alerts only come when they matter'],answer:0,why:'The tap is quick. The pinging goes on and on. Count the pings before you say yes.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'ONE TAP AT SETUP',prompt:'You install a delivery app and allow its notifications. If it sends three a day, how many interruptions has that tap bought you over two years?',options:['About 200','About 700','About 2,000'],answer:2,reveal:'Three a day for two years is over 2,000. You made one decision, in a second, and it has been carried out two thousand times, each at a moment you did not choose.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Enabling a notification is not one choice. It is a standing instruction to interrupt you, made once in the abstract and carried out dozens of times a day, in moments you never saw coming.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'When you decide',items:['One tap','During setup, in a hurry','The cost is invisible']},right:{title:'When it runs',items:['Dozens of times a day','In the middle of real moments','The cost is paid every time']},note:'The decision and its consequences happen in different places, so the decision never feels what it costs.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why most were never decided',text:'Most enabled notifications were not really decisions. They were defaults accepted during setup, when you only wanted to get into the app. So the state of your phone reflects a hundred low-attention taps, not any considered view about what deserves to interrupt you.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'The fix is to be more careful each time an app asks.',reality:'The piece argues for something else. You will always be in a hurry at setup. The reliable fix is a periodic audit: go through the whole list and ask of each one whether you would enable it today.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One signature on one sheet, and a long roll of duplicate slips spilling off the desk. The decision happened once. The copies keep coming.'},{kind:'try',text:'Open your notification settings tonight. For each app, ask: would I switch this on today, knowing how often it fires? Turn off every one where the honest answer is no.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A news app asks to send you alerts. Which way of deciding follows the idea?',options:['Estimate how often it will fire, and whether each one is worth an interruption','Allow it, since you can simply ignore the alerts you do not want','Allow it for now and trust yourself to notice later if it gets annoying','Judge it by how useful the very first alert turns out to be'],answer:0,why:'The price of a notification is every time it fires. An ignored alert still interrupts, and noticing later is exactly what tends not to happen.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHAT THE PIECE IS ABOUT',text:'This Mindroll editorial is about standing agreements: things decided once that keep running without you. Notifications come first because they are the clearest case. The piece sets itself against the usual advice, which is to be more disciplined each time an app asks.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A notification is a standing instruction, not a single choice. It is made once, in the abstract and often as a default, then executed indefinitely in concrete moments. Price it by its firings, not by the tap.'},{kind:'term',word:'Standing instruction',meaning:'An order given once that keeps being carried out until someone cancels it. Whoever gave it does nothing further, and nobody reviews it, so it can outlive the reason it was given.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE ASYMMETRY',nodes:['One tap at setup','Cost invisible, benefit speculative','Runs for years, unreviewed','The phone reflects defaults, not choices'],note:'Each step is reasonable taken alone. The result is a state of affairs that nobody chose.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why an audit, not discipline',text:'Install time is the worst moment to judge. You want the app, the cost cannot be seen yet, and the benefit is a guess. Later you know how often it fires and whether you ever acted on one. So the piece moves the decision to a periodic review, where that knowledge exists.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Counting firings is not the whole price. One alert a year, a fraud warning or a message from someone who needs you, can be worth more than a thousand others, so frequency must be weighed against value. And the audit is not done once: new apps keep arriving with new defaults, so it has to be repeated to keep working.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which notification has fired most often on your phone this month? Would you have agreed to that many interruptions if asked for all of them at once?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'Which of these has the same structure as an enabled notification?',options:['An email digest you signed up for at a conference years ago','A parcel you ordered once and have now received','A meeting you called once to settle a single problem','A book you bought last year and have not read yet'],answer:0,why:'The digest was decided once and keeps executing without review. The others happened once and stopped.'}]}},'notifications-you-agreed-to_2':{pictureCaption:'Every switch on this wall was set before anyone came into the room. The hand reaching up is the first to touch any of them.',bridge:'Idea 1 said most notifications were defaults accepted at setup. This one asks who set those defaults, and why.',basics:{title:'Who switched them on?',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A NEW TABLET',prompt:'You set up a new tablet. Who decided which apps can ping you?',options:['You did, by picking each one','Mostly the people who made the apps'],answer:1,reveal:'Most apps start with their alerts on, or ask at setup while you are tapping yes to get going. The choice was mostly made before you got there.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Once you tap yes, most apps switch on nearly all their alerts. That is on purpose. The apps want your time, and their makers know many people never change the first setting.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Every switch on this wall was flipped before anyone came in. The hand is the first to touch them.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why would they do that?',text:'Lots of apps make money when you look at them. Each ping pulls you back in. And app builders know a simple fact: lots of us leave a setting the way we find it.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE SCHOOL DINNER',text:'Imagine school dinner comes with chips unless you ask for salad. Most kids end up with chips. Not because they all chose chips. Because chips was the plan unless you spoke up.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'If my phone pings all day, it means I have no self-control.',reality:'Not really. The pings were set up before you got there. Turning them off is not beating your own weakness. It is saying no to a plan someone else made.'},{kind:'try',text:'With a grown-up, turn off all the alerts for one game. After a week, turn back on only the ones you truly missed.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A new game starts with every alert switched on. What does that tell you?',options:['Its makers chose that, and you can choose for yourself','Those must be the alerts you will need most','You should leave them, since that is normal'],answer:0,why:'The setting was picked by the people who made the game. It is where they want you to start, not advice.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE USUAL STORY',belief:'Too many notifications is a personal discipline problem.',reality:'The piece says you are not fighting your own weakness. You are declining an arrangement that was set up without you, by people who know what defaults do.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Notifications default to on because attention is the product, and defaults strongly shape what people end up with. Switching them off is not self-control. It is declining terms you never set.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What defaults do',text:'The piece calls this well established. Signing people up by default greatly increases how many join pension schemes and register as organ donors. But whether it raises actual donations is more contested. And the effect holds even when the choice is in plain view. People can see the option and still often leave it where it was.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'NOT AN OVERSIGHT',nodes:['Engagement is the metric','Notifications drive engagement','So they ship switched on','Many people never change them'],note:'Each step is a decision made by people who know how the last step usually goes.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A row of switches, every one set before anyone arrived. The hand reaching up is the first to touch any of them.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How to read a settings screen',text:'It is not a neutral control panel. It is the outcome of a bargain in which one side set every opening position. You accepted them by not acting. Read it that way, and switching things off feels less like a loss.'},{kind:'try',text:'Turn every notification off. Over the next week, switch back on only the ones you notice missing, and write down which they were.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A new app arrives with fifteen kinds of notification, all switched on. What does the idea suggest?',options:['Treat them as the maker\u2019s opening offer and switch them off','Leave them, since the makers know which alerts are useful','Keep them on and practise ignoring the ones you dislike','Switch off only the ones that have annoyed you so far'],answer:0,why:'The defaults reflect what the maker wants from your attention. Starting from off and re-enabling what you miss puts the opening position back with you.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',word:'Default',meaning:'What happens if you do nothing. Every setting has one, and somebody chose it. Because many people leave settings as they find them, choosing the default goes a long way towards choosing what people end up with.'},{kind:'idea',text:'In most apps, notifications ship switched on because attention is the product and defaults determine behaviour. The settings screen is not neutral: it records a negotiation in which one party set every starting position.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The evidence the piece leans on',text:'It calls the power of defaults well established. Enrolling people by default greatly raises pension-scheme membership and organ-donor registration (whether it raises actual donation is more contested), and the effect does not depend on hiding the choice. The piece gives no figures, and its point needs none: defaults move behaviour, and the people who set them know it.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Read as a control panel',items:['Neutral starting settings','Too many alerts means a weak will','Switching off feels like losing something']},right:{title:'Read as a negotiation',items:['Positions set by the other side','Too many alerts means accepted terms','Switching off is a counter-offer']}},{kind:'explain',title:'Why start from off',text:'The piece\u2019s advice inverts the default: turn every notification off, then re-enable only those you notice missing within a week. Missing an alert is evidence that it earns its place. Keeping one because it was already on is only the maker\u2019s choice, carried forward under your name.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Every switch was set before anyone arrived. The hand reaching up is the first moment anyone in the room has decided anything.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The week test has a blind spot. Alerts that matter rarely, a fraud warning or a severe weather alert, will not be missed in a week because they may fire once a year. Exempt those before you start. And not every default is hostile: some are on for your safety. The point is to know who chose, not to assume every choice is against you.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these is a misuse of the idea?',options:['Switching off an emergency alert because it never fired all week','Turning off a shopping app\u2019s alerts that arrived switched on','Reading a crowded settings screen as the maker\u2019s opening offer','Keeping a message alert you noticed missing within the week'],answer:0,why:'The week test works for alerts that fire often. A rare, vital alert will never be missed in a week, so it has to be judged on its value.'}]}},'notifications-you-agreed-to_3':{pictureCaption:'The project\u2019s plans are off the walls and the shelves are bare, but the jug is filled for the weekly meeting all the same.',bridge:'Idea 1 was one person\u2019s standing yes. A recurring meeting is a standing yes shared by a group, which makes it harder to stop.',basics:{title:'Why the Friday practice never stops',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'FRIDAY, AGAIN',text:'Your class started a Friday practice to get ready for the school play. The play was in March. It is now June. Everyone still turns up every Friday. Nobody is quite sure why.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why nobody stops it',text:'To keep it going, nobody has to do a thing. To stop it, someone has to stand up and say, *Can we stop?* That feels awkward. It can sound like saying the practice was a waste, or the people in it were.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Things that happen every week keep going on their own. Stopping them takes someone willing to speak up. So when you start one, give it an end date.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The plans are gone from the walls. The shelves are empty. But the water jug is still filled for the meeting, like every week.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Keeping it going',items:['Nobody has to do anything','Nobody feels awkward']},right:{title:'Stopping it',items:['Someone has to speak up','It can feel rude']},note:'That is why weekly things pile up and almost never go away by themselves.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A LIBRARY BOOK',text:'A library book comes with a return date. If you still need it, you renew it. Without the date, books would sit on shelves at home for years, long after anyone read them.'},{kind:'try',text:'Starting a club or a weekly game with friends? Agree when it ends: *Let\u2019s do this until half-term.* Then decide together if you want more.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friends start a weekly game every Saturday. What would this idea tell you to do at the start?',options:['Agree a date to stop, and renew it if you all want','Keep it going until someone gets fed up and says so','Never start weekly things, since they never stop'],answer:0,why:'With an end date, stopping takes no awkward speech. If you all still love it, you just say yes again.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'TUESDAY, 10 A.M.',text:'A weekly check-in was set up for a product launch. The launch happened two years ago. Six people still meet every Tuesday at ten. Each of them privately suspects it could go, and none of them has said so.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A recurring meeting has no expiry and no review, so it lasts until someone pays the social cost of cancelling it. Give it an end date when you create it.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'THE ASYMMETRY',left:{title:'Creating it',items:['One invitation','Nobody has to agree out loud','Says nothing about anyone']},right:{title:'Cancelling it',items:['A conversation','Someone has to go first','Implies the hour was wasted']},note:'Creation is cheap and cancellation is expensive, so the number of recurring meetings only ever grows.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the reason ends and the meeting does not',text:'The original reason usually had a duration: a launch, a problem, a new team. But nobody is accountable for noticing that the reason has ended. So the meeting carries on in silence, and the silence is read as agreement.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Pale patches where the launch plans hung, and bare shelves. The reason has gone, yet the table is laid and the jug filled for Tuesday.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The fix is structural',text:'Give every recurring invitation an end date when you send it. Then continuing needs a positive decision, and stopping needs nothing. Renewing a meeting people value costs one click. Ending one that nobody values costs nothing at all.'},{kind:'try',text:'Find one recurring meeting you organise. Add an end date three months out. If it still matters then, renewing it will take seconds.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A weekly sync you set up for a system migration is still running a year after the migration finished. Which response follows the idea?',options:['Cancel it, and put an end date on any recurring invite you send next','Keep it, in case a new problem turns up that needs the slot','Wait until somebody else in the group suggests stopping it','Cut it to fifteen minutes, so that it costs everyone less'],answer:0,why:'The reason ended, so the meeting should too. An end date at creation stops the next one from outliving its purpose.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'A CASE',text:'A team sets up a weekly hour to support a new hire through their first months. The hire is now senior. The hour continues. Nobody decided to keep it, and nobody decided to stop it. It is the shape idea 1 found in notifications: decided once, still executing.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A recurring meeting persists until someone pays the social cost of cancelling it. Creation costs one invitation and cancellation costs a conversation, so the population only grows. The fix is an expiry date set at creation.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why cancelling costs more',text:'Creating a meeting implies nothing about anyone. Cancelling one implies a judgement: that the hour was not worth it, and perhaps that the people in it were not either. So the cost is social rather than practical, and it falls on whoever speaks first.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'OUTLIVING THE REASON',nodes:['A reason with a duration','A meeting without one','The reason ends quietly','Nobody pays to cancel'],note:'The mismatch is built in at creation, which is why the piece fixes it there.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The reason has ended quietly: plans off the walls, shelves cleared. The meeting has not. Its jug is filled and its glasses set, as every week.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Flipping who has to act',text:'An expiry date reverses the burden. Continuing now needs a positive decision, and silence ends the meeting instead of preserving it. It is the same move as starting notifications from off in idea 2, applied to a calendar.'},{kind:'limits',text:'An end date moves the burden; it does not remove it. A valuable meeting can lapse because everyone assumed someone else would renew it. Some rhythms are useful because they are steady, and the reluctance to cancel sometimes reflects real value, such as the only hour a team talks. Expiry forces the question. It does not answer it.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which recurring commitment in your week would nobody now create from scratch, yet nobody has cancelled?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'A colleague objects: \u201cIf a meeting were useless, someone would have cancelled it.\u201d What is the best reply?',options:['Cancelling has a social cost that continuing lacks, so useless meetings survive','Most people cannot tell whether a meeting is useful until it is gone','Useful meetings are in fact cancelled more often than useless ones','Meetings only end when the organiser leaves, whatever their value'],answer:0,why:'The objection assumes cancelling is free. The piece\u2019s point is that it costs a social act that continuing never does, so survival proves very little.'}]}},'notifications-you-agreed-to_4':{pictureCaption:'Today\u2019s box is on the step. Behind the door, the earlier ones stand stacked and unopened. Each arrived without anyone deciding again.',bridge:'Idea 3 showed a meeting that lasts because stopping takes an act. A subscription has the same shape, with money instead of time.',basics:{title:'Paying again and again',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'POCKET MONEY',text:'Say you pay \xa32 a month for extra lives in a game. You signed up once, in the summer. By winter you hardly play. The \xa32 still goes out every month.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Paying every month is a yes that keeps running, just like an alert. You decide once. Then the money goes out each month, and you stop seeing it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Today\u2019s box sits on the step. Behind the door, the old ones are piled up, never opened. Nobody said yes again. They just kept coming.'},{kind:'equation',kicker:'SMALL ADDS UP',parts:['\xa32 a month','12 months'],result:'\xa324 a year',note:'Three small ones like that make \xa372 a year. Would you hand that over in one go?'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why stopping is harder',text:'Starting took one tap. Stopping can take lots of steps. The app makes money while you pay, so it has little reason to make stopping easy.'},{kind:'try',text:'Ask a grown-up to show you what your family pays for each month. For each one, ask: do we still use this?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A comic comes to your house every month. You stopped reading it ages ago. What does this idea say?',options:['Check it, since it keeps coming until you stop it','It will stop by itself once you lose interest','It is cheap, so there is no point looking'],answer:0,why:'A monthly yes never ends on its own. Only deciding again can stop it.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE STATEMENT',text:'You scroll a bank statement and count eleven monthly charges. Each is small. You recognise most of them. Two you cannot place at all. Together they come to more than you would ever have approved as one purchase.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A subscription is a standing agreement: decided once, executed every month, invisible after the first. Run the same audit on it that you would run on your notifications.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'BUILT IN',left:{title:'Signing up',items:['One click','Done in the moment','Your attention is on it']},right:{title:'Cancelling',items:['Often several steps','Sometimes a phone call','Your attention moved on long ago']},note:'The piece notes the business has little reason to remove this friction. Continuing needs nothing; stopping needs an act.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The sum nobody approved',text:'Each charge sits below the level at which anyone would bother to act. That is the trick of the shape. Ten of them add up to a figure that would never pass as a single decision, but no single decision was ever asked for.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Today\u2019s delivery on the step, and behind the door a stack of earlier ones, never opened. Each arrived without anyone deciding again.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why start with money',text:'The piece notes that this audit is usually easier to face than the attention one. Money has a number on it, so the cost is plain. Practise the audit there, and the same shape is easier to see where the cost is your attention.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pull up a year of statements and list every recurring charge. For each, ask whether it has earned the right to continue, rather than whether cancelling is worth the bother.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You find a \xa36 monthly charge for an app you last opened in spring. What does the idea suggest?',options:['Make it justify continuing, and cancel it if it cannot','Keep it, since \xa36 is too small to be worth the effort','Keep it until you have time to go through all of them','Keep it, since you might well start using the app again'],answer:0,why:'Each charge being too small to act on is exactly how the total grows. The default should be that it must earn its place.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'A COMMON VIEW',belief:'Forgotten subscriptions are just carelessness on the customer\u2019s part.',reality:'The piece argues the design tends to favour continuing. Sign-up is one click; cancelling is often several steps, sometimes a phone call, and the business has little reason to remove that friction. Blaming memory misses the structure.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A subscription has the structure of a recurring meeting: one decision that executes monthly, where continuing requires nothing and cancelling requires an act. The remedy is the same: a periodic review in which each item must justify continuing.'},{kind:'term',word:'Action threshold',meaning:'The size below which a cost does not seem worth acting on. Each monthly charge sits beneath it. The total does not, but the total is never presented, so it never meets the test.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE TOTAL GROWS',nodes:['Each charge below the threshold','None is ever reviewed','New ones are added','A total nobody would approve'],note:'No single step is a mistake. The total is simply never put to anyone as one decision.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How often to look',text:'The piece suggests once a year is frequent enough, and warns that the list is almost always longer than expected. The aim is not monthly vigilance. It is to make continuing a decision again, at intervals, rather than a default forever.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Usage is not always the right test. Some subscriptions work quietly: cloud backup, insurance, a security service. You rarely touch them, and that is the point. Judge those on what would happen without them. And do not read every hard-to-cancel plan as a deliberate trap; from outside you can see the friction, but rarely know the intent.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One new box on the step; a stack like it inside, unopened. Nobody refused them, and nobody agreed to them again either.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'WHERE IT BREAKS',prompt:'An annual review turns up four charges. Which should not be judged mainly by how often you use it?',options:['Monthly cover for your home against flood damage','A streaming service you watched twice this year','A meal-kit plan whose boxes you skip most weeks','A news app you open about once a month'],answer:0,why:'Insurance does its job without being used. The usage test fits services you consume, not protection you hope never to need.'}]}},'notifications-you-agreed-to_5':{pictureCaption:'One card taken out and held under the lamp, looked at properly. The drawer beside it holds the rest, unexamined for years.',bridge:'Ideas 1 to 4 each ended in an audit. This is the one question the audit asks, and why it beats asking whether to cancel.',basics:{title:'Would you start it today?',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TWO QUESTIONS',prompt:'You have gone to chess club every week for two years. You don\u2019t enjoy it much now. Which question helps you see what you want?',options:['Should I quit chess club?','If I had never gone, would I join today?'],answer:1,reveal:'*Should I quit?* makes you think of two years you would be throwing away. *Would I join today?* just asks if you want it now. That one is easier to answer honestly.'},{kind:'idea',text:'For anything that keeps going, ask: would I start this today, knowing what I know now? It is a fairer question than *should I quit?*'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One old card, held up to the light and looked at again. Would you join today? The drawer is full of ones nobody has checked.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why quitting feels so bad',text:'Quitting feels like losing something. You think of all the time you already gave. But that time is gone, whatever you choose. Asking *would I start today?* leaves it out and looks only at now.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A SHOP SHELF',text:'Imagine the club sat on a shop shelf with a price tag: one evening every week. Would you pick it up and buy it? If not, ask why you keep paying for it.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Use it on anything',steps:['Pick one thing you do every week.','Ask: would I start this today?','If yes, keep it, and enjoy it.','If no, talk it over with a grown-up.']},{kind:'question',prompt:'You have collected stickers for a year, but now you are bored of it. Which question is the one this idea gives you?',options:['If I were starting now, would I begin this?','How much have I spent on stickers so far?','Would my friends think I just gave up?'],answer:0,why:'This question looks only at now. The money you spent is gone, whatever you decide.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE GYM, MONTH NINETEEN',text:'You joined a gym nineteen months ago at a good price. You have been four times since Easter. Each time you think about cancelling, you think about how much you have already paid, and how you will surely start going again.'},{kind:'idea',text:'For each standing agreement, ask whether you would set it up today, at this price, knowing what you now know. It is a harder question than whether to cancel, and a fairer one.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'SAME GYM, TWO QUESTIONS',left:{title:'Should I cancel?',items:['Recalls what you have already paid','Cancelling feels like a loss','Tempts you to say not yet']},right:{title:'Would I start today?',items:['Looks only at the current price','Not starting feels like nothing','Judged on its merits']},note:'The two questions can give different answers about the same thing. That shows the framing matters, not by itself which answer is right.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it works',text:'Asking whether to cancel invites you to count what has already been spent and what would be lost. Asking whether to start removes the sunk cost. There is nothing behind you, only the price and what you would get for it.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'This is a trick for subscriptions.',reality:'It generalises to memberships, projects, tools, responsibilities and commitments of every kind. Anything that continues without a decision benefits from being put to one now and then, and this is the cheapest way to do it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One membership held up to the lamp and asked the renewal question. The drawer beside it holds the rest, unexamined for years.'},{kind:'try',text:'This week, ask the renewal question of one standing arrangement: a membership, a weekly commitment, a tool you pay for. Write the honest answer down.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You are two years into a side project that no longer excites you. Which question applies the idea?',options:['Knowing what I know now, would I start this project today?','How much of my time is wasted if I stop now?','Can I admit that the last two years were a mistake?','What will people say if I drop it at this point?'],answer:0,why:'The renewal question sets aside what is already spent and asks what the project is worth from here on.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'BEFORE THE ARGUMENT',prompt:'One person is asked whether to cancel a membership. Another is asked whether to take it up today, at the same price. Will they tend to answer alike?',options:['Yes, it is the same decision','No, the cancel question leans to keeping it','No, the start question leans to keeping it'],answer:1,reveal:'The piece argues the two questions can come apart. Cancelling feels like a loss and never starting does not, so the cancel question may lean towards keeping.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Ask of any standing agreement: would I start this today, at this price, knowing what I now know? Unlike asking whether to cancel, it removes the sunk cost and makes continuing a fresh decision.'},{kind:'term',word:'Sunk cost',meaning:'Money, time or effort already spent that cannot be recovered whatever you decide. Since it is gone either way, a sound decision about the future should not depend on it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The piece\u2019s argument',text:'Two questions about the same commitment ought to get the same answer. The piece says they can differ. The gap alone shows only that the framings disagree; the reason to distrust the cancel question is that it brings in what has been spent and what would be lost, while the start question weighs only the current price.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where it sits',text:'Ideas 1 to 4 each end in a periodic audit, of notifications, meetings and subscriptions. This is the audit\u2019s question. The piece extends it to memberships, projects, tools and responsibilities, and calls it the cheapest form of decision available for anything that continues without one.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The audit, one arrangement at a time: this card is out of the drawer and under the lamp. The keys, fobs and cards behind it still wait.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Sunk costs are irrelevant; switching costs are not. Restarting can cost more than continuing: a joining fee, a lost waiting-list place, data to move, an old price no longer offered. So the honest form counts what restarting would cost. And some commitments, to people above all, are not renewable on preference. A promise does not lapse because you would not make it today.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Ask whether you would start it today, at today\u2019s price.','Leave out what is already spent; it is gone either way.','Keep in what restarting would cost.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these is a misuse of the renewal question?',options:['Dropping a promise to a friend because you would not make it today','Cancelling a software plan you would not buy at today\u2019s price','Keeping an old cheap plan that new customers can no longer get','Ending a weekly meeting nobody would set up from scratch now'],answer:0,why:'The question tests arrangements you could freely start again. A promise to a person is not that kind of arrangement, and preference alone does not end it.'}]}}}},2990,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'novum-organum_1':{pictureCaption:'Through the old pane, the window\u2019s straight bars bend and swell. The bars are fine. The glass bent them before anyone looked.',basics:{title:'Your mind is like wavy glass',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'AT THE WINDOW',picture:!0,text:'Hold a piece of very old glass up to a window. The straight bars behind it now look wavy. The bars did not change. The glass bent them on the way to your eye.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Your mind is a bit like that wavy glass. It bends what you see before you even start to think. And it bends things in the same ways, again and again.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Who said this',text:'Francis Bacon was an English thinker. In 1620 he wrote a book called *Novum Organum*. That is Latin for *new tool*. The tool was a new way to find out what is true, built for minds that bend things.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'ONE FOUL, TWO VIEWS',text:'You and a friend watch the same match. You saw a clear foul. Your friend backs the other team, and saw a fair tackle. Neither of you is lying. Each of you saw it through your own glass. We tend to see what we want to be true.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'If I just try hard to be fair, I will see things as they are.',reality:'Trying hard does not fix wavy glass. Bacon said you need a method. That means a set of steps that checks your view from outside your own head.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you and a friend disagree about what happened, ask: what did each of us *want* to be true? That is often where the glass bends.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Two fans watch the same replay and see different things. Neither is lying. What would Bacon say?',options:['Each mind bends what it sees, often towards what it wants','One of them must not be paying attention','The replay itself must be broken somehow'],answer:0,why:'Bacon thought mistakes like this follow a pattern. Look for the bend before you blame the person.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'SAME REPORT, OPPOSITE VERDICTS',prompt:'Two capable colleagues read the same report and reach opposite conclusions. What is the most likely reason?',options:['One of them read it carelessly','Each mind shaped the evidence on the way in','The report is badly written'],answer:1,reveal:'Both read carefully. But each arrived hoping for something and fearing something. Those hopes decided what looked important before either began to reason.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The mind does not reflect the world evenly. It distorts what comes in, like an uneven mirror, and it distorts in patterns. Because the errors are patterned, they can be listed and corrected for.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Two images Bacon uses',text:'Francis Bacon, writing in 1620, compares the understanding to an uneven mirror. It mixes its own shape into everything it reflects. He also says it is no dry light: it is soaked in what we want. We believe more readily what we would prefer to be true.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Error as a failing',items:['Someone was careless','Fix: try harder','Blame the person']},right:{title:'Error as a pattern',items:['Every mind bends this way','Fix: a procedure that checks','Look for the distortion']},note:'Bacon\u2019s move from the left column to the right is where method begins.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why effort is not enough',text:'If the bending happens on the way in, before you start to reason, then reasoning harder starts from bent material. A careful thinker with a warped instrument still gets warped results. So the fix has to sit outside any one mind: a procedure, applied every time.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The bars are straight. The old pane bends them before they reach the eye, and no amount of squinting straightens them.'},{kind:'try',text:'In your next disagreement over shared evidence, ask each side what they hoped it would show, before arguing about what it does show.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A team keeps underestimating how long projects take, though everyone is experienced. What is the response in Bacon\u2019s spirit?',options:['Build in a check, such as comparing each estimate with past projects','Ask everyone to concentrate harder whenever they estimate','Replace the people who gave the worst estimates last time','Accept that estimating is guesswork and stop doing it'],answer:0,why:'A patterned error needs a procedure that corrects for it. Urging people on assumes the problem is effort. Bacon thought it was structure.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'LONDON, 1620',text:'Francis Bacon was Lord Chancellor of England when he published *Novum Organum*. The title answers Aristotle, whose logical works had long been gathered as the *Organon*, the instrument. Bacon offered a new instrument, and he began not with the world but with the flaw in the user: the mind itself.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Error is not mainly a personal failing. The understanding distorts what it receives, like an uneven mirror mixing its own nature with the nature of things, and it does so in patterns that can be catalogued.'},{kind:'term',word:'Idols',meaning:'Bacon\u2019s name for the mind\u2019s standing distortions, the false images it takes for the world. He sorts them into four kinds: of the tribe, the cave, the marketplace and the theatre. The next four lessons take one each.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Distortion on the way in',text:'The mirror puts the error at the point of reception. The will and the affections, what we hope and fear, soak into perception, so we more readily believe what we would prefer to be true. Observation cannot be treated as raw input, and logic applied afterwards inherits the bend.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHY HE WANTS A METHOD',nodes:['The world as it is','Bent on the way in','Careful reasoning on bent input','Confident, wrong conclusions'],note:'Telling people to be objective treats the second step as a lapse of effort. Bacon treats it as structural, so the correction must be procedural.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Pushed too far, the image undermines itself: if every view is bent, so is the critic\u2019s. Bacon\u2019s answer is not that some minds are clean but that procedures can check what no single mind can. And not every disagreement is distortion. Sometimes one side simply has more information, or has made a plain mistake.'},{kind:'recap',points:['The mind bends what it receives, before reasoning starts.','The bends follow patterns, so they can be catalogued.','Patterned error calls for a procedure, not an exhortation.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'A sceptic objects that if all minds distort, Bacon\u2019s own diagnosis must be distorted too. What is the best reply in his spirit?',options:['The cure is a procedure that checks minds from outside, not a cleaner mind','Bacon\u2019s own mind was an exception, being unusually careful and trained','Distortion only affects untrained observers, not scholars who know of it','Since all views are bent, no view can be better than another'],answer:0,why:'Bacon does not claim an undistorted vantage point. He claims that method can correct for distortion that introspection cannot see.'}]}},'novum-organum_2':{pictureCaption:'The dots are scattered at random. The hand has joined them into a familiar shape, and now it is hard to see anything else.',bridge:'Idea 1 said the mind bends what it sees. This is the first kind of bend: the one every human shares.',basics:{title:'Mistakes every brain makes',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE LUCKY SOCKS',prompt:'Sam wore red socks and his team won three games in a row. Now he says the socks are lucky. Is he right?',options:['Yes, three wins proves it','We can\u2019t tell from that'],answer:1,reveal:'Sam remembers the wins in red socks. Did he count the losses in red socks too? Or the wins in blue ones? His brain made a pattern before it checked.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Some mistakes come with being human. We see patterns that are not there. We notice what agrees with us and skip what does not. Francis Bacon called these the *idols of the tribe*.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why \u201ctribe\u201d?',text:'Bacon wrote about this in 1620. An *idol* here means a false picture the mind trusts. By *tribe* he meant the whole human race. These mistakes are not yours alone. Everyone makes them. So there is no one without them to point them out.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The dots are scattered at random. But once the hand draws a shape, it is hard to see anything else.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'A STORY BACON TELLS',text:'Long ago, a man was shown pictures of sailors who prayed in a storm and were saved. People said this proved that praying saves sailors. He asked a simple question. Where are the pictures of the ones who prayed and drowned? No one had painted those.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick something you think is lucky or unlucky. Ask: what would I see if I were wrong? Then go and look for that.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend says the bus is always late when you are in a hurry. What is the best first check?',options:['Count the times it came on time when you were in a hurry','Leave earlier, because the bus is clearly against you','Agree, since you have both seen it happen lots'],answer:0,why:'We remember the late buses because they hurt. The on-time ones slip away. Counting both is the check.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'SIX MONTHS OF NUMBERS',text:'You have found a clear upward trend in six months of sales figures, and built a plan on it. Ask yourself honestly: if the same figures had pointed down, would you have spotted that as quickly, or would it have looked like noise?'},{kind:'idea',text:'Some errors are built into being human, so everyone has them and no one is placed to point them out. Bacon cannot remove them, so his method assumes they are operating and corrects for them.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE LOOP HE DESCRIBED',nodes:['You adopt an opinion','Agreeing cases stand out','Contrary cases are set aside','The opinion feels better supported'],loop:!0,note:'Bacon described this loop in 1620, along with our habit of seeing more order than exists. Psychology now calls it confirmation bias.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'THE PICTURES IN THE TEMPLE',text:'Bacon retells an ancient story. A man is shown pictures, hung in a temple, of sailors who made vows during a storm and survived. Does he now admit the power of the gods? He asks where the pictures are of those who made vows and drowned.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Random dots, one confident line. The shape was supplied by the hand, which is how a trend can be supplied by the analyst.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why \u201cthe tribe\u201d',text:'Bacon\u2019s tribe is the human race. Because these errors are shared, comparing notes does not reveal them: everyone in the room is making the same mistake in the same direction. That is why he asks you to presume them rather than wait to notice them.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before you act on a pattern, write down what you would expect to see if you were wrong. Then check whether you looked for it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A manager notes that every star hire came from one university and wants to recruit only there. What is the first question to ask?',options:['How many hires from that university turned out badly?','Which other universities have famous graduates?','Does the manager simply prefer that university?','How many star hires does the company have overall?'],answer:0,why:'The failures from the favoured source are the missing pictures of the drowned. Without them the pattern cannot be judged at all.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'AN OLD STORY, RETOLD',text:'Bacon borrows an ancient story. A man is shown pictures of sailors who vowed to the gods during a storm and were saved, and is asked whether he now admits their power. He asks where the pictures are of those who vowed and drowned.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The idols of the tribe are errors rooted in human nature itself: seeing more order than exists, favouring confirmations over exceptions, believing what we wish. Being universal, they are invisible, so the safe course is to assume they are at work.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What he got right',text:'Bacon says that once the understanding has adopted an opinion, it draws everything else in to agree, and neglects or sets aside contrary instances even when they outnumber the supporting ones. That is confirmation bias, mechanism included, described centuries before experimental psychology gave it a name.'},{kind:'term',word:'Survivorship bias',meaning:'Judging from the cases that passed some filter, because the rest have vanished from view. The drowned sailors left no pictures, so the evidence on the temple wall was filtered before anyone looked at it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The marks are random; the line is not. Once a shape has been drawn, the eye cannot easily return to the scatter.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Correction, not cure',text:'Some distortions can be exposed by a person who does not share them. These cannot: there is no unaffected observer. So Bacon builds the correction into procedure, and the search for contrary cases happens whether or not anyone feels the need for it.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Pattern-seeking is not simply a defect. A mind that waited for complete evidence before seeing any regularity would learn nothing, and many patterns are real. The idol is the step from noticing a pattern to trusting it unchecked. And naming a bias does not switch it off, which is why Bacon wanted procedure rather than vigilance.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A study of successful start-ups finds that all their founders took big risks. What would the man in the temple ask?',options:['How many failed start-ups had founders who took the same risks?','Whether the founders took the risks before or after succeeding','Which of the founders was the most willing to take a risk','Whether these start-ups are still successful today'],answer:0,why:'The failures are the drowned sailors. Without them, risk-taking cannot be shown to explain success at all.'}]}},'novum-organum_3':{pictureCaption:'The country outside is wide and bright. The figure sees it only through the mouth of their own alcove, and cannot see the walls.',bridge:'The tribe\u2019s errors belong to everyone. The cave\u2019s belong to you alone, which is exactly what makes them fixable.',basics:{title:'Everyone sees from their own cave',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'PASS OR SHOOT?',text:'Your coach says the best players always pass. Your cousin learned from a different coach. She says the best players shoot. Both have played for years. Both are sure. Each learned it from the first person they looked up to.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Each of us thinks from inside a private cave. It is built from how we were raised, what we read and who we looked up to. From inside, we can\u2019t see its walls.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'This person sees the hills only through the mouth of their own cave. From inside, the walls are hard to notice.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Not the same as the tribe',text:'Francis Bacon wrote about this in 1620. The *tribe* mistakes are ones every human makes. The *cave* mistakes are yours alone. That is good news. Someone outside your cave can see what you miss.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE YOUR OWN ACCENT',text:'You can\u2019t hear your own accent. To you it just sounds normal. Someone from another town hears it at once. Your cave is like that. Other people can spot it more easily than you can.'},{kind:'try',text:'Find someone who thinks differently from you about something small. Ask what they see that you might be missing. Then just listen.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You think a book is boring. Your friend loves it. What would Bacon tell you to do?',options:['Ask your friend what they see in it','Decide your friend just has bad taste','Read it again alone until you get it'],answer:0,why:'Your friend sits in a different cave. They can see things your cave hides from you.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'DEADLOCK',text:'You are certain the project needs a detailed plan up front. Your colleague is just as certain it needs a rough start and fast changes. You have both done this for fifteen years. Each of you learned it somewhere early, from someone who impressed you.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Each person reasons from a private cave built from their education, reading and early influences. Two capable people can reach opposite conclusions from the same evidence because they stand in different caves, and neither can see the walls.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO TEMPERAMENTS',left:{title:'Drawn to differences',items:['Sees fine distinctions','Misses the larger likeness']},right:{title:'Drawn to resemblances',items:['Sees the larger likeness','Blurs real distinctions']},note:'Bacon saw a similar split between lovers of the new and lovers of the old. Each leaning has its own typical mistake.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why this one is fixable',text:'The tribal errors are shared, so no one can point them out. Cave errors are personal, which means somebody built differently can see what you miss. Introspection will not find them, because it works from inside the same cave.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A wide, bright country seen through a narrow opening. The person sitting there takes the opening for the view.'},{kind:'try',text:'In your next deadlocked disagreement, ask each side what they saw work early in their career. The answers often explain the deadlock better than the arguments do.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Two senior engineers have argued about the same design choice for weeks. What is the most useful next step?',options:['Ask each what experience taught them their view, then test both','Let the more senior of the two have the final word','Have each write down more arguments for their position','Ask each to reflect privately on their own biases first'],answer:0,why:'Private reflection happens inside the cave. Surfacing where each view came from lets the other person, and the evidence, see the walls.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',word:'Idols of the cave',meaning:'Bacon\u2019s name for the distortions particular to each individual, arising from their nature, upbringing, habits, reading and the people they admire. Each person has a cave of their own that bends and discolours the light.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Beyond the errors all humans share, each mind has its own, shaped by temperament and history. Because they differ from person to person, they are invisible from inside one cave and visible from another.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The borrowed image',text:'The cave nods to Plato, whose prisoners take shadows on a wall for reality. Bacon makes it personal. There is not one cave for humanity but one for each of us, furnished by our own history.'},{kind:'explain',title:'A catalogue of leanings',text:'Some minds are drawn to differences, others to resemblances; some revere antiquity, others love novelty. Each errs in its own direction. People also bend all their thinking to fit the subject they worked hardest at. Bacon\u2019s example is William Gilbert, who studied the magnet with great care and then built a whole philosophy out of it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The country outside is wide; the opening is narrow. The figure sees only what the mouth of their own cave allows.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The remedy',text:'Because cave errors are not shared, a differently formed mind can see them. Bacon adds a rule of his own: hold in suspicion whatever your mind seizes on with particular satisfaction. Both checks come from outside the comfortable view, not from looking harder within it.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Other minds only help if they really are formed differently. A team hired from the same schools shares a cave and calls it consensus. And the idea is easily turned into a weapon: tracing a view to someone\u2019s background explains why they hold it, not whether it is wrong.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these is a *misuse* of the idea?',options:['Dismissing a colleague\u2019s argument because of where they trained','Asking a sceptic from another field to review your plan','Doubting a conclusion you were especially pleased to reach','Asking where your own strongest instincts were first learned'],answer:0,why:'A view\u2019s origin explains why someone holds it, not whether it is true. The cave locates possible distortion; it does not settle the question.'}]}},'novum-organum_4':{pictureCaption:'Blank tokens with nothing on them, traded briskly as if each stood for something. Words can change hands the same way.',bridge:'The cave is private. The marketplace is the opposite: errors we pick up from each other, through the words we share.',basics:{title:'When words trick us',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'A LONG ARGUMENT',text:'You and your brother argue for twenty minutes. Is a hot dog a sandwich? Then you notice something. You never agreed what *sandwich* means. You were not arguing about food at all.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Words help us talk fast. But sometimes a word seems to name a real thing when it doesn\u2019t. Or we each mean something different by it. Then we argue about the word, not the world.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why a marketplace?',text:'Francis Bacon wrote about this in 1620. He called these mistakes the *idols of the marketplace*. A market is where people meet and swap things fast. Our words grew up like that, for quick talk. They were not made for careful thinking.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'These tokens have nothing on them. People still swap them as if each one stood for something. Words can be like that.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'TWO KINDS OF TRICK',text:'Some words name things that are not there at all. People talk about *luck* running out, as if luck were a thing you could use up. Other words name real things, but loosely. Is a friend someone you see every day, or someone you trust?'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you argue, stop and ask: what do we each mean by the main word? Say it in your own words. See if the fight is still there.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Two friends argue about whether chess is a sport. What would Bacon tell them to do first?',options:['Agree what they each mean by *sport*','Count who wins more chess games','Ask a grown-up who is right'],answer:0,why:'Until they agree what *sport* means, they are arguing about a word, not about chess.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'FORTY MINUTES',text:'A meeting spends forty minutes on whether a project counts as *strategic*. Positions harden. Nobody has said what strategic means. By the end it is clear that three people were using three different meanings.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Words are agreed in talk and then treated as though they name real things. Whole disputes turn out to be about a term, not the world. From inside the dispute, nobody can tell.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Arguments about definitions are just pedantry. The real issues lie elsewhere.',reality:'Bacon thought words the most troublesome distortion of all. A badly cut word carries its bad division into every argument that uses it. It corrupts the reasoning without any error of logic.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the marketplace',text:'Bacon locates the problem in exchange, not thought. Words were shaped to suit ordinary understanding and quick talk. They divide the world where a crowd happened to notice a difference. Then we use them for precise inquiry, a job they were never built for.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Blank tokens, traded with confidence. Nothing is printed on them; their worth is only what the traders agree. A loose word works the same way.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Before the next contested meeting',steps:['Name the word the argument keeps returning to.','Ask each person to define it in one sentence.','Compare the definitions out loud.','Only then go back to the question.'],note:'Watch how often the third step ends the dispute.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Two managers have disagreed for weeks about whether a team is *productive*. One counts tasks closed. The other counts customer problems solved. What is going on?',options:['They are using one word for two different things','One of them is reading the numbers wrongly','They need more data before they can decide','The team is productive in some weeks only'],answer:0,why:'Each definition is reasonable. The dispute is about the word, so more data on either measure will not settle it.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE WORST OF THE FOUR',text:'Of his four idols, Bacon singles out those of the marketplace as the most troublesome. The others distort perception or judgement. These enter through language, which every argument has to use, so they travel into reasoning that is otherwise perfectly careful.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Words are fitted to ordinary understanding, so they divide the world where a crowd noticed a joint, not always where one exists. Once in use they are taken for real distinctions, and they corrupt reasoning without any slip of logic.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO KINDS OF BAD WORD',left:{title:'Names of nothing',items:['Words for things that do not exist','His examples include Fortune and the element of fire']},right:{title:'Confused names',items:['Words for real things, badly bounded','His example: *wet*, stretched over many unlike cases']},note:'He thought the second kind harder to remove, because it is rooted in real things.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why logic cannot catch it',text:'A syllogism is built from propositions, propositions from words. If a word lumps together things that differ, every valid step carries the lump forward. The argument checks out and the conclusion is still wrong. This is one reason Bacon distrusted the syllogism as a tool of discovery.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Blank tokens changing hands at speed. Their worth is only what the traders agree, which is the marketplace\u2019s gift and its danger.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Definitions do not settle everything. Some disputes survive a shared definition because they were about the world all along, and insisting on definitions first can become a way of stalling. Nor can inquiry escape ordinary words entirely. Science refines them in use, and its new terms carry new risks.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which word in your work does everyone use, and nobody could define the same way twice?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'Two historians argue whether a medieval kingdom was a *nation*. Each piles up evidence. What is Bacon\u2019s diagnosis?',options:['The dispute may hinge on the word, so define it before weighing evidence','One historian is reading the sources less carefully than the other','Enough further evidence will eventually force one side to concede','Questions about the distant past cannot be settled by any method'],answer:0,why:'If the two mean different things by *nation*, the same evidence can support both. Agreeing the term is what lets the evidence decide.'}]}},'novum-organum_5':{pictureCaption:'The country on stage is painted, and so is the sun. The figure points at it as if it were a window onto real hills.',bridge:'The marketplace hands us words. The theatre hands us whole systems, ready-made and convincing for reasons that have nothing to do with truth.',basics:{title:'Ideas that are just a good show',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'THE SCHOOL PLAY',text:'At a school play, the backdrop shows hills and a bright sun. From your seat it looks real. But it is paint on a board. You can\u2019t walk into those hills, however good they look.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Some big ideas are like a painted backdrop. They explain things so neatly that people take them as true without checking. Francis Bacon called these the *idols of the theatre*.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The hills and the sun are painted. But the man on stage points at them as if they were real.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why a neat idea can fool you',text:'An idea that explains everything feels strong. But think about it. If it fits every single thing that happens, nothing could ever show it was wrong. Then it is not really checking the world at all.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE IDEA THAT CAN\u2019T LOSE',text:'Your friend says kids born in March are bossy. A bossy March kid proves it. A shy March kid is *bossy deep down*. A bossy May kid? *Just copying a March kid.* The idea can never lose. That is the warning sign.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of a rule you have always been told. Ask: has anyone I know ever checked it? What would show it was wrong?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A coach says his plan works for everyone. If you get better, the plan worked. If not, you did it wrong. What is the problem?',options:['Nothing could ever show the plan was wrong','The coach is far too strict with his team','The plan needs more kinds of practice'],answer:0,why:'An idea that wins whatever happens is not being tested. It is a backdrop, not a window.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'A theory that explains everything you notice must be a strong theory.',reality:'Bacon thought the opposite was often true. A framework that fits every observation may have lost touch with evidence. It may have become scenery.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Systems of thought we inherit are adopted whole and defended as if they were observations. Their pull comes from how well they hang together and how complete they are, not from truth. A framework that explains everything is usually weaker than it feels.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE METHODOLOGY',text:'Your team follows a well-known method with a dozen stages. It has a name, a book and a certificate. Ask the room which stages anyone has actually tested, here, against doing without them. The silence is the point.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why systems seduce',text:'A complete system hangs together. It answers every question in its own terms. It saves you from reasoning case by case. All three are comforts, and none is evidence. Bacon likened inherited philosophies to stage plays: worlds of their own making, staged to convince.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A lit backdrop, a painted sun and a figure presenting it as real country. The stage is convincing because it was built to be.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The test',text:'Ask whether any observation could embarrass the framework. If every outcome can be absorbed, success and failure alike, it is no longer describing the world. A useful framework forbids something, and you can say what.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one practice your team follows. Name the result that would show it is not working, then check whether anyone has looked.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A consultant\u2019s model predicts growth. Growth comes, and the model is praised. Growth stalls, and the client is said to have applied it badly. What does Bacon\u2019s idea flag?',options:['The model absorbs every outcome, so it is never tested','The client probably did apply the model badly','The model needs more data to become accurate','Consultants should not be judged on results'],answer:0,why:'A framework that claims success and explains away failure cannot be embarrassed by evidence. That is the mark of a backdrop.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'AGAINST THE SCHOOLS',text:'Bacon was writing against the scholastic philosophy that ruled the universities of his day, built on Aristotle and refined over centuries of commentary. It could answer any question in its own terms. In his view it had added almost nothing to knowledge of nature.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Idols of the theatre are received systems, adopted whole. Bacon likens them to stage plays: invented worlds, convincingly staged. Their completeness is what makes them attractive, and what makes them dangerous.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Even-handed targets',text:'He names three kinds. The sophistical builds on clever argument with little experience, and he puts Aristotle here. The empirical generalises from a few experiments, as the alchemists did. The superstitious mixes theology into natural philosophy. Experiment, his own cause, does not escape the charge.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A painted country, lit from the wings, with a figure presenting it as the real thing. A complete system can be staged just as well.'},{kind:'term',word:'Falsifiable',meaning:'Able to be shown wrong by some possible observation. The word belongs to Karl Popper, three centuries later, but it names the test Bacon\u2019s complaint implies: can anything embarrass this framework?'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why completeness warns',text:'A system that accommodates every finding has nothing at stake. It relieves the holder of reasoning case by case, which is precisely its appeal. Each new fact is absorbed rather than tested, and the framework grows more impressive as it grows less informative.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Every inquirer needs some framework; observation without one is shapeless, and Bacon underrated this. A framework may also rightly survive an anomaly while it is repaired. And the charge is easy to level at any view you dislike. The fair test is to name in advance what would embarrass it, then look.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE DEFENCE',prompt:'A defender says their theory has not been contradicted by a single case in fifty years. Why might Bacon be unimpressed?',options:['It may never have risked a case that could contradict it','Fifty years is too short to test any theory properly','Only ancient theories have earned that kind of trust','A theory should be contradicted now and then to stay fresh'],answer:0,why:'A clean record means something only if the theory forbade outcomes that could have happened. Otherwise it is the record of a stage set.'}]}},'novum-organum_6':{pictureCaption:'A tray of matching pale fish, and a hand lifting out the odd one. One exception tells you more than two dozen that agree.',bridge:'Idea 2 showed the mind drawing everything in to support its opinion. This is Bacon\u2019s counter-move: go looking for what does not fit.',basics:{title:'Hunt for the one that doesn\u2019t fit',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'CATS AND WATER',prompt:'You think all cats hate water. You have seen nine cats run from a bath. What would teach you the most?',options:['Seeing a tenth cat run away','Finding one cat that loves to swim'],answer:1,reveal:'A tenth cat running away adds almost nothing. One swimming cat shows the rule is wrong. And cats like that do exist.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Anyone can find examples that agree with them. The useful thing is to hunt for the one that would prove you wrong. Francis Bacon said to make that hunt on purpose.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why on purpose?',text:'Your mind likes being right. Once it has an idea, it notices what fits and skips what doesn\u2019t. So the hunt for what doesn\u2019t fit won\u2019t happen by itself. You have to plan it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Lots of fish that look the same, and one hand picking out the odd one. That odd one is the one to study.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE PLANT TEST',text:'You think your plant grows best by the window. Bacon would say: put a plant just like it away from the window too. If it grows just as well, your idea is in trouble. That second plant is the real test.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick something you are sure of. Write down one thing that would change your mind. If you can\u2019t think of anything, that is worth noticing.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Mia thinks sweets make her little brother wild. Which test would teach her the most?',options:['Watch him on days with no sweets too','Write down each time he is wild after sweets','Ask her friends if sweets make kids wild'],answer:0,why:'If he is just as wild without sweets, her idea is wrong. Only that test could show it.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'NINE OUT OF NINE',text:'You have a theory about why some customers stay and others leave, and nine examples support it. They came easily, which should worry you. You have not gone looking for the tenth case, the one that would kill it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Any theory can gather confirmations. Bacon\u2019s demand is to hunt for negative instances, the cases that would refute it, and to make that hunt a deliberate procedure rather than an attitude.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Piling up support',items:['Easy to find','Fits many theories at once','Leaves you where you were']},right:{title:'Hunting the exception',items:['Has to be sought','Fits only some theories','Can settle the question']},note:'Ten agreeing cases cannot prove a general claim. One clear exception can sink it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it has to be a procedure',text:'Bacon is blunt that this runs against the grain. A mind pleased with an opinion draws everything else in to support it. Resolving to be open-minded does not change that. A step in the method that requires looking for contrary cases does.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two dozen matching specimens and one that does not match, lifted to the light. The odd one is where the theory gets tested.'},{kind:'explain',title:'His tables',text:'Bacon proposed listing cases where a thing appears, closely similar cases where it does not, and cases where it varies in degree. The second list was the new move: it is where a wrong explanation gets caught.'},{kind:'try',text:'Take a belief you hold strongly. Write down what evidence would change it. If nothing would, it is not yet a belief about the world.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You believe a new welcome email cut cancellations. Which check is most in Bacon\u2019s spirit?',options:['Compare with similar customers who never got the email','Collect more stories from customers who liked the email','Ask the team whether the email seemed to work','Track cancellations among recipients for another month'],answer:0,why:'The customers without the email are the table of absence. If they cancelled just as rarely, the email did not do it.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE NATURE OF HEAT',text:'In the second book Bacon works through heat. He lists instances where heat is present: the sun\u2019s rays, flame, friction. Then cases as close as he can find where it is absent: the moon\u2019s rays give light without warmth. So light alone cannot be what heat is.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Discovery advances by rejection and exclusion. Supporting cases are easy to gather for any theory, so the decisive work is the deliberate search for instances that rule an explanation out.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Bacon\u2019s tables, then the verdict',steps:['Presence: cases where the thing occurs.','Absence: closely similar cases where it does not.','Degrees: cases where it varies in strength.','Exclusion: strike out every explanation the tables contradict.'],note:'The table of absence was his innovation. It is where explanations die.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Against the grain',text:'Bacon is explicit that the understanding, once pleased with an opinion, draws everything to its support. So the hunt for contrary instances cannot be left to temperament. It has to be built into the method, as a step no inquirer may skip.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Matching specimens fill the tray; the hand lifts the one that does not match. A good inquiry is organised around that single odd case.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What lasted',text:'The tables were clumsy, and Bacon underrated the bold guess: the hypothesis put forward first and tested afterwards. But the logic of exclusion survived. A claim is tested by what it forbids, not by what it can accommodate.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Refutation is rarely as clean as the image. An apparent exception may be a faulty measurement, or the work of a factor nobody had in mind, so one odd case does not always sink a sound theory. And Bacon hoped his tables could run almost mechanically. In practice, deciding which cases count as similar takes judgement and a guiding idea.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'A researcher finds one case that contradicts a well-supported theory. What does Bacon\u2019s logic, read sensibly, recommend?',options:['Check the case hard, and if it holds, change the theory','Ignore it, since one case cannot outweigh so many','Abandon the theory at once and start again','Gather more supporting cases to restore the balance'],answer:0,why:'A genuine exception outweighs any number of agreements. But it has to be genuine, so the first step is to test the exception itself.'}]}},'novum-organum_7':{pictureCaption:'Benches, a lectern and a stack of books, all still. On the floor, one small pump is actually raising water.',bridge:'Idea 6 tested an idea by what could refute it. This one adds a second test: what the idea lets you do.',basics:{title:'Can your idea make anything work?',beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'The saying *knowledge is power* means knowing secrets lets you boss people around.',reality:'Francis Bacon meant something else. If you truly know why a thing happens, you can make it happen. If you can\u2019t make it happen, maybe you don\u2019t really know.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The real test of knowing how something works is whether you can make it work. Talking well about it is not the same thing.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE BIKE CHAIN',text:'Two kids explain how a bike works. One uses lots of long words. The other says less. Then the chain falls off. The second kid puts it back on. Which one really knew?'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Lots of books and empty benches, all quiet. On the floor, one small pump is really pumping water.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What Bacon was cross about',text:'Bacon wrote about 400 years ago. In his day, top scholars could argue for hours. But their ideas did not cure the sick or build new tools. Bacon said: judge an idea by what it can do.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of something you say you know, like how to make pancakes. Can you actually do it? Try it this week and see.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Who best shows Bacon\u2019s kind of knowing?',options:['A girl who fixes the broken radio','A boy who wins every argument about radios','A kid who has read three books on radios'],answer:0,why:'Fixing it shows she knows why it works. Winning arguments and reading are good, but they do not prove it.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TWO MECHANICS',prompt:'One mechanic can explain any fault in impressive detail. Another explains less, but the cars she works on stop breaking down. Who understands engines better, by Bacon\u2019s test?',options:['The one who explains everything','The one whose repairs hold','You cannot tell from this'],answer:1,reveal:'Explaining after the fact costs nothing. Producing the result, a car that runs, is what shows you have hold of the cause.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Bacon\u2019s claim is narrower than the slogan: human knowledge and human power meet in one. If you understand a cause you can produce its effect, and if you cannot produce it, you do not yet understand it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The target',text:'The learning of Bacon\u2019s day could dispute any question at length. It was elaborate and internally consistent. What it had not done, he said, was produce works: things that operate, that light a room or cure a patient. He judged a doctrine by its fruits.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The lecture hall is still. Only the pump on the floor is doing anything, which is Bacon\u2019s point about the learning of his day.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Debating position',items:['Survives every argument','Explains any result afterwards','Changes nothing you can do']},right:{title:'Working knowledge',items:['Produces an effect on demand','Predicts before the result','Lets you build, cure or fix']},note:'Both can sound equally convincing in a meeting.'},{kind:'try',text:'Take a theory you hold about your own work. Name the thing it should let you build or predict. If there is nothing, it may only be a debating position.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A team has a detailed theory of why customers leave, but every fix based on it has failed. What does Bacon\u2019s test suggest?',options:['The theory does not yet grasp the cause, however well it explains','The fixes were probably carried out badly by the team','The theory is fine; customers leaving cannot be changed','More analysis will eventually make the same fixes work'],answer:0,why:'If knowing the cause should let you produce the effect, repeated failure to produce it is evidence against the knowledge.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'NEAR THE START OF BOOK ONE',text:'In an early aphorism of *Novum Organum*, Bacon states that human knowledge and human power meet in one, because where the cause is not known the effect cannot be produced. Nature, he adds, is commanded only by being obeyed. The three-word slogan is not how this book puts it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Understanding is tested by production. If you know the cause you can produce the effect; if you cannot produce the effect, you do not know the cause, whatever you can say about it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Against disputation',text:'The scholastic learning of the universities was elaborate, consistent and, in Bacon\u2019s judgement, barren. It could dispute but could not generate works. He insisted that a philosophy be judged by its fruits, and found the fruits of this one wanting.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'THREE INVENTIONS',text:'Bacon points to printing, gunpowder and the magnetic compass, which he says changed the whole face of the world. None came out of the philosophy of the schools. He credits such discoveries to experience and chance, and treats the contrast as evidence that disputation and discovery are different activities.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Rows of empty benches, a lectern of books, and one working pump. The pump is the only thing in the room being tested.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The test is strong in natural science and weak elsewhere. A historian cannot produce the fall of Rome, nor an astronomer a star; neither is thereby ignorant. Production can also run ahead of understanding: remedies often worked before anyone knew why. And treating knowledge purely as power over nature has drawn serious criticism since.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Name a body of expertise you rely on. What can it now build, cure or predict that it could not before?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which use of Bacon\u2019s test asks more of it than it can bear?',options:['Calling a historian\u2019s account worthless because it produces nothing','Doubting a medical theory whose treatments never seem to work','Asking an engineering model to predict the load a bridge can take','Testing a theory of plant growth by actually growing plants'],answer:0,why:'The test fits fields where effects can be produced. Applied to history, it mistakes a limit of the method for a failure of the knowledge.'}]}},'novum-organum_8':{pictureCaption:'Up to the stake, the footprints fit either road. Past it, only one road has prints. A deciding test is a stake at the fork.',bridge:'Idea 6 hunted for the case that refutes. This one builds that case on purpose, when two explanations are both still standing.',basics:{title:'The test that picks one answer',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'WHO ATE THE BISCUITS?',text:'The biscuits are gone. You think the dog ate them. Your sister thinks your little brother did. You find crumbs on the floor. But crumbs fit both ideas. They don\u2019t help at all.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Most clues fit more than one answer. The best clue is one where each answer says something different should be there. Then the clue can only point one way.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'A BETTER CLUE',text:'The biscuit tin is back on its high shelf, with the lid on. A dog can\u2019t do that. A boy can. Now the two ideas say different things, and one look tells you which is right.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Up to the post, both paths have been walked. After it, the footprints go only one way. A good test is like that post.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Bacon\u2019s signpost',text:'Francis Bacon wrote about this in 1620. He named this kind of test after a *fingerpost*. That is an old signpost where a road splits in two. It shows which way to go, and so which way not to go.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you and a friend have two different guesses, ask: what would we see if you are right, and what if I am? Then go and look.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You think the plant died from too little water. Your dad thinks it got too much sun. Which check helps most?',options:['Look at a plant in the same sun that got plenty of water','Look closely at the dead plant\u2019s brown leaves','Ask a friend which idea sounds more likely'],answer:0,why:'If that plant is fine, sun was not the problem. Brown leaves fit both ideas, so they can\u2019t decide.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'TWO STORIES, ONE DIP',text:'Sales dipped in March. One person blames the price rise; another blames a rival\u2019s launch. Both stories fit the dip perfectly, and every new monthly figure fits both as well. Three meetings later the argument is exactly where it started.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Most evidence is compatible with several explanations, so gathering more can leave a dispute where it was. The valuable evidence is what the explanations predict differently, so the result cannot be absorbed by both.'},{kind:'term',word:'Instance of the fingerpost',meaning:'Bacon\u2019s name for a deciding case, after the signposts set up where roads divide. It does not show you where you are going. It shows you which road you are not on.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHAT EACH STORY PREDICTS',left:{title:'If price drove the dip',items:['Loyal customers buy less too','Regions without the rival dip as well']},right:{title:'If the rival drove it',items:['Mostly new customers are lost','Only regions with the rival dip']},note:'Now the regional figures are a test. Before, they were just more data.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Both roads are worn up to the stake. Beyond it, the prints take one road only. The deciding test is the stake.'},{kind:'explain',title:'When no test exists',text:'Ask before you gather anything: what result would separate the explanations? Sometimes the honest answer is none. Then the disagreement is not about evidence, and collecting more will not settle it. Say so, and argue about what it is really about.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before your next test or analysis, write down what each competing explanation predicts. If the predictions match, the test will not settle anything.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Two doctors disagree whether a patient\u2019s headaches come from screen use or poor sleep. Which test would best decide?',options:['A week of normal sleep with heavy screen use','A careful diary of how bad each headache feels','A scan to check that nothing else is wrong','Asking the patient which cause seems likelier'],answer:0,why:'That week separates the causes: one predicts headaches, the other predicts relief. The other options fit either story.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'BEFORE ANY DATA',prompt:'Two theories both explain every observation so far. What should you gather next?',options:['More of the same observations','A case where they predict different results','Expert opinion on which is more elegant'],answer:1,reveal:'More of the same leaves the tie in place, however much you collect. Only a case the theories disagree about can break it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The decisive instance is one where rival explanations predict different outcomes, so the result must eliminate one. Ordinary observation rarely decides; the crucial case is built so that agreeing with both is impossible.'},{kind:'term',word:'Instantia crucis',meaning:'Latin for the instance of the cross, or fingerpost: a signpost where roads divide. Bacon lists it among his prerogative instances, the specially useful kinds of case. Through later writers it became the *crucial experiment*.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'HIS OWN CASE',text:'Bacon asks whether heavy bodies fall by their own nature or because the earth draws them. If the earth draws them, weight should change with distance from it. So he proposes comparing a clock driven by lead weights at the top of a tall steeple with one at the bottom of a deep mine.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The path forks and a stake marks the split. Both ways are worn up to it; only one carries prints beyond. That is what a crucial case does.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The discipline',text:'Before gathering evidence, write down what each hypothesis predicts. Where the predictions coincide, the evidence cannot help, however much of it there is. Where no possible result separates them, the dispute is not empirical, and should stop being conducted as though more data would settle it.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Crucial experiments are rarer than the name suggests. A theory can usually be rescued by adjusting an assumption around it, so what looks decisive at the fork is often contested for years. And there may be more than two roads: eliminating one rival does not prove the other.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'A critic says no single experiment ever really decides between theories. What is the fairest response?',options:['True in strict logic, but a well-designed test still shifts the burden sharply','False, because a crucial experiment settles the matter for good','True, so experiments should give way to careful argument','False, as long as the experiment is repeated often enough'],answer:0,why:'Rivals can be rescued by extra assumptions, but each rescue has a cost. A result one theory forbade still tells you which road you are probably not on.'}]}},'novum-organum_9':{pictureCaption:'Height marks climb a doorframe, and a hand rests on the top one. The lowest mark came first: what we call ancient was the world\u2019s childhood.',bridge:'The idols of the theatre were inherited systems. This is Bacon\u2019s argument for why being inherited gives them no authority.',basics:{title:'The old days were the young days',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'WHO IS OLDER?',prompt:'We call the Greeks of long ago *ancient*. Who has more past to learn from: them, or us?',options:['They do, because they are so ancient','We do, because we have them and everyone since'],answer:1,reveal:'We can read what they wrote. We also have all that people found out after them. In that way, we are the older ones.'},{kind:'idea',text:'We call the distant past *ancient*, as if it were old and wise. Francis Bacon said it was really the world\u2019s childhood. We are the older ones, with more behind us.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'YOUR FIRST DRAWINGS',text:'Think of your first school drawings. You keep them because you love them. But you would not ask your five-year-old self for help with your homework now. You know more than they did.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The first height mark is the lowest. That is the ancient world. We are the top mark.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it mattered',text:'Bacon lived about 400 years ago. Back then, many thought the old Greek thinkers had found out nearly all there was to know. If that were true, there was no point looking for new things. Bacon wanted people to start looking.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO TREAT THE PAST',left:{title:'Respect',items:['Read what they found','Thank them for it','Build on it']},right:{title:'Obey',items:['They said it, so it is true','Never check it','Stop looking']},note:'Respect costs you nothing. Obeying can cost you the right answer.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of a rule you follow because people have always done it that way. Ask: has anyone checked it lately?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Someone says an idea must be true because people have believed it for 2,000 years. What would Bacon say?',options:['It came from a time that knew less, so check it','Anything that old must be wrong by now','If it lasted that long, it is safe to trust'],answer:0,why:'Being old does not make an idea right or wrong. It came from a time that knew less, so it still needs checking.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE QUIET WORD',text:'In an argument about how to run something, someone points out that the current approach is the classic one. It has been used since the founders. The word *classic* settles nothing, but the room goes quiet. Age has just been treated as evidence.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Bacon reverses the authority of age. The ancient world was the world\u2019s youth, when it knew least. We are the older ones, with more experience behind us. So the ancients deserve respect but cannot settle a question.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The argument from naming',text:'We credit the old with wisdom because they have long experience. Apply that to whole ages and the conclusion flips. The age that has built up the most experience is the latest one: ours, not Aristotle\u2019s. Calling his era ancient gets the direction backwards.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Each mark on the doorframe was made at a different age. The earliest sits lowest. Ranked by experience, the ancient world is down there, and ours is the hand at the top.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Bacon was dismissing the ancient thinkers.',reality:'He kept his reverence for them. What he removed was their right to settle a question by authority. Respecting someone who came before is free. Deferring to them costs you the answer.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHY HIS PROJECT NEEDED IT',nodes:['The ancients reached the summit','Nothing important is left to find','No reason to build','Knowledge stands still'],note:'Bacon\u2019s whole project depends on knowledge building up. A culture that believes the summit is behind it has no reason to climb.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time something is defended as the classic, traditional or original approach, ask what evidence has built up since. Respect the source. Weigh the evidence.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A clinic still uses a treatment because its founder, a respected pioneer, designed it. Newer trials suggest an alternative works better. What does Bacon\u2019s argument imply?',options:['Honour the founder and follow the newer evidence','Keep the treatment, since the founder saw more deeply','Drop both, since neither can be fully trusted','Wait until someone of the founder\u2019s stature agrees'],answer:0,why:'The pioneer worked earlier, with less behind them. Respect for the person and deference to the method are separate choices.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'THROUGH THE PILLARS',text:'The title page of the 1620 volume that contains *Novum Organum* shows a ship sailing out between the Pillars of Hercules, the ancient limit of the known world at the Strait of Gibraltar. The Latin motto beneath promises that many will pass through and knowledge will be increased.'},{kind:'idea',text:'What we call antiquity was the youth of the world. If age confers wisdom through experience, then the present, with more behind it, is the elder. The ancients are owed respect, not the final word.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The inversion',text:'Bacon argues from naming. Our own times are the true ancient times, he says, since the world is now older; the ancients are ancient only when we count backwards from ourselves. The point is not wordplay. It moves the authority of experience from the past to the present.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Counted back from today, the lowest mark is the oldest. Counted as growth, it is the youngest. Bacon reads the ages of the world the second way.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the project needs it',text:'Everything else in the book assumes that knowledge accumulates. If the ancients had reached the summit, there would be nothing to build and no reason for new tables and instances. The ship on the title page makes the same claim: the old limit was local, and treating it as final was the error.'},{kind:'timeline',points:[{when:'4th century BCE',what:'Aristotle writes the logical works later gathered as the *Organon*.'},{when:'1620',what:'Bacon publishes *Novum Organum*, a new instrument to replace it.'},{when:'1660',what:'The Royal Society is founded, with Bacon as a guiding inspiration.'}],note:'By his own argument, Bacon\u2019s successors stood older than he did.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Accumulation is not guaranteed. Knowledge can be lost, and later ages can be worse at some things than earlier ones. Being later gives access to more experience, not the use of it; a modern who ignores the evidence is no elder than Aristotle. And an old idea that has survived long scrutiny may deserve some weight for that reason.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which predecessor in your own field do you defer to rather than merely respect? What would checking them cost?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these is a *misuse* of Bacon\u2019s argument?',options:['Rejecting an old finding simply because it is old','Retesting a long-held rule with better instruments','Reading the ancients for the questions they raised well','Building on a predecessor\u2019s results while checking them'],answer:0,why:'Bacon removes age as a reason to accept a claim. Treating it as a reason to reject one is the same mistake reversed.'}]}}}},2991,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'on-liberty_1':{pictureCaption:'A vase shut in a crate, the same kind as those displayed on the shelf. What one age silences, a later one often honours.',basics:{title:'Shutting people up means you think you can\u2019t be wrong',beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'ITALY, ALMOST 400 YEARS AGO',text:'Galileo was a scientist in Italy almost 400 years ago. He said the Earth moves around the Sun. The people in charge were sure he was wrong. They made him take it back. He was right all along.'},{kind:'idea',text:'If you stop someone from saying what they think, you act as if you can\u2019t be wrong. But people just as sure as you have been wrong many times.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO SAID THIS',text:'John Stuart Mill was an English thinker. In 1859 he wrote a short book called *On Liberty*. Liberty means being free. Mill saw that in every age, people have shut others up and felt sure they were right. Feeling sure is not the same as being right.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Someone is shutting a vase in a box. Look at the shelf. The same kind of vase is on show there. What one time hides, a later time may put on show.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'IN CLASS',text:'Sam says the answer to the puzzle is 12. Everyone laughs, and the class moves on. But what if Sam is right? Now no one will find out. Asking Sam why would teach them something either way.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'So no one is ever wrong.',reality:'No. Mill knew some ideas are wrong. But you can\u2019t always tell which ones ahead of time. So let them be said, and then answer them.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'At a club meeting, Mia says the club should change its name. The others tell her to be quiet. What would Mill say?',options:['Let her speak, then answer her reasons','Keep her quiet, since most people disagree','Change the name at once, so she is happy'],answer:0,why:'Telling her to be quiet acts as if the club can\u2019t be wrong. Hearing her out lets the best idea win.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE PLANNING MEETING',text:'A junior colleague says the new product will flop. Someone senior cuts in: we have been through this, let\u2019s move on. Nobody answers the point. The meeting moves on, and it feels efficient.'},{kind:'idea',text:'To silence a view is to claim you cannot be wrong about it. The case for hearing it does not need the view to be right, only the people silencing it to be fallible.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The track record',text:'Mill\u2019s point is historical. Every generation has suppressed views a later one judged correct, and did it with exactly the confidence you feel now. Certainty is not evidence. Being wrong feels the same from the inside.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'THE TWO WAYS TO BE WRONG',left:{title:'Silence a true view',items:['The truth is lost','Nobody ever finds out']},right:{title:'Hear a false view',items:['Answering it costs a few minutes','The true view is tested and understood']},note:'The two mistakes are not equal. Silencing costs most exactly when you are most sure.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'So every opinion is as good as every other.',reality:'Mill thought some opinions are simply false. His claim is narrower: we are bad at knowing in advance which ones, and wrongly silencing a true view costs far more than tolerating a false one.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A vase boxed away, the same kind as the ones on the shelf. What one age shuts up, a later one often puts on display.'},{kind:'try',text:'In your next meeting, when a view is shut down rather than answered, ask for the answer. If nobody has one, that is what the group needs to think about.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A team lead ends discussion of a rival plan because \u201cwe all know it won\u2019t work\u201d. What is Mill\u2019s objection?',options:['The team is treating its own confidence as proof, which it is not','The rival plan is probably the better of the two','Every plan deserves an equal share of the team\u2019s time','Ending a discussion is rude and damages team morale'],answer:0,why:'Mill\u2019s argument does not need the rival plan to be right. It needs only that confident teams have been wrong before.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'MILL\u2019S OWN EXAMPLES',text:'Mill chooses cases where the silencers were not villains. Athens condemned Socrates for impiety. Marcus Aurelius, in Mill\u2019s account one of the best rulers who ever lived, persecuted the Christians in good conscience. Both acted on beliefs they had every reason to think sound, and both are now judged wrong.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Suppressing an opinion assumes infallibility. The argument needs no claim that the suppressed view is true, only that those suppressing it can err, and history shows how often confident people do.'},{kind:'term',word:'Infallibility',meaning:'Being incapable of error. Nobody claims it outright. Mill\u2019s point is that anyone who silences a view acts as if they had it, on that question at least.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The objection he answers',text:'Mill expects the reply that we must act on our best judgement, and governments do so constantly. He accepts it. But holding a belief that stays open to challenge is one thing. Forbidding the challenge is another, because it removes the only process by which the belief could be corrected.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW CERTAINTY SEALS ITSELF',nodes:['You are certain','You silence the doubter','Nobody can test the belief','An error can no longer be found'],note:'The confidence that justifies the silencing is the thing the silencing stops anyone checking.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The argument protects opinion, not every act of speech. Mill himself says a claim that may be printed freely can be punished when shouted at an angry crowd outside its target\u2019s house. And fallibility cuts both ways: it is no evidence that the dissenter is right, and it can be abused to demand endless rehearing of questions that have been fairly tested.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which view would your group not let someone finish saying? What would it cost to hear it out and answer it?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which use of Mill\u2019s argument goes wrong?',options:['\u201cYou can\u2019t be certain, so my view is as likely to be right as yours\u201d','\u201cWe may be right, but let us hear the objection before we ban it\u201d','\u201cAct on the plan, and leave the doubters free to argue against it\u201d','\u201cOur confidence alone tells us little about whether we are right\u201d'],answer:0,why:'Fallibility is a reason to keep a question open. It says nothing about which side is right, so it cannot be used as evidence for the dissenter.'}]}},'on-liberty_2':{pictureCaption:'A blade left on its hook for years. Nothing has been ground against it, and the edge has gone to rust.',bridge:'Idea 1 defended a view that might be true. This one says even a view that is true needs its challengers.',basics:{title:'A true idea no one argues with goes weak',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'A QUESTION FROM YOUR COUSIN',text:'Your little cousin asks how you know the Earth goes round the Sun. You open your mouth. Nothing comes out. You know it is true. You just can\u2019t say why.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Even a true idea goes weak if no one ever argues with it. You keep the words but lose the reasons. Then you can\u2019t stand up for it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'This blade has hung on its hook for years. Nothing has rubbed against it, so it has gone dull and rusty.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A FOOTBALL TEAM',text:'A team that only plays much younger kids thinks it is great. Then it meets a real team and falls apart. It was never tested. Ideas need a real side against them too.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO SAID THIS',text:'John Stuart Mill was an English thinker who wrote a book about freedom in 1859. He said a true idea that no one argues with turns into a slogan. A slogan is a line you repeat without knowing why.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick something you are sure of. Say the best reason someone might give against it. Then say why you still think you are right.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Jake says cheating is wrong. When asked why, he can\u2019t say. What would Mill think?',options:['Jake is right, but he holds it like a slogan','Jake is wrong, since he has no reasons','It does not matter, since Jake is right'],answer:0,why:'Cheating is wrong, so Jake is right. But with no reasons, he will struggle the first time someone argues back.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'YEARS WITHOUT A CHALLENGE',prompt:'Your team has backed one strategy for years, and nobody has ever argued against it. Is that a good sign?',options:['Yes: the agreement shows it is right','Not necessarily: nobody has tested it'],answer:1,reveal:'Agreement is not evidence. A view nobody argues with has simply stopped being examined. Ask anyone why it is right and you will hear the slogan, not the reasons.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A true belief that is never contested decays. It survives as a phrase rather than an argument, and the people who hold it can no longer say why it is true, or where it stops.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What defending teaches you',text:'Someone who has met the strongest objection knows why the belief holds and where its edges are. Someone who has never met one knows only the conclusion. They cannot tell a good challenge from a bad one, so every challenge feels like an attack.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW A TRUTH GOES STALE',nodes:['A belief is right','Nobody argues with it','The reasons are forgotten','A capable critic arrives','It is defended badly'],note:'The real damage is done not by the question but by the weak answer.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A blade left on its hook for years. Nothing has been ground against it, and the edge has gone to rust.'},{kind:'try',text:'Take a view you hold firmly. Write the strongest case against it in three sentences. If you cannot, you hold a slogan.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A company\u2019s \u201ccustomer first\u201d value is on every wall, but no one can say what it rules out. What would Mill say?',options:['It may be right, but it is held as a slogan and will be defended badly','It must be wrong, since nobody can explain what it means','It is fine, since everyone in the company already agrees with it','It should be dropped and replaced with a more popular value'],answer:0,why:'Mill\u2019s worry is not that the belief is false. It is that nobody understands it well enough to use it or defend it.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'CHAPTER TWO, THE LATER GROUNDS',text:'Mill\u2019s chapter on thought and discussion gives several grounds for allowing dissent. The silenced view may be true. It may hold part of the truth. And even if the received view is wholly true, unless it is vigorously contested it is held as prejudice, and in time its meaning fades.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Even a correct belief decays without challenge. It is held as a conclusion rather than an argument, and so it fails at the moment a capable opponent appears.'},{kind:'term',word:'Dead dogma',meaning:'Mill\u2019s name for a true belief held by rote: repeated, even sincerely, but no longer understood or felt, so it no longer shapes how its holder thinks or acts.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'CICERO AND THE CHURCH',text:'Mill notes that the Roman orator Cicero studied his opponent\u2019s case as closely as his own. He also points to the Catholic Church, which appoints an advocate to argue against making someone a saint. Both build the challenge in on purpose.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Manufacturing the challenge',text:'This is where Mill goes further than most are comfortable with. If no real opponents exist, the case against must be imagined and put at its strongest, because agreement is not evidence, and a belief nobody argues with has stopped being examined at all.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Manufactured doubt can be used as a weapon. Staging a debate about a well-tested finding can mislead an audience about the state of the evidence, and has been used to do exactly that. Mill\u2019s remedy is for understanding a belief, not for pretending a question is open when it is not. Not every settled matter needs a standing opponent.'},{kind:'recap',points:['A truth without challengers survives only as a phrase.','Meeting the strongest objection is how you learn the reasons.','Build the challenge in, without faking a controversy.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HARD CASE',prompt:'A science teacher has her class argue the case against a well-established theory before studying the evidence for it. Mill\u2019s idea, or a misuse?',options:['Mill\u2019s idea: students learn the reasons by meeting the strongest objection','A misuse: it teaches students that the theory is seriously in doubt','A misuse: settled truths should simply be learned as established facts','Mill\u2019s idea: it shows students that all theories are equally uncertain'],answer:0,why:'The exercise is for understanding, not for creating doubt. It would become a misuse only if it were presented as a live scientific controversy.'}]}},'on-liberty_3':{pictureCaption:'Two hands bring together the broken halves of one plate. Neither half is the whole, and the join is where the work happens.',bridge:'Ideas 1 and 2 pictured one side right. This one takes the commoner case, where each side holds part of the truth.',basics:{title:'When both sides are a bit right',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'AN ARGUMENT AT HOME',prompt:'Your brother says screens are bad for you. Your sister says screens help you learn. Who is right?',options:['Your brother','Your sister','Both of them, a bit'],answer:2,reveal:'Screens can keep you up late and stop you moving. They can also teach you a lot. Each of them has a real piece. Each thinks their piece is the whole thing.'},{kind:'idea',text:'In most arguments, each side has a piece of the truth. Each thinks its piece is all of it. Arguing well puts the pieces together.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two hands, two broken halves of one plate. Neither half is the whole plate. Put them together and you can see it.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO SAID THIS',text:'John Stuart Mill was an English thinker who wrote about freedom in 1859. He saw that one side is rarely right about everything. So the point of an argument is not always to win. If you win, the other side\u2019s piece gets thrown away.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you argue, stop and say one thing the other person is right about. See if the fight gets smaller.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Two friends are planning the class trip. One says it should be fun. The other says it should teach you something. What would Mill suggest?',options:['Look for a trip that does both','Vote, and let the winner pick','Go with whoever sounds more sure'],answer:0,why:'Each friend has a real piece. A trip that is fun and teaches you something keeps both pieces.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'A MONTH OF DEADLOCK',text:'Two colleagues have argued for a month. One wants to ship fast and fix later. The other wants it right before anyone sees it. Each has watched the other\u2019s approach fail before, and each is right about why.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Most disputes are not truth against error. They are two partial truths, each mistaken for the whole, and the collision is how the parts get assembled.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Truth against falsehood',items:['One side is simply wrong','The job is to win','Losing costs nothing worth keeping']},right:{title:'Part against part',items:['Each side sees something real','The job is to assemble','Winning throws a fragment away']},note:'Mill thought the right-hand case far more common than the left.'},{kind:'explain',title:'His own example',text:'Mill applied this to the politics of his day. A party of order and a party of progress, he argued, are both needed. Each holds the half the other neglects, and each keeps the other within reason.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two hands, two pieces of the same broken plate. Neither is the whole. The line where they meet is where the work is.'},{kind:'try',text:'In the next deadlock, write one sentence on what each side is right about before you decide anything.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Two managers are stuck: one wants strict rules for remote work, the other wants full freedom. What does Mill\u2019s idea suggest first?',options:['Work out what each is right about before choosing a policy','Find out which manager has more experience and follow them','Split the difference exactly halfway between the two','Let them argue it out until one of them gives up'],answer:0,why:'Splitting the difference is not assembling. The task is to find the real fragment each holds, then build a policy that keeps both.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE USUAL PICTURE',belief:'An argument is a contest: one side has the truth, the other has an error.',reality:'Mill thought this the rare case. More often conflicting doctrines share the truth between them, and each side\u2019s error is about completeness, not content.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Each side usually holds a genuine fragment and mistakes it for the whole. Open collision is the only way the fragments get assembled, so a dispute is better treated as a joint operation than a contest.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where it sits',text:'This is one of Mill\u2019s grounds for free discussion: even a false opinion may carry a portion of truth that the prevailing view lacks. Since the prevailing view is seldom the whole truth, the missing part can only arrive through dissent.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Order and progress',text:'His worked case is political. A party of order and a party of progress, he argued, are both needed for a healthy political life. Each supplies what the other neglects, and each is kept within reason largely by the other\u2019s opposition.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'WHERE A DISPUTE SITS',low:'Truth against falsehood: win it',high:'Fragment against fragment: assemble it',note:'The test is whether you can say what the other side is right about. If you cannot, either it is the rare pure error, or you do not yet understand the dispute.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Some disputes really are truth against falsehood, and treating them as two halves gives error a share it has not earned. The idea also invites a lazy middle. Splitting the difference is not assembly: the true part on each side may lie anywhere, and rarely at the midpoint.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Pick a disagreement you are in now. What, exactly, is the other side right about?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which use of Mill\u2019s idea goes wrong?',options:['Giving a disproved claim equal airtime because \u201cboth sides hold part of the truth\u201d','Asking what a rival team\u2019s plan gets right before rejecting it','Combining two partial proposals into a single stronger plan','Admitting that your own side has neglected something real'],answer:0,why:'The idea is a reason to look for the true part on each side, not a rule that each side must have one. Some claims are simply false.'}]}},'on-liberty_4':{pictureCaption:'A neighbour\u2019s shears stop at the string. The wild plot is its owner\u2019s business; only the bramble that has crossed over is within reach.',bridge:'The first three ideas defended speech. This one draws the line for action: when power over a person is justified at all.',basics:{title:'When can someone make you stop?',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'A FLAT WITH THIN WALLS',text:'A woman lives alone in a flat with thin walls. She likes cake for breakfast. She also plays the drums at three in the morning. The town wants to stop both. Should it?'},{kind:'idea',text:'Mill\u2019s rule: you may force a grown-up to stop doing something only to keep *other* people from harm. Doing it for their own good is not enough.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHERE THE LINE FALLS',left:{title:'Can be stopped',items:['Drums at three in the morning, through thin walls','Throwing stones at windows']},right:{title:'Cannot be stopped',items:['Cake for breakfast','Staying up late, alone, on a Sunday']},note:'The line is not about what is wise. It is about who gets hurt.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The wild garden is its owner\u2019s business. The shears stop at the string. Only the thorny stem that grew into the next garden gets cut.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO SAID THIS',text:'John Stuart Mill was an English thinker. In 1859 he wrote *On Liberty*, a book about freedom. This rule is at the heart of it. He said it was for grown-ups. Children need more looking after.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The hard part',text:'Mill knew the rule is easy to say and hard to use. A man is about to step onto a broken bridge. Is that just his business? Mill said you may stop him and warn him. Nobody wants to fall in the river.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of one rule in your town. Ask: who would get hurt without it? If the answer is only the person who breaks it, Mill would ask why it is there.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Which rule fits Mill\u2019s idea?',options:['Grown-ups may not drive fast past a school','Grown-ups must be in bed by ten','Grown-ups may not eat too many sweets'],answer:0,why:'Fast driving puts other people in danger. The other two rules only protect people from themselves.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'A COMMON READING',belief:'Mill said you can do whatever you like as long as you hurt no one.',reality:'Close, but Mill\u2019s rule limits power, not conduct. Force may be used on an unwilling adult only to prevent harm to others. You may still argue, warn and disapprove. And he knew stating it was the easy part.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Power over an unwilling adult is justified only to prevent harm to others. Their own good, of body or of character, is not reason enough. Applying the rule is where the work is.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The neighbour may think the wild plot a disgrace, and say so. The shears still stop at the string. Only the bramble that has crossed it may be cut.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Mill\u2019s method',steps:['State the principle in one sentence.','Go looking for the cases that strain it.','Ask what counts as harm in each one.','Let the strain show where the rule really applies.']},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE POISON SHOP',text:'Poison kills rats, and people. Should its sale be banned? Mill says no, because most buyers have honest uses. But the seller may be required to label it and record each sale. The rule bends to the case without breaking.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The questions still open',text:'What counts as harm? Does harm to yourself count if your family bears it? Is offence a harm at all? Mill did not claim to settle these, and the same questions run through every argument about regulation, speech and public health.'},{kind:'try',text:'Take one rule you believe in and write down the three cases that strain it most. If you cannot find any, you have not understood the rule yet.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A city wants to ban a dangerous extreme sport whose risks fall only on the adults who take part. What does Mill\u2019s principle say?',options:['Make sure they know the risk, but do not ban it','Ban it, since protecting life is the state\u2019s first duty','Ban it only if most citizens find the sport offensive','Ban it until the participants can show it is safe'],answer:0,why:'The risk falls on people who chose it. Mill allows making sure they know the danger, not overriding their choice.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'THE ESSAY\u2019S CORE',word:'The harm principle',meaning:'Mill\u2019s rule that the only legitimate reason to use power over a member of a civilised community against their will is to prevent harm to others. Their own good, physical or moral, does not suffice.'},{kind:'idea',text:'One sentence fixes the boundary of legitimate coercion: preventing harm to others. Mill\u2019s real contribution is testing, case by case, where that boundary actually runs.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The string marks the boundary of legitimate interference. The wild plot is left alone; only the stem that crosses may be cut. Real brambles rarely stay on one side.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What he was arguing against',text:'His target was not only law. It was the habit of majorities, acting through law or through opinion, of enforcing their own likes and dislikes on everyone and calling the result morality. The principle draws one line that applies to both.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'HIS AWKWARD CASES',left:{title:'The case',items:['Selling poison','A man stepping onto an unsafe bridge','A soldier drunk on duty','Indecency in public']},right:{title:'What it tests',items:['Can you regulate what you will not ban?','Is stopping someone always coercion?','When does self-harm become harm to others?','Is offence a harm?']}},{kind:'explain',title:'Necessary, not sufficient',text:'Harm to others is required before society may interfere, but it does not settle that it should. Mill notes that winning a competition harms the losers, and nobody thinks competition should be banned for it. The principle opens the question rather than closing it.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The principle needs a clean line between conduct that affects only you and conduct that affects others, and almost nothing is purely the first. Harm is left undefined, so the argument tends to move to who gets to define it. And Mill limits the principle to adults in societies he judged ready for it, an exemption idea 7 takes up.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Name a rule you support. Which of Mill\u2019s awkward cases is it most like, and does it pass his test?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A town proposes banning a legal motorbike rally on a private field far from any homes, because many residents find it vulgar. How does the harm principle treat the ban?',options:['Finding something vulgar is not being harmed, so it cannot justify the ban','The residents\u2019 distress counts as harm, so the ban is justified','Any majority view may be enforced once it has been fairly voted on','The riders risk harming themselves, so the ban protects them'],answer:0,why:'Mill\u2019s line runs at harm to others, and disliking what others do in private is not, on his account, being harmed by it. Noise reaching homes would be a different case.'}]}},'on-liberty_5':{pictureCaption:'An open lawn anyone could cross, and one worn path that winds instead of cutting straight. Only habit keeps the walker on it.',bridge:'The harm principle limits law. Mill was more worried about a pressure that needs no law at all.',basics:{title:'The rules nobody wrote down',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'AT YOUR SCHOOL',prompt:'Which is harder to break: a school rule, or something that just isn\u2019t done?',options:['The school rule','The thing that just isn\u2019t done'],answer:1,reveal:'A rule has a reason you can ask about and argue with. But if you do something that isn\u2019t done, no one punishes you. People just look at you oddly. That can feel worse.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Some rules are written down. Others are just how things are done. Mill thought the unwritten ones control us more, because they never have to give a reason.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE PATH IN THE PARK',picture:!0,text:'No sign says keep off the grass. No one would tell you off for crossing it. But everyone walks the long way round. Ask why, and people shrug.'},{kind:'term',word:'Custom',meaning:'A habit a whole group shares, like shaking hands when you meet. Nobody voted on it. It is just what people do.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO SAID THIS',text:'John Stuart Mill was an English thinker who wrote about freedom in 1859. He worried that people stop asking what they like. Instead they ask what people like them usually do. In time, they forget how to ask the first question.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'So you should break every custom.',reality:'No. Lots of customs are good, like saying thank you. Mill wanted you to know the reason. If no one can give one, it is worth asking.'},{kind:'try',text:'Find one thing you never do because it just isn\u2019t done. Ask a grown-up why. See if they know.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Asha loves chess. At her school only the little kids play it. No rule stops her. What would Mill notice?',options:['Custom is holding her back, though no rule does','There must be a good reason no one plays','She should stop, since it just isn\u2019t done'],answer:0,why:'Nothing written stops Asha. But what is done can push like a rule, and it never gave its reason.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'NOT DONE HERE',text:'Nobody leaves the office before the manager does. It is in no handbook, nobody has ever been disciplined for it, and if you asked, everyone would say they are free to go. Everyone stays.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Social pressure can control more completely than law. A law is visible and can be opposed. A custom never has to explain itself, so there is nothing to appeal against.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Nobody fenced the lawn and no sign forbids the short cut. The path is worn by people walking where people walk, and it never has to say why.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'A law',items:['Written down','Has a penalty someone imposes','Can be argued with in public']},right:{title:'A custom',items:['Written nowhere','Costs arrive with no one deciding','Feels like simply how things are']},note:'Leaving a custom feels like eccentricity, not disagreement. That is why so few people do.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What it does to choice',text:'Mill\u2019s complaint is that people stop asking what they prefer and ask what is usually done by people in their position. Left unused, the first question withers. His name for this is the despotism of custom.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW A CUSTOM HOLDS',nodes:['A thing is simply not done','Nobody tries it','Nobody sees what would happen','It still looks unthinkable'],loop:!0,note:'Without someone departing from it, there is no evidence the custom was ever any good.'},{kind:'try',text:'Notice one thing you avoid because it is not done. Ask whether you have ever heard the reason.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A new hire notices that nobody ever questions the chief executive at staff meetings, though nothing forbids it. What would Mill say?',options:['A custom is controlling people without ever having to justify itself','There is no pressure here, since nothing forbids asking','The custom must exist for a reason, so it should be respected','The company should make a rule requiring questions'],answer:0,why:'The lack of a rule is the point. Custom works without anyone imposing it, which is why it is so rarely examined.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'CHAPTER ONE',text:'Mill opens the essay by noting that the old fear, of rulers against the people, had been partly answered by elected government. The new danger was the majority itself, acting not only through law but through opinion. He judged this social tyranny the more formidable, because it leaves fewer means of escape.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Custom constrains more completely than law because it coerces without any visible act. There is no penalty to appeal and no one to argue with, only the sense that a thing is not done.'},{kind:'term',word:'The despotism of custom',meaning:'Mill\u2019s phrase for the control a society exerts when people ask not what they prefer but what is usually done by people in their position, until the first question atrophies from disuse.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'No fence, no sign, no keeper. The worn path is the only constraint on the lawn, and it was made entirely by people following one another.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why departure matters to him',text:'Mill defends departing from custom not as self-expression but as a service. In an age of strong conformity, he argues, the mere example of refusing to bow to custom is useful, because it shows the custom can be questioned at all.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'A MODERN CASE',text:'Picture a team where nobody takes holiday in the busy quarter. No policy says so, and leave is rarely refused. But the cost of taking it, a cooler manager, a quiet note at review, arrives without anyone having decided to impose it. There is nothing to appeal.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The conservative reply, older than Mill, is that customs often carry knowledge nobody can now spell out, and some norms of courtesy and trust work precisely because nobody negotiates them. Nor is every departure evidence: much nonconformity is only fashion. The useful version asks for the reason, and keeps the custom if a good one turns up.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What is simply not done in your workplace or family? Have you ever heard anyone give the reason?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HONEST OBJECTION',prompt:'Which objection must Mill\u2019s view take most seriously?',options:['Some customs work well for reasons no one can now state','Law is always more coercive than social pressure','Customs disappear on their own once they stop being useful','People who break customs are rarely punished for it'],answer:0,why:'A custom without a stated reason is not thereby a bad one. Mill\u2019s best answer is that departure is how we find out which customs earn their place.'}]}},'on-liberty_6':{pictureCaption:'A trial field of labelled plots, each sown differently, some still bare. The bloom was nobody\u2019s forecast; it is what the trying turned up.',bridge:'Idea 5 showed how custom stops people trying. This one says why the trying matters: it is the only source of evidence.',basics:{title:'Let people try different ways to live',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'ON A FARM',text:'A farmer has six kinds of seed. Which grows best in her field? She can\u2019t know by guessing. So she plants each one in its own patch. Then she watches what comes up.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A field cut into patches, each planted with something different. Some are still bare. One is bursting with flowers. Nobody knew it would, until someone tried.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Nobody knows which way of living suits which person. The only way to find out is to let people try different ways, and see how it goes.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'IN YOUR FAMILY',text:'Your aunt quits her job to grow food on a small farm. Lots of people say it is silly. Maybe she loves it. Maybe she hates it and goes back. Either way, the whole family learns something real.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO SAID THIS',text:'John Stuart Mill was an English thinker who wrote about freedom in 1859. He said people trying different ways to live helps all of us. It shows what works and what doesn\u2019t. But it is only fine as long as they don\u2019t harm others.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'If someone tries an odd way to live and it goes wrong, they were just silly.',reality:'Not to Mill. A try that fails still teaches us something. And the person who tried paid for it, not you.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your grown-up sister wants to try being vegan for a month. Her friend says that\u2019s weird. What would Mill say?',options:['Let her try, and see how it goes','Stop her, since most people eat meat','Make the whole family try it too'],answer:0,why:'No one can know ahead of time if it suits her. Trying it is how she, and the rest of us, find out.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'NOT ABOUT SELF-EXPRESSION',belief:'Mill defended unusual lives because people should be free to express themselves.',reality:'His argument is about knowledge. Nobody knows in advance which ways of living suit which people. So the only evidence comes from people who try them.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Different ways of living should be allowed, not merely tolerated. It is the only way society can find out what works. Suppress the variation and you suppress the data.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A trial field, each plot sown with something different. Several came up bare, which is also a result. The one in bloom was nobody\u2019s prediction. It is what the trying turned up.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the eccentric is useful',text:'A failed experiment still tells you something. And its cost was paid by the person who ran it. What Mill objects to is a society that forbids the attempt. It then points to the lack of other options as proof that the custom was right.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE SENSIBLE ADVICE',text:'A friend plans to leave a secure job at forty to train as a carpenter. The sensible advice writes itself. But do you really know the secure job suits them, or only that it is the usual choice?'},{kind:'try',text:'Before advising somebody to do the sensible thing, ask whether you know it suits them, or only that it is common.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A neighbourhood group campaigns against a shared house for adults because \u201cthat isn\u2019t how people live here\u201d. What is Mill\u2019s objection?',options:['Blocking the attempt removes the only evidence of whether it works','Unusual ways of living are generally better than common ones','Neighbours should never have any say in what happens nearby','Everyone has a right to express themselves however they like'],answer:0,why:'Mill\u2019s point is about evidence. Forbidding the experiment and then pointing to its absence proves nothing about the custom.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'THE EPIGRAPH',text:'Mill set a passage from the German thinker Wilhelm von Humboldt at the front of the essay, on the importance of human development in its richest diversity. The third chapter, on individuality, builds an argument on that foundation.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Experiments in living should be permitted because nobody knows in advance which arrangements produce good lives. The evidence exists only when people try and the results are visible.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'HIS OWN IMAGE',text:'Mill contrasts a machine, built to a plan and set to do exactly its prescribed work, with a tree, which must grow on every side according to the forces within it. Human nature, he argues, is the tree.'},{kind:'term',word:'Self-regarding',meaning:'Conduct that concerns only yourself, or others only with their free consent. Mill protects experiments in living within this space and no further.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The form of the argument',text:'The reasoning is about knowledge. A society that forbids the attempt, then cites the absence of alternatives as proof that the custom was right, has manufactured its own evidence. Variation is how the information is produced, and failures are part of it, paid for by those who ran them.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The protection holds only for self-regarding conduct, and few experiments stay inside that line. A household\u2019s experiment shapes its children; a commune affects its neighbours. Results are also slow and noisy, so a life that looks like a failure may not be one. Most of the later argument about the essay lives here.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Nobody knows in advance which lives suit whom.','Variation produces the evidence; suppression destroys it.','The protection stops where others bear the cost.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which appeal to experiments in living would Mill reject?',options:['Giving a child no education at all because \u201cevery family is an experiment\u201d','An adult choosing to live without a car or a phone','A group of friends buying a house to share in later life','Someone retraining in middle age for a far less sensible career'],answer:0,why:'A child\u2019s education is not self-regarding territory: the parent\u2019s choice lands on someone else. Mill wanted education required for exactly that reason.'}]}},'on-liberty_7':{pictureCaption:'A long, clean page with one clause circled. The exemption is not a footnote; it shows what the whole argument assumed.',bridge:'Ideas 4 to 6 rest on Mill\u2019s line between your own business and other people\u2019s. Here is where his own text strains it.',basics:{title:'A rule with a hole in it',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE SWING RULE',text:'Your class makes a rule: everyone gets a turn on the swings. Then the oldest kids add a line at the end. Except the little ones. Is it still a rule for everyone?'},{kind:'idea',text:'John Stuart Mill said grown-ups should be free to run their own lives. But he left some people out. When a rule leaves people out, look hard at who, and why.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO HE LEFT OUT',text:'Mill wrote his book about freedom in 1859. He said his rule was not for children. Most people agree with that. But he also left out people in countries he called backward. Mill worked for the East India Company. It ruled much of India for Britain.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One line in a long page has been circled. It is the line that leaves people out. It matters as much as the rest.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO HOLES IN THE RULE',left:{title:'Children',items:['Still learning to look after themselves','Will grow into the rule']},right:{title:'Whole countries',items:['Grown-ups, just like anyone else','Some were ruled by Mill\u2019s own company']},note:'Ask who gains from each hole.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Should we throw the book away?',text:'No. Mill himself said ideas should be argued with, not just believed. So we keep the good part. And we say plainly where he got it wrong.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A club says anyone can join. Then it adds: except new kids. What does this idea tell you to do?',options:['Ask why new kids are left out','Ignore it, since it is just one line','Decide the whole club must be bad'],answer:0,why:'The line that leaves people out tells you a lot about the rule. Ask why it is there.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'LONDON, 1858',text:'The year before On Liberty appeared, Mill retired from the East India Company. It governed much of India from London. He had worked in its head office for some thirty-five years. For much of that time he drafted its official dispatches.'},{kind:'idea',text:'On Liberty exempts a great deal from its own principle: children, and what Mill calls backward states of society. Reading him honestly means holding the argument and the exemption in view together.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Two weak points',text:'First, the harm principle needs a clean line. On one side is conduct that affects only you. On the other is conduct that affects others. Almost nothing is purely the first. Mill admits that a man who ruins himself harms his family. Then he holds the line anyway. Second, the exemption does real work. It places whole peoples outside the principle\u2019s protection.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One clause circled on a long, clean page. The exemption is not a footnote to the argument. It tells you what the argument assumed.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO READ IT',left:{title:'As a footnote',items:['A prejudice of its time','Skip it, keep the principle']},right:{title:'As evidence',items:['Shows who the author counted','Tests how far the principle reaches']},note:'The honest reading is the second column. It does not require throwing the essay away.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time an author exempts a group from their own principle, write down who is left out and what reason is given. Treat it as part of the argument.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A new flexible-working policy \u201capplies to all staff\u201d, with a final clause exempting shift workers. What does this idea suggest?',options:['Read the exemption as evidence of who the policy was designed for','Ignore it, since every policy needs some practical exceptions','Reject the whole policy, since it is unfair to some staff','Assume shift workers did not want flexibility anyway'],answer:0,why:'The exemption is not a footnote. It shows who the policy was built around. That is exactly what to examine.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'timeline',kicker:'MILL AND THE COMPANY',points:[{when:'1823',what:'Mill, aged seventeen, joins the East India Company\u2019s London office.'},{when:'1858',what:'After the rebellion of 1857, the Crown takes over the Company\u2019s rule in India. Mill retires.'},{when:'1859',what:'On Liberty is published.'}],note:'The essay was written by a man who had spent his working life administering colonial rule.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The harm principle depends on two things Mill could not supply: a clean line between self-regarding and other-regarding conduct, and a reason for exempting whole peoples that is not simply the empire he served.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The line that will not stay clean',text:'Mill concedes that a man who ruins himself harms those who depended on him. His answer is to punish the breach of duty to them, not the self-ruin. It is a careful move, but it shows how little conduct is purely self-regarding.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The exemption',text:'The principle is said to apply only to people in the maturity of their faculties. It excludes children, and also societies Mill judged not yet capable of improvement through free and equal discussion. For those, he allowed that despotism was a legitimate form of government, provided its aim was their improvement.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A long, clean argument with one clause circled. Read honestly, that clause belongs to the argument as much as any other line.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The critique has limits too. The exemption for children is widely accepted, so the problem is not exemption as such but who decides maturity, and on what evidence. And reading Mill against himself uses his own method: the argument survives the objection in a weakened and more honest form, which is how he said contested truths should be held.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Almost no conduct is purely self-regarding.','The exemptions do real work, and reflect the empire Mill served.','Treat an exemption as evidence about the principle.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A charter grants free speech to all citizens \u201cexcept those not yet ready for it\u201d. What is the most useful first question?',options:['Who decides who is ready, and on what evidence?','Is free speech a good principle in general?','How many citizens are currently judged ready?','Is the charter legally binding at all?'],answer:0,why:'As with Mill\u2019s maturity test, the exemption carries the weight. Whoever sets the bar decides who the principle actually covers.'}]}},'on-liberty_8':{pictureCaption:'Two files on one desk: the thin, closed one is what others know about your life. The thick one is yours, and so is the pen.',bridge:'Idea 4 drew the line between your business and other people\u2019s. This one gives Mill\u2019s reason for respecting your side of it.',basics:{title:'Who knows best about your life?',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'YOUR COUSIN\u2019S PLAN',text:'Your grown-up cousin wants to leave college and train as a chef. Her parents think it is a mistake. Who knows most about what she loves? Who will live with the choice? Who cares the most?'},{kind:'idea',text:'When it comes to your own life, you usually know the most about it. You care the most. And you live with what happens. So others should be slow to choose for you.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A thin, closed file and a thick, open one bursting with pages. The thick one is the person\u2019s own life. The pen is on it.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO SAID THIS',text:'John Stuart Mill wrote a book about freedom in 1859. He said people often choose badly. But the person who steps in to choose for them knows less, and cares less. So they are no more likely to get it right.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'You',items:['Know your own life','Care the most','Live with the result']},right:{title:'Someone else',items:['Knows the general rule','Cares a bit','Walks away after']}},{kind:'myth',belief:'So no one should ever step in.',reality:'Mill said one thing is fine. If someone is about to choose without a key fact, you can tell them. Then it is their choice. And he meant grown-ups. Children need more help.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your grown-up neighbour wants to sell his car and cycle everywhere. You think he will hate it. What would Mill say to do?',options:['Tell him what you know, then let him choose','Hide his bike so he keeps the car','Say nothing, even if you know a key fact'],answer:0,why:'He knows his own life best and lives with the choice. You can share facts he might be missing. The choice stays his.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'WHO IS BETTER INFORMED?',prompt:'Your brother wants to leave a stable job to open a caf\xe9. You think it is a mistake. Who is better placed to judge?',options:['You: you can see it more objectively','He is: he knows far more about his own situation'],answer:1,reveal:'You know the general odds for caf\xe9s. He knows his savings, his skills, his appetite for risk and how much he dislikes his current job. And he lives with the outcome.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Mill\u2019s case against paternalism is about evidence, not manners. The individual knows most about their situation, cares most about the outcome and bears the consequence, so interference is likely to be wrong.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Not that people choose well',text:'Mill grants that people often choose badly. His claim is comparative. The person overriding them works from general presumptions, knows less and cares less, so there is no reason to expect fewer errors. And their errors land on someone who never had a say.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two files on one desk. The thin, closed one is what anyone else knows about your life. The thick one is yours, and so is the pen.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where Mill allows a hand',text:'If someone is about to choose without a material fact, you may make sure they know it. His example is a person stepping onto a bridge known to be unsafe: warn them, or stop them if there is no time. Then the choice is theirs.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before overriding somebody\u2019s decision about their own life, ask what you know that they do not. If the answer is nothing, say your piece once and stop.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'An adult friend plans an expensive solo trek. You think it is reckless. What does Mill\u2019s argument support?',options:['Check they know the risks you know about, then respect the choice','Keep raising it with them until they agree to cancel','Tell them it is their choice, and share nothing you know','Ask mutual friends to talk them out of it together'],answer:0,why:'Mill permits closing an information gap. Beyond that, you are the worse-informed party making their decision.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE USUAL OBJECTION',belief:'Mill\u2019s objection to paternalism is that it is disrespectful.',reality:'His case is evidential. The individual has the greatest interest in their own well-being, and on their own concerns knows far more than anyone else. Society\u2019s interest is fractional and indirect by comparison.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Interference in self-regarding choices is likely to be wrong, because the interferer is less informed, less invested and works from general presumptions. When it is wrong, it lands on someone who had no say.'},{kind:'explain',title:'General presumptions',text:'Interference rests on rules about what people like this usually want. Such presumptions are often wrong, and even when right in general, they are misapplied to the particular case. Mill adds that when the public interferes with purely personal conduct, the odds are that it interferes wrongly, and in the wrong place.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW AN OVERRIDE GOES WRONG',nodes:['A rule about people like you','Applied to one particular life','Missing what only you know','The error lands on you, not the rule-maker']},{kind:'explain',title:'The permitted exception',text:'Mill allows intervention to make sure a person knows what they are choosing. Poison may be labelled. Someone heading onto an unsafe bridge may be warned, or stopped if there is no time to warn them. The aim is an informed choice, not a different one.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The argument assumes the chooser has the information and the capacity to use it. Addiction, compulsion and manipulation strain both, which is where modern paternalism makes its strongest case. In practice, too, the line between supplying a missing fact and steering the choice is far less clean than in principle.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Whose decision about their own life are you trying to change at the moment? What do you know that they do not?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HARD CASE',prompt:'Which intervention fits within Mill\u2019s limit?',options:['Requiring a clear risk warning on a legal but dangerous product','Banning the product because many buyers come to regret it','Taxing it heavily so that fewer people can afford to buy it','Selling it only to buyers an official judges to be sensible'],answer:0,why:'A warning closes an information gap and leaves the choice with the person. The others replace their judgement with someone else\u2019s.'}]}},'on-liberty_9':{pictureCaption:'One exam hall, many doors. The test at the desks is the same for everyone; which door a pupil came through is left open.',bridge:'Experiments in living, from idea 6, applied to schooling: many ways of teaching, one public test of the result.',basics:{title:'School for all, but not one kind of school',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'WHAT MILL DID NOT SAY',belief:'A thinker who loved freedom must have been against making children learn.',reality:'John Stuart Mill, who wrote a book about freedom in 1859, thought every child must learn. He just did not want one group to run all the schools.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Mill wanted the law to make sure every child learns. But he did not want the government to run all the schools. Many kinds of school, one test.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Lots of doors lead into one exam hall. It does not matter which door you came in by. What counts is what you can do at the desk.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why not one kind of school?',text:'Mill worried about one group running every school. Then every child would be taught to think the same way. People would all come out alike. No one would see things a new way.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Mill\u2019s plan',steps:['The law says every child must learn.','Every child takes a test.','Families choose the school.','If a family can\u2019t pay, the state helps.']},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A SWIMMING TEST',text:'To join the swim team, you must swim two lengths. The coach does not mind who taught you. It could be your mum, a club, or a lake in summer. The test checks what you can do, not who taught you.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A town wants every child to read well. Which plan is most like Mill\u2019s?',options:['Test every child, and let families choose the school','Build one school, and every child must go there','Let each family decide if their child learns at all'],answer:0,why:'Mill wanted the result checked for every child. He did not want one school deciding how all of them think.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'OFTEN MISREPORTED',text:'Some cite Mill as against compulsory schooling. Others cite him as a champion of state schools. He was neither. He wanted the law to require that every child be taught. He was against the state doing all the teaching itself.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Require the outcome and test it. Leave the teaching plural. Requiring a result and owning the machinery that makes it are separate decisions.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One exam hall, many doors. The state checks what reaches the desks. Which door a pupil came through is left open.'},{kind:'explain',title:'His reason',text:'A single state system, he argued, moulds people to be like one another. The mould is whatever power happens to rule. Uniform teaching makes uniform minds. Mill calls this a despotism over the mind.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Enforcement without monopoly',steps:['The law requires every child to be educated.','Public exams check the result, starting early.','Parents choose how the teaching is done.','The state pays the fees where families cannot.']},{kind:'try',text:'When you need a standard met, set the outcome and the test, not the method. Owning the method is a separate and larger decision.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A company wants every member of staff competent in data security. Which approach follows Mill\u2019s distinction?',options:['Set a required certification and let teams choose how to prepare','Build one in-house course and make every team take it','Leave security entirely to each person\u2019s own judgement','Hire one outside firm to run all the training centrally'],answer:0,why:'Mill requires the outcome and checks it. He leaves the method plural. A single compulsory course owns the machinery as well.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'BEFORE YOU READ',prompt:'Mill, the great defender of liberty, on schooling. Which was his position?',options:['Against compulsion: parents should decide','For compulsion, and for state-run schools','For compulsion, but wary of state-run schools'],answer:2,reveal:'He wanted the state to require and verify education, and to pay where families could not, while keeping the teaching itself out of any single authority\u2019s hands.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Mandate the outcome, test it publicly, fund it where needed, and leave the teaching plural. Owning the method is a separate and larger power than requiring the result.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The argument',text:'Mill argues that a general state education is a device for making people alike. The mould is set by whatever power dominates, whether a monarch, a priesthood, an aristocracy or the majority of the day, and so it establishes a despotism over the mind.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Consistent with the rest',text:'This is experiments in living applied to schooling: many approaches, results visible, no single authority deciding what a mind should contain. He would allow a state school, but only as one competing experiment among many, kept to set an example and a standard.'},{kind:'term',word:'Public examination',meaning:'Mill\u2019s enforcement tool: state tests, beginning early, of what every child knows, with a fine for a parent whose child failed. On contested subjects, the tests would cover knowledge of the views, not belief in them.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Whether it would work is arguable. Examinations bring their own uniformity: whoever sets the test shapes the teaching. Plural provision can entrench inequality where families cannot judge or reach good schools. Mill himself allowed the state to run schools where a society could not otherwise provide them.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Require and verify the result; fund it where families cannot.','Keep the method plural, so no single mould forms.','Whoever sets the test still shapes the teaching.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HONEST OBJECTION',prompt:'Which objection cuts deepest into Mill\u2019s scheme?',options:['A single national exam can impose uniformity by the back door','Parents cannot be trusted to want an education for their children','Compulsory schooling contradicts his own defence of liberty','Private schools will always teach better than state ones'],answer:0,why:'Mill kept the teaching plural to avoid a mould. But the test can become the mould, since teaching follows whatever is examined.'}]}}}},2992,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'on-the-shortness-of-life_1':{pictureCaption:'One hand locks the money box with care. The other lets the sand, which is time, pour out to anyone holding out a hand.',basics:{title:'Life is long, if you don\u2019t waste it',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'YOUR MONEY BOX',text:'You keep your pocket money in a box. You know how much is in it. If a kid at school asked for some, you would say no. But when someone asks for an hour of your time, you just give it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'You guard your money, but you give your time away without a thought. Life is not too short. We waste so much of it that it feels short.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Look at the two hands. One locks the money box with care. The other lets the sand pour out of the door, to anyone who asks.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'TWO KIDS, SAME MONEY',text:'Two kids get the same pocket money. One spends it with care, and it lasts all month. The other spends a bit here and a bit there. By Tuesday it is gone. Nobody says the second kid was given too little.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where does the time go?',text:'Seneca was a thinker in Rome almost 2,000 years ago. He made a list of where people\u2019s time goes. Nothing on it was big. Too much sleep. Trying to impress people. Being jealous. Changing your mind. Small leaks add up, and you never see them go.'},{kind:'try',text:'Count how many times today you say yes to something without thinking. Just count. Don\u2019t change anything yet.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Mia says she never has time to draw. She spends two hours a day on short videos. What would Seneca say?',options:['She has the time, but it leaks away in small bits','She needs a longer school holiday','Drawing is not worth her time'],answer:0,why:'Mia\u2019s day is as long as anyone\u2019s. The time is there. It just goes in small bits she does not notice.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE USUAL COMPLAINT',belief:'The trouble is that there are not enough hours in the day.',reality:'Seneca\u2019s answer is that the hours are there. We have made our lives short, he argues, rather than found them short. The complaint describes how the time was spent, not how much of it there was.'},{kind:'idea',text:'You are not short of time. You are careless with it: you guard your money closely and hand out your hours to anyone who asks.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'HIS COMPARISON',text:'Seneca compares a life to an estate. A good manager makes a modest income go a long way. A royal fortune in a spendthrift\u2019s hands is gone in no time. When that happens, nobody says the spendthrift was underpaid. They say he spent it badly.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'ONE ORDINARY TUESDAY',text:'Ten minutes agreeing to a call you didn\u2019t need. Twenty scrolling after lunch. Half an hour in a meeting where you said nothing. An hour replaying an argument. None of it is a disaster, which is exactly why the total, about two hours, never gets noticed.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The money is locked away with great care. The sand, which is the time, goes out of the door to whoever holds out a hand.'},{kind:'try',text:'For one week, notice the requests you say yes to without thinking. Don\u2019t refuse them yet. Just mark each one. Those reflex yeses are the ones worth checking.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A friend says she would love to learn the piano but is simply too busy. What is Seneca\u2019s first move?',options:['Ask where the hours of her ordinary week actually go','Agree that some lives really are too full for hobbies','Suggest she sleeps less to make room for practice','Tell her the piano is not worth the effort anyway'],answer:0,why:'Seneca reads \u201cno time\u201d as a description of spending, not of supply. The first step is to look at where the hours go.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'ROME, MID-FIRST CENTURY CE',text:'Seneca was a Stoic philosopher and statesman who became tutor and adviser to the emperor Nero. He addressed this essay to a friend, Paulinus, who ran Rome\u2019s grain supply, urging him to step back from office. It opens by attacking the most common complaint of all: that life is too short.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Life is long enough if it is used well. It feels short because we spend it carelessly, in small amounts nobody counts, while guarding everything else we own.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Made short, not found short',text:'Seneca turns the complaint around. People say nature gave them too little time. He says the amount was adequate and the shortness was manufactured, by the person living it. The complaint is sincere, but it is a report on spending, not on supply.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the itemising matters',text:'The essay then lists where the time went: drink, sleep, ambition, flattery, envy, aimless study, changing course. None is a catastrophe. Each is ordinary and easy to defend. That is the point. Small, defensible losses never trigger alarm, so the total is never seen, and the end feels like a robbery rather than a bill.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The careful hand and the careless one belong to the same person. The care is real. It is simply pointed at the wrong possession.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Seneca writes as a rich man to a rich friend, both with real choice over their days. For someone working two jobs or caring for a sick parent, the hours are not carelessly spent; they are taken. And not every hour given to others is lost. Time freely given to people you love may be the best use of it.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which of your small, defensible habits would you least like to see added up over a year?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'Someone objects that people die young, so life really is short for some. What is the fairest reply on Seneca\u2019s behalf?',options:['His claim is about how most lives are spent, not a promise that every life is long','Early death is rare enough that the objection can safely be set aside','An early death shows that time was wasted earlier in that life','How long a life lasts has nothing to do with how it was lived'],answer:0,why:'His target is the complaint of people who had the years and spent them carelessly. He does not deny that some lives are cut short.'}]}},'on-the-shortness-of-life_2':{pictureCaption:'Head down in paper, back to the open door. The busy figure is working hard, and missing the only bright thing in the room.',bridge:'Idea 1 was about time leaking away in small amounts. This one is about time fully used, but used on other people\u2019s plans.',basics:{title:'Busy is not the same as living',beats:[{kind:'picture',kicker:'LOOK AT THE DOOR',caption:'This man is working very hard. Papers are piled all round him. But look behind him. The door to a sunny garden is open, and he has not looked up once.'},{kind:'idea',text:'You can be busy all day and still not live your own life. What counts is how much of your time you chose.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'ONE WEEK',left:{title:'Given to you',items:['School','Homework you were set','Chores','Clubs you were signed up for']},right:{title:'Chosen by you',items:['The book you picked','The game you made up','Time with a friend you asked']},note:'Both lists are fine. The question is how long each one is.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'AN OLD MAN ADDS IT UP',text:'Seneca was a thinker in Rome almost 2,000 years ago. He pictured an old man adding up his life. The man had been busy every day. But when he counted, very few years had been his own. He had given the rest to other people\u2019s plans.'},{kind:'try',text:'Look back at yesterday. Mark each part as *chosen* or *given*. Which list is longer?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Sam has football, piano and extra maths every day. He says he is never bored. What would Seneca ask him?',options:['Which of these did you choose?','Why not add one more club?','Are you good at all of them?'],answer:0,why:'Being busy is not the problem. Seneca wants to know whose plan the busy time belongs to.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'A FULL WEEK',text:'Your calendar is booked in half-hour blocks, nearly all of them set by other people. By Friday you are exhausted. Someone asks what you did this week that was yours, and you cannot name one thing.'},{kind:'term',kicker:'SENECA\u2019S WORD',word:'The occupied',meaning:'Seneca\u2019s name, *occupati* in Latin, for people whose days are full and belong to someone else: clients, patrons, lawsuits, social duties. Busy, respectable, and always about to start living.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A full diary is not a full life. The measure is not how much you did, but how many of your hours you chose.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it hides so well',text:'The occupied are not lazy. They are the opposite. Their lives look industrious from outside, and that is the trap: effort feels like progress. Seneca\u2019s complaint is that they mistake motion for direction, and keep promising themselves that real life will start once this busy season ends. It never ends.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'He is working flat out, with his back to the open door. Nothing in the picture is lazy. It is all pointed the wrong way.'},{kind:'try',text:'Look back at last week. Mark each block of time *chosen* or *assigned*. If almost all of it was assigned, you have found what Seneca is describing.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Which of these people best fits Seneca\u2019s idea of the occupied?',options:['A manager whose diary others fill, who plans to read \u201cafter this quarter\u201d','A retired teacher who spends long mornings gardening because she loves it','A nurse working a hard shift in the job she trained for and wanted','A student who takes a whole afternoon off to read a novel'],answer:0,why:'Being busy is not the fault; the nurse is busy too. The occupied are those whose hours belong to others while their own life waits.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'THE LATIN',word:'*Occupati*',meaning:'The occupied, from a verb meaning to seize or take over. Seneca\u2019s label for people whose time has been taken over by other people\u2019s business: patrons, clients, lawsuits, the round of social duty in Rome.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Activity is not life. The occupied give their days to other people\u2019s purposes and treat their own life as something that starts later, once the demands ease. The demands never ease.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE RECKONING',title:'The old man\u2019s sum',text:'Seneca imagines an old man asked to account for his years: how much went to creditors, patrons, clients, quarrels, errands across the city. Subtract it all, and he has far fewer years than his age suggests. He was not idle. He was industrious, and almost none of it was his.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW IT SUSTAINS ITSELF',nodes:['Others\u2019 demands fill the days','Effort feels like progress','Your own aims wait for later','Later fills with new demands'],loop:!0,note:'Nothing in the loop feels like failure. That is why it can run for decades.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'All effort, back turned to the open door. The occupied are not idle. Their industry is exactly what keeps them from turning round.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What he was writing against',text:'Seneca is attacking a respectable life, not a disreputable one: the morning calls on patrons, the courts, the public posts that Romans of his class were expected to fill. His target is the person everyone admires, which is why the essay still unsettles readers whose diaries are full for good reasons.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The test of chosen hours is a luxury. Most people, then and now, have days largely assigned by work and care, and Seneca wrote to men rich enough to retire. He was no model himself: he stayed at Nero\u2019s court for years and grew immensely rich. And assigned time is not always lost. Duties freely accepted can be the hours most your own.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISREADING',prompt:'Which reading of the idea goes wrong?',options:['Busy people are wasting their lives, so the answer is to do less','An hour can be completely full and still not be yours','Hard work towards an aim you chose is not what he attacks','The danger is that effort hides the question of direction'],answer:0,why:'His test is not the amount of activity but whose purpose it serves. Doing less only helps if the freed time becomes yours.'}]}},'on-the-shortness-of-life_3':{pictureCaption:'Coat buttoned, bag on lap, ready to leave. The door is right beside them, and the handle has not been touched.',bridge:'The occupied keep saying their real life will start later. This idea looks hard at that word, later.',basics:{title:'Later is how time gets lost',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'YOU WANT TO LEARN GUITAR',prompt:'You say you will start when things calm down. When will things calm down?',options:['After the tests','In the summer holidays','Maybe never, on their own'],answer:2,reveal:'After the tests there is a new term. After the summer there is a new year. Something always fills the space. *Later* keeps moving away.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Putting things off is the main way life slips by. We plan to start later. But later is not promised, and it is rarely calmer.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'This person has their coat on and their bag on their lap. They are all set to go. The door is right there. They have not tried the handle.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A WAITING ROOM',text:'Think of sitting in a waiting room all day, ready to be called. Nobody calls. The day ends and you never went in. Putting things off turns your days into a waiting room.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHAT SENECA SAW',text:'Seneca was a thinker in Rome almost 2,000 years ago. He saw people plan to start really living when they were old, once their work was done. But nobody knows how much time they have left.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one thing you keep saying you will start *later*. Do the smallest bit of it today. Five minutes counts.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Which of these is putting life off?',options:['I will learn to swim when I am older and braver','I will go swimming on Saturday at ten','I swam for twenty minutes today'],answer:0,why:'Only *when I am older* hangs on a *later* that may not come. The others have a real time.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'AFTER THIS PROJECT',text:'You will start properly after this project: the running, the course, the book you have meant to write. You said the same about the last project, and the one before. Each time, the reason was true.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Postponement is how most life is lost. It spends time without seeming to spend anything, and it draws on a supply of future that nobody has promised you.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE CLAUSE THAT REPEATS',nodes:['Now is too busy','Plan to begin later','Later arrives, also busy'],loop:!0,note:'Each pass round the loop feels reasonable. The loop is the problem, not any single turn.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it slips past you',text:'A bad day leaves a mark. A postponed thing leaves nothing. No hour was visibly wasted and nothing was done badly; it simply was not done, on the understanding that it would be done later. Seneca\u2019s point is that *later* is drawn on an account whose balance you cannot see.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Dressed to go and sitting still, with the door within reach. The waiting has become the whole visit.'},{kind:'try',text:'Name one thing you have been deferring to a calmer future. Take the smallest real step on it this week.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Which plan is the kind Seneca is warning about?',options:['Learning the language properly once life is less hectic','Booking a language class for next Tuesday evening','Deciding not to learn the language after all','Practising the language for ten minutes on the train'],answer:0,why:'It hangs a real wish on a condition with no date. The others are either a decision or a start.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'NOT A CALL TO HURRY',belief:'Seneca is saying: life is short, so cram it all in now.',reality:'His remedy is not urgency. It is noticing that one clause, *when things settle down*, has been running your plans for years, and will be just as available next year.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Postponement is the main way life is lost, because it consumes time at no visible cost and assumes a future that no one has guaranteed.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it is the central target',text:'Most waste is at least visible: an afternoon frittered, a night lost to drink. Postponement leaves no trace. Nothing is done badly; nothing is done at all. It is singled out because it is the one habit that spends time while appearing to save it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The unseen balance',text:'Every plan to begin later is a loan drawn on the future. Seneca\u2019s objection is that no one has seen the balance. The assumption that there will be enough time is never stated, so it is never examined. He mocks men who plan to retire into a better life at fifty or sixty, and asks who guaranteed them those years.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Coat on, bag on lap, the door within reach and unopened. A whole life can be spent like this, ready to begin.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Some deferral is simply good judgement: you save before you buy, and train before you race. The argument is aimed at the open-ended *later*, tied to no date and no condition that could ever be met. Pushed too far, it becomes a case for recklessness, or a source of guilt for people whose delays are forced on them.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Postponement spends time while looking like saving it.','Later is drawn on an account whose balance nobody has seen.','The cure is noticing the repeating clause, not panic.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'Which of these is closest to Seneca\u2019s worry?',options:['A plan to begin \u201conce things are quieter\u201d, repeated for five years','Saving for two years before starting a business','Delaying a hard conversation until both people are calm','Waiting for an injury to heal before running again'],answer:0,why:'The others defer for a reason with an end in sight. *Once things are quieter* defers to a condition that keeps renewing itself.'}]}},'on-the-shortness-of-life_4':{pictureCaption:'One reader, one lamp, one evening. The open book holds the years someone else spent thinking, and tonight they are added to his.',bridge:'The occupied, Seneca says, have no time to look back. This idea is about what looking back can give you.',basics:{title:'Borrow the years of people before you',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE BEST DEN',text:'You and your friends argue for weeks about how to build the best den. Then you find a book by someone who built dens for thirty years. In one evening you know what took them a lifetime to learn.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Reading what people long ago worked out is like adding their years to yours. You get a lifetime of their thinking for the price of an evening.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'ONE EVENING',caption:'One lamp, one book, one evening. The pages hold years of someone else\u2019s thinking. By bedtime, some of it is his too.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A MAP',text:'Someone walked a long, hard path. They drew a map of it, with the wrong turns marked. You only get to walk your own life once. But with their map, you can skip their wrong turns.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'CHOOSE YOUR FAMILY',text:'Seneca was a thinker in Rome almost 2,000 years ago. He said you can\u2019t choose the family you are born into. But you can choose the wise people of the past as your family. None of them will say no.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHAT YOU CAN LEARN FROM',left:{title:'Just your own life',items:['One try at each thing','Only your own mistakes']},right:{title:'Your life, plus books',items:['Hundreds of tries','Other people\u2019s mistakes too']}},{kind:'try',text:'Ask a librarian for a book by someone who spent their life on a thing you love. Read ten pages.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Ava wants to be a good goalkeeper. Which plan uses this idea best?',options:['Read how great keepers of the past trained, and what they got wrong','Learn only from her own mistakes in matches','Wait until she is older to start learning'],answer:0,why:'Her own matches give her one life of practice. Reading adds the lessons of many keepers before her.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THIRTY YEARS FOR AN EVENING',prompt:'Your team has argued for a month about a question someone else spent thirty years on. What does their thinking cost you?',options:['Thirty years','A course and a fee','An evening'],answer:2,reveal:'They paid for it in decades. You can have the result in a few hours. Seneca thought this the best bargain open to anyone.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Reading people who spent their lives on a question is the cheapest way to extend your own. You do not get more years. You get more lives to reason from.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'A LIFE, BORROWED',caption:'The shelves behind are in the dark. Under the lamp, one finished life lies open on the table, and the reader is borrowing it for an evening.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Adoption, not inheritance',text:'Seneca frames it as choosing your ancestors. You cannot pick the family you were born into, but you can adopt yourself into the family of the great minds of the past. Nobody refuses you, and nothing has to be repaid.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHAT YOU REASON FROM',left:{title:'Your own experience',items:['One life, lived once','No comparison','Your own mistakes only']},right:{title:'Plus the dead',items:['Hundreds of finished lives','Endings already known','Including the ones that failed']}},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the busy miss it',text:'Seneca sets this against the busy person, who has no time to look back. Consumed by the present, they reason only from what has happened to them. That is a single life, run once, with nothing to compare it to.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one writer who spent a lifetime on a question you care about, and give them one evening this week.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A new manager is struggling with how to give hard feedback. Which step best follows the idea?',options:['Read a writer who spent decades thinking about honesty and kindness','Keep trying different approaches until one seems to work','Ask her team every week how she is getting on','Let a few years of experience teach her in time'],answer:0,why:'Her own trial and error is one slow experiment. Others have already run many, and their results cost an evening.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'LATE IN THE ESSAY',text:'After pages of attack on the occupied, Seneca turns to who is actually living. Only those, he argues, who make time for philosophy. They alone are truly at leisure, because they add every past age to their own. The busy have no leisure to look back. The reader of the philosophers has all of it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Reading the dead adds to your life, not in years but in experience. The years you live are your own. The years others spent working things out are open to anyone, and free.'},{kind:'term',kicker:'A ROMAN PRACTICE',word:'Adoption',meaning:'Among the Roman elite, adopting an adult heir was common. Seneca borrows it: you cannot pick your birth family, but you can adopt yourself into the house of Socrates or Zeno, and none will refuse you.'},{kind:'explain',title:'More cases, not more years',text:'Someone reasoning only from their own life has one experiment, run once, with no comparison. A reader has hundreds of finished ones, including the lives that ended badly. That is the real extension: more cases to think with, inside the one life you get.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'ONE CASE OF HUNDREDS',caption:'The shelves hold hundreds of finished lives. One is open under the lamp tonight: another case to reason from, inside the one life the reader gets.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Seneca means philosophy, not reading in general. In the same essay he scorns scholars who spend their leisure on trivia, such as how many rowers Ulysses had. Reading can become one more form of busyness. And the dead bring their own blind spots: their lives are finished experiments to learn from, not verdicts to adopt whole.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which question are you trying to answer only from your own experience, when someone has already spent a life on it?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these would Seneca count as a misuse of his argument?',options:['Spending every evening collecting obscure facts about ancient battles','Reading a philosopher on grief after losing a parent','Studying how earlier leaders handled a crisis like yours','Comparing several writers who disagree on the same question'],answer:0,why:'Reading extends a life when it gives you more lives to reason from. Hoarding trivia is one more way of being busy.'}]}},'on-the-shortness-of-life_5':{pictureCaption:'The sofa is behind the glass, slumped and abandoned. The figure has chosen a hard step and an upright back: free time, used on purpose.',bridge:'Idea 4 said the busy have no leisure to look back. This one asks what leisure actually is.',basics:{title:'Free time is not the same as doing nothing',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'A COMMON MIX-UP',belief:'Free time means lying on the sofa and doing nothing.',reality:'Seneca, a thinker in Rome almost 2,000 years ago, said there are two kinds. Doing nothing is one. Real free time is time you use for something you chose, like thinking, reading or making plans.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Doing nothing just fills an empty hour. Real free time is an hour you decide how to use before it starts.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO SATURDAYS',left:{title:'Doing nothing',items:['Lie down and scroll','The day just drifts','Can\u2019t say where it went']},right:{title:'Real free time',items:['Pick one thing first','Build, read or think','Can say what it was for']},note:'Rest is good too. It just isn\u2019t the same thing.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The sofa is behind the glass. This person has sat up straight on the step, on purpose. They are using their free time, not just filling it.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'TEST IT',text:'Your mum asks what you did with your free afternoon. If you can say you built a ramp, or planned your story, it was real free time. If you can only shrug, it just drifted.'},{kind:'try',text:'This weekend, pick one hour. Before it starts, decide what it is for. Then see if it feels different.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Which hour is real free time, the kind Seneca meant?',options:['An hour you set aside to sketch','An hour of whatever comes on next','An hour you can\u2019t remember later'],answer:0,why:'It was chosen before it began, and you can say what it was for.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'A CLEAR SATURDAY',text:'You finally clear a whole Saturday. No plans. By evening it has gone: your phone, a nap, some tidying, a show you half watched. You feel no different. You have not failed at resting. You had the wrong kind of free time.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Leisure is not the absence of work. Idleness is an empty slot. Leisure is time turned, by a decision, to something you chose. Only leisure changes anything.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the slot fills',text:'An unoccupied hour does not stay empty. It fills with whatever is nearest. In Rome, Seneca says, that meant the games, grooming, and the endless managing of other people\u2019s opinions. The list translates without much adjustment. Leisure is harder than work, because nobody else is setting the task.'},{kind:'equation',kicker:'WHAT TURNS ONE INTO THE OTHER',parts:['Free time','A purpose set beforehand'],result:'Leisure',note:'Without the second part, the first fills with whatever is closest to hand.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Behind the glass, the sofa. On the step, someone sitting upright on purpose. The same free evening, put to two very different uses.'},{kind:'try',text:'Protect one hour a week that is neither work nor recovery. Decide in advance what it is for. Thinking about where your life is heading counts.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE TEST',prompt:'At the end of a free Sunday, which question best shows whether it was leisure in Seneca\u2019s sense?',options:['Can I say what the time was spent on?','Did I avoid doing any work at all?','Do I feel properly rested now?','Did I get through my list of chores?'],answer:0,why:'His test is whether the time can give an account of itself. Rest and chores have their place, but neither is the test.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'THE LATIN',word:'*Otium*',meaning:'Leisure: time free from public business. Its opposite, *negotium*, business, is literally not-leisure. Seneca does not praise *otium* as rest. For him, true leisure is demanding: reflection, study, attention to what a life is for.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Leisure and idleness look identical from outside. Idleness is a vacancy. Leisure is a decision, made in advance, about what the vacancy is for.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Busy idleness',text:'Seneca is sharpest on people who seem to be at leisure and are not: those who spend free days on their hair, their collections, the games, the management of what others think of them. Their hours are free of work and fully occupied by trivia. He counts them among the busy.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'WHERE AN HOUR SITS',low:'Idle: filled by whatever is nearest',high:'Leisure: aimed by a prior decision',note:'Recovery sits in between. It is necessary, and it is not what Seneca means by leisure.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The slumped sofa behind the glass is idleness, abandoned. The figure on the step is doing nothing visible, which is not the same as doing nothing.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Seneca\u2019s leisure assumed a household run by others, many of them enslaved. His impatience with mere recovery reads differently to someone who has just worked a double shift. Rest is not a failure of leisure; it is often what makes leisure possible. The idea works as a direction for some free time, not a rule for all of it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'Someone objects: so am I never allowed simply to rest? What is the best reply?',options:['Rest is needed, but it restores you; chosen time sets direction','No: Seneca thought any unplanned hour was a wasted hour','Rest already counts as leisure as long as you enjoy it','Leisure means filling every free hour with serious study'],answer:0,why:'The distinction is not a ban on rest. Rest restores you; leisure is where you decide what you are restored for.'}]}},'on-the-shortness-of-life_6':{pictureCaption:'The hands count every coin. Behind them, someone walks out of the door with a pair of clock hands, and nobody is watching.',bridge:'Idea 1 named this lopsided habit. This one asks why it happens, and how to correct it.',basics:{title:'Nobody guards their time',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'WHICH WOULD YOU NOTICE?',prompt:'Which would you notice first?',options:['A \xa35 note gone from your pocket','An hour gone from your Saturday'],answer:0,reveal:'You would spot the money at once. You can see it and count it. An hour slips away and leaves no gap you can see.'},{kind:'idea',text:'We guard our things, but we let anyone take our time. That is because time does not show how much is left.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'This person counts every coin with care. Behind them, someone is walking out of the door with a pair of clock hands. Nobody stops them.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHY IT HAPPENS',text:'Seneca was a thinker in Rome almost 2,000 years ago. He saw people fight over a strip of land, then give their days to anyone who asked. Money gets less where you can see it. Time does not. So a request for time never feels like much.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'ONE MORE LEVEL?',text:'A game asks if you want one more level. You tap yes. Then again. Each yes felt free. But the hour you meant for building your model has gone.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before you say yes to something today, say the time to yourself first: *this is twenty minutes.* Then decide.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Why do people give away time more easily than money?',options:['You can\u2019t see time running out','Time is worth less than money','People have more time than money'],answer:0,why:'Money leaves a gap when it goes. Time doesn\u2019t, so a request for it never feels like a big ask.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO KINDS OF PROPERTY',left:{title:'Your money',items:['Locked away','Counted and checked','Lent only after thought']},right:{title:'Your time',items:['Left in the open','Never counted','Handed to whoever asks']},note:'Seneca\u2019s point: no one would give an estate to a passer-by, yet everyone gives away their days.'},{kind:'idea',text:'People guard their property fiercely and give their time to anyone who asks, because time has no visible balance. Make the price visible and the giving changes.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the request doesn\u2019t register',text:'Money declines in a way you can see. A wasted afternoon leaves no mark on any account. So *just half an hour* is treated as a social move, not a transaction, and refusing feels rude precisely because nothing seems to be at stake.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'SAY THE PRICE',text:'A colleague asks you to sit in on a meeting. Before you answer, you say the cost to yourself: forty minutes, out of the four good hours I have today. Put that way, it is a real request. Sometimes you still say yes. Often you don\u2019t.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Every coin is counted. Behind the table, someone is carrying a pair of clock hands out of the door, and the person counting never looks up.'},{kind:'try',text:'For one week, before agreeing to any meeting, say its cost to yourself first: *this is forty minutes.* Notice how often you would not have paid it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A neighbour asks if you could help with a quick job on Saturday. Which response applies the idea?',options:['Ask how long it will take, and weigh that against your Saturday','Say yes at once, since refusing a neighbour would be rude','Say no, because your own time should always come first','Agree, then quietly resent the lost afternoon'],answer:0,why:'The idea is not to refuse. It is to make the price visible before you decide, so the yes or no is a real choice.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE ASYMMETRY',text:'Seneca observes that people will go to law over a boundary and take up arms over a strip of land, yet let anyone walk into their lives. No one would hand an estate to a passer-by. Everyone hands over their days. They are thrifty with possessions and lavish with the one thing where thrift would be a virtue.'},{kind:'idea',text:'We defend property because we can see it decline. Time has no visible balance, so a request for it never registers as a request for anything. The fix is to make the cost visible.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The missing ledger',text:'Money leaves a trace: the balance falls, the purse is lighter. An hour leaves none. A request for time is therefore handled as a social exchange rather than a transaction, and declining feels rude because nothing appears to be at stake. The failure is less moral than perceptual.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The counting is careful and the loss is unwatched. Attention follows what is visible, not what is valuable.'},{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE WRONG LESSON',belief:'So guard your time like money, and refuse whatever you can.',reality:'The corrective is visibility, not refusal. Say the price, forty minutes of the four good hours I have today, and then decide. Many requests survive the pricing. The point is that they become choices.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Pricing every request can turn generosity into bookkeeping. Friendship, care and citizenship do not run well as transactions, and someone who costs every favour may protect their hours and lose the people they would have spent them on. The idea also assumes power over your own diary, which many people do not have.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Property is guarded because its loss is visible.','Time has no visible balance, so requests for it slip through.','Say the price first; then the yes or no is a real choice.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these is a misuse of the idea?',options:['Refusing to visit a sick friend because the hour is not worth it','Asking how long a meeting will run before accepting it','Noticing that a weekly call costs a full working day each month','Declining a favour after honestly weighing what it would cost'],answer:0,why:'The idea makes the cost visible so you can choose. It does not say every hour given to someone else is a loss.'}]}},'on-the-shortness-of-life_7':{pictureCaption:'The water is churned all round, and the rope is still tied. A great deal of motion, and the boat has gone nowhere.',bridge:'Idea 2 asked whose plan your busy hours serve. This one asks a blunter question: did all that motion take you anywhere?',basics:{title:'Moving a lot is not getting somewhere',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A HAMSTER WHEEL',text:'A hamster runs and runs on its wheel. It gets tired. It works hard. But at the end it is in the same spot where it started.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Being busy can feel like getting somewhere when you are not. The real test is this: what is different now?'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Look at the water. The boat has thrashed about for hours. But the rope is still tied, and it has not left the harbour.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'SENECA\u2019S SHIP',text:'Seneca lived in Rome almost 2,000 years ago. He said some people are like a ship caught by a storm as it leaves harbour. It is blown round in circles. Lots of motion. No trip. Busy days feel full, so you never stop to ask if you moved.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'A WHOLE SUMMER',text:'Ask what is different after the summer. Maybe you can swim further now, or you made a new friend. If you can\u2019t name one thing, the summer was busy, but it went nowhere.'},{kind:'try',text:'Tonight, don\u2019t list what you did today. Write one thing that is different now. If you can\u2019t, that is worth knowing.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Ben did lots of things all term. Which one shows he got somewhere?',options:['He can now play three songs on guitar','He went to lots of clubs','He was tired every evening'],answer:0,why:'Clubs and being tired show he was busy. Only the songs show that something changed.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'picture',kicker:'SEEN FROM ABOVE',caption:'Churned water, a taut rope, no distance travelled. Seneca\u2019s image for a full life that goes nowhere is a ship caught by a storm leaving harbour and driven in circles.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Activity is not voyage. Busyness produces the feeling of a full life all day long, which is exactly why it can use up years without anyone noticing.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'WHERE DID MARCH GO?',text:'Someone remarks that the year has gone quickly, and you agree. Now try to account for March. You were busy every day of it. You cannot name a single thing that was different at the end of the month from the start.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why busyness hides itself',text:'The idle at least know they are idle. Busyness has a defence that satisfies everyone, including you: look how much I am doing. It feels like progress all day, so the question of direction never comes up. That may be why, on a fair reading, Seneca seems harder on the busy than on the idle.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'ONE MONTH, TWO LISTS',left:{title:'What is different',items:['One process fixed']},right:{title:'What I did',items:['Forty meetings','Three hundred emails','Two work trips']},note:'The right column is longer. The left one is the answer to Seneca\u2019s question.'},{kind:'try',text:'At the end of this month, write what is different rather than what you did. If the two lists tell different stories, believe the first.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Which line from a review of the year would pass Seneca\u2019s test?',options:['I can now run a meeting without any notes','I attended over two hundred meetings','I was busy almost every single weekend','I answered every email within a day'],answer:0,why:'Only running a meeting without notes names something different at the end. The rest measure motion.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'WHO GETS THE HARSHER WORDS?',prompt:'On a fair reading, Seneca is harder on one of these. Which?',options:['The idle, who do nothing','The busy, who never stop'],answer:1,reveal:'He never says so outright. But the idle know what they are doing. The busy have a defence that satisfies everyone, themselves included, so they never reach the question of where it is all going.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Busyness conceals itself. It produces the feeling of a full life continuously, so activity can stand in for a voyage for decades. The test is what is different now.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The image',text:'Seneca warns against judging a life by grey hair and wrinkles. A man who has lived long may only have existed long. He is like a ship caught by a storm as it leaves harbour and driven round in circles: plenty of motion, no voyage.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Every ring in the water is effort, and none of it is distance. Measured by motion, this boat has had a long day.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHY NOBODY NOTICES',nodes:['Constant activity','The feeling of a full life','Direction never questioned'],loop:!0,note:'Busyness is the output as well as the input. The loop needs nothing else to keep running.'},{kind:'explain',title:'A test that looks back',text:'The test is retrospective and blunt: what is different now that was not before? If a summer gives no answer, the problem is not that you lay down. You were almost certainly busy. Asked late, the question is harder to settle, which is the reason to ask it now.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Not every worthwhile year changes something. Caring for a dying parent, holding a business together, keeping a household afloat: these can leave everything the same, and the sameness is the achievement. The test catches drift. It is not a demand that every season show progress.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'A nurse says her year changed nothing: she spent it keeping people alive. What is the fairest response?',options:['Keeping things steady on purpose is itself a difference','She was busy, so by Seneca\u2019s test the year was wasted','The test only applies to people with office jobs','She should have used the year to change careers'],answer:0,why:'The test catches activity that stands in for direction. Keeping something from getting worse, on purpose, is a direction.'}]}},'on-the-shortness-of-life_8':{pictureCaption:'The hourglass sits at the top of the sheet, the coins at the bottom. Time is priced first, before the money.',bridge:'Idea 6 made the price of time visible. This one says where to put that price when you weigh a decision: first.',basics:{title:'Count the time first',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'A SUMMER JOB',text:'Your neighbour offers you \xa35 each time you walk her dog. The catch: it is every Saturday morning, all summer. \xa35 sounds good. But what about all those Saturday mornings?'},{kind:'idea',text:'When you decide on something, count the time first. Money and effort can come back later. Time you spend never comes back.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why time goes first',text:'Seneca was a thinker in Rome almost 2,000 years ago. He saw that people count money, effort and fuss. They leave time out. But you can earn money again. You can never get last Saturday back.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Before you say yes',steps:['Ask how much time it will take.','Ask if that time is worth it.','Only then think about what you get.'],note:'Sometimes the answer is still yes. That\u2019s fine. Now it is a real choice.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'TOP OF THE PAGE',caption:'Look at the page. The sand timer is at the top. The coins are at the bottom. Time comes first, and money comes after.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time someone asks you to join something, ask first: how long will it take? Then decide.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Two clubs want you. One gives a free T-shirt. One meets for less time. What should you look at first?',options:['How much of your week each one takes','Which T-shirt looks better','Which club your friends picked'],answer:0,why:'A T-shirt can be replaced. The hours each week cannot.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A WELL-PAID PROJECT',prompt:'A project pays well and will take most of your evenings for six months. What do most people weigh first?',options:['The fee against the effort','The six months of evenings','What their friends would think'],answer:0,reveal:'Almost everyone starts with the fee. Seneca would start with the six months, and only then look at the money.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Time is the only input that cannot be recovered, so it belongs at the top of the sum. Cost it first, not last.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the order changes the answer',text:'The fee is clear and large. The evenings are spread out and invisible until they have gone. Put the fee first and the time becomes whatever the money requires. Put the time first and you ask a different question: is there anything I would trade six months of evenings for?'},{kind:'steps',title:'Pricing a commitment',steps:['Write the time cost at the top of the page.','Ask whether you would pay that for anything.','Only then write the fee, or the benefit, beneath it.'],note:'Money lost can be earned back. Six months cannot.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'THE PAGE, LAID OUT',caption:'Before a word is written, the hourglass is set at the top of the page. The coins wait at the bottom, to be weighed against it.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'HOW IT PLAYS OUT',text:'Priced fee-first, the project is an easy yes. Priced time-first, it is half a year of evenings taken from your family, your training, your sleep. You might still take it. But you would take it knowing the real price, and you might ask for fewer evenings.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before accepting your next big commitment, write the time cost at the top of the page, above the money.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A friend is weighing a second job that pays well but takes every Saturday for a year. Which first question follows Seneca?',options:['Is a year of Saturdays worth giving up for anything?','Is the pay a fair rate for the work involved?','Will the job look good on my CV later on?','Can I cope with the extra tiredness on Mondays?'],answer:0,why:'The Saturdays are the input that cannot be recovered. Seneca puts them at the top of the sum, before the pay.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHAT PEOPLE COUNT',title:'An odd ordering',text:'Seneca notes that people prize salaries, gifts and favours, and hire out their labour to get them. Time, the thing they pay with, they treat as if it cost nothing, because nobody can see it. The most valuable input is the only one left out of the account.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Everything else lost can in principle be replaced. Time cannot. So it should be priced first, at the top of the sum, not left as the slack that absorbs whatever the other numbers require.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO DO THE SUM',left:{title:'Fee first',items:['Start from the money','Time absorbs whatever is needed','Six months is a detail']},right:{title:'Time first',items:['Start from the six months','Ask if anything is worth that','Then look at the fee']}},{kind:'explain',title:'Why order matters',text:'Whatever goes first anchors the rest. A large, legible fee makes diffuse evenings look small; they stay invisible until spent. Starting from the time reverses which number has to justify itself. Seneca is not arguing against work. He is arguing that the scarcest input belongs where scarce inputs go: at the top.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'TIME FIRST, DRAWN',caption:'The hourglass anchors the top of the sheet. The coins sit beneath it, and they are the number that has to justify itself.'},{kind:'keyline',text:'Money lost can be earned back. There is no shop that sells last Tuesday.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Time is not always the scarcest input. For someone in debt, money now may buy back far more time later, and a fee can purchase freedom. Hours also differ in value: an evening you would have spent drained is not one spent with your children. Pricing time first corrects a neglect. It is not a rule that time always wins.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which commitment are you in now that you priced fee-first? What would it look like priced the other way?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'Which decision best applies the idea outside paid work?',options:['Asking how many weekends a renovation will take before comparing quotes','Choosing the cheapest builder to keep the budget down','Comparing three quotes on price and finish alone','Picking the builder a trusted friend recommended'],answer:0,why:'The weekends are the part that cannot be recovered. Pricing them first changes what the quotes are being compared against.'}]}},'on-the-shortness-of-life_9':{pictureCaption:'Night, a rough path, and a spade already at the gate. Conditions are poor. The first spadeful is still possible.',bridge:'Idea 3 showed how postponement loses time. This one takes on the best reason for waiting, and answers it.',basics:{title:'Start small, start now',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE BAND',text:'You and a friend want to start a band. But you say you will wait. Wait until you have a real drum kit. Wait until you are older. Wait until you have more time.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Waiting for the perfect moment is risky, because nobody knows how much time they have. Start a small version now, with what you have.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'It is dark and the path is rough. Not a perfect time to dig. But the spade is right there by the gate.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHY NOT WAIT?',text:'Seneca was a thinker in Rome almost 2,000 years ago. He saw people plan to start living when they were old. Many never got there. Your reasons to wait may be good. But next year there will be new good reasons.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Find the small version',steps:['Name the thing you are waiting to start.','Ask: what tiny version could I do now?','Do it this week, even if it is rough.'],note:'Two friends and a box to bang is a band.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Kai wants to make films, but he has no camera. What fits this idea best?',options:['Make a short film on a phone this week','Wait until he can buy a proper camera','Read about films until he is older'],answer:0,why:'The phone version is small and rough. But it is real, and it starts now.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE EASY READING',belief:'Seneca thinks people who wait for the right moment are just making excuses.',reality:'He grants that the reasons are often sound. The money isn\u2019t there, the children are small, the job is demanding. His claim is narrower: the same sound reasoning will be there next year, and it will be used again.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Postponement assumes a future nobody has promised you, and the conditions you wait for mostly never arrive. Start the smallest real version under the conditions you actually have.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Sound reasoning, fatal conclusion',text:'Each year\u2019s case for waiting is good on its own terms. The trouble is the sequence: a good reason this year, another next year, and somewhere among them the deadline you cannot see. Seneca points to men who planned to begin living properly in old age, and asks what guarantee they had of reaching it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'It is dark, and the path is rough. Hardly ideal conditions for digging. The spade is by the gate anyway.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'THE QUESTION TO ASK',low:'Are conditions right yet?',high:'What can I start under these conditions?',note:'The first question can be asked forever. The second has an answer this week.'},{kind:'try',text:'Take the thing you are waiting to start and do the smallest real version of it this week, badly if necessary.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Someone has wanted to write a novel for ten years and is waiting until they can take a year off. What would Seneca suggest?',options:['Write for twenty minutes before work, starting this week','Apply for the year off first, then begin','Accept that the novel is not a real priority','Keep waiting, since the reasons for delay are sound'],answer:0,why:'He does not dispute the reasons. He asks what the novel looks like under the life they actually have.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'A REASONABLE PLAN',text:'You will start the business when the children are at school. It is a good plan. The money is not there, the hours are not there, and you are right about both. Then the children start school, and the case for waiting is just as good, for new reasons.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Postponement draws on a future nobody has guaranteed. Even when the reasons for waiting are sound, the same sound reasoning will be available next year, and it will be used.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Conceding the premise',text:'This is the strongest form of the argument because it gives the waiter everything. Seneca does not dispute the conditions. He treats waiting for them as a policy rather than a decision, and a policy runs until something stops it: here, a deadline no one can see.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE POLICY',nodes:['A sound reason to wait','A year passes','A new sound reason'],loop:!0,note:'No single step is a mistake. The loop is.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Dark, a rough path, a spade at the gate. The scene promises no better conditions. It offers only the first spadeful.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The reframe',text:'So the question changes. Not whether conditions are right, but what the smallest version of the thing looks like under the conditions you actually have, started now. A business becomes one paying customer. A book becomes a page a day.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Some things really do need conditions: a medical training, a move abroad, a child. Starting badly can cost more than waiting well. And for people whose delays are imposed by money or illness, the argument can harden into blame. It works best on one habit: deferring to a condition that renews itself.'},{kind:'recap',points:['The reasons for waiting may be sound.','Sound reasons recur, so waiting becomes a policy.','Ask what the smallest version looks like under real conditions.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'Someone says that starting small and badly might ruin the thing. What is the best reply?',options:['Sometimes, so ask if the condition will ever end; if not, start small','No, a rough start has never ruined anything worth doing','Yes, so wait until conditions are clearly good before starting','It does not matter, because effort counts for more than results'],answer:0,why:'The limit is real. The argument targets waiting on a condition that keeps renewing itself, not every careful delay.'}]}}}},2993,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'origin-of-species_1':{pictureCaption:'A keeper holds the one bird he has picked to breed from. Every reader in 1859 knew this worked, so Darwin began here.',basics:{title:'Start where they already agree',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'AT HOME ON A FRIDAY',text:'You want to stay up late on Fridays. You walk in and ask. Your mum says no before you have even finished. Now she is busy guarding her answer. She is not really hearing yours.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'What could you have said first?',options:['Ten good reasons to stay up','Something she already agrees with','That all your friends stay up'],answer:1,reveal:'She already thinks you are fine on Saturday mornings. She already let you stay up in the summer. Start there. Now *later on Fridays* is just one small step on.'},{kind:'idea',text:'To bring someone round to a big new idea, start with what they already believe. Then show that the new idea is just one more step.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE STEPPING STONES',text:'Nobody can jump a wide stream in one go. But if there are stones, each one is a short, safe step from the last. Soon you are on the far side.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'ENGLAND, 1859',picture:!0,text:'Charles Darwin was an English scientist. His book said living things slowly change. He knew lots of people would hate that. So he did not start with it. He started with pigeons. Everyone knew a keeper could pick which birds had chicks, and so make new kinds of pigeon.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Then one small step',text:'A keeper picks which birds have chicks. Then Darwin asked: what if no one picks? In the wild, the birds that live long enough are the ones that have chicks. Life does the picking. Readers had agreed to the first part. The rest was one step on.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You want your class to start a bin for cans. What is the best way to begin?',options:['Remind them they already save paper for the art room','Tell them the whole world is in danger','Ask the teacher to make it a rule'],answer:0,why:'They already sort paper. A bin for cans is one more step from what they do now.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'THE FIRST CHAPTER',text:'The book that changed biology does not open on wild animals. Its first chapter is about tame ones: pigeons, dogs, cattle, cabbages. Darwin bred fancy pigeons himself. Every farming reader knew that breeders reshape animals by choosing which ones breed.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Open a hard argument on the ground your audience already stands on. Show the mechanism where nobody disputes it. Then extend it, one step at a time, to the conclusion they would have resisted.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the move achieves',text:'By the time natural selection arrives, the reader has accepted two things. Selection changes animals, and small changes add up over generations. The only new claim is who does the selecting: survival instead of a farmer. The controversial step looks small because most of it was already granted.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A breeder holds the one bird he has chosen to breed from. Darwin\u2019s readers knew this worked. He had them agree to it before he asked for more.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Conclusion first',items:['The listener braces at once','Every point lands as an attack','You argue uphill all the way']},right:{title:'Common ground first',items:['The listener agrees early','The mechanism is accepted before it is named','The last step is small']}},{kind:'example',kicker:'AT WORK',text:'You want your team to drop a weekly status meeting. Open with that and people defend the meeting. Open instead with what everyone already says: the only useful part is the five minutes on blockers. Then your proposal arrives as the obvious next step.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before your next hard conversation, write down three things the other person already believes that point towards your conclusion. Start with those.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You want a sceptical council to approve a cycle lane. Which opening follows Darwin\u2019s method?',options:['Point out they already approved safer crossings near schools for the same reason','Lead with your most alarming figures on cycling accidents','Describe how much better cities with cycle lanes are','Admit that cycle lanes are controversial before you begin'],answer:0,why:'It starts from a decision the council has already made, and treats the lane as one more step along the same road.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'LONDON, 1859',prompt:'Darwin expected many readers to resist his theory. How does his first chapter begin?',options:['With the strongest evidence for natural selection','With the breeding of domestic animals','With replies to religious objections'],answer:1,reveal:'Chapter one is Variation under Domestication: pigeons, dogs, cattle, crops. Nothing in it was controversial, and much of his audience bred animals or plants themselves.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Establish the mechanism on ground already conceded, show how far it can go there, and only then extend it. By the time natural selection appears, the reader has agreed to everything except who holds the pen.'},{kind:'term',word:'Unconscious selection',meaning:'Darwin\u2019s name for change that breeders cause without aiming at it. Each keeps and breeds from his best animals, and over generations the breed shifts, though nobody planned the shift.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why that is the hinge',text:'Methodical selection, where a breeder aims at a set goal, still has a mind behind it. Unconscious selection already shows lasting change with no one intending it. From there, natural selection only swaps the breeder\u2019s preference for survival in the wild. The mechanism and its power have been granted; only the hand on it is new.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE ROUTE OF THE ARGUMENT',nodes:['Breeders create new varieties','Even without meaning to','In the wild, survival decides who breeds','So nature can make varieties too']},{kind:'picture',caption:'A breeder holds the one bird he will breed from. Selection by a person, which every reader granted, is the first half of the argument.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The technique is neutral. It persuades just as well when the extension is false: begin on agreed ground, then slip in a step that does not follow. Darwin\u2019s analogy also strained, since a breeder chooses and nature does not, and in later editions he had to explain that selection was a metaphor. Shared ground earns a hearing, not a conclusion.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'A writer wants readers to accept a radical change to the tax system. Which is a misuse of the technique?',options:['Starting from an agreed principle, then adding a step that does not follow from it','Starting from tax rules readers already accept, then extending their logic','Showing that the reform applies a principle readers already use elsewhere','Granting first what readers already believe about fairness'],answer:0,why:'Agreed ground earns attention for the next step. It does not make that step valid. Every extension still has to follow.'}]}},'origin-of-species_2':{pictureCaption:'A tray sown far too thick. Every seedling is alive, but only a few have pulled ahead into the light, and they differ from the rest.',bridge:'Idea 1 showed how Darwin brought readers to the starting line. This is the mechanism they arrived at, and how little it needs.',basics:{title:'Four plain facts, one big result',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A POND IN SPRING',prompt:'A frog lays a huge clump of eggs. How many grow up to be frogs?',options:['Nearly all of them','About half','Only a few'],answer:2,reveal:'Most get eaten, or dry out, or starve. Only a few make it. That is normal for almost every living thing. Far more are born than can live.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE MUSICAL CHAIRS',text:'In musical chairs there are always more kids than chairs. Some will be out. That is not anyone being mean. It is just the count.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Four plain facts explain how living things change. Too many are born to all live. No two are quite the same. Young ones take after their parents. Some differences help. Put them together and change follows.'},{kind:'equation',parts:['Too many born','Some differences help','Passed on to the young'],result:'Slow change',note:'Take away any one fact and the change stops.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Too many seeds in one tray. They can\u2019t all win. The few that pull ahead are a little bit different.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'ONE SMALL EDGE',text:'Back to the pond. Say one tadpole swims a bit faster than the rest. It is more likely to get away from a fish. So it is more likely to grow up and lay eggs. Its young may be fast too. Over a long time, the pond fills with fast ones.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Nobody planned it',text:'No one chose the fast ones. No one wanted the frogs to change. It just adds up. Charles Darwin saw that each of the four facts is easy to check. The big surprise is what they do when you put them together.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Say young ones were never like their parents at all. What happens in the pond?',options:['Fast frogs have young that are no faster than the rest','Fast frogs have young that are faster still','The pond fills up with fast ones sooner'],answer:0,why:'Being fast only spreads if parents pass it on. Take away one fact and the change stops.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'The struggle for existence means animals fighting each other to the death.',reality:'Darwin meant something duller and stronger: arithmetic. A population that breeds faster than it dies will outgrow its food. So most individuals fail to breed, whether or not anything fights.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Natural selection needs four observations: organisms vary, some of that variation is inherited, some of it helps its bearer survive, and more are born than can survive. Given those, change follows with no extra force and no purpose.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW IT FOLLOWS',nodes:['More born than can survive','Offspring vary','Some variation is inherited','Survivors differ from the rest','The next generation shifts']},{kind:'explain',title:'Why so few premises is a strength',text:'Each premise could be checked on any farm. None needed a new force. So to reject the conclusion, a critic had to deny one of four plain truths. Offspring differ. They resemble their parents. Some differences help. Most die young. That is why the theory was so hard to dislodge.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'BORROWED FROM AN ECONOMIST',text:'In 1838 Darwin read Thomas Malthus\u2019s essay on population. It argued that people multiply faster than their food supply can grow. Darwin saw that the same sum applies to every species. He credits Malthus in the book.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A tray sown far too thick. Not all of these can grow to full size. The few pulling ahead are not quite like the rest.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE SAME TEST AT WORK',text:'Sales at a shop fall. One explanation needs a rival\u2019s secret campaign, a supplier plot and a shift in taste. Another needs two facts from the till: prices rose in March, and regulars stopped coming in April. Start from the one built on facts nobody disputes.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A colleague explains a drop in sign-ups with a theory needing four untested assumptions. What does Darwin\u2019s method suggest first?',options:['Check whether two facts nobody disputes already explain the drop','Test each of the four assumptions in turn, starting with the first','Accept it, since a detailed theory is more likely to be right','Reject it outright and wait until more data comes in'],answer:0,why:'An explanation built from undisputed facts is harder to knock down than one built from guesses. Try the one that assumes least first.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'CHAPTERS ONE TO FOUR',text:'The opening chapters build the case in order. Domestic animals vary. Wild ones vary too. Every species produces more young than can survive. Then chapter four, titled Natural Selection, puts them together. No step needs anything a naturalist of 1859 could not observe.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Natural selection needs only variation, inheritance, overproduction and some variations that help. Given those, differential survival follows with no added force and no purpose. Its strength is that every premise is ordinary.'},{kind:'term',word:'Struggle for existence',meaning:'Darwin\u2019s phrase, which he says he uses in a broad and metaphorical sense. It covers dependence on other organisms and success in leaving offspring, not only combat.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'EVEN THE ELEPHANT',text:'Darwin chose the slowest breeder he knew, the elephant, and worked out how many descendants one pair would leave if every calf survived. Within some centuries the count ran into millions. Elephants are not multiplying like that, so most must fail to breed. The struggle is a sum, not a mood.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Overproduction made visible: far more seedlings than the tray can raise, and the few pulling ahead already differ from their neighbours.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Simple premises are not complete ones. Darwin could see that traits are inherited but not how, and his later guess at the mechanism was wrong. Fleeming Jenkin argued in 1867 that blending inheritance would dilute any useful variation away. The answer came from genetics after 1900. And the argument explains how life changes, not how it began.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Variation, inheritance, overproduction and advantage are each easy to observe.','Together they entail selection, with no purpose required.','Inheritance was the premise Darwin could not explain.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE COST OF REJECTING IT',prompt:'A critic wants to reject natural selection. Given how Darwin built it, what must they do?',options:['Deny one of the four premises, or show they do not jointly entail the result','Show that nature is mostly cooperative rather than violent','Find a species that has not changed for a very long time','Point out that nobody has watched a new species form'],answer:0,why:'The conclusion follows from the premises together. Cooperation, stable species and slow change are all compatible with it.'}]}},'origin-of-species_3':{pictureCaption:'A player studies the board for the opponent\u2019s most dangerous piece before it moves. Darwin searched his own theory for its strongest objections the same way.',bridge:'Idea 2 made the case hard to reject. Darwin still set out the strongest ways it might be rejected, in his own words.',basics:{title:'Say the weak spot first',beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'If your idea has a weak spot, keep quiet and hope no one sees it.',reality:'Charles Darwin did the opposite. In his famous book he wrote a whole chapter on the problems with his own idea. Then he gave his answers.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'IN FRONT OF THE CLASS',text:'You are telling the class why the town should build a skate park. There is one big problem. It would be loud for the houses next door. You know someone is going to bring it up.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Wait for them to say it',items:['They say it their way','It looks like you hid it','You are caught off guard']},right:{title:'Say it first',items:['You say it your way','You look honest','Your answer is ready']}},{kind:'idea',text:'Find the biggest problem with your own idea. Say it out loud before anyone else does. Then give your answer.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The player looks hard at the other side\u2019s most dangerous piece first. Darwin looked for the biggest problem with his own idea.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'DARWIN AND THE EYE',text:'Darwin said living things change bit by bit. But what about the eye? It is so finely made. Could that come bit by bit? Darwin asked this himself, and said it sounds silly at first. Then he pointed to animals with simpler eyes. Some only tell light from dark. Each kind is useful.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you want something, think: what will they say no about? Say that part first, with your plan for it.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You want your class to keep a hamster. What is the smartest thing to say first?',options:['Who will feed it in the holidays, and my plan for that','Hamsters are the cutest pets there are','The class next door has one already'],answer:0,why:'The teacher was going to ask about the holidays. By saying it first, you show you have thought it through.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE PITCH',text:'You are proposing a new supplier. It is cheaper and faster, but it has never handled an order your size. You plan to leave that out and hope nobody asks. Someone always asks, and when they do, it sounds like something you were hiding.'},{kind:'idea',text:'State the strongest case against your own position before anyone else can. You answer it on your own terms, and the reader trusts the rest of what you say a little more.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'DIFFICULTIES ON THEORY',text:'Darwin gave a whole chapter, Difficulties on Theory, to the best objections against himself. Where are the transitional forms? How could an organ as intricate as the eye be built by small steps? He concedes the eye case sounds absurd at first, then answers it with the range of simpler eyes in living animals, each useful to its owner.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it pays',text:'An objection you raise is framed in your words and placed where your answer follows. One that someone else raises is framed in theirs, at a moment of their choosing. Readers also notice who volunteered the hardest case, and that honesty buys credit for everything else.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The player studies the opponent\u2019s strongest piece before the opponent gets to use it. Raise the objection yourself and you have found the threat, and your reply, before anyone plays it.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Doing it yourself',steps:['Write the objection a sharp critic would make.','Make it stronger than they would.','Put it early, not in a footnote.','Answer it, or say plainly what you cannot answer yet.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Your plan depends on hiring two specialists in a tight market. Which approach follows Darwin?',options:['Raise the hiring risk yourself and set out your fallback','Leave hiring out unless somebody asks about it','Mention hiring briefly at the end, among minor risks','Present only the parts of the plan that are certain'],answer:0,why:'Raising the risk yourself lets you frame it and pair it with your answer. Hiding or burying it hands that framing to someone else.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'CHAPTER SIX, 1859',text:'Darwin opens the chapter by listing the difficulties a reader will already have thought of: the absence of countless transitional forms, organs as perfect as the eye, instincts as elaborate as the cell-building of the honeybee, and the sterility of hybrids. Some he answers there, others in the chapters that follow.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Set out the strongest objections to your own case, stated as well as an opponent could, and answer them. An objection you raise is answered on your terms, and volunteering it earns credit for the rest.'},{kind:'term',word:'Steelmanning',meaning:'A modern word for stating an opposing case in its strongest form before replying. The opposite of a straw man, where you defeat a weak version you built yourself.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE EYE',text:'He grants that an eye formed by selection sounds absurd at first hearing. Then he changes the question. If living species show eyes at every grade, from a patch of light-sensitive nerve to a lens, and each grade is useful to its owner, the difficulty lies in imagining it, not in the theory.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Not modesty, but position',text:'Raising the objection lets him choose its wording, its order and its neighbours, so each difficulty arrives next to its answer. It also takes the critic\u2019s best move off the board. A reader who watches an author volunteer the hardest case reads the confident chapters less suspiciously.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Studying the board for the opponent\u2019s best piece is ordinary preparation in chess. Darwin did it in print, setting out the strongest moves against his theory where readers could watch him answer them.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The tactic can be faked. An author can raise a weak version of an objection, demolish it, and look candid while dodging the strong one. Darwin\u2019s chapter earns its credit because the difficulties were real and some were only partly answered: the missing fossils stayed a problem for decades. Without that, the move is theatre.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE FAKE',prompt:'Which of these only looks like Darwin\u2019s method?',options:['Dwelling at length on a minor objection so the major one never comes up','Opening a report with the strongest argument against its own conclusion','Admitting plainly which objections you cannot yet answer','Restating a critic\u2019s point in a stronger form before answering it'],answer:0,why:'The credit comes from facing the hardest case. Volunteering an easy one to avoid it is the straw man in honest clothing.'}]}},'origin-of-species_4':{pictureCaption:'Hundreds of thin layers, each laid down slowly. The figure at the foot is for scale: Darwin\u2019s theory needed this much time, and more.',bridge:'Idea 3 set out objections Darwin could mostly answer. This is one he could not answer, and he held his ground anyway.',basics:{title:'The idea that needed a very old Earth',beats:[{kind:'picture',kicker:'A WALL OF ROCK',caption:'Look at all the thin layers of rock. Each one took a long, long time to form. The man at the bottom is tiny next to them.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why Darwin needed time',text:'Charles Darwin said living things change a tiny bit at a time. Tiny changes need a huge amount of time to add up. So his idea only works if the Earth is very, very old.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Every big idea rests on something that has to be true. Find that thing. If people doubt it, say so out loud. If the facts back you, hold on.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'A TOP SCIENTIST SAID NO',text:'A great scientist of the time worked out how long the Earth had taken to cool down. His answer was far too short for Darwin. Darwin could not show that the sums were wrong. But the rocks said the Earth was old. So he did not give up.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A CUP OF TEA',text:'You can guess how long tea has sat out by how cool it is. Good plan. But say someone hid a tiny heater under the cup. Your guess would be far too short.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Who was right',text:'Darwin was. Years later, scientists found that some kinds of rock make heat of their own. This is called radioactivity. Deep in the Earth it keeps adding heat. The cooling sums had missed it. Today we know the Earth is about four and a half billion years old.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Mia thinks the river cut the deep valley near her house. What does her idea need to be true?',options:['The water has had a very long time to wear the rock','There was a very big storm last winter','Lots of people agree with her'],answer:0,why:'Rivers wear rock very slowly. Like Darwin\u2019s idea, hers only works if there has been lots of time.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE WEAK JOINT',prompt:'Darwin\u2019s theory needed one thing above all that he could not prove. What was it?',options:['A very old Earth','A fossil of every species','A way to watch a species change'],answer:0,reveal:'Selection works through small changes, so it needs immense time. Darwin argued from rates of erosion for hundreds of millions of years. The physics of his day said the Earth was far younger.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Every argument rests on a premise it cannot do without. When current opinion contradicts that premise, name it yourself, show what supports it, and do not quietly drop it.'},{kind:'timeline',points:[{when:'1859',what:'Darwin\u2019s book needs an Earth hundreds of millions of years old'},{when:'1862',what:'William Thomson, later Lord Kelvin, publishes a cooling estimate that leaves far too little time'},{when:'1896',what:'Radioactivity is discovered'},{when:'1950s',what:'Radioactive dating puts the Earth\u2019s age near 4.5 billion years'}]},{kind:'explain',title:'Why he did not yield',text:'Kelvin\u2019s argument came from physics, the most prestigious science of the day, and Darwin could find no flaw in it. But the rocks, the fossils and the rates of erosion all pointed to vast time. He treated the clash as an unsolved problem, not a refutation, and said it was among the most serious objections to his theory.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Hundreds of thin layers, each laid down slowly, with a person at the foot for scale. This is the evidence Darwin would not give up.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'AT WORK',text:'Your business case assumes customers will renew after the first year, and the finance team believes renewal in your sector is low. Do not bury it. Put it on the first page, show your evidence, and say what result would prove you wrong.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Your caf\xe9 plan depends on a new office block filling up. Most people expect it to stay half empty. What does the lesson suggest?',options:['Name that premise up front, with your reasons for doubting the general view','Leave it out, since nobody can know for certain either way','Drop the plan, since the general view is probably right','Mention it only if an investor raises it first'],answer:0,why:'The premise is your weak joint. Naming it with your evidence is stronger than hiding it or abandoning the plan without testing it.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE WEALD',text:'In the chapter on the geological record, Darwin estimated how long rain and sea would take to wear away the Weald, a stretch of southern England, and arrived at roughly three hundred million years. The figure was meant to give readers a feel for geological time. Critics attacked it, and he removed it from the third edition.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Natural selection needs vastly more time than many of Darwin\u2019s contemporaries allowed. He knew it was his weakest flank, and he held the position the geology required against a physics he could not answer.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'THOMSON\u2019S COOLING EARTH',text:'From 1862 William Thomson, later Lord Kelvin, treated the Earth as a body cooling from a molten start. From the rate heat escapes at the surface he calculated an age far too short for selection, and narrowed it further over the following decades. Darwin counted it among his gravest objections and never answered it.'},{kind:'term',word:'Hidden premise',meaning:'An assumption an argument depends on without being about it. The Origin is about species, not the age of the Earth, yet nothing in it works unless the Earth is very old.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What settled it',text:'Radioactivity, discovered in 1896, revealed a source of heat inside the Earth that Kelvin\u2019s model left out, and later supplied a clock: radioactive dating puts the Earth at about four and a half billion years. A discovery in an unrelated field settled a dispute neither side could settle with its own tools.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A cliff of thin strata, each one slow to form, with a figure at the foot for scale. This was the evidence against which physics was weighed.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The textbook version says Kelvin\u2019s physics was sound and only radioactivity was missing. Historians now stress a second gap: heat moves through the Earth\u2019s interior by slow convection, which John Perry pointed out in 1895. And holding out against another field is only a virtue when your own evidence is strong. Most theories that defy physics are simply wrong.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HARD CALL',prompt:'When is a theory right to hold firm against a strong objection from another field?',options:['When its own evidence is strong and the objection rests on assumptions it cannot check','Whenever the theory has more supporters than the objection does','Never: physics is more basic, so it should always win','Only after the other field has admitted that it made a mistake'],answer:0,why:'Darwin held on because the geological evidence was strong and Kelvin\u2019s model rested on assumptions about the Earth\u2019s interior that nobody could then test.'}]}},'origin-of-species_5':{pictureCaption:'A thick bundle of notes, finished and tied up in a drawer. The hand rests on it, but does not lift it out.',bridge:'Ideas 3 and 4 were about what Darwin put in the book. This one asks why the book took twenty years to appear.',basics:{title:'Getting it right, or just scared?',beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'KEPT IN A DRAWER',picture:!0,text:'In 1838 Charles Darwin worked out how living things slowly change. It was one of the biggest ideas in science. Later he wrote it up and put it away. He waited twenty years to put out his book.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'Why do you think he waited so long?',options:['He was scared of what people would say','He wanted to be sure he was right','Maybe both, and people still argue'],answer:2,reveal:'Some think he feared people would be angry. But he also spent eight years on barnacles, small sea animals, to learn how much one kind can vary. That made his case much stronger.'},{kind:'timeline',points:[{when:'1838',what:'Darwin has his big idea'},{when:'1844',what:'He writes it up and asks his wife to print it if he dies'},{when:'1858',what:'A letter comes from another scientist, Alfred Wallace, with the same idea'},{when:'1859',what:'Darwin\u2019s book comes out at last'}]},{kind:'idea',text:'If you have put something off for a long time, ask yourself why. Are you making it better? Or are you scared? Be honest about which.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'YOUR STORY',text:'You wrote a story for a contest. It is done. But you keep fixing tiny bits and never send it in. Ask: am I making it better, or am I scared it will lose? If the fixes are tiny, it is probably fear.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Sam has redrawn his picture for the art show five times. Each change is tiny. What is the best guess?',options:['He is scared to show it','He is making it much better','He has not really started yet'],answer:0,why:'Big changes make work better. Tiny ones, over and over, are often a fear of being judged.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'NEARLY READY',text:'There is a plan you have worked on for two years. It is nearly ready. It has been nearly ready for a year. Every time you think of sending it, you find one more thing to check.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When you have sat on something for years, ask honestly whether the delay is rigour or dread. Rigour keeps producing new evidence. Dread keeps rechecking the old.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'1838 TO 1858',text:'Darwin had natural selection by 1838. In 1844 he wrote a long essay on it and left instructions for his wife to publish it if he died. He then spent eight years on barnacles, a full survey of one group of animals, and in 1856 began a huge book on species.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What ended the wait',text:'In 1858 a letter arrived from Alfred Russel Wallace, a naturalist working in the Malay Archipelago, setting out the same mechanism. Friends arranged a joint announcement of both men\u2019s work that summer. Darwin then cut his big book down to what he called an abstract, and published it in 1859.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Signs of rigour',items:['New evidence keeps coming in','The work is changing shape','You can name what is missing']},right:{title:'Signs of dread',items:['The same checks, repeated','The changes are cosmetic','Nothing specific is missing']}},{kind:'picture',caption:'The bundle is finished and tied. The hand rests on it and does not lift it out. That pause is the question this idea asks.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A colleague has delayed a report for months, each time adding new data that changes the conclusions. What is the best reading?',options:['Probably rigour: each delay is producing new evidence','Probably dread: any long delay is a sign of fear','Impossible to judge without asking how they feel','Probably perfectionism, which is the same as dread'],answer:0,why:'The test is what the delay produces. New evidence that changes the answer is the work getting better, not avoidance.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Darwin sat on his theory for twenty years purely out of fear of the reaction.',reality:'That is the usual story, and the evidence is mixed. He did dread the reaction, but the years also produced a barnacle monograph, a mass of evidence, and his standing as a working naturalist.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Darwin\u2019s twenty years bought thoroughness, but publication came when Wallace reached the same idea. The honest question about any long delay is which is doing the work: rigour or dread.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the barnacles bought',text:'Eight years classifying barnacles, living and fossil, did two things. It made him a respected specialist rather than a speculator, and it showed him first-hand how variable a single species is. Both mattered when the book came under attack.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The evidence for dread',text:'In 1844 he told a friend that admitting species change felt like confessing to a murder. The same year he wrote out his theory in full and left his wife instructions to publish it after his death. He knew what he had, and arranged for it to appear without him.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A finished bundle, tied and kept in a drawer, with a hand resting on it. Darwin\u2019s finished 1844 essay sat waiting in much this way.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The story can be told either way, which is the warning. Some historians argue there was no real delay: Darwin worked steadily, and was already writing his big species book when Wallace wrote. And rigour has no natural end. Without an outside push a thorough person may never publish, and Wallace supplied the deadline Darwin would not set himself.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What have you held back longest? Name, specifically, what it still needs before it is ready.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TELL THEM APART',prompt:'Which sign best separates rigour from dread in a long delay?',options:['Whether the delay is producing new evidence or repeating old checks','How many years in total the delay has lasted','Whether the person admits to feeling afraid','Whether a rival is working on the same thing'],answer:0,why:'Length, feelings and rivals fit either reading. What separates them is whether the time is adding anything the work did not already have.'}]}},'origin-of-species_6':{pictureCaption:'Two seedlings of the same kind, with one damp patch of soil between them. Their roots are reaching for the same water.',bridge:'Idea 2 said more are born than can survive. This one asks who, exactly, they are competing with.',basics:{title:'Your real rival is the one most like you',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'IN THE DRY GARDEN',prompt:'A young plant grows in dry ground. What is its biggest rival?',options:['A hungry rabbit','A big tree far off','The same kind of plant right next to it'],answer:2,reveal:'Both plants need the same water from the same small patch. Every drop one drinks, the other loses. A rabbit might come by once. The plant next door is there all day.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two plants of the same kind. One wet patch of soil. Look at the roots: they are reaching for the same water.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The hardest contest is with whoever needs just what you need. That is nearly always the one most like you.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'AT THE SCHOOL PLAY',text:'Two kids try out for the same part. They both sing well. They are each other\u2019s biggest rival. The kid who wants to work the lights is no threat at all. She wants something else.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The way out',text:'Charles Darwin saw this in plants and animals. A plant that is a bit different can get water the other can\u2019t reach. Say it has deeper roots. Then the two stop fighting. Being different is a way out.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your class sells cakes at the school fair. Which stall takes the most buyers from you?',options:['The class next door selling cakes too','The class running a game','The class selling old books'],answer:0,why:'The cake stall wants the very same buyers, with the very same thing. The game and the books want something else.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'The struggle for existence is mainly predator against prey.',reality:'Darwin says the struggle is fiercest between members of the same species, and between close varieties. That is because they live in the same places, eat the same food and face the same dangers.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Competition grows with overlap. The fiercest pressure comes from whatever needs exactly what you need. That is usually what most resembles you, not the dramatic predator.'},{kind:'scale',low:'Needs nothing you need',high:'Needs exactly what you need',note:'Pressure rises towards the right. The rival who most resembles you usually sits furthest along it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Darwin\u2019s example',text:'A plant at the edge of a desert struggles against drought. But two varieties of the same plant in one field struggle against each other. They need the same light, water and soil. That contest is harder, because each is the other\u2019s exact rival.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'ON THE HIGH STREET',text:'An independent bookshop worries about a giant online seller. Its sharper rival is the other bookshop two streets away. It has the same stock and the same Saturday customers. The escape is to differ: a children\u2019s specialism, a caf\xe9, events the other does not run.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two seedlings of the same kind in one damp patch. The dry ground around them is no threat. Each other is.'},{kind:'try',text:'List who competes for exactly your resource: the same customer, budget or slot. Then ask how you could need something slightly different.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A new gym opens in your town. Which existing business is likely to lose most?',options:['The other gym with similar classes and prices','The large sports shop on the edge of town','The park where people go running','The swimming pool at the leisure centre'],answer:0,why:'The other gym serves the same people with the same offer. The others overlap only partly, so they feel less of the pressure.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'CHAPTER THREE',text:'Darwin warns that struggle for existence is a loose phrase, then makes it precise. The struggle is most severe between individuals of the same species, and nearly as severe between varieties of one species, because they share districts, food and dangers.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Competition is a function of overlap in requirements. Near relatives compete hardest, and variants that differ from them escape the worst of it, so selection pushes related forms apart.'},{kind:'term',word:'Divergence of character',meaning:'Darwin\u2019s name for the tendency of descendants from a common stock to grow more different, because the more varied their needs, the more of them a place can support.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'FROM OVERLAP TO DIVERGENCE',nodes:['Close variants share every need','Competition between them is fiercest','Those that differ escape it','Lines drift apart over generations']},{kind:'example',kicker:'A PLOT OF GRASS',text:'Darwin points to an agricultural finding: a plot sown with several distinct kinds of grass raises more plants than a similar plot sown with one. Different needs mean less overlap, and less overlap means more room.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two seedlings of one kind drawing on the same damp patch while dry ground surrounds them. Overlap, not ferocity, is what makes them rivals.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Similarity is a stand-in for overlap, not the thing itself. Close relatives can split a resource by place or season, and very different organisms can want the same thing, as when birds and insects take one seed crop. The business version has the same flaw: your rival is whoever meets your customer\u2019s need, however unlike you it looks.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'Two closely related bird species feed on insects in the same trees. What does Darwin\u2019s argument predict over time?',options:['Variants that feed in different parts of the trees will tend to be favoured','Size alone will decide which of the two species survives','The two will interbreed and merge into one species','Nothing much, since the real struggle is with predators'],answer:0,why:'Overlap drives the competition, so variants that reduce it are favoured. That is divergence of character.'}]}},'origin-of-species_7':{pictureCaption:'A tree seen from below. Every branch splits and none rejoins. The one reaching furthest has moved furthest from its neighbours.',bridge:'Idea 6 explained why close relatives drift apart. This is the one picture Darwin drew to show where that drift leads.',basics:{title:'A tree, not a ladder',beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Living things sit on a ladder, from simple at the bottom to people at the top.',reality:'Charles Darwin drew a tree instead. Each branch splits, and splits again. No branch is the top. They are just different.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Look up into a tree. Each branch splits and goes its own way. No two branches ever join up again.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Kinds of plants and animals split apart over time, like branches. They do not join back up. The ones that move away from the crowd often do best.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the middle loses',text:'Think of three kinds of bird. One eats big seeds. One eats small seeds. One eats the in-between seeds, so it has to fight both the others. It gets squeezed out. The two at the ends do fine, and drift even further apart.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'AT BREAK TIME',text:'Lots of kids want to play football at break. The pitch is packed. One girl starts a skipping game in the corner. Soon she has a crowd of her own. She did not win the football fight. She moved away from it.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'At a busy market, five stalls sell the same sandwiches. What would this idea tell a new stall to do?',options:['Sell something the others do not','Sell the same sandwiches, a bit cheaper','Set up right in the middle of them'],answer:0,why:'The middle is where everyone fights for the same buyers. Moving to an end means fewer rivals.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE ONLY FIGURE',text:'The Origin is a book of sentences. It has one figure: a branching diagram of lineages splitting over many thousands of generations. Most lines end, and a few spread wide. Darwin used it to make a point that prose handles badly.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'What point does the branching diagram make better than prose could?',options:['That varieties spread apart rather than merging back','That every species was created separately','That fossils are too rare to tell us much'],answer:0,reveal:'You might expect varieties to drift back towards the average. The diagram shows lines spreading apart instead. That is because the extremes do better than the middle.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Descent with modification produces a tree, not a ladder. Varieties at the extremes escape the competition in the middle. So branches spread rather than merge, and there is no main line.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Seen from below, every limb divides and none rejoins. The branch reaching furthest is the one that moved furthest from its neighbours.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE MIDDLE EMPTIES',nodes:['A species varies','Middle forms compete with both sides','Middle forms die out','The extremes spread further apart']},{kind:'explain',title:'What the tree explains',text:'Why do species group into genera, and genera into families? On the tree, a group is simply a set of branches that share a fork. The filing system naturalists had built turned out to be a record of common ancestry.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'A CROWDED FIELD',text:'Imagine ten agencies selling general marketing to small firms at similar prices. The two that thrive are the one doing only restaurants and the one taking only very large accounts. The middle is where every rival overlaps.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You run one of six similar caf\xe9s on a high street. Which move follows the logic of the diagram?',options:['Move to one end: breakfast only, or late at night','Match the average caf\xe9, but at a lower price','Copy the menu of the busiest caf\xe9 nearby','Add a little of what every other caf\xe9 offers'],answer:0,why:'The middle is where you compete with everyone at once. Moving to an end leaves fewer rivals for exactly what you offer.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'A NOTEBOOK, 1837',text:'In a private notebook in 1837, before he had found the mechanism, Darwin sketched a small branching tree. The only figure in the Origin, twenty-two years later, is that idea worked out: lettered species, lines splitting across many thousands of generations, most ending, a few spreading.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Descent with modification draws a branching tree with no main line and no built-in direction. The branches that survive tend to be those that diverged most, which is why life falls into nested groups.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the diagram argues',text:'Each band of the diagram is a long run of generations. From one species varieties arise; the most different tend to survive and branch again, while intermediate forms, competing with neighbours on both sides, die out. Gaps open between groups, and those gaps are what classification records.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Limbs that divide and never rejoin, seen from below. The branch reaching furthest has moved furthest from its neighbours, as the diagram predicts.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Ladder',items:['One main line','Higher and lower forms','Progress built in']},right:{title:'Tree',items:['Many lines, none central','Different, not higher','Fit to local conditions only']}},{kind:'limits',text:'Darwin was not wholly consistent: in places he suggests later forms are in some sense higher, and readers have bent the tree back into a ladder ever since. Branches also sometimes rejoin. Hybridisation, and among microbes the transfer of genes between distant lineages, turn parts of the tree into a web.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'READ THE TREE',prompt:'On Darwin\u2019s account, why do species fall into genera, and genera into families?',options:['They are branches that share older and older forks','Naturalists sorted them that way for convenience','Each group was made to fill a separate role in nature','Similar habitats keep producing similar species'],answer:0,why:'Nested groups are the shape a branching tree leaves behind. Classification records ancestry, not a filing choice.'}]}},'origin-of-species_8':{pictureCaption:'A book with most of its pages torn out. What survives is real, but you cannot read the whole story from it alone.',bridge:'Idea 4 gave Darwin his time. This is the objection that time raised: if change was gradual, where are all the in-between fossils?',basics:{title:'Missing does not mean never there',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'GRAN\u2019S PHOTOS',text:'You find one old photo of your gran as a baby. You find one of her at your age. All the ones in between are lost. Did she jump from baby to eleven in a night? Of course not. The photos are just gone.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When something is missing, first ask: how likely was it to last, and be found? If the odds were low, missing tells you very little.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Darwin\u2019s problem',text:'Charles Darwin said animals changed slowly, step by step. So where were the fossils of all the in-between steps? It was the hardest question his idea faced.'},{kind:'steps',title:'What it takes to make a fossil',steps:['An animal dies in just the right mud.','Over a very long time, the mud turns to rock.','The rock is not worn away.','Someone digs in just the right spot.'],note:'Miss one step and there is no fossil. Most living things miss the very first.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A book with most pages torn out. A few are left. Darwin said the rocks are like this: a few pages of a very long story.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You find no fox tracks in the snow by school. The snow fell this morning, and it has been windy. What can you say?',options:['Not much: the tracks may have blown away','No fox has ever been near the school','No foxes live in this town'],answer:0,why:'Tracks were not likely to last in the wind. So finding none tells you very little.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'THE RECORD AS A BOOK',text:'Imagine a history of the world kept in many volumes. Only the last volume survives, and of that only a few scattered pages. Darwin, following the geologist Charles Lyell, says that is what the rocks give us.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Before treating missing evidence as evidence of absence, work out how likely it was to survive and be found. Darwin argued the fossil record has so many gaps that missing in-between forms prove little.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'A product team finds no complaints in a year of support tickets. What should they ask first?',options:['How many unhappy customers would bother to file a ticket','Whether the product is ready for a bigger launch','Which team member handled the most tickets'],answer:0,reveal:'If unhappy customers mostly just leave, silence is what you would see either way. The absence only means something if complaints were likely to be recorded.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Darwin\u2019s three filters',text:'Most living things leave nothing. To become a fossil, a body needs quick burial in the right sediment. Much of what is buried is later destroyed by erosion. And what survives must be exposed and then found by someone looking. Each filter is severe. Together they leave a few scattered pages.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Most of the pages are torn out. The one that survives is real evidence. But its neighbours are gone, so the story between them is lost.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time someone says there is no evidence for something, ask what evidence would have been left if it were true. Then ask whether anyone has looked.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A new drug shows no serious side effects in a trial of two hundred people. What does the idea suggest?',options:['A rare side effect would probably not show up in a trial that small','The drug can now be called safe for general use','Its side effects must be rarer than those of most drugs','The trial tells you nothing and should be ignored'],answer:0,why:'A rare effect had low odds of appearing in so few people. So its absence is weak evidence. The trial still tells you common effects are unlikely.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'BAVARIA, 1861',text:'Two years after the Origin, a fossil from the limestone quarries of Bavaria showed feathered wings together with a long bony tail and clawed fingers. Archaeopteryx was just the kind of intermediate Darwin had said should have existed, but might never be found.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The fossil record is so incomplete that missing intermediates are weak evidence against gradual change. A missing thing counts only once you know how likely it was to survive and be found.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The argument of the chapter',text:'In On the Imperfection of the Geological Record, Darwin grants that missing intermediates are perhaps the most obvious and serious objection to his theory. He answers with the odds: few organisms are buried well, most strata are later eroded, and only a small part of the Earth had been explored geologically.'},{kind:'term',word:'Evidence of absence',meaning:'Not finding something where it would very likely have shown up if it existed. Absence of evidence becomes evidence of absence only when the search was likely to succeed.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A book with most pages torn from the spine. Following Lyell, Darwin likened the rocks to a few scattered pages of the last volume of a history.'},{kind:'limits',text:'As stated, the defence is unfalsifiable: any gap can be blamed on preservation. Darwin was asking for credit. What repaid it was later evidence he could not have predicted: transitional fossils, and the independent agreement of molecular comparisons between living species. A defence that never meets new evidence is only an excuse.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'WHERE IT FAILS',prompt:'Which use of Darwin\u2019s argument is a misuse?',options:['Explaining away a failed search where the evidence would certainly have survived','Noting that soft-bodied animals rarely fossilise, so their absence says little','Asking how thoroughly a region\u2019s rocks were searched before concluding anything','Counting a newly found intermediate fossil as support for gradual change'],answer:0,why:'The argument depends on low odds of survival and discovery. Where those odds were high, a failed search really is evidence of absence.'}]}},'origin-of-species_9':{pictureCaption:'A page of type locked in place, and the last block going in. In Darwin\u2019s first edition, the book\u2019s final word was evolved, its only appearance.',bridge:'Idea 7 drew life as a tree with no top. Here Darwin tries to keep a top out of his vocabulary, and fails.',basics:{title:'Choosing the word with care',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'ONE WORD',prompt:'Which is more *evolved*: a shark or a human?',options:['A human','A shark','Neither: it is the wrong question'],answer:2,reveal:'Sharks and humans have both been changing for just as long. Each one fits its own life. Neither is higher. But the word *evolved* makes us want to rank them.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'A WORD HE KEPT OUT',text:'Charles Darwin wrote a famous book about how living things change. Yet when it first came out, the word *evolution* was not in it. He called his idea *descent with modification*: change down the family line. *Evolved* comes just once, as the very last word.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Books were once printed from rows of small metal blocks. Here the last block goes in. In Darwin\u2019s book, the very last word was *evolved*.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why leave it out?',text:'Back then, that word sounded like climbing a ladder, up to something better. Darwin did not think life works like that. Living things just change to fit where they live. If the place changes, a good trait can turn bad.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE POLAR BEAR\u2019S COAT',text:'A thick white coat is great in the snowy Arctic. Put that bear in a hot forest and the same coat is a problem. The coat is not better or worse. It fits one place.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Words carry hidden meanings. Darwin knew one word made change sound like going up a ladder. So he picked other words, to keep that wrong idea out.'},{kind:'keyline',text:'People forget your reasons. They remember your words.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend says fish are less evolved than people. What would Darwin say?',options:['Fish fit life in water, so they are not lower','Fish are less evolved, because they came first','Fish will turn into people one day'],answer:0,why:'Fish have been changing for as long as we have. They fit their own life. There is no top to climb to.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'term',word:'Descent with modification',meaning:'Darwin\u2019s own name for his theory: species descend from earlier species and change along the way. It says nothing about direction.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The word he avoided',picture:!0,text:'In the first edition the word evolution never appears. The last word of the book is evolved, its only use. In 1859 evolution suggested unfolding towards a set end, as an embryo unfolds into an adult. Darwin\u2019s mechanism has no set end.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Darwin avoided the word evolution because it implied progress towards a higher form, which his mechanism does not contain. The word won anyway, and brought that meaning with it.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'What the word suggests',items:['A ladder with a top','Higher and lower forms','Change towards something better']},right:{title:'What the mechanism does',items:['A tree with no top','Fit to local conditions','Change that reverses when conditions do']}},{kind:'example',kicker:'FISH IN THE DARK',text:'Fish living in dark caves have, over many generations, often lost working eyes. Eyes are no use in total darkness and may cost something to build. In the language of progress that looks like going backwards. In Darwin\u2019s terms it is fit to local conditions, which is all selection produces.'},{kind:'keyline',text:'The argument is read once. The word is repeated for ever.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick a claim you need people to remember accurately. Write down the one word they will use to repeat it, and check what that word implies.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You are launching a cheaper, simpler product, and staff keep calling it the lite version. What does Darwin\u2019s lesson suggest?',options:['Settle the name early, since lite will tell customers it is lesser','Ignore the name and let the features speak for themselves','Let customers settle on whatever name they like best','Use exactly the same name as the full version'],answer:0,why:'The name is what gets repeated, and it carries a judgement. Choosing it carefully is part of the claim, not decoration.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Darwin called his theory evolution and described life climbing towards higher forms.',reality:'He called it descent with modification. Evolution is absent from the first edition, and his mechanism produces fit to local circumstances, with no summit to climb towards.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Darwin\u2019s care over words was a claim in itself: his mechanism has no direction. The word he avoided became the theory\u2019s name, and carried in the progress he had written around.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the word meant in 1859',text:'Evolution then belonged largely to embryology, where it meant the unrolling of a structure already present in the germ, and to theories of development running from lower to higher. Either way it implied a destination. Selection has none: a trait is favoured only relative to present circumstances.'},{kind:'timeline',points:[{when:'1859',what:'First edition: evolution never appears, and evolved is the final word'},{when:'1872',what:'Sixth edition: Darwin uses evolution, by then the common name'}],note:'The word had won before he conceded it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The last block of type going into place. Darwin wrote evolution out of the first edition, yet evolved was its final word, and by the sixth edition the word was the theory\u2019s common name.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where the misreading lives',text:'Higher organisms, more advanced species, humans at the summit: the popular picture is a ladder, and the ladder lives in the word. Every lineage alive today has been descending and changing for exactly as long as every other.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Do not overstate the story. Darwin used higher and lower himself, was ambivalent about progress, and adopted the word later. Vocabulary alone did not cause the misreading; it also suited what readers wanted to believe about themselves. And the lesson cuts both ways: careful wording can clarify a claim, or quietly smuggle one in.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A scientist wants to describe a gene variant without implying it is an improvement. Which wording follows Darwin?',options:['Suited to its current environment','More evolved than the older variant','Superior, with a caveat in a footnote','More advanced than the ancestral form'],answer:0,why:'Suited to its environment names fit to circumstances. The others build a ladder into the description, which a footnote will not take back out.'}]}}}},2994,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'plutarch-lives_1':{pictureCaption:'A battle rages, tiny, on the horizon. Up close, someone ties a sandal. Plutarch would study the hands, not the battle.',basics:{title:'Small moments show who you are',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A NEW KID IN CLASS',prompt:'You want to know what a new kid is really like. Which tells you more?',options:['Their best test score','How they act when they lose a game','The prizes on their shelf'],answer:1,reveal:'A test score shows what they did on a big day. Losing a game shows what they are like when they are not trying to look good.'},{kind:'idea',text:'To see what a person is really like, look at the small moments. A joke, a kind word or a sulk can show more than a big win.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO SAID THIS',text:'Plutarch was a Greek writer who lived about 1,900 years ago. He wrote the life stories of famous leaders. He said plainly that he would not tell every battle. He was looking for the small moments that showed who they were.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Far away, a battle is going on. Up close, someone ties a sandal. Plutarch would watch the hands, not the battle.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A FOOTBALL STAR',text:'You can watch a player\u2019s best goals on a highlights clip. But you learn more from how they treat the ball boy after the game, when no camera is on them.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of a friend you trust. Find the small moment that first showed you what they were like.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Two players on your team both scored in the final. How could you best tell which one is the better teammate?',options:['Watch how each one acts when a game goes badly','Count who scored the most goals this year','Ask who has won the most cups'],answer:0,why:'Goals and cups are the big record. How someone acts when things go wrong is a small moment, and it shows what they are like.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE INTERVIEW',text:'Two candidates for the same job, both with impressive CVs. One was curt with the receptionist and talked over the junior colleague who showed them in. Nobody mentions it, but the panel now knows something neither CV could tell them.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The record of what someone achieved tells you what happened. To learn what they are like, look for the small, unguarded moment: a remark, a joke, a habit.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What Plutarch announced',text:'Plutarch, a Greek writer working around 100 CE, opens his Life of Alexander with a warning. He is writing lives, not histories. The great exploits often show little of virtue or vice, while a passing remark or a joke can show a great deal. So he will shorten campaigns and linger on remarks.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'A history asks',items:['What happened?','Who won, and how?','In what order?']},right:{title:'A life asks',items:['What was this person like?','What did they do unguarded?','What would they do next?']},note:'Different questions need different evidence. Plutarch picked the evidence for his question.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A battle, tiny on the horizon. A pair of hands, close up, fixing a sandal strap. Plutarch\u2019s attention goes to the hands.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of someone you must judge soon: a new hire, a builder, a landlord. Write down one small, unrehearsed moment you have seen. Weigh it alongside their record.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You are choosing between two builders with equally good reviews. Which evidence would Plutarch weigh most?',options:['How each one handled a neighbour\u2019s complaint about noise on a past job','Which one has finished the largest number of jobs','Which one has the more impressive list of past clients','Which one gives the most detailed written quote'],answer:0,why:'The others are the record, which both can polish. How they handled a small, unplanned annoyance shows what they are like when not performing.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'THE PREFACE TO ALEXANDER',text:'The Life of Alexander is paired with the Life of Caesar, and both are crowded with wars. So Plutarch opens with a warning. He will not tell every famous deed in full. He is writing lives, not histories, and he asks the reader not to complain when he cuts the battles short.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Plutarch chose the anecdote over the battle on purpose, and said so. A slight remark or a jest can reveal character more clearly than a battle in which thousands die.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'HIS OWN COMPARISON',text:'In the same preface he likens himself to a portrait painter, who takes the likeness from the face and the eyes, where character shows, and gives less care to the rest of the body. Plutarch asks to do the same: dwell on the signs of the soul, and leave the great contests to others.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why declare it in advance',text:'Announcing the method changes how the book should be judged. Plutarch is not a careless historian who drifted into gossip. He is a biographer telling you which question the book will answer, what this person was like, and which it will not: the full record of events.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The battle is pushed to the horizon. The foreground belongs to one small act. That is the proportion Plutarch announces for his book.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The method has a cost. Small anecdotes are the hardest evidence to check, and the most likely to have been polished in retelling. A joke may be revealing precisely because someone improved it. The choice makes Plutarch invaluable on character and unreliable as a chronicle, and a later lesson returns to how to read him.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Plutarch says outright that he writes lives, not histories.','He chooses small moments because they show character more clearly.','That makes him a guide to character, not a record of events.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE CRITIC',prompt:'A historian complains that Plutarch skips key details of a campaign. What is the fairest reply?',options:['He warned in advance that he was asking a different question, about character','He had no sources for the campaigns, so he had to skip them','Campaigns are never important for understanding a famous leader','He was writing entertainment, so accuracy was never his aim'],answer:0,why:'Plutarch announced the trade-off before he made it. He judged battles less useful for his question, not useless in general.'}]}},'plutarch-lives_2':{pictureCaption:'Two empty seats with a line between them. In Plutarch\u2019s book no life sits alone: each is read across the line from another.',bridge:'Idea 1 chose the question: what was this person like? This is the design Plutarch built to help answer it.',basics:{title:'Two lives, side by side',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'TWO PENALTIES',text:'Two kids take a penalty in the same match. One takes a long run-up and blasts it. One walks up calm and places it. The goal, the keeper and the crowd were the same. So the difference you see is the two kids.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Plutarch wrote his life stories in pairs, one Greek and one Roman. Putting two lives side by side shows what was really down to the person.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why one story is not enough',text:'Read about one leader who won, and you might think they were brilliant. Maybe they were just lucky. Put them next to someone in a similar spot, and you can see what each one did differently.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two empty seats, with a line between them. In Plutarch\u2019s book, no life sits alone. Each one faces another.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'A REAL PAIR',text:'Plutarch paired Alexander the Great, a king from the north of Greece who won a huge empire, with Julius Caesar, a famous Roman general. Both were bold. Side by side, you see how the same boldness played out in two different men.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of a test you did badly on. Find a friend who sat the same test and got ready a different way. What did they do?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your class garden did badly this year. How could you tell if it was the weather or what you did?',options:['Look at a class that grew the same plants in the same weather','Ask your teacher if she thinks the weather was bad','Plant the same seeds next year and hope'],answer:0,why:'The other class had the same weather. If their garden did well, the weather was not the whole story.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'ONE SUCCESSFUL FOUNDER',prompt:'A founder\u2019s company grew fast during a boom. What can you conclude about her judgement?',options:['That it is clearly excellent','Very little, until you compare her with others in the same boom','That she was simply lucky'],answer:1,reveal:'In a boom, many companies grow. Her result mixes her choices with the times. Only someone facing the same times, choosing differently, separates the two.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A single life invites you to judge by the outcome, and outcomes depend heavily on circumstance. Set two people in similar situations side by side, and the difference that remains is more likely to be the person.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How the book is built',text:'Plutarch\u2019s Parallel Lives sets a Greek beside a Roman: Theseus with Romulus, both legendary founders; Demosthenes with Cicero, both great orators; Alexander with Caesar. Many pairs end with a short formal comparison that weighs the two men against each other.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'One life read alone',items:['Success looks like talent','Failure looks like a flaw','Luck is invisible']},right:{title:'Two lives read together',items:['Shared conditions cancel out','Choices stand out','You see what was the person']},note:'The second case shows which parts of the first were the man and which were the moment.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two seats, one line between them. The reader sits on the line, looking from one life to the other.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick a decision you are judging, yours or someone else\u2019s. Find one person who faced nearly the same situation and chose differently. Compare what followed.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Two managers both cut their budgets by a fifth in the same year. One team thrived; one fell apart. What does pairing them let you ask?',options:['What each manager did differently with the same cut','Whether budget cuts are good or bad in general','Whether that year was simply a bad year for everyone','Which of the two companies had the better product'],answer:0,why:'The shared cut and the shared year are held roughly constant. What is left to explain is mostly the choices each manager made.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE STRUCTURE IS THE ARGUMENT',text:'Plutarch wrote the Parallel Lives around 100 CE, a Greek living under Roman rule. The surviving work sets Greeks beside Romans, and many pairs close with a short essay weighing the two. The design is not decoration. It forces a question a single biography lets you avoid.'},{kind:'term',word:'Synkrisis',meaning:'Greek for \u201ccomparison\u201d. Here, the short formal essay at the end of a pair, where Plutarch sets the two men against each other point by point and weighs them.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Comparison isolates the variable. Hold the situation roughly constant across two lives, and what differs is more plausibly character than circumstance.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW A PAIR DOES ITS WORK',nodes:['One life: outcome passes for character','Add a life in a similar position','Shared circumstances cancel','What remains is the person'],note:'The logic is closer to comparative method than to storytelling.'},{kind:'example',title:'Daring, twice',text:'Read alone, Alexander\u2019s daring is easy to admire in the abstract. Set beside Caesar\u2019s, the same quality works under different restraints and produces different consequences. You can no longer praise the trait without asking what it did, and within what limits.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two seats set apart, with a line between. The line is the comparison, and it is where the reader stands.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The experiment is loose. No two lives share conditions closely, and some pairs are matched as much for symmetry as for likeness: two founders, two orators. Not every pair has a surviving comparison, and some comparisons are brief and formulaic. The device shows where to look. It does not prove what the person was.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'Someone says: comparing two people from different centuries proves nothing. What is the strongest reply?',options:['Alone it proves nothing, but it shows what to credit to the person, not the times','Human nature never changes, so any two lives are directly comparable','Plutarch chose only pairs whose circumstances were nearly identical','The closing comparisons settle which man was better, so the proof is there'],answer:0,why:'The pairing proves nothing by itself. It narrows the question, which is more than a single life can do.'}]}},'plutarch-lives_3':{pictureCaption:'After the show, in a dim corridor, someone stops to set a fallen cup upright. Nobody is watching. That is where character shows.',bridge:'Idea 1 said small moments reveal character. This one says why: character is a habit, and habits show when nobody is performing.',basics:{title:'The real you shows when no one is looking',beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'You find out who someone is by watching them on their big day.',reality:'On a big day, people try hard to look good. The real them shows in small, everyday moments, when they are not trying at all.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Who you are is built from what you do again and again. So it shows best in small moments, when nothing big is at stake.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why small things tell the truth',text:'Anyone can be nice for one big moment, like prize day. But being kind every day takes a habit. A habit is hard to fake for long. It just pops out, even when you are not thinking about it.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'AFTER THE SHOW',caption:'The show is over and the stage is empty. Nobody is watching. One person still stops to pick up a cup that fell over. That is a habit.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'AT LUNCH',text:'Two kids both clap loudly when a friend wins a prize. At lunch, one of them saves a seat for the new kid. The other does not even notice the new kid. The lunch moment tells you more.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO NOTICED THIS',text:'Plutarch was a Greek writer who lived about 1,900 years ago. He liked tiny stories best: how a leader spoke to a servant, or what he did when a plan went wrong.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one small kind thing, like saying thank you to the dinner staff. Do it every day this week, even when nobody sees.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Which moment tells you most about whether your cousin is honest?',options:['He hands back extra change a shop gave him by mistake','He gives a nice speech at a family party','He promises he will always tell the truth'],answer:0,why:'The speech and the promise are planned. Handing back change when no one would know is a habit showing.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'AFTER THE PITCH',text:'A project lead has just watched a pitch collapse. In the corridor afterwards, she can blame the junior who built the slides, or say the plan was hers and it was wrong. Nobody senior is there. Whatever she does is not a performance. It is a habit, coming out.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Character is built by repetition, so it shows in the moments nobody is staging: how someone treats a servant, what they do when a plan fails. Give those more weight than the performance.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why Plutarch\u2019s stories are small',text:'His anecdotes are about what someone did with a messenger, how they ate on campaign, what they said when they thought the matter closed. He is not collecting trivia. He wants moments when the person was not performing, because the habit is what shows when attention is elsewhere.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Far off, the stage is lit and empty. In the dim corridor, someone stops to set a fallen cup upright. Nobody is watching, so nothing is being performed.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'The staged moment',items:['High stakes, full attention','Can be rehearsed','One effort can produce it']},right:{title:'The unguarded moment',items:['Low stakes, attention elsewhere','Cannot be rehearsed','Needs years of habit behind it']},note:'A great action can come from one effort. A small habit cannot be faked across years of witnesses.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you are getting to know someone at work, notice how they treat people who can do nothing for them: the cleaner, the new starter, the waiter.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You are checking references for a senior hire. Which detail would Plutarch value most?',options:['A former assistant says they stayed calm and fair when a deal fell through','They gave an excellent presentation at the final interview','They have led three successful company turnarounds','Their former chairman calls them an exceptional leader'],answer:0,why:'The presentation, the record and the praise are all performances. Calm fairness when a deal collapsed, seen by someone junior, is the habit.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'ask',kicker:'BEFORE YOU START',prompt:'Think of the last time you changed your view of someone you knew well. Was it a great act that did it, or a small one?'},{kind:'idea',text:'Character is a habit built by repetition, so it appears when attention is elsewhere. Low-stakes evidence is harder to fake than high-stakes evidence, and deserves more weight.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where the assumption comes from',text:'Plutarch was a Platonist, and he shared a view common in Greek ethics since Aristotle: virtue is formed by practice, as a skill is. If character is laid down by repetition, it should show most clearly where the person is not choosing how to appear.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The bet he is making',text:'A great action can be produced by a single effort, under full attention, before an audience. A small habit has to hold across a lifetime of witnesses who were not taking notes for posterity. Plutarch bets that the low-stakes evidence is the harder to falsify, and on the whole he is right.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The performance is over and the stage stands empty. In the corridor, unobserved, someone rights a fallen cup: the kind of unstaged act Plutarch looked for.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'FROM HABIT TO ANECDOTE',nodes:['Repeated choices','A settled habit','It shows when unwatched','Witnesses remember and retell'],note:'The first three steps give the method its strength. The last gives it its weakness.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Anecdotes are the least verifiable kind of evidence and the most likely to improve in the retelling. A small story that fits a character perfectly may fit because someone made it fit. The method is strongest on traits many witnesses saw, and weakest on the single perfect story.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'WEIGH THE EVIDENCE',prompt:'Which evidence about a long-dead general best suits Plutarch\u2019s method?',options:['Several unrelated sources say he shared his soldiers\u2019 rations','One vivid story has him making a witty remark before his greatest victory','His own inscription lists every city he captured','A rival\u2019s speech accuses him of cruelty to prisoners'],answer:0,why:'It is a small, repeated habit, and independent witnesses guard against improvement in the retelling. The single witty line is exactly the kind most likely to have been polished.'}]}},'plutarch-lives_4':{pictureCaption:'The climber passed the open window several rungs ago and is still pulling upward. Nothing on a ladder tells you that you have arrived.',bridge:'Ideas 1 to 3 were about how Plutarch looks at a life. This is the pattern he found most often when he did.',basics:{title:'Knowing when you have won',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'A TOWER OF BLOCKS',text:'You build a tower of blocks. It is already the tallest in the class. You add one more, then another. Each one worked, so you think the next will too. Then one block brings it all down.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Wanting to win is not bad. The danger is never stopping to see that you already have. Each win makes the next try bigger, until one is too big.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO SAID THIS',text:'Plutarch was a Greek writer who lived about 1,900 years ago. He wrote about many great leaders. The ones who failed were often not wicked. They were very able people who kept reaching for more, and lost it all.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The climber has already passed the open window. Look how far up he is now. He never noticed he had arrived.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why winning makes it worse',text:'When you win, people stop saying \u201cbe careful.\u201d You stop saying it to yourself too. So the next try is bigger, and nobody checks it. That is when a good player makes one big mistake.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you do well at something, stop. Say out loud what you did. Enjoy it for a day before you plan the next thing.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Mia won the school spelling contest. Which next step shows she knows when she has won?',options:['She enjoys the win, then thinks about what she wants next','She signs up for five harder contests that same night','She decides spelling is too easy and gives it up'],answer:0,why:'Stopping to notice a win is part of good sense. Rushing into bigger and bigger tries is how able people overreach.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'A KING AND HIS ADVISER',text:'Plutarch tells of Pyrrhus, a Greek king, planning war on Rome. His adviser Cineas asks what comes after Italy. Sicily, says the king. And then? Carthage and Africa, then Greece. And then? Then, at last, they will rest and enjoy themselves. Cineas asks why they cannot rest now. Pyrrhus is troubled, and goes to war anyway.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The recurring failure in the Lives is not wickedness but ambition without self-knowledge: capable people who cannot tell when they have won. Ambition magnifies whatever judgement is already there.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the failures come late',text:'Early success is not the danger. Each success widens the next undertaking, and removes some of the people and doubts that would have checked it. Eventually the scale outruns a judgement never tested at that size, and one decision ruins a record of victories.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW A WINNER OVERREACHES',nodes:['A success','A bigger next undertaking','Fewer people checking','Scale outruns judgement'],note:'Each step feels earned. That is why the pattern is hard to see from inside.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The open window is several rungs below. The climber is still pulling upward, past the point he was aiming for.'},{kind:'try',text:'After your next real win, write one line saying what you achieved before you write a single line of plan for what comes next.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A small firm wins three big contracts in a year. Which response fits Plutarch\u2019s warning?',options:['Pause to take stock of what worked before bidding for anything larger','Bid at once for a contract twice the size, while confidence is high','Turn down all new work, since ambition is dangerous in itself','Replace the cautious finance director who keeps raising doubts'],answer:0,why:'Plutarch does not condemn ambition. He warns that success removes the checks, so the moment of winning is the moment to look.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Plutarch\u2019s cautionary figures are villains brought down by their vices.',reality:'His recurring failure is closer to home: capable, often admirable people whose ambition outran their judgement. He dwells on it because it is the pattern his readers might be living.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Ambition is a multiplier, not a vice. It magnifies the judgement already present, so the drive that makes a capable person decisive makes a rash one catastrophic, usually late, after success has removed the checks.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'ALCIBIADES OF ATHENS',text:'Alcibiades is Plutarch\u2019s clearest case: brilliant in speech, war and charm, and trusted by nobody for long. He urged Athens into a vast expedition against Sicily, already dreaming of Carthage beyond it. Recalled to face charges, he went over to Sparta, then to the Persians, then returned to lead Athens, and fell again.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'WHERE THE RISK SITS',low:'An undertaking the size your judgement was tested at',high:'An undertaking far beyond any size tested',note:'Each success moves the next attempt to the right. Judgement does not move with it on its own.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A climber who has passed the window he was aiming for. Success gives no signal that you have arrived. You have to stop and look.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The pattern is easiest to see backwards. Knowing how a life ended, every earlier success looks like a step towards ruin, and Plutarch writes with that knowledge. Many bold people expanded and did not fall. The lesson is not that ambition fails, but that success stops testing the judgement it depends on.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISREADING',prompt:'Which conclusion would Plutarch reject?',options:['Ambition is itself a vice, and capable people should suppress it','Success can remove the checks that would catch a bad decision','The same drive can make one person decisive and another reckless','Ruin often comes late in a career, not at the start'],answer:0,why:'For Plutarch ambition multiplies judgement; it is not the vice. The danger is the judgement it outruns.'}]}},'plutarch-lives_5':{pictureCaption:'A worn old carving and a fresh copy in bold new lines. What reaches us is the copy, and some lines are the copier\u2019s own.',bridge:'Ideas 1 and 3 explained why Plutarch trusts small stories. This one asks how far you should trust his.',basics:{title:'A great storyteller, not a news report',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A STORY ABOUT GRANDAD',prompt:'Your gran tells a funny story about Grandad as a boy. She heard it from his brother, long ago. What is it best for?',options:['Knowing exactly what happened that day','Knowing what Grandad was like','Nothing, since it might not be true'],answer:1,reveal:'Some details may have changed over the years. But the story still shows what kind of boy people thought Grandad was. That is worth a lot.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Plutarch wrote long after the people in his stories lived. Some details may be wrong. So read his stories to learn what people were like, not as exact news.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why he can get things wrong',text:'Plutarch lived about 400 years after Alexander the Great. He read older books that are now lost, and some of those books took sides. He also liked a story with a lesson in it. So he may have picked the best story, not the truest one.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Someone copies an old, worn stone face. The new lines are bright and bold. Some of them are the copier\u2019s own guess.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Good for learning',items:['What people thought of a leader','What makes someone good or bad']},right:{title:'Not good for learning',items:['The exact words people said','Exact dates and numbers']}},{kind:'try',text:'Next time a friend tells a story they heard from someone else, ask kindly: who saw it happen?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You read a great story about a Roman general helping a beggar. It was written 300 years after he died. How should you use it?',options:['As a clue to what people thought he was like','As proof that this exact thing really happened','As a lie, so you should forget it'],answer:0,why:'It might not be exact. But it shows how people remembered him, and that still tells you something.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Plutarch is history, so his famous scenes tell you what happened.',reality:'He wrote centuries after most of his subjects. His sources are now lost, and they were already biased. He also shaped scenes for moral effect. He is a witness to what was believed about these men.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Treat a vivid story from history as an argument about character, not a fact about events. Plutarch is unreliable as reporting. He is still unmatched as a study of what people are like.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What he does well',text:'He reports rumours as rumours. He gives competing versions of an event. Sometimes he admits he cannot tell which is true. That is more care than his reputation suggests.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where he slips',text:'He also repeats claims that later evidence contradicts. And his wish to teach morals gives him a reason to prefer the more revealing story over the more likely one.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A worn carving and a fresh copy in bold new lines. The copy is clearer than the stone. That is both the problem and the value.'},{kind:'try',text:'When a story about a famous person seems perfect, ask two questions. How long after the event was it written? What lesson did the writer want it to teach?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A business book opens with a famous story about a founder. A biographer told it decades later. How should you use it?',options:['As evidence of what people came to believe about the founder','As a record of exactly what the founder said and did','Not at all, since anecdotes like this are always invented','As proof that the founder\u2019s methods will work for you'],answer:0,why:'Like Plutarch, the biographer had reasons to prefer the revealing version. The story is good evidence of reputation and weak evidence of fact.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'timeline',kicker:'THE GAP',points:[{when:'323 BCE',what:'Alexander dies'},{when:'44 BCE',what:'Caesar is assassinated'},{when:'c. 100 CE',what:'Plutarch is writing the Lives'}],note:'Over four centuries for Alexander, about one and a half for Caesar. In between, the sources were written, argued over and mostly lost.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Read Plutarch as a witness to what was believed and told about these men, by someone with sources we no longer have. Use his anecdotes as arguments about character, not reports of events.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The case for and against',text:'In his favour: he flags rumours, sets out rival versions, and sometimes says he cannot decide. Against him: he repeats material that later evidence contradicts, and his aim of moral instruction gives him a motive to prefer whichever story reveals most.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why you cannot simply check',text:'For several figures he is the principal surviving source. The difficulty cannot be resolved by going elsewhere. It can only be held in mind while reading, and reading him as biography rather than record is what he himself asked for.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A worn carving and a confident new copy. What survives to us is the copy, and some of its lines are the copier\u2019s.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The opposite error is as easy: treating everything he says as moral fable and discarding it. He had sources we lack, and much of what he reports is supported elsewhere. Reading with the source in mind means weighing each story, not dismissing them all.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which famous anecdote about a leader do you believe without question? How long after the event was it first written down?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A scholar shows that a famous Plutarch scene cannot have happened as told. What follows for its value?',options:['It may still be good evidence of how the man was remembered and judged','It is worthless and should be left out of serious study','The rest of Plutarch must be just as invented','It must have happened, since Plutarch had sources we lack'],answer:0,why:'A shaped story still records what was believed and what Plutarch thought it showed. Its value moves from fact to reputation and argument.'}]}},'plutarch-lives_6':{pictureCaption:'A boy watches a horse whose shadow lies on the ground ahead of it, where it can see it: what everyone else missed.',bridge:'This is idea 1\u2019s method in action: one small episode, kept because it shows a whole character.',basics:{title:'The boy who watched the horse',beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'A HORSE NO ONE COULD RIDE',text:'Long ago, a king named Philip was shown a fine horse. But it reared and kicked, and no rider could get on it. The king told his men to take it away. His young son, Alexander, said they were losing a great horse.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'What do you think the boy had spotted?',options:['The horse was hungry','The horse was scared of its own shadow','The men were holding it the wrong way'],answer:1,reveal:'He had been watching the horse, not the men. Each time it jumped, it was jumping at its own dark shadow on the ground.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Look at the ground. The horse\u2019s shadow lies right in front of it, where it can see it. The boy spotted it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What he did',text:'He turned the horse to face the sun. Now its shadow fell behind it, where it could not see it. The horse grew calm, and the boy got on. The horse was called Bucephalus.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When something keeps going wrong and everyone says why, take time to watch it yourself. You might see what they all missed.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHY THIS STORY WAS KEPT',text:'Plutarch, a Greek writer, told this story about the boy who grew up to be Alexander the Great. The men there were brave. But only the boy had looked closely. Plutarch thought that showed the kind of man he would become.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time something keeps going wrong, like a game you keep losing, stop and just watch for five minutes. What is really happening?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your bike chain keeps slipping. Your friends say it is old and you need a new one. What would the boy in the story do?',options:['Watch the chain closely to see when it slips','Buy a new chain, since everyone agrees','Stop riding the bike for good'],answer:0,why:'Everyone agreeing is not the same as someone looking. Watching might show a simple fix.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE REPORT THAT KEEPS FAILING',text:'A report fails most nights. The team agrees why: the server is overloaded, and the upgrade has sat on the wish list for months. A new hire asks if she can simply sit and watch it run once before anyone signs off the upgrade.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'MACEDON, A HORSE FOR SALE',text:'A horse was brought to King Philip of Macedon. It would let no one mount it, and Philip ordered it led away. His young son Alexander protested that they were losing a fine animal through inexperience. Challenged, he turned its head towards the sun, having seen it was shying at its own shadow, and mounted.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The shadow lies ahead of the horse, in its line of sight. The men saw an animal no one could ride. The boy saw what it was looking at.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The quality Plutarch picks out is not courage, which the grown men had, but observation. The others saw an unmanageable animal. The boy saw what the animal was reacting to.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Everyone else',items:['Saw a horse nobody could ride','Agreed on the reason','Tried harder, then gave up']},right:{title:'The boy',items:['Saw what the horse was reacting to','Watched before deciding','Changed one condition']},note:'Same horse, same field. The difference was who had looked.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why agreement hides causes',text:'When experienced people agree on an explanation, they stop looking. The agreed reason becomes the frame, and every new failure reads as more proof. A newcomer who simply watches is not cleverer. They are just not yet inside the explanation.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one recurring problem that everyone already has an answer for. Spend ten minutes watching it happen before you accept the answer.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Sales of one product have fallen, and the team agrees the price is too high. What does the Bucephalus story suggest first?',options:['Watch some real customers try to buy it before cutting the price','Cut the price, since the experienced team all agree','Ask the team to vote on the most likely cause','Drop the product, since nothing seems to fix it'],answer:0,why:'Agreement is not observation. Ten minutes watching real buyers might show what they are actually reacting to.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'EARLY IN THE ALEXANDER',text:'Plutarch tells of a horse brought to Philip of Macedon that would let no one mount it. As it is being led away, the boy Alexander says the men are losing it through inexperience. He turns it to face the sun, having noticed it fears its own shadow, and mounts.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The cause was on the ground the whole time: a shadow lying where the horse could see it. What the scene records is who looked.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The Bucephalus episode is Plutarch\u2019s method demonstrated. In a scene small enough to grasp whole, it shows the faculty that later drove the campaigns: looking while everyone else was busy being certain.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why this scene, not a battle',text:'The preface promised a remark or a jest over a battle. Here he delivers. A battle mixes the general with luck, terrain and thousands of other men. A boy and a frightened horse isolate one trait completely, and the reader can see the whole cause of the success.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHAT THE BOY DID',nodes:['Watch the failure itself','Find what the horse reacts to','Change that one condition','The problem goes away'],note:'Nothing here needs more courage than the men had. It needs a different kind of attention.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Plutarch writes with hindsight: knowing the conqueror, he chooses the boyhood scene that foreshadows him, and the telling may have improved it. In his version the boy also taunts the men for lacking nerve. And the lesson is not that the newcomer is always right. Often the agreed view is correct. Look before accepting it; do not assume it is wrong.'},{kind:'recap',points:['The horse was shying at its own shadow, and only the boy saw it.','Plutarch keeps the scene for the observation, not the courage.','It is the preface\u2019s method in action: a small scene, a whole character.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A hospital ward has more falls at night, and staff agree that patients are simply confused. Which step best applies the episode?',options:['Spend some nights watching where and when the falls actually happen','Add more staff at night, since the agreed cause is confusion','Accept the explanation, since experienced staff know best','Assume the agreed cause is wrong and find a different one'],answer:0,why:'The boy did not reject the experts on principle. He watched what the horse was reacting to. Watching the falls might reveal lighting, footwear or layout.'}]}},'plutarch-lives_7':{pictureCaption:'One post stands perfectly straight in an empty forum. Look at the ground: everything around its base has cracked.',bridge:'Idea 3 said character is habit. Cato shows a fine habit, never bending, turning into a cost.',basics:{title:'When never bending goes wrong',beats:[{kind:'scale',kicker:'HOW MUCH DO YOU BEND?',low:'Gives in on everything',high:'Gives in on nothing',note:'Most of us sit near the middle. A Roman named Cato sat right at the far end.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'ROME, 2,000 YEARS AGO',text:'Cato was a Roman leader about 2,000 years ago. He was famous for being honest, and no one could bribe him. But he hardly ever made deals with anyone, even to stop something worse from happening.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Sticking to what is right is a good thing. But if you never bend at all, you can make things worse, even when you are right.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The post stands perfectly straight. But look at the ground. Everything around it has cracked.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'ON THE TEAM',text:'Your team must pick a game plan. You think your plan is best, and maybe it is. But you will not give an inch on anything. Your teammates stop listening. The plan you like least wins, because no one would join up with you.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'BOTH SIDES',text:'Plutarch, the Greek writer who told Cato\u2019s story, liked him a lot. But he did not pretend Cato\u2019s stubborn ways had worked. He showed both sides and let readers think it through.'},{kind:'try',text:'Write down the things you would never give in on, not even to win. Is your list short? It should be.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friends want pizza and you want pasta. You also think cheating at cards is wrong. Which is worth never bending on?',options:['Not cheating at cards','Having pasta, not pizza','Both, because giving in is weak'],answer:0,why:'A few things matter enough never to bend on. Most things are fine to give way on, so keep that list short.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'THE MARRIAGE HE REFUSED',text:'Pompey the Great, Rome\u2019s most famous general, sought marriage ties with Cato\u2019s family. Cato refused to be bound to him. Pompey then married Julia, the daughter of Julius Caesar. Plutarch remarks that the alliance Cato blocked was replaced by one that helped bring the republic down.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Plutarch admires Cato the Younger\u2019s integrity and shows what it produced: a man so unwilling to compromise that he kept making the worse outcome more likely. A virtue that cannot bend can work as a defect.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'What Cato was right about',items:['Caesar was a danger to the republic','Bribery was corrupting politics','Principles should not be for sale']},right:{title:'What his refusals did',items:['Pushed Pompey towards Caesar','Drove away moderate allies','Left his side weaker when it counted']},note:'Both columns are true at once. Plutarch will not delete either.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One straight, unbending post in an empty forum. Plutarch admires the post, and still shows you the cracks around its base.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why Plutarch leaves it open',text:'He records Cato\u2019s suicide at Utica, after Caesar had won, with deep respect. He does not explain the political damage away either. The question he leaves is a real one: is a virtue that cannot bend still a virtue in a situation that requires bending?'},{kind:'try',text:'List the principles you would hold even at the cost of the outcome. If the list is long, some items are preferences dressed up as principles.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A councillor votes against a housing bill she mostly supports, over one clause she dislikes. The bill fails by one vote. What does Plutarch\u2019s Cato suggest?',options:['Her firmness is admirable, and it helped bring about the result she least wanted','She was simply right, and the failure is everyone else\u2019s fault','Principles are a luxury that politicians cannot afford','She should always vote with whatever has a majority'],answer:0,why:'Plutarch holds both: the integrity is real, and so is its cost. He does not settle it by simply praising or blaming her.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'BEFORE THE PORTRAIT',prompt:'Plutarch plainly admires Cato. How do you expect him to treat Cato\u2019s refusal to compromise?',options:['As the source of his greatness','As a fatal flaw that cancels his virtue','As a real virtue that kept making things worse'],answer:2,reveal:'He refuses both easy versions. The integrity is genuine, the respect is genuine, and the damage is recorded without excuse.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Cato the Younger is Plutarch\u2019s study of integrity as a limitation. Incorruptibility is a virtue, and in a situation that requires alliances it can function as a defect.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The record',text:'His refusal of a marriage alliance with Pompey helped push Pompey towards Caesar. His obstruction on principle drove moderates away. The republic he was defending fell, and Plutarch lets his rigidity stand among the causes, without explaining it away.'},{kind:'equation',parts:['Being right about Caesar','Refusing to compromise'],result:'Losing anyway',note:'Cato was right about Caesar, and his rightness was strategically useless.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'An unbending post, and a forum cracking around it. The straightness is not the fault. What it costs the ground around it is the question.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The portrait is easily misused as an argument against all principle: be flexible, deal with anyone. Plutarch does not say that. Cato was right about the danger, and Pompey, who did deal with Caesar, ended at war with him anyway. The real test is narrower: which principles are worth holding at the cost of the outcome, and is that list honest?'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which of your principles would you keep even if keeping it meant certain defeat? Name one you would trade.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'Someone argues that Cato proves principled people should always compromise. What is the best reply from Plutarch\u2019s portrait?',options:['Cato was right about Caesar; the lesson is to hold few principles absolutely, not none','Cato was simply wrong about Caesar, so his principles were mistaken','Plutarch condemns Cato outright, so the objection is already conceded','Compromise with Caesar would certainly have saved the republic'],answer:0,why:'Plutarch leaves the question open. Cato\u2019s rightness and its uselessness both stand, which argues for a short, honest list, not for giving up principle.'}]}},'plutarch-lives_8':{pictureCaption:'Head bowed over an account of another man\u2019s life. What weighs on the reader is the comparison in the scroll, not anything in the room.',bridge:'Idea 4 described ambition outrunning judgement. This shows where one famous ambition took its measure.',basics:{title:'Who are you measuring yourself against?',beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'A ROMAN IN TEARS',text:'Julius Caesar became one of the most famous Romans of all time. When he was in his thirties, he sat reading about Alexander the Great. Then he began to cry. His friends asked him why.'},{kind:'explain',title:'His answer',text:'By Caesar\u2019s age, Alexander had won a huge empire. Caesar felt he had done nothing people would remember. But Alexander had been dead for more than 250 years. Caesar was racing a man he could never meet.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'He bends over the story of another man\u2019s life. Nothing in the room is wrong. What hurts is the man he is measuring himself against.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Most of us measure ourselves against someone. Who we pick can push us hard or make us miserable. So it is worth knowing who it is.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A RACE',text:'Imagine running a race and checking the wrong runner. You might feel slow next to a champion on TV. But you are not in their race. You are running your own.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO TOLD THE STORY',text:'Plutarch, a Greek writer, told this story about 150 years after Caesar died. He liked small moments like this one. He thought they showed what was going on inside a person.'},{kind:'try',text:'Write down one person you compare yourself to. Did you pick them, or did someone else pick them for you?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Sam feels bad about his drawings because he compares them to a famous artist\u2019s. What would this idea ask him first?',options:['Who he is measuring himself against, and why','To stop drawing, since he will never be that good','To draw for hours every day until he catches up'],answer:0,why:'The first step is to notice the measuring stick. Then Sam can decide if it is a fair one to use.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'ask',kicker:'BEFORE YOU READ ON',prompt:'Whose career do you quietly check yours against? Name them.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'CAESAR IN SPAIN',picture:!0,text:'Plutarch reports that Caesar, in his late thirties and governing a Roman province in Spain, was reading about Alexander and wept. Asked why, he said that at his age Alexander had ruled so many peoples, while he himself had still done nothing remarkable.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Most ambition has a specific person underneath it, and it is rarely one the person would name. Caesar\u2019s measure was not his peers but a man dead for centuries.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'The ordinary measure',items:['Peers of the same age','A normal Roman career','What his class expected']},right:{title:'Caesar\u2019s measure',items:['A long-dead Macedonian king','An empire won by 32','Nothing short of world fame']},note:'Against the left column he was doing well. He was using the right.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the anecdote is so useful',text:'A person\u2019s reference point is usually invisible, even to them. Here it surfaces in one reported moment, and much of what followed becomes easier to read. It is exactly the kind of small, revealing scene Plutarch said he would choose over a campaign.'},{kind:'try',text:'Write down, by name, who you are measuring yourself against. Then ask whether you chose them, or inherited them from a parent, a school or a feed.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A successful 40-year-old lawyer feels like a failure. Which question goes to the heart of Caesar\u2019s story?',options:['Whose career is she measuring her own against?','Is she working enough hours each week?','Would a different firm make her happier?','Has she been promoted as fast as she hoped?'],answer:0,why:'The feeling depends on the yardstick. Naming the person behind it is the first step to judging whether it is a fair one.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE ANECDOTE',text:'In the Life of Caesar, Plutarch reports that Caesar, reading about Alexander during a quiet spell in Spain, burst into tears. Pressed by his friends, he said Alexander had conquered so many nations by his age, while he had done nothing memorable. Plutarch adds no comment.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The tears locate the engine of a career in a comparison with a man dead for centuries. That reference point is not a rational input, and it explains what followed more economically than the campaigns do.'},{kind:'term',word:'Reference point',meaning:'The standard against which a result is judged a gain or a loss. The same result can feel like triumph or failure depending on it, and people rarely choose theirs on purpose.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW A YARDSTICK DRIVES',nodes:['Read about Alexander','Adopt his timetable as the measure','Feel behind, mid-career and successful','Reach for ever larger achievement'],note:'The measure was set by reading, not by Caesar\u2019s circumstances.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Lamplight, a bowed head, two hands flat on an unrolled scroll. The yardstick is on the table: a life read about, not lived alongside.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it earns its place',text:'It does what the preface promised: a small scene revealing more than battles. Caesar\u2019s later career is easier to read once you know he was keeping time against Alexander. Plutarch pairs the two Lives, so the reader is invited to run the very comparison Caesar ran.'},{kind:'limits',text:'One reported moment is thin ground for a whole psychology. The story may have been shaped to suit the pairing, and Suetonius, a near contemporary of Plutarch, tells a version in which Caesar is moved by a statue of Alexander rather than a book. Ambition has many causes. Treat the anecdote as a strong clue, not a diagnosis.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Caesar\u2019s measure was Alexander, not his peers.','A reference point drives ambition, and it is usually invisible.','Name yours, and ask whether you chose it.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE OVERREACH',prompt:'Which conclusion goes further than the anecdote can support?',options:['Caesar crossed the Rubicon because he once wept over Alexander','Caesar judged himself by an unusually extreme yardstick','Plutarch saw the scene as revealing Caesar\u2019s inner measure','The story may have been shaped by the pairing with Alexander'],answer:0,why:'The anecdote reveals a reference point. Making it the cause of one specific decision asks a single story to carry more than it can.'}]}},'plutarch-lives_9':{pictureCaption:'One boundary stone has been dug up, for a good reason. Look down the row: every stone after it is already leaning.',bridge:'Cato would not bend his principles even to win. The Gracchi broke one to do good, and Plutarch counts that cost too.',basics:{title:'A rule you break stays broken',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE CUP FINAL',text:'Your team has a rule: everyone gets the same time on the pitch. For the cup final, the coach breaks it and keeps the best players on all game. You win. The next week, she does it again in a normal match. Then the other coaches start doing it too.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Sometimes a rule is broken for a good reason. But once it is broken, others can break it too, for worse reasons. So before you break a rule, ask who else will.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'IN OLD ROME',text:'Two brothers in Rome, called the Gracchi, saw that poor families had lost their land to the rich. They wanted to share the land out more fairly. Plutarch, a Greek writer, thought they had a real point.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How they did it',text:'One brother, Tiberius, was blocked by another leader who said no to his plan. So Tiberius had the people vote that leader out of his job. No one had ever done that. The law passed. But now everyone knew it could be done.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One stone marker has been dug up, for a good reason. Now look down the row. Every stone after it is leaning.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before you bend a rule for a good reason, stop and ask: who else will use this, and for what?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your club has a rule: no phones during games. Jo wants to text her mum about a lift, which seems a good reason. What does this idea ask the club to think about too?',options:['If we allow it, who else will use the same reason?','Is Jo\u2019s reason good enough to break the rule?','Who made this rule in the first place?'],answer:0,why:'Jo\u2019s reason may be fine. The idea asks you to think about everyone who will copy it next, perhaps for weaker reasons.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'THE GRACCHI, ROME',left:{title:'The cause',items:['Land piled up in the hands of the rich','Poor citizens pushed off the land','Legal routes to reform closed off']},right:{title:'The method',items:['A fellow official removed from office','A limit broken that had held for centuries','Open to anyone who came after']},note:'Plutarch\u2019s point is that both columns are true at once.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The exception you make for an obviously good cause does not stay attached to the cause. A remedy that damages the means of remedy is still a remedy, and it is still damage.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'TIBERIUS GRACCHUS, 133 BCE',text:'Tiberius Gracchus was a tribune, an official elected to protect ordinary citizens. He proposed giving public land to the poor. A fellow tribune vetoed it. Tiberius had the assembly vote him out of office. That had never been done before. The law passed. Within months Tiberius was killed by a crowd led by senators.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the precedent taught',text:'Rome\u2019s system rested on limits that held because nobody broke them. Once one had been broken for a good cause, it was simply open to people with worse causes. Plutarch is sympathetic to the brothers and unsparing about this.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One boundary stone pulled up for a good reason. Down the line, the others are already leaning.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before bending a rule at work for a good reason, name the next three people who could cite your exception. Then say what they might use it for.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A manager skips the hiring process once to bring in an obviously excellent candidate fast. What is Plutarch\u2019s worry?',options:['The next manager can skip it too, for a far weaker candidate','The candidate may not turn out as excellent as hoped','The hiring process was probably useless to begin with','Colleagues will resent the new hire\u2019s quick start'],answer:0,why:'The exception does not stay attached to the good case. It becomes available to everyone who comes after.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'ROME, 133 BCE',text:'Tiberius Gracchus brought a law to give public land to poor citizens. When his colleague Octavius vetoed it, Tiberius had the assembly depose him. The law passed. Before the year was out, Tiberius and many of his supporters had been killed by senators and their followers.'},{kind:'term',word:'Tribune',meaning:'An official elected to protect ordinary Roman citizens, able to veto the acts of other officials. A tribune\u2019s person was sacred, and removing one by vote had no precedent.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Plutarch holds two things together: the reform was necessary, and its method set a precedent later used by people with worse intentions. Before breaking a rule for a good reason, ask who else will now break it, and for what.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why he pairs them with Spartans',text:'Plutarch sets the Gracchi beside Agis and Cleomenes, kings of Sparta who also tried to restore land and the old equality by overriding the existing order. The pairing makes the pattern itself the subject: reformers who had to break the system to repair it, and what the breaking cost.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One marker dug out, and a long row behind it, every stone already leaning. A boundary moved once is easier to move again.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW A PRECEDENT TRAVELS',nodes:['A good cause is blocked','A limit is broken to pass it','The breaking becomes precedent','Worse causes use it'],note:'Plutarch notes that Tiberius\u2019s killing was the first Roman political quarrel since the kings to end in citizens\u2019 blood. Precedents ran both ways.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The argument can be turned into a defence of any injustice: never break a rule, since someone might copy you. Plutarch does not say that. The lawful routes had been closed, and the poor were really suffering. His point is that the cost is real even when it is worth paying, and must be counted, not denied.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'HOLD BOTH',prompt:'A government overrides a court ruling to push through emergency relief that is plainly needed. Which response matches Plutarch\u2019s judgement on the Gracchi?',options:['The relief may be right, and the precedent will outlast it; both must be weighed','The relief is needed, so the method hardly matters','The method is unlawful, so the relief should be abandoned','Precedents only matter when those in power mean harm'],answer:0,why:'Plutarch neither excuses nor condemns. The remedy is still a remedy, and the damage to the system is still damage.'}]}}}},2995,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'power-of-concentration_1':{pictureCaption:'Nothing clever is happening here: two hands on a bar, again and again. Attention gets stronger the same dull way.',basics:{title:'Focus is something you build',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE USUAL STORY',belief:'Some people are just born good at focus. You have it or you don\u2019t.',reality:'Most people who can focus for a long time got there by practice. They did a little, many times. Focus grows like a skill does.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Focus is not something you either have or lack. It is like a muscle. You build it by using it a little, again and again.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE PULL-UPS',picture:!0,text:'The first time you try a pull-up, you might not get up at all. Nobody says you were born weak. You hang on the bar a bit each day. Slowly, your arms get strong.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why this helps',text:'If you think you are just bad at focus, you give up. If you think you have not trained yet, you know what to do. You start small. Then you add a little more.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Build it like a muscle',steps:['Pick one task, like reading.','Stay on it for five minutes.','Stop when the time is up.','Next week, try seven minutes.']},{kind:'question',prompt:'Mia can read for ten minutes. Then her mind drifts off. What would this idea tell her?',options:['Read ten minutes a day, then slowly add more','Some people just can\u2019t focus, so stop trying','Wait until she finds a book she loves'],answer:0,why:'Ten minutes is where her training starts. A little more each week builds the muscle.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'FOUR MINUTES IN',text:'You sit down to read a long report. Four minutes in, you are checking your phone. You tell yourself you are just not a focused person, and resolve to try harder tomorrow. Tomorrow goes the same way.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Concentration behaves less like a trait you have and more like a capacity you build. You do not summon it by wanting it. You train it, in short sessions that grow.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'A character problem',items:['\u201cI am not a focused person\u201d','Fix: resolve to try harder','Fails, and proves the verdict']},right:{title:'A fitness problem',items:['\u201cI have not trained this yet\u201d','Fix: short sessions, slowly longer','Fails, and sets the next target']},note:'Same four minutes. Only the second reading tells you what to do on Monday.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the muscle picture works',text:'The comparison is loose: attention is not literally a muscle. But it gets the practical point right. People who can hold attention for an hour have usually practised, often without calling it practice. Wanting to focus is not the same as being able to.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Hands on a bar, again and again, nothing clever about it. That is how the people who can focus got there too.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one task that needs focus. Time how long you last before you drift. Tomorrow, aim for that number plus two minutes.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A colleague says she has always been scattered, and nothing has ever helped. What does the idea suggest?',options:['Start with short timed sessions and lengthen them over weeks','Accept it: some people are simply wired to be scattered','Find more exciting work so that focus arrives on its own','Try much harder each day by sheer force of will'],answer:0,why:'The idea turns a verdict about her character into a training plan. Resolve and excitement both skip the training.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE BOOK AND ITS PREMISE',text:'The Power of Concentration was sold as a self-improvement manual for working people, under the name Theron Q. Dumont. Beneath its many exercises runs one premise: attention can be built by repetition, the way a body is built by exercise.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Concentration is a trainable capacity, not a mood or a fixed trait. It is built by repeated short practice, extended over time, and not summoned by resolve.'},{kind:'term',word:'Sustained attention',meaning:'The ability to keep attention on one task over time, especially when nothing about the task is pulling you in. It is the capacity the book\u2019s exercises are aimed at.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE REFRAME CHANGES ACTION',nodes:['Attention drifts after ten minutes','Read as untrained, not as flawed','Practise ten, then twelve','The baseline rises'],note:'Read as a flaw of character, the same failure leads only to more resolve, and resolve is not a method.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A bar, two hands, repetition. The premise is that attention is built this way, and that its dullness is the point.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What later research found',text:'The book predates the research, which has broadly gone its way: sustained attention does improve with deliberate practice. But the gain tends to be specific. You get better at what you practised, under the conditions you practised it in, more than you get better at everything.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The muscle metaphor is imperfect. Attention also depends on sleep, health, stress and the task itself, and for some people on conditions such as ADHD that practice alone does not fix. The book also wraps this premise in much weaker material. The premise does not depend on that material, so judge it on its own.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What task do you blame your character for failing at? What would the first, shortest training session for it look like?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'Someone plays a focus game daily for months, and finds their attention to reading is no better. What does the idea, with the research, predict?',options:['Gains tend to be specific, so practise on the reading itself','Attention cannot be trained after all, so give up on it','The months were too few; any practice transfers in the end','Reading takes willpower, which is a different thing from training'],answer:0,why:'Attention is trainable, but the training is specific. Practise under the conditions you need it in.'}]}},'power-of-concentration_2':{pictureCaption:'One dull stone, two hands doing nothing else. Nothing here is interesting, which is exactly why it trains attention.',bridge:'Idea 1 said attention is trained. This one says what to train it on.',basics:{title:'Practise on something boring',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO PRACTISE',prompt:'Which one trains your focus more?',options:['Playing a fun game for an hour','Looking at a plain stone for five minutes'],answer:1,reveal:'The game holds you by itself. You don\u2019t have to try. The stone gives you no help, so your own focus has to do the work.'},{kind:'idea',text:'To train your focus, practise on something dull. Fun things grab your attention for you. Dull things make you hold it yourself.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A HEAVY BAG',text:'Carrying an empty bag up the stairs does not make you stronger. The weight is what trains you. When you train focus, the dullness is the weight.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A plain grey stone and two still hands. There is nothing here to grab you. That is the point.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'WHY HOMEWORK FEELS HARD',text:'A video game can hold you for hours. A page of maths loses you in five minutes. That is not because you are bad at focus. The game was doing the holding. Maths needs focus you hold yourself.'},{kind:'try',text:'Watch the second hand of a clock go round for five minutes. Don\u2019t look away. Don\u2019t pick up anything else. That is the whole exercise.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Sam wants to focus better in class. Which practice fits this idea?',options:['Five minutes a day on something plain, with no switching','An hour a day of his favourite game','Only reading the books he loves best'],answer:0,why:'Class is often dull. So he needs practice where the thing is dull too, and he holds on anyway.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'To build focus, start with material you love, so the habit sticks.',reality:'When material grips you, interest is doing the work. Your attention is being carried, not exercised. The capacity worth building is the one you use when nothing is pulling you in.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Practise attention on deliberately uninteresting material. Only dull material makes your attention do the work, and dull is the condition most real work happens under.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Gripping material',items:['The material holds you','Feels like focus','Trains very little']},right:{title:'Dull material',items:['You hold the material','Feels like effort','Trains the real skill']},note:'The effort you feel on the right is the training happening.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why focus built on fun does not carry over',text:'Skills carry over best between similar conditions. Focus practised on a thriller is practised with help you will not have on a spreadsheet, a contract or the fourth page of a dry report. The practice never met the conditions the skill is needed in.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A stone with nothing to offer, and hands that stay put anyway. Here the dullness is the resistance.'},{kind:'try',text:'Spend five minutes on something boring without switching: a plain object, a clock\u2019s second hand, a page of small print. Notice the urge to switch, and do not obey it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You want to concentrate better on the dry policy documents at work. Which practice fits the idea best?',options:['Short daily sessions on dull material, without switching','Reading gripping novels to build a habit of focus','Waiting for projects that interest you more','Playing music to make the documents more engaging'],answer:0,why:'The skill you need is focus without help from interest. Only practice on dull material trains that.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE BOOK\u2019S DRILLS',text:'The exercises are tedious on purpose. Watch the second hand of a clock go round. Hold a single object in mind and nothing else. Nothing in them rewards you, and that is not an oversight. It is the design.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Train attention under the conditions it is needed in. Interesting material lends you its pull, so it exercises nothing. Only material with no pull makes you supply the attention yourself.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A dull stone, chosen for its dullness, and hands that do nothing else. The resistance in this exercise is the boredom.'},{kind:'term',word:'Transfer',meaning:'In learning research, whether practice at one task improves a different one. Transfer is usually strongest when the practice and the real task share their conditions.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Better reasons than the book\u2019s',text:'The book\u2019s own reasoning is weaker than its instruction. The sound case is simple. Most real work offers no pull. If practice always came with interest attached, you trained with help that will be missing when it counts. Dull material makes practice and use match, as resistance does in strength training.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'HOW MUCH THE MATERIAL HELPS',low:'Gripping: the material holds you',high:'Dull: you hold the material',note:'Training happens towards the right, and most of the work you need focus for already lives there.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Dull practice trains a narrow thing: staying with low-interest material. It does not make dull work wise, and interest remains a strong ally of learning when you can get it. Taken too far, the idea becomes a cult of tedium, in which boredom is treated as a virtue rather than a training condition.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Interest lends you attention; it does not train it.','Practise where nothing pulls you in.','Dullness is a training tool, not a virtue.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these is a misuse of the idea?',options:['Making a class deliberately boring because boredom builds character','Practising focus on a plain object for five minutes daily','Reading dry documents without switching, as attention practice','Noting that a gripping novel is not much focus training'],answer:0,why:'Dullness is the resistance in a training exercise. It is not a teaching method, or a virtue in itself.'}]}},'power-of-concentration_3':{pictureCaption:'The bench is cleared for the night and one hammer is laid out. Tomorrow begins without any choosing.',bridge:'Idea 2 built attention. This one stops you spending the best of it on a choice you could have made last night.',basics:{title:'Pick tomorrow\u2019s job tonight',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'SATURDAY MORNING',text:'You wake up with a free morning. You could tidy your room. You could start your project. You could practise guitar. You think about it. You check your phone. An hour later, you have done none of them.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'Where did the hour go?',options:['Into the jobs','Into choosing'],answer:1,reveal:'The hour went on choosing. Your fresh morning brain was used up on a question you could have answered the night before.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Pick tomorrow\u2019s main job tonight. Then in the morning you just start. No time is lost on choosing.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The tools are put away for the night. One hammer is laid out, ready. In the morning, the choice is already made.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why tonight is better',text:'At night, the choice is quick and easy. In the morning, your head is fresh. That fresh time is best for doing, not for deciding. And a sleepy you is less likely to argue with a choice already made.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before bed, write tomorrow\u2019s one main job on a note. Put it where you will see it first thing.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Leo wants to work on his science project on Sunday. When does this idea say he should pick which part to do?',options:['On Saturday night','On Sunday, once he is up','Whenever he feels like it'],answer:0,why:'Picking the night before means Sunday starts with doing, not choosing.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'NINE A.M., COFFEE IN HAND',prompt:'You have a clear morning and six things you could do. What usually happens first?',options:['You start the hardest task','You look over the options and check email','You start the task you dread'],answer:1,reveal:'Choosing comes first, and it rarely feels like work. By the time you have chosen, the freshest part of the morning has gone.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Settle tomorrow\u2019s main task tonight, while the decision is cheap. A task chosen in advance is begun without a period of choosing, and that period is where most mornings are lost.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Decided in the morning',items:['Fresh attention spent on choosing','Open to renegotiation','Starts late, if at all']},right:{title:'Decided the night before',items:['Fresh attention spent on working','Already settled','Starts at once']},note:'The work is the same. Only the timing of one small decision moved.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What it really protects',text:'The book presents this as a concentration technique, and its explanation is vague. It is better understood as protecting attention from a decision. The choice costs a minute in the evening. In the morning it costs your best hour.'},{kind:'explain',title:'It also closes the debate',text:'A plan settled calmly the night before is carried out by a morning self who did not make it, and so has less standing to reopen it. It echoes the Roman emperor Marcus Aurelius arguing himself out of bed.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Bench cleared, one hammer laid out for the morning. The decision was made while it was cheap.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before you stop work today, write tomorrow\u2019s first task in one line, specific enough to start without thinking. Leave it where you will see it first.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Which note, written the evening before, best applies the idea?',options:['9 a.m.: draft section two of the report','Tomorrow: be productive and stay focused','Look at the list and pick something important','Get through as much of the list as possible'],answer:0,why:'Only the 9 a.m. note settles the decision. The others leave the choosing for the morning.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Deciding the night before is a concentration technique.',reality:'That is how the book presents it, with a vague explanation. It is better read as a decision technique: it moves a choice out of the hour when attention is freshest.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Settle the next day\u2019s main task in advance. The effect is not on concentration itself but on what comes before it: the morning starts with doing, not choosing, and the choice cannot cheaply be reopened.'},{kind:'term',word:'Decision fatigue',meaning:'The idea that making choices wears down the quality of later ones. The strongest claims made for it are disputed, but the everyday point stands: choosing takes time and attention.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Two mechanisms',text:'First, a task chosen in advance is begun without a preliminary spell of choosing, which is where most mornings go. Second, it removes the chance to renegotiate. The morning self did not make the decision, so it has less standing to revise it.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHERE THE DECISION SITS',nodes:['Evening: choose, with time to spare','Morning: the choice is waiting','Work starts without debate'],note:'It echoes Marcus Aurelius arguing himself out of bed.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A bench at night, every tool put away but one. The morning inherits a decision, not a question.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Old, and often rediscovered',text:'The advice was not new in this book, and nearly every productivity writer since has found it again. It holds up. What does not hold up is the book\u2019s account of why it works, which never names a mechanism.'},{kind:'limits',text:'A plan made at night can be wrong by morning: a deadline moves, a crisis lands. The rule is that reluctance cannot reopen the plan, not that new facts cannot. And where the work depends on what yesterday turned up, deciding too early can lock in a stale choice.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'WHAT MAY REOPEN IT?',prompt:'Last night you settled on starting with the budget. Which of these should change the plan this morning?',options:['An email saying the budget deadline has moved back a week','A strong preference for doing something easier first','A feeling that you are not in the mood for numbers','The thought that it would go better after lunch'],answer:0,why:'A moved deadline is a new fact. The others are the morning renegotiation that deciding in advance exists to prevent.'}]}},'power-of-concentration_4':{pictureCaption:'He is at his bench, but his head is turned to the door left open. An unfinished job pulls at attention the same way.',bridge:'Idea 3 cleared one decision out of the morning. This one clears the unfinished jobs that leak attention all day.',basics:{title:'Unfinished jobs keep tugging at you',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A DOOR LEFT OPEN',picture:!0,text:'You are trying to work, and the door behind you is open. Light and noise leak in. You keep turning to look. Jobs you have not finished do the same thing to your mind.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Jobs you have not finished keep tugging at your mind. They pull your focus away from what you are doing now. So finish small jobs, or write down when you will.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'DURING HOMEWORK',text:'You are doing maths. But you have not answered your friend\u2019s message. You have not packed your PE kit. Every few minutes, one of them pops into your head. Your maths keeps getting cut in half.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why a plan helps',text:'Your mind keeps reminding you because it is not sure the job will get done. Give it a clear plan, like *pack PE kit at 7 tonight.* Then it can stop nagging. You do not always have to finish the job right away.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Close the open doors',steps:['Write down each job that keeps popping up.','Small and quick? Do it now.','Bigger? Write when you will do it.','Go back to your work.']},{kind:'question',prompt:'While you read, you keep thinking about tidying your room. What does this idea say to do?',options:['Write down when you will tidy it, then read','Push the thought away and read harder','Stop reading and give up for today'],answer:0,why:'A clear plan tells your mind the job is handled. Then it stops tugging at you.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THREE THINGS SURFACING',text:'You are trying to write. Every few minutes something surfaces: the form you never sent back, the call you still owe, the bill you meant to check. Each time you push it aside. Each time it costs you the sentence you were in.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Unfinished tasks keep drawing attention, without your choosing, and weaken your focus on whatever is in front of you. The cost of an open item is paid all the time, not just when you do it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A man at his bench with his head turned to a door left ajar. Open tasks pull at attention like that gap of light.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the book says to do',text:'Its fix is unsentimental: finish small things rather than letting them pile up. A two-minute job left open is not two minutes saved. It is a small tax on every hour until it is done.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the book missed',text:'Later research suggests a refinement the book lacks. A specific plan for when a task will be done can ease much of the intrusion without finishing it. What nags is not the task itself. It is the vagueness about it.'},{kind:'steps',title:'For each nagging item',steps:['If it takes two minutes, do it now.','Otherwise, write the very next physical action.','Give that action a day and a time.','Put it where you will see it then.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You cannot get the broken boiler fixed today, but it keeps intruding on your work. What best applies the idea?',options:['Write \u201ccall the plumber, Tuesday 9 a.m.\u201d in your diary','Keep pushing the thought away until the boiler is fixed','Stop work and spend the afternoon reading about boilers','Promise yourself you will deal with it sometime soon'],answer:0,why:'Finishing is not possible today, but a specific plan removes the vagueness, which is what was doing the nagging.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'A NAME FROM THE 1920s',word:'Zeigarnik effect',meaning:'Named after the psychologist Bluma Zeigarnik, who reported that people recall interrupted tasks more readily than finished ones. The book describes the same pull before those experiments were run.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Open tasks are not stored quietly. They intrude, unasked, and every intrusion is taken from the task in hand. So close them: by finishing, or by fixing exactly when.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the book saw',text:'It observes that unfinished business keeps drawing attention and degrades focus on the present task. Its inference is reasonable: finish small things instead of letting them accumulate, because an open item\u2019s cost is paid continuously rather than once.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Hands at the bench, head turned to the open door. Every glance at the gap is attention taken from the work.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE LEAK',nodes:['A job is left open','It intrudes during other work','Focus on that work drops','Less gets finished, more stays open'],loop:!0,note:'The loop feeds itself: each open item makes the next one harder to close.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The refinement it lacked',text:'Some later studies suggest that a specific plan, with a when and a where, can ease much of the intrusion without finishing. That matters, because finishing is often not available. The book offers only completion, and so asks more of the reader than it needs to.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Popular accounts make the lab findings sound tidier and stronger than they are, so treat this as sensible practice rather than settled science. And the fix has its own failure: a long list of dated plans, constantly rescheduled, becomes a fresh source of nagging. A plan only quiets the mind if you trust yourself to keep it.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'List three things that surfaced during your last hour of work. For each, which would quiet it: doing it now, or giving it a date?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'WHERE THE BOOK OVERREACHES',prompt:'Which claim goes further than the idea, as refined since, supports?',options:['You must finish every open task before you can focus','A small open task costs attention until it is closed','A specific plan can quiet a task you cannot finish yet','Intrusions from open tasks arrive without being chosen'],answer:0,why:'That is the book\u2019s overstatement. A dated plan can ease much of the pull, so completion is not the only way out.'}]}},'power-of-concentration_5':{pictureCaption:'The gilded cover has been lifted off and set aside. What it was holding is small and plain.',bridge:'Ideas 1 to 4 are the book\u2019s useful core. This one steps back to look at the wrapping round them.',basics:{title:'Look under the fancy cover',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'A BOX TOO BIG',text:'You open a big, shiny present. Inside is lots of paper, then more paper. At the bottom is one small gift. The gift might be great. But the box made it look much bigger than it was.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Some old advice books wrap a few good ideas in lots of big talk. Take off the wrapping. Then judge the small part that is left.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'THIS BOOK',text:'This book about focus is about a hundred years old. It was sold to help people get on at work. A few parts are really useful, like training your focus. A lot of it is loud, bold talk that tells you nothing new.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The fancy cover has been lifted off and put to one side. What was inside is small and plain.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'HOW TO TELL',left:{title:'Wrapping',items:['Says it very loudly','Says it again and again','Never says how it knows']},right:{title:'The real part',items:['Tells you what to do','You can test it','Works when you try it']}},{kind:'explain',title:'Why loud can fool you',text:'When someone says a thing in a very sure voice, it sounds true. But saying it louder does not make it more true. Lots of ads and videos today work the same way.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A video gives five tips for learning spellings. It sounds very sure, but gives no reasons. What should you do?',options:['Try the tips you can test, and see if they work','Believe them all, since it sounds so sure','Ignore every tip, since it gives no reasons'],answer:0,why:'Sounding sure is just wrapping. Some tips might still be useful, so test them and see.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHAT KIND OF BOOK THIS IS',text:'It was published as part of a commercial self-improvement series. The packaging shapes the content: short chapters, confident claims, no sources. There is also a steady hint that every problem the reader has is within their control.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Strip the period packaging off an old self-help book and judge what is left. Here, what is left is a short, sober manual on attention that would fit well in a catalogue today.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'An ornate gilded cover lifted off and set aside, and the small plain book it was holding. Judge the plain one.'},{kind:'equation',parts:['A few sound techniques','Assertion repeated with emphasis'],result:'A persuasive self-help book',note:'Only the first part carries information. The second reads as conviction and adds nothing.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the format persuades',text:'Certainty, brevity and no hedges make a book feel authoritative. They are also the very features that suggest nobody has tested the claims. A tested claim usually arrives with conditions attached.'},{kind:'try',text:'Take a self-help book or post you liked. Mark each claim that comes with a reason or a test. See how much is left when you remove the rest.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A productivity book makes ten bold claims with no evidence. Three you could try this week. What does the idea suggest?',options:['Try the three and judge them by results, setting the rest aside','Trust all ten, since the author sounds so sure of them','Reject the whole book, because it is badly argued','Look for a newer book that says the same thing calmly'],answer:0,why:'Stripping the packaging means you neither swallow it whole nor throw out the useful part along with it.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'WHO WAS DUMONT?',text:'Theron Q. Dumont was a pen name. Behind it was William Walker Atkinson, a prolific American writer of the New Thought movement, who published under several names. The movement held that the mind shapes health and fortune, and it spread that belief through short, confident manuals.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The format, a commercial self-improvement manual, shapes the content. Read past it and you find a small sound core on attention, wrapped in assertion that informs no more for being repeated.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'The core',items:['Attention is trainable','Practise on dull material','Decide in advance']},right:{title:'The packaging',items:['Will and magnetism','Claims without sources','Repetition for emphasis']},note:'The core is a small share of the pages and nearly all of the value.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The ornate casing lifted off and set aside, far bigger than the plain book it held. The size of the wrapping is not the size of the content.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The implied promise',text:'Under every chapter runs a message: your difficulties are entirely within your control. It flatters the reader, and it is false. A reader who believes it ends up blaming themselves for what is partly circumstance.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the format still matters',text:'The features that make such a book persuasive, certainty, brevity and no qualification, are the same features that signal untested claims. The format did not end with this book. It runs through much of the self-help shelf and a good deal of what is posted online.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Stripping the packaging is a judgement, and it can go wrong both ways. A reader may keep whatever flatters them and call it the core. And plain writing is no proof of truth. The useful core here earns credit because later evidence supports it, not because it sounds sober.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of a confident claim you have repeated lately. Was it ever tested, or only said often?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'Which feature of a new self-help book should most raise your suspicion?',options:['Sweeping claims stated with certainty and no conditions','Short chapters that each end with an exercise','An author who admits that some ideas are borrowed','Advice that is old and has been widely repeated'],answer:0,why:'Tested claims usually come with conditions. Certainty without them is the packaging, not the content.'}]}},'power-of-concentration_6':{pictureCaption:'Hands flat on the knees, feet flat on the floor, nothing moving. The book starts training attention here, with the body.',bridge:'Back to the exercises. This is where the book itself begins them: with the body, not the mind.',basics:{title:'Sit still first',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A STRANGE CHALLENGE',prompt:'How hard is it to sit totally still for five minutes?',options:['Easy. You just sit there.','Much harder than it sounds'],answer:1,reveal:'Try it. Soon you want to scratch, shift or look round. Your body keeps trying to get up and go somewhere.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Before you try to keep your mind still, keep your body still. A fidgety body often goes with a busy mind. The body is easier to start with.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Hands on knees. Feet flat on the floor. Nothing is moving. This is where the book starts.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why start with the body?',text:'When your mind wanders, you often move too. You tap, shift or reach for something. You can see and stop the moving more easily than the thinking. So you start there.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'BEFORE HOMEWORK',text:'You sit down to do homework. You stay still for two minutes first. No phone, no snacks, no getting up. Now that you have sat down on purpose, getting up feels harder. Starting feels easier.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before your next hard job, sit still for two minutes. Hands on your knees. Feet on the floor. Notice how much you want to move.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Ana can\u2019t settle to read. She keeps getting up for snacks. What does this idea say to try first?',options:['Sit still for two minutes, then open the book','Read fast so she finishes before she gets fidgety','Eat all the snacks first, then start to read'],answer:0,why:'Getting the body still first takes away one way to escape. Then her mind has an easier start.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'FIVE MINUTES IN A CHAIR',text:'The book\u2019s first exercises are physical. Sit in a chair and do not move for five minutes. Hold one hand out level, without a tremor. Keep your eyes fixed on one point. Anyone who tries the first finds it far harder than it sounds.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Train physical stillness before mental stillness. Fidgeting is the visible form of a wandering attention, and the body is the easier of the two to get hold of.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHY IT WORKS',nodes:['You sit still on purpose','One route of escape is closed','Getting up now costs more','Starting the work is easier'],note:'No mysterious force is involved. It is a small behavioural trick with a plausible mechanism.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Two reasons',text:'Restlessness and distraction tend to travel together, so holding one still removes a route for the other. And having sat down deliberately, you have made a small commitment. Breaking it costs more than never making it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Hands flat on knees, feet flat on the floor. Unglamorous, and the right place to start.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before your next difficult task, sit completely still for two minutes. Then notice how much of the difficulty was restlessness, not the task.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You keep getting up to make tea whenever a report gets hard. What does the idea suggest?',options:['Sit still for a few minutes before starting, and stay seated','Make a whole pot of tea in advance so there is no excuse','Work standing up so the restlessness has an outlet','Accept that you simply think better when moving about'],answer:0,why:'The getting up is the visible form of the wandering. Holding the body still first closes that route.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'CLAIM AND PRACTICE',left:{title:'The book\u2019s explanation',items:['Nervous energy leaking away','A vital force brought under control']},right:{title:'A defensible account',items:['Restlessness and distraction travel together','One route of escape is closed','A small commitment is made']},note:'The explanation is nonsense. The practice survives it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Begin with the body. Sitting without fidgeting for a set time before focused work closes one channel of escape and makes a small commitment: a mechanism, not a mystery.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The order of the exercises',text:'The first drills are physical: stillness in a chair, a hand held out level without a tremor, the eyes fixed on a point. Attention held on an object comes later. The order is sound, because the body is easier to observe and correct than the mind.'},{kind:'term',word:'Commitment device',meaning:'An arrangement made in advance that raises the cost of changing course later. Sitting down deliberately and staying there is a small one: getting up now means breaking something you chose.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A UNIFORM',text:'A uniform does not change who you are. But putting it on changes what you are likely to do next. Deliberate stillness works for roughly the same reason: the physical arrangement shapes the behaviour that follows.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A figure seated square in an empty room, hands on knees, feet on the floor. The arrangement of the body comes before the work.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The effect is modest. That restlessness and distraction go together does not show that stillness causes focus. Some people think better moving, on a walk or at a standing desk, and some with ADHD may find enforced stillness costs attention rather than freeing it. It is a useful opening trick, not a law.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'A friend says she thinks best while pacing, so the stillness exercise is useless. What is the fairest reply?',options:['It may not suit her: it is a modest trick, not a law','She is wrong, since movement always means a wandering mind','Her pacing proves the book right about nervous energy','She should force herself to sit still until it works'],answer:0,why:'The practice works by closing an escape route. Where movement helps thinking rather than escaping it, the trick has nothing to close.'}]}},'power-of-concentration_7':{pictureCaption:'One plain button in the middle of an empty table. Nothing else to look at, for as long as the timer runs.',bridge:'Idea 2 explained why the material should be dull. This is the full drill, and the part most people get wrong: what to do when the mind wanders.',basics:{title:'The coming back is the training',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE BUTTON TEST',picture:!0,text:'There is one plain button on an empty table. Your job is to look at it for five minutes. That is all. After ten seconds, your mind has gone off to think about lunch.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'If your mind wanders, you are doing it wrong.',reality:'Your mind will wander. Every time. Noticing it and coming back is the exercise. Each time you come back is like one push-up.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Pick one dull thing. Set a timer. Look at it. When your mind wanders, bring it back, calmly. The coming back is the training.'},{kind:'steps',title:'The button drill',steps:['Put one plain thing in front of you.','Set a timer for five minutes.','Keep your eyes and mind on it.','When you drift, come back. Count it.']},{kind:'explain',title:'Why count the times you come back?',text:'Coming back is the part that builds focus. So lots of come-backs is not a fail. It means you did lots of training. Getting cross with yourself just wastes the time.'},{kind:'try',text:'Do the button drill today with any plain thing: a cup, a coin, a pencil. How many times did you come back?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Ravi tries the drill. His mind wanders twelve times, and he comes back each time. How did he do?',options:['Well: that was twelve bits of training','Badly: he should give up on it','Badly: he needs a more fun thing to look at'],answer:0,why:'Each time he came back was one push-up for his focus. Twelve wanders meant twelve push-ups.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO PEOPLE, ONE DRILL',left:{title:'Wandering is failure',items:['Mind drifts at twenty seconds','\u201cI\u2019m bad at this\u201d','Gives up by day three']},right:{title:'Returning is the rep',items:['Mind drifts at twenty seconds','Notices, and comes back','Still going in week three']},note:'Same mind, same drift. One belief decides who keeps practising.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Hold attention on one dull object for a fixed time, and bring it back each time it leaves, without frustration. Noticing the wander and returning is the exercise, not a failure of it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The three working parts',text:'A single object, so there is one clear place to return to. A fixed time, decided in advance, so the session does not end when it gets uncomfortable. And the return, which is the repetition the whole drill is built around.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A single button on an empty table. One object, so there is always exactly one place to come back to.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'TRAINING FOR A HILL',text:'Practising attention on something you find interesting is like training for a hill on flat ground. It feels good and prepares you for little. The dull object is the hill, and each return is a step up it.'},{kind:'try',text:'Set a timer for five minutes, look at one dull object, and bring your attention back each time it wanders. Count the returns, and write the number down.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'After a week of the drill, your mind still wanders about fifteen times a session. What does that show?',options:['You are getting about fifteen repetitions each session','The drill is not working for you and should stop','You need a more interesting object to hold you','You should keep extending the timer until wandering stops'],answer:0,why:'Each wander is followed by a return, and the return is the rep. Wandering is the condition the drill trains in.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'BEFORE THE DRILL',prompt:'Two five-minute sessions on the same object. Which was the more useful training?',options:['The mind wandered twice; both returns were easy','The mind wandered twenty times; each time, you returned'],answer:1,reveal:'If the return is the repetition, the second session held ten times as many. The first may only have been a good day.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The core drill: one object, chosen to be dull, for a fixed period decided in advance, with attention returned each time it wanders. The return is the exercise, not a failure of it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'An empty table and one plain button. A single object means the return always has the same destination.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the book gets right',text:'All three working parts are present: a single object, a duration settled beforehand, and the instruction to return without frustration. The last is the one modern mindfulness teaching also stresses. Someone who reads wandering as failure stops. Someone who reads the return as the repetition carries on.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'ONE REPETITION',nodes:['Attention on the object','It wanders','You notice','You return, without blame'],loop:!0,note:'Each pass round this loop is one rep. The aim is not to stay at the top. It is to go round.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where else it appears',text:'In structure, the drill is close to concentration practices in contemplative traditions, such as resting attention on the breath, and to attention-training methods developed much later. The likeness is one of structure: the same three parts, arrived at for the same reasons.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The drill trains staying with one dull object. How far that carries into other work is less certain, since attention gains tend to be specific. A session can also turn into a stream of self-criticism, which defeats it. And for some people, long stretches of inward focus stir up distress, so short sessions are the wiser start.'},{kind:'recap',points:['One dull object, for a fixed time decided in advance.','Wandering is expected. Returning is the rep.','Count returns, not failures.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISREADING',prompt:'Which instruction would undermine the drill?',options:['End the session early whenever the mind wanders too often','Choose the object before starting, and keep it the same','Decide the duration in advance, and stick to it','Return attention calmly, without scolding yourself'],answer:0,why:'Ending when wandering gets heavy drops the fixed period and treats wandering as failure, the two things the drill exists to avoid.'}]}},'power-of-concentration_8':{pictureCaption:'A window full of the same book, stacked high. Behind it, one pair of hands is actually working on one sheet.',bridge:'You probably half knew ideas 1 to 7 already. This one asks why knowing them has not been enough.',basics:{title:'Knowing is not doing',beats:[{kind:'picture',kicker:'THE SHOP WINDOW',caption:'The same book, piled high. Lots of people will read it. Behind the piles, one person is doing the work.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Most people already know the same tips. Knowing them is not what makes the difference. Doing them, again and again, is.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE FOOTBALL TEAM',text:'Everyone on your team knows that practice makes you better. The coach has said it a hundred times. But only some of them practise at home. Those are the ones who get better. They did not know more. They did more.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Knowing',items:['Read the tip','Nod along','Read one more tip']},right:{title:'Doing',items:['Pick one tip','Do it today','Do it again tomorrow']}},{kind:'explain',title:'The funny part',text:'This book says that more reading is not what you need. Then it keeps on giving you more to read! You don\u2019t need the next tip. You need to use the one you have.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of one thing you already know you should do, like practising your spellings. Do it for ten minutes today. Then do it two more days in a row.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Zoe has read five books on how to get better at chess. She has not played in weeks. What would help her most?',options:['Play some games, using what she knows','Read a sixth book with better tips','Watch videos of the best players'],answer:0,why:'She already knows enough. What she is missing is the doing.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'SAME COURSES, SAME BOOKS',prompt:'Two people in your field took the same courses and read the same books. One is clearly better at the work. What most likely explains it?',options:['One knows a secret the other missed','One applies what they both know, consistently','One is simply more talented'],answer:1,reveal:'Knowledge in a trade is widely shared. What is rarer is staying on one thing long enough for it to come to something.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Knowledge is widely shared; application is not. What separates people who know the same things is what they consistently do with them.'},{kind:'explain',title:'An awkward point for a book',text:'The advice in this book was not new when it was written and is not new now. You probably knew already that attention improves with practice and that open tasks distract. So the limit is rarely information, and no amount of further reading fixes a failure to do.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A window stacked with one title, a dozen times over. Behind it, one pair of hands working on a single sheet.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where the book fails its own test',text:'Having said that knowing is not the problem, it goes on supplying more knowing. That is what the format rewards. Another chapter can be sold; an instruction to stop reading and go and practise cannot.'},{kind:'try',text:'Name one thing you already know you should do about your focus. Spend your next three work sessions doing it instead of reading about it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You have finished your fourth book on writing and have written nothing this month. What does the idea point to?',options:['Write for the next three sessions with what you already know','Find the book that finally explains the method properly','Take a course to learn the techniques you may be missing','Keep reading until you feel ready to begin'],answer:0,why:'The constraint is not knowledge. More reading, or more teaching, treats the wrong problem.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'scale',kicker:'WHICH CONSTRAINT BINDS?',low:'Information-limited: you do not know what to do',high:'Application-limited: you know, and do not do it',note:'The book argues that most readers sit at the right-hand end, where more reading does nothing.'},{kind:'idea',text:'What separates people in the same trade is rarely what they know. It is the ability to stay on one thing long enough for it to come to something, and to keep doing it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Overstated, and pointing at something real',text:'Not everybody knows the same things: expertise is real, and some people lack basic information. But in most trades the core advice is common property. Where it is, the bottleneck is doing it, and that changes what helps.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The book\u2019s self-defeat',text:'Having found that knowing is not the constraint, the book keeps supplying knowing. A book can hardly do otherwise: it is made of information. Its own observation undercuts the reason to read further, and it never follows that through.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Identical stacks fill the window. The only thing here that will come to something is the sheet under one working hand.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The claim is overstated. Sometimes the problem really is information: a wrong method practised diligently stays wrong. And telling people their problem is application can turn into blame when the real obstacles are time, health or circumstance. The useful move is to ask which constraint actually binds, not to assume it is always effort.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What do you already know you should do, and have read about more than once? What would three sessions of doing it look like?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'DIAGNOSE THE CASE',prompt:'In which case is more reading actually the right move?',options:['A runner keeps getting injured following a training plan that is flawed','A writer knows the routine works but rarely sits down to it','A student knows spaced revision helps and keeps cramming','A manager knows check-ins matter and keeps cancelling them'],answer:0,why:'Only the runner lacks information: diligent practice of a wrong method. The others already know, and the gap is in the doing.'}]}},'power-of-concentration_9':{pictureCaption:'Two weights that look the same from the front. From the side, one is hollow. Some of the book\u2019s claims are like that one.',bridge:'Idea 5 described the packaging. This one names the claims inside it that are simply false, and what they can teach.',basics:{title:'Right about one thing, wrong about another',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'TRUSTING A BOOK',belief:'If a book is right about one thing, it is likely right about the rest.',reality:'No. A book can be right about one part and wrong about another. You have to check each kind of claim on its own.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'IN THIS BOOK',text:'This book has good tips, like training your focus with short, dull drills. You can test those. But it also says strong thoughts can change what other people do, from far away. That is not true. Nobody has ever shown it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Some books mix true things with false things. They say both in the same sure voice. So check each claim on its own, even when you trust the book.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'From the front, these two weights look the same. From the side, you can see that one is hollow.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A FRIEND\u2019S STORIES',text:'A friend tells you there is a test on Friday. That turns out to be true. Then she says she saw a dog drive a car. You don\u2019t believe the dog part just because the test part was true.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How to check',text:'Ask: can I test this myself? Has anyone shown it is true? A drill you can try is one kind of claim. A power you can\u2019t see is a very different kind.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A game guide gives you a cheat that works. It also says a secret level opens if you wish hard enough. What should you think?',options:['The cheat worked, but the wish part needs its own check','The cheat worked, so the wish part must work too','The wish part is silly, so the cheat must be fake'],answer:0,why:'Each claim gets checked on its own. Being right once does not make the rest right.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'A FEW CHAPTERS APART',text:'On one page, a clear drill: sit still, hold attention on one object, return it when it wanders. You try it, and it helps. A few chapters on, the same confident voice explains how concentrated thought can influence other people at a distance.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Some of the book\u2019s claims are simply false: thought transference, personal magnetism, mental effort producing material results. They sit beside the sound exercises, in the same confident voice.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How the trick works',text:'A book earns credibility with something you can check. The drill works, so you extend trust. Then it spends that trust on something you cannot check, and the voice never changes to warn you.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two weights that look identical from the front. Seen from the side, one is hollow. Check the side.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Checkable',items:['Practise attention on a dull object','Sit still before working','Try it and see']},right:{title:'Unsupported or refuted',items:['Thought moves other people at a distance','Will reshapes physical health','Mental effort produces material results']},note:'Same voice, same certainty. Different kinds of claim.'},{kind:'try',text:'Take a source you trust because it was right once. Pick its next claim and ask: is this the same kind of claim as the one I checked?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A podcast host\u2019s sleep tips have worked well for you. Now she says a supplement cures anxiety. What does the idea suggest?',options:['Check the supplement claim on its own evidence','Trust it, since her other advice has worked','Dismiss it, since podcasters tend to exaggerate','Try it, and judge it by how you feel afterwards'],answer:0,why:'Her sleep tips were checked. The supplement is a different kind of claim, and trust earned on one does not carry over by itself.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHAT THE BOOK ASSERTS',text:'Alongside the drills, the book claims that concentrated thought can influence other people directly, that will can shape physical health, and that enough mental effort produces material results. That last is the doctrine of the prosperity literature of the period.'},{kind:'idea',text:'These are not dated phrasings of defensible ideas. They were unevidenced then and have been refuted since. Their use now is as a contrast: they show where a text stops being supported and starts asserting.'},{kind:'term',word:'Halo effect',meaning:'The tendency to let a good impression in one area colour judgements in another. A book that is right about a checkable drill gets credited, unfairly, on claims of a different kind.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How the mix works on a reader',text:'The checkable material works, so the reader\u2019s trust rises. The unsupported claims arrive in the same tone, with the same certainty, and inherit that trust. Nothing in the voice marks the change from one kind of claim to the other.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One weight solid, one hollow, identical from the front. A source\u2019s confident claims can look alike until you check from the side.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Before extending trust',steps:['Name the claim you checked, and how.','Name the next claim you are asked to accept.','Ask whether it can be checked the same way.','If not, give it no credit from the first.']},{kind:'limits',text:'The lesson can be over-learned. One false claim does not make the rest false, and discarding the attention drills because the same book sells magnetism loses something real. The reverse error is the commoner one, though. Check each kind of claim on its own evidence, in both directions.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A health writer\u2019s advice on sleep is well supported. Her new book says positive thinking alone can cure serious illness. What is the best response?',options:['Treat it as a new kind of claim, needing its own evidence','Accept it, given her strong record on checkable advice','Reject all her advice, since this one claim is clearly false','Suspend judgement on everything she has ever written'],answer:0,why:'Her record covers claims of one kind. This is another kind and needs its own evidence. Neither her credit nor this claim spreads to the rest.'}]}}}},2996,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'principles-of-psychology_1':{pictureCaption:'Starting this great wheel took real work. Now its own weight keeps it turning, in the groove it has always run in.',basics:{title:'Habits keep you going',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'EVERY MORNING',text:'You brush your teeth every morning. You never stop to decide to do it. Even on a grumpy, sleepy day, it just happens.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A habit is like a big, heavy wheel. It is hard to get going. But once it turns, it keeps you going on days you don\u2019t feel like trying.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'This giant wheel took hard work to start. Now its own weight keeps it turning. A habit works the same way.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where the idea comes from',text:'William James was an American thinker. In 1890 he wrote one of the first big books on how the mind works. He said habit is like a flywheel. That is a heavy wheel in a machine. Once it spins, it keeps on spinning, even when nothing pushes it.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'SATURDAY FOOTBALL',text:'When you joined the team, getting up early on Saturday was hard. You had to talk yourself into it. Now you just get up and go. The pushing was done weeks ago. The wheel does the rest.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Habits only keep you doing good things.',reality:'A habit keeps you going in whatever way it was set. Watching videos in bed every night is a wheel too. So it is worth checking which wheels you have running.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Ana has read before bed every night for a year. Tonight she is tired, but she reads anyway. What keeps her going?',options:['The habit she built, which now runs by itself','A huge burst of willpower just for tonight','Someone at home making her read the book'],answer:0,why:'The hard work was at the start. Now the habit carries her, even on a tired night.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'HOW A FLYWHEEL WORKS',text:'A flywheel is the heavy wheel in an old engine. It soaks up energy while the engine is pushing, and spends it carrying the machine through the moments when nothing is pushing. It is slow to start and slow to stop.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Habit is the flywheel of a life. Momentum built while you were motivated carries you through the long stretches when you are not, in whatever direction it was first set.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Not grit, momentum',text:'Most days, motivation is simply absent. William James\u2019s examples are the fisherman who keeps going to sea through the winter and the miner who keeps going down into the dark. He is not praising their grit. Grit is not what is working. The decision was made long ago, and the wheel is still turning.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHERE EFFORT PAYS',left:{title:'Effort spent early',items:['Goes into the habit while it forms','Buys years you won\u2019t have to decide','Paid once']},right:{title:'Effort spent later',items:['Goes into arguing with yourself daily','Buys one day at a time','Runs out']},note:'The same effort, spent at different times, buys very different amounts.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Starting this wheel took real work. Keeping it turning takes very little. And the wheel does not care which way it spins.'},{kind:'try',text:'List what you do in a normal week. Mark each item: would you choose this again today, or are you only keeping it going?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A friend has run three mornings a week for five years and says it takes no willpower now. What does James\u2019s idea explain?',options:['Early effort built momentum that now carries the routine','She is simply a more disciplined person than most','Running has become fun, so effort is no longer needed','Willpower grows with age, so it costs her less now'],answer:0,why:'The willpower was spent while the habit was forming. What runs it now is the flywheel.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'HARVARD, 1890',text:'William James taught at Harvard. In 1890, after about twelve years of work, he published *The Principles of Psychology*, a two-volume book that helped found the subject. Its chapter on habit argues that habits are physical: repetition reshapes the nervous system, so the body comes to do on its own what once took a decision.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Habit is the flywheel of society. It stores the push of earlier decisions and keeps lives turning in their set direction long after anyone is deciding. The mechanism is neutral. The direction is not.'},{kind:'term',word:'Flywheel',meaning:'A heavy wheel in an engine that stores energy, so the machine keeps turning through the parts of each cycle where nothing is driving it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the fisherman shows',text:'James\u2019s examples are the fisherman at sea through the winter and the miner in the dark. He is not admiring their grit. He is observing that grit is not what is operating. He adds that habit holds each of us to the lines of our upbringing or our early choice, which is praise and warning at once.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE MOMENTUM IS STORED',nodes:['Motivation present: you choose','The action is repeated','Repetition is stored as habit','Habit carries you when motivation is gone'],note:'Effort at the first step buys years at the last. Effort spent arguing with yourself daily buys one day.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The wheel turns because it was started, not because anyone is choosing it now. Its rim has worn into a single groove.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The metaphor can make a constrained life look chosen. A fisherman may stay at sea because there is no other work, not because of momentum. James himself saw habit as what keeps people in the social station they were born to, and that observation slides easily into treating the station as natural. Momentum explains persistence. It does not justify it, and habits can still be changed, at a cost.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which of your weekly routines would you choose again today, and which are you only maintaining?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A team follows a weekly process that nobody remembers designing. Which reading is closest to James?',options:['It runs on old momentum, and should be checked against today\u2019s aims','It must be sound, since it has lasted so long without complaint','The team lacks the willpower to replace it with something better','Anything that has survived this long has earned exemption from review'],answer:0,why:'Habit keeps things turning whether or not the direction is still right. Lasting is evidence of momentum, not of merit.'}]}},'principles-of-psychology_2':{pictureCaption:'Hands have let a ball of string fall. It has rolled across the floor, unwinding as it goes: one slip undoes many careful turns.',bridge:'Idea 1 showed how habit carries you once it is built. This one is about the fragile weeks while it is being built.',basics:{title:'No skipping days at the start',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'DAY THREE',prompt:'You have just started practising guitar every day. On day three you are tired. Does skipping one day matter?',options:['No, one day is nothing','Yes, more than it seems'],answer:1,reveal:'At the start, a new habit is weak. One skip undoes more than one day of practice builds. And it teaches you that skipping is allowed.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When you start a new habit, don\u2019t skip a single day until it is strong. Early on, one skip undoes more than one good day builds.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE WINDING STRING',picture:!0,text:'Picture winding string into a neat ball. It takes lots of slow turns. Then you drop it. It rolls across the floor and unwinds. One slip undoes many turns. William James, who wrote a big book about the mind in 1890, used this picture for new habits.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Only at the start',text:'This rule is not for ever. Once a habit is strong, missing a day is just missing a day. Nothing unwinds. The strict part is only for the first few weeks.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Starting a new habit',steps:['Pick one small thing to do each day.','Do it every day. No skips.','On a bad day, do a tiny bit. It still counts.','Keep going until it feels normal.']},{kind:'question',prompt:'Sam has walked the dog every evening for one week. Tonight his friends ask him to play. What does James\u2019s rule say?',options:['Walk the dog first, as the habit is still new','Skip it tonight, because one day won\u2019t matter','Walk the dog only on days he feels like it'],answer:0,why:'A week-old habit is still weak. This is when a skip costs the most.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'JUST THIS ONCE',belief:'Missing one day of a new habit costs you one day.',reality:'William James argued the opposite for the early weeks. A lapse then undoes more than a successful repetition adds, which is why \u201cjust this once\u201d is dearer than it feels.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Allow no exceptions while a new habit is forming. In that window a lapse undoes more than a repetition builds, so strictness is cheapest exactly when it feels least necessary.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The ball of string',picture:!0,text:'James borrowed an image from the Scottish psychologist Alexander Bain. Winding a ball of string takes many careful turns. Drop it once, and it unrolls further than many turns will win back. That lopsidedness is the whole argument.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'HOW LONG THE RULE LASTS',low:'Week one: a lapse is expensive',high:'Year two: a lapse is one missed day',note:'The strictness is front-loaded and temporary. James applies it only until the habit is securely rooted.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE EARLY TRAIN',text:'You have started writing for twenty minutes before work. On day four the train comes early and the plan collapses. Instead of skipping, you write for five minutes on the platform. The habit gets its repetition, and \u201ctoday is different\u201d never becomes a rule.'},{kind:'try',text:'For your next new habit, decide in advance the smallest version that still counts, so that a bad day in the first weeks is never a day off.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Two weeks into a rule of no phone in bed, a friend says one night off won\u2019t hurt. Which reply fits James?',options:['Keep the rule for now: early lapses cost more than they look','Agree: one night out of fourteen is too small to matter','Take the night off, so the rule does not burn you out','Drop the rule, since needing it shows it isn\u2019t working'],answer:0,why:'Two weeks in, the habit is still forming. That is exactly when James says an exception is most expensive.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE MAXIMS',text:'Near the end of the habit chapter, James sets out practical maxims, drawn partly from the Scottish psychologist Alexander Bain. The first is to begin a new habit with as strong and decided a start as you can. The second is to allow no exception until the new habit is securely rooted.'},{kind:'idea',text:'While a habit is forming, each exception costs more than a repetition earns. So allow none until it is rooted, then relax. The strictness is temporary and front-loaded.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'BAIN\u2019S BALL OF STRING',picture:!0,text:'Each lapse is like dropping a ball of string you are carefully winding. One slip, and it unrolls further than many careful turns will recover. The image is about asymmetry: losing ground is far quicker than gaining it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the lapse costs more',text:'The new habit is competing with an old tendency that has a head start. Every win for the old tendency strengthens it, and early on the new one has little stored up to set against it. Hence the instruction James takes from Bain: in this phase, never lose a battle.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'While forming',items:['A lapse unwinds many repetitions','\u201cJust this once\u201d sets a precedent','Strictness is cheap']},right:{title:'Once rooted',items:['A lapse is one missed day','Nothing unwinds','Strictness is unnecessary']}},{kind:'limits',text:'Later research on people forming everyday habits is kinder. Missing a single day seemed to do little lasting harm, and habits often took longer to settle than people expect. Rigid all-or-nothing rules can also backfire: when the streak breaks, people abandon the whole effort. What survives is the case for extra care at the start, not zero tolerance.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which of your new habits is still in the window where an exception costs the most?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'Someone argues that studies show a missed day barely matters, so James was simply wrong. What is the fairest reply?',options:['The absolute rule overreaches, but the case for extra care early on survives','James was right, so the studies must have measured the wrong thing','James meant the rule for life, so studies of early weeks are irrelevant','The rule only ever applied to breaking bad habits, never to building good ones'],answer:0,why:'Later evidence softens the rule without reversing its shape. Early lapses still deserve more caution than late ones, even if one miss is not fatal.'}]}},'principles-of-psychology_3':{pictureCaption:'One beam, one object lit. The shelves are full, but they stay in the dark. Whatever the beam lands on is what you get.',bridge:'Ideas 1 and 2 were about habit, which runs without needing your attention. This one asks what attention is, and what it costs.',basics:{title:'Paying attention means picking one thing',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'HOMEWORK TIME',text:'You sit down to do your maths. Your phone buzzes. The TV is on next door. The dog wants to play. Twenty minutes later, the maths is still not done.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Paying attention means picking one thing out of many. When you pick one thing, you miss the others. If you don\u2019t pick, the loudest thing picks for you.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A torch in a dark room lights up one thing. The rest is still there, in the dark. Your attention works like that beam.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Who worked this out',text:'William James wrote one of the first big books about the mind, in 1890. He said we all know what attention is. Then he made it exact. The mind grabs one thing, clearly, out of several it could have grabbed.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The real skill',text:'Your mind will wander. That is normal. James thought the skill that matters is bringing it back, again and again. He said a school that could train this would be the best school of all.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before your homework, say out loud the one thing you will pay attention to. Each time your mind drifts, bring it back. Each return is practice.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Mia is reading. Three times her mind drifts to a game, and three times she goes back to the book. What would James say?',options:['She is practising the most useful part of attention','She has failed to focus and should give up','She should read only when her mind is calm'],answer:0,why:'Drifting is normal. Coming back, again and again, is the skill James cared about.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A MORNING OF SMALL THINGS',prompt:'By lunch you have answered messages, skimmed the news and joined two calls. You did nothing you planned. What happened?',options:['You lacked focus','You paid close attention to things others chose'],answer:1,reveal:'You attended, clearly and in turn, to forty things. You just did not choose any of them. That is not a failure of focus. It is a run of choices made for you.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Attention is the mind taking hold of one thing out of several possible ones. Every choice of object costs all the others, so distraction is a series of choices left to whatever was loudest.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why every choice costs',text:'William James defined attention as the mind seizing, clearly and vividly, one of several things or lines of thought that seem open to it at once. The cost is inside the definition. To hold one thing is to let the rest go. Every minute of attention is bought with everything you did not attend to.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW A MORNING GOES',nodes:['No object chosen in advance','The loudest thing takes hold','Then the next loud thing','The day spent on others\u2019 choices'],note:'Trying harder does not break this chain. Choosing first does.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The beam is your attention. Point it, and one thing appears. Leave it unpointed, and something else will move it for you.'},{kind:'try',text:'Tomorrow, choose your one object of attention before you open anything. Each time you notice you have been handed a different one, bring it back. The return is the skill.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You want to finish a report today. Which plan best applies James?',options:['Pick the report before opening anything, and keep returning to it','Try harder to concentrate each time you feel distracted','Keep email open so nothing urgent slips past while you write','Wait for a quiet stretch of the day with no interruptions'],answer:0,why:'James\u2019s remedy is to choose the object deliberately and early, then practise the return, rather than rely on effort or luck.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE CHAPTER ON ATTENTION',text:'In his chapter on attention, James says that everyone already knows what it is. Then he pins it down: the mind seizing, clearly and vividly, one of several things or lines of thought that seem open to it at once. Focusing on one thing means withdrawing from others.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Attention is selection. Everything attended to is bought with everything not attended to, so distraction is not an absence of focus but an unmanaged series of choices.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One beam, one object. The room is full, but selection means the rest goes dark. That is the price of seeing anything clearly.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Attention returns; it does not hold',text:'James also observed that voluntary attention to an unchanging object cannot be held for more than a few seconds. What we call sustained attention is repeated return to a topic that keeps developing. So the faculty that matters is bringing a wandering attention back, over and over.'},{kind:'keyline',text:'The skill is not the steady gaze. It is the return.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The education he wanted',text:'He called that faculty of return the root of judgement, character and will, and said an education that improved it would be the best education there is. He also admitted the ideal was easier to state than to provide. Modern attention training, such as some meditation practice, works on exactly this return.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Distraction as failure',items:['You lacked focus','Remedy: try harder','The person is at fault']},right:{title:'Distraction as choosing',items:['Something else chose for you','Remedy: choose early, practise return','The setup is at fault']}},{kind:'limits',text:'Selection has a cost, but so does too much of it. A mind that lets nothing else in misses the unexpected: the smell of smoke, the stray thought that solves the problem. Some work, such as caring for a small child, is interrupted by design, and choosing early cannot change that. The point is to own more of the choices, not to fence out the world.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISREADING',prompt:'A manager reads James and bans all interruptions so staff can hold one focus for hours. What has the manager missed?',options:['That attention returns rather than holds; the return is what trains','Nothing: banning interruptions is exactly the remedy James proposed','James believed attention could not be trained or improved at all','James thought only interesting work deserved anyone\u2019s attention'],answer:0,why:'For James, attention to one unchanging object lasts seconds. Long focus is many returns, and that is what can be trained.'}]}},'principles-of-psychology_4':{pictureCaption:'The river runs on without joints. The chain on the bank is the old picture of the mind: neat links that cannot describe water.',bridge:'Idea 3 described choosing one object of thought. This one describes what you are choosing from: a stream, not a row of separate thoughts.',basics:{title:'Thoughts flow like a river',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'BEADS OR A RIVER?',belief:'Thoughts come one at a time, like beads on a string.',reality:'William James, who wrote a famous book about the mind, said thoughts flow like a river. Each one melts into the next. The bits in between are part of thinking too.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Your mind is not a row of separate thoughts. It is a stream that keeps on flowing. That is why a break in the flow costs more than you think.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The river keeps flowing. The chain on the bank is made of separate links. James said thinking is like the river, not the chain.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'ON THE WAY HOME',text:'You think about lunch. Then the smell of chips. Then a beach trip last summer. Then your cousin. Where did one thought stop and the next one start? You can\u2019t say. It just flowed.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why being stopped hurts',text:'Say you are deep in a puzzle and someone talks to you. You don\u2019t just pause, like stopping a film. The flow breaks. When you go back, it takes a while to get going again.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you do homework, put everything else away for half an hour. Notice the moment the flow gets going.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Leo builds a model for an hour with no breaks. The next day he builds for an hour but stops five times to chat. Why does he get more done the first day?',options:['His flow of thinking was never broken','He was just luckier on the first day','The breaks gave him extra energy'],answer:0,why:'Each stop broke the stream. Every time, it had to start flowing again.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE MOMENT YOU DECIDED',text:'Try to remember the exact moment you decided to leave a job, or to drift from a friend. Most people cannot find it. There was no single moment. There was a long run of feelings and half-thoughts that, one day, had become a decision.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Thought flows as a stream, not a chain of separate ideas. The transitions carry meaning, so you rarely find one jointed moment where a thought began, and breaking the flow costs more than a pause.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'The chain picture',items:['Separate ideas, linked together','Transitions are empty joints','Interrupt, then resume at the same link']},right:{title:'The stream picture',items:['One continuous flow','Transitions carry content','Interrupt, and the current must rebuild']}},{kind:'picture',caption:'William James set the river against the chain. Laid out link by link, thought looks tidy, and misses the part that moves.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What this costs a working day',text:'If thinking is a current, an interruption is not a pause after which you pick up the same link. The current has to re-form. That helps explain something many people notice: four unbroken hours can produce work that eight broken ones do not. The hours were not the input. The continuity was.'},{kind:'try',text:'When you cannot find the moment a decision was made, stop looking for the moment. Ask instead what had been accumulating.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You cannot pinpoint when you stopped enjoying a hobby. What does James\u2019s picture of the mind suggest?',options:['The change built up in the flow, without one jointed moment','There must have been a moment you have since forgotten','Your memory of that whole period is simply unreliable','You probably never enjoyed the hobby in the first place'],answer:0,why:'In a stream, change accumulates through transitions. Looking for a single link misreads how thought moves.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'WHAT HE WAS ARGUING AGAINST',belief:'The mind is a chain of simple ideas, each joined to the next by association.',reality:'That was the reigning picture, inherited from British philosophers such as Hume. James answered that nothing in experience is jointed like that. Consciousness never shows up to itself in separate pieces.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Consciousness is a stream, not a chain. Thoughts flow, with transitions and half-formed edges that are part of the content, and there are no atoms of experience to be found.'},{kind:'term',word:'The fringe',meaning:'James\u2019s word for the faint halo around each thought: a sense of where it is heading and what it relates to. It is felt rather than seen, and the chain picture had no place for it.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE NAME YOU CAN\u2019T RECALL',text:'James uses a forgotten name as a test. The gap where the name should be is not empty. It is active: wrong names are rejected the moment they come up. A gap that can tell right from wrong has content, and no chain of discrete ideas can account for it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the metaphor mattered',text:'Many psychologists of the time were looking for the basic units of experience, much as a chemist looks for elements. James argued that this search was misdirected: the units were never there, only the flow. The image changed what psychology thought it was describing.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The river against the chain. The links are neat and countable, and that is exactly what makes them wrong for water.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The stream is a description of how experience feels, not a theory of how the brain produces it. Psychology later made real progress by breaking experience into measurable parts anyway. And the popular consequence, that interruptions are costly because they break the current, is an extension: James was describing consciousness, not studying office work.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A journaling app asks you to log every thought as a separate entry with a timestamp. What would James object to?',options:['It keeps the stopping points and loses the transitions between them','Thoughts move far too quickly to be written down with any accuracy','Logging every thought makes people too absorbed in themselves','Thoughts should be grouped by topic rather than by time'],answer:0,why:'For James the transitions are part of the content. A log of separate entries keeps the links and throws away the stream.'}]}},'principles-of-psychology_5':{pictureCaption:'The hands make the usual breakfast without supervision. The head is free to turn to the window. Routine bought that freedom.',bridge:'Back to habit, the subject of ideas 1 and 2, from another side: what routine frees up, and why every action trains you.',basics:{title:'Let habits do the boring jobs',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'BEFORE A TEST',prompt:'Which morning leaves you more ready for a big test?',options:['You decide everything: what to wear, eat and pack','Those things are already the same every day'],answer:1,reveal:'Every small choice uses up a bit of your attention. If the boring jobs are habits, your mind gets to the test fresh.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Every choice uses a little of your attention. Turn the small jobs that are the same each day into habits. Then you save your attention for things that need it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'These hands make the same breakfast as always. They don\u2019t need watching. So the cook can look out at the sunrise.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Your body learns anyway',text:'William James wrote a famous book about the mind in 1890. He said your brain and nerves learn from whatever you repeat. Do a thing many times and it gets easier, good or bad. So teach them on purpose.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE SCHOOL BAG',text:'You pack your bag the night before, in the same order every time. After a few weeks you don\u2019t think about it at all. Your mind is free for the match, or the story you are writing.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one small thing to make the same every day this week. The same time for packing your bag, or the same place for your shoes.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Which of these is best to turn into a habit?',options:['Where you put your shoes each day','What to write in a new story','How to cheer up a sad friend'],answer:0,why:'Shoes are the same job every day. The other two need fresh thinking, and habits save room for that.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'TEN IN THE MORNING',text:'By ten o\u2019clock you are tired, and you have done nothing yet except decide things: what to wear, which train, what to eat, which email first, whether to reply now. None was hard. All of them took something.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Make your nervous system your ally. It will act automatically whatever you do, so the only question is what you have trained it to do. Hand routine to habit, and attention is left for what needs it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'There is no neutral day',text:'William James saw habit as physical: repetition changes the pathway. That makes every repetition a deposit, including the ones you never meant to make. No day leaves the system as it was. The only choice is between deposits you chose and deposits you drifted into.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE RESOLUTION THAT BACKFIRES',text:'On Sunday you resolve to start running. Monday passes with no run. You have not merely failed to build a habit. You have practised resolving without acting, and taught yourself that deciding and doing are unrelated. James\u2019s rule is to act on a resolution at the very first chance.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The hands work from habit while the eyes are on the dawn. Routine is not the enemy of a thoughtful life. It is the budget for one.'},{kind:'try',text:'This week, automate one recurring daily decision: the same breakfast, the same time, the same route. Notice what your attention does instead.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'On a train, you decide to start writing a book \u201cproperly, next month\u201d. What does James suggest?',options:['Write a first paragraph now, at the first chance to act','Wait until next month, when there is time to do it well','Plan the whole outline first so that starting is easier','Renew the resolution each day until the urge is strong'],answer:0,why:'A resolution with no action attached trains the gap between deciding and doing. The first chance to act is the one to take.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'HIS PORTRAIT OF INDECISION',text:'James describes a person in whom nothing is habitual but indecision: every cup, every bedtime, every start of work is a fresh deliberation. Half his time goes on deciding, or regretting, things that should be automatic. James can think of few people more miserable.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The nervous system will act automatically either way, so train it on purpose. Every repetition leaves a trace, chosen or not, and routine frees the mind for work that needs it.'},{kind:'term',word:'Plasticity',meaning:'James\u2019s word for matter weak enough to yield to an influence but strong enough not to yield all at once. He held that the nervous system is plastic in this sense, which is why habits form.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE HABIT MOST PEOPLE BUILD',nodes:['A resolution is made','No action follows','Deciding and doing come apart','The gap itself becomes habit'],loop:!0,note:'Hence his maxim: act on every resolution at the first possible opportunity.'},{kind:'explain',title:'His fourth maxim',text:'James adds a curious one: keep the capacity for effort alive with a little unnecessary exercise every day, doing something hard simply because you would rather not. He compares it to insurance on a house. It costs a little now and pays out when a real test comes.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The routine runs without watching, which leaves the mind its view of the window. That is the trade James was arguing for.'},{kind:'limits',text:'James had no way to see pathways change. His physiology was an educated guess, and his channels worn by water are a metaphor. Later science supports the broad point that repetition reshapes the brain, not his details. The popular claim that every decision drains a fixed store of willpower has proved hard to repeat in studies. And what is automated is no longer examined: idea 1\u2019s warning.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which daily decision are you still making fresh that should have been handed to habit long ago?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these misuses James\u2019s advice?',options:['Putting every work decision on autopilot, including ones that change week to week','Wearing much the same clothes each weekday to save small decisions','Acting on a resolution the same day you make it','Fixing a time for exercise so it never has to be decided'],answer:0,why:'Automation is for what genuinely repeats. A decision whose right answer changes needs the attention that routine was meant to free.'}]}},'principles-of-psychology_6':{pictureCaption:'No single coat is heavy. Hung one on another, they bend the hook. Unfinished things add up the same way.',bridge:'Idea 5 freed attention by handing decisions to habit. This one finds where attention leaks away: things left unfinished.',basics:{title:'Unfinished things make you tired',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'A HEAVY SATURDAY',text:'It is Saturday. Nothing hard is going on. But you feel heavy. You still owe your friend a text. Your library book is late. You still haven\u2019t told your mum you broke a mug.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Jobs you haven\u2019t done keep tugging at your mind, even when you are not doing them. Lots of small jobs left undone can tire you more than one big job that is done.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Each coat on its own is light. Piled up, they bend the hook. Small unfinished things pile up on you like that.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why small things feel so heavy',text:'This idea is often linked to William James, who studied the mind long ago. Each job left undone sits at the back of your mind. Now and then it pops up: *I still need to\u2026* Each pop costs you a bit. And none of them goes away by itself.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'PEBBLES IN YOUR BAG',text:'Think of each job left undone as a pebble in your school bag. One pebble is nothing. Twenty pebbles make your back ache, even when you sit still.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Lighten the load',steps:['Write down each job left undone, even tiny ones.','Pick the three quickest.','Do them today.','See how you feel when they are done.']},{kind:'question',prompt:'Zoe feels tired but has done nothing hard all week. Which will most likely help her feel lighter?',options:['Answering a message she has put off for days','Doing extra homework she has already finished','Writing a longer list of things to do one day'],answer:0,why:'Answering the message closes something that was tugging at her. Each closed thing stops costing her.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A CRUSHING WEEK',prompt:'Last week felt crushing, though nothing in it was hard. What best explains that?',options:['You were secretly working too hard','Too many small things were left open','You simply needed more sleep'],answer:1,reveal:'Count the open loops, not the tasks: the unanswered message, the decision put off, the conversation not yet had. None is hard. Each keeps a claim on you.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Mental fatigue tracks unfinished business more than hard work. Each unresolved thing keeps a claim on your attention and charges for it continuously, whether or not you are working on it.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO KINDS OF DAY',left:{title:'A hard day, finished',items:['Demanding, complicated tasks','Each one completed and closed','You end exhausted, but finished']},right:{title:'An easy day, left open',items:['Trivial, uncomplicated tasks','Each one put off for another day','You end flattened']},note:'The second day is the one that flattens you. The observation is often credited to William James, though it is not in this book.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What sets the weight',text:'Not difficulty. The weight of an open loop grows with how long it has been open and how often it resurfaces. Each is cheap on its own, and none closes by itself. A week of them adds up to a load that has nothing to do with how hard anything was.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why tiny closures feel huge',text:'Closing a trivial thing can bring relief out of all proportion to its size. That looks irrational, and is not. You did not finish a small task. You stopped paying a standing charge.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'No single coat bends the hook. It is the pile, and how long it has hung there. Take three off and the strain eases.'},{kind:'try',text:'List everything currently unresolved, however small. Close the three cheapest today and notice the change.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Your list holds one hard report and a dozen tiny replies you have put off for weeks. You feel drained. What fits the idea?',options:['Clear several of the tiny, long-open replies first','Start the report, since hard work is what tires you','Move the tiny replies on to next month\u2019s list','Ignore the replies, as they cost nothing until started'],answer:0,why:'The long-open replies are charging you every day. Closing them removes a cost the report never had.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHAT FATIGUE TRACKS',text:'An observation often credited to James, though not one made in the *Principles*, is that mental fatigue does not follow the difficulty of the work. A day of hard but finished tasks leaves you tired; a day of easy unfinished ones leaves you flattened. The account is about attention: an unresolved thing keeps a claim on you, and the claim costs something continuously.'},{kind:'idea',text:'An unresolved thing keeps drawing on attention whether or not you are working on it. Fatigue therefore tracks the number and age of open loops more than the difficulty of any task.'},{kind:'term',word:'Open loop',meaning:'A later phrase, not James\u2019s: any commitment, to yourself or anyone else, that is not yet finished, decided or deliberately dropped.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE STANDING CHARGE',nodes:['Something is left unresolved','It resurfaces, unasked','Each return draws on attention','It is still unresolved'],loop:!0,note:'Nothing inside the loop closes it. Only a decision from outside does.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Weight is not size',picture:!0,text:'The weight of an open loop is set by how long it has been open and how often it comes back, not by how hard it is. Many light things, hung together long enough, outweigh one heavy one. So closing a trivial one brings relief out of all proportion to its size.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Experiments on unfinished tasks came later, beginning with Bluma Zeigarnik in the 1920s, and their results have been mixed. Not every loop should be closed fast either: some decisions deserve to stay open until you know more. A written plan for when you will deal with something may quiet its claim without forcing a hasty answer.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which open loop of yours is oldest? What would closing it, or deciding to drop it, actually take?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HONEST OBJECTION',prompt:'Someone concludes: \u201cSo I should close every open question as fast as I can.\u201d What is the best reply?',options:['Some loops should stay open; a clear plan can stop the drain without a rushed decision','Yes, since any decision is better than leaving something open','No, open loops cost nothing until you start thinking about them','No, because only difficult tasks cause real mental fatigue'],answer:0,why:'The cost comes from the claim on attention, not from openness itself. Deciding, scheduling or deliberately dropping can all end the claim.'}]}},'principles-of-psychology_7':{pictureCaption:'The top number stays. The cloth wipes away the marks under the line. Shrink the bottom of a fraction and it grows.',bridge:'Idea 6 was about the claims unfinished tasks keep on you. This one is about the claims you make on yourself.',basics:{title:'Two ways to feel better about yourself',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE SPELLING TEST',prompt:'Two friends both get 7 out of 10 in a spelling test. One is pleased. One is upset. Why?',options:['One of them studied harder','They expected different scores','The test was unfair on one'],answer:1,reveal:'One hoped for 6. The other told everyone she would get 10. Same score. What differs is what each expected.'},{kind:'idea',text:'How you feel about yourself depends on two things: what you do, and what you claim you should do. Doing more helps. So does letting go of a claim you don\u2019t need.'},{kind:'explain',title:'A fraction about you',text:'William James wrote a famous book about the mind in 1890. He wrote this idea as a fraction. On top is what you manage. Underneath is what you claim. A fraction gets bigger if the top grows. It also gets bigger if the bottom shrinks.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The top number stays the same. The cloth rubs out marks below the line. The bottom shrinks, so the fraction grows.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE PIANO',text:'You tell everyone you play the piano. But you haven\u2019t practised in months, and it nags at you. There are two honest fixes. Practise again. Or say, *I used to play*, and feel lighter.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Letting go of a claim is the same as giving up.',reality:'Nobody can be good at everything. James said he would hate it if others knew more about his own subject. But he did not mind knowing no ancient Greek. He had never claimed it.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Raj says he is great at chess, but he never plays, and it bothers him. What are his honest choices?',options:['Play again, or stop saying he is great at chess','Keep saying it and hope nobody asks for a game','Claim to be great at lots more games as well'],answer:0,why:'Raj can grow the top by playing, or shrink the bottom by dropping the claim. The claim without the playing is what hurts.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'TWO YEARS OF GUILT',text:'A guitar has stood in the corner for two years. You still call yourself someone who plays. Every time you walk past it there is a small stab of guilt. You have not played, and you have not stopped claiming to.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Self-esteem is a fraction: success divided by pretensions. Lowering what you claim works as well as raising what you achieve, so dropping a pretension can relieve as much as fulfilling it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Arithmetic, not consolation',text:'William James set it out as a fraction, and a fraction moves in both directions equally. The guitar leaves two honest exits: practise, or stop claiming to be someone who plays. The guilt comes from the third position, holding the claim without the hours. It costs every day and returns nothing.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Raise the top',items:['Schedule the practice','Do the hours','The claim becomes true']},right:{title:'Lower the bottom',items:['Stop making the claim','Put the guitar away','The guilt ends']},note:'Both raise the fraction. Most people only ever try the left.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The number on top stays put. The cloth rubs out the long row of marks below the line. The fraction grows with nothing added.'},{kind:'try',text:'Name one thing you claim to be that you are not currently doing. This week, either schedule it or stop claiming it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You have called yourself a runner for three years but have not run since spring, and it nags at you. What does James\u2019s fraction recommend?',options:['Either book runs this week or stop calling yourself a runner','Keep the label and promise yourself you will start again soon','Buy new running kit so the label feels more deserved','Raise the stakes by entering a marathon to force yourself'],answer:0,why:'Either exit ends the nagging. Keeping the claim without the running, or raising the claim, leaves the bottom of the fraction doing damage.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE SECOND-BEST BOXER',text:'James describes a man who is the second-best boxer, or rower, in the world, and is ashamed. Beating everyone on earth but one counts for nothing, because he has staked himself on beating that one. James adds that he himself, staked on psychology, is mortified when others know more of it, yet content to know no Greek.'},{kind:'idea',text:'James writes self-esteem as success over pretensions. Since its value depends equally on both, giving up a pretension raises it as surely as achieving one.'},{kind:'term',word:'Pretension',meaning:'In James\u2019s sense, not snobbery. It is what you have staked yourself on: the claims about who you are that your successes and failures are measured against.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The relief he noticed',text:'James remarks how light people feel on the day they give up striving to be young, or slender, and how seldom they think of doing it. Dropping a claim feels like losing, so people keep paying for claims they no longer act on, when the fraction says that letting go is a gain.'},{kind:'keyline',text:'There are two ways to raise a fraction. Most people only ever try one.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Nothing is added on top. The cloth works below the line, and the fraction grows anyway.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The fraction is a figure of speech, not a measurement. Pushed too far it licenses dropping anything hard, and some pretensions are what pull a person to grow. Others are obligations rather than vanities: a parent cannot lower the claim to be careful with a child. The arithmetic fits optional claims. For duties, only the top of the fraction is open.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these would James\u2019s fraction not justify?',options:['A parent dropping the aim of being patient with their child because it is hard','Someone giving up the claim to fluency in a French they never study','A writer who no longer writes poems ceasing to call herself a poet','A retired sprinter letting go of being the fastest in his club'],answer:0,why:'Lowering the denominator is honest for optional claims. A duty to someone else is not a pretension you are free to drop.'}]}},'principles-of-psychology_8':{pictureCaption:'A chair left by the wall, where someone sat and worried. Now they walk to the door, shoulders back. The brave feeling often follows.',bridge:'Like idea 5, this works through the body rather than by arguing with yourself.',basics:{title:'Move your body, change your feeling',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'WHICH COMES FIRST?',belief:'First you feel scared, and then your body reacts.',reality:'William James, who wrote a famous book about the mind, thought it often works the other way. Your body reacts first: your heart races, your legs run. Feeling scared is noticing that.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Your body is a big part of how you feel. You can\u2019t just decide to feel brave. But you can stand tall, breathe slowly and move, and the feeling often follows.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'BEFORE THE SCHOOL PLAY',picture:!0,text:'You wait backstage. You sit hunched over, thinking of everything that could go wrong. Your heart thumps. Now try something else. Stand up. Roll your shoulders back. Walk a little. Breathe out slowly. The thumping often calms down.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why this helps',text:'Sitting and worrying keeps your body tense. Then the tense body feels like proof that you should worry. You go round in a circle. Moving breaks the circle from the outside. Scientists still argue about how far this goes, but it is worth a try.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Five minutes before something scary',steps:['Stand up.','Pull your shoulders back.','Breathe out slowly, three times.','Say your first line out loud.']},{kind:'question',prompt:'Jo has a race soon and feels jittery. Which uses James\u2019s tip?',options:['Warm up, stand tall and breathe slowly','Sit still and think hard about losing','Tell herself firmly to stop being nervous'],answer:0,why:'She can\u2019t order the feeling to change. She can change what her body is doing, and the feeling tends to follow.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'FIVE MINUTES BEFORE',prompt:'You have five minutes before a call you are dreading. Which helps more?',options:['Sit still and rehearse what could go wrong','Stand up, walk and talk out loud'],answer:1,reveal:'Sitting and rehearsing leaves the body as braced as it was. Walking and talking change the body, and the feeling tends to follow.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The body\u2019s state is not just a result of emotion but a large part of it. You cannot decide to feel capable, but you can change what your body is doing, and the feeling follows the posture more readily than the reverse.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE SELF-CONFIRMING LOOP',nodes:['Sit still and rehearse the dread','The body stays braced','Bracing feels like evidence','More dread'],loop:!0,note:'Argument never reaches the body, and the body is where this loop is fed.'},{kind:'explain',title:'James\u2019s reversal',text:'Common sense says we see a bear, feel afraid, then run. William James argued that the bodily response follows the perception directly, and the feeling is our sense of that response. Whatever one makes of that strong version, the practical advice follows: to change a feeling, change the body.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where you have control',text:'You cannot order yourself to feel calm. You can stand, walk, slow your breathing, and speak as the confident version of you would. Those are inputs to the feeling rather than outputs of it, and they are directly in your hands.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'UP FROM THE CHAIR',caption:'The chair where the waiting was done is pushed back against the wall. Shoulders back, the figure walks to the closed door. Nothing was argued with; the body moved first.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before your next difficult call, spend the ten minutes walking and speaking aloud rather than sitting and thinking.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Before an interview you feel shaky and keep telling yourself to calm down. Which change fits James?',options:['Walk round the block, breathing slowly and saying your opening aloud','Reread your notes until you finally feel ready to go in','Sit completely still so the shaking has a chance to stop','Remind yourself firmly that nerves are irrational'],answer:0,why:'Telling yourself to calm down argues with the feeling. Walking and breathing change what feeds it.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'1884, THEN 1890',text:'James first set out this theory in an essay in 1884, and gave it a chapter in the *Principles*. The Danish physician Carl Lange reached a similar view at about the same time, so it is known as the James\u2013Lange theory.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The bodily change is not the consequence of emotion but much of its substance: the feeling is our perception of the body\u2019s response. So the lever on a feeling is what the body is doing.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'THE ORDER REVERSED',left:{title:'Common sense',items:['See the bear','Feel afraid','Run']},right:{title:'James',items:['See the bear','The body responds: run, tremble','Fear is the feeling of that response']}},{kind:'explain',title:'The advice he drew',text:'James drew practical rules from it himself. Refuse to act out a passion, and it tends to fade. To cultivate a mood you lack, adopt its outward signs: sit up, smooth the brow, speak in a brighter voice. He expected the feeling to follow, more often than not.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'THE OUTWARD SIGNS FIRST',caption:'The chair is left behind; the walk to the door is upright, shoulders back. The outward signs of nerve come first, on the bet that the feeling will catch up.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The strong version did not survive. In the 1920s the physiologist Walter Cannon objected that quite different emotions share much the same bodily changes, and that the body responds too slowly to explain how fast feelings arrive. Later studies of posture and facial expression found effects on mood that are small and inconsistent, and some popular claims failed to repeat. Acting cheerful can also become a way of burying grief.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Before the next thing you dread, what will your body be doing in the ten minutes beforehand?'},{kind:'recap',points:['For James, a feeling is largely the sensing of a bodily change.','The strong theory fell; a weaker link between body and mood remains.','Change the body\u2019s inputs, but never to bury a real feeling.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'A friend says: \u201cCannon disproved James, so the posture advice is nonsense.\u201d What is the fairest reply?',options:['The strong theory fell, but the body still nudges mood a little','Cannon was mistaken, and James\u2019s theory still stands in full today','The advice never depended on the body, so the theory is beside the point','Posture fully determines emotion, which makes the objection irrelevant'],answer:0,why:'Refuting the claim that feeling is nothing but bodily change does not show the body has no influence. The advice needs only the weaker link.'}]}},'principles-of-psychology_9':{pictureCaption:'One nail holds one thread: lose it and the fact is gone. The other can be found by any of twenty.',bridge:'Idea 3 found that what attention trains is the return. Here James says much the same of memory: the thinking can improve, not the hardware.',basics:{title:'Remember things by linking them',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'NAILS AND THREADS',picture:!0,text:'Think of a fact as a nail in a wall. Tie one thread to it and there is only one way to find it. Tie twenty threads, running everywhere, and almost any thread leads you back.'},{kind:'idea',text:'You can\u2019t make your memory bigger. But you can tie each new fact to lots of things you already know. More links means more ways to find it again.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Who said this',text:'William James wrote a famous book about the mind in 1890. He thought the memory you are born with can\u2019t be trained to grow. What can grow is the number of links. So, he said, remembering well comes from thinking well.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'LEARNING ABOUT VOLCANOES',text:'You could read the page five times. Or you could link it. Shaking a fizzy drink and then opening it is a bit like a volcano: the push builds up, then bursts out. Tell your little brother. Draw it. Now the fact has many threads.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Reading something again and again is the best way to remember it.',reality:'Reading it again feels like work, but it adds few new links. Explaining it, or tying it to something you know, adds many more.'},{kind:'try',text:'After your next lesson, spend five minutes writing how it links to something you already know well.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You need to remember that spiders have eight legs. Which way gives the fact the most links?',options:['Compare it with an insect\u2019s six legs and tell a friend','Read the sentence over and over ten times','Copy the sentence out neatly ten times'],answer:0,why:'Comparing and explaining tie the fact to other things. Repeating it adds no new threads.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE STUDY HABIT',belief:'Re-reading your notes is how you make them stick.',reality:'Re-reading adds exposure, which feels like progress. It adds almost no new connections, and connections are what memory runs on.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Your raw power to retain is fixed. What you can change is how many connections a fact is tied into, so the art of remembering is the art of thinking: link it, use it, argue with it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Not better hardware',text:'William James was blunt that the native retentiveness of a mind cannot be trained. People who remember a great deal are rarely people with better hardware. They have tied each fact into more of what they know. A fact attached to nothing has one thread to be found by. A fact attached to ten has ten.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Adds exposure',items:['Re-reading','Highlighting','Copying notes out']},right:{title:'Adds threads',items:['Explaining it to someone','Applying it to a case you know','Arguing against it']}},{kind:'picture',caption:'One nail, one thread. Another, twenty threads. Which would you rather have to find in the dark?'},{kind:'try',text:'After your next session, spend five minutes writing how it connects to something you already know well.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You have a meeting about a new policy next week. Which preparation will help you remember its details?',options:['Work out how it would change a project you know well','Read the policy document three more times','Copy its key paragraphs out by hand','Read it once, just before the meeting starts'],answer:0,why:'Applying the policy to a familiar project ties it into many existing threads. The other options add exposure, not connections.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'HIS OWN EXPERIMENT',text:'James tested whether memory could be trained. He timed himself learning lines of a Victor Hugo poem. Then for several weeks he learned part of Milton\u2019s *Paradise Lost* every day. Then he timed himself on more of the Hugo. He was no faster; if anything, slower. A few others who repeated the test got mixed results.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Native retentiveness is fixed. What changes is the number of connections a fact is woven into when it is learned, so memory is largely the residue of how hard you thought about the material.'},{kind:'term',word:'Retentiveness',meaning:'James\u2019s word for the brain\u2019s raw, physical tendency to hold on to impressions. He held that it differs between people but stays fixed for each, so drills cannot raise it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What can change',text:'What improves memory is the method of recording facts: weaving each one into a system of relations. Re-reading adds exposure, not threads. Using the material adds threads: explaining it, applying it to a known case, arguing against it, linking it to something learned last year.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two nails, two kinds of fact. One hangs by a single thread. The other is tied into twenty, and is hard to lose.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What forgetting means',text:'The view is more radical than a study tip. If memory is not a separate faculty you can drill, then forgetting a course usually shows that the thinking was never done, not that memory failed. You keep what you thought hard about.'},{kind:'limits',text:'His experiment was one man timing himself, too crude to settle anything. Later research broadly agrees that general memory capacity resists training, but the strong claim goes too far. Well-learned material still fades with time and interference. And practising recall, testing yourself rather than re-reading, strengthens a memory even without adding new links.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'An app promises to raise your \u201cmemory power\u201d with daily drills on random lists of digits. What would James predict?',options:['Better scores on digit lists, but no gain in remembering what you study','A general boost to memory, since retentiveness grows like a muscle','No change at all, since practice never improves any memory task','Worse memory overall, because the drills crowd out other facts'],answer:0,why:'For James, drills cannot change native retentiveness. Gains stay with the drilled task; remembering your studies depends on connections made while thinking.'}]}}}},2997,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'reading-in-public_1':{pictureCaption:'The stairs disappear into the dark, and the boots are on the first step anyway. Not seeing the top is how a hard climb starts.',basics:{title:'Feeling lost is part of it',beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'If a book confuses me, it is too hard for me, so I should stop.',reality:'Feeling lost at the start of a good, hard book is normal. It tells you the book is hard. It does not tell you that you are not clever enough.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'CHAPTER ONE',text:'You start a book your aunt loved at your age. Chapter one is full of names and a strange place. You can\u2019t tell who anyone is. You think: this book is not for me.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Any book worth reading will be too hard for you at some point. Feeling lost is not a sign to quit. It is how hard books begin.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why are hard books like that?',text:'A comic or a text message is made to be understood at once. A hard book is often written for people who will finish it. The early pages set things up. They make sense later on.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The stairs go up into the dark. You can\u2019t see the top. The boots step on the first stair anyway.'},{kind:'explain',title:'So when should you stop?',text:'Being lost is fine. Being lost and going nowhere is different. If, after a few chapters, nothing at all is starting to fit, that is the time to put it down.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You are three pages into a hard book and you don\u2019t get it yet. What does this idea say?',options:['Keep going. Being lost at the start is normal.','Stop. It means the book is too hard for you.','Swap it for an easier book straight away.'],answer:0,why:'Three pages is far too soon to judge. Hard books often make sense later, once the early parts click into place.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'TWENTY PAGES IN',text:'A friend recommends a classic. Twenty pages in, you have met terms you cannot define and an argument whose point you cannot see. You conclude it is not for you and put it back on the shelf.'},{kind:'idea',text:'If a book is worth reading, it will at some point be beyond you. The confusion is information about the book\u2019s difficulty, not about your capacity.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where the expectation comes from',text:'Most of what we read, news, messages, emails, is written to be understood immediately, and it trains us to expect that. A serious book is often written for readers who will finish it. Its early chapters lay down terms that only resolve later.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Confusion going somewhere',items:['Terms start to recur and settle','You sense roughly what is at stake','Each chapter adds something']},right:{title:'Confusion going nowhere',items:['Nothing connects after several chapters','No sense of direction at all','Each chapter as opaque as the first']},note:'The essay\u2019s signal for quitting is the right-hand column, not confusion itself.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The stairs disappear into the dark, and the boots are on the first step anyway. Not seeing the top is how a hard climb starts.'},{kind:'try',text:'With your next hard book, expect not to follow the opening. Read a few chapters before judging, then ask only: is this confusion going anywhere?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'By this idea, which is a good reason to abandon a book?',options:['After several chapters, no sense the confusion is heading anywhere','The first chapter used several words you did not know','You could not summarise chapter one when you finished it','It felt much harder than what you normally read'],answer:0,why:'Confusion is the expected state. Confusion with no direction, after a fair trial, is the signal.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE ESSAY\u2019S STARTING POINT',text:'How to Read a Difficult Book is a short essay on what to do when a book is beyond you, which it says is most worthwhile books, most of the time. It opens by rejecting the usual reading of being confused.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Confusion at the start of a difficult book is the correct state, not evidence of unsuitability. Treating it as failure is why people abandon the books that would have taught them most.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW GOOD BOOKS GET DROPPED',nodes:['Most reading is instantly clear','So we expect instant clarity','A serious book confuses','Confusion is read as failure','The book is abandoned'],note:'The essay breaks the chain at the fourth step.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why serious books confuse early',text:'They are frequently written for readers who will finish them, so the early chapters lay down terms that only resolve later. The honest instruction, then, is to expect not to follow and to continue anyway, which is precisely what most readers do not do.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The real test for quitting',text:'Not confusion, but the absence of any sense that the confusion is going somewhere. The essay says the two feel different after a few chapters, and that telling them apart is a skill that only develops by pushing past the first chapter a few times.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A staircase vanishing upward, and a first step taken without sight of the top. Confusion here is the expected view.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Some confusion is the writer\u2019s fault, not the subject\u2019s, and the principle can excuse a badly written book. Some books are beyond you for want of background, a technical field for instance, and pressing on will not supply it; a simpler book first might. And the going-somewhere test is a felt judgement the essay admits takes practice.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'CORRECT THE OVERREACH',prompt:'A reader says: \u201cI never abandon a book, however lost I am.\u201d What would the essay say?',options:['Confusion is fine, but confusion going nowhere is a reason to stop','Right: confusion should never be a reason to stop reading','Wrong: being lost means the book was too advanced from the start','Push on only with short books, where the cost is small'],answer:0,why:'The essay defends confusion, not endurance. Its quitting signal is confusion that has stopped going anywhere.'}]}},'reading-in-public_2':{pictureCaption:'From the ridge the whole valley shows at once, and none of its detail. The walker is moving fast: this trip is for the shape.',bridge:'Idea 1 said to expect confusion. This one says what to do with it on a first read: keep moving.',basics:{title:'Read it all once, fast',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'STUCK ON PAGE TWELVE',prompt:'You are reading a hard book. Page twelve makes no sense. What is the best move?',options:['Stop until you understand page twelve','Keep reading, and come back to it later','Give up on the book'],answer:1,reveal:'Often a later chapter explains page twelve. If you stop and stay stuck, you never get to the part that would have helped.'},{kind:'idea',text:'With a hard book, read it all the way through once, quite fast, even if you miss a lot. Then you know its shape. Read it again slowly after that.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A JIGSAW BOX',text:'Before a jigsaw, you look at the picture on the box. You don\u2019t know where each piece goes yet. But now you know what you are making, so every piece has a place to go.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'From up on the hill you can see the whole valley, but not the small things. That comes later. First, the shape.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE SECOND TIME',text:'Think of a mystery story. The first time, you just find out what happens. The second time, you spot clues you missed. They were there all along. You just had nowhere to put them yet.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time a book gets hard, put a small mark by the bit you don\u2019t get. Keep reading. Come back to your marks at the end.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your class book has a hard chapter near the start. What does this idea say to do?',options:['Read on to the end, then go back to it','Read that chapter again and again until it is clear','Skip the rest and look up the ending'],answer:0,why:'A later chapter may explain it. Once you know the whole shape, the hard part is easier.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Read a hard book carefully, and do not move on until each page is clear.',reality:'That often ends with the first fifty pages read carefully and the rest never read. The essay advises the reverse: once through, quickly, accepting that much will be missed.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The first pass is not for understanding. It is for shape: roughly what the argument is, and where the hard parts are. Stop to resolve every difficulty and you never reach the chapter that would have explained it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why speed beats care, first time',text:'Later sections explain earlier ones. Terms resolve. The shape of the argument only becomes visible from the end. So careful attention paid at the start is paid without the context that would make it efficient.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Two passes',steps:['Read straight through at speed. Mark the hard parts, but do not stop.','At the end, note roughly what the argument was.','Read again, slowing down at your marks. Now the detail has somewhere to go.']},{kind:'picture',caption:'From the ridge the whole valley shows at once, and none of its detail. The walker is moving fast: this trip is for the shape.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where the advice comes from',text:'The essay credits this to Mortimer Adler\u2019s *How to Read a Book*, and suggests it may be the most useful single technique for difficult material. It asks for the same tolerance as idea 1: carrying on while not understanding.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick the hard book you stalled on. Finish it once at speed before you slow down for anything.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You are forty pages into a dense book and stuck on a key term. What does the idea suggest?',options:['Mark it and read on; the term may resolve later','Research the term thoroughly before turning another page','Go back to page one and read more carefully','Abandon the book, since you missed something basic'],answer:0,why:'Terms in serious books often resolve as the argument unfolds. The first pass is for the map, not for every detail.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO START',left:{title:'Careful from page one',items:['Attention spent without context','Stuck at the first hard part','Often stops near page fifty']},right:{title:'A fast first pass',items:['Builds a rough map first','Hard parts flagged, not solved','Second pass has somewhere to put things']}},{kind:'idea',text:'Reading a difficult book through once quickly, accepting much will be missed, usually beats reading the opening carefully and stopping. The first pass builds the map. The second has somewhere to put things.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHOSE ADVICE IT IS',text:'The essay does not claim this as its own. It credits the advice to Mortimer Adler\u2019s *How to Read a Book*, and suggests, carefully, that it may be the single most useful technique for difficult material.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The structural reason',text:'A difficult book is often not built to be understood in order. Later sections explain earlier ones, terminology settles as it is used, and the shape of the argument becomes visible only from the end. Careful attention at the start is paid before the context exists that would make it count.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The walker covers the ridge quickly and takes in the valley as one shape. The detail is still down there, waiting for the second trip.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The link to idea 1',text:'A fast pass is only possible if you can tolerate the discomfort of carrying on without understanding. That is the same tolerance idea 1 asks for. Without it, every hard paragraph becomes a reason to stop.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Some texts are built step by step, as in mathematics or law, where each part depends on the last. A fast pass through them yields no map. Read too lightly, a first pass can leave a wrong map that the second must undo. And the method assumes a second pass happens. The essay\u2019s own claims are hedged, usually and may be, and should stay so.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'WHERE IT FITS LEAST',prompt:'For which text is a fast first pass least suited?',options:['A maths textbook where each proof relies on the one before it','A long history built around a single overarching argument','A philosophy book whose key terms are defined by later use','A dense novel whose many characters only make sense later'],answer:0,why:'The method works because later parts explain earlier ones. Where each step needs the previous one, skimming builds nothing to stand on.'}]}},'reading-in-public_3':{pictureCaption:'Someone else\u2019s neat drawing, and a second hand arguing with it in orange. The orange lines are the reader\u2019s own thinking.',bridge:'Ideas 1 and 2 were about getting through a hard book. This one is about answering back as you go.',basics:{title:'Argue with the book',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'ON A STICKY NOTE',text:'Your class reads an article saying school should start an hour later. You think: *but then it ends later too!* You write that on a sticky note next to it. Two paragraphs on, the writer answers your point.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When you read, argue back. Write your objection next to the words. Often you will turn out to be wrong, and finding out why is how you learn.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Colouring it in',items:['Makes the words bright','Feels busy','Needs no thinking']},right:{title:'Arguing back',items:['Adds your own words','Makes you work out the point','Leaves you with a thought']}},{kind:'picture',caption:'Someone drew a neat plan. A second hand is arguing with it in orange. Those orange lines are the reader thinking.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why being wrong helps',text:'To argue, you must understand what the book says well enough to find a weak spot. That is harder than just reading along. So even when your point is wrong, you had to think hard to make it.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you read something that makes a point, write three words of argument on a sticky note. Later, check if the writer answered it.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Which of these is arguing with a book?',options:['Writing \u201cbut what about rainy days?\u201d by a page','Colouring the key line in yellow pen','Putting a star by a page you liked'],answer:0,why:'Only the rainy-days question adds a thought of your own. The others mark the page but need no thinking.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO KINDS OF MARK',left:{title:'Underlining',items:['Marks what the author said','Feels like engagement','Contains nothing you produced']},right:{title:'A margin objection',items:['Records where you disagree','Requires finding the weak point','Contains a thought of yours']},note:'The test is whether the mark contains anything you produced.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Writing objections as you go turns reading from reception into conversation. Many of the objections turn out to be wrong, and finding out why is often where the understanding happens.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why objecting works',text:'It works the way explaining does. To object, you must have understood a claim well enough to locate where it fails. That is a much higher bar than following it, and clearing it tends to make the reading stick.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Marks worth making',steps:['Where you disagree.','Where a claim seems unsupported.','Where this contradicts something earlier.','Where you can think of a counterexample.']},{kind:'picture',caption:'Someone else\u2019s neat drawing, and a second hand arguing with it in orange. The orange lines are the reader\u2019s own thinking.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THREE WORDS',text:'A book argues that small teams move faster. In the margin you write: *what about coordination?* Two chapters later the author deals with exactly that. Your objection was wrong, and you now know precisely why the author thinks so.'},{kind:'try',text:'In your next hard chapter, write one objection as an objection. Then read on and find out why it was wrong, or whether it was.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Which margin mark best follows the idea?',options:['Isn\u2019t this the opposite of chapter two?','A double underline beneath the main claim','A highlighted paragraph marked as important','A tick beside a sentence you agree with'],answer:0,why:'It holds a thought of yours: a possible contradiction to check. The others record the text, not your response to it.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'A clean copy, carefully highlighted, shows a book read with attention.',reality:'The essay suggests a clean copy of a hard book is often an unread one, and that highlighting feels like engagement while requiring no processing at all.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Arguing in the margin converts reading from reception into exchange, and more of it tends to stay with you. Many of the objections turn out to be wrong. Finding out why is often where the understanding happens.'},{kind:'term',word:'Reception',meaning:'Reading as taking in what is said, with the reader passive. The essay sets it against exchange, in which the reader answers back and the book\u2019s next pages answer in turn.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The mechanism',text:'Objection works like explanation. You cannot say where a claim fails without first grasping the claim well enough to locate the failure, which is a far higher bar than following along. Specific marks force that grasp: a disagreement, an unsupported claim, a contradiction with something earlier, a counterexample.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The test for a mark',text:'Does it contain anything you produced? A highlight contains nothing. Three words of objection contain a thought, and on the essay\u2019s account the thought is what you will still have in a year.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The original lines are neat and confident. The orange ones cross, loop and object. Only the orange ones were made by the reader.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The essay\u2019s claims here are tendencies: many objections wrong, more tends to stay. It offers no measure. The habit can also harden into reading to refute, objecting before understanding and never checking. The takeaway guards against that: find out later why the objection was wrong. And borrowed books need notes, not ink.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which reader has turned the idea into a bad habit?',options:['One who objects to every claim and never checks the objections','One who notes a counterexample, then sees how the author handles it','One who flags an unsupported claim and looks for support later','One who marks a contradiction with an earlier chapter'],answer:0,why:'The value is in finding out why the objection fails. Objecting without checking is reception in reverse: still no exchange.'}]}},'reading-in-public_4':{pictureCaption:'No notes, no book, just a bare wall and two hands explaining. Where the hands stop usually marks the part that was never understood.',bridge:'Idea 3 argued with the book while it was open. This one tests what is left once it is shut.',basics:{title:'Explain it with the book shut',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'TELLING THE DOG',text:'You read a chapter about volcanoes. You feel sure you get it. You shut the book and explain it to your dog. Halfway through you stop: *and then the lava\u2026 um.* That *um* is the bit you did not really understand.'},{kind:'idea',text:'After you read, shut the book and explain it out loud. The place where you get stuck is usually the part you did not understand. It is often not where you thought.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'No book. No notes. Just a wall and two hands explaining. Where the hands stop is likely the part he missed.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why out loud?',text:'When you write, you can stop and think and fix it. When you talk, you have to keep going. You can\u2019t hide the gaps. They show up straight away.'},{kind:'steps',title:'The shut-book test',steps:['Shut the book.','Say what the chapter said.','Say how it showed it.','Notice where you get stuck.','Look up just that part.']},{kind:'explain',title:'Getting stuck is useful',text:'The first few times, you will get stuck a lot. That can feel bad. But now you know which part to read again. Finding out today is much better than finding out in a test.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You read a chapter for homework. How can you tell which part you really understood?',options:['Shut the book and explain it out loud','Read the chapter again more slowly','Look at the pictures in the chapter'],answer:0,why:'Explaining with the book shut shows the exact place you get stuck. Reading again can feel like knowing without showing you the gap.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'WHICH TEST IS STRICTER?',prompt:'You have just read a chapter. Which is the hardest test of whether you understood it?',options:['Summarising it in writing with the book open','Summarising it in writing from memory','Explaining it aloud with the book closed'],answer:2,reveal:'Writing lets you pause, hedge and rearrange. Speech runs in real time, so a gap shows the moment you reach it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Close the book and explain the chapter aloud. The point where you falter usually marks what you did not understand, and it is often not where you thought.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'A written summary',items:['You can pause','You can hedge','You can rearrange']},right:{title:'Saying it aloud',items:['Runs in real time','Hedges are audible','Gaps show at once']}},{kind:'steps',title:'After each chapter',steps:['Close the book.','State what the chapter claimed.','State what the evidence was.','State where the argument was weakest.','Note exactly where you stalled.']},{kind:'picture',caption:'No notes, no book, just a bare wall and two hands explaining. Where the hands stop usually marks the part that was never understood.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The humbling is the information',text:'First attempts are usually humbling. A chapter you read attentively and cannot summarise was not retained, and discovering that now is far cheaper than discovering it when you need it. Explain to a person if one is around, to an empty room if not.'},{kind:'try',text:'After the next chapter you read, explain it aloud with the book closed. Write down where you stall.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You stall when explaining a chapter\u2019s evidence, though its main claim came easily. What should you do?',options:['Reread the evidence section, since that is the gap','Reread the whole chapter from the start','Move on, since you got the main claim','Write a summary instead, which is fairer'],answer:0,why:'The stall located the gap. The test is useful precisely because it tells you where to look again.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'WHERE WILL YOU STALL?',prompt:'You explain a chapter you have just read carefully. Where will you falter?',options:['Roughly where you expected to','Somewhere you did not expect'],answer:1,reveal:'The essay says it is often not where you thought. That is why the test is worth doing: it finds gaps that feeling cannot.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Saying the argument aloud without the book is a demanding test, and few other checks find out as quickly what you actually have. The point where you falter usually marks what you did not understand.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'FROM FEELING TO KNOWING',nodes:['Read the chapter attentively','Feel you understood it','Explain it aloud, book closed','Stall at a specific point','That point is the gap']},{kind:'explain',title:'Why speech, not writing',text:'A written summary permits pausing, hedging and rearranging, so a gap can be quietly routed around. Speech runs in real time and exposes the gap immediately. The full version states three things: what the chapter claimed, what the evidence was, and where the argument was weakest.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the humbling matters',text:'A chapter you read attentively and cannot summarise was not retained. The essay\u2019s point is economic: discovering that immediately is much cheaper than discovering it later, when you rely on it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The explainer faces a blank orange wall. Nothing on it to lean on, so every gap in the explanation is his own.'},{kind:'limits',text:'That few other checks locate the gap as quickly is the essay\u2019s claim, offered without evidence; being questioned or working problems can do the same. Fluency is not accuracy: you can explain smoothly and wrongly, and an empty room cannot correct you, which is why a listener is better. Some people stall from nerves, not ignorance.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'READ THE RESULT',prompt:'You explain a chapter to an empty room, fluently and without stalling. What can you fairly conclude?',options:['You can state it, though an error may have gone unchallenged','You have fully understood it and can safely move on','The test was useless, since nothing was found','You should reread it anyway, since fluency proves nothing'],answer:0,why:'Not stalling shows you retained an account. With no listener, it cannot show the account was right.'}]}},'reading-in-public_5':{pictureCaption:'Two different measuring tools on the same block. The circle marks where their readings disagree, which is where to look hardest.',bridge:'Ideas 3 and 4 tested you against one book. This one tests the book against another.',basics:{title:'Try a second book, not the same one twice',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE TWO FRIENDS AT A MATCH',text:'Two friends tell you about a match. Both say it ended 2\u20131. Fine, that part is clear. But one says the goal was a foul and one says it wasn\u2019t. Now you know which bit people argue about.'},{kind:'idea',text:'If a book on a subject is hard, read a different book on the same subject, not the same book again. Where they agree, relax. Where they disagree is the tricky part.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two different tools measure the same block. The orange circle shows where they don\u2019t agree. Look there.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why one book is not enough',text:'One book says everything in the same sure voice. So you can\u2019t tell which parts everyone agrees on. You can\u2019t tell which parts are just this writer\u2019s view.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'TWO DOG BOOKS',text:'Imagine two books about training a dog. Both say: reward good behaviour. That part is probably settled. One says use a crate, and one says don\u2019t. Now you know that is the bit to think hardest about.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you learn about something, find a second book or website by someone else. Look for one thing they agree on and one thing they don\u2019t.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A book about the Romans confuses you. What does this idea say to do?',options:['Read a different book about the Romans','Read the same book again, slowly','Give up on the Romans for now'],answer:0,why:'A second writer shows you which parts are agreed and which are argued about. Rereading only gives you the same view again.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'ONE STORY OF MONEY',text:'You read a popular history of money and come away sure of its account. A second book, by a different author, tells parts of the story differently. It stresses other causes. You can now see which parts are settled and which were one writer\u2019s view.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A second book on the same subject, by someone else, is usually more use than rereading the first. Where two accounts differ is where the real difficulty lives. Where they agree you can stop worrying.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What one book cannot tell you',text:'A single book gives you no way to tell the subject apart from the author\u2019s account of it. Everything arrives with the same confidence. So you have no basis for telling a settled finding from a disputed one.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Rereading the first',items:['The same framing again','The same confidence throughout','Disputes stay hidden']},right:{title:'Reading a second author',items:['A different framing','Disagreements become visible','Thin evidence shows up']}},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two different measuring tools on the same block. The circle marks where their readings disagree, which is where to look hardest.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The failure it prevents',text:'The essay names a common failure in reading non-fiction. You take on an author\u2019s framing whole, simply because you had no other one to hand while reading. A second book gives you the other one.'},{kind:'try',text:'When a book defeats you, find a different author on the same subject before you reread it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You finished a persuasive book on sleep and want to really understand the subject. What does the idea suggest next?',options:['Read another author on sleep and note where they differ','Reread the same book, taking careful notes this time','Read a summary of the same book to fix its points','Trust it, since the author is clearly an expert'],answer:0,why:'One persuasive account gives you its framing and nothing to test it against. A second author shows what is settled and what is disputed.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'A WORD FIRST',word:'Framing',meaning:'The way an author sets up a subject: what counts as the question, which facts come first, what is taken for granted. With only one book, a reader usually absorbs it without noticing it is there.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The essay argues that reading two books on the same subject is often more useful than reading one twice. One book cannot show you the difference between the subject and the author\u2019s account of it. Two can.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The argument',text:'A single book presents everything with the same confidence, so the reader cannot tell a settled finding from a contested position. A second makes the disagreements visible, and the essay locates real understanding there: what is disputed, what turns on which assumption, where the evidence is thin.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Where they agree',items:['Probably settled','You can move faster','Less to worry about']},right:{title:'Where they differ',items:['Where the difficulty lives','What turns on which assumption','Where evidence is thin']}},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two instruments, two methods, one object. Their agreement is reassuring. The circled place where they part is where the work is.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What it protects against',text:'A common failure in reading non-fiction, on the essay\u2019s account, is taking on an author\u2019s framing wholesale because nothing else was on hand. A second author is a simple protection against that.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Two books can share a framing, from the same school or the same sources, so agreement is not proof; choose authors who start from different places. A difference may be emphasis, not dispute. For a beginner, two conflicting accounts can confuse before they clarify. And this sits beside idea 2\u2019s second pass: it is advice for when a book defeats you.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'WEIGH THE AGREEMENT',prompt:'Two books on a subject agree on a point. What is the careful conclusion?',options:['Probably settled, unless both rely on the same sources or school','Certainly true, since two separate authors confirm it','Not worth attention, since only disagreements matter','Suspicious, since agreement usually means one copied the other'],answer:0,why:'Agreement lets you stop worrying only if the two accounts are independent. Shared sources can make one view look like two.'}]}}}},2998,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'reading-that-sticks_1':{pictureCaption:'The hand finds this handle without looking. The room beyond is dark. Knowing the way in is not knowing what is inside.',basics:{title:'Easy to read is not the same as remembered',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE NIGHT BEFORE',prompt:'You read your notes three times. By the third time they feel really easy. What does that easy feeling show?',options:['That you will remember them in the test','That the words look familiar now'],answer:1,reveal:'Reading something again makes it feel familiar, and familiar feels like knowing. But it only shows the words are easy to read. It does not show you can bring them back.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When reading feels easy, it is tempting to think you have learned it. But easy only means familiar. To find out if you remember, close the book and try.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A FACE IN A CROWD',text:'You can spot your friend\u2019s face in a crowd in a second. But could you draw it with nothing to look at? Spotting is easy. Bringing it back from memory is much harder.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The hand knows this handle well. But the room behind the door is dark. Knowing the way in is not knowing what is inside.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it fools you',text:'The second time you read a page, it goes down more smoothly. That smooth feeling is real. But it comes from seeing the words again, not from keeping them. A week later, almost none of it may come back.'},{kind:'try',text:'After you read a page, shut the book. Say what it was about. If you get stuck, that is useful. Now you know what to look at again.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend says, *I read the chapter twice, so I know it.* What would you tell them?',options:['Close the book and try to say it back first','Read it a third time just to be really sure','If it felt easy, then you must know it'],answer:0,why:'Feeling easy only shows the words are familiar. Saying it back shows what you really kept.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'A WEEK LATER',text:'You finish a chapter on negotiation and close the book sure you have it. A week later a colleague asks what it said. You can manage the title and a sense that it was good. Not one sentence of the argument comes back.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The ease of reading something is not evidence that you have stored it. Re-reading makes text familiar, and familiarity is mistaken for knowing. Test the feeling before you trust it.'},{kind:'term',word:'Fluency',meaning:'How easily material goes down as you read. Clear writing, familiarity and recent exposure all raise it. None of them shows that anything has been stored.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why re-reading misleads',text:'The piece says re-reading raises fluency substantially and retention barely. That is why it is both the most common technique and among the least effective. The second pass feels much better than the first, but the improvement is in ease, not in what you can get back out.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'What fluency measures',items:['How easily the words go down','Whether the page looks familiar','Rises fast with re-reading']},right:{title:'What memory needs',items:['Whether you can produce it','With the book closed','Barely moved by re-reading']}},{kind:'picture',caption:'A handle the hand knows by touch, and a room beyond it in darkness. Familiar with the way in, unable to describe what is inside.'},{kind:'try',text:'After your next chapter, close the book and write its argument in five lines. Then compare with the text. The gap between the two is the useful information.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Which is the best evidence that you have retained a chapter?',options:['You can rebuild its main argument with the book closed','The chapter felt clear and easy on the second reading','You recognise its key phrases when you skim it again','You highlighted the important lines as you went'],answer:0,why:'Ease, recognition and highlighting can all come from familiarity. Only producing it from memory shows it was stored.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHAT THE PIECE ARGUES',text:'This Mindroll editorial starts from an uncomfortable claim: reading something again feels like learning and mostly is not. Its first idea names the illusion behind that. The feeling of understanding while reading is not evidence of retention, and the gap between the two is, it says, the central problem in reading for learning.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Fluency is produced by clear writing, familiarity and recent exposure, none of which shows that anything has been stored. Re-reading raises it a lot and retention barely, so confidence after a second pass measures ease, not recall.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE ILLUSION FORMS',nodes:['You re-read the chapter','It goes down more easily','Ease feels like knowing','You stop, confident'],note:'Only the rise in ease is real. The knowing was inferred from it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Recognition is not recall',text:'Following a text needs only recognition: each sentence, met again, fits. Using it needs recall: producing the argument with nothing in front of you. Re-reading exercises the first and tests nothing. That is how a confident reader can fail to produce one sentence a week later.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The corrective',text:'Test rather than trust: close the book and reconstruct. The piece expects the result to be worse than you predicted, and treats that discrepancy as the useful information. It measures what the fluency was hiding.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Fluency is not worthless. Clear writing is still better than murky writing, and a second reading helps when the first was confused or interrupted. The claim is narrower: ease is not evidence of storage. And a failed reconstruction can understate what you know, since some things come back only when something cues them.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A handle worn familiar, a room beyond it in the dark. Fluency is the handle; what you could describe of the room is the memory.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE ERROR',prompt:'A student says: \u201cI know this chapter. It read so smoothly the second time.\u201d What is the error?',options:['Treating ease of reading as evidence of what is stored','Reading the chapter twice rather than three times','Choosing a chapter that was too easy to learn from','Reading it at a time of day when they were tired'],answer:0,why:'Smoothness on a second pass is what re-reading reliably produces. It measures familiarity, not whether the chapter can be recalled.'}]}},'reading-that-sticks_2':{pictureCaption:'The box stays shut. The hands draw what is inside from memory, getting some of it wrong. The reaching is what makes it stick.',bridge:'Idea 1 showed that easy reading fools you. This is what to do instead: close the book and try to bring it back.',basics:{title:'Trying to remember is how you remember',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'TEN SPELLING WORDS',text:'You have ten spelling words to learn. Your sister reads the list over and over. You cover the list and try to write the words out. You get six right. She feels sure she knows all ten.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'Who will remember more of the words next week?',options:['Your sister, who read the list lots','You, who tried and got some wrong'],answer:1,reveal:'Trying to bring the words back makes them stick, even when you get some wrong. Reading the list again mostly makes it feel familiar.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Shut the book and try to remember what it said. It feels harder than reading again, but days later you will remember more. It helps even when you get bits wrong.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The box stays shut. The hands draw what is inside from memory. Some of it is wrong. The trying is what helps.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A PATH IN THE GRASS',text:'Each time you walk across a field, the path gets clearer. Each time you reach for a memory, the way to it gets clearer too. Reading it again is like being carried across.'},{kind:'steps',title:'After each page',steps:['Shut the book.','Say what it said, in one sentence.','Stuck? Read it once more.','Then shut it and try again.']},{kind:'question',prompt:'You want to learn a poem for class. Which way works best?',options:['Cover it, try to say it, then check','Read it over ten times in a row','Copy it out neatly as you read it'],answer:0,why:'Trying to bring it back is what makes it stick. Reading it again mostly makes it feel familiar.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE NEXT TEN MINUTES',prompt:'You have just read a chapter. Which use of the next ten minutes leaves you remembering more of it later?',options:['Reading it a second time','Closing the book and reconstructing it','About the same either way'],answer:1,reveal:'The piece says reconstruction wins by a wide margin when you are tested days later rather than straight away. The effect is known as retrieval practice. The piece calls it one of the better-replicated findings in learning research, across ages and materials.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Closing the book and trying to reconstruct the argument does more for retention than reading it again. It feels less productive. And it works even when the attempt partly fails.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the attempt matters',text:'On the piece\u2019s account, the effort of reaching for something is what strengthens the path to it. That is why a partial attempt still helps. The reaching happened, even if the whole answer did not arrive.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why re-reading feels better',text:'Re-reading produces fluency, the sense of material going down easily. Retrieval strips that familiarity away. It shows what is really there, which is usually less than you thought. That discovery is the point, not a discouragement.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The box stays closed while the hands draw its contents from memory, some of it wrong. The attempt does the work, errors and all.'},{kind:'steps',title:'A routine for this app',steps:['After each idea, stop before moving on.','Say in a sentence what it claimed.','Say what you would do with it.','If you cannot, read it once more and try again.'],note:'The question at the end of each idea here exists for exactly this reason.'},{kind:'try',text:'After the next idea you read, close the app and say the claim and the takeaway out loud. If you cannot, that idea needs one more pass, and now you know which.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You need to absorb a report before tomorrow\u2019s meeting. Which plan applies the idea?',options:['Read it once, then write its main points from memory and check','Read it twice, carefully, highlighting the key lines as you go','Read it once slowly, then reread only the summary at the end','Ask a colleague who has read it to send you their notes'],answer:0,why:'Recalling from memory is what strengthens retention. Checking afterwards shows you exactly where the gaps are.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'THE NAME FOR IT',word:'Retrieval practice',meaning:'Learning by bringing material back out of memory, rather than putting it in again. Closing the book and reconstructing the argument is its simplest form.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Trying to reconstruct what you read does more for retention than reading it again, by a wide margin on tests days later. The effort of reaching for the material strengthens the path to it, so the attempt helps even when it partly fails.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The evidence, as the piece states it',text:'It calls the retrieval-practice effect among the better-replicated findings in learning research, across ages and materials. It gives no figures. The claim is comparative: set against a second reading of the same material, reconstruction leaves more behind, by a wide margin when the test comes days later rather than straight away.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Second reading',items:['Feels productive','Raises familiarity','Hides the gaps']},right:{title:'Reconstruction',items:['Feels uncomfortable','Strengthens the path back','Shows the gaps']},note:'The method that feels better is the one that does less.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why a gap is good news',text:'Retrieval strips away familiarity and shows what is actually there, usually less than you thought. The piece insists this is the point. A gap found by trying is one you can now fill on purpose. A gap hidden by fluency stays hidden.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Retrieval needs something to retrieve: with hard, new material, a careful first reading comes first. An attempt with no check afterwards can leave you holding a wrong version, which is why the piece\u2019s routine sends you back to the text when you fail. And the effort is unpleasant enough that people quietly drop it. A method works only if it survives being used.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The box stays shut; the drawing beside it is partly wrong. Reaching for what is inside, and then checking, is the whole method.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'A colleague objects that testing yourself only shows what you don\u2019t know, and wastes time better spent reading. What is the best reply?',options:['Finding the gaps is the point, and the attempt itself strengthens memory','Testing only helps once you already know the material well','Reading is better for learning; testing is better for confidence','Testing works only when you can get every answer right'],answer:0,why:'The piece treats the discovery of gaps as information, and the reaching as what does the work, even when it fails.'}]}},'reading-that-sticks_3':{pictureCaption:'The stone drags at the blade, and the drag is what sharpens it. The work is awkward, and the edge is better for it.',bridge:'Ideas 1 and 2 showed that the method that feels worse can work better. This one asks which kinds of difficulty help, and where they stop.',basics:{title:'The right kind of hard',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A CLIMBING WALL',text:'On a climbing wall, the easy route is fun, but you learn nothing new. A route a bit too hard makes you stretch, and you get better. A route far too hard just leaves you stuck at the bottom.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Some ways to learn feel harder, but more of it sticks. The hard part has to be the right kind, though. Working to remember helps. Struggling just to work out the words does not.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Harder ways that work',text:'Here are three hard ways that work. Leaving gaps between study times. Mixing up different topics. Trying to remember, not just reading again. They feel slow, but more tends to be left later.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'FIND THE MIDDLE',low:'Too smooth: feels good, but less sticks',high:'Too hard: stuck on every word',note:'The best spot is in between. You work at remembering, not at working out the words.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The stone drags at the knife. That drag is what makes it sharp. Hard work, at the right angle, makes it better.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE TELLING SIGN',text:'You are reading a book about space. If you can follow it but have to think hard, it is the right level. If you read each sentence three times and still can\u2019t tell what it means, try an easier book first.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You have a times-tables test next week. Which way to practise will likely leave more in your head?',options:['Mix the tables up, and say each answer before you check','Read the seven times table over until it feels easy','Only practise the tables you already know well'],answer:0,why:'Mixing and remembering feel harder, but they tend to leave more behind. Feeling easy does not show it worked.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE NATURAL TEST',belief:'A study session that felt smooth and productive was a good one.',reality:'Conditions that make learning feel harder, such as spacing sessions out, mixing topics and recalling rather than reviewing, often improve what remains later while feeling worse at the time. Judging by smoothness selects against them.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Some kinds of difficulty help, and they are fairly specific. Judge a study session by what stays, not by how smooth it felt, and make sure the effort goes into recalling, not decoding.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Useful difficulty',items:['Recalling before you look','Telling one topic from another','Effort goes into retrieval']},right:{title:'Wasted difficulty',items:['Material far beyond you','Re-reading sentences to parse them','Effort goes into decoding']},note:'Both feel like struggling. Only one is doing the work.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why these help',text:'The piece\u2019s reason is that each forces effortful retrieval and comparison, which smooth review skips. That is also why the difficulty has to be the right size. Effort spent recalling, or telling one topic from another, does the work. Effort spent merely decoding material far beyond you does not.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE WRONG KIND OF HARD',text:'You open a dense book on economics and spend twenty minutes on two pages. That is hard, but the effort is going into decoding. An easier book first, with a recall attempt after each chapter, puts the effort where it does the work.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A blade drawn across a stone at an awkward angle. The resistance is the point, as long as the angle is one the hand can hold.'},{kind:'try',text:'After your next study session, ignore how smooth it felt. Close the book and test what stayed. Next time, split the session in two with a gap, or mix in a second topic, and test again.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You are studying for an exam. Which session is most likely to help, even though it feels worst?',options:['Mixing three topics and recalling answers before checking','Re-reading one topic\u2019s notes until they feel easy','Copying out a summary of the chapter you know best','Watching a clear lecture on a topic you already know'],answer:0,why:'Mixing and recalling feel harder at the time and often leave more behind. Smoothness is a poor guide to what worked.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE OVERCORRECTION',belief:'If difficulty helps learning, then harder is always better.',reality:'The piece says the useful difficulties are fairly specific, and the difficulty has to be the right size. Effort spent recalling, or telling one topic from another, does the work. Effort spent merely decoding material far beyond you does not.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Conditions that make learning feel harder, such as spacing, mixing topics and recalling, often improve what remains while feeling worse. But the helpful difficulty is specific: effort spent recalling and comparing, not effort spent decoding.'},{kind:'term',word:'Blocked practice',meaning:'Finishing one topic before starting the next. It feels smooth and productive. The piece sets it against mixing related topics, which feels slower and tends to leave more behind when you are tested later.'},{kind:'scale',low:'Smooth: fluent, less retained',high:'Punishing: decoding, little retained',note:'Retention is best in between, where the effort goes into recalling and comparing rather than decoding.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The selection problem',text:'Judge study by how smooth it felt and you drift towards re-reading and away from spacing, mixing and recall, because those feel worse at the time. The feedback you trust points the wrong way. Hence the piece\u2019s instruction: stop using smoothness as evidence that a session worked.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why these three',text:'The piece gives one reason for all of them. Spacing, mixing and recalling each force effortful retrieval and comparison, which smooth review skips. Mixing makes you tell one topic from another; recall makes you produce material rather than recognise it; spacing makes you fetch what has started to fade. That is the effort worth having.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The right size is easier to describe than to find. Telling useful effort from mere decoding is a judgement about yourself, and the fluency illusion from idea 1 can make a text feel followed when it was not. The piece says these methods tend to help, not that they always do. And difficulty caused by bad writing is not useful; it is only bad writing.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A blade on a whetstone at a difficult angle. The resistance sharpens it, but only at an angle the hand can hold steady.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these misuses the idea that difficulty helps?',options:['Starting a new subject on its most advanced book, to maximise effort','Spacing revision across a week instead of one long evening','Mixing problem types in practice instead of one type at a time','Reading an intermediate text before tackling the classic'],answer:0,why:'The useful difficulties are specific. Far beyond your competence, the effort goes into merely decoding, and the piece says that effort does not do the work.'}]}},'reading-that-sticks_4':{pictureCaption:'A path walked once before, half grown over since. Finding it again takes effort, and treading it again is what keeps it open.',bridge:'Idea 3 listed spacing among the useful difficulties. This idea is about when to come back: after a gap, not straight away.',basics:{title:'Come back later, not straight away',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO LEARN A DANCE',text:'Say two friends learn the same dance. Sam does it five times in a row on Monday. Ali does it once on Monday, once on Wednesday and once on Saturday. On Monday, Sam looks better. A week later, Ali is more likely to still know it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Coming back to something after a gap works better than going over it again straight away. And the gap can be longer than you think.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the gap helps',text:'Straight away, it is all still fresh. Going over it is easy, so it does little. After a gap, you have forgotten a bit. Pulling it back takes effort. That effort seems to be what helps it stick.'},{kind:'flow',nodes:['Learn it','Wait a few days','Forget a little','Work to bring it back','It sticks better'],note:'The bit of forgetting is not a problem. It may even be part of how it works.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A path walked a while ago, now half grown over. Finding it takes a little work. Walking it again keeps it open.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'If I wait, I will forget it, so I should go over it now.',reality:'Forgetting a bit is fine. Coming back after a gap and reaching for it is what helps you keep it.'},{kind:'try',text:'Learned something today you want to keep? Test yourself on it tomorrow, then again next week. Try to remember it before you look.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You have three days before a quiz. Which plan uses this idea?',options:['A short go each day, trying to remember first','Three hours on the night before the quiz','Reading your notes five times in a row today'],answer:0,why:'Short goes with gaps between them work better. Each time you reach for it, and that helps it stick.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'TWO SCHEDULES',text:'Two people read the same article on Monday. One reads it twice in a row. The other reads it once, then on Thursday tries to recall its points before glancing back. On the piece\u2019s account the second keeps more, though Thursday felt slower and harder.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Returning to material after a gap beats returning immediately, and the gap can be surprisingly long. A single well-timed revisit is worth several consecutive ones.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Immediate re-read',items:['Everything still active','Easy and quick','Tells you little']},right:{title:'Revisit after a gap',items:['Partly forgotten','Slow and effortful','The retrieval strengthens it']},note:'The effort appears to be what does the work.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Back on a path half grown over since the last walk. It is slower going than a fresh one, and pushing through is what clears it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Settled effect, contested mechanism',text:'The piece is careful here. Why spacing works is contested; that it works is not. One account it offers is that a gap allows partial forgetting, so the return requires retrieval, and retrieval is what strengthens retention.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it is hard to adopt',text:'The delayed pass feels less efficient. It is slower and more effortful than the immediate one, and the effort appears to be what is doing the work. The piece notes the same pattern runs through everything that actually improves learning.'},{kind:'steps',title:'The modest version',steps:['Read it once, properly.','About a day later, recall it before looking.','About a week later, do it again.','Each time, check what you missed.']},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one thing from this week you want to keep. Set two reminders: tomorrow and next week. At each one, recall first, then look.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Which revision plan applies the idea?',options:['One pass now, and a recall attempt three days later','Three passes tonight, while it is all still fresh','One long pass on the night before you need it','A pass now, and another one an hour later'],answer:0,why:'Returning after a gap beats returning straight away. On one account, the gap allows some forgetting, and retrieving it strengthens retention.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'BEFORE THE ARGUMENT',prompt:'You have time for two readings of a chapter. When should the second happen, for the best retention?',options:['Straight after the first','An hour later','A few days later'],answer:2,reveal:'The piece says returning after a gap beats returning immediately, and that the gap can be surprisingly long. One well-timed revisit is worth several consecutive ones.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Space the second look. An immediate re-read is easy and uninformative because everything is still active. After a gap you have to retrieve, and one account is that this retrieval is what strengthens retention.'},{kind:'term',word:'Spacing',meaning:'Spreading study of the same material over time instead of packing it into one block, as in a second look a day after the first rather than straight away.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'ONE ACCOUNT',nodes:['Read it once','A gap: partial forgetting','Return: recall before looking','Effortful retrieval','Stronger retention'],note:'The step that feels like loss is the one that sets up the gain.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The grass has crept back over the path since the last walk. Re-treading it now takes effort, and that effort is what keeps it open.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What is and is not settled',text:'The piece draws a careful line: the effect is not contested, the mechanism is. It then offers one account, that the retrieval needed after a gap is what strengthens memory. That fits its case for retrieval in idea 2, but by its own admission it is not the settled explanation.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How long a gap',text:'The practical version is modest: a second pass about a day later and another about a week later, each time recalling before looking rather than re-reading from the top. Beyond saying the gap can be surprisingly long, the piece offers no formula.'},{kind:'limits',text:'A gap can be too long. If nothing can be retrieved, the revisit is just a first reading again, which is why a short first gap makes sense. Spacing also needs a plan that survives real life: ignored reminders space nothing. And the day-then-week timing is a rough rule, not a schedule the piece claims is best.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these misreads the idea?',options:['Waiting a year before the first revisit, so the effort is greatest','Reviewing new vocabulary the next day, then the next week','Recalling a chapter\u2019s points days later, before rereading it','Spreading three hours of revision across a whole week'],answer:0,why:'On the account the piece offers, the gap works through partial forgetting. Wait until nothing can be retrieved and the revisit is a fresh start, not spacing.'}]}},'reading-that-sticks_5':{pictureCaption:'Someone rebuilds the mechanism piece by piece for a listener across the bench. Copying its surface would not do; they need its structure.',bridge:'Idea 2 tested what you can bring back. Explaining it to someone is the hardest form of that test, and so the most informative.',basics:{title:'Explain it to someone',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'AT DINNER',text:'You watched a great show about volcanoes. At dinner, your little brother asks how they work. You open your mouth and find you are not sure. You knew it while you watched. Now you can\u2019t put it in order.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Explaining something in your own words is the best test of whether you know it. It shows the gaps straight away. And it makes you sort out what matters most.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it works',text:'When you read, you can just nod along. When you explain, you have to choose what comes first and what the point is. If you don\u2019t know, you notice at once.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'This person is building the machine again for someone across the bench. To do it, they need to know how the parts fit, not just how it looks.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Reading',items:['You can nod along','Gaps stay hidden']},right:{title:'Explaining',items:['You must put it in order','Gaps show up at once']}},{kind:'try',text:'Tonight, tell someone at home one thing you learned today, in your own words. If they look puzzled, that is the part to check.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You want to check if you really know how plants make their food. What is the best test?',options:['Explain it in your own words to a friend','Read the page again until it feels clear','Copy the drawing out of the book neatly'],answer:0,why:'Explaining shows you the gaps straight away. Reading again only makes it feel familiar.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE HARDEST TEST',title:'Why explanation is hard to fake',text:'Recognition and familiarity are enough to follow a text. They are not enough to rebuild its argument for someone who will ask questions. So when you explain, the gaps surface at once, and exactly where they are. The piece calls it the most demanding test available. So it is also the one that tells you most.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Restate what you read in your own words, to an actual person. Explaining forces you to find the structure rather than the phrasing. And it exposes the gaps at once.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'OVER COFFEE',text:'A friend asks about the book you finished last week. You start with the second chapter, double back to the first, and realise mid-sentence that you cannot say what the main claim was. Her puzzled look tells you exactly where you lost it.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Reading',items:['Follows the author\u2019s order','Borrows the author\u2019s phrasing','Lets vagueness pass']},right:{title:'Explaining',items:['You decide what comes first','You find your own structure','Confusion shows at once']}},{kind:'explain',title:'Why a real listener matters',text:'A summary nobody reads permits vagueness. A person\u2019s confusion is immediate feedback about exactly which part was not understood, including by you. Even so, the piece says the listener is largely a device. Writing as though for them does most of the work.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Hands rebuilding a mechanism for someone across the bench. You cannot rebuild what you only recognise; you need to know how it fits.'},{kind:'try',text:'Explain what you just read to somebody today. If nobody is around, write it as though you were about to send it to them.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Which of these tells you most about what you understood from a report?',options:['Explaining its argument to a colleague who asks questions','Writing a private summary that only you will read','Re-reading the conclusion and its key figures','Highlighting the parts that seem to matter most'],answer:0,why:'A listener\u2019s questions and confusion show exactly where your understanding breaks. The others let vagueness pass.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE SUMMARY THAT HID IT',text:'You finish a chapter and write a summary for your notes. It reads well. Then you explain the chapter to a friend, who asks why the second point follows from the first. You do not know. The summary had kept the author\u2019s phrasing, and the phrasing had hidden the gap.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Restating material in your own words, to an actual person, is the most demanding test of understanding and so the most informative. It forces you to find the structure rather than the phrasing, and the gaps surface immediately.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Two things explaining does',text:'It tests: familiarity is enough to follow a text but not to rebuild its argument for someone who will ask questions. And it reorganises: you must decide what comes first, what can go and what the point is. That is structural work passive reading never demands.'},{kind:'equation',parts:['Your own words','A listener\u2019s questions'],result:'The gaps, exposed',note:'Own words remove the borrowed phrasing. Questions remove the vagueness.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Real or imagined listener',text:'The piece holds two positions. A real listener matters more than it seems, because confusion is immediate feedback. Yet the person is largely a device, and writing as though for them does most of the work. What both need is the pressure of someone who could ask.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Explaining can be faked more than the piece allows. A fluent talker can satisfy a polite listener with confident phrasing, and an imagined listener asks nothing awkward. The test is only as hard as its questions. Explaining to someone who knows less can also reward simplifications that quietly become errors.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What did you read this week that you could not explain to a friend in three sentences? Which sentence would fail first?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE WEAK VERSION',prompt:'Which version of the method is weakest?',options:['Talking it through with a friend who nods politely and asks nothing','Writing an explanation addressed to a named colleague','Explaining to a friend who stops you whenever they are lost','Teaching the idea to a small group who then try to use it'],answer:0,why:'The method works through pressure: questions and visible confusion. A listener who only nods removes the feedback that exposes the gaps.'}]}},'reading-that-sticks_6':{pictureCaption:'A few well-worn books in daily use beside a tall, untouched stack. The stack is the bigger number; the worn ones get used.',bridge:'Ideas 2, 4 and 5 all cost time. This one does the sum: that time should come out of how much you read.',basics:{title:'Read less, keep more',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE READING CHART',belief:'The more books you read, the more you know.',reality:'Only if you keep them. Five books you remember are worth more than thirty you have forgotten.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE SUMMER CHALLENGE',text:'Your class has a summer reading chart. One girl reads thirty books and gets a sticker for each. By September she can\u2019t tell you about most of them. Another reads five, and can tell you all about each one.'},{kind:'idea',text:'What counts is not how many books you read. It is how much you can bring back out. So read fewer, and spend the time on keeping them.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A few books are worn from use. The tall stack looks as if it was never opened. The stack is bigger, but the worn ones are the ones that help.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the count tricks us',text:'A number is easy to show off: *I read thirty books!* What you kept has no number. So people chase the number, and the keeping gets left out.'},{kind:'try',text:'When you finish a book, don\u2019t grab the next one yet. First, tell someone what it was about. Then look back at one part you forgot.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Which reader will know more by next year?',options:['Ana, who reads five books and talks about each one','Ben, who reads thirty and never thinks of them again','Both the same, since they both did the reading'],answer:0,why:'Five books you can bring back beat thirty you have lost. Keeping is what counts.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE YEAR\u2019S TOTAL',prompt:'Reader A finishes thirty books and recalls almost none of them. Reader B finishes five, using recall, spacing and explanation. Who ends the year with more?',options:['Reader A, by twenty-five books','Reader B, by five books','Roughly even'],answer:1,reveal:'The piece\u2019s arithmetic: thirty books read and forgotten leave roughly nothing, while five kept leave five books\u2019 worth of usable material. That is five more than the thirty.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Reading fewer books with more attention produces more than reading many with less. The time it takes to get them back out is small next to the time already spent reading. The bottleneck was never how fast you read.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'What gets counted',items:['Books finished','Easy to state','Easy to compare']},right:{title:'What gets used',items:['Ideas you can recall','No number attached','Invisible to others']},note:'The obstacle, the piece says, is that the count is visible and the retention is not.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why retrieval is cheap',text:'A book takes hours to read. Recalling it, coming back to it after a gap and explaining it to someone take a fraction of that. Skipping them to save time risks wasting the hours already spent.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A few worn books in constant use beside a tall, untouched stack. The stack wins on count. The worn few win on everything else.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The reframe',text:'Treat finishing a book as the beginning of the work, not its completion. The piece says this changes the pace substantially, and changes what the pace is for: not a total, but what you can use.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before you start your next book, take the last one you finished. Write its three main ideas from memory, then check. Only then pick up the new one.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A colleague reads fifty books a year but struggles to apply any of them. What does the idea suggest?',options:['Read fewer, and spend the time recalling and explaining each','Read faster, to fit in more books and meet more ideas','Keep the pace, since the ideas will sink in over time','Switch to shorter books that are easier to remember'],answer:0,why:'The bottleneck is not speed. Without getting them back out, the books leave little behind, however many there are.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE YEAR-END LIST',text:'In December you post your reading total: thirty-two books. Someone asks which one changed how you work. You name one, then struggle to say what it argued. The total is easy to state. What the thirty-two left behind has no number at all.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Fewer books, read with retrieval, spacing and explanation, produce more than many books read and forgotten. The count is visible and the retention is not, which is why this arithmetic is unfashionable.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The piece\u2019s arithmetic',text:'Thirty books read and forgotten leave roughly nothing. Five read with retrieval, spacing and explanation leave five books\u2019 worth of usable material, five more than the thirty. The numbers are illustrations; the structure is the claim.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE COUNT WINS',nodes:['The count is visible','Counts get compared','Pace rises to raise it','Retrieval is skipped','Little is kept'],note:'Each step makes sense if the count is the goal. The piece\u2019s move is to change the goal.'},{kind:'keyline',text:'Finishing a book is where the work begins, not where it ends.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it comes last',text:'This idea gathers the others. Fluency misleads; retrieval works; useful difficulty and spacing help; explaining is the hardest test. All of them cost time. The arithmetic says where that time should come from: the number of books.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The arithmetic is too clean. Books read and not recalled are rarely worth nothing: they leave background familiarity, vocabulary and a sense of where to look, and some reading is for pleasure, not retention. Breadth also has value when you do not yet know which book matters. The claim holds best for books you read in order to use.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Worn books in use at one end of the shelf, a pristine stack at the other. The piece\u2019s whole argument, arranged by wear.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'Someone objects that reading widely is how you find the few books worth keeping. What is the best response?',options:['Fair: skim widely to choose, then read the chosen few properly','Wrong: a book you have forgotten contributed nothing at all','Fair: so the real answer is simply to read faster','Wrong: nobody should read more than five books a year'],answer:0,why:'The piece\u2019s point is about books you mean to keep. Breadth can select them; retrieval is what makes the selected ones last.'}]}}}},2999,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'repair-not-victory_1':{pictureCaption:'Something has just cracked. The fall is over, and it is not the news. The news is whether the hands reaching in will mend it.',basics:{title:'It\u2019s the making up that counts',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TWO GOOD FRIENDS',prompt:'You want to guess if two friends will still be close next year. What should you watch?',options:['How often they fall out','What they do just after they fall out'],answer:1,reveal:'Friends who stay close still fall out. The big clue is what comes next. Does one of them move back? Does the other let them?'},{kind:'idea',text:'Close friends and families still argue. What counts most is the few minutes after. Does someone move back towards the other? And is that move taken?'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Something just broke. That part is over. What counts now is the hands that reach in to fix it.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'AFTER THE GAME',text:'You and your brother fight over a video game. You both storm off. Ten minutes later he comes back. He asks if you want some toast. That is him moving back. What you say next matters a lot.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why counting fights does not help',text:'People who care about each other will not agree on everything. So there will be fights. Counting them tells you less than you think. The ten minutes after a fight tell you much more.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you fall out with someone, watch the next ten minutes. Who moves back first? Could it be you?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Lily and Kai had a big fight at lunch. Which sign is the best news for them?',options:['Kai saves Lily a seat on the bus, and she takes it','They had not had a fight for a whole month','Lily won the fight fair and square'],answer:0,why:'Kai moved back, and Lily let him. That tells you more than how often they fight.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'TWO COUPLES YOU KNOW',text:'One couple you know almost never argues in front of anyone. The other bickers about the route, the heating, the dishwasher. Asked which will last, you would probably bet on the quiet pair. The research this piece draws on suggests that bet tells you less than you would think.'},{kind:'idea',text:'How often a close couple disagrees predicts less than you would expect. What separates the relationships that last is the minutes after things go wrong: whether someone moves back towards the other, and whether the move is accepted.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE MINUTES THAT MATTER',nodes:['Something goes wrong','One person makes a move back','The other accepts it','The fight stops growing'],note:'Break either middle link and the fight carries on. Both people have a part in repair.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why counting fights misleads',text:'Two people who share a life will disagree, so the bare fact of conflict says little about any one couple. What varies is the recovery. A pair who can come back from a bad evening can absorb the next one. The number of bad evenings matters less.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The thing has already cracked, and that is not the news. The news is whether the hands reaching in are moving towards a repair.'},{kind:'try',text:'After your next disagreement, notice the first small move either of you makes back. Name it to yourself. Then notice whether it was taken.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A friend is worried because she and her partner argue most weeks. Which question would tell her the most?',options:['When one of you reaches back afterwards, does the other take it?','Do other couples you know argue less often than you two?','Which of you usually turns out to be right in the end?','Are the weekly arguments mostly about the same thing?'],answer:0,why:'Frequency predicts less than people expect. What separates lasting relationships in this research is the move back, and whether it is accepted.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHERE THE PIECE STARTS',text:'This piece is built on observational research on couples: studies that watch how people actually behave when they disagree. Its opening claim is that disagreement itself is a weak signal. What distinguishes relationships that last is what happens in the minutes after things go wrong.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Conflict frequency predicts little about whether a close relationship lasts. The stronger signal is repair: after things go wrong, does either person make a move back towards the other, and is that move accepted?'},{kind:'term',word:'Observational research',meaning:'Studies that record what people actually do, rather than assigning them to conditions. They can show what tends to go with what. On their own, they cannot show what causes what.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why repair, not frequency',text:'Any two people with shared lives will disagree, so the presence of conflict does little to tell one couple from another. What varies is recovery. And repair has two halves, an offer and an acceptance. Where offers are made and taken, a bad evening is absorbed. Where they are not, bad evenings pile up.'},{kind:'equation',parts:['A move back','Its acceptance'],result:'Repair',note:'Neither half is enough on its own. The next idea takes both: making the move badly, and accepting it anyway.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The piece is careful here, and so should you be. This literature is observational, and some of its more precise claims have not replicated well. The broad finding about repair is the part that has held up best. Read it as a tendency across many couples, not a forecast for any one, and not as proof that repair causes the lasting.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of a disagreement with someone close that ended well. What was the first move back, and who made it?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'READ THE CLAIM CAREFULLY',prompt:'Someone sums up the piece: \u201cCouples who repair well are guaranteed to last.\u201d What is the best correction?',options:['It is a tendency in observational data: repair predicts, it does not guarantee','It is backwards: couples who rarely argue are the ones guaranteed to last','It is right, because the repair finding has replicated in every study','It is too weak: repair is the only thing a relationship needs'],answer:0,why:'The piece claims repair predicts better than frequency, from observational research, and is candid that some precise claims have not replicated. Prediction is not a guarantee.'}]}},'repair-not-victory_2':{pictureCaption:'Fists still on the table, and a mug of tea pushed across it. Not an apology, but plainly an offer to stop.',bridge:'Idea 1 said the move back matters most. This one is about what that move usually looks like: not much.',basics:{title:'Making up is often clumsy',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'AT THE KITCHEN TABLE',picture:!0,text:'You and your dad just had a row at the kitchen table. You are both still cross. Then he slides a mug of hot chocolate across to you. He has not said sorry.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'What is he doing?',options:['Trying to end the fight','Pretending it did not happen','Just making a drink'],answer:0,reveal:'He is trying to stop the fight. It is not a sorry, and it is a bit clumsy. But it points one way: back towards you.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When someone tries to make up, it often comes out clumsy. A bad joke. A snack. A new topic. Don\u2019t judge how well they did it. Look at which way it points.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Making up in films',items:['A big, perfect sorry','Just the right words','Tears and a hug']},right:{title:'Making up at home',items:['A silly joke','\u201cWant some toast?\u201d','Talk about something else']},note:'The ones on the right count too. They are just harder to spot.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Two jobs for you',text:'First, when you want to make up, just try. Don\u2019t wait for the right words. While you wait, the fight goes on. Second, when someone else tries, take it. Even if it was clumsy.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time a row goes on too long, make one small move. Offer a snack, or crack a joke. It does not have to be good.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'In the middle of a fight, your friend says, \u201cDid you see the match last night?\u201d What might it mean?',options:['She might be trying to end the fight','She does not care that you are upset','She has forgotten what the fight was about'],answer:0,why:'A new topic can be a clumsy way to make up. Check that before you decide she is dodging.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'A real repair looks like an apology. Anything less is a dodge.',reality:'Attempts to calm a fight are often clumsy: a joke that lands badly, an offer of tea, an abrupt change of subject. What matters is the direction. Someone is trying to leave the fight.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A repair attempt is any move to calm a conflict while it is still going on. How well it is made matters far less than whether the other person accepts it.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'TUESDAY, HALF PAST NINE',text:'You are mid-argument about money. Out of nowhere your partner says the neighbour\u2019s cat is on the shed roof again. You hear a dodge, and say so. The argument runs another hour. That remark may have been the exit, offered badly.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why waiting for a good one fails',text:'A graceful repair needs calm, and calm is exactly what is missing mid-argument. If you wait until you can do it well, the escalation carries on while you wait. So make the attempt now, badly if need be.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Fists still on the table, and a mug pushed across it. No words of sorry, and still unmistakably an offer.'},{kind:'try',text:'When someone changes the subject mid-argument, ask before you react: could they be reaching rather than dodging? If it might be, take it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Mid-row, your housemate makes a weak joke about the state of the kitchen. Which response applies the idea?',options:['Laugh, even slightly, and let the temperature drop','Point out that joking is a way of avoiding the issue','Hold out for a proper apology before you ease off','Ignore it and finish the point you were making'],answer:0,why:'The joke may be an exit. Accepting a clumsy attempt is what lets it work. Refusing it on quality is how a fixable row stops being fixable.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'THE KEY TERM',word:'Repair attempt',meaning:'Any move by either person to de-escalate a conflict while it is still going on. It need not be an apology, or even mention the issue.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The quality of a repair attempt is almost irrelevant to whether it works. What decides the outcome is whether the other person accepts it. So the attempt should not wait to be good, and a poor one should not be refused.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the piece claims',text:'The attempts that work are frequently clumsy: a bad joke, an abrupt change of subject, an awkward apology that does not quite address the issue. The piece presents this as the research finding that matters. The signal is not the content but the direction.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO HALVES, TWO FAILURES',left:{title:'Making the attempt',items:['Failure: waiting until you can do it well','Meanwhile the escalation continues','Fix: make it now, clumsily']},right:{title:'Receiving it',items:['Failure: refusing it as not enough','The exit closes','Fix: accept the direction, not the polish']}},{kind:'explain',title:'Why receiving is the harder half',text:'The piece calls receiving the more useful half. A poorly made olive branch is easy to miss, or to read as insufficient, especially by someone still angry. Refusing it on quality grounds is how conflicts that could have been repaired stop being repairable.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Not every change of subject is a reach. Some are evasion, and a joke that always arrives just as one issue is raised may be exactly that. Accepting a repair ends the escalation, not the issue: the subject still needs returning to, which idea 5 takes up. And no one owes acceptance to a move that skips past harm done to them.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of a recent argument. Was there an attempt to leave it that you missed or refused at the time? What did it look like?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISREADING',prompt:'Which of these gets the idea wrong?',options:['Refusing a joke mid-row because it did not address the real issue','Offering tea mid-argument without waiting for the right words','Taking a change of subject as a possible exit, not proof of dodging','Accepting a clumsy truce, then returning to the issue later'],answer:0,why:'Refusing an attempt because it was not good enough is the very failure the piece names. The other three are the idea applied.'}]}},'repair-not-victory_3':{pictureCaption:'A hole in a wall can be patched. A beam cut through is damage to the structure. A complaint is the first kind; criticism, the second.',bridge:'Ideas 1 and 2 were about how a disagreement ends. This one is about the words inside it, and which ones leave nothing to repair.',basics:{title:'About what they did, or who they are',beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO SAY IT',left:{title:'About what they did',items:['\u201cYou didn\u2019t feed the cat.\u201d','\u201cYou said you\u2019d call, and you didn\u2019t.\u201d']},right:{title:'About who they are',items:['\u201cYou never do anything.\u201d','\u201cYou always let me down.\u201d']},note:'The left side is about one thing that happened. The right side is about the whole person.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Telling someone what they did is a complaint, and it can be fixed. Blaming who they are is called criticism. It leaves them nothing to fix.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why \u201cnever\u201d makes it worse',text:'Say, \u201cYou didn\u2019t feed the cat,\u201d and your brother can go and feed it. Done. Say, \u201cYou never do anything,\u201d and what can he do? Nothing he does now can undo *never*. So he fights back instead.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A small hole in a wall is easy to patch. A beam cut right through is much worse. Words about who someone is are the cut beam.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Even worse: contempt',text:'Contempt is worse still, and it is not only in what you say. Rolling your eyes, a mocking voice, or talking down to someone can all say *I am better than you* without the words.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you complain, check your sentence. Does it have *always* or *never* in it? Swap that for the one thing that happened.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend forgot your birthday. Which one is a complaint, not criticism?',options:['\u201cYou forgot my birthday, and that hurt.\u201d','\u201cYou always forget about me.\u201d','\u201cYou\u2019re the worst friend ever.\u201d'],answer:0,why:'It is about one thing that happened, so she can say sorry and make it right. The other two are about who she is.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE SAME GRIEVANCE',prompt:'Your partner said they would book the car in for its service, and did not. Which sentence is likelier to start a worse fight?',options:['\u201cYou didn\u2019t book the car in, and now I\u2019m stuck.\u201d','\u201cYou never do anything you say you will.\u201d','Neither: the grievance is the same'],answer:1,reveal:'The grievance is identical. But the first is about an event, and they can fix it by tomorrow. The second is about their character. No action can answer it, so they defend themselves instead.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A complaint is about a behaviour: this happened, and it affected me. Criticism is about the person: you are the kind of person who does this. The first can be resolved. The second leaves nothing to fix, so it escalates.'},{kind:'explain',title:'One step further: contempt',text:'Contempt goes beyond criticism by adding superiority. It shows up as sarcasm, mockery, eye-rolling and a tone of talking down. Each carries a verdict on the person from above, which is why it lands harder than any specific complaint.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it matters so much',text:'The piece cites the psychologist John Gottman\u2019s research on couples, which found contempt to be the strongest single predictor of a relationship ending, and not by a small margin. So watch how a complaint is said, rather than how often you complain.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'ONE GRIEVANCE, TWO REGISTERS',low:'About the event: what happened, and its effect',high:'About the person: what kind of person they are',note:'The same grievance can sit anywhere on this line. A few words and a tone move it.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before you raise something that is bothering you, write the sentence down. Strike out *always* and *never*. If what is left is one event and its effect on you, say that.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'The bins have gone out late for the third week running. Which version keeps the complaint survivable?',options:['\u201cThe bins went out late again, and it annoyed me.\u201d','\u201cOf course the bins are late. Why would they not be?\u201d','\u201cYou are incapable of remembering the simplest thing.\u201d','\u201cI suppose I\u2019ll just do the bins myself, as usual.\u201d'],answer:0,why:'Complaining often is survivable. The other three turn the same grievance into a verdict on the person, by sarcasm or by a sweeping claim.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'The danger in a relationship is complaining too often.',reality:'The piece argues that frequency is the wrong thing to police. Complaining more often is survivable. What does the damage is the register: the same complaint delivered as a verdict on the person. The difference is usually a few words and a tone.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Complaint and criticism are structurally different acts. A complaint concerns an event and can be resolved by action. Criticism concerns the person, conveys a general assessment, and leaves the other with nothing to fix. Contempt adds superiority on top.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'THE STRUCTURE',left:{title:'Complaint',items:['Object: one behaviour','Claim: this happened, and it affected me','Answerable by an action']},right:{title:'Criticism',items:['Object: the person','Claim: you are this kind of person','Answerable by no action']}},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it escalates',text:'A sentence like *you never do what you say* cannot be disproved by anything done today. One kept promise does not undo *never*. With no repair on offer, the only moves left are defence or counter-attack. The escalation is not a lapse of temper. It follows from the shape of the sentence.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The evidence the piece cites',text:'It reports the psychologist John Gottman\u2019s finding that contempt is the strongest single predictor of relationship dissolution, with an effect it says is not subtle. Keep idea 1\u2019s caution in mind: this is observational research, and its broad findings have held up better than its precise ones.'},{kind:'limits',text:'A rule against *always* can be overdone. A pattern is real and fair to raise; raise it as a run of events, not as a verdict on character. And policing words without tone achieves little. A sentence carefully kept to the event, said with a sneer, is still contempt.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Take a complaint you have made more than once. Rewrite it as one event and its effect. What did you have to remove?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A manager tells a colleague, \u201cThis report was late, and I had to delay the meeting.\u201d Later: \u201cThis is typical of you.\u201d Which sentence did the damage?',options:['The second: a verdict on the person that no action can answer','The first: naming the delay out loud is already a personal attack','Neither: both describe the same lateness, so they are equivalent','Both equally: any criticism of someone\u2019s work attacks them'],answer:0,why:'The first names an event and its effect, which can be fixed. The second generalises to character, which leaves nothing to fix.'}]}},'repair-not-victory_4':{pictureCaption:'The papers are squared off and the point is won. Across the table, the chair is empty and the tea untouched.',bridge:'Idea 3 kept the complaint about the event. This one asks what you are aiming at once the argument has started.',basics:{title:'Winning the row, losing the friend',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE BEST PLAYER',picture:!0,text:'You and your best friend argue about who is the best footballer. You have all the facts. You win. She goes quiet and says, \u201cFine, you\u2019re right.\u201d Then she walks home on her own.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'Did she change her mind?',options:['Yes, she said you were right','Probably not'],answer:1,reveal:'She gave in. That is not the same as agreeing. A row you give in to does not go away. It tends to come back later, as a new fight about something else.'},{kind:'idea',text:'With someone you want to keep, winning a row is the wrong goal. They may give in, but giving in is not agreeing. And the row is often about something else.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What was it really about?',text:'Maybe the football row was not about football at all. Maybe she felt you never listen to her. More facts won\u2019t fix that. Asking what it is really about might.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A GAME OF CATCH',text:'In tennis you try to make the other person miss. In a game of catch you both try to keep the ball going. With a friend, a row should be more like catch.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you are winning a row with someone you care about, stop. Ask, \u201cWhat is this really about for you?\u201d Then listen.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You and your brother argue about whose turn it is to pick the film. You prove it is your turn. He sulks. What would this idea say?',options:['Ask him what is really bugging him','Remind him once more that you were right','Pick your film, since you won fair and square'],answer:0,why:'You won the point, but he is still upset. Something else may be going on, and asking is how you find out.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE THIRD TIME THIS MONTH',text:'You are arguing, again, about whose turn it was to call the plumber. You have the texts. You read them out. Your partner concedes. Ten days later the same argument breaks out, this time about the car insurance.'},{kind:'idea',text:'In a relationship you intend to keep, trying to win an argument mistakes the objective. What is at stake is not the point but whether the two of you can handle the next disagreement.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHERE A WIN GOES',nodes:['You win the point','They concede under pressure','A concession is not agreement','It returns in different clothes'],loop:!0,note:'This is why the same argument keeps coming back with a new subject.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'You have the papers, and the point. Across the table, an empty chair and a cup of tea nobody drank. A win can look like this.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Aiming to win',items:['One point resolved','One person who gave way','Hard to raise again']},right:{title:'Aiming at a shared account',items:['What happened, as both see it','What each person needs','Either can raise it again']},note:'The alternative is not giving in. The original point may stay open, and that is allowed.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The test',text:'Ask what the other person could do afterwards. If they cannot come back to the subject without reopening a defeat, it was not resolved. It is still live, and now harder to name.'},{kind:'try',text:'When you notice you are winning an argument with someone you mean to keep, stop adding evidence. Ask what it is really about.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'After a long row about holiday plans, your partner says, \u201cFine, we\u2019ll do it your way.\u201d What does the idea suggest?',options:['Treat it as a concession, and ask what the holiday stood for','Book it quickly, before they have a chance to change their mind','Take it at face value: they agreed, so the matter is settled','Offer to do it their way next year, as a fair trade'],answer:0,why:'A concession under pressure is not agreement, and it tends to come back. Asking what it was about aims at a shared account instead.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE OBJECTIVE, MISNAMED',text:'Treating a disagreement as something to win assumes the point at issue is what is at stake. The piece argues that, with someone you intend to keep, what is at stake is the relationship\u2019s ability to handle the next disagreement. A win can settle the point and damage that.'},{kind:'idea',text:'With someone you mean to keep, winning is the wrong objective. Being proved right and being close are often in tension, and the stated subject is usually not what the argument is about.'},{kind:'term',word:'Concession under pressure',meaning:'Giving up a point because carrying on has become too costly, not because you were persuaded. It ends the exchange without producing agreement.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The evidence sits squared on the table and the point is settled. The chair opposite is empty. The stated objective was met; what was at stake was not.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the same row recurs',text:'The piece observes that a concession is usually retrieved later. The person who gave way still holds the view, and the next disagreement is a chance to reopen it. So the same conflict returns in different clothes, and each round is harder to name than the last.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'If you are not trying to win, you are giving in.',reality:'The alternative is a shared account of what happened and what each person needs. It may leave the original point unresolved. But it leaves both people able to raise it again, which a defeat does not.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Some arguments are about facts that matter: a debt, a medical decision, a child\u2019s safety. There, being right is not vanity, and asking what it is really about can become a way to dodge a plain question. The idea also assumes good faith. It is not advice to yield to someone who habitually wins by pressure.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of an argument you won with someone close. Could they raise the subject again today without reopening a defeat?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY THE TEST',prompt:'Which outcome passes the piece\u2019s test of a real resolution?',options:['The point is still open, but either of you could raise it calmly next week','One of you produced evidence that the other could not answer','You both agreed never to mention the subject again','The person who was wrong apologised and conceded the point'],answer:0,why:'The test is what the other person can do afterwards. A point left open that both can revisit counts. A defeat, however clean, does not.'}]}},'repair-not-victory_5':{pictureCaption:'Not put away, just stuffed out of sight in a drawer that will not shut. A pause nobody comes back to looks like this.',bridge:'Idea 2 said to accept a truce, even a clumsy one. A truce ends the fight, not the issue. This idea is about going back to it.',basics:{title:'Take a break, then come back',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'COOLING OFF',belief:'If you stop a fight and never talk about it again, it has gone away.',reality:'It has not gone. It is just out of sight. It tends to come back later, stuck to some other small thing.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE REMOTE, AGAIN',text:'You and your sister had a row about the TV remote last week. You both stopped. No one went back to it. Today she leaves her bag on your bed, and you shout. The remote row came back, stuck to the bag.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Taking a break from a fight is a good idea. But it only works if you come back and talk once you are calm. So say when you will.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why stop at all?',text:'When you are really angry, your body buzzes and your mind gets narrow. You don\u2019t listen well. You forget what was said. So a break helps. But the talking still has to happen.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'This cloth was not put away. It was pushed in a drawer that will not shut. A fight you never come back to is like that.'},{kind:'steps',title:'A good break',steps:['Say, \u201cI need a break.\u201d','Say when you will come back: \u201cAfter tea.\u201d','Calm down. Do something else.','Come back at that time, and talk.']},{kind:'question',prompt:'You and your friend fall out at break time. You say, \u201cI don\u2019t want to talk now.\u201d What makes this a good pause?',options:['You say you\u2019ll talk after school, and you do','You never bring it up again, so it\u2019s over','You wait for her to bring it up first'],answer:0,why:'A break works when the talk really happens later. Saying when, and then turning up, makes it a plan, not running away.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'FOUR DAYS LATER',text:'On Sunday you both said, \u201cLet\u2019s not do this now.\u201d Sensible. It is Thursday, and neither of you has mentioned it. Tonight a small remark about the washing-up lands far harder than it should, and you both know why.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Pausing a conflict works only if it is genuinely resumed. A pause that turns into avoidance stores the disagreement, and it comes back attached to the next unrelated thing.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why pause at all',text:'Mid-escalation, both people are physically worked up and their thinking narrows. It is a poor state for accuracy, for generosity, even for remembering what was said. So stopping is right. But the return is where the resolution actually happens.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'An escape',items:['\u201cI can\u2019t do this.\u201d','No time given','The issue is stored']},right:{title:'An appointment',items:['\u201cLet\u2019s stop. Saturday morning?\u201d','A time named','The issue is kept live']},note:'A permanent pause adds something worse than the original issue: the knowledge that it cannot be discussed.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Out of sight, not put away. The drawer will not shut on it, and the cloth will still be there next time it is opened.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you call a pause, attach a time to it in the same sentence. Then put that time somewhere you will see it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A row about a family visit is getting heated. Which pause is most likely to lead to repair?',options:['\u201cLet\u2019s stop, and talk it through after dinner tomorrow.\u201d','\u201cI can\u2019t talk about this right now, I\u2019m sorry.\u201d','\u201cLet\u2019s just drop it. It\u2019s honestly not worth it.\u201d','\u201cFine. Do whatever you think is best, then.\u201d'],answer:0,why:'Naming when you will come back turns an escape into an appointment. The others store the issue, and add the sense that it cannot be discussed.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE TOWELS',text:'An argument breaks out over towels left on the floor. It is ferocious, far out of proportion. Halfway through it emerges what it is really about: something said at a family lunch a month ago, paused that afternoon and never resumed.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A pause in a conflict is useful only if later actually arrives. Calling the pause should name the return, so that an escape becomes an appointment. Resolution happens on the return, not in the pause.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE PAUSE THAT WORKS',nodes:['Escalation: aroused and narrowed','Pause, with a time named','Return, calmer','Resolution can begin'],note:'Remove the named time and the chain tends to stop at step two. The issue stays, joined by the sense that it cannot be raised.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the pause is right',text:'During escalation both people are physiologically aroused and cognitively narrowed. The piece names three costs: the state is poor for accuracy, poor for generosity, and poor even for remembering what was actually said. Pressing on means arguing in exactly that state.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the return gets skipped',text:'The piece calls the return the part most often skipped. One plausible reason is that the pause brings relief, and going back threatens it. Each quiet day makes raising it feel more like starting a new fight. Naming the time when you pause commits you before the relief sets in.'},{kind:'limits',text:'A named time is not a magic word. Return while one of you is still worked up and the escalation simply restarts; the appointment may need to move, and moving it openly keeps it an appointment. And not every pause must end in a return. A truly trivial matter can be dropped, if both agree. The failure is a drop only one person chose.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Stop when escalation has narrowed you both.','Name the return in the same breath as the pause.','Resolution happens on the return. The pause only makes it possible.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE FAILURE',prompt:'Which of these is the failure the piece describes?',options:['A pause that was sincere when called, and has quietly lasted a month','Stopping a heated row and agreeing to resume it on Saturday','Moving an agreed return by a day because one of you is still angry','Agreeing together that a trivial point is not worth resuming'],answer:0,why:'Sincerity when pausing is not enough; later has to arrive. The others keep the return real, or drop the issue by shared choice rather than avoidance.'}]}}}},3000,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={republic_1:{pictureCaption:'The other side\u2019s best piece, set in the best light on the bench. You study an argument properly only at its strongest.',basics:{title:'Beat the best version, not the worst',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE CLASS DEBATE',text:'Your class is debating whether school should start later. You are against it. You could pick the silliest thing the other side might say and knock it down. Everyone would laugh. But would you have won?'},{kind:'idea',text:'Before you argue against an idea, say it in its best and strongest form. If you can beat that, you have really won.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'A BOOK FROM LONG AGO',text:'Plato lived in Athens, in Greece, about 2,400 years ago. He wrote a book called the *Republic*. It asks what justice is. Justice means being fair and doing what is right.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The other side gets good lines',text:'In the book, a man named Thrasymachus says justice is just whatever helps the strong. They make the rules to suit themselves, he says. Plato lets him argue hard and well. He does not make him an easy target.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE FOOTBALL PRACTICE',text:'Beating a team of tiny kids feels great. Then you meet a real team and lose. Beating the weak side taught you nothing. You learn by facing the best players they have.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The other side\u2019s best piece, put in the best light. You look hard at it before you plan your own move.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick something you disagree with. Say the other side\u2019s view so well that someone who holds it would nod. Then decide what you think.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend wants a pet dog. You think it is a bad idea. Which way of arguing follows this lesson?',options:['Say the best reasons for a dog, then answer them','Point out that dogs sometimes chew shoes','Say no and quickly change the subject'],answer:0,why:'Answering the best reasons is a real win. Picking on a small point only beats a weak version of their case.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'BEFORE A BIG DECISION',prompt:'You are about to recommend a plan. Which test of it is more useful?',options:['Answering the objections you find weakest','Answering the objection its best critic would make'],answer:1,reveal:'Beating a weak objection proves only that the weak objection is weak. The critic you will actually meet brings the strong one, and you have not prepared for it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Give the view you mean to defeat its strongest form before you answer it. A weak version produces a weak refutation, and leaves you unready for the real opponent.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'ATHENS, BOOK I',text:'The *Republic* opens with a question: what is justice? Thrasymachus breaks in with a hard answer. Justice is whatever serves the stronger: rulers write laws for their own benefit and call obeying them justice. Plato gives him a forceful case, not a straw man.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Then the challenge gets harder',text:'Socrates seems to win. But Glaucon, Plato\u2019s own brother, is not satisfied. He says the win only looked like one, and restates the case better than Thrasymachus did. He asks Socrates to show that justice is good in itself, not just for what it brings.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'The weak version',items:['Easy to knock down','Feels like winning','Leaves you exposed later']},right:{title:'The strongest version',items:['Hard to answer','Shows where you are weak','Prepares you for the real critic']},note:'The rest of the Republic answers a challenge Plato wrote for his own opponents.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The opponent\u2019s best piece, centred in the best light. Study the strongest move against you before you plan your own.'},{kind:'try',text:'In your next proposal, write the case against it as its best advocate would. Show that paragraph to someone who disagrees and ask whether you got it right.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You are recommending a move to a new office. Which write-up applies the idea?',options:['It sets out the strongest case for staying, then answers it','It gives every benefit of moving, in careful detail','It notes in passing that a few staff have doubts','It answers the weakest objection, since that is quickest'],answer:0,why:'Only the case for staying prepares the decision for its real opponent. The others skip the objection or pick the easiest one.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'BOOK I, NEAR THE PIRAEUS',text:'The dialogue opens at a house by Athens\u2019 port. Polemarchus proposes that justice means helping friends and harming enemies, and Socrates takes it apart. Then Thrasymachus, a professional teacher of speaking, breaks in: justice is the advantage of the stronger.'},{kind:'explain',title:'A case made at full strength',text:'Thrasymachus offers no straw position. He argues that rulers make laws in their own interest, that the just man always comes off worse, and that injustice on a large enough scale is admired rather than punished. These are serious claims, and Plato lets them stand in full.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Plato states the position he means to defeat in its strongest form, then has his own characters make it stronger still. The argument is built to survive the real opponent, not a convenient one.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Glaucon raises the stakes',text:'At the start of Book II, Glaucon says Socrates has only seemed to win. He restates the case: justice is a compromise people accept because they are too weak to do wrong safely. He says he does not believe it, but has never heard it properly answered. The rest of the book answers him.'},{kind:'term',word:'Steelmanning',meaning:'A modern name for the practice: stating an opposing view in the strongest form its own supporters would recognise. The opposite of attacking a straw man.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The opponent\u2019s strongest piece in the centre of the bench, in the best light, with the owner\u2019s own work pushed to the edges.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Steelmanning can be a performance: a generous statement of the other side, followed by a reply that never touches it. Glaucon himself treats Socrates\u2019 Book I replies as unconvincing, and many readers agree. It can also stall into endless fairness, where no view is ever rejected. The test is whether the answer meets the strong version.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of a view you reject. Could you state it well enough that someone who holds it would sign your summary?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these only looks like stating the other side at its strongest?',options:['Setting out the opponent\u2019s best argument, then replying to a weaker point','Asking a supporter of the other view to check your summary of it','Rewriting an objection until its advocates say you have it right','Answering the strongest objection even when it costs you a point'],answer:0,why:'Stating the strong case and then answering a weaker one is the performance version. The strong case has to be the one you answer.'}]}},republic_2:{pictureCaption:'A ring too small to inspect sits beside a huge model of itself. The finger points into the large one, where detail can be read.',bridge:'Idea 1 ended on Glaucon\u2019s challenge. To answer it, Socrates first has to see justice clearly, and this is how he tries.',basics:{title:'Too small to see? Look at a bigger one',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TWO SIGNS',prompt:'The same words are on two signs. On one they are tiny. On the other they are huge. Which do you read first?',options:['The tiny ones, up close','The huge ones, then check the tiny ones'],answer:1,reveal:'Read the big ones first. Then you know what the tiny ones say, and you can check. Plato used this very trick.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When something is too small to see clearly, find a bigger thing with the same shape. Look at the big one first. Then check the small one.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'WHAT PLATO DID',text:'Plato lived in Greece about 2,400 years ago. In his book, the *Republic*, friends try to work out what makes a person fair and good. That is hard to see inside one person. So they look at a whole city first.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A tiny ring sits by a huge model of the same ring. The hand points into the big one, where you can see how it is made.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'AT SCHOOL',text:'Your class group starts five projects and finishes none. It is easy to see why. Each time it gets hard, someone says *let\u2019s try a new one.* Now think of the books you start and never finish. Same pattern, just smaller.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why bigger helps',text:'Inside a problem, you are too close to see it. A big version sits outside you. You can walk round it and point at the parts. Then check yourself for the same parts.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You keep falling out with your best friend and don\u2019t know why. Which way uses this idea?',options:['Watch how fights start in your whole class, then look for that in yours','Stop seeing your friend for a while and hope it fades','Promise yourself to be extra nice every day'],answer:0,why:'A whole class is a bigger version of the same thing. The pattern is easier to spot there. Then you can check it in your own friendship.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE PROJECT FOLDER',text:'Your folder holds six half-finished projects. Each was dropped for a good reason at the time. You cannot see a pattern, because you were standing inside every one of those decisions.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When a thing is too close or too small to examine, find a larger instance with the same structure and read it there. Then check whether the pattern holds at the smaller scale.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE SAME THING, LARGER',text:'Now picture a team that launches everything and finishes nothing. From outside you see it at once: starting is rewarded, finishing is not, and every hard middle stretch looks like a reason to begin again. Go back to your folder. Same mechanism.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'PLATO\u2019S MOVE',text:'In Book II of the *Republic*, justice proves hard to see in one person. Socrates suggests looking at a whole city first, as you would read large letters before small ones, and then checking the individual against what they found.'},{kind:'flow',nodes:['Too close to see','Find a larger instance','Read the structure there','Check it back at small scale'],note:'The last step matters. Without the check, the big picture is just a story you have borrowed.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'The larger case is just a metaphor for the smaller one.',reality:'For the technique to work it has to be the same mechanism, not a resemblance. A team that never finishes and a person who never finishes can fail for the same reason. A storm and a temper only look alike.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The tiny ring cannot be read up close. The giant model of it can, and the hand points into the detail there.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick a habit of yours you cannot explain. Find a team or institution that does the same thing at scale. Name the mechanism there, then test it on yourself.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You cannot work out why your household keeps avoiding one obvious conversation. Which applies the idea?',options:['Study a workplace where nobody may say the obvious, then check home against it','Ask each family member separately how they personally feel about it','Read a novel about a family that argues a great deal','Wait until the issue is too big for anyone to ignore'],answer:0,why:'The larger instance has to share the mechanism, here a rule against saying the obvious, and what it shows has to be checked back at home.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'BOOK II, THE PROPOSAL',text:'Glaucon and Adeimantus have just demanded that justice be defended for its own sake. Socrates says the search needs sharp sight. A short-sighted person told that the same text was written larger elsewhere would read the large letters first. So they should look for justice in a city first.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When a structure is too small or too close to examine, find a larger instance of it, read it there, and check the result back at the smaller scale. The larger instance is not a metaphor. It is the same mechanism.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'The city',items:['Rulers who deliberate','Auxiliaries who defend','Producers who supply']},right:{title:'The soul',items:['Reason','Spirit','Appetite']},note:'Book IV claims the same three-part arrangement at both scales, and defines justice in each as every part doing its own work.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What makes it valid',text:'The move works only if the structure really is shared. Plato does not simply assume it. Having found justice in the city, he says that if it looks different in the individual they must go back and test the city again, rubbing the two together until justice shows.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where it turns uncomfortable',text:'The city built to make justice visible is rigid. Poetry is censored, classes are assigned by the rulers and justified by a myth, and a trained class rules. Readers have argued for two thousand years about how seriously Plato meant it as a political proposal.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The analogy can run in a circle. If the city is designed to match the soul, finding the soul in it proves nothing. And scale changes things: a group has politics, money and history that a single mind lacks. A larger instance tells you where to look. It cannot tell you what you will find.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The tiny ring cannot be read up close; the large model can. The method depends on the large one having the same structure.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TEST THE ANALOGY',prompt:'Someone argues: a company is like a body, so cutting staff is like surgery and will heal it. What is wrong with this use of the technique?',options:['It borrows a resemblance, not a shared mechanism, and never checks back','It studies a larger case instead of looking at the company directly','It moves from a larger scale to a smaller one, which reverses the method','It treats a company as more important than the people in it'],answer:0,why:'The technique needs the same structure at both scales, then a check back. A body and a firm only resemble each other.'}]}},republic_3:{pictureCaption:'One hand reaches for the cup; the other grips its wrist. Both belong to the same person. Something in them wants, and something refuses.',bridge:'Idea 2 built a city of three classes to read the soul. This is the soul read back: three parts, the same arrangement.',basics:{title:'Three voices inside you',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'KNOWING IS NOT ENOUGH',belief:'If you know something is bad for you, you will not do it.',reality:'You know too many sweets make you feel sick. You eat them anyway. Knowing is one voice. Wanting is another, and it is often louder.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Plato, a thinker in ancient Greece, said you have three parts inside you. One thinks. One wants things. One cares about being brave and proud. Doing right is hard when they pull different ways.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Meet the three parts',text:'**Reason** is the part that thinks ahead. **Appetite** is the part that wants things now: food, games, fun. **Spirit** is the part that gets fired up. It feels proud, or angry, or ashamed.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE LAST HOT CHOCOLATE',picture:!0,text:'You promised to leave the last hot chocolate. It is right there. One part of you wants it. Another part says no. You feel both at once. Plato said that is how we know they are different parts.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A TEAM',text:'Think of a team. The captain makes the plan. The keen players run hard. Some just want a break. A good team has each doing its own job, with the captain leading.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The helper you forget',text:'Thinking alone is often too weak to beat wanting. But spirit can join in. When you think *I am not the kind of person who cheats*, that is spirit taking the side of reason.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you don\u2019t do something you meant to, don\u2019t say *I\u2019m just weak*. Ask: which part won?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You planned to revise, but you played games all night. What would Plato say happened?',options:['Wanting beat thinking, and pride did not step in','You must not have known that revising mattered','You just have no willpower at all'],answer:0,why:'You knew. The wanting part was louder, and nothing joined the thinking part to stop it.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'ONE IN THE MORNING',text:'This morning you decided to be asleep by eleven. It is now one, and you are still scrolling. You did not forget the plan. You knew, the whole time, exactly what you were doing.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A person is not one will but three parts, reason, spirit and appetite, each wanting different things. Most failures to act well come not from ignorance but from one part wanting something else, loudly.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How Plato knows there are parts',picture:!0,text:'His evidence is ordinary conflict. A thirsty man refuses a drink. Something in him wants it and something refuses, and one thing cannot both pull toward and push away from the same object at once. So there must be at least two parts.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'THE MAN AT THE WALL',text:'Plato tells of a man who saw bodies lying by the place of execution. He wanted to look, and was disgusted at wanting to. He covered his eyes, then gave in, and was furious at his own eyes. That fury is a third part: spirit, siding with reason against appetite.'},{kind:'equation',parts:['Reason','Spirit'],result:'Enough to outvote appetite',note:'Knowing a thing is bad for you has little force on its own. Self-respect is what moves.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Failing to stick to a plan just means weak willpower.',reality:'*Willpower* names the result, not the cause. Name which part won and you can act: change what appetite runs into, or recruit spirit by tying the plan to who you want to be.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you fail to do what you intended, write down which part won and what it wanted. Then ask what would bring spirit in on reason\u2019s side.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You keep breaking a promise to yourself to stop snacking at your desk. Which response draws on Plato\u2019s account?',options:['Tie the habit to self-respect, so spirit joins reason against appetite','Read more about why snacking is bad for your health','Accept that you were simply born without much willpower','Wait for a stretch of time when you feel motivated'],answer:0,why:'More knowledge feeds reason, which was already on side. Spirit is the ally that actually moves.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'THE PART THAT GETS MISSED',word:'Thumos',meaning:'The Greek word usually translated as spirit: the seat of anger, honour and indignation. Neither reason nor appetite, it is the part that bristles at an insult and is ashamed of giving in.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The soul has three parts, reason, spirit and appetite, shown by our conflicts with ourselves. Justice in a person is each part doing its own work under reason, not one part crushing the others.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The argument from conflict',picture:!0,text:'In Book IV Plato argues from a principle: the same thing cannot do or undergo opposites, in the same respect, at the same time. A thirsty man who refuses to drink is drawn toward the drink and held back from it. So the pull and the refusal come from different parts.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'LEONTIUS',text:'Plato\u2019s evidence for spirit is a man named Leontius, who passed bodies lying by the place of execution. He wanted to look, fought it, then ran up and looked, furious at his own eyes. Anger at oneself for giving in: spirit allied with reason against appetite.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Suppression',items:['Reason fights appetite alone','Appetite starved, then rebounds','A soul at war']},right:{title:'Arrangement',items:['Reason leads','Spirit enforces','Appetite supplied, within limits']},note:'Plato\u2019s justice is the right-hand column: a claim about order, not about which part wins a war.'},{kind:'limits',text:'As psychology it is crude: three neat parts for what is really many overlapping systems, and the division conveniently mirrors the class structure of Plato\u2019s city. Spirit is also double-edged. Pride that backs reason can as easily back appetite, or cruelty. Use it as description, not as anatomy.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Recall a recent time you acted against your own judgement. Which part won, and what was spirit doing?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A manager says: our staff know the safety rules, so any breach must be carelessness. What does Plato\u2019s account suggest?',options:['Knowing a rule may be the weakest force; ask what pride and pressure are backing','The staff cannot really know the rules, or they would follow them','Breaches show the rules are badly written and should be simplified','Carelessness is a fourth part of the soul that Plato overlooked'],answer:0,why:'Plato\u2019s point is that knowledge alone has little force. What moves people is which side their pride and their appetites are on in the moment.'}]}},republic_4:{pictureCaption:'A freed prisoner reaches the cave mouth and throws up a hand against the daylight. The climb is the famous part. The way back is harder.',bridge:'Idea 3 put reason in charge of the soul. The cave shows what Plato thinks reason has to climb out of, and what happens when it comes back.',basics:{title:'The cave, and the way back',beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'A STORY PLATO TOLD',text:'Plato was a thinker in Greece, long ago. He told a story about people chained in a cave all their lives. They face a wall. A fire behind them throws shadows onto it. They have seen nothing else, so they think the shadows are the real world.'},{kind:'explain',title:'One gets out',text:'One prisoner is set free and dragged up into the sun. At first the light hurts his eyes. Slowly he sees real trees, real people, and at last the sun.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'He has just reached the light. His hand is up because it hurts to look. Soon he will see the real world.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'He goes back down to tell the others. What happens?',options:['They cheer and ask to be freed','They laugh and don\u2019t believe him'],answer:1,reveal:'In the dark, his eyes no longer work well. He is bad at the shadow game now. The others decide the trip outside ruined him.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When you come back with something new, expect people not to believe you at first. That is not because they are bad. It is because of where they stand.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'AT THE POOL',text:'You spend the holidays learning to swim with your face in the water. Back with friends, you are slower at first. They laugh and say the old way is better. They have only ever seen the old way.'},{kind:'try',text:'Got a new idea? Bring something people can check. Show, don\u2019t just tell.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Mia found a better way to sort the class recycling. The class says her idea is silly. What does the cave story say?',options:['That is normal. Show them a small test that works','She must be wrong if nobody agrees with her','She should explain it louder and for longer'],answer:0,why:'People who have only seen one way often doubt a new one. Something they can check works better than talking harder.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'BACK FROM SIX MONTHS AWAY',text:'You return from six months somewhere that works very differently. At the first meeting you explain what you saw. People are polite, then dismissive. Worse, you have lost the knack of the old routine. You are now visibly worse at the job they can judge.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The famous part of the cave is the climb into daylight. The part that matters is the return: whoever has seen more comes back worse at the old game, and is disbelieved.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE ROUND TRIP',nodes:['Freed and dragged into the light','Blinded, then slowly sees','Returns to the dark','Stumbles at the shadow game','Disbelieved'],note:'Each step follows from the one before. Nobody in the cave has to be stupid for the last one to happen.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the disbelief is predictable',text:'The prisoners are expert at the only game they know: predicting shadows. The returner is now bad at it, and his only evidence is his own word. From where they sit, the trip made him worse. Their doubt is a fact about their position, not their character.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The freed prisoner at the cave mouth, hand raised against the glare. Everything he will say later rests on what he sees here, and nobody below can check it.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Planning the return',steps:['Expect to be worse at the old game for a while.','Bring something they can check, not just your word.','Start with one small change that works on their terms.','Read doubt as a sign of position, not of character.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You come back from a secondment convinced your team\u2019s process is outdated. Your first pitch falls flat. What does the cave suggest?',options:['Run one small test they can check, and expect early doubt','Explain the new approach again, in more detail and with more force','Conclude the team are too closed-minded to be worth persuading','Drop the idea, since the team\u2019s doubt shows it was wrong'],answer:0,why:'The doubt was predictable from where the team stands. Evidence they can check moves people. More explanation is still only your word.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE USUAL READING',belief:'The cave is about enlightenment: escaping illusion into the light.',reality:'That is the ascent, and it is half the image. Plato makes the return compulsory for those who escape, then describes in detail how badly it goes.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'BOOK VII, THE IMAGE',text:'Prisoners chained since childhood see only shadows, cast by objects carried in front of a fire behind them. One is freed, dragged up, blinded, and slowly sees objects, then the sun. He returns, stumbles in the dark, and is mocked. Socrates adds that they would kill anyone who tried to free them.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Whoever comes back with a better view is predictably disbelieved: worse at the old game, with only his own word as evidence. The disbelief follows from the audience\u2019s position, not a flaw in their character.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What Plato\u2019s readers knew',text:'Plato\u2019s first readers knew that Athens had put Socrates to death in 399 BCE. The remark about killing the one who would free them is widely read as pointing at that. The image is not abstract. Its author had lived through a version of it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why he must go back',text:'For Plato the climb is education, ending in the Form of the Good, which the sun stands for. The return is a duty. Those the city has educated must go back down and govern, though they would rather stay in the light.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The image flatters whoever tells it. Anyone who is disbelieved can cast themselves as the one who saw the sun, and most people who come back with news are simply wrong. The cave explains why a true report would be doubted. It gives no way to tell a true report from a false one. Only evidence others can check does that.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A hand up against the glare at the cave mouth. The climb has worked. The harder journey is the one back down into the dark.'},{kind:'recap',points:['The ascent is famous. The return is the hard part.','The returner is worse at the old game, with only his word.','Disbelief follows from position, so bring evidence others can check.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'Someone says: the cave proves that critics of my new idea are just stuck in the dark. What is the best reply?',options:['It explains why truth gets doubted, but being doubted does not prove you saw the truth','The image applies only to philosophers, not to practical ideas','Critics are sometimes right, so the cave image is irrelevant here','Plato meant the cave as a picture of Athens, not a general lesson'],answer:0,why:'Disbelief is what both a true and a false report would meet. The cave explains the doubt; it cannot settle who is right.'}]}},republic_5:{pictureCaption:'A cloak of office left on an empty chair at the end of the table. Nobody seated here is reaching for it.',bridge:'In the cave, the freed prisoner had to go back down. Plato says the people fit to rule must do the same, and will not want to.',basics:{title:'The best leader may not want the job',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'CHOOSING A CAPTAIN',prompt:'Sam really, really wants to be team captain. Priya is good at it but would rather just play. Who might be the better pick?',options:['Sam, because wanting it shows he cares','Priya, because she would do it for the team'],answer:1,reveal:'Plato, a thinker in ancient Greece, would ask why Sam wants it so much. Maybe to be the boss. Maybe to pick his friends first. Priya would just do the job.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The people best at leading are often the ones who don\u2019t want to. If someone wants power a lot, ask what they want it *for*.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why wanting it is a clue',text:'People who chase being in charge usually want something from it. It might be to look important, or to get their own way. When that clashes with the job, the job loses.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The leader\u2019s cloak is left on an empty chair. Nobody at this table is grabbing for it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Then why would anyone lead?',text:'Plato\u2019s answer was simple. If good people refuse to lead, someone worse will. So they say yes. Not because they want to, but to keep the job out of the wrong hands.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'So the kid who says no is always the best leader.',reality:'No. Not wanting the job does not make you good at it. You need both: able to do it well, and not doing it just for yourself.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your class is picking someone to look after the class fund. Who fits Plato\u2019s idea best?',options:['Leo, who is careful and only said yes because no one else would','Ava, who has wanted the job for ages and talks about it a lot','Max, who said no and is not very good with money'],answer:0,why:'Leo can do the job and wants nothing from it. Max does not want to lead, but he could not do it well.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE USUAL ASSUMPTION',belief:'The person who wants the role most will try hardest, so should get it.',reality:'Plato reads the wanting as evidence. Someone eager for power wants it for something, and that something will be served whenever it conflicts with the job.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The people fit to govern are those with least appetite for it. Reluctance is not proof of skill, but wanting power tells you what it would be used for.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The motive problem',text:'Ambition always has an object: status, money, the feel of being in charge. Most days it lines up with the work. The test comes when it does not, when the right call costs the leader face. The reluctant leader has no rival interest to protect.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Eager candidate',items:['Wants something from the role','Pitches hard for it','Serves that want under pressure']},right:{title:'Reluctant, capable candidate',items:['Wants nothing the role supplies','Has to be asked','Has no rival interest']},note:'A process that rewards pitching hard is measuring appetite for the role.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the reluctant say yes',text:'In the *Republic*, good people serve for one reason: if they refuse, they will be ruled by someone worse. Later in the book, Plato\u2019s philosopher-rulers take up governing as a duty that interrupts something they value more.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A leader\u2019s cloak left on an empty chair. Nobody at the table is reaching for it, and in Plato\u2019s terms that is the qualification.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you help choose someone to lead, ask each candidate privately what they expect to get from the role. Weigh the answers as evidence.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A club needs a new treasurer. Which candidate does the idea favour?',options:['The capable member who agreed only because nobody else would','The member who campaigned hardest and wants it on their CV','The member who refused outright and has never handled money','Whoever volunteers first, since keenness predicts effort'],answer:0,why:'Reluctance counts only alongside capability. The CV-builder wants something the role supplies; the refuser lacks the skill.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'BOOK I, THEN BOOK VII',text:'Early on, Socrates says good people will not rule for money or honour, so what moves them is a penalty: being ruled by someone worse. In Book VII the idea returns. The philosophers who have left the cave must be made to go back down and govern.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The best rulers are those least eager to rule, because wanting power is evidence about what it would be used for. Reluctance qualifies only alongside competence.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The argument',text:'Plato says the city whose rulers are least eager to rule will be governed best and with least faction. Where people come to office hungry for private gain, they fight over it, and the fight ruins both them and the city.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW SELECTION GOES WRONG',nodes:['The process rewards eagerness','The ambitious push hardest','Appetite for the role wins','The role serves that appetite'],note:'The trait being measured is the one Plato treats as disqualifying.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Plato\u2019s own fix',text:'He does not rely on volunteers. The philosophers are compelled, on the ground that the city educated them and they owe it a return. They govern in turns, then go back to what they would rather be doing.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The fix exposes the weak point. A system that installs the unwilling has to compel them, and compulsion needs someone with power to apply it, who may be anything but reluctant. Reluctance can also be performed: a show of refusing can itself be a bid for the role. And the unwilling may lead badly through neglect.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The cloak of office on an empty chair, and nobody reaching for it. The harder question is who in the room is only pretending not to.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE FLAW',prompt:'A board picks the candidate who said they never wanted the job. Two years later the organisation is drifting. Which lesson fits?',options:['Reluctance counts only with competence, and it can be put on','Plato was wrong, and the keenest candidate is always best','The board should have compelled a different person to serve','The drift proves the reluctant candidate was secretly ambitious'],answer:0,why:'Plato pairs unwillingness with fitness for the job. Reluctance alone selects nothing, and a show of it is easy to put on.'}]}},republic_6:{pictureCaption:'The ring that makes its wearer invisible lies within reach. The hands have not picked it up yet. The whole question lives in that pause.',bridge:'Idea 1 ended on Glaucon\u2019s challenge. This is the story he tells to make it, and the soul of idea 3 is Plato\u2019s answer.',basics:{title:'What would you do if no one could see?',beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'THE MAGIC RING',text:'Plato was a thinker in Greece, long ago. In his book, a young man named Glaucon tells an old story. A shepherd finds a ring. When he turns it, no one can see him. He uses it to do terrible things, and makes himself king.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'If you found that ring, would you still be honest?',options:['Yes, always','Most of the time','I\u2019m not sure'],answer:2,reveal:'Glaucon thought almost everyone would cheat. His point was a hard one. Maybe we are only good because we might get caught.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Being good counts most when no one could ever find out. What you do then shows who you really are.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The magic ring is right there. The hands have not picked it up yet. What would yours do?'},{kind:'explain',title:'Plato\u2019s answer',text:'Plato said the shepherd did not really get away with it. Each bad thing he did made him worse inside. He could fool everyone else. He still had to live as the person he had become.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'AT HOME',text:'You are told to tidy your room. You could stuff it all under the bed, and no one would check. If you tidy it anyway, that tells you something real about you.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one good thing you do. Ask yourself: would I still do it if no one could ever know? Be honest with the answer.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You find the answers to a test on the teacher\u2019s desk. Nobody is around. What does this idea say about the moment?',options:['What you do now shows who you really are','It only matters if a camera might see you','Taking a look is fine if nobody finds out'],answer:0,why:'That is the ring test. With no chance of being caught, your choice shows what you are like.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'NOBODY WILL CHECK',text:'The expense form is never audited. The survey is anonymous. The work will be signed off without anyone opening it. In each case the honest version costs you a little, and the other costs you nothing.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Glaucon asks whether anyone would stay just if they could never be caught. If not, justice is only a way of protecting your reputation. Plato\u2019s whole book is the answer.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'THE RING OF GYGES',text:'A shepherd finds a ring that makes him invisible when he turns it. He uses it to seduce the queen, kill the king and take the kingdom. Glaucon says the just man would do the same with the ring, and that this shows what justice really is.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'GLAUCON\u2019S TWO LIVES',left:{title:'Unjust, seen as just',items:['Does as he likes','Keeps a good name','Wins every reward']},right:{title:'Just, seen as unjust',items:['Never does wrong','Has a bad name','Is punished for it']},note:'Strip away reputation, he says, and show me which life is better.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Plato\u2019s answer',text:'The unjust man is not getting away with it. The damage is to the order of his own soul, the three parts from idea 3: he has let appetite rule. A soul arranged that way is wretched whether or not anyone is watching.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The ring lies within reach, and the hands have not picked it up. The test is entirely in what they do next.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one thing you do because it is right. Ask honestly whether you would still do it if it could never be discovered.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A colleague says they always log their hours exactly, because timesheets get spot-checked. What does the ring test ask?',options:['Whether they would log them exactly if no spot checks ever happened','Whether the spot checks happen often enough to deter cheating','Whether logging hours exactly is required by their contract','Whether other colleagues log their hours less carefully'],answer:0,why:'Spot checks explain the behaviour by being watched. The ring test removes the watching and looks at what is left.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'GLAUCON\u2019S THOUGHT EXPERIMENT',left:{title:'Perfectly unjust',items:['Does wrong skilfully','Has the best reputation','Gets every reward']},right:{title:'Perfectly just',items:['Does no wrong','Is thought the worst of men','Is punished, even tortured']},note:'Glaucon strips justice of every reward, then asks Socrates to show it is still the better life.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Glaucon\u2019s ring removes the risk of being caught, to test whether justice is valued for itself or only for its reputation. Plato answers that injustice damages the soul itself, seen or unseen.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'BOOK II',text:'Glaucon, Plato\u2019s brother, says he does not hold the cynical view but has never heard it properly answered. He tells of a shepherd whose ring makes him invisible. The man seduces the queen, kills the king and takes the throne. Anyone would, Glaucon says.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The answer is structural',text:'Plato\u2019s reply takes the rest of the book. Justice is the right order of the soul: reason ruling, spirit allied with it, appetite in its place. The unjust man has let appetite rule. That is a condition he lives in, not a risk of being found out.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the test outlives the answer',text:'Unaudited expenses, anonymous forms and work nobody will check are small rings. What you do there describes what you are, rather than what you are willing to be seen as.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Hands hovering over the ring, not yet taking it. Glaucon bets on what they do next. Plato argues about what it would cost.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Plato\u2019s answer persuades only if you accept his account of the soul, and many readers find his case that the just life is happier strained. The ring also tests behaviour without consequences, which may reveal fear as much as character. Most people are honest partly because they are watched, and that is not worthless.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE CYNIC',prompt:'A cynic says: people are honest only because of the risk of getting caught. Which reply is closest to Plato\u2019s?',options:['The unjust person harms their own soul, whether or not anyone finds out','The risk of getting caught is always higher than it looks','Most people are naturally good and would not use the ring','Honesty pays off in the long run through a good reputation'],answer:0,why:'Plato concedes nothing about being caught. His claim is that injustice is a harm in itself, done to the order of the soul.'}]}},republic_7:{pictureCaption:'Two bars from the same furnace, the same metal. Only one carries a stamp, and the stamp is the story told about it.',bridge:'Idea 6 asked whether you would be honest unseen. Here Plato himself proposes that the rulers tell a lie, and calls it one.',basics:{title:'A story told to keep order',beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'PLATO\u2019S MADE-UP STORY',text:'Plato, a thinker in ancient Greece, imagined a perfect city. He said its rulers should tell everyone a story. People are born with metal mixed inside them: gold, silver, or iron and bronze. Your metal decides your job for life.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Plato knew the story was false. He wanted people to believe it anyway, to keep the city calm. People still argue about whether that spoils his whole plan.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why would he want that?',text:'Plato thought people would accept their place if it felt natural. *I am bronze, so I farm* sounds fixed. *The rulers told me to farm* sounds unfair. The story makes the rules feel like nature.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two bars of the same metal. One has a stamp on it. The stamp is the story, not the metal.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'If rulers can lie to you for your own good, how could you check they are helping you?',options:['Ask them','You couldn\u2019t, really'],answer:1,reveal:'To check, you would need the truth. But the truth is the very thing being kept from you. That is the big problem with this idea.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'IN THE PLAYGROUND',text:'A group says only kids from one street can use the best corner, because *that\u2019s how it\u2019s always been*. Nobody remembers why. The day someone asks, the rule looks much less solid.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A club tells new members a story about why only some of them can vote. What does this lesson suggest?',options:['Ask what the rule rests on if the story is false','Believe it, since stories help keep groups calm','Leave the club at once without asking anything'],answer:0,why:'The lesson is to check what holds a rule up. If only a story holds it, it may fall the day someone looks.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE FOUNDING MYTH',text:'At the founding of Plato\u2019s ideal city, the rulers tell a story. All citizens were formed in the earth, with gold, silver, or iron and bronze mixed into them. The metal decides their class. Plato says plainly that it is false, and proposes it anyway.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Plato\u2019s ideal city depends, at its founding, on everyone believing something its founders know is untrue. The aim is stability: an order that feels natural, not imposed.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHY THE CHECK FAILS',nodes:['Rulers may deceive for your good','You want to check it serves you','Checking needs the truth','The truth is what is withheld'],note:'Once the principle is granted, the ruled lose any way to audit the rulers.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The case for it',text:'Plato thought every society already runs on founding stories. His proposal is to choose the story on purpose rather than inherit one. That explains the move. It does not answer the objection.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two ingots from the same furnace, identical in metal. One is stamped. The difference between them is the mark, not the material.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'CLOSER TO HOME',text:'Imagine a firm that tells every new hire its pay bands reflect careful measurement of each role. Nobody has seen the measurements. It all runs smoothly, until the day someone asks for the data.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of an arrangement you are part of that rests on a story everyone repeats. Ask what would happen on the day somebody checked.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A director says staff are happier not knowing how promotions are really decided. What is the strongest objection, on this lesson\u2019s terms?',options:['Staff then have no way to check whether the system serves them','Staff will usually find out the truth in the end anyway','Leaders are never justified in keeping anything private','Happy staff tend to be less productive than informed ones'],answer:0,why:'The core problem is the audit. Deceiving people for their good removes the means of finding out whether it is for their good.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'BOOK III',text:'Plato has already said that only rulers may use falsehood, as a doctor uses a drug, for the city\u2019s good. Now Socrates, after some hesitation, proposes a myth for the new city: citizens were formed in the earth, with gold, silver, or iron and bronze mixed into them.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Plato proposes a founding myth he openly calls false, so that a fixed social order feels natural. Once rulers may deceive for the good of the ruled, the ruled lose the means to check them.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The detail people forget',text:'The myth is not purely hereditary. Children may be born with a different metal from their parents, and the rulers must then move them up or down. The order is fixed by nature, but nature as the rulers judge it.'},{kind:'term',word:'Noble lie',meaning:'The usual English name for the myth. The Greek phrase can also be rendered as a grand or high-minded falsehood. On every translation, Plato does not pretend it is true.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'The defence',items:['Every society runs on founding stories','Better to choose one deliberately','Stability serves everyone']},right:{title:'The objection',items:['The ruled cannot audit the rulers','The check would need the truth','Who decides what serves everyone?']},note:'The defence explains the move. It does not dispose of the objection.'},{kind:'limits',text:'This passage has done more damage to Plato\u2019s reputation than any other, and fairly. Karl Popper, writing during the Second World War, read it as a charter for state propaganda. Some scholars answer that the word means something nearer a civic myth than a lie. The text does not settle it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Same furnace, same metal, two bars. One is stamped. The myth works by making the stamp look like part of the metal.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'Which modern arrangement has the noble lie\u2019s structure?',options:['A ranking its makers know is arbitrary, presented to everyone as measured merit','A national anthem that celebrates a country\u2019s founding with pride','A mission statement that staff all know is aspirational','A fairy tale told to children that everyone knows is fiction'],answer:0,why:'The structure is a known falsehood that makes an order look natural, and that nobody can check without the truth it withholds.'}]}},republic_8:{pictureCaption:'The poet walks out through the city gate carrying a lyre. Nobody is pushing, and the gate is left open behind.',bridge:'Idea 7 showed Plato deciding which stories the city may tell. Here he turns the same scrutiny on the poets, and on his own love of Homer.',basics:{title:'Giving up something you love',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'A STRANGE BAN',belief:'Plato banned poems because he hated them.',reality:'He loved them. He grew up on Homer\u2019s old poems about heroes and wars. He still said his perfect city should send most poets away. He did it even though it hurt.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why send them away?',text:'Plato, a thinker in ancient Greece, had two reasons. First, poems copy how things look, not how they really are. Second, sad and angry stories train your feelings. Weep at a hundred sad plays, he thought, and you may get used to giving in to grief.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Sometimes the right answer is one you don\u2019t like. Plato followed his reasons, even when they took away something he loved.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A poet walks out of the city with a lyre, a kind of small harp. Nobody is pushing, and the gate stays open.'},{kind:'explain',title:'He left the door open',text:'Plato said that if anyone could show poems are good for a city, they could come back. He would be glad to be proved wrong. People have been making that case ever since.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'ON YOUR PHONE',text:'You love a game on your phone. You notice it makes you snappy and tired. You don\u2019t want that to be true. But once you are sure of it, the honest thing is to act on it.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of something you believe even though you wish it were not true. That might be one of your most honest thoughts.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Ben loves staying up late. He sees he is grumpy every day after he does. What would Plato do in Ben\u2019s place?',options:['Go to bed earlier, even though he\u2019d rather not','Keep staying up, since he enjoys it so much','Decide the grumpy days must have another cause'],answer:0,why:'Plato followed his reasons even when he disliked where they led. Ben\u2019s reasons point to bed.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'BOOK X',text:'Near the end of the *Republic*, Plato sends the poets out of his ideal city, and in the same passage admits he has loved Homer since childhood. He says the argument compels him, against his affection.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Plato expels the poetry he loves because his argument requires it, and invites anyone to argue it back in. A conclusion you would rather were false is often one you reached honestly.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The two charges',text:'First, imitation is far from truth: a carpenter copies the idea of a bed, and a painter copies the carpenter\u2019s bed. Second, drama trains the feelings. Watching grief performed exercises the part of us that indulges emotion, in a setting where indulging it costs nothing.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The serious one',text:'The second charge is not obviously wrong. It says that feeling things again and again at second hand shapes who you become. That is broadly what we now assume about media, without accepting his ban.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A figure walks out through an open gate with a lyre. No one is forcing them, and nothing stops a return.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'TWO KINDS OF CONCLUSION',low:'Arguments you enjoy making',high:'Arguments you wish were false',note:'The further right, the less your preferences are doing the work. Those are your most reliable arguments, and your least enjoyable.'},{kind:'try',text:'List two things you believe that you would rather were not true. Ask what put them there, given that your wishes did not.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A manager loves a product she launched. The numbers say it should close. Which response shows Plato\u2019s attitude?',options:['Close it, say plainly what it costs her, and invite a case for reopening','Keep it, since her affection is a sign it has hidden value','Close it quietly and never admit she was attached to it','Wait for numbers that fit her hopes before deciding anything'],answer:0,why:'Plato enforces the conclusion, admits the cost and leaves the door open. The affection is acknowledged, not obeyed.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'WHAT HE ACTUALLY BANNED',belief:'Plato banned all poetry from his city because he despised it.',reality:'He keeps hymns to the gods and praise of good people. What goes is imitative poetry, Homer and tragedy above all, and he says he loves it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Plato expels the imitative poets against his own affection, because his argument demands it, and invites a defence of poetry in prose. The reluctance is a sign that the argument, not taste, is doing the work.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Third from the truth',text:'In Book X Plato ranks three beds: the Form of bed, the carpenter\u2019s bed that copies it, and the painter\u2019s picture of the carpenter\u2019s bed. The painter, and the poet like him, makes copies of copies, and needs no knowledge of the thing itself.'},{kind:'term',word:'Mimesis',meaning:'Greek for imitation or representation. For Plato it covers painting, poetry and acting: making something that looks like a thing without being it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The psychological charge',text:'The weightier claim is that tragedy feeds the part of the soul that wants to weep and lament, the part reason is meant to govern. In private grief we try to hold it back. In the theatre we let it run, because the grief is someone else\u2019s, and the habit comes home with us.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The first charge rests on the theory of Forms, which few now accept. The second proves too much: if feeling at second hand shapes us, good art can also train sympathy. Aristotle, Plato\u2019s own student, argued that tragedy can purge or clarify emotion rather than feed it. And a city that licenses art by its effects has handed someone the licence.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The lyre leaves by an open gate, under no compulsion. Plato\u2019s invitation to argue it back in is the open door.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'WHAT SURVIVES THE VERDICT',prompt:'What is the most defensible lesson in the passage, even for someone who rejects the ban?',options:['Enforce a conclusion you dislike, and say what would change your mind','Art should be judged mainly by its effects on behaviour','Imitation is always inferior to direct knowledge of things','Strong affection for a thing is a reason to distrust it'],answer:0,why:'The method outlives the verdict: follow the argument against your preference, and name the defence that would reverse it.'}]}},republic_9:{pictureCaption:'The old stalls and barriers are gone, leaving only their outlines. In the cleared centre stands one new platform, waiting for whoever climbs it.',bridge:'Idea 3 described a soul where one part takes over. Plato\u2019s history of cities ends with the same thing happening to a whole state.',basics:{title:'When nobody can say no',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE SUPPLY TEACHER',text:'Your class gets a supply teacher who lets everyone do anything. Day one is fun. By day three it is loud and messy, and nobody can work. Then one loud kid starts telling everyone what to do. And people let him.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When a group throws out every rule, it does not stay free for long. Sooner or later someone offers to take charge, and the group lets them in.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What Plato saw',text:'Plato lived in Athens about 2,400 years ago. There, citizens voted on things together. He warned that too much freedom can go wrong. When nobody can say no to anything, people start to want someone strong to fix it.'},{kind:'flow',nodes:['Every rule is gone','Things fall apart','People want someone strong','A strong boss is invited in'],note:'Nobody grabs the power. It is handed over.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'All the old stalls and fences in the square have been cleared away. One new platform stands in the middle, waiting for someone to climb it.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A GARDEN',text:'A garden that nobody weeds does not stay a happy mix of flowers. The fastest weed spreads and takes over. Leaving it alone did not make it free.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your football club drops every rule, even who plays where. Soon one pushy player runs everything. What does Plato\u2019s idea say?',options:['When no one could say no, someone strong was let in','The pushy player sneaked in and took over by force','Fewer rules always make a group fairer and happier'],answer:0,why:'Nobody stopped him, because nothing was left to stop him with. The group handed him the job.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'flow',kicker:'PLATO\u2019S SEQUENCE',nodes:['Rule by the best','Rule by the honour-loving','Rule by the rich','Democracy','Tyranny'],note:'Book VIII traces how each form of rule decays into the next. The last step is the one people still quote.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A society organised around freedom can come to treat every constraint as oppression. With nothing left that can say no, it welcomes whoever promises to supply authority. That person is invited, not imposed.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How freedom overreaches',text:'Plato\u2019s democracy prizes freedom above everything. In time any constraint feels like oppression: the father fears his son, the teacher fears the pupils. Nothing carries authority. So nobody can impose anything, including limits on the next person who wants power.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How the tyrant arrives',text:'He does not invade. He appears as the people\u2019s champion against the wealthy. He asks for a bodyguard to protect him, and is given one. By the time the danger is visible, he has the only organised force.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Every old stall and barrier has been cleared from the square. One new platform stands in the middle, built for whoever is invited up.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Tyrants seize power from free people.',reality:'In Plato\u2019s account the demand for a strong protector comes first, after a stretch of disorder. The protector is usually invited in, and arrives as a champion of the people.'},{kind:'try',text:'Look at a group you belong to. Is there anything it can no longer say no to? Notice who is offering to fill that gap.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A club abolishes its committee and its rules. A year later one member controls the money and fixtures, and most members are relieved. What does Plato\u2019s account highlight?',options:['Control was handed over because nothing else could say no','The member must have taken control by breaking the rules','Clubs work best when a single member runs everything','Abolishing the rules made the club freer and so more stable'],answer:0,why:'With no authority left, the group welcomed whoever supplied one. Nothing was seized.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'BOOK VIII',text:'Socrates traces the decay of constitutions: from rule by the best, through rule by the honour-loving, the rich and the many, to tyranny. Oligarchy is undone by its greed for wealth, and democracy by its greed for its own defining good, freedom.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Plato argues that democracy, organised around freedom, comes to treat every constraint as oppression, and that a city with no settled authority hands itself to a champion who promises to supply one.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The mechanism',text:'Excess freedom dissolves every authority: parents, teachers, finally the laws. The people then want a protector against the wealthy. He asks for a bodyguard, is given it, and turns it on them. Too much freedom, Plato concludes, passes over into too much slavery.'},{kind:'term',word:'Tyrant',meaning:'In Greek usage, a ruler who took power outside the constitution, not necessarily a cruel one. Plato\u2019s tyrant is cruel as well: ruled by his own appetites, as the city is ruled by him.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The barriers are gone and only their outlines remain. The new platform in the centre was built for someone the crowd will invite up.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Plato is openly hostile to democracy and does not describe Athens fairly. His sequence is not history: regimes do not reliably decay in his order, and democracies have often lasted. What survives is narrower: the call for a strong protector tends to follow disorder, and the protector is usually invited rather than imposed.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'What Plato claimed',items:['A fixed sequence of decay','Democracy breeds tyranny','Freedom is the fatal excess']},right:{title:'What survives',items:['No fixed sequence','Disorder breeds a wish for protectors','Protectors are invited, not imposed']},note:'Carry the mechanism, not the prophecy.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE OVERREACH',prompt:'Which conclusion goes further than the defensible core of Plato\u2019s argument?',options:['Democracies inevitably end in tyranny, so experts must limit freedom','Calls for a strong protector tend to grow after a period of disorder','A would-be tyrant often arrives as the people\u2019s champion','An institution that can refuse nothing is open to whoever takes charge'],answer:0,why:'Plato\u2019s sequence is not a prediction, and he was openly hostile to democracy. The mechanism is worth carrying; the inevitability is not.'}]}}}},3001,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'richest-man-in-babylon_1':{pictureCaption:'Pay day. Before a single note leaves the envelope, one coin goes into a dish of its own. The order is the whole idea.',basics:{title:'Save first, not last',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'SATURDAY TO FRIDAY',text:'You get your pocket money on Saturday. You plan to save what is left by Friday. On Friday you check. There is nothing left. There never is.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'Why is there never any left on Friday?',options:['You bought one thing that cost too much','Money gets spent on whatever is there','You just need more pocket money'],answer:1,reveal:'Each day there was money, so each day some went. A snack. A pack of cards. None of it felt big. More pocket money would go the same way.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Put your saving aside first, on the day the money comes. Then spend from what is left. It sounds like a small change. It is the whole trick.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE PACKING YOUR BAG',text:'Put the big book in your school bag first. Then the small things fit round it. Leave the book till last, and it never fits.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Pay day. Before any of the money is touched, one coin goes into a dish of its own.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHERE THIS COMES FROM',text:'George Clason wrote a book about money in 1926. He set his stories in Babylon, a great city of long ago. In it, a rich man called Arkad says part of all you earn is yours to keep. He means at least one coin in ten.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you get money, put one coin in ten into a jar straight away. Do it before you buy a thing. See what the jar holds in a month.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You get \xa320 for your birthday. Which plan follows this idea?',options:['Put \xa32 away today, then spend from the rest','Spend with care, and save what is left','Keep it all until you know what you want'],answer:0,why:'Saving first means the saving is done. Saving what is left usually means saving nothing.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE 28TH OF THE MONTH',text:'Your salary lands. Rent, the phone, the car loan and a card bill are all due within the week. The plan, as every month, is to save whatever survives until the next payday. By the 27th, what survives is about forty pounds.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Keep a part of all you earn, and take it on the day you are paid, before any bill or purchase. Saving what remains produces almost nothing, because spending grows to fill whatever it is given.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Save what is left',items:['Spending gets the first claim','Savings are the leftover','The leftover is usually near zero']},right:{title:'Save first',items:['Saving is taken on payday','Spending works with a smaller sum','The month bends around it']},note:'Same income, same bills. The only change is which claim comes first.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why order beats discipline',text:'Money left at the end of a month is whatever survived every small decision made during it. Each decision looked at the balance, saw room, and used some. Take the saving out first and every later decision sees a smaller balance, and quietly fits itself to that.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'ARKAD\u2019S LESSON',text:'In Clason\u2019s parable, Arkad is a young scribe in Babylon who asks a rich moneylender how to become wealthy. The answer is plain. You pay the baker and the sandal-maker; pay yourself too. A part of all you earn is yours to keep, not less than a tenth.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The wage envelope is open and untouched. The first thing the hand does is put one coin in a dish of its own.'},{kind:'try',text:'Set a standing transfer to savings for the day you are paid, not the day before the next one. Start with a tenth, or less if a tenth is impossible, and let the rest of the month adjust.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Jo earns more than last year but still saves almost nothing. Which change does this idea point to?',options:['Move her savings transfer to the morning she is paid','Track every purchase more strictly through the month','Wait for the next pay rise before trying again','Save a larger lump whenever a month goes well'],answer:0,why:'Her problem is order, not income or effort. Taken first, the saving happens. Taken last, it competes with every other wish and loses.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'BABYLON, IMAGINED IN 1926',text:'George S. Clason set plain rules about money in an imagined ancient Babylon, on the theory that a rule attached to a character is remembered. Arkad, the richest man in the city, teaches seven cures for a lean purse. The first: of every ten coins you put in, take out only nine.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The order of saving and spending matters more than the size of either. Take a fixed part first and the month adjusts around it. Leave saving until last and it is whatever survived, which is reliably close to nothing.'},{kind:'term',word:'Residual',meaning:'What is left after every other claim has been met. Saving as a residual depends on all your other decisions. Saving as the first claim makes spending the residual instead.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The mechanism',text:'Spending expands to the funds available, not to needs. The constraint that binds is therefore what appears in the account, not what is earned. That is why saving the remainder fails at almost any income, and why the cure is a change of sequence rather than an effort of will.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One coin set aside before anything else is touched. The arithmetic is ordinary. The sequence is what people get wrong.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The rule assumes a margin. On an income that does not cover rent and food, a tenth taken first comes out of necessities and returns as borrowing at a worse rate. With debts outstanding, the book itself later splits income between saving and repayment. And the tenth is a convention, not a law. The order is the point.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Saving what is left yields almost nothing, at almost any income.','Take the saving first; the rest of the month adapts.','The order is the idea. The tenth is a convention.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'FIND THE REAL CAUSE',prompt:'Two friends earn the same and both mean to save a tenth. One saves on payday, the other at month\u2019s end. A year later only one has savings. What best explains it?',options:['Spending fitted itself to whatever balance each of them saw','The month-end saver must have had larger bills that year','The payday saver simply has more self-control than the other','Saving once a month is too rare a habit to stick'],answer:0,why:'Income and character are the same in the story. The payday saver changed what every later decision saw in the account; the other left saving to compete with all of them.'}]}},'richest-man-in-babylon_2':{pictureCaption:'The same jars and bottles, one shelf higher. What counts as necessary rose to meet the income, and nobody decided it.',bridge:'Idea 1 set the saving aside first. This one is about the other nine-tenths, and why they always seem to be needed.',basics:{title:'When a want starts to feel like a need',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'MORE POCKET MONEY?',belief:'If I got more pocket money, I would always have enough.',reality:'You would buy more things, and soon they would feel normal. Wants grow to fit what you get. So more money alone never feels like enough.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'IN A PHONE GAME',text:'At first, buying extra lives in a game is a treat. You do it once. Then you do it every week. A few months later it feels like part of the game. Nobody chose that. It just happened.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Things you buy often start to feel like things you need. To stay in charge, write down your plan for money before you get it. Then you choose what to spend on.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The bottom shelf is what felt needed last year. The top shelf is the same things again. Nobody chose to need more. It crept up.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Needs',items:['Food','Shoes that fit','Bus fare to school']},right:{title:'Wants that feel like needs',items:['The newest trainers','Snacks every day','Game extras every week']},note:'Wants are not bad. You just need to see which is which.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why write it down?',text:'A plan on paper lets you see what you spend. Then you can pick. Maybe game extras matter to you. Fine. But now it is a choice, not a habit.'},{kind:'try',text:'Write down what you spent money on this week. Put N by each need and W by each want. Do any of the Ws surprise you?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Mia\u2019s pocket money goes up. A month later she has no more left over than before. What most likely happened?',options:['Her wants grew to fit the new amount','Prices in the shops all went up that month','She lost some of the money at school'],answer:0,why:'This is what wants do. They grow to fit what you get, unless you plan first.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'EIGHTEEN MONTHS AGO',prompt:'Your pay rose eighteen months ago. Your savings did not. Where did the rise most likely go?',options:['One large purchase you can name','Many small upgrades that now feel necessary','Higher bills you had no control over'],answer:1,reveal:'Usually no single decision. A better phone plan, more takeaways, a gym you still pay for. Each felt normal within a few months, and now reads as necessary.'},{kind:'idea',text:'What people call necessary expenses grow to match income, because a thing bought regularly soon feels like a need. The defence is a budget written before the money arrives, which turns that drift into a choice.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW A WANT BECOMES A NEED',nodes:['A treat, bought once','Bought again, then regularly','Felt as normal','Listed as a necessary expense'],note:'No step involves a decision. That is why the list grows at every income.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What Clason actually claims',text:'His second cure separates necessary expenses from desires, and says desires always exceed income however high it rises. He does not call this greed. It is simply how wanting behaves. So the question is never how to satisfy every desire. It is which ones you will fund.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The same jars and bottles, one shelf higher. What counts as necessary rose to meet the income, and nobody decided it.'},{kind:'steps',title:'A budget that sorts, not just limits',steps:['Before payday, list every regular expense.','Mark each one as needed or wanted, honestly.','Note roughly when each wanted item became regular.','Choose in writing which wants this month will fund.'],note:'The point is not to cut everything. It is to make the sorting visible, so it can be revised.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Which use of a budget does Clason\u2019s second cure have in mind?',options:['Deciding in advance which desires this month\u2019s income will fund','Cutting every expense that is not strictly needed to survive','Recording what was spent so the totals can be checked later','Setting one overall limit and hoping the month stays under it'],answer:0,why:'The budget\u2019s job is to turn spending into a choice made before the money arrives. Recording afterwards, or cutting everything, misses that.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE SECOND CURE',text:'Arkad\u2019s second cure for a lean purse is to control your expenditures. He separates necessary expenses from desires and makes an unusual claim about desires: they will always outrun income, at any level of income. Nobody escapes them by earning more.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Necessities and desires get confused by habit, so necessary spending quietly rises to meet income. A written budget is not there to restrict. It makes the classification explicit, and therefore open to revision.'},{kind:'term',word:'Hedonic adaptation',meaning:'A term from modern psychology for getting used to an improvement, so that it stops feeling like a gain and becomes the new baseline. Clason describes the effect without the name.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The honest part',text:'Much budgeting advice promises that, with care, you can have what you want. Clason states the reverse more plainly than most: some desires will stay unmet at any income. Once that is accepted, the work is choosing which to fund, and a budget is where the choice becomes visible rather than automatic.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Last year\u2019s necessities below, the same things again above. The reclassification needed no decision, which is why reversing it needs a written one.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Some expenses do become genuinely necessary: a child, a longer commute, care for a parent. Treating every rise as drift can turn a budget into a scorecard of guilt. And for people whose income does not cover real needs, the problem is not desire at all. The cure assumes a margin that is being misspent.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'WHERE AN EXPENSE SITS',low:'Automatic: necessary by habit',high:'Decided: chosen in writing this month',note:'A budget need not remove anything from the list. It moves items from the left end to the right.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'CORRECT THE MISREADING',prompt:'A reader concludes that the second cure means living as cheaply as possible. What is the best correction?',options:['It means choosing which desires to fund, since some will always go unmet','It means spending freely once the first tenth has been set aside','It means letting a rising income absorb rising expenses over time','It means buying only what is strictly needed to stay alive'],answer:0,why:'The cure sorts rather than forbids. A desire you have chosen to fund is fine; one mistaken for a need without anyone deciding is the problem.'}]}},'richest-man-in-babylon_3':{pictureCaption:'The coins shut in the box have gone dull. The bright one has been taken out, so that it can be sent to earn.',bridge:'Ideas 1 and 2 were about keeping money. This one is about what the kept money should do next.',basics:{title:'Make saved money earn more',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE SEEDS IN A DRAWER',text:'A seed kept in a drawer stays one seed. Plant it and it grows a plant. That plant makes more seeds. Plant those too, and soon you have a garden.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Saved money should not just sit. Put it somewhere it earns a little more money. Then let that extra money earn too.'},{kind:'term',word:'Interest',meaning:'Money a bank pays you for keeping your savings there. It pays you because it can lend your money to other people while it has it.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'TWO PILES OF \xa3100',text:'You and your cousin each save \xa3100. You keep yours in a jar. She puts hers in a bank that pays a little interest each year. She leaves the interest in, so it earns interest too. Each year her pile grows a bit faster. Yours stays at \xa3100.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The coins shut in the box have gone dull. The bright one has been taken out, so it can go and earn.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why not just keep it safe?',text:'A jar keeps money from being lost. But prices slowly go up. So the same \xa3100 buys a bit less each year. Money that sits still is slowly shrinking.'},{kind:'try',text:'Ask a grown-up where your savings are kept. Do they earn anything? If not, ask what safe place would pay some interest.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Sam saves \xa35 a week in a shoebox. What does this idea say he should change?',options:['Put it somewhere safe that pays interest','Spend it now, before prices go up','Hide the shoebox somewhere safer'],answer:0,why:'Saving is step one. Step two is letting the savings earn, and leaving what they earn to earn as well.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO SAVERS, THIRTY YEARS',left:{title:'Leaves it idle',items:['Saves a tenth of income','Keeps it in a current account','Ends with the deposits, less inflation']},right:{title:'Puts it to work',items:['Saves the same tenth','Earns a modest, boring return','Reinvests everything it earns']},note:'Same habit, same deposits. After thirty years the second pile can be roughly twice the first, or more, depending on the rate.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Saved money must be put to work: lent, invested, earning. Then its earnings must be put to work too. Money left idle is not standing still. It is slowly shrinking.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE LOOP CLASON DESCRIBES',nodes:['Save a coin','Send it out to earn','It brings back more coins','Send those out too'],loop:!0,note:'The book pictures it as an income that keeps coming whether you work or not. The loop is why the later years matter so much.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'ARKAD\u2019S SECOND MISTAKE',text:'In the parable, Arkad\u2019s savings are at last put to work, lent to a craftsman who pays for the use of them. Arkad spends those payments as they arrive. His teacher points out that he has eaten the very coins that should have been earning for him.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the book leaves out',text:'Clason says nothing about diversification or weighing return against risk. He insists only on the first step, that saved money must work at all, because that is where most people stop. Choosing what to put it in is a later, harder question he does not try to answer.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The coins left in the box have dulled. The bright one has been taken out: saved, and now ready to be sent to work.'},{kind:'try',text:'Look at where your savings actually sit. If any of it earns nothing, decide this week where it goes to work, even if the answer is boring.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Priya saves steadily and keeps it all in her current account, safe and to hand. What does this idea say?',options:['The saving is done; the second step, making it earn, is not','She is right, since safety comes before any thought of return','She should move it all to whatever pays the highest return','She saves enough that where it sits makes little difference'],answer:0,why:'Clason\u2019s point is the step most people skip. Safety matters, and a later idea is about it, but idle money quietly loses value.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'THE WORD THE BOOK DESCRIBES',word:'Compounding',meaning:'Earning a return on past returns as well as on the original sum. Growth then builds on growth, so the curve is slow early and steep late.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Saving is only the first half. Money kept idle loses ground to rising prices; money sent out to earn, with its earnings sent out again, grows on itself. Clason insists on the step most readers skip: putting savings to work at all.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the late years matter',text:'With returns reinvested, each year\u2019s gain is calculated on a larger base than the last. Early on the extra is small and easy to dismiss. Over thirty years of steady deposits at a modest rate, most of the gap over idle money opens in the later years, long after many people have decided it does not matter.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'ARKAD LEARNS IT TWICE',text:'Arkad\u2019s first savings are lost on a bad venture. His next are lent out and earn, but he spends what they earn. Only when he leaves the earnings working too does his wealth begin to grow on itself. The parable makes the second step a lesson of its own.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The dull coins have sat safe in the box all year. The bright one has been taken out. Clason asks only that it be sent to work.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The book is silent on risk, diversification and fees, and sending money out to work is exactly where it can be lost. Read for the portfolio, it is no guide; read for the behaviour, it is sound. A modest return is never guaranteed, and a poor investment can do worse than cash in a box. What to put money in needs better sources than Clason.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Idle money loses value; working money can grow on itself.','Reinvesting the earnings is what makes the late years large.','Clason answers whether to invest, not what to invest in.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'Someone has saved for ten years and earned nothing on it. They are waiting until they understand investing perfectly. What would Clason say?',options:['Waiting is itself a choice, and idle money is quietly losing value','They are right to wait, since the book warns most against risk','Any investment beats none, so move it all somewhere at once','The sum is too small for where it sits to make a difference'],answer:0,why:'His insistence is on the first step. Choosing well matters, and the book cannot help with it, but waiting forever has a cost too.'}]}},'richest-man-in-babylon_4':{pictureCaption:'The listener hangs on every gesture. The one thing these hands truly know about, the brick, sits ignored on the bench between them.',bridge:'Idea 3 said to send savings out to work. This one is about whose advice decides where they go.',basics:{title:'Ask someone who has done it',beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'ARKAD\u2019S FIRST YEAR',text:'In Clason\u2019s story, a young man called Arkad saves money for a whole year. A brickmaker he knows is going on a trip to buy jewels. Arkad gives him all his savings to buy jewels for him too. The brickmaker is honest. But the jewels turn out to be worthless.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The listener hangs on every word. But the one thing these hands really know about, the brick, sits forgotten on the bench.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Take advice from someone who has really done the thing, not just someone who sounds sure. Knowing about bricks does not mean knowing about jewels.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Someone who talks a lot about a thing must know a lot about it.',reality:'Talking is easy. Doing is the test. Ask what they have done themselves, and how it went.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'A SLIPPING BIKE CHAIN',text:'Your bike chain keeps slipping off. One friend talks about bikes all day. He says you need a new bike. Another friend says less, but she fixes bikes every weekend. Ask her first. She has done it.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time someone gives you advice, ask in a kind way: have you done this yourself? How did it go?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You want to get better at swimming. Who is the best person to ask?',options:['A swimmer who has trained for years','A friend who watches lots of swim videos','Your uncle, who is great at football'],answer:0,why:'The swimmer has done the thing. Watching a lot, or being good at a different sport, is not the same.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'AT A FAMILY LUNCH',text:'Your cousin works at a bank and talks about money with great confidence. Over lunch she tells you exactly where to put your savings. She handles other people\u2019s accounts all day. Surely she knows.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Take advice on money from people whose own money is competently managed, not from those who merely work near the subject. Being close to a field is not the same as being good at it, and the gap costs money.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Near the subject',items:['Works with it every day','Knows the words','Sounds sure']},right:{title:'Competent in it',items:['Has done it with their own money','Over years, not one lucky spell','Results you can actually see']},note:'From outside, the left column looks just like the right.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'THE BRICKMAKER AND THE JEWELS',text:'Arkad\u2019s first year of savings goes with a brickmaker travelling to buy jewels. The brickmaker is honest and knows nothing about jewels; the stones are worthless. Arkad\u2019s teacher asks why anyone would trust a brickmaker about jewels. Ask him about bricks.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the mistake is so common',text:'Confidence and closeness are easy to see. A track record is not: you would have to ask, and asking feels rude. So people default to the nearest person who sounds sure. Clason\u2019s parable makes the gap visible by making it absurd.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The listener is hanging on every gesture. What these hands truly know, the brick, sits ignored on the bench between them.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Before acting on advice',steps:['Ask what they have done in this specific area.','Ask whether they did it with their own money.','Ask for how long: long enough to rule out luck?']},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Who passes Clason\u2019s test as an adviser on investing your savings?',options:['A neighbour who has grown her own savings steadily for twenty years','A friend who works as a cashier at a high-street bank','A colleague who made a fortune on one share last year','The confident presenter of a popular money podcast'],answer:0,why:'Only the neighbour has done the thing herself, for long enough to rule out luck. Closeness, one big win and confidence are not evidence.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THREE ADVISERS',prompt:'You want advice on growing your savings. Whom would Clason\u2019s parable tell you to trust?',options:['A loan officer who handles money all day','A friend whose own savings have grown steadily for decades','A commentator with strong, confident views'],answer:1,reveal:'The loan officer deals with money; the commentator talks about it. Only the friend has done the specific thing, with their own resources, for long enough to exclude luck.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Take counsel on money only from people who have handled their own money competently, over time. Proximity to a subject is routinely mistaken for competence in it, and from outside the two look the same.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'THE MONEYLENDER\u2019S REBUKE',text:'Arkad entrusts his first year\u2019s savings to a brickmaker buying jewels abroad. The jewels are worthless. His teacher, the moneylender Algamish, is unsympathetic: why ask a brickmaker about jewels? Advice is given away freely, he says, so take only the advice that is worth having.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Evidence, not credentials',text:'The test the parable implies is not a title or a job. It asks whether this person has done the thing successfully with their own resources, over a period long enough to exclude luck. That is a higher bar than most advice is held to, including a good deal of professional advice.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'An eager listener, a pair of confident hands, and the brickmaker\u2019s real expertise lying ignored on the bench. Closeness to a subject is easy to see; competence is not.'},{kind:'limits',text:'A track record can be luck that has not yet run out, and those who failed at the same thing are rarely the ones giving advice. Someone\u2019s results may also depend on a situation unlike yours. And the test can wrongly dismiss a trained adviser bound by rules to act in your interest. It raises the bar without removing the guesswork.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Closeness to a subject looks like competence from outside.','Ask what they have done, with their own money, and for how long.','Even a good record can be luck. Hold the verdict loosely.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE SAME MISTAKE, AGAIN',prompt:'Which of these people is repeating Arkad\u2019s mistake?',options:['Someone following a brilliant surgeon\u2019s tips on which shares to buy','Someone asking a long-time saver how she built her savings','Someone checking a fund\u2019s results over fifteen years first','Someone ignoring a hot tip from a stranger at a party'],answer:0,why:'Excellence in one field says nothing about another. The surgeon is the brickmaker: honest, able, and outside their subject.'}]}},'richest-man-in-babylon_5':{pictureCaption:'One mark in every square, week after week. Nothing clever in any of them. The result is in the unbroken run.',bridge:'The first four ideas were the rules. This one is the book\u2019s claim about them: that keeping them matters more than knowing them.',basics:{title:'Small rules, kept for a long time',beats:[{kind:'picture',kicker:'A MARK IN EVERY SQUARE',caption:'One mark in every square, day after day. Nothing clever in any one of them. What counts is that there are no gaps.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'Which saves more by the end of the year?',options:['A big burst of saving in January','A small amount every single week'],answer:1,reveal:'Big bursts tend to stop. Small amounts can keep going. Just \xa32 a week for a year is \xa3104, and it is easy to keep up.'},{kind:'idea',text:'You do not need a clever trick to have money. You need a few plain rules. Then you keep them going for a long time, without stopping.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A PATH ACROSS GRASS',text:'Walk across the grass once and nothing changes. Walk the same way every day and a path appears. No single walk made it. All of them did.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What breaks it',text:'The danger is the gap. You skip one week because you want something. Then it is easier to skip the next. Soon the rule is gone. Keeping it going matters more than how big each bit is.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one money rule you know, like saving one coin in ten. Make a mark on a calendar each week you keep it. Try not to leave a gap.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Leo saves \xa31 a week, but stops when he wants something. Ava saves 50p a week and never stops. Who is following this idea?',options:['Ava, because she never stops','Leo, because he saves more each week','Both of them, in just the same way'],answer:0,why:'The idea is about keeping a plain rule going. A rule full of gaps is not really a rule.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE MISSING SECRET',belief:'Getting out of money trouble takes a clever insight that most people do not have.',reality:'Most financial difficulty is not a knowledge problem. The rules are few and widely known. What is rare is applying them without interruption for years.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A thin purse is a fixable condition, and the fix is a plan followed consistently, not a stroke of fortune. Small sums, saved and put to work by rule over a long period, produce a result out of proportion to what went in.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'WHERE THE RESULT COMES FROM',low:'One brilliant move',high:'The same dull rule for years',note:'The book sits at the right-hand end. Its parables are memorable so that the rules get kept, not just admired.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why consistency beats cleverness',text:'The book\u2019s rules work through time. Saving first only pays if it happens every payday. Money put to work only compounds if it is left alone. A plan kept for five years and dropped for one loses not just that year but everything that year would have earned later.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'JUST THIS ONCE',text:'A couple has saved a tenth every month for three years. A holiday comes up, and they pause the saving, meaning to restart in the spring. The pause becomes a year. Nothing about the plan was wrong. It was simply not kept.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The same small mark in every square, week after week. No single square matters much. The unbroken run is the whole result.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick the plain rule you already know, and run it without interruption for a year. Mark each month that it holds.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Which of these people is following the book\u2019s real argument?',options:['Someone who has saved the same tenth every month for six years','Someone who has read every book on money and saves when inspired','Someone who saved very hard for a year and then took a break','Someone who keeps switching to the newest savings trick'],answer:0,why:'The book is about repetition, not insight. Only the six-year saver has run a plain rule without interruption.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE BOOK\u2019S ACTUAL ARGUMENT',text:'The parable form invites reading the book as a collection of maxims: save a tenth, guard your gold, own your home. That undersells it. The maxims are secondary. The frame of the whole book is that a lean purse is a fixable condition, and that the fix is a plan, not luck.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Nothing here is clever, and that is the point. Small sums, saved and deployed to a rule over a long period, produce a result that feels out of proportion to the inputs. The difficulty is repetition, not insight.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Read as maxims',items:['Rules to agree with','Each one judged alone','Admired, then forgotten']},right:{title:'Read as a plan',items:['Rules to run','Each one feeding the next','Kept without a break for years']},note:'Agreeing with the rules costs nothing. The book is about the cost of keeping them.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Compounding, without the formula',text:'The disproportion is compounding, met in idea 3. Clason describes it again and again through stories of gold that earns and earnings that earn, never as a formula. Because growth builds on growth, a plan kept for twenty years ends well over twice the size of one kept for ten. Interruption is expensive for the same reason.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A mark in every square, with no gaps. Each mark is trivial. The run is what the book is really about.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The plan requires a surplus, and the book is silent about incomes too low to produce one. It is written for someone who has a margin and is misspending it, which is a real condition but not a universal one. Told to someone with no margin, the promise that a lean purse is fixable can sound like a judgement on their character.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which money rule do you already know and agree with, but have never run for a full year without a break?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HONEST OBJECTION',prompt:'Which objection to the book\u2019s central claim is strongest?',options:['A plan kept for years needs a surplus that some incomes never produce','The rules are too simple to be of any use to most readers','Clever investing usually beats a plain rule kept consistently','Parables are a poor way to teach anything about money'],answer:0,why:'The book is right that most difficulty is not knowledge, but its plan starts from a margin. Where there is none, consistency has nothing to work on.'}]}},'richest-man-in-babylon_6':{pictureCaption:'Most of the chest is its solid base: the money you started with. The few coins on top are what it earned. Guard the base first.',bridge:'Idea 3 sent savings out to work and idea 4 asked who advises. This one decides what comes before either: not losing the money.',basics:{title:'First, don\u2019t lose it',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'HALF IS GONE',prompt:'You had \xa320. You lose half, so now you have \xa310. How much must your \xa310 grow to get back to \xa320?',options:['It must grow by half','It must double'],answer:1,reveal:'Half of \xa310 is only \xa35. That gets you to \xa315. To reach \xa320, the \xa310 must double. Losing is quick. Winning it back is slow.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Before you think about how much your money could grow, make sure you will not lose it. Getting back what you lost is much harder than losing it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Most of this chest is the solid base: the money you started with. The few coins on top are what it earned. Guard the base first.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'A DEAL AT SCHOOL',text:'A boy in your class says: give me \xa35 today and I will give you \xa310 next week. He says he is selling sweets. It sounds great. But ask yourself one thing first. If he does not pay, how would I get my \xa35 back?'},{kind:'steps',title:'Before you hand over money',steps:['Ask: how would I get it back?','Ask: who has done this before, and how did it go?','Only then ask: how much could I get?']},{kind:'explain',kicker:'FROM THE BOOK',text:'In Clason\u2019s stories about Babylon, the people who end up poor often made the same mistake. They wanted big winnings. They did not check that their money was safe. The book says to check safety first.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A friend says your \xa310 could be \xa330 by the summer if you give it to her. What should you ask first?',options:['How would I get my \xa310 back if it goes wrong?','Could it turn into even more than \xa330?','Can I give you \xa320 instead of \xa310?'],answer:0,why:'Safety comes before winnings. If you cannot get it back, a big promise does not help you.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'A TIP AT A WEDDING',text:'An old school friend tells you about a scheme paying three per cent a month. He has been in it a year and the payments have always arrived. You have a few thousand pounds saved, the first you have ever had. He can get you in this week.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Guard the money before you think about the return. Invest only where you can get it back, after asking people experienced in handling it, and distrust earnings that look large. An avoidable loss costs more than an equal missed gain.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Return first',items:['How much could I make?','How fast?','Who else is getting in?']},right:{title:'Safety first',items:['How would I get it back?','Who has done this before?','What happens if it fails?']},note:'Every story of ruin in the book starts in the left column.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why caution is not timid',text:'Losses and gains are not symmetrical. Lose half of \xa34,000 and you have \xa32,000; to get back to \xa34,000 you now need a gain of a hundred per cent, not fifty. So an avoidable loss costs more than a gain of the same size is worth. Guarding the principal is arithmetic, not nerves.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The heavy base is the money you put in; the small pile on top is what it has earned. Lose the base and the pile goes with it.'},{kind:'steps',title:'The questions, in order',steps:['State how you would get the money back, and how quickly.','Name who has done this before, and how it went for them.','Ask why the return is as high as it is.','Only now compare returns.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Back to the wedding. What does this idea say to do about the three-per-cent scheme?',options:['First find out how you would get the money back if it failed','Put a small amount in first and see whether payments keep coming','Join quickly, since a year of payments is solid evidence','Compare it with other schemes that pay even higher returns'],answer:0,why:'The return is the last question. A high return with no clear way to reclaim the principal is the shape of every ruin in the book.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'equation',kicker:'THE ARITHMETIC OF RUIN',parts:['\xa3100, down 50%','then up 50%'],result:'\xa375, not \xa3100',note:'A fall and a rise of the same size do not cancel. The loss is taken from a larger base than the recovery has to work with.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Before any question of return comes the question of survival: can this money be reclaimed? A loss needs a larger gain to repair it, so avoiding losses is worth more than chasing gains of the same size.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHERE IT SITS IN THE BOOK',text:'One of Arkad\u2019s cures is to guard your treasure from loss, and the book\u2019s laws of gold warn that money flees those who chase impossible earnings or invest in what they do not understand. The rule: study each investment before parting with money, invest only where the principal can be reclaimed, and consult people experienced in its handling.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it matters most for a beginner',text:'Most popular writing about money is about returns. This is about survival. The distinction matters most for the reader the book addresses: someone with a small first surplus that took years to build and cannot absorb losing it. For that reader, one avoidable loss can undo the habit, not just the sum.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A chest that is mostly base, with a small pile of earnings on top. The order of attention runs bottom to top: the principal, then the return.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Nothing is perfectly safe, and safety has its own cost: money kept where it cannot fall usually earns little and loses ground to rising prices, the danger in idea 3. Taken to the extreme, the rule becomes never investing at all. And fraudsters know this rule; many schemes advertise safety above everything. A promise of safety is not evidence of it.'},{kind:'recap',points:['A loss needs a bigger gain to repair it.','Ask how you would get it back before asking what it pays.','Safety has a cost too. Idle money is not free of risk.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE ERROR',prompt:'A fund lost 40% last year and gained 40% this year. Its manager says investors are back where they started. What is wrong?',options:['\xa3100 fell to \xa360, and 40% of \xa360 only brings it to \xa384','Nothing, since a 40% fall and a 40% rise cancel each other','Only that fees would have eaten part of the recovery','The gain was larger in pounds than the loss had been'],answer:0,why:'The recovery works on a smaller base. Losses cost more than gains of the same size, which is why the book guards the principal first.'}]}},'richest-man-in-babylon_7':{pictureCaption:'The key is what Clason thinks rent money should buy instead. The curling receipt is rent, paid and gone. Whether the trade is wise is unsettled.',bridge:'Idea 6 guarded the principal. This cure aims at the largest payment most people make, and is the one to read most critically.',basics:{title:'Renting or owning your home',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'A BIKE EVERY SATURDAY',text:'You hire a bike every Saturday for \xa33. After a year you have paid \xa3156. And you still own no bike. If you had saved up and bought one, you would have a bike to keep.'},{kind:'term',word:'Landlord',meaning:'The person who owns a home and lets other people live in it. The people living there pay the landlord every month. That money is called rent.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Clason said money paid as rent is gone, but money paid to own your home builds up into something you keep. That is partly true. Owning a home costs money too.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The key is what Clason thinks home money should buy. The long paper is rent, paid and gone. Is the key always the better deal?'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Renting',items:['The money is gone','The landlord fixes the roof','Easy to move away']},right:{title:'Owning',items:['You build up something you keep','You pay to fix the roof','Harder to move away']},note:'Both have costs. The book only talks about one side.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHAT IS STILL TRUE',text:'For most families, the home is the biggest thing they pay for each month. So choices about the home matter more than most other money choices. That part of the idea holds up well.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Mo says renting is always a waste, so owning is always better. What is the best reply?',options:['Owning has costs too, like repairs, so it depends','Yes, rent money is gone, so owning always wins','No, owning a home is always a bad idea'],answer:0,why:'Clason was right that rent is gone once paid. But owning costs money too, so neither is always better.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE BIGGEST PAYMENT',prompt:'For most households, which regular payment is the largest?',options:['Food','Housing','Transport','Energy bills'],answer:1,reveal:'Housing, usually by a distance. Clason\u2019s fifth cure is aimed squarely at it, and that part of his instinct is sound.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Clason argues you should own the roof you live under: rent paid is gone, while payments towards ownership accumulate, and security lengthens how far ahead you plan. The financial claim needs more care than he gives it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'His argument',text:'Rent buys a year of shelter and nothing more. Payments towards a home of your own, he says, build into an asset. He adds that a man whose home is his own thinks differently about his work, and plans further ahead.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHAT THE ARGUMENT LEAVES OUT',left:{title:'What it counts',items:['Rent paid and gone','Payments building an asset','The security of owning']},right:{title:'What it ignores',items:['Repairs, insurance and buying costs','Interest on the loan','Prices that can fall','Being tied to one place']},note:'The comparison is real, but it has two sides and the book weighs one.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What survives',text:'The narrower version is true. Housing is usually the largest recurring expense, and decisions about it shape a household\u2019s finances more than any investment choice. Clason\u2019s conclusion, therefore buy, does not follow automatically. It depends on prices, interest rates, costs and how long you will stay.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A key on the step, and a long receipt curling away. Clason sees only these two. The repair bills and the costs of moving are not in the picture.'},{kind:'try',text:'Identify the largest regular payment you make that buys you nothing at the end, and work out what replacing it would actually take, costs included.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Sam expects to move cities for work within two years. A friend says rent is dead money, so buy now. What is the sound reply?',options:['Buying and selling costs could outweigh two years of rent, so it depends','The friend is right, since every rent payment is money gone for good','Renting is always better, because owning ties you to one place','Buy anyway, because property prices can be relied on to rise'],answer:0,why:'The book\u2019s true point is that housing decisions dominate. Its conclusion, always buy, ignores the costs of buying and moving, which matter most for a short stay.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE WEAKEST CHAPTER',belief:'Rent is dead money, so buying your home is always the prudent choice.',reality:'This is Clason\u2019s fifth cure, and his least reliable. Rent buys shelter, flexibility and freedom from repair bills, and ownership carries costs and risks the chapter never counts.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Clason recommends owning your home, since payments build an asset where rent is simply gone. The true kernel is narrower: housing is usually the largest recurring cost, so housing decisions dominate a household\u2019s finances. That buying follows from this does not.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The argument as made',text:'Arkad presents ownership as both a financial and a psychological gain. Payments that would have gone to a landlord build an asset, and a man secure in his own home thinks differently about his work and plans further ahead. The second claim is a reasonable observation. The first carries the weight.'},{kind:'term',word:'Leverage',meaning:'Buying with borrowed money, so that a small deposit controls a large asset. It magnifies gains when prices rise and losses when they fall.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What does not follow',text:'The chapter assumes property appreciates, and ignores maintenance, insurance, the costs of buying and selling, and lost mobility. It treats a heavily leveraged purchase of a single asset as plainly prudent, which sits oddly beside the caution of idea 6. Written in 1926 for one market, its confident generality has not travelled well.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Key against receipt: the whole of Clason\u2019s comparison. What the picture leaves out, like what the chapter leaves out, is where the decision is actually made.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The idea fails wherever its assumptions fail: falling prices, a short stay, high interest, large repair bills. It can still be right for someone staying put for many years where buying costs less than renting over that span. The error is presenting a conditional judgement as a rule, and forgetting that rent buys flexibility and hands the price risk to someone else.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE BOOK AGAINST ITSELF',prompt:'Which other rule in the book sits most uneasily with the advice to buy a home?',options:['Guard the principal: a leveraged single asset is not cautious','Pay yourself first: a loan payment comes before any saving','Control your expenses: a home is a desire, not a need','Take competent advice: estate agents know about property'],answer:0,why:'The book says to invest only where the principal is safe. Putting a first surplus into one asset bought with borrowed money is the kind of concentrated risk that rule warns against.'}]}},'richest-man-in-babylon_8':{pictureCaption:'He walks down the middle of the market, head level. At every stall where he owes, a small payment is already waiting.',bridge:'Idea 1 took a tenth first. Dabasir\u2019s plan takes every share first, debts included, and works for the same reason.',basics:{title:'Seven to live, two to pay back, one to keep',beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'A CAMEL TRADER IN DEBT',text:'In one of Clason\u2019s stories, Dabasir is a camel trader in Babylon. He owes money to lots of people. He feels so ashamed that he hides from them in the market. Then he makes a plan.'},{kind:'equation',parts:['7 to live on','2 to pay back','1 to keep'],result:'All 10 coins',note:'Of every 10 coins Dabasir earns, 7 are for living, 2 are shared out among all the people he owes, and 1 is saved.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Split your money the same way every time it comes in. Some to live on, some to pay back what you owe, some to keep. Pay everyone you owe a little, every time.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'He walks down the middle of the market, head up. At each stall where he owes money, a small coin is already waiting.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'WITH YOUR POCKET MONEY',text:'Say you owe your brother \xa34 and a friend \xa32. You get \xa310. You keep \xa31. You spend up to \xa37. The last \xa32 is split: \xa31 to your brother, \xa31 to your friend. Next week, the same again.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why pay everyone a little?',text:'If you pay one person and skip the other, the other one feels forgotten. They might get cross. When everyone gets something each time, nobody is left out. You never need to hide.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Zara owes three friends money. Her pocket money comes in. What would Dabasir\u2019s plan say?',options:['Give each friend a little, and keep some','Pay one friend back in full first','Keep it all until she can pay everyone'],answer:0,why:'Every friend gets something each time. And Zara still keeps her one part in ten.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE OTHER SIDE OF THE STREET',text:'You owe money to four people: a card company, a relative, an old landlord and a friend. You pay whichever is shouting loudest. When you see the friend\u2019s car outside the shop, you wait until it has gone.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Fix the split before the money arrives: seven-tenths to live on, two-tenths divided among every creditor, one-tenth kept. Everyone you owe gets something every time, so nobody is being avoided.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Dabasir\u2019s plan',steps:['When money comes in, set one-tenth aside to keep.','Live on seven-tenths, whatever cutting that takes.','Divide two-tenths fairly among everyone you owe.','Tell each creditor the plan, and keep to it.']},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the twenty matters',text:'Retellings often drop the debt share and keep only the saving. But without it the saving is offset by borrowing, and the arithmetic fails. The debt share is what makes the scheme hold together, and what lets the man walk through the market again.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'One creditor at a time',items:['Whoever pushes hardest gets paid','The rest hear nothing','You avoid half the street']},right:{title:'Everyone, every time',items:['Each creditor gets something','Each one knows the plan','Nobody to hide from']}},{kind:'picture',caption:'Walking down the middle of the market, not along the edges. Each stall he owes already has its share.'},{kind:'try',text:'If you owe several people, pay all of them something on the same day each month, however small, rather than clearing one at a time.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Which part of the seventy-twenty-ten plan does most to change how the debtor lives from day to day?',options:['The two-tenths shared among every creditor','The one-tenth he keeps for himself','The seven-tenths he is allowed to live on','The exact sizes of the three shares'],answer:0,why:'Paying every creditor something each time ends the avoiding. The numbers matter less than the structure, decided once and kept.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE CLAY TABLETS',text:'One chapter is framed as clay tablets dug up and translated, recording how Dabasir, a camel trader of Babylon, climbed out of debt. Around them Clason sets letters from a modern professor who tries the plan on his own debts. It is the book\u2019s most concrete prescription.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Seventy per cent to live on, twenty to creditors, ten kept. The value is not in the numbers but in fixing them in advance and applying them to income as it arrives, so the split is never renegotiated under pressure.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WITHOUT THE DEBT SHARE',nodes:['Save a tenth, ignore the debts','Short each month','Borrow to cover it','The saving is cancelled out'],note:'Drop the twenty and the ten is undone by new borrowing. With it, debt falls while saving grows.'},{kind:'term',word:'Pre-commitment',meaning:'Deciding a rule in advance so that a later, pressured self does not get to choose. The rule is set once, applied automatically and revisited rarely.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The same mechanism as idea 1',text:'Paying yourself first works because the order is fixed before spending begins. Dabasir\u2019s plan extends that to debt: every share is taken from each payment at once, so the month adjusts to seven-tenths. Whether seventy is right for a given person is plainly arguable. The structure is what transfers.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'No longer keeping to the edges of the market. The shares were set before he arrived, so every stall he owes is already paid something.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Paying everyone a fixed share is not always the cheapest route. Where one debt charges far more interest than the others, clearing it first usually costs less overall, and some debts carry heavier penalties or legal priority. Where seven-tenths cannot cover living costs, the plan cannot start. What it does best is the human side: it ends the hiding.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'WEIGH THE TRADE',prompt:'Someone owes a friend \xa3500 interest-free and a card company \xa33,000 at a high rate. What is the fairest view of Dabasir\u2019s split here?',options:['It keeps both relationships whole but may cost more in interest','It is clearly best, since every creditor must be treated alike','It fails entirely, because only the card debt really matters','It does not apply, since the plan only works for small debts'],answer:0,why:'The plan\u2019s strength is that nobody is avoided. Its cost is that a share sent to the interest-free debt is not cutting the expensive one.'}]}},'richest-man-in-babylon_9':{pictureCaption:'The blade does the cutting; the stone, worn from use, keeps it able to. This cure sharpens the earner rather than counting the earnings.',bridge:'Every earlier rule divides an income. This last cure is the only one that makes the income larger.',basics:{title:'Get better at the thing that earns',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'WASHING CARS',text:'You wash cars on your street for \xa33 each. At first one car takes you an hour. Then you learn a few tricks. A better sponge. Rinse from the top. Soon a car takes half an hour, and it looks better too.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Saving is about the money you have. This idea is about you. Get better at what you do, and you can earn more. Then the same habits give you more to save.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The knife does the cutting. The stone keeps it sharp. This idea is about sharpening the one who earns the money.'},{kind:'flow',nodes:['Learn and practise','Do better work','Earn a bit more','Have more to save'],note:'One coin in ten of a bigger pile is a bigger coin.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the book puts it last',text:'Earning more sounds like the best trick. But if you have not learned to save, more money just means more spending. So the book teaches saving first. Then getting better at your work.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one job you do that someone values, like a chore or a job for a neighbour. Find one way to do it better this week.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Ali earns \xa35 a week walking a dog. Which step is this idea about?',options:['Learning to walk two dogs at once, safely','Saving 50p of his \xa35 each week','Spending less of it on sweets'],answer:0,why:'Saving and spending less matter too. But this idea is about growing what he can earn.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE SAME TENTH',text:'Two people each save a tenth of their pay. One earns the same for ten years. The other spends a few evenings a week getting better at her work, and within a few years earns a third more. Her tenth is now a third larger, with no change in the habit.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Cultivate your own ability to earn: study, become more skilful, learn more of your craft. It is the only cure that raises the number every other rule is a percentage of.'},{kind:'equation',parts:['A growing skill','A kept saving habit'],result:'A growing surplus',note:'Either alone falls short. Skill without the habit becomes bigger spending; the habit without skill saves a tenth of a fixed sum.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it comes last',text:'Arkad\u2019s seventh and final cure turns from the money to the earner. The order has a logic: a larger income without the saving habit simply produces larger spending, so the habit comes first. Then the effort goes into the thing that produces the money.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where the advice runs out',text:'It is also the book\u2019s thinnest chapter. It amounts to working harder and learning more of your craft. It says nothing about which skills, how to choose between them, or what to do when the craft itself stops being needed. The principle is sound; the guidance stops where the difficulty starts.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A blade drawn across a stone worn hollow from use. The sharpening is slow, repeated and unglamorous, like the saving it is meant to multiply.'},{kind:'try',text:'Put the same discipline into one skill this year that you put into saving: a fixed amount of time, taken first, every week.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Dan saves a tenth faithfully, but his pay has not changed in six years. What does the seventh cure suggest?',options:['Invest steadily in a skill that would raise his earning power','Raise his saving to a fifth to make up the difference','Move his savings into something with higher returns','Accept it, since the saving habit is what really matters'],answer:0,why:'The other cures work on a fixed sum. Only this one changes the sum itself.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'keyline',kicker:'A PERCENTAGE OF WHAT?',text:'Every rule about how much to save is a percentage of something, and only one rule changes the something.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The earner is the underlying asset. Improving your own ability to earn returns more than any allocation of a fixed income, because it raises the figure that every other cure divides up.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE SEVENTH CURE',text:'Arkad\u2019s last cure for a lean purse is to increase your ability to earn. He treats skill as the asset that produces the surplus everything else depends on: the man who keeps learning his craft, and works at doing it better, is richly rewarded for it.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO READINGS OF THE ORDER',left:{title:'Last on purpose',items:['The habit must come first','More income without it means more spending','Earn more once you can keep it']},right:{title:'Last by mistake',items:['Saving needs a surplus to exist','This chapter is about making one','For many readers it is the first step']},note:'Both are defensible. Which applies depends on whether you have a margin yet.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A whetstone worn hollow by years of strokes. The tool that earns is kept able to earn, and the upkeep is never finished.'},{kind:'limits',text:'It is the thinnest chapter. It amounts to working harder and learning more, with no account of which skills, how to choose, or what happens when a craft becomes obsolete. Skill does not always find a market, and earnings depend on far more than effort. The principle is sound; the guidance stops exactly where the difficulty starts.'},{kind:'recap',points:['The other cures divide income; this one enlarges it.','Without the saving habit, a raise turns into spending.','The book gives the principle, not the method.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'WHICH ORDER, FOR WHOM?',prompt:'For whom is the book\u2019s order, the saving habit first and earning power last, most clearly the wrong way round?',options:['Someone whose income does not yet cover basic needs','Someone with a good income who still saves nothing','Someone whose spending rises with every pay rise','Someone who saves well and earns a steady wage'],answer:0,why:'With no surplus there is nothing to save first. For that reader, raising earning power is the only cure that can start.'}]}}}},3002,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'science-and-hypothesis_1':{pictureCaption:'Nothing in this empty field says where to start measuring. The peg is placed, not found, and every measurement afterwards depends on it.',basics:{title:'Some rules are picked, not found',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'ON THE ROAD',prompt:'In Britain, cars drive on the left. In France, they drive on the right. Which side is the true side?',options:['The left','The right','Neither. Each country picked one.'],answer:2,reveal:'No test could show that the left is right. People picked a side. But the choice still matters. If drivers picked sides at random, there would be crashes.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Some rules in science were picked, not found. People chose them because they make the work easier. No test forced them.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'There is nothing in this field to measure from. So the worker picks a spot and hammers in a peg. All the measuring starts there.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'ON A THERMOMETER',text:'On one scale, water freezes at 0. On another, it freezes at 32. Neither scale is wrong. Someone chose where zero goes. The water does not care which one you use.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Picked, but not silly',text:'A picked rule is not a wild guess. A bad choice makes the work harder, like a ruler with odd marks on it. A good choice makes the rest simple. So people pick with care, then stick to it.'},{kind:'try',text:'Find a rule at school that was picked, not found. The start time? The order of lessons? Ask: does it make things easier? Could it have been picked another way?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Which of these was picked by people, not found by looking?',options:['That a week has seven days','That ice melts when it gets warm','That the moon goes round the Earth'],answer:0,why:'Nothing in nature makes a week seven days long. People chose it. Ice melting and the moon\u2019s path were found by looking.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE TEAM HANDBOOK',text:'A new colleague asks why the team writes dates as year, then month, then day. One person says that is simply the correct way. Another says it was agreed years ago because the files then sort in order. Only the second answer is about what the rule is.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Some principles in science are neither discoveries nor truths of logic. They are conventions: chosen because they make everything else simpler. They cannot be true or false, only more or less convenient.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Discovered',items:['Found by looking','Could have turned out otherwise','Can be shown false']},right:{title:'Adopted',items:['Chosen by people','Kept because it helps','Judged by how well it works']},note:'A single theory usually contains both. The skill is telling them apart.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Free, but not arbitrary',text:'A convention is chosen, not forced, but it is not random. Pick a clumsy one and every calculation after it gets harder. Experiment does not test the convention on its own. It tests the whole package: the convention plus the laws written in its terms.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'No mark in this field says where to begin. The peg is placed, not found, and every measurement after it depends on that choice.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one long-standing rule in your team. Ask two questions: was it discovered or agreed? And does it still make things simpler than the alternatives?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Two teams argue that their own way of numbering software versions is the correct one. What is the better question to ask?',options:['Which scheme makes the work simpler for everyone involved','Which scheme was the first one anybody used','Which scheme the experts agree is the true one','Which scheme can be proved from basic logic'],answer:0,why:'A numbering scheme is a convention. It cannot be true or false, so the useful question is how much it simplifies.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'PARIS, 1902',text:'Henri Poincar\xe9 was one of the leading mathematicians of his age, at work on celestial mechanics and the new non-Euclidean geometries. In 1902 he published a book for a wide public asking a careful question: which parts of science are learned from the world, and which parts did we put there ourselves?'},{kind:'idea',text:'Some scientific principles are neither empirical discoveries nor logical necessities. They are conventions, adopted because they are convenient, and they are not the kind of thing that can be true or false.'},{kind:'term',word:'Convention',meaning:'In Poincar\xe9\u2019s sense, a principle we are free to choose, guided by experience but not forced by it. It is judged by how much it simplifies, not by whether it is true.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Experiment tests the package',text:'His key move is that experiment never touches a convention alone. It tests the whole system: geometry plus physics, for instance. You may keep any geometry you like and adjust the physics to compensate. The data constrain the combination, not each part separately.'},{kind:'equation',parts:['A chosen convention','Physical laws'],result:'What experiment tests',note:'Change the first and the second can be rewritten to keep every prediction the same. That freedom is what makes the first a choice.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'An empty field with no landmark to align against. The first peg is a decision, and every line measured later inherits it.'},{kind:'limits',text:'This is not relativism about facts. Experiment still rules on the system as a whole, and a convention that makes physics hopelessly complicated gets abandoned. The harder problem is spotting conventions in practice. The label should be argued for, case by case, because calling a claim a convention also exempts it from evidence.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'Someone says: if parts of science are conventions, scientists can believe whatever they like. What is the best reply?',options:['Experiment still judges the whole system, and clumsy conventions get dropped','Conventions are rare, so the worry hardly ever arises in practice','Conventions are true by definition, so they cannot mislead anyone','Only mathematics has conventions; physics is pure discovery'],answer:0,why:'The choice is free only within what the evidence allows for the whole package, and convenience is a real constraint.'}]}},'science-and-hypothesis_2':{pictureCaption:'A vast, perfect heap of stones beside the plan of a house nobody has started. The heap will never build itself into the house.',bridge:'Idea 1 showed that some rules are chosen. This one shows that even which facts to collect is a choice the facts cannot make.',basics:{title:'A heap of stones is not a house',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'PROJECT NIGHT',text:'You have a project on birds. You write down every bird fact you can find. Sixty facts! Then you sit down to write, and you have no idea what to say.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Facts are like stones. You need them to build. But a heap of stones is not a house. You need a plan that says which stones you need and where they go.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A huge, neat heap of stones. Next to it, the plan of a house. Nobody has started building. The heap alone will never turn into a house.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the plan comes first',text:'There are more facts than you could ever collect. Most of them are no use to you. A good question tells you which ones to look for. Without one, you just keep piling.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE BIRD PROJECT AGAIN',text:'Now start with a question: *why do some birds fly south for the winter?* At once you know what to look for. Food, cold, how far they go. Most of your sixty facts can stay in the notebook.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you start a project, write your question before you look anything up. Then only collect facts that help to answer it.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Sam has every score from every match this season. He wants to know why his team keeps losing. What does he need next?',options:['A question, like whether they lose more away','More scores from older seasons','A neater way to write out the list'],answer:0,why:'More facts will just make the heap bigger. A question tells him which facts matter.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Science starts by collecting facts. Once you have enough, the theory emerges.',reality:'Poincar\xe9 argues the reverse. Facts are endless and most are worthless. Choosing which to gather is the real work, and that choice cannot come from the facts themselves.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Facts are to science what stones are to a house: needed, but not enough. The organising idea is not decoration on top of the data. It is what makes the data mean anything.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE WEEKLY DASHBOARD',text:'Your team tracks forty numbers about customers. At every review someone asks why sales dipped, and someone else says you need more data. A year later you have far more data and exactly the same question.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What makes a fact worth having',text:'Poincar\xe9\u2019s answer is that a good fact recurs. It tells you about more than itself, so one observation carries weight for cases you have not yet seen. Only a hypothesis tells you which facts are likely to do that.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Collecting first',items:['Gather whatever can be measured','Wait for a pattern to appear','More data, same confusion']},right:{title:'Framing first',items:['Name what might explain the dip','Gather what would settle it','Fewer facts, each doing work']},note:'The second team is not more biased. It is the only one that can be shown wrong, and so the only one that can learn.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'An immaculate heap of stones, and beside it a house plan nobody has begun. The stones are ready. What is missing is the plan.'},{kind:'try',text:'Stuck on a decision despite plenty of information? Write one sentence that would explain the situation if it were true. Then ask which single fact would test it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A school records every detail about pupils who leave early, hoping a pattern will appear. A year on, none has. What is missing?',options:['A hypothesis about why they leave, to decide what to record','A larger sample, since patterns need many more cases','Better software to search the records for patterns','More years of records before drawing any conclusion'],answer:0,why:'More records make a bigger heap. A hypothesis says which facts matter, which is what turns records into an answer.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHERE THE IMAGE COMES FROM',text:'In his chapter on hypotheses in physics, Poincar\xe9 compares facts to the stones of a house. The image is often repeated as praise of data. Its point is nearly the opposite: stones are necessary, and an accumulation of them is still not a house.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The choice of which facts to gather cannot be made by the facts. Theory comes before data in the order of work, and collecting without a hypothesis produces heaps, not science.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE ORDER OF WORK',nodes:['A guess about what matters','Facts chosen to test it','A result that generalises','A sharper guess'],loop:!0,note:'The popular account of method starts with the facts. Poincar\xe9\u2019s point is that you cannot choose them without the first box.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why recurrence matters',text:'Facts are infinite, and most say nothing beyond themselves. The ones worth having recur: one observation lets you predict others. Deciding which facts are likely to recur is already a judgement about how the world is organised, which is to say a hypothesis.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A builder\u2019s yard from above: the heap is complete, the house is not begun. Nothing in the heap says where any stone should go.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Theory first has its own failure. A frame chosen too early decides what you notice, and can hide the very fact that would have overturned it. Open exploration has a place, especially when a field is young. The claim is about what turns observations into science, not a ban on looking around.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of a pile of information you are sitting on. What question was it collected to answer? If none, what should that question be?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HONEST OBJECTION',prompt:'Which objection to putting theory before data does Poincar\xe9\u2019s view have to take seriously?',options:['A frame chosen too early can stop you seeing what would refute it','Facts are finite, so collecting all of them is always possible','Hypotheses are guesses, so they have no place in science','Theories are only summaries of facts already gathered'],answer:0,why:'A hypothesis selects facts, and selection can exclude the awkward ones. The answer is to keep the hypothesis at risk, not to collect without one.'}]}},'science-and-hypothesis_3':{pictureCaption:'A key filed smooth slides into every lock on the wall. A key that fits everything tells you nothing about which door is yours.',bridge:'Idea 2 said you need an idea before facts mean anything. This one asks how to tell whether an idea says anything at all.',basics:{title:'An answer that fits anything says nothing',beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'The best answer is one that fits whatever happens.',reality:'If it fits whatever happens, it never takes a risk. It can\u2019t tell you what will happen next. A good answer says what should *not* happen.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE LUCKY SOCKS',text:'Jo says her socks are lucky. Her team wins: the socks worked. Her team loses: the socks were tired. A draw: the socks were half lucky. Jo is never wrong.'},{kind:'idea',text:'An idea that fits every result tells you nothing. A real idea says what should not happen. Then you can check it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'This key has been filed smooth. It slides into every lock on the wall. That is exactly why it can\u2019t tell you which lock is yours.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How to check an idea',text:'Ask: what would show this is wrong? Say Jo claimed the socks only work at home games. Then an away win would prove her wrong. Now her idea says something. Now you can test it.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick something you believe about yourself, like *I\u2019m bad at maths.* Ask: what would prove it wrong? If nothing could, it is not telling you much.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Which of these ideas could a test show to be wrong?',options:['Our class plant grows faster by the window','Things happen when they are meant to','Whatever happens, it was for the best'],answer:0,why:'If the plant by the window grows slower, the idea is wrong. The other two fit anything, so they tell you nothing.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TWO FORECASTS',prompt:'One forecaster says tomorrow will be dry. Another says it may be dry, or wet, or somewhere between. Which tells you more?',options:['The first','The second, because it cannot be wrong'],answer:0,reveal:'The second is never wrong and never useful. The first rules out rain. It can fail, and that is exactly why it is worth hearing.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A hypothesis flexible enough to fit any result has stopped being testable. A theory\u2019s value lies in what it forbids, and one that forbids nothing is a vocabulary rather than a claim.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE QUARTERLY REVIEW',text:'A manager explains every quarter by market sentiment. Good quarter: positive sentiment. Bad quarter: negative. Flat: mixed. The phrase has fitted every result for three years, which is the problem. It has never once said in advance what would happen.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'A claim',items:['Names results that would sink it','Can fail','Predicts']},right:{title:'A vocabulary',items:['Absorbs every result','Cannot fail','Describes after the fact']},note:'A vocabulary can still be handy for talking. It just is not a finding.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A key filed down to a blank shaft opens every lock on the rack. Its fit with any one lock proves nothing.'},{kind:'try',text:'Take an explanation you rely on and ask: what result would make me drop it? If you cannot name one, treat it as a way of talking, not a finding.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A consultant\u2019s model has explained every outcome so far, including two that contradict each other. What should worry you most?',options:['It forbids nothing, so its fit tells you nothing','It has not yet been tested on enough cases','The contradictory outcomes are probably errors','It is too simple to capture the details'],answer:0,why:'A model that fits opposite results would have fitted anything. Its success is a fact about the model\u2019s flexibility, not about the world.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'TOO MANY MODELS',text:'In Poincar\xe9\u2019s day, physicists built mechanical models of light and electricity out of hidden wheels, springs and fluids. Several reproduced the known results equally well. Poincar\xe9 pointed out that once a phenomenon has one mechanical explanation, it has infinitely many.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When a hypothesis can accommodate any observation, its fit is evidence about its flexibility, not about nature. A hypothesis earns its keep by being at risk: by naming results that would finish it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What a good fit can hide',text:'If many models fit equally, the fit cannot choose between them. Worse, it flatters each one. A model that would have fitted any data has learned nothing from the data it fits. The useful question is what each one rules out.'},{kind:'term',word:'Falsifiability',meaning:'A theory\u2019s capacity to be refuted by some possible observation. The philosopher Karl Popper later made the word famous. Poincar\xe9 was working with a practical version of the idea decades earlier.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A key with every tooth filed away opens the whole rack. Its success with any single lock is a fact about the key.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Real science bends. When a result clashes with a theory, it is often reasonable to adjust a side assumption rather than drop the core, and where rescue ends and evasion begins is a judgement, not a rule. Poincar\xe9\u2019s own conventions also forbid nothing, by design. He thought that was fine, so long as nobody mistakes them for claims.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which explanation in your work has never once been embarrassed by a result? Is that strength, or flexibility?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HARD CASE',prompt:'A theory meets an awkward result, and its defenders add an assumption to absorb it. When is that legitimate?',options:['When the added assumption makes predictions of its own that could fail','Whenever the theory already has a long record of success','Never: any adjustment made after the fact is evasion','When most experts in the field agree to the change'],answer:0,why:'An added assumption is fine if it is itself at risk. Postulating an unseen planet to save Newton\u2019s laws was testable, and led to Neptune.'}]}},'science-and-hypothesis_4':{pictureCaption:'Several maps, each flattening the round world a different way. The hand is not asking which is true. It takes the one that suits the job.',bridge:'Idea 1 stated the claim. This is the case it came from, and what happened to it afterwards.',basics:{title:'Which map is the true one?',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'FLAT MAPS OF A ROUND WORLD',text:'The Earth is round. A map is flat. You can\u2019t flatten a ball without stretching it somewhere. So mapmakers choose where to stretch. One map keeps shapes right. Another keeps sizes right. None of them is the one true map.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Lots of maps, each one flattening the world a different way. The hand is not asking which is true. It picks the one that suits the job.'},{kind:'idea',text:'There is more than one set of rules for shapes and space that works. Asking which set is *true* makes no sense. Ask which is handiest for the job.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Rules about shapes',text:'At school you learn that the angles of a triangle add up to 180 degrees. That is one set of rules about shapes. Other sets work too, with no mistakes in them. Draw a big triangle on a ball, and its angles add up to more.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO SAID THIS',text:'Henri Poincar\xe9 was a great French maths thinker. In 1902 he wrote a book about science. He said asking which set of shape rules is true is like asking if metres are truer than feet.'},{kind:'try',text:'Measure your desk in hand spans, then in pencils. You get two different numbers. Is one of them wrong? Which would you use to tell a friend?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You have two maps of your town. One shows bus routes clearly. One shows street names clearly. Which is the true map?',options:['Neither. Pick the one that suits your trip.','The one with the street names','The one that was made first'],answer:0,why:'Both maps are right about the town. They are made for different jobs, so you choose by the job.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'A NEW KIND OF SPACE',text:'For two thousand years, Euclid\u2019s geometry was taken as the truth about space. In the nineteenth century, mathematicians built other geometries, just as free of contradiction, in which parallel lines behave differently. Suddenly there were several consistent options.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When several geometries are each consistent, asking which is true is the wrong question. Experiment tells us about bodies, not about space itself, so the question becomes which geometry is most convenient.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why experiment cannot settle it',text:'Suppose you measured a vast triangle with light rays and its angles did not add up to 180 degrees. You could say space is curved. Or you could keep Euclid and say light rays bend. The data fit both. You choose whichever keeps the rest simplest.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Several maps of the world, each on a different projection. None is the true one. The hand takes the one that lies flattest for the job.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'So Poincar\xe9 thought geometry was a matter of taste.',reality:'The choice is constrained: experiment judges geometry and physics together. Within that, choosing a geometry is like choosing the metric system over older measures. Neither is truer. One may be far handier.'},{kind:'try',text:'When a debate about which framework is right has stalled, check whether both fit the evidence. If they do, change the question to which one makes the work easier.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Two teams plan the same project, one by tasks and one by deliverables. Both predict the same deadlines. What does Poincar\xe9\u2019s view suggest?',options:['Stop arguing about which is true and use the handier one','Run both until events prove one of them false','Adopt whichever plan the senior people prefer','Merge them, since the truth must lie between'],answer:0,why:'If both fit everything that can be checked, there is no truth left to argue about. Only convenience remains.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'timeline',kicker:'THE QUESTION AND ITS ANSWER',points:[{when:'c. 300 BCE',what:'Euclid sets out the geometry that is taught for two thousand years'},{when:'1820s\u20131850s',what:'Lobachevsky, Bolyai and Riemann develop consistent non-Euclidean geometries'},{when:'1902',what:'Poincar\xe9 argues that asking which geometry is true has no meaning'},{when:'1915',what:'Einstein\u2019s general relativity describes gravity with a non-Euclidean geometry'}]},{kind:'idea',text:'Geometry is a system of definitions and conventions, not a report about space. A result that seems to refute a geometry can be absorbed by revising the physics instead, so the real question is convenience.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The argument',text:'Experiment measures bodies: rods, light rays, the paths of things. It never measures bare space. A surprising measurement can be read as a fact about space or about how rods and light behave. Hence his conclusion that one geometry cannot be more true than another, only more convenient.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The same world on different projections. Each distorts something; none is the true one. The choice is made by the job.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What relativity did',text:'Poincar\xe9 expected Euclid\u2019s geometry to remain the most convenient. General relativity made a curved geometry the convenient way to describe gravitation. The answer changed. On a friendly reading, it changed in the way his framework allowed: on grounds of convenience for the whole system of geometry plus physics.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Whether relativity vindicated him is still argued. Einstein later granted the point in principle but held that, in practice, geometry can be treated as something experiment tests. Poincar\xe9 described the structure of the question well, and was wrong about which choice would prove convenient.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Experiment tests geometry and physics together, never geometry alone.','Between consistent geometries, choose by convenience.','The convenient choice can change. His framework allowed for that; his forecast did not.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'One physicist describes some results with curved space. Another uses flat space plus forces that bend rulers and light. What would Poincar\xe9 say?',options:['Both are admissible; prefer whichever keeps the laws simplest','Only one matches reality, so more data will settle it','The curved description is true, as relativity confirmed','Neither is science, since neither can be tested'],answer:0,why:'If both make the same predictions, experiment cannot choose. The combination is testable; the split between geometry and physics is a choice.'}]}},'science-and-hypothesis_5':{pictureCaption:'The piece goes in on a hunch. Checking whether it truly fits comes next, and is not optional.',bridge:'Idea 2 said the hypothesis has to come before the facts. This one asks where a hypothesis comes from.',basics:{title:'Guess first, then check',beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'ON THE BUS',text:'Henri Poincar\xe9 was a great French maths thinker. Once, he worked for days on a hard problem and got nowhere. He gave up and went on a trip. As he stepped onto a bus, the answer popped into his head.'},{kind:'explain',title:'But he still checked',text:'He felt sure he was right. But feeling sure is not proof. When he got home, he worked it all out on paper. Only then did he know.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Big ideas often start as guesses. Good guesses come to people who have worked hard on a problem. Then you have to check the guess with care.'},{kind:'flow',nodes:['Work hard on it','Take a break','A guess pops up','Check it carefully'],note:'Skip the first step and no guess comes. Skip the last step and you might be wrong.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The orange piece goes in on a hunch. Now comes the next job: does it really fit?'},{kind:'try',text:'Stuck on a puzzle? Say your best guess out loud, even if you are not sure. Then find a way to test it.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Mia has a feeling she knows the answer to a riddle. What should she do?',options:['Say her guess, then check if it works','Keep quiet until she is totally sure','Trust it, since the feeling is so strong'],answer:0,why:'Saying the guess gives her something to test. Checking it tells her if the feeling was right.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Discoveries come from following the scientific method, step by step.',reality:'Poincar\xe9 was unusually frank that discovery involves guessing, and that the guesses come from taste and long immersion. Method arrives afterwards, to check them. Teaching only the checking makes science look inhuman.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Discovery runs on informed guesses, which grow out of long work on a problem. Method is how guesses get checked, not how they get made. Both halves are needed.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'STEPPING ONTO A BUS',text:'In a later essay, Poincar\xe9 described days of fruitless work on a mathematical problem, a break to go on a trip, and the solution arriving whole as he stepped onto a bus. He felt certain at once. He still checked it carefully when he got home.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Discipline on both sides',text:'He insists the flash never comes without the hard work before it. And the certainty it brings is not evidence: a sudden insight can feel just as sure and still be wrong. The romantic moment sits between two stretches of graft.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A hand presses a piece into the mechanism on a hunch. Measuring whether it fits is the next job, and it is not optional.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you have a hunch at work, say it out loud and design the check. Throwing it away because it is only a guess discards your only candidate.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'An engineer has a strong hunch about the cause of a fault but no proof yet. What does Poincar\xe9\u2019s account suggest?',options:['State the hunch and design a test that could rule it out','Set it aside until a systematic search turns something up','Act on it at once, since strong hunches are usually right','Ask the team to vote on whether the hunch is plausible'],answer:0,why:'The hunch is the candidate. The test is what turns it into knowledge, or rules it out.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'A LATER ESSAY',text:'The fullest version of this account is Poincar\xe9\u2019s essay on mathematical invention, collected in *Science and Method* (1908). It draws on his own early work on a class of functions he named Fuchsian functions, and it is among the most detailed first-hand descriptions of discovery a mathematician has left.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Conscious work prepares the ground, the unconscious keeps combining possibilities, and a sense of mathematical beauty lets only promising ones through. Verification afterwards is obligatory, because the feeling of certainty is not evidence.'},{kind:'steps',title:'The sequence he describes',steps:['Long conscious work that seems to produce nothing','A pause: the problem is set aside','Sudden illumination, with a sense of certainty','Deliberate verification, worked through in full'],note:'The psychologist Graham Wallas later drew on accounts like this for his four-stage model of creative thought.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why beauty',text:'Poincar\xe9 thought the useful combinations are also the beautiful ones, and that a feeling for elegance is what filters them before they reach awareness. That is why taste, not method, decides which guesses arrive.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The piece is placed on a hunch, in a dark workshop. The instruments for checking it are still on the bench.'},{kind:'limits',text:'This is introspection by one exceptional mind, and introspection is weak evidence about how thinking works. An unconscious that sorts by beauty is his hypothesis, not a finding. Illumination stories are also easy to romanticise: they hide the flashes that turned out wrong and the years of preparation behind the right one.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these is a misuse of Poincar\xe9\u2019s account of discovery?',options:['Skipping verification because the insight felt certain','Stepping away from a problem after long, hard work','Writing down a hunch before you can justify it','Letting taste decide which idea to test first'],answer:0,why:'He is explicit that certainty is not evidence. The romantic middle only counts with the discipline on both sides.'}]}},'science-and-hypothesis_6':{pictureCaption:'A smooth curve through scattered points, and a jagged zigzag drawn beside it. The points do not decide between them; we choose the smooth one.',bridge:'Idea 4 chose a geometry because it was convenient. Here the same move is made over every law: preferring the simple one.',basics:{title:'We pick the simple answer on purpose',beats:[{kind:'picture',caption:'Dots on the left page, with one smooth curve through them. On the right, a hand draws a sharp zigzag.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'Which line would you use to guess where the next dot will go?',options:['The smooth curve','The zigzag'],answer:0,reveal:'Most people pick the smooth one. But notice: the dots did not tell you to. You chose it because it is simple and easy to use.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When two answers both fit what we see, we pick the simpler one. That is our choice, not a rule of nature. Nature does not have to be simple.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE MISSING BISCUITS',text:'Biscuits are gone. Answer one: your brother ate them. Answer two: a mouse got in, a bird took some, and Gran had a few. Both could be true. You check the simple one first, because it is easier to check.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why call it a choice?',text:'Because simple answers are sometimes wrong. Scientists have had simple rules that later needed fixing. If you call the simple answer a choice, you stay ready to change it.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you pick the simple answer, say *I\u2019m choosing this one* instead of *this is what happened.*'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Two ideas explain why the class plant died. One is simple. One is messy. What is the honest thing to say?',options:['I\u2019ll go with the simple one, but I\u2019m not sure','The simple one must be the true one','The messy one must be true, since life is messy'],answer:0,why:'Picking the simple one is fine. Just say it is your pick, not a fact the plant told you.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TWO EXPLANATIONS',prompt:'Sign-ups to a website fell. One account: a broken button. Another: five small factors together. Both fit the data. Which do you look into first?',options:['The broken button','The five small factors'],answer:0,reveal:'Almost everyone starts with the button, and sensibly. But notice what drove the choice: it is easier to check and to act on. The data did not favour it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Preferring the simpler of two accounts that fit the same observations is a decision about how we work, not a discovery about the world. Nature is not obliged to be simple.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Infinitely many curves',text:'Plot any set of measurements and infinitely many curves pass through the points. We draw a smooth one and treat the small misses as measurement error. That is a decision, not a deduction from the data.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Scattered points with one smooth curve laid through them, and beside it a hand sketching a jagged zigzag. The points do not choose between the two; we do.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Elegance is evidence: the simpler theory is more likely to be true.',reality:'Poincar\xe9 says the preference is justified by use. Complicated hypotheses are unusable, not shown to be false. And laws that looked simple have often been replaced by messier ones.'},{kind:'try',text:'When you choose the simpler explanation, say so: we are choosing this one because we can test it and act on it. Do not write that the evidence favoured it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A report says the data clearly favour the simpler of two models. In fact both fit equally well. What should it say?',options:['That both fit, and the simpler was chosen because it is easier to use','That the simpler is probably true, as simple models usually are','That no conclusion is possible until a third model is tried','That the more complex model wins, since it has more detail'],answer:0,why:'The choice is legitimate. Reporting it as a verdict of the data is not, because the data did not give one.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',word:'The curve-fitting problem',meaning:'Any finite set of data points is matched by infinitely many curves. Choosing one of them, usually the smoothest, cannot be justified by the points themselves.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The preference for simple laws cannot be justified by evidence. It is a working rule we adopt because complicated hypotheses are unusable. A good rule, but ours, so elegance should never be reported as evidence.'},{kind:'explain',title:'His argument',text:'Poincar\xe9 notes that the history of science shows simple-looking laws replaced by more complicated ones. Yet without assuming some simplicity, no generalisation is possible at all: the next point could lie anywhere. So simplicity is a precondition of the work, not a result of it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A smooth curve through scattered points, and a jagged zigzag being drawn beside it. The points alone do not decide between them. Choosing the smooth one is a working rule, not a finding.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Simplicity as a rule',items:['Chosen because it is usable','Stated as a choice','Revised without fuss']},right:{title:'Simplicity as evidence',items:['Read as a sign of truth','Reported as a finding','Defended when the data turn']},note:'The same preference. Only the second mistakes our convenience for a fact about nature.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Some argue simplicity does carry evidential weight: a model with fewer adjustable parts is less able to fit noise, so its success is harder to fake. That is idea 3 again. And simple depends on notation: a law that is short in one language can be long in another, which weakens any claim that nature itself prefers it.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which explanation do you favour mainly because it is tidy? What would you expect to see if the untidy one were true?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE ERROR',prompt:'A physicist argues that a theory must be right because its equations are beautiful. What is Poincar\xe9\u2019s objection?',options:['Beauty is a reason to use a theory, not evidence that it is true','Beautiful equations are usually a sign of a mathematical error','Only experiments on new particles can count as evidence','Physics should prefer complicated theories to avoid bias'],answer:0,why:'Simplicity and elegance justify adopting a theory as a working choice. They do not show that nature is built that way.'}]}},'science-and-hypothesis_7':{pictureCaption:'The gauges in this case are set by the reference block. So no reading from them could ever find the block wrong.',bridge:'Idea 3 asked what an explanation forbids. Here the answer is nothing, and that is fine, as long as you know it.',basics:{title:'Some rules can\u2019t be wrong, and that matters',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE SCHOOL RULE',text:'Your school says a pupil is on time if they arrive before the bell. Can that rule ever turn out false? No. It just tells you what *on time* means. Nobody would go out and test it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Some rules in science look like things people found out. But really they say what a word means. No test could ever prove them wrong.'},{kind:'explain',title:'A rule about pushes',text:'Scientists say a harder push makes a thing speed up more. But how do you tell how hard a push was? By how much the thing sped up! So the rule can\u2019t fail. It tells you what a push is.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The tools in this box are set by the orange block. So they can never show the block is wrong. It is what *right* means for them.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Is that bad?',text:'No. A rule about what words mean can still be very useful. It sets up the game. Just don\u2019t treat it as if a test had proved it.'},{kind:'try',text:'Take a rule like *real friends always stick up for you.* Ask: could anything prove it wrong? Or does it just say what *real friend* means?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Which of these could a test prove wrong?',options:['Dogs can hear sounds people can\u2019t','A triangle has three sides','A week has seven days'],answer:0,why:'A test could show dogs hear no more than we do. The other two just say what the words mean, so no test could prove them wrong.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE ENGAGEMENT METRIC',text:'A team defines an engaged user as one who returns within a week. A year later someone presents a finding: engaged users come back within a week. The room nods. Nobody notices that the finding was the definition.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Some apparent laws of nature are definitions in disguise. They cannot be refuted, because the thing they seem to describe is identified by the law itself. That can make them useful, but never evidence.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Poincar\xe9\u2019s example',text:'Newton\u2019s second law says force equals mass times acceleration. It looks like a finding. But we have no way to measure force or mass apart from that very relation. So the law ends up defining what a force is, rather than reporting a discovery.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The gauge is calibrated against the block, so no reading from it can show the block is wrong. The block is the standard, not a measurement.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'A finding',items:['Some observation could count against it','Tested against the world','Usable as evidence']},right:{title:'A definition',items:['No observation could count against it','Judged by usefulness','Usable to organise the work']},note:'Both are legitimate. The mistake is presenting the second as the first.'},{kind:'try',text:'For a principle you rely on, ask what observation would count against it. If none could, label it a definition and judge it by its usefulness.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A firm defines its best performers as the staff with the highest ratings, then reports that its top-rated staff are its best performers. What is wrong?',options:['The finding repeats the definition, so it is not evidence','Ratings are subjective, so the sample must be larger','Ratings should be collected more often to be reliable','The firm should compare itself with its competitors'],answer:0,why:'Nothing could have come out otherwise. A result that could not have failed tells you nothing new.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'THE PRINCIPLES OF MECHANICS',text:'Poincar\xe9\u2019s chapters on mechanics take the founding principles one at a time and ask of each: is this a result of experiment, a truth of logic, or something else? His answer unsettled readers. Several, he argued, are neither. They are definitions, and so cannot be refuted.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Some apparent laws of nature are definitions in disguise. A definition can organise a productive framework and do real work, but it is not empirical work, and it should not be cited as evidence.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The circle',text:'Take the second law: force equals mass times acceleration. To test it, you would need to measure force and mass some other way, and there is no way independent of the relation itself. So no observation can catch the law out. It fixes what we mean by a force.'},{kind:'term',word:'Constitutive principle',meaning:'A principle that helps define the terms of a framework rather than describing the world inside it. You do not test it. You ask whether the framework it makes possible is useful.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The measuring tools and the block they are calibrated against, side by side in one case. The block defines correct; it cannot be measured wrong.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The diagnosis is cleaner on the page than in a laboratory. Whether a principle is a definition can depend on how the rest of the theory is set up, and principles have moved between the two roles. Calling a claim just a definition can also shield a real assumption from scrutiny, which is the evasion idea 9 warns about.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Find one principle in your own field that is cited as evidence. What observation would count against it?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE DIAGNOSTIC',prompt:'A theory holds that every action is selfish, and counts any generous act as hidden self-interest. What does Poincar\xe9\u2019s diagnostic say?',options:['Nothing could count against it, so it works as a definition, not a finding','It is well supported, because it has explained every case so far','It is refuted by any single clearly generous act','It needs a larger survey of human behaviour to settle'],answer:0,why:'Every possible act is classified as selfish in advance. That makes it a way of describing actions, not a discovery about them.'}]}},'science-and-hypothesis_8':{pictureCaption:'A ball balanced on the ridge of a roof. A nudge too small to see decides which side of the house it ends up on.',bridge:'The last few ideas were about what we choose. This one is about a limit we do not choose: how precisely we can know.',basics:{title:'Why tiny things can decide big ones',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'HEADS OR TAILS',text:'You flip a coin. Heads or tails? You call it luck. But the coin just follows the flick of your thumb. Flick a tiny bit harder, and it lands the other way up.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Chance often means a tiny cause we can\u2019t see is having a big effect. The result still has a cause. We just can\u2019t measure the tiny thing well enough.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A ball sits on the very top of a roof. The tiniest nudge decides which side it rolls down. Too small to see, but it decides it all.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO SAID THIS',text:'Henri Poincar\xe9, a French maths thinker, wrote about this over 100 years ago. He used the weather as one example. A tiny change in the air today can grow into a big change later. That is why weather is hard to predict far ahead.'},{kind:'flow',nodes:['A tiny change','It grows and grows','A big difference'],note:'The middle step is the key. Some things keep small changes small. Some things make them grow.'},{kind:'try',text:'Roll a marble down a slope three times from what feels like the same spot. Does it stop in the same place? Look for the tiny change that made the difference.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A ball balanced on a fence post falls to the left. Why did it not fall to the right?',options:['A tiny push we did not notice sent it left','Nothing caused it. It was pure luck.','Balls always fall to the left'],answer:0,why:'Something tipped it, even if it was too small to see. That is what we mean by chance here.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE ROULETTE WHEEL',text:'A roulette wheel is pure mechanics: a spin, a ball, friction. Nothing in it is uncaused. Yet nobody can predict the number. That is because a difference in the spin far too small to notice changes where the ball lands.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Chance is not an absence of cause. In some systems tiny differences grow. So a cause too small to notice produces an effect we cannot ignore. The randomness lives in the gap between our precision and the precision needed.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Balanced on the ridge, the ball must fall one way or the other. Which way depends on a difference nobody could measure.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Forgiving systems',items:['Small errors stay small','A rough measurement is enough','Forecasts hold']},right:{title:'Sensitive systems',items:['Small errors grow','No measurement is precise enough','Forecasts decay']},note:'The difference is not in the causes. It is in what the system does with a small error.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Random is a diagnosis',text:'Calling something random usually ends the search. Poincar\xe9\u2019s account starts one: somewhere a small, unmeasured difference is being amplified. Find the point where it grows. You may still not predict the outcome. But you can often make the system less sensitive.'},{kind:'try',text:'When something keeps going wrong with no pattern, look for the step where a small unmeasured difference gets amplified: a setting, a timing, a hand-off.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A production process fails about one run in ten, with no visible pattern. What does Poincar\xe9\u2019s view suggest?',options:['Look for a small unmeasured difference the process amplifies','Accept it as random, since some failures have no cause','Run it more often so that the failures average out','Replace the whole process, since it cannot be predicted'],answer:0,why:'Unpredictable is not uncaused. The failures point to a sensitivity somewhere, and that is something you can look for.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'FROM THE PLANETS',text:'Poincar\xe9 came to this through celestial mechanics. Studying three bodies pulling on one another by gravity, he found that their orbits could be tangled beyond any simple formula, and extremely sensitive to exactly where the bodies started. An abstract difficulty became a general account of why some things cannot be forecast.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Where small differences in starting conditions produce large differences in outcome, prediction fails even though the system is fully determined. Chance is the name we give to that amplified ignorance.'},{kind:'flow',nodes:['A tiny difference at the start','The system amplifies it','A large difference in outcome','We call it chance'],note:'Every step is caused. Only the first is invisible to us.'},{kind:'explain',title:'His examples',text:'He gives the weather, the roulette wheel, and a cone balanced on its point, which must fall but in a direction nobody can foresee. In each case the error in our knowledge of the start does not stay bounded. It grows.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'An unstable equilibrium: the ball must fall, and which side of the house it reaches turns on a difference below any measurement.'},{kind:'term',word:'Sensitive dependence',meaning:'The property of a system in which tiny differences in starting conditions grow into large differences in outcome. Chaos theory, decades later, made it a central idea.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Not all chance is like this. Quantum physics, which came after him, appears to involve randomness that is not hidden detail. And some unpredictability comes from very many small causes adding up, as in a gas, rather than one cause being amplified; Poincar\xe9 discussed that kind too. Knowing which kind you face changes what you can do about it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'Forecasters cannot say whether it will rain in three weeks. Astronomers can say when an eclipse will happen in fifty years. Why the difference?',options:['Over those spans the planets\u2019 motion barely amplifies small errors','Astronomers have far better instruments than weather forecasters','Eclipses are rarer, so there is less to get wrong','Weather is uncaused, while the planets follow laws'],answer:0,why:'Both systems are governed by laws. The difference is how fast each one turns a small error into a large one.'}]}},'science-and-hypothesis_9':{pictureCaption:'Three compartments for three kinds of assumption. The hand sets the risky one apart, because that is the one the evidence can destroy.',bridge:'This pulls the book together: the safe assumptions, the free choices of ideas 1 and 7, and the risky claims idea 3 asked for.',basics:{title:'Not every guess is the same kind',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'BAKING A CAKE',prompt:'You are baking a cake. Which of these guesses could ruin it if it is wrong?',options:['Weighing in grams, not ounces','The oven is hot enough','The moon won\u2019t change the taste'],answer:1,reveal:'Grams or ounces makes no real difference if you stick to one. The moon is so far away you never think about it. The oven is the guess that could ruin the cake.'},{kind:'idea',text:'There are three kinds of guess in science. Some are so safe we hardly notice them. Some are just handy choices. Some are real claims a test could prove wrong.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Three trays for three kinds of guess. The orange one gets a tray of its own. It is the kind a test could prove wrong.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The three kinds',text:'One: safe guesses, like *things very far away hardly matter here.* Two: handy choices, like grams or ounces, which change nothing you can see. Three: real claims, like *the oven is hot enough.* Only the third kind can let you down.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'IN AN ARGUMENT',text:'Your friend says your class trip plan is bad because you wrote the times in the 24-hour clock. That is just a handy choice. It is not the problem! Ask instead: which part of the plan could really go wrong?'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you argue about a plan, sort it first. Which parts are just handy choices? Which part could really fail? Argue about that one.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your team plans to win by passing more. Which part is a real claim that a match could prove wrong?',options:['Passing more will help us score','We call our idea Plan B','The pitch will still be there on Saturday'],answer:0,why:'The name is a handy choice. The pitch is a safe guess. Only the passing idea could fail on the day.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHY ARGUMENTS MISFIRE',text:'When people argue about a theory, they are often arguing about different parts of it. One side attacks an assumption the other treats as bookkeeping. The other defends a risky claim as if it were mere convention. Poincar\xe9 offers a way to sort them.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Hypotheses come in three kinds. Natural ones are those we barely notice. Indifferent ones are handy scaffolding. Real generalisations are ones that evidence can confirm or destroy. Mixing them up is where most arguments about a theory go wrong.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Ask of each assumption',steps:['Would anyone really doubt it? If not, it is natural.','Could you swap it without changing any prediction? Then it is indifferent.','Otherwise it is a real generalisation, and it carries the risk.']},{kind:'example',kicker:'A SALES FORECAST',text:'A forecast assumes the shop is open on weekdays, which is natural. It is set out in months rather than weeks, which is indifferent. And it assumes customers will pay ten per cent more without leaving: a real generalisation. A critic attacks the months. The forecast stands or falls on the price.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A sorting tray with three compartments. Most tokens could be swapped for each other. The hand sets the one that carries real risk apart.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before defending a model, list its assumptions. Sort them into the ones you could change freely and the ones the result depends on. Defend the second kind.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A critic attacks your sales forecast for using monthly rather than weekly periods. How should you respond?',options:['Show the choice changes no prediction, and ask which real claim they doubt','Redo it weekly, since every criticism deserves a full answer','Argue that months are the true way to measure sales','Concede the forecast is unproven until the periods are justified'],answer:0,why:'The periods are scaffolding. Pointing that out moves the argument to the assumption that can actually fail.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'A sound theory is one whose every assumption has been tested.',reality:'Poincar\xe9 argues that many assumptions are not the kind of thing that could be tested. Demanding proof of all of them is a category error. So is hiding the testable ones behind that fact.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Hypotheses are not one kind of thing. Natural ones are almost inevitable, indifferent ones are interchangeable scaffolding, and only real generalisations carry risk. Criticism aimed at the wrong class misses.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The three classes',text:'His natural example is that the effect of very distant bodies can be neglected. Indifferent hypotheses are those where the choice changes nothing observable, such as two ways of picturing the same calculation. Real generalisations are claims experiment can confirm or destroy, and they alone carry real risk.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Category error',items:['Attacking an indifferent choice as unproven','Demanding evidence for bookkeeping']},right:{title:'Evasion',items:['Defending a real claim as mere convention','Shielding a risk from the evidence']},note:'Each mistake puts an assumption in the wrong compartment.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two compartments of interchangeable tokens, and one token lifted out on its own. Knowing which assumption is load-bearing is the skill.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How it ties the book together',text:'Indifferent hypotheses share something with the conventions of idea 1: a free choice no observation can overrule. Real generalisations are the risky claims idea 3 asked every theory to contain. Researchers sort these implicitly. Poincar\xe9 says it out loud.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The boundaries move. A hypothesis that looks indifferent can become testable when new instruments arrive: for decades many physicists treated atoms as a convenient picture before experiment made them a real claim. And the sorting is itself a judgement, open to the same evasion it is meant to catch.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HARD CASE',prompt:'A researcher, challenged on an assumption that drives all her results, replies that it is only a convenient convention. What is the verdict?',options:['Evasion: an assumption the result depends on is a real claim','Fair: any assumption may be called a convention if it is useful','Fair, so long as the assumption is mathematically simple','A category error by the critic, who should test results instead'],answer:0,why:'If changing the assumption would change the result, it is not indifferent. It carries the risk and must face the evidence.'}]}}}},3003,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'science-of-getting-rich_1':{pictureCaption:'Every tool of a laboratory is laid out and nothing is being tested. The book has the look of a science and no experiment.',basics:{title:'A science with no tests',beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'A BOOK FROM 1910',text:'In 1910 an American writer, Wallace Wattles, wrote a book called *The Science of Getting Rich*. He said that if you think about money in a certain way, and act in a certain way, you will get rich.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'He called it a science. What makes something a science?',options:['It sounds clever and sure of itself','It is tested, and a test could show it wrong'],answer:1,reveal:'Science means testing. You check an idea against what really happens. If the idea is wrong, the test can show it. Wattles never tested his idea.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The book calls itself a science, but it is not one. Nothing in it was tested. And no result could ever prove it wrong.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A LUCKY SOCK',text:'Say you have a lucky sock. Your team wins? The sock worked. Your team loses? You did not believe in it enough. The sock is never wrong. That does not mean it works. It means nobody can check it.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE SAME TRICK',text:'The book works like the sock. If you get rich, the method worked. If you do not, you did not believe hard enough. There is no proof that thinking a certain way brings money.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A lab table with all the tools set out. But nothing is being tested. It looks like science, and that is all.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time an advert promises something, ask one question: what would show that it does not work?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A poster says a drink makes you better at sport. If it does not work, it says, you drank it the wrong way. What is wrong with that?',options:['No result could ever show the drink does not work','The drink probably does not taste very nice','Nothing, as long as lots of people buy it'],answer:0,why:'A fair test could show the drink does nothing. The poster blames you first, so no test can ever count against it.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'A book called a science has been tested like one.',reality:'The title is the only scientific thing about it. Wattles, writing in 1910, offers no measurements, no trials and no results. He states that thought shapes wealth and builds a method on the statement.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The Science of Getting Rich is not a science. It has no evidence, measures nothing, and is built so that no result could ever count against it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How it protects itself',text:'The method comes with total certainty and a repeatable formula. When it fails, the failure belongs to the reader: you doubted, you wavered, you did not hold the picture clearly enough. Each failure becomes proof that the reader was at fault, never the method.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE CLOSED LOOP',nodes:['A confident promise','You follow the formula','It does not work','You must have believed too little'],loop:!0,note:'Nothing that happens can break the loop. That is what makes the method untestable.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why say it first',text:'The title has spent more than a century lending the book an authority it never earned. Clearing that up first lets the later lessons here take its useful parts on their own merits, and leave the rest.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Microscope, test tubes, flask, all set out. No sample, no reading. The equipment of a science, with no experiment running.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one method you have been sold: a diet, a course, a productivity system. Ask what result its author would accept as disproof. If the answer is none, treat it as a claim, not a finding.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A sales course promises results to anyone who follows it. Those who fail are told they lacked commitment. What is the strongest objection?',options:['No outcome could count against it, so it has never been tested','Commitment is hard to measure without a proper survey','Some of the people who took it did get results','It costs far too much for what it actually offers'],answer:0,why:'Success counts for the course and failure is blamed on the buyer. A claim that survives every possible outcome has not been tested.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'AMERICA, 1910',text:'Wallace D. Wattles published this short book in 1910. He wrote within New Thought, a movement of the period that taught the mind shapes material reality. The book borrows the word *science* for the prestige it carried, and none of its methods.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The book has the vocabulary of a science and none of its substance: no evidence, no measurement, and a structure in which no outcome could ever count against the method.'},{kind:'term',word:'Falsifiable',meaning:'A claim is falsifiable when some possible result would show it to be wrong. Testing depends on that: a claim compatible with every outcome cannot be checked against any of them.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The structure',text:'Three parts carry the book: certainty, a repeatable formula, and failure assigned to the reader\u2019s insufficient belief. The third makes the other two safe. Every failure is relabelled as the reader\u2019s lapse, so the method is confirmed by success and untouched by failure.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'A tested claim',items:['Names a result that would refute it','Is checked against outcomes','Failure counts against the claim']},right:{title:'Wattles\u2019s method',items:['No result could refute it','Is asserted, never checked','Failure counts against the reader']},note:'The right-hand column is the template. Recognise it here and it is much easier to spot in current wealth and success writing.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The full apparatus of a laboratory, and no experiment connected to it. The title does the same work.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Be plain about what is unsupported. The central claim, that thought held in a certain way draws wealth, has no evidence, and the book offers none. Calling the method unscientific does not make every sentence false: some practical advice in it is ordinary and sound, as later lessons show. That advice stands on its own grounds, not his.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A wellness coach says her method heals anyone who trusts it fully, and that relapses reveal hidden doubt. Which question exposes the problem most directly?',options:['What result would you accept as showing the method does not work?','How many clients have you treated with it so far?','Have any doctors publicly endorsed the method?','How long does it usually take to see a result?'],answer:0,why:'Relapse is already explained away as doubt, so counts and endorsements cannot reach the flaw. Asking for a possible disproof does.'}]}},'science-of-getting-rich_2':{pictureCaption:'A stack of the same plan, redrawn again and again, left on the bench. He walks out into daylight with one tool to start.',bridge:'Idea 1 cleared away the false science. Under it is one instruction worth keeping, and it needs none of the rest.',basics:{title:'Stop getting ready. Start.',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE PROJECT DUE FRIDAY',text:'You have a school project due on Friday. On Monday you make a plan. On Tuesday you make a neater plan. On Wednesday you pick new colours for it. On Thursday night you still have not started.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Pick one clear thing you want to do. Then start it today, where you are. Do not keep getting ready.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'He drew the same plan again and again. Now he leaves the pile behind. He walks out the door with one tool, to start.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why planning feels so good',text:'Planning feels like work, but it is safe. A plan can never fail, because nobody has tried it yet. Doing is scarier, because you might get it wrong. So people keep on planning.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHERE THIS COMES FROM',text:'This is the useful part of an old book by Wallace Wattles. He also claimed that thinking the right way brings money. There is no proof of that. But this part is just good advice, and it works without the rest.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE THE DIVING BOARD',text:'Standing on the diving board does not teach you to dive. You can bounce and look down and wait all day. You only learn the jump by jumping.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one thing you keep getting ready for. Do the first real step of it today, even a small one.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Mia wants to learn the guitar. She has watched twenty videos about which guitar to buy. What does this idea say?',options:['Borrow a guitar and learn one chord today','Watch more videos until she is sure','Wait until she can buy the best one'],answer:0,why:'The videos feel like progress, but they keep her safe from trying. One chord today is real progress.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TWO NEWSLETTERS',prompt:'Two people want to start a newsletter. One spends three months on the plan. The other sends a rough first issue this week. Who is likelier to have a good one in a year?',options:['The careful planner','The one who sent the rough issue'],answer:1,reveal:'The rough issue gets real responses, and each one teaches something no plan could. By the end of the year the second writer has a year of feedback. The first may still be planning.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Form one specific intention and act on it today, in your present circumstances, rather than preparing indefinitely or waiting for better conditions.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why planning is tempting',text:'Planning feels productive and carries no risk of visible failure. That is exactly why it slides into the place where acting should be. A plan never meets reality, so it can never be shown not to work.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Preparing',items:['Feels productive','Cannot visibly fail','Revises the plan again','Waits for better conditions']},right:{title:'Acting',items:['Feels exposed','Produces real feedback','Carries out the current plan','Creates better conditions']},note:'The better circumstances people wait for usually arrive because they acted, not before.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'OUT OF THE WORKSHOP',caption:'Every sheet in the stack is the same plan, slightly revised, and the top one is half redrawn yet again. He has turned his back on it and steps into daylight with a single tool.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What to keep from the book',text:'Wattles wraps this in claims that thought shapes reality, and those claims are unsupported. The instruction itself is ordinary advice about getting things done. It is correct without them.'},{kind:'try',text:'Write down one specific thing you intend to do. Then take the first concrete step on it before the day ends, using the plan you already have.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Sam has rewritten his business plan four times and has not yet spoken to a customer. What would this idea suggest?',options:['Talk to five possible customers this week, using the current plan','Hire a consultant to check the plan before doing anything','Wait until the market is clearly ready for the idea','Make one more revision so the plan is finally right'],answer:0,why:'Each revision felt like progress but avoided contact with reality. Acting on the current plan produces the information he needs.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'UNDER THE METAPHYSICS',text:'Most of the book argues that a clear mental picture, held with faith, impresses itself on a formless substance and draws wealth to you. None of that has evidence behind it. Beneath it sits a plain instruction about execution: form a specific intention, then act on it now, where you are.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The defensible core of the book is a claim about decisiveness: act today on the current plan, rather than waiting for better circumstances or revising the plan once more.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE FAILURE MODE',nodes:['A goal feels risky','Planning feels productive and safe','Planning replaces acting','Nothing is tested, nothing improves'],note:'The substitution is comfortable precisely because a plan cannot fail in public.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why circumstances follow action',text:'People wait for the right job, the right capital, the right moment. Wattles argues that the better position usually comes as a consequence of acting in the present one. That holds on ordinary grounds: action produces results, contacts and evidence, and waiting produces none.'},{kind:'keyline',text:'A plan cannot fail. That is what makes it so comfortable to keep writing.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The stack of near-identical plans stays on the bench in the dark; one tool goes out through the door into daylight. Only the second can meet reality, so only it can teach.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The advice survives without the metaphysics; the metaphysics does not survive at all. There is no evidence that holding an intention moves anything outside you, and nothing here depends on it. The advice has its own edge: some work rewards preparation, and acting today on a dangerous or irreversible plan is not decisiveness.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What have you planned at least twice and not started? What first step would expose it to reality?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'A reader objects: if the book\u2019s core theory is false, its advice must be worthless too. What is the best reply?',options:['The advice about acting rests on ordinary evidence and never needed the theory','The theory may be true after all, so the advice keeps its authority','Advice can never be worth more than the theory it came from','The advice only works for readers who also accept the theory'],answer:0,why:'Acting on a plan produces feedback whether or not thought shapes matter. The advice can be judged on its own, and it holds.'}]}},'science-of-getting-rich_3':{pictureCaption:'A crowd of hands grabs at one stall. At the quiet edge, one person builds a stall nobody has thought of yet.',bridge:'Idea 2 said act now. This one is about what to act on: something new, rather than a share of something that already exists.',basics:{title:'Make something new instead of fighting',beats:[{kind:'picture',caption:'Look at the crowd fighting over one stall. Now look at the edge. One person is building a new stall, and nobody is fighting them for it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'You can fight for a share of what is already there. Or you can make something new. Making something new often means you do not have to fight.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE BAKE SALE',text:'At the school bake sale, six tables sell chocolate cake. Each one sells a little. One kid sells cold lemonade. On a hot day, that table is the busiest. It was not fighting the cakes. It sold something else.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO SAID IT',text:'Wallace Wattles wrote this in a book in 1910. He said a person should be a maker, not a fighter. His reason was odd. He thought there was an endless supply of everything. That is not true. But the main point still helps.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'You can always just make something new.',reality:'Not always. Making something new can take money, time or help, and not everyone has those. The idea is a good question to ask, not a promise.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time a game or a club feels too crowded, ask: what is nobody here doing yet?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Ten kids in your class want to run the class newsletter. What would this idea suggest?',options:['Start a class podcast that nobody is doing','Push hard to beat the other nine kids','Give up, since there is no room for you'],answer:0,why:'You are not fighting for one spot. You are making a new one.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'FOUR CAF\xc9S ON ONE STREET',text:'You want to open a caf\xe9. The street already has four. Your plan is to be slightly cheaper than the cheapest. Every sale you make, you take from somebody fighting just as hard to keep it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Competing means fighting for a share of what already exists. Creating means making something that did not exist. When a market feels crowded, ask what is not being made.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Competing',items:['Fights incumbents on their terms','Wins by taking a slice','Price is the main weapon']},right:{title:'Creating',items:['Sets its own terms','Adds something that was missing','Nobody is defending the ground yet']},note:'Wattles drew this line in 1910, and versions of it still run through business writing.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it works',text:'Fighting for an established market means meeting the incumbents where they are strongest: their customers, their prices, their scale. Making something new avoids that fight. This is reasonable strategy, and it needs nothing from the rest of the book.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What he rested it on',text:'Wattles grounds the idea in a claim that supply is unlimited, so nobody need take from anyone. That is not true of most things people want. The strategy survives; the reason he gives for it does not.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The crowd fights over one stall. At the empty edge of the square, someone sets up something nobody is selling.'},{kind:'try',text:'Look at a field you work in. List three things people there want that nobody is making. Is any of them within your reach?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A freelancer finds dozens of designers bidding on the same logo jobs. Which move applies the idea?',options:['Offer a service clients need that nobody nearby provides','Undercut every other bid on the site by a fifth','Bid on even more of the same kind of job','Wait for enough of the competitors to give up'],answer:0,why:'The other moves all fight over the same work. Offering what is missing steps out of that fight.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE DISTINCTION',text:'Wattles separates two minds. The competitive mind tries to take a share of what already exists. The creative mind brings into being what did not exist. He tells his reader to become a creator and never a competitor.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Wealth sought by taking a share of what exists has to be fought for. Wealth sought by making what did not exist need not be taken from anyone.'},{kind:'explain',title:'His grounds',text:'Wattles rests this on his cosmology: a formless substance whose supply is unlimited, so that nobody need take from anyone. No evidence supports that substance, and the supply of most things people want is plainly limited. Read as metaphysics, the idea fails.'},{kind:'explain',title:'As strategy',text:'Read as strategy, it holds. Competing directly for an established market means fighting incumbents on their terms. Making something that did not exist avoids that. The observation survives the removal of his cosmology.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Dozens of hands fight over one existing stall. The person at the edge is building a new one, and has no rival yet.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The unlimited supply on which he builds this has no evidence, and it hides a cost. Creation is not always available: it takes capital, time, skill or the freedom to fail. The advice to stop competing reads very differently to someone without those. Wattles addresses a reader with options and writes as though everyone has them, a pattern throughout the genre.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these is a *misuse* of the idea?',options:['Telling a worker in a one-employer town to stop competing for jobs and create','Asking what a crowded market lacks before entering it','Building a product for a need nobody yet serves','Choosing not to copy a rival\u2019s exact offering'],answer:0,why:'The advice assumes the resources to build something. Handed to someone without them, it becomes a rebuke rather than a strategy.'}]}},'science-of-getting-rich_4':{pictureCaption:'Nothing here is broken. The hands have stopped to look after a machine that already works.',bridge:'Like the advice to create, this one arrives with a mystical reason attached. Again, the habit can be kept and the reason dropped.',basics:{title:'Notice what is already going right',beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Being thankful for what you have makes more good things come to you.',reality:'That was Wallace Wattles\u2019s claim, in his book from 1910. There is no proof of it. Being thankful does not pull in money or luck. But the habit can still help you, for a plain reason.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The plain reason',text:'If you only look at what is going wrong, you get half the picture. You forget what is working. Then you make worse choices, because you are choosing with half the facts.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Spend a little time noticing what is already going well. It does not bring good luck. It helps you see clearly what you have.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'AFTER THE MATCH',text:'Your team lost on Saturday. You only think about the goals you let in. But your passing was the best it has been all year. Forget that, and you might change the very thing that was working.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'This machine is not broken. The hands are looking after it anyway, because it works and is worth caring for.'},{kind:'try',text:'Tonight, write down two things that went well today. Small things are fine.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Leo got a bad mark in maths. Which is the useful kind of noticing?',options:['His homework marks went up, so he keeps doing homework the same way','If he feels thankful enough, his next mark will be better','He forgets the bad mark and only thinks happy thoughts'],answer:0,why:'Noticing what works helps him choose well. Feeling thankful will not change a mark by itself, and ignoring the problem helps nobody.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE QUARTERLY REVIEW',text:'A quarter ends. Sales are down in one region, and the whole review is spent on it. Nobody mentions that two other regions grew, or why. Next quarter everything is reorganised, including what was working.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Make a deliberate habit of accounting for what is already working. Not because thanks attract anything, but because a decision made on half the inventory is a worse decision.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Gratitude puts you in harmony with a creative force and attracts more.',reality:'That is Wattles\u2019s mechanism, and nothing supports it. What survives is the behaviour: a regular, deliberate look at what is going well.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the habit works',text:'Attention is limited. Someone always attending to the gap is working from an incomplete picture of their position. A deliberate accounting of what works restores the missing half, so choices are made on the whole picture.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Watching only the gap',items:['Sees what is missing','Breaks what works by accident','Decides on half the facts']},right:{title:'Counting what works too',items:['Sees what is missing and what is held','Protects what is working','Decides on the whole picture']}},{kind:'picture',caption:'The hands tend a machine that is already running. Attention spent, on purpose, on what is not broken.'},{kind:'try',text:'At the end of this week, list three things in your work that went well and why each did. Check whether any of your plans would change them.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A team missed one deadline. Which response reflects the habit rather than the mystical version?',options:['Reviewing what the team did well before deciding what to change','Feeling grateful so that the next deadline goes better','Refusing to discuss the missed deadline, to protect morale','Discussing only the missed deadline until it is fixed'],answer:0,why:'The habit adds what works to the picture before deciding. It does not replace looking at the problem, and it does not summon anything.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE CHAPTER ON GRATITUDE',text:'Wattles gives gratitude a chapter of its own and presents it as a mechanism, not a sentiment. Gratitude, he claims, brings the mind into harmony with the creative energy behind all things, and so draws more to it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The mechanism is unsupported; the practice is not. A deliberate accounting of what is working shifts attention from the gap to the position, and that improves decisions on ordinary grounds.'},{kind:'equation',parts:['What is missing','What is working'],result:'An accurate inventory',note:'Attend only to the first and you decide on half the facts. Attention is the one variable in his system that plainly exists.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the habit holds',text:'Someone attending continuously to what is missing decides on an incomplete inventory. They may dismantle what was working, or misjudge what they can afford to risk. Counting what is held is not optimism. It completes the record.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The hands tend a working machine. Deliberate attention to what is not broken is the defensible half of his chapter.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Be plain about the trade. You are adopting an attention practice with plausible effects, not engaging a law of nature. There is no evidence that gratitude attracts wealth or anything else, and the book does not permit the distinction: it sells the habit only through the law. The habit also fails when it becomes a reason not to look at what is wrong.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What part of your work is going well that you have not examined in months? What would you lose if you changed it by accident?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SEPARATE THE TWO CLAIMS',prompt:'A friend keeps a gratitude journal and says her business grew because the universe rewards thankfulness. What is the most accurate response?',options:['The journal may help her see clearly; nothing shows it summons growth','The journal cannot have helped, since the book\u2019s theory is false','Her growth confirms that the book\u2019s mechanism really works','Gratitude only works when it is felt sincerely enough'],answer:0,why:'The habit can improve decisions on ordinary grounds. Her growth is not evidence for the mechanism, because nothing about it needs the mechanism to explain it.'}]}},'science-of-getting-rich_5':{pictureCaption:'A small original in a glass case, surrounded by the huge machines that grew from it. Seeing the first makes the rest easier to read.',bridge:'Idea 1 showed the book\u2019s structure. This one explains why it is worth studying anyway: that structure is still being sold.',basics:{title:'Why read a book that is wrong?',beats:[{kind:'guess',prompt:'This book\u2019s big claim is not true. So why would anyone still read it?',options:['To find out how to get rich','To spot the same trick in newer places'],answer:1,reveal:'Lots of newer books, videos and posts repeat its main claim. Seeing the first, plain version helps you spot the claim when it turns up in new clothes.'},{kind:'idea',text:'We study this book to see how it works, not to follow it. It is the first plain version of a claim that many later books copy.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE BIG CLAIM',text:'Wallace Wattles said there is an unseen stuff that all things are made from. He said that if you hold a clear thought, this stuff takes its shape. So thinking hard about being rich would make you rich. There is no proof of any of this.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The little machine in the glass case came first. All the giant machines around it grew out of it.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A FIRST SKETCH',text:'Think of a cartoon character you know. Its first drawing was simple. Later ones got shiny and detailed. Once you have seen the first sketch, you can spot the same shape in every new version.'},{kind:'try',text:'If a video says wishing hard brings money, ask: is this the old claim in new clothes?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A video says: picture a new bike every night and it will come to you. What should you notice?',options:['It is the old claim again, still with no proof','It must be true, because lots of people watched it','It is new, so it must have been tested by now'],answer:0,why:'Newer and more popular does not mean tested. The claim is still that thinking alone brings things, and there is still no proof.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'IN YOUR FEED',text:'A podcast guest explains that wealth begins with a clear vision, held daily. They add caveats: effort matters too, and mindset is only part of it. It sounds measured. It is hard to say what, exactly, is being claimed.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The book belongs here because of its influence, not its soundness. It states plainly the claims that later get-rich writing softens. So reading it makes those later versions easier to judge.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The claim, unsoftened',text:'Wattles claims that all things come from a thinking stuff, and that a thought held in it takes form. Wealth follows from thought. Everything else in the book flows from that. None of it has evidence behind it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One small original under glass, with its huge descendants crowding round it. The structure is clearest in the first.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the original helps',text:'Later versions add hedges that make the claim harder to pin down. The original has none. So you can see the premise and everything that follows from it. Once you have seen that, you can spot the premise under the hedges.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you meet a wealth or success method, find its central claim. Ask what result its author would accept as proof it was wrong.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A course promises that aligning your energy with abundance builds wealth, alongside some sensible budgeting tips. How does knowing Wattles help?',options:['You can spot his unevidenced premise under the sensible tips','You can trust the course, since its ideas are a century old','You can dismiss the budgeting tips along with the rest','You can assume the energy claim has since been tested'],answer:0,why:'The tips can be judged on their own. The abundance claim is Wattles\u2019s premise in new words, and it is still unsupported.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'timeline',kicker:'THE LINE OF DESCENT',points:[{when:'1910',what:'Wallace D. Wattles publishes *The Science of Getting Rich*, out of the New Thought movement.'},{when:'2006',what:'*The Secret*, a best-selling film and book on the law of attraction, names Wattles\u2019s book as its inspiration.'}],note:'In between and since, the claim has been restated many times, usually with more qualification and less clarity.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Read the book as an origin document, not as advice. The claims later sold as the law of attraction appear here unqualified, and seeing them unqualified is the cheapest way to assess what is built on them.'},{kind:'term',word:'Law of attraction',meaning:'The modern name for the claim that thoughts draw matching circumstances to the thinker. It has no scientific support. Wattles\u2019s version is earlier and blunter.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The premise',text:'Wattles posits a thinking stuff from which all things are made, which takes the form of any thought held in it. Hold a clear picture of wealth, with faith and gratitude, and you impress it on the stuff and receive it. Every method in the book follows from this premise. The premise has no evidence.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The plain original under glass, and the elaborate descendants around it. The derivation is visible only in the first.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the original is useful',text:'Later versions add hedges: effort matters too, mindset is only part of it. The hedges make the claim harder to test and easier to accept. The original has none, so its derivation is in plain view, and a reader who has seen it can recognise the premise beneath the hedges.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Influence is not soundness. A claim repeated for a century is evidence of its appeal, not its truth, and nothing in the original or its descendants shows that thought attracts wealth. Studying the book here is not endorsing it. The risk runs the other way too: a reader who dismisses the whole genre may miss the ordinary advice some of it carries.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A modern book says visualising success is just one ingredient, alongside hard work. Which question best uses what the original shows?',options:['Does it claim visualising acts on the world, or only on your own effort?','Is hard work more important than visualising, in percentage terms?','Has the author personally become wealthy using the method?','Is it more cautious than Wattles, and therefore more reliable?'],answer:0,why:'Visualising that sharpens your own effort is an ordinary claim. Visualising that moves the world is Wattles\u2019s premise, hedged, and still unsupported.'}]}},'science-of-getting-rich_6':{pictureCaption:'Every tool in the small room is in use and the work on the bench is finished well. The door to a bigger room stands open.',bridge:'Idea 2 said act now rather than keep planning. This one says act here, in the place you already have, rather than waiting for a better one.',basics:{title:'Do your best where you are now',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A HERMIT CRAB',text:'A hermit crab lives in a borrowed shell. It does not move to a bigger shell until it has grown too big for the one it has. It grows first. Then it moves.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Do not wait for a better team or job before you try your hardest. Do your best where you are now. That is how you grow out of it.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'ON THE B TEAM',text:'You got put on the B team. You could sulk and play half-hearted until the coach sees sense. Or you could play every B team game as hard as you can.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'Which player is more likely to move up?',options:['The one who sulks and waits','The one who plays every game well'],answer:1,reveal:'Coaches pick players by what they see them do. Playing well where you are is the only way to show you are ready for more.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'This small workshop is full and tidy, and the work on the bench is done well. Now the door to a bigger room is open.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'AN OLD IDEA',text:'Wallace Wattles wrote this in 1910. It is the most useful line in his book. Most of the rest says thinking can make you rich, and there is no proof of that. This part needs no magic. It is plain good sense.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You were picked to help in the school library, but you wanted to be on the sports team. What does this idea say?',options:['Do the library job really well','Do it badly until they move you','Wait for sports before trying hard'],answer:0,why:'Doing the job you have well is how people see that you are ready for more.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'You will do your best work once you have the right role.',reality:'Wattles argues the reverse. You move on only by becoming larger than your present place, and the way to become larger is to fill the place you have, right to its edges.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Do what can be done now, with what is to hand, in the job you have, and do it fully. The habit of filling your present place is the qualification for the next one.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The small room is fully used and the work is finished to a high standard. The door to the bigger room stands open.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why waiting backfires',text:'Wait for the right position before doing your best work, and the people deciding about that position never see your best work. Whatever you are trusted with next is judged on what you do now.'},{kind:'explain',title:'An ordinary truth',text:'This is not peculiar to Wattles. Marcus Aurelius, the Roman emperor, kept urging himself to do the task in front of him well, and most serious accounts of how work gets done say the same. It needs nothing mystical, which is its strength.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one ordinary task this week and do it as though somebody deciding your next role were watching. Notice what changes in how you do it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'An analyst feels her role is beneath her and does the minimum until a promotion comes. What does this idea predict?',options:['Promotion is less likely, since nobody sees what she can do','Promotion will come once her potential is properly noticed','Her effort is irrelevant as long as her results are adequate','She should leave at once, since the role is wasting her'],answer:0,why:'The next role is decided on what she does in this one. Doing the minimum hides exactly what she is waiting to be seen for.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'keyline',kicker:'THE QUIETEST CHAPTER',text:'The best part of this book is its least exciting instruction.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Act in your present place: do what can be done now, with what is to hand, in the job you have. You advance by outgrowing your position, which means filling it completely first.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The argument',text:'Wattles argues against waiting for the right position from which to begin. A person moves on only by being larger than their present place, and becomes larger by doing everything in it fully. The improvement in circumstances comes after the action, not before it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A small workshop with every surface in use and the work finished well. The larger room is reached through the small one, not instead of it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The inconsistency',text:'The advice sits beside his central doctrine: that a thought impressed on the formless substance produces material results. If that were so, present action would be unnecessary. The book asserts both and never makes them consistent. Readers remember the practical chapter, which suggests they quietly drop the doctrine.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The doctrine beside this advice is unsupported: there is no evidence that thought impressed on any substance produces wealth. The advice itself has limits. Filling a role well does not guarantee advancement where promotion is unfair or the role is a dead end, and the instruction can be used to extract extra effort from people who are never moved on.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which part of your present role have you been doing at half strength while waiting for a better one?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'Someone says: if thought shapes reality, as the book claims, working hard in your present job is pointless. What is the best response?',options:['They have found a real contradiction; the advice stands and the doctrine falls','There is no tension, since thought and action are the same thing in the book','The objection fails, because results have confirmed the doctrine','They are right, so the advice to act should be dropped'],answer:0,why:'The two claims are inconsistent. The advice to act holds on ordinary evidence; the doctrine has none, so it is the one to drop.'}]}},'science-of-getting-rich_7':{pictureCaption:'Rows of small pieces, each made to the same standard. The one out of line is a small act done badly, and it shows.',bridge:'Idea 6 said fill your present place. This one says how: through the quality of ordinary acts, not occasional big ones.',basics:{title:'Small things done well add up',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO PRACTISE',text:'Two kids are learning the piano. One plays for ten careful minutes every day. The other does nothing all week, then plays for two hours on Sunday. After a month, the first one usually plays better.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Big wins are made of small, everyday things done well. Each small thing is done well or badly. None of them counts for nothing.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Lots of small pieces, all made the same careful way. One is out of line. In a row of small things, that one shows.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO SAID IT',text:'Wallace Wattles wrote this in 1910. He said success is not a few great moments. It is ordinary things done well, one after another. This part of his book needs no magic. It is how skills really grow.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Not all at once',text:'He also said to do all you can today, but not to try to do tomorrow\u2019s work as well. Cramming a whole week into one night does not work well. Steady wins.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one small thing you do every day, like packing your school bag. Do it properly for a week. Then look back.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your spelling test is next Friday. Which plan fits this idea best?',options:['Learn three words each day this week','Learn all the words on Thursday night','Skip it, since one test is a small thing'],answer:0,why:'Small, steady acts done well add up. Cramming tries to do the week\u2019s work in one night.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO A GOOD YEAR',prompt:'Which does more for a year of work: a few heroic weeks, or the ordinary hours done slightly better?',options:['The heroic weeks','The ordinary hours'],answer:1,reveal:'Most of a year is ordinary hours. Improve them a little and the gain applies to nearly everything you do. A heroic week, however good, is one week.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Success is made of ordinary acts done well, not occasional extraordinary ones. Every act is either efficient or not; nothing in a day is neutral.'},{kind:'flow',nodes:['An ordinary task done properly','Repeated every day','Small gains compound','Competence that looks sudden'],note:'The visible success comes at the end. Its cause was spread across hundreds of unremarkable days.'},{kind:'explain',title:'No neutral acts',text:'Wattles\u2019s claim is that each act either moves the work forward or does not, however small it looks. Most days are made entirely of small acts, so the quality of the small acts is the quality of the day.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Against the sprint',text:'He adds a check: do all you can today, but do not try to do tomorrow\u2019s work today. The heroic push that borrows from next week tends to be paid back, with interest, out of next week.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A bench of small parts, all finished to one standard except one. A day is like this: many small acts, none of them neutral.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one routine task you do daily and do it properly for a week. Judge the effect at the end, not during.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A writer produces almost nothing for a month, then writes for thirty hours straight before a deadline. What would this idea suggest?',options:['Write a set amount, well, every working day','Plan a longer sprint that starts a little earlier','Wait for a stronger burst of inspiration','Lower the standard so that sprints go faster'],answer:0,why:'The month of ordinary days was where the work could have been done. The idea moves effort from the sprint into those days.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'scale',kicker:'WHERE ACHIEVEMENT LIVES',low:'Rare, exceptional acts',high:'The routine acts of every day',note:'Wattles moves achievement from the left end of this line to the right. Most of a working life sits at the right end anyway.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Achievement comes from the accumulated quality of routine acts, not from moments of exceptional performance. Each act is efficient or inefficient, and an inefficient act is a failure however small.'},{kind:'explain',title:'His best paragraph',text:'This is the strongest passage in the book. It matches how competence is actually built: most people\u2019s output is limited by the quality of their ordinary hours, not by the absence of extraordinary ones. It sounds trivial, and it is not.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The rule against sprinting',text:'Wattles adds that you should do all you can each day but not attempt tomorrow\u2019s work today. It is an argument against the heroic sprint that borrows from the following week, and for a pace that can be kept up.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Dozens of identical parts, one out of line. In a day made of small acts, the small failures are the ones that accumulate.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Wattles ties this to his doctrine that efficient action, joined to the right thought, draws wealth through the formless substance. That doctrine has no evidence behind it. Taken alone, the claim has edges: not every act matters equally, treating every small act as success or failure can become perfectionism, and routine work done well still does not guarantee reward.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Most of a day is made of small acts.','Their quality, compounded, is the result.','The claim stands without his metaphysics.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these is a *misuse* of the idea?',options:['Spending an hour perfecting every email so none is inefficient','Doing the daily review properly instead of skimming it','Declining to cram next week\u2019s tasks into tonight','Improving a routine report a little every week'],answer:0,why:'The idea concerns routine quality, not perfection. Over-polishing small acts is itself an inefficient use of the day.'}]}},'science-of-getting-rich_8':{pictureCaption:'A blind pulled most of the way down. Below it, the street outside is still there to be seen.',bridge:'Idea 4 used attention well. This is the same tool turned the wrong way: attention withdrawn from what needs to be seen.',basics:{title:'Looking away does not fix things',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE NOTE IN YOUR BAG',text:'You get a note to take home from school. You know it is bad news. So you leave it in your bag and try not to think about it. A week later, the problem is bigger.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHAT THE BOOK SAYS',text:'Wallace Wattles, writing in 1910, told readers not to think about being poor. Do not read about it. Do not talk about it, not even about other people. He claimed that thinking about a thing makes it happen. There is no proof of that.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Not looking at a problem does not make it go away. It just means you cannot help fix it, whether it is yours or someone else\u2019s.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The blind is pulled almost all the way down. But the street outside is still there, just as it was.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Fine',items:['Not fretting about a bad game all week','Moving on from a mistake once you have learned from it']},right:{title:'Not fine',items:['Hiding the note from school','Ignoring a friend who is having a hard time']},note:'Letting go of your own bad day can be wise. Refusing to see someone else\u2019s trouble is something else.'},{kind:'try',text:'Is there a problem you have been trying not to look at? Tell one grown-up you trust about it this week.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A kid in your class often has no lunch. Your friend says: don\u2019t think about it, it\u2019s sad. What does this lesson say?',options:['Noticing is the first step to helping','Your friend is right, so just look away','Thinking about it could make you poor too'],answer:0,why:'Looking away keeps you comfortable but helps nobody. You cannot help with something you refuse to see.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE INSTRUCTION',text:'Wattles tells his readers to give no attention to poverty, illness or failure: not to study them, discuss them or dwell on their causes, including in other people\u2019s lives. His reason is that attending to a thing brings it about. There is no evidence for that.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The book presents looking away as mental hygiene. In practice it is advice to stop noticing problems, your own and other people\u2019s, and that is where the book becomes actively harmful.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW IT GOES WRONG',nodes:['Do not look at the problem','The bill or the symptom grows','The method does not work','You looked at the wrong things'],note:'The last step blames the reader and protects the doctrine, which is why it can never be disproved.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Two different things',text:'Not dwelling on your own setbacks can be discipline: you stop replaying a failure and get back to work. Refusing to look at somebody else\u2019s situation is something else. It turns a way of managing your mood into a licence to ignore how other people are living.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A blind pulled most of the way down in a bright carriage. The street outside has not changed; only the view of it has.'},{kind:'try',text:'Name one thing you have been avoiding looking at: an account, a symptom, a project in trouble. Look at it this week, plainly, and decide one next step.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A friend has stopped opening letters from her bank because thinking about money makes her anxious. What does this lesson suggest?',options:['Open them, since the information is what lets her act','Keep them closed so that she can stay positive','Put them away somewhere they cannot affect her mood','Wait until she feels confident enough to read them'],answer:0,why:'Avoiding the letters protects her mood for now and lets the problem grow. The information is what makes a fix possible.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'The chapter is harmless advice to stay positive.',reality:'It goes much further. It tells the reader not to study poverty, illness or failure, or discuss their causes, even in other people\u2019s lives, on the grounds that attention brings them about.'},{kind:'idea',text:'By forbidding attention to poverty and failure, the book removes the information needed to address them, and supplies an explanation for failure that blames the person. It is unfalsifiable and cruel in the same move.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it is harmful',text:'The instruction targets exactly what a problem needs if it is to be addressed: the unpaid bill, the failing venture, the symptom. A reader who obeys it learns of trouble later, when it is larger. Extended to others, it asks the comfortable reader not to see the poor.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How it protects the system',text:'If the method fails, the doctrine already has the reason: you attended to the wrong things. Every failure becomes evidence of the reader\u2019s lapse, never of the method\u2019s. It is the closed loop from the first lesson, made personal.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A blind pulled three-quarters down. The street is still out there; the passenger has only arranged not to see it.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Be plain. There is no evidence that attending to poverty or illness causes them, and avoiding the information is a reliable way to make problems worse. The only defensible residue is narrow: not dwelling on your own setbacks once you have learned from them. Read the chapter as an object lesson in how a system shields itself from disconfirmation, not as advice.'},{kind:'keyline',text:'Not dwelling on your own setback is discipline. Not looking at someone else\u2019s is something else.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY THE DISTINCTION',prompt:'Which of these keeps the defensible residue rather than the harmful instruction?',options:['After reviewing a failed project, choosing not to replay it every night','Unsubscribing from news about poverty to protect a positive mindset','Not asking a struggling colleague how they are, to avoid negativity','Leaving a worrying symptom unchecked so as not to dwell on illness'],answer:0,why:'Only the project review looks at the problem before letting it go. The others avoid the information, which is exactly the harm in the chapter.'}]}},'science-of-getting-rich_9':{pictureCaption:'A hand rests on its own knight, and nowhere near the other side. You choose your own move; their moves are theirs.',bridge:'After the book\u2019s worst chapter, one of its sanest: a rule that confines your will to the one thing you control, yourself.',basics:{title:'You can only steer yourself',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE GAME AT BREAK',prompt:'Your friend wants to play a different game from you. Which works better?',options:['Keep pushing until they give in','Decide what you will do either way'],answer:1,reveal:'Pushing makes people dig in. Deciding your own move works whatever they choose. You can join their game, or ask someone else to play yours.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Use your willpower on yourself, not on other people. You can choose what you do. You cannot make someone else choose.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'In chess, your hand moves only your own pieces. You cannot move the other player\u2019s. Your willpower works the same way.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'A SURPRISING RULE',text:'Wallace Wattles wrote a book in 1910 full of claims with no proof. But it has one sensible rule. He said you should never try to force your will on other people. Use it only to steer what you think and do.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A TV REMOTE',text:'You have a remote for your own TV. Pointing it at your neighbour\u2019s house does nothing. Your willpower is like that remote. It only works on you.'},{kind:'steps',title:'When you want someone to change',steps:['Notice that you are trying to make them decide.','Ask: what will I do if they say no?','Ask: what will I do if they say yes?','Do your part, whatever they choose.']},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your brother keeps leaving a mess in the room you share. Which uses this idea?',options:['Ask him once, then keep your own side tidy','Nag him every hour until he cleans up','Take his games away until he tidies up'],answer:0,why:'You can ask, but only your own actions are yours to control. Forcing him rarely works and often makes things worse.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE STALLED DEAL',text:'You want a client to sign. You send reminders, rework the pitch, imagine the call where they finally agree. Weeks pass. Their decision is theirs, and all your effort has gone into something you do not control.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Apply will only to your own attention and conduct. Effort aimed at your own actions is the part you control; effort aimed at making others decide is costly and mostly fails.'},{kind:'explain',title:'A surprising restriction',text:'Wattles forbids using will power on other people or on external things, calling it both wrong and ineffective. Much of the genre that followed promises the opposite, so it is a notable restriction.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Willing others',items:['Will they sign?','Chase, push, pitch again','The outcome depends on them']},right:{title:'Willing yourself',items:['What will I do either way?','Set a deadline, then move on','Your part depends on you']},note:'The right column is the only part of the book\u2019s apparatus a person can actually do.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'YOUR SIDE OF THE BOARD',caption:'The hand rests on its own knight. No hand reaches across the board; the other player\u2019s moves are theirs to make. All the effort goes into the one move you control.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it works',text:'Your own conduct responds to effort directly. Other people often respond to pressure by resisting, delaying or agreeing grudgingly. Switching from the first question to the second turns waiting into a decision you can make today.'},{kind:'try',text:'When you catch yourself trying to make somebody else decide something, switch to deciding what you will do either way.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A parent wants their adult son to take a steady job and keeps arguing the case. Which response applies the idea?',options:['Decide what support to offer whichever path he takes','Argue more persuasively until he finally agrees','Picture him taking the job, vividly, every day','Stop speaking to him until he changes his mind'],answer:0,why:'His choice is his. What the parent controls is their own response to it, and deciding that ends the tug of war.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'BEFORE THE CHAPTER',prompt:'A book promising wealth through the power of thought has a chapter on will power. What do you expect it to say?',options:['Use your will to bend people and events','Use your will only on yourself'],answer:1,reveal:'Wattles says the second. Imposing your will on other people or on external things is, in his view, both wrong and ineffective. Will is for holding your own attention and conduct.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Will is to be applied only to yourself, to your own attention and conduct, never to other people or external things. It is the one part of his apparatus that matches something a person can actually do.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Sound conclusion, odd grounds',text:'His reasoning is metaphysical; the conclusion is sensible on ordinary grounds. Effort directed at your own conduct is effort you control. Effort directed at changing others is expensive and mostly fails, because their decisions are theirs.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A hand on its own knight, none reaching over the board. The rule as a chess player keeps it: decide your own move with care, and leave the opponent\u2019s to them.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The tension',text:'The rule sits oddly in a book whose central claim is that thought impressed on the formless substance produces material results. That is willing the external world rather directly. The book forbids will on outside things, then teaches a method for exactly that.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Be plain about which half is unsupported. There is no evidence that thought impressed on any substance produces wealth or moves events, and that is the claim the book rests on. The restriction on will is sensible but has its own limit: some outcomes rightly need you to persuade, negotiate or organise others, and confining effort to yourself can become passivity.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Will works on your own attention and conduct.','Aimed at other people, it is costly and mostly fails.','The book\u2019s central doctrine breaks its own rule.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'A reader says the chapter on will proves the book was sound all along. What is the best response?',options:['The chapter is sound, but it contradicts the doctrine the book rests on','The chapter shows that thought really can move outside events','The chapter must be unsound, since it comes from an unsound book','The chapter and the doctrine say the same thing in different words'],answer:0,why:'The restriction holds on ordinary grounds. The doctrine wills the outside world directly and has no evidence, so one sound chapter does not rescue it.'}]}}}},3004,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'self-help-smiles_1':{pictureCaption:'The case holds the butterflies someone chose to keep. The heap on the floor is all the rest. Smiles\u2019s book is the case.',basics:{title:'Only the winners are in the book',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE WALL OF WINNERS',prompt:'Your school puts up a photo of every spelling bee winner. Each one says they practised every night. Does that prove practice makes you win?',options:['Yes, the photos prove it','The photos can\u2019t tell you'],answer:1,reveal:'Lots of kids practised every night and still lost. Their photos are not on the wall. You only ever see the winners.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A book full of winners can\u2019t show you what made them win. For that, you need the people who did the same thing and lost.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO WROTE IT',text:'Samuel Smiles wrote a book called *Self-Help* in 1859. It tells hundreds of true life stories. In each one, someone keeps on trying and wins in the end.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The case holds the butterflies someone chose to keep. The heap on the floor is all the others. Smiles only showed us the case.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What is missing?',text:'Smiles never asks about the people who kept trying and still lost. Nobody wrote their life story. So his book feels like proof. But a big part of the story is missing.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time an advert shows a row of happy winners, ask one question: how many people did this and did not win?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A video star says: I left school at sixteen and got rich, so you can too. What is the best thing to ask?',options:['How many people left school and did not get rich?','How much money does the star have now?','Which school did the star leave at sixteen?'],answer:0,why:'One rich person tells you nothing about the odds. You need to know about everyone who tried it.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE BUSINESS SHELF',text:'You are thinking of leaving a steady job to start something. You read five founder memoirs. Every one tells the same story: they bet everything, ignored the doubters and kept going. By the fifth, the case feels closed.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A collection of success stories shows what winners did. It cannot show what made them win, because the people who did the same and failed are not in the collection.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where the method comes from',text:'Samuel Smiles\u2019s *Self-Help* (1859) builds its case from hundreds of short lives: engineers, potters, surgeons, the self-taught. Each persists through obscurity and is rewarded. Read in a run, they start to feel like a law of nature.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'In the book',items:['Persisted, and succeeded','Left a biography','Hundreds of cases']},right:{title:'Not in the book',items:['Persisted, and failed','Left no record','An unknown number']},note:'The right-hand column decides whether persistence works. Smiles never fills it in.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The name for it',text:'This is survivorship bias: judging by the cases that came through a filter and forgetting the filter. Each memoir on your shelf is true. None tells you how often the same bet failed, because those people do not write books.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The mounted specimens are the survivors, arranged beautifully. The drift on the floor is the rest. Every success shelf has one, out of sight.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time a set of success stories persuades you, ask how many people did exactly this and are not in the book. If nobody can say, you have testimony, not evidence.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A conference has ten speakers who all quit secure jobs and built successful companies. What can you safely conclude?',options:['Little about the odds, since founders who failed were not invited','That quitting a secure job raises your chance of success','That these ten were more talented than most founders','That persistence matters more to founders than funding'],answer:0,why:'The speakers were chosen because they succeeded. Without the ones who quit and failed, there is nothing to compare them with.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Hundreds of matching cases add up to proof.',reality:'Hundreds of cases picked through the same filter add up to a portrait of the filter. More of them make the argument feel stronger without making it stronger.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Smiles\u2019s evidence is biography chosen after the outcome. It records what successful people did and believed, and is silent on how often the same conduct failed, which is the question that matters.'},{kind:'term',word:'Survivorship bias',meaning:'Drawing conclusions from the cases that passed some filter, such as success or fame, while the cases the filter removed stay invisible. The visible sample then misleads about the whole.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why accumulation fools us',text:'Each life is true. The missing number is the base rate: of everyone who persisted at an invention, how many succeeded? Failure rarely produces a biography, so the book cannot answer. Another success adds no information on that point, yet each one makes the conclusion feel firmer.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHERE THE FILTER SITS',nodes:['Many people persist','A few succeed, most do not','Only successes get biographies','Smiles samples biographies','Persistence looks sufficient'],note:'The filter is the third step. Nothing added after it can correct the result.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The case is the book: specimens chosen, mounted, lit. Underneath, swept aside, are the identical ones that did not make it in.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Testimony, not evidence',text:'None of this makes the book worthless. It is a rich record of what successful Victorians believed had mattered: work, thrift, attention. Read as testimony about the inside of success, it is valuable. Read as proof of what causes success, it fails.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The critique cuts less than it first seems. It shows the book cannot prove persistence works, not that persistence is useless; it may still raise your odds. And it can become its own lazy reflex, dismissing every success story unheard. The fair question is how big the failure pile is, not merely that one exists.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A survey of a hundred people who lived past 100 finds most drank coffee daily. What is the strongest objection?',options:['Without the habits of those who died younger, there is nothing to compare','A hundred people is far too few to show anything at all','Long life is mainly genetic, so habits can never matter','Very old people tend to misremember their own habits'],answer:0,why:'Only the survivors were asked. If most people who died at seventy also drank coffee daily, the finding says nothing.'}]}},'self-help-smiles_2':{pictureCaption:'Four attempts hang on the wall, each a little less wrong. The hands are on the fifth. Smiles\u2019s heroes are the people who make the fifth.',bridge:'Idea 1 questioned the evidence. This is the claim the evidence was gathered for, and the part of it that survives.',basics:{title:'Keep going beats being clever',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'LEARNING TO JUGGLE',prompt:'Two kids learn to juggle. One gets it fast on day one, then gets bored. The other drops the balls a lot, but practises every day. Who is better after a month?',options:['The one who was quick on day one','The one who practised every day'],answer:1,reveal:'Being quick at the start helps a little. Practising every day helps a lot more. A month of drops teaches your hands what one good day can\u2019t.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Plain hard work, kept up, beats being clever now and then. You can\u2019t choose how clever you are. You can choose to keep going.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE BOOK',text:'Samuel Smiles wrote a book of true life stories in 1859. His inventors are not shown as geniuses. They are shown as people who failed, failed again, and kept going.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Four tries hang on the wall, each a bit better. The hands are on the fifth. That is the kind of person Smiles praised.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'If I fail three times, I\u2019m just not good at it.',reality:'Three fails is normal when you learn something hard. Try once more, in a new way, before you decide.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Is that the whole story?',text:'Not quite. Some people try hard and still don\u2019t win. Smiles left them out of his book. So hard work is needed. But it is not a promise.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You fail a hard maths puzzle three times. What would Smiles say?',options:['Try again a new way before you give up','Stop, since you are not a maths person','Wait until you feel clever enough to try'],answer:0,why:'Three fails is not proof you can\u2019t do it. Going again, a bit smarter, is the part that is up to you.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'The people who transform a field are the most gifted people in it.',reality:'Smiles\u2019s engineers are not shown as clever. They are shown as people who went again after the third failure, and the fourth.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Ordinary ability, applied steadily, beats brilliance applied now and then. Of the two inputs, talent and application, only application is yours to increase.'},{kind:'equation',parts:['Talent','Application'],result:'What you bring',note:'You were handed the first term. The second is the one you can turn up. That is why the book sold.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the claim persuades',text:'Any one case of a plodder overtaking a gifted rival is just a story. Smiles gives hundreds, across trades, sciences and centuries. A pattern that wide is hard to wave away.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it overreaches',text:'Every example is persistence that worked. Each is drawn from a wider group that also holds persistence that failed and left no life story. The honest version is narrower: persistence is needed, but not enough on its own. It is still worth having.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Four versions of the same mechanism on the wall, each a little less wrong. The unremarkable hands are on the fifth.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick something that has failed three times. Change one thing about the approach. Then go again once more before you decide it is a matter of aptitude.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'After failing a spreadsheet course twice, a colleague says she is just not a numbers person. Which reply fits the honest version of the claim?',options:['Try once more with a different approach before deciding it is aptitude','Keep repeating the course, since persistence always works in the end','Accept it, since two failures are clear evidence of missing talent','Hand the work to someone else, since effort cannot replace a gift'],answer:0,why:'Two failures are not yet evidence about aptitude. But persistence is no guarantee either. So the move is another changed attempt, not endless repetition.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'LEEDS, THE 1840s',text:'*Self-Help* grew out of talks Smiles gave to young working men in Leeds who were teaching themselves in the evenings. He was not addressing the gifted. He was addressing people with little schooling, who needed a reason to believe their effort would count.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Smiles claims that ordinary abilities applied persistently outperform brilliance applied fitfully. As a law it is overstated. As a statement that persistence is necessary, and the one input a reader can raise, it holds.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'The claim as made',items:['Application beats brilliance','Proved by hundreds of lives','Implies failure means too little effort']},right:{title:'The claim that survives',items:['Application is necessary','Not shown to be sufficient','Failure may have other causes']}},{kind:'explain',title:'Strength and weakness',text:'The strength is accumulation: one patient mediocrity beating a gifted rival is anecdote, but the pattern across trades and centuries is harder to dismiss. The weakness is selection, and it is severe. He chooses persistence that succeeded from a population that also contains persistence that failed, and the failures left nothing to draw on.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Four failed versions, hung in order, each slightly less wrong. The fifth is on the bench. The book is a gallery of fifth versions.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Even the narrow claim has gaps. Persistence in a wrong direction is waste, and the book says little about when to stop or change course. And the contrast between talent and application is too clean: steady practice is often how ability is built, so the two inputs are less separate than the slogan suggests.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What have you labelled a lack of talent after only two or three attempts? What would a changed fourth attempt look like?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE OVERREACH',prompt:'Which statement goes further than Smiles\u2019s evidence can support?',options:['Anyone who works steadily enough at something will succeed at it','Most of the achievers he studied had failed repeatedly first','Application is an input that a reader can choose to increase','His subjects believed persistence mattered more than gifts'],answer:0,why:'His cases can show that persistence came before success. They cannot show it guarantees success, because the persistent who failed are missing.'}]}},'self-help-smiles_3':{pictureCaption:'On the way out, the hand goes back to shut a drawer left an inch open. Nothing depended on it. That is the point.',bridge:'Idea 2 was about keeping going. This is where Smiles thinks the capacity to keep going is built.',basics:{title:'Small things build who you are',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'ON THE WAY OUT',picture:!0,text:'You are about to leave. A drawer is open a little. Nobody will notice. Nobody will care. You go back and shut it anyway.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Who you are is built from small choices. Each time you do the small right thing, it gets easier. Then, when a big choice comes, you are ready.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A PATH IN THE GRASS',text:'Walk across a field once and the grass springs back. Walk the same way every day and a path forms. Soon your feet take it without you thinking.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO SAID IT',text:'Samuel Smiles wrote a famous book in 1859. He said tiny things make a person strong: telling the truth about small stuff, being on time, and finishing what you start.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE WRONG CHANGE',text:'A shop gives you 20p too much. Saying so feels silly. But each time you say so, being honest gets a bit easier. One day it will be a big sum. You won\u2019t need to think.'},{kind:'try',text:'Find one small thing you always let slide. A bed not made. A job left nearly done. Do it properly today.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Mia tells the truth even about tiny things, like who ate the last biscuit. Then a big test of honesty comes. What does this idea predict?',options:['Telling the truth will come more easily to her','She will need a long time to decide','Small things won\u2019t help her with big ones'],answer:0,why:'All those small truths built a habit. When the big moment comes, the habit is already there.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'WHERE CHARACTER COMES FROM',prompt:'Which builds more character: one dramatic act of courage, or a thousand refusals of a tiny temptation?',options:['The dramatic act','The thousand small refusals','They build it equally'],answer:1,reveal:'Smiles bets on the thousand. Each refusal is easy and achieves almost nothing alone. What accumulates is a disposition that acts before you deliberate, and that is what decides the dramatic moment.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Character is built from small refusals rather than large decisions. Resisting minor temptations is what forms the capacity to resist major ones, so the small cases are where the work is done.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW IT ACCUMULATES',nodes:['A trivial temptation','A trivial refusal','Slightly easier next time','A habit needing no deliberation','The large case decides itself']},{kind:'explain',title:'What he means by small',text:'Honesty in trivial matters. Punctuality. Finishing what you started. The Victorian tone is heavy, but the instruction is practical: attend to the small instances, because the large ones will be settled by whatever the small ones built.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The hand has stopped halfway to the coat to close a drawer left an inch open. Nobody would have known. The habit would have.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the small thing is not neutral',text:'The thing you let slide is a rehearsal of letting things slide. You are building a disposition either way. The only choice is which one, and it is being made in moments that feel too minor to count.'},{kind:'try',text:'Name the smallest thing you consistently let slide, the one you have decided is minor. This week, stop letting it slide, and notice how often it comes up.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A manager rounds his expense claims up by a few pounds each month, saying it hardly matters. By Smiles\u2019s argument, what is the real cost?',options:['He is practising a habit that will decide larger cases','Nothing, since the sums are too small for anyone to notice','Only the money, which adds up to a lot over a year','The risk of being caught, which could cost him his job'],answer:0,why:'For Smiles the small dishonesty matters less for its size than for what it trains. The disposition it builds is the one that will meet the large temptation.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'AN OLD ARGUMENT',text:'Aristotle argued in the *Nicomachean Ethics* that we become just by doing just acts and brave by doing brave ones. Virtue is not a gift we then use; it is a habit we build. Smiles restates the argument, more than two thousand years later, for a Victorian readership.'},{kind:'term',word:'Habituation',meaning:'The forming of a settled disposition through repeated action. You do not first become honest and then act honestly. Acting honestly, again and again, is how you become so.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Character is not decided in the large moments. It accumulates in small ones, as a disposition that operates without deliberation. By the time something consequential arrives, the answer has largely been built in advance.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What Smiles adds',text:'The examples. He gives the argument industrial-era detail: the clerk, the apprentice, the mechanic, honest in trivial matters, punctual, finishing what was started. That made it concrete for readers who would never open the *Ethics*.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A trivial refusal: the coat can wait while the drawer is shut. One such act builds nothing. The thousandth is a character.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Behaviour first',text:'The order is the point: identity follows repeated behaviour rather than preceding it. Modern research on habits agrees on one part of this at least. Behaviour repeated in a stable setting becomes automatic, and needs less deliberation each time.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The argument is easily turned harsh. If character is the sum of small acts, small lapses can be read as proof of bad character, a judgement Victorians readily passed on the poor. A habit built in calm can still give way under fear or need. And not every small rule is a virtue: punctuality is as much a convention as a moral fact.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Dispositions are built by repetition, not by resolution.','Small refusals train the response to large temptations.','A lapse is evidence about a habit, not a verdict on a person.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'TEST THE MECHANISM',prompt:'Someone says: I am honest where it matters, so small lies are harmless. What is Smiles\u2019s strongest reply?',options:['The small lies train the disposition the large case will rely on','Small lies are morally exactly as wrong as large ones','Other people will eventually notice the small lies','Nobody can know in advance which cases really matter'],answer:0,why:'His argument is about mechanism, not about how wrong each lie is. The habit formed in small cases is the one that answers in the large.'}]}},'self-help-smiles_4':{pictureCaption:'The runner\u2019s lane happens to be clear. The lanes beside it are full of things nobody removed. Watch only the runner and you credit the legs.',bridge:'Idea 1 asked who was left out of the book. This asks what was left out of the lives that are in it.',basics:{title:'What else was true?',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'SPORTS DAY',text:'Two girls run the same race. One has good trainers and a dad who takes her to the track each week. The other runs in school shoes and has never trained. The first one wins. Everyone says she just tried harder.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When someone wins, hard work is part of it. Luck and the life they were born into are part of it too. Smiles mostly left those parts out.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'This runner\u2019s lane is clear. The other lanes are full of things in the way. If you only watch the runner, you think it was all legs.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE BOOK',text:'In 1859 Samuel Smiles wrote a book of true stories about people who worked hard and did well. Hard times are in it. But they are always beaten. Nobody in it is stopped for good.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'If someone did not do well, they did not try hard enough.',reality:'Some people try very hard and still lose. They may be ill, or poor, or just unlucky. Hard work counts. It is not the only thing that counts.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you hear about someone who did well, believe the hard work. Then ask: what else helped them?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A girl wins a chess contest. She trains hard. Her grandad is a chess coach who plays her every day. What is the fair way to see it?',options:['She worked hard, and she had big help too','She won only because her grandad coaches','She won only because she worked so hard'],answer:0,why:'Both things are true. Her work counts, and so does the help she had.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE PROFILE',text:'A magazine profile of a successful founder dwells on the 5 a.m. starts and the rejections. One sentence near the end mentions a family loan that covered the first year. The article treats that sentence as colour. It may be most of the difference.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Smiles\u2019s biographies leave out luck and circumstance. Read as a law, the book turns every failure into a shortage of effort. That reading was convenient in 1859, and it is false.'},{kind:'equation',parts:['Effort','Circumstance','Luck'],result:'Outcome',note:'The book writes as if only the first term varies. The other two are where most people\u2019s stories differ.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the omission is built in',text:'Smiles did not simply forget circumstance. His method could not see it. He collected the lives of people who got through, so every obstacle in the book is one that was overcome. Obstacles that won were never written up.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Who it was written for',text:'He wrote for and about people for whom, in that period, effort genuinely did convert into advancement. For them the book was largely true. Stretched into a general law, it reads anyone else\u2019s failure as a flaw of character.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One lane is smooth; the lanes beside it are strewn with obstacles nobody cleared. A race seen only from the clear lane looks like a contest of legs.'},{kind:'try',text:'When you read a success story, believe the effort. Then list what else was true of the situation: money, timing, health, contacts, who was already in the room.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A friend out of work for a year is told he just needs to try harder. What does this idea say the advice is missing?',options:['Any account of his circumstances, which the advice simply assumes away','Evidence that effort makes any difference at all to finding work','An admission that finding a job is almost entirely a matter of luck','Practical tips on how to apply for jobs more efficiently'],answer:0,why:'The idea keeps effort in the picture. What it adds is everything else that was true of his situation, which the advice treats as if it were not there.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'BEFORE READING',prompt:'In a book of several hundred success stories, how often would you expect circumstance to be shown deciding an outcome?',options:['Often, since circumstance shapes most lives','Rarely, because of how the stories were chosen','Never, because Victorians ignored it entirely'],answer:1,reveal:'The method all but guarantees it. Lives chosen because they ended in success show hardship only as something overcome. Circumstance that won produced no biography.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The book\u2019s central omission is luck, and it is structural: a collection of successes cannot show circumstance deciding anything. Read as a law, it makes failure a verdict on effort.'},{kind:'explain',title:'From cases to a law',text:'Smiles compounds the selection. He treats his successes as demonstrations of a general law, rather than as the visible tail of a distribution: the few who had both the effort and the conditions it needed to pay off.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE DOCTRINE FORMS',nodes:['Only successes selected','Hardship shown only as overcome','Effort looks sufficient','Failure reads as idleness'],note:'Each step looks reasonable alone. Together they produce a moral claim the evidence never tested.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it suited 1859',text:'A doctrine that reads failure as insufficient effort was politically convenient. It placed the cause of poverty in the poor, and its remedy there too. That is the reading the book lends itself to, and it is false.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The clear lane and the cluttered ones, seen from above. Smiles describes the runners. He rarely describes the lanes.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The correction can overshoot. Saying luck decides everything is as untestable as saying effort does, and it throws away the one input a reader controls. Smiles is right that effort matters. The fix is to read with the missing population in mind, which turns a moral claim into a useful observation about effort, not to discard effort altogether.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of your own best result. What was true of your situation that someone equally hard-working might not have had?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE DEFENDER',prompt:'A defender says: Smiles describes plenty of poverty and hardship, so he did not ignore circumstance. What is the best reply?',options:['He shows only hardship that was overcome, never hardship that won','He describes hardship, but exaggerates most of it for effect','He describes too little hardship to support his own argument','Hardship and circumstance are entirely different things'],answer:0,why:'Circumstance appears only as a hurdle cleared. A book that never shows it deciding an outcome cannot weigh it against effort.'}]}},'self-help-smiles_5':{pictureCaption:'The carved cover comes off; the plain works underneath are what run the thing. Read Smiles that way: lift off the moral, keep the method.',bridge:'Ideas 1 and 4 found the book\u2019s faults. This one asks what is still worth taking from it.',basics:{title:'Keep the useful part',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A FANCY CLOCK',picture:!0,text:'Some old clocks have a fancy carved cover. Lift it off and you find plain gears inside. The cover is just for show. The plain gears keep the time.'},{kind:'idea',text:'An old book can hide a useful idea under an old-fashioned lesson. Lift off the old lesson. Keep the part that still works.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE OLD BOOK',text:'Samuel Smiles wrote *Self-Help* in 1859. Parts of it are out of date. He thought people who stayed poor had not tried hard enough. That part is wrong.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What still works',text:'Underneath is something useful. Plain practice, done again and again, makes you better. The book also shows how a pile of good stories can feel like proof when it isn\u2019t.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Lift off',items:['Poor people just didn\u2019t try','Old-fashioned lectures']},right:{title:'Keep',items:['Practise plain things often','Ask if the stories prove anything']}},{kind:'try',text:'Think of an old rule a grown-up once told you. Which part is out of date? Which part still helps?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Gran says: do homework at the same time each day, because idle hands get into trouble. What is the useful part?',options:['A set time each day helps you get it done','Children who rest will get into trouble','Homework matters more than anything else'],answer:0,why:'The habit of a set time works. The scary bit about trouble is just the old-fashioned cover.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'CHAPTER TEN',text:'You are halfway through a business bestseller. Chapter one was a founder rejected again and again. Chapter two, an athlete training before dawn. By chapter ten you are convinced, though you could not say exactly of what.'},{kind:'idea',text:'*Self-Help* is worth reading now for its method more than its morals. The conclusions are dated. The technique was new. And the gap between how convincing it feels and how little it proves is worth learning to notice.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the method was',text:'Build an argument from hundreds of concrete cases, in ordinary language, for readers with no formal education. In 1859 that was genuinely new. It is why the book sold enormously and set the pattern for a whole genre.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'The moral',items:['Failure means too little effort','Victorian improvement','Dated, in places wrong']},right:{title:'The method',items:['Many concrete cases','Plain language','Deliberate, unglamorous repetition']}},{kind:'picture',caption:'The ornate casing is lifted off and set aside. What is left is plain, dull and entirely functional. That is the part to take.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The skill that transfers',text:'The book feels overwhelming as evidence and proves very little. Learning to feel both at once, persuaded and unproven, is the thing to carry into every book that copied its structure, which is most of the business shelf.'},{kind:'try',text:'With the next self-improvement book you read, write down one concrete practice it recommends. Test that. Ignore how many famous people it says did it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A productivity book is padded with stories of famous early risers. What does reading it for method look like?',options:['Try the routine it describes, without treating the stories as proof','Dismiss the book entirely, since its evidence is all anecdote','Accept its conclusion, since so many successful people agree','Copy the habits of whichever person in it you admire most'],answer:0,why:'The method is the part you can test on yourself. The stories persuade, but they were selected, so they cannot settle whether the routine works.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'timeline',kicker:'A BOOK THAT TRAVELLED',points:[{when:'1812',what:'Samuel Smiles born in Haddington, Scotland'},{when:'1840s',what:'Talks to young working men in Leeds who are educating themselves'},{when:'1859',what:'*Self-Help* published, the same year as Darwin\u2019s *On the Origin of Species*'},{when:'1870s',what:'A Japanese translation is widely read in Meiji Japan'}],note:'It travelled because its method needed no schooling to follow.'},{kind:'idea',text:'*Self-Help* is now most valuable as a study in method: how persuasive accumulated anecdote is, how little it establishes, and what plain technique survives once the dated moral is stripped away.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What was new',text:'The moral conclusions are dated and in places actively wrong. The technique was innovative: an argument built from hundreds of concrete cases, in ordinary language, aimed at readers with no formal education. That is why it sold enormously and founded a genre.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What it proves',text:'Very little. Selected cases establish what the successful did and believed, not what caused their success. The book is a clear specimen of how a genre built on inspiring stories stops checking its own arithmetic.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Decorative casing off, functional works exposed. The lesson of the book is in the mechanism, not the ornament around it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Its descendants',text:'Much of the modern business and self-improvement shelf uses Smiles\u2019s structure: chapter after chapter of chosen success, generally without his range, and with the same selection problem unaddressed.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Separating method from moral is not always clean. Choosing only successes is itself what makes failure look like a vice, so the method carries some of the moral inside it. Take the habit of repetition and ignore circumstance, and the dated moral comes back in. Strip the frame carefully, and check what came off with it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SEPARATE THEM',prompt:'Which of these keeps Smiles\u2019s method while dropping his moral?',options:['Practising a skill daily, while accepting that results also depend on luck','Practising daily, since anyone who fails has not practised enough','Abandoning daily practice, since his evidence for it is anecdotal','Gathering more success stories until the conclusion is certain'],answer:0,why:'Daily practice with luck admitted keeps deliberate repetition and drops the claim that failure is a moral shortfall. Blaming everyone who fails keeps the moral; abandoning practice throws out the method with it.'}]}},'self-help-smiles_6':{pictureCaption:'The finished one is covered and out of reach. He is making his own, because the making is what teaches the hands.',bridge:'Idea 5 separated method from moral. This is the sharpest case: one sentence, with one use that holds and one that does not.',basics:{title:'When help helps, and when it doesn\u2019t',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE MATHS HOMEWORK',text:'Your big brother does your maths homework for you. It is right, and it is done fast. Then next week there is a test. He can\u2019t sit it for you.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Doing a thing yourself is how you learn it. But that is about learning. It is not a reason to refuse help to people who need it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The finished one is under the cloth. He is making his own, because that is how his hands learn.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE BOOK\u2019S BIG SAYING',text:'Samuel Smiles wrote *Self-Help* in 1859. His main idea was this. Help you give yourself makes you strong. Help from other people, he said, makes you weak.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where he went wrong',text:'He used that idea for everything. Some people used it to say poor families should not get help. But a hungry family needs food, not a lesson. Food does not stop anyone from learning.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Learning a skill',items:['Try it yourself first','Ask for hints, not answers']},right:{title:'Real need',items:['Food, a home, being safe','Help is just help']}},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend broke her arm. She can\u2019t carry her bag. What does this idea say?',options:['Carry it for her, since this is need','Don\u2019t, or she will never learn to carry it','Show her how to carry it with one arm'],answer:0,why:'Carrying a bag is not a skill she is learning. She just needs a hand, so help.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TWO REQUESTS',prompt:'A new hire asks you to just fix their bug. Separately, a neighbour has lost their job and cannot pay the rent. Does the same rule apply to both?',options:['Yes: doing it for them makes them dependent','No: one is about learning, the other about need'],answer:1,reveal:'Smiles runs the two together. The first is about capability. The second is about whether someone has enough to live on, and the logic of the first does not carry over.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Help from within builds capacity; help from without can take it away. That is a useful truth about learning. It does not follow that assistance to people in need harms them.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The half that holds',text:'A problem solved for you leaves you unable to solve the next one. A problem you solve builds the capacity, and the capacity is worth more than the solution. Good teachers, managers and parents work on this every day.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The half that does not',text:'Smiles stretched it into a political principle: that laws and outside help do little for people. In the nineteenth century his readers applied it against relieving poverty. It does not follow from the point about skill, and the evidence since has not supported it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A half-made piece in his hands, the finished one covered beside him. For learning a craft, this is right. It says nothing about who should eat.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'FOR LEARNING ONLY',low:'Do it for them: fast, nothing learned',high:'Leave them to it: slow, skill built',note:'When the question is skill, lean right, with hints. When the question is need, this scale does not apply.'},{kind:'try',text:'When you are tempted to say someone must do it themselves, check: are you describing how skill forms, or excusing yourself from helping?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A councillor cites Smiles against free school meals, saying children must learn self-reliance. What is the flaw?',options:['Being fed is not a skill that help stops a child from learning','Smiles never wrote anything about the needs of children','Self-reliance can only be learned by adults, not by children','Free meals cost money that could be spent on teaching instead'],answer:0,why:'The true half of the doctrine is about building capability. Eating is not a capability that a meal takes away, so the argument borrows authority it has not earned.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE FIRST CHAPTER',text:'The book opens with the doctrine its title names. Help from within, Smiles says, always invigorates; help from without enfeebles. He goes on to argue that laws and institutions can do little to raise people, and that national progress is the sum of individual effort.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Two claims travel as one. The first, about how capability forms, is largely right. The second, that outside help does little good, was turned against relieving the poor, and is wrong. Stated together, the second borrows the credibility of the first.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'The capability claim',items:['Solving builds skill','Being solved for does not','Holds in teaching and management']},right:{title:'The political claim',items:['Outside help does little','Turned against poor relief','Not borne out since']}},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the second does not follow',text:'A skill is built by practising it. Having enough to eat is not a skill, and being given food stops nobody practising anything. Need, if anything, consumes the time and attention that self-improvement requires.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The finished example is covered so the half-made one can teach. A sound picture of apprenticeship, and of nothing else.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How the smuggling works',text:'Because the capability claim is true and familiar, citing it lends weight to the political one. The book does not separate the two uses, and so the useful claim is often discarded along with the bad one, or used to slip the bad one in.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Even the capability claim has edges. Help that is never offered is not self-reliance but neglect: learners need models, scaffolding and feedback, all of which come from without. The real question is the kind of help, hints rather than answers, never whether to give any.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE SMUGGLING',prompt:'Which argument uses the true half of the doctrine to carry the false half?',options:['Struggle builds skill, so the unemployed should get no benefits','Struggle builds skill, so give the trainee hints rather than answers','Struggle builds skill, so let your child tie their own shoes','Struggle builds skill, so a tutor should not sit the exam for you'],answer:0,why:'The other three are about learning a skill. The benefits argument moves from skill to need, which is exactly the step that does not follow.'}]}},'self-help-smiles_7':{pictureCaption:'Every frame on the wall holds a gentleman. The workman\u2019s apron lies on the floor, unframed. Smiles\u2019s book is an attempt to hang it up.',bridge:'The earlier ideas questioned the book\u2019s evidence. This is the claim in it that has held up best.',basics:{title:'Who really built the machines?',beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'A BOY AT A COAL MINE',text:'George Stephenson grew up poor in the north of England. He worked the engines at a coal mine. At eighteen he still could not read. He paid for lessons at night. Later he built some of the first railways.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Samuel Smiles said the great inventions of his time came from working people, not rich gentlemen. Back then, that was a bold thing to say.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Every frame holds a gentleman. The work apron on the floor has no frame. Smiles wanted to hang it up.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Who got the credit?',text:'In 1859, history books were mostly about kings, lords and generals. Smiles wrote about a potter, a barber, a man who made measuring tools, and an engine worker. He said they changed Britain more.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Who history praised',items:['Kings','Lords','Generals']},right:{title:'Who Smiles praised',items:['Potters','Engine workers','People who taught themselves']}},{kind:'try',text:'Think of a school play. Who gets the clapping? Now list who built the set and ran the lights.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your class paints a big mural. The paper prints a photo of the head teacher next to it. What would Smiles ask?',options:['Who actually painted it?','Did the head teacher pay for it?','Was it a good photo of the head?'],answer:0,why:'Smiles wanted credit to go to the people who did the work, not just the ones in charge.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'*Self-Help* is a scrapbook of inspiring anecdotes.',reality:'Its cast is the argument. Watt the instrument maker, Wedgwood the potter, Stephenson the engineman at a coal pit. Smiles is saying that tradesmen, not gentlemen, remade Britain. The history books had praised the wrong people.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The great changes of the industrial age were mostly made by working men from plain backgrounds. In 1859, saying so was a claim about who counted.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'THE ENGINEMAN',text:'George Stephenson tended steam engines at coal pits in the north-east of England. He could not read at eighteen. He paid for lessons at night school. He went on to build some of the first public railways.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What has held up',text:'The record does show that a great many of the key advances came from people who made things with their hands, not from men of theory. Often they had little schooling. Here Smiles was right, and ahead of the histories of his day.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What has not',text:'He drew a further lesson: that the same road was open to his readers on the same terms. What let a self-taught mechanic remake a trade in 1780 was not what faced a young worker in 1859. The book never admits the door had narrowed.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A gallery of painted gentlemen, and on the floor beneath them, unframed, a workman\u2019s apron. Smiles is arguing about which belongs on the wall.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you list the people who made something happen, check who you listed. Was it the people who did the work, or the people who were present?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A company history credits its success to three founders, though an unnamed team of engineers designed the product. What is Smiles\u2019s point applied?',options:['The list names who was present, not who did the work','Founders deserve the credit because they took the risk','Engineers are paid for their work, so credit is irrelevant','Company histories are marketing and need not be accurate'],answer:0,why:'Smiles\u2019s argument was about where credit goes. A history that names only those in charge has credited the wrong people.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'timeline',kicker:'THE CAST, IN DATES',points:[{when:'1769',what:'Watt patents his separate condenser; Arkwright patents the water frame'},{when:'1769',what:'Wedgwood opens his Etruria pottery works'},{when:'1829',what:'Stephenson\u2019s *Rocket* wins the Rainhill trials'},{when:'1859',what:'Smiles publishes *Self-Help*, telling their stories to working readers'}],note:'None of the four was a gentleman by birth. Smiles writes them up a lifetime later.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Smiles\u2019s choice of subjects is itself the argument: the industrial age was made by artisans and engineers, not by the gentlemen history had been about. That has held. The inference that the route stayed open has not.'},{kind:'explain',title:'A polemic in a cast list',text:'The histories Smiles was reacting against centred on rank, politics and war. He fills his pages instead with potters, instrument makers, millwrights and enginemen, and argues that they, not their patrons, transformed the country.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The portraits are the history Smiles inherited. The apron on the floor is the history he wanted written instead.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the record supports',text:'The biographical record does show an unusual concentration of major industrial advances made by practitioners rather than theorists, many with little formal schooling. On who did the work, Smiles has largely been borne out.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The step from these lives to his readers\u2019 prospects is the weak one. Conditions that let a self-taught mechanic transform an industry around 1780 were not the conditions of 1859, and the book offers old openings to new readers without saying so. Its cast is also a selection: those who reached the record, not every artisan who contributed.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Pick something you were part of that worked. Who would its official history name? Who actually did the work?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'WHAT HELD, WHAT DID NOT',prompt:'Which claim from the book has held up best?',options:['Major industrial advances often came from practitioners with little schooling','Any self-taught mechanic of 1859 could still transform an industry','Men of rank played no part whatever in industrial change','Formal education was a handicap to the inventors of the period'],answer:0,why:'That practitioners with little schooling often led is the documented pattern. That any self-taught mechanic still could is the inference Smiles drew from it, which ignores how conditions had changed by his own time.'}]}},'self-help-smiles_8':{pictureCaption:'The bulbs are lined up in order. The cracked ones came first. Only the last would ever make it into a history book.',bridge:'Idea 2 said go again after the third failure. This shows what the years of failure looked like, and what they do not prove.',basics:{title:'Most wins start with lots of fails',beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'A POTTER IN FRANCE',text:'Bernard Palissy was a French potter in the 1500s. He wanted to make a shiny white glaze for his pots. He tried for years and failed again and again. Smiles tells how he burned his own furniture to keep his oven hot.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Failing is the normal way to get somewhere. It is not a sign you are bad at it. Most famous wins come after many fails we never hear about.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why other people look fast',text:'When you hear about a win, the years of fails get one short sentence. So other people seem to win fast. Then your own slow progress feels wrong. It isn\u2019t.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The cracked bulbs came first. The good one came last. A history book would only show you the last one.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A HARD GAME LEVEL',text:'When you finally beat a hard level, the game shows one win. It does not show the forty times you fell off the ledge. But those forty tries taught you the jumps.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before you start something hard, decide how many fails you will allow before you stop. Write the number down.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You watch a skater land an amazing trick online. What does this idea tell you?',options:['There were lots of falls you did not see','She has a gift that you just don\u2019t have','If it took lots of tries, it doesn\u2019t count'],answer:0,why:'The clip shows the one that worked. The falls before it were left out, like the cracked bulbs.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHAT GETS RECORDED',left:{title:'What the record shows',items:['The achievement','A date','A sentence about the struggle']},right:{title:'What happened',items:['Years of versions that failed','Debts and rejections','The achievement, last on the list']},note:'The left column is what you compare yourself with. The right is what it cost.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Failure is the ordinary route to achievement, not the exception. The record compresses the failed years into a sentence, which makes every success look faster than it was and your own progress look abnormal.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What Smiles does about it',text:'He supplies the omitted years, again and again: bankruptcies, rejected patents, decades of work that came to nothing before the thing he is describing. He holds that we learn more wisdom from failure than from success, as description rather than consolation.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'PALISSY\u2019S GLAZE',text:'Bernard Palissy, a sixteenth-century French potter, spent years trying to make a white enamel glaze. Smiles tells how, out of fuel, he fed his kiln with his own furniture. The glaze is the famous part. The years are most of the story.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A row of discarded attempts in order of age, all broken except the last. The record keeps the last one and loses the row.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The caveat',text:'Every failure in the book was eventually followed by success, or there would be no biography. Failure that stayed failure is absent. So the book shows that failure precedes success, not that it predicts it.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before starting something ambitious, write down how many failed attempts you are prepared to fund. If the number is nought, choose something else.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'After two rejected funding applications, a researcher decides her project is hopeless. What does this idea add?',options:['Two rejections are an ordinary stage of the route, not yet a verdict','Rejection guarantees that the third application will succeed','Rejection shows the project must be ahead of its time','Rejection means the reviewers did not read it properly'],answer:0,why:'Early failure is the normal shape of the route. That does not promise success, but it means two rejections are not yet evidence of hopelessness.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Smiles shows that failure leads to success.',reality:'He shows that success was usually preceded by failure. That is a different claim, because his sample contains only failures that ended well.'},{kind:'idea',text:'In Smiles\u2019s lives the achievement is almost always the last item on a long list of failed attempts, which the public record hides. The book establishes that failure precedes success, not that it predicts it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The problem of visibility',text:'The public record shows the outcome and compresses the preceding years into a sentence. Every success then looks faster and more direct than it was, and a reader\u2019s own slow progress looks anomalous. Smiles\u2019s correction is to restore the years, case after case, until the pattern registers.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE YEARS DISAPPEAR',nodes:['Years of failed versions','One success','The record keeps the success','The years shrink to a sentence','Others\u2019 success looks quick']},{kind:'picture',caption:'Prototypes in order of age, broken until the last. Smiles restores the row. He cannot show the benches where the row never ended.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Precedes, not predicts',text:'Of everyone who failed for years, how many ever succeeded? The book cannot say, since failure that stayed failure left no biography. So it cannot tell you that persisting through failure makes success likely, only that success rarely came without it.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Taken as a promise, the idea becomes a reason never to stop. Some failures are information: the design is wrong, or nobody wants the thing. The useful form is a budget, set in advance: how many failed attempts you will fund, and what result would count as evidence to stop.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Achievement is usually the last item on a long list.','The record hides the list, so others look quick.','Failure precedes success. It does not predict it.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'PRECEDES OR PREDICTS?',prompt:'A founder tells investors: every great company failed many times first, and we have failed many times, so we are on track. What is the flaw?',options:['Failure coming before success does not make failure a sign of success','Great companies in fact rarely fail before they succeed','Investors care about revenue, not about earlier failures','The founder has not yet failed enough times to know'],answer:0,why:'Most companies that failed many times simply failed. The pattern in the survivors says nothing about which group this one belongs to.'}]}},'self-help-smiles_9':{pictureCaption:'A man in a heavy coat holds the door for someone carrying boxes, who can do nothing for him. For Smiles, that is the test.',bridge:'Idea 3 said character is built in small acts. The last chapter says that character, not rank, is what makes a gentleman.',basics:{title:'Being good is what you do',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'BRITAIN, THE 1800s',prompt:'Back then, which of these would most people have called a gentleman?',options:['A rich man from an old family','A kind, honest farm worker'],answer:0,reveal:'Back then, a gentleman meant a man born rich, who did not work with his hands. Samuel Smiles wanted to change what the word meant.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Smiles said a gentleman is not someone born rich. It is someone who is honest and kind, even to people who can do nothing for them.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'He stops to hold the door for a man with heavy boxes. The man can\u2019t do anything for him. That is why it counts.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why that was bold',text:'It meant a poor worker could be a gentleman. And a lord could fail to be one. A word for rich people became a word for good behaviour. Anyone could earn it.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'AT LUNCH',text:'Watch how someone treats the dinner staff, or the new kid nobody talks to. Those people can\u2019t give them anything back. That is where you see what a person is really like.'},{kind:'try',text:'Today, be kind to someone who can do nothing for you. Hold a door. Thank the bus driver. Sit with someone on their own.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Who acts most like Smiles\u2019s gentleman?',options:['A boy who helps the new kid find his class','A girl who is extra polite to the head','A boy whose family owns a huge house'],answer:0,why:'The new kid can do nothing for him in return. Being polite to the head might win you something.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'ON THE WAY OUT',text:'A candidate is charming to the interview panel. On the way out he snaps at the receptionist. He walks past the cleaner as if she were furniture. The panel\u2019s view of him changes more than any answer he gave could have.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A gentleman is a way of behaving: truthfulness, thought for others, self-respect and refusing to take unfair advantage. It is open to a labourer and can be missing in a lord.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What Smiles did to the word',text:'The last chapter of *Self-Help* takes the most class-bound word in English. It cuts the word loose from birth, money and manners. A word that described a rank becomes a word that describes conduct. The standing it names comes within his readers\u2019 reach.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Gentleman as rank',items:['Fixed at birth','Shown by wealth and manners','Closed to his readers']},right:{title:'Gentleman as conduct',items:['Earned by behaviour','Shown by honesty and consideration','Open to anyone']}},{kind:'picture',caption:'A man in a good coat holds the door for someone carrying boxes. Nothing in it for him, which is exactly what makes it evidence.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the powerless are the test',text:'Courtesy to someone who can promote you proves little, because it pays. How you treat someone who can do nothing for you has no motive but the conduct itself. So it is the clearest evidence of what that conduct is.'},{kind:'try',text:'This week, notice how you treat the person in the room with the least power. That is the version of you that everyone else is reading.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Two equally strong candidates. One was polite to the panel and curt with the receptionist. The other was polite to both. By Smiles\u2019s standard, what does this show?',options:['The second showed conduct that does not depend on who is useful','Nothing, since the receptionist plays no part in the hiring','The first was simply nervous, and that should be excused','The first was being strategic, which is a strength at work'],answer:0,why:'Courtesy to the panel was in the candidate\u2019s interest. Courtesy to the receptionist was not, so only that tells you about character.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'THE WORD HE INHERITED',word:'Gentleman',meaning:'In early Victorian usage, chiefly a matter of rank: a man of good family and independent means, who did not work with his hands. Manners were expected of him, but birth and money came first.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Smiles turns a word of rank into a word of conduct. The honorific attaches to behaviour rather than position, and the standing it describes becomes achievable by anyone.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the last chapter does',text:'It defines the gentleman by truthfulness, consideration, self-respect and the refusal to take unfair advantage, above all in how he treats people who can do nothing for him. The examples are chosen to fit: working men who behaved well under pressure, set against men of rank who did not.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Heavy coat, held door, a stranger with boxes. No rank is visible, only conduct, which is the only thing the last chapter will count.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it mattered in 1859',text:'His readers were artisans and clerks for whom the rank was closed and always would be. Repurposing the vocabulary of rank as a vocabulary of conduct made the standing it named achievable by them. That is why the chapter was doing real work, and why it is the most radical in the book.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The content of the conduct has dated: the Victorian virtues of respectability and self-command are not quite ours. And the thought that true nobility lies in conduct rather than birth is older than Smiles; what he gave it was a mass working readership. The structure survives so completely that we no longer notice it needed arguing.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which title in your world still attaches to position when it ought to attach to conduct?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER THE MOVE',prompt:'Which modern change makes the same move Smiles made with the word gentleman?',options:['Calling someone a leader for how they act, whatever their job title','Keeping the title of leader for people in the most senior roles','Dropping the word leader because it sounds old-fashioned','Paying leaders more so that the title matches the rank'],answer:0,why:'Smiles detached an honorific from position and tied it to behaviour. Calling someone a leader for how they act does exactly that to a modern honorific.'}]}}}},3005,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'self-reliance_1':{pictureCaption:'A crumpled page, smoothed out, carries the same marks as the framed work on the wall. What you threw away is what you now admire.',basics:{title:'The idea you threw away',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'IN CLASS',text:'Your teacher asks a question. An answer pops into your head, but it seems too easy. So you say nothing. Then your friend says the same thing. The teacher says it is a great answer.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'Why did you keep quiet?',options:['The answer was wrong','It was yours, so it felt too plain'],answer:1,reveal:'The answer was fine. It came out of your own head, so it felt too simple to count. When your friend said it, it sounded smart.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Your own ideas can seem too plain just because they are yours. Don\u2019t throw them out so fast. Keep them long enough to test them.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Someone threw this page in the bin. Now look at the frame on the wall. The same marks are hanging there.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO SAID THIS',text:'Ralph Waldo Emerson was an American writer. Long ago, in 1841, he wrote an essay called *Self-Reliance*. That means trusting yourself. He saw that in great books we meet thoughts we once had and threw away.'},{kind:'try',text:'Keep a small notebook. When you think *that\u2019s too obvious*, write it down anyway. Read the list again in a month.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You have an idea for the school play, but it seems too simple. What does this lesson say?',options:['Write it down and test it before you drop it','Drop it, since simple ideas are never good','Keep it secret until you know it is great'],answer:0,why:'Simple is not the same as bad. Give the idea a fair test before you decide.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'LAST YEAR\u2019S THOUGHT',prompt:'You read an acclaimed book and meet an idea you had last year and dropped. Why did you drop it?',options:['It was not good enough yet','It seemed obvious, because it was yours','Someone had surely said it already'],answer:1,reveal:'The second and third are the same answer in two forms. *Too obvious* and *surely done already* are how a discount on your own thinking feels from the inside.'},{kind:'idea',text:'You mark down your own thoughts because they are yours, and you do it before examining them. The same thought, returned by someone else, arrives with authority.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE DISCOUNT WORKS',nodes:['A thought arrives','It is yours, so it seems cheap','\u201cToo obvious\u201d or \u201calready done\u201d','Dropped, untested'],note:'The discount runs before any test. All you ever notice is its conclusion.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What changed between the two',text:'Emerson says that in great work we meet our own rejected thoughts, and they come back to us strange and grand. The thought did not improve on the way. Only the owner changed. Your name took value off it, and someone else\u2019s put it back.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The page in the hand came out of the bin. The framed work on the wall bears the same marks. Only the owner differs.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'So you should trust every idea you have.',reality:'Emerson asks for something narrower. Hold your first impression firmly and cheerfully, long enough to test it, even when the room disagrees. The test can still end with you dropping it.'},{kind:'try',text:'This week, note each thought you dismiss as obvious. Revisit the list in a month and see which ones someone else has since said.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'In a meeting you think the fix is simply to ask customers, but you stay quiet because it seems too simple. What would Emerson say happened?',options:['You marked the thought down because it was yours, before testing it','You were right to stay quiet, since obvious ideas are usually covered','You lacked the confidence that only comes with more experience','You should have pushed the idea hard, whatever anyone else said'],answer:0,why:'The failure was not shyness. The thought was dropped before it was examined, because of whose it was.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'MASSACHUSETTS, 1841',text:'Emerson had resigned as a Boston minister in 1832, unable in conscience to keep administering communion. Nine years later he published *Self-Reliance* in his first book of essays. It is a sustained case for trusting the mind\u2019s own verdict over the crowd\u2019s.'},{kind:'idea',text:'In great work we meet our own rejected thoughts, returned with an authority they lacked when they were ours. The fault was never in the thought. It was in a discount applied before the thought was examined.'},{kind:'term',word:'Alienated',meaning:'In Emerson\u2019s use, made foreign: something that was yours, handed back as if it belonged to someone else. He uses it of thoughts that return to us from great work with a borrowed grandeur.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why you never catch the discount',text:'It runs before the idea is examined, so there is nothing to catch. What reaches you is only its verdict: obvious, or surely said before. Emerson names the cause bluntly. A person dismisses a thought without a hearing for the single reason that it is his own.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The sheet from the bin and the framed work carry the same marks. In Emerson\u2019s account, only the signature changed.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What he asks instead',text:'Not belief in every passing notion. He asks you to keep your spontaneous impression with a cheerful stubbornness, most of all when every voice is on the other side. The tone is good-humoured, not defiant. The aim is that the thought gets a hearing.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Most dismissed thoughts deserved it. The essay offers no test for telling an overlooked insight from the merely obvious, and memory flatters the argument: you recall the thought that later turned up in a great book, not the hundred that rightly died. As licence to trust every hunch, it is self-flattery. As a demand for a fair hearing, it holds.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which thought did you drop last year as too obvious? Has anyone said it since?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE OVERREACH',prompt:'Which conclusion goes beyond what Emerson argues here?',options:['Your first impressions are usually right, so act on them','You often mark a thought down simply because it is yours','A dismissed thought deserves a fair test before you drop it','The same idea can seem grander when someone else says it'],answer:0,why:'He asks for a hearing, not a verdict in advance. Trusting every impression is the opposite error to dismissing them all.'}]}},'self-reliance_2':{pictureCaption:'The path has curved away. The walker stands in set concrete where it used to run, keeping faith with where they once were.',bridge:'Idea 1 was about trusting a new thought of yours. This one is about letting go of an old one.',basics:{title:'You are allowed to change your mind',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'SOUNDS RIGHT, ISN\u2019T',belief:'Once you say what you think, you should stick to it.',reality:'Sticking to it is good if it is still true. But if you learn something new, keeping the old view is not being strong. It is just being stuck.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'AT LUNCH',text:'On Monday you told your friends the new game was boring. On Friday you play it, and you love it. Now you feel you can\u2019t say so. You said it was boring!'},{kind:'idea',text:'Don\u2019t keep saying a thing just because you said it before. Say what you think now. If you learn more, you can change your mind.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The path has moved on. This walker\u2019s feet are stuck in the spot where they stood before.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO SAID THIS',text:'Ralph Waldo Emerson was an American writer. He wrote almost 200 years ago. He said it is silly to keep an old view just to look steady. What you think is true now matters more.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of one thing you changed your mind about. Tell someone: *I used to think that, but now I think this, because\u2026*'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Mia always said cats are better than dogs. Then she looked after a dog for a week and loved it. What fits this lesson?',options:['Saying she changed her mind, and why','Sticking to cats so she does not look silly','Never saying what she thinks again'],answer:0,why:'She learned something new. Saying so is honest, not weak.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'MARCH, THEN JULY',text:'In March you argued hard for the new system in front of the whole team. It is July. The numbers are in, and you now think you were wrong. In the meeting, you hear yourself defending it again.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Do not hold a position merely because you stated it before. Changing your mind on evidence is not weakness, and defending an old view to look steady is not strength.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Emerson said consistency is for small minds, so being reliable does not matter.',reality:'He wrote that a *foolish* consistency haunts little minds. The adjective carries the argument. His target is agreeing with your past self for the sake of agreeing, not being someone others can rely on.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Integrity',items:['Your view follows the evidence','You say why it changed','Your promises are kept']},right:{title:'Foolish consistency',items:['Your view follows the record','You hide that it changed','Your old positions are kept']},note:'Integrity is about what you owe. Foolish consistency is about how you look.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why organisations get stuck',text:'A reversal looks like inconsistency, and inconsistency is treated as worse than being wrong. So positions outlive their evidence. Each person defending one is protecting a record, not deciding the question in front of them.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The path curved away long ago. The walker is still standing in set concrete where it used to run.'},{kind:'try',text:'Once this week, say out loud that you have changed your mind about something, and give the reason.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A manager who backed a supplier last year now has data showing they are unreliable. Which response fits Emerson\u2019s argument?',options:['Switch suppliers and say plainly what the new data showed','Stay with them, since changing course would undermine trust','Switch quietly, so that nobody notices the reversal','Wait for more data so the change looks less abrupt'],answer:0,why:'The view follows the evidence and the reason is stated. Switching quietly still protects the record rather than the decision.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE LINE EVERYONE CUTS',text:'It is one of Emerson\u2019s most quoted sentences, and it is nearly always cut short. Emerson does not attack consistency. He attacks a *foolish* consistency, which he calls the hobgoblin of little minds. A hobgoblin is a petty, nagging sprite: something that pesters you rather than guides you.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Consistency with the evidence is integrity. Consistency with your own past statements, kept for its own sake, lets the record vote on a question it never examined.'},{kind:'explain',title:'His alternative',text:'Say what you think today in hard words, and tomorrow say what you think tomorrow in hard words again, even if it contradicts today. The hardness matters. This is not drift. Each statement is meant in full and answers to the question in front of you, not to the record.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE OBJECTION HE EXPECTS',text:'You will be misunderstood, Emerson anticipates. His reply is a list of the misunderstood: Pythagoras, Socrates, Luther, Copernicus, Galileo, Newton. Being misread, he argues, is the ordinary price of thinking in the present tense.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The walker keeps faith with the spot where the path used to run. The path itself has moved on.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The essay underrates being predictable. Others plan around your word, and someone who reverses often is costly to work with even when each reversal is right. The line is also quoted as licence for carelessness. The test is whether a change answers to evidence or to mood. Without that test, it excuses anything.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Emerson attacks foolish consistency, not consistency.','A past statement is not evidence about the present question.','Change on evidence, and say why.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these is a misuse of Emerson\u2019s line?',options:['Breaking a promise because you no longer feel like keeping it','Dropping a forecast you defended once the results contradict it','Admitting in public that an argument you made was mistaken','Refusing to defend a view only because you once held it'],answer:0,why:'A promise is an obligation, not an opinion. Emerson frees you from your past views, not from what you owe other people.'}]}},'self-reliance_3':{pictureCaption:'One more identical cast joins the row. Under the cloth, ignored, sits the one shape nobody else on the shelf could have made.',bridge:'Idea 1 rescued a thought you had dismissed. This one asks what you lose by working on someone else\u2019s instead.',basics:{title:'Copying hides what only you can make',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A GARDEN PATCH',text:'Picture a big garden. Everyone gets one small patch. Your patch only grows food if *you* dig it. If you spend all day digging next door, your patch stays bare. Nobody else was going to dig it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'If you copy someone, you make a weaker version of a thing that is already there. The thing only you could make never gets made.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'AT ART CLUB',text:'Sam\u2019s comic is a hit, so you draw one just like it. People say yours is fine, but not as good as Sam\u2019s. Your own idea, a comic about your grandad\u2019s pigeons, sits in a drawer. Nobody else could draw that one.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The hand adds one more copy to a long row. Under the red cloth sits something no one else could have made.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO SAID THIS',text:'Ralph Waldo Emerson was an American writer. He said copying kills off the best thing you have. He said each of us has our own patch of ground. Good things only grow there if we work it.'},{kind:'try',text:'List three things you love that your friends don\u2019t. Pick one, and make something from it this week.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend wins the poem prize with a poem about the sea. What does this idea say to do?',options:['Write about something only you know well','Write a sea poem too, but a bit longer','Stop writing, since she is better at it'],answer:0,why:'A copy of her poem will always come second. Your own subject is the one nobody else has.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE SAFE BET',belief:'Copying what already works is the safe choice.',reality:'It is safe only in that it rarely fails outright. It reliably produces a slightly worse version of something that exists, and it uses up the effort your own angle needed.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The angle only you have is the one thing not already covered. Spend your effort reproducing someone else\u2019s, and your contribution is made by nobody.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO START',text:'A rival\u2019s newsletter is thriving. You could copy its format and write it slightly better. Or you could build yours around what you know and they do not: ten years working in the trade they only report on. The first is easier to start. Only the second is not already on offer.'},{kind:'explain',title:'An argument about supply',text:'Emerson\u2019s complaint is not that copying is dishonest. It is that it is unproductive. Effort spent reproducing a thing that exists is effort not spent on the one thing that exists only in you. Nobody else was going to make that.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Another identical cast joins the row. Under the cloth, ignored, sits the one shape nobody else on the shelf could have made.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Copying',items:['Easy to start','Judged against the original','Adds a near-duplicate']},right:{title:'Your own angle',items:['Feels smaller at first','Judged on its own terms','Adds what was missing']},note:'Your angle feels less impressive from the inside, because you are used to it.'},{kind:'try',text:'Write down one thing you could do that a competitor structurally cannot, because of what you know or where you stand.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A caf\xe9 opens near a popular rival. Which plan follows Emerson\u2019s argument?',options:['Build the menu around the owner\u2019s own regional cooking','Copy the rival\u2019s best sellers and price them lower','Copy the rival\u2019s look so customers feel at home','Watch what works for the rival, then follow it'],answer:0,why:'The other three spend effort on a version of what already exists. Only the owner\u2019s own cooking offers something that would not exist otherwise.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'A PAIRED VERDICT',text:'In one passage Emerson sets two blunt verdicts side by side. Envy is ignorance; imitation is suicide. He means them as one error seen from two sides: attending to someone else\u2019s ground instead of your own. Idea 5 takes up the envy half.'},{kind:'idea',text:'What you could contribute exists only in you. Imitation does not merely produce a weaker copy. It withdraws the effort from the one place your contribution could have come from.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE PLOT OF GROUND',text:'Emerson\u2019s image: the universe is full of good, yet no kernel of nourishing corn reaches you except through your own labour on the plot of ground you were given to till. You did not choose the plot, and cannot swap it. Working someone else\u2019s is not only unoriginal. You are simply not on yours.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it feels smaller',text:'Your own angle is familiar to you, so it lacks the shine of someone else\u2019s success. That is the discount from idea 1 again, now applied to a whole direction of work rather than a single thought.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The row grows by one identical cast. The covered shape at the elbow is the only thing on the shelf that is not already there.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Nearly everyone learns by imitation first: apprentices copy masters, writers copy the writers they love. Emerson\u2019s rhetoric leaves no room for that stage, and it can sound like a promise that everyone hides a genius. The defensible claim is narrower. Past apprenticeship, effort spent duplicating is effort withheld from what only you could add.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What does your plot of ground grow that the people you admire cannot?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'A student says: \u201cBut every painter learns by copying the masters.\u201d What is the best reply on Emerson\u2019s behalf?',options:['Copying to learn a craft is fine; copying instead of your own work is the error','Students should never copy, since imitation always damages originality','Copying the masters is fine, because their work is worth more than yours','He would grant that imitation and originality are equally valuable'],answer:0,why:'The essay\u2019s target is effort that replaces your contribution. Learning a craft by copying prepares that contribution rather than standing in for it.'}]}},'self-reliance_4':{pictureCaption:'Under the meeting table, one hand folds a card smaller and smaller: an opinion paid away, out of sight, to keep the room smooth.',bridge:'Ideas 1 to 3 traced what you give up inside your own head. This one is the other side of the trade: the crowd\u2019s.',basics:{title:'Small yeses that cost you',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'PICKING A FILM',text:'Your friends pick a film. You think it looks bad, and you want to see the other one. You say, \u201cSure, that one\u2019s fine.\u201d Nobody asks again. It was just easier.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Each time you hide what you think to keep people happy, you pay a tiny price. The tiny prices add up. Most of the time, saying it would have been fine.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'UNDER THE TABLE',caption:'Up on the table, all is calm. Down below, a hand folds a small card again and again. The card is what you think. Each fold makes it smaller, and nobody sees.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO SAID THIS',text:'Ralph Waldo Emerson was an American writer. He said groups push us to fit in. We swap a bit of what we think for being liked. We do it so often that we don\u2019t even notice.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Count for one day',steps:['Each time you hide what you think, make a mark.','At bedtime, count the marks.','For each one, ask: would saying it have been so bad?']},{kind:'myth',belief:'So I should always say just what I think.',reality:'No. Being kind still matters. Sometimes you go along with friends because you care about them. The trick is to spot when you hide a view out of habit, not kindness.'},{kind:'try',text:'Tomorrow, pick one small moment. Say what you really think. Keep it kind and short.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your class votes on a trip. You want the museum, but everyone says the zoo. What fits this idea?',options:['Say you would like the museum, then go with the vote','Vote for the zoo so nobody thinks you are odd','Say you won\u2019t go unless it is the museum'],answer:0,why:'Saying what you think costs very little. You can still go along with the group once you have said it.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'ONE ORDINARY DAY',prompt:'In a normal day, how often do you soften or swallow an opinion to keep things smooth?',options:['Almost never','Once or twice','More often than you would guess'],answer:2,reveal:'People who actually count tend to be surprised by the total, and more surprised by how small the stakes were each time. That is the trade Emerson is describing.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Conformity is a trade made without noticing the price. You pay it in small daily instalments: an opinion softened, an objection not raised, a preference left unstated.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Emerson\u2019s image',text:'He describes society as a joint-stock company. Each member gives up some liberty and independent judgement, and in return gets a surer share of bread. Nobody signs this contract. It is made by default, one comfortable silence at a time.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'THE PAYMENT, IN PRIVATE',caption:'Above the table, cups and papers and a meeting going smoothly. Below it, one hand folds a card smaller and smaller until it fits out of sight. Nobody in the room sees the folding.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'The instalment',items:['Backing a plan you doubt','Not raising the objection','Ordering what everyone ordered']},right:{title:'What it bought',items:['A smoother five minutes','No awkward pause','Nothing at all']},note:'Each price is tiny. That is why nobody notices the total.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Emerson wants you to refuse every convention.',reality:'He overstates his case, and he knew it. Nobody functions without some deference to others. Read as a corrective rather than a rule, the essay is at its best on the small scale.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Count the instalments',steps:['For one day, mark each time you soften or swallow an opinion.','Beside each mark, note what was really at stake.','Tomorrow, pick one of those moments and say the thing.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Which of these is an instalment in the trade Emerson describes?',options:['Nodding along to a plan you expect to fail, to avoid a pause','Deferring to a colleague who knows the subject far better','Keeping a secret that a friend asked you to keep','Not arguing about a film you have not seen yourself'],answer:0,why:'Only the nod trades a view you actually hold for smoothness. Deferring to expertise or keeping a promise is not conformity.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'EMERSON\u2019S METAPHOR',word:'Joint-stock company',meaning:'A business owned in shares by many members, each pooling something in exchange for a safer return. Emerson uses it as his picture of society: a firm whose members pay in independence.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE CHARGE',text:'Society everywhere, Emerson claims, is in conspiracy against the independence of its members. Each surrenders liberty and self-cultivation for a surer share of bread. It prizes labels and habits over real people and real makers. Hence his demand that anyone who would be fully a person must be a nonconformist.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Conformity is a transaction made by default and paid in small daily instalments. The essay is weakest as a manifesto against convention and strongest as an audit of those instalments.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the price goes unnoticed',picture:!0,text:'Each instalment is too small to register as a choice. The opinion is softened before it is spoken, so there is no moment of deciding. And each buys something real, a smooth evening or an easy meeting, so it feels like courtesy rather than cost.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW IT COMPOUNDS',nodes:['A view softened to keep things smooth','The room hears agreement','Your silence becomes your position','Next time, dissent costs more'],loop:!0,note:'Each instalment raises the price of the next honest sentence.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Emerson overstates, and the overstatement is real, not decorative. Much deference is earned: to expertise, to shared rules, to people you love. As a rule, refuse all convention is unlivable and can license plain rudeness. Dissent also costs some people far more than others, and the essay rarely asks who pays.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What did you last agree with that you did not think? What did the agreement buy you?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A new hire sees a flawed process that everyone follows. Which response reads the essay as a corrective, not a rule?',options:['Raise the flaw once, clearly, then work within the decision','Follow it silently, since newcomers should not question things','Refuse to follow the process until somebody changes it','Ignore the process quietly and do the work their own way'],answer:0,why:'The instalment Emerson wants refused is the silence. Refusing all convention is the overstated version he cannot defend.'}]}},'self-reliance_5':{pictureCaption:'From the dark garden you see one bright, arranged room. The house around it is far larger, and almost all of it is out of view.',bridge:'Emerson pairs envy with imitation, the subject of idea 3. This lesson takes envy on its own and asks what it is trying to tell you.',basics:{title:'What envy is telling you',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A FRIEND\u2019S NEW BIKE',prompt:'Your friend gets the bike you wanted. You feel hot and sour inside. What is that feeling telling you?',options:['That your friend is lucky and you are not','That you want something, and should work out what'],answer:1,reveal:'The feeling points at a want. Maybe it is the bike. Maybe it is riding to places on your own. That is useful to know.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Envy is a sign that you want something. It is not a reason to think badly of the other person. Work out what you want, then do something about it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'You can see one bright, tidy room. The rest of the house is dark. You don\u2019t know what goes on in there.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Two things you can\u2019t see',text:'First, you only see the best bit of their life, like that one bright room. Second, you have not asked what you really want. Ralph Waldo Emerson, an American writer, said envy comes from not knowing.'},{kind:'steps',title:'When envy shows up',steps:['Name what you envy: the thing, not the person.','Ask: do I really want it?','If yes, do one small thing to get it.']},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your cousin plays in a band, and you feel envy. What does this lesson say to do?',options:['Ask what you want, like music, and take one step','Tell yourself your cousin is just showing off','Stop going to see the band play'],answer:0,why:'The envy pointed at something you want. Now you know, you can go after it.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE ANNOUNCEMENT',text:'Someone you started out with announces the promotion you quietly wanted. You spend the evening comparing your whole life with their one paragraph of news.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Envy compares your whole life with an edited version of someone else\u2019s. Read properly, it is a signal about what you want and are not pursuing, not a verdict on them.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One arranged front room, lit for anyone passing. The rest of the house, and most of the life in it, stays dark to the garden.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Right signal, wrong address',text:'Envy reliably points at something you want. What it gets wrong is the target. It fixes on the person who has the thing, and that produces comparison, which produces nothing. Moved from the person to the thing, the feeling becomes useful.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Read as a verdict',items:['\u201cThey don\u2019t deserve it\u201d','Comfortable, and closed','Your own ground stays untilled']},right:{title:'Read as a signal',items:['\u201cWhat exactly do I want?\u201d','Uncomfortable, and open','A next step appears']},note:'Emerson calls envy ignorance, not sin. Ignorance is cured by finding something out.'},{kind:'try',text:'When envy shows up, ask what you would have to give up to have the whole of that life, not just the visible part.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A friend\u2019s travel photos leave you sour for days. Which response reads the envy as a signal?',options:['Ask what in it you want, such as freedom, and whether you pursue it','Remind yourself the photos are edited and the trip was probably dull','Mute the friend so the feeling has nothing to attach itself to','Plan a bigger trip so that your own photos compare well'],answer:0,why:'Dismissing the trip or outdoing it keeps the focus on them. The useful question is about what you want.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'NOT A SIN',belief:'Envy is a moral failing, to be ashamed of and pushed down.',reality:'Emerson calls it ignorance, not wickedness. That changes the response. A failing calls for shame. A lack of information calls for finding something out.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Envy is ignorance in two directions: about the other life, seen only in its edited front room, and about yourself, since it points at a want you are not pursuing.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Ignorance of them',text:'You compare the whole of your life, known from the inside, with the visible part of theirs. The comparison is rigged before it starts. You are envying a life you have not actually seen.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Ignorance of yourself',text:'This is the half Emerson stresses. In the passage that pairs envy with imitation, his point is that your good can only come through your own plot of ground. Envy is not knowing what yours would grow, and so watching someone else\u2019s harvest instead.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The garden sees one arranged front room. The house is far larger, and the life in it is lived mostly where no passer-by can look.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Some envy points at injustice rather than a private want: unequal pay, a rigged process. Treating every case as self-knowledge can talk you out of a fair complaint. And the advice to tend your own ground is easier for someone whose ground is fertile. The essay has little to say to people with very few options.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Envy compares your inside with their edited outside.','Its real information is about what you want.','Ask what the whole of that life would cost you.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE COMFORTABLE MISREAD',prompt:'Which response to envy turns it into a verdict that seals itself?',options:['Deciding the other person must be unhappy underneath','Asking which part of their life you actually want','Checking whether you are doing anything toward it','Asking what that whole life would cost you to have'],answer:0,why:'It keeps the focus on them and leaves your own ground untilled. The other three turn the feeling back into information about you.'}]}},'self-reliance_6':{pictureCaption:'A bright new harbour through the window. On the bed, the case is still packed, the same coat folded exactly as it left home.',bridge:'Idea 3 said your plot of ground cannot be swapped. This one tests the commonest attempt to swap it: going somewhere else.',basics:{title:'You take yourself with you',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A NEW SCHOOL',prompt:'Leo hates his school. He thinks he is shy because of the kids there. He moves to a new school. What happens?',options:['He turns into a new person','He is still shy, in a new place'],answer:1,reveal:'New school, new kids, same Leo. His shyness came with him, like a coat packed in his bag.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Going somewhere new changes what is around you. It does not change you. So check what the new place can really fix.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Out of the window: a bright new place. Now look at the bag. Everything in it came from home.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO SAID THIS',text:'Ralph Waldo Emerson was an American writer. He said people travel to get away from a sad feeling. Then they wake up far away, and the same sad self is right there with them.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'WHEN MOVING DOES HELP',text:'Some things really are about the place. If a bully is in your class, a new class can fix that. The bully stays behind. Being shy, or being cross, comes along with you.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you think *it would be better somewhere else*, name the one thing the new place would take away.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Ava wants to change teams because she gets nervous before games. What does this idea say?',options:['A new team won\u2019t stop the nerves; they come with her','A new team will fix it, since the old team made her nervous','She should stop playing so the nerves go away'],answer:0,why:'The nerves belong to her, not to the team. A new team can\u2019t take them away.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE OFFER IN ANOTHER CITY',text:'Your job has been grinding you down. A post comes up in another city, and you feel lighter just reading it. The question is not whether to move. It is what the move can actually deliver.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A change of scene changes the inputs, not the thing processing them. Moving can remove what is truly tied to a place. It cannot remove what travels with you.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Left behind',items:['A genuinely bad manager','A long commute','A market with no work in it']},right:{title:'Packed with you',items:['How you handle ambiguity','Taking on more than you can hold','The feeling you hoped to escape']},note:'The habits on the right tend to rebuild the old conditions within a year.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Emerson\u2019s line',text:'He calls travelling a fool\u2019s paradise. You pack, sail, and wake in Naples, and beside you is the same sad self you fled. He is not against travel. He is against travel asked to do a job it cannot do.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A bright new harbour through the window. On the bed, the case is still packed, the same coat folded exactly as it left home.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before a big move, write down the specific thing the new place removes. If you cannot name one, the move is not the fix.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Which reason for moving survives Emerson\u2019s test?',options:['The only firms in my field are based in that city','I just need a fresh start somewhere completely new','I always feel restless here, and I would not there','Everyone I know who moved there seems happier'],answer:0,why:'It names a specific, place-bound thing the move removes. The others hope a place will change a feeling that is coming too.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'BEFORE THE TICKET',prompt:'Which of these can a move to a new city actually remove?',options:['A habit of overcommitting','A manager who undermines you','A low mood that follows you'],answer:1,reveal:'Only the manager stays behind. The other two are ways of processing whatever the new place supplies, and they board the train with you.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Travel changes the inputs and not the processor. Asked to change the self, it fails, because the self is the one thing certain to come along.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The passage',text:'Emerson calls travel a fool\u2019s paradise. In his telling, you pack your trunk, say goodbye, cross the sea and wake in Naples, and beside you is the very self you were fleeing, sad and unchanged. His own giant, he says, goes with him wherever he goes.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What he was against',text:'Not travel itself. He allows that a journey for study, art or doing good can serve well, and he had been to Europe himself. His target is the traveller who goes abroad to find what he does not carry within, and finds he has carried his trouble instead.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The harbour is new. The case on the bed is not unpacked, and what is in it is exactly what left home.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Sometimes the place really is the problem: a hostile town, a dead job market, an unsafe home. The line can be used to keep people stuck where leaving is right. Place also shapes habit, and new surroundings can make old routines easier to break. The fair test is narrower: name the specific thing the move removes.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What change of scene have you hoped would change you? What actually came along?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A team keeps missing deadlines and plans to fix it by switching to a new project tool. What would Emerson\u2019s test ask?',options:['What specific problem the tool removes that the team will not carry over','Whether the new tool is better reviewed than the current one','Whether the team will feel more motivated after the switch','How quickly the team can finish moving to the new tool'],answer:0,why:'A new tool is a change of scene. If the cause is how the team plans, that habit migrates along with the data.'}]}},'self-reliance_7':{pictureCaption:'The vault stands open, its key left in the lock. What walks out into the light is what was never stored inside.',bridge:'Idea 6 asked what comes with you when you move. This one asks what is still yours when the supports are taken away.',basics:{title:'What is still yours?',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE ARMBAND',text:'You have been team captain all year. You wear the armband at every game. Then a new coach picks someone else. The armband is gone. What is left?'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Leaning on things',items:['The armband','Your new boots','Being on the top team']},right:{title:'Leaning on yourself',items:['How well you pass','How you treat your team','Friends who like you anyway']},note:'The left side can be taken away. The right side comes with you.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Leaning on things you have, like a badge or nice stuff, is not the same as leaning on yourself. You find out which one you had when the things go.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The safe is open, and the key is left in it. The person walks out with empty hands. All they really are goes too.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO SAID THIS',text:'Ralph Waldo Emerson was an American writer. He said that leaning on what you own is not the same as trusting yourself. Things are nice to have. But they are not you.'},{kind:'try',text:'Make a list: what would still be yours if you lost all your stuff? Skills, friends, things you know. Add one thing to the list this month.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Jay is proud of being the best at a video game. Then the game shuts down. What is still his?',options:['How quick he got at thinking and learning','His top score in the game','His place at the top of the game\u2019s list'],answer:0,why:'The score and the place went with the game. What he learned while playing is his.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'ask',kicker:'A STOCKTAKE',prompt:'If your balance went to zero and your job ended tomorrow, what would still be true about you?'},{kind:'idea',text:'Depending on property, position or an institution is reliance, and it works. Self-reliance is what remains when those are withdrawn. From the inside the two feel identical, until they do not.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Emerson is saying security is a trap, and you should give it up.',reality:'Savings and a stable employer are genuine security, and giving them up on principle would be foolish. He is not recommending poverty. He is asking you to know which kind of support you are standing on.'},{kind:'steps',title:'The inventory',steps:['Skills that would transfer to another employer.','Relationships that are yours, not the role\u2019s.','A sense of yourself that no job title supplies.'],note:'Not a fear exercise. A stocktake.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it changes how you act',text:'Someone with that inventory can negotiate without defending the arrangement at any cost. They can say no, or ask for more, because losing the job would not mean losing themselves. Oddly, that tends to make the arrangement safer, not riskier.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The vault stands open, its key left in the lock. What walks out into the light is what was never stored inside.'},{kind:'try',text:'List what would still be yours if the job ended tomorrow. Spend this quarter adding one item to that list.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Two colleagues have the same title and salary. Which is more self-reliant in Emerson\u2019s sense?',options:['The one whose skills and contacts would survive leaving','The one with the larger savings account behind them','The one who has been at the firm the longest','The one with the most senior manager backing them'],answer:0,why:'Savings, tenure and a patron are all reliance. They work, but they are supports, not what remains when supports go.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO KINDS OF SUPPORT',left:{title:'Reliance',items:['Property','Position','An institution\u2019s backing']},right:{title:'Self-reliance',items:['What you can do','What you are','What remains when the rest goes']},note:'On an ordinary day the two columns produce exactly the same calm.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Reliance on property or position is the want of self-reliance, not the evidence of it. The test is what would remain if those supports were withdrawn.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the essay says',text:'Emerson argues that leaning on property, and on the governments that protect it, shows a lack of self-reliance. A cultivated person, he says, comes to be ashamed of property. What a man has is an accident and can be taken. What he is goes with him and keeps renewing itself.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why they feel the same',text:'Both give the same calm on an ordinary day. The difference shows only under withdrawal: a job lost, a market closed, a title removed. By then it is late to learn which you had. Hence the inventory: transferable skills, relationships that are yours, an identity no title supplies.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The strongroom stands open, its key abandoned in the lock. The figure leaving carries nothing out, and is not made smaller by it.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Emerson underrates how much of anyone\u2019s self is built from circumstance: health, schooling, money that bought the time to learn. Read harshly, the idea blames people for needing supports they had no choice about. Skills and relationships also rest on markets and on other people. As an inventory it is useful. As a purity test it fails.'},{kind:'keyline',text:'The supports are real. Mistaking them for yourself is the error.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HONEST OBJECTION',prompt:'Which objection to the idea is the strongest?',options:['Skills and relationships also depend on circumstance and on other people','Savings are always worthless, so relying on them is pointless','Self-reliance cannot be felt, so there is no evidence it exists','Emerson himself never had any property or position to lose'],answer:0,why:'The line between what you have and what you are is less clean than Emerson suggests. That is why it works better as an inventory than as a verdict.'}]}},'self-reliance_8':{pictureCaption:'Leaflets for far-off causes, smoothed flat and read. Pushed to the corner, unopened, a letter in handwriting you know.',bridge:'Idea 7 separated real support from the kind you lean on. This one separates costly generosity from the cheap kind.',basics:{title:'Kind far away, kind close by',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'TWO KINDS OF KIND',text:'Mia puts her pocket money in the charity box at school. That is kind. At home, her little brother keeps asking her to play, and she always says no. Which kind thing would cost her more?'},{kind:'idea',text:'Being kind to people far away can be easy, because nothing in your day has to change. Being kind to people close by is harder. Do both.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The hand is busy with leaflets about faraway places. In the corner sits a letter from someone close, still not opened.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO SAID THIS',text:'Ralph Waldo Emerson was an American writer. He saw that some people cared a lot about faraway causes but were unkind at home. Caring far away cost them little. Caring at home cost them time and effort.'},{kind:'scale',low:'Far away: a coin in a box',high:'Close by: an hour with your brother',note:'The closer the person, the more being kind costs you: your time, your patience, being there.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of someone close who needs a bit of you: a call, a game, a hand with something. Do it this week.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Leo raises money for a far-off animal charity. His gran has asked him to visit for weeks. What does this idea say?',options:['Keep raising money, and visit Gran too','Stop the charity work, since it is fake','Keep raising money and skip the visit'],answer:0,why:'The far-off giving does good. It just should not stand in for the harder thing close by.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE STANDING ORDER',text:'Your monthly donations go out like clockwork, and they do real good. Meanwhile there is a difficult phone call to your brother that you have been putting off since the spring.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Distance makes virtue cheap: giving to strangers far away asks nothing of your life. Proximity makes it expensive. The risk is that the cheap act quietly stands in for the costly one.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE LEDGER BALANCES',nodes:['You feel you ought to help','You give to a distant cause','The feeling is discharged','The nearby call is not made'],note:'Nothing is wrong with the gift. What goes wrong is the bookkeeping.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Emerson\u2019s complaint',text:'He pictures a hard, uncharitable man who parades his tenderness for people a thousand miles away. Emerson tells him to go and be kind to his own child and the man who chops his wood. Love kept far off, he says, can be spite at home.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Leaflets for far-off causes, smoothed flat and read. Pushed to the corner, unopened, a letter in handwriting you know.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'So giving to distant causes is a dodge.',reality:'No. The donations do good; keep them. The question is what the giving might be standing in for. If every form of your generosity can run by standing order, it never asks you to be present.'},{kind:'try',text:'Keep the donations. This week, also do the nearby thing you have been avoiding.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Which sign best suggests that distant giving has become a substitute?',options:['Every form of your generosity can run without you being present','You give more to distant causes than to local ones','You chose your charities by reading about their impact','You give on a regular schedule rather than when asked'],answer:0,why:'Proportions and schedules are not the issue. The warning sign is generosity arranged so that it never asks for you in person.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'THE REFORMER AT THE DOOR',text:'Emerson imagines a zealot who arrives with the latest news of abolition in Barbados, a hard, uncharitable man wrapped in tenderness for people a thousand miles away. Emerson tells him to love his own child and his woodcutter, to be good-natured and modest. Love that is all far away, he says, is spite at home.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Distance makes virtue cheap and proximity makes it costly. Generosity that never requires your presence is worth examining, not for what it does, but for what it may be standing in for.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The wicked dollar',text:'A few lines on, Emerson admits he sometimes gives to popular charities he does not believe in, and calls each such dollar wicked. The fault he names there is giving under social pressure, the conformity of idea 4. The modern reading adds a second risk: the cheap act discharging the feeling that would drive the costly one.'},{kind:'equation',parts:['A standing order','An avoided call'],result:'A ledger that feels balanced',note:'The sum is false. The two entries were never in the same currency.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The leaflets are read and smoothed flat. The envelope in familiar handwriting has been pushed to the far corner, unopened.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Emerson\u2019s own position is harsher than the lesson drawn from it. He asks whether the poor are really his to help, and grudges money given to people he feels no bond with. Distant giving can do more good than anything nearby, and used to belittle it, this idea becomes an excuse to give nothing. The fair claim is only about substitution.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which part of your generosity asks you to be present? Which part could run without you?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which use of this idea goes wrong?',options:['Cancelling your donations because distant giving is only cheap virtue','Noticing that your giving never requires you to show up','Making the difficult call you had been putting off','Asking what a monthly donation might be standing in for'],answer:0,why:'The idea targets substitution, not the gift. Cancelling the donations removes the good and still leaves the nearby duty undone.'}]}},'self-reliance_9':{pictureCaption:'Trophy in hand at the top, and the landing matches the one below, with the next flight already starting. Success moved him up, not anywhere new.',bridge:'Idea 6 said a new place cannot fix you. The essay\u2019s last lines say the same of good fortune and success.',basics:{title:'Getting it doesn\u2019t settle it',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE BIG DAY',prompt:'You have wanted a games console for a whole year. On your birthday, you get it. How do you feel a week later?',options:['Happy for good','About the same as before'],answer:1,reveal:'A lot of the fun was in the waiting and hoping. Once it comes, the waiting ends, and so does that feeling. Soon you want the next thing.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Getting the thing you wanted feels great, but it does not make you calm inside for long. That kind of calm has to come from you.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE WANTING LOOP',nodes:['Want a thing','Wait and hope','Get it','The good feeling fades'],loop:!0,note:'Wanting the next thing starts the loop again.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'AT THE TOP',caption:'He climbed all those stairs to win the cup. At the top, the floor looks just like the one at the bottom. And more stairs start right there.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO SAID THIS',text:'Ralph Waldo Emerson was an American writer. He ended his essay *Self-Reliance* with this idea. Nothing outside you can give you peace, he said. Only you can, by living by what you think is right.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'So wanting things, or trying to win, is bad.',reality:'No. Working hard and winning is great. Just don\u2019t expect the prize to fix how you feel inside. Then it can simply be a nice thing.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you get something you waited for, check how you feel a week later. Then ask what makes you feel calm on a normal day.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Zara wins the school race. A few days later she feels flat. What does this lesson say?',options:['That is normal; a prize can\u2019t give lasting calm','Something is wrong, so she must win a bigger race','Winning was pointless, so she should stop racing'],answer:0,why:'Winning was good. It just can\u2019t give the inside kind of calm. That comes from her.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'HOW THE ESSAY ENDS',text:'Emerson closes *Self-Reliance* on luck. A political win, a rise in income, a sick friend recovering: some good turn lifts your spirits, and you think good days are coming. Do not believe it, he says. Only you can bring yourself peace, by holding to your principles.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'TWO YEARS FOR THIS',text:'The promotion you worked two years for is announced. There is a round of congratulations. By mid-afternoon you are sitting at your desk feeling oddly flat, and slightly ashamed of it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Achievement cannot deliver the peace people expect from it. The anticipation was doing the work, and anticipation ends on arrival. The flatness is the ordinary case, not a malfunction.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Set the next target',items:['Restores the anticipation','Works, for a while','The same afternoon, one rung up']},right:{title:'See where peace was',items:['Takes weight off the next goal','Sounds bleak, turns out freeing','Achieving goes on, unburdened']},note:'The left column works. That is exactly why it repeats.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'ONE RUNG UP',caption:'The climb is over and the trophy is in hand. The landing looks exactly like the one at the bottom, and the next flight of stairs already starts beside it.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'So achievement is worthless.',reality:'Emerson is not saying that. Achievement simply cannot supply one specific thing. Seeing that is freeing, because the next goal no longer has to carry a weight it was never going to carry.'},{kind:'try',text:'When the next goal lands flat, resist setting a new one for a week. Notice what the flatness is actually about.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'After a long-awaited house purchase, a friend feels oddly empty and starts planning an extension. What would Emerson suggest?',options:['Pause and notice what the emptiness is about before chasing the next goal','Start the extension quickly, so that the momentum is not lost','Sell the house, since it has plainly failed to bring happiness','Accept that nothing is worth wanting, and stop striving altogether'],answer:0,why:'The house was not a mistake, and striving is not wrong. The peace was simply never located in the outcome.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'THE LAST PARAGRAPH',text:'The essay ends not on effort but on fortune. Emerson lists the turns of luck people pin their hopes on: a political victory, rising rents, a sick person recovering, an absent friend coming home. Each lifts your spirits, and you think good days are being prepared for you. He tells you not to believe it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Nothing external, success included, can bring you peace. Only you can, by holding to your principles. Achievement is real; it just cannot deliver the particular thing expected of it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Two halves of the last line',text:'Peace comes from yourself, and from the triumph of principles. The first half is about location: peace is not stored in outcomes. The second is about content: it comes from living by what you hold true, which no event can award or take away.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHY THE FIX REPEATS',nodes:['Anticipation does the work','The goal arrives','Anticipation ends; flatness','A new target restores it'],loop:!0,note:'The fix works, which is the trap. It guarantees the same afternoon, one rung up, indefinitely.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'THE LANDING',caption:'A trophy carried up a long flight arrives on a landing identical to the one below, where the next flight is already waiting. Arrival changes the height, not the place peace would have to come from.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Bleak, then freeing',text:'Seen once, the point lifts a weight. The next promotion was never going to carry the load of peace, so it no longer has to. You can want it, work for it and enjoy it, without asking it to do what it cannot.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The turn inward can sound like contempt for circumstance. Some outcomes, a recovery, a safe home, enough income, really do change how a life feels, and telling someone in real need that peace is internal is cold. The claim fits best where basic needs are met. Pushed too far, it also makes achievement feel pointless, which is not what he meant.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Success cannot supply peace; flatness on arrival is normal.','Setting the next target restores anticipation and repeats the flatness.','Emerson places peace in living by your principles.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'A reader objects that this is easy for Emerson to say, and that a pay rise really would make their life calmer. What is the fairest reply?',options:['The rise may ease real strain; it cannot supply the peace Emerson means','The reader is wrong, because money never changes how a life feels','Emerson would say the reader should simply stop wanting the rise','The objection shows Emerson was mistaken about peace altogether'],answer:0,why:'Relief from a real pressure and inward peace are different goods. Emerson denies only that the second can come from outside.'}]}}}},3006,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'seven-minutes-with-the-doctor_1':{pictureCaption:'On the way out, the handle already turning, he swings back to say the thing he came in for. The idea is to say it first.',basics:{title:'Say what you are scared it is',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'AT THE DOCTOR\u2019S',text:'Your tummy has hurt for a week. At the doctor\u2019s, you say where it hurts and when. But what you really want to ask is this: is it the same thing that put your cousin in hospital?'},{kind:'idea',text:'When you see a doctor, say what is wrong. Then also say what you are scared it might be. The worry is often the real reason you came.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why say the worry?',text:'If you only say where it hurts, the doctor answers that. If you also say what scares you, the doctor can check for that and talk to you about it. Then you do not go home still wondering.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'He is leaving, but he turns back at the door. The thing he really came to say is coming out last. Try saying it first.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'HOW IT SOUNDS',text:'Try two short parts. What is happening: *My knee has hurt for two weeks.* What you are scared of: *I\u2019m worried I broke something.* That is all it takes.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you see a doctor, plan your first sentence with a grown-up. Put the worry in, even if it feels silly to say.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Leo has a cough. He is scared it is asthma, like his brother has. What should he say first?',options:['I\u2019ve had a cough for a week and I\u2019m scared it\u2019s asthma','I\u2019ve had a cough for a week and it\u2019s worse at night','Nothing yet, and wait for the doctor to ask the right things'],answer:0,why:'Saying it is worse at night is useful too, but it leaves out the worry. Saying the worry lets the doctor deal with what Leo really came about.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'HAND ON THE DOOR',text:'The appointment goes smoothly. You describe the headaches, the doctor asks about sleep and work, and suggests some changes. You stand, reach for the door, and turn back to ask whether this could be what your father had. That was the reason you came.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Say the worry alongside the symptom. A symptom gets a clinical answer. The worry is often a different question, and unless you say it, it may never be raised.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Symptom only',items:['Doctor answers a clinical question','Time goes on what you said','Real fear raised at the door, or never']},right:{title:'Symptom and worry',items:['Doctor can address why you came','It changes what gets examined','The fear is dealt with in the room']},note:'One extra sentence. A different appointment.'},{kind:'explain',title:'A familiar pattern',text:'It is common enough to be widely recognised: a minor complaint first, and the real concern mentioned at the door, or not at all. If the worry is the hardest sentence to say, it is easy to save it for last, and last is when there is no time left.'},{kind:'steps',title:'The opening sentence',steps:['Say what is happening: the symptom, and for how long.','Say what you are afraid it is.','Then let the doctor lead.']},{kind:'picture',caption:'Turning back with the door already open. The question the visit was for, arriving when there is no time left to answer it.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before your next appointment, write down the sentence you are most nervous about saying. Make it your first.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A patient has found a lump. Their real fear is that it is what their sister had. Which opening applies the idea?',options:['I\u2019ve found a lump, and I\u2019m worried it\u2019s what my sister had','I\u2019ve found a lump on my arm, about two weeks ago now','I\u2019ve found a lump, but it is probably nothing much','I\u2019ll bring up the lump at the end if there is time'],answer:0,why:'Only naming the sister gives the doctor the actual question. The others leave the fear for the door, or out altogether.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHAT THE ESSAY IS FOR',text:'This short Mindroll essay looks at what actually happens in a short appointment, and what a patient can do in the first ninety seconds that changes the rest of it. Its first move is to change what the appointment is for.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Stating the worry alongside the symptom changes what the consultation is for. A symptom gets a clinical answer; the worry is often a different question, and otherwise it is never raised.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Two different questions',text:'A doctor given a symptom answers a clinical question: what is causing this? A doctor given the worry can answer the question you actually brought: is this the thing my father had? The two are frequently different, and the second changes what gets examined.'},{kind:'term',word:'Presenting complaint',meaning:'The symptom a patient gives as the reason for a visit. The essay\u2019s point is that it is often not the whole reason they came.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The real concern, raised at the door on the way out: the widely recognised pattern the essay sets out to prevent.'},{kind:'keyline',text:'The symptom is what you report. The worry is why you came.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Saying the worry does not mean it will be tested for, or should be. Sometimes the right answer is that the feared thing is unlikely, explained rather than investigated. And the worry goes alongside the symptom, not in place of it: the doctor still needs a clear account of what is happening to answer either question.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISREADING',prompt:'Which of these misapplies the idea?',options:['Opening with the feared diagnosis and skipping the symptoms','Giving the symptom, then naming the fear in one sentence','Mentioning a family history because it is the real concern','Saying the hardest sentence first rather than at the door'],answer:0,why:'The worry goes alongside the symptom, not instead of it. The doctor still needs to know what is happening to answer either question.'}]}},'seven-minutes-with-the-doctor_2':{pictureCaption:'One person is still explaining with open hands. The other has already picked up one tester to check one idea. That is an early interruption.',bridge:'Idea 1 said what to say first. This one explains why first matters: you may not get long before you are interrupted.',basics:{title:'When the doctor cuts in',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'AT THE BIKE SHOP',prompt:'You tell the bike mechanic about a squeak, a wobble and the gears. He stops you after the squeak and grabs a tool. Why?',options:['He is being rude','He has a guess and wants to test it','He is bored of you'],answer:1,reveal:'He heard *squeak* and thought of a likely cause. He wants to check it now. He is not ignoring the rest. He just has not got to it yet.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Doctors often cut in early. Usually it is not rude. They have a guess and want to test it. So say the most important thing first, and say when you have more.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One person is still explaining. The other has picked up a tool to check one thing. Cutting in can mean testing a guess.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why doctors do it',text:'Doctors have lots of people to see and not much time. They make a guess early and check it with a question. When the guess is right, it saves time. When it is wrong, they may miss the thing you had not said yet.'},{kind:'steps',title:'So you get heard',steps:['Say the most important thing first.','Say how many things you have: *I have three things.*','If you get cut off, answer the question.','Then say: *I haven\u2019t finished yet.*']},{kind:'question',prompt:'The doctor cuts in while you are still talking. What is the best thing to do?',options:['Answer, then say you have not finished','Stop talking, because doctors know best','Get cross and say they are being rude'],answer:0,why:'The doctor is testing a guess. Answer it, then get back to what you still had to say.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'IT FEELS LIKE A BRUSH-OFF',belief:'When a doctor interrupts you early, they have stopped listening.',reality:'Usually they are testing a hypothesis. A clinician in a short appointment is pattern-matching under time pressure. You are not being dismissed. You are being narrowed, sometimes too early.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The early interruption is usually a hypothesis being tested, not rudeness. Because it can come within the first minute, put the most important thing first, and say when you have not finished.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How early, and why',text:'Studies of consultations have repeatedly found that patients are interrupted early, often well within the first minute. The doctor forms a hypothesis and interrupts to test it. That is efficient when the hypothesis is right, and costly when the unmentioned item was the important one.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Mid-explanation, the expert has already picked up one tester for one idea. Narrowing, not dismissing.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Building up to it',items:['Background first, main point last','The build-up gets interrupted','The main point may never land']},right:{title:'Front-loading',items:['Main point first','The interruption tests the right thing','Other items can follow']},note:'The fix is in the order you speak, not in a fight.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Name the shape',text:'Start by saying how many things you have: *there are three things.* That sets an expectation which survives the interruption. It also makes coming back to the list feel natural rather than pushy.'},{kind:'try',text:'When you are interrupted, answer the question first. Then say: *That was the first thing. I have two more.*'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Thirty seconds in, the doctor cuts across your second point to ask about your diet. What is the best response?',options:['Answer about diet, then say you have one more thing to raise','Say you had not finished, and ask them to let you speak first','Drop the second point, since the doctor has chosen a direction','Ignore the question and carry on with your second point'],answer:0,why:'The question is a hypothesis worth answering. Saying you have not finished keeps your list alive without a fight.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'WHAT THE DOCTOR IS DOING',word:'Testing a hypothesis',meaning:'Forming a likely explanation early, then asking a targeted question to confirm or rule it out. In the essay\u2019s account, the early interruption is usually one of these tests.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Patients are often interrupted well within the first minute, usually because a clinician is testing an early hypothesis. The answer is structural: front-load what matters, and name the shape of your list.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The evidence and the trade-off',text:'Studies of consultations have repeatedly found early interruption. The essay reads it as working method, not rudeness: an early hypothesis, tested at once, under a schedule. That pays off when the hypothesis is right. When the important item was still unmentioned, it is lost.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW AN ITEM GETS LOST',nodes:['Patient builds up to the point','Doctor forms an early hypothesis','Interruption narrows the conversation','The main point is never reached'],note:'The essay puts its fix at the first step, the one the patient controls.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The interrupting hands have picked up one specific tester for one specific idea, while the other person is still mid-sentence.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why naming the shape works',text:'Saying there are three things sets an expectation before the first interruption. Afterwards, coming back to item two is following an agreed plan, not reclaiming the floor. Returning becomes natural rather than assertive.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The reading is charitable on purpose, and usually is not always. Some interruptions are dismissive, and a patient repeatedly cut off is entitled to say so plainly. Front-loading also has a cost: if you do not yet know which symptom matters, the most important item may not be the one you can name first.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'A patient objects that if they must manage the doctor\u2019s interruptions, the system has failed. What does the essay\u2019s approach do?',options:['Accepts the time pressure as given and offers a way to be heard within it','Denies that short appointments put doctors under any time pressure','Tells patients to complain until appointments become longer','Says interruptions are always right, so nothing needs managing'],answer:0,why:'The essay does not defend the schedule. It works inside it: front-load and name the shape, because both survive an interruption.'}]}},'seven-minutes-with-the-doctor_3':{pictureCaption:'A small card on a waiting-room seat, three short lines on it and no more. Three things, written down, picked up on the way in.',bridge:'Ideas 1 and 2 both said lead with what matters most. This one turns that into something you prepare the night before.',basics:{title:'Bring three things, in order',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'IN THE CAR PARK',text:'Mia went to the doctor about an itchy rash. She also meant to ask about her headaches and her sore ankle. In the room, her mind went blank. She remembered both in the car on the way home.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Before you see the doctor, write down three things, most important first. A short list beats a long story, and paper does not go blank.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A SMALL BAG',text:'If your bag only fits three things, you pack the most important first. A short visit to the doctor is a small bag. Whatever is at the bottom might not fit.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why only three?',text:'A short visit has room for about three things. If you bring ten, time may run out before the big one. So put the most important thing at the top.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why write it down?',text:'When you are nervous, it is easy to forget. Lots of people remember their main question after they leave. That is not bad luck. It is what nerves do. A note fixes it.'},{kind:'steps',title:'The night before',steps:['Write three short lines.','Put the one that worries you most at the top.','Take the paper with you.','Read it out at the start.']},{kind:'picture',caption:'A small card with just three short lines on it. That is all you need to take in with you.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You have five things to ask the doctor, and the visit is short. What is the best plan?',options:['Pick the three that matter most and write them in order','Say all five fast, in the order you thought of them','Ask just the easiest one and leave the rest'],answer:0,why:'Three, in order, fits a short visit. If time runs out, it runs out on the least important thing.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TEN MINUTES TO PREPARE',prompt:'You have a short appointment tomorrow and ten minutes to prepare. What is most useful?',options:['A full account of everything since it started','Three items, most important first','Reading up on possible causes'],answer:1,reveal:'A full account will not fit, and wherever it is cut off decides what gets missed. Three ordered items means that if the appointment ends early, it ends on the least important one.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Arrive with a short ordered list rather than a full account: three items, most important first, because the appointment is unlikely to reach a fourth. Write it down.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Unordered, in your head',items:['The ending falls anywhere','Item one may be the trivial one','Going blank loses everything']},right:{title:'Ordered, on paper',items:['The ending cuts the least important','The worry is item one','The paper survives going blank']}},{kind:'explain',title:'Why write it down',text:'Recall under stress is poor. Remembering the main question in the car park is a predictable failure, not bad luck, and going blank costs the most when time is short. Reading from paper is a little awkward, and worth it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Three short lines on a card, picked up from the waiting-room seat. Paper does not go blank when the door opens.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Say the list at the start',text:'Read all three out first. The clinician can then allocate the time, and tell you straight away that item three needs a separate appointment. That is more useful than finding out as the appointment ends.'},{kind:'try',text:'The night before your next appointment, write three lines. Put the one that scares you at the top.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Your list has four items and the appointment is short. Item four is the one that frightens you most. What should you do?',options:['Move it to the top and drop the least important item','Keep the order and raise it at the end if there is time','Add more detail to items one to three before you go','Leave the list at home and simply speak freely'],answer:0,why:'The order decides what gets cut. The frightening item belongs first, where the appointment is sure to reach it.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE THOROUGH PATIENT',belief:'The best preparation is a complete account, start to finish, so that nothing is missed.',reality:'In a short appointment a full account is what gets something missed: it runs out of time in an arbitrary place. The essay recommends three items, ordered by importance, written down.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A short ordered list is the most useful preparation available: three items, most important first, written down, and read out at the start so the time can be shared out sensibly.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Each part has a reason',text:'Three, because it is roughly what fits. Ordered, because the consultation may end early, and an unordered list makes the ending arbitrary. Written, because recall under stress is poor. Stated at the start, so the clinician can allocate the time, and say early if item three needs its own appointment.'},{kind:'equation',parts:['Three items','Most important first','Written down'],result:'A list that survives',note:'Remove any one and the list fails in a predictable way: too long, cut at random, or forgotten.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The whole method fits on a small card: three lines, no more, carried into the room. What the card cannot show is the order. That is set by writing the most important line first.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The car park problem',text:'Remembering the main question after you have left is a common experience. The essay treats it as a predictable failure, not bad luck: recall under stress is poor, and a short appointment leaves no time to recover. Paper is the fix.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Three is a rule of thumb, not a finding: the essay says only that it is roughly what fits. Some problems will not compress into one line. And the essay asks for two orderings that can differ: most important first, and the one that scares you at the top. What you fear most is not always what matters most clinically.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'The same logic works outside the surgery. Which case fits it best?',options:['Opening a five-minute call with the one decision you need made','Sending a full project history ahead of a short call','Raising the main issue at the end, once rapport is built','Listing every concern so nothing could be called hidden'],answer:0,why:'Short, ordered, most important first: the call may end early, and the decision is what has to survive.'}]}},'seven-minutes-with-the-doctor_4':{pictureCaption:'He is not looking through the doorway. He is feeling for its edge. The question asks the doctor where the diagnosis stops.',bridge:'Ideas 1 to 3 were about the start of the appointment. This one is for the end, just before you leave.',basics:{title:'Ask what would change their mind',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A SMOKE ALARM',text:'A smoke alarm does not just say *be careful of fire.* It goes off at one clear sign: smoke. You never have to guess when to act. A good answer from a doctor can work like that.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Before you leave the doctor, ask: *What would make you think it was something else?* The answer tells you what to watch for, and when to come back.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'A fuzzy answer',items:['Come back if it gets worse','Worse how? You have to guess']},right:{title:'A clear sign',items:['Come back if the rash spreads','You know just what to watch']}},{kind:'picture',caption:'He is not looking through the doorway. He is feeling for its edge. This question asks the doctor where the edges are.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Is it rude to ask?',text:'No. You are not saying the doctor is wrong. You are asking how they worked it out. Most doctors are happy to tell you. When they are busy, they just don\u2019t always say it unless you ask.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you see a doctor, ask with your grown-up: *What should make us come back?* Listen for one clear sign.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'The doctor says your sore ankle is a sprain. What is the best thing to ask before you go?',options:['What would make you think it was something else?','Are you really sure that it isn\u2019t broken?','How long until I can play football again?'],answer:0,why:'*Are you sure?* just gets a yes or no. Asking what would change the doctor\u2019s mind gets you clear signs to watch for at home.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO LEAVE',text:'The doctor says it is probably a viral infection. One patient leaves with *come back if it gets worse.* Another asks one question first, and leaves knowing which two changes would mean it is something else.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Ask what would change their mind. It turns a verdict into conditions you can watch for at home, and a vague instruction to return into a specific trigger.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the answer contains',text:'It shows what the diagnosis rests on, what would prompt a second look, and which developments matter. That is exactly the information you need in order to know when to come back.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Asking what would change their mind is challenging the doctor.',reality:'It does not challenge the conclusion. It asks for the reasoning, which most clinicians are willing to supply and rarely volunteer under time pressure.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Not looking through the doorway: feeling for where its edges are. The question asks the same of a diagnosis.'},{kind:'keyline',text:'A specific trigger is something you can act on. A vague one, reliably, is not.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before you leave your next appointment, ask: what change in my symptoms should bring me back?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'The doctor diagnoses a muscle strain. Which question best applies the idea?',options:['What would we expect to see if this were something else?','How sure are you that it really is just a strain?','Could you refer me for a scan, just to be safe?','How long does a strain like this usually take?'],answer:0,why:'Asking what something else would look like finds the edges of the diagnosis, which gives you signs to watch for. The others ask for confidence, a test or a timeline.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'ONE QUESTION BEFORE YOU GO',prompt:'Which question gets you the most usable information about a diagnosis?',options:['How confident are you?','What would we see if this were something else?','What usually happens next?'],answer:1,reveal:'Confidence gives you a feeling to trust. The edge question gives you conditions to watch. It turns a judgement into a specification.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Ask what would change their mind. In a consultation, as elsewhere, it converts a judgement into a specification: what the diagnosis rests on, and what should bring you back.'},{kind:'term',word:'Specification',meaning:'A set of conditions precise enough to check. Here, the developments that would mean the diagnosis should be looked at again, so you know when to return.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it is not adversarial',text:'The question does not dispute the conclusion. It asks for the reasoning behind it, which most clinicians are willing to supply and rarely volunteer under time pressure. You are asking to share the doctor\u2019s list of what to watch, not to reopen the verdict.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Vague instruction',items:['Return if it gets worse','Worse is never defined','Reliably not acted on']},right:{title:'Specific trigger',items:['Return if this particular change happens','Defined before you leave','Actionable at home']}},{kind:'picture',caption:'Attention on the frame, not the opening: the question maps the boundary of a diagnosis rather than asking for it to be restated.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The answer is only as good as the time and candour behind it, and some diagnoses have no clean edges to name. A list of warning signs may also feed anxiety in someone prone to checking. And a trigger is not the only reason to return: something new and alarming still counts, even if it was not on the list.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'The essay says the question works outside medicine too. Which use keeps its point?',options:['Asking a mechanic what noise would mean the repair had failed','Asking a mechanic how sure they are that the repair will hold','Asking a mechanic to check the whole car over, to be safe','Asking a mechanic whether there is a cheaper way to fix it'],answer:0,why:'Only asking about the noise turns a verdict into a trigger you can watch for. The others ask for confidence, more work or a lower price.'}]}},'seven-minutes-with-the-doctor_5':{pictureCaption:'Two chairs on the patient\u2019s side of the desk, not one. The second person, in the terracotta chair, is there to hear what is said.',bridge:'The first four ideas help you say and ask. This last one is about remembering the answers.',basics:{title:'Take someone to listen',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'WHY GO WITH SOMEONE?',belief:'You only take someone to the doctor so you are not on your own.',reality:'That helps. But a big reason is memory. Two people are likely to remember more of what the doctor said than one.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When a doctor visit might bring scary news, take someone along to listen. When you are worried, it is hard to take in what you hear.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why worry makes you forget',text:'When you are scared, your mind fixes on the scary thing. You stop hearing the rest. The person with you is not caught in the same way, so they hear what was really said.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two people sit on the patient\u2019s side of the desk. The one in the orange chair has come to listen.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'AT GRAN\u2019S CHECK-UP',text:'Your mum goes with Gran to the doctor. Gran asks the questions. Mum writes down the answers. At home, Gran can read what was said, not just what she was afraid of.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Decide the jobs first',steps:['Decide who asks the questions.','Decide who writes things down.','Read the notes together after.'],note:'If you both just chat, nobody writes anything down.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your mum is going with Grandad to hear some test results. What is the most useful job for her?',options:['Listen closely and write down what the doctor says','Do most of the talking so Grandad can rest','Chat with Grandad so he feels less nervous'],answer:0,why:'Company is kind, but the big help is a second pair of ears that are not so scared.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE DRIVE HOME',text:'The consultant explained the results, the options and the next steps. You nodded throughout. In the car, your partner asks what she said about the second scan, and you realise you heard one word clearly and very little after it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Recall after a consultation is poor, and worse when the news is frightening. Taking someone with you, purely to listen, is likely to improve how much is remembered.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHY THE NEWS GETS LOST',nodes:['Frightening news','Anxiety narrows attention','You hear what you feared','Much of the rest is lost'],note:'Studies generally find patients forget a good deal of what they were told, and distress makes it worse.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the second person adds',text:'They are not subject to the same narrowing. They hear what was said rather than what was feared, and they can ask the question you did not think of.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two chairs on the patient\u2019s side. The second person is there to hear, not just to keep you company.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Agree the jobs beforehand',steps:['One person asks the questions.','The other writes down the answers.','Read the notes back together afterwards.'],note:'Without roles, the second person tends to join the conversation and record nothing: company, and not much else.'},{kind:'try',text:'If an appointment might bring difficult news, ask someone to come, and tell them in advance that their job is to write.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Your friend is going with her mother to hear biopsy results. What is likely to help most?',options:['Agree beforehand that her mother asks and she writes','Help her mother by asking most of the questions herself','Keep her mother calm with conversation throughout','Wait outside, so the room feels less crowded'],answer:0,why:'A second person helps memory most when they record. Without a role they tend to join in, which gives company and not much else.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO PEOPLE, ONE ROOM',left:{title:'The patient',items:['Anxious about the news','Attention narrowed by fear','Hears what was feared']},right:{title:'The companion',items:['Not under the same narrowing','Attention free to range','Hears what was said']},note:'The essay\u2019s whole case rests on this difference.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Recall of medical information is poor, and worse under distress, because anxiety narrows attention. A second person, given the job of recording, is likely to improve what is retained.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The evidence, as stated',text:'Studies generally find patients forget a good deal of what they were told. The essay adds a reason: anxiety narrows attention, so the moment you most need to listen is the moment you are least able to. It claims a likely improvement, not a measured one.'},{kind:'term',word:'Retention',meaning:'How much of what was said is still remembered afterwards. The essay\u2019s concern is retention, not comfort, which is why it gives the companion a job.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The second chair is the terracotta one. Two people on the patient\u2019s side of the desk, and the point of the second is to hear.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the roles matter',text:'Without an agreement, the second person tends to participate rather than record: they ask, reassure, react. That supplies company and not much else. The fix is settled before you walk in: one asks, the other writes.'},{kind:'limits',text:'A companion is not always possible, or wanted. Some patients need privacy, and the person who comes may bring their own fear into the room, or answer for the patient. And a note is only as good as what was said: writing down an unclear explanation preserves the unclear version.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these misses the point of bringing someone?',options:['A friend who comes along and keeps the patient chatting throughout','A partner who writes while the patient asks the questions','A sibling who asks the question the patient forgot','A friend who reads the notes back afterwards'],answer:0,why:'Company is pleasant, but the essay\u2019s case is about retention. A companion with no recording role supplies company and not much else.'}]}}}},3007,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'sleep-and-the-next-day_1':{pictureCaption:'The morning after a short night. He moves a little slowly, holding a glass of water, with no remedy anywhere. The day will be fine.',basics:{title:'One bad night is okay',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE MORNING AFTER A SLEEPOVER',text:'You stayed up talking until very late. Now it is morning and your head feels foggy. You start to worry. Is the whole day going to be ruined?'},{kind:'idea',text:'One bad night makes the next day a bit harder. That is all. It is not a big problem, and you do not need a plan to fix it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What one short night does',text:'You may be a bit slower. You may get grumpy more easily. Paying attention takes more effort. These things are real. But they are small, and they fade after a normal night.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'This is the morning after a bad night. He is a bit slow. He has a glass of water, not a cure. The day will be fine.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'After a bad night, you must go to bed really early to catch up.',reality:'You don\u2019t need to. Go to bed at your normal time and get up at your normal time. Your sleep will sort itself out if you let it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why worry can be worse',text:'If you worry about sleep, bedtime gets tense. Then sleep can get harder, and you worry more. So the worry about a bad night can be a big part of the harm.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you sleep badly, go easy on the day. Do the things that matter and skip the extras. Then go to bed at your usual time.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Maya slept badly before a football match. What does this idea say she should do?',options:['Play, expect to be a bit slow, and sleep at her usual time','Skip the match, because a bad night is a big problem','Go to bed at seven tonight to make up for it'],answer:0,why:'One bad night has small effects that fade. Keeping her normal times lets her sleep settle by itself.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A SHORT NIGHT, A BIG DAY',prompt:'You slept badly before an important day. Which is more likely to turn one bad night into several?',options:['The short night itself','Monitoring and worrying about your sleep','Going to bed at your usual time'],answer:1,reveal:'One short night has modest effects that recover. What can carry forward is the interpretation. Monitoring, effort and anxiety about sleeping are widely thought to be part of how occasional bad nights become lasting insomnia.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A single short night has real but modest effects, and they fade if the next nights are normal. The right response is to carry on, because treating it as a problem to solve can cause a good part of the damage.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'What the night does',items:['Slower reactions','Worse mood regulation','More effort for the same focus','Fades after normal nights']},right:{title:'What the worry does',items:['Checking how you slept','Working at falling asleep','Anxiety at bedtime','Can carry into the next night']},note:'The left column is the night. The right column is your response to it, and it is the one that can last.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why fixing it backfires',text:'An early night, a planned nap, a shifted schedule: each treats the night as a problem to be solved. That framing is what produces the monitoring, effort and anxiety about sleep. A bad night left alone is just a bad night.'},{kind:'steps',title:'The morning after',steps:['Expect the day to be somewhat harder, and lower its ambition.','Don\u2019t fight it with extra caffeine.','Skip the compensations: no early night, no nap plan.','Go to bed and get up at your usual times.']},{kind:'picture',caption:'A little slow, a glass of water in hand, no remedy in the frame. This is what carrying on looks like.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A colleague slept four hours and plans a 9 p.m. bedtime, a lunchtime nap and a new sleep-tracking app. What fits the idea?',options:['Lower today\u2019s ambitions and keep their usual times tonight','Keep the plan: a short night needs a full recovery','Skip the early night but keep the nap and the app','Drink extra coffee so the day can go as normal'],answer:0,why:'One short night recovers by itself. The compensations turn it into a project, and that is how the worry builds.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHERE THE ESSAY STARTS',text:'This short Mindroll essay asks what one bad night actually does, and which popular fixes hold up. It begins with the most ordinary case, a single short night, and argues that here the best fix is none at all.'},{kind:'idea',text:'One short night is a normal event with modest, recoverable effects. What can accumulate is the interpretation: treating each poor night as a problem breeds the monitoring and anxiety that may make bad nights last.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the night does, and does not do',text:'Performance after a single short night is somewhat worse: slower reactions, poorer mood regulation, more effort for the same focus. These are real. They are also modest, and they recover if the following nights are normal. One bad night is not a medical event.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW A NIGHT BECOMES A PATTERN',nodes:['One poor night','Treated as a problem to solve','Monitoring, effort, bedtime anxiety','Harder to sleep next time'],loop:!0,note:'The essay puts the risk at the second step, not the first. This route is widely thought to be part of how occasional bad nights turn into lasting insomnia.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the instruction is to do nothing',text:'Every compensation, an early night, a napping strategy, a change of schedule, is a way of treating the night as a problem. So the essay\u2019s advice is deliberately passive: return to the usual timing and let it resolve. Lower the day\u2019s ambition rather than fighting it with caffeine.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'No remedy anywhere in the frame. An ordinary morning, moved through a little slowly, which is the whole of the essay\u2019s advice.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The claim covers one night. Short nights that keep repeating are a different matter, and the essay\u2019s later ideas turn to them. Slower reactions also matter more on some days than others: lowering the ambition should include the risky tasks, like a long drive. And persistent poor sleep is worth taking to a doctor, not waiting out.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE OVERREACH',prompt:'Which reading of this idea goes further than the essay does?',options:['Sleep loss has no real effect, so it can be ignored','One short night lowers performance modestly, then recovers','Worry about a bad night can be a good part of the harm','Keeping usual times beats an early night to compensate'],answer:0,why:'The effects are real, only modest. The essay\u2019s point is about proportion, not denial.'}]}},'sleep-and-the-next-day_2':{pictureCaption:'A tally of lost sleep. The weekend scrubbed out one patch, the old marks still show beneath it, and most of the count is untouched.',bridge:'Idea 1 was about one short night. This one is about many of them, and the weekend plan to pay them back.',basics:{title:'A lie-in doesn\u2019t pay it all back',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'SATURDAY MORNING',prompt:'You went to bed late all week. On Saturday you sleep until eleven. Are you all caught up now?',options:['Yes, all of it','Only part of it'],answer:1,reveal:'A long lie-in does help a bit. You feel more awake. But it does not pay back everything you missed.'},{kind:'idea',text:'You can\u2019t fully pay back lost sleep at the weekend. A lie-in fixes some of it. It also moves your body clock, so Sunday night gets harder.'},{kind:'term',word:'Body clock',meaning:'The timer inside you that makes you sleepy at night and awake in the day. When you wake up much later, it moves later too.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Each mark is lost sleep. The weekend rubbed out one patch. Look closely: the old marks still show, and most are not touched.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE WEEKEND LOOP',nodes:['Late nights all week','A long lie-in on Saturday','Not sleepy on Sunday night','Tired on Monday'],loop:!0,note:'That is why the lie-in feels like it should work, and the week still starts badly.'},{kind:'explain',title:'So what does help?',text:'The real fix is in the school week, not the weekend. If you get to bed on time on school nights, there is less to pay back. A small lie-in is fine. Just don\u2019t expect it to fix the whole week.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Sam slept badly all week. He plans to sleep until noon on Saturday and Sunday. What will most likely happen?',options:['He feels a bit better, but Monday is still hard','He pays back every hour and starts the week fresh','Nothing changes at all, so the lie-in is pointless'],answer:0,why:'A lie-in pays back some sleep, not all. And the big shift in his body clock makes Sunday night and Monday harder.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE FRIDAY PLAN',text:'A heavy week: five short nights. The plan is simple. Sleep until eleven on Saturday, again on Sunday, and start Monday even. At seven on Monday morning, it does not feel even.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Sleep debt is not a balance that a long Saturday clears. Weekend lie-ins restore some things and not others, and a big shift in timing moves your body clock, which makes Monday harder.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'What a lie-in tends to restore',items:['How alert you feel','Some of your performance']},right:{title:'What it leaves',items:['The rest of the deficit','A body clock pushed later','A harder Sunday night']},note:'Studies of weekend recovery sleep generally find partial recovery, not a clean reset.'},{kind:'term',word:'Social jetlag',meaning:'A name sometimes given to the cost of shifting your sleep times a lot at the weekend. Your body clock drifts with the weekend, then has to shift back for Monday.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the pattern keeps itself going',text:'The lie-in feels like repayment because you do wake more alert. Then on Sunday night you are not sleepy at the usual time. You fall asleep late, wake short on Monday, and the week begins in debt again.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One big weekend erasure. Underneath it the old marks still show, and most of the tally was never touched.'},{kind:'try',text:'This week, protect the weeknights instead of planning a weekend rescue. Take a modest lie-in if you need one, but keep Saturday\u2019s wake time close to Monday\u2019s.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A friend says they never worry about short weeknights because they always catch up at the weekend. What is the most accurate reply?',options:['It helps partly but shifts your clock, so weeknights are the real fix','It works fully, as long as the total hours across the week add up','It doesn\u2019t help at all, so weekend lie-ins are a waste of time','It works, as long as you sleep late on both Saturday and Sunday'],answer:0,why:'Recovery sleep restores some things, not all, and the timing shift has its own cost. The schedule you can actually fix is the weekday one.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Sleep is a bank account: miss hours in the week, deposit them on Saturday, and the balance is restored.',reality:'Studies of weekend recovery sleep generally find partial restoration. Alertness and some performance come back; other things do not fully. The deficit is not a simple balance that one long Saturday clears.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Weekend catch-up is partly effective and partly self-defeating. It restores some of the deficit, but a large shift in timing moves the body clock, so the fixable part of the problem is the weekday schedule.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Two effects pulling against each other',text:'The lie-in repays something real, which is why it feels as if it works. But sleeping hours later moves the clock later. On Sunday night you are not tired at the usual time, and Monday starts short. The repair creates part of next week\u2019s debt.'},{kind:'term',word:'Social jetlag',meaning:'A name sometimes used for the mismatch created when weekend sleep times differ a lot from weekday ones. The body clock drifts with the weekend and then has to shift back.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The account the bank-balance view promises to clear. One weekend erasure, the old marks showing through, and most of the count still standing.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where the essay moves the lever',text:'Because the weekend repair is partial and costly, the essay shifts attention to the weekday schedule. The problem worth fixing is the one that creates the debt, not the one that tries to repay it. That sets up the next idea: steady timing.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The essay does not call lie-ins useless. It says to recover what you can and stop expecting the account to settle. Shift workers and new parents may not control their weekdays at all, and for them a weekend lie-in may be the best on offer. The essay also says partial without saying how partial: it gives no figures.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TURN IT INTO ADVICE',prompt:'Someone sleeps short on weeknights and very long at weekends. Which advice follows from the essay?',options:['Work on weeknight sleep, and keep weekend wake times nearer weekday ones','Sleep even longer at weekends to clear the balance that remains','Stop lie-ins entirely, because recovery sleep makes no difference','Change nothing, as long as the hours add up across the week'],answer:0,why:'Weekend recovery is partial and shifts the clock. The weekday schedule is the lever, and a smaller weekend shift limits the Monday cost.'}]}},'sleep-and-the-next-day_3':{pictureCaption:'Saturday: football and weekend clothes on the chair, and the alarm has still gone off. The wake time holds at weekends too.',bridge:'Idea 2 pointed at the weekday schedule. This one says which part of the schedule to fix first.',basics:{title:'Get up at the same time every day',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'MORE IS BETTER?',belief:'The best way to sleep better is always to sleep longer.',reality:'Often the bigger help is getting up at the same time every day. Yes, even on Saturday and Sunday.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Wake up at the same time every day, weekends too. A steady wake-up time keeps your body clock steady. Then it gets easier to fall asleep and stay asleep.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A SCHOOL TIMETABLE',text:'When lunch is at the same time each day, you get hungry just before it. Your body clock likes a set time too. The time you get up is the part that sets it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why start with waking up?',picture:!0,text:'You can\u2019t make yourself fall asleep at a set time. But you can get up at a set time. The light you see in the morning helps set your body clock too.'},{kind:'scale',low:'Wake-up time jumps around',high:'Same wake-up time every day',note:'At the steady end, it is easier to fall asleep. It is easier to stay asleep too.'},{kind:'explain',title:'But don\u2019t stay up late',text:'If you get up at seven but go to bed at midnight, you just get less sleep. A steady time helps. You still need enough hours in bed.'},{kind:'try',text:'Ask a parent to help you pick one wake-up time. Keep it for two weeks, weekends too. Notice whether bedtime gets easier.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Lily gets up at seven on school days and at eleven on Saturdays. What would help her sleep most?',options:['Getting up at about the same time every day','Sleeping until eleven on every day of the week','Staying in bed until noon on Sundays as well'],answer:0,why:'A steady wake-up time keeps her body clock steady. Big jumps at the weekend move it.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'TWO SLEEPERS, SAME HOURS',text:'Two people get about the same amount of sleep. One goes to bed and wakes at the same times every day. The other\u2019s times swing by hours. Studies of sleep regularity generally find the irregular pattern goes with worse outcomes, even allowing for how much sleep is obtained.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Consistent timing appears to matter at least as much as total duration, and it is usually the lever you can actually pull. Fix the wake time first, weekends included, and let bedtime follow.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Duration',items:['Set by work, children, circumstance','Often hard to change','What people usually try first']},right:{title:'Timing',items:['Often more adjustable','Keeps the body clock steady','Its morning end is yours to set']},note:'A steady clock makes the sleep you do get easier to fall into and to keep.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the wake time, not the bedtime',text:'You cannot make yourself fall asleep at ten. You can get up at seven. Wake time, and the morning light that comes with it, helps anchor the body clock. It is the end of the night you actually control.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Weekend kit on the chair, morning light at the window, and a hand switching off the alarm. The end of the night you control, held on a Saturday too.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Two weeks, one change',steps:['Choose a wake time you can keep every day, weekends included.','Get up at it, and let the morning light in.','Let bedtime follow, going to bed when sleepy.','Change nothing else until the two weeks are up.']},{kind:'explain',title:'The honest caveat',text:'A fixed wake time with a late bedtime simply produces short sleep. This is a scheduling instruction. It is not a substitute for enough hours.'},{kind:'keyline',text:'Fix the end of the night you control, and let the other end follow.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'WHAT WENT WRONG?',prompt:'Someone keeps a strict 6.30 wake time but has drifted to 1 a.m. bedtimes and feels worse. What has gone wrong?',options:['The steady wake time is now just producing short sleep','The wake time should have moved later at weekends','Regular timing simply does not work for some people','They need to vary the wake time to reset the clock'],answer:0,why:'Consistency is a scheduling rule, not a replacement for enough sleep. Bedtime has to follow the wake time.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'ONE LEVER, TWO WEEKS',prompt:'You can change one thing about your sleep for two weeks. Which does the essay back first?',options:['Sleeping an hour longer','Waking at a fixed time every day','A new bedtime routine'],answer:1,reveal:'Duration is often fixed by work, children and circumstances that do not negotiate. Timing is usually more adjustable, and the wake end most of all.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Consistency of timing appears to matter at least as much as total duration. Fixing the wake time, weekends included, steadies the body clock and is usually the more available lever.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The evidence, as the essay states it',text:'Studies of sleep regularity generally find that irregular schedules go with worse outcomes, even allowing for how much sleep people get. Notice the wording. Irregularity goes with worse outcomes, and regularity appears to matter at least as much. That is a careful claim, not a proof that one causes the other.'},{kind:'term',word:'Body clock',meaning:'The internal timing that makes you sleepy and alert at roughly the same hours each day. In the essay\u2019s account, a regular wake time and the morning light that comes with it help anchor it.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHY THE WAKE END',nodes:['A fixed wake time','Morning light at a steady hour','A steadier body clock','Sleep easier to start and keep'],note:'Bedtime is not fixed directly. It is left to follow.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A fixed wake time, weekends included: the football is on the chair, the light is up, and the alarm has still gone off. Bedtime is not in the picture, because it is left to follow.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where it fits the argument',text:'Idea 2 found the weekend repair partial and costly. This is the alternative it pointed to: stop creating the swing in the first place. A fixed weekend wake time is the same advice seen from the other side.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The essay names its own caveat. A fixed wake time with a late bedtime simply produces short sleep, so this is a scheduling rule, not a substitute for enough sleep. Some lives cannot hold a fixed wake time at all, such as rotating shifts or a newborn. And the evidence is stated as appearing to matter, not as settled.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'Someone objects that waking early on Sunday will just make them sleep less. What is the best reply?',options:['It can, unless bedtime follows, so let bedtime move with the wake time','No, a fixed wake time always adds to your total sleep','True, so regular timing only suits people who sleep easily','Sleep less, then, since timing matters far more than hours'],answer:0,why:'The objection is the essay\u2019s own caveat. Regular timing is a scheduling instruction and works only alongside enough time in bed.'}]}},'sleep-and-the-next-day_4':{pictureCaption:'The bed is empty, covers turned back. Next door, one low lamp is on over something dull. The wakeful hour has left the bedroom.',bridge:'Ideas 2 and 3 were about when you sleep. This one is about what the bed itself learns when sleep will not come.',basics:{title:'Can\u2019t sleep? Get up for a bit',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'STILL AWAKE',text:'It is late. You have been lying in bed for ages. You turn over. You check the time. You turn over again. Your brain just will not switch off.'},{kind:'idea',text:'If you lie awake in bed for a long time, your brain starts to link bed with being awake. So get up, do something boring in dim light, and come back when you feel sleepy.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A DOG AND ITS LEAD',text:'A dog sees its lead and gets excited, because the lead always means a walk. Your bed can work the same way. If bed keeps meaning lying awake and cross, bed starts to mean awake.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The bed is empty. Next door, one small lamp is on. Someone got up to do something dull, and will come back when sleepy.'},{kind:'steps',title:'If you can\u2019t sleep',steps:['Get out of bed.','Go somewhere with a dim light.','Do something boring, like a dull book.','Go back to bed when you feel sleepy.'],note:'If this keeps happening night after night, tell a parent.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why boring?',text:'Something fun or exciting would keep you awake. Something dull gives sleepiness room to come back. And your bed stays a place for sleep, not for lying there cross.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Ben has been lying awake for an hour and is getting cross. What does this idea say he should do?',options:['Get up, read something dull in dim light, then go back','Stay in bed and try much harder to fall asleep','Play a game on his tablet until he feels tired'],answer:0,why:'Lying in bed awake teaches his brain that bed means awake. A dull, dim break lets sleepiness come back.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE USUAL INSTINCT',belief:'If you can\u2019t sleep, at least stay in bed. Lying there is still resting.',reality:'Lying there awake is teaching you something. The bed becomes a cue for whatever you reliably do in it, and an hour of frustrated wakefulness is a very effective lesson.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Staying in bed awake trains an association between the bed and alertness. After a long stretch awake, get up, do something dull in low light, and return when sleepy.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE BED LEARNS',nodes:['Lying awake in bed','Frustration, clock-checking, effort','Bed paired with being alert','Getting into bed wakes you'],loop:!0,note:'Each round repeats the lesson.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it works this way',text:'This is simple conditioning, the same learning that makes a familiar smell from the kitchen make you hungry. Your brain links a place with what usually happens there. Spend enough nights fretting in bed and the bed starts to call up fretting.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Empty bed, covers back. One low lamp next door over something dull. The wakeful hour has been moved somewhere else.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Unappealing, and better supported',text:'Nobody wants to leave a warm bed at three in the morning, which is why this advice gets skipped. It is also better supported than most of what is sold alongside it.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you have been awake a long while, get up. Choose something dull, such as folding laundry, under one low lamp. Go back only when sleepy.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Someone often lies awake for hours and now reads work email in bed to use the time. What does the idea suggest?',options:['Get up and do something dull elsewhere, so the bed stops meaning alert','Carry on, since the time would otherwise be wasted lying there','Stay in bed with eyes closed and try harder to drop off','Keep reading in bed, but stop the email by midnight'],answer:0,why:'Work email in bed is more alertness paired with the bed. The point is to move the wakeful time out of it.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'THE MECHANISM, NAMED',word:'Conditioning',meaning:'Learning by pairing. When a place or signal is repeatedly followed by the same state, it comes to call up that state by itself. The essay says the bed is a cue of exactly this kind.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The bed becomes a cue for whatever you reliably do in it. Lie awake there often enough and it cues alertness, so after a long stretch awake, get up, do something dull in low light, and return when sleepy.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where it sits in the essay',text:'The essay is sorting popular fixes into those that hold up and those that do not. It rates this one as unappealing and better supported than most of what is sold alongside it. Its cost is plain: it asks you to leave a warm bed exactly when you least want to.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Stay and fight it',items:['Bed paired with frustration','Bed paired with effort','Lying down starts to wake you']},right:{title:'Get up and wait',items:['Wakefulness happens elsewhere','Bed kept for sleepiness','Return only when sleepy']},note:'The same hour awake. What changes is where it is spent, and so what the bed is taught.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why something dull',text:'The point is not to make good use of the time. Something absorbing would give the wakefulness a reward. The instruction is deliberately plain: a dull task, in low light, until sleepiness returns on its own.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The wakeful hour relocated: an empty bed, covers back, and one low lamp next door over something undemanding.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The essay gives no clock time for a long stretch, and watching the clock to decide can itself feed alertness. A pairing is learned over many nights, so one night of getting up proves little. And the advice is for ordinary wakeful nights. Insomnia that persists is worth taking to a doctor rather than managing alone.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these gets the instruction wrong?',options:['Getting up to clear emails under bright light until tired','Reading a dull book in a dim room, then going back','Leaving the bed after a long stretch lying awake','Keeping the bed for sleep rather than for worrying'],answer:0,why:'Getting up is right, but an engaging task rewards being awake, and bright light is the opposite of what the instruction asks. It says dull and low-lit.'}]}},'sleep-and-the-next-day_5':{pictureCaption:'Bottles on a shelf by the door, ignored. Through it, the room itself: its light, its window, its warmth. That is where to look first.',bridge:'Idea 4 moved the wakeful hour out of the bed. This one asks where the causes of a bad night really sit.',basics:{title:'Look at the hour before bed first',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'WHAT CHLOE WANTS',text:'Chloe sleeps badly. She wants to buy a sleep spray and a sleep app. But every night she plays a bright, exciting game right up until bedtime.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Before you buy anything to help you sleep, look at the hour before bed. How bright it is, how warm the room is, and what you are doing then probably matter more.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The bottles sit on a shelf by the door. Through the door is the room itself, with its light and its window. Look there first.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Things you buy',items:['Sleep sprays and pills','Sleep apps and trackers']},right:{title:'The hour before bed',items:['How bright it is','How warm the room is','What you are doing']},note:'The right side costs nothing to try.'},{kind:'explain',title:'But the whole day counts too',text:'Sleep is not only about the last hour. Getting up at the same time helps. So does daylight in the morning. So does no caffeine late in the day. Caffeine is in coffee, tea and some fizzy drinks.'},{kind:'try',text:'Tonight, look at your last hour before bed. Is it bright? Is the room too warm? Are you doing something exciting? Change one thing.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Josh can\u2019t sleep and wants a sleep gadget. He drinks cola with dinner and plays loud games until bed. What should he try first?',options:['Change his evening and his drinks, which cost nothing','Buy the gadget, since it is made for helping sleep','Go to bed two hours earlier than usual tonight'],answer:0,why:'The free things, like light, drinks and what he does before bed, are the likely place to look first.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A PERFECT HOUR, A BAD NIGHT',prompt:'Someone has a careful wind-down hour: dim lights, a quiet room, a book. They still sleep badly. Where should they look next?',options:['A better supplement','The rest of their day','A longer wind-down'],answer:1,reveal:'The firmest support is for things spread across the day: consistent timing, morning daylight, when caffeine is drunk, and enough time in bed at all. The last hour is where the problem is felt, not always where it starts.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The hour before bed is a better place to look than anything you can take or track. It is still not the whole story: the causes of a bad night are often spread across the day.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Where people look',items:['Supplements and trackers','The last sixty minutes']},right:{title:'Where causes often sit',items:['Consistent timing','Morning daylight','When caffeine is drunk','Enough time in bed']},note:'The left column is where the problem is felt. The right is spread across the sixteen hours before it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the last hour gets the attention',text:'It is where you feel the problem: lying there, not sleepy. So it is where you look for the solution. The causes are often in the hours before, and those are not felt at all.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'COFFEE AT FOUR',text:'Picture sixty careful minutes before bed, and a coffee at four in the afternoon. The routine is polishing the visible part of a problem located somewhere else. That is why a routine on its own can fail to help.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The bottles by the door, ignored. Beyond it, the room: its light, its window, its warmth. Cheaper to change, and a better first look.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before buying anything for sleep, change one thing in your last hour, and check one thing earlier in the day: when was your last coffee?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A friend sleeps badly. They have bought a tracker and a sleep supplement, and drink coffee until five. Which suggestion best fits the idea?',options:['Move the coffee earlier and look at the evening before buying more','Add a second tracker to check the first one\u2019s readings','Keep the supplement, since that is where the evidence is','Focus on the last hour before bed and nothing else'],answer:0,why:'Free changes to the day and the evening come before anything bought. The last hour alone would miss a cause sitting at five o\u2019clock.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE ESSAY\u2019S LAST TURN',title:'Two claims at once',text:'The final idea makes two claims that pull in different directions. First, the hour before bed is a better place to look than anything taken or tracked. Second, it is still not the whole story, and treating it as the whole story is its own mistake.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Light, temperature and what you do in the last hour likely matter more than anything taken or tracked. But the firmest support is for factors spread across the day, so a perfect final hour can still fail.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHY ADVICE GATHERS AT BEDTIME',nodes:['The problem is felt at bedtime','So the fix is sought there','Earlier causes go unfelt','The visible part gets optimised'],note:'This is the essay\u2019s explanation for why sleep advice clusters in the last sixty minutes.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'The final hour',items:['Light','Temperature','What you are doing']},right:{title:'Spread across the day',items:['Consistent timing','Morning daylight','Caffeine timing','Enough time in bed']},note:'The essay says the right-hand list has the firmest support. Both beat the supplement aisle.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE FOUR O\u2019CLOCK COFFEE',text:'Someone does sixty flawless minutes before bed and drinks coffee at four. By the essay\u2019s account they are optimising the visible part of a problem located somewhere else, which is why the routine on its own can fail.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Supplements waiting by the door. The room beyond is the part of the problem you can see. The essay asks you to look further back as well.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The essay ranks without measuring. It says likely and firmest, not by how much, and gives no figures for any factor. It argues against supplements in general terms and assesses no particular one. Some people sleep badly for medical reasons no routine will reach. Its claim is about where to look first, not where to stop.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE PATTERN',prompt:'Which plan would the essay call optimising the visible part of the problem?',options:['A ninety-minute wind-down for someone who sleeps until noon at weekends','Fixing a wake time before trying any kind of supplement','Moving the afternoon coffee to earlier in the day','Getting daylight soon after waking every morning'],answer:0,why:'The wind-down is aimed where the problem is felt. The irregular weekend timing, a cause spread across the week, is left untouched.'}]}}}},3008,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'small-talk-is-not-small_1':{pictureCaption:'A brief handshake on the threshold, with the real doorway open behind. The greeting is not the room. It is how you get let in.',basics:{title:'Chit-chat is a check, not a waste',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'AT THE SCHOOL GATE',text:'Your friend\u2019s mum says, \u201cHi! Cold today, isn\u2019t it?\u201d You don\u2019t care about the weather. Neither does she. So why does everyone say this stuff?'},{kind:'explain',title:'What is really going on',text:'The weather is not the point. The chat is a quick check. Are you both friendly? Will you both be polite? Once you know that, you can talk about real things.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Small talk, like chat about the weather, is a safety check. It shows you are both friendly. That makes a real talk possible next.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A KNOCK ON A DOOR',text:'You don\u2019t just walk into someone\u2019s room. You knock first. The knock says nothing at all. But it makes it okay to come in.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A quick handshake at the door. Nobody has gone in yet. The handshake is what lets them in.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time someone says, \u201cNice day!\u201d, answer properly. Smile and say something back, like \u201cIt is! Have you been out?\u201d You are passing the check.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A new girl sits next to you and says, \u201cThis room is so hot.\u201d What is she really doing?',options:['Checking you are friendly, so you can chat','Asking you to open the window right now','Telling you she doesn\u2019t like the school'],answer:0,why:'The heat is not the point. She is checking you are friendly. Answer kindly and the real chat can start.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE USUAL COMPLAINT',belief:'Small talk is a poor substitute for real conversation, and a waste of everyone\u2019s time.',reality:'It has a different function. Mistaking it for a bad version of real conversation is exactly why people find it unbearable.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Small talk establishes safety: that both people will observe the rules, respond appropriately and not impose. The content is low-stakes on purpose, because the exchange itself is the test.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why skipping it lands badly',text:'Opening with something substantive asks for disclosure before the check has run. The other person has no basis yet for reciprocating, so they hold back. Skipping the weather does not save time. It removes the step that makes the next conversation possible.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE NEW DESK MATE',text:'On your first day you skip the pleasantries and ask the person next to you what they really think of the manager. They answer in a word. Two minutes on the commute and the coffee machine would have told them you were safe to be candid with.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A brief, formal handshake on the threshold, the real doorway open behind it. The greeting is the check, not the room.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time someone opens with the weather, answer it properly and add one small thing back. You are passing the check, not wasting time.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A neighbour you barely know stops to chat about the rain. You want to ask them about the shared fence. What is the best move?',options:['Chat about the rain for a minute, then raise the fence','Skip the rain and ask about the fence straight away','Talk about the rain and leave the fence for another day','Mention the fence first, then politely ask about the rain'],answer:0,why:'The rain is the check. Run it briefly, and the fence question arrives in a conversation that already feels safe.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHAT THE ESSAY DEFENDS',title:'The dismissed conversation',text:'This short essay is about the conversations we dismiss, and why the people good at them are not performing. Its first move is to reclassify small talk. It is not a weak form of real conversation. It is a different thing with a different job.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Small talk is a protocol for establishing that a conversation is possible. Its triviality is a feature: low-stakes content lets both sides test the rules without risking anything.'},{kind:'term',word:'Protocol',meaning:'An agreed sequence of steps that must run before an exchange can go ahead. What is said matters less than that each step is answered properly.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO KINDS OF TALK',left:{title:'Small talk',items:['Tests whether a conversation is possible','Content deliberately trivial','Takes a minute or two']},right:{title:'Substantive talk',items:['Exchanges what people think and feel','Content is the point','Needs the test passed first']}},{kind:'explain',title:'What goes wrong without it',text:'Open with something substantive and you ask for disclosure before the check has been run. The other party has no basis for reciprocating. The essay\u2019s claim is that this, not the weather, is what fails: the substantive conversation never properly starts.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The handshake happens on the threshold, not in the room. It is brief and formal because its only job is to let both people through.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The check is cultural. What counts as a proper opening, and how long it runs, varies between places and people, and some find the ritual itself a barrier. It can also become a place to hide: a relationship that never gets past the weather has passed the test and stopped. The check is a doorway, not a destination.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'Which of these works most like small talk, in the essay\u2019s sense?',options:['A pilot and a controller exchanging routine call signs first','A lecture that opens directly with the speaker\u2019s main argument','A survey that asks every respondent exactly the same questions','A comedian trying out new jokes on a small audience'],answer:0,why:'The call signs carry almost no content. They confirm both sides are present and following the rules, so the real exchange can begin.'}]}},'small-talk-is-not-small_2':{pictureCaption:'The ball has come over, and the hands are open for it, not turned away. A second question keeps their answer in play.',bridge:'Idea 1 explained the opening. This is what marks out the people who are good at what comes after it: the second question.',basics:{title:'Ask one more question',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'AFTER THE HOLIDAYS',prompt:'Your friend says, \u201cI went to the seaside.\u201d Which reply would make her happiest?',options:['\u201cCool. I went to my nan\u2019s.\u201d','\u201cCool! What did you do there?\u201d','\u201cOh, nice.\u201d'],answer:1,reveal:'The first reply turns the talk to you. The last one ends it. The middle one asks about *her* answer. It shows you were really listening, and want to hear more.'},{kind:'idea',text:'People who are good to talk to ask a second question. They ask about the answer they just heard, before they talk about themselves.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the second one counts',text:'Anyone can ask a first question without listening. But you can\u2019t ask a second one unless you heard the answer. So it shows you care what they said.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The ball has just been passed. The hands are open, ready for it, not turned away. That is what a second question does.'},{kind:'steps',title:'How to do it',steps:['Ask a question.','Listen to the whole answer.','Pick the last thing they said.','Ask about that.'],note:'No clever words needed. Just listening.'},{kind:'try',text:'Today, when someone tells you something, ask one more question about it before you say anything about you.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend says, \u201cI got a puppy at the weekend.\u201d Which reply is a second question?',options:['\u201cWhat\u2019s the puppy called?\u201d','\u201cI want a dog too.\u201d','\u201cWhat did you have for lunch?\u201d'],answer:0,why:'It asks about what she just said. The others change the subject.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'WAITING FOR YOUR TURN',text:'Someone at a party tells you they have just spent a month in Portugal. You say *lovely*, and then, because you have been holding it ready, you mention your own trip. The conversation stays polite and goes nowhere.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The single reliable marker of someone good at conversation is the follow-up: asking about the answer they just got, rather than moving on to the next topic or to themselves.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it lands',text:'A first question is a formality and can be asked without listening. A second cannot. It shows the answer was heard and found worth pursuing. Most people are braced for the exchange to stay formal, and the follow-up signals that it need not.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Waiting for your turn',items:['Half-hear the answer','Prepare your own story','Move to the next topic']},right:{title:'Following up',items:['Hear the whole answer','Pick the last specific thing','Ask about it']},note:'It costs nothing, and it is rare enough to be memorable.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The ball has come over and the hands are open for it, not turned back to their own side. That is the follow-up.'},{kind:'try',text:'In your next conversation, take the last specific thing the other person said and ask about it, before you say anything about yourself.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A colleague mentions they spent the weekend repainting their kitchen. Which reply is the follow-up the essay means?',options:['\u201cWhat colour did you go for in the end?\u201d','\u201cWe redid ours last year. A nightmare.\u201d','\u201cNice. Busy week ahead for you?\u201d','\u201cPainting is so satisfying, isn\u2019t it?\u201d'],answer:0,why:'It asks about their answer, specifically. The others swap in your own story, change the topic, or offer an opinion.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO QUESTIONS, TWO JOBS',left:{title:'The first question',items:['A formality','Can be asked without listening','Keeps things formal']},right:{title:'The second question',items:['Shows the answer was heard','Cannot be asked without listening','Signals it need not stay formal']},note:'The essay calls the second the single reliable marker of someone good at conversation.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Follow up on the answer rather than moving on. The second question cannot be asked without listening, which is exactly why it counts as evidence of listening.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the effect is disproportionate',text:'Most people enter a conversation braced for it to stay formal. The follow-up breaks that expectation at once. The cost is nothing and the signal is large, because it is rare, and because it is hard to produce without the attention it advertises.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where the difficulty really is',text:'The practical version needs no skill: take the last specific thing they said and ask about it. The difficulty is entirely in listening to the answer rather than preparing your own contribution while it is being given. It is a problem of attention, not of wit.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Open hands reaching for the ball rather than turning back to their own side. The second question returns the turn.'},{kind:'limits',text:'A follow-up can be counterfeited: a stock *tell me more*, asked while still thinking about yourself. It can become an interrogation if it never stops, leaving the other person doing all the disclosing. And some answers are short because the person wants to stop there. Following up on those is pressing, not listening.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'In your last conversation, what was the last specific thing the other person said? Could you have asked about it?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE COUNTERFEIT',prompt:'Which of these is a follow-up in name only?',options:['\u201cInteresting! Anyway, as I was saying about my week\u2026\u201d','\u201cHow did your sister take the news in the end?\u201d','\u201cWhat made you choose that course over the other?\u201d','\u201cYou said the move was hard. Which part was hardest?\u201d'],answer:0,why:'It acknowledges the answer and goes straight back to you. The others could only be asked by someone who had heard it.'}]}},'small-talk-is-not-small_3':{pictureCaption:'Monday, and the goggles in the bag are still damp. The question to ask is about the race, not the weekend.',bridge:'Idea 2 followed up within one conversation. This one follows up across conversations: remember the specific thing, and ask about it next time.',basics:{title:'Ask about the thing they told you',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'MONDAY MORNING',text:'Last week, Leo told you his gran was coming to stay. Today you could ask, \u201cGood weekend?\u201d He will say, \u201cFine.\u201d Or you could ask, \u201cDid your gran come?\u201d Now he has a real answer to give.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A question about something they told you before works better than a general one. It shows you remembered. And it is easy to answer.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Anyone could ask',items:['Good weekend?','How are you?','What\u2019s new?']},right:{title:'Only you could ask',items:['Did your gran come?','How was the match?','Has the puppy settled in?']},note:'The right side only works if you listened last time.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The goggles are still wet from Saturday. You know about the race, so ask about the race.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'To be good to talk to, you need to ask clever, interesting questions.',reality:'Big questions like *what would you do with a million pounds?* are hard to answer on the spot. People often just shrug. A small question about their real life is easier, and gets more back.'},{kind:'explain',title:'It\u2019s about remembering',text:'This is not about being funny or cool. It is about remembering one thing a person said. Anyone can do that if they try.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of a friend. What did they tell you about last time you talked? Ask them about it next time you see them.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend said last week she had a big swimming race on Saturday. What is the best thing to ask her on Monday?',options:['\u201cHow did your race go?\u201d','\u201cDid you have a good weekend?\u201d','\u201cWhat is your favourite sport?\u201d'],answer:0,why:'It is about her real life, and it shows you remembered. The others could be asked of anyone.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THREE OPENERS',prompt:'Which question gets the fullest answer from a colleague on Monday?',options:['\u201cIf you could live anywhere, where would it be?\u201d','\u201cDid you get the flat sorted on Saturday?\u201d','\u201cHow was your weekend?\u201d'],answer:1,reveal:'The big question demands a performance. Most people produce something bland. *How was your weekend* gets *fine.* The specific one is easy to answer at once. It proves you were listening last time.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Specific questions outperform interesting ones. A question about someone\u2019s actual life is easy to answer. It produces real information and shows attention was already paid.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Damp goggles on a Monday bench. *How was your weekend?* gets *fine.* *How did the race go?* gets the story, and shows you listened.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why interesting questions misfire',text:'A question like *what is your philosophy of work?* arrives without warning and asks for a performance. Most people handle that with something bland. The question was meant to open them up. It made them reach for a safe answer.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why specific ones work',text:'A question about what they are working on this week, how the move went or what the new team is like can be answered at once. It could only be asked of this person. That is what makes a conversation feel personal rather than procedural.'},{kind:'keyline',text:'Specificity is a memory problem, not a charisma problem.'},{kind:'try',text:'After a conversation, note one specific thing the other person mentioned. Next time, open with it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You are meeting an old friend you see twice a year. Which opener fits the idea best?',options:['\u201cLast time you were thinking of changing jobs. Did you?\u201d','\u201cSo, what\u2019s the most interesting thing you\u2019ve done lately?\u201d','\u201cWhat\u2019s your big plan for the next five years, then?\u201d','\u201cHow have you been? It\u2019s been far too long.\u201d'],answer:0,why:'It is about their actual circumstances, and only someone who listened could ask it. The others demand a performance or stay a formality.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE CHARISMA THEORY',belief:'Good conversationalists have a talent for fascinating questions.',reality:'The essay argues the opposite. Specific questions beat interesting ones, and specificity is a memory problem rather than a charisma one.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A specific question about someone\u2019s actual circumstances beats a clever general one, because it is answerable at once and proves attention has already been paid.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'FROM ANYONE TO THIS PERSON',low:'A question anyone could ask anyone',high:'A question only you could ask them',note:'*How was your weekend?* sits at the left. *Did you get to the thing you mentioned?* sits at the right. The essay\u2019s claim is that what works happens towards the right.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The goggles are the specific thing. A finger on them is the question only you could ask, because you were listening when the race came up.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Two ways the general question fails',text:'The bland version, *how was your weekend*, is too open to answer usefully and gets nothing. The interesting version demands a performance and arrives without warning, so people produce something generic. Both fail for the same reason: neither is about this person.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the specific question carries',text:'It produces real information: the move, the new team, this week\u2019s work. And it carries proof. Nobody could ask it without having listened last time, which is what makes the exchange personal rather than procedural.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Specificity can overstep. Asking about a medical appointment or a family crisis mentioned in passing may raise more than someone wants raised, especially in company. Remembered details can feel like surveillance when the relationship is thin. And some people enjoy the big question; the claim is about what usually works, not a ban.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Who will you speak to next? What specific thing did they tell you last time?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HARD CASE',prompt:'A colleague mentioned last month that a parent was ill. You meet them by the lifts with others around. What fits the idea best?',options:['Ask quietly how their parent is, or wait for a private moment','Ask the whole group what they all did at the weekend','Ask loudly about the parent, to show that you remembered','Avoid the subject for good, since it is far too personal'],answer:0,why:'Specificity shows you listened, but the point is the person, not the proof. Choosing the moment keeps a specific question from becoming an intrusion.'}]}},'small-talk-is-not-small_4':{pictureCaption:'One side of the table is covered in things they were invited to lay out. The listener\u2019s side holds a single cup, untouched.',bridge:'Ideas 2 and 3 were about how to ask. This one explains why asking works so well: being interested is what reads as interesting.',basics:{title:'Be interested, and people enjoy you',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE WORRY',belief:'To be fun to talk to, you need lots of cool stories.',reality:'Think of the people you love talking to. Mostly, they ask you things. You leave feeling good, even if they hardly talked about themselves.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Being interested in someone makes them enjoy talking to you. You don\u2019t need great stories. You need good questions.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One person has laid out all their things to show. The other has just a cup, and is listening. The one showing will leave happy.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why does it work?',text:'Most people like talking about themselves. It feels good. And it is rare for someone to really listen. So when you listen, you are giving them something nice.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A GOOD CROWD',text:'A school show is more fun with a good crowd. The crowd doesn\u2019t tell the jokes. But they laugh and lean in, and the people on stage get better.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before you see a friend, think of two questions to ask them, not two things to tell them.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You are nervous about meeting your new class. What does this idea say will help most?',options:['Plan some questions to ask the others','Plan some funny stories to tell them','Stay quiet until someone talks to you'],answer:0,why:'People enjoy talking with someone who is interested in them. Questions do more than stories.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'ask',kicker:'BEFORE YOU READ ON',prompt:'Think of the person you most enjoy talking to. In your last conversation with them, who did most of the talking?'},{kind:'idea',text:'Being interested is what reads as being interesting. The reputation for good company mostly goes to the people asking questions, not to the ones with the best material.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the research suggests',text:'The essay notes that studies of conversation generally find a pattern. People who ask more questions, and follow-up questions especially, are liked better by the person they talked to. It calls the pattern strange and fairly dependable, not a law.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHY ASKING WORKS',nodes:['You ask, and follow up','They talk about themselves','That is rewarding; attention is scarce','They leave liking the conversation'],note:'The essay offers this reason as probable, not proven.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One side of the table is covered in what they were invited to lay out. The listener\u2019s side is almost bare. Guess who enjoyed it.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before your next social event, prepare three questions instead of three stories.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You are going to a work dinner. You worry you have nothing interesting to say. What does the idea suggest?',options:['Prepare questions; your material matters less than you fear','Prepare a few strong anecdotes so you never run dry','Say little, since quiet people are more often liked','Talk about your work, since that is what people expect'],answer:0,why:'The anxiety is aimed at the wrong variable. Interest, shown through questions, is what reads as interesting. Silence on its own does not.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'AFTER THE DINNER',prompt:'Two guests leave a dinner. One talked mostly about themselves; the other mostly asked questions. Which is likelier to say the conversation went well?',options:['The one who talked','The one who asked','Both equally'],answer:0,reveal:'The essay\u2019s point is that the talker will generally report a good conversation, while the asker is the one remembered as good company. The listener supplies the enjoyment and receives the credit.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Being interested reads as being interesting. Asking more questions, especially follow-ups, tends to make the other person like you more, so preparation is better spent on questions than on material.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The evidence, as the essay states it',text:'Studies of conversation, the essay says, generally find that people who ask more questions, and follow-up questions in particular, are liked better by their partner. It calls the asymmetry strange and fairly dependable, and claims no more than that.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Two probable mechanisms',text:'First, talking about yourself is rewarding, so whoever invites it supplies a pleasure. Second, attention is scarce, so whoever gives it provides something valuable regardless of what else they contribute. The essay offers these as probable and partial, not settled.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One side covered, the other almost bare. The bare side did the work of the evening.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The asymmetry can be gamed. Questions asked as a technique, to be liked rather than to learn, can slide into interviewing, and a conversation where one side never discloses is not quite mutual. The pattern is about being liked, not being known. A friendship built only on your questions leaves the other person knowing little of you.'},{kind:'recap',points:['People generally like those who ask, and follow up, more.','Talking about yourself is rewarding; attention is scarce.','Prepare questions, not material, but do not stop disclosing.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HONEST OBJECTION',prompt:'Which objection does the idea need to take seriously?',options:['Someone who only asks may be liked but never really known','People generally dislike being asked questions by strangers','Good stories matter more than questions in every setting','Attention is plentiful, so offering it is worth very little'],answer:0,why:'The pattern is about liking. A relationship also needs disclosure from both sides, which questions alone do not supply.'}]}},'small-talk-is-not-small_5':{pictureCaption:'Both hands on the door, closing it with care rather than letting it swing. That is what a deliberate ending looks like.',bridge:'Ideas 1 to 4 were about starting and keeping a conversation going. The last is about leaving it, where most people put no effort at all.',basics:{title:'End a chat on purpose',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE CHAT THAT FIZZLED',text:'You are chatting with a friend at the park. It was fun. Then you both run out of things to say. It goes quiet. Someone mumbles, \u201cWell\u2026\u201d and you drift off. Later, the whole chat feels a bit flat.'},{kind:'idea',text:'How a chat ends matters a lot. Ending on purpose, with one kind, clear line, tends to make the whole chat feel better when you remember it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Both hands on the door, closing it gently. Not slammed, not left to swing. That is a good ending.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Fizzling out',items:['You run out of things to say','Awkward quiet','Someone just slips away']},right:{title:'Ending on purpose',items:['Stop while it is still fun','Say what you liked','Say what happens next']}},{kind:'example',kicker:'ONE GOOD LINE',text:'Try this: *I\u2019ve got to go, but that was fun. Tell me how your match goes!* One line. It says you liked it. And it gives you something to talk about next time.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Stop while it\u2019s fun',text:'It sounds odd, but it is good to stop while there is still more to say. Then next time, you can just pick up where you left off.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You and a new friend have been chatting at lunch, and the bell is about to ring. What is the best way to end?',options:['\u201cGot to go! Tell me how your test goes.\u201d','Just walk off when the bell goes','Keep talking until you run out of things'],answer:0,why:'It ends on purpose, shows you care, and sets up the next chat.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'TWO ENDINGS',text:'Twenty good minutes with someone at a conference. Version one: the topics thin, you both glance at your phones, and one of you escapes to the coffee. Version two: you say you should let them get on, name the point of theirs you found useful, and promise to send the article. Same twenty minutes. Different memory.'},{kind:'idea',text:'How a conversation ends shapes how it is remembered, often more than how it began. Most people put all their effort into the opening and none into the exit.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the drift does',text:'In a drift, both people run out of material, the exchange thins, and someone escapes. That ending then attaches itself to the whole interaction. Twenty good minutes can be remembered as awkward.'},{kind:'steps',title:'A deliberate close',steps:['Name it: say you should let them get on.','Say specifically what you enjoyed or took from it.','Where it fits, propose the next contact.'],note:'One concrete sentence can do all three.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Both hands on the door, closing it with care rather than letting it swing. The exit, done on purpose.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The timing',text:'The counterintuitive part is to end while there is still energy. That leaves both of you with an unfinished conversation, a better state to leave someone in than a completed one, and it makes the next one easy to start.'},{kind:'try',text:'End your next conversation with one concrete sentence: what you took from it, or what happens next.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A coffee with a new acquaintance is going well, and you have ten minutes before you must leave. When does the idea say to wrap up?',options:['Soon, while it is still lively, with one clear line','At the last minute, to get the most time in','When the topics run out, so nothing is cut short','Whenever they start to look ready to go'],answer:0,why:'Ending with energy left leaves an unfinished conversation, which makes the next one easy. Waiting for the drift lets the ending colour the whole thing.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'scale',kicker:'HOW CONVERSATIONS END',low:'The drift: material runs out, someone escapes',high:'The close: named, specific, with energy left',note:'The essay\u2019s claim is that where an ending falls on this line colours the memory of everything before it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Endings shape memory, often more than openings. Close deliberately, before the energy runs out: say you should let them get on, say what you took from it, and leave with energy still in the room.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The essay\u2019s argument',text:'Most effort goes into openings and none into exits. The common failure is the drift, and its ending attaches to the whole interaction after the fact. A deliberate close avoids it: naming that you should let them get on, saying specifically what you enjoyed, and where appropriate proposing a next contact.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The counterintuitive timing',text:'End while there is still energy. A finished conversation has nowhere to go. An unfinished one leaves both people with something pending, which the essay calls a far better state to leave someone in. It also loops back to idea 1: a good ending makes the next opening easy.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two hands closing a door with care. The last movement of an exchange is the one the whole exchange is remembered by.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The essay states this as a tendency and gives no measure of how much endings outweigh openings. A close that is too polished can feel like a dismissal, and a promised follow-up that never happens does more harm than none. Ending early has a cost too: some conversations only reach what matters once the easy material is used up.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of a conversation you remember as awkward. Was it awkward throughout, or only at the end?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which ending misuses the idea?',options:['Promising to send an article you have no intention of sending','Saying what you enjoyed, then letting them get on with their day','Leaving while the talk is lively, with a plan to continue it','Naming one thing you took from it as you say goodbye'],answer:0,why:'A deliberate close is only worth something if it is true. An empty promise turns it into the kind of ending people remember badly.'}]}}}},3009,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'souls-of-black-folk_1':{pictureCaption:'A man at a lit shop window. The figure in the glass is not quite him: it is him as other eyes see him.',basics:{title:'Seeing yourself twice',beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'THE UNITED STATES, 1903',text:'W. E. B. Du Bois was a Black writer and scholar. In 1903 he wrote a book called *The Souls of Black Folk*. Slavery had ended less than forty years before. But Black people were still treated as less than white people, by laws and by many white people.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When people look down on you all the time, you start to see yourself twice: as you are, and as they see you. Du Bois called this *double consciousness*.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What it is like',text:'Think of a Black girl in 1903 who loves to read. She knows she is clever. She also knows many white people think she is not. So part of her mind keeps checking: how do they see me now?'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A HOSTILE CROWD',text:'Think of a game where the crowd wants you to lose. You play, but you hear them too. Soon you watch yourself the way they do. Now think of a crowd that never goes home.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A man looks into a shop window. The figure in the glass is not quite him. It is him as other people see him.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why a name helps',text:'Millions of people had lived with this. Few had put it into words. Du Bois gave it a name. A name helps you spot the second view, and that is the first step to setting it aside.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'In 1903, a Black student gives a talk at her college. What does double consciousness add to her task?',options:['Watching herself through the eyes of people who expect her to fail','Making sure that the talk is not too long for the room','Picking which of her friends to invite along to hear it'],answer:0,why:'It is a second view of yourself, taken from people who look down on you. It runs on top of all you do.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE ONLY ONE IN THE ROOM',text:'You are the first in your family in this profession, or the only woman on the panel. You answer the question. At the same moment you hear your answer as the doubters in the room will hear it, and you adjust. Nobody else at the table is doing that.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Double consciousness is the forced habit of seeing yourself partly through the eyes of people who hold you in contempt. It is a second job done on top of the first, and it is exhausting in a way the first is not.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'WHERE THE NAME COMES FROM',text:'Du Bois named it in 1903, writing about Black Americans a generation after slavery. He described a split inside one person: wanting to be both Black and American, in a country that treated those as opposed. The scene above is the same structure. His subject was this particular history.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'The first job',items:['Do the work','Answer the question','Make the case']},right:{title:'The second job',items:['Watch yourself doing it','Hear it as a doubter would','Adjust before anyone objects']},note:'Only one of these is in the job description. Both use up the day.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it is so tiring',text:'The second view never switches off, because the people who hold it never leave. You are running a copy of their judgement in your own head and checking each move against it. That copy was put there by contempt, not earned by any evidence about you.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'He looks into the window and sees himself, but as another set of eyes would place him. Two views of one man, in one glance.'},{kind:'try',text:'In one situation this week, notice whether you are doing the work or watching yourself do it through someone else\u2019s eyes. Name the second job when it starts.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A Black doctor, whose patients often doubt her, rehearses each sentence twice before she speaks. What does Du Bois\u2019s idea say is happening?',options:['She is doing the job and also managing how doubters will see her','She is simply a careful person who likes to prepare her words','She lacks confidence and should work on believing in herself','She is being polite in the way that any professional should be'],answer:0,why:'The extra rehearsal is the second job: a view of herself borrowed from people who expect her to fail. It is not a flaw in her confidence.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'THE TERM',word:'Double consciousness',meaning:'Du Bois\u2019s name for always seeing yourself partly through the eyes of a world that holds you in contempt, so that you carry two views of yourself at once.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'A MAGAZINE ESSAY, 1897',text:'Du Bois first set out the idea in a magazine essay in 1897, then revised it into the first chapter of *The Souls of Black Folk*. He was writing about Black Americans one generation after emancipation, in a country that had freed them and then refused them equal standing.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Living under contempt forces a person to keep two views of themselves: their own, and the one held by a world that looks down on them. Maintaining that second view is a real, continuous cost.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Two aims in one person',text:'Du Bois describes more than an outside gaze. He describes two aims pulling against each other: to be Black and to be American, in a nation that treated them as opposed. He would give up neither. He wanted a country in which a person could be both without being shut out.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the passage lasted',text:'The condition had been lived by millions, and Black writers before him had described life under racism. What Du Bois supplied was an exact name for its inner shape. The structure has since been recognised wherever someone is assessed by people who expect their competence to be absent.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Two misuses. The first dissolves it: applied to every outsider, the concept loses the history it came from, of slavery, Jim Crow laws and racial violence. The second treats the double view as only a wound. Du Bois also wrote of a second sight that came with it. And the phrase was not new. Emerson and the psychology of the day used it; Du Bois gave it this meaning.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Where, if anywhere, do you carry a second view of yourself that someone else put there? Whose eyes are they?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which use of the concept would most distort Du Bois\u2019s essay?',options:['Treating it as the ordinary shyness anyone feels in a new place','Using it for a first-generation student judged by doubting examiners','Reading it first as an account of Black life after slavery','Noting that the second view of oneself takes effort to maintain'],answer:0,why:'Du Bois describes a view imposed by a world of contempt, with a particular history behind it. Calling it shyness removes both the contempt and the history.'}]}},'souls-of-black-folk_2':{pictureCaption:'A page of figures and a page of written lines, given equal room and tied together. Du Bois\u2019s argument needed both.',bridge:'Idea 1 named an inner experience. This one is about how Du Bois made readers believe and feel such things: figures and prose together.',basics:{title:'Numbers and stories, side by side',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'YOU WANT A SAFER ROAD',prompt:'The road by your school is not safe. You write to the town. What will change their minds?',options:['A chart of how many near misses there were','The story of one kid who was nearly hit','Both, side by side'],answer:2,reveal:'A chart alone is easy to shrug off. It is just numbers. A story alone is easy to wave away. It is just one kid. Together, the chart shows it keeps happening, and the story shows what it means.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Numbers prove a problem is real and common. A true story makes people feel it. W. E. B. Du Bois, a Black American writer, put both in one book, because each one alone could be ignored.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'DOOR TO DOOR',text:'Du Bois was trained to study how people live, using facts. In the 1890s he went from house to house in Philadelphia, a big city. He asked Black families about their homes, jobs and lives. He turned the answers into tables of numbers. But numbers alone did not change enough minds.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A page of numbers and a page of writing, tied with one ribbon. Du Bois needed both.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What this book does',text:'So *The Souls of Black Folk* mixes the two. Some chapters count farms, rent and debt. Another tells how his own baby son died. The numbers say this happens to many people. The story says these are real people.'},{kind:'try',text:'Want to win someone over? Only facts? Add one true story. Only a story? Add one fact.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your class wants the school to put in a new water fountain. Which plan uses Du Bois\u2019s idea?',options:['Count how many kids get thirsty, and tell one kid\u2019s story','Make a long list of numbers about kids who get thirsty','Ask one very thirsty kid to go and tell the head teacher'],answer:0,why:'The numbers show it is common. The story shows what it is like. Each one covers the other\u2019s weak spot.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE COMFORTING BELIEF',belief:'Good evidence speaks for itself. Show people the numbers and they will act.',reality:'Du Bois found otherwise. Statistics can be read, accepted and set aside, because the reader can hold them at arm\u2019s length. Evidence that is believed and changes nothing was the problem he set out to solve.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Put the data and the lived account in the same book. The data shows the conditions are general. The account makes it impossible to read them as being about nobody in particular.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'PHILADELPHIA, 1890S',text:'Du Bois had studied in Berlin, then carried out a house-by-house survey of Black Philadelphia, published in 1899 as *The Philadelphia Negro*. It was careful, original social science. Four years later he put work of that kind beside memoir, history and elegy in a single book.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Numbers alone',items:['Shows the scale','Easy to accept and shelve','About everyone, so no one']},right:{title:'Story alone',items:['Shows the cost','Easy to call a one-off','About someone, so maybe nobody else']},note:'Each one closes the other\u2019s escape route.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How the book moves',text:'Chapters on land, rent and debt in rural Georgia are followed, a little later, by a chapter on the death of his infant son. The order is deliberate. When you meet the grieving father, you know he is no exception. When you read the next table, you know what it is counting.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Figures on the left, prose on the right, equal room, one ribbon. The pairing was the argument.'},{kind:'try',text:'Take a case you are making that is not landing. Check whether you are offering only evidence or only story, and add the missing half.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A charity\u2019s report on child poverty is full of accurate figures and changes nothing. What would Du Bois\u2019s method add?',options:['One family\u2019s account, placed beside the figures it belongs to','More figures, drawn from a larger and more careful sample','A moving story told instead of the figures, which are dull','Bolder charts in brighter colours, so the figures stand out'],answer:0,why:'The figures already establish the scale. A real account makes the reader feel they are about someone, without dropping the evidence.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'GEORGIA, 1899',text:'Du Bois later wrote that a lynching in Georgia in 1899 changed his work. A Black man, Sam Hose, was murdered by a white mob. Du Bois had been working as a detached scientist. He concluded that calm study alone could not answer a country where such killings went on.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Rigour and force are not rivals. Accurate evidence without vividness gets accepted and ignored; feeling without evidence gets dismissed as anecdote. Du Bois built one book to close both escapes.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The design of the book',text:'Its fourteen chapters move between history, sociology, biography, memoir and one short story. The Black Belt chapters count land, rent and debt in a Georgia county. A few chapters later he is burying his son. Each kind of writing blocks the reader\u2019s easiest way out of the one before.'},{kind:'equation',parts:['Evidence that is believed','Detail that is felt'],result:'An argument that moves',note:'Either part alone left the reader a way out.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What it broke with',text:'Social science of the time prized detachment, and much of what it said about Black Americans was neither careful nor fair. Du Bois matched its rigour and refused its distance. Pairing measured fact with personal witness is now normal in serious non-fiction. In 1903 it was a considered break.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The method can be turned round. A single vivid case can stand in for a pattern that does not exist, and feeling can carry a weak claim past a reader\u2019s guard. Du Bois\u2019s pairing works because his data was real and could be checked. Remove that half and what is left is only persuasion.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of a case you made that was believed and then ignored. Which half was missing?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these uses the form but betrays the method?',options:['One moving story used to stand for a trend the figures do not show','A survey of a town published with interviews of three residents','A history of a policy that includes one family it affected','A table of figures followed by an account of one case in it'],answer:0,why:'The method pairs a real pattern with a real case. A story standing in for a pattern the data does not support keeps the force and drops the rigour.'}]}},'souls-of-black-folk_3':{pictureCaption:'Two hands meet in a polite greeting. Between them hangs the question neither person will ask out loud.',bridge:'This is how the book actually opens, and where double consciousness from idea 1 begins: with a question nobody asks out loud.',basics:{title:'The question nobody asked',beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'WHAT PEOPLE SAID TO HIM',text:'W. E. B. Du Bois was a Black American writer. He noticed how some white people talked to him. They were polite. They might say they knew a fine Black man in their town. Or how bad things were in the South. But they never asked what they wanted to ask.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The real question',text:'At that time, white leaders and newspapers often talked about Black people as a *problem*. Something to sort out, like a leaking roof. So under every polite chat sat one question: what is it like to be treated as a problem?'},{kind:'idea',text:'When a hidden idea sits under every talk, say it out loud and answer it. Du Bois did. Once it was said plainly, people could see what it did to real people.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two hands meet to say hello. Above them hangs a question. Neither person says it.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A NOTE ON A FILE',text:'Think of every teacher getting a note about you that says *trouble*. They are all nice to you. But they all read the note first. Du Bois was the one who said: here is the note, and here is what it does to me.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'In 1903, a newspaper writes a long piece on what to do about Black workers. It asks none of them. What would Du Bois point out?',options:['It treats people as a problem to fix, and never asks them','It needs more numbers about how many workers there are','It is fine, because the writer is trying hard to help'],answer:0,why:'He named the idea under the talk: real people were being handled as a problem. Then he answered it from the inside.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE POLITE ROUTE',prompt:'People approached Du Bois with careful sympathy, praising a Black man they knew or condemning outrages in the South. What were they circling?',options:['A rude question about his work','What it is like to be treated as a problem','Any talk of politics at all'],answer:1,reveal:'The sympathy went round one question and never put it plainly. Du Bois put it plainly, and then answered it himself.'},{kind:'idea',text:'To be called a problem is to be made a category instead of a person: managed and legislated about, while your own experience never enters the discussion. Du Bois named that hidden framing and answered it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the wording is the analysis',text:'A problem is something other people discuss and solve. In 1903 the whole debate about Black Americans could run in newspapers and in Congress without Black experience entering it. By asking what being a problem feels like, Du Bois turned the subject of the debate into its witness.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Talked about',items:['A problem','Managed and legislated about','Their view is not needed']},right:{title:'Speaking for themselves',items:['A person','Has an inner life','Their view is the evidence']},note:'Du Bois answers from the right-hand column. That is how he gets to double consciousness.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A polite greeting at the door. The question that matters hangs between the hands, and nobody asks it.'},{kind:'try',text:'In the next discussion where one assumption sits under everything, say it out loud in plain words. Then answer it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A council holds months of meetings on \u2018the homelessness problem\u2019 without hearing from anyone who is homeless. What is Du Bois\u2019s move?',options:['Name the framing: people are being treated as a problem, not heard','Gather better figures on how many people in the town are homeless','Keep the meetings polite, since naming it would only cause offence','Rename the agenda item so that it sounds kinder to outsiders'],answer:0,why:'The move is to say the hidden framing plainly and answer it from inside. Better numbers or kinder labels leave the framing untouched.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE FIRST PAGE OF CHAPTER ONE',text:'The first chapter opens with an encounter, not a thesis. Du Bois describes people who approach him with careful sympathy, mention a Black man they admire or deplore Southern outrages, and never ask what is really on their minds.'},{kind:'term',word:'Framing',meaning:'The assumption that sets the terms of a discussion before anyone speaks. Here: that Black Americans were a problem for white America to solve, rather than citizens with claims of their own.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The book\u2019s organising question is the one never put directly: what it is like to be a problem. The phrasing is the analysis. A problem is a category, discussed and solved, whose own experience never enters the discussion.'},{kind:'explain',title:'From question to concept',text:'Du Bois answers from inside. He recalls a schoolroom moment in childhood when a girl coldly refused his visiting card, and he saw that he was shut out of her world. From there he builds the veil and double consciousness: the cost of always keeping a second, outside view of yourself.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Hands almost meeting at a door. Above them, unasked, a question shaped from an ordinary coat hook.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Naming the frame is necessary, not sufficient. It changes what a discussion is about; it does not change laws, wages or votes. Du Bois knew this. The rest of the book argues about remedies, and he spent decades in political organising. Naming can also be misused, as a substitute for engaging with the substance of a policy.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What question sits under a debate you know well, asked about people rather than with them?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'Which of these is Du Bois\u2019s move, applied elsewhere?',options:['A report on \u2018the migrant question\u2019 that begins by asking migrants what it is like','A report on migration that gathers more careful figures than earlier studies','A report that swaps \u2018problem\u2019 for \u2018challenge\u2019 and keeps the same framing','A report that avoids the subject entirely because it is too divisive'],answer:0,why:'He did not just add data or soften the label. He named the frame and answered it from inside, making the supposed problem a witness.'}]}},'souls-of-black-folk_4':{pictureCaption:'Two chairs on the same side of the table, and one small mark against a single line. Allies disagreeing, at one exact point.',bridge:'The first ideas faced a hostile world. This one is an argument inside Du Bois\u2019s own side, with its most powerful leader.',basics:{title:'Disagreeing with a friend, fairly',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'ON THE SAME TEAM',belief:'If you are on the same side, you should never argue in public.',reality:'W. E. B. Du Bois, a Black writer, argued in public with Booker T. Washington, the best-known Black leader of the time. He was fair, but he did not water down his view.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'TWO LEADERS',text:'Booker T. Washington was born into slavery. He built a famous school in Alabama. It taught Black students trades like farming and building. He told Black people to work hard and save. And he told them to stop pushing, for now, for the vote and equal rights.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where they split',text:'Du Bois agreed that work and skills mattered. But he said giving up the vote was a bad deal. People with no vote have no say in the laws. Then what they earn can be taken away. So the thing given up was the thing that kept the rest safe.'},{kind:'idea',text:'You can disagree with someone on your side without being mean and without going soft. Say what they get right. Then say the one exact place you think they are wrong.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two chairs sit side by side. One small mark sits by one line. That is where they disagree.'},{kind:'steps',title:'How to disagree fairly',steps:['Say their view so well that they would nod.','Say what they got right.','Point to the one exact place you disagree.','Say why, with no name-calling.']},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your best friend wants the team to skip practice and rest before the final. You disagree. What would Du Bois do?',options:['Say why resting makes sense, then the one thing it gets wrong','Go along with it, so that the two of you stay friends','Tell the whole team that your friend is lazy and scared'],answer:0,why:'He would grant what is right in the plan, then name one exact problem. No flattery, and no insults.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'ON YOUR OWN SIDE',text:'A leader you admire, on your side of a cause, is following a strategy you think will fail. Say nothing, and you let it fail. Attack them, and you split the side. Most people choose one of those two.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'ATLANTA, 1895',text:'Booker T. Washington, born into slavery, founded Tuskegee Institute and became the most powerful Black leader in America. In an 1895 speech in Atlanta he offered white Southerners a bargain. Black Americans would set aside demands for the vote and civil equality, for now. In return they would get room to build skills and wealth.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Disagree with an ally by stating their case at its strongest, crediting what is right in it, and then placing the disagreement at one exact point. No flattery, and no talk of bad motives.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The one point',text:'Du Bois credits Washington\u2019s achievement and the hard conditions that made his plan look sensible. Then he finds the flaw: a group without political power cannot protect its economic gains. What was given up was the very thing that would have secured what was received.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'ONE MARK, ONE LINE',caption:'Two chairs drawn up on the same side, as allies sit. An open hand rests on the page, and one mark sits against a single line: the place where they part.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHY THE TRADE FAILS',nodes:['Give up the vote','Lose any say over the laws','Gains can be taken away','Nothing left to protect them'],note:'The objection is that the bargain fails on its own terms, which is far harder to dismiss than an attack on the man.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before your next disagreement, write the other person\u2019s position so that they would recognise it. Then underline the one sentence where you part ways.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A colleague you respect proposes cutting training to save money. You think it will cost more later. Which reply follows Du Bois\u2019s model?',options:['Credit the savings, then show exactly where the cut undoes them','Agree in the meeting and raise your doubts privately with others','Point out that they have never really cared about training','List every flaw you can find anywhere in their whole budget'],answer:0,why:'He states the case fairly, then puts the disagreement at a single point. Doubting motives or scattering objections makes an enemy without making the argument.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'CHAPTER THREE',text:'The chapter on Booker T. Washington argues with the most powerful Black leader in the country, at the height of his influence, with wealthy white patrons and the ear of the White House. Du Bois takes care to state the case for him before dissenting.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Dispute with an ally as Du Bois did: the strongest version of their case first, real credit, then the disagreement at a single identifiable point. The substance is not softened, and the person is not attacked.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What Washington asked',text:'As Du Bois sets it out, Washington asked Black Americans to give up, for the present, three things: political power, the insistence on civil rights, and higher education for their young. In return they would get peace with the white South and room for industrial training and property.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where Du Bois cuts',text:'He grants the achievement and the conditions that made the policy reasonable. Then he argues the trade fails on its own terms. In the years of Washington\u2019s ascendancy, he notes, came disenfranchisement, civil inferiority written into law, and shrinking support for Black colleges. A people without votes cannot defend their property.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Chairs side by side, not facing each other. An open hand, not a fist. One mark against one line. The shape of the dissent is its method.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Washington: set aside, for now',items:['The vote','Pressing for civil rights','Higher education for the young']},right:{title:'Du Bois: insist on',items:['The right to vote','Civic equality','Education according to ability']},note:'They agreed about work and thrift. The split was over what could safely be traded.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Three cautions. The even tone did not keep the dispute even: within a few years the two camps were in open, bitter conflict. Washington\u2019s public line was not his whole strategy; historians have found he quietly funded court challenges to disenfranchisement. And this courtesy is a model for allies. It is not a politeness owed by the wronged to those wronging them.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of someone on your side you disagree with. Could you write their case well enough that they would sign it?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE FLAW',prompt:'Which of these breaks Du Bois\u2019s model of arguing with an ally?',options:['Hinting that the ally\u2019s real motive is their own career','Stating the ally\u2019s case before raising any objection to it','Crediting the ally\u2019s record while rejecting one central policy','Arguing that the policy fails by the ally\u2019s own stated aims'],answer:0,why:'Du Bois never imputes bad motive. Moving the dispute from the policy to the person turns an ally into an enemy and leaves the argument unmade.'}]}},'souls-of-black-folk_5':{pictureCaption:'A curtain across the room. Each side can make out the other, dimly, and only one side has to look.',bridge:'Idea 1 named the inner cost and idea 3 the unasked question. The veil is Du Bois\u2019s image for the barrier itself.',basics:{title:'A curtain, not a wall',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'A THIN CURTAIN',text:'Think of a thin curtain hung across a room. It is not a wall. You can see shapes through it. But it blurs everything and splits the room.'},{kind:'idea',text:'W. E. B. Du Bois, a Black American writer, called the line between Black and white America *the veil*. Like a thin curtain, it let people see each other a little. But it kept them apart and blurred the view.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'This curtain splits the room. You can see a person on the other side, but only as a blur.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Who sees more?',text:'Here is the sharp part. Black people had to watch the white world closely, just to stay safe in it. Many white people hardly looked through the veil at all. Most did not even notice it was there.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What held it up?',text:'In the South, laws kept Black and white people in separate schools and train cars. Most Black men were stopped from voting. Mobs attacked and killed Black people, and were hardly ever punished.'},{kind:'recap',points:['The veil is a curtain, not a wall.','One side has to watch closely. The other side hardly looks.','Laws and violence kept it there.']},{kind:'question',prompt:'In a story, kids from one street must learn every rule of the next street to stay safe. The kids on the next street never think about them. What is this like?',options:['The veil: one side has to watch, the other does not','A wall: nobody can see across it at all','A fair split: both sides know just as much'],answer:0,why:'Like the veil, it lets sight through, but not fairly. One side has to learn the other. The other side does not have to look.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHY NOT A WALL?',left:{title:'A wall',items:['Blocks sight','Everyone sees it']},right:{title:'A veil',items:['Lets sight through, distorted','One side barely notices it']},note:'Du Bois chose the second image, and the choice is the insight.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The veil is a separation that is not a wall. Both sides can partly see through it, everything passing through is distorted, and only one side is obliged to look.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The asymmetry',text:'Those behind the veil had to see the other world clearly, because their safety and living depended on it. Those on the other side saw only the veil, and rarely noticed it was there. One side had to be expert in the other. The other side did not have to look at all.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'ONE SHOP COUNTER',text:'Picture a Black shopkeeper in a Southern town in 1903. To stay in business, and safe, he must know the habits, moods and unwritten rules of his white customers and the local officials. Few of them know anything of his life beyond the counter. The knowledge runs one way.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A curtain across the room. Figures show through it on both sides, blurred, and only one side has to look.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Built and enforced',text:'In 1903 the veil was enforced by law and by violence: segregation laws, the stripping of votes, violence that went unpunished. The image adds something further: what that separation does to sight on each side. So describe what it does, not only who maintains it.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick a divide you live near. Ask which side has to understand the other to get by, and which side can safely ignore it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Which detail best shows a veil, rather than a wall?',options:['One group must study the other closely; the other barely notices the divide','A law forbids the two groups from meeting in public at all','Both groups know each other equally well but choose to stay apart','Nobody on either side can see across the divide in any way'],answer:0,why:'A wall blocks sight. The veil lets it through, distorted and unequal: one side must look, the other need not.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'NOT JUST A LINE',belief:'The veil is simply Du Bois\u2019s word for segregation.',reality:'Segregation was part of it, but the image is more exact. A veil lets sight through both ways, partially and distortedly. What it captures is an unequal relation of seeing, not only a line of separation.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The book\u2019s controlling image is a membrane, not a wall: both sides see dimly through it, and only one side is required to. Its use is that it describes what a barrier does, not only who maintains it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where it comes from',text:'Du Bois introduces it in the first pages, drawing on a folk belief that a child born with a veil over its face has second sight. Black Americans, he says, are born with such a veil. The gift cuts both ways: they see the other world, but see themselves only as it reflects them.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The asymmetry of attention',text:'Those within must know the world outside in detail to survive it. Those outside see the veil, if at all, as part of the scenery. Knowledge flows one way. That is why the image held: it explains how a divide can be total on one side and nearly invisible on the other.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A curtain hanging free, its hem the only colour. A figure behind, a figure in front: each sees the other, blurred.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The veil can be misread as a barrier without authors. In 1903 it had many: legislators who passed Jim Crow laws, officials who struck Black voters off the rolls, mobs who lynched without punishment. The image shows what that divide does to sight. It must not be used to hide that people built it, and that people can take it down.'},{kind:'keyline',text:'A wall is seen by everyone. A veil is seen mostly by those it confines.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HARDER CASE',prompt:'Someone argues: \u2018Nobody I know is keeping any barrier up, so there is none.\u2019 How does the image of the veil answer?',options:['A barrier can be nearly invisible to one side and plain to those it confines','Every barrier needs a person deliberately holding it in place','If one side does not notice a divide, it cannot be very real','Barriers fade on their own once the laws that made them are repealed'],answer:0,why:'The veil is seen mostly by those it confines, and barely noticed by the other side. Not noticing it is a symptom, not a disproof.'}]}},'souls-of-black-folk_6':{pictureCaption:'One line traced through the Americas, Africa and Asia, beside a new diary open at its first pages. One problem, named as a century began.',bridge:'The veil is the barrier seen up close. Here Du Bois steps back and draws the same line around the whole world.',basics:{title:'Naming the big problem early',beats:[{kind:'timeline',kicker:'WHAT CAME AFTER',points:[{when:'1903',what:'The Black American writer W. E. B. Du Bois says the colour line will be the great problem of the new century.'},{when:'1947',what:'India wins freedom from British rule.'},{when:'1950s\u201360s',what:'The civil rights movement fights unfair race laws in the US.'},{when:'1960',what:'Many African countries win freedom from European rule.'},{when:'1994',what:'South Africa ends apartheid, its system of rule by race.'}],note:'He wrote his line before any of this happened.'},{kind:'term',word:'The colour line',meaning:'The line drawn between people by skin colour. People with darker skin were kept apart, kept poor and kept out of power.'},{kind:'idea',text:'In 1903, the new century had just begun. Du Bois said its biggest problem would be the colour line. He meant the whole world, not just the US. He was right.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The whole world',picture:!0,text:'Back then, a few countries in Europe ruled most of Africa and much of Asia. In the US South, laws kept Black people apart. Most Black men there could not vote. Du Bois saw one problem in all of it. People with light skin ruled people with dark skin.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How could he tell?',text:'Du Bois was trained to study facts about how people live. So he could spot one pattern in many places. And as a Black man, he lived on the hard side of the line. He was in the right place to look.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A girl in 1903 says the colour line is just a problem in a few towns in the South. What would Du Bois say?',options:['It runs round the world, wherever lighter-skinned people rule darker','She is right, and it is only a small problem in a few towns','It will fade away by itself within a few short years'],answer:0,why:'He saw the same pattern in the US, Africa, Asia and beyond. That wide view let him see what the century would be about.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'1903, THE THIRD YEAR',text:'In the opening pages of *The Souls of Black Folk*, Du Bois declares the colour line the central problem of the twentieth century. The century was barely two years old. The world wars, the end of empires and the civil rights movement all lay ahead.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Du Bois named the colour line as the defining problem of the twentieth century when it had barely begun. The forecast held. Naming the central issue before the evidence piles up is risky. It is also most valuable when it lands.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Wider than America',text:'The scope is often missed. Du Bois meant the relation of darker to lighter peoples in Asia, Africa, the Americas and the islands. He meant the whole order of empire, not only segregation in the American South.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'ONE LINE, MANY PLACES',caption:'The pen draws one unbroken line through Africa, Asia, the Americas and the islands. Beside the map, a new diary lies open at its first pages: a century just begun.'},{kind:'timeline',kicker:'HOW IT HELD',points:[{when:'1903',what:'Du Bois names the colour line as the century\u2019s problem'},{when:'1947',what:'India wins independence from Britain'},{when:'1954\u201368',what:'The civil rights movement in the United States'},{when:'1960',what:'Seventeen African countries become independent'},{when:'1994',what:'South Africa holds its first elections open to all races'}],note:'Each was a struggle over the line he had named.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why he could see it',text:'It was not prophecy. He had the training to see a pattern as structural rather than local. He also had the position to see it at all. He was a Black scholar who lived on one side of the line and studied both. The best-placed observer was also trained to measure.'},{kind:'try',text:'Write down what you think the defining problem of your field will be over the next ten years. Date it, and keep it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Which habit would best help you make a forecast the way Du Bois did?',options:['Looking for the same pattern across many places, not just one','Waiting until the evidence is complete before saying anything','Choosing the most dramatic prediction you can still defend','Trusting whoever is most comfortable inside the current system'],answer:0,why:'He saw a local injustice as one case of a global structure. That is what let him name the century\u2019s problem in its third year.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE NARROW READING',belief:'Du Bois was forecasting race relations in the American South.',reality:'That too, but far more. He names the relation of darker to lighter peoples across Asia, Africa, the Americas and the islands. The forecast is about the global order of empire, with the United States as one case.'},{kind:'idea',text:'In the century\u2019s opening years, Du Bois named its organising conflict: the colour line, at home and across the empires. It was a forecast made before the evidence existed, and it held.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'LONDON, 1900',text:'He had used the formulation before, in an address to the nations of the world at the Pan-African Conference in London in 1900. Setting it at the front of *Souls* in 1903 turned a conference statement into the frame for a book about American life.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One continuous line through the Americas, Africa, Asia and the islands, and beside it a diary with nearly every page still blank. The pattern was named before the century filled it in.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What made it possible',text:'Two things together. Sociological training let him see segregation in Georgia and colonial rule in Africa as one structure, not a set of local troubles. His position meant he was looking where most white scholars were not. Training without position misses it; position without training cannot prove it.'},{kind:'timeline',kicker:'TESTED BY THE CENTURY',points:[{when:'1947',what:'Indian independence, a turning point in the end of European empire'},{when:'1960',what:'Seventeen African states gain independence in one year'},{when:'1964\u201365',what:'US Civil Rights Act and Voting Rights Act'},{when:'1994',what:'Apartheid ends with South Africa\u2019s first all-race election'}],note:'Each is a fight over the line he named in 1903.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Forecasts that land are remembered and ones that miss are forgotten, so judge the method, not only the hit. The colour line was not the century\u2019s only great conflict: class, nationalism and the Cold War shaped it too, often crossing the line. The claim was organising, not exclusive, and Du Bois himself later gave economics far more weight.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What is the defining problem of the next decade in your field? What gives you, specifically, the position to see it?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'A critic says Du Bois simply got lucky with a bold guess. What is the best reply?',options:['His forecast rested on a pattern he had studied across many places','Any forecast about race made in 1903 was bound to come true','Bold guesses are always worth more than careful predictions','He knew the future from his own personal experience alone'],answer:0,why:'Luck explains a single hit. Du Bois combined training with position and saw one structure where others saw local troubles. That is a method, and it can be checked.'}]}},'souls-of-black-folk_7':{pictureCaption:'An empty cot by the window, a blanket folded on the rail and left untouched. The book\u2019s argument, at the size of one room.',bridge:'Idea 2 explained why Du Bois set figures beside lived accounts. This is the account that carries the most weight.',basics:{title:'A father\u2019s grief as evidence',beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'ATLANTA, 1899',text:'W. E. B. Du Bois was a Black American writer. He and his wife had a baby son. They lived in Atlanta, a city in the US South. When the boy was about a year and a half old, he fell ill and died.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What he wrote',text:'Du Bois wrote a chapter about his son. It is full of love and grief. Then, near the end, he says something very hard. Part of him was glad. His son would never grow up to face the hatred and unfair treatment Black people faced.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Numbers can show a problem is big. One true story from one home can show how deep it goes. Du Bois\u2019s grief is part of his proof.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A small empty bed by a window. A blanket folded on the rail. One family\u2019s loss.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why that matters',text:'No parent wants to feel even a little glad that their child died. Racism put that thought into the mind of a loving father. That shows how cruel it was. No chart of numbers could show that.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'What does the chapter about his son show that the numbers in the book cannot?',options:['What racism did inside one family, even to a father\u2019s grief','How many Black children were born in Atlanta that year','That Du Bois had grown tired of writing about facts'],answer:0,why:'Numbers show how wide a problem is. His grief shows how deep it went, into the most private place a person has.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'CHAPTER ELEVEN',text:'Du Bois\u2019s first child, a son, died in Atlanta at about eighteen months old. In the middle of a book of history, economics and political argument, Du Bois gives him a chapter of his own.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Here the argument stops being statistical without becoming less rigorous. A father\u2019s grief, honestly reported, is evidence of what the system does inside a home: it can put relief into mourning.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The hard admission',text:'Near its end the chapter turns. Du Bois reports, with evident discomfort, a thought he cannot push away: his son will never have to live behind the veil. A loving father is half-glad his child is gone. That admission is the evidence.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'An empty cot at dawn, a blanket on the rail. The structures the book measures, at the size of one room.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'What the surveys show',items:['How many','How often','How widespread']},right:{title:'What this chapter shows',items:['What it is like','How far in it reaches','Even into grief']},note:'Neither column alone is the whole truth.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Not a plea for pity',text:'Du Bois is not seeking sympathy. He is closing a gap in the evidence. Structures can be described forever in the abstract. This chapter shows their cost at the only scale where it is actually felt: one household.'},{kind:'try',text:'Take a problem that feels abstract to you. Find how it shows up in a single household. That is the size it is actually lived at.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A report on a housing shortage is accurate but abstract. Following this chapter, what should it add?',options:['An honest account of what the shortage does inside one home','A larger survey that covers every city in the country','An appeal asking readers to feel sorry for those affected','A forecast of how the shortage will change over ten years'],answer:0,why:'The chapter is evidence, not an appeal. One household shows the cost at the scale where it is actually lived.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHERE IT SITS',text:'The chapter on the death of his infant son stands among chapters of economic history, sociology and political argument. It is the book\u2019s emotional centre, and arguably its evidential one: the place where the argument stops being sociological without becoming less rigorous.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Placed among the book\u2019s statistics, the chapter proves that the structures described have an interior. A father\u2019s honest admission of relief at his son\u2019s death is evidence no survey can produce.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What he reports',text:'He writes of the birth, the child\u2019s short life and his death in Atlanta. Then comes a thought he reports with great discomfort: a kind of relief that the boy has escaped life behind the veil. He does not excuse it or explain it away. He sets it down.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'An empty cot at dawn, a blanket folded and untouched. The book measures structures; this is one of them, ending in a single room.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE CHAPTER ARGUES',nodes:['A father\u2019s love, fully shown','His son dies','Relief enters the grief','The system has reached inside it'],note:'The admission works as proof only because the love is never in doubt.'},{kind:'limits',text:'One private grief cannot establish a general claim alone; it leans on the surveys around it. It can be misread as despair, as if Du Bois thought Black life not worth living. He did not: he spent a long life fighting for it. And testimony like this costs the witness. Readers should not demand it of every sufferer before believing them.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Where do you know a problem only as a figure? What would the one-household version of it show?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'READ IT RIGHTLY',prompt:'What is the strongest reason this chapter belongs in a work of sociology?',options:['It supplies evidence of inner cost that surveys cannot capture','It softens the book so that readers finish the harder chapters','It shows that Du Bois had abandoned scientific method by 1903','It asks for the sympathy that the statistics failed to win'],answer:0,why:'The chapter closes a gap in the evidence. Set among the data, it shows that the structures measured reach inside a home.'}]}},'souls-of-black-folk_8':{pictureCaption:'Lines of verse at the top of the page; beneath, a bar of music with no words. Two traditions given the same rank.',bridge:'Idea 2 showed Du Bois arguing through what he set side by side. Here the pairing is on the first page of every chapter.',basics:{title:'Music at the top of every chapter',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'OPEN THE BOOK',prompt:'W. E. B. Du Bois wrote a book about Black life in America. Each chapter starts with two things: a few lines of a poem, and a line of music with no words. Why add the music?',options:['To make the page look nice','To say songs made by enslaved people are great art too','Because he ran out of poems'],answer:1,reveal:'The music comes from songs made by enslaved Black people. Du Bois put them beside famous poets, as equals.'},{kind:'term',word:'Sorrow songs',meaning:'Du Bois\u2019s name for spirituals: songs made and sung by enslaved Black people in America. Many are about pain, faith and the hope of freedom.'},{kind:'idea',text:'How you lay something out can make a point. By putting these songs beside great poems, Du Bois said they were great art too.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A few lines of a poem at the top. Below, a line of music with no words. Side by side, as equals.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Songs as a record',text:'In many states, it was against the law to teach enslaved people to read or write. So most could not keep diaries or letters. But they kept their songs and passed them on. Du Bois said the songs were a record of their lives, and the most beautiful music America had made.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why no words?',text:'The music is printed with no words. If you can\u2019t read music, you can\u2019t tell what it says. This may be on purpose. For a moment you are shut out, on the wrong side of a barrier. That is what the whole book is about.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your class is making a wall display about music. Which layout makes Du Bois\u2019s point?',options:['A local folk song beside a famous symphony, the same size','The famous symphony big, and the folk song small in a corner','No folk song at all, since it was never written down'],answer:0,why:'Giving them the same place says they deserve the same respect. Du Bois did that on every chapter\u2019s first page.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'EVERY CHAPTER\u2019S FIRST PAGE',text:'Each chapter of the book opens with two epigraphs. One is a passage of poetry, often by a famous European poet such as Tennyson or Schiller. Below it is a bar of music from a spiritual, printed as notes, with no words and no title.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The form of a book can carry part of its argument. A spiritual is set beside famous poetry, in the same place and the same type. That claims equal standing for both before a word of argument is read.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The case in the last chapter',text:'Du Bois makes the argument openly at the end. He calls the sorrow songs the one truly American music. He reads them as a record kept by people forbidden to keep records. The epigraphs had been making that case all along.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Verse at the top, a stave of wordless music below: the same page, the same standing. The layout makes the claim.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why no words',text:'Most readers cannot read written music. They meet a line they cannot access, set beside one they can. On one persuasive reading, on every opening page the reader briefly stands on the wrong side of a barrier. That barrier is the book\u2019s subject.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before you next present something, check whether the format is arguing with your content or supporting it. Readers notice the form first.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A museum shows quilts made by enslaved women in a back corridor and oil portraits in the main hall. What would Du Bois\u2019s layout argue?',options:['Hang them in the same hall, with the same care in display','Keep the corridor, but give each quilt a longer label','Move the portraits into the corridor to make things even','Say in the guidebook that the quilts are valuable too'],answer:0,why:'Du Bois let the layout make the claim of equal standing. Praising something in words while keeping the old order undoes the point.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'BEFORE YOU READ ON',prompt:'Why might Du Bois print each bar of music without the song\u2019s words or title?',options:['He could not get permission to print the words','So readers meet a text they cannot read beside one they can','The words had been lost by 1903'],answer:1,reveal:'One persuasive reading: most readers can follow the verse but not the stave. Each opening page puts them, briefly, on the wrong side of a barrier.'},{kind:'term',word:'Epigraph',meaning:'A short passage set at the head of a chapter, usually borrowed from another writer, which frames what follows.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The paired epigraphs are an argument about whose culture counts as culture. A spiritual sits beneath Tennyson or Schiller in the same register, and the form makes its claim before the prose does.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The final chapter',text:'The last chapter, on the sorrow songs, makes the case outright. Du Bois argues that they are the finest artistic expression the country has produced and its true spiritual inheritance. He traces what the songs carried: grief, faith, and a hope of freedom held under slavery.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Verse at the head of the page, and beneath it a stave with no words. Two traditions, one page, one rank.'},{kind:'explain',title:'A record without writing',text:'In many slave states it was a crime to teach enslaved people to read and write. The songs carried memory, faith and protest anyway, from voice to voice. Printing them as notation beside canonical verse treats that oral record as a document of equal weight.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The pairing is less tidy than it sounds. Not every epigraph is European: some are by American poets, one is from the Bible, and the last chapter\u2019s verse is itself from a spiritual. The reading of the wordless notation as a deliberate barrier is an interpretation; Du Bois does not say so. And many readers simply skip the bar.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'Which design choice makes an argument the way Du Bois\u2019s epigraphs do?',options:['An anthology setting folk ballads in the same type and rank as famous poets','An anthology with a preface praising folk ballads as underrated','An anthology placing folk ballads in an appendix with full notes','An anthology printing folk ballads in larger type than the poets'],answer:0,why:'Du Bois claimed equal standing through placement, not commentary or reversal. Set beside the poets in the same register, the ballads make the claim before anyone argues it.'}]}},'souls-of-black-folk_9':{pictureCaption:'One ledger, one hand: ticks for what worked, crosses for what failed, and the pen still adding to the crosses. An honest account keeps both.',bridge:'Idea 4 disagreed with an ally fairly. Here Du Bois brings the same honesty to history, crediting and criticising his own cause.',basics:{title:'Telling the whole truth about a hard time',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'AFTER THE FINAL',text:'Your team lost the final. You could say the ref ruined it. You could say your team was perfect. Or you could tell the whole truth: the ref made bad calls, and your team also missed easy shots.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'AFTER SLAVERY ENDED',text:'In 1865 the US Civil War ended, and slavery was ended by law. About four million people were now free. Most had no land, no money and no schooling. The government set up an office to help them. It was called the Freedmen\u2019s Bureau.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'What it did well',items:['Opened schools for freed people','Set up courts where they could be heard','Helped make fair work deals']},right:{title:'Where it failed',items:['Dropped the plan to give them land','Had too little money and power','Some of its staff were dishonest','Was shut down too soon']}},{kind:'idea',text:'When you tell the story of something that went wrong, tell all of it: what worked, and what your own side got wrong. The Black historian W. E. B. Du Bois did that, and it made his account easier to trust.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One hand fills both pages. Ticks for what worked, crosses for what failed. The pen does not skip the crosses.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Who let it fail?',text:'Du Bois said the Bureau did not have to fail. Leaders in Washington, the US capital, lost interest and shut it down too soon. In the years after, Black people in the South lost the vote and many other rights they had won.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You are writing about a school fair that raised less money than hoped. Which report follows Du Bois?',options:['Say what went well, what went wrong, and what we got wrong','Only say what went well, so that no one feels bad','Blame the rain, since none of it was our own fault'],answer:0,why:'Owning your side\u2019s mistakes is what makes the rest of your report believable.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'TWO EASY STORIES',belief:'Reconstruction was either a noble success that was betrayed, or a corrupt disaster.',reality:'Du Bois refused both. In his account of the Freedmen\u2019s Bureau it was a serious attempt, partly effective, and ended early. That is harder to tell, and the only version the evidence supports.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'1865',text:'As the Civil War ended, Congress created the Freedmen\u2019s Bureau to help some four million newly freed people. It ran schools, settled disputes and oversaw labour contracts across the defeated South. Writing in 1903, Du Bois gave it a chapter.'},{kind:'idea',text:'An honest account of a lost cause credits what was achieved and names what failed, including failures on your own side. Refusing the easy story is what makes the account believable.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Achieved',items:['Founded schools across the South','Courts for people who had none','Free labour contracts']},right:{title:'Failed',items:['Land redistribution abandoned','Starved of money and authority','Some agents corrupt','Ended with the work half done']},note:'Du Bois fills both columns in the same voice.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'THE SAME HAND',caption:'Both pages are in the same hand, ticks on one side and crosses on the other, and the pen is at work on the crosses. Same voice, both columns.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Not inevitable',text:'His verdict is pointed. The failure was not fate. It was chosen in Washington, by people who lost interest while the work was half done. That charge is credible only because he has already admitted the Bureau\u2019s own faults.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you write up something that went wrong for your side, include what your side got wrong. It is what makes the rest believable.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You are reviewing a campaign you led that lost. Which write-up follows Du Bois?',options:['What worked, what the other side did, and where we failed','How the other side\u2019s tactics made our defeat unavoidable','Everything that went wrong, to show we have learned humility','Our achievements only, since the failures are well known'],answer:0,why:'Du Bois credits and criticises the same institution and blames the right people. A one-sided account, in either direction, is easier to dismiss.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'timeline',kicker:'THE BUREAU\u2019S LIFE',points:[{when:'1865',what:'Congress creates the Freedmen\u2019s Bureau as the Civil War ends'},{when:'1865',what:'The Thirteenth Amendment abolishes slavery'},{when:'1869',what:'Most Bureau work ends; schooling continues a little longer'},{when:'1872',what:'Congress closes the Bureau'},{when:'1877',what:'Federal troops leave the South; Reconstruction ends'}],note:'Du Bois wrote in 1903, as the stripping of Black votes spread across the South.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Assess the institution, not the myth. Du Bois credits the Bureau\u2019s schools, courts and contracts, names its failures, and judges its end a choice made in Washington, not a fate.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What he was writing against',text:'Around 1900 an account gaining ground in American universities told Reconstruction as a corrupt disaster imposed on the South. Du Bois answers it without simply inverting it. He grants corruption and incompetence where he finds them, which is why his credit counts.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The verdict',text:'He judges that the failure was not inevitable. Land redistribution was abandoned, money and authority were withheld, and the Bureau was ended with its work half done by people in Washington who had lost interest. Each is a decision, not a law of nature.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The ledger is kept in one hand throughout, and the pen is adding to the page of crosses. The ticks are believed because the crosses are entered just as carefully.'},{kind:'keyline',text:'A lost cause is believed when its advocate names its own mistakes.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The chapter is short and early. Du Bois\u2019s full reckoning came in *Black Reconstruction in America* in 1935, which argued that freed people were central actors and attacked the historians who had maligned them. And balance can become false balance: two full columns do not mean the faults and the achievements weigh the same.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of a cause you belong to that has lost ground. Could you write its failures in the same voice as its achievements?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these misuses Du Bois\u2019s method?',options:['Listing one minor flaw per major injustice so both sides look equal','Crediting a programme\u2019s schools while naming its abandoned land policy','Blaming a programme\u2019s early end on officials who withdrew support','Admitting corruption among a cause\u2019s own agents while defending its aims'],answer:0,why:'Honest accounting weighs failures by their actual size. Matching flaws one for one to look fair is false balance, which the evidence does not support.'}]}}}},3010,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'spacing-effect-evidence_1':{pictureCaption:'An old stone bridge still carries today\u2019s lorries without a crack. A finding that has borne a century of testing is built like that.',basics:{title:'An old finding that keeps passing its tests',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO STUDY',prompt:'Sam and Mia each study spellings for one hour. Sam does the whole hour on Sunday night. Mia does fifteen minutes on four days. Who remembers more a week later?',options:['Sam, because it was all fresh','Mia, because she spread it out','Both the same, since it was one hour each'],answer:1,reveal:'Most likely Mia. They spent the same time. Mia just spread hers out. That alone helps the words stay.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Spread your study over days, and you remember more than if you do it all at once. This has been tested for over a hundred years, and it still works.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO FOUND IT',text:'A scientist named Ebbinghaus wrote about it in the 1880s. That is more than a hundred years ago. Since then, people have tested it again and again. Young and old. Lots of school subjects. Lots of countries. It keeps coming out the same.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why that is a big deal',text:'Scientists try old results again to check them. Lots of results about the mind do not work the second time. This one does. It has passed its test many, many times.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'This bridge is very old. Big lorries still drive over it, and it holds. An idea tested for a hundred years is like that.'},{kind:'try',text:'Got a test coming up? Split your study into short bits on different days. Same total time. Just spread out.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Which idea about learning should you trust most?',options:['One tested lots of times, on lots of kids, for years','One brand new test that was on the news today','One that a famous person says works for them'],answer:0,why:'One test can be a fluke. An idea that works again and again, for all sorts of people, is much safer to trust.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THREE WEEKS OUT',text:'You have an exam in three weeks and about ten hours to give it. The plan forming in your head is the usual one: clear the final weekend and do all ten hours then, when the pressure will make you focus.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The same total study time produces more retention when it is spread out than when it is packed into one session. It is one of the sturdier findings in psychology.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Massed',items:['Ten hours on the final weekend','Feels intense and productive','Less is still there later']},right:{title:'Spaced',items:['The same ten hours, spread out','Feels less dramatic','More is still there later']},note:'Nothing extra is asked of you. Only the calendar changes.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why believe this one?',text:'Psychology has a serious replication problem: many findings fail when someone runs the study again. The spacing effect was first described in the 1880s and has since been reproduced across ages, subjects and cultures. The effects are substantial, not marginal.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'TESTED BY TRAFFIC',picture:!0,text:'A new bridge comes with a promise from its engineers. An old bridge that has carried lorries for a century comes with a record. The spacing effect has the record.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Spacing works because it means studying more.',reality:'The claim is about the same total time, scheduled differently. That is why it is close to free, and why it is worth noticing that deadline pressure pushes almost everyone the opposite way.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time someone tells you a psychology finding is solid, ask two things: how old is it, and across how many kinds of people has it held?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A colleague shares a striking new study about learning. What is the most useful first question?',options:['Has it been repeated, over years, with different kinds of people?','Was the result large enough to make the news?','Is the researcher well known in the field?','Does it match what I already find works for me?'],answer:0,why:'A single result can fail to repeat. Age and repetition across populations are what make the spacing effect trustworthy.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'FROM THE 1880s ONWARD',text:'Ebbinghaus documented the spacing effect in the 1880s: distributed practice beat massed practice. In the century and more since, it has been tested across materials, ages and domains, and it has held. This piece starts there because everything else it says rests on that record.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Distributing the same study time produces more retention than concentrating it. The claim is narrow, and the evidence for it is unusually strong by the standards of behavioural science.'},{kind:'term',word:'Replication',meaning:'Running a study again, ideally with new people and materials, to see whether the result appears a second time. A finding that keeps replicating is far more credible than one striking result.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why durability is evidence',text:'Much of the psychological literature has replicated poorly. Against that background, a result that has not only held for more than a century but been extended to new populations and materials is worth weighing heavily. And its effect sizes are substantial rather than marginal: it holds and it matters.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'HOW MUCH TO TRUST IT',low:'One striking study, once',high:'A century of replications across ages, subjects and cultures',note:'The spacing effect sits near the right-hand end. Many headline findings sit much nearer the left.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'An old stone bridge under modern lorries, its masonry sound. The test that matters is not how it was designed but what it has carried.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The record secures one narrow claim: spacing beats massing for the same total time. It does not settle why it works, what the best gap is, or how far it extends beyond simple material, and the later ideas show each of those is less certain. Age alone proves nothing either. Old ideas can be wrong; what counts is age plus repeated, varied replication.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'WHAT THE RECORD SUPPORTS',prompt:'Which conclusion does the evidence described here actually support?',options:['Spreading the same study hours out will usually improve retention','Spacing has been shown to be the best way to learn any skill','Because the finding is old, any app built on it must work','The effect is real but too small to be worth changing habits for'],answer:0,why:'The record supports the narrow claim about retention for the same total time, with a substantial effect. The broader claims go past it.'}]}},'spacing-effect-evidence_2':{pictureCaption:'The machine runs perfectly. Four hands point at different parts as the reason. Nobody doubts that it works; the argument is about why.',bridge:'Idea 1 showed that spacing works. This one asks why it works, and finds the answer is still open.',basics:{title:'It works. Nobody is sure why.',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A FRIDGE MAGNET',text:'A magnet sticks to the fridge. You use it every day. You do not need to know how magnets work to put up a drawing. Knowing why is fun. But the magnet sticks either way.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Scientists agree that spacing out your study works. They still argue about why it works. You can use it now, while they argue.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The machine runs fine. Each hand points to a different part and says: that is why it works. They agree it runs. They argue about why.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO GUESSES ABOUT WHY',left:{title:'Guess one: new moments',items:['Each study time is a bit different','A new day, a new mood','So the memory gets more ways in']},right:{title:'Guess two: hard work',items:['After a gap, you forget a bit','Pulling it back takes effort','The effort helps it stick']},note:'Maybe both are true. Scientists have not settled it yet.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why does the why matter?',text:'If we knew why, we could work out the very best gap between study times. We do not know that yet. But you do not have to wait. Spacing helps while the scientists keep arguing.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend says: scientists can\u2019t agree why spacing works, so it is probably not real. What is right?',options:['They agree it works. They argue about why.','Your friend is right. It might not be real.','Nobody has ever tested spacing properly.'],answer:0,why:'Two questions got mixed up. Does it work? Yes, tested many times. Why does it work? Still being argued.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'If scientists can\u2019t explain why something works, the finding must be shaky.',reality:'*Whether* an effect happens and *why* it happens are separate questions. For spacing, the first is settled. The second is still argued over.'},{kind:'idea',text:'That spacing works is settled; why it works is not. Use the effect now, and be wary of anyone who says they have its single best schedule worked out.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Settled',items:['Spaced study beats massed study','It holds across ages and subjects']},right:{title:'Still argued',items:['Why it works','So, exactly how to time the gaps']}},{kind:'explain',title:'Two competing explanations',text:'One says each spaced session happens in a slightly different context, so the material is taken in in more varied ways. The other says that after a gap you have partly forgotten, and the effort of pulling it back is what strengthens the memory. Both may be operating at once.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the argument has stakes',text:'The mechanism decides the best schedule. If effortful retrieval is the key, a gap should be long enough for some forgetting to happen. Until the question is settled, any precise schedule rests partly on a guess about which explanation is right.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The machine runs. Several hands point at several parts as the reason. Nobody at this table disputes that it works.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one thing you are learning and space it out this week. The argument about why does not need to finish first.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A revision app advertises \u201cthe one scientifically optimal schedule\u201d. What is the reasonable response?',options:['Use it for the spacing, but doubt the \u201cone optimal\u201d claim','Trust the claim, since spacing is very well supported','Avoid the app, since nobody knows why spacing works','Wait to study until scientists agree on the mechanism'],answer:0,why:'The effect is reliable, so the spacing is worth having. The mechanism is not agreed, so no schedule can yet claim to be the single best.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'TWO QUESTIONS, NOT ONE',text:'Any effect raises two questions: does it happen, and why? For spacing this piece keeps them sharply apart. The first has more than a century of replication behind it. The second has competing answers and no agreement. Running the two together produces errors in both directions.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A well-supported effect can have a contested mechanism. Rely on the effect now, but distrust any claim to a uniquely optimal schedule, because optimal scheduling depends on the mechanism.'},{kind:'term',word:'Encoding',meaning:'How material is taken into memory when you study it: what it gets linked to, and in what setting and state. The same fact can be encoded differently on different occasions.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The competing accounts',text:'One points to variable encoding contexts: spaced sessions happen in different settings and states, so the material is laid down in more varied ways. The other points to retrieval after partial forgetting: the gap lets some of it fade, and the effort of recovering it strengthens the memory. Both may be operating.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'ONE ACCOUNT, STEP BY STEP',nodes:['You study the material','A gap: some of it fades','Effortful retrieval brings it back','The memory is strengthened'],note:'This is only one of the candidates. Its rival puts the gain in varied encoding contexts, and the two are not exclusive.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The practical rule is sound: a well-replicated effect can be used before it is explained. But the open mechanism has a cost. Because it determines how intervals should be scheduled, claims about precise optimal timing, including those built into software, rest partly on a bet. The opposite error is just as real: treating an unsettled why as doubt about whether.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Several hands, several gears, one running machine. Agreement that it works; no agreement about what makes it work.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'WHICH CLAIM OVERREACHES?',prompt:'Which statement goes beyond what the evidence supports?',options:['This algorithm is the uniquely optimal schedule, derived from how memory works','Spacing reviews out usually beats cramming the same hours','Researchers have not agreed on why spacing works','Retrieval after some forgetting may be part of the explanation'],answer:0,why:'The effect is settled and the mechanism is not. A schedule cannot be shown to be uniquely optimal while the mechanism that would define optimal is still disputed.'}]}},'spacing-effect-evidence_3':{pictureCaption:'Three stones across a narrow stream, close together. Three across a wide river, far apart. The further you have to get, the bigger each gap.',bridge:'Idea 2 said the mechanism would decide the best schedule. Until it is settled, this is the working rule of thumb.',basics:{title:'How long to wait depends on when you need it',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'TWO THINGS TO LEARN',text:'This week you have two jobs. Learn ten spellings for a test in ten days. And learn your times tables, which you will need for years and years.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'Should you go back over both after the same gap?',options:['Yes, the same gap for both','No, wait longer for the times tables'],answer:1,reveal:'The gap should match how long you need to keep it. Need it soon? Short gaps. Need it for years? Long gaps.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The best gap between study times depends on when you need the stuff. Need it soon, use short gaps. Need it for a long time, use longer gaps.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'STEPPING STONES',caption:'Three stones cross a little stream, close together. Three cross a wide river, far apart. A longer way needs bigger gaps.'},{kind:'steps',title:'A rule to find the gap',steps:['Count the days until you need it.','Divide by ten. That is the short end.','Double that. That is the long end.','Go back over it somewhere in between.'],note:'Test in 10 days? Wait 1 to 2 days. Test in 30 days? Wait 3 to 6 days.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Does it have to be exact?',text:'No. Scientists found the best gap changes a bit from test to test. Close enough is fine. The big win is spacing at all, not doing it all in one go.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your class has a quiz in 20 days. Which gap between study times fits the rule?',options:['About 2 to 4 days','About 2 hours','About 15 days'],answer:0,why:'Twenty days divided by ten is 2. Double it and you get 4. So a gap of 2 to 4 days fits.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'TWO DEADLINES',text:'You are preparing a presentation for next Thursday, and starting a language you want to use on a trip in a year. Both should be spaced out. But how far apart? The usual advice does not say.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The best gap between reviews scales with how long you need to remember the material. Decide the horizon first, then set the gap at roughly ten to twenty per cent of it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The rule of thumb',text:'The literature\u2019s rough rule is a gap of about ten to twenty per cent of the time until you need the material. The relationship is roughly proportional: longer horizons call for longer gaps.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Needed in a week',items:['Horizon: 7 days','Gap: a day or so']},right:{title:'Needed in a year',items:['Horizon: 365 days','Gap: roughly 5 to 10 weeks']},note:'Same rule, very different schedules. One fixed gap would be wrong for at least one of them.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'THE SAME RULE IN STONES',caption:'Three stones for a narrow stream, close together; three for a wide river, far apart. The gap grows with the distance to be covered.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why this beats \u201cspace it out\u201d',text:'\u201cSpace it out\u201d gives a direction. The ratio gives a schedule: a date you can put in a calendar. It also shows why a generic schedule is only an approximation. It was fitted to a horizon that may not be yours.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Getting the interval exactly right is where the big gains are.',reality:'The precise ratio varies by study and material, and fine-tuning within a sensible range gains little. Moving from massed to spaced is the large win. Tuning the gap is a refinement.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one thing you are learning. Write down when you need it by, take a tenth to a fifth of that time, and book the first review.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You must know new safety procedures for an audit in 30 days. Which first gap between reviews fits the rule?',options:['About three to six days','About an hour later','About twenty days','None: one long session the night before'],answer:0,why:'Ten to twenty per cent of 30 days is 3 to 6 days. The rule is rough, so anything near that range will do.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A YEAR FROM NOW',prompt:'You need some material in a year\u2019s time. Which first gap between reviews is closest to the rule of thumb?',options:['About a day','About a week','About a month or two'],answer:2,reveal:'The rule is a gap of ten to twenty per cent of the horizon. A tenth of a year is about five weeks; a fifth is about ten. A gap that suits next week\u2019s test is far too short here.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The best gap is not fixed. It scales roughly in proportion to how long you need the material, at about ten to twenty per cent of it, which turns the advice to space things out into a schedule.'},{kind:'term',word:'Retention interval',meaning:'How long you need to keep the material: the time from studying it to the moment you must use it. A test next week has a short one; a skill you want in five years has a long one.'},{kind:'equation',parts:['How long you need it','The ten-to-twenty per cent rule'],result:'Gap between reviews',note:'A week gives a day or so. A year gives roughly five to ten weeks. Five years gives roughly six months to a year.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'PROPORTIONAL',caption:'Three stones over a stream, three over a river: the number is fixed, the gap scales with the crossing. The interval is a fraction of the horizon, not a constant.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What it is arguing against',text:'The common advice is simply to space things out. That supplies a direction and no schedule, and generic schedules fill the gap with defaults. The argument here is that any default approximates a curve that depends on your actual goal. So start from the goal, and derive the gap from it.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The ratio is a rule of thumb, not a constant: the precise figure varies by study and by material. And the gains from optimising within a reasonable range are modest. Getting from massed to spaced is the large win; tuning the interval is a refinement. Agonising over twelve versus fifteen per cent gets the priorities upside down.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Decide how long you need the material first.','Set the gap at roughly a tenth to a fifth of that.','Spacing at all matters far more than the exact gap.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'WHERE THE EFFORT GOES',prompt:'A student spends an evening tuning one subject\u2019s review gap from 12 to 15 per cent, while cramming three other subjects the night before. What does the evidence say?',options:['She is refining a small gain and skipping the large one','She is right: precise intervals hold most of the benefit','Nothing: the ratio rule only applies to software schedules','She should drop the ratio and use one fixed gap everywhere'],answer:0,why:'Moving from massed to spaced is the large win. Tuning within a sensible range is a refinement, so the three crammed subjects matter far more.'}]}},'spacing-effect-evidence_4':{pictureCaption:'Rows of small, simple blocks tested to breaking point. The big, complicated piece beside them has not been tested at all.',bridge:'Ideas 1 to 3 rest on a large body of studies. This one asks what those studies mostly tested.',basics:{title:'Tested on simple things',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'WHAT HAS BEEN TESTED',belief:'Scientists have proved that spacing works for learning anything.',reality:'Most tests used simple things, like word pairs and facts. For big ideas and skills it may help too. There are just fewer tests, so we are less sure.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Most of the proof for spacing comes from simple things, like lists of words. For hard ideas and skills, it probably helps, but the proof is thinner.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Lots of small, simple blocks have been tested on this bench. The big, tricky piece next to them has not been tested at all.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why test simple things?',text:'A word pair is easy to check. You know it or you don\u2019t. Right or wrong. But how well do you *understand* fractions? That is much harder to measure. So scientists test the easy-to-check stuff most.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A RAINCOAT',text:'You test a raincoat under the shower, and you stay dry. Now you know it works in a shower. It will probably work in a storm too. But nobody has checked that yet.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend says spacing is proved to be the best way to learn chess. What is fair to say?',options:['It might help, but most tests used simple facts','Yes, it has been proved for every kind of learning','No, spacing only works for lists of words'],answer:0,why:'Spacing is well tested on simple stuff. For chess it may help, but the proof is thinner. Thinner proof is not the same as no proof.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'BEFORE YOU READ ON',prompt:'What kind of material do most spacing studies use?',options:['Word pairs, vocabulary and simple facts','Complex problem-solving','Physical and creative skills'],answer:0,reveal:'Mostly simple material, because it can be tested cleanly. That is the main limit on how far the finding can be stretched.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The spacing effect is very well established for retaining discrete material such as facts and vocabulary. For complex understanding and skills it is plausible. But it is less thoroughly shown.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the evidence is lopsided',text:'A word pair is either recalled or not. So the result is clean. Conceptual understanding, problem-solving and skill are harder to define as outcomes, let alone measure. So the evidence there is thinner. And the effects are less consistently measured.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Strong evidence',items:['Word pairs','Vocabulary','Simple facts']},right:{title:'Thinner evidence',items:['Conceptual understanding','Problem-solving','Skill acquisition']},note:'Thinner does not mean absent. It means lower confidence.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The simple blocks have been tested to destruction. The complicated assembly beside them has not been tested at all. Confidence should follow the testing.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What to do with this',text:'Use spacing freely for anything made of facts: terms, names, definitions. For learning to analyse or to write, try it too, since it plausibly helps. Just hold the expectation more loosely. And distrust anyone selling a precise schedule for learning to think.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you meet a claim about learning, ask what material the studies used. Then ask how close that is to what you want to learn.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A course says its spaced schedule is proven to build strategic thinking. What is the fair verdict?',options:['Plausible, but the evidence is mostly from simpler material','Proven, since spacing is one of the sturdiest findings','False, since spacing only works for word lists','Irrelevant, since thinking skills cannot be learned'],answer:0,why:'Spacing plausibly helps there, but the strong evidence is for simple material. Proven goes too far one way. False goes too far the other.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE OVERSTATEMENT',belief:'Spacing is a universal law of learning.',reality:'It is very well established for retaining discrete material. Presenting it as a universal law overstates a genuinely good result.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The strength of the spacing evidence is concentrated in simple material. Extending it to complex understanding is plausible but less thoroughly shown, so confidence should fall as the material gets more complex.'},{kind:'term',word:'Generalising',meaning:'Carrying a result from the conditions it was tested in to conditions it was not. Every generalisation is a bet that the untested case behaves like the tested one.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the evidence skews',text:'Studies favour material that can be tested cleanly, and word pairs, vocabulary and facts can. For conceptual learning, problem-solving and skill, outcomes are harder to define, so the evidence is thinner and effects are less consistently measured. The skew comes from what is measurable, not from evidence that spacing fails there.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'CONFIDENCE BY MATERIAL',low:'Retaining discrete facts: very well established',high:'Complex understanding and skill: thinner evidence',note:'Confidence should slide down as you move right. It should not drop to zero.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Simple blocks tested to destruction, and one complex assembly beside them untested. The bench shows exactly where the knowledge runs out.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The limit cuts both ways. Thinner evidence is not evidence of absence, so dismissing spacing for complex learning is as unjustified as calling it universal. The honest position sits between: very well established for discrete material, plausibly applicable beyond it. A precise schedule for learning to think goes past the data.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'WEIGH THE CLAIMS',prompt:'Which summary best matches the evidence as this piece describes it?',options:['Strong for recalling vocabulary; plausible but less shown for complex reasoning','Shown to double how fast people learn to reason well','Proven for word pairs only, with no likely use beyond them','Unable to help complex learning, as its outcomes are hard to define'],answer:0,why:'The evidence is strong for discrete material and thinner, not absent, for complex learning. The other summaries overstate it in one direction or the other.'}]}},'spacing-effect-evidence_5':{pictureCaption:'One hand keeps the card unturned while the other writes the answer from memory. The attempt comes first; the look comes after.',bridge:'Ideas 3 and 4 qualified the evidence. This one strips the finding down to what you actually do.',basics:{title:'Remember it, without looking, now and then',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'AFTER CLASS',text:'Today in class you learned the eight planets in order. You can say them all right now. But will you still know them at the end of term?'},{kind:'idea',text:'Try to remember it with your eyes off the page. Do it after a day, then a week, then a month. That is the whole trick.'},{kind:'steps',title:'The simple plan',steps:['Tomorrow: say the planets without looking.','In a week: try again. Check what you missed.','In a month: try once more.'],note:'Missed some? Look, then try again another day.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO GO BACK',left:{title:'Reading it again',items:['Eyes on the page','Feels easy','Helps a little']},right:{title:'Trying to remember',items:['Eyes off the page','Feels harder','Helps much more']}},{kind:'picture',kicker:'TRY, THEN LOOK',caption:'One hand keeps the answer hidden. The other hand writes it from memory. Look only after you have tried.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Do the days have to be exact?',text:'No. A day, a week and a month are rough. Going back at all matters much more than picking the perfect day. Being a day late is fine. Never going back is the real problem.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one thing you learned this week. Put a note on the calendar to say it from memory in seven days.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Which is the best way to go back over your spellings each week?',options:['Cover them and try to write them out','Read the list again three times','Copy them out while looking at the list'],answer:0,why:'Trying to remember without looking is what makes spacing work best. Reading and copying keep your eyes on the answer.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE COMMON MIX-UP',belief:'Spacing means going back over your notes every so often.',reality:'Spaced re-reading is much weaker than spaced recall. The two are often confused. The strong version is trying to remember, at intervals, without looking.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Try to recall it, at growing intervals, without looking: roughly a day, then a week, then a month. Doing it at all matters far more than the exact schedule.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'ONE ITEM, OVER A MONTH',nodes:['Learn it','After a day: recall it unseen','After a week: recall again','After a month: recall again'],note:'Let the gaps grow as the material becomes reliable.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'TRY BEFORE YOU TURN',caption:'The card stays turned away while the other hand writes the answer from memory. That attempt, repeated at growing gaps, is the whole technique.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What software is for',text:'An app can track the dates. Scheduling is the only part that benefits from automation. The effect does not depend on any one algorithm. And clever interval maths gains little over a simple expanding schedule.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'A REPORT FOR NEXT MONTH',text:'You read a long report on Monday for a meeting in a month. Rereading it on Friday feels productive. Closing it and writing down its five main points from memory feels worse. The second is the one the evidence backs.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one thing you learned this week. Set a reminder to recall it in seven days, before you look at it again.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You have a month to learn the main points of a new policy. Which plan follows the practical version?',options:['Recall the points unseen after a day, a week, then again later','Reread the document carefully every evening for a week','Load it into the app with the most advanced algorithm','Study it hard for a whole day just before you need it'],answer:0,why:'Recall at growing intervals is the whole technique. Rereading is the weak form, and a cleverer algorithm adds little.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'A WEEKEND OF SET-UP',text:'Someone spends a weekend comparing flashcard apps, reading about their algorithms and importing hundreds of cards. Then, in every review, they flip each card, glance at the answer and mark it as known.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The whole finding reduces to one instruction: try to remember it, at intervals, without looking. Everything else is optimisation around a technique that works without it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the evidence makes simple',text:'Review at expanding intervals instead of in one session, and let the intervals grow as the material becomes reliable. The effect does not depend on any particular algorithm, and the gains from sophisticated interval calculation over a simple expanding schedule are small. Software helps with the scheduling, the one part that benefits from automation.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Spaced re-reading',items:['Answer in view','Feels fluent','Much weaker']},right:{title:'Spaced recall',items:['Answer hidden until you try','Feels effortful','Much stronger']},note:'The two are frequently conflated, and no app stops you doing the weak one.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'THE PART ONLY YOU CAN DO',caption:'No algorithm in sight. One hand holds the card back, the other writes from memory: the retrieval the opening scene skipped by flipping straight to the answer.'},{kind:'equation',parts:['Spacing','Recall without looking'],result:'The practical technique',note:'The schedule is the part software can automate. The recall is the part only you can do.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Simple is not the same as complete. The day, week, month schedule is rough, and the evidence behind it is strongest for discrete material, as idea 4 showed. Calling an algorithm\u2019s advantage small is not calling it zero. The risk here is misplaced effort: tuning the machinery while skipping the retrieval that does the work.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Recall it, without looking, at growing intervals.','Doing it at all beats doing it perfectly.','Re-reading on a schedule is the weak version.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'DIAGNOSE THE SET-UP',prompt:'In the opening scene, which change would help the most?',options:['Trying to recall each answer before turning the card','Switching to the app with the most advanced algorithm','Reviewing every card daily instead of at growing gaps','Importing more cards so that more ground is covered'],answer:0,why:'Glancing at the answer turns spaced recall into spaced re-reading. Restoring the attempt matters far more than the algorithm.'}]}}}},3011,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'tao-te-ching_1':{pictureCaption:'People are already crossing the bridge. Its builder packs up at the edge, unthanked. The work is in the bridge, not the credit.',basics:{title:'The best leader you hardly notice',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TWO TEAM CAPTAINS',prompt:'Captain A shouts orders all game, and the team thanks her a lot. Captain B sets things up before the game, then says little. Her team plays well and feels they did it all. Which one is better?',options:['Captain A, because the team thanks her','Captain B, because the team did it'],answer:1,reveal:'An old book says B. When the best leader is done, the team says: we did it ourselves. That is the sign she did her job well.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHERE THIS COMES FROM',text:'The book is the Tao Te Ching. It comes from China, and it is over 2,000 years old. People say a wise man called Laozi wrote it. It is made of 81 short parts, and many are about how to lead.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The best leader does not need to be seen. She sets things up so well that people feel they did it on their own.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'FOUR KINDS OF LEADER',low:'Worst: people hate the leader',high:'Best: people hardly notice the leader',note:'In between, people fear the leader, and then people love the leader. Being loved is good. Being hardly noticed is best.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The bridge is done and people walk across it. The builder packs up at the side. Nobody turns round to say thanks.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'IN CLASS',text:'A good teacher plans a task with care. Each group knows its job. The right things are on each table. Then she steps back, and the groups get on alone. At the end, the class feels proud. Nobody thanks the plan.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You lead a group project. Which plan fits this idea best?',options:['Share out the jobs clearly, then let each person get on','Check what each person is doing every ten minutes','Do the hard parts yourself so they are done right'],answer:0,why:'Set things up, then step back, and the group does it. If you do it all for them, they just wait for you.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE WEEK SHE WAS AWAY',text:'A manager joins every thread and makes every small call. The team is grateful. Then she takes a week off, and nothing moves, because every decision was waiting for her.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The text ranks leaders: despised, then feared, then loved, and best of all, barely noticed. When the best leader\u2019s work is done, the people say they did it themselves.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why being loved ranks lower',text:'Incentives. A leader who needs visible gratitude keeps stepping in where nobody needs her, because stepping in is what earns thanks. Her team becomes grateful and dependent in equal measure. The thanks and the dependence grow together.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Works on the credit',items:['Makes every small call','Visible in every thread','Thanked often','Team stalls when she is away']},right:{title:'Works on the conditions',items:['Hires well and sets priorities','Removes a blocker weeks early','Rarely thanked','Team ships without her']},note:'The second manager is working just as hard. You simply cannot see where.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'So the best leader stays out of the way and does nothing.',reality:'The text is not recommending absence. The effort is real. It goes into the conditions (who is hired, what matters, what is in the way) rather than into being seen.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The bridge carries people who will never meet its builder. That is what the work was for.'},{kind:'try',text:'This week, do one thing for your team that nobody will be able to trace to you: remove a blocker, fix a process. Notice how it feels to get no thanks.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'After a hard launch, which team\u2019s reaction shows the leader the text ranks highest?',options:['\u201cHonestly, we just got on with it and it worked.\u201d','\u201cWe threw her a party for saving the launch.\u201d','\u201cWe worked late because nobody wanted her review.\u201d','\u201cWe followed her plan exactly, step by step.\u201d'],answer:0,why:'Gratitude means the help was visible. The text ranks highest the leader whose help was built so far into the conditions that the team felt the success was its own.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'CHINA, WARRING STATES',text:'The Tao Te Ching took shape in an age of rival Chinese states and rival advisers telling their rulers how to govern. Much of it is addressed to a ruler. Tradition credits a sage called Laozi, though whether one person wrote it is doubtful.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Of four kinds of ruler, the one the people barely know is there ranks highest, above the loved, the feared and the despised. His success shows up as the people\u2019s sense that they did it themselves.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Chapter seventeen, top to bottom',steps:['The people barely know he is there.','The people love and praise him.','The people fear him.','The people despise him.'],note:'Most advice to rulers aims at the second rung. This chapter puts something above it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The case against being loved',text:'Love has to be earned in view. A ruler who wants it must keep acting where he can be seen, and the people learn to wait for him. The barely noticed ruler acts early and out of sight, and is sparing with his words, so what the people experience is their own competence.'},{kind:'term',word:'Ziran',meaning:'Often translated *so of itself*. It is what the people say at the end of chapter seventeen: it happened of its own accord. The book treats this as the sign that things were done well.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A finished bridge, crossed by people who will never ask who built it. Chapter seventeen counts that as success.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Invisibility can hide neglect as easily as skill: a leader nobody notices may simply be doing nothing. It also leaves good work unprotected, since credit decides budgets and promotions. And a crisis often needs a leader who is seen. The chapter describes a peak of skill, not a reason to disappear.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'A manager reads chapter seventeen, then stops setting priorities or answering questions, so as to be barely noticed. What has gone wrong?',options:['He copied the invisibility and skipped the work on conditions that produces it','Nothing: being unseen is the whole of what the chapter teaches','He should have aimed to be loved, which the chapter ranks highest','He was right to step back, but did it too quickly for his team'],answer:0,why:'Being barely noticed is the result of good arrangement, not the method. Absence without the work beneath it is only absence.'}]}},'tao-te-ching_2':{pictureCaption:'Every spoke is finished, but the hand rests in the empty hub. That hole is the part that lets the wheel turn.',bridge:'Idea 1 put the leader\u2019s work where nobody sees it. This one finds the useful part of things in what is not there at all.',basics:{title:'The empty part does the work',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'POURING A DRINK',text:'You pour juice into a cup. Think about which part of the cup you are using. Not the sides. Not the bottom. You are using the empty space in the middle, where the juice goes.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The useful part of a thing is often the empty part. A cup works because it is hollow.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The hand rests in the hole at the middle of the wheel. The rod the wheel spins on goes there. With no hole, the wheel can\u2019t turn.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'THREE THINGS IN AN OLD BOOK',text:'The Tao Te Ching is a very old book from China. It gives three examples. A wheel turns because of the hole in its middle. A pot holds things because it is hollow. A room is useful because of the space inside its walls.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'YOUR WEEK',text:'Think of a week full of clubs, homework and trips. There is no gap left. Then a friend needs help, or you catch a cold. A week with some empty time can take it. A full week just spills over.'},{kind:'try',text:'Look at your plans for this weekend. Leave one hour empty on purpose. See what it gets used for.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend packs her school bag so full that her lunch won\u2019t fit. What went wrong?',options:['She filled the empty part, and that was the useful part','She has a bag that is far too small','She put the wrong things in first'],answer:0,why:'A bag is useful because of the space inside. Filling all of it left no room to use.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A POTTER\u2019S QUESTION',prompt:'A potter can make a cup thicker, heavier and more solid. Where does its usefulness live?',options:['In the clay, which is what the potter made','In the hollow, which the potter left out'],answer:1,reveal:'Fill the cup with clay and you have more cup and no vessel. The function lives entirely in what was left out.'},{kind:'idea',text:'We work with what is there, but what we use is what is not. The useful part of a thing is often its empty part.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'CHAPTER ELEVEN',left:{title:'What we make',items:['Thirty spokes and a hub','Clay walls','Walls, doors and windows']},right:{title:'What we use',items:['The empty centre of the hub','The hollow inside the pot','The space of the room']},note:'Three objects, one pattern. The solid part gets the praise. The gap does the job.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Not mystical',text:'In each example someone made the solid part, and the solid part is what gets valued. But the function lives in what the maker left out. The verse is an observation about objects, and it transfers to anything that has to hold something.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'A FULL CALENDAR',text:'Apply it to a week. The meetings are the clay. The capacity to respond, think or absorb a problem is the hollow. A calendar with no space is a cup filled in: more substance, no use. The first surprise of the week has nowhere to go.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A perfect wheel, and the hand goes straight to the hole. Every spoke points at the part that does the work.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before adding to a plan, ask what the gap in it is doing. You may be about to fill the working part.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A team\u2019s week has one empty afternoon, and a manager wants to fill it with training. What does the cup argument ask first?',options:['What that empty afternoon is quietly absorbing at the moment','Whether the training is good enough to justify the time','Whether the team would actually enjoy the session','How to shorten the training so it fits the afternoon'],answer:0,why:'Gaps do work. Before filling one, find out what it is doing; it may be where every surprise currently lands.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',word:'You and wu',meaning:'Chinese for *having* (or being) and *not having* (non-being). Chapter eleven ends by setting them side by side: what is there gives the benefit, what is not there gives the use.'},{kind:'idea',text:'We shape what is there, but we use what is not. In a wheel, a pot or a room, the value is credited to the made part, while the function lives in the gap the making left.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Three objects, one structure',text:'Thirty spokes share one hub, and the wheel works by its empty centre. Clay is shaped into a pot, which works by its hollow. Doors and windows are cut for a room, which works by its space. In each, the solid part is what got made and admired.'},{kind:'explain',title:'A pattern in the book',text:'The same move recurs. The valley receives because it is low. The bellows is empty yet never exhausted. The Way itself is pictured as an empty vessel that use never fills. Again and again the book prefers what is open to what is full.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE MODERN CASE',text:'A schedule is the cleanest example. The meetings are the clay; slack, the room to absorb a problem, is the hollow. Fill every slot and you have more schedule and less use. The first surprise has no space to land, so it lands on the next thing.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Every spoke points at the empty hub. The made part exists to frame the useful part.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Emptiness is only useful when something solid shapes it. A hollow with no walls is not a cup, and a calendar of nothing is drift, not slack. The verse does not say the solid part is worthless; it says the two depend on each other and we tend to see only one. Read as a case for doing less, it forgets the clay.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which reading of chapter eleven is the misuse?',options:['Empty space is good in itself, so less of everything is always better','A gap in a plan may be doing work that is easy to overlook','The solid part matters because it shapes the space we use','A room\u2019s value lies partly in what its walls leave open'],answer:0,why:'The verse needs both halves. The clay is what makes the hollow possible; emptiness without a frame is not a vessel.'}]}},'tao-te-ching_3':{pictureCaption:'One drop at a time, falling in the same place. The terracotta channel is what soft water did to hard stone.',bridge:'Idea 2 praised what is empty. This one praises what is soft.',basics:{title:'Soft water beats hard stone',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'UNDER THE DRIP',picture:!0,text:'Look at an old stone step under a drip. Over many years, a smooth dip has formed in it. No hammer made that. Only drops of water, falling on the same spot again and again.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Water is soft, but in the end it beats hard rock. It does not push. It goes round and keeps coming back. Steady and gentle can outlast force.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Pushing hard',items:['Shove the rock','Get tired fast','The rock stays put']},right:{title:'Like water',items:['Go round the rock','Keep coming back','The rock wears away']}},{kind:'example',kicker:'IN AN ARGUMENT',text:'Your friend says homework is a waste of time. If you just say *no it isn\u2019t*, she argues harder. Instead, say her point back: *you mean it eats your free time?* She says yes. Then ask: *what about tests?* Now she is thinking, not fighting.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Water wins because being gentle is being good.',reality:'That is not the point. The Tao Te Ching, an old book from China, says soft and steady *works*. Force runs out. Water keeps going.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you disagree with someone, say their view back to them first. Wait for them to say yes. Then ask one question.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your little sister won\u2019t share a toy. Which way is most like water?',options:['Ask what she likes about it, then offer to swap turns','Grab it, since you are bigger and stronger','Shout until a grown-up comes to sort it out'],answer:0,why:'Water goes round and keeps at it. Grabbing is force, and force makes her hold on tighter.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE MEETING',text:'You think a colleague\u2019s plan is wrong and you say so, directly. By the end of the meeting she is defending it harder than when she walked in. You did not change her mind. You gave her a position to hold.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Nothing is softer than water, and nothing is better at wearing down the hard. Persistence along the line of least resistance outlasts force applied against the grain.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why pushing hardens',text:'Push straight against someone\u2019s position and they now have to defend it in front of you. Their pride is attached to it. Restate it fairly, concede what is true, then move sideways, and nothing has been attacked that they need to protect.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Disagreeing like water',steps:['Restate their view so well they say yes.','Concede the part that is true.','Ask about one case it does not cover, or a cost it does not count.','Stop, and let the question work.']},{kind:'picture',caption:'The drip never pushed. It fell in the same place, again and again, until the stone gave way.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'BEYOND ARGUMENTS',text:'The same shape holds for habits and institutions. A change sticks when it is placed where the system is already soft: the meeting everyone already dislikes, the form nobody defends. Push against what people are proud of and it pushes back.'},{kind:'try',text:'In your next disagreement, do not push back. Restate their view so well they say yes, then ask about the one case it does not cover. Watch what moves.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A friend insists one diet is the only healthy way to eat. Which approach is most like water?',options:['Agree on what the diet gets right, then ask about a case it misses','Send them three articles showing that the diet is wrong','Avoid the subject for good, since it will never go anywhere','Tell them firmly and clearly that they are being unreasonable'],answer:0,why:'It attacks nothing they need to defend and still keeps returning to the question. The articles and the firm word are force; avoiding it never comes back.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'CHAPTER SEVENTY-EIGHT',text:'Near the end of the book, the text says that nothing in the world is softer or weaker than water, yet nothing beats it at attacking the hard and strong. Then it adds a dry remark: everyone knows this, and nobody acts on it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The soft overcomes the hard, not by being better but by lasting longer. Water goes where the ground allows, comes back, and in time the rock is gone.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW A RIVER BEATS A ROCK',nodes:['Water meets rock','It goes round, not through','It returns, again and again','The rock wears away'],note:'No step involves force. Every step involves time.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Every drop lands where the last one did. The channel is not force; it is time spent in one place.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Not a moral claim',text:'The chapter is not saying gentleness is nicer. It is a claim about what wins. Force has to be supplied continuously and runs out. Persistence along the line of least resistance costs little each time, so it can go on longer than the hard thing can stand.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'APPLIED TO A DISAGREEMENT',text:'Arguing head-on makes the other person defend their view in front of you. Restating it fairly, conceding what is true, then turning to the case it does not cover attacks nothing, so there is nothing to protect. The application is a modern one, but it has the river\u2019s shape.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Water works on the timescale of rivers. Some situations do not have it: a decision due on Friday, a harm happening now. Yielding is also easily confused with avoidance, but water returns and avoidance does not. The chapter is about persistence, not passivity. A river with no flow wears nothing away.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'WATER, OR A MISREADING?',prompt:'Which of these is water as chapter seventy-eight means it?',options:['A reformer who keeps returning, year after year, to the one rule nobody defends','A colleague who never raises a concern, so as to keep things gentle','A negotiator who gives way at once, because softness always wins','A manager who forces a change through fast, before resistance forms'],answer:0,why:'The text pairs softness with persistence. Yielding that never returns wears nothing away, and force is the opposite of the point.'}]}},'tao-te-ching_4':{pictureCaption:'A bowl filled past its rim, carried with care, and still a trail of drips behind it. Stopping a little short would have spilled nothing.',bridge:'The cup from idea 2 returns. Its hollow is there to be filled, but filled to the brim it spills.',basics:{title:'Know when to stop',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A CUP FULL TO THE TOP',prompt:'You fill a cup right to the brim. Then you carry it across the room. What happens?',options:['It spills on the way','It gets there fine','It depends on the cup'],answer:0,reveal:'It spills. A cup filled a bit less would get there. Here, more was worse.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Some things get worse if you keep going too long. Part of being good at something is knowing when to stop.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A bowl filled past the top and carried across the room. Look at the drips on the floor. A bit less, and none would spill.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'THREE PICTURES',text:'The Tao Te Ching is an old book from China. It gives three pictures of this. A cup filled to the brim spills. A knife made too sharp goes blunt sooner. A house full of gold can\u2019t be kept safe.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'ONE MORE BIT',nodes:['Your drawing gets better','You add more and more','It starts to look messy','You wish you had stopped'],note:'The hard part: adding more still *feels* like making it better, right up until it isn\u2019t.'},{kind:'explain',title:'So what can you do?',text:'Decide when to stop before you start. When you are calm, you can see it. In the middle, one more bit always seems like a good idea.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before your next drawing or piece of homework, decide what *done* looks like. When you get there, stop.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your sandcastle is great. You want to add one more tall tower. What does this idea say?',options:['Ask if one more tower could make it fall','Add it, because more towers is always better','Knock it down and build a new one'],answer:0,why:'More is not always better. Past a point, the next tower is the one that brings it down.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE FOURTH EDIT',text:'It is the fourth pass on the report. It was good after the second. Each pass adds a clause, a caveat, a qualification, and each feels like an improvement. The reader will finish it less clear than draft two.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Every good quality has a point past which more of it works against itself. Treat that point as something to recognise, not a limit to push through.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Filled past the rim and carried with care, the bowl still leaves a trail across the floor. The extra was not more water delivered. It was water lost.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why nobody sees the top',text:'The direction of improvement stays the same up to the peak and past it. More sharpening is still sharpening. Nothing announces the top of the curve, which is why it is usually found afterwards, from the wreckage.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Before the peak',items:['More effort, better result','Feels like progress']},right:{title:'After the peak',items:['More effort, worse result','Still feels like progress']},note:'The feeling is the same on both sides. That is the whole difficulty.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The text\u2019s answer',text:'Do not trust yourself to notice in the moment. Build the stop into the practice. The chapter ends by saying: when the work is done, withdraw, for that is the way of heaven. Decide the stopping point while calm, because the moment will always argue for one more.'},{kind:'try',text:'Name the point at which more effort on your current task would make it worse. Write it down before you get there.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You are preparing a presentation. When is the best time to decide it is finished?',options:['Before you start, while you are calm about it','When it feels as good as it can possibly get','When you have run out of time to change it','When a colleague finally tells you to stop'],answer:0,why:'In the moment, one more change always looks like improvement. A stopping point chosen in advance is the only one the moment cannot talk you out of.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Chapter nine is against ambition: want less, do less.',reality:'Nothing in the verse condemns fullness, sharpness or wealth. Each image describes an optimum passed. The failure is continuing past the point where the quality starts working against itself.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Holding a vessel full to the brim is worse than stopping in time. Past a certain point, fullness, sharpness and wealth turn against whoever holds them.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The images in order',picture:!0,text:'The brimming vessel spills. The keenest blade loses its edge first. A hall full of gold and jade cannot be guarded. Wealth and rank held with pride bring their own ruin. Then the close: when the work is done, withdraw. That is the way of heaven.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHY THE PEAK IS MISSED',nodes:['Each step improves things','So you take another','One step passes the peak','It still feels like improving'],loop:!0,note:'The signal you would use to stop looks the same on both sides of the top, so the loop does not end by itself.'},{kind:'explain',title:'A rule, not a perception',text:'So the text puts the stop into the practice rather than trusting the moment. You decide while calm where *done* lies, and then honour it. The only reliable stopping point is one chosen before the argument for one more begins.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Some work has no early peak: skill, savings and care often keep improving long after they feel like enough. A rule to stop early can become an excuse to stop when it gets hard. And a stopping point set in advance can simply be wrong. The verse names a risk to price, not a ceiling on all effort.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What are you improving at the moment that may already be past its best? What would tell you?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'DIAGNOSE IT',prompt:'A founder keeps adding features because each one tested well on its own. The product grows harder to use. What does chapter nine diagnose?',options:['Each step looked like progress, so nobody saw the peak go by','The features were badly made and needed more testing','Ambition itself is the error the chapter condemns','The team stopped too early and needs more features'],answer:0,why:'The verse is about passing an optimum unnoticed, because each step still points the same way. Nothing in it condemns building.'}]}},'tao-te-ching_5':{pictureCaption:'The current moves the boat; one hand on the oar steers it. The rope for dragging lies coiled and unused.',bridge:'Idea 3 used water for arguments. This one makes water the model for every kind of effort.',basics:{title:'Steer, don\u2019t drag',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'A COMMON MIX-UP',belief:'This old book tells you to do nothing.',reality:'No. The Tao Te Ching says don\u2019t *force* things. Steering a boat down a river is easy. Dragging it along the bank is hard. Both move the boat.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Don\u2019t fight against the way things are going. Find a way that works with them. Then you get more done with less push.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The river moves the boat along. One hand on the oar is enough to steer. The rope for dragging lies unused.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'PRACTISING THE PIANO',left:{title:'Dragging',items:['Practise late when you are tired','Fight about it every night','Stops when nobody nags']},right:{title:'Steering',items:['Practise after breakfast, when you like it','Leave the music out, ready','Keeps going by itself']}},{kind:'explain',title:'A simple test',text:'Ask this: does it keep working when I stop pushing? If it falls apart when you look away, you were dragging. If it keeps going, you found the current.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one thing you keep having to force. Ask: is there an easier way round?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You want to drink more water. Which plan is steering, not dragging?',options:['Keep a full bottle on your desk where you can see it','Set ten alarms a day to tell you to drink','Make yourself drink a whole jug at bedtime'],answer:0,why:'The bottle keeps working even when you forget. The alarms and the jug need you to keep pushing.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'term',word:'Wu wei',meaning:'Chinese, usually translated *non-action*. In the text it means acting without striving: not working against the grain of a situation. It does not mean doing nothing.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The sage acts without forcing, and nothing is left undone. The difference is between steering a boat and dragging it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What forcing costs',text:'Forcing works against the grain. So it needs constant effort to keep it going. It often shows faster results, but it needs you forever. The unforced version is slower to show, and then runs without you.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The current does the moving and one hand does the steering. The rope for hauling the boat is there, and nobody needs it.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'A TEAM HABIT',text:'A manager wants weekly written updates. Version one: she chases each person every Friday. It stops the week she is away. Version two: the update becomes the first item in a meeting people already attend. They fill it in together. Nobody is chased, and it carries on without her.'},{kind:'scale',low:'Dragging: it stops when you stop',high:'Steering: it keeps going without you',note:'Most exhausting work sits at the left end. That is because the right-hand option was never looked for.'},{kind:'try',text:'Ask of a hard task: is this truly hard, or am I pushing against something I could go around?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Which of these arrangements is acting without forcing?',options:['A reading habit that carries on in weeks you forget to think about it','A fitness plan that works as long as you check the app every day','A team rule that holds for as long as the manager reminds everyone','A diet that succeeds on the days you happen to feel strong-willed'],answer:0,why:'The test is whether it holds when you stop attending to it. The other three all need someone pushing.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TRANSLATING WU WEI',prompt:'Wu wei is often rendered *non-action*. Which reading does the text support?',options:['Doing nothing and letting things happen','Acting without forcing, so nothing is left undone','Withdrawing from public life'],answer:1,reveal:'The text is careful: the sage acts without striving, and nothing is left undone. Real inaction would leave things undone. The claim is that unforced action leaves nothing.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Act along the grain of a situation, not against it. Action that has to be forced needs you forever; action that fits the situation keeps going when you stop.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Water again',text:'Water is the book\u2019s recurring image here too. It achieves more than force does by going where the ground allows, and wears down stone without ever pushing. Idea 3 used it for disagreement. Here it is the model for effort of every kind: put it where the situation already wants to go.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The boat is moved by the current and steered by one hand. The coiled rope is the other way of moving a boat.'},{kind:'explain',title:'A test you can apply',text:'Does the arrangement hold when you stop attending to it? One that collapses the moment you look away was being forced. One that continues was set along the grain. Most management, habit-building and persuasion fails on exactly this line.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Some things worth doing run against the grain and stay that way: stopping an injustice, enforcing a safety rule, learning something hard. They need sustained effort, and calling that effort forcing excuses quitting. As advice to rulers, an ideal of light intervention can also slide into neglect.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Wu wei is unforced action, not inaction.','Forced arrangements need you forever; unforced ones run without you.','Some right things stay hard. Effort is not the same as forcing.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'CORRECT THE MISREADING',prompt:'A manager decides to practise wu wei by simply no longer running the weekly review, to see what happens. What is the best reply?',options:['Wu wei means redesigning it so it runs without pushing, not just stopping','Right: the text says things are best left to run themselves, unattended','Wrong: the text says a leader should keep firm control at all times','Right, as long as the team is told about it well in advance'],answer:0,why:'Unforced is not the same as absent. The work goes into setting things along the grain, so that they then run without you.'}]}},'tao-te-ching_6':{pictureCaption:'The label is in the hand and the jug sits there unmarked. Whatever the label ends up saying, it will not be the jug.',bridge:'These are the book\u2019s opening lines. They warn that everything in the earlier lessons is a pointer, not the final word.',basics:{title:'A name is not the thing',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE NEW GIRL',text:'Someone tells you the new girl is *the shy one*. Now, each time she is quiet, you think: see, shy. You stop looking at her. You only see the name.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A name helps you point at something. But once you have the name, you can stop looking at the thing. The name is not the thing.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'HOW AN OLD BOOK BEGINS',text:'The Tao Te Ching is a book from China, over 2,000 years old. Its first lines are odd. They say the Way you can talk about is not the lasting Way. A name you can say is not the lasting name. So the book warns you: its own words only point.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A MAP',text:'A map of your town is useful. But you can\u2019t swim in the blue line that means a river. The map points to the town. It is not the town.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The hand holds a label. The jug is plain. Whatever the label says, the jug stays just what it is.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of a label you use for someone, like *the funny one*. Next time you see them, look at what they do, not the label.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your team is called *the best in the league*. Now you keep losing. What would this idea say?',options:['Look at how you are playing, not at the name','Keep the name, so you will play like the best','Pick a new name and the losing will stop'],answer:0,why:'The name was a pointer. When it stops fitting, look at the real thing: how the team plays.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE STRATEGY SLIDE',text:'A team names its strategy *customer first*. Two years on, every proposal is argued in terms of the phrase. Nobody checks whether customers are actually better served. The name is being maintained; the thing it pointed at has stopped being examined.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The way that can be spoken is not the constant way. Once something is named, the name tends to replace the thing as the object of attention.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'The opening means nothing true can be said, so why speak at all?',reality:'It is not a claim that nothing can be said; the book goes on for eighty more chapters. It is a warning about what happens after something is said.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why names take over',text:'A name is easier to hold than the thing. It can be repeated, agreed with and defended. The thing it pointed at keeps changing and has to be looked at again. So attention drifts to the easier object.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Maintaining the name',items:['Defending the label','Arguing about the phrase','Asking \u201cis this on-brand?\u201d']},right:{title:'Examining the thing',items:['Asking if it still fits','Checking what actually happens','Asking \u201cis this true?\u201d']}},{kind:'picture',caption:'A label, not yet tied on, held beside a plain jug. Useful for pointing, and never the jug.'},{kind:'try',text:'When you finally find the right word for something difficult, notice that the word is now doing some of your thinking for you.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Someone has called themselves \u201ca morning person\u201d for years, and lately dreads every early start. What does the idea suggest?',options:['Check whether the label still fits, instead of defending it','Keep the label, since a settled identity gives life stability','Drop every label, since no word is ever accurate','Try harder to live up to the label they chose'],answer:0,why:'The label was useful once. The warning is against maintaining it after it has stopped describing anything.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE FIRST LINES',text:'The book opens by undermining itself. The way that can be spoken of is not the constant way; the name that can be named is not the constant name. Everything in the eighty chapters that follow is offered knowing it is approximate.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Whatever can be named is not the thing itself. A name is useful for pointing, and becomes a hazard once it is what you attend to instead.'},{kind:'term',word:'Dao (Tao)',meaning:'Literally *way* or *path*, and also *to speak*; the first line plays on both. In the text it stands for the way things are and unfold, which it says no name can capture.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How the book practises it',text:'The text keeps changing metaphor: water, the valley, the uncarved block, the infant. Each is allowed to be partial, which stops any of them hardening into the answer. The opening is a method, not only a disclaimer.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The label is ready; the jug is unmarked. Tie it on and people will read the label, not the jug.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Against fixed names',text:'Confucius taught that order starts with using names correctly, so that a ruler acts as a ruler and a son as a son. The Tao Te Ching is often read as pushing the other way: a fixed name invites people to maintain the name.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Taken too far, this becomes an excuse for vagueness: if nothing can be pinned down, nothing can be tested. People need names to coordinate at all. The opening is among the most abused lines in the book, quoted to wave away any definition. Its warning concerns attention after naming, not naming itself.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A company\u2019s first value is \u201cmove fast\u201d. Which response fits the opening chapter best?',options:['Ask now and then what the phrase was meant to protect, and whether it still does','Remove all written values, since words cannot capture what really matters','Define the phrase more tightly so that nobody can ever misread it','Repeat it more often, so that it guides more of what people do'],answer:0,why:'The chapter allows names but warns that they replace the thing. The cure is to keep looking past the phrase at what it pointed to.'}]}},'tao-te-ching_7':{pictureCaption:'After the storm, the stalk that grew above the rest is bent double. The ones just below it still stand: the front takes the weather.',bridge:'Like the barely noticed leader in idea 1, the third treasure keeps out of the conspicuous place on purpose.',basics:{title:'Why first is not always best',beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THREE TREASURES',text:'The Tao Te Ching, an old book from China, names three treasures. These are three things worth keeping. One: be kind. Two: don\u2019t waste. Three: don\u2019t rush to be first. The first two sound normal. The third sounds odd.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'Why would a wise book say don\u2019t rush to be first?',options:['Being first often costs a lot','Being first is rude to others','Winners are never happy'],answer:0,reveal:'The book is not talking about manners. The front spot takes the most effort. Everyone stares at it. And it is the easiest one to knock over.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'After the storm, the tallest stalk is bent right over. The ones just below it are still standing.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Being first is not always worth it. The front spot costs the most and gets the most blame. Sometimes second is nearly as good, and much cheaper.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'ON BIKES',text:'Ride your bike into a strong wind with friends. The rider at the front pushes through all of it. The riders just behind save lots of effort. The leader gets tired fast.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Going first',items:['Takes the most effort','Everyone sees your mistakes','Others aim to beat you']},right:{title:'Going just behind',items:['Saves effort','You learn from their mistakes','You can still do well']},note:'But sometimes first really matters. If there is one seat left, only the first one gets it.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your class is trying a new game that nobody knows. Which choice fits the third treasure?',options:['Watch one round first, then join in','Always go first so people think you are brave','Never play, so you can\u2019t make mistakes'],answer:0,why:'You still play. You just let someone else find the tricky bits first. That is not fear. It is learning cheaply.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Not daring to be first is a failure of nerve.',reality:'The text presents it as a practical advantage. Being first means being the exposed position: the target, the precedent, the one whose mistakes are public and hard to correct.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The book\u2019s three treasures are compassion, frugality, and not daring to be first in the world. The third treats restraint as a strong position, not a timid one.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What leading costs',text:'The first mover pays for everything nobody knew yet: the dead ends, the public mistakes, the full attention of rivals. And much of the chase for first place is for status rather than advantage. The status version carries all the exposure and none of the return.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The storm found the stalk that stood above the rest and bent it double. Those just below it are untouched. The exposed position is the one that pays.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'First',items:['Pays to find the dead ends','Mistakes are public','Becomes the target for rivals']},right:{title:'A close second',items:['Learns from the first\u2019s dead ends','Mistakes are mostly private','Chooses where to compete']}},{kind:'explain',title:'When first really wins',text:'There is a real limit. In some situations the first mover takes a lasting position and the cautious never catch up: a network everyone has already joined, a standard everyone has adopted. The treasure is a reason to price the lead, not to refuse it every time.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before taking the leading position on something, price what leading costs, and check whether second is cheaper and nearly as good.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A team rushes to be the first in the company to adopt a new tool, mainly to look innovative. What would the third treasure ask?',options:['Whether being first buys anything beyond being seen to be first','Whether the team is brave enough to take the risk','Whether it can move fast enough to stay ahead of rivals','Whether the tool is cheap enough to be worth the risk'],answer:0,why:'The text\u2019s target is primacy chased for status. Asking what the lead actually buys separates the advantage from the exposure.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',word:'Bu gan wei tianxia xian',meaning:'*Not daring to be ahead of the world.* The third of the three treasures in chapter sixty-seven, after compassion (*ci*) and frugality (*jian*).'},{kind:'idea',text:'Refusing the leading position can be a source of strength. The front is expensive, conspicuous and fragile, and the text offers restraint as a position to compete from, not only as a virtue.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How the chapter argues',text:'Each treasure is paired with what it makes possible: compassion gives courage, frugality gives room to be generous, and not daring to be first makes one able to lead. Then the warning: courage without compassion, or pushing to the front while abandoning the rear, ends in ruin.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'TWO REASONS TO LEAD',low:'Taking the front for status',high:'Taking the front because it pays',note:'The chapter\u2019s target is the left end, where you buy all the exposure and none of the return.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the front is fragile',text:'The leader is the precedent and the target. Every rival measures against them, and every error is public and hard to undo. A close second learns from those errors at no cost, and keeps the freedom to choose where to compete.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'After the storm, the one stalk that rose above the field is bent double, while the stalks a little lower stand untouched. Its height bought exposure, and exposure was the cost.'},{kind:'limits',text:'In some situations the first mover takes a lasting position and the cautious party never catches up. Where a market tips to one winner, or a standard locks in, second can mean nothing. And the advice was written for rulers in a very different world; moved wholesale into modern competition, it can become an excuse for timidity.'},{kind:'recap',points:['The three treasures: compassion, frugality, not daring to be first.','The front is costly, exposed and fragile.','Price the lead. Sometimes it is worth it.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HONEST OBJECTION',prompt:'Which case is the strongest objection to the third treasure?',options:['A market where the first product everyone adopts becomes very hard to displace','A race in which the runner sitting in second place saves energy','A team that waits to watch a rival\u2019s launch before building its own','A student who lets classmates ask the first questions in a new subject'],answer:0,why:'The other three show the treasure working. Where the first mover locks in a lasting position, staying back costs the whole prize.'}]}},'tao-te-ching_8':{pictureCaption:'The shelves were emptied by giving, and people still reach towards this stall. The one full bowl is what came back.',bridge:'Idea 4\u2019s hall of gold and jade returns: what you hold has to be guarded. This one asks what giving does instead.',basics:{title:'Some things grow when you give them away',beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'If you give something away, you always have less of it.',reality:'Not always. Teach a friend a card trick and you still know it. Now two of you know it. And your friend may teach you one back.'},{kind:'idea',text:'An old book from China, the Tao Te Ching, says a wise person does not hoard, or keep piling things up. The more they do for others, the more they have. This is not true of money. It is true of skills, trust and friends.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why keeping lots is hard work',text:'Everything you keep needs looking after. A huge pile of toys needs space and tidying. It needs guarding from your little brother. The more you keep, the more time the keeping takes.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The stall is empty because it gave things away. But people still reach for it. They trust it. And the bowl is full again.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'AT SCHOOL',text:'You are good at fractions. You could keep it to yourself and be the only one who gets them. Or you could help your friend. Now she can help others too. And when you are stuck on spelling, she helps you.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Which of these do you still have after you give it away?',options:['Knowing how to do a hard maths trick','The last slice of birthday cake','Your pocket money for the week'],answer:0,why:'Cake and money run out. A skill does not. When you share it, it spreads, and help often comes back.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE ONLY ONE WHO KNOWS',text:'One engineer is the only person who understands the billing system. It makes her hard to replace. It also means she cannot take a holiday, is pulled into every incident, and spends her weeks guarding what she knows instead of building anything new.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The sage does not accumulate. The more he does for others, the more he has; the more he gives, the greater his abundance. As economics it is false. As a claim about attention, it is exact.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Everything held needs guarding',text:'An earlier chapter says a hall full of gold and jade cannot be protected. Past a point, the protecting becomes the occupation and your holdings set your schedule. The American writer Henry David Thoreau reached much the same conclusion in *Walden*, some two thousand years later.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why giving compounds',text:'Capability given away keeps working when you are not there. What you taught others keeps producing; what you hoarded needs you present to be worth anything. Standing, trust and usefulness collect around the person whose help circulates.'},{kind:'flow',nodes:['You teach what you know','Others can do it without you','You are trusted, and freed up','You learn something new to teach'],loop:!0,note:'Hoarding runs the other way: the more only you know, the less you can leave it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'An emptied stall that people still reach for. What it gave away is why they keep coming.'},{kind:'try',text:'Give away the useful thing you have been holding as an advantage, and watch what comes back that you could not have asked for.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A consultant keeps her best method secret so clients must always hire her. By the closing chapter\u2019s logic, what is the cost?',options:['The method is only worth anything while she is in the room','She will eventually run out of new clients to sell it to','Secrecy is dishonest, and her clients will find her out','Her fees will have to rise to cover the extra risk'],answer:0,why:'What is hoarded needs its owner present. Taught, the method would keep working without her, and her standing would grow with it.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE LAST CHAPTER',text:'The book\u2019s final chapter closes on the sage. He does not accumulate. The more he does for others, the more he has; the more he gives to others, the greater his abundance. It is a paradox in form and an observation in practice.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Holding things costs the attention needed to guard them, while what is given away keeps working without you. So the one who does not accumulate ends up with more of what counts.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Hoarded',items:['Needs guarding','Needs you present','Sets your schedule']},right:{title:'Given away',items:['Needs no guard','Works while you are absent','Frees your time']},note:'Read as economics the verse is false. Read as a claim about attention, it is exact.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Two mechanisms',text:'The keeping half: chapter nine\u2019s hall of gold and jade cannot be guarded; past a point, the guarding becomes the occupation. The giving half: capability given away compounds, because what you taught goes on working. Neither needs mysticism. Both are about where attention goes.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Empty shelves, a full bowl, and hands still reaching in. The stall\u2019s standing outlasted its stock.'},{kind:'limits',text:'This is a claim about a threshold, not an argument for owning nothing. The text is against accumulation as a reflex, not against provision: a household with no savings is not wise, only exposed. And generosity with skill can be exploited by people who take and never return it.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What are you guarding at the moment that has started to set your days? What would happen if you taught it to someone?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which reading of the closing chapter is the misuse?',options:['Give away your savings, since the sage has more by having less','Teach a skill widely so the work no longer depends on you','Notice when guarding your holdings has begun to set your days','Share the knowledge you have been holding as private advantage'],answer:0,why:'The verse is against hoarding as a reflex, not against provision. Read as economics, it stops being true.'}]}},'tao-te-ching_9':{pictureCaption:'A crack as thin as a hair, and a small tool set down beside it. Today the fix is tiny. Later it will not be.',bridge:'Like the leader in idea 1, acting early works out of sight: nobody sees the problem that never grew.',basics:{title:'Fix it while it\u2019s small',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'A LOOSE THREAD',text:'A thread comes loose on your jumper. Snip it now, and that is the end of it. Leave it, and it catches on things. In a week there is a hole.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Deal with a problem while it is still small. Small problems are easy to fix. Every big problem started small.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The crack is as thin as a hair. One small tool can fix it today. Leave it a winter, and the stone may split.'},{kind:'explain',title:'A famous line, and what it means',text:'The Tao Te Ching is an old book from China. It says a journey of a thousand miles begins under your feet. People use this to mean *keep going*. But the book is about starting early. A huge tree grew from a tiny shoot.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it is hard',text:'When a problem is small, it does not look like a problem yet. Fixing it can feel silly. And if you do fix it, nobody sees the big mess that never came.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of one small thing you hope won\u2019t get worse: a loose button, a fall-out with a friend, a topic you are behind on. Spend ten minutes on it today.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You and a friend had a small fall-out at lunch. What does this idea say to do?',options:['Talk to her today, while it is still small','Wait a week and see if it goes away','Tell your other friends your side first'],answer:0,why:'Today it is one short chat. In a week it may be a big fight with lots of people in it.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'The thousand-mile line means keep going, however long it takes.',reality:'The passage is about timing, not persistence: deal with a thing while it is easy, act while it is small. The journey sits beside a great tree that grew from a shoot and a high terrace begun with a basket of earth.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Handle the difficult while it is easy and the great while it is small. Intervene at the stage when intervention is cheap.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'COST AGAINST CERTAINTY',low:'Early: cheap to fix, hard to justify',high:'Late: obviously needed, expensive to fix',note:'Evidence and cost rise together. By the time you are sure, the cheap moment has gone.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why early looks like overreaction',text:'Early is when intervention is cheap and also when you are least sure it is needed. So acting early looks like overreaction, and often cannot be told apart from it. And the cost of being right early is never visible: nobody thanks you for the crisis that did not happen.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A hairline crack and the small tool that fixes it. The size of the job is set by when you do it.'},{kind:'try',text:'Name the thing you are currently hoping will not become a problem, and spend twenty minutes on it today.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A new hire has seemed a little lost in two meetings. Nothing has gone wrong yet. What does the passage suggest?',options:['A short check-in now, while it costs ten minutes','Wait for a real problem, so you know it is worth raising','Raise it at the formal review in three months\u2019 time','Assume they will settle in, and say nothing for now'],answer:0,why:'The signal is weak, which is exactly when acting is cheap. A ten-minute conversation now may save a much harder one later.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'CHAPTERS 63 AND 64',text:'Three images run together. A tree that fills your arms grew from a shoot. A terrace nine storeys high began with a basket of earth. A journey of a thousand miles began beneath your feet. They are usually quoted as encouragement. Read in place, they are about timing.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Deal with things while they are easy, and act on them while they are small. Every large difficulty passed through a stage when it was cheap to handle.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Four easy states',text:'The text lists them. What is at rest is easy to hold. What has not yet appeared is easy to plan for. What is brittle is easy to break. What is minute is easy to scatter. Each is the early stage of something that will later resist.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The problem of evidence',text:'Early is when intervention is cheap and when you are least sure it is needed. So acting early always looks like overreaction, and is often indistinguishable from it. The only legible version of a problem is the one you let grow, which is why the early version goes unthanked.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The crack is still a line, the tool still small. Wait, and the evidence will be clearer and the job bigger.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Acting on every weak signal is its own failure. Most small things stay small, and someone who intervenes on all of them is exhausting and often wrong. The text asks for judgement about which shoots will become trees and gives no rule for telling. It is honest that the right calls go unrewarded.'},{kind:'recap',points:['The thousand-mile line is about timing, not persistence.','Early action is cheap but always looks premature.','Not every shoot becomes a tree. Judgement is the hard part.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HONEST OBJECTION',prompt:'Which is the strongest objection to acting while things are small?',options:['Most small signals never grow, so acting on all of them wastes effort','Small problems are, by definition, not worth anyone\u2019s attention','Large problems are easier to see, and so easier to fix','Persistence matters far more than timing in the long run'],answer:0,why:'The others misread the passage. This one is real: the text depends on judging which signals matter, and offers no rule for it.'}]}}}},3012,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'the-argument-you-keep-having_1':{pictureCaption:'Two people still trying to shift a pillar that holds up the house. The scrape marks round its base are years of trying.',basics:{title:'Some fights never get fixed',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE SAME FIGHT AGAIN',prompt:'You share a room with your brother. He likes it messy. You like it neat. You fight about it every week. What will next week\u2019s fight do?',options:['Fix it for good','Probably nothing new'],answer:1,reveal:'Lots of fights have not fixed it. That is a clue. Maybe this is not a problem with an answer.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Some fights keep coming back because two people are just different. There is no answer that ends them. So stop trying to win, and find a way to live with it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two people push at a post that holds up the house. Look at the marks round the bottom. They have been trying for years. It will not move.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Has an answer',items:['Who sits in front today','Whose turn it is to wash up','Which film to watch']},right:{title:'Just different',items:['He likes mess, you like neat','She is loud, you like quiet','One of you is always early']},note:'The left side can be settled. The right side needs a plan.'},{kind:'explain',title:'So what do you do?',text:'Stop asking, \u201cHow do I win?\u201d Ask, \u201cHow do we share this room and still get on?\u201d Maybe his half can be messy and yours neat. You are still different. But the fight gets much smaller.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of a fight you keep having. Ask: is this a thing we could settle? Or are we just two different people?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You are always early. Your friend is always late. You argue about it each time you meet. What does this idea say to do?',options:['Make a plan that works for both of you','Keep telling her off until she changes','Stop meeting up with her at all'],answer:0,why:'Being early or late is part of who you each are. A plan, like meeting somewhere warm to wait, works better than a fight.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE THERMOSTAT, AGAIN',text:'It starts over the heating. One of you is always cold, the other always warm. You both know the lines by now. Someone will say *you always do this*, and it will end exactly where it ended last month.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Many arguments that keep coming back are not problems with a solution. They are permanent differences. Naming one as permanent fixes nothing, but it changes the goal from winning to managing, and managing can actually be done.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it never ends',text:'Each round is treated as the chance to settle it at last. So each round that fails to settle it feels like a failure, and that failure is carried into the next one. The difference stays the same size. What grows is the resentment.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'WHERE THE GOAL SITS',low:'Win: one of you finally changes',high:'Manage: you both live well with the difference',note:'The left end is not on offer for a permanent difference. The right end is, which is its whole advantage.'},{kind:'explain',title:'One estimate, one category',text:'Research on couples, most prominently John Gottman\u2019s, has put the share of disagreements that are permanent at roughly two thirds. Treat that as one group\u2019s estimate, not a law. The useful part is the category: some arguments are not waiting for a solution.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Years of pushing, and the pillar has not moved. It holds the house up. The real choice is how the two of them live around it.'},{kind:'try',text:'Take the argument you have had most often. Before the next round, ask one question: is this solvable at all? If not, what would managing it look like?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A couple have argued for years about money. She feels safe with savings; he wants to enjoy it now. Which response applies the idea?',options:['Agree a split that respects both, and stop trying to convert each other','Find expert advice that settles once and for all who is right','Keep having the argument until one of them is finally persuaded','Agree never to talk about money with each other again'],answer:0,why:'Years of rounds suggest a stable difference, not a missing fact. Managing it is achievable. Converting each other is not.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE CLAIM',text:'The piece starts from a distinction. Some disagreements are problems: a decision or a fact would end them. Others are permanent differences between two people. Its argument is that the costly mistake is treating the second kind as the first, and spending years on a solution that does not exist.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Before spending another year solving a recurring disagreement, ask whether it is solvable at all. Calling it permanent changes nothing about the difference, but it moves the goal from winning to managing, which can be achieved.'},{kind:'term',word:'Perpetual problem',meaning:'A disagreement rooted in a stable difference between two people, of personality or of need, that returns in new forms. Set against a solvable problem, which a decision or a fact can end.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The evidence, and its weight',text:'The piece leans on research on couples, most prominently John Gottman\u2019s, which has put the share of perpetual disagreements at roughly two thirds. It asks you to treat that figure as one group\u2019s estimate. What carries the argument is the category, not the number.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW A DIFFERENCE SOURS',nodes:['A stable difference','Treated as a problem to solve','Each round fails to solve it','Each failure adds resentment'],note:'The difference never grows. The record of failed attempts does, and the next round inherits it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A pillar that holds the house up, and the scrape marks of years spent trying to shift it. The effort was real. The goal was impossible.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The category is easy to misuse. Calling something permanent can be a way to stop discussing a problem one person could and should fix: a debt, a broken promise, unkindness. Some differences cost one side far more than the other, and managing them can mean one person absorbing it. Contempt or cruelty is not a difference of temperament.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of a disagreement you have had many times. If it were permanent, what would managing it well look like?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these is a misuse of the idea?',options:['Filing a partner\u2019s repeated broken promises as a difference to live with','Agreeing a routine because one partner needs more time alone','Splitting the heating schedule because one is always colder','Accepting that one of them will always be tidier than the other'],answer:0,why:'A broken promise is something a person can stop doing. Calling it permanent uses the idea to avoid a problem that has a fix.'}]}},'the-argument-you-keep-having_2':{pictureCaption:'The leafy top comes away in the hand. The root stays in the ground, already sending up a new shoot.',bridge:'Idea 1 asked whether an argument can be solved. This one asks what it is really about, which is often why it can\u2019t be.',basics:{title:'The fight under the fight',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'AFTER DINNER',text:'Your mum asks you to clear the table. You say, \u201cWhy is it always me?\u201d Last week it was the bins. The week before, it was the dog. The job changes. The fight feels the same every time.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A fight that keeps coming back is often not about the thing it seems to be about. Under it sits a bigger worry, and that worry does not change.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A WEED',picture:!0,text:'You pull up a weed. Next week it is back. You only pulled off the top. The root is still in the ground. A fight that keeps coming back has a root too.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'WHAT IT MIGHT REALLY MEAN',text:'*Why is it always me* might not be about plates at all. It might mean: *does anyone see how much I do?* Sort out the plates, and that worry is still there next week. So the fight comes back.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'This week\u2019s job',items:['Plates','Bins','Walking the dog']},right:{title:'Same worry each time',items:['Is this fair?','Does anyone see what I do?']},note:'The left side keeps changing. The right side stays the same.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time a fight comes back, ask: what is the same every time? That is where the root is.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You and a friend argue about who picks the game. Then who sits where. Then who goes first. What might it really be about?',options:['Whether one of you always gets your way','Which game is the best one to play','Where the best seat in the room is'],answer:0,why:'The topic changed each time. A worry about fairness stayed the same, so that is likely the root.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'If we settle the chores properly, the chore arguments will stop.',reality:'Often they don\u2019t, because the chores were never the subject. Settle them and the same argument turns up next week, about lateness, or the car.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A recurring argument is rarely about what it appears to be about. The surface topic changes weekly. The real one, such as whether one person\u2019s time is treated as worth less, never does.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THREE TOPICS, ONE SUBJECT',text:'Monday: the dishes were left. Thursday: you were twenty minutes late. Saturday: you took the car without asking. Three topics, and one question running under all of them: *does my time count as much as yours?* A dishes rota leaves that question untouched.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHY SETTLING SETTLES NOTHING',nodes:['The real subject goes unspoken','A surface topic carries it','The topic gets settled','The subject finds a new topic'],loop:!0,note:'Each settlement feels like progress and changes nothing underneath.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Each settled topic is the top of the weed, pulled off. The root stays under the soil and puts up a new shoot by next week.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Finding the real one',text:'Look for what stays the same across rounds. Then ask the piece\u2019s question: what would this mean about me if you were right? If the honest answer is *that I don\u2019t value your time*, you have found the subject, and it can be talked about directly.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time it recurs, ask yourself, or them: what would this mean about me if you were right?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A couple argue about his phone at dinner, a missed call, and him working late. What is the most useful next step?',options:['Name what the three have in common and talk about that','Agree a firm rule about phones at the dinner table','Deal with each complaint fairly, one at a time','Decide which of the three complaints is most serious'],answer:0,why:'Each rule settles one topic. If the subject is something like *do I come first*, only talking about that will reach it.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'A ROTA THAT WORKED',text:'Take a couple who argue about chores. They draw up a rota, and it works. A month later the argument is back, now about lateness. Then the car. Each round has a fair answer, and each answer is reached. The argument goes on anyway, which is the evidence that it was never about chores.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The content of a recurring argument is not its subject. Under a changing surface topic sits a stable one, such as whose time counts, and settling the surface leaves it exactly where it was.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where it sits in the piece',text:'The first idea separated solvable problems from permanent ones. This one explains why the two are so easily confused. The permanent subject arrives dressed as a solvable topic. Chores look solvable, so they get solved, and the subject survives untouched.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'The surface topic',items:['Changes week to week','Has a fair answer','Can be settled']},right:{title:'The subject',items:['Stays the same for years','Is about what something means','Is managed, not settled']}},{kind:'picture',caption:'The surface topic is the leafy top, and it does come away. The subject is the root, untouched below, already sending up the next round.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the evidence adds',text:'The piece draws on the psychologist John Gottman\u2019s distinction between solvable and perpetual problems. He reports that most conflicts in long relationships are perpetual, rooted in stable differences of personality or need, and that in a healthy version both people can discuss them with some humour and without escalating.'},{kind:'keyline',text:'Expecting a fix turns a manageable difference into a grievance, because every round becomes another failure.'},{kind:'limits',text:'This can become a licence not to listen. Sometimes the argument about chores really is about chores: the work is unequal and a rota would end it. Reading every complaint as code for something deeper is a way of not hearing it. Settle the surface fairly first. If it keeps coming back, look underneath.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'In your most recent recurring argument, what would it mean about you if the other person were right?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'Someone objects that sometimes an argument about the dishes is just about the dishes. What is the best reply?',options:['True: settle the surface fairly, and look underneath only if it keeps returning','No: an argument that recurs is always about something deeper than it seems','True, which shows the idea is mostly wrong about how couples argue','No: the dishes are a symptom and should not be discussed directly'],answer:0,why:'The claim is about arguments that come back after the surface is settled. Recurrence is the signal, not a rule that nothing means what it says.'}]}},'the-argument-you-keep-having_3':{pictureCaption:'The first bowl leaves the hand with its line already drawn. Every bowl after it runs along the same path.',bridge:'Once you know what an argument is really about, the next question is how to raise it. The first minute decides more than it seems.',basics:{title:'How you start is how it goes',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'YOUR HOODIE IS MISSING',prompt:'Your sister took your hoodie again without asking. Which first thing to say will go better?',options:['\u201cYou always steal my stuff!\u201d','\u201cMy hoodie is gone. Can you ask me first next time?\u201d'],answer:1,reveal:'The first one is an attack, so she will likely stick up for herself. Then you both get louder. The second says what happened and what you want. It gives her much less to fight.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The way you start a talk shapes how it ends. Start with blame and you will often get a fight. Start with what happened and what you want, and a real talk is much more likely.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Bowls is a game where you roll heavy balls along the grass. Here the first ball sets the line. Look at the dotted path. Every ball after it rolls the same way.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW A FIGHT GROWS',nodes:['You start with blame','They stick up for themselves','You both get louder'],note:'The hoodie did not change. Only the start did.'},{kind:'steps',title:'A soft start',steps:['Say what happened.','Say how you feel.','Say what you would like.'],note:'Leave out *always* and *never*. Those words sound like blame.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Wait till you are calm',text:'Here is the hard part. You want to speak when you are most cross. That is when the blaming words come out first. So wait a bit. Then start soft.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend keeps reading over your shoulder. Which start fits the idea?',options:['\u201cCan I read on my own for a bit, please?\u201d','\u201cYou are so nosy. Stop it!\u201d','\u201cWhy do you always annoy me?\u201d'],answer:0,why:'It says what you want, with no blame. The other two start with an attack, so a fight is likely.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'ONE COMPLAINT, TWO OPENINGS',left:{title:'Opening A',items:['\u201cYou\u2019re so selfish. You never think of anyone.\u201d']},right:{title:'Opening B',items:['\u201cI waited an hour. Can we agree times?\u201d']},note:'Same complaint, same person. The piece\u2019s claim is that these tend to produce two different conversations.'},{kind:'idea',text:'How the first minute goes tends to set the course of everything after it. An opening with an accusation gets defence, and defence gets escalation. The same complaint opened as a request often gets a different conversation entirely.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the opening wins',text:'An accusation is about what the other person is. Nobody can accept that without losing face, so they defend. A request is about what happened and what you want. It can be agreed to without anyone admitting to being selfish.'},{kind:'steps',title:'The almost mechanical version',steps:['Describe what happened, not what they are.','Say how it left you feeling.','Ask for something specific.'],note:'There is no blame in it, and the whole complaint is still there.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The first bowl has barely left the hand, and its line is already drawn. Everything after it follows.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The catch',text:'You deliver the opening when you are most annoyed, which is exactly when the harsh version comes most easily. So the useful move is often to delay the conversation, not to try to polish your wording in the heat of it.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before your next difficult conversation, write only the first sentence. Spend your preparation there, not on building the case.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Your flatmate has left the kitchen a mess for the third night running, and you are furious. What does the idea suggest?',options:['Wait until you are calm, then open with what happened and a request','Raise it at once, while the evidence is still on the counter','List every past occasion, so the pattern cannot be denied','Say nothing, since any complaint will start an argument'],answer:0,why:'The opening sets the course, and the harsh one comes easiest when you are angry. Waiting makes the soft start possible.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',word:'Start-up',meaning:'The opening of a conflict conversation, when one person first raises the complaint. Harsh if it opens with criticism or contempt; softened if it describes the situation, states a feeling and makes a specific request, without blame.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The opening of a disagreement forecasts its end. A harsh start-up produces escalation regardless of the content. A softened one produces a conversation that can go somewhere.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The evidence, and how far it goes',text:'Research on couples in conflict, Gottman\u2019s in particular, reports that the first few minutes forecast the outcome of the whole discussion far better than chance. The piece puts it more loosely: the first minute tends to set the course. Both are claims about tendency, not certainty.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE HARSH PATH',nodes:['Criticism in the first sentence','The other person defends','The defence is heard as denial','Escalation, whatever the topic'],note:'The content barely enters into it. The path is set by the shape of the first move.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why timing beats phrasing',text:'The harsh version is most available exactly when you are most annoyed, and that is usually when you raise things. Hence the piece\u2019s practical conclusion: the useful fix is to delay the conversation, not to improve your wording in the moment.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The first bowl leaves the hand with its line already drawn. Every later bowl runs along the same dotted path.'},{kind:'limits',text:'A soft start lowers the odds of escalation. It does not guarantee a good outcome or make the other person reasonable. It is also easily misread as a duty to be gentle about everything, serious wrongs included, and some things should be said plainly. Delay has a limit too: postponed forever, a complaint simply goes unmade.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these is a misuse of the idea?',options:['Never raising a serious grievance because it cannot be said softly','Waiting until the evening so you can raise a complaint calmly','Opening with what happened rather than with what they are','Writing your first sentence down before a hard conversation'],answer:0,why:'Softening is about how to open, not whether to. A complaint never raised is not a soft start. It is no start.'}]}},'the-argument-you-keep-having_4':{pictureCaption:'The bridge is frayed from constant use and still holds, because its anchor was fixed into the rock before anyone needed to cross.',bridge:'Idea 3 was about how an argument starts. This one is about how it stops, and why that has to be settled in advance.',basics:{title:'Agree a pause sign before the fight',beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Good friends and happy families never fight.',reality:'All close friends and families fight sometimes. The ones that work out have a way to stop a fight and come back to each other.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Agree a stop sign while you are both calm. Then, in a fight, either of you can use it to pause. Using it does not mean you gave in.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'A PAUSE WORD',text:'Say two brothers pick a word, like *pause*. Either one can say it in a fight. Then they go to different rooms for twenty minutes. Nobody wins. Nobody loses. They come back calmer.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why pick it first?',text:'If you storm off in the middle of a fight, it looks like a trick. It can look like sulking, or a way to win. A sign you both picked before is different. You both know what it means.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'This rope bridge is old and used a lot. Look at the thick rope tied to the rock. It was tied on long before anyone had to cross.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick a pause word with someone you live with. Agree it today, while nobody is cross.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'In a fight, your friend walks off without a word. Why might that make it worse?',options:['It looks like a move in the fight','Walking off always ends a friendship','It means your friend has won the fight'],answer:0,why:'You never agreed on it, so it looks like sulking or a trick. A sign you agreed on first would not.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE CHAIR',text:'Imagine a household rule: whoever sits in a particular chair is asking to stop, and the other person is not allowed to read anything into it. It sounds absurd. In the middle of a row, it is the one move that cannot be mistaken for a tactic.'},{kind:'idea',text:'No close relationship runs without conflict. The ones that work have a way back: a word, a rule or a gesture, agreed while nobody is angry, that either person can use to stop without it counting as conceding.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Improvised mid-row',items:['Walking out','Going quiet','Read as a tactic']},right:{title:'Agreed in advance',items:['A word meaning twenty minutes','Nothing decided after eleven','Read as the agreed pause']},note:'In the moment, a pause you invent looks exactly like a move. One you agreed earlier cannot.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why in advance',text:'Anything done during a row is read as part of the row. Walking out may be an honest need for air, but it cannot be told apart from punishment. An agreement made while calm takes the pause out of the argument, so using it says nothing about who is winning.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A bridge frayed from constant use, and still holding, because its anchor was set before anyone needed to cross.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What it does not do',text:'It does not fix the underlying difference. And the piece is plain that this is an argument from how rows tend to go, not a measured finding. What it offers is an argument that ends, rather than one that goes somewhere neither of you meant it to.'},{kind:'try',text:'Agree one signal now, while nothing is wrong, that either of you can use to pause. Decide what happens next too: how long, and who picks it up again.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Which of these is the strongest way back from a row?',options:['A word agreed last month that means \u201ctwenty minutes, then we continue\u201d','Leaving the room the moment the argument starts to get heated','Going silent until the other person is the first to apologise','Promising each other that you will simply never argue again'],answer:0,why:'Only the agreed word was settled in advance. The others may be meant well, but they read as moves in the argument, or promise the impossible.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'A good relationship is one without serious rows.',reality:'The piece starts from the opposite premise: no close relationship runs without conflict. What marks the ones that work is not avoiding rupture but having a reliable route out of it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The skill is not never rupturing. It is a route out, agreed before you need it, so that using it is not a move in the argument.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The problem it solves',text:'Mid-row, anything improvised is read as strategy. Walking out looks like punishment, going quiet like contempt. The intention behind an act cannot be seen from outside. Only an agreement made earlier can tell the other person what the act means.'},{kind:'term',word:'Precommitment',meaning:'Deciding in advance, while calm, how you will act in a situation where you expect your judgement to be worse. The choice is settled before the pressure arrives.'},{kind:'steps',title:'The unglamorous forms',steps:['A word that means: I need twenty minutes.','A rule that nothing gets decided after eleven at night.','A chair: sitting in it asks to stop, and means nothing more.'],note:'Each is dull on purpose. Its meaning was fixed before anyone needed it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The bridge is frayed from constant crossing and still holds. Its strength is the anchor, set into the rock before the first crossing.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The piece is candid that this is reasoned from how rows tend to go, not a measured finding, and that it leaves the underlying difference untouched. A pause can also be abused: called whenever one person is losing, or used to avoid ever coming back. It only works if it includes a way back to the subject.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'If you agreed a signal with someone tonight, what would it be, and what exactly would happen after it was used?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HARD CASE',prompt:'One partner keeps calling the agreed pause, and the conversation never resumes. What has gone wrong?',options:['The pause has become a way to avoid the subject, not a way back','Pauses are always a mistake and should be dropped altogether','The other partner should simply stop agreeing to the pause','Nothing: a pause works by ending the argument for good'],answer:0,why:'The signal stops a row from going somewhere bad so that it can return. Without the return it is just leaving by another name.'}]}},'the-argument-you-keep-having_5':{pictureCaption:'Hands set a heavy block in the middle of the table, not asking for it to be moved, only for its weight to be felt.',bridge:'Idea 2 looked for what an argument is really about. This one looks at what the other person is actually asking for.',basics:{title:'Sometimes people want to be heard, not fixed',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'AFTER SCHOOL',text:'Your friend says, \u201cMaths was awful. I got it all wrong.\u201d You say, \u201cJust ask the teacher for help!\u201d Your friend goes quiet. You were only trying to help. So what went wrong?'},{kind:'explain',title:'What your friend wanted',text:'Your friend did not want a fix yet. They wanted you to see that the day was hard. When you jump straight to the fix, it can feel like you skipped them.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Often people do not want you to fix the problem. They want you to see how hard it is. So show that you get it first.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two hands put a heavy block on the table. They are not asking anyone to move it. They want someone to see how heavy it is.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Fixing too soon',items:['\u201cJust do this.\u201d','Feels like: you are not listening']},right:{title:'Showing you get it',items:['\u201cThat sounds really hard.\u201d','Feels like: you see me']},note:'Once you have shown you get it, you can ask: *do you want ideas?*'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time someone tells you a problem, say back what you heard first. Then ask if they want help to fix it.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your little brother says his tower fell down and he is sad. What fits the idea best?',options:['\u201cOh no. You worked hard on that.\u201d','\u201cJust build it again. It\u2019s easy.\u201d','\u201cTowers fall down all the time.\u201d'],answer:0,why:'It shows you see how he feels first. You can help him build it again after that.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A GOOD IDEA, BADLY RECEIVED',prompt:'Your partner says the commute is wearing them down. You suggest an earlier train and a podcast. They go cold. Why?',options:['The suggestions were poor ones','They wanted the cost acknowledged, not solved','They were not really bothered by the commute'],answer:1,reveal:'The suggestions may have been fine. But they skipped the part being asked for. A solution offered to someone who wanted to be understood reads as dismissal.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Often the other person does not want the thing changed. They want it acknowledged as costly. Offering a fix where an accurate description is wanted reads as dismissal, which is how a good solution makes things worse.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The tell',text:'If offering a solution makes the conversation worse, that is the sign. Someone who wanted a fix receives a solution as help. Someone who wanted to be understood receives it as a way of skipping past them.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Showing I understand their position means agreeing with it.',reality:'No. Stating accurately a position you reject is a different act. You can say back exactly what they think and why, and still disagree with every word.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A heavy block set down in the middle of the table. Not a request to move it. A request for its weight to be felt.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Before you respond',steps:['Restate what they said, in your own words.','Ask if you have it right, and adjust until they say yes.','Then ask: do you want this changed, or just known?']},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A colleague says, for the third time, that the new system is slowing their work. You have a workaround. What should come first?',options:['Say back what it is costing them, and check you have it right','Explain the workaround clearly so they can start using it today','Point out that everyone else is struggling with the system too','Agree that the system is bad, whatever you actually think of it'],answer:0,why:'Showing you understand comes before the fix. Calling the system bad when you do not think so confuses understanding with agreeing.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO REQUESTS, SAME WORDS',left:{title:'Asking for a fix',items:['Wants the thing changed','Hears a solution as help','Satisfied by the outcome']},right:{title:'Asking to be understood',items:['Wants the cost acknowledged','Hears a solution as dismissal','Satisfied by accuracy']},note:'The two often arrive in identical words, which is why they are so routinely confused.'},{kind:'idea',text:'In a recurring argument, the real request is very often to be understood rather than agreed with. Show you have understood before responding, and arguments that no problem-solving touches can resolve.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the fix backfires',text:'A solution jumps over the description. To someone who wanted the problem fixed, that is efficient. To someone who wanted its cost seen, it says the cost was not worth dwelling on. So a perfectly good answer lands as dismissal.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The practice',text:'Demonstrate comprehension before responding: restate their position until they confirm it is right, conceding nothing. The test is their confirmation, not your own sense of having listened.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A heavy block pushed into the middle of the table. The hands are not asking for it to be moved. They are asking for its weight to be felt.'},{kind:'keyline',text:'Stating a view accurately is a different act from agreeing with it.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Sometimes the person does want it changed, and endless acknowledgement becomes its own dismissal: sympathy offered in place of action. The piece handles this by asking which is wanted. A restatement used as a technique to manage someone is soon recognised, and makes things worse. And the claim is that this is often the ask, not always.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'Someone objects that restating the other person\u2019s view until they agree you have it right is just giving in. What is the best reply?',options:['It concedes nothing: describing a view accurately is different from accepting it','It is giving in, but giving in is usually worth it for the sake of peace','They are right, so restate only the parts of it you happen to agree with','It only counts as giving in if you go on to offer them a solution'],answer:0,why:'Confusing understanding with agreeing is the very mistake the idea names. You can state their case well and still reject it.'}]}}}},3013,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'the-art-of-war_1':{pictureCaption:'Hours before anyone arrives, hands are setting the room. The deciding happens now, quietly, so nothing is left to fight over later.',basics:{title:'The best win is the fight you never had',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE TV AT SEVEN',prompt:'You and your brother both want the TV at seven. Which result is best for you?',options:['You win a big row and get the TV','You sort it out at lunch, so there is no row'],answer:1,reveal:'If you win the row, you get the TV. But your brother is cross all night. Next time he fights harder. Sorting it out early gets you the TV and keeps the peace.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Winning a fight is good. Getting what you need with no fight at all is better. A fight always breaks some of what you were fighting over.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A TUG OF WAR',text:'Two kids pull hard on one jumper. One of them wins. But now the jumper is stretched out of shape. The win was real. The jumper is ruined.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO SAID THIS',text:'The Art of War is a book from China. It is about 2,500 years old. It is said to be by a general called Sun Tzu. You might think a war book would praise big battles. It says the best general wins with no battle at all.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Someone is setting up a room long before anyone comes. Things are often best sorted out early like this, before anyone sits down to argue.'},{kind:'try',text:'Is a row coming up about something? Talk to the other person on their own, early, before anyone is cross.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You and a friend both want to be captain in a game at break. What would Sun Tzu\u2019s idea say?',options:['Agree a fair way to pick before break starts','Argue hard at break until your friend gives in','Let your friend be captain every single time'],answer:0,why:'Sorting it out early means nobody has to lose a row. Giving in every time is not winning at all.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE NIGHT BEFORE',text:'The meeting tomorrow will settle it, and two people already disagree with you. You are sharpening your arguments and planning how to win in the room.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Winning a fight is not the best outcome. Getting what you need without one is, because every fight damages the thing you were fighting over.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHAT A WIN COSTS',nodes:['You win the argument','The team learns you argue to win','Next time they arrive armed'],note:'The win goes on the record. The damage rarely goes on the same page.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why nobody praises it',text:'A famous victory is visible. A conflict that never happened leaves no trace, so nobody credits it. That is why Sun Tzu, the ancient Chinese general credited with *The Art of War*, rates the general who never needed a siege above one who won a famous siege.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Hours before anyone arrives, the room is being set. The best version of tomorrow\u2019s meeting is decided here, early and one-to-one.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE DAY BEFORE, INSTEAD',text:'Instead of rehearsing your case, you have two short conversations today. One colleague has a worry you can meet. The other wants a change you can live with. Tomorrow you walk in with nothing left to win.'},{kind:'try',text:'List the people who might oppose you at your next contested meeting. Next to each, write one thing you could settle with them beforehand.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You need a neighbour to agree to a new shared fence. Which approach follows the idea?',options:['Ask what worries them about it before making any formal request','Prepare a strong case and present it firmly at their door','Get the fence approved first and let them object afterwards','Drop the plan, since any disagreement means you have lost'],answer:0,why:'Meeting the worry early leaves nothing to fight about. Winning the argument at the door could still cost you the neighbour.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'CHAPTER THREE OPENS',text:'Sun Tzu begins his third chapter with a ranking. Taking a state whole is better than shattering it. Capturing an army intact is better than destroying it. Then the turn: winning every battle is not the summit. The summit is breaking the other side\u2019s resistance without fighting.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A battle won is second best, because a fight consumes what it was fought over. The highest skill gets the outcome with the prize intact, and so leaves no trace of having been skilful.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Won by fighting',items:['Visible, and praised','The prize arrives damaged','The loser remembers']},right:{title:'Won without fighting',items:['Invisible, and unpraised','The prize arrives whole','No grievance to inherit']},note:'The left column gets the credit. The right column gets the result.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Accounting, not pacifism',text:'Sun Tzu still wants the other side subdued. His point is that the cost of a fight is real but booked somewhere else. You win the negotiation and inherit a supplier looking for the exit. The victory and the damage rarely appear on the same page.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The work that wins happens here, in an empty room, hours early. By the time people sit down, there should be nothing left to fight about.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Sun Tzu\u2019s other side is an enemy to be subdued. A colleague is not, so what carries over is real agreement, never quiet pressure. Nor is avoiding the fight avoiding disagreement. Settle everything privately and the meeting becomes theatre, and the people not consulted learn that open argument is pointless. Some disagreement needs airing, because it is itself information. And some people cannot be brought round.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of an argument you won recently. What did winning it cost that did not show up at the time?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these is a *misuse* of the idea?',options:['Settling a decision privately so that critics never get to raise objections','Meeting a colleague\u2019s worry before the meeting rather than during it','Choosing a deal both sides can live with over a crushing win','Dropping a point that would cost more to win than it gains'],answer:0,why:'The aim is to remove the need for a fight, not to remove people\u2019s voice. Shutting objections out leaves the damage in place, only hidden.'}]}},'the-art-of-war_2':{pictureCaption:'Before any climbing, the hands test the one buckle that has to hold. The rope waits. Securing yourself comes first.',bridge:'Idea 1 counted what a fight costs. This one asks what to do first when a contest cannot be avoided.',basics:{title:'First, make sure you can\u2019t lose',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'A GAME OF DRAUGHTS',text:'You are playing draughts. You spot a clever jump, and you go for it. But you forgot to check your own pieces. Your friend takes three of them in one go.'},{kind:'idea',text:'There are two jobs, in order. First, make sure you can\u2019t lose. Then wait for the other side to make a mistake you can use.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHOSE JOB IS IT?',left:{title:'Not losing',items:['Up to you','You can do it now']},right:{title:'Winning',items:['Needs their mistake','You have to wait for it']},note:'Do the job that is yours first.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why this order?',text:'Not losing is all in your hands. You can guard your pieces right now. Winning needs the other player to slip up. You can\u2019t make that happen. So start with the part you control.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'BEFORE THE CLIMB',caption:'The climber checks the buckle before going up. The rope can wait. Being safe is the part that is up to you, so it comes first.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Waiting means doing nothing.',reality:'No. You wait with your pieces safe and your eyes open. When the other player slips, you are ready to move.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you play a game, ask one thing before each move: does this leave me open?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You and a friend race to build the tallest tower of blocks. What does this idea say to do first?',options:['Make the bottom of your tower solid','Watch your friend and hope theirs falls','Build as high and as fast as you can'],answer:0,why:'A solid base is the part you control. Whether their tower falls is up to them.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE CLEVER MOVE',belief:'Contests are won by finding the brilliant move.',reality:'Sun Tzu puts that second. First you make yourself impossible to beat. Only then do you look for the opening, and the opening is something the other side gives you.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Secure your own position against defeat first, which is fully in your hands. Then wait for an opportunity to win, which only the other side can provide.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'THE PART IN YOUR HANDS',caption:'Nothing has been climbed yet. The rope lies coiled while the hands test the one buckle that has to hold. Your own position comes first.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'A WEEK ON THE WRONG JOB',text:'You have spent the week on a bold pitch to a new client. You have not once looked at the contract with your biggest existing client, which runs out next month.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'In your hands',items:['Your weakest point','Your cash runway','Your own worst argument']},right:{title:'In theirs',items:['Their mistake','Their weak moment','The opening you need']},note:'Most people spend their energy on the right column and leave the left exposed.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Three translations',text:'In a career: make yourself hard to remove before you make yourself hard to ignore. In a business: runway before growth. In an argument: know your own weakest claim before you hunt for theirs. The waiting that follows is not passive. It is what you can afford once you cannot lose.'},{kind:'try',text:'Name the one thing that would most damage your position if it went wrong this quarter. Fix it before you start anything new.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You plan to ask for a promotion. On this idea, which step comes first?',options:['Make sure your current work has no gap your manager could point to','Find the moment when your manager is most likely to say yes','Research what the rival candidates are likely to offer','Ask straight away, before a stronger candidate appears'],answer:0,why:'Closing your own gaps is fully yours. The good moment comes from the other side, and you can only be ready for it.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'A WORD FIRST',word:'Invincibility',meaning:'In Sun Tzu\u2019s sense, not being able to be defeated. It is a property of your own position, which you can build. It says nothing about whether you will win.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'CHAPTER FOUR',text:'Sun Tzu describes the skilled fighters of old as doing two things, in order. First they made themselves impossible to defeat. Then they waited until the enemy could be defeated. The first, he says, lies in your own hands. The second is supplied by the enemy, through the enemy\u2019s own errors.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Not losing and winning are separate jobs with separate owners. Build the first yourself, completely. Treat the second as an opening that only the other side can give you.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE SEQUENCE',nodes:['Close your own weak points','Hold, and watch','They make an error','Strike there'],note:'The waiting is affordable only because the first step is done.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'STEP ONE, DRAWN',caption:'The first step of the sequence: the buckle is tested before the rock is touched. Whatever the climb offers comes after, and depends on this.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The common inversion',text:'Most people hunt for the clever move, which they do not control, and leave their own position exposed while they hunt. The result is a good plan resting on nothing. Sun Tzu reverses the effort: the part you own gets it first.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Waiting has a cost. Chapter two warns that long campaigns drain everything, so a position that waits for ever may slowly lose. The opponent may never err. Defence can become a habit that never takes the opening. And outside a real contest the second half transfers badly: most people you deal with are not enemies, and a colleague\u2019s slip is not an opening to exploit. The first half, securing yourself, carries over far better.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What would sink you this quarter if it went wrong? How much of last week went on it?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HARD CASE',prompt:'Your rival is careful and makes no mistakes. What does Sun Tzu\u2019s framework imply?',options:['You cannot force a win, so stay secure and do not attack on hope','Take a large risk, since waiting will never produce an opening','Copy their caution exactly and the contest will end in your favour','Their care proves your position is already weaker than theirs'],answer:0,why:'He says plainly that the chance to win depends on the enemy. Against an opponent who offers none, the right result is not losing.'}]}},'the-art-of-war_3':{pictureCaption:'The night before: papers finished and squared, one card face down, the chair opposite still empty. The deciding is done before anyone sits down.',bridge:'Idea 2 put not losing first. This one goes further: by the time the contest starts, it should already be decided.',basics:{title:'Win before the game starts',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'SPORTS DAY',belief:'The race decides who wins.',reality:'Mostly, it is decided before. Who trained. Who slept well. Who knows the track. The race just shows what was already true.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Do the work that decides a contest before it starts. Then the contest just shows the win you already made.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE BAKING A CAKE',text:'Once the cake is in the oven, it is too late to fix it. The mixing decided how it turns out. Opening the oven door and hoping does not help.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO START',left:{title:'Win, then fight',items:['Know why you will win','Then begin']},right:{title:'Fight, then hope',items:['Begin first','Then look for a way to win']},note:'An old Chinese book, The Art of War, says winners do the first. Losers do the second.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'A SPELLING TEST',text:'Sam learns the words all week. On the day, the test is easy. Jo opens the paper and hopes the words will come. Most of them don\u2019t. The test did not decide it. The week did.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'It is the night before. The papers are ready and neat. The other chair is still empty. The win is made now, before anyone sits down.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before a game or a test, finish this line: *I will do well because\u2026* If you can\u2019t finish it, you have work to do first.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your class has a quiz against another class on Friday. When is the quiz really won?',options:['In the days before, by learning the topics','On Friday, by thinking fast on the day','At the end, when the scores are added up'],answer:0,why:'Friday only shows what the class did before it. That is where the win is made.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'ONE HOUR TO GO',text:'The negotiation starts in an hour. You know what you want. You do not know your walk-away point, or what happens to them if you walk away. You plan to read the room.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The winner seeks the contest only once the victory is already secured. The loser starts the contest first, then looks for a way to win inside it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Not a paradox',text:'It sounds like a riddle and is really a description of a process. Position, resources, information and timing are settled before the contest begins. The contest does not decide the outcome. It reveals what those things already decided.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'A plan',items:['You can say why you will win','Built the week before','Survives a bad hour']},right:{title:'A hope',items:['You will find a way','Built in the room','Needs a good hour']}},{kind:'explain',title:'Why improvising feels good',text:'Starting and working it out on the way feels like courage. Usually it is missing preparation, dressed as decisiveness. The negotiator who lost rarely lost in the room. They lost the week before, when the thinking did not get done.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The other way round: the night before, the papers finished and squared, one card kept face down. The chair opposite is still empty, and the thinking is already done.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before committing to anything contested this month, write one sentence: *I will win this because\u2026* If you cannot finish it, do not start yet.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You want to propose a new process to a sceptical team. Which approach shows the idea at work?',options:['Learn their objections and answer them before proposing anything','Propose it boldly and handle objections as they come up','Wait until the team is in a good mood before raising it','Make the case in the meeting with all the energy you have'],answer:0,why:'By the time you propose it, the objections are already met. The meeting only shows what the preparation settled.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO GENERALS',left:{title:'Destined to win',items:['Secures the victory first','Then seeks battle']},right:{title:'Destined to lose',items:['Seeks battle first','Then looks for victory']},note:'Sun Tzu\u2019s fourth chapter sets these side by side. The only difference is the order.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Battles do not decide wars. They reveal decisions already made about position, supply, information and timing. So seek battle only when those earlier decisions favour you.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHERE THE OUTCOME IS MADE',nodes:['Position and supply','Information','Timing','The battle shows the result'],note:'By the last box, almost nothing is left to choose.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Its place in the chapter',text:'It sits in the same chapter as the instruction to make yourself unbeatable first. Read together, they describe one discipline. Secure yourself, wait, and commit to the contest only when the work already done favours you.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE LOST NEGOTIATION',text:'The person who lost the negotiation rarely lost in the room. They arrived without a walk-away number and without knowing the other side\u2019s alternatives, planning to think on their feet. The meeting merely displayed a result settled the week before.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The other side of that evening: the papers squared, one card held back face down, the chair opposite still empty. The meeting will only show what is settled here.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Some contests cannot wait until you are sure: you are attacked, or the window closes. And much of life is not one decisive battle: in new or uncertain fields, starting small is how you learn whether you can win at all. And a fluent sentence about why you will win is not proof. Overconfident plans are easy to write. The test catches missing preparation, not wrong preparation.'},{kind:'recap',points:['The contest reveals what was decided before it.','If you cannot say why you will win, you are not ready.','Being able to say it is necessary, not sufficient.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'Someone says: \u201cBy this logic, no new venture should ever launch.\u201d What is the best reply?',options:['Start small, so the real contest begins only once you know you can win it','They are right: uncertain ventures should never be started at all','Sun Tzu only meant war, so the idea has no use outside it','Launch anyway, because boldness is what wins in a new market'],answer:0,why:'The idea does not forbid acting under uncertainty. It warns against staking everything before the earlier work favours you, and a small test is part of that work.'}]}},'the-art-of-war_4':{pictureCaption:'Others charged straight up and slid back, leaving red scars. This walker skips the dramatic climb and looks for a way with no fall in it.',bridge:'Ideas 2 and 3 were about preparing. This one is about what decides a long run of contests: the big errors you never make.',basics:{title:'Don\u2019t make the big mistake',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TWO GOALKEEPERS',prompt:'One goalie makes amazing diving saves, but lets in a silly goal most games. The other never dives, but never lets in a silly goal. Who would you pick?',options:['The one who dives','The one with no silly goals'],answer:1,reveal:'The diving looks great. But one silly goal can lose the game. The calm goalie wins more, even though nobody cheers.'},{kind:'idea',text:'You often win by not making big mistakes, not by doing amazing things. One bad mistake can undo lots of good moves.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Lots of people ran straight up this hill and slid back down. See the red marks? This walker is looking for a safer way up.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A TOWER OF BLOCKS',text:'You spend an hour building a tall tower. One clumsy knock, and it falls in a second. Building took an hour. Undoing took a moment.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why nobody claps',text:'An old Chinese book, The Art of War, says the best general wins this way. No big show. No famous move. So people don\u2019t call him clever or brave. Nothing exciting happened. That is the point.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of the last time something went badly. What was the one mistake? Make a rule to stop it next time.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You are doing a long maths test. Which plan follows this idea?',options:['Check each answer so you make no silly slips','Rush to finish first so you look clever','Do only the hardest sums to impress your teacher'],answer:0,why:'Each silly slip throws away marks you had already earned. Checking keeps them.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE BOLD PLAN',text:'The bold plan is on the table and the room is excited. It could double the business. Nobody has asked what happens if it lands badly, because asking feels like the wrong mood.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Over a long game, results depend less on your best move than on your worst. The strongest players win by avoiding the mistake they cannot undo.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the sums are lopsided',text:'One serious error can erase a run of good decisions, and recovering usually costs more than the original gain. The bold plan is only available to someone who has not already spent their position on an avoidable loss.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'TWO KINDS OF MISTAKE',low:'One you can recover from',high:'One you cannot undo',note:'Accept the left end as the price of acting. Treat the right end as the thing to avoid.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it loses arguments',text:'Avoiding errors sounds unambitious, so it loses debates in rooms full of people who want to be impressive. And the people who do it best are rarely celebrated. A long record without disasters does not make a good story.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The red scars on the slope are other people\u2019s bold attempts. The walker at the bottom skips the dramatic climb and looks for the route with no fall in it.'},{kind:'try',text:'Review your last three significant decisions. Find the mistake that cost most, and the rule that would have prevented it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Your team is choosing between two plans. Which question does this idea add?',options:['Which plan could hurt us in a way we cannot undo?','Which plan has the biggest possible upside for us?','Which plan would impress the board the most?','Which plan could we get started on soonest?'],answer:0,why:'The idea does not rule out boldness. It asks what kind of mistake each plan exposes you to.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE GREAT COMMANDER',belief:'The greatest commanders are the ones remembered for brilliant moves.',reality:'Sun Tzu describes his ideal general flatly: he wins by making no mistakes. Such victories, he adds, earn him no reputation for wisdom and no praise for bravery, because nothing dramatic happened.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Long contests are decided more by the worst decision than the best. The skilled commander wins by never giving the opponent an error to use, and is under-credited for it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How it fits chapter four',text:'The same chapter tells you to make yourself unbeatable and wait for the enemy\u2019s mistake. This is the other side of that coin. Your opponent is waiting for your error too. Give them none.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The scars on the slope are the bold attempts that failed. The figure at the foot is doing the unglamorous thing: looking for the route with no fall in it.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'What gets noticed',items:['The famous move','The rescue','The comeback']},right:{title:'What decides it',items:['The error not made','The crisis avoided','The loss never taken']},note:'The right column does not photograph well, which is why it is undervalued.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Avoiding mistakes is not avoiding risk. A commander who never moves has made a mistake too, only a slow one. The idea also invites hindsight: after a disaster, every decision looks like an error. The useful question is narrower. Which mistakes could not be undone? Those deserve caution. On a battlefield one defeat can end everything; most of ordinary life is more forgiving, and learning needs errors. Recoverable ones are the price of acting.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Long games are lost by the worst move more than won by the best.','Guard hardest against mistakes you cannot undo.','Too much caution is a mistake of its own.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISREADING',prompt:'Which reading gets Sun Tzu wrong?',options:['Never commit to anything that carries any chance of error','Judge a plan by the worst mistake it exposes you to','Expect a careful record to earn less praise than it deserves','Give an opponent who is waiting for your error nothing to use'],answer:0,why:'He wants mistakes avoided, not action avoided. Doing nothing is itself an error an opponent can use.'}]}},'the-art-of-war_5':{pictureCaption:'The kit was laid down long ago and the cloak spread for rest. On the horizon, the other side\u2019s dust is still coming.',bridge:'Idea 3 said the outcome is settled before the contest. This one names the plainest way to settle it: get there first.',basics:{title:'Get there early, and get there fresh',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE SCHOOL TRIP',text:'Two friends are meeting the bus for a school trip. Mia left home early and walked. Leo got up late and ran all the way. Same bus. Same trip.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'Who will enjoy the first hour more?',options:['Mia','Leo','Both the same'],answer:0,reveal:'Leo is hot and out of breath, and he forgot his lunch. Mia is calm and ready. The trip is the same. How they start it is not.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Being there first, and ready, gives you a big edge. It is decided by when you set off, long before the thing begins.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'FROM AN OLD BOOK',text:'An old Chinese book, The Art of War, talks about two armies going to fight on the same field. One got there first and rested. The other had to rush, and arrived worn out. Same field. Same size. The early army had the edge.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'WHO GOT HERE FIRST?',caption:'Someone got here early. Their boots are off and their cloak is spread out to rest on. Far away, the dust shows the others are still on the way.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it is hard',text:'Getting ready for something that has not happened yet never feels urgent. Something else always feels more urgent. So you have to plan your early start on purpose.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one thing coming up this week. Pack your bag or read your notes the night before.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You have a swimming gala on Saturday morning. What does this idea say to do?',options:['Pack your kit on Friday night and go to bed early','Swim as fast as you can once your race starts','Get to the pool just as your race is called'],answer:0,why:'How fresh you are on Saturday is decided on Friday. By race time it is too late to change.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE SAME MEETING',prompt:'Two people go to the same meeting with the same skills. What most decides which of them does better?',options:['Who is better at thinking on their feet','Who read the papers the night before','Who speaks first in the meeting'],answer:1,reveal:'One has been in the room twenty minutes and read everything. The other arrives running and skims it on the way in. The schedule shows one meeting. They are in two different ones.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Whoever arrives first and waits is fresh. Whoever has to hurry arrives exhausted. The difference is set by who started earlier, and it is fixed before anyone meets.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'ALREADY SETTLED',caption:'This kit was put down a long time ago, and its owner has rested. The dust on the horizon is the other side, still hurrying to get here.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE INTERVIEW',text:'Two candidates, one job. One spent an evening on the company: its products, its problems, the person interviewing. The other read the job advert on the train. Neither difference appears on the timetable. Both decide the interview.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why early loses to urgent',text:'Preparing for something that has not happened yet always looks less pressing than something already on fire. So it loses, every week, unless you protect it on purpose. Sun Tzu\u2019s framing removes the moral tone. This is not about diligence. It is about the state you are in when it starts.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Protect the early start',steps:['Pick the most consequential thing in the next two weeks.','Book the preparation time now, before the week fills.','Treat that slot like a meeting you cannot move.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A big client pitch is ten days away, and this week is full of fires. Which response fits the idea?',options:['Book two preparation sessions now and defend them','Deal with the fires first and prepare once they are out','Prepare the night before, when pressure will focus you','Skip preparation and rely on knowing the client well'],answer:0,why:'The fires will never all be out. Preparation survives only if it is booked before the week fills.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'CHAPTER SIX OPENS',picture:!0,text:'The chapter on weak points and strong begins with two armies heading for the same battle. Whoever holds the field first and waits for the enemy will be fresh. Whoever arrives second and must hurry into the fight will be exhausted.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Arriving first is not about punctuality. It decides the state you fight in, and the state of the other side, and the gap is fixed before contact.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where the argument goes',text:'Sun Tzu goes on to say that the clever fighter makes the enemy come to him, rather than being made to go to the enemy. Arriving first is how you get that choice. The early side picks the ground and rests. The late side marches on the early side\u2019s terms.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE GAP OPENS',nodes:['Set off earlier','Hold the ground and rest','They hurry to reach you','Fresh against tired'],note:'Same ground, same numbers, same weapons. Only the start time differed.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'IN AN OFFICE',text:'The person who read the document the night before is in a different meeting from the one who skimmed it walking in. Nothing on the agenda shows it. It decides who sets the terms of the discussion.'},{kind:'limits',text:'First is not always best. Arriving early can mean committing before you know where the contest will be, or showing your hand, and some fields reward the second mover. Off the battlefield, what carries over is your own readiness. Tiring the other side on purpose, say by springing a meeting on them, turns the idea into a trick. The claim is about the state you arrive in, not earliness for its own sake.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What is the most consequential thing in your next fortnight? Is the preparation for it in your diary yet?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'Which case is closest to Sun Tzu\u2019s point?',options:['A team rests at the venue while their rivals travel overnight to reach it','A shop opens at seven because its rival opens at eight','A student hands in an essay a week before the deadline','A runner sets off fast so as to lead the race from the start'],answer:0,why:'His point is the condition each side is in when contact happens. Being early in name, or fast at the start, is not the same thing.'}]}},'the-art-of-war_6':{pictureCaption:'Rows of fires and tents with nobody at any of them. From across the valley, nobody can tell where the army really is.',bridge:'Idea 5 was about where you are when it starts. This one is about what the other side can work out about it.',basics:{title:'Don\u2019t let them guess your next move',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE PENALTY',prompt:'You always kick penalties to the left. The goalie knows. What happens?',options:['You still score most times','The goalie dives left and saves it'],answer:1,reveal:'If the goalie knows, they only have to guard one side. If they don\u2019t know, they must guard both. Then each side is weaker.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The other side is weaker when they can\u2019t tell what you will do. They have to guard everything, so they are thin everywhere.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Lots of fires, but nobody sits at them. Armies long ago did this, so that from far away no one could tell where they really were.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE ROCK, PAPER, SCISSORS',text:'If you always play rock, your friend will soon always play paper. You lose every time. Mix it up, and they can\u2019t plan against you.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'So it is fine to lie to people.',reality:'No. In a game, keeping your next move to yourself is fair. Everyone knows that is part of the game. Lying to people about real things is different. It breaks trust.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your class is playing dodgeball. Which plan uses this idea?',options:['Throw at different players so no one knows who is next','Always throw at the same player to get good at it','Shout out who you will throw at, to be fair'],answer:0,why:'If no one knows where the next ball will go, everyone has to watch out. Nobody can just guard one spot.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'AN EARLY QUESTION',text:'The recruiter asks, early and pleasantly, what salary you would accept. You want to seem open, so you name a number. From that moment, the whole conversation happens below it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Skill in a contest can be measured by what the other side cannot work out. If they do not know where you will act, they must cover everything, and are weaker at every point.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE MECHANISM',nodes:['They can\u2019t tell where you will act','They cover several places at once','Each place gets less','You meet a divided opponent'],note:'You have not grown at all. Their strength was divided by your ambiguity.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A camp of fires with nobody at them. From the far side of the valley, the enemy cannot tell where the army is, so must guard against all of it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Not lying, just not giving it away',text:'You control how much of your intention is visible, and most people give it away for free, out of a wish to seem open. The everyday version is announcing the offer you would accept, or the concession you would make, before you need to. Once stated, it is spent.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before your next negotiation, decide what you will not reveal about your priorities, and hold it until it buys something.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A supplier asks which of three terms matters most to you. What does the idea suggest?',options:['Discuss all three without ranking them until a trade is on the table','Tell them straight away, since openness builds trust quickly','Pretend that a term you don\u2019t care about matters most','Refuse to discuss any of the terms until they make an offer'],answer:0,why:'Keeping your ranking to yourself is fair, and keeps all three in play. Refusing to talk only stalls the deal, and inventing a false priority is deception.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'SKILL, REDEFINED',text:'In chapter six Sun Tzu defines skill in attack as leaving the opponent unsure what to defend, and skill in defence as leaving them unsure what to attack. Strength, speed and numbers are absent from the definition. Skill is measured in the other side\u2019s uncertainty.'},{kind:'idea',text:'An opponent who knows where you will act concentrates there. One who does not must spread out, and is weaker everywhere. Ambiguity divides their strength without adding to yours.'},{kind:'term',word:'Legibility',meaning:'How easily someone else can read your intentions from what you say and do. Low legibility forces them to prepare for several possibilities instead of one.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Fires lit, tents pitched, nobody home. Every empty fire is a place the enemy must still watch.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The line in chapter one',text:'This pairs with the blunt claim in chapter one that warfare rests on deception. Applied outside war, the useful core is legibility. You control what can be read about your intentions, and nothing obliges you to publish it early.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Withholding and misleading are different acts, and the idea goes wrong at the line between them. Not volunteering your priorities is fair. Building a false picture in the mind of someone who relies on you is lying. In any relationship that lasts, a name for being unreadable also costs trust. In war that trade may be worth it. Among colleagues, friends and customers it rarely is.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What did you last reveal before you needed to? What could it have bought if you had held it?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'WHERE IS THE LINE?',prompt:'In an ordinary negotiation, which of these stays on the right side of the idea?',options:['Declining to say which concession you would trade first','Inventing a rival offer to push their price down','Claiming a deadline that you do not actually have','Feigning anger so they think you are about to leave'],answer:0,why:'Holding back what you value is legitimate. The others manufacture false facts, which turns reticence into lying.'}]}},'the-art-of-war_7':{pictureCaption:'The ladders were for the strongest part of the wall, and they have been left. The walker goes calmly to the gap, where it is weak.',bridge:'Idea 6 showed how uncertainty splits the other side. This one is what to do with a split opponent: be whole where they are not.',basics:{title:'Put your strength in one place',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A GARDEN HOSE',text:'Set a hose to spray, and it wets the whole lawn a little. Set it to jet, and it can blast mud off a wall. Same water. Different result.'},{kind:'idea',text:'You don\u2019t need to be stronger than the other side everywhere. You need to be stronger in the one place that counts. So put your strength there.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'FIVE THINGS AT ONCE',text:'You have a model to build, a poem to learn, a room to tidy, a book to read and a song to practise. You do ten minutes of each. A week later, none of them is done.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Spread out',items:['A bit of everything','Busy all week','Nothing finished']},right:{title:'All in one place',items:['One thing at a time','Some things wait','That thing gets done']}},{kind:'explain',kicker:'FROM AN OLD WAR BOOK',text:'The Art of War, a book from old China, says to keep your army together in one group. Make the enemy split up. Then you are many against their few, even if they have more in total.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'WHICH PLACE COUNTS?',caption:'Not the high wall. The ladders for it are left in a heap. He takes all his effort to the gap, where the wall is weakest.'},{kind:'try',text:'Look at what you have to do this week. Pick one thing to put most of your effort into. Let one other thing wait.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'In capture the flag, your team has ten players. Which plan uses this idea?',options:['Send most players together to one side of their base','Spread all ten players out evenly across the field','Keep every player at home to guard your own flag'],answer:0,why:'Ten players spread out are weak everywhere. Most of them together can beat the few guarding that side.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'SIX PRIORITIES',left:{title:'Six projects, even effort',items:['Each gets a sixth','All are defensible','None is finished']},right:{title:'One overwhelming',items:['It gets most of the week','Two others are left thin','It is done']},note:'The left feels responsible. The right wins.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A smaller force wins by being stronger at the point of contact, not overall. Concentrate on one point, avoid the other side\u2019s strength, and strike where they are weak.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'CHOOSING THE POINT',caption:'The ladders were for the strongest stretch of wall, and they lie abandoned. The point of contact has moved to the gap, where the wall is weakest.'},{kind:'explain',title:'A scheduling problem',text:'You do not need more than your rival in total. You need more than them in the one place where the contest actually happens. A team working on six priorities has none, and will be beaten at each by someone doing just one.'},{kind:'keyline',text:'Spreading effort evenly is the surest way to be weak everywhere while feeling busy.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The hard part',text:'Understanding this is easy. Choosing to be visibly weak somewhere, on purpose, is not. Leaving one thing underserved so another can be overwhelming feels like negligence. It reads as focus only afterwards.'},{kind:'try',text:'Name the things you are currently spreading across. Pick one to be overwhelming at this month, and choose two you will deliberately underserve.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A small bakery competes with a supermarket. Which strategy follows the idea?',options:['Be the best in town at one thing the supermarket does badly','Match the supermarket\u2019s range across every product it stocks','Cut prices on everything to compete on cost alone','Add a caf\xe9, a deli and a delivery service all at once'],answer:0,why:'The bakery cannot be bigger overall. It can be overwhelming at one point, chosen where the supermarket is weak.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'TWO LINES, ONE METHOD',picture:!0,text:'Chapter six puts two thoughts close together. First, keep your force as a single body while the enemy is made to divide, so that your whole meets a fraction of theirs. Second, like water running downhill, avoid what is strong and strike at what is weak.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Total strength matters less than strength at the point of contact. Concentrate your own force, make the opponent divide theirs, and choose the point where they are weakest.'},{kind:'term',word:'Local superiority',meaning:'Being stronger than the opponent at one particular place and time, whatever the overall balance. It is something you arrange, not something you own.'},{kind:'equation',parts:['Your whole force','Their divided force'],result:'Many against few',note:'Neither side\u2019s total changed. Only the arithmetic at the point of contact did.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How it links to idea 6',text:'Being hard to read supplies the division: an opponent who cannot tell where you will act must cover every place. Concentration supplies the use. Once they are spread, you meet one of their parts with all of yours.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Concentration is a bet. Whatever you leave thin is exposed, and an opponent who reads your choice can strike there instead. It also assumes you chose the right point; concentrate on the wrong one and you have lost everywhere at once. Some things cannot safely be left thin: safety, the law, the people doing the work. And striking where they are weak transfers to problems and markets, not to people. Aimed at a person\u2019s weak spot, it is just unkindness.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Win where contact happens, not in the totals.','Being unreadable divides them. Concentration uses the division.','Choose on purpose what you leave thin.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these misapplies the idea?',options:['Cutting back safety checks so the launch can get full attention','Pulling two people off minor work to finish the main project','Entering one market fully instead of three markets thinly','Letting a nice-to-have feature slip to perfect the core one'],answer:0,why:'Concentration means choosing what to leave thin. Some things cannot be left thin without a loss larger than the win.'}]}},'the-art-of-war_8':{pictureCaption:'Deep ruts, a road running on to the horizon, and the last sack empty. The long haul has eaten what it needed to finish.',bridge:'Idea 5 was about when you set off. This one is about how long you stay out, because time spent has a price.',basics:{title:'Dragging it out has a cost',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE POSTER',text:'You are making a poster for a class project. You redo the title four times to get it just right. By Thursday you are bored of it, and the rest is still blank.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The longer a job drags on, the more it uses up: your time, your energy, your interest. A good job that gets done can beat a perfect one that never does.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHAT DRAGGING DOES',nodes:['The job drags on','You get tired and bored','There is less of you left to finish it'],note:'The job eats the very things you need to finish it.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'A LONG TRIP',caption:'This cart has been on the road a long time. Look in the back. The food is all gone, and the road still goes on.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE AN ICE LOLLY',text:'You can\u2019t decide which end to bite first. You turn it this way and that. All the while, it is melting down your hand.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'So you should always rush.',reality:'No. The Art of War, the old book this comes from, admits that rushing can be silly too. The point is that time is not free. Ask what waiting will cost, as well as what it will add.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You wrote a story for a contest. It is good, but you want it perfect. The deadline is tomorrow. What does this idea say?',options:['Fix the one weakest part, then send it in','Keep polishing until it is perfect, even if you miss it','Send it now without reading it through once'],answer:0,why:'Waiting for perfect would cost you the whole contest. But sending it with no check is the silly kind of rush.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'EIGHTY OR NINETY',prompt:'You can launch today at eighty per cent, or in three months at ninety. What is the usual mistake in choosing?',options:['Treating the three months as free','Overrating the extra ten per cent','Underrating the risk of launching'],answer:0,reveal:'The three months come out of the same account that pays for the launch: money, momentum, the team\u2019s energy. Most plans count what the delay buys and never what it costs.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Long efforts use up their own conditions for success. Enthusiasm, money and attention all run down with time, so a better but slower plan can lose to a worse one that finishes.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'CHAPTER TWO',picture:!0,text:'Sun Tzu\u2019s second chapter is about how long a war lasts. If victory is slow, he argues, weapons dull and spirits sag, and a long campaign drains the state. He knows of no country, he says, that has gained from a long war.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'What the delay buys',items:['A more polished product','Fewer known flaws']},right:{title:'What the delay costs',items:['Three more months of salaries','A team that has gone stale','A window a rival may take']},note:'Put both columns in the same units before you decide.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'So the lesson is simply to hurry.',reality:'Sun Tzu grants that foolish haste exists. His claim is narrower: duration is a cost like any other, and most planning treats it as free.'},{kind:'try',text:'For your current project, work out what one more month costs in money, momentum and attention. Then compare it with what that month adds.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A team wants another quarter to perfect a report that is already useful. What would Sun Tzu\u2019s argument ask first?',options:['What will the extra quarter cost, set against what it adds?','How much better will the report be with more time?','How quickly could the report be finished if rushed?','Will anyone notice the remaining flaws once it is out?'],answer:0,why:'Asking only what time buys treats time as free. The argument is to price the delay as well.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'A COMMON SHORTENING',belief:'Sun Tzu says faster is always better.',reality:'He grants that foolish haste exists. His target is duration: a campaign that runs long eats the very means it needs, so delay is never free.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The bill',picture:!0,text:'Chapter two opens with the cost of putting an army in the field: chariots, armour, supplies and a heavy daily outlay of silver. From there the argument is plain. Every day the campaign lasts is paid for, and the money, the weapons and the soldiers\u2019 spirit all wear down.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Duration is a cost. A long effort consumes the enthusiasm, money and attention it depends on, so a slower, better plan can lose to a quicker, worse one that actually concludes.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE LOOP',nodes:['The campaign drags','Resources and morale wear down','Progress slows further'],loop:!0,note:'The delay feeds itself. That is why he treats it as a danger, not a nuisance.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'ONE MORE MONTH OF POLISH',text:'Another month of polish is paid from the same account that funds the launch: budget, the team\u2019s energy, the attention of the people waiting for it. Priced that way, the extra month often costs more than the polish is worth.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Sun Tzu is talking about wars that drain a state. Some work rewards patience: research, trust, anything that compounds. Shipping fast where errors are dangerous, in medicine or safety, passes the cost to other people. And the idea is easily used to excuse sloppy work. The honest version prices both sides of the delay.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What are you polishing right now? What has the extra time cost so far?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'WHERE IT STOPS',prompt:'In which case does the argument for speed apply *least*?',options:['A drug trial that wants a further year of safety data','A website redesign stuck in a third round of reviews','A product launch delayed for features nobody asked for','A team away-day that has been postponed three times'],answer:0,why:'There, the delay buys something large, and the cost of rushing falls on others. Pricing both sides favours waiting.'}]}},'the-art-of-war_9':{pictureCaption:'Alone in a quiet room, two rows of stones laid side by side. Each side is weighed against the other before anything happens.',bridge:'Idea 3 said a contest is decided before it starts. This is where Sun Tzu says the deciding is done: somewhere quiet, beforehand.',basics:{title:'Do your thinking before the big moment',beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'A BOOK FROM OLD CHINA',text:'The Art of War is about 2,500 years old. It is said to be by a Chinese general called Sun Tzu. At the end of its first part, he says the winner does lots of careful sums before the battle, in a quiet temple.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Do your big thinking early, somewhere quiet, before the moment comes. You think better when nobody is rushing you.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO PLACES TO THINK',left:{title:'In the moment',items:['Rushed','People watching','Easy to panic']},right:{title:'Before, somewhere quiet',items:['Time to think','No one watching','Easy to see clearly']}},{kind:'steps',title:'Before a big match',steps:['Sit somewhere quiet the day before.','Write down what your team is good at.','Write down what the other team is good at.','Make a plan that uses both lists.']},{kind:'picture',kicker:'TWO ROWS',caption:'A quiet room, and nobody rushing. One row of stones for each side, set out next to each other. Now the two sides are easy to compare.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it works',text:'You are not cleverer at home than on the pitch. You are just not rushed. That calm makes most of the difference.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You have to tell a friend you can\u2019t come to their party. When should you work out what to say?',options:['The night before, when it is quiet','Right when you see them at school','Only if they ask you about it'],answer:0,why:'In the moment you will feel rushed. Thinking it through first means you say it kindly and clearly.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TUESDAY',prompt:'The difficult conversation is on Friday. It is Tuesday, and nothing is written down. When will your judgement about it be best?',options:['Today, while nothing is happening','On Friday, when you can read the room','Thursday night, with a little pressure on'],answer:0,reveal:'Judgement under pressure is unreliable. Judgement in advance is not. You are not smarter on Tuesday. You are just not being rushed.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The work that decides an outcome is done before it, somewhere quiet, with no opponent present. Because there is no pressure and no audience, it is the easiest work to skip.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Structure is not bureaucracy',text:'Sun Tzu\u2019s first chapter lists what to weigh: five constant factors, then seven comparisons between the two sides. It is a checklist, run on purpose. The modern instinct calls that slow. His claim is the reverse: the structure is what makes quick decisions affordable later.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Your own temple',steps:['Pick a quiet hour well before the decision.','List the factors that will matter.','Compare your side with theirs, one factor at a time.','Note where you are weaker, and plan for it.']},{kind:'picture',kicker:'THE TEMPLE, TODAY',caption:'No opponent, no audience, no hurry. Two rows of stones, one for each side, compared one at a time in an empty room.'},{kind:'keyline',text:'The question is not how well you think on your feet, but how much you have arranged not to have to.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before your next high-stakes decision, write the comparison down, your position against theirs, factor by factor, while nothing is happening.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A manager is proud of handling tough meetings off the cuff. What would Sun Tzu say?',options:['Quick thinking is a backup, not the place to make the decision','Thinking on your feet is the most valuable skill a leader has','Tough meetings should wait until they can be fully scripted','Preparation matters only when the opponent is stronger'],answer:0,why:'He does not dismiss quick thinking. He says the outcome should be settled earlier, where judgement is more reliable.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'BEFORE A CAMPAIGN',word:'Temple calculations',meaning:'The formal weighing of both sides done in the temple before a campaign. Sun Tzu closes his first chapter on it: the general who calculates much wins, and the one who calculates little loses.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Outcomes are mostly settled by deliberate comparison done in advance, away from pressure. The temple matters because it is quiet, early and without an enemy present.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What gets calculated',text:'Earlier in the chapter he lists five constant factors: the unity of ruler and people, the weather and seasons, the terrain, the commander\u2019s qualities, and organisation and discipline. Then come seven questions comparing the two sides on those factors. It is a checklist, run deliberately.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'SIDE BY SIDE',caption:'Two rows laid side by side, one stone per factor, in an empty room. The comparison is made here, slowly, with no enemy present.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHY IT WORKS',nodes:['Judgement under pressure is unreliable','So move judgement to a calm hour','Decide there, in advance','Acting fast later becomes cheap'],note:'You are not smarter in the temple. You are just not being rushed.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where it sits in the book',text:'Idea 3 said battles reveal what was already decided. The temple is where that deciding happens, and Sun Tzu puts it in the very first chapter, before any talk of tactics. The book begins with the work that is easiest to skip.'},{kind:'limits',text:'A checklist weighs only the factors on it, and a precise comparison built on poor information is still wrong, which is why the book ends on getting information from people. Calculation can also become delay. And the frame is a war\u2019s: two sides and a fight. Before a hard talk with a friend or colleague, weigh the problem rather than their strengths against yours, or you prepare for a contest that need not happen.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Decide in advance, in quiet, by explicit comparison.','The gain is the absence of pressure, not extra cleverness.','The comparison is only as good as the information.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'Someone says: \u201cChecklists slow down decisive people.\u201d What is Sun Tzu\u2019s reply?',options:['The checklist is done early, so the decision can be fast when it counts','Decisive people should be slowed down, since speed causes defeat','Checklists are only for commanders who lack natural talent','Speed stops mattering once the comparison has been written down'],answer:0,why:'The structure moves the slow work to a calm hour. That is what lets the commander act quickly once things begin.'}]}},'the-art-of-war_10':{pictureCaption:'Clear sky, flat sea, and the last stone going into the storm wall. Readiness does not wait for the forecast to turn.',bridge:'Idea 2 said not losing is in your own hands. This one says what that rules out: plans that rely on the other side holding back.',basics:{title:'Get ready, don\u2019t just hope',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'IT PROBABLY WON\u2019T',belief:'If something probably won\u2019t happen, you don\u2019t need to get ready for it.',reality:'A guess like that can be right for ages, and then wrong once. The once is what hurts. Being ready works either way.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Don\u2019t count on the bad thing not happening. Count on being ready if it does. That part is up to you.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE GAME SAVE',text:'You have played a game for weeks. You never keep a spare copy of your save, because it has never been lost. Then one day it is. Weeks of play, gone. A copy would have taken a minute.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'HOPE OR READY?',left:{title:'A hope',items:['It probably won\u2019t rain','I won\u2019t need a pencil','My bike won\u2019t get a flat']},right:{title:'Ready',items:['A coat in my bag','A spare pencil','I can fix a flat']},note:'A hope needs luck. Being ready does not.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'ON A CALM DAY',caption:'The sea is calm, but he still sets the last stone in the wall. Now he is ready, storm or no storm.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'FROM AN OLD BOOK',text:'The Art of War, a book from old China, says a general should not trust the enemy to stay away. He should trust his own readiness. A plan that needs the other side to hold back is not a plan. It is a wish.'},{kind:'try',text:'Find one thing you are just hoping won\u2019t go wrong. Do one small thing today so you are ready if it does.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You are going on a long walk with your family. The sky is clear. What does this idea say?',options:['Pack a coat anyway, in case it rains','Leave the coat, since it will probably stay dry','Look at the sky halfway and then decide'],answer:0,why:'A clear sky is a guess about the weather. A coat in the bag works whatever the weather does.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'ONE PERSON',text:'Your whole pipeline runs through one person who has never once let you down. Nobody else knows how their part works. You have never needed anyone else to.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Do not rely on the other side not acting. Rely on being ready if they do. If a plan needs someone else to hold back, it is a hope.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'A forecast',items:['Can be right for years','Wrong once, and it counts','Not yours to control']},right:{title:'A preparation',items:['Works whether you guessed right','Unaffected by the once','Fully yours']}},{kind:'picture',kicker:'A GOOD FORECAST',caption:'A clear sky and a flat sea: the forecast is good. The wall is finished anyway, because a wall works whatever the weather decides.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Bets you did not notice placing',text:'No backup, because the drive probably won\u2019t fail. No second supplier, because prices probably won\u2019t rise. No savings, because the contract will probably renew. Each is a bet that the world stays ordinary, placed without noticing a bet was made.'},{kind:'steps',title:'The test',steps:['Find the place where your plan needs someone not to act.','Imagine that they act.','Change the plan until it survives that.']},{kind:'try',text:'Pick the one person or supplier your work most depends on. Write down what you would do on the day they are gone.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A small firm earns most of its income from one loyal client. What does the idea recommend?',options:['Build enough other business to survive if the client goes','Keep the client so happy that they never think of leaving','Ask the client to promise to stay for another five years','Trust the relationship, since it has never once failed'],answer:0,why:'Keeping the client happy is good, but still relies on their choice. Only other business keeps the firm safe whatever they decide.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'CHAPTER EIGHT',picture:!0,text:'In the chapter on varying tactics, Sun Tzu states what a general may rely on. Not the likelihood that the enemy will stay away, but his own readiness to meet them. Not the chance that they will not attack, but the fact that his position cannot be taken.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Confidence belongs in preparations, not forecasts. A plan that needs the other side to hold back is a hope. A plan that survives their acting is a plan.'},{kind:'term',word:'Tail risk',meaning:'The chance of a rare, severe event at the far end of the range of outcomes. Easy to ignore because it almost never happens; costly because when it does, it dominates.'},{kind:'scale',low:'Relies on what they will probably do',high:'Survives whatever they do',note:'Sun Tzu allows only the right-hand end as grounds for confidence.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why you only own one kind',text:'A forecast can be right for years and then wrong once, and the once is what counts. A preparation does not care whether you guessed correctly. That is the asset Sun Tzu says you may rely on, because it is the only one in your hands.'},{kind:'limits',text:'You cannot make every position unassailable. Readiness costs money and attention, and a plan defended against everything does nothing else. The skill is choosing which tails are severe enough to cover. Pushed too far, the idea becomes paranoia, and treats every partner as an enemy about to strike.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Where does your current plan quietly need someone else not to do something?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE HOPE',prompt:'On Sun Tzu\u2019s test, which of these is still a hope?',options:['A strong contract that makes the supplier unlikely to walk away','A second supplier already approved and tested','Enough stock to cover three months without supply','A process that two different people can each run alone'],answer:0,why:'The contract lowers the odds of them acting but does not survive it. The others work whether or not the supplier walks.'}]}},'the-art-of-war_11':{pictureCaption:'The map shows the ground as it usually is. The sketch, made by someone who was there, shows it as it is now.',bridge:'Idea 9 weighed both sides in advance. This one asks where the facts about the other side should come from.',basics:{title:'To know what\u2019s going on, ask someone who knows',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A NEW SCHOOL',prompt:'You are starting at a new school. How can you find out which teacher is strict?',options:['Guess, from the teachers at your old school','Ask a kid who has been there a year','Wait and see what happens'],answer:1,reveal:'Your old school tells you what teachers are often like. It can\u2019t tell you about *this* teacher. The kid who has been there knows.'},{kind:'idea',text:'To know what is really going on, ask a person who knows. Your past can tell you what usually happens. It can\u2019t tell you what is happening now.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE LAST CHAPTER',text:'The Art of War is an old book from China. It ends on this idea. It says you can\u2019t learn what the enemy is doing from spirits. You can\u2019t work it out from old battles. You can only learn it from people who know.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'MAP OR SKETCH?',caption:'The big map shows what the land is usually like. The small muddy sketch was made by someone who was just there. That is the one to trust.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE THE WEATHER',text:'Last week was sunny every day. That does not tell you about today. To know about today, look out of the window, or ask someone who just came in.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it pays',text:'Asking takes a minute. A wrong guess can cost a lot more. The book is harsh about leaders who would not pay to find out.'},{kind:'try',text:'Is there something you are just guessing about? Ask one person who really knows.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your class is planning a bake sale. How should you find out what to bake?',options:['Ask some kids what they would buy','Bake what sold well at your old school','Bake whatever you like to eat best'],answer:0,why:'Your old school and your own taste are guesses. The kids who will buy can tell you.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'EXPERIENCE',belief:'With enough experience, you can tell what the other side will do.',reality:'Sun Tzu rejects this outright. Experience tells you what usually happens. It cannot tell you what this particular counterparty intends this week. Only someone who knows can.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Knowledge of a specific situation cannot come from signs or your own past. It comes from people who actually know, and getting it is almost always cheaper than going without.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Where it can\u2019t come from',items:['Spirits and omens','Your own past','What usually happens']},right:{title:'Where it does',items:['People who know','About this situation','As it is this week']}},{kind:'picture',kicker:'FROM THE GROUND',caption:'The map is what usually holds. The muddy sketch in the hands is what someone actually saw on the ground, and it outranks the map.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The economics',text:'Sun Tzu is scathing here. Armies face each other for years over a victory settled in a single day, yet commanders grudge the cost of finding out what they face. The information is almost always cheaper than the ignorance.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE QUARTER',text:'You are about to commit a quarter to what the market probably wants. The plan rests on assumptions made inside the building. A conversation with five actual customers was available the whole time.'},{kind:'try',text:'Identify the assumption your current plan most depends on. This week, get it from a person who knows rather than from inference.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A council plans a new bus route from how people travelled ten years ago. What does the idea recommend?',options:['Ask current residents where they actually need to go','Trust the old figures, since habits rarely change much','Copy the route that worked well in a nearby town','Run the route and adjust it after a year of complaints'],answer:0,why:'Old figures and other towns are inference. The people who will ride the bus know where they need to go.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'THE FINAL CHAPTER',word:'Foreknowledge',meaning:'Knowing the enemy\u2019s situation before you act. Sun Tzu names it as what lets a ruler and general achieve what ordinary people cannot, then spends the chapter on how to get it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Foreknowledge cannot be had from spirits, from comparison with past events, or by calculation. Knowledge of the other side\u2019s position can only come from people who have it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'A chapter about spies',text:'The chapter is about spies, and Sun Tzu sorts them into kinds by how they are recruited and used. The principle beneath it is broader. Your experience tells you what usually happens. It cannot tell you what this opponent intends now. For that you need someone who knows.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'TWO KINDS OF KNOWING',caption:'The map is comparison with what is usually true. The field sketch is knowledge from a person who was there. Only the second is foreknowledge.'},{kind:'keyline',text:'Information is almost always cheaper than the ignorance it replaces.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The economics',text:'He is harsh about the cost. Armies may face each other for years over a victory decided in a single day. A commander who stays ignorant to save the price of finding out is, in his view, failing his people. The modern version is a strategy built on what the market probably wants, with no customer asked.'},{kind:'limits',text:'People can be wrong, partial or lying, and Sun Tzu knew it: part of the chapter is about passing false reports to the enemy through spies. So a source is evidence to weigh, not an oracle. Five customers are not a market. And the chapter\u2019s methods, bribes and turned agents, do not transfer to ordinary life. Ask people openly. Do not spy on them.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What does your plan assume about someone else\u2019s intentions? Who could simply tell you?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A founder is sure customers want a feature because her last company\u2019s customers did. What is the Sun Tzu objection?',options:['Her past is inference by comparison, not knowledge of these customers','She should rely on market reports rather than on her instincts','Her experience is too recent to be a reliable guide yet','Founders should never build what customers ask them for'],answer:0,why:'The objection is not that experience is worthless. It is that it cannot tell you about this particular case. Only these customers can.'}]}},'the-art-of-war_12':{pictureCaption:'Summit close and bare, storm coming in. The climber heads down, flag still rolled: a retreat chosen on the situation, not on how it looks.',bridge:'Idea 4 noted that careful victories earn no credit. This one asks what goes wrong when a commander decides for the credit.',basics:{title:'Decide as if no one is watching',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE SCHOOL SHOW',text:'You told everyone you would do a hard magic trick at the school show. In practice it keeps going wrong. An easier trick would work. But then everyone would know you gave up.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Don\u2019t choose what to do because of how it will look. Go for things because they are worth it. Stop things when they stop working, even if people see.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO TRAPS',left:{title:'Showing off',items:['I want people to clap','So I pick the flashy thing']},right:{title:'Saving face',items:['I don\u2019t want to look silly','So I keep doing what fails']},note:'Both traps are about the people watching, not the thing itself.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE BEST GENERAL',text:'The Art of War, an old book from China, says the best general goes forward without wanting to be famous. He pulls back without being scared of shame. He only thinks about keeping his people safe.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The top is close. A storm is coming. The climber walks back down, the flag still rolled up on the pack. It may look like giving up. It is the right call.'},{kind:'try',text:'Is there something you only keep doing because stopping would look bad? Ask: would I choose this if nobody knew?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You joined a club and told everyone you love it. Now you dread it every week. What does this idea say?',options:['Decide as if nobody would ever know you left','Keep going, because you said you loved it','Keep going until someone else leaves first'],answer:0,why:'How it looks is not a good reason to stay or to go. Decide on whether the club is worth it to you.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE PROJECT YOU BACKED',prompt:'You argued publicly for a project. It is now clearly failing, and everyone can see that you know. What is mainly keeping it alive?',options:['The chance it might still recover','The cost of being seen to stop','The work already put into it'],answer:1,reveal:'The work already spent is gone either way. The chance of recovery is small, and you know it. What remains is the audience: stopping would be visible.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Decide on the situation, not the audience. Advance because the prize is worth winning, not because winning would look good. Retreat when you should, even though the retreat will be seen.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'CHAPTER TEN',text:'Sun Tzu prizes above all a general who moves forward without chasing glory and pulls back without dreading shame, whose only concern is protecting the country and serving it well. Such a commander, he says, is a treasure to the state.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Advancing for fame',items:['Engages because winning looks good','Picks fights not worth winning']},right:{title:'Fearing disgrace',items:['Continues because stopping is visible','Pays to avoid admitting it']},note:'The second is the more common failure, and the more expensive.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Sunk cost with a reputation',text:'Once quitting has a social price, people keep paying an operational one to avoid it, and the operational price compounds. The audience is usually real. Sun Tzu\u2019s point is not that reputation does not matter, but that it cannot be an input to the decision.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The summit is a short climb away, and bare. With the storm closing in, the climber heads back down, the flag still rolled on the pack. Planting it would have looked better.'},{kind:'try',text:'Identify one commitment you are continuing mainly because stopping would be embarrassing. Put a price on what continuing costs this quarter.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A manager is about to close a failing product line they launched with fanfare. Which test does the idea supply?',options:['Would I make this call if nobody ever learned who made it?','Will closing it now look worse than closing it later?','How much has already been spent on this product line?','Can I present the closure as a strategic success?'],answer:0,why:'The others ask about appearances or money already gone. The test removes both and leaves only the situation.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'ask',kicker:'BEFORE YOU READ ON',prompt:'Which of your current commitments would you drop tomorrow if nobody would ever know you had dropped it?'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'CHAPTER TEN',text:'In the chapter on terrain, after listing the ways an army is ruined by its general\u2019s faults, Sun Tzu describes the general worth most to a state. He advances without seeking glory, retreats without fearing shame, and thinks only of protecting the people and serving the ruler well.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Reputation must not be an input to the decision. Advance for the objective, not the glory. Retreat when the position is lost, whatever the shame. A commander deciding for appearances decides worse, and in the end loses the reputation too.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The footprints run up close to the bare summit. Now, with the storm coming in, the climber descends with the flag unplanted: a visible retreat, chosen on the situation alone.'},{kind:'term',word:'Sunk cost',meaning:'Effort or money already spent that cannot be recovered whatever you do next. A sound decision ignores it. Fear of disgrace attaches a reputation to it, which makes it much harder to ignore.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE FEAR COMPOUNDS',nodes:['Stopping would be visible','So the losing position continues','The losses grow','Stopping now looks even worse'],loop:!0,note:'Each round raises the social price of quitting, so the real price keeps climbing.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why he frames it this way',text:'Notice whom the ideal general serves: the country and its people, not his own name. Sun Tzu is defining what goes into the decision. The situation and the objective go in. The general\u2019s reputation stays out, because a commander optimising for how decisions look will reliably make worse ones.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Reputation is sometimes a real asset: a retreat can embolden an opponent, or cost trust you will need later. Then appearances are part of the situation, not vanity. The ideal also assumes a cause worth serving; loyal service to a bad ruler is no virtue. Off the battlefield, a commitment others rely on is more than face: quitting it can let real people down. And the test of acting as if unobserved is hard to run honestly on yourself.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HARD CASE',prompt:'A leader keeps a struggling office open, saying closing it would alarm investors. When is that consistent with the idea?',options:['When investors\u2019 alarm would cause real harm the decision must weigh','Never, because reputation can play no part in any decision','Always, since a leader\u2019s standing is part of the firm\u2019s value','Only if the leader was the one who chose to open the office'],answer:0,why:'The question is whether the effect on others is a real consequence or only the leader\u2019s pride. Real consequences belong in the decision. Vanity does not.'}]}}}},3014,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'the-attention-budget_1':{pictureCaption:'One page full, one page blank, and the pencil has stopped. The good hours went into the first page.',basics:{title:'Your brain has a few strong hours a day',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A LONG SATURDAY',prompt:'You have a whole Saturday to build a hard model kit. How many hours of really careful work can you do?',options:['All day, if you keep going','A few hours, then you get sloppy'],answer:1,reveal:'After a few hours of hard thinking, the work usually gets worse. You can keep going, but wanting it more does not seem to fix it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Your brain has only a few good hours of hard thinking each day. People who kept careful notes on their work found about three to five. So use those hours for the hardest thing.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE POCKET MONEY',text:'Your thinking power is like pocket money for the day. Once it is spent, it is spent. If you spend it all on sweets in the morning, there is none left for the thing you really wanted.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One page is full. The next is blank. The pencil has stopped. The strong hours went into the first page.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'AFTER SCHOOL',text:'You get home and play games, then chat with friends, then start your hard maths at nine at night. You are tired, and it takes ages. Do the maths first, while your brain is fresh. Save the easy stuff for later.'},{kind:'try',text:'Tomorrow, notice when your brain feels sharpest. Do your hardest job then. Leave easy jobs, like tidying, for when you feel tired.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You have a hard project and some easy chores. When should you do the project?',options:['When your brain is freshest, before the chores','After the chores, so they are out of the way','Late at night, when the house is quiet'],answer:0,why:'Your best hours are few. Spend them on the hard thing. Chores can use the tired hours.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THREE IN THE AFTERNOON',text:'It is three o\u2019clock. You have answered forty emails, sat through two meetings and tidied a spreadsheet. The hardest thing on your list has not been started, and when you open it, nothing comes.'},{kind:'idea',text:'For demanding work, the limit is not time but attention, and you get roughly three to five good hours a day. Everything after that is real work, but a different kind. The question is what gets the good hours.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where the figure comes from',text:'Writers, researchers and composers who kept detailed records show a consistent pattern: three to five hours of genuinely demanding work a day, then sharply diminishing returns. The article adds research, mainly on violinists, whose daily deliberate practice clustered around the same ceiling.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why wanting it more does not help',text:'One suggested mechanism is depletion rather than motivation, though that idea is contested. Sustained effortful attention seems to have a cost that builds up through the day, and it does not respond to wanting it more. So the answer is allocation, not extension.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'How the day usually goes',items:['Good hours: email and meetings','Depleted hours: the hard work','Hard work done badly, or not at all']},right:{title:'Budgeted',items:['Good hours: the hardest task','Depleted hours: email and meetings','Email barely suffers']},note:'Same day, same hours. Only the allocation changed.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One page full, one page blank, and the pencil has stopped. The good hours went into the first page.'},{kind:'try',text:'Identify your best three hours and put the hardest work in them before you schedule anything else. Defend them as you would a meeting you cannot move.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Your best thinking happens between eight and eleven. Which plan applies this idea?',options:['Write the strategy paper from eight to eleven, emails after lunch','Clear the inbox first so the paper has your full attention','Work on the paper until nine at night to finish it faster','Spread the paper across short slots right through the day'],answer:0,why:'The hard work gets the good hours. Clearing email first spends them on work that did not need them.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE ARTICLE\u2019S TARGET',text:'Most productivity advice treats time as the constraint: more hours, better scheduled. The article argues that for most knowledge work the constraint is attention, and that attention behaves like a budget rather than a clock. A budget can be spent well or badly, but not stretched.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Demanding cognitive work draws on a roughly fixed daily allowance of about three to five hours. The useful question is not how to work longer but what gets those hours.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The evidence it cites',text:'Two sources point the same way. Writers, researchers and composers who kept detailed records cluster at three to five hours of demanding work a day. Research on deliberate practice, mainly among violinists, found it clustering around the same ceiling regardless of ambition or discipline. How far that carries to other fields is less clear.'},{kind:'term',word:'Deliberate practice',meaning:'Effortful work aimed at improving a particular skill, done at the edge of your ability with full attention. It is the kind of work the ceiling describes, not every hour spent at a desk.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'TWO KINDS OF WORK',low:'Demanding: draws on the budget',high:'Routine: barely touches it',note:'Hours beyond the good ones are real work, but of the right-hand kind. Treating the two as interchangeable is why the day disappears.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The finished page took the good hours. The blank one is waiting for a mind that has already spent them.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The article is careful here. The evidence is strongest for demanding cognitive work and does not generalise to everything a working day contains; plenty of useful activity does not draw on the same account. The figure is a range, not a rule; the research behind it is mainly on violinists, and depletion as the mechanism is contested. Treat four hours as a planning estimate, not a law.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE OVERREACH',prompt:'Which conclusion goes beyond what the idea supports?',options:['Nobody should work more than four hours a day','Put the hardest work where attention is freshest','Hours after the peak suit more routine tasks','Wanting it more will not stretch the good hours'],answer:0,why:'The ceiling applies to demanding work. Other work does not draw on the same account, so a four-hour working day does not follow.'}]}},'the-attention-budget_2':{pictureCaption:'He is turning to the phone, but one hand stays on the page. The thread is the attention he has not yet brought with him.',bridge:'Idea 1 said the good hours are few. This one shows how switching quietly eats them.',basics:{title:'Switching has a hidden cost',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'ONE QUICK LOOK',text:'You are reading a book for school. Your phone buzzes. You look for a few seconds. Then you go back to the book, and read the same line three times.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When you switch from one thing to another, part of your mind stays on the first thing. So each switch costs more than it seems. Lots of switches can eat a whole afternoon.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'He is turning to the phone. But one hand stays on the page. Part of him has not moved yet.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE STICKY HANDS',text:'After you eat honey, your hands stay sticky. Whatever you touch next, a bit of honey comes too. Your mind is like that after a switch. A bit of the last thing sticks to the next one.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why you can\u2019t see it',text:'Each switch feels quick. A look takes seconds. But finding your place again takes a few minutes. No clock shows those minutes, because you were sitting there the whole time.'},{kind:'try',text:'Do your homework with the phone in another room. Check messages once, at the end. See if it feels different.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Jo checks her messages after every sum. She has twenty sums. Why does it take her so long?',options:['Each check costs extra time to get back into the sums','Reading messages is just very slow','Sums are always slow for everyone'],answer:0,why:'The looks are short, but getting back each time is not. Twenty switches add up.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'NINE TO SIX',prompt:'You were busy all day, nine to six, and produced nothing you could point to. What is the likeliest cause?',options:['You did not work hard enough','The day was broken into small pieces','The tasks were simply too hard'],answer:1,reveal:'Twenty small interruptions did not cost twenty small amounts. They cost the long stretch that would have produced the thing.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Moving between tasks leaves a residue: part of your attention stays with the previous problem. Each switch feels instant, so the cost is invisible, but across a fragmented day it compounds into being busy without producing.'},{kind:'term',word:'Attention residue',meaning:'A name used in research for the part of your attention that stays with an unfinished task after you move on. While it lingers, performance on the new task drops.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Seconds and minutes',text:'Glancing at a message takes seconds. Returning to the paragraph, finding your place and rebuilding the argument you had in your head takes, by the article\u2019s estimate, closer to three minutes. Those minutes never appear on a clock, because you were at your desk the whole time.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW A DAY DISSOLVES',nodes:['A long task begins','A quick switch','Residue, then a slow return','Another switch before it settles'],note:'The stretch that would have produced something never gets long enough to exist.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Fewer fragments, not more discipline',text:'The fix is not trying harder inside the fragments. It is having fewer of them: batching messages and other switches into a few known moments, so the long stretches between them can exist at all.'},{kind:'try',text:'Tomorrow, pick one two-hour block and count every switch: every glance at a message or a tab. Multiply the count by three minutes. That is roughly the fee.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Your team answers chat messages within a minute, all day long. What change does this idea point to?',options:['Agree set times to check messages, with long gaps between','Ask everyone to reply faster so that threads close sooner','Encourage people to concentrate harder between messages','Move the chat to email so that it feels more formal'],answer:0,why:'The cost is in the switching. Batching it into known moments makes room for long, unbroken stretches.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE CLOCK VIEW',belief:'A three-second glance costs three seconds.',reality:'Each switch leaves part of your attention behind, and coming back means rebuilding what you had in your head. The glance is the visible part of a cost that mostly never appears on any clock.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Task switching charges an invisible fee: residue lowers performance on the next task, and the return to the first costs minutes, not seconds. Across a day the fees compound into busyness without output.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What is established',text:'The laboratory effect is well documented: move on before the first task is finished, and part of your attention stays with it while performance on the second drops. How large the effect is across a real working day is harder to measure. The article holds only that its direction is not in dispute.'},{kind:'equation',parts:['Twenty small switches','Residue after each'],result:'No long stretch left',note:'The loss is not twenty small amounts. It is the sustained stretch that would have produced the thing.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why discipline misses',text:'If the cost lives in the switch, then concentrating harder within each fragment changes little. The lever is structural: fewer fragments, with switches batched into a few known moments, so that long stretches can exist at all.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Turning away, one hand still on the page. The thread between them is the residue: attention that has not yet followed him.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The three-minute figure is the article\u2019s estimate, not a measured constant, and multiplying by it is a way to make the fee visible, not an exact bill. Some work is short by nature and loses little to switching. And batching has a cost: a message that needed an answer now waits, which suits some jobs and not others.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A manager finds staff spend only ten minutes a day reading messages, and concludes messages cost little. What has the measure missed?',options:['The return time after each check, which no clock records','The time spent writing longer replies at the end of the day','The chance that some staff under-report their message time','The stress caused by reading messages, not their duration'],answer:0,why:'The visible time is the glances. The residue and the rebuilding after each one never appear on a timesheet.'}]}},'the-attention-budget_3':{pictureCaption:'One switch, flicked once. Behind it, bells queue out of sight, each waiting to ring at a moment you did not choose.',bridge:'Idea 2 priced each switch. This one finds where many of the switches were decided.',basics:{title:'Every ping is a promise you made',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'AN OPEN DOORBELL',text:'Imagine you told everyone in town: ring my bell any time, and I will come to the door. At dinner. During homework. In the middle of a film. That is what turning on alerts is like.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Each alert you switch on is a promise: I will stop and look whenever this pings. You made the promise once. It gets used many times a day, at times you did not pick.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One switch, flicked once. Look at the line of bells behind it. Each one will ring later, when you are busy.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE GAME ALERT',text:'A game pings you: *Your energy is full!* You were halfway through a drawing. You look, and play for a bit. The game chose the moment, not you.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The ping is not the cost',text:'Reading an alert takes a few seconds. The cost is getting back to what you were doing. That takes much longer, and you don\u2019t feel it happening.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Clean up your alerts',steps:['Turn every alert off.','Go through them one at a time.','Turn back on only the ones worth stopping for.']},{kind:'question',prompt:'Which alert is worth keeping on, by this idea?',options:['A message from your parent about picking you up','A game telling you a new prize is ready','A video app saying someone posted a video'],answer:0,why:'Ask: would I really stop what I am doing for this? For a lift home, yes. The others can wait until you choose to look.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'JUST A SETTING',belief:'Notification settings are a small matter of preference.',reality:'Each notification you switch on is a standing agreement to hand over your focus to whoever asks next. You made the decision once, in the abstract. It now runs dozens of times a day.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A notification is a decision about your attention made in advance, to fire at a moment the sender cannot see. You are handing over an unknown future minute to whatever arrives. You do not know what you will be doing when it does.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One switch, flicked once. Behind it, bells queue out of sight, each waiting to ring at a moment you did not choose.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'When you decide',items:['Once, in a settings screen','In the abstract','Calm, with nothing at stake']},right:{title:'When it fires',items:['Dozens of times a day','In specific moments','Mid-paragraph, mid-thought, mid-call']}},{kind:'explain',title:'Where the cost is',text:'Not in the seconds spent reading. In the re-entry: rebuilding the state you were in. That takes much longer than the interruption. Yet it is not felt as a cost, because nothing visibly happens while it runs.'},{kind:'explain',title:'A budget, not a preference',text:'Treat each notification you allow as a standing claim on a limited account. The sensible default is then that almost nothing qualifies. That is the opposite of how every device ships.'},{kind:'try',text:'Turn off every notification. Then turn back on only the ones you would knowingly pay for.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A new app asks to send you notifications. Which question best applies this idea?',options:['Would I pay to be interrupted by this, at any moment?','Will its notifications be useful from time to time?','Can I easily switch them off later if they annoy me?','Are they quiet enough not to disturb the people near me?'],answer:0,why:'A notification claims unknown future moments. Only what is worth that price should get the claim.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'A SETTINGS SCREEN, MONTHS AGO',picture:!0,text:'Months ago, setting up a new phone, you tapped Allow on a dozen prompts in a minute. Each tap took a second and felt like nothing. Today those taps will interrupt you again and again, each at a moment the sender could not see and you did not choose.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Every enabled notification is pre-committed attention: a decision made once, in the abstract, that executes again and again at moments nobody chose. Budget them as standing claims; the right default is that almost nothing qualifies.'},{kind:'term',word:'Pre-commitment',meaning:'Binding your future self to an action in advance. Usually a tool of self-control; here it runs the other way, committing future minutes to whoever happens to send something.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'ONE DECISION, MANY COSTS',nodes:['You allow notifications, once','A sender fires one','It lands mid-task','Re-entry: rebuild the state'],note:'The first step happens once. The last three repeat, dozens of times a day.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The asymmetry',text:'The sender knows what they want to say and nothing about what you are doing. You, at the moment of allowing, knew neither. The only one who could judge whether this minute should be given up is you, at that minute, and that is the one judgement the arrangement never asks for.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the default matters',text:'Devices ship with almost everything enabled. If each notification is a standing claim on a limited budget, that default is backwards. Turning everything off and re-enabling only what you would pay for reverses the burden of proof.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Some notifications are worth their cost: a call from your child\u2019s school, an alert that a system is down. For some jobs, being reachable is the work. And switching everything off can simply move the problem: check by hand every few minutes and you have rebuilt the interruptions without the bell. The budget only helps if checking is deliberate too.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HARD CASE',prompt:'A doctor on call must respond to emergencies within minutes. How does this idea apply to her?',options:['Keep the emergency channel on, as it is worth the price; cut the rest','Turn every notification off, since all interruptions cost focus','Leave everything on, since her job requires being reachable','Check every app by hand every few minutes instead of alerts'],answer:0,why:'The idea is a budget, not a ban. A claim truly worth its re-entry cost earns its place; the rest still have to justify theirs.'}]}},'the-attention-budget_4':{pictureCaption:'Hands in the washing-up, mind on nothing. Above, loose threads are tying themselves into one knot. That is the work idle time does.',bridge:'Ideas 1 to 3 protected focused attention. This one argues for attention that is not aimed at anything.',basics:{title:'Bored time is thinking time',beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'A MATHEMATICIAN\u2019S HABIT',text:'Henri Poincar\xe9 was a famous French mathematician who lived more than a hundred years ago. He wrote about how answers to hard problems came to him. Often they came when he was not working on them at all.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When you are doing nothing much, like walking or washing up, your mind seems to join up things you learned before. If you fill every gap with a screen, that joining up has no time to happen.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Hands in the washing-up. Mind on nothing. Above, loose threads are tying themselves into a knot.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE SORTING YOUR ROOM',text:'All day you throw things into your room. Only when you stop adding new things is there time to sort them. A mind with a screen always on is a room that never gets sorted.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'IN THE QUEUE',text:'You are stuck on a riddle. You give up and walk to the shop. In the queue, the answer just pops into your head. You were not trying. The empty time did the work.'},{kind:'try',text:'Today, do one boring job with no phone: a walk, the washing-up, waiting for the bus. Keep a note of any idea that pops up.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Why might it be good to walk home sometimes with no music and no phone?',options:['Your mind gets time to join up things it learned','Walking is faster when you are not listening to music','Being bored is good for you because it is hard'],answer:0,why:'Empty time is when ideas can link up. Filling every gap takes that time away.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'AT THE SINK',text:'You spent the afternoon stuck on a problem at your desk. That evening, hands in the washing-up, the answer arrives whole. You were not thinking about it. You were not thinking about anything.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Unfilled time, such as walking, washing up and waiting, is when the mind connects things it took in earlier. Filling every gap with a feed does not steal time. It steals the processing that makes what you read useful.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What seems to be going on',text:'Periods of undirected attention appear to be when the brain does a particular kind of work: consolidating, connecting and retrieving material that focused effort cannot reach directly. The answer in the shower is the familiar sign. The mathematician Henri Poincar\xe9 described the experience precisely from his own work.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Gaps, once',items:['The queue','The commute','The wait']},right:{title:'Gaps, now',items:['The queue, with a feed','The commute, with a feed','The wait, with a feed']},note:'The filling is pleasant enough that the absence of the other thing is never noticed.'},{kind:'explain',title:'An argument without guilt',text:'Most reasons to put the phone down rely on feeling bad. This one does not. Empty time is not rest from work but a different mode of work, and it is the mode that produces conclusions you were not looking for.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Hands in the washing-up, mind on nothing. Above, loose threads are tying themselves into one knot. That is the work idle time does.'},{kind:'try',text:'Leave one gap today genuinely empty, phone out of reach. Afterwards, write down anything that surfaced.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You have been stuck on a design problem for two hours. Which next step fits this idea best?',options:['Take a walk without your phone and let it sit','Read articles about the problem on your phone','Push on for another hour until it finally breaks','Answer emails for a while until you feel fresh'],answer:0,why:'Undirected time is when the mind connects what it already has. A feed or an inbox would fill exactly that gap.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'THE MATHEMATICIAN\u2019S ACCOUNT',text:'Henri Poincar\xe9, the French mathematician, described from his own work the experience this article builds on: a solution that would not come at the desk arriving later, during an undemanding moment away from it. It is a famous account of something most people have felt.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Undirected attention appears to do work that focused effort cannot: consolidating, connecting and retrieving. The gaps that used to provide it are now reliably filled, so it has to be protected on purpose, as a different mode of work rather than as rest.'},{kind:'term',word:'Undirected attention',meaning:'Attention not aimed at any task or input: the mind on a walk, in a queue, at the washing-up. Neither sleep nor concentration, but a third state.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What changed',text:'The process is not new; its availability is. Queues, commutes and waiting once supplied this time for free. Now they are filled, and the filling is pleasant enough that nobody notices what is missing. The loss is not time but processing: what you read stays unconnected.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'WHAT FILLS A GAP',low:'Filled: a feed in every gap',high:'Empty: nothing coming in',note:'The article asks for some time at the right-hand end: not rest, but a mode in which nothing new arrives and the mind works on what it already holds.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Nothing is being consumed at this sink. That is why the threads overhead have room to knot.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The claim is hedged for good reason: the article says such periods appear to do this work, not that they always do. Empty time makes nothing from nothing; it connects material you have already taken in through focused effort. And some gaps, such as driving, are no place for drifting. The test the article offers is fair: do you ever reach a conclusion you were not looking for?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TEST THE CLAIM',prompt:'On the article\u2019s own test, how would you know whether protected empty time is working for you?',options:['You reach conclusions you were not looking for','You feel more rested at the end of each week','You get more done in your focused hours','You spend fewer hours in total on your phone'],answer:0,why:'That is what undirected time produces and directed effort does not. Rest, output and screen time measure other things.'}]}},'the-attention-budget_5':{pictureCaption:'The desk is cleared for the night, one pen set where the hand will find it first. Tomorrow starts with doing, not deciding.',bridge:'Idea 1 said the good hours are few. This one stops the first of them being spent on choosing.',basics:{title:'Pick tomorrow\u2019s job tonight',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'MORNING PLANNING',belief:'The best time to decide what to do is the morning, when your head is clear.',reality:'Your clear head is precious. If you use it up on choosing, there is less left for doing. Choose the night before instead.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'SATURDAY MORNING',text:'You wake up fresh. You could do your project, or practise piano, or tidy up. You think about it. You check a message. Half an hour later you still have not started.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Before bed, pick the most important job for tomorrow and write it down. Then in the morning you just start. Your fresh brain goes on doing, not choosing.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The desk is cleared for the night. One pen waits in the middle. In the morning, the hand knows where to start.'},{kind:'explain',title:'It also stops the wriggling',text:'In the morning, part of you may want to do something easier. If the choice is already made, that part has to argue against it. That is harder than just picking the easy thing.'},{kind:'try',text:'Tonight, write tomorrow\u2019s most important job on a piece of paper. Put it where you will see it first.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Mia wants to make her science poster on Saturday. When is the best time to decide what she does first?',options:['On Friday night, and write it down','On Saturday morning, when she feels fresh','Whenever she happens to feel like starting'],answer:0,why:'Deciding on Friday saves her fresh Saturday brain for making the poster.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO MORNINGS',left:{title:'Deciding in the morning',items:['The fresh hour goes on choosing','Overnight emails steer the choice','Urgent beats important']},right:{title:'Decided the night before',items:['The fresh hour goes on doing','Chosen with the whole day in view','Important is already chosen']}},{kind:'idea',text:'Remove the decision from the morning. Choose tomorrow\u2019s priority the evening before, while you still have judgement and the day\u2019s context, so the fresh hours go on doing rather than choosing.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why choosing is expensive',text:'Two reasons. It happens when attention is freshest, so it consumes the best hour. And it is made under the influence of whatever arrived overnight, which pulls towards the urgent rather than the important. The evening version of you has the whole day\u2019s context and no incoming pressure.'},{kind:'explain',title:'It raises the bar for backing out',text:'Arriving with the choice made means the morning starts with execution. The version of you tempted by something easier now has to overturn a prior decision rather than simply make a fresh one, and that is a meaningfully higher bar.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The desk is cleared for the night, one pen set where the hand will find it first. Tomorrow starts with doing, not deciding.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before you finish today, write tomorrow\u2019s single most important task on paper and leave it where you will see it first.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You arrive to twelve new emails, two marked urgent, though both could wait till noon. Last night you chose the budget forecast. What now?',options:['Start the forecast; the evening decision had the wider view','Deal with the urgent emails first, then decide afresh','Reconsider the plan, since new emails have arrived','Do the quickest tasks first to clear your head'],answer:0,why:'The overnight arrivals are exactly the pressure the evening decision was made without. Nothing here is an emergency, so the plan stands.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHERE THE MORNING GOES',nodes:['You wake with a clear head','Overnight messages are waiting','You spend the head on choosing','Urgent wins; the good hour is gone'],note:'The article\u2019s point: the morning\u2019s most expensive activity is the choosing itself.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Deciding the next day\u2019s priority the night before removes the morning\u2019s most expensive activity, which is choosing. It hands the decision to someone with the whole day\u2019s context and no incoming pressure, and it makes backing out harder.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Two costs of choosing at dawn',text:'The first is spending: choosing happens when attention is freshest, so it uses part of the scarce budget from idea 1. The second is bias: whatever arrived overnight is vivid, and a choice made in its presence leans to the urgent over the important.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The renegotiation',text:'A decision made the evening before changes the morning\u2019s question. It is no longer what to do, which any passing temptation can answer. It is whether to overturn a prior decision, which needs a reason. The article calls that a meaningfully higher bar.'},{kind:'keyline',text:'Spend the evening\u2019s judgement so the morning can spend its attention.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Cleared at night, one pen squared up in the middle. The deciding has already happened here.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The evening self has context but not news. Sometimes what arrives overnight really does change the priority, and the plan should give way to a fact. The rule is that a temptation cannot reopen it, not that new information cannot. Judgement can also be poor after a draining day, so keep the evening decision small: one task, not a timetable.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'FACT OR TEMPTATION?',prompt:'Last night you decided to draft the proposal first. Which of these should be allowed to change that this morning?',options:['An email from the client saying the proposal is cancelled','A colleague\u2019s message that feels urgent but can wait','A sense that something easier would be a gentler start','An inbox with more unread messages than usual'],answer:0,why:'A cancelled proposal is a new fact. The others are the overnight pressure and the easier option the evening decision was made to resist.'}]}}}},3015,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'the-case-against-notes_1':{pictureCaption:'A shelf of filled notebooks, dust along every top, not one pulled out since. And a hand is filing the next one.',basics:{title:'Do you ever read your notes again?',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'LAST YEAR\u2019S NOTEBOOK',prompt:'Think of a notebook you filled last year. How many times have you opened it since?',options:['Lots of times','A few times','Hardly ever'],answer:2,reveal:'For lots of people, the honest answer is hardly ever. The notes were made, put away and not read again.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Look at the dust on these notebooks. Nobody has opened them. The new one is about to join them.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Most notes are never read again. So the good part of a note is not the page you keep. It is what writing it did to your head.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A PACKED BAG',text:'You pack a big bag for a day out. Snacks, a map, a spare jumper. You get home and most of it was never taken out. Packing felt useful. But only what you used did any good.'},{kind:'explain',title:'So should you stop?',text:'No. The point is not to stop. The point is that the help comes from the writing. When you put a thing in your own words, you have to think about it. The page you keep matters much less.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHERE THE GOOD PART IS',left:{title:'What we think counts',items:['The notebook you keep','Neat pages','Lots of notes']},right:{title:'What counts more',items:['The thinking while you write','Your own words','One clear line']}},{kind:'try',text:'Find one old notebook. Count the pages you have looked at since you wrote them. Be honest. That number is the lesson.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Sam spends an hour making neat notes on a topic. He never opens them again. Did the hour help?',options:['Only if writing them made him think','Yes, because the notes are so neat','No, notes are always a waste of time'],answer:0,why:'The notes just sat in a drawer. So any help came from the writing. If he thought hard while he wrote, it helped.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THREE YEARS OF FOLDERS',text:'You set up a note app with folders, tags and templates. Three years on it holds a few thousand notes. Now name the last time you opened one to use it, not to file it or tidy it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Most notes are never read again, so the routines built around them are mostly ceremony. Where a note has value, it is usually in the act of writing it, not in the thing you keep.'},{kind:'explain',title:'An assumption nobody tests',text:'Note-taking comes with an assumption that it is useful. The essay asks you to test it on yourself: not whether notes could be useful, but whether yours have been. For many people, honestly counted, the answer is a handful of times across years.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHERE THE TIME GOES',nodes:['Read something worth keeping','Write a careful, tagged note','File it in the system','Never open it again'],note:'Every note-taking step costs time. Only the writing could have changed what you understand.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Every notebook here was filled with care, and dust has settled on all of them. The new one is going in beside them, to the same end.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What that changes',text:'If a note will probably never be reopened, its length, its folder and its searchability stop mattering. What matters is what producing it did to your understanding. That is a different job, and it calls for a different kind of note.'},{kind:'try',text:'Count the notes you have reopened in the past year to actually use. Not filed, not tidied: used. Write the number down.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A colleague spends Friday afternoons moving their notes into a better folder structure. By this idea, what is the problem?',options:['The effort goes into an archive they rarely open, not into thinking','Their folder structure is probably not the most efficient one','Notes ought to be kept on paper rather than in an app','They should delete every old note and start again from zero'],answer:0,why:'Tidying an archive improves something that is seldom used. The essay puts the value in the writing, so that is where the time should go.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE ESSAY\u2019S OPENING MOVE',text:'This short essay, written for Mindroll, opens not with a theory but with a count: how many of your notes have you returned to in the past year? Its target is the elegant note-taking system, admired for its structure and rarely tested on whether it is used.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Most notes are never reopened, so most note-taking practice is ceremonial. Any value lies in the act of writing, which means a note should be judged by what making it did to your understanding.'},{kind:'term',word:'Write-only',meaning:'Made but never read back. The essay\u2019s claim is that most notes taken to understand something are, in practice, write-only, so they should be designed for the writing rather than for the reading.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Write-only, on a shelf: dust along every top and not a gap in the row. The new notebook joins an archive nobody reads.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The argument in three steps',text:'Notes carry an assumption of usefulness that is rarely tested. Tested honestly, many people find they reopen almost none. So the time spent on a note\u2019s form, length and filing buys little, and the only return left is whatever the writing did to the writer.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Judged as an archive',items:['Is it complete?','Is it well organised?','Is it easy to search?']},right:{title:'Judged as an act',items:['Did it make you think?','Is it in your words?','Could you say it without looking?']},note:'If the archive is never opened, only the right-hand column was ever doing anything.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The count proves less than it seems. It measures how often notes are used, not what a use is worth. One reopened contract, recipe or reference can outweigh a thousand idle notes. The essay concedes this in its fourth idea: the case is against notes taken to understand, not against records kept to retrieve.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Pick one note you made last year. What did writing it change in your thinking, if anything?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HONEST OBJECTION',prompt:'Which objection does the opening argument most need to answer?',options:['A rarely opened note may still be worth it if its one use matters enough','Notes kept in apps are easier to search than notes kept on paper','Some people enjoy organising notes for its own sake','Writing notes by hand is slower than typing them'],answer:0,why:'The count measures frequency, not value. That is why the essay later separates notes for understanding from records for retrieval.'}]}},'the-case-against-notes_2':{pictureCaption:'A perfect copy of the pattern, and the copier is gazing out of the window. The hand did all the work.',bridge:'Idea 1 put the value of a note in the writing. This one shows a kind of writing that delivers almost none.',basics:{title:'Copying is not learning',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'IN CLASS',text:'The teacher fills the board. You copy every word into your book. Your hand is busy the whole time. Your mind is on lunch.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'At the end, you have a full page. How much of it is in your head?',options:['Most of it, since I wrote it all','Not much, since my hand did the work'],answer:1,reveal:'Writing it all felt like hard work. But the work was in your hand, not your head. The words went from the board to the page without stopping in your mind.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Copying something out feels like learning. But you can copy without thinking, so very little sticks. The busy hand fools you.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The copy is perfect. But look where the eyes are: out of the window. The hand did all the work.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A PHOTOCOPIER',text:'A photocopier can copy a whole book. It does not learn one word of it. When you copy without thinking, you are being a photocopier.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Make it cost you something',steps:['Read the bit you want to keep.','Close the book.','Write what it said, in your own words.','Open the book and check.']},{kind:'question',prompt:'Mia wants to learn a page about volcanoes. Which plan will help her most?',options:['Read it, shut the book, then write what she can','Copy the whole page out neatly, word for word','Copy it out twice, so it sinks in better'],answer:0,why:'Copying can be done without thinking. Writing with the book shut makes her brain do the work, and that is what sticks.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'ONE TALK, TWO NOTE-TAKERS',prompt:'One person types almost every word of a talk. Another writes a few lines in their own words. A week later, who is more likely to explain its main idea?',options:['The one with every word','The one with a few lines of their own','There is no difference'],answer:1,reveal:'Probably the second, though not certainly. Studies of student note-taking have found that word-for-word notes can go with weaker answers on questions about ideas. The findings are mixed, so treat it as a lean, not a law.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Transcribing gives you the feeling of learning with little of the substance. Copying can be done without comprehension, and the sense of effort comes from the hand, not from any thinking.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE ROUTE THE WORDS TAKE',nodes:['Words on the page','Through the hand','Into the notebook'],note:'Nothing on that route requires understanding. That is how a full page can leave an empty head.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it feels like work',text:'Copying is tiring, tiredness reads as effort, and effort reads as learning. The essay calls it the same illusion as re-reading, in a more expensive form. You spend more time and come away with the same false confidence.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'An immaculate copy, and a copier gazing out of the window. Effort in the hand, nothing asked of the mind.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you read something worth keeping, close it before you write a word. Write only what you can produce from memory, then check.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You are studying for a work exam by copying key paragraphs from the manual into a notebook. What change does this idea suggest?',options:['Read a section, close the manual, and write what you can recall','Copy more slowly, so that each word has time to sink in','Copy into a document instead, so it is searchable later','Highlight the paragraphs instead, which takes less time'],answer:0,why:'Slower or searchable copying is still copying. Closing the source forces the material through your understanding, the route by which it gets retained.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Writing something down helps you remember it, whatever kind of writing it is.',reality:'The essay draws a line. Writing that passes through your understanding helps. Writing that passes only through your hand mostly does not, and copying is the clearest case of the second.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Copying a passage produces a strong sense of having engaged with it and very little memory of it. The hand is busy and the mind is idle: the illusion of re-reading, at a higher price.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The mechanism',text:'Copying can be done without comprehension. The words move from page to notebook through the hand, and the feeling of effort is produced by the transcription itself. Because effort usually comes with learning, we take it as evidence of learning. Here it is not.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How strong the evidence is',text:'The essay is careful here. Studies of student note-taking have found that verbatim notes can go with weaker performance on conceptual questions than fewer notes in the student\u2019s own words. It adds that the findings are mixed. That supports a lean towards fewer, own-word notes, not a firm rule.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'ONE PARAGRAPH, TWO NOTES',left:{title:'Transcribed',items:['Long','Accurate','Possible without understanding']},right:{title:'Reformulated',items:['Short','Rougher','Impossible without understanding']},note:'The corrective is a prohibition: close the source before writing anything.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The copy is flawless and the copier is looking out of the window. Effort is visible on the page, but none of it was asked of the mind.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Copying has honest uses. Exact wording sometimes matters: a definition, a formula, a line you must cite. And copying slowly can be a way of reading closely, if you stop to think as you go. The claim is not that the hand never helps. It is that a busy hand is no proof the mind took part.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE ILLUSION',prompt:'Which of these is the illusion the essay describes?',options:['Feeling you know a chapter because you copied out its key passages','Struggling to recall a chapter and finding you know less than you hoped','Writing one sentence from memory, then checking it against the book','Copying a formula exactly because a single error would matter'],answer:0,why:'The effort of copying felt like knowing. Copying a formula exactly is a fair use of copying, and the two attempts at recall are retrieval doing its job.'}]}},'the-case-against-notes_3':{pictureCaption:'The book is shut and pushed away. The hand writes one short line on a card from memory. That is the note that took thinking.',bridge:'Idea 2 showed the writing that fails. This one shows the writing that works, and a test for telling them apart.',basics:{title:'A good note takes thinking',beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'The best notes are long and copied out just right.',reality:'A good note is short and in your own words. You can only write it if you have thought about the thing. That thinking is what helps it stick.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A useful note is one you had to think to write. Shut the book first. Then write what you know, in your own words.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The book is shut. One short line goes on the card, from memory. Only someone who got it could write it.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'A PAGE ABOUT BEES',text:'You read a page on how bees make honey. Book shut, you write: *Bees drink sweet juice from flowers, take it home, and dry it out in the hive.* One line. You could only write it because you got it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The copy test',text:'Ask: could someone write this note without getting it? A copied line, yes. They just copy it. A note in your own words, no. You have to know what it means to say it your way.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'NOTES THAT TAKE THINKING',left:{title:'No thinking needed',items:['Copying a sentence','Copying a list','Copying the title']},right:{title:'Thinking needed',items:['Saying it your way','A question it made you ask','How it links to what you know']}},{kind:'try',text:'After your next lesson, shut your book. Write two lines on what it was about. Then open the book and see what you missed.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Which note could only be written by someone who got the story?',options:['*The fox lied to the hen because he was scared of the farmer.*','*The fox said he was the hen\u2019s best friend.*','*Chapter 3: The Fox and the Hen, page 41.*'],answer:0,why:'The other two could be copied straight from the book. The note about why the fox lied needed thinking about why he did it.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'TWO NOTES, ONE PARAGRAPH',text:'You read a paragraph arguing that towns grow faster when rents are cheap. Note A copies its best sentence. Note B says: *Or are rents cheap because the town is growing slowly? Which way round is it?* Only one of these could have been written by someone who had not understood.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The useful note is the one that required thinking to write: a reformulation, an objection, a connection or an unanswered question. The note is a by-product of the thinking, not a substitute for it.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Four notes worth writing',steps:['Say it in your own words.','Write the strongest objection to it.','Link it to something you already know.','Ask what it raised and left unanswered.'],note:'None of these can be produced without processing the material. That is why they work.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The test',text:'Could this note have been produced by someone who had not understood the passage? A quotation could. A reformulation could not. The tool and the format do not matter. The surest way to pass is to write with the book closed.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Book closed and out of reach, one line on a card. Nothing here could be copied, so whatever is on the card had to be worked out.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why short is a good sign',text:'Real processing yields a sentence or two, not a page. So a short note is not proof that you skimped. More often it is the sign that the work was done, because the long version would only have been the source again.'},{kind:'try',text:'With the next thing you read, close it and write one objection or one open question. Copy nothing.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You are asked to share notes from a training day. Which note best shows the training was understood?',options:['A short list of where its advice clashes with how your team works','A full set of the slides, saved and labelled by session','A word-for-word record of the trainer\u2019s key phrases','Photos of every whiteboard, with the dates in the file names'],answer:0,why:'Only the list of clashes could not exist without understanding the material. The rest are records anyone in the room could have made.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'A WORD TO HAVE FIRST',word:'Retrieval practice',meaning:'Bringing material back out of your own memory rather than looking at it again. The essay calls this the thing that actually builds memory, and counts an own-words note made without looking as one form of it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where this sits',text:'The essay has argued that most notes go unread and that copying teaches little. Here it turns from critique to prescription. If the value is in the writing, the question becomes which writing does the work. Its answer is the note that could not exist without thought.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A note is useful in proportion to the thinking it required. Reformulations, objections, connections and open questions all work, because none can be produced without processing the material.'},{kind:'equation',parts:['Book closed','Your own words'],result:'Retrieval practice',note:'Open the book and nothing has to be recalled. Drop your own words and the note can be produced without understanding.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Book closed, own words, one line. The card holds very little, and that is the point: writing it took recall, not copying.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The test, and its corollary',text:'Could someone who had not understood the passage have produced this note? A quotation passes for anyone; a reformulation does not. The corollary is brevity. Effortful processing yields a sentence or two, so a short note is a sign the work was done, not evidence that it was skimped.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The test is weaker than it looks. A paraphrase can be made mechanically, by swapping words, without grasping the point, and a note can be short because it was lazy. Own words are a reliable sign of thought mainly when they say something the source did not: an objection, a link, a question. Brevity is a tendency, not a proof.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Take the last note you made. Could someone who had not understood the source have written it?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY THE TEST',prompt:'Which note fails the essay\u2019s test, even though it is in the writer\u2019s own words?',options:['A line-by-line rewording of the passage, made with the book open','An objection the passage did not anticipate, written from memory','A question the passage raised and then left unanswered','A link between the passage and an argument in another book'],answer:0,why:'Rewording line by line with the source in view can be done without grasping the argument. The other three cannot exist without it.'}]}},'the-case-against-notes_4':{pictureCaption:'Tools hung within reach, worn from use. Nobody learns a wrench by heart. Some things belong on the wall, not in your head.',bridge:'The first three ideas made the case against notes. This one marks where the case stops, and it is narrower than the title.',basics:{title:'When you should write it down',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'A NEW PHONE NUMBER',text:'Your friend has a new phone number. It is eleven digits long. You will need it next week. You are not trying to *learn* it. You just need to *find* it. So you write it down. That is a good note.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Some things you need to keep, not learn: numbers, dates, plans, and why you chose something. Write those down. The case against notes is only about notes made to learn.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Notes to learn',items:['Help you think','Done once written','Short, in your words']},right:{title:'Notes to keep',items:['Help you find things','Must be kept safe','Exact and clear']}},{kind:'picture',caption:'Tools hang on the wall, close to hand. You don\u2019t learn a tool by heart. You keep it where you can reach it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why mix-ups go wrong',text:'Treat every note like one to keep, and you get piles you never read. Treat every note like one to learn, and you lose the phone number. So ask first: which kind am I making?'},{kind:'try',text:'Write down three things you must not forget this week. Keep the list where you will see it. That kind of note is worth having.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Which of these is worth writing down and keeping?',options:['The day and time of your swimming test','A page copied out of your science book','Every word your teacher said today'],answer:0,why:'You will need the date again, and it is easy to forget. The other two are copying, and copying teaches very little.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'The case against notes means you should stop writing things down.',reality:'It fails for anything you cannot hold in your head and will need again: references, numbers, decisions and their reasons. The case was never against recording. It was against confusing recording with learning.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Notes taken to understand are mostly never reread, so make them for the writing. Notes taken to retrieve are a different tool. For them the record itself matters as much as the writing.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO TOOLS, ONE WORD',left:{title:'Notes for understanding',items:['Made to think','Rarely reopened','Short, in your own words']},right:{title:'Records for retrieval',items:['Made to find again','Reopened when needed','Exact, complete, dated']}},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE DECISION LOG',text:'A team picks one supplier over another. Six months later someone asks why. If the reasons were written down at the time, the answer takes a minute. If not, the team rebuilds the argument from memory, which may tell a tidier story than the one that happened.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where else it fails',text:'Long projects, where the context is more than anyone can hold. Accountability, where what was decided has to be shown to someone else, not just remembered. And anything where being wrong is expensive. In each case a durable record earns its keep.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Worn tools on a wall, within reach. They earn their place by being used, not by being learnt.'},{kind:'try',text:'Sort your notes into two piles: made to learn, and needed to find. Keep the second pile properly. Stop filing the first.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Which of these most clearly calls for a careful, durable note?',options:['The reasons your team chose one contractor over another','Your thoughts on a chapter of a management book','The main points of a podcast you enjoyed','A passage from an article you found interesting'],answer:0,why:'Decisions and their reasons are needed again and costly to get wrong. The others are notes for understanding, where the writing is the point.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE ESSAY\u2019S OWN LIMIT',text:'Having argued against notes for three ideas, the essay marks where its own case fails. The honest position, it says, is narrower than the provocation, and the line it draws turns out to be the most practical thing in it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The argument fails for material you must retrieve rather than understand: references, decisions and their reasons, commitments, anything costly to get wrong. Systems go wrong when they treat these two kinds of note as one.'},{kind:'term',word:'Artefact',meaning:'The note as a kept object, as opposed to the act of writing it. For notes made to understand, the essay says the artefact hardly matters. For records made to retrieve, it matters as much as the writing.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Three places the case fails',text:'First, reference material and commitments you will need again, exactly. Second, long projects whose context cannot be held in memory. Third, accountability, where what was decided must be demonstrable to others. In each, the note\u2019s real reader is someone later, perhaps yourself, who cannot rely on memory of the moment.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHY MIXED SYSTEMS FAIL',left:{title:'Built for understanding',items:['Made for the writing','Short, rough, personal']},right:{title:'Built for retrieval',items:['Made for the finding','Exact, complete, labelled']},note:'One system trying to be both tends to produce long, tidy notes nobody rereads, and records too rough to rely on.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A wall of tools, worn from handling and kept in reach. A record for retrieval earns its place the same way: by being used.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The line between the two kinds is not always visible when you write. A note made to understand can turn out to be the only record of a decision, and you rarely know in advance which page will matter years later. The sensible reading keeps the distinction but leans towards recording wherever being wrong would be expensive.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'CLASSIFY IT',prompt:'A researcher writes, from memory, what a paper changes for her, and separately records its full citation. How should each be judged?',options:['The first by what writing it did, the second by whether it can be found and trusted','Both by how complete and how well organised they are','Both by how much thinking went into writing them','The first should be kept carefully, the second can be thrown away'],answer:0,why:'They are two tools. The reflection is a note for understanding; the citation is a record for retrieval, and only it needs to be durable and exact.'}]}},'the-case-against-notes_5':{pictureCaption:'One short line on a card at the kitchen table. The thing it is about has already been put away.',bridge:'Idea 3 said the useful note is the one you had to think to write. This is the smallest version of it, made at the right time.',basics:{title:'One line, the next day',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'MONDAY MORNING',picture:!0,text:'You finish a good book on Sunday night and put it back on the shelf. On Monday, at the kitchen table, you take a card. You write one line about it. Just one.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'Why wait a day, and not write it straight away?',options:['So you can copy from the book','So you have to dig it out of your head','Because you will be less busy'],answer:1,reveal:'The book is back on the shelf. You can\u2019t copy it. You have to dig the idea out of your own head, and digging it out is what helps it stay.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A day after you finish a thing, write one line on what it changed for you. Just one. It is so small that you will really do it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why only one line?',text:'One line makes you choose. What was it really about? To pick the one big thing, you have to see how the whole thing fits. A long note lets you skip that choice.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE TELLING A FRIEND',text:'A friend asks about a film you saw last week. You don\u2019t tell every scene. You pick the one thing that mattered. To pick it, you had to get the film.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of the last book, film or lesson you liked. Write one line on what it changed for you. Don\u2019t look it up.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Ava finishes a book about space. Which plan fits this idea?',options:['The next day, write one line on what it changed for her','Right away, copy her best page into a notebook','That night, write a long note on every chapter'],answer:0,why:'Waiting a day means she has to dig it out of her head. One line means she has to choose. Both make her think.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE HALF-FILLED TEMPLATE',text:'You finished a book last month and meant to write it up properly, with chapter summaries and key passages. The template is still open, half filled in. You have since finished two more books.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The practice worth keeping is small: a day after finishing something, write one sentence on what it changes for you. It is retrieval, it is spaced, and it is short enough that you will actually do it.'},{kind:'equation',parts:['A delay','A one-sentence limit'],result:'A judgement you must make',note:'The delay stops you copying. The limit stops you dodging the question of what mattered.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the limit does the work',text:'Deciding what to leave out requires understanding the structure of the thing: what holds it up and what is decoration. A long note lets you avoid that decision. One sentence forces it, and that judgement is the valuable part.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it lasts',text:'An elaborate system is often abandoned within months. One sentence a few days later is small enough to survive a busy week. A year of those sentences is a genuinely useful document; a year of abandoned systems is not.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One line on a card, at the kitchen table. The book it is about is already back on the shelf, out of sight.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick something you finished this week. Tomorrow, without opening it, write one sentence: what does this change for me?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You finish a two-day course on Friday. Which plan follows this practice?',options:['On Monday, write one sentence on what the course changes in your work','On Friday evening, type up all your notes while the course is fresh','During the course, record every slide so that nothing is missed','Next month, reread the course handbook from start to finish'],answer:0,why:'The delay forces retrieval and the single sentence forces a judgement. Typing up notes that evening keeps the source in reach, which invites copying rather than recall.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE ESSAY\u2019S LAST WORD',prompt:'After arguing against most note-taking, what do you expect the essay to keep?',options:['Nothing at all','A lighter version of the full system','One sentence, written later'],answer:2,reveal:'It keeps one practice, chosen because it joins what the earlier ideas valued: writing that costs thought, and writing small enough to survive a busy life.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The most efficient practice is a single sentence written some time after finishing, not during. The delay forces retrieval; the limit forces a judgement about what the thing was actually about.'},{kind:'term',word:'Spaced',meaning:'Done after a gap, rather than straight after meeting the material. The essay counts the delay as part of the practice\u2019s value: the sentence is retrieval, and it is spaced.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHAT EACH LIMIT FORCES',nodes:['The source is put away','You must recall, not copy','Only one sentence allowed','You must judge what mattered'],note:'Each constraint closes an escape route. The delay closes copying; the limit closes the long note that dodges judgement.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why size is part of the argument',text:'Sustainability is not an afterthought here. An elaborate system is often abandoned within months, and an abandoned system records nothing more. One sentence a few days later survives a busy period, and a year of them becomes the useful document the elaborate system promised.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A single line on a card. The thing it refers to is already out of sight, so the line had to come from memory.'},{kind:'limits',text:'One sentence suits something read for its ideas. It does not suit material you must retrieve in detail, which the previous idea handed to a different tool. And the essay leaves the delay loose: a day in one place, a few days in another. Leave it too long and there may be little left to recall.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE WEAK VERSION',prompt:'Which version of the practice loses what makes it work?',options:['Writing the sentence as you finish, with the book still open','Writing it three days later, from memory','Writing it on a phone instead of on a card','Writing it about a film instead of a book'],answer:0,why:'With the source open and no delay, the sentence can be lifted rather than recalled. The medium and the subject do not matter; the gap and the limit do.'}]}}}},3016,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'the-cost-of-being-reachable_1':{pictureCaption:'One hand slides a small, light token across. The person on the other side is bowed under it. Sending is light; receiving is not.',basics:{title:'Quick to send, slow to get',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'HOMEWORK, SEVEN O\u2019CLOCK',text:'You are halfway through a hard maths question. Your phone buzzes. A friend has sent a funny video. It took her two seconds. You watch it and reply. Then you look back at the sum. Where were you?'},{kind:'idea',text:'A message costs the sender almost nothing. Getting it costs you your place in what you were doing. Nobody means it, but the cost moves to you.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A TOWER OF CARDS',text:'You are building a tall tower of cards. Someone taps your shoulder to say hi. The tap took them one second. But you jumped, the top fell, and now you have to build it again.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One hand slides a tiny token across the table. The person on the other side bends under it. Small to send. Heavy to get.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Nobody is being mean',text:'Your friend did nothing wrong. Sending was easy, so she sent it. Lots of friends do the same. Each message on its own is fine. All of them together chop your evening into bits.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What you can change',text:'You can\u2019t make everyone send less. They never feel the cost, so it is hard for them to see it. What you *can* choose is when you read. The video will still be funny in an hour.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you do homework, leave your phone face down in another room. Read your messages when one whole task is done.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Leo\u2019s friends message him all through his homework. What does this idea say is the best fix?',options:['Leo picks set times to read his messages','Leo asks all his friends to stop sending','Leo replies to each one as fast as he can'],answer:0,why:'His friends don\u2019t feel the cost, so asking them to stop rarely works. Leo can choose when he reads.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'A FIVE-SECOND QUESTION',text:'You are an hour into a forecast, holding a dozen numbers in your head. A colleague writes: quick one, which version of the deck is final? It took them five seconds. Answering takes you thirty. Finding your place in the forecast again takes much longer.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A message costs the sender seconds and costs the recipient a context switch. Because sending is nearly free, volume rises to fill whatever responsiveness you show.'},{kind:'term',word:'Context switch',meaning:'Dropping what your mind was holding for one task to deal with another, then rebuilding it to go back. The rebuilding is the cost, and it falls on the person interrupted.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW A DAY FRAGMENTS',nodes:['Sending costs the sender nothing','So each sender sends','Many reasonable messages arrive','Your day is in pieces'],note:'Nobody behaves badly at any step. The structure pushes the cost onto you, and no sender ever sees it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A light token slid across the counter. The weight lands on the far side, with the person who did not choose it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the fix is at your end',text:'Asking people to send less means asking them to absorb a cost they never experience, so it rarely holds. The remedy has to be at the receiving end. That is why it is about when you read, not about how much others write.'},{kind:'try',text:'When the next message lands mid-task, notice that it has just handed you a cost. Then decide whether to accept it now or at a time you choose.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Your team agrees that everyone should send fewer messages. A month later nothing has changed. What best explains it?',options:['Senders never feel the cost, so the request asks them to absorb something unseen','People are careless, and the team needs stricter rules on message etiquette','The agreement was too vague, and needed a fixed daily quota of messages','Messages were never the problem, and meetings were using up the time'],answer:0,why:'The cost of sending lands on the recipient, so senders have no felt reason to change. That is why the piece puts the remedy at the reading end.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHERE THE PIECE STARTS',text:'The piece is about the cost of being permanently reachable: who pays it, and why they are not the people who benefit. It opens not with bad habits but with arithmetic, the price of one message at each end.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A message costs the sender almost nothing and the recipient a context switch. That asymmetry, not anyone\u2019s bad behaviour, drives the volume, so the remedy cannot sit with senders.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'ONE MESSAGE, TWO PRICES',left:{title:'The sender',items:['Seconds to write','Gets an answer sooner','Never sees the switch it caused']},right:{title:'The recipient',items:['A task dropped mid-thought','Its state rebuilt afterwards','Multiplied by every sender']},note:'The cost is real. It has simply moved to the one person who did not choose it.'},{kind:'term',word:'Externalised cost',meaning:'A cost created by one party\u2019s choice but paid by someone else. The sender decides and the recipient pays, so nothing in the sender\u2019s decision weighs the cost.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Reasonable one by one',text:'For any single sender, sending is the rational strategy: it is free and it gets an answer. The argument is about the sum. Many reasonable decisions, each harmless alone, add up to a recipient whose day is in fragments. Nobody intends it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A light token slid across with two fingers, and someone bowed on the other side. The weight was never on the sender\u2019s side of the table.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the fix sits with the reader',text:'Asking senders to write less asks them to carry a cost they do not experience, which is an unstable request. So the piece moves the intervention to the recipient: control when you read, not how much others write. Its later ideas build on this.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The claim that volume rises to fill your responsiveness is argued from incentives, not measured. And the sender\u2019s end is not quite hopeless: a team can agree shared norms that change what gets sent. What the argument does show is narrower. Asking individuals to absorb a cost they never feel is unlikely to hold on its own.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'A manager says the real problem is people sending too much, so she will ask everyone to think before they send. What is the piece\u2019s strongest reply?',options:['Senders never feel the cost, so the request asks them to absorb something unseen','People ignore managers, so only a strict ban on messaging would work','Thinking before sending makes messages longer, which costs readers more','The volume is harmless, since every single message is reasonable alone'],answer:0,why:'The cost lands where the sender cannot see it, so restraint asks for a sacrifice nobody feels the need for. The piece puts the remedy with the reader instead.'}]}},'the-cost-of-being-reachable_2':{pictureCaption:'The fast needle is out in the open where anyone can read it. The slow gauge beside it sits behind a closed panel.',bridge:'If the cost lands on you, why keep replying fast? Because replying fast is what gets noticed.',basics:{title:'Why fast replies feel so important',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TWO CLASSMATES',prompt:'Mia answers every question in class, fast. Raj spends three days on a really hard puzzle, on his own. Who does the class notice more?',options:['Mia','Raj'],answer:0,reveal:'Mia. Quick answers are easy to see. Three days of hard thinking happen inside Raj\u2019s head, where nobody can see them.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Fast replies are easy to see, so people notice them and praise them. Slow, hard work is hard to see. That is why it feels risky to stop replying fast.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One dial is out in the open, and everyone can see it move. The other is behind a little door. Fast replies are the open dial.'},{kind:'explain',title:'It is not just in your head',text:'Maybe your friends say you are a good friend because you always reply fast. You are right to notice that. The praise is real. That is what makes it hard to stop.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE THE SCORE ON A SCREEN',text:'In a game, the score is up on the screen, so everyone watches it. Good teamwork is not on the screen. A fast reply is like the score. It is easy to count. It is not the whole game.'},{kind:'explain',title:'So what can you do?',text:'Just stopping is hard. You lose the thing people notice, and you lose it on your own. Instead, see a fast reply for what it is: a sign people look for. Then choose on purpose how much of it to give.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Ana answers the class group chat in seconds. Everyone says she is so reliable. What does this idea say?',options:['Fast replies get noticed, but they are only one sign of being reliable','Fast replies prove she is the most reliable person in the class','Fast replies are bad, so she should stop replying at all'],answer:0,why:'People praise what they can see. A fast reply is easy to see, but it is not all there is to being reliable.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE USUAL ADVICE',belief:'If messages are eating your day, just check them less.',reality:'That advice fails for a reason. It asks you to give up a signal your workplace recognises for one it does not, and to carry the reputational cost alone.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Response time is read as reliability. Replying quickly is visible; doing something difficult over three days is not. Organisations reward what they can see, and people correctly infer what gets noticed.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHAT GETS SEEN',left:{title:'A fast reply',items:['Visible within minutes','Easy for anyone to judge','Noticed and thanked']},right:{title:'Three days of hard work',items:['Invisible until it is done','Hard to judge from outside','Noticed late, if at all']},note:'The incentive is real, not imagined. People who keep replying fast are reading their workplace correctly.'},{kind:'term',word:'Proxy',meaning:'A measure that stands in for the thing you care about because it is easier to see. Response time is a proxy for reliability: related to it, but not the same thing.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The needle everyone can read is reply time. The gauge for hard work sits behind a closed panel, so nobody checks it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'So what is the move?',text:'Not to drop the signal by accident, and not to keep supplying it by default. Recognise that you are being rewarded for a proxy. Then decide deliberately how much of it to keep supplying, knowing what it costs the work nobody sees.'},{kind:'try',text:'Look back at yesterday. How much of what you were thanked for was speed? How much was the hardest thing you did?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A colleague who answers everything within minutes is praised as the most dependable person on the team. What does the idea suggest?',options:['The praise tracks a visible proxy, which may not reflect the harder work','She is plainly the most dependable, since fast replies are reliability','The praise is imagined, since workplaces do not really reward speed','She should stop replying fast, since speed is a sign of shallow work'],answer:0,why:'Reply time is easy to see, so it gets rewarded. It is related to reliability, but it is not a measure of the difficult work nobody can see yet.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A ONE-PERSON EXPERIMENT',prompt:'You start checking messages twice a day instead of constantly, and your hard work improves. What happens to how you are seen in the first month?',options:['It rises, because the work is better','It may fall, because the slow replies show and the better work does not yet','Nothing changes either way'],answer:1,reveal:'The piece\u2019s argument points to the second. The lost signal is visible at once; the gain is not. You also pay that cost alone, which is why simply checking less fails as advice.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Quick replies are a legible signal of reliability; difficult work over days is not. Organisations reward the legible signal and people correctly infer it, so the habit is a rational response, not a lapse of discipline.'},{kind:'term',word:'Legible',meaning:'Readable from outside, without effort or expertise. A reply time is legible to anyone. The quality of three days of hard thinking is legible to almost nobody until it is finished.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the usual advice fails',text:'Telling someone to check less treats the habit as a weakness. The piece treats it as a correct reading of incentives. Stopping means swapping a recognised signal for an unrecognised one, and taking the reputational cost alone while colleagues keep supplying it.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE PROXY TAKES HOLD',nodes:['Fast replies are easy to see','They get noticed and rewarded','People supply more of them','Fast replies become the norm'],loop:!0,note:'Each turn makes the signal more expected, so its absence is more noticeable for whoever stops first.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'An open dial and a covered gauge. What a workplace rewards depends partly on which of the two it can read.'},{kind:'limits',text:'This explains why the habit persists; it does not make fast replies worthless. In some roles responsiveness is the job, and quick answers do help other people. The harder problem the argument exposes is collective: one person opting out pays alone. That is why the piece\u2019s fix, in the next idea, is a stated window rather than quiet withdrawal.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A support team is judged on how fast it closes tickets. What does this idea warn about?',options:['Effort drifts to the visible measure, while slower, harder fixes go unrewarded','Closing tickets faster always means customers are being served better','The measure is harmless as long as the staff are never told about it','Staff will ignore the measure, because people dislike being watched'],answer:0,why:'A legible proxy draws effort toward itself. Work that is hard to see loses out, and the people doing it are reading the incentives correctly.'}]}},'the-cost-of-being-reachable_3':{pictureCaption:'The shop is shut, and still easy to trust: the clock card in the door shows when it reopens, and the bell is for real emergencies.',bridge:'Idea 2 showed why quietly checking less fails. This is the version that works: say when.',basics:{title:'Say when you will reply',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE SILENT FIX',belief:'To get peace from messages, just stop answering for a while.',reality:'If you go quiet without a word, people worry. They message again and again, or ask someone else. You end up with more messages, not fewer.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Don\u2019t just go quiet. Tell people *when* you will reply, and how to reach you if something is really urgent. Then they can wait without worrying.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'This shop is shut, but the clock in the door shows when it opens. The bell is for a real emergency. Shut, but easy to trust.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'KAI\u2019S PLAN',text:'Kai tells his friends: I read messages after homework, at about six. If it\u2019s really important, call me. Now his friends know when to expect him. Nobody thinks he is ignoring them.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the call matters',text:'Friends might think: what if something is really urgent? The phone call answers that. It is like the bell by the shop door. There is one way through for real emergencies, so the rest can wait.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Keep your word',text:'This only works if Kai sticks to it. If he says six but often answers at nine, his friends stop trusting the plan. Then they start sending messages again and again, just to check.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Nia wants fewer messages while she practises piano. Which plan fits this idea best?',options:['Tell friends she replies after practice, and to call if it\u2019s urgent','Turn her phone off and say nothing to anyone','Reply to every message the moment it comes in'],answer:0,why:'Her friends know when she will reply, and they have a way to reach her in a real emergency.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TWO COLLEAGUES',prompt:'Both check messages only twice a day. One says so in their email signature, with a number for emergencies. The other says nothing. Who gets more follow-ups?',options:['The one who said nothing','The one who announced it','About the same'],answer:0,reveal:'Unannounced unresponsiveness creates its own load. Senders escalate, follow up and route around you, so the volume rises and the relationships absorb the damage.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The fix is a stated window, not silence. Unannounced unavailability reads as unreliability. A stated pattern, with a route for genuinely urgent things, reads as a system.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What senders actually need',text:'Most senders do not need speed. They need to know when. A stated pattern, messages checked at these times and urgent matters by this route, supplies that predictability, and predictability is what people were after all along.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Shut, and plainly reliable. The clock card in the door shows when it reopens, and the bell is there for the real emergency.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Setting a window',steps:['Choose when you will check messages, such as late morning and late afternoon.','Publish it where people see it: a signature, a status, a team page.','Name one route for genuine emergencies, such as a phone call.','Keep to it, every day.']},{kind:'explain',title:'The condition',text:'The window has to be honoured. A pattern announced and then followed unreliably is worse than none, because people learn to test it. Testing it, with nudges and follow-ups, is exactly what the announcement was meant to stop.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You announce that you reply at 11 and 4. Within a week you are answering at all hours again. What does the piece predict?',options:['People learn to test the window, so the follow-ups come back','Nothing changes, since the announcement already set expectations','People enjoy the bonus replies and trust the window even more','The window still works, because the urgent route is still there'],answer:0,why:'An announced window followed unreliably is worse than none. People learn that pushing gets an answer, and the testing returns.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO BE AWAY',left:{title:'Silence',items:['Reads as unreliability','Senders follow up and escalate','Volume rises','Relationships take the damage']},right:{title:'A stated window',items:['Reads as a system','Senders know when to expect you','Urgent matters have their own route']},note:'You are equally unavailable in both columns. Only the announcement differs.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Unavailability is not the problem; unpredictability is. A stated window, with a named route for genuine urgency, supplies the predictability senders need, provided it is honoured.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why silence backfires',text:'Unannounced unresponsiveness creates its own load. Senders cannot tell a delay from a dropped ball, so they escalate, follow up and route around you. The total volume rises and relationships absorb the damage. Going quiet moves the cost rather than removing it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The objection it answers',text:'The usual case against any boundary is: what if something is genuinely urgent? The stated window gives that a route, such as a phone call, and so removes the objection that otherwise sinks it. The next idea argues the urgent list is shorter than it feels.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A clock card in the door showing when it reopens, and a bell beside it. Closed and predictable are not opposites.'},{kind:'keyline',text:'Most senders do not need speed. They need to know when.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Everything rests on honouring the window. An announced pattern followed unreliably is worse than none, because people learn to test it, the very behaviour it was meant to prevent. Not everyone can publish one, either: some roles really do require constant cover, and someone junior may need agreement before setting terms for others.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these is a *misuse* of the idea?',options:['Announcing a window, then answering at all hours whenever someone pushes','Checking messages at ten and three, as your signature says you will','Telling the team that genuinely urgent matters should come by phone','Agreeing a shared message window with your team before you use it'],answer:0,why:'A window followed unreliably is worse than none. People learn that pushing works, and testing it is what the window was meant to prevent.'}]}},'the-cost-of-being-reachable_4':{pictureCaption:'A month of messages heaped on the table. The few that truly could not wait fit in one small tray of their own.',bridge:'Idea 3\u2019s window needed a route for urgent things. This one argues that there are few of those, and that they can be named.',basics:{title:'Very few things can\u2019t wait',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'A WEEK OF URGENT',text:'Jo gets lots of messages that say *urgent!* Which game tonight? Have you seen this video? Can I borrow your pen tomorrow? Only one could not wait. It was from Mum: the bus home was cancelled.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Almost nothing is really urgent. The few things that are can be listed ahead of time. Give those their own way to reach you, and the rest can wait.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A big heap of messages. Only a few go in the small tray. Those are the ones that truly could not wait.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Make the list',text:'Ask: what really can\u2019t wait a few hours? Someone is hurt or unsafe. The plan for getting home has changed. That is about it. The list is usually short, and it stays much the same week after week.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Feeling urgent is not being urgent',text:'A friend may *feel* their message is urgent. That does not make it so. With a list, the thing itself decides, not how worked up the sender is.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE 999',text:'You only call 999 for a real emergency, like a fire or someone badly hurt. Everyone knows the list, and it has its own number. Everything else goes a different way.'},{kind:'try',text:'With your family, write down three things that can\u2019t wait. Agree that those come by phone call. Everything else can be a message.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You are at football practice. Which message truly can\u2019t wait until it ends?',options:['Your lift home is cancelled, so you need a new plan','A friend wants to pick a film for Saturday','Your cousin sent a photo of her new puppy'],answer:0,why:'Getting home safely is on the short list. The film and the puppy can wait an hour.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'ask',kicker:'BEFORE YOU READ ON',prompt:'Think back over last month\u2019s messages marked urgent, or sent as if they were. How many truly needed an answer within the hour?'},{kind:'idea',text:'Almost nothing is urgent, and the exceptions can be specified in advance. Write them down, share the list, and give them a route you do watch.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What an audit tends to show',text:'Go through a month of \u201curgent\u201d messages, the piece suggests, and the share that needed an answer within the hour is likely to be small and to fall into the same few categories. It offers this as an observation to test on your own messages. If it holds, that is what makes a message window safe rather than reckless.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE PIECE\u2019S SAMPLE LIST',text:'Production is down. A person is unsafe. A deadline today with an outside party. The list is usually that short, and usually the same every quarter. Everything else can usually wait four hours.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'A DEFINITION, AND A ROUTE',left:{title:'Definition only',items:['Everything still arrives by email','Every message looks equally pressing','Urgency stays a feeling']},right:{title:'Definition plus route',items:['Urgent matters come by phone','Silence elsewhere is safe','Urgency becomes a fact about the situation']},note:'Without a channel the definition is only advice. The route is what makes it work.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A month of messages heaped up, and the few that could not wait sorted into a small tray of their own.'},{kind:'try',text:'List the three things in your work that genuinely cannot wait four hours. Route those to a channel you do watch, and tell people what it is.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Your team agrees a list of what counts as urgent, but every message still arrives by email. What is missing?',options:['A separate route for urgent items, so the list changes how things arrive','A longer list, so that fewer real emergencies slip through the net','Stricter wording, so senders stop overusing the word urgent','Nothing, since a shared definition is the whole of the fix'],answer:0,why:'Without its own channel, everything arrives the same way with the same implied priority. The route is what makes the definition operational.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE USUAL OBJECTION',belief:'You cannot keep set hours for messages, because emergencies happen.',reality:'They do, but they are rare and predictable in kind. That is what makes a window safe rather than reckless: the exceptions can be named in advance and sent another way.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Urgency should be a property of the situation, not of the sender\u2019s feeling. Few things qualify, the categories that do can be listed in advance, and a dedicated route makes the list work.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The claim, and its basis',text:'The piece suggests that if you audit a month of urgent messages, the share needing an answer within the hour is likely to be small and to fall into the same few categories. It gives no figures and cites no audit. It calls this an observation worth testing on your own messages, not a measured rate.'},{kind:'term',word:'Operational',meaning:'Built into how things actually happen, not just stated. A definition of urgency stays advisory until there is a channel that urgent things, and only urgent things, travel by.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'FROM FEELING TO RULE',nodes:['Audit a month of urgent messages','Name the few categories','Share the definition','Give it its own route'],note:'Each step moves urgency away from how the sender feels and toward what the situation is.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A month heaped on the table, and the handful that could not wait in a tray of their own. The tray is the route.'},{kind:'limits',text:'A list fitted to last quarter can miss a genuinely new crisis, so it needs revisiting. A route also invites misuse: once the phone means urgent, some senders will call about anything they feel strongly about, and the definition has to be defended. And in work built around emergencies, much really is urgent. The claim is about ordinary work.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TEST THE DEFINITION',prompt:'A senior client emails at 6pm, marked urgent, asking for slide changes for a meeting next week. How does the idea treat it?',options:['As not urgent: a meeting next week sets the priority, not the label','As urgent, because the sender is senior and has marked it so','As urgent, because anything from an outside client is on the list','As ignorable, since overusing the word urgent cancels the request'],answer:0,why:'The list makes urgency a property of the situation. A deadline next week does not need an answer within the hour, whoever sends it.'}]}},'the-cost-of-being-reachable_5':{pictureCaption:'The bench by the door is clear; every quick job is done. The half-built boat sits untouched, its tools still on the floor.',bridge:'The first four ideas were about messages. This one asks where their cost finally lands.',basics:{title:'The work nobody is waiting for',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE MODEL CASTLE',text:'You want to build a model castle. Nobody asked you to. Each evening, things come up: a friend needs help, a chore, a quick message. Each one has someone waiting. The castle has nobody waiting. A month later, it is still a pile of card.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Interruptions hurt big, slow work the most: the work nobody is asking for yet. Nobody is waiting for it, so it loses every time.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the big work loses',text:'Small jobs have someone waiting. When you finish them, someone says thanks. Big work is slow, and each time you stop, you have to find your place again. So it is the easiest thing to put off.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE CASTLE GETS LEFT',nodes:['Someone waits on a small job','You do the small job','The castle waits again','A month goes by'],note:'You never chose to drop the castle. It just kept losing, one evening at a time.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The quick jobs by the door are all done. The big boat at the back has not been touched.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Keep a spot for the big work',steps:['Pick a time each week just for the big thing.','Treat it like a club: you don\u2019t skip it because a message came.','Do the small jobs after.']},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one big thing nobody is asking you for. Give it a fixed time this week, like a club, and keep that time for it.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Sam\u2019s week was full of small favours for friends. What does this idea say he should check at the end of it?',options:['Whether his own big project moved on at all','How many favours he managed to finish','Whether his friends all said thank you'],answer:0,why:'A busy week can look full and still leave the big work untouched. That is the part to check.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO SPEND A YEAR',text:'Picture two people in the same job. One answers everything promptly and keeps up all year. The other also answers, but blocks two mornings a week for a project nobody has asked for yet. By December, one has kept up. The other has done something.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The cost of constant availability lands on the work nobody is asking about yet. It has the most to rebuild after an interruption and nobody waiting for it, so it loses every contest for the same hour.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHO WINS THE HOUR',left:{title:'Requested work',items:['Someone is waiting','Finishing it is thanked at once','Little to rebuild after a break']},right:{title:'Unrequested work',items:['Nobody is waiting yet','Finishing it is noticed later, if ever','Much to rebuild after each break']},note:'Hour after hour goes to the left column, and no single choice is to blame.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Every quick job is cleared from the bench by the door. The half-built boat at the back, which nobody is asking about, has not moved.'},{kind:'explain',title:'A drift nobody decided',text:'The result is a steady slide toward reactive work, with no decision ever taken to make it happen. That is why the cost is never accounted for: it lands on work that is not yet on anyone\u2019s list.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why intention is not enough',text:'The incentive is stable, so the corrective has to be structural. Block time in advance for the unrequested work, and defend it as you would a meeting. Good intentions lose to the person currently waiting, every time.'},{kind:'try',text:'At the end of today, ask what happened to the work nobody is asking about yet. If the answer is nothing, block an hour for it tomorrow and treat it as a meeting.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Thursday morning is free, and you mean to spend it on a long-term project. By Wednesday night three colleagues want quick help. What does the idea suggest?',options:['The requests will win unless the morning is blocked and defended','The project will be fine, since you formed the intention early','Help the colleagues first, since requested work matters more','Start the project only once the inbox is completely clear'],answer:0,why:'Someone is waiting on the requests and nobody is waiting on the project, so intention alone loses. Time blocked and defended like a meeting is the structural fix.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'WHAT AN INTERRUPTION COSTS',word:'State',meaning:'Everything a task needs held in mind: where you were, what you were trying, what you had ruled out. Long, unrequested work carries the most of it, so it has the most to rebuild.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Interruption does not damage all work equally. It damages long, unrequested, hard-to-explain work most. Over a year, that is the difference between having done something and having kept up.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Two disadvantages at once',text:'Such work has the most state to rebuild, so each interruption costs it more. And it has no one waiting, so it loses every contest for the same hour against work where someone is. Either would hurt alone. Together they make it the default casualty.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE DRIFT',nodes:['An hour comes free','Someone is waiting on small work','The small work takes the hour','The long work slips again'],loop:!0,note:'No step is a decision to neglect anything. The slide toward reactive work happens by default.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The drift at the end of a day: the small jobs by the door all done, the long work at the back with its tools still laid down.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it is never accounted for',text:'Responsive work brings immediate acknowledgement, so its value is recorded. Unrequested work has nobody tracking it, so its loss appears on no list. This is idea 2 again from the other side: the visible signal is rewarded, and the invisible work pays for it.'},{kind:'keyline',text:'Judge a fragmented day by what happened to the work nobody is asking about yet.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Not all unrequested work deserves protecting: some of it goes unasked for because it is not worth doing. Blocked time can also become a way to dodge colleagues who really need you. The year-end contrast is the piece\u2019s framing, not a measured result. And the structural fix assumes you control enough of your calendar to block time at all.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'Someone says: I don\u2019t need to block time, I will just make my long-term project a priority. What is the piece\u2019s strongest reply?',options:['Intention loses to whoever is waiting, so the time must be blocked and defended','Priorities only work when written at the very top of a to-do list','Long-term projects matter less than requests from colleagues anyway','Interruptions hurt all work equally, so blocking time changes nothing'],answer:0,why:'The incentive favouring requested work is stable, so the fix has to be structural. A priority held only in the head loses to the person currently waiting.'}]}}}},3017,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'the-cost-of-fees_1':{pictureCaption:'The scoop comes off the whole heap, not off the small pile of this year\u2019s growth. That is how most annual fees are taken.',basics:{title:'The fee comes off everything',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'A YEAR WITH NO GROWTH',text:'You keep \xa3100 in a savings pot. This year it grew by nothing at all. Then you see the pot has taken \xa31 as its yearly fee. Nothing grew, but the fee still came out.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Most yearly fees are a slice of all the money you have. They are taken in good years and bad years. They are not a slice of what your money earned.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The small pile is what grew this year. The scoop does not touch it. It takes from the big heap: all of it.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO KINDS OF YEAR',left:{title:'Good year',items:['Your money grows','The fee comes out']},right:{title:'Bad year',items:['Your money shrinks','The fee still comes out']},note:'The fee does not care how the year went.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it sounds so small',text:'A fee of 1p for each \xa31 sounds tiny next to all your money. But compare it with what the money earns. If your money earns 5p per \xa31, a 1p fee takes a fifth of that.'},{kind:'try',text:'Ask a grown-up who has savings: is the fee taken from all the money, or only from what it earned?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your pot of \xa3200 earns nothing this year. The fee is 1p for each \xa31. What happens?',options:['The pot pays \xa32 anyway','There is no fee, as nothing grew','The fee waits until a good year'],answer:0,why:'The fee is a slice of all the money, so \xa3200 pays \xa32 whether the year was good or not.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'A FLAT YEAR',text:'You hold \xa350,000 in a fund charging one per cent. The markets go nowhere and your return is zero. The fund still takes \xa3500. Next year the markets fall and you lose money. The fund still takes its one per cent.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A management fee is charged on the whole balance every year, not on the gain. It is taken whatever the year was like, which is why a small-sounding number is not small.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Independent of performance',text:'The return the fee is nominally paying for goes up and down. The fee does not. In good years it takes a slice of the gain; in flat years it takes money you already had; in bad years it adds to the loss.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'ONE PER CENT, TWO WAYS',left:{title:'Against the balance',items:['1% of what you hold','Sounds trivial']},right:{title:'Against a 4\u20135% real return',items:['A fifth to a quarter of it','Every year, compounding']},note:'Measured against what the money earns after inflation, the same fee looks very different.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'This year\u2019s growth sits in its own small pile, untouched. The scoop takes its measure off the whole heap.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it is quoted this way',text:'Fees are stated as a share of assets because that is the smaller-looking number. And the charge is deducted rather than paid, so it is easy never to see it as a payment in pounds.'},{kind:'try',text:'Check whether the charges on your largest account are levied on the balance or on the gain. Then work out last year\u2019s charge in pounds.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A fund lost five per cent this year. Its annual fee is 0.8% of assets. What did the fee do?',options:['It was taken anyway, adding to the year\u2019s loss','It was waived, since there was no gain to share','It was taken only from the money lost','It was reduced to match the fund\u2019s poor return'],answer:0,why:'A fee on the balance does not depend on performance. In a losing year it is simply an extra loss.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'A one per cent fee means the manager keeps one per cent of what they make for you.',reality:'It is one per cent of everything you hold, taken every year regardless of results. Measured as a share of what the money earns, the same fee is a fifth or more.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Because the fee is levied on the balance rather than on the gain, it is independent of performance, while the return it pays for is not. That is what makes small headline numbers large.'},{kind:'term',word:'Real return',meaning:'What an investment earns after inflation. The piece measures the fee against an expected real return of four or five per cent, the growth that actually adds to what you can buy.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The piece\u2019s reframing',text:'Against the balance, one per cent is small. Against a real return of five per cent it is a fifth of the outcome; against four, a quarter. And it recurs every year, so the slice compounds over the whole holding period.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A scoop off the top of the whole heap, with the year\u2019s growth piled separately and untouched. The fee is measured against the wrong pile.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The piece calls a fee on the balance much harsher than a share of profits. That holds in flat and falling years. In strong years a profit share can cost more: a fifth of a seven per cent gain is more than one per cent of the balance. The point is that a balance fee is certain, whatever the year does, not that it is always larger.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What did your accounts charge last year, in pounds rather than percentages? How did that compare with what they earned?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'COMPARE THE CHARGES',prompt:'Fund A charges 1% of the balance. Fund B charges 15% of any gain. In a year when both return zero, which costs more?',options:['Fund A, because its fee is taken whatever the return','Fund B, because fifteen is larger than one','Neither, because nothing was earned to charge on','They cost the same, as both are percentage fees'],answer:0,why:'With no gain, a share of profits is zero, but a charge on the balance is still taken. That independence from performance is the piece\u2019s point.'}]}},'the-cost-of-fees_2':{pictureCaption:'Two identical jars: one full, one a quarter short beside a small scoop. A small yearly fee, multiplied over decades, takes about that much.',bridge:'Idea 1 showed the fee is taken from everything, every year. This one puts a number on thirty years of that.',basics:{title:'Do the sum once',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TWO POTS, 30 YEARS',prompt:'Two pots each start with \xa3100 and grow 7p per \xa31 a year. Pot A pays a fee of a fifth of 1p per \xa31. Pot B pays 1p per \xa31. After 30 years, how far apart are they?',options:['About \xa31 apart','About \xa310 apart','About \xa3150 apart'],answer:2,reveal:'Pot A ends with about \xa3720. Pot B ends with about \xa3570. The fees looked almost the same. The gap is about \xa3150, from a start of just \xa3100.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A small yearly fee looks tiny next to how fast money grows. But it is taken every year, from all of it. Do the sum once and you see how big it gets.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the gap gets so big',text:'Each year the fee takes a bit of your pot. That bit is gone for good. It can\u2019t grow any more. So each year the gap is bigger than the year before.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A SLOW LEAK',text:'A bike tyre with a tiny leak seems fine each morning. You hardly see it go down. But after a month it is flat. Small and every day is what does it.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'ONE SMALL SCOOP A YEAR',caption:'Two jars that could have been the same. A small scoop came out of one every year. See how much less it holds.'},{kind:'try',text:'With a grown-up, find a savings sum online. Put in \xa3100 at 7 per cent for 30 years. Then try 6. Write the two answers side by side.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A fee is 1p for each \xa31 a year. Why does it take far more than 1p in the end?',options:['It comes off all the money every year, and that money never grows','The fee goes up a little every single year','Banks add extra fees later that you were not told about'],answer:0,why:'Each year\u2019s fee is taken from the whole pot, and what it takes can never grow again. So the cost keeps building.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE PIECE\u2019S WORKED CASE',title:'Thirty years at seven per cent',text:'Take \xa310,000 growing at seven per cent a year for thirty years. With no fee it reaches about \xa376,100. With a 0.2% fee it reaches about \xa372,000. With a 1% fee, about \xa357,400.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Do the multiplication once. At a 0.2% fee you keep roughly 94% of the no-fee result; at 1% you keep about 75%. The fee was one per cent a year. The cost was a quarter of the money.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHAT YOU KEEP AFTER 30 YEARS',left:{title:'0.2% fee',items:['About \xa372,000','Roughly 94% kept']},right:{title:'1% fee',items:['About \xa357,400','About 75% kept']},note:'A gap of under one point a year, and about \xa314,500 apart at the end.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'A QUARTER SHORT',caption:'The full jar is the no-fee result. The other is what a one per cent fee leaves after thirty years. The scoop is small; the empty space is not.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why people underestimate it',text:'The fee is quoted in the same units as the return, and one looks tiny beside seven. But the return is what the money does. The fee is a slice of the whole balance, including all the growth from every earlier year.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The shape does not change',text:'The exact figures depend on the return assumed and on when you pay in. The shape is the same: the gap widens with time, because the fee is charged on money that would otherwise have kept compounding.'},{kind:'try',text:'Run your own balance through a compound calculator twice, at the fee you pay now and at 0.2%, over the years you have left. Write the two final numbers side by side.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A colleague says the difference between a 0.3% and a 1.1% fund is under one per cent, so it hardly matters. What is the best reply?',options:['Over decades that gap compounds into a large share of the final balance','It matters only if the dearer fund also performs worse','They are right, as long as the money is held for under thirty years','It matters only in years when the market falls'],answer:0,why:'The gap is charged on the whole balance every year, and what it removes never compounds. Over thirty years at seven per cent, a gap that size costs about a fifth of the final balance.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'ONE POUND, THIRTY TIMES',text:'Seven per cent a year multiplies a pound by about 7.6 over thirty years. Six per cent, the same return less a one per cent fee, multiplies it by about 5.7. The ratio of the two is about three-quarters, whatever the starting balance.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A fee stated in the same units as the return looks negligible beside it. Multiplied out over decades, it removes a share of the final total many times its headline rate.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE GAP WIDENS',nodes:['The fee takes a slice','That slice stops compounding','Next year\u2019s balance is smaller','The fee repeats on the rest'],loop:!0,note:'The loss each year includes all the growth the earlier slices would have had.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'SMALL SCOOP, LARGE GAP',caption:'The scoop is small beside the jar. The space at the top of the right jar is what thirty years of it add up to, once the growth it removed is counted.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why almost nobody does it',text:'The piece notes that the multiplication takes two minutes with any online calculator, and almost nobody does it once. Having done it, you cannot un-know it, which is why it is worth doing before the next fund choice rather than after.'},{kind:'limits',text:'These are lump-sum figures at a steady seven per cent. Regular contributions shrink the share lost, since later money is charged for fewer years, and a lower assumed return changes the numbers. The piece concedes this. The calculation also counts cost only. Whether a fee buys anything worth having is the next idea\u2019s question.'},{kind:'recap',points:['At seven per cent for thirty years, 0.2% keeps about 94%.','At one per cent you keep about three-quarters.','Run your own numbers before choosing, not after.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'CHECK THE CLAIM',prompt:'Someone says a one per cent fee costs about a quarter of the money over thirty years. When does that figure overstate the cost?',options:['When most of the money is paid in during the later years','When the starting balance is very large','When the fee is taken monthly instead of yearly','When the investor checks the balance rarely'],answer:0,why:'Money paid in late is charged for fewer years, so the share lost is smaller. The balance size and checking habits do not change the ratio.'}]}},'the-cost-of-fees_3':{pictureCaption:'The contract is pinned under a stone. The weather vane turns with every draught. One is what you pay; the other is what you were promised.',bridge:'If a fee costs that much, it had better buy something. This idea weighs what a higher fee is sold on.',basics:{title:'A sure cost for a maybe',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'AT THE FAIR',text:'A boy says: give me two of your sweets now, and my trick will help you win more at the fair. The two sweets are gone for sure. The win is only a maybe.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Some money helpers charge a bigger fee and say they will earn you more. The fee is sure. The extra money is only a guess. So be careful with that deal.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The paper is held down by a stone. It is not going anywhere. The weather vane spins every way. That is the fee next to the promise.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO SIDES OF THE DEAL',left:{title:'The fee',items:['Written down','Always taken','You know how much']},right:{title:'The extra money',items:['A hope','May not come','No one knows how much']}},{kind:'explain',title:'Is a bigger fee always bad?',text:'No. Sometimes it is worth it. But the bigger fee has to prove it. Don\u2019t pay more just because someone says you will get more back.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Helper A charges \xa31 a year. Helper B charges \xa33 and says he will earn you more. What is true?',options:['The \xa33 is sure, but the extra money is only a guess','B will earn more, because he charges more','They are the same, since both are just fees'],answer:0,why:'The fee will be taken for sure. The extra money is only something B hopes for.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'A higher fee buys a better manager, who will earn it back.',reality:'The fee is in the contract and will be charged. The outperformance it is sold on is a forecast that may not arrive. You are paying a known amount now for an unknown amount later.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The fee is certain. The outperformance is not. A higher fee justified by expected returns trades a sure cost for an unsure gain. That is the trade to be most sceptical of.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the evidence says',text:'The piece describes the evidence on actively managed funds as consistent. Most do worse than their benchmarks over long periods after fees. Those that do better rarely keep doing better.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'HOW SURE EACH SIDE IS',low:'A forecast: the outperformance',high:'A contract: the fee',note:'The marketing asks whether the manager is good. The real question is whether a hope is worth a certainty.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where the burden sits',text:'Active management is not always wrong. But the burden of proof sits with the fee. The honest comparison is against the low-cost option, not against doing nothing.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A signed contract pinned by a stone beside a weather vane in the draught. You are weighing one against the other.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time a higher fee is justified by expected returns, say to yourself: I am weighing a certainty against a hope. Then ask what the cheap option would cost.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A fund charging 1.5% shows ten years of beating its benchmark. A tracker of the same market charges 0.1%. What does the piece suggest?',options:['The fee is certain; past outperformance is a weak guide','Choose the active fund, since ten years of results prove the skill','They are equal, because both funds hold the same market','Choose neither, since all fees are a guaranteed loss'],answer:0,why:'The piece says persistence among the funds that outperform is weak. The 1.4-point extra cost is certain, every year.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'CERTAINTY AGAINST PROBABILITY',text:'The piece reframes a familiar sales argument. A higher fee is usually defended by the claim that the manager will beat the market. That makes the decision a comparison between a contractual cost and a forecast, not between a good manager and a bad one.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A higher fee is a known payment for an unknown benefit. With the evidence on long-run active returns as it is, the fee carries the burden of proof.'},{kind:'term',word:'Benchmark',meaning:'The market index a fund is measured against. A low-cost tracker aims to match it, so beating it after fees is what a dearer active fund must do to justify itself.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Two findings, as the piece states them',text:'First, the majority of actively managed funds underperform their benchmarks over long periods after fees. Second, persistence among those that outperform is weak, so past winners are a poor guide to future ones. The piece calls the evidence consistent; it does not cite a figure.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A contract under a stone and a vane turning in the draught. The fee is fixed in place; the promised return moves with the wind.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The piece itself says active management is sometimes justified. Some markets have no cheap tracker, and some services, such as advice, are not sold on outperformance at all. A minority do beat their benchmarks. The argument shifts the burden of proof; it does not settle every case.'},{kind:'recap',points:['The fee is contractual; the outperformance is a forecast.','Compare against the cheap alternative, not against doing nothing.','The burden of proof sits with the fee.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'FIND THE WRONG COMPARISON',prompt:'A brochure says its fund turned \xa310,000 into \xa318,000 in ten years, so the 1.5% fee paid for itself. What is missing?',options:['What a low-cost fund in the same market would have returned','How much the fund manager was personally paid','Whether the fee was taken monthly or yearly','How many other investors chose the same fund'],answer:0,why:'The honest comparison is with the cheap alternative, not with holding nothing. If a tracker did as well, the fee bought nothing.'}]}},'the-cost-of-fees_4':{pictureCaption:'Four small taps, each dripping modestly, all filling the same bucket. Each charge looks reasonable; the bucket is what you actually pay.',bridge:'So far the fee has been one number. In practice it is several, and some are hard to find.',basics:{title:'Hidden extras add up',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'A \xa310 TOY ONLINE',text:'The toy says \xa310. At the end you see \xa32 for post. Then \xa31 as a service fee. Then \xa31 to pay by card. Each one is small. The \xa310 toy now costs \xa314.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The fee you are told about is often not the whole cost. Small extra charges hide in other places. Added up, they can cost as much as the fee you were told about, or more.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Four taps, each with just a small drip. But they all drip into one bucket. The bucket is what you really pay.'},{kind:'steps',title:'How to find the real cost',steps:['Find the fee they tell you about.','Look for every other charge.','Add them all up.','If no one can tell you the total, be careful.']},{kind:'explain',title:'Why be careful?',text:'If a shop can\u2019t tell you what you will pay in the end, that is a clue. It is rarely a good one. Honest prices are easy to say out loud.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A ticket says \xa320. There is a \xa33 booking fee and a \xa32 card fee. What is the real cost?',options:['\xa325','\xa320','\xa323'],answer:0,why:'The real cost is everything added up: \xa320 plus \xa33 plus \xa32.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE NUMBER ON THE FACTSHEET',text:'You chose a fund because its management charge was low. Then you notice the platform charges its own fee. The fund has trading costs of its own. You hold it in another currency. Nobody has added these up for you.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Headline fees are not the whole cost. Platform charges, fund charges, trading costs and currency conversion are each reasonable alone. The piece says that, stacked, the extras together can rival or exceed the headline charge.'},{kind:'equation',kicker:'AN ILLUSTRATIVE STACK',parts:['0.45% fund + 0.25% platform','0.2% trading + 0.1% currency'],result:'1.0% all in',note:'A made-up but ordinary example. A 0.45% headline becomes more than double once everything is counted.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where else to look',text:'Beyond the management charge sit bid-offer spreads. Some products also take performance fees, charged on gains that only tracked the market. Together, the extras can outweigh the headline charge itself.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Four modest drips from four taps on one pipe, all into one bucket. Read the bucket, not the taps.'},{kind:'explain',title:'When the total is hard to get',text:'Pension and insurance products are often the least transparent. The total can be hard to work out even when you try. Treat that difficulty as information. A product whose total cost cannot be stated on request is rarely hiding good news.'},{kind:'try',text:'Add every charge on one account into a single yearly percentage. Most people have never seen that figure for their own money.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A provider cannot tell you the all-in annual cost of a pension when you ask. How should you read that?',options:['As a warning: an unstatable total is rarely a favourable one','As normal, since pensions are complicated and charges vary','As reassuring: no figure means the charges are very small','As irrelevant, since only the headline fee is ever charged'],answer:0,why:'The piece treats difficulty getting the total as information in itself. What it tells you is rarely good.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE REST OF THE STACK',prompt:'On the piece\u2019s account, how do platform fees, trading costs, spreads and currency conversion compare with the headline charge?',options:['Together they can rival or exceed it','Together they are tiny beside it','They are already included in it'],answer:0,reveal:'The piece says the extras, taken together, can rival or exceed the headline charge. They sit where a reader would not think to look.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Cost is a stack, not a number. Each layer is defensible on its own; together they can rival or exceed the headline. The figure to find is the all-in one, and difficulty finding it is itself a signal.'},{kind:'term',word:'Bid-offer spread',meaning:'The gap between the price at which you can buy something and the price at which you can sell it. It is a cost of every trade, though it never appears as a fee.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'VISIBLE AND EMBEDDED',left:{title:'Usually visible',items:['Management charge','Platform fee']},right:{title:'Usually embedded',items:['Trading costs inside the fund','Spreads and currency conversion','Some performance fees']},note:'A performance fee on gains that only tracked the market charges you for the market.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Opacity as information',text:'The piece\u2019s sharpest point is about products where the total cannot be stated on request. Pensions and insurance wrappers are often the worst. The piece reads that opacity not as a nuisance but as evidence about the product.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Some embedded costs are unavoidable: any fund that trades pays spreads, including cheap ones. The aim is to know the total, not to reach zero. And the claim that the extras together can rival or exceed the headline charge is the piece\u2019s own judgement, stated without figures; how the stack splits varies from account to account.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'WHICH IS THE REAL FIGURE?',prompt:'Fund X advertises 0.15% but you pay 0.4% to the platform and it trades heavily. Fund Y advertises 0.5% on a platform with no charge. What should you compare?',options:['The all-in annual cost of each, every layer included','The advertised charges, since those are the fund costs','Only the platform fees, as those differ most','Past returns, since costs are already in them'],answer:0,why:'Headline charges hide the stack. Only the all-in figure tells you which actually costs less.'}]}},'the-cost-of-fees_5':{pictureCaption:'A wall of dials nobody can move, and one lever at the side that turns easily in the hand. The fee is that lever.',bridge:'The first four ideas showed what fees cost. This one says why cutting them is an unusually good use of your time.',basics:{title:'The one easy win',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'GETTING MONEY TO GROW',belief:'To do well with money, you have to guess what will happen next.',reality:'Most choices are guesses. But one is not. Paying a smaller fee leaves you more money for sure, with no guess needed.'},{kind:'idea',text:'You can\u2019t control how much your money grows. But you can control the fee you pay. Lower fees leave you more, for sure. So look at fees first.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Lots of dials nobody can move. One lever you can. Fees are the lever.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHAT YOU CAN AND CAN\u2019T DO',left:{title:'Out of your hands',items:['How fast money grows','What prices do','Good or bad years']},right:{title:'In your hands',items:['How big a fee you pay']}},{kind:'explain',title:'Then why don\u2019t people do it?',text:'Checking fees takes an afternoon now. The extra money only shows up years later. So it feels like a chore with no prize. The prize is real. It is just far away.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Two pots hold the same things. One fee is low, one is high. Which leaves you more?',options:['The low-fee one, for sure','You can\u2019t know without guessing','The high-fee one, as it costs more'],answer:0,why:'If what is inside is the same, the only difference is the fee. Less fee means more left for you.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'AN AFTERNOON\u2019S WORK',prompt:'You move \xa350,000 from a 1% fund to a 0.2% fund holding the same market, and leave it 25 years at 7%. Roughly what is that afternoon worth?',options:['About \xa3400','About \xa34,000','About \xa344,000'],answer:2,reveal:'About \xa344,000: roughly \xa3259,000 against \xa3215,000. If the comparing and switching took four hours, that is around \xa311,000 an hour.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Fees are one of the few things in investing you control fully and at once, with no forecasting needed. That makes them the first thing to look at, not the last.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it is close to unique',text:'Most money choices trade risk against return, and sensible people disagree. Cutting fees does not. For the same holdings, it raises expected return without adding risk. The gain is arithmetic, not a prediction.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A wall of dials that respond to nobody, and one lever that turns under one hand. Spend your effort on the lever.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it goes undone',text:'The gain is unseen and comes later. The effort comes now. Nobody feels the extra balance in thirty years. Everybody feels the afternoon spent comparing platforms. The piece\u2019s answer is to see it as a one-off task with a very high hourly rate.'},{kind:'try',text:'Spend your next hour of financial attention on fees rather than on picking anything. List what you pay, and one cheaper equivalent for each.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You have one free afternoon for your finances. Which use fits the piece best?',options:['Comparing what you pay on each account with cheaper equivalents','Reading forecasts to decide which sector will do best next year','Watching the markets to pick a better moment to invest','Researching which fund manager has the best recent record'],answer:0,why:'Only the fee review produces a gain that needs no prediction to be correct. The others depend on forecasts.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO KINDS OF DECISION',left:{title:'Most financial choices',items:['Trade risk for return','Benefit depends on forecasts','Reasonable people disagree']},right:{title:'Cutting fees',items:['No added risk','Benefit is arithmetic','Better option known in advance']},note:'The piece calls the second kind close to unique in investing.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Lowering fees raises expected return with certainty and without added risk. It is the rare financial decision whose better option is identifiable in advance, so it should come first.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where the piece ends',text:'Having shown what fees cost, where they hide and why higher ones often fail to earn their keep, the piece closes on what to do. It does not say expensive products are always wrong. It says the fee is the first variable to examine, because it is the one you fully control.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The behavioural obstacle',text:'The gain is invisible and arrives decades later; the effort arrives this afternoon. People weigh the immediate cost heavily and the distant gain lightly. Framing it as one task with a high hourly rate brings the gain forward into terms you can feel.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Dials that respond to no one, and a single lever that moves easily. The fee is the only control on the panel that is fully yours.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The certainty holds only like for like. A cheaper product that holds different assets is a different bet, not just a cheaper one. Switching can itself cost money, through exit charges, tax or time out of the market. And the cheapest option can lack things worth paying for, such as advice you would actually follow.'},{kind:'recap',points:['You control fees completely, with no forecast needed.','Like for like, a lower fee is a certain gain.','Check for switching costs and real differences first.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'TEST THE CERTAINTY',prompt:'Which switch does NOT deliver the certain gain the piece describes?',options:['Moving from a global equity fund to a cheaper bond fund','Moving to the same index fund on a cheaper platform','Moving to a cheaper tracker of the same index','Moving to a share class of the same fund with a lower charge'],answer:0,why:'Switching to bonds changes what you hold, so the outcome depends on how the two markets perform. The certainty only applies when the holdings stay the same.'}]}}}},3018,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'the-exercise-findings-that-held_1':{pictureCaption:'One step is lit: the first one up off the floor. For someone who does nothing, that is where most of the gain is.',basics:{title:'The first bit of exercise helps the most',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'WHO GAINS MOST?',prompt:'One extra short walk a day. Who gets the biggest health boost from it?',options:['Someone who does no exercise at all','Someone who already runs every day','They both gain the same'],answer:0,reveal:'The person who does nothing. Scientists who study this found the biggest gains right at the start: going from almost nothing to a little.'},{kind:'idea',text:'For health, the first little bit of exercise does the most good. Going from nothing to a short walk helps more than going from fit to very fit.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What did they look at?',text:'They looked at the risk of dying early. That risk dropped the most for people who went from almost no exercise to a little. After that, each extra bit still helped, but by less and less.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A DRINK WHEN THIRSTY',text:'When you are really thirsty, the first glass of water feels amazing. The second is nice. By the fifth, it does almost nothing. The first bit of exercise is like that first glass.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The lit step is the first one up off the floor. If you do nothing, that small first step is the one that counts most.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why big goals can backfire',text:'Big goals are made for people who are already fit. If you do nothing, a huge goal can feel like a wall, so you never start. But a small start is where you would gain the most.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your gran never goes out for walks. Which plan fits this idea best?',options:['A ten-minute walk with her, most days','A long hike every weekend, or nothing','Nothing, since short walks are too small to count'],answer:0,why:'For someone who does nothing, a little is where the biggest gain is. A huge plan might never start at all.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE NEW YEAR PLAN',text:'A friend who has not exercised in years decides to get fit. The plan is five gym sessions a week, an hour each. By the third week it has stopped, and he concludes that exercise is not for him.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The biggest health gains come from going from doing nothing to doing a little. After that the returns shrink. So the person with the most to gain is the one doing nothing, and the amount they need is small.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'WHERE THE GAIN SITS',low:'Doing nothing',high:'Very active',note:'The largest drop in mortality risk sits at the far left, in the step from almost nothing to a little. Further right, each extra hour buys less.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why this gets missed',text:'Most exercise targets are pitched at people who are already active. To someone starting from zero they read as a wall. The review argues they can put off exactly the people for whom a small change would do the most.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One step lit: the first one off the floor. On the research curve it is also the steepest climb.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it is unexciting, and solid',text:'A short daily walk is not a story anyone wants to sell. The review calls it the least motivating finding in the field, and also one of the best supported. Unimpressive and true is still worth acting on.'},{kind:'try',text:'If you do little or no exercise, set the full guideline aside for now. Walk for ten minutes, most days, and count that as the whole plan.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A colleague who never exercises asks where to start. Which advice best fits the evidence?',options:['Ten minutes of walking most days, and keep it up','Wait until you can commit to the full guideline amount','Start with hard interval sessions, since intensity counts most','Skip short walks, which are too little to make a difference'],answer:0,why:'For someone starting from nothing, a small amount sits on the steepest part of the curve. Waiting for the full amount means missing the largest gain.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHAT THIS REVIEW ASKS',text:'This Mindroll review sorts exercise research into claims that held up at scale, claims that shrank under scrutiny, and claims that came from who volunteers for studies. It opens with the finding it calls the most practically important in the whole literature.'},{kind:'term',word:'Dose-response curve',meaning:'A graph of how an outcome changes as the amount of something rises. Here the dose is physical activity and the outcome is mortality risk.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The curve for activity is steepest at the bottom. The largest reductions in mortality risk come from moving from nothing to a little, amounts well short of the usual guidelines, and it flattens considerably after that.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'The usual framing',items:['Aim for the full guideline','Gains go to the committed','Less than the target is failure']},right:{title:'What the curve says',items:['A fraction of it already helps most','Gains go mostly to the sedentary','A little is the biggest win']},note:'The review calls this an inversion of the usual framing.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why standard advice can backfire',text:'If the steep part of the curve sits below the guidelines, targets pitched at the fully active carry a cost. They tell the sedentary that anything less than the full amount falls short, when a small amount is where their gain is largest.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The first step off the floor, lit. On the dose-response curve, that rise is the steepest one there is.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The finding is about populations, not a promise to one person. The review does not say what kind of studies the curve rests on, and its next idea is a reason to ask before trusting exact sizes. A flatter curve is not a flat one: gains shrink, they do not stop. And it concerns mortality, not fitness or performance.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISREADING',prompt:'Which conclusion does the dose-response finding *not* support?',options:['Very active people gain nothing at all from doing more','A sedentary person\u2019s first ten minutes matter most','Targets pitched at the active can put beginners off','A short daily walk is worth suggesting to the inactive'],answer:0,why:'The curve flattens after the first step; it does not go flat. The finding is about where the gain is largest, not about later gains vanishing.'}]}},'the-exercise-findings-that-held_2':{pictureCaption:'One plot started with rich soil, so of course it grows more. Comparing the harvests says little about the gardening.',bridge:'Idea 1 described a curve. This one asks how far to trust the numbers behind findings like it.',basics:{title:'Who chose to exercise?',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'A BIG CLAIM',text:'You read that people who exercise are much healthier than people who don\u2019t. It sounds simple. Exercise makes you healthy. But stop for a second and ask who the exercisers are.'},{kind:'idea',text:'People who choose to exercise are often different in other ways too. They may have more money, sleep more and eat better. So some of their good health may come from those things.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One plot had rich soil from the start, so it grows more. That does not prove its gardener is better.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'AT SCHOOL',text:'Kids in an after-school maths club get better maths marks. Is it the club? Maybe. But kids who pick a maths club may already like maths. The club gets the credit for what they brought with them.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'So exercise does not really work.',reality:'It does work. The point is smaller. The big numbers you read are often too big, because other good things come along with being a person who exercises.'},{kind:'explain',title:'A fairer test',text:'Scientists can pick people by chance, like names from a hat. One group is told to exercise and one is not. Now the groups start out alike. These tests tend to find smaller gains. They are the ones to trust most.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A study finds that kids who play sport sleep better. What is the best first question to ask?',options:['What else is different about kids who play sport?','How much sport do I need to sleep well?','Which sport gives the best sleep?'],answer:0,why:'Kids who choose sport may differ in other ways that also help sleep. Ask that first, before you give sport all the credit.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A HEADLINE',prompt:'A study reports that people who run are far healthier than people who don\u2019t. What most likely makes the gap so large?',options:['Running is extremely powerful','Runners differ from non-runners in other ways too','The study was badly faked'],answer:1,reveal:'Running probably helps. But people who run by choice also tend to differ in income, sleep, diet and how often they see a doctor. All of that travels into the gap.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Most exercise research compares people who chose to exercise with people who did not. The headline effects are inflated by everything else that comes with being the sort of person who runs.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Observational study',items:['Watches people who chose','Adjusts for what it measured','Usually the headline numbers']},right:{title:'Randomised trial',items:['Assigns exercise by chance','Groups start out alike','Smaller, less flattering results']},note:'The review says the trials are the ones to weight.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why adjusting is not enough',text:'Good observational studies try to subtract the differences, adjusting for income, say. But they can only adjust for what they measured, and that is never all of it. Whatever was left out still travels with the exercise and gets counted as its effect.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The rich plot outgrows the poor one, but it started with better soil. A harvest comparison cannot separate soil from skill.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'So the benefits of exercise are a myth.',reality:'No. The review is clear that exercise works. What is inflated is the size of the effect, because headline numbers are almost always the observational ones.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you see a big exercise claim, ask one thing: did these people choose to exercise, or were they assigned to it by chance?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A report says people who cycle to work are much healthier than people who drive. Which reading fits the idea?',options:['Cycling may help, but cyclists may differ in other ways that matter','Cycling causes the whole difference, since the gap is so large','The finding is worthless and tells us nothing at all','Any driver who switches can expect the full gap'],answer:0,why:'Exercise probably contributes, but people who choose to cycle bring other advantages with them. The gap probably overstates the effect rather than measuring it.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',word:'Self-selection',meaning:'When people choose for themselves whether to do the thing being studied, so the groups being compared differ in ways besides that thing.'},{kind:'idea',text:'People who exercise voluntarily differ from those who do not in income, sleep, diet and how likely they are to see a doctor. Observational studies adjust for what they measured, which is never all of it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where it sits in the review',text:'The review promises to separate what replicated, what shrank, and what was an artefact of who volunteers. This idea names the third category, and it is a lens for the rest: before trusting any effect size, ask how the groups came to be.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE EFFECT GROWS',nodes:['A person chooses to exercise','Income, sleep, diet and doctor visits come too','Better health is measured','All of it credited to exercise'],note:'Adjustment removes the differences that were measured. The rest stays in the estimate.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the review recommends',text:'Weight the randomised trials. Because they assign exercise by chance, the groups start alike. They tend to be smaller and less flattering. Headlines almost always carry the observational numbers instead, and those are inflated by everything that travels with being a runner.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two plots that did not start equal, and a hand comparing what they grew. That comparison is what most observational exercise research makes.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The review is explicit that this does not mean exercise fails to work. The inflation is in the size of the effect, not its existence. Trials have limits of their own: smaller studies give less precise answers. The habit to learn is weighting the evidence, not dismissing it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'Someone says a study adjusted for income and diet, so self-selection is dealt with. What is the best reply?',options:['Adjustment covers only what was measured, and some differences never are','Adjustment makes the bias worse, so the result should be ignored','Income and diet are the only differences that really matter','Self-selection only affects studies of elite athletes'],answer:0,why:'Adjusting helps with the differences someone thought to record. Whatever went unrecorded still rides along with the exercise.'}]}},'the-exercise-findings-that-held_3':{pictureCaption:'An older woman carries a heavy crate of logs up her front steps. Hard work, no gym: the kind of strength work the advice now includes.',bridge:'Ideas 1 and 2 were about how much exercise and how to read the evidence. This one is about a kind of exercise the guidance added.',basics:{title:'Strong muscles count too',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'WHAT COUNTS AS EXERCISE?',belief:'Exercise just means running about until you are out of breath.',reality:'That is good for your heart. But health advice now asks for strength work too: making your muscles push or lift something heavy.'},{kind:'idea',text:'For a long time, exercise advice was mostly about the heart. Now strength work is part of it too. The advice changed because the evidence changed.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why do muscles matter so much?',text:'As people get old, their muscles and bones get weaker. Then falls get more likely. Older people with stronger muscles are more likely to stay able to look after themselves. And muscles grow stronger with hard work, at any age.'},{kind:'steps',title:'What the advice asks for',steps:['About twice a week.','Work all the big muscles, not just one.','Use a load that makes the last few lifts hard.']},{kind:'example',kicker:'NO GYM NEEDED',picture:!0,text:'You do not need a gym or fancy weights. Press-ups, climbing, or carrying heavy bags can all make muscles work hard. What counts is that it feels hard by the end.'},{kind:'try',text:'Try slow press-ups against a wall. Stop when the last few feel hard. That hard feeling is the part that counts.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your uncle says strength work is only for bodybuilders. What does the newer advice say?',options:['Grown-ups should do some, about twice a week','Only young people need to do it','Running is enough, so muscles don\u2019t matter'],answer:0,why:'The advice now asks grown-ups for strength work twice a week. It matters most as people get older.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'THE ADVICE CHANGED',text:'For a long time, the public message about exercise was mostly cardiovascular: get your heart rate up. Strength work sat among the optional extras. Then the major guidelines moved it to explicitly recommended. The review counts this as one of the clearer cases of guidance changing because the evidence changed.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Resistance training is now recommended, not optional. Muscle and bone decline with age, they matter for falls and frailty, and muscle responds to loading at any age.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHY STRENGTH WAS ADDED',nodes:['Muscle and bone decline with age','Falls and frailty become more likely','Later independence is at risk'],note:'Muscle responds to loading at any age, so the first link is not fixed.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A crate of logs carried up the front steps. Muscle responds to load at any age, and a load like this counts if it is hard by the top.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where the evidence came from',text:'The revision owed a good deal to research on ageing. Muscle mass and grip strength turn out to predict later independence well, and both respond to loading, even late in life. That made strength a matter of staying able to live on your own.'},{kind:'steps',title:'The modest version',steps:['Twice a week.','Cover the major muscle groups.','Use load that is genuinely hard by the final repetitions.'],note:'That is the threshold most guidelines settled on.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Strength training means a gym and free weights.',reality:'The evidence supports the stimulus, not the equipment. What counts is load that is challenging by the last repetitions, done regularly, in whatever form you will actually keep up.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick two days this week for strength work. Use whatever you will really do, at home or anywhere, and make the last few repetitions hard.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A 70-year-old relative thinks they are too old to build strength. What does the evidence in the review suggest?',options:['Muscle responds to loading at any age, so it is worth starting','Strength work only helps if it was started before middle age','Regular walking already covers all that strength work would do','At that age only gym machines can provide enough load'],answer:0,why:'The review says muscle responds to loading at any age, and later life is where strength matters most for independence.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'A FINDING THAT GREW',text:'Much of this review is about claims that shrank under scrutiny. This one went the other way. Resistance training moved from optional to explicitly recommended in the major guidelines, and the review judges the evidence behind the change reasonably strong.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Strength work belongs in the guidance because muscle mass and bone density both decline with age and both matter for falls and frailty, and because muscle responds to loading at any age.'},{kind:'term',word:'Resistance training',meaning:'Exercise in which muscles work against a load: a weight, a band, your own body. The evidence points at the load, not at the tool.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Muscles working against a load: here, a crate of logs and a flight of steps. The guidance cares about the load, and it holds late in life too.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why ageing research drove it',text:'The revision owed much to studies of ageing. Muscle mass and grip strength predict later independence well. That tied strength to an outcome people care about, staying able to live on their own, and gave the guidance a reason to change.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'What held',items:['Twice a week','Major muscle groups','Hard final repetitions']},right:{title:'What did not',items:['It must be in a gym','It must use free weights','There is one right piece of kit']},note:'The evidence supports the stimulus, not the equipment.'},{kind:'explain',title:'A clause about doing it',text:'The review adds that the version that gets done is whatever the person will actually do. That anticipates its last idea, where keeping going outweighs choosing the best version of a programme.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Prediction is not cause. That grip strength predicts independence does not show that training would secure it; strength may partly be a sign of general health, the self-selection problem of idea 2. The review calls the evidence reasonably strong, not settled. And two sessions is a threshold guidelines settled on, not a measured optimum.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'Someone has no gym access and dislikes weights. Which plan best fits the evidence?',options:['Bodyweight and household loads twice a week, hard by the end','Skip strength work, since without a gym it does not count','Daily light stretching, since any movement builds muscle','Wait until gym access is possible, then begin properly'],answer:0,why:'The guidance is about challenging load on the major muscles twice a week. The equipment is whatever gets that done.'}]}},'the-exercise-findings-that-held_4':{pictureCaption:'The ring is switched off and still warm. That heat is real. It is never going to boil that kettle.',bridge:'Idea 3 was a finding that grew. This one is a real finding that was sold as far bigger than it is.',basics:{title:'The after-burn is real, and small',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A COOKER RING',picture:!0,text:'Turn off a cooker ring. It stays warm for a while. That heat is real. You could feel it with your hand. But it will not boil a big kettle of water.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What is the after-burn?',text:'After very hard exercise, your body keeps using a bit of extra energy for a while. Scientists can measure it. People call it the after-burn. Energy is counted in calories, the same units used on food packets.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'Some ads said the after-burn uses loads of energy. In careful tests, how big was it?',options:['Huge: more than the workout itself','Small: tens of calories','Zero: it was made up'],answer:1,reveal:'It is real, but small. Most careful tests found tens of calories, not the hundreds some ads promised.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The after-burn is real, but much smaller than people were told. Hard workouts are still worth doing. Just not for that reason.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'What was sold',items:['Hundreds of extra calories','Short and hard burns the most']},right:{title:'What tests found',items:['Tens of calories, most often','The numbers do not show that']}},{kind:'explain',title:'So what went wrong?',text:'Nobody made the after-burn up. A real, small thing was made to sound big, because it helped sell a kind of workout. Real is not the same as large.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A friend only does short, hard workouts because she thinks the after-burn lasts for hours. What is the best reply?',options:['The after-burn is real but small, so pick for other reasons','The after-burn is fake, so hard workouts are pointless','She is right, it burns more than the workout'],answer:0,why:'Hard workouts can be good. But the after-burn is too small to be the reason to choose them.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE SALES PITCH',belief:'A short, intense session keeps burning hundreds of extra calories for hours afterwards.',reality:'The afterburn exists and is measurable. But in most controlled work it is modest: typically tens of calories, a small fraction of the session itself.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The afterburn is real and much smaller than advertised. Interval training is worth doing; the mechanism used to sell it was oversold.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the claim spread',text:'The afterburn supported a tidy story: that a short intense session outperforms a longer moderate one on energy spent. That made it a selling point. The review says the numbers do not support the story.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The ring is off and still warm. The heat is real. It is not going to boil that kettle.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Even the best case is narrow',text:'The effect is largest after very intense work, the kind few people sustain regularly. So the biggest afterburn applies to sessions most people rarely do, and is still a modest fraction of the session.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'CHOOSING A CLASS',text:'You are choosing between a short interval class and a longer steady ride. If the afterburn is a rounding error, it should not decide. Choose on what you enjoy, what fits your week and what you will keep doing.'},{kind:'try',text:'Take the last fitness claim you believed. Ask two questions: is the effect real, and is it as big as the claim needs it to be?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'An app says its 15-minute workout beats an hour\u2019s walk on energy burned, thanks to afterburn. What is the best response?',options:['Doubt it: the afterburn is too small to carry that claim','Believe it: the afterburn is a real, measured effect','Believe it, provided the workout is very intense','Dismiss interval training as a waste of time'],answer:0,why:'The afterburn exists, but tens of calories cannot carry a claim like that. Being real is not the same as being big enough.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'BEFORE THE NUMBERS',prompt:'Measured properly, how does the extra energy spent after a session compare with the session itself?',options:['Usually larger than the session','Roughly equal to it','A modest fraction of it'],answer:2,reveal:'A modest fraction, largest after very intense work. In most controlled work it is typically tens of calories, not the several hundred sold alongside interval training.'},{kind:'term',word:'EPOC',meaning:'Excess post-exercise oxygen consumption: the raised metabolic rate after exercise. It is the technical name for the afterburn.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The afterburn is real and much smaller than the marketing suggests. The effect exists and the studies are legitimate; the conclusion popular fitness content draws from them is not one the data licenses.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW A FINDING GETS OVERSOLD',nodes:['A real, measurable effect','A claim it seems to support','The claim is marketed widely','The conclusion outruns the data'],note:'The review offers the afterburn as a good example of this pattern.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The claim it was asked to carry',text:'The afterburn became a selling point because it supported one claim: that a short intense session beats a longer moderate one on energy expenditure. To carry that, the effect would need to be large. It is not, so the claim fails even though the effect is real.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A switched-off ring, still giving off real heat, beside a kettle it could never boil. Real and big enough are different tests.'},{kind:'limits',text:'This is a verdict on one argument, not on interval training, which the review says is worth doing. A small effect is not nothing, and it is largest after the hardest work. The lesson is proportion, and the mistake runs both ways: inflating a real effect, or dismissing it outright because it was once inflated.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE SAME PATTERN',prompt:'Which case shows the same flaw as the afterburn story?',options:['A real but small effect sold as the main reason to buy something','A large effect found in one trial and never repeated','A result that its own authors turned out to have invented','A small study that honestly reports its own small result'],answer:0,why:'The afterburn flaw is amplification: a genuine finding asked to support more than its size allows. Fabrication and failed replication are different problems.'}]}},'the-exercise-findings-that-held_5':{pictureCaption:'Smart pairs sit unworn on the rack. The battered pair by the door is the one that went out all winter.',bridge:'Idea 3 noted that the strength work that counts is the work that gets done. This idea makes that the main point.',basics:{title:'The best plan is the one you keep',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE PERFECT PLAN',text:'Your cousin gets the best running shoes in the shop. He finds the perfect training plan online. He runs hard for two weeks. Then it rains. He stops, and he never starts again.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The best exercise plan is the one you will still be doing in a year. Keeping going matters much more than picking the perfect plan.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Perfect plan, dropped',items:['Best on paper','Stopped in March']},right:{title:'Okay plan, kept',items:['Fine on paper','Still going next winter']},note:'The okay plan wins. Plans differ a little. Keeping going or stopping differs a lot.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The shoes on the shelf look smart and new. The worn pair by the door is the pair that really gets used.'},{kind:'explain',title:'So how do you choose?',text:'Ask if you enjoy it. Ask if it is easy to fit in. Ask if friends will do it with you. Ask if it feels like you. Those things help you keep going more than a clever plan does.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of a sport or game you would still play on a cold, wet day. That is a good clue to the exercise you will keep.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Mia can join a dance class with her friends, or follow an expert plan alone that bores her. Which fits this idea?',options:['The dance class, since she will likely keep going','The expert plan, since it is best on paper','Neither, until she finds a perfect plan'],answer:0,why:'A plan she keeps doing will help her more than a better plan she drops.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'JANUARY TO JANUARY',text:'Two people start exercising in January. One researches the best programme and follows it to the letter, until March. The other joins a friend for a fairly hard weekly session and is still going the next January. By this review\u2019s logic, the second is well ahead.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Studies that compare training programmes mostly find small differences. The gap between people who keep going and people who stop is far larger. So the best programme is largely the one you will still be doing in a year.'},{kind:'equation',parts:['A decent programme','Years of doing it'],result:'The result',note:'Swap in an optimal programme and drop the years, and the sum collapses.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What keeping going depends on',text:'Whether you keep going depends on enjoyment, ease, the people around you and who you feel you are. It does not depend on physiology. A plan that fits your week, involves people you like and feels like who you are will outlast a cleverer one that does not.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Specialist shoes, pristine on the rack. The worn ordinary pair by the door is the one doing the work.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the advice ignores it',text:'Tuning a programme is easy to study and easy to publish: run a trial, report a winner. Getting people to stick with one is neither. So advice writers keep answering the question that matters less. They sell methods on the answer.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before starting a routine, ask: would I do this in bad weather, on a tired day, in March? If not, change it until the answer is yes.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A friend who hates early mornings joins a 6 a.m. bootcamp because a trial rated it best. He has missed four of the first six sessions. What fits the idea?',options:['Switch to something he will turn up for, even if it rates lower','Stick with it, since the trial shows it works better than others','Add an extra session each week to make up for the missed ones','Hold off until a trial finds a programme that suits him better'],answer:0,why:'Differences between programmes are modest. Whether he is still turning up in a year is the larger difference. The bootcamp is already failing that test.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'The key to results is finding the optimal training protocol.',reality:'Comparisons between protocols generally find modest differences. The large differences are between people who continue and people who stop.'},{kind:'term',word:'Adherence',meaning:'Whether, and for how long, a person keeps doing what a programme asks. It belongs to the person and their circumstances as much as to the programme.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Adherence is the variable that actually distinguishes outcomes between people, and the one the literature is weakest on. A moderately effective programme performed for years beats an optimal one abandoned in March.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The reframe',text:'The selection question changes. Not which protocol wins in a trial, but which one you will still be doing in a year. That depends on enjoyment, convenience, social context and identity rather than physiology, so the answer differs from person to person.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the literature misses it',text:'Optimising the protocol is tractable and publishable. Optimising adherence is neither. So research keeps producing confident answers to the question with the smaller stakes. It echoes idea 1: in both, the gain sits with the unglamorous option.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A rack of specialist shoes, all pristine, and one battered pair by the door. Only one of them has a year of results in it.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The review admits the literature is weakest here: it is clear that keeping going matters, much less clear how to make people keep going. Idea 2 applies too, since people who continue may differ from those who stop in other ways. And an easy-to-keep plan can still miss something, like the strength work of idea 3.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HARD CASE',prompt:'A coach says to pick the programme with the best trial result and let discipline handle the rest. What is the strongest reply?',options:['Protocol gaps are small; whether you continue matters far more','Trials are always wrong about which programmes work','Discipline has no bearing on exercise outcomes at all','The best programme is the one with the newest study'],answer:0,why:'The review finds the differences between protocols modest and the gap between continuers and stoppers large. Choosing for adherence beats choosing for a trial win.'}]}}}},3019,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'the-meeting-that-was-a-decision_1':{pictureCaption:'Eight pairs of hands on one heavy block, each touching it so lightly that none of them is really taking the weight.',basics:{title:'When everyone decides, nobody does',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE CLASS PARTY',text:'Your class must choose a theme for the end-of-term party. The class rep could just pick one. Instead, the whole class talks about it for three lunchtimes. The theme you end up with is one nobody much wanted.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Sometimes a group talks about a choice one person could make. Then if it goes wrong, nobody gets the blame. That feels safe, but it wastes time and often gives a worse choice.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Lots of hands on one heavy block. Each hand touches it so lightly that nobody is really carrying it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why people do it',text:'If you choose alone and it goes wrong, it is your fault. If the group chooses, it is nobody\u2019s fault. So people take choices to the group. Nobody plans it. It just feels safer.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What it costs',text:'It takes much longer. And groups often pick the choice that is easiest to explain if someone complains, not the best one. So the safe, boring theme wins.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of a group choice you were part of. If it had gone badly, whose fault would it have been?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your football team spends a whole practice voting on new kit colours. The captain could have chosen. What might the vote really be doing?',options:['Spreading the choice so no one gets blamed','Making sure the team ends up with the best colours','Giving the captain a rest from choosing'],answer:0,why:'When a group decides, no one carries the blame. That can feel safe, but here it cost a whole practice.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE WEEKLY SYNC',belief:'A standing meeting is where decisions get made.',reality:'A recurring meeting with no decision on the agenda is often doing a different job. It spreads ownership of a call across enough people that nobody carries it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A standing meeting is often a device for spreading responsibility, not for deciding. That is a real function. But it is a costly way to buy it.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW A CALL DISAPPEARS',nodes:['One person could decide','They bring it to the group','The group discusses it','The outcome belongs to no one'],note:'Nobody chose this, and each person in the room gains. A decision made alone can be wrong in a way a group one cannot.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What it costs',text:'Speed, first. A call one person could make in a day can take weeks of meetings. Then quality. Group decisions drift toward what is defensible rather than what is correct. That is because the defensible option is the one nobody will be blamed for.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Eight pairs of hands on one block, all touching lightly. It looks carried. Nobody is taking the weight.'},{kind:'try',text:'Take one recurring meeting in your week. Ask: if the last decision it made turns out badly, whose name is on it?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A weekly review has run for a year. Asked who is accountable if its last decision goes wrong, nobody can name anyone. What does that suggest?',options:['It is mainly distributing responsibility, whatever it is called','It is working well, since the whole team shares ownership','It needs more attendees, so that decisions are better informed','It needs a longer slot, so that decisions get more discussion'],answer:0,why:'Where no name can be given, the meeting is spreading ownership until nobody carries it. That holds whatever it is called.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHERE THE PIECE STARTS',text:'The piece argues that many recurring meetings exist because someone did not want to make a call alone. It begins by naming the job such meetings quietly do, then turns to what that costs and how to test for it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Many standing meetings are distributed responsibility: a call one person could make is brought to a group, and the outcome becomes attributable to no one. A real function, bought at a high price.'},{kind:'term',word:'Distributed responsibility',meaning:'Ownership of a decision spread across enough people that no single person carries it. Nobody chooses the arrangement, and everyone benefits locally, because a decision made alone can be wrong in a way a collective one cannot.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it persists',text:'Each participant gains cover and nobody pays a visible price. The stated purpose, an update or a review, need not match what the meeting does. The piece\u2019s point is that the function runs regardless of the name on the invitation.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'DEFENSIBLE OR CORRECT',left:{title:'What the group drifts to',items:['The defensible option','The one nobody will be blamed for','Reached slowly']},right:{title:'What one owner can choose',items:['The correct option, if they see it','A call someone answers for','Made when it is needed']},note:'The cost the piece names is speed and quality: group decisions regress toward what can be defended.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Many hands, one block, no weight taken. Shared that widely, the load is held by no one in particular.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Some decisions genuinely need a group, where the information is spread across people and has to be assembled live. Shared ownership can also be right: a decision that commits everyone may need everyone\u2019s agreement. And cover is sometimes worth buying. The objection is to buying it by habit, without noticing the price.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'USE THE DIAGNOSTIC',prompt:'Which question best reveals whether a meeting is distributing responsibility?',options:['If this decision turns out badly, who will be accountable?','Does everyone feel that they have been heard in the room?','Is the agenda sent out early enough before the meeting?','Are all the right departments represented at the table?'],answer:0,why:'The piece\u2019s diagnostic is accountability. Where no name is available, the meeting is spreading ownership whatever its agenda says.'}]}},'the-meeting-that-was-a-decision_2':{pictureCaption:'One chair at the table, one sheet and a pen. The other chairs are stacked by the wall: this call needed only one seat.',bridge:'Idea 1 named the hidden job a meeting can do. This is the question that exposes it, and what to do instead.',basics:{title:'Could one person have decided?',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A DAY OUT',prompt:'Your family spends an hour choosing between two days out. Mum could have picked. Why might she ask everyone instead?',options:['She wanted to hear what you all like','She was not sure it was her choice','She did not want the blame if it rained','Any of these'],answer:3,reveal:'Each one is a real reason. And each one has a quicker fix than an hour of talking.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Before a group talks about a choice, ask: could one person have just decided and told everyone? Very often, the answer is yes.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One chair, one sheet, one pen. The other chairs are stacked away. This choice only needs one person.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'THREE REASONS, THREE FIXES',left:{title:'Why it came to the group',items:['Wants to share the blame','Wants to hear ideas','Not sure it is their choice']},right:{title:'A quicker fix',items:['Someone says clearly: you decide','Ask people by message first','Say out loud who decides']},note:'None of these fixes is a long meeting.'},{kind:'explain',title:'When a group is needed',text:'Sometimes you do need everyone. That is when each person knows a different piece, and the pieces must be put together there and then. Most choices are not like that.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE QUIZ TEAM',text:'A school quiz team needs everyone at the table. One knows sport, one knows music, one knows space. But choosing the team name? The captain can just do it.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time your group starts a long chat about a choice, ask: who could just decide this?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your class spends a whole lesson choosing who waters the plants each week. What does this idea say?',options:['The teacher could choose and tell the class','Everyone should vote, so that it is fair','The class should meet again next week to check'],answer:0,why:'One person could make this choice. Nobody needs to bring a different piece of knowledge to it.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'FORTY MINUTES ON A SUPPLIER',text:'Six people spend forty minutes choosing between two print suppliers. Everyone agrees the call sits with the office manager, who holds the budget and the contracts. She opens the meeting by asking what everyone thinks.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The most useful question about a meeting is whether one identifiable person could have made the call and told everyone. If so, the meeting is buying them cover. Sometimes worth it, but it should be a purchase, not a habit.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One seat pulled up, one sheet, one pen; the rest of the chairs stacked against the wall. Most calls need only the person who owns them.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'THREE REASONS, THREE REMEDIES',left:{title:'Why it came to the room',items:['They wanted cover','They wanted input','They did not know they could decide']},right:{title:'What fixes it instead',items:['Explicit delegation','Input gathered in writing, beforehand','A clear statement of who decides']},note:'None of the remedies is a meeting.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The ones that do need a room',text:'Some decisions genuinely require a group: the information is spread across people and has to be assembled live. The question\u2019s value is that it separates those from the much larger set that do not. Only the first kind justifies the room.'},{kind:'try',text:'For your next recurring meeting, write down the name of whoever could have decided it alone. If you cannot find one, it may be the rare kind that needs the room.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Ask who could have decided alone.','Match the reason to its remedy: delegation, written input, or stated authority.','Keep the room for decisions whose information is spread across people.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A team lead brings every hiring call to a weekly panel because she is unsure she may decide alone. Which remedy fits?',options:['State clearly that the hiring call is hers','Add more people to the panel for balance','Collect everyone\u2019s views in writing first','Keep the panel, but make it shorter'],answer:0,why:'Her reason is uncertainty about authority, and that is fixed by stating it. Written input would answer a different need.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'WHAT THE MEETING BUYS',word:'Cover',meaning:'Protection from blame gained by having others share a decision. It has real value when a call is risky or contested. The piece\u2019s point is that it has a price, paid by everyone in the room.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Ask who could have decided alone. Usually a name is available, and the reason it did not happen points to the remedy. Only decisions whose information must be assembled live justify the room.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the question is efficient',text:'It needs no audit and no data, and it applies to any recurring meeting. In most cases a name is available. The interesting part is why that person did not simply decide: they wanted cover, wanted input, or did not know they were empowered.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Applying it',steps:['Name who could have decided alone.','If nobody could, check that the information really is spread across people.','If someone could, find why they did not: cover, input or authority.','Apply the matching remedy instead of the meeting.']},{kind:'picture',caption:'Where the question usually ends: one chair at the table with the sheet and pen, and the rest stacked by the wall, unneeded.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why none of the remedies is a meeting',text:'Cover is supplied by explicit delegation: someone with authority says this one is yours. Input can be gathered asynchronously, in writing. Uncertainty about authority is fixed by stating it. Each meets the real need more cheaply than an hour of everyone\u2019s time.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The question can be used to strip out consultation that matters: people affected by a decision may deserve a voice even when one person could decide. A name being available does not mean that person has the knowledge. And delegation only gives cover if those above honour it when a call goes wrong.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which of your recurring meetings would fail this question? For one of them, which of the three reasons is at work?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'DIAGNOSE AND PRESCRIBE',prompt:'A product lead could set a feature\u2019s priority alone, but keeps calling a meeting because she wants engineering\u2019s view. Which remedy fits?',options:['Gather engineering\u2019s view in writing, then decide herself','Formally delegate the decision to a senior engineer','State in writing that she has the authority to decide','Keep the meeting, since wanting input always justifies one'],answer:0,why:'Her reason is input, which can be gathered asynchronously. Delegation answers a need for cover; stating authority answers uncertainty about who decides.'}]}},'the-meeting-that-was-a-decision_3':{pictureCaption:'A queue still pressing into a room that is already full. Being inside is what counts, whatever the room is doing.',bridge:'Idea 2 asked who should decide. This one asks why so many other people end up in the room.',basics:{title:'Why meetings keep getting bigger',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE PARTY LIST',text:'You plan a small party for five friends. Then you think: if I leave out Sam, he will feel bad. And Sam\u2019s best friend. And the two from your table. Now there are fourteen, and the game you planned won\u2019t work.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Being invited shows you matter. So invite lists grow, and nobody wants to leave. Soon the group is too big to do its job.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'People still squeezing into a room that is already full. Being inside matters to them more than what the room is doing.'},{kind:'explain',title:'It only goes one way',text:'Inviting someone feels kind. Taking someone off the list feels mean. So lists grow, and they almost never shrink.'},{kind:'explain',title:'A kind fix',text:'Keep the group small, but tell everyone else what was decided. Then nobody misses out on the news, and the small group can get on with the job.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you make a plan with friends, keep the planning group small. Then tell everyone the plan.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your club\u2019s planning group has grown to fifteen, and nothing gets decided. Which fix fits this idea?',options:['A small group plans, and everyone gets the news after','Ask the people who joined last to leave the group','Add more meetings so that everyone gets a turn'],answer:0,why:'Nobody is left out of the news, and the group is small enough to decide.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE INVITE LIST',prompt:'A project meeting started with five people. A year on, what size is it most likely to be?',options:['Smaller, as people drop off','About the same','Larger, and still growing'],answer:2,reveal:'Being in the room is read as mattering. So invitations grow and nobody says no. The cost never shows up as a line in any budget. So nothing pushes back.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Attendance is a status signal. So invite lists swell and rarely shrink. Meeting size grows unless something actively pushes back on it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it only goes one way',text:'Being in the room shows you matter. Being left out shows the opposite, and people read it correctly. So inviting someone is generous. Uninviting them is a demotion. The cost is spread across salaries. So it is never seen as a single figure.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'HOW BIG IS TOO BIG',low:'A handful: a real discussion',high:'Fourteen: an audience',note:'Somewhere along this line the meeting stops being able to do what it was for. A discussion with fourteen people is not a discussion.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A full room, and a queue still pressing through the door. Getting in is the point, whatever happens inside.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The fix that works',text:'Separate the signal from the attendance. Publish the notes to a wider group than the attendee list. People still get the information. The room shrinks to a size that can work, and nobody has to be removed.'},{kind:'try',text:'On your next invitation, say in writing who is optional. Promise the notes to everyone. That says outright that declining is safe.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A steering meeting has grown to sixteen people and has stopped deciding anything. Which change fits the idea best?',options:['Cut it to the deciders and send the notes to all sixteen','Remove the least senior people from the invitation','Split it into two separate meetings of eight each','Keep everyone, but limit each person to two minutes'],answer:0,why:'The notes keep everyone informed. So the room can shrink without anyone being demoted.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'WHY THE ROOM IS FULL',belief:'Meetings grow because more people genuinely need to be there.',reality:'Some do. But attendance also works as a status signal: being in the room reads as relevance, and being left out reads as the opposite. That alone is enough to make lists grow.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Because attendance signals status, inviting is generous and uninviting is a demotion. The asymmetry points one way, so lists inflate until the meeting can no longer do what it was for.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHY NOTHING PUSHES BACK',nodes:['Being invited signals relevance','Inviting costs nothing visible','Uninviting reads as demotion','The list only grows'],note:'The cost is spread across salaries rather than appearing as a line, so nothing in the system resists.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the reading is accurate',text:'People are not simply being vain. The piece says they read the signal correctly: being left out does indicate the opposite of relevance. That is why simply asking people to decline is a hard sell. It asks them to give up a true signal of standing.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Separating signal from seat',text:'The workable fix publishes the notes to a wider group than the attendee list. Inclusion in the information survives, so the signal survives, and the room returns to a size that can function. It is far easier than removing people.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A room already full and a queue still at the door. What they are queuing for is the signal, not the work.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Notes are not a perfect substitute. What gets written down is filtered, and reading is not the same as being present. Some status concerns are about influence, not information, and notes do not restore influence. Optional attendance only feels safe if nobody is quietly marked down for taking up the option.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A company\u2019s all-staff planning day has grown too large to plan anything. Which response applies the piece\u2019s mechanism?',options:['Plan with a small group and publish the full notes to all staff','Keep everyone, since attendance shows staff that they matter','Cut the least senior staff, since they decide the least','Run it twice as often, so each session has fewer topics'],answer:0,why:'It separates the signal from the seat: everyone stays included in the information while the room shrinks to a working size.'}]}},'the-meeting-that-was-a-decision_4':{pictureCaption:'A written proposal on the table, and hands marking objections straight onto it instead of talking across it.',bridge:'With fewer people in the room, the next question is what the room should actually be doing.',basics:{title:'Write the plan down first',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE MARKING A STORY',text:'Imagine your class writing a story from nothing, all shouting ideas at once. It is slow, and the loudest voices win. Now imagine your teacher hands out a story and asks what is wrong with it. Hands go up fast.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Before a group meets to decide, write the plan down and share it. Then the group only has to find what is wrong with it. That is faster.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Quiet people get a say',text:'Some people won\u2019t say *I don\u2019t agree* out loud. But they will write a note on the page. So a written plan lets more worries come out.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A plan written on paper, and hands marking problems right on it. Nobody has to shout to be heard.'},{kind:'explain',title:'A bonus',text:'Sometimes just writing the plan down solves the problem. You spot the answer while you write. Then you may not need to meet at all.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you plan something with friends, write the plan in a message first. Then ask: what is wrong with it?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your group must plan a class assembly. Which start fits this idea best?',options:['One person writes a plan, and the rest mark problems','Everyone meets and shares ideas out loud','The loudest person decides what happens'],answer:0,why:'Finding problems in a written plan is faster than building one from nothing, and quiet people can add their worries too.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO RUN THE HOUR',left:{title:'No proposal',items:['Options built live','Slow','Favours the most fluent speaker','Shaped by what was raised first']},right:{title:'Proposal sent first',items:['Options already exist','The room looks for faults','Objections written in the margin','Faster']},note:'Same people, same hour. The written proposal changes what the room is doing.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Sending out a decision in writing first turns a meeting from generation into critique. Critique is faster. And it brings out disagreement that a spoken round-table tends to smooth over.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why critique is easier',text:'Building options live is slow and favours whoever speaks most fluently. Finding what is wrong with options that already exist is a task groups are truly good at. And people will jot down an objection they would never voice across a table.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A written proposal with hands marking objections straight onto it. The disagreement goes on the page, not round the table.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The hidden benefit',text:'Writing the proposal frequently resolves the question. A decision that survives being written down with its reasoning often needs no meeting at all. The ones that do not survive were worth catching before the room met.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before your next decision meeting, send the proposal and your recommendation. Ask for objections, not for thoughts.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You are calling a meeting to choose a new booking system. What should you send in advance?',options:['A written proposal with a recommendation, asking for objections','An open agenda inviting everyone\u2019s thoughts on booking systems','A list of every available system, with no recommendation','Nothing, so that the discussion stays fresh and unbiased'],answer:0,why:'A proposal with a recommendation gives the room something to critique. Asking for objections rather than thoughts keeps it from sliding back into building options live.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'WHERE DISSENT SHOWS',prompt:'A team must settle a contested question. In which format is a junior member\u2019s objection most likely to be raised?',options:['A live round-table discussion','Comments on a written proposal sent beforehand','Both about the same'],answer:1,reveal:'The piece\u2019s claim is that people will annotate an objection they would not voice. A spoken round-table tends to smooth disagreement over.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A written proposal sent first changes what the room is doing: from constructing options to finding what is wrong with them. Groups are genuinely good at critique, and generating live is slow and skewed.'},{kind:'term',word:'Generation and critique',meaning:'Generation is producing options from nothing; critique is testing options that already exist. A meeting with no written proposal is forced to generate, live, which is the slower and more distorted of the two tasks.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What live generation gets wrong',text:'Constructing options in the room is slow. It favours whoever speaks most fluently, and the discussion is shaped by the order in which things happened to be raised. None of that has anything to do with which option is best.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Objections marked straight onto the page: crosses, queries, arrows. Disagreement that might never be said aloud is now on the record.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Writing as a filter',text:'Writing the proposal often resolves the question. A decision that survives being set down with its reasoning often needs no meeting; one that does not was worth catching before the room assembled. Either way, the writing did work the meeting would have done worse.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Whoever writes the proposal frames the options, and critique tends to stay inside that frame, so a better option nobody wrote down may never appear. A recommendation can anchor the room. Writing favours fluent writers, as talk favours fluent speakers. And some problems are genuinely open, where generating together is the point.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which practice undercuts what the written proposal is for?',options:['Sending it an hour before, then asking for general thoughts in the room','Sending it two days before, with a recommendation and a request for objections','Inviting comments directly in the margins of the document','Cancelling the meeting when nobody objects in writing'],answer:0,why:'Asking for thoughts in the room turns it back into live generation, and an hour leaves too little time to annotate. The point is to collect objections on the page.'}]}},'the-meeting-that-was-a-decision_5':{pictureCaption:'The chairs are stacked and the meeting is off. In the doorway, two people settle the one thing that actually needed settling.',bridge:'The first four ideas give reasons and fixes. This one is a test you can run without winning an argument first.',basics:{title:'Stop it for a month and see',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE FRIDAY MEETING',prompt:'Your club has met every Friday lunch for a year. Nobody is sure it helps. You stop it for a month. What happens?',options:['Everything falls apart','Nothing at all changes','A few things are missed, and get sorted another way'],answer:2,reveal:'That is what usually happens. The things people really needed pop back up. They often come back smaller, so they are easier to sort out.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Not sure a meeting is useful? Stop it for a month. What people really need will come back. What doesn\u2019t come back wasn\u2019t being used.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The chairs are stacked: no meeting today. But two people still sort out the one thing that really needed it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why not just ask?',text:'Ask people what a meeting is for, and they will struggle to say. A lot of what it does is small and unplanned. When it stops, people notice exactly what is missing.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it is safe',text:'If the meeting turns out to be needed, you just start it again. Trying costs very little. Arguing about it can take ages.'},{kind:'try',text:'Is there a club meeting or a routine nobody is sure about? Suggest a month off, and write down what people miss.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your class stops its weekly news circle for a month. Two things come up that people miss. What does the test tell you?',options:['Keep just those two things, in a smaller way','Bring the whole circle back straight away','Forget the two things, since the circle has gone'],answer:0,why:'The test showed what was really needed. Those two things can be kept without the whole circle.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'A MONTH WITHOUT MONDAYS',text:'Say a team suspends its hour-long Monday status meeting for a month. In week one, nobody knows the release date, and a two-line weekly post fixes it. In week two, a designer misses a check with engineering and books fifteen minutes. Nothing else comes up.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Cancelling a recurring meeting for a month is the cheapest test of whether it was doing anything. What people needed reappears, usually smaller and better targeted. What does not reappear was not being used.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why you cannot just ask',text:'A meeting\u2019s functions are mostly undocumented. Much of what it produces is incidental: coordination in the margins, visibility, the thing someone mentions at the end. Nobody can list it, so arguing about its value goes nowhere.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE TEST',nodes:['Suspend the meeting for a month','Functions show up by their absence','Name each specific gap','Serve each one more cheaply'],note:'The absences are specific and nameable in a way the functions never were.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Chairs stacked by the door, and two people settling the one thing that needed settling, standing up, in a minute.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it beats arguing',text:'The risk is low because the meeting can be reinstated. Running the test costs a month; debating can take far longer and settle nothing. That asymmetry is what makes the test worth running.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one suspect recurring meeting. Cancel it for a month, tell people why, and keep a list of what surfaces.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'In a month without the weekly planning meeting, one real need surfaces: a quick check on deadlines. What does the idea suggest?',options:['Serve that need with something smaller, and leave the meeting off','Bring the full meeting back, since it was clearly needed','Ignore the need, since the trial month is already over','Extend the trial for a year before deciding anything'],answer:0,why:'The test did its job: it named a specific need. That need can be met more cheaply than the meeting that used to carry it.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'DAY THIRTY, NO MEETING',text:'The Thursday review has been off for a month. Nobody has missed the review as such. But on day nine someone asked who was tracking supplier dates, and on day twenty a manager asked how she would now hear about slips. Two gaps, both specific.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Suspending a recurring meeting is the cheapest available test of whether it was doing anything. Its functions are mostly undocumented, so they become visible only by their absence, and then they can be named.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the functions are invisible',text:'Much of what a meeting produces is incidental: coordination in the margins, visibility, the thing someone mentions at the end. Nobody can list it in advance. Ask what the meeting is for and you get its stated purpose, not its working ones.'},{kind:'keyline',text:'The absences are specific and nameable in a way the functions never were.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'DEBATE OR TEST',left:{title:'Arguing about the meeting',items:['Rests on guesses about its value','Has no end point','Costs goodwill either way']},right:{title:'Suspending it for a month',items:['Shows what is actually missed','Ends after a month','Fully reversible']},note:'The risk is low because the meeting can be reinstated. That asymmetry is what makes testing better than debating.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The chairs are stacked. What remains is two people settling a real question quickly, without the room.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Some functions surface slowly, or only in a crisis: a meeting kept for the rare emergency can look useless for a month. Gaps can go unnoticed by those who feel them most, such as newer staff. And what is missed may be trust built in the room, which no smaller replacement serves. A month is a test, not a proof.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'READ THE RESULT',prompt:'After a month without the weekly sync, nothing at all has surfaced. Which conclusion is best supported?',options:['Little was being used, though rare needs may not show yet','It was essential, and people kept quiet about it out of loyalty','The test failed, since a good test always surfaces something','It should come back, since stopping it was always too risky'],answer:0,why:'What does not reappear was probably not being used. The honest caveat is that rare or slow functions may need longer to show.'}]}}}},3020,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'the-money-conversation_1':{pictureCaption:'Built for years by eye, the frame has gone crooked. The tape measure that would have kept it true lies beside it, never unrolled.',basics:{title:'Say the number, not the word',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'A PRESENT FOR MUM',text:'You and your brother want to buy Mum a present. You ask how much he has. He says *loads*. You say *some*. You pick a nice scarf. At the shop, *loads* turns out to be \xa33.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Words like *loads* and *not much* feel safe. But they hide the real number, so people guess. Saying the number feels odd for a moment. Then it helps.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'The word',items:['Loads','Not much','I\u2019m fine']},right:{title:'The number',items:['\xa33','\xa320, saved for a bike','\xa30 until Friday']},note:'The right side is what the other person needed to know.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Someone built this by eye and never measured. Now it is bent. The tape measure sat right there the whole time.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why do we use the words?',text:'A number can feel a bit scary to say. A word feels softer. And on any one day, there is no big reason to stop. So the guessing just goes on, until a day when it costs you.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What happens when you say it',text:'You might think the other person will judge you. Often they don\u2019t. Often they are glad. They were guessing too, and now they can stop.'},{kind:'try',text:'This week, swap one money word for the real number. Not *some*. Say *\xa36*. Then see what the other person does.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend asks if you can go to the cinema on Saturday. You have \xa35. A ticket is \xa38. Which answer uses this idea?',options:['I\u2019ve got \xa35, so I\u2019m \xa33 short','I\u2019m a bit short right now','Yes, I\u2019ll sort it out somehow'],answer:0,why:'Only the answer with \xa35 in it gives the number. Now your friend knows the real problem, and you can fix it together.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'YEARS OF \u201cFINE\u201d',text:'Asked about money, you have said *fine* for years, and so has your partner. They have quietly assumed your savings would cover a gap if one of you lost a job. You assumed the same about theirs. Neither of you ever checked.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Vague money words feel safer than numbers, but they are not neutral. They let two people hold assumptions that do not match for years, and the mismatch shows up at the worst moment.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE COST BUILDS',nodes:['Words instead of numbers','Each person fills the gap with a guess','Plans quietly rest on the guesses','A crisis tests every guess at once'],note:'A redundancy, an illness or a separation is when the guesses get checked, all on the same day.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it lasts so long',text:'Vagueness is comfortable for both people in every single moment. On any given day there is no reason to end it today. So it never ends by itself, and a resolution to be more open rarely changes that. It needs a set occasion.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Years of building by eye have bent the frame out of true. The tape measure lies beside it, still coiled.'},{kind:'keyline',text:'A number is uncomfortable for a moment. What it often meets is not judgement but relief.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one vague word you use about money, such as *tight* or *comfortable*. This week, say the number it stands for to the person it affects.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Your partner asks whether you can afford a shared holiday this year. Which reply applies the idea?',options:['We have about \xa31,200 spare after bills this year','Things are a bit tight, but we\u2019ll probably manage','We\u2019re comfortable, so there\u2019s no need to worry','Let\u2019s not spoil a nice evening talking about money'],answer:0,why:'Only the figure lets your partner plan with what is real. *Tight* and *comfortable* leave them guessing, which is how mismatched plans start.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE ESSAY\u2019S CLAIM',text:'This short essay starts from an observation: most households, and most working relationships, run on words like *comfortable*, *tight* and *fine*. Its argument is that this default is not neutral. It has a cost, and the cost is deferred.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Vagueness lets incompatible assumptions survive for years, and lets plans rest on the other person\u2019s money behaving in ways nobody confirmed. The bill comes when every assumption is tested at once.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'How vagueness feels',items:['Comfortable in every moment','Polite','Avoids a small awkwardness']},right:{title:'What vagueness does',items:['Hides assumptions that do not match','Lets plans rest on unchecked money','Saves the test for a crisis']}},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the timing is the worst',text:'The essay names redundancy, illness and separation. These are the moments when each person most needs to know what the other can cover, and they often come without warning. Assumptions that could have been compared calmly are tested together, under pressure, and found not to match.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it survives',text:'Each moment of vagueness is comfortable for both people, so there is never a reason to end it today. That is why the essay prescribes a scheduled occasion rather than a resolution to be more open. A resolution needs someone to choose the moment, and no moment ever looks right.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Built for years by eye, the frame has warped. The tape measure, which would have settled every question, lies coiled and unused.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Numbers are not always safe to share. Where one person controls or punishes the other, a figure can be used as a weapon, and openness is not the first step. At work, disclosing pay can carry real risks. And a number settles only what it measures: two people can agree the figure and still disagree about what it means.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Vague money words are comfortable, and not neutral.','They let mismatched assumptions survive until a crisis tests them.','Ending them takes a set occasion, not a resolution.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'PICK THE FIX THAT LASTS',prompt:'A couple realise they have been vague about money for years. Which plan best fits the essay\u2019s argument?',options:['Book a recurring monthly slot to share the actual figures','Promise each other to be more open from now on','Wait for the next big expense and talk it through then','Keep separate budgets and trust that they add up'],answer:0,why:'A promise still needs someone to pick a day, and no day ever seems right. A recurring slot supplies the reason; the others leave it to chance or to a crisis.'}]}},'the-money-conversation_2':{pictureCaption:'Two marks on a wall: where this person really stands, and where everyone assumes. The hand covers neither mark. It covers the gap.',bridge:'Idea 1 said vague words hide the number. This one asks why we hide it: usually the number is not the problem.',basics:{title:'It\u2019s the gap that feels bad',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TWO FRIENDS',prompt:'Sam gets \xa32 a week. Jo gets \xa310, but she lets her friends think she gets \xa320. Who feels worse when pocket money comes up?',options:['Sam, who gets the least','Jo, who gets five times as much'],answer:1,reveal:'Sam has nothing to hide. Jo has a gap to hide: the \xa310 between what she gets and what people think. That gap is what makes her squirm.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The bad feeling about money is usually not about how much you have. It is about the gap between what you have and what people think you have.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two lines on a wall. One is where this person really is. One is where people think they are. The hand is hiding the gap.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it gets harder',text:'Each time you go along with the bigger story, it grows. You buy the snack you can\u2019t afford. You say you have the game when you don\u2019t. Soon the secret is not the money. It is all the pretending.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE STANDING ON TIPTOE',text:'Standing on tiptoe to look taller is easy at first. But the longer you stay up there, the more it hurts. And the harder it is to drop down without people seeing.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where did *should* come from?',text:'Ask where your idea of what you *should* have came from. Often it is three kids with the newest phones. That is not *everyone*. Just saying so out loud makes the gap feel smaller.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Mia feels bad that her trainers are old. She says, *everyone has new ones.* What would help her most?',options:['Asking who *everyone* really is','Telling her that her trainers look fine','Helping her save up for new ones fast'],answer:0,why:'Saying her trainers are fine is about the trainers. Her bad feeling is about the gap, and *everyone* is usually just a few people.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'People who are embarrassed about money are embarrassed because they earn too little.',reality:'The embarrassment is rarely about the figure. It is about the distance between what you earn and what people assume you earn, or what you think you should.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Money shame attaches to the gap between where you are and where you believe you should be, not to the amount. That is why it does not track income.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'TWO SALARIES',text:'Picture two people. One earns modestly and has never hidden it. The other earns well, but less than friends assume, and has spent years keeping up the restaurants and holidays to match. The second carries far more shame, because the gap is larger.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why reassurance misses',text:'Telling someone their salary is perfectly decent addresses the amount, and the amount was never the subject. What helps is naming the comparison: what standard you are measuring against, and where it came from.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Where this person stands, and where everyone assumes. The hand is not hiding either mark. It is covering the distance between them.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Making the comparison explicit',steps:['Name the figure you feel you should be at.','Ask where that figure came from: parents, friends, a feed.','Ask how typical that source really is.','Say the standard aloud, to yourself or someone close.'],note:'A standard that was inherited, or drawn from a few visible people, loses much of its authority once it is said out loud.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time money talk makes you tense, ask: is this about the amount, or about the gap? Only one of them is about money.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A friend on a good salary dreads telling her partner what she earns. What is most likely to help?',options:['Helping her name the standard she is measuring herself against','Telling her that her salary is well above the average','Suggesting she wait until she earns a little more','Agreeing that the figure is nobody\u2019s business but hers'],answer:0,why:'Her shame is not about the amount, so praise for the amount misses. Naming the standard, and where it came from, is what shrinks it.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHAT THE SHAME IS ABOUT',text:'The essay makes a precise claim about the emotion in money conversations. It attaches not to the absolute figure but to the gap between where someone is and where they believe they should be. One consequence follows, and the essay draws it: the emotion does not track income.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Money shame measures distance from a standard, not the amount itself. So reassurance about the amount cannot touch it. Only examining the standard can.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'WHERE THE SHAME SITS',low:'Earnings match the standard you apply',high:'Earnings far below the standard you apply',note:'Income is not on this line. A high earner with a higher standard can sit further right than a low earner with a modest one.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The performance',text:'Once a gap exists, a performance grows up around it: the holidays, the rounds of drinks, the silences that let people keep assuming. The secret stops being the salary and becomes the performance. Every month it runs, dropping it means admitting more.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two marks, close together: where this person stands, and where everyone assumes. The hand covers neither. It covers the gap.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why naming the standard works',text:'The essay\u2019s remedy is to make the comparison explicit: say what standard is being applied and where it came from. Often it is inherited, or drawn from a visibly unrepresentative sample, such as the few friends whose spending you can see. Said aloud, it loses more authority than any argument about the figure could take from it.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Sometimes the amount really is the problem. If the rent cannot be paid, examining your standards does not pay it, and treating real hardship as a matter of perception would be cruel. The analysis fits shame about status better than fear about survival. And sometimes the comparison is fair, and the gap is information worth acting on.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'DIAGNOSE IT',prompt:'A well-paid friend knows she earns plenty, yet feels sick whenever anyone asks what she makes. Which response follows the essay?',options:['Ask what standard she measures herself against, and where it came from','Show her the figures proving she earns far more than most people','Tell her the feeling will fade once she earns a little more','Advise her to keep the figure private, since it clearly hurts'],answer:0,why:'She already knows the amount is ample, so the shame cannot be about it. The gap is against some standard, and naming that standard is what weakens it.'}]}},'the-money-conversation_3':{pictureCaption:'An ordinary evening: no bill on the table, no problem anywhere. Two chairs drawn up anyway. This is the evening the talk should happen.',bridge:'Idea 1 said vagueness never ends by itself and needs a set occasion. This one says when that occasion should be.',basics:{title:'Talk about money on a calm day',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'ASKING FOR MORE POCKET MONEY',prompt:'You want to ask your parents for more pocket money. When will it go best?',options:['Right after you break something','On a calm Sunday when nothing is wrong','When they are paying a big bill'],answer:1,reveal:'After you break something, everyone is cross, and the talk is about the broken thing. On a calm day, nobody is cross. You can just share ideas.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Money talks go best when nothing is wrong. So don\u2019t wait for a problem. Pick a calm day ahead of time, and stick to it.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'A problem starts it',items:['Someone is already upset','Something is already lost','It can turn into a fight']},right:{title:'Nothing is wrong',items:['Nobody is upset','Nothing is lost yet','You can just share ideas']}},{kind:'picture',caption:'A kitchen table on a normal night. No bills on it. No problem. Just two chairs, ready for a calm talk.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The catch',text:'On a calm day, there is never a reason to bring money up. So nobody does. The trick is to fix a day in advance, like the first Sunday of the month. Then the date is the reason.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A FIRE DRILL',text:'Schools practise fire drills on normal days, not during a fire. With no smoke and no panic, everyone can learn calmly what to do.'},{kind:'try',text:'Ask a grown-up at home to pick one calm day a month to talk about your pocket money and saving. Write it on the calendar.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your family has a money chat on the first Sunday of each month. This month nothing is wrong. What should happen?',options:['Have the chat anyway, since calm is best','Skip it, since there is nothing to fix','Wait until someone spends too much'],answer:0,why:'A calm month is the best month for it. Nobody is upset, so it is easy to share ideas.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'SAME TOPIC, TWO DAYS',left:{title:'Triggered by a bill',items:['Someone is already defensive','Something is already lost','The aim is damage control','A negotiation']},right:{title:'Scheduled for no reason',items:['Nobody has anything to defend','Nothing is lost yet','The aim is to explore','Just information']},note:'Same two people, same money. The day decides what the talk can be.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A money conversation held during a money problem is a negotiation. Held in a quiet week, it is just information. So hold it when nothing is wrong.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE STATEMENT ARRIVES',text:'The card statement comes in higher than either of you expected. The talk that follows opens with *what did you spend that on?* and never gets near what you both want the money for.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it doesn\u2019t happen',text:'Everyone agrees the calm version is better. It still does not happen, because raising money needs a reason and an ordinary week supplies none. So the first reason that arrives is a bill.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'An ordinary evening. Nothing on the table, no bill, no problem. Two chairs drawn up anyway.'},{kind:'steps',title:'A fix that needs no willpower',steps:['Pick a recurring date, such as the first Sunday of the month.','Put it in both calendars, set to repeat.','Meet even when nothing is wrong. Especially then.','Let the date supply the reason, so nobody has to raise it.']},{kind:'try',text:'Put a money conversation in the diary for a week when nothing is due, and set it to repeat.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Your monthly money check-in falls in a week with no bills and no worries. What does the idea suggest?',options:['Hold it: a quiet week is when it works best','Cancel it, since there is nothing to discuss','Move it to the week the rent is due','Hold it only if one of you feels worried'],answer:0,why:'The quiet week is the point. Disagreement costs nothing then, and skipping it brings back the step where it always fails: someone deciding to raise it.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'WHAT STOPS IT',prompt:'Two people agree they should talk about money more. What usually stops them?',options:['Fear of what they will learn','No ordinary week ever gives a reason to start','Not having the right tools or spreadsheets'],answer:1,reveal:'Fear may play a part, but the essay puts the failure somewhere more ordinary. In a normal week there is no reason to raise it, so it is never raised, until a bill provides one.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The conversation goes best when nothing is wrong, which is exactly when it never happens. The fix is procedural, not motivational: let a recurring date supply the reason.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Two different conversations',text:'A discussion prompted by a problem begins with someone defensive and something already lost, and its purpose is damage control. A discussion in an ordinary week can be exploratory, and disagreement in it costs nothing. The essay calls timing the single most actionable point.'},{kind:'term',word:'Procedural, not motivational',meaning:'A fix that changes the arrangement rather than anyone\u2019s resolve. Instead of trying harder to remember, you set things up so the action no longer depends on someone deciding to do it.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHERE IT ALWAYS FAILS',nodes:['Both agree it matters','Someone must decide to raise it','No ordinary week gives a reason','A bill gives the reason instead'],note:'A recurring date removes the second step. Nobody has to decide, so the step where it always failed is gone.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'No bill, no problem, nothing on the table at all. The calm is the point: this is the evening the talk should happen.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Problems do not wait for the calendar. When a bill arrives it has to be dealt with then, and a slot next month is no excuse to postpone it. A recurring date can also harden into a ritual where the real subjects are skipped. And if one person dreads every meeting, the schedule will not fix that; something else needs attention.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'Someone objects that a scheduled money talk is cold and artificial, and that it should come up naturally. What is the best reply?',options:['Left to come up naturally, it usually comes up with a bill','A schedule guarantees the talk will be warm and productive','Money is purely practical, so warmth does not matter here','Natural is better, so schedule it only as a last resort'],answer:0,why:'Waiting for a natural moment means waiting for a reason, and the first reason is usually a problem. The schedule is artificial on purpose: it puts the talk in a week when it is still information.'}]}},'the-money-conversation_4':{pictureCaption:'One jar of savings between a locked box and a packed rucksack. Keeping it safe and spending it on a trip are both sensible.',bridge:'Once you are talking in a calm week (idea 3), you may find you disagree. This is about why both of you can be right.',basics:{title:'Same money, different jobs',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'BIRTHDAY MONEY',picture:!0,text:'You and your sister share \xa340 of birthday money. She wants to keep it in the jar. You want to spend it on a day out. You argue about it every week. Neither of you will budge.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What is each of you after?',text:'Ask your sister why she wants to keep it. Maybe she likes knowing there is money there if something breaks. That is feeling safe. Now ask yourself why you want to spend it. Maybe you want fun now. That is feeling free.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Two people can both be right and still disagree about money. Often they want the money to do different jobs. So ask what the money is *for*.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Keep it',items:['Money in the jar feels safe','Ready if something breaks','Worried about later']},right:{title:'Spend it',items:['Money is for doing things','Ready for a fun day out','Worried about missing out']},note:'Neither side is silly. They just want different things from the same \xa340.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A FOOTBALL TEAM',text:'A good team needs a strong defence and quick attackers. A defender and a striker might argue about how to play. Neither is wrong. They just have different jobs.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you argue about money, ask: *what do you want this money to do?* Then answer it yourself, too.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Two friends share a pot of money for their club. One wants to save it. One wants a new ball now. What is the best first step?',options:['Ask what each wants the money to do','Vote, and the loser has to accept it','Decide that saving is always right'],answer:0,why:'They might both be right. Once each says what the money is for, it is easier to find a plan. Maybe a bit of both.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE SAME ROW, EVERY MONTH',text:'A couple argue about savings for years. She wants a bigger cushion before they spend anything. He wants to use the money while it can still change their lives. Every row is about a number: how much to put away this month.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Arguments about amounts are rarely about amounts. Often one person is solving for security and the other for freedom. Neither is wrong; they have never named what the money is for.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHY THE ROW REPEATS',nodes:['Each argues for an amount','The amount is not the real issue','Neither feels understood','The same row next month'],loop:!0,note:'Arguing the figure keeps the loop turning, because the figure comes after the goal.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The question that usually ends it',text:'Ask what this money is protecting against. She might say: losing a job with nothing in reserve. He might say: years going by with nothing done. Both answers are reasonable, and both can now be planned for.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Solving for security',items:['Money as a cushion','Fears the sudden shock','Calmer when saving']},right:{title:'Solving for freedom',items:['Money as options','Fears the wasted years','Calmer when using it']},note:'Both are sound. They are answers to different questions.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The same jar of savings, two sound places for it. The locked box guards against the sudden shock; the packed bag guards against the wasted years.'},{kind:'keyline',text:'A spreadsheet answers *how much*. The argument was about *what for*.'},{kind:'try',text:'In a money disagreement that keeps coming back, ask each other what this money is protecting against. Write both answers down before anyone names a figure.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Two business partners have argued for months over whether to keep profits or reinvest them. What would the idea suggest first?',options:['Ask what each wants the money to protect against','Build a spreadsheet that shows the right amount','Split the difference and stop discussing it','Let the partner with the bigger stake decide'],answer:0,why:'A spreadsheet answers how much, but the dispute is about what for. Once each goal is named, a plan can serve both.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'When a money argument lasts for years, one person must be getting the numbers wrong.',reality:'Often both are reasoning soundly from different goals. The essay\u2019s example is a couple arguing about savings: one solving for security, the other for freedom. Neither position is wrong.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A long money disagreement is often two sound positions solving for different things. Naming what the money is for is most of the value, and usually ends the argument faster than any spreadsheet.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the amount is the wrong ground',text:'Each figure someone proposes is the output of a goal they have not stated. Arguing the output leaves the goal hidden, so each side hears the other as careless or anxious rather than as solving a different problem. The reframe moves the argument to the goals, where it can be settled.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE REFRAME AT WORK',text:'Same couple, new question: what is this money protecting against? One says a sudden loss of income. The other says a life that never got lived. Now there are two goals on the table, and a plan can give each a share: a fixed cushion first, then money set aside to be spent.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'TWO SOUND ENDS',low:'Every pound kept as a cushion',high:'Every pound used for options now',note:'Neither end is wrong. The real question is where on the line this couple wants to be, and why.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The jar sits between a locked box and a packed rucksack. Both are sensible destinations. The question is not which is right, but what this money is for.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Naming the goals does not remove the trade-off: a pound saved is still a pound not spent. And not every position is sound. *Freedom* can be a label for spending that cannot be afforded, and *security* a label for controlling the other person\u2019s choices. The reframe settles arguments between two reasonable goals. It does not make every goal reasonable.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of a money disagreement you have had more than once. What was each side protecting against?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'Two siblings who inherited a house argue about whether to sell it or keep it. Which step reflects the idea most closely?',options:['Ask what each of them wants the house to do for them','Get a valuation to settle what the house is worth','Let the sibling with the stronger argument decide','Sell it, since money is easier to share than a house'],answer:0,why:'A valuation answers how much. The dispute is likely about what for, such as income, security or memory, and naming that is where agreement starts.'}]}},'the-money-conversation_5':{pictureCaption:'A brand-new jar, and the lid will not budge. The jars behind it stand open, lids already off. Only the first opening is this hard.',bridge:'Idea 3 set the date for the first conversation. This one is about what to do when that first one goes badly.',basics:{title:'The first time is the hardest',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'AFTER ONE TRY',belief:'If something felt awkward the first time, it will always feel awkward.',reality:'With a money talk, the first time is nearly always the worst. The second time is often fine. By the fourth, it feels normal.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE FIRST MONEY CHAT',text:'You sit down with your dad to talk about your pocket money. Nobody knows what to say. You both go quiet. It feels weird. You never want to do it again.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The first money talk is the hardest one. After that it gets easier, fast. So don\u2019t judge it after one try. Judge it after three.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The new jar is the hard one. See the others? Their lids came off easily. The first money talk is like the new jar.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why is the first one so hard?',text:'Everything is new at once. You don\u2019t know what the other person thinks. You don\u2019t have the words for it yet. By the second time, you do. That is why it gets easier.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A NEW SCHOOL',text:'Your first day at a new school is the hardest. You don\u2019t know where anything is, or who anyone is. By the second week, it is just school.'},{kind:'steps',title:'The three-try rule',steps:['Expect the first talk to be clumsy.','Have the second one soon.','After the third, decide if it works.']},{kind:'question',prompt:'Leo had a money talk with his mum. It was awkward, so he says he will never try again. What does this idea say?',options:['Try again soon, since the first is the worst','He is right, since awkward means it won\u2019t work','Wait a whole year, then try again'],answer:0,why:'The first talk is nearly always the hardest. Leo did the hard part, then stopped just before the easy part.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'AFTER THE FIRST ATTEMPT',prompt:'Your first proper money conversation with your partner was stiff and clumsy. What does that tell you about the fifth?',options:['It will probably be just as awkward','Very little: the first is a poor guide','That the subject is best left alone'],answer:1,reveal:'The first is worst because everything is unsaid at once. The second is usually easier, and by the fourth it is routine. The first tells you about starting, not about the practice.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The difficulty is almost all in the first conversation. The cost of starting is paid once and the benefit recurs, so stopping after one awkward attempt means paying the price and leaving before the return.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the first is the worst',text:'Everything is unsaid at once. Neither of you knows the other\u2019s position. And you have not yet found shared words: what counts as *spare*, what *saving* means to each of you. The second conversation starts with whatever the first one settled.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW IT USUALLY GOES',nodes:['First: clumsy, sometimes goes badly','Second: usually easier','Fourth: routine'],note:'The worst one comes first, which is exactly why it is so tempting to stop there.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The first turn of a new lid takes real force. The jars behind it have been opened before, and their lids come off in a moment.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Stop after one',items:['Pay the full cost of starting','Conclude it does not work','Never reach the easy ones']},right:{title:'Judge after three',items:['Pay the cost once','See what the practice is really like','Keep the benefit each time']}},{kind:'try',text:'If you have had one awkward money conversation, book the second soon, before the memory of the first hardens into a rule.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A friend and her partner tried a money check-in once. It was tense, so they have decided money talks are not for them. What is the best advice?',options:['Have the second one soon, then judge after three','Accept it: tension shows the subject is off-limits','Wait until both of you feel ready to try again','Only talk about money when a problem comes up'],answer:0,why:'Tension in the first is expected, not evidence. The cost has already been paid; stopping now means leaving before the return.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'ONE TRY, ONE VERDICT',text:'Two people finally sit down to talk about money. It is clumsy. One gets defensive, the other goes quiet, and afterwards they agree it did not work. They do not try again. The experiment ran once, and its result was recorded as final.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The first money conversation is the worst one, and knowing that in advance changes whether people persist. The cost of starting is paid once; the benefit recurs.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the first is worst',text:'The essay gives three reasons. Everything is unsaid at once. Neither person knows the other\u2019s position. And the vocabulary has not been established. So the first is frequently clumsy and occasionally goes badly. None of those conditions carries into the second conversation in the same form.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHAT THE FIRST ONE MEASURES',left:{title:'The first conversation',items:['The cost of starting','Everything unsaid at once','No shared words yet']},right:{title:'The practice',items:['Usually easier the second time','Routine by the fourth','A benefit every time']},note:'Judging the practice by the first is measuring the wrong thing.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A sealed lid resists once. Judging the jar by that first struggle would be judging the practice by the cost of starting.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The usual mistake',text:'Most people, the essay says, run the experiment once, conclude it does not work, and stop. They read the awkwardness as evidence that the subject cannot be discussed. The essay\u2019s rule is to expect the first to be awkward and to judge only after three.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Awkwardness and harm are different. If the first conversation brought contempt, fear or threats, the problem is not inexperience, and persistence is not the answer. *Judge after three* is also a rule of thumb, not a promise; some couples find it stays hard and need outside help. The claim is that clumsiness is expected, not that every difficulty fades.'},{kind:'recap',points:['The first conversation is the worst, and predictably so.','Its cost is paid once; the benefit recurs.','Judge after three, and tell clumsiness apart from harm.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'CLUMSY, OR SOMETHING ELSE?',prompt:'After a first money talk, which report is a reason to stop and rethink, rather than simply try again?',options:['One partner mocked the other\u2019s figures and threatened to leave','Both kept losing track of what they each meant by spare money','It ran long and ended without any decision being made','Each felt embarrassed saying their actual numbers aloud'],answer:0,why:'Lost words, no decision and embarrassment are the clumsiness the essay predicts. Mockery and threats are not inexperience, and repeating the talk will not fix them.'}]}}}},3021,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'the-number-you-say-first_1':{pictureCaption:'The first marker sits near one end. The second could have stopped anywhere along the rule, and it stopped right beside the first.',basics:{title:'The first number pulls',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'SELLING YOUR OLD BIKE',prompt:'You are selling your old bike to a neighbour. Which start usually gets you a better price?',options:['You say a fair price first','You wait for them to say a price','It makes no difference'],answer:0,reveal:'The first number said pulls the final number towards it. If they start low, you end up bargaining up from low.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The first number someone says pulls the final number towards it. People start from it and move only a little. So if you are asked for a number, give one.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it pulls',text:'To agree a price, people start somewhere and then move. They usually stop moving too soon. So wherever the talk started, the end tends to stay near it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The first marker is near one end. The second could stop anywhere along the ruler. It stops right next to the first.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE GUESSING SWEETS IN A JAR',text:'Someone guesses how many sweets are in a jar: *about 500?* Now everyone else\u2019s guesses tend to creep towards 500. They do it even if the first guess was wild.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Saying a number first is a sneaky trick.',reality:'It isn\u2019t. It works even on people who know all about it. It is just how people look for a deal. Saying a fair number first is not cheating.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time someone asks what you want for something, have a fair number ready, and say it.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your aunt asks what you would charge to wash her car. What does this idea suggest?',options:['Say a fair price, since the first number pulls','Say *whatever you think*, to be polite','Wait and let her pick a number first'],answer:0,why:'If you don\u2019t say a number, she will, and the final price will be pulled towards hers.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE QUESTION YOU DREAD',text:'Ten minutes into the final interview, the recruiter asks what salary you are looking for. You say you would rather hear what they had in mind. It feels safe. You have just handed them the first number.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The first credible number stated pulls the final one towards it. It is one of the more robust findings in judgement research, and it is not a trick. If you are asked for a number, give one.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it works',text:'People find a settlement by starting from a number and adjusting, and they under-adjust. So the first number shapes the range everything else is negotiated within. It is an artefact of how the search works, not a manipulation.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The first number goes down near one end of the rule. The second, with the whole length to choose from, stops just beside it.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Anchoring only works on people who do not know about it.',reality:'It operates on people who know about it, experts included, and awareness does not much reduce it. It holds even when the first number is obviously arbitrary.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHO SAYS IT FIRST',left:{title:'You decline to answer',items:['Their number opens','The range forms around it','You adjust up from there']},right:{title:'You give a number',items:['Your number opens','The range forms around it','They adjust from there']},note:'Whether a number gets stated, and by whom, matters more than how it is delivered.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before your next pay conversation, decide the number you will give if asked, and say it out loud once so it is not new on the day.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Asked for her expected salary, a candidate says she is flexible and would like to hear their range first. What does the idea suggest?',options:['She has handed the anchor to the employer','She has kept her options open at no cost','She is safe, since anchoring needs a naive target','She has made a stronger case by staying vague'],answer:0,why:'Declining to answer lets the other side state the first number, and the settlement will form around theirs.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'A WHEEL OF FORTUNE',text:'In classic demonstrations, people watched a wheel of fortune being spun to a number, then made an unrelated estimate. Their estimates shifted toward the number on the wheel. The number was obviously arbitrary, and it still moved them.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Anchoring is one of the more robust findings in judgement research. The first number pulls the final one toward it because people adjust from a starting point and under-adjust, and knowing this does not much reduce it.'},{kind:'term',word:'Anchor',meaning:'The first number in play, from which later estimates or offers are adjusted. In a negotiation, the first credible number shapes the range in which a settlement is searched for.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW A SETTLEMENT IS FOUND',nodes:['A first number is stated','Both sides adjust from it','Adjustment stops too soon','The deal lands near the anchor'],note:'Nobody is fooled at any step, which is why knowing about it does not switch it off.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Under-adjustment, laid out: the second marker had the whole rule to travel, and it stopped almost touching the first.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Structural, not psychological',text:'Because the effect works on the informed, including experts, the piece draws a structural conclusion. The decisive choice is whether a number gets stated at all, and by whom. Delivery and confidence matter less than who opens.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The anchor has to be credible: a figure far outside the plausible range can end the conversation instead of framing it. The classic demonstrations are about estimates, and real negotiations add walk-away options, relationships and pay rules. Going first also risks naming less than they would have offered, so the advice assumes you know your market.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'Someone says anchoring is a cheap trick that good negotiators see through. What is the piece\u2019s best reply?',options:['It operates on people who know about it, and awareness barely reduces it','True, so it should only be used against inexperienced negotiators','It works only when the first number is obviously reasonable','It depends on confident delivery, which experts can detect'],answer:0,why:'The effect does not need a naive target. It comes from how everyone searches for a settlement, so seeing it does not cancel it.'}]}},'the-number-you-say-first_2':{pictureCaption:'Three prices on the board. The hand goes straight to the lowest, as if it were the only one written.',bridge:'Idea 1 said to give a number. This one is about a common way of half-giving one.',basics:{title:'People hear the lowest number',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'ASKING FOR MORE POCKET MONEY',text:'You ask for more pocket money. You say: between \xa35 and \xa38 a week? Your parents smile and say \xa35 sounds fine. Nobody even talks about \xa38.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When you give a range, people often hear only the lowest number. So if you give one, make the bottom number one you would be happy with.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A sign with three prices. The hand goes straight for the lowest one, as if the others were not there.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the top gets ignored',text:'The other person would rather pay less, so why pick the top? Saying *5 to 8* tells them 5 is fine. The 8 is easy to ignore.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Another way',text:'Say one number, and what could change it. For example: *\xa37 a week. I\u2019d take \xa36 if I didn\u2019t have to do the bins.* Now the talk starts from 7.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you ask for something with a range, look at the bottom number. Would you be happy with it?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Mo wants about \xa320 for his old game. If he gives a range, which is best?',options:['\xa320 to \xa325','\xa315 to \xa320','About \xa315, maybe more'],answer:0,why:'People often hear only the lowest number in a range. So the lowest number should be one Mo is happy with.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'SIXTY TO SEVENTY',prompt:'You tell an employer you are looking for sixty to seventy thousand. Where is their offer most likely to land?',options:['Near seventy','Around sixty-five','At or near sixty'],answer:2,reveal:'The other side has no incentive to consider the top. Saying sixty to seventy tells them sixty is acceptable, and the conversation goes on from there.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A range tends to be heard as its lowest number, with a decoration attached. It feels cooperative, but it can concede the bottom before any negotiation has happened.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Three prices on the board, and a hand already on the lowest, as though it were the only one written.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why ranges tempt us',text:'A range seems flexible and cooperative, a way to show you are reasonable. What it can do is tell the other side the lowest figure you will accept, before they have offered anything.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO SHOW FLEXIBILITY',left:{title:'A range',items:['Sixty to seventy','Can concede sixty up front','The bottom often becomes the start']},right:{title:'One number, plus a condition',items:['Seventy','Plus what would move it','Anchor kept, trade still open']},note:'The second keeps the anchor intact while showing a trade is possible. It is a different structure from conceding the low end.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'WHEN A RANGE IS FORCED',text:'An application form insists on a salary band. If sixty-five is what you would actually accept, write sixty-five to seventy-five, not fifty-five to seventy-five. The number you would accept belongs at the bottom, not in the middle.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of the last range you quoted, for pay, a fee or a price. Would you have been happy with its bottom number?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You would accept \xa340,000 for a role, and a form demands a range. What does the idea recommend?',options:['\xa340,000 to \xa346,000','\xa336,000 to \xa344,000','\xa334,000 to \xa346,000','Leave the box blank'],answer:0,why:'A range tends to be read as its lowest number, so the figure you would accept belongs at the bottom. A blank box hands the anchor to them.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE COOPERATIVE RANGE',belief:'Offering a range shows flexibility and invites a fair meeting in the middle.',reality:'The other party has no incentive to consider the top. A range is often heard as its lowest number with a decoration attached, and it can concede that number before negotiation begins.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A range tends to be heard as its lowest number. If flexibility must be signalled, one number plus what would move it keeps the anchor. If a range is unavoidable, the acceptable number belongs at its bottom.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the bottom is what lands',text:'Idea 1 said the search for a settlement starts from a credible number. A range offers two, and the listener is free to start from the one that suits them. You have also just declared it acceptable. The top costs them nothing to ignore.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'HOW A BAND IS HEARD',low:'The bottom: what the listener hears',high:'The top: decoration',note:'Put the figure you would accept at the left end, never in the middle.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Three prices and one hand, and it goes to the lowest. The board offered a range; the buyer read a single number.'},{kind:'explain',title:'One number, plus what would move it',text:'Signalling flexibility does not need a range. A single figure with a statement of what would change it, such as a start date or the scope, keeps the anchor intact while making plain that a trade is possible.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The piece argues this from incentives rather than citing a study of ranges, so it says a range *tends* to be heard this way, not that it always is. A range whose bottom is already your target can do as well as a single number. Where published pay bands are the norm, a lone figure may simply be fitted into the band. The rule targets conceding the low end in advance, not ranges as such.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISTAKE',prompt:'A freelancer would accept \xa3500 for a job. Which quote makes the mistake this idea warns against?',options:['\xa3400 to \xa3600, to look reasonable','\xa3500 to \xa3600, with \xa3500 the real floor','\xa3550, or \xa3500 if paid upfront','\xa3550 flat, with a short written reason'],answer:0,why:'It puts the acceptable figure in the middle, so the listener is likely to hear \xa3400. The others keep the bottom at or above what the freelancer would accept.'}]}},'the-number-you-say-first_3':{pictureCaption:'On its own the request is a blank sheet. The document under the hand is its reason, and the two go across together.',bridge:'Ideas 1 and 2 were about which number to say. This one is about what you put beside it.',basics:{title:'Give a reason with your number',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO ASK',text:'Two kids ask to stay up later. Ana says: can I stay up till nine? Leo says: can I stay up till nine? Swim club ends at eight now, and I need time to eat. Who is more likely to get a yes?'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why Leo has the better chance',text:'Ana gave a wish. It is easy to say no to a wish. Leo gave a reason. To say no to him, his parents need a reason of their own. Often they don\u2019t have one.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A number with a reason is harder to turn down than the same number on its own. Getting the reason ready matters more than picking the number.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'On its own, the ask is a blank sheet. The smaller paper under the hand is the reason. They go across together.'},{kind:'explain',title:'A reason they can pass on',text:'Sometimes the person you ask has to check with someone else. A reason gives them something to say. *She needs it because\u2026* works better than *she just wants it.*'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you ask for something, find one fact that backs it up. Say the fact along with the ask.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Isla asks the head teacher for a bigger budget for the art club. Which ask is most likely to work?',options:['Ten more pupils joined the club this term','Art really matters, so we deserve more','Please could we have a bit more this year?'],answer:0,why:'A fact gives the head a reason to say yes, and something to tell others. A wish is easy to turn down.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'WHAT PREPARATION MEANS',belief:'Getting ready for a pay talk is mostly about choosing the right number.',reality:'The work that matters is building the basis: market data, a similar role, a record of results. Once the evidence is in, the number mostly follows from it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The reason behind a number matters more than its size. A request with a reason is heard differently from the same request without one. That holds even when the reason is not especially strong.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'THE SAME FIGURE, TWICE',left:{title:'No basis',items:['Reads as a wish','Declined without argument','A high figure looks like chancing it']},right:{title:'With a basis',items:['Reads as a position','Declining needs a counter-argument','A high figure gets negotiated']},note:'Often, there is no counter-argument.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why reasons travel',text:'The person opposite often cannot approve the figure alone. A reason gives them something to take to whoever does. It might be market data, a job that has grown, or a similar role elsewhere. Your request becomes their case.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A request and its reason slid across the desk together. The reason is what the other person can carry upstairs.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before you next ask for a number, find one outside figure that supports it: a market rate, a similar role, or a result you delivered.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Two colleagues ask for the same raise. One cites how her scope grew this year. The other says he feels he deserves it. What does the idea predict?',options:['Hers is harder to refuse: declining needs a counter-argument','Both get the same answer, since the amounts are identical','His does better, since confidence matters more than evidence','Neither reason matters, since budgets decide everything'],answer:0,why:'An unexplained figure can be declined without argument. A figure with a basis needs a counter-argument, and often there is none.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHERE THE EFFORT GOES',text:'The piece moves the work of preparing for a pay conversation away from the number and onto its basis. Its claim is that the reason attached to a figure decides how it is received, more than the figure itself does.'},{kind:'idea',text:'An unexplained figure is a preference and can be declined without argument. The same figure with a basis needs a counter-argument to decline, and frequently none exists. Assemble the basis, and the number largely follows.'},{kind:'term',word:'Basis',meaning:'The external support for a figure: market data, a comparable role, a record of results. It turns a number from something you want into something the other side has to answer.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW A REASON TRAVELS',nodes:['You state a figure with a basis','Refusing needs a counter-argument','Often none exists','They carry your basis upwards'],note:'The reason gives the person opposite something to take to whoever approves. Your request becomes their argument.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How high can the number go?',text:'The basis also sets the ceiling. A high figure with no basis reads as opportunism; the same figure with evidence reads as a position, and positions get negotiated rather than dismissed. This is what makes an ambitious anchor from idea 1 credible.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A request and the document that backs it, slid across together. Without the second sheet, the first is easy to push back.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The piece says even weak reasons change how a request is received, and that cuts both ways: a thin reason may win a hearing and then fail under scrutiny. A basis invites a counter-basis, so cherry-picked data can backfire. And no reason creates budget that is not there. The claim is about how a request is received, not a guarantee that it succeeds.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A team wants a larger budget next year. Which preparation does this idea favour?',options:['Gather one external comparison and build the figure from it','Pick a bold figure and practise saying it with confidence','Ask for double, expecting to be talked down to the real need','Wait for finance to propose a figure first, then respond'],answer:0,why:'The basis does the work: it makes the figure something that needs a counter-argument to refuse. Waiting for finance would also hand them the anchor.'}]}},'the-number-you-say-first_4':{pictureCaption:'Every step is built on the one below it. Where the bottom step sits decides where all the others begin.',bridge:'Ideas 1 to 3 apply to any number you say. This one says which conversation deserves them most.',basics:{title:'The first number keeps counting',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE FIRST DEAL',belief:'The first deal does not matter much. You can fix it later.',reality:'Later rises are usually worked out from what you already get. So a low start stays low, and the gap gets bigger with every rise.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When each rise is worked out from what you get now, the starting number matters most. It is the base for every rise that comes after it.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE TWO SNOWBALLS',text:'Two snowballs roll down the same hill. One starts a bit bigger. Both pick up snow as they roll, and the bigger one picks up more. At the bottom, the gap is far bigger than at the top.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'WITH NUMBERS',text:'Say you start on \xa35 a week and your cousin on \xa36. Each year you both get a rise of one tenth. You go up to \xa35.50. Your cousin goes up to \xa36.60. The gap was \xa31. Now it is \xa31.10, and it keeps growing.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Every step is built on the first one. Where the bottom step sits decides where all the others start.'},{kind:'explain',title:'So be brave at the start',text:'The first talk about money can feel awkward, so people rush it. But it is the one that counts most. A few awkward minutes then can matter for years.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your gran will pay you each week for helping out, with a small rise each year. When does a fair amount matter most?',options:['At the very start','After a few years','It makes no difference when'],answer:0,why:'Each rise is built on the amount before. A fair start makes every later rise bigger.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'TWO HIRES, SAME DAY',text:'Say two people join the same team on the same day, one on \xa340,000 and one on \xa342,000. Both get 3% a year. After the first raise the gap is already \xa32,060, and it widens with every raise that follows.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Much of what a job pays over time can be set at entry, especially over a long stay, because later increases are usually applied as percentages to the starting figure. The first conversation is the one not to be relaxed about.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE START COMPOUNDS',nodes:['The entry figure is agreed','Each raise is a percentage of it','The gap grows every year','It can also shape the next employer\u2019s band'],note:'An hour\u2019s conversation sets a trajectory, not a year.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Every step is built on the one below. The first decides where all the others start.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why internal raises rarely fix it',text:'Internal increases are typically capped as a percentage, so they are a poor way to correct a low start. That is why the largest single adjustments often come from changing employer.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before accepting your next offer, work out what a small difference at entry becomes after five years of the same percentage raises. Then decide whether an awkward hour is worth it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You are offered a job and feel awkward asking for more. A friend says you can push at your first review instead. What does the idea suggest?',options:['Negotiate now, since later raises are capped percentages of it','Wait for the review, since you will have proved yourself by then','Accept, and plan to make up the gap through bonuses later','Ask for a shorter probation period instead of more pay'],answer:0,why:'A review usually applies a capped percentage to the figure you agree now. The entry number is where a difference is cheapest to make and keeps paying.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO GAIN',prompt:'Over several years in a job, which is usually worth more: a higher entry figure, or the same sum paid once as a bonus in year one?',options:['The higher entry figure','The one-off bonus','They come out the same'],answer:0,reveal:'A one-off sum is paid once. A higher entry figure is paid every year, and each percentage raise is applied to it, so the difference grows.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Much of the total pay over a job can be determined at entry, especially over a long stay, because increases are usually applied as percentages to the starting figure. The difference persists, grows, and can also set the band the next employer uses.'},{kind:'equation',parts:['Starting figure','Percentage raises'],result:'Pay for the whole stay',note:'Every raise multiplies whatever the start was, so a gap at entry is multiplied along with it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it follows you out',text:'Your current figure tends to become the reference point for the next offer, so a low start can follow you to another employer. Inside the job, increases capped as percentages correct a low start poorly.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The disproportion',text:'The entry conversation is short and uncomfortable, and often treated as the one to be relaxed about. The piece argues the opposite: an hour\u2019s conversation sets a trajectory rather than a year, so that is where the effort belongs.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Every step rests on the first. The bottom step fixes the height the whole staircase starts from.'},{kind:'limits',text:'How much is decided at entry depends on staying a long time and on raises really being percentages; fixed pay scales, promotions and flat awards loosen the link. Where offers are fixed, pushing hard at entry gains little. And the largest jumps coming from changing employer is a common pattern, not a law.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TEST THE ARGUMENT',prompt:'In which situation does the case for fighting hardest at entry lose most of its force?',options:['Pay is a fixed scale where everyone moves up by set steps','Raises are a percentage of the current figure each year','The next employer will ask what you were paid before','You expect to stay in the role for many years'],answer:0,why:'The argument rests on raises compounding from the entry figure. On a fixed scale of set steps, the starting point carries far less forward.'}]}},'the-number-you-say-first_5':{pictureCaption:'The offer is across the counter and the near side has gone still. Opposite, a hand rests on it, thinking. Nobody has said no.',bridge:'Ideas 1 to 4 decide the number. This one is about the few seconds after you say it.',basics:{title:'Say your number, then stop',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'AT THE SCHOOL FAIR',text:'You are selling your old comics. A boy asks how much. You say \xa33. He looks at the comics and says nothing. After a few seconds you blurt out: or \xa32, if you like. He had not even answered.'},{kind:'idea',text:'After you say a number, stop and wait. A quiet pause is not a no. If you fill it, you usually end up lowering your own price.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the quiet means',text:'When someone goes quiet, they are often just thinking. Maybe they are adding up. Maybe they are checking their money. The quiet is usually not a message. It is just a pause.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Your offer is on the table. Their hand rests on it. They are thinking, not saying no. Your side stays still.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why we fill it',text:'Silence feels awkward, so we rush to end it. And the quickest way to end it is to make our own offer worse.'},{kind:'steps',title:'How to wait',steps:['Say your number clearly.','Stop talking.','Count slowly in your head.','Let them speak next.']},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you make an offer or ask for something, say it once. Then wait for the other person to talk first.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Jay asks his dad for \xa310 for a school trip. His dad goes quiet. What should Jay do?',options:['Wait for his dad to answer','Quickly say \xa35 would be fine too','Say sorry for asking at all'],answer:0,why:'His dad is probably just thinking. If Jay fills the pause, he may give away half of what he asked for.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'flow',kicker:'A FEW SECONDS, ONE CONCESSION',nodes:['You state your number','They go quiet','You read the quiet as a no','You soften your own offer'],note:'Nobody asked for the reduction. It was given to end a silence that carried no message.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The pause after an offer is usually just a pause. Reading it as hostility is what produces the unforced concession. Say your number, then let them speak next.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What they are actually doing',text:'The person opposite is frequently doing arithmetic, checking a band, or composing a reply. Their silence carries no message. It only feels like one from your side of the table.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The offer sits in the middle, and the hands opposite rest on it, working something out. The near side, where the urge to add something lives, stays empty.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'FILL IT, OR LET IT RUN',left:{title:'Filling the pause',items:['Softening the number','Adding a qualification','Offering a discount nobody asked for']},right:{title:'Letting it run',items:['The number stands','They respond to it as stated','Nothing is given away']},note:'The most common failure is improving your own offer before anyone has responded to it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Not a power play',text:'This needs no confidence, and it is not a tactic to unsettle anyone. It needs only the recognition that the silence is neutral, and that the instinct to interpret it is what costs money.'},{kind:'try',text:'Practise once with a friend: state a number, then stay silent until they speak. Notice how strong the urge to add something is.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You quote a client \xa32,000 for a project. They pause and look down at the proposal. What is the best next move?',options:['Wait for them to respond to the figure','Mention you could do \xa31,800 if needed','Explain again why the price is fair','Ask whether the figure is a problem'],answer:0,why:'They are probably reading or working something out. The other three all fill the pause, and each one invites a reduction nobody has asked for.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'THE EXPENSIVE MOMENT',word:'Unforced concession',meaning:'A reduction in your own position that nobody asked for. In pay talks the classic case is softening a number, or offering a discount, while the other side is still silent.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The silence after an offer is usually neutral. The cost comes from interpreting it: discomfort prompts whoever made the offer to fill the pause, and filling it almost always means conceding.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why silence gets misread',text:'Whoever has just named a number is listening for a verdict, so a pause sounds like one. Meanwhile the other side is usually doing arithmetic, checking a band, or composing a reply. The message exists only on one side of the table.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The near side waits for a verdict. The hands opposite are simply still, considering the offer. Only one side hears the silence as a message.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What filling it throws away',text:'Filling the pause gives up the anchor from idea 1. The number you chose with care, and backed with a basis as idea 3 asked, is replaced by a lower one offered against nobody\u2019s counter. The other side has not had to argue for anything.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'HOW YOU READ THE PAUSE',low:'Silence heard as hostility',high:'Silence heard as thinking',note:'The discipline is to move yourself to the right. It needs no confidence, only the recognition that the pause is neutral.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Silence is not always neutral: sometimes it is a deliberate tactic, and sometimes the number really is out of range. Letting it run does not mean refusing to talk once they respond. Norms about pauses also differ between people and places. And the piece\u2019s view that this is the most repeatable advice in the area is a judgement, not a measured result.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which behaviour misapplies this idea?',options:['Staying silent after a fair counter-offer, to make them sweat','Stating your figure, then waiting for them to respond','Not adding a discount while they check their budget','Answering their question as soon as they ask one'],answer:0,why:'The discipline is to let a neutral pause run, not to use silence as pressure. The piece is explicit that this is not a power play.'}]}}}},3022,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'the-people-you-stop-calling_1':{pictureCaption:'Nobody cut this rope. It has just gone slack along the whole street. That is how most friendships end.',basics:{title:'Most friendships fade, they don\u2019t snap',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'A ROPE GONE SLACK',picture:!0,text:'Look at this rope. Nobody cut it. Nobody pulled it apart. It just went loose, bit by bit, until it lay on the ground. Nothing broke.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'A NEW SCHOOL',text:'Your best friend moves to a different school. At first you text every day. Then a reply takes four days. Then eleven. There was no fight. You just stopped.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Most friendships do not end with a fight. They fade, and nobody chose it. Since nothing went wrong, there is nothing to fix. You can just say hi.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why does it get stuck?',text:'When a friend goes quiet, you may think: *they don\u2019t like me any more.* So you go quiet too. Then they think the same thing. A small gap turns into a big one.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of a friend you just stopped talking to. Was there a fight? If not, send them a hello today.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You and an old friend have not spoken for a year. There was no fight. What is most likely true?',options:['You drifted, and a hello could start it again','They must be cross with you about something','They have found new friends and do not want you'],answer:0,why:'Most quiet endings have no cause. With nothing to fix, saying hello is often enough to start again.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'If a friendship ended, something must have gone wrong.',reality:'Mostly nothing did. A move, a baby, a new job, then a reply that took four days, then eleven. No one was wronged. Friendships almost never end in an argument.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Adult friendships end in a slowly lengthening interval, with no decision taken by anyone. Because there is no event, there is nothing to resolve, and that is both what makes it hard and what makes it easier to undo than it feels.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW A GAP BECOMES PERMANENT',nodes:['An ordinary gap','Silence read as a judgement','You withdraw further','The gap widens'],loop:!0,note:'The silence carried no message. Reading one into it is what keeps the loop turning.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why there is nothing to repair',text:'A row leaves you something to do: apologise, explain, make up. A drift leaves only a gap that has become conspicuous. People search for the incident so they know what to address. Usually there was none.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The good news inside this',text:'If the friendship was not lost over anything, the barrier to resuming is much lower than it feels. You are not reopening a dispute. You are just ending a pause.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one friend you lost touch with. Stop trying to remember what went wrong. Get in touch as if nothing did, because probably nothing did.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You realise an old university friend has not been in touch for two years. What does the essay suggest?',options:['Assume it was a drift and get in touch without looking for a cause','Work out what you did wrong before you contact them again','Wait for them to get in touch, since they went quiet first','Accept that their silence is their answer and let it go'],answer:0,why:'There was probably no incident. Searching for one, or reading the silence as a verdict, is what keeps the gap open.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHAT THE ESSAY IS ABOUT',text:'The essay\u2019s subject is how adult friendships end: almost never in a rupture, almost always in a drift that nobody chose and both people noticed. This first idea sets out the shape. The rest of the essay explains why it holds and how to undo it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Most lost friendships have no cause. They end in a gradually lengthening interval, and the absence of an event is what makes them hard to repair and easy to misread.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Two consequences of no event',text:'First, there is nothing to apologise for and nothing to resolve, only a gap that has become conspicuous. Second, the ending carries no information. The silence says nothing about what either person thinks, though both are tempted to read it as a judgement.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE MECHANISM',nodes:['A gap with no cause','Read as a judgement','Withdrawal in response','The gap becomes permanent'],note:'The essay locates the damage in the reading, not the gap.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'An unbroken rope lying slack down a long street. Nothing was cut; the tension simply went out of it over distance.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Some friendships do end over something: a betrayal, a real hurt, a choice. Then there is something to address, and treating it as drift skips it. Some drifts also reflect people who have genuinely grown apart. The essay\u2019s claim is about the usual shape, not every case.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of a friendship you lost. Can you name the incident? If you can\u2019t, what story have you told yourself instead?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISREADING',prompt:'Which conclusion misreads the essay\u2019s idea?',options:['A friend\u2019s long silence is a clear sign they have decided to end it','A friendship can be resumed without first identifying what went wrong','Searching for the cause of a drift can itself keep the gap open','A drift usually has no single moment at which it began'],answer:0,why:'The essay says the ending carries no information. Reading silence as a decision is the mistake that makes the gap permanent.'}]}},'the-people-you-stop-calling_2':{pictureCaption:'The ball has stopped on the net, and each player is waiting at their own end for the other to fetch it.',bridge:'Idea 1 said a drift has no cause. This one explains what keeps it going: both people waiting.',basics:{title:'You both think it is their turn',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'AN OLD FRIEND',prompt:'You and an old friend have not messaged for months. Who sent the last message?',options:['Me','Them','I\u2019m not sure'],answer:2,reveal:'People often say *me*. The odd part is that your friend probably says *me* too. You can\u2019t both be right.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The ball sits on the net. Each player waits for the other one to get it. So nobody plays.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When a friendship goes quiet, both people often think it is the other one\u2019s turn to message. At least one of them is wrong. So send it anyway.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why does this happen?',text:'You remember your own message well. You were there when you sent it. Their last message is fuzzier. They feel the same way about yours. Nobody is lying. Both memories lean the same way.'},{kind:'try',text:'Is there a friend you think owes you a message? Pretend you are the one who owes it. Send it today.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your cousin has not texted you since the summer. You are sure it is her turn. What is best?',options:['Text her anyway, in case you have it wrong','Wait, since it really is her turn','Ask your mum who texted last'],answer:0,why:'She may be just as sure it is your turn. Texting first ends the wait for both of you.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE LAST MESSAGE',text:'You think about an old friend and feel a small sting. You were the one who suggested dinner last spring. She said yes and never followed up. The ball is in her court. Across town, she is thinking almost exactly the same about you.'},{kind:'idea',text:'In lapsed friendships both people commonly believe the other owes the next contact. Each remembers being the last to reach out, and at least one is wrong.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'What you remember',items:['I suggested dinner','She never followed up','She owes me']},right:{title:'What she remembers',items:['I sent that birthday message','They only replied with a thumbs up','They owe me']},note:'Both accounts are sincere. Each is lopsided in the same direction.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why both are sure',text:'You remember your own efforts more clearly than the other person\u2019s. So both people sincerely believe they reached out most recently. The result is a stable deadlock nobody chose, held in place by a mild grievance on both sides.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What to do with the ledger',text:'Disregard it. It is not just unreliable; it is unreliable in a known direction, tilted in your favour. Whoever notices first is in the better position to act, and acting costs very little compared with the state it ends.'},{kind:'try',text:'Name one person you feel owes you a message. Assume you are misremembering who owes whom, and send it anyway.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A friend says: \u201cI always make the effort. This time it\u2019s on him.\u201d What does this idea suggest?',options:['He likely feels the same, so whoever notices should just get in touch','She is right to wait, since effort should be balanced over time','She should check old messages to settle who reached out last','She should tell him plainly that it is his turn to make the effort'],answer:0,why:'The ledger tilts towards yourself on both sides. Checking or enforcing it keeps the deadlock; acting ends it.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'flow',kicker:'ONE SIDE OF THE DEADLOCK',nodes:['I recall my last effort clearly','So they owe the next one','Their silence reads as an answer','I wait'],loop:!0,note:'The same loop is running on the other side, with the names reversed.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Both people in a lapsed friendship usually think the ball is in the other\u2019s court. The symmetry is the surprise: once that belief sets, silence is read as an answer rather than as the same hesitation on the other side.'},{kind:'term',word:'The ledger',meaning:'The informal account each person keeps of who reached out last. The essay\u2019s point is that both ledgers are skewed the same way: each remembers its owner\u2019s efforts best.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why \u201ca known direction\u201d matters',text:'If memory erred at random, your ledger would sometimes favour you and sometimes your friend, and on average it might be fair. The essay\u2019s claim is sharper: the error leans towards crediting yourself. A record biased in a known direction should be discounted, not consulted.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why nobody breaks it',text:'The deadlock is stable because each side holds a small, sincere grievance. Breaking it feels like conceding a point. The essay\u2019s answer is that there is no point to concede: the person who notices first is simply better placed to act.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Sometimes the ledger is right. If one person has done all the reaching out for years, that imbalance is real information, and this idea is not a reason to carry a one-sided friendship forever. It addresses the lapsed deadlock, not a pattern of neglect.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'Someone objects that if they always make the first move, they teach the other person they never have to. How does the essay answer?',options:['Your sense of always moving first is the thing most likely to be skewed','Making the first move is good manners and should never be resented','The other person will notice and make the next move without being asked','Friendships matter more than fairness, so the balance can be ignored'],answer:0,why:'The essay\u2019s point is that both people feel they always move first. The feeling is not good evidence, though a long, real imbalance would be.'}]}},'the-people-you-stop-calling_3':{pictureCaption:'The huge, carefully wrapped parcel sits in the hall. The small card is the thing that actually gets through the door.',bridge:'Idea 2 said to send the message anyway. This one is about how small that message can be.',basics:{title:'A tiny message is enough',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'AN OLD PHOTO',text:'You find a photo of you and an old friend at a birthday party. You want to get back in touch. But you think you need to write something long. So you put it off. A month goes by.'},{kind:'idea',text:'To get back in touch, you only need a tiny message. Send the photo with one line. The long message feels like the right one, but it often never gets sent.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'The big message',items:['Says sorry for the gap','Explains why','Asks to meet up','Often never sent']},right:{title:'The tiny message',items:['A photo','\u201cFound this! Remember?\u201d','Sent today']}},{kind:'picture',caption:'The big parcel is still in the hall. The small card got through the door. Small things arrive.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why does small work?',text:'A tiny message asks for nothing. So your friend can send a tiny reply. Then you can chat a bit. You do not have to fix everything at once.'},{kind:'try',text:'Send one old friend a photo, a joke or one line about something you both remember. Today, not when it is perfect.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You want to get back in touch with a friend from your old club. Which message is best to start?',options:['\u201cSaw a poster for the club and thought of you!\u201d','A long letter saying sorry for not writing','A plan for a whole day out next month'],answer:0,why:'The short one is easy to send and easy to answer. The others are big jobs for both of you.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THREE DRAFTS',prompt:'Which message is most likely to restart a friendship that has been quiet for two years?',options:['A long note explaining the silence','A photo and one line about it','An invitation to a weekend away'],answer:1,reveal:'The photo. It asks for nothing and allows a small reply. The others are harder to write, harder to answer, and the first often never gets sent.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The message that re-establishes contact is much smaller than people assume: short, specific and unburdened. Something remembered is enough.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the overdue message is so hard',text:'You imagine the debt as large: two years of silence to account for. So the message has to be large too. But the size of the debt is the thing that is wrong. Usually nobody is waiting for an explanation.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'The elaborate message',items:['Explains the gap','Apologises for it','Proposes a big meeting','Becomes a project']},right:{title:'The small one',items:['Something that reminded you','No accounting for the silence','No request','Sent in a minute']}},{kind:'explain',title:'Why small works',text:'It asks for nothing and permits a small reply. The exchange restarts at a low level, rather than both people having to restore the friendship to where it was in one go.'},{kind:'try',text:'Send the two-line version today, to one person, rather than the good version never.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You pass a caf\xe9 where you and an old colleague used to meet. What does this idea suggest?',options:['Send a photo of it with a line saying it made you think of them','Draft a proper message once you have time to explain the gap','Suggest a long lunch there to make up for the lost time','Wait for a birthday, so the message has a better reason'],answer:0,why:'The photo is short, specific and asks for nothing. The other options turn reconnecting into a project or a delay.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'After a long silence, you owe the other person an explanation before anything else.',reality:'The essay argues that this imagined debt is the error. Reconnection rarely starts with a considered letter. It starts with a photograph, a link, a sentence about something remembered.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The message that works is embarrassingly small. The overdue message is hard to write because the debt has been imagined as large, and the size of the debt is what is wrong.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The failure mode',text:'The elaborate message explains the gap, apologises for it, and proposes a substantial meeting. Each element adds weight for both people. It is harder to write and harder to answer, and because writing it is a project, it frequently goes unsent.'},{kind:'equation',kicker:'WHAT THE SMALL MESSAGE IS',parts:['Something remembered','No request'],result:'An easy reply',note:'Nothing in it requires the other person to restore the friendship in one move.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why low is the right level',text:'A small message lets the exchange resume at a low level and rise from there. A large one asks both people to jump straight back to where the friendship used to be, which is a lot to ask of two people who have been apart for years.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The large, careful parcel never leaves the hall. The small card, pushed through the letterbox, is what arrives.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Where the silence followed a real hurt, a breezy photo can read as pretending it never happened, and something more direct is owed. A small message restarts contact; it does not by itself rebuild depth. That takes the slower work of the essay\u2019s later ideas.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE FLAW',prompt:'Which message looks small but carries the weight the essay warns about?',options:['\u201cSorry I\u2019ve been so useless. I know it\u2019s been ages. We must sort a proper catch-up.\u201d','\u201cWalked past our old flat today. The green door is now bright yellow.\u201d','\u201cThis song came on and I thought of that terrible road trip.\u201d'],answer:0,why:'It is short, but it accounts for the silence, apologises and makes a request. The other two simply share something remembered.'}]}},'the-people-you-stop-calling_4':{pictureCaption:'Two doors on one landing made meeting automatic. Brick up one, and nothing brings the neighbours together any more.',bridge:'Ideas 1 to 3 were about the gap. This one explains a common reason it opened: what kept you in contact went away.',basics:{title:'Being near each other did a lot of the work',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'A NEW CLASS',text:'Last year you sat next to your best friend every day. This year you are in different classes. You see each other less. You both still like each other. So why does it feel harder?'},{kind:'idea',text:'Lots of friendships grow because people see each other all the time without planning it. When that stops, the friendship needs planning for the first time. That is a new skill, not a sign you did something wrong.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two doors on one landing: the neighbours met every day. Brick one up, and nothing brings them together now.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it feels hard',text:'Before, you met without trying. Now you have to ask, pick a time and turn up. You never had to do that before. So of course you are not good at it yet.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one friend you don\u2019t see by chance any more. Agree on a regular time, like Saturday mornings at the park.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend joined a different football team. You see each other less. What would help most?',options:['Set up a regular time to meet each week','Wait to bump into each other again','Decide the friendship was not that strong'],answer:0,why:'The team used to bring you together. Now a plan has to do that job.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'AFTER SHE LEFT THE JOB',text:'For four years you had lunch together most days. Then she moved jobs. You both said you would keep in touch, and meant it. Six months on you have met twice, and each time it took a dozen messages to arrange.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Most durable adult friendships are built somewhere with forced repeat contact: a workplace, a team, a shared street. When that structure goes, the friendship has to be maintained deliberately for the first time.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What proximity was doing',text:'In classic studies of housing, how close together people lived strongly predicted who became friends. Repeated, unplanned contact does much of the work, and the people in a friendship consistently underestimate how much.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Proximity supplied',items:['Contact without planning','A reason to be in the same place','Small talk that built up']},right:{title:'Now it needs',items:['A standing arrangement','Someone to suggest it','A habit, not goodwill']},note:'Goodwill was never what produced the contact, so goodwill alone will not replace it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Not a verdict',text:'A friendship that fades after a move or a job change is usually not a judgement on the relationship. The structure that sustained it was removed, and nothing replaced it.'},{kind:'try',text:'For one friendship that ran on proximity, propose a standing arrangement: a monthly call, a recurring date. Put it in both calendars.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You have moved city, and your closest friend is now three hours away. What is the idea\u2019s advice?',options:['Set a recurring call, since the old daily contact will not happen on its own','Trust that a strong friendship will keep itself going without planning','Expect it to fade, since friendships rarely survive this kind of distance','Message whenever you happen to think of them, so it stays natural'],answer:0,why:'Proximity did the work before. A deliberate substitute has to take its place; goodwill and spontaneity were never what produced the contact.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'THE WORD',word:'Propinquity',meaning:'Nearness in space: how physically close people are in their daily lives. In this essay, the forced repeat contact of a shared workplace, team or street.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A large share of friendship is produced by proximity, and the people in it consistently underestimate this. Once the structure goes, the friendship must be actively maintained, often for the first time.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The classic findings',text:'The essay calls the early findings on propinquity stark: in studies of housing, how close together people lived strongly predicted who became friends. The implication is that repeated unplanned contact, not just affinity, does much of the work of forming a friendship.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What follows',text:'If proximity was producing the contact, a friendship ending after a move is usually not a verdict on it. And the remedy changes: goodwill will not do, because goodwill was never what produced the contact. A deliberate substitute is needed, such as a standing call or a recurring date.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The same landing twice: two doors, then one bricked up and the stairs in darkness. The friendship did not change; the structure around it did.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Not a character flaw',text:'People who formed every friendship inside a structure have never had to maintain one outside it. Being bad at it is a missing skill, not a moral failing, and skills can be learned.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The housing findings concern who becomes friends, not how friendships survive a move; applying them to maintenance is the essay\u2019s extension. Not every friendship rests on proximity. And a standing arrangement can harden into an obligation both people dread, which is its own way of ending.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A team moves from one office to fully remote work. Which prediction follows from this idea?',options:['Casual working friendships will thin unless something deliberate replaces the daily contact','Friendships will be unaffected, since colleagues can message each other just as easily','Only weak friendships will fade, which reveals which ones were never real','Friendships will improve, since people can now choose whom they talk to'],answer:0,why:'Remote work removes the forced repeat contact. Without a substitute, the essay predicts drift, and not as a verdict on anyone.'}]}},'the-people-you-stop-calling_5':{pictureCaption:'The knuckles stop just short of a door not knocked on for years, and the door is already opening.',bridge:'Idea 3 was the smallest way back in. This one is the most direct.',basics:{title:'Say it out loud: we drifted',beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Saying \u201cwe don\u2019t talk any more\u201d out loud will make it weird.',reality:'It feels scary. But your friend has probably felt the same weird feeling. Saying it often makes both of you feel better.'},{kind:'idea',text:'You can just say it: we drifted apart, it was nobody\u2019s fault, and I would like to be friends again. It feels hard, but it usually works.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'AT THE DOOR',picture:!0,text:'You go to knock on the door of a friend you have not seen for ages. Your hand stops. What if they don\u2019t want to see you? Then the door starts to open. They saw you coming.'},{kind:'steps',title:'What to say',steps:['It has been ages.','It was nobody\u2019s fault.','I miss hanging out.','Want to come to the park on Saturday?']},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it works',text:'When friends stop talking, both of them wonder what the silence means. Saying it out loud clears that up. Now it is just a plain fact: we got busy. A plain fact is easier to deal with than a guess.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of a friend you drifted from. Try saying the four lines out loud, to yourself first.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You want to make up with a friend you drifted from. Which line follows this idea?',options:['\u201cWe haven\u2019t talked in ages. I\u2019d like that to change.\u201d','\u201cWhy did you stop talking to me?\u201d','\u201cI guess you have new friends now.\u201d'],answer:0,why:'It names the drift and blames nobody. The others turn it into a fight.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'THE FEAR AND THE USUAL RESULT',left:{title:'What you dread',items:['An awkward silence','They don\u2019t want it back','You look needy']},right:{title:'What is more common',items:['Relief','They felt the same','They had the same reasons not to say it']},note:'The fear is real. The essay\u2019s claim is that it is usually larger than the risk.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The most direct way to restart a lapsed friendship is to say the awkward sentence: that you drifted, that it was nobody\u2019s fault, that you would like it back. It is uncomfortable, and it usually works.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why naming it works',text:'Both people have been managing an ambiguity: the silence means something, but neither knows what. Naming it collapses that into an ordinary fact. We drifted. Once it is a fact, it is no longer a question each person has to keep answering alone.'},{kind:'steps',title:'The workable form',steps:['Acknowledge it has been a long time.','Say that you would like to fix it.','Propose something specific.'],note:'Brief and non-accusatory. No inventory of who stopped calling whom.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'ONE VERSION',text:'*We\u2019ve drifted, and I don\u2019t think either of us meant to. I\u2019d like to fix that. Are you free for a walk on Sunday week?* Three sentences, no blame, one concrete plan.'},{kind:'try',text:'Write the three sentences for one person you have drifted from. Send them, or keep them ready for the next time you think of that person.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You bump into a drifted friend at a wedding. Which line applies the idea?',options:['\u201cIt\u2019s been too long, and I\u2019d love to fix that. Lunch next week?\u201d','\u201cYou\u2019ve been so hard to get hold of these last few years!\u201d','\u201cWe should catch up at some point, it\u2019s been far too long.\u201d','\u201cI wondered whether I\u2019d done something to upset you.\u201d'],answer:0,why:'It names the drift, blames nobody and proposes something specific. The others accuse, stay vague, or reopen the guessing.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'BEFORE THE ARGUMENT',prompt:'When someone names a drift directly to a friend, what does the essay say is the most common response?',options:['Rejection','Indifference','Relief'],answer:2,reveal:'Relief, because the other person has usually been carrying the same discomfort and had the same reasons for not raising it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Naming the lapse directly is uncomfortable and usually works. It removes the ambiguity both people have been managing, and turns an undefined silence into an ordinary fact.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The ambiguity',text:'The earlier ideas showed how silence gets read: as a judgement, as an answer, as the other person\u2019s turn. Each reading is a guess, maintained alone. Naming the drift replaces both people\u2019s guesses with one shared fact.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The feared risk',text:'The fear is that the other person does not want it back. The essay treats this honestly: the risk is real, probably rarer than it feels, and the information is worth having. Knowing ends the guessing either way.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The hand hesitates short of the wood, and the door is already opening. The essay\u2019s claim is that the other side was usually waiting too.'},{kind:'limits',text:'If the other person has clearly chosen to end things, naming it again is pressure, not repair. If there was a real hurt, *nobody\u2019s fault* is not true, and saying it skips what needs saying. And the essay\u2019s claim about relief is a judgement about what is usual, not a promise.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Name the drift, say it was nobody\u2019s fault, propose something.','It works by ending a shared ambiguity.','The feared rejection is real but rarer, and still worth knowing.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'Someone fears that if they name the drift and the friend says no, the friendship is lost for good. What is the essay\u2019s best answer?',options:['The friendship was already lapsed, and knowing where you stand is worth having','A refusal is very unlikely, so the risk is not worth considering at all','You can always pretend you never raised it and carry on as before','Naming it rarely changes anything, so there is little to lose either way'],answer:0,why:'The essay concedes the risk is real but says the information is worth having. The silence was not a friendship being preserved.'}]}}}},3023,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'the-prince_1':{pictureCaption:'A wasp held in tweezers and written up with care. The naturalist is not admiring it or crushing it, only recording what it is.',basics:{title:'Saying what happens is not saying it is good',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TWO SENTENCES',prompt:'Which of these tells you what you *should* do?',options:['Kids often copy whoever is popular','You should copy whoever is popular'],answer:1,reveal:'The first one just says what happens. It is like a report. The second one tells you to do it. They sound alike, but they are very different.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO WROTE IT',text:'Niccol\xf2 Machiavelli lived in Florence, a city in Italy, about 500 years ago. He worked for its government and watched rulers up close. Then he wrote a short book called *The Prince*, about how rulers get power and keep it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'He wrote about how rulers really act, not how they ought to act. People often mix the two up. They think he is telling rulers to be cruel, when often he is only reporting what rulers do.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A WASP SCIENTIST',picture:!0,text:'A scientist who studies wasps writes down how they sting. She is not saying stinging is good. She is not trying to squash every wasp. She just wants to know how wasps work.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'SORT THEM',left:{title:'Says what happens',items:['Groups often leave out the new kid','Teams often blame one player']},right:{title:'Says what to do',items:['Ask the new kid to join in','Share the blame fairly']},note:'Knowing what happens helps you spot it. What to do about it is still your choice.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time someone tells you how the world works, ask one thing. Are they saying what happens, or what I should do? Keep the two apart.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A book says that teams often blame the newest player when they lose. What is the book doing?',options:['Saying what often happens','Telling teams to blame new players','Saying new players are worse'],answer:0,why:'It reports a pattern. It does not say the pattern is fair, and it does not tell anyone to copy it.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'IN THE MEETING',text:'A senior colleague defends a round of quiet cuts by saying Machiavelli would approve. It lands as a clever point. Nobody in the room has read the book. Nobody checks whether it recommends anything of the kind.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Machiavelli separated how rulers actually behave from how they ought to behave. He wrote about the first. Most of the book\u2019s reputation comes from reading his observations as recommendations.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it was new',text:'Earlier advice books for rulers described an ideal prince: just, generous, loved. Machiavelli says he will leave imagined kingdoms to others. The gap between how people live and how they ought to live is so wide, he argues, that studying only the second leads to ruin.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The naturalist records the wasp, sting and all. Writing it down is neither admiring it nor destroying it.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'What he observes',items:['A new ruler often has to break promises','Love is withdrawn when it becomes costly']},right:{title:'What readers hear',items:['Break your promises','Do not bother being liked']},note:'The left column can be true without the right column following from it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it matters beyond him',text:'Separating *what happens* from *what should happen* is the first move in understanding any system. Skip it and you go wrong one of two ways. You deny unpleasant facts because you dislike them. Or you obey them as rules because they are true.'},{kind:'try',text:'Take one piece of advice about power, people or money you read this week. Mark each sentence D for describing or R for recommending. Notice which ones are dressed as the other.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A business book observes that teams with a common enemy pull together faster. A reader decides to invent one. What went wrong?',options:['An observation was read as a recommendation','The observation itself must have been false','The reader should have found a real enemy','Teams should never be studied in this way'],answer:0,why:'Even if the pattern is real, nothing in it says you should produce it. That step is the reader\u2019s, and so is the responsibility.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'FLORENCE, 1513',text:'Machiavelli had served the Florentine republic for fourteen years as an official and envoy. When the Medici family returned to power in 1512 he lost his post, and early the next year he was briefly imprisoned and tortured. On his farm outside the city he wrote a short book on principalities.'},{kind:'idea',text:'His method is to describe what rulers actually do and what follows from it, and to leave the moral verdict to the reader. The book\u2019s notoriety comes largely from collapsing that distinction.'},{kind:'term',word:'Mirror for princes',meaning:'The older genre of advice books for rulers, which set out the virtues an ideal king should have. *The Prince* borrows the form and empties it of the ideal.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The fifteenth chapter',text:'He says plainly that he is departing from writers who imagined republics and principalities never seen to exist. The gap between how people live and how they ought to live is so wide, he argues, that a ruler who studies only the second learns his own ruin.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Forecast, or advice?',text:'When he observes that a new prince who wants to hold power will usually have to break faith, readers hear an instruction. The page is closer to a forecast: this tends to happen, here is why, here are the mechanics. Why he wrote it is another matter. It has been read as a bid for Medici employment and as satire, and five centuries have not settled it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Tweezers, notebook, specimen tray. The work is to see the thing exactly, which is not the same as wanting more of it.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The distinction protects the reader more than the author. Machiavelli often moves from *this happens* to *a prince must*, and many chapters do prescribe. Description is never fully neutral: choosing what to describe, and for whom, is already a choice. A cold account of what works can still serve as a manual, and this one has been used as one.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE COLLAPSE',prompt:'Each reader starts from his observation that cruelty can hold power. Which one collapses description into recommendation?',options:['So cruelty is justified whenever it keeps a ruler in place','So we can see why tyrants keep reaching for it','So a reader still has to judge whether it was right','So it is worth understanding in order to resist it'],answer:0,why:'That cruelty works is a description. That it is therefore justified is a verdict, and the observation alone cannot supply it.'}]}},'the-prince_2':{pictureCaption:'One jar emptied in a single pour and set aside, finished. The other gives one drop at a time, and is noticed with every drop.',bridge:'Idea 1 separated what happens from what should. This is one of his sharpest descriptions, and it is also a description of manipulation.',basics:{title:'Bad news all at once, good news bit by bit',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THREE NEW RULES',text:'Your coach has three new rules for the team. She could tell you all three on Monday. Or she could add one each week, for three weeks.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'Which way keeps the team grumpy for longer?',options:['All three on Monday','One a week for three weeks'],answer:1,reveal:'One a week. Each new rule reminds everyone of the last one. The team keeps waiting for the next. All at once hurts, but then it is over.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Machiavelli wrote a book about rulers 500 years ago. He noticed that hard news hurts less if it comes all at once. Good things feel bigger if they come bit by bit.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One jar was poured out at once and put away. The other drips slowly. You notice every single drop.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A PLASTER, LIKE SWEETS',text:'Pulling off a plaster slowly hurts for longer than one quick pull. Treats work the other way. One sweet a day feels nice all week. A whole bag at once is gone by tea time.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it helps to notice',text:'This is a real pattern in how people feel. But it can also be used to trick people. A leader can hand out small treats one by one, so you feel more thankful than you should. Knowing the pattern helps you see it.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A club leader cuts four trips, one each month. Everyone stays upset all term. Why, by this idea?',options:['Each new cut brought back the old ones','The cuts were bigger than they seemed','People were upset about the leader, not the trips'],answer:0,why:'Bad news in drips keeps opening the hurt again. All at once, it would have stung, then settled.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'FIVE CHANGES TO ANNOUNCE',prompt:'A manager has three unwelcome changes and two good ones to announce. What does Machiavelli\u2019s observation predict will feel least bad?',options:['Spread all five out evenly','Bad ones together, good ones spread out','Good ones first, then the bad ones'],answer:1,reveal:'He observes that harm done all at once is tasted less, and benefits given little by little are enjoyed more. Even spacing gets both halves wrong.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Harm repeated keeps people bracing; harm delivered at once ends and is absorbed. Benefit bundled becomes the new baseline; benefit spread out is felt again each time. Timing changes the experience, not the substance.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHY DRIPPED HARM COMPOUNDS',nodes:['A new cut arrives','It revives the earlier ones','People read a pattern','Everyone braces for the next'],loop:!0,note:'Each injury confirms the last, so the fear never settles.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Cuts across four quarters',items:['Four rounds of rumour','Each round reopens the last','Nobody stops bracing']},right:{title:'One restructure',items:['One bad month','A clear new baseline','Work resumes']},note:'The total cut is the same. The experience is not.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One jar poured out at once and set aside, finished. The other releases a drop at a time, and each drop is felt.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Both readings are correct',text:'This is a true observation about how people process events. It is also a recipe for managing how a decision feels rather than whether it was right. The rule says nothing about whether the harm was justified. Anyone using it should notice that they are managing an experience.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you are on the receiving end of a run of changes, notice how they were sequenced. Ask whether the sequencing is doing work the decision could not do on its merits.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A company cuts pay in one round, then announces small perks one at a time across the year. What is the honest description?',options:['It is managing how the year feels, separately from whether it is fair','It is proving the pay cut was justified by the later perks','It is making a mistake, since perks work best all at once','It is being transparent, since every change was announced'],answer:0,why:'The sequencing follows Machiavelli\u2019s rule exactly. It shapes the experience, and leaves the question of fairness untouched.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'CHAPTER EIGHT',title:'Where the rule comes from',text:'The rule sits in a chapter on men who came to power through crime. Machiavelli discusses Agathocles, who made himself ruler of Syracuse by having its leading citizens killed. He separates cruelty used well, done once from necessity and then turned to the subjects\u2019 benefit, from cruelty used badly, which is renewed again and again.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Do harm all at once, so it is felt once and then fades; give benefits a little at a time, so each one is felt afresh. It describes how people experience events over time.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The mechanism',text:'Harm compounds when repeated: each new injury revives the earlier ones and confirms a pattern, so fear never settles. Benefit decays when bundled: a large one-off is absorbed and becomes the new normal. Both run on the same tendency: people respond to change more than to level.'},{kind:'term',word:'Integrate losses, segregate gains',meaning:'A principle from Richard Thaler\u2019s later work on mental accounting: losses should hurt less combined and gains please more separated. Tests of the losses half are mixed. It echoes Machiavelli by a different route.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The emptied jar is finished and set aside. The dripping one is noticed afresh with every drop, which is the whole asymmetry.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Read in its chapter, the injuries are killings and the benefits are a usurper buying quiet. The rule describes how to make wrongdoing tolerable to its victims, and that is how it is misused: harm front-loaded so outrage peaks once and fades. It measures how harm is felt, never whether it was justified.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of a run of changes you lived through. Was the sequencing chosen for you, and did it change how you judged the decision itself?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which use of the rule is a misuse?',options:['Bundling unjustified cuts so the outcry peaks once and is forgotten','Giving a patient all the bad test results in one conversation','Noticing that drip-fed changes are keeping a team anxious','Recognising that perks were spread out to seem larger'],answer:0,why:'The rule changes how harm is felt, not whether it was deserved. Using it to make an unjust act easier to swallow is the manipulation the chapter describes.'}]}},'the-prince_3':{pictureCaption:'An armoured glove rests beside a family\u2019s open money box without touching it: power close enough to fear, kept off the property whose taking breeds hatred.',bridge:'Idea 2 was about timing. This is the book\u2019s most quoted line, and the part usually left off is the part that matters.',basics:{title:'Read the whole sentence',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'A FAMOUS LINE',belief:'Machiavelli said it is better to be feared than loved.',reality:'Not quite. He said it is best to be both. If a ruler can only have one, fear is safer. Then he adds a big warning: never be hated, because hate will ruin you.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where it comes from',text:'This line comes from *The Prince*, the book Machiavelli wrote for rulers in Italy 500 years ago. People still repeat it. But they usually cut it short, and that changes what it means.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The famous line is only half of what he said. He told rulers it was best to be both loved and feared, and never hated. Before you repeat a famous line, check the whole sentence.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why he thought fear was safer',text:'He saw that people\u2019s love can change when it costs them something. He thought fear stayed steadier for a ruler. That was his view of kings and princes. It is not a rule for friends.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHAT GETS LEFT OFF',left:{title:'The short version',items:['Better to be feared than loved']},right:{title:'The whole thing',items:['Best to be both','If not, fear is safer','But never be hated','Taking people\u2019s things makes them hate you']},note:'The last two lines change the meaning.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A ruler\u2019s iron glove lies by a family\u2019s money box and leaves it alone. That is how he avoids hate.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'YOU MAY HAVE SEEN THIS',text:'A team captain grabs the best kit for himself and bosses people around. Soon the team hates him. Then nobody listens to him, however tough he acts. That is the warning most people forget.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend says, \u201cMachiavelli says be scary, not nice.\u201d What is the best reply?',options:['He said be both, and never be hated','He said being nice is always a mistake','He said being scary works for anyone'],answer:0,why:'Checking the whole sentence shows a warning most people leave out.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'FINISH THE SENTENCE',prompt:'Machiavelli asks whether it is better for a prince to be loved or feared. What does he answer?',options:['Feared, without qualification','Both; failing that, feared, but never hated','Loved, if the prince is good enough'],answer:1,reveal:'The usual quotation keeps one clause. He says both is best. Since that is hard, fear is safer. And then he makes avoiding hatred the condition everything rests on.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Fear is more reliable than love because you hold it. Love is held at the other party\u2019s convenience. But a ruler who is hated is finished, however feared. Reliability without resentment is the real claim.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why fear is the safer bet, in his view',text:'Love, he argues, is a bond of duty. People break it whenever breaking it pays. Fear rests on dread of punishment, which does not depend on anyone else\u2019s mood. It is an argument about reliability, not a taste for cruelty.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The condition that does the work',text:'Hatred, he says, comes from taking people\u2019s property and interfering with their families. Avoid those and a ruler can be feared without being hated. He adds that people forget a father\u2019s death sooner than the loss of an inheritance.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The armoured glove is the prince\u2019s power, close enough to be felt. The family\u2019s money box sits open beside it, untouched. Feared, but giving no cause for hatred.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'WHERE HE PUTS THE LINE',low:'Loved, and abandoned when it costs',high:'Hated, and soon overthrown',note:'He aims between the two: relied on, never resented. The famous line drops the right-hand warning.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time someone quotes a famous line to win a point, find the paragraph it sits in. Read it before you agree or argue.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A new manager cites this line to justify humiliating staff in public. What does the full passage imply?',options:['It makes avoiding hatred the overriding condition, and humiliation breeds it','It supports him, since fear is safer than love in every case','It says nothing, since it only concerns princes and armies','It supports him, provided the team\u2019s results improve'],answer:0,why:'The passage makes avoiding hatred the condition everything rests on. Conduct that breeds resentment fails his own test, whatever the shortened line suggests.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'CHAPTER SEVENTEEN',text:'The chapter asks whether cruelty or mercy serves a prince better. It opens with Cesare Borgia, thought cruel, whose cruelty brought order to the Romagna, and the Florentines, who to avoid seeming cruel let Pistoia tear itself apart. The loved-or-feared question follows from that.'},{kind:'idea',text:'It is desirable to be both loved and feared; since the two rarely go together, fear is safer, because it depends on the prince and love depends on the subjects. The overriding condition is to avoid hatred.'},{kind:'term',word:'Hatred, in his sense',meaning:'Not dislike but the resentment that makes people willing to act against a ruler. In this chapter its causes are concrete: seizing subjects\u2019 property and interfering with their wives and daughters.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The structure of the argument',text:'Love is a bond of obligation that people break when it suits them. Fear rests on dread of punishment, which does not fail. So a prince should rely on what is in his own power. The same chapter insists he punish only with clear cause, and keep his hands off what is theirs.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Power within reach of a family\u2019s money, leaving it where it lies: the restraint on which being feared without being hated depends.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'THE QUOTATION AND THE CHAPTER',left:{title:'As quoted',items:['Fear beats love','Severity is the method']},right:{title:'As written',items:['Both is best','Fear is the fallback','Hatred is fatal','Restraint about what you take']},note:'The honest summary is narrower: be reliable rather than liked, and be scrupulous about what you take.'},{kind:'limits',text:'His reliability argument assumes subjects who cannot leave and a ruler who controls punishment. Where people can withdraw, fear buys compliance and quiet exits, not loyalty. And restoring the qualification makes the line accurate, not humane: it is still an argument for ruling by dread. Repeat it as a description of how such rule works, not as advice.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Where have you seen a famous line used to win an argument that the full passage would not support?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'FAITHFUL OR NOT',prompt:'Which summary is faithful to the chapter?',options:['Fear is safer than love only so long as it does not become hatred','Fear is always better than love for anyone in charge of others','Love is worthless to a ruler, since people break every bond','Cruelty is justified whenever it produces order, as in the Romagna'],answer:0,why:'The ruling and its condition come as a pair. Drop the condition and you are quoting a sentence he did not write.'}]}},'the-prince_4':{pictureCaption:'A fortress, with a door left open to the daylight. The coat on the peg belongs to someone who can leave whenever they choose.',bridge:'Ideas 2 and 3 described tactics built for a world with no way out. This one asks what changes when people can leave.',basics:{title:'When people can walk away',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE BOSSY CAPTAIN',text:'A new captain takes over your football club. He shouts. He picks favourites. He makes the younger kids carry all the kit. By half term, five players have joined another club.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Machiavelli wrote about rulers whose people could not leave. In a club, a class or a team, people can walk away and tell others why. So the harsh tricks that kept a prince in power stop working.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'This is a castle, but someone has opened a door in the wall. Whoever owns that coat can walk out any time.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO WORLDS',left:{title:'His world',items:['People could not just leave','Nobody above the prince to tell']},right:{title:'Your world',items:['You can quit a club','You can tell a teacher','Friends hear what happened']},note:'When people can leave, being fair is what keeps them.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What is still useful',text:'Some of what he noticed is still true. People remember who was kind to them. They remember who took from them. Noticing how people act is useful. Copying his harsh tricks is not.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time a leader in a group you know is harsh, watch what happens. Do people stay? Do they tell others? Notice how the group changes.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A girl runs the class quiz team by being mean. Why is it likely to fail?',options:['The others can quit and tell people why','Being mean makes you worse at quizzes','Teachers never let anyone be in charge'],answer:0,why:'In his world, people were stuck. In yours, they can leave, and they will.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE BOOK ON THE DESK',text:'A newly promoted manager keeps *The Prince* on the desk and quotes it in one-to-ones. Within a year the two strongest people on the team have left. The stories about why have reached the next employer before the manager has.'},{kind:'idea',text:'His prince could not resign, and his subjects could not leave. Almost everyone you lead can leave, and will tell others why. Where exit and reputation exist, the strategies that work are close to the opposite of his.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO GAMES',left:{title:'His world',items:['No exit, no appeal','Opponents who kill','Defeat is final']},right:{title:'A workplace',items:['People resign','Disputes go up a level','Reputations follow you','The same people, again and again']},note:'Under his conditions, pre-emption and early severity were proportionate. Under yours they are expensive.'},{kind:'term',word:'Repeated game',meaning:'A situation in which the same people deal with each other again and again. Cheating may win one round, but it is remembered in the next. So trust can become the stronger strategy.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'High walls, but an open door. The coat on the peg belongs to someone who can take it, walk out, and tell people why.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What does transfer',text:'His descriptive half: how people behave under pressure, how obligations decay, how a favour is remembered and a debt resented. Those observations last. The prescriptions were written for a game you are not playing. Following them produces someone nobody will work with twice.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before borrowing a tactic from a ruthless setting, ask one question: can the people it will be used on walk away?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Which part of *The Prince* is most likely to survive the move into a modern team?',options:['Its close attention to how people behave under pressure','Its advice to remove possible rivals before they grow strong','Its preference for fear over goodwill to keep people in line','Its advice to concentrate harsh measures early in a new role'],answer:0,why:'The observations transfer. Prescriptions built for a world without exit backfire where people can leave and talk.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE BUSINESS SHELF',belief:'*The Prince* is a management handbook whose tactics work in any organisation.',reality:'Its advice is fitted to a ruler with no exit and no appeal, facing opponents who kill. Most of it does not transfer, and the reason is structural before it is moral.'},{kind:'idea',text:'His prescriptions assume a closed world: subjects who cannot leave, a ruler who cannot resign, defeat that is final. Where people can exit and reputations travel, zero-sum contests become repeated games, and his prescriptions perform badly.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it was proportionate then',text:'Italy in 1513 was a patchwork of small states, foreign armies and violent successions. A prince who lost did not move to another post; he was exiled or killed. Under those conditions pre-emption, distrust of goodwill and early severity were responses to real stakes.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW EXIT CHANGES THE GAME',nodes:['A harsh tactic','People can leave','They tell others why','Talent and trust drain away'],note:'Remove the exit and the second step disappears. That is the world he wrote for.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'His fortress, with one thing added: a door. Every tactic that relied on the walls now has to reckon with the coat on that peg.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What survives',text:'The descriptive half: how people behave under pressure, how obligations fade, how favours are remembered and debts resented. Read as an account of how power behaves, it remains sharp. It is the prescriptions that were built for a game most readers are not playing.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Exit is not always real. Where people cannot afford to leave, where one employer dominates a town, or where complaints have nowhere to go, the old conditions return, and so does the logic. That is a reason to watch for closed systems, not to use the tactics in them. And the test cuts one way only: that a tactic would work does not make it right.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'Where would his prescriptions fit most closely today?',options:['A setting where people cannot leave and have no one to appeal to','A start-up whose talented staff could each walk into another job','A volunteer group that anyone may quit at any time','A firm in a small industry where everyone talks to everyone'],answer:0,why:'The test is exit and appeal. Remove them and you rebuild his world, which is a warning sign rather than an opportunity.'}]}},'the-prince_5':{pictureCaption:'The river is quiet and nothing is wrong. That is exactly when this embankment is being built.',bridge:'After the tactics, the idea in the book that travels best: not cruelty, but timing.',basics:{title:'Build the bank before the flood',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A RIVER BY A TOWN',prompt:'When is the best time to build a bank to hold back a flood?',options:['While the flood is coming','When the river is calm','After the flood, to fix things'],answer:1,reveal:'Once the water is rising, it is too late to build. The only time to build is when the river is calm and nobody feels worried.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Machiavelli said luck decides about half of what happens to us. The other half is ours. Much of what looks like luck was decided by what we got ready before trouble came.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The river is calm. Nothing is wrong. That is why this is the right time to dig.'},{kind:'explain',title:'His picture for luck',text:'Machiavelli, who wrote about rulers 500 years ago, said luck is like a river in flood. You can\u2019t stop it. But a bank built in calm weather decides how much it wrecks.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'TEST WEEK',text:'Your test is in three weeks. Right now nothing feels urgent, so it is easy to put off. But the night before, you cannot learn three weeks of work. The calm weeks were your time to build the bank.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of one thing you can see coming, like a test or a match. Do one small thing for it this week, while it still feels far away.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your bike tyre is getting a bit soft. The big ride is on Saturday. What fits this idea?',options:['Pump it up now, while there is time','Wait and see if it goes flat','Hope you have good luck on Saturday'],answer:0,why:'The calm days are when fixing is easy. On Saturday it may be too late.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'EIGHT MONTHS OUT',text:'You can see the problem coming: a contract that ends in eight months, a key person who has hinted at leaving. Nothing is wrong yet, so nothing is being built. Every week it is easier to put off.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Fortune governs about half of what happens; the other half is ours to direct. Much of what looks like luck when the flood comes was decided by preparation made earlier, before there seemed any reason for it.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'HIS IMAGE',picture:!0,text:'He compares fortune to a river in flood that sweeps everything away. Nobody can stop it once it rises. But in calm weather people can build banks and dykes, so that when the water comes it is channelled and does less damage.'},{kind:'explain',title:'An argument about timing, not control',text:'You cannot influence a flood while it is happening. You could have influenced what it did to you months earlier, when nothing was wrong. The difficulty is that calm periods are exactly when preparation feels least necessary and is easiest to defer.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'HIS ESTIMATE',low:'All luck: nothing you do matters',high:'All control: you can plan for everything',note:'He chose half, deliberately unheroic. Enough that preparing matters; not so much that it guarantees anything.'},{kind:'try',text:'Name one embankment you could build this month for a flood you can already see coming. Put the first hour of it in your diary.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'The only engineer who understands your billing system has mentioned job hunting. Things are calm. What is the embankment?',options:['Have someone else learn the system now','Wait until a resignation actually arrives','Offer a bonus once they hand in notice','Accept that losing them would be bad luck'],answer:0,why:'The calm is the only time this is cheap. Once the flood starts, every option costs more.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'FLORENCE, 1512',text:'Machiavelli knew a flood when he saw one. In 1512 a Spanish army forced the Florentine republic to fall, and the Medici returned. After fourteen years in its service he lost his post and was soon imprisoned. In the book he wrote next, a chapter asks how much of life fortune decides.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Fortune is arbiter of roughly half our actions and leaves the other half to us. Her power shows where no preparation was made, so the half that is ours is exercised early, in calm weather.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The river and the dykes',text:'His image is a river in flood that destroys everything in its path. While it is calm, he says, people can build dykes and embankments so that the next flood runs in a channel. He turns it on Italy: a country with no such banks, unlike Germany, Spain and France.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Flat water, an empty bank, one person with a spade. The preparation happens when there is no visible reason for it.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHAT HALF RULES OUT',left:{title:'Fatalism',items:['Fortune decides everything','Preparation is pointless']},right:{title:'Hubris',items:['Skill decides everything','Bad outcomes prove bad planning']},note:'Half is his correction to both. Preparation matters, and still guarantees nothing.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The same chapter ends on a harsher image: fortune as a woman who favours the bold and must be beaten into submission. It is a period trope and plainly ugly, and it pulls towards recklessness, away from the patience of the dykes. Preparation has costs too: you can build for floods that never come and starve the present.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which flood can you already see coming? What would an embankment for it look like, and why has it not been built?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A firm came through a crisis well and calls it luck. What would Machiavelli ask first?',options:['What had been built before the crisis arrived','Whether the crisis could have been predicted exactly','How much of the outcome came down to pure chance','Whether the firm deserved its good fortune'],answer:0,why:'In his account fortune shows her power where nothing was prepared. Coming through well is usually evidence of earlier embankments.'}]}},'the-prince_6':{pictureCaption:'The gate stands open and the sentry post is empty. The hired guard\u2019s cloak lies where he dropped it on his way out.',bridge:'Idea 5 was about building before the flood. This one asks whose strength you build with: your own, or someone else\u2019s.',basics:{title:'Whose ball is it?',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'AT THE PARK',text:'Every game at the park uses Sam\u2019s ball. When Sam\u2019s side starts losing, Sam picks up the ball and goes home. The game is over. Nobody else gets a say.'},{kind:'idea',text:'If you depend on something that belongs to someone else, they are in control. Machiavelli said a ruler needs his own army, not hired or borrowed soldiers.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Two kinds of help he did not trust',text:'In Machiavelli\u2019s Italy, rulers often hired soldiers for pay. But pay is not enough to make you risk your life, so they often ran. Other rulers borrowed a friend\u2019s army. That army obeyed its real owner.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The hired guard has gone. His cloak lies in the gate, and his post is empty. He was only there for the pay.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'HIRED OR BORROWED',left:{title:'Hired help',items:['Stays while the pay is good','Leaves when it gets hard']},right:{title:'Borrowed help',items:['Works well for you','But obeys someone else']},note:'Either way, someone else decides when it stops.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of a group you are in. What does it need to keep going? Who owns that thing?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your class play uses lights that belong to another school. What is the risk, by this idea?',options:['The other school decides if you can use them','The lights might not be bright enough','Your class will not learn to act'],answer:0,why:'The play depends on something you don\u2019t control. Whoever owns it holds the switch.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE ONLY COPY',text:'A small firm sells software built entirely on one supplier\u2019s platform. The platform is excellent. Then the supplier doubles its prices, and the firm discovers that it has no way to leave.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Rented strength serves its owner, not you. Hired soldiers have no reason to die for you; borrowed allies win for you and then own you. What you depend on absolutely, you should hold yourself.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Mercenaries (hired)',items:['Loyal to their pay','No reason to die for you','Leave when it turns hard']},right:{title:'Auxiliaries (borrowed)',items:['Disciplined and loyal','To someone else','If they win, you are theirs']},note:'He thought the second danger was the worse one.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why competence is the trap',text:'A capability rented from another company is good at its job and aligned with its owner\u2019s interests. It performs, and that is how dependence grows: every success makes it harder to replace. The danger is not that it fails you. It is that it works.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The post is empty and the gate open. A hired guard\u2019s cloak lies where he dropped it: he stayed exactly as long as the pay was worth it.'},{kind:'try',text:'List what your work depends on absolutely. Against each item ask: what happens to me if this supplier changes its terms?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Which arrangement does his warning about borrowed troops fit best?',options:['A partner holds your whole customer list on its own system','A contractor repaints your office over one weekend','A freelancer designs a single advert for a campaign','A courier firm delivers your parcels across the country'],answer:0,why:'The partner is competent, loyal to itself, and holds the one thing you cannot lose. That is borrowed force.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'TWO KINDS OF BORROWED ARMS',word:'Auxiliaries',meaning:'Troops lent to you by another power, still under their own ruler\u2019s command. Mercenaries are hired; auxiliaries are borrowed. Machiavelli distrusts both, and the second more.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A state resting on hired or borrowed arms is never secure. Mercenaries fail you because pay is not a reason to die; auxiliaries fail you even when they win, because then you are their prisoner.'},{kind:'explain',title:'His charge sheet',text:'Mercenaries, he says, are disunited, ambitious, undisciplined and faithless, and he blames Italy\u2019s ruin on reliance on them. The cause is structural: nothing keeps them in the field but their pay. Auxiliaries are worse. They are united and disciplined, and obey someone else.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'HIS OWN EXAMPLE',text:'Pope Julius II, disappointed by his mercenaries at Ferrara, turned to troops from the king of Spain. They were beaten at Ravenna. He escaped dependence on his enemies and his allies alike only because Swiss troops arrived and drove the French out. Machiavelli calls that luck, not policy.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'An open gate, an empty sentry box, a dropped cloak. The paid defender left when staying cost more than he was paid.'},{kind:'limits',text:'He practised what he preached. As a Florentine official he built a citizen militia, which helped bring Pisa back under Florence in 1509 and was routed at Prato in 1512, after which the republic fell. Own arms are not automatically good arms. Translated to business the rule overreaches: renting most things is sensible. The real test is which dependencies could end you.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Hired help leaves when it gets hard.','Borrowed help can win, and then own you.','Hold yourself what you cannot afford to lose.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE SHARPER CASE',prompt:'Why does he rank auxiliaries as more dangerous than mercenaries?',options:['Their strength and discipline serve another master','They are less skilled than soldiers hired for pay','They cost more to keep through a long war','They refuse to fight once the pay runs out'],answer:0,why:'Mercenaries are weak in ways you can see. Auxiliaries are strong, and the strength belongs to someone else, so winning with them still leaves you dependent.'}]}},'the-prince_7':{pictureCaption:'Shears held ready, not yet used, beside a rose bush where one diseased stem is spreading. The ability to cut must exist; necessity decides its use.',bridge:'Idea 1 introduced his split between how rulers live and how they ought to. This is the claim he built on it, and his most dangerous.',basics:{title:'When always being soft goes wrong',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE REFEREE',prompt:'A referee never gives a card, because he wants to be kind. What happens to the game?',options:['Everyone plays fairly','The rough players take over','Nothing changes'],answer:1,reveal:'The rough players learn they can do anything. The players who keep the rules get hurt. His kindness made the game less safe.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Machiavelli said a ruler who is always gentle, whatever others do, can be ruined by people who are not. So, he said, a ruler must be able to do hard things when there is truly no other way.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What he saw',text:'Machiavelli watched rulers in Italy 500 years ago. He saw that a soft ruler who lets trouble grow can cause more harm than a firm one. Sometimes, he thought, a ruler has to be hard to protect others.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The shears are ready but not yet used. A sick stem is spreading into the healthy ones. Cut only when there is no other way to save the plant.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'So he means it is fine to be mean.',reality:'No. He did not say be bad. He said a ruler must be able to do hard things, and do them only when forced. People often take his idea as an excuse to be mean. That is the danger in it.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO KINDS OF RULE',left:{title:'Rules you never break',items:['Never hurt someone on purpose','Never lie to get your way']},right:{title:'Habits you can change',items:['Always letting things slide','Never saying no']},note:'Knowing which is which keeps you from being pushed around.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A class helper never tells anyone off, even when kids are being unkind. What would Machiavelli notice?',options:['Being soft lets the unkind kids win','The helper is the best kind of leader','The helper should be harsh to everyone'],answer:0,why:'He saw that softness which lets trouble grow can hurt more people. That does not mean being harsh to everyone.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE MANAGER WHO NEVER SAYS NO',text:'A manager prides herself on never confronting anyone. One team member bullies the others, and she hopes it will pass. Within a year the good people have left. Her kindness has cost everyone except the bully.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A fixed commitment to acting well, whatever the other side does, is a strategy. It has a known failure mode. Machiavelli says a ruler must learn how not to be good, and use it or not as necessity requires.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What he does and does not say',text:'He does not say be bad. He says the ability must exist and be ready for use. That is because someone who cannot do a hard act will be destroyed by people who are not so constrained. His examples: generosity that empties the treasury, mercy that lets disorder spread.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW MERCY TURNS CRUEL',nodes:['Disorder is tolerated','It spreads','More people are harmed','Harsher measures are needed later'],note:'His claim: mercy that permits disorder can be crueller than severity that prevents it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The diseased stem is already spreading into the healthy ones. The shears are ready but closed. Leave it, and more of the bush will need cutting later.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'This licenses whatever gets results.',reality:'His safeguard is necessity: the harsh act must be forced by the situation, not chosen for convenience. It is a weak safeguard. Necessity is easy to claim. That is why this is the sentence most often borrowed as an excuse.'},{kind:'try',text:'Write down two of your principles. Mark each one absolute or default. Being unclear about which is which is what gets exploited.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Which case comes closest to what he means by necessity?',options:['Removing a colleague who keeps endangering others after every warning','Cutting a rival out of a meeting because it saves some time','Shouting at the team to make clear who is in charge','Overruling everyone because you are sure you are right'],answer:0,why:'The hard act is forced by the situation and protects others. The rest are convenience dressed as necessity.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'CHAPTER FIFTEEN',text:'Having said he will write about how rulers actually live, Machiavelli draws the conclusion. A man who wants to act entirely as a good man among so many who are not will come to ruin. So a prince who means to keep his position must learn to be able not to be good, and use it or not as necessity requires.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The private virtues and the duties of office genuinely conflict. A ruler incapable of a harsh act will be destroyed by those who are not, so the capability must exist, governed by necessity rather than preference.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'THE CONFLICT HE NAMES',left:{title:'Private virtue',items:['Generosity','Mercy']},right:{title:'Its cost in office, he argues',items:['An empty treasury, then heavy taxes','Disorder that harms more people']},note:'Some things that look like virtues lead to ruin, he says, and some that look like vices bring security.'},{kind:'term',word:'Necessity',meaning:'The constraint of the situation, and the condition he sets for departing from the good. The harsh act must be forced on the ruler, not chosen because it is convenient.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Shears held ready beside a spreading diseased stem: the argument at its most persuasive, because here the disease is plain to see. In politics, deciding which stem is diseased is the real dispute.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it is his most serious claim',text:'Philosophers still argue about the problem he named, now often called the problem of dirty hands: whether office can require what would be wrong in private life. Machiavelli was among the first to state it without softening, and without pretending the conflict away.'},{kind:'limits',text:'It is also his most dangerous sentence, because it is the one most easily borrowed to excuse whatever someone wanted to do anyway. Necessity is judged by the person who gains from claiming it, and he offers no outside check. The honest reading takes the conflict seriously and treats every claim of necessity as suspect until it is shown.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which of your principles are absolute, and which are defaults you have never had to test? What would make you break a default?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which appeal to this idea is a misuse?',options:['A leader cites necessity for a harsh step that mainly benefits himself','A judge accepts that mercy to one offender may endanger others','A historian explains a ruler\u2019s severity as a response to disorder','A reader concludes that private virtue and office can conflict'],answer:0,why:'The safeguard is that necessity is forced by the situation. When the person claiming it is the one who gains, the claim is doing the work of convenience.'}]}},'the-prince_8':{pictureCaption:'The fortress on the ridge is dark. The lit window is down among the houses, close enough to see trouble while it is small.',bridge:'Idea 6 said hold your own strength. This one says where to stand: close enough to see.',basics:{title:'Stay close and see trouble early',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE CLASS PLANTS',text:'You are in charge of the class plants. If you look at them every morning, you spot one yellow leaf and give it water. If you check once a month, you find a dead plant.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Machiavelli said a ruler who takes a new land should go and live there. Up close, you see small problems while they are easy to fix. From far away, you hear about them when they are big.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The castle on the hill is dark. The lit window is in a house down in the town. That ruler lives where the people are.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why living there beat a castle',text:'He compared it with other plans. Keeping soldiers in a castle cost a lot, and people hated them. A ruler living in the town was harder for his officials to cheat. People could bring their problems straight to him.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A GOOD COACH',text:'A coach who watches every practice sees the player who is limping. A coach who only reads the scores finds out when that player can\u2019t play at all.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one thing you look after: a pet, a plant, a game you are building. Check on it up close every day this week. Notice what you catch early.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A teacher sits with each group instead of staying at her desk. What does she gain?',options:['She sees problems while they are small','She makes the groups work faster','She shows everyone she is the boss'],answer:0,why:'Being close lets you see trouble early, when it is still easy to fix.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'A leader turning up in person is a gesture, a way to show that they care.',reality:'For Machiavelli presence is an information channel. The ruler who lives there sees trouble as it starts; the one who stays away hears of it when it is past fixing.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE INHERITED TEAM',text:'You take over a team in another city. You read the weekly reports, which are fine, until the one that says a key client has gone. Everyone in that office saw it coming for months.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Distance does not just delay news of problems; it filters it, removing the small early signals that were cheap to act on. So for anything newly yours, the first remedy is to be there.'},{kind:'steps',title:'His remedies, best first',steps:['Go and live there yourself.','Plant a few settlers, which costs little.','Station troops, which is expensive and resented, as the last resort.'],note:'Living there, he says, you see disorders as they spring up. Away, you hear of them when they are past remedy.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why presence is information',text:'A report arrives once something is reportable, which is usually after it was cheap to fix. Being nearby picks up what no report contains: the tone in a room, the question nobody asks. He also notes that a ruler in residence is harder for officials to rob, and easier for people to reach.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The garrison on the ridge is dark and shuttered. The ruler\u2019s window is lit among the houses, where small troubles can still be seen.'},{kind:'try',text:'For anything newly your responsibility, spend the first month physically close to it. Sit with the team rather than reading about it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A director buys a small company and manages it through monthly dashboards. What is he most likely to miss?',options:['The small early signs of trouble','The overall financial performance','The headcount and hiring figures','The big decisions needing approval'],answer:0,why:'Dashboards report what has become measurable. The cheap signals come before that, and only being close catches them.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'THE SULTAN IN GREECE',text:'In his chapter on mixed principalities, Machiavelli turns to conquered lands of a different language and custom, the hardest kind to hold. His model for the best remedy is the Ottoman sultan, who went to live in the Greek lands he had taken. Without that, Machiavelli says, he could not have kept them.'},{kind:'idea',text:'For territory newly taken, the ruler should live there. Presence shortens the path from a disorder to its remedy, protects subjects from officials and gives them access to the ruler. Settlers are second best; garrisons are worst.'},{kind:'term',word:'Latency',meaning:'The delay between something happening and the person able to act hearing of it. Presence is a channel with far lower latency than any report.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Distance as a filter',text:'Living there you see disorders as they spring up and can remedy them; away, you hear of them when they are great and beyond remedy. Distance does more than delay. It filters out exactly the small early signals that were cheap to act on, and passes on only what has become large enough to report.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A dark fortress above, one lit window below. The resident ruler and the garrison are his first and last remedies.'},{kind:'limits',text:'His second remedy is cheap for a reason. Settlers cost little because they take land and houses from a few existing inhabitants, who, poor and scattered, can do no harm. That is dispossession described as economy. And presence has its own failure: an owner who hovers can crowd out the people closer to the work.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Distance filters out the cheap early signals.','Presence first, settlers second, garrisons last.','Read the cheapness of settlers for what it was.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'The new head of a charity takes over a distant regional office. Which plan best follows his ranking?',options:['Work from the regional office for the first months','Send a trusted deputy to report back every week','Install a compliance team to monitor everything','Require detailed written reports every Friday'],answer:0,why:'Presence comes first. A few trusted people sent there are second best; a monitoring force is his last and most resented option.'}]}},'the-prince_9':{pictureCaption:'An empty throne with two plain chairs drawn close beside it, and the far doors shut. A few people near enough to speak plainly.',bridge:'Idea 8 put the ruler close to the trouble. This one asks whether anyone close will tell him about it.',basics:{title:'Who tells the leader the truth?',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE GROUP PROJECT',text:'Maya is in charge of a group project. She gets cross when anyone points out a mistake. Soon nobody does. The project looks fine to her, right up until the teacher marks it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'People in charge often hear only good news, because others are scared to bring bad news. Machiavelli said a ruler should pick a few people and let them tell the truth without getting in trouble.'},{kind:'explain',title:'A word to know',text:'Rulers 500 years ago were surrounded by people who flattered them. To flatter means to say nice things that are not true, just to please someone. Machiavelli saw that this left rulers blind.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two plain chairs pulled up close to the big chair. They are for the few people allowed to say what they really think.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW IT GOES WRONG',nodes:['Leader gets cross at bad news','People stop saying it','Leader thinks all is fine','A big surprise later']},{kind:'try',text:'Ask one friend to tell you honestly what could be better about something you made. Then just say thanks.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A team captain never hears about problems. By this idea, what should you check first?',options:['How the captain reacts to bad news','Whether the team is lazy','Whether the problems are real'],answer:0,why:'If people are scared to speak, they stay quiet. The silence tells you about the captain.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE BIND',prompt:'You lead a large team. Which policy gets you the most truth?',options:['Let anyone tell you anything, any time','Keep your distance so only big issues reach you','Name a few people whose job is to tell you'],answer:2,reveal:'Open access to everyone costs you respect, he argues; keeping your distance costs you the truth. His answer is a narrow, deliberate channel.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Everyone has a reason to flatter you, and the obvious defence, being hard to approach, leaves you uninformed. Choose a few people, require the truth from them on what you ask, and make speaking freely cost them nothing.'},{kind:'steps',title:'His procedure',steps:['Choose a small number of wise advisers.','Give them, and only them, licence to speak candidly.','Ask about specific matters, and listen patiently.','Decide alone, then hold to the decision.'],note:'He adds that a ruler should show anger if he learns that someone held back the truth when asked.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The detail most people miss',text:'He holds the ruler responsible for the quality of advice. A wise prince can be well advised; an unwise one cannot tell good counsel from bad, and will be steered by whoever advises most confidently. Good advice comes from the prince\u2019s judgement, not the other way round.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two chairs pulled close to an empty throne, the doors to the hall shut. A small, private channel for the truth.'},{kind:'try',text:'Name the two people whose job it is to tell you when you are wrong. Tell them it is the job. Then take it well, once, where others can see.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A director notices that no bad news has reached her in six months. What does this idea suggest?',options:['The silence is a fact about how she receives news','The organisation is probably running unusually well','Her team is hiding problems out of plain dishonesty','She should invite every employee to email her directly'],answer:0,why:'In his account the flow of bad news is an output of the leader\u2019s behaviour. Opening the door to everyone is the other failure he warns against.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'THE BIND',left:{title:'Everyone may speak freely',items:['The truth reaches you','Respect for you falls']},right:{title:'No one may',items:['Respect stays intact','The truth stops arriving']},note:'His twenty-third chapter treats flattery as a danger every court produces, not a flaw in particular courtiers.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The defence against flattery is a narrow channel: a few chosen advisers, licensed to speak truthfully on what they are asked, at no cost to themselves. And the quality of advice depends on the prince, not the prince on the advice.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The inversion',text:'The usual view is that a ruler is wise because he has good advisers. Machiavelli reverses it. An unwise prince cannot tell good advice from bad. With several advisers he cannot reconcile them; with one, that one will soon take his state. Good counsel is born of the prince\u2019s own prudence.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two chairs drawn up to the throne, the doors shut on the hall. The channel is narrow on purpose.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What it implies for anyone senior',text:'If you are not being told things, that is a fact about you. The flow of bad news is an output of your own behaviour: how you reacted last time, who saw it, what it cost them. Redesigning the channel without changing the reaction changes nothing.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The design leaves the ruler in charge of what may be discussed. Advisers speak only on what they are asked, so the most important bad news, the question nobody thought to ask, has no way in. A small fixed circle can harden into a court of its own. And the chapter is advice to an autocrat on managing information, not a case for accountability.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Who in your working life is licensed to tell you that you are wrong? When did one of them last do it, and how did you respond?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE WEAK POINT',prompt:'What is the most serious weakness in his remedy?',options:['Advisers speak only when asked, so unasked questions stay hidden','Too many people are given licence to speak freely to the prince','The prince is bound to follow whatever the majority advises','Advisers are given every reason to flatter him even more'],answer:0,why:'He limits candour to what the prince asks about. The dangerous news is often the thing nobody knew to ask.'}]}}}},3024,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'the-quiet-hours_1':{pictureCaption:'One lit doorway, and hands reaching for it from every side. The quiet hour is wanted by everything, so it has to be claimed first.',basics:{title:'Claim your quiet time first',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'SATURDAY, SEVEN O\u2019CLOCK',text:'You wake early on Saturday. The house is quiet. You mean to work on your drawing. But you check a game first. Then your brother wakes up. Then it is breakfast, then noise. The quiet time has gone.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The quiet time at the start of the day is the time everything wants. If you don\u2019t claim it first, something else will take it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One door is lit up. Hands reach for it from every side. Your quiet time is like that door. Lots of things want it.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Spare time',items:['What is left after your jobs','You can use it any time']},right:{title:'Wanted time',items:['Lots of things try to grab it','You have to claim it first']},note:'Early morning feels like spare time. It is really wanted time.'},{kind:'explain',title:'So what works?',text:'Trying harder is not the trick. Decide the night before: *at seven I will draw.* Start before you look at anything else. And tell your family, so they know that time is taken.'},{kind:'try',text:'Tonight, pick what your first half hour tomorrow is for. Say it out loud to someone at home.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You want to read your new book before school, while it is quiet. What makes it most likely to happen?',options:['Decide the night before, and read before the TV goes on','Hope you feel like it when you wake up','Read only if nothing else comes up first'],answer:0,why:'Quiet time gets grabbed fast. Claiming it ahead of time is what keeps it.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'The early morning is free time: whatever is left before the day properly starts.',reality:'It is the only uncontested block in the day, and the contest starts the moment anyone else is awake. Treating it as free is exactly what loses it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The first two hours are the only reliably uninterrupted ones, which is why everything else tries to occupy them. Keeping them is not a scheduling preference. It is a refusal, repeated daily, that other people notice.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Free time',items:['What remains after obligations','Used when you get round to it','Defended by intending to']},right:{title:'Contested time',items:['Claimed before the claims arrive','Scheduled in advance','Started before the first message']}},{kind:'explain',title:'It is rarely willpower',text:'The essay\u2019s observation is that the person who uses the hour and the person who loses it differ less in willpower than in one thing: whether the hour was allocated before it began. An unallocated hour tends to go to the first email, message or request that reaches it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One lit doorway, and hands reaching for it from every side. The quiet hour is wanted by everything, so it has to be claimed first.'},{kind:'try',text:'Decide what tomorrow\u2019s first two hours are for, then say it out loud to the people it affects.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You keep losing your early hour to messages. Which change applies the idea?',options:['Book the hour the night before and start before opening any inbox','Try harder to ignore the messages as they come in','Get up earlier so that there is more free time to lose','Spend the hour on whatever looks most urgent that day'],answer:0,why:'Contested time is kept by claiming it before the claims arrive, not by resisting them once they have.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE ESSAY\u2019S OPENING MOVE',text:'The Quiet Hours is a short essay about keeping the first two hours of the day for your own work: what has to be given up, who pays, and how to make the claim small enough to hold. It begins by changing what kind of time the morning is.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The early morning is not free time but contested time: the one block reliably without interruption, which other demands will fill unless it is claimed before they arrive.'},{kind:'term',word:'Contested time',meaning:'Time that other claims will fill unless you claim it first. Free time is what remains after obligations. Contested time has to be taken before the obligations reach it.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW AN HOUR IS LOST',nodes:['The block is uninterrupted','So every demand wants it','Others wake and inputs start','The unclaimed hour goes to the first arrival']},{kind:'explain',title:'How it is defended',text:'Two practical moves follow. The block is protected by scheduling it, not by intending to use it, and by starting before the first outside input rather than after. Because this is a refusal repeated every day, it is also social: other people notice, which is where idea 3 picks up.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Hands reach in from every side towards the one lit door. Nothing in the picture is free. Everything in it is wanted.'},{kind:'limits',text:'That the first two hours are the only reliably quiet ones fits a common office pattern, not every life. Parents of small children, shift workers and people working across time zones may find their uncontested block late at night, or nowhere. And the essay\u2019s claim that keeping the hour is rarely about willpower is an observation, not a finding.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A nurse working night shifts wants to use the idea. What carries over?',options:['Find her own reliably quiet block and claim it before demands arrive','Nothing, since the idea only applies to the early morning','Wake two hours before every shift, whatever time that is','Treat her days off as free time to fill as they come'],answer:0,why:'The morning is the essay\u2019s case, not the principle. The principle is that quiet time is contested, so it must be claimed in advance.'}]}},'the-quiet-hours_2':{pictureCaption:'A phone shut in a box on the hall table, the door closed behind it. Nothing here needs deciding, and that is the point.',bridge:'Idea 1 said the morning must be claimed. This one is about what keeps the claim from wearing away.',basics:{title:'Make it boring and it sticks',beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'To keep a good habit, you need lots of willpower.',reality:'Willpower means deciding again every day. And every time you decide, you might say *not today*. A dull rule you never have to think about works better.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'PIANO, SOMETIME',text:'Some days you practise piano at four. Some days at six. Some days after TV. Each day you ask, \u201cShall I do it now?\u201d Quite often the answer is *later*. Then it is bedtime.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A habit lasts when it is boring. Same time, same place, same first step. Then there is nothing to decide, so there is nothing to argue about.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The phone is in the box. The door is shut. There is nothing to choose here. That is why it works.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'MIA\u2019S RULE',text:'Mia gets home from school. Her phone goes in a box in the hall. She plays piano for twenty minutes. Every day, the same. It is boring. But she never has to decide, so it always happens.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one thing you want to do every day. Give it a fixed time and a fixed place. Write the rule down in one short line.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You want to read every night. Which plan is most likely to last?',options:['Read in bed at eight, every night','Read whenever you feel like it','Read a lot when you have a free day'],answer:0,why:'A fixed time leaves nothing to decide. The others ask you to choose each day, and some days you will choose not now.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'BY THURSDAY',text:'You plan to start each morning with an hour of writing. Monday you check email first, just quickly. Tuesday there is an early call. By Thursday, each morning begins with a debate about whether today counts.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Willpower is not what keeps a morning. A rule so dull it needs no decision does: no meetings before ten, phone in another room, the work chosen the night before. The dullness is the mechanism.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why dull works',text:'A morning practice survives on sameness: same time, same place, same first action. Every variation brings back a decision, and each decision is another place for the morning to slip away. A rule you have to think about is a rule you will eventually negotiate with.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'A resolve',items:['\u201cI\u2019ll write first thing\u201d','Decided again every morning','Loses to whatever is easiest']},right:{title:'An arrangement',items:['No meetings before ten','Decided once','Needs no decision at eight']}},{kind:'picture',caption:'A phone shut in a box on the hall table, the door closed behind it. Nothing here needs deciding, and that is the point.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The honest trade',text:'A boring routine produces consistent output and no stories. That is the right trade for anything you want to still be doing in a year, and the wrong one for anything you want to enjoy describing.'},{kind:'try',text:'Replace one morning resolution with an arrangement: a fixed start time, a fixed place, or the phone left in another room.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Your morning writing keeps slipping. Which change fits the idea best?',options:['Start at 7:30 at the kitchen table every day, phone upstairs','Each morning, decide what the day most needs and do that','Reward yourself whenever you manage a full writing hour','Try a new location each week to keep things fresh'],answer:0,why:'Only the fixed time and place removes the decision. The others keep asking the morning to choose, which is where it slips.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO KINDS OF MORNING',left:{title:'The interesting version',items:['Variety','Responds to what the day needs','Judgement every morning']},right:{title:'The boring version',items:['Same time, place, first action','Ignores the day\u2019s mood','No judgement needed']},note:'The essay argues the left column, however appealing, tends to degrade into whatever is easiest.'},{kind:'idea',text:'What keeps a morning is an arrangement dull enough that keeping it needs no decision. The dullness is the mechanism, because a rule you must think about is a rule you will negotiate with.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The mechanism',text:'Variation reintroduces decisions, and each decision is a small renegotiation of the rule: another place for the morning to slip away, and a chance for the easy option to win. Its examples remove the decision entirely: no meetings before ten, the phone in another room, the work chosen the night before.'},{kind:'keyline',text:'Every rule you have to think about is a rule that is open to negotiation.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The phone is in a box, the box is on a table, the door is shut. A dull arrangement, and nothing left to argue with.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The trade the essay admits',text:'Being boring is the requirement people resist most, and the essay does not pretend otherwise. The boring routine buys consistent output and costs you the stories. It is the right trade for something you want to be doing in a year, the wrong one for something you want to enjoy describing.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Some lives cannot be made this regular: a parent of a sick child, a job on call, a week of travel. A rigid rule can also outlast its purpose and carry on from habit alone. And the claim that each decision is where a morning slips is asserted, not shown. The argument holds best for steady work that rewards repetition.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'PREDICT IT',prompt:'By the essay\u2019s argument, which change would most weaken a working morning routine?',options:['Choosing each day\u2019s start time according to how you feel','Leaving the phone in another room every single morning','Choosing the next day\u2019s task before you stop for the evening','Keeping every meeting out of the diary until ten'],answer:0,why:'It puts a decision back into the morning every day. The other three remove one.'}]}},'the-quiet-hours_3':{pictureCaption:'Someone waits outside a shut door with a question and nowhere to put it. The quiet hour behind that door is partly paid for out here.',bridge:'Idea 1 said protecting the morning is a refusal people notice. This one is about who pays for it.',basics:{title:'Your quiet time costs someone',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'DOOR SHUT, GUITAR OUT',prompt:'You shut your door to practise guitar for an hour. Who pays for that hour?',options:['Nobody','Only you','Someone who wanted you during it'],answer:2,reveal:'Your little brother wanted help with his model. Your mum wanted a hand with dinner. They had to wait. Your quiet hour came partly from their time.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When you keep time just for yourself, someone else has to wait. That can be fine. But be honest about it, and tell them when you will be free.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'He has a question. The door is shut. He has to wait. Somebody\u2019s quiet time is costing him something.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'BUSY TILL TEN',text:'You tell your brother: *I\u2019m practising till ten. After that I\u2019m all yours.* Now he knows when. He can do something else first. Waiting is much easier when you know how long it will be.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why tell them?',text:'If they don\u2019t know, they knock again and again. Or they feel left out. If they know the time, they can plan. And your quiet time stays quiet.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you need quiet time, tell the people at home the exact time you will be free. Then be free at that time.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You read in your room before school. Your sister keeps knocking to ask things. What does this idea say to do?',options:['Tell her you are free at eight, and stick to it','Lock the door and say nothing at all','Give up reading so she never has to wait'],answer:0,why:'Your reading time does cost her a wait. Saying when it ends makes the wait fair and easy to plan for.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'NINE FIFTEEN',text:'Your eight-to-ten block is protected. At quarter past nine a colleague needs one answer from you before sending a quote to a client. They wait until ten. So does the client.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Protecting a morning has a cost, and other people pay it. Being unavailable means someone waits. The honest response is to say so, and to make the window predictable.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The part advice leaves out',text:'Most advice about deep work talks about the time you gain. The essay\u2019s point is that the time comes from somewhere, usually from other people\u2019s convenience. You may stop apologising for that, or find you cannot. Either way the cost is real and someone else is paying it.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHERE THE HOUR COMES FROM',nodes:['You go unavailable','A question arrives','Someone waits','Their work waits too'],note:'None of this means you should not do it. It means the hour is not free.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Someone waits outside a shut door with a question and nowhere to put it. The quiet hour behind that door is partly paid for out here.'},{kind:'try',text:'Tell the people affected by your quiet hours what those hours cost them, and exactly when they start and end. Then keep the same times every day.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You want to keep 8 to 10 each morning for focused work. Which approach fits the idea?',options:['Tell the team it costs them a wait, give the times, and keep them','Go quiet without notice, since focused work matters more','Stay available all morning so that nobody ever waits','Protect the hours on some days, depending on how busy it is'],answer:0,why:'The cost is real either way. Naming it and making it predictable is what makes it fair to ask others to bear it.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Protecting your focus time is a private choice. It costs nobody but you.',reality:'The essay calls this the part most advice about deep work leaves out. The time has to come from somewhere, and it usually comes from other people\u2019s convenience.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Being unavailable in the morning means someone waits. Whether or not you apologise, the cost is real and somebody else is paying it, so the honest course is to name it and make the window predictable.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the essay concedes',text:'It offers two responses: stop apologising for the wait, or find that you cannot. It treats neither as changing the fact. The essay does not argue that the trade is always worth making. It insists only that it is a trade, and that the other side of it is borne by colleagues.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'An unannounced window',items:['Others cannot plan around it','Each wait is a surprise','Feels like a personal refusal']},right:{title:'A predictable window',items:['Others can route round it','Waits are known in advance','Reads as an arrangement']},note:'Predictability does not remove the cost. It makes it something people can plan for.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The question waits in the corridor. Whatever is being made behind the orange door, part of its price is being paid out here.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The essay is brief here and offers no way to weigh the cost. Who pays depends on position: a senior person\u2019s quiet hour can cost a junior one far more than the reverse. In roles that exist to be available, protecting the morning may simply move work onto colleagues. Saying the cost out loud makes it honest, not fair.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Who waits when you are unavailable? Have you ever told them what your quiet hours cost them?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HARD CASE',prompt:'A manager announces she is offline 8 to 10 daily. Each morning her team sits blocked, waiting for her approvals. What does the idea suggest?',options:['The cost is still real: she should weigh it and look for ways to reduce it','Announcing the window means there is no longer any cost','Her team should protect their own mornings in return','She should drop the window, since any cost to others is unacceptable'],answer:0,why:'Making the window predictable is necessary, not sufficient. The essay asks you to see the cost, which means weighing it when it is large.'}]}},'the-quiet-hours_4':{pictureCaption:'A long fence, thin and gappy, and one short solid section at the very start. That small part is the one that holds.',bridge:'Ideas 2 and 3 made the claim dull and honest. This one makes it small enough to keep.',basics:{title:'A small rule you always keep',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A GARDEN WALL',text:'You could put a thin fence round your whole garden. Or you could build one short, strong wall by the gate. The thin fence gets knocked down in a week. The short wall stays up.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Don\u2019t try to guard your whole day. Pick a small part, like the first half hour, and guard it every single day. A small rule you always keep beats a big one you keep sometimes.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A long thin fence, and one short solid wall at the start. The short wall is the part that will hold.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'NO SCREENS ALL DAY',text:'Ben made a rule: no screens all Saturday. At eleven he played one game. *Well, that\u2019s ruined*, he thought, and played all afternoon. A smaller rule, like reading before any screens, would have held.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why breaking a rule matters',text:'Each time you break a rule, you learn it is not really a rule. So does everyone around you. A small rule you never break stays a real rule.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of a rule you keep breaking. Make it smaller until you could keep it every day. Then stop making it smaller.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You want to practise football skills. Which rule is most likely to last?',options:['Ten minutes of keepy-ups every day after school','Two hours of practice every single day','Practise loads whenever you get the chance'],answer:0,why:'Ten minutes is small enough to keep every day. A rule you keep every day stays a real rule.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TWO RULES',prompt:'Which rule is more likely to still be working in a month?',options:['Protect nine to twelve every day','Protect nine to half past ten every day, no exceptions'],answer:1,reveal:'The big rule breaks early. Something lands at ten, the plan feels ruined, and the rest of the morning is written off. The narrow one fits before most demands can reach you.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Trying to defend entire days usually fails. What survives is a smaller claim: the first block, every day, without exception. A small rule kept perfectly beats a large one kept sometimes.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW A BIG RULE COLLAPSES',nodes:['A rule for nine to twelve','An interruption at ten','The plan feels broken','The rest is written off'],note:'The rule does not bend. It snaps, and then it is gone.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why exceptions are fatal',text:'Exceptions are what teach everyone, you included, that a rule is negotiable. A rule with a large scope collects exceptions fast. A narrow one can be kept perfectly, and a rule that is kept perfectly is never up for discussion.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A long fence, thin and gappy, and one short solid section at the very start. That small part is the one that holds.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the start',text:'The essay suggests the first two hours, or less if two will not hold, can often be defended, because they come before most outside demands reach you. The first block is also often your freshest attention, so a narrow protection captures much of the value. Stretching it further gains less and risks a rule that fails.'},{kind:'try',text:'Shrink your protected time until you can keep it every single day. Then stop shrinking.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Your rule of no meetings all morning has been broken three times this week. What does the idea suggest?',options:['Shrink it to the first hour or two and keep that without exception','Keep the full morning and just enforce it more firmly','Drop the rule, since mornings clearly cannot be protected','Protect whole days instead, so one meeting matters less'],answer:0,why:'A smaller rule that holds beats a larger one that keeps breaking. Each break was teaching people the rule was negotiable.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'scale',kicker:'HOW BIG A CLAIM?',low:'Defend the whole day',high:'Defend one block, perfectly',note:'The essay places the workable rule near the right-hand end: the first block, every day, without exception.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Protecting the whole morning tends to fail; protecting the first block more often holds. The size matters less than whether the rule is ever broken, because exceptions are what teach everyone that a rule is negotiable.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The argument from scope',text:'A rule with a large scope tends to be broken early and then abandoned entirely. An interruption at ten invalidates a plan covering nine to twelve, and the rest is written off. A narrower claim survives contact better. The essay suggests the first two hours, or less if two will not hold, can often be defended, because they come before most outside demands can reach you.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The argument from value',text:'The first block is often the freshest attention you will have, so a narrow protection captures much of what is available. Extending it risks diminishing returns and a rule that fails, which the essay counts as worse than a smaller rule that holds.'},{kind:'keyline',text:'It is the exceptions, not the size, that teach people a rule can be argued with.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The long fence would take constant mending. The short orange section at the start needs none. That is the claim worth making.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Freshest in the morning is true for many people, not all. Some think best later, and for them the right first block may not be first. Two hours is the essay\u2019s rough suggestion, not a measured figure. And shrinking can overshoot: a rule kept perfectly but too short to do real work protects nothing, which is why the advice ends with when to stop.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TOO FAR?',prompt:'Someone keeps a fifteen-minute rule perfectly, but their work needs an hour to get anywhere. What does the idea suggest?',options:['They have shrunk too far: widen it to the smallest block that does the work','The rule is ideal, since it is never broken','Go back to protecting the whole morning instead','Drop the rule, since small rules achieve nothing'],answer:0,why:'The advice is to shrink until the rule can be kept, then stop. A rule too small to hold the work has gone past that point.'}]}},'the-quiet-hours_5':{pictureCaption:'Night at the workbench. The tools are put aside and one job is left out in the light, chosen before anyone went to bed.',bridge:'Idea 2 listed choosing the work the night before as one dull rule. This one explains why the evening matters so much.',basics:{title:'Choose tonight, start fast tomorrow',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'TWO SATURDAYS',text:'First Saturday: you sit down for homework and spend fifteen minutes choosing which bit to do. Second Saturday: you chose last night. The maths sheet is already on the desk. You start straight away.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Decide tonight what you will do first tomorrow. Just one thing, not a list. Then in the morning you can start at once.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The tools are put away for the night. One job is left out in the light. Tomorrow starts right there.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why not choose in the morning?',text:'Choosing takes time. If you choose in the morning, you use up your best, fresh time just picking. So do the picking the night before, and save the fresh time for the job.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE NIGHT BEFORE',nodes:['Pick one job tonight','Go to bed on time','Wake up fresh','Start the job straight away'],note:'Getting up early but going to bed late just makes you tired.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before bed tonight, write one job for tomorrow on a note. Leave it where you will see it first.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You want to start your project first thing on Sunday. What is the best thing to do on Saturday night?',options:['Choose the one part you will start with','Stay up late so you get ahead','Make a long list of every job'],answer:0,why:'One job chosen the night before means no choosing in the morning. A long list puts the choosing back. Staying up late leaves you tired.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'equation',kicker:'WHAT DECIDES A MORNING',parts:['When you stopped','What you decided'],result:'The morning you get',note:'Both happen the evening before.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Choose the next day\u2019s work before finishing the current one. Not a list, one thing. You arrive at the desk already knowing, so the freshest part of the day is not spent choosing.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE FIRST TWENTY MINUTES',text:'You sit down at eight with good intentions. You glance at the inbox to see what is urgent, rewrite the list, reorder it. At twenty past, you start. The best twenty minutes of the day went on deciding.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Two evening decisions',text:'When you stop sets whether the early hour is usable at all, because it sets when you sleep. What you choose to work on, decided the night before, removes the morning\u2019s most expensive activity, deciding, from the time your attention is worth most.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Night at the workbench. The tools are put aside and one job is left out in the light, chosen before anyone went to bed.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where the failure happens',text:'So this is really an evening practice with a morning payoff. The evening is where the failure occurs, and where you can change it. Resolving to get up earlier without moving the evening is a resolution to be more tired, which is not a plan.'},{kind:'try',text:'Write tomorrow\u2019s one thing before you close the laptop tonight.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Your early starts keep producing little. Which change does the idea point to?',options:['Stop at a set time and write down tomorrow\u2019s one task before you do','Set the alarm half an hour earlier to buy more morning','Start each morning by drawing up a careful list of priorities','Push on later in the evening so there is less left for tomorrow'],answer:0,why:'The morning is decided the night before. An earlier alarm without an earlier evening only adds tiredness, and a morning list brings the choosing back.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'WHERE IT GOES WRONG',prompt:'When a protected morning fails, when does the failure usually happen?',options:['At eight, when you sit down to work','The evening before'],answer:1,reveal:'The essay\u2019s answer is the evening. That is when you decided how late to stop and whether to choose the next day\u2019s work. The morning only shows the result.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The morning is largely determined the previous evening, by when you stopped and what you decided. The practice is not really a morning one at all. It is an evening practice with a morning payoff.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE CHAIN, START TO FINISH',nodes:['Stop at a set time','Choose one task, not a list','Sleep enough to use the hour','Arrive already knowing','Fresh attention goes to the work']},{kind:'explain',title:'Why one thing, not a list',text:'A list brings the decision back: the morning opens with choosing between its items. One thing removes deciding, the morning\u2019s most expensive activity, from the period when attention is most valuable. It is idea 2\u2019s logic applied to the night before.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Everything else is put aside in the dark. One job is left in the light, so the morning has nothing to choose.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the reframing matters',text:'It moves attention to where the failure actually occurs and where you can intervene. The common fix, getting up earlier, changes nothing in the evening, so it amounts to a resolution to be more tired.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Evenings are not always yours: children, shifts and other people shape them. A task chosen overnight can be overtaken by something genuinely urgent, so it is a default, not a vow. And the essay states the role of sleep in general terms only; it gives no figures, and none should be read into it.'},{kind:'recap',points:['When you stop sets whether the early hour is usable.','Choosing tonight removes deciding from the freshest time.','An earlier alarm without an earlier evening is not a plan.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'NOT A PLAN',prompt:'Which of these would the essay call a resolution to be more tired?',options:['Setting the alarm an hour earlier while going to bed at the usual time','Stopping work at a fixed time and noting tomorrow\u2019s one task','Going to bed earlier so that the early hour is usable','Leaving tomorrow\u2019s job out on the desk before switching off'],answer:0,why:'An earlier start that leaves the evening untouched only shortens sleep. The other three move the evening, which is where the morning is decided.'}]}}}},3025,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'the-salary-conversation_1':{pictureCaption:'The person behind the desk has turned away from the visitor to argue with the closed door. The decision is behind it, not across the desk.',basics:{title:'The person you ask may be on your side',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE CLASS TRIP',text:'Your class wants a trip to the zoo. You ask your teacher. She says she will see. You feel cross, as if she is the one saying no. But she does not hold the money. The head of the school does.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Often the person you ask is not the one who decides. They have to ask someone else. So treat them as a helper, not an enemy, and give them what they need to ask well.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The man at the desk has turned to the closed door. He is asking whoever is behind it, on the visitor\u2019s behalf.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO ASK',left:{title:'As if she is the enemy',items:['It\u2019s not fair!','Why won\u2019t you let us?']},right:{title:'As if she is a helper',items:['What would help you ask?','We found a cheap day to go']}},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it works',text:'Your teacher likes your class. She would like the trip too. If you give her good reasons, she can take them to the head. You have turned one more person into someone on your side.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you ask for something, ask first: who really decides? Then ask the person in front of you what would help them say yes.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You want your football club to buy new nets. The coach must ask the club. What is the best first move?',options:['Ask the coach what would help him make the case','Tell the coach he is being unfair to the team','Keep asking the coach until he gives in'],answer:0,why:'The coach does not decide alone. Helping him ask makes him your ally, not your opponent.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'The manager across the desk is the person you are negotiating against.',reality:'They usually did not set the budget and do not keep the difference. They are often the one who will argue your case upwards, to whoever really decides.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The person opposite is usually not the counterparty. Treating them as an obstacle wastes the one ally in the process. Ask what they need to make your case, before you offer a number.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the manager is working within',text:'Most managers in a pay conversation work within a band set elsewhere. They want to keep you, and they did not set the limit. Treating it as a fight pits you against someone whose interest may already line up with yours.'},{kind:'steps',title:'What to hand them',steps:['Evidence of what you have delivered.','Comparables: what the role pays elsewhere.','A specific figure they can take to whoever decides.'],note:'You are equipping their argument, not winning one against them.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The manager has turned his back on the visitor to argue with the closed door. That is often where the real conversation is.'},{kind:'explain',title:'It changes timing too',text:'If the figure is fixed in a planning cycle, the conversation that matters comes before that cycle. Then your manager can still carry your case into it. Idea 3 takes this further.'},{kind:'try',text:'In your next pay discussion, ask: what would you need from me to make this an easy argument to win upstairs?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Your manager says the raise is out of their hands. What response fits the idea?',options:['Ask who decides, and what evidence would help them make your case','Push harder, since \u201cout of my hands\u201d is just a way of saying no','Drop it, since nothing can be done if they do not decide','Go over their head to the director straight away'],answer:0,why:'Out of their hands usually means they are not the decider. They can still carry your case, if you give them what they need.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHAT THE PIECE ARGUES AGAINST',text:'Most pay advice, the piece says, is about confidence: sit up, ask big, hold firm. Its claim is that the parts that reliably matter are information, timing and what the other side is optimising for. The first of these is who the other side actually is.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The manager in the room is usually an agent working within a band set elsewhere, not a principal deciding your worth. The useful move is to equip them to argue upwards.'},{kind:'term',word:'Counterparty',meaning:'The person or body on the other side of a deal, whose interests are opposed to yours on the terms. In a pay talk, that is usually whoever sets the budget, not the manager across the desk.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHERE THE NUMBER IS DECIDED',nodes:['You make the case to your manager','Your manager carries it upwards','The budget holder decides','The answer comes back down'],note:'The meeting you attend is the first link. Much of what decides the outcome happens in the second and third.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Turned away from the visitor, the manager argues with a closed door. The piece\u2019s point is that the door, not the manager, is the counterparty.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Sometimes the person opposite is the decider: a founder, or a recruiter whose job is to hold costs down. And a manager can be aligned with you in wanting to keep you while also being measured on staying within budget. The idea is a default worth checking, not a reason to assume goodwill.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'In your organisation, who actually sets pay bands? Does your manager have any discretion within them, and when?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'FIND THE COUNTERPARTY',prompt:'In which case is the person opposite most likely to be the real counterparty?',options:['The owner of a five-person firm who pays everyone from her own profits','A team lead at a large company with bands set by finance','A department head waiting on next year\u2019s budget allocation','A line manager asked to pass your request up to HR'],answer:0,why:'An owner who sets the budget and keeps the difference is a principal. In the other cases the person opposite is carrying your case to someone else.'}]}},'the-salary-conversation_2':{pictureCaption:'One card sits alone on bare wood. The other slides across on a thick stack of papers. Same request; only one has something under it.',bridge:'Idea 1 said to give your manager what they need to argue for you. This one says what that is: a basis.',basics:{title:'Give a reason people can check',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'ASKING FOR MORE POCKET MONEY',prompt:'You want \xa35 a week instead of \xa33. Which way of asking is most likely to work?',options:['I just really want \xa35','I\u2019ll be so happy if I get \xa35','Most kids in my class get about \xa35, and I now walk the dog'],answer:2,reveal:'The third one gives reasons your parents can check. They can ask other parents. They can see you walk the dog. A wish is easy to say no to. Reasons are harder.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Don\u2019t just ask for a number. Tie it to something that can be checked. The reason does the work of persuading. The number is just where the reason ends up.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A SCIENCE ANSWER',text:'In science, a teacher wants more than the answer. She wants to see how you got it. An answer with working shown is hard to mark wrong. A number on its own is just a guess.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'WHAT IS UNDER IT',caption:'Two cards with the same ask. One is on its own. One sits on a big pile of reasons. Which is harder to say no to?'},{kind:'steps',title:'Good reasons to give',steps:['What others like you get.','Something new you now do.','Something good you have already done.']},{kind:'explain',title:'Do reasons always work?',text:'No. If there really is no more money, good reasons will not make some. But with no reason at all, you will usually get a no, even when the money was there.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You want a later bedtime. Which way of asking uses this idea?',options:['My friends my age go at nine, and I get up fine','I\u2019m old enough now, so please just let me','I really, really want to stay up much later'],answer:0,why:'It gives reasons that can be checked: what friends do, and how you manage in the morning.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO REQUESTS, ONE FIGURE',left:{title:'A wish',items:['I\u2019d like \xa358,000','I feel I deserve it','Easy to decline']},right:{title:'A position',items:['The market range is \xa355\u201362k','My scope grew by a team','Needs a counter-argument']},note:'Same number. Only one of them can be carried upstairs.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A number without a basis is a wish. The request that works is attached to something checkable: a market range, a change in scope, a comparable role. The basis does the persuading.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why a basis moves people',text:'Declining a number is easy. Declining a documented case needs a counter-argument. If no counter-argument exists, the position tends to move. And your manager, from idea 1, now has something to take to whoever decides.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Building the basis',steps:['Market data for the role and location.','A record of what you have delivered.','Ideally, a comparable role you can name.'],note:'Then let the number land where the basis puts it, not at the largest figure you could defend.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'SOMETHING TO CARRY UPSTAIRS',caption:'The lone card is the wish: a figure on bare wood, easy to push back. The other arrives on a stack of evidence your manager can pick up and take upstairs.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The honest caveat',text:'A basis is necessary, not sufficient. A well-evidenced request against a genuinely fixed budget still fails. But the piece argues that an unevidenced one usually fails, even where the money was available.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before you say your number out loud, write next to it the one checkable fact it rests on. If you cannot, you are not ready.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Which request is most likely to move a manager who has some room in the budget?',options:['Roles like mine in this city pay \xa348\u201354k, and I now run the client account','I would like \xa354k, which I think is fair for everything I do','I have been here three years and feel it is time for a rise','A recruiter told me I could probably get a lot more elsewhere'],answer:0,why:'Only the \xa348\u201354k request ties the figure to things that can be checked: a market range and a change in scope. The others are preferences or vague claims.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'The trick is to ask for the largest figure you can defend.',reality:'The piece puts the work elsewhere. The figure is just where the basis lands. A large number with a thin basis is still a preference, and preferences are easy to decline.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Evidence converts a request from a preference into a negotiating position, because it obliges the other side to produce a counter-argument rather than just a no.'},{kind:'term',word:'Comparable',meaning:'A role, at your employer or elsewhere, similar enough to yours that its pay is evidence about what yours should be. The piece says a good basis ideally includes one you can name.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The shift in burden',text:'Without a basis, the person opposite needs no reason to decline. With one, declining needs an answer to your evidence. Where no answer exists, the piece says, the position tends to move. The burden of argument has changed sides.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'HOW A REQUEST LANDS',low:'A figure alone: declined without reason',high:'A figure on a checkable basis: needs a counter-argument',note:'The further right, the more your manager can carry upwards.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'THE BURDEN, MOVED',caption:'A card on bare wood can be declined without a word. A card that arrives on a thick stack has to be answered: someone must deal with what is underneath.'},{kind:'limits',text:'A basis cannot create money in a fixed budget, as the piece concedes. Market data can be cherry-picked, and a sceptical reader will check it. A named colleague\u2019s pay may be confidential and awkward to cite. And the claim that an unevidenced request usually fails is the piece\u2019s judgement, not a measured finding.'},{kind:'recap',points:['A number alone is a preference, easy to decline.','A checkable basis forces a counter-argument.','Necessary, not sufficient: a fixed budget still says no.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'WEAKEST BASIS',prompt:'Which of these is the weakest basis for a pay request?',options:['A friend in another industry earns far more for similar hours','Published salary ranges for the same role in the same city','A documented expansion of your responsibilities this year','An internal posting for the same role at a higher band'],answer:0,why:'A different industry is not a comparable, so it is easy to counter. The others are checkable and directly about the role.'}]}},'the-salary-conversation_3':{pictureCaption:'A traveller arrives at the quay, right on time by his own reckoning. The ship has already passed the harbour wall.',bridge:'Idea 1 hinted that the number is set in a planning cycle. This idea is about getting your case in before it closes.',basics:{title:'Ask before it is decided',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE SUMMER CAMP',text:'In June you ask to go to a summer camp. But your family planned the summer back in March. The days are booked and the money is spent. However well you ask, the answer is already no.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Big choices are often made before anyone tells you. If you ask after that, it is too late. Find out when the choice is made, and ask before then.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'He got to the dock on time, he thought. But the ship left long ago. It went when the choice was made.'},{kind:'explain',title:'A second kind of timing',text:'It also helps to ask soon after you did something good. Just after a great school report, everyone can see it. Six months later, they have half forgotten.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Getting the timing right',steps:['Ask when the plan gets made.','Ask a few weeks before that.','If you can, ask just after you did well.']},{kind:'try',text:'Is there something you want this year? Find out when your family will plan it, and ask before then.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your school picks its clubs for next year in May. You want a chess club. When should you ask?',options:['In April, before the clubs are picked','In September, when the year starts','Any time, as long as you ask nicely'],answer:0,why:'After May the clubs are already picked. Asking before means your idea can still be part of the plan.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE REVIEW MEETING',text:'You prepare for weeks. In the review you make your case well, and your manager agrees with all of it. Then they explain that the budget for next year was settled a month ago. There is nothing left to allocate.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Timing is most of it. Pay is usually decided in a budgeting window that closes weeks before anyone is told. A conversation after that cannot succeed, however well it goes.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'THE SAME CASE, TWO DATES',left:{title:'Before the cycle closes',items:['Your manager can carry it in','The number is still open']},right:{title:'After it closes',items:['The answer is already fixed','Only next year is open']}},{kind:'picture',caption:'On time for the departure he was told about. The ship left when the real decision was made, weeks earlier.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Your own timing',text:'A request made soon after a visible delivery is judged differently from the same request six months later. The contribution has not changed. The evidence is immediate rather than recalled.'},{kind:'try',text:'Ask when compensation is decided at your organisation. It is usually information you can get by asking. Have your conversation a month before that.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Budgets are set in October; reviews are held in January. You land a big project in September. When should you raise pay?',options:['In September, while the result is fresh and the budget is open','In January, since that is when reviews are formally held','In November, once next year\u2019s budget has been confirmed','Next September, after a full year of strong results'],answer:0,why:'September gives both kinds of timing: the budget window is still open, and the evidence is fresh rather than recalled.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHERE THE OUTCOME IS SET',nodes:['Budgets are planned','The window closes','Weeks pass','You are told the review date','The meeting happens'],note:'By the last step, the answer was fixed three steps earlier.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Most of the outcome is determined before the conversation starts: by when budgets are set, when your performance is most visible, and whether you have an alternative.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Three timing points',text:'The piece names three. The budget cycle, which fixes what is available. The recency of your evidence, which changes how a request is judged. And whether you have an alternative, which changes your position. None of the three is the meeting itself.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Work backwards',text:'The practical move is to find out when the cycle runs and work back from it. The piece notes this is usually available just by asking. The cost of not asking is arriving, prepared and persuasive, three weeks too late.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A punctual traveller and an empty mooring. The departure that mattered was weeks before the one he was told about.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Not every employer has a fixed cycle. Small firms often decide case by case, and some large ones keep money back for exceptions, such as matching an outside offer. And a request timed only to a fresh success can look opportunistic. Timing improves a sound case; it cannot replace one.'},{kind:'recap',points:['The answer is usually fixed before you are told.','Find the cycle and work backwards from it.','Fresh evidence is judged better than recalled evidence.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'DIAGNOSE IT',prompt:'Someone made a strong, well-evidenced case at their review and got nothing. Their manager agreed with every point. What is the most likely explanation?',options:['The budget had already been fixed in an earlier cycle','They did not sound confident enough in the meeting','They named a figure that was far too high','Their evidence was not specific enough to persuade'],answer:0,why:'A manager who agrees with the whole case but cannot act usually has no money left to move. That is a timing failure, not a failure of argument.'}]}},'the-salary-conversation_4':{pictureCaption:'The offer has been pushed across the table and both hands have come back to rest. The other chair has not answered yet.',bridge:'Ideas 2 and 3 got the right number in at the right time. This one is about the moment just after you say it.',basics:{title:'Say your offer, then stop',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'A CARD SWAP',text:'You want your friend\u2019s shiny card. You say: I\u2019ll swap you two of mine for it. He goes quiet. It feels awful. So you add: or just one is fine. He never said no. You did that yourself.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When you make an offer, stop talking. Let the other person answer first. If you fill the quiet, you usually make your own offer worse.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the quiet feels bad',text:'Quiet feels like a no. So you want to fix it. But the only things you can add are reasons to give less. The quiet is just the other person thinking.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The offer is on the table. The hands are still. Now it is the other side\u2019s turn to speak.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO SAY IT',left:{title:'Soft and wobbly',items:['Um, maybe two cards?','Or one, if that\u2019s easier']},right:{title:'Clear, then quiet',items:['Two cards for it.','(Then wait.)']}},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you offer a swap or a deal, say it once, clearly. Then count to ten in your head and wait.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You offer to do the dishes all week for a later bedtime on Saturday. Your mum says nothing. What should you do?',options:['Wait for her to answer','Say you\u2019ll do them for just three days','Say a later bedtime does not really matter'],answer:0,why:'She is thinking. Anything you add now would only make your own offer smaller.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE PAUSE',prompt:'You have just named your figure. Nobody speaks for six seconds. What are you most likely to say if you break the silence?',options:['A stronger reason for the figure','A softer version of it','A request for their view'],answer:1,reveal:'Silence feels like rejection. The instinct is to fix it: hedge, give reasons, go lower. Almost anything you add is a reason to offer you less.'},{kind:'idea',text:'After naming a number, stop. Going on talking almost always means negotiating against yourself. The only new thing you can add is a reason to offer less.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Not a power move',text:'Staying silent is usually sold as a mind trick to unsettle the other side. The mechanism is simpler. Once a figure is on the table, whoever speaks next tends to be the one who moves. The discipline is just not bidding against yourself.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'HOW IT SOUNDS',left:{title:'A hope',items:['I was hoping for something around 60','if that\u2019s at all possible']},right:{title:'A figure',items:['Sixty thousand.','Then stop.']},note:'With the softening clause, it is a different offer from a figure. You can hear the difference.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The offer pushed across, both hands withdrawn and still. Moving them now would only pull the offer back.'},{kind:'try',text:'Practise saying your figure aloud with no clause before or after it. Then practise saying nothing for ten seconds afterwards.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You say your figure and the recruiter pauses. Which next sentence would the piece warn against?',options:['Though I could be flexible if that is a stretch','Nothing: wait for them to respond first','If they ask, the market range it is based on','If they counter, a question about what is fixed'],answer:0,why:'Offering to bend before you are asked is bidding against yourself. Answering their question or their counter is different: they spoke first.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE STANDARD ADVICE',belief:'Silence after an offer is a tactic for putting pressure on the other side.',reality:'The piece reads it as arithmetic. With a figure on the table, anything you add can only qualify, justify or reduce it. Silence just stops you subtracting from your own offer.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The pause after a proposal is not a contest of nerve. Speaking into it can only bring new information that lowers your position, so the rational move is to let the other side answer first.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'NEGOTIATING AGAINST YOURSELF',nodes:['You name a figure','Silence feels like rejection','You qualify or soften it','They have not moved; you have'],note:'Nobody across the table did anything. The concession came from your own discomfort.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The corollary',text:'The piece pairs the silence with a second discipline: say the figure without softening clauses. A hoped-for range wrapped in apologies is already a concession before the pause begins. The figure and the silence work together.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The object pushed across, both hands flat and still, the other chair empty. The next move belongs to the other side.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Whoever speaks next moves is a tendency, not a law. A long silence can read as hostile, especially in a warm relationship or a culture that values easy talk. And it is not a reason to refuse questions: if they ask for your basis, answer it. The rule is narrow. Do not add concessions nobody asked for.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of the last time you named a price or a request. What did you say in the seconds after it, and did it help you?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'WHERE THE RULE ENDS',prompt:'After your figure, the hiring manager asks what it is based on. What does the idea say?',options:['Answer it: the rule is against unprompted concessions, not questions','Stay silent, since whoever speaks next is the one who moves','Lower the figure slightly, as a question signals it is too high','Ask them to name a figure first before you explain anything'],answer:0,why:'They have spoken and asked for information, so supplying your basis is not bidding against yourself. Silence protects the figure; it is not a refusal to engage.'}]}},'the-salary-conversation_5':{pictureCaption:'The cash drawer is locked. The shelf behind it is full of other things of real value, and none of them is money.',bridge:'Sometimes the right number, well timed and well held, still meets a fixed budget. This idea is what to do then.',basics:{title:'Money is not the only prize',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'WHEN THEY SAY NO',belief:'If they say no to more pocket money, there is nothing left to ask for.',reality:'Money is just one thing. You could ask for a later bedtime on Fridays, or to pick the film, or more time with friends. Those are worth something too.'},{kind:'idea',text:'If the money can\u2019t change, other things often can. Know which ones you really care about. Then ask for those instead of giving up.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The money drawer is locked. But look at the shelf behind it. It is full of other good things.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why other things are easier',text:'Pocket money comes out of the family\u2019s money. A later bedtime does not. So a parent who can\u2019t give more money may still be happy to say yes to something else.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Know what you want first',text:'Before you ask, list the things you would like. Pick the ones that matter most to you. If you don\u2019t know, you can\u2019t ask for the right thing when the money is a no.'},{kind:'try',text:'Write down three things that are not money but that you would really like. Put them in order.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your gran can\u2019t give you more birthday money this year. What does this idea say to do?',options:['Ask if she could teach you to bake instead','Say it is fine and ask for nothing','Keep asking until she finds more money'],answer:0,why:'The money is fixed, but her time and skills are not. Those are worth a lot too.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE BAND IS FIXED',text:'Your manager has pushed and the salary will not move: it is at the top of the band. You thank them and get up to leave. In doing so, you have walked away from title, scope, a training budget and an earlier review, which often come from other budgets than salary.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Salary is one component of pay and often not the most adjustable. Titles, scope, budget and flexible hours are real compensation, often from a different budget. If cash is fixed, the conversation is not over.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHAT ELSE IS ON THE TABLE',left:{title:'Often fixed',items:['Base salary','The pay band']},right:{title:'Often movable',items:['Title, scope, training budget','Start date, holiday, remote work','A review at six months, not twelve','Leaving at four on a Thursday']}},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it helps them too',text:'A manager who cannot move salary but can move three other things is in a position to close the deal, and will often do so. But only if they know which three things are worth anything to you.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A locked till, and behind it a shelf of valuables that are not cash. Fixed salary does not mean an empty shelf.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before your next pay conversation, rank the non-cash items you actually value. If the salary is fixed, move to them in the same conversation instead of ending it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Your manager says the band is maxed out. Which reply applies the idea?',options:['Then could we bring my review forward to six months and add a training budget?','I understand. Let us revisit it at next year\u2019s review then.','In that case I will need to think about whether I stay.','Could you ask for an exception to the band on my behalf?'],answer:0,why:'It moves straight to things that come from other budgets, in the same conversation. The others either end the talk or push on the one fixed item.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO PREPARE',left:{title:'Most people',items:['Prepare a salary figure','Hear \u201cthe band is fixed\u201d','End the conversation']},right:{title:'The piece\u2019s way',items:['Rank what else you value','Hear \u201cthe band is fixed\u201d','Trade on what can move']},note:'The difference is preparation the piece says most people skip.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Compensation is a bundle, and its parts come from different budgets. When salary hits a genuine limit, knowing which other parts you value determines what a good trade looks like.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Which constraint you have hit',text:'A no on salary is a no to one component, not to the request. The piece\u2019s instruction is to ask which constraint you have actually hit. A band at its ceiling says nothing about the title, the review date or the training budget.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Giving them a way to say yes',text:'A manager who wants to keep you but cannot move salary needs something to offer. If you have said which items matter, you have handed them a path to agreement. If you have not, the meeting can end with nothing despite goodwill on both sides.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The till is locked; the shelf behind is full. The work is knowing which of the things on that shelf you actually want.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Not everything is worth what it looks like. A title without pay can be a cheap way to close you out, and equity can be worth nothing. Base salary also tends to anchor future raises and offers in a way a one-off perk does not. Trading cash for items you do not genuinely value is losing, however politely it is done.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'If your salary could not move this year, which three other things would you most want, and why those?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'A GOOD TRADE?',prompt:'Salary is fixed. Which offer is most likely to be a genuinely good trade?',options:['A paid course you had planned to fund yourself, plus an earlier review','A grander title with no change in responsibilities or pay','Share options in a company you doubt will ever be sold','An extra team to manage, with no change in budget or support'],answer:0,why:'It has real value to you and comes from other budgets. The rest look like compensation but may be worth little, or cost you more than they give.'}]}},'the-salary-conversation_6':{pictureCaption:'A kitchen table at night, long before any meeting. One hand draws the line calmly, so the room does not get to draw it later.',bridge:'Idea 3 named having an alternative as part of timing. This last idea is about deciding, before the meeting, what you will refuse.',basics:{title:'Decide your line at home',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A SHOP LIST',text:'You go to the shops with \xa35 and a list. If you decided at home, you stick to it. If you decide in the shop, the sweets by the till win. Deciding while calm keeps you on track.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Before a deal, decide at home the least you will accept. Write it down. In the moment it is hard to say no, so the choice has to be made before.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'SELLING YOUR OLD BIKE',text:'A neighbour offers \xa320 for your bike. You wanted \xa340. He is nice and he is waiting. Saying no feels rude. Without a number decided at home, you say yes, and wish you hadn\u2019t.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Late at night, at the kitchen table, a hand draws a line. It is decided here, not when someone is waiting.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The line must be real',text:'Only pick a line you will truly keep. Ask yourself: if they offer less, what will I do instead? If you have a good answer, your line is real.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you swap or sell something, write your lowest price at home first. Bring it with you.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You plan to swap your game at school. When is the best time to decide what you would take for it?',options:['At home, before you bring it in','At school, once you see the offers','Only when someone makes an offer'],answer:0,why:'At home you are calm. At school, with someone waiting, it is much harder to say no.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'IN THE ROOM',prompt:'You hoped for \xa360,000. After three interviews they offer \xa352,000, warmly, and wait. What are you most likely to do if you have not decided your walk-away?',options:['Decline, since it is well below your hope','Accept, or nearly accept','Ask for time and think it over calmly'],answer:1,reveal:'The process has cost you weeks, you like the person opposite, and refusing is uncomfortable now. None of that was part of your thinking when you were calm, and all of it pushes towards yes.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Decide the walk-away before, not during. The one thing that genuinely changes your position is a considered alternative, and it can only be worked out while you are calm.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHAT DECIDES, AND WHERE',left:{title:'At home, beforehand',items:['Your alternatives','Your real costs and needs','A clear head']},right:{title:'In the room',items:['Sunk cost of the process','Liking the person opposite','The discomfort of refusing']},note:'The right-hand column was never part of the assessment.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Commitment, not preference',text:'Written down in advance, with the reasoning, the number works as a commitment rather than a preference. In the room you only have to check the offer against it, not make a decision under pressure.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A hand drawing a line on the kitchen table at night. This is where the walk-away gets decided.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before your next negotiation, write your walk-away figure and the alternative behind it. Bring the note and check offers against it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Your written walk-away is \xa355,000. The final offer is \xa353,000 and the manager is lovely. What does the idea say?',options:['Decline or keep negotiating: the figure was set for this moment','Accept, since \xa32,000 is small and the relationship matters','Lower the walk-away now that you know the people better','Accept now and plan to raise pay later in the role'],answer:0,why:'Warmth in the room is exactly what the advance decision was protecting against. If the offer is below the line you set while calm, it is below the line.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHY IT MUST COME FIRST',text:'The piece makes a strong claim: the walk-away cannot be decided during the conversation at all. In the room you are subject to the sunk cost of the process, the relationship with the person opposite and the discomfort of refusing. None of these belonged in the assessment.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Your position is set by your alternative, and your walk-away has to be fixed while you are calm. Deciding in the room is how people accept what they had privately ruled out.'},{kind:'term',word:'Walk-away point',meaning:'The least favourable terms you would accept before turning to your best alternative instead. It is only as strong as that alternative is real.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE LINE DRIFTS',nodes:['No line decided beforehand','Weeks of process already spent','Refusing feels costly in the room','You accept what you had ruled out'],note:'Each pressure in the room pushes the same way, towards yes.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Night, a quiet kitchen, a line drawn across the table. The decision is made where none of the room\u2019s pressures can reach it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The honest part',text:'The number must be real. A walk-away you would not act on gives no protection, and the piece says it is usually detected. So the exercise is partly about finding out whether you have an alternative at all, which is what really determines your position.'},{kind:'limits',text:'A fixed line can be too rigid. New facts in the room, such as a larger scope or a better title, can change what an offer is worth. The rule is that pressure cannot move the line; information can. And if the exercise shows you have no alternative, a bluffed line is risky; the honest move is to improve the alternative first.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'PRESSURE OR INFORMATION?',prompt:'Your walk-away was \xa350,000 in salary. Which development should be allowed to change it in the room?',options:['The offer includes a pension contribution worth \xa36,000 a year more than you assumed','The interviewers are warm and you can picture yourself on the team','You have spent six weeks on interviews and do not want to start again','The hiring manager says they hoped you would accept today'],answer:0,why:'A larger pension is new information that changes the value of the offer. The others are the pressures of the room the advance decision exists to resist.'}]}}}},3026,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'the-second-in-command-problem_1':{pictureCaption:'A loose sweep of intention on one sheet, the same thing as a dated list on the other. Making the list is the job.',basics:{title:'Turning a big idea into a plan',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE CLASS PLAY',text:'Your friend has a great idea: *Let\u2019s put on a pirate play!* Everyone cheers. But who learns which lines? When do you practise? Who builds the ship, and by what day? Someone has to work all that out.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A big idea is not a plan yet. Someone has to turn it into who does what, in what order, by when. That job is easy to miss, but it is where most things go wrong.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The swirl is the big idea. The list is the plan, with days on it. Someone had to turn one into the other.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A RECIPE',text:'*Make a birthday cake* is a wish. The recipe is the plan: what to buy, what to mix first, how long it bakes. Without the recipe, the wish stays a wish.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why nobody notices',text:'When the plan is good, things just happen on time. So it looks like the big idea made itself happen. The person who made the plan stays out of sight.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time someone has a big idea for your group, offer to write the list: who does what, and by which day.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your team captain says, *Let\u2019s win the cup this year!* Which job is the one this lesson is about?',options:['Working out the training days and who plays where','Cheering the loudest at every match','Coming up with the idea of winning the cup'],answer:0,why:'The captain gave the goal. Turning it into days, places and order is the planning job.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE ANNOUNCEMENT',text:'The founder tells the company: we will open in three new cities next year. Applause. The next morning someone has to decide which city comes first and who moves there. They must decide what has to be in place before anything else, and the dates it all hangs on.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The second-in-command\u2019s job is mostly turning intention into a schedule: the order, staffing and dates that make a direction into a plan. That step is where most of the failure happens.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Administration',items:['Implements a plan','Follows the sequence','Output: done tasks']},right:{title:'This role',items:['Produces the plan','Decides the sequence','Output: a schedule that works']},note:'This role\u2019s outputs are calendars. That is why it is mistaken for administration. It needs judgement about what can be done, in what order, with whom.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it goes unseen',text:'Done well, the work is invisible. A working schedule makes it look as if the direction carried itself out. The essay says the role is nearer to translation than administration.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it is hard to hire for',text:'Most of what you need to know is about this one organisation: who can really do what, which teams depend on which, what has failed before. That is why the role is hardest to fill from outside.'},{kind:'try',text:'When you are handed a vague direction, resist sending it back for more detail. Draft the order and dates yourself. Then check them with whoever set the direction.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Your director says, \u201cWe need to be greener this year.\u201d What does the essay say your half of the job is?',options:['Turn it into steps, owners and dates, and check the plan with her','Ask her to specify exactly which changes she wants and by when','Pass the message to every team and let each one decide for itself','Wait for a written strategy before starting any of the work'],answer:0,why:'The direction is hers. Turning it into a plan is yours. Sending it back or passing it on leaves that half undone.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHAT THE ESSAY CLAIMS',text:'The essay starts from an observation: every institution that lasted had someone one rung below the person it is named after. It asks what that job consists of, and why it is structurally impossible to do well and be credited. This first idea answers the first question.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The founder supplies direction. Someone else converts it into sequence, dependencies and dates. That conversion is where most of the failure happens, and it is treated as administration because its outputs are calendars.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE CONVERSION',nodes:['A direction, stated in the abstract','Sequence: what comes first','Dependencies: what waits on what','Dates and people: a plan'],note:'Each arrow is a judgement about what can actually be done, in what order, with whom.'},{kind:'term',word:'Translation',meaning:'The essay\u2019s word for what the role really does: rendering an intention into another language, the language of sequence and dates, where meaning can be kept or lost.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A loose gesture at one end of the bench, a dated list at the other. The essay\u2019s point is that getting from one to the other is a craft, not clerical work.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Two consequences',text:'The work is invisible when done well, since a working schedule looks self-executing. And the role is hardest to fill from outside, because the knowledge it needs is about the specific organisation, not about planning in general.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The essay argues from the shape of the role, not from cited cases. In some organisations the founder does the conversion too. And taking the conversion as your half can go wrong: if a direction is genuinely incoherent, quietly patching it into a plan hides a problem that should go back up.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE CONFUSION',prompt:'A board replaces a departing deputy with an outside expert in project management. What does the essay predict?',options:['Trouble, because the role\u2019s knowledge is about this organisation, not planning in general','Improvement, because the role is mostly administration and experts do it better','No change, because a clear direction from the founder makes the schedule obvious','Trouble, because outsiders are rarely loyal enough for a deputy\u2019s role'],answer:0,why:'The essay says the job is hardest to fill from outside because what it needs is specific organisational knowledge. Loyalty is not the reason.'}]}},'the-second-in-command-problem_2':{bridge:'Idea 1 described turning a direction into a plan. Doing that honestly means sometimes reporting that the plan does not fit.',pictureCaption:'A tape measure across the doorway, and a sofa wider than the gap. Someone has to say the number before the lifting starts.',basics:{title:'Saying the number nobody wants to hear',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE SCHOOL FAIR',text:'Your class wants to build a giant model castle for the school fair. The fair is in one week. You know it will take three. Everyone is excited. If you say so, you will look grumpy.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Part of the planning job is saying the number nobody wants to hear, like *this takes three weeks, not one.* The trick is to bring a plan that fits the real number.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'MEASURE FIRST',caption:'The sofa is wider than the door. The tape says so. It is kinder to say it now than after everyone has lifted.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Just the bad news',items:['\u201cWe can\u2019t do it in a week.\u201d','Sounds like moaning','Everyone feels flat']},right:{title:'Bad news and a plan',items:['\u201cA castle takes three weeks.\u201d','\u201cIn one week, we can do the gate.\u201d','Everyone has a job']}},{kind:'explain',title:'Why is it so hard?',text:'Everyone else wants the big plan to work, and they mean it. So when you say it won\u2019t fit, you sound negative, even when you are right. People often only see you were right much later.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you think a plan is too big, say what can be done in the time, not only what can\u2019t.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your group has one lunchtime to make a video that needs three. What is the best thing to say?',options:['\u201cThat\u2019s three lunchtimes. Today we could film the first scene.\u201d','\u201cThat\u2019s impossible. We should give up on the video.\u201d','\u201cSure, let\u2019s try. Maybe we\u2019ll go really fast.\u201d'],answer:0,why:'It gives the real number and a plan that fits. Saying only *impossible* sounds like moaning, and pretending helps nobody.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'SIX WEEKS OR FOUR MONTHS',prompt:'The room wants the launch in six weeks. You know it takes four months. Which response is most likely to be heard?',options:['\u201cThat\u2019s four months, not six weeks.\u201d','\u201cFour months, but here is what we can ship in six weeks.\u201d','Say nothing and hope it comes together.'],answer:1,reveal:'An objection alone reads as blocking, however accurate. The objection with an alternative reads as a plan.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The job that sets a second-in-command apart is not loyalty. It is stating the unwelcome number in the room. And it is doing it again and again without being relabelled as an obstacle.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why nobody else will say it',text:'Everyone else in the room has a reason to be optimistic. The optimism is usually sincere. That is why this duty cannot be handed to someone else. If the deputy does not say the date cannot be met, nobody else is likely to.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'THE ONE HOLDING THE TAPE',caption:'Everyone wants the sofa in the room. One person has measured the doorway. Saying the measurement is the job.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it costs you',text:'In the moment, being reliably right about limits looks just like being negative. The proof comes later, when the six weeks pass. It is rarely traced back to you.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'WHAT FITS THE NUMBER',text:'*The full launch is four months. In six weeks we can run a pilot with two customers. That tests the riskiest part.* The objection is still there. It just arrives with a plan attached.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before your next unwelcome estimate, write the version of the plan that fits it. Bring both into the room together.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Your boss adds a third priority to a team already at full stretch. What does this idea recommend?',options:['Say the team cannot absorb it, and propose which priority to pause for it','Accept it, since questioning a priority will look disloyal to the boss','Say clearly the team cannot absorb it and leave the decision there','Accept it quietly, then let the deadlines slip to make the point'],answer:0,why:'The unwelcome number has to be said. It lands as a plan only with an option attached. An objection alone gets filed as attitude.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO SAY NO',left:{title:'The objection alone',items:['\u201cThe date is not achievable.\u201d','Heard as obstruction','Filed as attitude']},right:{title:'Objection with option',items:['\u201cNot by then; this part can be.\u201d','Heard as a plan','Survivable, repeatedly']},note:'The essay\u2019s point is that accuracy does not decide which column you land in.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A defining duty of the role is saying the unwelcome number: the date is not achievable, the budget does not cover it, the team cannot absorb another priority. The skill is doing it again and again without being reclassified as an obstacle.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it cannot be delegated',text:'Everyone else present has an incentive to be optimistic, and their optimism is usually sincere, which makes it more persuasive, not less. The deputy is the one person whose job includes the constraint.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'THE CONSTRAINT, MEASURED',caption:'The doorway does not care how much the room wants the sofa. The deputy is the one holding the tape.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The attribution problem',text:'Being reliably right about limits is, in the moment, indistinguishable from being negative. The proof arrives only later, when the date slips or the budget runs out, and by then it is rarely attributed. This anticipates the essay\u2019s next idea, about credit.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Sometimes no version of the plan fits the number, and the honest answer is just no. Bringing an alternative can also slide into quietly accepting a target that should be refused. And the deputy can be wrong: caution is not the same as accuracy.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'When did you last hear an unwelcome number at work? Did it come with a plan, and how was it received?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISREADING',prompt:'Someone concludes the deputy\u2019s real value is loyalty to the founder. What does the essay say?',options:['The distinguishing function is saying the unwelcome number, which loyalty alone would not do','Loyalty is the core of the role, and the unwelcome number tests that loyalty','The distinguishing function is staying optimistic so the team keeps its morale','Loyalty and accuracy are the same, since a loyal deputy is always honest'],answer:0,why:'The essay says plainly that the distinguishing function is not loyalty. It is being the one who states the constraint.'}]}},'the-second-in-command-problem_3':{pictureCaption:'Trophies for the things that happened. The bare floor below is where the disasters that never happened would stand.',bridge:'Ideas 1 and 2 describe the work. This one explains why it is so rarely seen.',basics:{title:'Nobody thanks you for the thing that didn\u2019t go wrong',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE AN UMBRELLA',text:'When an umbrella works, you just stay dry. You don\u2019t think about it at all. You only notice an umbrella when it breaks and you get soaked.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Prizes on the wall for things that happened. The empty floor is for the bad things that never did. Nobody gives a prize for those.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Some jobs stop bad things from happening. But when nothing goes wrong, there is nothing to see. So people who do these jobs rarely get thanked.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE SCHOOL TRIP',text:'The school trip goes perfectly. Everyone thanks the teacher in charge. Nobody thanks the helper who checked the coach was booked and packed the spare lunches. Nothing went wrong, so nobody saw what she did.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What goes wrong if you need thanks',text:'Some people get upset about this. A few even let a small thing go wrong on purpose, to show they are needed. That hurts everyone. It is better to be proud of the job itself.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of someone whose job is to stop things going wrong, like a dinner lady or a caretaker. Say thank you to them this week.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Why do people who stop problems rarely get thanked?',options:['When they do it well, there is nothing to see','Their jobs are not really very important','They do not want anyone to thank them'],answer:0,why:'A problem that never happened leaves nothing behind to point at. That is why their work is easy to miss.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'If you do excellent work for long enough, the recognition eventually comes.',reality:'Not in this role. Its outputs are absences: the crisis that did not happen, the hire who did not leave. Absences cannot be credited to anyone.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The shape of the role keeps credit away from a second-in-command. Pretending otherwise eats away at you. The people who last decided early that the satisfaction had to come from the work itself.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Not unfair, just invisible',text:'Outcomes are credited to the person whose direction it was. That is not unfair: the direction was theirs. What nobody sees is that the direction would have produced nothing without the work of turning it into a plan.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Chasing credit',items:['Making the difficulty visible','Letting things fail to prove need','Narrating your part to others']},right:{title:'Finding it elsewhere',items:['A few people who can see the work','The outcome itself','Deciding early that is enough']},note:'The essay calls the left-hand column damaging. Each one trades the work for credit.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Who should stay',text:'The essay is blunt. Those for whom quiet recognition is truly enough tend to last. Those for whom it is not tend to leave bitter. They should do something else.'},{kind:'try',text:'Name the two or three people who can really see your work. Ask yourself honestly whether their view is enough.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A deputy notices the chief executive gets praised for a smooth year she made possible. What does the essay suggest?',options:['Accept the credit structure, and find recognition among the few who see her work','Let a minor problem surface so the board can see what she prevents','Brief board members privately on how much of the year was her doing','Tell the chief executive the praise is unfair and ask for it to be shared'],answer:0,why:'The other options are versions of the damaging ways the essay lists. It argues for accepting the structure, or leaving the role.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHERE THE CREDIT GOES',nodes:['A crisis is prevented','So nothing happens','Nothing is attributable','Success credited to the direction'],note:'Every link is reasonable. The result is that the conversion disappears from the record.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The role produces absences, and absences are not attributable. Credit is therefore structurally unavailable, and the people who expect it eventually tend to leave bitter.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The damaging substitutes',text:'People who need the credit tend to seek it in ways that harm the work: making the difficulty visible, letting things fail briefly to demonstrate their necessity, or narrating their contribution to third parties. The essay describes these as corrosion, not strategy.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A wall of trophies for what happened, and a bare floor for what did not. The essay\u2019s point is that the deputy\u2019s best work lives on the floor.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The alternative',text:'Recognition from a small number of people who can see the work, and from the outcome itself. The essay\u2019s claim is that those who last in the role are generally those for whom that is genuinely sufficient.'},{kind:'limits',text:'*Structurally unavailable* can become an excuse for organisations never to look. A good leader can and should credit the deputy. Needing recognition is ordinary, not a defect. And the essay\u2019s claim about who lasts is an observation, not a measured result.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Whose work in your organisation consists mainly of things that did not happen? How would anyone know?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'Someone objects that it is simply unjust for the founder to get the credit. What would the essay reply?',options:['It is not unjust, since the direction was the founder\u2019s; what is unseen is the conversion','It is unjust, and the deputy should keep a record to correct it later','It is unjust, but founders always take credit, so nothing can change it','It is not unjust, because the deputy\u2019s work matters less than the direction'],answer:0,why:'The essay calls the attribution fair as far as it goes. The problem is invisibility, not theft, and invisible work is not less important.'}]}},'the-second-in-command-problem_4':{pictureCaption:'Scissors trim a finished pattern with skill. Beside it, a blank sheet and an untouched pencil: the job of drawing the pattern in the first place.',bridge:'If the work goes uncredited, the obvious reward is promotion. This idea explains why that so often fails.',basics:{title:'A great helper is not always a great leader',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE NEW CAPTAIN',prompt:'The vice-captain has been brilliant all year. The captain leaves. Will the vice-captain be a great captain?',options:['Yes, for sure','Not for sure'],answer:1,reveal:'Not for sure. The two jobs need different skills. Being great at one does not prove you will be great at the other.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A helper turns someone else\u2019s big idea into a plan. A leader has to come up with the big idea. These are nearly opposite skills, so a great helper may not be the best leader.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'The helper',items:['Gets a big idea','Makes it smaller and clearer','Turns it into steps']},right:{title:'The leader',items:['Starts from nothing','Has to think of the idea','Decides where to go']}},{kind:'picture',kicker:'CUTTING, NOT DRAWING',caption:'One hand cuts out a pattern someone else drew. It does it well. Next to it is a blank sheet and a pencil nobody has picked up. Drawing a new pattern is a different job.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A COOK',text:'Some cooks are amazing at following a recipe exactly. Others are great at making up new dishes. Both are good cooks. But being good at one does not mean you are good at the other.'},{kind:'try',text:'Which do you enjoy more: having the idea, or making the plan? Notice this next time you work in a group.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Sam is brilliant at turning the club leader\u2019s ideas into plans. The leader leaves. What should the club ask?',options:['Can Sam come up with new ideas, not just plans?','Has Sam been in the club the longest?','Is Sam the most popular person here?'],answer:0,why:'The leader\u2019s job needs ideas. Sam\u2019s great planning does not tell you whether Sam can do that.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO JOBS, OPPOSITE DIRECTIONS',left:{title:'The deputy\u2019s job',items:['Starts from a wide intention','Narrows it into a plan','Asks: how, when, who?']},right:{title:'The top job',items:['Starts from nothing','Generates the intention','Asks: where, and why?']},note:'One narrows. The other opens. The essay calls the skills close to opposite.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Deputies promoted into the top job often struggle, because the skills are close to opposite. Being excellent at narrowing an intention into a plan is weak evidence that you can generate the intention in the first place.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why everyone expects it to work',text:'The deputy knows the organisation best, has run it day to day, and has earned a reward. Promotion looks like the obvious fit. But all that evidence is about the narrowing job, not the generating one.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What worked better',text:'Institutions that handled this well tended to promote sideways rather than upwards: more scope for the deputy\u2019s real skill, rather than a different job. And they accepted that the best deputy is frequently the wrong successor.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE FIRST MONTH',picture:!0,text:'A newly promoted chief, formerly an excellent deputy, waits for a direction to arrive so she can plan it. None comes. That is the moment the job has changed under her.'},{kind:'try',text:'If you are a deputy weighing the top job, write down a direction you would set that nobody has handed you. If you cannot, notice that.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A board must replace a departing founder. The deputy is superb. What does the essay advise the board to check first?',options:['Whether the deputy has ever generated direction, not just carried it out','Whether the deputy has the most experience of the organisation','Whether the deputy\u2019s team would be loyal to them as leader','Whether the deputy has executed plans well for long enough'],answer:0,why:'Experience, loyalty and execution are all evidence about the deputy\u2019s job. The top job needs the opposite skill.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'THE ALTERNATIVE',word:'Promoting sideways',meaning:'Rewarding an excellent deputy with greater scope for the skill they already have, rather than moving them up into a job that needs a different one.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Succession from deputy to leader often fails, and for a consistent reason: one role narrows a wide intention into a plan, the other generates the intention. Excellence at the first is weak evidence for the second.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The inference that misleads',text:'Organisations reason: this person ran everything well, so they can run everything. But the deputy\u2019s record is a record of narrowing someone else\u2019s direction. It contains almost no observations of generating one, which is the top job\u2019s core.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'WHAT THE RECORD SHOWS',caption:'The deputy\u2019s record looks like this hand: skilled cutting along lines someone else drew. The blank sheet beside it, pencil untouched, is the part of the top job the record never tested.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'TWO SKILLS ON ONE LINE',low:'Narrowing: intention into plan',high:'Generating: nothing into intention',note:'The essay places the two jobs near opposite ends. Being far towards one end tells you little about reaching the other.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What good institutions did',text:'The essay says those that handled this well tended to promote sideways and accept that the best deputy is frequently the wrong successor. That acceptance is the hard part, because promotion is the standard reward.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The essay says *often*, not always: some people have both skills, and generating direction can be learned. A deputy also brings deep knowledge of the organisation, which an outsider lacks. And endless sideways moves can turn the deputy role into a ceiling, the problem the next idea takes up.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of someone promoted from a number-two role. Did their record show them setting direction, or only carrying it out?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'Which situation has the same structure as the deputy-to-leader problem?',options:['Promoting the best editor of other people\u2019s books to write the next bestseller','Giving the fastest salesperson a larger territory to sell in','Moving a skilled surgeon to a hospital with harder operations','Giving a reliable accountant a larger set of client accounts'],answer:0,why:'Editing narrows someone else\u2019s work; writing generates it. The other moves give more scope to the same skill, which is closer to promoting sideways.'}]}},'the-second-in-command-problem_5':{pictureCaption:'The coat peg by the door is empty, and every machine in the workshop is still running. Things working without you is the goal.',bridge:'Idea 4 said the best deputy is frequently the wrong successor. This one is about not becoming trapped in the role.',basics:{title:'Make sure things work without you',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE CLASS HAMSTER',text:'You look after the class hamster. You know when to feed it and how to clean its cage. Then you go on holiday for two weeks. Does the hamster get fed?'},{kind:'idea',text:'If something only works when you are there, you have not built a system. The good ones write things down and teach others, so it keeps working when they are away.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'AWAY, AND STILL RUNNING',caption:'The coat peg by the door is empty. Its owner is away. But look inside: every machine is still running. That is the goal.'},{kind:'steps',title:'How to do it',steps:['Write down what you do and when.','Teach someone else to do it.','Step away for a while.','See what goes wrong, and write that down too.']},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it feels wrong',text:'Being the only one who knows feels safe. People need you. But if only you can do the job, you can never do anything else. You are stuck with it.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one job you always do at home or school. Write the steps on a card so someone else could do it.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You run the class library. You are off sick for a week and nobody can find anything. What should you do next?',options:['Write down how it works, so others can run it','Keep doing it yourself, since only you know how','Close the library, since it is too much work'],answer:0,why:'The things that went wrong are the things you had not written down yet. That list is your next job.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'TWO WEEKS AWAY',text:'A deputy takes her first real holiday in three years. In her absence, a supplier goes unpaid, a weekly report does not go out, and nobody knows the password to the booking system. She returns to find her job description written in breakages.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A deputy whose absence stops the organisation has built a dependency rather than a system. The good ones deliberately make themselves briefly unnecessary, and treat whatever breaks as the list of what has not been written down yet.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'A dependency',items:['Works only when you are there','Lives in your head','Cannot be improved or scaled']},right:{title:'A system',items:['Runs while you are away','Written down and taught','Can be improved, handed on, grown']}},{kind:'picture',kicker:'THE EMPTY PEG',caption:'An empty peg by the door, and beyond it every machine running and attended. Whoever hangs a coat there is away, and nothing has stopped. That is a system, not a dependency.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why indispensable is a trap',text:'Indispensability feels like security. The essay argues it is the opposite: the person who cannot be replaced cannot be moved. The role becomes a ceiling. It is a description of job security, and a poor description of a career.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Making yourself briefly unnecessary',steps:['Document the process.','Train a deputy of your own.','Take leave and let the structure run.','Write up what broke.']},{kind:'try',text:'List what would break if you were away for a fortnight from tomorrow. Pick the top item and write it down this week.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A colleague is proud that nobody else can run the monthly payroll. What would the essay say?',options:['That is a dependency, and it will keep her stuck in that job','That is job security, and she is wise to protect it','That is a sign she is the best person for the job','That is normal, since some tasks can only be done by one person'],answer:0,why:'A task only one person can do cannot be improved, handed on or grown, and the person doing it cannot be moved.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'WHICH DEPUTY IS SAFER',prompt:'Which deputy is in the stronger long-term position?',options:['The one the place cannot run without','The one it runs fine without for two weeks'],answer:1,reveal:'The essay\u2019s answer is the second. The first has built a dependency, and a dependency is a ceiling as well as a shield.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The best operators make themselves briefly unnecessary on purpose: documenting, training a deputy, taking leave and letting the structure run. A function that only works when you are present is not a function. It is a dependency.'},{kind:'term',word:'Dependency',meaning:'Work done as a person rather than as a system. It works only while that person is present, so it cannot be improved, promoted out of, or safely scaled.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The mundane test',text:'Take two weeks off and see what breaks. If things degrade immediately, the work was being done as a person. What breaks is not a failure to hide. It is the list of what has not been written down yet, which is to say, the real job description.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'THE TEST, PASSED',caption:'The peg by the door is empty and the workshop runs on, every machine attended. The work has been turned from a person into a structure, which is what the absence was meant to prove.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it runs against instinct',text:'Indispensability feels like security, and in the short run it is. But the person who cannot be replaced cannot be moved. The essay\u2019s distinction is between job security and a career: the dependency buys the first by spending the second.'},{kind:'limits',text:'In some workplaces indispensability really is protection, and making yourself replaceable can make you easier to cut. Not everyone can take two weeks off. Some knowledge is tacit and resists writing down. The test is a good diagnostic, not a guarantee of promotion.'},{kind:'recap',points:['If it stops when you leave, it is a dependency, not a system.','Indispensable feels safe but works as a ceiling.','What breaks in your absence is your real job description.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these misuses the idea?',options:['Hoarding a key process so that you cannot be made redundant','Training a junior colleague to run the weekly planning meeting','Writing up the supplier payments before going on leave','Taking a fortnight off and fixing whatever broke on your return'],answer:0,why:'Hoarding builds exactly the dependency the essay warns against. The others turn personal knowledge into a system.'}]}}}},3027,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'the-two-lists_1':{pictureCaption:'A shelf already packed with jars. The hand pushes one more in, and another drops off the edge. A yes does not make the shelf longer.',basics:{title:'Every yes pushes something out',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'A SATURDAY ASK',text:'Your friend asks you to help at her cake stall on Saturday morning. It is only two hours. You say yes straight away. It feels like it costs nothing.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'What did that yes really cost you?',options:['Nothing. It is only two hours.','Whatever you would have done in those two hours'],answer:1,reveal:'Saturday morning was going to be something. Your match. Your model kit. A lie-in. You did not say which, so the yes looked free.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Saying yes to one thing means saying no to something else. You just can\u2019t see the no when you agree. So give it a name first.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A FULL LUNCHBOX',text:'Your lunchbox is already full. To fit in an apple, something has to come out. Your week is like that. A yes does not make it bigger. It swaps one thing for another.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The shelf is full. Push one more jar in, and a different jar falls off the end. Your week works the same way.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Before you say yes',steps:['Ask: when would I do this?','Ask: what would I be doing then instead?','Say that thing\u2019s name out loud.','Now pick between the two.']},{kind:'try',text:'Next time someone asks you to do something, finish this line in your head: *If I say yes, I am saying no to\u2026*'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A friend wants you to play a new online game with him every night after school. What should you ask yourself first?',options:['What would I stop doing to make the time?','Is the game any good?','Will he be cross if I say no?'],answer:0,why:'Every night is a lot of time. You can only weigh it once you know what it would push out.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A SMALL FAVOUR',prompt:'A colleague asks you to review a draft. About two hours, any time this week. What does it cost?',options:['Two hours','Two hours of something you have not named','Nothing, if you are quick'],answer:1,reveal:'Those two hours were going to be spent on something. At the moment of agreeing, that something has no name, so the trade looks free.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Agreeing does not add capacity. It displaces whatever would have used that time, and because the displaced thing is unnamed when you agree, the yes feels free and later does not.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why yes feels free',text:'The request is concrete: a person, a task, a reason. The cost is deferred and anonymous. So the decision is made with only one side of it visible, and it is hard to weigh something against nothing.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'What you see when asked',items:['The person asking','A task, clearly described','A reason it matters']},right:{title:'What you don\u2019t see',items:['The project that slips','The evening that goes','The slack that made the week bearable']},note:'The right-hand column is real. It just has no name yet.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The shelf is already full, so pushing one more jar in sends another off the edge. The falling jar is the displaced thing, unnamed as the yes is made.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'NAMING THE TRADE',text:'Thursday is your only free evening this week. You are asked to join a planning call then. Named plainly, the choice is the call or the one evening you rest. The call is worth doing in itself. It is not worth that.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before your next yes, finish the sentence: *by agreeing to this, I am declining\u2026* If you cannot fill the gap, you do not yet know the price.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Asked to coach a youth team on Tuesday evenings, which response applies the idea?',options:['Ask what Tuesday evenings hold now, and weigh the team against that','Agree, because the cause is good and the request is reasonable','Decline, and decline everything else for the next month too','Agree, and plan to make up the time by working faster'],answer:0,why:'The request may well be worth it. You can only judge that once the thing it displaces has a name.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHERE THE ESSAY STARTS',text:'This piece begins from an observation: most over-committed people did not get there through one bad decision but through a run of reasonable yeses. Its first task is to explain why each one looked reasonable at the time.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Every acceptance spends time that was available for something else, and the something else is rarely named at the moment of accepting. The trade is real. It is made with one side hidden.'},{kind:'term',word:'Displacement',meaning:'What happens when a new commitment takes time that something else would have used. In the short run your capacity is fixed, so a yes never enlarges the week. It changes what the week contains.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Fixed capacity, drawn plainly: on a full shelf, each jar pushed in sends another off the edge. The essay\u2019s question is which jar.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW A FREE YES IS MADE',nodes:['A concrete request arrives','The cost is deferred and unnamed','It is weighed against nothing','The yes feels free','The displaced thing is missed later']},{kind:'explain',title:'The corrective',text:'Make the trade explicit: what am I declining by accepting this? The essay says the answer is often specific: a project, an evening, or the margin that keeps the rest of the week survivable. Many requests are worth doing in isolation and not worth what they cost, and that judgement needs the cost to have a name.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The claim that many requests fail the comparison is the essay\u2019s practical observation, not a measured finding. Sometimes what is displaced is worth little, and some requests outrank what they displace. Pushed too far, the habit becomes accounting that refuses every favour. Naming the cost is meant to inform a yes, not to forbid one.'},{kind:'recap',points:['A yes never adds time. It rearranges it.','The displaced thing is unnamed when you agree.','Name it, then decide. Some yeses survive.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HIDDEN COST',prompt:'Someone says: \u201cIt cost me nothing to agree. I had no plans that evening.\u201d What does the idea reply?',options:['An empty evening is still something: rest and margin are what it displaced','Nothing was displaced, so this particular yes really was free','Every yes is a mistake, whatever the evening held','Empty evenings are exactly the ones to give away first'],answer:0,why:'The essay counts margin as a real thing a yes can displace. An unplanned evening is often what keeps the week survivable.'}]}},'the-two-lists_2':{pictureCaption:'A far-off square looks empty, so giving it away feels free. When that week arrives, it will be as full as this one.',bridge:'Idea 1 named what a yes pushes out. This one is about why that cost is easiest to miss when the date is far away.',basics:{title:'Would you say yes for tomorrow?',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO ASK',prompt:'Your coach asks you to help run a fun day for little kids. Which is easier to say yes to?',options:['A fun day in six weeks','A fun day tomorrow','They feel the same'],answer:0,reveal:'Six weeks feels easier. Tomorrow you can see your homework. You can see your practice and how tired you are. Six weeks away, you can\u2019t see any of that yet. But it will be there.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Plans far away feel easy because you can\u2019t see how busy you will be. So ask: would I say yes if this was tomorrow?'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The orange square is weeks away. Nothing is written near it, so it looks free. By the time it comes, it will be as busy as today.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why far away tricks you',text:'A week far off looks empty. That is only because nothing is planned yet. When it comes, it will be a normal week, as busy as this one. Future you is not less busy. Future you is just you, later.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE COSTUME',text:'In May, Sam says yes to making a costume for the play in June. June comes. He has tests, football and now a costume too. He should have asked: would I do this if it was tomorrow? Then he would have seen how big the job was.'},{kind:'try',text:'The next time someone asks you to do something weeks away, picture it starting tomorrow. Then give your answer.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A friend asks you to help with her project next month. How do you use this idea?',options:['Picture it starting tomorrow, then answer','Say yes, as next month you will have lots of time','Say no to anything that is far away'],answer:0,why:'Next month will be as busy as now. The tomorrow test shows you the real size of the job.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'AGREED IN JANUARY',text:'In January a friend asks you to give a short talk at an event in March. March is blank, so you say yes. In March you are writing slides at eleven at night, in a week that looks exactly like every other week.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A request for weeks away feels weightless because the future self who pays for it is abstract. Ask whether you would accept if it started tomorrow morning. It is the same commitment.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the far calendar lies',text:'The calendar six weeks out is empty because nothing has been booked yet, not because you will be free. When the date arrives it will look like this week. Saying yes for then is a prediction that a future you will have more room, and it is often wrong.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Answered for March',items:['A blank diary','An imagined, rested you','The yes costs nothing']},right:{title:'Answered for Thursday',items:['This week\u2019s real load','You, as you are now','The price is visible']},note:'The commitment is identical. Only the tense has changed.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A far-off square looks empty, so giving it away feels free. When that week arrives, it will be as full as this one.'},{kind:'try',text:'For the next request with a distant date, ask: would I say yes if this began tomorrow? Answer that question instead.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'In spring you are asked to judge a competition in autumn. You would refuse if it were this week. What does the idea suggest?',options:['Treat the refusal as your honest answer, since it is the same job','Agree, because autumn is bound to be quieter than now','Agree now and work out later whether you can manage it','Agree, since a busy week now says nothing about autumn'],answer:0,why:'Autumn will arrive looking like this week. The present-tense answer is the one you will actually live with.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'If I agree far enough ahead, I will find room for it when the time comes.',reality:'The essay calls this a prediction, and often a wrong one. The distant calendar is empty only because nothing has been booked into it yet.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Distant requests are judged as if a future self with more capacity will carry them out. Asking in the present tense removes that assumption and leaves the commitment as it really is.'},{kind:'equation',parts:['A distant date','An empty-looking calendar'],result:'A yes that feels free',note:'Neither part changes what the commitment will cost when it arrives.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Two things doing the work',text:'The cost falls on a self you cannot picture in detail, and the weeks around the date are blank. Both make the yes feel weightless, and neither is evidence of free time. The test strips both away by moving the date to tomorrow, the tense in which the commitment will be experienced.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the essay concludes',text:'It reports that a good number of things accepted for a distant date turn out to be ones you would decline when asked in the present tense, and reads that as a sign that the distance was doing much of the work. That is offered as the writer\u2019s observation of how the test goes, not as a measured result.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Sometimes the future really is lighter: an exam season ends, a project ships. Then the tomorrow test is too harsh and should be adjusted for what you actually know. It can also overweight a bad day, so ask it on an ordinary one. And it measures cost, not value. Some commitments are worth a heavy Thursday.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What have you agreed to for next month that you would refuse if it started tomorrow? What made it feel different?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'WHEN THE TEST BENDS',prompt:'When is it reasonable to accept a distant request you would refuse for tomorrow?',options:['When a known change, such as a project ending, really frees that week','When the calendar for that month is still mostly empty','When refusing now would feel awkward for both of you','When you expect to have a lot more energy by then'],answer:0,why:'A known fact about the future can justify a different answer. An empty calendar, awkwardness or hoped-for energy are the illusions the test exists to remove.'}]}},'the-two-lists_3':{pictureCaption:'Someone has waited in the hall a long time, coat still on. The hand on the orange handle could open the door now, and does not.',bridge:'Ideas 1 and 2 were about reaching the answer. This one is about when to give it once you know it is no.',basics:{title:'A quick no is kinder than a slow one',beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Saying \u201cmaybe, I\u2019ll let you know\u201d is nicer than saying no.',reality:'If you already know it is no, a maybe just makes them wait. They can\u2019t make other plans. And you have to carry the no around all week.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE PARTY',text:'Your friend asks you to her party on Saturday. You know you can\u2019t go. You say, \u201cMaybe!\u201d All week she counts you in. On Friday you tell her. Now she is upset, and it is too late to ask someone else.'},{kind:'idea',text:'If you already know your answer is no, say it now. Waiting does not make a no softer. It just makes it land later, and harder.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'A quick no',items:['She knows today','She can ask someone else','You stop worrying']},right:{title:'A slow no',items:['She waits all week','She thinks you might come','You dread telling her']}},{kind:'picture',caption:'Someone has waited in the hall for ages, coat still on. The hand on the orange handle could open the door. It doesn\u2019t.'},{kind:'try',text:'Is there a no you have been putting off? Say it today, kindly: *Thanks for asking. I can\u2019t come, but I hope it goes well.*'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A friend asks you to join his team for a quiz next week. You know you will be away. What is the kind thing to do?',options:['Tell him now, so he can find someone else','Say \u201cmaybe\u201d so he does not feel bad','Wait and tell him the day before'],answer:0,why:'Telling him now gives him time to find someone. A maybe only keeps him waiting.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE CAT',text:'A neighbour asks if you can feed their cat while they are away next month. You know you will be away too. You say, \u201cLet me check and come back to you.\u201d They hear a likely yes and do not book anyone. A week later you say no.'},{kind:'idea',text:'\u201cLet me check and come back to you\u201d feels softer than no, and is usually worse. It costs the other person a week of uncertainty and costs you a week of low-grade dread.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHAT A DEFERRED NO DOES',nodes:['You put off a no you have reached','They hear a soft yes','They stop making other arrangements','The no arrives late and lands harder']},{kind:'explain',title:'The cost to you',text:'An undecided request does not sit quietly. It comes back each time you think of it, and the attention it takes is often more than the task itself would have. The discomfort you were avoiding is not avoided. It is spread across the week.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Someone has waited in the hall a long time, coat still on. The hand on the orange handle could open the door now, and does not.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Speed in both directions',text:'The discipline is to answer quickly whichever way the answer goes. A prompt no is more useful than a slow one, and far more useful than a yes given only to avoid the discomfort of saying no.'},{kind:'try',text:'Find one request you are sitting on where you already know the answer. Send it today.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You are invited to speak at a conference you already know you will not attend. Which reply follows the idea?',options:['Thank you, but I can\u2019t do this one, so please don\u2019t hold a place','Let me look at my diary and get back to you next week','Probably not, but I will confirm nearer the time','Yes, and I will try to find a way to fit it in'],answer:0,why:'You know the answer, so the courteous move is to give it now. The others keep them waiting, or trade a quick no for a reluctant yes.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'WHO PAYS FOR THE WAIT',prompt:'When you delay a no you have already reached, who bears the cost?',options:['Mostly you','Mostly them','Both of you'],answer:2,reveal:'The essay says both. They cannot plan around an open question. You carry an undecided request that keeps asking for your attention.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Delaying a refusal is usually framed as consideration and is generally the opposite. When the answer is already known, speed is the courtesy.'},{kind:'term',word:'Soft yes',meaning:'A delay or hedge the other person reasonably hears as probable agreement. Because they stop looking elsewhere, the cost appears only when the real answer, a no, finally arrives.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The argument',text:'The other party cannot make alternative arrangements while waiting, and often reads the delay as a soft yes, so the eventual no lands later and harder. Meanwhile you pay too: an open request occupies attention continuously, often more than the task would have needed.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'THE ESSAY\u2019S RANKING',low:'A yes given to escape saying no',high:'A prompt no',note:'A slow no sits between them. It beats a reluctant yes, but costs both sides more than a quick one.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The visitor waits, coat on, unable to plan anything else. The hand on the handle has known the answer all along.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The idea applies when you already know the answer. If you genuinely need to check, say when you will reply and keep to it. A large request may deserve thought before any answer. And how a refusal is heard varies between people and workplaces: the essay\u2019s claim that a prompt no suits almost everyone is a generalisation, not a rule.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these is a misuse of the idea?',options:['Refusing a big decision on the spot to seem decisive, before you know','Telling a colleague today you won\u2019t join a project you\u2019ve ruled out','Promising an answer by Friday because a date needs checking, then giving it','Declining an invitation within the hour instead of letting it sit'],answer:0,why:'The idea is about not delaying an answer you already have. It does not ask you to answer before you have one.'}]}},'the-two-lists_4':{pictureCaption:'A short orange card goes across the counter. The long page of reasons is crumpled. The fewer the reasons, the less there is to argue with.',bridge:'Idea 3 said to give a no quickly. This one is about how to word it so the friendship survives.',basics:{title:'Say no to the plan, not the person',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO SAY NO',text:'Your friend asks you to come and build a huge model on Saturday. You can\u2019t. You could say: *I can\u2019t, I\u2019m busy, and I\u2019ve got no lift, and my mum said\u2026* Or you could say: *I can\u2019t on Saturday, I have swimming. Could I come on Sunday?*'},{kind:'idea',text:'When you say no, keep it short and clear. Give one true reason. Offer something else if you can. Show it is the plan you are saying no to, not your friend.'},{kind:'equation',parts:['What you can\u2019t do','One true reason','What you can do'],result:'A kind, clear no'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why only one reason?',text:'Every extra reason is a problem your friend can fix. You say, \u201cI have no lift.\u201d They say, \u201cMy dad can drive you!\u201d Now you need a new reason. One true reason is enough.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One hand passes over a short orange card. The other hand crumples a long page of reasons. Short is kinder.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you need to say no, use three parts. *I can\u2019t do this. Here is why. Here is what I can do.* Then stop talking.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A friend asks to borrow your new bike for the whole weekend. You don\u2019t want to lend it. What is the best reply?',options:['\u201cI don\u2019t lend my new bike, sorry. Want to ride together?\u201d','\u201cMaybe. It might have a flat. And my dad might need it.\u201d','\u201cYou always break things, so no.\u201d'],answer:0,why:'It is clear, gives one reason and offers something else. The *maybe* invites a fix for each excuse. *You always break things* makes it about your friend.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'A kind refusal needs a full explanation, so the other person can see you really can\u2019t.',reality:'Elaborate justification reads as guilt and invites negotiation. Every reason you offer is an obstacle the other person can try to remove.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Decline the request, not the person. Say what you cannot do, give one true reason, offer what you can instead if anything, and stop.'},{kind:'steps',title:'A refusal in four moves',steps:['Say clearly what you cannot do. A hedge invites renegotiation.','Give one true reason, briefly.','Offer something real if you have it: another person, a smaller version, a later date you would honour.','Stop. Do not add reasons.']},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE OFFICE MOVE',text:'Asked to lead the office move, you reply: *I can\u2019t take this on, because I\u2019m committed to the audit until June. I can pass on my notes from the last move.* That is the whole message. There is nothing in it to solve.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What actually damages relationships',text:'In the essay\u2019s view, a refused request is often taken without much difficulty. What tends to sting is feeling declined personally, as though asking was the mistake. Much of the difference is in the phrasing: talk about the task and your capacity, not about them.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A short orange card goes across the counter. The long page of reasons is crumpled. The fewer the reasons, the less there is to argue with.'},{kind:'try',text:'Write your next refusal, then delete every reason but the truest one. Read what is left aloud.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A friend asks you to help them move house on Saturday. You can\u2019t. Which reply follows the idea?',options:['I can\u2019t on Saturday, I\u2019m away. I could bring food round on Sunday.','I would love to, but I\u2019m tired, my back hurts and the car is booked.','Honestly, you should have hired movers rather than asking friends.','Possibly. Let me see how the week goes and I\u2019ll let you know.'],answer:0,why:'Clear, one reason, a real offer. The list of excuses gives three problems to solve, the remark about movers declines the person, and *possibly* is a hedge.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'THE SAME NO, TWICE',left:{title:'Declining the request',items:['Names the task and your capacity','One reason, briefly','Offers what you can']},right:{title:'Declining the person',items:['Implies they were wrong to ask','Hedges, then over-explains','Leaves nothing on the table']},note:'The answer is no in both columns. Only one of them costs the relationship.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A refusal can end the request and keep the relationship. The mechanics are ordinary: be clear, give one true reason, offer something real where you can, and never imply the person was the problem.'},{kind:'term',word:'Handhold',meaning:'The essay\u2019s word for an extra reason in a refusal: something the other person can grab and solve. One reason is an explanation. Five are a list of problems to work through.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why more reasons make it worse',text:'Each reason turns the refusal into a problem with a possible solution, so the conversation becomes a negotiation. A long justification also reads as guilt, which suggests the no is not settled. A vague refusal fails the same way: neither side is sure what was decided.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The offer must be real',text:'The third part is optional, and it only works if it is true: a different person, a smaller version, a later date you would actually keep. An offer you do not mean is a delayed no, and it brings back the problem idea 3 described.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A short card, handed over. The page of reasons stays behind, crumpled, so there is nothing on the counter to argue with.'},{kind:'limits',text:'One reason works only if it is true. A convenient excuse gets solved or found out. Some relationships are owed more explanation: a close friend, a partner, someone who depends on you. Phrasing cannot remove real consequences when the person asking has power over you. And the claim that a refusal is often taken well is the essay\u2019s judgement, not data.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE COMEBACK',prompt:'You decline to host a meeting because your flat is small. They reply that they will bring folding chairs. What does the idea suggest?',options:['Restate the no plainly, and this time without a solvable reason','Add two further reasons so they see how firm the no is','Agree, since the obstacle you named has now been removed','Switch to a maybe so the conversation can end politely'],answer:0,why:'The reason became a handhold. More reasons give more handholds. A clear restatement closes the negotiation the first reason opened.'}]}},'the-two-lists_5':{pictureCaption:'One folder taken down from a full shelf of arrangements nobody has looked at in years. The question is whether it would go back up today.',bridge:'Idea 2 asked whether you would say yes if it started tomorrow. This asks the same of yeses you gave long ago.',basics:{title:'Check your old yeses too',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'EVERY TUESDAY',text:'You joined chess club two years ago because your best friend did. She left last year. You still go every Tuesday. You never chose to keep going. You just never chose to stop.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Old yeses keep going on their own. So every now and then, ask about each one: if I was asked today, would I say yes?'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why old yeses pile up',text:'Starting a thing needs a yes. Stopping it needs you to do something. Keeping on needs nothing at all. So things keep going long after the reason for them has gone.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A TOY BOX',text:'You keep putting toys in a box but never take any out. One day the lid won\u2019t shut. When you look, you have not played with most of them in ages.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A hand takes one old folder off a full shelf to look at it. Would you put it back if you were choosing today?'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one thing you do every week. Ask: if someone asked me to start this today, would I say yes? If not, talk to your family about it.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You have gone to drama club for years. Lately you dread it. Which question does this idea say to ask?',options:['If I was asked to start today, would I say yes?','How many years have I been going?','What will the teacher think if I stop?'],answer:0,why:'The years you have already done can\u2019t be changed. The useful question is whether it is worth it from now on.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'WHERE THE WEEK WENT',prompt:'Where does most over-commitment come from?',options:['New requests you agreed to recently','Old commitments that never ended'],answer:1,reveal:'The essay\u2019s answer is the old ones. Each was agreed once, and each renews silently, because nothing in the arrangement ever asks for a review.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Anything recurring should face the renewal question now and then: would I agree to this today, at this cost, knowing what I know now?'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why they never end',text:'The recurring meeting, the committee, the mentoring, the newsletter: each was agreed for a reason that may have expired. Each continues because ending it requires an act and continuing requires nothing.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Asked as \u201cshould I stop?\u201d',items:['Weighs the years already given','Feels like letting people down','Usually ends in carrying on']},right:{title:'Asked as a new request',items:['Weighs only the cost from now','Is just a decision','Often ends in a no']},note:'It is the question that works on subscriptions, and for the same reason.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE MONTHLY CATCH-UP',text:'You have mentored a former colleague monthly for three years. Asked fresh, you would not start it: she has outgrown it. You raise it, and find she had kept coming partly so as not to let you down.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One folder taken down from a full shelf of arrangements nobody has looked at in years. The question is whether it would go back up today.'},{kind:'try',text:'Take one recurring commitment and ask whether you would agree to it today, as a new request.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You have chaired a book group for five years and it now feels like a chore. Which question applies the idea?',options:['If they asked me today to start chairing it, would I agree?','Have I put in too many years to walk away now?','Would the group be disappointed if I stepped down?','Can I make the meetings shorter so it feels lighter?'],answer:0,why:'The re-ask treats it as a new request. Years given and the awkwardness of leaving are the weights it is designed to remove.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE SUBSCRIPTION TEST',text:'Many people know the move with subscriptions: would I sign up for this today, at this price? If not, cancel. The essay applies the same question to the commitments that fill a week, and argues that it works for the same reason.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Most over-commitment is not new requests but old ones that never ended. Standing commitments renew silently, so they need a periodic re-ask: would I agree to this today, knowing what I know now?'},{kind:'term',word:'Sunk cost',meaning:'Time, effort or money already spent that cannot be recovered. It feels like a reason to continue, but no choice made now can change it, so it should not decide that choice.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'OUTLIVING ITS REASON',nodes:['Agreed once, for a good reason','Renews with no prompt to review','The reason quietly expires','Ending needs an act, so nobody acts']},{kind:'explain',title:'What the re-ask removes',text:'Asked as a new request, the commitment loses two weights: the sunk cost of what you have already given, and the social weight of withdrawing. The essay says the answer is frequently no, and that several commitments often stopped being worth doing some time ago without anyone noticing, including the people they are done for.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Every folder on the shelf was agreed once. Only the one in the hand is being asked whether it still would be.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Some commitments are valuable because they persist: caring for a relative, a long friendship, a promise meant to be kept. Their worth is reliability, and re-asking them often erodes it. So the re-ask is periodic, not constant. Ending something still needs doing well, as idea 4 describes. And when the essay says the answer is frequently no, that is an impression, not a count.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these is a misuse of the re-ask?',options:['Ending weekly visits to a lonely relative because they fail as a new request','Leaving a committee whose original purpose ended two years ago','Cancelling a standing meeting that nobody has needed in months','Ending a newsletter that almost none of its readers now open'],answer:0,why:'For some commitments, steadiness is the point. The re-ask removes sunk cost and awkwardness, not the value of being someone who keeps turning up.'}]}}}},3028,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'up-from-slavery_1':{pictureCaption:'The students\u2019 own tools rest on a wall they are building. The bell beside it is for a school that is still going up.',basics:{title:'Build the thing, not just the argument',beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'VIRGINIA, AROUND 1856',text:'Booker T. Washington was born enslaved in Virginia, in the United States. That meant the law let another person own him. He was forced to work and had no say over his own life. Slavery there ended in 1865, when he was a young boy.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHAT HE DID NEXT',text:'He fought hard to get to school. Then, in 1881, he was asked to start a new school for Black students in a town called Tuskegee. There were no proper buildings. There was almost no money.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Washington did not spend his life trying to win arguments. He built a school, and then kept building. A thing that is built keeps working, even when people stop listening.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A TREEHOUSE',text:'Two friends argue about whether they can build a treehouse. One keeps arguing. The other starts nailing boards. By next summer the argument is forgotten. The treehouse is still there.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The students used these tools to build this wall. The bell will call them to a school they are building with their own hands.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE HARD PART',text:'It was not a free choice. In the South then, Black people faced cruel, unfair laws. To build, he needed money from rich white people, so he could not say all he thought. Some said he gave up too much.'},{kind:'try',text:'Got a big plan? Make the smallest real version of it this week, with what you already have. One page. One shelf. One practice.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your class wants a reading corner. Which choice follows Washington\u2019s idea?',options:['Bring in some books and cushions, and start one','Write a long letter asking the school to build one','Argue in class until everyone agrees it is a good idea'],answer:0,why:'A small, real reading corner works from day one. It also shows people what you mean better than any argument.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO SPEND A YEAR',text:'Your area has nowhere for teenagers to go after school. You could spend a year writing to the council about it. Or you could open a hall two nights a week, with borrowed tables, and see who comes.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Washington\u2019s method was to build institutions rather than win arguments. An argument persuades the people listening now. An institution keeps working whoever happens to be convinced.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'TUSKEGEE, ALABAMA, 1881',text:'Born enslaved and freed as a child, Washington was asked to open a school for Black students in Alabama. It began with no proper buildings and almost no money. Up From Slavery, published in 1901, is largely the account of what came next: buildings, a brickyard, farms, and donors across the country.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'An argument',items:['Persuades whoever is listening','Must be made again each generation','Stops when the speaker stops']},right:{title:'An institution',items:['Works whoever is convinced','Trains people who train others','Keeps running after its founder']},note:'The case for building is durability.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Students\u2019 tools laid on a wall they are raising themselves, and a bell for the school it will become.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why this route',text:'It was also chosen under constraint. In the American South of the 1890s, a Black educator had very few open roads, and demanding rights in public could be dangerous. Building something that white donors would fund was one of the routes that stayed open.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The price',text:'Critics, W. E. B. Du Bois above all, named the cost. A school built with that money is built on terms its funders set, and those terms become part of what gets built. Later lessons in this book return to that argument.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one argument you keep having. Ask what the smallest real version of the thing would be, made with what you already have, and start it this week.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A charity has spent years lobbying for a free legal clinic in its town. Which move follows Washington\u2019s method?',options:['Open a small clinic one evening a week with volunteer lawyers','Publish a longer, stronger report on the need for a clinic','Wait until the law changes before doing anything at all','Hold a public debate on whether legal clinics really work'],answer:0,why:'A small working clinic helps people now and keeps running whoever is persuaded. That is the durability Washington was after.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHAT THE BOOK IS',text:'Up From Slavery, published in 1901, is an autobiography and a contested political argument at once. Washington was born enslaved in Virginia, educated at Hampton Institute, and from 1881 led Tuskegee in Alabama. The book\u2019s through-line is construction: a school with no buildings, then buildings, a brickyard, a curriculum, a national network of donors.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Build institutions rather than win arguments. An argument persuades those already listening and has to be remade for each generation. An institution keeps operating regardless of who is currently convinced.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE SEQUENCE IN THE BOOK',nodes:['Classes with no proper buildings','Students raise the buildings','A brickyard, farms, a curriculum','Donors across the country'],note:'Each step made the next one fundable. None of it waited on winning a debate.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The case for durability',text:'Persuasion decays. Each generation, and each new legislature, has to be convinced again. A working school produces graduates, who teach others, and it goes on doing so without anyone remaking the original case. Much of the book is the practical detail of getting that started with nothing.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The conditions',text:'This was a strategy chosen under constraint, not simply a preference. In the 1890s Southern states were stripping Black men of the vote and writing segregation into law, and lynching was at its worst. Building something white donors would pay for was one of the few routes that stayed open.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Du Bois named the cost precisely: institutions built by accommodation are built on terms someone else sets, and the terms are part of what gets built. And building did not replace arguing. The rights Washington set aside were won later by protest, court cases and new laws, alongside institutions like his.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What are you trying to win by argument that could instead be built, in a small real version, and left running?'},{kind:'recap',points:['Arguments must be remade. Institutions keep operating.','Washington built because few other routes were open to him.','The funders\u2019 terms get built in too, and they are part of the price.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HONEST COST',prompt:'A campaign group is offered a large grant to run a youth centre, on condition it stops criticising the council that funds it. What does Du Bois\u2019s objection say?',options:['The condition becomes part of what is built, so weigh it as a cost','Building always beats arguing, so take the grant without hesitation','Any money with conditions attached should always be refused outright','Conditions do not matter, since the centre will outlive the council'],answer:0,why:'Du Bois did not say never build. He said the terms are built in with the walls, and must be counted as part of the price.'}]}},'up-from-slavery_2':{pictureCaption:'Nobody in this queue has been persuaded of anything. They need what is done at that bench. That was Washington\u2019s bet.',bridge:'Idea 1 described the method. This one states the bet behind it, and the strongest answer to it.',basics:{title:'Be needed first?',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A BIG QUESTION',prompt:'Black people in the South were treated very unfairly in Washington\u2019s time. What did he think would win them fair treatment first?',options:['Asking loudly for their rights','Having skills the town could not do without','Leaving the South for good'],answer:1,reveal:'He bet on being needed. If a town needs your work, he thought, in time it must treat you better.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHAT IT WAS LIKE',text:'After slavery ended, Black people in the South were free by law. But white people in power soon made new, unfair laws. Black and white people were kept apart. Most Black men were stopped from voting. Mobs murdered Black people, and the killers were rarely punished.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Washington\u2019s plan was this: become needed first, and fair treatment will follow. He did not think rights had to be earned. He thought this was the order they would come in.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Everyone in this line needs what that one worker can do. Washington bet that being needed like this would bring respect.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO DISAGREED',text:'W. E. B. Du Bois was another great Black thinker of the time. He said the rights were owed now. Waiting gave up today for a later that might never come. Both men wanted fairness. They disagreed about the way.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Washington',items:['Skills and work first','Rights will follow','Don\u2019t push too hard yet']},right:{title:'Du Bois',items:['Rights now, and keep asking','Waiting gives up today','Every kind of learning, not just trades']},note:'Reading both teaches more than reading either.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Two classmates want an unfair rule changed. One says, \u201cLet\u2019s first become the class everyone counts on.\u201d The other says, \u201cAsk now. It\u2019s only fair.\u201d Who thinks like Washington?',options:['The first one: be needed first','The second one: ask for it now','Neither: he thought rules never change'],answer:0,why:'Washington\u2019s plan was about order: be needed, then fairness follows. The second argues like Du Bois.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Washington thought Black Americans had to earn their rights.',reality:'He never argued the rights were not owed. His claim was about sequence: being economically necessary would secure rights that demands alone would not. Become necessary first, and standing follows.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Washington bet that economic indispensability would win rights that demanding them could not. It is a theory about the order in which rights arrive, not about whether they were owed.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Nobody in this queue has been persuaded of anything. They need what is done at that bench. That was the bet.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE BET, LINK BY LINK',nodes:['Learn skills the region needs','Own land, run trades','Neighbours come to depend on you','Rights follow the dependence'],note:'Every link is a prediction. The argument is over whether the last one ever holds.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Du Bois\u2019s answer',text:'In The Souls of Black Folk, in 1903, W. E. B. Du Bois replied at length. The plan, he argued, gave up the present for a future nobody guaranteed. Black Americans were urged to set aside the vote, civil rights and higher education for now, in the very years all three were being taken from them.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO THEORIES OF ORDER',left:{title:'Washington',items:['Build economic footing first','Avoid open confrontation','Rights follow need']},right:{title:'Du Bois',items:['Demand rights now','Protest openly, and keep protesting','Without the vote, gains can be taken']}},{kind:'try',text:'Read the third chapter of The Souls of Black Folk alongside this book. Note one point where you think each man was right.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Workers are told: accept low pay quietly for five years, and the firm will depend on you so much it must then treat you fairly. What is the Du Bois objection?',options:['Nothing guarantees the firm keeps its side once the five years are up','Workers should never try to make an employer depend on them','Low pay is acceptable as long as the work itself is dignified','The workers have not yet shown they deserve fair pay'],answer:0,why:'Du Bois\u2019s point was about guarantees. You give up the present now, and the future you were promised is not in your hands.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'timeline',kicker:'THE ARGUMENT, IN DATES',points:[{when:'1895',what:'Washington\u2019s Atlanta address offers a white Southern audience his bargain.'},{when:'1901',what:'Up From Slavery tells his life as evidence the plan works.'},{when:'1903',what:'Du Bois answers him in The Souls of Black Folk.'},{when:'1909',what:'The NAACP, a civil rights organisation, is founded with Du Bois among its leaders.'}],note:'The disagreement became a fork in strategy, not just a quarrel.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Economic indispensability would secure rights that demands alone could not: become necessary first, and standing follows. The quarrel was never about whether the rights were owed, only about the order in which they could be won.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Stated fairly',text:'The theory is not that Black Americans had to deserve their rights. It is a prediction about leverage. A region that depends on your skills, land and trade has a reason to concede what it would refuse to an appeal. Given the South of the 1890s, Washington judged that appeals alone would fail.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The objection',text:'Du Bois valued work and property too. His charge was that the plan conceded the present for a future that had no guarantee. While Black Americans were urged to wait, the vote was being stripped from them, segregation written into law, and support drawn away from Black colleges.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The queue is the theory: nobody here has been persuaded. Du Bois\u2019s question is what happens when they find another workshop, or decide to close this one.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Both sides have real answers. Economic footing did matter, and many of Tuskegee\u2019s graduates built it. But leverage can be cancelled by force. Prosperous Black communities were sometimes destroyed by white mobs, as in Tulsa in 1921. Without political power, what dependence earns can be taken back.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Where are you betting that being needed will bring the standing that asking would not? What guarantees the last step?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TEST THE THEORY',prompt:'Which fact is the strongest evidence against the theory\u2019s last link?',options:['Prosperous Black districts were sometimes burned by white mobs','Tuskegee graduates went on to start schools and businesses of their own','White Southern buyers paid for bricks made by Tuskegee students','Northern donors funded schools for Black students across the South'],answer:0,why:'The theory assumes need protects the needed. Violence against prosperous communities shows need could be overridden by force, which is why Du Bois insisted on political power.'}]}},'up-from-slavery_3':{pictureCaption:'Hands hold a new drawer to the light, its joint closed with no gap. The pride lives in work done this well.',bridge:'Idea 2\u2019s bet needed skills the region could not do without. This one is about how Washington wanted those skills valued.',basics:{title:'The pride is in doing it well',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'BIG JOBS, SMALL JOBS',belief:'Some jobs are worth being proud of, and some are not. It depends on the job\u2019s name.',reality:'Washington thought the pride is in how well you do a job. A field dug well can be worth as much as a poem.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO THOUGHT THIS',picture:!0,text:'Booker T. Washington ran a school for Black students in the American South. His students put up the buildings, grew the food and made the bricks. He made sure they learned each job properly.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The pride is in doing work well. A job done badly has no pride in it, whatever its name. A job done well has plenty, even if no one calls it grand.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'ON THE PITCH',text:'The striker gets the cheers. But a defender who blocks every shot played just as well. Her pride is in how well she played, not in the name of her position.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHY HE CARED SO MUCH',text:'Some people then thought anyone with schooling should never work with their hands. Washington thought that was wrong. He also thought skills the town needed would give his students a stronger place in it.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE CATCH',text:'Many white people then wanted Black people kept in hard, low-paid work only. Some Black thinkers feared his plan helped them. They wanted Black doctors and teachers too.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick a chore you think is boring. Do it really well today, better than you have to.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Two pupils paint the school fence. One paints neatly to every edge. The other rushes, saying it is only painting. Who would Washington praise?',options:['The neat painter did work to be proud of','Neither, since painting is not an important job','The fast one, since he finished first'],answer:0,why:'For Washington, the pride is in how well a job is done. Painting can carry plenty.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'WHAT DO YOU DO?',text:'At a party someone asks what you do. You say you are an electrician, and you watch them file you below the lawyer standing next to you. They have never seen either of you work.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Dignity is in the doing, not in the category. Work done well carries it, whatever it is called. Work done badly cannot, whatever title is on the door.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How he made it concrete',text:'At Tuskegee the students built the buildings, grew the food and made the bricks. Washington insisted they learn each trade properly, because work done badly cannot carry dignity, whatever attitude you bring to it. He was openly hostile to education pursued only for the status it brings.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Dignity from the category',items:['Comes with the job title','Survives poor work','Granted by other people']},right:{title:'Dignity from the doing',items:['Comes from how well it is done','Must be earned each time','Visible in the work itself']}},{kind:'picture',kicker:'NO GAP IN THE JOINT',caption:'Held up to the window, the joint has slid home with no gap. You do not need the maker\u2019s job title to see the dignity in it. It is in the work.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The tactical half',text:'The argument was also strategy. A community holding skills the local economy needs has leverage that no appeal can produce. The book carries both motives, the genuine and the tactical, and you can feel them pulling against each other in the prose.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The objection at the time',text:'Critics answered that in a society set on confining Black Americans to manual work, praising manual work risked supplying the argument for the confinement. Du Bois wanted colleges for those who could use them as well. Both positions can be defended.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you size someone up by their job title, ask instead how well they do the job. Then ask it of your own.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A company renames its cleaners \u201cfacilities associates\u201d but changes nothing about their training, tools or pay. By Washington\u2019s idea, has the work gained dignity?',options:['No: dignity comes from how well the work is done, not its name','Yes: the new title earns the cleaners more respect from others','Yes: the company has shown that it values the job more highly','No: cleaning is not the kind of work that can carry dignity'],answer:0,why:'A new name changes the category, not the doing. Washington put dignity in the doing, which also means cleaning can carry plenty of it.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE ATLANTA ARGUMENT',text:'In his 1895 Atlanta address Washington argued that a people could not prosper until it saw as much dignity in working a field as in writing a poem. Up From Slavery spends much of its length showing what that meant in practice at Tuskegee.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Attach the dignity to the work itself rather than to what the work is called. Skilled work done properly carries dignity; work done badly cannot, whatever its standing.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Shavings on the bench, a drawer corner held to the light, the dovetail closed with no gap. Dignity located in the doing: visible without knowing what the maker is called.'},{kind:'term',word:'Industrial education',meaning:'The model Hampton and Tuskegee taught: academic study joined to a trade, such as building, farming or sewing, learned to a working standard. Students often laboured at the school to pay their way.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Two motives in one argument',text:'Part of the argument is genuine. Washington had been shaped by work, from the salt furnace and coal mine of his boyhood to the broom at Hampton. Part is tactical: a community holding skills its neighbours need has leverage no appeal can produce. The prose carries both, and they do not always agree.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'WHERE HE STANDS',low:'Dignity from the title',high:'Dignity from the doing',note:'Washington sits at the far right. The danger was that others read his position as a ceiling on what Black Americans might do, not a floor.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The objection was made at the time and it is serious. In a society determined to confine Black Americans to manual trades, an emphasis on manual trades risks supplying the argument for the confinement. Du Bois insisted a people also needs scholars, teachers and leaders. Both positions are defensible, and the book is best read alongside the disagreement.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which work around you is given less respect than its quality deserves, and which is given more?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which use of Washington\u2019s idea would his critics most fairly object to?',options:['Citing it to argue a group should be trained only for manual trades','Citing it to insist an apprentice learn the trade to a high standard','Citing it to question respect paid to job titles over results','Citing it to defend a carpenter\u2019s pride in a well-made joint'],answer:0,why:'The idea says dignity can live in any work. Turned into a rule about which work a group may do, it becomes the confinement his critics feared.'}]}},'up-from-slavery_4':{pictureCaption:'Drafts struck through, a calm final copy, and a school under scaffolding outside the window. The tone was chosen for the school\u2019s sake.',bridge:'The first three ideas describe what Washington built. This one is about how he wrote about it, and for whom.',basics:{title:'Who was he writing for?',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'TWO KINDS OF MESSAGE',text:'You write a thank-you card to your gran for a jumper. Then you text your best friend about the same jumper. The facts are the same. The words are very different. You picked them for the person reading.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The way a book sounds can be a choice. Ask who it was written for, and what the writer needed from them.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'HOW HIS BOOK SOUNDS',text:'Booker T. Washington was born enslaved. Yet his book about his life is calm and kind. He speaks well of white people, even of the family that owned him. He says he will not let anyone make him hate.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'Why do you think the book sounds so calm?',options:['Nothing very bad ever happened to him','His school needed money from the people reading it','He did not notice the unfair laws'],answer:1,reveal:'His school ran on gifts, many from white people. Angry words could lose that help. So he chose a calm voice on purpose.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Old drafts, crossed out. A calm last copy. Outside, the school he needs is still being built.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHAT GOT LEFT OUT',text:'While he wrote, Black people in the South were losing the right to vote. Mobs were murdering Black people, and the killers were rarely punished. Almost none of this is in his book. The calm voice helped his school. It also kept the truth out of sight.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time something you read sounds odd, ask two things. Who was this for? What did the writer need from them?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A letter home says the school trip will be \u201camazing\u201d. It asks parents to pay for it. What is the best question to ask?',options:['Who it is for, and what it needs from them','Whether the writer is a cheerful person','Nothing, since school letters are always right'],answer:0,why:'The happy tone may be chosen for the parents who pay. Knowing the reader helps you read the words.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A BOOK BY A MAN BORN ENSLAVED',prompt:'Up From Slavery has almost no bitterness in it. Former owners get credit and grievances are understated. What explains the tone best?',options:['Washington was a forgiving man by temperament','Life in the South had become fairly peaceful','It was written for readers who funded his school'],answer:2,reveal:'He may well have been forgiving. But the book was addressed to Northern donors and Southern whites, and he was among the most skilled fundraisers of his era. The tone was a decision.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The book\u2019s conciliatory tone is a strategy, not a temperament. Every page is written for an audience that could withdraw its support, and reading it that way changes every page.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Read as temperament',items:['A gentle man writes gently','The book seems sentimental','Its silences look innocent']},right:{title:'Read as strategy',items:['A careful man writes for funders','The book becomes intelligible','Its silences become choices']}},{kind:'picture',kicker:'THE LAST DRAFT',caption:'The drafts at his elbow are struck through; the copy going out is calm and even. Through the window, the school that the calm voice is paying for is still going up.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What was left out',text:'The book was written in the years lynching was at its worst and Southern states were completing the removal of Black men\u2019s votes. Almost none of that appears. The tone bought the school, and it distorted the testimony.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Public voice, private action',text:'Historians working in his papers later found that Washington secretly helped pay for court challenges to segregation and to the loss of the vote. In public he said little. The calm voice was a policy, not the whole man.'},{kind:'try',text:'Take something written for an audience you are not part of, such as a fundraising letter. Ask who it was for and what it needed from them. Then reread it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A charity\u2019s annual report is upbeat throughout and barely mentions a scandal that year. What is the best reading?',options:['The tone is aimed at donors, so its silences need checking','The scandal must have been minor, given how calm it reads','The writers are simply optimistic people by temperament','Upbeat reports are always dishonest and should be ignored'],answer:0,why:'A tone chosen for an audience is not a lie, but it tells you what may be missing. The silence is the thing to check.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'IN INSTALMENTS, 1900\u20131901',text:'Up From Slavery first ran in instalments in a magazine before appearing as a book in 1901. Its readers were mostly white: Northern donors, and Southern whites whose tolerance Tuskegee depended on. Washington was by then the best-known Black American, and among the most sophisticated fundraisers of his era.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The book\u2019s relentlessly conciliatory tone is a decision, not a disposition. Read as temperament it is sentimental. Read as strategy it is intelligible, because every paragraph is calculated for readers who could withdraw support.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The stated policy',text:'Washington gives the tone a principle: he refuses to let anyone, of any race, shrink his soul by making him hate them. That may be true of the man. In a book addressed to donors it is also an instrument, and he was skilled enough to know it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Struck-through drafts at the elbow, an even final copy under the pen, scaffolding through the window. What reaches the reader is the last draft, written for what it had to buy.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHAT THE TONE BOUGHT',nodes:['A calm, grateful public voice','White donors and officials reassured','Money and tolerance for Tuskegee','The school survives and grows'],note:'Every link worked. The cost sits outside the chain, in what the book does not say.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The record it leaves understates what was happening. It was written in the years lynching peaked and disenfranchisement was being completed across the South, and almost none of that appears. Taken as testimony about the South around 1900, it misleads. The tone bought the school and it distorted the testimony.'},{kind:'explain',title:'A second record',text:'Historians working in his private papers found that he secretly funded legal challenges to segregation and disenfranchisement. The conciliatory voice was a public strategy run beside private resistance. That supports the strategic reading, and shows its limit: what he would not say in public, the public never heard.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of a document you rely on. Who was it written for, and what did it need from them?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A historian reads a 1950s company history that praises its founder and never mentions a long strike. What does this lesson suggest?',options:['Ask who the history was for, and treat its silences as evidence','Discard it, since anything written for an audience is worthless','Trust its tone, since a calm voice suggests a calm period','Assume the founder was gentle, as the prose itself suggests'],answer:0,why:'Writing for an audience does not make a source useless. It tells you what to check, and the silences become part of what it shows.'}]}},'up-from-slavery_5':{pictureCaption:'The walls are close and will not move. The walker has stopped pushing at them and is going forward through the space they leave.',bridge:'Idea 4 read the book\u2019s tone as a choice made under pressure. This one asks what skill that pressure taught.',basics:{title:'Find what you can do inside the rules',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'A SMALL PATCH OF GARDEN',text:'You get one tiny patch of garden. You can\u2019t make it bigger. So you look closely. Which corner gets the sun? What grows in shade? By summer, your small patch is full of food.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WASHINGTON\u2019S WALLS',text:'Booker T. Washington lived when Black people in the South had very little freedom to speak or act. If he said the wrong thing, his school could lose its money, or worse. He could not move those walls by himself.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When you can\u2019t change the rules, work out exactly what you can still do inside them. Then do as much of that as you can.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The walls are close, and they don\u2019t move. This walker is not pushing at them. He walks on through the space they leave.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'A SPEECH IN 1895',text:'In 1895 Washington gave a famous speech in the city of Atlanta to a mostly white crowd. He told them what they wanted to hear: Black people would not push to mix with white people. In return he asked for their help with jobs and schools.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WAS HE RIGHT?',text:'Many think he gave away far too much. Others think he got the most he could. People still argue. But almost all agree he was very good at this skill.'},{kind:'try',text:'Stuck with a rule you can\u2019t change? Make two lists: what the rule stops, and what it still lets you do. Start on the second.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'It is raining, so everyone must stay inside at break. You can\u2019t change that. What fits this idea?',options:['Work out what you can do inside, then do it','Spend break arguing that you should go out','Sit still and wait for the rain to stop'],answer:0,why:'The rule is fixed for today. The skill is finding the best thing you can still do inside it.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'ATLANTA, 1895',text:'Speaking to a largely white Southern audience, Washington told of a ship lost at sea, its crew dying of thirst. A passing ship signalled that they should lower their buckets where they were. They did, and drew up fresh water: they had drifted into the mouth of a great river. His point was to build here, with what was at hand.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Map precisely what is possible inside your constraints before spending everything on moving them. Then take the most that space allows.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The reassurance and the ask',text:'In the same speech he said the races could be as separate as the fingers in social life, yet one as the hand in shared progress. That gave his audience the reassurance it required. In return he secured white support for Black work and schooling, and his own national standing.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Close walls that are not going to move this year. The walker is not arguing with them. He has found the line he can walk.'},{kind:'steps',kicker:'THE METHOD IN THE BOOK',title:'Mapping the room',steps:['Find out what will be funded.','Find out what will be tolerated.','Find out what can be said, and in which room.','Put your effort into that space, not into the walls.']},{kind:'explain',title:'The price',text:'Whether the price was too high is the heart of the argument with Du Bois. Accepting separation in public, critics said, helped make segregation look acceptable. Both sides have real answers. What is not in dispute is the skill.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick a constraint you can\u2019t change this year. Write down what it forbids, then what it still allows. Spend this week only on the second list.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A new manager inherits a frozen budget and a sceptical board. Which response applies the idea?',options:['Find what the board will back now and put the effort there','Spend the year campaigning to have the budget unfrozen','Refuse to start anything until conditions improve','Ignore the board and spend as if there were no limit'],answer:0,why:'The budget will not move this year. The skill is mapping what is possible inside it and taking all of that.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'A WORD FOR IT',word:'Accommodation',meaning:'The name given to Washington\u2019s approach: accepting for now limits you object to, such as segregation, in exchange for room to build. His critics called the 1895 speech the Atlanta Compromise for this reason.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Up From Slavery is a manual for acting when the range of moves is genuinely narrow: be precise about the constraint, then extract the maximum from the space inside it rather than spending yourself on the walls.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Precision, not defiance',text:'Washington works out what will be funded, what tolerated, what can be said in which room. The Atlanta address is the clearest case. Its image of fingers separate in social matters but one hand in shared progress gave a white audience the reassurance it required, while securing support for Black economic and educational advance.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'WHERE THE ENERGY GOES',low:'All of it on the walls',high:'All of it inside them',note:'Washington stood far to the right. Du Bois argued that someone also has to push on the walls, or they close in.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A narrow space, walked at full height. The walls are not being argued with, and that is both the skill and the question.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The cost of working skilfully inside a constraint is that you can end up endorsing it. The year after the Atlanta address the Supreme Court upheld segregation in Plessy v. Ferguson, and critics argued that public acceptance from the most prominent Black leader made segregation easier to defend. Whether the price was too high has real answers on both sides.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which constraint are you spending this year fighting that you could instead map, and work inside, until it can be moved?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HONEST OBJECTION',prompt:'Which objection does the idea have to take most seriously?',options:['Working skilfully inside a limit can make the limit look acceptable','Constraints are never real, only a failure of imagination','Mapping constraints takes time that should go on action','Skill inside limits cannot produce anything that lasts'],answer:0,why:'That was the core of the case against the Atlanta address: the reassurance it gave was itself a contribution to the walls.'}]}},'up-from-slavery_6':{pictureCaption:'The room is already clean, and the hands are still working, up on a ledge nobody would check. The broom by the door was the examination.',bridge:'Idea 3 put the dignity in the doing. This is the test that began Washington\u2019s own education, years before Tuskegee.',basics:{title:'The test was a broom',beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'SETTING OFF, 1872',text:'Booker T. Washington had been born enslaved. Now he was free, about sixteen, and hungry to learn. He set off for a school called Hampton, about 500 miles from home. He had very little money. When it ran out, he walked and begged rides.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'He got there dusty and tired. The head teacher was not sure about him. What test do you think she gave him?',options:['A hard written exam','Reading out loud','Cleaning a room'],answer:2,reveal:'She told him to sweep the classroom next door. He swept it three times. He dusted it four times. She wiped a hanky over the wood. No dust. He was in.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The small job you are given first is often the real test. Do it as if it counts, because it usually does.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The floor is clean, but the hands are still wiping a high ledge. Nobody is watching. The broom by the door was the test.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHY IT WAS A GOOD TEST',text:'You can\u2019t fake a clean room. The only way to get one is to clean it. And no one stood and watched him. So the test showed how careful he was when nobody was checking. Later he called it his college exam, and he was proud he passed.'},{kind:'try',text:'Today, do one small job you were given as if someone will check every corner. See how long it takes to do it properly.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'On your first day at a new club, the coach asks you to pack away the cones. What does this idea say?',options:['Do it carefully, because it may be a test','Do it fast, because it is not real training','Leave it, since new members should not have to'],answer:0,why:'Small first jobs often show the most about you. Nobody can fake doing them well.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE FIRST WEEK',prompt:'In a new job, your manager\u2019s first request is to tidy a shared drive nobody has touched in years. What is it most likely to be?',options:['Busywork to keep you occupied','A test of how you work unsupervised','A sign they don\u2019t rate you'],answer:1,reveal:'Often it is a task and a test at once. Washington\u2019s entrance examination at Hampton was exactly this kind of job, and he saw that it was.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Treat the small assigned task at the start of something as the assessment, because it usually is. It shows how you work when nobody is watching.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'HAMPTON INSTITUTE, 1872',text:'Washington arrived after a journey of about 500 miles, partly on foot, with almost no money. The head teacher hesitated over him, then told him to sweep the room next door. He swept it three times and dusted it four. She rubbed her handkerchief over the woodwork and found nothing. He calls it his college examination, and reports passing with pride.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The floor is done, and the hands are still on the high ledge nobody would check. The broom by the door was the examination.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it is a rare kind of test',text:'It was unfakeable: the only way to produce a clean room is to clean it. And it measured thoroughness under no supervision. Most exams measure preparation for the exam. This one could not be studied for.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why he told it',text:'The story also does a job. It sets out Hampton\u2019s philosophy, character shown through work rather than recitation, and it flattered readers who valued exactly that. Neither purpose cancels the other.'},{kind:'try',text:'Find the smallest task someone else assigned you this week. Do it as if it were the assessment, and finish the parts nobody will check.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A new volunteer at a food bank is asked to sort donated tins by date. Which approach applies the idea?',options:['Check every date, since the sorting shows how you work','Sort quickly, since the real work will start later','Ask for a task that uses your skills better instead','Sort the front shelves, since nobody checks the back'],answer:0,why:'The first small task is where people learn how you work. Thoroughness nobody checks is the thing it reveals.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'The broom story is just a charming anecdote about hard work.',reality:'It is the book\u2019s most retold passage because it does several jobs at once. It states Hampton\u2019s philosophy, it flatters an audience, and it makes a genuine point about how to assess people.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The small assigned task at the start of something is usually the real assessment. A test that can only be passed by doing the thing measures what an exam that can be prepared for cannot.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'THE EXAMINATION',text:'Arriving at Hampton in 1872, worn and dirty from the road, Washington was not admitted at once. The head teacher told him to sweep an adjoining recitation room. He swept it three times and dusted it four. She went over the woodwork with a handkerchief, found no dust, and let him in. He calls it his college examination, without irony.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What made it a good test',text:'It was unfakeable, because a clean room can only be produced by cleaning. It measured thoroughness under no supervision. And it could not be studied for. Most examinations measure preparation for the examination. This one measured the habit itself, which is rarer than it sounds.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Clean floor, hands still on the high ledge. The test measured exactly what nobody would have checked.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it is in the book',text:'The anecdote also works on its reader. It presents character demonstrated through work rather than recitation, the philosophy Washington carried to Tuskegee, and it reassures donors who valued exactly that. Both functions are real.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Unfakeable tests are not automatically fair. Who is handed the broom and who the written paper is a choice, and critics of the Hampton model argued it steered Black students toward labour rather than learning. Small tasks also favour people already trained in them. Treat the first task as an assessment, but do not assume every assessor is fair.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What was the first small task you were given in your current role? What was it really testing?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A hiring manager wants a test that cannot be prepared for. Which comes closest to the broom?',options:['Give a real small task from the job and judge the result','Ask candidates to describe how thorough they usually are','Set a written exam on the company\u2019s published methods','Pick the candidates with the most relevant qualifications'],answer:0,why:'The broom worked because the only way to pass was to do the thing well. Descriptions, exams and credentials can all be prepared or borrowed.'}]}},'up-from-slavery_7':{pictureCaption:'A register of names, and a pen beside the empty page. The next line was one Washington had to fill from nothing.',bridge:'This idea goes back before Hampton, to a schoolroom in West Virginia, and to what slavery had taken before he arrived.',basics:{title:'The boy who chose his own last name',beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'AFTER SLAVERY ENDED',text:'Booker was born enslaved. When slavery ended in 1865, his family moved to West Virginia. Booker was still a boy, but he had to work in a salt works. Then, at last, he got to go to school.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE FIRST DAY',text:'The teacher called out the names. Every other child gave two: a first name and a family name. Booker had only ever been called Booker. His turn was coming.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'What do you think he did?',options:['Said he only had one name','Made up a last name on the spot','Stayed quiet and said nothing'],answer:1,reveal:'When his turn came, he said Booker Washington. He picked the name right then, and kept it all his life.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Some rules expect everyone to have the same things. Booker\u2019s school expected a family name. Slavery had left him without one, so he had to make his own.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHY HE HAD NO LAST NAME',text:'Under slavery, people were owned like things. The law did not care about their families. Parents and children could be sold away from each other. Booker did not even know who his father was.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A list of names, and a pen by an empty page. Booker had to fill in his line with a name he made up.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'YEARS LATER',text:'He found out his mother had given him another name at birth: Taliaferro. He put it in the middle. So he became Booker T. Washington. The T came from his mother.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A new pupil is asked to bring a baby photo for a class display. She has none: her family had to flee their home. What does Booker\u2019s story help you see?',options:['The task expected every child to have a baby photo','She should have found a photo from somewhere','Baby photos are not important at all'],answer:0,why:'Like the school register, the task expected something not everyone has. Noticing that is fair, and kind.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'TEN MINUTES, FOR MOST',text:'A form asks for your previous addresses, your parents\u2019 names and a reference from a former employer. For most people it takes ten minutes. For someone just out of care, or newly arrived, every box asks about something they never had.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'MALDEN, WEST VIRGINIA',text:'Freed as a child, Booker started school and noticed that the other children had two names. He had only ever had one. When the teacher asked his full name, he answered Booker Washington, chosen on the spot and kept for life. He later learned his mother had named him Taliaferro at birth, and took it as a middle name.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A routine moment, a teacher writing down a name, showed what slavery had taken: a recognised family line. A process built on an assumption tests for that assumption, whether it means to or not.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why he had no surname',text:'A surname records a family line. Slavery refused to recognise enslaved families. Their marriages had no legal standing. Parents and children could be sold apart. Washington writes that he never knew who his father was.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A register of names, and a pen beside the empty page. The next line was one Washington had to fill from nothing.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How he tells it',text:'Washington reports the moment without comment. That is typical of him, and it makes the moment land harder. This small scene says more about what slavery took than any outright attack in the book.'},{kind:'try',text:'Look at one form, sign-up page or first-day routine you control. List what it assumes everyone already has. Who arrives without it?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A bank requires three years of address history to open an account. What should its designers notice?',options:['The rule tests for a settled past, not for trustworthiness','The rule is fair, because it applies to everyone equally','People without the history should simply wait three years','The rule hardly matters, since most applicants can meet it'],answer:0,why:'People who have moved often, fled, or left care cannot meet it however honest they are. The process is testing for the assumption.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'A ROLL CALL, AFTER 1865',text:'In a schoolroom in Malden, West Virginia, a boy who had only ever been called Booker heard the other children answer with two names. When the teacher reached him he said Booker Washington. Newly free, he was supplying from nothing what the register assumed every child brought with them.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The register assumed a surname, and a surname assumes a family line that slavery refused to recognise. When someone arrives without what a process takes for granted, the process is testing for the assumption.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What a surname records',text:'A surname is a record of lineage. American slavery denied enslaved families any standing in law: marriages had no legal force, a child\u2019s status followed the mother\u2019s, and families could be divided by sale. Washington never learned who his father was.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The force of understatement',text:'Washington tells the scene flatly and moves on. The flatness is the argument. A denunciation invites a hostile reader to resist; a teacher writing down a name does not. The passage says more about what slavery took than any condemnation in the book.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Names already written, a page still blank, a pen waiting. The register assumed every child arrived with what one child did not have.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The scene is easily misread as cheerful self-invention, a boy choosing who to be. That reading erases the loss it records. And not every formerly enslaved person lacked a surname: many families kept names among themselves that the law ignored. The point is what the institution refused to see, not that no names existed.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which process you take part in assumes everyone arrives with the same things? Whom does it quietly fail?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A college requires a parent\u2019s signature on every scholarship application. What does the surname passage suggest it is really testing for?',options:['Having a parent available to sign, rather than merit or need','The applicant\u2019s honesty about their family\u2019s income','Whether the applicant\u2019s family supports their education','Nothing in particular, since the rule applies to everyone'],answer:0,why:'Students who are orphaned, estranged or in care cannot meet it. Like the register, the form tests for an assumption, whatever it was meant to measure.'}]}},'up-from-slavery_8':{pictureCaption:'One good brick lifted clear of the new stack. Behind it, in the dark, stand the kilns that came before.',bridge:'Idea 1 sketched the whole building. This is one part of it up close, and idea 2\u2019s bet at work in a single county.',basics:{title:'Three kilns failed. The fourth worked.',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TUSKEGEE, 1880s',prompt:'Washington\u2019s new school needed buildings, and it had almost no money. Where should the bricks come from?',options:['Buy them from a shop in town','Ask rich people to send bricks','The students make them'],answer:2,reveal:'The students dug the clay and made the bricks. Bricks are baked hard in a big oven called a kiln. At first it went badly wrong.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHAT HAPPENED',nodes:['The first kiln fails','The second kiln fails','The third kiln fails','He pawns his watch','The fourth kiln works'],note:'To pawn something is to hand it over for cash, and buy it back later if you can.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Learning to make something real takes failing and trying again. And making something people need can change how they treat you.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One good brick, lifted from the new pile. Behind it, in the dark, stand the kilns that came before.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE BRICKS WENT TO TOWN',text:'Soon the school had bricks to spare. White families nearby began to buy them. Washington said this did more to win over his white neighbours than any speech. It did not end the unfair laws. But it made the school matter to the town.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A BAKE SALE',text:'Your class runs a bake sale. The first cakes sink. The next ones burn. You keep at it. By the third week, other classes queue up to buy. Now they know your class for something good.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your class\u2019s first garden dies. Then the second one dies too. Which move fits Washington\u2019s brickyard?',options:['Work out what went wrong and plant again','Give up, since gardens are just not for you','Buy plants from a shop and say you grew them'],answer:0,why:'The failed kilns were how the students learned. Each try taught them something the next one used.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'THE BRICKYARD',text:'Tuskegee needed buildings, and Washington decided the students would make the bricks. The first three kilns failed. With no money left, he pawned his watch to pay for a fourth attempt. That one worked, and the brickyard became a lasting part of the school.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The failures are the argument: some things can only be learned by making them. And producing what the surrounding society needs can change how a group is regarded where speeches cannot.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the failures stay in',text:'Washington keeps the failed kilns in the story on purpose. A brick that holds depends on clay, heat and repetition. The students who finally made good ones understood something no lecture could have given them.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE BRICKS CHANGED THINGS',nodes:['Students learn to make good bricks','Spare bricks sold to white neighbours','A trading relationship now exists','Tuskegee matters to the county'],note:'Washington says this did more for local relations than any appeal could. It is his whole method in small.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A good brick lifted clear of the stack. Behind it stand the kilns that came before, and what they taught.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What it could not do',text:'A trading relationship is not equal rights. In 1901 Alabama adopted a new constitution designed to take the vote from Black citizens, brickyard or not. The need bought goodwill in a county. It did not buy protection in law.'},{kind:'try',text:'If you need a group to see your team differently, list what your team can supply that they actually need. Start supplying it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Local shopkeepers see a neighbourhood youth group as a nuisance. Which move follows the brickyard method?',options:['Offer a service the shops need, such as weekend deliveries','Write an open letter defending the group\u2019s reputation','Hold a meeting to explain how misunderstood they are','Avoid the shops until the bad reputation fades away'],answer:0,why:'Supplying a real need builds a relationship that argument cannot. That was how the bricks worked in the county.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'ONE YARD, THE WHOLE METHOD',text:'Tuskegee\u2019s buildings were raised by students from bricks they made themselves. The first three kilns failed; Washington pawned his watch to fund a fourth, which worked. The yard then sold bricks to the surrounding white community, a commercial tie where none had existed.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Produce something the surrounding society needs, and let the need do the work argument cannot. The brickyard is Washington\u2019s strategy in small: skill learned through failure, then turned into a relationship.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Learning that cannot be told',text:'Washington keeps the failures because they are the evidence. A brick that holds depends on clay, temperature and repetition, knowledge that lives in hands that have watched kilns fail. The students who learned it could not have been given it by instruction.'},{kind:'equation',parts:['Skill learned by failing','Something neighbours need'],result:'A changed relationship',note:'Either part alone falls short. Skill with no buyer stays private; need with no skill is only a promise.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A sound brick lifted clear, the earlier kilns dark behind it. The skill in that brick was paid for by the ones before.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Need is leverage only while it lasts and only as far as it reaches. White neighbours could buy Tuskegee\u2019s bricks and still support the 1901 Alabama constitution, written to strip Black citizens of the vote. Commerce changed relations in a county; it did not change the law. This is Du Bois\u2019s objection to the whole strategy, seen in one yard.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What could your group supply that another group actually needs? What would that change, and what would it leave untouched?'},{kind:'recap',points:['Some skills are learned only by failing at them.','Supplying a real need can change how a group is regarded.','Need can win goodwill. It does not secure rights.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'WHERE IT STOPS',prompt:'A firm owned by a minority group becomes a major supplier to a town whose council shuts that group out. What does the brickyard lesson predict?',options:['Better trade relations, but no political standing without other action','Full political inclusion, once the town comes to depend on the firm','No change at all, since trade never alters how groups are regarded','The town will drop the supplier rather than accept dependence'],answer:0,why:'The bricks changed relations in Macon County, but not Alabama\u2019s laws. Need wins goodwill; rights take political power.'}]}},'up-from-slavery_9':{pictureCaption:'A bare room with one thing on the shelf: a standard its owner can keep perfectly, from the first day, with no money.',bridge:'Idea 5 mapped what was possible inside narrow limits. This is the smallest version of it: one standard a student could hold completely.',basics:{title:'Start with one small thing',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'A NEW SCHOOL, WITH NOTHING',text:'Picture arriving at a new school with almost nothing. No money. One set of clothes. You can\u2019t control much at all. So what can you control, starting today?'},{kind:'idea',text:'When big things are out of your hands, pick one small thing you can do every day, and never skip it. Good habits can grow from there.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHAT WASHINGTON DID',text:'At his school, Booker T. Washington made every student keep a toothbrush and use it. He wanted baths, clean shirts and tidy rooms too. His old teacher, General Armstrong, called it the gospel of the toothbrush. Gospel here means a message you believe in deeply.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A bare room, and one toothbrush on the shelf. It is small, but its owner can keep it up every single day.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHY A TOOTHBRUSH?',text:'A student with no money could still do it, from the very first day. Washington thought one habit kept every day helps you keep the next. He believed it changed his students, and even their families back home.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHY SOME PEOPLE DISAGREE',text:'Some say this taught Black students to please the white people who treated them unfairly. Others say it was about health and pride. You can find both in his book. That is why people still argue about him.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one small thing you can do every day this week. Make your bed. Pack your bag the night before. Don\u2019t skip a day.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your family is moving house and everything feels out of your hands. What fits this idea?',options:['Keep one small daily habit, like reading at bedtime','Wait until the move is over to start any habits','Try to fix every problem with the move yourself'],answer:0,why:'You can\u2019t control the move. You can control one small thing each day, and that gives you solid ground.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Washington\u2019s fuss over toothbrushes and clean shirts was just fussiness about appearances.',reality:'He chose standards a student could control completely, on the first day, with no money. Then he used one standard firmly held as the foundation for the rest.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When nothing large is in your control, pick one small standard you can hold absolutely, and hold it. The habit of keeping one standard becomes the base for others.'},{kind:'explain',title:'His reasoning',text:'Washington argued that habits of order transfer: a student who keeps their person in order keeps their work in order. He credits the toothbrush with changing the habits of students, and through them their families.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A bare room with one thing on the shelf: a standard its owner can keep perfectly, from the first day, with no money.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Not trivial then',text:'In this period, access to sanitation was itself a marker of class and race. Respectability was one of the few kinds of protection open to Black Americans. It was often not enough to protect them.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO READINGS, ONE TEXT',left:{title:'Self-command',items:['A standard you fully control','Builds the habit of order','Dignity under constraint']},right:{title:'Acceptability',items:['Meeting others\u2019 idea of decent','Answering prejudice by conforming','The burden falls on the wronged']},note:'The text supports both readings. That is why the argument about Washington has never settled.'},{kind:'try',text:'Choose one standard so small you cannot fail it, such as a cleared desk at six. Hold it for two weeks before adding anything.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'After losing their job, someone feels that nothing is in their control. Which response applies the idea?',options:['Fix one daily standard, such as dressed and at a desk by eight','Wait until a new job brings back a sense of control','Draw up a full plan to change every part of their life','Accept that routines matter little until things improve'],answer:0,why:'One standard they fully control gives them firm ground now. The bigger things can be built on it.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'BEFORE YOU READ ON',prompt:'Washington gives cleanliness more space than many readers expect from a political leader. What was it chiefly for?',options:['Making students acceptable to white employers','Giving students a standard they controlled completely','Both, and the text supports each reading'],answer:2,reveal:'The book makes both cases. He chose a standard a student could hold from the first day with no money, and he wrote for readers who prized respectability.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When nothing large is in your control, pick one small standard you can hold absolutely, and hold it. Cleanliness came first at Tuskegee because it was the standard a penniless student fully controlled.'},{kind:'term',word:'The gospel of the toothbrush',meaning:'Washington\u2019s name, which he credits to Samuel Armstrong, the head of Hampton, for the rule that every Tuskegee student keep and use a toothbrush, alongside baths and clean clothes, as the base of the school\u2019s training.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The theory of transfer',text:'His reasoning is that habits of order transfer. A student who maintains their person maintains their work, and the habit travels home to families. Cleanliness also carried social meaning: access to sanitation marked class and race, and respectability was one of the few protections available.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One toothbrush, and nothing else. The smallest standard there is, held completely, and the question of whose standard it is.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Respectability protected less than it promised. Clean, educated, property-owning Black Americans were still lynched and still lost the vote. And the gospel is the part of Washington most easily read as teaching the oppressed to be acceptable to their oppressors, putting the burden of prejudice on its targets. The text supports that reading as well as the other.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'THE SAME TOOTHBRUSH',low:'A discipline held for yourself',high:'A conformity performed for others',note:'The habit is identical at both ends. What changes is who set the standard and why.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which small standard do you keep for yourself, and which do you keep because someone is watching? Can you tell them apart?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'A critic says the gospel of the toothbrush simply trained students to please their oppressors. Which reply stays honest?',options:['That reading is supported, but so is self-command under constraint','The critic is wrong, since hygiene carries no social meaning at all','The critic is right, so the habit had no value to the students','Washington never cared what white readers thought of his students'],answer:0,why:'Both readings are in the text. An honest answer grants the critic\u2019s point and keeps the other one in view.'}]}}}},3029,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={walden_1:{pictureCaption:'The buyer is not paying in coins. What crosses the counter is drawn out of their own sleeve: a length of their life.',basics:{title:'Pay in hours, not money',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A NEW GAME',prompt:'A game costs \xa330. You earn \xa35 for each car you wash for a neighbour, and each car takes an hour. What does the game really cost you?',options:['\xa330','Six hours of washing cars','Nothing, if you saved up'],answer:1,reveal:'The shop says \xa330. But you got that money by working. So the game costs six hours of your Saturdays. That is the real price.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The real price of a thing is the time you must give to pay for it. Count it in hours of your life, not in money.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Look at what the buyer pays with. It is not coins. It comes out of their own sleeve. It is their time.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO SAID THIS',text:'Henry David Thoreau was a writer in the United States. In 1845 he went to live in a small hut by a pond called Walden. He wanted to find out what he really needed. He worked out that six weeks of work a year paid for it all.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'TRY IT ON TRAINERS',text:'You want trainers that cost \xa360. You get \xa35 a week of pocket money. So they cost twelve weeks. A \xa330 pair costs six weeks. Are the fancy ones worth six more weeks?'},{kind:'myth',belief:'This means you should never buy anything.',reality:'No. Thoreau just wants you to see the real price. Some things are worth the hours. Then buy them, and enjoy them.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend wants a phone case for \xa310. She gets \xa32 a week for chores. How would Thoreau want her to think about it?',options:['It costs five weeks of chores','It is only \xa310, so it is cheap','She should never spend her money'],answer:0,why:'Turning the price into time shows what she is really trading. Then she can decide if it is worth it.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE NICER FLAT',text:'A flat with a second bedroom costs \xa3300 more a month. You take home \xa320 an hour. That is fifteen hours of work a month, every month you rent it: close to a working month of your life every year.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The cost of anything is the amount of your life you have to exchange for it, now or later. Price decisions in hours, and the comparison changes.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the price tag misleads',text:'A price in pounds sits next to other prices in pounds, so \xa3300 looks small beside the rent. A price in hours sits next to the rest of your life: evenings, a year off, a job you might leave. That is the comparison Thoreau wants made, and the price tag never invites it.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE CONVERSION',nodes:['Price in pounds','Divide by take-home pay per hour','Hours of your life','Would you trade those hours?'],note:'The answer is sometimes yes. The point is that you are now answering the real question.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'WALDEN POND, 1845',text:'Thoreau, a writer from Concord, Massachusetts, built a one-room house by Walden Pond and kept his accounts to the cent. He found that about six weeks of paid work a year covered his needs. The rest of the year, by his reckoning, was his own.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The buyer pays with something drawn from their own sleeve. That is Thoreau\u2019s price: not coins, but a length of your life.'},{kind:'try',text:'For the next thing you consider buying, divide the price by what you earn in an hour after tax. Ask whether you would hand over that many hours for it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A sofa costs \xa31,200 and you take home \xa315 an hour. Which thought applies Thoreau\u2019s rule?',options:['It costs eighty hours of work: is it worth two working weeks?','It is cheap compared with what other sofas cost','Spend nothing beyond bare need, whatever the item','It is fine as long as it fits the monthly budget'],answer:0,why:'The rule turns the price into the life spent earning it, then asks if the trade is worth it. It does not forbid the sofa.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'THE FIRST CHAPTER',text:'Walden, published in 1854, opens not with the pond but with a long chapter called Economy. Thoreau, who lived beside the pond from 1845 to 1847, sets out his accounts and argues with the way his Concord neighbours spend their lives earning.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The cost of a thing is the amount of life that must be exchanged for it, at once or in the long run. Every purchase is a trade of hours, and should be judged as one.'},{kind:'term',word:'Cost, in Thoreau\u2019s sense',meaning:'Not the price, but the amount of your life you must give up, now or later, to get a thing. Money is only the go-between.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The method, not the number',text:'He worked out that about six weeks of labour a year covered his needs at the pond, and treated the rest of the year as his own. The figure belongs to one man with no family, in one place. What transfers is the conversion: every price restated as time.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE EXTRA BEDROOM',text:'\xa3300 a month more, at \xa320 an hour after tax, is fifteen hours a month: roughly a working month in every year of the tenancy. The bedroom now competes with a month of your year, not with other flats. You may still take it. You will have chosen it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Nothing crosses the counter but a ribbon drawn from the buyer\u2019s sleeve. In Thoreau\u2019s accounts, that is what every price is paid in.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The conversion assumes you can trade hours at the margin. Many people cannot work less, so the time a cheaper choice \u2018saves\u2019 never reaches them. Hours are not all equal: work you enjoy is not pure loss. And the rule buys freedom only for those with income to spare. Thoreau wrote as a single man with few obligations.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these is a misuse of Thoreau\u2019s rule?',options:['Refusing all spending, since every pound is life lost','Choosing a cheaper flat in order to work a four-day week','Deciding a course is worth forty hours of earnings','Pricing a car in months of work before buying it'],answer:0,why:'The rule makes the trade visible. It does not say every trade is bad; some things are worth the hours.'}]}},walden_2:{pictureCaption:'Every object in the hallway is fine and costly. Together they stand between the man and the one door he is trying to reach.',bridge:'Idea 1 priced a purchase in hours. This one adds the hours a thing keeps costing after you have bought it.',basics:{title:'When nice things get in the way',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE FISH TANK',text:'You wanted a big fish tank so you could watch the fish. Now it needs food every day and a clean every week. When the filter breaks, it needs a new one. Your Saturday mornings go on the tank.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Some nice things get in the way of the life they were meant to make better. Each one needs time to look after. That time comes from the things you really wanted to do.'},{kind:'term',word:'Hindrance',meaning:'Something that gets in your way. Thoreau said most fancy things are not just harmless extras. They are hindrances.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Every object in this hall is fine and costly. But they are all in the way of the one door he wants to reach.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW IT HAPPENS',nodes:['You buy a nice thing','It needs cleaning and fixing','That takes your free time','Less time for what you wanted']},{kind:'myth',belief:'Thoreau thought all nice things are bad.',reality:'No. He knew people need warmth, shelter and food. He meant that after a point, each new thing costs more time than it gives back.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Sam buys a fancy bike to ride with his friends. Now he spends each Saturday cleaning it, and he won\u2019t take it on muddy paths. What is going on?',options:['The bike is now in the way of the riding','Sam just needs to take more care of it','Bikes are a waste of money for everyone'],answer:0,why:'He bought it to ride with friends. Now looking after it stops him riding. That is a nice thing turning into a hindrance.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE BIGGER PLACE',prompt:'You move to a bigger home so you will finally have room for your own projects. A year on, what is most likely?',options:['You use the room for your projects most evenings','You work longer hours to pay for the room','Nothing much has changed'],answer:1,reveal:'The room arrived with rent, heating, furniture and cleaning attached. The hours to pay for them came out of the evenings it was meant to free.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Many comforts do not just cost money. They get in the way of the very thing they were bought to make possible, because their upkeep eats the time.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'An indulgence',items:['A harmless extra','Costs you once','Can be enjoyed or ignored']},right:{title:'A hindrance',items:['Something in the way','Costs you again every week','Demands attention, used or not']},note:'Thoreau chose the second word on purpose. The argument is in that choice.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where the time goes',text:'Every comfort comes with upkeep. It must be paid for, stored, insured, repaired and in time replaced. Each of those takes hours, and the hours come from whatever the comfort was meant to free you for. Past some point, each addition costs more attention than it returns.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A hallway of fine, costly things, and a man squeezing between them towards the one door he actually wanted.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'HIS EVIDENCE',text:'Thoreau pointed to the ancient philosophers of China, India, Persia and Greece. By choice, they were poorer in outward things than anyone, and he admired them above all. If comfort produced clear minds, he argued, the pattern would run the other way.'},{kind:'try',text:'List the five most expensive things you own. Next to each, estimate the hours a year it takes to pay for, keep and fix. Does any of them buy a life you would not otherwise have?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Which of these is most clearly a hindrance in Thoreau\u2019s sense?',options:['A boat bought for relaxing weekends, now repaired most weekends','An expensive meal enjoyed once, on a birthday','A warm coat that gets you through the winter','A secondhand bike ridden to work every day'],answer:0,why:'The boat now eats the weekends it was bought to fill. A one-off treat or a real need is not in the way of anything.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Thoreau is simply telling people to spend less, out of thrift.',reality:'His claim is stronger. He calls most luxuries, and many so-called comforts, positive hindrances: not harmless extras but things standing in the way of a better life.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Past a threshold, comforts impede the very thing they were bought to enable, because each brings upkeep that consumes the attention it was meant to free.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The evidence he offers',text:'He points to the ancient philosophers of China, India, Persia and Greece, who chose to be poorer in outward things than the poor of his own town. If comfort produced clarity of mind, the admired would be the comfortable. The pattern, he argues, runs the other way.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE UPKEEP LOOP',nodes:['A comfort bought to free time','Upkeep: pay, store, insure, repair','Longer hours to cover it','Less time, so buy another comfort'],loop:!0,note:'The loop closes when the remedy for lost time is another purchase.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where the threshold is',text:'Thoreau is not against warmth, shelter or food. Some comforts earn their place. His point is that the threshold, where an addition starts costing more than it returns, is almost never noticed, because each step past it looks like an improvement.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Fine things, each worth having, packed so close that the man must turn sideways to pass. The door he wants is the small one at the end.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The argument is easier for someone without dependants. A family car or a second room can be exactly what makes a shared life work, and upkeep is not always a loss: some people enjoy it. His evidence is also selective. Admired philosophers who lived simply show the pattern is possible, not that comfort causes muddle.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'A critic says Thoreau just disliked nice things. What is the best reply?',options:['His test is whether upkeep eats what the thing was bought to enable','He thought any comfort beyond bare survival was a moral failing','He only objected to luxuries that other people could see','He meant that cheap things are always better than dear ones'],answer:0,why:'The objection is about function, not taste. A comfort becomes a hindrance when its upkeep blocks its own purpose.'}]}},walden_3:{pictureCaption:'The room has been emptied of everything but one chair, set in the window light. The clearing was done so that something could happen there.',bridge:'Idea 2 said comforts can get in the way. This one asks what clearing them away is actually for.',basics:{title:'Clear things out for a reason',beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Living simply means owning as little as you can.',reality:'Thoreau did not think having less was good in itself. He cleared things away so he had time to think, read and write. The point is what the space is for.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE BIG CLEAN',text:'You tidy your whole room on Saturday. It looks great. Two weeks later it is a mess again. Nothing in your days changed, so the mess came back.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Before you clear things out of your life, decide what the space is for. If you clear with no reason, it soon fills up again.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Everything has gone from this room but one chair in the light. Someone cleared the room so they could sit there.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Two or three, not a hundred',text:'Thoreau wrote that your life should hold two or three main things, not a hundred. He thought life gets wasted in tiny bits. Lots of small jobs, none of them big, eat up the whole day.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one thing you want more time for. Maybe drawing, or football. Now find one small thing to drop to make room for it.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Leo quits two clubs to get more free time. A month later he is just as busy with other things. What did he skip?',options:['Deciding what the free time was for','Quitting even more of his clubs','Making a neater list of what he does'],answer:0,why:'Space with no purpose fills up again. Thoreau cleared things so that one thing could happen.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'FOUR MONTHS LATER',text:'You clear the whole flat. Bags to the charity shop, bare shelves, a weekend of work. It looks wonderful. Within four months it is exactly as it was, and you cannot say where it all came from.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Simplicity is a means, not a virtue. Clear the clutter so that something specific can happen, such as attention, work or thought. Otherwise it simply returns.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The half nobody quotes',text:'Thoreau\u2019s famous triple repetition of the word simplicity comes with an instruction: keep your affairs to two or three, not a hundred or a thousand. That is a number, and a small one. He is not asking for a mood of simplicity while you keep forty commitments.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Simplifying as a mood',items:['Clear the shelves','Keep every commitment','A tidier version of the drift']},right:{title:'Simplifying for a purpose',items:['Name what the space is for','Cut affairs to two or three','The time goes to that']}},{kind:'explain',title:'Why working harder fails',text:'His diagnosis is that life is frittered away by detail. Not ruined, frittered, in pieces too small to notice. So effort spread across a hundred affairs gives you a hundred slightly better-kept affairs, and the same fritter.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One chair kept, set square in the light. Everything else went so that someone could sit there and do one thing.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before you clear anything, finish one sentence: this space, or this time, is for\u2026 If you cannot finish it, hold off clearing.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A friend drops three standing commitments to simplify. Which next step fits Thoreau\u2019s idea?',options:['Decide which one or two pursuits the freed evenings now serve','Keep cancelling commitments until the diary is empty','Buy a better planner to manage what remains','Enjoy the lighter diary for its own sake'],answer:0,why:'Simplifying is for something. Without a named purpose the evenings refill, or the emptiness itself becomes the goal.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE SECOND CHAPTER',text:'In the chapter on where he lived and what he lived for, Thoreau repeats one word three times, simplicity, and is usually quoted no further. The next clause is the practical half: keep your affairs to two or three, not a hundred or a thousand.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Simplicity is an instrument. The count of your affairs is the problem, and reducing it matters only because it frees attention for something you have chosen.'},{kind:'term',word:'Fritter',meaning:'To waste in pieces too small to notice one at a time. Thoreau\u2019s diagnosis is that life is frittered away by detail, not ruined by disaster.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why effort cannot fix it',text:'Applied across a hundred affairs, more effort yields a hundred slightly better-tended affairs and the same fritter. The cure is subtraction, not management. His test is whether you could keep your accounts on a thumbnail. If the list needs a system to hold it, the system is hiding the problem.'},{kind:'scale',low:'A hundred affairs, well managed',high:'Two or three affairs, chosen',note:'Moving along this line is not tidying. It changes what your days are made of.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A cleared room with one chair kept and placed in the light. The emptiness is not the point. The chair is.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Not every life can shrink to two or three affairs. Caring for children or parents arrives as a hundred small duties that cannot be cut. Simplicity can also become a project of its own, with its own gear and rules, which is the very drift the idea warns against.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A team with forty open projects buys a new tool to track them all. What would Thoreau say?',options:['The tool tends the forty; the problem is that there are forty','Good tools are how a busy team achieves simplicity','Forty projects is fine if each one is well documented','Simplicity is a private matter, not a question for work'],answer:0,why:'If the list needs a system to hold it, the list is the problem. Cutting the count beats managing it.'}]}},walden_4:{pictureCaption:'A still pond, one leaf and a few spreading rings. Nothing here demands attention, and the watcher has chosen to give it anyway.',bridge:'Idea 3 cleared space for something. Here is what Thoreau used his for: looking closely, and living on purpose.',basics:{title:'Look closely, on purpose',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE WAY TO SCHOOL',text:'You walk the same way to school every day. Hundreds of times. But can you say what is in the third garden on the left? Most people cannot. They walked past it but never looked.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Paying attention is a skill, and it grows when you use it. Most people only look hard at things that shout at them. Thoreau looked hard at plain things, on purpose.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'AT WALDEN POND',picture:!0,text:'Henry David Thoreau lived by a pond for two years. He watched it in every season. He wrote about its ice, its birds and its fish. People said the pond had no bottom, so he measured it. It did have one.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A MUSCLE',text:'Looking closely is like a muscle. If you never use it, it stays weak. Use it a little each day, and you start to see things you missed before.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why he did it',text:'Thoreau said he went to the woods to live on purpose. He did not want to reach the end of his life and find he had been busy all the time, but never really there.'},{kind:'try',text:'Spend ten minutes looking at one plain thing. A tree, a window, a puddle. Keep looking until you see something new. Write it down.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Which of these is paying attention on purpose?',options:['Watching one tree for ten minutes to see what lives on it','Watching a film because it is loud and exciting','Flicking through lots of photos of trees'],answer:0,why:'The film grabs your attention for you. Choosing a plain thing and looking hard is the skill Thoreau practised.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'EIGHT HUNDRED TRIPS',prompt:'You have walked the same route to work hundreds of times. How much of its left-hand side could you describe?',options:['Most of it, after so many trips','Very little, unless something made me look'],answer:1,reveal:'Repetition does not produce attention. You saw the route hundreds of times and looked at it almost never, because nothing on it demanded to be looked at.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Attention is a skill that improves with use, and most people only practise it on what demands it. Thoreau practised it on purpose, on ordinary things.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'NOTHING DEMANDING',caption:'No alarm, no screen, no deadline. A pond, a leaf, rings spreading on the water, and someone who has decided this is where his attention goes.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'THE POND IN WINTER',text:'Locals said Walden Pond had no bottom. Thoreau cut holes in the ice, let down a weighted line, and measured it: a little over a hundred feet at the deepest. Much of the book is like that. A pond, its ice, its birds, its visitors, watched closely.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The word that matters',text:'His best-known statement of purpose says he went to the woods to live deliberately, so that he would not find, at the end, that he had not lived. The fear is not death. It is being busy the whole time and never present, which can happen without a single day going wrong.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Already running',items:['Started before you chose it','Pulled by whatever demands attention','Busy all day']},right:{title:'Deliberate',items:['Chosen now','Aimed at what you picked','Present for some of it']},note:'The stance needs no cabin. You can take it on a Tuesday, in a city.'},{kind:'try',text:'Several times today, ask: what am I doing right now on purpose, and what am I doing because it was already running?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Which habit best fits this idea?',options:['Spending ten minutes a day studying one ordinary street closely','Reading the news thoroughly every morning without fail','Moving somewhere quiet so that nothing can distract you','Keeping a long list of things you mean to notice'],answer:0,why:'The news already demands attention, and no move is needed. The practice is choosing something ordinary and attending to it on purpose.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'INDEPENDENCE DAY, 1845',picture:!0,text:'Thoreau moved into his cabin at Walden Pond on 4 July 1845 and stayed two years, two months and two days. Much of what he wrote there is close observation: the ice, the birds, the visitors, the depth of the pond, which he sounded and mapped.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Attention is a skill, improved by practice, and most people exercise it only on what demands it. To live deliberately is to decide where it goes.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The clause people skip',text:'His statement of purpose is famous for its first half: he went to the woods to live deliberately. The second half explains it. He did not want to discover, when he came to die, that he had not lived. The fear is continuous busyness without presence.'},{kind:'term',word:'Deliberately',meaning:'On purpose, after weighing. Thoreau pairs it with fronting only the essential facts of life: a decision about where attention goes, not a feeling of calm.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'WHERE ATTENTION COMES FROM',low:'Pulled: by alarms, screens and deadlines',high:'Placed: on what you chose to study',note:'Most days sit near the left end. The pond chapters are an exercise in living at the right.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'NO CABIN REQUIRED',text:'A commuter picks one tree on the route and looks at it properly each day for a month: bark, birds, what changes with the weather. Nothing about the commute has changed except that one part of it is now chosen.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The ideal assumes spare attention. A carer or a shift worker short of sleep may have none to place. Close observation can also become withdrawal, a way of studying a pond instead of the people who need you. Thoreau\u2019s leisure rested on few obligations, which is not everyone\u2019s condition.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What did you attend to today only because it demanded it? What did you choose?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISREADING',prompt:'Which reading of living deliberately is closest to Thoreau\u2019s?',options:['A decision about where attention goes, possible in any setting','A calm mood that comes from living close to nature','A plan to withdraw from society to avoid distraction','A promise to fill each day with memorable events'],answer:0,why:'He describes a decision, not a feeling or a place. The cabin was his setting, not his method.'}]}},walden_5:{pictureCaption:'The cabin is in the woods, yet the village is in plain sight, footprints cover the path, and three chairs wait by the door.',bridge:'Idea 4 called the stay an exercise in attention. This one shows how close to town it was run, and why that matters.',basics:{title:'The hut was near the town',beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Thoreau lived all alone, deep in a wild forest, far from anyone.',reality:'His hut was about a mile and a half from his town. He walked into town most days. Friends came to visit often.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The hut is in the woods. But look at the path. It is full of footprints. The town is just over there. And three chairs wait by the door.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Thoreau\u2019s two years at the pond were a test, not a survival story. He wanted to see how little he could live on, and he did it close to town.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A FAIR TEST',text:'In a fair test in science at school, you change one thing and keep the rest the same. Thoreau changed one thing: how much he spent. He kept his town, his friends and his family close.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why this is good news',text:'If he had lived in a wild forest, you could say: I could never do that. But he did not. He tried living on less, close to home. You could try a small piece of that too.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of something amazing a person did that you could never do. Find out what really happened. The true story is often smaller.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Someone says Thoreau proved you can live alone in the wild. What is a better way to say it?',options:['He showed you can live on very little, near a town','He showed that nobody needs friends or family','He showed you must live far away to be free'],answer:0,why:'He stayed close to town and to people. His test was about spending less, not about being alone.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'picture',caption:'A cabin in the woods, as promised. But the village is in plain sight, the path is trodden with footprints, and three chairs wait by the door for visitors.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the stay involved',text:'Walden Pond was about a mile and a half from Concord. Thoreau walked into town most days and had a steady stream of visitors. His family in town is often said to have done some of his washing. The land itself belonged to his friend, the writer Ralph Waldo Emerson.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Walden was a controlled experiment in spending less, run close to town. Reading it as a hermit\u2019s survival tale misses the argument it makes.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'The myth',items:['A hermit in the wilderness','Survival against nature','Out of reach for most people']},right:{title:'The arrangement',items:['A cabin near town','An experiment in spending less','Repeatable on a small scale']},note:'Which version you believe decides whether the book is any use to you.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the myth does damage',text:'The hermit version lets readers wave the book away: I could never live in the woods. The real version makes a precise claim instead. A person willing to want less can buy back much of their time, and the test can be run in a small way, almost anywhere.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time someone describes a dramatic retreat, a year off or a move abroad, ask what the arrangement actually was before measuring yourself against it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A colleague says Walden is pointless because nobody can live alone in the woods now. What is the best reply?',options:['It was never about isolation; it tested how little a life could cost','Thoreau did live alone in the wild, but times have changed since','The book is about nature, so the isolation was never important','Anyone could still live alone in the woods if they wanted to'],answer:0,why:'The stay was close to town. The claim it tests, that wanting less buys back time, travels anywhere.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'timeline',kicker:'TWO YEARS, NEAR TOWN',points:[{when:'1845',what:'Moves into the cabin at Walden Pond, on land owned by Emerson'},{when:'1846',what:'Spends a night in Concord jail for refusing to pay a tax'},{when:'1847',what:'Leaves the pond after two years and two months'},{when:'1854',what:'Publishes Walden, after years of revision'}],note:'The arrest happened on one of his ordinary walks into town.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The stay was a controlled experiment in reduced expense, run close enough to town to be repeatable. The hermit myth turns a precise economic claim into a fantasy.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The arrangement',text:'The pond was about a mile and a half from Concord. He walked into town most days and received a steady flow of visitors, and his family is often said to have done some of his washing. Little of this is hidden: the book has chapters on his visitors and on the village.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A cabin with the village in plain sight, a path trodden by footprints, and three chairs by the open door: the town ties the myth leaves out.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the myth costs',text:'The exaggeration lets readers dismiss the book as impractical instead of engaging with its claim: that a person willing to want less can buy back most of their time. Read as an experiment, the book becomes testable. Read as a feat, it only needs admiring.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Critics who use the laundry story to mock Thoreau as a fraud overcorrect; he never claimed to be cut off. But the experiment did rest on things not everyone has: free use of a friend\u2019s land, no dependants, family nearby. Repeatable is not the same as available to all.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which admired experiment, someone else\u2019s or a plan of your own, have you judged by its myth rather than its arrangement?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A founder\u2019s story says she built her company alone in a garage. What does this idea suggest you do first?',options:['Find out what support and money the arrangement really had','Assume the story is false, since such stories usually are','Conclude you would need a garage to begin your own venture','Accept it as told, since founders know their own histories'],answer:0,why:'The habit is to check the arrangement before comparing yourself. The honest version is usually smaller, and more useful.'}]}},walden_6:{pictureCaption:'Hands squaring a column of figures, a brick holding down the page. The house is argued for one item at a time.',bridge:'Idea 5 called Walden an experiment. An experiment needs its results written down, and this is where Thoreau writes them.',basics:{title:'Show the list, not just the claim',beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'A HUT FOR $28',text:'When Thoreau built his hut, he wrote down every cost. Boards. Nails. Hinges. Chalk. Bricks. Getting it all to the pond. The total was twenty-eight dollars and twelve and a half cents. He even kept the half cent.'},{kind:'idea',text:'If you say something costs little, show the list. A list of real numbers can be checked. A claim with no numbers is only a feeling.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Hands lining up a column of numbers. The brick is part of the house. Every piece of it is on the list.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'AT HOME',text:'You tell your family you could all save money on snacks. They shrug. Then you write down a week of snacks and what each one cost. Now they can see it. Now you can talk about it.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'A claim',items:['We spend too much on snacks','Nobody can check it','Easy to shrug off']},right:{title:'A list',items:['Crisps \xa31, juice 80p, and so on','Anyone can check it','Hard to shrug off']}},{kind:'try',text:'Write down all you spend for one week. Every sweet, every top-up. Then look at the list.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Two friends want the class to raise money for a trip. Who will most likely win the teacher over?',options:['Amir, who lists each cost and adds it up','Bea, who says it will not cost very much','Cal, who says lots of other classes did it'],answer:0,why:'A list can be checked. That is what makes people trust it.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO ARGUE',left:{title:'A principle',items:['We could live on much less','Sounds wise','Cannot be checked']},right:{title:'A receipt',items:['Boards, nails, lime, hinges, chalk','Total: $28.12\xbd','Can be argued with']},note:'Thoreau chose the right-hand column, and that is why the book survived.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A claim about what a life costs needs receipts. Until it is itemised, an argument for spending less is a preference dressed as analysis.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'THE HOUSE ACCOUNT',text:'Thoreau did not just say a person could live on very little. He listed the house: boards, nails, lime, hinges, chalk, transport. He totalled it at twenty-eight dollars and twelve and a half cents. Then he listed his food, item by item, for months.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the half-cent matters',text:'A specific number invites attack, and either survives it or does not. A general claim about simplicity cannot be attacked, so it convinces nobody who was not already convinced. Thoreau\u2019s figures have been argued over ever since, which is the point. They can be.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A column of figures squared with a rule, a brick on the corner. The case for a cheap house, made one line at a time.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'AT THE KITCHEN TABLE',text:'You want the household to cut back. Saying \u201cwe spend too much\u201d starts an argument about values. Bringing three months of statements, totalled by category, starts an argument about \xa3140 a month on takeaways. The second argument can actually be settled.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before making the case for spending less, itemise three months of actual spending. Argue from the list.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You want your partner to agree to cancel some subscriptions. Which approach follows Thoreau?',options:['List every subscription with its monthly cost, and total them','Explain that a simpler life tends to be a happier one','Point out that your friends manage with far fewer','Cancel the wasteful ones first, then explain why'],answer:0,why:'The list turns a disagreement about values into one about numbers, which can be checked and settled.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'A LEDGER, NOT A SERMON',text:'Most writing in praise of simple living offers a principle. Walden\u2019s first chapter offers a ledger. Thoreau prints the cost of his house line by line and his food by the item, and he keeps the half-cent.'},{kind:'idea',text:'An argument about what a life costs is only as strong as its accounts. Itemised, it can be attacked and can survive. Unitemised, it is a mood.'},{kind:'term',word:'Itemise',meaning:'To list each cost separately, with its amount, so the total can be checked line by line. A total without items has to be taken on trust.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'$28.12\xbd',text:'The house account runs: boards, refuse shingles, laths, two second-hand windows, a thousand old bricks, lime, hair, nails, hinges, a latch, chalk, transport. The total is twenty-eight dollars and twelve and a half cents, for a shingled, plastered room about ten feet by fifteen.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Hands squaring a column of figures, a brick holding the page. The house survives as an argument because it survives as a list.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it survived',text:'A specific figure invites attack, and Thoreau\u2019s have been argued over ever since: what he left out, what he borrowed, whose land it was. That argument is possible only because he gave numbers. A general claim about simplicity cannot be refuted, and so persuades nobody who was not already persuaded.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Itemising proves what was spent, not what was needed. Thoreau\u2019s account leaves things out: the land was lent and the axe borrowed. A precise list can lend false authority to an incomplete one. And some costs, such as care, risk and lost income, never appear on a receipt at all.'},{kind:'recap',points:['An unitemised claim is a preference dressed as analysis.','A specific number can be attacked, and so can persuade.','Always check what a precise list leaves out.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE WEAK CASE',prompt:'Which of these arguments would Thoreau\u2019s method treat as the weakest?',options:['\u201cWe could easily live on half,\u201d with no figures behind it','A budget showing housing takes 48% of monthly pay','Twelve months of energy bills, listed and totalled','A costed plan that omits the car, and says so'],answer:0,why:'Only the claim with no figures cannot be checked. The others can be argued with, which is what makes them worth hearing.'}]}},walden_7:{pictureCaption:'Every window lit, every curtain drawn, every door alike. Only one lets its light out onto the street.',bridge:'Idea 6 put the numbers on the table. This one asks you to put a shared opinion there too.',basics:{title:'Say what everyone is thinking',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE NEW LUNCH RULE',text:'Your school brings in a new rule at lunch. Everyone thinks it is silly. At the table, everyone says so. But when the teacher asks how it is going, nobody says a word.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'Why does the rule stay?',options:['Because everyone likes it','Because nobody said what they think'],answer:1,reveal:'The teacher heard silence. From the outside, silence looks just like agreeing. So the rule stays, and everyone helped keep it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When everyone thinks the same thing but keeps it quiet, nothing changes. Saying it out loud, once, can change it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Every house is lit, but every curtain is shut. Only one door lets its light out where people can see it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Who said this',text:'Henry David Thoreau was a writer in the United States. He said lots of people live with quiet unhappiness. One way to read it: the key word is quiet. People felt it, but hid it, and acted as if all was fine.'},{kind:'try',text:'Is there a thing your friends all say in private but never out loud? Next time it comes up with a grown-up, say it once. Be calm and kind.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your whole team thinks practice starts too early. What fits this idea?',options:['One of you tells the coach, calmly, what you all think','You all keep moaning to each other after practice','Each of you just starts turning up late'],answer:0,why:'Moaning in private changes nothing. Saying it once, to the person who decides, puts it in the room.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE REVIEW MEETING',text:'Over coffee, every person on the team agrees the new process is worse. In the review meeting, someone asks how it is going. A pause. \u201cFine,\u201d says someone. Everyone nods.'},{kind:'idea',text:'On one reading, Thoreau\u2019s complaint was not that his neighbours held wrong opinions, but that they held them privately and acted as if they did not. The arrangement lasts because nobody makes it a fact in the room.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Quiet is the key word',text:'Thoreau wrote that most people lead lives of quiet desperation. Read for its working word, the line is less about unhappiness than about why nobody mentions it. Each person knows. The room does not.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW IT KEEPS GOING',nodes:['Everyone privately disagrees','Nobody says so in the meeting','Silence reads as agreement','The arrangement continues'],loop:!0,note:'From outside, silence and assent look the same. Your silence is counted as a vote.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A street of matching doors, every curtain drawn. One door lets its light out onto the pavement. That is all it takes to differ.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it costs less than you fear',text:'You are not introducing a new view. You are making public one the room already holds. That usually costs less than expected, because the people who might object are the people who agree.'},{kind:'try',text:'In the next meeting, say the thing everyone already agrees about in private. Once, plainly, without softening it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Everyone on a project privately thinks the deadline is impossible. Which move fits the idea?',options:['Say so plainly in the planning meeting, once','Work late and hope the deadline quietly slips','Raise it with each colleague privately, one by one','Wait for someone more senior to mention it first'],answer:0,why:'The private agreement is already there. What is missing is the fact being said where the decision is made.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'The quiet desperation passage is a lament about how unhappy most people are.',reality:'On this reading the working word is quiet. Thoreau is pointing at concealment: people who privately know their arrangement is wrong and act in public as if it were not.'},{kind:'idea',text:'An arrangement everyone privately doubts survives because no one makes the doubt public. Silence is not neutral: from outside it cannot be told apart from assent.'},{kind:'term',word:'Common knowledge',meaning:'Something everyone knows, and knows that everyone else knows. A private view becomes common knowledge only when it is said in public. Until then, each person can only guess.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why saying it once works',text:'Each person in the room holds the view. None can be sure the others do. Stated once, on the record, it becomes common knowledge, and the arrangement loses the look of consent that kept it going. You are not adding an opinion, only removing a pretence.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Identical doors, identical lit windows, every curtain drawn. One strip of light escapes onto the street, and that is the only difference.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'HIS OWN REMEDY',text:'Thoreau\u2019s own remedy was drastic: he rearranged his life around his doubts and then published his reasons, at length. Few can do that. The smaller version is open to anyone: state plainly, once, what the room already believes.'},{kind:'limits',text:'This reading leans hard on one word of a famous line, which also speaks of resignation and despair as such. And speaking up is not always cheap. Where dissent is punished, or where you have misjudged the room, the cost falls on you. Check that the private agreement is real before treating it as shared.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HARD CASE',prompt:'You are not sure the room agrees with you. What does the idea still allow?',options:['Testing the agreement privately, then stating it once if it holds','Speaking for everyone, since they probably agree with you','Staying silent, since silence is the safe and neutral choice','Assuming the others are content, since nobody has spoken'],answer:0,why:'The idea depends on a view the room actually holds. Silence is not neutral, but nor is claiming support you have not checked.'}]}},walden_8:{pictureCaption:'An empty chair faces a window at first light. Nothing in the room has started yet, and that is the condition Thoreau calls morning.',bridge:'Idea 7 was about what you say to the room. This one is about the time before you let the room in.',basics:{title:'Your own work first',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'SATURDAY, FIRST THING',prompt:'You wake up with a great idea for a story. There are also new messages on your phone. What should you do first?',options:['Read the messages, then write','Write first, then read the messages'],answer:1,reveal:'The messages pull you into other people\u2019s plans. By the time you finish, the idea feels far away. Write first, while your head is still your own.'},{kind:'idea',text:'For Thoreau, morning is not a time on the clock. It is the fresh state before you pick up yesterday\u2019s worries. Use it for your own work first.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'First light, an empty room, one chair waiting. Nothing has started yet. That fresh moment is what Thoreau meant by morning.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A CLEAN PAGE',text:'Morning is like a clean page in a new notebook. The first thing you put on it matters. Put someone else\u2019s list there first, and it is not clean any more.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Not about getting up early',text:'Thoreau loved early mornings. But he said morning is not really about the clock. You can feel fresh at four in the afternoon. What ends the morning is when you start to answer other people.'},{kind:'try',text:'Tomorrow, do one thing that is yours before you look at any message. Draw, read or plan. Then check your phone.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Jo wakes at 11 on Sunday. She reads a book she loves, then checks her messages. Is that Thoreau\u2019s morning?',options:['Yes, she did her own thing first','No, because 11 is too late for morning','No, she should check messages first'],answer:0,why:'Morning is a state, not an hour. She kept it by doing her own thing before anyone else\u2019s.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'FORTY CLEAR MINUTES',text:'You have forty minutes before anyone needs you. You open your messages, just to check. Half an hour later the block is technically still there. It is worth much less.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Morning is not an hour but a condition: not yet having resumed yesterday. Re-entering the world\u2019s business ends it, so do your own thinking before anything connects you to other people\u2019s agendas.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where the forty minutes went',text:'Checking messages imports yesterday wholesale. After that the time is still there, but you are responding rather than thinking. The block was not lost to interruption. It was spent in the first ninety seconds.'},{kind:'steps',title:'A cheap ordering rule',steps:['Decide tonight what tomorrow\u2019s first block is for.','Do that before opening any message or feed.','Only then connect to other people\u2019s agendas.'],note:'Nothing here depends on the clock. It depends on the order.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A bare room at first light, one chair facing the window, nobody in it yet. Morning, in Thoreau\u2019s sense, is the room before anything comes in.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'This is advice to get up earlier.',reality:'Thoreau loved rising early, but the clock is not his point. He says few people are awake enough for real thinking, and the clock does not decide who. A late start, kept unresumed, is still morning.'},{kind:'try',text:'Protect the first block of tomorrow by doing your own work before opening any message.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Who is keeping Thoreau\u2019s morning?',options:['A night-shift nurse who writes for an hour before checking her phone','An early riser who clears the inbox at six, then starts work','A student who reads the news at dawn to feel informed','A manager who answers all messages first, to clear the mind'],answer:0,why:'The hour does not matter. What matters is doing your own work before taking on anyone else\u2019s.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'A REDEFINITION',word:'Morning, in Thoreau\u2019s sense',meaning:'Not an hour but a condition: being awake and not yet having resumed yesterday. For him it is morning whenever there is a dawn in you, whatever the clock says.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Morning is the state of not having resumed yesterday, and it ends when you re-enter the world\u2019s business. Whatever needs you unresumed should come first.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'HIS CLAIM',text:'In the chapter on where he lived and what he lived for, Thoreau says millions are awake enough for physical labour, but only a tiny fraction are awake enough for real intellectual effort. Being awake, for him, is the scarce thing, and the clock does not hand it out.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW MORNING ENDS',nodes:['Awake, unresumed','Open messages','Yesterday imported wholesale','Responding, not thinking'],note:'The block of time survives this. The state does not.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'An empty chair, a bare room, first light along the floor. Nothing of the day\u2019s business has come in, and that is what makes it morning.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why ordering beats willpower',text:'The rule asks nothing heroic. It only fixes the order: whatever needs you unresumed goes before whatever connects you to other people\u2019s agendas. Most people break it from habit, not weakness, because checking feels like a harmless glance.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Many people do not control their first hour. Parents, carers and shift workers often start already resumed, and calling that a failure is unfair. The picture is also romantic: some thinking is better after contact, sparked by a conversation or a problem someone else set. The rule is a default for those with the choice.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'When did you last think something through before anyone else\u2019s needs reached you? What came first that day?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A writer starts at 2 p.m. after a slow lunch, phone off until four. By Thoreau\u2019s account, is she working in morning?',options:['Yes, if nothing of the day\u2019s business has reached her yet','No, because morning ends at noon whatever her state','No, because a rest after lunch spoils concentration','Only if she also got up early that day'],answer:0,why:'The condition, not the clock, defines morning. Unresumed at two is morning; resumed at six is not.'}]}},walden_9:{pictureCaption:'Most of the prints march in step. One line crosses them at its own stride, towards a drum the others never reached.',bridge:'Idea 7 was about views hidden in a room. This one is about reasons hidden in a person: what they are keeping pace with.',basics:{title:'Maybe they hear a different beat',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'LAST TO FINISH',prompt:'Your friend is always the last to finish the class reading. What is going on?',options:['She is slow','She is not trying','You can\u2019t tell from the outside'],answer:2,reveal:'Maybe she is slow. Maybe she stops to think about each page. Maybe she is tired from helping at home. From where you sit, all of these look the same.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When someone seems behind, they might be keeping time to a different beat. From the outside, the two look the same. So ask before you judge.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Most footprints walk in step. One line crosses them at its own pace. It heads for a drum the others did not hear.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where this comes from',text:'At the end of his book, Thoreau wrote that if someone does not keep pace with the others, maybe they hear a different drummer. Let them step to the music they hear.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'AT BREAK TIME',text:'Sam never joins the football at break. Some kids say he is no good at it. But Sam is learning chess, and he plays every break with a friend. He is not behind. He is busy with something else.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you think someone is slow or behind, ask them what they are working on. Then ask yourself the same thing.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your cousin takes ages to finish his model plane. What fits this idea?',options:['Find out what he is trying to get right','He is just slow at making models','He should work as fast as you do'],answer:0,why:'He might be adding detail you did not notice. You can only know by asking.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A YEAR BEHIND',prompt:'A colleague is a year behind their peers on promotion. What does that fact alone tell you?',options:['They are underperforming','They are pursuing something else','Not enough to say which'],answer:2,reveal:'The fact fits both. They may be struggling, or choosing depth over speed, or carrying obligations you cannot see. From outside, these look identical.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The different drummer passage is usually read as permission to be odd. On our reading, its sharper point is a measurement error: falling behind and keeping pace with something you cannot hear look the same from outside.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where the confidence comes from',text:'If the facts you can see fit both explanations equally, then any certainty about which is true did not come from the facts. It came from your assumptions: what the work is for, and which race you think everyone is in.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A beach of footprints in one rhythm, and one line crossing at a different stride. Seen from the main track, it looks like straying.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Falling behind',items:['Slower than peers','Same goal, less progress']},right:{title:'Another pace',items:['Slower than peers','Different goal, steady progress']},note:'The top line is all you can see. The second line is what you need to know.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before concluding someone is behind, ask what they are pacing against. Then ask what you are pacing against.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A friend has not settled down at forty, unlike your other friends. Which response fits the idea?',options:['Ask what they have been aiming at before drawing a conclusion','Conclude they are struggling and quietly offer advice','Praise them for being refreshingly different from everyone','Decide it is none of your business and think no more of it'],answer:0,why:'Both pity and praise assume you already know which drum they hear. The idea is to find out first.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE LAST CHAPTER',text:'In the conclusion of Walden, Thoreau suggests that a person who does not keep pace with companions may be hearing a different drummer, and should step to that music however measured or far away. It is usually read as a licence for eccentricity.'},{kind:'idea',text:'On our reading, the passage diagnoses a measurement problem. Falling behind and keeping another pace produce the same observable data, so confidence about which you are seeing comes from somewhere other than the data.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'The passage celebrates being different for its own sake.',reality:'Read closely, its claim is narrower. On our reading, it warns observers that they cannot tell from pace alone whether someone is failing at their race or running another one.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One line of prints crosses the common track at its own stride, towards a drum. From the track, nothing tells you the drum is there.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The error runs both ways',text:'The same mistake is available about yourself. You may judge yourself behind in a race you never chose, or excuse real slippage as a different drum. The honest question is the same in both directions: what am I keeping pace with, and is it what I meant to?'},{kind:'limits',text:'Sometimes the usual reading is right: the person is behind, and calling it a different drum is a kind flattery that withholds help. The idea suspends judgement until you ask. It does not forbid the conclusion, and it can be abused by anyone who would rather not be measured at all.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Behind and differently paced look the same from outside.','Certainty about which one comes from assumptions, not data.','Ask what sets the pace, for them and for you.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which is a misuse of the different drummer idea?',options:['Never checking on a struggling student, since they may hear another drum','Asking a slow colleague what they are aiming at before judging','Questioning whether your own career timetable is one you chose','Holding off a verdict until you know what someone is pursuing'],answer:0,why:'The idea says ask before concluding. Using it to avoid asking at all turns a caution into neglect.'}]}},walden_10:{pictureCaption:'Old boots laced, spade by the door. The new suit stays in the window. The work can start in the old ones.',bridge:'Idea 8 put your own work before other people\u2019s. This one catches work that only looks like your own: getting ready.',basics:{title:'Start in your old clothes',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE NEW HOBBY',text:'You decide to start running. First you need new trainers. Then a water bottle. Then a cool watch. Three weeks later you have all of it. You have not been for a run.'},{kind:'idea',text:'If a new plan needs new stuff before you can start, be careful. Try it first with what you already have.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What Thoreau said',text:'Thoreau said to watch out for any plan that needs new clothes. He meant: if it is real, you can start it as you are. The change has to be in you first. The stuff can come later.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'OLD BOOTS, NEW SUIT',caption:'Old boots, laced up. A spade by the door. The smart new suit stays in the shop window. The digging starts today, in what he has.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Getting ready',items:['Buying the kit','Picking a name','Planning the look']},right:{title:'Starting',items:['Going for a run','Writing the first page','Playing the first song']},note:'Getting ready feels like starting. It is not.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A NEW PENCIL CASE',text:'A new pencil case at the start of term feels great. But it does not do your homework. The work only starts when the pencil touches the page.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick something you keep meaning to start. Do a tiny bit of it this week, using only things you already have.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Ana wants to start making a comic. Which step passes Thoreau\u2019s test?',options:['Draw one page today with a pencil and paper','Buy a set of special pens before she begins','Wait until she gets a drawing tablet'],answer:0,why:'She can start now with what she has. If she still loves it, the pens can come later.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THREE WEEKS IN',prompt:'You have been about to start a side project for three weeks. So far you have a domain name and two fonts. Have you started?',options:['Yes, the groundwork is done','No, none of that is the project'],answer:1,reveal:'Each purchase was a real action and felt like progress. None of them was the thing. Preparation is the most comfortable form of avoidance.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Beware of any enterprise that needs new clothes rather than a new wearer of clothes. If you can try it in your old ones, do: the costume may be most of what is on offer.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'NOTHING BOUGHT',caption:'The new suit waits in the window, untouched. The old boots are being laced, the spade is by the door, and the real work is about to begin.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the test works',text:'Buying the equipment, the wardrobe, the software: each is a real action, and each gives the feeling of having started. None of them is the work. A purchase is also the one step that never risks finding out you are bad at the thing.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW STARTING GETS REPLACED',nodes:['Decide to start','Buy what it seems to need','Feel that you have begun','Never do the first real piece'],note:'Each step is reasonable. Together they replace the thing they were for.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The order matters',text:'Thoreau follows up by asking how new clothes could fit if there is no new person to wear them. The change happens in the person first. Then the outward things follow, or turn out not to have been needed.'},{kind:'try',text:'Take the thing you have been about to start. Do the smallest real version of it this week without buying anything.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You want to start a podcast. Which first step passes the old-clothes test?',options:['Record an episode on your phone and listen back','Buy a good microphone so the sound is right','Design the logo and choose the theme music','Spend a week comparing hosting platforms'],answer:0,why:'Only recording an episode is the thing itself. The others may be needed later, but each can stand in for starting.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'This is a passage about clothes and thrift.',reality:'It is a test for any new undertaking. In the Economy chapter Thoreau warns against enterprises that require new clothes rather than a new wearer, and asks what the costume is doing.'},{kind:'idea',text:'If a venture needs you to look different before it can begin, the looking may be most of what is on offer. The change must happen in the person first.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The sharp line',text:'His follow-on question is the real argument: if there is no new person, how can the new clothes be made to fit? Order matters. Outward things follow an inward change, or turn out not to have been needed. Bought first, they stand in for it.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'THE ORDER, DRAWN',caption:'The start comes before the costume: old boots laced, spade ready. The new suit stays in the window, and may turn out never to have been needed.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE NOVEL',text:'Someone means to write a novel. They buy writing software, a desk and a course, and set up a website in their name. Each purchase is defensible. Months pass. The test asks one thing: could they have written a chapter in a notebook on the first day?'},{kind:'scale',low:'Can start today with what you own',high:'Cannot begin until something is bought',note:'The further right a venture sits, the more carefully you should ask whether the buying is the venture.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Some enterprises do need equipment before anything real can happen: a pilot needs an aircraft, a lab needs instruments. And for some people new kit is an honest signal of commitment. The test is a question about avoidance, not a ban on preparation. Applied rigidly, it becomes a refusal to invest in things worth doing well.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What have you bought for a project you have not yet begun? Could you have begun without it?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY THE TEST',prompt:'Which case would Thoreau\u2019s test most clearly flag?',options:['A would-be painter who has bought every brush but painted nothing','A surgeon who needs instruments before operating','A runner who has run for a year, then buys better shoes','A cook who borrows a pan to try a recipe tonight'],answer:0,why:'The surgeon needs the kit and the runner began first. The painter\u2019s shopping has taken the place of painting.'}]}},walden_11:{pictureCaption:'Each tool on the back was a sensible choice. Strapped on together, they bend the carrier double and do the steering.',bridge:'Idea 2 showed comforts getting in the way. This one shows how it happens without anyone choosing it, one sensible step at a time.',basics:{title:'When your stuff starts to boss you',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE TOMATO PLANTS',text:'You plant a few tomatoes to have a snack in summer. They grow well, so you plant more. Now you water them every evening and pull weeds at the weekend. You can\u2019t go away. The plants decide your summer.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Thoreau said people become the tools of their tools. You get a thing to help you. Then it needs looking after, and it starts to run your time.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'WHO CARRIES WHOM?',caption:'He got each of these tools to help him. Now look who is carrying whom. The load is so big it is steering him.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THOREAU\u2019S OWN EXAMPLE',nodes:['Pick fruit when you are hungry','Plant a field of fruit','Work the field every day','The field sets your day'],note:'Each step was better than the last. At the end, the farm was the boss.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Nobody chose it',text:'Each step was a good idea. More tomatoes is nice. But every tool comes with jobs that keep going. You picked the tool. The jobs came with it.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one thing you look after, like a game, a club or a collection. Ask: if it vanished, would I want it back, or would I feel free?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Tom gets a phone to keep in touch with friends. Now he checks it every few minutes, even at dinner. What is happening?',options:['The tool has started to run his time','He just likes his friends a lot','Phones are bad for all of us'],answer:0,why:'He got the phone to help him. Now it decides what he does. That is being the tool of your tool.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'You would notice if you started serving your own tools.',reality:'You would not, because no single step looks like service. Each new tool is a sensible improvement. The obligations arrive bundled, and they outlive the moment that justified them.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Each tool arrives with obligations that outlive the need that justified it. Repeat that sensibly enough times and the tool sets your schedule. Thoreau\u2019s phrase is that men become the tools of their tools.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Thoreau\u2019s chain',text:'The man who picked fruit when he was hungry becomes a farmer. The man who sheltered under a tree ends up keeping a house. Nothing was stolen, and each step was better. At the end, the farm must be worked whether or not anyone is hungry today.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'THE LOAD',caption:'Spades, saws, a ladder, a wheel: each one a sensible buy. Strapped on together, they bend the carrier double and set the direction.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE FRIDAY PROBLEM',text:'A team adopts a reporting system to save time. It works. Then it needs someone to keep the data clean, run the reports and train new staff. Now most of a Friday, every Friday, goes to the tool that was meant to free time.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'The choice',items:['Made once','Made for a good reason','Easy to see']},right:{title:'The service',items:['Paid every week','Continues after the reason fades','Never actually chosen']}},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one tool, system or commitment you maintain. Ask whether you would choose it again today, knowing its upkeep.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Which question best applies Thoreau\u2019s test to a tool you rely on?',options:['If it vanished tonight, would I rebuild it or feel relieved?','Is it the best tool of its kind on the market?','How much did it cost when I first bought it?','Do other people in my position use one too?'],answer:0,why:'The test weighs the tool\u2019s value now against its ongoing upkeep, not against its price or its popularity.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE INVERSION',nodes:['Hungry: pick the fruit','Plant a field','Work it daily','Work it, hungry or not'],note:'Thoreau\u2019s chain, from the Economy chapter. Each link is an improvement. The last has reversed who serves whom.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Men have become the tools of their tools. Each sensible acquisition brings obligations that outlive the moment that justified it, until the tool sets the schedule.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it is invisible from inside',text:'At no point does anyone choose to serve the tool. They choose the tool, repeatedly and sensibly, and the service comes bundled. Because every step is justified, there is no single step at which to object.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'WHO SERVES WHOM',caption:'No single tool on that back was a mistake. Together they have reversed the arrangement: the load steers, and the carrier serves it.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'EXACT, NOT ANALOGOUS',text:'The modern cases fit precisely. A system adopted to save time now needs an administrator. An audience built to support the work now dictates what the work can be. Neither was a mistake when it was chosen.'},{kind:'keyline',text:'Nothing was stolen. Every step was an improvement. And at the end, the tool sets the schedule.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Some service to tools is the price of things worth having: farming fed people that gathering could not. Thoreau wrote as someone free to own little; most people depend on shared tools they cannot abandon alone. The test can tell you a tool has inverted. It cannot always free you from it.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which tool in your life would you quietly be relieved to lose? What keeps it there?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HONEST OBJECTION',prompt:'Which objection does the idea have to concede?',options:['Some tools are worth serving, for what their upkeep buys','Tools never really shape behaviour; people simply choose badly','Thoreau himself used no tools, so the claim is hypocritical','Modern tools are too unlike farms for the idea to apply'],answer:0,why:'Service is not always a loss. The question is whether you would still choose the bargain, knowing its upkeep.'}]}},walden_12:{pictureCaption:'A farm behind every gate, and he turns in at none of them. He can walk past, hands in pockets, and lose nothing.',bridge:'Ideas 2 and 11 counted what things cost to keep. This one turns that count into a definition of wealth.',basics:{title:'Rich in what you can walk past',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TWO FRIENDS',prompt:'Ali has lots of games, but feels he must play every new one or miss out. Bea has a few, and happily skips new ones. Who is richer?',options:['Ali, because he has more','Bea, because she can let things go'],answer:1,reveal:'Thoreau would pick Bea. Every game Ali feels he must keep up with takes his time and worry. Bea\u2019s time is still her own.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Thoreau said you are rich in the number of things you can leave alone. The fewer things you feel you must have, the freer you are.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why wanting costs',text:'Each thing you have to keep up with pulls at you. A game, a club, a fight with a friend you won\u2019t drop. Each one takes a bit of your time and thought, every day.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE CARRYING BAGS',text:'Picture walking home with a bag in each hand and three more on your back. Now picture walking home with none. Things you feel you must keep are like bags. Things you can leave weigh nothing.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'THE FARMS HE DID NOT BUY',picture:!0,text:'Thoreau spent a long time dreaming about buying farms near his town. In the end he bought none of them. He could enjoy the fields just by walking past them. Not owning them left him free.'},{kind:'try',text:'Make a list of things you keep up with. Mark one you could let go of and not miss. Try leaving it alone for a week.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'By Thoreau\u2019s idea, who is richest?',options:['Kai, who can skip the new trainers and not mind','Lin, who owns the most trainers in the class','Max, who saves up for every new pair'],answer:0,why:'For Thoreau, being rich is how much you can leave alone. Kai can walk past without it costing him a thing.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'SAME SAVINGS',prompt:'Two people have identical savings. One runs four side projects, two subscriptions and a long feud. The other runs none of these. Are they equally rich?',options:['Yes, the money is the same','No, one is carrying far more claims'],answer:1,reveal:'By Thoreau\u2019s measure, wealth is the set of things you can afford to let alone. The money is the same. The freedom is not.'},{kind:'idea',text:'You are rich in proportion to the number of things you can afford to let alone. Richness is not what you hold but what you can walk past without it costing you.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'WALKING PAST',caption:'A fine farm behind every gate, and he walks past them all, hands in pockets. Not one of them has a claim on him.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it can be measured',text:'Every held thing is a standing claim on attention: a subscription, a property, a side project, an argument you have not dropped. The claim is paid continuously, whether or not the thing is giving you anything. Count the claims and you have a figure net worth never shows.'},{kind:'scale',low:'Many open claims: everything must be kept up',high:'Few claims: most things can be walked past',note:'Two people with the same assets can sit at opposite ends of this line.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where the claim begins',text:'Thoreau adds a quieter point. The farms he could not afford to let alone were the ones he had already started imagining owning. Wanting is where the claim begins, before anything is bought.'},{kind:'try',text:'List everything you currently maintain. Mark what you could drop tomorrow without loss, then drop one of them.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You are offered an interesting, well-paid extra project. What does Thoreau\u2019s measure ask?',options:['Whether you can afford to let it alone, and what it would claim','Whether the fee is high enough to justify the hours','Whether turning it down would look bad to others','Whether it is more interesting than what you do now'],answer:0,why:'The question is not only the pay. It is how much of your attention it would hold, and whether you are free to decline it.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'THE FARMS HE DID NOT BUY',picture:!0,text:'In the chapter on where he lived, Thoreau describes imagining buying farms all around Concord: walking them, pricing them in his head. He came close to buying one before the deal fell through. Then he declines them all, and states the principle under the daydreams.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A person is rich in proportion to the number of things they can afford to let alone. Wealth is the size of the set you can walk past without cost.'},{kind:'explain',title:'A measurable definition',text:'Net worth counts what you hold. Thoreau counts what holds you. Every possession, project, rivalry or unresolved argument is a standing claim, paid in attention whether or not it gives anything back. Two people with identical assets can differ widely in this kind of wealth.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Net worth',items:['What you own','Rises with each acquisition','Visible on a statement']},right:{title:'Thoreau\u2019s wealth',items:['What you can let alone','Falls with each new claim','Visible only in your attention']}},{kind:'explain',title:'Desire comes first',text:'The farms he could not let alone were those he had already begun owning in imagination. The claim starts with the wanting, not the buying. That is why letting alone is a skill and not a budget line: it works on desire before any purchase.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The definition flatters those who already have enough. For someone poor, being unable to let things alone is not a failure of desire: rent and food cannot be walked past. Thoreau\u2019s freedom rested on having few dependants. And some claims, like children or friends, are what a life is for, not a deduction from it.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What could you drop tomorrow without flinching? What could you not, and why?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISREADING',prompt:'Which reading misuses Thoreau\u2019s definition?',options:['Calling someone in poverty rich because they own little','Turning down a lucrative role to keep attention free','Dropping a side project that had become a standing claim','Noticing that a daydream about a house has begun to claim you'],answer:0,why:'The measure is what you can afford to let alone. Lacking things is not the same as being free of their claims.'}]}}}},3030,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'walking-as-thinking_1':{pictureCaption:'Loose parts that could fit together many ways, beside one lock and its one key. Walking helps most with the loose parts.',basics:{title:'Walks help with ideas, not every problem',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TWO KINDS OF HOMEWORK',prompt:'Would a walk help more with thinking up ideas for a story, or with a hard sum that has one right answer?',options:['Ideas for a story','The hard sum','Both the same'],answer:0,reveal:'The story ideas. Walking helps most when there are lots of possible answers. It helps much less when there is just one right answer to find.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Walking helps you think up lots of ideas. It does much less for problems with one right answer. It does not make you smarter at everything.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'On the left, lots of parts that could fit together in many ways. On the right, one lock and one key. Walking helps most with the left side.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHEN TO WALK',left:{title:'A walk helps',items:['Ideas for a story','Names for a new pet','Ways to build a den']},right:{title:'A walk helps less',items:['A long sum','A spelling test','A times-tables quiz']}},{kind:'explain',title:'Not about the view',text:'You might think walks work because of trees and sky. But tests found the boost even on a treadmill, a walking machine, facing a blank wall. So it seems to be the walking, not the view.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you need ideas, go for a short walk first. Save the sums for when you sit back down.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Priya must plan a fun stall for the school fair, then add up the prices. When should she take a walk?',options:['While she thinks up ideas for the stall','While she adds up all the prices','Only once the fair is over'],answer:0,why:'Stall ideas have lots of possible answers. That is where walking helps most. The adding has one right answer.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE POPULAR VERSION',belief:'Walking makes you smarter, so any hard thinking goes better on foot.',reality:'The finding is narrower and more useful: walking reliably helps with tasks that involve generating many possible answers, and does much less for tasks with one correct answer.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Walking improves open-ended, idea-generating thinking. It does much less for problems with a single right answer. Use it for the first kind.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'ONE PROJECT, TWO JOBS',text:'You are planning a product launch. Brainstorming angles for the campaign is open-ended: dozens of answers could work. Checking that the budget adds up is closed: one answer is right. The walk belongs to the first job.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why not the scenery?',text:'Studies have found the improvement holds outdoors and also on a treadmill facing a blank wall. That argues against the effect being about fresh air or a nice view. Something about the walking itself seems to matter.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Loose parts that could combine a hundred ways, beside one lock and its one key. A walk helps with the loose parts.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'WHERE A WALK PAYS',low:'One right answer: a sum, a date, a password',high:'Many possible answers: names, angles, plans',note:'The further right a task sits, the more a walk is likely to help.'},{kind:'try',text:'Split your next stuck problem into its open part and its closed part. Take the open part for a walk. Do the closed part at the desk.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Which task is the best candidate for a thinking walk?',options:['Coming up with possible names for a new service','Checking a contract for the correct payment date','Working out the exact total of this month\u2019s expenses','Finding which of two spreadsheet formulas is wrong'],answer:0,why:'Naming has many acceptable answers, which is where walking reliably helps. The others each have one correct answer.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE ESSAY\u2019S STANCE',text:'The essay takes a careful line from the start. Solving a problem while walking is a near-universal experience and more than folklore. But the explanations usually offered are more confident than the evidence supports. This first idea sets out what the evidence does support.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Walking reliably improves performance on tasks that involve generating many possible answers, and does much less for tasks with one correct answer. The popular claim that walking makes you smarter overstates a specific, useful finding.'},{kind:'term',word:'Divergent thinking',meaning:'The usual name for producing many possible answers to an open question. Its counterpart, convergent thinking, narrows to a single correct answer. Walking helps the first far more than the second.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the blank wall shows',text:'Studies have found the improvement holds both outdoors and on a treadmill facing a blank wall. If the effect came from scenery, light or nature, the blank wall should have removed it. It did not, which points to something about walking itself, though not to what that something is.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'CLAIM AND FINDING',left:{title:'The popular claim',items:['Walking makes you smarter','Any thinking improves','It is the fresh air']},right:{title:'The finding',items:['Walking helps idea generation','Single-answer tasks gain much less','It holds facing a blank wall']},note:'The finding is smaller than the claim, and of more use, because it tells you when to walk.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Scattered parts on one side, a lock and its single key on the other. The finding is about the scattered side.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The finding says walking helps you generate options, not that the options are good. Judging between them is a closed task, and may go better at the desk. And the essay itself warns that accounts of why walking works are usually more confident than the evidence. Knowing that it helps is not knowing how.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE OVERREACH',prompt:'Which conclusion goes beyond what the finding supports?',options:['A walk before an exam of single-answer questions should raise the score','A walk is worth trying when brainstorming options for a decision','A treadmill walk may help idea generation as well as an outdoor one','Walking may do little for checking which of two answers is correct'],answer:0,why:'Single-answer tasks are exactly where walking does much less. The other three stay inside the finding.'}]}},'walking-as-thinking_2':{pictureCaption:'The mug stays on the ledge. The problem goes with him: head down, still turning it over.',bridge:'Idea 1 said when a walk helps. This one gives part of the why: it takes you away from the screen without taking you away from the problem.',basics:{title:'Stop staring, keep thinking',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'STUCK ON A PUZZLE',text:'You have stared at the same tricky puzzle game on a tablet for ages. You keep trying the same few moves. Then you take the dog out. Halfway round the block, you see the answer.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When you stare at a problem, you keep trying it the same way. A walk breaks that loop, but the problem stays in your head. That is often when the answer pops up.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A RUT IN THE MUD',text:'Push a bike through mud on the same line again and again, and you make a deep rut. The wheel keeps sliding back into it. Lift the bike out, and you can try a new line.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The mug stays behind on the ledge. The problem does not. Look at his head: down, still thinking it over.'},{kind:'explain',title:'One idea about why',text:'Looking at a bright screen up close is hard work for your eyes. On a walk, your eyes look far off, at things that move. Your mind loosens up, and new links can show up.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Any walk works, even one spent looking at your phone.',reality:'No. If you look at a phone the whole way, the screen is still in front of you. You get the walk, but not the help.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Jake is stuck on a drawing he is doing on a computer. Which walk is most likely to help?',options:['Round the park, still thinking about it','Round the park, playing a phone game','No walk. He forgets it and watches TV'],answer:0,why:'He keeps the problem but loses the screen. The game brings a screen back. TV drops the problem.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'MINUTE FORTY',text:'You have spent forty minutes on a paragraph that will not come right. Each attempt is a small variation on the last. You get up for a walk, mostly out of frustration, and ten minutes in the right structure arrives.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A problem you look at directly is one you keep approaching the same way. Movement interrupts that loop without making you drop the problem, which is why the answer arrives during the walk rather than after it.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHAT THE WALK CHANGES',nodes:['The screen leaves your view for a while','Distance, movement, changing edges','Attention shifts from directed to diffuse','New connections surface'],note:'This is the essay\u2019s account of the mechanism: an explanation, not a proof.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the screen, specifically',text:'Focused attention on a bright rectangle at a fixed distance is a narrow, demanding way of looking. A walk supplies the opposite: things far away, movement, change at the edge of vision. The essay argues that this is part of what a walk gives you.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The mug stays on the ledge. The problem leaves with him; his head is down, still turning it over.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO WALKS',left:{title:'Eyes on a phone',items:['Movement: yes','Screen: still there','Benefit: mostly lost']},right:{title:'Eyes up',items:['Movement: yes','Screen: gone','Problem: still carried']},note:'That is why the instruction has to mention the screen, not just the walking.'},{kind:'try',text:'When you have been stuck for forty minutes, get up and move, without putting the problem down and without picking up your phone.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You are stuck on a design problem. Which break best fits the idea?',options:['A walk round the block, the problem in mind, phone in pocket','A walk round the block, answering emails on your phone','Staying at the desk and trying ten more variations','Switching to a different task until tomorrow morning'],answer:0,why:'It removes the screen and keeps the problem. The phone brings the screen back; switching tasks drops the problem.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'TWO MODES',word:'Directed and diffuse attention',meaning:'Directed attention is held on purpose on one thing. Diffuse attention is looser and wider. The essay\u2019s claim is that associative connections, unexpected links between ideas, surface in the second.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Staring keeps you approaching a problem the same way. A walk removes the screen for a sustained period and shifts attention from directed to diffuse while the problem stays loaded, so answers tend to arrive during the walk.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The essay\u2019s account',text:'A bright rectangle at a fixed distance is a narrow and demanding visual mode. Walking supplies distance, movement and change at the edge of vision, which the essay calls the condition the visual system spent most of its evolutionary history in. The result is a shift in attention, and loosened attention is where associations surface.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why during, not after',text:'The walk does not ask you to abandon the problem. That is the difference from a complete break. The problem stays loaded while the approach loosens, so the answer has both something to attach to and room to arrive.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'He has left the ledge and the mug behind, but not the problem. Head down, he is still working; only the way of looking has changed.'},{kind:'limits',text:'This idea is the most exposed to the essay\u2019s own warning that explanations of the walking effect outrun the evidence. Idea 1\u2019s blank-wall finding sits awkwardly with an account built on distance and changing views, so the screen can be at most part of it. Treat the mechanism as a working story. The practical point survives without every detail: a walk spent on a phone keeps the screen the walk was meant to remove.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Where were you the last time a stuck problem came loose? Was a screen in front of you?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TEST THE ACCOUNT',prompt:'If the essay\u2019s account is right, which walk should help least with a stuck problem?',options:['A walk spent reading messages on a phone','A walk through a quiet, unfamiliar town','A slow walk around a car park at night','A walk with the problem held loosely in mind'],answer:0,why:'In the account, a walk helps by taking the screen away. A phone keeps the narrow visual mode in place, so the walk supplies movement without the shift.'}]}},'walking-as-thinking_3':{pictureCaption:'Nothing in his ears, nothing in his hands, a quiet path ahead. The only thing he has brought is a question.',bridge:'Idea 2 said a walk works by loosening your attention. This one says what not to fill that space with.',basics:{title:'Take a question, not your headphones',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'HEADPHONES IN?',belief:'A walk with music or a story in your ears is just as good for thinking.',reality:'It is still a nice walk. But your head is full of the music. There is little room left for your own ideas.'},{kind:'idea',text:'To get ideas on a walk, take one question with you and nothing else. No music, no videos. Just you and the question.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Nothing in his ears. Nothing in his hands. Just a quiet path and one question he is carrying.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE AN EMPTY BASKET',text:'Go out with an empty basket and you can pick up what you find. Go out with a full one, and there is nowhere to put anything new.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Carry it, don\u2019t push it',text:'You don\u2019t have to work hard on the question as you walk. Just keep it in mind, loosely. Ideas have something to stick to, but you are not forcing them.'},{kind:'explain',title:'So is music on a walk bad?',text:'No. A walk just to rest, with your music on, is fine. It is just a different kind of walk. What you bring decides which kind it is.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Ava wants ideas for her art project. Which walk should she take?',options:['A quiet walk, thinking about her project now and then','A walk with her favourite songs on the whole way','A walk while she chats to a friend on the phone'],answer:0,why:'The quiet walk keeps one question in mind and nothing else in the way. Songs and calls fill the space.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'SAME ROUTE, TWO WALKS',left:{title:'With a podcast',items:['Pleasant, and a good walk','The channel is full','Time becomes consumption']},right:{title:'With one question',items:['Nothing competing for space','The question is loaded','Time becomes thinking']},note:'Both are walks. Only one is the kind that tends to generate answers.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The condition that seems to matter is having the problem loaded and nothing else competing. Bring a single question, and leave the input at home.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the podcast does',text:'It fills the channel the walk was supposed to free. It is enjoyable, and it turns the time into consumption, which is the mode you were already in at the desk. You come back better informed, not unstuck.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'HOLDING IT LOOSELY',text:'Before leaving, you frame one question: *What is the real reason customers cancel?* You do not grind at it. You just carry it. Twenty minutes in, a remark from a support call last month surfaces and connects.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'No headphones, empty hands, a quiet path. The only thing this walker has brought is the question.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Not every walk',text:'This is not always what you need. A walk purely for rest is legitimate, and turning every walk into a thinking session is its own kind of optimisation. The two are different activities, and what you bring decides which one you are on.'},{kind:'try',text:'Once a week, walk with the question and without headphones. Fifteen minutes is enough.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You face a hard decision about a job offer. Which walk best fits the idea?',options:['Twenty minutes, no headphones, the decision held loosely in mind','Twenty minutes with a podcast about making career decisions','Twenty minutes on the phone, talking it through with a friend','Twenty minutes making a list of pros and cons on your phone'],answer:0,why:'Only the silent walk leaves the channel free with one question loaded. Even a relevant podcast fills the space the walk was meant to clear.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE EFFICIENT WALK',belief:'A walk with a podcast doubles its value: exercise and input at once.',reality:'The essay argues the input cancels the part of the walk that generates answers. It fills the channel the walk was meant to free, and turns the time back into the consumption you were doing at the desk.'},{kind:'idea',text:'What makes a walk generative is the problem loaded and nothing else competing. One question, held loosely, gives diffuse attention something to attach to without directing it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Held loosely, not worked',text:'This follows from idea 2. If answers surface in diffuse attention, then working hard at the question on the walk brings the directed mode back. Carrying it without working on it keeps attention loose but gives it a target. Too little and nothing attaches; too much and you are at the desk again, outdoors.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'HOW TIGHTLY TO HOLD IT',low:'Nothing loaded: a pleasant walk',high:'Grinding at it: the desk, outdoors',note:'The essay places the productive walk in between: one question, carried but not worked.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Empty ears, empty hands, a quiet path ahead. One thing has been brought, and it cannot be drawn: the question.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The essay supplies its own caveat. A walk purely for rest is legitimate, and turning every walk into a thinking session is its own kind of optimisation. Note the hedge in the claim as well: the condition *seems* to matter. This is a practical rule drawn from an account of attention, not a measured verdict on podcasts.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'Someone says they listen to podcasts about their own field on walks, so they are still thinking about work. What is the essay\u2019s best reply?',options:['That is input, not thinking; it fills the space your own question needed','That works, as long as the podcast is about the problem itself','Any walk counts, since the movement is what matters most','Podcasts are fine if you take notes on your phone as you go'],answer:0,why:'Relevant input is still input. The condition the essay describes is one question loaded and nothing else competing.'}]}},'walking-as-thinking_4':{pictureCaption:'A card and a pencil, already in the pocket. Close enough to write one line without stopping.',bridge:'Idea 3 told you what to leave at home. This is the one thing to take.',basics:{title:'Catch the idea before it goes',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE PERFECT IDEA',text:'On the walk home, you think of the perfect idea for your story. It is so clear. You will never forget it. By the time your shoes are off, it is gone.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Ideas on a walk come fast and go fast. Carry something to catch them with, and use it straight away. Don\u2019t wait.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why clear ideas still vanish',text:'An idea can feel very sharp when it comes. That does not mean it will stay. Two more thoughts come along and push it out.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A card and a pencil, already in the pocket. Close enough to write one line without even stopping.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Catch it',steps:['Carry a card and pencil, or use a voice note on a phone.','When an idea comes, write or say one line.','Do it now, not at the next corner.','Decide if it is any good later, at home.']},{kind:'try',text:'Put a small card and a pencil in your coat pocket today. Leave them there for next time.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'On the way to school you think of a great name for your band. What should you do?',options:['Write it down or say it into a phone now','Keep it in your head until you get to school','Wait and see if it still seems good later'],answer:0,why:'Ideas leave as fast as they come. Catch it now. You can decide if it is good later.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE LINE ON THE BRIDGE',text:'Crossing a bridge, you find the exact opening line for a talk you have been dreading. It is so obviously right that writing it down feels unnecessary. By the time you are home, you have the feeling of having had it, and not the line.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Ideas on a walk arrive without warning and leave the same way. Anything that captures a sentence in five seconds will do, as long as capturing is faster than deciding whether it is worth capturing.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Clear is not the same as lasting',text:'The idea feels too clear to forget. But how clear it feels when it arrives does not predict whether it will last. The next convenient moment to write it down is usually after two more thoughts have overwritten it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Card and pencil already in the pocket, within reach without stopping. The system has to be faster than your hesitation.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'CHOOSING A TOOL',left:{title:'Works',items:['A voice memo','A card and a pencil','Anything that takes five seconds']},right:{title:'Fails',items:['Stopping to type in an app','Planning to remember','Waiting until you sit down']},note:'A voice memo has an extra merit: no screen to look at, which would end the state that produced the thought.'},{kind:'try',text:'Carry something that captures a sentence in five seconds, and use it before deciding whether the thought is worth it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Mid-walk, a half-formed idea arrives. You are not sure it is any good. What does the essay advise?',options:['Record it now and judge it later','Judge it first, then record it if it passes','Keep walking and see if it is still there at home','Stop, open a notes app and write it up properly'],answer:0,why:'Capturing must be faster than deciding. Judging first spends the seconds in which it disappears; a notes app brings back the screen.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A PREDICTION',prompt:'An idea arrives on a walk and feels vivid and certain. What does that vividness predict about whether you will still have it in an hour?',options:['That you will keep it','Very little'],answer:1,reveal:'This is the essay\u2019s point: clarity at the moment of arrival is not a predictor of durability. The feeling of certainty is a feeling about now.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Useful thoughts arrive unbidden and do not survive the rest of the walk, so capture has to be arranged beforehand, and it must be faster than the judgement about whether the thought is worth keeping.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The specific failure',text:'The idea feels too clear to forget and is gone by the time you are home. The essay\u2019s explanation is competition: the walk keeps producing thoughts, and the next convenient point to record one is usually after two more have overwritten it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why speed beats judgement',text:'Deciding whether a thought is worth recording takes longer than recording it, and the deciding is when it slips away. So the rule reverses the usual order: record first, judge at home. Many captured thoughts will be thrown out. That is the price of keeping the few.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Card and pencil already in the pocket. Capture was arranged before the walk, because there is no time to arrange it during.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHY THE SCREEN MATTERS AGAIN',nodes:['A thought arrives in diffuse attention','You stop and look at a screen','Directed attention returns','The state that produced it ends'],note:'A voice memo avoids the screen, which is its extra advantage in the essay.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The habit can tip into a walk spent waiting to capture, which is directed attention by another route. And recording everything moves the sorting to later, where it then has to happen; a pile of unheard voice memos is no better than a forgotten thought. Capture is the first step, not the result.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'The same logic applies wherever ideas arrive unbidden. Which habit follows from it?',options:['A notepad by the bed for ideas that come as you fall asleep','A rule to record only ideas that still seem good after a minute','A weekly session for writing up whichever ideas you remember','Keeping the phone in hand on walks so that nothing is missed'],answer:0,why:'It makes capture faster than judgement in another place where thoughts arrive unprompted. The others judge first, lean on memory, or bring the screen back.'}]}},'walking-as-thinking_5':{pictureCaption:'A pair of ordinary shoes by an open door at dawn. No kit, no plan. That is the whole cost of starting.',bridge:'Ideas 1 to 4 were about thinking. This one steps back: walking would be worth doing even if it did nothing for ideas.',basics:{title:'Ideas are the bonus',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A TOY IN THE BOX',text:'Think of a cereal box with a toy inside. You would buy the cereal anyway, because you like it. The toy is a bonus. Walking is like that.'},{kind:'idea',text:'There is strong evidence that walking often helps you feel better and sleep better. Good ideas on the way are a bonus on top.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Just a pair of ordinary shoes by the door. No kit, no plan. That is all walking needs.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why easy matters',text:'Lots of tricks for thinking better are hard work. People try them for a week, then stop. Walking is free and easy, and you can do it almost anywhere. So you keep doing it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Famous walkers',text:'Some famous people walked most days and said it helped. Charles Darwin, who worked out how living things change over time, walked a loop near his home. The writer Charles Dickens took very long walks. That alone proves little. But it is a good sign.'},{kind:'try',text:'Walk somewhere today that you would usually get a lift to. Notice how you feel afterwards.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Tom walks to school but says it is a waste, since he never gets any good ideas. What would you tell him?',options:['It still helps his mood and sleep, so keep going','He is right, so he should get a lift instead','He should walk faster so the ideas will come'],answer:0,why:'The mood and sleep help come anyway. Ideas were always the bonus.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'WHICH HABIT LASTS?',prompt:'Two habits promise better thinking. One needs an app, a timer and a quiet room. The other needs shoes. Which are you more likely to keep up?',options:['The app, timer and quiet room','The one that needs shoes'],answer:1,reveal:'Demanding techniques tend to be used briefly. Walking costs almost nothing to start or to keep going, which is a benefit in its own right.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Separately from any effect on thinking, the evidence that regular walking helps mood and sleep is strong, and it needs no equipment or plan. The thinking is a bonus on top.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The unglamorous benefit',text:'Walking is available, free, needs no equipment, and works in ordinary weather in an ordinary place. Most techniques for producing insight are demanding, and demanding techniques get dropped. Walking has almost no cost to adopt and very little that can go wrong.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Ordinary shoes by an open door at dawn. No kit bag, no plan. The whole cost of starting is in this picture.'},{kind:'equation',parts:['Better mood and sleep','Sometimes, an idea'],result:'A walk worth taking',note:'Count the first part as the reason. The second is the bonus.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The people who relied on it',text:'Many writers and thinkers relied on walking and said so: Nietzsche, Dickens, Wordsworth, Darwin with his daily circuits. The essay treats each as weak evidence on its own, and the collection as reasonably suggestive.'},{kind:'try',text:'Walk because of the mood and sleep evidence. Treat any thinking that comes with it as a bonus.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A friend gave up walking because two weeks of walks produced no breakthroughs. What is the best response?',options:['The walks were worth it for mood and sleep anyway','The walks failed, so a more intensive technique is needed','Two weeks is too short; ideas take a month to start','The walks should be longer, to force the ideas to come'],answer:0,why:'The main case for walking does not depend on ideas. They were always the bonus.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'HOW THE ESSAY ENDS',text:'The essay closes by stepping back from its own subject. Whatever walking does for thinking, it has a separate and stronger case: the evidence that regular walking helps mood and sleep is strong. The cognitive part is a bonus on top of something already worth doing.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Walking is a very cheap practice with a long record and a plausible account of why it helps thinking. That, with its separate evidence for mood and sleep, is a good enough basis for something that costs forty minutes.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why cheapness is an argument',text:'Most techniques for producing insight are demanding, and demanding techniques are used briefly. A practice with essentially no adoption cost can be kept up for years. A modest effect kept up for years can matter more than a strong one abandoned in a week.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'HOW STRONG IS EACH CLAIM?',left:{title:'Firmer',items:['Walking helps mood and sleep','It costs almost nothing','It helps idea generation']},right:{title:'Weaker',items:['Why it helps thinking','Famous walkers, taken one by one']},note:'The essay is careful to say which is which.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Ordinary shoes by an open door at dawn. The argument of this idea is that nothing else in the picture is needed.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Reading the anecdotes properly',text:'Nietzsche, Dickens, Darwin with his daily circuits, Wordsworth: practitioners who relied on walking and said so. The essay calls each weak evidence and the collection reasonably suggestive. That is the right weight. Famous walkers are remembered partly because they were famous, and walkers who achieved little left no record.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The honest framing is the essay\u2019s own: walking is not a productivity technique with a mechanism established in a lab. And the claim that it has almost no downside assumes a safe place to walk and a body that walks easily, which not everyone has. For them the principle, a cheap habit kept up, applies to whatever movement is available.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'WEIGH THE CASE',prompt:'Which statement matches how strongly the essay supports walking?',options:['A cheap habit, with good evidence for mood and sleep and a plausible thinking bonus','A lab-proven technique for raising both intelligence and productivity','A folk belief with no support beyond the anecdotes of famous people','A useful habit, but only for people whose work depends on new ideas'],answer:0,why:'The essay is confident about mood, sleep and cost, and careful about mechanism. It rejects both the lab-proven reading and the mere-folklore one.'}]}}}},3031,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'walking-is-the-underrated-one_1':{pictureCaption:'Every window here is full of kit for sale. The walk down the middle, out to the hills, costs nothing, and nobody advertises it.',basics:{title:'Why you hear so little about walking',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'ON TV AND ONLINE',prompt:'You see lots of ads for sports kit, sports drinks and fitness apps. You almost never see an ad for walking. Why?',options:['Walking does not do much for you','Nobody makes money when you walk','Walking is too dull to show'],answer:1,reveal:'Walking is free. There is nothing to buy, so nobody pays for an ad. That tells you about money. It does not tell you how good walking is.'},{kind:'idea',text:'You hear most about the things someone can sell you. How loud a tip is tells you who is paying for it. It tells you very little about whether the tip works.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Every window here is full of kit to buy. The path out to the hills is free. Nobody is paid to point you to it.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE THE SWEETS BY THE TILL',text:'In a shop, the sweets sit by the till, right by your hand. The apples sit at the back. Being near the till does not make sweets better for you. It just means someone wanted you to see them.'},{kind:'explain',title:'A question to ask',text:'When you hear a tip again and again, ask: who gets money if I believe this? If the answer is *nobody*, the tip got to you with no help. That is a small point in its favour. It is not proof.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time an ad tells you how to get fit, ask what it is selling. Then think of one free thing you could do.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend sees ads for a sports drink all the time. So she thinks it must help you play better. What does this idea say?',options:['Lots of ads show someone paid, not that it helps','It must help, or there would not be so many ads','Ads are always lies, so the drink must make you worse'],answer:0,why:'Lots of ads tell you about money. They tell you little about whether the drink helps. They do not prove it is bad, either.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'ONE WEEK OF YOUR FEED',text:'In one week your phone shows you a protein powder, a home rowing machine, a fitness app and a ring that scores your sleep. In the same week, nobody suggests a thirty-minute walk. Nobody had a reason to.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Advice reaches you in rough proportion to what it sells. How often you hear a recommendation says a lot about its commercial backing and very little about its evidence.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why walking goes missing',text:'Walking has no equipment, no subscription, no supplement and no content built around it. No company gains when you do it, so nobody pays to put it in front of you. The channels that carry health advice are mostly funded by things you can buy.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO DIFFERENT SIGNALS',left:{title:'How loud it is',items:['Tells you who is paying','Rises with the marketing budget','Easy to notice']},right:{title:'How well it works',items:['Tells you about results','Not set by any budget','Takes digging to find']},note:'The two can come apart entirely. A loud claim can be weak, and a quiet one strong.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Windows on both sides crammed with kit for sale. The plain route down the middle, out to the hills, costs nothing, so nobody points you to it.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'If nobody is selling it, it must be the best option.',reality:'Too strong. A claim that spreads with nobody promoting it has survived without help. The piece counts that as weak evidence in its favour: weak, but better than none. It still has to stand on its own record.'},{kind:'try',text:'Take the last health tip that reached you. Ask who benefits from your believing it. Then ask what free alternative you have never heard pushed.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A new supplement is in every advert you see and recommended by dozens of creators. What should its prominence tell you?',options:['That it has strong commercial backing, which says little about whether it works','That it probably works, since that many people would not recommend a dud','That it is probably useless, since heavy marketing usually means a weak product','That it is safe, since anything sold that widely must have been tested'],answer:0,why:'Prominence measures backing, not evidence. It does not show the product works, and it does not show it fails. For that you have to look elsewhere.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHERE THE PIECE STARTS',text:'The piece argues that walking is one of the most consistently supported things you can do for body and head, and one of the least promoted. Its first move is to explain that gap. Not by doubting walking, but by looking at how health advice travels.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The prominence of a recommendation is information about its commercial backing and very little about its evidence. Advice arrives in proportion to what it sells, so some of the best-supported options are among the least discussed.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW ADVICE TRAVELS',nodes:['A product to sell','Money for promotion','Channels carry the message','The advice feels everywhere'],note:'Walking stalls at the first step. There is nothing to sell, so nothing downstream moves.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Both sides of the street are stocked with things to buy. The route down the middle, out to the hills, has nothing in it to sell.'},{kind:'explain',title:'A filter worth applying generally',text:'The point is not about walking only. Most advice you meet has passed through channels funded by things you can buy. So what reaches you is a sample shaped by money, not by results. Treat how often you hear something as a fact about the sample, not about the claim.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The corrective',text:'Ask who benefits from your believing it. Where the answer is nobody, the claim has spread without promotional support. The piece treats that survival as weak evidence in its favour, and is careful to call it weak. Better than none is the whole of its strength.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The test is easy to overplay. Not being sold does not show that something works, and plenty of unpromoted ideas are simply wrong. Heavy marketing does not make a product bad, either. And walking is not wholly unsold: step counters and fitness watches are built around it. The filter tells you where to look, not what to conclude.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which health habit do you hear about most often? Who would lose money if you stopped believing in it?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these is a *misuse* of the idea?',options:['Rejecting a well-tested medicine because its maker advertises heavily','Discounting a gadget\u2019s buzz until you have seen what it actually does','Noticing that a free habit gets little coverage and checking its record','Asking who profits before weighing a tip you keep seeing'],answer:0,why:'Prominence says little about evidence in either direction. Heavy marketing does not show that something fails. It only means the volume cannot tell you.'}]}},'walking-is-the-underrated-one_2':{pictureCaption:'The number is painted on the display stand, not shown on the device. It is a sales label, not a measurement.',bridge:'Idea 1 was about what nobody sells. This one is about a number that was sold, and stuck.',basics:{title:'Where ten thousand steps came from',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'A NUMBER YOU KNOW',belief:'Doctors worked out that you need ten thousand steps a day.',reality:'No. The number began as the name of a step counter sold in Japan in the 1960s. It was picked to sell the gadget, not because of a test.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The name on the box',text:'In Japanese, the gadget\u2019s name meant *ten-thousand-step meter.* It is said that the written sign for ten thousand looks a bit like a person walking. So it made a good name. It was easy to remember.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Ten thousand steps was a name to sell a gadget. It is not a line you have to cross. Fewer steps still count.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A step counter in a shop window. The big number is on its stand, like a price tag. It is there to sell, not to measure.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What later tests found',text:'Years later, scientists checked. They found that walking helps well below ten thousand steps. Good gains start far lower. And past a point, more steps add less.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A RUMOUR AT SCHOOL',text:'A rumour goes round your school. Lots of people say it, so it starts to sound true. But nobody checked where it came from. Saying a thing a lot does not make it tested.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your gran walks about four thousand steps a day. She feels bad that it is not ten thousand. What could you tell her?',options:['Her steps still count, and ten thousand was a sales name','Her steps do not count until she gets to ten thousand','Steps do not matter at all, so she can stop walking'],answer:0,why:'Walking helps well below ten thousand steps. The number came from a gadget\u2019s name, not from a test.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'BEFORE YOU READ ON',prompt:'Where does the ten-thousand-steps target come from?',options:['A large health study','A government guideline','The name of a 1960s gadget'],answer:2,reveal:'A Japanese pedometer sold in the 1960s was branded *manpo-kei*, ten-thousand-step meter. The number is usually said to have been chosen because it was memorable and the written character looked like a walking figure.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The ten-thousand-step figure was marketing that later attracted evidence, in that order. It is not a threshold, and steps below it still count.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the evidence found afterwards',text:'Later research found benefits building up well below ten thousand, with substantial gains starting far lower. The point where extra steps stop cutting the risk of death by much differs between studies, and is often under ten thousand.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW A SLOGAN BECOMES A RULE',nodes:['A memorable product name','Repeated in adverts and advice','Built into devices as a goal','Cited as if it were a finding'],note:'Once a number is embedded, nobody asks where it came from. Repetition stands in for an origin.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The number is painted on the plinth, not shown on the device. It is a product name on display, not a reading.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'WHAT THE TARGET DOES',text:'On a busy day you manage six thousand steps, and your watch shows the ring unfilled. It feels like a failed day. By the evidence the piece describes, those steps still counted. The target turned a benefit into a shortfall.'},{kind:'try',text:'If your phone or watch is set to ten thousand, change the goal to a number you reach on most days, and treat anything above it as extra.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A colleague says: \u201cNo point walking today, I\u2019ll never get to ten thousand.\u201d What is the best reply?',options:['Steps below ten thousand still count; the figure began as a product name','Fair enough: the benefits only really start once you pass ten thousand','Skip today and double up tomorrow, so the weekly average still works','Ten thousand is only the minimum, so aim for fifteen thousand instead'],answer:0,why:'The number is not a threshold. Benefits build up well below it, and its origin was a sales name rather than a finding.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'JAPAN, THE 1960s',text:'A Japanese pedometer of the 1960s was sold as *manpo-kei*, the ten-thousand-step meter. The figure is usually said to have been chosen because it was memorable, and because the written character for ten thousand resembles a walking figure. It was a product name.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A figure repeated everywhere, in advice and on devices, can have no empirical origin at all, and once embedded it is cited as though it did. Ten thousand steps is the plainest case.'},{kind:'term',word:'Threshold',meaning:'A line below which something does not work and above which it does. Treating ten thousand as a threshold implies that nine thousand steps is a failure. The evidence does not describe a line like that.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What research found later',text:'Benefits accrue well below ten thousand, and substantial gains start far lower. The point where the curve for risk of death flattens differs between studies and is often under ten thousand. The number later attracted evidence. It did not come from it.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'A SLOPE, NOT A GATE',low:'Very few steps a day',high:'Many thousands of steps a day',note:'Gains begin far towards the left and the slope flattens towards the right. Nothing happens suddenly at ten thousand.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The number sits on the plinth, where a price or a brand would go. Nothing in this window has measured anything.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The number is not harmful. For someone who likes a clear target it may even help. The objection is to treating it as a finding or a pass mark. And the origin story is itself reported rather than settled: the walking-figure detail is what is usually said, so hold it loosely too.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of another round number you treat as a rule for health or work. Do you know where it came from, or only that everyone says it?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'An app sets you a round daily target and marks you down for falling a little short. What does this idea suggest you check first?',options:['Where the figure came from, and whether it marks a real line','Whether most people you know manage to hit the same daily target','Whether the app is popular enough for its targets to be trusted','How to reach the target, since an expert must have set the figure'],answer:0,why:'A figure on a device can be marketing with no empirical origin. The first question is where it came from, and whether falling just short really is a failure.'}]}},'walking-is-the-underrated-one_3':{pictureCaption:'The bike and the gym bag have not moved all week. The ordinary shoes by the door have still been going out.',bridge:'With the target set aside, the next question is which exercise keeps happening when life gets in the way.',basics:{title:'The one that keeps going in a bad week',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'A ROUGH WEEK',text:'It is a bad week. You have two tests. Your swim class moves to a time you can\u2019t make. Your football boots are lost. By Friday you have done no sport at all.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why plans fall apart',text:'Some sports need a lot: kit, a lift, a booked time, a team. Each of those things can go wrong. In a hard week, one of them often does, and the whole thing stops.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Walking needs almost nothing, so it is the thing most likely to keep going when a week goes wrong. Keep that one going, even if the rest stops.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The bike and the sports bag have not moved all week. The plain shoes by the door have still been going out.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE THE BOTTOM RUNG',text:'Think of a ladder. On good days you climb high. On bad days you slip down. Walking is the bottom rung. You can almost always stand on it, so you don\u2019t end up on the ground.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next bad week, don\u2019t try to keep everything. Keep a ten-minute walk. Let the rest wait.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Ella has football, swimming and a walk to school. This week she has three tests, and swimming is off. What should she keep?',options:['The walk to school, since it needs almost nothing','Football, since it is the hardest work','Nothing, and start again next week'],answer:0,why:'The walk needs no kit, no lift and no booking. That is why it can keep going when the rest stops.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE USUAL RANKING',belief:'The best exercise is the one that does the most per session.',reality:'Over a year, the comparison favours the one still happening. A demanding routine dropped in March does less than a modest one that never stops.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Walking is the activity most likely to survive a difficult week, and durability is what decides outcomes. Hold it as the floor, not the aspiration.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why demanding routines break',text:'Every requirement is a point of failure: kit to pack, a trip to the gym, a class to book, recovery afterwards. Under stress one of them gives, and the routine goes with it. The more a form of exercise needs, the sooner it is dropped.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHAT EACH ONE NEEDS',left:{title:'The gym session',items:['A change of clothes','Travel and a booking','Time to recover','A clear hour']},right:{title:'The walk',items:['What you are wearing','Your front door','Nothing afterwards','Ten minutes, if that is all']},note:'A walk can also be done while doing something else. Nothing on the left allows that.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A week that went badly: bike untouched, kit bag unopened. The ordinary shoes by the door are the ones still going out.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why ten minutes matters',text:'Walking scales down without feeling like a failure. Ten minutes on a bad day is still the habit, not a broken version of it. That is the property that counts when everything else has gone wrong.'},{kind:'try',text:'Decide now what your floor is for a bad week. Make it small enough to survive one: a ten-minute walk from your own door.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Your gym membership lapses during a hard month at work. What does this idea recommend?',options:['Keep a short daily walk going as the floor until things settle','Rejoin at once and train harder to make up for the lost month','Stop for now, and restart properly once work calms down','Buy home equipment so the full routine can carry on as before'],answer:0,why:'The floor is the thing with no equipment and no scheduling. Protect it, and let the rest go until there is room again.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'OVER TWELVE MONTHS',prompt:'Plan A: a demanding routine that does more per session, dropped by spring. Plan B: a daily walk that does less, and never stops. Which does more over the year?',options:['Plan A, because each session does more','Plan B, because it is still happening','They come out about even'],answer:1,reveal:'The piece argues that the comparison over a year favours the one still happening. Strength per session is not the outcome. What adds up over time is.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Durability, more than intensity per session, determines outcomes. Walking has almost no failure modes, so it is what remains when everything else has been dropped. That makes it the floor worth defending.'},{kind:'term',word:'Failure mode',meaning:'An engineering phrase for one specific way a system can break. A routine that needs a car, a booking and a free hour has at least three. Walking has almost none.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW A ROUTINE COLLAPSES',nodes:['A hard week arrives','One requirement fails','The session is skipped','Skipping becomes the pattern'],note:'Each extra requirement is another place this chain can start. Walking gives it very few.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Floor, not aspiration',text:'The piece is precise about the role. Walking is not offered as the ideal programme. It is what continues when the gym membership lapses, a state a great many people reach. In a bad week, protect the thing with no equipment and no scheduling, and let the rest go.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The bike and the kit bag sit unused. The plain shoes have still been going out. That is a floor holding.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The argument is about durability, not effect. More demanding activities may do more per session, and for some goals walking is no substitute for them. Nor is walking free of barriers for everyone: injury, illness, unsafe streets or long hours can remove it too. It is the floor most likely to hold, not one that always does.'},{kind:'recap',points:['A routine\u2019s requirements are its points of failure.','Over a year, the one still happening comes out ahead.','Hold walking as the floor, and let the rest flex.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'Someone objects: a walk does far less than a hard workout, so this is just settling for less. What is the best answer?',options:['Per session, yes; but a year\u2019s outcome depends on what continues','Wrong: a walk does as much as any workout, as long as it is long enough','True, so walking should be kept for people who cannot train hard','Effort per session is irrelevant, so intensity never matters at all'],answer:0,why:'The piece concedes that harder sessions may do more each time. Its claim is that durability decides the outcome over a year.'}]}},'walking-is-the-underrated-one_4':{pictureCaption:'He has brought the problem with him. The questions keep pace while he walks, which is when walking helps the thinking.',bridge:'The piece turns from body to head, with the same care not to oversell.',basics:{title:'Stuck? Take the problem for a walk',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'A BLANK PAGE',text:'You have to think up an idea for a class story. You sit at your desk. Nothing comes. Twenty minutes go by, and you have one line you don\u2019t even like.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'What is most likely to help you think of ideas?',options:['Sit still and try harder','Walk about, thinking about it as you go','Stop thinking about it for a week'],answer:1,reveal:'In tests, people thought of more new ideas while walking than while sitting. It worked indoors and outdoors. The help came mostly during the walk.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Walking can help you think up new ideas when you are stuck. The help is real but small, and it comes mostly while you are walking.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'He is not leaving the problem behind. The questions come with him, and he works on them as he walks.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What kind of stuck?',text:'It helps when you need *new* ideas: things to write, ways round a problem, a plan. That is different from doing a set task, like a page of sums.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Walking makes you cleverer for good.',reality:'That is not what the tests show. They show something smaller. While you walk, and for a short time after, ideas come more easily.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you are stuck for an idea, take the problem with you on a short walk round the house or garden. Keep it in your head as you go.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Sam needs a name for his new club. He is stuck. What does this idea say to do?',options:['Walk about while he thinks of names','Go for a walk, then sit down to think','Walk every day so he gets cleverer'],answer:0,why:'The help comes mostly while you walk, so take the problem with you. Walking first and thinking later misses most of it.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'FORTY MINUTES, ONE IDEA',text:'You need a name for a new project, or three ways out of a scheduling knot. You have stared at the same document for forty minutes. Every option you think of is a variation on the first one.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The thinking benefit of walking is real, modest, and mostly during. If you are stuck on something that needs generating rather than executing, think about it while you walk.'},{kind:'term',word:'Divergent thinking',meaning:'Producing many different possible answers to an open problem, rather than finding the one right answer. A classic test asks for as many uses for an everyday object as you can think of.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the experiments found',text:'In experiments, people came up with more alternative uses for objects while walking than while seated. This held indoors or out. The effect lasted briefly afterwards. That is the better-supported part.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'HONEST CLAIM, INFLATED CLAIM',left:{title:'Better supported',items:['More ideas while walking','Lasts a short while after','Helps with generating options']},right:{title:'Less well supported',items:['Lasting gains in healthy adults','A brain booster to take daily','Sharper at every kind of task']},note:'The honest version is a working instruction, not a health claim.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The questions keep pace with him while he walks. That is where the help is: on the path, not back at the desk.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you are stuck on something that needs new options, note the question. Then leave your desk and walk for fifteen minutes with only that in mind.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You have a report to proofread and a campaign slogan to invent. Where is a walk most likely to help?',options:['Inventing the slogan, by thinking about it as you walk','Proofreading, because the walk sharpens your attention','Both equally, as long as you walk before you start','Neither, since the effect is too small to bother with'],answer:0,why:'The effect is on generating options, and it arrives mostly during the walk. Checking a document is execution. That is not what the evidence describes.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'WHAT IT IS NOT',word:'Nootropic',meaning:'A substance or product claimed to improve thinking, such as a focus supplement. The piece is careful to say walking is not one. It makes no claim that a walk upgrades the brain.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The cognitive effect of walking is real, modest and largely concurrent: divergent thinking improves during and shortly after a walk. That is a smaller claim than lasting improvement, and a more useful one.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The evidence the piece cites',text:'The best-known finding comes from experiments in which people generated more alternative uses for objects while walking than while seated. It held indoors as well as out, so it is not only about the scenery. The effect persisted briefly after the walk ended.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'HOW BIG A CLAIM',low:'Walking helps while you walk',high:'Walking makes you sharper for good',note:'The evidence sits near the left. Lasting improvement in healthy adults, on the timescales usually implied, is the less well-supported end.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the small claim is more useful',text:'A vague promise of a better brain gives you nothing to do today. A modest, concurrent effect gives you an instruction: when stuck on something that needs generating, take the problem for a walk rather than a break. The help arrives during the walk, so the problem has to come along.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Possible answers walking alongside him. They keep pace only while he moves, which is the whole of the claim.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The finding is about generating options, not executing them, in controlled tasks. It does not show that walking cracks hard problems, replaces focused work, or improves thinking in the long run. Modest means modest: a walk is likely to help, not certain to. Selling it as a brain hack is the inflation the piece warns against.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE OVERREACH',prompt:'Which statement goes beyond what the piece\u2019s evidence supports?',options:['A daily walk will steadily improve your memory over the coming years','Walking while thinking may help you list more options for a problem','Some of the boost to idea generation lasts briefly after the walk','The effect showed up whether people walked indoors or outdoors'],answer:0,why:'Lasting improvement in healthy adults is the claim the piece calls less well supported. The other three describe the modest, concurrent effect it does cite.'}]}},'walking-is-the-underrated-one_5':{pictureCaption:'The path leaves straight from a doorstep crossed every day anyway. The walk needs no trip of its own, only the one already happening.',bridge:'Idea 3 made walking the floor for a bad week. This one is about making sure it happens on an ordinary one.',basics:{title:'Hook your walk onto something you already do',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TWO PLANS',prompt:'Ava and Ben both want to walk more. Ava will go for a walk at some point each day. Ben will get off the bus one stop early on the way home from school. Who is still walking in a month?',options:['Ava','Ben','Both the same'],answer:1,reveal:'Most likely Ben. Ava\u2019s walk has to fight homework, games and friends for a spot. It usually loses. Ben\u2019s bus ride home happens anyway, so his walk comes with it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A walk on its own is easy to skip. Hook it onto something you already do every day. Then the walk happens when that thing happens.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The path starts at a doorstep you cross every day anyway. The walk does not need its own trip. It starts where you already are.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A TRAIN CARRIAGE',text:'A train carriage has no engine. It still gets there on time, every day, because it is hooked to the engine. Hook your walk onto something that already runs every day, and it gets pulled along too.'},{kind:'steps',title:'How to hook on a walk',steps:['Pick something you do every day at about the same time.','Add a short walk straight after it, or as part of it.','Keep it small. Ten minutes is plenty.','Walk each time that thing happens.']},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one thing that happens every day, like getting home from school or finishing tea. Add ten minutes of walking straight after it.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Zara wants to walk more. Which plan is most likely to stick?',options:['Walk the long way home from school each day','Go for a walk whenever she has free time','Take one long walk on Saturdays if it is sunny'],answer:0,why:'She walks home from school every school day anyway, so the walk comes with it. Free time is easy to fill with other things.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE USUAL PLAN',belief:'To walk more, decide firmly that you will fit in a walk each day.',reality:'A walk at some point today is a standalone plan. It competes with everything else in the day and usually loses. The piece\u2019s fix is not a firmer decision. It is a better place to put the walk.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Attaching a walk to something already fixed in your day is what turns it from an intention into a habit. The walk inherits the reliability of the thing it is attached to.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why attaching works',text:'Lunch, the school run and the end of the working day already happen at reliable times, with no decision from you. Tie the walk to one of them and it borrows that timing. Tying a new behaviour to an existing cue is one of the better-supported ideas in the habit literature.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'SAME TEN MINUTES',left:{title:'A standalone walk',items:['Needs a slot of its own','Needs a fresh decision daily','Competes with everything else']},right:{title:'An attached walk',items:['Rides on something already fixed','Starts when that thing happens','Inherits its reliability']},note:'The walk is the same. What differs is whether it has anything holding it in place.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE STRONGEST FORM',text:'You have a regular call that needs no screen. You take it on foot. No new time had to be found: half an hour already committed became half an hour of walking. The piece calls this the strongest form, because it converts time you have rather than asking for more.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The walk starts at a doorstep you already cross every day. No separate trip, no new slot: it is attached to what already happens.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one fixed daily event, such as finishing lunch or closing the laptop at the end of the day, and attach ten minutes of walking to it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You want to walk more through a busy month. Which plan is most likely to survive it?',options:['Walk for ten minutes straight after lunch every working day','Set a reminder to take a walk at some point each afternoon','Plan one long walk each weekend whenever the diary allows','Buy a step tracker so you stay motivated to fit walks in'],answer:0,why:'Lunch already happens at a reliable time, so the walk inherits that reliability. The other plans still leave the walk competing with everything else.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'NINE IN THE EVENING',text:'The walk you meant to fit in at some point today has not happened, again. Nothing stopped it. Everything else simply arrived first, and each of those things had a time attached. The walk did not.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A walk scheduled in the abstract competes with everything else in the day and usually loses. Attached to an existing event with reliable timing, it inherits that reliability, and an intention becomes a habit.'},{kind:'term',word:'Cue',meaning:'Something that already happens and can set off a behaviour: finishing lunch, getting home, a call starting. The piece describes tying a new behaviour to an existing cue as one of the better-supported ideas in the habit literature.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE WALK BORROWS TIMING',nodes:['A fixed daily event','It happens without a decision','The walk is tied to it','The walk happens too'],note:'A standalone walk has no timing to borrow. Each day it has to win a fresh contest against everything else.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Which anchors work',text:'The piece names existing events with reliable timing: after lunch, after the school run, the end of the working day, a call that does not need a screen. What they share is that they happen whether or not you feel like walking, at roughly the same point each day.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the call on foot is strongest',text:'Most anchors still ask for new time, just at a fixed point. A call taken on foot asks for none. It converts time already committed. The general rule follows: anything that does not require the day to be longer is far more likely to survive than anything that does.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The path leaves straight from a doorstep crossed every day anyway. Nothing new is added to the day except the turn onto the path.'},{kind:'limits',text:'An anchor lends only the reliability it has. If the commute, the lunch break or the school run changes, the walk can vanish with it. Some anchors cannot carry a walk, such as a call that needs a screen. And the evidence the piece points to is for cue-linked habits in general. That the call on foot is strongest is its reasoning, not a tested result.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'DIAGNOSE THE FAILURE',prompt:'Someone\u2019s walk was tied to their commute and held for a year. They began working from home, and within a fortnight the walks had stopped. What happened?',options:['The anchor went, so the walk went; tie it to another fixed event','Their willpower faded; they should recommit to walking at some point daily','The habit was never real, since habits only form with a daily step target','Home working is less active, so the walks no longer mattered to them'],answer:0,why:'The walk inherited its reliability from the commute, so it went when the commute went. The fix is a new anchor, not a firmer intention.'}]}}}},3032,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'wealth-of-nations_1':{pictureCaption:'A heap of pins, and two hands repeating the one small step that made them. The heap is the gain; the hands carry the cost.',basics:{title:'Splitting the work: faster, but at a cost',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'CARDS FOR THE FAIR',text:'Your class is making cards to sell at the school fair. At first, each child makes a whole card. It is slow. Then you split the jobs. One child folds. One draws. One writes. One adds glitter. Now the pile grows fast.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'A BOOK THAT STARTS WITH PINS',text:'Adam Smith was a Scottish thinker. In 1776 he wrote a famous book about how countries get rich. It starts with pins. One man alone could hardly make twenty pins a day. Ten men who split the steps could make thousands.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Splitting work into small jobs makes far more things. But doing one tiny job all day can leave a person knowing less. Both are true.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Look at the huge pile of pins. Now look at the hands. They make the same small move, again and again.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The part people forget',text:'Later in the same book, Smith wrote about the cost. If your whole job is one tiny step, you never have to think. Over the years, your mind gets less use. Smith said the country should help pay for schools, so that every mind got used.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'BACK AT THE FAIR',text:'The child who only adds glitter is very fast. But ask her to make a whole card, and she can\u2019t. And if the folder is off sick, no one else knows how to fold.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your group splits a big school project. Your only job is the colouring. What does Smith\u2019s idea warn you about?',options:['You may be quick but not learn how it all fits','Splitting up the jobs will make the work slower','Colouring is the least useful job, so it does not count'],answer:0,why:'Splitting the jobs makes the work fast. The cost is that each of you only sees your own small part.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'THE PIN FACTORY, 1776',text:'Adam Smith opens The Wealth of Nations in a small pin works. Making a pin takes about eighteen separate operations. Ten workers, each handling only a few, turn out tens of thousands of pins a day. One person doing every step alone could scarcely make twenty.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Dividing work into narrow steps multiplies output enormously. It also narrows the people doing the steps. Smith saw both, and treated the result as a trade-off to be paid for, not a free gain.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHY SPLITTING WORKS',nodes:['Each person repeats one step','They become quick and skilled at it','No time lost switching tasks','Output multiplies'],note:'Smith adds a third reason: simple, repeated motions are easy to hand over to machines.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The other half',text:'Hundreds of pages later, Smith returns to the worker. Someone who spends a life on a few simple operations, he argues, never has to use their understanding, and loses the habit. His answer was not to undo the division of labour but to pay, publicly, for schooling to offset it.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'A TEAM, REORGANISED',text:'A support team is split so each person owns one narrow step: sorting, refunds or escalation. Tickets close faster. A year later the refund system changes, and nobody understands the whole process well enough to redesign it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Look at the size of the heap, then at the hands. The narrow motion that built the heap is all those hands now practise.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick the most specialised role on your team, perhaps your own. Name one thing that person no longer gets to understand, and put it back this month: a rotation, or a shared review.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A manager splits a five-person team into five narrow roles, and output rises. What would Smith say she should also do?',options:['Budget for what the narrow roles wear away, such as rotation','Nothing more: the higher output settles the question','Undo the split, since narrow work always harms people','Pay the narrowest roles less, since they need less skill'],answer:0,why:'Smith kept the division of labour and paid for its cost on purpose. Cheering it alone, or abandoning it, both miss his position.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'BOOK I AND BOOK V',text:'The Wealth of Nations opens with the division of labour and treats it as the main source of rising wealth. The pin factory sits in the first chapter. The warning sits in the fifth book, in a discussion of what the state should pay for.'},{kind:'term',word:'Division of labour',meaning:'Splitting one job into many narrow tasks, each done by a different person. Smith credits it with three gains: skill from repetition, time saved by not switching, and machines invented for simple motions.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The division of labour is the engine of productivity and, carried far enough, a slow injury to the people inside it. Smith\u2019s answer was to keep the engine and pay publicly for the damage.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The warning, in his terms',text:'In Book V Smith argues that a worker confined to a few simple operations meets no problem that calls for thought. He loses the habit of exerting his understanding, and becomes about as dulled and ignorant as a person can be, unfit to judge even his country\u2019s larger interests.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Book I: the gain',items:['Output multiplies','Skill deepens in one step','Machines take over simple motions']},right:{title:'Book V: the cost',items:['Understanding goes unused','Judgement outside the job weakens','Nothing in the market repairs it']},note:'Read one book and you meet a cheerleader for efficiency. Read both and you meet a man weighing a trade-off.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The heap is Book I. The hands, making the same small move for the thousandth time, are Book V.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Smith had the eighteenth-century factory hand in mind, and much narrow work today is skilled and absorbing. Specialisation also lets knowledge go deeper than any generalist could. And his remedy, schooling in youth, does little for a mind dulled at forty. The modern equivalents, rotation and training, cost output now, which is why they get cut.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A firm narrows each engineer to one service so it can ship faster. Which proposal is closest to Smith\u2019s position?',options:['Keep the narrow roles, and fund rotation and reviews to rebuild the overview','Keep the narrow roles; if understanding matters, the market will reward it','Abolish the narrow roles, because specialisation damages the people in them','Narrow the roles further, since output is the only real measure of success'],answer:0,why:'Smith kept the division of labour and argued that its remedy must be paid for deliberately, because the process causing the harm will not fix it.'}]}},'wealth-of-nations_2':{pictureCaption:'Bread and meat set out at dawn before anyone asked. The butcher is turning away to his own business, and your dinner is there anyway.',bridge:'Idea 1 showed how splitting work makes more. This one asks why strangers bother to supply each other at all.',basics:{title:'Why the baker bakes for you',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'SATURDAY MORNING',prompt:'You buy a bun at the bakery. Why was the baker up at four in the morning to make it?',options:['The baker cares about you','Selling buns is how the baker earns a living','The shop has a rule about it'],answer:1,reveal:'The baker has never met you. But buns pay the bills. So the buns are there, every day, for anyone who comes in.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Most things you buy are there because selling them helps the seller. Adam Smith saw this as a kind of machine. He did not say that being selfish is good.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The bread and meat are out before you ask. The butcher is busy with his own day. Your dinner is there anyway.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A WINDMILL',text:'A windmill does not grind flour to be kind. The wind pushes it round. Put it in the right place, and the wind does the work. A shop is a bit like that. The wish to earn is the wind.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Adam Smith said that being greedy is good.',reality:'No. Smith, a Scottish thinker, also wrote a whole book about how we feel for each other, and about right and wrong. Here he was just saying what you can count on from a stranger.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'AT HOME',text:'Your little brother keeps forgetting to feed the cat. Asking nicely does not work. So you make a deal. If he feeds the cat all week, he picks the film on Friday. Now the cat gets fed.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Why is there nearly always milk in the shop, even though the shop owner has never met you?',options:['Selling milk helps the owner earn money','Shop owners are kinder than other people','A law says every shop must sell milk'],answer:0,why:'The owner\u2019s own interest keeps the milk on the shelf. You can count on that, even from a stranger.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'BEFORE YOU WAKE',picture:!0,text:'By dawn the bread is out and the meat is cut. None of the people who did it know your name, and nobody arranged it for your sake. Nothing has been asked for. The goods are there because it pays to have them there.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Smith\u2019s butcher and baker passage describes a mechanism: a system that turns private motives into public supply. It is a claim about what can be relied on, not praise for selfishness.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why build on interest',text:'Smith\u2019s point is reliability. Kindness is real, but it is unevenly spread, and a stranger cannot count on it. Interest is steady. If supplying you pays the baker, you can expect bread from someone who has never met you. A system can be built on that.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Adam Smith taught that greed is good.',reality:'His other book, The Theory of Moral Sentiments, is largely about sympathy and moral judgement. In this passage he is doing engineering, not ethics: saying what will reliably turn up, not what makes a good person.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO GET HELP',left:{title:'Relying on kindness',items:['Works among friends and family','Uneven from person to person','Hard to ask of strangers']},right:{title:'Relying on interest',items:['Works among strangers','Steady and predictable','Only as good as its conditions']},note:'The last line matters most. Later ideas in this book are about those conditions.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of something you need someone to do reliably. Ask whether it is plainly in their interest to do it. If not, change that before you lean on their goodwill.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A charity\u2019s volunteer rota keeps collapsing. Which response draws on Smith\u2019s point?',options:['Arrange shifts so turning up also serves the volunteer, such as training','Decide volunteers are selfish and stop relying on them altogether','Remind the volunteers more often that generosity is a duty','Avoid offering anything back, since rewards spoil kindness'],answer:0,why:'Goodwill is real but uneven. Smith\u2019s point is that an arrangement holds when it also serves the people keeping it going.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'The butcher and baker passage praises self-interest as a virtue.',reality:'It describes a mechanism. We expect our dinner, Smith says, from the trader\u2019s attention to his own interest rather than his goodwill. That is a claim about what works between strangers, not about what is good.'},{kind:'timeline',kicker:'TWO BOOKS, ONE AUTHOR',points:[{when:'1759',what:'The Theory of Moral Sentiments: sympathy, conscience and moral judgement'},{when:'1776',what:'The Wealth of Nations: the butcher, the brewer and the baker'},{when:'1790',what:'A heavily revised edition of the moral book, the year Smith died'}],note:'He wrote the moral book first and kept working on it. The economist never replaced the moralist.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Self-interest is dependable where kindness is not, so a system can be built on it. But it is only a mechanism: it delivers good outcomes under conditions, and without them the same motive delivers monopoly.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The mechanism has conditions',text:'Interest turns into supply only when sellers compete, buyers can judge, and sellers cannot agree among themselves. Remove those and the baker\u2019s interest points elsewhere: towards fixing prices, limiting supply or winning a legal privilege. Smith spends long chapters on exactly that.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Nobody here asked for anything. The goods are out because putting them out pays. That is the whole mechanism, and all it promises.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Interest is not the only thing holding a society together, and Smith never said it was: trust, conscience and law do work that prices cannot. The passage is also routinely misused. Quoted as a moral endorsement, it gets used to defend monopolies and trade privileges that Smith himself attacked at length.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which use of the butcher and baker passage would Smith reject?',options:['Defending a merger to one supplier, as self-interest serves the public','Writing a contract so the supplier earns more by delivering on time','Expecting a stranger\u2019s shop to stay stocked without any appeal to kindness','Noting that customers depend daily on bakers they have never met'],answer:0,why:'The mechanism serves the public only under competition. A single supplier removes the condition, and Smith attacked monopoly directly.'}]}},'wealth-of-nations_3':{pictureCaption:'Three chairs drawn close, cups pushed together, the shops outside shut. One set of scales sits between them: the sellers have been talking prices.',bridge:'Idea 2 said self-interest needs conditions. This one is about the people best placed to remove them: sellers who agree among themselves.',basics:{title:'Watch out when sellers team up',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'AT THE SCHOOL FAIR',text:'Three stalls sell lemonade at the fair. Each wants your money, so each keeps its price low. Then the three stall owners have a chat behind the tent. Now all three charge double. You have nowhere else to go.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When people who sell the same thing get together, they are tempted to work against the buyers. So when sellers ask for a rule, look at it closely.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Who said this?',text:'Adam Smith was a Scottish thinker. In 1776 he wrote a famous book about trade. Business people often quote him. But he was tough on them. He said sellers of the same thing rarely meet without plotting to raise prices.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'AFTER CLOSING TIME',caption:'After closing time, the shop owners sat close at this table. The one set of scales in the middle is the clue. They were agreeing on prices.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHY SELLERS WIN',left:{title:'Sellers',items:['Just a few of them','They know each other','Each gains a lot']},right:{title:'Buyers',items:['Lots of them','Most are strangers','Each loses a little']},note:'So sellers find it easy to team up. Buyers hardly notice what it costs them.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Smith thought that people who run shops are bad people.',reality:'No. He thought anyone in their place would be tempted. The danger comes from where they stand, not from who they are.'},{kind:'try',text:'When a group asks for a new rule, ask who the rule helps most. If it helps the group that asked, look twice.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'The two ice cream vans in your town agree to charge the same high price. Who loses out?',options:['The people who buy ice cream','The two ice cream van owners','Nobody, since the prices match now'],answer:0,why:'When sellers agree, buyers lose the choice that kept prices low.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Adam Smith was a pure defender of business interests.',reality:'He is very hard on merchants and manufacturers. He warns that people in the same trade seldom meet, even socially, without the talk turning to a scheme. It is a plan against the public, or a way to raise prices.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Treat any rule proposed by the people it protects with extra suspicion. Smith thought a trade\u2019s interest is never quite the public\u2019s. And traders are far better placed to press theirs.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'THE BACK ROOM',caption:'Rivals in one trade, after hours, around one table and one shared set of scales. Nobody here needs to be a villain. Few sellers with a lot at stake find the talk turning to price.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it is structural',text:'Producers in one trade are few. They know each other, and each stands to gain a lot. Consumers are many and scattered, and each loses only a little. So producer interests get organised and heard. Consumer interests mostly do not. Nobody\u2019s character has to be bad for this to happen.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'WHO GETS HEARD',low:'Few, organised, a lot at stake each',high:'Many, scattered, a little at stake each',note:'The nearer a group sits to the left, the louder it is in any debate about rules, whatever its numbers.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'IN THE COMMITTEE ROOM',text:'A trade body asks for a costly new licence for anyone joining the trade. It says this is for safety. The safety case may be real. But the licence also keeps rivals out, and every customer pays a little more. Smith\u2019s test: who proposed it, and who gains?'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you read about a proposed rule, find out who asked for it. If it is the group the rule protects, read their case twice.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Established taxi firms lobby for a cap on new licences. They cite passenger safety. What does Smith\u2019s test suggest?',options:['Examine it closely, since the firms proposing it gain from fewer rivals','Accept it, since the firms know the trade better than anyone else','Reject it, since every regulation of a trade does harm','Ignore the safety claim, since firms only pretend to care'],answer:0,why:'Smith asks for suspicion, not automatic refusal. The rule may be right, but it comes from the people it protects.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'BOOK I, ON WAGES AND PROFITS',picture:!0,text:'In his chapters on wages and profits, Smith observes that people of the same trade seldom meet, even for amusement, without the talk ending in a scheme against the public or to raise prices. At the close of Book I he adds that any law proposed by merchants deserves the most suspicious attention before it is adopted.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Producers are few, organised and share an interest; consumers are many and scattered. So the producer\u2019s case reaches lawmakers and the consumer\u2019s mostly does not. A rule proposed by those it protects deserves special scrutiny.'},{kind:'term',word:'The mercantile system',meaning:'Smith\u2019s name for the policies of his day that protected home producers: import duties, export bounties, monopoly trading companies. He argued they were largely designed by merchants and manufacturers, and paid for by consumers.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What he was arguing against',text:'Book IV is a long attack on that system. Its target is not government as such but government that merchants have captured. His distrust of business and his distrust of state favours are one argument, because the favour is what the merchant lobbies for.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'The popular image',items:['Champion of business','Against government action','Trusts markets to police themselves']},right:{title:'The text',items:['Suspicious of merchants\u2019 proposals','Against favours won by lobbying','Expects sellers to collude']}},{kind:'limits',text:'The comparison in this idea\u2019s title is too neat. Smith was also scathing about rulers: he calls kings and ministers the greatest spendthrifts in society, and doubts any statesman could direct private investment wisely. And the asymmetry is a tendency, not a law. Consumers do sometimes organise, and a rule an industry proposes can still be right. Suspicion means closer examination, not refusal.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'Someone says Smith simply wanted less government. What is the best reply from the text?',options:['He opposed favours merchants won from government, and backed some public action','He wanted government to own and run the main industries directly','He trusted merchants to regulate their own trades fairly and openly','He believed government and business should never deal with each other'],answer:0,why:'His target was capture: laws written by producers for producers. He still expected the state to provide defence, justice, public works and some schooling.'}]}},'wealth-of-nations_4':{pictureCaption:'A small pot held up to the light. Its shadow is the size of the workshop: the tools, skill and hours behind it.',bridge:'The first ideas looked at how goods are made and supplied. This one asks what their price is made of.',basics:{title:'What is a price made of?',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TWO PAIRS OF SOCKS',prompt:'One pair of wool socks costs five times more than another. Why might that be?',options:['The shop is just being greedy','More work and rarer wool went into them','Prices have no reason at all'],answer:1,reveal:'Maybe someone knitted them by hand. Maybe the wool is hard to get. Before you call it a rip-off, ask what went into it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Behind a price there is usually work: the hours and skill it took to make the thing. Things that are rare or hard to get cost more too.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A small pot. Now look at its shadow. It is as big as the whole workshop: the tools, the skill and the hours.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO SAID THIS',text:'Adam Smith was a Scottish thinker. In 1776 he wrote a famous book about trade. He said the real cost of a thing is the toil and trouble of getting it. Toil means hard work. So, he thought, a price is a quick way to count work.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'YOUR POCKET MONEY',text:'Say you earn five pounds a week for chores. A game costs thirty pounds. So the game really costs six weeks of chores. That is Smith\u2019s idea, turned on you. Is the game worth six weeks?'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one thing you want to buy. Work out how many weeks of pocket money it costs. Then decide if it is worth it.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A cake from a bakery costs more than a cake mix from the shop. What is the best first guess about why?',options:['The bakery cake has more work and skill in it','The bakery just wants more of your money','Cake mix always tastes worse than real cake'],answer:0,why:'Someone baked and iced that cake for you. Their work is part of what you pay for.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE PRICE TAG',text:'A handmade chair costs ten times as much as a flat-pack one. Your first thought is that someone is overcharging. A better second thought is a question: what went into each of them?'},{kind:'idea',text:'Smith treats labour as the real measure of value: what a thing costs is the toil and trouble of getting it. The theory has been superseded, but its question survives. When a price looks strange, ask what work and scarcity sit behind it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Hold the object up and its shadow fills the room: the workshop, the tools and the hours. A price is that shadow, compressed.'},{kind:'explain',title:'More careful than the slogan',text:'Smith complicates his own idea at once. In a developed economy a price must also pay for capital and land. Skilled labour counts for more than unskilled. And the price on any given day moves around a natural level, with supply and demand, rather than sitting on it.'},{kind:'equation',kicker:'WHAT A PRICE PAYS FOR',parts:['Wages','Profit on capital','Rent on land'],result:'The natural price',note:'Smith\u2019s breakdown. The market price moves around it, pushed by how much is wanted and how much is offered.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Turn it on yourself',text:'The same reframing works for spending. The real price of a thing is not the number on the tag but the hours you worked to pay for it. On a take-home wage of fifteen pounds an hour, a six-hundred-pound phone is forty hours of your life.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A plumber charges a lot for a twenty-minute fix. What does Smith\u2019s question suggest you consider first?',options:['The years of training, and how few people can do the job','Whether the bill is more than you would like to pay','That plumbers must be fixing prices among themselves','That only the twenty minutes should count towards the price'],answer:0,why:'Smith\u2019s question is what work and scarcity sit behind a price. The twenty minutes rest on years of skill that few people have.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'BOOK I, CHAPTERS 5 TO 7',text:'Smith argues that labour is the real measure of exchangeable value. What a thing truly costs is the toil and trouble of acquiring it; what it is worth to its owner is the toil it saves him, or lets him command from others. Money is only the usual way of counting.'},{kind:'term',word:'Natural price',meaning:'The price that just covers the ordinary wages, profit and rent needed to bring a good to market. The market price, set by supply and demand on the day, is pulled towards it but rarely sits on it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Labour is the real measure of what a thing costs, though not the whole story of its price. The lasting move is to read a price as a question: what work and what scarcity sit behind it?'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The pot is the price; the shadow is the labour. Smith asks you to look at the wall, not the hand.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'What Smith claimed',items:['Labour measures real value','Price splits into wages, profit and rent','Market price circles a natural price']},right:{title:'What later economists changed',items:['Value depends on usefulness at the margin','Demand shapes price, not only cost','Labour alone cannot explain scarce goods']}},{kind:'limits',text:'As a theory of price it is superseded. A rare stamp or a plot of city land can be worth a great deal with almost no labour in it, and a year of work on something nobody wants is worth little. Smith knew some of this. Use the idea as a question about cost, never as proof that a price is fair or unfair.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these is a misuse of Smith\u2019s idea?',options:['Concluding a painting is overpriced because it took only an afternoon','Asking what skill and scarcity lie behind a surprisingly high quote','Pricing a purchase of your own in the hours you worked for it','Expecting a market price to drift above and below its natural level'],answer:0,why:'Labour is a measure, not a ceiling. Skill, scarcity and demand also set price, so hours alone cannot prove a price unfair.'}]}},'wealth-of-nations_5':{pictureCaption:'Before the market opens, someone walks the empty square chalking the lines. A market runs on ground somebody has to prepare.',bridge:'Idea 2 called self-interest a mechanism. This one names the conditions the mechanism needs, and says they are somebody\u2019s job.',basics:{title:'A market needs fair rules first',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A FOOTBALL MATCH',text:'A football match only works if some things are set up first. There are lines on the pitch. There is a ref. Both teams have the same number of players. Take those away, and the game falls apart. That does not mean football is bad.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A market, where people buy and sell, works well only when some things are true. There must be more than one seller. Buyers must know what they are getting. Sellers must not team up.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Before the market opens, someone draws the lines on the ground. A market needs this kind of work done first.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Three things a fair market needs',steps:['More than one seller, so you can choose.','Buyers can see what they are getting.','Sellers do not secretly agree on prices.'],note:'If one of these is missing, the market can go wrong.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE TUCK SHOP',text:'Your school has one tuck shop. It is the only place to buy snacks at break. So it can charge what it likes. The problem is not buying and selling. The problem is that there is only one seller.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO SETS IT UP?',text:'Adam Smith was a Scottish thinker who wrote about trade in 1776. People think he said markets fix themselves. He did not. He said some jobs need the government, like roads, bridges and schools. Someone has to draw the lines.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A new game is sold in just one shop, and the shop doubles the price. What is missing?',options:['A second seller, so buyers have a choice','More people who want to buy the game','A bigger shop with more room inside'],answer:0,why:'With only one seller, no one can beat the price. One of the things a fair market needs is missing.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Smith believed markets sort themselves out if they are left alone.',reality:'He spent many pages on what happens when the conditions are missing. Where they fail, he expected government to act. He named public schooling, roads and bridges, and the rules for banking.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Markets are the best available mechanism when competition is real, buyers can judge what they buy, and sellers cannot collude. Those are prerequisites, not guarantees. Setting them up is somebody\u2019s job.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Before you say the market failed',steps:['Is there real competition, or one seller big enough to set the price?','Can buyers actually judge what they are buying?','Can new sellers get in, or do the firms already there keep them out?','Are sellers fixing prices among themselves?'],note:'A no to any of these is a missing condition, not proof that markets cannot work here.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'ONE PROVIDER IN TOWN',text:'A town has a single broadband provider. Prices rise, service slips, and people conclude the market has failed. Run the checklist: there is no rival, no cheap way for a new firm to lay cables, and no easy way to compare. The mechanism never had its conditions.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The square is empty and the stalls are not yet set. Someone is already at work, marking out the ground everyone will trade on.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The clearest case',text:'Smith has described how narrow factory work dulls the people doing it. He does not leave that harm to the market that caused it. He proposes publicly supported schools. The market is the tool. Deciding where it needs help is a separate job.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Patients cannot compare clinics\u2019 prices or results, and fees keep climbing. What would Smith check first?',options:['Whether buyers have enough information to judge','Whether the clinics\u2019 owners are unusually greedy','Whether demand for treatment is simply too high','Whether to replace the market with fixed prices'],answer:0,why:'Buyers who cannot judge cannot discipline sellers. A missing condition explains the failure better than bad character does.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'THE SLOGAN AND THE BOOK',left:{title:'The summary',items:['Markets regulate themselves','Government should stay out','Self-interest is enough']},right:{title:'The book',items:['Markets need conditions','Government supplies some of them','Self-interest without them breeds monopoly']},note:'The right-hand column takes up far more of the book than of its summaries.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Smith\u2019s case for markets is conditional. Competition, adequate information, open entry and the absence of collusion are prerequisites. Where they fail he expects public action, and he never suggests they arise by themselves.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where he wants the state',text:'Book V sets out the duties of the sovereign: defence, justice, and public works and institutions that benefit society but would never repay a private owner. Under that last heading come roads, bridges and canals, and the education of the common people. Elsewhere he backs rules on how banks issue notes.'},{kind:'term',word:'Natural liberty',meaning:'Smith\u2019s name for an order where each person pursues their own interest in their own way, provided they keep within the laws of justice. That proviso matters: the freedom assumes a legal frame someone maintains.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The chalk line goes down before the first stall opens. In Smith\u2019s account, a market trades on ground somebody else has to prepare.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The conditions are never all fully met, so applied loosely the framework can justify intervening almost anywhere. And the government that sets the conditions can be captured by the sellers it oversees, which Smith also warned about. The honest use is diagnostic: name the missing condition, then ask whether the proposed fix restores it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'A critic says Smith\u2019s support for public schooling contradicts his case for markets. What is the best reply?',options:['His case was conditional, and markets do not repair the harms they cause','He abandoned his case for markets in the last years of his career','Schools in his day were already run as competitive private markets','He meant schools only for the children of merchants and landowners'],answer:0,why:'There is no contradiction. The case for markets was always conditional, and schooling answered a harm the market would not correct.'}]}},'wealth-of-nations_6':{pictureCaption:'One pair of hands rests easy on the counter. The other grips the edge, its money tin already empty. The easy hands can wait longer.',bridge:'Idea 3 showed sellers combining against buyers. Here Smith turns to masters and workers, and asks who wins when both sides hold out.',basics:{title:'Whoever can wait longer usually wins',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'SWAPPING CARDS',text:'You want your friend\u2019s rare card. She wants one of yours. But her mum is picking her up in two minutes. You have all afternoon. Who do you think will give in first?'},{kind:'idea',text:'When two sides try to make a deal, the side that can wait longer is stronger. What they say matters less than how long each can hold out.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO NOTICED THIS',picture:!0,text:'Adam Smith was a Scottish thinker. In 1776 he wrote about bosses and workers arguing over pay. The bosses had money saved. They could last a year or two. Many workers could not last a week. So the bosses usually won.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'HOW LONG CAN YOU WAIT?',low:'I need a deal today',high:'I am fine without a deal for months',note:'The further right you sit, the easier it is to say no. And being able to say no is what moves the other side.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'The best talker always wins the deal.',reality:'Clever words help a bit. But if you need the deal right now, the other side can just wait. The talking sits on top of who can wait.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you want to swap or trade something, ask first: who needs this deal more, and who needs it sooner?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You want to sell your old bike. You need the money by Friday for a trip, and the buyer knows it. What is likely to happen?',options:['The buyer waits, and you drop your price','You get more because you are in a hurry','Your deadline makes no difference at all'],answer:0,why:'The buyer can wait and you cannot. That makes the buyer the stronger side.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE CONTRACT RENEWAL',prompt:'Your firm\u2019s biggest client says they are very relaxed about renewing. What should you weigh most?',options:['How relaxed they sound','Whether they have a replacement ready','How long you have worked together'],answer:1,reveal:'Anyone can claim to be relaxed; it costs nothing, so it tells you nothing. What informs is structure: who has an alternative, and whose plans break if this lapses.'},{kind:'idea',text:'In a negotiation, the outcome is mostly decided by who can afford to hold out longer. The arguments are made on top of that, and are judged against it.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'SMITH ON WAGES',picture:!0,text:'Smith describes wage bargaining without sentiment. Masters and workmen have opposing interests, and both combine. But masters could live a year or two on their stock, while many workmen could not last a week without pay. The law of his day also banned workmen\u2019s combinations while leaving masters\u2019 alone.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHAT TO LOOK AT',left:{title:'Free signals',items:['How calm they sound','Talk of other offers','Deadlines they announce']},right:{title:'Costly facts',items:['A replacement ready to go','Budget set aside for a gap','Whose quarter breaks if this lapses']},note:'Smith\u2019s question: what does each side lose for every week without a deal?'},{kind:'explain',title:'What to work on between deals',text:'Nothing improves your position in the room like an alternative arranged long before you need it: a second supplier, a cash buffer, another offer. That work is done months earlier, not in the meeting.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before your next renewal, write down how long each side could comfortably go without a deal. Negotiate from that, not from the tone of the emails.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Your landlord proposes a large rent rise. Which fact matters most to how this goes?',options:['How quickly each of you could find a new tenant or home','How politely the landlord worded the letter','How long you have personally lived in the area','Whether the rise is above the general rate of inflation'],answer:0,why:'Holding power decides the outcome. Whoever can more easily walk away can afford to wait.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'BOOK I, CHAPTER 8',text:'In the chapter on wages, Smith asks what decides the level of pay. Workers want as much as possible and masters as little, and both combine to get their way. It is not hard, he says, to see which side will usually win.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Bargaining outcomes are decided chiefly by holding power: who can endure longest without agreement. Rhetoric is downstream of that, so establish holding power on both sides before preparing a single argument.'},{kind:'explain',title:'His reasons',picture:!0,text:'Masters are fewer, so they combine easily, often tacitly. The law of his day forbade workers\u2019 combinations and not masters\u2019. And masters could live a year or two on their stock, while many workers could not last a week. The last reason is the one this idea generalises.'},{kind:'term',word:'BATNA',meaning:'Best alternative to a negotiated agreement: what you will do if this deal fails. A term from modern negotiation writing, not from Smith, but it names his point. The better your alternative, the longer you can wait.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW HOLDING POWER WORKS',nodes:['Reserves and alternatives','How long you can wait','Willingness to say no','The terms you get'],note:'Arguments act only at the last step. Smith\u2019s point is that the first step has usually settled it.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Smith described a specific, unequal world, where workers had no savings and no legal right to combine. Holding power is not fixed: savings, unions and welfare all shifted it, which fits his structure rather than refuting it. But treating every deal as a siege is a misuse. Where both sides gain from a long relationship, squeezing the weaker side poisons it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A startup negotiating with its only big customer has three months of cash left. What does Smith\u2019s analysis say to work on first?',options:['Lengthening the runway or finding a second customer','Rehearsing a stronger pitch for the next meeting','Appearing unconcerned so the customer cannot tell','Accepting any terms quickly before the cash runs out'],answer:0,why:'Pitch and poise sit on top of holding power. Changing how long you can wait changes the negotiation itself.'}]}},'wealth-of-nations_7':{pictureCaption:'Two dense pages, and one finger on a single short line. That is how much of the book the famous phrase occupies.',bridge:'Ideas 2 and 5 showed self-interest working only under conditions. This one is about the slogan that dropped them.',basics:{title:'One famous line, and what it really says',beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Adam Smith\u2019s big idea was an unseen hand that makes markets fix themselves.',reality:'The words come just once in his long book. They make one small point about where traders put their money. Most of the book is about other things.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHO WAS SMITH?',text:'Adam Smith was a Scottish thinker. In 1776 he wrote a very long book about trade and money. One line in it talks about an invisible hand. Today that line is famous. People quote it all the time.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A famous phrase can grow much bigger than what its writer meant. Smith\u2019s line is just one line. People now use it to end arguments it was never about.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A huge book, and one finger on one short line. That line is the famous one. Look how much of the book is not it.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A FILM TRAILER',text:'A trailer shows one exciting second of a film. Then you watch the film, and it is about something else. The famous line is like the trailer. The book is the film.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'IN AN ARGUMENT',text:'Your friend ends every argument by saying, *My dad says so.* But what did his dad say, and about what? Ask that, and the argument can start again.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Someone says Smith\u2019s famous line means shops should never have rules. What is the best reply?',options:['Smith used the line once, about something else','Smith did say that shops should have no rules','Smith never wrote that line at all'],answer:0,why:'The line is real, but it makes one small point. It is not a rule about everything.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE MEETING ENDS',text:'A debate about a new rule is going nowhere. Someone says the market will sort it out: the invisible hand. People nod, and the talk stops. Yet no one has said a thing about this rule.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The invisible hand appears once in The Wealth of Nations, inside one argument. When a famous principle is used to close a debate, ask what it really claims about this case.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the passage is about',text:'Smith is asking why merchants tend to prefer to invest at home rather than abroad. Each one seeks only his own safety and gain. Yet he ends up helping home industry without meaning to. It is a step in a case about limits on trade. It is not a general theory of markets.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One short line in a very long book. Much of what surrounds it is about cases where self-interest goes wrong.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'The phrase\u2019s reputation',items:['Markets always correct themselves','Self-interest serves everyone','So stop debating']},right:{title:'Smith\u2019s book',items:['One remark about investing at home','Collusion and monopoly hurt the public','So examine the case']}},{kind:'try',text:'Next time someone settles a debate with a famous principle, ask one question. What does that claim about this case, exactly?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A colleague waves off a plan to check suppliers for price-fixing. The invisible hand will handle it, they say. What is the best response?',options:['Smith himself warned that sellers conspire to raise prices','Agree, since self-interest always protects the buyer','Point out that Smith never used the phrase at all','Argue that markets never work and need full control'],answer:0,why:'The book the phrase comes from expects sellers to collude. The slogan cannot carry the weight being put on it.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'BOOK IV, CHAPTER 2',text:'The phrase occurs once in the book, in a chapter against restraints on imports. Smith argues that a merchant who prefers home investment for his own security is steered, without meaning to be, towards supporting the country\u2019s industry. The invisible hand is his image for that unintended result.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The invisible hand is one image in one argument, not a general theory. Its fame now does work the passage cannot support, so recover the discussion by asking what is being claimed about this case.'},{kind:'timeline',kicker:'ONCE IN EACH BOOK',points:[{when:'1759',what:'The Theory of Moral Sentiments: once, about how the rich end up sharing the necessities of life'},{when:'1776',what:'The Wealth of Nations: once, about merchants who prefer to invest at home'}],note:'Two passing images, in two different arguments. Neither is set up as a doctrine.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the caricature misses',text:'Around that sentence the book is full of cases where self-interest harms the public: sellers who meet and plot prices, monopolies, merchants writing trade law in their own favour. A writer who believed self-interest always served everyone would not have written those chapters.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The finger rests on one line. The rest of the spread, unread, holds most of what Smith actually argued.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The correction can be overdone. Smith did believe that self-interest, under the right conditions, often serves the public without anyone intending it. That is a real and central idea of the book, even if this phrase is not its home. Dismissing the idea because the phrase is rare is the same error in reverse: arguing about a reputation instead of a claim.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'BACK TO THE SOURCE',prompt:'Which statement fits the original passage best?',options:['A merchant\u2019s choice to invest at home ends up aiding home industry','Governments should never act in any market under any conditions','Prices are always fair because competition is always present','Any business that earns a profit must be serving society'],answer:0,why:'The passage is about an unintended benefit from one specific choice. The others stretch an image into a doctrine Smith argued against.'}]}},'wealth-of-nations_8':{pictureCaption:'A row of identical parts, one step repeated. At the end, a closed schoolbook: the remedy the bench itself will never supply.',bridge:'Idea 1 weighed the gain against the cost. This one asks who bears the cost, and whose job it is to fix it.',basics:{title:'When a good change has a hidden cost',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE NEW LUNCH QUEUE',prompt:'Your school speeds up lunch. Now each kitchen helper does one job. One scoops peas. One hands out trays. Lunch is faster. Who pays a cost?',options:['No one, it is just better','The helpers, whose work is now dull','The pupils, who wait longer'],answer:1,reveal:'The queue is quicker, so everyone is pleased. But the helpers now do one dull thing all day. The faster queue does not show that cost.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A change can do real good and real harm at the same time. The harm can land on people who did not choose it. If no one plans to fix it, it stays.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A long row of the same part, made by the same small step. At the end sits a closed schoolbook. That was Smith\u2019s fix.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHAT SMITH SAID',text:'Adam Smith was a Scottish thinker who wrote about trade in 1776. He loved how splitting work made more goods. But he saw that a worker doing one tiny job all day stops using their mind. The factory would never fix that. So he said the country should help pay for schools.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE HARM STAYS',nodes:['Work is split into tiny jobs','More goods get made','Workers stop using their minds','No one is paid to fix it'],note:'So someone has to choose to fix it. Smith\u2019s answer was school.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your club always makes one girl do all the tidying, because she is fastest. What does this idea say?',options:['Fine, but plan for her to learn other jobs too','It is fine, since the tidying gets done fast','Stop tidying, since it is not fair on her'],answer:0,why:'The speed is real. So is the cost to her. The fix has to be planned on purpose.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE PROCESS CHANGE',text:'A firm splits its claims team so each person handles one narrow step. Claims are processed faster, and the dashboard shows it. Over a year, staff lose the overall picture. Nobody can handle an unusual case, and two good people leave. None of that appears in the figure that justified the change.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A genuine gain can produce a genuine harm. It falls on people who did not choose it, and nothing in the mechanism will pay for the remedy. So the remedy has to be carried on purpose, by whoever made the change.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Smith\u2019s structure',text:'Smith admired the division of labour. Then he wrote unusually grimly about what it does to a worker kept to a few simple tasks. He did not treat that as a price to pay quietly. He argued the state must fund schooling. His reason: the system producing the harm would never correct it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The bench does its job: part after part, identical. The schoolbook at the end is the one thing the bench will never make.'},{kind:'equation',kicker:'THE HONEST PROPOSAL',parts:['The efficiency gain','The cost of the remedy'],result:'The real case for change',note:'Smith\u2019s structure, applied at work: budget the rotation, training or wider scope into the same proposal.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you narrow a role for efficiency, write down who loses what. Then put the fix into the same plan.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A hospital moves nurses into single-task roles to speed up its wards. By Smith\u2019s logic, who should plan the remedy?',options:['The managers who designed the change, in the same plan','The nurses themselves, in their own spare time','No one, since faster wards prove the change was right','The job market, which will reward nurses with broad skills'],answer:0,why:'The harm comes from the design, and the mechanism will not price the remedy. So whoever designed the change carries it.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Smith treated the costs of the division of labour as a fair price for growth.',reality:'He calls the dulling of the worker a real harm, and argues that in every advanced society it will fall on most working people unless government deliberately acts against it. That is a call for action, not acceptance.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Efficiency gains can produce harms that fall on people who did not choose them and that no market will price. Smith\u2019s conclusion: the remedy must be carried deliberately, by whoever is responsible for the system.'},{kind:'explain',title:'His remedy, in detail',text:'He points to the parish schools of Scotland as a model: a small school in every district, teaching reading, writing and arithmetic cheaply enough for a labourer\u2019s children. The teacher is to be paid partly by the public and partly by fees, so that he stays diligent.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHY NOBODY FIXES IT',nodes:['Narrow work raises output','The worker\u2019s understanding goes unused','The worker bears the cost','The employer\u2019s accounts never show it'],note:'The harm is invisible in the numbers that drive the decision, so no one inside the system is paid to fix it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Part after part, identical: the bench does exactly what it was built for. The closed book is what Smith said someone else must supply.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Smith\u2019s factory and his remedy belong to their century. Schooling in childhood may do little for a mind dulled at forty, and his concern was partly the worker as citizen and soldier, not only as employee. The responsibility can also be overstretched: blaming a designer for every effect of a change can stall useful reform. The claim is about costs the system will not price.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HARDER CASE',prompt:'An automation project strips the varied parts out of a job and leaves only monitoring. Which response follows Smith\u2019s structure most closely?',options:['Include retraining or wider scope in the project budget from the start','Proceed, since the workers can retrain themselves if they choose','Abandon automation, since narrowing work always harms people','Wait to see whether any harm shows up in the output figures'],answer:0,why:'The harm will not appear in the figures, and the mechanism will not pay for it. So the remedy has to be built into the change.'}]}},'wealth-of-nations_9':{pictureCaption:'Crates stacked into a wall and loaded for shipping, and behind them an empty plate on a doorstep. Production counts only if it fills plates.',bridge:'Idea 3 showed producers bending the rules to suit themselves. This is the standard Smith judged them by.',basics:{title:'Things are made for the people who use them',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE BAKE SALE',text:'Your class bakes 200 cupcakes for a bake sale. Everyone is proud of the big number. But the cakes are far too sweet, and most end up in the bin. Was the bake sale a success?'},{kind:'idea',text:'Things are made so that people can use them and enjoy them. That is the point. How much you made is not the point on its own.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A wall of crates, being loaded onto a ship. But look behind the crates. There is an empty plate. The crates only matter if they fill plates like that.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHAT SMITH SAID',text:'Adam Smith was a Scottish thinker. In 1776 he wrote a famous book about trade. He said the whole point of making things is for people to use them. He said this was so plain it hardly needed saying. Then he showed how often rulers forgot it.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Easy to count',items:['How many we made','How busy we were','How hard we worked']},right:{title:'What matters',items:['Did people use it?','Did it help them?','Were they glad of it?']},note:'The left side is easy to count. That is why people talk about it so much.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of a job you did this week. Ask: who was it for, and did it help them?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A school club boasts that it made 50 posters. What would Smith want to know?',options:['Did the posters help the people who saw them?','How long did the club spend making them?','Could the club make 100 posters next time?'],answer:0,why:'Making lots is not the point. The point is what the posters did for the people they were for.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE ANNUAL REVIEW',prompt:'An IT team reports it closed far more support tickets this year than last. What is the most useful question to ask next?',options:['How many staff did it take?','Do people\u2019s computers actually work better?','Can it close even more next year?'],answer:1,reveal:'Closing tickets is output. Working computers are the purpose. A team can close more tickets precisely because the same problems keep coming back.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Consumption is the sole end of production. Output matters only for what it does for the people it is for. Producers\u2019 interests deserve attention only so far as they serve that.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the two come apart',text:'Output is easy to count and sits inside the team\u2019s control. The effect on users is harder to measure and sits outside it. So the measurable one becomes the defence. It can be fully satisfied while the purpose goes unmet.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The crates pile up and the ship fills. The plate on the doorstep is still empty, and the plate is the point.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'SMITH\u2019S TARGET',text:'Smith states the principle as too obvious to argue. Then he shows policy doing the opposite. Under the mercantile system of his day, he says, the consumer\u2019s interest is almost always sacrificed to the producer\u2019s. The tools are import duties and export bounties, which make goods dearer at home.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time a team defends its budget by its output, ask what changed for the people the output was for.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A council boasts that it resurfaced more roads than ever this year. Which question applies Smith\u2019s standard?',options:['Are journeys safer or quicker for road users now?','How did the council finance all the extra work?','Which contractor did most of the resurfacing?','Will next year\u2019s total be higher still?'],answer:0,why:'The roads exist for the people who use them. Output answers a producer\u2019s question. Smith asks the consumer\u2019s.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'BOOK IV, CHAPTER 8',text:'Near the end of his attack on the mercantile system, Smith states a principle he calls too self-evident to need proof. The only purpose of producing anything is that someone consumes it, and producers\u2019 interests deserve attention only so far as they advance consumers\u2019.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Production is a means; consumption is the end. Judge any producer by what its output does for the people it is for, because output alone can be fully achieved while that purpose goes unmet.'},{kind:'term',word:'Bounty',meaning:'In Smith\u2019s day, a government payment to producers for making or exporting a good. He argued that consumers paid for bounties twice: through the taxes that funded them and through higher prices at home.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The ship is the producer\u2019s measure; the plate is the consumer\u2019s. Smith thought policy kept looking at the ship.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'WHERE A MEASURE SITS',low:'Pure output: units made, hours worked',high:'Pure effect: what changed for users',note:'Good measures sit further right, even though the right end is harder to count. The left end can be maximised while the right stays empty.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The principle can be pushed too far. Work matters to the people who do it, as Smith himself recognised when he worried about factory labour dulling minds. And what consumers want this minute is not always their interest. Use the test to expose output defending itself, not to declare that producers\u2019 lives do not count.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A university department defends its budget by the number of papers it publishes. What is the Smithian challenge?',options:['What did those papers do for the students and readers they were for?','Could the department publish more papers on the same budget?','Are the department\u2019s staff working long enough hours?','Is the department publishing more papers than its rivals?'],answer:0,why:'Paper counts answer a producer\u2019s question. Smith\u2019s standard asks what the output did for those it was meant to serve.'}]}}}},3033,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'what-a-study-shows_1':{pictureCaption:'A megaphone aimed at a vast hall with four seats taken, each by the same jacket. A loud claim from a few people, all alike.',basics:{title:'How many people, and who?',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE CLASS SURVEY',text:'Zara asks five friends from her football team which sport is best. All five say football. So she tells everyone that every kid in the school loves football best.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'What is wrong with Zara\u2019s claim?',options:['She asked too few people','She asked only football players','Both of those'],answer:2,reveal:'Five people is very few. And they were all from the football team, so of course they like football. To learn about the whole school, she needs lots of kids, and all sorts.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Before you believe a study, ask two questions. How many people were in it? And were they like the people the claim is about?'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why small groups trick you',text:'Flip a coin four times. Nearly one time in three, you get three or four heads. That looks like a pattern, but it is just luck. With small groups, luck makes big, exciting results all the time.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A loud megaphone on the stage, and a huge hall with only four seats taken. All four have the same jacket. Too few people, and all the same sort.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you hear that a study found something, ask: how many people? Who were they? If nobody can say, be careful.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A study of 12 kids at one sports camp finds that bananas make you run faster. What should you think?',options:['Interesting, but too few kids, all from one camp','It must be true, because scientists did it','Bananas must be bad for running'],answer:0,why:'Twelve kids is a tiny group, and sports-camp kids are not like everyone. It is a reason to check with a bigger study.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE HEADLINE',text:'A headline says a simple morning habit makes people much more focused. Deep in the article you find the study behind it. It tested forty undergraduates at one university, for one afternoon.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Sample size and composition decide what a study can support. Both are routinely left out of the reporting. A striking result from forty undergraduates is a reason to run a bigger study, not to change your behaviour.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why small samples mislead',text:'Small samples give unstable estimates. By chance alone some will show large effects. Those are the ones that look striking, get published and get written about. An effect found in thirty people may not survive three hundred.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW A FLUKE BECOMES NEWS',nodes:['Many small studies are run','Chance gives a few big effects','Those few get published','Then they get written about'],note:'No step is dishonest. Together they tilt what you read towards flukes.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Who were they?',text:'Composition matters as much. A result from undergraduates, from one country or from patients may not transfer. And psychology has a long history of drawing broad claims from a narrow slice of humanity. Ask whether the people studied are like the people the claim is about.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A megaphone pointed at a near-empty hall: four seats taken, each by the same jacket. The claim is loud. The sample is small and all of one kind.'},{kind:'try',text:'Find the sample size before you read the conclusion. If the article does not say, that is an answer.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A study of 2,000 surgical patients finds a supplement speeds their recovery. A healthy friend wants to take it for more energy. What is the main problem?',options:['The people studied are not like your friend','The sample is far too small to trust','Supplements can never be studied properly','Large samples always exaggerate effects'],answer:0,why:'Two thousand is a decent sample. The issue is composition. A result from surgical patients may not transfer to healthy people looking for energy.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'A CHECKLIST FOR HEADLINES',text:'This piece starts from a plain observation: most people meet research through a headline, usually written by someone who did not read the paper. Its answer is a short checklist of questions to ask of any finding. The first is the cheapest: how many people, and who were they?'},{kind:'idea',text:'A sample limits what a study can support twice over: by its size, because small samples give unstable estimates, and by its composition, because a result transfers only to people who resemble those studied.'},{kind:'term',word:'Publication bias',meaning:'The tendency for striking or positive results to be published more readily than dull or null ones, so that the published record overstates effects.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why small and striking go together',text:'Small samples give estimates that swing widely, so some land far from the truth by chance. The large, striking ones are the ones written up, so the literature leans towards effects that looked big in small samples and may not survive three hundred people.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO SEPARATE QUESTIONS',left:{title:'How many?',items:['Decides how stable the estimate is','Thirty is fragile, three hundred less so','Fixed by a bigger study']},right:{title:'Who?',items:['Decides whom the result applies to','Students, one country, patients','Not fixed by size alone']}},{kind:'picture',caption:'Both limits in one hall: four seats taken out of hundreds, and all four by the same jacket. The megaphone is the headline, louder than either allows.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Small is not the same as wrong. Some small studies are careful, and some effects are large enough to show in few people; one small study is weak evidence, not none. Nor does size cure composition. A vast sample of the wrong people still answers the wrong question.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'WEIGH IT',prompt:'Which finding gives a working adult the strongest grounds to change what they do?',options:['A modest effect in several thousand working adults','A dramatic effect in forty undergraduates','A dramatic effect in a million hospital patients','A modest effect in forty working adults'],answer:0,why:'Only the large study of working adults gets both questions right: enough people for a stable estimate, and people like the reader. Drama is no substitute for either.'}]}},'what-a-study-shows_2':{pictureCaption:'A hand drawing lots from a box to decide which plot each plant goes in. Chance, not the plants, decides who gets what.',bridge:'Idea 1 asked who was studied. This asks how they ended up in each group, which decides whether a study can show cause at all.',basics:{title:'Did people pick their own group?',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE CHESS CLUB',prompt:'Kids in the chess club get better maths marks than other kids. Does chess make you better at maths?',options:['Yes, chess must cause it','Maybe, but we can\u2019t tell yet'],answer:1,reveal:'The kids chose to join. Kids who like puzzles may pick chess, and also be good at maths. The club and the marks could both come from liking puzzles.'},{kind:'idea',text:'If people pick their own group, you can\u2019t tell what caused the difference. To find out, the groups must be picked by chance, like drawing names from a hat.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A hand draws slips from a box to decide where each plant goes. The plants don\u2019t choose. Chance does.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why a hat works',text:'Put every kid\u2019s name in a hat. Half get chess lessons and half don\u2019t. Now puzzle lovers end up in both groups. If the chess group then does better at maths, chess is the likely reason.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO KINDS OF STUDY',left:{title:'People choose',items:['Kids join the club if they want','Lots of reasons mixed together','Can\u2019t show the cause']},right:{title:'Chance chooses',items:['Names drawn from a hat','Groups alike apart from chess','Can show the cause']}},{kind:'question',prompt:'Kids who walk to school are fitter than kids who get a lift. Which study could show if walking makes kids fitter?',options:['Pick by chance who walks and who gets a lift','Ask the walkers and the others how fit they feel','Count how many fit kids walk to school each day'],answer:0,why:'Kids who walk might be sportier to start with. Picking by chance makes the groups alike, so walking is the main difference.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'A FAMILIAR KIND OF FINDING',text:'Suppose a report finds that people who eat a certain breakfast cereal live longer. Nobody was told what to eat. The cereal eaters chose it. People who choose it may differ from others in money, exercise, smoking and health before they ever start.'},{kind:'idea',text:'If people chose their own group, a study cannot tell the thing being studied apart from whatever made them choose. Observational work can suggest causes. It cannot prove them.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Randomised trial',items:['The researcher assigns the condition','Groups differ only by chance','Supports a causal claim']},right:{title:'Observational study',items:['People select themselves','Groups differ in why they chose','Supports an association']},note:'Who decided who got what? That one question tells you which column you are in.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why diet findings flip',text:'Observational findings about diet, exercise and habits are overturned so often for one reason. People who do the healthy thing differ from those who don\u2019t, and not at random. Those differences also affect the outcome. The habit gets the credit that belongs to the person.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A hand drawing lots to assign each plant to a plot. When chance decides, nothing about the plant can steer where it goes.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'A big enough, careful enough observational study can prove cause.',reality:'Size and care reduce some errors, not this one. If the people in it decided who got what, the study can show association. It cannot show cause, however large or careful it is.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before reading any conclusion about cause, ask: who decided who got what? If the answer is the participants, read the claim as association.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'People who meditate daily report less stress. Which design would test whether meditation causes the drop?',options:['Randomly assign people to meditate or not, then compare stress','Survey thousands more daily meditators about their stress','Compare meditators with non-meditators of the same age','Ask meditators whether they felt calmer after starting'],answer:0,why:'Matching on age removes one difference but not the others that led people to meditate. Only assignment by chance separates the practice from the person.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'THE STRUCTURAL QUESTION',word:'Confounder',meaning:'A factor that influences both who ends up in a group and the outcome being measured, so it produces a difference that the study then credits to the group.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Whether anything was assigned is the most important structural question about a study. Random assignment leaves groups differing only by chance, which supports a causal claim. Self-selection leaves any difference open to whatever drove the choice.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why randomisation works',text:'When the researcher assigns the condition by chance, everything else that might influence the outcome, including things nobody thought to measure, is spread across the groups by chance too. That is what lets a difference in outcome be credited to the condition.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why observation cannot',text:'In an observational study people select themselves, and their reasons are bound up with health, money, temperament and habit. Researchers can adjust for the confounders they measure, not for the ones they miss. So no amount of size or care turns association into cause.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW A HEADLINE CONVERTS IT',nodes:['People choose a habit','The habit tracks better outcomes','The study reports an association','The headline reports a cause'],note:'The last step is where the error enters, and the piece says it happens constantly.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Drawing lots to assign each plant to a plot. The point of the lot is that nothing about the plant can influence where it goes.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Observational work is valuable. It is often the only ethical or practical option, and it suggests causes worth testing. Randomisation has its own costs: trials are often small, short or run on unusual volunteers, so idea 1\u2019s questions still apply. Assignment answers whether something works, not for whom.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE OVERREACH',prompt:'Suppose a careful study follows 500,000 people for twenty years and finds that those who take cold showers take fewer sick days. What does the design support?',options:['An association, since people chose their own showers','A cause, since the sample is so very large','A cause, since it ran for twenty years','Nothing, since observational studies are worthless'],answer:0,why:'Neither size nor duration changes who decided. The finding is a real association, worth testing in a trial, and not yet a cause.'}]}},'what-a-study-shows_3':{pictureCaption:'A balance with one pan missing. Whatever goes in the other pan, there is nothing to weigh it against.',bridge:'Idea 2 asked how the groups were formed. This asks what the treatment group was set against, which decides what its win is worth.',basics:{title:'Better than what?',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE SPELLING APP',text:'An advert for a new spelling app says kids who used it for a term got 10 more words right on their test. It sounds great. But one question is missing.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A result only means something next to a comparison. Better than what? Without that answer, you can\u2019t tell if the thing worked.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What were they compared with?',text:'Kids learn spellings over a term anyway, app or no app. Maybe kids with no app also got 10 more right. Then the app did nothing extra. You need a group without the app to know.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A weighing scale with one pan missing. You can put things in the other pan, but there is nothing to weigh them against.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'BEATING WHAT?',left:{title:'Not much of a win',items:['Better than doing nothing','Better than a bad way']},right:{title:'A real win',items:['Better than the usual way']},note:'A new way only matters if it beats the way you already have.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time an advert says something works, ask one question: compared with what?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A coach says a new drill made the team score more goals this season. What should you ask first?',options:['Did teams without the drill score more too?','How long does the drill take each week?','Did the players enjoy doing the drill?'],answer:0,why:'Maybe every team scored more this season. Without that comparison, you can\u2019t tell what the drill did.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THREE TRIALS',prompt:'Three trials of a new painkiller all report success. Which result is the strongest?',options:['It beat a sugar pill','It beat the standard painkiller','It beat a half dose of an older drug'],answer:1,reveal:'Beating the current standard is the strong result. Beating a sugar pill shows it does something, but it may still lose to what doctors already use. Beating a rival that was weakened on purpose means close to nothing.'},{kind:'idea',text:'An effect only exists relative to a comparison. A treatment that beats nothing may lose to the usual alternative. And a trial with no control group tells you what happened, not what the treatment did.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'WHAT A WIN IS WORTH',low:'Beat a deliberately weak alternative',high:'Beat the current standard',note:'Beating nothing sits near the low end. The comparison decides how much the claim is worth.'},{kind:'explain',title:'No control, no claim',text:'Without a control group you see that people improved, not why. Many complaints ease with time, and people may feel better simply for being treated. A control condition shows what would have happened anyway.'},{kind:'explain',title:'A control must be credible',text:'A placebo works as a control only if participants cannot tell it from the real thing. If they can, the comparison collapses. One group knows it is being treated, and the other knows it is not.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A balance with one pan removed. Whatever sits in the other pan, there is nothing to weigh it against.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A study reports that a new reading scheme raised pupils\u2019 scores. The comparison group used no scheme at all, though most schools already use one. What can you conclude?',options:['It beats nothing, but may not beat what schools already use','It is the best reading scheme available to schools','It does not work, since the comparison was unfair','Nothing, since education research is unreliable'],answer:0,why:'Beating no scheme is a weak result. The useful comparison is against the usual alternative. This study did not test that.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'A TECHNICALLY TRUE HEADLINE',text:'Imagine a headline: new drug cuts symptoms. True. The trial compared it with an older drug at a dose nobody uses in practice, and the summary does not mention the dose. Every word is accurate, and the comparison is one nobody would have chosen if asked in advance.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The comparison determines the claim. Beating nothing is weak, beating the current standard is strong, and beating a deliberately weak alternative is close to meaningless. So the first question about any effect is: compared with what?'},{kind:'term',word:'Comparator',meaning:'Whatever a treatment is tested against in a trial: nothing, a placebo, or another treatment. The choice of comparator fixes what the result can claim.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Credible controls',text:'A trial needs a control condition, and the control must be credible. A placebo that participants can identify is not a control, because the groups now differ in what they believe as well as in what they received.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where motivated design hides',text:'Choosing a favourable comparator is legal, common and rarely reported in the summary. Nothing is falsified. The design simply sets the bar where the treatment can clear it, and the abstract reports the clearance.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A balance with one pan gone, the beam left with nothing to weigh against. An effect without a comparison looks like this.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Sometimes beating nothing is exactly the right test: where no treatment exists, a placebo comparison is the honest one. The fault is not a weak comparator as such but one chosen to flatter and left out of the summary. The test is whether you would have picked it yourself, asked in advance.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'FIND THE WEAK LINK',prompt:'Which of these summaries most needs a follow-up question about its comparison?',options:['A new app beat an older app its makers withdrew years ago','A new app beat the standard therapy in a randomised trial','A new drug beat a placebo for a condition with no treatment','A new drug matched the standard drug at a lower cost'],answer:0,why:'Beating a superseded alternative picked by the makers is close to meaningless. The others are set against the standard, or against the only honest option.'}]}},'what-a-study-shows_4':{pictureCaption:'A magnifying glass held over a ruler makes a tiny gap look large. The gap is real. The size is in the glass.',bridge:'Ideas 1 to 3 asked whether an effect is real. This one asks the question that comes after: if it is real, how big is it?',basics:{title:'Real is not the same as big',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'A BIG WORD',belief:'If scientists call a result significant, it must be a big deal.',reality:'In science, significant means it is probably not just luck. It does not mean big. A real effect can be tiny.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE SUPER TRAINERS',text:'Imagine a study finds that new trainers make kids run faster, and the result is significant. How much faster? One tenth of a second over a long cross-country run. It is real. But nobody would notice it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Always ask how big a result is, in things you can picture: minutes, marks, or kids out of a hundred. A real result can be too small to matter.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A magnifying glass over a ruler makes a tiny gap look huge. Some results are like that: real, but very small.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Watch out for doubles',text:'You read that something doubles your chance of winning a prize. Exciting! But if your chance was 1 in 100, double is 2 in 100. That is still not very likely.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you hear a result, turn it into something you could notice in your own week. How many minutes? How many marks?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Suppose a study says a snack doubles your chance of catching a cold. Your chance was 1 in 1,000. What is it now?',options:['2 in 1,000: still very small','500 in 1,000: very likely','Twice as likely, so almost certain'],answer:0,why:'Double 1 in 1,000 is 2 in 1,000. The word doubles sounds big, but the real change is tiny.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE PRESS RELEASE',text:'A press release says a common food doubles the risk of a rare illness, and the finding is statistically significant. Both may be true. If the risk goes from one in a million to two in a million, they are also nearly useless for deciding what to eat.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Statistically significant means unlikely to be chance, not large. Ask what the effect is in real terms, because a genuine effect can be far too small to be worth acting on.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why significant is not big',text:'Significance says an effect is probably not zero. It says nothing about size, and with a large enough sample even a trivially small effect comes out significant. The word answers *is it there?*, not *does it matter?*'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'ONE RESULT, TWO WAYS',left:{title:'Relative',items:['Risk doubles','Sounds alarming','Chosen by the press release']},right:{title:'Absolute',items:['One extra case per million people','Sounds like what it is','What you need to decide']},note:'Both are true. Only one of them is informative.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A magnifying glass over a ruler makes a tiny gap look large. The gap is real. The size is in the glass.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Translate it',steps:['Find the effect in the study, not the headline.','Turn it into minutes, pounds, percentage points or people per hundred.','Ask whether you would notice that in your own week.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A large study finds a sleep app adds a statistically significant 4 minutes of sleep a night. What is the best reading?',options:['Probably real, and probably too small to be worth much','A big effect, since it was statistically significant','Probably chance, since 4 minutes is so small','Meaningless, since apps cannot change sleep'],answer:0,why:'Significance says the effect is probably not zero. Four minutes says how big it is, and that is the number that decides whether to bother.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'ONE RESULT, THREE WAYS',prompt:'A risk rises from 1 in a million to 2 in a million. Which description is a press release most likely to use?',options:['Risk doubles','One extra case per million','Risk up by 0.0001 percentage points'],answer:0,reveal:'All three are true. The relative figure is the one most often chosen, and the only one that sounds alarming. The absolute figures tell you whether to care.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Effect size, not significance, tells you whether a real finding matters. Significance says an effect is probably not zero. Size says how big it is, and only size can be weighed against cost and effort.'},{kind:'term',word:'Statistical significance',meaning:'A result is called significant when it would be unlikely to arise if there were no real effect. It speaks to whether an effect is there, not to how large it is.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why big samples flag small effects',text:'The more people a study includes, the more precisely it can detect a difference, so a big enough sample will flag even a trivial effect as significant. Precision about a tiny effect is still a tiny effect. That is how the word significant comes to stand in for the number that matters.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A magnifying glass over a ruler, making a genuinely tiny gap look large. Significance can do the same to an effect.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Relative and absolute',text:'Relative figures, such as doubles or halves, describe change in proportion to the starting risk. Absolute figures describe the change itself: percentage points, or people per hundred. With a rare outcome, a dramatic relative change can be an absolute change too small to notice.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Small is not always negligible. A tiny effect spread across a whole population can add up to many people, which is why a public-health body may care about what an individual can ignore. And an absolute figure without its baseline misleads too. The demand is for both numbers, in units you understand.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'READ THE RELEASE',prompt:'A press release says a supplement halves the risk of a condition, a statistically significant finding. What is the most useful thing to find next?',options:['The absolute risk with and without the supplement','Whether the result was very highly significant','How many times the study has been cited','Whether halving counts as a large effect'],answer:0,why:'Halving a risk of one in a hundred thousand and halving a risk of one in four are different worlds. Only the absolute figures tell you which this is.'}]}},'what-a-study-shows_5':{pictureCaption:'The same experiment on five benches. One flask turned; four did not. A single result is where checking starts, not where it ends.',bridge:'The first four questions are asked of one study. This one asks whether there is more than one.',basics:{title:'Has anyone else found it?',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE LUCKY PENCIL',text:'Your friend used a new pencil in a test and got top marks. Now she says it is a lucky pencil. She wants to use it in every test from now on.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'How could you find out if the pencil really helps?',options:['Believe her, since she got top marks','Try it in lots of tests, and with other kids too'],answer:1,reveal:'One good test could be luck. If the pencil works again and again, for different kids, that would be worth noticing. If it doesn\u2019t, it was luck.'},{kind:'idea',text:'One result on its own is a maybe, not a fact. Trust a finding more when other people test it again and find the same thing.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Five tables, the same test on each. Only one bottle turned orange. The other four found nothing. One result is just a start.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why famous results can be wrong',text:'Scientists have gone back and redone some well-known studies. Quite a few did not work the second time. Usually nobody cheated. Exciting results just get noticed more, even when they were luck.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The best kind of check',text:'The best check is done by people who would be happy to find nothing. If even they find it, you can start to trust it.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'One study finds that a brain game makes kids cleverer. What is the best thing to say?',options:['Interesting. Has anyone else found the same?','It\u2019s true, because it was a real study','It\u2019s false, because games can\u2019t help you'],answer:0,why:'One study is a maybe. If other people test it and find the same thing, then it is worth believing.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'THE REPLICATION CRISIS',text:'Researchers set out to repeat published findings in a planned, careful way, famous ones included. A large share did not hold up. The failure rate was high. So a single study, on its own, should shift your beliefs only a little.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A single result is a hypothesis with evidence attached. The finding to trust is the one repeated by people who would have been pleased to find nothing.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Structure, not fraud',text:'The reasons are mostly built into the system. Journals favour new, positive findings. Flexible analysis offers many paths to a significant result. Null results are hard to publish. Each pushes the record towards striking results that may not hold.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW A RESULT EARNS TRUST',nodes:['One study finds it','Others try to repeat it','Sceptics find it too','Now it is a finding'],note:'Many famous results fell at the second step. A good number have passed.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Five benches, one experiment. One flask turned and four did not. Which result made the news?'},{kind:'myth',belief:'A study that is published, well written and by someone famous can be trusted.',reality:'None of that is replication. Gripping, well-written papers by people you have heard of fail to hold up like any others. Weight meta-analyses and pre-registered replications far above any single paper.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A widely shared study found that a study method sharply raises exam scores. Two pre-registered replications by independent teams found no effect. How should you weigh it?',options:['Weight the replications far more heavily than the original','Trust the original, since it was published first','Split the difference and assume a moderate effect','Ignore all three, since studies contradict each other'],answer:0,why:'Pre-registered replications by teams with no stake in the result are the stronger evidence. The original is now a hypothesis that has twice failed to hold up.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'FINDING OR RESULT?',belief:'A peer-reviewed study is an established finding.',reality:'A single study is a result. Replication is what turns a result into a finding, and many published results, famous ones among them, have not survived it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Treat a lone study as a hypothesis with evidence attached, and let it shift your beliefs only slightly. Keep real confidence for findings replicated by people who would have been pleased to find nothing.'},{kind:'term',word:'Pre-registration',meaning:'Recording a study\u2019s hypotheses and analysis plan publicly before the data are collected, so that the analysis is much harder to bend towards significance afterwards.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why results fail to reproduce',text:'The piece\u2019s diagnosis is structural, not fraudulent. Publication favours novel positive findings. Analytic flexibility permits many paths to significance: which outcome, which subgroup, which exclusions. Null results are hard to publish. Each is defensible alone. Together they tilt the record towards results that will not hold.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHERE TO PUT THE WEIGHT',left:{title:'Weight lightly',items:['One striking paper','A famous name on it','Good writing, a great story']},right:{title:'Weight heavily',items:['Meta-analyses','Pre-registered replications','Teams with no stake in it']},note:'A meta-analysis pools the studies of one question, so no single paper decides it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One experiment on five benches by five pairs of hands. One flask turned; four did not. The lone result is where checking begins.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Replication is no magic seal. A meta-analysis of biased studies inherits their bias, and a failed replication can itself be too small or done differently. Waiting is also a luxury: some decisions cannot wait, and then one good study is the best evidence there is. The rule is about how far to move, not whether.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'WEIGH THE EVIDENCE',prompt:'Which of these should move your beliefs the most?',options:['A pre-registered replication by a sceptical team, confirming the effect','A celebrated original study from a famous lab','Three small studies from the same lab, all positive','A widely shared, beautifully written single paper'],answer:0,why:'Replication by people who would have been pleased to find nothing is the test the piece trusts. Fame, polish and repeats from one source are not independent checks.'}]}},'what-a-study-shows_6':{pictureCaption:'The stall has its own scales, but the bag goes onto a second, public scale to be weighed again. Not refused, not taken on trust: checked.',bridge:'The first five questions are about the evidence itself. This one asks who wanted a particular answer, which tells you how hard to press the other five.',basics:{title:'Who wants you to believe it?',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE CRISP TEST',text:'A crisp company runs a taste test. It says kids like its crisps best. But the company wants you to buy its crisps. Does that mean the test is wrong?'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'Should you throw the result away?',options:['Yes. They paid for it, so it is lies','No, but check it more carefully'],answer:1,reveal:'Being paid for does not make a result false. But it is a reason to look closely. How did they test? Who did they ask? What did they compare with?'},{kind:'idea',text:'When someone gains if you believe a result, look harder at it. Don\u2019t just swallow it, and don\u2019t just throw it out.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How a test can tilt',text:'Nobody has to lie. The company could pick a rival crisp that nobody likes. It could ask kids at its own party. It could share only the tests that went well. Each choice looks small.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The seller has a scale. You can still check the apples on a different scale. You don\u2019t throw the apples away. You weigh them again.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'TWO MISTAKES',low:'Believe it all',high:'Throw it all out',note:'Stand in the middle. Check it extra carefully.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time an advert says tests prove something, ask: who paid for the test? Then ask how it was done.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A toothpaste company\u2019s study says its toothpaste is the best. What is the smart thing to do?',options:['Check what they compared it with, and how','Believe it, since a study is a study','Ignore it, since they must be lying'],answer:0,why:'They might be right. But they gain if you believe it, so it is worth checking how the test was done.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'FOLLOW THE MONEY?',belief:'If a company paid for the study, you can ignore the result.',reality:'Funding and interest do not make a result wrong, and treating them as automatic disqualification is its own error. What they tell you is how hard to look.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Asking who benefits is not cynicism. A conflict of interest tells you which questions to ask first and how much weight one study can bear. It sets how hard you look, not whether the finding is false.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How funding shapes results',text:'Funding shapes the literature before any paper is written, by deciding what gets studied. It also tracks outcomes: industry-funded trials report favourable results more often than independently funded ones on the same questions.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Without anyone faking anything',text:'This does not mean funded research is fabricated. The effect works through ordinary choices: the design, the comparator, how the outcome is defined, and what goes unpublished. Each is defensible on its own.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The stallholder\u2019s scales may well be honest. The bag still goes onto the public scale, because the seller gains from the reading. Interest sets how hard you check.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Use it as a prompt',steps:['Find the funding statement, usually at the end of the paper.','If the finding favours the funder, press the earlier questions harder.','Who was studied? Was anything assigned? Compared with what? How big?'],note:'Interest decides how hard to look. The design decides what is true.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A trial funded by a drug\u2019s maker finds that the drug works. What is the sensible response?',options:['Check its comparator and outcomes more closely than usual','Dismiss it, since the funder wanted that result','Accept it, since funding cannot change the data','Wait until the maker funds a second trial'],answer:0,why:'The funding is a reason for scrutiny, not a verdict. The places to look are the design choices where interest usually operates.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHICH STUDIES EXIST',text:'The last question on the piece\u2019s checklist is about the literature as much as the paper. Funding decides what gets investigated in the first place, so before you read any single study, interest has already shaped which studies there are to read.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A conflict of interest is a prior, not a verdict. A finding that favours its funder deserves closer scrutiny of the design questions, while funding alone is no reason to reject it.'},{kind:'term',word:'Conflict of interest',meaning:'A situation in which those producing or paying for research stand to gain from a particular result. It is usually declared at the end of the paper, where few readers look.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the funding evidence shows',text:'Industry-funded trials report favourable results more often than independently funded trials on the same questions. The piece is careful about what that means. It is not evidence of fabrication. The effect runs through design, comparator selection, outcome definition and non-publication, each defensible on its own.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHERE INTEREST ENTERS',nodes:['Choose what to study','Choose the comparator','Define the outcome','Decide what to publish'],note:'Every step is legitimate. Watch the pattern across them: comparators were idea 3, publication idea 5.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO OPPOSITE ERRORS',left:{title:'Ignoring interest',items:['Funding treated as irrelevant','A favourable design goes unexamined']},right:{title:'Disqualifying on interest',items:['Funding treated as proof of error','Sound work dismissed unread']},note:'The piece rejects both. Interest sets the scrutiny. The design settles the question.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Neither error: the bag is not refused and not taken on trust. It is weighed again on a scale nobody at the stall owns. Interest sets the scrutiny; the second scale settles it.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Interest is not only financial. Researchers have careers, reputations and favourite theories, and independent funders have agendas too, so independent is not the same as disinterested. And the rule can be turned into a weapon: citing a funder to dismiss findings you dislike is the automatic disqualification the piece warns against.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE ERROR',prompt:'Which response to an industry-funded study commits the error the piece warns against?',options:['Rejecting it unread because of who paid for it','Checking its comparator against current practice','Looking for its registered outcomes and unpublished trials','Giving it less weight until it is independently replicated'],answer:0,why:'Automatic disqualification is its own error. The other three use the funding as the piece intends: as a reason to look harder at the design.'}]}}}},3034,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'what-changed-my-mind_1':{pictureCaption:'A seed pressed into bare ground. Nothing shows yet, and nothing will for weeks. That is what a good argument looks like at the time.',basics:{title:'Minds change later, not in the moment',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE BEST FILM EVER',text:'You and your friend argue about a film. You think it is great. She thinks it is boring. You both dig in. Nobody gives way. You go home sure you were right.'},{kind:'explain',kicker:'A WEEK LATER',text:'Then one night, on your own, you think about what she said. One of her points was quite good. Maybe the middle part was slow. You do not text her. You just think a bit differently now.'},{kind:'idea',text:'People hardly ever change their mind in the middle of an argument. If they change it at all, it happens later, on their own, when no one is watching.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why not right away?',text:'Giving way while someone is arguing with you feels like losing. You would lose the point and the fight at the same time. So people hold on, and do their changing in private.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A seed goes into the ground. Nothing shows. That does not mean nothing happened. It just has not come up yet.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you disagree with a friend, say your best point once, kindly. Then let it go. Do not wait for them to say you are right.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You tell your brother why his plan is a bad idea. He says, \u201cNo, I\u2019m still doing it.\u201d What does this idea tell you?',options:['He might still think about it later, on his own','Your point was bad, or he would have agreed','Keep arguing until he says you are right'],answer:0,why:'People rarely give way while you are still arguing. The thinking often comes later, when nobody is watching.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'AN HOUR AT DINNER',prompt:'You argue with a relative for an hour. Your points are good. They concede nothing. What does that tell you?',options:['Your points did not land','Nothing yet, either way','They are closed to reason'],answer:1,reveal:'A concession in the moment is rare even when a point lands. If anything shifts, it tends to shift weeks or months later, in private. Their face at the table is no evidence either way.'},{kind:'idea',text:'People rarely change their mind in the conversation that challenges it. The update, when it comes, comes later and alone. So a conversation is for leaving something behind, not for winning a concession.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the room is the wrong place',text:'Changing position in front of the person arguing against you costs twice. You give up the point, and you give up the exchange: you are seen to lose. Alone, only the first cost is left. So people defend at the table and reconsider in the car.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW PUSHING BACKFIRES',nodes:['You expect a concession','None comes','You push harder','They defend harder','The private rethink grows less likely'],note:'Looking for a visible win makes the invisible one less likely.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A seed goes into bare ground and nothing visible happens. In a disagreement you are planting, not harvesting.'},{kind:'try',text:'In your next disagreement, make your best point once and clearly, then let the subject drop. Leave them something worth thinking about on the way home.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A colleague firmly rejects your proposal in a meeting. On this idea, what is the most useful next step?',options:['Leave your strongest point with them and stop pressing for now','Raise it again before the meeting ends, more forcefully','Drop the proposal, since the meeting showed it had failed','Ask the room for a vote so the matter is settled today'],answer:0,why:'A firm no in the room says little about what happens later. Pressing raises the cost of changing; leaving the point gives the rethink room.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHERE THE ESSAY STARTS',title:'The shape of a change of mind',text:'This short essay asks how people come to abandon a belief. Its first observation is about timing. A change of mind tends to have one shape: the position is defended at the time, then shifts weeks or months later, alone. The conversation mattered. It just did not produce the change while anyone was watching.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Treat the absence of a concession as the default, not as failure. The update happens later and privately, so a conversation succeeds by leaving something to think about, not by winning.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The mechanism',text:'Conceding in front of your opponent concedes two things: the point, and the exchange. The second is a social loss with a witness, and it is the expensive one. Take away the opponent and only the question is left. That is why the update, when it comes, is private.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO AIMS FOR ONE CONVERSATION',left:{title:'Arguing to harvest',items:['Success is a visible yes','Silence reads as failure','Escalates to force the concession']},right:{title:'Arguing to plant',items:['Success is a point that stays with them','Silence is the expected result','Stops once the point is made']},note:'The essay\u2019s claim is that the first aim tends to destroy the second: escalation also blocks the private update.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Bare soil, one seed, nothing to show. The essay asks you to judge a conversation by what it planted.'},{kind:'limits',text:'This is a tendency, not a law. Some people do change their minds on the spot, especially when little is at stake for them. And the planting frame has its own trap: since no signal is expected, it can excuse never asking whether your point was any good. Silence is not proof you were right, and a later rethink may go against you.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of a belief you have dropped. Where were you when it actually went: in the argument, or somewhere afterwards?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'CORRECT THE MISREADING',prompt:'Someone says that if minds don\u2019t change during arguments, then arguing is pointless. What is the best reply from this essay?',options:['The argument can matter; it tends to work later, out of sight','True, so persuasion has to rely on pressure rather than talk','False, since a strong enough case wins a concession on the spot','True, because beliefs are fixed once a person has formed them'],answer:0,why:'The essay says the conversation mattered. What it rarely does is produce the change while anyone is watching.'}]}},'what-changed-my-mind_2':{pictureCaption:'Muddy boots, a winner\u2019s trophy, and a half-read book lying among them. Hard now to believe only dull people read.',bridge:'Idea 1 was about when minds change. This one is about what usually sets the change off: a person, more often than a point.',basics:{title:'It\u2019s who, not what',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'CHESS CLUB',prompt:'You think chess is dull and only dull kids play it. What is most likely to change your mind?',options:['A long list of reasons chess is good','Finding out your cool cousin loves it','Someone telling you that you are wrong'],answer:1,reveal:'A list is easy to wave away. But your cousin is not dull. You like her. Now *only dull kids play it* can\u2019t be true.'},{kind:'idea',text:'People often change their mind because of a person, not a reason. When someone you like and look up to thinks the other way, you can\u2019t tell yourself only silly people think that.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why a person works',text:'It is easy to ignore an idea if you think only silly or mean people believe it. You never really look at it. A person you look up to breaks that rule. Now you have to look.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'AT LUNCH',picture:!0,text:'Maya thinks reading is for boring kids. Then she sees Jake, the best striker on her team, reading every lunchtime. Nobody gave Maya a reason. Jake just made her old idea hard to keep.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Telling them',items:['Gives a reason','Easy to ignore','Can make them dig in']},right:{title:'Being someone they look up to',items:['Gives an example','Hard to ignore','Lets them look again']}},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of something you changed your mind about. Was there a person behind it? Who was it?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend says only show-offs join the school play. What is most likely to change his mind?',options:['Finding out his big brother, who he looks up to, loved it','A poster listing all the good things about the play','The whole class telling him he is wrong about it'],answer:0,why:'Someone he looks up to thinks the other way. Now he can\u2019t believe only show-offs join.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'NO ARGUMENT WAS MADE',text:'You have long assumed that people who teach their children at home are cranks. Then you learn that a colleague you admire does exactly that. They are sharp, funny and plainly sensible. Nobody has argued with you. Something has shifted anyway.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The trigger for changing a position is often a person rather than an argument. Someone you respect holding the other view makes it impossible to keep believing only fools believe it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the person actually does',text:'Most opposing views are never really considered. We file them as what stupid or spiteful people think. A respected person who holds the view breaks the filing. They do not supply a better argument. They make the view worth thinking about.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Minds change when someone finally hears the better argument.',reality:'Often the argument was available all along. What changed was who was holding it. That is why a shifted opinion so often has a relationship behind it rather than a new piece of evidence.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The star player\u2019s bench: boots, a trophy, and a book left open mid-chapter. Nobody made a case for reading. The owner just made the old view hard to keep.'},{kind:'keyline',text:'Being right, clearly and publicly, is a weak way to move someone. Being present and plainly not contemptible is a strong one.'},{kind:'try',text:'Name a view you dismiss. Then ask honestly: do I know anyone I respect who holds it? If not, that may be why you have never really examined it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You want a sceptical friend to take your view on a local issue seriously. Which approach fits the idea best?',options:['Keep being someone they respect who openly holds the view','Send them the most rigorous article you can find on it','Post your view publicly so they see how well you argue it','Avoid the subject entirely until they come round alone'],answer:0,why:'The essay\u2019s claim is that presence and respect move people more than being clearly, publicly right.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE USUAL ASSUMPTION',belief:'People change their minds once the evidence or argument becomes strong enough.',reality:'The essay locates the trigger elsewhere. People who have changed their mind can often name an individual rather than a point: someone they knew, trusted or liked who held the other view.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A position held by someone you respect cannot be dismissed as the province of fools or knaves. That, more than a better argument, is what opens it to serious thought.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The filter before the argument',text:'The main reason opposing views go unconsidered, the essay argues, is that they are sorted in advance as what stupid or malicious people believe. Arguments rarely get past that sorting, because they arrive as more of what such people say. A respected holder does get past it, because the sorting has nowhere to put them.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE FILTER BREAKS',nodes:['The other view = what fools think','Someone you respect holds it','The equation fails','The view gets examined'],note:'The person does not prove the view. They make it thinkable.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'No argument lies on this bench, only a book among a winner\u2019s boots and trophy. It proves nothing about reading. It breaks the filing that said only dull people read.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The uncomfortable corollary',text:'If this is right, contact can matter more than debate, and being clearly and publicly right is a weak intervention. The strong one is to be present, patient and evidently not contemptible, and to let the relationship do work the argument cannot.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Respect is a reason to take a view seriously, not a reason to believe it. The same mechanism carries bad ideas through admired people as easily as good ones. And it can stop halfway: if you hold a view only because someone you like holds it, the filter has broken but no examining has followed.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Which of your own views would survive learning that someone you deeply respect holds the opposite one?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A public health team wants sceptics to reconsider a safety measure. Which plan draws most directly on this idea?',options:['Ask respected local figures to explain why they support it','Publish the full data set alongside a clear summary','Run adverts mocking the most common sceptical claims','Repeat the official guidance more often and more loudly'],answer:0,why:'The mechanism is a respected holder of the other view. Data may help, mockery works against it, and volume is just being right more loudly.'}]}},'what-changed-my-mind_3':{pictureCaption:'Someone shoves hard at the door. Inside, a hand drives the bolt home. The push is what made them lock it.',bridge:'Idea 2 showed what opens a view up: respect. This is the reverse. Contempt shuts it, and can undo what an argument had gained.',basics:{title:'Calling someone stupid backfires',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'IN THE PLAYGROUND',belief:'If someone is wrong, telling them they are stupid will help them see it.',reality:'It does the opposite. Now they are not just sticking up for an idea. They are sticking up for themselves. And people do that very hard.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'WHALES',text:'Sam says a whale is a fish. Leo laughs. \u201cThat\u2019s so dumb. It\u2019s a mammal!\u201d The others laugh too. Sam goes red. \u201cIt *is* a fish,\u201d he says, louder.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Calling someone stupid does not change their mind. It makes them hold on tighter. If you want someone to change, make it easy for them.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it backfires',text:'Leo was right about the whale. But he turned a question about whales into a question about Sam. Is Sam dumb? Sam will fight that one hard. Giving way on the whale now feels like agreeing he is dumb. So he won\u2019t.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Someone pushes hard on the door. So the person inside slams the bolt shut. Pushing made it harder to get in.'},{kind:'explain',title:'And a crowd makes it worse',text:'When others are watching, giving way feels even worse. So a quiet word, just the two of you, works better than a laugh in front of everyone.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your little brother says bats are birds. Which way gives him the best chance to change his mind?',options:['Tell him kindly, on your own, what you know about bats','Laugh and tell the family what he just said','Call him silly until he says you are right'],answer:0,why:'Kind and private makes it easy to change. Laughing in front of others makes him dig in.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE REPLY THAT GOT LIKES',text:'You see a post repeating a claim you know is false. You reply with the facts, plus a line about how anyone could believe this. Your reply does well. The original poster digs in, and now points to you as proof of what they are up against.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Contempt does not just fail to persuade. It turns a question about what is true into a question about who you are, and people defend that far harder.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What contempt does to the question',text:'An attack on a belief that also says something about the believer changes the question. It was *is this true?* Now it is *am I the kind of person who falls for this?* People will reconsider the first. They defend the second.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW SCORN BACKFIRES',nodes:['A correct argument','Delivered with contempt','Now it is about who they are','They defend themselves, and the belief','And distrust the source next time'],note:'The scornful version of a correct argument can be worse than not making it at all.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why an audience makes it worse',text:'Public correction does especially badly. Witnesses raise the stakes for the person\u2019s sense of who they are, which is exactly the thing you need low. Backing down in private is a change of mind. Backing down in public is a defeat.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The harder the shove from outside, the firmer the bolt goes home inside. Force is what made them lock it.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before you correct someone, ask: could they agree with me without admitting they are a fool? If not, change the words, or the setting, first.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A friend shares a dubious health claim in a group chat. Which response is most likely to move them?',options:['A private message with the facts, and no hint of scorn','A reply in the group showing clearly why the claim is false','A joke in the group about people who fall for this','Silence, since nothing changes anyone\u2019s mind anyway'],answer:0,why:'Private and respectful keeps the stakes low. The group reply may be accurate, but the audience raises the cost of agreeing.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'THE MECHANISM, NAMED',word:'Identity protection',meaning:'Defending a belief because of what giving it up would seem to say about you. The question shifts from what is true to what kind of person you are.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Contempt converts a factual question into a question of loyalty and self. People do not abandon a position while it feels like abandoning themselves, so the scornful version of a correct argument can do more harm than silence.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The essay\u2019s claim',text:'Most people, the essay observes, can recall an encounter that hardened them. Being told they were stupid set them back rather than moving them, and the setback could undo whatever a good argument had already gained. It also added a reason to distrust the source.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'SENT AND RECEIVED',left:{title:'What the arguer thinks they sent',items:['The facts','A correction','Some urgency']},right:{title:'What arrives',items:['A verdict on the person','A loyalty test','A reason to distrust the source']},note:'The content can be entirely right and still entrench the belief it was aimed at.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why audiences matter',text:'Public correction raises the identity stakes, the one variable that needs to be low. Conceding before witnesses confirms the verdict in front of them. Hence the practical rule: if you want someone to move, first make it cheap for them to.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A shove from outside, a bolt driven home inside. Force producing the very resistance it meets.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Some views deserve no respect, and the claim is not that error must be flattered. It is narrower: scorn rarely moves the person scorned. Onlookers are a separate audience, and a public correction may fail with its target yet still inform them. And making it cheap to move must never mean pretending the facts are softer than they are.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HARD CASE',prompt:'A colleague\u2019s belief is plainly false and is causing harm at work. What does this idea recommend?',options:['Correct it firmly and in private, with a dignified way to agree','Let it stand, since any correction will only entrench it further','Correct it in the team meeting, so that everyone hears the facts','Soften the facts until they are easy for the colleague to accept'],answer:0,why:'The idea lowers the cost of moving, not the truth of the correction. Firmness is fine. Contempt and an audience are what backfire.'}]}},'what-changed-my-mind_4':{pictureCaption:'A high wall, and a small gate standing open in it. Nobody has to climb over in front of a crowd to get through.',bridge:'Idea 3 warned against raising the cost of changing. This one lowers it: give the person somewhere to go.',basics:{title:'Leave a way out',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'AFTER THE MATCH',text:'Ben said your team should play with five defenders, to be safe at the back. It went badly. You could tell him: *you were wrong.* Or you could say: *you were right that we needed to be safe. Five was just too many.*'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'Which one makes it easier for Ben to change his plan?',options:['\u201cYou were wrong.\u201d','\u201cYou were right about being safe. Five was too many.\u201d'],answer:1,reveal:'The second one lets Ben keep his good reason. He only has to drop the number. That is a small step. *You were wrong* asks him to take a big one.'},{kind:'idea',text:'People change their mind more easily when they don\u2019t have to say they were wrong. Give them a way to keep their good reasons and change just the answer.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A high wall with a small gate open in it. You don\u2019t have to climb over the top while everyone watches. You can walk through.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Three ways out',text:'Maybe they learned something new. Maybe things have changed since. Or maybe they were right about the part they cared about most. All three are real ways minds change. None of them means losing.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you disagree, find the part the other person got right. Say that first. Then talk about the rest.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend said a new game would be too hard for you. You love it. Which reply lets him change his mind most easily?',options:['\u201cYou were right it\u2019s hard. I just like hard games.\u201d','\u201cHa! You were totally wrong about that one.\u201d','\u201cSay you were wrong and I\u2019ll forgive you.\u201d'],answer:0,why:'\u201cYou were right it\u2019s hard\u201d lets him keep his reason: the game is hard. He only has to drop his guess about you.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'WHAT HONESTY SEEMS TO DEMAND',belief:'A real change of mind means admitting you were wrong all along.',reality:'Most beliefs change slowly, with the old position still defensible given what was known. People who change their minds generally need a route that does not require that admission.'},{kind:'idea',text:'What a person changing their mind needs is somewhere to go. That means a version of the new position that lets them keep their reasons and drop only the conclusion.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Three honest exits',steps:['New information: this was not available before.','New circumstances: the old view was reasonable then and is wrong now.','A reframing: you were right about the thing you actually cared about.'],note:'None of these is a trick. Each is an accurate account of how beliefs usually change.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE SIZE OF THE RISK',text:'A manager resisted remote working because it would hurt communication. Six months in, communication is mostly fine. One reply: *you were wrong.* Another: *you were right that communication was the risk. It turned out smaller than it looked.* The second is a step they can take.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why demanding the admission backfires',text:'Insist on a confession before you accept the change. You have now made changing cost more than keeping the belief. Choosing the cheaper option is not irrational. Demanding the admission is a reliable way to preserve the very belief you wanted gone.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The wall is high. The gate is open and to one side. You can come through without climbing over in front of anyone.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time someone starts to come round, say what they were right about before you say anything else.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A friend opposed a new cycle lane, fearing traffic jams. Now they admit the jams have been minor. Which reply supplies the exit?',options:['\u201cYour worry about traffic was fair. It just turned out smaller.\u201d','\u201cSo you finally admit you were wrong about the whole thing?\u201d','\u201cGood. I did tell you so at the time, if you remember.\u201d','\u201cLet\u2019s just forget about who was right and who was wrong.\u201d'],answer:0,why:'The right reply lets them keep their reason, the worry about traffic, and drop only the conclusion. The others make changing cost more.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO GREET A CHANGE',left:{title:'Closing the exit',items:['Admit you were wrong first','The change costs more than the belief','The belief survives']},right:{title:'Supplying the exit',items:['Keep your reasons, drop the conclusion','The change is a small step','The belief can go']},note:'The essay\u2019s claim is that the left column reliably preserves what it attacks.'},{kind:'idea',text:'People who change their minds generally need a route that does not require admitting they were wrong. Supply the exit rather than closing it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The three routes',text:'The essay names familiar forms. New information that was not available before. A change in circumstances, under which the old view was reasonable then and is wrong now. Or a reframing, under which they were right about the thing they actually cared about: right about the risk, wrong about its size.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why these are not dishonest',text:'Each is an accurate description of how beliefs usually change: gradually, with the earlier position still defensible on what was known. Demanding a confession misdescribes the change. It treats a reasonable update as the unmasking of a fool, and so prices it out of reach.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The gate sits in the wall, not over it. Changing becomes a walk through rather than a climb in front of witnesses.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Exits can be abused. A reframing that lets someone keep a false claim under a new name is not a change of mind at all. And sometimes the earlier view was not defensible and did harm; an apology may be owed, and an exit should not quietly cancel it. The exit is for the reasons, not for the consequences.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'When did you last change your mind about something that mattered? Which route did you take: new information, new circumstances, or a reframing?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these is a misuse of the exit rather than an honest one?',options:['Letting a colleague relabel a false claim so they never drop it','Pointing out that the new figures were not available last year','Agreeing that they were right about the risk, if not its size','Noting that the situation changed after they formed their view'],answer:0,why:'An honest exit keeps the reasons and drops the conclusion. Relabelling keeps the conclusion, which is no change at all.'}]}},'what-changed-my-mind_5':{pictureCaption:'A hand in the river, testing it, while the thermometer lies unused on the bank. What you find out yourself needs nobody\u2019s word.',bridge:'Idea 4 offered a route that costs little to take. Evidence you found yourself costs least of all: there is nobody to concede to.',basics:{title:'Let them find out for themselves',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'IS THE POOL COLD?',prompt:'Your friend says the pool is fine, not cold at all. You don\u2019t believe her. What would make you believe it?',options:['She says it again, louder','You put your own toe in','She shows you a sign saying it is warm'],answer:1,reveal:'Once your toe is in, nobody has to talk you round. You felt it. You can\u2019t argue with your own toe.'},{kind:'idea',text:'What changes people\u2019s minds most is often what they find out for themselves. So, instead of telling someone, help them find out.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The thermometer lies on the bank, not used. The person is testing the water with their own hand. Now they know.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it works',text:'When someone tells you a thing, you can doubt them. Maybe they are wrong. Maybe they just want to win. But you don\u2019t doubt yourself in the same way. And you don\u2019t have to say anyone else was right.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE WAY TO SCHOOL',text:'Your dad thinks the new way to school is slower. You could argue. Or you could say, \u201cShall we time both ways this week?\u201d If he times it himself, he will believe the clock. And if it is slower, you learn something too.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you want to change someone\u2019s mind, swap your point for a question. Ask something that helps them check for themselves.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend is sure he can\u2019t run round the whole park without stopping. Which way is most likely to change his mind?',options:['Ask him to try it with you this weekend','Tell him five times that he can do it','Show him a video of other kids doing it'],answer:0,why:'If he runs it himself, the proof is his own. Nobody had to win the argument.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE TAKEAWAY BILL',text:'Your partner is sure the weekly takeaway comes to about twenty pounds. You think it is nearer fifty. You could show them the bank statement. Or you could ask: *what do you reckon it came to last month? Shall we look together?* Same figures. Different owner.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The evidence that moves people is most often their own: something they saw, experienced or worked out. The most persuasive move is often to help them put themselves in its way.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'A statement',items:['Has a source to be suspicious of','Accepting it concedes something to you','Easy to reject']},right:{title:'A question',items:['Leads to their own conclusion','Nothing to concede to anyone','They own the answer']},note:'Same content. One produces a claim they can refuse; the other, a conclusion they hold.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why your own evidence wins',text:'A conclusion you reached yourself has no source to be suspicious of. And defending it does not mean conceding anything to anyone. The two usual obstacles, distrust of the messenger and the cost of losing, are simply not there.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The thermometer would give an answer. The hand in the water gives an answer they own.'},{kind:'try',text:'Turn your next assertion into an experiment: a question to answer, a thing to measure, a place to go and look. Then let them run it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A colleague insists a weekly meeting is essential. You think it is mostly wasted time. Which approach fits the idea?',options:['Suggest skipping it for a month and seeing what is missed','Send a well-argued email listing everything wrong with it','Gather complaints from others and present them together','Say nothing, since only their own evidence could move them'],answer:0,why:'A trial month lets them see the result first-hand. They may also find the meeting was needed, which is the price of the method working at all.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE ESSAY\u2019S LAST CLAIM',title:'Shown versus seen',text:'The earlier ideas keep meeting one obstacle: a change of mind that costs the person something in front of someone. The essay\u2019s final claim removes the someone. What moves people, it argues, is usually something they saw personally rather than something they were shown.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The strongest evidence is the person\u2019s own. So instead of the argument, offer the experiment: help them put themselves in the way of the evidence rather than handing it over.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'QUESTION BEATS CLAIM',nodes:['A question about their own case','They examine it','They reach a conclusion','No source to distrust','Nothing conceded to anyone'],note:'A statement of the same content produces a claim they can reject.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Inconvenient, by its own account',text:'The essay admits this is awkward for anyone who wants to persuade. The most persuasive thing you can do is often not to present evidence but to arrange for someone to meet it. The persuader\u2019s job shrinks to setting up conditions.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The thermometer is right there. The hand goes into the water anyway. The reading that counts is the one taken yourself.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The essay names the price: this is slow and cannot be aimed. You can create the conditions for someone to look; you cannot decide what they will conclude. That is also its safeguard. A question built so only one answer is possible is a statement in disguise. And personal experience can mislead: one cold morning says little about the climate.'},{kind:'recap',points:['People trust conclusions they reached themselves.','A question yields an owned conclusion; a statement, a claim to reject.','You set the conditions. You do not choose the result.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HONEST OBJECTION',prompt:'Which objection does this approach have to concede?',options:['You cannot control what the person concludes from their own look','Personal evidence never moves anyone who already holds a view','Questions are always manipulative, so they should never be used','Experiments only work on people who have been trained in science'],answer:0,why:'The essay says so itself: the lack of control is the price of the method working at all.'}]}}}},3035,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'what-friendship-studies-found_1':{pictureCaption:'A row of light tools and one heavy, dependable hammer at the end. Count the rack and you miss the one you would reach for.',basics:{title:'One friend you can count on',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TWO KIDS',prompt:'Ava has thirty friends online. Sam has two, and one of them would help him with anything. Who has more of what matters?',options:['Ava, because she has more friends','Sam, because he has someone to rely on','Neither, it comes out the same'],answer:1,reveal:'Studies that follow people for many years suggest that having someone you can count on matters more than how many friends you have. The results are not all the same, but they tend to point that way.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Having one person you can really count on seems to matter more than having lots of friends. When things go wrong, one good friend may help more than a crowd.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Lots of light tools, and one big hammer at the end. When there is real work to do, that is the one you reach for.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How do they know?',text:'Some scientists follow the same people for most of their lives. One famous study did this with a group of men. Its leader looked at how happy the men were with the people close to them at fifty. He said that told you more about their health at eighty than a common blood test did.'},{kind:'explain',title:'But be careful',text:'These studies watch people. They do not change anything to see what happens. So we can\u2019t be sure which thing causes which. Maybe healthy people just find it easier to keep close friends.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of who you could go to if something went really wrong. Do something kind for them this week.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Jake is sad that he is not popular. He has one friend who always sticks by him. What does this idea say?',options:['One friend he can count on matters a lot','He needs many more friends to be okay','Friends don\u2019t really matter for being happy'],answer:0,why:'The studies suggest one person you can rely on counts for more than a big crowd.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE SOCIAL SCORE',belief:'The more people you see, the healthier your social life.',reality:'In the longer cohort studies, the number of relationships tends to predict less than whether you have someone you could rely on in a crisis. The evidence is not uniform, but quality appears to beat count.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The quality of close relationships seems to track health and wellbeing better than their number. One reliable person appears to count for more than a wide network.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'A STUDY THAT RAN FOR DECADES',text:'The Harvard Study of Adult Development followed the same men for decades. Its director has reported this: how satisfied the men were with their relationships at fifty predicted health at eighty better than cholesterol readings did. What mattered was satisfaction, not count.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Maintaining a network',items:['Many contacts, kept warm','Effort spread thin','Judged by how many']},right:{title:'Deepening a few',items:['One or two you could call','Effort concentrated','Judged by reliance']},note:'Effort spent deepening a few relationships appears to return more than effort spent maintaining many.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A rack of light tools and one heavy one that does the real work. Counting the rack tells you little about that.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The caveat',text:'These are observational findings. People in better health may simply keep up better relationships. So which causes which is not cleanly established. The next idea takes that problem up properly.'},{kind:'try',text:'Ask yourself whether there is someone you could rely on in a crisis. Then spend this week\u2019s social effort there, not on the widest circle.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A friend who has just moved city plans to join five clubs to rebuild a social life. Which advice fits the idea?',options:['Fine, but aim to turn one contact into someone you could rely on','Join as many as possible, since the number of contacts is what counts','Skip the clubs, as relationships make little difference to health','Wait for a close friend to turn up without making any effort'],answer:0,why:'Contact is a way in. What the studies track is whether there is someone you could rely on, not how many people you see.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'WHAT GETS QUOTED',text:'The best-known source here is the Harvard Study of Adult Development, which followed the same men for decades. Its director has reported that satisfaction with relationships at fifty was a better predictor of health at eighty than cholesterol readings. This piece starts from the fact that such findings are quoted constantly and read rarely.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Across much of the literature, the quality of close relationships predicts health and wellbeing better than their number. The variable doing the work is satisfaction or reliance, not count.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the finding actually says',text:'Note the variable. It is not how many relationships the men had but how satisfied they were with them. In the longer cohort studies more broadly, whether a person reports someone they could rely on in a crisis tends to predict more than network size. The evidence is not uniform.'},{kind:'term',word:'Cohort study',meaning:'A study that follows the same group of people over time, measuring them again and again. It can show what predicts what. On its own it cannot show what causes what.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'What the usual measures count, a rack of light tools, and what they miss: the one heavy tool you would actually rely on.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The practical turn',text:'If satisfaction matters more than count, the question becomes allocation. Effort spent deepening a few relationships appears to return more than effort spent maintaining many, which is the opposite of what a network is usually optimised for.'},{kind:'limits',text:'These are observational findings. People in better health may sustain better relationships, so health could be producing the satisfaction rather than the reverse. A director\u2019s summary of a long study is not a measured effect size, and one cohort of men may not speak for everyone. The pattern recurs; its size and its cause are not established.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE OVERREACH',prompt:'Which claim goes further than the evidence described here?',options:['Improving your relationships will lower your cholesterol','Relationship satisfaction predicted later health in one long study','Having someone to rely on tends to predict more than network size','The causal direction between health and relationships is unclear'],answer:0,why:'The study compared predictors of later health. It did not show that relationships change cholesterol, or that improving them causes better health.'}]}},'what-friendship-studies-found_2':{pictureCaption:'Two wheels joined by one linkage, each driving the other. The hand with the pliers cannot find a place to cut that stops only one.',bridge:'Idea 1 ended on a caveat about cause and effect. This idea is that caveat, taken seriously.',basics:{title:'Which one came first?',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'A TERM OFF SCHOOL',text:'Rosa was ill for a whole term. She missed school and saw her friends less. Then she felt lonely, and that made her feel even worse. If you only met her at the end, you would see a girl who was lonely and ill. Could you tell which came first?'},{kind:'idea',text:'Being alone goes with worse health. But worse health also makes people more alone. The arrow points both ways, and nobody can fully pull the two apart.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two wheels joined together. Each one turns the other. Where would you cut to stop just one of them?'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE THE CHICKEN AND THE EGG',text:'Which came first, the chicken or the egg? Each one makes the other, so the question has no neat answer. Being alone and being ill can be a bit like that.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Other things push on both',text:'Being short of money can make someone lonelier and less healthy at once. So can finding it hard to get out, or an illness called depression, which makes people feel low for a long time.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'So friends don\u2019t really matter for your health.',reality:'That is not it either. Scientists check for those other things as best they can, and the link is still there. They just can\u2019t give an exact number for it.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A news story says *being lonely makes you ill.* What is the best thing to think?',options:['It may be partly true, but being ill can cause loneliness too','It must be true, because a study found it','It must be false, because studies are often wrong'],answer:0,why:'The link is real, but the arrow points both ways. A simple *this causes that* is more certain than the studies are.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE HEADLINE',text:'Picture a headline: isolation raises your risk of illness by some exact percentage. Behind it is a study that watched one group of people over time. The number travels well. The study design that limits it does not travel at all.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Isolation predicts worse health. But poor health also leads to isolation. And income, mobility and depression predict both. No study of this kind can fully pull them apart. So a clean figure for cause and effect claims too much.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE ARROW RUNS BOTH WAYS',nodes:['Isolation','Worse health','Less able to see people','More isolation'],loop:!0,note:'Income, mobility and depression push on every step at once.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What adjustment can and cannot do',text:'Studies adjust for the factors they measure, as far as they can. That removes some of the tangle, not all of it. Anything measured badly, or not measured at all, stays inside the result.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'If the causation is unclear, the finding can be dismissed.',reality:'No. Effects survive adjustment, and the ways it could work are plausible. The doubt belongs to the exact numbers passed around, not to the idea that connection matters.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Two mechanisms joined by one linkage, each driving the other, and a hand that cannot find the place to cut.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you read that isolation causes something, look for the arrow back: could the outcome also be producing the isolation?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A colleague cites a study showing isolated people die sooner, and says making friends adds a set number of years. What is the fair response?',options:['The link is real, but a precise causal figure is more than the design supports','The finding is worthless, because observational studies prove nothing','The figure is right, because the study adjusted for income','The finding only shows that people in poor health dislike company'],answer:0,why:'Effects survive adjustment, so the link deserves weight. But an exact causal number still claims more than observational data can give.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHERE THE NUMBERS COME FROM',text:'The long cohort studies are observational: they measure people and wait. They cannot assign anyone to isolation. So every finding about connection and health inherits one problem, and this piece puts it at the centre: the direction of cause.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Isolation predicts worse health, poor health produces isolation, and both track income, mobility and depression. Adjustment helps but cannot fully separate them, so treat the finding as real and the circulating numbers with suspicion.'},{kind:'term',word:'Confounding',meaning:'When a third factor drives both of the things being compared, making them look more linked than they are. Income, mobility and depression can each act this way here.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Why the finding stands',items:['Effects survive adjustment','The mechanisms are plausible']},right:{title:'Why the numbers wobble',items:['Illness can produce isolation','Shared causes push on both','Adjustment reaches only what was measured']},note:'The piece holds both columns at once.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Each wheel turns the other through one linkage. The pliers are ready, but no single cut would stop just one.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What a clean figure claims',text:'A precise causal number says: change this, and that changes by so much. Observational cohorts cannot deliver that sentence. Anyone quoting one is quoting more confidence than the design supports, however good the study.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The scepticism has its own limit. Used carelessly, the arrow argument becomes a way to dismiss every observational finding, and a good deal of health knowledge rests on observational evidence. Here the effects survive adjustment and the mechanisms are plausible. The doubt is aimed at the precision, not the existence.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE MOST DEFENSIBLE READING',prompt:'Which statement fits the evidence best?',options:['Isolation and poor health are linked, and each may feed the other','Isolation causes a known, measured share of early deaths','The link is an artefact of income and nothing more','Poor health causes isolation, but not the other way round'],answer:0,why:'The link survives adjustment, and the arrow runs both ways. The other three each claim a separation nobody has achieved.'}]}},'what-friendship-studies-found_3':{pictureCaption:'Every seat in the room is taken. One person sits among them in the terracotta chair, surrounded, and still alone.',bridge:'Ideas 1 and 2 were about isolation. This one separates it from loneliness, which is a different thing.',basics:{title:'Lonely is not the same as alone',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE BIRTHDAY PARTY',text:'The party is full. Music, games, your whole class. You are right in the middle of it all. And you feel completely on your own.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Being lonely and being alone are not the same. You can be with lots of people and feel lonely. You can be on your own and feel fine.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Being alone',items:['How many people you see','You can count it']},right:{title:'Feeling lonely',items:['How you feel inside','Wanting more closeness than you have']},note:'One is a number. The other is a feeling.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Every chair is full. One person sits right in the middle of the crowd. Surrounded, and still alone.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it matters',text:'If a friend feels lonely, just adding more people may not help. What may help more is feeling that someone really knows you. Lots of people is not the same as being known.'},{kind:'try',text:'If a friend with lots of friends says they feel lonely, believe them. Ask them about it, instead of telling them they can\u2019t be.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Zara is always with a big group, but she says she feels lonely. What does this idea say?',options:['Believe her, because you can feel lonely in a crowd','She can\u2019t be lonely with so many friends around','She just needs to join one more group'],answer:0,why:'Loneliness is a feeling, not a head count. A big group does not always mean feeling known.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO DIFFERENT MEASURES',left:{title:'Isolation',items:['Seen from outside','How much contact you have','Counted']},right:{title:'Loneliness',items:['Felt from inside','The gap between contact had and wanted','Reported as a feeling']},note:'They are measured separately. The link between them is only moderate.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Loneliness and isolation are different variables. Someone can be isolated without being lonely. They can also be lonely in constant company. So adding contact does not reliably fix loneliness.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'A FULL DIARY',text:'A colleague has drinks twice a week, a close team and family nearby. She says she is lonely. It sounds impossible until you pull the two apart. Her contact is high. Her sense of being known is not.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why programmes underperform',text:'A scheme that adds time with people, but not the sense of being known, moves one measure and not the other. That is one reason well-meant programmes that simply add contact have tended to fall short.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A full room, every chair taken, and one person alone in the middle of it. High contact, and loneliness anyway.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Both still matter',text:'Both predict poor health. They do so partly on their own, which suggests they work in different ways. Neither one can stand in for the other.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time someone with a full diary says they are lonely, believe it without needing it to make sense. Ask what they are missing, not who.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A care home adds a weekly group activity for a lonely resident, and her loneliness does not change. What is the best explanation?',options:['More contact did not give her the sense of being known','She needs two activities a week rather than one','She cannot really be lonely if she goes along','Group activities make loneliness worse in older people'],answer:0,why:'The activity moved her isolation, not her loneliness. The felt gap is about being known, not about the count.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'TWO WORDS, TWO VARIABLES',word:'Loneliness',meaning:'In this literature, a subjective state: the gap between the contact a person has and the contact they want. Isolation, by contrast, is the objective amount of contact.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Loneliness and isolation are measured separately and are only moderately correlated. Both predict poor health, somewhat independently, so they call for different responses.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the separation reveals',text:'Because they come apart, there are people with full calendars who report loneliness, and people who see almost nobody who do not. And because each predicts poor health somewhat independently of the other, the piece infers distinct mechanisms: not one problem measured twice.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Adding contact moves',items:['The number of interactions','Isolation scores']},right:{title:'It may not move',items:['The sense of being known','Loneliness scores']},note:'Hence the observation that programmes which only add contact have tended to underperform.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Not isolated by any count, every chair full, and one person who would still report being lonely.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Taking the felt state seriously',text:'The practical upshot is to believe a lonely person with a full diary, without needing it to make sense. Their report is the measure. Arguing from their contact count is arguing about the wrong variable.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The separation can be overdone. The two do correlate, if only moderately: for many people more contact and less loneliness go together. A subjective measure also depends on what a person expects, so similar lives can report differently. And the claim is that contact-only programmes have tended to underperform, not that adding contact never helps.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE ERROR',prompt:'A council judges its loneliness scheme by how many visits volunteers make. What is wrong with that?',options:['Visits count contact, not whether loneliness changed','Visits are too costly to be counted accurately','Loneliness cannot be measured in any way at all','Nothing, since more visits mean less loneliness'],answer:0,why:'The scheme is measuring isolation and reporting it as loneliness. Only the felt state shows whether loneliness moved.'}]}},'what-friendship-studies-found_4':{pictureCaption:'Two plants on one sill. The one beside the watering can is green and upright; the one left without water has wilted. Nothing else differs.',bridge:'Ideas 1 and 3 said closeness is what counts. This one is about how closeness is kept once life stops arranging it for you.',basics:{title:'Friendships need looking after',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A PLANT ON THE WINDOWSILL',picture:!0,text:'A plant does not die because you stopped liking it. It dies because nobody watered it. Friendships can be like that. They need small bits of care, again and again.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Friendships fade when you stop being in touch. That is normal, not anyone\u2019s fault. So when life moves you apart, you have to keep in touch on purpose.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'WHEN A FRIEND MOVES SCHOOL',text:'At school you saw Ben every day without trying. Then he moved to a new school. You both meant to stay friends. A few months later, you hardly ever talk.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What changed?',text:'School was doing the work. It put you both in the same room every day. When that stopped, nothing was keeping you in touch. The friendship did not break. The thing holding it up went away.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Keep a friend',steps:['Pick one friend you don\u2019t see much now.','Choose a set time, like every Sunday.','Send a message or call them then.','Plan to meet up when you can.']},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your best friend moves to a new town. What gives the friendship the best chance?',options:['Planning a call at a set time, like every Saturday','Waiting to see if you both remember to call','Finding a new best friend straight away'],answer:0,why:'Being in touch often is a good sign that a friendship will last. A set time makes it much more likely to happen.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'TWO YEARS AFTER THE MOVE',text:'You were close to a colleague for years. Then one of you changed jobs. You both said you would keep in touch. Two years on there have been three messages, all about birthdays, and calling now would feel awkward.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Adult friendships decay without maintenance, and the decay is the default, not a failure. How often you are in contact is one of the better predictors of whether a friendship survives a move or a job change.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW A FRIENDSHIP FADES',nodes:['Shared context ends','Spontaneous contact stops','Contact gets rarer','Closeness fades'],note:'Nothing in the relationship went wrong. The structure that was producing the contact went away.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Same sill, same light. The plant still being watered stands upright; the one left alone has wilted. Nothing went wrong with it. The watering stopped.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The structure was doing the work',text:'School, a shared office, a street you both lived on: these produced contact without anyone deciding to. Studies of network turnover find substantial replacement over several years, driven largely by changes in shared context rather than by friendships going bad.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Spontaneous contact',items:['Depends on shared context','Lost with a move or new job','Feels natural']},right:{title:'Scheduled contact',items:['Not tied to a place','More dependable','Feels unromantic']},note:'Spontaneous contact is exactly what the loss of shared context removes.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick two people. Put a recurring reminder in your calendar to contact each of them. Treat it as maintenance, not as a test of the friendship.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A close friend moves abroad. Which plan best fits the evidence?',options:['A monthly call, booked into both calendars','Trusting the friendship to survive, since it is strong','Messaging only when something big happens','Visiting once, then seeing how things go'],answer:0,why:'Contact frequency is one of the better predictors of survival, and scheduled contact replaces the spontaneous kind the move removed.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE ROMANTIC VIEW',belief:'Real friendships survive distance on their own. If one fades, it was never strong.',reality:'Adult friendships decay without maintenance, and the decay is the default rather than a failure. Without contact, closeness fades.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Adult friendship is closer to a maintained system than a stable state. Contact frequency predicts survival through a move or job change, and scheduled contact is more dependable than spontaneous contact.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The evidence',text:'Studies of network turnover find substantial replacement over periods of several years. The driver is largely change in shared context, such as job, neighbourhood and life stage, rather than deterioration in the relationship. How often two people are in contact is one of the better predictors of which friendships survive.'},{kind:'term',word:'Shared context',meaning:'A setting that brings people together without anyone arranging it: a school, a workplace, a neighbourhood, a stage of life. It produces contact as a by-product.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why scheduling, specifically',text:'Spontaneous contact is exactly what the loss of shared context removes, so the replacement cannot be spontaneous. Friendship in structured settings took no deliberate effort because the structure was doing the work. In adulthood the effort has to be deliberate.'},{kind:'equation',parts:['Shared context','Spontaneous contact'],result:'Friendship without effort',note:'Remove the first and the second goes with it. Scheduled contact is the substitute.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Decay as the default, drawn plainly: two identical plants, and the only difference is that one stopped being watered. Nothing in the plant failed. The maintenance ended.'},{kind:'limits',text:'That contact frequency predicts survival does not show it causes survival: people who already care more may simply keep in touch more. Some friendships also lapse for good reasons and need no rescue. And a reminder can produce contact without closeness. Idea 1\u2019s point still applies: what the studies track is quality, not the count of calls.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'Someone objects that a friendship kept alive by calendar reminders is not a real friendship. What is the best reply?',options:['The reminder replaces the structure that produced contact, not the caring','They are right, so friendships should be left to survive on their own','Real friends never need reminders, which shows the objection is right','Scheduled contact is better than closeness in every way that counts'],answer:0,why:'School and work used to generate contact for free. Scheduling replaces that structure. The closeness still has to come from the people.'}]}},'what-friendship-studies-found_5':{pictureCaption:'A dark hallway at night, one telephone, and a blank card beside it waiting for a name. The question is whether you could write one.',bridge:'Idea 1 said one reliable person counts for more than many. This last idea turns that into a single question you can ask yourself.',basics:{title:'Who could you call?',beats:[{kind:'ask',kicker:'A QUESTION FOR YOU',prompt:'If something went really wrong, even in the middle of the night, who could you turn to?'},{kind:'idea',text:'One of the most useful questions about friends is simple. Who could you call in the middle of the night? If a name comes to you straight away, that is a good sign.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why this question works',text:'Counting friends is easy to fake. You can add anyone to the list. But this question is hard to fool yourself with. Either a name comes to mind straight away, or it doesn\u2019t.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A phone in a dark hall at night. Next to it, a blank card for one name. Could you fill it in?'},{kind:'explain',title:'Nobody has to call',text:'The point is not to make the call. Almost nobody does. The point is knowing that you could. That tells you a lot about your friendships.'},{kind:'try',text:'Say the name of someone you could turn to. If no name comes, that is worth knowing too. Talk to a parent or a teacher about it.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Which question tells you most about whether you have a good friend?',options:['Who could I turn to if something went really wrong?','How many people follow me online?','How many people came to my party?'],answer:0,why:'Numbers can be padded with anyone. A name you could really turn to cannot.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THREE WAYS TO CHECK',prompt:'Which is the most honest check on your social life?',options:['How many people you saw this month','Whether you could name someone to call at 3 a.m.','How busy your weekends are'],answer:1,reveal:'A count can be padded, and a diary can be full. The middle-of-the-night question is unambiguous and hard to talk yourself into.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The most useful single question from this research is whether there is someone you could call in the middle of the night. It works because it is unambiguous: a count of friends can be padded, and this answer cannot.'},{kind:'equation',parts:['A close enough bond','Belief it\u2019s welcome','They are available'],result:'A name, without hesitating',note:'One question tests all three at once. If any one fails, the name does not come.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Diagnostic, not literal',text:'Almost nobody makes the call. The question is doing diagnostic work rather than describing an event. What matters is whether you could name someone without hesitating.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A hallway at three in the morning, one telephone, and a blank card beside it. The test is whether a name goes on it.'},{kind:'try',text:'Name the person, now. If you hesitated, or could not, that is the finding, and it is something you can act on.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'READ THE ANSWER',prompt:'Someone answers the 3 a.m. question with a long pause, then a name they are unsure of. How should they read that?',options:['The hesitation is itself information worth acting on','A name came in the end, so the answer is fine','The question is unfair, since nobody calls at 3 a.m.','They should count their friends instead to be sure'],answer:0,why:'The question is diagnostic. Hesitating over it is the result, and it points to what to work on.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHERE THE PIECE ENDS',text:'The piece began with studies that are quoted constantly and read rarely. After the caveats about cause and measurement, it asks what one person can honestly take from all of it. Its answer is a single question.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A question sometimes used in research on social support is whether you have someone you could call in the middle of the night. It tests several things at once, and an honest answer cannot be padded.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'A count of friends',items:['Can be padded','Measures contact','Easy to talk yourself into']},right:{title:'The 3 a.m. question',items:['Unambiguous','Tests closeness, welcome, availability','Hesitation shows']},note:'This is idea 1\u2019s finding, quality over count, made into a personal check.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it works as an indicator',text:'It tests three things together: a relationship close enough to permit the imposition, your belief that the call would be welcome, and the person\u2019s practical availability. It needs no instrument, and it is diagnostic rather than literal: almost nobody makes the call.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One telephone, one blank card, a hallway at three in the morning. The card stands for the name you could, or could not, write.'},{kind:'limits',text:'It is a self-check, and the piece says only that the question is used in research, not how well it predicts anything. It can mislead both ways. A confident answer may be wrong about how welcome the call would be. Someone with no single name may have real support spread across several people. Treat hesitation as a prompt, not a verdict.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'Which would make a good substitute for the 3 a.m. question, for the same reasons?',options:['Who would you ask to collect you from hospital at short notice?','How many people would come to your birthday this year?','How many contacts are saved in your phone right now?','How many evenings a week do you usually go out?'],answer:0,why:'Like the original, it tests closeness, expected welcome and availability at once. The others are counts, which can be padded.'}]}}}},3036,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'what-predicts-saving_1':{pictureCaption:'A sluice set once. The water runs through it every time, and nobody has to touch it again.',basics:{title:'Make saving happen without deciding',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO SAVE',prompt:'Priya has to decide each week to put some pocket money in her money box. Tom\u2019s dad puts some aside for him first, unless Tom says no. Who saves more?',options:['Priya','Tom','About the same'],answer:1,reveal:'Most likely Tom. Deciding takes effort, so some weeks Priya won\u2019t do it. Tom does not have to do anything. He saves even in the weeks he forgets.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Set saving up once, so it happens by itself. Then doing nothing still means you save. Of all the saving tricks, this one has the best tests behind it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'A big real test',text:'In many countries, workers used to have to sign up to save for when they are old. Then the rule flipped. They were signed up unless they said no. Far more people saved. Nobody was talked into anything.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'This gate was set once. Water runs through it every day, and nobody has to touch it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it works',text:'Doing something takes effort. Doing nothing takes none. So whatever happens when you do nothing usually wins. The trick is to make saving the thing that happens when you do nothing.'},{kind:'try',text:'Ask a grown-up to help you set this up. Each time you get pocket money, a bit goes aside first, before you spend any.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Lily is saving for a bike. Which plan is most likely to work?',options:['A bit goes in her bike jar each pocket-money day, unless she stops it','She decides each week how much to put in her bike jar','She reads a book about why saving is a good idea'],answer:0,why:'Lily saves even when she forgets, because doing nothing means saving. The other plans need her to act every time.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE MONTHLY DECISION',text:'Every month you mean to move some money into savings. Some months you do. Other months the car needs a tyre, or you forget until the money has gone. By the end of the year the account holds far less than you planned.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Defaults are among the best-supported findings on saving. Make saving the thing that happens if you do nothing, because whatever needs a monthly decision will eventually not get one.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The evidence',text:'When pension saving switched from opt-in to opt-out, participation rose dramatically, with no change in the financial incentives and nothing new said to anyone. The result has held across countries, schemes and populations. It is among the most replicated results in applied behavioural work.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'THE SAME SCHEME, FLIPPED',left:{title:'Opt-in',items:['You act to join','Doing nothing means not saving','Participation lower']},right:{title:'Opt-out',items:['You act to leave','Doing nothing means saving','Participation far higher']},note:'Same money, same rules, same information. Only the default changed.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A sluice set once. The water goes through it every time, and nobody has to decide again.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it holds',text:'It does not rest on any contested theory. Acting takes effort and not acting does not, which is about as safe an assumption as behavioural science offers. Anything that relies on a fresh decision every month is up against that, and tends to lose.'},{kind:'try',text:'Set up a standing transfer into savings on the day you are paid. Choose an amount small enough that you will not need to undo it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Which change is most likely to raise what a household saves over a year?',options:['An automatic transfer into savings every payday','A monthly reminder to transfer whatever they can spare','A budgeting course on why saving matters','A savings goal written up on the fridge door'],answer:0,why:'The transfer happens unless someone acts to stop it. The other three still depend on a decision being made each month.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHERE THE PIECE STARTS',text:'The piece surveys a decade of savings interventions and sorts them: one robust finding, several that shrank on replication, and a persistent confusion between what predicts saving and what causes it. It begins with the one that held.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Automatic enrolment is among the most robust findings in the behavioural literature on saving. It works without persuading anyone of anything, because it changes what happens when people do nothing.'},{kind:'term',word:'Default',meaning:'What happens if you take no action. In an opt-in scheme the default is not saving; in an opt-out scheme it is saving. Either way, many people never move from it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it survived replication',text:'A finding that rests on a contested theory is only as safe as the theory. This one rests on almost nothing: acting is effortful and not acting is not. Participation moved dramatically with no change in incentives or information, across countries, schemes and populations.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHAT A DEFAULT REVERSES',nodes:['Saving needs a monthly decision','One month the decision is skipped','Skipping costs nothing at the time','In time, it stops being made'],note:'Under a default the chain runs the other way: a skipped month means saving continues.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Set once, and the water keeps running through. The design works because nobody has to remember it.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The finding is about participation, not adequacy. A default sets how much as well as whether, and nothing in the result says the default amount is enough. It also moves people who would have chosen otherwise, so who sets it matters. And for someone with no slack, an automatic transfer can simply move the shortfall somewhere else.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'An employer wants more staff to take up a free health check. Which design does this finding favour?',options:['Book everyone a slot by default, and let them cancel it','Send a well-designed leaflet explaining the benefits','Offer a small prize to anyone who signs up for one','Hold a lunchtime talk on why health checks matter'],answer:0,why:'A default changes what happens when people do nothing. The other designs leave the effort of acting in place, and ask people to overcome it.'}]}},'what-predicts-saving_2':{pictureCaption:'Well-made teaching models of piggy banks and charts, in an empty room. Everything a course supplies is here. The spending happens elsewhere.',bridge:'Idea 1 was the finding that held. This one does less than hoped: teaching people about money.',basics:{title:'Knowing is not the same as doing',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'A COMMON IDEA',belief:'If you learn how money works, you will save more.',reality:'Learning helps you *know* more. Some big reviews of money classes found people knew more afterwards. But what they did changed only a little, and the change faded fast. Later reviews found a bit more change. Still not a lot.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Most people already know they should save. The hard part is doing it when other things want the money. A lesson gives you facts, but facts don\u2019t do the saving.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE BRUSHING YOUR TEETH',text:'You know you should brush your teeth for two minutes. Knowing is easy. Doing it when you are tired and late is hard. A talk about teeth won\u2019t fix that. A timer on the brush might.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Neat models of piggy banks and charts in an empty classroom. All the know-how is here. Nothing here does the saving.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What does help',text:'What works is help at the moment you decide. Money that goes into savings by itself on pocket-money day is one example. That is the set-it-once trick from the last lesson.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of something you know you should do but often don\u2019t. What could help at the moment you decide, not just in your head?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Max took a class on money. He can explain saving really well. But he still spends all his pocket money. Why?',options:['Knowing is easy, but doing it when you want things is hard','He did not listen properly in the class','Saving is too hard for anyone his age'],answer:0,why:'Max has the facts. What is missing is help at the moment he spends.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A WORKPLACE COURSE',prompt:'A company runs a well-reviewed course on saving. Staff score much higher on a money quiz afterwards. A year later, how much more are they saving?',options:['A lot more','A little more, if anything','Less than before'],answer:1,reveal:'Some meta-analyses of financial literacy programmes find clear gains in knowledge but small effects on behaviour that fade within months. Later, larger analyses found modest positive effects. Either way, not a lot.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Financial education on its own does less than hoped. Knowing seems not to be what holds most people back. The likelier failure is in doing it under competing demands, and information does not address that.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHAT A COURSE CHANGES',left:{title:'Knowledge',items:['Rises clearly','Shows up on a quiz','Easy to deliver']},right:{title:'Behaviour',items:['Moves a little','May fade within months','Decided under pressure']},note:'The course aims at the left column. Saving happens in the right one.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it is uncomfortable',text:'Education is the intervention institutions find easiest to deliver, and a whole industry is built on it. So evidence that it changes behaviour only modestly is unwelcome. The model sounds reasonable. The evidence suggests it is aimed at the wrong point in the process.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What works instead',text:'Intervention at the moment of decision: defaults, automatic transfers, prompts timed to when income arrives. These do not try to change what you know. They change what happens when the decision comes.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Fine teaching models in an empty room. A course can supply everything on that table, and none of it is there when the money gets spent.'},{kind:'try',text:'Take one saving decision you keep meaning to make. Instead of reading more about it, change the moment itself: automate it, or set a prompt for payday.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A friend is signing up for an evening course so that she will finally start saving. What does the evidence suggest?',options:['She will likely learn a lot, but her saving may barely change','The course is pointless, because she will learn nothing useful','The course will work, as long as she studies hard enough','She will save more, provided the course is well taught'],answer:0,why:'Knowledge gains are clear; changes in behaviour are small or modest, and may fade. She can take the course, but should not expect it to change what she does.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'THE KEY TERM',word:'Binding constraint',meaning:'The limit actually holding an outcome back. Loosen it and the outcome moves; loosen anything else and it does not. The piece suggests that for saving, knowledge is seldom the binding one.'},{kind:'idea',text:'How well financial education works on its own is contested: some analyses find small effects that fade within months, later and larger ones modest gains. Either way, knowledge seems not to be the binding constraint. Execution under competing demands is the likelier one.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the meta-analyses found',text:'The piece reports a split. Some meta-analyses find clear gains in knowledge, very little effect on what people later do with money, and effects that decay within months rather than years. Later and larger analyses found modest positive effects: better, though still modest. Neither reading makes knowledge the main thing holding saving back.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHERE A COURSE ACTS',nodes:['Knowledge','An intention to save','The moment of decision','Saving, or not'],note:'Education works on the first link. The likelier failure is at the third, under competing demands. That is where defaults and timed prompts act.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the piece calls it uncomfortable',text:'Education is what institutions find easiest to deliver, and an industry is built on it. It also sounds right. The evidence suggests the model is aimed at the wrong point in the process, however reasonable it sounds.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Fine models in an empty room. A course supplies the table. The spending happens somewhere else, later.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The finding is about education alone, and about behaviour. Its size is contested: small in some analyses, modest in later, larger ones, and zero in neither. Knowledge may matter where it really is missing. Understanding has value of its own, and may help people choose well once a default is in place. The claim is narrower than it first sounds: do not expect a course, by itself, to change what you do.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE OVERREACH',prompt:'Which conclusion goes further than the evidence the piece describes?',options:['Teaching people about money is a waste of time and should stop','A course alone is unlikely to change saving by very much','Knowledge gains from courses are real, even if behaviour lags','Help at the moment of decision tends to do more than a course'],answer:0,why:'The evidence is about education alone changing behaviour. It does not show that understanding is worthless, only that it seems rarely to be the binding constraint.'}]}},'what-predicts-saving_3':{pictureCaption:'The water pouring down the channel decides more of what happens than the paddle does. The paddle in the hand can steer a little of it.',bridge:'Ideas 1 and 2 asked which tools work. This one asks what the tools have to work with.',basics:{title:'Saving is easier with more coming in',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'TWO FRIENDS',text:'Ella gets pocket money every week. Sam gets less, and some weeks none at all. This month Sam also had to pay for a lost library book. At the end of the month, Ella has saved more.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Is Ella better at saving?',text:'Maybe not. She has more coming in. It comes every week. And nothing went wrong. Those things matter more than good habits.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Who saves depends more on how much money comes in, and how steady it is, than on good habits. So before blaming someone\u2019s habits, look at the money first.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Lots of water rushes down the channel. The little paddle can only steer a bit of it. How much water comes matters more.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE FILLING A BUCKET',text:'A careful hand helps you fill a bucket without spilling. But if the tap only drips, being careful won\u2019t help much. Habits are the careful hand. Money coming in is the tap.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Be fair',text:'If someone is not saving, don\u2019t just say they need more willpower. Maybe there is not enough money left over. Blaming them would be wrong. It would be unkind, too.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your cousin has a weekend job. Her hours change every week, and some weeks there are none. She saves very little. What should you look at first?',options:['How much money comes in, and how steady it is','Whether she buys too many snacks','Whether she tries hard enough to save'],answer:0,why:'Money coming in, and how steady it is, matter more than habits. Look there before you judge her.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE USUAL DIAGNOSIS',belief:'People who do not save mostly lack discipline.',reality:'Behavioural factors explain a modest share of who saves. Income, how stable it is, and unexpected expenses explain more. Treating a structural problem as a discipline problem is both wrong and unkind.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When somebody is not saving, look at their income and its stability before their habits. Behavioural tools work best where there is slack, so aim them where they can bind.'},{kind:'term',word:'Slack',meaning:'Money left over once the essentials are paid. Habits, defaults and nudges act on slack. Where there is none, there is nothing for them to act on.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'THE SAME TOOLS, TWO HOUSEHOLDS',left:{title:'Steady salary, some slack',items:['An automatic transfer sticks','Small habits add up','A nudge has room to work']},right:{title:'Irregular pay, no slack',items:['A transfer is undone next week','One broken boiler wipes out progress','A nudge has nothing to move']},note:'Same tools, very different results, for reasons that have nothing to do with character.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The volume of water down the channel decides more of the outcome than the paddle at the side, which adjusts what is left.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Not fatalism',text:'This does not say behaviour is irrelevant. Behaviour still explains part of who saves, and the tools in this piece still work. It says where to aim them: at people with room to save, not as a general answer for people without it.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you judge someone\u2019s finances, your own included, ask three things first: how much comes in, how steady is it, and what went wrong unexpectedly?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A charity wants to help people on unpredictable shift work save more. What does this idea suggest it looks at first?',options:['Income stability, since habits can only act on slack','Discipline, since poor habits are the main problem','A savings app that rewards daily streaks','Nothing, since behaviour makes no difference'],answer:0,why:'Income and its stability explain more than behaviour. Behavioural tools work best where there is slack, and stability is often what is missing here.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'WHO GAINS FROM THE NUDGE',prompt:'Two people save nothing. One has a steady salary. The other has low, irregular pay and frequent surprise bills. Both get the same well-designed saving nudge. Who is it more likely to help?',options:['The steady earner','The irregular earner','Both equally'],answer:0,reveal:'Behavioural interventions work best where there is slack. The same tool lands very differently depending on what it has to work with.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Behavioural factors explain a modest share of who saves; income, its stability and unexpected expenses explain more. So behavioural tools should be targeted where they can bind, not offered as a general answer.'},{kind:'term',word:'Variance explained',meaning:'How much of the difference between people a factor accounts for. To say income explains more of the variance in saving is to say it does more than habits to separate savers from non-savers.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Predicting is not causing',text:'The piece\u2019s wider warning is against confusing what predicts saving with what causes it. Savers may look disciplined, but discipline is easier to sustain with steady income and slack. A habit that goes with saving can partly reflect the income behind it.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'WHERE A NUDGE CAN BIND',low:'No slack: nothing left after essentials',high:'Plenty of slack: room every month',note:'Behavioural tools gain force toward the right. At the left end the binding constraint is income, and no nudge reaches it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'More of the outcome comes from the flow down the channel than from the paddle. The hand on the paddle matters, at the margin.'},{kind:'limits',text:'This can be misread as fatalism. Behaviour still explains part of who saves, and the tools still work where there is room, so it is no reason to ignore habits. Nor does the piece give figures for the shares. Treat modest and more as directions, not sizes.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'CORRECT THE MISREADING',prompt:'Someone concludes: income matters more than habits, so saving advice is pointless. What is the best correction?',options:['Advice and tools still help, but work best where there is slack to act on','Income matters least, so advice is the main thing after all','Advice is pointless only for people on high incomes','Saving advice works the same for everyone, whatever their income'],answer:0,why:'The point is targeting, not giving up. Behaviour explains a modest share of who saves, and the tools bind best where there is room to save.'}]}},'what-predicts-saving_4':{pictureCaption:'The same contents in every jar. The hand goes for the one with no marking: a pot without a purpose is the easy one to raid.',bridge:'Back to tools that work where there is slack. This one works for a reason that should not work at all.',basics:{title:'Give your savings a name',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'TWO JARS',text:'Zoe has two money jars. One says *savings*. The other says *new football boots*. When she wants sweets, she takes from the savings jar. The boots jar stays full.'},{kind:'explain',title:'But money is money',text:'A pound in one jar buys the same as a pound in the other. So the labels should make no difference. But they do. People with a pot for one clear thing take from it less.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Keep your savings in a pot with a clear name, for one clear thing. It shouldn\u2019t matter, but it does. A named pot is harder to dip into.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Every jar holds the same stuff. The hand goes for the one with no mark. A pot with no job is the easy one to take from.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it works',text:'To spend the boots money, Zoe has to give up on the boots. That feels bad, so she stops. Money with no job has nothing to give up. Spending it just feels like spending.'},{kind:'try',text:'If you save, give the pot a clear name, like *bike by summer*. Not just *savings*.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Ollie has three jars. Which one is he least likely to dip into?',options:['A jar called *trip to see Gran*','A jar called *spare money*','A jar with no name on it'],answer:0,why:'A jar with a clear job is harder to raid. Taking from it means giving up the trip.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE HOLIDAY FUND',text:'You keep a holiday fund of a few hundred pounds. At the same time you carry a balance on a credit card. On paper it makes no sense. The card costs more in interest than the fund earns. Yet people keep set-ups like this, and the fund survives the year.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Mental accounting means treating money in different pots as if it were not interchangeable. It is irrational in the strict sense and useful in practice. People with a named account for a named purpose raid it less.'},{kind:'term',word:'Fungible',meaning:'Interchangeable. Any pound can do the job of any other. Because money is fungible, giving it labels should change nothing about how it gets spent.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the label works',text:'A labelled pot has a purpose. Spending it means overriding that purpose, not merely deciding to spend. That extra step is where the saving happens. The departure from rationality is the very thing doing the work.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Identical contents, different markings, and a hand reaching for the unmarked jar. Money without a named job is the easiest to spend.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'NAME IT NARROWLY',left:{title:'A broad label',items:['Emergency','Savings','Rainy day']},right:{title:'A specific label',items:['January rent','Car insurance in March','Dad\u2019s seventieth']},note:'The piece\u2019s own illustration: January rent, say, rather than a vague Emergency. It is a suggestion, not a measured comparison.'},{kind:'try',text:'Rename one savings account after the specific thing it is for, with a date if it has one.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Which arrangement is most likely to keep money saved?',options:['An account named for next January\u2019s rent','A general account for whatever comes up','Spare money left in the current account','A note in an app that says save more'],answer:0,why:'A named purpose has to be overridden before the money goes. The others have no purpose to override. So spending them is just spending.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE ECONOMIST\u2019S OBJECTION',belief:'Labelling money cannot matter, because a pound is a pound.',reality:'In strict terms the objection is right: money is fungible. In practice, labels reliably change what people do. The piece\u2019s position is that both things are true at once.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Mental accounting is irrational in the strict sense and useful in practice. It is a workaround exploiting a bias, not a correction of one: worth keeping where it helps, and dropping where it is expensive.'},{kind:'term',word:'Mental accounting',meaning:'Treating money in different pots as non-interchangeable: this for rent, that for holidays. A strict view of money says the pots are one pot with several names.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The mechanism',text:'A labelled pot has a purpose. To spend it you must override that purpose, which is a heavier act than simply deciding to spend. So the departure from rationality is not a flaw in the method. It is the method.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'KEEP IT OR DROP IT',left:{title:'Keep the pots when',items:['They produce better behaviour','The inefficiency costs little','They protect a clear purpose']},right:{title:'Drop them when',items:['High-interest debt sits beside the savings','The interest gap is genuinely expensive','The label is so broad it gets raided']},note:'The test is what the inefficiency costs, not whether it is tidy.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The same substance in every jar, and a hand reaching for the plain one. The marks change nothing inside the jars, and still change what gets taken.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The workaround has a price. A fund held beside a high-interest card balance can cost more in interest than it protects, and that is usually where the inefficiency becomes genuinely expensive. The claim is also modest: labelled pots appear to help, and the advice to prefer a specific name over a broad one like emergency is a suggestion, not a measured result.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY THE TEST',prompt:'Someone has \xa32,000 in a pot labelled *wedding* and \xa32,000 on a high-interest credit card. What does the piece\u2019s honest position suggest?',options:['Weigh the interest cost: here the pot may be too expensive to keep','Keep both, since labelled pots always beat paying off debt','Spend the pot, since labels are irrational and best ignored','Open more labelled pots, so that the debt feels smaller'],answer:0,why:'Mental accounting is worth keeping where it improves behaviour and worth dropping where the inefficiency is genuinely expensive, which is usually when high-interest debt is involved.'}]}},'what-predicts-saving_5':{pictureCaption:'A wide, easy slot on top and a stiff, awkward handle on the door. Easy to put money in, slow to take it out.',bridge:'Idea 1 made paying in automatic. This one adds the other half: slow down the way out.',basics:{title:'Easy to put in, slow to take out',beats:[{kind:'picture',kicker:'A STRANGE MONEY BOX',caption:'Look at this box. The slot on top is wide, so money goes in easily. The handle on the door is stiff and slow. That is on purpose.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'Where should a money box make things a bit hard?',options:['Putting money in','Taking money out','Both'],answer:1,reveal:'Taking money out. If putting money in is hard, you just don\u2019t save. If taking it out is a bit slow, you get time to think first.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Make saving easy, and taking money out a little slow. A short wait turns a sudden want into a real choice.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the wait works',text:'You choose to save when you are calm. You want to spend when you see something you like. A one-day wait gives your calm self a say. If you really need the money, you still get it.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'GITA\u2019S PLAN',text:'Gita keeps her savings with her mum, not in her room. To get them, she has to ask and wait until tomorrow. Quite often, by tomorrow, she does not want the toy any more.'},{kind:'try',text:'Keep your savings somewhere that takes a day to reach, like with a grown-up you trust. Keep only your spending money close by.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Which plan helps Kai save the most?',options:['Money goes in easily, but taking it out means waiting a day','He has to walk to the bank each time he adds money','His savings sit in his wallet next to his spending money'],answer:0,why:'Saving is easy, and the wait gives Kai time to think before he spends. A long walk to pay in would just stop him saving.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'WHERE TO ADD FRICTION',prompt:'You are designing a savings account to help people save more. Where should a small hassle go?',options:['On paying in','On taking money out','Nowhere: make both effortless'],answer:1,reveal:'Schemes that made saving hard work failed. Schemes that made saving automatic and taking money out a little awkward did better. It is the design principle most often got backwards.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Put the friction on withdrawal, not deposit. An account that works is easy to pay into and a little awkward to take out of. A small, well-placed obstacle tends to beat encouragement.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Two decisions, two states',text:'Paying in is decided calmly and in advance. Taking money out is decided under pressure. Something has gone wrong, or something is wanted. The friction belongs on the decision made in the worse state.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A wide slot on top and a stiff, slow handle on the door. Easy in, awkward out: the friction sits where the impulse is.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Building it',steps:['Automate the deposit, so paying in needs nothing from you.','Keep the savings with a different provider from your everyday account.','Check that a withdrawal takes about a day to arrive.','Leave everyday money easy to reach.']},{kind:'explain',title:'How small is enough',text:'The inconvenience need not be large. A twenty-four hour delay is enough to turn an impulse into a decision. That is the whole mechanism. And when the withdrawal is really needed, a day costs almost nothing.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Your savings sit in an account you can empty instantly from your phone. What change does the idea recommend?',options:['Move them somewhere that takes a day to withdraw from','Add a fee each time you pay money into the account','Check your balance daily to keep yourself motivated','Lock the money away so it cannot be touched for years'],answer:0,why:'Friction belongs on withdrawal, and it only needs to be mild. A day\u2019s delay turns an impulse into a decision. A lock for years is far more than the mechanism needs.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'A SALE ENDS AT MIDNIGHT',text:'It is late, the sale ends at midnight, and your savings are one tap away. Months ago, calmly and in advance, you decided what that money was for. Now the decision is being taken again, under pressure, in the worst state to make it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Friction should sit on withdrawal, not deposit, because the two decisions are made in different states. The principle most often got backwards is also the simplest: easy in, mildly inconvenient out.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'THE TWO DECISIONS',left:{title:'Depositing',items:['Decided calmly','Decided in advance','Friction here stops the saving']},right:{title:'Withdrawing',items:['Decided under pressure','Something has gone wrong, or is wanted','Friction here creates a pause']},note:'The same small obstacle helps on one side and does harm on the other.'},{kind:'term',word:'Friction',meaning:'A small cost in effort or time attached to an action: a form, a delay, a transfer to another institution. Friction forbids nothing. It changes how easily something happens.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The evidence the piece cites',text:'Schemes that made saving effortful failed. Schemes that made saving automatic and withdrawal mildly inconvenient, through a separate institution or a day\u2019s delay, performed better. The piece says a small, well-placed obstacle tended to outperform encouragement.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A wide slot and a stiff handle. The design does not stop the money coming out. It makes taking it out a decision.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The piece does not list the schemes it compares, so *tended to outperform* is a summary, not a tally. A day\u2019s delay works against impulse, not against need: when money is short, friction on the way out may only push someone toward costlier borrowing. As idea 3 warned, the design presumes slack. Heavier locks are a different thing, with real costs in an emergency.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE BACKWARDS DESIGN',prompt:'Which savings design gets the principle backwards?',options:['Paying in needs a branch visit; withdrawals are instant online','Pay goes in automatically; withdrawals take a day to clear','Savings sit at a separate bank from the everyday account','Transfers in are automatic; taking money out needs a short form'],answer:0,why:'It puts the friction on the calm, advance decision and none on the pressured one. That is the reverse of what the piece recommends.'}]}}}},3037,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'what-sleep-debt-does_1':{pictureCaption:'Smoke rises from the toaster, and the alarm on the ceiling has no battery in it. The warning you rely on is what failed.',basics:{title:'Tired people can\u2019t tell how tired they are',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'A WEEK OF LATE NIGHTS',prompt:'You have gone to bed late every night this week. You feel only a bit tired. How well is your brain working?',options:['About as well as you feel','Worse than you feel','Better than you feel'],answer:1,reveal:'Scientists have tested people who sleep too little night after night. They feel a bit tired, and stop there. But on the tests, they keep doing worse and worse.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When you are short of sleep, the part of you that checks how you are doing stops working well. You feel a bit tired. You are doing worse than that.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The toast is smoking, but the alarm has no battery. Short sleep does this to the part of you that should warn you.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A STUCK FUEL GAUGE',text:'Picture a car whose fuel gauge gets stuck at half full. The tank keeps emptying. The gauge keeps saying half. The driver feels fine, right up until the car stops.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'HUNGER AND SLEEPINESS',left:{title:'Hunger',items:['Gets louder the longer you wait','Hard to ignore']},right:{title:'Too little sleep',items:['Goes quiet after a while','Easy to think you are fine']},note:'The quiet feels like you got used to it. You didn\u2019t.'},{kind:'explain',title:'So what can you trust?',text:'Not how you feel. Count instead. How much did you sleep last night, and the night before? That number does not get tired.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before a test or a big game, don\u2019t ask, \u201cDo I feel okay?\u201d Ask, \u201cDid I sleep enough this week?\u201d'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your brother stayed up late gaming three nights in a row. He says he feels fine for his match. What does this idea say?',options:['After short nights, how he feels is a poor guide','If he feels fine, then he must be fine','He would feel very sleepy if it were a problem'],answer:0,why:'Short sleep makes it hard to notice how much worse you are doing. The hours slept are a better guide than the feeling.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE THIRD SHORT NIGHT',text:'It is the third night this week you have slept five hours. You feel a bit tired, no worse than on Tuesday. You decide you have adjusted, and agree to drive a friend to the airport after work.'},{kind:'idea',text:'On restricted sleep, performance keeps dropping while your sense of how alert you are levels off at \u201ca bit tired\u201d. The instrument you would use to check on yourself is the one that breaks first.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the studies keep finding',text:'The essay rests on a pattern in restricted-sleep studies. That performance drops is expected. What stands out is that people\u2019s ratings of their own alertness stop dropping with it. They report being a bit tired while their measured performance keeps sliding.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHY IT FEELS SURVIVABLE',nodes:['Sleep is cut short, night after night','Performance keeps sliding','Felt tiredness levels off','The quiet feels like adapting'],note:'Hunger gets louder the longer it goes. Sleep debt goes quiet, which is what makes it misleading.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Smoke reaching the ceiling, and an alarm with its battery gone. Silence is not the same as no smoke, and feeling a bit tired is not the same as fine.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'After a few short nights, your body adapts and you get used to it.',reality:'What levels off is the feeling. You stop feeling worse while you keep doing worse, so the sense of having adapted is itself a product of the debt.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before something that matters, a drive, a decision, an exam, check the hours you have slept this week rather than how you feel. Let the number decide.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A colleague has slept badly all week but says they feel sharp enough to sign off a contract tonight. What does the idea suggest?',options:['Feeling sharp is weak evidence after a week of short nights','If they feel sharp, they are almost certainly fine','They would feel much worse if they were impaired','A strong coffee would settle the question either way'],answer:0,why:'After repeated short nights, felt alertness stops tracking actual performance. Hours slept is the better guide.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'TIRED VERSUS IMPAIRED',title:'The essay\u2019s distinction',text:'The essay separates two things people treat as one: feeling tired and being impaired. Its argument is that the gap between them, not the tiredness itself, is the dangerous part, because the gap is the one thing you cannot see from the inside.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Sleep restriction degrades the faculty you would use to notice it. Self-rated alertness plateaus at \u201ca bit tired\u201d while measured performance keeps sliding, so judging your fitness by how you feel fails just when it matters.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO MEASURES, ONE WEEK',left:{title:'How you rate yourself',items:['Drops at first','Then levels off','Settles at \u201ca bit tired\u201d']},right:{title:'How you actually perform',items:['Drops at first','Keeps dropping','Still sliding at the end']},note:'This divergence is what the essay calls the consistent finding in restricted-sleep studies.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The smoke is real; the alarm meant to report it has an empty battery slot. That is the gap the essay describes: the reading stays quiet while the problem grows.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it feels like adapting',text:'Most deprivations escalate. Hunger gets louder until you act. Sleep debt does the opposite: the signal goes quiet. A quiet signal reads as adaptation, so the longer the debt runs, the more confident a person can become that they have adjusted.'},{kind:'keyline',text:'The gauge you would use to check yourself is the first thing sleep loss breaks.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The essay\u2019s alternative, counting hours slept, is a proxy too. People differ in how much sleep they need, and the essay gives no threshold. The finding describes people in controlled studies, not a precise reading for you. And it cuts one way only: feeling fine is weak evidence of being fine, but feeling badly impaired is still worth trusting.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'When did you last judge yourself fit for something after a run of short nights? What did you base that judgement on?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE WEAK INFERENCE',prompt:'Which reasoning does the idea most directly undermine?',options:['Feeling okay after a week of short nights, so deciding you are fit to drive','Sleeping little all week, so handing the car keys to someone else','Feeling exhausted today, so resting before you get behind the wheel','Sleeping well all week, so looking elsewhere to explain feeling slow'],answer:0,why:'After repeated short nights, feeling okay is the very reading the essay says you cannot trust. The other three rely on hours slept or on a strong signal.'}]}},'what-sleep-debt-does_2':{pictureCaption:'The ball has been pumped part of the way, and the pumping has stopped. It is rounder, but the dent is still there.',bridge:'Idea 1 showed that feeling alert is a poor guide in a short week. The same gap opens at the weekend: you feel caught up before you are.',basics:{title:'A lie-in helps, but it doesn\u2019t fix it all',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A PHONE ON CHARGE',text:'Your phone is nearly flat. You plug it in for a few minutes. The low battery warning goes away, so it seems fine. But the battery is still only partly full.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE SATURDAY LIE-IN',text:'All week you went to bed late. On Saturday and Sunday you sleep in until lunch. By Sunday night you feel great. Back to normal, you think.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Sleeping in at the weekend helps, but it does not fix everything. You feel caught up before you really are.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Some air went back in, so the ball looks better. But see the dent? A lie-in is like that. It helps, but it does not fill you up.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Fast and slow',text:'Feeling awake comes back fast. Being able to keep your mind on one thing for a long time comes back more slowly. In some studies, it was still not fully back after a weekend.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE LIE-IN TRAP',nodes:['Late nights all week','A big lie-in at the weekend','Your body clock shifts later','Sunday bedtime is late, Monday is short'],loop:!0,note:'A huge lie-in can stop you feeling sleepy on Sunday night. So the next week starts short too.'},{kind:'try',text:'Keep count this week: how many school nights were late? A lie-in pays back some of that, not all of it.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Jay stays up late every school night. He says it\u2019s fine because he sleeps in at the weekend. What does this idea say?',options:['The lie-in helps, but it does not undo it all','The lie-in fixes it, so the late nights are fine','The lie-in does nothing, so he should skip it'],answer:0,why:'Weekend sleep pays back part of what was missed. It is a part fix, not a full one.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE WEEKEND RESET',belief:'Two long nights at the weekend cancel out a short-slept week.',reality:'Recovery sleep restores how alert you feel fairly quickly, which is why it feels like a reset. Sustained attention comes back more slowly, and in several lab studies it had not fully returned after a weekend.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Weekend recovery sleep restores some of the deficit, not all of it. The feeling of being caught up arrives before being caught up does, and treating a partial repair as a full one licenses a week whose costs are never cleared.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHAT RECOVERS, AND HOW FAST',left:{title:'Recovers fairly well',items:['How alert you feel','Some performance measures']},right:{title:'Slower, or unclear',items:['Sustained attention','Metabolic markers: evidence mixed']},note:'For markers such as insulin sensitivity, some studies find recovery after a couple of long nights and others do not.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Some air is back and the ball looks nearly right, which is when the pumping tends to stop. The dent is what a weekend leaves unrepaired.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The timing cost',text:'A large lie-in tends to push the body clock later. On Sunday night you are not sleepy at your usual time, so Monday starts short. The repair brings back a portion of the problem it was repairing.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO READ A WEEKEND',text:'You slept six hours a night from Monday to Friday and ten on Saturday and Sunday. Read as a reset, next week can run the same way. Read as partial recovery, you plan next week from what you actually slept, and the weekday pattern is what needs to change.'},{kind:'try',text:'At the end of this week, add up the hours you actually slept on weeknights. Plan the next week from that number, not from how Sunday felt.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A friend sleeps very little on weeknights and very long at weekends, and says the weekend squares it. What is the most accurate reply?',options:['It squares part of it, and the lie-ins may make Mondays harder','It squares all of it, as long as the total hours add up','It squares nothing, so the lie-ins are simply pointless','It squares it, provided Sunday evening feels refreshed'],answer:0,why:'Recovery is real but partial, and big lie-ins push the body clock later. Feeling refreshed on Sunday is the part that recovers first.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'SUNDAY EVENING',prompt:'After a short-slept week and two long weekend nights, which is most likely back to normal by Sunday?',options:['How alert you feel','Your sustained attention','Both, equally'],answer:0,reveal:'Subjective alertness recovers fairly quickly. Sustained attention comes back more slowly, and in several lab studies it had not fully returned after a weekend of recovery.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Recovery sleep is partial and uneven. The feeling of being restored returns first, some measures lag, and the lie-in itself shifts the body clock, so the repair brings back part of the problem.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Idea 1, at the weekend',text:'This is the gap from idea 1 in a new setting. During the week, felt alertness stops falling while performance keeps falling. At the weekend, felt alertness comes back first. Either way the feeling runs ahead of the facts, and decisions made on it are made too early.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Pumped partway, the ball firms up long before it is round. The dent is the part of the deficit still there when the pumping stops.'},{kind:'term',word:'Body clock',meaning:'The internal daily rhythm that makes you sleepy and alert at roughly the same times each day. Sleeping much later than usual tends to push it later, so the next bedtime comes late too.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'A PARTIAL REPAIR',nodes:['Short weeknights build a deficit','A large weekend lie-in','The body clock shifts later','Monday starts short'],loop:!0,note:'The lie-in pays back part of the debt and adds a little to the next week\u2019s.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The essay is careful here. Recovery sleep is not pointless, and the metabolic evidence is genuinely mixed: some studies find markers like insulin sensitivity recover after a couple of long nights, others do not. The safest claim is the narrow one: the sense of being restored is not proof of it.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Weekend sleep repairs part of a weekday deficit.','Feeling caught up arrives before being caught up.','Big lie-ins shift the body clock and make Monday harder.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE OVERREACH',prompt:'Which conclusion goes beyond what the essay supports?',options:['Weekend lie-ins are pointless, so they should be skipped','Weekend recovery restores some of a deficit, not all','Feeling refreshed on Sunday does not prove full recovery','The metabolic effects of recovery sleep are still debated'],answer:0,why:'The essay says recovery is partial, not pointless. The error it warns against is treating it as a full reset.'}]}},'what-sleep-debt-does_3':{pictureCaption:'A loaf with its end cut away, and the end is not like the rest. A short night loses one end, not an even slice.',bridge:'Ideas 1 and 2 counted sleep in hours. This one asks which hours, because they are not all alike.',basics:{title:'Not every hour of sleep is the same',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE EARLY SCHOOL TRIP',text:'You usually sleep until seven. For a school trip, you get up at five. You lost two hours. But which two? Not a little bit from all over the night. You lost the last part.'},{kind:'explain',title:'A night has parts',text:'Sleep is not the same all night. Early on, you get lots of very deep sleep. Near morning, you get more REM sleep. That is when most of your vivid dreams happen. REM is named after the quick eye movements that come with it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A night of sleep has different parts, and they seem to do different jobs. If you wake up early, you mostly lose the dreaming part near the end.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A loaf with the end cut off. The end piece is not like the rest. A short night loses one end, not a bit of everything.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'EARLY AND LATE',left:{title:'Early in the night',items:['Lots of deep sleep']},right:{title:'Near morning',items:['Lots of dreaming (REM) sleep']},note:'Scientists have linked both kinds to memory, and dreaming sleep to feelings. They are still working out the details.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of your last very early morning. Which part of the night did you lose?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Sam wakes up two hours early for a match. Which part of the night did he mostly lose?',options:['The part near morning, with lots of dreaming sleep','A little bit from every part of the night','The deep sleep from the start of the night'],answer:0,why:'An early alarm cuts off the end of the night, and the end has lots of dreaming sleep.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE EARLY FLIGHT',prompt:'You normally sleep from eleven to seven. For an early flight you are up at five. What did you lose?',options:['Two average hours of sleep','Mostly the REM-rich end of the night','Mostly deep sleep'],answer:1,reveal:'Sleep is not uniform. Deep slow-wave sleep is concentrated early, and the share of REM rises towards morning. Cutting the end removes mostly REM-rich sleep.'},{kind:'idea',text:'An hour of sleep is not an even slice of the night. Which end you cut changes what you lose, so a short night is not simply a smaller version of a full one.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'ONE NIGHT, END TO END',low:'Start: deep slow-wave sleep is concentrated here',high:'Towards morning: REM, in longer stretches',note:'An early alarm cuts from the right-hand end. What a late bedtime removes is less clear-cut than popular accounts suggest.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it matters what is cut',text:'The stages appear to do different work. Both deep sleep and REM have been linked to memory, and REM to emotional processing. So two hours off the end is not the same loss as two hours spread evenly across the night.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A loaf with its end cut clean off, and the end piece is different from the rest. Cutting a night short takes one end, not an average slice.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Six hours is just a slightly smaller version of eight.',reality:'Six hours that end at an early alarm are eight hours with much of the REM-rich end removed. The total is smaller, and the mix is different too.'},{kind:'try',text:'When you think back on a short night, note which end was cut: a late bedtime or an early alarm. They are not the same loss.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Two people each sleep six hours. One went to bed late; the other woke early. What does the idea say?',options:['They may have lost different kinds of sleep, not just the same amount','They had identical nights, since the total is exactly the same','The early riser lost mostly deep sleep from the start','Only the total counts, so neither is any worse off'],answer:0,why:'An early alarm removes mostly REM-rich sleep. What a late bedtime removes is less clear-cut, but the hours are not interchangeable.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'A WORD FOR THE SHAPE',word:'Sleep architecture',meaning:'The structure of a night: the order and proportion of sleep stages through it. Deep slow-wave sleep is concentrated early; REM comes later, in longer stretches towards morning.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Because sleep is structured, the hour you lose is taken disproportionately from one part of the night. An early alarm removes mostly REM-rich sleep, so a short night is not a scaled-down full one.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Careful verbs',text:'The essay chooses its words with care. Both deep sleep and REM have been *linked* to memory, and REM to emotional processing. Linked, not shown to do the job alone: the mapping between stages and functions is less clean than popular accounts suggest.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO LOSE AN HOUR',left:{title:'Early alarm',items:['Cuts the end of the night','Removes mostly REM-rich sleep','Fairly clear']},right:{title:'Late bedtime',items:['Cuts into the start','What is lost is less clear-cut','Popular accounts overstate it']},note:'The essay separates what is known from what is merely said with confidence.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A loaf with the end cut away and coloured apart from the rest. The structural point: which end is cut changes what is lost.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The structural point is sturdier than any particular consequence drawn from it. The essay warns that popular accounts claim more than the evidence supports, about what each stage does and about what a late bedtime removes. Use the idea to stop treating hours as interchangeable, not to diagnose what a given short night cost you.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of your last short night. Which end was cut, and did you count it as a full night minus a little?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'OVER-READING IT',prompt:'Which claim goes further than the essay allows?',options:['Losing REM on Tuesday is why you felt irritable on Wednesday','An early alarm removes mostly REM-rich sleep','A short night is not simply a smaller full night','What a late bedtime removes is less clear-cut'],answer:0,why:'The essay says the stages are linked to functions, and that the mapping is not clean. Tracing one mood to one lost stage claims more than that.'}]}},'what-sleep-debt-does_4':{pictureCaption:'A huge, heavy door and a small one on a simple latch. The hand goes for the small one: same times daily, no extra hours.',bridge:'Ideas 1 to 3 showed what short sleep costs. This one is the lever most people can actually pull.',basics:{title:'Same time, every day',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE MONDAY FEELING',text:'On school days you get up at seven. At the weekend you stay up late and sleep till ten. Every Monday morning feels awful.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Two ways to sleep better',text:'One way is to sleep for longer. That is hard. Homework, clubs and family plans get in the way. The other way is to sleep at the same times each day. That is much easier to do.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Going to bed and getting up at about the same time every day seems to help on its own. It is also easier than finding more hours to sleep.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A big, heavy door, and a small one with an easy latch. Same times every day is the small door. It is the one you can open.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Start with getting up',text:'Getting up at the same time is the easiest part to keep. An alarm can do it for you. Falling asleep is harder to control. So pick a wake-up time and keep it, even on Saturday.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick a wake-up time you could keep on a Saturday. Ask a grown-up to help you keep it this weekend.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Which plan follows this idea best?',options:['Get up at about the same time every day, weekends too','Sleep as late as you can on Saturday to catch up','Go to bed at a different time each night'],answer:0,why:'Keeping the same times is the easy lever. It can help without needing any extra hours.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE EARLY COMMUTE',text:'Your commute means leaving at seven, and your evenings are already full. Advice to sleep an extra hour lands as one more thing you cannot do. But you could get up at the same time on Saturday as on Tuesday. That costs no extra hours at all.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Sleeping and waking at roughly the same times is more tractable than adding an hour, and the evidence for regularity as a factor in its own right has grown. It asks for a schedule, not a sacrifice.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO LEVERS',left:{title:'More sleep',items:['Needs hours you may not have','Often set by others','Asks for a sacrifice']},right:{title:'Regular times',items:['Uses hours you already sleep','Often more adjustable','Asks for a schedule']},note:'The evidence suggests regularity carries benefit independently of the total.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why anchor the wake time',text:'The essay\u2019s instruction is to fix the wake time, weekends included, and let the rest follow. The wake time is the end of the night most under your control: an alarm can set it, while falling asleep cannot be ordered.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A huge, heavy door and a small one on a simple latch. The hand reaches for the small one: same times, no extra hours needed.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it survives real life',text:'Advice about getting more sleep is defeated by circumstances: a commute, a child, a deadline. A rearrangement of hours you already sleep asks for nothing extra, which is why it holds up when those circumstances arrive.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick a wake time you can keep on a Saturday, and keep it this Saturday.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Someone cannot sleep longer because their job starts early. What does the idea suggest?',options:['Keep the wake time steady, weekends included, and let bedtime follow','Accept that nothing can be done until the job changes','Sleep in as long as possible every weekend to make up','Focus only on finding one extra hour every night'],answer:0,why:'Here the length of the night is fixed but its timing is not. The evidence suggests regularity helps on its own, and it needs no extra hours.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE ONE-LEVER VIEW',belief:'The only sleep advice that really matters is to sleep more.',reality:'The essay argues for a second lever: regularity. The evidence for it as a factor in its own right has grown, and unlike duration it is often within your control.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Consistent timing counts independently of total sleep, and it is the more achievable lever, because duration is often constrained by things you do not control.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The argument from constraint',text:'Duration is often set from outside: a young child, a shift pattern, a commute. The timing of whatever sleep is available is often more adjustable. Advice that asks for nothing additional, only a rearrangement of hours already slept, survives circumstances that defeat advice about getting more.'},{kind:'steps',title:'The essay\u2019s instruction',steps:['Choose a wake time you can keep on a Saturday.','Keep it every day, weekends included.','Let bedtime settle around it.'],note:'The wake time is the anchor because it is the end of the night most under your control.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The heavy door is more sleep; the small latched one is regular times. The essay\u2019s case is that the small door is real advice, and the big one often an aspiration.'},{kind:'limits',text:'For some people timing is no freer than duration. Rotating shifts and night feeds set both, and for them this advice may be out of reach too. The essay also says only that the evidence for regularity has grown; it does not rank regularity above enough sleep. A regular short night is still a short night.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What sets the length of your nights, and what sets their timing? Which of the two can you actually move?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE HONEST OBJECTION',prompt:'Which objection does the idea have to take seriously?',options:['For rotating shift workers, timing may be as fixed as duration','Regular timing cannot matter unless total sleep rises too','People resent a schedule more than they resent a sacrifice','For most people, wake times are harder to fix than bedtimes'],answer:0,why:'The case for consistency rests on timing being adjustable. Where it is not, the easy lever is not easy.'}]}},'what-sleep-debt-does_5':{pictureCaption:'A shelf of confident-looking remedies. Not one comes with a gauge or dial to show whether it did anything.',bridge:'Idea 4 named one of the dull, well-supported levers. This one explains why the more exciting advice deserves less trust.',basics:{title:'Sleep tips: try them, don\u2019t trust them too much',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'A GOOD REASON',belief:'If a sleep tip comes with a good reason, it must work.',reality:'A good reason is a story about why it *should* work. It is not a test that shows it *does*. Lots of sleep tips have the story, but only small or unclear results.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Most sleep tips are worth a try, but not worth believing too hard. The tips with the best support are the boring ones: enough sleep, at the same times.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE BEDTIME STRETCH',text:'Your cousin says a special stretch at bedtime will make you sleep well. You try it, and still sleep badly. She says you must have done it wrong. Now nothing could ever show the tip does not work.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why that is a problem',text:'A fair test can come out either way. If every bad night gets blamed on you, the tip can never fail. And a tip that can never fail cannot be checked at all.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A shelf of bottles that all look like they work. Not one of them comes with a way to check.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before you try a new sleep tip, write down what would show it didn\u2019t help. Then try it for a week and check.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A friend says a bedtime song always helps you sleep. If it doesn\u2019t, you sang it wrong. What should you think?',options:['Be careful: nothing could ever prove that tip wrong','It must work, because the reason sounds good','Sing it more times until it works'],answer:0,why:'If every bad night is blamed on you, the tip can\u2019t be tested. That should lower your trust in it.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE SLEEP ARTICLE',text:'An article promises better sleep: a cooler bedroom, no screens before bed, a wind-down ritual. Each comes with a convincing explanation of why it should work. None mentions how big the effect actually was.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Much sleep advice is supported by mechanism rather than outcome: a plausible story about why it should work, not a trial showing that it does. Try it, but do not believe it hard. The best-supported advice is the dull kind.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO KINDS OF SUPPORT',left:{title:'Mechanism',items:['A story about why it should work','Easy to write and to believe','Says nothing about size']},right:{title:'Outcome',items:['A test showing that it does','Slow and dull to produce','Could come out against it']}},{kind:'explain',title:'The tell',text:'Ask what result would count against the advice. If sleeping badly after the routine is blamed on not following it properly, no result ever can. The claim has become unfalsifiable, and that should lower your confidence in it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A shelf of confident shapes, none attached to any gauge. Plausible remedies, with nothing to show whether they worked.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the shelf looks like this',text:'The better-supported items are boring: timing, light, caffeine and enough time in bed. They are less interesting to read about, which is roughly why so much of the advice you meet is the other kind.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick one sleep tip you follow. Write down what result over two weeks would make you drop it. If you cannot think of one, hold it more loosely.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A friend swears by a wind-down routine. When they sleep badly, they say they rushed it. What does the idea suggest?',options:['It may help a little, but this reasoning means it can never be tested','It is proven, since it only fails when done badly','It is worthless and should be dropped at once','It works, as long as it has a plausible mechanism'],answer:0,why:'Wind-down routines may have small effects. But blaming every bad night on how it was done means no result could count against them.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE LAST NOTE',title:'The essay turns on its own subject',text:'Having made its own claims with care, the essay asks how far to trust the much larger body of sleep advice. Its answer is that the category as a whole deserves lower confidence, and its reason is about the form the claims take.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A great deal of sleep advice is stated so that no result could count against it. Backed by mechanism rather than outcome, it is worth trying and not worth believing hard. The best-supported advice is dull.'},{kind:'term',word:'Unfalsifiable',meaning:'Stated so that no possible result could show it to be wrong. Not the same as false: it may be true. It just cannot be tested in the form given.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW A CLAIM SEALS ITSELF',nodes:['Follow the routine','Sleep badly anyway','You did not follow it properly','The claim survives untouched'],loop:!0,note:'Each failure is absorbed as user error, so no failure can ever count against the claim.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHERE THE SUPPORT IS',left:{title:'Better supported',items:['Timing','Light','Caffeine','Enough time in bed']},right:{title:'Plausible, small, noisy',items:['Wind-down routines','Screen curfews','Bedroom temperatures']},note:'The left column is duller to read about, which is roughly why the advice you meet is shaped the way it is.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Remedies with confident shapes and no gauge attached. The complaint is not that they fail, but that nothing is set up to show whether they do.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Unfalsifiable as stated is not the same as useless. Small, noisy effects can still be real, and the essay recommends trying these things. Its target is the confidence attached, not the practice. And the standard cuts both ways: a claim that sleep advice is mostly worthless would need its own test before being believed hard.'},{kind:'recap',points:['A plausible mechanism is not a demonstrated outcome.','If no result could count against a claim, hold it loosely.','The best-supported advice is the dull kind.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY THE TEST',prompt:'Which claim is closest to unfalsifiable as stated?',options:['The routine works if done properly, and a bad night shows it was not','A cooler bedroom reduced night waking in one particular trial','Caffeine late in the day delays sleep for many people','People who keep regular wake times report steadier sleep'],answer:0,why:'Only the routine claim is stated so that every bad night counts as user error. Each of the others could be shown wrong by a result.'}]}}}},3038,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'what-sleep-does_1':{pictureCaption:'Night in the archive: the day\u2019s loose papers carried from the tray into their proper slots. The learning goes on after the lights are out.',basics:{title:'Sleep helps you keep what you learned',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE NIGHT BEFORE A TEST',text:'You have a spelling test tomorrow. You could stay up late and go over the words again and again. Or you could read them once more, then go to bed on time.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'Which plan helps you remember more in the morning?',options:['Stay up late going over them','Read them, then go to sleep'],answer:1,reveal:'Scientists have tested this a lot. Things you learn just before sleep stay with you better than things you learn before the same time awake.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Sleep is not a break from learning. Scientists think your brain keeps working on the day\u2019s learning while you sleep, so it lasts. Learn it, then sleep on it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'At night, two hands carry the day\u2019s messy pile of papers to the right slots. Your sleeping brain does a job a bit like that.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How do we know?',text:'Scientists teach people something new. It might be a list of words, or a skill with their hands. Then some people sleep, and some stay awake for the same time. When they are tested, the ones who slept usually do better.'},{kind:'explain',title:'So what about staying up late?',text:'Staying up late gives you more time with the words. But it takes away the sleep that helps you keep them. That is why it is a bad swap.'},{kind:'try',text:'Tonight, read over the one thing you most want to remember just before bed. Then sleep. Check it in the morning.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Mia has a times tables quiz on Friday. When is the best time for her last look at the tables?',options:['On Thursday, just before she goes to bed','At lunch on Thursday, and not after that','Late on Thursday night, past her bedtime'],answer:0,why:'A last look just before bed lets sleep help her keep it. Staying up late would cut the sleep that does the keeping.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TWO STUDY PLANS',prompt:'Two people spend an hour on the same material and are tested twelve hours later. One studies in the evening and sleeps before the test. The other studies in the morning and is tested that night. Who remembers more?',options:['The morning learner, who had a whole day to let it settle','The evening learner, who slept in between','Neither, since both studied for the same time'],answer:1,reveal:'On average, the one who slept. Material learned before sleep is kept measurably better than material learned before the same stretch awake. It is one of the better-established findings about sleep.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Sleep is when the day\u2019s learning is stabilised and fitted in, not just rested through. Learning carries on after you stop, so when you study, relative to sleep, matters in its own right.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the evidence shows',text:'Two kinds of result support it. People improve more on physical skills and on remembered facts after sleep than after the same hours awake. And when particular stages of sleep are disrupted, particular kinds of memory suffer.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Trade sleep for study',items:['More hours with the material','Less sleep to stabilise it','Tired when it counts']},right:{title:'Study, then sleep',items:['The same material, reviewed in the evening','Sleep does the stabilising','Rested when it counts']},note:'The extra late hour is paid for out of the process that keeps the earlier hours.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The day\u2019s papers, carried to their slots after dark. Studying fills the tray. Sleep is when it gets put away.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick the thing you most need to remember this week. Review it in the last half hour before bed rather than at lunchtime, and test yourself on it the next morning.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You give a talk at nine tomorrow. You can do your final run-through at 2 p.m. or at 10 p.m., shortly before bed. What does the idea suggest?',options:['The 10 p.m. slot, so that you sleep on it','The 2 p.m. slot, while you are most alert','Both slots, then a third pass past midnight','Neither: time spent is all that matters'],answer:0,why:'Material reviewed and then slept on is kept better than the same material reviewed earlier in the day. A pass past midnight spends the sleep that does the keeping.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHERE THE PIECE STARTS',text:'This short piece sorts what is well established about sleep from what is only suggestive, and from the popular claims that have outrun the evidence. It opens with its strongest card: the effect of sleep on memory, which it ranks among the better-established findings in the field.'},{kind:'term',word:'Consolidation',meaning:'The process by which a newly learned memory is stabilised and integrated with what you already know, so that it lasts. The consolidation account holds that much of this happens during sleep.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Learning does not stop when studying stops. Material that is studied and then slept on is retained better than the same material followed by the same time awake, which makes sleep part of the learning rather than a pause in it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Two lines of evidence',text:'First, performance on motor tasks and on declarative ones, such as facts and word lists, improves more across sleep than across an equal waking interval. Second, disrupting particular sleep stages degrades particular kinds of retention. The second is harder to explain as mere rest.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Reasonably established',items:['Sleep after learning aids retention','It holds for skills and for facts','Stage disruption harms specific retention']},right:{title:'Still argued over',items:['Exactly how it works','How large the effect is','Why it varies so much by task']},note:'The scheduling advice rests only on the left column, so it holds whichever account of the mechanism wins.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The archive at night: loose papers moved from the tray into slots. A metaphor for consolidation, not a diagram of how the brain does it.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The piece is careful here, and so should we be. The mechanism is disputed and effect sizes vary considerably by task, so there is no fixed gain to promise. What survives is narrow: studying then sleeping beats the same study without sleep in between. It does not say that more sleep means proportionally more learning.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'READ THE CLAIM CAREFULLY',prompt:'A friend reasons that since sleep consolidates memory, sleeping ten hours will double what they learn. What is the best reply?',options:['The evidence is about studying before sleep, not about more sleep meaning more learning','Right: the longer the sleep, the more of the day gets filed','Wrong: the effect has only been found for physical skills','Wrong: nobody knows the mechanism, so the finding cannot be used'],answer:0,why:'The studies compare sleep with an equal time awake after learning. That supports scheduling study before sleep, whatever the mechanism, and says nothing about doubling anything.'}]}},'what-sleep-does_2':{pictureCaption:'Seven clocks, one for each night of the week. Six agree; the seventh is a quarter-turn out. The idea is about that night out of step.',bridge:'Idea 1 was about what sleep does. This one is about when it happens, which may matter as much as how long.',basics:{title:'Same bedtime, every night',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'TWO SLEEPERS',prompt:'Sam goes to bed at the same time every night. Jo goes to bed early on school nights and very late at weekends. They sleep about the same hours in all. Who is likely to be better off?',options:['Sam, with the same bedtime','Jo, who gets late nights for fun','Neither, since the hours are the same'],answer:0,reveal:'Probably Sam. Studies find that steady sleep times seem to matter, not just the number of hours.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When you sleep may matter as much as how long. Going to bed and getting up at the same times each day seems to help in its own right.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why would the time matter?',text:'Your body has a clock inside it. It makes you sleepy at night and helps wake you up. Studies suggest this clock pays attention to your pattern, not only to your hours. A steady pattern is easy for it to follow.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'ONE NIGHT OUT OF STEP',caption:'Seven clocks, one for each night. Six are set the same. The odd one is like a late Saturday. Your body clock likes the six.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE WEEKEND LIE-IN',text:'Lots of people stay up late on Friday and Saturday, then sleep in. It feels like catching up. But a lie-in does not fully make up for short nights in the week. And it makes your pattern jumpy.'},{kind:'try',text:'This week, pick one bedtime and one getting-up time. Keep them on Saturday and Sunday too, or as close as you can.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Which of these is the steadiest sleep plan?',options:['Bed at nine every night, up at seven every day','Bed at eight on school nights, eleven at weekends','Bed whenever you feel tired that day'],answer:0,why:'The same times every day, weekends too, give your body clock a steady pattern. That steadiness seems to help on its own.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'A FAMILIAR WEEK',text:'Six hours on each of five work nights, then nine and a half on Friday and Saturday. Add it up: thirty plus nineteen is forty-nine hours, an average of exactly seven a night. On paper, a respectable week.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Regularity, sleeping and waking at consistent times, appears to matter independently of total hours. A steady seven hours is likely better than an erratic pattern that averages eight.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the studies find',text:'Studies that track how much people\u2019s sleep times vary find that irregular schedules predict worse health outcomes even when total sleep is adequate. That suggests the body clock is responding to the pattern, not only to the amount.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO MANAGE SLEEP',left:{title:'Protect the total',items:['Count the hours','Let the timing drift','Sleep in to catch up']},right:{title:'Protect the timing',items:['Fix the window first','Same times at weekends','Add hours once it is steady']},note:'Most people work from the left column. The evidence appears to favour the right.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Seven nights, seven clocks. Six agree and one has drifted a quarter-turn. The weekly total can look fine while the pattern does not.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why this is the easier lever',text:'How long you sleep is often set by things outside your control: a commute, a baby, a shift pattern. When your sleep window falls is frequently more adjustable, and appears to carry real benefit on its own.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'A weekend lie-in pays back the week\u2019s lost sleep.',reality:'Only partly. Weekend catch-up does not fully settle the account, and the swing in timing it creates is exactly the kind of irregularity the studies point at.'},{kind:'try',text:'Pick a wake-up time you can hold seven days a week, Saturday included. Hold it for a fortnight before you try to add any hours.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A colleague averages eight hours, but goes to bed anywhere between 10 p.m. and 2 a.m. What does the idea suggest she change first?',options:['Fix a consistent bedtime and wake time','Add another hour, since eight is on the low side','Nothing, since eight hours is the target','Track her sleep stages before changing anything'],answer:0,why:'Her total is fine. Her timing is not, and the evidence suggests that regularity carries benefit of its own.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'THE BODY CLOCK',word:'Circadian rhythm',meaning:'The body\u2019s roughly daily internal cycle, which governs when you feel sleepy and when alert. The circadian system is the machinery that keeps it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Regularity appears in the evidence as an independent factor, not simply a stand-in for getting enough. If so, the pattern of sleep matters as well as the amount, and a steady seven is likely better than a variable eight.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The evidence and the inference',text:'Studies tracking sleep variability find that irregular schedules predict worse outcomes even when duration is adequate. From this the piece infers, cautiously, that the circadian system responds to the pattern as well as the amount. Note its verbs: appears, predicts, suggests.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Six clocks agree and the seventh is a quarter-turn out. The claim is about that drift, not the hours: the body clock appears to respond to the pattern as well as the amount.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'WHAT YOU CAN MOVE',low:'Duration: often fixed by work, children, commute',high:'Timing: frequently adjustable',note:'The piece\u2019s practical case is that the reachable lever sits at the timing end, and appears to carry benefit by itself.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Inverting the usual habit',text:'Most people protect the total and let the timing drift: late nights, then a lie-in to repay them. The piece reverses the order. Fix the window first, weekends included, and only then try to add hours. Weekend catch-up, it notes, does not fully settle the account.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Notice what kind of evidence this is. Studies that track people\u2019s schedules are observational, and the piece\u2019s own later idea warns that such data cannot settle direction. Irregular sleep might harm health, or ill health and chaotic lives might produce irregular sleep. Hence the careful wording: may matter, likely better, not certainly.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Regularity appears to matter beyond total hours.','Timing is often the more adjustable lever.','The evidence is associational, so the claim is may, not does.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'WEIGH THE EVIDENCE',prompt:'A headline says irregular sleepers have worse health, so irregular sleep causes illness. By the piece\u2019s own standards, what is the fairest response?',options:['The link is real, but direction is not settled, which is why the piece says may','The cause is proven, since the studies allowed for total hours','The finding is worthless, because it comes from observational data','Timing cannot matter, because only total hours affect health'],answer:0,why:'Allowing for hours rules out one explanation, not every one. The piece treats regularity as a likely factor worth acting on, not a settled cause.'}]}},'what-sleep-does_3':{pictureCaption:'Every jacket on the rail is cut to one size, but the ruler is held against a real arm. Fit is measured on the person.',bridge:'Idea 2 set timing beside duration. This one asks how much duration you personally need, and how you can tell.',basics:{title:'How much sleep do you need?',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE MAGIC NUMBER',belief:'Everyone needs exactly eight hours of sleep.',reality:'Eight hours is an average for grown-ups. Some people need a bit more, and some a bit less. Children usually need more than grown-ups. It is not a rule for each person.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The number of hours is not the best test. A better test is how you feel. Do you wake up on your own? Do you stay awake and sharp all afternoon?'},{kind:'picture',caption:'All the jackets on the rail are one size. But the ruler is held against a real arm. A good fit means measuring the person.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO CHECK',left:{title:'The number',items:['Hours on a clock or an app','The same for everyone','Easy to worry about']},right:{title:'The two questions',items:['Did I wake up before my alarm?','Was I sharp all afternoon?','Only you can answer']}},{kind:'explain',title:'Why chasing a number goes wrong',text:'Some people lie in bed and worry. Am I getting enough hours? But worry keeps you awake. So the worry makes the number worse, not better.'},{kind:'try',text:'For one week, ask yourself two questions each day. Did I wake up before my alarm? Was I wide awake in afternoon lessons?'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend sleeps nine hours. You sleep a bit less, but you wake before your alarm and feel sharp all day. What does this idea say?',options:['You are probably getting enough for you','You should add hours to match your friend','You need exactly eight, no more and no less'],answer:0,why:'People need different amounts. Waking on your own and staying sharp are better signs than matching someone else.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE MORNING REPORT',text:'Your watch says you slept seven hours and twelve minutes, with a disappointing slice of deep sleep. You felt fine when you woke. Now you are not sure, and you are comparing yourself with a figure meant for somebody else.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The eight-hour figure is a population average, not a personal prescription. The useful signal is whether you wake without an alarm most days and stay alert through the afternoon without caffeine.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'TWO SLEEPERS',text:'Someone who wakes naturally and stays alert on seven hours is not sleep-deprived. Someone who manages neither on eight is. The hours did not decide it. The two signals did.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What a tracker can tell you',text:'Consumer trackers are reasonable at estimating total sleep and poor at staging it. The light, deep and REM breakdown is inferred from movement and heart rate, and validation studies find it disagrees substantially with laboratory measurement. Treat the total as a rough guide and the stages as entertainment.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE METRIC BITES BACK',nodes:['A poor score','Worry about tonight\u2019s number','Lying awake worrying','A worse score'],loop:!0,note:'Chasing the number reliably makes the number worse.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A rail of jackets in one size, and a ruler held against a real arm. The average hangs on the rail. Your need is measured on you.'},{kind:'try',text:'For a week, ignore the tracker. Each day answer two questions: did I wake before the alarm, and was I alert at four in the afternoon? That is your sleep score.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Your tracker reports only 40 minutes of deep sleep, but you woke before the alarm and stayed sharp all afternoon. What should you conclude?',options:['You are probably rested, and the stage figure is unreliable','You are short of deep sleep and need to fix it','You should sleep longer until the deep figure rises','Your tracker needs replacing with a better model'],answer:0,why:'Both signals say rested. A stage breakdown inferred from movement and heart rate is the least reliable thing the device reports.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'A FIGURE MISREAD',belief:'Eight hours is what a person needs.',reality:'Eight hours is an average across a population. Individual requirements vary meaningfully around it, and an average describes a group, not the person in front of you.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Judge rest by its effects, not by a number. Waking without an alarm most days and staying alert through the afternoon without caffeine tell you more than an hour count, and far more than a device\u2019s stage breakdown.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What the device can and cannot see',text:'A wrist tracker does not observe sleep stages. It infers them from movement and heart rate. Validation studies find that the inferred breakdown disagrees substantially with laboratory measurement, while total sleep time is estimated reasonably. Hence the piece\u2019s ranking: the total is a rough guide, the stages are entertainment.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the two questions work',text:'They test the outcome sleep is for, not a stand-in for it. Someone who does both on seven hours is not sleep-deprived, and someone who does neither on eight is. Both questions are answerable without a device.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'The proxy',items:['Hours on the tracker','A population average','An inferred stage split']},right:{title:'The outcome',items:['Waking without an alarm','Alert at four, without caffeine','Answerable without a device']},note:'Optimising a metric is not the same as being rested. Chasing it can even make it worse.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One size on the rail, a ruler on a real arm. The average describes the stock. Fit is measured on the person.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The questions need honest conditions. Daily caffeine or a fixed early start can make them unanswerable, so they may take a deliberate week to read. Nor is the average useless: it is a fair starting point, just not a target. And the piece faults the stage breakdown, not trackers wholesale, since the total is still a rough guide.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE SOUND USE',prompt:'Which of these uses the idea correctly?',options:['Judging a week\u2019s sleep by waking before the alarm and afternoon alertness','Adding an hour in bed because the tracker shows too little REM','Concluding you need eight hours because that is the average','Dismissing your tiredness because the tracker says you slept eight hours'],answer:0,why:'The two outcome questions are the test the piece recommends. The others each let a number, an average or an inferred stage overrule how rested you actually are.'}]}},'what-sleep-does_4':{pictureCaption:'The coffee was drunk in the afternoon, but its shadow runs across the floor and into the dark bedroom. That is how far it reaches.',bridge:'Idea 1 said sleep is when learning is filed. This one is about a common drink that quietly cuts the deep sleep most tied to that filing.',basics:{title:'Caffeine stays longer than you think',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'FOUR O\u2019CLOCK COFFEE',text:'Your mum has a big mug of coffee at four o\u2019clock. Coffee has caffeine in it, which helps you feel awake. At bedtime she falls asleep fine. So the coffee must be gone by then, right?'},{kind:'explain',title:'Half, then half again',text:'Not quite. In grown-ups, about half of the caffeine is still in the body five or six hours later. Bedtime at ten is six hours after four. So close to half of that coffee is still there.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Caffeine stays in the body for a long time. A drink in the afternoon can still be working at bedtime, even if you fall asleep fine.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The coffee was drunk in the afternoon. But look at its long shadow. It stretches all the way into the dark bedroom.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The sneaky part',text:'Caffeine can cut down your deepest sleep. That deep sleep seems to matter most for keeping what you learned. But you may not notice a thing. You wake up and think you slept fine.'},{kind:'try',text:'Ask a grown-up which drinks at home have caffeine. Check cola, tea and energy drinks as well as coffee. Then try to have none of them after lunch.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your uncle says coffee at five never bothers him, because he falls asleep easily. What does this idea say?',options:['It may still cut his deep sleep without him noticing','He is right, so the coffee does him no harm','Coffee only matters if you drink it in bed'],answer:0,why:'Falling asleep is not the whole story. Caffeine can cut deep sleep while you feel you slept fine.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'I SLEEP FINE AFTER COFFEE',belief:'If you fall asleep easily after an afternoon coffee, it is doing no harm.',reality:'Falling asleep is not usually the problem: people with high tolerance often sleep fine. The effect that matters is less deep sleep, with no feeling that anything happened.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Caffeine\u2019s half-life is roughly five to six hours in most adults, so an afternoon coffee is still meaningfully present at bedtime. It tends to reduce deep sleep without making you feel you slept badly.'},{kind:'term',word:'Half-life',meaning:'The time it takes the body to clear half of a dose. After one half-life, half is left. After two, a quarter.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'DO THE SUM',text:'Coffee at 4 p.m., bed at midnight: eight hours, or about one and a half half-lives. Roughly a third of the caffeine, or a little more, is still circulating when you turn out the light.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it hides',text:'Slow-wave sleep, the deepest kind, is the portion most implicated in consolidating what you learned. Caffeine tends to reduce it measurably, often without changing the hours. Same duration, lower quality, and possibly no signal from inside that anything happened.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A coffee finished in the afternoon, and a shadow that runs across the floor and into the bedroom. The drink is gone. Its reach is not.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Test it on yourself',steps:['Set your last caffeine at least eight hours before your usual bedtime.','Hold that cutoff for two weeks.','Judge by how you wake and how alert you stay.'],note:'Metabolism varies a lot between people, so test the rule rather than adopt it wholesale.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You go to bed at 11 p.m. and want little caffeine left by then. Which plan follows the piece\u2019s rule?',options:['Last coffee by about 3 p.m.','Last coffee by about 7 p.m.','Any time, if you cut to one cup a day','Any time, as long as you fall asleep quickly'],answer:0,why:'The rule is a cutoff time, not a quantity: about eight hours before bed. A 7 p.m. coffee would still be more than half there at eleven.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'BEFORE THE NUMBERS',prompt:'Coffee at 4 p.m., bed at midnight. With a half-life of five to six hours, how much of the caffeine is still circulating at bedtime?',options:['Almost none','About a tenth','Roughly a third or more','All of it'],answer:2,reveal:'Eight hours is about one and a half half-lives. Half remains after one, a quarter after two, so roughly a third to two-fifths is still there at midnight.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The cost of afternoon caffeine falls mainly on sleep quality, not on falling asleep, and it can be invisible from inside. So the useful rule is a cutoff time, earlier than most people expect, rather than a limit on quantity.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where the damage falls',text:'The effect that matters is not usually trouble falling asleep. People with high tolerance often report sleeping fine. It is a measurable reduction in slow-wave sleep, the portion most implicated in consolidation. Duration unchanged, quality reduced, and no subjective signal.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHY IT GOES UNNOTICED',nodes:['Afternoon coffee','Still circulating at bedtime','Less slow-wave sleep','Same hours, no felt difference'],note:'Every step after the first is hidden from the drinker.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why a cutoff, not a quantity',text:'What matters is what remains at bedtime, and that depends on timing. The piece suggests roughly eight hours before bed. Even then, the half-life arithmetic leaves roughly a third of the dose or more, so the cutoff reduces the residue rather than removing it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'An afternoon cup whose shadow runs across the floor and through the bedroom door. The drink is finished. Its reach is not.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Individual variation in caffeine metabolism is large, so the five-to-six-hour figure and the eight-hour cutoff are starting points, not personal facts. The piece\u2019s answer is a two-week personal test. But a self-test cannot see slow-wave sleep either. It can only show whether the cutoff changes how you wake and how alert you stay.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'Someone says they drink coffee at six every evening and sleep eight solid hours, so the argument does not apply to them. What is the best response?',options:['Sleeping through does not show deep sleep was untouched, so a cutoff test is still worth running','They are right, since tolerance removes the effect of caffeine on sleep','They are wrong, since everyone clears caffeine at the same rate','They should cut the quantity, since the dose matters more than the timing'],answer:0,why:'The piece\u2019s point is that the cost is invisible from inside. Their metabolism may genuinely differ, and a two-week test of how they wake and feel is how to find out.'}]}},'what-sleep-does_5':{pictureCaption:'A small, genuinely good pot set on a plinth many times its size. The pot is the finding. The plinth is the certainty added to it.',bridge:'Idea 3 showed the eight-hour figure is an average, not a rule. This one turns that into a test for any confident sleep claim.',basics:{title:'Sleep facts that say too much',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'A MADE-UP HEADLINE',text:'Imagine a headline: Kids who sleep less get worse marks! It sounds as if short sleep causes bad marks. But wait. Could something else explain it?'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'Which other story could also fit the headline?',options:['Worry about school could cause bad nights and bad marks','Nothing else could explain it','Marks and sleep can never be linked'],answer:0,reveal:'Worry could cause both. Or bad marks could cause worry, and worry could cause bad nights. Numbers like these can\u2019t tell you which way the arrow points.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Sleep really does matter. But some famous claims about sleep say more than the facts show. Sleep is important enough to be told about truthfully.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A small, good pot on a huge stand. The pot is real and worth having. The huge stand is the part someone added.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Going together is not causing',text:'Two things can go together without one causing the other. Ice cream sales and sunburn both go up in summer. Ice cream does not cause sunburn. The sun causes both.'},{kind:'explain',title:'So should you ignore sleep advice?',text:'No. The main facts still stand. Sleep helps you keep what you learn. Steady bedtimes seem to help too. Just take care when someone sounds very, very sure.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A video says people who sleep less get ill more, so sleeping less makes you ill. What is a good question to ask?',options:['Could being ill make people sleep less?','How many people watched the video?','Is the person in the video famous?'],answer:0,why:'That is the arrow pointing the other way. If it could, the video has not shown which one causes which.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'AT THE DINNER TABLE',text:'A friend tells you, with complete confidence, that short sleep causes a long list of diseases. They read it in a popular book, stated as settled fact. You cannot tell whether it is true, and it turns out neither can they.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Popular sleep writing often claims more than its evidence supports. Sleep is genuinely important, and that is reason enough to describe it accurately.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How claims get inflated',text:'Three moves recur. Single studies are reported as settled. Correlations, such as short sleepers having worse outcomes, are presented as causes. And effect sizes are given without the confidence intervals that would show how uncertain they are.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'ONE CORRELATION, THREE STORIES',left:{title:'What the data show',items:['Short sleepers have worse outcomes']},right:{title:'What could explain it',items:['Short sleep harms health','Ill health disturbs sleep','Something else drives both']},note:'Correlational data cannot tell these apart. The confident version usually picks the first.'},{kind:'term',word:'Confidence interval',meaning:'The range of values the true effect could plausibly take, given the data. A wide range means the headline figure could be much smaller, or larger, than it sounds.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A modest, genuinely good pot raised on a plinth many times its size. The pot is the finding. The plinth is the certainty added to it.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'If popular accounts overstate sleep science, the science must be wrong.',reality:'The core findings are not wrong. The confident version circulating is stronger than the evidence, and knowing that leaves you better placed to act on what is actually established.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Suppose an article reports that people who sleep under six hours have worse heart health, and concludes that short sleep damages the heart. What is the most useful first question?',options:['Could poor health, or something else, explain the short sleep?','Was the article written by a well-known sleep expert?','Did the study include a very large number of people?','Is short sleep common enough for this to matter?'],answer:0,why:'Size and fame do not settle direction. The first thing to know is whether the data could separate cause from correlation at all.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'A CLAIM, TAKEN APART',text:'Take a typical line from popular sleep writing: short sleepers have worse outcomes, so short sleep causes them. The first half is a correlation. The second is a causal claim. Between them sit rival explanations the data cannot rule out.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Popular sleep writing overreaches in consistent ways: single studies reported as settled, correlations presented as causes, effect sizes without their uncertainty, averages sold as requirements. The core findings survive. The certainty does not.'},{kind:'term',word:'Reverse causation',meaning:'When the supposed effect is really the cause. If illness disturbs sleep, short sleep will track illness without causing it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Three rival readings',text:'Short sleep might cause poor outcomes. Poor health might cause short sleep. Or a third factor, stress or shift work for instance, might drive both, which is confounding. Correlational data are consistent with all three, so by themselves they cannot establish direction.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The quieter errors',text:'Single findings are reported as settled. Effect sizes appear without confidence intervals, which would show how uncertain they are. The eight-hour figure, a population average, is presented as a personal requirement. And the piece notes that a few well-known figures have been criticised for overstating such claims.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A modest, genuinely good pot on a plinth many times its size. The finding is real. The plinth is the overstatement doing the work.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Two misuses follow. One is to treat this as a reason to dismiss sleep research, when the piece insists the core findings are not wrong. The other is to apply it unevenly, doubting the claims you dislike and waving through the rest. The regularity evidence in idea 2 is observational too, and gets the same test.'},{kind:'keyline',text:'Sleep is important enough to be described accurately.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY THE TEST EVENLY',prompt:'A study finds that people with irregular bedtimes have worse health. A reader says correlational data prove nothing, so the study can be ignored. What is wrong with that?',options:['They cannot prove cause, but they are still evidence worth weighing','Nothing: correlational findings should simply be ignored','The study must be causal, since health was measured','Irregular bedtimes have already been shown to cause harm'],answer:0,why:'The piece targets overstated certainty, not evidence itself. An association that cannot settle direction is still a reason for cautious action, which is how it treats regularity.'}]}}}},3039,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'what-the-room-rewards_1':{pictureCaption:'The frame holds what the company says it values. The five hooks below are the last five promotions: what it actually does.',basics:{title:'Watch who gets picked',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE TEAM SHEET',text:'Your coach always says effort matters most. Then the team for the big match goes up. You think back over who got picked the last five times. They are not the hardest workers. They are the ones who score goals.'},{kind:'idea',text:'To learn what a place really rewards, don\u2019t go by what it says. Look at who gets picked. What they have in common is the real rule.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The frame is what a place says it cares about. The five hooks below are the last five people it picked. Look at the hooks.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'What the coach says',items:['Effort matters most','Everyone gets a fair chance']},right:{title:'Who gets picked',items:['The top scorers','Kids who play up front']},note:'When the two sides don\u2019t match, the right side is the rule that counts.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Is the coach lying?',text:'Probably not. The words are what the coach hopes the team is like. The picks are what the coach actually chooses. When they don\u2019t match, the picks tell you more.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A PATH IN THE GRASS',text:'A sign in the park says to keep to the path. But a muddy track runs across the grass. The sign says what someone wanted. The track shows where people really walk.'},{kind:'try',text:'Think of a club, class or team you are in. Who got the last five prizes or places? Write down what they have in common.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'A club says it cares most about teamwork. But the last five prizes all went to the fastest runners. What does this idea say?',options:['Speed is what really wins prizes here','Teamwork is what really wins prizes here','The prizes tell you nothing at all'],answer:0,why:'The words say teamwork. The prizes say speed. The prizes show the real rule.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'YOUR FIRST MONTH',prompt:'You join a firm whose values poster says collaboration, candour and customer focus. You want to know what gets people promoted. Where should you look?',options:['The poster and the staff handbook','The last five people promoted','What your manager says at your first review'],answer:1,reveal:'The poster describes how the firm would like to be seen. The promotions describe what it really chose. Where the two clash, the promotions are what is at work.'},{kind:'idea',text:'What an organisation rewards shows in who gets promoted, not in what it says it values. The posters describe an intention. The promotions describe a decision.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What to look for',text:'Take the last several promotions and ask what the people had in common. Not their qualities in the abstract, but what the firm could read off them: which projects they ran, how visible they were, and who backed them. The shared pattern is the real job spec.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The frame on the wall says what the company values. The five hooks beneath it belong to the five people it actually promoted.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'ONE PATTERN',text:'Say the poster says customer focus. Of the last five promoted, four led internal launches that senior leaders watched, and none worked closely with customers. That is not proof of hypocrisy. It tells you something: launches seen by leaders are what is being rewarded.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why so few people do this',text:'The facts are public. Anyone can see who was promoted. Almost nobody puts it together. That is why the stated values go on being believed.'},{kind:'try',text:'List the last five people promoted near you. Write down what they really share: projects, visibility, sponsors.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Your company\u2019s values stress mentoring junior staff. The last five promoted all ran high-profile projects and did little mentoring. Which reading follows the idea?',options:['High-profile projects are what is actually rewarded here','Mentoring is rewarded too, just more slowly than projects','The values are a lie and the company cannot be trusted','Five promotions are too few to tell you anything at all'],answer:0,why:'Where stated values and promotions clash, the promotions show what is at work. That is a description, not a charge.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE ESSAY\u2019S METHOD',text:'The essay opens with a method rather than an opinion. To read a company, ignore the values on the wall and study the last five people promoted. What they have in common is the real specification, and it is often not something anyone would say out loud.'},{kind:'term',word:'Revealed preference',meaning:'What someone\u2019s choices show they value, as opposed to what they say they value. The term comes from economics; the essay applies it to organisations.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Stated values describe how an organisation would like to be seen. The promotion record is its revealed preference. Where the two conflict, the second is what is operating.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The frame holds what the company says. The five hooks below, one per promotion, hold what it did. The hooks are where to look.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Legible, not abstract',text:'The method asks what was legible about the people promoted, not what they were like. Which projects, which visibility, whose sponsorship. Abstract qualities such as *drive* or *judgement* can be read into anyone after the fact. Projects and sponsors are on the record.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the posters survive',text:'The record is public and almost nobody assembles it. Each person sees one promotion at a time, usually explained in the language of the values. Only the pattern across several contradicts the poster, so the poster goes on being believed.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Five is a small sample. A pattern in five promotions may be chance, or the habit of one manager, so look further back and wider before concluding. What was legible is not always what was decisive: the people promoted may also have lived the stated values, unseen. And the method describes the room. It does not tell you whether to play to it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE OVERREACH',prompt:'You have studied the last five promotions. Which conclusion goes further than the method supports?',options:['The firm\u2019s leaders are deliberately lying about their values','Visibility to senior people seems to be rewarded here','The stated value of mentoring is not what decides promotions','Sponsorship appears in most of the recent promotions'],answer:0,why:'The method shows what is rewarded, not why. A gap between posters and promotions can come from drift or habit as easily as from deceit.'}]}},'what-the-room-rewards_2':{pictureCaption:'The finer piece sits under a cloth in the corner. The rougher one sits under the lamp. Whatever is in the light is what gets judged.',bridge:'Idea 1 showed how to read what a place rewards. Here is one of the first things that reading usually turns up.',basics:{title:'Work nobody sees doesn\u2019t get counted',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE GROUP PROJECT',text:'You and Max do a project on volcanoes. You find all the facts at home. Max holds up the poster in class. The teacher says, *Great work, Max.* Your part happened. Nobody saw it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Work people can see often gets more credit than good work they can\u2019t see. That is not fair, but it is what happens. So when you finish something, tell the person who needs to know.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The best piece is under a cloth in the corner. A rough one sits under the lamp. Guess which one people will talk about.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'So I should show off, or do messy work loudly.',reality:'No. Keep doing good work. Just don\u2019t hide it. A quick note to say *I did this part* is not showing off. It is just telling.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Fair, or just true?',text:'You can think this is unfair and still know it is true. Both at once. Knowing how things work is not the same as saying they are right. It just stops your work from being missed.'},{kind:'try',text:'When you finish something, tell one person who would never find out. *I found all the facts for our project.* One line is enough.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You spent all week sorting the class library. Nobody noticed. What does this idea say to do?',options:['Tell your teacher in one line that it is done','Make a big show so that everyone sees','Say nothing, since good work speaks for itself'],answer:0,why:'A short note lets the right person know. It is not showing off, and it stops your work counting as if it never happened.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'Good work gets noticed in the end.',reality:'Often it does not. Work nobody can see is scored as if it did not happen. This is not how it should work. It is how it usually does.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Visible work beats good work more often than anyone admits. The useful response is not to do worse work loudly. It is to notice that unseen work is being scored as zero.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Under the cloth, the finer piece. Under the lamp, the rougher one. Whatever sits in the light is what gets judged.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'The cynical lesson',items:['Polish matters, quality does not','Do less, announce more','Choose work for how it looks']},right:{title:'The useful lesson',items:['Quality still matters','Unseen work scores zero','Close the gap with a note']}},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE NOTE',text:'You spent three weeks fixing a reporting process that kept breaking. Now it just works, which means nobody thinks about it. A four-line note to your manager says what was broken, what you changed and what it saves. That is the whole fix.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Accept the description, not the value',text:'You can believe this is not how it should work and still act on how it does. Describing what gets rewarded is not endorsing it. Refusing the description on principle leaves your work invisible and changes nothing.'},{kind:'try',text:'The next time something you did is finished, send a short written note to the one person who would otherwise never learn it happened.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A colleague does careful, invisible maintenance and refuses to mention it, saying the work should speak for itself. What does the idea suggest?',options:['A short note when each piece finishes, to whoever would not know','Moving to flashier projects, even if they are less useful','Staying quiet, since mentioning work is self-promotion','Complaining that the process overlooks quiet people'],answer:0,why:'Work cannot speak if nobody sees it. A note closes the gap without changing the work or turning it into a performance.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO CLAIMS, KEPT APART',left:{title:'A description',items:['Visible work usually beats good work','Unseen work is scored as zero','Few admit it']},right:{title:'A response',items:['Not worse work done loudly','A short note when work finishes','Sent to whoever would not know']},note:'The essay keeps these apart on purpose. You can accept the first without approving of it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Accept the description of what gets rewarded without endorsing it, and act on the description. The action is small: make finished work visible to the person who would otherwise never learn of it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it is not cynicism',text:'The cynic concludes that quality does not matter and appearance does. The essay concludes something narrower. Quality still matters, but only work someone can see enters the scoring. The remedy changes what is reported, not what is done.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The finer piece under a cloth, the rougher one under the lamp. Nothing is wrong with the covered work. It is just not in the light.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the note takes this form',text:'It is sent when the work finishes, so it is accurate rather than remembered. It goes to one person, so it is a report, not a broadcast. And it is written, so it can be read later by someone who was not there.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The description can turn into an excuse. A place that scores only visible work ends up with too little invisible work done, and a team where everyone narrates may be one where nobody maintains. The note is meant to report work, not to choose it. Once visibility starts deciding what you work on, the description has become your value. And it is still worth arguing, where you can, that the scoring is wrong.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'What did you finish last month that the person who judges your work does not know about?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'WHERE IT TIPS OVER',prompt:'Which behaviour has crossed from acting on the description into endorsing it?',options:['Choosing only projects that leaders will see, whatever their value','Sending a short note when a long fix is finally complete','Telling a new manager what you delivered last quarter','Writing up the outcome of quiet work for your review'],answer:0,why:'A note reports work you would have done anyway. Choosing work for its visibility lets the scoring decide what gets done, which is the endorsement the essay avoids.'}]}},'what-the-room-rewards_3':{pictureCaption:'A solved mechanism, finished months ago, lying in a drawer. It works perfectly, and nobody outside the drawer has ever seen it.',bridge:'Idea 2 said unseen work goes unscored. This one asks why, and the answer is not unfairness but missing information.',basics:{title:'Missed is not the same as rejected',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'PICKING A HELPER',prompt:'Your teacher picks a class helper, and it isn\u2019t you. But you tidy the art cupboard every week after school. Why weren\u2019t you picked?',options:['She thinks your work is bad','She never knew you did it'],answer:1,reveal:'Most likely, she never knew. You tidied the cupboard after everyone had left, and nobody told her. That is not the same as saying no.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When quiet people get missed, it is usually not on purpose. The people choosing just don\u2019t know what they did. So help them know.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How choosing works',text:'People often choose in a room you are not in. They can only think of what they remember. If they never heard about your work, it does not come up. Nobody said no to it.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Something clever, finished long ago, kept in a drawer. It works. But nobody outside the drawer has ever seen it.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE HOMEWORK LEFT AT HOME',text:'You did your homework, but you left it on the kitchen table. Your teacher marks it as missing. She is not saying it was bad. She just never saw it.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Telling people what you did is showing off.',reality:'Not if you say it plainly. *I sorted out the art cupboard* is just news. It gives the people choosing something to remember.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Ana helped three new kids find their way around school. She was not picked for the welcome team. What most likely happened?',options:['The people choosing never heard what she did','They saw what she did and thought it was bad','They wanted to leave her out on purpose'],answer:0,why:'Most missed work was never judged at all. It just never reached the people doing the choosing.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'EIGHT MONTHS LATER',text:'Picture someone who solved a problem that kept coming back for the whole team, then mentioned it to no one. Eight months later their manager finds out by chance. She had not been ignoring them. She had no way of knowing.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A quiet person being passed over is usually not a deliberate judgement. It is an absence of information: the work never reached the person doing the judging.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW GOOD WORK DISAPPEARS',nodes:['Excellent work, never mentioned','A decision in a room you are not in','People discuss what they can recall','Your name does not come up'],note:'Nobody rejected the work. It simply gave them nothing to recall.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Finished months ago, the mechanism sits in a drawer. It works perfectly, and nobody outside the drawer knows it exists.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Legible is not the same as loud',text:'The remedy is not to become self-promoting. It is to make the work legible to people who were not there when it happened. That is a different act, and a far more bearable one for someone who dislikes talking about themselves.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE MINIMUM VERSION',text:'A short running record of what you did and what changed because of it. *Rewrote the weekly import; it has not failed since March.* Supplied to whoever is in the room. That is not politics. It is the input the process needs and does not otherwise get.'},{kind:'try',text:'Ask yourself: does my manager know what I finished last month? If not, send two lines today: what you did, and what changed.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A talented, quiet engineer is passed over again and concludes that her manager dislikes her. What does the idea suggest is more likely?',options:['Her work never reached the people making the decision','The manager judged her work carefully and found it weak','The process is designed to shut quiet people out','She has to become far more outgoing in meetings'],answer:0,why:'Most overlooked work was never judged and dismissed. A written record puts it in the room without asking her to change her personality.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',belief:'If someone is passed over, the people deciding must have weighed them and found them wanting.',reality:'The essay argues the usual cause is duller. Most overlooked work was never judged and dismissed. It simply never reached the people doing the judging.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Decisions about people are made in rooms they are not in, from what the people present can recall. Excellent, unnarrated work supplies nothing to recall, so it is not rejected. It just does not come up.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the reframe matters',text:'If being overlooked were a judgement, the remedy would be to change the judge\u2019s mind, or yourself. If it is missing information, the remedy is to supply it. The essay is careful here: it asks for legibility, not self-promotion, and the first is open to people who find the second intolerable.'},{kind:'term',word:'Legible',meaning:'Readable by someone who was not there. Work is legible when a person outside it can tell what was done and what changed because of it, without having watched it happen.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Finished months ago and still in the drawer. Nobody judged this mechanism and set it aside. Nobody outside the drawer ever saw it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Not politics',text:'The minimum version is a written record of what you did and what changed, supplied to whoever is in the room. The essay insists this is not politics. It is the input the process requires and has no other way of getting.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Sometimes the overlooking is deliberate. Bias, favouritism and dislike exist, and a record will not cure them; then the problem is the room itself, which the last idea takes up. Supplying information also takes time and confidence that are not evenly shared, so a process that relies on it favours those who report. The essay names a common cause, not the only one.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'ANSWER THE OBJECTION',prompt:'Someone objects that a written record of your own work is just self-promotion with better manners. What is the best reply?',options:['It reports what was done and changed, for people who were absent','Self-promotion is fine, because the process is unfair anyway','Records only matter at review time, so the objection is moot','Managers should find out for themselves, so it is optional'],answer:0,why:'Self-promotion argues for you. A record supplies facts the decision needs and would not otherwise have. The difference is what it contains and whom it serves.'}]}},'what-the-room-rewards_4':{pictureCaption:'A short written list handed across the table, and an open hand ready to take it. Not a nod or a pat on the back: conditions.',bridge:'The first three ideas were about reading the room from outside. This one asks it directly, and turns the answer into a specification.',basics:{title:'Ask for a list, not a pat on the back',beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO QUESTIONS FOR THE COACH',left:{title:'Am I doing okay?',items:['Coach says: *Yeah, keep it up*','Feels nice','Tells you nothing to do']},right:{title:'What would get me on the A team?',items:['Coach says: *Use your left foot more*','And: *Come to every practice*','Gives you a list']},note:'The left question gets a nice feeling. The right one gets a list you can work on.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Don\u2019t ask *am I doing okay?* Ask *what would I need to do?* The first gets you a kind word. The second gets you a list you can work on.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One hand passes a short list across the table. The other hand is open to take it. A list is something you can use.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why a list is better',text:'A kind word feels good for a minute. Then it is gone. A list stays. You can tick things off. You can bring it back next month and ask, *have I done these now?*'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE SCHOOL PLAY',text:'Priya wants a bigger part in the school play. She asks, *What would I need to do to get a speaking part?* Her teacher says: learn lines fast, speak up, come to every rehearsal. Now Priya knows just what to work on.'},{kind:'steps',title:'How to ask',steps:['Ask: what would I need to do to get there?','Write the answers down.','Work on them.','Go back with the list and check.']},{kind:'question',prompt:'You want to join the choir\u2019s solo group. Which question will help you most?',options:['What would I need to do to get in?','Do you think I sing well?','Am I good enough yet?'],answer:0,why:'The other two get a yes, a no or a *maybe*. Asking what you would need to do gets a list you can work on.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE ONE-TO-ONE',text:'You ask your manager whether you are ready for the next level. She says you are doing really well, it is definitely on the cards, and these things take time. You leave feeling good. A week later you realise you have nothing to do differently.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Ask what would have to be true for you to be promoted, rather than whether you are doing well. The first produces a list. The second produces reassurance, which is pleasant and useless.'},{kind:'explain',title:'How it works',text:'*Am I ready?* invites a yes or no, and usually gets a hedge. *What would have to be true for me to be at that level in a year?* is much harder to hedge. It asks for specifics, and specifics can be worked on. A judgement has become a specification.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A short list handed across the table, and an open hand to take it. Not *you\u2019re doing great*. Conditions.'},{kind:'explain',title:'It also tests the answer',text:'If your manager cannot name anything specific, that is worth knowing. It tells you something important about how the decision will actually be made, and you have learned it early.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'THE FOLLOW-UP',left:{title:'Kept in your heads',items:['Agreed in the room','Quietly renegotiated','Usually downward','Nothing to point at']},right:{title:'Written down',items:['Agreed and recorded','Returned to regularly','Changes are visible','Something to point at']}},{kind:'try',text:'At your next career conversation, ask what would have to be true for you to reach the next level in a year. Write the answer down, and return to it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Which question is most likely to give you something you can act on?',options:['What would need to be true for me to lead a team next year?','Do you see me as someone with leadership potential?','How do you think I have done so far this year?','Am I broadly on track for a promotion, would you say?'],answer:0,why:'Only *what would need to be true* requires a list. The others invite a yes, a no or a hedge, and none of those can be worked on.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'NO ANSWER IS AN ANSWER',prompt:'You ask your manager what would have to be true for you to be promoted. She cannot name anything specific. What have you learned?',options:['Nothing: the question did not work','Something about how the decision will be made','That you are not ready yet'],answer:1,reveal:'The question works either way. A list gives you a plan. No list suggests the decision may not be made against a clear standard, which is worth knowing early.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Asking what would have to be true converts a judgement into a specification. A specification can be worked on, checked and returned to. Reassurance cannot.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the wording matters',text:'*Am I ready for the next level?* invites a yes or no and usually gets a hedge, because a hedge costs the person answering nothing. *What would have to be true for me to be at that level in a year?* requires a list. It moves the work of being specific onto whoever holds the standard.'},{kind:'term',word:'Specification',meaning:'A list of conditions something must meet, written so that anyone can check whether it does. Here, the next level stops being a feeling about you and becomes a set of conditions.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Conditions, written and handed across. The open hand is not asking for a verdict. It is asking for something it can work on.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW A SPOKEN LIST SHRINKS',nodes:['Specifics agreed out loud','Months pass and memories differ','Quietly renegotiated, usually downward','Nothing written to point at'],note:'The essay says the follow-up matters as much as the question: write the list down and return to it.'},{kind:'limits',text:'A list is not a promise. Budgets, reorganisations and other candidates can change the outcome even when every condition is met, and a manager may name conditions they do not control. A specification can also narrow you, so that you meet the letter of the list and miss what the role needs. And in a room that rewards the wrong things, the list will simply describe them.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'WHEN THE BAR MOVES',prompt:'Your manager gave you three conditions. Six months later you have met them all, and she now mentions a fourth. What does the essay\u2019s advice equip you to do?',options:['Point to the written list you both agreed six months ago','Accept the fourth condition, since standards always evolve','Go back to asking whether you are doing well overall','Conclude that asking for a list was a mistake all along'],answer:0,why:'An unrecorded specification is renegotiated silently. A written one gives you something to point at, which is why the follow-up matters as much as the question.'}]}},'what-the-room-rewards_5':{pictureCaption:'A coat taken down off its peg for the last time, with the workshop lights still on behind. Leaving, after reading the room clearly.',bridge:'Ideas 1 to 4 taught you to read the room and work with it. This one asks what to do when the room is wrong.',basics:{title:'Sometimes the right move is to leave',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'JUST TRY HARDER?',belief:'If you don\u2019t fit in somewhere, you just have to try harder to fit.',reality:'Not always. Sometimes a place praises the wrong things. Then fitting in would mean turning into someone you don\u2019t want to be.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE CLUB',text:'At one football club, the coach cheers most for kids who shout at the ref and argue every call. You could learn to do it. You would get picked more. But that isn\u2019t what you want to get better at.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Seeing clearly what a place rewards doesn\u2019t mean you must do it. If what it rewards is wrong, leaving is a fair answer. It is not a failure.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO KINDS OF RULE',left:{title:'A rule you dislike',items:['The coach wants more passing','You like to dribble','But passing wins games']},right:{title:'A rule that is wrong',items:['The coach cheers arguing with the ref','Arguing helps nobody play','It does not help the team']},note:'Ask: does what gets praised help the team? If yes, learn it. If no, think about leaving.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Someone takes their coat off the hook for the last time. The lights are still on inside. They have decided to go.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Leave for a reason',text:'If you leave, leave because you looked carefully, not because you were cross one day. Then you know why you went. And you can pick the next club better.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'At drama club, the best parts go to whoever learns lines fastest. You hate learning lines. What does this idea say?',options:['Learn it, since quick lines really help a play','Leave, since the rule is wrong','Complain until the rule changes'],answer:0,why:'Learning lines helps the play, so this rule makes sense even if you dislike it. Leaving is for rules that help nobody.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THREE HEADS OF DEPARTMENT',text:'Picture someone who has read her workplace right. What gets rewarded is long hours in the office and agreeing with the director in meetings. The people shipping the real work are rarely mentioned. It has been this way under three different heads of department.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Sometimes the honest conclusion is that the room rewards the wrong things. Reading it right does not mean you must please it. If you leave, you leave on information, not on frustration.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Signs the room is wrong',steps:['The promoted behaviour is plainly unrelated to results.','The people doing the real work are invisible, time after time.','The pattern has survived several changes of leadership.'],note:'Any one alone may be passing. Together, they describe the room.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The cost of getting better at it',text:'If the rewarded behaviour has nothing to do with results, getting good at it costs you years. You spend them on a skill that transfers badly. The next place is unlikely to reward it, and the years do not come back.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A coat taken off its peg for the last time, the workshop lights still on behind. Leaving on information, not on a bad day.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'THE HARD DISTINCTION',left:{title:'Rules you dislike',items:['Legible','Linked to what the place needs','Learn them']},right:{title:'Rules that are wrong',items:['Legible too','Linked to nothing it needs','Consider leaving']},note:'The much more common case is the left one. The test is whether the rewarded behaviour connects to anything the place needs.'},{kind:'try',text:'Name one behaviour your workplace rewards. Ask whether it connects to anything the organisation really needs. Write down your honest answer.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Your firm promotes people who write polished strategy papers. You find the writing tedious, but decisions do improve after good papers. What does the idea suggest?',options:['Learn it: the rewarded skill is linked to results','Leave: you dislike what the room rewards','Ignore it and wait for the rules to change','Argue that writing should not count at all'],answer:0,why:'The rule is legible and connected to what the firm needs. That is the common case, and the essay says to learn it.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE THIRD NEW DIRECTOR',text:'A new director arrives, the third in five years. Within months the same people are being praised for the same thing: being seen to be busy late. The team that keeps the service running is, again, not mentioned. You notice you have started staying late too.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Reading the room accurately does not oblige you to satisfy it. Where the rewarded behaviour bears no relation to what the organisation needs, leaving is a legitimate answer, not a failure to adapt.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Where the essay ends up',text:'The earlier ideas taught you to read the room and act on the reading. This one sets the limit. Adapting is right when the rules are legible and merely disliked. It is wrong when the room rewards the wrong things, because no amount of adapting fixes that.'},{kind:'scale',kicker:'THE TEST',low:'What is rewarded tracks what the organisation needs',high:'What is rewarded tracks nothing it needs',note:'Near the left, learn it even if you dislike it. At the right, the honest move is to leave. The left is far more common.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The coat comes off its peg for the last time. The lights behind stay on; the place will carry on as it is. The decision is made.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The skill that does not travel',text:'The essay\u2019s sharpest point is about cost. Getting better at a game unrelated to results is a decision to spend years on a skill that transfers badly. And most people, it says, take considerably too long to reach that conclusion.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The test is easy to fail in your own favour. Frustration makes a disliked rule look like a wrong one, which is why all three signs matter: no link to results, invisible workers, survival across leaders. Leaving also has costs the essay does not weigh, such as income and dependants, and for many it is not a quick option. And a wrong room can sometimes be changed from inside.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'DISLIKED OR WRONG?',prompt:'Which situation most clearly meets the essay\u2019s test for leaving?',options:['Loyalty is rewarded over results, and has been under three directors','Sales figures are rewarded, and you happen to dislike selling','Written proposals are rewarded, and you prefer to talk','A new manager rewarded the wrong person once this year'],answer:0,why:'Only the loyalty case is unrelated to results and has survived changes of leadership. Sales and written proposals are legible rules you dislike; the new manager\u2019s choice is one case, not a pattern.'}]}}}},3040,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'why-feedback-fails_1':{pictureCaption:'A hedgehog rolled tight, and a strawberry held out right in front of it. Frightened, it takes in nothing, however good the offer.',basics:{title:'Scared ears can\u2019t hear',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'SAME WORDS, TWO PEOPLE',prompt:'Your best friend says your goal kick was a bit weak. Then a kid who teases you says the same words, in front of everyone. Which one do you really listen to?',options:['My best friend','The kid who teases me','Both the same, since the words are the same'],answer:0,reveal:'Same words. But with the teaser, you feel under attack. You get ready to fight back. You stop hearing the bit about the kick.'},{kind:'idea',text:'When advice makes you feel attacked, you stop listening. You defend yourself instead. So *how* advice is given decides if it gets through.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A HEDGEHOG',picture:!0,text:'A scared hedgehog rolls into a tight ball. You could hold out the best food in the world. It will not notice. It is too busy staying safe.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What it looks like',text:'You hear the first bad thing, and the rest goes fuzzy. Later you remember *how* they said it, not *what* they said. And you argue back instead of asking a question.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Telling a friend something hard',steps:['Find a quiet place, away from others.','Tell them you have something to say.','Say you want to help, not judge.','Give it time. Don\u2019t rush it.']},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your coach wants to tell you that you keep dropping your shoulder. When are you most likely to really hear it?',options:['Just the two of you, after practice','Shouted across the pitch in a match','In front of the whole team at half time'],answer:0,why:'In private, you don\u2019t feel shown up. So you are not busy defending yourself, and the advice can get in.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE SAME POINT, TWICE',text:'Over coffee, a peer you trust says your report buried the key number. You fix it that afternoon. A month later a director makes the same point in a team meeting. You spend the evening drafting a defence, and the report stays as it is.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Feedback that feels like a threat to your standing triggers defence, and a person who is defending is not processing content. The relationship decides whether anything gets through.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHAT HAPPENS IN THE ROOM',nodes:['Criticism lands as a status threat','Attention may narrow onto the danger','The content stops registering','A defence comes out, not a question'],note:'Afterwards the recipient remembers the tone, not the substance.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'CLOSED, NOT CARELESS',caption:'Rolled up, the hedgehog cannot take the strawberry, however good it is. A listener who feels threatened is in the same state: the offer is there, and nothing comes in.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why accuracy is not enough',text:'Correct feedback delivered into a threat state accomplishes nothing and costs the relationship. So the framing matters more than the accuracy. Not because truth is unimportant, but because it only counts once it has arrived.'},{kind:'steps',title:'The unglamorous fixes',steps:['Make it private.','Give warning that the conversation is coming.','Say plainly that you are there to help, not to judge.','Leave enough time that it does not feel like a verdict.'],note:'None of these improves the content. All of them decide whether the content arrives.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before giving difficult feedback, ask honestly whether this relationship can carry it at all. If it cannot, the first job is the relationship.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You need to tell a colleague that their email to a client was confusing. Which plan gives the point the best chance of being heard?',options:['Ask for ten minutes later today, in private, and say you want to help','Reply to all with corrections, so the whole team can learn from it','Mention it in passing at their desk, to keep things light','Write the most accurate critique you can and send it straight away'],answer:0,why:'Privacy, warning, a stated intention to help and enough time all lower the threat. Accuracy alone does not get the point in.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE ESSAY\u2019S PUZZLE',text:'The essay starts from a puzzle: organisations invest heavily in feedback and get remarkably little change in behaviour for it. Its answer is that the failure is usually structural, not a matter of phrasing. This first idea is the foundation: the state of the listener decides what is heard.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Feedback that triggers a threat response is often not processed as information, plausibly because the state that follows may narrow attention onto the social danger rather than the content. Framing therefore matters more than accuracy.'},{kind:'term',word:'Threat response',meaning:'The defensive state that follows when something is felt as a danger to your standing. In the essay\u2019s account, attention may narrow onto the danger, and much of what was said can go unprocessed.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the same sentence varies',text:'If the words carried the effect, a sentence would land the same from anyone. It does not. From a trusted peer it is information; from someone whose judgement of you has consequences, it can be a danger. Hence the essay\u2019s conclusion that the relationship determines whether anything gets through.'},{kind:'picture',kicker:'THE STATE OF THE LISTENER',caption:'Nothing is wrong with the strawberry. The hedgehog is rolled tight and takes none of it. In the essay\u2019s account, accurate feedback fails the same way: the listener\u2019s state decides.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHAT THE FIXES TOUCH',left:{title:'Improves the content',items:['Better wording','More accurate evidence']},right:{title:'Decides whether it arrives',items:['Privacy','Warning','A stated intention to help','Enough time']},note:'The essay calls the right-hand column unglamorous, and puts its weight there.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The idea can become an excuse never to say anything uncomfortable, or a way to read every defensive reaction as proof the delivery was wrong. Careful framing lowers the threat; it cannot guarantee the listener drops their guard. And framing cannot rescue feedback that is mistaken. The claim is that framing matters more than accuracy, not that accuracy stops mattering.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'Think of a piece of feedback you rejected. Do you remember its substance, or mainly its tone?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A manager\u2019s accurate criticism is acted on by one team member and ignored by another. By the essay\u2019s argument, what should she look at first?',options:['Her relationship with the person who ignores it','The precision of the wording she uses with each','Whether the one ignoring it is simply less capable','How much evidence she supplies for each point'],answer:0,why:'The same content works with one and not the other, so the content is not the variable. The relationship, and the threat it carries, is.'}]}},'why-feedback-fails_2':{pictureCaption:'The hand does not wave at the whole frame. One finger rests on one joint: this part, here. That is specific feedback.',bridge:'Idea 1 was about whether feedback arrives. This one is about what it should contain once it does.',basics:{title:'Say exactly what, not just \u201cgood\u201d',beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'TWO NOTES ON A STORY',text:'Your teacher writes *Good work!* on your story. Your friend\u2019s story gets: *The part where the dog runs off made me laugh. Add more bits like that.* Which of you knows what to do next time?'},{kind:'idea',text:'Vague words like *great* or *try harder* don\u2019t help. Useful advice says what you did, what it caused, and what to try next.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The hand does not wave at the whole thing. It points at one joint. *This bit, here.* That is what useful advice does.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'VAGUE OR CLEAR',left:{title:'Vague',items:['\u201cYou were great!\u201d','\u201cBe more careful.\u201d','\u201cThat was bad.\u201d']},right:{title:'Clear',items:['\u201cYour pass to Jo set up the goal.\u201d','\u201cYou missed two full stops on page one.\u201d','\u201cYou spoke fast, so I lost the end.\u201d']},note:'You can act on the right side. The left side is just a mood.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it hurts less, too',text:'*You are messy* is about you. *Your bag was left in the hall* is about one thing you did. The second is much easier to hear, even when it means the same.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Clear is not enough on its own',text:'Clear advice said in a mean way still feels like an attack. So it still gets shut out. Clear and kind work as a team.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your friend asks what you thought of the song she wrote. Which answer helps her most?',options:['\u201cThe chorus was catchy, but the verse went too fast.\u201d','\u201cIt was really, really good. Well done, you!\u201d','\u201cIt was okay. Maybe just try a bit harder.\u201d'],answer:0,why:'Naming the chorus and the verse tells her what to keep and what to change. The other two are just moods, a happy one and a flat one.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE SAFE DEFAULT',belief:'Warm, general praise always helps. It is criticism that needs care.',reality:'Vague praise and vague criticism are equally useless. Neither tells the person what to repeat or what to change. The only difference is the mood they leave behind.'},{kind:'idea',text:'What changes behaviour is a specific action others can see, its specific effect, and a specific thing to do instead. Everything else is a mood.'},{kind:'equation',parts:['The action','Its effect','The alternative'],result:'Feedback someone can use',note:'Name the behaviour and the occasion, not the person.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'BE MORE PROACTIVE',text:'*Be more proactive* gives the person nothing to do on Monday. *In Tuesday\u2019s meeting, raising the risk before the decision would have helped* gives them a moment, an effect and a different move. Same concern. Only one can be acted on.'},{kind:'explain',title:'It also takes the sting out',text:'A remark about a thing done is far easier to accept than a judgement of who you are. That holds even when the point beneath is the same. *You are unclear* is a verdict on you. *Section three lost the room* is a fact about one part of the talk.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'One finger on one joint, not a hand waved at the whole frame. Being precise tells the maker exactly where to work.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why specific is not enough',text:'Being specific does not replace care. Precise criticism given carelessly still sets off the threat response from idea 1. Specific on its own gives you feedback that is accurate, usable and still not heard.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'Which comment on a colleague\u2019s presentation is most useful?',options:['Your chart on slide four hid the trend; a line graph would show it','Great energy today, really engaging, honestly just keep it up','It felt a bit unfocused overall; tighten things up next time','You are a natural presenter, but do work on the details'],answer:0,why:'The slide-four comment names one action, its effect and an alternative. The others are moods, warm or cool.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'term',kicker:'THE WORD THE IDEA TURNS ON',word:'Actionability',meaning:'Whether the person receiving feedback can do something differently because of it. The essay makes this the main reason specificity works.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Specificity is the most reliable improvement to feedback: name the behaviour, the occasion and the effect, not the person. But it improves what is said, not whether it is heard, so it only works alongside care.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Two reasons it works',text:'First, actionability. A person told they were unclear cannot act; a person told which section lost the room, and what the audience appeared to think it meant, can. Second, it removes the identity charge. A point about a thing done is easier to accept than an assessment of who you are, even when the point is the same.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'PERSON OR ACTION',left:{title:'About the person',items:['You are disorganised','You lack confidence','You are a strong writer']},right:{title:'About the action',items:['You moved the deadline twice without notice','You let the client name a price first','Your opening made the case in two lines']},note:'The right-hand column can be repeated or changed. The left can only be argued with.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'A finger on one joint of a finished frame. Precision locates the problem, and leaves the maker, and the rest of the work, out of it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The qualification in the title',text:'Specificity helps, the essay says, but it does not substitute for care. Precisely stated criticism delivered carelessly still triggers the threat response of idea 1. The two work together; specificity alone produces feedback that is accurate, actionable and still not heard.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Specificity can be overdone into an inventory of every small fault, which reads as a case being built rather than help being offered. And some feedback that matters is about a pattern, not one occasion. The answer then is two or three specific instances of the pattern, not a retreat to a vague label about the person.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE MISSING HALF',prompt:'A manager has learned to give highly specific criticism, but delivers it in front of the whole team. What does the essay predict?',options:['Accurate, actionable points that are still not heard','Points that get acted on, because they are so precise','Defensiveness only where the points are inaccurate','Calm acceptance, since specific points carry no identity charge'],answer:0,why:'Specificity improves the content, not its arrival. Delivered as a public threat, precise criticism is still defended against.'}]}},'why-feedback-fails_3':{pictureCaption:'The bread is kept and the filling tipped into the bin. Wrapped in praise, the criticism is the part that gets lost.',bridge:'Idea 2 said what good feedback contains. This one looks at a popular way of packaging it, and why the packaging backfires.',basics:{title:'Don\u2019t hide the hard bit in nice words',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'HERE IT COMES',text:'Your dad says, *You are such a good helper.* You tense up. You know what comes next. *But\u2026* Your room. Your shoes. Something. Now nice words make you nervous.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The praise sandwich',text:'Some people use a trick for hard news. Say something nice. Then say the problem. Then say something nice again. The problem sits in the middle, like the filling in a sandwich.'},{kind:'idea',text:'If you always hide a problem between two nice things, people stop trusting the nice things. They just wait for the *but*.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The bread is kept. The filling goes in the bin. Hidden between nice words, the real message often gets lost.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO DO IT',left:{title:'The sandwich',items:['Nice, problem, nice','You wait for the *but*','Nice words stop counting']},right:{title:'Kept apart',items:['Praise when it is earned','Problems said plainly and kindly','Both are believed']}},{kind:'myth',belief:'Saying a problem straight out is just being mean.',reality:'No. You can be clear and kind at the same time. The sandwich is neither. It just mixes people up.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You want to tell your friend that the arms in his drawing are too long. What does this idea say?',options:['Tell him about the arms, plainly and kindly','Say you love it, then the arms, then you love it','Say nothing, so that his feelings are not hurt'],answer:0,why:'Plain and kind works best. Save praise for when you mean it, so it still counts.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'YOUR REVIEW BEGINS',prompt:'Your manager opens by telling you that you have done some really strong work this quarter. What is your honest reaction?',options:['Pleased: that is good to hear','Braced: what is the bad news?'],answer:1,reveal:'Many people brace. They have learned the pattern, so praise is heard as a warning. The compliment, which may well have been true, has stopped working.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Wrapping criticism in praise mainly teaches people to discount praise. Being direct and being kind are not in tension; the sandwich tends to manage neither well.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'HOW THE SANDWICH BACKFIRES',nodes:['Praise, then criticism, then praise','The recipient learns the pattern','Opening praise becomes a warning','All praise gets discounted'],note:'A genuine compliment has been turned into a signal that bad news is coming.'},{kind:'explain',title:'It hides the message too',text:'To fit the structure, the criticism gets softened, and recipients routinely leave believing the conversation was positive. The giver thinks the point was made. The receiver mostly heard the bread.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Lift the slices and the filling slides into the bin. Packed in praise, the criticism is the part nobody keeps.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'THE ALTERNATIVE',left:{title:'Sandwich',items:['Praise used as a cushion','Criticism softened to fit','Neither fully believed']},right:{title:'Separated',items:['Praise when earned, on its own','Criticism direct, with its own framing','Both heard better']},note:'The alternative is not brutality. It is not using either one to manage the other.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you have both praise and a criticism for someone, give them on different days. Notice how the praise lands when nothing follows it.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A team lead needs to raise missed deadlines with a strong performer. Which approach does the essay support?',options:['Raise the deadlines directly; praise good work separately, when it happens','Open with praise, raise the deadlines briefly, close with more praise','Mention only the strong work, and hope the deadlines improve','Put the deadlines in an email, so no conversation is needed'],answer:0,why:'Kept apart, the criticism is clear and the praise still means something. The sandwich would blur one and spoil the other.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'A WIDELY TAUGHT TECHNIQUE',belief:'Cushioning criticism between two pieces of praise makes it easier to hear.',reality:'The essay argues it often does not work as intended, for a reason that is easy to state: recipients tend to learn the pattern quickly, and from then on the cushion is itself the warning.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The praise sandwich turns genuine compliments into warning signals and blurs the criticism it was meant to deliver. Separating praise from criticism serves both.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Two costs, not one',text:'The first falls on praise. Once the pattern is learned, opening praise is received as a preamble to bad news, and people are trained to discount positive feedback generally, not only inside sandwiches. The second falls on the criticism, softened to fit the structure until recipients leave thinking the conversation went well.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What it was trying to solve',text:'The sandwich exists because people sense the problem in idea 1: criticism threatens. Its answer is to dilute the criticism. The essay\u2019s answer is to lower the threat by other means and keep the message whole, since direct and kind are not in tension.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The bread is kept and the filling tipped away. The structure survives; the message it was built to carry does not.'},{kind:'keyline',text:'Praise and criticism are each heard better when neither is being used to manage the other.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Separating the two assumes there will be another occasion for the praise. Where feedback is rare, the subject of idea 5, the separate praise may never come, and people hear only criticism. And naming a real strength in a hard conversation is not a sandwich when it is relevant to the point. The problem is the formula, not the presence of praise.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE SANDWICH',prompt:'Which of these is the pattern the essay warns against?',options:['Opening every critique with a compliment so that the critique feels softer','Praising a colleague\u2019s analysis on Monday and raising a late report on Thursday','Noting that a strong opening made a weak ending more noticeable','Giving criticism directly, while saying plainly that you want to help'],answer:0,why:'The trouble is praise used to manage criticism. Praise given on its own, or relevant to the point being made, is not a cushion.'}]}},'why-feedback-fails_4':{pictureCaption:'The blank page and the pen are pushed across the table first. The other notes wait at the edge. Their answer comes before yours.',bridge:'Idea 1 said threat closes the channel. Asking first lowers the threat: a problem someone names themselves is not an attack.',basics:{title:'Ask first, then tell',beats:[{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A GOOD DOCTOR',text:'A good doctor does not walk in and say what is wrong. First she asks, *Where does it hurt?* You know things she can\u2019t see.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Before you tell someone what went wrong, ask what they think. Often they say it first. And people don\u2019t argue with their own words.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'AFTER THE MATCH',text:'Your friend\u2019s team lost. You were going to say she stayed too far back. So you ask, *How do you think it went?* She says, *I stayed too far back.* Now you are on the same side.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The pen and blank page are pushed across first. Your own notes can wait. Their answer comes before yours.'},{kind:'explain',title:'You might learn something',text:'Maybe the coach told her to stay back. Maybe her boot was broken. Asking first can show you a reason you did not know.'},{kind:'myth',belief:'Asking first is a clever way to make them say what you want.',reality:'If you only ask to make them say your answer, they can tell. That feels worse than being told. Ask because you want to know.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your brother\u2019s cake came out flat. What does this idea say to do first?',options:['Ask him what he thinks went wrong','Tell him he forgot the baking powder','Say it looks great so he feels okay'],answer:0,why:'He may already know. Or he may know a reason you don\u2019t. Asking first helps either way.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'example',kicker:'THE PROJECT REVIEW',text:'A project went badly and you have three points to raise. You open with: *How do you think it went?* Your colleague names two of your three, plus one you had missed: the deadline was moved without anyone telling them.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Starting with the other person\u2019s own view usually surfaces most of the feedback you were going to give, from a source they will not argue with. It also tells you what you could not see.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why a self-named problem is different',text:'A problem someone has named themselves is not a threat. It is a shared observation. The conversation stops being a verdict and becomes two people looking at the same thing.'},{kind:'compare',kicker:'WHAT THEIR ANSWER TELLS YOU',left:{title:'They name the problem',items:['Awareness is not the issue','Ask what got in the way','Talk about capability and support']},right:{title:'They miss the problem',items:['Awareness is the issue','Now you tell them','Specifically, as in idea 2']},note:'Awareness and capability need different responses. Asking shows you which one you face.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The blank page and pen go across the table first. The other notes stay at the edge until the answer is in.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The failure mode',text:'Asking only to steer someone to your answer is a rhetorical question, and people detect it quickly. It is worse than telling directly, because it adds the feeling of being managed to the criticism itself.'},{kind:'try',text:'Open your next feedback conversation with *How do you think it went?* and listen to the whole answer before adding anything.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You ask a colleague how their pitch went. They say it went well, though you watched the client lose interest. What next?',options:['Ask what they noticed about the client, then share what you saw','Agree, since the point was to let them judge it for themselves','Keep asking leading questions until they name the problem','Drop it, since they are clearly not ready to hear it yet'],answer:0,why:'Their answer showed an awareness gap. One genuine question about the client, then your own view stated plainly, fills it. Fishing for your answer would feel like being managed.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE QUESTION THAT ISN\u2019T',text:'A manager asks, *So how do you think the launch went?* The tone makes clear there is a right answer. The employee guesses at it, guesses wrong, and is then told. They leave feeling criticised and handled.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Asking for the other person\u2019s assessment before giving yours changes the mechanics of feedback: it turns criticism into a shared observation and supplies information you lacked. It works only if the question is real.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Three things the question does',text:'Most people identify the problem you meant to raise, and a self-named problem is not a threat. Their answer shows whether the issue is awareness or capability, which need different responses. And it surfaces what you did not know: why it happened, that it is already fixed, or constraints you could not see.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Blank sheet and pen go across the table first; the other notes wait at the edge. The order is the method.'},{kind:'term',word:'Rhetorical question',meaning:'A question asked for effect, not for an answer. In feedback, it means asking in order to be told what you already meant to say.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The essay names the failure mode itself. People detect a rhetorical question quickly, and it is worse than telling directly, because it adds the sense of being managed to the criticism. Asking can also misfire with someone anxious and unaware, for whom a long silence to fill feels like a test. Then the kinder move may be to say it plainly.'},{kind:'ask',prompt:'In your last feedback conversation, did you want to hear their answer, or to hear them say yours?'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TELL THEM APART',prompt:'Which opener is most likely to work as the essay intends?',options:['\u201cBefore I say anything, how did that feel from your side?\u201d','\u201cDon\u2019t you think the second half dragged a little?\u201d','\u201cSo what would you say went wrong with the slides?\u201d','\u201cTalk me through why you thought that was ready to send.\u201d'],answer:0,why:'Asking how it felt is open and comes before your view. The others already contain the verdict, and the listener can hear it.'}]}},'why-feedback-fails_5':{pictureCaption:'At the far end, a heavy formal door: the rare, dreaded review. In front, an open doorway people pass through all day without a thought.',bridge:'Ideas 1 to 4 improve a single conversation. This one changes how often it happens, which changes what each conversation means.',basics:{title:'Little and often',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE BIG TALK',belief:'A big, serious talk once in a while is the best way to fix things.',reality:'A rare big talk feels scary. A quick word every week does not. Small and often works better.'},{kind:'idea',text:'If advice only comes once in a while, it feels like a big scary event. If it comes every week, it is just a chat. Little and often is easier to hear.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The big door at the end is the rare, scary talk. The open doorway in front is the everyday chat you walk through without a worry.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'TWO COACHES',text:'One coach says nothing all term. Then she calls you in for a big talk, and your heart races before she starts. The other coach gives you one small tip at every practice. You hardly notice it.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why rare feels scary',text:'When a talk only happens once in a while, just being called over tells you something is wrong. You spend the whole time wondering, *How much trouble am I in?* You miss the rest.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why often feels easy',text:'With a tip every week, each one is small. Mistakes get fixed early, before they grow. Nobody has to get ready for it.'},{kind:'try',text:'Helping someone with something, like a little brother\u2019s reading? Give one small tip each time, not one big talk later.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Your team captain wants everyone to get better. Which plan follows this idea?',options:['A quick tip for each player after every practice','One long talk with each player at the end of the year','No tips at all, so that nobody gets upset'],answer:0,why:'Little and often keeps each tip small and normal. A rare big talk feels like trouble.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWICE A YEAR OR WEEKLY',left:{title:'Twice a year',items:['Every instance is an event','Being called in is a signal','Big corrections, made late']},right:{title:'Weekly',items:['Each instance is low-stakes','Nobody has to prepare','Small corrections, made early']},note:'Same people, same feedback. Only the rate has changed.'},{kind:'idea',text:'How often feedback happens determines what each instance means. Twice a year it is an event, and events are threatening. Weekly it is a conversation. Often the more useful change a manager can make is cadence, not phrasing.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE CALENDAR INVITE',text:'A meeting called *Quick chat* appears in your calendar from a manager who has not given you feedback in five months. You spend two days reading the title for clues. In the meeting you hear the framing, not the content.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why rate beats skill',text:'Frequency may be one of the more promising ways to improve feedback, and it does not require anyone to get better at delivering it. When feedback is routine, no single instance carries much weight, so the temperature tends to drop on its own.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'At the far end, the heavy door of the twice-yearly review. In front, an open doorway used all day. Make feedback the doorway.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The catch',text:'It has to be genuinely routine. Short bursts of frequent feedback, started and dropped, are read as a campaign, which produces more threat than the rare version did.'},{kind:'try',text:'Start giving feedback weekly and informally, a sentence or two at a time, so it stops being an occasion. Keep it going when nothing is wrong.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'After a bad quarter, a manager suddenly gives one person daily feedback for two weeks, then stops. What does the essay predict?',options:['It reads as a campaign, and produces more threat than before','It lowers the stakes, because the feedback is now frequent','It works, provided that the daily feedback is specific','It has no effect either way, because only phrasing matters'],answer:0,why:'Frequency lowers the temperature only when it is routine. A sudden burst aimed at one person signals trouble.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'ONE CHANGE ONLY',prompt:'You may make one change to how a manager gives feedback. Which does the essay favour?',options:['Training in better phrasing','Making feedback weekly and routine','A standard written template'],answer:1,reveal:'Cadence. The essay suggests frequency may be one of the more promising changes, and it asks nobody to become better at delivering it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Frequency changes the meaning of each instance. Rare feedback is an event, read for how much trouble you are in; routine feedback is low-stakes, small and early. Raising the rate tends to lower the temperature.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHEN FEEDBACK IS RARE',nodes:['Feedback is rare','Being pulled aside is itself a signal','The framing is read for trouble','Content arrives into a threat state'],note:'This is the threat of idea 1, produced by the calendar rather than the wording.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it is structural',text:'The essay opened by saying feedback usually fails for structural reasons rather than phrasing. Cadence is its clearest case. The same sentence, delivered weekly, carries low stakes. Delivered after five silent months, it is a verdict before a word is said.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The heavy door at the end is an occasion. The doorless frame in front is used constantly, and nobody prepares to walk through it.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The routine must be genuine. Intermittent bursts of frequent feedback are read as a campaign, producing more threat than the rare version. Frequency also does not rescue bad content: weekly vague praise is still a mood, and weekly sandwiches still teach people to discount praise. Cadence lowers the stakes; ideas 2 to 4 still decide what is said.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Rare feedback is an event, and events threaten.','Routine feedback is small, early and low-stakes.','It must be genuinely routine, or it reads as a campaign.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'THE OBJECTION',prompt:'A manager objects that there is no time to give feedback every week. What is the strongest reply from the essay\u2019s argument?',options:['Routine feedback is small and needs no preparation, unlike rare reviews','Phrasing matters more than frequency, so time is not the real issue','Saving everything for one long review is the efficient approach','Feedback should only be given when something has gone wrong'],answer:0,why:'In the essay\u2019s account, routine feedback is small and neither side has to prepare. The rare kind needs preparation and carries threat.'}]}}}},3041,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SCRIPTS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={'why-you-reopen-the-same-tab_1':{pictureCaption:'The half-built machine went into a box. Now the hands lay it out again, piece by piece. That rebuilding is the real cost.',basics:{title:'The break is short. Getting back is not.',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'HALFWAY THROUGH A SUM',text:'You are halfway through a hard maths problem. You have done two steps. You know what comes next. Then your mum calls you to help carry the shopping in. It takes one minute.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'When you sit back down, how much time has the break cost you?',options:['One minute, the time you were away','More than one minute'],answer:1,reveal:'You look at the page. What was I doing? Why did I write that? You have to work it all out again. That part takes longer than the break did.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A break from a task costs more than the break. When you come back, you have to rebuild in your head where you were. The rebuilding is the big cost.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A BUMPED BOARD GAME',text:'Your little brother bumps the table and the board game pieces slide about. The bump took one second. Putting each piece back where it was takes much longer.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The machine was half built. Then it got swept into a box. Now the hands must lay it all out again, piece by piece.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why nobody notices',text:'Rebuilding looks just like working. You are at your desk. You are looking at your work. No one can see that you are only finding your place again. Not even you.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before you look away from your homework, write one line: what you were about to do next. When you come back, read it first.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You are writing a story for school. A friend messages you, and you reply. Where does most of the lost time go?',options:['Getting back into the story in your head','Typing your reply to your friend','Picking up your phone and putting it down'],answer:0,why:'The reply took seconds. Finding your place in the story again is the slow part, and it is easy to miss.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'FOUR SECONDS',text:'You are halfway through checking a set of figures that do not add up. You have ruled out two causes and have a hunch about the third. A message lights up your phone. You glance at it. Four seconds, then back to the sheet.'},{kind:'idea',text:'The cost of an interruption is not the glance. It is the reconstruction afterwards: rebuilding what you were doing, why, and what was about to happen next.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'What you counted',items:['Four seconds on the message','Straight back to the sheet']},right:{title:'What it cost',items:['Which two causes were ruled out?','What was the hunch about the third?','Minutes finding your place']},note:'The glance was the cheap part.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it does not shrink',text:'The rebuilding is the same job whether you were gone four seconds or four minutes. You still have to recover what you were trying to do and what you had ruled out. So the cost does not scale down with the length of the interruption. Estimates of its size vary. The direction does not.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why you never see it',text:'Reconstruction feels like working. You are at the desk, looking at the document, and nothing you could observe separates rebuilding from progressing. So a day of many short interruptions can produce very little while feeling fully occupied, and nothing tells you why.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before you look away from a task, write one line about what you were about to do next. It buys back most of the cost of returning.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'A colleague says quick questions are harmless because they only take ten seconds. What does this idea say they are missing?',options:['The time you spend rebuilding your place afterwards','The time it takes them to type out the question','The chance that your quick answer turns out wrong','The noise it makes for everyone else in the room'],answer:0,why:'The ten seconds are real but small. The cost they cannot see is yours: getting back to where you were.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE USUAL ACCOUNTING',belief:'A distraction costs the time you spend distracted.',reality:'The essay argues that this is the cheap part. The expensive part is coming back: rebuilding the state you were in. That is why a four-second glance can cost several minutes.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Returning to complex work means rebuilding a working state. That takes considerably longer than the interruption itself, it does not shrink when the interruption does, and it stays invisible because it feels like work.'},{kind:'term',word:'Working state',meaning:'Everything you were holding in mind mid-task: what you were trying to do, why, what you had already ruled out, and what was about to happen next. Almost none of it is on the page.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHAT A GLANCE SETS OFF',nodes:['Deep in the task, state held','A four-second glance','The state is lost','Rebuild: what, why, what next','Progress resumes'],note:'Only the second step shows up when you count your distractions.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it hides',text:'Rebuilding and progressing look identical from outside, and nearly identical from inside. You are at the desk, reading the document. So the cost never registers as a cost. A day broken into many short pieces feels fully occupied and produces little, and the person living it has no signal explaining the gap.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Nothing here was lost. It was only swept into a box. The cost is the work of laying it all out again.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The essay is careful about size: estimates of the return cost vary, and only the direction is consistent. The cost also depends on how much state there was. Routine work has little to rebuild, so this bites hardest on complex tasks. And it is not an argument that every interruption is waste. Some are worth the price. The point is to know there is one.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A team has two days. On one, a single ninety-minute meeting. On the other, thirty three-minute check-ins. Which day probably cost more focused work?',options:['The check-ins: thirty returns, each needing its own rebuild','The meeting: one long block uses up the most focus','Neither: both days lose exactly ninety minutes of work','It depends only on how interesting each meeting was'],answer:0,why:'The rebuild is paid on every return and does not shrink with a short interruption. Thirty returns mean thirty rebuilds.'}]}},'why-you-reopen-the-same-tab_2':{pictureCaption:'The hand is back at the door. What draws it is the thing on the shelf, still undecided. Shutting the door leaves it there.',bridge:'Idea 1 counted the cost of each return. This one asks why you keep returning to the same place.',basics:{title:'Why you keep checking the same thing',beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'JUST A HABIT?',belief:'You keep opening the same page because it is a bad habit.',reality:'Most of the time it is a choice you have not made yet. Your mind keeps going back to it, hoping to settle it.'},{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE PARTY MESSAGE',text:'A friend asks if you can come to her party on Saturday. You read the message. You don\u2019t answer. Ten minutes later you read it again. Then again at dinner.'},{kind:'idea',text:'If you keep going back to the same thing, there is a choice you have not made. Make the choice, or pick a time to make it. Then the going back stops.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The hand keeps opening this door. The thing on the shelf is why. It is a choice still waiting to be made.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A STONE IN YOUR SHOE',text:'Taking your shoe off and putting it straight back on does not help. The stone is still in there. You have to tip it out. Closing the page is like putting the shoe back on.'},{kind:'steps',title:'When you catch yourself going back',steps:['Stop and ask: what have I not decided?','Say the choice in a few words.','Make it now, or pick a time to make it.']},{kind:'question',prompt:'You keep looking at a game you could buy with your pocket money, but you never buy it. What is the best next step?',options:['Decide yes or no, or pick a day to decide','Close the page and try not to think about it','Keep looking at it until you feel sure'],answer:0,why:'Closing the page leaves the choice open, so you will be back. Deciding, or setting a day, ends it.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'THE FOURTH READ',prompt:'You have read the same email four times today and not replied. What would actually stop you opening it again?',options:['Closing it and archiving it','Turning off email alerts','Deciding what your answer is'],answer:2,reveal:'Closing it removes the email, not the reason you keep going back. The reason is a decision you have not made, and it will produce the urge again.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A tab you keep reopening usually marks a decision you have not made: whether to buy it, reply to it, believe it. Closing the loop closes the tab. Closing the tab does not close the loop.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'THE LOOP',nodes:['A decision left open','An urge to look again','You reopen it','Nothing is settled'],loop:!0,note:'Each return is an attempt to resolve what the last return did not.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Symptom, not distraction',text:'The pattern is familiar: the message read four times, the document you keep checking, the comparison you have looked at again and again. Treat the reopening as a distraction and you fight the urge. Treat it as a symptom and you look for the decision underneath.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Deciding later counts',text:'There are two ways out: make the decision, or schedule it explicitly, with a set time to decide. The essay says the second appears to work nearly as well as the first, and rests this on a fairly small set of studies about unfinished tasks that keep intruding on the mind.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE LAPTOP COMPARISON',text:'Two laptops have sat side by side in your browser for a week. The decision underneath is not which is better. It is how much you are willing to spend. Name that, set the limit, and the comparison stops pulling at you.'},{kind:'try',text:'When you catch yourself reopening something, ask what decision you are avoiding. Then make it, even badly, or write down when you will.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You keep checking a job advert you saved weeks ago. Which move does this idea recommend?',options:['Decide whether to apply, or set a date to decide','Delete the bookmark so the temptation is gone','Read the advert again until it feels clearer','Block job sites for the rest of the working day'],answer:0,why:'The advert pulls you back because a decision is open. Deleting it leaves the decision open; deciding or scheduling closes it.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'THE ESSAY\u2019S CLAIM',text:'The essay makes a diagnostic argument. People rarely reopen the same tab out of habit alone. The return is the mind going back to an open loop: something it holds as unfinished and keeps trying to settle. Read that way, the tab shows you where the unfinished business is.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Repeated returns are a symptom of an unresolved decision, not a distraction in themselves. Resolve the decision or schedule it explicitly. Removing the tab treats nothing.'},{kind:'term',word:'Open loop',meaning:'An item the mind holds as unfinished: a reply not sent, a purchase not settled, a claim not yet believed or rejected. It keeps prompting you until it is resolved or given a definite place.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Closing the tab',items:['Removes the cue','Leaves the decision open','The urge returns']},right:{title:'Closing the loop',items:['Settles or schedules the decision','Removes the reason to return','The tab can close itself']},note:'Only one of these acts on the cause.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why scheduling is enough',text:'The essay\u2019s most useful claim is that an explicit plan to decide later stops the returning nearly as well as deciding now. It rests this on a fairly small set of studies about intrusive unfinished tasks, so hold it lightly. The word that matters is *explicitly*: a time, and the question to answer then. A vague intention to think about it later is the open loop again.'},{kind:'keyline',text:'Make the decision badly rather than not at all.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Not every return is avoidance. Sometimes you reopen something because new information has arrived, and looking again is the work. Deciding badly is cheap for a reply or a small purchase and expensive for one you cannot undo; there, scheduling is the honest move. And a scheduled decision only stays closed if you keep the appointment.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The hand is back at the door. What draws it is the thing on the shelf, still undecided. Shutting the door leaves it on the shelf.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISREADING',prompt:'A friend reads this and decides to close every tab at the end of each day. What is the best reply?',options:['Closing tabs leaves the decisions open, so the urges come back','Good plan: a clean browser breaks the habit of reopening','Only worth it for tabs that were opened by accident','Better to keep them open so that nothing gets forgotten'],answer:0,why:'The tabs are the symptom. Each one returns until its decision is made or given a time.'}]}},'why-you-reopen-the-same-tab_3':{pictureCaption:'Half a page written, the pencil dropped, the phone face down and silent. Nobody called them away. They left on their own.',bridge:'Idea 2 traced one kind of return to an open decision. This one looks at the moment you leave in the first place.',basics:{title:'Who interrupts you? Often, you',beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'WATCHING PEOPLE WORK',prompt:'Scientists watched grown-ups working at their desks. Where do you think a big share of the stops came from?',options:['A phone buzzing','Other people talking to them','They stopped themselves'],answer:2,reveal:'A big share of the stops came from the person. Nothing buzzed. Nobody spoke. They just stopped.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Lots of the time, nobody interrupts you. You interrupt yourself. And it tends to happen just when the work gets hard.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'THE HARD BIT',picture:!0,text:'You are writing a story. The first part comes easily. Then you reach the bit where you don\u2019t know what happens next. All at once you feel thirsty, or you want to check one quick thing. It feels like a choice. It is really a way out of the hard bit.'},{kind:'flow',nodes:['The work is going well','You hit a hard bit','You want to do something else','You leave'],note:'The urge comes right at the hard bit. That is not by chance.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the hard bit matters',text:'The hard bit is where you learn the most. So leaving right then costs you the most. That is why it is worth learning to spot.'},{kind:'try',text:'Next time you stop your homework, ask: what was I doing just before? Was it a hard bit? Write it down. See if it is the same each time.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'Maya keeps stopping her reading to check her phone. What is the best first step, by this idea?',options:['Notice what she was reading just before she stopped','Ask her parents to take the phone away','Tell herself to try harder to focus'],answer:0,why:'The stops are not random. Spotting what comes just before them, like a hard page, shows her what to fix.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE PARAGRAPH YOU CAN\u2019T WRITE',text:'The report is going well until you reach the paragraph you do not yet know how to write. A few seconds later you are reading the news. Nothing pinged. It did not feel like running away. It felt like deciding what to do next.'},{kind:'idea',text:'A large share of interruptions are self-started, and they cluster at moments of difficulty. The switch arrives exactly when the work stops being fluent, which is also when it starts being valuable.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Outside interruptions',items:['Notifications','Noise','Meetings','What most tools target']},right:{title:'Self-interruptions',items:['Nothing prompts you','Arrive at the hard or unclear bit','Feel like a choice','What no tool addresses']},note:'In studies that watched people at work, a large share of interruptions belonged on the right.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The phone is face down and silent, and nobody else is in the room. The work stopped partway down the page because the writer got up.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it feels like a decision',text:'Leaving rarely feels like escape. You are choosing what to do next, and checking the news looks like a reasonable next thing. That keeps it invisible. The trigger is a few seconds of difficulty, ambiguity or minor tedium, and it is forgotten by the time you notice you have left.'},{kind:'steps',title:'Catching your trigger',steps:['Notice the moment you leave.','Note what you were doing in the seconds before.','After a week, look for the repeat.','Work on that one trigger, not on concentrating in general.'],note:'For a given person it is almost always the same kind of trigger, and a specific trigger is far easier to work on than a general resolve.'},{kind:'try',text:'Today, each time you leave a task, jot down what you were doing just before. Five entries is enough to start seeing a pattern.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You buy noise-cancelling headphones and turn off every notification, but you still lose focus. What is the likeliest reason?',options:['You are leaving the work yourself at the hard moments','The headphones are not blocking enough of the noise','You need a much stronger general resolve to concentrate','Some notifications must still be getting through somehow'],answer:0,why:'Those fixes target outside interruptions. The self-started kind, arriving at the hard bit, is untouched.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'WHERE DISTRACTION COMES FROM',belief:'Distraction is mostly something done to you: alerts, noise, other people.',reality:'The essay shifts the weight. In observational studies of knowledge work, a large share of interruptions are self-initiated. They are also the part no tool addresses.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Much interruption is self-started, and it is not random. It arrives at moments of difficulty, ambiguity or tedium, and it feels like a decision about what to do next rather than an escape.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the timing matters',text:'The switch comes precisely when the work stops being fluent. That is also when it starts being valuable: the unclear paragraph, the fault you do not understand yet. So self-interruption is not spread evenly across the work. It concentrates its cost on the parts that most needed you.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Nothing pinged and nobody came in. The writing stops partway down the page, and the writer is leaving the room on their own decision.'},{kind:'term',word:'Trigger',meaning:'Here, the specific thing in the seconds before you leave: a sentence you cannot finish, an ambiguous instruction, a dull stretch. For a given person it tends to be the same category each time.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why the trigger is tractable',text:'A general resolve to concentrate gives you nothing to act on. A trigger does. If you always leave when the next step is unclear, the fix is making next steps clear. The essay calls the trigger far more tractable than the resolve, because it is specific and it repeats.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Not every self-interruption is an escape. Sometimes leaving is a real decision that something else matters more. The difficulty is that an escape always presents itself as exactly that kind of decision. Noticing is also harder than it sounds: the trigger lasts seconds and is forgotten by the time you see that you have left.'},{kind:'recap',points:['A large share of interruptions are self-started.','They cluster where the work turns hard, unclear or dull.','Find your trigger; resolve alone gives you nothing to act on.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'TRANSFER IT',prompt:'A company bans phones in meetings and adds quiet hours, yet staff report no better focus. Which explanation fits this idea best?',options:['Quiet hours do nothing about people leaving tasks at hard moments','The bans were too short to make any real difference to focus','Staff resent rules, and the resentment itself damages focus','Focus cannot be changed by anything about the workplace'],answer:0,why:'The measures target external interruption. The self-started kind, set off by difficulty, is left exactly where it was.'}]}},'why-you-reopen-the-same-tab_4':{pictureCaption:'He fitted the bolt himself, to keep himself at the work. Now his own hand is sliding it back.',bridge:'Idea 3 found where the urge to leave comes from. This one explains why blocking the exits rarely holds.',basics:{title:'Why blocking stuff doesn\u2019t last',beats:[{kind:'scene',kicker:'THE HIDDEN CONSOLE',text:'You ask your dad to hide your games console until your project is done. Day one, it works. Day two, you read comics instead. By day three you have found where he hid it.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Blocking the fun thing does not fix why you wanted to leave. The real reason is that the work feels hard. Make that part easier, and you will want to escape less.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'He put the bolt on the door himself, to keep himself working. Now his own hand is sliding it back.'},{kind:'analogy',kicker:'LIKE A FENCE ON A SHORTCUT',text:'The park puts a fence across a muddy shortcut. People just walk round the end of the fence. They still want the shortcut, because the long way is a pain.'},{kind:'explain',title:'What works better',text:'Most people leave when they don\u2019t know what to do next. So make the next step really clear. Not *do the project*. Try *write three facts about frogs*. A clear step is much easier to stay with.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Blocking',items:['Hide the game','Works for a day or two','The want to leave stays']},right:{title:'Fixing the hard bit',items:['Make the next step clear','Tends to last longer','Less reason to leave']}},{kind:'question',prompt:'Leo keeps leaving his project to watch videos. His mum blocks the video site, so he reads sports news instead. What would help most?',options:['Make his next step clear, like *write the first line*','Block the sports news site as well','Take away all screens for a whole week'],answer:0,why:'The want to leave just moved. A clear next step takes away the reason he wanted to leave.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'myth',kicker:'THE OBVIOUS FIX',belief:'Distraction is a problem of access, so the fix is to make distractions harder to reach.',reality:'Blockers work briefly and then get switched off, because they leave the cause alone. The urge to leave comes from the task itself: this is hard, and I would like it to stop.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Blocking tools treat the symptom. The impulse to leave is made by something in the task, so block one exit and it finds another, or you disable the block. Change what happens at the moment of difficulty instead.'},{kind:'flow',kicker:'WHERE THE URGE GOES',nodes:['The task turns hard','Urge to leave','Favourite site is blocked','Another site, or one click to unblock'],note:'The block changed where the urge went. It did not change the urge.'},{kind:'explain',title:'When blockers do help',text:'There is a narrow case. If the habit itself is the main problem, and the underlying work is fine, a blocker can break the habit. That case exists. The essay argues it is less common than the tools assume.'},{kind:'example',kicker:'MAKING THE NEXT STEP PLAIN',text:'Your list says *work on the proposal*. At the first hard moment there is nothing obvious to do, so you drift. Rewrite it as *draft the two-sentence summary of the budget*. Now the next action is plain, and there is far less ambiguity to escape from.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'He fitted the bolt himself, to keep himself at the work. Now his own hand is sliding it back.'},{kind:'try',text:'Before you start a block of work, write the very next action so specifically that you could begin it without deciding what it means.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You install a site blocker, and within a week you are switching it off most afternoons. What does this idea suggest you change?',options:['What happens at the hard moment: make the next step clear','The blocker: choose one that is much harder to disable','Your hours: work only in the mornings, when it holds','Nothing: keep at it until the habit finally breaks'],answer:0,why:'A stronger lock leaves the urge in place. A specific next step removes much of what the urge was escaping.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'explain',kicker:'WHERE THIS FOLLOWS FROM',text:'The previous idea placed the urge to leave inside the task: difficulty, ambiguity, distaste. This one draws the consequence for a whole category of tools. If the cause sits in the task, anything that acts on the destination is treating a symptom.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Blockers fail over time because they remove an exit, not the reason for leaving. Durable fixes act upstream, at the moment of difficulty, by making the next action specific enough that there is nothing unclear to escape from.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'What blockers act on',items:['The destination','What is reachable','The habit of going there']},right:{title:'What drives the leaving',items:['The task','Difficulty, ambiguity, distaste','This is hard; I want it to stop']},note:'Act on the left and the right column is untouched.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why durability is poor',text:'Two routes defeat a blocker. The impulse finds another outlet, because it was never about that site. Or the tool is disabled, and disabling is one click, made at exactly the moment you most want to leave. The person you are guarding against holds the key.'},{kind:'term',word:'Upstream',meaning:'Acting on a cause before its effect appears. Here: changing the task at the point it becomes unclear, rather than guarding the places you run to once it has.'},{kind:'limits',text:'The essay concedes a real case for blockers: where the habit is the main problem and the work itself is fine. Its claim about trials is also general; it says only that what holds up tends to act at the moment of difficulty. And a specific next action is not always available. In a truly open problem, working out the next step is the hard part.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'The bolt was his own idea, fitted to keep himself in. The hand sliding it back is his too.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'WHICH CASE IS IT?',prompt:'For which person is a blocker most likely to be the right tool?',options:['Someone who checks a site by reflex, even when the work is clear','Someone who leaves whenever the next step of a report is unclear','Someone who disables every blocker within days of installing it','Someone who finds the task distasteful and flees it when possible'],answer:0,why:'That is the essay\u2019s narrow case: the habit is the problem and the work is fine. In the others the cause is in the task and will outlast the block.'}]}},'why-you-reopen-the-same-tab_5':{pictureCaption:'Work left mid-joint on purpose, the next cut already marked. Tomorrow starts from a known point, not from a blank.',bridge:'Idea 1 named the cost of coming back. This one shrinks it in advance, by choosing where you stop.',basics:{title:'Stop in the middle, on purpose',beats:[{kind:'story',kicker:'A WRITER\u2019S HABIT',text:'Ernest Hemingway was a famous American writer. He had an odd habit. He stopped writing for the day while it was still going well, and while he knew what would happen next.'},{kind:'guess',prompt:'Why would anyone stop while it is going well?',options:['So the next day starts easily','Because he got bored of it','To keep the story short'],answer:0,reveal:'The next morning he did not face a blank page. He knew just what to write first. So starting again was easy.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Don\u2019t stop at a neat finish, or when you are worn out. Stop in the middle of an easy bit, when you know what comes next. Then coming back is quick.'},{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Stop at the end',items:['Next time starts from nothing','You must work out what to do','Easy to put off']},right:{title:'Stop in the middle',items:['Next time starts from somewhere','You know the first step','Easy to begin']}},{kind:'picture',caption:'The cut is half made. The next bit is already marked. Whoever comes back knows just where to start.'},{kind:'try',text:'Tonight, stop your homework one step early, at an easy bit. Write the next step on a sticky note. Tomorrow, start there.'},{kind:'question',prompt:'You are drawing a comic over a few nights. Where is the best place to stop tonight?',options:['Halfway through a box you know how to finish','At the end of a page, all neat and done','When you get stuck and feel too tired to go on'],answer:0,why:'You will know just what to draw first next time. Stopping when stuck means coming back to the hard bit with no plan.'}]},standard:{beats:[{kind:'guess',kicker:'FRIDAY, HALF PAST FIVE',prompt:'A long piece of work needs several more days. Where is the best place to stop this evening?',options:['At a clean finish, with the section done','Mid-way through an easy part, next step noted','Whenever you run out of energy'],answer:1,reveal:'A clean finish feels tidy, but it means Monday starts from nothing. Stopping mid-way, knowing what comes next, means Monday starts from somewhere.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Stop deliberately in the middle of something, at a point where you know exactly what comes next, and write that next step down. Coming back becomes far cheaper.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why it works',text:'The cost of returning is mostly the cost of working out where you were. A note that states the next action removes most of it, and writing the note takes about fifteen seconds. You pay a tiny cost now to cancel a large one later.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'A WRITER\u2019S RULE',text:'Ernest Hemingway\u2019s version was to stop while the writing was still going well and while he knew what would happen next. The next session was then not a blank to start but a known point to continue from.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The part that feels wrong',text:'Most people stop at exhaustion. That means resuming from a point of difficulty with no plan, which is exactly the setup most likely to send you off to some other tab. Stopping while it is going well feels wasteful. It is what makes the next start easy.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Left mid-joint on purpose, the next cut already marked. Tomorrow starts from a known point.'},{kind:'question',kicker:'APPLY IT',prompt:'You finish a tough section of a report at six and feel drained. Which way of stopping applies this idea?',options:['Write a line naming the first step of the next section, then stop','Push on into the next section until you cannot think straight','Just stop, since the section is finished and everything is tidy','Plan out the whole of next week in detail before you leave'],answer:0,why:'You cannot stop mid-way now, but a note naming the next action still gives tomorrow a known starting point.'}]},deep:{beats:[{kind:'compare',kicker:'TWO WAYS TO END A SESSION',left:{title:'Clean finish',items:['Section done, desk clear','Next session starts from a blank','First move must be worked out']},right:{title:'Deliberate loose end',items:['Stopped mid-task, on purpose','Next action written down','First move already known']},note:'The essay\u2019s claim is that the second is far cheaper to return to.'},{kind:'idea',text:'Leave a deliberate loose end. Stop where you know exactly what comes next, ideally while the work is still going well, and resuming becomes far cheaper than starting from a clean finish.'},{kind:'explain',title:'The mechanism',text:'This idea completes the first one. If the return cost is largely the cost of working out where you were, the cheapest fix is to record where you were before leaving. A note stating the next action does that in about fifteen seconds. It is an instruction to your future self, written by the only person who knows.'},{kind:'story',kicker:'A WRITER\u2019S PRACTICE',text:'Hemingway\u2019s version was to stop while it was still going well and while he knew what would happen next. Writers have used the habit for about a century. The essay argues that it generalises to most work with a state to rebuild: code, analysis, design, any long piece.'},{kind:'explain',title:'Why not stop at exhaustion',text:'Stopping at exhaustion feels natural, because it is when you cannot go on. But it means the next session opens at a point of difficulty with no plan. That is the configuration the earlier ideas warned about: the hard, unclear moment where self-interruption clusters and the tab gets reopened.'},{kind:'limits',text:'Some work has little state to rebuild, and there a clean finish costs nothing. Stopping while it goes well also has a price: you give up a fluent stretch, and the flow may not return on cue. And the note only helps if it is specific. *Carry on with section 3* names a place, not a next action.'},{kind:'picture',caption:'Left mid-joint on purpose, the next cut already marked. Nothing tidied, because tidiness was never the point.'},{kind:'recap',points:['Return cost is mostly working out where you were.','Write the next action down before you stop.','Stop while it is going well, not when it turns hard.']},{kind:'question',kicker:'SPOT THE MISUSE',prompt:'Which of these misapplies the idea?',options:['Ending each day on the hardest problem, to face it fresh','Stopping halfway through an easy task, next line noted','Leaving a draft mid-sentence when you know how it ends','Ending a meeting with a written list of who does what next'],answer:0,why:'Stopping on the hard problem means resuming at a point of difficulty with no plan, the setup most likely to send you elsewhere.'}]}}}},3042,[]);
